You should talk to her.Talk to Brútha'Time to get going. If you're ready, I mean. I've rested for long enough.'Brútha surveys the busy scene.

'Wish I could lock myself away to carve in peace -- anything to avoid talking to people! Let someone else take care of the business side of things, so I can focus on what I actually care about. Not much that can be done about it though, unless you want to...? Hah, just kidding.

'I've written several notices promoting my work. Could you could post them around the market-place? In the meantime, I'll have to negotiate with the town criers.

'Once we've finished, let's meet back here.''Phew, that's finally done. One of the town criers tried to wheedle as much coin as he could out of me, but he relented eventually. Why is handling this sort of thing so difficult?

'Let's rest a bit; all of that chatter has me spent.'

After some digging around in her pockets, Brútha produces a small piece of wood along with a whittling knife. Upon closer inspection, the wood appears to be a still-undefined animal figurine.

'Remember the woodcutter I made a deal with? Obviously the fox isn't quite there yet -- been making lots of rough cuts -- but it's taking shape! Slowly but surely.''Well, we've done all that we can here today. Now it's just a matter of waiting for someone to show interest in my work. If something like that even happens, that is.

'Compared to crafts like chairs and tables, toys aren't a necessity. But I can't help it -- maybe I'm being stubborn, but I like toy-making.'With skill, help, and a little luck, Brútha has secured her first patron.You have accompanied Brútha to the home of Aslaug and Astrith.

