You should talk to her.Talk to BrúthaThe woodcutter has come to meet Brútha and discuss their deal.Listen to the conversation'Go on ahead, ${PLAYER}. I'll catch up in a bit!'When you ask about Astrith's fascination with horses, Aslaug lets out a bone-weary sigh. 

'Oh, my silly child... she dreams constantly of riding a horse one day. Owning a horse is out of the question, and besides, how would she even be able to ride one in her delicate state?

'It's natural that you would come to the conclusion of making her a horse toy. Though she already has enough of them, I won't stop you from creating another. However, I have a single request: please do not make something that would encourage physical activity. The added stress would not be good for her.

'That must be enough information, yes? Then I shall see you out.''Thank you for coming.

'As I've mentioned, I'd like you to make a toy for my daughter, Astrith. Illness often claims her and she languishes indoors for days; thus her friends are few and far between.

'If you'll follow me, she might have more thoughts to share on the matter.''What a dilemma I've landed myself in. Don't you reckon Astrith would enjoy having a hobby horse? Her dreams might not be realistic, but she can still pretend -- she probably already does!

'However, Aslaug's request has put a damper on the idea... going against her would sour things between us. I could create a horse figure with adjustable legs instead.

'Still, how can she be the judge of Astrith's strength? Only Astrith would know, and she can't learn when to catch her breath if she's not given that chance at all.

'What's your opinion on this, ${PLAYER}?''While we're here, there's one more thing I need to do.'

Brútha pulls out the fox figurine from her pocket. The rough cuts that had previously formed the fox have long since vanished, replaced by smoother, more deliberate cuts. She rotates the figurine in her hand, a bright grin forming on her face. 

'Remember the woodcutter we helped? I hope he's happy with this. There's nothing better than the feeling I get from a job well done.

