'Go on without me. I trust your judgement and your abilities.'Golwen scratches the underside of the cat's chin, her normally rigid frown softening.

'What noble and beautiful creatures. Dutiful in their hunt for pests, regardless of whether they join us on our ships, or stay behind on the piers.

'I hope you do not mind lingering here for a bit longer. My leg protests at the thought of taking another step.''I do not believe I have shown you this yet.'

Golwen hands over the flute for you to examine: wooden, rudimentary, but still quite functional.

'It was carved by a friend of mine. In fact, she carved all of our instruments.'

As she gazes out towards the sea, something catches in her throat. She swallows hard.

'If you do not mind -- while we rest here, I have a story to tell about her.

'During one voyage, a bird landed in her care....'The bird's beady eyes follow Rikháwi's every movement, but it makes no attempt to avoid her careful hands. Its laboured breathing further exposes its exhausted state.The bird does not stir.Golwen absentmindedly runs her hands through the purring cat's fur.

'This story did not have a happy ending, I'm afraid. I hope you have a better one to share.'As you tell your story, time slips away into the deepening shadows of the evening. Golwen does not seem to notice.

It is only when you finish your story that she finally speaks.

'Thank you for indulging me, ${PLAYER}.

