Dawn breaks, before long.'Our arrival here was tardier than I'd hoped, but there is none other than myself to blame. I should have accounted for my leg and the slow pace I would set.

'I will work quickly to prepare our bed-rolls and the camp-fire. In the meantime, there should be dry branches nearby for you to gather.'

Golwen peeks over her shoulder at the sea and wipes her palms across her trousers.

'Make haste, before the dark sets in.'Before long, a camp-fire crackles happily upon the sand.

'This will keep the night chill at bay.'

Golwen's eyes have already left the crackling fire, instead fixating on the sea once again.

'It is time to retire, I believe. Walking here took quite some effort, and further aggravating my leg would not be ideal.

'Good night, ${PLAYER}. Until the morning.'You drift off to sleep...'I am sorry I interrupted your rest, ${PLAYER}. Old memories of the times Rikháwi and I spent here began to plague me, so I gave up on sleeping.

'I neglected to fully explain why I wanted set up camp here. I wished to be close to the ocean, but more than that: I wanted to see if I was still afraid of it.

'Of course, any good sailor is afraid of the sea. However, that fear is borne of respect. My fear stems from elsewhere: a paralyzing terror that grips me when I take a single step into the water. I -- I tried to ignore that feeling, as you can see. But...'

She sighs.

'Have you had dreams of the dead before, ${PLAYER}?''I have a dream that returns like the tides, time and time again, of standing on a nameless, empty shore. I see Rikháwi standing in the water far from shore, looking at me. I try to reach her, yet I only sink into the depths. Then I wake.'

A dry chuckle escapes from her, but there is no humour to it.

'You do not need to tell me what such dreams mean.

'During one storm, the ship sprang leaks in the lower decks. Heavy cargo pinned down my leg and prevented my escape. Struggling did nothing; the water rose ever higher. But Rikháwi helped free me, gave me my life in exchange for losing hers. What have either of us done to deserve such fates?

'The crew and I were the only ones who mourned her. Like me, she had no one left on land.

'I spent the last year trying to avoid thoughts of her. However, the harder I tried, the more furious these memories became. They would not let me forget.

'Tell me ${PLAYER}: do you think peace is possible for the living?''The sea still scares me. Even while I worked on the ship, I could not ignore it -- that faint dread itching at the back of my mind. To depend so heavily on a force that could easily claim my life... that has claimed the life of one close to me.

'I wonder if such feelings will ever fade. Maybe they will dull, with time. However, tonight, I will remain awake and watch the stars.

