Most are certainly local to the Shire; others originate from elsewhere, particularly the northern region of Ered Luin.These notes consist of loose, but orderly, pages with information on various plants and their applications in cooking. Everything mentioned appears to be local, but the author meticulously details everything there is to know about each plant. It is as if she were addressing these notes to one who has never stepped foot into the Shire.Inside this nondescript wooden box, you discover pieces of parchment. Each features a recipe written in small, tidy lettering.

A smattering of splotches and smudges adorn the edges of the recipes, signaling frequent usage in the past. You're doubtful that's the case now.'Some of those plants in the journal came from up north? As in, where the dwarves live? My grandmother often left the Shire in order to collect and try new cooking ingredients, but that's all I know. Nobody was interested in finding out where she went or what she did. Nor was she willing to tell me anything.

'But why go north? Surely the dwarves have nothing that would warrant travelling so far from home for!

'It's just... I simply cannot imagine anything out there being better than what we already have here. Why, I've already been almost attacked by bandits and a bear -- a bear! And these incidents happened right outside the Shire!'

Haldo pauses to breathe deeply and quell his rising distress.

'Apologies again... how embarrassing. If I'm to solve this mystery, I need to be as brave as my grandmother was.

'Which, speaking of the mystery...'

He rummages through the recipe box before pulling out one in particular.

'This is it, right here.'The recipe calls for pork and turnips as its main ingredients. While the recipe appears complete at first glance, you realize the instructions only make vague references to seasonings. The ingredients list does not contain any relevant information.'My grandmother used to cook this for me because it was the only way I'd eat turnips. I've been trying to complete the recipe for years now, but I can't replicate the seasoning mixture. Nor can I accurately describe how it tasted -- all I have to refer to are faint memories.

'If the dwarves are the only clue we've got, then we'll make plans to visit them. We can ask about local seasonings, hopefully get the answers I've been seeking, then leave straightaway.

