You should speak with Gil Plover, for you can show him that you are the fastest maze-runner of all.Talk to Gil Plover outside the maze in the Horse-fields'Yes, well. You really can't seem to get enough of that hedge maze. No matter! No one will ever beat my best time. Only one minute and a few seconds more!

'I am sorry to have to break this to you, but it is folly to think that you can match my time. Try if you must, but I simply do not wish for your hopes to be disappointed. It can be so discouraging to fail.

'You still mean to try, do you? Very well. You have one minute. I am not worried. No one will ever beat my time. Now please, leave me be! I am trying to enjoy the spring before it fades into another sweltering summer, and I cannot do it if you are hovering about me like a gnat.''Don't talk to me.''Please do not come around and bother me anymore, ${PLAYER}. Your very presence somehow saps the beauty out of a fine spring day, and I find that I do not enjoy it quite so much when you are near.

'Come to think of it, I do not like mazes after all. They are rather too green for my liking. Too much green is unpleasant, in certain lights.

'Here, take your reward. Enjoy your silly maze if such a silly, silly, pointless thing can truly be enjoyed.'Birthdays should be joyous celebrations, but when you find yourself amid danger and adventure they can be easily forgotten.One of the guards at the entrance to Lake-town might be able to tell you more about the last days of Esgaroth, before the dragon Smaug was defeated. On this anniversary of Bilbo's arrival at Esgaroth, what do the residents of the town remember of the Burglar?Travel to the entrance of Lake-town and speak to the guardsOne of the guards at the entrance to Lake-town might be able to tell you more about the last days of Esgaroth, before the dragon Smaug was defeated. On this anniversary of Bilbo's arrival at Esgaroth, what do the residents of the town remember of the Burglar?Talk to Várthrek at the entrance to Lake-townLóskarl is the oldest resident of Lake-town, and he might be able to tell you what he remembers of the death of Smaug. Várthrek told you that Lóskarl often holds court in the Dragonbone Inn.Find the oldest resident of Lake-town and ask him about the death of SmaugLóskarl told you there were Raft-elves present when Bilbo and the dwarves came to Lake-town, and if they are still alive they might be able to tell you more than he can. You should visit Loeglond and see if you can find these Raft-elves.Travel to Loeglond, the city of the Raft-elvesYou learned that a number of Raft-elves were present at Esgaroth when the dwarves and Bilbo arrived to reclaim the Lonely Mountain. If you can find some of these Elves in Loeglond, they might tell you more about their experience.Celethros is somewhere near the dungeons of Felegoth, in Eryn Lasgalen.Find Celethros somewhere near the dungeons of Felegoth, in Eryn LasgalenBilbo Baggins is in the Hall of Fire, in Rivendell.Talk to Bilbo in the Hall of Fire, in RivendellAs you stand on the shore of the Long Lake, your thoughts turn to the adventures of Bilbo Baggins and the Dwarves of Thorin's Company. The old hobbit has shared some of the tale with you, and you have heard portions of it from others who were there or knew of the events. Today must be quite near to the anniversary of Bilbo's arrival at Esgaroth, mustn't it? The city did not stand for long after that, but neither did the dragon Smaug.

Your curiosity stirred by these musings, you decide to speak with the guards at the entrance to Lake-town and see what they might tell you about Bilbo's role in the last days of Esgaroth.As you stand on the shore of the Long Lake, your thoughts turn to the adventures of Bilbo Baggins and the Dwarves of Thorin's Company. The old hobbit has shared some of the tale with you, and you have heard portions of it from others who were there or knew of the events. Today must be quite near to the anniversary of Bilbo's arrival at Esgaroth, mustn't it? The city did not stand for long after that, but neither did the dragon Smaug.

Your curiosity stirred by these musings, you decide to speak with the guards at the entrance to Lake-town and see what they might tell you about Bilbo's role in the last days of Esgaroth.As you stand on the shore of the Long Lake, your thoughts turn to the adventures of Bilbo Baggins and the dwarves of Thorin's Company. The old hobbit has shared some of the tale with you, and you have heard portions of it from others who were there or knew of the events. Today must be quite near to the anniversary of Bilbo's arrival at Esgaroth, mustn't it? The city did not stand for long after that, but neither did the dragon Smaug.

Your curiosity stirred by these musings, you decide to speak with the guards at the entrance to Lake-town and see what they might tell you about Bilbo's role in the last days of Esgaroth.'What do I remember about the death of Smaug? Are you having a laugh at my expense, friend? That was more than seventy-five years ago! Smaug died before I was ever born!'

Várthrek eyes you warily for a moment, but then he decides you mean no harm by it and he relaxes his guard.

'I have heard stories, of course. When I was just a boy we used to sneak down to the remains of Esgaroth and spy on the wreckage of the old city. We used to think Smaug himself might rise from the water and chomp us whole! His bones are visible now, but it was not always so. Perhaps the Lake does not want to hold him any longer.

'Now that I think on it, there is one with whom you might speak. Old Lóskarl was a young boy when Smaug died; now he is the oldest resident of our city. His memory is not what it once was, but he might share what he does remember with you. He often holds court in the back of the Dragonbone Inn. You might find his talk of interest, friend.''When I was just a boy we used to sneak down to the remains of Esgaroth and spy on the wreckage of the old city. We used to think Smaug himself might rise from the water and chomp us whole! His bones are visible now, but it was not always so. Perhaps the Lake does not want to hold him any longer.

'Now that I think on it, there is one with whom you might speak. Old Lóskarl was a young boy when Smaug died; now he is the oldest resident of our city. His memory is not what it once was, but he might share what he does remember with you. He often holds court in the back of the Dragonbone Inn. You might find his talk of interest, friend.''You're looking for Lóskarl? For years it was his custom to sit in the corner chair upstairs, but climbing the steps has proven beyond his capability in recent years, and now I allow him to sit in the back. He'll nurse a mug of beer for the better part of an afternoon and desires naught else but a bowl of soup to slake his appetite.

'Speak to him freely, but do not upset him. He is a welcome patron and I do not want him unduly bothered within my establishment.''Eh? What'sh that?'

Lóskarl squints at you distrustfully, but when you explain that you'd like to hear what he remembers of the death of Smaug he favours you with a toothless grin.

'I wash jusht a boy back then, no more'n sheven yearsh old. But shtill, I remember it like it were yeshterday. A pack of dwarfsh came to Old Lake-town, my dad took me to shee them. They were going to claim the Mountain back from Shmaug, they were. It washn't sho eashy, wash it? No. It wash Bard the Bowman who kilt the Dragon, not any of thoshe dwarfsh or their little friend.'

You ask the old man about the friend of the dwarves, and he shakes his head.

'At firsht I thought he wash jusht a lad, shame ash me, or a very young dwarf. Hish name wash Bilbo, it wash, and he didn't look like much of a hero. I don't remember much more than that. If'n you want to learn more about him, you should talk to shome of the Raft-elfsh. I remember they had wordsh with the Mashter, and went off inna huff. Being Elfsh, they might shtill be alive, and might tell you more'n I can.''If'n you want to learn more about that Bilbo fellow, you should talk to shome of the Raft-elfsh. I remember they had wordsh with the Mashter, and went off inna huff. Being Elfsh, they might shtill be alive, and might tell you more'n I can.''Did you enjoy speaking with Lóskarl? I hope he was helpful to you, and you did not find his speech difficult to understand. You get used to his manner of speaking in time.''If'n you want to learn more about that Bilbo fellow, you should talk to shome of the Raft-elfsh. I remember they had wordsh with the Mashter, and went off inna huff. Being Elfsh, they might shtill be alive, and might tell you more'n I can.''I understand you are seeking information about Thorin Oakenshield? He has been dead these many years, and the circumstances of his return to Erebor are shrouded in mystery. Is that why you have come to me?

'I and a number of other Elves from Loeglond were dining with the Master of Esgaroth when Thorin presented himself to the guards of the city. We knew at once that some underhanded business was afoot, for these same dwarves had been apprehended in the wood and imprisoned by King Thranduil. He had not set them free; how came these dwarves to escape?

'I set this question aside during the events that followed, and while some evil came of them so too did much that was good. But know this: my companion Celethros did not forget the question of Thorin's peculiar escape, or the curious fellow who accompanied his band. This was no dwarf, but a hobbit. His name was Bilbo, as I recall. My friend Celethros has spent a great deal of time puzzling over the mystery, and every few years he makes it a point to return to Felegoth to search the dungeons for some clue. He is likely there right now! If you find him, he may be able to tell you more.''You speak of these events from yesteryear as if they had just transpired, ${PLAYER}. You wish to hear if any of my people witnessed the return of Thorin Oakenshield to Esgaroth and the Lonely Mountain? For what purpose do you seek this knowledge?

'Ah, the reason matters not to me. But I do have the information you desire, for a number of my Raft-elves did see the arrival of Thorin and his dwarves; they were bothered enough by the mystery of his arrival that they spoke of it to me angrily and often. Speak to Aeleth if you want to hear the account from one who was there. I saw her last on the eastern side of Loeglond, so you may seek her there.''You seek Raft-elves who witnessed the return of Thorin Oakenshield to Esgaroth and the Lonely Mountain? I am sorry I cannot be of help to you, but I do not know of any such Elves.''The arrival of Thorin Oakenshield at Esgaroth, nearly eighty years ago? That is quite a long time to wait for answers, ${PLAYER}! Some of my people do travel to Lake-town from time to time. If you ask around Loeglond you may learn if any were present when the dwarves came back to Erebor.''My companion Celethros did not forget the question of Thorin's peculiar escape, or the curious fellow who accompanied his band. This was no dwarf, but a hobbit. His name was Bilbo, as I recall. My friend Celethros has spent a great deal of time puzzling over the mystery, and every few years he makes it a point to return to Felegoth to search the dungeons for some clue. He is likely there right now! If you find him, he may be able to tell you more.''Celethros? He was just here, studying one of the cells. I heard him say something about the wine cellar. Perhaps that is where he has gone.''What do I know about Thorin Oakenshield's return to Erebor almost eighty years ago? Not as much as I wish I did! He and the others of his band emerged as from myth, no more corporeal than the stories of the champions I was told in my youth. How else could they escape from my king's dungeon? I have laboured for years on the unravelling of this impossible problem, and I am no nearer to its solution than I was on that day in the Master of Esgaroth's feasting hall! I returned at once to Felegoth with Aeleth, and after reporting what we had seen to King Thranduil, she put it from her mind. But I could not, and every year on the anniversary of this mystery I have returned to Felegoth to try and puzzle out a solution. But always it eludes me!

'It is true that the chief guard and Galion the butler shared too much of the king's wine the night the dwarves disappeared from their cells, but when the discovery was made the keys were still upon the guard's belt. He would not have let them out. And yet, presupposing that a dwarf managed to burgle the keys, what burglar would have given back the ring of keys once the deed was performed? Furthermore, no dwarf could have gotten the key-ring from within his own cell, not even Thorin Oakenshield, who was kept separately from the others. King Thranduil believed that the dwarves did not know Thorin was imprisoned, and neither did he know that they were captured and held. If one or the other were to escape, that should have resulted in one or the other being left behind. They could not have passed messages to each other, ${PLAYER}, unless they managed it through the guards somehow. But I can conceive of no means by which any Elf-guard would have willingly done this!

'There is more. The gates of Felegoth at that time were sealed by magic, and none but an Elf could open them. Even if the dwarves somehow escaped from their cells, they should have been trapped within the cavern and easily recaptured! Could one dwarf have slipped through the gate in the wake of an unobservant Elf? Perhaps, but unlikely. Could such a number of dwarves pass unnoticed? Absolutely not!'

Celethros sees you looking around at the barrels and casks, and he shakes his head.

'I see your thinking, friend, but that would not work either. These wine-barrels might be large enough to hold a single dwarf, but they are difficult and time-consuming to open. One year I spent the greater part of an evening trying to open a number of them, but they make a great deal of noise and cannot be easily closed, and certainly not from the inside. One of the butter tubs or apple tubs might serve, but it would require a dwarf to remain outside. If there were an accomplice to seal the casks, it could nearly be done, but how then could the accomplice follow his embarrelled friends? I am no closer to solving this mystery. Perhaps next year I will think of something!''I cannot think of any way these dwarves could have escaped, ${PLAYER}! Not without powerful magic, at least, but that is ridiculous! What dwarf could weave a spell to befuddle an Elf? None, and especially not Thorin Oakenshield or any of his companions!''Birthday wishes? Why, thank you very much, ${PLAYER}! It quite slipped my mind that it was that time of year again. It is not the first time that has happened to me. I spent my fifty-first birthday with a terrible cold, you know. I had just arrived at Esgaroth on Long Lake with Thorin and the dwarves, and was quite soaked through and water-logged from the trip down the Forest River. The dwarves were treated to many a banquet by the Master there, and the food was most delicious, but my cold was such that I could not thank anyone properly. "Thag you very buch," I remember saying.'

He smiles at the memory. You tell the old hobbit that one of the Raft-elves who was at Esgaroth has spent every year at this time trying to determine how the dwarves escaped from the Elvenking's dungeon without magic. Bilbo laughs at that.

'Well, he seems to have forgotten that there was another in Thorin's Company present at those feasts, but I would be lying if I said there was no magic involved at all. I should tell him the truth of it, or show him the account in my book, and unravel the mystery. Ah, I do not want to deprive him of this, ${PLAYER}. Every year at this time he tries to puzzle it out? Why, that sounds like I have given him a present with which to mark my birthday each year!

'When I was fifty-one, birthdays did not seem quite as important as they do now. Thank you for helping to mark the occasion with me, ${PLAYER}! It is good to celebrate one's birthday when it arrives in peace, for when you are in the middle of an adventure it can be harder to remember! Thank you for reminding me not to forget each birthday as it comes, my friend, mid-adventure or no!'There is much to do during the Yule Festival! Participate often to earn wonderful gifts!

<rgb=#FF0000>(There are twelve days worth of Festive Flurry and Spirit of Yule participation quests. Plan accordingly!)</rgb>Every day there is something to do in Frostbluff. Every day there is Yule! Come and join everyone in Frostbluff and participate in many Yule Festival activities to receive gifts!Complete 'A Festive Flurry (Intro)'Every day there is something to do in Frostbluff. Every day there is Yule! Come and join everyone in Frostbluff and participate in many Yule Festival activities for three more days!Complete 'A Festive Flurry (Intermediate)'Every day there is something to do in Frostbluff. Every day there is Yule! Come and join everyone in Frostbluff and participate in many Yule Festival activities for three more days!Complete 'A Festive Flurry (Advanced)'Every day there is something to do in Frostbluff. Every day there is Yule! Come and join everyone in Frostbluff and participate in many Yule Festival activities for three more days!Complete 'A Festive Flurry (Final)'Completed.Talk to Mara Sandydowns'Welcome to Frostbluff, my friend!

'If you're here for the Yule Festival, I'd recommend you get in the spirit of the season and participate in many of our fun Yule activities. If you're not here for the Yule Festival, maybe you should be!

'Festive folks that participate in many of our activities and show their true holiday spirit might just earn themselves some wonderful gifts!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: this quest cannot be cancelled.</rgb>'Goodness me, where does the time go? The Yule Festival is almost over!

'I must thank you, ${PLAYER}. You've done so much for this little town, and really made the festival special! I hope to see you next year, when I'll have a whole new set of gifts ready for you! Until then, Happy Yule!'There is much to do during the Harvest Festival! Participate often to earn wonderful gifts!

<rgb=#FF0000>(There are twenty days worth of  Bountiful Harvest and Spirited Harvest participation quests. Plan accordingly!)</rgb>Every day there is something to do in the Harvest Festival, both at the Party Tree, in Wistmead, and for Treats and Trickery.  Join in the autumnal activities to receive gifts!Complete 'A Bountiful Harvest (Intro)'Every day there is something to do in the Harvest Festival, both at the Party Tree, in Wistmead, and for Treats and Trickery.  Join in the autumnal activities for five more days!Complete 'A Bountiful Harvest (Intermediate)'Every day there is something to do in the Harvest Festival, both at the Party Tree, in Wistmead, and for Treats and Trickery.  Join in the autumnal activities for five more days!Complete 'A Bountiful Harvest (Advanced)'Every day there is something to do in the Harvest Festival, both at the Party Tree, in Wistmead, and for Treats and Trickery.  Join in the autumnal activities for five more days!Complete 'A Bountiful Harvest (Final)'You should return to Rosa Hornblower, who can be found near the Party Tree and in Wistmead.'I say, there is so much fun to be had around here, isn't there? I could go about from the first day until the very last and not get my fill of Haunted Burrows, Hedge Mazes, and Treats or Trickery!

'You feel the same, do you not, ${PLAYER}? Well, do not be shy! Seize the season. Persistent effort reaps rich rewards, as I like to say.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: this quest cannot be cancelled.</rgb>'Is the Harvest Festival almost over? Well, we have certainly had our fill, you and I. To be entirely honest, I think I am ready for a brief rest. After all, Yule is hardly around the corner!

'Jests aside, I thank you for joining me. You have made this year's Harvest all the richer by your joining in, I assure you, ${PLAYER}.'Walaric Goldworthy has become concerned that hobbits visiting Wistmead are not having fun - whether it is spooky or not!Spiritless hobbits can be found throughout Wistmead.

Walaric Goldworthy is concerned that hobbits visiting Wistmead are not celebrating in the proper spirit of the Harvest Festival. You should help them.Help hobbits celebrate in the spirit of the Harvest Festival (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Walaric Goldworthy can be found in Wistmead.

You have helped many hobbits rediscover the spirit of the Harvest Festival. You should return to Walaric Goldworthy.Talk to Walaric Goldworthy'It is horrible! Just horrible!

'I have been beset by complaints, and just a moment ago, I saw another hobbit sleeping in the field!

'Have I done something wrong? It seems that no matter what festivities I think up... I cannot impress them!

'Why don't you try to help them get in the spirit, ${PLAYER}? Show them what spooky fun can be had in Wistmead!'Well, well! I can hear the hoots and cheers from here!

'Perhaps I was a little too hasty in my thinking....

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. That will be all!'Hobnanigans is simple. You and your teammates must help urge a chicken into the blue goal as many times as possible within the allotted time. Protect your red goal from the other team scoring.The red sticks are on the field.

Haggo Boffin, the Captain of the Flying Feathers, has asked you to gather a red stick from the field and get ready to begin.Take a red stick from the fieldScore as many goals as you can within the allotted time.Play a game of HobnanigansDo not leave the field of play'So you want to join the Flying Feathers, do you? Fine then, come on and play a round of Hobnanigans. You must wait in the area for five others to join your team, and then you shall play for the right to become a winner in the great Chicken Chasing League of Eriador. This is no small honour!

'Simply gather a red stick from the field and get ready to begin. Hurry, hurry -- only the first six people to join the Flying Feathers can play this round.  The game will start when all twelve sticks have been taken!'Hobnanigans is simple. You and your teammates must help urge a chicken into the blue goal as many times as possible within the allotted time. Protect your red goal from the other team scoring.The red sticks are on the field.

