You have collected ten bottles of wine as Legolas asked. You should now challenge Gimli to 'battle' Legolas in a contest of fortitude.<rgb=#ff00ff>Challenge</rgb> Gimli to 'battle' LegolasGimli can be found in Merethrond in Minas Tirith (Midsummer).

Your challenge seems to have gotten Gimli's attention. You should talk to Gimli.Legolas can be found in Merethrond in Minas Tirith (Midsummer).

Gimli has enthusiastically accepted Legolas's challenge. You should talk to Legolas.'Mae govannen, ${PLAYER}. It is good to see you!

'Even just a short time ago, I could hardly have imagined sharing such a night with so many friends. Indeed, in the time of Elves, it has been but a moment since we battled together at the Hornburg.'

Legolas smiles to himself.

'Did you know that Gimli and I held a contest of sorts during that long night? Those we slew were counted in each of our favours, and by the dawn, Gimli had bested me by a mere Orc. Forty-two to my count of forty-one, as I recall.

'It seems to me that we are overdue for a rematch. Let us consider it a contest of fortitude. Wine for wine, we shall see which of us is the victor!

'Would you ask him on my behalf?'A contest of fortitude? Have you any idea how much I've had already!?

'No, no. You can tell Legolas I appreciate the offer, but this is not the time for such things!''I did not grudge him the game at the Hornburg. Perhaps Gimli fears a different outcome this time?'

Legolas smiles wryly.

'Ah, I fear I have chosen my words poorly to the mind of a dwarf. I think I know something certain to win Gimli's attention.

'Will you go down to the kitchens of Merethrond and find some bottles of wine for us? Ten ought to do.

'Once you have done that, challenge Gimli to 'battle' against me. If I have supposed correctly, I think he shall find himself unable to refuse!''Oh, that's how it's to be then? I'll show both of you!'

Gimli takes five of the bottles you're holding and slides them over to his end of the table.

'Do you see this, Legolas? I'll show you a thing or two about fortitude! Ha-ha!''Well, well. I suppose we shall see who is the victor by the end of Midsummer's Day!

'Thank you for playing along with our little game, ${PLAYER}.'With the One Ring destroyed and the Enemy undone, the Free Peoples of Middle-earth gather to Minas Tirith for a grand banquet commemorating the wedding of King Aragorn II Elessar to Queen Arwen Undómiel. Among the many in attendance, there sits a hobbit of some renown -- Meriadoc Brandybuck.

