'It is good to see you. Look about you: there is many a dwarf here, and all with something or other to do. I never thought that seeing these mountains would restore such vigour in me, but I feel better already.

'If you are looking for work, we have it, I promise you this.'The people of Trestlebridge need help, north of Bree-land along the Greenway.Trestlebridge can be found north of Bree along the Greenway. You should make your way there and speak with the folk who make the beleaguered town their home.Travel to the Trestlebridge in the North DownsTrestlebridge can be found north of Bree along the Greenway. You should make your way there and speak with the folk who make the beleaguered town their home.Talk to Marla Shelton in TrestlebridgeThe town of Trestlebridge is in a terrible state. They are besieged by Orcs at their northern and southern borders and are sending out general calls to any that they feel may be able to assist them.'Hullo, are you here to help? So many have gone missing or have wandered out to seek help, and fewer still have returned. Every night, it seems that the Orcs grow more aggressive and threaten us more.

'Trestlebridge could use a hero.'Candelleth, an Elf dwelling in the southern part of the Trollshaws, has need of aid.Echad Candelleth in Tâl Bruinen sits atop a cliff overlooking the great river. You should seek out Candelleth there and lend her what aid you may.Travel to the Southern TrollshawsEchad Candelleth in Tâl Bruinen sits atop a cliff overlooking the great river. You should seek out Candelleth there and lend her what aid you may.Talk to Candelleth at Echad Candelleth in Tâl BruinenThe Elf Candelleth may be found at a ruin bearing her name. The ruin stands atop a cliff overlooking the Bruinen, south beyond the Bruinen Gorges. She requires aid from any who will answer the call.'I am pleased that you have arrived. Angmarim skulk through this land, and there are refugees who need assistance. I will remain vigilant for any news of why these ill-meaning Men crawl through our lands.

