'Why this is no request at all, but a note of gratitude. Somehow, my tasks seem to be a bit lighter now.''Ah, thank you, my friend. Much better than delivering debt notices, is it not?

'If you have the time on the morrow, do come back. I shall have more letters to deliver, of that you can be certain.'The majestic plains of Rohan are in need of farmers and horse-friends. Burginda has suggested you might take the opportunity to purchase your own home in Rohan.Burginda can be found at Edoras or the Kingstead neighbourhoods.

You should talk to Burginda.Talk to Burginda'Good ${RACE}, you seem a well-travelled one, I dare say! You have just moved into the Households of the Kingstead, have you not? If you are looking for a home with a slow pace to life, you have come to the right place. Still, we pride ourselves on keeping the neighbourhood both clean and friendly. I hope that you will join us in building a brighter community, my friend!''Good, good! I have many things that need doing. If we all pitch in, they will be done in no time at all!'Darôma's helpers are not being very helpful and there are chores to be done.Darôma has offered to pay you to make the beds and clean the floors in the guest rooms of The Cracked Flagon.Make the beds (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Scrub the floors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have made the beds and scrubbed the floors of the guest rooms of The Cracked Flagon. You should return to Darôma inside the inn to collect your reward.Talk to Darôma'I am supposed to have bahâr here to help me, but those good-for-nothing freebooters seem allergic to work when there is land under their feet. If you would like to earn some coin, I will pay you to help me with the housekeeping. We have several guest cabins aboard The Cracked Flagon, and they all need their daily tidying up.

'They need their bunks made, and the floors scrubbed clean. It should not take long to do at all. Maybe once those worthless swabs see that they've been outdone by a visitor, they will pick up their lazy bones and get to work! I would not wager on it, though.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the current festival ends.</rgb>'Back already, Bahâr? I am glad someone around here will do some work. May you be an example to others! Here is the payment I promised. It will need to be done again tomorrow if you want more.'The Elves of Duillond have been told that the farmers of the Shire have infinitely more wisdom when it comes to dealing with garden pests.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the current festival ends. </rgb>The Elf Maengamiel can be found in the Garden of Duillond, watching helplessly over the shrew-ridden field.

You should tell Maengamiel about Bodo Burrows' proposition.Speak with Maengamiel in the Festival GardenNibs Cotton is at the Cotton's farm in Bywater, while Wald Sandson is at Sandson's Farm near Michel Delving. The Gaffer Hamfast Gamgee is in Hobbiton, of course.

You should speak with the gardeners of the Shire to learn about how hobbits deal with shrews.Speak with Nibs Cotton at the Cotton Farm in BywaterSpeak with Wald Sandson at Sandson's FarmSpeak with the Gaffer in HobbitonMaengamiel is in the Festival Garden in Duillond, watching over the shrew chaos.

You should tell Maengamiel what you learned from the three hobbit gardeners.Speak with Maengamiel in the Festival Garden'Well, I never thought I'd see the day when we hobbits would have these graceful old Elves beat, but it seems this is just such a day! This will make a grand story at the Ivy Bush.

'As you can imagine, we have many farms and gardens in the Shire, and naturally we have shrews to pester us as well. But I guarantee you that even the simplest hobbit has more wisdom in dealing with shrews than all these Elves combined!

'Don't believe me? Go talk to the farmers around Hobbiton and Michel Delving! See what happens when shrews dare to pester them! Then you can come back and tell these poor, hapless Elves what you've learned.''He said what? Why, that little....

'Nay, don't listen to me. There is no reason for me to take offence. Perhaps our little dirty-footed, cheeky, gap-toothed guest is correct. Perhaps we Elves have something to learn from these hobbits.

'They must be truly gifted farmers to have grown so fat. Yes -- do go to the Shire. Learn what you can from the dumplings -- I mean, halflings -- on how to best get rid of shrews.''You want to know how we keep shrews away from our fields? It's very simple! My cat here, Waldy, is the finest shrew-hunter in all the Shire. Watch what he can do!''Hello there! Shrews? Yes, we've been known to see a shrew or two. I'll tell you the secret to getting rid of them...talk VERY LOUDLY about winter being just around the corner.

'Shrews hibernate in winter, you see. Just talk about how cold it's getting, and how it's supposed to snow tomorrow. You'll see. They'll be running for their warm, cozy burrows in no time!''If you're wanting advice on gardening, you've come to the right hobbit. Shrews are nasty critters, determined to ruin my work. There are two ways to deal with them. Either trap them, or chuck an apple at them! Aim for the head if you decide to chuck an apple. I suppose stomping on them would work too, if you're quick.'Maengamiel listens keenly as you tell the tale of your journey in the Shire.

'That sounded like a productive venture, ${PLAYER}. Sleeping cats, lies about the season...but at least one hobbit resorts to similar methods to those we use here. I believe an apple hurled at the unruly beasts would give me a great sense of satisfaction.

'Know-it-all hobbits don't seem to know so much after all.'You have acquired a wonderful collection of Huorns.You have acquired a wonderful collection of Huorns.Call the Black Huorn with Honeycomb to your sideCall the Fangorn Huorn to your sideCall the Snowy Fangorn Huorn to your sideBingo has asked you to procure a scary book from the library in the Great Smials, in Tuckborough.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Donnamira Took is in the library of Great Smials, in Tuckborough.Talk to Donnamira Took in the library of Great SmialsOne of the book-shelves in the library of Great Smials may have a suitably scary book to serve Bingo's needs.Donnamira Took is in the library of Great Smials, in Tuckborough.Talk to Donnamira Took in the library of Great SmialsBingo Boffin is near the Party Tree, in Hobbiton.'You have already agreed to join us on the Greenfields for the sharing of spooky stories, but now that I know Dinodas believes himself to be above scaring I fear my own stories might not be, well, frightening enough! I need to do some more research, ${PLAYER}!

'Can I ask you to make the trip to Tuckborough and to secure the assistance of Donnamira Took, the librarian of Great Smials? She has an unrivalled understanding of the contents of the library, and she will certainly know where to find a tome or two with the scariest of hobbit stories within!

'Find me suitably scary stories, and I will make one or two further arrangements, and we will be ready for the telling of tales when it is dark enough!''Can I ask you to make the trip to Tuckborough and to secure the assistance of Donnamira Took, the librarian of Great Smials? She has an unrivalled understanding of the contents of the library, and she will certainly know where to find a tome or two with the scariest of hobbit stories within!

'Find me suitably scary stories, and I will make one or two further arrangements, and we will be ready for the telling of tales when it is dark enough!''You are looking for a book of scary stories? I cannot say that any come immediately to mind, for hobbits do not generally write down such things, though they do enjoy sharing them in person... especially in warm comfort, with the dark of night safely beyond shuttered panes and far away.

'You are welcome to look for yourself, ${PLAYER}.''You are welcome to look for a scary book yourself, but none come to mind at the moment.''Well, this is very curious. I do not believe I have ever seen this book before. The letters on the cover are very faded and most indistinct; I cannot read very much of the title, but the first word seems to be "Chill." I believe it promises "Chilling Tales," and a cursory examination seems to bear out this presumption!

'Feel free to take it with you, ${PLAYER}, and bring it to Bingo Boffin for his perusal. May it increase his enjoyment of the Harvest Festival! I know he will see it returned to me... eventually.''Feel free to take that scary tome with you, ${PLAYER}, and bring it to Bingo Boffin for his perusal. May it increase his enjoyment of the Harvest Festival! I know he will see it returned to me... eventually.''Ah, this book looks perfect, ${PLAYER}, thank you! I shall spend some time becoming acquainted with the tales within, and it will be time to share spooky stories before you know it!'It is almost time for Bingo and his cousins to meet on the Greenfields for the sharing of scary stories.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Bingo is near the Party Tree, in Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo and help him scare his many cousins with tales of terrorBingo is on the Greenfields.Talk to Bingo Boffin on the GreenfieldsDinodas Boffin ran off to the north-west of the Greenfields, and is now somewhere within the Bindbole Wood.Find Dinodas Boffin in the Bindbole WoodDinodas Boffin is in the Bindbole Wood.Talk to Dinodas Boffin in the Bindbole Wood'It is nearly time, ${PLAYER}! Let me know when you are ready, and we will go to the Greenfields together.''Are you ready? We will wait until it is properly dark, and be off!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Ha ha! That was great, ${PLAYER}! I think we finally managed to frighten the unfrightenable! The spirit of the scare is still alive and well in the Shire, despite rumour to the contrary!

'You had better go and check on him. We don't want him falling into any trouble on our account, after all. Dinodas ran off to the north-west. He's probably laughing about it by now, although he'd never admit it.''You had better go and check on Dinodas. We don't want him falling into any trouble on our account, after all. He ran off to the north-west. He's probably laughing about it by now, although he'd never admit it.''Oh dear, oh dear!''That was horrible, ${PLAYER}, and very mean! I expect a full apology. You should not go around trying to scare people for no reason!'

You explain that Bingo believed his cousin might enjoy the Harvest Festival more if he let himself be scared from time to time, and Dinodas flails his arms wildly.

'Bingo thinks nothing scares me? I have become a victim of my acting skills, ${PLAYER}, and gave too fine a performance! Do you remember when we explored the Haunted Burrow? I worked so hard to see through the spooky atmosphere and lay it bare as a sham, but deep down I was full of fear! Every bump, every sudden movement, every deep shadow seemed to promise some new form of terror!

'Foolish ${PLAYER}! I do not fear nothing... I fear everything!'Bingo is very interested to learn about the mysterious vision that appeared in the Bindbole Wood.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Bingo wants to see the spectral vision for himself, and asked you to lead him to it.Return to the location of the spectral vision in the Bindbole WoodBingo and Dinodas have come to the location where you saw the spectral apparition, but now there is no sign of it.Talk to Bingo in the Bindbole WoodTalk to Dinodas in the Bindbole WoodBingo thinks you might find some sign of the apparition if you climb on top of the old building on the hill where it appeared.Bingo is in the Bindbole Wood, where the spectral apparition appeared.Talk to Bingo in the Bindbole Wood'What fun! A mysterious apparition appeared in the wood? How delightful!

'I simply must see it for myself! Let's go, ${PLAYER}!''I simply must see it for myself! Let's go, ${PLAYER}! To the north-west!''It's gone? I'm disappointed, but that just makes me even more curious about it! What is going on here, ${PLAYER}?

'What does Dinodas think? Perhaps afterwards you can circle around to that building on top of the hill and see some sign of the apparition.''There's... there's nothing here? That's... that's even more scary! I do not like this, ${PLAYER}!

'I mean... it is all nonsense! There was nothing here to begin with, and it's very cruel of you to pretend otherwise!''Perhaps you can circle around to that building on top of the hill and see some sign of the apparition.''It is all nonsense! There was nothing here to begin with, and it's very cruel of you to pretend otherwise!''You didn't see anything? I am not surprised. Whatever was here, it clearly does not want to be seen again. I wonder why?

'My mind is full of questions, ${PLAYER}! This is truly an autumnal mystery! I need some time to think it over. I will meet you back at the Party Tree. This wood has become very spooky, and while Dinodas may not fear anything, I have to confess that this place has given me the chills. I love it! But perhaps we should head back to Hobbiton all the same.'Camellia Boffin is aghast that her brother Griffo has broken the rules of the apple-bobbing game at the Harvest Festival, and wants your help making it right.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>A spare apple-bobbing tub can be found near the base of the Hill, but be sure to avoid Bobbers-to-Be while you carry it back to Camellia Boffin.Pick up the spare apple-bobbing tub at the base of the Hill<rgb=#FF0000>Avoid Bobbers-to-Be or you will need to restart the quest</rgb>Camellia Boffin is by the apple-bobbing game near the Party Tree.Talk to Camellia Boffin near the Party TreeA spare apple-bobbing tub can be found within Appledores, the orchard on the Hill, but be sure to avoid Bobbers-to-Be while you carry it back to Camellia Boffin.Pick up the spare apple-bobbing tub in Appledores<rgb=#FF0000>Avoid Bobbers-to-Be or you will need to restart the quest</rgb>Camellia Boffin is by the apple-bobbing game near the Party Tree.Talk to Camellia Boffin near the Party Tree'${PLAYER}, we need your help! Have you participated in the apple-bobbing here at the festival yet? There are very important rules in place: no using your hands, and you only get one chance to play. But my brother Griffo did not listen to the rules, and now he has ruined it for everyone!

'Griffo thought these apples were just for the taking, and he reached right in to one of the tubs! He grabbed an apple and took a big bite, and then, as if that wasn't bad enough, he did it again! Honestly, if there were any grounds for being asked to leave the festival at once, I'd think that would qualify!

'But they're being very nice about the whole thing, and I've offered to bring some of their spare apple-bobbing tubs up here so the game can continue. Now that you're here, you can help me! There's a spare tub at the base of the Hill. Go pick it up and carry it back here, but beware of Bobbers-to-Be... much like Griffo, they won't be good at following the rules and they will keep you from carrying the tub. You'll have to start all over!''I've offered to bring some of their spare apple-bobbing tubs up here so the game can continue. Now that you're here, you can help me! There's a spare tub at the base of the Hill. Go pick it up and carry it back here, but beware of Bobbers-to-Be... much like Griffo, they won't be good at following the rules and they will keep you from carrying the tub. You'll have to start all over!''Thank you, ${PLAYER}! Can I ask you to get one more tub of apples? They should be freshly picked and waiting at the orchard not far from here. Remember, use caution to avoid Bobbers-to-Be while carrying it back here!''Can I ask you to get one more tub of apples? They should be freshly picked and waiting at the orchard not far from here. Remember, use caution to avoid Bobbers-to-Be while carrying it back here!''Thanks once again, ${PLAYER}! I have carefully explained the rules to Griffo once more, and he assures me he will follow them now, no matter how hungry he might get!

'Now that Griffo is no longer embarrassing me, I have to admit that this festival has been very fun so far. I like ensuring everything goes smoothly more than I like participating myself. My mother says I have always been like this, even when I was very little.

'Thanks again! I will tell you if we need any more help. Tonight we're going to share spooky stories with cousin Bingo, and you're welcome to come!'Dinodas does not believe in spooks, and thinks those who do are being silly.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Dinodas can be found within the four wings of the Haunted Burrow.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest requires access to the Haunted Burrow, which is available by completing the quest chain that begins with 'A Cellar Door Appears.'</rgb>Find Dinodas in the Cobwebbed Wing of the Haunted BurrowFind Dinodas in the Creeping Wing of the Haunted BurrowFind Dinodas in the Basement of the Haunted BurrowFind Dinodas in the Attic of the Haunted BurrowDinodas is outside the Haunted Burrow, near the Party Tree.Talk to Dinodas near the Party Tree'Well, ${PLAYER}, my mother said we were going to visit cousin Bingo, which is fine, but there has been a most sudden and unexpected reversal! Imagine my surprise when she told me we were going not to Michel Delving but to Hobbiton instead, there to partake in the Harvest Festival!'

Dinodas shakes his head.

'Now, I understand there are some things about this festival that are worthwhile. Good crops, and the promise of plenty? Worth celebrating! I have no patience for all the rest of it: spooks, haunts, and frights? Silly rubbish and nonsense! I told Celandine that same thing not two weeks ago!

'My mother says cousin Bingo is set on us sharing spooky stories, so there's no getting out of it. While we wait, I might as well see what this Haunted Burrow they've got is all about. I'll meet you inside.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest requires access to the Haunted Burrow, which is available by completing the quest chain that begins with 'A Cellar Door Appears.'</rgb>'My mother says cousin Bingo is set on us sharing spooky stories, so there's no getting out of it. While we wait, I might as well see what this Haunted Burrow they've got is all about. I'll meet you inside.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest requires access to the Haunted Burrow, which is available by completing the quest chain that begins with 'A Cellar Door Appears.'</rgb>'Will you look at this, ${PLAYER}? Who could be convinced by this poor excuse for a spider? It's big, yes, I'll grant you that, but I can see the wire from which it hangs! That does not look like spider-silk at all. It looks like a fishing line at best, and mere twine at worst!

'The webs are all right, I suppose, but this is not frightening at all. It's silly to think any hobbit would be scared of this wing.''Let me ask you a question, ${PLAYER}. Do you think these hobbits are trying to scare us? Because these glowing bottles could be filled with anything, which causes the imagination to run, but their illumination has another effect. It causes me to plainly see this crudely-painted mask and know it for what it is: just a mask! Now, if it was darker in here, perhaps I would think this was a real goblin... but not when I can see it so clearly!

'This is not frightening at all!''This basement is a little damp, but it's no more scary than others I have seen! Is this the best these hobbits can do? It is a little confusing to find the right path, but if you set your mind to it you shouldn't have any trouble finding the way through.

'It would need to be much darker and more maze-like to scare this hobbit, I'll tell you that!''Most burrows do not have attics, being delved in the ground and not unnaturally elevated like the houses of some folk, but this one is no more frightening than I would expect of the usual sort! The paint is peeling, and I hear rats scuttling about, but it will take more than those small irritations to raise the hair on my toes!''That Haunted Burrow does not seem very haunted to me! It is all for show, and the whole thing strikes me as being very silly, and not frightening at all.

'When my mother revealed that we were meeting cousin Bingo at Hobbiton for the Harvest Festival and not at his home in Michel Delving, I was very cross. But I have to admit that I very much enjoyed exploring the Haunted Burrow with you and revealing it as an unfrightening display. I thank you for that, ${PLAYER}!

'I look forward to disproving with you the scary stories cousin Bingo has for us this evening. I will see you there!'Esmeralda Boffin is worried that festival-goers might be too frightened by the spooky atmosphere of the Harvest Festival to engage in the dance contests and other activities.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Bashful festival-goers can be found around the Party Tree.Encourage bashful festival-goers to dance (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Esmeralda Boffin is near the dance contest stage by the Party Tree.Talk to Esmeralda Boffin near the Party TreeEsmeralda Boffin is near the dance contest stage by the Party Tree.Use the time to creatively express yourselfEsmeralda Boffin is near the dance contest stage by the Party Tree.Talk to Esmeralda Boffin near the Party Tree'I do not see very many people dancing, ${PLAYER}! My mother said that there would be dance contests held regularly during the festival, but so few people are participating! Is the spooky atmosphere to blame? Why is everyone afraid to dance?

'We must combat this fear of dancing with encouraging words! Find bashful festival-goers and tell them there is nothing to fear. That will surely work!''I do not see very many people dancing. My mother said that there would be dance contests held regularly during the festival, but so few people are participating! Is the spooky atmosphere to blame? Why is everyone afraid to dance?

'We must combat this fear of dancing with encouraging words! Find bashful festival-goers and tell them there is nothing to fear. That will surely work!''It didn't work, ${PLAYER}. People are still too bashful to get up and dance! What a disaster!

'Wait a moment. I have had an idea! Perhaps they just need to see someone else having a good time, dancing and cheering and who knows what else? It's up to you, ${PLAYER}! Take a moment to do whatever you want, any creative expression at all, and maybe that will help these bashful hobbits come to their senses!

'If they see you doing it and having a good time, perhaps they will be able to do it themselves!''It is your time, ${PLAYER}! Show these bashful festival-goers there is nothing to be embarrassed about!''That was quite a performance, ${PLAYER}.

'I hope these bashful hobbits have learned how fun it can be just to be yourself, and not worry about what anyone else is thinking! Thank you for being such a good sport! Will I see you at cousin Bingo's spooky stories tonight?'Dinodas Boffin has revealed his secret shame: that his insistent disbelief of anything scary was an attempt to hide his deep fear of it.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Dinodas heard something scary to the north-west and dropped his pack in the Bindbole Wood, and asked you to retrieve it for him.A spectral vision has appeared in the Bindbole Wood.Talk to the spectral visionDinodas Boffin is to the south-east, in the Bindbole Wood.Bingo Boffin is in the Bindbole Wood, near his cousin Dinodas.Talk to Bingo in the Bindbole Wood'Now you know my secret shame, and I trust you will keep it to yourself! It all began some time ago, when I offered to walk Celandine Brandybuck back to her home after a dinner party my mother hosted. Celandine was very happy to have the company, but as we began the walk I saw that the hour was much later than I had thought! The night was dark and the shadows were deep, and my blood ran cold at every creepy sound! I knew if I did not do something, Celandine would know me for a coward. Cry disaster! In order to mask my fear, I adopted a brazen attitude and mocked every fear I felt. It was all an act, an act at which I have become all too practiced!'

Dinodas frowns.

'Still, Bingo is my cousin, and that was a mean trick for any family member to perform, at any remove and no matter the distance! Speaking of distance, I was on my way back to the Greenfields when you caught up to me. I turned around because I heard something scary and dropped my pack, and ran back this way. Do you think you can retrace my steps to the north-west and find my pack? I don't think I have the courage to go back and look for it!''I was on my way back to the Greenfields when you caught up to me. I turned around because I heard something scary and dropped my pack, and ran back this way. Do you think you can retrace my steps to the north-west and find my pack? I don't think I have the courage to go back and look for it!''You saw what? You... you... you should go see what it wants!'The spectral vision stares past you, into the dark space among the trees. When it speaks, the sound reverberates in your ears and sets your teeth on edge.

'There can be no reparations for the hurt that has been done. In one year, all will fear the parting of the wood! None will escape the curse of the eerie acres! Despair and repair to your holes, for none but one will laugh when all is done!'

The vision stops speaking, and the space between the trees falls silent. All is cold.'Hullo there, ${PLAYER}! You found him!''Bingo has come here, and he apologized for the trick, and all is well now and we don't need to discuss that thing I told you, ${PLAYER}, all right? Good.'

You give Dinodas his pack. After a moment's thought, you tell him that you saw something unusual nearby.

'Oh? What... what was it? Actually... perhaps you should... tell cousin Bingo. I don't think I need to hear about it. I don't believe in... in that sort of rubbish! No indeed!''Perhaps you should... tell cousin Bingo... what you saw. I don't think I need to hear about it. I don't believe in... in that sort of rubbish! No indeed!''I apologized to Dinodas for our scary trick, but I think it did him some good. Now, what has whirled you up into such confusion?'

You quietly tell Bingo about the spectral vision you saw in the wood, and he looks at you skeptically.

'Is this a joke, or a trick of your own? You really saw this thing?'Bingo's cousin Prisca has brought her children to celebrate the Harvest Festival in Hobbiton, and Bingo wants to make sure they all have an enjoyable and fright-filled time.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Bingo's cousin Prisca and her children can be found enjoying the various activities of the Harvest Festival near the Party Tree.Help Prisca Boffin with 'Scary Dreams'Help Camellia Boffin with 'Boffin for Apples'Help Esmeralda Boffin with 'Fear Not To Dance!'Help Dinodas Boffin with 'Dinodas Does Not'Bingo is near the Party Tree, waiting to hear how his cousins are enjoying the Harvest Festival.Talk to Bingo near the Party TreeBingo is near the Party Tree, in Hobbiton.Help Bingo Boffin with 'A Little Spooky Research'Bingo is near the Party Tree in Hobbiton.Help Bingo Boffin with 'A Night for Ghost Stories'Dinodas Boffin is in the Bindbole Wood, in the Shire.Help Dinodas Boffin with 'Frightened Beyond Disbelief'Bingo Boffin is in the Bindbole Wood, in the Shire.Help Bingo Boffin with 'An Autumnal Mystery'Bingo said he would meet you back near the Party Tree, in Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo near the Party Tree'${PLAYER}, hello! I see that you have taken some time from your busy days of adventuring to celebrate the Harvest Festival! Good, good, how very right and proper! This has become one of my very favourite times of the year.

'My cousin Prisca has brought her children to visit, and we are all going to enjoy the sights and sounds of the Harvest Festival together. You simply must join us! I will not take "no" for an answer, and you know how stubborn Boffins can be when we set our minds to it. Legendarily so!

'You can find my various cousins enjoying the games around the Party Tree. I would appreciate it if you help them make the most of their time here, and later in the evening we will all meet on the Greenfields to tell spooky stories. I can't wait!''My cousin Prisca has brought her children to visit, and we are all going to enjoy the sights and sounds of the Harvest Festival together. You simply must join us! I will not take "no" for an answer, and you know how stubborn Boffins can be when we set our minds to it. Legendarily so!

'You can find my various cousins enjoying the games around the Party Tree. I would appreciate it if you help them make the most of their time here, and later in the evening we will all meet on the Greenfields to tell spooky stories. I can't wait!''How are my cousins finding the festival? Ah, excellent! It seems they are all enjoying it in their own particular ways. But what's this? Young Dinodas does not frighten easily, does he? We will have to do something about that, ${PLAYER}! This is a season for spooks and a festival for fright! He is missing out on a great deal of fun at this time of year if he does not allow himself to be scared!''I am very excited about this autumnal mystery, ${PLAYER}! I have read many scary stories, and they always delight my imagination, but I have never been involved in one myself! Tell me once again what the ghostly fellow had to say?'

You recount the words of the spectral vision.

'Hmm. There can be no reparations for the hurt that has been done. In one year, all will fear the parting of the wood! None will escape the curse of the eerie acres! Despair and repair to your holes, for none but one will laugh when all is done! Do I have that right?

'I have to admit I do not know what any of this means, ${PLAYER}, but it sounds to me like we have a year to figure it out! I will do some thinking. It sounds to me as if next year's Harvest Festival might be something, though! I can't wait... but I guess I must!'Prisca has performed the routine head-count of her children and come up one short, and she feels certain that her drowsy son Berilac is the missing hobbit.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends.</rgb>Berilac is probably sleeping among the bushes that line the road that climbs up to the Party Tree.Find Berilac Boffin somewhere on the HillYou found Berilac Boffin, sound asleep by the side of the road. A hobbit wearing a scary costume prepares to scare the sleeping Boffin.Talk to the costumed hobbitBerilac is sleeping by the road that leads up to the Party Tree.Wake up Berilac BoffinYou have awoken Berilac Boffin for the Harvest Festival.Talk to Berilac Boffin on the HillPrisca Boffin is by the Party Tree.Talk to Prisca Boffin near the Party Tree'It is always good to visit cousin Bingo, and it has been many years since last I made it out to Hobbiton for the Harvest Festival. I have rather more children now than I did then, but I have become quite practiced at travelling with so many! The key to a successful journey is to perform a hobbit-count before you leave your starting-point, and to perform a second count when you arrive at your destination.

'This is how I know that one of my children has gotten lost on the way! I am pretty sure Berilac is the child to blame, on account of it usually being my drowsiest boy who leaves my count short. We climbed the hill up to the festival; Berilac is probably sleeping among the bushes that line the road we took.

'If you don't mind, could you find Berilac and wake him up? We did not come all the way here from Newbury for him to sleep through the festival!''We climbed the hill up to the festival; Berilac is probably sleeping among the bushes that line the road we took. If you don't mind, could you find Berilac and wake him up? We did not come all the way here from Newbury for him to sleep through the festival!''Hullo there, friend! Do you know this sleepy fellow? I haven't seen him around Hobbiton or Bywater before, so I believe he has come from somewhere else in the Shire to enjoy the Harvest Festival. But will you look at this? He has fallen asleep, and is bound to miss the whole thing. That would be a shame, no mistake about it.

'Here is my plan. I have been scaring folk with my ferocious bear costume all day. You wake up this fellow and I will let out a loud roar and give him a fright. Hee! It will be a great welcome to the festival, and will also teach him not to waste so many hours sleeping, or at least not when a festival is underway!''Oh, I can't wait! This is going to be great!''Raaaaaaawr!'Berilac yawns sleepily and rubs his eyes.

'Oh, hello there, ${PLAYER}. I was just taking a quick nap before the festival gets underway. Cousin Bingo suggested that we all come visit and see the famous Hobbiton Harvest Festival, and sometime tonight we will all share spooky stories around a camp-fire. I had better get some rest if we're going to stay up so late!''That was not what I thought would happen! Hmmph. Do you think my costume is not... is not scary enough?

'No, that can't be it. This costume is terrifying!''Well, I can't say I am not surprised, ${PLAYER}. Thank you for finding Berilac's latest napping spot. I'll let him sleep until it is time for Bingo's frightening stories. Perhaps they will contain enough thrills and chills to keep my drowsy son awake where the promise of festival fun does not!'Bingo has invited you to Wistmead, Walaric Goldworthy's new field of Harvest Festival fun, but he is also concerned about the words of the apparition you saw in the Bindbole Wood, and he wants you to keep your eyes peeled for suspicious activity.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Angelica and Berilac Boffin are somewhere in the Harvest Festival field of Wistmead.Talk to Angelica Boffin by the ponies in WistmeadTalk to Berilac Boffin by the edge of the maze in WistmeadCamellia Boffin is by a number of dining tables in Wistmead, helping to serve snacks to hungry patrons.Talk to Camellia Boffin by the tables in WistmeadCamellia asked you to pick up a pie from across the field in Wistmead and carry it back to her. She claimed that hungry hobbits would not bother you if you let them see the pie-filling, which has been made to look like spiders and creepy-crawlies.Pick up a spider pie in Wistmead and carry it to Camellia BoffinGriffo Boffin is by the tables in Wistmead, looking unhappily at the spider-filled pies.Talk to Griffo Boffin in WistmeadMarigold Boffin is on the western side of Wistmead, by the fog-enshrouded path that leads among the trees.Find Marigold Boffin by the misty path on the western edge of WistmeadMarigold Boffin is on the western side of Wistmead, by the fog-enshrouded path that leads among the trees.Talk to Marigold Boffin by the misty path out of WistmeadMarigold asked you to walk the foggy path west of Wistmead and then to report where it leads.Travel the misty path through the woods west of WistmeadMarigold Boffin is on the western side of Wistmead, by the fog-enshrouded path that leads among the trees.Talk to Marigold Boffin by the misty path out of WistmeadBingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo in Wistmead'I must say, Walaric Goldworthy has done quite a nice job of creating a spooky atmosphere for the Harvest Festival here at Wistmead! This is where we should have told our scary stories in the first place, ${PLAYER}! Can you imagine what Dinodas might have done if we tried scaring him here instead? Even the least frightening story might be more convincing when told in this atmosphere!

'I encourage you to find my various cousins, the lot of them once-removed, and see what they think of the games here in Wistmead, but do not forget to remain alert for any suspicious activities! There may be an apparition on-hand seeking to conduct some nefarious purpose, and if this curse of his is real we want to know about it in time to prevent it from being carried out! Even so, try to have fun anyway, ${PLAYER}. It would be a shame to spoil all of Mr. Goldworthy's work with an excess of caution and worry. Wistmead really does seem like a fitting location for spooky fun, after all.

'I saw Angelica making a bee-line for the ponies to the north-west, and Berilac mentioned something about taking a snooze near the maze. Find them first and see what they think of Wistmead.''I encourage you to find my various cousins, the lot of them once-removed, and see what they think of the games here in Wistmead, but do not forget to remain alert for any suspicious activities! There may be an apparition on-hand seeking to conduct some nefarious purpose, and if this curse of his is real we want to know about it in time to prevent it from being carried out! Even so, try to have fun anyway, ${PLAYER}. It would be a shame to spoil all of Mr. Goldworthy's work with an excess of caution and worry. Wistmead really does seem like a fitting location for spooky fun, after all.

'I saw Angelica making a bee-line for the ponies to the north-west, and Berilac mentioned something about taking a snooze near the maze. Find them first and see what they think of Wistmead.''Hello there, ${PLAYER}! Will you look at these beautiful ponies? Aren't they simply delightful? I understand that Carl Proudfoot has loaned them to Walaric Goldworthy for the duration of the Harvest Festival, and they have been painted with these frightening but adorable designs! If I did not love horses and ponies as much as I do, I might be quite chilled to see them... but I do and I am not!

'I am having a lovely time here in Wistmead, and everything scary I have seen seems to be on purpose, ${PLAYER}. Cousin Bingo's curse must have nothing to do with this place after all, and he likely worries for nothing.

'I will stay here with the ponies. Everything seems fine to me!''Oh, it's you, ${PLAYER}. I came with my brothers and sisters to Wistmead, but I am quite ready to leave! I was promised Harvest Festival fun, but this does not seem terribly enjoyable to me. All I want to do is to curl up here outside of this maze and take a restful nap, but I have not been able to get comfortable. There is just something about this place that keeps me awake, and that is MOST unusual! Why, I would say it is a first!

'I will keep trying. Are my siblings having a better time than I am? I hope so, but above all I just wish I could take a nap... this is usually not a problem!''I am having a lovely time here in Wistmead, and everything scary I have seen seems to be on purpose, ${PLAYER}. Cousin Bingo's curse must have nothing to do with this place after all, and he likely worries for nothing.

'I will stay here with the ponies. Everything seems fine to me!''All I want to do is to curl up here outside of this maze and take a restful nap, but I have not been able to get comfortable. There is just something about this place that keeps me awake, and that is MOST unusual! Why, I would say it is a first!

'I will keep trying. Are my siblings having a better time than I am? I hope so, but above all I just wish I could take a nap... this is usually not a problem!''Welcome to Wistmead, ${PLAYER}! I spoke with Walaric Goldworthy earlier and he told me that it took quite a long time to clear this field and prepare it for the new Harvest Festival activities. I am very glad he took the time, because the spooky atmosphere here cannot be matched by anywhere in the Shire, and everyone is having a wonderful time!

'Naturally I asked if there was anything that still needed to be done, and it turns out there are still some baked goods waiting to be set out on these tables. Could you go pick up one of Holly's pies from the tents on the other side of the field?'

Camellia lowers her voice conspiratorially.

'I must warn you about something, ${PLAYER}. The pie-filling has been fashioned with blackberries and currants, and should be delicious, but Holly Hornblower has made it look as if the pies are filled with creepy-crawlies and spiders! A number of hungry hobbits may try to get in your way, but give them a good look at the pie and they will surely leave you alone!''Could you go pick up one of Holly's pies from the tents on the other side of the field?'

Camellia lowers her voice conspiratorially.

'I must warn you about something, ${PLAYER}. The pie-filling has been fashioned with blackberries and currants, and should be delicious, but Holly Hornblower has made it look as if the pies are filled with creepy-crawlies and spiders! A number of hungry hobbits may try to get in your way, but give them a good look at the pie and they will surely leave you alone!''I thought Wistmead was going to be full of fun activities and delicious food, ${PLAYER}, but it seems I was mistaken! I wanted nothing more than to dig into one of these pies, because the pie-crust is golden-brown the way I like it, and the smell makes my mouth water, but on closer inspection you can plainly see that the pies are filled with spiders! It is an affront to good taste! I do not understand why folk still buy pies from Holly Hornblower, honestly!

'I am not enjoying my time in Wistmead, ${PLAYER}. I am just so hungry, and I do not like that feeling! If you want to find someone who is having a good time, perhaps you should find Marigold. I expect she is enjoying herself, because it is dark and a little scary. I saw her looking at the misty woods on the other side of the field, and she might still be there.

'I will try and rummage up some edible food. Wish me luck, because I am not eating one of those spider pies, no matter how delicious it might smell! Can't fool me!''I am not enjoying my time in Wistmead, ${PLAYER}. I am just so hungry, and I do not like that feeling! If you want to find someone who is having a good time, perhaps you should find Marigold. I expect she is enjoying herself, because it is dark and a little scary. I saw her looking at the misty woods on the other side of the field, and she might still be there.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, how are you finding Wistmead? I am not especially interested in the Harvest Festival activities that Walaric Goldworthy has set up here, but I am very curious to know where this path leads! The fog hangs thickly all about the trail, and the darkness seems almost to pool among the trees... it feels like adventure to me!

'Cousin Bingo made me promise to stay in Wistmead or he would tell my mother and she would be very cross, so for now I should not follow this misty path. But you made no such promise, did you? You should walk along the path and see where it leads, and then come back and tell me what you saw!

'That is a capital idea, is it not? I'll be waiting for your report, my friend!''Cousin Bingo made me promise to stay in Wistmead or he would tell my mother and she would be very cross, so for now I should not follow this misty path. But you made no such promise, did you? You should walk along the path and see where it leads, and then come back and tell me what you saw!''${PLAYER}! How did you get back here without my seeing you? You got turned around in the woods? This must not be ordinary fog after all, but a magical and mysterious mist that lends itself to confusing even the most perceptive of adventurers!

'How delightful! I will do some thinking and see if I can come up with some means of countering this curious canopy of cloud! For now you should return to Bingo and tell him that I am having a great time. Spooky mysteries abound!''I will do some thinking and see if I can come up with some means of countering this curious canopy of cloud! For now you should return to Bingo and tell him that I am having a great time. Spooky mysteries abound!''The longer we stay here the more worried I become, ${PLAYER}. How do my cousins feel about Wistmead?'

You tell Bingo that Angelica, Camellia, and Marigold seem to be enjoying themselves, for various reasons, but that Berilac and Griffo do not seem to be having as fine a time.

'At least some of my cousins are enjoying the Harvest Festival here, even if that cannot be said for all of them! I suppose this cannot be the curse at work, unless it is a curse that has chosen to mildly inconvenience a few hobbits, which seems unlike any curse of which I've heard! Curse or no, I still wish more of my cousins were having a good time!'Bingo has a plan for you to get to the bottom of the haunting at Wistmead, but it will involve a frightening journey into the mist-shrouded forest that surrounds the old Cleary farm.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Dinodas is by the fire on the western side of Wistmead.Talk to Dinodas in WistmeadDinodas has reluctantly agreed to go with you along the misty path to the old Cleary farm. He suggested that you pick up the torch at his location and lead the way west along the mist-shrouded path into the forest.'If there is a spectre haunting the Cleary farmhouse, you need to be prepared to weather some frightening apparitions, ${PLAYER}. Who better to take with you than my cousin Dinodas? He is not frightened of anything, and will be best-suited to adventure with you through the misty forest to the Eerie Acres, no matter what the haunt brings! He would disbelieve a ghost right to its face, and I think his clear head might be the secret to dispelling this curse!

'I will not hear another word about it! Find Dinodas by the fire here in Wistmead, and I look forward to your successful return!''No, I will not hear another word about it! Find Dinodas by the fire here in Wistmead, and I look forward to your successful return!''What is going on, ${PLAYER}? Why do you have that look on your face?'

You explain Bingo's idea that a ghost is responsible for the haunting of Wistmead, and that he wants you and Dinodas to travel through the woods to the Cleary farm to dispel the curse. Dinodas laughs, but then his smile drains away.

'You're serious? Oh no, ${PLAYER}! Is there no way to get out of this? I shared my secret to you in confidence: I do not fear nothing... I fear everything! But if I tell cousin Bingo the truth, he will know my secret and I will be revealed as a liar! But if I do not tell him, I will have to brave the spooky forest and risk the ghostly curse! This is terrible!

'There is nothing for it, I suppose. The path is foggy, so you should pick up this torch and use it to light the way. I will be right behind you... but I'm not happy about it! Not at all!''For data.''The path is foggy, so you should pick up this torch and use it to light the way. I will be right behind you... but I'm not happy about it! Not at all!'A year after your encounter with the spectral apparition in the Bindbole Wood, Bingo and his cousins are equal parts nervous and excited to learn what the appearance of the mysterious figure portends.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>A pony to Wistmead can be found to the east of the Party Tree.Travel to Wistmead and find BingoBingo has asked to meet you at Wistmead, Walaric Goldworthy's new field of Harvest Festival fun.Help Bingo Boffin in Wistmead with 'Fun and Games?'Bingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Help Bingo Boffin in Wistmead with 'The Parting Fog'Bingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Help Bingo Boffin in Wistmead with 'The Haunting of Wistmead'Bingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Help Bingo Boffin in Wistmead with 'None But One'Bingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo in Wistmead<rgb=#00FF00>It is recommended, though not required, that you complete the 'Frightful Tales to Curl the Hair On Your Toes' quest chain before beginning 'The Curse of Eerie Acres.'</rgb>

'Where has the time gone, ${PLAYER}? It feels as if we were just discussing the strange apparition you saw in the Bindbole Wood, but the year of which it spoke has already come and gone! I fully intended to spend the time researching the phantom's words, but each day was too full of chores and errands and visits, and don't you know it: here we are again, and no closer to solving the mystery!

'What did the apparition say? Ah, here it is, I wrote it down: "There can be no reparations for the hurt that has been done. In one year, all will fear the parting of the wood! None will escape the curse of the eerie acres! Despair and repair to your holes, for none but one will laugh when all is done!"

'Brrr, it still gives me the chills. Well, we will not forget those words, and I recommend that you keep your eyes open as we participate in the Harvest Festival this year. If you see anything at all that seems to relate to this curse, come see me at once! Walaric Goldworthy has opened a new field of festival games for the Harvest Festival this year, at a place called Wistmead. I have seen nothing to indicate that he knows anything about that apparition or this worrisome curse, so we should go to Wistmead and have some fun, but I will keep on the look-out for anything suspicious and you should as well!

'There's a pony to Wistmead just to the east and up the slope. I'll meet you there, and hopefully Wistmead will have plenty of spooky fun and very little in the way of scary curses!''Walaric Goldworthy has opened a new field of festival games for the Harvest Festival this year, at a place called Wistmead. I have seen nothing to indicate that he knows anything about that apparition or this worrisome curse, so we should go to Wistmead and have some fun, but I will keep on the look-out for anything suspicious and you should as well!

'There's a pony to Wistmead just to the east and up the slope. I'll meet you there, and hopefully Wistmead will have plenty of spooky fun and very little in the way of scary curses!''Well, this has been a Harvest Festival to remember, ${PLAYER}, and no mistake! I am glad we have managed to dispel the curse, and sent the Skulking Ire packing! I wish Halson Cleary could know what we did for him, but of course that is impossible.

'Perhaps it is enough that we remember this one thing, easy enough as it is to forget: we should strive to be kind to our neighbours, in every season. Hey presto! It just occurred to me that some of the spookiest stories I can think of come from our failure to do that, this one included!

'The Curse of the Eerie Acres has been dispelled, and now we can safely enjoy the Harvest Festival without fear... or perhaps with just the right amount of fear, if you know what I mean! Hobbit-sized spooks and scares are more to my liking, and to that of my cousins, I think! Thank you for all the help, ${PLAYER}! Now I believe I will turn my attention from investigating ancient curses to another matter: looking into these spider pies everyone is talking about!'Bingo has decided that the Curse of Eerie Acres is real, and he wants your help to get to the bottom of it.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Mayor Will Whitfoot is outside the Town Hole in Michel Delving, and may know something about Cleary's Acres.Talk to Will Whitfoot in Michel DelvingBrombard Foxtail is the keeper of the Mathom-house, which stands on a rise overlooking the Town Hole in Michel Delving.Talk to Brombard Foxtail in Michel DelvingPostmaster Proudfoot is just outside the Quick Post, by the Town Hole in Michel Delving.Talk to Postmaster Proudfoot in Michel DelvingPostmaster Proudfoot is outside the Quick Post, near the Town Hole in Michel Delving.Talk to Postmaster Proudfoot and he will help you research the mystery of Cleary's AcresPostmaster Proudfoot is outside the Quick Post, near the Town Hole in Michel Delving.Talk to Postmaster Proudfoot in Michel DelvingClem Underhill is near the Town Hole in Michel Delving.Talk to Clem Underhill, the Escrow Broker in Michel DelvingBingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path from Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo in Wistmead'We have to get to the bottom of this business, ${PLAYER}, and find a way to combat this curse before it becomes too late! What we need is a clue, or better yet, more than one!'

Bingo snaps his fingers.

'Maybe we have one! You said that the writing on the sign you found had been crossed out with red paint, and someone had written "Eerie Acres?" What words did this mysterious vandal seek to replace? "Cleary's Acres," you said? Well, that is one place to start. We need to find out anything we can about a hobbit named Cleary, and perhaps we will learn what happened to his homestead for it to be known as Eerie Acres!

'It may be a long shot, but there might be some record of Cleary's Acres at the Town Hole in Michel Delving. Consult with Mayor Whitfoot and you might learn something useful! I will stay here and keep an eye out for anything suspicious! Good luck to us both, ${PLAYER}!''It may be a long shot, but there might be some record of Cleary's Acres at the Town Hole in Michel Delving. Consult with Mayor Whitfoot and you might learn something useful! I will stay here and keep an eye out for anything suspicious! Good luck to us both, ${PLAYER}!''Hullo, hullo! How are you enjoying the Harvest Festival this year? Have you found the time to head over to Wistmead for the new activities there? I understand Walaric Goldworthy has really outdone himself, and there are many fun games to play and delicious treats to enjoy. I was about to make my way there to sample some of the offerings for myself! But I will of course take the time to help you with whatever you need, so long as you make it quick, ${PLAYER}.

'A hobbit named Cleary? Is that used as a first name or as a surname? Well, I can't say it makes a difference, because neither case rings a bell; I have no memory of either. When did you say this Cleary lived in the Shire? You didn't? If he is an old resident, it could be some belongings of his have ended up in the Mathom-house. You could ask Brombard if he's seen anything belonging to a Cleary.

'Hmmm. I have given more than enough thought to this matter, and it seems well-settled to me. Sorry I could not be of more help, but I need to get going if I am to sample some of the food-platters at Wistmead. Good day to you!''When did you say this Cleary lived in the Shire? You didn't? If he is an old resident, it could be some belongings of his have ended up in the Mathom-house. You could ask Brombard if he's seen anything belonging to a Cleary.

'Hmmm. I have given more than enough thought to this matter, and it seems well-settled to me. Sorry I could not be of more help, but I need to get going if I am to sample some of the food-platters at Wistmead. Good day to you!''Cleary, eh? I don't think I know of any hobbits by that name in the Shire, although it does sound familiar for some reason. It strikes me as being a name from many years ago, perhaps when I became keeper of the Mathom-house as a young hobbit. But that was long ago, and if I then saw mathoms labeled with the name Cleary they certainly have been long-since distributed or buried beneath other more recent items.

'Cleary, Cleary, Cleary... No, I can't say I know of anyone with that name. Perhaps you could check with the Postmaster? His occupation requires that he have a thorough knowledge of hobbit names and addresses, and if anyone knows of a Cleary in the Shire, he is just the hobbit who will do so!

'Postmaster Proudfoot can usually be found outside the Quick Post, right near the Town Hole.''Perhaps you could check with the Postmaster? His occupation requires that he have a thorough knowledge of hobbit names and addresses, and if anyone knows of a Cleary in the Shire, he is just the hobbit who will do so!

'Postmaster Proudfoot can usually be found outside the Quick Post, right near the Town Hole.''You are looking for a Cleary? Dare I hope that you seek word of one Halson Cleary? This is an unexpected joy, ${PLAYER}! At last! I thought this day would never come, and I would go to my grave with this task unfulfilled, one more disappointer in a long line of shamed postmasters! But it is not to be! The mail shall at last be delivered!

'When I first came to work at delivering the mail, when I was just a young hobbit, old Ned Harfoot the Postmaster showed me the ropes. It took me a few weeks to learn the ins and the outs of the Quick Post, and I never forgot his lessons. One day he took me down to the cellar and showed me a box of old envelopes. "These letters ain't ever been delivered, Hugo," he said to me, "but my old boss told me we weren't to throw them away. Could be one day we'll find out how to deliver them, and when that day comes the Quick Post will truly have earned a fine reputation, even if it puts the lie to its name!" Well, Ned passed on not long after that, and I took a vow to keep those undelivered letters safe, until my own time was up or the intended recipient came to claim them.

'It seems to me you might be the closest person we're likely to get to Halson Cleary, ${PLAYER}. If you are willing to follow me, I will show you these stacks of undelivered letters and you can decide what to do with them! Let me know!''Follow me, ${PLAYER}! I can still hardly believe that someone has come in search of those letters, after all this time!''It is highly unusual for letters such as those to not have been delivered, ${PLAYER}. We run a respectable post here, and I would have said we always have done... but those undelivered letters seem to cast some doubt on it, to my shame and that of my predecessors. Why would those letters not be delivered?

'At any rate, we do not keep records of home ownership at the Quick Post, but there is a small chance that someone might know the state of the Cleary farmhouse, even if knowledge of its one-time resident has been lost during the ensuing years. That was so long ago. I speak of Clem Underhill, the Escrow Broker here in Michel Delving! He may know if the deed to the Cleary farmhouse ever changed hands, and if the land was sold he might tell you the name of the current owner, if there is one.''We do not keep records of home ownership at the Quick Post, but there is a small chance that someone might know the state of the Cleary farmhouse, even if knowledge of its one-time resident has been lost during the ensuing years. That was so long ago. I speak of Clem Underhill, the Escrow Broker here in Michel Delving! He may know if the deed to the Cleary farmhouse ever changed hands, and if the land was sold he might tell you the name of the current owner, if there is one.''The Cleary farmhouse, you say? Oh, that goes back quite a long way, almost three hundred years if I remember a-right! The records from those days are rather incomplete, but there is a deed for a parcel of land in that name. It is a wooded lot, yes? I have had no cause to go out there, but the description makes it sound quite deep into the forest. Near Walaric Goldworthy's new cleared field of Wistmead, isn't it? That is an interesting coincidence, since the field has only recently been put to use after being left alone for so long.

'There have been no transactions relating to that parcel, so I expect if it was owned by a Cleary once then it must be owned by a Cleary still. A descendant, surely, although that is a long time for anyone to stay in one place without interacting with his neighbours. That is no way to live, not for any respectable hobbit! It must do you some harm, surely, to see only the faces of your family and no others. And if you live by yourself, with no kin around you, why, imagine it! And who would take care of you in your twilit days, until finally your last day arrives and you meet it alone...?'

Clem shivers suddenly.

'Mayhap it is just the time of year, but thinking about this sends a chill down my spine and raises the hairs on my feet. It sounds like a spooky Harvest Festival story to me, and not something that could happen to real folk in the Shire! I cannot say as I like it, ${PLAYER}. I hope I was a help to you, and now I would like to think no more of these troubling thoughts!''There have been no transactions relating to that parcel, so I expect if it was owned by a Cleary once then it must be owned by a Cleary still. A descendant, surely!''At last you have come back, ${PLAYER}! Fosco yelled at me again, and this time he had the support of several of his brothers. This is so unlike them! What did you learn in Michel Delving? Did you find out anything about Cleary's Acres or about the curse?'

You explain to Bingo what you learned about Halson Cleary and the parcel of land he once owned, not far from Wistmead, and the hobbit nods.

'It all makes sense to me now! The spectral vision you saw in the Bindbole Wood must be related in some way to Halson Cleary, and somehow he managed to put a curse on the Shire! "None but one will laugh when all is done?" That must refer to the ghost of Halson Cleary, scorned and forgotten by his neighbours! In fact, that may very well be why the Boffin boys are so uncharacteristically angry! The ghostly haunt has affected them without their knowing, and the close proximity of Wistmead to the Cleary farm has enflamed their tempers!

'Are you up for another adventure, ${PLAYER}? I have the perfect hobbit in mind to go with you!'Bingo is concerned about the apparition you saw in the Bindbole Wood and the curse of which it spoke, and he is afraid that it might work some evil upon the hobbits celebrating the Harvest Festival at the new field of Wistmead.

<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the event it is associated with ends. </rgb>Walaric Goldworthy is in the field of Wistmead.Talk to Walaric Goldworthy in WistmeadWalaric Goldworthy told you that you could see the junk-pile to the south-west, across the main path that runs through Wistmead.Find the location where Walaric Goldworthy moved some of the original fences and other debris found in WistmeadA face-down wooden sign lying among the debris of the junk-pile has caught your attention.Examine the junked wooden signA wooden sign lying among the debris of the junk-pile has caught your attention.Read the newly-revealed writing on the discarded wooden signBingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo in WistmeadWalaric Goldworthy is in the field of Wistmead.Talk to Walaric Goldworthy in WistmeadBingo is in Wistmead, near the eastern path that leads back to Hobbiton.Talk to Bingo in Wistmead'Perhaps I am making a great mountain from a shrew's burrow, ${PLAYER}, but that apparition you saw in the Bindbole Wood has me all a-flutter with worry. What if the curse of which it spoke is just around the corner, and it waits to level its full effect upon the hobbits celebrating the Harvest Festival here at Wistmead?

'Do you think you might speak with Walaric Goldworthy in front of the maze, and ask him if he encountered any difficulties while setting up the festivities here? I hope I am just being silly. I will not be able to enjoy the games here at Wistmead until I set my mind at ease, and right now it is all a-bustle with spooks and scary shenanigans... but not in a good way!''Do you think you might speak with Walaric Goldworthy in front of the maze, and ask him if he encountered any difficulties while setting up the festivities here? I hope I am just being silly. I will not be able to enjoy the games here at Wistmead until I set my mind at ease, and right now it is all a-bustle with spooks and scary shenanigans... but not in a good way!''Yes, what do you want? If you are here to complain, I do not want to hear about it. I have listened to more than my share of grumbles today, and my temper is at its lowest ebb. Move along, you!'

You try to set Walaric at ease by telling him that several of the hobbits with whom you have spoken seem to be thoroughly enjoying the festival at Wistmead, and he softens somewhat. When he seems less annoyed, you ask him about the process by which Wistmead was made ready for the activities and games of the Harvest Festival.

'Ah, yes, well, it was quite a lot of work, so I am pleased there are some who have enjoyed the games here. I put together a team from Hobbiton and from Bywater, and for the better part of a fortnight we worked to clear the field of stones and stumps. We had to move a few fences; hobbits might once have lived among the trees here, because we also moved one or two low walls that could have belonged to hobbit-homes, although the houses themselves are long gone. If you want to see how much work we put in, you can take a look at the junk-pile where we moved everything. It is to the south-west, across the main path that runs through Wistmead.

'And enjoy yourself! I would not have put so much work into this if I did not love this spooky season so much!''I put together a team from Hobbiton and from Bywater, and for the better part of a fortnight we worked to clear the field of stones and stumps. We had to move a few fences; hobbits might once have lived among the trees here, because we also moved one or two low walls that could have belonged to hobbit-homes, although the houses themselves are long gone. If you want to see how much work we put in, you can take a look at the junk-pile where we moved everything. It is to the south-west, across the main path that runs through Wistmead.''If you want to see the junk-pile where we moved everything, you can find it to the south-west, across the main path that runs through Wistmead.''If you want to see the junk-pile where we moved everything, you can find it to the south-west, across the main path that runs through Wistmead.''You look as if you've seen a ghost, ${PLAYER}! Another one, I mean. You haven't, have you?'

You tell Bingo that Walaric Goldworthy cleared away several fences, stones, and wooden structures when he prepared the field of Wistmead for the Harvest Festival, removing the debris to a junk-pile. You go on to describe the wooden sign you found among the trash.

'What's that? You say there was a sign for "Cleary's Acres," but someone had crossed that out and painted the words "Eerie Acres" on top? But that means...

'It's just as I feared, ${PLAYER}! This field may be called Wistmead now, but at some point in its history it must have been called Eerie Acres! We have wandered right into the waiting jaws of the curse! You have to tell Walaric that we may be in danger! He has to cancel the celebrations here, and at once!''This field may be called Wistmead now, but at some point in its history it must have been called Eerie Acres! We have wandered right into the waiting jaws of the curse! You have to tell Walaric that we may be in danger! He has to cancel the celebrations here, and at once!''Cancel the Harvest Festival? Absolutely not! Why should I do that?'

You tell Walaric Goldworthy that a malevolent force has declared that no one in the Shire will escape the curse of the Eerie Acres, and you have found proof that Wistmead was once known by that name. He snorts with disbelief.

'What nonsense! I know the sign of which you speak; it stood by the western path out of the clearing, and has not been thought of by anyone for a very long time. No one objected when we pulled it from the ground and tossed it aside, did they? This whole place has been uninhabited for dozens of years at least, ${PLAYER}, and no hobbits have called this place Eerie Acres since then, take my word on it.

'I am sorry you are bothered by all this talk of curses, but I am sure it is nothing. Someone is playing a holiday prank on you, I wager. I will not shut down Wistmead and I will not cancel the Harvest Festival. If that Bingo Boffin put you up to this, you can just go and tell him his plan to ruin my festival has failed! And that is all I will say about it!''I know the sign of which you speak; it stood by the western path out of the clearing, and has not been thought of by anyone for a very long time. No one objected when we pulled it from the ground and tossed it aside, did they? This whole place has been uninhabited for dozens of years at least, ${PLAYER}, and no hobbits have called this place Eerie Acres since then, take my word on it.

'I am sorry you are bothered by all this talk of curses, but I am sure it is nothing. Someone is playing a holiday prank on you, I wager. I will not shut down Wistmead and I will not cancel the Harvest Festival. If that Bingo Boffin put you up to this, you can just go and tell him his plan to ruin my festival has failed! And that is all I will say about it!''He refused to cancel the festival, didn't he? Of course he did... that is just the sort of day I am having! Why, I saw Fosco just now, and I told him we should probably get ready to leave Wistmead, and do you know what he did? He yelled at me and stamped his feet! He has always been one of my favourite cousins, but he raised his voice at me and put me right out of sorts! Something is not right. This is quite unlike Fosco, and I think the curse may be to blame!

'What is going on here, ${PLAYER}? Mark my words: there is more to this than we know. The Curse of Eerie Acres is real, and it has affected some of the hobbits here already. There can be no mistaking it!'By completing this quest, you will receive a Shagâni Ghín barter currency allowance.'Get barter currency for the Ikorbân Valley!''Ride back here like a bunny if you need more!'Talk to Ransroth.Speak to Ransroth'Talk to me to increase your reputation with Phetekâri of Umbar.''20,000 Reputation with Phetekâri of Umbar, coming right up!'Talk to Ransroth.Speak to Ransroth'Talk to me to increase your reputation with the Adúrhid.''15,000 Reputation with the Adúrhid, coming right up!'Talk to Ransroth.Speak to Ransroth'Talk to me to increase your reputation with the Ikorbâni.''15,000 Reputation with the Ikorbâni, coming right up!'By completing this quest, you will receive an Ill Omens barter currency allowance.'Get barter currency for Ill Omens!''Ride back here like a bunny if you need more!'Obsessed with partridge, you think of it day and night.  Your only solace is to eat it.  Yummy, yummy partridge.Partridge PieBacon-wrapped Roast PartridgePartridge Hot PotBaked Beans with PartridgePartridge and a Pear TreatAs Midsummer's Day approaches, the peoples of Middle-earth come together to celebrate the great festival of Midsummer. Revel in the celebration of dance, merriment, and love with your family and fellows!Midsummer celebrations can be found in Minas Tirith (Midsummer) as well as Thorin's Hall, Duillond, Bree-town, at the Party Tree in the Shire, and Furtherholm in the Shield Isles.

