<rgb=#FF0000> NOTE: This quest will be removed from your quest log when the current festival ends. </rgb>Mistletoe can be found on the young fir-trees along the northern road to Thrasi's Lodge.

Saervereth asked you to gather some mistletoe for her so she may remember what it is Yule is all about.Gather mistletoe (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Saervereth is at Duillond, in the Festival area.

Saervereth will be eager to see the mistletoe and muse on the true meaning of Yule.'I have watched the turning of more Yule-seasons than a child such as you could fathom, friend. Every year, we mark its passing by gathering the mistletoe from the young fir-trees. Some of it is used in infusions, some for decoration, but mostly we use it to remind us of the fleeting passage of time.

'I have not yet gone gathering this year, for I fear there may be too much for me to do here. If I cannot see the mistletoe, I will mourn much, when I should be celebrating. Winter is the beginning of the cycle of spring's rebirth, not a death, as many mortals see it. Yule and mistletoe serve to remind us of this.

'If you could be so kind as to follow the road north towards Thrasi's Lodge, you will find young fir-trees just before the bend, adorned with freshly-grown mistletoe. Bring some back to me so I may rekindle my hope of the season's rebirth.''Have you found any mistletoe yet? It can be collected to the north of here.''You have my greatest thanks for bringing me this mistletoe, ${PLAYER}. One must never forget that life means death, yet death brings new life.'Seven swords have been stolen from Krúslë Lannan  and must be returned to their proper resting place.Recover the Sword of Abb from OwenRecover the Sword of Kolmak from RuraikhRecover the sword of Ánraig from TobaidRecover the sword of Guinokh from BrekhinRecover the sword of Innis from DugaldRecover the sword of Kevoka from FranganRecover the sword of Mánas from HuisdánLêkhar has brought Hamâti horses to Furtherholm and set up a challenging race course around the island for skilled riders to test their mettle on horseback.

