You should return to Bryony Elmwood with word of your successes at Troves and Trickery.Talk to Bryony Elmwood at the Bree Festival Grounds'Harvestmath is such a wonderful time of year. One of my favourite festival traditions is the game of Troves and Trickery.

'To play, you go about finding people to trick throughout Bree-town. They will tell you something, and you must do your best to act out something related to it.

'Are you interested in playing?''People who are interested in playing will find a way to catch your eye, ${PLAYER}. All you need to do is go looking for them in Bree-town.''Don't I have news for you, traveller! My neighbour, Provincia, was quarreling with her husband so loudly that it quite caught my attention! And you'll never guess what about. Can you guess? I shall tell you what: apparently he would not let her buy silks for a new dress. Whyever would she need a new dress? I cannot imagine why; she has ten already, and they are in decent shape as they are. Do you want to know what I think? I'll warrant she just wants to compete with Bessie Hawthorne's dresses, for I hear Bessie has eleven. I, for one, am quite content with my eight dresses. What do you say to that?''I spent all my week's pay in the Auction Hall, I did. You should lend me some money. A fine traveller like yourself must have some money, and I shall turn it into a handsome profit for you. What do you say to that: yes or no?''My poor head, how it throbs! Pray, do not disturb me, unless you brought me more ale. I know I ought to be ashamed of myself for drinking so much, but what's a man to do? Bill Ferny kept goading me on. Look where it got me....

'My mother will give me an earful when I wander home stinking of beer. Alas, alas!''I dance all day! If I ever stop, my legs will turn to trees, so I dance, dance, dance all day, every day! What can a man do with two trees for legs? They tell me I'm mad, but they will not think so when their legs turn to trees.''That sounds like an interesting experience you had, ${PLAYER}. I'm not sure that it's quite what I had in mind to keep with the spirit of Harvestmath, but it sounds like you did some good in Bree-town nonetheless.'Troves and Trickery is a festival tradition among many cultures, where participants try to please a number of celebrants with their tricks in order to receive a prize.

