Are you sure you want to continue?Merion wonders if the day-to-day life of a Mossward town guard is about to change... but until then, the townspeople have complained about squeaky doors in the village, about which something needs to be done.Skardí Bendblade is inside the blacksmith shop in the centre of town.Talk to Skardí Bendblade in his blacksmith shopThe hinges of Skardí's interior door has developed a bit of a squeak.Apply some grease to the hinges of Skardí's interior door and end the squeakingThe door to the healer's shop in the town square is in need of oiling.Apply some grease to the hinges of the healer's shop door and end the squeakingThe squeaky hinges of the door to Cadogan's house are located west of the town square in Mossward.Merion of the town guard stands at the east gate of Mossward, waiting to hear of your success against the squeaky doors of the village.'They do say change comes slowly, and for now I have other matters to which I must attend. Or perhaps you could help me once again?'

Merion smiles broadly.

'That is the spirit! Now, there won't be any Uruks to face this time, but until last night this was the most common complaint among the townspeople. Eleri says we should handle simple things first? Well, what could be more simple than doing something about the squeaky hinges on doors in Mossward?

'Cadogan insists he ran outside to attack the raiding Uruks but they were alerted to his approach by the loud squeak of the door to his home. I'm not sure I believe that story, for it is much more likely that he was running INTO his house when the hinges gave him away... but who can say for certain?

'All the same, Skardí Bendblade should have a bottle of grease you can use to oil the hinges of the door into the healer's shop and the door of Cadogan's house, which are the two squeakiest offenders.''Skardí Bendblade, town blacksmith, should have a bottle of grease you can use to oil the hinges of the door into the healer's shop and the door of Cadogan's house, which are the two squeakiest offenders.''You need a bottle of grease to use on the hinges of squeaky doors throughout Mossward? Whenever anyone needs something, they come first to me instead of checking the store-house!'

You apologize to Skardí for assuming he had such an item.

'I do have a bottle of just what you need. That's not the point! Ah, fine, take it. While you're at it, how about you test it out on the hinges of my door there? I have noticed a bit of a squeak in the hinges lately, with everyone coming in and out at all hours in search of fine goods!''While you're at it, how about you test it out on the hinges of my door there? I have noticed a bit of a squeak in the hinges lately, with everyone coming in and out at all hours in search of fine goods!''The door to the healer's shop is in the town square, and its hinges have been the source of a great deal of squeaking.''You just need to put an end to the squeaking, and then perhaps Cadogan will cease his complaining to the town guard. Ah, if only that were true.''How fared your efforts against the squeaky doors of Mossward?'

You tell Merion about what transpired, and he covers his eyes with a weary hand.

'Rats in the village? I don't like the sound of that, ${PLAYER}. Having rats loose in the town could be a danger to the food-stores. I'll see what I can do. Between the Uruks and the rats, it's a wonder to me that I ever thought the life of a guard might be an uneventful one!

'Thanks all the same, friend. We here in Mossward will manage. We always do.'Ned Pruner is Archet's jailor. He is a good fellow charged with discovering the secrets Calder Cob traded to the Blackwold brigands.Ned Pruner is at the jail, north and east of the town-gate.

Constable Thistlewool suggested that you may be able to assist Ned Pruner to discover what Calder Cob told the Blackwold brigands.'Ned's a good fellow with a difficult job. With the discovery of Calder Cob's betrayal, he's been given the task of learning what secrets Cob, one of his friends, divulged to the Blackwolds.

'If you get a chance to pay him a visit, you might be able to assist him. Calder Cob has said little to none since he was taken prisoner.

'You can find Ned within the jail. It is hard to miss...you'll likely hear that Otto whining in the stock outside the door before you reach the jail. Head north and east under the arch through the iron gates. You'll recognize the jail by the stocks -- and Otto in them -- on the porch.''Old Thistlewool knows me too well. Calder was like a hero to a great many folk round here, me not the least. I'm still shocked about all this, and I'm having a bad time getting him to talk.''Ned is more than likely going to need help loosening Cob's tongue. If you think you can spare him a few moments, you might give him a hand.

'You can find him in the jail.'Merion, one of the town guards of Mossward, tries to help repair the defences of the village following the Uruk raid.Suitable branches for repairing the village's defences can be found north-west of Mossward, at the bases of trees.Collect suitable branches north-west of Mossward (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Merion is waiting by the east gate of Mossward.'The raid on Mossward by that band of Uruks has left our defences in a terrible state, friend. If it wouldn't be too much trouble, could I ask you to collect fallen branches from the bases of trees outside the town? With them we will be able to patch up holes in the outer palisade and replace some of the wood burned during the attack.

'I know it does not sound like a very important task, but it would truly be a big help to us. With so many good folk injured and slain during the raid, Eleri thinks we should focus on the small things we can do something about. I suppose I see her point.''Could I ask you to collect fallen branches from the bases of trees outside the town? With them we will be able to patch up holes in the outer palisade and replace some of the wood burned during the attack.''Thank you for these branches, ${PLAYER}. I will see to making use of them to patch the holes in our defences, and perhaps some of the larger branches can be used for tool-making; small items have gone missing of late, for no reason I can tell, and we need to replace them.

'This has been the life of a Mossward town guard, ever since they took me on! If Uruks are loose in Swanfleet, that may be about to change....'As the Dark Lord's fell sorcery raises Barad-dûr ever higher over the lands of Mordor, terrible tremors have shaken loose the stones of the Dark Tower.You are fated to labour in service to the Dark Lord.Fallen rubble can be found throughout the dungeons of Barad-dûr.Break fallen rubble (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You are fated to labour in service to the Dark Lord.The flood of refugees into Archet has caused a supply shortage, and Captain Brackenbrook's quartermaster, Peg Pruner, is having difficulty providing for the refugees.Peg Pruner is at the north end of the Archet town square.

Constable Thistlewool mentioned that Peg Pruner may need assistance gathering food for the displaced inhabitants of Archet.'Peg Pruner, Captain Brackenbrook's quartermaster, needs some assistance gathering enough food for the refugees that have poured into the city since the brigands started their attacks.

'Now that Calder Cob's been exposed as a traitor, thanks to you, we need to make certain that we're watching out for the safety of the poor folk that lost their homes and livelihoods. If you think you might have the time or the desire to help, why not visit Peg and offer a hand?

'Peg is taking recruits at the north end of the main square of Archet. Just follow the main road further into town.''Constable Thistlewool sent you? I'll be sure to thank him. Able bodies willing to assist us are certainly the order of the day.

'If you are willing, I have the work.''Peg is north of here, just up the road.'Many Stout-axes cannot endure the harsh treatment of the Dark Lord.You are fated to labour in service to the Dark Lord.A bucket can be found in the Foul Larder in the dungeons of Barad-dûr.Collect a bucket from the Foul LarderA washing-tub can be found in the Foul Larder in the dungeons of Barad-dûr.Fill the bucket with water from the washing-tubExhausted Stout-axes can be found throughout the dungeons of Barad-dûr.You are fated to labour in service to the Dark Lord.A poisonous plant called "Skorgrím's Bloom." threatens the wildlife of Ered Luin. Without someone to halt the spread, the lives of many creatures may be at risk.Skorgrím's Bloom can be found flowering along the path leading to Mirkstone Tunnels, north-west of Frerin's Court.

Destroy the poisonous blossoms, lest they spread down the mountainside.Destroy Skorgrím's Bloom plants (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Erynwen is found at the centre of Frerin's Court.

You destroyed some of the Skorgrím's Bloom plants, as Erynwen asked.'There is a dangerous plant spreading from the goblin cave north-west of here. The dwarves call it "Skorgrím's Bloom" after that vile dwarf slain many years ago when Edhelion fell. 

'This foul weed is foreign to Ered Luin, and its spread must be contained. The plant is quite deadly, and I fear that many unknowing creatures will be poisoned if they graze upon it.

'We cannot wipe it out in one blow, but we can halt its spread. The plants can be found flowering along the path leading to Mirkstone Tunnels, north-west of here. Uproot some of these plants, and you will be rewarded.''A poisonous plant called Skorgrím's Bloom flowers along the path leading to Mirkstone Tunnels, just north-west of here. Stop the spread of the plant by destroying any blossoms you find.''Thank you, kind ${RACE}. If we can keep the plants from spreading, perhaps they will die out on their own. The creatures of Thorin's Gate are safer thanks to you.'Celandine made some medicinal bilberry tea for Amdir, hoping that it will help to strengthen and cheer him against whatever shadow has come over him.Amdir sits across the lane from Celandine, in the centre of Archet.

You should bring Celandine's restorative bilberry tea to Amdir to help him gather his strength and wits.Celandine is across the lane from Amdir, in Archet's centre.

You should return to Celandine and lie to her on Amdir's behalf. He did not drink her tea, but he does not wish her to be disappointed....Return to Celandine and lie for Amdir's sake'Doesn't the bilberry tea smell delightful? It still reminds me of my Gammer, who made this for me when I was under the weather, even though my Gammer is long gone.

'You were such a dear to collect those wild berries for me, so I shan't be taking all the credit! Why don't you bring this nice brew to Amdir so he can thank you himself?

'You make sure he drinks it all up!''Quickly now! You mustn't let the tea get cold. I hope it makes Amdir feel better!''Does... that tea smell of bilberries? That was very... sweet of Celandine, and very good of you to fetch her the makings. I am all the sorrier, for I cannot drink this now. I... I do not feel well enough just now.

'I will pretend to drink it. You must tell Celandine I enjoyed her tea. Please... do not let her know that her kindness was wasted on me!''...'

Amdir's eyes are now closed, and he makes no more effort to speak at this time. His face is as pale as ice.'Did that tea perk him up? I knew it would! Bilberry tea always fixes me right up, and I know it would be especially sweet if it came from both of us, ${PLAYER}.

'I know Amdir will be back on his feet soon -- he is so brave!'Bevan, one of the town guards of Mossward, has heard that craban feathers might serve a number of useful purposes.Crebain hassle travellers on the Old South Road, north of Mossward, and Bevan believes their feathers might be put to a number of uses.Collect feathers from crebain near the Old South Road (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bevan is at the north gate of Mossward, waiting for you to return with the craban feathers for which he asked.'I have heard there are many uses to which unassuming objects might be put, and one of these seemingly ordinary items is in great supply just outside Mossward! I speak of the crebain that trouble the hills just to the north, ${PLAYER}! If you defeat some of the winged pests and bring me their feathers, doubt not that you will marvel at the wide variety of potential uses those feathers might be put!

'And it will do the town some good to have fewer of the crebain hassling travellers along the Old South Road. Some folk whisper that the birds spy for Orcs and other evil creatures, but I do not have any proof of that.''If you defeat some of the crebain that flit about the Old South Road and bring me their feathers, you will marvel at the wide variety of potential uses those feathers might be put!''There you are, ${PLAYER}! Let us see these feathers!'

You hand the feathers to Bevan and he grimaces.

'They really are quite dirty, aren't they? No matter. These will serve very nicely for making quills... see, you'd just dip this part into your ink... no, there's a bit too much give on the feather here. Well, perhaps as a... as a paintbrush? Hmmm, it feels like the ridges here on the end would leave streaks, doesn't it? Maybe as an arrow? See how it compares to these arrows of mine? Hmmm... rather poorly, I suppose.'

Bevan scowls.

'Still! Many fine uses! Mayhap I will put them all in a sack and see how it serves as a pillow?'Mundo Sackville-Baggins, a fellow prisoner from the Blackwold camp, is naturally very hungry after the ordeal, for hobbits have insatiable appetites.Wild boars, perfect for pork or sausage, can be found roaming the outskirts of Archet.

You should collect boar-meat for Mundo's next meal so that he doesn't waste away from starvation.Collect boar-meat for Mundo's next meal (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mundo Sackville-Baggins awaits your return in the village of Archet.

You should bring the boar-meat you collected to Mundo, who will probably eat it all. You only hope that it is enough to satisfy him.'Hey, you there! Yes, you there! I'm very hungry!

'I haven't eaten since some woman gave me a bit of bread and jam for breakfast this morning. Such fare is hardly meant to feed a rabbit, let alone a full-grown hobbit like me.

'If you get me proper food, I will reward you handsomely. I want something hearty and delicious!''What are you still doing here? Are you putting food in my belly right now? Do you have some hidden store of vittles? No? Then get moving, if you please!''Hooray, you brought me pork! Or bacon! Or perhaps sausage... oh, it doesn't matter; I can make a little of each.'

Mundo weighs the parcel of meats in his hands.

'This will give me something to munch on for a bit; hopefully this provender will last me until I can go back home.'The stone beneath Thorin's Gate is honeycombed by cave-claw burrows. If the damage continues, the entire area could be weakened enough to collapse in upon itself.Cave-claws are found near the buildings and caves to the east of Thorin's Gate and inside the Rockbelly Pit caves near Winterheight, south-east of the Frerin's Court.

Bolli asked you to defeat cave-claws found throughout Thorin's Gate.Defeat cave-claws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bolli can be found in Frerin's Court.

You should return to Bolli with news of your success.Talk to Bolli in Frerin's Court<rgb=#00e4ff>This quest has been restored for Legendary Servers!</rgb>

'Blasted nuisances! Even the dullest dwarf knows that you don't over-mine a cavern. Riddle it with holes, and the whole floor is apt to collapse. Well, the cave-claws don't know that and their incessant digging is going to be the ruin of us all! 

'What? You expect the Dourhands to do something about it? A clever jest, but I'm not laughing. Can you lend us some aid in dealing with the pests? The cave-claws, mind you...not the Dourhands. Although....

'Be that as it may, the Rockbelly Pit is overrun with cave-claws. Head east and up the hill to Winterheight. The entrance to the Pit is there.''Cave-claws! I hate them! Always ripping up the ground, leaving piles of earth in their wake. And who has to clean up their mess? The Longbeards, that's who!

'Give us a hand, will you?''You certainly took care of that problem. I'll see to it that the Dourhands do their part from now on.'Explore the historical and cultural places as well as the farmsteads that can be found throughout the Shire.Explore the Farms of the ShireExplore the Sights of the ShireThe flood of refugees into Archet has caused a supply shortage, and Captain Brackenbrook's quartermaster, Peg Pruner, is having difficulty providing for the refugees.There are boars in the fields south and east of Archet.

Peg Pruner has asked you to hunt boars to help her provide meat for the refugees.'With all these folk coming in from the outlying farms, there's just not enough food to go around! My Ned's got his hands full trying to keep the peace inside the walls, and what with everyone else drilling on the training grounds, there's no one left to do the hunting.

'Maybe you could help out? Boars used to be plentiful in the fields south and east of town, if the Blackwolds haven't slaughtered them all. Could you hunt a few and bring me their meat?

'It will take a good amount of meat to feed all these people. I'd appreciate any help you could give me.''Did you hunt every boar in the fields? I'll not complain. This'll do well for quite a while! Thank you!

'Here, you should share in this bounty, and if you ever find yourself needing more, just come and see me.'The Dourhands have been lax in their guard and have allowed goblins to roam the slopes of Ered Luin.Goblins can be found to the south-west of Frerin's Court, up on the slopes of the mountains.

Dwalin has asked you to deal with the goblins encamped near Thorin's Hall.Defeat goblins (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dwalin can be found in Frerin's Court, north-east of the goblin-camp.

You should return to Dwalin with news of your success.Talk to Dwalin at Frerin's Court<rgb=#00e4ff>This quest has been restored for Legendary Servers!</rgb>

'While this matter of Dourhands and goblins being in league with one another cannot go ignored, we must set our attention on more pressing matters.

'To the south-west, there is an encampment of goblins on the slopes above Frerin's Court. Go there and deal with the lot of them.

'In the meantime, I'm going to look into this Dourhand matter further.''Excellent work, ${PLAYER}. With the threat of the goblins lessened, we can deal with more sinister matters here in the valley.

'Thank you for all of your hard work. I trust this will prove an ample reward.'Cal Sprigley has asked you to gather what help you can from the nearby town of Combe.The Combe Gate is not far to the west of Sprigley's Farm, along the road south from the town of Archet.

You should ask for assistance in Combe, for they have many well-equipped soldiers there due to their proximity to the dangerous Chetwood.Go to Combe to plead for aid against the Blackwold threatCal Sprigley is at his farm in the southernmost tip of Archet.

You should return to Cal and inform him that the road to Combe is blocked at the gate, and Archet will be on its own.Return to Cal Sprigley with your dire newsCaptain Brackenbrook is within the walls of the town of Archet.

You should return to Brackenbrook with Cal Sprigley's letter as proof of the Blackwolds' intentions. He must be told that the southern part of Archet is rife with signs of an impending raid.'I'm glad you're here! Some Blackwolds tried to attack us this morning, but we drove them off.

'We must get help from Combe, the next town over. It's south-west of here, and not more than a short journey away. They must bring their soldiers to Archet at once to help stave off the raid!

'Please make haste -- I fear the Blackwolds mean to attack tonight. The road here is crawling with brigands, but I pray you can make it safe to Combe.''Please hurry, ${PLAYER}! I think the Blackwolds mean to attack tonight. I don't know how it came to be, but they are organized now. They pose a greater threat than I could have imagined.''B-blocked? No, it cannot be! My men travelled to Combe only yesterday for a delivery.... That is bad news, ${PLAYER}. The captain must be informed of what's going on at once!

'Here is a note for Brackenbrook. He knows my scrawl; he will believe you now.''Brackenbrook needs to know what the Blackwolds have done! You must get back to Archet -- I fear there isn't much time!'As Brackenbrook reads the letter from Cal Sprigley, his face is blank. He reads it a second time, and only then does he look at you.

'I have been a fool. Oh, what have I done?'Long ago, Skorgrím, lord of the Dourhands, allied himself with goblins and trolls. Dwalin believes the Dourhands are returning to their old ways.Goblin-prowlers are found to the south-west of Frerin's Court, lurking in the hills of the region.

Dwalin has asked you to slay a goblin and search it. If you find anything interesting, return it to Dwalin at once.<rgb=#00e4ff>This quest has been restored for Legendary Servers!</rgb>

'The Elves told me about goblins digging in the Silver Deep, and what with the mine guards and the red-robed man...well, it's got me thinking. Are the Dourhands involved? The twitch in my whiskers says yes, but I need solid evidence before I start hurling accusations.

'The first thing to do is learn if the Dourhands are using goblins cohorts. Kill one of the goblins prowling about west of here and search its body for proof of the Dourhands' involvement.

'The goblin prowlers are found in the hills south-west of Frerin's Court.''I knew it! Goblins with dwarf-craft weapons? This has the stink of Dourhand devilry. They've gone back to their old ways, have they? Well, we'll give them something to think about!

'It's time to get to the bottom of this, once and for all...'The trail of flowers that grow near Skorgrím's corpse have led you to Gormr, the Steward of Thorin's Hall.Gormr guards the door to the tomb at the Berghold, in the south of the Thorin's Gate area.

You should speak with Gormr and find out what you can about his initiatives as Steward of Thorin's Hall and why Skorgrím's body has been moved.Talk to Gormr Doursmith, Steward of Thorin's Hall'I heard one of the guards here talking -- down yonder is Gormr, the Steward appointed by Thorin Oakenshield before he left. Gormr is the reason we have come, and the leader of this Dourhand rabble you see all over the place.

'You are the well-spoken one in our party, ${PLAYER}. You might as well go speak with him and find out what he has done here...and why he has grown silent over the years.''Gormr should be here in this keep, though I don't know why anyone would want to spend his time in such a dreadful, foreboding place....''Is that ${PLAYER}? It has been long indeed since last we met. I remember you: you were the dwarf that discovered Skorgrím's body the day I discovered the relics he sought. The Longbeards of Dwalin's party are not welcome here, but you...you I might allow, for your good deed.

'Do you like what I have done with the place? Welcome to Skorgrím's Tomb!'Gormr, Steward of Thorin's Hall and leader of the Dourhands who now inhabit it, will not grant you access to Skorgrím's Tomb. He has, however, given you leave to visit the shrine instead.Bats can be found in the Rockbelly Pit cave, which can be entered in the courtyard near the Berghold or from the overlooking Winterheight.

You should defeat bats in the Rockbelly Pit to ensure that the Shrine to Skorgrím remains clean.Defeat bats in the Rockbelly Pit (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Shrine to Skorgrím is in the Rockbelly Pit.

You should visit the shrine to see what all the fuss over Skorgrím is about.Visit the Shrine to Skorgrím in the Rockbelly PitGormr awaits you at the Berghold in Thorin's Gate.

You should return to Gormr after having completed the task he gave you.Talk to Gormr Doursmith at the Berghold'I'm afraid you have come here for nothing anyway, ${PLAYER}. No one is to gain admittance to Skorgrím's Tomb. It is a sacred ground, and only descendants of Skorgrím -- that is, the Dourhands -- have leave to enter.

'You are more than welcome to pay your respects to our fallen king at his Shrine, which is just east of here, in the Rockbelly Pit. Skorgrím was a great king, after all, and deserves tribute.''Why do you waste your time talking to me? I have nothing more to say to you at present. You must do as I asked and get the bats out of our shrine if you wish to speak with me again.''So you walked in our shrine, did you? As you can see, the Dourhands' reverence of our fallen king is very great. The Longbeards and the Elves do not understand such loyalty, so this was perhaps a good lesson for you.

'But do not go away just yet. I'm sure we can think of more tasks to occupy you while you trespass, eh, ${PLAYER}?'Captain Brackenbrook has been estranged from his son, Jon, ever since they fought over the loyalty of Calder Cob. Captain Brackenbrook trusted Cob, while Jon had been suspicious of him. The orders you found proved Jon right.Jon Brackenbrook can be found at the Hunter's Lodge that lies along the road east of Archet.

Captain Brackenbrook, uncertain of whom to trust, has asked you to speak with his son Jon and earn his aid. He promised to reward you handsomely if you succeed.'I have been such a fool. With Calder Cob turned traitor, I do not know whom to trust anymore. How many more spies dwell among us?

'I would ask a favour of you. Go speak with my son Jon and ask for his help against the Blackwolds. Jon spends most of his time at the Hunter's Lodge to the east of Archet. From the town-gate, follow the road that wends to the east, and you will eventually make your way to the lodge.

'If you can sway my son's heart, I will reward you handsomely.''So my father's eyes have been opened to the truth? And none too soon! The Blackwolds are preparing to move against Archet, and the defences are ill-prepared.''Go to the Hunter's Lodge and speak with my son, Jon. The east road that leads from Archet's gate will take you there.'Captain Brackenbrook, in his loyalty to Calder Cob, allowed his own son to become estranged from him after his son and Calder Cob had a falling out. The Captain took Calder Cob's side, and his son has not set foot in Archet since.Strider has not yet left Archet, but he is anxious to leave so that he can pursue his true charge.

Strider is a wise Ranger who has seen many evil things come to pass; you should ask him what to do.Brackenbrook is hopeless -- you should ask Strider what to doJon Brackenbrook, the exiled son of the Captain, can be found in the Hunter's Lodge to the north-east of Archet.

You should appeal to Jon Brackenbrook and his hunters to come to the aid of the besieged town.Ask the exiled hunter to come to Archet's aid'You cannot fathom just how wrong I have been. I have trusted Calder Cob and heeded his advice for a long time... even after my own son tried to warn me of his duplicity.

'My son, Jon, is a worthy hunter, but he has not set foot in this town for over three years. He will never forgive me for the things I said to him.

'O, what a fool I am! I told him he was not welcome in Archet until he stopped behaving like a jealous child. Now he and his hunters are our only hope. But what can I do? He does not deserve to be dragged deeper into my folly. Nay, Archet must withstand this storm without help....''Nay, I will not ask Jon to come to our aid. I am not worthy to ask favours of my son. I will fall with Archet.''Do not despair, ${PLAYER}! You have done remarkably well -- indeed, better than I could have done. You earned Captain Brackenbrook's trust.

'You must appeal to his son Jon against his wishes. I must leave soon, and Archet will need all the help it can get even if a father's pride is at stake.

'I am confident you will be able to bring the hunters in and give this town a fighting chance. In the meantime, I must make haste. Amdir assures me he just needs rest....''Nay, I will not ask Jon to come to our aid. I am not worthy to ask favours of my son. I will fall with Archet.''You have proven yourself to be both wise and brave, ${PLAYER}. I trust Archet's fate to you in my stead! You have given me a little more peace of mind.''You come from the town, do you? I have been watching from the bounds... the skies have turned dark and fell, and there is a shadow of unrest over all of Archet, over Men and beasts and trees alike. Let me guess: my father has not the wisdom to observe such things, so you heard of his exiled son and came to me for aid.

'Come, then, out with it! I desire to hear what you have to tell.'Red flowers have grown around Skorgrím's crumbled grave and around the place where he fell. Elladan believes that the flowers could possibly be what Elrond saw as blood-red footprints in his dream and will lead to Skorgrím's body.The "Blood-red footsteps" begin at the Silver Deep Mine. Elladan suspects that they lead to wherever Skorgrím now lies.

You should follow the red flowers to their end, examining each for signs, for they grow where Skorgrím's corpse corrupted the land as he was moved over it.Explore the gate at the Silver Deep Mine's courtyardThe "Blood-red footsteps" begin at the Silver Deep Mine and grow along the road. Elladan suspects that they lead to wherever Skorgrím now lies.

You should follow the red flowers to their end, examining each for signs, for they grow where Skorgrím's corpse corrupted the land as he was moved over it.Follow the trail of red flowers down the roadThe "Blood-red footsteps" begin at the Silver Deep Mine and grow along the road. Elladan suspects that they lead to wherever Skorgrím now lies.

You should follow the red flowers to their end, examining each for signs, for they grow where Skorgrím's corpse corrupted the land as he was moved over it.Follow the trail of red flowers down the roadThe "Blood-red footsteps" begin at the Silver Deep Mine and grow along the road. Elladan suspects that they lead to wherever Skorgrím now lies.

You should follow the red flowers to their end, examining each for signs, for they grow where Skorgrím's corpse corrupted the land as he was moved over it.Follow the trail of red flowers down the roadThe "Blood-red footsteps" begin at the Silver Deep Mine and grow along the road. Elladan suspects that they lead to wherever Skorgrím now lies.

You should follow the red flowers to their end, examining each for signs, for they grow where Skorgrím's corpse corrupted the land as he was moved over it.Follow the trail of red flowers down the roadThe "Blood-red footsteps" begin at the Silver Deep Mine and grow along the road. Elladan suspects that they lead to wherever Skorgrím now lies.

You should follow the red flowers to their end, examining each for signs, for they grow where Skorgrím's corpse corrupted the land as he was moved over it.Follow the trail of red flowers down the roadAn unfriendly-looking keep now stands before you.

You should scout the keep, for the trail indicates that Skorgrím's body may have been moved here.Talk to the longbeard dwarf spying nearby'A trail, ${PLAYER}! Red flowers leading a trail through the snow...do you think these are the footprints of blood?

  "Blood-red footsteps
  Upon snow coloured black,
  Where the Dour King walks
  to take back his throne
  and finish what was begun."

'We have black snow and the body of the Dour King has been moved, leaving the fell flowers as footprints. Something evil is at work here, and I am sure something evil was at work when he sought our relics for immortality.

'We must follow him again. My brother is scouting for signs as well -- seek him as you search.''Head first to the Silver Deep Mine and be sure you follow the red flowers wherever they lead. Be careful, ${PLAYER}! I suspect a great evil is afoot here.''${PLAYER}! I did not expect to see you here. I asked to speak with the Steward, and the Dourhands directed me here. This was once a great storage-house of Thorin's...I don't think I like what the Dourhands have done with the place.'You have been asked to collect black stones for a ritual Gormr has planned for the commemoration of Skorgrím, king of the Dourhands of old, before their fall into obscurity.The ritual stones can be found in and around the Rockbelly Pit, east of the Berghold at Thorin's Gate.

You should collect black stones for the commemorative ritual being prepared by the Dourhands.Collect black stones for the Ritual of Commemoration (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bjarni is at the Berghold in the southern area of Thorin's Gate.

You should speak with Bjarni and give him the black stones you have collected.'Lord Gormr has told us that you were the one to first discover Skorgrím's body the day the Silver Deep Mine nearly collapsed, when Gormr took his Stewardship. This has earned you a small amount of favour with us, even though your party of Longbeards has come unbidden and unwelcome.

'We are having a commemoration of Skorgrím's many achievements as king of the Dourhands, but we need ritual stones, black as pitch. I was not able to find enough for everyone that plans to attend. They are a form of tribute to Skorgrím-king.

'You should make yourself useful and collect some more. They can be found in and around the Winterheight cave, east of here.''The stones must be black as shadows. Nothing else will do. I believe you can find such stones in the Rockbelly Pit cave that overlooks this keep.''These are the very stones we need. Not bad for a Longbeard! Lord Gormr was right -- you are not our enemy. Your loyalty to the Dourhands will not be forgotten, ${CLASS}.'Meneldir is concerned that Uruks threaten the safety of the folk who dwell in Swanfleet after years of peace and quiet.Kingsfoil can be found on the hills east of Mossward, as can beasts that might threaten the town.

Meneldir tasked you with a number of things you can do to help provide for the needful folk of Mossward.Collect kingsfoil near Mossward (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat beasts near Mossward (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Meneldir is by the east gate in Mossward, waiting for you to return with the kingsfoil he requested you gather.The stable-master of Mossward can be found to the south-west of Meneldir.

Meneldir recommended that you speak to the stable-master of Mossward and ride one of the horses to the village of Clegur, in the Stoor-vales, to warn that folk of their potential danger.Find the Stable-master of Mossward to the south-westThe stable-master of Mossward offers an affordable and efficient travel route to Clegur, in the Stoor-vales.Hire one of the stable-master's horses and follow the travel route to Clegur, in the Stoor-valesBriallen Wynbuch is in the village of Clegur. She should be told the reason for your arrival.Talk to Briallen Wynbuch in the village of Clegur'I have been away from the land that was my home for many years now, ${PLAYER}. I was once of the Dúnedain, my friend, the Rangers of the North, and one of their several charges was to safeguard these lands and their peoples from harm. Their other duties... well, they are likely as far away as they have always been, and not my concern any longer. I will not speak of those today.

'But a peaceful village has been attacked and some of its people slain. Will you help me replenish my supplies of the healing herb called kingsfoil, and defeat any of the creatures nearby that may threaten Mossward? You will know kingsfoil by its sweet smell and the feel of its leaves. Look for it on the rolling hills to the east of the village.''Will you help me replenish my supplies of the healing herb called kingsfoil, and defeat any of the creatures nearby that may threaten Mossward? You will know kingsfoil by its sweet smell and the feel of its leaves. Look for it on the rolling hills to the east of the village.''Thank you for the kingsfoil, and for helping dissuade some of the more aggressive creatures from straying too near the village. Against such beasts Eleri and Bevan and the others of the guard are more than a match, but against Uruks I fear they will be out-classed. The question now is whether Madthrug's band was alone, or if there are others of his kind waiting for their next chance to strike?

'Mossward is not the only village in Swanfleet, ${PLAYER}. Can I ask you to check on the Stoors who live nearby? Visit the stable-master here in the village for a quick means of travelling to the village of Clegur, in the Stoor-vales. They should be told what happened in Mossward, and guard themselves against possible attack.

'The stable-master is to the south-west, and provides efficient travel options for reaching distant lands.''Can I ask you to check on the Stoors who live nearby? Visit the stable-master here in the village for a quick means of travelling to the village of Clegur, in the Stoor-vales. They should be told what happened in Mossward, and guard themselves against possible attack.

'The stable-master is to the south-west, and provides efficient travel options for reaching distant lands.''Welcome to Clegur, finest of the villages to be found in the Stoor-vales! May I ask the reason for your visit?'

You explain that the village of Mossward was recently attacked by a raiding party consisting of goblins and Uruks, and the Ranger Meneldir fears other similar bands may endanger the villages of the Stoor-vales.

'Oh my! Are you certain? We have not had such troubles for... well, longer than I can remember, that's for sure! Who would threaten the Stoors?'Concerned about the recent attack on Mossward by the Uruks, Eleri wants you to scout for more of the creatures up past the nearby wolf-camp.Eleri asked you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> out into the distance from the hill south-east of Howlingstones, east of Mossward.Eleri is on the eastern side of Mossward, within view of the gate.Talk to Eleri on the eastern side of Mossward'I want you to understand something, ${PLAYER}, something that may be hard to believe given the events of last night. Mossward is a quiet town. It has always been a quiet town. Winters can be hard if we don't fill the store-house with sufficient food, but it has been many years since the folk of Mossward failed in their preparations.

'Mostly we've had to worry about the wolves of Howlingstones growing over bold and straying too near the walls, but the Uruks loose in Swanfleet have changed all that. What will we do if another war-captain like Madthrug brings his band to Mossward? Could they remain hidden from our watch-towers until they were nearly upon us, as they did before?

'Beyond Howlingstones to the south-east, a hill rises above the plain. Can I ask you to go to that hill and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> out into the distance? You may not see anything... but even that will be good for me to know.''Could another band of Uruks remain hidden from our watch-towers until it was nearly upon us, as happened before? Beyond Howlingstones to the south-east, a hill rises above the plain. Can I ask you to go to that hill and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> out into the distance? You may not see anything... but even that will be good for me to know.''All appears quiet? That is good, ${PLAYER}, but I will not relax my guard. There are more locations to scout, for there are several places from which the Uruks or others of their ilk might approach.'Eleri has a more dangerous request for you now, but if Tharbad serves as a camp for the Uruks that raided Mossward, she wants to know about it.Eleri asked you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for the mark of the White Hand at the gates of Tharbad, north-west of Mossward. She was very insistent that you avoid battle if the foes there seem too dangerous.Eleri is on the eastern side of Mossward, in view of the gate.Talk to Eleri on the eastern side of Mossward'I have one more request of you, ${PLAYER}, and then I will be satisfied. This may be more dangerous than your earlier scouts, and if you hesitate to accept this task I will understand.

'The old city of Tharbad is to the north-west of Mossward. It has not been the thriving centre of trade it was during the height of earlier kingdoms for hundreds of years, and it has been abandoned ever since flood-waters took the last of its great bridges....'

Eleri raises an eyebrow.

'Abandoned by good folk, ${PLAYER}. Has it become a den of Uruks? Is that from where Madthrug's raiding band came? This, then, is my dangerous request. I ask that you go to the gates of Tharbad and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for some sign of the insignia he wore: the mark of the White Hand. Beware, for if Tharbad is truly held by forces of evil, you may be better served by keeping from their notice and not engaging in battle.

'Tharbad is to the north-west of Mossward. Go just to the gate, mind you, and no further.''Has Tharbad become a den of Uruks? Is that from where Madthrug's raiding band came? This, then, is my dangerous request. I ask that you go to the gates of Tharbad and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for some sign of the insignia he wore: the mark of the White Hand. Beware, for if Tharbad is truly held by forces of evil, you may be better served by keeping from their notice and not engaging in battle.

'Tharbad is to the north-west of Mossward. Go just to the gate, mind you, and no further.''I am not surprised there are Orcs crawling throughout Tharbad, but it is good to hear you did not see the mark of the White Hand while scouting near the gate. Orc-kind are always a danger to the Free Peoples, but it is the organization revealed by Madthrug's war-band that most troubled me. We can deal with Orcs, for we have before, but the Uruks of the White Hand seemed different to me.

'Thank you for helping ease my mind with these scouting missions, ${PLAYER}! You will always be welcome in Mossward.'Eleri believes the Old South Road is a boon for Mossward, but if it speeds the progress of Uruks across Swanfleet, she wants to know about it.Eleri asked you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> into the distance at the intersection of the Old South Road with the track that leaves Mossward from the north gate of the village.Eleri is on the east side of town, in view of the gate.Talk to Eleri on the eastern side of Mossward'You know the road that passes through Swanfleet, of course. It begins on this side of the old city of Tharbad and runs east past the Wadewater until it reaches Enedwaith. There are not as many travellers along it as once there were, but Mossward is still a sheltered place of rest for those who come this way. Might it also serve to speed Uruks and other Orc-kind on their raids?

'Will you follow the track that runs from the north gate of Mossward until it meets up with this road? Once there, I would appreciate if you took a <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb>, as you did before. You should be able to see both east and west for quite a distance, and if there are more raiders about, you might see them.''Will you follow the track that runs from the north gate of Mossward until it meets up with the road that runs east and west through Swanfleet? Once there, I would appreciate if you took a <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb>, as you did before. You should be able to see for quite a distance, and if there are more raiders about you might see them.''You say you saw no sign of Uruk war-bands along the road? These are good tidings, for it means the road is still a boon for Mossward, and not a bane, as I feared it might become.

'That road has been key to Mossward's survival for my entire life, ${PLAYER}. The portion of it we know begins at Tharbad, and although the road itself is broken in places and obscured by foliage, it runs through Swanfleet and passes on into Enedwaith. From there it enters Dunland, leads through the Gap of Rohan, and continues on into Gondor. They say it passes Minas Tirith and eventually ends at a port-city along the coast, although I have never journeyed in that land.

'North of Tharbad the road has another name. It is called the Greenway, and only Meneldir's people are like to remember when it and our own Old South Road were one and the same: the North-South Road between the kingdoms of Arnor and Gondor. That was long ago, even by their reckoning.'Bogi is concerned that the Longbeards do not have enough food for a prolonged stay. He needs aurochs meat to help stock the larder at Thorin's Hall.Herds of aurochs are found throughout the hills to the west of Frerin's Court. 

Kill some of the beasts and bring their meat back to Bogi.'The Lonely Mountain seems so far away. Ah, how I wish I were there now! Our larders here in the Blue Mountains are scant, and we Longbeards are going to be here for some time. 

'If you want to make yourself useful, go out and hunt some of the aurochs for me. There are plenty of those beasts wandering west of Frerin's Court to last all season, and we could use the meat.''Bravo, ${PLAYER}! We'll have a grand feast tonight and salt the rest of the haunches to last the rest of the season.

'Here, you may find use of some of this as well. It will keep you strong. If ever you are in need of such provisions, come and see me.'Celandine Brandybuck believes that the medicinal qualities of bilberries will make a fine tea to stave off Amdir's apparent infection.Celandine smelled bilberries by water to the east of Archet as she passed through last night.

You should collect bilberries, for they make a sweet tea to stave off infection, or so the hobbits' lore will tell you.Collect bilberries for Celandine's medicinal tea (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Celandine Brandybuck awaits you in the centre of Archet.

You should bring Celandine the bilberries you collected, for she intends to make a sweet medicinal tea to help strengthen Amdir's will.'Amdir looks so changed since last night! I almost feel like he's naught but a shadow when I look at him, like he isn't really there... how it makes me shiver!

'I saw Strider make a salve of kingsfoil for his wound, but my Gammer always used a different remedy when a young Brandybuck would get a nasty splinter -- sweet bilberry tea!

'I smelled bilberries by the water to the east of here as we passed in the night, which means they're in season. I would dearly like to make a tea for poor Amdir.''I hope my Gammer's tea will make Amdir feel a bit better. It always cheered me up when I was ill!''Well done! You have found more than enough for a good strong tea. This is sure to cheer Amdir right up! Give me a moment, and I will steep the berries in some boiling water.'Some brave farmhands chased off the Brigands that attacked Sprigley's farm, but they have not yet returned, and Wil Wheatley fears the worst.Cal Sprigley's farmhands went off towards the east after some attacking Blackwold brigands.

You should search for the missing farmhands and bring them back safe to the farm so that Wil and Cal can ease their fears.Search for Walt WheatleySearch for Bill HayspearSearch for Thornton WoodleyWil Wheatley is at Cal Sprigley's farm in the south of Archet, awaiting news of his fellow workers and his father.

You should break the news to Wil that his father and the other farmhands were murdered in their bold pursuit of the Blackwolds.Return to Wil Wheatley with the tragic news'My dad and the other farmhands, they gave chase to the brigands after the attack on our farm. I don't think the brigands expected us to put up such a fight after they surprised us. There weren't many of them, but I never saw them act so bold afore today.

'Only problem is, they ain't back yet. They ran off to the east, chasing the brigands into the woods. It was hours ago, though, and I'm very worried!

'Could you go look for them and bring them back safe?''My dad ain't as young as he used to be. He should know better than to go off chasing folk around like that. I am very worried for him and the others.''No! It cannot be! My poor father... and the others, no, it cannot be, it cannot.'

Wil is silent for a long moment, his eyes closed, but after a great pause he is roused.

'I will have my revenge upon those Blackwolds, or I will die trying! Archet will never fall to such murderous fiends.'Farming represents the heart of the Shire, and farmsteads can be found throughout -- the largest of which surround the town of Michel Delving.Explore the Farms of the Shire.Dora Brownlock owns one of the largest farms near the sleepy village of Waymeet. She has been having troubles with wolves recently.Bamfurlong is the land owned by Farmer Maggot, one of the most respected farmers in the Marish.Old Odo Burrow once held the largest leaf farm in the Southfarthing, though it has now fallen to ruin.The Delving Fields are the largest farmhold in the Westfarthing, providing foodstuffs for much of the Shire.Farmer Appledore grows some of the sweetest fruit in all the Shire.Cam Puddifoot grows a variety of vegetables, but he is best known for his honey. Unfortunately, his bees are often threatened by bears.The pastoral peace of the Shire presents travellers with an excellent opportunity to explore its historical and cultural places.Explore the Sights of the Shire.This enormous tree has long been known as a place where festive occasions can be celebrated by the Hobbits of the Shire. Standing on the outskirts of Hobbiton proper it can be seen for quite some ways, and during festivities it is often hung with dozens of bright streamers and lanterns providing a cheery atmosphere for most any event.It is said that when old Bilbo Baggins returned from his wanderings abroad, he came back with a good deal more wealth than he had before he left.

While rumours abound as to the source and improper nature of his riches, among the works he built within the Shire was the Methel-stage upon which hobbits of an artistic leaning are invited to perform and entertain their fellows during festive occasions.

Of course, Bilbo has disappeared again recently - bringing up all the old rumours of his improper adventures and such - but many of the folk of the Shire nevertheless continue to appreciate the causes to which he put his money.The Stocktower is a curious old Arnorian ruin standing guard near the Brandywine Bridge.

Few such ruins still stand in the Shire - having long ago been dismantled by industrious hobbits to construct more modern residences, but the Stocktower has become something of an icon and mischevious hobbit children have been known to frequent the ruin despite their parents' admonishments to the contrary.For a long time, dwarves were not seen with much frequency in the Shire and bringing stone from far abroad was expensive, so the hobbits constructed their own quarry to supply the needs of builders and masons.

Recently, however, better quality stone has been available from the West, so the quarry has fallen into disuse and has become infested with dangerous vermin.Bridgefields Wall is one of the few remaining signs of the ancient Kingdom of Arnor within the bounds of the Shire.

Farmer Puddifoot has been threatening to pull it down for years now to build walls for his own fields, but some of the richer hobbit families of Michel Delving have prevented him from doing so in the Shire councils, demanding that the wall be preserved for historical purposes. So it stands there still.While not as well known as the Party Tree, the Great Willow is considered a good deal more mysterious and romantic by the younger Hobbits of the Shire.

Folk tales abound of Elves travelling into the West who spend their evenings in mysterious revels beneath its boughs, and young hobbit couples often come there to declare their romantic intentions, perhaps hoping a bit of fairy magic might give them luck and bravery enough to say the words.In the dungeons of Barad-dûr, the Stout-axes are made to toil without end for the Dark Lord of Mordor.You have been ordered to labour in the dungeons of Barad-dûr.Zôreth can be found in the dungeons of Barad-dûr.

You have performed all of your duties and should now report to Zôreth.Present yourself to Zôreth'Dwarf!'

'Why do you stand idle when you know what is demanded of you? Are you content to let the rest of your malformed kind bear these burdens for you? If that is so, the Dark Lord has little use for you. You are not useless, are you?'

You shake your head.

'I thought not.

'When your labours are finished, you are to present yourself to Zôreth.

'Do as you are bidden.''Are you the dwarf Núluzagar promised me?'

You nod. Zôreth looks you over, and a smirk forms at the corner of her mouth.

'Yes, you will do.'Now that you have dealt with the local wildlife, Nos Grimsong has asked you to help with restocking supplies for Frerin's Court.Lumber can be found to the south-west of Frerin's Court.

Nos Grimsong has asked you to gather lumber for him.Collect wood-bundles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nos Grimsong is at Frerin's Cour, north-east of where you gathered the lumber.

You should return to Nos Grimsong to collect your reward.'You have done such tremendous work helping us in Frerin's Court that I cannot help but ask you to help with one final task.

'There are fallen pieces of lumber to the south-west, up the hill. Take this axe with you to chop them up into manageable piles and bring them back to me.

'Keep a wary eye out for danger...the Dourhands have not kept safe the lands surrounding Thorin's Hall.''Oh well done, well done! With this wood, we should be able to keep warm for the next few days.

'Thank you for your help, ${PLAYER}. I hope it wasn't too much trouble.'After a long journey from the lands of Misthallow led you to the border village of Mossward in Swanfleet, you have encountered a most curious hobbit! Not a fellow River Hobbit of Lyndelby, nor one of the Stoors of Swanfleet, nor still their elusive cousins rumoured to dwell in Enedwaith: he is a hobbit of a land called the Shire.Mayor Will Whitfoot can be found outside the Town Hole in Michel Delving, in the Shire.

Despite your protests to the contrary, Fridolin Took believes you to be a long-lost and unusually tall cousin of the Tooks and thinks you ought to visit the lands of the Shire. You should talk to the Mayor of the Shire, Will Whitfoot.Talk to Mayor Will Whitfoot outside the Town Hole, in Michel Delving'Well, well! That is more than enough excitement for today, I think! I don't recall seeing you around Mossward, traveller. Although, I imagine it would've been hard for me to see anything at all while I was hiding in one of these fine folks' cabinets during that raid! I hear you helped to put an end to those oversized goblins, though, and I must thank you for that. What is your name?'

You introduce yourself, and Fridolin Took introduces himself in turn.

'${PLAYER}... a curious name for a most curious hobbit. Why, you bear a striking resemblance to old Bandobras Took—the Bullroarer, as we Tooks call him—yes, very alike indeed, at least where your height is concerned! Might you be one of my long-lost cousins? No? Perhaps you're of the North-tooks, then?'

You tell Fridolin that you are not one of the Tooks, a North-took, a Bullroarer—whatever that is—or anything of the sort. You say that you hail from a place beyond the Misty Mountains, and that you have travelled to Mossward as but one of many destinations on the long journey from your home-land.

'Yes, yes, that is all well and good, but you might still be a Took of some sort. Hah! I jest, of course. Even if you are not a Took, I say you are a cousin of mine, and it would be terribly rude of me not to invite you to visit us in the Shire. You'll need to follow the road up to Bree-town, and then you'll want to head west. Once you cross the Brandywine River, you're in the Shire! Ah, but first, you'll want to stop into Michel Delving to talk to the Mayor, old Will Whitfoot. He'll want to give you a proper Shire welcome!

'Oh, and if any of the Bounders give you trouble, just tell them Fridolin's invited you for tea!''Hullo, hullo! Are you a visitor come to the Shire?

'It is my duty and pleasure to welcome you to our great country, then! I am Will Whitfoot, Mayor of Michel Delving, and of all the Shire. Who are you and from where to do you hail?'

You introduce yourself to Mayor Whitfoot, and he bows in turn. You tell him of your home, saying only that it lies beyond the Misty Mountains, and of your long journey through countless lands that led you to the village of Mossward in Swanfleet. It was there you met Fridolin Took, you say, and Mayor Whitfoot chortles to himself.

'It would be Fridolin, wouldn't it? Tsk. It is always a Took! Well, I, for one, am glad you have come to visit us. If you and your kin are indeed distant cousins of ours, then I say you are kin of mine! You are welcome to remain in the Shire for as long as you wish, ${PLAYER}. There are many fine towns and villages in the Shire, so I would suggest that you visit them all. After all, I have heard more than enough complaints about my purported favouritism!

'Nevertheless, I am certain the good folk of the Shire would welcome the help of a ${CLASS} of your... stature!'The LOTRO Store offers cosmetics, buffs, content access, and a variety of other goodies. Be sure to visit it often to see what's new!

