'I am here to provide you with information and assistance in becoming a crafter, a very important role for many of the folk in Middle-earth.'Explore the places and settlements held by the Dwarves of the Blue Mountains.NOT USEDThis courtyard was named after the brother of the great Thorin Oakenshield. It leads to the entrance of Thorin's Hall to the north.Thráin, Thorin's father, named the gates of these halls after his eldest son, Thorin Oakenshield, long before the city itself was known as Thorin's Hall.The Silver Deep is a hall that was abandoned long ago before it was completed. The discovery of ancient Elf-ruins and a troll prompted Thorin and Gandalf the Grey to seal it off.The way-station of Noglond serves as a provisioners' outpost and resting place for merchants and weary travellers.The fortress of Gondamon was once an Elven fortress, abandoned when the Elves of Edhelion abandoned their former refuge for Duillond, after Skorgrím Dourhand's treacherous assault. The dwarves rebuilt the stronghold to their liking.When the Elves left Edhelion, evil was able to regain a foothold in the valley. One of the creatures to return were the wolves. They now stalk the valley and make misery for the dwarves of Thorin's Hall.Wolf-packs may be found inhabiting the frozen lake in the valley beneath Edhelion and the Berghold, south-west of the ruins of Edhelion.

Laergil has asked you to collect wolf-hides and deliver them to Nos Grimsong, west at Frerin's Court in the centre of the valley below Edhelion.'The Dourhands do nothing to stem the growth of the wolves in this area. It is no surprise, to be honest. Despite their newfound ties to their cousins the Longbeards, the Dourhands are the same dwarves that Skorgrím led against Edhelion centuries ago. We cannot expect them to be proper stewards of the land.

'Go into the wilds and cull the wolf population. You can find them on and near the frozen lake down the road from here. You can also find them within the confines of the Berghold, once a holding of the dwarves now lost to these wolves.' 

'Collect the pelts from these creatures and see them delivered to Nos Grimsong, a Longbeard, within Frerin's Court. Nos, a friend of the thrushes, will understand the meaning behind the furs.''Ho there, ${RACE}. What brings you to Frerin's court? Ah, grim business I see.

'I understand well that nature is thrown into imbalance here. Our cousins have not been the best caretakers of this land. I only hope that Lord Dwalin can talk some sense into them.

'The lesson will not go to waste on me. You have my thanks.'You arrived in Celondim with the sons of Elrond one month ago, and the time soon approaches to sail to Mithlond. However, Elrond's decision to offer you a choice of whether to leave Middle-earth or remain to aid its peoples has weighed heavily on you during your travels.Talk to Taerthon in CelondimTaerthon can be found near the docks in Celondim.

You have travelled to Celondim, and the time soon approaches to sail to the Uttermost West.Talk to Taerthon in CelondimYou arrived in Celondim with the sons of Elrond one month ago, and the time soon approaches to sail to Mithlond. However, Elrond's decision to offer you a choice of whether to leave Middle-earth or remain to aid its peoples has weighed heavily on you during your travels. The Elf, Taerthon, has also come to sail to the West and you have spent much time together in preparation for your journey since Elladan and Elrohir departed for Edhelion.

The time has at last come to journey to the West, and Taerthon awaits you at the docks of Celondim.'${PLAYER}, have you heard the good tidings? The time has come at last to sail to Mithlond and to blessed Valinor!

'We have waited only one month, but each day has seemed a year since we arrived in Celondim. The burdens of this realm shall soon be behind us, but I am curious if the sons of Elrond found what they sought in the ruins of Edhelion. I have heard many tales of the sorrowful history of that place, and how it came to ruin by the hand of a disreputable dwarf named Skorgrím Dourhand, but it has laid empty for many long years now. I wonder if anything remains to be found there....

'I have never walked upon the shores of the blessed realm, but I have heard many tales of Valinor. Your forebears who once dwelt there, did they not? Did you as well?'

Taerthon pauses a moment and studies your face.

'You look restless, my friend. What troubles you on such a joyous day?'You were greeted by an Elf-maiden as you paused for reflection in Celondim.Cardavor waits for you by the piers north of where Eilian keeps watch. 

Cardavor, an Elf of Celondim, needs assistance with some matter. Perhaps you should speak with him.'As I said, I am sorry to intrude upon your reflection, but perhaps you can speak with Cardavor in Celondim proper? He seems quite worried about something, and, from what I know of the tales of your bravery, you may be able to help him.

'You may find him by the piers, not far from here.''Did you speak to Cardavor yet? He seemed worried about something.''Ah, hello there. We have not had time to get acquainted. My name is Cardavor. And you are...?'You were greeted by the Dwarf Skógi as you arrived at Thorin's Hall.Unnarr is waiting for you in Frerin's Court.

Skógi told you to speak to Unnarr, the Captain of the Guard.'The Captain of the Guard, Unnarr, has been looking for you for an hour, at least. And, what's worse for you, he's already angry due to a guard having abandoned his post.

'You may find Unnarr in Frerin's Court. If I were you, I would tread carefully around him.''Unnarr will have my beard if you're late. Get a move on... you don't want to keep him waiting.''Good, you received my summons. At least someone knows their duty.

'Come now, we must speak of circumstances most dire and worrisome.'Many of the good folk of Archet perished during the assault by the Blackwold brigands.Dirk Mudbrick's body can be found at the sheep-farm, south-west of Archet. Nate Whisperwood's body can be found near the Hunter's Lodge, east of Archet. The body of Wil Wheatley can be found just to the west of Bronwe's Folly, south of Archet.

Jon Brackenbrook has asked that you bury the remains of the fallen who perished in the Blackwold raid with honour.Bury Dirk Mudbrick's bodyBury Nate Whisperwood's bodyBury Wil Wheatley's bodyJon Brackenbrook can be found by the ruins of The Mad Badger Inn in Archet.

You should return to Jon Brackenbrook and let him know that you have buried the fallen as he asked.'As we prepared for the Blackwold assault and stood at the defence of Archet, many of the good folks of this town perished, both within the gates and beyond.

'The battle was great, but in the end we defeated the brigands' onslaught. Some of the good men who were lost still remain unburied, but the people of this town have their hands full with their own great losses. It would be to our shame if those unburied bodies were dishonoured by wild beasts.

'I would ask this one last favour of you: seek out those poor men and grant them honour in death. Dirk Mudbrick fell at the sheep-farm south-west of Archet, Nate Whisperwood died defending the Hunter's Lodge, and Cal Sprigley's farmhand -- Wil Wheatley -- perished in battle just to the west of Bronwe's Folly.''Dirk never made it out of the sheep-farm, Nate died just outside of the Hunter's lodge, and Wil Wheatley died just to the west of Bronwe's Folly.''Good, it is done. Those men died bravely and deserved a proper burial. Once more, Archet is in your debt.

'Now, before you leave, there is something I must tell you. A queer man came through Archet while you were away, and I believe he was looking for you!After helping to restore Thorin's Hall, Dwalin has given you leave to embark on your own adventures.Nagli can be found near Frerin's Court before Thorin's Hall.

Dwalin has urged you to embark on your own adventures, but you should bid farewell to Nagli before you depart.Find Nagli near Frerin's CourtNagli can be found near Frerin's Court before Thorin's Hall.

Nagli appears to be packing for a journey of his own. You should talk to Nagli.Talk to Nagli'Oh, I almost forgot!

'Your Dourhand companion, Nagli, is planning to depart on a journey of his own. He is a curious, helpful dwarf... unlike many of the Dourhands I have known. I must admit, I will miss seeing him in Frerin's Court.

'Go on, ${PLAYER}. Best try to catch him before he sets off!''${PLAYER}!

'It is good to see you, my friend. I have enjoyed our time together in Ered Luin, but this is not my home.

'You of all dwarves must understand! What has become of your people? Did Lethi and Vaski win their freedom? What of the others? I know it must seem a terrible thought, but I am sure one day you shall return to Mordor. But, it will be to see that your people at last live free from the Enemy!

'I know not where my journey shall take me, but I hope our paths cross again. After all, you shall have even more tales to tell then... and I think I shall have a few of my own!

'Farewell, ${PLAYER}.'As the time approaches to sail to Mithlond, the decision of whether to remain in Middle-earth or sail to the Uttermost West has led you to reflect on your past and consider the choices of your dearest friends and allies.You may reflect on your friends and kin near the docks of Celondim.

After realizing that you have become uneasy in your thoughts and might desire to remain in Middle-earth, Taerthon believes you would do well to reflect on the paths of your friends and kin.Taerthon can be found near the docks of Celondim.

You have reflected on the paths of your friends and kin, and have come to find a new sense of purpose in Middle-earth. You should return to Taerthon.Talk to Taerthon in CelondimElladan and Elrohir can be found to the west of the docks by the stable-master of Celondim.

Taerthon is sorrowful, but understands your choice to remain in Middle-earth. He has also mentioned that Elladan and Elrohir have suddenly arrived in Celondim and wish to speak with you. You should find them by the stable-master of Celondim.Find Elladan and Elrohir by the stable-master of CelondimElladan and Elrohir can be found by the stable-master of Celondim.

Elladan and Elrohir have come to Celondim seeking you, and you should now speak to them.Talk to Elladan in Celondim'What troubles you, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Taerthon that your thoughts are drawn to your friends and kin and to the choice that was given to you to remain in Middle-earth if you so wished it.

'Why would you wish to remain here? As you must know well, the lands of Valinor heal all ills and replenish our spirits. The concerns of this realm are ours no longer!'

Taerthon senses that his words have not convinced you.

'I see. Perhaps you should reflect upon the paths of your friends and kin to guide you?'You reflect upon the path of Gil-galad, the High King of the Elves, and the bravery he possessed as he fought and fell to purge the evils of Morgoth and Sauron from the world. You consider the loss of the Elf, Thelaron, and the pain of losing a companion during the War as Gil-galad did. You consider the loss of your King, and you understand what his sacrifice meant for the peoples of Middle-earth.You reflect upon the path of Glorfindel, who has remained in Middle-earth to stand against the evils of the Enemy and the most vile of his servants. You think upon Glorfindel's deeds in the Elder Days of the world, and how his resolve has never faltered, even when confronted with death.You reflect upon the decisions of Círdan the Shipwright, who remained in Middle-earth that others of his kin might depart for the blessed realm of Valinor.You reflect upon the decisions of your dearest friend, Harthalín, who has chosen to remain in Middle-earth and free it of evils before the time comes to bequeath it to Men. You consider her duty to the people of this realm, and that she hoped that you might remain to aid her in the battles against the evils of the world.You reflect on the decisions of Elrond Halfelven, once the herald of Gil-galad, whose time in this realm draws near to its ending. You consider Elrond's words, for he said that you might depart for the Uttermost West if you truly wished it and that the burdens of this realm no longer fall to you. You consider that he kept you in Imladris that the choice might one day be yours, rather than sending you to revive in the Uttermost West.'${PLAYER}, I am glad you have not yet departed! I feared we would be too late....'

Elladan and Elrohir's faces betray grave concerns.

'There are grim forces at work in the Ered Luin, my friend. The terrible Gaunt-lord, Ivar, has bound vile spirits to the corpse of the Dwarf-lord, Skorgrím, and now the Dourhands think him their leader reborn! Our foes escaped, and now the Enemy has beguiled the Dourhands into his service!'

Elrohir gives Elladan a knowing look.

'Indeed, the truth must be told, brother. When Father spoke of the shadow in the East, he spoke of Sauron. The Dark Lord has returned once again, ${PLAYER}, and he has sent his servants across the many lands of this realm in search of the Ring that was lost. If Sauron recovers the Ring, no host shall remain to stand against him and all will be lost.

'The time shall soon come when the peoples of this realm are called to war, and I beg that you remain to guide them and make safe the lands of Middle-earth. It is a great demand to make of one as storied as you and at so final an hour, but I would not ask it if the need were not dire. It shall soon be a realm of Men, and it must survive for them to inherit it! I beg you -- will you remain?'

You tell Elladan and Elrohir that you have chosen to remain in the realm of Middle-earth.

'To have you among the forces that stand against Sauron brings me new hope for the days that lie ahead. You should aid the folk in Celondim and the lands beyond to hone your skills for the battles that surely await us. When the time comes, we shall call on you to join us!''Ah, so you return. Where has your reflection led you, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Taerthon that you desire to remain in Middle-earth.

'It is not the choice I make, but I understand it, friend. You have made it your purpose to confront the forces of evil wherever they take hold, and though it does not seem so here in Celondim, it is true that great evils are again at work in this realm.

'You see, while you were reflecting, Elladan and Elrohir arrived in Celondim and asked to speak with you at once. I told them I would deliver their request when you returned, and they now wait for you near the stable-master.

'My path leads to the Uttermost West, but it seems yours now leads elsewhere. May the Valar guide you, and may you one day come to find peace in Valinor!'You have done a great deal to help the horse settle into Combe, but it is time for him to prepare for the next step in his journey.Éogar may be found at Hengstacer Farm in the Horsefields of Bree, a bit north of Bree-town.Talk to Éogar in the Horsefields'The stable-master has put new horseshoes on the horse, but we need the stall back to house Combe's travel-mounts. It's time to take the new horse to Hengstacer Farm in the Horsefields of Bree.

'Last time Éogar was here, he left behind an old saddle, and I've been meaning to return it. This seemed like a good opportunity for taking care of two tasks at once, so I tried to fit the saddle on this horse, but he almost killed me in the process. It appears this horse is not ready to be ridden.

'Could you take this saddle to Éogar at the horse-farm and let him know the horse will be heading there soon?''Thank you for this saddle! I've been meaning to stop by Combe to pick it up.

'Did you not say the horse would be heading here soon? It looks like the horse from Combe arrived just as you did. I do not think the Stable-master of Combe would have let the horse go free on its own, and I suspect it followed you here. This horse might be connected to you more than you know....'Any herd of horses will establish a pecking order, and the strongest amongst them leads the others. It is time to meet the master stallion of the Horsefields to prove yourself to him.The master stallion is perched upon a hill overlooking the horse-farm.  He has a good vantage point from which to keep an eye on his herd.

