You should talk to Gytha Beatbrush in Gamwich.Talk to Gytha Beatbrush in Gamwich'You there! Do you think you have what it takes to become the best hunter of game in all of Yondershire? Bold words, bold words! Well, I'll be the judge of whether you're worthy of boasting or not.

'How's about I put you to the test? Gamwich can always use more food in the stores for winter. Hunt each of the creatures to be found throughout Yondershire and return to me, and perhaps I will make you an honourary hunter of Gamwich.''You have returned from the hunt! As I am a hobbit of my word, I say you are now an honourary hunter of Gamwich. However...'

Gytha peers at you slyly.

'You are NOT the best hunter of game in Yondershire, for that title belongs to me! If you wish to become my equal, you will have to track down the most elusive and dangerous creatures in this land! Do this, and perhaps we will stand on equal footing: the finest game hunters in Yondershire. I will at least be impressed.''Your hunt of those elusive and fearsome creatures was successful? I am impressed! I did not really expect you to succeed, but I am pleased that you have. Perhaps we are equals in the hunt after all, and even better: the villages of Yondershire are made safer by your efforts, which is the most important thing. Well done!'Morna "Old Mother" Goldworthy could use some help around the garden.Help Morna "Old Mother" Goldworthy tend to the garden.Defeat garden pests (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Check on beans (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pick delicious berries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Morna "Old Mother" Goldworthy can be found in the heights of Tighfield in the southern part of town.

Talk to Morna.'You! I'm old and tired, so come help me with this farm work. My family and I usually do the chores together, but they're all busy what with all the tumult to the south. Why am I telling you this? You wouldn't care.'

Before you can tell her whether you do or do not care about her story, she pushes on.

'First pick some berries. The juiciest ones are low to the ground and it's hard for me to reach them. Do I look like I'm still a tween? Don't answer that.

'Second, inspect the beans for any sign of blight or disease. If you spot any discolouration on the underside of the leaves, it's no good!

'Lastly, and most importantly, cull some of the pests in the field. Are they dangerous? Pah! Not even a little, but they're nuisances. I swat at least a dozen a day! They're gross and squishy though and I'm tired of cleaning the gunk off my favourite shovel. You do it.''Oh it's so nice to have help with the chores again, even if it's only for a day.

