Something happened at Herne that has upset the townspeople. Perhaps answers await inside Crossway House, the inn at the cross-road.Enter Crossway House, the tavern of Herne, in search of answersMaddoc, the innkeeper of Crossway House, stands behind the counter.Talk to Maddoc inside Crossway HouseBoromir stands within Crossway House.Talk to Boromir inside Crossway HouseMaddoc, the innkeeper of Crossway House, stands behind the counter.Talk to Maddoc inside Crossway HouseMeneldir stands inside Crossway House.Talk to Meneldir inside Crossway HouseAberthol lives in the northern part of Herne.

Maddoc told you something happened to Aberthol four nights ago, and the old man may speak to you about it, if you loosen his lips with a mug of his favourite spiced beer.Talk to Aberthol inside his home in the northern part of HerneA mug of spiced beer, set on his table, may encourage Aberthol to tell you what happened to him.Set the mug of spiced beer on Aberthol's tableAberthol is inside his home, considering you, your companions, and the mug of spiced beer you brought him.Talk to Aberthol inside his homeAberthol waits for you to introduce him to your companions.Introduce Boromir to AbertholIntroduce Egfrith to AbertholAberthol, apparently awestruck by his visitors, is now willing to tell you his story.Talk to Aberthol inside his home'Our first destination will be the town of Herne, a village of farmers and traders that grew up at a crossing of the ways to the north-west of here, where the Greenway coming south meets with the road coming north out of Enedwaith. When last I came this way, the tavern at Herne was a pleasant enough place, although it could not truly compete with the Prancing Pony for entertainment and company. Crossway House it was called, a two-story building near the south gate.

'Let us be off.''Our first destination will be the town of Herne, a village of farmers and traders that grew up at a crossing of the ways to the north-west of here, where the Greenway coming south meets with the road coming north out of Enedwaith.''These tavern names are so quaint. Crossway House? Prancing Pony? None of them will compare to the Mûmak and Keep of my city.''To Herne, then! Am I saying that correctly?''I don't want any trouble! Can you just leave me alone?''You had better leave, stranger. This is a bad time to pass through Herne! Trust me when I tell you this!''I don't want any trouble! Can you just leave me alone?''There is not much cheer to be found here, it seems.''An ill feeling has settled upon the whole town. It was not like this when last I came this way.''Are all taverns in the north so... quiet?''You and your friends might want to keep walking, stranger. Herne is not the place it once was, and you will not find welcome here.'

You ask the innkeeper what happened to the town, and he frowns, shaking his head.

'Four nights ago, something... evil... passed through the centre of Herne. No one knows exactly what it might have been, but every night since a terrible fear has seized the town. Old man Aberthol says he saw something that first night, but... I don't know. He was an adventurer in his younger days, but he has such a reputation for tale-spinning that it has always been hard to tell the truths from the falsehoods in his stories.

'But something happened that night, and the town has not been the same ever since. You had best be moving on, you and your friends, before the dark comes again.''Four nights ago, something... evil... passed through the centre of Herne. No one knows exactly what it might have been, but every night since a terrible fear has seized the town. Old man Aberthol says he saw something that first night, but... I don't know. He was an adventurer in his younger days, but he has such a reputation for tale-spinning that it has always been hard to tell the truths from the falsehoods in his stories.

'But something happened that night, and the town has not been the same ever since. You had best be moving on, you and your friends, before the dark comes again.''If this man speaks true, it sounds as if some evil came to Herne... but what could it have been?''Some evil passed through Herne, but the ill feeling remained behind? What does it mean?''I have seen something like this before, ${PLAYER}. Well, I say "seen," but I did not see it; say instead that I felt it, something dark and evil; it filled man and horse with dread. We stood in defence of the river at Osgiliath when this presence came upon us, and even the boldest among us were seized by a terrible fear. We cast down the bridge behind us, and only a few of us escaped.

'I have never felt a fear like that before, ${PLAYER}. Some of my men claimed they saw the figure from which the terror spread... but I do not know if I believe it. Might that evil have come north from Gondor, somehow? I did feel as if I were being watched at times as I rode through the empty lands... or were they not as empty as they appeared?

'I should speak with the old man the innkeeper mentioned. If his story matches those of my soldiers... that will tell me something of the evil that came to Herne.''I should speak with the old man the innkeeper mentioned. If his story matches those of my soldiers... that will tell me something of the evil that came to Herne.''If this man speaks true, it sounds as if some evil came to Herne... but what could it have been? And is it the same presence Boromir describes?''Some evil passed through Herne, but the ill feeling remained behind? What does it mean?''I hope you won't use old Aberthol too harshly. He is something of a peculiar fellow, but despite his strangeness most of Herne's folk are quite fond of him... and he has always brought his custom to Crossway House, which I appreciate.

'Let me give you a mug of his favourite spiced beer. It may loosen his tongue. To be truthful, that is not usually a problem for our Aberthol, but he might not want to speak about whatever happened to him that night. The beer may make him more willing to talk with you.''Some evil came to Herne. Is it the same as that encountered by Boromir in Gondor?''I should speak with this old man and learn what he encountered.''I hope Aberthol will speak with you, and perhaps you can ease his mind.''You go with Boromir and Egfrith to speak with this man, ${PLAYER}. I will ask around Herne to see what else I can learn about what happened here.''You go with Boromir and Egfrith to speak with this man, ${PLAYER}. I will ask around Herne to see what else I can learn about what happened here.''I will go with you and Boromir to speak with this man.''I must speak with this man and learn what befell him.''Aberthol lives in the northern section of our town. Treat him kindly, please. We are very fond of him, despite his strangeness.''Why have you come to bother me? I speak to no one!

'Not since...''Maybe I had better know the names of your companions.''I speak to no one!''I have not had any visitors in quite some time. What brings you to my home? You... you want to hear about... about the crawling darkness?'

Aberthol shakes in his chair, and then takes a bracing gulp from the mug of beer.

'Maybe I had better know your names. Who are these companions you bring with you?''Boromir, you say? Surely you are not the same Boromir who is the son of the Steward of Gondor? Some twenty years ago I travelled in the south-lands, adventuring, you know, and I had the privilege of meeting the Steward. I remember his sons, both of lordly mien; you do bear a resemblance to the elder of the two! But you travel now in secret, it seems. I will say no more of it. A Guard of the Citadel, if not its Captain, in my own home! I can hardly believe it.

'And Egfrith, a proud woman of Rohan! In the days of my youth, I rode across the Riddermark at joyful gallop. Is Théoden still King of Rohan? It was a beautiful land, and he was stern but strong, loved by his people and respected by his foes. It must still be so.'

The set of Egfrith's jaw tightens, but she says nothing. Aberthol continues.

'I will tell you what happened to me. With such fine protectors in my home, what have I to fear?'The woods of Bree-land can be dark and mysterious. Some places are darker than others....Walt Whitrose mentioned that Gil Sandheaver had come from the north and east, where the lake meets the woodland.

Walt Whitrose believes it is worth looking into Gil Sandheaver's claims that trees were walking near Nen Harn, if for no other reason that Gil's peace of mind.Find Gil Sandheaver's walking treeWalt Whitrose is at his camp on the western edge of Nen Harn.

Your discovery should be explained to Walt Whitrose at his camp, if only to warn him that such things exist out here in the northernmost parts of Bree-land.Talk to Walt Whitrose in Nen HarnGil Sandheaver is at the Hengstacer Farm, west of Walt Whitrose's camp at Nen Harn and north of Bree-town.

Walt Whitrose told you to take news of your victory to Gil Sandheaver, with the reassurance that he may once again walk the woods in peace.Talk to Gil Sandheaver at the Hengstacer Farm'Old Gil was frightened and seemed out of sorts when he brought us the soup. We didn't pay him no mind though... we figured he was nervous from being lost and had his mind playing tricks.

'Still, he's not one given to exaggeration and if he is still on his wit's end about this, then maybe we should look into it. 

'Gil came to us from the east, where the lake meets the woodland. Search in that area for his mysterious walking tree. Maybe you will find something. If not, then Gil can have his peace of mind back at the least.''It was probably nothing, but no reason not to check. Have you found anything?''I knew it wasn't a trick of me eyes! There is something up there!''A troll of wood! Gil was right then. What queer things are happening here in Bree-land. Orcs moving through the day and night in the north, woodland animals spooked from their homes -- and now walking tree-people.

'I have to admit, had I seen such a thing I don't know that I would have been as well as Gil. Go and tell him to rest easy. Tell him that you dealt with the creature, and there is nothing more to fear.''I get chills every time I think how close I came to that thing. I'm sure glad it's gone. I'd hate to imagine what that thing could have done to me or one of my horses.

'No, I think I will take the comfort of the farm for a while before venturing back into the woods. If it is all the same to Mrs. Rosethorn, adventure like that is not truly my desire. I will leave that to folks like you.'Clotild refuses to leave her house until her bothersome neighbours allow her a moment's rest.Clotild's bothersome neighbours can be found in various places throughout Gamwich.

You should scold them for harassing her.You have properly scolded Clotild's neighbours.

You should let her know.Talk to ClotildClotild has asked you to let Lassa know she'll be arriving shortly.

You should return to Tighfield to pass on the good news.Talk to Lassa in Tighfield'Ever since I shared my family's special coney stew with my neighbours, they haven't stopped trying to hound me for the recipe. As much as I'd love to have tea at Lassa's house, I'm afraid to take a single step outside.

'Unless... unless you could tell my neighbours off for me? A few sharp words will make them less eager to harass me so!''Finally, some peace and quiet. Would you mind letting Lassa know that I'll be arriving shortly?''Thank goodness, our tea can continue as planned. But don't tell me what happened to Clotild -- I'm sure she'll want to retell that story herself!'The discovery of Orcs in Bree-land has concerned Andreg the Ranger.You have been instructed to search east along the gorge far to the north of the Bree-fields, beyond Hengstacer Farm, for an Orc-leader and his entourage.

Andreg fears that the Orcs have pushed too deeply into Bree-land, though only a small number of Orcs came through Trestlebridge. This concerns him greatly. He sent you to investigate and to strike a blow against the Orcs.Defeat GazbúrzDefeat Tarkrîp bow-masters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Tarkrîp bone-smashers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Andreg is at the Old Greenway Fort

You should return to Andreg and tell him about the Orc camp that you discovered along the gorge in the northern most reaches of Bree-land.Talk to Andreg at the Old Greenway Fort'I need you to head northwards, beyond Hengstacer Farm and through the lands where hunters go to make their living. If you encountered a small scouting group of Orcs just outside of Hengstacer Farm and even more encamped on the southern edge of the gorge, then I fear that a larger encampment may have found its way to the ruins in the east -- near the northern lakes of Bree-land.

'Go there and seek out the Orcs' leader and his minions. Defeat them, if you can, and return to me so that I know this deed is done. If you find an encampment there... then we know the North Downs have fallen.

'Please hurry.''We cannot afford to stay this investigation any longer. The Orcs have already proven more resourceful than we expected. If they organize quickly in the northern fields of Bree-land, then there is no telling how far they will travel southwards.

'Investigate the southern ridge of the gorge to the north.''It is done then. The Orcs must have overrun the valleys of the North Downs. The borders are breached.

'We must send a warning to my brethren far and wide. Your efforts are of great importance, ${PLAYER}.'Lassa's friend Clotild has not shown up for their weekly tea.You should find Clotild's house in Gamwich.

It is located across from the Brimful Quiver Inn.Find Clotild's house in GamwichAt first glance, Clotild doesn't seem to be home.

You should try knocking on her door.Knock on Clotild's doorYou should wait to see if Clotild will answer the door.Wait for Clotild to answer the doorIt's possible Clotild didn't hear you knocking.

You should try again.Knock on Clotild's doorYou should wait to see if Clotild will answer the door.Wait for Clotild to answer the doorWell... third time's the charm?Knock on Clotild's doorSomeone is home -- most likely Clotild.

You should try to talk to her through the door.Talk to Clotild'Ah, hello! Might I ask, are you headed to Gamwich? My friend Clotild hasn't shown up for our weekly tea, and I'm growing quite worried. She's never missed one before, not once!

'I'm afraid I can't step away now; I'm preparing our food so it'll be warm once she arrives. Please, make sure she's all right! Checking her home might be a good start; she lives just across from the inn there.''Oh! I'm sorry for being so rude -- I thought you were one of my horrible neighbours. I hear them knocking on my front door at all hours of the day! The nerve!'Oddvarr finally remembered that he requested aid prior to your arrival.Andreg is at the Old Greenway Fort, west of Hengstacer.

Oddvarr has asked you to tell the Ranger Andreg that he is grateful for the Rangers' assistance and that Orcs have come down from the eastern stretches of the North Downs as well.Talk to Andreg at the Greenway Fort'With your help and the help of those Rangers... the Rangers, you came to me because of them didn't you? Well I should thank them, but now that I have my notes and focus, I want to get to the task of investigating these ruins.

'Would you be willing to make your way west down the hill to the Old Greenway Fort to tell that Andreg fellow that I am grateful for his assistance?

'You may want to make mention of the Orcs skulking about in the north too.''Oddvarr? I cannot say that I recall the name or the dwarf. If you say that he was forgetful, then perhaps he meant to send you on to Saeradan.

'This other bit of news is disconcerting. We must investigate, I fear.'An unlikely collection has been requested by one of the workers at The Forsaken Inn. The request is for a large collection of blackened skin. This skin can be found on many of the beasts within the western sections of the Lone-lands.You accepted a task to acquire patches of blackened skin for Lieva Dourlilly, a worker at The Forsaken Inn in the Lone-lands. You can find the skin from the beasts in the area of the western Lone-lands.Wanted: Blackened skin for a personal project. Deliver the skin to Lieva Dourlilly at The Forsaken Inn. No coin involved, but the effort would be appreciated.Wanted: Blackened skin for a personal project.'These will do nicely. I wish that I had something to offer you, but until I earn the profits from the latest sales -- which could take a while -- I have nearly nothing.

'I thank you, heartily, friend.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gadaric Munce of the Eglain offers favour with his people if one can collect blackened sword sheaths from the goblins and Orcs of the western Lone-lands.You accepted a task to acquire blackened sword sheaths for Gadaric Munce. Orcs and goblins in the western stretches of the Lone-lands are in possession of these items. You can find Gadaric within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Blackened sword sheaths carried by the Orcs and goblins that overran the ruins where the Eglain made their way in the world. Deliver ten of these sheaths to Gadaric Munce to earn favour with the Eglain.Wanted: Blackened sword sheaths carried by the Orcs and goblins that overran the ruins where the Eglain made their way in the world.'For all the horror their wicked blades have caused, we will find solace in this collection. It proves that these enemies can be beaten back. 

'I thank you, heartily, friend.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gadaric Munce of the Eglain offers favour with his people if one can collect cracked beaks from the local birds.You accepted a task to acquire cracked beaks for Gadaric Munce. The crebain throughout the western Lone-lands can sometimes have beaks such as these. You can find Gadaric Munce within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Cracked beaks. Crebain are terrible beasts that herald the arrival of vile enemies. The Eglain wish to see that these creatures are brought low. If you collect their beaks and deliver them to me, Gadaric Munce, at The Forsaken Inn, you will earn favour with my people.Wanted: Cracked beaks. Crebain are terrible beasts that herald the arrival of vile enemies.'Vile creatures...it is good to know that so many are meeting a fitting end at the hands of those who will aid us. You have earned some favour with our people, and I will see that our elders learn of your deeds.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Bob Redthistle believes that the trees of the Old Forest in the southern Barrow-downs may yet be saved after a fashion.There are corrupted trees from the Old Forest in the southern Barrow-downs. Bob Redthistle is at the Dead Man's Perch.

Bob Redthistle has asked you to collect seeds from the corrupted trees in the Barrow-downs and return them to him.'The corrupted trees must be purged out of the southern Barrow-downs but they may yet return to the natural fold. To determine this, I will need their seeds.

'Collect their seeds and return them to me. If they can be saved, I will see that the seeds are brought to a place where they can grow into natural creatures once again.

'You will find the corrupted trees deep within the southern Barrow-downs where the walls of the Old Forest are too thick to pass beyond.''Yes, it appears that some of these seeds are not destroyed or marred by corruption.

'It is possible that they will grow strong and true and return to nature one day. I thank you for what you have done, ${PLAYER}.'Defeat goblins in the North Downs.Defeat goblins in the North DownsDefeat many goblins in the North Downs.Defeat goblins in the North DownsGraeme Tenderlarch asks you to visit him after finishing a task that plagued Bree.Graeme Tenderlarch is at the Bree Town Hall.

You received a post requesting that you meet with the Mayor of Bree, Graeme Tenderlarch.${PLAYER},

I ask, at your convenience, that you visit me in the Bree Town Hall. I wish to thank you personally for attending to an issue that has long plagued the folk of Bree. I hear that it was by your hand that the haunting of our town is ended, and I wish to see the hero of Bree for myself.

Graeme Tenderlarch, Mayor'I had thought the reports of the haunting were drunken ramblings. Imagine my surprise when I was approached by the gate Watchers at the South-gate. They told me it was you, ${PLAYER}, that quelled the spirit once wandering the alleys of Bree.

'For that I am in your debt, as is this town. Please, accept this as payment for your deeds, which until now had gone unnoticed.'Tom Bombadil has brought you to the barrow named Othrongroth. A servant of the Nazgûl, Andraste, said that the Lord of the Nazgûl would be found here.The entrance to Othrongroth is nearby to the west.

Tom Bombadil led you safely to the entrance of Othrongroth, the Great Barrow, in the Barrow-downs. Your search for the Witch-king will lead you into the barrow.Go to the entrance of the Great Barrow, OthrongrothThe Witch-king and the dwarf Skorgrím have entered Othrongroth. You should follow them into the barrow and search for them there.Follow the Witch-kingYour path through the Great Barrow led you to the Black Rider, who took notice of your presence and called the Dead to attack you. Defeat these creatures and continue on through Othrongroth!Pursue the Witch-kingYour pursuit of the Witch-king has led you to a room with a wight seated upon a great throne. The creature bid you welcome and said that if you sought an audience, you could approach.Speak to the Wight-lordThanks to the arrival of Tom Bombadil, the Wight-lord has been defeated! Tom waits to speak with you.'Here we are, my hearties! I've no time for wasting, saving foolish travellers! My Goldberry will be coming home, and I must set her lily bowls!

'The mouth of Othrongroth lies west. Be careful. Heed no deadly voices!''Well met! My Master sends thee greetings. I grant thee the boon of an audience before I kill thee. Thou seemed desirous of certain knowledge, so I will give it thee. For the Dead speak not!' The wight cackles.

'My Master seeks a great Power for the Dark Lord, but the Dark Lord has more designs than this. The Pale Dwarf shall go to the north and gather an army in the name of Angmar and the Witch-king!

'The gaunt one, a great power himself and to whom I owe this form, goes to the east. There, in Agamaur, he shall awaken a Power that lies sleeping in the waters. With her under his command, the skies will turn to blood and all shall despair!

'Now thine audience comes to an end. Thy death awaits thee.''Was it worth the trouble? Did you learn your lesson? Leave the Dead to sleep their dreamless sleep and walk yourself upon the green grass under the sun!'Guardsman Otley, in Trestlebridge, is awaiting delivery of coarse skin.You have been tasked with collecting coarse skin from the beasts near Trestlebridge.

You can deliver the items to Guardsman Otley at Trestlebridge.Wanted: Coarse skin from the beasts near Trestlebridge. Deliver the items to Guardsman Otley in the town of Trestlebridge.Wanted: Coarse skin from the beasts near Trestlebridge.'These skins will be put to good use - perhaps as shoes for those who have none. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Guardsman Otley, in Trestlebridge, is awaiting delivery of jagged beaks at the war-torn town of Trestlebridge.You have been tasked with collecting jagged beaks from the birds near Trestlebridge.

You can deliver the items to Guardsman Otley at Trestlebridge.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the birds near Trestlebridge. Deliver the items to Guardsman Otley in the town of Trestlebridge.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the birds near Trestlebridge.'We may not be able to recover their farms or homes but this may hearten some of the farmers. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Wandering Shade in the Barrow-downs asked you to deliver the ring you collected to its brother's tomb in Bree.The Wandering Shade's brother now wanders through the streets of Bree. If the sun is shining, the shade may be summoned by troubling its tomb; at night, search the alleys for the shade.

A tomb rests somewhere in Bree, and a shade leaves its confines at night to haunt the streets. The Wandering Shade wishes for you to bring his ring to his brother.'Duty done, again,
to rest at last
I yearn.

'So too my brother,
wandering by night.
A spirit lost
brings naught but fright.

'Provide this ring,
show him it is done.

'Then we rest
'neath Moon and Sun.''Our duty done, brother,
hear the call of slumber.
Know at last peace,
rest, interred again eternal.''Shining and gleaming,
I hath seen this before
on hand of my kin
as death pulled him forth.

'In the stone alcove,
where life ebbed away,
we had hid treasure
and summoned forth death.

'Now shall I rest,
as my brother the same.
At last at peace,
at last to dream.'After some thought, Alwena believes the recent rise of ruffians in Ruddymore is to blame for her lack of custom in the Hall of Crafts.Ruffians can be found throughout Ruddymore, and are a danger to the people of the area.Defeat ruffians in Ruddymore (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Alwena is outside the Hall of Crafts in Herne.Talk to Alwena outside the Hall of Crafts in HerneThe new guard hired by Alwena can be found inside the Hall of Crafts.Talk to the guard of recent employ in the Hall of CraftsAlwena is outside the Hall of Crafts in Herne.Talk to Alwena outside the Hall of Crafts in Herne'I have done some thinking, and I believe the recent increase in ruffian activity near Herne is partly to blame for the townsfolk being reluctant to work in the Hall of Crafts.

'Can you do something about the ruffians in Ruddymore? With fewer of those thieves and cutpurses nearby, folk may feel more comfortable about frequenting my place of business!''Can you do something about the ruffians in Ruddymore? With fewer of those thieves and cutpurses nearby, folk may feel more comfortable about frequenting my place of business!''Thank you for helping clear some of the ruffians out of Ruddymore. Meanwhile, I finally managed to hire a guard to stand watch in the Hall of Crafts! He will ensure that folk feel safe while they are working.

'You can go say hello to him, if you like. He's just inside the Hall.''I finally managed to hire a guard to stand watch in the Hall of Crafts! He will ensure that folk feel safe while they are working.

'You can go say hello to him, if you like. He's just inside the Hall.'The new guard's surprise is evident, but then he adopts a more neutral expression.

'Hello, stranger. Cause no trouble. I wouldn't want to have to throw you out. Missus Alwena was very clear that this is to be a safe place for craftsmen to work, and she doesn't want fighting.''Oh, we've never met before, I assure you. I am a respectable fellow, of course I am.''Thank you for all your help! My new guard will keep watch over the Hall of Crafts, and hopefully this malaise that has settled upon Herne will soon pass!'

You tell Alwena that her new guard does bear a certain resemblance to someone you recently faced in battle, and she shrugs.

'I am confident it will turn out fine. He has turned over a new leaf. And if young Edhan happens to stop by and finds he is jealous of my new guardsman, that is only a secondary consideration, ${PLAYER}.

'Thank you once again. If you wish to work your trade in the Hall of Crafts, you are welcome to do so at any time.'Emma Rosethorn has asked for your assistance hunting snapper-turtles for their shells and meat for her famous snapper soup.Snapper-turtles can be found on the islands and along the shore of Nen Harn, to the east of the Hengstacer Farm. Emma Rosethorn is at the Hengstacer Farm, west of Nen Harn.

Emma Rosethorn asked you to bring her some snapper shells and meat for her soup.'Hello there! Are you here to help out Éogar? If you're looking for work to do around the farm, I could use your help. I need some assistance tracking down some of the ingredients for my famous snapper-soup.

'My soup is made with the meat of the snapper-turtle that lives in the lake to the east of Hengstacer. I usually send one of the hands to fetch me the meat, but they have been so busy, I would feel awful making them hunt down their dinner however slow it may be. I could also use  more snapper-shells to make soup-bowls. Better not to waste anything, I always say.

'Hurry along now and fetch the snapper-shells and meat... the farmhands get awful hungry after a hard day of tending the horses, and I would hate to make them wait.''Ah! You were able to find the shells and the meat. Wonderful! I will get started on my soup right away.'Andreg is concerned about the Orcs which have breached the borders of Bree-land from the North Downs.Saeradan is at his cabin, south of the Old Greenway Fort.

Andreg told you to speak with Saeradan regarding the spread of the Orcs from the North Downs.Talk to Saeradan at his cabin'Saeradan must know that though our efforts here are successful, we cannot know the breadth of the invasion southwards. He must also know that our brethren to the north have been unable to prevent the spread of the Orc-tribes.

'Make your way south along the Greenway to his cabin and bring him these tidings.''Where we succeed in stopping their spread here, we fail in the north. This is dire news. Worse yet is the news that we likely did not prevent all of the Orcs from moving through Bree-land.

'I fear for the lives of the Rangers watching the passes and roadways throughout the southern reaches. I will continue to hold my post, but I must ask you for more assistance.

'For all your efforts thus far, however, I offer you thanks and this small token of our appreciation.''Quickly now! Saeradan must be told of what we have learned. This is of utmost importance.'After encountering the elusive goblin, Skrizug, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Gaerion can be found at Sírlond in Cardolan.

You have acquired a trophy from an elusive foe in Cardolan. You should deliver it to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Deliver Skrizug's dagger to Gaerion in CardolanAfter encountering and defeating the elusive goblin, Skrizug, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.

As you inspect Skrizug's dagger, you notice what appears to be a crude, white hand carved into its hilt. It seems even the goblins of Cardolan have begun to fall under the sway of the White Hand.

You should bring this trophy to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.'Greetings! Have you found something of note?'

Gaerion inspects Skrizug's dagger, pausing when he sees the insignia of the White Hand.

'This bodes ill for Cardolan, friend. If the White Hand seeks for allies among the goblins and Orcs of the North, it will not need to try hard to find them. It seems to me the best course is to destroy these objects or keep them hidden from sight—or perhaps I am wrong, and the best course is to bear them in sight of the goblins and confuse their loyalties.

'Nay, I shall have to think upon this lest I play into the hands of the Enemy. Thank you, ${PLAYER}!'Corrupted trees in the southern Barrow-downs, broken free of the bounds of the Old Forest, have fallen to corruption.Corrupted trees may be found deep within the southern Barrow-downs, at the eaves of the Old Forest.

Bob Redthistle asked you to deal with the threat of the corrupted trees found in the southern Barrow-downs, where the barrows meet the Old Forest.Defeat corrupted trees in the southern Barrow-downs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bob Redthistle can be found encamped at the Dead Man's Perch.

Bob Redthistle awaits your return and information on the trees he sent you to destroy in the southern Barrow-downs.Talk to Bob Redthistle at the Dead Man's Perch'Long have I lived within the bounds of Bree-land, and my family has long exchanged tales of the people that lived in these lands. We spoke of their relationship with the Old Forest and how it was by their hand that the Forest began its slide to ruin.

'It is for these reasons that I find sorrow in these lands and not fear. Deep to the south, where the eaves of the wood are too strong to penetrate, you will find moving trees who have long been separated from their brethren.

'These once-noble trees are ruined and rotted from within, and I must ask that you destroy them. It is for the good of all that we end them before they become something even more vile than they already are.''Corruption eats away at the roots of the once noble things that watched the Old Forest. It pains me to know that they must meet an end. I cannot abide them to fester... they must be destroyed. Head to the south and see that they are destroyed.''There is no joy in the destruction of things natural. However, when the natural order is corrupted, it becomes a necessity to stop the corruption from spreading.

'Thank you for all that you have done.'Pengail says that he meant to trade with the goblins, meeting his obligations to Thomas Thistlewool in Bree-town. The goblins double-crossed him and took him captive and now plan on trading him to the Orcs encamped in the Midgewater Pass.The Forsaken Inn is south-west of the goblin encampment.

Pengail has asked you to help him escape the goblins and take him to The Forsaken Inn.Speak with PengailAnlaf the Forlorn is at The Forsaken Inn.

After you helped him escape and find his father's sword, Pengail told you that Anlaf the Forlorn has the valuables that he left in his room. He told you to let Anlaf know that he is free and that he offered you a reward from his belongings.'Oi! Lend a man a hand, won't you? These cursed goblins captured me after I made honest effort to trade. Oh, I know it's wrong, but a man's got needs, and one of those needs is eating, friend.

'I ain't had myself a square meal since heading north with those fellows what moved on the Bree-fields, and I'm not like them. I just want to get by and earn enough coin to purchase a patch of land to call my own.

'If you don't help me, I'll be lucky to last a night. These goblins keep saying that they want to trade me to Orcs at Midgewater Pass. If you take me south-west to The Forsaken Inn, I'll give you some coin and a pick from the treasures I found in the ruins. We have a deal?''He was captured by the goblins. Ha! Fools running off to make deals with goblins deserve what they get! You rescued him though, and that makes you a better man than I, I guess.

'I did empty his room when he didn't come back and that note appeared. I took what was owed me but you can have the rest.

'Such a good person as yourself ought to be given something for helping a fool like him.'You discovered a pendant carried by a goblin and recognized immediately that the object could not possibly be of goblin-make.The pendant is far too well-crafted to belong to a goblin. You should ask folk at The Forsaken Inn or the Ranger Candaith at his camp in the western shadow of Weathertop if they know to whom the pendant may belong.The pendant sparkles in the sun. This is no goblin-made item; the craft is too symmetrical and clean. Someone has recently lost this item.

Perhaps Candaith or someone at The Forsaken Inn knows something of this object.'I've seen that before... it belongs to Pengail. It looks like he got himself caught.''This looks like the handiwork of the Men of Bree. It is possible that the goblins have taken a trader out of Bree hostage. Perhaps you could search the valley to the south of my encampment.''Those goblins took that from me even after we had agreed on payment. I have learned my lesson dealing with these creatures. Can you help me?'Ancient dead have arisen within the Great Barrow.Barrow-wight Bow-lords can be found within the Flooded Wing of the Great Barrow.Defeat Barrow-wight bow-lords (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Arinora has become overwhelmed with the many responsibilities she bears for the maintenance of The Forsaken Inn.Wolves can be found in the ruins of Minas Eriol south of The Forsaken Inn, among the Weather Hills to the north-east, and in the Midgewater Pass to the north.

Arinora of The Forsaken Inn asked you to collect wolf-hides so she can make blankets for the inn's guests.'There are too many travellers, even through this remote land, and the nearest water sits foul and stagnant. We've the well, of course, but water is rationed among all the guests and does not go as far as it might.

'Blankets need making and mending, and there are no better hides to be found than those of the wolves. The hide of a Warg is too coarse, but a wolf's fur can be made soft with a comb. If you've the time and a good heart, I would ask for your assistance; you'll not see me stomping through the brush to get to the wolves, as deadly as they are!

'There are a few places you may want to look for wolves with proper hides. Wolves creep among the ruins of Minas Eriol to the south, though that place is also crawling with Wargs and goblins. Wolves hunt too among the Weather Hills, to the east and north. Finally, you could travel even further north, but the Midgewater Pass is dangerous and full of even worse things than wolves. When you have the wolf-hides, return to me, and I'll see that you are rewarded.''These are fine furs indeed! I'll cure these and sew a nice batch of new blankets for the guests.

'I'll thank you for your time with a bit of coin from my purse.'Candaith, the Ranger who watches the Great East Road as it passes through the Lone-lands, has learned of an Orkish presence in the Weather Hills.White Hand Orcs infest the Weather Hills, west of Candaith's camp.

The Ranger Candaith offered you assistance in finding Radagast the Brown in exchange for aid against the Orcs in the Weather Hills.Defeat melee White Hand Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat ranged White Hand Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Candaith is at his camp near the foot of Weathertop, among the Weather Hills.

While you were fighting the Orcs, Candaith began his search for Radagast the Brown.Talk to Candaith at his camp'Saeradan's word comes late to the Lone-lands. If Radagast the Brown passed into these lands then perhaps hope is not lost in this place. I have returned only recently from a journey in lands far to the east, lands held firmly in the grip of evil. When I returned from that journey, I learned to my dismay that evil does not stir in the East alone.

'Goblins now roam to the south, while their larger and more ferocious cousins among the Orcs spread through the Weather Hills like a festering plague.

'I must find the main body of the Orcs. If you will assist me in driving back the Enemy in these hills, I will begin the search for Radagast the Brown. What say you?''Search for Orcs among the Weather Hills, west of my camp, and thin their ranks. I will begin my efforts to find and track Radagast the Brown.''I thank you, ${PLAYER}, but my thanks are tempered with concern; I have never seen a force of Orcs like this in Eriador before today: organized and well-provisioned. They hold an encampment in an outlet within the Midgewater Pass and bear a strange charge on their banners and shields... one that I have not seen before.

'Their numbers at this camp are great, but the day may come when we can drive them howling from the Lone-lands. Not today, I fear, but perhaps not so far off. There is another matter that demands our attention, an urgent matter; we will see to the destruction of their camp in the Midgewater Pass later.'In his search for signs of Radagast, Candaith tracked a messenger from the Orc-encampment in the Midgewater Pass, but was unable to learn what message the Orc carried before his hunt was interrupted by winged crebain.The Orc-messenger is surely at one of the enemy camps in Glumhallow, west of Candaith's own encampment at the foot of Weathertop.

An Orc-messenger made his way from the Midgewater Pass to the outlying Orc-camps located throughout the Weather Hills. Candaith asked you to intercept the Orc-messenger and return with the orders he must bear.'As I began my search for Radagast, near the Midgewater Pass, I witnessed an Orc-messenger depart in great haste. Quickly, I followed, trailing him eastwards along the shoulder of the hills and then south, but my search was interrupted. I could follow no further, for foul crebain circled above, and to be discovered would do greater injury to our work here than I can permit.

'Once again, I must call upon your aid. The messenger likely carries with him orders to the outlying camps. If you can intercept the messenger, we may learn the nature of these orders. So armed, we will be better equipped to deal with the Orc-threat.

'Search among the Orc-camps in Glumhallow, to the west, and return to me with the orders this messenger surely possesses. I will seek sign of Radagast's passage elsewhere.''These orders are written in the Black Speech, ${PLAYER}, a tongue I have never desired to learn. I should have been prepared for this, but I hoped that we would learn something useful immediately. While you were seeking these orders, I followed signs of Radagast through the hills.

'I was not alone in this pursuit as a patrol of Orcs soon happened upon my location! I was forced to break off my pursuit, and I lost my bow as I evaded the Orcs beneath the eaves of the wood. It was a close thing, ${PLAYER}. A close encounter that yielded insight into the Orc's leadership.

'You did well, for your part, and soon we will understand the intentions of these Orcs as well as their leadership.'Candaith has learned of the existence of a War-master among the Orcs named Uzorr. His defeat would be a powerful blow against them.War-master Uzorr can be found in Bleakrift, north-west of Candaith's camp at the foot of Weathertop.

Candaith asked you to find and slay War-master Uzorr and search the camp for a letter containing his orders.Defeat War-master UzorrAcquire War-master Uzorr's ordersCandaith is at his camp at the foot of Weathertop, among the Weather Hills.

You were victorious over War-master Uzorr and have obtained a letter that surely contains his orders from whatever power commands the Orcs that have ventured into the Lone-lands.'I would have easily evaded the Orcs I pursued, ${PLAYER}, were it not for the War-master that travelled with them. "Uzorr," they called him and gave to him what respect that their kind reserve for their strongest and fiercest warriors.

'It is likely that Uzorr returned to Bleakrift within the Midgewater Pass, for that seemed to be his domain. He must be slain, and whatever orders were given him recovered.

'Bleakrift is north-west of here, on the north-eastern edge of the Midgewater Pass, surrounded by a shallow body of water. Defeat War-master Uzorr and look for a letter of some kind near his person. Return to me victorious and we will discuss further what must be done. Bleakrift is likely to be a dangerous place, ${PLAYER}, be careful. I will continue my search for Radagast.''Find War-master Uzorr and slay him. We need to weaken their position, and without Uzorr commanding Bleakrift they will be helpless. Search also for a letter containing his orders.

'Bleakrift is north-west of here, at the north-eastern end of Midgewater Pass.''Another letter written in the Black Speech, ${PLAYER}, and its meaning evades me as did that of its fellow. This one, however, bears the mark of the White Hand.

'I must know what message this letter bears, ${PLAYER}.

'This is not a task for you, ${PLAYER}. I have finally discovered information on the whereabouts of Radagast, but there is something that you should see first.'As Candaith returned from his journey in the lands to the east, he saw flashes atop Weathertop of white light, akin to lightning, though there was no storm.Weathertop towers above Candaith's camp.

Candaith asked you to search Weathertop for anything out of the ordinary and to bring any news or evidence that you may find back to him.Search the summit of Weathertop for anything unusualCandaith's camp is at the foot of Weathertop.

You found a rune-covered rock and took a rubbing of the markings. You should return to Candaith and learn if he knows what the runes might mean.'As I was returning from the lands to the east, the night sky over Weathertop was lit by bright flashes of white light, akin to lightning, yet no clouds darkened the sky and of thunder there was no sound.

'I think that no ordinary storm visited Amon Sûl that night. I ask you to search the ruins atop Weathertop and see if there is anything to find, my friend.

'If you find anything out of the ordinary, return to me immediately.''Something went on atop Weathertop that night, ${PLAYER}, and I want to know what it might have been. Search the summit for anything out of the ordinary and return to me if you find anything.''Ah, you have found something? Let me see this rock-rubbing you have made.

'The runes are common, but I do not know what they signify. I am afraid this does not tell me much of the lightning, ${PLAYER}, but it tells me something of the source. Likely the same person responsible for sending you to me.'Aberthol told you of his encounter with the dark riders that passed through Herne, and of the fear that has remained upon the town ever since. Can anything be done to save Herne?Meneldir is inside Crossway House.

You should return to Meneldir and tell him what you learned from Aberthol.Talk to Meneldir inside Crossway HouseMeneldir suggested that you and your companions hunt the animals of Sedgemead for meat, collect firewood for the town braziers, and tell the folk of Herne to gather for the night behind the safe walls of Crossway House.Collect choice cuts of meat from animals in Sedgemead (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect firewood in Sedgemead (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to Bledri inside his homeTalk to Aldyth inside her homeTalk to Linnet by the fountain in the northern part of HerneBraziers can be found in front of Crossway House in the town of Herne, waiting for you and your companions to fill them with firewood.

Meneldir hopes to keep at bay the evils that threaten Herne by keeping the town braziers lit all night.Refill town braziers near Crossway House with firewood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Meneldir is inside Crossway House, waiting to hear about the town's preparations for the approaching night.Talk to Meneldir inside Crossway HouseMeneldir is inside Crossway House.

The preparations have been completed, and night approaches.Talk to Meneldir inside Crossway House and wait for night to fallMeneldir stands outside Crossway House after the events of the long night.Talk to Meneldir outside Crossway House'Four nights past, after the setting of the sun, I left my home to walk along the cobbled streets of Herne, as is my custom. This I have done for years, every night, ever since I gave up the adventuring life. You may think it odd, but I have found these moonlit strolls to keep something of my old wanderlust at bay. "Perhaps this is the night I keep a-walking, Aberthol, old fellow?" I've asked myself on occasion, but thus far I have always come back to my home and sleep comes quickly.

'But on that night, while strolling along the lane west of my home, smelling the night-flowers and listening to the sound of frogs down by the river-bank, I could sense that something was different. I can't tell you exactly what it might have been, but I was seized by a tremendous feeling of... I don't know... wrongness. I felt it in the pit of my stomach. There is a grassy place on the left between two buildings along that lane, and a single tree grows there. My eye was drawn to that tree, and as I watched I swear to you a deeper shadow moved out from behind the trunk.

'It made a strange sound as it moved, a whispering just beyond the edge of my hearing. It reminded me of being lost in distant woods, stalked by a dangerous creature I could not yet see, but this was no mountain-lion. I drew on all my reserves of courage, I tell you, and I turned to run, but even as my muscles tensed I knew it was too late. While I stood transfixed by the creeping shadow in front of me, a second dark figure had come up behind. This one towered above me, for it sat astride a great black steed, but I could not tell you how I had not heard the sound of its hooves on the cobbles as it approached.

'I must have fainted then, for the next thing I remember is being shaken awake by Bledri, one of the townspeople, carrying a lantern. He said he found me lying at the base of the tree between those two buildings on his way home, and with no sign of either dark figure anywhere about. But every night since, a terrible sensation of dread has fallen upon Herne as the sun disappears beneath the horizon.

'Do you know what they were, friends? Has anything of this sort been seen in Gondor or in Rohan? What business could draw such terrible creatures to Herne?''Do you know what they were, friends? Has anything of this sort been seen in Gondor or in Rohan? What business could draw such terrible creatures to Herne?''What did you learn from the old man, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Meneldir of Aberthol's frightening night-time encounter. He frowns and strokes his beard.

'This does align with the story Boromir told us about the power he sensed at Osgiliath, but that is leagues away... what has brought it here a-horse at such speed? I may know one name of these creatures, but I will not speak it here.'

He turns to the innkeeper.

'Maddoc, might I ask you to open your doors to all the townsfolk of Herne this evening? Some evil has left its mark upon the village, and I think it would be safest if the townsfolk sheltered behind the strong walls of Crossway House tonight.'

Meneldir turns back to you.

'As for us, ${PLAYER}, I think we should hunt some game in Sedgemead to resupply Maddoc's pantry, gather up firewood so we can keep the town braziers burning all night, and bring word to the townspeople that they should shelter within Crossway House tonight. What say you to this plan? I will not leave these people undefended... I cannot.''As for us, I think we should hunt some game in Sedgemead to resupply Maddoc's pantry, gather up firewood so we can keep the town braziers burning all night, and bring word to the townspeople that they should shelter within Crossway House tonight. What say you to this plan? I will not leave these people undefended... I cannot.''You want the townsfolk to shelter within Crossway House tonight?

'If I hadn't found old Aberthol lying on the ground the night this started happening, I might think it wasn't necessary, stranger. But I believe something evil had him in its clutches, and it only departed when I drew near with my lamp.

'I will tell my neighbours your plan.''You want the folk of Herne to shelter within Crossway House tonight?

'If I hadn't felt the terrible dread that settles upon Herne when the sun goes down, I might object, stranger. But I will do as you say, and let my neighbours know to do the same. Something is not right in Herne.''Shelter in Crossway House tonight? Preposterous! My family will do as we have always done, stranger, and stay in our own home. There's a bad feeling about Herne of late, it is true, but with my door barred and the candles snuffed, my family has nothing to fear.

'Others can do as they will, but we will not cower in the tavern all night. This is some scheme of Maddoc's to fill his coffers, you mark my words! And old Aberthol has always been mad, ask anyone.

'Now good day to you, and do not bother me again!''Perhaps if we keep the braziers in front of Crossway House lit all night, we will keep the evils at bay.''The braziers are refilled and Maddoc's pantry has been newly-filled? Good. As for whether the townsfolk will shelter within Crossway House tonight, let us hope more of them do than do not. It is for their own good, though they may not all believe it.

'Let me know when you are ready, ${PLAYER}, and we will wait for night to fall.''I hope we have done enough, my friend. This night may be a hard one.''It is as I feared, ${PLAYER}. The creatures that passed through Herne four nights ago were the wraiths once called Nazgûl, and they left something behind to draw the Dead hither.

'But why have these creatures of legend returned to life, and where did they go? And most importantly, and of greatest concern to me: for what purpose have they come so far north?'With your help and that of your companions, the townsfolk of Herne survived the dangerous night.Meneldir thinks you should clean up the evidence of the wight attack on Herne, to help ease the minds and fears of the townsfolk.Collect tarnished jewellery left by the wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect rusted blades left by the wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Meneldir thinks you should clean up the evidence of the wight attack on Herne, to help ease the minds and fears of the townsfolk.Meneldir stands in the town of Herne, turning the events of the night over and over in his mind.Boromir stands in Herne, disgusted by the dead that walk and the events of the previous night.Talk to Boromir outside Crossway House in HerneMeneldir is in Herne, thinking deeply about the wight attack of the previous night.Talk to Meneldir outside Crossway House in HerneMeneldir has not seen Egfrith since the wights attacked. Perhaps she returned to the inn?Find Egfrith inside Crossway HouseEgfrith is missing. Perhaps one of the townspeople knows where she has gone.Egfrith left a note for you and your companions. It lies face-down on Aberthol's table.Flip over the parchment Egfrith left with AbertholEgfrith wrote you a letter, entrusting it with Aberthol.Read the words of Egfrith's letterMeneldir is outside Crossway House in the town of Herne.Talk to Meneldir outside Crossway House in HerneEgfrith is likely to have hurried directly southward in order to avoid the difficult ford at Tharbad.

You and your companions should try to prevent Egfrith from escaping as she makes for Isengard and her master Saruman.Catch Egfrith south of Herne before she can escapeMeneldir stands by the dirt path leading south into Minhiriath.Talk to Meneldir on the edge of MinhiriathBoromir stands by the dirt path leading south into Minhiriath.Talk to Boromir on the edge of Minhiriath'We should examine the northern approach to the town, since that's the direction from which the Dead came. Let us clean up whatever detritus fell from the wights so as not to spark alarm in the townsfolk... though it may seem too late for that!''We should examine the northern approach to the town, since that's the direction from which the Dead came. Let us clean up whatever detritus fell from the wights so as not to spark alarm in the townsfolk... though it may seem too late for that!''We should examine the northern approach to the town, since that's the direction from which the Dead came. Let us clean up whatever detritus fell from the wights so as not to spark alarm in the townsfolk... though it may seem too late for that!''This equipment is very old, ${PLAYER}. Even in the light of day I find it hard to believe such things are possible! Ancient terrors stalk the land once again, and the Dead walk!

'While you were gone, I found a skull-shaped token buried in the dirt: a shallow grave, of sorts, just beneath the topsoil. I think it was left here on purpose to draw those creatures hither.''Even in the light of day I find it hard to believe such things are possible! Ancient terrors stalk the land once again, and the Dead walk!''I share Meneldir's amazement, ${PLAYER}, but I do not like it. Let the Dead sleep, and trouble not the living! I am filled with disgust that such things may be.

'But where has Egfrith gone? I thought she went with you, but she has not returned. Do you think she ran afoul of some among the wights we might have missed?''I am sure Egfrith is fine, but it is worrisome she has not yet returned.''Boromir is right. I have not seen Egfrith since we came outside. I thought she went with you to collect the fallen items of the wights, but you say you have not seen her? Perhaps she went back inside the inn?''Perhaps she went back inside Crossway House?''One of the townsfolk must have seen Egfrith somewhere. Herne is not that big a village.

'Try speaking to the villagers we spoke to before, ${PLAYER}. Someone will have seen something!''I told you we would be fine, stranger! There was no need to hide within Maddoc's tavern after all, and I plan on telling all my neighbours so after you have gone!'

You ask Linnet if she has seen Egfrith this morning.

'That's that woman of Rohan in your company? Aye, I saw her. She called upon old Aberthol shortly after I came outside. Mad, the lot of them!''The woman of Rohan? No, I haven't seen her since you all went outside.''The woman of Rohan? I have not seen her this morning.''Egfrith? She is the woman of Rohan with whom you came to Herne? No, I have not seen her this morning.

'Do you think the curse on our town has been lifted?''I hope I have not done wrong, ${PLAYER}. She told me not to give you the letter for a day or two, but if you already know to expect it I suppose there is no harm in you having it early.

'I did not read it! She told me not to do so, and her expression was so grim I took her at her word. I hope I have not done wrong, ${PLAYER}.''I hope I have not done wrong. She told me not to give you the letter for a day or two, but if you already know to expect it I suppose there is no harm in you having it early.

'I did not read it! She told me not to do so, and her expression was so grim I took her at her word. I hope I have not done wrong, ${PLAYER}.''I hope I have not done wrong.''My friends...

'No, I cannot address you so, for our friendship was based upon a deception. I am sorry. It causes me great pain to write this, but I am left no other choice. I could not forgive myself if I abandoned you without explanation. But leave you I must, for...'

The ink here is smudged.

'${PLAYER}, Meneldir of the North, and Boromir of Gondor... my errand to this land is not as I told you. It is true that I was born in Faldham of the Norcrofts, and I hold the good of Rohan above all else, but my mission was not to seek the Dúnedain. Théoden King is ill, that much is true, but there is no cure to be found for his ailment, neither in the Riddermark nor in the Dúnedain's wilderness. He has grown weak, and has himself become a grave threat to the survival of Rohan! His people need a strong King, but he languishes in his sick-bed! He has forgotten the strength he once possessed, and can no longer lead the people who cry out to him! In days past perhaps he would have ridden out in glory, to die in battle and stir the hearts of the Rohirrim, but no longer.

'It was with the Wizard Saruman that I consulted on this, for he has long been a friend to Rohan, and he confirmed that which I feared: it is a weakness of the blood that troubles Théoden, and it must run too in the blood of Théodred his heir. The Wizard has much wisdom, for he has lived to see many lifetimes, and he can see that which the rest of us fail to recognize: Rohan itself suffers from an illness. He will treat this illness with a difficult remedy. He has found numerous Orcs, likely from their mountain strongholds, and prepares to send them against the kingdom. Through the crucible of this testing, the mighty men of Rohan will prove who among them is strong enough to lead. I say "men," but perhaps it will be a queen who proves able to save Rohan from this peril? The old lineages are not enough.

'Even with all his wisdom, Saruman cannot see all. When Boromir of Gondor rode near to Isengard, the Wizard sought to learn what caused the son of Denethor to depart his own land. Was Saruman's plan to save Rohan in jeopardy? He charged me to follow the son of Gondor north and to learn the purpose behind his quest. I kept a day's journey behind him, out of sight in the empty lands, but always near enough to find his course, until the crossing of the Greyflood at Tharbad. That is where I encountered you, Meneldir, and ${PLAYER}.

'It was an act of desperation to join your band, but I feared Boromir would escape my reach and I would fail my own quest. And yet I did fail, for in the brief time we journeyed together, I came to understand what drove Boromir to this land, so far from his home. He travelled these many leagues at a dream's behest, for nothing so certain as the slimmest hope that it might aid Gondor. So too is it with me and Rohan. Each of us would do the same for our own home-land.

'Even now I try to excuse my treachery, as if I could avoid judgement by not writing it down. There was more to my errand: once I learned Boromir's purpose, I was to slay him. I could not have done it in battle, for all my weapon-skill, but one of these nights, while he slept, I might have managed it.

'But I will not. I go now to bring Saruman the knowledge he craved. He will learn something from Boromir's dream, and it will need to be enough. I thought I could slay anyone, if it meant saving my kingdom, but I was wrong.

'I will command Aberthol to give you this letter in a few days. I want you to know what I have done, and not done, but I will not risk you stopping me.

'For Rohan, always,

'Egfrith.''Are you all right, my friend? I hope I have not done wrong.''What happened, ${PLAYER}? Did you find Egfrith? Is she in some danger?'

You hand the letter to Meneldir, and as he reads it his expression darkens, a storm-cloud of anger enveloping his face.

'This cannot be. This cannot be!''She will not return to Tharbad, for the crossing will take too long. We must catch her along the southern road!''Hold!''We are too late.''I see the print of hooves in the dirt. She must have stolen a horse in Herne and has already escaped into Minhiriath. She is beyond our reach now.

'Saruman will learn what he sought.''I cannot believe it! We have been played for fools.''I did not see it, ${PLAYER}. I thought Egfrith was trustworthy. I did not see it.'

Boromir speaks quietly, but you can hear the strain in his voice.

'I know what I must do.'Radagast the Brown, in concert with the Rangers of the North, has journeyed east into the Lone-lands, seeking the source of a great evil.Saeradan is at his cabin north along the Greenway in Bree-land.

Saeradan the Ranger might know where you should meet up with Radagast the Brown.Talk to Saeradan at his cabin along the GreenwayCandaith is at his camp in the Lone-lands.

Saeradan does not know where exactly Radagast was bound when he left the Ranger's cabin. He suspects that Candaith, the Ranger who watches the Lone-lands, may have information on the Wizard's whereabouts.Talk to Candaith at his camp in the Lone-lands'You have done much good for the people of Bree-land, but the Shadow out of Angmar spreads far and wide. I have learned from Gwaihir the Windlord that another of my order has found corruption in the Lone-lands, east of Bree. It may even be related to the dangers you encountered in the Great Barrow.

'Unfortunately, Radagast is given to wandering and will be difficult to find. I would ask that you enlist the assistance of the Rangers in finding his location. There is one here in Bree-land, Saeradan, who will be able to aid you.

'Head out of Bree through the West-gate and turn north along the Greenway. Seek Saeradan at his cabin on the east side of the Greenway across from Thornley's Work Site.''Having enlisted the aid of Strider, the continued defence of Bree-land and its borders falls to Saeradan. I think he will be amenable to helping you, ${PLAYER}.

'Travel through the West-gate of Bree and follow the road north to where you see Thornely's Farm to the west. Leave the road and head to Saeradan's hidden cottage to the north-east of that farm.

'Speak with Saeradan and ask him for aid in this matter.''I must confess that I do not know where Radagast was bound when he left here, other than eastward into the Lone-lands. I do know that he was concerned with lightning which he saw upon the peak of Weathertop.

'One of my kinsmen, Candaith, has already journeyed to Weathertop to investigate... perhaps Radagast has joined him there.

'If you wish, my horse would bear you to Candaith's camp.''Take my horse. She will lead you through the northern stretches of Bree and through the Midgewater Pass to Candaith's camp.''Radagast the Brown? I have not seen him and know not of his passing, but there is a cold and a shadow that has come to these lands of late.'Some of the guests within The Forsaken Inn have developed a taste for Arinora's pork dishes.Boars can be found in the area surrounding The Forsaken Inn and north-east among the Weather Hills.

Arinora asked you to collect boar-feet and leg-bones for a dish she serves at The Forsaken Inn.'Old Mugwort does the cooking around here, though he has been in the wild for weeks, but I have some recipes of my own that tickle the fancy of some of the guests... so much so that they have bought out all of my latest fare! The recent commotion ran off our modest herd of swine, and I need more pork!

'I need boar-feet and leg-bones. Though I'll not share with you all of my recipe, I will tell you that the marrow from the bones gives the soaked feet a richer taste. Once you pull the fur away and cure the feet in salt, you've a tasty treat. 

'You should find boars in the surrounding area and north-east among the Weather Hills. Be careful, though... not every foot is good for eating.''Oh, these will do nicely. We can pull the marrow from the bones and wrap the peeled feet in marrow and salt. Add a dash of my secret spices, and we'll once again have several helpings of "Arinora's Boar Surprise!"

'You did very well! Take these coins as your payment.'Lily Sandheaver, a well-to-do hobbit of Bree, collects pottery gathered from old ruins in the Lone-lands. She is waiting for her latest delivery, but it has not arrived.Candac Brightwood is at the Forsaken Inn, east of the Midgewater Marshes along the Great East Road.

Candac Brightwood supplies Lily Sandheaver with pottery and other collectibles from the ruins of the kingdom of Arnor. Lily gave you a letter for Candac and sent you to The Forsaken Inn to deliver it and inquire about the delay.'Hello there. I am in need of assistance from one not too familiar with the upper class here in Bree-town. I collect pottery from the ruins of the kingdom of Arnor... well, to be honest, I purchase the pottery. It is a sign of my standing, and for years I have had a greater and higher quality collection than that dreadful Mirabella Underhill!

'The man from whom I purchase the pottery is named Candac Brightwood. I haven't heard from him for a dreadfully long time and am worried about my next delivery. And about Mr. Brightwood's well-being, of course. I need someone to carry a letter to Candac at the Forsaken Inn. Look for the inn along the Great East Road, just past the Midgewater Marshes.

'Mirabella will visit me soon, and if I have nothing new to show her, I will look a fool! You don't want that, do you?''Quickly, deliver that letter. I must have those new plates from Candac. I will not be the subject of mockery from the insufferable Mirabella Underhill!''You bring a letter from Mrs. Sandheaver? Of course you do. If only she understood what is happening here. Allow me to explain. I make coin trading with the Munce brothers -- you will see them about the inn. They find relics amongst the ruins in the Lone-lands and trade them to myself and others who visit this place for foodstuffs and supplies.

'For years, our trade went uninterrupted, but recent events have caused the trade to cease altogether, and there is nothing to sell to the collectors in Bree-town.

'The Munces cannot reach the ruins any longer. Their livelihood is threatened, as is ours. All Mrs. Sandheaver sees is that she will not have new Arnorian collectibles with which to impress her friends, while the Munces and their people may lose their lives.'The ancient Dead have arisen within the Great Barrow.Chilled Barrow-wights can be found within the Flooded Wing of the Great Barrow.Defeat Chilled Barrow-wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Candaith explained that the crebain circling the areas near Weathertop are not some innocuous bird, but that they are, in fact, creatures of ill-omen sent from afar to spy upon these lands. To ensure that they cannot return their information to their masters, they must be slain and their eyes plucked from their heads.Crebain can be found throughout the Weather Hills. Candaith's camp is at the foot of Weathertop in the Lone-lands.

Candaith directed you to seek out and destroy the crebain found throughout the immediate areas surrounding his camp. He believes that they are here at the behest of some unseen master and that they will report all they have seen.'These lands have become much darker in recent days. Since my time wandering in the eastern stretches of the Lone-lands a dark shadow has flocked down from the sky. Now they patrol the western lands as well as the east in search of something for their unseen masters.

'I speak of the crebain. Vile creatures with a gift of intelligence greater than they should possess. We must do what we can to thwart their efforts. Kill these vile birds and collect their eyes. It is the only way to ensure that they cannot return the information that they have gathered to their masters.

'You will find them all throughout the Weather Hills.''Though it seems an ignoble task, I tell you plainly that this was necessary. The crebain are spies for eyes from afar and they seek some grand prize here. With all that I have seen in these lands as of late, I fear that there is some great wheel turning, and I can only hope that our part can slow its progress.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}, for all you have done.There is much to do while travelling through the lands of the North Downs.Complete Explorer of the North Downs deedComplete Of Glories Long Past (Final) deedComplete Slayer of the North Downs deedAncient dead have arisen within the Great Barrow.Disturbed graves can be found deep within the Flooded Wing of the Great Barrow.Tend the open graves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hunters in the Midgewater Pass may know the Ranger Candaith... it is certainly worth a try to see if they know the Ranger who watched the Lone-lands.Candaith is at his camp on the western side of Weathertop.

The hunters do not know the name Candaith, but they have seen the makings of a camp at the western foot of Weathertop.Talk to Candaith at his camp'Candaith? The name doesn't seem familiar to me. I know this. We have hunted in the shadow of Weathertop and seen signs of a camp. It is possible that the man you seek camps there.

'The camp can be found at the western base of Weathertop.''Search the camp at the western base of Weathertop. Maybe the person you are looking for is there.''Hunters out of Bree knew of my whereabouts? I shall need to be cautious and redouble my efforts to hide my supplies in the future. 

'Now, what brings you to the foot of Weathertop? Surely you did not realize that there was a lone Ranger in the shadow of Amon Sûl who needed your aid?'Lieva Dourlily posted a desire for coarse fur, found on some beasts in the central Lone-lands. She desires to make cloaks from the furs.This task requires the acquisition of coarse furs which can be found on the heartier beasts found along the road east into the central Lone-lands. Lieva Dourlily wants the furs to make cloaks. Lieva can be found at The Forsaken Inn.Needed: Coarse fur from the heartier beasts near and within the central Lone-lands. The furs will be used to craft cloaks. If you have enough furs, see Lieva Dourlily.Needed: Coarse fur from the heartier beasts near and within the central Lone-lands.'A bit rough, still they will do the trick. Perhaps in time, I will earn enough to leave this forsaken place.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gadaric Munce of the Eglain offers favour with his people in exchange for the large filth collected from spiders in the western Lone-lands.You accepted a task to acquire large filth for Gadaric Munce. Spiders beneath the ruins in the western Lone-lands likely teem with the filth. You can find Gadaric within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: The Eglain desire proof that spiders making their home within and beneath ruins in the Lone-lands are destroyed. Deliver large filth to me, Gadaric Munce, at The Forsaken Inn, and you will earn our favour.Wanted: The Eglain desire proof that spiders making their home within and beneath ruins in the Lone-lands are destroyed.'My people have long suffered and feared the fangs of the spiders in these lands. This will do well to give us some solace and you favour.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gadaric Munce of the Eglain offers favour with his people if one can collect large wings from the local crebain.You accepted a task to acquire large wings for Gadaric Munce. The wings can be taken from the gore-crows which flock along the road east, beneath the shadow of Weathertop. You can find Gadaric within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Large wings from the gore-crows watching the roads to the east. The vile crebain are enemies of the Eglain, and if you deliver the wings to me, Gadaric Munce, at The Forsaken Inn, you will earn our favour.Wanted: Large wings from the gore-crows watching the roads to the east.'These stronger crebain are unafraid of our number and attack in force. Clipping their wings may make them think twice about fighting our people.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Lieva Dourlily has an intense dislike for the goblins that have recently arrived in the Lone-lands and seeks to drive them away.There will be barrels of uneaten food at the goblin-camps of Weatherfoot, north-east of The Forsaken Inn and south-west of Weathertop.

Lieva Dourlily has asked you to take a special draught she brewed to the goblin-camps in the north-east and introduce it into their food supply. The draught is supposed to have properties that Lieva thinks will encourage the goblins to abandon the Lone-lands.Lieva Dourlily awaits your return at The Forsaken Inn, south-west of Weatherfoot.

You added the contents of Lieva's draught to the food-barrels found at the goblin-encampments north-east of The Forsaken Inn. Lieva will want to know of your success.Talk to Lieva Dourlilly at The Forsaken Inn'I hate the goblins, I do! Skulking thieves, the lot of them, sneaking about in the night so they can pick the best herbs and mushrooms! They'll soon pick the Lone-lands dry if they go unhindered -- not that there was much out here to begin with!

'I made a special draught for them, though. It'll make them sluggish and unruly -- maybe unruly enough to fight amongst themselves! And if not... well, it'll have other effects that just might make them change their minds about staying here.

'Take these draughts and pour them into the barrels located in the goblins' camps to the north-east of the inn at Weatherfoot, at the base of Weathertop. Return to me when the deed is done.''Those goblins have got to go! Get my draught into their food, and quickly, before they get all the good mushrooms and spices! They have camps to the north-east at Weatherfoot, south-west of Weathertop.''The goblins didn't seem to show any ill effects from the draught? Well, I guess I shouldn't really be surprised. Goblins eat anything and everything!

'No sense fussing about that right now. You did well by me, ${PLAYER}. I owe you a little coin at the very least.'Arinora's duties became even greater when Gadaric Munce arrived at The Forsaken Inn. Anlaf is an unforgiving innkeep, according to Arinora, and she needs help to keep up with everything.Thorn-talon and Sharp-eye crebain can be found on and around Weathertop, in the Lone-lands.

In an effort to see to all the work before her, Arinora asked you to collect craban-feathers to help her stuff the pillows of The Forsaken Inn.'Ever since Gadaric Munce showed up, I've had no rest! Normally, we see no guests for months on end, but the glut of guests here has me stretched to my limit!

'Anlaf is under great strain and takes his worries out on me, driving harder to see that the chambers are cared for, the workers watched, and the guests fed. Well, I'm just not able to do it all!

'One thing that still needs to be done is the replacing of the pillows. All the stuffing's gone missing with the use they are getting, and I need to get the fluff back into them. We don't have the luxury of ducks, but the crebain circling about in the north might do in a pinch. There are plenty of the troublesome thorn-talon and sharp-eye crebain lurking about. Bring me some of their feathers, and I'll pay you for your trouble. The birds circle often over the vales surrounding Weathertop and the summit of the great hill.''Is that awful smell coming from these feathers? Oh my, I had never thought that those terrible, little birds could give off such a stench!

'No matter, I suppose. With the cooking that Old Mugwort does, this stench'll fade into the background of the curious smells that haunt the inn in no time!'The ancient Dead have arisen within the Great Barrow.Gaunt Battle-callers can be found within the Flooded Wing of the Great Barrow.Defeat Gaunt Battle-callers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hunters out of Bree tracked what they thought was a large wolf through the northern stretches of the Midgewater Marsh and into the Midgewater Pass only to find that it was something far worse. Now, they are trapped and in dire need of assistance.There are Orcs within the Midgewater Pass. Aggie Honeysuckle is at her camp to the west of the Orc-haunts.

Aggie Honeysuckle has asked you to find the weapons the Orcs stole from the hunters when they entered the Midgewater Pass.'Dale and Sam are too busy whimpering about losing our traps and our equipment. Fools! What we need to do is gather up our weapons and trudge right on back to Bree. We've lost traps before. In fact, most times you'll lose several traps when hunting prey that is worth hunting. We do have little means, but we should have enough to survive. What we do not have is our weaponry.

'The Orcs made off with everything of value. Some things, I am willing to part with; other items, like my bow, Sam's halberd, and Dale's mace, we cannot go on without. The journey home is treacherous as well, and knives will likely not see us through that journey.

'I do not have much, but if you can find it within you to assist us, then I will give you what I can share. The Orcs likely are still carrying all of our goods.''These will certainly help. Now, if I can only get these two to stop licking their wounds, gather their composure, and head back towards Bree, I might sleep in a real bed within a few nights.'The arrival of a goblin-tribe in Annunlos has made maintaining business at The Forsaken Inn nearly impossible for its owner, Anlaf the Forlorn.Goblins can be found in Annunlos north and east from The Forsaken Inn.

Anlaf the Forlorn, proprietor of The Forsaken Inn, suggested that you might be the solution to his goblin-problem. He has asked that you show the goblins of Annunlos that they are not welcome in the Lone-lands.Defeat goblins in the Lone-lands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Anlaf the Forlorn resides at The Forsaken Inn along the Great East Road.

Anlaf may be pleased to learn that you have slain a good number of the goblins that trouble him.Talk to Anlaf the Forlorn at The Forsaken Inn'Pay no mind to the name of the Inn; not all who pass here never return. Only ones that stray too far off the road and find themselves in the clutches o' them goblins.

'Bloody-handed creatures deserving of a good beating, those goblins! You're an able-bodied sort... why don't you go show those brutes they're not wanted here? The lands around the inn are just crawling with them.

'People scare easily, you see. With the goblins creeping all about the ruins and so close to the inn, I'm destined to use the wood falling from the roof to bar the doors unless someone gives me a hand. You'll find goblins on the fields of Annunlos, and a good number of them have established a foothold in the old ruins of Minas Eriol, to the south-east.''Those goblins are a menace, if you ask me! Driving them off will help travellers find their way into the Lone-lands with less fear in their hearts, and I'll lose fewer customers. Anyone of any importance will win and only the goblins will lose, my friend! Isn't that what we want?''You've done me a favour, friend, and I feel that I owe you something. I've not much to spare other than a little coin, and that's not worth much this far from Bree.

'There are some others here at the inn who might have need of help. You should speak with our server, Lieva Dourlily. Lieva has been working on a scarecrow meant to scare the goblins away.

'Why not speak with her? You've been a help to me, but you're not willing to leave it at that, are you?'It's just a game of Hiders and Seekers. What can go wrong?Gildor has invited you to play a game of Hiders and Seekers.

You should go to the gazebo in Tighfield to begin the game.Go to the gazeboTime to close your eyes and count!Count down from tenYou should find the hobbit children hiding throughout Tighfield.

Gildor has assured you that you do not need to venture past Tighfield's walls.You've found all but one of the hiders. Where could the last child be?Find and <rgb=#00ff00>poke</rgb> hiders (5/6)Gildor is resting in Tighfield by the gazebo.

He might know where the last hider is.Talk to Gildor'Hail, friend! Would you like to join in our game of Hiders and Seekers? You may decline, but I will warn you that the children are quite tenacious! I had no choice but to surrender when they found me.

'Alas, they're still as energetic as they were when we started playing, and I've grown weary. Could you assume the role of seeker for a while? When you're ready, climb up to the gazebo and begin counting down. Rest assured, there is no need to venture beyond Tighfield's walls to find the children.

'Good luck!''One of the children has disappeared? Yes, I believe I know of whom you speak; her name is Periwinkle.'In search of the strange tree-creatures she saw, Marigold has taken you deep into the Old Forest.Marigold is waiting for you to catch up before venturing further into the Old Forest.Talk to MarigoldYou should defend Marigold as she guides you through the forest.Defend MarigoldMarigold has found the tree-creatures.

You should talk to her and come up with a plan.Talk to MarigoldMarigold has suggested eavesdropping on the tree-creatures.

If one of the creatures leaves, it may be possible to stop the remaining two together.Eavesdrop on the tree-creaturesMarigold has challenged the tree-creatures.Defend MarigoldThe tree-creatures have been defeated.Talk to Marigold'Let's leave before another ghost shows up!''I didn't think we'd be so lucky, getting here without being attacked by forest animals!

'The place where I saw those tree-creatures isn't far from here. Don't worry -- I know the Old Forest like the back of my hand.''It looks like they're talking about something important. We can't fight all three of them at once, but if just one leaves, I'm sure we can stop the remaining two.

'For now, we ought to listen to their conversation. It might help us figure out what is going on.''Well, this day keeps getting stranger and stranger! If ghosts are being drawn to the forest because of a sword, we need to find it. The Bounders are already worried about the forest animals; we don't need to add Barrow-downs ghosts to that list!

'At the very least, I know someone who might be able to help us. I'll take you straight to him!'The folk of Herne shelter within Crossway House for the night, fearful that whatever evil passed through Herne might return.Meneldir wants to speak with you in Crossway House while you wait for the hours to pass.Talk to Meneldir in Crossway HouseMeneldir has asked you to speak with some of the townsfolk who gather within Crossway House, and to assure them that they will be safe.Talk to Maddoc at the counterTalk to Bledri upstairsTalk to Aldyth upstairsTalk to Aberthol in the main roomThe residents of Herne are in fairly high spirits considering the dangers that may threaten their town.Return to your tableMeneldir has invited you to <rgb=#00FF00>sit</rgb> at his table.<rgb=#00FF00>Sit</rgb> at Meneldir's tableListen to the conversation of your companions and remain on guard.Listen to the conversation of your companionsBoromir stands at one of the windows. Has he seen anything outside?Talk to Boromir at the windowsListen to the conversation of your companions and remain on guard.Listen to the conversation of your companionsBoromir wears a concerned expression.Talk to BoromirListen to the conversation of your companions and remain on guard.Listen to the conversation of your companionsA feeling of dread has settled upon Crossway House. What has happened outside?Go outside Crossway House and investigateYour companions stand ready to defend the folk of Herne.Talk to Meneldir outside Crossway House<rgb=#FF0000>Meneldir, Egfrith, and Boromir must survive</rgb>'It is over, ${PLAYER}. We are alive.''It is a relief to me that so many of Herne's folk listened to us, ${PLAYER}. I hope the walls of Crossway House prove strong enough to keep them safe... or that the evil has moved on, and this plan is made unnecessary. But I cannot risk that.

'These first hours have not been difficult for the people of Herne, for Maddoc made the wise decision to let his ale flow freely, and to set plenty of food upon his tables. The real test will come in the hours ahead.

'Will you speak with some of the folk here in Crossway House and determine their mood?''I have not seen Crossway House this busy for many months! Would that it could be in better circumstance.

'Some of my neighbours believe this is some scheme of mine, cooked up to increase my custom. Ha! If only I could claim to have thought of it! Alas, I am not that smart.

'It does seem as if nothing will come to Herne this night. Still, you and your companions have my thanks. It is better to be safe than it is to be sorry, yes?''There's nothing out there. At least, nothing I can see.

'I have given myself a couple frights already tonight, when the wind kicks up and throws back the shutters on the inn. But that is all... for now, anyway.

'I am glad to be in here with my neighbours, and not out there in the dark!''It is comforting to be inside on a night such as this, even if whatever came here before does not return.

'Sometimes, the conversation ebbs, and I think I can hear wailing outside the walls of the inn. But that's just my imagination... or perhaps it is the wind.

'We are safe in Crossway House, I hope. Your companions have their weapons, and so do you. You will keep us safe, if anything happens. Won't you?''I will tell you the honest truth, ${PLAYER}, for you and your companions have my respect, and I cannot keep secrets from you. I wish I had not come here tonight. Maddoc has been free with his spiced beer, and I appreciate it, but that changes nothing.

'I can see in the eye of my mind the crawling shape I encountered that night in town, and I know that if I look over my shoulder I will see its companion, sitting astride a steed black as night! They are waiting just outside the walls of the inn, waiting for me to emerge!

'Oh... I am sorry... it's just such a strong feeling. I was an adventurer, and now I am undone by the faintest whisper of a memory! I am shamed. There are so many folk here, and some of them heroes: Boromir of Gondor, Egfrith of Rohan... and I do not exclude you from that list, ${PLAYER}!

'Forgive me. I will find my courage. There is safety among so many good folk, after all. Those shadows will not dare to trouble us here.''There's nothing out there, ${PLAYER}.

'Just wind and darkness.''I told you of the assault upon Osgiliath in the days of June, did I not, and of the strange omens surrounding the battle? One of these was a dream, a dream with the feel of prophecy. This I withheld from you, for I did not know what to think; in Gondor we rely upon the bright sword and the sturdy shield, and we are right to do so! A dream is a fleeting thing, and what man would put his trust in something that lives only in darkness, and vanishes with the sunrise?'

Boromir strokes his beard thoughtfully.

'I say dreams are fleeting, but this one was peculiar. It came to my brother Faramir on the eve of the attack upon Osgiliath, and then many nights afterward, always the same. It came once to me, and although my brother told me of the dreams, it was with mine own eyes that I beheld these details; they seem so real to me.

'In the dream I saw the eastern sky growing dark, and there was a rumbling as of thunder. But a light lingered in the western sky, and a clear voice called to me. "Seek for the Sword that was broken," it said, "in Imladris it dwells. There shall be counsels taken stronger than Morgul-spells."

'That is of great interest to any son of Gondor, ${PLAYER}! Any means of countering the evils that pour from the Morgul Vale are welcome in Minas Tirith! But the voice did not stop there. Speaking of Imladris, it continued.

'"There shall be shown a token that Doom is near at hand, for Isildur's Bane shall waken, and the Halfling forth shall stand."'

Boromir stares at you solemnly.

'For my brother to dream of this doom, the doom of Minas Tirith, on so many nights... and then for the same dream to come to me... I fear that we are running out of time. Gondor needs the wisdom that lies hidden in Imladris. But now we waste the hours hiding in this inn, when we should be moving ever onward!''The feeling of dread grows stronger! Prepare yourselves!

'Something approaches!'Hunters out of Bree tracked what they thought was a large wolf through the northern stretches of the Midgewater Marsh and into the Midgewater Pass only to find that it was something far worse. Now, they are trapped and in dire need of assistance.Wolves can be found within the Midgewater Pass. Sam Thistelbur is at his camp, west of the wolf-haunts.

Sam Thistlebur asked you to strip choice cuts of meat from the wolves you hunt within the Midgewater Pass.'We had hoped to be spending a few days at The Forsaken Inn, eating some of Old Mugwort's stews, but now we are just hoping that the strips of dried meats we have left will last us. That is, unless you would be willing to help get us some meat. Beggars are not choosers, and we would be pleased to get even the stringiest of wolf-meat. Though, I will share a secret... there is choice meat on a wolf.

'If you are hunting them anyway, would you collect some choice cuts that I can slice into strips and further treat so that we have enough food to return to Bree?

'There are wolves prowling all throughout the hills of the Midgewater Pass.''These are fine cuts indeed. I shall be able to cure enough meat for a trip to Bree and back to this accursed land.

'Thank you so, ${PLAYER}. You have shown me that there still is hope though the skies above are darkening.'Guardsman Otley, in Trestlebridge, is awaiting delivery of flawed ears at the war-torn town of Trestlebridge.You have been tasked with collecting flawed ears from the beasts near Trestlebridge.

You can deliver the items to Guardsman Otley at Trestlebridge.Wanted: Flawed ears from the beasts near Trestlebridge. Deliver the items to Guardsman Otley in the town of Trestlebridge.Wanted: Flawed ears from the beasts near Trestlebridge.'I am certain that this will be a welcome delivery. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.A cart bound for Bree and due to return to the Forsaken Inn is several days overdue. The recent arrival of goblins and other foes has Anlaf the Forlorn worried that something happened to the cart and its driver.Anlaf's missing cart travelled west towards Bree along the Great East Road.

Anlaf has asked that you search north and south of the cart's estimated route to learn its fate and the fate of its driver.Find Anlaf's missing cartFind the driver of the missing cartAnlaf is at the Forsaken Inn.

You discovered that the cart was destroyed, its supplies stolen, and the driver killed by the nearby goblins. Anlaf should be given this unfortunate news.'Good day, ${CLASS}. What do you need? Did I summon you? Anyways, it doesn't matter. My supplies have not come back from Bree. I sent a cart out a week ago -- or earlier -- I'm not sure anymore. But I did send a cart, I know that! Now where is it? Tell me that!

'The cart was travelling west towards Bree, and it should have come back by now! I want to know what happened to the supplies I sent and to the cart's driver. I hope it wasn't the goblins... there are so many of them here now.

'Will you help? Will you travel west towards Bree? Search both north and south and bring me any news you discover.''What? Goblins attacked my cart? This is no good at all. How am I supposed to feed my patrons? This is simply no good at all! Let me think on this.''Have you found the cart yet? Do you know what happened? Don't come back until you do! It should be somewhere north or south of the road to the west.'Trouble of all manner surrounds the Forsaken Inn, and a retired Constable needs help protecting the Inn from the threats.Wolves can be found in the Midgewater Pass. Orcs prowl north in the Weather Hills and the Midgewater Pass. Boars wander in Annunlos and the Weather Hills surrounding Weathertop.

Bram Ashleaf has asked you to help protect The Forsaken Inn by clearing the threat of wolves, Orcs, and boars from the area surrounding the inn.'Greetings, young ${RACE}. I'm the Constable here at the Forsaken Inn. My body's too old for adventure, but my mind is young enough to know there's work to be done around here.

'Up north, beyond Weathertop, there are wolves and Orcs in the Midgewater Pass and boars scattered throughout Annunlos, all causing no end of trouble. I've a bit of a proposition for you: clear out the threats. If you bring me proof of your noble deeds, I will see that you get a fair reward.

'Are you interested?''Wolf-tails, boar-tusks, and Orc-shields... you are quite the skilled warrior. It may seem a menial task to you, but believe me when I say this is much appreciated.

'The people here at the Inn struggle enough without worrying about the wildlife and evils that are spreading unhindered. Ever since those lights appeared atop Weathertop, we've had one trouble after another, and there isn't a sign of relief in sight.

'Take this small reward... you've earned it.'Defeat Orcs in the North Downs.Defeat Orcs in the North DownsDefeat many Orcs in the North Downs.Defeat Orcs in the North DownsCandaith's pursuit of an Orc-company turned deadly when he was discovered, and in his haste to escape, he lost his bow to the Orcs.White Hand archers scout the Midgewater Pass, and can be found also within Bleakrift, north-west of Candaith's camp at the foot of Weathertop.

Candaith has asked you to recover his bow from the archer that has taken it.'The Orcs took my bow from me, and while it would not be difficult to make a new one, I am loath to be parted from it. It is very dear to me, for it was passed down from my grandfather to my father, and from my father to me.

'One of War-master Uzorr's archers has surely taken it for his own. Search the vicinity of the Midgewater Pass for archers bearing the mark of the White Hand. If you are truly brave, look too within Bleakrift, to the north-west. I have seen many archers in that place, from a fair distance, and one of them might have taken my bow as a spoil of war.

'Bring caution with you, ${PLAYER}.''Thank you, my friend. This bow will yet slay many Orcs before its time is ended. I thank you for its happy return.'Hunters out of Bree tracked what they thought was a large wolf through the northern stretches of the Midgewater Marsh and into the Midgewater Pass only to find that it was something far worse. Now, they are trapped and in dire need of assistance.Wolves can be found throughout the area of the Midgewater Pass. Dale Sageford can be found to the west of the wolf-haunts.

Dale Sageford and his companions make their living in pelt-trading, and the Orcs took everything they had and left them to die. He has asked that you aid them in recovering some of the pelts so they can put food into their stomachs upon their return to Bree.'I've been tracking wolves and other prey for the greater part of my life. Never had I seen anything like it, and now I hope to never see anything like it ever again. It stood nearly the height of a man at the shoulder with jaws twice the size of my head!

'Worse, it was led by Orcs! Orcs! I've heard tales, but never had the misfortune to see one. Now, I have the displeasure of their acquaintance and a terribly light set of packs to show for our misbegotten hunt.

'I'm asking, not without humility, that you offer up some assistance to those of us who have so recently seen our fortunes dashed. There are wolves all throughout these hills. Their pelts will fetch a good amount of coin back in Bree. I would be willing to pay you an up front sum for your assistance in keeping our livelihood intact. What say you?''You are a truly generous hero! We thought our lives lost, then our livelihoods, now we have a small bit of hope that we can recover. We shall ever be in your debt.'Candaith asks you to search among the Orc-encampment at Bleakrift for anything that may be of worth. He suggests that this may be something that will help a group of people you will likely deal with in the future.The Bleakrift lies to the north and west of Candaith's camp at a split near the small stream separating the Weatherway and the Weather Hills.

Candaith asked you to look out for anything that may appear to have value in the area of the Bleakrift asking that you return it to him.'While you trek within Bleakrift searching for Uzorr, be on the look-out for lockboxes or anything that has the appearance of value that the Orcs may have stolen.

'If my hunch of Radagast is correct then this will prepare you well for the future and the possibility of dealing with a variety of people here in the Lone-lands. I will say nothing more of them now as they are a private people and it is not for me to reveal their secrets.

'You will know what I speak of should you see them, but you keep your search centred around the Bleakrift.''Yes, I think that these will do nicely. More on that in a short while, for there is much more to do about these Orcs before I send you onward to the inhabitants of the Lone-lands.'The clothes Gruffo has sewn are ready for his children and grandchildren, but he'll need a bit of help delivering them.Gruffo's family lives in homes across Long Cleeve.

You should find and deliver the clothes to drop-off spots at their doorsteps.You have delivered all the clothes.

You should return to Gruffo and let him know. He lives in the house across from the Long Cleeve inn.Talk to Gruffo'Dear me, you gave me quite a shock there. I'm afraid I was completely consumed by my work!

'Oh yes, I'm very proud of the clothes I've sewn here! Picked up the hobby recently. The clothes are for my children and grandchildren, but trying to deliver to their doorsteps has been a vexing problem. Walking up and down Long Cleeve takes a toll on my old legs, you see. I'd never finish the job!'

'If you'd like to assist me, you can deliver to some of them. I'll deliver the rest!''Phew, that's enough walking for me today. You have my thanks!

'Now that that's out of the way... maybe I ought to start sewing clothes for my neighbours, just for fun. Ooh, I've got ideas already!'Defeat shades in the North Downs.Defeat shades in the North DownsDefeat many shades in the North Downs.Defeat shades in the North DownsWith Gildor, you have tracked Periwinkle to the crumbling ruins of Tham Amothir.Gildor needs you to cause a distraction so that he can rescue Periwinkle.

You should defeat half-orcs at Tham Amothir.Defeat half-orcs at Tham Amothir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have caused enough of a distraction.

You should return to Gildor outside of Tham Amothir.Return to Gildor outside of Tham AmothirThanks to your help, Gildor has successfully rescued Periwinkle from Tham Amothir.

Periwinkle seems to have something she wants to show you.Talk to PeriwinkleGildor has something to add.Talk to Gildor'It is fortunate that the half-orcs have not yet found Periwinkle, but I fear this will not last.

'A distraction is in order. Defeat the half-orcs -- I'll return here with her.'Periwinkle waits until she's certain she's caught your attention, then extends a closed fist towards you. With a gleeful grin, she reveals a handful of shiny metal knick-knacks. The objects are very old and warped beyond recognition -- relics from a different time.

'I found these mathoms in the ruins! Mr. Gildor said I was very lucky. Um, he also said that what I did was very dangerous, and I have to apologize to you, so... I'm sorry!''Do not worry, Periwinkle. There may be others in the Shire who can teach you how to stay safe on your adventures.'

Gildor lowers his voice, so as not to be heard by her.

'I suspect the half-orcs intended to melt down her mathoms; there is little value to be found otherwise. We cannot stop them from combing the ruins, but my heart is glad to see some artifacts have been saved from such a bleak fate.

'Thank you for your timely aid, ${PLAYER}. I'll escort Periwinkle back to Tighfield, then I must take my leave. I still have a long journey to Lin Giliath ahead of me.'Nidgyl is concerned with the movements of goblins, Orcs, and brigands in the surrounding areas.Weatherfoot lies far to the east of Bree-land, near Weathertop in the Lone-lands.

Nidgyl has asked you to scout the Orc-encampment at Weatherfoot.Scout WeatherfootGlumhallow is far to the east of Bree-land, near Weathertop in the Lone-lands.

Nidgyl has asked you to scout the Orc-encampment at Glumhallow.Scout GlumhallowEchad Sûl is a small hill that lies north of Weathertop in the Lone-lands, far to the east of Bree-land.

Nidgyl has asked you to scout the ruins of Echad Sûl.Scout Echad SûlWeathertop is in the Lone-lands, far to the east of Bree-land.

Nidgyl has asked you to climb Weathertop itself to examine the surrounding lands.Scout WeathertopNidgyl is at Bree-town in Bree-land, far to the west of Weathertop.

You should report to Nidgyl and tell them what you have seen from the heights of Weathertop.Talk to Nidgyl'The Enemy is on the move... do you know of the hill called Weathertop overlooking the western Lone-lands? The Orcs have raised a number of small camps around its base and launch raids upon travellers and villages from them.

'I need you to scout the area of Weathertop and keep an eye on the strength of the Enemy's forces there. We must watch for an opportunity to strike against them should they lower their guard.

'Weathertop lies far to the east of here.''The Orcs continue to maintain their camps, do they? No matter for now, their forces in the Lone-lands are too small to represent a real threat -- yet.

'We will keep an eye on them, though. The Enemy would not have sent them so far afield without reason. They will make their move soon enough.'A farmer's sheep have fled their pen.Before you go searching for Hulfryn's sheep, you should fill the trough outside his home with hay.Hulfryn's five sheep have fled their pen.

You should find them around Herne and pet them to calm them down.Hulfryn's five sheep have fled their pen.

You should find them around Herne and pet them to calm them down.Find and <rgb=#00ff00>pet</rgb> sheep in Herne (4/5)The fifth sheep remains missing.

You should tell Hulfryn. His home is in the south-west corner of Herne.Talk to Hulfryn'What were you doing out on the streets, stranger? Don't tell me you haven't heard about the evil things roaming about. I-I may not be a brave man, but something ain't right about those Riders. If I can live my life without ever seeing them again, I'll die content.

'If you're intent on returning outside, could you look around Herne for my sheep? The five of them got spooked and escaped the pen. Just pet them a bit and they'll calm down. I don't want them running off into the wilderness.

'Here -- before you go looking, take some of this hay and fill up their trough outside in the pen.''You couldn't find the last one? There can't be that many places for a sheep to hide in Herne....'There are many villainous monsters roaming the North Downs, and the Free Peoples must do their part to slay them.Slay many goblins in the North DownsSlay many Orcs in the North DownsSlay many shades in the North DownsSlay many trolls in the North DownsSlay many Wargs in the North DownsSlay many worms in the North DownsA few seeds may become the start of something new.Cadi has asked you to collect beech nuts around Wylvenclove, north-west of Herne.Collect beech nuts around Wylvenclove (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected a number of beech nuts for Cadi.

She waits in Rendal's home in the south-west corner of Herne.You should help Cadi harvest seeds from the beech nuts.Harvest seeds from beech nuts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The beech seeds have been harvested.

You should talk to Cadi.'You're an adventurer, aren't you? Just like Mister Rendal. He warned me to stay away from the beech trees in Wylvenclove because of the wolves... but wolves aren't scary for you, right?

'Wylvenclove is that way! Um... is that north-west?

'There must be lots of beech nuts on the ground there. And then, and then -- after you come back, we can take the seeds out together!''Oh, you found a lot!'

Cadi eagerly takes the beech nuts from you and spreads them out onto the table.

'I take half, and you take half. If you don't know how to get the seeds out, I'll teach you!''When Mister Rendal comes back, I'm going to plant so many trees with him!' Cadi beams.

'Thank you!'Bingo wants to bring Marigold back to her home in Newbury before continuing his journey.Tom Bombadil can be found outside his house in the Old Forest during the day, and relaxes inside during the evening.

Bingo has asked you to retrieve his manuscript from Tom Bombadil, and to learn what the mysterious fellow thought of it.Talk to Tom Bombadil and retrieve Bingo's manuscriptBingo is outside the house of Tom Bombadil, and now you should give him the news that Tom has misplaced Bingo's manuscript.Talk to Bingo outside the house of Tom BombadilTom Bombadil may have set Bingo's manuscript down outside somewhere near his house, and Bingo has asked you to see if you can find it.Find Bingo's manuscript somewhere outside near Tom Bombadil's houseYou found some pages of Bingo's manuscript on the path leading north from Tom Bombadil's house. Did the wind bring other pages further north?Search north of Tom Bombadil's house for more pages of Bingo's manuscriptYou found some pages of Bingo's manuscript, but there are still some pages missing. You should return to speak with Bingo and Marigold outside the house of Tom Bombadil to decide upon a course of action.Talk to Bingo outside the house of Tom BombadilTalk to Marigold outside the house of Tom BombadilThe wind carried pages of Bingo's manuscript into the northern Barrow-downs, and you should search for them. Marigold has also asked you to defeat barghests and crawlers while you search.Defeat barghests in the Barrow-downs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat crawlers in the Barrow-downs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find the section of manuscript concerning Bingo's swimming lessonFind the section of manuscript concerning Bingo's departure from the ShireBingo said he would meet you outside his cousin Prisca's home in Newbury once you were finished in the Barrow-downs.Talk to Bingo outside the home of Prisca Boffin, in NewburyMarigold and Prisca Boffin are outside their home in Newbury, and each of them wishes to speak with you.Talk to Marigold Boffin outside her mother's home in NewburyTalk to Prisca Boffin outside her home in NewburyBingo is outside his cousin Prisca's home in Newbury, across the Brandywine Bridge from the Shire.Talk to Bingo outside Prisca's home, in Newbury'All right, ${PLAYER}. I would say it is time to see Marigold safely back to Newbury, and for our own journey to continue! I have enjoyed this brief stay at the house of Tom Bombadil, but there are many other places to see and people to meet!

'Would you mind speaking to Tom and retrieving my manuscript from him? I am most interested to hear what he thought of it, now that he has had plenty of time to read!''Would you mind speaking to Tom and retrieving my manuscript from him? I am most interested to hear what he thought of it, now that he has had plenty of time to read!'You ask Tom what he thought of Bingo's manuscript, but the strange fellow is only puzzled.

'Your words are all a-scramble, young one. Tom remembers a great many things: the sun in the morning, the breezes of mid-day, the swelter of afternoon, and the cool of evening; but he does not know the pages of which you speak. There is no need for writing! If you spend all the day writing, that is a day you have missed!

'Do not look so glum! I am sure this writing will turn up. I may have set it down outside. There are so many things to see, happy distractions all, and sometimes things get lost. All is found in the end, hey!''Do not look so glum! I am sure this writing will turn up. I may have set it down outside. There are so many things to see, happy distractions all, and sometimes things get lost. All is found in the end, hey!''Tom has misplaced my manuscript? Oh, bother! I know there wasn't yet much of it, but I still keenly feel its loss! I will not be able to recreate exactly what I wrote the first time! Perhaps I should just abandon this endeavour. I am not a writer, and I am not an adventurer!'

You seek to console the unhappy hobbit, and soon Bingo is slightly less despondent.

'Perhaps you're right, ${PLAYER}. Tom is most at home outdoors. Perhaps he set it down somewhere nearby. Can you look around in the vicinity and see if you see any pages of my manuscript? I do not want to lose the record of this journey so near the start of it!''Tom is most at home outdoors. Perhaps he set it down somewhere nearby. Can you look around in the vicinity and see if you see any pages of my manuscript? I do not want to lose the record of this journey so near the start of it!''You found some of my pages, but think the wind may have carried more of them further north? Can you go check for me, ${PLAYER}?''Thank you for finding part of my manuscript, ${PLAYER}. Tom must have set it down outside and the wind took it, carrying pages north among the trees. You found some of them, but it seems to me that the rest of the pages must have been carried further north... into the Barrow-downs.'

Bingo shivers.

'That place has a frightful reputation and I did not plan to explore it, but now it seems I have no choice! We have to search the Barrow-downs for the remainder of the missing pages! It looks as if I am missing the section of my journal that concerns my swimming lesson and the section dealing with our departure from the Shire. After we are finished in the Barrow-downs, we should travel back to Newbury to bring Marigold home. I will see you there.''You found some of cousin Bingo's missing pages on the path to the north, didn't you? That way leads to the Barrow-downs! If you are going to brave that spooky place, so am I! A hero never runs away from a challenge, no matter how scary!

'I have heard that some frightful creatures make their homes among the burial mounds of Men in the Barrow-downs, and we will need to face some of them if we go there. Watch out for ravenous barghests and giant crawlers, for they will be watching out for you!''We have to search the Barrow-downs for the remainder of the missing pages! It looks as if I am missing the section of my journal that concerns my swimming lesson and the section dealing with our departure from the Shire. After we are finished in the Barrow-downs, we should travel back to Newbury to bring Marigold home. I will see you there.''I have heard that some frightful creatures make their homes among the burial mounds of Men in the Barrow-downs, and we will need to face some of them if we go there. Watch out for ravenous barghests and giant crawlers, for they will be watching out for you!''I will see you back at my cousin Prisca's house in Newbury, ${PLAYER}! I do not want to stay here a moment longer!''We have all returned safely from the Barrow-downs, and I have exhausted my supply of adjectives with which to describe them, ${PLAYER}! There are only so many ways I can convey how unhappy I was to be walking among those mounds!

'I learned a valuable lesson, too: do not let folk read my book before it is quite finished! Tom did not mean any harm by it, but I do not want to risk losing any more of these pages in frightful places. Tracking down pages is quite a tiresome activity, wouldn't you say?

'Prisca is quite cross with me, as well. She thinks that it was my idea to go jaunting about in the Barrow-downs, when it was the furthest idea in my head. That was mostly Marigold's idea!''Prisca thinks it was my idea to go jaunting about in the Barrow-downs, when it was the furthest idea in my head. That was mostly Marigold's idea!''That was amazing, ${PLAYER}! The Barrow-downs were everything I had hoped they would be! I was never scared, not really.

'I will need to come up with something truly exciting to top this most recent adventure!''I have scolded Marigold before, and it never seems to do any good. The girl is always going off on these adventures. Adventures! More like mischief and bother, if you ask me!

'It was good to see my cousin Bingo for a short while, but I must ask him to be off as soon as he can. Either he has been a bad influence on Marigold or she has been a bad influence on him, but either way there is entirely too much adventurous spirit between them!

'We Boffins have been many things, but heroes? I don't know about that!''That was amazing, ${PLAYER}! The Barrow-downs were everything I had hoped they would be! I was never scared, not really.

'I will need to come up with something truly exciting to top this most recent adventure!''It was good to see my cousin Bingo for a short while, but I must ask him to be off as soon as he can. Either he has been a bad influence on Marigold or she has been a bad influence on him, but either way there is entirely too much adventurous spirit between them!

'We Boffins have been many things, but heroes? I don't know about that!''Ah, Prisca will calm down in time. She always does. Now I will visit with all my various cousins, once removed, and do my best to talk Marigold out of the adventuring business.

'I don't think I will succeed, and just between you and me, ${PLAYER}, I don't think I want to! She will make a fine hero, if that is what she wants to be, and Prisca will just have to get used to it!

'But don't tell her I said that. We still need a place to stay until our journey resumes!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'The Two Taverns' (Recommended Level: 24)</rgb>Several young trees haven't been tended to for a few days.Saplings and trees can be found outside Rendal's house.

Cadi has asked you to water the saplings and talk to the trees.Water saplings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to trees...? (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You've taken care of the plants in the garden.

You should return to Cadi. She waits in Rendal's home in the south-west corner of Herne.Talk to Cadi'Um... who are you? Are you looking for Mister Rendal? He hasn't been home for a few days....

'O-oh, my name is Cadi. Mama let me come here to take care of the trees. I watered some but then... then there were dark and scary shapes on the road, so I ran in here. I think they're gone, but I don't want to leave yet.

'Could you help me? Mister Rendal planted them just outside. There are some sap... sap-lings? He waters them, then he talks to the other trees. At least, I think he does. The trees must be very good listeners.''The trees must have been so lonely. I think you made them happy again!

'A long, long time ago, there used to be more trees here, all over. Did you know that? Mister Rendal told me. But then his and-sisters -- that means people in his family from a long time ago -- cut all those trees down and didn't plant more. How could they be so mean? Didn't they know there wouldn't be anything left after, because of them?

'That must be why Mister Rendal plants so many trees by himself -- he wants to fix what his family did. But he shouldn't have to do this alone. I want to help him!'The ancient Dead have arisen within the Great Barrow.Thadúr the Ravager, an ancient warrior, has arisen deep within the watery depths of the Great Barrow.Defeat Thadúr the RavagerWhile you were reassembling the mayor's bed, a new person arrived and is speaking with the mayor.Rhiangar has asked you to bring wine to her father, the mayor, and to see if you can learn anything of import.

The wine is in the kitchen of the mayor's house, in Herne.Pick up the bottle of wineRhiangar has asked you to bring wine to her father, the mayor, and to see if you can learn anything of import.

The mayor is at the dining table, in the mayor's home, in Herne.Speak to OsianYou should let Rhiangar know what you learned from Osian and the Mayor.

Rhiangar is in the kitchen in the mayor's house in Herne.Talk to RhiangarRhiangar has asked you to bring her mother's journal to her from the library.

The library is in the mayor's house in Herne.Search the library for the journalYou should bring the journal to Rhiangar.

Rhiangar is in the kitchen of the mayor's house, in Herne.While the stew stews, Rhiangar has asked you to meet her at Herne's inn, The Crossway House.'While you were taking apart the bed, father had a guest arrive, as I am sure you noticed. That is Osian Beddow, and I am certain that he is the reason father was down at Tharbad, snooping around. I suspect he is also the reason father got injured years ago. Osian has always wanted to be Mayor, and though he whispers honeyed words into father's ears, his actions have led me to believe he is more of a wasp than a bee.

'Well, I have some work yet to do on the stew. Will you bring the wine to the table? Oh and, perhaps you could speak with them? I want to know what is going on, but Osian and I are not on speaking terms, and father clamps his mouth shut as soon as I enter the room.''Why are you still here?! Hmph, well the wine is welcome, even if you are not. No offence meant, but my daughter does not need someone like you around giving her crazed ideas. She already ran off to the big city and abandoned her duties and me. She is back, but for how long?

'Her mother was the same way. Never content to settle down. Always running off to look at crumbled fortresses and searching for Elves and the little folk down south in Swanfleet.''I have no problem with Rhiangar, and I wish she would warm up to me. I have made sure to care for her dear father in her absence, but Cadwallon missed her a great deal.

'It has been so difficult for him since the accident. Well, his troubles go back decades before that, to when his wife ran off. Oh, I should not speak of it.''Well, what did they say? Oh, Osian cared for my father in my absence, did he? I did not realize my father needing tending until now. No, he is a grown man and was capable when I left. I will not let that guilt creep in and take away the joy I had in travelling and studying in Bree.

'But what my father said is intriguing. Mother was going to look at ruins? His words are stirring a memory within me! ${PLAYER}, will you do me a favour and look for my mother's journal? It should be on the shelf in my father's library.''You found it! What memories this brings back! After mother disappeared, I would pore over her written words for hours, searching for some clue as to where she went. I never believed she left us on purpose, as my father insists. No, I think she met some darker fate.

'The stew needs to cook over a low fire for many hours, and I also wish to check on the townsfolk and see how they are faring. Will you meet me at the Crossway House? Many people are still holed up in the inn, taking comfort from one another.''Thank you for meeting me here! The town is still reeling from the passage of those black riders. I want to help wherever I can. If you have time, perhaps you, too, could lend some aid.'A desperate mother seeks help looking for her lost child.Wenna's son Cai has gone missing, but perhaps one of her neighbours has seen him. She wants you to ask around to see if the boy is with them.

Wenna's neighbours are located in Herne.Ask if anyone in Herne has seen CaiMoll stated that she saw Cai sleepwalking and told you to confirm her story with her father.

Moll's father Rodric is in the Inn at Herne.Talk to RodricRodric suggested that you should ask the Dunlending Gurthern whether he has seen the missing child.

Gurthern has a room upstairs, in the inn in Herne.Talk to Gurthern'My baby! My child is missing! Please! You must help me!

'Everything was fine last night. I put Cai down to sleep at the usual time, but when I woke this morning he was gone! I have been trying to work up the courage to leave the inn to search for him, but the riders. The riders! What if the riders took him!? Oh please, will you ask if any of my neighbours have seen him? Perhaps he went to one their houses?''What? A missing child? Of course I have not seen them. She should go look for the brat herself and stop putting her problems on the shoulders of others. We already have enough going on.''A missing child? Oh how awful! This is the first I am hearing of it. I will try to ask around as well, but no, I do not know where the child is.''No, I am sorry. I have not seen the little one today. Have you asked his friends? They are all playing together in the inn.''I did see Cai! I did! He was sleepwalking, I think. His face looked all dreamy and he was kind of mumbling. He was reaching out for the glow ball. ….We are not supposed to follow the glow balls. The Wailing Lady will get you. Ask father! I told him all about what I saw!''Hmph. Yes, Moll did tell me that she saw Cai wandering after glowing lights. Honestly, Moll is a bit of a dreamer herself. I would not lend too much weight to what she said.

'If you ask me, though, there is someone who might know something. You should talk to that Dunlending. Their kind steal children all the time! I would ask him myself, but he looks rather dangerous. But it would not be a problem for you, would it?

'He's upstairs in his room. But don't tell him I sent you!''Yes, I am Gurthern. A missing child, you say? No, I have not seen them, but tell me everything you know. Perhaps I can help.'One sheep seems to have fled Herne entirely.The last thing the missing sheep did before fleeing was eat from the trough outside Hulfryn's house.

You should check the area near it for tracks.Look for tracks near the trough outside of Hulfryn's houseYou have noticed faint hoof-prints near the trough outside Hulfryn's house.

You should examine them.Examine the sheep hoof-printsThe hoof-print trail leads north-west out of Herne.

You should follow it.Follow the hoof-print trailThe hoof-print trail you'd been following ended abruptly at a pool of blood.

You should examine the blood.Examine the bloodA trail of blood splatters continues north.

You should follow it.Follow the blood splattersYou've unfortunately found Hulfryn's last sheep.

You should examine its corpse.Examine the dead sheepYou have defeated the wight that killed Hulfryn's sheep.

You should return to Herne and tell him.Talk to Hulfryn in Herne'Ever tracked down an animal before? I've heard them Ranger folk are mighty good at it, but I don't know much. You ought to check the pen -- before it fled, the last thing that sheep did was eat at the trough.

'Take care, stranger. There're wolves outside Herne... and I've heard tell of worse things lurking beyond. Just thinking about it is enough to make my skin crawl.''What happened? Did you find the sheep?'

As you recount your story, the blood drains from Hulfryn's face.

'Black Riders and dead things walking... omens of worse times ahead. Still, I haven't got the means to leave Herne and find someplace safer. This town is my whole world. I just hope those Riders don't come back....

'${PLAYER}, was it? Stay safe out there.'The game of Hiders and Seekers has ended badly. It's up to you and Gildor to find Periwinkle.The other children might know where Periwinkle has gone.

They're all playing near the Elbow's Bend Inn.Gildor is waiting for you by Tighfield's western gate.

You should discuss what you've discovered with him.Talk to Gildor at Tighfield's western gateGildor has suggested regrouping outside the ruins of Tham Amothir, to the south-west of Tighfield.Meet up with Gildor outside Tham AmothirYou've found Gildor outside Tham Amothir.

You should talk to him to decide on what to do next.Talk to Gildor'Periwinkle is rather skilled at hiding. During our previous games, we only found her once she became bored and wandered off. I would not be surprised if this was the case again, but if you could not find her anywhere in Tighfield, then the situation is grave indeed.

'I shall ask the Bounders if they've seen her. Perhaps you could ask the other children; they've gone to play near the inn.''Oh, I saw Periwinkle! She came to my hiding spot and asked if I wanted to explore the scary ruins nearby. I said no, and shoo, before both of us get caught! I don't think she took that very well....

'Are you going mathom-hunting together? Is that why I see Mr. Gildor waiting at the western gate?''The Bounders have not seen Periwinkle, but I expected as much. After some investigation, I discovered faint footprints leading to the west.

'If what you learned is true, then she must have run off to Tham Amothir. 'Tis a dark place, certainly no place for a child! Let us part for now; we can cover more ground on our way south-west, and regroup outside the ruins.''${PLAYER}! I've spotted Periwinkle sneaking about, though she escapes my sight more often than not.'Thomas Thistlewool received a letter from Anlaf the Forlorn at The Forsaken Inn, mentioning money owed and a man he employed to collect some artifacts from the area.Anlaf the Forlorn can be found at The Forsaken Inn, eastward along the Great East Road.

Thomas Thistlewool has asked you to deliver a letter to Anlaf the Forlorn, the proprietor of The Forsaken Inn, promising payment for a debt incurred by a Man he hired to seek ancient relics for him.'You there, I have heard of your deeds about town. I am in need of a ${RACE} of honour, like yourself.

'I made an error in judgement recently that leaves me in a difficult position. I collect rare finds from the ruins of Arnor and in an effort to save coin, I employed a Man from the South. The Man, using my name, now owes a great deal of coin to the proprietor of The Forsaken Inn.

'If you are willing, take this letter to Anlaf the Forlorn at the Forsaken Inn before word reaches the guilds here in Bree-town. It lies eastward along the Great East Road. I'll not allow one indiscretion ruin my good name.''Quickly now, see that letter delivered. I will not have my name sullied another day because of this layabout.''Mr. Thistlewool sent me a letter of credit for Bree? I'll not look down my nose at it, but my message wasn't asking for money. Times are troubled here, and his man could be in a heap of it.

'See, the Munces deal in artifacts. That's Gadaric and Hunulf over there. They're good folk... they don't thieve. They got run off their land by goblins or some such. I heard there are Orcs, too. But the Munces have no more trade for Bree, so Thistlewool's Man said he was going to trade with the goblins direct.

'What I sent Thistlewool was a note from the goblins! Well, likely they made Thistlewool's man write it. His man's been captured. If he's still alive, he's likely up north-east of here. There's goblins camped up there, or so he said. You look a likely sort to go rescue him, eh?'After encountering the elusive wolf, Drugodech, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Gaerion can be found at Sírlond in Cardolan.

You have acquired a trophy from an elusive foe in Cardolan. You should deliver it to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Deliver Drugodech's paw to Gaerion in CardolanAfter encountering and defeating the elusive wolf, Drugodech, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.

As you inspect Drugodech's paw, you take note of its softness and of the dried blood caked between its toes.

You should bring this trophy to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.'Hail, traveller. What's that you've got there?'

Gaerion inspects Drugodech's paw closely, taking note of its softness.

'A fine trophy, friend. It is well-known that only a fool would turn their back upon one of the wolves of Cardolan. If you would be willing to part with this trophy, I will see to it that you are compensated.

'Yes? Very good. You have my thanks!'Frideric the Elder has received an urgent request from an old companion of his -- Captain Trotter of Trestlebridge -- requesting any aid that he might muster from Ost Guruth in staving off a threatened Orkish incursion upon the town.

Speak with Captain Trotter and see what aid you can offer him!Speak with Frideric in Ost Guruth to arrange a fast mount to Trestlebridge.

<rgb=#FF0000>WARNING: Completing this quest will immediately send you to Trestlebridge in the North Downs.</rgb>'Ah, I thank you for your recent help in dealing with our difficulties here in the Lone-lands, but I fear that I have received an urgent missive from my old companion, Captain Trotter of Trestlebridge.

'It seems that he fears an imminent attack upon the town by Orcs out of the North Downs, and is asking for any able-bodied warriors to aid them as soon as possible.

'While we have no men to spare ourselves, I could arrange for a fast horse to bring you to Trestlebridge if you feel you might be able to aid Captain Trotter.''So you're off to aid Captain Trotter in Trestlebridge are you? We'll be sorry to see you go, but hopefully you can do as much good there as you have for us here.'

<rgb=#FF0000>WARNING: Completing this quest will immediately send you to Trestlebridge in the North Downs.</rgb>Leofwenna was trapped in the ruins in the south-east when the goblins overran the Eglain's camp there. She has been hiding among the ruins since that time and is need of assistance.The arch leading from the ruins lies to the west.

Leofwenna has asked you to escort her to the arch leading out of the ruins and to freedom. She is in the area east beyond the first of the goblin camps south-east of The Forsaken Inn.Talk to LeofwennaGadaric Munce is at The Forsaken Inn, north-west of the ruins.

Leofwenna suggested you seek Gadaric Munce, and that he would reward you for assisting her.Talk to Gadaric Munce'${RACE}, you are real...my eyes are not deceived! I am Leofwenna of the Eglain. I have been trapped here for weeks, wounded and unable to make my way out past the goblins.

'My people were overrun by the goblins and were forced to flee. I defended them as best I could, saving some who reached the archway to the west, but my wounds were grievous, and I was fortunate to find this hiding place. I have been luckier still that the goblins that have gone missing thanks to my blade were not missed and had food, though ill fare it was.

'I am still weak and cannot make my way from this place on my own. Will you aid me? You need only take me as far as the archway to the west.''Leofwenna is alive?

'My friend, you cannot understand the joy this brings me. You have given me a great gift this day, and I shall never forget what you have done.

'Please, take this. It is a gift with much meaning, my friend.'Gadaric Munce is a member of the Eglain, a tribe of Men living in the Lone-lands. They make their livelihood by trading with travellers through the Lone-lands.Gadaric's crates can be found within the ruins of Minas Eriol, south-east of The Forsaken Inn, where Gadaric waits for your return.

In an effort to assist the Gadaric of the Eglain, you agreed to collect the trade-goods that were left behind at Minas Eriol when the goblins attacked.'I am Gadaric Munce. A member of the Eglain. I trade artifacts found in the Lone-lands to collectors in Bree in return for food and other necessities. Until recently, our trade has been brisk, but darkness has come to these lands and our people, the Eglain, face starvation and deal with murder.

'Goblins have crept into the Lone-lands and have made trade difficult; they attacked us, murdering many of our number and seized many of our trade-goods. Ruins of ancient Arnor overlook a large valley to the south-east of The Forsaken Inn. Minas Eriol, that place is called, and it was long dear to my people. Would you go there and retrieve our trade-bundles from the crates that remain and return to me?

'The crates are all that remain of our last collection.''Your efforts are much appreciated, ${CLASS}. Long ago, my people left the bustle of city-life behind, instead choosing to live our lives here in the place of our ancestors.

'We took the name Eglain -- the Forsaken -- and lived here in peace with nature, taking only what the ruins offered to us. There was peace, and we did not think to fear for it. But then... a Ranger and some others crossed the Last Bridge into the Trollshaws, and evil has spread in their wake.

'From the north came goblins and dwarves in league with Orcs -- unheard of, but true. From the south, Orcs bearing a symbol of a White Hand spread into Annunlos. All of these foes ravaged the land and drove us from the ruins where we once lived in peace.'Thornegil believes the passage of the terrible Riders has stirred the creatures of Ruddymore to madness.The creatures that live in Ruddymore act with a madness they never have before, and must be dealt with before they do harm to the folk who live nearby.Defeat creatures in Ruddymore that pose a danger to folk nearby (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thornegil is at Sarn Ford.Talk to Thornegil at Sarn Ford'The passage of the terrible riders across Ruddymore has done something to the animals that live here. Beasts that once lived peacefully alongside farms and villages are now driven to madness, attacking folk they never had before. If you wish to help us keep these lands safe, you will help us deal with these creatures that have been newly driven to madness.''The passage of the terrible riders across Ruddymore has done something to the animals that live here. Beasts that once lived peacefully alongside farms and villages are now driven to madness, attacking folk they never had before. If you wish to help us keep these lands safe, you will help us deal with these creatures that have been newly driven to madness.''Thank you for helping keep these lands safe, ${PLAYER}. Never before in my memory have we had to deal with such evil as these riders in black possess.'Rothavron has a need for stones, and he has asked you to collect them from the walls at Sarn Ford.The stone walls at Sarn Ford should not miss a few of their number.Collect stones from the walls at Sarn Ford (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rothavron is at Sarn Ford.'An anger burns within me, stranger. Many of my friends died defending the ford against the evil force that rode us down some days past, and I want those dreadful riders to face the heat of my vengeance.

'But they are not men. Whatever they might appear to be, I know in my heart they are not men. Could my sword even pierce their black cloaks, or the physical frames they must hide? I know not.

'So my anger must remain unsatisfied, and it is sorrow instead that must serve. Will you bring me stones from the walls that line the road here at Sarn Ford? I have need of them.''My anger must remain unsatisfied, and it is sorrow instead that must serve. Will you bring me stones from the walls that line the road here at Sarn Ford? I have need of them.''Thank you for these stones, friend. There is talk of building a cairn in memory of those who died at Sarn Ford, and I think it would suit the fallen for the stones to be taken from the walls here.

'It must serve... at least until the riders in black again cross my path. Then I will have my vengeance, or I will not. But at least I will be freed of my anger.'While you climbed to the peak of Weathertop, Candaith busily worked to translate the letter you recovered from Bleakrift. His translation is complete and the news he uncovered is grave.Candaith is at his camp at the foot of Weathertop.

The Ranger Candaith asked you to assist him in driving a company of Orcs from Weathertop. He told you to gather allies and return to him when you are prepared.Talk to Candaith and retake Weathertop with a fellowshipCandaith is at his camp at the foot of Weathertop.

Candaith is waiting to discuss with you the strange events that occurred atop Amon Sûl.Talk to Candaith'${PLAYER}, I have news of Radagast, but I must ask you once again for assistance here. The letter you recovered from Bleakrift speaks of the Orcs' true goals: taking control of Amon Sûl! Orc-forces are to move towards the crest of Amon Sûl under cover of night and will move in a large group, establishing small units along the paths from that larger force.

'We cannot stand against the force when they are assembled, but we can once they are splintered. They wish to establish an encampment that will allow them a vantage over the entirety of the Lone-lands. We must foil their plans, ${PLAYER}!

'Gather allies and return to me when you are ready. Together we shall scale Amon Sûl and defeat Rigûl, their leader, before he can carry out his master's will!''Do not concern yourself with the Orcs that remain. This far removed from the South-lands, they will dwindle and become only a small danger to travellers through the Lone-lands.

'We have won a pivotal battle, my friend. You have earned some rest, I think, for the great threat posed by these Orcs is now finished!

'Now, let me aide you in your endeavour to find Radagast the Brown.''If you are prepared, I will lead the way. We must be swift, else the Orcs may succeed at whatever foul mischief they have planned!'While you climbed to the peak of Weathertop, Candaith busily worked to translate the letter you recovered from Bleakrift. His translation is complete and the news he uncovered is grave.Candaith is at his camp at the foot of Weathertop.

The Ranger Candaith asked you to assist him in driving a company of Orcs from Weathertop.Talk to Candaith and retake WeathertopCandaith is at his camp at the foot of Weathertop.

Candaith is waiting to discuss with you the strange events that occurred atop Amon Sûl.Talk to Candaith'${PLAYER}, I have news of Radagast, but I must ask you once again for assistance here. The letter you recovered from Bleakrift speaks of the Orcs' true goals: taking control of Amon Sûl! Orc-forces are to move toward the crest of Amon Sûl under cover of night and will move in a large group, establishing small units along the paths from that larger force.

'We cannot stand against the force when they are assembled, but we can once they are splintered. They wish to establish a base that will allow them a vantage over the entirety of the Lone-lands. We must foil their plans, ${PLAYER}!

'Return to me when you are ready. Together we shall scale Amon Sûl and defeat Rigûl, their leader, before he can carry out his master's will!''Do not concern yourself with the Orcs that remain. This far removed from the South-lands, they will dwindle and become only a small danger to travellers through the Lone-lands.

'We have won a pivotal battle, my friend. You have earned some rest, I think, for the great threat posed by these Orcs is now finished!

'Now, let me aide you in your endeavour to find Radagast the Brown.''If you are prepared, I will lead the way. We must be swift, else the Orcs may succeed at whatever foul mischief they have planned!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest can only be attempted by solo players.</rgb>Stung by a companion's betrayal, the Captain of Gondor has decided to continue his journey alone.Meneldir stands on the road that runs into Minhiriath, contemplating Boromir's decision.Talk to Meneldir by the edge of MinhiriathMeneldir will meet you back at the east gate of Herne, where the two of you will continue your journey to find his friend Narndir.Meet up with Meneldir at the east gate of HerneMeneldir agreed to meet up with you at the north gate of Herne, where the two of you will continue your journey to find his friend Narndir.Talk to Meneldir at the east gate of Herne'I began this journey alone, and I must finish it alone.'

Meneldir seeks to protest, but Boromir holds up a hand.

'I have made up my mind. Egfrith's betrayal has opened my eyes to the sad truth that I can only rely upon myself. If the doom of Minas Tirith is truly at hand, I cannot risk casting aside this one chance to prevent it. I am sorry. It is for the best that I journey on to the hidden vale of Imladris... alone.

'I wish you both well! Use caution if you find yourself in the path of the evil power I felt at Osgiliath, the one that sent the Dead against Herne!

'I take my leave of you, though it leave my heart heavy... but I must. Farewell.''Farewell, my friends. After this I deem we will not see each other again until our journeys are done.''I wish it were otherwise, Boromir, but I respect your decision. Good luck to you on your journey, and I echo your words of caution!

'${PLAYER}, unless you too wish to leave me, I think we should return to the east gate of Herne before we continue in search of my friend Narndir, who will be able to guide us to the chieftain of the Dúnedain.''I think we should return to the east gate of Herne before we continue in search of my friend Narndir, who will be able to guide us to the chieftain of the Dúnedain.''I have been thinking about Egfrith, ${PLAYER}, and about her lies. We have already been through much, and more still lies ahead... I don't want there to be any deception between us. Secrets have a way of growing when they are left in the darkness. A friend of mine described them like mushrooms, but less delicious. Aye, he was a hobbit, although that is not the only folk who appreciate cooked mushrooms!

'But I am stalling. Of course I am.'

Meneldir sighs heavily.

'I was born in Tornhad, a settlement of the Dúnedain in the Angle of Mitheithel, and I was one of that folk. I grew up playing in the woods and caves nearby, and when I came of age I swore the oath they all did: to protect the line of the king, as had been done for centuries, and to do as the chieftain of the Dúnedain and his trusted lieutenants commanded in all things.

'I failed in this, ${PLAYER}. I was commanded to keep watch on a certain house, for it was feared that trolls had come down from the mountains and might endanger the folk who lived there. I let my watchfulness lapse, and on an overcast day the trolls moved much further than anyone expected and a family was slain. I had broken my oath, and they suffered the consequence of my failure. I have not considered myself a Dúnadan since that day.

'Narndir and a few others promised to speak on my behalf to the chieftain, but my own shame burned like a fire, and I fled. I would not return to these lands unless our warning was dire... and it is. So now you know, ${PLAYER}. Judge me as you will, but the lesson of Egfrith has shown to me that it is better you know.'Meneldir is ready to continue his journey to find the Dúnedain in Bree-land.Meneldir has asked you to continue northward along the Greenway in the direction of Bree-land.Travel north along the Greenway in the direction of Bree-landMeneldir has come to a halt on the Greenway.Talk to Meneldir on the GreenwayMeneldir told you he felt eyes upon the two of you. Spies?What has Meneldir discovered?Search the surrounding area with MeneldirWhat has Meneldir discovered?Learn what Meneldir has discoveredThe body of a Ranger lies in the brush.Talk to Meneldir by the body'We can stay on the Greenway for another day's travel before we reach the ruins at which Narndir makes his camp. Our warning that Saruman has betrayed the good folk of Middle-earth must reach the chieftain of the Dúnedain, and Narndir will know where he can be found. It will not be a pleasing reunion for me... but it must be done.

'The road leaves Herne from the eastern side but circles the town to the north, and that is the way we should go.''Our warning that Saruman has betrayed the good folk of Middle-earth must reach the chieftain of the Dúnedain, and Narndir will know where he can be found. It will not be a pleasing reunion for me... but it must be done.

'The road leaves Herne from the eastern side but circles the town to the north, and that is the way we should go.''Do you sense that? I feel eyes watching us.''I feel eyes upon us, ${PLAYER}. Someone watches us, and I am put in mind of what Egfrith wrote: that she tracked Boromir for many days as he journeyed across the empty lands.

'No, I do not think she has returned to plague our steps; she was intent on reaching Saruman with what she learned of Boromir when she left us. But this might be another spy.

'See what you can find nearby, my friend. Perhaps some clue will identify this unseen watcher for us.''See what you can find nearby, my friend. Perhaps some clue will identify this unseen watcher for us.''Are these feathery spies or only carrion birds?''No... no! What cruel jest is this?'

Meneldir stares in horror at the body of a Ranger lying in the grass.

'This is the man we seek, ${PLAYER}. This is Narndir.'The body of a slain Ranger has revealed that something terrible has happened to the Dúnedain in Cardolan.The body of Narndir lies off the road in Cardolan. What happened to this friend of Meneldir?Examine the body of NarndirMeneldir stands by the body of his slain friend, filled with grief, anger, and confusion.Talk to Meneldir by the bodyWhat happened at the river-crossing of Sarn Ford to result in the death of Narndir, so far from his usual dwelling-place?Travel to Sarn Ford and learn what transpired thereThe Ranger Halbarad shouts commands to his kin at Sarn Ford.Talk to Halbarad at Sarn Ford'I don't understand it! We are still some days' travel from the camp where Narndir made his home, studying the ruins of Cardolan. He was always writing in that journal of his, documenting the details of artifacts and architecture he found worthy of note. We used to gather pigments together, he and I, as children in the Angle, for even then he exhausted such quantities of ink that he was always running out. Narndir...'

Meneldir clenches his hands into fists.

'Can I ask you to examine his body, ${PLAYER}? I cannot bring myself to do it, but we must know the manner of his death.''Can I ask you to examine his body, ${PLAYER}? I cannot bring myself to do it, but we must know the manner of his death.'It is apparent that Narndir has been dead for a number of days. He suffered several sword-strokes to the back, and from impressions in the dirt around his body it appears he was ridden down by someone on horseback as he fled.'I don't understand it. Narndir should not have been here at all, ${PLAYER}. What brought him so far from his usual dwelling-place, and who would intend to harm him at all? Furthermore, he was no coward... to what foe would he turn his back?'

Nearly without pause, Meneldir answers his own question.

'Aye, ${PLAYER}, we know the answer already: the creeping shadows Aberthol saw that night in Herne, one of which sat astride a great black horse. I named the creatures, once, and now I do so again: Nazgûl. Such a foe would inspire fear in even the stoutest of the Dúnedain, and such a foe might even ride down a fleeing man to slay him with a sword-stroke to the back. It would be as nothing to such a creature.

'Sarn Ford is to the west of here, and it seems that might be the site from which Narndir fled. There have at times been a few Rangers at the ford there, though never in great number. I think we should divert our course to Sarn Ford and learn what might have befallen its defenders.'Meneldir says a few quiet words by the body of his friend, and you give him the courtesy of not listening to them.'I have not met you before, ${RACE}, but if you are a friend to the Rangers of the North, you are welcome here, though we are sorely-pressed.

'But I do know your companion, and I am surprised he has returned to show his face in these lands.'Gadaric Munce has asked for vengeance to be meted out to the goblins now infesting Minas Eriol. They are responsible for the death of many of Gadaric's people, and he has offered rewards for their destruction.Goblins can be found north-east in the shadow of Weathertop or in the ruins of Minas Eriol, south-east of the Forsaken Inn.

Gadaric Munce wants to collect the bounty on the scabbards carried by goblins in the Lone-lands.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Bounty quests are repeatable quests, but have a finite number of completions. This quest can be completed three times.</rgb>'My charge is clear: revisit a measured vengeance upon the goblins. Still, I feel this is not enough.

'The goblins are responsible for the deaths of many. I want to revisit upon them what they wrought upon us. I will pay you in trade and recognition among my people if you are willing to assist me in this endeavour.

'Any goblin from the north-east of this encampment to the depths of Minas Eriol will suffice as many will carry the proof that I desire: scabbards, leather scabbards used to house their murderous daggers. Seek out the goblins in those places and claim their scabbards. Return them to me, and I will see that you receive a fitting reward.''You have brought a number of goblin-scabbards to me, friend? Very good! I am a man of my word and will pay my debt as I promised! These will do well to ease the pain of our losses and will be used to remind our people that there are still ways to strike out at the evil spreading through these lands.'Goblins in the ruins of Minas Eriol, south-east of The Forsaken Inn, cause a great deal of trouble for people at the inn. Lieva Dourlily is sewing a scarecrow intended to frighten away the goblins.There are goblins in the ruins of Minas Eriol, to the south-east of the Forsaken Inn, and on the fields of Annunlos.

To finish her scarecrow, Lieva Dourlily requires some of the tabards worn by the goblins of Annunlos, but no one at The Forsaken Inn is brave enough to risk themselves to collect the cloth for her. She has asked you to gather the tabards instead.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Bounty quests are repeatable quests, but have a finite number of completions. This quest can be completed three times.</rgb>'I'm not just the server and maid here at The Forsaken Inn, I'm also a fair hand with needle and thread. At the moment, Anlaf has me making a special treat for the goblins of Annunlos.

'I've been trying to finish this terrible, little scarecrow for weeks, but there are none here willing to risk their lives against the goblins to bring me what I need to complete it. I have all the pieces I need except cloth from enough goblin-tabards to complete the scarecrow.

'If you are willing, go to the ruins of Minas Eriol to the south-east or to the plains of Annunlos and bring me some tabards from the goblins there so I can finish this dreadful thing.''I have little more to offer you than my thanks. My scarecrow will stand outside The Forsaken Inn as a warning to the goblins of Annunlos; they'll think twice when they see that we've made such a mockery of them!

'Thank you for all you have done, ${CLASS}.'Candac Brightwood buys pottery and other trinkets from the Munces at The Forsaken Inn, but they are having trouble gathering enough pieces with the goblins creeping into the Lone-lands.Ancient bits of pottery may be found in the valley beneath the ruins of Minas Eriol, south-east of The Forsaken Inn. Candac Brightwood is at The Forsaken Inn.

Candac Brightwood has asked you to search for bits of pottery with which he can continue his trade.Collect pottery shards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Candac Brightwood is at The Forsaken Inn.

Candac Brightwood has asked you to search for bits of pottery with which he can continue his trade.'I will tell you of my troubles, traveller, and see if you can lend me some assistance. The Munces supply me with pottery and other trinkets found throughout the ruins in the Lone-lands. They count themselves folk of these lands, these Eglain, and they depend on such trade for their livelihood. Goblins have overrun the ruins where they are accustomed to gathering the pieces! They cannot collect the pottery shards I require, and I find my deliveries impossible to fulfil! If I cannot send a delivery of pottery to Mrs. Sandheaver in the town of Bree, I will not be paid!

'There is a valley beneath the ruins of Minas Eriol, south-east of The Forsaken Inn, where wolves have been known to prowl. There may be some shattered bits of pottery there and perhaps some whole pieces, as well. If you can bring me some, I will see you paid for your efforts.

'Do be careful... the Lone-lands never cease to be dangerous, especially in these troubled times.''Have you found those pottery-bits I asked for?''You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}. With these fragments of pottery, I will be able to continue my trade; I and my customers, such as the vocal Mrs. Sandheaver, will each be happy!

'I do fear for the Eglain, though. Scavengers they may be, but they have a right to make a living as they see fit, and the increase in danger across the Lone-lands threatens that right.

'If need be, I could return to Bree and take up my father's axe and become a woodsman. For the people living here in this unforgiving stretch of the wild, there is no such work to be found.'Tobold Leafcutter learned of ancient relics which the Men of Cardolan buried with their dead in the Barrow-downs, suggesting they might be of some use in defending against creatures such as the barrow-wights.The Great Barrow is at the heart of the Barrow-downs, beyond the Dead Man's Perch in the southern Barrow-downs.

Tobold Leafcutter has asked you to gather ancient chalices from the Great Barrow located in the heart of the Barrow-downs.'Greetings again, ${PLAYER}! I have been studying the materials you brought me from the Barrow-downs and have learned much more since last we spoke.

'I would ask one last favour of you. This journal that you found for me contains a vast amount of knowledge. Through it, I have learned that the Men of Cardolan buried many relics with their dead -- it may be that some of these things may be made to help defend against such creatures as the barrow-wights.  

'The journal speaks of chalices that were buried in the Great Barrow itself. I wonder if you could go there and gather some for me. I am sure there are many of them deep inside the catacombs of the Great Barrow.''I confess that I was half-afraid you would not return! You found the chalices? Ah, wonderful!

'I cannot say that these would help at all against such dread fiends as the barrow-wights, but they might still have some value -- strictly historical, of course! I will take these at once to someone who might find them of interest.'Lieva Dourlily has asked for a collection of coarse skins to be delivered to her at The Forsaken Inn.You accepted a task from Lieva Dourlily to acquire coarse skins from the beasts in the Lone-lands. You can find Lieva Dourlily within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Coarse skin necessary to make tougher gloves for workers here in the Lone-lands. The skins can be found from beasts that wander the Lone-lands to the east. Deliver to Lieva Dourlily at The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Coarse skin necessary to make tougher gloves for workers in the Lone-lands.'This is exactly what I was looking for...they will make excellent gloves.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gadaric Munce of the Eglain offers favour with his people if one can collect jagged beaks from the local crebain.You accepted a task to acquire jagged beaks for Gadaric Munce. They can be found on the gore-crows which flock along the road east, beneath the shadow of Weathertop. You can find Gadaric within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the gore-crows watching the roads to the east. The vile crebain are enemies of the Eglain, and if you deliver the beaks to me, Gadaric Munce, at The Forsaken Inn, you will earn our favour.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the gore-crows watching the roads to the east.'So often these foes have torn the flesh from our people. This effort will not go unrewarded.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gadaric Munce of the Eglain offers favour with his people if one can collect tough carapaces from the spiders in the Lone-lands.You accepted a task from Gadaric Munce to acquire tough carapaces from the spiders within the Lone-lands. You can find Gadaric within The Forsaken Inn.Wanted: Tough carapaces from the spiders of the Lone-lands. Deliver them to me, Gadaric Munce, at The Forsaken Inn, you will earn our favour.Wanted: Tough carapaces from the spiders of the Lone-lands.'We are not very good with weapons, though some of us were warriors once, and we have long waited to see the spiders fall.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Falster the Fox, a denizen of The Forsaken Inn, needs assistance acquiring certain curiosities for Ollie Redbrush of Bree.Coins and heirlooms can be found in the Arnorian rubble located in the valley beneath Minas Eriol, south-east of The Forsaken Inn. Wolves also stalk the valley.

Falster the Fox, a trader working for a man named Ollie Redbrush, has asked you to collect some old coins and heirlooms for him. Falster wants you to hunt the wolves there for teeth that may help to make up for the delay of his delivery to Ollie Redbrush.'I make my living trading with the patrons of The Forsaken Inn... or rather, I used to, until the goblins arrived! Their presence strikes fear into my suppliers and has made trade impossible. Sadly, that doesn't remove my obligation to Ollie Redbrush, my employer in Bree-town!

'A vale beneath the ruins of Minas Eriol, south-east of The Forsaken Inn, contains the coins and heirlooms I need, and more! Wolves inhabit the valley, and Ollie may forgive me the delay if I include some pristine wolf-teeth with the shipment.

'If you assist me in collecting these curiosities, I will ensure that you are paid for the favour! Make your way to the valley beneath Minas Eriol and obtain coins, heirlooms, and wolf-teeth for me.''Ollie will be pleased with these curiosities, for certain, and the wolf-teeth should help sweeten the deal.

'I must admit I was not certain that you would succeed! The arrival of the goblins has done much to change the nature of my work here in the Lone-lands.

'You have earned this reward, friend, and I hope you find this sufficient. I know that Redbrush will be pleased with this delivery.'Candaith directed you to an inn along the Great East-West Road and told you that the people there may be in need of assistance as much has happened in the past few weeks.Make your way to The Forsaken Inn. It can be found west along the the Great East Road, south of Candaith's camp.

Candaith advised you to seek out the inhabitants of an Inn in the Lone-lands to offer your aid to them.Talk to the proprietor of the Forsaken Inn'To the south, over the hill and through the vale that lay just beyond my camp, you must take care to avoid goblins. Their number has grown swiftly in recent days. Beyond them, a good distance south you will find a road stretching east and west. This is the Great East Road.

'Follow it west, towards Bree, and over a ruined bridge you will find a crumbling and nearly collapsed inn...The Forsaken Inn as it is called. The owner therein and his workers and guests will likely need assistance as this part of the world has changed greatly in recent weeks.

'Do not tell them I sent you...only offer your aid. They will likely and sorely need it.''More strangers passing through? No? Well then, if you aim on staying you might want to find a nice parcel of the floor to take up. The inn is dangerously close to full for some reason. I am not complaining -- coin is coin, even if there is little to spend it on in these parts.''Remember, do not speak of me. Head west along the Great East Road after you find it a fair distance to the south beyond the shadow of Weathertop. You will see the inn beyond the ruins of an ancient bridge.'Poor Reginard has been down on his luck, but he's determined to cook a meal for you.While Reginard finds more kindling, you should collect mushrooms in the surrounding marsh.Collect mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected enough mushrooms for Reginard.

You should return to him.You should sit by Reginard as he cooks.You should wait for Reginard to finish cooking.Wait for Reginard to finish cookingThe food seems to be done cooking.

You should talk to Reginard.Talk to Reginard'Well, no use letting that dampen my spirits! I'll just try again.

'In the meantime, would you mind harvesting some mushrooms in the marsh? I assure you, the ones that grow here are quite safe.''Perfect! If you'd like, you can sit with me. It'd be nice to have the company as I cook!

'This reminds me of the time when boars ate all of my food! I'd gotten lost somewhere north of Bree....''...and that's how a few of the Big Folk helped me out of a deep hole in the middle of nowhere. You should have seen the look on their faces!

'Ah, there we go! I must say, these mushrooms are some of the best I've cooked yet. I hope you like them!

'By the way... thank you for joining me. I used to venture out here with a close friend of mine -- the one who gave me that lucky clover. He's... no longer around to share in this tradition, but I'm glad you could. Maybe I'm not so unlucky after all!'Lieva Dourlily asked you to keep a watchful eye out for a goblin leading not only the goblins, but the many Wargs that bolster their number.Seek out the goblin, Lûz, at the goblin-camps north-east of The Forsaken Inn.

Lieva Dourlily told you to be wary of the goblin pack-master, Lûz, and requested that you defeat him if you find him.Defeat LûzLieva Dourlily is at The Forsaken Inn, south-west of the goblin-camps.

You found and defeated the goblin pack-master Lûz. Now you only need to return to Lieva Dourlily.Talk to Lieva Dourlilly at The Forsaken Inn'Since you are headed north-east into the goblin infested hills and vales, I think I ought to ask you one more favour and issue you one more warning.

'There are Wargs among the goblins, and a good many of them. Old Mugwort -- a dear friend of mine and the cook here at The Forsaken Inn -- hates them fiercely. I hate the goblins, though, and seeing that I do, I want you to be mindful of the pack-master, Lûz.

'If you see him, steel your nerve and grab your steel. It will be a difficult battle, but you should defeat him. When you do, return to me with news of the fight!''Ha! What great news to know that such a menace has been dealt with so swiftly! You have proven that you are truly a good ally to the people in this inn. I wonder if there is any more that I can have you do....''I like hounds. Hounds can be friendly, and they say were once noble beasts. But these Wargs are big hulking beasts, larger and meaner than other wolves. We cannot allow the pack-master, Lûz, to continuing raising these beasts! Get out there and deal with him.'The goblins in Annunlos used Wargs, evil beasts of war, to hunt members of the Eglain in the ruins of Minas Eriol. Gadaric wants vengeance on the foul beasts for the murders of his people.Wargs can be found among the ruins of Minas Eriol to the south-east of The Forsaken Inn.

Gadaric Munce believes that taking the tails of the Wargs who murdered his people will renew the will of the Eglain and humiliate the Wargs and their goblin-masters.'My people suffered such horrors as Wargs tore through the ruins before the lashes of their masters. Ferocious and unyielding, the beasts devoured many members of my tribe, even as they lay dying.

'The beasts must be punished, and my people must find their strength again. The Wargs dwell now among the ruins of Minas Eriol, the home they stole from my people! We must humiliate the enemy, take their tails, and show them they are weak.

'Hunt down the Wargs, collect their tails, and bring them to me so I can see smiles once more upon the faces of those few that remain of my people!''This was a fitting end for such vicious creatures as these cruel Wargs. My people will be heartened to know they have allies like you in the Lone-lands. We will find some way to repay our debt to you, ${PLAYER}.'The Ranger Candaith has enlisted your aid in a quest to remove the threat of Orcs from the Weather Hills. Candaith believes that the presence of Orcs from the South upon Weathertop is a precursor to an invasion of Eriador.You have arrived at the foot of Weathertop to discover that Orcs from the South have made their way to the crest. Candaith awaits your assistance in driving the war-band from the ruins of Amon Sûl.Speak with CandaithA small force of goblins greeted you at the foot of Weathertop. Candaith has sent you upon the path to the left, while he will clear the path to the right, meeting once again upon the crest of Weathertop.Speak with CandaithCandaith sent you up one branch of the path up Weathertop, while he took the other, in search of the leaders of the Orc war-band.Somewhere above you on the path, Candaith waits to speak with you again.

You have defeated two of the leaders of the Orc war-band.Speak with Candaith at Weathertop's peakYou have found the leader of Saruman's expedition, the Uruk Rigûl. You must defeat Rigûl and his minions to break their will and drive them from the Weather Hills.Defeat RigûlDefeat Rigûl's minionsThe Uruk-hai expedition lies in ruins. You have won the day. You should speak with Candaith.Speak with Candaith'The path diverges ahead. I shall follow the trail to the right, you follow the path to the left. If all goes well, we will meet again upon the peak of Weathertop. Wait until I have passed from sight before you proceed.

'Remember, we must defeat the leaders of this war-band if we are to discourage them. Use the torches you find on the unlit pyres along the path. They may draw the attention of nearby Orcs.''My wounds are grievous, but I shall recover. Come, we shall return to my camp.''It's time for you to go.''We must make haste, ${PLAYER}. We have the leaders of these Uruk-hai cornered like rats upon the slopes of Weathertop. If we can defeat them, we may yet break the will of this war-band and drive them whimpering to their masters in the South. Be prepared for anything.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}, better than I, it appears. Uruk-hai and Orcs nearly bested me and my wounds run deep.

