<rgb=#FF0000>Small fellowship recommended.</rgb>You have defeated a number of crebain from Nan Wathren.The town of Herne is safer now that the Grey Fear has been defeated. But will Mayor Nevit understand the part his daughter played in the downfall of an evil that has plagued the country for hundreds of years?Rhiangar has asked you to tell Mayor Nevit that you found his wife's body outside of Sarch Vorn.

Mayor Nevit is inside the Crossway Inn, in Herne.Talk to Mayor Nevit'What you found at Sarch Vorn brought me no joy, but it did provide some peace. Please, tell my father what you found there as well. He deserves to know that she did not abandon us on purpose.

'Father is sitting by the door. He apparently helped himself to the stew while we were gone as there is none left.'

She chuckles softly, shaking her head.'You again, eh? What mischief have you and my daughter been up to?'

You tell him of Sarch Vorn, Luilloth, and the Grey Fear. Then you tell him of the weathered skeleton. He is silent for a very long time.

'Her necklace? So... she did not leave us after all? It was an accident?'

He sighs deeply.

'You and Rhiangar did all of that and you helped to cook my favourite stew as well. I... I am afraid that I have been unkind to you, ${CLASS}. For so many years, anger has been the only thing I have known. But today, today you brought hope.

'You and Rhiangar have shown that history is important, but that dwelling on the past is not what is needed. Using what we learn from our history here, in the present, is what is important.

'That, and the people we have. Luilloth lost her life to avenge her family and keep Cardolan safe, as did my wife. You and my daughter followed in their footsteps, and yet surpassed what they were able to do. Rhiangar was never chasing some silly dream, nor was her mother. They both knew there was danger, and they took action to meet it. I am... sorry, and grateful. You will always be welcome here, in Herne, and in our home. I thank you.'The Greenway is an ancient road from Bree-land, north of Cardolan, to Tharbad in Cardolan's southern region. Travel has always been difficult along its broken stones, but even more so in recent days, thanks to the dangerous spirits that haunt the ruins along the roadside.Coel has asked you to defeat wights in the ruins along the Greenway.

Wights can be found in Dol Ernil and Gond Orchal, in north-eastern Cardolan.Defeat Wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should let Coel know that you have defeated many wights.

Coel is in the caravan, south of Andrath in northern Cardolan.Return to Coel'Brigands may have landed us where we are now, but our way south is impeded as well. There are ruins on either side of the Greenway, and of late they have become infested with the risen Dead. If you could put these spirits back to rest, it would aid us immensely in our travels.''I must admit, I did not expect you to return. ...Ah, so quickly, that is.

'Truly our progress is blessed to have had someone like you come along.'Since long before Orcs and undead crept into the Lone-lands, the Eglain has had to deal with the menace of spiders dwelling within the ruins across the lonely stretch of Eriador. Now as the spiders are driven from the depths of their ruined homes they creep closer and closer to Ost Guruth and threaten the Eglain who dwell there.Spiders dwell in the shade of Amon Ros, west of Ost Guruth.

With undeniable hatred towards spiders, Tortwil asked you to enter the ruins of Amon Ros to strike at the vile creatures dwelling there.Defeat spiders in Amon Ros (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tortwil awaits your return at Ost Guruth, east of Amon Ros.

You should return to Tortwil and tell him of the destruction wrought upon the spiders. He should be pleased with your success.'No doubt you've seen spindly crawlers weaving their webs and trapping their prey, which happens to be anything that has life within its veins. Crawling monstrosities is what they are! Nothing good comes from them, and I have seen too many fall prey to their hidden nests.

'These foul vermin do not deserve the life they take so freely. Bring my vengeance upon them!''I have not been wholly forthcoming with you, ${PLAYER}, but I was afraid you might not be as honourable as rumour tells. But I shall now tell you my tale, so that you may understand my hatred.

'I was at Naerost when the goblins came. My son and I fled as the Wargs and goblins poured into the ruins, slaughtering all who could not escape them. My son stumbled at the edge of a ruin and slipped into the chasm below. I struggled to reach him, but there was no hope, the spiders were upon him nearly as soon as he touched their webs.

'They took my son from me as they took his mother many years back. My heart is heavy with shame for not telling you the truth.''Filthy creatures! Snatching the life from whatever they ensnare. They deserve death.'While hunting down the half-orcs, you discovered a necklace bearing an inscription and the mark of the Eglain.You discovered a necklace upon one of the half-orcs in the central Lone-lands. Perhaps you can ask some of the Eglain if they know who the true owner of the necklace could be.At first glance, this looks like a piece of worthless jewellery, but upon further inspection you discovered an inscription engraved on the back of the pendant. 

'I will ever and always love you.

- D'

Beneath this inscription is carved a symbol of the Eglain. Perhaps someone of the Eglain can assist you with finding the owner of this necklace?Daegwalt's face contorts briefly in a look of anguish, before he fights back the pain and issues a smile.

'This is my wife's. I gave it to her before we came to live with the Eglain. It was lost when she was struck down at Naerost. 

'You have given me something that I cannot truly repay, but I will give you something of import to me in an effort to repay you.'Blood-eye, a shadow beast of Fornost, roams near with his pack of snarling barghests. You should drive off the deadly Blood-eye.Blood-eye roams the south-eastern fields of Fornost.

You should defeat Blood-eye and his pack of barghests.Defeat Blood-eyeBlood-eye roams the south-eastern fields of Fornost.

You should defeat Blood-eye and his pack of barghests.Defeat Blood-eyeYou have defeated the evil Blood-eye, silencing the chilling howls that haunt the southern Fields of Fornost.CompletedBlood-eye, a shadow beast of Fornost, roams near with his pack of snarling barghests. You should drive off the deadly Blood-eye.You have defeated the evil Blood-eye, silencing the chilling howls that haunt the southern Fields of Fornost.Goblins and half-orcs speak in hushed tones about something called the Grey Fear, an apparent servant of the Witch King responsible for some great evil in the years following the fall of Arnor.Malenfang is at Scurloc Farm, waiting to hear what you learned from the papers you deciphered.Talk to Malenfang at Scurloc FarmHandarod is at Sírlond, across the river from Caranost, and he may know someone who might be able to tell you about the Grey Fear.Talk to Handarod at SírlondNarndir kept his journals at a secret place in the South Downs, but Handarod was trusted with its location. The hiding spot is beneath one of the eastern towers of Caranost, in a hollow place beneath the tower.Find the hiding spot for Narndir's journal in a hollow place beneath the easternmost tower of CaranostSearch the hiding spot for Narndir's journalYou have recovered Narndir's journal, in which he recorded many thoughts about the history of Arnor and the kingdoms that followed after.Read Narndir's journalNarndir's journal describes the throne room of Caranost, located at the highest point of the ruined city.

You should travel to the location described by the journal, and read the appropriate pages while you are there.Narndir's writing is so evocative you can almost imagine King Tarondor II is present in the throne room he established at Caranost following the breaking of Arnor.Consider the image of King Tarondor IINarndir's journal contains an account of the Battle of the Deadmount, and describes a clear path on the northern side of the hill that permits travellers to get quite near the monument atop Amon Firn.

You should travel to the location described by the journal, and read the appropriate pages while you are there.Narndir's writing has brought important figures of Cardolan's past to your imagination at the site of the Battle of the Deadmount, Amon Firn.Consider the image of King ArtandilNarndir's writing has brought important figures of Cardolan's past to your imagination at the site of the Battle of the Deadmount, Amon Firn.Consider the image of Amondir, Lord of Tyrn GorthadNarndir's journal describes a location in the south-eastern reaches of Tyrn Gorthad.

You should travel to the location described by the journal, and read the appropriate pages while you are there.Narndir's writing speaks of terrible deeds done by forces of great evil among the barrows of Tyrn Gorthad.Consider the image of the Witch-kingNarndir's writing speaks of terrible deeds done by forces of great evil among the barrows of Tyrn Gorthad.Consider the image of AmondirNarndir's writing speaks of terrible deeds done by forces of great evil among the barrows of Tyrn Gorthad.Witness Prince Amondir's final adventureMeneldir stands nearby, gazing out at the barrows of Tyrn Gorthad.'This has given us a lot to think about, but I am most concerned about these mentions of something called the Grey Fear. Is it a person, a spirit, or something else? Whatever it may have been, if it is responsible in some way for the fall of Cardolan or the breaking of Arnor, we need to prevent it from working a similar evil against us today!

'What does Malenfang have to say about this?''What has Malenfang to say about this?''The Grey Fear? No, I have never heard of anything by that name. Is it possible it might be a disease, or an ailment of some kind?

'I seem to remember there was a scholar among the Dúnedain who dwelt among the ruins of Old Cardolan, though his name escapes me. Handarod may know of his whereabouts. He was encamped outside Caranost, was he not? Perhaps you should return and ask him.''I seem to remember there was a scholar among the Dúnedain who dwelt among the ruins of Old Cardolan, though his name escapes me. Handarod may know of his whereabouts. He was encamped outside Caranost, was he not? Perhaps you should return and ask him.''Someone or something called the Grey Fear, you say? I have not heard of such a one being involved with the ruination of the old kingdom, but I am no scholar. You should speak with Narndir, if you can find him. He always possessed a keen interest in the history of Cardolan, and was always to be seen writing in one of his many journals.'

Something in your expression brings Handarod to a halt, and he stares at you questioningly. Reluctantly, you tell him that Narndir was slain by Black Riders during the flight from Sarn Ford, and that you and Meneldir discovered his body just off the road north-west of Herne.

'I am very sorry to hear that, but especially so for Meneldir; I know they were close. Narndir never had a bad word to say about Meneldir, even after he left us. It was not so for many others.'

Handarod idly drums his fingers upon the hilt of his sword.

'Perhaps Narndir can still help us, ${PLAYER}. I told you of the journals he kept. Well, I know at least one place where he hid some of them. To the east, beneath one of the towers of Caranost, there was a hollow place. Search there and see if Narndir recorded anything of this Grey Fear in the years before he perished.''Perhaps Narndir can still help us, ${PLAYER}. I told you of the journals he kept. Well, I know at least one place where he hid some of them. To the east, beneath one of the towers of Caranost, there was a hollow place. Search there and see if Narndir recorded anything of this Grey Fear in the years before he perished.''Narndir's journal describes the throne room in Caranost? That is located very high up, at the highest point of the ruined city. You should go there and read what Narndir had to say about it.''Narndir's journal describes the throne room in Caranost? That is located very high up, at the highest point of the ruined city. You should go there and read what Narndir had to say about it.''This is the throne room of Caranost. What does Narndir have to say about it, ${PLAYER}?''Narndir's journal describes the throne room in Caranost? That is located very high up, at the highest point of the ruined city. You should go there and read what Narndir had to say about it.''You will think I have taken leave of my senses,' Narndir writes, 'but after so much study I feel as if I have come to know these figures of the ancient past. When they suffer, I suffer. When they mourn, I mourn. And when they triumph... oh, no one could understand the joy I feel at their triumphs, few though they might have been in those dark years!

'I feel as if I know them: Amlaith the Builder, once Prince of Cardolan and then later Prince of Arthedain at Fornost. Eldamacil, Prince of Rhudaur, kin-slayer and accursed.

'And Prince Tarondor, with whom I feel a close bond! The third and youngest son of King Eärendur of Arnor was doubted not only by his brothers but also by his own people. Those who lived in Cardolan during his rule as prince called him Tarondor the Afterthought, but it was an epithet he would overmaster. When corrupt nobles promised plunder to the hill-folk tribes in the south if they rose up against the prince, he rode forth and vanquished the invaders. Tales of his valour grew, and Tarondor the Afterthought became Tarondor the Forthright, beloved by his people.

'So it was that when Eärendur, King of Arnor and father to these three princes, died of an illness without naming a successor, there was more than one claimant to the throne. There might have been blood spilt then, as there would be later, but Amlaith suggested a compromise. "Let the kingdom of Arnor be divided," he said to his brothers, "with each of us to rule a province in friendship and alliance with each other."

'Tarondor then became not a prince but a king, and he was known as King Tarondor II, for there had already been a Tarondor in the line. As the King of Cardolan he ordered a throne built and a crown forged at Caranost, and the fortress became the capital and the new ruling seat of the young kingdom. And even now as I write these words, I feel tears welling in the corner of my eye, for I see the seed of treachery and death growing in the very foundation, and know what next must transpire.''Narndir writes of a clear path leading up the northern side of Amon Firn? When you are near the top of the hill you should read the corresponding account in his journal.''What does Narndir write concerning the Battle of the Deadmount? Oh that he were here to tell us in person!''Narndir writes of a clear path up the northern face of the Deadmount? That is Amon Firn, ${PLAYER}... a dangerous place, far to the east.''Narndir writes of a clear path leading up the northern side of Amon Firn? When you are near the top of the hill you should read the corresponding account in his journal.''Arthedain, Cardolan, and Rhudaur were enmeshed in bloody fighting for near all the years of their existence,' writes Narndir, 'but few battles had such a cost as the Battle of the Deadmount.

'Four hundred years after hated Eldamacil slew his brother Tarondor, another king of Rhudaur marched deep into the kingdom of Cardolan. King Malgun it was who broke the gates of Caranost at the head of an army of hill-folk. Much of the royal family was slain on that day, but Malgun was unable to find King Artandil; sensing his strength was near to over-reach, the King of Rhudaur and his armies fled across the South Downs.

'Maddened by his grief, King Artandil pursued them, and at last the soldiers of Cardolan caught up to the fleeing Rhudaurrim at a hill among the barrows. It is said they fought so fiercely that blood ran down the sides of the hill in great rivulets, and bodies filled the gullies at its feet, and when the fighting was done both Artandil and Malgun were dead, each having dealt the death-blow to each other.

'The hill is known today as the Deadmount. Red flowers bloomed there ever after, and do to this day. I picked one of these flowers on an overcast afternoon, when the sky was grey and it seemed rain must soon fall, but it never did. I enclose the flower within my journal; it will be a reminder to me that there can be beauty even in sadness.'

A faded red blossom is tucked between two leaves of Narndir's journal, and it crinkles as you turn the pages.'Narndir writes of a clear path leading up the northern side of Amon Firn? When you are near the top of the hill you should read the corresponding account in his journal.''After King Artandil fell at the Deadmount,' Narndir writes, 'it was Amondir, the Lord of Tyrn Gorthad and Keeper of the Barrows, who rallied what remained of the Cardoladhrim host. Crying the King's name as he charged, Amondir rode down the fleeing men of Rhudaur. The son of Malgun was captured and later ransomed, and a costly peace between the kingdoms was purchased.

'The crown of Cardolan was offered to Amondir then, but he refused it. He would not name himself king, and instead he took the title of Prince; he would rule from Dol Ernil, his own keep in Tyrn Gorthad. The primacy of Cardolan waned, and Elendil's line now only held true in Arthedain. Amondir rode to Fornost to offer his fealty, and with the reunification of Arthedain and Cardolan beneath Argeleb, the newly-crowned King of Arnor, there was once again hope for the line of Elendil and the troubled kingdoms of the Dúnedain.

'But it would not last, for there was a power behind Rhudaur's evil, and it walked in secret.''The south-eastern reaches of Tyrn Gorthad? What might Narndir write of that fell place?''What does Narndir have to tell us about Tyrn Gorthad? I almost dread to hear it.''Narndir writes of a place in the south-eastern reaches of Tyrn Gorthad? It seems we must return to the Greenway, ${PLAYER}. From there we can climb up to Tyrn Gorthad, but it has been a forsaken place for hundreds of years.''The south-eastern reaches of Tyrn Gorthad? What might Narndir write of that fell place?''After Rhudaur's most recent defeat,' writes Narndir, 'the armies of the Witch-king required time to recover and rebuild. During that time, their evil master turned to sorcery to bedevil his enemies. In secret he came to the barrows and walked among them, whispering to the dead. Unknown to those living nearby, long-buried lords and kings stirred in their tombs, infested with fell spirits out of Angmar's darkest pits. At the Witch-king's bidding they emerged, legions of the Dead moving silently through the fog, preying on any who strayed too near.''The south-eastern reaches of Tyrn Gorthad? What might Narndir write of that fell place?''The plague of the Dead worsened,' writes Narndir, 'and even the children of Cardolan were not safe from it: countless children were stolen from their beds, or vanished from within otherwise sturdy and unyielding walls.

'Desperate to put an end to this nightmare, Prince Amondir set forth on a quest to find the cause of the Dead's unrest. He was the Lord of Tyrn Gorthad, after all, the Keeper of the Barrows. What had he to fear from the mist that settled upon the burial mounds? Was it not his people who were buried there? Were they not his own ancestors, his own kind, his own subjects?

'If he could not ease their burden and restore their restful peace, who could? Even as I write this, I find myself hoping, beyond hope, that the Lord of Tyrn Gorthad could put an end to it. But this has been my lifelong study, and I know how this tale ends... and it does not end with Amondir, for all his courage and his duty.''It is hard to listen to the words of my friend Narndir, and know he will write no more. I will keep this journal in memory of him, and I would do so even had we no need of the knowledge contained within!'

You hand the journal to Meneldir and he flips through its pages.

'...was never seen again,' he reads, and turns the page. He looks up at you, puzzled.

'What happened to the rest of Narndir's account? There are several pages missing here. Even if that is the end of Amondir's story, there must have surely been more for Narndir to write! See, the account resumes here, beyond the missing pages. But what was removed, and who removed it?'The Dúnedain of Cardolan have posted a task to glossy essences.You have accepted a task to acquire glossy essences.Wanted: Glossy essences from the Dead who have returned to trouble the lands of Cardolan. Those who deliver these trophies will receive modest compensation from the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Wanted: Glossy essences from the Dead who have returned to trouble the lands of Cardolan. Those who deliver these trophies will receive modest compensation from the Dúnedain of Cardolan.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Dúnedain of Cardolan have posted a task to collect large finger bones.You have accepted a task to acquire large finger bones.Wanted: Large finger bones from the barrow-wights stalking Tyrn Gorthad in Cardolan. Claim them from their wretched hands—be they attached or not, and beware those who crawl with their fingers beneath the mist. Those who deliver these trophies will receive modest compensation from the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Wanted: Large finger bones from the barrow-wights stalking Tyrn Gorthad in Cardolan. Claim them from their wretched hands—be they attached or not, and beware those who crawl with their fingers beneath the mist. Those who deliver these trophies will receive modest compensation from the Dúnedain of Cardolan.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Worried that the Dourhands and Orcs have struck some nefarious bargain, dwarves from an expedition in the east have stopped to investigate a ruin.The leader of the Dourhands might be found within Iorvinas.

Logi has asked you to hunt down and defeat the leader of the Dourhands, who is trying to form an alliance with the Orcs.Defeat Angár IrongutLogi is at the entrance to the ruins of Iorvinas.

Defeating the leader of the Dourhands proved a relatively simple feat. With the leader defeated, the Dourhands will likely prove no more difficulty here.Talk to Logi at the entrance to Iorvinas'The Dourhands must be stopped, and I am willing to bet that there is a leader among them who directs them to deal with the Orcs... a terrible cur who needs to be removed so that dwarves everywhere can hold their heads up with pride!

'This is my task. Find this leader, likely in some hidden passage or lair, eliminate him, then return to me.

'I think you should look first within Iorvinas... the ruins there would be an ideal lair for such a mongrel.''Find the leader of the Dourhands within Iorvinas and see that he is ended quickly and with prejudice. We cannot abide such curs to sully the names of the Seven Fathers!''Already, I feel the fire of my blood beginning to cool. The fool Dourhands need to remember their place. It is bad enough what they did to Thorin's Hall.

'I and all dwarves of good repute owe you a debt of gratitude, ${PLAYER}. Take this and know that you have made a friend this day and wiped a black smudge from the world.'Worried that the Dourhands and Orcs have struck some bargain, dwarves from an expedition in the east have stopped to investigate a ruin.Orcs and Dourhands can be found in the area of Iorvinas. Logi is at Iorvinas.

Upon discovering that the Orcs and Dourhands were living in close proximity, Logi began to fear that the two were working together as well. He has asked you to collect any proof that this collusion may be happening.'We left the expedition moving towards the east to investigate what the Dourhands are doing here in the Lone-lands. We have seen both Orcs and dwarves in this place, and nothing here makes a dwarf feel welcome!

'Our fears are that the Orcs and Dourhands are trading with one another. Nay... we fear that they are working together, supplying one another with materials, tools and weapons, and we want a stop put to it!

'Go into the ruins below and see if you can find any Orc-made weapons on the Dourhands in the section to the west. Return to me if you find any. This will tell me plainly what we face.''I can hardly believe what I am seeing! Dwarves and Orcs are enemies! This is something that should never have occurred, never! 

'Our cousins have fallen so far in these long years. Now it is clear to me that their leader must meet an end....'Hunulf and Gadaric Munce agree that your efforts have aided their people, and they believe you should meet with their leader.The ruins of Ost Guruth lie near the Great East Road, where Nain Enidh meets Talath Gaun.

Hunulf Munce, convinced you could be of great assistance to his people, has asked you to go to the home of the Eglain, Ost Guruth, and speak with Frideric the Elder.Talk to Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth'My brother and I agree that you are a proven ally, ${PLAYER}. Though there is much to do here in Annunlos, I would ask that you find your way to the east. There are others of my kind who will want to speak with you. 

'Where Nain Enidh meets Talath Gaun, look to the north, for there you will find Ost Guruth, the home of the Eglain. Our people are not accustomed to visitors, and they will not treat with you in a manner to which you are used. Do not be deterred by their hesitance. They are unused to outsiders, but they have need of adventurers willing to help with their troubles.

'Speak with Frideric the Elder and tell him of the deeds you have performed on behalf of Hunulf Munce and his brother Gadaric, and Frideric will explain the threat facing our people.''Hail to you, ${PLAYER}. I am Frideric, Elder of the Eglain. I know of your deeds. We are already in your debt.

'You have done much to assist the people of the Eglain, and I thank you for your efforts. As Gadaric and Hunulf explained, the passage of Black Riders out of the west brought a shadow to our home. Orcs came from the south, careful not to cross paths with the Orcs from the north and their dwarf-allies. The half-orcs have long been a threat, as well as the spiders, but the Wargs and crebain all came with the Orcs and goblins.

'Worse still, a kind of troll -- Jorthkyn, or Earth-kin, they call themselves -- sought refuge from the Trollshaws over the Last Bridge. They are not violent, but they have trailed evil with them. There is also another matter, but I will not yet speak of that to you. In time, perhaps, you will learn of this greatest danger to the Eglain.''I am pleased to know you. You are a proven warrior and ally to the Eglain. Journey to Ost Guruth and speak with Frideric the Elder and make yourself useful to all of my people.''I wish you well, ${PLAYER}. My people await the arrival of a hero who can free us from the terror that plagues us.'Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth desires coarse fur from the beasts found throughout the central and eastern Lone-lands.You accepted a task from Frideric the Elder to acquire coarse fur from the beasts in the central and eastern Lone-lands. You can find Frideric the Elder within Ost Guruth.Wanted: Coarse fur from the beasts in the central Lone-lands. Bring the fur to Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth.Wanted: Coarse fur from the beasts in the central Lone-lands.'With this, we can mend many clothes and bed rolls.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth desires large filth from the insects and spiders found throughout the central and eastern Lone-lands.You accepted a task from Frideric the Elder to acquire large filth from the spiders and insects in the central and eastern Lone-lands. You can find Frideric the Elder within Ost Guruth.Wanted: Large filth from the spiders and insects in the central Lone-lands. Bring the filth to Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth.Wanted: Large filth from the spiders and insects in the central Lone-lands.'This is proof that you desire to help our people.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth desires tough carapaces from the insects and spiders found throughout the central and eastern Lone-lands.You accepted a task from Frideric the Elder to acquire tough carapaces from the spiders and insects in the central and eastern Lone-lands. You can find Frideric the Elder within Ost Guruth.Wanted: Tough carapaces from the spiders and insects in the central Lone-lands. Bring the carapaces to Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth.Wanted: Tough carapaces from the spiders and insects in the central Lone-lands.'It may seem strange, but these can be used effectively against our enemies.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Since long before Orcs and undead crept into the Lone-lands, the Eglain have had to deal with the menace of spiders dwelling within the ruins across the lonely stretch of Eriador. Now as the spiders are driven from the depths of their ruined homes, they creep closer and closer to Ost Guruth and threaten the folk who dwell there.The elders of the spider-brood can be found in Amon Ros, west of Ost Guruth.

Tortwil asked a favour of you: that you go to Amon Ros and slay the elders of the spider-brood.Defeat the ruin-web spinnerDefeat the ruin-web trapperDefeat the ruin-web weaverTortwil awaits your return in Ost Guruth, east of Amon Ros.

Tortwil will be pleased to hear of your victory against the spider-elders of Amon Ros.Talk to Tortwil at Ost Guruth'All that you have done for the Eglain proves your honour. I shall not doubt your intentions again, but I shall ask a favour of you.

'I am sure that you have seen the variety of spiders throughout the Lone-lands. You may not know of the elders that lead this vile brood. It is they who are to be feared the most.

'I ask now that you go to Amon Ros and slay the elders. They have lived for too long, preying upon the helpless, and must be destroyed.''You will find the elders of the spider-brood within the ruins of Amon Ros. Some are harder to find than others and will move from place to place. Hunt them down and destroy them.''This news will ease the suffering of many. Though it seems a task without thought, we are not a people accustomed to venturing against such foes.

'You are a hero to the Eglain and deserve all that you shall receive in reward.'Foul, corrupted beasts of all kinds wander the ruined and crumbling streets of Fornost, twisted and unnatural mockeries of the wildlife of the North Downs. These creatures pose a threat to all that lives in the surrounding lands.Corrupted bears, boars, and aurochs roam the ruined streets of Fornost in the north-western North Downs.

You should defeat many of the tortured, unhappy animals that roam northern Fornost.Defeat bears in Fornost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat boars in Fornost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat aurochs in Fornost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Reynold Potts is at the Treasure Hunters' Camp, west of Fornost's keep.

You should return to Reynold Potts.Return to Reynold Potts'Surely you have seen the corrupted, miserable beasts that roam here. It is the long influence of Fornost's corruption, I think, that hurts them so.

'It would be a mercy to put them down, for it is no life to graze in these lands.''Ah! Well done, ${PLAYER}. I only hope the effects of your endeavour may last.'Thaliollang in the Elf-ruin of Meluinen at Lin Giliath requires the return of flawed goo.You are on a task to collect flawed goo from the insects of Meluinen and to deliver them to Thaliollang. Thaliollang can be found at Lin Giliath, the Elf-ruins of Meluinen.Needed: Flawed goo from the insects found within Meluinen. Deliver the items to Thaliollang at Lin Giliath.Needed: Flawed goo from the insects found within Meluinen.'I will begin my studies forthwith, you have my thanks.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Thaliollang in the Elf-ruin of Meluinen at Lin Giliath requires the return of scarred heartwood.You are on a task to collect scarred heartwood from the bog-lurkers of Meluinen and to deliver them to Thaliollang. Thaliollang can be found at Lin Giliath, the Elf-ruins of Meluinen.Needed: Scarred heartwood collected from the bog-lurkers found within Meluinen. Deliver the items to Thaliollang at Lin Giliath.Needed: Scarred heartwood collected from the bog-lurkers found within Meluinen.'It pains me to see such noble creatures twisted so. I hope that my research will find a way to bring the creatures peace; you have my thanks.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The worms infesting the ruins of Fornost are led by an elder named Gajarpan by the Orcs. Gajarpan must be destroyed if the worms are to be driven from Fornost.Gajarpan can be found far north of the treasure hunters' camp.

Erl Fisher has asked you to slay Gajarpan, the oldest and most fearsome of worms.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Small fellowship recommended.</rgb>Defeat GajarpanErl Fisher is at the treasure hunters' camp in the ruins of Fornost City.

You should speak with Erl Fisher.Return to Erl Fisher'Elsie hates all them worms, but I tell you, Gajarpan is the worst. We heard the Orcs one night as they whispered... scared to come too close, they were. They didn't want Gajarpan to eat them.

'I ain't seen the great worm, but he must be fearsome to scare those Orcs.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Small fellowship recommended.</rgb>'Very well done, ${RACE}! That was no small deed.'Orthonn worries that a traveller who passed him might have been attacked by the Wargs during the raid on the farmlands.A large pack of Wargs has moved into northern Annúndir, and the bones of their kills litter the burial mounds in the north.

Orthonn is concerned that a small traveller did not make it safely to Amon Raith, and has asked you to search Haudh Eglain for any sign of problems.Collect bones from the bone-piles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orthonn is at Ost Lagoros, on the eastern edge of Annúndir.

You should speak with Orthonn.'Come to think of it, ${PLAYER}, I would ask another favour of you before you go. Another traveller passed along the road, a short little fellow. He went west not long before the Warg attacks on the farmlands.

'I fear that he may have been hurt... or worse. Will you search for him in Annúndir?''I see you've found some bones, ${PLAYER}. Some of these are fairly small, but I wouldn't say any of them would belong to the traveller I saw. Unless I'm very much mistaken, I'd say most of these are goblin bones.

'It seems to me that the Wargs and the goblins must not get along well all of the time -- and that is good for us. Let them fight among themselves, and there will be fewer for us to worry about.'Orthonn has asked you to collect firewood to fuel his camp-fire.Firewood and driftwood can be found near Ost Lagoros.

You should collect firewood and driftwood to help Orthonn keep his fire burning hot.Collect driftwood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect firewood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orthonn is at his camp-site on the south-eastern edge of Annúndir.

You should bring the firewood to Orthonn.Return to Orthonn'Welcome to Ost Lagoros, traveller. I wish that I could offer you more comfort, but as you can see, it is a sparse little ruin, with little to offer.

'It is so nice to have some company! Would you be willing to collect some firewood and driftwood nearby? It would bring some cheer to this guttering flame. We shall be warm and comfortable then.''Ah! This will do well, ${PLAYER}. Do pull up a seat, rest, and let us talk. It has been long since I have had company at my fire.'Two Uruk-generals passed through the Ram Dúath with the Orc-horde. One of these Uruks passed to the west and disappeared into the ancient ruins of Fornost.An Uruk-general was spotted entering Fornost, where he set up camp near the Great Fountain in the courtyard. Daisy Harbrook warns that the fountain has likely been desecrated and turned into an idol to Sauron.

Daisy Harbrook advised you to place armour she gave you somewhere near the fountain in order to challenge the Uruk-general.Daisy Harbrook awaits your return at the Free Peoples' Camp outside of Fornost.

You have proven victorious over the second Uruk-general. You should return to Daisy Harbrook at once with news of your success.'It is unlikely that the second Uruk-general will show his face to us in Fornost; nevertheless, we must attempt to lure him out and defeat him.

'Take this armour into Fornost, and display it somewhere near the Great Fountain. That may be enough to goad the general into revealing himself. The Great Fountain may not appear as such -- it has surely been desecrated by the Orcs -- so you may want to look for something that appears like an idol to Sauron.

'The battle will likely be difficult. I would advise you not to even dare entering Fornost without allies. Return to me after you have defeated the Uruk-general.''This victory will hearten everyone throughout the North Downs. A pity we did not stop these monsters before they achieved their goals.

'Still, we cannot let such a victory go without recognition. You have my thanks and the gratitude of all the North Downs, ${PLAYER}.'A legend of the Battle of Fornost mentioned a great captain of the armies of Fornost who led his men onto the field, only to betray his oath and lead them against King Arvedui instead.Riamul is somewhere within Fornost. 

Emma Slee has asked you to seek the shade-captain, Riamul, and bring him word of his pardon. She hopes that his freedom will allow his men to follow.Find Riamul and release his spiritEmma Slee is at the Fornost camp.

Riamul, loyal to Angmar even in death, refused his pardon, believing that the Witch-king would restore him. You should return to Emma Slee and let her know you had to defeat him to free his men.'It troubles me that so many of the shades remain within Fornost. Are there so many that have not repented of their betrayal? It is hard for me to believe this. Perhaps the will of one keeps them here.

'There is a legend of the Battle of Fornost which tells of a great captain, Riamul, who led his men forth from the city, only to betray his oath and lead them instead against King Arvedui in favour of Angmar. Perhaps you should seek Riamul and bring him word of his pardon?

'I do not know whether he remains unwittingly or willingly, for I cannot know the mind of the Dead, but I cannot see why he should choose to suffer so.''Please, you must find Captain Riamul. These shades need no longer suffer!''I am sorry that it had to come to such an end, but some things must be done.  Already Fornost grows lighter. Thank you, kind ${RACE}. You have done the North Downs a great service!'The trolls of Fornost are terrible foes, strong and vicious.Trolls can be found in Fornost, north of the Treasure Hunters' Camp.

Will Widdup has challenged you to defeat a number of trolls within Fornost.Defeat trolls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Will Widdup is at the Treasure Hunters' Camp in the north-western Fields of Fornost.

You should return to Will Widdup and tell him of your success.Return to Will Widdup'Have you seen the big ol' trolls in Fornost? They be fearsome beasts. I took one look at them and ran back here as fast as my legs would carry me. Warn't my proudest moment.

'You have a look about you as if you'd seen some fair battles. What do you think of a wager: If you can best any of them trolls, I'll offer you a right reward. If you don't, well, you'll get no mockery from me.''You're much tougher than you look, ${PLAYER}! Well done, well done. You did what the rest of us could not.'Sara Oakheart was found prisoner in the depths of Fornost. She needs your help getting free and back to the safety of her home.Sara Oakheart was apparently taken captive by the Blogmal Orcs while she was gathering wild herbs. She needs you to escort her out of Fornost.Talk to Sara OakheartSara Oakheart was apparently taken captive by the Blogmal Orcs while she was gathering wild herbs. She needs you to escort her out of Fornost.Defeat Shiruk and Kamordî'Hurry let's get out of here before more Orcs come. Follow me!'Maedhrusc ventured briefly into Fornost and saw that it was overrun with the dead.Shades can be found within the heart of Fornost.

You should enter Fornost and defeat many of the dead of which Maedhrusc spoke.Defeat shades and dreadmists (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Maedhrusc is at the Treasure Hunters' Camp in north-western Fornost.

You have defeated many of the evil dead roaming in Fornost and should return to Maedhrusc.Return to Maedhrusc'Hail, fellow adventurer! I can't tell you how good it is to see a stranger who's real flesh and blood, and not of the Orc-made kind.

'You have found us a humble band of adventurers stranded here in a dark place. We came for treasure, and to fight through the fabled paths of Fornost, but found ourselves overwhelmed by the dead right quick.

'Now we're only trying to survive. We haven't been the same since we set foot in that evil place....''I can't believe you drove off all those shades, ${PLAYER}. Is it really true? You have given us hope.'The bulk of Angmar's host in Fornost is comprised of Orcs and goblins, a formidable force that Arch Widdup fears may be brought to bear on Trestlebridge if they are allowed to march south.In order to improve Trestlebridge's chances against the armies of Angmar, Arch Widdup has asked you to enter Fornost and slay as many Orcs and goblins as you can.Defeat goblins in Fornost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orcs in Fornost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have dealt a serious blow to Angmar's host, and while not a complete victory, your efforts should at least lend courage to Arch Widdup.'Hello there. Perhaps if you are going into Fornost, you could help me? I'm from Trestlebridge, and Nellie and the rest of us are very worried about the forces amassing here, particularly the Orcs and goblins.

'If such a large host is allowed to leave Fornost, Trestlebridge won't stand a chance against it. Perhaps you could go into Fornost and deal a blow to the Orcs and goblins?

'Come back to me when you have completed the task.''Goblins... I hate goblins. Killing goblins will help us defend our homes, but just ridding the world of them would be cause enough!''You've done well indeed, my friend. I don't doubt these brutes will think twice about pressing towards Trestlebridge now.'Ranger-scouts reported that there were wraiths within the ruins of Fornost.Istuienn can be found outside the library in Esteldín.

Halbarad suggested that you speak with Istuienn, a lore-master of the Rangers. She might know how to defeat the wraiths of Fornost.The morgul-hilts are in the possession of the four wraiths within Fornost.

Istuienn believes that you may be able to find and recover the hilts of the four morgul-blades discussed in Laerdan's text inside the ruins of Fornost.Istuienn is outside the library in Esteldín.

You should bring the four morgul-hilts to Istuienn at once.'Word has come to me from our scouts outside the gates of Fornost that powerful wraiths have been seen within the city. I do not doubt the words of my men, and if these spirits are indeed who I believe them to be, there is a greater evil there that we have not yet perceived.

'These wraiths must be the spirits of the four traitors that aided Angmar in the downfall of the Kings' Norbury. In the last stand against the Enemy, a great treachery occurred and the gates of the city were thrown down. Legend tells that the men were bewitched by the Witch-king's champion, a woman named Amarthiel. If these wraiths are indeed these men they must be dealt with.

'I would ask that you visit Istuienn, who is learned in these matters. She may be able to assist you in finding some way of ending the threat of these new foes.''Istuienn is a scholar of our people... she may know of a means to banish the wraiths of Fornost.''The fall of the Kings' Norbury? In my youth, I transcribed an ancient text written by the Elf Laerdan. Alas that Laerdan is no longer with us, for he left many years ago with Golodir and no word has returned of them. He was a great lore-master and had long studied Fornost and Amarthiel.

'Before its downfall, Amarthiel had entered Fornost under a false guise.  She appeared as a great smith, offering her skills against the works of the Enemy. Her words and council were heeded by many. She aided in the crafting of armaments and gear of war. Laerdan believed that before the downfall of the city, Amarthiel wielded a great power to sway the minds of Men. Four of the captains of the city-guard were enthralled by Amarthiel and taken into her service. When the Witch-king's horde pressed its final assault on Fornost, these men and their followers overthrew the door-wardens and opened the gates.

'One account has been preserved from those who escaped. The ancient text of Laerdan gives the account of the last of the king's rear-guard to leave the city. The text claims the four men captive to Amarthiel's will swore an oath to her upon morgul-blades, pledging their unending service and then threw themselves upon the swords. If the wraiths have returned to fulfil their oath, Halbarad is right to fear. I would ask you to gather companions and seek the wraiths in the ruins of Fornost. If you can, recover the hilts of the morgul-blades. I am sure that they would still possess them and guard them fiercely. If the account is true, then the blade-hilts bind these wraiths to Middle-earth, and their destruction may bring about an end to their evil.''Bring me the hilts of the morgul-blades used to corrupt the wraiths... perhaps by destroying them, the spell will be undone.''You have returned with the morgul-hilts! Give them up quickly. It is not wise even for those strong of will such as you to handle these hilts for long. You have my thanks in recovering them... you must recount the story of their finding.

'So, you say you have defeated the wraiths? I fear that they were not wholly destroyed, as the oath that binds them has not yet been fulfilled. The malice of their spirits still remains, and they will return in time. I have read all I can from the morgul-hilts. They are twisted and black, but they bear strange devices for works of the Enemy. Thank you, for your aid in this endeavour.'The Tooks in the camp at Fornost are desperately looking for proof that hobbits fought in the Battle of Fornost. They have been ridiculed for their beliefs, so they have come to find the proof they so desperately need.You might find hobbit arrowheads among the skeletons within Fornost, north of the Fornost camp.Collect arrowheads (${VALUE}/20)You have been encouraged to learn the wilds around Ost Guruth in the Lone-lands, so that you may better find your way there in all seasons.Trumswith is at Ost Guruth, the Ruin-hold in the Lone-lands, far to the east of The Forsaken Inn, just north of the Great East Road.

You have been told to speak with Trumswith, one of the Eglain at Ost Guruth, who can train you in the ways of the wild.Hogahild left Ost Guruth near Unni, the dwarf stable-master there, but is now somewhere in the wilderness of the Lone-lands.

Trumswith has challenged you to follow the trail of his sister Hogahild. If you can return to him with the name of the only beast he has hunted and spared, information known to Hogahild, he will teach you secret paths through the Lone-lands to Ost Guruth.Trumswith is in Ost Guruth, the Ruin-hold in the Lone-lands.

You have followed her trail to Hogahild, the sister of Trumswith, and she told you the name of the only beast her brother has hunted and spared: the aurochs known as Trampletusk. You should return now to Trumswith with this information and he will teach you what he knows about the wild paths of the Lone-lands.'Ah, a Hunter! Trumswith will be wanting to speak with you. It is not often that a good Hunter travels through these parts. I am sure you will be a boon to us here at Ost Guruth!''Your name is ${PLAYER}, and you are a hunter? The Eglain have lived in these wilds for many a year; the ways of the wild are the ways of the Eglain. If you seek to learn of secret paths through the Lone-lands, you are right to come to me.

'I, Trumswith, can show you the signs that will allow you to make your way through the Lone-lands at great speed, leading the way for those in your fellowship who are not skilled in the ways of the hunt. I will do this for you if you can prove to me that you have the necessary skill. My sister Hogahild left Ost Guruth this morning. If you can find her trail, follow it until you meet with her. Ask her the name of the only beast I have ever hunted and yet spared, and I will teach you what I can.

'Hogahild left Ost Guruth near Unni, the dwarf stable-master by the stairs. You can speak with him to get your bearings, but will then need to find her trail on your own.''I will not tell you where Hogahild has gone. You are a hunter! If you cannot follow her trail, I will teach you nothing!

'Unni, the dwarf stable-master just outside Ost Guruth, likely saw her as she left. Speak with him if you need to get your bearings, but following my sister's trail from that point will be up to you.''I did see Hogahild leave this morning. Lovely lass that Hogahild. Waved to me, she did! She was standing right over there, on those hills slightly east of here. If you're looking for her, I'd start your search to the east.''Another of my brother's ridiculous challenges? Well, I must offer you my congratulations, even if I do not appreciate the stream of hunters he always sends following me on my errands! He insists that he does not do it to keep an eye on me, but I know better.

'Trumswith once hunted Trampletusk, a great aurochs, but when he finally encountered the beast he was so struck by its majesty that he spared its life. I understand that the beast has left these lands now, but I do not know where it might have gone.

'Return to my brother in Ost Guruth and tell him of Trampletusk; he will know that your hunt has been successful. I hope too that he will cease with these silly challenges! You hunters have enough to do without following me!'The trail here is very confused, and the signs are difficult to read. You cannot tell in which direction the trail might continue from here.The trail here is quite clear. Whoever made these tracks was moving without haste eastward.The trail here is quite clear to your skilled eyes. Whoever made this trail passed north-east.'Return now to my brother Trumswith, and he will give you whatever reward he promised. You will find him at Ost Guruth, our Ruin-hold here in the Lone-lands.''You speak of Trampletusk? Very good, ${PLAYER}! You have followed the trail of my sister and proven yourself of worthy skill!

'I will teach to you what you must know of finding your way in the Lone-lands. You will now be able to show your fellowship the quickest paths to return to Ost Guruth. You have earned my respect, and I will repay you with these teachings.'The Ranger Amarion was encamped at Amon Raith, watching over the surrounding lands. He was not prepared to care for the farmers fleeing Angmar's invasion of Annúndir.Boars have taken over the ruins of Minas Vrûn, east of Amon Raith.

Amarion has asked you to hunt the boars of Minas Vrûn so that he can feed the farmers that have taken refuge at Amon Raith.Collect tough boar-meat at Minas Vrûn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Ranger Amarion is at Amon Raith, west of Annúndir and east of the Greenway.

Amarion is waiting for you to return with the boar-meat with which he hopes to feed the farmers under his care.'You are welcome to rest here for a time, friend, but I am afraid that you will not find much peace. Evil things stir in Fornost, while the hills of Annúndir to the east are ravaged by Wargs. These people look to me for their defence....

'Alas, food is in short supply, and I fear to leave them alone while I hunt. Amon Raith was of old a defensible position, but I am only one man.

'If you choose not to heed my warning and travel east, might I ask that you hunt the boars that roam Minas Vrûn? They have taken over those ruins, and show no sign of leaving.''You are as skilled in the hunt as you are welcome, ${PLAYER}! This meat will last for a long while! The Wargs and goblins cannot stay in Annúndir forever, and with this meat I can feed the families of Annúndir who have come to me for protection. You have my thanks.'The townsfolk in Oatbarton await the delivery of heavy carapaces to Ponto Oats at the centre of the town.You have been tasked with collecting and delivering heavy carapaces from the insects and spiders surrounding Oatbarton. You are to deliver the items to Ponto Oats at the centre of the town of Oatbarton.Wanted: Heavy carapaces from the insects and spiders surrounding Oatbarton. You can make the delivery to Ponto Oats at Oatbarton centre.Wanted: Heavy carapaces from the insects and spiders surrounding Oatbarton.'We will find a way to use these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Worried that the Dourhands and Orcs have struck some bargain, dwarves from an expedition in the east have stopped to investigate a ruin.The Orc-leader Gnâshrakh is within Iorvinas.

Henir advised that you not waste the opportunity to find the Orc-leader and put an end to him. One fewer Orc in the world is a good thing, and one fewer leader among the Orcs is a great thing.Defeat GnâshrakhHenir is at the entrance to the ruins of Iorvinas.

Defeating the Orc-leader proved simple enough. Henir will be pleased to know that you have carried out the duty he placed before you.Talk to Henir at the entrance to Iorvinas'I would wager that there is a leader in the depths of this place... likely a larger Orc, hiding in the ruins, but ruling from the darkness. 

'I have oft heard the name Gnâshrakh spoken among the Orcs. Perhaps this is the name of their leader.

'Search through these ruins; find and destroy this leader, then return to me.''It's best to kill as many Orcs as possible. When the opportunity arises to bring down their leaders, you cannot stop from carrying out the deed.''Orc-leaders are a dangerous sort. It is good to know that you are as hearty an adventurer as first I thought, ${PLAYER}.

'You have earned this reward, and so too have you earned the respect of this dwarf and the respect of my kin.'Worried that the Dourhands and Orcs have struck some bargain, dwarves from an expedition to the Misty Mountains have come westward to investigate a ruin.Dourhands and Orcs can be found within the halls of Iorvinas.

Logi has asked you to bring an end to the alliance of the Dourhands and Orcs.Defeat Tarkrîp Orcs in Iorvinas (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Dourhand Dwarves in Iorvinas (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Logi is at the entrance to the ruins of Iorvinas.

Many of the Dourhands and Orcs were seen in close proximity. It is clear that Logi is right, the Orcs and Dourhands are in league. He should be told of this.Talk to Logi'Refr Quicksilver, outside, is no member of the Longbeards. He is no member of the Dourhands either. I know only this, he is a dwarf, and an honourable one at that.

'He may have left his family's lands, but he remembers that he is first and foremost a dwarf! The Dourhands can no longer be considered part of the greater dwarf-kingdom, having fallen enough to live so close to Orcs without the sound of battle.

'Because of this, I am requesting that you lend us your aid. Defeat the Dourhands and the Orcs in this ruin and return to me.''A true dwarf would never suffer the stench of an Orc this close to him! Fool Dourhands, betrayers! They deserve the punishment they will receive.''I knew it! Fool Dourhands! We should rid the world of the lot of them! There must be a leader here... one that makes them do these things.'The army of Orcs and goblins inhabiting Fornost constantly press upon the Treasure Hunters' refuge.Orcs and goblins can be found near the base of the keep within the city of Fornost.

Gareth Copp has asked you to help the forces of the North Downs by reducing the numbers of the Orcs and goblins at Fornost.Defeat Orcs in Fornost City (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat goblins in Fornost City (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gareth Copp is at the camp of the Free Peoples in the southernmost part of Fornost.

Your efforts have weakened the forces of Angmar, as Gareth Copp requested. You should return to Gareth at once with news of your victory.Return to Gareth Copp'Lots of Orcs and goblins in these parts, especially around the gates to the city. They tried to cut off our retreat when the treasure hunt wasn't going well, but we escaped. They come looking for us at night, but luckily we've remained hidden.

'If you take off a head or two of those evil creatures, lots of folk here will thank you. It'll make this place a great deal safer.''A bold warrior, you are! You did not look it, but I stand here corrected.'Since long before Orcs and undead crept into the Lone-lands, the Eglain have had to deal with the menace of spiders dwelling within the ruins across the lonely stretch of Eriador. Now as the spiders are driven from the depths of their ruined homes they creep closer and closer to Ost Guruth and threaten the Eglain who dwell there.The spiders' eggs can be found among their nests in Amon Ros, west of Ost Guruth.

After telling you of the loss of his wife and son to the spiders of Amon Ros, Tortwil asked you to return to the vale and destroy any egg sacs you might find.Destroy egg sacs in Amon Ros (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tortwil awaits your return in Ost Guruth, east of Amon Ros.

You found the egg sacs in the shadows of Amon Ros and destroyed them as Tortwil requested. You should return to Ost Guruth with the news of your success.'By my word, know that my wife and son fell to the brood to the west, but they were not the only ones to fall to the spiders in that vale. The Eglain have lost so many through the years. Other families have lost siblings and loved ones to the spiders and, more recently, to the Wargs stalking the wild.

'I have a request for you, which may prove fruitless, yet I hope it shall yield solace for many. In Amon Ros, where the spiders dwell, you likely saw many sacs of eggs. These sacs will bring more evil into the world and the grief will continue.

'Will you go and destroy the eggs before they hatch? The spiders' numbers grow each day, while the good folk of Ost Guruth dwindle.''Your efforts to curb the spread of the spiders may bring at least some respite from our grief. You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.''Scour the ruins of Amon Ros and destroy any egg sacs you may find. Hope may be scarce, but at least you may slow the spread of the spider-brood in Nain Enidh.'Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of jagged beaks to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect jagged beaks from the birds in the area surrounding Esteldín to the north and west. Once collected you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the birds to the north and west of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the birds to the north and west of Esteldín.'These look like a good collection, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of large wings to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect large wings from the birds in the area surrounding Esteldín to the north and west. Once collected, you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Large wings from the birds to the north and west of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Large wings from the birds to the north and west of Esteldín.'We will get to work on these, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Rock-worms, drawn to the North Downs by Angmar's passing, have begun to spawn in the hills and many have wandered into Fornost itself.Worms can be found in the ruins of Fornost, north of the Treasure Hunters' Camp.

Elsie Whitman has mentioned a nagging worry about the worms to the north, and will sleep sounder if you can manage to slay some.Defeat Rock-worms in Fornost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Elsie Whitman is at the Treasure Hunters' Camp in the ruins of Fornost.

You should return to Elsie and tell her that she may sleep easier.Return to Elsie Whitman'I don't care one whit about Orcs, goblins, treasure, or beasts. It's them worms that keep me up at night. Have you seen the size of them? It's unnatural.

'They're up north of here, slithering around. You can hear them hissing when all's quiet.''Well, that's a start! Well done, ${PLAYER}. You're more useful than these lugs.'Reynold Potts suspects the Blogmal Orcs may have a defiler in their midst, poisoning the lands around them and causing the horrible disfigurations in the grazing animals.The Blogmal Defiler can be found in the ruins of Fornost City, east of the treasure hunters' camp.

In order to end the corruption of the local wildlife, Reynold Potts has asked you to kill the Blogmal Defiler.Defeat the Blogmal DefilerReynold Potts is at the treasure hunters' camp in Fornost.

Potts will be relieved to know that the Blogmal Defiler met his end at your hands, removing the threat of his corrupting influence.Return to Reynold Potts'Such vile corruption... you'd think the poor beasts have been poisoned. Unclean water and grass, would be my guess. This is no doubt the work of some Orc, and a smart one at that.

'I've heard of defilers and poisoners and the like amongst the Orcs. Do you think the Blogmal have such a one in their midst?''I'm mighty relieved that you were able to do away with that defiler. If the blight he caused was getting to the animals, soon it would have got to us too.'Henbran has tastes and cravings that are quite peculiar.Henbran has asked you to collect bear meat, spider chitin, and huorn branches from creatures in the Old Forest.

The Old Forest is in northern Cardolan.Collect spider chitin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bear meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect huorn branches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected all of the items Henbran asked you to gather for her soup recipe.

You should bring them to her in the Old Forest.'Since you shall be trapessing about the woods, you might as well help me out with a few other tasks. I want to make my favourite dish, bear meat soup, but I lack some ingredients.

'Obviously, I shall need bear meat. Bring me also chopped branches from the wicked huorns and chitin from the lurkers and creepers.''You have done it? Hee hee hee, splendid.'Ranger Faradan is concerned about the spirits that animate the Dead in Tyrn Gorthad.Fell spirits and wights threaten the peace of Tyrn Gorthad.Defeat barrow-spirits in Tyrn Gorthad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat barrow-wights in Tyrn Gorthad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ranger Faradan is south-west of Calarphel in Tyrn Gorthad.Talk to Ranger Faradan south-west of Calarphel in Tyrn Gorthad'Something has stirred to madness the spirits of Tyrn Gorthad, and it is not safe for travellers to come this way. No, do not misunderstand me: these hills have not been truly safe for hundreds and hundreds of years. But I deem you are an adventurer, and can handle yourself.

'Still, these are spirits fell and terrible. Sometimes they move through the grass and rustle the branches of the bare trees, and then you know they are there. Other times they house themselves in the body of a corpse, and become the animate Dead. Whichever form they take, they are dangerous, and must be ended by those of us brave enough, or foolish enough, to take the chance. If you are either of these two... I wish you luck.''Whichever form these spirits take, they are dangerous, and must be ended by those of us brave enough, or foolish enough, to take the chance. If you are either of these two... I wish you luck.''Please allow me to apologize, stranger. I called you foolish, for sometimes there are fools who come this way, but I see now that you belong not to that number. It is courage you possess, and not madness.

'What is your name? ${PLAYER}? Then know that I am called Faradan of the Dúnedain, and Tyrn Gorthad is under my and my kinswoman Gorwen's watchful protection.'Amarion is worried about the goblin presence in southern Annúndir and fears what will happen if the creatures learn of the refugees at Amon Raith.The goblins have established a camp on the southern hills of Annúndir, east of Amon Raith.

Amarion has asked you to scout the goblin camp and assess the threat it poses to those under his care. He has warned you to take care. If a goblin sees you, it might track you back to Amon Raith.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Take care to remain unnoticed by the enemy.</rgb>Scout the goblin camp<rgb=#FF0000>Avoid goblins</rgb>The man in need of aid can be found just outside of the goblin stronghold in the central North Downs.

You should speak with the man.Approach the distraught man'We have enough food now to last a good while, but the people under my care will not be satisfied until they have reclaimed their lands from the Wargs and goblins that have invaded Annúndir.

'We must be cautious. There is a camp of goblins on the slopes of the southern hills. Returning the refugees to their homes must start with the destruction of those goblins, but first I need to know their numbers.

'Travel east into Annúndir and get as close to the goblin camp as you can without being seen. I do not want the goblins following you back to Amon Raith, so use stealth.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Take care to remain unnoticed by the enemy.</rgb>'Ha!''Help!''Thank goodness! I thought I was done for. I ran from the Wargs, and was ambushed by goblins instead.'

You explain that the way to Amon Raith is clear of goblins, and urge the man to head that way to come under Amarion's protection.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}! I will. You're not... you're not going further, are you?'The Blogmal Orcs are building siege equipment in their camp outside of the once-great city of Fornost.The siege weapon parts are being assembled in the Orc camp of northern Fornost.

You should destroy the siege weapon parts being assembled by the Blogmal Orcs.Destroy siege parts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gareth Copp is at the camp of the Free Peoples in the southern reaches of Fornost.

You should return to Gareth Copp and let him know that you have destroyed a number of siege-parts.Tell Gareth Copp of the siege-weapons you destroyedThe Blogmal Orcs are building siege equipment in their camp outside of the once-great city of Fornost.

Whatever their purpose, it cannot be good. You should destroy the siege equipment.'You have thwarted Angmar's plans, ${PLAYER}! It will take them much longer now to amass the siege weapons they need. For a time, at least, Trestlebridge and Bree-land should be safe.'Bingo has come to the Lone-lands in search of adventure, and the folk of the Forsaken Inn are certain to provide it.Theodore Gorse and Haley Meadowsweet have attracted Bingo's attention in the Forsaken Inn, and he wants to learn more about them.Talk to Theodore Gorse in the Forsaken InnTalk to Haley Meadowsweet in the Forsaken InnBingo is in the Forsaken Inn, in the Lone-lands.Talk to Bingo in the Forsaken InnTheodore Gorse is in the Forsaken Inn.

Bingo wants to know if Theodore Gorse, self-proclaimed Master Treasure Seeker, will teach him the profession.Talk to Theodore Gorse in the Forsaken InnTheodore gave you a parchment with a riddle on it, apparently a clue to the location of a treasure in the Lone-lands. The parchment reads:

'The torrent flows, blanketed in the shadow of only morning.
A sentinel stares from on high; only he still watches.
The red squirrel once dared the climb, but Time fells all.'Theodore Gorse is in the Forsaken Inn.

You found the treasure that Theodore Gorse could not, but it was only a parchment of paper with a cryptic message on it. You should bring him the sad news that there was no treasure.Bingo Boffin is in the Forsaken Inn.Talk to Bingo in the Forsaken Inn'This is a still and silent crowd, and it is very different than that of the Prancing Pony! Milo was a friendly fellow, and I hope he was not upset by our hasty departure, but it is important not to stay in comfort for too long or the whole journey will be endangered!

'I said this was a dark and dreary place, but look at those two in the corner. They seem to be colourful characters, and do not fit in with the rest of the assemblage. Let us see what has gotten them so worked up, shall we?''I said this was a dark and dreary place, but look at those two in the corner. They seem to be colourful characters, and do not fit in with the rest of the assemblage. Let us see what has gotten them so worked up, shall we?''Hello there! Can I help you with something? Unless... you must have heard my name and desired to hear more of my exploits? It is always a pleasure to speak with admirers! I was just telling Haley here of some of my greatest finds, and she was held in rapt attention, as no doubt you would be as well!'

A quick glance at Haley Meadowsweet proves that this may not have been entirely the case.

'Yes, I am the great Theodore Gorse, Master Treasure Seeker and wanderer of the wild lands. If there is treasure to be found in the hidden places of this world, I have already been there and found it. My deeds are extensive and well-documented, and my virtues similarly well-known. I am so accomplished in this field that only one thing remains for me: to retire.''Theodore claims to be a Master Treasure Seeker, and will do so at great length to anyone who will listen. Sadly, that appears to be me for the moment.

'Anyone can claim to be a great seeker of treasure. In point of fact, anyone can do the seeking! It is the finding that separates those with actual accomplishments from the pretenders!''Yes, I am the great Theodore Gorse, Master Treasure Seeker and wanderer of the wild lands. If there is treasure to be found in the hidden places of this world, I have already been there and found it. My deeds are extensive and well-documented, and my virtues similarly well-known. I am so accomplished in this field that only one thing remains for me: to retire.''Theodore claims to be a Master Treasure Seeker, and will do so at great length to anyone who will listen. Sadly, that appears to be me for the moment.

'Anyone can claim to be a great seeker of treasure. In point of fact, anyone can do the seeking! It is the finding that separates those with actual accomplishments from the pretenders!''Theodore is a Master Treasure Seeker? That sounds very exciting, and could make for a promising entry in the account of my adventure.

'${PLAYER}, I have had a great idea! Will you ask if Theodore will train me how to be a Treasure Seeker? It has occurred to me that so far much of my manuscript involves spending time in taverns, and becoming a Treasure Seeker will get us out into the wild lands!

'It will make for better reading, that is for certain! Will you please ask him?''Will you ask if Theodore will train me how to be a Treasure Seeker? It has occurred to me that so far much of my manuscript involves spending time in taverns, and becoming a Treasure Seeker will get us out into the wild lands!

'It will make for better reading, that is for certain! Will you please ask him?''Your hobbit-friend would be better off asking Theodore for training to become a Blustery Self-Important Braggart, if you ask me.''What's that? Your hobbit-friend wants to become a Treasure Seeker? I would love to teach him, but alas, it is not something that can be easily taught! If he does not have the natural proclivity for such adventures, he will be resistant to any training I might give to him. Also, I am about to retire, and intend to spend my time regaling Haley here with tales of my interesting and eventful life. So I would appreciate being left alone to do so!'

Theodore thinks for a moment, and then speaks apologetically.

'I do not mean to be so brusque. Here, take this riddle. It is apparently a clue to a treasure somewhere in the Lone-lands, a treasure I never found. If the two of you can solve this riddle and find the treasure, perhaps I will be able to teach the both of you something after all. Good luck to you, and good-bye!''The parchment I gave you is apparently a clue to a treasure somewhere in the Lone-lands, a treasure I never found. If the two of you can solve this riddle and find the treasure, perhaps I will be able to teach the both of you something after all. Good luck to you, and good-bye!''May I see the riddle?'

You show Bingo the riddle, and he closes his eyes, deep in thought.

'This is a tough one, ${PLAYER}! Maybe you should study the riddle some more and see if it seems to describe anything specific.''The riddle seems to describe a number of things that might be significant. It sounds like there may be a waterfall nearby, and the last line makes me think of fallen trees.

'Blanketed in the shadow of only morning' sounds like it is describing a direction or a time of day. Where do shadows fall in the morning but not in the afternoon?

'It does not seem so hard to me, but I am delighted that Theodore was stumped by this riddle!''You found the treasure Theodore could not? Incredible! Perhaps I should ask you for Treasure Seeking Lessons, ${PLAYER}!''What? You mean you solved the riddle, and found the treasure? I do not believe it!'

You tell Theodore that, disappointingly, there was no actual treasure, and the strongbox contained only a parchment with a cryptic message on it.

'Disappointed? I am not disappointed at all! Do you see what this means? This means that someone else found the treasure first, and replaced it with this message! Someone beat the Master Treasure Seeker at his own game! The retirement is off, ${PLAYER}! I have a treasure to find!''Someone else found the treasure first, and replaced it with this message! Someone beat the Master Treasure Seeker at his own game! The retirement is off, ${PLAYER}! I have a treasure to find!''You beat Theodore at his own game, ${PLAYER}! I cannot thank you enough for that! Perhaps now he will stop talking my ear off about how masterful a Treasure Seeker he believes himself to be.''You say there was no treasure in that strongbox, ${PLAYER}, but I think you are not quite correct on that score. I think you gave Theodore the best treasure of all: a reason to keep going! Now he is excited about his profession once again, and does not need to spend his days reliving the adventures of his youth... he can have all-new adventures! Perhaps his will intersect with ours for a time, too. There is still a treasure out there, after all!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Goose and Gander' (Recommended Level: 28)</rgb>The goblins pose a threat to the people of the North Downs, and should be punished for wandering so far afield.The goblin camp is east of Amon Raith, on the southern hills of Annúndir.

You should enter the goblin camp and defeat a number of goblins there.Defeat goblins (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy goblin-tents (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Ranger Amarion is at Amon Raith, west of Annúndir.

Amarion is waiting to hear about your attack on the goblins of Annúndir.'I would turn back, if I were you! It's naught but danger up ahead.

'The way you came is safe, right? Well, I'll head that way. To this Amarion fellow. Sounds better than trying to fend for myself out here.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}, and almost better than I had hoped! The goblins have surely heard our message: they are not welcome here, nor ever will be!

'I do not have the means to reward you as richly as you deserve, but I hope this small token will serve as a reminder of the good you have accomplished for Amarion and the people of Annúndir.'The Stonehold Chief lies at the centre of all the goblins' mischief in Annúndir. You should defeat the Stonehold Chief, and send a strong message to the goblins menacing these lands.The Stonehold Chief is in the goblin camp on the southern hills of Annúndir, east of Amon Raith.

You should find and defeat the Stonehold Chief.Find and defeat the Stonehold ChiefThe Stonehold Chief is in the goblin camp on the southern hills of Annúndir, east of Amon Raith.

You should defeat the Stonehold Chief.Defeat the Stonehold ChiefYou have defeated the Stonehold Chief, eliminating the greatest weight of the goblin threat in Annúndir.CompletedThe Stonehold Chief lies at the centre of all the goblins' mischief in Annúndir. You should defeat the Stonehold Chief, and send a strong message to the goblins menacing these lands.You have defeated the Stonehold Chief, eliminating the greatest weight of the goblin threat in Annúndir.Worried that the Dourhands and Orcs have struck some bargain, dwarves from an expedition to Rivendell have come westward to investigate a ruin.Orcs can be found within Iorvinas.

Henir believes that there is something of value within the ruins where he hides. He does not think that the Orcs can get the materials on their own and wants to know if the Dourhands are providing them with tools needed for their work.'There is something within these walls, something of value. I can feel it! Orcs likely can too, and of course the Dourhands know, but they seem more interested in the ruins to the north and west.

'The Orcs are fine at making weapons and crude tools, but if my feeling is right, they require specific tools. I bet that is what the Dourhands are giving them.

'Hunt down the Orcs and see what they are getting from the Dourhands. Return to me when you have evidence that there is something going on between the Dourhands and Orcs in this ruin.''As I thought, sturdy and bearing the fine sharp point needed to cut new holes in thick stone. Orcs would rather use their swords and axes. 

'This is ill news indeed, but it speaks to an issue that has long haunted the Dourhands and one that we can likely stop here and now. Logi will handle the issue with our ill-fated cousins. 

'I want to focus on the Orcs and seeing that they fail at whatever nefarious purpose they have here.'Orthonn has asked you to gather furs to be sent to the refugees of Amon Raith in their time of need, and Annúndir is full of wild beasts with ideal hides.Bears and wolves can be found on the northern hills of Annúndir, west of Ost Lagoros.

Orthonn wishes to send warm furs to the refugees guarded by Amarion at Amon Raith.Collect furs from wolves or bears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orthonn awaits you at Ost Lagoros, south-east of Annúndir and across the river.

You should bring the furs you gathered to Orthonn.Bring the hides you gathered to Orthonn'I look at what happened at the farmlands as a failing of my own. Amarion and I guard this road, but the Wargs moved between us, and we did not espy them until it was too late for the farmers.

'Amarion guards them now, but I wish to make amends for my negligence. Will you help me gather furs to send those poor folk? It is not much, but it will keep away the chill of night.''A very kind deed! Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You are a skilled hunter, and speedy at that.'Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of broken wood spears.You are on a task to deliver broken wood spears from the Dourhands in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Broken wood spears from the Dourhands found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar.Wanted: Broken wood spears from the Dourhands found throughout the northern North Downs.'Ah yes, I see it now...ten spears completes the task as expected. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of coarse furs.You are on a task to deliver coarse furs from the beasts in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Coarse furs from the beasts found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaster at Othrikar.Wanted: Coarse furs from the beasts found throughout the northern North Downs.'Completed: A simple task to obtain coarse furs from beasts. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of large wings.You are on a task to deliver large wings from hendrevail in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Large wings from the hendrevail found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaster at Othrikar.Wanted: Large wings from the hendrevail found throughout the northern North Downs.'Let me see here...yes, a task to collect large wings. The Dourhands needed this lesson. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Dourhands and Orcs are working together. This news is not welcome by Refr Quicksilver, a dwarf living among the Eglain of Ost Guruth.Frideric the Elder is at Ost Guruth, north-east of Refr Quicksilver.

Refr Quicksilver is concerned about the Orcs and Dourhands working together and thinks that the leader of the Eglain must be made aware of this development.Talk to Frideric the Elder at Ost Guruth'The Orcs are working with the Dourhands? This is terrible news. The Orcs are recent arrivals in these lands and have been busy digging at a ruin in the east. They have slain many Eglain and taken another of the ruins that we once claimed as our own.

'Could it be that the Dourhands assisted them in this? Friend ${PLAYER}, will you make your way along the road to the east?

'Some distance down the road, you will find Ost Guruth and the leader of the Eglain, Frideric the Elder. Tell him this news.''This news is unwelcome. We knew only that these Orcs came from the north and west. They came under cover at night, lit fires at the ruins, and slaughtered our folk who were there.

'Since that time, they have turned their attentions east towards the great river and south to the mire. They leave the northern swamp for the Dead....

'You, ${PLAYER}, are in the home of the Eglain. We ask that you treat our people and the land about you with respect.''Please make your way east along the road, bearing north where you see the pond covering some ruins. Frideric the Elder must be made aware of this development between the Orcs and Dourhands.'After encountering the elusive barrow-wight, Penthúl, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Gaerion can be found at Sírlond in Cardolan.

You have acquired a trophy from an elusive foe in Cardolan. You should deliver it to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Deliver Penthúl's skull to Gaerion in CardolanAfter encountering and defeating the elusive barrow-wight, Penthúl, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.

You inspect Penthúl's skull closely, searching for any sign of the fell spirit that once took hold of it. Its empty eyes do not meet your gaze, and you believe the bones to finally be at rest.

You should bring this trophy to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.'What are you doing with that? A bit grim, isn't it?'

Gaerion inspects Penthúl's skull closely, studying it in much the same way as you did. After a time, he places the skull gently on a nearby table.

'Alas for the Barrow-downs! Even after so many years of sorrow, the dead do not rest. For my part, I shall see to it that this poor soul is buried where the foul magic of old cannot find them.

'Thank you, friend.'Despite a small supply of arms and armour, Stanric of the Eglain is attempting to organize at least a meagre defence against the wild and dangerous beasts and other enemies which stalk the ruins the Eglain call their homes.Ruin-web hunters and ambushers dwell within the ruins of Amon Ros, west of Ost Guruth.

Stanric explained that spider-venom could be used to create a medicine against poisons and possibly save lives. He sent you to Amon Ros to collect poison from the spiders there.'Always we struggle to survive -- and we have done well in all the years I have dwelled at Ost Guruth -- but this is something the like of which I've not seen before. With the Elders working to defend against the evil in Agamaur, it falls to the rest of us to see to other defences.

'This, my friend, is where I come to you. Our numbers are few, and those capable of wielding a weapon are best kept close to Ost Guruth. You, however, are able to walk freely through the land and assist us in ways in which we cannot help ourselves.

'In the west, beneath the shadow of Amon Ros, lie great nests of venomous spiders among the ruins. Some are more potent than others. Now we have ways passed down through the ages of distilling the venom of the lesser spiders and making a medicine which helps to cure poison. If the venom is too potent it cannot be distilled. I need you to collect the poison sacs from ruin-web hunters and ambushers and bring them to me.''You have done well, my friend. This will aid us in preventing the deaths of many should we find ourselves beset by spiders.'The Wargs have free reign of the unguarded lands of Annúndir, especially upon the toothed hill of Haudh Eglan.Haudh Eglan lies north of a cliff called "Amlaith's Scar," which likewise lies west of Ost Lagoros in Annúndir.

You should defeat Wargs and search Haudh Eglan.Defeat Wargs in Annúndir and investigate Haudh EglanHaudh Eglan lies north of a cliff called "Amlaith's Scar," which likewise lies west of Ost Lagoros in Annúndir.

You should defeat Wargs and search Haudh Eglan.Search the burial mound of Haudh EglanDefeat Wargs in Annúndir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orthonn can be found at Ost Lagoros, bordering the eastern edge of Annúndir (across the river).

You should bring news of Haudh Eglan to Orthonn.Talk to OrthonnThe Wargs have free reign of the unguarded lands of Annúndir, especially upon the toothed hill of Haudh Eglan.

You should defeat the Wargs of Annúndir and investigate Haudh Eglan.'You found evidence of digging among the Wargs at that large mound? That's very strange. I wonder if they found what they were looking for?

'I wonder what it is they seek....'An unusual number of hendrevail, North-hawks, have begun to flock near Othrikar, and Hlája Sagasong fears the Dourhands may be using them to spy on the mine.There are North-hawks all throughout the hills around Othrikar.

Hlája Sagasong has asked you to assist the Dwarves of Othrikar by slaying as many hendrevail as you can.Defeat north-hawks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hlája Sagasong is at Othrikar.

Hlája Sagasong sent you to slay the hendrevail flocking in the hills around Othrikar. You should return to him with the news of your success.Return to Hlája Sagasong'Good day to you! Have you seen all the hendrevail, or North-hawks as some call them?  These wicked birds have always been dangerous, but it seems there are more and more of them about nowadays. I think that the Dourhands have started taming them. I'm afraid they are being used as spies.

'If you would earn favour with us, go out and kill as many of the foul creatures as you can. You can find them all throughout the hills around Othrikar.

'If you will do this for us, I'll see to it that you are rewarded for your work.''I am sure the Dourhands are using those wretched birds to spy on us.''Aye, a fine job you've done for us. I'm still worried about those Dourhands, but at least now perhaps they have been somewhat blinded.'With Mellanion safely back at Sírlond, the search for Annaethel must be renewed, lest she lure away another child.Annaethel has been seen amidst the ruins south of Sírlond. You should bring her the hobby horse and its message from her son.

Sírlond is in central Cardolan.Mellanion's toy and letter have cleared the madness from Annaethel's eyes. You should let her know her son is safe in Sírlond.

Annaethel is in central Cardolan, south of Sírlond.Talk to Annaethel once moreThe knowledge of her son's safe return to Sírlond has disrupted the madness that ailed Annaethel. She is returning to the camp and asks that you meet her there.Meet Annaethel in Sírlond'My mother's grief is a dangerous thing. Here, take back my hobby horse. I have attached a letter to its bridle, letting mother know that I am safe and well.

'Grethron said my mother is at the ruins south of here, but he will not let me go to her. Please! You must find her before she is able to lure away any more children.''I saw a light in the sky! I see so many lights, but this one's bellow tore through the air!'

You inform Annaethel that Mellanion is alive and well, handing her the hobby horse.

'Mellanion? My child? I had a child! But he ran away. No, the Orcs took him! But this is his toy! And there is a note on the bridle you say?''Hail, stranger. You bring joyous tidings! Long have I wandered with a fell gloom blinding my eyes, but now that light has passed and my thoughts are mine once more! My son is safe and nearby! He is at Sírlond, you say? I will go there at once!

'Come and find me in Sírlond, and I shall reward you for all of your efforts!''Well met, honoured friend. You have done so much for my family, and for me! I thank you a thousand times over, and it is not enough. Please, take this as well as my gratitude. You brought my son back to me. You brought me out of my madness. How could I ever repay you enough?'Despite a small supply of arms and armour, Stanric of the Eglain is attempting to organize at least a meagre defence against the wild and dangerous beasts and other enemies which stalk the ruins the Eglain call their homes.The source of the plague can be found in the encampments of the Orcs at Ost Cyrn, south of Ost Guruth.

The Orcs care little about the disease spreading from the corpses and barrels of viscera littering their camp. Stanric gave you his tinderbox and bid you burn out the disease spreading from Ost Cyrn.Stanric awaits the return of his tinderbox at Ost Guruth, north of Ost Cyrn.

You set fire to the corpses and barrels at the Orc-camp in an effort to avoid the spread of plague. You should return to Stanric with word of your success.'You likely know by now that there are Orcs in the south holding vigil on the ruins where once Men stood proud... and foolish. Now disease threatens to spread from Ost Cyrn. The Orcs have no concern for what they breed in their filth. Meat is left to rot and spoil and the innards of the foetid dead bloat and give birth to all manner of disease and corruption.

'We must seek to curb the plague before it spreads, and so I turn once again to you. There is only one way to destroy the plague-bearing filth left by the Orcs: fire.

'Take this tinder and flint and make your way to Ost Cyrn in the south, where the Orcs dwell. Set fire to the bloated corpses and barrels of filth therein.''Be cautious as you set the fires. It will likely draw the attention of nearby Orcs. Return the tinderbox to me when you are done.''You have our gratitude, friend. It pains many to know that the Orcs have claimed the lives of many Eglain who fought against them.

'We will breathe easier knowing that the winds will not carry pestilence from the South.'With the migration of Orcs and goblins to the western Lone-lands, the Eglain attempted to rekindle old trade relations with an Elf from the east. However, they encountered great, lumbering beasts upon the Road and dared not continue forwards.Boars wander to the east between Ost Guruth and the Last Bridge, and Wargs wander the area near Ost Guruth along Great East Road. Frideric resides at the Eglain Ruin-hold of Ost Guruth, north of the Great East Road in the Lone-lands.

Frideric asked you to help him gather a peace offering of boar-haunches and Warg-fur for the strange creatures living near the Last Bridge at the eastern end of the Great East Road.'Our paths cross again, ${PLAYER}. There is something I would like to ask of you, a favour that may prove dangerous. Our trades were not limited entirely to Bree-town in the west. We have, in our time, traded with an Elf from the east as well.

'When the raids in the west began we sent some of our kindred eastward to rekindle our trade with the Elf, but they returned after seeing great, lumbering creatures near the road east.

'The creatures did not attack and acted in an intelligent manner. I hope to gather goods for them to offer as a promise of peace with them. If you are willing, I will need meat from the boars roaming to the east and fur from the Wargs nearby.''Aye, these will do nicely. They will fill our stores in time and allow us to part with some of the meats we have treated and the furs will prove that we share a common enemy.

'We have prepared the offering already, but needed to replenish our stock before moving forwards.'The Dwarves of Othrikar are beset by supply troubles, stemming from the incursion of Orcs and Wargs from the north and the betrayal of the Dourhands.Bears live in the hills south of Othrikar.

To help allay Othrikar's supply shortage, Hornbori has asked you to hunt bears for their meat.'Listen, friend, we have problems here. Big problems. Those cursed Dourhands weren't content to ruin the operation of the mines while they were here, but when they left, they ruined or stole what food we had.

'Then there's Orcs and Wargs that came down from the north, driving the farmers who used to supply us out of their fields. And when we venture out of Othrikar, we have to do so in force, due to the Dourhands camped nearby. In short, we are having a lot of trouble with supplies.

'We need meat. I can give you a fee in good silver, if you could hunt some bears for us. They wander the hills to the south.''Good, good. That meat should last us quite a while. Thank you for your help, friend.The hendrevail have taken to nesting in Kamskut, a sinister-looking structure nestled in the mountains north-east of Othrikar.Falconer Brúni is at Kamskut in the North Downs, north-east of Othrikar.

You should defeat Brúni and his falconers.Defeat Brúni and his falconersFalconer Brúni is at Kamskut in the North Downs, north-east of Othrikar.

You should defeat Brúni and his falconers.Defeat Dourhand Falconers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated Brúni, a Dourhand falconer, and his fellows at Kamskut.CompletedThe hendrevail have taken to nesting in Kamskut, a sinister-looking structure nestled in the mountains north-east of Othrikar.

You should investigate Kamskut, for this nesting behaviour is surely not natural.You have defeated Brúni, a Dourhand falconer, and his fellows at Kamskut.A large contingent of Orcs has moved south-west, spearheading the main host as they march through the North Downs. These are Orcs of the Ongbúrz tribe out of Angmar, cunning builders of siege weapons and defences.The Ongbúrz camp is south-west of Esteldín across the Kingsfell.

You should slay the Orc-chieftain in hopes that his death will throw the Ongbúrz into disarray.Defeat the Ongbúrz ChieftainThe Ongbúrz camp is south-west of Esteldín across the Kingsfell.

You should slay the Orc-chieftain in hopes that his death will throw the Ongbúrz into disarray.You have defeated the Ongbúrz Chieftain, a cruel Orc who stood at the head of Gurzlum's might.CompletedA large party of Orcs has established a camp south-west of Esteldín, a spearhead pushing north-west from their main host at Dol Dínen.

You should slay the chieftain of the Orcs at this camp. With his defeat, any remaining  Ongbúrz Orcs will be unable to act until a new leader arrives.You have defeated the Ongbúrz Chieftain, a cruel Orc who stood at the head of Gurzlum's might.After earning the trust of the Eglain, they confessed that Radagast the Brown, another of Gandalf's order, currently resides in a tower within their homestead of Ost Guruth.Radagast the Brown dwells in seclusion within a tower in the ruins of Ost Guruth, the place the Eglain call home. Seek out his counsel.

Gadaric and Hunulf Munce believe that your intentions are good and have provided you with the whereabouts of their most esteemed guest.Talk to Radagast the Brown'You have been patient, ${PLAYER}. You must understand, we are solitary people. We hold our friends dear to us and protect them as we can.

'Radagast is such a friend and here at the behest of our leader to aid us with a terror emerging from a long slumber. He has taken residence in the ruins of Ost Guruth, the place we call home. 

'You are welcome to make the journey to the east. The ruin is north of the Great East Road before the road snakes between the northern and southern swamp. Seek Radagast within the tower. Perhaps there is a way that you can aid him as he will surely aid you.''${PLAYER}, you have proven over and again the sincerity of your claims. It is only fair then that we honour your request and provide you the information that you require.

'Radagast is ever a friend to our people. He comes to us now as a favour to our leader who called him when the wildlife in Agamaur turned foul. He is in private study in the last tower in the back of the ruins of Ost Guruth, the place where we make our home.

'Seek him out. Perhaps there is a way that both of you can aide the other.''You will find Radagast alone with his notes and companions within the tower at the back of the ruins of Ost Guruth. Make your way there and speak with him. You have the blessing of the Eglain.''You will find Radagast alone with his notes and companions within the tower at the back of the ruins of Ost Guruth. Make your way there and speak with him. You have the blessing of the Eglain.''Radagast the Brown can likely be found at Ost Guruth, far to the east of Weathertop. If he has already moved on, look for him then at the camp of the Eglain in Agamaur. He may be able to tell us the meaning of the runes left on Weathertop.''If you seek our visitor, you will likely find him in the tower at the end of the main avenue. On occasion, he stays at our camp in Agamaur.

'Be quick about your business, ${RACE}. We do not enjoy strangers in our midst.''The Eglain let you in, did they? I have been expecting you, though sooner rather than later. You have in fact taken your time. I understand you found something upon Weathertop?

'This rubbing... I do believe it is a G-rune, a mark oft used by Gandalf the Grey, another of my order. I guessed from the description of those three lines that he was at Amon Sûl on October the third, naught but a few days past.

'I, too, saw the lightning upon Weathertop and fear that Gandalf found trouble there. I have not seen him since we parted at Sarn Ford, so I can tell you no more of him or his travels. Of this place, I can tell you much, but make reason of very little.'An aura of corruption drew Radagast the Brown to the Red Swamp. He needs help to uncover the source of this corruption.The bog-prowlers wander in Haragmar, east of Ost Guruth.

In order to learn what might be causing the corruption of the swamp, Radagast needs bits of moss from the nests of the bog-prowlers.Collect bog-moss from bog-prowler nests (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Radagast waits in Ost Guruth, west of Haragmar.

You should return to Radagast the Brown with the moss you collected.'The land itself is turning against us, and I know not the reason why. Even the shepherds are twisted shadows of their former selves. I speak of the bog-prowlers, those that tend to the trees. 

'If I can discern what is twisting the shepherds, I may be able to determine what is fouling the land. Bring me the moss that they use to line their nests. They roam the swamp to the east of here.

'Remember, I do not wish to harm the shepherds, no matter how confused they appear. They are innocent victims of the corruption and know not the harm they do.''Act with all due haste and return the bog-moss to me.''You did well. This moss will help me to understand how the evil is tainting the Red Swamp.

'This is but the first step in determining what caused the shepherds to become confused and irrational.

'There is more still to do, my friend. It will not be easy.'Several of the next pages have been torn from the journal. Were they removed by Narndir or by some other hand, and for what purpose?Handarod is at Sírlond, the Elf-camp across the river from Caranost.

Meneldir wonders if Handarod might know what happened to the missing pages of Narndir's journal.Talk to Handarod at SírlondHandarod once saw Narndir acting strangely near the lone square ruin beside the road in Ruddymore, north-west of Herne.Search the strange square ruin beside the road in Ruddymore where Handarod once saw Narndir acting strangelyYou may have found another secret cache of Narndir's, but it is possible that Orcs or their allies found it first.Open the small wooden box and search for the missing pages of Narndir's journalOrcs, half-orcs, or goblins throughout the South Downs or Ruddymore may have stolen the missing pages of Narndir's journal, and you should recover them if you can.Collect the missing pages of Narndir's journal from Orcs, half-orcs, or goblins in the South Downs or Ruddymore (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The table at Scurloc Farm will be a suitable workspace for reassembling Narndir's journal.You have restored Narndir's journal, and perhaps now you can learn why pages were removed from it.Read Narndir's restored journalMeneldir told you he would meet up with you at outside Dol Ernil after you learned what you could from Narndir's journal.Narndir's journal describes the throne room of Dol Ernil, located north of the Ranger Gorwen's camp-site and up some flights of stairs.

You should travel to the location described by the journal, and read the appropriate pages while you are there.Narndir's writing has conjured the sight of the Grey Fear to trouble your mind.Consider the image of the Grey FearNarndir describes the death of Ostir, Last Prince of Cardolan, with such detail that the account would pain any friend of the Dúnedain or their folk.Consider the death of Ostir, Last Prince of Cardolan, as described in the writings of NarndirMeneldir is waiting at Gorwen's camp south of Dol Ernil for you to return from your investigation inside the keep.'After the missing pages, Narndir writes of Dol Ernil, the keep from which Prince Amondir ruled. I will go ahead and see what I can learn from there, but I think you should return to Sírlond to speak with Handarod. He knew the location where Narndir kept this journal. Perhaps he will know why some of the pages were removed. If you solve that mystery, I will meet up with you again outside Dol Ernil.''We should return to Sírlond to speak with Handarod about this. He knew the location where Narndir kept this journal. Perhaps he will know why some of the pages were removed.''Missing pages? I cannot say I know anything about that, ${PLAYER}, but I do not think Narndir would have torn pages from his own journal. Someone else must have removed them... although I cannot guess why such a one would return the journal to its hiding-place.'

Handarod rubs the side of his face, thinking deeply.

'There was one day that I spoke with Narndir and marked that he acted quite strangely, however. I encountered him on the Greenway, not far from the lone square ruin that stands beside the road to the north-west of Herne. Now that I think on it, his manner was quite peculiar, and it seemed he was not his usual self. What did he say? He said something strange to me, but I cannot remember it!

'If he did remove pages from his journal, which was not like him at all, that is the only day on which I would believe it. I wish I could remember what he said, but perhaps it will come to me. You should search that square ruin beside the road in Ruddymore. Maybe you will find something to shed light upon this mystery.''You should search that square ruin beside the road in Ruddymore. Maybe you will find something to shed light upon this mystery.''You should search that square ruin beside the road in Ruddymore. Maybe you will find something to shed light upon this mystery.''Creatures of Orc-kind have ransacked Narndir's secret coffer? There is no shortage of the beasts throughout the South Downs, but whether you will find what you seek in the clutches of Orcs, half-orcs, or goblins, I cannot say.''Meneldir said he would meet up with you just south of Dol Ernil, in Tyrn Gorthad? Be careful... that place has been a site of ill omen for hundreds of years.''There you are, my friend! I have been speaking with Gorwen, a Ranger who has taken it upon herself to keep watch upon Dol Ernil, and we are both very interested to hear if you have learned anything more from Narndir's journal. She did not know him when he lived, so I have been telling her stories of our childhoods. It is... it is good to remember, I have to admit it. But what have you learned?'

You hand Meneldir the journal and at your direction, he reads several of the pages.

'There it is again: the Grey Fear. There is more to learn of this foe, I know it. But listen to this! "The battle-plan of Angmar was three-fold, but Prince Ostir learned too late that more than just Amon Sûl was endangered; he rode north to the defence of Weathertop with most of his household, and only when the southern sky filled with smoke from a great burning did he realize that Cardolan had been ravaged while he was away. He fought the Battle of Weathertop through eyes bleary with tears, and only when it was done did his King give him permission to return. He galloped with utmost haste back to Dol Ernil, but he was unprepared for the sight that awaited him in the throne room."

'These pages speak of the throne room of Dol Ernil. That is where you should read the rest of the account, ${PLAYER}. Gorwen tells me the throne room is north of here, and up several flights of stairs.''These pages speak of the throne room of Dol Ernil. That is where you should read the rest of the account, ${PLAYER}. Gorwen tells me the throne room is north of here, and up several flights of stairs.''By the time he finally reached Dol Ernil,' Narndir writes, 'Prince Ostir's keep was a ruined shell. Its people lay heaped and bloody in the streets, the heads of its defenders mounted on stakes above its gates. Of his mother Luilloth, there was no sign at all. And in the shattered hall, seated upon Ostir's throne, was the Grey Fear.

'I hesitate to write what I have learned, but I must. I must! My duty compels me to write it, to write the truth! But can it be right to put into words such horror, such shame as this? It wars within me.

'Prince Ostir stood in the charnel-house that was Dol Ernil, and he looked in the eyes of the creature that sat upon the throne, and he knew the identity of the Grey Fear. It was Prince Amondir who sat there, Ostir's own grandfather, but the servant of evil that stared back was a stranger entire. With a horrible smile upon its mocking visage, the apparition raised its hand, pointing at Amondir's terrified grandson.

'And the Dead rose.''The wights had simply been waiting for their master's command,' Narndir writes, 'Now they emerged from among the slain, including some freshly killed in the recent battle, and they fell upon Ostir and his knights. Panicking, the Prince fled the burning stronghold, escaping into the Barrow-downs, but he might as well have tried escaping a smith's hammer by fleeing to his anvil. The Angmarim were waiting there in ambush for him, and among their number were hill-folk, Orcs, and cultists of the Witch-king. The Dead drove Ostir's dwindling company straight toward this enemy force, and there in the darkness, caught between the living and the dead, every last one of Cardolan’s defenders died. Prince Ostir perished last of all, sobbing with despair as one of Carn Dûm's warriors drove a spear of cold iron through his heart. His bones, and those of his company, vanished among the downs.

'I have written it! Is that what you wanted of me? I have finished it! My duty is fulfilled... but the account is too terrible. I will tear these pages from my journal, and I will hide them! Yes, let none know how badly the Men of Cardolan failed. I will bury the Witch-king's victory as deep as deep allows, and so triumph over Angmar myself. The Grey Fear has not been seen in hundreds of years, and I will not give the creature life by letting this account be read by anyone. If there is any need for these pages in the days to come, I will retrieve them myself. But I can see no such day ahead of us, and that is a blessing.''Narndir was at Sarn Ford when the Black Riders attacked, ${PLAYER}, and he fled, as the others of the Dúnedain did. We found his body not far from the Greenway, and I thought perhaps he made for the gathering-places of his kin. But what if it was not so? What if he made instead for the lone square ruin that stands further south beside the Greenway? I wonder....

'I think he saw the return of the wraiths and believed the Grey Fear would once again haunt its lands of old, Cardolan-that-was. I think he was running to retrieve the torn pages of his journal, ${PLAYER}, so the Dúnedain would know the terror they might be up against. He wanted to arm them with the knowledge of what they might face, but he was struck down by the Black Riders, by the Nazgûl, before he could reach the place where he hid the true account.

'I think Narndir knew the peril, and died trying to warn us of it: the Lord of the Nazgûl plans to raise the Grey Fear from its slumber, endangering all of the north.'Your attacks on the Orcs in Gatson's fields has stirred their peers' wrath. Gatson expects a raid at any moment.Gatson's man Alwin is with the herd, south of the farm.

Gatson has asked you to help his farmhand, Alwin, defend his herd from the marauding Orcs. Make sure neither Alwin nor any of the herd come to harm.Talk to Gatson's farmhand AlwinGatson is at his farm, north of the fields where his herd grazes.

You and Alwin managed to stave off the Orcs' raid on Gatson's herd. You should return to the farm and tell Gatson.'It's my fault, I guess, but when you killed those Orcs in my fields, the rest of their ilk got pretty stirred up. I think they're getting ready to raid my herd, grazing to the south of here.

'I'd like you to go down there and help my man Alwin defend the herd from those Orcs. Alwin's a fair hand with a bow, but he's no fighter. Them Orcs will finish my herd quick, and maybe worse. Make sure Alwin doesn't get hurt on my account!

'I don't think the Orcs will be caught unawares this time.''Alwin's a fair shot with that bow of his but them Orcs will make a meal of him and the herd if you don't help.''Alwin's with the herd a-ways south of here. Make sure he's not hurt because of my stubborn pride.''Oh, it's good to see ye! There's Orcs gathering out there. We best be on our guard!''Ye tell Master Gatson o' the drubbing what we gave them Orcs!''You have my thanks for helping out and keeping Alwin safe, but I'm afraid this doesn't solve the problem. Those Orcs'll be back. You can be sure of that.'Hana the Young at Ost Guruth desires glossy essences from the Dead found in the eastern Lone-lands.You accepted a task from Hana the Young to acquire glossy essences from the Dead in the eastern Lone-lands. You can find Hana the Young within Ost Guruth.Wanted: Glossy essences from the Dead in the eastern Lone-lands. Bring them to Hana the Young at Ost Guruth.Wanted: Glossy essences from the Dead in the eastern Lone-lands.'The Lore-master in Brown said that there is magic to be found in this dust. I wonder if it true.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Hana the Young at Ost Guruth desires large finger bones from the Dead found in the eastern Lone-lands.You accepted a task from Hana the Young to acquire large finger bones from the Dead in the eastern Lone-lands. You can find Hana the Young within Ost Guruth.Wanted: Large finger bones from the Dead in the eastern Lone-lands. Bring them to Hana the Young at Ost Guruth.Wanted: Large finger bones from the Dead in the eastern Lone-lands.'It is good to know that there are still some in this world willing to help each other.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Now that fish have been procured for Henbran, she is willing to talk to you about Luilloth. Memory, however, fades as time goes by. And much time has passed for Henbran.Henbran Amon has suggested you retrieve the Amon family grimoire from her sister, Cedrach the Cruel.

Cedrach lives in the Old Forest, in Cardolan.Find Cedrach the CruelCedrach Amon has asked you to gather willow bark, yarrow, and slippery cap mushrooms while she searches her cottage for the trunk.

You should look for these items in the Old Forest.Collect slippery cap mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect willow bark (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect yarrow (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the yarrow, willow bark, and mushrooms to Cedrach.

Cedrach is in the Old Forest.'Very well. You did as I asked. I will allow you to ask in turn. What is it that you want from me?'

You explain your search for information about Luilloth.

'Luilloth? How old do you think I am!? I am no Elf, to live thousands of years! ...Now that I think on it, however, the name does dredge up some old memories.'

Henbran ponders, her wrinkled face crinkling even more as she thinks.

'Yes, I do recall Luilloth having some dealings with my great ancestor. But I cannot help you. The knowledge of what they spoke is lost. Stolen! By my wicked sister, Cedrach the Cruel. T'was she who stole the Amon Grimoire - the book of secrets my family has passed down for generations. Among its pages I recall an account of the Lady Luilloth, but what she came here for, I no longer recall. Time has stolen my memories, my sister has stolen the account. Along with so very much more! She left me, you know. All alone in this dark forest. An old woman with no one. So lonely beneath the black branches, so alone! So very alone!'

She pauses dramatically, as though hoping you might offer some words of comfort, but then she rushes on before you are able to speak.

'She lives down by the Brandywine, so perhaps the grimoire is not wholly lost. I have never been to her new home, which I am sure is a hovel. I am too old to make the journey, she made sure to be far enough away to ensure that! You could go looking for the book, to get your answers. If you seize it back, bring it to me! I would reward you greatly! But beware of my sister, ${CLASS}. She has had decades to study the grimoire. Who knows what dark powers she now wields.''Hello there. Why yes, I am Cedrach Amon. I see you have been over to speak with my sister. Henbran is as dramatic as ever, it seems! Well now, I do not know about the grimoire she is speaking of. I would never have dreamed of stealing it from her! I know how import- oh dear.'

She stops speaking, her brow furrowed.

'Oh dear. When I decided to start keeping my own house with my husband, I took few things with me. I did, however, take a large chest full of blankets that I had knitted, as well as the wedding dress I was sewing. Oh my, that was so long ago now! My husband is many years passed and my own daughter has gone on to wed and lose her own husband. But I digress - perhaps the grimoire was within the chest when I left to come here! Oh, if so, I feel awful. I never intended to take Henny's grimoire. She was always so much more dedicated to the family history and all the teachings from our ancestors. No wonder she thinks I am cruel! Oh dear!

'Listen, I would impose a boon upon you, if you will. My daughter has been sick for many days and I was just about to head out to gather herbs with which to treat her ailments. If you will gather what I need, I will go look for the chest.

'I need willow bark, yarrow, and slippery cap mushrooms. I will head inside now to look for that chest.''You found everything? Why thank you! The willow bark and yarrow will help my daughter feel better, while the mushrooms will be part of tonight's meal.'Silvery strands of gloom-web from the weavers of the Old Forest can both save and endanger lives.Hafren has asked you to collect gloom-web from Nettinglade.

Gloom-web can be found in Nettinglade, in the Old Forest.Collect gloom-webs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the gloom-web you collected to Hafren.

Hafren is at Gerwyn's Convoy, in northern Cardolan.'Greetings, fellow traveller. I am Hafren, healer for Gerwyn's Convoy. When we set off for Cardolan I knew it was going to be a bit rougher than travelling through Bree, but not quite this rough! We were attacked two nights ago by wights and were forced to circle our waggons. We have injured here, and I am nearly out of bandages.

'On the far side of the road lies the southern reaches of the Old Forest. Beneath its ancient trees lies a deadly foe, but one who spins a thing most priceless. Spider-silk works incredibly well as a bandage for wounds, did you know? If you are going to brave the Old Forest, please bring me back any clean spider-silk that you might find. Together, we can save the lives of my fellow travellers.''Welcome back! The spider-silk you gathered is exactly what I needed! I feel slightly more confident that I will be able to keep up with the injuries my companions keep coming down with.'The Dwarves of Othrikar are beset by supply troubles, stemming from the incursion of Orcs and Wargs from the north and the betrayal of the Dourhands.Stolen sacks of grain can be found at Gurzlum, south-west of Gatson's Farm in the Kingsfell.

You should recover stolen sacks of grain from Gurzlum.Collect stolen grain (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gatson is at his farm in the Kingsfell, in the central North Downs.

You should speak with Gatson at his farm.Bring the grain to Gatson'There may just be a chance that you could help me. It seems to me that all of my stolen grain may still be at the Orcs' camp not far from here. If you can recover that grain for me, I'll be glad to sell it off to Esteldín or Othrikar.

'The Orcs have a big camp to the south and west of here. I don't know where in the camp they would be keeping the stolen grain, but you should retrieve four large sacks.

'Bring me those sacks, and I'll see what I can do for the dwarves.''Our grain! I had forgotten, for we were attacked while you were away. There -- you see? That Elf-fellow came in the nick of time, and took out several invading Orcs.

'It grows ever more turbulent, despite our good fortune in receiving aid of late. I worry that we will have to abandon this farm soon.'You discovered a stone marker in the midst of a barren, but fertile land. The marker declared the surrounding land to be the property of Bardulf Gilmar and his heirs. If any such heirs yet live, it is unlikely that they know of their legacy.An heir to Bardulf Gilmar may yet be found somewhere in the North Downs.Talk to Gilmar at Gatson's FarmOrthonn is at the ruins of Ost Lagoros, north-west of Gatson's Farm.

You found a stone marker bequeathing land to the forebear of Gatson's workhand, Gilmar. Gilmar has asked you to ask the Ranger Orthonn about his forebear.Talk to Orthonn at Ost LagorosIstuienn is at Esteldín, east of Gatson's farm.

You found a stone marker bequeathing land to the forebear of Gatson's workhand, Gilmar. Gilmar sent you to speak to the Ranger Orthonn, but Orthonn was unable to help. He did, however, suggest you seek Istuienn, the archivist at Esteldín.Talk to Istuienn at EsteldínGilmar is at Gatson's farm.

Istuienn, who keeps the Rangers' archives, provided you an excerpt of some old chronicles explaining how Gilmar's ancestor saved the life of Eärnur, an ancient Prince of Gondor, and was granted land in perpetuity for his valour.On the face of this marker is a weathered engraving: 'This land is granted to Bardulf Gilmar and his line, now and forever, in recognition of his valiant deeds. Eärnur, son of Eärnil, Prince of Gondor, T.A. 1975.'

The surrounding land is barren and abandoned. If Gilmar's heirs yet live, they may not be aware of their legacy.'I own land? A farm o' me own? I always wanted a farm o' me own. But...I durstn't leave Mr. Gatson.

'What did he do, I wonder? The old fellow, I mean. That one what the old rock tells about. What did he do to get that land? I can't rightly take it if I ain't heard how he got it. I mean, he might've done something wicked and cruel....

'Say, maybe those Rangers know something about this. They seem to know lots o' old stories. Maybe that fellow we sell provisions to, Orthonn, will know something about this. He's out in those ruins to the west -- Ost Lagoros, he calls 'em.''Maybe that Orthonn fellow out in Ost Lagoros will know something o' me ancestor.''Herbs, plants, and animals, I know well. Ask me the names of all the kings of Arnor and Gondor, and I can tell you. Of Gilmar's forebear, I know naught.

'You are a friend of the Rangers, and if you do not know of it already, you should learn that my kindred dwell in some ruins to the east of Gatson's farm. Esteldín, we call it. Istuienn, a kinswoman of mine, keeps watch over our archives there. She may be able to help you.''If you wish to know the history of herbs, speak with me...for the history of Men, you should go to Istuienn in Esteldín.''Bardulf Gilmar...the name rings familiar.

'If it was Eärnur -- then Prince of Gondor -- who granted this land to Bardulf Gilmar as you say, then it might have been for some deed during Eärnur's battle with Angmar at Fornost. Our archive does possess the remnant of a chronicle of the Battle of Fornost.

'I will make a fair copy of the pages related to your search for you to carry away with you.''You should bring that excerpt to Gilmar...it should please him greatly.''So it was a good deed me forebear did!

'And I know just what I'll do with his land. Me and Mr. Gatson talked about it while you were gone away to see them Rangers. We'll be partners, we will! We'll hire some hands and work both farms, soon as we finish clearing out them Orcs and such.

'Thank'ee for the helping hand!'Orcs bearing the standard of Angmar poured into the Lone-lands from the North and have occupied Ost Cyrn, a ruin south-west of Ost Guruth.Wargs stalk the stretches of land to the south and west of Ost Guruth, especially in the areas surrounding Ost Cyrn.

Grimdeal has asked you to hunt the Wargs for their paws to prove to the Eglain that the fear they inspire can be overcome.'Your first efforts against the Orcs in the South proved more than capable. Already, I have seen some faces through the camp bearing smiles where once there were none.

'I am grateful for your assistance, truly, but I would ask another favour of you now. The Orcs train beasts of war which have discovered our scouts, injuring some and slaying others. These stalkers strike fear into the hearts of my people, and if we can show them courage, we can further the work you have begun.

'I speak of the Wargs, roaming the lands south and west of Ost Guruth, especially in the areas surrounding Ost Cyrn. The Orcs mark the beasts that they favour by scarring their paws. Gather such scarred paws from this foe, and we will show the Eglain there is little to fear from this enemy.''A fine collection -- one that shall inspire the people assuredly. They will know that this foe can be brought low, and that you are the hero who has done so.

'The stretch of road will be somewhat safer, I think, and perhaps we can afford to send runners to Hunulf and Gadaric Munce with supplies for trade. Your efforts inspire and assist us all, ${PLAYER}.'It may be too late to rescue the people who have been dragged into Gond Orchal, but they can still be saved in death.Delun has asked you to find what's left of her friends in Gond Orchal and burn their bodies. They're somewhere north-west of Delun's location.

You should start with Einion.Find and burn Einion's corpseDelun has asked you to find what's left of her friends in Gond Orchal and burn their bodies. They're somewhere north-west of Delun's location.

You should search for Curig next.Find and burn Curig's corpseDelun has asked you to find what's left of her friends in Gond Orchal and burn their bodies. They're somewhere north-west of Delun's location.

Melwyn is the last person you must find.Find and burn Melwyn's corpseThe remains of Delun's friends have been found.

You should return to her location outside of Gond Orchal.Return to Delun outside of Gond OrchalYou returned too late.

The stranger seems to have something to say to you.Talk to MunaThe bloodied woman looks up in shock. She flinches and almost scrambles to flee before something dawns upon her.

'You -- you're no wight. Are you here to rescue us? I-I beg of you, find my friends. They... they killed Muna first, then dragged the rest of us into the barrows. I managed to crawl out, but... Einion, Curig, Melwyn... they're somewhere further north-west.

'Please, if you find... what's left of them, burn them. My injuries can wait.''You must be one who offered to help Delun. She told me what happened before... before she...

'At least... she realized I had survived, after all.

'I'll do what I can for her. Please, leave me. I wish to be alone.'Farmer Gatson's farmhand, Gilmar, says that new blood is needed for the cattle herd. An aurochs-bull would do nicely, but the wild bulls are far too aggressive to drive to his pasture. Gilmar has asked you to obtain a yearling instead.Aurochs yearlings can be found amidst the small herd to the south-east of Gatson's farm.

Gilmar, Gatson's farmhand, has asked you to pick out an aurochs-yearling from the local herd and drive it over to his pasture.Find an aurochs-yearlingGilmar is back on Gatson's farm.

You have brought the aurochs-yearling to Gatson's pasture, now you should return to Gilmar to tell him of your success.'Everyone on a farm needs to pull their own weight, even fancy strangers out o' the south. We've got our hands full here dealing with the harvest and worrying about them Orcs nearby.

'Anyways, Master Gatson's little herd of cattle could use some new blood. Wild aurochs would do nicely, but the bulls are far too aggressive to drive into the pasture. So what I want you to do is to pick out a good-looking yearling from the wild aurochs herd not far to the south-east.

'A young aurochs should be pretty docile... just don't get in the way of any of the bulls and you should be fine. And, oh yes, I've seen some Orcs out there lately, so be careful of them too, hear me?''An aurochs-yearling's just the thing we need to revive the herd. I saw a wild herd with a yearling the other day, just over the hill there.''If ye want to prove your worth round here you'll go get that aurochs for me? Thankee kindly.''Ah, it's true the Orc raids have whittled the herd to almost nothing. An aurochs-yearling would go a long way to bringing it back.'The young aurochs lows plaintively and runs off ahead of you.'Yearlings spook easily. It's best to keep him somewhere quiet. Don't you worry though, he'll be running about soon enough.''Good work herding that yearling. You let Gilmar know you're back, he's been fretting since you left.''That's a fine job, a right fine job, I say! Thanks for your help with that there aurochs. Master Gatson asked me to give you this small payment for your work.'Orcs bearing the standard of Angmar poured into the Lone-lands from the North and have occupied Ost Cyrn, a ruin south-west of Ost Guruth.The Orcs can be found in the ruins of Ost Cyrn, south-west of Ost Guruth.

Grimdeal asked that you help defend the Eglain by assaulting the Orcs of Ost Cyrn.Defeat angered Tarkrîps (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat dreadful Tarkrîps (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Grimdeal awaits word of your success at Ost Guruth, north-east of Ost Cyrn.

You should return to Grimdeal with word of your victory.'You are a fine friend to the Eglain as is proven by your deeds on behalf of Hunulf and Gadaric Munce. This is why I feel that I can share my concerns with you. Orcs came from the North and moved beneath cover of night into Ost Cyrn, the ruin beyond the road to the south and west. If you have spoken to Refr Quicksilver, then you know that these Orcs are in league with the dwarves occupying Thandobel.

'The threat of Orcs so close to Ost Guruth shakes the confidence of my people. We have suffered so much recently, and the rise of these ravening hordes has upset my people greatly.

'If you are willing to assist us, I would ask that you go to Ost Cyrn and make war upon the Orcs to show the Eglain that there are folk willing to aid us. What say you?''If you are willing to aid us, you may help in the restoration of the Eglain's courage.''Though it will travel slowly, the news of your victory will spread, and the Eglain will come to know you.

'I offer you my thanks for all that you have done and hope that you will continue to aid us into the future.'Orcs bearing the standard of Angmar poured into the Lone-lands from the North and have occupied Ost Cyrn, a ruin south-west of Ost Guruth.The Orcs' leader and their weapons may be found somewhere within the ruins of Ost Cyrn, south-west of Ost Guruth.

Grimdeal believes that if you can slay the leader of the Orcs and steal their weapons, they will collapse in upon themselves, and the Lone-lands will be freed from their threat.Grimdeal awaits your return at Ost Guruth, north-east of Ost Cyrn.

You should return at once to Grimdeal, bringing him the Orc-swords you collected as evidence of your victory.'You have strength within you to inspire and deliver hope where there is none. You have proven this time and again.

'I ask now that you again assail Ost Cyrn and tear their heart from them. Within the walls of the ruin there is a taskmaster who drives the Orcs with fear and strength of arms. His evil knows no bounds, and his reign is unyielding. However, we have learned that his force is limited in arms, and if we can defeat the taskmaster and steal enough of the Orcs' weapons, they may collapse upon themselves.

'Go to Ost Cyrn and collect weapons from the Orcs you face. If you find their leader, slay him.''We may yet strike a blow against the Orcs that will aid not only the Eglain, but all of Eriador. Please see to the task I have given you and defeat the Orc-leader at Ost Cyrn.''We shall see if the Orcs are able to rally behind another, or if they falter as we hope. Regardless, this has been a great victory.

'Your deeds will be recounted throughout the Eglain, and you will be known to all. I offer you my gratitude and the gratitude of my people.

'We wish to honour all that you have done for us, ${PLAYER}. As we have not much to offer, I hope this will suffice.'A number of Dourhand miners have gathered at Thurûm, too close to Othrikar for comfort. You should defeat the Dourhands that have amassed at Thurûm.Dourhand miners can be found at Thûrum, north of Othrikar.

You should defeat miners at Thurûm.Defeat Dourhands at Thurûm (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated a number of the dangerous Dourhand miners at Thurûm, reducing the grave threat to Othrikar.CompletedA number of Dourhand miners have gathered at Thurûm, too close to Othrikar for comfort.

You should defeat the Dourhands that have amassed at Thurûm.You have defeated a number of the dangerous Dourhand miners at Thurûm, reducing the grave threat to Othrikar.Orcs and evil Men out of Angmar have overrun the virtually empty North Downs, threatening the safety of the few farmers who remain there.Complete quests within North DownsOrthonn has directed you to Othrikar, the nearest outpost that would welcome a weary traveller.Othrikar can be found to the north-east of Orthonn's camp, in the northern hills of the Kingsfell.

You should seek the dwarf fortress of Othrikar.Find the dwarf keep of Othrikar'You must be weary from your efforts and long travels, ${PLAYER}. Loth as I would be to see you leave, I feel obligated to tell you that there is a dwarf-keep not far from here that offers far more by way of comforts than this sad little camp-fire ever could.

'Othrikar is the name the dwarves have given it. They are friendly, as dwarves go. If you wish to find a more habitable place to stay, it is a very short distance to the north-east, a stone dwelling nestled into the hills.''Well met, traveller! Welcome to Othrikar. You may find rest here, or work, if it suits you. What do you say to that?'The dwarf-mine at Othrikar is almost played out. Regin has heard of a rich "father-lode" somewhere in Nan Amlug, and he hopes to determine the location of this lode.The lost mining records are somewhere around Othrikar, probably in a chest or locker.

Regin has asked you to recover old mining-records that may give a clue to the location of the legendary "father-lode".'I'm Regin, in charge of smelting here at Othrikar. The yields are down again -- we're running out of silver in this mine -- but I've heard that somewhere in these hills there is a "father-lode" with veins rich with gold, and maybe even mithril -- though I find it unlikely, as Moria-silver's not been found anywhere but Khazad-dûm.

'The Dourhands ruined the old prospecting records when they left, though. We need to find the proper records to find the father-lode. I suspect these records are still around Othrikar someplace, perhaps in a chest or locker.

'If you can discover them, you'll be doing us Longbeards a real service!''Aye! That looks like it could be it!

'But wait... what is this? Most of these records are just ordinary prospectors' reports, not too useful, but this one....

'I cannot make this page out at all! Could this be the key to the mystery of the father-lode?'You have arrived at Thurûm, and it is crawling with Dourhands. These foes are surely organized, and pose a dire threat to Othrikar.Thurûm is north of Othrikar.

You should search Thurûm for the source of the Dourhand presence, and defeat the leader of this rabble.Find and defeat the leader of the DourhandsThurûm is north of Othrikar.

You should search Thurûm for the source of the Dourhand presence, and defeat the leader of this rabble.Hannar is at Othrikar.

Torfi Hammerhorn perished during the battle at his camp, routing his forces. You should return to Hannar with news of your victory.You have arrived at Thurûm, and it is crawling with Dourhands. These foes are surely organized, and pose a dire threat to Othrikar.'You defeated Torfi Hammerhorn? Truly? This is welcome news for us Longbeards! Long have we suffered the oppression of those loutish Dourhands, and we do not wish to suffer it again.

'You have done us a noble service, my friend. The Longbeards of Othrikar will long remember your name!'Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of flawed ears to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect flawed ears from the beasts in the area surrounding Esteldín to the west. Once collected, you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Flawed ears from the beasts west of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Flawed ears from the beasts west of Esteldín.'It may seem a strange request, but these do hold some value, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of ruffled feathers to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect ruffled feathers from the birds in the area surrounding Esteldín to the north and west. Once collected, you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Ruffled feathers from the birds to the north and west of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Ruffled feathers from the birds to the north and west of Esteldín.'We will get to work on these, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Regin is unable to make sense of the runes in the mining records you found. Strangely enough, they appear to be written in an Elvish script.Dwarves can be found all over Othrikar.

Surely one of them can translate the Elvish mining records.Regin is at Othrikar in the North Downs.

The old records have been translated, but apparently a map is required before the directions will make sense. You should return to Regin with the deciphered records.'I can't ken these runes at all! Why, unless I miss my mark entirely, I think these are some kind of Elf-writing.

'Now, how could directions to a dwarf-mine have ever been written in some Elvish script? Do you think anyone in these parts could understand this language?''Elvish, eh? Sorry, friend. I can't help you with that.''Yes, I can read a spot of Elvish. My father was a scholar, and he learned many things from Balin. He passed a fair bit of it on to me. Let me have a look!

'Ah, it appears that it was an Elf who found the dwarves' lode in the first place! That explains this writing, and perhaps also why the dwarves of old never mined the lode, for they would not have wished to ask an Elf for help. It appears that these records refer to a map that must have once been included with them. I hope Regin has that map, for the records are not detailed enough to stand on their own.''So, we're missing a map, eh? I wonder....

'Well, thank you again for your work.'A monstrous breed of spider has built a nest south of Esteldín, threatening the security of the outpost's inhabitants.The Kingsfell Spider-queen is somewhere in the spider-lair south of Esteldín.

You should slay the queen of the spiders who threaten the people of Esteldín, thereby lessening the danger posed by the foul creatures.Defeat the Spider-queenThe Kingsfell Spider-queen is somewhere in the spider-lair south of Esteldín.

You should slay the queen of the spiders who threaten the people of Esteldín, thereby lessening the danger posed by the foul creatures.Dagoras is at Esteldín.

You should speak with Dagoras and inform him that you have slain the spider-queen.A monstrous breed of spider has built a nest south of Esteldín, threatening the security of the outpost's inhabitants.

You should defeat the queen of the spiders in the Snares.'You have done well, ${PLAYER}. The death of the spider-queen should throw the entire lair into disarray.'Gurzlum lies too near to Gatson's farm, threatening the last inhabited farmlands of the North Downs.The Orc-camp Gurzlum is south-west of Gatson's fields.

Farmer Gatson is sure the Orcs will attack his farm again if they are not deterred first.Defeat Dead-aim Ongbúrz in Gurzlum (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Grim Ongbúrz in Gurzlum (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gatson is back at his farm, north-east of the Orc-camp.

You should tell Gatson of your success in thinning the Orc threat at Gurzlum.Gurzlum lies too near to Gatson's farm, threatening the last inhabited farmlands of the North Downs.

You should defeat Orcs at Gurzlum drive back the enemy threat in the Kingsfell.'What? You defeated the Orcs at their source? Wonderful! You've saved me! Hooray!

'I know it's not much, but I want you to take this reward. You're a true hero, and there's no denying it!'You have found a large sack of stolen yeast in the mining camp outside of Thurûm, bearing signs of having once belonged to Othrikar.Sacks of yeast can be found around the camps at Thurûm, north of Othrikar.

You should collect sacks of yeast stolen from Othrikar.Collect stolen yeast (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hornbori is at Othrikar.

You should bring the stolen yeast to Hornbori.Bring the yeast to HornboriYou have found a large sack of stolen yeast in the mining camp outside of Thurûm, bearing signs of having once belonged to Othrikar. The good dwarves of Othrikar will surely be missing the key ingredient for their beer-brewing.'Our stolen yeast delivery! Morale was growing poor indeed with the prospect of no beer to sweeten our days. Thank you very much for bringing this home to us, ${PLAYER}.'The Dourhands, an ancient, but declined dwarf-family, were recently outed from Thorin's Halls and the mines of Othrikar for their betrayal of Durin's Folk, the Longbeards. The rogue clan continues to be a nuisance in the North Downs.There are Dourhand scouts scattered throughout the area north of Othrikar.

In an effort to reduce the threat of the Dourhands becoming more than a nuisance, Hannar has asked you to put an end to the unhindered spying of the Dourhand scouts.Defeat Dourhand scouts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hannar is in Othrikar.

You should return to Hannar.Return to Hannar'Ever since we threw those cursed Dourhands out of Othrikar, they've been skulking around the area. We think they're planning to raid us and retake the mines!

'They have several large camps around the northlands, and they have scouts spying on us here as well as carrying messages between the camps.

'Soon we will be ready to move against the Dourhands directly, but for now I need you to put an end to this spying. Defeat as many of the Dourhands' scouts as you can find around Othrikar and the northlands of Nan Amlug.''I do not like the report you bring back of all the scouts who have amassed around Othrikar. The Dourhands are gathering in the hills... that much is clear. We must drive them out!'Despite a small supply of arms and armour, Stanric of the Eglain is attempting to organize at least a meagre defence against the wild and dangerous beasts and other enemies which stalk the ruins the Eglain call their homes.Gore-crows can be found all along the Great East Road, which lies south of Ost Guruth.

Stanric has asked you to search along the Great East Road for gore-crows and collect their eyes as evidence that they can no longer spy upon the people of Ost Guruth.'Death, pestilence, and terror stalk us, ${PLAYER}. Two have been dealt with, thanks to your effort, the third still soars above us with ever watchful eyes.

'It is the eyes that I want. I speak of the gore-crows, the black birds with sharp beaks and piercing eyes. They are spies along the road east and west from this place, and they are ever vigilant.

'Take their eyes and they cannot see and what they cannot see they cannot spread to their masters, the Orc and half-breed. Seek the gore-crows along the Great East Road and bring me their eyes to prove that the creatures will no longer speak of us to their fell masters.''Dead, yes? No more will these eyes spy down on us from above. Can we not find a bit of peace in that? I think that we shall.

'You have done us a great service once again, ${PLAYER}. There is little else needed for us to become well-prepared for what lay ahead.'While you were gathering ingredients for Cedrach, she was able to locate the old chest she took when she moved into her own home. It hasn't been opened in ages, but perhaps the Amon family grimoire is hidden beneath the moth-eaten blankets and old clothing contained within.Cedrach has suggested that you search the chest for the Amon family grimoire.

The chest is in Cedrach's house, in the Old Forest.Search the chestWhile searching the chest for the Amon family grimoire you found a blue cloak! Keep searching the chest for the book.

The chest is in Cedrach's house, in the Old Forest.Continue searching the chestYou should let Cedrach know that you found the grimoire at the bottom of the old trunk.

Cedrach is in the Old Forest.Tell Cedrach you found the bookCedrach Amon has given you her blessing to take the grimoire to her sister, Henbran, with her apologies for accidentally taking it with her when she left home.

Henbran's cottage is in the Old Forest.'Do you know I searched my entire house while you were gone? Then my daughter reminded me that the chest was in the cellar. Hooh, my memory is not what it once was. Go on ahead and search the chest for the book. If it's in there, you can bring it to my sister.''You found the grimoire? Oh dear. I cannot believe I have had it all this time! So many years of my sister thinking I stole it from her when I left. I would never have done such a thing! I can understand now why she never visited, even when my daughter was born! How very sad - we missed out on so many years of each others' lives.

'Please, bring the book to my sister. With my blessing and my apology, if you will carry it. It is not enough, but I hope one day she will forgive me.''So you return! And no more the worse for wear, it seems. Has my sister gone soft?'

You hand her the grimoire and explain what happened. Henbran is quiet for a long while, looking down at the large book in her hands.

'Decades without her. Over an accident. What have I done?'With the map you recovered from Foreman Nyrad and the runes from the old records, Regin may have determined the general location of the father-lode.The father-lode is somewhere to the north-west of Othrikar.

  Find the father in the raw rock
  The lode lies amongst high hills
  Fell falcons soar through skies and
  Savage lynx guard the gorges
  Shining the silver, glittering the gold
  A cliff's crags keep the secret

The old records combined with the mining map suggested to Regin the location of the father-lode, but the directions were far from precise. Regin has asked you to find a suitable rock formation and take an ore sample.Locate the father-lode and take an ore sampleRegin is at Othrikar.

You found a rock formation matching the description Regin compiled from the map and old records. You should bring the ore sample you took from the site back to Regin.'Ha! Now we can finally find the father-lode! 

'Hm. Wait a moment. These directions are not exactly what I was expecting:

  Find the father in the raw rock
  The lode lies amongst high hills
  Fell falcons soar through skies and
  Savage lynx guard the gorges
  Shining the silver, glittering the gold
  A cliff's crags keep the secret
  
'They had a poetic turn back in the old days. But I wish they were more precise....

'Well, I think we at least have some idea how to proceed. The map and those directions together speak clearly to me of a cliff north-west of Othrikar. All you have to do is find a suitable rock formation that is rich with ore. If you do, take a sample, and we'll see if it's the true father-lode.''I wish I could give you better directions. But please head out there and look for that lost lode!''Let's see that rock! I think....

'Aye! This is it! High-quality, gold-bearing ore! We still have to deal with those cursed Dourhands, and there are all kinds of other dangers to take care of as well, but if we can manage, we'll all be rich!

'I cannot thank you enough, friend! You certainly deserve a reward for your work here, and little though it may be, know that you will always be a welcome guest in the mines of Othrikar!'Mellanion wishes to forge a bond of friendship with the young boy his mother lured away.Mellanion has asked you to bring his hobby horse to Cai.

Cai is in the inn, in Herne.'My heart is heavy, thinking of how scared poor Cai must have been. I know my hobby horse is much the worse for wear, but I would still like to gift it to him. I rinsed off all traces of Orc and have brushed out its mane once more.

'I know it is a poorly gift, given the danger he was in, but perhaps it will bring him comfort, and let him know that he has a friend among the Elves. Will you bring it to him, if you happen to pass through Herne again?''I can never, never thank you enough for saving my son! Or for what you did for that poor Elf. I am glad in my heart that we both got our sons back, but gladder still that she will not tempt away any more children.''You have done a great deed for Wenna! Your tale of the Elf-woman is haunting, and I am glad that her sorrow has also come to an end.''Oh hello. You have something for me from the Wailing Woman's son?'

You hand him the hobby horse and his eyes widen.

'Bless me, but this is the best gift ever! You are sure I can have this? Oh how wonderful! Look mom! I am a rider of Rohan!'The runes on the old mining records reference a map that appears to be missing.Foreman Nyrad can be found in the Dourhand camp north of Othrikar. Regin is at Othrikar.

Regin believes that Nyrad, the previous foreman, stole the map, but left the records, since he could not read the runes. Regin has suggested that you may find the map either in Foreman Nyrad's tent or on Nyrad himself.Collect the Dourhand mining map (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have found the map stolen by Foreman Nyrad.Return the map to Regin'I believe the map must have been taken by the Dourhands. They must have left the old records you found because they couldn't read them. But I'd guess that the map is in the hands of the Dourhand foreman who used to have my job.

'Unless I miss my mark, I'd say that Foreman Nyrad is with many of the other Dourhands at their camp to the north.

'This is a big request, but if you can get that map back from Foreman Nyrad, I think we'll be able to find the father-lode at last! I suspect you'll find the map in a chest in Foreman Nyrad's tent at the camp, but he might also be carrying it around on his person.''You have found it! This is wonderful news. Let me see, let me see.'Orc scouts have been sneaking around Gatson's farm, stealing food, and Gatson is sure it is only a matter of time before they raid his farm in force.Orcs can be found in Gatson's south fields.

Farmer Gatson is resigned to the knowledge that the Rangers cannot help him, but he is still concerned for the safety of his workhands. He hopes that by dealing with the Orcs scouts that have been stealing from his fields, you might be able to deter a violent raid on his farm.Defeat Orcs in Gatson's south fields (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gatson is at his farm, north of the fields.

You found and defeated many Orcs in Gatson's south fields. You should return to Gatson and tell him of your success.'There's been Orcs around here lately. So far they've just skulked around, stealing things, but I'm sure as soon as they get their courage up, they're going to raid us. I guess I'm just too stubborn to run, and my hands, like Gilmar here, are too loyal to leave me, so I need you to get rid of these Orcs for me.

'You'll like as not find them sneaking around in my fields to the south. Get those foul creatures off my land!''I heard Master Gatson send you after them Orcs in the south fields. You be careful...they fight like a wolf cornered in a barn.''Them Orcs smell weakness...If you can chase more of them off, they might think twice before bothering us again!''You've done well, my friend. We've got more problems, but at least you taught those Orcs a lesson.'Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of coarse skin.You are on a task to deliver coarse skins from the beasts in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Coarse skins from the beasts found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaster at Othrikar.Wanted: Coarse skins from the beasts found throughout the northern North Downs.'Completed: a simple task to obtain coarse skins from beasts. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of tough sword sheaths.You are on a task to deliver tough sword sheaths from the Dourhands in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Tough sword sheaths from the Dourhands found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar.Wanted: Tough sword sheaths from the Dourhands found throughout the northern North Downs.'This fulfills the request for the sword sheaths. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.After encountering the elusive spider, Agerbráth, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Gaerion can be found at Sírlond in Cardolan.

You have acquired a trophy from an elusive foe in Cardolan. You should deliver it to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Deliver Agerbráth's mandible to Gaerion in CardolanAfter encountering and defeating the elusive spider, Agerbráth, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.

As you inspect Agerbráth's mandible, you take care to avoid touching what appears to be lingering venom upon it. It seems the dangers of the Old Forest are not to be underestimated, and you think to yourself that any place with that many spiders might not be worth exploring at all.

You should bring this trophy to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.'What have you brought here? What is that?'

Gaerion inspects Agerbráth's mandible closely, taking the same care as you did to avoid touching the mostly-dried venom. As he turns the mandible about in front of him, his face contorts in thinly-veiled disgust.

'I cannot say that I have any interest in this trophy, but there are some among the Dúnedain with more peculiar interests—such as the collection of spiders and insects. If you would be willing to part with it... and I don't know why you wouldn't be, I'm certain at least one among my kin would be interested in possessing it.

'Is that all then?'Orgrin, the quartermaster at Othrikar, requires your aid in maintaining the camp's stores.Lynx roam the hills surrounding Othrikar.

Hunt the lynx that roam the hills around Othrikar in western Nan Amlug and bring them to Quartermaster Orgrin at the entrance to the dwarf-mine.'There's a cold winter coming. I feel it in my bones. We dwarves are a hardy folk, but if we're going to survive the winter, we will need more supplies.

'Hunt the troublesome lynx that seem to cover the lands hereabouts and bring me their fur. I'll craft their hides into warm gloves for our miners.''Thank you for your help, friend. I will fashion these furs into gloves to protect the miners from the bitter cold of the deep mines. Old Nine-fingered Doli will be happy about that, I can tell you.'Ranger Faradan is worried about a pair of powerful wights that now walk within Dol Ernil.Two powerful wight-lords, Bregach and Daethos, now lurk somewhere within Dol Ernil, in Tyrn Gorthad.Defeat the wight-lord Bregach in Dol ErnilDefeat the wight-lord Daethos in Dol ErnilRanger Faradan is south-west of Calarphel in Tyrn Gorthad.Talk to Ranger Faradan south-west of Calarphel in Tyrn Gorthad'Gorwen and I were charged with keeping the watch on Dol Ernil, further to the north, and she is surely still there. I came south to bring word that some new evil seems to be at work within the fortress. Someone... or something... is opening the tombs. I have seen two such terrible wight-lords walking beneath the moon, figures that should have remained buried away forever.

'I know the names of those I saw. Bregach is one, "quickbone," and the other is named Daethos, "shadow fear." They are somewhere within Dol Ernil, I know not where. If you encounter them and are able to return them to the slumber of death, you will have my deepest thanks, and the appreciation of any who live near. Few are they, for this is a land of barrows, but think of this: the Dead have no need for sleep, and do not tire. If Bregach or Daethos desired to reach a settlement of peaceful folk, what town could possibly be beyond their reach?

'Good fortune to you if you do find yourself in Dol Ernil, ${PLAYER}. Beware of the wight-lords I have named.''Good fortune to you if you do find yourself in Dol Ernil, ${PLAYER}. Beware of the wight-lords I have named: Bregach and Daethos.''Bregach and Daethos are no more? I can hardly believe it, ! Such was their evil that I would have thought them impossible to best, and yet you have done so!

'They were fearsome foes from out of legend, and now they are ended. Perhaps you are yourself to be a figure of legend, my friend. I will await the word of your further deeds, and perhaps in a day to come I will sit by a warm fire and say "I knew her/him in earlier days, when the legend was young!"

'Ah, perhaps, my friend. I cannot believe you faced the wight-lords and survived. What a wonder!'The ruins of Nan Dhelu have often given the Eglain ill feelings, but the arrival or emergence of the Dead from within the ruins has them even more fearful. A brave warrior, Cwendreda, was sent to watch and learn about the ruins and her tasks now fall to you as terror begins to grow from within those once great halls.Nan Dhelu lies to the north of the ruins of Dol Vaeg.

Cwendreda has asked you to keep a watchful eye out for any treasures that you may find within the ruins of Nan Dehlu. She believes that the wights within the ruins may carry them.'The Eglain have always kept their distance from Nan Dhelu. The ruins have an ominous feel that cannot be avoided. 

'Since coming to this location, I have fought against some of the creatures there and found that they carry treasures that will bring us much in the way of trade. 

'While you are fighting against these foes, I would ask that you collect these items so that we may earn something from the aid you are providing.''Yes, these are a very valuable find. If we can determine that none of these are ruined or corrupted, then I think they will earn us a great deal of supplies from the traders in Bree-land.

'Thank you for this, ${PLAYER}.'With the migration of Orcs and goblins to the western Lone-lands, the Eglain attempted to rekindle old trade relations with an Elf from Rivendell to the east. However, they encountered great, lumbering beasts upon the Road and dared not continue forwards.The creatures' encampment is south of the Great East Road near the Tornstones, south and a little east of Ost Guruth.

Frideric the Elder provided you with a crate of salted meats and furs to deliver as a peace offering to the creatures near the Tornstones.'We have salted-meats and furs collected from the Wargs. We would like to offer these to the creatures, in hopes that they are not hostile.

'I ask you to be cautious, because this could prove very dangerous. We do not know what the creatures are, but we know they are not trolls. 

'Search to the south of the Great East Road, south and a little eastward of here, near the Tornstones. You should find a small encampment of the creatures there. Seek out their leader and offer him the supplies on our behalf.''We know that the creatures have made quick enemies of the Orcs and Wargs. So we hope that this offering will show them that we are not their enemy, and in truth, would prefer to be their allies. You will find them near the Tornstones, south and east of Ost Guruth.''What is this, little stranger? It smells of meats and furs. You say it is a gift to the Jorthkyn?

'Kekkonen accepts the gift, little one, yet we cannot give anything in return. We cannot give help to others until we have no more troubles. We are chased from our home and must find a place that is safe.'Radagast is concerned with the cause of the corruption in the swamps. He feels that the wights are a symptom of a greater evil.The gaunt-men dwell in the ruins to the far east of Haragmar.

Radagast told you of the gaunt-men, necromancers in the service of Sauron, who are responsible for the emergence of these wights. He asked you to defeat the war-singers and bring him their sigils. He can then determine which of the Gaunt-lords they serve.'The sheer number of wights in Haragmar indicates the presence of gaunt-men, vile necromancers who serve the Dark Lord. In ages past, they dwelled in the Witch-realm of Angmar, but the White Council believed them all destroyed. Clearly, they were wrong.

'Only the strongest of the gaunt-men, the Gaunt-lords, have the power to summon the spirits of the Shadow Realm. The war-singers carry the sigil of their Gaunt-lord. Search the ruins in the far east of Haragmar, defeat these creatures, and bring me their sigils.

'We must learn which Gaunt-lord these creatures serve.''These sigils bear the mark of Ivar the Blood-hand. He is a powerful lord of the gaunt-men, and a dangerous and vile creature. It is he who is behind the corruption of the Red Swamp.

'Why would a creature such as Ivar  travel so far from Angmar? Power, perhaps. But what drew him here?'Your discovery of wights in Haragmar, the southern reaches of the Red Swamp,  alarms Radagast the Brown. He suspects the involvement of powers from Angmar.Wights can be found deeper in Haragmar, the southern reaches of the Red Swamp, east of Ost Guruth.

Radagast sent you back into the swamp to defeat the wights that are fouling the land with their evil.Defeat wights in Haragmar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Radagast the Brown waits at the tower at the northern end of the main thoroughfare in Ost Guruth.

You should return to Radagast the Brown with news of your battle with the wights of Haragmar.Talk to Radagast the Brown in Ost Guruth'I can smell death in the moss, as if it was plucked from an ancient grave. This stench of decay and evil is not a thing from nature. I believe that the wights are to blame.

'Wights are the bones of the dead, stirred by fell spirits out of Angmar and Rhudaur. The wars here long ago made Haragmar as ripe for wights as the Barrow-downs in the west. Return to Haragmar, the swamp in the east, and destroy these foul creatures.

'If there are wights, I fear there are other fell creatures haunting the swamp, as well. For now, though, you need only concern yourself with the wights.''The wights' destruction can only prove to help us in the time to come. Now listen, for there is much more to do.''Unlike the living, wights grow stronger and more powerful with time and age. Destroy them, while I determine their origins.'The Witch-king has worked great evil in Tyrn Gorthad, and the Rangers need your help to oppose his servants and his dark purpose.Gorwen is at her camp south of Dol Ernil, in Tyrn Gorthad.

Meneldir asked you to see what help Gorwen needs to combat the evils of Tyrn Gorthad.Talk to Gorwen at her camp south of Dol Ernil, in Tyrn GorthadGorwen gave you a number of tasks that will help her combat the evils of Tyrn Gorthad.Defeat wights in Tyrn Gorthad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat crebain, crawlers, or gaunt-men in Tyrn Gorthad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect ancient swords among the ruins in Tyrn Gorthad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gorwen awaits your return at her camp south of Dol Ernil, in Tyrn Gorthad.Talk to Gorwen at her camp south of Dol Ernil, in Tyrn GorthadMeneldir is at Gorwen's camp south of Dol Ernil, interested to hear of your accomplishments throughout Tyrn Gorthad.Talk to Meneldir at Gorwen's camp south of Dol ErnilHalbarad is at Sarn Ford.

Meneldir asked you to bring the ancient swords you recovered to Halbarad so they can be destroyed and their evil diminished.Halbarad suggested that the two of you return to Gorwen's camp south of Dol Ernil.Return to Gorwen's camp south of Dol Ernil, in Tyrn GorthadHalbarad and some of his company have come to Gorwen's camp in Tyrn Gorthad, but both Meneldir and Gorwen have already vanished. Have they gone to face the evil alone?Talk to Halbarad at Gorwen's camp south of Dol Ernil'I will tell Gorwen what we have learned of our foes, but she has kept watch on Dol Ernil for longer than we have. I suggest that you ask her what needs doing in the keep and in blighted Tyrn Gorthad. I see the hand of the Witch-king all about us, and it must be opposed, if we are able to oppose it.''I will tell Gorwen what we have learned of our foes, but she has kept watch on Dol Ernil for longer than we have. I suggest that you ask her what needs doing in the keep and in blighted Tyrn Gorthad. I see the hand of the Witch-king all about us, and it must be opposed, if we are able to oppose it.''I am younger than Meneldir by some years. Though we both grew up in the Angle of Mitheithel, I did not meet him before he left us. His name was always spoken in whispers, a warning to the children that not everyone should take the oath, that not everyone should swear to the life of a Dúnadan.'

Meneldir sighs heavily, and Gorwen raises a gloved hand.

'I do not mean to injure your feelings, Meneldir! What I mean is this: I expected a monster. When you came to my camp, I thought you must be fleeing from hardship, escaping from some trouble of your own making! Such were the tales we were told in childhood. But I find instead you are a man, not a monster, and you fight on behalf of those who cannot, willing even to return to your former kin to bring them warning, though they sting you with barbs and biting tongues. I see honour in that.

'But there are monsters out there, ${PLAYER}, real monsters. I do not need to hear the full account of Meneldir's fallen friend to know that, for I knew it already, if not its true extent or depth. Wights stalk Tyrn Gorthad, and there are other dangerous threats as well: spying crebain, pestilential crawlers, and the gaunt men who prowl among the barrows. Each troubles the Dúnedain and their allies in this land. There are swords both within the keep and without that exude frightening auras of evil, and they must be collected and destroyed, despite the presence of their terrible protectors. Can all this be done?''Wights stalk Tyrn Gorthad, and there are other dangerous threats as well: spying crebain, pestilential crawlers, and the gaunt men who prowl among the barrows. Each troubles the Dúnedain and their allies in this land. There are swords both within the keep and without that exude frightening auras of evil, and they must be collected and destroyed, despite the presence of their terrible protectors. Can all this be done?''It is no small feat to brave the dangers of Tyrn Gorthad and the keep of Dol Ernil, ${PLAYER}! I am grateful for your efforts on behalf of the Dúnedain.

'Meneldir has shown me the account in Narndir's journal of the Grey Fear, and if what the scholar wrote is true, the creature is a spirit drawn from the pits of Angmar, bound to the body of Prince Amondir. In life he was the Lord of Tyrn Gorthad, and Dol Ernil was his home. Surely this site will still have some meaning for him. Does the Grey Fear remain somewhere within the keep, or has the presence of the Witch-king and the riders in black already disturbed his slumber?''I think the Grey Fear remains somewhere within Dol Ernil... but where?''Those swords should be brought to Halbarad at Sarn Ford, ${PLAYER}. He will know how best to deal with them, and when you return perhaps we will have learned where the Grey Fear may be found. I agree with Gorwen: the creature must be somewhere within Dol Ernil.'

You tell Meneldir to be careful, for Narndir's journal paints a frightening and deadly account of the creature that brought death to Cardolan and the line of its princes.

'I will, my friend, fear not. I will see you when you return.''Now go, ${PLAYER}. I will see you when you return.''${PLAYER}! I have not forgotten you, nor the warning you brought to us of Saruman. Let me now put your mind at ease: it seems he is the only of the Wizards to betray us, and thank the Powers it be so!

'While you were gone, a rider came up out of the south: Gandalf the Grey, astride a mighty horse, a steed of Rohan! We spoke only briefly, but it was enough to assure me that Gandalf still aligns himself on the side of the Free Peoples. And yet, the Wizard's tidings were not all so rosy, for he said that evil forces are now in motion that could threaten the peace and safety of all of Middle-earth; he could spare no more time before hurrying into the Shire in pursuit of the Nazgûl that preceded him.

'But what brings you back to this place?'

You briefly tell Halbarad what you have learned of the Grey Fear and that Gorwen and Meneldir believe it remains hidden somewhere within the keep of Dol Ernil.

'These swords you bring to me will be destroyed, for the runes that cover their faces are evil, but after I see that done you must return to Dol Ernil. Aye, and not alone: I and several of my company will go with you. I fear the two who remained may be in great danger!''These swords you bring to me will be destroyed, for the runes that cover their faces are evil, but after I see that done you must return to Dol Ernil. Aye, and not alone: I and several of my company will go with you. I fear the two who remained may be in great danger!''This is what I feared, ${PLAYER}. Did some evil come to them, or did they foolishly depart in search of it?

'Gorwen, at least, would have the sense to remain until your return, but of Meneldir I cannot say. He has fled his duty before. Might he not do the same today?'North-men from Angmar have wandered into Taur Gonwaith.The mysterious Angmarim are outside of the troll-cave in the southern part of Taur Gonwaith, south of Lin Giliath.

Baranwen has asked you to collect weapon-chests from the Angmarim and to return them to her so she can destroy the foul weapons.Collect weapon-chests (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Hillmen defenders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Baranwen is at Lin Giliath, north-west of Taur Gonwaith.

Baranwen is awaiting your return with the Orc-weapons, which she will destroy.'North-men have been seen carrying chests laden with weapons into a cave, in the south. While the others dwell upon the malefactions of the trolls, I am concerned by the very presence of Men from Angmar not to mention their arms.

'Every sword-hilt we remove from the grasp of an Angmarim is one fewer blade that may be brought to bear against the Free Peoples. Seek out weapon-chests in the troll-cave and bring them to me. I shall destroy the foul blades.

'If even one of the blades we seize would have otherwise slain an innocent, I shall count this day's labour worth the toil.''Taur Gonwaith was once a shady vale to the south-east of Lin Giliath, but the trolls have despoiled it at the hands of their Angmarim masters. If you can return here with several chests of the Angmarim weapons, I will destroy them.''These weapons are poorly made, and none of them exhibit the care needed to make a truly fine sword. I am not surprised by the lack of craftsmanship, for the Angmarim care little about such things, and these blades are as effective as they need to be to slay the innocent people who were their intended prey.

'You have done well to bring me these weapons. I will destroy them, and by so doing I hope we have together saved some lives. You have my thanks for this errand.'Elsa the Bold, at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur, desires heavy carapaces from the twisted creatures of nature found in Agamaur.You accepted a task from Elsa the Bold to acquire heavy carapaces from the creatures of nature within the swamp south of Agamaur. You can find Elsa the Bold within the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Heavy carapaces from the twisted creatures of nature in Agamaur. Bring them to Elsa the Bold at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Heavy carapaces from the twisted creatures of nature in Agamaur.'These will be used to reinforce our armour so that we may stand a little longer against the evil spreading here.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Elsa the Bold, at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur, desires scarred heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature found in the swamp south of Agamaur.You accepted a task from Elsa the Bold to acquire scarred heartwood from the creatures of nature within the swamp south of Agamaur. You can find Elsa the Bold within the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Scarred heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature in the swamp south of Agamaur. Bring them to Elsa the Bold at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Scarred heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature in the swamp south of Agamaur.'We will give all of these to Radagast to determine if there is hope for any of these poor creatures.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Elsa the Bold, at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur, desires tough branches from the twisted creatures of nature found in Haragmar and Agamaur.You accepted a task, for Elsa the Bold, to acquire tough branches from the creatures of nature within the swamp of Agamaur. You can find Elsa the Bold within the Eglain-camp at Agamaur.Wanted: Tough branches from the twisted creatures of nature in Haragmar and Agamaur. Bring them to Elsa the Bold at the Eglain-camp at Agamaur.Wanted: Tough branches from the twisted creatures of nature in Haragmar and Agamaur.'This is a painful effort, but one that must be done.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Despite your efforts, the threat of the hendrevail remains as a blight in the hills around Othrikar.Orthonn is collecting herbs in the Kingsfell, likely at his camp in the ruins of Ost Lagoros north-west of Gatson's farm, some distance south and west of Othrikar.

Hannar explained that his people did not know enough about hendrevail to know how to deal with them, since hunting them did not appear to work. He asked you to go speak with a hunter named Orthonn who might have some insights about the birds.Talk to Orthonn at Ost LagorosHannar is at Othrikar.

You managed to find the Ranger who Hannar sent you to seek, and he suggested you look for the hendroval roosting ground. You should return to Hannar with this information.Talk to Hannar at Othrikar'Despite your work, it seems like there are more hendrevail than ever in the hills, and some say they have actually been seen in the Dourhand camps.

'We know so little about these creatures beyond the fact that they are dirty, evil, and vicious birds. We'll need to know more if we are to do anything about them. There's a hunter who often roams the Kingsfell by the name of Orthonn who might know a bit more about wild creatures like the hendrevail. Calls himself a Ranger, strange fellow. He can often be found at a camp in the old ruins of Ost Lagoros not far north-west of Gatson's farm, a distance south and west from Othrikar.

'I would appreciate it if you could go speak with him for me.''If Orthonn out in the Kingsfell can tell us something of the hendrevail, we might have better success dealing with them.''Ah, yes, hendrevail. Strange birds, violently hostile, even to creatures much larger than themselves, and very territorial. Yet they can be trained. Hendrevail have been used occasionally by Orcs and evil Men in the past, but for the most part their malice is wholly natural.

'So the Longbeards of Othrikar fear their Dourhand rivals are using these birds as spies? It may be so in truth. Unlike some other hawks and falcons, the hendrevail of the northern hills favour a mass roosting ground. They care little for their chicks, and like as not this is where the Dourhands gathered the fledgelings to be trained.

'I would counsel you to seek out this roosting spot, no doubt in a secluded corner of the hills.''The dwarves should be wary of the hendrevail, for they are oft used as spies by the Enemy.''A breeding-ground, is it? I wonder....

'Thank you for your assistance, ${PLAYER}. When we have discovered the location of this roosting ground, I may have need of your aid again.'The scourge of the goblins appears to be everywhere these days, even on the borders of the Shire itself. Rumour has it that in Evendim a new breed of the foul creatures can be found in the odd-looking Boggarts -- cowardly snivelling things that hunt in packs to bring down their prey.Defeat goblins in EvendimIt seems that the goblins in southern Evendim have not yet come in great numbers, perhaps they are only here to harry the folk of the Shire and distract the Rangers from more important goals elsewhere? Nonetheless, the goblins use deep tunnels to move about beneath the sight of the keenest-eyed scout, so they can come forth suddenly in great numbers to wreak havoc upon the unprepared.Defeat goblins in EvendimBingo is in the Forsaken Inn, trying to help Theodore Gorse solve the riddle you recovered from a strongbox in the Lone-lands.Theodore Gorse and Haley Meadowsweet are in the Forsaken Inn, puzzling over the cryptic message you recovered from the strongbox in the Lone-lands.Talk to Theodore Gorse in the Forsaken InnTalk to Haley Meadowsweet in the Forsaken InnBingo is in the Forsaken Inn, trying to figure out the meaning behind the cryptic message you recovered.Talk to Bingo in the Forsaken InnBingo gave you the cryptic message, and challenged you to unravel its mystery and find the treasure somewhere in the Lone-lands.Theodore Gorse is in the Forsaken Inn, and will want to see the treasure you found, even if it is only a parchment with the words 'The End Now' on it.Bingo is in the Forsaken Inn, and you should tell him about the conclusion of your treasure hunt.Talk to Bingo in the Forsaken Inn'I have been trying to help Theodore Gorse unravel this latest cryptic message, ${PLAYER}, because it may be that solving it will lead to the real treasure! Here, I will read you the message.'

Bingo clears his throat and begins dictating the strange message.

'What's good for the goose is good for the gander, and what's bad for the cad is a tad over yonder. Goblins and spiders and plotting and fighters. Lest our relative novice seem perplexed and nonplussed, too many spell danger, so look to the spell for the answer.'

Bingo shakes his head in confusion.

'It makes no sense to me! Perhaps Theodore or Haley have come up with something.''What's good for the goose is good for the gander, and what's bad for the cad is a tad over yonder. Goblins and spiders and plotting and fighters. Lest our relative novice seem perplexed and nonplussed, too many spell danger, so look to the spell for the answer.'

Bingo shakes his head in confusion.

'It makes no sense to me! Perhaps Theodore or Haley have come up with something.''Geese? Goblins? Spiders? This most recent riddle, if it is actually a riddle and not just nonsense, is quite a perplexing one! I am quite perplexed, just as the writer of this riddle must have expected!

'We can't tell what's 'over yonder' if we don't know where to start, can we? Oh, I will never find this treasure! Should I have stayed retired, ${PLAYER}?''I listened to your friend Bingo's recitation of the message once again, but it did not clear anything up for me. There is something unusual about that message, but I cannot say what it might be!''Geese? Goblins? Spiders? This most recent riddle, if it is actually a riddle and not just nonsense, is quite a perplexing one! I am quite perplexed, just as the writer of this riddle must have expected!

'We can't tell what's 'over yonder' if we don't know where to start, can we? Oh, I will never find this treasure! Should I have stayed retired, ${PLAYER}?''I listened to your friend Bingo's recitation of the message once again, but it did not clear anything up for me. There is something unusual about that message, but I cannot say what it might be!''I do not know, ${PLAYER}. I will give you the message so you can try and figure it out. Unfortunately, I think the meaning is quite above this hobbit's head!

'All we know for certain is that if this does point to a second strongbox, it must surely be in the Lone-lands. Other than that, I have no idea! Find it if you can, my adventurous friend!''All we know for certain is that if this does point to a second strongbox, it must surely be in the Lone-lands. Other than that, I have no idea! Find it if you can, my adventurous friend!''The treasure must be in the Lone-lands, but I had no luck figuring out the meaning of that cryptic message.''Can you unravel the mystery of that message and find this treasure?''You solved the cryptic message? Good job, ${PLAYER}, you have done it! Now let me see that treasure!'

You present the parchment to Theodore, but to your surprise he does not seem disappointed.

'The End Now? Well, I guess that is it. We have the victory! This is the treasure, and our mysterious riddle-master has acknowledged that we had success. I am happy, and I hope you are too!''The End Now? Well, I guess that is it. We have the victory! This is the treasure, and our mysterious riddle-master has acknowledged that we had success. I am happy, and I hope you are too!''That's it? The end now? Theodore may be happy with this result, but I am not! Now that we have had a taste of treasure hunting, I will not be happy until we follow this chain of riddles to the end! Surely this cannot be it!

'Oh, I am not happy about this, ${PLAYER}. There has to be more! The end now? I do not believe it!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'The End Now?' (Recommended Level: 32)</rgb>The Dwarves of Othrikar are beset by supply troubles, stemming from the incursion of Orcs and Wargs from the north and the betrayal of the Dourhands.Gatson's Farm is south of Othrikar in the plains below the hills.

Almost all of the farmers of the North Downs have abandoned their lands in the face of the Orc incursion. Hornbori learned that one, a man named Gatson, is refusing to leave, however. Hornbori has asked you to speak to Farmer Gatson about purchasing grain.Find Gatson at his farm'I have just received word that there is, after all, one last farmer in the North Downs. A fellow named Gatson refuses to leave his land to the Orcs.

'It may be that he still has grain to sell us. If you would, pay Gatson a visit to learn if this is true. Tell him we will pay him good silver for his produce.

'Gatson's farm is not too far south of the hills.''Aye, it's true, I had a rich harvest this year, and I'd gladly sell it off, if I could. I'd not done business with the dwarves in the past, because those Dourhands were nasty fellows, but now that Durin's Folk are in charge, I'd be more than willing to do business with them. 

'Or rather, I should say, I would happily sell the grain if I still had it! Those cursed Orcs have raided my farm and taken most of the harvest. Alas, it looks like I will not be able to help you. Unless....'Medlichen is concerned that the Stone-trolls will return when they learn that Elves have come back to Lin Giliath and believes that they should bolster their defences.The first pedestal is east of Lin Giliath, and two others are to the south of it in a great curve through the marsh.

Medlichen has given you several runes to place on the stone pedestals throughout Meluinen, which he hopes will bring the glade's guardians to its defence.Medlichen is in Lin Giliath.

Medlichen is waiting to hear of your successful placement of the canel upon the stone pedestals throughout Meluinen.'When the Stone-trolls learn that Elves have returned to Lin Giliath, they are sure to attack again. There are too few of us, and we cannot hold out long. There is one defence which we can call upon, but there is no certainty that those I speak of will still answer the summons.

'I give you these runes -- "canel" they are called -- and ask you to lay them upon the pedestal stones found throughout the marshes. Long ago, many of these stones were set east of Lin Giliath in a great curve bending to the south, but only three have survived the ravages of time and the elements. We must hope the guardians remember the meaning of the "canel".

'Return to me when you have placed all the runes, and we will learn if the errand was for naught.''There are three stone pedestals located throughout the marshes south of Lin Giliath. Seek each of them out and place a "canel" upon it, and we shall learn if the bog-guardians will aid us.''You have placed the "canel", ${PLAYER}? That is well, though it seems the bog-guardians have chosen not to come to our aid.

'I suppose I was too eager for the old friendship between Elves and bog-guardians to be rekindled that I was hopeful to the point of blindness. Only disappointment can come of... wait, what is that?

'Look! A bog-guardian has come! And more! I shamed myself with despair, but now I see that it is never wrong to hope. You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}!'Frideric asked that you speak with Hana the Young in Ost Guruth. Since visiting the swamp north-east of Ost Guruth, the girl has had terrible dreams.Hana can be found within Ost Guruth, near the tower at the end of the main avenue.

Frideric the Elder informed you that Hana the Young took an object from the swamp and that her dreams since that time fill her with terror. He asked that you speak with the girl to discover what her nightmares entail.'${PLAYER}, if you are to assist us, then you must know what has transpired thus far.

'Hana, a girl within our encampment, visited the swamp to the north and east. She discovered an ancient relic lying out upon one of the ruins. We collect that which the ruins offer, as you know, and she thought nothing of consequences, as we have never seen reprisal.

'On this day there was something different in the swamp, something stirred and frightened the girl so that she now has fits in her sleep. I fear for her, friend. Will you find Hana and speak with her to discover what visions plague her dreams?''Speak with Hana, ${PLAYER}, for her mind is troubled.''Did Frideric send you? He worries too much now that my sister is away. Ever since I took the statue of the lady they have treated me differently. Even the visitor in brown robes treats me differently. They fuss about saying that they must protect me. I do not know how they can protect me from my dreams. 

'It is true I am frightened come the morning. I hear the swamp calling me at night. The Woman makes the nerbyg in the swamp sing to me. She is trying to bring me to her, and that is why my sister left... to find her and stop her.

'She is a bad person. I know she means me harm because her songs sing of blood and death. It is the nerbyg, they sing for her. I don't tell anyone, but I can hear the nerbyg singing my name even now.'You have helped Aglardir resupply Lin Giliath, but his worries are not yet eased. The original mission of Gildor Inglorion's company remains unfulfilled.Esteldín is north-east of Lin Giliath, hidden within the eastern mountains between the Kingsfell and Nan Amlug.

Aglardir has sent you to Dagoras, the watch-captain of Esteldín, to tell him of the passing of the Nazgûl.'Far to the north and east, the Dúnedain of the North have established a hidden encampment which they call Esteldín. These Men do their best to fight threats in the North Downs, but their numbers are now too few and the dangers too great.

'If you are willing to go, I would have you complete the original mission of our company. Go to Esteldín and speak with the watch-captain, Dagoras. Tell him that the Nine are abroad and have crossed the River. The Enemy is on the move.

'Esteldín is hidden in the mountains on the border of the Kingsfell and Nan Amlug. This is a message that should not be delayed.''Esteldín is hidden in the mountains north and east of Lin Giliath, near the border of the Kingsfell and Nan Amlug. Dagoras must be told of the passing of the Nazgûl.''The Nine are abroad, after all this time? This is fell news, for it can only mean one thing: the Ring of the Enemy has been found.

'We can only hope that the Enemy himself has not already reclaimed it, or the world as we know it is at an end. I think that if this were so, our chieftain, Aragorn, or Elrond Halfelven would have sent word.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I will deliver this news to Halbarad, our steward in the absence of Aragorn.'The sickle-flies in the marshes of Meluinen have been disturbed by the recent aggression of the Stone-trolls.Sickle-flies can be found throughout the marshes of Meluinen, south of Lin Giliath.

You should abate the threat of the sickle-flies of Meluinen, which have been stirred to anger by the Stone-trolls.Defeat sickle-flies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated several angered sickle-flies in Meluinen, which should give the Elves of Lin Giliath some small respite from the hostile creatures in the glade.CompletedThe sickle-flies in the marshes of Meluinen have been disturbed by the recent aggression of the Stone-trolls, creating a threat for the Elves of Lin Giliath.

You should defeat sickle-flies in Meluinen.You have defeated several angered sickle-flies in Meluinen, which should give the Elves of Lin Giliath some small respite from the hostile creatures in the glade.Londrandir, an Elf from the refuge of Lin Giliath, has come to Amon Raith, seeking the aid of the Rangers in a small matter.Londrandir stands at Gatson's Farm in the Kingsfell.

You should speak with Londrandir.Lachenn and Aglardir are at Lin Giliath in Meluinen, south of Esteldín.

Since it is clear that the Rangers have their own burdens to bear, Londrandir has asked you to journey east to speak with Lachenn, the Master of Lin Giliath. If Lachenn is unavailable, he told you to speak instead with Aglardir.Talk to Lachenn or Aglardir at Meluinen'We are so lucky this Elf passed this way when he did, and lucky you came as well. I wish there was something we could do to help him in return, but we cannot abandon our farm.

'Perhaps you could speak with him, if you wish to help.''Londrandir sent you to speak with Lachenn? You have come too late then.

'Lachenn is dead. He was killed when Stone-trolls attacked Lin Giliath, sometime before my company arrived.''I saw Orcs when I passed this way, and could not turn a blind eye to them. I am glad I came here in time to save these Men.

'I came seeking aid on behalf of Lachenn, the Master of Lin Giliath, a small refuge of my kindred in the lush land of Meluinen. It is a small thing, no more than a slight stirring of an ill wind. The Rangers are not in a position to send someone to Lachenn, but perhaps you would go?

'Journey south; Lin Giliath is very near the border of Meluinen. If Lachenn is not able to speak with you, speak with Aglardir instead.'The townsfolk in Oatbarton await the delivery of broken short bows to Ponto Oats at the centre of the town.You have been tasked with collecting and delivering broken short bows from the brigands north of Oatbarton. You are to deliver the items to Ponto Oats at the centre of the town of Oatbarton.Wanted: Broken short bows from the brigands north of Oatbarton. You can make the delivery to Ponto Oats at Oatbarton centre.Wanted: Broken short bows from the brigands north of Oatbarton.'I will make certain to show these to the other townsfolk. It is good to know that there are people out there willing to help. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The townsfolk in Oatbarton await the delivery of very sharp claws to Ponto Oats at the centre of the town.You have been tasked with collecting and delivering very sharp claws from the lizard-like creatures north of Oatbarton. You are to deliver the items to Ponto Oats at the centre of the town of Oatbarton.Wanted: Very sharp claws from the lizard-like creatures north of Oatbarton. You can make the delivery to Ponto Oats at Oatbarton centre.Wanted: Very sharp claws from the lizard-like creatures north of Oatbarton.'These are very strange to see. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Since visiting Agamaur, the swamp north-east of Ost Guruth, Hana the Young has had terrible dreams. She spoke mysteriously about a Woman in the swamp and the song of the nerbyg.Nerbyg can be found in Haragmar, the southern reaches of the Red Swamp, in a place called the Circle of Blood.

Hana claims that the Woman her people found in the swamp wishes to harm her and uses the nerbyg to call to her and draw her forth from Ost Guruth. Hana has asked you to slay the insects and bring her their legs so that she will know they can no longer call to her.'We came upon the maiden in the waters north beyond the wall. She was draped in vines and tattered robes and smiled beautifully before she cringed and shook the water apart and drove us from the swamp.

'She cornered me, though for only a moment, before my sister found me, but I saw the hatred in her eyes. She means me harm and sends the nerbyg to call me to her.

'Will you help me? Can you help me? The nerbyg are in the east, in the Bloodmire, rubbing their legs and singing the song she sang to me, calling for me to come. Please, help me. Bring me their legs so that I know they will no longer sing my name.'Hana looks over the legs and casts them into a nearby fire.

'They are silent now. I cannot hear them chirping. For your help, ${PLAYER}, I wish there was more I could do in return. Please, take this with my gratitude.'Meluinen is threatened by the presence of a large encampment of Ongbúrz Orcs in the valley to the south-west.Orc-scouts have crept into Meluinen from the valley to the south-west of Lin Giliath.

Brethilwen has asked you to slay any Orcs you find in Meluinen, thus reducing the Ongbúrz threat.Defeat Orcs in Meluinen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brethilwen is in Lin Giliath.

Brethilwen will be heartened to hear of your victory over the Orcs that had entered Meluinen.'Thaliollang and Lagorlam are so intent upon vengeance against the Stone-trolls that they are blind to all other threats, and I fear they will not retain enough strength of will to fight the other dangers that lurk nearby.

'Orcs of the Ongbúrz clan have established a foothold in Nan Wathren, the valley to the south-west, and their scouts intrude further into Meluinen with each passing day.

'Slay any Orcs you find that have crossed into Meluinen, ${PLAYER}, for they pose a great threat to Lin Giliath. You will surely find them south and south-west of here, along the line of the mountains.''Orcs of the Ongbúrz tribe have begun creeping into Meluinen from Nan Wathren, south-west of here. Defeat the Orcs that have already crossed into Meluinen to the south and south-west, and we may prevent, or at the least delay, any further incursions.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}! I hope your efforts will serve as a warning to the Ongbúrz: they are not welcome in Meluinen, nor ever will be. This company of Elves may only be temporary stewards of Lin Giliath, but we shall oppose them as surely as Lachenn would, were he still alive.'The Rangers have requested help in securing the lands around Esteldín and warding off any threats.The Orcs threatening the central North Downs can be found around the farm fields and rolling hills of Kingsfell.

You should foray against the Orcs, and drive them away.Defeat Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have assisted the Rangers of Esteldín in their daily vigilance, maintaining that the lands of the North Downs are free from enemy control.CompletedOrcs have begun to increase in number and move northwards, becoming a terrible threat to the peoples of Eriador.

Any who could make forays against their camps around the lakes in the southern plains will discourage these foes from spreading further. The kindred of Rangers are stretched thin across the North Downs and beyond on missions of great import, and are unable to deal with this threat.

Whoever completes this request shall be rewarded.You have assisted the Rangers of Esteldín in their daily vigilance, maintaining that the lands of the North Downs are free from enemy control.Many of the provisions stored at Lin Giliath were ruined by the Stone-trolls during their attack.Aurochs roam the plains of the Kingsfell, north of Lin Giliath.

Aglardir has asked you to hunt aurochs in Kingsfell for meat to replenish the stores of Lin Giliath.Aglardir is in Lin Giliath, south of the Kingsfell.

Aglardir is awaiting your return with the aurochs meat to replenish the lost provisions kept at Lin Giliath.'We must restore the stores of Lin Giliath as soon as we can. Grief can cause both body and spirit to suffer, but against one of these hurts we can prepare.

'The Stone-trolls ruined the food that was stored here, but there are other sources of sustenance. Hunt the aurochs on the plains of the Kingsfell, to the north of Lin Giliath. Their meat will go a long way to restoring Lin Giliath's stores.

'An aurochs is a dangerous foe, and you must hunt several to obtain enough steaks.''You have done very well, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for your aid. This aurochs meat will keep us for as long as it takes to restore Lin Giliath.

'I would have a further word with you. The grief of my friends is very deep, for Lachenn was beloved of many. Help them if you can and do not hold against them any harsh feelings if their despair seems to you strident. Thaliollang and Brethilwen were particularly close to Lachenn and held him in high regard. Give them comfort in their grief, for they have lost much to the evil that lurks in Taur Gonwaith.

'Again, I thank you for your aid, ${PLAYER}, and wish you well.'Master Good-heart, concerned citizen, wants you to do something about the raiders on the Greenway.Brigand raiders trouble the Greenway where it passes into Bree-land.Defeat brigand raiders on the Greenway (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Master Good-heart is south-east of the South-guard Ruins.Talk to Master Good-heart south-east of the South-guard Ruins'Ho there, friend! Are you heading north? Oh, you may want to reconsider. There are brigands all along the Greenway, and they even squat in the ruins between here and Bree-land! The South-guard Ruins, aye, they are full of brigands, and they only get even more dangerous the further north you go!

'Who am I? My name is Good-heart. Master Good-heart, to my friends, and I hope you will prove to be one of those. You see, I am only a merchant, and if I am to make my way north to Bree, I would have to pass by those ruffians! Well, in my younger days perhaps I could have done something about those villains, but sadly my sword-swinging days are likely behind me.

'But you are an adventurer! No, do not try to deny it: you have the look of one. If you can defeat some of these Greenway raiders, I could see fit to part with some of my coin. I only need you to face the brigands that are here on the road, so no need for you to pass into the South-guard Ruins; it's the villains on the road that are the problem. What do you say?''If you can defeat some of these Greenway raiders, I could see fit to part with some of my coin. I only need you to face the brigands that are here on the road, so no need for you to pass into the South-guard Ruins; it's the villains on the road that are the problem. What do you say?''Good job, my very capable friend! Those brigand raiders must surely be regretting their choice of occupation now, I would say!

'Here is your reward, as promised. I will continue north just as soon as I am able, but for now I have a few more preparations to make. Perhaps we will meet again?'Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of broken short bows to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect broken short bows from the Orcs, Angmarim, and Earth-kin in the area surrounding Esteldín to the south and east. Once collected, you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: broken short bows from the Orcs, Angmarim, and evil Earth-kin south and east of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: broken short bows from the Orcs, Angmarim, and evil Earth-kin south and east of Esteldín.'We can fashion these bows back into something that will assist us in this fight. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of leathery fur to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect leathery fur from the beasts in the area surrounding Esteldín to the north and east. Once collected, you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Leathery fur from the beasts north and east of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Leathery fur from the beasts north and east of Esteldín.'I will make certain that we put this to good use, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Ferrif, a Ranger, has asked for a collection of scarred ears to be delivered to him at the Ranger's home at Esteldín.You have accepted a task to collect scarred ears from the beasts in the area surrounding Esteldín to the north and east. Once collected, you are to deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Scarred ears from the beasts north and east of Esteldín. Deliver the items to Ferrif within Esteldín.Wanted: Scarred ears from the beasts north and east of Esteldín.'It may seem a strange request, but these do hold some value, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Lagorlam, a member of the company of Elves who came north with Gildor Inglorion, entered Taur Gonwaith, seeking revenge against the trolls who attacked Lin Giliath.Lagorlam was injured while visiting his vengeance on the trolls. With his anger sated he has offered to leave Taur Gonwaith with you.Escort Lagorlam to safetyThaliollang is at Lin Giliath, north-west of Taur Gonwaith.

Thaliollang will be pleased to hear that you rescued Lagorlam from the forest of Taur Gonwaith.'I am pleased to see a face that does not belong to a troll. I came to Taur Gonwaith to seek vengeance on the trolls that attacked the glade of Lin Giliath and murdered my fair friend Lachenn. I can tell you that many of the Stone-trolls that attacked Lin Giliath will never again leave this place.

'They proved a challenge, and that one was about to squash me when you arrived. You have my thanks.

'Let us leave this place, friend, so I might return to Lin Giliath and tell my friend, Thaliollang, the course of my vengeance.''You survived a journey to Taur Gonwaith, ${PLAYER}, and lent your strength to Lagorlam!

'Aye, he has already spoken to me of what transpired. I am grateful that you were able to find him, though he insists he could have returned to Lin Giliath without any aid.'You have found a book that describes the Founding Stones of Arthedain's Five Towns. Now mostly in ruins, these towns are 'Henneth Rhún, south of Fornost; Duintham, astride an ancient stream; Carnoglin, in the northern hills; Ost Ardúlin, in the lake-lands; and Dolindîr, now called Esteldín.Ask Istuienn about the Book of the Five Towns.Find the Founding Stone of Dolindîr, now called Esteldín.Find the Founding Stone of Dolindîr, now called EsteldínSpeak further with Istuienn about the Book of the Five Towns.Find the remaining Founding Stones.Find the Stone of Henneth RhúnFind the Stone of DuinthamFind the Stone of CarnoglinFind the Stone of Ost ArdúlinReturn to Istuienn to tell the tale of your exploration.Stamped across the front of this book is the title, 'Of Arthedain and its Settlements.' The book's cover is worn, as if many hands have held it. It opens easily to a dog-eared page, which reads:

'...In those days, north-eastern Arthedain was dominated by five great settlements, known commonly as The Five Towns. These were Henneth Rhún, south of Fornost; Duintham, astride an ancient stream; Carnoglin, in the northern hills; Ost Ardúlin, in the lake-lands; and Dolindîr, now called Esteldín.

'Amongst the Rangers of the North Downs, we encourage our people to visit all five of the Towns and discover the locations of their Founding Stones. It is important to remember who we are....''So, you have found that old journal?' Istuienn arches an eyebrow. 'It is not customary for those not of the Rangers to do so, but if you wish to know more about my kindred, you may want to undertake the journey yourself.

'This first of the settlements mentioned in the book was called Dolindîr; today we call it Esteldín.

'Return to me after you have found the Founding Stone of Dolindîr, and we will speak further.''Esteldín was a city once known as Dolindîr. Find the Founding Stone of Dolindîr, and you may learn more about what my people once were.'This old monument is well kept, and is free of dirt and vines. Upon its face is an inscription:

'Here once stood the town and Founding Stone of Dolindîr, in the kingdom of Arthedain, which has passed.  

'In this place was established the refuge of Esteldín, by Arassuil, son of Arahad, that these lands might never again stand unwatched.''Ah, I see you have chosen to begin the journey. Very well then.

'The book speaks of five towns which were Henneth Rhún, south of Fornost; Duintham, astride an ancient stream; Carnoglin, in the northern hills; Ost Ardúlin, in the lake-lands; and Dolindîr, now called Esteldín.

'You have seen the Founding Stone of Dolindîr, now seek you the other four.''Remember, the book says the five towns were Henneth Rhún, south of Fornost; Duintham, astride an ancient stream; Carnoglin, in the northern hills; Ost Ardúlin, in the lake-lands; and Dolindîr, now called Esteldín.'The monument's face is inscribed with faded words:

Henneth Rhún

Established in the first year of the reign of Araphor, son of Arveleg.

'Folk shall gather where commerce thrives.'The monument's face is inscribed with faded words:

Duintham

Founded in the year 1249 of the Third Age.

'At this place did King Celebrindor establish the Town of Duintham, that his people might cultivate the land and feed the people of his realm.'The monument's face is inscribed with faded words:

Carnoglin

Founded in the name of Malvegil, King of Arthedain. With the skill of Thorhaddur the Dwarf and his kindred was this town built.

'United in purpose, may dwarves and Men be ever bonded in friendship.'The face of this monument is marred by what appear to be large tooth-marks, as if several man-like creatures decided to gnaw upon the stone.

Though most of the writing has been destroyed, you can discern the words 'Ost Ardúlin' at the top of the monument, and at the bottom the phrase, 'that we might retreat from our cares, and take solace in such beauty.''Really? You made the circuit of all five towns? I am impressed; I had not really expected you to make the attempt.

'We often assign novice Rangers this task, as the beasts that lair in such places make such exploration dangerous indeed. Perhaps the Rangers will be able to make further use of your skills in the future.'Aglardir has undertaken the difficult task of restoring Lin Giliath to peace in the wake of the Stone-troll attack.Shattered barrels can be found strewn throughout the fens leading to Taur Gonwaith in the south of Lin Giliath.

Aglardir has asked you to recover whatever supplies you can from the barrels the Stone-trolls scattered throughout the marshes.Search shattered barrels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Aglardir is in Lin Giliath, the glade on the border of Kingsfell and Meluinen.

Aglardir is waiting for you to return with the possessions you salvaged from the shattered crates strewn about Meluinen.'Gildor Inglorion brought our company north to spread word of a great evil stirring in the south, but we found here only death. The keeper of this glade was friend to many of us, a fair Elf named Lachenn; he was slain by Stone-trolls before we arrived, and the refuge beset by ruin.

'I do not know what called the trolls out of Taur Gonwaith, but we must rebuild this place as best we can, for it is what Lachenn would have us do were he here to ask it.

'Revenge? It must wait. The trolls scattered the possessions that were stored here throughout Meluinen. Look for shattered barrels between here and Taur Gonwaith to the south. I need you to bring their contents to me.''The Stone-trolls left much destruction in their wake. The food they likely consumed or wasted. The relics that were stored here may have been broken, but some may have survived.

'Look for the shattered barrels littered about the marshes to the south, in Meluinen, and bring back what you can. Without Lachenn, this glade will never be what it was, but we must do what we can.''I did not think we could hope for as much as you have returned with, ${PLAYER}; I thank you for your aid.
The trolls ruined or consumed much of the food that was stored here, and many relics were lost. Lachenn would grieve for these losses even as we grieve for him.'The Rangers have requested help in securing the lands around Esteldín and warding off any threats.The foul spiders reside in the south-eastern area of the Kingsfell in the shadow of the mountain.

You should hunt down and slay the spiders that plague the area.Defeat spiders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have assisted the Rangers of Esteldín in their daily vigilance, maintaining that the lands of the North Downs are free from enemy control.CompletedMany vile spiders dwell in the south-eastern Kingsfell, near the mountain, and have long been a threat and a plague to Esteldín. The kindred of Rangers are stretched thin across the North Downs and beyond on missions of great import, and are unable to deal with this threat.

Any who slay spiders in south-eastern Kingsfell shall be rewarded.You have assisted the Rangers of Esteldín in their daily vigilance, maintaining that the lands of the North Downs are free from enemy control.While searching the Amon family chest you came across a blue cloak! Perhaps someone at Cedrach's house knows who it belongs to.Derwen has asked you to bring the bread she baked to her aunt, Henbran.

The bread is on the table in Cedrach Amon's house in the Old Forest.Pick up the breadYou should bring the fresh bread to Henbran and let her know who the blue-cloaked stranger was.

Henbran is in the Old Forest.'So, you found my blue cloak, eh? Yes, I was the one bringing food to auntie Henbran. My mother suspected, but didn't ask me about it. I had to stop this week because my daughter and I both fell ill and I have been here watching over her. I am so grateful you were able to help fish for auntie in my stead. I will take up the task again as soon as I am feeling better. Perhaps this time I will not have to go cloaked and hiding who I am. I desperately wish to meet my mother's sister.

'I was able to bake some bread earlier today. Will you bring the loaf on the table to auntie Henbran? May it help to even further mend the gap between our family.''Bread? From Blue Cloak?'

You explain Derwen's role as the blue-cloaked stranger and her wish for mending things between the sisters.

'This whole time, it was Derwen? And my sister's daughter has a daughter of her own? I have missed so much! Has so much time really passed while I sat in my anger?

'This seals it! I will go and see them, and bring herbs of my own to help with this sickness. In truth, I know a path my knees will be able to tolerate. All this time I could have gone to Cendrach, but I closed my heart, just as I closed my door when she would come by. When was it that she stopped coming, I wonder. So long ago, it seems. I wonder if she will forgive me - but from what you have said, she already has? Joy creeps into my heart. I must make haste! As much haste as this old body can make, that is.'

She laughs softly.Many ruins lie forgotten in lands east of the Baranduin. Explore them and determine if they have fallen into the hands of the Enemy.NOT USEDOst Lagoros was a watch-tower which once held vigil over the eastern passage to the great city of Fornost Erain.Ost Galumar once stood as a forward outpost of the kingdom of Arthedain, a steadfast guardian against the shadow from the North.Dol Nendír was a small Arthedain fortress on the north-eastern edges of Meluinen, before it was razed by the armies of Angmar, long ago.Merenost was once a small, walled town of the North-realm of Arnor long before the marshes of Meluinen swallowed the land.Ost Crithlanc had been one of the forwardmost outposts of the kingdom of Arthedain. Now it lies in ruins, home to evil Men out of Angmar.Master Good-heart, concerned citizen, has learned that the leader of the brigands welcomes challenges to his leadership.Maelgun Redclaw has sworn to answer with death any who blow a challenge on the horn he left to the west of the gate to the South-guard Ruins.Master Good-heart is to the south-east of the entrance to the South-guard Ruins.Talk to Master Good-heart south-east of the South-guard RuinsThe bounty hunter Melona has come up the Greenway, and stands now south-east of the South-guard Ruins.Talk to Melona beside the Greenway'My preparations are just about complete, and I am nearly ready to move on! But word has reached my ears of something extraordinary. I have learned of a way by which I might be able to give back to this land something of great value. I am nothing if not charitable, and my generosity knows no bounds! Did I not reward you with more coins than you deserved for the work you did for me earlier? I mean, after all: you would have faced down those brigands even without a reward, would you not? But I sweetened the deal with some coin, and in that way everyone wins!

'It seems the leader of the raiders on the Greenway is a man named Maelgun Redclaw, and he is a very bad sort. He is so certain of his leadership that he has left a horn on some masonry to the west of the entrance to the South-guard Ruins, and he claims that he will allow anyone to challenge him for leadership of his band of brigands! Now, a few folk have tried this in the past, and he has slain them all... but he has never answered a challenge from one such as you, has he?

'Let this be my farewell gift to this land! Together we can put an end to the brigand menace on the Greenway!''The leader of the raiders on the Greenway is a man named Maelgun Redclaw, and he is a very bad sort. He is so certain of his leadership that he has left a horn on some masonry to the west of the entrance to the South-guard Ruins, and he claims that he will allow anyone to challenge him for leadership of his band of brigands! Now, a few folk have tried this in the past, and he has slain them all... but he has never answered a challenge from one such as you, has he?

'Let this be my farewell gift to this land! Together we can put an end to the brigand menace on the Greenway!''No... what are you doing here?''No... what are you doing here?'

Melona the bounty hunter stares at you with dismay. You tell her that you and Master Good-heart have put an end to Maelgun Redclaw, leader of the brigands that blockade the Greenway. She shakes her head in disbelief.

'Am I to be so plagued with ill fortune? How many bounties am I cursed to lose because of your interference? "Master Good-heart?" Do you mean Geralt Goodblossom, noted ruffian?'

You tell Melona that Geralt is a very common name, possessed by many folk in the south, and it is surely a coincidence. She shakes her head.

'I have been hunting Geralt and Eilian Goodblossom for several days now, but thanks to the injury I sustained in Sedgemead I have been always a step behind. While I hunted them, they hunted targets of their own: the Redclaw brothers, three powerful brigands who together directed much of the villainy present in these lands. Now that the three Redclaws are no more, the Goodblossoms are free to enact the next step of their plan: seizing control! They do not wish to free these lands of brigand control... husband and wife wish to consolidate it beneath their own leadership!'

You tell Melona that you have met both Eilian and Geralt, and would be willing to help her track them down.

'No! I mean... no thank you, ${PLAYER}. It's just that I think you might be bad luck. I have only had ill fortune ever since you stumbled upon me in Sedgemead, and I fear it may get worse. You understand. In fact, I'll reward you for staying away! How does that sound?'Gurmagath has in his possession an ornament once belonging to Lachenn, Master of Lin Giliath.Gurmagath can be found on a hillock in the north-eastern area of Taur Gonwaith, south-east of Lin Giliath.

Thaliollang has asked you, in memory of Lachenn, to hunt down the chieftain of the Stone-trolls, Gurmagath, and retrieve Lachenn's ornament so that it can be restored to a place of honour.Collect the broken ornamentThaliollang is at Lin Giliath, north-west of Taur Gonwaith.

Thaliollang is waiting for you to return with the ornament you retrieved from Gurmagath.'I have heard whispers amongst the trees of a Stone-troll chieftain of great size, called by the others Gurmagath. This Gurmagath had in his great fist a sparkling ornament that once belonged to poor Lachenn!

'This wretched creature cannot be permitted to keep Lachenn's ornament, ${PLAYER}. Seek out this Gurmagath and wrest Lachenn's ornament from him, so it may be kept in Lin Giliath in a place of honour.''Gurmagath can be found on a rise in the north-eastern part of Taur Gonwaith. He cannot be allowed to keep Lachenn's ornament any longer, ${PLAYER}. You must defeat him and bring it to Lin Giliath, where it belongs.''Aye, this is Lachenn's ornament. It is truly a shame that it has been broken by Gurmagath's brutish fingers.

'There is something odd about these markings on the back of the ornament. These scratches almost look to form letters. A... N... G....

'The rest is broken off, but I believe I know what it would say.'Someone evil has stirred the Stone-trolls, rousing them to wreak havoc upon the fair glades of Meluinen.Angmar's emissary must reside deep within the cave in Taur Gonwaith, south-east of Lin Giliath.

You should defeat the emissary of Angmar that has gained the allegiance of the Stone-trolls.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: A fellowship is recommended for this quest.</rgb>Search the Etten Caves for the leader of the trollsAngmar's emissary must reside deep within the cave in Taur Gonwaith, south-east of Lin Giliath.

You should defeat the emissary of Angmar that has gained the allegiance of the Stone-trolls.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: A fellowship is recommended for this quest.</rgb>Thaliollang is at Lin Giliath, north-west of Taur Gonwaith.

Thaliollang will be pleased to know that he was correct in his belief: there was an emissary from Angmar among the trolls. To the benefit of all, you have defeated him.You have discovered the Etten Caves at Taur Gonwaith. Whatever has stirred the trolls surely hides within these vast caverns.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: A fellowship is recommended for this quest.</rgb>'You have earned more thanks than I have to give, ${PLAYER}, for defeating the troll-keeper of Angmar.

'Lachenn would have been pleased to know you. I name you Elf-friend and will not forget what you have done here, ${PLAYER}. I expect the Stone-trolls are not likely to forget either. Lin Giliath may have peace for a time.'A number of trolls roam the woods of Taur Gonwaith. These are dangerous and evil creatures, servants of the enemy. Who has roused them from the deep caves in which they slumber?Trolls roam Taur Gonwaith in Meluinen, in the southern North Downs.

You should defeat the deadly trolls of Taur Gonwaith.Defeat trolls at Taur Gonwaith (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated a number of menacing, deadly trolls in the woods of Taur Gonwaith.CompletedA number of trolls roam the woods of Taur Gonwaith. These are dangerous and evil creatures, servants of the enemy. Who has roused them from the deep caves in which they slumber?You have defeated a number of menacing, deadly trolls in the woods of Taur Gonwaith.Lagorlam, a member of the company of Elves who came north with Gildor Inglorion, entered Taur Gonwaith, seeking vengeance against the trolls who attacked Lin Giliath. He has not returned, which concerns Thaliollang.Lagorlam has gone to the forest of Taur Gonwaith, south-east of Lin Giliath.

Thaliollang has sent you to find Lagorlam and make certain he is well.Search for Lagorlam in Taur Gonwaith'When we arrived here, there was much debate concerning what should be done about the Stone-trolls, and many thought, as I did, that they should be driven from their fastness in Taur Gonwaith.

'There were some of us, among them Aglardir and Gildor, who counselled restraint. I regret the stern words I had for them, but at the time their plan to rebuild this place seemed to do Lachenn's memory a disservice.

'My friend Lagorlam believed as I and journeyed forth to slay the Stone-trolls, but he has not returned. It would put my mind at ease if you could find him. Look for him in the forest of Taur Gonwaith to the south-east.''The death of Lachenn has troubled us all, and Lagorlam may not use as much caution as he ordinarily would. Seek him in Taur Gonwaith to the south-east and lend him whatever aid he may need.'Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of jagged beaks.You are on a task to deliver jagged beaks from the hendrevail in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the hendrevail found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaster at Othrikar.Wanted: Jagged beaks from the hendrevail found throughout the northern North Downs.'I will mark the task as complete in my lists. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Birgir Listmaker, one of the miners at Othrikar in the northern reaches of the North Downs, awaits the delivery of ruffled feathers.You are on a task to deliver ruffled feathers from the hendrevail in the North Downs to Birgir Listmaker at Othrikar. Othrikar is north of Esteldín in the North Downs.Wanted: Ruffled feathers from the hendrevail found throughout the northern North Downs. Deliver the items to Birgir Listmaster at Othrikar.Wanted: Ruffled feathers from the hendrevail found throughout the northern North Downs.'I have marked this task as complete. We are grateful.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Orcs of Merenost in Meluinen use spider venom to coat their blades and arrows.The Ongbúrz Orcs can be found in the ruins at Merenost, in southern Lin Giliath.

Gandelin asked you to slay the Ongbúrz-defiler and return with the Ongbúrz venom-pouches.Collect Ongbúrz venom-pouches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gandelin is back at Lin Giliath.

Gandelin asked you to slay the Ongbúrz-defiler and return with the Ongbúrz venom-pouches.'I have need of your assistance, ${PLAYER}. An Orc defiler, skilled in the use of poisons, joined the ranks of the  Ongbúrz in southern Lin Giliath. He has begun to poison the waters of this glade, and all will be ruined.

'Slay the Orcs in Merenost and collect their venom pouches. I will make sure the pouches are never again used for evil purposes.

'While you are in the camp, put an end to the source of the vile poison once and for all by killing the Ongbúrz-defiler. Be careful, ${PLAYER} -- these Orcs are brutal and without mercy, and you may have need of allies.''${PLAYER}, we must have some samples of the Orcs' poison so that we may create a cure for it.''The venom pouches. I take it that the defiler was slain as well? Many lives will be saved through your efforts.

'I find it strange that the Orcs would hunt their spider allies. They make themselves stronger at others' expense... and in the end, weakening the Enemy as a whole. Such is the way of evil things, for the spiders would not hesitate to drink of the blood of their Orkish fellows.'Defeat trolls in the North Downs.Defeat trolls in the North DownsDefeat many trolls in the North Downs.Defeat trolls in the North DownsAfter encountering the elusive bear, Crúgaul, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Gaerion can be found at Sírlond in Cardolan.

You have acquired a trophy from an elusive foe in Cardolan. You should deliver it to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.Deliver Crúgaul's claws to Gaerion in CardolanAfter encountering and defeating the elusive bear, Crúgaul, you have claimed a trophy that may be of value to the Dúnedain of Cardolan.

As you inspect Crúgaul's claws, you notice they are far larger than the average bear's claws and a sort of shadow seems to cling to them. For a moment, you wonder if Crúgaul might have been one of the mighty lath-bears of old, but those were just stories... or so you thought. It was surely a trick of the dark that shaped this bear into a creature far more foul!

You should bring this trophy to Gaerion, a trophy-hunter and lore-keeper of the Dúnedain of Cardolan.'Greetings, traveller. W-What are those?'

Gaerion inspects Crúgaul's claws closely, almost giving them a sort of reverence. He takes caution not to cut himself on the sharp claws before setting them down.

'I have heard tales of these creatures... the lath-bears. When I was a boy, I was told they prowled the lands of Rohan after nightfall, clothed in shadow and feasting upon the young. These claws are quite a find, though part of me wishes you had never crossed paths with that creature.

'Although I am no weaponsmith, I'd wager these claws could be fashioned into a deadly tool. I shall have to see what the others make of them. You have my thanks!'The ruins of Nan Dhelu have often given the Eglain ill feelings, but the arrival or emergence of the Dead from within the ruins has them even more fearful. A brave warrior, Cwendreda, was sent to watch and learn about the ruins, and her tasks now fall to you as terror begins to grow from within these once great halls.North and east of Dol Vaeg are the ruins at Nan Dhelu.

Cwendreda informed you to be wary as there are a good many of dangerous Dead within the ruins of Nan Dhelu and has asked you to banish them.Defeat the Dead at Nan Dhelu (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cwendreda is at Dol Vaeg.

The perils of Nan Dhelu are behind you now, but the memories will remain. The Dead overrun the ruins, and though there is a rich history in that place, an evil exists there also.Talk to Cwendreda at Dol Vaeg'We know that there are a great number of Dead within this place. We have learned of these foul beings from our stone-speaker and our friend Radagast. 

'We know that they are evil and that they hate all that live. I must ask that you make your way to the ruins and destroy the Dead there, for they are a blight on these lands and should be purged.

'You will find a great number of these creatures, some bloated, some barely sheathed in flesh, and others bereft of flesh within the borders of Nan Dhelu. Banish them from the ruins, then return to me.''This cannot have been an easy task. The Dead are creatures that inspire fear within the most courageous of hearts.

'We Eglain are indebted to you, ${PLAYER}. I offer you this meagre gift for your efforts.''Be cautious in the ruins for the Dead are not alone. There is some ancient evil within as well. Be vigilant and destroy the wights there.'The Rangers have requested help in securing the lands around Esteldín and warding off any threats.Wargs prowl the plains and foothills of Nan Amlug and Rhunenlad, east of Esteldín.

You should hunt down and slay the cruel hounds of the Enemy.Defeat Wargs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have assisted the Rangers of Esteldín in their daily vigilance, maintaining that the lands of the North Downs are free from enemy control.CompletedWargs, the hounds of the Enemy, prowl the foothills and eastern plains of Nan Amlug and Rhunenlad, threatening inhabitants and travellers alike.

Any who could slay a goodly number of them may frighten the Wargs enough to cease their hunts for the flesh of Men. The kindred of Rangers are stretched thin across the North Downs and beyond on missions of great import, and are unable to deal with this threat.

Whoever completes this request shall be rewarded.You have assisted the Rangers of Esteldín in their daily vigilance, maintaining that the lands of the North Downs are free from enemy control.With the migration of Orcs and goblins to the western Lone-lands, the Eglain attempted to rekindle old trade relations with an Elf from Rivendell to the east. However, they encountered great, lumbering beasts upon the Road and dared not continue forwards.Frideric the Elder awaits word from you at Ost Guruth, north of the Tornstones.

After assisting the Earth-kin, Kekkonen sent you to speak with Frideric and let him know that they wish to become allies.'Kekkonen will accept the offer from this tribe you help, these Eglain. You have shown the Jorthkyn that there is strength in the little people who dwell here. 

'Return to your folk and tell them that they may come to us, and we will speak with them and be friends.

'Go to their rocks to the north and give them my words.''You have proven a choice unmatched, ${PLAYER}. We have found new allies in this world and will work with them to form a bulwark against the evil creeping here.

'Now we can focus our attention towards the Red Swamp in the north.''Kekkonen asks that you make his words known to the tribe you help. Go to them, north of the Tornstones, and tell them.'The two factions of the Earth-kin -- the free Suuri-lehmä and the Rauta-lehmä, who serve Angmar -- are preparing for war. Iivari, the War-master of the Suuri-lehmä, and his brother Oskari have found themselves on opposite sides of the conflict.Oskari is somewhere among the camps of the Rauta-lehmä at Fashat Laug in the eastern North Downs.

Iivari has given you a message to take to his brother Oskari. Iivari hopes that his words may be able to sway his brother and others of the Earth-kin to return to the Suuri-lehmä.Iivari is in the Suuri-lehmä encampment, west of Fashat Laug.

Oskari refused his brother's plea and gave you a rune to take back to Iivari.'Greetings, traveller. I must ask your aid, for my tribe cannot help me. I am preparing the Suuri-lehmä for war, but I am not prepared myself.

'My brother stands across from me, armed with the weapons of Angmar, his mind filled with their lies. I have not been able to speak with him since this rift grew between my people and the Rauta-lehmä, who serve Angmar. I do not know where my brother's heart truly lies, whether he is torn within, as am I.

'I cannot lead my people to war while this doubt gnaws at me! Take this message to Oskari - he is somewhere within the Rauta-lehmä encampment at the place called Fashat Laug, well to the east of here. Perhaps he and others may yet be convinced to return to us before the end.''Please carry the message to my brother.''You bring a message from my brother? I see.

'Iivari is a fool who refuses to see the world as it truly is. He thinks that words alone can chase away the darkness - he does not understand the meaning of power.

'Take this to him. It is all the answer from me that he will receive. Now go, or the Rauta-lehmä shall crush you.''Go. Take the rune. I have nothing more to say.''Ah. Now I know the truth. The rune you carry is a gift I gave my brother many years ago. The symbol upon it in our language means "kinship". It has been etched over with a symbol we use to mean "without value."

'The lies of the Angmarim run too deep, and my brother is beyond my reach now. I find my heart no less troubled by what I must do, but now I know that there is no choice....'You have been told that you may learn more secrets of how to communicate with the creatures of the wild from Radagast the Brown.Radagast the Brown can be found within his tower at the Eglain camp in Agamaur.

You have been instructed to find Radagast the Brown and learn from him the art of speaking to the creatures of nature.'While you have come far as a Lore-master, ${PLAYER}, and have surpassed the abilities of many, there are those of such ability that none shall ever surpass. They are the Wizards, who were aged and wise when they first came to this Middle-earth and whose knowledge has only grown since then. Their arts lie beyond most understanding, although some insight can be gained from them.

'For instance, it is said that one of the Wizards, Radagast the Brown, resides among the Eglain in their camp near the cursed swamp of Agamaur. He is a friend of the wild and all the creatures of nature speak to him. It might be possible to gain insight into this ability from him, if you but ask him.

'If you would gain such a boon, travel to the Eglain encampment, which lies in the Lone-lands, and speak to Radagast there.''While you have come far as a Lore-master, ${PLAYER}, and have surpassed the abilities of many, there are those of such ability that no ${RACE} shall ever surpass. They are the Wizards, who were aged and wise when they first came to this Middle-earth and whose knowledge has only grown since then. Their arts lie beyond most understanding, although some insight can be gained from them.

