The sorted pipe-weed needs to be placed on the drying racks.Place the pipe-weed on the drying racks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Blossom Gamgee is on Northcotton Farm.

Blossom Gamgee will want to know the pipe-weed has been placed on the drying racks.Talk to Blossom Gamgee'Now that the leaves are sorted, the next step is to dry them. The longer the pipe-weed dries, the stronger the taste becomes. It also darkens the colour of the smoke.

'Once it's dried, we'll blend different amounts of dried and fresh pipe-weed to make some unique flavours. That's how you get the best out of the plant.

'The drying racks are just around the corner to the north-east, and there's already some pipe-weed on it. Add what you've sorted to the racks.''Good, now comes the best part. Time for samples!''We won't dry all of it all the way, but it needs to be out there for at least a day or two.'Elsa the Bold, at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur, desires black heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature found in Agamaur.You accepted a task from Elsa the Bold to acquire black heartwood from the creatures of nature within Agamaur. You can find Elsa the Bold within the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Black heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature in Agamaur. Bring them to Elsa the Bold at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Black heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature in Agamaur.'Perhaps there is a way that we can save them still. It is good to see that there is still some measure of purity in the hearts of the Free Peoples.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Eriac the Strong, at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur, desires heavy sword sheaths from the intelligent foes found in Agamaur.You accepted a task from Eriac the Strong to acquire heavy sword sheaths from the intelligent foes within Agamaur. You can find Eriac the Strong within the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Heavy sword sheaths from the intelligent foes in Agamaur. Bring them to Eriac the Strong at the Eglain-camp in Agamaur.Wanted: Heavy sword sheaths from the intelligent foes in Agamaur.'These will do nicely to shield our weapons from the ravages of this land.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Eriac the Strong at the Eglain-camp at Agamaur desires shimmering essences from the Dead found in Agamaur.You accepted a task from Eriac the Strong to acquire shimmering essences from the Dead within the swamp south of Agamaur. You can find Eriac the Strong within the Eglain-camp at Agamaur.Wanted: Shimmering essences from the Dead in Agamaur. Bring them to Eriac the Strong at the Eglain-camp at Agamaur.Wanted: Shimmering essences from the Dead in Agamaur.'They will rise no more. We will see that Radagast is given these prizes.

'Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.While Orcs dislike the sun nearly as much as their lesser brethren, the goblins, they have more tolerance for it and can be forced to endure it for a time when necessity—or the lash of their masters—demands. Despite this, the River Hobbits of Misthallow know all too well that Orcs and goblins of the North often stalk the mists that linger upon the Wells of Langflood.Defeat goblins and OrcsLike all the great tales, your battles end in an epic conclusion.Use Ceaseless Argument, Epic for the Ages, Essay of FireYou must further your tutoring of students who will follow the tradition and art of music in Middle-earth.Complete the previous tier of music mentor.You must further your tutoring of students who will follow the tradition and art of music in Middle-earth.Teach the art of music to your fellowsIn the way of the spear, one always desires to have the option of using the best attack at the right time.Strike with javelin skillsIn the way of the spear, one always desires to have the option of using the best attack at the right time.Strike with javelin skillsExplore the blasted wastes with bastions of resistance well-hidden among the hills of this dark land, and the great road that leads to the ancient fortresses of the Witch-king of Angmar.Explore the Bastions of HopeExplore the Circle of DespairExplore the Road to WarFind the Seven SwordsExplore the wilds of Evendim, the tombs of the kings that ruled over Arnor, and the ruins of the ancient city of Annúminas.Explore the City of the KingsExplore the Ruins of EvendimExplore the Tombs of EvendimExplore the Wilds of EvendimFind the Markers of the Sunken CityThe Blade-storm is considered one of the most exacting and difficult manoeuvres a Champion can execute, it is worth learning it well.Land critical blows with Blade-stormSomehow the very essence of the Red Swamp has been corrupted. Radagast the Brown, a Wizard who has come to Agamaur, seeks to learn the cause.Gloomleaf can be found in the north-west of Agamaur.

Radagast the Brown has tasked you with destroying the corrupted creatures of Agamaur in an effort to cleanse the Gaunt-lord Ivar's evil from the swamp. In your absence, he learned of a great Huorn that was corrupted and is the force stirring the other Huorns. He has asked you to destroy Gloomleaf.Radagast is in the Eglain camp in the southern reaches of Agamaur, near the entrance.

Radagast sent you to destroy the great Huorn Gloomleaf in an effort to cleanse Ivar's evil from Agamaur. The deed done, it is time to return to Radagast with the tragic news of your success.'This is a most terrible day for all of us. I have learned from Dannasen that the swamp was corrupted long ago during the wars, and the continued corruption is the work of a creature he calls the Red-maid. I glean that this Red-maid was once one of the gentle River-maidens who fell to the terrible corruption of her lands. Her hand now destroys all it touches.

'Alas, there is a great Huorn here, Gloomleaf, that has been swayed by the Red-maid's grasp, and its hatred stirs the other Huorns. Sadly, this old and great creature must be destroyed. I am sure you will find Gloomleaf somewhere to the north-west among the other Huorns.

'You must put this poor creature to rest.''Oh poor, poor Gloomleaf. What has happened to this world that even the greatest Huorns are now twisted and rotten? Nay, you need not answer... there is a Shadow in Mordor that is answer enough.

'Gloomleaf must be destroyed... it is time you were about your task.''Thank you, my good ${RACE}. It was a terrible deed, but one that I deemed necessary. With Gloomleaf's death, the Red-maid's hold on Agamaur will be weakened.'Locusts are a bane to the livelihood of the oat-farmers of Oatbarton.Nibs Chubb's fields are east and north-east of his farm.

Nibs Chubb has accepted your offer of aid and has pointed you towards his fields.Defeat swarming locusts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat locust-queens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find anything amiss in Nibs Chubb's fieldsNibs Chubb is at his farm, east of Oatbarton.

You should return to Nibs Chubb and tell him of the elk-carcass and the wolf that attacked you.'The North Moors are a dangerous place... not two days ago, I saw a wolf right on the border of Oatbarton! I haven't heard naught like it since the Fell Winter, though I gather Hal over there might tell you one of his stories of stranger creatures.

'Now, my oat-farm has been having trouble with locusts, and the bounder expects me to drive them off! Finding a lost cow or chasing off a fox is my business; I suspect you might have more of a mind for removing pests.

'The fields are east and north-east of here. If you can clear those locusts away from my crop, I would offer you a bit of coin. Also, keep on the look-out for anything else amiss in my fields... animals have been turning up dead all over Oatbarton recently.

'I have never had to worry this much in all my days farming oats! But business has had a turn for the better of late and, "the best crop only comes with the rain" or so my dad always said.''Have you cleared out those locusts ${PLAYER}? Remember, you won't be seeing a penny until all the work's done. My fields are just to the east and north-east of here, if you've forgotten.''You cleared out the locusts, and that's well and good, but I am very worried about those wolves you saw prowling my fields. Insects eating the crop are an easy enough matter, but wolves on my land is something else!'Sometimes you do not have time to work yourself up to a task; you must simply hurl yourself into the breach.Use Battle-frenzyAn explosive start can be ruined if you do not follow through. Make sure you finish what you started.Use Critical Hit chain finishing skillsA Guardian would rather be nowhere else than the front line, ready for battle.Use ChargeThe terrible slaughter of the Orc invasion has driven away the farmers from the western Nan Amlug, and with them any trade the Rangers had maintained. Short on supplies and provender, the Rangers of Esteldín must rely on their own resourcefulness to restock their supplies.Aurochs can be found in Rhunenlad and Nan Amlug, east of Esteldín.

Mallennor has been tasked with providing shelter and food for the Rangers of Esteldín and has asked you to help them by hunting aurochs for hide and meat.'I was asked to take stock of our provisions and appraise the repair of our tents. I have found that both are lacking. Without preparations, we will be forced to split our forces both to hunt the remaining game in the region and to fight the Orcs. Our numbers are too few for such a task.

'My request is simple: gather your allies and head into Rhunenlad and Nan Amlug, east of Esteldín, where the Aurochs are more plentiful. From the bulls collect hides...they can be used as shelter. The yearlings and cows will provide the best meat.

'Our needs will be great, so do not be frugal in your hunt.''This will help solidify our stores and allow us to remain focused on the trial at hand. We are moving closer to the inevitability of war each day. 

'Take heart, for you have aided us in our hour of need, and you are to be thanked and rewarded, ${PLAYER}.'A great host of Orcs spilled through Ram Dúath, driving the Men of the North Downs from their farms. Esteldín, home of the few remaining Dúnedain of the North, was spared discovery. It is from there that the Rangers begin to plan their counter-offensive against the black tide of Angmar.Goblins can be found on the outskirts of the Orc-encampment at Dol Dínen, south-east of Esteldín.

According to Elúr, the goblins of the North Downs have been forced into servitude by the Orcs of Angmar and act as the advance forces of the Orc-host. The Ranger has asked you to dispose of the goblins, bringing him their slave collars as evidence that you have completed your task.'Until recently, small goblin raiding-parties were the worst threat faced by the North Downs. But with the arrival of the Orcs out of Angmar, the goblins have themselves been forced by their greater brethren into servitude.

'Now, they watch the front-lines of the host at Dol Dínen, under command of the Orcs, awaiting their turn to be sacrificed for the greater glory of the Iron Crown. We should not make them wait overlong. If you are willing, make your way south-east out of Esteldín to the outskirts of the Orc-encampment at Dol Dínen and kill the goblins there.

'Bring me the slave collars worn by the goblins as evidence that your task has been accomplished.''Ah, you have returned... and successful, I see. Perhaps the Orc-generals will feel less secure with the wall of goblin-flesh thinned around them.

'In truth, though, this is but a small victory. The Orc army is vast, and the Dúnedain of the North are a dwindling people.'Legend tells of a powerful Warg once offered the ability to rule all Wargs in return for service to the Iron Crown. A collar fashioned of iron was made and powerful sorceries used to enchant the item. The great beast was slain and the collar removed, but tales of the beast have been passed down through the ages amongst the Men of the North Downs.Krithmog is somewhere within Fornost in the North Downs.

If Krithmog has been returned to life and taken to Fornost he will be found somewhere where the Wargs are plentiful. Place the collar in a suitable location, then spring your trap upon him. Will Widdup named the collar as the ancient binding of Krithmog and sent you in search of a place to offer it to the revived Warg-king.Will Widdup is at the Treasure Hunters' Camp, in the northern North Downs.

Will Widdup awaits news of your battle against Krithmog.'If it is true and the forces of Orcs and Angmarim within Fornost have created a fell wight from the corpse of Krithmog, then we must ferret out the beast and end its existence once more. Fortunately, this old collar may prove the key. It bound the Warg once and may do so again.

'There. I have inscribed an ancient rune I learned in my travels. Take the collar up once more. It will draw the beast to you, if you can find the right place to set it down.'

'You will need to search for a location within Fornost that Krithmog may be hiding. No doubt it is a deep, dark warren abounding with Wargs. Find the proper place to set down that collar, then destroy the evil Warg a final time.''${PLAYER}, I bid you enter Fornost and find Krithmog...the Warg-king must be destroyed!''The beast is quiet again. We must remain vigilant, for as long as there are enemies within Fornost there is a chance that the beast will return once more.

'We must discover the intentions of this invasion force. For the moment, you have given us a great victory over an ancient and deadly foe.'A wash of egg-whites is needed to cover the top of the crust of the pies.Eggs can be found near the chickens on the Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse asked you to gather several eggs to help with the pie making.Gather eggs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rosa Brockhouse is at the Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse will want to begin working with the eggs.'The next step is to get eggs. Eggs are used to make a wash that goes over the top of the pie. This makes it cook golden brown and gives the top that delicious crunch.

'Eggs are, of course, laid by chickens. You can find them over by the animal-pens to the east.

'I'll need several eggs if I am to make all the pies I need to for the market.''The chickens should be used to people removing the eggs by now.''Just enough to meet my needs. Excellent! Now only the butter is left.'The first step in making a pie is to get flour for the crust.Wheat can be found in the fields of the Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse asked you to gather some wheat to make flour for pie-crusts.Collect wheat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Edulf Tunnelly is at the windmill on the Northcotton Farm.

Edulf Tunnelly will help you grind the wheat into flour.Rats can be found around the windmill at the Northcotton Farm.

Edulf Tunnelly asked you to drive off some rats while she grinds the wheat into flour.Defeat rats at the windmill (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Edulf Tunnelly is at the windmill at the Northcotton Farm.

You should return to Edulf Tunnelly.Talk to Edulf Tunnelly at the Northcotton FarmRosa Brockhouse is at the Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse will be pleased to see the flour you helped make.'I am supposed to make several pies for the market, but I haven't had the time to gather up all the ingredients that I need to begin baking. 

'The first step is to make the crust. For that, we will need a few things, but the first is flour. To make that, we will need to gather some wheat, then bring it to the windmill for grinding.

'Wheat can be found in the fields here at the Northcotton Farm. Edulf Tunnelly is at the windmill to the west.''Wheat can be found in the fields here at the farm.''Bring this wheat to Edulf Tunnelly, and she will help you grind it into flour.''You want to have this wheat here ground into flour? I can certainly do that for you. It's going to take some time, though, and I can't guarantee you'll get all of it back.

'We've been having trouble with rats getting into the windmill and eating the wheat and flour. We always seem to end up short no matter how much we put in.

'If you could scare off a bunch of the little rodents, I can make sure you get all the flour you need.''If those rats are here I doubt you'll end up with enough flour to make the pies you need.''You're back just in time. I just finished up grinding the wheat. Looks like you've got plenty of flour for those pies.''There's naught more that I can do with that flour. I work the windmill, not the oven.''This flour is perfect. Soft and fluffy, not lumpy. Very nice job. Now, on to the filling.'Jam is used to make the filling for the pies.The vineyard is north of Rosa Brockhouse on Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse asked you to help her make jam for her pies. The first step is to gather grapes.Collect grapes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The crushing tubs are next to the vineyard at the Northcotton Farm.

The grapes need to be crushed in the tubs before they can be turned into jam.You were told that by <rgb=#FF0000>dancing</rgb> on the grapes in the tubs, you could crush them effectively.

The grapes are in tubs by the vineyard. The next step is to crush them.The crushed grapes need to be collected from the tub.Collect the crushed grapes from the tubTheobald Longfurrow is next to the vineyards on Northcotton Farm.

Theobald Longfurrow will help you turn your crushed grapes into jam.Rosa Brockhouse is south of the vineyards on the Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse will want to get the jam put into the pie.'I have started working the flour to make a crust for the pie, but I don't have anything to fill it with. Can you imagine an air pie? Wouldn't be very filling, I imagine.

'I use grape jam to fill my pies. There are some vineyards here on the farm where you can pick fresh grapes, which will then need to be crushed. To crush them you will want to place them in the tubs at the vineyard, then <rgb=#FF0000>dance</rgb> upon them. I know it sounds like madness, but it makes the texture perfect and feels so fun between your toes. After they are crushed, bring them to Theobald Longfurrow. He will add some syrups and sugars to make the perfect jam.

'The vineyard is north from here. Start by picking the grapes. You can't have jam without grapes!''You can't make a pie without filling, you can't make filling without jam, and you can't make jam without grapes!''The crushing tubs are next to the vines from which you picked the grapes. Once they are placed within the tubs, you will want to <rgb=#FF0000>dance</rgb> upon them to crush them.''You crushed these pretty well... almost too well. You still want a few chunks here and there to give the jam -- and thus the pie -- a good consistency.

'Let's see... a little bit of sugar... some syrup... stir that up... some rhubarb... seal the lid... and there you go! Jam fit for a pie.''Well, go on and bring it back. Pies won't fill themselves you know.''This jam is delicious. Usually we would let it sit and thicken for a few days before using it, but we don't have time for that this year. Now, I believe that eggs are next on the list.'One of the main features of the Northcotton Farmer's Market is its animal petting area.Chickens can be found around the animal pens on Northcotton Farm.

Gerard Deephole asked you to gather up the chickens around the farm.Gather up the chickens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gerard Deephole is at the animal pens on Northcotton Farm.

Gerard Deephole will be pleased to hear the chickens have all been caught.Talk to Gerard Deephole'The sheep are all set, and the next step is the chickens. The children don't so much get to pet those but they do enjoy throwing feed in and watching them run like crazy to try and get it all.

'The chickens got out in the same storm that the sheep did last week. Unlike the sheep, they won't follow you. You'll have to grab them and carry them back over here. They're squirmy though, so move quick.''The little ones love to watch the chickens run around trying to get all the corn they throw in for them.''The chickens will try and get away, so move quickly once you've grabbed them.'If the Northcotton Farm is to have a market, a lot will need to be cleaned up.Hoes, sickles, trowels, and hay-forks can be found in the fields of the Northcotton Farm.

The tools strewn about the Northcotton Farm need to be cleaned up before the market can be opened.Collect hoes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sickles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect trowels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect hay-forks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Milo Longfurrow is at the Northcotton Farm.

Milo Longfurrow will be pleased to see the tools have been collected.'We have been in full production for a while now and focusing on the harvest. Making sure we have enough for the farmer's market has meant we haven't had time to keep up with making the farm look nice. If we are to have guests, we'll want to make sure appearances are tip-top.

'The first thing to clean up is the tools. We just finished the harvest, and there are tools strewn over many of the fields. Hoes, sickles, trowels, and hay-forks have been left lying about.

'If you could wander the fields and pick up all the tools you find, then bring them back, that would be a great help.''People care about things looking nice when they visit the farm.''With these cleaned up and stored for the winter, we can now focus on some other areas that need a little help.'One of the main features of the Northcotton Farmer's Market is its animal petting area.The sheep have roamed into the fields surrounding the animal-pens.

Gerard Deephole asked you to gather the sheep up and bring them back to the pen.Bring sheep back to the pen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gerard Deephole is at the animal-pens on the Northcotton Farm.

Gerard Deephole will want to know all the sheep are back in the pen.Talk to Gerard Deephole'The market is more than just a place to come and buy and sell things from the farm. It's also a chance to experience farm-life... to see what it's like to work the land.

'One of the most important parts of that is letting the little ones feed and pet the animals. This is a major event for them. Many of them have seen these animals before but never gotten a chance to get up close.

'There's a lot of work to do before the animals are ready, though. First up are the sheep. They got out of their pen in a storm last week, and I need your help gathering them back up. They're pretty docile. If you go up and talk to them, they should follow you for a short bit. Just lead them back to the pen.''The sheep'll follow you for a little bit, but they tend to lose focus pretty quick.''Good! With the sheep all in, we can turn to the next step... chasing chickens.'The Ranger Amlan has learned that you assisted Dob Sandheaver with the giant Svalfang.Svalfang's hut is north of Buckland, overlooking the Brandywine River.

Amlan has asked you to drive the giant Svalfang from the land.Bring news of your deeds to Amlan'You are the one that helped the halfling, Dob Sandheaver, are you not? It seems the situation is much graver than I first thought, and it has gone beyond stolen food-bags.

'The giant that attacked Dob is very small for his kind, but he is a giant nonetheless. How and when he wandered down to the borders of the Shire is a mystery, even to one such as myself, who sees many comings and goings unnoticed by other folk. He has begun to waylay many travellers north of Buckland and has even been sighted on the borders of the Bree-fields. At first I was content to watch him and wait, but I see now that he is a great threat.

'I would ask you to gather companions and face Svalfang. We Rangers have protected the borders of the Shire for years, but there are too few of us here to face this foe. I would not have Aragorn return to find the land he guarded for so many years in ruin because of my inaction. Find the giant and drive him forth from these lands.''You have defeated the giant? Well done! There have been many strangers lurking on the borders of that peaceful land.  I do not doubt he would have caused many more problems than a few scared hobbits, had he not been dealt with.'Every year the hobbits of Northcotton Farm, in Oatbarton, have a farmer's market.Oatbarton is in the Shire, on the southern border with Evendim.Talk to Nod Gardener in Oatbarton'The hobbits in Oatbarton have a market every year at the Northcotton Farm. By now, word has usually spread to us of the event, and many will change their trip to Evendim to be sure to catch it.

'We have not yet heard anything from the Small Folk, and I am beginning to worry something is the matter or may have happened.

'Oatbarton is to the West of here on the southern border with the Shire. Once you get there, speak with the Bounder in charge of the town.''I believe you might find the Farmer's Market in Oatbarton an interesting diversion.''Well, I would certainly hope the market is still going to happen. I'm nearly out of pipe-weed!'Pipe-weed is something hobbits take very seriously. A market would never be visited unless it had a suitable supply and variety.The pipe-weed and crows can be found around the fields of the Northcotton Farm.

Blossom Gamgee asked you to pick some pipe-weed and drive off the crows that are stealing it.Harvest pipe-weed (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Drive off crows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Blossom Gamgee is at the Pipe-weed House on the Northcotton Farm.

Blossom Gamgee will want to see the pipe-weed you picked.'Pipe-weed smoking is older than you and I. It is a tradition long held by us hobbits. Pipe-weed is a plant we grow here at the Northcotton farm. It is a particular leaf, different than the southern farms in the Shire.

'The time has come to harvest and dry the pipe-weed to get it ready for the market. This is a multiple step process, and each part must be handled with care.

'The first step is to pick the pipe-weed. There are still some fields that we have not picked yet. While you are out there, drive off some of the crows that have been taking what few leaves we have left.''The crows seem to really like the pipe-weed we grow here. Some years, we lose nearly half the crop.''This smells wonderful... the last harvest of the season!'Your sister Langhár has been sent to Ost Guruth, in the Lone-lands, to track down one of Beorn's blood-line who has turned to evil.Langhár is at Ost Guruth, the ruin-hold in the Lone-lands.

Langhár has asked you to help her confront Ofolmóth, a Beorning who has turned to evil.Talk to Langhár and travel with her to confront OfolmóthLanghár is at Ost Guruth, the ruin-hold in the Lone-lands.

You have defeated Ofolmóth and ended his danger to the Free People of Eriador, but not without consequence.Talk to Langhár in Ost Guruth'No pleasant journey draws me hither, my sister/brother. I have been sent to put an end to one of our folk. I held no warm feelings for Ofolmóth, and I do not think you did either, but he is still blood. It saddens me that this action is necessary, but Grimbeorn has commanded it and I think it just.

'Some weeks ago, Ofolmóth began straying further and further afield, and I think he encountered someone beneath the trees of Mirkwood. From this mysterious stranger he heard cruel whispers, and Grimbeorn believes these whispers took root within Ofolmóth's heart. He spoke vile words to Grimbeorn and others of our folk, and fled from our land. Now we hear that he has killed without mercy, striking down innocent folk without care or reason.

'I am glad you answered my letter. Ofolmóth is strong and fierce, but he is of our blood, and I need someone at my side if I am to go through with this unpleasant duty. He deserves this judgement, but I do not know that I could carry it out alone. Speak to me when you are ready, and we will make for where he was last seen.''Let us go at once. I am eager to have this unhappy duty behind me.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a solo quest and cannot be completed with a fellowship.</rgb>'We have the victory, sister/brother, but it is not a happy one. I know that Ofolmóth was a danger to innocent folk and had to be confronted, but I wish it had not been so.

'His words were cruel, it is true. But I can also understand from where they came, and know that it was not Ofolmóth alone that chose this path. Others preyed upon his inclinations. Some great evil crossed his path in Mirkwood, and made use of him for its own purposes. He had no love for dwarves, but this evil aligned him with these Dourhands and their master Skorgrím. That is the evil we must face.

'I will stay here for a time to rest from my hurts, , and then I will bring these tidings to Grimbeorn. I thank you once more, and hope to see you again and in health. Guard your spirit well, and do not follow the dark path that brought Ofolmóth to his end!'Many claim that the Champion knows the greatest joy in battle. Those who believe this have never seen a Guardian wading into a throng of foes, sinews singing to the thrill of mortal peril.Use Thrill of Danger in battleIt is believed that by lightening the heart with song, one can more easily weather the physical ills of the world.Use Song of AidIt is believed that by lightening the heart with song, one can more easily weather the physical ills of the world.Use Song of Aid or Inspire FellowsThe bite of cold can fade swiftly or slow. It is a matter of practice to let it linger.Strike with Chilling RhetoricEven when the situation seems most grim, the Captain is a light that pierces through the darkness and inspires hope in the hopeless.Land a Critical Hit or Devastate with Blade of ElendilA Captain is someone to look up to and admire. Their sound technique and valour inspires others to keep fighting.Land a Critical or Devastate with Valiant Strike or InspirePalma Brownlock has convinced you to reacquire the sword Medhrod, stolen by half-orcs at Naerost in the Lone-lands.Palma Brownlock is waiting to speak with you on the hills outside Naerost.

Palma has somehow convinced you to sneak into the ruins of Naerost to reacquire the sword Medhrod.The sword Medhrod is somewhere within the ruins of Naerost.

The half-orcs that stole Medhrod are surely keeping the sword under close watch inside the ruins.Collect MedhrodPalma Brownlock is on the slope outside the ruins of Naerost.

Palma is eagerly awaiting your return with Medhrod.'Are you ready, ${PLAYER}? If you're a burglar worth your salt, you won't have any difficulty sneaking into Naerost over there and coming back here with Medhrod. You are worth your salt, aren't you, ${PLAYER}? Go on, make me proud!

'I'd do it myself, but I figure you could use the practice. You're welcome!''You did it! Well, that's a relief, isn't it, ${PLAYER}? I'm sure you didn't have any trouble!

'Now let's go back to the Inn and spread the good news -- the half-orcs aren't in possession of Medhrod any longer, and it serves them right!

'Let me know when you're ready to go.''Medhrod is probably being kept somewhere deep inside the ruins, ${PLAYER}. Try not to be seen, or you'll have a difficult fight on your hands!''I'm ready to leave if you are! The Inn awaits!'You have journeyed to Ost Galumar with Jarl Copperbrace to raise the standards of the Free Peoples over the Dourhand-occupied ruins.Jarl Copperbrace is waiting for you outside Ost Galumar.

Jarl Copperbrace wishes to speak with you.Three standard-poles are positioned on the walls of Ost Galumar.

Jarl Copperbrace has asked you to raise three of the standards of the Free Peoples over Ost Galumar.Hang standards of the Free Peoples on standard-poles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Jarl Copperbrace is outside Ost Galumar.

Jarl Copperbrace is waiting to speak with you about your successful errand.'Ost Galumar lies ahead, ${PLAYER}, and you will find standard-poles at various places along the walls. The Dourhands must have some way of climbing onto the walls, for they removed the standards that once flew over this keep.

'Raise three of the standards I have given you above Ost Galumar...and watch out for the Dourhands that hide within!''Look at them, ${PLAYER}! The sight of those standards flying once again over Ost Galumar is a happy one indeed. And you are a captain of great worth to have accomplished this thing!

'Let me know when you are ready to return to Othrikar, ${PLAYER}, and we shall give these happy tidings to its people!''Are you ready to return to Othrikar, ${PLAYER}? We should travel with haste and tell the people there that the standards of the Free Peoples fly once again over Ost Galumar!'You have come to Ost Cyrn with Slade Ransford to cast out the Orcs that have taken up residence within the ruins.Slade Ransford is at Ost Cyrn, in the Lone-lands.

Slade is waiting to speak with you about fighting deep into Ost Cyrn in search of Mosal-ruk, the vile Orc that leads this war-band.Talk to Slade RansfordSlade Ransford is at Ost Cyrn, in the Lone-lands.

Slade is taking a quick rest atop one of the ruined buildings of Ost Cyrn, and is waiting to speak with you about proceeding further into the ruins in search of Mosal-ruk.Talk to Slade RansfordMosal-ruk stands defiantly before you.

You have fought deep into Ost Cyrn with Slade Ransford, and the time has come to face Mosal-ruk, the leader of the war-band that occupies the ruins.Defy Mosal-rukSlade Ransford stands triumphantly within Ost Cyrn.

Together, you and Slade journeyed into the ruins of Ost Cyrn and put an end to Mosal-ruk and his vile war-band.Talk to Slade Ransford'We are victorious, ${PLAYER}, true champions of the Lone-lands! Mosal-ruk will no more threaten the folk who try to make their living in these hard lands, and we have sent a message to his masters this day -- we will stand against you, no matter the odds!

'Tell me when you are ready, and we will journey back to Ost Guruth with this happy news!''What we accomplished today is a thing of which legends are made -- "The Song of Slade and ${PLAYER} at Ost Cyrn," if you like!

'Are you ready to return to Ost Guruth? Perhaps I'll find a minstrel to whom I can relate this tale!''Ost Cyrn lies ahead, ${PLAYER}, and if we are true of heart and strong of arm, the Orcs within will not remain there by this time tomorrow!

'Let us take a circuitous route into the ruins and slay as many Orcs as lie within our path. Mosal-ruk will test our courage even by himself, and the fewer of his band that remain when we face him, the more confident I will be of our victory!''Let us continue, ${PLAYER} -- the deadliest leg of our journey lies ahead, for Mosal-ruk has surely learned of our coming by now. We will show him he and his kind are not welcome in these lands!'You have come to the Stone-troll caves near Lin Giliath to defend Andyri's Elves while they recover from their pitched battle.Andyri is waiting to speak with you inside the Stone-troll caves.Talk to AndyriYou must prevent the Elves from being defeated while they rest from their battles.Defend the Elves until they have recovered their strengthAndyri is in the Stone-troll cave.

You have protected Andyri's Elves while they recovered and should now speak with her.Talk to Andyri'At last, ${PLAYER}! We must hurry to the Elves of my company, for they are weak from their battle and will not long be able to defend themselves.''Endless thanks, ${PLAYER}. If you had not been here...well, there is little point in walking that sad road.

'You have saved the Elves of my company, friend Warden, and I thank you for it.''I am ready to leave this place, ${PLAYER}. We were victorious this day, thanks to your able defence, but it was almost not so. You have my thanks and the thanks of my friends for it.'Severin Wheeler has enlisted your help in the defence of Trestlebridge. The town is hard-pressed by the Orcs, and guardians are needed to keep them from crossing into Bree-land.Severin Wheeler is standing by the Trestlebridge.

Severin Wheeler is waiting to assign you your position in the defence of the town.Severin Wheeler told you that several men have come to aid in the defence of Trestlebridge.

Before he left to aid a group of farmers trapped in the North Downs, Severin Wheeler charged you with keeping any marauding Orcs away from the bridge. You should speak with the men nearby to assign them their positions.Hilton Harper is standing in front of the Trestlespan.

Hilton Harper is waiting to speak with you about organizing the defence of Trestlebridge.Talk to defenders of TrestlebridgeHilton Harper is outside Trestlebridge.

Hilton Harper is waiting to speak with you about your defence of the town.Talk to Hilton Harper'Are you ready to return to the town? We will await the return of Severin Wheeler and recount to him the great deeds that were done today!''I received word that some farmers on the North Downs have holed up in a small cave and are requesting assistance from Trestlebridge. Before your arrival, I feared they would be lost, for I could not leave the town with so few defenders -- but with your timely arrival, I see now a chance to save those farmers.

'I will ride to their defence, ${PLAYER}, and I entrust the safety of Trestlebridge to you. If any Orcs attack while I am away, you must keep them away from the bridge at any cost! If the bridge is taken, we lose what small advantage we have. Do not let them reach the bridge!

'A few men have pledged to aid in the defence. Speak to these hardy few and assign them their positions. I will see you again, if both our tasks lead to victory!''We meet once again, ${PLAYER}, and I must tell you that my spirits are raised by your presence! After you helped me fight off the Blackwold brigands that threatened my farm, I got to thinking. It isn't right for good people to live in fear. It was time for me to repay the favour you did me.

'Travellers passing through Bree spoke of the Orc-raids against Trestlebridge, and I realized that this was where I was needed. If the Orcs dare show their ugly faces again, we'll turn them away and send them howling back across the Downs, I don't doubt it!

'My son is here, too. He has a keen eye and is handy with his bow, if you remember. I'll stand over in the shade of that tower and keep my weapon at the ready!''You might not remember me, ${PLAYER}, but I remember you! You helped my dad and me fight off the Blackwolds at our farm not so long ago. Cole Culvert would have been eating off our dishes and enjoying the fruits of our labours by now if it hadn't been for you... and don't think we don't appreciate it!

'My dad got to thinking, and one day he just up and asked if I wanted to go with him to fight the Orcs. The way I see it, I'd rather fight Orcs up north, Trestlebridge way, if it means we can avoid fighting them in the streets of Bree-town, or in our own fields! Everyone's got to do his own part.

'I've been practicing with this bow since I was just a boy, and I reckon I can fell a few Orcs before they get too close. I'll call out their positions from that rise over there, if they're so bold as to attack the town while we're here.''A guardian like yourself has also come to aid in the defence. His name is Hilton Harper, and I saw him checking the structure of the bridge not long ago. You should speak with him about the plan for defending this place, should the Orcs attack.''My dad was talking to another guardian a short time ago -- Harper, his name was. He's probably around here somewhere. You should speak to him about the plan for defending the town, should the Orcs choose to attack while Severin Wheeler is away.''It is a pleasure to meet a fellow guardian, ${PLAYER}, and the defences will be stronger for your presence here!

'I am ashamed to admit that not long ago I had given up hope -- the darkness that presses in on us seemed too great, and I was filled with despair. But a young captain showed me that hope always remains, even in situations most dire.

'So I took up my sword once more and vowed to fight on in the defence of good people, even when all others have failed. I have come to Trestlebridge, last bulwark between Bree-land and the marauding Orcs of the north; I will defend this place until I can defend it no more. We will prevail, ${PLAYER}, but we must remain alert. The Orcs could attack at any time, and if any reach the bridge, it is lost. Keep them away from the bridge at any cost!''The town is safe, for the moment, so I count it a victory, ${PLAYER}. The Orcs will attack again, and when they do we will be ready!''We have repelled the Orcs, for now, and we will repel them again if needed! I hope it is not for a while yet -- I need a good night's sleep!

'My dad may be able to fight tirelessly, but I do not yet have his endurance, though I hope to claim I have his courage!''We have done it, ${PLAYER}, and Trestlebridge is safe, for the moment. I do not think the Orcs will launch another attack for some time -- it is hard to remember, sometimes, that they, too, have need of rest. Though their masters may press them onward with whips and threats, even an Orc can succumb to exhaustion if pressed for too long in service of war.

'Tell me when you are ready to return to the town, and we can await the return of Severin Wheeler. I hope his task went as well, and the farmers he left to save have survived their dangers.

'Some of us were wounded this day, but still we stand against the Orcs, and will continue to do so for as long as it is necessary!'You have travelled with Gytha Lainey to the town of Overhill, in the Shire, to defend its people from the ravages of a prowling group of Wargs that have come down from the north.Gytha Lainey is on the road outside Overhill.

You have come to Overhill with Gytha Lainey to save its people from the danger that threatens them. Gytha is waiting to speak with you about how to proceed.Talk to Gytha LaineyThe people of Overhill are going about their business, oblivious to the danger that lurks somewhere north of their town.

Gytha has told you to warn the people of Overhill to stay inside their houses until the danger has passed.Gytha Lainey is in the town of Overhill.

Now that you have told the people of Overhill to go inside their houses, it is up to you and Gytha to stop the intruding Wargs.Talk to Gytha LaineyGytha Lainey is in Overhill.

You have successfully defeated the Warg hunting-party that intruded into the Bindbole Wood and should now speak of your success to Gytha Lainey.Talk to Gytha Lainey'What's that you said? Wargs? Oh my, what kind of creature is that? Well, whatever they are, they don't have any business coming to Overhill, and I don't mind saying it!

'It's late, and I was planning on going inside soon anyway. I'll turn in for the night, and I hope that in the morning these creatures will have come to their senses and left Overhill alone!''Wargs? If these creatures are anything like the big black bear that's caused me no end of grief, they're sure to be fierce!

'I don't want any trouble with none of these Wargs. I'll go inside now, and no questions asked!''Well, you bring disturbing news, ${PLAYER}, don't you? Of all the times for my cart to run into trouble, too! I'm sure these Wargs you mentioned wouldn't come anywhere near my home on Bree-hill!

'I'll stay with my friend Everard until this blows over. Thanks for letting me know!''Wargs? What could have ever brought such frightening creatures into the Shire?

'I'm going to go inside at once -- you don't need to tell me twice, not this hobbit!''It seems that we're not too late, ${PLAYER} -- we have arrived before the Wargs, but I do not know how long it will be before they reach the town.

'Quickly, run into town and tell everyone you see to go inside their homes and not to come back out until we say the danger has passed. Do not take "no" for an answer, ${PLAYER} -- their lives may depend on doing what we say.

'I will try and determine how far away the creatures may have come since I lost their trail.''You have done it, ${PLAYER}, and I cannot thank you enough! The people of Overhill would have been helpless without our warning and subsequent aid, and though they might not have appreciated the danger, it does my heart good to know that such a place is safe once again from the ravages of war.

'I think the Wargs will not come again into this land, not for a time, yet. I wonder what it is that brought them here?

'Let me know when you are ready to go, and we can travel back to Adso's camp.''Hello? The danger has passed, then? I am glad to hear it. You and your friend have my thanks, of course.''So the Wargs have gone, then? Good, good. I would have been out here too, of course, just to see what the fuss was all about, but I had the tea on and couldn't get away.

'Good work, though, no mistake about that!''I am ready to return to Adso's camp, if you are ready as well. The folk here are always polite, even if they do not always appreciate the presence of "Big Folk" like myself. Today, however, I think they do. It makes me smile.''Good, you have done well, ${PLAYER} -- now, at least, the hobbits here should be safe for a time should we fail to stop the Wargs.

'Judging from the direction of their tracks before I lost the trail, the Wargs are surely in the Bindbole Wood by now. Look to the north, and try to stop them before they reach the town! They will have split up by this time, and are surely prowling cautiously southward.

'I will try and stop any that reach the town, ${PLAYER}, but I am counting on you to defeat as many as you can as they approach -- if too many of the creatures arrive at Overhill at once, I will surely be overwhelmed, and then all hope of stopping the creatures will be gone.'You have come to the cave where Isferon has been keeping the boxes left to him by Berenin.Isferon is standing inside the cave.

Isferon is waiting to speak with you before he opens up the three boxes and learns what secrets his friend Berenin left behind when he sailed into the West.Isferon is standing inside the cave.

Isferon is opening the boxes left to him by Berenin.Watch Isferon open the boxes and learn their secretsIsferon is standing inside the cave.

Isferon has opened the three boxes his friend Berenin left behind when he sailed into the West.'I have the three keys at the ready. Let us see what secrets Berenin left for me when he sailed into the West!''I thank you, ${PLAYER}. I have learned more than I hoped to, and I feel it has made me richer than anything else could have. Let me know if you are ready to go, and we can return to Duillond.''Let us return to Duillond, ${PLAYER}. I have much to think about.'You have travelled to a nearby cave with Leigh Putnam to help raise the spirits of the refugees seeking shelter within and convince them that Trestlebridge would be a safer place to hide.Leigh Putnam is just inside the entrance of the cave.

You have travelled with Leigh to the cave where refugees are seeking shelter from Orc-attacks.The refugees are hiding in the back of the cave.

Leigh Putnam has asked you to try and lift the spirits of the refugees hiding in the cave and to convince them to come to Trestlebridge.Severin Wheeler has arrived in the cave with grim tidings.

You have tried to convince the refugees to come to the relative safety of Trestlebridge, but they refused.Talk to Severin WheelerLeigh Putnam is inside the refugee cave.

You have aided in the defence of the refugees, and now Leigh wishes to speak with you.'The Orcs didn't see you, did they? If you've endangered us by coming here, I'll make sure you regret it!

'Trestlebridge? No, we're not going there -- it's death! At least here, we might remain unseen by the Orcs. Trestlebridge is the main goal of the raiding-parties, and we'd be fools to try and hide there!''I don't think it's a good idea to go to Trestlebridge, and Ford agrees. We're safer here.

'Thank you for your concern, but I think we're going to stay where we are.''The Orcs are searching for survivors from their initial raid, ${PLAYER}, but if we stay hidden for a while, we should be safe. I hope they didn't follow you here.''This is the cave, ${PLAYER}. Let us go quickly -- I want to convince these people to come to Trestlebridge. The town may not be safe, but it has to be safer than this cave.

'Let us hurry -- my brother will warn us if any Orcs come too near.''That was too close for comfort, ${PLAYER}. We have lent the aid we could, and it is past time we returned to Trestlebridge. Perhaps now these people will listen to reason.

'Let me know when you are ready to return to the town, and we will be off.''The Orcs know about this cave now. We have no choice but to go to Trestlebridge. I thank you for your help, but I wish it was not necessary. When will this land be safe once again for good folk to live in peace?''Is nowhere safe from these vile creatures? Why can't they leave us alone!''This band of Orcs thought to have no trouble rooting us out, but Captain Wheeler, Edward Putnam, and yourself showed them their error. I applaud you, friend minstrel!''Let us return to Trestlebridge, ${PLAYER}. This cave is no longer safe, if ever it was.''${PLAYER}, it's too late -- a party of Orcs is on its way here! Lend us the aid you can, for they will be here in moments! If Edward and I fall, you and Leigh will be the only defence for the people here -- do not let the Orcs reach them!'You have come with your sister Langhár to the southern bogs of the Lone-lands on a sad mission: to put an end to the evils done by your kinsman Ofolmóth.You have come with Langhár to the southern bogs in search of Ofolmóth. She waits to speak with you before continuing to the ruins where your kinsman was seen.Talk to Langhár in the southern bogsYou have found Ofolmóth, and he wonders if you have come to join him.Talk to OfolmóthYou have found Ofolmóth, but he is no longer your kin. He is now your enemy.Survive your encounter with OfolmóthOfolmóth has told you his intentions. He will not abide  by the laws of Men.

Langhár wishes to speak with you before the fighting begins.Talk to Langhár in the ruinsOfolmóth has grievously injured Langhár, and must be defeated.Defeat Ofolmóth for his crimes against innocent folkLanghár has been grievously injured, but she is alive.Talk to Langhár in the ruins'Let us return to Ost Guruth. I am hurt, but I can walk.''Ofolmóth was last seen among the ruins just ahead. Let us go forward with caution, my sister/brother.''Your expression gives me your answer, ${PLAYER}. I thought as much. It was a vain hope that you would cherish the memory of our people and the blood of Beorn. Instead you side with those unworthy of you.

'I thought I could make you strong, ${PLAYER}. I failed in that, and I am sorry.''Are you ready, ? Ofolmóth will not come with us, but we cannot let him go, for he is a danger to the innocent folk of Middle-earth. I do not know much about these dwarves, but I can tell that this Skorgrím corrupts any who come into contact with him!

'I will handle Ofolmóth if you can deal with these dwarves, my sister/brother!''I know what we came here to do, but even so I found myself not wanting to hurt Ofolmóth. He felt no such restriction, and his claws cut deep.'

Langhár winces at the pain of her injury.

'I do not know much about Skorgrím, but what little I have learned is troubling. It is clear to me that he swayed our former kinsman in some way, preying upon his sensibilities.

'We have done as Grimbeorn asked, and ended the threat Ofolmóth posed to innocent folk. That must be enough for today. Perhaps tomorrow we will do something about Skorgrím Dourhand, for his danger remains.'Ruins of the ancient kingdom of Rhudaur, twisted by time and the touch of a dark evil, lay before you. Alongside Radagast the Brown, you must find Elsa and Eriac and assist them against the Gaunt-lord, Ivar the Blood-hand, and his undead minions.You have travelled with Radagast into the corrupted swamp of Agamaur. Aid him as he travels to the Eglain-encampment to the west.Talk to RadagastThe leader of the shades resides at the seemingly abandoned Eglain-encampment.

The defeat of Dour-root has opened the way into the western ruins, where the shades Radagast spoke of dwell. Radagast asked that you entreat with Dannasen, their leader, for their aid.Neven's throne is further northward into the ruins.

Neven holds some sway over the shade-captain, Dannasen. Perhaps if you speak with him you may find a way to alter Dannasen's orders.Dannasen lingers at the Eglain-encampment to the south of Neven's throne.

Neven, the shades' commander, was actually a wight summoned by Ivar to contain the shades. With his destruction, Dannasen may now be willing to aid you.Speak to DannasenFollow Radagast as he moves deeper into Agamaur.

Dannasen, renewed, called forth his men to battle and opened the way westward. It may be that now you will be able to reach Elsa and Eriac.Follow Radagast deeper into AgamaurSpeak to Elsa to learn more about her battles in Agamaur.

Thanks to Dannasen and his men, the way westward into Agamaur was opened, and you were able to reach Elsa and Eriac.The gate into Garth Agarwen lies to the north.

Elsa has sent you to seek Ivar at Garth Agarwen and put an end to him.Speak to RadagastSpeak to Radagast to see if there is something else he would have you do.

Radagast has driven the Gaunt-lord Ivar from Agamaur and stopped his nefarious plans...for now.Speak to Radagast'Shall we return to the Eglain-encampment now?''We must go west to the Eglain-encampment. Elsa and Eriac will be waiting, and I can better attend to the corruption from there.

'The power which continues to corrupt this swamp is terrible, but we may not have to face it alone. The beasts tell me that there are shades of Emelin's kind which dwell here and may be convinced to aid the Eglain. I do not know why they do not remain with their own kind, but they at least have not brought harm to the Eglain.

'Now, we must hurry... the very trees scream in pain. I must see what I can do to help them as quickly as possible. Follow me, though I cannot promise aid, for I fear I will need all of my strength to face Ivar the Blood-hand.''Neven orders me to hold my post, and I must obey. He is our commander. I am under his charge, as are my men. Honour binds us, even greater than the curse of Iarwain Ben-adar.

'I will not forsake my commander, though he is so very changed. I cannot disobey his orders while he remains. If thou wish us to fight, thou must entreat with Neven. His seat is to the north.''I will not forsake my commander, though he is so very changed. I cannot disobey his orders while he remains. If thou wish us to fight, thou must entreat with Neven. His seat is to the north.''A wight, a fell creature of Angmar...how could I have not seen this? So long have I lingered in this swamp, cursed by Iarwain Ben-adar to bring the salvation -- or the destruction -- of the Red-maid, she who dwells within Garth Agarwen, a Power now under the influence of the gaunt-men.

'I was blind to what Neven had become. Now, I shall make amends. Now, we shall make our battle and strive to fulfil our curse.''Elsa and I fought as best we could, but there were too many wights. Speak with Elsa. She may know how we should proceed against the evil in Agamaur.''We lost so many. The wights, there were too many. The Gaunt-lord...the Gaunt-lord cannot be defeated.

'We should flee...I-I am sorry. Speak with Elsa, my will is too badly shaken to speak more.''We were beaten. There is no way to hide this. This Ivar commands the water, the wights, the forest itself. He cannot be beaten by my meagre force, and I fear there is something far more terrible within the ruins to the north.

'I know there is little time before a great evil spills forth from Garth Agarwen. Our only hope is Radagast, only he will be able to stop this Ivar. Please, go north to the gate of Garth Agarwen with him. Stop the Gaunt-lord Ivar.''Hurry now, the Gaunt-lord Ivar is not far from here, near the gates of Garth Agarwen to the north. He may not reveal himself immediately, so we may need to draw him forth.

'The way ahead will be difficult, but do not give up hope. We shall triumph this day if we do not lose heart!''That creature commands great power, but against a heart full of hope and trust in one's allies, such power means nothing. Thank you for your help here, my friend. We have done well this day. Ivar's spreading corruption is stayed, at least for the moment.

'I must return now to the Eglain-encampment and help end this evil forever. There is more that you ought to do here, if you are willing. But we will not speak of such matters now.'Meneldir and Gorwen have vanished from the camp-site outside Dol Ernil. Did they run afoul of the Grey Fear?Halbarad seeks some clue to the disappearance of Meneldir and Gorwen.Talk to Halbarad in Dol ErnilThe tracks have become even more confused in the throne room of Dol Ernil.Search the throne room of Dol Ernil for cluesHalbarad stands in the throne room of Dol Ernil, searching for some clue to the whereabouts of Gorwen and Meneldir.Talk to Halbarad in the throne room of Dol ErnilThe trap-door in the corner of the throne room leads to catacombs beneath the fortress.Use the trap-door in the corner of the throne room to descend into the catacombsDid Meneldir and Gorwen come to the catacombs beneath Dol Ernil?Arrive in the catacombs beneath Dol ErnilDid Meneldir and Gorwen come to the catacombs beneath Dol Ernil?Search the catacombs beneath Dol Ernil for Meneldir and GorwenSomething is amiss in the catacombs beneath Dol Ernil, and more than one life endangered.Talk to Gorwen in the catacombs beneath Dol ErnilA terrible evil lurks within the catacombs beneath Dol Ernil.Survive your encounter with the Grey FearMeneldir has sustained a grievous wound. He has little time left.Hear Meneldir's last words'The creature has fled, ${PLAYER}, and we cannot pursue it from here. But we will see its designs undone, and the schemes of its master overthrown. I swear it.

'For now we will bring Meneldir from this place. We will not leave him here.''The tracks lead into Dol Ernil, but the message I read from them is confused. I cannot determine if Gorwen and Meneldir came this way in peace, or if their progress was hindered by an onslaught of foes. They were together, at least. Of that I am certain.

'We must follow them. Prepare for battle, friends.''There must be some clue here in the throne room of Dol Ernil, ${PLAYER}, if only we can find it.''A trap-door leading to catacombs beneath the fortress? I do not think Gorwen would follow Meneldir into such a place, ${PLAYER}.

'We will continue the search up here, but if you think there is a chance the two of them descended into the catacombs...

'If that is what you believe, I will not stop you from there continuing your search. We will join you below when we have finished looking for them up here... and with fortune we will bring you word that they are found, and your own search of the catacombs was needless.''Something is wrong, ${PLAYER}.

'While you were gone, Meneldir and I passed the time in quiet conversation. Nothing seemed amiss for hours! But then, all at once, he leapt to his feet, upsetting his cup and dropping his crust of bread in the dirt. "It is not dark enough for such creatures!" he shouted, drawing his sword. "What do you see?" I asked him, but he answered me not; I do not think he heard my words, and he dashed away from the camp and out among the ruins.

'I gathered up my own equipment and followed him, but for the risk of being cut by one of his blind swings I dared not get too near. It is a wonder he did not blunder into the wights that prowl the ruins above, or perhaps it is not. They might not have understood the words he spoke, and believed his swings were made at them.

'But I heard the words. He does not know where he is, ${PLAYER}. He thought the throne room above was a farmhouse in the woods, and the trap-door the entrance to a root cellar. I do not know what illusory foe he believes he faces, but whatever form it takes, I know this: it must be the work of the Grey Fear.'Meneldir's breathing is shallow, his heart beat slow. He does not have much time left.

'There were trolls, ${PLAYER}... like there were before. My watch... my vigil... I would not abandon those folk. Not... not this time.

'Not for my... not for my oath. For me.'You have travelled with Halbarad to Trestlebridge to aid in its defence against Orcs of the Ongbúrz tribe.Aid Halbarad in the defence of Trestlebridge.Talk to HalbaradHalbarad stands on the Trestlebridge, near Nellie Boskins.

Alongside Halbarad and the people of Trestlebridge, you have defended the town against the Ongbúrz Orcs.Talk to Halbarad'I'll take you back to Nellie's house.''The Orcs are nearly upon the town. Come with me quickly, we must convince Miss Boskins of the threat that is nearly upon her!''We have done well this day! I am indebted to you as well for your aid, as I fear the battle would have turned against the Free Peoples had you not been here.

'I must leave now, and I hope it is not too much to ask of you to stay behind. I would ask that you ensure that Trestlebridge remains safe through the night, then return to me in Esteldín.

'This was a great victory, my friend, a great victory indeed!'The Rangers of the North in Evendim have long sought for the wreck of the Ectheldur, the ancient flagship of Annúminas, but their efforts have not gone unnoticed by a band of tomb-robbers from the South.Tarneth has travelled to an islet on Lake Evendim.Talk to TarnethTarneth has travelled to an islet on Lake Evendim. You should follow her and explore the islet.Follow TarnethThe wreck is located on the southern end of the islet on Lake Evendim.Tarneth has travelled to an islet on Lake Evendim.Talk to TarnethNaegir has ambushed you at the islet on Lake Evendim.Confront NaegirNaegir and the wreck-hunters have attacked you! You must defeat them.Defeat Naegir and the wreck-huntersTarneth has travelled to an islet on Lake Evendim.Talk to TarnethGwindeth, the Blue Lady, has appeared in the waters of Lake Evendim.Witness Tarneth's meeting with the Blue LadyTarneth is preparing to travel back to Tinnudir.Talk to Tarneth'It is time we returned to Tinnudir, my friend.''We have made it, ${PLAYER}. It was a longer journey than I expected, but your rowing was a welcome boon. After all, I did not relish the thought of swimming across Lake Evendim!

'From what I could discern, Naegir's map appeared to point to this islet, so let us see what we can find.''I... have never before met the Blue Lady, my friend. And to be gifted Alwedion's forestaff... it is an honour beyond anything I could have imagined.

'I am thankful our search did not come to naught, and more thankful still that we know the true fate of the Ectheldur. It gladdens my heart to know that it will remain out of reach of the tomb-robbers of the South.

'My kin will want to know what has happened here, ${PLAYER}. Let us return to Tinnudir!''Are... are you all right, ${PLAYER}?'

Tarneth takes a moment to catch her breath.

'If what Naegir said was true, then we have been taken for fools. It was all for nothing, was it not?'

Tarneth kicks at the sand.

'All for nothing....''No, ${PLAYER}. I do not think it is the Ectheldur, either. Something is not right....'

Tarneth looks around, and a sudden crack near the trees on the islet draws her attention.

'Wait... did you hear that?''Well now, what do we have here?'

Naegir glares at you.

'Followed my map, did you? Clever bit of work, that. I had to draw up so many of them, but I knew I'd snare one of those Rangers in the end.

'Now, I know this wreck here isn't the Ectheldur. If I'm right, it's at the bottom of the lake just off the southern tip of this islet. I figured if it was above the water, it would have been picked clean ages ago. I left that detail out, of course, but now that we know where it is, I'm sure my crew can find a way down to it. They'd better, anyway.

'I almost had it, you know! I just needed a little help to find my way, and you two did a fine job leading us here.'

Naegir sneers.

