'This confirms the message we just received from Bree. I hope you are prepared, ${PLAYER}.'Blending into your surroundings provides both defensive and strategic bonuses.Use CamouflageRuins of the ancient kingdom of Rhudaur, twisted by time and the touch of a dark evil, lay before you. Alongside Radagast the Brown, you must find Elsa and Eriac and assist them against the Gaunt-lord, Ivar the Blood-hand, and his undead minions.You have travelled with Radagast into the corrupted swamp of Agamaur. Aid him as he travels to the Eglain-encampment to the west.Talk to RadagastThe leader of the shades resides at the seemingly abandoned Eglain-encampment.

The defeat of Dour-root has opened the way into the western ruins, where the shades Radagast spoke of dwell. Radagast asked that you entreat with Dannasen, their leader, for their aid.Neven's throne is further northward into the ruins.

Neven holds some sway over the shade-captain, Dannasen. Perhaps if you speak with him you may find a way to alter Dannasen's orders.Dannasen lingers at the Eglain-encampment to the south of Neven's throne.

Neven, the shades' commander, was actually a wight summoned by Ivar to contain the shades. With his destruction, Dannasen may now be willing to aid you.Speak to DannasenFollow Radagast as he moves deeper into Agamaur.

Dannasen, renewed, called forth his men to battle and opened the way westward. It may be that now you will be able to reach Elsa and Eriac.Follow Radagast deeper into AgamaurTalk to Elsa to learn more about her battles in Agamaur.

Thanks to Dannasen and his men, the way westward into Agamaur was opened, and you were able to reach Elsa and Eriac.The gate into Garth Agarwen lies to the north.

Elsa has sent you to seek Ivar at Garth Agarwen and put an end to him.Speak to RadagastSpeak to Radagast to see if there is something else he would have you do.

Radagast has driven the Gaunt-lord Ivar from Agamaur and stopped his nefarious plans... for now.Speak to Radagast'Shall we return to the Eglain-encampment now?''We must go west to the Eglan-encampment. Elsa and Eriac will be waiting, and I can better attend to the corruption from there.

'The power which continues to corrupt this swamp is terrible, but we may not have to face it alone. The beasts tell me that there are shades of Emelin's kind which dwell here and may be convinced to aid the Eglain. I do not know why they do not remain with their own kind, but they at least have not brought harm to the Eglain.

'Now, we must hurry... the very trees scream in pain. I must see what I can do to help them as quickly as possible. Follow me, though I cannot promise aid, for I fear I will need all of my strength to face Ivar the Blood-hand.''Neven orders me to hold my post, and I must obey. He is our commander. I am under his charge, as are my men. Honour binds us, even greater than the curse of Iarwain Ben-adar.

'I will not forsake my commander, though he is so very changed. I cannot disobey his orders while he remains. If thou wish us to fight, thou must entreat with Neven. His seat is to the north.''I will not forsake my commander, though he is so very changed. I cannot disobey his orders while he remains. If thou wish us to fight, thou must entreat with Neven. His seat is to the north.''A wight, a fell creature of Angmar... how could I have not seen this? So long have I lingered in this swamp, cursed by Iarwain Ben-adar to bring the salvation -- or the destruction -- of the Red-maid, she who dwells within Garth Agarwen, a Power now under the influence of the gaunt-men.

'I was blind to what Neven had become. Now, I shall make amends. Now, we shall make our battle and strive to fulfil our curse.''Elsa and I fought as best we could, but there were too many wights. Speak with Elsa. She may know how we should proceed against the evil in Agamaur.''We lost so many. The wights, there were too many. The Gaunt-lord... the Gaunt-lord cannot be defeated.

'We should flee... I-I am sorry. Speak with Elsa, my will is too badly shaken to speak more.''We were beaten. There is no way to hide this. This Ivar commands the water, the wights, the forest itself. He cannot be beaten by my meagre force, and I fear there is something far more terrible within the ruins to the north.

'I know there is little time before a great evil spills forth from Garth Agarwen. Our only hope is Radagast, only he will be able to stop this Ivar. Please, go north to the gate of Garth Agarwen with him. Stop the Gaunt-lord Ivar.''Hurry now, the Gaunt-lord Ivar is not far from here, near the gates of Garth Agarwen to the north. He may not reveal himself immediately, so we may need to draw him forth.

'The way ahead will be difficult, but do not give up hope. We shall triumph this day if we do not lose heart!''That creature commands great power, but against a heart full of hope and trust in one's allies, such power means nothing. Thank you for your help here, my friend. We have done well this day. Ivar's spreading corruption is stayed, at least for the moment.

'I must return now to the Eglain-encampment and help end this evil forever. There is more that you ought to do here, if you are willing. But we will not speak of such matters now.'Before the dawn of the Third Age, the kingdoms of Rhudaur, Arthedain, and Cardolan stood in opposition to one another at the cross-roads of the Lone-lands. As Rhudaur fell to vile corruption, some of the Men of Arthedain were held responsible for the desecration of the land, and they have long waited to see a time when they could make amends for their greatest failures.Narthan's ring might be found upon one of the wights roaming west of Ost Haer.

Narthan told you of his wife, her passing, and the ring that he carried during his life to remind him of the woman. He asked that you find it for him.'In the ruins to the west, where once my booted feet walked the stone halls, I lost one very dear to me. The last remembrance I had of her was a ring, now lost.

'Should you enter that place... search for my ring. Though it cannot end this curse, perhaps I will recall the joy that once filled my heart.

'I fear that it now rests upon the finger of one of the bloated creatures within the ruins or southern bog.''Were there warmth to one such as I, I would know it again. I will treasure this little bit thou hast done for me. I will recall thine efforts always.'Pipe-weed is something hobbits take very seriously. A market would never be visited unless it had a suitable supply and variety.The pipe-weed barrels are next to Blossom Gamgee.

The pipe-weed needs to be sorted before it can be dried.Sort the pipe-weed (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Blossom Gamgee is at the Northcotton Farm.

Blossom Gamgee will want to know that the pipe-weed has been sorted.Talk to Blossom Gamgee'I think we have just enough here to do what we must. The next step is to sort the pipe-weed out. We've got a couple of barrels over there.

'Pipeweed gets sorted by the quality and size of the leaf. The larger the leaf, the subtler and softer the taste. The smaller the leaf, the stronger and more bitter the flavour.

'Use the three bins to sort out the pipe-weed you picked earlier.''Everything is sorted? And not a leaf out of place, I hope.''Don't put the wrong leaves in the wrong barrel. There is nothing worse than a nice taste of smooth interrupted by a shot of bitter.'Only the most daring would risk spitting into an open flame.Use any Breath of Fire or IceUse any Breath of Fire or IceSome traps will strip off the natural armour of their targets increasing the damage taken from other sources.Use your Set Trap or Set Snare skillsThere are few things as simple and beautiful as fire, and the Rune-keeper knows how to use its majesty to their advantage.Use Distracting FlameMany fear Hunters not for the death they bring, but for the silence with which it approaches.Use Beneath NoticeMany fear Hunters not for the death they bring, but for the silence with which it approaches.Use Beneath NoticeThe most powerful music is also the most tiring, and it cannot be sustained for long.Use Anthem of WarThe most powerful music is also the most tiring, and it cannot be sustained for long.Use Anthem of WarWhen preparing for intense exertion, it is advisable to take a deep breath before diving into the fray.Use Deep BreathSometimes it is best to sacrifice precision and power for a great deal of speed.Use Needful HasteLore-masters are dreaded for their ability to ignite any object in their hands.Use Burning Embers or Sticky Gourd, or either of the Improved versions of these skillsLet your enemy look upon your face and see the fear spread across theirs.Strike with Fierce ResolveLet your enemy look upon your face and see the fear spread across theirs.Strike with Fierce ResolveWhile you are known for your defensive technique, you are not to be underestimated when on the offensive.Use War-chant, Thrust, or Smashing StabBolstered by Goldberry, your quest leads you to Sarch Vorn, Luilloth's final known destination. The way there is hidden and guarded by darkness. There is one, however, who knows the secret paths that wind into the mountain.Rhiangar has asked you to trace Luilloth's final footsteps to Sarch Vorn. The way there is unknown to her, but she suspects the Ranger Gorwen may know how to reach the grave-site.

You should talk to Gorwen at the caravan south of Andrath.Talk to GorwenSarch Vorn can be found west of Gerwyn's convoy in Cardolan, built into a hill of black stone.

You should meet Gorwen at the entrance to Sarch Vorn in Cardolan.Meet Gorwen outside Sarch VornGorwen can be found near the entrance to Sarch Vorn.

Sarch Vorn can be found west of Gerwyn's convoy in Cardolan, built into a hill of black stone. You should talk to Gorwen.Talk to GorwenLuilloth's remains or some other sign of her adventures long ago can likely be found somewhere in Sarch Vorn.

You should search for some sign of Luilloth in Sarch Vorn.Enter Sarch Vorn and search for some sign of LuillothLuilloth can be found in Sarch Vorn.

You have discovered Luilloth's remains animated by some fell spirit within the depths of Sarch Vorn. You should free Luilloth from the Grey Fear and return her remains to rest.Defeat Luilloth in Sarch Vorn, the Black GraveGorwen can be found in Sarch Vorn.

You have freed Luilloth's remains from the control of the Grey Fear. You should talk to Gorwen.The Grey Fear can be found in Sarch Vorn.

You must defeat the Grey Fear within Sarch Vorn before its evil can spread across Cardolan and the lands of the Free Peoples.Defeat the Grey Fear in Sarch Vorn, the Black GraveGorwen can be found in Sarch Vorn.

You have banished the Grey Fear. You should talk to Gorwen.Luilloth's chamber can be found in Sarch Vorn.

After banishing the Grey Fear, Gorwen has heard a sound echoing from the halls of Sarch Vorn. You should return to Luilloth's chamber and investigate it.Investigate Luilloth's chamber in Sarch VornGoldberry can be found in Sarch Vorn.

After banishing the Grey Fear, you discovered that Goldberry also travelled to Sarch Vorn. You should talk to Goldberry.Talk to Goldberry in Sarch VornGorwen can be found in Sarch Vorn.

After encountering Goldberry in the depths of Sarch Vorn, you have seen Luilloth's remains laid to rest at last. You should talk to Gorwen.You have triumphed over the Grey Fear and laid Luilloth's remains to rest in Sarch Vorn. You should now return to Rhiangar.

Rhiangar is at the inn in Herne.Talk to Rhiangar'I still cannot believe you found so much evidence of Luilloth's final hours! You even worked with someone who had spoken directly to her! This is astonishing! Everything you have accomplished in such a short time is more than I have been able to do in my many years of research!

'We are so close! We now know where Luilloth went to fight the Grey Fear. I suspect this creature was not defeated as was thought! They may even be in Sarch Vorn now!

'I hesitate to ask you to go but... no, you have proven yourself over and again. I believe you can follow in the footsteps of Luilloth, and in so doing, perhaps we can put the dead to rest, as she once did. This time for good!

'I do not know the way to Sarch Vorn, but I know one who might. I have been speaking with the Ranger, Gorwen. Ask her if she knows the path to that unholy place.''You have reason to believe the Grey Fear truly dwells within Sarch Vorn? It is worse than I feared!

'I have not given thought to that terrible place since I was a young girl. It was spoken of as almost a myth among my kin—a tale to steel the hearts of children should the Dead ever again crawl from the barrows of Tyrn Gorthad.

'I know the way to Sarch Vorn, ${PLAYER}, but it is treacherous. It is to the west of here, built into a hill of black stone. If you spy an old wooden bridge crossing a chasm before a waterfall, you have found the way.

'If you seek to defeat the Grey Fear, meet me there, my friend.''In all my years, I have never dared to stray any closer to Sarch Vorn.

'But if this is where the Grey Fear dwells... it is where we must go! There is no other choice, lest we allow this evil to spread through all of Cardolan—perhaps even all of Eriador.

'If this is truly the work of Angmar, then my vengeance is twice-earned. Cardolan has suffered enough loss, and we cannot allow the Grey Fear to bring it to ruin once more! If Luilloth's quest led her to these ancient grounds, perhaps we can find some sign of her passing in Sarch Vorn or some tool to aid us to overcome the Grey Fear. Even the most insignificant thing may prove a great boon!

'Let us go, ${PLAYER}. If we wait any longer, I fear my courage will fail me!''You return! Glad am I to see you, my friend!'

You tell her of your deeds.

'The tale you bring chills me to my very bones. I knew things were dire, but never did I imagine such a creature, with so much history. You not only saved my homeland, you also found justice for Luilloth and her family.

'May this unholy cycle of grief be ended!''Alas for Luilloth!

'I cannot say for certain, but I think she collapsed these tunnels herself... almost as if to trap the Grey Fear within Sarch Vorn. And poor Astoryn! So loyal was he that even then he did not abandon Luilloth.

'From her wounds, I suspect that she was killed when the tunnels collapsed. That is my hope at least, for it would be a preferable fate to any other she might have found in Sarch Vorn. Better that than to be tormented by the Grey Fear until she died of starvation or met some other sorrowful end.

'But her remains do not appear to have decayed as any others would, and that knowledge brings many unpleasant thoughts to my mind. Either Sarch Vorn was so well-sealed when its tunnels collapsed that she was preserved or the Grey Fear took hold of her remains in the same manner as those of Prince Amondir.

'So many were the horrors visited upon her family, it can scarcely be believed. We must put an end to the Grey Fear, my friend, once and for all!''Alas for Luilloth!

'I cannot say for certain, but I think she collapsed these tunnels herself... almost as if to trap the Grey Fear within Sarch Vorn. And poor Astoryn! So loyal was he that even then he did not abandon Luilloth.

'From her wounds, I suspect that she was killed when the tunnels collapsed. That is my hope at least, for it would be a preferable fate to any other she might have found in Sarch Vorn. Better that than to be tormented by the Grey Fear until she died of starvation or met some other sorrowful end.

'But her remains do not appear to have decayed as any others would, and that knowledge brings many unpleasant thoughts to my mind. Either Sarch Vorn was so well-sealed when its tunnels collapsed that she was preserved or the Grey Fear took hold of her remains in the same manner as those of Prince Amondir.

'So many were the horrors visited upon her family, it can scarcely be believed. We must put an end to the Grey Fear, my friend, once and for all!''Is the Grey Fear truly gone? Is it banished from Cardolan evermore?

'If the stories of old speak true, the Grey Fear may yet return with the passing of the years. Perhaps all we can do is remain vigilant and pray that it never returns—neither in the guise of Prince Amondir nor any other.

'Nay, I must believe that it is gone, ${PLAYER}. We have won this victory for Cardolan... for Meneldir...'

As Gorwen speaks, a sound echoes through the halls of Sarch Vorn.

'Did you hear that, my friend? It sounds like it came from the chamber where we discovered Luilloth. I don't know about you, but I will not leave this place while the Dead still roam its halls.

'Let us find out what made that sound!''Is the Grey Fear truly gone? Is it banished from Cardolan evermore?

'If the stories of old speak true, the Grey Fear may yet return with the passing of the years. Perhaps all we can do is remain vigilant and pray that it never returns—neither in the guise of Prince Amondir nor any other.

'Nay, I must believe that it is gone, ${PLAYER}. We have won this victory for Cardolan... for Meneldir...'

As Gorwen speaks, a sound echoes through the halls of Sarch Vorn.

'Did you hear that, my friend? It sounds like it came from the chamber where we discovered Luilloth. I don't know about you, but I will not leave this place while the Dead still roam its halls.

'Let us find out what made that sound!''What a joy it is to see you well, dear friend.

'You have triumphed over the Grey Fear, have you not? If you had not, I do not believe I would have found my way into this old barrow unhindered.

'Indeed, the shadow upon this place has lifted, but one matter remains unfinished.''It is over, my friend. May Meneldir and so many others at last find peace!

'Is that the river-daughter of whom you spoke? Even in so dark a place, I feel a lightness upon my heart in her presence.

'But, truth be told, I do not wish to remain here any longer than we must. The Grey Fear is no more, but that does not erase the hurts he has brought upon us or any of the peoples of Cardolan.

'I trust Rhiangar will wish to hear of this? I will escort you to Herne, but I am afraid our paths must diverge after we arrive. I am a Dúnadan, after all, and my duty is to my kin. Far too many troubles have come to Cardolan of late, and it shall be our task to set them right.

'Nevertheless, I cannot thank you enough for what you have done for us... and for Meneldir.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will exit Sarch Vorn, the Black Grave and swiftly travel to Herne when you advance this quest.</rgb>'It is over, my friend. May Meneldir and so many others at last find peace!

'Is that the river-daughter of whom you spoke? Even in so dark a place, I feel a lightness upon my heart in her presence.

'But, truth be told, I do not wish to remain here any longer than we must. The Grey Fear is no more, but that does not erase the hurts he has brought upon us or any of the peoples of Cardolan.

'I trust Rhiangar will wish to hear of this? I will escort you to Herne, but I am afraid our paths must diverge after we arrive. I am a Dúnadan, after all, and my duty is to my kin. Far too many troubles have come to Cardolan of late, and it shall be our task to set them right.

'Nevertheless, I cannot thank you enough for what you have done for us... and for Meneldir.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will exit Sarch Vorn, the Black Grave and swiftly travel to Herne when you advance this quest.</rgb>The Rangers of the North in Evendim have long sought for the wreck of the Ectheldur, the ancient banner-ship of Annúminas, but their efforts have not gone unnoticed by a band of tomb-robbers from the South.Tomb-robbers can be found at Rantost, on the northern islands of Lake Evendim.

Tarneth has asked you to defeat the tomb-robbers at Rantost and see why they have begun to search the old ruins.Defeat tomb-robbers at Rantost (${NUMBER}/8)Tomb-robbers can be found at Rantost, on the northern islands of Lake Evendim.

Tarneth has asked you to defeat the tomb-robbers at Rantost and see why they have begun to search the old ruins.Defeat tomb-robbers at Rantost (8/8)Tarneth can be found in Tinnudir, on the shores of Lake Evendim.You should examine Naegir's map and see if you notice anything else that Tarneth missed.Examine Naegir's mapTarneth can be found in Tinnudir, on the shores of Lake Evendim.Tarneth has asked you to survey Lake Evendim from several locations using her spy-glass.

You should travel to Tinnudir, the Eavespires, Men Erain, and the isle of Tyl Ruinen and survey Lake Evendim. You can survey Lake Evendim near each of the dock-masters at these locations.Tarneth can be found in Tinnudir, on the shores of Lake Evendim.Tarneth can be found in Tinnudir, on the shores of Lake Evendim.Talk to Tarneth and search for the wreck of the EctheldurTarneth can be found in Tinnudir, on the shores of Lake Evendim.Talk to Tarneth in Tinnudir'From what we have learned, the tomb-robbers have gathered at Rantost. If the wreck of the Ectheldur were there, we would have found it long ago, so they must have some other purpose in the ruins.

'It is no simple journey to reach Rantost, but you are a practised Mariner, are you not? Lake Evendim is vast, indeed, but it is a mere drop in the Great Sea.

'Drive out the tomb-robbers at Rantost and see if any of them are carrying anything of note. It seems they must again be reminded that they have no claim to the ruins of Arnor!''Come, my friend! We must reach the Ectheldur before the tomb-robbers figure it out for themselves!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest, and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I can hardly believe that we found the Ectheldur, let alone recovered Alwedion's forestaff, but we must keep secret what we have learned this day, my friend. The Blue Lady has long been a friend to the Dúnedain, and we must repay her kindness in turn. I only hope that one day Gwindeth will deem it safe enough to lower the waters of Lake Evendim and that Annúminas might one day have its beauty restored.

'You have done a great service to the Dúnedain, ${PLAYER}, and your deeds will not soon be forgotten. A Mariner of your skill and expertise will be a great ally in the days to come, my friend. I expect we shall call upon you again soon!''What is this? A map? Some sort of plan?

'From what I can tell, it looks like there was some great deluge long ago, and that the Lake has never receded. But how can that be? Why don't you take a closer look at it?''I am not certain what to make of this, my friend. I would ask that you travel around the edges of Lake Evendim to survey its waters and make note of what you find. There is something unusual about this tide!

'I think you shall need to survey Annúminas from a few locations to make sense of these markings. You should begin your survey here in Tinndur, and then continue your work in the Eavespires, Men Erain, and on the isle of Tyl Ruinen. That should be enough! Here, you may borrow my spy-glass to aid in your survey, but do take care of it. It is quite valuable to me!

'You are no stranger to such work, are you? Very good. I shall await your return, ${PLAYER}!''Did you notice anything interesting in the course of your survey, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Tarneth that based on your survey, some of the markings on Naegir's map appear to correspond to islands on Lake Evendim. However, the locations provided appear to denote islands and other land that has since been overtaken by the waters of Lake Evendim. Tarneth takes the map, and studies it once again.

'Wait a moment, if that marking is correct, I think we may have found the wreck of the Ectheldur! There is no time to explain, ${PLAYER}. There is an island near one of these markings, and I suspect we will find what we seek there!'Handarod told you that the Stone-troll chieftain known as Gurmagath has been boldly raiding nearby Elvish settlements in order to amass enough wealth and food to sate his enormous greed and appetite. The Elves have asked for the aid of the Rangers of Esteldín in stopping his thievery.The Stone-troll chieftain Gurmagath is in the forests south-east of Dol Nendír.

Handarod has asked you to find Gurmagath and defeat him. He suggested that you take allies with you.Defeat GurmagathHandarod is in Esteldín, north of Dol Nendir.

You should return to Handarod and tell him of Gurmagath's defeat.Talk to Handarod'It is well known that Stone-trolls are a greedy, selfish breed, but it seems that a troll of particularly loathsome habits has settled camp not far to the south and has begun raiding the remaining settlements of the Elves of Lin Giliath.

'They have asked us for aid, and so I am looking for a group of warriors to go root out this particular villain and ensure that he harrasses the Elves no longer. This Stone-troll is known by the name Gurmagath and is the chieftain of a band encamped in the forests and hills somewhere south-east of the ruins of Dol Nendír.

'Gather your allies and see that he is dispatched or driven off. Without him, the Stone-trolls will be dismayed.''So, Gurmagath the stone-troll has been dispatched? Good, good. Hopefully his ilk will never hinder the Elves again.'Legend tells of a powerful Warg once offered the ability to rule all Wargs in return for service to the Iron Crown. A collar fashioned of iron was made and powerful sorceries used to enchant the item. The great beast was slain and the collar removed, but tales of the beast have been passed down through the ages amongst the Men of the North Downs.Will Widdup is at the treasure hunters' camp outside of Fornost City, in the northern North Downs.

You should speak with Will Widdup.You recall the legend of the Warg-king Krithmog. The treasure hunters' camp, occupied by folk who harbour an interest in Fornost, would be a good place to bring the iron collar you discovered.'Ah, this collar is a fell thing. But it is good that you have found it!

'This is Krithmog's collar, lost for half an age. I gather it has been passed from Warg to Warg, and you found it by chance. It was once used to bind Krithmog to the service of Angmar. Perhaps it will once again bind him, but this time to his doom!'Descendants of Beorn have not traditionally left the skin-changer's home-land, but your journey has brought you to Eriador in defence of the Free Peoples. You should now go to meet your sister Langhár at Ost Guruth, in the Lone-lands.Your journey continues in Eriador.Reach Level 30 as a BeorningBrom Hawksbeard likes to portray himself as a tough Brawler with few cares, but the more you work with him the more you realize his gruff exterior is mostly an act.Derwent Bindweed is outside the town of Stoneheight, north-east of Amon Raith in the North Downs.Talk to Derwent Bindweed outside the town of StoneheightDerwent Bindweed is outside the town of Stoneheight, at Annúndir in the North Downs.Talk to Derwent Bindweed and tour the town of StoneheightDerwent Bindweed is in the town of Stoneheight in the North Downs.Talk to Derwent Bindweed in StoneheightBrom Hawksbeard is in the Bree Jail.Talk to Brom Hawksbeard in the Bree Jail'I received word that Derwent Bindweed has run into another spot of trouble, ${PLAYER}. Now, I don't much care, having my own concerns, but as an up-and-coming Brawler you should really do what you can to improve your own reputation among the people of Bree-land.

'It seems Derwent set out looking for a new home for his family after you stood up to Bill Ferny's ruffians, but the town in which he stopped is perhaps even more dangerous than the one he left! Why don't you go check on him and see if you can help? I fear he might have bit off more than he can chew....'

Brom coughs suddenly.

'Fear? Did I say fear? Bah! Brom Hawksbeard fears nothing! I just meant Derwent might be afraid, and could use a strong Brawler to help him get settled! Seek him out at the town of Stoneheight, ${PLAYER}, in the North Downs. I understand you can follow a sloping path north-east of Amon Raith, along the curve of the mountains, right to the town. Once again, making good use of your brawling abilities is the only way to improve them! Surely you want to one day equal my own skill, do you not?''Seek out Derwent Bindweed at the town of Stoneheight, ${PLAYER}, in the North Downs. Stoneheight is atop a sloping path north-east of Amon Raith. Once again, making good use of your brawling abilities is the only way to improve them! Surely you want to one day equal my own skill, do you not?''Brom Hawksbeard sent you to me? That was very kind of him, ${PLAYER}, but he's wrong: I am not in any trouble at all, and I am quite enjoying my new home here in Stoneheight!

'And if my neighbours have any needs or concerns at all, I am more than prepared to do my part to set things a-right here; I pulled my weight at Thornley's Work Site for weeks. Were I still there it would no doubt be finished by now! But Bill Ferny and his ruffians saw to that, didn't they?

'So there's no trouble hereabout, but if you are interested I could give you a tour of the town. It's the least I can do, my brawling friend!''Right this way, my friend! While we walk, you can tell me why Brom thinks Stoneheight is a place of danger... it seems peaceable to me!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest, and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'That's two I owe you, my friend! Either trouble follows a Brawler close behind, or you have a knack for finding it! Whichever might be the truth, I am glad you were here! But what will I do if Bill Ferny learns that his Orc-friends were unsuccessful? I cannot keep cowering whenever a fight breaks out....

'Perhaps I will take up the fighting style you demonstrated, ${PLAYER}, and seek to train the folk of Stoneheight in the brawling arts. It could be that Rose and Ned will take to it, and become natural Brawlers in their own right! I begin to wonder if Brom Hawksbeard was right about this town, and we may need skills such as yours to defend ourselves!

'Thank you again, ${PLAYER}, and convey my gratitude to Brom as well! We are lucky you were here! When next I see you, perhaps we will be a brawling pair that fears neither Orc-swords nor the underhanded schemes of villains such as Bill Ferny!''Thank you again, ${PLAYER}, and convey my gratitude to Brom as well! We are lucky you were here!''Derwent and his family are faring well in Stoneheight? Very good. I was not really concerned, but if you were going up there anyway....'

You tell Brom that you weren't actually going to Stoneheight already, and you only journeyed there on his recommendation.

'Yes, well, you must have been worried about him all the same. At any rate, I have a reward for you. May it help you in whatever brawls lie ahead, whether they be in service of the helpless... or just for fun.'Different stances have different advantages. Master them and you will never be unprepared.Use your Posture skillsThere is a great road that leads through the wastes of Angmar to the very gates of Carn Dûm. It is along this road that the dark armies march forth to bring the Free Peoples of Eriador under the yoke of the Enemy.Explore the road leading through the Ram Dúath into Angmar -- to the very gates of Carn Dûm itself.The armed camp of Skathmúr is responsible for patrolling the canyons of Ram Dúath, ensuring that it is clear and the road well-maintained so that Angmar's armies may move undisturbed through its length.The fortress of Bail Róva commands the exit from Ram Dúath as it spills into Angmar. For the armies of Angmar, this is the last major fortress they will pass through before embarking on their campaigns to the south.Bail Boglakh is a major staging camp for the armies of Angmar as they gather from the surrounding lands and move into the south.

Even the inhabitants of this vast encampment avoid the valley to the north, however, and those who question the commands of their superiors are hurled over the cliffs there into the unsettling silence and fog below where their screams are soon cut short by dreadful silence.This watch-tower overlooks the great sulfur lake of Duvuinen to the south. From here, scouts and commanders can survey the watch-fires of the gathering armies and observe their movements as they draw up their plans.This small camp acts as a checkpoint through which travellers and messengers to Carn Dûm must pass before approaching the vast gates of the city.The Gate of Shadows is the vast outer gate of the fortress city of Carn Dûm.

Stories abound of the horrors within, but here far from the war the small town settled around the gate might seem almost commonplace were it not for the symbols of Angmar's power upon every street and tower.

The harsh countenance upon the faces of the guards suggests that they brook no hint of rebellion or expression from the citizenry that runs contrary to the wishes of the False King.Knowledge of the natural world has given you subtle influence over its natural fluctuations, but there is deeper knowledge still to be gleaned through study.Strike with Gust of WindKnowledge of the natural world has given you subtle influence over its natural fluctuations, but there is deeper knowledge still to be gleaned through study.Strike with Gust of WindSomehow the very essence of the Red Swamp has been corrupted. Radagast the Brown, a Wizard who has come to Agamaur, seeks to learn the cause.There are many corrupted stumps in the north-west of Agamaur.

Radagast has asked you to gather bark from the corrupted tree-stumps in Agamaur, so that he may continue his study into Ivar's means of causing the corruption. He warns you that Agamaur should not be traversed alone. You should gather allies.Collect bark strips from corrupted stumps (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Radagast is in the Eglan-camp in the southern parts of Agamaur.

The task was not simple, for enemies were abundant in the swamp, but you managed to gather nine strips of bark for Radagast. You should return to the Wizard with your bounty.'Your efforts here in Agamaur are commendable, my good ${RACE}, but perhaps not enough. The very swamp itself has been corrupted. By corrupted, I do not mean decayed, for that is simply the natural progression of life in such places, but rather evil has seeped into the very essence of Agamaur.

'If you would, return to the swamp and gather the bark from the tree-stumps. I must collect something of the living things that inhabit Agamaur if I am to discover how Ivar corrupted this place. There are many bare stumps north-west of here.

'I should warn you: because of the corruption here, you should not traverse Agamaur alone.''Have you done as I asked? Must I point you to each and every stump? There are many corrupted stumps in the north-west of Agamaur.''${PLAYER}, you have done well yet again. Your name will be written down in the annals of the lore-masters before your days are done. My thanks to you.

'I will need to study these strips, but they will help greatly in understanding the corruption.'Handarod is concerned with the threatened safety of Esteldín.Ost Galumar lies to the north-west of Esteldín, in the North Downs.

Handarod has asked you to scout the ruins of Ost Galumar.Scout Ost GalumarOst Crithlanc lies to the north-east of Esteldín, in the North Downs.

Handarod has asked you to scout the Angmarim-outpost of Ost Crithlanc.Scout Ost CrithlancKar Lak lies in the hills well to the east of Esteldín, in the North Downs.

Handarod has asked you to scout the Earth-kin village of Kar Lak.Scout Kar LakDol Nendir lies to the south-west of Esteldín, in the North Downs.

Handarod has asked you to scout the ruins of Dol Nendír.Scout Dol NendírHandarod is at Esteldín in the North Downs.

You should report to Handarod and tell him what you have seen during your patrol around Esteldín.Talk to Handarod'Greetings, ${PLAYER}. I fear that with all that has been happening in recent months we have been remiss in our own defence... just recently a small group of Orc-scouts were slain by our Rangers on the very doorstep of Esteldín. I need not tell you that it is only the secrecy of Esteldín that protects it from the full brunt of Angmar's assault. We cannot take any further risks!

'I need you to patrol the lands immediately surrounding Esteldín and report back to me with any movements the Enemy makes.

'I would stay clear of the large Orc-camp at Dol Dínen if I were you -- we have already lost too many scouts there, and I cannot afford to lose any more.''So, we continue to be surrounded by enemies -- though the enemies that most concern me are those who lie to the east of us, as the Orcs continue to stream down out of the Ram Dúath.

'In time, they will find us -- I only hope we are ready by then.'Rune-keepers are masters of the written word, and the words of healing captured in runic form are especially powerful and useful in a dangerous world.The Rune-keeper Huldar is at Othrikar, in the North Downs.

You have been told that the dwarf Huldar may be able to teach you about beneficial runes of healing.Huldar is at Othrikar, in the North Downs.

