 the Jailhouse will provide a convenient place to lockup those that remain once the city is back under the control of the Free Peoples.Claim the JailhouseWithout control of the courtyard, you cannot hope to assault and claim Town Hall.Claim the courtyardAll that remains is to claim Town Hall and see the banner of Bree-town fly high over it.Claim the Town Hall'There is more to do. The world is besieged by evil, but for tonight, we may celebrate a great victory!'Heithur Ironfist, in the company and relative safety of Barachen's Camp, awaits your return with broken longswords.You have accepted a task to collect broken longswords from the Hill-men and trolls in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. You are to deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist, a dwarf taking refuge at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Broken longswords from the Hill-men and trolls in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. Deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Broken longswords from the Hill-men and trolls in the western stretches of the Trollshaws.'Ha! Look at these, serves those beasts right. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Heithur Ironfist, in the company and relative safety of Barachen's Camp, awaits your return with extremely sharp claws.You have accepted a task to collect extremely sharp claws from the cave-claws in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. You are to deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist, a dwarf taking refuge at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Extremely sharp claws from the cave-claws in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. Deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Extremely sharp claws from the cave-claws in the western stretches of the Trollshaws.'They are not much, but I am certain that I can work with them. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Handarod is concerned with the growing threat of the Dourhands in western Nan Amlug.Dourhands can be found throughout the hills of western Nan Amlug, north-west of Esteldín.

Handarod has asked you to disrupt the Dourhand mining efforts in Nan Amlug.Defeat Dourhands in Nan Amlug (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Handarod is at Esteldín in the North Downs, south-east of Nan Amlug.

You should return to Handarod and tell him of your battles against the Dourhands.Talk to Handarod'The Longbeards of Othrikar had mentioned to us recently that their wayward kin, the Dourhands, have been attempting to open mines here in the hills of Nan Amlug. Now my scouts tell me that, despite our efforts, their work is nearly complete, and they are preparing to send shipments of iron north to their allies in Angmar.

'I need you to make a foray into Nan Amlug to attack the Dourhands in and around their mines, and do as much as possible to disrupt their operations there. The last thing we want is for Angmar to gain yet another supply of metals for their armies.''My scouts tell me that the Dourhand operations are in some measure of disarray thanks to your efforts. Keep up the good work -- we need to disrupt the flow of war materials into Angmar as much as possible if we are to have any hope of preventing their Orcs from sweeping over us in the months to come.'Some champions have been known to hurl themselves into battle with such vigour that they injure themselves more grievously than the enemy does.Use Dire NeedSome champions have been known to hurl themselves into battle with such vigour that they injure themselves more grievously than the enemy does.Use Dire Need, Great Cleave, or Controlled BurnAgamaur is a place full of foul corruption and grave danger, home to a terrible evil.Agamaur is east and north of Ost Guruth. It lies beyond the Red Pass in the Haragmar swamp.

Frideric has bid you journey to Agamaur to speak with Eriac the Strong.'There is a place you should know of, a place full of great corruption and grave danger. It is called Agamaur, the Red Swamp. The water of this place is red as blood, blood which leaks from a wound called Garth Agarwen.

'Yet there are those who have dared the heart of Agamaur. Perhaps you will be able to aid them. Some of my kindred, the Eglain, have encamped in the midst of the darkness, and I would bid you go to their aid. Speak with Eriac the Strong, and I am sure you will find he can make use of your strength.

'Agamaur is east of here, and north. Find the Red Pass in the Haragmar swamp, and you shall find your way to Agamaur.''You come from Frideric? Then you are welcome. There are many threats here against which you will be able to test your strength.'Few things are quite as terrifying as the sight of a Beorning bounding into battle.Use RushTomb-robbers have long sifted through the ruined paths of Annúminas and the tombs of the fallen Kings of old. The Rangers of the North ally with any who would take up arms to keep these thieves and scoundrels at bay.Defeat tomb-robbers in EvendimThose who would defile the tombs of the ancient Dúnedain grow ever bolder as chaos and the fear of war grip the lands of the North. To take up arms against such unprincipled villains is as noble a cause as any.Defeat tomb-robbers in EvendimThe kings of Annúminas were intered within tombs along the road to the gate of the city.

Nine were the kings that ruled over Arnor from this ancient seat of power, but not all of them were buried in the Way of Kings...NOT USEDThe tomb of Eärendur, son of Elendur.

Eärendur was the tenth and last of the Kings of Arnor - after his death the kindgom of Arnor was divided by his contending sons into the kingdoms of Arthedain, Cardolan and Rhudaur. It was during this period that the capital was moved to Fornost, and the old city of Annúminas was finally abandoned after declining in importance for many years.The tomb of Elendur, son of Valandur. As the ninth king of Arnor Elendur had one of the longest and most enduring reigns of all, ruling over the people of Arnor for one hundred and twenty five years.The tomb of Valandur, son of Tarondor. History mentions relatively little about the eighth king of Arnor, except that he is said to have died in violent and mysterious circumstances sometime around the 50th year of his reign - a foreboding sign perhaps, of the strife that was to fall upon the Northern Kingdoms in the years to come.The tomb of Elendil, son of Amandil. Elendil was the first and founding King of Arnor, whither he had fled into Middle-earth from the great ruin of Númenor. Elendil fought alongside Gil-galad as the leader of the Last Alliance and died during the Siege of Barad-dûr at the hands of Sauron.

 After his death Elendil was secretly interred at Halifirien far to the south, but some of his most important standards and personal effects were symbolically placed within the tomb at Annúminas in his stead..The tomb of Tarondor, son of Tarcil. The reign of the seventh king of Arnor was marked by a time of comparative peace, for the strife that would consume and in time destroy the Kingdoms of the North had not yet arisen.The tomb of Eldacar, son of Valandil. Eldacar ruled as the fourth king of Arnor, and was the direct grandson of Isildur himself. It is believed that Eldacar ruled during a time of peace that extended from the defeat of Sauron in the War of the Last Alliance at his grandfather's hand, but most of the records of this time in Arnor have been destroyed.The tomb of Tarcil, son of Arantar. Tarcil was the sixth king of Arnor and his reign lasted for about eighty years before the crown passed to his son, Tarondor.The tomb of Arantar, son of Eldacar. Though the reign of the fifth King of Arnor was said to last for nearly a century, most of the records of this time were lost in the eventual fall of the Northern Kingdoms, and little is now known of his rule.Sometimes taking that extra second to concentrate and focus on your target makes all the difference.Use Intent ConcentrationThe western pass of Ram Dúath has long been home to worms, least of dragon-kind. Dwelling in the crags and caves of the mountain pass, they prey upon any who dare that path.Worms can be found in the western pass of Ram Dúath, north of Esteldín.

Melthindir has asked you to clear the worms from the western pass of Ram Dúath, making it safe to bring a force through the pass into Angmar.Defeat cold-worms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Melthindir awaits your return at Esteldín.

You made your way into the valley and slew the worms you found there. You should return to Melthindir with news of your victory.Return to Melthindir'You are the one who has been aiding my kin against the army of Angmar... ${PLAYER}, is it not? We have need of your services again.

'The passage through Ram Dúath north-east of here is blocked by the Hillmen of Angmar; however, not all paths are closed to us. To the north of Esteldín lies another pass, though it also is fraught with danger. You saw for yourself that worms hide amid the crags and crevices, preying on unwary travellers.

'We need you to clear the pass of these worms, so that we may safely take a force through. Be careful.''You have proven capable, my friend. Though there are other dangers in Ram Dúath which may yet prove too great, even for you.'Dannasen, released from Neven's control, seeks not only to fulfill his curse, but also to exact revenge upon the Hillmen of Rhudaur who serve the enigmatic Red-maid of Garth Agarwen.The Hillmen of Rhudaur can be found within Agamaur, the Red Swamp.

Dannasen, sent you to bring vengeance upon the Hillmen of Rhudaur, who serve the Red-maid. He asked you to deliver vengeance upon them for the pain and suffering that their ancestors caused upon the land and the same which they do even now.Defeat Rhudauran Hillmen loyal to the Red-maid (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dannasen is at the Eglan-camp in Agamaur.

Unwilling to yield, the Rhudauran Hillmen fought you on sight. Dannasen's request completed, the time has come to return to him.Talk to Dannasen'Long have we awaited our chance to strike down the Men who caused the destruction of this land. Now, the time draws close where we will fulfill our duty and end this curse. 

'We shall not end the curse and nothing else. We will seek out vengeance. We will deliver unto the descendants of our Enemy who still feed hatred and sacrifice innocents to this land.

'Tarry not, deliver this vengeance. The Hillmen are gathered again to the east and north. Strike them down and show them that our memories are not gone, and we do not forgive their action.''Ever we draw closer to absolution. This vengeance complete, another shall open. One against the foes who shackle the Red-maid in bonds of innocent blood. We shall see them ended as well, you and I.''The pain wrought upon the land and her people was immeasurable, and we can only hope that our efforts to purify the maid will succeed. Else we shall ever linger under this curse. 

'Even now, the descendants of the first Men to poison the maid continue this practice. We must stop these atrocities. Seek out these Men and destroy them.'The lands of Evendim -- once the capital of the North Kingdom of Arnor -- have long lay uninhabited by Men, besides small bands of scavengers and robbers who pick through the ancient ruins and tombs that lie along the edge of the great lake.

As the forces of the North are aroused for war, those who gain control over this ancient seat of power may gain a critical advantage in the conflict to come.Complete Quests in Evendim.Complete quests in EvendimThough there is no industry, agriculture, or indeed any great strategic bastion to be found in Evendim, it could nonetheless become a great symbol among the Men of the North were the great capital of Annúminas ever to rise again under the banner of the ancient kings.Complete Pilgrim of Evendim.Complete Evendim QuestsComplete quests in EvendimTinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of shimmering essence.You have been tasked with collecting shimmering essence from the Dead surrounding Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Shimmering essence from the Dead in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Shimmering essence from the Dead in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'The Dead will not rise again to threaten any in this world.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Rumours have reached the ears of the Rangers at Esteldín that a chieftain has arisen within the ranks of the Wargs at Dol Dínen, uniting the packs.Raugzok, chief of the Wargs, can be found at Dol Dínen in the south-eastern North Downs.

You should defeat Raugzok.Defeat RaugzokRaugzok, chief of the Wargs, can be found at Dol Dínen in the south-eastern North Downs.

You should defeat Raugzok.Defeat RaugzokYou have defeated Raugzok, chief among the Wargs of Dol Dínen. Your efforts have done much to preserve the North Downs from the ravages of war.CompletedYou become aware of a presence; a chieftain has risen up among the Wargs of Dol Dínen, uniting the packs that run with the Orcs gathered there. Even a tenuous unity among such foul beasts is a danger to all.

You should slay the chieftain of the Wargs and shatter the courage of these beasts. The vile creatures linger near the Orcs, protecting their masters, and as such you will need the strength of allies to fight along with you.You have defeated Raugzok, chief among the Wargs of Dol Dínen. Your efforts have done much to preserve the North Downs from the ravages of war.Ongbúrz Bone-speakers can be found in the deepest reaches of Dol Dínen, beyond the siege-engines, south-east of Esteldín.Ongbúrz Bone-speakers can be found in the deepest reaches of Dol Dínen, beyond the siege-engines, south-east of Esteldín.

These sorcerers among the Orcs of Dol Dínen must be defeated.You have defeated a number of Ongbúrz Bone-speakers in Dol Dínen.CompletedOngbúrz Bone-speakers can be found in the deepest reaches of Dol Dínen, beyond the siege-engines, south-east of Esteldín.

These sorcerers among the Orcs of Dol Dínen must be defeated.You have defeated a number of Ongbúrz Bone-speakers in Dol Dínen.Once the capital of the North, Evendim has nonetheless always had its wild places and mysteries -- and those have grown stranger and more dangerous still in the long years since Annúminas was abandoned.

Far across the waters of lake Evendim lie lands long unexplored by Men.NOT USEDNan Orngon is a great stone outcropping discovered by the original settlers of Evendim long ago. While its position overlooking the paths into the Fields of Fornost might have afforded it some importance its steep sides proved impossible to build upon even for the skilled dwarf-masons of the time and it was eventually abandoned to the wilds.The valley of Tûm Fuin and its surroundings are known to be a favourite haunt of local trappers and hunters  out of Ost Forod. It lies well off the beaten path and has an abundance of local wildlife - but there is also little recourse or chance of rescue for any hunter unfortunate enough to be injured while travelling so far afield.The Even-rills are a series of connected pools and falls fed by ice melting high in the mountains north of Lake Evendim. They are as frigid as ice even on the warmest days of summer, and bands of rugged North-men have been known to challenge each other to foolhardy contests of endurance to see how long each can withstand the bone-chilling temperatures in the upper pools.Few hunters or trappers come this far west of Ost Forod - tales speak of trees that walk and bears that build houses in these remote wilds. While there is little truth to most of these fanciful tales, there is certainly an aura of strange enchantment about the secluded grove called the Eavespires....The Eave-mere represents the lower stretch of the largest tributary to lake Evendim. Fed by rapids from higher up the mountain and crashing down in great falls towards the lake below, the mere itself is a calm and placid place that was said to be much admired by travellers seeking quiet solace in the old days of Annúminas before the city was abandoned and many of the further shores of Evendim were reclaimed by the wilds.This cold and isolated stream cuts deeply through the rock of the mountains high above the western side of Lake Evendim before descending through tumultuous rapids to the Eave-mere below and thence to Lake Evendim itself.

Legends speak of a beautiful maiden who - distraught over the abandonment of her ancestral home and the encroachment of the wilds upon the once glorious city of Annúminas - came here and threw herself into the waters from the high walls of the chasm above.

Alas, even the old stories do not guess at the truth of this tale, or whom the woman might have been.The Twisted Grove is a dark and forboding place, set in the deepest wilds west of Lake Evendim. Even during the reign of the Kings of Arnor, it was considered an unlucky place and was avoided by most. Now it seems certain that an unwholesome power is at work here, and the very trees seem to radiate a malice towards living things that intrude upon their dark solitude.Though visited infrequently even in the time of Arnor, the pool now known as the Rushingdale was held in reverence by the local people who would leave offerings floating upon the edge of the waters.

Despite the roar of the nearby falls, a sense of uplifting calm does seem to settle upon your spirit as you gaze into the shifting reflections in the water here.You were able to defeat a number of the darkwater, but the corrupted swamp is not lacking in water. There are other threats in the swamp also growing, such as the army of wights Ivar is raising.The gaunt-men protectors who command the wights might be lurking in the ruins in the south-western reaches of Agamaur.

Prove your valour to Eriac. He has set you the task of dealing with the gaunt-men who lead Ivar's wights in the hopes that the curses binding the wights might be broken.Eriac awaits you at the Eglain camp in the southern reaches of Agamaur.

Eriac set you to the task of killing the gaunt-men protectors, a task to which you proved equal. You should return to Eriac with your report.'Prove yourself fit to help rid us of the armies that are being raised here. The strange creature, Ivar, has the power to summon evil spirits and instill them in the remains of the dead, of which this swamp is not lacking. We cannot let this happen. This evil cannot be allowed to grow in power.

'In the south-western reaches of Agamaur there lie many ruins teeming with wights. Lurking in those ruins there must be the gaunt-men who command them! If you kill the gaunt-men protectors, the curses binding the wights might be broken.

'I do not know if this will succeed, but it cannot be a bad thing for creatures such as the gaunt-men to be removed from the Enemy's service.''Are the gaunt-men so powerful that you cannot destroy them? Please, it may be the only way to stop the creatures they lead.''Very well done, ${PLAYER}! I can only hope that it is enough to undo the evil that has been done here. Perhaps stronger measures are needed... do you think you are prepared for a greater challenge?'All of the animals have been taken care of and are ready for the market.Albin Northcotton is at the Farmer's Market on Northcotton Farm.

Albin Northcotton will be pleased to hear that the animals are ready for the market.Talk to Albin Northcotton'The sheep and chickens are back in their pens, the pigs have been mudded, and the cows are fed. I daresay we are ready for the market.

'Please go and let Albin Northcotton know that we are ready.''The animals are ready you say? We may just do well this year.''Go and let him know that we're ready for the market.'With the aid of the shades of Arthedain, the Eglain held fast against Ivar's forces.Elsa is in the Agamaur camp.

Radagast thanked you for your help and asked you to speak with the Eglain woman named Elsa.Speak with Elsa the BoldThe passage to Haragmar lies to the south. Ost Guruth is west of Haragmar.

Elsa gave you a letter to deliver to Frideric requesting supplies for her encampment. She also asked you to deliver a pack to her sister, Hana. Both can be found in Ost Guruth.Frideric abides in the courtyard of Ost Guruth.

Hana was pleased to receive the pack from Elsa, knowing that her sister is watching out for both her and all of the Eglain.'The Eglain and our Arthedain allies were able to stop Ivar's forces... at least for the time being.

'I am needed elsewhere and must bid you farewell for now. Now go, for Elsa wishes to speak to you again.''You should go speak with Elsa.''You have done much to help us keep this foothold in Agamaur, ${PLAYER}. Thank you. If we are to hold this position, we will need supplies.

'Bring this letter to Frideric and this pack to my sister Hana to let her know I still live. She is alone now, and I worry for her.

'To the south is a passage back to Haragmar, the southern reaches of the Red Swamp. From there, go west, and you will find your way back to Ost Guruth. Hurry back and inform my people that all is well... for the moment!''Make your way south to the wall. There you will find a passage that will take you into Haragmar, the southern swamp. From there, Ost Guruth lay to the west. The others may be worried, so make haste.''Oh, I am well. But I believe Elsa had something for you to do, did she not?''My sister is well? I am very pleased to hear this. What is it you bring me?

'Thank you so much for bringing this to me. I fear for my sister, for the swamp is a threatening place, full of terrors, but I know that what she does is for the good of the Eglain.

'Now, I suppose you should take Elsa's letter to Frideric.''Please make certain that Frideric gets my letter. It is vital that we receive  additional supplies.''You should take that letter to the Elder of the Eglain, Frideric.''If Elsa is going to get the supplies she needs, you will need to take her letter to Frideric. Do please hurry and take it to him!''Crossing the swamp is not easy, ${PLAYER}! You have shown remarkable courage! Ah yes, Elsa confirms as much in her letter to me. She speaks quite highly of you.

'I will begin preparing a shipment of supplies for Elsa and Eriac.'All of the chores have been done to get the farm ready for the market.Albin Northcotton is at the market area.

Albin Northcotton will want to know that all the chores are done and the farm is ready.Talk to Albin Northcotton'You have done well helping me out, and I think the farm is ready for the market!

'The tools are put away, the weeds pulled, the scarecrows out and the flowers planted... I cannot think of anything else I would do!

'Please let Albin Northcotton know that the chores are done.''The chores are finally done!''So the farm is in a presentable state? We had been ignoring those chores for weeks. I'm impressed you got them done so quickly.'Ivar left a captain in command of the wights, more powerful than the protectors.Reykur will likely be found at the furthest chambers of the ruins where the wights are encamped.

Eriac has asked you to kill the gaunt-man Reykur, Ivar's captain among the wights. Because of your victory over other wights, Eriac believes Reykur may be waiting for you.Defeat ReykurEriac awaits you at the Eglan-camp in the southern reaches of Agamaur.

You have killed Reykur, throwing the remnant of the wight-army into confusion. Eriac will want to hear of your success.Talk to Eriac at the Eglan-camp in Agamaur'${PLAYER}, there is a terror in this swamp leading the dead foe here. Reykur remains in command of the wight-army, and they are not broken. According to Radagast the Brown, he is one of Ivar's most trusted minions. Slay him, and the wights will be without guidance.

'I have no doubt you will find Reykur skulking in the ruins beyond the wights' camp. Since you have proven able to slay his underlings, he will be more cautious and make you come to him.

'Be wary, but do not delay. This threat must be brought to an end.''You are a legend already among the Eglain. I am impressed with your prowess, ${CLASS}. Please take this as a token of my appreciation.''Reykur must be defeated if the wights are to be weakened.'With the terrible presence of wights, Hill-men of Rhudaur, and the darkwater menace, the foe has become too great to be held back by the small Eglain camp.The Gloomwater are in the eastern reaches of Agamaur.

Eriac has asked you to help keep the Gloomwater, creatures formed from the waters of Agamaur, at bay. As evidence of your efforts, he wants you to bring him the ancient tokens of Rhudaur and Arthedain that are caught up into the Gloomwater as they swirl up from the swamp.'Your presence is welcome, ${PLAYER}. I fear for my people's safety. The old ones you brought to our aid foray against the enemy in their efforts to find the Red-maid they speak of, but they cannot hold back the great tide alone.

'In the eastern reaches of Agamaur, the water itself has risen up, as though it has taken on a life of its own. Creatures of water, which we call Gloomwater, attack us with little warning. How it is possible to fight the waters of the swamp, I do not know, but I would have you try.

'As they swirl up from the depths, they pull with them ancient tokens of Rhudaur and Arthedain. Bring me these tokens as a sign of your efforts.''Well done, ${CLASS}. Thank you for doing this. I can rest a little easier but there is still much work to be done.

'These tokens will also help our people to survive, for they may have value to the Men of Bree. Now, I have more for you to do....'There is much to do while travelling through the lands of Evendim.Complete Explorer of Evendim deedComplete Warden of Evendim deedComplete Slayer of Evendim deedThe pies are freshly baked and ready to be delivered.The tables for the pies are at the Farmer's Market at Northcotton Farm.

The pies are warm and smell delicious. They must be delivered to the tables at the market grounds.Albin Northcotton is at the Farmer's Market on Northcotton Farm.

Albin Northcotton will want to know the pies are ready for the market.Talk to Albin Northcotton'I think these are some of the best pies I have ever made. The crust is fluffy, the filling is thick, and the smell is delicious.

'We best be getting these over to the market grounds quick before the rest of the farm starts coming around and trying to eat them.

'Do me a favour and take them over and set them up on the tables. Then let Albin Northcotton know that the pies are ready. You have been a great help.''One more piece of the puzzle is in place. We will be ready for the farmer's market soon.''Don't tempt me! I could eat all of those pies myself. Please place them on the tables.''I think I hear hobbit-feet scurrying over to sample those pies. Better get a move on!'The tools to blow glass are unique and require much maintenance. A hobbit who lived in Dwaling used to repair them.The satchel of glassblowing tools can be found on the stoop of a hobbit-hole on the north side of Dwaling.

Hob Hillbrow has asked you to collect a satchel of glassblowing tools for him.Collect the satchel of glassblowing toolsHob Hillbrow is at his home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling, south of the Sand-marshes.

You should return to Hob Hillbrow with the satchel of glassblowing tools.'${CLASS}, I beg your pardon, but I haven't any other to turn to in these dark times. Please hear me out. Dwaling has never been a rich town, but we have always gotten by on our own. Many of the best glassblowers in the Shire were found in these holes. My dad before me was a glassblower -- best in the North and Eastfarthings -- and I learned the trade from him.  

'Recently, things turned for the worse for our little town. What with all the wild creatures about, folk are plain afraid of the north Bounds nowadays. Most of the folk have moved away and sold off their property after business stopped.  A hobbit out of the Westfarthing sent letters and contracts up this way purchasing holes and land until he bought up almost the whole town... that's when all the trouble began.

'I used to get my tools for glassblowing repaired by a dear old friend who lived in Dwaling, but he moved away not a week ago. Before he left he swore he would finish fixing them up. If you would, head into Dwaling and see if he left them on his stoop. His hole was on the north side of town, the tools should be in a satchel or bag. I would be deeply in your debt.''Have you gotten the satchel yet? Dwaling is just to the north of here.''You have the tools... very good. I can now get back to working again.'The hobbits living in Dwaling will be eager to hear the Northcotton Farmer's Market is open for business.Hob Hillbrow is at Dwaling, north-east of Oatbarton.

Albin Northcotton asked you to venture to Dwaling and let them know the farmer's market will be opening early this year.Talk to Hob Hillbrow in Dwaling'You have helped us greatly, and for that I am very thankful. I have one last thing to ask of you.

'The market will be opening tomorrow. I was hoping you would head up the road to Dwaling and let the hobbits there know that, thanks to some outstanding help, we are starting the market a little early this year.

'Just follow the road east from Oatbarton, and soon enough you will come to Dwaling. Find Hob Hillbrow and let him know.''I am very happy to hear that the market is starting tomorrow, but I fear we will not be able to make it. There is too much afoot here to leave.''If you do not let them know, we might as well not open yet! Hurry it up!'One of the main features of the Northcotton Farmer's Market is its animal petting area.The shrews and feed bags can be found near Gerard Deephole on Northcotton Farm.

Gerard Deephole asked you to gather the feed bags and drive off the shrews stealing from them.Defeat shrews on the Northcotton Farm (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect feed (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The troughs are at the animal pens on Northcotton Farm.

The feed needs to be placed in the feeding troughs for the cows.Gerard Deephole is at the animal pens on Northcotton Farm.

Gerard Deephole will be pleased to hear the troughs have been filled.Talk to Gerard Deephole'The pigs are the happiest I've seen them in a long time. I think the mud is the thickest I've ever seen. They will be happy in their wallow.

'There's one last animal on the farm we need to take care of: the cows. There's not much we need to do than bring some feed over and fill the troughs. We've had some problem with the feed lately, though: shrews.

'If you could drive off a bunch of the shrews while you're picking up the feed, then fill the troughs, I think the animals will all be ready for the market.''With the troughs filled and the cows fed, the animals are now all ready for the market.''I know the shrews have got to eat too, but without that feed we don't have cows, and no cows means no milk, cheese, butter, cream....''Go ahead and place the feed in the troughs so the cows can eat.'Butter is the final ingredient for the pies. With that, the baking can then begin.The cows are at a farm to the east.Milk the cows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The butter-churn is near the cows on Northcotton Farm.

The next step to making butter is to churn the milk.Rosa Brockhouse is at the southern part of the Northcotton Farm.

Rosa Brockhouse will want to get the butter so she can finish the pies.'The final thing I need is butter. It is the most important of all ingredients. It will bind the flour for the crust, thicken the jam into the filling, and the eggs into the wash.

'Butter is made by first milking the cows. Once you have enough milk, go to the butter-churn and begin churning. It may take some time, but you'll know it's ready once it begins to thicken.

'The cows are at a farm just east of here. The churn is not far from them.''Butter starts as milk, and milk starts in a cow.''Now you need to churn the milk. It might take a while.''The fresher the butter, the better the pies, I always say. Now, let's get these put into the oven.'Bob Greeneaves is familiar with the troubles the Greenfields have seen with goblins and is concerned that those troubles are spreading further into the Northfarthing.The goblin-camp is at the Bleakleaf Crest, south of Dwaling.

Bob Greeneaves has asked you to find the corpse of the spider the goblins dragged off, in hopes that you might learn something important about the goblins.Bob Greeneaves is at Dwaling, north of the Bleakleaf Crest.

You should return to Bob Greeneaves with the information you discovered.Talk to Bob Greeneaves in Dwaling'Although not many know this, I heard about that business down south of here with the goblins. They are up to no good around these parts too! I swear I saw one of them kill one of them spiders and drag it back to their camp, though why they would want something so vile, I surely can't say.

'I wonder if those goblins consider spider a delicacy of some kind, though that thought makes me shudder. Maybe you ought go out to their camp at Bleakleaf Crest, out south of here, and see if you can find that spider they dragged off.

'Mayhaps that spider-corpse might tell us something about these goblins.'A quick examination of this spider's remains reveal that the goblins have let it lie, virtually untouched. All of the ichor and venom of this spider seem to have been collected by the goblins.A quick examination of this spider's remains reveal that the goblins have let it lie, virtually untouched. All of the ichor and venom of this spider seems to have been collected by the goblins.'They took the venom from the spiders? Well that's no good. And you found hobgoblins as well? That's even worse....''The goblins' camp is south of here, in the Bleakleaf Crest. Go find that spider they dragged off. It might give a clue as to what the goblins are up to!'The Limrafn are a lesser, weaker form of fell spirit. What draws them to Evendim in such numbers, not even the Elves can say.Defeat Limrafn in EvendimYou have seen many of these strange lights in Evendim lately -- they seem almost to be multiplying with the presence of Men and other forces. Is it possible that these strange entities are being awakened or attracted by the skirmishes being fought in this land, which was once nearly desolate and empty?Defeat Limrafn in EvendimRonald Dwale's son lost a small toy dog and is very upset.The Barandalf is north and slightly east of Dwaling.

Ronald has asked you to search the banks of the Brandywine in the Sand-marshes to find his son's lost toy. He has told you that one of the burrowing insects may have taken it.Find the small lead dogRonald Dwale is at Hob Hillbrow's home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling, south of Barandalf.

You should return the toy dog to Ronald Dwale.'It must have been the beginning of fall; I was with my son on the banks of the Brandywine, enjoying the sun, a fine meal, and a full pipe, while he sat and played with his favourite toy -- a piece of lead fashioned into a small dog -- by the side of the river.

'My boy was very fond of this toy, but when it came time to leave, it wasn't anywhere to be found. I tried to console him with one of my stories. I created quite a wonderful tale about the toy's whereabouts, but if you are heading that way, perhaps you could search for it?

'The toy has to be somewhere in the Barandalf, the sand marshes north and east of Dwaling.  I have heard that strange burrowing creatures have since inhabited the area. There is a good chance one of them could have swallowed it up, thinking it to be food.''Did you find the toy yet?  The Barandalf is north and east of Dwaling.''Were you able to find the toy, ${PLAYER}? Ah, I see you did. Many thanks to you!'Ronald Dwale is indisposed and will be unable to attend the next meeting of his writing society.The Bird and Baby Inn is in Michel Delving, west across the road from Town Hole.

Ronald Dwale has asked you to speak with his friends at The Bird and Baby Inn and let them know that he will not be able to make their next meeting.Talk to Ronald Dwale's friends at The Bird and Baby InnRonald Dwale is at Hob Hillbrow's home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling.

You should return to Ronald Dwale and inform him that you completed his task.Talk to Ronald Dwale at the Bullroarer's Sward'Well now, you have been helpful haven't you? If you could, I do have one last task for you to assist me with. Among the Hobbits of the Shire, a love of languages and written works is quite uncommon. A few of my fellow colleagues and I have formed a small informal gathering of sorts for those of us who revel in such art as the writing and listening of fictional stories.

'We usually meet in the evenings, down at the Bird -- that is, The Bird and Baby Inn in Michel Delving. With the return of my lost paper, I really should get started on my new book, but I haven't an inkling how I should reach my friends in time to tell them of my absence.

'If you would, go quickly and visit Jack Lewisdown at The Bird and Baby before the meeting starts and let him know that I will not be making it tonight.''You were able to make it before the meeting started? Wonderful! I already have the first chapter started.

'Old Lewisdown thought I would be playing games instead of working? I daresay he knows I would never think of getting involved in something so silly.''Why hullo there.''The Bird and Baby Inn is in Michel Delving, hurry before the meeting starts!'If the Northcotton Farm is to have a market, a lot will need to be cleaned up.Flowers can be found around the Northcotton Farm.

Milo Longfurrow asked you to gather up flowers from around the farm.Collect flowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The planters are at the market-grounds.

The flowers need to be placed in the planters.Milo Longfurrow is at the Northcotton Farm.

Milo Longfurrow will be pleased to hear the flowers have been planted.Talk to Milo Longfurrow'With the scarecrows in place, there is only one last thing I want to do. I wandered by the market area, and while it looks nice, I think it could use some sprucing up.

'I'd like to add some flowers to the area. That will give it a splash of colour, brightness, and cheer. I think that will finish the lot preparations!

'I noticed several flowers around the farm that I think would look very good at the market-grounds. Gather some up and place them in the planters. Be careful, though... dunno what else might come crawling up out of the hole you make when you yank the flower out.''Flowers can be found around the farm.''Go ahead and plant them at the market-grounds.''I think all the chores around the farm are done, and we are ready to welcome visitors!'Ronald Dwale has misplaced an important leaf of paper.Ronald Dwale remembers dropping his paper somewhere in Dwaling, north of the Bullroarer's Sward.

Ronald Dwale has asked you to find his lost leaf of paper.Find the lost leaf of paperRonald Dwale is at Hob Hillbrow's home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling.

You should return the leaf of paper to Ronald Dwale.'If my last task was not too taxing, I am in great need of some assistance from a fellow lover of the written word. I would hope that a ${CLASS} of your reputation would be interested in assisting me.  

'Not long ago, I seemed to have misplaced a very important leaf of paper while I was walking with my notes. This paper contains the beginning lines of a story I planned on writing for my children that has been conjuring in my head for some time now. Now that most of the town has been sold off, I have not been able to search for my paper. The last time I tried to have a look around, I was chased off by a group of those ruffians. 

'I would rewrite it, but I cannot seem to recall the exact wording. If you could find it, I would be deeply in your debt. I think I dropped it somewhere in town.''I know I lost that paper somewhere in Dwaling. It should be just down the hill. Be careful in town with all those ruffians about!''I simply cannot start my book without that page. Have you found it yet?

'Ah, I see you have. Many thanks!'Dannasen, released from Neven's control, seeks not only to fulfil his curse, but also to exact revenge upon the Hillmen of Rhudaur who serve the enigmatic Red-maid of Garth Agarwen.The Hillmen of Rhudaur are encamped in the north-east corner of Agamaur. Dannasen is at the Eglan-camp south-west of the Hillmen.

Dannasen has asked you to bring him the satchels borne by the Hillmen of Rhudaur so that he might find evidence to remind his brethren in the south of the curse that binds them to Middle-earth.'Thou hast freed me and my brethren, ${PLAYER}, and for that we owe thee a debt until we have been released or the world is righted. It was the Angmarim and the Hillmen of Rhudaur who caused the bloodshed which corrupted the maiden. They are to blame for our curse.

'The time has come for retribution. My brethren to the south must be made to understand. They must be brought evidence of the evil. Go to the Rhudauran camp in the north-east of Agamaur and gather for me the satchels which the Hillmen carry.

'With thy help, they shall learn of the corruption... they will remember our curse!''Thou hast done well, ${PLAYER}. With these, I am sure we will discover the evidence I require. I am in thy debt once more.'The Salamanders of Evendim have always been a rare sight. This odd offshoot of the family of dragons has a strong affinity for fire, which it wields with abandon against any enemy that disturbs it.Defeat Salamanders in EvendimOddly, Salamanders are frequently found near bodies of water such as rivers or lakes -- though they may also be found in wet marshes or sandy deserts. Scholars believe this may be because of the creature's tendency to burn away all nearby vegetation, thus rendering an area uninhabitable unless bodies of water or otherwise fire-proof terrain serves to constrain the fires they so frequently set.Defeat Salamanders in EvendimPipe-weed is something hobbits take very seriously. A market would never be visited unless it had a suitable supply and variety.The first bag of pipe-weed is on the table.

You should get some pipe-weed from the first bag.Take a sample from the first bagYou should use the pipe you were given to sample the pipe-weed.The second bag of pipe-weed is on the table.

You should get some pipe-weed from the second bag.Take a sample from the second bagUse the pipe you were given to sample the second pouch of pipe-weed.The third pouch is on the counter.

You should take a sample of pipe-weed from the third pipe-weed pouch.Take a sample from the third bag of pipe-weedUse the pipe to sample the third pipe-weed blend.Blossom Gamgee is at the Pipe-weed House on the Northcotton Farm.

Blossom Gamgee will want to know what you thought of the pipe-weed blends.'Now for the best part. You cannot put a product out without a thorough testing and sampling. You have to make sure that what you're selling is only the best.

'I've put together three blends on the table there. One that is all freshly-picked pipe-weed, one that is all dried pipe-weed, and one that is a half-and-half blend.

'Take this pipe and sample the blends. If you think they are good, then I will agree that they should be sold at the market.''Take a sample from the first bag.''Use the pipe I gave you to sample the first bag of pipe-weed.''Take a pinch from the second bag, it's a half-and-half blend.''This should be a little more bitter than the first, but have more flavour.''The third bag is all dried for a season; it might be too strong for those not used to it.''Don't be ashamed if you cough a little.''So the first was smooth, the second flavourful, and the third you couldn't quite handle? Sounds like we are ready for the market then!'Sand is needed in the art of glassblowing, and the white sand of the Sand Marshes is the best.White sand can be found in the Sand Marshes north of Dwaling.

Mundaric Sandheaver has asked you to gather some white sand for him.Collect white sand (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mundaric Sandheaver is at Hob Hillbrow's home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling, south of the Sand-marshes.

You should return to Mundaric Sandheaver with the white sand.'${CLASS}, I am glad you have come by. We have been trying to slowly rebuild what we can. It has taken much effort but I am afraid it will soon be all for naught.  

'We are glass-blowers here in Dwaling. Always have been, always will be. Well, I hope we always will be. We have been running gravely low on supplies as of late. The new 'townsfolk' and creatures about the sands have kept us from venturing too far. We barely have enough to finish our next order.

'I used to get white sand for my glass-blowing from a dear old friend who would travel into the Barandalf to the north-east of Dwaling, but he moved away not a week ago. If you would, travel up to the Sand Marshes and get me some. I would be deeply in your debt.''Have you found any sand yet ${PLAYER}? The Barandalf is north and east of here.''You have the sand... and just in the nick of time too! My lads were just finishing up the last of our supplies.'There are many villainous monsters roaming Evendim, and the Free Peoples must do their part to slay them.Slay many Angmarim in EvendimSlay many Dead in AnnúminasSlay many Gertheryg in AnnúminasSlay many Brigands in EvendimSlay many limrafn in EvendimSlay many Gauradan in EvendimSlay many Giants in EvendimSlay many Goblins in EvendimSlay many Kergrim in EvendimSlay many Salamanders in EvendimBob Greeneaves is confused by your discoveries among the goblin-camps.There are spiders in the North Moors, south of Dwaling.

Bob Greeneaves asked you to go out and slay spiders, in the hopes that the goblins will leave.Defeat Wood-web spinners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Wood-web hunters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bob Greeneaves is in Dwaling, north of the North Moors.

You should return to Bob Greeneaves with news of your success.Talk to Bob Greeneaves in Dwaling'What those goblins want with the spiders' innards is a puzzle, to be sure, but there's one thing I do know for certain: they are up to no good!

'Maybe if you kill off the spiders, the goblins will go away? There's more than anyone can handle out east of here. The North Moors are crawling with them.

'It certainly won't make any of us sorry to see the beasts dead. You just be careful though.''Good, you're back, and you've dealt with a few of those spiders, I see.

'Well, I've had me an idea about what those goblins are up to with the spiders' innards...and it ain't a pretty thought!''I don't know what those goblins are up to, but maybe they'll leave off when there's no more spiders to hunt.'Pipe-weed is something hobbits take very seriously. A market would never be visited unless it had a suitable supply and variety.Albin Northcotton is at the market-grounds at the Northcotton Farm.

Albin Northcotton will be pleased to hear the pipe-weed is ready.Talk to Albin Northcotton'We are ready for the market. All three blends are of exceptional taste and flavour, and we have enough to last at least the first day, presuming we don't smoke it all ourselves first!

'Go to the market-grounds and let Albin Northcotton know the pipe-weed for the market is ready.''Good pipe-weed guarantees a good market!''The sweet smell of fresh pipe-weed is almost as nice as the smell of fresh-baked pie... almost.'The road has been cleared and the wolf defeated.Albin Northcotton is at the Northcotton Farm.

Albin Northcotton will be pleased to hear that Nibs is ready and the road is clear.Talk to Albin Northcotton'With the wolf gone, the road to Dwaling is now clear. This is good news because many from Dwaling usually come down for the market. They bring down their glassware and trade it for our produce.

'Albin Northcotton up at the Northcotton Farm will want to know that we're ready down here and that the road is clear.''Everything is all set with Nibs? And the road to Oatbarton is safe, as well, you say? You have been busy!''The road is finally clear.'Heithur Ironfist, in the company and relative safety of Barachen's Camp, awaits your return with fine skin.You have accepted a task to collect fine skin from the beasts and cave-claws in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. You are to deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist, a dwarf taking refuge at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine skin from the beasts and cave-claws in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. Deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine skin from the beasts and cave-claws in the western stretches of the Trollshaws.'They are not much, but I am certain that I can work with them. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Heithur Ironfist, in the company and relative safety of Barachen's Camp, awaits your return with new sword sheaths.You have accepted a task to collect new sword sheaths from the Hill-men and trolls in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. You are to deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist, a dwarf taking refuge at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: New sword sheaths from the Hill-men and trolls in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. Deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: New sword sheaths from the Hill-men and trolls in the western stretches of the Trollshaws.'Ha! Look at these, serves those beasts right. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Big Folk have bought a large amount of land in the Shire on behalf of a hobbit from the Westfarthing, and it is troubling Hob Hillbrow.Pearlina Gamgee's residence is located in Dwaling, east of Hob Hillbrow's home.

Hob Hillbrow asked you to check on the home of one his friends, Pearlina Gamgee.Ensure Pearlina Gamgee's home is safeRemove the ruffians living on Pearlina Gamgee's property.

Hob Hillbrow asked you to check on the home of one his friends, Pearlina Gamgee.Defeat the Chief's watcherHob Hillbrow is at his home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling, west of Pearlina Gamgee's property.

You were attacked by ruffians outside Pearlina Gamgee's residence. You should inform Hob Hillbrow.Talk to Hob Hillbrow in the Bullroarer's Sward'Now, this hobbit I was telling you about before, he let Big Men buy up all the land... right here in the Shire! I have talked to Ronald Dwale about sending a letter to old Will Whitfoot, the mayor, but I don't know what he can do against a sound contract like the one sent to my door.

'One of my dear friends, Pearlina Gamgee, is away in the Westfarthing looking for a snug hole to start over in, and I told her that I would look after her home while she was away. Those new Men in town have a foul look about them, and I don't much trust them, even if they claim to be here to "help".

'If you could, go down and check on Pearlina Gamgee's residence just east of here in Dwaling.  You look like you could hold your own if any of those ruffians gave you any trouble.''Pearlina Gamgee's residence is just east of here, please make sure nothing has happened to her home.''Well I never...! These Big Folk are violating their contracts! What are these times coming to?'Dannasen sent you to the west to collect an urn that the Red-maid used to calm the water before her fall. He directed you to make your way to the eastern side of Agamaur and locate an altar, natural in appearance and guarded by darkwater, and fill the urn with water from near the altar then return to him.The urn is in the south-western part of Agamaur.

The shade Dannasen has asked you to recover the Red-maid's urn, fill it with water from an ancient, natural altar, and bring it back to him.Collect the Urn of AgamaurThe altar is in the south-eastern part of Agamaur.

Fill the urn you collected with water from the area surrounding a natural altar guarded by darkwater.Dannasen is at the Eglain camp, north-west of the Red-maid's altar.

Now that you have filled the urn with water from the area surrounding the altar, you should return to Dannasen.'Perhaps thou hast heard whispers of the Red-maid? She was once an innocent creature, but was twisted long ago by the war which raged between Angmar and the North Kingdom. Now the very land has fallen to the Red-maid's pain. To heal that pain, we must first bring innocence and beauty to her sanctum. 

'Before her fall, the Red-maid would use an urn to calm the water. The urn is in the west guarded by Ivar's minions. 

'Retrieve the urn, then travel to the south-eastern most part of Agamaur. There, seek out a natural altar... thou wilt know it when thou hast seen it, for the wretched darkwater will guard it in force. Fill the urn with water from that place and return to me.''First thou must retrieve the Red-maid's urn. Then travel to the south-easternmost part of Agamaur. There, seek out a natural altar... thou wilt know it when thou hast seen it, for the wretched darkwater will guard it in force. Fill the urn with water from that place and return to me.''Now that thou has obtained the urn, thou must fill it. Take it to the altar of the Red-maid and draw water into it there.''At the altar in the east, the Red-maid came forth from the water when the wars encroached upon her spring, and she made herself known unto the Men of Rhudaur, but they did not heed her pleas.

'Her visage was not then as it is now, for the corruption of war had not tainted her spirit then. Ever we fought and ever did the blood of Rhudaur and Arthedain pour into the land. The spring of the maiden became filth, and over time, the maiden herself became diseased by the blood which drowned her home.

'She arose as a terrible creature and slew any who beheld her, satisfying her new hunger. It was then that Iarwain Ben-adar charged us with our great task... only we failed. And now, Ivar Blood-hand has gained power over the Red-maid, though I think he does not understand the danger to himself.'Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of broken short bows.You have been tasked with collecting broken short bows from the brigands in the ruins surrounding Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Broken short bows from the brigands in the ruins surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Broken short bows from the brigands in the ruins surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'The preservation of our heritage is very important to us. This brings us much solace.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of very sharp claws.You have been tasked with collecting very sharp claws from the turtles and toads west of Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Very sharp claws from the turtles and toads west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Very sharp claws from the turtles and toads west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'We will make use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Defeat worms in the North Downs and Ram Dúath.Defeat worms in the North Downs and Ram DúathDefeat many Worms in the North Downs and Ram Dúath.Defeat many Worms in the North Downs and Ram DúathThe hobbits do not know why or how the essence of the limrafn retains its glow, and even less of a clue as to how it can be put into glass. All they know is it does, and it can.Essences can be found on the limrafn among the Barandalf, north of Dwaling.

Pearl Goodbody has asked you to gather essences from the limrafn within the dunes.Collect essences (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pearl Goodbody is at Hob Hillbrow's just outside of Dwaling.

Pearl Goodbody will be eager to see the limrafn essences you have gathered for her.'The most challenging part of making glass is getting it to sparkle just so. You don't want to do this with all glass, but most you do.

'We have found that the limrafn within the dunes carry, or have, or are made of something we call 'essence.' I don't know exactly what it is, nor how they got it. All I know is that it somehow makes glass sparkle... and on that I'm not even really sure how!

'You can find the limrafn among the dunes of the Barandalf, just north of here. Bring me a bunch of their essences, and I'll be quite appreciative.''The limrafn are tricky creatures. Watch for glowing lights.''I still do not understand how these essences work. Perhaps one day I will. But for now, all we need to know is they make glass sparkle.'Ferrod has asked you to make the dangerous journey to Rivendell to speak with Aragorn.Aragorn is in Rivendell, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House, far to the east within the Trollshaws.

Ferrod has asked you to speak with Aragorn, telling you that with dedication, sticking to the roads, and travelling only by day, you can safely reach Rivendell.Talk to Aragorn in Rivendell'I understand you're an adventurer, ${PLAYER}? Have your travels ever taken you to Rivendell?

'Ha! You look at me with such surprise! I understand your dismay, but do not fear! Yes, the Last Homely House is far to the east, beyond the Lone-lands and within the wilderness of the Trollshaws, but I will tell you this and true: you can make the journey. It will be dangerous, and you will need to stay alert, but I believe that you too can find your way to the House of Elrond. Stay to the road wherever possible, and do not travel by night!

'If you push yourself to the limit and do find your way to Rivendell, look for Aragorn, my chieftain, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House. Tell him that Ferrod hopes he will return before long to Annúminas. It has been many years since he was here, I have heard it said.''You can make the journey to Rivendell, ${PLAYER}. Stay on the road wherever possible, do not travel by night, and be watchful of the dangerous creatures of the Trollshaws! Speak with Aragorn in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House and tell him that I look forward to the day he will return to Evendim!''I am pleased to hear your tidings of Ferrod, ${PLAYER}. It will be some time before I can spare a journey to Evendim, but I would look upon Annúminas again. Thank you, my friend.'The ruffians which have moved into Dwaling are lawless and brutal, and Hob Hillbrow has had enough of their wicked ways.Ronald Dwale is at Hob Hillbrow's home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling.

Hob Hillbrow has asked you to retrieve the formal complaint he asked Ronald Dwale to draft.Retrieve the formal complaint from Ronald DwaleWill Tuffin is in the big hole in Dwaling centre.

Deliver the formal complaint to Will Tuffin.Hob Hillbrow is at his home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling.

You should return to Hob Hillbrow and tell him of Will Tuffin's response.Talk to Hob HillbrowBob Greeneaves is at Hob Hillbrow's home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling.

Talk to Bob Greeneaves to gain access to the big hole to deliver the letter.Talk to Bob GreeneavesHob Hillbrow is at his home at the Bullroarer's Sward in Dwaling, south of town centre.

You should return to Hob Hillbrow with word of your success and his letter.'Never in all my days have I heard of decent folk getting attacked in the Shire. Even if this is the Northfarthing, folk should still be decent and civil, though I wouldn't put such treachery past these Big Men.

'I've asked Ronald Dwale to draft a formal letter of complaint to the leader of these Men, Will Tuffin. He is always in and out of the big hole down in Dwaling centre. I would like you to get the letter from Dwale and take it to Will Tuffin straightaway. Just go down to the big hole down yonder and ring the bell until Will comes out. Make sure you are careful, I don't trust these Big Folk.

'Take care that you don't let them bully you this time.''What's that now? Shut the door in your face? Well, if that isn't the rudest thing I have heard all day!  

'Bob Greeneaves has a spare key to the big hole. It's a public building, and even if they own the whole town, they haven't the right to close up a public hole!

'You march right in there and make sure he reads every word of that letter. Talk to Bob Greeneaves here when you are ready to go down to the big hole.''Ah, yes, Hob asked me to draw up a letter of complaint after you found out about that business with Pearlina Gamgee.  

'Business letters are not my usual fare; I am more used to crafting more fanciful stories and letters, but I daresay I did as fine a job as any could have asked for.''Go over and talk to Ronald...he should have the letter ready.''Well? That letter is not going to deliver itself.''Are you ready for me to let you into the big hole?

'You be careful; who knows what those Big Folk will do once you're inside.''Hob wanted you to bring the letter to Will Tuffin down at the big hole in Dwaling centre.''So their leader wouldn't take the letter? I can't say I didn't expect trouble from these ruffians. I am going to have another formal complaint written to this 'investor' and tell him just what I think about the character of these Men he has let into the Shire.

'You mentioned something about a goblin-faced fellow and someone named Sharkey? I'm sure you must be mistaken; there haven't been naught like them in the Shire since the Bullroarer's time, and I have never heard of anyone with a name such as Sharkey.

'But what with all the excitement, I am sure you can't be blamed for your mistake.'When ground up, scales from the salamanders can be used to make red glass.Sand-swept scales can be found on salamanders within the southern dunes of the Barandalf, to the north and east of Dwaling.

Wald Hillburrow has asked you to collect sand-swept scales from salamanders, so he can make red glass.Collect sand-swept scales (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Wald Hillburrow is at Hob Hillbrow's just outside of Dwaling.

Wald Hillburrow will want to begin grinding down the scales as soon as possible.'We use many materials to make different shades of glass. One of the colours I'm tasked with is red. It is not an overly difficult colour to make, but it does require a fair amount of supplies in order to bring out a bright crimson.

'Salamander-scales are the best material I've found around here for producing red glass. Unfortunately, our stores are low, so we cannot make any more red or orange.

You can find sand-swept scales on the salamanders within the southern dunes of the Barandalf, to the north and east of here. If you could collect me a bunch, I would be grateful.''Salamanders are found within the dunes to the north and east. Make sure you bring back their scales.''These scales will do perfectly. I will begin grinding them down at once so we can use them in the next batch of glassware. Thank you.'Aragorn has told you that he knows the location of a Silithar with which Hemeldir can reforge Narsil. He gave you a mysterious message to bring to Elrond and assured you that the Elf-lord would understand it.Elrond is in the library on the top floor of the Last Homely House.

Aragorn told you to bring a message to Master Elrond, with assurances that the Elf-lord will understand his words. The message Aragorn told you to speak is this: 'I know now what Gwindeth sought of me in the watery darkness, and I am prepared to give it. Elendil was a wiser man than I.'Talk to Elrond'When I was very young, my father Arathorn was slain by Orcs and my mother brought me to stay here at Rivendell. I was called Estel by my mother Gilraen and by all those who dwelt in the Vale, and I rode far afield seeking adventure with the sons of Elrond; I considered him my father as much as did Elladan and Elrohir.

'At the age of twenty, Lord Elrond took me aside and told me my true name and of my heritage, that I descended in unbroken line from Elendil of old. He gave to me the ring of Barahir and the shards of Narsil, but he withheld the Sceptre of Annúminas, saying I had yet to earn it. He withheld, too, another treasure, but I will not speak of that.

'Soon afterward, I went in search of a Silithar with which Narsil might be reforged. Yes, I knew then of the ancient Silithair and of the need for one. But I was young, and I did not pass the test that was set before me; the time had not come for Narsil's remaking.

'I have a message for Master Elrond. Bring it to him faithfully and return with his words, and I will better know how to proceed. Here is my message: "I know now what Gwindeth sought of me in the watery darkness, and I am prepared to give it. Elendil was a wiser man than I." I await your return, ${PLAYER}.''Find Master Elrond in his library and tell him my message: "I know now what Gwindeth sought of me in the watery darkness, and I am prepared to give it. Elendil was a wiser man than I."

'He will understand these words and can better guide our course.''Aragorn has travelled many roads since his early failure in the tomb of his great ancestor. What would Gwindeth make of him now, I wonder?

'You look puzzled, young one. Do not despair. I will explain my words to you and give counsel to him whom I once called Estel.'Aragorn has decided that the time has come to reforge Narsil, the sword that was broken when Elendil fell at Orodruin.Hemeldir is at the forges of Rivendell, in the north-west corner of the Vale.

Aragorn has decided that the time has come to reforge Narsil, one of the heirlooms of his line and the blade that broke the power of Sauron in the final battle of the Last Alliance during the Second Age.Talk to HemeldirAragorn is in one of the rooms of the Guest House located south-west of Elrond's house.

The Elf-smith Hemeldir told you that without one of the Silithair, any sword forged from the shards of Narsil would be a lesser weapon.Talk to Aragorn in Rivendell'I have borne this broken blade for many long years, ${PLAYER}, always unused, for these are the shards of Narsil. Yes, Narsil, the sword wielded by Elendil of Westernesse in battle before the Black Gate of Mordor, the sword that broke beneath him in battle with Sauron upon the slopes of Orodruin.

'Though Gil-galad had fallen, and Elendil was slain, two still stood against the might of Sauron in the shadow of that fell tower: Isildur, the son of the king, and Elrond Halfelven, herald of Gil-galad. It was with the hilt-shard of Narsil that Isildur cut the One Ring from Sauron's finger, and the power of the Dark Lord was broken.

'The light of Narsil was extinguished, but its shards came again to the North, though Isildur himself never returned from the Gladden Fields. The shards have been passed from father to son in his line for years beyond count, but the sword has never been remade.

'The old victories did not last, ${PLAYER}, and the Shadow has returned; we have need of Narsil's light once more. The blade must be reforged. Speak with Hemeldir at the forges here in Rivendell, in the north-west corner of the Vale, and tell him of my decision. I would have him know that Narsil is to be remade.''We will have need of the light of Narsil to combat the darkness on the horizon, ${PLAYER}. Seek out Master Elrond's trusted smith Hemeldir and ask him what must be done if the sword is to be reforged.

'You will find Hemeldir at the forges here in Rivendell, in the north-west corner of the Vale.''Narsil is to be reforged? My heart would be filled with joy at this news, friend, for Aragorn and for all of Middle-earth, if it were possible! The shards were kept a while in Imladris for safe-keeping, and I often gazed upon them. It is with sadness that I must tell you that reforging the sword may not be possible. Its history is known to me, long and storied as it is, but I will tell you only the portion that causes me to grieve.

'Of old, Elves of the house of Fëanor crafted numerous wonders, items of great power and beauty. Among these were the Silithair, adamants possessed of the light of the stars. The dwarf-smith Telchar used one of these stones in the creation of Narsil, and the Silithar it was that caused the blade to glow with silver light when wielded in battle.

'All of the Silithair were lost during the long centuries since the forging of Narsil. Without such a stone to use in the reforging, the sword will never achieve the greatness it once had. I am sorry. Bring this news to Aragorn with my sorrow.''I am sorry, friend, but any sword forged from the shards of Narsil will be a lesser weapon without a Silithar to use in the forging.

'Return to Aragorn in the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris and tell him the cause of my sorrowful news.''You have returned from speaking with Hemeldir? What did he have to say about the reforging of Narsil?

'I see. Hemeldir is a fine smith, but he shares the dour outlook of his people. If he needs a Silithar to reforge the sword of Elendil, we will find for him a Silithar!'You found a pile of soggy weeds within the remains of the bog lurker. They soothe your skin when you touch them. One of the Rangers at the colossus may be interested in looking at them.The colossus-camp is north of the Barandalf.

One of the Rangers at the colossus-camp may be interested in seeing the weeds you have found.Find a Ranger at the colossus-camp to speak with about the weeds you foundThe weeds are slimy and do not appear to be any different from any other river weeds; however, when they touch your skin, you feel a soft, soothing feeling. A Ranger at the colossus may be interested in this.'These look like any other river weeds that I have seen dozens of times. You say they soothed your skin upon touching them? Yes, I feel that....'Blue river-stones can be crushed into a powder that can make glass look like water.Tarnished river-stones can be found in the possession of the goblins at Bleakleaf Crest, south of Dwaling.

Wald Hillburrow has asked you to collect tarnished river-stones the goblins have taken from the Brandywine.Collect tarnished river-stones (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Wald Hillburrow is at Hob Hillbrow's just outside of Dwaling.

Wald Hillburrow will be eager to see the tarnished river-stones that you have collected for him.'We use many materials to make different shades of glass. My favourite colour to make is blue. It is a hard and tricky colour, particularly when you want to make a glass which looks like a flowing river.

'I've only found one thing that can make it look how I like, and that is river-stones the goblins have taken from the Brandywine. Simple, you say, but not so. I tried to gather them myself, but the colour didn't come out the same. Something the goblins must do changes the stone somehow.

'If you can go to Bleakleaf Crest and collect several of the stones from the goblins for me, I would be grateful. I could make the most beautiful tea set you have ever seen.''The goblins are camped at Bleakleaf Crest. They are sure to have more stones on them.''These stones are amazing... they are the best batch I have yet seen. I wonder if it is because I have not tried to get any lately. Maybe the longer the goblins have them the more blue they become?'Sand-norbog shells may look brown on the outside, but beneath is a bright and brilliant green.Norbog-shells can be found on the sand-nerbyg within the southern dunes of the Barandalf, north of Dwaling.

Pearl Goodbody has asked you to gather shells off the nerbyg living within the dunes.Collect norbog-shells (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pearl Goodbody is at Hob Hillbrow's just outside of Dwaling.

Pearl Goodbody will want to begin extracting the green from the norbog-shells as soon as possible.'Glass is a difficult thing to colour. There are lots of materials to gather up to make any individual shade. Green is not all that hard to make, but what we use may surprise you.

'I know the sand-nerbyg look brown and blend in with the dunes. However, if you crack their shells and look at the underside, you see a colour as green as the grass itself.

'You can find the sand-nerbyg all over the southern dunes of the Barandalf, just north of here. Bring me a bunch of their shells, and I can mix up the powders for some green glass.''The nerbyg are all over the dunes. You cannot really miss them. Just head north.''These will do. They are not the best set I have ever seen, but I think I can get enough green out of them to do what I need for now.'Many Rangers perished at Sarn Ford and in Tyrn Gorthad, and those who survived will never forget their fallen friends and kin.Gorwen stands by a number of recently-constructed cairns outside the village of Tornhad, remembering those who were lost at Sarn Ford and in Tyrn Gorthad.Gorwen stands by a number of recently-constructed cairns in the village of Tornhad, remembering those who were lost at Sarn Ford and beneath Dol Ernil.Talk to Gorwen by the cairns in Tornhad'It is good to see you outside Tyrn Gorthad, ${PLAYER}, far from the unquiet keep of Dol Ernil.'

Gorwen motions at the cairns built nearby.

'These stones mark the sacrifice of those Dúnedain who fell at Sarn Ford and in Tyrn Gorthad at the end of September. Whenever I return to Tornhad, I take some time to come here and remember those we lost.''These stones mark the sacrifice of those Dúnedain who fell at Sarn Ford and in Tyrn Gorthad at the end of September. Whenever I return to Tornhad, I take some time to come here and remember those we lost.'Gorwen looks at you sadly.

'My thoughts keep turning to Meneldir, ${PLAYER}. Could he have been saved? If I had kept him from searching for the Grey Fear, if I had sent him from my camp-site when he first arrived...'

You tell Gorwen that not even binding Meneldir with strong ropes could have prevented him from seeking out Old Cardolan's great enemy, and she laughs through tears.

'Perhaps not, ${PLAYER}. He was stubborn, wasn't he? But kind to his friends. He must have had friends in the south, hadn't he? He did not live as a hermit, surely?'

You tell Gorwen that when you first met him, he had taken it upon himself to safe-guard the folk of Mossward, a small village on the edge of Swanfleet. You speak of his friendship with the village's blacksmith, a dwarf named Skardí Bendblade, and of how you and Meneldir sought to prevent the children of Mossward from getting into mischief... mostly unsuccessfully. Meneldir always said he was not very good with children, but in your experience they seemed to be fascinated by him. Gorwen smiles.

'I am glad, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for these memories. Remembering can be painful, but perhaps it can bring peace as well.'Mundaric Sandheaver is concerned about the number of nerbyg that have begun to appear throughout the Barandalf and fears that the creatures may be preparing to swarm.Sand-nerbyg can be found in great numbers on the Barandalf, on either side of the road that runs through the sand dunes along the west side of the Brandywine.

Mundaric Sandheaver has asked you to defeat sand-nerbyg on the Barandalf before they swarm, bringing destruction in whichever direction their paths take them.Defeat sand-nerbyg in the Barandalf (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat huge sand-nerbyg in the Barandalf (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mundaric Sandheaver is at Dwaling, south of the Barandalf.

You have defeated many of the sand-nerbyg that have been breeding on the Barandalf and should return to Mundaric Sandheaver with news of your victories.Talk to Mundaric Sandheaver in Dwaling'I have lived here for a great time, ${CLASS}, and have studied the behaviour of many of the local creatures. Listen to me, then, when I tell you that a great danger is breeding among the sands of the Barandalf!

'I speak of the nerbyg, vile insects that can be found now among the dunes in great numbers. If we do not do something, and quickly, I fear that the nerbyg will swarm -- and whether they swarm north or south is immaterial, for there are good people in both directions!

'Travel north of Dwaling onto the sands of the Barandalf that line the west bank of the Brandywine river and defeat any sand-nerbyg that you find!''You have destroyed some of the sand-nerbyg? Thank you for this, ${PLAYER}. You have put my mind at ease, and I cannot say that often enough in these dark days. The nerbyg will always be a danger, but it is to be hoped that we have forestalled their swarming.''Sand-nerbyg can be found all throughout the sand dunes that line the western bank of the Brandywine river, north of here.

'Destroy the vile creatures before they swarm, ${PLAYER}!'Torogethir has asked you to bring word of his struggles in the North Downs to Calenglad, a leader of the Rangers.Calenglad is at the Ranger camp on the island of Tinnudir, west of the road that runs through Parth Aduial and the cross-roads of Canadiach.

Torogethir has asked you to bring word to Calenglad that his efforts on the Fields of Fornost are going well, and that Calenglad should focus on combatting the tomb-robbers that plague Evendim.'Hold, friend. Are you passing into Evendim? I would have you deliver a message to Calenglad, a friend of mine and true. The fighting on the Fields of Fornost continues, but we are holding our own -- tell Calenglad he need not spare any Rangers to come to my aid.

'Calenglad is in charge of the Ranger camp on the island of Tinnudir, far to the west along the road that passes through Parth Aduial, and beyond the cross-roads of Canadiach. The Rangers of the North have an encampment on Tinnudir, just off the eastern shore of Lake Nenuial.

'Tomb-robbers and other unsavoury Men plague the shores of Evendim, and I would have Calenglad dedicate his forces to combatting them, rather than dividing his forces and preparing less-effective defences! Go now, and good fortune go with you into Evendim!''You will find Calenglad at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, west of the road that runs through Parth Aduial and the cross-roads of Canadiach.

'Tell him not to spare any men for battle on the Fields of Fornost; it is more important to safe-guard the treasures and history of Annúminas!''Torogethir speaks the truth: the tomb-robbers that plague Evendim must be fought. I cannot spare any men for the Fields of Fornost, unless the situation becomes more grave. It would need to be dire indeed for me to abandon Annúminas to the tomb-robbers!'The lighthouse used to watch over the ships on the northern stretch of the Baranduin. The keeper was tasked with keeping detailed records of wrecks and tragedies.The lighthouse is at the northern edge of the Barandalf, south-east of the colossus.

Gaelnarthan asked you to venture to the ruins of an ancient lighthouse and find any remains of the log-books kept there.Gaelnarthan is at the Colossus, north-west of the lighthouse ruins.

Gaelnarthan will be eager to examine the page you found.'There are some ruins on the hill above the turn in the river of which we only recently discovered the purpose. There was a lighthouse there -- believe it or not -- to guide ships as they made the turn from the lake into the Baranduin.

'The keeper of that flame was tasked with recording all of the wrecks and tragedies he witnessed from his perch so search parties could be sent to attempt to retrieve what was lost. But in the waning days of the kingdom, they could not have been focused on recovering too much.

'Head to the lighthouse and see if you can find any old papers from those log-books. If we can get a lead as to where some of the wrecks happened, we might be able to recover some ancient valuables.''Look among the rubble of what is left of the lighthouse. There must surely be something useful left within there, even if it be but a scrap.''You were able to find a page? I was worried that they would have all been lost to time, much like the lighthouse itself. Now, let us see what we can learn from this....'The shades that wander these marshy lands were cursed to remain in Middle-earth, houseless and restless, until they either redeemed or destroyed the Red-maid of Agamaur.Make your way to the entrance to the area known as Garth Agarwen, north-east of the Eglan-camp, and speak with Hartrím.

Dannasen asked you to seek out Hartrím at the entrance to Garth Agarwen in an effort to discover if he has found any of the shields once used by Dannasen and his brethren.Talk to Hartrím at the entrance of Garth AgarwenThe Hillmen of Rhudaur who serve the Red-maid and their brigand allies can be found in Garth Agarwen, the fortress of the Red-maid in the north-east of Agamaur. Hartrím awaits you outside the gates of Garth Agarwen.

Dannasen, one of the shades of Arthedain cursed by Iarwain Ben-adar, fears that the others have lost their resolve to break their curse, believing that the Red-maid is too powerful. To strengthen their resolve, Dannasen enlisted Hartrím of the Eglain to collect the shields they lost to Rhudaur long years ago. Hartrím, too weak to face these foes, turns to you for assistance.'In ages past, we were cursed by Iarwain Ben-adar for our inaction as the Red-maid slipped further from the gentle creature she was to the corrupt thing she now is. Until she is redeemed or removed from the world, we are cursed to wander these swamps, with no hope of rest.

'But we have no hope. She is too powerful, or so the men believe. I do not believe this is so. I must find a way to restore their resolve. One of the Eglain, Hartrím, has gone to the place where she dwells. He goes to secure items lost as my brethren and I battled the Angmarim and Hillmen of Rhudaur: our shields. I believe they are still held by the Hillmen who worship the Red-maid and the brigands who have allied themselves with them.

'Perhaps you would seek Hartrím at the outskirts of Garth Agarwen and ask him how his efforts progress.''Once, we were proud men. Now, we are less than the dust of the earth and just as long-lived. Our failure was inaction, and still we fulfil that role. Without courage, we are nothing.

'Please, seek Hartrím and see how he progresses.''Dannasen will be displeased to know that I am not able to perform this task. The creatures beyond these walls are far beyond me.

'Perhaps, these Men will prove less a challenge to you. Would you take on this challenge and aid the shades who have lingered in these lands since they were cursed?

'You will find that the Men, the Créoth, beyond these walls are stronger than any other, indoctrinated deeply in the service of the Red-maid. If any carry the shields that Dannasen speaks of, it will be these Men.''Once we were proud Men. Now we are less than the dust of the earth and just as long-lived. Our failure was inaction, and still we fulfil that role. Without courage we are nothing.''This crest is familiar to me, it is the crest of Arthedain, noblest of the Men who lived in these lands.

'Dannasen and his brethren are from a lineage that was the strongest of the Men, during their time. Seeing these will surely restore their resolve. I will see that they receive them forthwith.

'I am indebted to you ${PLAYER}.'Once all the materials have been gathered, the glass itself must be worked within the fire and blown into shape.The glass forge is next to Mundaric Sandheaver in Dwaling.

Mundaric Sandheaver has asked you to use the forge to blow some glass.Use the glass forge (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mundaric Sandheaver is at Dwaling.

Mundaric Sandheaver will be interested to hear how your first glass-blowing experience went.Talk to Mundaric Sandheaver in Dwaling'My assistants tell me that all the supplies have been gathered, and we are able to once more make glass. I thank you for your help there.

'Blowing glass is an art, one that can be learned quickly, but must be refined over many years of trial and error. I can teach you the basics, if you would like.

'Head over to the forge and, using this rod, work the glass several times. You should see it start to take a shape.''Well, go ahead: use the forge and try it! That glass won't blow itself!''Well, I am not quite sure what we can do with this. Perhaps it could be used as a tray to ash one's pipe after smoking. A useful thing, if not the most glamorous of objects.'Halbarad has named you a friend to the Dúnedain, and encouraged you to help his people wherever you can.Halbarad believes that you will provide much help to the Dúnedain as you grow stronger and continue your adventures in the Lone-lands or the North Downs.Continue your adventures in the Lone-lands or the North Downs, and reach Level 32Halbarad told you to meet with another of the Dúnedain, a Ranger named Candaith who camps by the base of Weathertop, in the Lone-lands.Talk to Candaith at his camp near the base of Weathertop'We have shown the Grey Fear that this land will not surrender without resistance, but it seems other dangers spring forth even now. If it is true that the realm of Angmar rises again, and its new leader serves the Lord of the Nazgûl... we are about to become very busy, ${PLAYER}.

'This is what I think we should do. I will travel to the North Downs, to see if there is activity along the border with Angmar. I will go with a number of my kin to reinforce the old city of Esteldín, abandoned for many years in a hidden cleft among the hills. If it need become a thriving settlement once again, and draw the eye of new foes away from other peaceful villages... well, I will make that judgement when I get there.

'As for you, ${PLAYER}, you have proven yourself a friend of the Dúnedain. I suggest that you aid the people of the North Downs and the Lone-lands, increasing your strength and the good you can do for the people who dwell in those places. When you have achieved as much as you can for them, seek out the Ranger Candaith, who keeps watch on Amon Sûl, the old hill of Weathertop, in the Lone-lands. Tell him what you have seen here, and warn him of the rising dangers that now threaten Middle-earth.

'Go with the good will of the Dúnedain, my friend. May we meet again in happier times.''You have proven yourself a friend of the Dúnedain. I suggest that you aid the people of the North Downs and the Lone-lands, increasing your strength and the good you can do for the people who dwell in those places. When you have achieved as much as you can for them, seek out the Ranger Candaith, who keeps watch on Amon Sûl, the old hill of Weathertop, in the Lone-lands. Tell him what you have seen here, and warn him of the rising dangers that now threaten Middle-earth.'The Great East Road has seen much more travel than usual in recent days! What brings you to the base of Weathertop?'

You briefly explain that you were sent by Halbarad to warn of increasing dangers in the wild, and that a new shadow rises in the realm of Angmar. Candaith nods.

'I have heard rumours that this might be so from others of my kin that have passed this way, and I have seen evils enough with my own eyes not to disbelieve it!'Candaith suggested that you make for Rivendell without delay.Candaith has words of warning and advice for you before you depart on the last leg of your journey.Talk to Candaith and depart for Rivendell with hasteElrond is in the library of his home in Imladris.Talk to Elrond in his library'There is a hidden dale in the Trollshaws, far to the east and across the Last Bridge, where the wisest of lore-masters resides. Elrond is his name, and he is the master of Rivendell. It was in his house that my chieftain Aragorn was raised, and if any will know what must be done about the growing threat of Angmar, it will be he. You must make for Rivendell.

'I will tell you what I can of this last leg of your journey, ${PLAYER}. Speak to me when you are ready.''Orcs have grown bolder and ferocious in recent days, but when you cross the Last Bridge into the Trollshaws you will face greater peril. Something has stirred the beasts of the wild into madness, and if it travels ahead of you there may be great evil left in its wake.

'Luck be with you as you travel the road through the Trollshaws and begin the climb towards Rivendell. The path will not be easy, but Elrond must be told of what befell you in Cardolan, and of the growing threat of Angmar.''You have woven quite a tale, ${PLAYER}, and if you should wish it to be ended, none will judge you for it. But in your words I sense something else... indeed, two somethings!

'I sense that you possess a desire for adventure, and it remains unquenched even after your trials in the wilderness. You could not remain in this peaceful haven for long before that desire compelled you once again to seek out and oppose great dangers.

'And I sense too that you wish to continue your work on behalf of the Free Peoples of Middle-earth. We enter a dangerous time, and mighty foes seek to return to the height of their powers, heights that were stolen from them in centuries past by great heroes. Are you to be one of these heroes in this age, ${PLAYER}? I wonder.

'So rest then, for as long as you wish, and return to me when you are ready to resume your adventure! The shadow that rises in Angmar must be faced, and defeated, and I fear the Black Riders that troubled Cardolan, and pursued Frodo Baggins hither, have not been defeated for good. One of their number has not been found.'Aragorn has charged you with bringing word to the Ranger Calenglad that Narsil will be reforged.Calenglad is at Tinnudir, an island on the eastern shore of Lake Nenuial, in Evendim, far to the west of the Trollshaws.

Aragorn has given you a letter to present to Calenglad and advice concerning the nature of respect: that it cannot be bought, but must be earned. With the aid of Calenglad, Aragorn hopes that you can recover the Silithar needed to reforge Narsil.'Long years have passed, and I have not seen Calenglad since that day; he did not return with me. He had become enamoured of Gwindeth and Lake Nenuial, and chose to remain behind. I offered to free him from his oath to me, but he refused, saying, "You are my lord and have power over me, but I ask that you allow me to remain here. I will keep watch over the city and safe-guard the lake until such time as you return. In this way, I will uphold my oath."

'Calenglad has remained at Tinnudir ever since. I would rejoice to speak with him again, but preparations are underway for a journey of great danger and importance -- more important even than the reforging of Narsil -- and I cannot journey to Annúminas. But you, my friend, you can bring to Calenglad my words and tell him that we now have true need of the Silithar where once we did not.

'Bring this letter to Calenglad, ${PLAYER}, and remember that you act in my stead -- you are my agent to Tinnudir, an island on the eastern shore of Lake Nenuial, in Evendim, and we speak with one voice. Always remember that respect cannot be bought, but must be earned. Do not undervalue it, and we shall yet see Andúril forged from the shards of Narsil.''Calenglad is at Tinnudir on the shores of Lake Nenuial, in Evendim, far to the west. Give him my letter, and I hope he will aid you in recovering the Silithar. Remember this, too: that respect cannot be bought, but must be earned. Do not undervalue such a quality.''What is that you have there? A letter from Aragorn? Let me see it....

'What is it you call yourself? ${PLAYER}? I am sorry, but this note is not enough. Tomb-robbers have plundered the vaults and tombs of Annúminas, seeking to despoil the heritage of the Dúnedain. This could be a forgery intended to relax our guard!

'Nay, I am afraid I cannot trust the words of this letter. How am I to know you are true? Deeds, not words, matter to me.'Not all who occupy Garth Agarwen are given over to corruption. Some are taken from their homes or the sites where they worked and brought to the ruins where they are enchanted and taught a new way of thought through foul magic. The Créoth warlord, Dúnlang, is possessed of the gifts of Angmar and captures members of the Eglain to corrupt and turn into mindless minions of the Red-maid.Dúnlang was given the power to enslave weaker minds, and many of the Eglain have gone missing over the years. He makes his home deep within the ruins of the Garth Agarwen Fortress and is in the process of converting members of the Eglain into devoted members of the Créoth clan. You should save them from the influence of this man, but not harm them in the process.Defeat DúnlangDefeat no Eglain WarriorsDefeat no Eglain Tale-weaversDefeat no Eglain ArchersIt may seem impossible that so small a creature can have so large an impact on the world, but it is the case that the small insects driven to madness by the caretaker of the woods of Agamaur are such creatures. Grimbark has long held control of the Arboretum at the behest of the Red-maid and with his corruption has driven all living denizens of the swamp mad.The Huorn Grimbark is lost to the corruption that has long plagued this land. It is an unfortunate end for a once noble creature, but Grimbark cannot be redeemed.

You are tasked with destroying Grimbark, but you should avoid harming the bees under his dominion.Defeat Grimbark, but do not destroy the beehivesIvar the Blood-hand is a Gaunt-lord, a being of great and dark power, made in mockery of the Wizard Radagast the Brown. He is weakened at the moment, not only by his meddling with Radagast, but also as he continues to raise his army of Dead within the bounds of Garth Agarwen.Ivar the Blood-hand must fall. His strength must fade, and he must become what he should have been all along: a memory of a bygone age. His strength is born of raising the Dead and returning those that have fallen back to battle. 

This is his role, and stopping him from achieving this role, no matter how minutely, will see that the mark of his hand is removed from the land. To do this, you must ensure that his banners remain so that at his death his power is split between them.Defeat Ivar the Blood-hand without destroying his bannersThe Red-maid is lost -- or so it seems -- to the corruption wrought upon her land at the hands of corrupt Men. These Men stand before her, ready to sacrifice themselves for the Red-maid, and they deserve this fate. However, the nobility of your blood will be a greater reward for her, and when these Men fall before her willingly, she will see that the noble are attempting to destroy her and ignoble keeping her this way.The Créoth will sacrifice themselves to the Red-maid to restore her strength and connexion to the corruption within the swamp. These Men deserve nothing better than death, and your presence in standing against the Red-maid may alert some part of her to the folly of her ways. You should let these Men die and strengthen the Red-maid, then defeat her. Perhaps a piece of her will understand that the corruption has grown too great, and she must return to the natural world which she was once part of.Aid in the cleansing of the Red-maidAllow the Créoth to sacrifice themselves to the Red-maid'My sisters are far from me...I cannot hear their songs or feel their gentle breath among the trees. Barren are those trees, nearly lifeless, choked out by roots bloated with something that has a bitter taste of iron. Where are you my sisters, and what is before me?

'I cannot see beyond the red of this land, but I sense something sweet among the bitter ferns and swollen roots. Are you real? Am I still imprisoned in the land?

'There is life in you -- I feel this. There must be life elsewhere in these lands. Perhaps I can find this life anew and nurture it back to health.'Herthdúr had an unfortunate encounter with the sand-lurkers that prowl among the dunes of the Barandalf and wishes to end the threat they pose to travellers.Sand-lurkers can be found throughout the Barandalf, but especially at Lithost, south-east of the Colossus, on the other side of the river from the ruins of Barad Tharsír.

Herthdúr has asked you to defeat some of the sand-lurkers that make travel through the Barandalf dangerous for him and his inventory of goods.Defeat sand-lurkers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Herthdúr is at the Ranger-camp at the colossus, north-west of the Barandalf.

You have put an end to some of the sand-lurkers that prowl among the dunes of the Barandalf and should report of your success to Herthdúr.Talk to Herthdúr at the colossus camp'I pride myself on the wide array of supplies I carry, ${PLAYER}, and I do my best to maintain my inventory. You can imagine the disappointment if I were to travel to the southern lands and not have the various knick-knacks of which the hobbits there are fond! No, I must maintain my stock at all times.

'But it's not easy! To get to Dwaling and Oatbarton, you have to travel south-east of Tinnudir and pass through the Barandalf; the sands and dunes of that place are now home to creatures vile and dangerous. On my last journey, I was quite surprised by the grasping tendrils of a number of sand-lurkers -- I barely managed to escape with my life and my inventory!

'That is unacceptable, ${PLAYER}. If you can defeat some of the sand-lurkers, I would greatly appreciate it and reward you in kind! You will find many of the creatures near Lithost, on the banks of the Brandywine. Lithost is on the other side of the river and slightly south of Barad Tharsír, if you are familiar with those ruins.''You have defeated the sand-lurkers of the Barandalf? I am glad to hear it and will reward you for your efforts accordingly!''The sand-lurkers can be found throughout the Barandalf south-east of here, but especially near Lithost. Lithost is on the opposite side of the Brandywine from Barad Tharsír.'A great host of Orcs spilled through Ram Dúath, driving the Men of the North Downs from their farms. Esteldín, home of the few remaining Dúnedain of the North, was spared discovery. It is from there that the Rangers begin to plan their counter-offensive against the black tide of Angmar.Mincham has a small camp overlooking the Greenway just south of the Fields of Fornost.

Daervunn returned the orders to you and sent you to speak with Mincham, who is proficient in the Black Speech of Mordor, at his camp bordering the Fields of Fornost in the west. He hopes that Mincham will be able to translate the orders.Ask Mincham to translate the Uruk-ordersDaervunn awaits your return at Esteldín.

Mincham returned the orders to you and sent you back to Daervunn with his report.'We are close to finding out the intentions of the invading Orcs, friend. We need to learn what is written upon these pages. While there are none currently here in Esteldín that can decipher these notes, there is a Ranger in the North Downs who can.

'Mincham has long studied the Enemy and their Black Speech. Would you carry the orders to him at his camp in the west? He is encamped near the Fields of Fornost at the south-west edge.

'Hurry, ${PLAYER}. Time is of the essence.''We must learn what is written on those pages, ${PLAYER}. We must know how strong their forces are and what their true intentions are within the North Downs.''I smelled the foul documents when you made your way into my camp. Esteldín needs me again, I see. Give them here.

'These are indeed orders, orders to dig trenches. It also mentions a leader in the south and another who made his way into Fornost. This news is troublesome, friend. The Orcs are organized under a unified leadership. The North Downs was a prize to claim for Angmar, and the Orc-tribes each want to win the prize, but despite their nature they are working together.

'Their assault on Trestlebridge and burning the fields was a diversion so they could enter Fornost. I wonder what they desire there? Quickly return to Daervunn.''The Black Speech is a foul and vile tongue, but I suppose my knowledge of it will aid my kindred....''We must form a plan and strike quickly. If there are Uruk-leaders amongst the Orcs, then we must find them and end them before they claim the North Downs. This is no time for hesitation.

'I will see that Halbarad is informed of what you learned, ${PLAYER}. Thank you for your assistance. Please take this reward for your efforts.'Cudhaer is working on repairing the catapult, a sensitive and timely process.Scouts can be found in the stands of trees south-west and south-east of the bluff overlooking the colossus.

Cudhaer asked you to flush out several scouts while he repairs the catapult.Defeat scouts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cudhaer is at the bluff overlooking the colossus from the south.

Cudhaer will be pleased to hear the scouts have been driven off and his position is secure.Talk to Cudhaer near the colossusSardan is at the Colossus, north of the catapult's bluff.

Sardan will want to know the catapult is repaired and ready to fire.'I am going to begin repairing the catapult. This is going to take me some time, for while you have gathered the correct parts, they will need some adjustments in order to fit properly.

'This is going to be a loud process, and I recently saw some scouts searching through the stands of trees surrounding us. If they were to find us, they would quickly overrun this bluff and destroy the catapult. This is something we cannot afford.

'Head out into the trees south east and south west of here and flush the scouts out so we can be sure this position is secure.''So they are gone? Just in time too. I have finished making the repairs, and we are ready to fire. 

'Head down to Sardan at the base of the colossus and let him know we are ready to launch the ropes.''I already know what you are here to tell me, seems Cudhaer has a quick trigger finger and his message beat you here.''If those scouts find us, we are done for...make sure you find them first.''Sardan is down at the feet of the Colossus. He will want to give the signal himself.'Unlike the goblins, the Orcs have been raised with a martial outlook and form the backbone of the Enemy's armies. They are neither cowards nor weaklings -- though they cannot hope to match the martial prowess of the Elves -- but their numbers and war industry are vast when motivated by their shadowy lords.Defeat OrcsExplore the Fields of Fornost, the villages of the Earth-kin, and the many ruins that lie forgotten in lands of the North Downs.Explore the Villages of the Earth-kinExplore the Eastern RuinsExplore the StrongholdsExplore the Western RuinsThe very waters of Agamaur have risen up to protect Ivar and the Red-maid, animated by fell spirits of Morgoth from beyond the world.Darkwater can be found within the area of Garth Agarwen.  

Hartrím asked that you assist him in appeasing the spirit at Agamaur, Dannasen, by destroying the fell spirits corrupting the lands within the area of Garth Agarwen.Defeat gloomwaters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hartrím can be found at the entrance to the area of Agamaur called Garth Agarwen.

The darkwater have the power to strike fear even within the cursed shades of Arthedain, preventing them from advancing on Garth Agarwen and fulfilling their curse. At Hartrim's request, you went forth to destroy them and met with some success. You should return to Hartrím with the report of your victory.Talk to Hartrím'Dost thou fear us? Do not so; there is naught for thee to fear. We have dwelt too long in this Middle-earth, too long without our rightful rest. Yet here we remain, shackled by a debt unpaid, an oath unfulfilled. Would thou lend us thy aid?

'In Garth Agarwen, fell spirits stir the waters of Agamaur and take form. Many are spirits from beyond the world, such as those that stir the wights, but the worst of them are the cursed spirits of mortal Men, Men of Númenor who committed a great act of evil against the Valar before Númenór fell and were cursed by the Valar themselves.

'Would thou assist us by destroying the fell darkwater?''Though I fear it, I think that the spirit at our encampment in Agamaur speaks true. There are ancient and evil things in this land.

'Beyond these doors you will find fell spirits taking on the form of the water that drowns this land in eternal corruption. The spirit, Dannasen, told me that ridding these creatures from the land may aid in the restoration of the natural order.

'You will find the spirits throughout this area and even within the bounds of the fortress and arboretum, though I think that you might find them throughout the early ruins of Garth Agarwen as well.''The spirits of the Enemy haunt even the waters of Agamaur.''Dannasen said that the very waters of Agamaur rose against them and fought them back in ages past. Since that time, he and his brethren have longed to rest but have been denied the ability. Perhaps we can help them....''Speak with Hartrím... he will know what you should do.''${PLAYER}, this service is great indeed. If the shades are able to fulfil their curse, then that will mean that the land here will begin to heal.

'This was a crucial first step to seeing that happen. Thank you.'Dannasen said that you should consult with Radagast the Brown. Perhaps he could give mercy to purify the water and provide a chance to redeem the Red-maid.Radagast is within his tower in the Eglan-camp in Agamaur.

Dannasen instructed you to present the full Urn of Agamaur to Radagast the Brown and ask him to purify the water within so you might restore the Red-maid.Dannasen is at the Eglan-camp in Agamaur.

You should return to Dannasen and tell him that Radagast has purified the water from Agamaur with a sister's love.'The Red-maid's sisters are as wide as the world, for each way the water flows there are River-maidens. To call the Red-maid back to nature thou shalt need to enlist the closest friend to nature, Radagast the Brown.

'The winds tell that he is close by. Perhaps he will find a way to give the land a chance to commune with their mother-sister and let light back into the heart of the Red-maid.''Take the urn to Radagast. Long has he walked the woods and knows all the creatures of the world equally.''The Red Swamp and its inhabitants know that there is terror here, ${PLAYER}. The water you hold is the source, or close to this source. I know this corruption, but I cannot see the face or hear the sister's voice. She is as strange to me as one I have never seen or known.

'I do, however, know one like her, and so I shall do as you ask.'

Radagast produces a small phial and drops a single drop into the urn. 'She has her sister's love... may this tear heal her pain and offer her forgiveness. She is lost and forgotten, but if you can feed the place where she dwells this pure water as she was fed the water which poisoned her, she may one day be known to me again. Go now, ${PLAYER} speak with the cursed men and save the River-daughter.''Return to Dannasen, ${PLAYER}.''We near the fulfillment of our oath to Iarwain Ben-adar, and we have thee to thank, ${PLAYER}. Radagast's assistance will hearken that bit of the Red-maid which remains pure.

'Thou hast proven to be a great ally to all within this land.'Radagast the Brown told you a little of the land that has become the Red Swamp and of the fall of the Red-maid. He also spoke of Oakenbark, a great tree that once grew there, now called Grimbark.Grimbark can be found at Garth Agarwen in the north of Agamaur, in the Lone-lands. Radagast is at Barad Dhorn, in the Eglan-camp.

Radagast has asked you to bring him acorns from Grimbark's branches, so that he may plant them in the Old Forest, reclaiming some small part of the kinswoman's domain.'Good ${RACE}, will you stay and speak with me a moment? I would tell you a sad tale and ask a favour of you before you go.

'Long ago, a kinswoman dwelt by a lake in a land lush and green, where great fields of wildflowers and an ancient forest once grew. The tides of war swept through, and the waters of the lake ran red with blood. The lakelands decayed and became the Red Swamp, and the kinswoman the Red-maid of Agamaur.

'It saddens me, but I do not believe that either the kinswoman or her land may be saved, but at the least, some part of the land may be reclaimed. A great tree once grew there, which was called Oakenbark. It is twisted and corrupted now, but if it still produces acorns, they might be used to bring new life, uncorrupted, to the Old Forest. Go to Garth Agarwen, to the east, and fetch me the seeds of the tree that is now Grimbark.''These seeds share the corruption of the tree which produced them, but I believe they may still be reclaimed. Thanks to you, a little part of Oakenbark shall grow to be free of the Red-maid's corruption.'Crawlers like to bury themselves beneath rocks.Lithost is south of the colossus, on the west bank of the Baranduin.

Herthdúr asked you to go to Lithost, flip over some rocks, and defeat the crawlers you find.Flip over rocks and defeat the crawlers beneath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Herthdúr is at the colossus camp, north of Lithost.

Herthdúr will be pleased to hear the crawlers' population has been reduced.Talk to Herthdúr at the colossus camp'There is a small ruin on the west side of the Barandalf called Lithost. It is nothing more than a shell of the walls now, but I feel we should make an effort everywhere we can.

'Crawlers have filled these ruins. This normally would not be a terrible thing, nature is generally kind, however these crawlers are diggers. They burrow holes throughout the ancient bricks and stone. In time they could bring down this very colossus.

'Head to Lithost and search out the crawlers. The ones we are interested in stopping will not be on the surface. Flip over some rocks and defeat whatever you find beneath them.''You will have to flip over the rocks to find the ones we want... they will not be out in the open.''We will still need to get down there at some point to reinforce the walls, but you have provided us some much needed time. Thank you.'Bob Greeneaves believes he has discovered the plans of the goblins.The goblin-camp is in the Bleakleaf Crest, south of Dwaling.

Bob Greeneaves wants you to burn the pots the goblins are using to cook down the spiders, as well as recover the recipe for their tar.Collect the hobgoblins' recipeBurn bubbling pots with torches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bob Greeneaves is in Dwaling, north of the Bleakleaf Crest.

You should return to Bob Greeneaves with the news of your success.'You see, I think I finally figured out what those goblins are up to. It was the presence of the hobgoblins that cleared it up for me. Stories always said that they were the worst of the lot, scheming up new ways to hurt and kill folk! I think they are taking the spiders and cooking them down, making them into that tar of theirs that they throw! Never heard of them using spiders' innards for such a thing, but wouldn't surprise me a bit.

'They are probably sore from their defeat south of here and are itching for another fight. But this time they plan on burning hobbits out of house and hole! You've got to put a stop to this!

'You should head back to the Bleakleaf Crest and burn the pots they are cooking the spiders in -- they'll probably have torches or such nearby that you can use. And you'll need to take the recipe for the tar away from them as well, or burning the pots won't do any good. Defeat one of them hobgoblins out there and search him for the recipe!''What are you doing about here? Those hobgoblins are up to no good! You should burn their pots to get rid of the foul stuff they've made so far. And get rid of the recipe to make more of the tar.''Good, you've done it! I'll take that recipe there and destroy it. That should keep them from making any more of the foul stuff.

'Never would have thought I'd be saving spiders from a horrible death. Well, if you don't tell anyone, I won't either!'Hob Hillbrow has used his spare key to let you into the big hole in Dwaling.  He has asked that you deliver the formal complaint drafted by Ronald Dwale to the ruffians, being forceful if necessary.Hob Hillbrow has asked you to confront Will Tuffin, the leader of the Big Folk in Dwaling, with his letter.Deliver the formal complaintElrond of Rivendell must be told of the shadow that rises in Angmar.Half-orcs harry travellers on the road through the Lone-lands.

Candaith was right: the road to Rivendell is dangerous.Defeat the half-orcs on the road (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Galasebdir, a Ranger of your acquaintance, has caught up to you on the road through the Lone-lands.Talk to Galasebdir on the road through the Lone-landsYou have travelled with Galasebdir into the woods of the Trollshaws.Talk to Galasebdir on the road through the TrollshawsYou have travelled with Galasebdir into the woods of the Trollshaws.Talk to Galasebdir on the road through the TrollshawsYou have climbed up through the high moor with Galasebdir and are now near to the hidden vale of Rivendell.Talk to Galasebdir on the road through the High MoorAt the entrance to Rivendell, the Elf-warrior Bregoleth is pleased to see you.Talk to BregolethBregoleth told you a friend waits for you further along the path that descends into Rivendell.Find the friend of whom Bregoleth spoke further along the pathIt would seem Boromir's own journey came to a successful end, and the soldier of Gondor is pleased to see you.Talk to BoromirBoromir wants to know what transpired after the three of you parted ways.Walk with Boromir and tell him what transpired after you partedYou told Boromir some of what befell you and Meneldir after the three of you parted, and the soldier of Gondor has spent much of the walk in silence.Talk to Boromir by the Last Homely HouseGandalf the Grey has greeted you outside the Last Homely House, desiring to speak with you.Talk to Gandalf the Grey outside the Last Homely HouseElrond, the master of the house, desires to hear your story.Talk to Elrond outside the Last Homely House'I have not seen you since you departed Sarn Ford in the company of Halbarad, ${PLAYER}! When he returned to us he told us what befell Meneldir beneath Dor Ernil, and we wept for him, despite his past. But evil stirs in the land and there is little time to mourn. What brings you into the Lone-lands?'

You tell Galasebdir that you seek to bring word to Elrond of Rivendell that the Witch-king and his servants have evil designs upon the realm of Angmar. The Ranger thinks for a moment.

'I had planned to return to Tornhad in the Angle of Mitheithel, ${PLAYER}, but your journey feels to be of great import to me. I would accompany you on this quest, if you would have me. The road to Rivendell is like to be dangerous.'

You tell Galasebdir that his company would be welcome as you make for Rivendell.

'Then we are agreed. Lead on, my friend.''There it is again, ${PLAYER}, among the trees just off the road. Do you hear that rustling? It sounds to me like something large... pretending to be small.

'A troll, to be certain. Is it beyond hope to think we might pass its notice?''Trolls are not known to be clever, ${PLAYER}, and for the most part they do not hunt together. It may have been misfortune alone that we had to face two in succession, or perhaps the days grow darker and the rising threat of the Shadow makes the creatures grow more bold.

'We should proceed. Soon we will need to climb, and the footing may become treacherous.''I have only been to Rivendell once, in the company of my chieftain Aragorn, but I believe we are nearly there. Soon we will see the Elves that guard the pass, and you will bring your warning of the shadow that rises in Angmar to Elrond.''You are welcome to Imladris, ${PLAYER}, and my heart sings to see you safe. Scouts saw you and Galasebdir of the Dúnedain as you climbed to the high moor, and they brought word to me and to others in the vale as you neared the pass. I begged leave of Lord Elrond that I be the one to greet you.

'You did not make this journey to give him the tidings of Saruman I have already delivered, I trust. No, I see in your eye there is something else. I told you once we stood on the very precipice of danger, and the events of recent days have shown to me I was more right than I knew!

'Others await your arrival in Imladris. One such friend made haste to greet you at the entrance, but it seems he has not yet arrived. Look for him further along the path.''I see your own journey finds its end in the same northern dale as does mine, ${PLAYER}! When I heard the Elves calling among themselves that a ${RACE} neared Rivendell in the company of a Ranger, I knew that you and Meneldir approached! But where is he? Why does he tarry?'

Boromir sees something in your expression and falls silent.

'His journey too has ended, is that it? Tell me what befell him as we walk, ${PLAYER}. It is still a long way to the house of Elrond.''He was a good man and brave, ${PLAYER}, and he died fighting evil. May each of us do the same, if that is where our roads must lead.

'Elrond awaits.''Yes, I know something of the road you took to get here, ${PLAYER}, and some of the tidings you bring. Not all, no! But some. Folk of different lands have come to Rivendell in recent days, and I see in that a hopeful sign. Counsels will be taken in the days to come, and decisions of great import must be made.

'But this is not my house. You should speak with Elrond, its master, and give to him the message you have brought across so many miles.

'I will see you again, ${PLAYER}.''Welcome to Rivendell, ${PLAYER}. This is a place of safety and of refuge, and you are welcome to take what rest you can within the vale. But first, are you willing to tell me what brings you here? Many are the dangers you have faced to get here, and if your tale is like others I have heard of late, I believe they signify trouble ahead for the peoples of Middle-earth.'

You tell Elrond you have much to tell, and he nods, a thoughtful expression on his face.Radagast knows the ancient evil of the Gaunt-lord Ivar, called the Blood-hand, who is raising an army of wights in Agamaur.Ivar may be found in Garth Agarwen in Agamaur, within the Lone-lands.

Radagast is unable to directly interfere in the war by seeking out and destroying Ivar the Bloodhand. He has asked you to go in his stead and to bring him Ivar's helm as proof of your victory.'You have made quite the name for yourself, ${PLAYER}. Word of your exploits has reached me even here. Indeed, I am surprised with all you have accomplished. Still, perhaps you can assist me again.

'I know what began the plague in Agamaur, the Red Swamp. A Gaunt-lord, a dire creature in the service of Angmar, has raised an army of wights here and has stirred this ancient evil, not the Eglain. I must ask you to intervene on my behalf, for I cannot. Would you go in my stead?

'In the east are the ruins called Garth Agarwen. I would search there for Ivar the Blood-hand. If you find him and defeat him, bring his helm to me as proof.''You have made quite the name for yourself, ${PLAYER}. Word of your exploits has reached me even here. Indeed, I am surprised with all you have accomplished. Still, perhaps you can assist me again.

'I know what began the plague in Agamaur, the Red Swamp. A Gaunt-lord, a dire creature in the service of Angmar, has raised an army of wights here and has stirred this ancient evil, not the Eglain. I must ask you to intervene on my behalf, for I cannot. Would you go in my stead?

'In the east are the ruins called Garth Agarwen. I would search there for Ivar the Blood-hand. If you find him and defeat him, bring his helm to me as proof.''What is this, ${PLAYER}? The helm of Ivar the Blood-hand? Long has my order awaited this day.

'In ages past, Morgoth twisted enslaved Men and instilled them with fell spirits. These became the gaunt-men, but among them he selected five who would bear unimaginable power in mockery of the Wizards.

'Ivar was, in fact, made in mockery of me, and his death serves as justice for my kind. You have well earned this reward, ${PLAYER}.'You found a dull ancient ring within the remains of crawler. A Ranger at the colossus would be interested in seeing this.The colossus camp is north of the Barandalf.

One of the Rangers at the colossus camp may be interested in seeing the ring you have found.Find a Ranger at the colossus camp to speak with about the ring you foundThe ring is dull, and the design feels ancient. You cannot discern anything else about it. You should show it to one of the Rangers at the colossus camp.'You found this within a crawler, you say? I wonder how it got there....'While digging through the dunes, the crawlers may have devoured other ancient pieces of jewellery.Crawlers can be found in the Barandalf, south of the colossus.

Herthdúr asked you to search crawlers within the Barandalf for pieces of jewellery.Collect pieces of jewellery from crawlers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Herthdúr is at the colossus, north of the Barandalf.

Herthdúr will be eager to see the ancient jewellery you have reclaimed from the sands.'I examined the ring you found, and it is certainly from the old kingdom. It must have somehow gone overboard from a ship carrying it. The tide slowly carried it to shore where it was buried within the dunes, forgotten until a crawler accidentally devoured it.

'There were many ships that travelled the waters, and many storms that harried or wrecked them, particularly along this stretch of water. I wonder how many other pieces of jewellery fell overboard and made their way into the sands?

'There is one way to know. Head back to where you found this piece and search the crawlers there for other pieces of jewellery. If any of them swallowed one, odds are more of them have as well. Return to me with whatever you find.''The jewels you found could have belonged to one of the royal families.''These are extraordinary. They will need some cleaning and polish, but the workmanship is exquisite. These can teach us a lot about how to place intricate detail into rings and earrings.'Dannasen's desire to be avenged upon the Hillmen of Rhudaur is shared by Emelin, the leader of the shades of Arthedain who dwell in the southern bog.The Hillmen of Rhudaur occupy the ancient ruins called Garth Agarwen in the north-east of Agamaur. Adherents follow the Red-maid but are not truly Hillmen, they are drawn from the the Grim Créoth, Créoth Apprentices and the Créoth Disciples. The devoted are drawn from the Créoth Devout and Créoth Wardens. Lastly, the masters are drawn from the Créoth Chieftains and Leaders.

The shade explained that the Rhudaur were responsible for raising the Red-maid, an evil creature of great power, and were in some way also responsible for the curse that holds the shades of Arthedain in Middle-earth. He has asked you to destroy the Hillmen, allowing him and his brethren to fulfil their curse.Defeat Créoth apprentices and disciples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Créoth wardens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Créoth chieftains (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Emelin resides with his brethren in the southernmost reaches of the Harloeg.

You were sent to destroy the Hillmen of Rhudaur who dwell within Garth Agarwen. Your victory was not complete, but you struck a telling blow against the Hillmen. Emelin should be told of your efforts.'In years long past, the Angmarim and the Hillmen of Rhudaur did war upon the Kingdom of Arthedain, my people. It was an unjust war and the cause of much needless bloodshed, but the Hillmen worshipped the Witch-king of Angmar and did whatever he bade them. And so it was that they made sacrifices to him and defiled the land until the waters themselves seemed as blood.

'And so arose the Red-maid. We knew not what she was, nor what she had been. Many even among the Hillmen fled from the creature, but many also remained and worshipped her.

'The Hillmen caused this creature to come into being, and our curse as well. Justice is long overdue. Destroy them, and we shall fulfil our curse. They guard Garth Agarwen, the fortress of the Red-maid in the north-east of Agamaur.''In years long past, the Angmarim and the Hillmen of Rhudaur did war upon the Kingdom of Arthedain, my people. It was an unjust war and the cause of much needless bloodshed, but the Hillmen worshipped the Witch-king of Angmar and did whatever he bade them. And so it was that they made sacrifices to him and defiled the land until the waters themselves seemed as blood.

'And so arose the Red-maid. We knew not what she was, nor what she had been. Many even among the Hillmen fled from the creature, but many also remained and worshipped her.

'The Hillmen caused this creature to come into being, and our curse as well. Justice is long overdue. Destroy them, and we shall fulfil our curse. They guard Garth Agarwen, the fortress of the Red-maid in the north-east of Agamaur.''The Hillmen were traitors in my day, and even they remain so.''Speak now with Emelin in Harloeg.''${PLAYER}, again thou dost hearten me to return to my fight against the Red-maid. To find rest we must redeem the Red-maid or destroy her. I wish to see her redeemed, but as long as these men continue their bloody rites, it cannot be so.'The Men of Tinnudir used to trade with the hobbits of Oatbarton and Dwaling, mostly desiring glassware and pipe-weed.The Ranger-camp is believed to be at the colossus guarding a bridge where Lake Evendim flows into the Brandywine.

Hob Hillbrow is worried about the Rangers to the north, having heard nothing from them in a great deal of time. He would like you to seek them out.Talk to a Ranger at the colossus in Evendim'You have done much to help us, and for that, I am grateful. I feel it will be a long time before Dwaling feels like the rolling hills of the Greenfields, but we will continue to do our best.

'One thing that does concern me is the Big Folk to the north. Not the ones in town, but the ones beyond that live by the big lake. They were friendly to us. They used to come down to trade for pipe-weed and glass. Once or twice, I ventured up to see them.

'They used to make a camp at a bridge that crosses the river, where a giant Man of stone watches all the boats that pass. Would you venture up there and see if they are well?''I do worry about the Big Folk... well, at least the nice ones.''Hob Hillbrow is a name I have not heard in some time. I do miss him and his little jests, but I am pleased to hear that he is doing well. When a scout reported seeing Men in Dwaling, I feared the worst. Thank you for helping them with their situation.

'It is precious to think that in these dark times a hobbit is concerned about the welfare of Man. If only he knew how dire things have truly become. I envy the simple folk.'You have found a familiar face as a prisoner within Garth Agarwen: the strange old woman, Sara Oakheart.Follow Sara Oakheart out of Garth Agarwen and protect her along the way! She seems to have been captured by Ivar and the wights.Talk to Sara OakheartNow that you have successfully escorted Sara Oakheart to safety, you should speak with someone at the Eglan-camp.Tell someone at the Eglain camp about Sara's rescue'It is a great and wonderous thing that you have rescued me, ${PLAYER}. I had feared I would be lost here forever.

'I'm not sure that I can make it out on my own.  Do you think you could help me get to the front gates of Garth Agarwen? 

'Perhaps if you follow me, I can find a way out of this maze.''Hurry! Let's get out of here before more of them come. 

'Follow me!''You found who?  Oakheart?  Never heard the name, I'm afraid.  But an older woman matching your description did, in fact, come by here and dropped some things off.  I suppose she must have left them for you.'Once the loyal caretakers to the elder Huorns of the great wood, the bog-lurkers of Agamaur are a fallen and corrupt group of beings beyond saving. They have learned to relish the blood of the living, and in so doing, have become the very evil they once stopped from spreading through the lands.Once noble guardians of the forest, the bog-lurkers in the Arboretum of Garth Agarwen are now twisted and terrible creatures thriving on blood. They must be slain for they cannot be saved.Defeat bog-lurkers in the Arboretum (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})When Men first settled in these lands, the Hillmen of Rhudaur settled Garth Agarwen and treated nature as they should. As time passed and the influence of the Witch-king spread throughout the kingdom, the Men fell to corruption and turned against nature, poisoning the land and its caretakers.Créoth watch the entrance to the barrow of Garth Agarwen at the behest of the Gaunt Lord, Ivar. They are the first line of defence and a formidable opponent. These Men, loyal to the corrupt being that their ancestors helped to create, are without conscience and filled with malice. Their redemption is unlikely, and it falls to you, in this place, to defeat them.Defeat Créoth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Within the fortress at Garth Agarwen, you will find the main force of the Créoth Hillmen loyal to the Red-maid. It is these Men, descendents of the Rhudaur, who poisoned the land and drove the once peaceful Red-maid mad.You should hunt through the fortress and the ruins for the Créoth Hillmen. Purge the true-blooded minions of Angmar and punish them for defiling the natural order in the Lone-lands.Defeat Créoth Hillmen within the Garth Agarwen fortress (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lurking beneath the surface of the reflective red pools of Agamaur are fell spirits from ancient times. These spirits manifest in the form of darkwater. These terrifying spirits lay waste to all living things that they happen upon and are servants, not only of Angmar, but also the darker forces of the world.Terrifying foes of ancient times, the darkwater use water to form themselves into horrible creatures of darkness. They do not suffer the living and must be destroyed wherever they are found.Defeat darkwater in the Arboretum (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The pool within the fortress of Garth Agarwen is full of darkwater and their leader. Cleansing this pool will do well to assist the land in recovering from the corruption that has for so long seeped into every facet of the land.You should force the darkwater from their hiding places within the pool of the Garth Agarwen fortress and confront their master, Raugzdrok.Defeat darkwater within the Garth Agarwen fortress (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat RaugzdrokNot all Dead are reanimated bodies returned from the grave to lay waste to the innocent and pure. Some take whatever form they can, even if it is only part of a whole. When no body can be found, the fell spirits will seek another refuge and finding only an arm, or other body part, they will inhabit the creation and destroy life through this means.Decayed can be found within unlikely locations, lurking in the crevasses and darkness of stone corridors throughout the barrows. Exposing these creations will require you to spring their trap, but do not be surprised; keep your wits about you, and you will destroy these vile creations.Defeat the decayed (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})In the short-lived time before Rhudaur fell beneath the sway of Angmar, there were three champions and a king who led the Men of Rhudaur with honour and justice. In the Arboretum, the three were interred in a place where the peaceful woods would watch over them and the people could visit them in quiet reflection.The Garth Agarwen Arboretum was home to many important locations. These included the tombs of three of their champions: Fuirvaw, Hargram, and Halvegil, a statue to their first king, Nailion, and the founding stone that served as the marker for the bounds of their land: the Stone of Mingond.Discover the Memorial of NailionDiscover the Stone of MingondDiscover Fuirvaw's resting placeDiscover Hargam's resting placeDiscover Halvegil's resting placeNot all Men of Rhudaur were Men of evil. Some were heroes or Men of noble deeds in their time. They are lost now, within the mire of this land and lost to the corruption of their people. Still, they exist in unmarked graves in these barrows.Though the Rhudaur fell quickly, they had heroes, and not all the Rhudaur were part of the malignancy that crept into the land. There are five such heroes entombed within the Garth Agarwen barrows. You should visit their graves. If for no other reason than to know that these Men were not the culprits behind the great tragedy of this land.Discover the first unmarked tombDiscover the second unmarked tombDiscover the third unmarked tombDiscover the fourth unmarked tombDiscover the fifth unmarked tombGarth Agarwen is overrun by the servants of the Red-maid and Ivar the Blood-hand. Some of these servants are living entities corrupted by the horror wrought upon them by the fallen Men of Rhudaur, others are the Dead. Wights, animated through the dark sorcery of Ivar the Blood-hand, spread corruption to the land with every footfall. In the Arboretum, they befoul the earth and cause all within to suffer the pain of corruption.Wights within the Arboretum continue to spread corruption to the flora and fauna in the area. To begin healing the land these foul creatures must be cut out of the world.Defeat wights in the Arboretum (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The time of Men is not so long, and the time of Rhudaur before its fall to corruption and alliance with Angmar was shorter still. Heroes, warriors, sacrifices, thieves, villains, and many others were buried in the barrows near the Red-maid's spring. Here, the Gaunt-lord Ivar has chosen to begin raising his army.Wights patrol the befouled land in an endless journey through the mire. They wait until they will be called on by their dread lord, Ivar, to make war upon the living and began their campaign of corruption through the land. It need not be this way. Armed as you are, you can aid the Free Peoples of the world by ridding the land of these foul creatures.Defeat wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Since the arrival of Angmar, the Dead have occupied this land and their leader Vatar has existed since the Red-maid began to slip beyond the reach of nature.You should cleanse the ruins of Garth Agarwen of the wights who have so long stood a vigil over the continued corruption of the land. With them removed, perhaps healing can begin within the swamp of Agamaur.Defeat wights within the Garth Agarwen fortress (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hal Gamgee told you of a camp of Rangers on the island of Tinnudir, north of the Shire and beyond the Brandywine.The Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir is north of Oatbarton along the road that runs through the Barandalf. Hal told you to follow the road north across the Brandywine until you reach a cross-roads and then to follow the westward road until you reach Tinnudir.

Hal Gamgee has mentioned the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir in Lake Evendim and suggested that you speak to the Ranger Calenglad.'Do you know what? I've had a conversation or two with those strange Big Folk, those Rangers, up on the moors. Queer folk, to be sure, but they always spare an ear for any news I might be willing to give to 'em.

'They have a camp a good ways north, on an island off the shore of Lake Evendim they call Tinnudir. It seems that if you follow the road northwards across the Barandalf sand-marshes and across the Brandywine, you'll come to a cross-roads. If you take the left fork -- that'll be the westward road -- you'll eventually come to Tinnudir.

'Or so this Calenglad fellow tells me. I've never been there myself. If you do find your feet taking you up that way, be sure to give him a "hullo" from me, Hal Gamgee!''The Ranger-camp I've heard tell of is on an island called Tinnudir off the shore of Lake Evendim, far to the north. Just follow the road through the Barandalf sand-marshes and across the Brandywine and then take the westward road when you reach the cross-roads. Give Calenglad my greetings when you arrive!''Hal Gamgee always has an interesting story to tell. He is unusually stout-hearted for one of his folk -- perhaps it runs in his family.

'Make yourself useful around Tinnudir or move along. We have many concerns at the moment and cannot allow for sloth in Evendim.'Harmony Grayson has volunteered to help the Rangers collect relics from the ruins of Barad Tharsír.Barad Tharsír is south-east of Tinnudir, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the Brandywine.

Harmony Grayson wants you to bring her any relics that you can find from Barad Tharsír.Harmony Grayson is at the Ranger camp on the island of Tinnudir, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

You have gathered several relics from the ruins of Barad Tharsír and should return to Harmony Grayson.Ranger Astiul is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

Harmony Grayson has asked you to bring the relics you collected to Astiul, as he will appreciate their significance.Harmony Grayson is in the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, north-west of Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

Astiul has expressed his gratitude for your efforts and wants you to thank Harmony for him.'I want to help the Rangers here, ${PLAYER}. They have gone through so much hardship during the long years...they deserve now our aid and friendship.

'To that end, I have been keeping an eye on the ruins of Barad Tharsír, in the Barandalf far to the south-east. The ruins are on the far side of the river Brandywine. The Ranger Astiul collects pottery, relics, and other artifacts that once belonged to his people, and I have offered to help him with this task.

'If you can recover certain relics from Barad Tharsír, I know that Astiul will be pleased. Do not worry yourself with the condition they may be in, or how worn by time: broken swords, shields, or bottles will be equally well-received. Look for these things among the ruins and beware of the robbers that now dwell there, unconcerned for the history of the Dúnedain.''Astiul is so well-spoken, like most of his people. Thank you for his kind words, ${PLAYER}!

'As I promised, I will reward you for your efforts. Thank you again, and do what you can to aid the people here and in all of Evendim!''Barad Tharsír is south-east of Tinnudir, on the far side of the Brandywine, in the Barandalf. If you search the grounds you are sure to find old relics of the sort for which I have asked.''Astiul is just over there, ${PLAYER}. Bring him the relics you recovered from Barad Tharsír.''These relics do not seem of any real value to my eyes, but bring them to Astiul. He is here at the camp and will be pleased to have them.''These are from Barad Tharsír? Thank you very much for collecting them. It saddens me to know that the tomb-robbers of Evendim have taken that place, but there are too few of us left to protect every Dúnedain site.

'Tell Harmony I appreciate her help -- and yours -- with this matter.'The Eglain depend upon the trade of ancient relics for food and other necessities of life. Arienh believes that Garth Agarwen holds such a wealth of relics that her people will be sustained for many years.Arienh is encamped at the entrance to the area called Garth Agarwen. She asked that you search the areas outside of the barrows, arboretum, and fortress of Garth Agarwen for trinkets that will help provide for the Eglain.

Arienh asked that you watch for relics of the ancient days as you venture within Garth Agarwen.'Hail, ${PLAYER}! Word has reached our small band here of your assistance to Elsa and Eriac. We think well of you and your deeds.

'As you know, the Eglain depend upon the trade of ancient relics for food and other necessities. I can think of no better place in these swamps to find the relics of the old kingdoms than here in Garth Agarwen. The wealth of relics within could sustain my people for years to come. Should you venture beyond these doors, but not too deeply into the barrows, fortress, or arboretum, I would ask that you remain watchful for small trinkets amongst debris. My people could make use of anything you may find.

'This task, of course, would not go unrewarded. Be careful upon your journeys, friend.''Sometimes, my friend, it is a pleasant surprise not to be proven wrong. There are more, you say? Yes, ${PLAYER}, the wealth of this place would serve my people well.

'First, however, we must ensure that the Red-maid is destroyed. There are enough relics among the other ruins to help feed my people, but Agamaur's corruption will surely swallow them should the Red-maid's evil remain unchecked.'The forces of Angmar left behind a catapult on the bluff overlooking the colossus bridge. The Rangers intend to use it.Bundles of rope, pieces of driftwood, small flywheels, and oil pouches can be found along the banks of the Baranduin.

Sardan asked you to gather some supplies to help fix a catapult.Collect bundles of rope (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect pieces of driftwood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect small flywheels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect oil-pouches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cudhaer is on a bluff overlooking the colossus from the south.

Cudhaer will be eager to use these supplies to repair the catapult.'On the bluff above us is a catapult that Angmar brought along on its way to the city. We managed to damage it enough that they left it behind, and now we intend to repair it and use it ourselves.

'It certainly won't reach Annúminas, but that is not our goal. We wish to use it to fling ropes over the colossus. That will allow us to climb and begin repairing the areas we cannot currently reach. 

'Search the debris scattered along the banks of the river and see if you can find the following supplies: Several bundles of rope, some pieces of driftwood, a few small flywheels, and a couple of pouches of oil. Once you have found them, bring them to Cudhaer at the catapult on the bluff just to the south of here.''You should be able to find all you need along the banks of the river. Useful things tend to be strewn along them.''Ha! I had heard that someone was coming to help me out, but I did not know you would have everything with you as well. This is a very fortuitous event!'You have recovered a number of pottery shards and other relics from the jetsam that washes up on the shores of Lake Nenuial, and now Ranger Astiul wants you to sort the valuable pieces from the rest.Different-sized relics and bits of pottery are lying on the mats by Astiul at Tinnudir, on Parth Aduial in Evendim.

Astiul has asked you to sort the pottery fragments and relics you collected from the shores of Lake Neniul by size on the mats he uses for that purpose.Sort small relics on the matSort medium relics on the matSort large relics on the matAstiul is at Tinnudir, on Parth Aduial in Evendim.

Ranger Astiul will be pleased that you sorted out the relics and pottery shards you collected from the shores of Lake Nenuial.'You have recovered quite a few artifacts and bits of pottery from the shores of the lake, ${PLAYER}, so now we need to sort out the valuable pieces from the rest.

'I have been separating the pieces I have collected by size on each of these mats, so if you do the same I would appreciate it. Then I can examine them easily for clues to their history.

'It should not take long.''Just use these mats to sort out the pieces you collected, ${PLAYER}. It should not take very long.''Thank you for your help, ${PLAYER}! Naethmunn thinks such efforts are fruitless, and that any time spent out of battle with the tomb-robbers is time wasted, but I disagree. We must salvage whatever remnants of our history from them we can, whether through combat or the avoidance of it.

'You have a good heart and a helpful spirit, ${PLAYER}. I believe that you are indeed a friend of Aragorn, and I will tell Calenglad as much. Thank you again for your efforts.'Arienh knows that there is a leader amongst the Créoth in the ruins of Garth Agarwen, a terrifying woman who the Men revere as a conduit to the Red-maid.

She has asked that you disprove this fallacy by defeating the woman and proving to the Créoth that their faith is misplaced.Temair can be found before the entrances to the Arboretum and Fortress within the area of Garth Agarwen.

Arienh asked that you bring the leader of the Créoth, a terrible woman named Temair, to justice. She preaches within the confines of Garth Agarwen.Defeat Temair the DevotedReturn to Arienh at her encampment at the entrance to Garth Agarwen.

Temair was found exactly where Arienh told you she would be. Dispatching Temair proved simple enough and you should now return to Arienh.Return to Arienh'The Men who dwell beyond these walls are misguided and lost. So lost that they cannot be redeemed according to Radagast; their atrocities against nature are so great that there is no forgiveness. 

'They are lead by a woman. A terrible woman named Temair who they believe is a link to the Red-maid and because of this she is revered as something greater than they are. 

'I ask that you push beyond these walls and seek out this woman. Bring her down and prove these misguided Men that they have misplaced their faith.''It is good to know that she is gone. It is wrong of people to place themselves above others and claim divinity when doing so.

'You have our respect, ${PLAYER}.''You will find Temair before the entrances to the Fortress and Arboretum of Garth Agarwen. She preaches there by a large pyre. Seek her out and destroy her.'Elrond told you of Aragorn's youthful journey to Annúminas and has given his approval that Narsil be reforged.Aragorn is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Master Elrond told you of Aragorn's youth and agreed that Narsil must be reforged. He has decided on a name for the new sword: Andúril, Flame of the West.Talk to Aragorn in Rivendell'Aragorn came to us when he was only two years of age, his father Arathorn having been slain by the arrows of the Orcs. Gilraen feared for her child's safety and brought him to Imladris, seeking to keep the fact of his birth from the eyes of the Enemy. I bade Gilraen keep his true name and lineage secret, and she called him instead Estel, or "Hope."

'When he reached twenty years of age, I saw that he had come early to manhood, and I gave to him knowledge of his lineage and the name his father had given him: Aragorn, a strong name. Full of hope was he on that day, but he soon would be laid low with a love far above his station, for walking in the woods near sunset of the next day he encountered Arwen, my daughter, and was stricken with love for her.

'I do not know why he went to Annúminas, but I always believed he made the journey to prove to himself he was worthy of loving such a maid. He went to that ancient city, long-deserted, in the company of my sons and with Calenglad, a Ranger that came at times to Imladris. Calenglad did not return from Aragorn's journey to the city of his fathers, and when next I saw Aragorn, the experience had left its mark upon him.

'Return to Aragorn and tell him that I agree the time has come for Narsil to be reforged, and I will give to it a new name: Andúril, Flame of the West. We will have need of its light before the end.''Return to Aragorn and tell him of my decision. The time has come to reforge the sword as surely as Aragorn has himself already been reforged.''I am glad that Master Elrond agrees with my decision, for his counsel is near to my heart.

'It is true that I journeyed in my youth to Annúminas, the city of my fathers. Even then it had stood empty for long years, but it was once the shining jewel of my people, and I desired to see it with my own eyes. I thought too that if I recovered the Silithar that lay within Annúminas -- the only one of the stones to remain in this bend of the world -- I might reforge Narsil and prove myself worthy of the fate to which I had been born. Elladan and Elrohir accompanied me on my journey to Evendim, and my kinsman Calenglad also; he had sworn an oath to my father and renewed it in my name before we departed.

'Much of the city had already been reclaimed by the waters of Lake Nenuial when we arrived, and I despaired of finding the Silithar within the flooded tomb where it lay. But lo! The maiden Gwindeth appeared before us and charged me with a task to prove my worthiness of receiving the Silithar.

'My youthfulness betrayed me; though I had the form of a man, I had not wisdom or experience, and Gwindeth found me wanting. The Silithar was not to be mine that day. When I stood before the flooded passages of Elendil's empty tomb on the outskirts of Annúminas, I believed it right that Narsil be reforged; I could not understand why Gwindeth refused me access to the Silithar. Now I believe I understand.'Bingo is troubled by the dull conclusion to the treasure hunt, and wonders if this truly is The End Now, or if it is simply another riddle.Anlaf the Forlorn is the proprietor of the Forsaken Inn, and can be found behind the counter.

Bingo is disappointed that the treasure hunt has come to a disappointing end, and is convinced that there is more to the story.Talk to Anlaf the Forlorn in the Forsaken InnAnlaf the Forlorn has asked you to carry some of the supplies from outside the Forsaken Inn down to his cellar storage room.Pick up a box of goods outside the Forsaken InnAnlaf the Forlorn has asked you to carry some of the supplies from outside the Forsaken Inn down to his cellar storage room.Pick up a basket of eggs outside the Forsaken InnAnlaf the Forlorn will want to hear that you brought the supplies down to his storage room.Talk to Anlaf the Forlorn in the Forsaken InnBingo is in the Forsaken Inn, wrestling with the meaning of the three words on the parchment you recovered.Talk to Bingo in the Forsaken InnBingo has asked you to hold the three-word parchment before the fire in the Forsaken Inn.Bingo is in the Forsaken Inn, in the Lone-lands.Talk to Bingo in the Forsaken InnBingo told you that the parchment with THE END NOW written on it contained the location to go next, and he said he would meet you there once you figured it out!Find the location mentioned on the three-word parchment and meet Bingo thereYou deciphered the string of letters on the parchment and followed the clue to Ost Guruth. Bingo is here as well, and wishes to speak to you.Theodore Gorse came to Ost Guruth with Bingo, and is excited to meet whoever has been leaving you these riddles.Talk to Theodore Gorse outside the tent in Ost GuruthHaley Meadowsweet is the real Master Treasure Seeker, and is ready to speak with you in Ost Guruth.Talk to the Master Treasure SeekerTheodore Gorse is in front of Haley Meadowsweet's tent in Ost Guruth.Talk to Theodore Gorse outside the tent in Ost GuruthBingo is outside Haley Meadowsweet's tent in Ost Guruth.Talk to Bingo outside the tent in Ost Guruth'I have been puzzling over the parchment you found in the last strongbox, ${PLAYER}, the one that simply reads 'The End Now.' That cannot possibly be the end of the hunt, can it? There has to be more to this than we were seeing, there just has to be!

'Well, I will keep studying the words for clues, but I do not want the keeper of this place to think us ungrateful! Go to Anlaf and see if there is anything that needs doing, and if I learn anything useful about this disappointing paper I will let you know!''I will keep studying the words for clues, but I do not want the keeper of this place to think us ungrateful! Go to Anlaf and see if there is anything that needs doing, and if I learn anything useful about this disappointing paper I will let you know!''You wish to help out? Well, I will not turn down the offer. After all, you never know when the offers will stop, and my back is not as strong as it was in my youthful days!

'I have had problems with the supply carts from time to time. The inn is a good ways from Bree-town, and the road can be treacherous in poor weather. Still, my most recent supply should be here by now, and when he is in a hurry to return my man sometimes leaves the crates and baskets outside next to the inn.

'Can you retrieve any of the supplies he has left and carry them down to the cellar storage room? It is on your left as you descend the stairs, and is right now storage for mainly old chairs. I would appreciate the help.''My most recent supply should be here by now, and when he is in a hurry to return my man sometimes leaves the crates and baskets outside next to the inn.

'Can you retrieve any of the supplies he has left and carry them down to the cellar storage room? It is on your left as you descend the stairs, and is right now storage for mainly old chairs. I would appreciate the help.''You have agreed to carry some of Anlaf's supplies from outside the inn down to his cellar storage room? That sounds perfect, ${PLAYER}! I will stay here and study this old parchment for clues.''Nothing yet, ${PLAYER}. I am beginning to wonder if this is not truly the end now, as says the parchment!''You brought the supplies down to my storage room? Ah, very good. I have been particularly waiting for the basket of lemons.'

You tell Anlaf that there was no basket of lemons with the other supplies, and perhaps it will arrive with another shipment.

'Hmm... that is very peculiar. I was expecting it on this cart. Well, no matter. They are only lemons, though I was looking forward to them.''Thank you for the work. I do wonder what happened to those lemons, though.''Nothing yet, ${PLAYER}. Can it be that this paper speaks the truth, and this is truly the end of the treasure hunt on which we embarked? It is frustrating, but I should not dwell upon it. Did Anlaf appreciate the work you did for him?'

You tell Bingo about the supplies you brought downstairs, and also about the missing basket of lemons.

'Some lemons have gone missing? That is curious. It reminds me of an old trick I learned... in childhood... when I was small... and...'

Bingo's voice trails off and he stares closely at the parchment in his hand.

'${PLAYER}! Will you take this and hold it before the fire? Not too close, mind you, but just close enough to warm the paper? I wonder...''Take that parchment and hold it before the fire, ${PLAYER}! Not too close, mind you, but just close enough to warm the paper! I wonder...''I knew it, ${PLAYER}! When I was just a young hobbit, my father showed me the trick of writing invisible letters using the juice of lemons. When the paper is warmed, the letters appear as clear as day!'

Bingo studies the parchment for a moment, and his face lights up.

'I know where to go, ${PLAYER}, and I believe we will discover the solution to our treasured mystery at the location written on this parchment! Study the paper and see if you can learn where to go, and I will meet you there! I am so glad to know that this paper was not The End Now!''You figured it out!''Well, here we are. Now what?''Meet me by the tent in Ost Guruth, ${PLAYER}! We were just there!''I am excited to meet whoever has been leaving us these riddles, ${PLAYER}!''You figured it out, ${PLAYER}! I knew you would!

'Theodore wanted to come along as well. He is very excited to meet the one responsible for leaving us these clues and riddles.''The Master Treasure Seeker is about to emerge!''I am nervous, ${PLAYER}. Your friend Bingo believes that the riddle has led us to this place, and that the true Master Treasure Seeker awaits within! I am so excited to meet him, for I have so much to learn!

'Listen! I hear movement within! He is coming out!''Haley? Can it be? I don't believe it!''I thought so!''You found me, ${PLAYER}. I have to admit, I am impressed! I did not know that you would be able to solve the riddles I left for you. Theodore never had a chance, of course. He often bragged about his abilities as a treasure seeker, but of course I knew the truth. One of us spoke of such deeds, and one of us actually did them!

'I would discover these treasures and leave riddles to their locations for Theodore to find, but he never did. Until you and Bingo came along, I believed I would have no challenger to my title. But there it is!''Well now!''I am impressed you solved my riddles, because I doubted Theodore ever would!''Haley was the real Master Treasure Seeker all along? I don't believe it, but she got to all of those treasures before I did, and left the riddles there for me to unravel... but I never did!'

Theodore looks crestfallen for a moment, and then he clenches a fist.

'I will learn from her, ${PLAYER}, if she will accept me as a student! With her training, I will be the Master Treasure Seeker one day! I swear it on all the hidden treasures of Middle-earth! One day, with Haley as my tutor, I will be the best!''Teach me to be a Treasure Seeker, Haley! With you to teach me, I am certain that one day I will be the best!''What have I gotten myself into, ${PLAYER}?''Our treasure hunt is at an end, ${PLAYER}, and I am satisfied with its conclusion! I hope that Haley knows what she has gotten herself into, for I suspect that Theodore may not be the most attentive of pupils. Can he be taught the skills of Haley's trade? I think they both may have a lot to learn!

'My own journey is not at The End Now... it is at The Beginning, ${PLAYER}!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Restful Night' (Recommended Level: 34)</rgb>'My most recent supply should be here by now, and when he is in a hurry to return my man sometimes leaves the crates and baskets outside next to the inn.

'Can you retrieve any of the supplies he has left and carry them down to the cellar storage room? It is on your left as you descend the stairs, and is right now storage for mainly old chairs. I would appreciate the help.''You have agreed to carry some of Anlaf's supplies from outside the inn down to his cellar storage room? That sounds perfect, ${PLAYER}! I will stay here and study this old parchment for clues.'Unity Tilden is concerned that tomb-robbers have taken control of the ruins of Barad Tharsír in the Barandalf.Barad Tharsír is located south-east of Tinnudir, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the Brandywine.

Unity Tilden has asked you to defeat members of the robber-band that has taken Barad Tharsír, as well as their most infamous members: Ansley Atwood and Ogden Oakes.Defeat tomb-robbers at Barad Tharsír (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Ansley AtwoodDefeat Ogden OakesUnity Tilden is at the Ranger camp on the island of Tinnudir, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

Unity is waiting to hear that you have defeated the robbers at Barad Tharsír.'We have to do something about the tomb-robbers, ${PLAYER}. I have spoken with the Rangers about this matter, but they are stretched thin. They must focus on the threats to the tombs of their ancestors in Men Erain and the crypts of Annúminas, even while other areas fall into danger.

'South-east of Tinnudir, the sands of the Barandalf and the dune-grasses are swept by the breezes that come off the Brandywine, but now the gentle breezes carry too the harsh cries and commands of a band of tomb-robbers. They have established a camp in the ruins of Barad Tharsír, on the far side of the Brandywine, and I fear that before long they will cross the river and pass into the peaceful lands to the south.

'You will be rewarded if you can defeat the tomb-robbers at Barad Tharsír, including some of their more infamous members: the archer, Ansley Atwood, and the hound-keeper, Ogden Oakes. You may want to bring a friend or two, for the place is crawling with the unsavoury louts.''You have defeated Ansley and Ogden? I am pleased to hear it and to know that the robbers have less of a hold on Barad Tharsír than they did!

'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}! Please take this reward with my gratitude.''Barad Tharsír is south-east of Tinnudir, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the river Brandywine. You will be rewarded if you can defeat the tomb-robbers there before they cross the river and pass into the peaceful southern lands. Look too for Ansley Atwood and Ogden Oakes and put an end to their cruel legacies. You wouldn't be remiss to bring an ally or two, for many tomb-robbers crawl about that ruin.'The Ranger Culang has learned of a great treasure buried long ago by a Dúnadan-landholder and wants it recovered before it is found by tomb-robbers.A stone marker in Evendim may hide treasure. Culang told you this riddle, which may hold a clue to the marker's location:

  "Two roads pass through green fields.
   Four kings watch the meeting of the ways."

You should examine the marker when you find it for some clue to the location of the treasure.Find the solution to the riddle'I have come into possession of a riddle, ${PLAYER}, that may lead the way to buried treasure. A Dúnadan-landholder buried something somewhere in Evendim, fearful that it might be taken by the Witch-king's forces if Fornost fell. It seems that the landholder's line was extinguished in the battle, his lands abandoned. But I believe that his treasure remains unfound.

'Here is the riddle, ${PLAYER}, the only directions I have found, and those not easy to come by:

  "Two roads pass through green fields.
   Four kings watch the meeting of the ways."

'I do not know where this might be, but there is a cross-roads east of Tinnudir. Perhaps this riddle speaks of the cross-roads of Canadiach?''This riddle may hold the secret of the treasure's location:

  "Two roads pass through green fields.
   Four kings watch the meeting of the ways."

'Perhaps these words speak of the cross-roads of Canadiach, east of Tinnudir?'The stone marker is surrounded by signs of digging; if it once marked the location of the treasure, it has surely been found.

The marker bears an inscription, all but worn away by the passage of time.Heithur Ironfist, in the company and relative safety of Barachen's Camp, awaits your return with fine fur.You have accepted a task to collect fine fur from the beasts in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. You are to deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist, a dwarf taking refuge at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine fur from the beasts in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. Deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine fur from the beasts in the western stretches of the Trollshaws.'They are not much, but I am certain that I can work with them. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Heithur Ironfist, in the company and relative safety of Barachen's Camp, awaits your return with grey ears.You have accepted a task to collect grey ears from the beasts in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. You are to deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist, a dwarf taking refuge at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Grey ears from the beasts in the western stretches of the Trollshaws. Deliver the items to Heithur Ironfist at Barachen's Camp, south of the road in the western Trollshaws.Wanted: Grey ears from the beasts in the western stretches of the Trollshaws.'They are not much, but I am certain that I can work with them. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Mundaric Sandheaver is concerned about the salamanders that crawl among the dunes of the Barandalf.Salamanders slither among the dunes and sands of the Barandalf, north and east of Dwaling.

Mundaric Sandheaver wants you to defeat salamanders in the Barandalf and bring him their sand-crusted teeth.Collect salamander-teeth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mundaric Sandheaver is at Dwaling.

You have collected the teeth that Mundaric Sandheaver asked you to gather and have thinned the population of deadly salamanders that make dangerous the sands of the Barandalf. You should return now to him so he may study the teeth.'We use the salamander-scales as a material to grind down to make glass, and for that, they are important to us. But since Dwaling has been filled with the Big Folk, we haven't been as focused on gathering materials as we were before.

'This has caused the salamanders to run rampant. They are beginning to number an amount we cannot control, and I fear any hobbit who ventures out into the dunes may fall victim to one of them. While you are out there, defeat some of them and gather up their teeth. We may be able to find a way to make some clothing that can resist the cutting more.

'Be careful, ${PLAYER}. The salamanders can be vicious if you are not prepared.''Salamanders slither among the dunes of the Barandalf, to the north and east of here. Defeat the creatures and bring me their teeth.''These are most impressive, ${PLAYER}: the teeth are sharp indeed! Take my thanks with the knowledge that you have made the Barandalf safer for all of us hobbits.'Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of heavy sword sheaths.You have been tasked with collecting heavy sword sheaths from the brigands surrounding Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Heavy sword sheaths from the brigands in the ruins surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Heavy sword sheaths from the brigands in the ruins surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'It is good to know that the number of these defilers is thinning every time you step foot near our ancestral homes.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of scarred ears.You have been tasked with collecting scarred ears from the beasts surrounding Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Scarred ears from the beasts in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Scarred ears from the beasts in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'I will see that these are given to those who can put them to good use.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.While the Free Peoples of the North Downs were occupied with the threat of the Orc-army in Dol Dínen, they were nearly oblivious of the second host which marched west to Fornost.Amarion is at his camp atop Amon Raith, east of the Fields of Fornost.

Mincham believes the Orcs at Fornost have some greater plan than the occupation of the old ruins and has asked you to carry word of warning to Amarion, a young Ranger assigned to keep watch upon Amon Raith.'These Orcs are not just a simple, ravaging horde. They are united in a cause. Angmar has arisen and desires the North Downs as its prize. While the eyes of the Free Peoples were distracted to the host at Dol Dínen, a second army came here and claimed the ancient ruins of Fornost.

'Fortunately, they are unaware that not all of the eyes upon the Fields of Fornost were dead and sightless. My kindred are scattered, and it will take too long for my brethren at Esteldín to gather enough strength to defend against Angmar.

'I need you to go to Amon Raith in the east, upon which young Amarion keeps watch, and warn him of Angmar's presence here at Fornost.''I will not relax my vigilance -- Mincham does not admit fear without cause -- though I know not of what help I may be. Since these refugees came to Amon Raith, I have been able to do little else than protect them.

'Nevertheless, I thank you for your warning, friend.''Word must be taken to Amarion upon Amon Raith. Orcs have come to Fornost, and I do not believe they will stop there.'The Ranger Astiul has asked you to search along the shoreline for jetsam and to collect any damp pottery shards you find.Jetsam from Lake Nenuial often washes onto the western shore of Tinnudir, north of the Ranger-camp.

Astiul has asked you to follow the island's shore north of the Ranger-camp in search of jetsam and to search any you find for damp pottery shards.Search jetsam along the shore of Tinnudir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Astiul is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir in Evendim, south of the jetsam-littered shore.

The Ranger Astiul waits for you to return with the results of your search along the shore of Lake Nenuial.'I am not as suspicious of your identity as Calenglad, but I will do as he wishes; you must prove to me that you have an honest heart and are not allied with those who seek to plunder my Dúnedain heritage.

'Jetsam sometimes washes onto the shore of this island from out in the lake, and the tomb-robbers seek it out in search of jewels and other valuable trinkets. These pieces once belonged to the law-abiding people of Arnor and to their kings in Annúminas; we cannot allow the robbers to get their hands on any more of them.

'Follow the island's western shore northward, ${PLAYER}, and look for jetsam among the reeds. I would be interested in seeing any damp pottery shards you can recover. Beware of tomb-robbers, for they seek the same things, though for very different reasons! Return to me when you have gathered enough fragments, and I will see if you have recovered anything of interest.''You will find jetsam along the island's western shore, north of the Ranger-camp. Return to me with any damp pottery shards you can recover and beware of the tomb-robbers that prowl the lakeside!''You have returned, ${PLAYER}, and with some shards of Evendim's history, I trust?'It appears when sand-lurkers digest the river-weeds of the upper Baranduin, it gives the plants a mild healing ability.Sand-lurkers can be found in the Barandalf, south of the colossus.

Herthdúr asked you to search sand-lurkers in the Barandalf for piles of soggy weeds.Collect piles of soggy weeds from sand-lurkers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Herthdúr is at the colossus, north of the Barandalf.

Herthdúr will be eager to begin making poultices from the weeds you collected.'I examined the weeds you found, and I am glad you brought them to me. Normally, the river-weeds are of little consequence. One can eat them if hungry enough, but there was nothing special about them of themselves.

'It seems something happens when the sand-lurkers begin to digest the weeds. Somehow, that brings out a mild healing ability from the plants. I have tried them on several scrapes and cuts around camp here, and the results are undeniable.

'There is no sense in going swimming to get more, since they are undigested. I need you to go out and get more from the sand-lurkers themselves. A slightly foul proposition, I realize, taking dinner right after it has been devoured, but I would like to have more of these weeds to make some much-needed poultices.''We never have enough poultices, because we never have enough supplies. These weeds could help greatly.''These will do perfectly. Just barely digested, and there is plenty here to make poultices for weeks. Thank you.'While the Free Peoples of the North Downs were occupied with the threat of the Orc-army in Dol Dínen, they were nearly oblivious of the second host which marched west to Fornost.Captain Trotter leads the people of Trestlebridge, along the Greenway far to the south of the Fields of Fornost.

The town lives under constant watch and fear that Orcs of the Tarkrîp tribe will return and complete their massacre and destruction of the town. Mincham's words may sway Captain Trotter to call upon the aid of others from the south. Mincham directed you to explain the true danger of the Orc-tribe facing Captain Trotter and the besieged town of Trestlebridge.Relay Mincham's concerns to Captain Trotter'Though it will bring him no solace, we must inform Captain Trotter that the Orc-tribe he faces is part of the larger horde within the North Downs. The orders you presented me specifically mention the nature of the Orcs in Nan Wathren.

'They are savages. Their tribe, the Tarkrîp -- or Man-flayers -- wish only to taste the flesh of Men, they will not cease their assaults.

'Please, make your way south to Trestlebridge and inform Captain Trotter that he should send a courier south to my brethren in the Bree-fields, they may yet have strength or influence enough to aid him.''Warnings from Men who will not come to our aid mean little to me ${PLAYER}. If this, Mincham, wishes to offer Trestlebridge aid, he can bring his sword here to stand by our side.

'I will not sacrifice another spear without it first biting Orc-flesh. Your warnings are well-heard, but there are none to carry a message to the south.''Make haste! If the weight of two Orc-tribes cannot force the Tarkrîp to heed orders from their masters, then Trestlebridge faces an enemy that will not cease. You must warn Captain Trotter and implore him to call upon aid.'Barachen has a small camp east of Evendim, in the Trollshaws.Barachen's camp is far to the east of Evendim, in the Trollshaws, just beyond the Last Bridge from the Lone-lands.

Orchalwë has asked you to speak with his friend Barachen.Talk to Barachen at his camp in the Trollshaws'We oft keep in touch with the Elves of the Rivendell. One called Barachen I consider a close friend. A letter I received from him not long ago indicated that trouble was afoot within the Trollshaws, and he was seeking my advice. I worry for him.

'He has a small camp far to the east of here, in the Trollshaws, just beyond the Last Bridge from the Lone-lands. Would you go to him and learn what troubles him?''So, the Rangers of Evendim have sent you to help?''Have you spoken to Barachen yet? He has a camp in the Trollshaws.'With the ropes strung you can now make your way to the peak of the colossus and survey all of Evendim.The rope to climb the colossus is nearby.

Make your way to the top of the colossus and have a <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb>.Sardan is at the base of the colossus.

Sardan will be interested to hear what you have seen.Talk to Sardan near the colossus'Thanks to some kindly aid from yourself, the ropes have been strung, and we are able to reach new places on the colossus in need of repair. But that is a task we can handle ourselves.

'For you, a treat... a vision I doubt like any you have seen before. Use the rope behind me and ascend to the peak of the colossus.

'Once you are up there make sure to <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> around and enjoy the view.''You will have to go up there and see the view for yourself. I do not have words to describe it.''The beauty of the lake is enchanting. Yet I saw the same you did: the ruins to the west and north are in desperate need of our aid.'The island of Tyl Ruinen has been overrun with salamanders.Salamanders can be found all over Tyl Ruinen.

Lúgethir asked you to defeat several salamanders around Tyl Ruinen, hoping to contain their numbers.Defeat salamanders on Tyl Ruinen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lúgethir is at Humphrey Rumming's camp on Tyl Ruinen.

Lúgethir will be pleased to hear the number of salamanders has been reduced.Talk to Lúgethir on Tyl Ruinen'I know Humfrey thinks this is all manageable, but the situation has become dire. That should be clear to anyone.

'We need to reduce the number of salamanders roaming around here... indeed they are an evil breed of draconic nature. Very little of the natural fauna remains... a few turtles on the northern shore, and that is all. The salamanders have nearly devastated everything else.

'Grant me a favour and drive off a few of the beasts. Hopefully, that will reduce their numbers enough to allow the natural fauna to return.''Why did Humphrey have to breed salamanders? If he had bred pigs, at least we would be having bacon right now.''I know you defeated a number of them, but I think you are right... I do not believe it made a difference either.'The island of Tyl Ruinen has been overrun by salamanders.Salamander-nests can be found along the shoreline of Tyl Ruinen.

Lúgethir asked you to find salamander-nests and smash the eggs within.Smash the salamander-eggs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lúgethir is with Humphrey on Tyl Ruinen.

Lúgethir will be pleased to hear you have smashed many a salamander-egg.Talk to Lúgethir on Tyl Ruinen'Attempting to reduce the salamanders' number did not work. I swear that I have seen more in the past hour than I did the entirety of the day yesterday. I thank you for your efforts, but we must find a different approach to dealing with this problem.

'The salamanders make nests along the shoreline, and in them, they lay their eggs. From these eggs, new salamanders hatch. The salamanders here seem to be especially aggressive breeders.

'Wander the shore, find these eggs, and smash them. Perhaps we can stop the salamanders by ensuring that the next generation fails to spawn.''I can still see flecks of shell in your hair. This plan will take a while to resolve, as we have not done anything to the current spawning of salamanders, but hopefully as they are driven out -- with none to replace them -- we can reclaim this island.''You may wish to rinse your hands off in the lake afterwards. I have smashed an egg or two myself, and it is not pleasant.'The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of fine fur to be made to them.You have accepted a task to collect fine fur from the beasts in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Fine fur from the beasts surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine fur from the beasts surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of smooth wings to be made to them.You have accepted a task to collect smooth wings from the birds in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Smooth wings from the birds surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Smooth wings from the birds surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Barad Tharsír was once an important shipping port that linked Evendim with the south along the Baranduin.Barad Tharsír is south-east of the colossus on the east bank of the Brandywine River.

Daerol asked you to scout the ruins of Barad Tharsír and report back on what you find.Discover the first entrancewayDiscover the second entrancewayDiscover the third entrancewayDiscover the fourth entrancewayDiscover the fifth entrancewayDaerol is at the colossus, north-west of Barad Tharsír.

Daerol will be very interested to hear what you learned about the doors of Barad Tharsír.Talk to Daerol at the colossus'Barad Tharsír is a place we know little about. There was not much left in the record, but I know it was a site of value. It controlled the shipping lanes and access to the lake from the Baranduin. Every boat travelling the river passed through it.

'The robbers there are a nuisance, but they should not distract from the larger goal: to learn as much as we can and preserve as much history as possible. We shall never know where we are going if we do not know where we came from.

'Head down to Barad Tharsír and do some scouting for me. I know there are five doorways, but I do not know what they lead to. Knowing the makeup of the structures would be of great help.''Until we know what those entrances lead to, we cannot know how valuable Barad Tharsír really is.''So, a store-room, a dining hall, barracks, an armoury, and one room that was unmarked... either intentionally or lost to time. Interesting.'With the key to the cellar of the lighthouse, you can strike against what now calls it home.The ruins of the lighthouse are south-east of the colossus.

Gaelnarthan asked you to venture into the lighthouse and defeat the creature you find within.Defeat the creature beneath the lighthouseGaelnarthan is at the colossus camp.

Gaelnarthan will be pleased to hear the kergrim hunting them has been defeated.Talk to Gaelnarthan at the colossus camp'I was able to open all of the containers you recovered and found the key in the final one. These are great tidings for us as we can finally go on the offensive.

'As I mentioned before you left to get the containers, we have been constantly harassed by a creature we believe lives beneath the ruins of the lighthouse. The door is locked, so we cannot enter. We have found no other entrance. At this point we do not know how it comes and goes. We are sure that is where it lives, though.

'We have lost enough good men to this creature. Take this key, venture beneath the lighthouse and slay whatever you find down there. Do not return until we are safe from its threat.''Please, defeat whatever this thing is. We cannot truly be safe at this camp until it is no more.''The creature is defeated! I can see that from your clothing and the filth that covers it. I am very thankful for your efforts. I believe this will be the first full night of sleep I get at this camp.'Adleithian is concerned that tomb-robbers have taken control of the ruins of Barad Tharsír in the Barandalf.Barad Tharsír is located south-east of the colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the Brandywine.

Adleithian asked you to find a room hidden within the ruins of Barad Tharsír and discover what is within. If any are to disturb you, they should be dealt with.Search the ruinsAdleithian is at the Ranger-camp at the colossus, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

Adleithian is waiting to hear what you found within the collections room.Talk to Adleithian'Now I know what they are seeking. There is a room somewhere within the ruins of Barad Tharsír where anything not documented on a ship's register of goods would be taken and held until ownership could be proven. While most of those items have probably been lost to history, there could still be a wealth of small trinkets and jewellery hidden within.

'The room is reported to be on the south side of the ruins. From the reports, the door looks like any other and the room is not that deep within. I am amazed it has lasted this long, but as I said earlier, we ourselves thought Barad Tharsír to be empty and picked over. We were clearly wrong.

'Return to the ruins on the beach and find this room. Search within and return with what you find. If any disturb you while you are there, put an end to them. Barad Tharsír shall be returned to us.''So, their leader attempted to ambush you? It appears like this was all just a ruse then. The robbers have been getting increasingly clever in their attempts to ambush us. Not clever enough to best you, though. One day, with the rest of Evendim, Barad Tharsír will see her glory restored.''Barad Tharsír is south-east of this colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the river Brandywine.'Barad Tharsír was once an important shipping port that linked Evendim with the south along the Baranduin.Barad Tharsír is south-east of the colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the Brandywine.

Daerol wants you to bring him any relics that you can find from Barad Tharsír.Collect a shieldCollect a swordCollect a vaseDaerol is at the Ranger-camp at the colossus, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

You have gathered several relics from the ruins of Barad Tharsír and should return to Daerol.'While you were examining those entrances, I was reading up on some of the things that went through the port. The records indicate weapons and pottery were the primary things shipped along the river. I wonder if there is anything still left?

'There are many pieces of knowledge that were once known and have left this land, smithing and artisanship among the greatest. If we could recover some items from the past, we may be able to make greater things for the future.

'Please, return to Barad Tharsír and see if you can find some things left behind and overlooked by the robbers there. A shield, weapon, and vase would be of the most use to my studies.''Barad Tharsír is south-east of the colossus, on the far side of the Brandywine, in the Barandalf. If you search the grounds you are sure to find old relics of the sort for which I have asked.''These relics do not appear on the surface to hold anything new or different, but only a fool would stop there. I will continue to examine them.'Adleithian is concerned that tomb-robbers have taken control of the ruins of Barad Tharsír in the Barandalf.Barad Tharsír is located south-east of the colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the Brandywine.

Adleithian has asked you to collect orders off of two robbers, Atwood and Oakes.Collect Atwood's ordersCollect Oakes' ordersAdleithian is at the Ranger-camp at the colossus, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

Adleithian is waiting to examine the orders you collected.'My research shows Barad Tharsír was the north port of my ancestors on the shipping lane down the Baranduin. I do not know what the tomb-robbers could be interested in there... there should be not much of value left. But if they are interested, then I am interested. What do they know that we do not?

'Your earlier work has served us well, my scouts tell me that two of the more prominent robbers have emerged from the ruins and are leading the digs themselves. They are the key to understanding the tomb-robbers' interest in Barad Tharsír.

'Return to the ruins and collect the orders off of two particular brigands, Atwood and Oakes. Those orders will be the clue we need to solve this mystery.''Barad Tharsír is south-east of this colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the river Brandywine.''These tell of most troubling things, indeed. There is a room where goods not listed on the manifests were taken and stored. If this room still exists, it could hold an untold fortune of history.'The lore-master Humfrey Rumming must know something about the salamanders of Tyl Ruinen, though he seems to be hiding some sort of secret.There are chests hidden all around Tyl Ruinen, and one of them contains the solution to Rumming's riddle.

The riddle goes:

Lesser get of Ancalagon's spawn
Taloned claws and fang-gaped yawn.
Scatha's tears would surely flow
Saw he his children laid so low.Humfrey Rumming is by the ruins of Tollobel on Tyl Ruinen.

You have obtained the token of the worm from a chest on Tyl Ruinen. Bring it back to Humfrey Rumming, if you believe it is the solution to his riddle.'All right. All right. One more riddle. This one will truly challenge your lore-mastery. Again, I give you a key to a chest. Again, I challenge you to show even a thousandth part of the brilliance of Humfrey Rumming, wisest of the Wise!

'So, er, here you go:

'Lesser get of Ancalagon's spawn
Taloned claws and fang-gaped yawn.
Scatha's tears would surely flow
Saw he his children laid so low.''You solved those first two riddles by asking someone, didn't you? I thought so! Don't do that! Solve this one on your own, even if the solution requires some splitting of hairs and some precise thinking!''Well. I suppose you think you've won? Well, you haven't won! Not by a long shot! You'll never beat me! Not Humfrey Rumming! No!

'Oh, yes, right. The salamanders. Ah, I had forgotten. Well, since you acknowledge my greatness, my pre-eminence amongst all lore-masters....

'I suppose it would be all right to share my secret, since you have proven yourself worthy... barely. Not one in a thousand, one in a million, could have solved my riddles! So, now... listen closely....'The lore-master Humfrey Rumming has posed a second riddle, insisting that if you solve this one, he will tell you what he knows.There are chests hidden all around Tyl Ruinen, and one of them contains the solution to Rumming's riddle.

The riddle goes:

Foul great beasts with sharpened claws;
Black-furred pelts and fanged maws;
Coursing the woods, they take their prey;
Torment their victim, at last to slay.Humfrey Rumming is by the ruins of Tollobel on Tyl Ruinen.

You have obtained the token of the Warg from a chest on Tyl Ruinen. Bring it back to Humfrey Rumming if you believe it is the solution to his riddle.'Oh, very well. We both know you solved that riddle through pure luck, but I suppose you deserve another chance. What? Challenge me? Humfrey Rumming? Impossible! You are not in my class, you know! It wouldn't be fair! I'll just give you another riddle and another key.

'Foul great beasts with sharpened claws;
Black-furred pelts and fanged maws;
Coursing the woods, they take their prey;
Torment their victim, at last to slay.

'That one is easy enough, eh? If you think that solving a single riddle is enough to satisfy me, you are quite mistaken! I didn't haul all of these chests out here to give up my knowledge at the first inquiry! Humfrey Rumming is nothing if not resolute!''Can't figure it out, eh? I knew that first riddle was just a lucky guess! Look for the chests that I've hidden around Tyl Ruinen -- only one of them correctly solves my riddle!''Bah! That riddle was too easy. Even a child could solve it!

'Well anyway, the point is, Humfrey Rumming's secrets are not for you! You have no conception of the magnitude of my wisdom! The terrible secret of the salamanders is not for your ears!'The lore-master Humfrey Rumming must know something about the salamanders of Tyl Ruinen, but he seems to be hiding some sort of secret. There must be some way to get him to tell what he knows.There are boxes hidden all around Tyl Ruinen, and one of them contains the solution to Rumming's riddle.

The riddle goes:

Fluttering shreds rent from the fabric of night;
Fell shrieking beasts that shun the sun's light;
Dwelling in darkness they fly hidden from sight;
Clouds obscure the stars when first they take flight.Humfrey Rumming is by the ruins of Tollobel on Tyl Ruinen.

You have obtained the token of the bat from a chest on Tyl Ruinen. Bring it back to Humfrey Rumming if you believe it is the solution to his riddle.'Yes, yes, naturally I know all about the salamanders. I'm a great lore-master, am I not? Or am I? Ha! Little joke there. Of course I am. All the Wise know the name of Humfrey Rumming! But I can't divulge the great secrets of lore to just anyone, can I? No, no, no, no, no! Not at all! And how can Humfrey Rumming tell if you are worthy? How, indeed?

'Let's try a bit of a challenge, shall we? I have hidden a number of chests all about this island. One of them contains the answer to my riddle. Take this key -- but beware, it can only be used once! Here's the riddle:

'Fluttering shreds rent from the fabric of night;
Fell shrieking beasts that shun the sun's light;
Dwelling in darkness they fly hidden from sight;
Clouds obscure the stars when first they take flight.

'It's not easy, is it? Oh, no! But if you explore this island from top to bottom, and from bottom to top, you are sure to find the solution!''Can't figure it out? You're not a lore-master at all, are you. Ha! Look for the box that solves my riddle -- I've hidden them all over Tyl Ruinen!''Humfrey Rumming? A lore-master? I have never heard the name. Perhaps you can learn what he knows? Return to Tyl Ruinen and attempt to solve his riddle, no matter how ridiculous it may seem.''Ha! Fool! That's the wrong token! I should have known you were no lore-master! Oh, wait. That's the right one, isn't it?

'Bah! Just luck! Beginner's luck! Fool's luck! I won't tell you my dreadful secret at all now!

'Well, not that Humfrey Rumming has a dreadful secret, you know; it's just that if I did, I wouldn't tell you. You see? You see?'Adleithian is concerned that tomb-robbers have taken control of the ruins of Barad Tharsír in the Barandalf.Barad Tharsír is located south-east of the colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the Brandywine.

Adleithian has asked you to defeat members of the robber-band that has taken Barad Tharsír.Defeat tomb-robbers at Barad Tharsír (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Adleithian is at the Ranger-camp at the colossus, north-west of the Barandalf and west of Parth Aduial.

Adleithian is waiting to hear that you have defeated the robbers at Barad Tharsír.Talk to Adleithian at the colossus'We have to do something about the tomb-robbers, ${PLAYER}. I have spoken with my kinsmen about this matter, but we are stretched thin. We must focus on the threats to the tombs of our forebears in Men Erain and the crypts of Annúminas, even while other areas fall into danger.

'South-east of this colossus, the sands of the Barandalf and the dune-grasses are swept by the breezes that come off the Brandywine, but now the gentle breezes carry, too, the harsh cries and commands of a band of tomb-robbers. They have established a camp in the ruins of Barad Tharsír, on the far side of the Brandywine, and I fear that before long they will cross this river and pass into the peaceful lands to the south.

'You will be rewarded if you can defeat the tomb-robbers at Barad Tharsír. We cannot allow them to ransack and carry away our past, but we cannot afford to do anything about it ourselves.''You have driven off several of the contingent? I am pleased to hear it and to know that the robbers have less of a hold on Barad Tharsír than they once did!

'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}! But do not rest yet, we have just begun to wrest those ruins back.''Barad Tharsír is south-east of this colossus, in the Barandalf, on the far side of the river Brandywine.'The page you found pointed to a shipwreck along the river that might hold something of value.The wreckage is strewn along the Baranduin, flowing south from the colossus.

Gaelnarthan asked you to swim the Baranduin and collect several containers lost from ancient shipwrecks.Collect containers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gaelnarthan is at the colossus, north-west of the lighthouse ruins.

Gaelnarthan will want to see the containers you collected from the river-bed.'After much time and patience, I was able to read what was on the page you found. It was very faded and in a bad state. I nearly destroyed it trying to get the last few sentences.

'It was from near the end of the kingdom and detailed a shipment of keys from down the river to the Rangers encamped here. They knew they were leaving, but they wanted to safe-guard as many of their treasures as possible. The locks came on a separate ship that arrived safely. The keys never made it. They still swapped the locks out, believing it was safer that no one could get in. One of the doors locked was to the cellar of the lighthouse itself. We need to get inside urgently, for an evil has made its den there.

'This page gives the location of the wreck that should contain those keys. Search the waters and bring back whatever containers you can find.''The wrecks should be along the bottom of the river in front of Barad Tharsír.''Now, let us see about getting these open and seeing if the key we want is inside.'Many strange creatures have begun to appear in Evendim, not least of which are the fearsome salamanders.The island of Tyl Ruinen on the west side of the lake, opposite Tinnudir, has become infested with salamanders.

Calatherdir has asked you to make the journey to Tyl Ruinen and the ruin of Tollobel to investigate the salamanders that have appeared there.Investigate Tyl Ruinen'Have you seen the creatures called salamanders? They are rather akin to worms, but seem to me to be an entirely new breed of creature. We have not seen them in Evendim until recently, when Tyl Ruinen, the island on the west side of the lake, became infested with the creatures. At night, the island seems to be burning with the flames of these strange beasts.

'A number of salamanders have also been seen in the Barandalf, south and east of Tinnudir -- could it be that the creatures are spreading? Venture to Tyl Ruinen, if you are willing, and discover the source of this plague of salamanders.

'There is a ruin in the centre of the island called Tollobel. Perhaps some clue to the origin of the salamanders may be found there.''Salamanders? Why would you ask me about salamanders? Me, of all people! Why should a poor wandering lore-master like Humfrey Rumming know anything of them? Why, I don't even know what a salamander is. Never heard of them before.

'Ha! Got you there! You thought the whole thing was Humfrey Rumming's fault, didn't you? Didn't you? But now you know, I know nothing. Nothing at all. Not about salamanders.

'Wait a minute. That doesn't make sense. Who would say such a thing? Humfrey Rumming is a great lore-master, you know! I know all the lore there is! So I must know something about salamanders. But what? That's the question. And a good question it is, too!''There are salamanders on Tyl Ruinen, ${PLAYER}, and we must learn why! Tyl Ruinen is just off the western shore of the lake -- a long swim from here, to be sure!

'There is a ruin called Tollobel in the centre of Tyl Ruinen. That is where you should begin your search.'The Ranger Culang has learned of a great treasure buried long ago by a Dúnadan-landholder and wants it recovered before it is found by tomb-robbers.The treasure may lie beneath another stone marker, somewhere in Evendim, if this riddle is to be believed:

  "To settle disputes, men fight in the ring of stone.
   Outside the wall, my marker lies hidden."

You should examine the marker when you find it for some clue to the location of the treasure.Find the solution to the riddleThe inscription on this stone marker is barely legible, but these words can just be made out:

  "To settle disputes, men fight in the ring of stone.
   Outside the wall, my marker lies hidden."

This new riddle may reveal the true location of the treasure.The stone marker is surrounded by signs of digging; if it once marked the location of the treasure, it has surely been found.

The marker bears an inscription, all but worn away by the passage of time.The inscription on this stone marker is barely legible, but these words can just be made out:

  "To settle disputes, men fight in the ring of stone.
   Outside the wall, my marker lies hidden."

This new riddle may reveal the true location of the treasure.'Another riddle? How did it go?

  "To settle disputes, men fight in the ring of stone.
   Outside the wall, my marker lies hidden."

'I do not know for certain, but these words may be speaking of the arena in Ost Forod, to the north-east of Tinnudir, among the hills of Tyrn Fornech.'Since the awakening of the evil in Agamaur, fell spirits have stirred the waters, taking the form of darkwater.Darkwater can be found throughout Agamaur and Garth Agarwen, far to the north.

Aric the Stonespeaker wishes to help the cursed men of Arthedain and has asked you to banish the darkwater found in Agamaur. He has also asked you to bring back their swords for him to display, to bolster the flagging spirits of the cursed men.'Ah yes, greetings. The stones here speak to me and tell me of an ancient struggle that has played out here in the Lone-lands. On one side are cursed men, of the same lot as Emelin, doomed for their inaction. On the other side there is a..."power", I suppose you could call it, who makes its home in Garth Agarwen. From what I can discern, these cursed men have tried to set to right what resulted from their inaction, yet have failed again and again.

'These men, these "shades," are not wholly evil, as you may know, and my heart is moved by their lot. I would like to help them set matters right, to help free them from their curse. Perhaps you could help with that?

'In recent days, fell spirits have stirred the waters of the swamps, taking the form of creatures called darkwater. They have appeared in great numbers in Garth Agarwen and driven out many of the shades. If you were to enter Garth Agarwen to defeat some of the darkwater there and bring their swords to me, I would display them and bolster the flagging resolve of these cursed men.''Speak not of destroying the dead, for what am I? Undead, houseless and feeble, bereft of all that makes a Man a Man. Begone with thee!''Astounding! I thought to see but three or four of the darkwater's blades, yet you have brought the blades of an army! When these cursed men see that flesh and blood can defeat the monsters that inhabit Garth Agarwen, then perhaps their will shall be strengthened and they shall one day be freed from their curse!'Now that you have defeated Humfrey Rumming in his riddle-contest, he may at last be willing to tell you the terrible secret of the salamanders.The two original salamanders can be found in the cellar of Tollobel, on Tyl Ruinen.

Humfrey Rumming confided in you that he was the one responsible for the plague of salamanders on Tyl Ruinen and gave you the key to the cellar where he trapped the two original creatures. He asked you to slay the two originals to help reduce their numbers.Humfrey Rumming is by the ruins of Tollobel on Tyl Ruinen.

Humfrey Rumming is waiting to hear of your victory over the two original salamanders he brought to Tyl Ruinen.Calatherdir is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, east of Tyl Ruinen.

Having learned the origin of the salamanders and helped to slow their proliferation, you should return to Calatherdir to tell him the tale of your deeds.Talk to Calatherdir at Tinnudir'It happened like this: as one of the world's greatest lore-masters, I happened to be doing some research on the ruins of Tollobel. It seems the last owner was a great warrior, and he took a number of trophies from the Angmarim at the Battle of Fornost. These trophies included a pair of eggs....

'Such little eggs, too! Well, naturally, it was a challenge to see if they could be quickened after all these generations. You can imagine my pride when they hatched! Cute little salamanders, tiny little efts, they seemed. Truly learned lore-masters call them "efts," you know, and I encourage you to do the same! But these little efts just would not stop growing! And soon they began to grow hostile, and after that came the fire....

'The things breed so fast! I managed to pen the original dam and sire in a cellar in the ruins here. Since then, well, I've been trapped... I mean, I've been studying them. Yes! But now you've come. You can kill them! You're a hero, aren't you? Right! Here, take this key! Slay the dam and sire -- the other salamanders breed much more slowly. Now that you know the terrible secret of the salamanders, it's up to you to stop them!''Well, what are you waiting for? Get to it! Kill those two salamanders! Scholarship requires it!

'Use the key I gave you to unlock the Tollobel cellar-door. The two original salamanders are vicious creatures and have no respect for authority.''Oh! You killed them! Poor things! I mean, well done! Well done, indeed. At last, I can leave this accursed place. I mean, well, perhaps there is a bit more to study here after all.

'Anyway, you've done a great deed here and helped the pursuit of knowledge immensely. Immensely, I say! Of course, it was nothing I couldn't have handled myself. I was just letting you learn a little yourself.

'Go talk to the Rangers on the island far to the east and tell them that I am very displeased with their handling of the salamander problem. A great lore-master can only do so much, and at some point, the Rangers should have stepped in! Now give me back my key.''You return to the Rangers and tell them that Humfrey Rumming is very displeased with their treatment of the salamander problem! They should have done something earlier and not left it all to Humfrey Rumming, great lore-master though he is!''Welcome back, ${PLAYER}! I had begun to fear for your safety. What have you learned?

'Indeed? An amazing story. That mad old man. It is a pity we are unable to patrol these lands better, or we should never have permitted him to go near those ruins.

'It may be that these salamanders will never be fully driven from the area now, but at least we know their origin. If what Rumming said is true, it also may be that your actions have slowed their spread. I thank you too for that.'Tinnudir is where the Rangers now base all of their operations from within Evendim.Orchalwë is at Tinnudir, north and west of the colossus.

After thanking you for aiding them, the Rangers at the colossus directed you to the island of Tinnudir.Talk to Orchalwë at Tinnudir'You have done much to aid us here, and for that, I am grateful. But this colossus and Barad Tharsír are but minor points within Evendim. Annúminas to the west and the estates to the north hold more value than the ruins here.

'Follow the road north, then head west at the cross-roads until you reach Tinnudir. There, speak to Orchalwë to learn more.''Tinnudir is just up the road... you will know it when you come to it.''Ah, fresh blood. You may be just what we need to begin reclaiming the eastern estates.'Barad Tharsír was once an important shipping port that linked Evendim with the south along the Baranduin.Barad Tharsír is across the Baranduin, south-east of the colossus.

Daerol asked you to return to Barad Tharsír, enter the store-room, and discover what the robbers are doing there.Search within the store-roomDaerol is at the colossus, north-west of Barad Tharsír.

Daerol will want to know that the robbers were excavating the ruins, and the carts were full of nothing but dirt and rubble.Talk to Daerol'Most of the items you recovered had the same mark you told me was outside the store-room. I think we should focus our efforts there. I have spied a great many robbers entering and exiting that doorway, but they never seem to be carrying anything out.

'They must be loading it all in crates and bags on carts. We must know what they have found in there, it must be a great deal if they are waiting to move it until they have it all.

'Head within the store-house and search whatever containers you find there. We must know what they have found.''This store-room is obviously important.''Hmm, so there was nothing but dirt and rubble in the carts and an ambush awaiting those that sought them out? If the robbers are this devious here, I shudder to think what troubles will await us within the estates to the north.'The island of Tyl Ruinen has been taken over by salamanders and lake-skimmers.Lake-skimmers can be found around the shore of Tyl Ruinen.

Lúgethir asked you to defeat lake-skimmers to prevent the salamanders from using them to get to shore.Defeat lake-skimmers on Tyl Ruinen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lúgethir is at Humphrey's camp on Tyl Ruinen.

Lúgethir will be pleased to hear that the lake-skimmers have been destroyed, though you fail to see how they could have carried any salamanders.Talk to Lúgethir on Tyl Ruinen'I might be seeing things -- I have been out here a long time and Humphrey is not exactly the sanest of company -- but I would swear I saw a young salamander riding along the back of a lake-skimmer as it danced over the water.

'If I did see this -- and this can actually happen -- it could be disastrous if somehow the salamanders were carried across to the western shore of the lake, where they could begin to multiply and spread without any natural bounds to stop them.

'Head to the beach and destroy any lake-skimmers you find. We cannot take a chance on this.''I agree that it was probably all in my head and in no way possible, especially now that I think about the size of a lake-skimmer and the size of a salamander. But I thank you for entertaining me.''I know I sound as mad as Humphrey, but I cannot ignore what I saw...or think I saw, as I am still not sure I believe it myself.'Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of broken longswords.You have been tasked with collecting broken longswords from the brigands and goblins at Tyrn Fornech, north of Tinnudir, or to the south near Annúminas. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Broken longswords from the brigands in the ruins on Tyrn Fornech, north of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir, or to the south, near Annúminas. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Broken longswords from the brigands in the ruins on Tyrn Fornech, north of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir, or to the south, near Annúminas.'We will rework this metal into something that can be used by our hands.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of heavy carapaces.You have been tasked with collecting heavy carapaces from the insects to the west of Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Heavy carapaces from the insects in the lands west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger, Astiul, at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Heavy carapaces from the insects in the lands west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'I will see that these are given to those who can put them to good use.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of leathery fur.You have been tasked with collecting leathery fur from the beasts surrounding Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Leathery fur from the beasts in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Leathery fur from the beasts in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'I will see that these are given to those who can put them to good use.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of soft skin.You have been tasked with collecting soft skin from the beasts and lizard-like creatures to the west of Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Soft skin from the beasts and lizard-like creatures in the lands west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Soft skin from the beasts and lizard-like creatures in the lands west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'I will see that these are given to those who can put them to good use.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of sticky filth.You have been tasked with collecting sticky filth from the insects west of Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Sticky filth from the insects west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Sticky filth from the insects west of the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'We will make use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tham Ornen has been occupied by forces from Angmar. What value they see in the ruin is unknown.Tham Ornen is north-east of Tinnudir.

Brogdagnir implored to you to deliver a resounding defeat to the Angmarim encamped at Tham Ornen.Defeat Angmarim at Tham Ornen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brogdagnir is at Tinnudir, south-west of Tham Ornen.

Brogdagnir will be pleased to hear that the Angmarim have been driven from Tham Ornen.Talk to Brogdagnir at Tinnudir'I suppose you have been informed of the situation? It is a disgrace that vile men have taken one of our ancient estates, ruined or not. I would go there myself had I not orders to stand this post.

'I know this place is not your home. I know these halls are not where your ancestors were born, walked, laughed, ate, loved, and ultimately died. This is not where your history lives...but you must be able to relate to how this feels.

'I ask you, in my stead, to venture to Tham Ornen and drive the Angmarim from within its walls with a force they have never seen. I wish you to send them a defeat so strong it echoes all the way to the gates of Carn Dûm.''I could hear the screams of terror from here. They will certainly think twice before they return, though I fear they still shall.''I only wish it could be my blade by your side as you make them regret ever stepping foot into the lands of my fathers.'The Ranger Ferrif has asked you to bear his compliments and regrets to Calenglad, the leader of the Rangers at the island of Tinnudir on Lake Evendim.Calenglad is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, off the east shore of Lake Nenuial, west of Parth Aduial in Evendim. Evendim is west of the North Downs and north of the Shire.

The Ranger Ferrif has asked you to speak with his leader Calenglad in his stead.'Greetings. You have the look of a wayfarer who has walked many a long road, and I wonder if perhaps I could convince you to walk another in my stead.

'In the normal course of things, I should be on my way to Lake Nenuial, in Evendim, to serve for a time in caring for the old Dúnedain manors and crypts found near Annúminas. Truly, for most of my brethren, this duty is a pilgrimage to the past, to another time when Arnor was a great kingdom that ruled all these lands in peace and prosperity.

'However, what with the amassing Orc-army just on our doorstep, I will not be able to make my journey just yet. I would ask you to seek out Calenglad at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, just off the east shore of Lake Nenuial, and give him my regrets and this explanation.''Please give my regrets to Calenglad at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir on Lake Nenuial, in Evendim.''Ah. Thank you for carrying Ferrif's message. We are already so few in number and hemmed in on all sides by danger -- this unwelcome news comes as little surprise.'At the request of the Ranger Gondranc, you went to the camp of the Earth-kin north-east of Esteldín to retrieve a book of leatherworking lore Asikko, the Earth-kin's chieftain, had promised him. With this lore, he has crafted armour for the people of Trestlebridge.Jarred Mason is an armourer in Trestlebridge, far to the west and south of Esteldín, along the Greenway.

Gondranc completed the armour he had promised for the people of Trestlebridge and has only to deliver it. He has asked you to act as his courier and deliver the armour to a man named Jarred Mason.Gondranc resides at Esteldín, far to the north and east of Trestlebridge.

Jarred Mason has asked you to return to Gondranc with his thanks for the armour.'This task, at least, is finished. I have much work to do to help my kindred prepare for the coming war, but my oath to Trestlebridge shall at least be fulfilled.

'I need you, if you are willing, to deliver this armour to Jarred Mason in Trestlebridge. He is a man of some crafting skill himself. I believe he is acting as the armourer for his people.

'I am sure you have crossed through Trestlebridge and know well their plight, but should you have lost your direction, it lies far to the west and south of Esteldín, along the Greenway leading to Bree-land.''Make your way west and south to the town of Trestlebridge at the border of the North Downs and Bree-land. Deliver his armour to Jarred Mason.''What is this? You say this came from Gondranc? I will admit I had not thought to hear from him again!

'My friend is a wanderer, rarely staying in one place too long, and when he decided to pass north, I could not help but wonder what he hoped to find in the barrens. Then the Orcs came, and I was sure he had perished.

'My people, what few are left here, need it more than ever. If you know where Gondranc is, return to him with my thanks!''Please, tell Gondranc that he has helped to restore hope to us in this beleaguered time.''Jarred is a good man. I have often wanted to tell him of our home here at Esteldín, but our way of life is too dangerous to explain to those who know it not already.

'If this war which rages about us ends and my Lord Aragorn takes his rightful place, perhaps there will be a time when I may tell Jarred my whole tale, but for now, his safety -- and ours -- must hold precedence.

'I have something for you. Your efforts are deserving of a greater gift. I used the remnants of what you brought me to craft you this fine armour.'Bingo has decided to continue his journey and leave the Lone-lands behind, passing into the wooded hills of the Trollshaws.Bingo has decided to reassess his course when you come to the Last Bridge, on the border between the Lone-lands and the Trollshaws.Talk to Bingo on the Last Bridge, east of Ost GuruthBingo's legs are sore from the walk from Ost Guruth, and wants to find a good place to stop for the night. He said to speak with him on the Last Bridge when you are ready to look for a good place to make camp.Talk to Bingo on the Last Bridge and find a good place to make campBingo is north of the road, a short distance east of the Last Bridge, in the Trollshaws.

Bingo had a restful night, but you did not.Talk to Bingo in the Trollshaws, east of the Last Bridge, north of the road'I am happy for Theodore and Haley, and am proud of the small part we played in their newfound appreciation for each other. I wonder for how many Treasure Hunting Lessons that appreciation will last?'

Bingo laughs, and consults his map.

'Our own journey must continue, ${PLAYER}! Judging from these distances, we will be able to leave the Lone-lands today and come into the wooded hills and dales of the Trollshaws. It sounds like an ominous place! Are you ready? Let us walk eastward, my adventurous companion, and we will reassess our course when we come to the Last Bridge, marked here on my map!''Our own journey must continue, ${PLAYER}! Judging from these distances, we will be able to leave the Lone-lands today and come into the wooded hills and dales of the Trollshaws. It sounds like an ominous place! Are you ready? Let us walk eastward, my adventurous companion, and we will reassess our course when we come to the Last Bridge, marked here on my map!''That was quite a walk, and now my legs are sore. It does not look like so great a distance on my map, but it is one thing to see the land depicted in tiny pictures, and quite another to walk it step by step!

'We should look for a good spot to stop for the night, ${PLAYER}. Speak to me and we can look for such a place just off the road.''All right, let us find a spot not too far from the road where we can make camp for the night.''That was quite a restful sleep, my friend, and I am rejuvenated and ready to continue the journey! Wait... you have such dark circles under your eyes, and look like you are dead on your very feet! Do you need another rest, ${PLAYER}? I can hardly believe it!'

<rgb=#00FF00>Bert Bartleby has arrived at the Rivendell stables.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A New Friend' (Recommended Level: 35)</rgb>Tham Ornen has been occupied by forces from Angmar. What value they see in the ruins is unknown.The Rangers are at Tinnudir, south-west of Tham Ornen.

One of the Rangers will be eager to examine the satchel and determine what its contents mean.Looking into the satchel you see several documents written in the Black Speech. You cannot read what they say, but believe if you brought them to the Rangers at Tinnudir they may be able to translate them.'Interesting. It will take some time to go through this, as I can sense evil on most of the documents, and it is all written in the tongue of the Enemy.'At the request of the Ranger Gondranc, you went to the camp of the Earth-kin north-east of Esteldín to retrieve a book of leatherworking lore Asikko, the Earth-kin's chieftain, had promised him. With this lore, he will be able to make much better armour for the people of Trestlebridge.Aurochs graze the plains and hills of Rhunenlad and eastern Nan Amlug.

Gondranc learned from Asikko's book that the Earth-kin use the fur mantles which grow upon the shoulders of the aurochs to pad their armour. He has asked you to hunt aurochs and bring him these mantles.'According to Asikko's book, Earth-kin hunters use the mantles of thick fur that grow upon the shoulders of the aurochs to pad the inside of their armour. This fur provides both warmth and comfort.

'I need you to make your way into the fields of Rhunenlad or eastern Nan Amlug in search of these mantles. You will have the best results hunting the aurochs bulls. Do not attempt this hunt alone, for the aurochs are mighty beasts.

'Be careful, ${PLAYER}. You need not sacrifice yourself for the sake of gathering furs.''Ah, four fine mantles. You have taken great pains to see these delivered, and you have earned my gratitude. I will soon be done with my work, and the people of Trestlebridge will have the armour that they need.

'This assault from the North has taken a difficult toll on the people of the North Downs, but with your help, I believe they will prevail.'Tham Andalath is the furthest of the estates from Tinnudir. What was once one of the most opulent houses in all of Evendim is now desecrated by goblins.Tham Andalath is north-east of Tinnudir and east of Tham Ornen.

You found a ceremonial weapon in Tham Andalath and believe you should gather more to take to the Rangers of Tinnudir.Collect ornamental blades (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Rangers are at Tinnudir, south-west of Tham Andalath.

One of the Rangers will be eager to examine the ceremonial blades you found.This weapon is dull, but not worn. It looks old, but ceremonial. Perhaps you should collect a few more and bring them to the Rangers at Tinnudir?'These are in much worse shape than I could have hoped. Time and the goblins have not been kind to them. Hopefully, we can still glean some value from them.'The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of fine skin to be made to them.You have accepted a task to collect fine skin from the beasts in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to a collection box. There is a collection box at Thorenhad in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine skin from the beasts surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to a collection box. There is a collection box at Thorenhad in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine skin from the beasts surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of very sharp beaks to be made to them.You have accepted a task to collect very sharp beaks from the birds in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Very sharp beaks from the birds surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Very sharp beaks from the birds surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Ranger Forchon is not sure of your trustworthiness. He has asked you to defeat some of the bats and barghests that prowl into Parth Aduial from the Fields of Fornost as a way of proving yourself to him.Bats and barghests can be found in Parth Aduial, especially near the pass leading to the Fields of Fornost.

Forchon has expressed his distrust of you, but might become less hostile if you fulfill his request.Defeat bats in Parth Aduial (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat barghests in Parth Aduial (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ranger Forchon is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, in Evendim.

You have defeated bats and barghests in Parth Aduial, as Forchon requested. The Ranger waits to hear of your success.'You say you have been sent here by Aragorn? Sure you have, and I am Arvedui Last-king.

'Calenglad is right not to trust you, if you ask me. Still, I am not going to turn down a helping hand. If you can convince me that you are not a robber trying to make off with precious relics of the Dúnedain, I will put in a good word for you with Calenglad. But you will not have it easy!

'Bats and barghests have begun creeping into Parth Aduial from the Fields of Fornost, and we Rangers have been too busy dealing with the tomb-robbers of Evendim to drive off the creatures. Head east toward Fornost, defeat the winged beasts and their fell hounds in Parth Aduial, and then return to me with word of your deeds.''You return, and in victory? I confess that I did not fully expect either of those tidings.

'There is more work to be done, ${PLAYER}.''Every minute you stand here allows bats and barghests from the Fields of Fornost to pass into Parth Aduial, ${PLAYER}. You are not improving my opinion of you with inaction!'Ost Forod was once a stronghold protecting the trade routes and gateway to Forochel. Now it is a shell of its former glory, and there is much blame to go around.Kelsey West is in Tinnudir.

Orchalwë is distrustful of the young man loitering at the Ranger's camp on Tinnudir and wants to know his motives.Talk to Kelsey West at TinnudirOrchalwë is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë will be interested to hear what Kelsey had to say.Talk to Orchalwë at TinnudirKelsey West is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë asked you to inform Kelsey that the Rangers would help.Talk to Kelsey West at TinnudirBasil Gummidge is in Ost Forod, north-east of Tinnudir.

Kelsey West and Orchalwë directed you to seek out the arbiter of Ost Forod and let him know the Rangers are ready to uphold their end of the pact.Talk to Basil Gummidge at Ost Forod'That boy over there -- Kelsey is his name, I believe -- showed up in camp recently and has been lurking around. I am not sure if he is perhaps scouting us to see what of value he can make off with, but I do not trust him.

'Do me a favour: approach him and converse with him... perhaps you can discover his motives.''Kelsey is just over there. Do not worry... I will not take my eyes off of him.''I have come to this camp to enlist the aid of the Rangers with the problems that face my people, but these Men seem so distrustful!

'It's almost as if they think Ost Forod is full of tomb-robbers! Certainly times are hard, and some of my people have turned to plunder, but there are far more decent people at my home than the unsavoury sort these Rangers fear.''Will no-one here help us? The winter will come soon, and the stores are low.''It pains me to admit that the boy is not wrong. My very impression of him proved that just now. He comes asking for our help, something we used to openly give to all, and we watch from the distance and shadows, distrustful and on guard. Today, we shall end that.

'We once had an agreement in place with the people of Ost Forod. They would watch the northern lands and protect the road from Forochel. In return, we would supply them and see they were taken care of. We have both lapsed in our parts of the agreement.

'Tell Kelsey that the Rangers will uphold their portion of the agreement and will help Ost Forod. He may not know what that means, but I suspect he will direct you to see someone who does.''I only hope it is not too late for this pact to be restored. With Angmar coming, we cannot afford for it not to be.''They said that they would help? This is marvelous news to me. But words are not of much value. The winter is coming, and the winds from Forochel are cold this year.

'There is much to do in Ost Forod. There are some rewarding bounties posted, and an adventurer of your skills could well find yourself gainfully employed in no time! Just cross the bridge back to the mainland, then follow the road to the cross-roads. Once you reach it, take the northward road up into Tyrn Fornech, and you'll see Ost Forod in no time!

'One of the arbiters, Basil Gummidge, will be pleased to see you and hear of your news from the Rangers.''We have let our part of this bargain slip for too long. Please help us restore it. Make Basil see that I am genuine in my intent.''Basil is the arbiter of Ost Forod... he will know what all has to be done around the town to prepare.''Kelsey finally got someone to come help us? He's a good boy, but has taken his time finding aid.

'You say Orchalwë knows you are here as well? He has a desire to restore the pact that we once both honoured? The Rangers let their end go long before we did.

'It will take some time for me to determine if I believe he is being truthful.'Tham Andalath is the furthest of the estates from Tinnudir. What was once one of the most opulent houses in all of Evendim is now desecrated by goblins.Tham Andalath is north east of Tinnudir and east of Tham Ornen.

Brogdagnir asked you to venture to Tham Andalath and defeat many of the goblins encamped there.Defeat goblins at Tham Andalath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brogdagnir is at Tinnudir, south west of Tham Andalath.

Brogdagnir will be pleased to hear the goblins at Tham Andalath have been defeated.'Tham Andalath is an important place for rangers. It is there the blade we wield was designed and the first set was made and presented to our king. A ranger holds his blade with the highest of esteem. Within combat, a ranger and his blade are one.

'I cannot stand by while goblins desecrate the home of the smith that gave us our blades. Foul creatures, they eat and destroy everything, not thinking for what happens when they are gone. A stain is left upon the earth that will never be cleansed.

'I ask you to go to Tham Andalath for me and see if it can be saved from that fate. To do that, the goblins must not be allowed to stay within its walls any longer.''They have been driven off you say? That pleases me. We have honoured the memeory of a hero of my people.''Just thinking about goblins makes me want to bathe.'Forchon is not yet convinced of your good intentions and has sent you to acquire rubbings of the graves at Gorthost as further proof.Gorthost is by a collapsed wall in Parth Aduial, north of Ost Heryn and the road that runs east towards the Fields of Fornost.

Forchon has sent you to the small graveyard at Gorthost to make rubbings of the stones there with charcoal and parchment. He bade you beware of the bats and barghests that surely prowl nearby.Make rubbings of graves at Gorthost (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Forchon is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, in Evendim.

Ranger Forchon is waiting for you to return with the grave-rubbings you made at Gorthost.There is a book-shelf containing lineages at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, in Evendim.

Forchon has asked you to file the grave-rubbings you made in the book-shelf containing lineages at the Ranger-camp on Tinnudir.Ranger Forchon is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, in Evendim.

Having filed the grave rubbings with the lineages, you should now return and speak with Forchon.'It is not yet enough, ${PLAYER}. I need you to show me that you hold the history of the Dúnedain as important as I or Astiul. If Calenglad does decide to help you, I will not be held responsible if you turn to plunder.

'In Parth Aduial, just north of the road that runs to the Fields of Fornost, there is a small graveyard called Gorthost; the names, birth-dates and death-dates listed on the stones there have not been recorded. Take this charcoal and this parchment and make rubbings of the graves at Gorthost. Beware of the bats and barghests that are sure to prowl nearby. Gorthost is by a collapsed wall north of the ruins of Ost Heryn, and also north of the road.

'If you return from this errand, our ancestral records will be more complete, and you may have proved your worth to me. Perhaps.''Gorthost is by a collapsed wall in Parth Aduial, ${PLAYER}, north of Ost Heryn and the road that runs eastward toward the Fields of Fornost. I am still waiting for you to use the charcoal and parchment I gave you to make rubbings of the graves there.''Very good. These rubbings are clear and easy to read, and the information will be most helpful in piecing together some gaps in the histories.

'Go across the way to the book-shelf containing our lineages and place these grave-rubbings on the right shelf. Come back and speak to me when you have done this.''We keep the lineages right over there, ${PLAYER}. Go put the rubbings you made on the right shelf. It will not take more than a moment.''Thank you for your efforts, ${PLAYER}. For my part, I believe that you have been sent by Aragorn, and I hope that Calenglad will help you with due haste.

'I will tell him that you are not a tomb-robber, and that your assistance has been of great help to me.'The fields of Evendim, also called the Twilight Fields, feature several estates and an ancient fortress. After much hard work, all of them have been reclaimed for the Rangers.Orchalwë is at Tinnudir, west of Torogethir's camp.

Orchalwë will be pleased to hear that the problems at Ost Heryn have been resolved.Talk to Orchalwë at Tinnudir'You should return to Tinnudir and speak with Orchalwë. He will be pleased to hear about our progress here in Ost Heryn. If I am correct, with the issues here resolved, the Twilight Fields are silent for the first night in a very long time.''Talk to Orchalwë on the isle of Tinnudir.''Ost Heryn is silent, and the fields are peaceful. This is a great day, and one that would be celebrated were there not so much more to do. Annúminas, the Way of Kings, the Eavespires... all are in need of aid. Our inaction here for so long has left a great deal to do. First, however, we must look to the north and Ost Forod.'Bingo is ready to stop for the night, and wants to set up a camp just within the Trollshaws.You have left the road and now Bingo wants to set up a camp-site for the night.Talk to BingoBingo has asked you to look for suitable tinder for building a fire.Collect suitable tinder (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bingo must have chosen a good place for you to build a fire by now.Bingo is sitting by the fire you built in the Trollshaws, ready to turn in for the night.Talk to Bingo by the camp-fireBingo is asleep, but someone needs to stay awake and alert to any dangers. That someone is you!The hours of the night have passed... eventfully... and now Bingo has finally stirred.Talk to Bingo by the camp-fire'It is a shame I woke up when I did. I would like to have experienced more of that lovely dream! Breakfast was nearly served, and a breakfast in the Shire is better than one anywhere else... including and especially the Trollshaws, I expect!''It is getting dark. I will find a good place for us to make a fire, if you will find some tinder for it!''Have a seat! We may be adventurers now, ${PLAYER}, but there is no reason to always be on our feet! A rest is a rest, and we are deserving of it by now, wouldn't you say? We have come a good distance today, and the Lone-lands are behind us. The Trollshaws await!''Ah, I am most refreshed, and had quite a lovely dream. It put me in mind of being a young lad again, with the smell of breakfast drifting into my bedroom. I heard many folk rattling about outside my room, and the banging of pots and pans as the kitchen was being made ready.

'Did you have any pleasant dreams, my friend?'The identity of those who once resided within Tham Varan has been lost to history. Perhaps the ruins still hold the answer.Tham Varan is east of Tinnudir and beyond Tham Nambarth.

Gúrhebnir asked you to venture to Tham Varan and inspect several crumbling seals to determine the history of the ruins.Inspect the crumbling seals (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Rangers are at Tinnudir, west of Tham Varan and Tham Nambarth.

One of the Rangers will be interested to learn of the few lines you were able to read.Talk to Gúrhebnir at TinnudirThe crumbled remains of this seal offer little knowledge to whom it might have belonged. Perhaps if you took rubbings of several of the seals around Tham Varan and brought them to the Rangers, they might be able to identify them.'Those lines you read to me indicate Tham Varan was owned by a family from the south, likely Gondor. This is very interesting indeed.''I wonder who the lords and ladies of Tham Varan once were....'Ost Heryn is an ancient fortress built to support Fornost. In the time of need, however, its leader betrayed his oath.Torogethir is at his camp in Parth Aduial.

Torogethir has several tasks he wishes you to complete for him.Complete Writs of HonourComplete Unfulfilled DutyComplete Payment of an Ancient DebtTorogethir is at his camp in Parth Aduial.

Torogethir will want to speak to you about what happened at Ost Heryn.Talk to Torogethir in Parth Aduial'Ost Heryn is an ancient fortress made to watch the pass between the North Downs and Evendim. She was to answer the call of Fornost and support it if the need ever came. The need -- and the call -- did come, but support did not. Ost Heryn abandoned Fornost.

'For that, all who served within were cursed, branded as Oath-breakers, and left to stand their posts until they redeemed themselves. The time for redemption has come, and we are going to deliver it.

'I have devised a couple of plans for doing this. I do not know if any will succeed, but I believe they are all worth trying.''It will take a few days for everything at Ost Heryn to settle down. When things of this power and nature are stirred, they do not immediately calm. I will watch the progress and report back if we were succesful.'Ost Heryn is an ancient fortress built to support Fornost. In the time of need, however, its leader betrayed his oath.Ost Heryn is south of Torogethir's camp.

Torogethir asked you to venture to Ost Heryn and defeat the Oath-breaker commander there.Defeat the Oath-breaker commanderTorogethir is at his camp, north of Ost Heryn.

Torogethir will be pleased to hear that the Oath-breaker commander has tasted defeat and has vanished.Talk to Torogethir at his camp in Parth Aduial'When Fornost sent forth the call, Ost Heryn did not answer. She remained dormant, allowing the fortress of the North Downs to fall to the Enemy. That decision rested solely with its commander.

'He has paid dearly for his decision that day. His spirit stands before the doorway into Ost Heryn, ever watchful of all around. He has never been allowed to stop or rest. The more I watch him the more I pity him... punished for eternity for a decision made in a moment ages ago.

'I feel he has paid his due and wish to end his penance. For this to be done, I believe that he must share in the fate that Fornost suffered on that dire day: he must taste the pain of the defeat himself.''The commander will not know peace until he knows defeat.''I sensed the commander leave this place not long ago. I believe he has finally found peace after all these long years. I thank you for delivering it to him.'Ivy has grown over the ruins of Tham Nambarth, threatening to rip the building brick by brick.Tham Nambarth is east of Tinnudir.

Gúrhebnir asked you to go to Tham Nambarth and apply his concoction to the ivy you find there.Use the Concoction on the Ivy (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gúrhebnir is at Tinnudir, west of Tham Nambarth.

Gúrhebnir will be discouraged to hear his concoction did nothing immediately.'Tham Nambarth was once a great hall. You can see that in what still stands today. This will not be the case for long though, and not because of looters, or goblins, or anything else like that. It is the ivy! It grows deep into the cracks in the walls and slowly pushes the bricks apart until nothing is left standing.

'I have brewed a concoction which I believe the ivy will absorb, draw into its roots and then slowly perish. Thus saving the ruins for a great deal of time to come.

'Head to Tham Nambarth and use this on the ivy, let me know what happens.''That ivy is not going to wither away itself...trust me on that.''It did nothing you say? That perplexes me. It definitely affected the ivy I used it on. I wonder if the ivy at Tham Nambarth is resistent somehow or maybe it just takes longer. I will check on it in a few days I think...'Elk have taken over Tham Nambarth and soon they shall bring larger predators.Tham Nambarth is east of Tinnudir.

Brogdagnir asked you to venture to Tham Nambarth and drive off the elk within.Defeat Elk at Tham Nambarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brogdagnir is at Tinnudir, west of Tham Nambarth.

Brogdagnir will be pleased to hear the ruins of his family are now empty.'Tham Nambarth was where my family once resided, in an age long ago. It saddens me to see it in such a state of disrepair, though I know it is amongst the nicer estates within the hills.

'It is threatened now, though by a source that itself is not threatening. A herd of elk have settled within its walls. They are not a problem alone, however soon they will bring predators. Wolves, hungry robbers, Wargs and goblins from the northern hills.

'For Tham Nambarth to be safe it must be kept empty. Head there and defeat the elk you find within. It is the only way for the ruins to survive further.''So the deed is done? It is a shame those creatures had to perish, for they knew naught the trouble they would bring down up on those ruins.''Though it saddens me to ask you to do this, the elk must be forced from Tham Nambarth, by whatever means necessary.'Tham Andalath is the furthest of the estates from Tinnudir. What was once one of the most opulent houses in all of Evendim is now desecrated by goblins.Tham Andalath is north east of Tinnudir, east of Tham Ornen.

Gúrhebnir asked you to quench the camp-fires at Tham Andalath believing if they are not burning the goblins will flee.Put out the camp-fires (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gúrhebnir is at Tinnudir, south west of Tham Andalath.

Gúrhebnir will be pleased to hear the lights of Tham Andalath are now dark.'Goblins are amusing creatures. Easily misunderstood, they are more clever than often given credit for and very devious. However, if done correctly, one can trick them into believing something much larger is going on around them and scare them into a panic.

'That is what we will do to the goblins at Tham Andalath. We can defeat as many as we want, but goblins are thicker than the flies at night, and will only return. We must make them not want to.

'The flames at Tham Andalath are the key. Night and day they are constantly burning, the goblins never let them go out. They must fear the darkness and believe something out there will get them if the fires are not lit. If you were to put them out, I believe the goblins may leave all on their own.''I wonder what it is in the dark they are afraid of.... I know those fields well....''A scout that swung through just before you arrived reported seeing goblins fleeing Tham Andalath already. Great work.'Tham Varan is slowly being dismantled by a group of tomb robbers, stripping it of everything of even the smallest value.Tham Varan is east of Tinnudir and Tham Nambarth.

Brogdagnir asked you to venture to Tham Varan and drive off the tomb robbers there.Defeat Tomb-robbers at Tham Varan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brogdagnir is at Tinnudir, west of Tham Varan and Tham Nambarth.

Brogdagnir will be pleased to hear the robbers will think twice before returning to Tham Varan.'It is not enough that we battle nature and time as enemies to our history, we have to deal with greedy robbers who want to deny us our past and identity. It cannot be tolerated.

'Right this very moment a group of tomb robbers is combing through what is left of Tham Varan. Everything they find will be sold to whoever will give them a spare copper. They think nothing of the history that is lost.

'Go to Tham Varan and drive them off. Show them that we, the rangers, have returned to claim our lands.''Do you think they got the message to not come back? I fear it is only a matter of time until they return. Greed is a powerful force.''If they would but come to us we would help them but instead they rob and steal. They must be shown the error of their ways.'Defeat snow-beasts in the Misty Mountains.Defeat snow-beasts in the Misty MountainsDefeat many snow-beasts in the Misty Mountains.Defeat snow-beasts in the Misty MountainsTham Andalath is the furthest of the estates from Tinnudir. What was once one of the most opulent houses in all of Evendim is now desecrated by goblins.Nadfaron, Brogdagnir and Gúrhebnir are all at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë has asked you to work with three of the other Rangers in driving the goblins from Tham Andalath.Work with NadfaronWork with BrogdagnirWork with GúrhebnirOrchalwë is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë will be pleased to hear Tham Andalath has been cleared of goblins.'Angmar we must put aside and move on to the other threats we face while we still have the time. There is only one estate left within the fields. Getting it back within our control will be a great boon to us and allow us to focus our resources on preparation and routing out the last of the tomb robbers.

'Tham Andalath was the ancient home of a great smith, one who made the weapons for the line of kings themselves. While most of his work has been lost to history, his influence is seen in every blade we wield.

'Goblins now camp within the walls of the ruins of Tham Andalath. Foul, stupid, smelly creatures. Compared to the organized forces of Angmar, cleaning them out should be an easy task for you.''The goblins will not leave Tham Andalath on their own, if only life were that easy for us these days.''With Tham Andalath the last of the estates falls back within our control. You have done great work here for us in the fields, but it is not done yet.'Tham Andalath is the furthest of the estates from Tinnudir. What was once one of the most opulent houses in all of Evendim is now desecrated by goblins.Tham Andalath is north east of Tinnudir, east of Tham Ornen.

Orchalwë's journal speaks next of Tham Andalath, a ruin of a great weaponsmith's home, now defiled by goblins.Defeat goblins at Tham Andalath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Extinguish the camp-fires (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'Tham Andalath was the ancient home of a great smith, one who made the weapons for the line of kings themselves. While most of his work has been lost to history, his influence is seen in every blade we wield. Goblins now camp within the walls of the ruins... foul and wicked creatures.

'They are more clever than I often give them credit for and very devious. However, if done correctly, one can trick them into believing something much larger is going on around them and frighten them into a panic. Night and day, the flames of Tham Andalath are constantly burning... the goblins never let them go out. They must fear the darkness and believe something out there will get them if the fires are not lit.

'If we were to be both the quencher of those flames and the object of terror in the darkness, the goblins will flee from the ruins with great haste. Once they are gone, another part of our history falls back within our hands.'Tham Nambarth is the most well kept of the estates that lie in ruin within the Twilight Fields.Nadfaron, Brogdagnir and Gúrhebnir are all at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë has asked you to work with three of the other rangers to help Tham Nambarth.Work with NadfaronWork with BrogdagnirWork with GúrhebnirOrchalwë is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë will be pleased to hear that Tham Nambarth should be safe for the near future.'The Twilight Fields are dotted with several ruins of ancient estates, places where the opulent life was on full display during the height of the kingdom. They also managed the vast farming lands that once covered these hills.

'Tham Nambarth is the nearest of those estates and our first priority. While the threats to it are not great they must not be trivalized in their importance, for Tham Nambarth is being reclaimed by nature herself.

'Speak to my colleagues here, they each have a different plan for ensuring the estate's longevity.''I have heard back from my men and they all are pleased with the work you have done in Tham Nambarth. There is much yet to do within the fields.''While it is in the best shape, Tham Nambarth must not be ignored and provides us a good starting spot.'Tham Nambarth is the most well-kept of the estates that lie in ruin within the Twilight Fields.Tham Nambarth is in the Twilight Fields, east of Tinnudir.

Orchalwë's journal details several things that are currently assailing the ruins of Tham Nambarth.Defeat elk at Tham Nambarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tear down ivy at Tham Nambarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tham Nambarth is the nearest of the ruined estates and our first priority. While the threats to it are not great, they must not be trivialized in their importance, for Tham Nambarth is being reclaimed by nature itself.

Tham Nambarth was once a great hall... you can see that in what still stands today. This will not be the case for long, though, and not because of looters or goblins. It is the ivy! It grows deep into the cracks in the walls and slowly pushes the bricks apart until nothing is left standing.

It is also threatened by a source that itself is not threatening: a herd of elk has settled within its walls. They are not a problem of themselves; however, soon they will bring predators: wolves, hungry robbers, Wargs, and goblins from the northern hills.Tham Ornen has been occupied by forces from Angmar. What value they see in the ruins is unknown.Nadfaron, Brogdagnir and Gúrhebnir are all at  Tinnudir.

Orchalwë has asked you to work with three of the other rangers to discover Angmar's intent in Tham Ornen.Work with NadfaronWork with BrogdagnirWork with GúrhebnirOrchalwë is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë will be interested to hear what you discovered within Tham Ornen.'You turn a blind eye to a corner of this region for just a moment and something momentous happens that you missed. Somehow, with all of our focus on the tomb raiders and bandits in the south, a scouting force from Angmar has slipped into one of the ruins.

'This is a disaster for us on several fronts. Something here is of enough value for Angmar to send a force to find it. Something that we do not yet know of ourselves. If they were to find something it could tip the tides of war. Not to mention what could happen if they find the refuge of Tinnudir. If they use that to somehow find Esteldin... I cannot entertain those thoughts.

'We must discover the intent of this scouting force and determine if more are following them. This must be done with posthaste. No delay can be afforded.''I have talked with the others and gone over what you found. There is indeed a force coming to Evendim but there are two things we have in our advantage.

'First, they will take a while to get here, giving us needed time for preparations. Second, they are headed for Annuminas, a battleground no-one knows better than we.

'We must get the rest of Evendim in order before they arrive. There are several other threats and issues we must resolve, for once they are here, war will come with them.''We must know what Angmar wants with Tham Ornen lest we risk losing everything.'Tham Ornen has been occupied by forces from Angmar. What value they see in the ruins is unknown.Tham Ornen is north east of Tinnudir.

Orchalwë's journal speaks next of Tham Ornen, a ruin now controlled by a scouting force from Angmar.Defeat Angmarim at Tham Ornen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy Angmarim orders at Tham Ornen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'You turn a blind eye to a corner of this region for just a moment and something momentous happens that you missed. Somehow, with all of our focus on the tomb-raiders and brigands in the south, a scouting force from Angmar has slipped into one of the ruins.

'This is a disaster for us on several fronts. Something here is of enough value for Angmar to send a force to find it, something that we do not yet know of ourselves. If they were to find this thing, it could turn the tides of war, not to mention what could happen if they find the refuge of Tinnudir. If they use that knowledge to somehow find Esteldín...I cannot entertain those thoughts.

'We must drive them out, and we must sow confusion among those who remain. Their plans have been tacked to the walls of the ruins. If their numbers were thinned and their plans destroyed, perhaps we will stand a greater chance.'Tham Varan, the south easterly estate, is shrouded in mystery. Its identity has been lost to time and the robbers there carry off more clues every day.Nadfaron, Brogdagnir and Gúrhebnir are all Tinnudir.

Orchalwë has asked you to work with three of the other rangers to recover the identity of Tham Varan.Work with NadfaronWork with BrogdagnirWork with GúrhebnirOrchalwë is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë will be pleased to hear that Tham Varan's identity has been learned and its history protected.'The next estate within the fields is Tham Varan. It is a place we know little about. The records we have found only describe its name. It is an enigma indeed.

'The only thing I know is that the tomb robbers have been swarming over it as of late. Given our focus elsewhere it has been ripe for the picking and they have slowly stripped out any valuable information we could find to glean its identity.

'Work with the other rangers here to see what we can learn. I would very much like to rescue the identity of Tham Varan from its current place, another nameless faceless forgotten ruin.''I am beginning to like you and can see why my fellow rangers speak so kindly about you. You have done good work with the first two estates, however the next are in more dire situations.''History will not delay in stealing Tham Varan from us, I suggest you not either.'Tham Varan, the south-easterly estate, is shrouded in mystery. Its identity has been lost to time, and the robbers there carry off more clues every day.Tham Varan is east of Tinnudir and Tham Nambarth.

Orchalwë's journal speaks next of Tham Varan, a ruin of unknown ownership besieged by tomb-robbers.Defeat tomb-robbers at Tham Varan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'The next estate within the fields is Tham Varan. It is a place we know little about. The records we have found only describe its name. It is an enigma indeed.

'The only thing I know is that the tomb-robbers have been swarming over it as of late. Given our focus elsewhere, it has been ripe for the picking, and they have slowly stripped out any valuable information we could find to glean its identity.

'It is not enough that we battle nature and time as enemies to our history, we have to deal with greedy robbers who want to deny us our past and identity. It cannot be tolerated.'Tham Ornen has been occupied by forces from Angmar. What value they see in the ruins is unknown.Tham Ornen is north east of Tinnudir.

Nadfaron asked you to venture to Tham Ornen and gather orders from the Angmarim camp.Nadfaron is at Tinnudir, south west of Tham Ornen.

Nadfaron will be eager to examine the orders you found at the Angmarim camp in Tham Ornen.'Surely you have been informed of the incursion? This is drastic news. I fear this small force is just a scouting party. Many more will follow, far more than we can handle in open combat.

'If we are to defeat them we must know what their goal is. Where in Evendim are they going? Why are they coming now? What is going on to prompt this?

'Head to the small encampment in Tham Ornen. Our scouts witnessed the angmarim tacking things to the walls there. I believe it to be copies of plans and discovered information. Gather up several of these and bring them back to me.''We cannot go into battle against Angmar blind. We must know what they are after.''So, they are heading to Annuminas itself and are seeking something within the ancient flooded ruins. Fortunately, it looks like we have some time to prepare before the main force arrives.'The estates east of Tinnudir, within the Twilight Fields, once belonged to families of great wealth and standing within the North Kingdom.Orchalwë's journal can be found among your possessions.

Orchalwë's journal details what needs be done to reclaim the Fields of Twilight.Read Orchalwë's journal and reclaim Tham NambarthOrchalwë's journal can be found among your possessions.

Orchalwë's journal details what needs be done to reclaim the Fields of Twilight.Read Orchalwë's journal and reclaim Tham VaranOrchalwë's journal can be found among your possessions.

Orchalwë's journal details what needs be done to reclaim the Fields of Twilight.Read Orchalwë's journal and reclaim Tham OrnenOrchalwë's journal can be found among your possessions.

Orchalwë's journal details what needs to be done to reclaim the Fields of Twilight.Read Orchalwës journal and claim Tham AndalathOrchalwë is at Tinnudir.

Orchalwë will be eager to get his journal back and discuss your exploits within the Fields of Twilight.Talk to Orchalwë at Tinnudir'The land to the east of here is called the Fields of Twilight. Within it are the ruins of several ancient estates. These places were once home to powerful families within the kingdom. They grew grain and raised animals used to feed the city and provided work for many of the people.

'Now, they are disgraced by robbers, overtaken by nature, desecrated by goblins, and one now even serves as a camp for a force from Angmar. It is an affront to all who draw their heritage from the shores of this lake.

'I have detailed in this journal notes about the estates and the threats availing them. Please, take it and read from it as you explore the Fields of Twilight. It will guide you if you choose to help us reclaim these ancient ruins.''The Estates within the Twilight Fields are now within our control. There is still some work to do at Tham Ornen, but I believe we can now handle the situation that remains.

'You have done great work for us. Perhaps one day my blade will join with yours, and we will stand before our enemies together.''My journal contains all the information you will need.''My journal contains all the information you will need.''My journal contains all the information you will need.''My journal contains all the information you will need.'The faded tapestries of Tham Nambarth tell of a heraldry long since lost to Evendim.Tham Nambarth is east of Tinnudir.

You should seek scraps of ancient tapestries for the Rangers of Tinnudir.Collect scraps of ancient tapestries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Rangers are at Tinnudir, west of Tham Nambarth.

One of the Rangers at Tinnudir will be eager to examine the pieces of tapestry you collected.The tapestry shred you have found is old and faded but some details can still be made out. Perhaps if you can find more, it would be of interest to the Rangers of Tinnudir.'Look at how even after these years the colours have still retained their vibrance. I wonder if the pieces you found all belong to one tapestry or to many....'Torogethir monitors the pass from the North Downs into Evendim and watches over the fortresses of Fornost and Ost Heryn.Torogethir's camp is along the road to the North Downs, east of Tinnudir and north of Ost Heryn.

Orchalwë asked you to find Torogethir and help him in his efforts to solve the problems facing Ost Heryn.Talk to Torogethir in Parth Aduial'The estates are back in order, and while there is still much to do within them, that is work we can handle ourselves over time. The Twilight Fields are not yet safe, however. There still remains Ost Heryn.

'Torogethir is charged with watching over this ruined fortress, and while he has been able to do some work to prevent the problems there from growing, he has not had the time nor support to solve them.

'Follow the road east, towards the North Downs. You will find Torogethir on a small hill north of the road above the ruins of Ost Heryn.''So, after dealing with every other problem they could find first, they finally send help to me. Better late than never, I suppose.''Torogethir has long watched over Ost Heryn. He knows much of its history and secrets.'Defeat trolls in the Misty Mountains.Defeat trolls in the Misty MountainsDefeat trolls in the Trollshaws.Defeat trolls in the TrollshawsDefeat many trolls in the Misty Mountains.Defeat trolls in the Misty MountainsDefeat many trolls in the Trollshaws.Defeat trolls in the TrollshawsOst Heryn is an ancient fortress built to support Fornost. In the time of need, however, its leader betrayed his oath.Ost Heryn is south of Torogethir's camp.

Torogethir asked you to defeat some of the tomb-robbers skulking about Ost Heryn.Defeat tomb-robbers at Ost Heryn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Torogethir is at his camp, north of Ost Heryn.

Torogethir will be pleased to hear a number of tomb-robbers have been driven from Ost Heryn.Talk to Torogethir at his camp in Parth Aduial'I have spent many a day observing the spirits within Ost Heryn. They move as if they are still on patrol, walking the walls and paths, seeking out the Enemy. Many times they found one.

'Tomb-robbers have tried to ransack Ost Heryn, correctly presuming the ruins of the fortress hold valuables. They have not accomplished much, though... they fear the spirits and try to avoid them, for when the spirits find them, they do attack, and the robbers lose more than win.

'The robbers within the ruins present two problems. They are slowly carting off our legacy, and they are not allowing the spirits to be at rest. I wish you to drive the tomb-robbers out... we shall see if that helps to quiet the ruins.''Trust me, those tomb-robbers will not leave of their own will. Even the spirits are not enough to deter them from the treasures they seek.''Good! With those robbers out of the ruins, we shall see if the spirits calm.'Sealed vases might still contain notes, papers, or spices from a long-forgotten time.Tham Varan is east of Tinnudir and Tham Nambarth.

You should go to Tham Varan and collect several vases for the Rangers of Tinnudir.Collect vases at Tham Varan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Rangers are at Tinnudir, west of Tham Varan and Tham Nambarth.

One of the Rangers will be excited to begin examining the contents of the vases you found at Tham Varan.The seal on this vase is still tight, even after all the weather-worn years it has sat among the ruins here. Looking around, you see a few others. Perhaps the Rangers can learn something of value from their contents.'If we can discover the secrets of how our forebears ran these estates, we can begin to restore them ourselves.''It looks like at least three of these are still sealed, and the fourth may yet hold some secrets. This is wonderful!'Parr Chopley, an out-of-place hobbit at Ost Forod, awaits the delivery of broken longswords at the haven outpost of Ost Forod in Evendim.You have accepted a task to collect the broken longswords from the goblins or tomb-robbers surrounding the haven outpost of Ost Forod in Evendim. You are to deliver the items to Parr Chopley, a hobbit, living in the settlement.Wanted: Broken longswords from goblins or tomb-robbers threatening the areas surrounding the haven of Ost Forod. Deliver the items to Parr Chopley at the centre of the fortress.'It looks as though you showed this bunch a thing or two.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Parr Chopley, an out-of-place hobbit at Ost Forod, awaits the delivery of fine fur at the haven outpost of Ost Forod in Evendim.You have accepted a task to collect the fine fur from the beasts surrounding the haven outpost of Ost Forod in Evendim. You are to deliver the items to Parr Chopley, a hobbit, living in the settlement.Wanted: Fine fur from the beasts surrounding the haven of Ost Forod. Deliver the items to Parr Chopley at the centre of the fortress.Wanted: Fine fur from the beasts surrounding the haven of Ost Forod.'This will help us very nicely, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinnudir, a Ranger-outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Evendim, is home to the Rangers of Evendim. There, at the outpost, a Ranger named Astiul awaits the return of muted dust.You have been tasked with collecting muted dust from the Dead surrounding Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul within the lakeside outpost.Wanted: Muted dust from the Dead in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir. You are to deliver the items to the Ranger Astiul at the outpost on the shores of Lake Evendim.Wanted: Muted dust from the Dead in the lands surrounding the Ranger-outpost at Tinnudir.'They will not rise again to threaten any in this world.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Defeat wolves in the Trollshaws.Defeat wolves in the TrollshawsDefeat many wolves in the Trollshaws.Defeat wolves in the TrollshawsOst Heryn is an ancient fortress built to support Fornost. In the time of need, however, its leader betrayed his oath.Ost Heryn is south of Torogethir's camp.

Torogethir asked you to venture within Ost Heryn and collect several Writs of Honour from the spirits within.Collect Writs of Honour (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Torogethir is at his camp, north of Ost Heryn.

Torogethir will be eager to examine the Writs and see if there is a way he can honour them.'All of the soldiers who served at Ost Heryn signed documents that swore their allegiance and service to the North Kingdom. To truly honour that, they would have gone to defend Fornost when it was besieged. 

'Most of the soldiers at Ost Heryn were cursed due to nothing of their own doing. It is a difficult position to be in as a soldier: do you follow the orders of your commander even when you know they are wrong? Can you blame a soldier for doing so?

'They still carry those Writs of Honour after all of these years. Gather them from the spirits and return them to me. Perhaps I can find a way to consider them honoured and release them from this torment.''We must find a way to honour the oath of these spirits and release them.''For being as old as they are, the spirits have kept these writs in amazing shape. I can read every letter of the contract. Now, to see if I can find a way to honour them.'The Rangers have heard rumours that agents of Angmar plan to cut the roads to Rivendell where travellers would pass through the Trollshaws. They have asked you to patrol the areas west of the Ford of Bruinen to provide warning should such an intrusion occur.Begin your patrol at Barachen's Camp, which can be found in the hills South of the road as it runs East from the Last Bridge.Visit Barachen's CampFind the glade occupied by a set of three petrified Stone-trolls. It lies somewhere in the hills North-east of Barachen's camp.Scout the Stone-trolls' GladeScout the ruins of Drauglad, south-east of the Stone-troll's Glade.Scout the ruins of DraugladScout the Ford of Bruinen, which lies South-east of the ruins of Drauglad.Scout the Ford of BruinenReport to Handarod and tell him the results of your patrol in the Western Trollshaws.'Ah, ${PLAYER}, perhaps you can help us - we have overheard rumour that the Angmarim may soon move to cut off the road to Rivendell as it passes through the western Trollshaws. We cannot allow them to cut us off from our strongest allies! I need you to patrol the areas west of the Ford of Bruinen and keep a sharp eye out for Angmarim forces.''While the Trollshaws remain hazardous as always, it seems that the hand of the Enemy still has difficulty reaching there.

'Still, your reports of evil apparitions wandering the ruins there gives me pause. This is a new development and it does not bode well.'While Calenglad meets with Gwindeth about obtaining the Silithar for Aragorn, he wants you to render your aid to Robin Dunkley in Ost Forod.Robin Dunkley lives in Ost Forod, north-east of Tinnudir in Tyrn Fornech, in Evendim.

Calenglad has decided to meet with Gwindeth about acquiring the Silithar and has asked you to aid Robin Dunkley while he makes the journey.'Many years have passed since I first beheld Gwindeth, the Blue Lady of Nenuial, but on that day I resolved to do anything in my power to preserve her peace and the safety of the lake she loved. My duties here are twofold, oaths folding in on oaths, for I swore to Aragorn in his youth the vow I swore to his father: to uphold the line of Elendil using all of my strength.

'The protection of Gwindeth and Lake Nenuial also serves this oath, for Gwindeth is the protector of the Silithar. Long has she maintained the flood that keeps the stone safe from robbers and plunderers. True-hearted she is, but slow to trust -- she shares that in common with the Dúnedain. I must speak with her alone, but the work of keeping Evendim safe will not stand for the delay caused by my journey to her home.

'I would have you travel north-east to Ost Forod in Tyrn Fornech and offer your aid to a friend of the Rangers, Robin Dunkley. He is our ally against those who would plunder Annúminas. I will send word to Robin when I have returned from my meeting with Gwindeth.''Robin Dunkley lives in Ost Forod, among the hills of Tyrn Fornech to the north-east. I will send word to Robin when I return from my meeting with Gwindeth.''You've come from Calenglad? I am very glad to hear it, for friends of the Rangers are fewer now than they were of old. There's no shortage of trouble among the hills of Tyrn Fornech, and it seems that more and more of these ruffians appear every day!

'If we don't turn them out of Evendim, they're sure to plunder every last relic that remains from the height of the old kingdom! That would be a real shame, if you ask me.'Bingo's journey has brought the two of you into the Trollshaws, and the wooded hillsides provide a new setting for the hobbit's adventure.The Glade of Three Smiling Figures is north-east of where you and Bingo camped for the night. Bingo suggested that you might need to return to the road before striking north for the glade.Find the Glade of Three Smiling FiguresBingo is in the Stone-trolls' Glade, in the Trollshaws.Talk to Bingo at the Stone-trolls' GladeBingo has seen a lynx caught in a cruel trap at the Stone-trolls' Glade, and has asked you to free the poor creature.Free the lynx from the trapBingo is at the Stone-trolls' Glade, in the Trollshaws.

You have freed a lynx from a cruel animal trap at Bingo's request, and should speak now to the hobbit.Talk to Bingo at the Stone-trolls' GladeBingo wants to return to the road south-east of the Stone-trolls' Glade in order to continue his journey.Talk to Bingo back on the road south-east of the Stone-trolls' GladeBingo has asked you to <rgb=#FF0000>wave at the lynx</rgb> that has followed the two of you back to the road, in order to shoo it away.Bingo is on the road south-east of the Stone-trolls' Glade, in the Trollshaws.

You attempted to wave away the lynx that is following Bingo, but the creature refused to leave.Talk to Bingo on the road through the TrollshawsGlen-hinds and glen-bucks can be found throughout the Trollshaws, and from them you should be able to get the venison that Bingo has requested.Collect venison in the Trollshaws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Willem Whisker is on the road south-east of the Stone-trolls' Glade.

You have collected some venison, and should bring it to the lynx to see if that appeases his hunger.Bingo is on the road through the Trollshaws, south-east of the Stone-trolls' Glade.Talk to Bingo on the road through the TrollshawsThe entrance to the hidden valley of Rivendell is marked on Bingo's map, in the eastern heights of the Trollshaws.

Bingo is ready to travel to Rivendell, and has suggested that you go to the entrance to that hidden valley.Find the entrance to Rivendell, the hidden valleyYou have found the entrance to the hidden valley of Rivendell, and the sentinels eye your small party with suspicion.Talk to the sentinel at the entrance to the hidden valleyBingo is outside the entrance to the hidden valley of Rivendell.Talk to Bingo at the entrance to the hidden valley'I have been looking at the map that started my grand adventure, and I know where I want to go next, ${PLAYER}! If I am reading this correctly, we should be just about here. Now, do you see this area here, among the trees to the east and a little north? The author of this map has made a little drawing of three figures here in this glade. Look at their large smiles!

'I want to find this Glade of Smiling Figures, my friend. It should be to the east of our location, and a little north, but there may be some impassable rock-faces in our way. We may need to return to the road before we can strike north-east to the glade.''If I am reading this map correctly, we should be just about here. Now, do you see this area here, among the trees to the east and a little north? The author of this map has made a little drawing of three figures here in this glade. Look at their large smiles!

'I want to find this Glade of Smiling Figures, my friend. It should be to the east of our location, and a little north, but there may be some impassable rock-faces in our way. We may need to return to the road before we can strike north-east to the glade.''Well, look at that! I am glad they are made of stone, and not real trolls at all!''Look at those mighty stones! Trolls! We are fortunate indeed that they are mere statues, and not the real thing!'

Bingo's smile fades as he peers to the north-west between two of the figures, and he frowns deeply.

'Oh no, ${PLAYER}. A helpless creature has been caught in a trap! Can you help the poor thing? Such cruelty! It pains me to see it!''Oh no, ${PLAYER}. A helpless creature has been caught in a trap! Can you help the poor thing? Such cruelty! It pains me to see it!''Well, that was a good deed, my friend. The poor thing! I understand the need to catch animals for food, of course, but the construction of that trap seemed unnecessarily cruel to me. I am glad that lynx is free, and I hope no permanent damage was done to the creature!

'Let us travel to the south-east, and we can decide where next to go when we have returned to the road.''I am glad that lynx is free, and I hope no permanent damage was done to the creature!

'Let us travel to the south-east, and we can decide where next to go when we have returned to the road.''${PLAYER}, I do not want to worry you but... well, we are being followed! No, do not make any sudden movements!

'It is that lynx... it has been following us for a short while now, and it is staring at us from across the road! Can you... do you think you can <rgb=#FF0000>wave at it</rgb> to shoo it away?''That lynx has been following us for a short while now, and it is staring at us from across the road! Can you... do you think you can <rgb=#FF0000>wave at it</rgb> to shoo it away?''That did not work, ${PLAYER}! He is still staring at us! Hmm... do you think that Willem Whisker is hungry?'

Bingo turns a bright shade of red at your quizzical expression, and becomes suddenly sheepish.

'What? He needed a name, didn't he? He looks like a Willem Whisker to me. Just look at that face! Anyway, I think he must be hungry. See if you can get some suitable venison from the glen-hinds or glen-bucks in these woods. I am sure Willem would appreciate it!''I think Willem must be hungry. See if you can get some suitable venison from the glen-hinds and glen-bucks in these woods. I am sure he would appreciate it!''Why the delay? Bring that venison to Willem Whisker! He must be hungry!'The lynx happily devours the venison, and then stares up at you enigmatically.

He does look a little like a Willem Whisker, now that you think about it.'I knew it! He loved the venison! Good work, ${PLAYER}!

'I have been thinking it over, and I think we should allow him to tag along with us for as long as he wants. He is a good boy, I daresay, and it will be good to have another companion on this journey. No offence intended to you, of course! But you are not as fuzzy as Willem Whisker, are you?

'This way, W.W.! You are welcome to journey with us for as long as you wish! We go now to the next stop on my map: the entrance to Rivendell, the hidden valley!''The hidden valley of Rivendell is marked on my map. It looks as if it is among the heights of the Trollshaws, to the east.''According to my map this is the entrance to the hidden valley!''I hope they will let us into the valley! I greatly wish to see it!''What purpose brings your party to the hidden valley, ${PLAYER}? Are you on friendly terms with the one who follows you? He does seem to be well-behaved, for the moment, but I wonder if that will change when he gets hungry?'

The sentinel laughs, a musical sound.

'The lynx? No, I do not speak of the lynx, ${PLAYER}. I speak of the hungry-looking hobbit there!''I trust that you will cause no trouble within Rivendell, my friend?''Really? This Elf believes I am more of a danger to the peace of Rivendell than our new friend W.W.?'

Bingo's face is full of outrage, but he cannot maintain the mask for long, and soon he is his usual jovial self.

'These are perceptive Elves, ${PLAYER}! Willem Whisker will cause no trouble for them, but if there are any dinners to be had within Rivendell I will menace them most effectively!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Hospitable Home' (Recommended Level: 39)</rgb>Cave-claws have come down out of the Misty Mountains, plaguing the North Trollshaws.Cave-claws can be found in the North Trollshaws, in the gorges south of Thoroniel's encampment.

Thoroniel has asked you and your companions to hunt the foul cave-claws of the North Trollshaws.Defeat cave-claws in the North Trollshaws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thoroniel is posted north of the cave-claw infested gorges in the North Trollshaws.

You should return to Thoroniel and let her know that you have done as she has asked.Talk to Thoroniel'There are more threats in the North Trollshaws than just spiders, ${PLAYER}. Cave-claws have come down out of the Misty Mountains, infesting the gorges to the south. They are foul vermin, which have before only been troublesome to dwarves and Orcs, but now they defile the very stone of our lands.

'Would you help us by hunting the vile creatures?''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. Alas, there are some living creatures which do more harm than good.'Sickle-flies infest the South Trollshaws, bringing the threat of disease to the land.There are sickle-flies in the South Trollshaws.

While in the South Trollshaws, you should purge the infestation of sickle-flies.Defeat sickle-flies in the South Trollshaws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})There are sickle-flies in the South Trollshaws.

While in the South Trollshaws, you should purge the infestation of sickle-flies.Many scouts were dispatched across the Trollshaws to keep watch for signs of evil, and the sons of Elrond want to know if anything has been found.Arrod is keeping watch somewhere among the Bruinen Gorges, south-east of Thorenhad, at a position where he can overlook the road.

Narlinn has asked you to check on Arrod, currently scouting the Bruinen Gorges, though likely more focused on wooing the Elf-maiden Faimir.'You are here to help Elladan and Elrohir? I am glad of it, but I would ask you to help me as well, if you have a moment.

'My friend Arrod is a good Elf, but he is not always as cautious as he is well-intentioned, and he often gets into trouble. Master Elrond recommended that he help keep watch over the Trollshaws, and indeed he has been assigned to scout the Bruinen Gorges, but I fear that his mind is not entirely on his duty.

'All of his thoughts dwell on Faimir, an Elf-maiden of our acquaintance in Imladris, and I fear he is not paying the attention he should to his own well-being and safety. Would you seek him out in the Bruinen Gorges and make sure he has not come to harm, ${PLAYER}? He is sure to have taken up a post overlooking the road.''Please look to Arrod for me, ${PLAYER}. He is east of here, probably somewhere in the Bruinen Gorges, and though he should be keeping alert for signs of evil and ensuring his own safety, I fear he is likely daydreaming about the Elf-maiden Faimir.''You are from Thorenhad? Elladan and Elrohir entrusted me to scout the Bruinen Gorges for signs of evil, and I am doing so. I have not found much, but I must admit that I hope to find something -- there is an Elf-maid I hope to impress with my valour and my prowess in battle!'Many scouts were dispatched across the Trollshaws to keep watch for signs of evil.

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: This quest arc is intended for a full fellowship.</rgb>Thoroniel is south of the Wovenvales, a particularly dense spider-lair in the north-western corner of the North Trollshaws. Rochwen told you to take the road eastward to the marker-stone, then strike north into the wild from there.

Rochwen has asked you to aid Thoroniel, currently scouting a lair of spiders in the North Trollshaws. You should take allies with you.Talk to Thoroniel in the North Trollshaws'There have always been spiders in the North Trollshaws, but in recent days their number has multiplied, and the creatures have been spotted far afield. Barachen sent Thoroniel to investigate, and she has sent word that the situation is more grave than we had expected.

'You should travel north into the wild lands of the North Trollshaws to aid Thoroniel. Spiders are evil creatures that threaten to overwhelm good people wherever they are found.

'Thoroniel described a particularly large spider-lair, the Wovenvales, in the north-west corner of the North Trollshaws. You are certain to find her just south of the Wovenvales. Bring allies with you, for the situation sounds most grim. If you take the road east to the marker-stone, you can reach the Wovenvales by heading north off the road and making your way into the wild.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: This quest arc is intended for a full fellowship.</rgb>'At last you have arrived! The threat these spiders pose is too great. We have much work to do.'Parr Chopley let his stomach lead him too far, wandering the northern ways into Evendim, where he chanced upon the rough town of Ost Forod. Nevertheless, he has not let it daunt him. He is in the mood for a nice, hearty boar-steak, but he is concerned that he has not seen any boars in all of Evendim.Evendim is a big place. There should be boars somewhere.

Parr Chopley is convinced that there must be boars somewhere in Evendim and wants you to spend some time looking for one.Spend time looking for boars in EvendimParr Chopley is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have found no sign of any boars in Evendim and should report your sad discovery to Parr Chopley.Talk to Parr Chopley at Ost Forod'Well, another stranger to this town! Now I'm not the only one! My stomach is always getting me into trouble, and this is no exception. I was in the mood for a nice, thick boar-steak -- cooked to perfection, sizzling in its own juices -- and so I decided to go hunting in the North Moors. Well, I went a little further than planned and wound up here! Never knew anyone lived in this place... they say it's haunted!

'Well, anyway, I decided I never had Evendim-boar before, so I thought I might as well try some while I'm here... it should have been a simple matter of finding a nice, healthy boar, but you know what? I haven't seen a single boar since I've come here! That can't be right. How do people up here manage without plenty of boars to satisfy their cravings for delicious steaks? That's just unnatural!

'There have to be boars in Evendim; there just have to be! Go look for boars, ${PLAYER}, and don't come back until you've searched high and you've searched low! I'm not interested in boar-meat from other places, either; it's Evendim or nothing! Now it's a matter of professional responsibility and curiosity -- find me an Evendim-boar, and find one quick!''There must be boars in Evendim somewhere... spend some time looking for them! I can't believe that there isn't a single boar in the entire place!''No boars? No boars at all? How odd, ${PLAYER}. Now, you really looked for them, right? You didn't just waste time doing errands or working on some craft or another, right?

'No, I trust you. But this means I'll have to do without a delicious Evendim boar-steak, and that just won't do!'The Trapdoor and Flesh-gnawer spiders of the North Trollshaws suffered a terrible defeat at your hands.Rochwen is encamped near the Last Bridge in the South Trollshaws, far to the south of Thoroniel's camp in the North Trollshaws.

Thoroniel bade you carry a report of your campaign against the spiders of the North Trollshaws back to Rochwen.Talk to Rochwen'You should return now to Rochwen at the Last Bridge. Bring her a report of our endeavours here...she will want to know what has happened. She is likely still encamped with Alphlanc at the Last Bridge in the South Trollshaws, far to the south of here.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}, and may the stars light your path.''It is as we feared...the evil of the Enemy is summoning all dark things to its banner.

'Thank you for your aid, ${PLAYER}. I will send word to Master Elrond.'Ballard Wilmer had an encounter with white Wargs in the hills of Tyrn Fornech and is now deathly afraid of the creatures.White Wargs stalk the hills of Tyrn Fornech, but can especially be found in the vicinity of the Duskencleft, north of Ost Forod.

Ballard Wilmer told you of his harrowing encounter with a Warg and has asked you to defeat some of the vicious white Wargs of Tyrn Fornech.Defeat White Wargs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ballard Wilmer is at the old watch-post of Ost Forod, in the hills of Tyrn Fornech, south of the Duskencleft.

You have defeated many white Wargs and should now report your victory to Ballard Wilmer.'Have you encountered the fearsome white Wargs of Tyrn Fornech yet, ${PLAYER}? The creatures are vicious, bloodthirsty beasts, and they strike without warning! A few nights ago, I was taking in the air around the northern wall of Ost Forod, and I thought I saw something gleaming in the moonlight. I wandered towards it, foolishly, so foolishly, but I was curious as to what it might be. Before too long, I had strayed beneath the eaves of the forest and had become quite turned around.

'Suddenly, I heard a snarl from my left! As I spun to face the sound, I saw only a splash of bright white fur, matted with blood and mud. I ran, and the great brute pursued me! It sprang towards me with a great leap; I felt its hot breath steaming in the cool night air! And then -- lights! Lights from Ost Forod! Basil Gummidge had come with a party and lanterns to the outskirts, and on seeing them, the Warg gave up its pursuit!

'Those beasts are dangerous, ${PLAYER}, and something must be done. Not everyone will have the benefit of a fortuitous encounter with our arbiter, you know. If you're up to it, I'd ask you to defeat some of the ferocious white Wargs. You'll find them all throughout Tyrn Fornech, but especially at the Duskencleft, north of Ost Forod.''Find and defeat the white Wargs of Tyrn Fornech, ${PLAYER}! You'll find them throughout the hills, but especially up near the Duskencleft, north of Ost Forod!''You survived your encounters with the white Wargs, ${PLAYER}, I am quite glad to see -- good work! One day, perhaps, a traveller will be able to walk in these lands without fear of danger!

'One day, perhaps. For now, I will give you this reward and hope that day comes soon rather than late.'Defeat bears in the Misty Mountains.Defeat bears in the Misty MountainsDefeat many bears in the Misty Mountains.Defeat Bears in the Misty MountainsThe Angmarim, under the command of Black Númenóreans, have occupied the ruined city of Annúminas.Angmarim can be found within the ruined walls of Annúminas. Cannuion is at Tinnudir.

Cannuion has asked you to bring him the black badges of the Angmarim at Annúminas.'Troubling news comes from our agents within the ruined city of Annúminas. The Angmarim have moved forces within the walls in large numbers. Rumour has it that many of those within serve under the banner of a Black Númenórean sent from Mordor itself, wearing black badges of allegiance to the Dark Lord.

'If you can infiltrate the city, defeat those who defile its streets, and return their black badges to me, you shall earn much praise among the Wardens of Annúminas!''You are indeed a courageous adventurer to have braved the ruined streets of the Annúminas. I commend you for your courage, though we have many battles left to fight before we can wrest the city from their grasp.'Your encounter with a ferocious boar not far from the Great East Road yielded a boar-tusk of surprising strength and beauty.A weaponsmith in Rivendell might be able to craft the boar-tusk you obtained into a deadly weapon.

Rivendell might be the home of a weaponsmith able to work with the boar-tusk you obtained.You can see no flaw in the tusk. A tusk such as this might command coin from Men or admiration from the dwarves, but in the hands of an Elven weaponsmith this tusk could become a deadly weapon. A weaponsmith of sufficient skill might dwell in Rivendell.'I have lived many years and seen many gifts pass from the creatures of the wood to the Elves of Imladris, but I have never seen a boar-tusk of such beauty.

'I would be honoured to fashion this into a dagger for you, friend ${RACE}. It will be a worthy remembrance of its mighty former owner.'A bounty has been taken out on Andy Idden, a tomb-robber rumoured to have dealings with goblins.Poppy Seaton is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

A poster advertising a bounty for the capture or defeat of a tomb-robber named Andy Idden caught your eye. If you would like to apply for this bounty, you should speak with Poppy Seaton.Andy Idden and his wolf-hound are meeting with the goblins of Tham Andalath, hill-top ruins east of Canadiach in Parth Aduial, south of Ost Forod and north of the road.

You have accepted the commission of claiming the bounty on Andy Idden.Poppy Seaton is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Andy Idden and should now report back to Poppy Seaton.Basil Gummidge is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Andy Idden and should now report to Basil Gummidge to collect your reward.The poster advertises a bounty on a man named Andy Idden, accusing him of dealing with goblins and conspiring to murder decent folk in Ost Forod. It invites interested parties to seek out Poppy Seaton in Ost Forod for details and to apply for the commission.'You're interested in claiming the bounty on Andy Idden? If you can defeat him, you'll certainly have earned it. Andy is mean-tempered and brutal and has friends of the same mind: goblins, if you believe the tales, and I do!

'I've heard that Andy Idden has gone with his wolf-hound to the ruins of Tham Andalath on some business with the goblins that lurk there. He'd sell out his neighbours to the goblins, if he thought there was profit in it, and is probably doing just that right now!

'Tham Andalath is on a hill-top a short distance east of Canadiach, the cross-roads south of here in Parth Aduial, and north of the road. They're crawling with goblins, so claiming the bounty on Andy Idden won't be easy. Good luck to you, ${PLAYER}!''Remember, the ruins of Tham Andalath are south of here, in Parth Aduial. If you travel to the cross-roads of Canadiach, you'll find the ruins a short distance east of there, on a hill north of the road.''Andy Idden was a deadly foe, ${PLAYER}, meeting with goblins, and I know that you've earned your bounty!

'If you report to Basil Gummidge, the arbiter here in Ost Forod, he'll give you your reward.''Basil Gummidge, our arbiter, is here in Ost Forod. Speak to him about the business of your reward.''I heard that you put an end to Andy Idden, ${PLAYER}, and none too soon! He's always been a lout, but by befriending goblins he went too far. The folk of Ost Forod are safer for your assistance, and it is my pleasure to reward you for your service.'A bounty has been taken out on Bill Tripper, a notorious tomb-robber known for his skill with blades.Clark Standish is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

A poster advertising a bounty for the capture or defeat of a tomb-robber named Bill Tripper caught your eye. If you would like to apply for this bounty, you should speak with Clark Standish.Bill Tripper is in the ruins of Tham Varan in Parth Aduial, south of the cross-roads of Canadiach.

You have accepted the commission of the bounty on Bill Tripper.Clark Standish is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Bill Tripper and should now report back to Clark Standish.Basil Gummidge is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Bill Tripper and should now report to Basil Gummidge to collect your reward.The poster advertises a bounty on a man named Bill Tripper, described as a 'notorious tomb-robber, highly-skilled with blades,' and invites interested parties to seek out Clark Standish in Ost Forod for details and to apply for the commission.'You're here to see about the bounty on Bill Tripper? I'm glad to hear it -- the sooner we're rid of him, the better, if you ask me!

'Bill has been robbing and murdering around the hills of Tyrn Fornech for some months now, and it's well-past time someone stopped him. Last I heard, he'd travelled south into Parth Aduial and was holed up in a big ruin south of the cross-roads... Tham Varan, I think it was called.

'If you do go looking to collect the bounty, keep an eye on Bill's sword -- he's deadly with it, and I'd just as soon not have your death on my conscience!''Good luck with that villain, Bill Tripper. You should find him in Parth Aduial, in the big ruins south of the cross-roads. The ruins are called Tham Varan, I think.''So you're the one that finally put an end to old Bill Tripper? Very good! If you report to Basil Gummidge, the arbiter of Ost Forod, he'll give you your reward.''Basil Gummidge, our arbiter, is here in Ost Forod. Speak to him about the business of your reward.''You have put an end to Bill Tripper, I understand? Very good! Here is your reward, and well-deserved it is, too!'A bounty has been taken out on Harry Hinchcliffe, a tomb-robber known to be deadly accurate with the bow.Ballard Wilmer is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

A poster advertising a bounty for the capture or defeat of a tomb-robber named Harry Hinchcliffe caught your eye. If you would like to apply for this bounty, you should speak with Ballard Wilmer.Harry Hinchcliffe can be found near the gate to Forochel at Cirith Rhîw, at the end of the northern road through Tyrn Fornech.

You have accepted the commission of claiming the bounty on Harry Hinchcliffe, a deadly shot with the bow.Ballard Wilmer is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Harry Hinchcliffe and should now report back to Ballard Wilmer.Basil Gummidge is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Harry Hinchcliffe and should now report to Basil Gummidge to collect your reward.The poster advertises a bounty on a man named Harry Hinchcliffe, described as 'a tomb-robber possessed of uncanny accuracy with his bow,' and invites interested parties to seek out Ballard Wilmer in Ost Forod for details and to apply for the commission.'You're interested in applying for the commission on Harry Hinchcliffe? Very good, very good! There aren't many travellers up to Forochel these days, but what few there are have been harried by his arrows.

'Some of his friends linger on the road near the Forochel gate at Cirith Rhîw. Just follow the northern road through Tyrn Fornech to reach it.

'If you can capture or defeat Harry, you'll be doing the law-abiding folk of Ost Forod a good turn. Be warned, however -- you aren't the first to attempt to break Hinchcliffe's bow! Perhaps you will be the last, eh?''You bested Harry Hinchcliffe? Ha! To be honest, I didn't think you would be successful! It just goes to show, doesn't it?

'Basil Gummidge, the arbiter of Ost Forod, is here in town. Speak to him about the business of your reward.''Harry Hinchcliffe is no more? Splendid, ${PLAYER}! He was a menace, and if travel to Forochel becomes commonplace again, it is good to know the road will be safer without the like of him on it!

'Here is your reward, and thank you for your service.''Good luck with Harry! He's a deadly shot with a bow, he is, and has made short work of others seeking to claim the bounty on him.

'You'll likely find him near the gate to Forochel, at Cirith Rhîw. It's at the end of the road that runs north through Tyrn Fornech.''Basil Gummidge, our arbiter, is here in Ost Forod. You should speak to him about the business of your reward, ${PLAYER}.'A bounty has been taken out on Thauk, a vicious Warg in league with the goblins that prowl Tyrn Fornech.Rowena Parker is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

A poster advertising a bounty for the capture or defeat of a vicious Warg called Thauk caught your eye. If you would like to apply for this bounty, you should speak with Rowena Parker.Talk to Rowena Parker in Ost ForodThe Warg Thauk is in the goblin-camp at Tûm Fuin, north of Ost Forod.

You have accepted the commission of the bounty on Thauk.Defeat Thauk at Tûm FuinRowena Parker is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated the Warg Thauk and should now report back to Rowena Parker.Talk to Rowena Parker in Ost ForodBasil Gummidge is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech.

You have defeated Thauk and should now report to Basil Gummidge to collect your reward.Talk to Basil Gummidge at Ost ForodThe poster advertises a bounty on a vicious Warg named Thauk, described as a 'bloodthirsty creature known to be in league with the goblins that stalk Tyrn Fornech,' and invites interested parties to seek out Rowena Parker in Ost Forod for details and to apply for the commission.'You have come to inquire about Thauk? He is a dreadful creature, bloodthirsty and dangerous! It is said that he is in league with the goblins that prowl the hills of Tyrn Fornech. If you can defeat him, they might be affected as well.

'Look for Thauk within the crude goblin-camp at Tûm Fuin, north of Ost Forod. He likely prowls throughout the camp, keeping an eye on the goblins and stirring them to greater mischief.

'Thauk is a great beast, and dangerous! You will need your wits about you if you hope to claim his bounty!''You have returned from Tûm Fuin? That means that you have defeated Thauk?

'Amazing, ${PLAYER}! If you report to Basil Gummidge, the arbiter here in Ost Forod, he'll give you your reward.

'Thauk will trouble us no more? I cannot believe it!''You defeated Thauk? Then you deserve this reward, ${PLAYER}. That bounty has remained unclaimed for a long time. I thank you on behalf of the law-abiding folk of Ost Forod!''Good luck with Thauk. He is a vicious monster and will not be an easy bounty to claim!

'Look for him at the goblin-camp at Tûm Fuin, north of Ost Forod.''Basil Gummidge, our arbiter, is here in Ost Forod. Speak with him about the business of your reward.

'I still cannot believe that you managed to defeat vile Thauk! How brave you are, ${PLAYER}!'The Rangers of the North confer ceremonial blades upon the greatest of their heroes.Cannasgam is at Tinnudir in Evendim.

Cannasgam has asked you to forge the ceremonial blades, which the Rangers of the North confer upon their greatest heroes.'In the matter of weaponsmithy, you have few peers, and I would ask an important favour of you.

'Among those of our kindred who have joined the Wardens of Annúminas, there are those who must be recognized for feats of valour above and beyond the call of duty. Upon these heroes, we bestow ceremonial blades fashioned in a likeness similar to the sword of kings, which was wielded by Elendil and broken at Barad-dûr.

'If you have the skill to forge these blades, it would gain you great favour among my people.''You have brought me the ceremonial blades as I have asked, and they are among the finest crafted I have yet seen! My thanks to you, ${PLAYER}. You have earned much honour in the lands of Evendim.'The Rangers of the North once sent their youths coming of age upon pilgrimages to notable sites of their forefathers, but war has necessitated a change.Cannasgam is at Tinnudir in Evendim.

Cannasgam has asked you to forge ceremonial daggers and bring them to her.'As members of our kindred come of age, we once would send them on pilgrimages to notable places of our forefathers. In this time of war, however, we must make changes.

'I have heard that you have some considerable skill in the craft of weaponsmithy, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps you could forge for us ceremonial daggers which we can bestow upon our young warriors? It would earn you considerable respect among the Rangers of the North if you could do this for us.''I see you have completed the ceremonial daggers which I asked of you. I thank you for your time and effort on our behalf, yet we are always in short supply here, so I will gladly accept any more you may be able to craft for us.'The bog-lurkers of the North Trollshaws, rumoured to be the shepherding hounds of the legendary Onodrim, have become corrupted by some dark power and have begun working in concert with Wood-trolls and other servants of the Enemy.Bog-lurkers can be found in the Gladdalf, in the North Trollshaws.

Thoroniel has asked you and your companions to put down the bog-lurkers of the North Trollshaws.Defeat bog-lurkers in the North Trollshaws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thoroniel is posted to the south and west of the Gladdalf in the North Trollshaws.

You should return to Thoroniel and let her know that you have done as she has asked.Talk to Thoroniel'There are more threats in the North Trollshaws than just spiders, ${PLAYER}. Far to the north and east of here is a marsh we call the Gladdalf. Here, Wood-trolls have appeared, dangerous servants of the Enemy. As well, creatures known as bog-lurkers dwell here, which legend tells are the shepherding hounds of the Onodrim of old.

'Alas, these bog-lurkers, which were once peaceful, have been corrupted by some dark power, and they have begun to work in concert with the Wood-trolls. I do not believe that there is any hope left for them.

'If you and your companions would journey to the Gladdalf, the poor creatures there should be put to rest. Will you do this?''Thank you, ${PLAYER}...it is tragic that these poor creatures had to be destroyed, but it is sometimes the way of things when shadows grow.'Defeat crawlers in the Trollshaws.Defeat crawlers in the TrollshawsDefeat many Crawlers in the Trollshaws.Defeat crawlers in the TrollshawsUndertake quests within the Trollshaws.Complete quests within the TrollshawsThe Prancing Pony Inn is a cornerstone of Bree-town. All who come through the village stop there for at least a pint, if not the night.Complete all encounters in the Defence of The Prancing Pony Skirmish.Defeat SouthpawDefeat the Old HunterDefeat UfglupDefeat Rat-catcherDefeat MukghashanDefeat KamhontDefeat BanosnákhDefeat Willie HawthornDefeat BlackfeatherThe women and children of Bree have barricaded themselves within The Prancing Pony Inn. The brigands and ruffians who took the town over are seeking to punish those who stand against them.Second-watcher Heathstraw is eager to discuss the plan over how to protect The Prancing Pony with you in the moments before the assault.Talk to Second-watcher HeathstrawThe first assault has begun. The Prancing Pony, and those within, must be protected all costs.Defeat the first assaultThe first assault has been defeated, but the enemy is not broken. The defences must be kept up.Defeat the second assaultThe enemy forces are appearing weak and ragged. Their numbers are dwindling.Defeat the third assaultFor Bree to be a free town once more, Goilag, the leader of the brigands, must be defeated.Defeat Goilag'Thank goodness you have come! The time is short, and our need is dire.''You have fought a great battle here today and done well. All of Bree owes you a debt.'Bats have come down from the Misty Mountains into the Trollshaws, spies of the Enemy seeking the hidden vale of Imladris.There are bats in the South Trollshaws.

While in the South Trollshaws, you should put an end to the spying of the Enemy's bats in the Trollshaws.Defeat bats in the South Trollshaws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})There are bats in the South Trollshaws.

While in the South Trollshaws, you should put an end to the spying of the Enemy's bats in the Trollshaws.The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of fine feathers to be made to them.You have accepted a task to collect fine feathers from the birds in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Fine feathers from the birds surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Fine feathers from the birds surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of grey ears to be made to them.You have accepted a task to collect grey ears from the beasts in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Grey ears from the beasts surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Grey ears from the beasts surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Some can fall to the Shadow, but these dwarves have allied themselves with the Enemy by choice.Defeat DourhandsEven with but a fraction of their elder kin's power, a drake is a deadly enemy indeed. Even among the Firstborn, it is a mark of honour to stand against such a creature victorious. No one knows from whence the drakes arose -- what creature did the Enemy find to twist into such a fearful shape and power as Dragon-kind? Their origins are hidden, even to the Elves.Defeat drakesIt looks like the Shire will need a new 'Bullroarer' soon, because there is no doubt that goblins are in force upon the land once again. The Bounds of the Shire are no longer safe, and the thought of your peaceful folk facing a tide of these foul creatures unawares makes your skin crawl. There will have to be a reckoning soon enough if that is to be prevented.Defeat goblinsGoblins are an odd study in group-thought. As individuals, they are snivelling cowards who recoil from anyone who appears remotely stronger than themselves -- in large masses, they are known to hurl themselves with unthinking savagery onto pike and axe. The common wisdom is that in large groups the goblins in front simply have no choice, as they are pushed forward by the teeming masses behind them, and so they fight like cornered animals. From what you have seen, there may be some truth to this.Defeat goblinsIt seems that old superstitions and fear of the Enemy's dark power are what drive these Hillmen to flock to the banners of the Enemy -- ancient seeds sown long ago in the hearts of Men by the Dark Lord, that he might have willing servants to hand when he prepares to arise again. It has long been both the strength and downfall of Men that their hearts are their own, and that they may set their feet upon whatever path they choose....Defeat Hillmen and DunlendingsDuring their thralldom in Barad-dûr, the Stout-axes were held to be lesser than even the lowliest Orc. Even as the Stout-axes endured the cruelty of the Enemy's servants, the old hatreds between dwarves and Orcs were not forgotten.Defeat OrcsSome Orcs are aware of Beornings' unique ability. They have yet to figure out how to stop the mauling, though.Defeat OrcsWhile Orcs dislike the sun nearly as much as their lesser brethren, the goblins, they have more tolerance for it and can be forced to endure it for a time when necessity -- or the lash of their masters -- demands. Despite this, it has long been an artifice of the Enemy to conjure forth vast clouds of smoke or darkness both to protect their troops from the rays of the sun and to conceal their movements from spies and scouts.Defeat OrcsFrom your youth you recall an old Elven tale of some great spider that tried to devour all the light in the world...and how though she was driven off and disappeared forever, her brood was left to swarm and multiply in dreadful, dark places. You remember how that tall-tale used to send a chill down your spine every time your dad told it -- but with all that you have seen of late, you wonder if maybe there might be some truth to it....Defeat spidersIn your travels, you have found that spiders plague more than just your humble home. They are a threat that needs to be answered across Middle-earth.Defeat spidersQuite the opposite of goblins, most trolls have little cunning or intelligence of any kind. They are, however, almost mindlessly brave, crushing anything in their path with abandon. Only if they are badly injured are they likely to rebel -- though the sight of a Cave-troll running amok through its goblin handlers is enough to bring a chuckle to even the most hardened of veterans.Defeat trollsIt is unclear what the true relationship is between goblins and Wargs. Some say that they are allied against common enemies, while others believe that the goblins actively breed and raise the creatures for war. It seems likely that both stories are partly true -- long ago, perhaps, the goblins bred the Wargs, which in time escaped to form their own packs under the most powerful and intelligent leaders among their kind...and now these packs continue to work with the goblins out of convenience.Defeat WargsClark Standish encountered a gang of tomb-robbers at Duskencleft, and believes they might have hidden a satchel of stolen relics there.The satchel of stolen pottery is somewhere in the Duskencleft, south-west of goblin-infested Tûm Fuin. Tûm Fuin is north of Ost Forod.Clark Standish is in Ost Forod, the old watch-post in the hills of Tyrn Fornech, south of the Duskencleft.

You have reclaimed the satchel of stolen relics and should return it to Clark Standish.'A few days ago, I encountered a band of tomb-robbers outside Tûm Fuin, rejoicing over a haul of relics they had stolen from some Dúnadan-tomb. Well, you can be sure that I ran them off, but when I caught up to them there was no sign of the satchel over which they had gloated.

'I think one of them dropped the satchel of stolen relics during the chase, but I was too winded to go back and look for it. You can be sure that their allies will return for it, if we don't recover it first!

'Tûm Fuin is north of Ost Forod and full of goblins. Look for the satchel somewhere among the bushes, south-west of Tûm Fuin, possibly in the break in the hills called the Duskencleft. The satchel of relics may have fallen somewhere in there.''The tomb-robbers were in a hurry to escape. I'm sure they didn't mean to drop the satchel of relics they had stolen, and their brothers will surely return to reclaim it when they find out.

'Go to the Duskencleft and search through the bushes for the dropped satchel! Start your search by Tûm Fuin, north of Ost Forod, and work your way south-west to Duskencleft.''You found the satchel of relics! Thank you, ${PLAYER}! I'll have this delivered to Astiul at the Ranger-camp right away.

'Take this reward for your trouble -- if only I had thought to return to the Duskencleft right away, it wouldn't have been necessary. Alas, I was winded after the chase and did not consider it until I had come home.'Cam Applewood has told you that you can exchange your ashen horse for another ashen horse or pony.Cam Applewood is at the Hengstacer Farm, north of the Bree Festival Grounds.

Cam suggested you take a moment to consider whether or not you wish to exchange your horse. If you decide you do want to exchange your horse, you should speak to Cam again.'So you are not happy with your ashen horse? Well, I can offer you an exchange, as I would not want one of my horses where it was not desired. However, this is not a decision to take lightly, so I will give you a moment to think about this.

'If you are really sure you wish to exchange your ashen horse, speak to me again, and I will give you a receipt in return. You can then exchange the receipt for an ashen horse or pony.''So you have decided you want to exchange your ashen after all? So be it. Look over this receipt and tell me if it looks fine to you.

'And this time, I hope you are more sure of your choice. Choosing a horse should not be something done lightly!'Cam Applewood has told you that you can exchange your ashen pony for another ashen horse or pony.Cam Applewood is at the Hengstacer Farm, north of the Bree Festival Grounds.

