While searching the ruins at Echad Gauradan for Tim Strawley and his stolen figurines, you discovered that there are ritualists among them possessed of some sorcerous power. You should defeat these evil sorcerers, removing the threat they pose to those who dwell in Tâl Bruinen.You have defeated the Gauradan-ritualists, removing the threat they pose to those who dwell in Tâl Bruinen.You found a barrel full of pipe-weed just off the road that runs through Tâl Bruinen. Most unusual....Someone in Rivendell, north of Tâl Bruinen, may know how the barrel of pipe-weed came to rest off the road in the wilderness.

You found a barrel of pipe-weed in the wilderness and have taken a pouch from it to show someone in nearby Rivendell.This barrel is lying just off the road that runs through Tâl Bruinen, but most curiously, it is full of pipe-weed! It is already a rare thing to see waggons pass this way, but waggons carrying pipe-weed are even more rare.

You take a pouch of pipe-weed from the barrel and resolve to bring it to nearby Rivendell. Perhaps someone there will know what caused this barrel to come here.'You found a barrel of pipe-weed out in the wild? That's odd, but I say it's a pleasant sort of odd, don't you?

'I have enough already, but Frodo or one of his friends might have run out and would thank you kindly for some more. They are in my room on the ground floor at the moment, I believe.''A barrel of pipe-weed, just lying by the road? I know that Pippin would bring a whole barrel of the stuff with him, if he could, but I'm fairly certain he didn't on this trip!

'Just yesterday he did tell me his own supply of pipe-weed was running low, and I expect he would thank you for this pouch of it, despite the curious circumstances surrounding it.''You found a barrel of pipe-weed out there in the wild? You've got some fortune, and no mistake!

'Pippin has just about run out of his own supply, and he'd thank you kindly for that pouch you've got there.

'I wonder how that barrel came to be lying by the road like that?''A barrel of pipe-weed, just lying out there in the wilderness? That's queer, and I don't care how well-preserved the weed seems to be, I don't trust it.''You found this pipe-weed in a barrel by the side of the road? How strange, and how lucky...my pouch is just about empty!

'I thank you for this, friend! I wouldn't worry too much about the mystery, though -- the smoking of pipe-weed must be catching on in all circles, not just those of hobbits or Wizards. And why not?

'Take this with my thanks!'Gwindeth has presented you with her final task: she wants you to give her what she desires more than all else.The Blue Lady is at Gwindethrond, behind the waterfall at Rushingdale in western Emyn Uial.

Gwindeth has given you her final task, the task that Aragorn was unable to complete seventy years before: to give her that which she values most.The Blue Lady is at Gwindethrond, behind the waterfall at Rushingdale in western Emyn Uial, in Evendim.

You have given Gwindeth that which she desires most, and that which the kings of Arnor gave to her -- respect. She is waiting to speak with you.Calenglad is at the Ranger-camp on the island  of Tinnudir, east and slightly north of Rushingdale, in Evendim.

Gwindeth has agreed to allow you access to the tomb of Elendil, where you can recover the Silithar for Aragorn.'Listen well, Messenger: Elendil never returned from the wars to which he marched in the south, but the Silithar came by various keepers to Valandil his heir, who placed it in the deepest chamber of the tomb. I wished often that the body of Elendil might be brought to rest in Annúminas, but it was not to be.

'When the Dúnedain abandoned the city, Amlaith spoke to me as the kings were wont to do: "Gwindeth," he asked, "Long have you aided the line of kings descended unbroken from noble Elendil. Your counsel is true, and the respect we bear for you unquenchable. I fear that Arnor cannot stand divided. There are too few of us left to hold Annúminas; we will move the people to Fornost, in the east, and I hope we can survive the dangerous days that lie before us. It is said that you have the gift of foresight. I beseech you, Gwindeth, to give me an answer: will Arnor survive the days ahead?"

'I will tell you the rest of the tale if you are able to satisfy my final demand, the demand that Aragorn could not. I ask you to give me that which I value most. If you cannot, the Silithar will remain forever out of your reach and out of the reach of Aragorn son of Arathorn. Give me that which I value most, Messenger, and I will tell you what I beheld for Arnor and the line of kings.''You have returned, ${PLAYER}! What is Gwindeth's decision?''You are no mere messenger. Tell me your name....

'Welcome at last to Gwindethrond, ${PLAYER}. Your time among the Dúnedain has not been wasted, and I see that Aragorn made his choice of agents with care. He has indeed learned much since I beheld him deep within the tomb of noble Elendil. He was young then, and did not understand the greatest gift of all, the gift that Elendil and his line gave sparingly, if at all: the gift of respect.

'I told you I would finish the tale of Amlaith and the Silithar, and I will do so. Amlaith asked me if Arnor would survive the coming darkness, and I told him what I saw. "Your kingdom will fall, Amlaith, king and son of kings," I said to him. "I cannot see what misfortune awaits you at Fornost, but my heart tells me that if you bring the Silithar from Annúminas, it will come by unknown paths to rest beneath the deepest ocean."

'He received these sad tidings with grace, and after a moment appeared to strengthen his resolve. "My kingdom may be already lost, Gwindeth, but I would ask you one last favour. Raise the waters of the lake over the walls of the city and allow the flood to preserve what treasures remain here. The Silithar will stay where it lies, at the bottom of the tomb of Elendil, and none shall disturb its rest until one of my line has need of it beyond all hope. You shall be its caretaker, fair Gwindeth, if you will accept this duty."

'It was done as Amlaith requested; when the last of his people had left the city, I raised the waters and drowned the city beneath the deluge. So it has remained for hundreds of years. Return now to Calenglad, ${PLAYER}, and tell him that I will lower the waters of the flood to allow you access to the deepest chambers of the tomb of Elendil. Aragorn will have his Silithar.''Give to me what I desire most, Messenger, and I will tell you the rest of my tale concerning the Silithar and the line of kings.''Return to Calenglad at Tinnudir, and tell him that I have agreed to lower the level of the water in the tomb of Elendil. You will be able to claim the Silithar in Aragorn's name.'Lithuifin gave you a cleansing draught with which to purify the corrupted statue of Elendil on Tyl Ruinen.The corrupted statue of Elendil is on one of the islands of Tyl Ruinen, to the south.

Lithuifin gave you a draught to cleanse one of the statues of Elendil on Tyl Ruinen.'You have come from Gwindeth? She does not often pass the waterfall that covers the entrance of her home, but I have seen her beneath the trees now and then.

'I know too of the great respect she bore for the kings of Annúminas, and that they bore for her. It is said that the last king of that city asked her to raise the waters of Nenuial over the walls, but I do not know why he would ask such a thing.

'You seek a draught that may cleanse stone of the corruption of the salamanders? I can give such a draught to you. Take it to Tyl Ruinen and use it to restore the statue of which you speak. I have no need of thanks! It is enough to have a refreshingly brief conversation with someone new -- Longbough is slow of speech in the nature of his kind, and Erchiel does not have the patience she once had for my mischief, though that is to her credit!''I have given you a cleansing draught to use on the statue of Elendil on Tyl Ruinen, to the east. Did you change your mind and decide to leave it to the salamanders?''The statue of Elendil on Tyl Ruinen has not yet been cleansed, Messenger.'You tracked the creature which had devoured the white hart some distance along the river before you lost the tracks. The creature may have crossed to the eastern shore.There may be signs of the creature's passage upon the eastern shore.

You should continue searching for signs of the creature.Continue searching for signs of the creatureThere may be signs of the creature's passage upon the eastern shore.

You should continue searching for signs of the creature.Continue searching for signs of the creatureThe cottage is on the western shore of the river.

You have found barren fishbones. The creature must surely be lurking nearby, so perhaps it has wandered into the cottage on the western shore.Search the cottage on the western shore for sign of the creatureThe cottage is on the western shore of the river.

You should warn the inhabitants of the cottage of the danger lurking near.Warn the inhabitants of the cottage of the danger lurking nearThere may be signs of the creature's passage upon the eastern shore.

You should continue searching for signs of the creature.'You have come late to warn us, for the danger is known to us already.

'Last night, just after dark, we had snuffed out the candles, locked the door, and were just getting into bed, when I thought I heard something moving around in little Whelan's room. Whelan is just a babe, so you can understand my worry: I thought a bird might have flown into his room through the window, which we always leave open a crack so the breeze can come in, and I did not want him to be frightened. But when I went into his room to check on him, I saw that it was not a bird at all, but a monster: a terrible, creeping thing hunched over on bony legs! It was moving towards Whelan's cradle and making a terrible noise in its throat.

'Its intent was clear -- it meant to harm my child! I let forth a cry such as you have never heard and leaped towards it; I do not know what I thought to do, but the thing sprang out the window through which it had crawled, vanishing into the night.'Robin Dunkley has given you the statue of Elendil he remade from the pieces you brought him during your time in Ost Forod.There is a pedestal on the islands of Tyl Ruinen, in the middle of Lake Nenuial, where the statue of Elendil you bear once stood. Tyl Ruinen is across the lake from the island of Tinnudir.

While you journeyed to meet with Gwindeth, Robin Dunkley set about remaking the broken statue you recovered from robbers in Tyrn Fornech. This is the statue that once stood upon Tyl Ruinen.'${PLAYER}! I had not thought to see you again so soon, but I am glad indeed to have been mistaken. In point of fact, it seems that a fortuitous wind has brought you here to me on this day, for I have good news: I have finished remaking the statue of Elendil you brought to me in pieces!

'You say that you know whence this statue came? How wonderful! You would do well to return it to its rightful place, ${PLAYER}!

'I will give you the statue and trust you to bring it to the pedestal on which it once stood. Tyl Ruinen, you say? Very well! Go carefully, and let us set right what the robbers of Evendim have despoiled!''The statue of Elendil that once stood upon the island of Tyl Ruinen no longer graces that place with its presence. Find the statue, in whatever condition it may now be, and restore it to its pedestal.''I am still amazed at this turn of fortune, ${PLAYER} -- for you to return just when I have finished remaking the broken statue of Elendil proves to me that there are forces for good in this world that have not abandoned Evendim!

'Take the statue now to the islands of Tyl Ruinen and restore it to its proper place!'Calenglad of Tinnudir has bidden you speak with Nethúr of Esteldín in the North Downs concerning the Rangers of Esteldín and the Orkish forces pouring down from Angmar.Nethúr is at Esteldín, far to the east of Evendim.

Calenglad sent you to speak with Nethúr.'Have you yet been to the encampment of our brothers in the North Downs? Once fair Esteldín still serves us honourably even now as a base for our Rangers to train and prepare as the forces of Angmar pour down from the northern wastes.

'Not that we don't need your help here, but you may wish to speak with Nethúr in Esteldín -- I've heard that matters there have become quite dire as the Orcs encamp in ever greater numbers and threaten to overwhelm our people.''Nethúr at Esteldín will wish to speak with you, ${PLAYER}.''Calenglad sent you? Hah, and here I thought they might need your help even more than we - but it is true that matters here are not to my liking, for the Orcs outnumber our forces greatly, and it is only a matter of time before the last bastions of the North come under attack.'The rulers of the great city of Annúminas are buried within the Way of Kings.The Rangers at the camp within the Way of Kings need help scouting the surrounding area.Aid Maladan with Gateways to the PastAid Nethrandir with Plunderers of HistoryAid Rodlam with Foulness from the DeepAid Maladan with Keys to the CryptsAid Nimbellas with Eastern Gate to AnnúminasNimbellas will be pleased to hear that the Way of Kings has been thoroughly scouted.Talk to Nimbellas in the Way of Kings'You did excellent work with the tomb-robbers, ${PLAYER}. I believe the hillside is now secure, and we can begin to make some moves without expecting a contingent of Angmarim waiting for us.

'Before we delve into the crypts and tombs within the Way of Kings, we must first do some scouting as to the state of the various buildings and the strength and position of the troops here.

'Speak with my fellow Rangers here, and we will help guide you through what needs to be done.''So, you have thinned their numbers, found the various tomb-entrances, and secured keys to them? I think it is time you went underground.''Will you aid us, ${PLAYER}?'Wistan told you of the white hart which he tracked through many lands and believes that he finally brought it down, though he could not find the body.Wistan thinks the carcass must be somewhere to the west of Echad Candelleth, between the dwelling-places of the Gauredain and the Wood-trolls.

Wistan has asked you to search for the carcass of the white hart, which he tracked across the Trollshaws, and return to Echad Candelleth with both it and the arrows with which he brought it down.Collect arrows from the carcass of the white hart'It is good to meet you, ${PLAYER}. I was just telling Indor here of my latest kill: a mighty and noble white hart, the likes of which I have never before seen and will never see again! I tracked him down from the Gladdalf and into Nan Tornaeth, but he became frightened, and I lost the trail. I picked it up again amid the Bruinen Gorges and followed him south into the hills alongside this camp. The light was failing, and as he passed westward and I behind him, I loosed a number of my arrows. I know I felled him, ${PLAYER}, but I could not follow in the dark.

'He is out there, my friend: the hunter Wistan's greatest kill! If you come across the body of the white hart, collect my arrows and the carcass and bring them back to me. My hunt of the white hart will surely make for a fine tale or three about the fire, and there will be a place for you, as well!

'The hart was making westward, but even taking his last strides, he would have steered clear of the Gauredain or the Wood-trolls. Look for the body to the west of Echad Candelleth and between the dwelling-places of those creatures.'The Ranger Culang has learned of a great treasure buried long ago by a Dúnadan-landholder and wants it recovered before it is found by tomb-robbers.The treasure may lie beneath another stone marker, somewhere in Evendim, if this riddle is to be believed:

  "The end of your search lies at the beginning.
   My treasure was always there, but you did not see."

You should examine the marker when you find it for some clue to the location of the treasure.Find the solution to the riddleCulang is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, west of the cross-roads of Canadiach.

You found a hollow compartment in the stone marker that you believed to mark the location of the treasure and recovered a mouldy book from the hidden space. You should bring this book to Culang.The inscription on this stone marker is barely legible, but these words can just be made out:

  "The end of your search lies at the beginning.
   My treasure was always there, but you did not see."

This new riddle may reveal the true location of the treasure.'Another riddle? I hope this is the last one! How does it go?

  "The end of your search lies at the beginning.
   My treasure was always there, but you did not see."

'I don't know what this means, ${PLAYER}. The treasure lies at the first marker you found, the one by the cross-roads of Canadiach? I don't know about this. Perhaps the treasure has already been found, after all.'You notice something you did not notice on your earlier examination of the stone marker -- a small removable block from the rear of the marker itself.

Inside the hollow space is a mouldy book.'What is that you bear? A book, reclaimed from the stone marker itself? Amazing, ${PLAYER}! Give it here, so I may examine it!

'Time has not been kind to this book, but some of it appears to be legible, the diary of some member of the landowner's family. A daughter, unless I miss my guess.

'This is a true treasure, ${PLAYER} -- a record from out of our distant past! This is far more precious than I expected you to find! I thank you for your efforts and the weariness of your limbs -- it was a long journey, but for such a find to be returned to us from out of the darkness is a thing of joy. Please, take this reward along with my heartfelt gratitude.'Grave-robbers and brigands abound in Evendim, looting the tombs of the Dúnedain.The robber-camp is on a tiny island at the mouth of the Brandywine river in Lake Evendim.

Having successfully defeated the brigands at Rantost Island, you have been sent to another small brigand camp, where it is hoped you will be able to recover some more stolen treasure.Recover the stolen treasureYou have discovered a tomb-robber ambush.Balchelos is back at Tinnudir.

You overcame the tomb-robber ambush and should report the incident to Balchelos.'You have done so well for us, that my brethren and I have thought of a way that we can reward you and serve our own ends at the same time.

'We have learned that the robbers have hidden some of their loot on a tiny island near the mouth of the Brandywine in Lake Evendim. We believe that the loot they have gathered there contains not only stolen relics, but stolen coin as well. If you raid that cache, then perhaps we can make your reward a fair share of the recovered wealth; the rest we propose to return to the remaining law abiding people of Ost Forod.''Your news grieves me more than I can say. I am grateful that you were able to overcome the ambush! But there are other urgent tidings....''Please seek out that camp on the Way of Kings.''An ambush! I am happy you escaped the trap! I would ask you, though, to return and defeat Ned Leggett if you can.'The dwarves of the Misty Mountains have requested a collection of broken halberds.Collection boxes for this task can be found at the camps within the Misty Mountains.

