You should continue to follow the creature's tracks.Follow the creature's trail eastward along the river-bankWhile you were able to recover Wistan's arrows, something has devoured much of the white hart's corpse already. The markings upon the carcass do not look like those of an animal, nor of a wood-troll, however. Something else is roaming Tâl Bruinen, and you should discover what it is.Defeat wights in the Trollshaws.Defeat wights in the TrollshawsDefeat many wights in the Trollshaws.Defeat wights in the TrollshawsGauredain out of the northern wilds of Forochel have come south to the Trollshaws, answering the call of Mordor.Gauredain are encamped at Echad Gauradan in Tâl Bruinen, in the Trollshaws.

While in Tâl Bruinen, you should defeat the Gauredain who have encamped there.Defeat Gauredain in Tâl Bruinen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gauredain are encamped at Echad Gauradan in Tâl Bruinen, in the Trollshaws.

While in Tâl Bruinen, you should defeat the Gauredain who have encamped there.Defeat worms in the Misty Mountains.Defeat worms in the Misty MountainsDefeat worms in the TrollshawsDefeat worms in the TrollshawsDefeat many worms in the Misty Mountains.Defeat worms in the Misty MountainsDefeat many worms in the Trollshaws.Defeat worms in the TrollshawsRock-worms dwelling on the heights of Cirith Ulunn have grown in number and threaten all that lives in Tâl Bruinen.Cirith Ulunn, the home of Sarlug, is directly east of Echad Candelleth, but to reach it you will need to climb the sloping path to the north-east, which can be reached by following the Tâl Bruinen road north and east and crossing to the eastern shore of the Bruinen. Directly west of Cirith Ulunn, a monument stands on the edge of the cliffs.

Daervellas has asked you to travel to Cirith Ulunn, defeat Sarlug and many of the rock-worms that plague Tâl Bruinen, and to <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> at the cliff's edge by a monument for Sarlug's mate, Sarnemil.Defeat rock-worms of Tâl Bruinen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Sarlug'Have you seen them, ${PLAYER}, the worms that crawl throughout the woods and crags of Tâl Bruinen? They are born in the rocks high above the Bruinen, to the east of Echad Candelleth. Their sire is Sarlug, a most vile creature, and their dam is Sarnemil, ferocious and protective.

'I sought to end their terror once, but I was alone and could not defeat them -- I rue now the decision that made me take flight, but I thought them grievously wounded, unable to cause more harm. I was wrong. Their hatchlings have now matured, and more are brought into the world every day. Soon, Tâl Bruinen will be overrun with the creatures.

'I would be grateful if you would finish what I began. Cirith Ulunn lies directly to the east of Echad Candelleth, upon the high mountains. You will need to return to the Tâl Bruinen road and travel to the north-east, down to the Bruinen, and there cross the river to the eastern bank. You will see a long path winding up into the mountains. Follow it to its height, then turn south into Cirith Ulunn. Sarlug you will find there, but Sarnemil will likely flee. West of Cirith Ulunn, on the very edge of the cliffs, a single monument rises from the earth. Stand there on the cliff-edge, and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> into the distance: you will likely see where Sarnemil has fled. Return to me when you have defeated both Sarlug and Sarnemil and have thinned the numbers of their offspring.'The giant Arifael did not seem to know what would bring a giant down into Tâl Bruinen, though he did seem concerned.Arifael lives on the slope of Amon Nendir, south of the High Moor on the eastern edge of the Trollshaws.

Arifael has asked for your aid against his distant kinsmen from the mountains.Talk to ArifaelArifael lives on the slope of Amon Nendir, east of the High Moor on the eastern edge of the Trollshaws.

You have aided in warding off Arifael's distant kinsmen from the Misty Mountains. You should speak to him.Talk to Arifael'In truth, little ${RACE}, Ringhul was good to send you to me. I am sore-pressed by my distant kin. I should not hide the truth from you. My kin have begun to come down from the mountains, bringing with them anger and war!

'I am beset, for their deeds are evil, and I refuse to join them. Will you help me? Perhaps then I may be of greater service to you.''I hear them approach even now. If little folk like you can fight, make yourself ready!''You have my thanks, little ${RACE}. I am saddened to have to resort to such violence to defend myself from my own kinsmen, but I cannot allow their evil to spread down from the mountains.

'Our deeds were good. Do not fret, little one.'You and Bingo have arrived at the hidden valley of Rivendell, and the hobbit is excited to explore.Elrond is in his library in the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

Bingo has gone in search of food for himself and W.W., and left you the task of telling Elrond that another hobbit has come to Rivendell.Talk to Elrond in his library, in the Last Homely HouseElrond suggested that you introduce Bingo Boffin to Bilbo Baggins in the Hall of Fire.Find Bilbo Baggins in the Hall of FireBingo Boffin is in the Hall of Fire, merrily conversing with Bilbo Baggins.Talk to Bingo in the Hall of FireBilbo Baggins is in the Hall of Fire, in the Last Homely House.Talk to Bilbo in the Hall of Fire, in the Last Homely HouseBilbo has provided you and Bingo with a map of notable landmarks in Rivendell.Find the Market of RivendellFind the lake of HidhuinenFind the Spire of MeetingFind the Haven of OrladionFind the Falls of Imladris by the south-eastern bridgeFind Imdolen on the road north of RivendellYou and Bingo have explored several of the most notable sights in Rivendell, and should now return to the Hall of Fire, in the Last Homely House.Bilbo is in the Hall of Fire, in the Last Homely House.

Bingo has noticed that the map of Rivendell sketched by Bilbo bears some resemblance to the map he found back in the Shire.Talk to Bilbo in the Hall of FireBingo is in the Hall of Fire, in the Last Homely House.

You have learned that Bilbo Baggins is responsible for drawing the map that was left in the cookbook that started Bingo on his long adventure.Talk to Bingo in the Hall of Fire'I am excited to explore the hidden valley of Rivendell, my friend, but right now my stomach is rumbling! The Elves must surely have a dining-hall or a store-house for their meals. I am going to take W.W. and find a bite for us to eat!

'You should introduce yourself to the top Elf and tell him that we have come for a short visit. I have heard nothing of the hospitality of Elves, but it must not be for its lack! He will not turn us away, surely.''You should introduce yourself to the top Elf and tell him that we have come for a short visit. I have heard nothing of the hospitality of Elves, but it must not be for its lack! He will not turn us away, surely.''More guests? This is what happens when you allow one hobbit into your home, ${PLAYER}: more arrive soon after, and more after that! I will not turn this Bingo away, as long as he remains a polite and respectable house-guest.

'You should show him to Bilbo Baggins, in the Hall of Fire. They must have a great deal in common; despite my jest, hobbits remain an unusual sight in Imladris.''You should show this Bingo Boffin to Bilbo Baggins, in the Hall of Fire. They must have a great deal in common; despite my jest, hobbits remain an unusual sight in Imladris.''Look who it is!''You should show this Bingo Boffin to Bilbo Baggins, in the Hall of Fire. They must have a great deal in common; despite my jest, hobbits remain an unusual sight in Imladris.''Look who it is, ${PLAYER}! It is Bilbo Baggins, the famed adventurer from up Hobbiton way! I have been filling him in with tidings of home, and he has been telling me about his stay with the Elves of Rivendell!'

Bingo looks at you conspiratorially and quickly whispers, 'He seems a right ordinary fellow to me, not mad at all!' before proceeding in a normal tone of voice.

'Bilbo has had the capital idea of drawing me a map so I can best explore Rivendell and not miss anything. What fun!''Bilbo has had the capital idea of drawing me a map so I can best explore Rivendell and not miss anything. What fun!''It is always a pleasure to see a hobbit outside the Shire, ${PLAYER}! Bingo seems to be quite an interesting fellow, and I am glad to see him testing his limits!

'I have drawn a quick map of some notable sights here in Rivendell. Find each of these locations and you will have seen some of the most notable locations in this beautiful valley. If I had more time, I could make a much prettier map, but I made this one with some haste. Go now, before Bingo loses his taste for exploration and adventure! It is a fear of mine for all hobbits, that the wanderlust will depart as quickly as it arrived!''Bilbo has had the capital idea of drawing me a map so I can best explore Rivendell and not miss anything. What fun!''I have drawn a quick map of some notable sights here in Rivendell. Find each of these locations and you will have seen some of the most notable locations in this beautiful valley.''That was great fun, my friend, and at each stop I was amazed anew at the sheer beauty of Rivendell! I hope we did not prove a nuisance to the Elves that live here, for I felt myself gawking at several points and had to restrain my tongue from wagging!

'Thank you again for holding onto the map, for I was able to keep my eyes up on the scenery instead of down on the paper. It is quite a well-drawn map, now that I look more closely at it.'

Bingo stops talking and takes the map from you.

'Wait a moment, ${PLAYER}... there is something most distinctive about this map! See how the artist has used these lines here to form the shape of the mountains? Why, that is most familiar to me, for the mountains on my own map are drawn in that very same fashion! You remember that map, right? It is the map that started my own adventure, so long ago now! Can it be that we have found the very hobbit who drew the map I found in that cookbook?''Can it be that we have found the very hobbit who drew the map I found in that cookbook?''Ah, do you mean to tell me that Bingo found my map of Eriador? I am delighted to hear it, ${PLAYER}!

'Before I left the Shire for good, I spent some time visiting my old haunts and fixing the dear place in my memory. I can still see many of my favourite sunny paths and peaceful benches, even now. But one thing brought a sadness to my heart, and it was the belief that none of the dear hobbits I was leaving behind would ever experience the same joys I had in seeing the world. Oh, I knew Frodo would be all right; he'd always been an adventurous boy, but he was unusual in that regard.

'I conceived of a plan, ${PLAYER}. I would draw a map of the wider world, and I would leave it in a cookbook in the library at Tuckborough. Perhaps it would be found by an inquisitive hobbit, one who needed only the slightest of pushes to embark on a grand adventure.

'And look! I was right! Gandalf would be so proud of my inventiveness, ${PLAYER}! I do hope, for Bingo's sake, that there will be no dragons involved, as there were for me. Then I would feel most responsible, but for now it seems to me a lark and a joy!''Before I left the Shire for good, I spent some time visiting my old haunts and fixing the dear place in my memory. I can still see many of my favourite sunny paths and peaceful benches, even now. But one thing brought a sadness to my heart, and it was the belief that none of the dear hobbits I was leaving behind would ever experience the same joys I had in seeing the world. Oh, I knew Frodo would be all right; he'd always been an adventurous boy, but he was unusual in that regard.

'I conceived of a plan, ${PLAYER}. I would draw a map of the wider world, and I would leave it in a cookbook in the library at Tuckborough. Perhaps it would be found by an inquisitive hobbit, one who needed only the slightest of pushes to embark on a grand adventure.

'And look! I was right! Gandalf would be so proud of my inventiveness, ${PLAYER}! I do hope, for Bingo's sake, that there will be no dragons involved, as there were for me. Then I would feel most responsible, but for now it seems to me a lark and a joy!''I can hardly believe it, ${PLAYER}. Bilbo Baggins, the great author and adventurer, is the one responsible for getting me started on this grand journey of mine! I will have so many things to ask him, about his adventures and his writing both!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Notes by the Author' (Recommended Level: 40)</rgb>At the behest of Elrohir, you brought word of the gaunt-man to his father, Elrond Halfelven, who assured you he would think on a plan to dispose of that the evil in Nan Tornaeth.Bilbo Baggins is in the Hall of Fire on the north side of the ground floor in the Last Homely House. Gandalf the Grey is in Bilbo's apartments on the south side.

Elrond bade you take some rest while in Rivendell and to speak with two of his guests: Bilbo Baggins and Gandalf the Grey.Complete "The Riddle-game" quest arcComplete "Frodo's Burden"Master Elrond is in his library on the top floor of the Last Homely House.

You should return to Elrond and let him know that you have done as he had suggested.Talk to Elrond'Be assured, ${PLAYER}, the evil that remains in Nan Tornaeth will be dealt with, though I must consider the best course of action to ensure that it is so.

'In the meantime, you should take the opportunity to rest. Rivendell is a refuge to all who come in peace. There are, indeed, some in this House already who have taken a pause from their trials. You should seek them out and speak with them.

'Bilbo Baggins spends much of his time in the Hall of Fire, performing the songs and poems he writes for his friend, Lindir. You may also find Gandalf the Grey in Bilbo's apartments... he has many concerns, but Frodo Baggins is his greatest. You should seek them both out while you are here.''It is good to see familiar faces, is it not?

'I am glad that you have returned, for I have decided what course to take against the evils in Nan Tornaeth....'As you made your way through the High Moor outside of Rivendell, you discovered a dangerous lynx. After a short battle in which you bested the beast, you discovered his hide was unmarred.Elves dwell in the hidden valley of Rivendell in the eastern reaches of the Trollshaws.

Attacked by the Lynx of the High Moor, you bested the beast and discovered that your blow left nary a mark upon its hide. Having skilfully removed the hide, you should seek a light armoursmith that could fit the hide into a magnificent cloak.The lynx-hide is beautiful and may make a strong and elegant cloak. You must find an Elf skilled with light armour to craft a cloak from this hide.'In all my years, I have not seen the lynx stalking amongst the stones. Yet I have long lived within the safety of Imladris. The beasts grow more aggressive as each day passes and Evil stirs in the heart of Middle-earth. 

'Your victory shows your cunning, and I shall weave a cloak from this mantle worthy of your feat.'Eirieniel has told you of Agarochir, the Black Númenórean that leads the Angmarim who control the court of Tirband.Agarochir can be found in the courtyard of Tirband at the base of the great watch-tower, on the highest level of the city.

Eirieniel has asked you to defeat Agarochir, one of Amarthiel's Black Númenórean lieutenants so the Rangers may be able to seize the court of Tirband.Eirieniel is at Echad Garthadir.

You have defeated Agarochir, one of Amarthiel's Black Númenórean lieutenants, and should bring news of your victory to Eirieniel.'We have launched many offences to seize control of Tirband, the court beneath the great watch-tower on the highest level of the city, but always Agarochir repels us.

'He is one of Amarthiel's Black Númenóreans, and he is a fearsome sorcerer. If we are to seize control of Tirband, Agarochir must be defeated.

'If you and your allies can put an end to Agarochir, our fortunes might change for the better; the strategic importance of Tirband cannot be overstated!''Agarochir is a mighty sorcerer and must not be underestimated, ${PLAYER}. It will be no easy feat to seize control of Tirband, the court at the base of the great watch-tower on the highest level of the city.''Agarochir is no more? I can hardly believe our good fortune, ${PLAYER}! You have done much already for the Dúnedain, but this truly fills me with amazement!'The Elf-scout Canaddal is concerned with outfitting and provisioning a party of visitors to Rivendell as they prepare to depart.Badgers can be found throughout the High Moor, in the Trollshaws.

Canaddal has asked you to hunt badgers for their pelts.Collect badger-pelts on the High Moor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Canaddal is posted at the head of the path leading down to the Bruinen Gorges from the High Moor.

You should bring the badger-pelts you collected to Canaddal.'As you may or may not be aware, a party of visitors from far away lands is preparing to depart. Some of them are of high name, and it is meet that they set forth with all of the provisions they need.

'For that reason, I would have you hunt the badgers of the High Moor and bring me their pelts. These will make warm gloves and hood-linings for the travellers.

'Will you aid me with this?''Ah, thank you, ${PLAYER}! These will do nicely.'The forces of Angmar commonly use banners displaying the visage of the Iron Crown to identify their camps.Orchalwë wants you to burn the banners of the Iron Crown in the courtyard.Burn banners of the Iron Crown (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will want to talk about the burning of the banners.Talk to Orchalwë'The Angmarim use these banners to mark what they have claimed. The Iron Crown reminds all that they serve the Witch-king of Angmar, servant of the Dark Lord.

'Even when the troops are not present, those banners are a foul reminder of their occupation here. Let us send them a clear message by burning them to the ground. For today, at least, this courtyard will be ours!''The smell of burning fabric is usually not one you look forward to, but in this case, it smells as good as anything I can remember.'A rare flower grows in a place along the Bruinen called the Blooming Fens, a place where bog-lurkers dwell. The flower is most beautiful and is purported to have wondrous healing properties.The rare flower grows in the Blooming Fens along the Bruinen.

Canaddal has asked you to collect a rare flower to make medicine for the visiting travellers.Collect the rare flowerCanaddal is posted at the head of the path leading into the High Moor, high above the Blooming Fens.

You should bring the flower you collected to Canaddal at once.'Pelts and meat are all necessary for our visiting travellers, but we would be remiss if we sent them away without medicines. Along the Bruinen below there is a place called the Blooming Fens. A rare flower grows there which is purported to have wondrous healing properties. It is not athelas, mind you, but it would serve better than most herbs.

'Would you travel to the fens and bring me that flower? Be careful, for bog-lurkers dwell there, and they have become wild in recent days.''Indeed, this flower is wondrous fair to look upon. Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I am certain our friends will appreciate your efforts.'While you were searching the caves of the Trollshaws for some sign of the missing Nazgûl, Elladan heard tell of one of the Onodrim in the wilderness that might know the Rider's whereabouts.Barachen is at his camp south of the Great East Road that runs through the South Trollshaws. Scouts along the road can direct you to him.

Elladan told you that Barachen reported seeing one of the Onodrim somewhere in the wild and wants you to talk to him about it.One of the Onodrim, a creature of ancient days, was seen in the valley of the giants south of the High Moor, high above the Bruinen. Barachen reportedly saw the Onod standing next to some mighty trees as he came south into the valley.

Barachen reported seeing one of the Onodrim somewhere in the Trollshaws, and Elladan thinks it possible that this Onod might know something of the missing Nazgûl's whereabouts.Find the OnodElladan is at Thorenhad, his camp among the Bruinen Gorges, north of the bear-dens.

Elladan will be troubled to learn the fate of the Onod that Barachen saw in the valley of the giants.'While you were searching the stone-troll caves -- a productive search! Do not be dismayed that you did not reveal the Nazgûl's location! -- I received word from Barachen in the South Trollshaws. My brother dispatched him to watch over the lands south of the road, and it seems that duty has been rewarded.

'Barachen has reported seeing one of the wandering Onodrim. If this is so, older and wiser eyes than our own may have seen the passing of the Nazgûl and could tell us much.

'Travel west along the road that runs through the South Trollshaws. Barachen has scouts along the road, and one of them can direct you to his camp. Barachen can then tell you where to find our wayward Onodrim.''Barachen's camp is in the South Trollshaws, west of here. Look for Rochwen along the road... she has been keeping watch for those journeying in these lands and can guide you to Barachen's camp.''Barachen's camp is to the south, at the top of a sloping rise in the shadow of those tall cliffs -- bear to the right as you leave the road. He can tell you about the Onodrim he saw, if that is why you seek him.''I have long believed that the Onodrim of legend have walked among the wooded areas of the Trollshaws, but I have never seen one for myself until now.

'As I climbed the High Moor towards Imladris to bear messages from Rochwen and Alphlanc, a curious mood took hold of me. I strayed from my path, walking south down a great slope into the valley of the giants. From a great distance I beheld one of the Onodrim standing like a mighty tree, its leaves turning to red. As I watched, it turned and slowly moved behind a great boulder and was lost to my view.

'Be careful on your journey, for the giants of that valley are formidable, and speak with this Onod. He may know the location of the Nazgûl Elladan seeks.''The High Moor is far to the east, above the Bruinen, and a path slopes southward into the valley of the giants. As I entered the valley, I saw the Onod standing beside a tree with red leaves before it turned and vanished behind a large boulder. He might know of the Nazgûl's presence in this land.'The Onod stares at you with expressionless eyes. Its bark-like skin is coiled with corruption, and its mouth opens and closes soundlessly. Suddenly, the deep wells of its eyes ripple with anger!'The Onod attacked you? That is dire news, for it is not the way of the tree-herders.

'These are fell tidings, ${PLAYER}. You must tell Elladan at once. Seek him out at Thorenhad, north of the bear dens of the Bruinen Gorges.''The Onod attacked you? The tree-herders of old were peaceful and more wont to stand unnoticed among the trees than treat with strangers, with peace or violence.

'I do not know the meaning of these signs, ${PLAYER}. Our problems may be more far-reaching than I had supposed.'The Elf-scout Canaddal is concerned with outfitting and provisioning a party of visitors to Rivendell as they prepare to depart.Bears can be found throughout the High Moor, in the Trollshaws.

Canaddal has asked you to hunt bears for their pelts.Collect bear-pelts on the High Moor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Canaddal is posted at the head of the path leading down to the Bruinen Gorges from the High Moor.

You should bring the bear-pelts you collected to Canaddal.'As you may or may not be aware, a party of visitors from far away lands is preparing to depart. Some of them are of high name, and it is meet that they set forth with all of the provisions they need.

'For that reason, I would have you hunt the bears of the High Moor and bring me their pelts. These will make warm blankets and coverings for the travellers.

'Will you aid me with this?''Ah, thank you, ${PLAYER}! These will do nicely.'The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of sleek fur to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver sleek fur from beasts surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Sleek fur from beasts surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Sleek fur from beasts surrounding Aughaire.'These will be used for crafting much for our people. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of toughened carapaces to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver toughened carapaces from the insects and spiders surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Toughened carapaces from the insects and spiders surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Toughened carapaces from the insects and spiders surrounding Aughaire.'These will be used for crafting much for our people. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Complete most quests within the Trollshaws.Complete 30 Trollshaws Quests.Complete quests within the TrollshawsYou discovered a series of giant footprints in Tâl Bruinen and a brooch belonging to Ringhul, marchwarden of the High Moor.Ringhul is at his post on the High Moor, north and east of Tâl Bruinen.

Candelleth told you that the brooch you found belong to a former scout of hers named Ringhul, who is now posted upon the High Moor, and suggested that you bring it to him and inquire about the giant.Arifael lives on the slope of Amon Nendir, south of the High Moor on the eastern edge of the Trollshaws.

Elrohir is concerned about the giant footprints you saw in Tâl Bruinen and wants you to ask Arifael if he has heard of giants wandering that far south.Talk to Arifael'If you believe you have finished here, you might journey to the High Moor. I am certain that Ringhul would appreciate the return of his brooch. As well, he may know something of the giant who travelled through Tâl Bruinen not long ago, for it is said that there is a dwelling place of giants not far from his post.

'If you take the path north back to the main road, then head eastward, you will come to the Ford of Bruinen. Cross the Ford and travel southward, following the path up into the hills. Atop the plateau of the High Moor, you will find Ringhul's post.''The little Elf Ringhul sends you to me? He is a friend, so I will not step on you, methinks.

'A giant in lands to the south? I do not think so. If he is still there, you would know! Maybe this giant failed to catch a boulder and had to come out of the mountains to find it?

'It is strange, but there many giants now who do strange things....''Welcome, traveller. You found this brooch in the footprint of a giant? I did indeed lose this in Tâl Bruinen, but I saw no giant footprints there. The giants do not usually travel from the mountains that far south; Tâl Bruinen has been clear of them for as long as I can remember.

'If there is a giant wandering those lands, I would like to know what it sought.

'You speak with Arifael -- a better sort of giant, if you will -- at his home upon the slopes of Amon Nendir. The hill lies to the south of the High Moor. He may know something of this giant's wanderings.'Orchalwë has told you of a cruel practice of the Angmarim that have occupied the ruins of Annúminas: drowning their prisoners off the docks at the Port of Annúminas.Prisoners are being drowned by Angmarim torturers off the docks of the Port of Annúminas, on the lowest level of the city, by the water.Rescue tortured prisoners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will be pleased to hear that the prisoners have been saved.Talk to Orchalwë'The Angmarim are cruel beyond measure, ${PLAYER}, drowning the prisoners they have taken off the docks of the Port of Annúminas. They take pleasure in the suffering of their victims and must be stopped! We must put an end to these barbaric acts!

'I beg of you, if you are a friend to the Dúnedain and to the people of this land, hurry and put an end to these practices!

'Such cruelty deserves to be repaid tenfold, ${PLAYER}, but we must be content to free the prisoners from their cruelty. Would that we could devise even worse punishments for these servants of the Iron Crown!''I thank you from the very bottom of my heart, ${PLAYER}. I am relieved that some of the prisoners, at least, were given freedom.

'A day will come when Angmar will pay for the full extent of its crimes!'Bainthir, an Elf at the camp of Echad Candelleth, is awaiting a delivery of extremely sharp claws.You have accepted a task to collect extremely sharp claws from the river-toads surrounding Echad Candelleth. You are to deliver the items to Bainthir at the camp or to a collection box.Wanted: A collection of extremely sharp claws as proof for the defeat of river-toads surrounding Echad Candelleth. All items should be delivered to Bainthir at the ruins.'Though it brings me no joy, I am grateful for what you have done. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of grey heartwood to be delivered to them.You have accepted a task to collect grey heartwood from the Wood-trolls and bog-lurkers in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Grey heartwood from the Wood-trolls and bog-lurkers surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Grey heartwood from the Wood-trolls and bog-lurkers surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Elves at Thorenhad desire a delivery of knotted branches to be delivered to them.You have accepted a task to collect knotted branches from the Wood-trolls in the areas surrounding Thorenhad. The items are to be delivered to the collection box at the outpost.Wanted: Knotted branches from the Wood-trolls surrounding Thorenhad. All items should be delivered to the collection box at the outpost in the Trollshaws.Wanted: Knotted branches from the Wood-trolls surrounding Thorenhad.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Bainthir, an Elf at the camp of Echad Candelleth, is awaiting a delivery of silky filth.You have accepted a task to collect silky filth from the insects surrounding Echad Candelleth. You are to deliver the items to Bainthir at the camp or to a collection box.Wanted: A collection of silky filth from the insects surrounding Echad Candelleth. All items should be delivered to Bainthir at the ruins.'Know that this will be used well. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Ringlor and the rest of the Dúnedain are fighting a losing battle against the Angmarim that have taken Annúminas.Angmarim can be found everywhere among the streets of Annúminas, on the south-west shore of Lake Nenuial.

Ringlor hopes that by lending your arm to the struggle, you might help turn the tide of the battle for Annúminas.Defeat Angmarim in Annúminas (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ringlor is at the Ranger-camp on the island of Tinnudir, in Evendim.

The Ranger Ringlor asked you to join the battle against the Angmarim in the city of Annúminas, and you took part in the struggle. Now you should return with news of your victories.'My kinsmen are fighting a losing battle within the walls of Annúminas, ${PLAYER}; unless something changes, we will lose the city to Angmar for good!

'If you are willing, you and your allies might be able to make the difference. Defeat the Angmarim wherever you find them within Annúminas, on the south-west shore of Lake Nenuial, and perhaps things will change for the better!

'The battle rages across the streets of the city on each level, and the Angmarim push us back at every opportunity. Let us give them a taste of defeat, ${PLAYER}!''Thank you for your efforts, ${PLAYER}! Every sword-swing, staff-thrust, and bow-shot that finds its mark ensures that there will be fewer Angmarim to fight tomorrow!

'Annúminas will be ours once again, and the Angmarim will be thoroughly defeated! I swear it!''Annúminas is on the south-west shore of Lake Nenuial, ${PLAYER}. I encourage you and your allies to join the battle against the Angmarim in its streets!'The Angmarim are using boats to transport their troops around the ruins and Evendim.Orchalwë wants you to burn the boats at the dock.Burn the boats (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will want to know the boats have been burned.Talk to Orchalwë'It looks like the Angmarim have been using this dock to ferry troops around the lake. See the boats down at the end?

'Being able to quickly shift their forces like that has presented a problem for us. We cannot react fast enough, and they can flank us with ease.

'Let us set these boats ablaze and hamper their ability to do that.''Very good. Now that they do not have boats to use, I wonder if they will try to swim to get to us....'The Angmarim keep their stores and supplies in large tents.The two large tents supply the eastern gate forces with supplies.Burn the supply tents (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will be pleased to hear that the tents are ablaze.Talk to Orchalwë'Do you see those two large tents up ahead there? The Angmarim keep supplies and goods within them. This must be the camp to support the eastern gate.

'If those tents were to catch fire, everything within them would go up as well and be destroyed. The troops at the gate would lose morale and be ineffective in just a few days. That would allow a perfect opening for an ambush.

'Set the two large tents ablaze to give them something to keep them busy for a while.''I will make sure to let the commander know that, thanks to you, we can earn a victory at the east gate in just a few short days.'The bog-lurkers of the Blooming Fens have become wild and corrupt without the guidance of the Onodrim.There are bog-lurkers in the Blooming Fens, along the Bruinen in the Trollshaws.

While in the High Moor, you should put an end to the wild and corrupt bog-lurkers of the Blooming Fens.Defeat bog-lurkers in the Blooming Fens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})There are bog-lurkers in the Blooming Fens, along the Bruinen in the Trollshaws.

While in the High Moor, you should put an end to the wild and corrupt bog-lurkers of the Blooming Fens.Gandalf the Grey is concerned for his hobbit-friend Frodo Baggins, who has agreed to undertake a dangerous task of great secrecy.Frodo Baggins is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

Gandalf the Grey has asked you to speak with Frodo Baggins and gauge his state-of-mind.Talk to Frodo in RivendellFrodo Baggins is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

Frodo is waiting to take a walk with you in the cool air.Talk to Frodo in Rivendell and take a walk with himGandalf the Grey is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

You and Frodo walked the grounds of Rivendell, and he expressed his concerns to you. Gandalf is waiting to hear your opinion of the hobbit's state-of-mind.Talk to Gandalf in Rivendell'I have need of an adventurer, ${PLAYER}, and it may be that you will serve nicely. My friend Frodo here will soon be leaving on an errand both urgent and secret.

'Hobbits are remarkable creatures, and Frodo is more remarkable than most -- he has stepped forward to accept a charge that would daunt even those counted most brave in all the annals of the world.

'Do not ask me about his task! And do not ask Frodo, either. He may wish to speak of it, but it is better that he not do so. I do ask you this: speak with him and gauge his feelings. Much depends upon his courage and good hobbit-sense, but I am loath to send him into danger if he is not prepared for what he may face.''I am pleased to meet you, ${PLAYER}. You are a friend of Gandalf? Indeed, he is very mysterious.

'I am sorry, I'm not entirely myself today. There are a lot of things on my mind, you might say. This is a place of rest, and I know that Bilbo is very happy spending his days riddling and singing in the Hall of Fire, but...it just feels like I am so far from home.

'Would you take a walk with me? The cool air will do me good, and I would very much like to hear about your adventures.''A walk will do me good, I think. Are you ready to go? Rivendell is a beautiful place, and I have very much enjoyed strolling along its paths.''Frodo remains as high in my esteem as ever he did, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for accompanying him. He is made of stern stuff, our Frodo, and his worries are natural. They will give him strength in hard times, rather than bring him to weakness. Frodo will be fine.

'I thank you, ${PLAYER}. May your travels bring you out of danger quickly if they chance to bring you into it!'A tribe of Stone-giants appears to have taken up residence in the western hills above Evendim. While not so great in stature as their legendary cousins in the Misty Mountains, they are nevertheless powerful opponents when aroused and more than a match for any lone soldier.Defeat giants in EvendimDefeat many giants in the Trollshaws.Defeat giants in the TrollshawsNo-one seems to know where the allegiance of the giants lies -- indeed, it seems likely that they serve none but themselves given their capricious nature. Nevertheless, should these lands fall to the Enemy, the giants would doubtless be pressed into his service as terrible soldiers.Defeat giants in EvendimThe Angmarim have been plundering the tombs of Annúminas.Strongboxes full of relics are outside the gates of the tombs of Annúminas on Tyl Annûn.Collect strongboxes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will want to hold onto the strongboxes until he can return them to Tinnudir.'Do you see those strongboxes over there? They could not possibly be filled with relics from our tombs, could they? I thought dealing with the tomb-robbers would have secured our legacy... apparently, I was wrong.

'We are not going to stand idly by while they cart off the property of our fallen kings. Let us take those strongboxes from them.''I will make sure these are brought to Tinnudir where they can be sorted and protected.'The discovery of a gaunt-man and his undead minions in Nan Tornaeth cause Elladan and Elrohir great concern.Anhebir is in Rivendell, far to the east of Thorenhad.

Elrohir asked you to bring word of the gaunt-man to his father, Elrond, instructing you to speak with the marchwarden, Anhebir, for directions to the Last Homely House.Talk to AnhebirMaster Elrond is in his library in the Last Homely House, north and north-east from Anhebir's post.

Anhebir gave instructions on how to find the Last Homely House. You should hurry to bring Master Elrond Elrohir's message.Talk to Elrond'${PLAYER}, the evil you found in Nan Tornaeth cannot be ignored. My father will need to know of it, for we will need reinforcements here at Thorenhad. Where there is one gaunt-man, there will be more... or worse yet, a Gaunt-lord.

'Bring word of this terror to my father, Elrond, in Rivendell. Return to the main road and journey eastward until you cross the Ford of Bruinen. There, turn south and follow the winding path up to the High Moor. The entrance to Rivendell is in the north-eastern corner of the High Moor... the path is marked, as usual, with white stones.

'Hurry... do not delay for anything! Speak with the marchwarden, Anhebir. He will give you directions to the Last Homely House.''Mae govannen! You come from Elladan and Elrohir with messages for Master Elrond? Then I will send you speedily on your way.

'From here, follow the road east, crossing the first bridge. Continue to follow the road north and north-east, and you will soon come within sight of the Last Homely House. Master Elrond is often in his library upon the top floor.''Greetings, good ${RACE}, how may the Master of Rivendell be of service to you?

'What is this you say? A gaunt-man has been raising wights in Nan Tornaeth? My son was correct. This deed cannot go unchallenged.

'Thank you for bringing word of this to me. I will see that the evil is purged from the ruins.'Gandalf the Grey has asked you to speak with Frodo Baggins about his troubles.Frodo is outside the Last Homely House, taking in the night air.

Gandalf the Grey asked you to walk with Frodo Baggins and to gauge his state-of-mind.Talk to FrodoFrodo is on the paths outside the Last Homely House, taking in the night air.

Gandalf the Grey asked you to walk with Frodo Baggins and to gauge his state-of-mind.Walk with FrodoFrodo is on the paths outside the Last Homely House, taking in the night air.

Gandalf the Grey asked you to walk with Frodo Baggins and to gauge his state-of-mind.Walk with FrodoFrodo is on the paths outside the Last Homely House, taking in the night air.

Gandalf the Grey asked you to walk with Frodo Baggins and to gauge his state-of-mind.Walk with FrodoFrodo is standing at a crossing of paths not far from the Last Homely House.

Your walk with Frodo seems to have soothed some of his anxieties.Talk to Frodo'Thank you again for the walk, ${PLAYER}. Maybe things are not quite so grim after all.''A walk is just the thing, ${PLAYER}. You can tell me about your adventures. There is nothing I like quite so much as a good adventure-story.

'Real adventures somehow aren't the same, are they?''I've always said that a nice walk in the night air will do wonders to clear one's head, and it seems that I was right -- I feel much better now, ${PLAYER}.

'I enjoyed our conversation. If I see you again, we'll have to have another.

'When I see you again, I mean.'You have accompanied Lord Glorfindel to Ost Brandras to confront the Gaunt-lord, Ferndúr the Virulent.You should speak with Glorfindel before proceeding against the enemy.

Glorfindel is standing outside Ost Brandras and waiting for your aid in confronting Ferndúr the Virulent.Talk to GlorfindelGlorfindel is at Ost Brandras.

You should follow Glorfindel into the ruins and confront Ferndúr the Virulent.Follow Glorfindel into Ost BrandrasGlorfindel is at Ost Brandras.

You should follow Glorfindel into the ruins and confront Ferndúr the Virulent.Fight your way to the altar with GlorfindelGlorfindel is standing at the base of the altar in Ost Brandras.

You have fought your way through many fell spirits and should now confront Ferndúr when you are able.Talk to GlorfindelThe gaunt-men can be found atop the altar in Ost Brandras.

You have reached the base of the altar, and should now confront the gaunt-men.Approach the gaunt-men atop the altarFerndúr the Virulent can be found atop the altar in Ost Brandras.

You have confronted Ferndúr and should now defeat him.Defeat Ferndúr the Virulent and his gaunt-men'It is as I feared... Ferndúr has escaped, ${PLAYER}. Let us return to Rivendell.''The time has come, ${PLAYER}. If this Gaunt-lord's fell powers are truly the cause of the evil in the Trollshaws, he must be dealt with.

'We cannot allow him to muster any more of the dead and bolster his strength.

'Ready yourself, ${PLAYER}. None shall keep us from this task!''Our foe expects us, ${PLAYER}... this much is clear.

'Ferndúr's vile influence has claimed too many of my people and sickened our lands. His disease must be purged from the Trollshaws.

'I see only his foul servants here, but the Gaunt-lord must be nearby. Let us advance with care....'Many Rangers have fallen defending Annúminas from the forces of Angmar.Many Rangers have fallen while defending the city from Angmar. They should be honoured.Honour the fallen Rangers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will be pleased to hear that you honoured the fallen Rangers.Talk to Orchalwë'So many have died to protect this place. They have made the ultimate sacrifice to preserve ruins, memories, and scraps and shreds of a history that is slipping further away every day.

'That is why we must do all we can to remember, to preserve what is left, to honour their sacrifices. 

'These flowers have long grown only here in Evendim. Take them and place one on each of the fallen. From Evendim they came, and to Annúminas they shall return.''How many more good men must perish? And all in the name of power....'Elrond believes that there may, in fact, be one of the dreaded Gaunt-lords behind the evils in the Trollshaws.Glorfindel is outside the Last Homely House in Rivendell, near the waterfall.

Elrond bade you speak with Glorfindel and obtain his aid against Ferndúr the Virulent.Talk to GlorfindelGlorfindel is outside the Last Homely House in Rivendell, near the waterfall.

You should speak with Glorfindel when you are ready to march against Ferndúr and his minions.Talk to Glorfindel to march against Ferndúr and his minionsGlorfindel is outside the Last Homely House in Rivendell, near the waterfall.

You should speak with Glorfindel regarding the encounter with Ferndúr the Virulent.Talk to GlorfindelElrond is in his library on the top floor of the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

Glorfindel bade you return to Elrond and give a report of your encounter with Ferndúr.Talk to Elrond'After much consideration and pondering of your reports from the western parts of the Trollshaws, I am convinced that the servants of the enemy were not, in fact, drawn here by the brief presence of the Nazgûl who passed through a short while ago, but rather answer to one of the Gaunt-lords... mockeries of the Wizards made by Morgoth long ago.

'Based on the powers of this Gúrdring you encountered in Nan Tornaeth, I believe the Gaunt-lord to be Ferndúr the Virulent, master of pestilence. He must be defeated at all cost, but I cannot send one such as yourself alone to face him. His power is too great.

'There are only three within this vale strong enough to oppose a Gaunt-lord, and neither Gandalf nor I may leave at this time. Therefore, I bid you speak with Glorfindel and let him know what we have learned. I have no doubt that he will accompany you against this evil.''So, it is as I feared. A Gaunt-lord has come to the Trollshaws in the wake of the Nazgûl and raises an army against Rivendell.

'Master Elrond speaks truly. This creature must be dealt with at once, and I must be the one to do so. I ask that you accompany me to help put down the minions of the Gaunt-lord... but do not attempt to confront Ferndúr yourself, for he would destroy you.

'Speak with me when you are ready to depart.''Let us leave at once, then!''Alas that Ferndúr escaped. He is a dire foe and presents a threat to even the power of Rivendell. We will need to remain vigilant.

'Return to Master Elrond and let him know how we fared against the Gaunt-lord.''Lord Glorfindel is correct. Ferndúr's escape means that the Elves of Rivendell must remain vigilant. With such a great evil loose on the world, Rivendell's security becomes of much greater import.

'Nevertheless, thank you for your aid... for a time, at least, Ferndúr has been hindered.'On a clear day, you can see nearly across the expanse of Lake Evendim.Orchalwë spoke of spending time by the shore looking to the far horizon.Orchalwë will want to hear what you thought while looking to the horizon.Talk to Orchalwë'Before the city became a battleground, I used to come to this place to sit and watch the water. I would spend hours letting my mind wander as I gazed over the small, rippling waves.

'I have not felt peace and contentment like that in a long time. It is something I dearly miss, and I hope that one day I can sit and enjoy that feeling again.

'Let us take a small moment to rest and forget these terrible times we live in. <rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> to the horizon with me and let us wish for happier times.''Did you see it too? The small light on the horizon amid the darkness of the clouds? There is hope yet for us all. We will withstand this storm.'Along the high moors you delivered a swift and deadly blow to a lynx with a beautiful hide. The beast set upon you and after besting it, you claimed a fair trophy hoping to find an Elven tailor to use the hide to fashion an elegant wrap.Elves dwell in the hidden valley of Rivendell in the eastern reaches of the Trollshaws.

Attacked by a lynx of the High Moor, you bested the beast and discovered that your blow left nary a mark upon its hide. Having skilfully removed the hide, you should seek an Elven armoursmith that could fit it into a magnificent cloak.Though beautiful, there is little that you can do with this Lynx-hide. If you were to find an Elven tailor of skill you might convince him to work the hide into an elegant cloak.'In all my years, I have not seen the lynx stalking amongst the stones. Yet I have long lived within the safety of Imladris. The wild beasts grow more aggressive as each day passes and Evil stirs in the heart of Middle-earth. 

'Your victory shows your cunning and skill, and I shall weave a cloak from this mantle worthy of your feat.'Hostile giants in the Stonemere have violated an ancient treaty by coming down from the mountains and into the Trollshaws.Hostile giants may be found in the Stonemere, near Arifael's dwelling.

Arifael has asked you to make your way into the Stonemere to defeat the hostile giants and defeat their leader.Defeat giant stone-breakers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat giant rock-throwers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat giant head-smasherArifael is at his dwelling near Amon Nendir, not far from the Stonemere.

You should return to Arifael and tell him of your successes.Talk to ArifaelRinghul is at his post upon the High Moor, north and west of Amon Nendir.

Arifael asked you to return to Ringhul and tell him that his distant kinsmen have begun to come down out of the mountains with the intent of waging war upon the Trollshaws.Talk to Ringhul'There are giants in the Stonemere. Their strength must be broken, and their leader defeated. Do not think to approach them alone, for they are as dangerous as they are foolhardy, and fools they be if they risk the anger of the Elves.

'These, my distant kinsmen, have been goaded into bringing war to the Trollshaws. Something evil guides them, but I cannot say what. Nevertheless, they must be dealt with. Will you do this?''You have done deeds of great valour, ${PLAYER}, but I am still worried that my kinsmen felt free to come down from the mountains at all.

'While I care not for the conflicts of Elves or Men or any little folk, Ringhul is my friend, and I would fain be remiss in my duties as a friend. Would you bring news of my kinsmens' deeds to him upon the High Moor?''So, the giants have been beguiled into waging war against Imladris? These are evil tidings indeed....'The ancient ruins of Evendim and those who watch over them are endangered by tomb-robbers.Glirion makes his camp in a ruin high atop the hills of Men Erain, overlooking the lake.

Helechír has sent you to aid Glirion, a Ranger helping to guard the Dúnadan-ruins near Annúminas. He warned you that the roads through the highlands have been mostly lost to time, and it will not be easy to climb to the heights.Talk to GlirionHelechír is on a little island just off Men Erain.

You helped Glirion fight off a band of tomb-robbers and should bring word of your success back to Helechír.'Glirion, one of my kinsmen, guards a third ruin. Seek him out and warn him of these freshly-emboldened tomb-robbers.

'Glirion is at a ruin atop one of the highest hills in Men Erain, overlooking the lake. You will have to go to some effort to work your way up through these highlands to find him, as only faint traces of the old roads through the hills still remain.

'Make haste, ${PLAYER}! Glirion may already be in danger!''It was happy chance, or some benevolent fate, that brought you to Glirion in time to aid him! You have my thanks once again, ${PLAYER}.

'We must strike against these tomb-robbers, friend, before they attack again.''Thank you for your assistance during the battle! It would have gone ill for me had you not fought at my side!

'Return now to Helechír at Sid Waring's camp, just off Men Erain, and tell him that we have fought off the tomb-robbers.''Please hasten to Glirion's side. I am concerned that the tomb-robbers may already threaten him!

'You will find him at the ruins atop the highest peak in Men Erain, overlooking the lake. It will be no easy feat to climb the heights, for the roads have been largely lost to time.''It is good that you have come. These robbers are fools indeed if they expect to find treasure in these ruins, or at least, any treasure in which they would be interested!

'I trust you are ready to fight? Surely you have seen the signs... they are preparing to make their attack even now.

'Look to your weapons! Here they come!'Any support that the Rangers would hope to receive at Annúminas by boat would be cut down by the Enemy's archers throughout the bay.Angmarim look-outs can be found within the ruins of Annúminas.

Orchalwë is desperate to defeat the Angmarim in Annúminas.Defeat Angmarim look-outs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will be pleased to hear the look-outs have been removed.Talk to Orchalwë'See those archers on the ruins? We have tried to bring several boats through in the past few days, and the archers have cut down all of them... even small ones under the cover of night.

'We are in desperate need of resupply and reinforcement. Defeat some of those archers to open up a route our boats can take through them.''Now maybe we can get some supplies and reinforcements from Tinnudir.'When they returned from a battle, the Men of Arnor would etch the names of the fallen into the pillars of an ancient building.Orchalwë has asked you to read the names etched into the pillars.Read the names etched in the pillars (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë wants to speak with you about the etched pillars.Talk to Orchalwë'When the warriors of the kingdom of Arnor would come home from battle, they would etch the names of the fallen into these pillars, a permanent memorial of those who gave the greatest sacrifice.

'I spent many of my younger days wandering between these columns, my fingers sliding over the names etched into them, wanting to be one of the heroes of the past.

'Take some time and read the names that are left before they join their fallen companions, lost in time.''One day, we will rebuild this temple, and those who have fallen to preserve it will be etched into the new pillars.'The Elf-scout Canaddal is concerned with outfitting and provisioning a party of visitors to Rivendell as they prepare to depart.Boars can be found throughout the High Moor, in the Trollshaws.

Canaddal has asked you to hunt boars for meat to dry for rations.Collect boar-flanks on the High Moor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Canaddal is posted at the head of the path leading down to the Bruinen Gorges from the High Moor.

You should bring the boar-flanks you collected to Canaddal.'As you may or may not be aware, a party of visitors from far away lands is preparing to depart. Some of them are of high name, and it is meet that they set forth with all of the provisions they need.

'For that reason, I would have you hunt the boars of the High Moor for meat. This can be dried as rations for the travellers.

'Will you aid me with this?''Ah, thank you, ${PLAYER}! These will do nicely.'The Rangers here tried to bargain and make deals with Angmar.The corpses should be removed from the crow's cages to allow the fallen to rest peacefully.Remove the corpses from the cages (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will be pleased to see his dishonoured brethren will now rest peacefully.Talk to Orchalwë'The Angmarim are savage men whose cruelty knows no bounds. The corpses in these cages are of Rangers who attempted to bargain with Angmar to preserve whatever of Annúminas they could.

'In response, the Angmarim hung them in these cages until they died, and now they leave their corpses there for the birds and animals to devour.

'Let us get them out of there and allow them to rest peacefully. Even if you tried to make a deal with the Enemy, you do not deserve this for eternity.''Now they can spend the rest of time in peace.'The torches burning along the bridge are used to honour the fallen leaders of Annúminas.Burn incense in the torches on the bridge leading to Tyl Annûn.Burn incense in torches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will be pleased to hear his ancient kings have been honoured.Talk to Orchalwë'This bridge leads to the island of Tyl Annûn, the home of our most valued crypt and the former palace. The torches along the bridge burn blue in memory of the fallen kings.

'It was custom to sprinkle incense into the flames as you crossed the bridge. The scent would waft into the palace and honour the leaders.

'I encourage you to partake in this practice.''I smelled the incense, and I am sure they did as well. My ancestors and kings have been respected and honoured.'The Angmarim use the rubble to hide behind when they attack, making it difficult for the Rangers to drive them off.The rubble is just to the east of Echad Garthadir.Collect rubble (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë may have a use for the rubble.'The Angmarim often assault us down this path from the east. They dash from stone to stone, staying protected from our arrows until they are close. It makes defeating them a dangerous task.

'Let us clear some of this rubble out of the way to make that a more difficult proposition for them. I will take it once it is gathered up. I am sure we can put it to some use.''I am sure we can put this to good use fortifying a position or stabilizing a building.'Drakes have come down from the Misty Mountains to dwell among the giants east of the High Moor.There are drakes dwelling east of the High Moor in the Trollshaws.

While in the giants' valley, you should attempt to drive off the drakes which have come down from the Misty Mountains.Defeat drakes in the giants' valley (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})There are drakes dwelling east of the High Moor in the Trollshaws.

While in the giants' valley, you should attempt to drive off the drakes which have come down from the Misty Mountains.Sickle-flies pervade the High Moor, bringing the threat of disease...and possibly a cause for the lynx' feral behaviour.Sickle-flies infest the High Moor in the Trollshaws.

Canaddal has asked you to purge the High Moor of sickle-flies.Defeat sickle-flies upon the High Moor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Canaddal is encamped on the High Moor in the Trollshaws.

You should return to Canaddal and let him know that you have done as he asked.Talk to Canaddal'${PLAYER}, while I am most concerned with provisioning and outfitting our visitors, I am troubled by something else. The lynx which dwell upon the High Moor have become feral -- no doubt due to the shadow rising in the East. Unfortunately, they pose a great threat to those who journey to Rivendell seeking refuge.

'However, there may be another possible cause that I would have you investigate. Stinging sickle-flies have begun to come up from the riverbanks, bringing the threat of disease...but their incessant stinging might also be a cause for the lynx' aggression.

'Perhaps if the sickle-flies were purged, the lynx might find peace.''Alas, there are still too many flies to make an accurate assessment. Thank you for your aid, nonetheless.'Several ancient urns are still under the water, protected from the waves by the ruins of the flooded buildings.Orchalwë saw some urns beneath the waves.Collect sunken urns (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orchalwë will want to look at the vases.'Did you see that glint beneath the waves? Could anything really still be down there?

'It looks like a vase. I wonder how it managed to survive all this long? I suppose the ruins must have protected it from the waves and currents.

'Look there! Another one! Go and see if you can recover some of them. If they are sealed, the contents could be a great boon.''Nothing here but empty, soaked pottery. These are the reason we must retake the city...we lose enough of our past to the waters of the lake that we cannot afford to let Angmar take any as well.'Giants have come down from the Misty Mountains, mustering in a valley east of the High Moor, breaking the treaties they had with the Elves of Rivendell.There are giants mustering in a valley east of the High Moor in the Trollshaws.

While in the giants' valley, you should attempt to drive off the giants which have come down from the Misty Mountains.Defeat giants in the giants' valley (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})There are giants mustering in a valley east of the High Moor in the Trollshaws.

While in the giants' valley, you should attempt to drive off the giants which have come down from the Misty Mountains.In these dark times, marchwardens guard the paths into the hidden vale of Imladris.Ringhul is at his post in the High Moor, far to the north and east of Echad Candelleth.

Candelleth bade you speak with Ringhul, marchwarden of the High Moor, to gain entrance into Rivendell.Talk to Ringhul'You have been of great aid to us, ${PLAYER}, and we are grateful. We would welcome your continued aid, but if you feel the time has come for you to continue your journey, I would suggest that you speak with Ringhul, marchwarden of the High Moor. Let him know that I sent you and tell him of your deeds... friends of Rivendell are always welcome.

'To reach the High Moor, you should return to the Tâl Bruinen road and take it north all the way to the main road in the Bruinen Gorges. Follow the main road east to the Ford of Bruinen and cross the river, turning south. You will find a path that leads up into the hills... this will bring you to the High Moor and Ringhul's post.

'Farewell, ${PLAYER}.''Candelleth sent you? How fares her company?

'Ah! It seems that Master Elrond's concerns have proven true. Tâl Bruinen and the road to Hollin are beset by our enemies. It is in these days that I yearn to roam abroad, for my post lends itself to loneliness and tedium.'Lindir has found a new riddle for you to solve. Again, he is sure the answer can be found somewhere in Imladris.Lindir is with Bilbo in the Hall of Fire at Rivendell.

Bilbo told you that Lindir has a new riddle to offer, one out of Gondor.Talk to LindirThe answer to Lindir's riddle can be found somewhere in Rivendell.

Lindir has asked you to find an item that provides an answer to his riddle.


Breath is needed
