Elladan has asked that you speak with his father about arranging an audience with the visiting Elves from Mirkwood, who might have some experience with creatures of the manner which you encountered.Legolas is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Master Elrond has told you of an Elf-prince named Legolas, currently staying in Imladris for a time. He hails from Mirkwood and may have some knowledge of the creature you faced in the Trollshaws.Talk to Legolas in Rivendell'You are fortunate. The creature which you faced could not have been a true Onod. You could not possibly have faced the might of one of the Old Ones.

'Nor would you have to, for the Onodrim are friendly to the Free Peoples on the rare occasion their paths meet. The great forests are less-travelled now than they were in days past, and few now ever see one of the great tree-herds.

'A small company of Elves from Mirkwood came to Imladris to consult with my father. Perhaps one of them will know what manner of creature you faced. Speak with my father to arrange an audience.''Speak with my father at Imladris, far to the east and above the Bruinen. He can arrange an audience with the Elves from Mirkwood.''I am glad you so ably serve my sons, ${PLAYER}. I feel that you are close to locating the Nazgûl that escaped the raging waters of the Bruinen.

'And yet more evils reveal themselves. The Elves of Mirkwood have encountered such evil before. My son was right to send you to me.

'Speak with Legolas, son of Thranduil, in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris. He has been often in thought while he remains here and may be able to help you find and defeat the source of the evil.''I believe Legolas is in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris. You may find him there and speak to him about the creature you encountered.''Hail to you, ${PLAYER}. If you did not bring such dire news I would greet you more heartily, but your tidings are troubling.

'We of Mirkwood have indeed encountered these creatures before, but it is not something I expected to see again. A tribe of trolls once lived near Dol Guldur, and we who fought them called these creatures Wood-trolls. Once these were wandering, individual creatures, loath to unite or even live near to their fellows. At Dol Guldur, however, something caused them to join together. A king was raised among them, and he unified the Wood-trolls into a terrible force. My father sent many warriors to defeat the Wood-trolls, and it was done.

'I believe something has once again united the Wood-trolls of the Trollshaws, ${PLAYER}. But this place is far from Mirkwood -- my father's warriors will not avail us.'Legolas recognized the creature you described as one of wood-trolls and has agreed to help you track the rest down.Legolas is in Rivendell, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

The Elf-prince from Mirkwood, Legolas, is waiting to help you track down the Wood-trolls in the Trollshaws and defeat them.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Talk to Legolas in Rivendell and track down the wood-trollsLegolas is in Rivendell, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

The Elf-prince from Mirkwood, Legolas, is waiting to speak with you about what the two of you saw in the lair of the wood-trolls.Talk to Legolas in Rivendell'We must track the wood-trolls to their lair, ${PLAYER}, if we are to cleanse the corruption that scars the Trollshaws.

'I can take you to them. Their tracks are easily distinguishable in the muddy earth, and Wood-trolls are not known for their subtlety or intelligence. But ask me not in haste! I know of the Nazgûl that Master Elrond has lost, and I fear that it may have lent some of its strength and power to these creatures for them to be so bold. We must be watchful.

'Speak with me when you have gathered such friends as you can trust and are ready to be led into the wild in search of the wood-trolls, and the evil power I fear may be their ally.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Are you ready? I can follow the tracks of the wood-trolls and can lead you to their lair. Bring what friends you can trust, for wood-trolls are dangerous creatures, and we may find more than one or two.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'We have ended the Nazgûl's designs by slaying the wood-troll Taushakh, but the escape of so great a servant of the Enemy casts a shadow upon my heart.'Legolas has been dismayed by the events in the lair of the Wood-trolls and thinks Elrond should be told of them at once.Master Elrond is within the Last Homely House.

Legolas has asked you to go to Elrond at once and tell him what transpired in the lair of Taushakh, king of the Wood-trolls.'I do not think this is the end of our search for the Nazgûl that escaped the waters of the Bruinen, ${PLAYER}. Indeed, we have only seen the beginning of the trouble he may cause.

'We must tell Master Elrond what has transpired. A task of great secrecy has been set before some of us at Imladris, and I fear we have been too long delayed already.

'Speak to Master Elrond inside the Last Homely House and tell him of our journey into the lair of Taushakh and what we beheld there. The time for delays is past.''Master Elrond must be told of our adventure within the lair of Taushakh, king of the Wood-trolls. That Taushakh was bested is small consolation for losing the Nazgûl so quickly after revealing his location.''I am glad to hear your news, ${PLAYER}. Do not grieve overmuch for the loss of the Nazgûl -- his fell designs with the Wood Trolls were ended by your brave actions and the skills of Legolas.

'Yes, your encounter with the Nazgûl has revealed to me what I must do. I will speak with Gandalf, for the errand of which you heard whispers has become all the more urgent because of the news you bring.

'For you though, I would ask that you keep your attention focused on the Nazgûl.'Defeat the Gertheryg within Annúminas.Defeat the Gertheryg within AnnúminasDefeat the Gertheryg within Annúminas.Defeat the Gertheryg within AnnúminasYou have become ensnared in the Halls of Night and must defeat Nightmare and his lieutenants before it is too late.Discover the Halls of Night.Complete the following deeds within the Halls of Night: Facing Your Fears and Raising Spirits.Complete Facing Your FearsComplete Raising SpiritsReko, the Earth-kin hunter, desires the beaks of blade-beak ravagers, though for what, the creature would not say.Blade-beak ravagers can be found throughout Ram Dúath.

Reko has asked you to collect the blade-beak ravager beaks he desires.'You there... you help Reko?

'Ravager claws. No, how do you say it? Beaks? Like birds? Yes, ravager beaks.

'You go get ravager beaks. I will give you shiny pieces of metal taken from Orc-folk. Many blade-beak ravagers not far.''You have brought me many beaks. That is good. Here is the shiny metal.'Study how to make an epic conclusion to your arguments.Strike with Epic ConclusionHaving the final word does not guarantee a positive outcome, but it certainly helps.Use Final WordYou have picked up a few new conundrums in your travels, but you will not be able to polish the weaker points until they fall flat a few times.Target must resist your Riddle or Confound skillsThe Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of sleek skin to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver sleek skin from beasts and cave-claws surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Sleek skin from beasts and cave-claws surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Sleek skin from beasts and cave-claws surrounding Aughaire.'We will ensure that these are used well. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of studded sword sheaths to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver studded sword sheaths from the Orcs and Angmarim surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: studded sword sheaths from the Orcs and Angmarim from the lands surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: studded sword sheaths from the Orcs and Angmarim from the lands surrounding Aughaire.'We shall sheath our weapons within these now. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Glinghant was once the most beautiful place in all of Annúminas -- no small feat given the splendour of the ancient city at its height. Descriptions of the pools and gardens of Glinghant hold a special place in the hearts of my people. Even after the long years of decay, you could see the memory of its beauty.

The coming of Amarthiel and her legions to Annúminas is cause itself for sorrow, but of all the harm they have caused to this city, their possession and despoiling of Glinghant stirs me most to rage. I would cast them out of this place and restore the splendour it had of old, even should the rest of Annúminas fall. In that, I am not joined by most of my kindred, but such is my horror at the spoiling of Glinghant.

Amarthiel has many servants, but to Glinghant she has sent the darkwater: foul spirits of corruption. If you should venture beyond the gate into the terraced steps of Glinghant, I would account you highly should you defeat them.Darkwater are within the pools and gardens of Glinghant, beyond the court of Clorhir.

You should enter Glinghant and defeat the darkwater that have despoiled its beauty.Defeat darkwater in Glinghant (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Easing the fears of your allies is an important role in any fellowship.Tell a Story of CourageA dazed and confused enemy is a much easier target than one who is alert and aware.Use the Dazing Blow skillThere is much to do while travelling through the lands of the Misty Mountains.Complete Explorer of the Misty Mountains deedComplete Peril of the Mountains (Final) deedComplete Slayer of the Misty Mountains deedGlorenglir believed that snow lurkers could be used to unearth fragments of history from the snows of the Misty Mountains, and you have proven her correct.The scholar Deluros is within the library of the House of Elrond in Rivendell.

Glorenglir has asked you to bring the relic fragments you recovered from the snow lurkers of the Misty Mountains to Deluros with the hope that he will recognize her readiness to advance as a scholar.'Once Master Deluros hears I was correct in my belief that pieces of pottery and other relics could become entangled in the root-like legs of the snow lurkers of the Misty Mountains, he is certain to give me more interesting assignments!

'Bring these relic fragments to him, ${PLAYER}, and tell him from whence you retrieved them! Perhaps he will send you back to me with an invitation to examine the most precious artifacts kept at Imladris. I cannot wait to study them with my own eyes!

'You will find Master Deluros within the library of Lord Elrond's house. He is always to be found there, deep in thought.''Master Deluros is a scholar and jeweller of great skill. Look for him in the library of Master Elrond's House and show him the relic fragments you retrieved from the snow lurkers.

'I eagerly await what he has to say!''You retrieved these relic fragments from snow lurkers in the Misty Mountains? That is very interesting, ${PLAYER}, and Glorenglir is to be commended for thinking it possible -- in one blow, the mountain passes are made safer for those who must travel them, and we collect pieces of our history for further study. Worthy accomplishments.

'And yet, I would ask you one question -- did Glorenglir examine these fragments before she sent you to me? I thought not. This has always been her main fault, and it is the reason I refuse to allow her access to the most interesting artifacts kept here.

'Glorenglir is more concerned with boasting of her finds, with celebrating her accomplishments, than with examining the pieces she has uncovered. Until she learns to contemplate and appreciate the history of the pieces, she will never become a true scholar.'A pair of refugees seem in need of some supplies.Onora has tasked you with collecting some firewood and her husband's satchel, somewhere among the trees nearby.Collect firewood for Onora and Rúdhan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find Rúdhan's satchelOnora is waiting by the river for you to return with the supplies you collected.'Don't come any closer! Wait... you don't look like a spy... who are you?'

You explain yourself to the frightened woman and she lowers her hands.

'If what you say is true, then perhaps there is no need to panic. My husband Rúdhan and I have been running for days now, but we were separated from those who fled with us. We tried to keep going, but my husband hurt his leg quite badly. Now we have no supplies and it will be only a matter of time before the vile creatures that pursue us catch up!

'My name is Onora. Will you help us? We need firewood, and the satchel my husband dropped during our flight. We will be forever grateful for your aid.''You return! And you found Rúdhan's satchel? And firewood as well!'

'I don't know what to say... I did not expect to find such virtue in the heart of a complete stranger. Yet here you are. Thank you, stranger.'You have discovered the Halls of Night.You have discovered the Halls of Night.Discover the Halls of NightGrants completion of deed: Discover the Halls of NightBy completing this quest, you will be marked as completing Discover the Halls of Night'Now, my little fellows, where be you a-going to, puffing like a bellows? What's the matter here then? Tell me what's your trouble!

'You need to play the instances? Naught worse that that, eh? That can soon be mended.''Well, my little fellows! You shall have your deeds completed!'Dwalin is concerned about rumours that he has heard about stirrings in the Misty Mountains.Seek out Glóin at his camp in the western foothills of the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell, far to the east of Ered Luin.

Dwalin wants you to bring word of warning to his friend Glóin in the Misty Mountains. He believes that the increased goblin activity may not be restricted to the goblins in Ered Luin.Talk to Glóin at his camp in the Misty Mountains'Hail, friend, I would have a moment of your time, if you please. I have a request that may seem out of the ordinary. Long ago, I travelled through the Misty Mountains with Thorin's Company. We found ourselves in trouble within the mouth of a cave in those Mountains and ended up in a terrible place filled with goblins.

'I fear that the recent increase of goblins here in the Blue Mountains, specifically at Sarnur, might mean a greater mustering of goblins throughout Middle-earth. I have heard reports that a friend from the same company is in the area now, and I was hoping that you could seek him out and see if there is anything that he has seen with regards to the goblins.

'His name is Glóin, and he can be found at his camp in the western foothills of the Misty Mountains, far to the east of here, beyond the Lone-lands and the Trollshaws. Seek him out and warn him of the changes in the goblins' behaviour here.''Be swift! There could be great danger afoot in the Mountains.''My old friend Dwalin sends words of concern! Ha! 

'To be true, his concerns are well-founded. The goblins here are as riled as those in the Blue Mountains...moreso perhaps. There is word that the Elves under Master Elrond's care have even sent scouts to see what transpires within the halls of Goblin-town.

'I have a mind to investigate myself, but the dwarves here need my assistance as the Dourhands and their allies are our main concern. Still... it may merit investigation.'Bainthir, an Elf at the camp of Echad Candelleth is awaiting a delivery of fine skin.You have accepted a task to collect fine skin from the worms surrounding Echad Candelleth. You are to deliver the items to Bainthir at the camp or to a collection box.Wanted: A collection of fine skin from the worms surrounding Echad Candelleth. All items should be delivered to Bainthir at the camp.'It is good to know that these have been destroyed. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Bainthir, an Elf at the ruins of Echad Candelleth, is awaiting a delivery of hardened carapaces.You have accepted a task to collect hardened carapaces from the insects surrounding Echad Candelleth. You are to deliver the items to Bainthir at the camp or to a collection box.Wanted: A collection of hardened carapaces from the insects surrounding the Elf-camp of Echad Candelleth. All items should be delivered to Bainthir at the ruins.'Know that this will be used well. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.We have learned some troubling news, ${PLAYER}. It seems that Dolvaethor, one of Amarthiel's Black Númenórean lieutenants, has entered Haudh Valandil. I can think of no good reason for Dolvaethor to enter the tomb, but many terrible ones!

Whatever Dolvaethor plans within the depths of wise Valandil's crypt, he must be stopped. You must do this, ${PLAYER} -- fighting your way across the bridge of Ariant and onto the island upon which sits Ost Elendil and Haudh Valandil will be terrible, but the Dúnedain lack the force of arms needed for this task. You and your allies, however, might be able to fight through.

Once you have defeated Dolvaethor within Haudh Valandil, set right whatever harm he has caused, then return to me. If the course of battle has drawn me elsewhere, speak instead to Captain-General Daerdan at Echad Garthadir.Dolvaethor is inside Haudh Valandil on the island upon which stands Ost Elendil, beyond the bridge of Ariant, in Annúminas.

Glangilith has asked you to defeat Dolvaethor and put an end to whatever evil he plans for Haudh Valandil.Restore Valandil's remains to peaceThroughout the history of Middle-earth, there have been Men who have shunned the rule of Kings and forsaken their own heritage. Far too often they fall under the sway of the Dark Lord, who plays upon their distrust of the great Kingdoms, threatens them with hidden force, or sways them with false riches and power. However, there are some Men who freely offer themselves into his service and swear unerring loyalty to the Dark Lord.Defeat cultists sworn to the Dark LordFor thousands of years, the Angle played host to countless devastating battles, earning the name Haudh Erthaid, the Tomb of Kingdoms. Now mostly wilderness once more, it offers sanctuary for those who do not wish to be found. Nestled in the arms of two difficult to pass rivers, the Angle is home to Rangers, Elves, and refugees alike. But a recent infiltration of Orcs, brigands, and other servants of the Enemy has brought war back to the hills and riversides of the Angle once more.The road to Eitheldir is in the northern Angle.

You should look for the road to Eitheldir.Find the road to EitheldirMerileg is located near the ruins of Eitheldir, in the northern Angle.

Elladan has asked you to deliver a letter of introduction to Merileg.'Welcome to Haudh Erthaid, the Tomb of Kingdoms. This is Gaerond, a camp from which we watch over the northern reaches of the Angle. You have arrived just in time. My father has called me to his side, but I have been loth to leave the Angle, as there is yet much work to do. If you are able to take up my tasks, however, I will return to Rivendell with a lighter heart.

'South of this camp, perched high above the river, is Eitheldir. Long have swarming enemies defiled that once grand stronghold of the Noldor. I bid you travel up the winding way to Eitheldir. Somewhere outside its gates, you will find my scout, Merileg, who has been waiting for assistance. Deliver to her this letter of introduction and lend her what aid you may.''If you are able to take up my tasks, I will return to Rivendell with a lighter heart.''Elladan sent you, has he? He writes that you have helped our cause and are to be trusted. Very well. If you are of a mind to, I could certainly use the aid.'I grieve to see Ost Elendil in the hands of the Enemy, ${PLAYER}. The great palace of the kings should lie untainted by the forces of evil, not brought low by the machinations of Angmar and its regent!

Fell spirits and their Angmarim masters now stalk the halls of Ost Elendil at Amarthiel's command. We must drive them forth.

Defeat the spirits within the palace and take from their Angmarim masters the blood-stained runes with which they are commanded. That may lessen Angmar's grip on the palace, shining jewel-that-was. If I am not here when you return, I ask that you bring the runes to Captain-General Daerdan at Echad Garthadir, the Ranger-encampment here in the city. He will be able to destroy the foul things.Fell spirits and their Angmarim masters can be found in Ost Elendil, beyond the bridge of Ariant.

Legrindavor has asked you to defeat the fell spirits inside Ost Elendil and bring him the blood-stained runes used by the Angmarim there to command them.Defeat spirits in Ost Elendil (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Explore the mountain passes of the Misty Mountains with ruins built long ago by the dwarves. However the way is treacherous, often choked with snow, knife-like winds, and other more aggressive hazards.Explore the Forbidding HeightsExplore Goblin-townExplore the High PassesExplore the Ruins of the Misty MountainsExplore Where Giants DwellFind Bilbo's ButtonsYou have become ensnared in the Halls of Night and must defeat Nightmare and his lieutenants before it is too late.To earn this deed you must defeat Terror, Panic, and Nightmare.Defeat Nightmare, his lieutenants, and escape the Halls of Night.To earn this deed you must defeat Terror, Panic, and Nightmare.Defeat TerrorDefeat PanicDefeat NightmareYou have become ensnared in the Halls of Night and must defeat Nightmare and his lieutenants before it is too late.Defeat Terror and Panic.Defeat TerrorDefeat PanicDefeat Nightmare.Defeat NightmareA bard, Declan has asked that you investigate the Halls of Night and return with any clues as to the disappearance of his grandfather.The Halls of Night may be found in the hills to the east of Aughaire, beyond the northern gate.

Discover what became of Durell in the Halls of Night.Discover what became of DurellYou have discovered that Shadow is the grandfather of Declan, his spirit trapped for centuries in the Halls of Night.Return to Declan with news of his grandfather'Hello there! You are ${PLAYER}, are you not? The hillmen spoke of you, which is how I came upon your name. I recognized you by your gear; it is not that of the bleak North-men to be sure.

'My name is Declan. I am a minstrel by trade, though I daresay my family wishes it weren't so.  I arrived in Aughaire by chance, or fate, or perhaps random luck! I daresay I don't wish to ponder the long roads that led me in search of my family's history.

'My grandfather was a famous explorer, Durell... perhaps you've heard of him? He launched an expedition in search of ancient relics of the north. Though we received one cryptic letter about some of his findings, Durell never returned.

'So here I stand, quill in hand. While safe at home, I dreamed of uncovering grandfather's secrets and writing them into a fitting tale, but I fear, having looked myself upon these hills, that I have not the will to seek out the barrow his letter speaks of. He tells of something called the Halls of Night... will you seek it for me?''He must be within the Halls of Night. I am sure of it.''Durell? Yes... that was my name once, though I fear I cannot recall how long it has been since I last heard it....

'We discovered the tomb quite by accident. A terrible storm forced us to take shelter in what we thought to be a shallow cavern. When the squall passed, we discovered the entrance of the barrow. I sent my apprentice back with a letter noting our findings.

'It is a comfort to know that my family has never forgotten me.''Let my family know I have found peace.''What you describe is... difficult to take in.

'As a child I always expected one day to discover grandfather had returned with a cart of gold and jewels parked in the stable. As I grew older I became filled with other feelings; anger, sadness, regret.

'To hear that you have spoken with him... I... I am not even sure how I now feel. You have my sincerest thanks for all that you have done.'A letter from a Ranger hiding in the north among a band of Hillmen who have thrown free the yoke of Angmar tells that they are in need of help beyond the Ram Dúath.Aughaire is an encampment of Hillmen, north through the Ram Dúath.

