Mainio, acting against his elders, has asked for assistance in Forochel.Talk to Mainio at Taur Orthon in Forochel${CLASS},

I am Mainio, a man of the north. I disobey my elders by sending this south with traders, but I know that we are in need of aid, and I hope that someone will hear this plea. 

Seek me in Forochel outside of our camp Kauppa-köti in the place known as Taur Orthon.

Mainio'You have come because of my letter. This is good to know.

'You are a strange-looking person. I am sorry...I am glad you are here to help.'The point may always be made sharper.Complete Expert HurlerStrike with AmbushThe point may always be made sharper.Strike with AmbushPottery has a long history of usefulness.Use any thrown pot skill.By completing this quest, you will become a Commander among the Free Peoples.'So maggot, you want to be ranked up a bit? Well I'll allow it, but just this one time. Don't get used to it!''Alright, here you go. Now leave me in peace!'Cendelleth, an Elf dwelling in the Trollshaws, sent a message to you, asking for aid.Echad Candelleth in Tal Bruinen rests atop a cliff overlooking the great river. You are to seek out Candelleth there.

Candelleth's message found you, and she awaits your arrival at the ruins of Echad Candelleth.Talk to Candelleth at Echad Candelleth in Tal Bruinen${CLASS},

You will find me at a ruin bearing my name. The ruin stands atop a cliff overlooking the Bruinen, south beyond the Bruinen Gorges. 

I look forward to meeting you.

Candelleth'I am pleased that you have arrived. Angmarim skulk through this land, and there are refugees who need assistance. I will remain vigilant for any news of why these ill-meaning Men crawl through our lands.

'If you desire to help, there would be those who are grateful and would offer rewards.'As you travel throughout Middle-earth, you learn the customs and ways of the other races. Using that knowledge effectively makes you a better leader.Use HeartenThe League of the Axe are a distrustful group, but they no longer consider you a threat to their livelihood, and this could be a good thing.You must earn Neutral standing with the League of the AxeGuardians don't always stand out, but if underestimated, they've been known to get violent.Strike with BashBefore entering battle you must visualize your triumph to ensure the desired outcome.Crit or Devastate with Sure StrikeThe Black Númenórean Dolvaethor has gone to Haudh Valandil at Amarthiel's bidding, ${PLAYER}, and I fear the mischief he may cause in that hallowed place. The halls and tombs are lined with precious relics of the past -- relics of both great historical and personal value to the Dúnedain -- and they must not be despoiled!

I ask you to recover as many of these priceless relics from the depths of Haudh Valandil as you can; my brethren will keep them in a safe place until the Angmarim have been defeated and the tomb is once more a place of peace.

The entrance to Haudh Valandil is located on the island, beyond the bridge of Ariant. Beware of Dolvaethor and the kergrim that have awakened within its depths and recover the relics with all haste! If I am not here when you return, report to Captain-General Daerdan at Echad Garthadir, the Ranger-encampment here in the city.The entrance to the tomb is on the island beyond the bridge of Ariant, on the highest level of the city.

Glangilith has asked you to recover priceless relics from Haudh Valandil before the Angmarim ruin them.Collect priceless relics (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bingo is proud of the account he has written so far, and wants to see what Bilbo Baggins, noted author and adventurer, thinks of his manuscript.Bilbo Baggins is in the Hall of Fire, in Rivendell.

Bingo has asked you to show the manuscript he has been writing to Bilbo Baggins, noted author and adventurer.Bilbo is reading through Bingo's manuscript.Wait for Bilbo to finish reading Bingo's account of the journeyBilbo Baggins is in the Hall of Fire in the Last Homely House.

Bilbo has finished reading Bingo's manuscript, and is ready to give you his thoughts on it.Talk to Bilbo Baggins in the Hall of Fire, and note his feedback for BingoBingo is in the Hall of Fire, in the Last Homely House.Bingo has asked you to defeat dangerous creatures in the High Moors or Giant Valley, in the Trollshaws, and also to take notes on the parchment he gave you at the Ford of Bruinen.Defeat dangerous creatures in the High Moor or Giant Valley (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bingo is waiting in the Hall of Fire in the Last Homely House for you to return.Bilbo Baggins is in the Hall of Fire in the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.Talk to Bilbo Baggins in the Hall of FireBingo is in the Hall of Fire in the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.Talk to Bingo in the Hall of Fire'I wonder what Bilbo would think of my account of our journey so far, ${PLAYER}? He is both an author and an adventurer, and I would be very interested to hear any feedback he might have to offer! Can you give him my manuscript so far and ask if he will read through it? I am too shy to ask him myself, but if you ask perhaps he would be willing to take the time to read it!''Can you give Bilbo my manuscript so far and ask if he will read through it? I am too shy to ask him myself, but if you ask perhaps he would be willing to take the time to read it!''Ah, Bingo spoke to me of the account he is writing of his journey. This must be it!

'I would be pleased to read it and give him my thoughts. Give me a few moments to look over the manuscript. It should not be too long, but I will need some time to read it carefully. I will give it my undivided attention!''I am all a-flutter, ${PLAYER}! I cannot wait to hear what Bilbo Baggins thinks of my account of our adventure to this point!''I have finished reading Bingo's manuscript, ${PLAYER}, and I made a few notes. I very much enjoyed reading about the Shire again. I confess that I do miss the peace and quiet of my home, and Bingo's writing has reminded me of it in fine fashion: methodical and calm.

'I also enjoyed his account of the people he has met along the way: Tom Bombadil, the treasure-seekers in the Forsaken Inn, and also his lynx-friend Willem Whisker! Bingo is fortunate he has faced little adversity to this point, and I hope his journey remains danger-free as it progresses! My own adventure was full of trolls and goblins and dragons, as well as obstinate dwarves and Elves alike!

'Tell Bingo I enjoyed reading his account very much, ${PLAYER}, and will look forward to the rest when his journey is done!''I very much enjoyed reading about the Shire again. I confess that I do miss the peace and quiet of my home, and Bingo's writing has reminded me of it in fine fashion: methodical and calm.

'I also enjoyed his account of the people he has met along the way: Tom Bombadil, the treasure-seekers in the Forsaken Inn, and also his lynx-friend Willem Whisker! Bingo is fortunate he has faced little adversity to this point, and I hope his journey remains danger-free as it progresses! My own adventure was full of trolls and goblins and dragons, as well as obstinate dwarves and Elves alike!

'Tell Bingo I enjoyed reading his account very much, ${PLAYER}, and will look forward to the rest when his journey is done!''Well? What did the esteemed Bilbo think of my account to this point?'

You tell Bingo what Bilbo had to say about his writing, but the more you speak the more disappointed Bingo becomes.

'He liked the peace and quiet of the Shire? I am writing an adventure story, ${PLAYER}! Calm? That is the last thing I want my account to be! He hoped that the rest of my journey would be 'danger-free?' What a disaster!

'We have to do something. My journey has not been exciting enough, and I have not been including enough descriptions. Find dangerous creatures in the High Moor or Giant Valley and defeat them, making note of how exciting you find the battles! And return to the Ford of Bruinen and take some better notes of its appearance. I was so distracted thinking about W.W. that I wrote only a cursory piece about it! I need some help fleshing out that description, ${PLAYER}!''Bilbo did not like my manuscript! We have to do something! My journey has not been exciting enough, and I have not been including enough descriptions. Find dangerous creatures in the High Moor or Giant Valley and defeat them, making note of how exciting you find the battles! And return to the Ford of Bruinen and take some better notes of its appearance. I was so distracted thinking about W.W. that I wrote only a cursory piece about it! I need some help fleshing out that description, ${PLAYER}!''You have returned, my friend, and I hope you have had plenty of exciting and dangerous battles with which to spice up my manuscript! And I see that you also have taken down some notes on the Ford of Bruinen. Very good! I will use these notes to expand my description of that crossing. Bilbo will not believe how flowery my description will be now!''I will use the notes you brought me to expand my description of the Ford of Bruinen. Bilbo will not believe how flowery my description will be now!''You must be speaking in jest, ${PLAYER}! That is what Bingo took away from my feedback concerning his account of the journey? I did not mean that at all!

'Bingo has missed the point completely. Can you tell him that what is really important in writing is how the writer conveys to the reader the experience of the adventure? I am most interested in how the journey made Bingo feel, not in the number of dangers he faced or the quantity of adjectives he uses to describe the places he found!

'I enjoyed Bingo's account, and want to read more of it. But he has completely misunderstood my notes! Tell him not to worry about the reception to his book, or he will never write another sincere word!''Bingo has missed the point completely. Can you tell him that what is really important in writing is how the writer conveys to the reader the experience of the adventure? I am most interested in how the journey made Bingo feel, not in the number of dangers he faced or the quantity of adjectives he uses to describe the places he found!

'I enjoyed Bingo's account, and want to read more of it. But he has completely misunderstood my notes! Tell him not to worry about the reception to his book, or he will never write another sincere word!''I do not know about that. I respect Bilbo's opinion, so I will not press the issue, but I do worry that my journey has not been exciting enough to hold a reader's interest, ${PLAYER}. I will try and come up with some way to thrill my future readers, and I hope you will think on it as well. I have come to Rivendell, but my journey has only just begun! Where should we go next?'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Snowy Climb' (Recommended Level: 42)</rgb>The Misty Mountains are dotted with dwarf-ruins.There are some ruins to the north-west. There may be something of value to be found within them.Search the ruins to the north-west for treasureThere are ruins in the distance to the north-west that may contain treasures of great value.Search the ruins to the north-west for treasureAn old dwarf strongbox catches your eye. Word is, Larus Sharpshard is looking for a specific treasure and this may be the one he seeks.

Larus Sharpshard is located in a camp just north of High Crag.There are ruins in the distance to the north-west that may contain treasures of great value.'This is the box Heikki Goldwatcher of Thorin's Hall has ordered me to retrieve! It appears to be damaged, and not by the passage of time, I think. These marks might have been caused by goblin-weapons. Perhaps a party of goblins sought to carry the strongbox away and quarrelled among themselves?

'Why then did the victors not take the strongbox, I wonder? Ah, no matter. At least it still remains, ${PLAYER}?

'I'll just open it up and compare the contents with the record that Heikki gave to me before I set out.'Defeat Orcs in Angmar.Defeat Orcs in AngmarDefeat many Orcs in Angmar.Defeat Orcs in AngmarOrcs marching through Ram Dúath have unsettled the Earth-kin dwelling there and have cut off any hope of safe return for the Earth-kin hunters sojourning in the North Downs.There are Blogmal Orcs in the south-east part of the Ram Dúath.

The Tuokki, the Earth-kin elder leading the Suuri-lehmä tribe while their chieftain is away south in the North Downs, has asked you to help drive the Blogmal Orcs from Ram Dúath.Defeat Blogmal fighters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Blogmal skirmishers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Chief Tuokki is at the Suuri-lehmä camp in Ram Dúath.

You should return to Chief Tuokki and tell him of your victory against the Orcs of the Blogmal tribe.'For much time now, Orcs and evil Men and other evil things have come through Ram Dúath from the North, from Angmar. They go to fight in the South, but they cause much trouble here, too.

'We hate Orcs and their friends, and we try to fight them when we can, but now there are too many for us, and our hunters cannot return from the southern plains. Will you fight the Orc-folk, too? Help us, and kill the Orcs in Ram Dúath. Many Orcs of the Blogmal tribe can be found in the south-east part of the pass.

'We will give you the coins that we take from the dead Orcs. We have little need for them.''You have shed much Orc blood. This is good. We thank you for your help.''As long as the Orcs fill the pass, our hunters cannot return, and our people will starve.'Perform valiant deeds among the snows of the Misty Mountains.Complete quests within the Misty Mountains.Practise your stamp to interrupt your foes, for using the skill repeatedly will help you to perform it with greater ease in the future.Strike with StampPractice your stamp to interrupt your foes, for using the skill repeatedly will help you to perform it with greater ease in the future.Strike with StampNightmare has corrupted countless adventurers, sapping their strength to weaken their resolve. When Nightmare attempts to weaken your spirit, steel your will and fight through. Defeat him without harming any of the tortured spirits.Complete Challenge: Raising SpiritsSnow-beasts can be found throughout the Misty Mountains and are a hazard to any travellers making their way through.Snow-beasts can be found throughout the Misty Mountains, especially near the source of the Bruinen.

Defeat snow-beasts wherever you encounter them to make the mountain passes safer for those that travel through.Defeat snow-beastsSnow-beasts can be found throughout the Misty Mountains, especially near the source of the Bruinen.

Defeat snow-beasts wherever you encounter them to make the mountain passes safer for those that travel through.Defeat snow-beasts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Malthenor of Rivendell has been stationed in the Misty Mountains to keep watch over Glóin's camp.

Malthenor will be pleased to hear that you have defeated many snow-beasts.Snow-beasts can be found throughout the Misty Mountains, especially near the source of the Bruinen.

Defeat snow-beasts wherever you encounter them to make the mountain passes safer for those that travel through.'Snow-beasts are ferocious creatures that fight with tooth and claw on the high ridges, ${PLAYER}, and they are a grave danger to those forced to travel through the mountains. They must be defeated wherever they are found.''You have survived the claws of the snow-beasts and returned with a tale to tell, ${PLAYER}! I am glad to hear it and to tell you that you have performed a great service for those forced to travel into the mountains.'Orchalwë has returned to Tinnudir and wishes to speak with you.Orchalwë is at Tinnudir, north of Annúminas.

You have been instructed to speak with Orchalwë.'Much good work has been done this day within these ruins, and from what I hear, that is only the latest you have done to aid our cause. Evendim and Annúminas hold no special meaning to me -- my line is drawn from the lands to the south -- but I know what it means to those whose forebears called this place home.

'Orchalwë is one such Ranger. He has gone through much these past few months: helplessness, despair, doubt, anguish, anger, rage. I did not think he would come through to the other side and still be standing within the light... that is, until you came here. History, ruins, relics, memories of the past are all important and valuable things, but I would trade every one of them in a moment to see the hope in Man restored. That is something you have done, and I will forever be grateful.

'Orchalwë has returned to Tinnudir to the north. I ask that you venture to the small island once more and speak with him before your path takes you beyond the shores of Evendim.''You have done much for us all, and for that, the shores of Evendim will always be a home for you.''If you had asked me but a month ago where we would stand today, I would say here on this spot, but not in this place. I would have told you that we would be besieged, trapped within the tower on this island. Tomb-robbers would be plundering everything in sight, and Angmar would stand firm, ruling over Annúminas. We would be a withering shell waiting for the final blow.

'But I look over the waters today, and that is not the morning I awoke to. Our estates and ruins lie in peace and undisturbed, and while the city still echoes with battles, I believe we shall carry the day. Even if we never rebuild what has been physically destroyed here, we have already regained what was lost. We have had pride in our heritage. We have stood and fought like our forebears did. We have earned the privilege to be called Rangers. In that, we have reclaimed Evendim and Annúminas. From the ashes of the ruins, through our deeds and acts, the Kingdom of the North has begun to rise again.

'You will forever be in my thoughts, ${PLAYER}. There is much to do yet, but I believe now that we can accomplish our goals. I wish you the best on your journeys.'Rivendell is nestled hidden in a valley in the east of the Trollshaws.You have been called to Rivendell, in the Trollshaws, for there is much to do there.Talk to Elrond in Rivendell'We have just gotten word from Rivendell that Master Elrond wishes to speak with you. I know not what about, but I have found it is best not to keep powerful Elf-lords waiting.''You should go at once to Rivendell and speak with Master Elrond.''It is good you have come. We are in dark times, and there is much to do. I have heard of your deeds in Evendim and believe you can help bring a light to these lands as well.'Deep beneath the fields and rolling hills of the Angle lie the remnants of battles fought long ago. There are those who have come to plunder the Angle, and those who wish to protect and study its relics.Complete quests in the Angle for Narnien:

"The Lost Treasures of Haudh Erthaid"
"The Crumbling Halls of Glynafon"Complete "The Lost Treasures of Haudh Erthaid"Complete "The Crumbling Halls of Glynafon"Narnien is located in Gaerond, in the northern Angle.

You should tell Narnien that you have found enough artifacts for her studies to continue.Return to Narnien'Mae govannen. My name is Narnien and I am here to study the ruins of Haudh Erthaid. Alas, they are occupied by looters, thieves, and worse. As I am delayed in my explorations until the land is cleared, might I impose a boon upon you? If you find anything interesting while you are among the rocks or delving into the hills, I would appreciate it greatly if you would bring it to me.''You have done so much more than I could have hoped for, my friend! These artifacts will most certainly tide me over until the Angle is a safer place for my studies. I hear that you are doing what you can to further that cause as well. I thank you.'No matter how hard your opponents fight back, your resolve will always win out in the end.Use your Riddle or Trick Removal skillsCultivate the precise and dextrous movements to draw and fire at speed, no matter the distraction.Use the Needful Haste skillThe banks of the rivers that surround the Angle are home to deadly avanc. Deadly... and hungry.Riverside avanc can be found along the banks of the rivers in the Angle.

Thalathril has asked you to dispose of the deadly avanc who plague the river-banks.Defeat riverside avanc (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thalathril is located in Tham Lumren, in the northern Angle.

You should let Thalathril know that you have disposed of many riverside avanc.Return to Thalathril'My scouts have returned with reports of dangerous creatures lurking on the shores of the local rivers. Since you will be heading in that direction on Glatharn's foolish errand, why not see what you can do to dispose of the creatures?''Thank you for your effort here in the Angle. It has allowed me to focus on other things of great import, and for that I thank you.'There are not many ruins to be found in the Misty Mountains, for few indeed are those willing to build anything of note in such an inhospitable environment. Those that are to be found here were built long ago by the dwarves.Explore stuff.Only here can the Longbeards still be found in the Misty Mountains, and only in this small lonely camp built amongst the crumbled ruins of older, far grander dwarf-holdings. The few brave dwarves who have come here face insurmountable odds in the taming of these wild peaks, however, and the task seems impossible so long as the power of Angmar continues to grow in the far North.Once this ruin led into a haven used by dwarf-hunters and scouts who roamed the Misty Mountains in their incessant battles against the goblins. Now it lies sealed and abandoned, the sturdy warriors who once manned it long ago driven from the Mountains.Iskeld's Look-out is one of the oldest dwarf-outposts in the northern Misty Mountains. It was built to stand guard over the source of the river Bruinen and ensure that no enemy would be able to interfere with the life-giving flow of waters down into the lands below. After the fall of the Longbeard's fortress to the south, Iskeld's Look-out was abandoned, and only wild and terrible beasts roam here now.The Bitter Stair is a treacherous switchback that leads to the gate of the dwarf-fortress of Helegrod -- once the largest of the holdings of Durin's Folk in this region of Middle-earth, until in the Second Age it was sacked by the dragon Thorog and his brood, who claimed Helegrod as his lair.

Some years later, a force of dwarves led by a great hero fought their way up these stairs to the fortress. Many of their number were flung to their deaths below by the drakes which defended the approach, but in the end the hero fought his way into the fortress and did indeed slay Thorog.

Alas, so many dwarves were lost in the battle that they were forced to abandon it to the dragon's brood, and they have never returned thence.Rúdhan is feeling better enough to walk, but he and his wife will need the path north cleared if they are to survive the rest of the journey.Many are the dangerous beasts that remain between the Dunlending refugees and the safety of Gaerond, on the northern border of the Angle of Mitheithel. You have agreed to try and clear a path so they can reach the camp-site in safety.Defeat many creatures of the Angle and clear a path to Gaerond (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Elladan remains at the camp-site of Gaerond, in the Angle.Talk to Elladan at GaerondRúdhan and Onora have made it safely to Gaerond, but the future unfolds before them with all its mystery and its danger.Talk to Rúdhan at GaerondTalk to Onora at Gaerond'My leg is well enough now for me to walk, ${PLAYER}, but I am worried about the rest of the journey. You say there is a camp-site to the north-west, where friends will help us as you have done?

'I will entrust my life and that of my wife to you now, my friend, and I hope that trust is not misplaced. Will you go ahead of us and clear a path to this camp, this Gaerond of which you speak? Many are the threats that lurk still ahead of us, and I may not be able to move fast enough to evade them. But if you clear the way, perhaps we will make it.''No, I have not left the Angle just yet, ${PLAYER}. Always I believe myself ready to leave, and always, it seems, something comes up anew! Why, just now my arrows saved the lives of two Dunlendings fleeing from some villainy in the south! A pair of half-orcs nearly put blades to their flesh, but my bow was the swifter.

'Their names are Onora and Rúdhan. They say they know you, and owe you their lives. I said they owe them to me as well, but I made the jest and they stared at me with frightened eyes, so I think I shall leave the jokes for my brother.

'You should speak to them, and then I really must return to Imladris. But something always comes up!''We are in your debt, ${PLAYER}, for the path to Gaerond was mostly clear of the foes we feared, and I know that was your doing. We are fortunate for the quick bow of the Elf who greeted us as well, though Onora distrusts him. If he is friend of yours he will be friend of ours as well, in time.

'Unless he is prone to jest. Perhaps not then.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. We could not have made it this far without you.

'I know much of the north remains ahead of us, and the journey is not yet ended, but it feels good to have come so far from the dangers we left behind. Whatever awaits us, Rúdhan and I will face it together.

'Thank you for aiding us. You are a friend to our family and our folk from this day forward. You did not need to help us, but you did, and for that we are both grateful.'Kindle the fires of your passion to increase your art's impact.Strike with Smouldering WrathCachúnir has expressed a desire to take as much history and lore of Middle-earth into the West as is possible.Cachúnir is at Rivendell in the Trollshaws.

Cachúnir has asked you to pen some scholar's writs to be preserved in the libraries of Valinor when the Elves of Rivendell sail into the West.'It has come to my attention that your studies of the mysteries of Middle-earth have progressed considerably, ${PLAYER}. For many long centuries, those of us here in Rivendell have had limited opportunities to explore the world beyond, and soon the time shall come for us to take our leave and sail into the West with our brethren.

'Even those of us who live lives without measure cannot hope to know all the mysteries of the world, but we hope to carry with us into the West as great a trove of knowledge and history as we may, and so if you could aid us in this endeavour, we would be most grateful.

'To this end, I would ask you to pen some scholar's writs for us, so that we might add them to our libraries and ensure that they will forever adorn the shelves of luminous Valinor.''Ah, you have penned a few scholar's writs. Very good! These are a few more slivers of knowledge that shall not be lost to us when we embark on our journey into the West.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. We shall not forget your efforts on our behalf.'Arifael's tidings were of great concern to Ringhul.The scout Gwaemithrin is in the highlands north and east of Rivendell, at Glóin's camp.

Ringhul has asked you to take Arifael's warning to Gwaemithrin and learn what she might know of the giants in the Misty Mountains.Talk to Gwaemithrin'Master Elrond sent a scout into the Misty Mountains to watch the activities of the giants. At present, Gwaemithrin is encamped at the small outpost our dwarf-visitors have established north of Imladris.

'Bring Gwaemithrin word of your adventures in Arifael's vale and of the hostile giants there. She may have learned something more about them, though you would think she would have already reported this to Master Elrond if she had.

'Glóin's camp is north and east of Rivendell. Farewell, ${PLAYER}, and go with haste.''Ringhul sends word from Arifael of his kinsmen? I cannot say that I am surprised. I had attempted to journey to their halls in the south and east, but I was driven back.

'The journey is treacherous, and I do not recommend attempting it now, but if you should find yourself travelling that way in the future, the Elves of Rivendell would welcome any news you could bring back.

'When the time comes, try to make for the Giants' Table, a place of parley in elder days...and do not travel alone.'All signs point to a grisly end for your foes.Use any Rune-Sign of XYou have heard that there may be a more complete form of the sign you uncovered previously.Use Pet Command SkillsThe Earth-kin, rugged, practical, and earthy as they are, make extensive use of silk, which they obtain almost exclusively from spiders.There are spiders throughout Ram Dúath.

