The dwarves of the Misty Mountains have requested a collection of huge scales.Wanted: Huge scales from the worms found throughout the Bruinen Source.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Defeat trolls in Angmar.Defeat trolls in AngmarDefeat many trolls in Angmar.Defeat trolls in AngmarThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

It is purported that A Guiding Star was penned by Círdan the Shipwright, Master of the Grey Havens, but this claim has often been disputed. It seems many readers insist that it is not written in the mode of the Elves—though curiously such criticisms have not often come from Elves—but much praise has been given to the included sketches and diagrams that attempt to map the stars of Middle-earth. Regardless of its true authorship, this book contains a wealth of knowledge about the positions of stars, constellations, and how they might be used as guides for navigation upon the Sea.Talk to Mick Maidenhair in the Dwarrow-ward, in Bree-townA Guiding Star, Page 5A Guiding Star, Page 9A Guiding Star, Page 12A Guiding Star, Page 18A Guiding Star, Page 24A Guiding Star, Page 27A Guiding Star, Page 33A Guiding Star, Page 37'Is something wrong, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Mick Maidenhair that 'A Guiding Star' appears to be missing more than a few of its pages, and he nods solemnly.

'You have a keen eye, my friend. Perhaps you think I have sold you a book unworthy of its price, but I disagree, for there is still much knowledge to be gained from it. Even in such an incomplete condition, it has served me well for many years!

'If you somehow manage to find the missing pages, I would be most interested in learning what they have to say!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

A Hobbit's Holiday was written by Bilbo Baggins, a hobbit from the Shire who went on an unexpected and surprising adventure. You leaf through the pages with interest to read his account of meeting your ancestor Beorn, but you are unable to find it.

Many of the pages in the middle of the book have gone missing. An entire chapter seems to be missing. Is it the chapter where he met Beorn?Bilbo Baggins is in Rivendell, and will be most interested that you found a copy of his book.Talk to Bilbo Baggins at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.You should search for missing pages from A Hobbit's Holiday throughout Angmar and the Misty Mountains.A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 66A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 67A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 68A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 69A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 70A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 71A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 72A Hobbit's Holiday, Page 73'Ah, very interesting! You found a copy of my book! Not many were made, you know. It was a painstaking process and, to be honest, I did not think there would be a great deal of interest.'

You tell Bilbo that you are very interested to read about his encounter with your ancestor Beorn, but the chapter seems to be missing. He blanches somewhat.

'Well, yes, of course. You could defeat enemies in dangerous places and see if some of them have picked up the pages. I hope you will not be too upset by anything I may have written about your relative! It is important to remember that the dwarves and I were very frightened at the time, and Beorn was an imposing and quite scary fellow in his day! You will forgive me any unfortunate language I may have used in my portrayal of him, I hope!'Ravaedron has requested you accompany him to bid farewell to the last friends he made in Middle-earth, before he departs for the West.Ravaedron stands outside Fernbrake Cot, steeling himself for a sad parting.Talk to Ravaedron and help him say good-bye to his friendsRavaedron stands outside Fernbrake Cot, confused and unsatisfied.Talk to Ravaedron outside Fernbrake Cot'I have steeled myself for a sad parting, but your presence here should help. I am ready if you are, my friend.''Then let there be an end to my delays! "Sooner done, sooner past," as they say. It is not an Elven expression, of that I am certain.''I... do not understand, ${PLAYER}. I expected my farewell to contain a measure of sorrow for all of us, of course, but I did not think to quarrel. I do not want to leave it like this! But what can I do?'

Ravaedron frowns at the closed door of Fernbrake Cot.

'I am dissatisfied, ${PLAYER}. How can my friends wish for me to depart in this fashion? Did I not make clear to them that they will be protected by such adventurers as you even when I am gone? I am certain I did. I meant no insult.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

This journal was written by a hunter who seems to have spent much of his time alone somewhere on the western borders of Mirkwood across the Misty Mountains, where few Men live and goblins present a frequent threat. With no allies to rely upon and always outnumbered, he discovered the value of uncertainty and fear as a weapon to keep his pursuers off balance as he strove to survive in that rugged country. Having no-one to talk to, it seems that he chose to create a record of his life and experiences.

This journal is badly weathered and torn -- perhaps by the self-same goblins whom the hunter claims to have frequently out-witted -- and several key passages are missing. Another hunter of renown might be able to shed some light on this unusual book. Legolas of Mirkwood is said to be in Eriador on a mission of some import.This journal is badly weathered and torn -- perhaps by the self-same goblins whom the hunter claims to have frequently out-witted -- and several key passages are missing. Another hunter of renown might be able to shed some light on this unusual book. Legolas of Mirkwood, who is said to be in Eriador on a mission of some import.Talk to Legolas in RivendellIt may be that the missing pages could be found in the high crags of the Misty Mountains or in the ruins of Angmar.A Shot in the Dark, page 5A Shot in the Dark, page 10A Shot in the Dark, page 14A Shot in the Dark, page 16A Shot in the Dark, page 19A Shot in the Dark, page 22A Shot in the Dark, page 23A Shot in the Dark, page 25'Well met, ${PLAYER}. What have you there? A journal of some sort? The title is not familiar to me, but I may have heard of the man who wrote it. He was one who shunned the company of his own kind and chose to live far from paths well travelled. Still, he did have some contact with my people, and it is through this that I know of him.

'He was a remarkable hunter, particularly for one of the race of Men. I would not wonder to learn that he had no small lineage of the Dúnedain within him. In those days, before the Battle of Five Armies, the goblins would frequently come down from the Misty Mountains to hunt in the western forests and cause no little harm to the Elves that lived there if they could. Rather than avoid these raiding parties, this hunter would stalk them through their entire course, choosing his moments to strike with care and cunning. It is said that he became so adept at striking without warning or mercy, that the mere whistle of his arrow came to cause panic amongst the goblins and Wargs that he hunted.

'Alas, the lives of men are short, so it seems unlikely that he still lives, given the weight of so many winters, but it is interesting to see that this journal of his survived. To an aspiring hunter his writings might be of great interest, if the remaining pieces of the journal could be found.'Bingo is concerned that readers of his manuscript will not be enthralled by the tale of his journey, and wants to add some excitement to its pages.Snow-beasts and snow-lurkers can be found throughout the Misty Mountains.

Bingo is worried that his adventure will lull future readers to sleep, and wants to add some spice to his journey.Defeat snow-beasts in the Misty Mountains (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat snow-lurkers in the Misty Mountains (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bingo has decided to stop for a short rest at Glóin's camp, in the Misty Mountains, near the southward road to Rivendell.Talk to Bingo at Glóin's campBingo wants to travel north of Glóin's camp into the Northern Bruinen Source area of the Misty Mountains, in an effort to add some excitement to his journey.Travel north of Glóin's camp, toward the Northern Bruinen Source area of the Misty MountainsYou have encountered an injured dwarf in the narrow pass between the western and northern Bruinen source regions of the Misty Mountains, and should do what you can to help him.Talk to the injured dwarf in the narrow passBingo is in the narrow pass north of Glóin's camp, worrying about the wounds of the injured dwarf you encountered.Talk to Bingo in the narrow passBingo has asked you to search the Bruinen Source areas for clumps of herbs that may help heal the injured dwarf you encountered. He said you should watch Willem Whisker's reactions as you examine such herbs to determine if they will be helpful or hurtful.Bingo is in the narrow pass north of Glóin's camp, worrying about the wounds of the injured dwarf you encountered.

You have a handful of (possibly) healing herbs, and should bring them to the injured dwarf.Bingo is in the narrow pass north of Glóin's camp, worrying about the wounds of the injured dwarf you encountered.Talk to Bingo in the narrow pass'Oh, I am certain Bilbo meant no harm by his criticism of my manuscript to this point, but his words echo some of my own concerns! Will this account of my journey put my future readers to sleep, ${PLAYER}? I do not want that! This is to be a thrilling tale of adventure, not a soporific account suitably accompanied by a mug of warm milk before bedtime!

'I have a new idea, my adventurous friend. I propose that we climb the very mountains in which this Elf-haven is nestled! We will climb the Misty Mountains and have such adventures that any reader with the unfortunate idea of reading just before bed will be rewarded with nightmares and a poor sleep indeed!

'I have spoken with a number of the Elves here, and they tell me that the mountains are filled with many dangerous creatures. Snow-beasts, snow-lurkers... I have never seen any of these foes before, but we should do our part to defeat any that threaten as we climb. That will make for an exciting read when it is written down, will it not?''We will climb the Misty Mountains and have such adventures that any reader with the unfortunate idea of reading just before bed will be rewarded with nightmares and a poor sleep indeed!

'I have spoken with a number of the Elves here, and they tell me that the mountains are filled with many dangerous creatures. Snow-beasts, snow-lurkers... I have never seen any of these foes before, but we should do our part to defeat any that threaten as we climb. That will make for an exciting read when it is written down, will it not?''A good start, a good start! Those creatures were most ferocious, ${PLAYER}, and I think I can safely say that any hobbit hoping to read THIS book before bedtime will be dreaming of frightful snow-beasts and terrifying snow-lurkers, even in summer!

'We should travel even further north now, if you think you can bear the climb. Let us travel northward, to the Northern Bruinen Source. See where it is marked on this map? Our travels will only become more dangerous from here on out, I wager, and that will make for a good tale indeed!''We should travel even further north now, if you think you can bear the climb. Let us travel northward, to the Northern Bruinen Source. See where it is marked on this map? Our travels will only become more dangerous from here on out, I wager, and that will make for a good tale indeed!''This poor fellow looks to be seriously wounded!''This poor fellow looks to be seriously wounded!'The dwarf appears to have been in a very recent battle, and he struggles to speak. His wounds are very severe.

'Are you... to help...?' he manages, with great effort. 'The Corcur... ambushed...'

The pain is too much, and he is unable to say anything else.'This fellow must have been in a battle, and recently! We have to do something to help! That is what heroes do, is it not?'

Bingo thinks for a moment, and then visibly brightens.

'I know what we should do! I have heard that animals in the wild have a special sense for the kind of herbs and roots that will provide sustenance and heal their hurts. I am willing to wager that Willem Whisker has just this sort of ability, and could tell you if you are looking at helpful or unhelpful herbs! You should search the slopes of the Bruinen Source regions for such clumps of herbs, and see if Willem can tell you anything about them.

'You can do it, W.W.! Go with  and help her/him with the search!''This fellow must have been in a battle, and recently! We have to do something to help! That is what heroes do, is it not?'

Bingo thinks for a moment, and then visibly brightens.

'I know what we should do! I have heard that animals in the wild have a special sense for the kind of herbs and roots that will provide sustenance and heal their hurts. I am willing to wager that Willem Whisker has just this sort of ability, and could tell you if you are looking at helpful or unhelpful herbs! You should search the slopes of the Bruinen Source regions for such clumps of herbs, and see if Willem can tell you anything about them.

'You can do it, W.W.! Go with  and help her/him with the search!''You found some herbs that might do the trick? Well, no sense in waiting around! Give them to him, and quickly!'His wounds are too severe for him to speak right now, but he seems to understand that you mean to help. You apply a compress of the herbs, and now his healing will be a matter of time... one way or the other.'We have done what we can, ${PLAYER}, but I am not ready to leave just yet! We should stay and see if our efforts have been enough! I am sure they will prove sufficient. This dwarf is fortunate we happened along when we did!

'I wonder if he is an adventurer, as we are? He does have that look, doesn't he? We will have to ask him when he has recovered!'

<rgb=#00FF00>Bert Bartleby has arrived at Glóin's camp, in the Misty Mountains.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Dangerous Detour' (Recommended Level: 43)</rgb>Ravaedron has had a revelation, and now he knows just how to set things right with his friends the Stagshorns before he departs Middle-earth.Ravaedron has asked you to sift the banks of the rivers that enclose and pass through the Angle in search of suitable pigment stones for making paints and dye.Find red pigment stones along river-banks in the Angle (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find grey pigment stones along river-banks in the Angle (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find yellow pigment stones along river-banks in the Angle (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ravaedron agreed to meet you outside Thelgarth, the ruins atop the southernmost of the Angle's hills, when you found enough pigment stones.Ravaedron has chosen a location just to the east of Thelgarth, below the hill on which stand the ruins, to paint his gift for Maddoc and Glenys Stagshorn.Find the location chosen by Ravaedron, just to the east of Thelgarth and below the hill on which stand the ruinsRavaedron has begun to paint his gift for Maddoc and Glenys Stagshorn, east of Thelgarth.Talk to Ravaedron at the location he has chosen to paintDemúr has agreed to help you fend off any threats that may hinder Ravaedron while he paints, and is waiting for you to the south-west.Talk to Demúr to the south-westRavaedron is painting a gift for Maddoc and Glenys Stagshorn to the east of and below the ruins of Thelgarth.Talk to Ravaedron to the east of ThelgarthBrungos has agreed to help you fend off any threats that may hinder Ravaedron while he paints, and is waiting for you to the north-east.Talk to Brungos to the north-eastRavaedron is to the east of and below the ruins of Thelgarth.Talk to Ravaedron to the east of Thelgarth'I see. Yes, Prestadír spoke true: this land holds many unhappy memories for me. It was many years before I walked these hills again, and my friends among the Stôrs eased those hurts somewhat, but they did not long stay here. When they departed I did too, and wished not to return. Only the wish of Elrond or his sons could bring me back to Mitheithel, and so it was. I found no sign of the black riders they sought, but I did befriend Maddoc and Glenys Stagshorn, and that friendship was a bright memory unlike those I have of Sauron's war upon the Elves. I hope I have not damaged its foundation!

'But that has given me an idea, ${PLAYER}. The Stagshorns have lived in the Angle for most of their years, but they have not the long lives of the Eldar. This has always been a land of beauty for them, unmarred by the memory of war. I will give to them a gift, a painted landscape of Mitheithel, and that will repair the hurts done by our quarrel!

'I have not forgotten the lessons of Wenna, the painterly Stôr I once knew in Glynafon. We will fashion the pigments I need from natural materials found throughout Mitheithel, and so will the painting be made of the landscape it represents! This is going to work, ${PLAYER}. Can I ask you to help me collect the soft stones I will need to make these paints? You will find them alongside river-banks throughout the Angle. I will meet you outside Thelgarth when we have enough, for I believe from that high place I will find a vista worth painting!''I have not forgotten the lessons of Wenna, the painterly Stôr I once knew in Glynafon. We will fashion the pigments I need from natural materials found throughout Mitheithel, and so will the painting be made of the landscape it represents! This is going to work, ${PLAYER}. Can I ask you to help me collect the soft stones I will need to make these paints? You will find them alongside river-banks throughout the Angle. I will meet you outside Thelgarth when we have enough.''These are perfect, my friend! I will grind them up in the right quantities to make just the shades I need to capture the landscape of Mitheithel.

'I have chosen a good location to paint, ${PLAYER}. It is just to the east of Thelgarth, below the hill on which the ruins stand.''I have chosen a good location to paint, ${PLAYER}. It is just to the east of Thelgarth, below the hill on which stand the ruins.''This will do nicely.''Is this not a lovely spot? I envy those who can stride between these rivers and see only the beauty of the land, and not the war-wrought scars I have seen here. It will be my challenge to paint the view I see and not the view I remember, but I will let my brush lead the dance, and see where it takes me.

'Several of the Dúnedain have agreed to keep watch so I will not be disturbed while I paint. Demúr is just to the south-west. If you would like to help as well, I know he would appreciate the company, and I have learned in this short time that you are a ${CLASS} of no small skill.''Hail, ${PLAYER}. Let us prevent any curious threats or dangerous foes from disturbing our artist.''It is coming along, ${PLAYER}, but I am not yet finished. Perhaps Brungos could use your assistance while I continue my work? He is just to the north-east, and will appreciate your company as much as did Demúr, I am sure.''From here I heard the record of your efforts alongside Demúr, ${PLAYER}, and now I am prepared to best my kinmate's work! Let neither beast nor foe disturb Ravaedron's endeavours!''I have finished my work! I dare not attempt any further strokes, for I fear my painting shall resist any further improvement. Were I to continue I would risk spoiling it all!'Arohir has asked you to traverse the Ram Dúath into Angmar to seek out a place called Aughaire. Rangers named Golodir and Corunir have made this place their home, but no one has spoken to either in many years.The Rangers Golodir and Corunir are in the Hillman town of Aughaire, a place west of the north exit of the Ram Dúath.

Arohir has asked you to seek out the lost Rangers Golodir and Corunir who long ago were sent to Angmar.Seek out Golodir and Corunir'Many years ago, two Rangers named Golodir and Corunir went to Angmar to investigate rumour that Evil had arisen in the North once more. Unfortunately, we have heard nothing from them since the Angmarim raised their Orc-armies and occupied the Ram Dúath.

'I would ask you to make your way north and to seek out a place called Aughaire. This is a home to the Hillmen of the North, at least some of whom Golodir reported to be hospitable, unlike their kin in other places. If you find our two lost kindred, assure them that we have not forgotten them, and that we shall soon require their services again.

'Once you pass through the dangerous Ram Dúath to the north, you should be able to find Aughaire by heading west.''Please seek out Golodir and Corunir at the Hillman town of Aughaire in western Angmar.''You come from the south? It is long since I have had words from Esteldín. I had come to think that perhaps they had lost interest in my fate. From what I have heard of the Orc invasion of the North Downs, though, I am not surprised. My kindred have perhaps had more pressing matters to consider of late.

'What? Nay, the Angmarim have not yet troubled themselves overmuch with the Free Folk who live in this place. Perhaps you should talk to Chief Crannog if you would learn more, as these Hillmen have little but trouble to deal with in these dangerous days.

'Golodir? Alas, I fear that he has fallen. At least, it has been five years since last I saw his face. Beyond the tragedy of his passing, he was a leader to the Hillmen here, and his loss has caused them in turn to lose heart.'Elrond feels that the Nazgûl you drove off from the Trollshaws has fled into the Misty Mountains. He has asked you to continue your pursuit of the Nazgûl with the aid of Glóin.Glóin can be found at the camp at the top of the pass that leads from Rivendell into the Misty Mountains.

Elrond suggested that you seek Glóin's aid in the hunt for the Nazgûl.'I fear that while the Nazgûl you drove off has fled the Trollshaws, he still schemes to assault Rivendell. To the north and east of Rivendell, there are goblins and worse that he might bend to his will and use against us.

'I would ask that you continue in your pursuit of the Nazgûl and travel into the Misty Mountains. There you may seek the aid of Glóin, who has established a camp at the end of the pass out of Rivendell that leads to the Misty Mountains.

'Go now, for not only the safety of Rivendell is at stake. All of Middle-earth depends on your actions.''A Nazgûl? One of the Nine Ringwraiths they spoke of at the Council of Elrond? I have not forgotten the honeyed words conveyed through one of those Riders to us back at the Lonely Mountain! Such poison! They wished us to betray old Bilbo Baggins to Mordor.

'I'll help you as best I can, although  Dáin did task me with another matter. I can share some information though.''The threat presented by the Nazgûl has not yet passed. Please continue your pursuit of the creature with the aid of Glóin at his camp atop the pass that leads to the Misty Mountains.'Glóin told you that the goblins seem very active of late, and that he would not be surprised if the Nazgûl you are pursuing were the cause.The goblins have many camps among the ridges of Pinnath Fenui, far to the east and north of Glóin's camp.

Glóin told you that if you search for the goblins' camps in the Misty Mountains, you should find a goblin named Gurzmat. He appears to be their leader, and by defeating him, you should throw the goblins into disarray and perhaps find the trail of the Nazgûl.Find the goblin-campGurzmat is somewhere deep within the goblin-camp that sprawls along the ridges of the Pinnath Fenui.

Glóin told you that by defeating the goblin Gurzmat, you would throw the goblins into disarray and perhaps find the trail of the Nazgûl you seek.Glóin is at his camp north of Rivendell.

In defeating Gurzmat, you find him accompanied by hostile Dourhand Dwarves. Glóin should be made known of this development immediately.'So you say that Elrond mentioned goblins in his direction to come here? It doesn't surprise me. I ran into some troublesome goblins up here when I crossed the Misty Mountains in Thorin's great quest. That time we were sorely beset by goblins.

'Now, it does seem the foul things are even more stirred up lately, and perhaps your Rider is the cause. One of the scouts here reported them moving far from their holes far to the east and heard them chanting "Gurzmat"... their leader no doubt.

'Aye, that's what needs to be done. Slay this Gurzmat, and the goblins will be in such a disarray they won't present a problem to anyone -- and maybe you'll find the trail of this Rider you seek. Seek out the goblin fires to the north and east along the Pinnath Fenui, and you'll likely find Gurzmat holed up deep in one of the camps. I wish I could help you more, but as I said, there's a more pressing task from Dáin that I must give my attention to.''The goblins have a camp out there someplace, hidden somewhere among the snows. Look east and north of here, along the ridges of the Pinnath Fenui. Once you find this camp, work your way deep inside. If you can put an end to this Gurzmat, you'll likely put an end to that Rider's schemes.''You've found the camps, but Gurzmat still lives? ${PLAYER}, that just will not do. You need to ascend into that goblin-camp and put an end to Gurzmat!''What! Dourhand Dwarves with the goblins? Oh, yes, it's all coming together now!

'While you were away, one of my scouts reported something interesting. You see, the task given to me by King Dáin was to track down the Dourhands' last refuge. The scout seems to have found it, as there were a large number of Dourhands around an old dwarf-fortress called Gabilazan. But that wasn't all that he found!

'As the scout watched from a high ridge, two figures rode up to the fortress. One was a dwarf, the other a cloaked figure. At first he didn't know what he was seeing, but when all the Dourhands stopped what they were doing and cheered, he knew the dwarf could only be one person: Skorgrím! Now, with what you've told me, the other figure with him must have been the Ringwraith. It would seem our two tasks are but one, friend.'The woodworkers of Bree pride themselves on their craftsmanship.Maggie Smallwood is at the Bree Hunting Lodge, west of the South-gate.

Maggie Smallwood has requested that you bring her a crafted Bree-spear to help the woodworkers of Bree arm the Watchers.'The woodworkers of Bree have been worked to the bone ever since those brigands started stirring up trouble. You see, Chief Watcher Grimbriar has ordered all the woodworkers in Bree and the surrounding towns to craft Bree-spears to protect against any further attacks.  

'Now, I am not one to disobey Chief Watcher Grimbriar's orders, but I simply won't take any new spears that aren't crafted from black ash. It has been getting harder and harder to find black ash, what with all the newcomers to Bree-land coming in and clearing the best trees to make new houses. Without black ash, I am afraid the spear-hafts won't be strong enough.

'If you are lucky enough to find any black ash, the Men of Bree would be in your debt if you were to bring us any Bree-spears for us to help defend against further attacks.''I tasked you with bringing me a Bree-spear, but I never expected you to actually finish the order so quickly! Let us see here...well balanced, sharp, and a haft crafted with black ash! This Bree-spear will be a strong weapon against the brigands.

'The woodworkers of Bree are honoured to have this weapon in our stores, ${PLAYER}.'The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of broken great swords to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver broken great swords from the Orcs and Angmarim surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Broken great swords from the Orcs and Angmarim in the lands surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Broken great swords from the Orcs and Angmarim in the lands surrounding Aughaire.'We will melt these weapons down and reforge them anew. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of pure black fur to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black fur from beasts surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Pure black fur from beasts surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Pure black fur from beasts surrounding Aughaire.'These will be used for crafting much for our people. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Bainthir, an Elf at the camp of Echad Candelleth, is awaiting a delivery of broken longswords.You have accepted a task to collect broken longswords from the goblins, Orcs, and other intelligent life surrounding Echad Candelleth. You are to deliver the items to Bainthir at the camp or to a collection box.Wanted: A collection of broken longswords from the goblins, Orcs, and other intelligent life surrounding Echad Candelleth. All items should be delivered to Bainthir at the camp.'It is good to know that these have been destroyed. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Bainthir, an Elf at the camp of Echad Candelleth, is awaiting a delivery of new sword sheaths.You have accepted a task to collect new sword sheaths from the goblins, Orcs, and other intelligent life surrounding Echad Candelleth. You are to deliver the items to Bainthir at the camp.Wanted: A collection of new sword sheaths from the goblins, Orcs, and other intelligent life surrounding Echad Candelleth. All items should be delivered to Bainthir at the camp.Wanted: A collection of new sword sheaths from the goblins, Orcs, and other intelligent life surrounding Echad Candelleth.'It is good to know that these have been destroyed. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.While Orcs dislike the sun nearly as much as their lesser brethren, the goblins, they have more tolerance for it and can be forced to endure it for a time when necessity -- or the lash of their masters -- demands. Despite this, it has long been an artifice of the Enemy to conjure forth vast clouds of smoke or darkness both to protect their troops from the rays of the sun and to conceal their movements from spies and scouts.Defeat OrcsA Sage of Eriador has invited you to recollect your experience with Legolas deep in the marshy fens of the Gladdalf.The Reflecting Pool in Rivendell will allow you to revisit 'The Unmarked Trail.'

A Sage of Eriador has challenged you to revisit your experience with Legolas by using the Reflecting Pool in Rivendell.Peer into the Reflecting Pool and revisit 'The Unmarked Trail'A Sage of Eriador is by the Reflecting Pool in Rivendell.

A Sage of Eriador in Rivendell is waiting to discuss what you have learned from revisiting your experience with Legolas in the Gladdalf.'Always you press forwards, ${PLAYER}, eager to accept new challenges and face new evils; but there is value in remembering your past deeds. From them, there is much to learn!

'Do you remember your pursuit of the missing Black Rider through the forests of the Trollshaws? You and Legolas followed the trail deep into the marshes of the Gladdalf, but were confronted by the monstrous Caller of the Bog and its corrupted fen-crawlers. Still, you and Legolas were victorious and put an end to Taushakh, king of the wood-trolls and thrall to the Nazgûl.

'Peer into the Reflecting Pool and experience this memory anew, ${PLAYER}. You will find that recalling your past may do you much good in the present.''I am pleased that you have revisited your experience with Legolas, ${PLAYER}. Only through study of our past deeds can we gain knowledge that will aid us in the future.''You must look into the Reflecting Pool, ${PLAYER}.'Chief Tuokki has asked you to seek out one of the Earth-kin -- a scout who was sent into the next valley to spy upon a band of deadly bat-women who have made lair somewhere in the hidden clefts of the Ram Dúath. The Chief informed you the scout had a small camp hidden high up along the wall of the valley to the East.'Some days ago our bravest scout leave to spy upon the Orcs as they travel through the pass.

'He sent message that he found something worse - a dark cleft filled with evil bat-creatures he has not seen before... but that is last we hear from him.

'Can you find our scout? See that he is safe? He had small camp hidden high up along wall of the valley to the east - if he lives you find him there.''Chief Tuokki send you to help me? Could have sent someone bigger, I think...but I am hurt and will take your help gladly!

'Evil things gather here in Ram Dúath. More evil than Orcs, more evil than Men who serve dark lords. I shall tell you of them!'Ravaedron has asked you to accompany him as he bids farewell to his friends the Stagshorns, before he leaves Middle-earth forever.Ravaedron is outside Fernbrake Cot, the home of his friends the Stagshorns.Talk to Ravaedron outside Fernbrake CotStand with Ravaedron and provide him the comfort he needs as he bids farewell.Accompany Ravaedron as he bids farewellMaddoc Stagshorn has welcomed you to Fernbrake Cot.Talk to Maddoc StagshornGlenys Stagshorn has welcomed you inside her home.Talk to Glenys Stagshorn inside Fernbrake CotStand with Ravaedron and provide him the comfort he needs as he bids farewell.Visit with the Stagshorns as Ravaedron bids farewellThe air has grown chilly inside Fernbrake Cot.

Maddoc Stagshorn motions for you to speak with him.Talk to Maddoc Stagshorn inside Fernbrake Cot'You will find the Stagshorns most agreeable, my friend. They are a charming couple, and their son Bran is quite a lively lad.

'I will let them know we are here.''Come inside, come inside! There are few roads in this land, and you must be weary from your travels!''Have you known Ravaedron long? We met him only recently, but he is dear to us, and the tales he has told us of long ago times and distant places have captured our imaginations! Did he ever find the prey he sought, the dark-robed villains that drew him to the Angle?

'We are sworn to secrecy, and will speak of them to no one else. But I confess the nighttime shadows seemed deeper after he spoke of the horseless riders loose in the wild!

'Still, we fear them not. My husband and I come from tough stock; none could long live in this land without being made hardy, or beginning so!''I am sorry you had to see that, ${PLAYER}. It pains me to say this, but perhaps Ravaedron was not as close a friend as we thought. His people and ours must simply be too different.

'Did he befriend us... out of pity? Do all long-lived folk look upon the men and women of the Angle as mere mayflies, alive for so short a time they must be safe-guarded and shown sympathy? I thought our friendship with Ravaedron was different, but I suppose it was not.'You have come with Prestadír to conduct his patrol around the ruins of Eitheldir, and to hear what he wishes to speak with you about.Prestadír is outside the ruins of Eitheldir.Talk to Prestadír outside EitheldirPrestadír stands in the ruins of Eitheldir.Talk to PrestadírPrestadír stands in the ruins of Eitheldir.Talk to PrestadírPrestadír has asked for you to follow him outside.Follow Prestadír through the hole in the wallPrestadír stands above the rushing waters of the Hoarwell, just outside the walls of Eitheldir.Talk to Prestadír outside the walls of Eitheldir'After the war, the Elves searched both the Hoarwell and the Greyflood for the remains of Oranoril or Celebrimbor, but they found no sign of either. This precipice became a place of pilgrimage for many of the Noldor, particularly for those whose flight to Rivendell she had bought with her life.

'Ravaedron was one of these, ${PLAYER}, and I know it is not easy for him to walk today in the Angle, with such memories as these still fresh in his mind. Eregion remained desolate after the War of the Elves and Sauron, its shattered halls crumbling with the passage of the years, becoming overgrown with grass and vine, but the stones remember what transpired, and so do those who were there.

'This is why I do not gainsay Ravaedron his choice to leave Middle-earth. If that is the peace he desires, I want him to have it.''I am not as well-versed in the history of this land as some of my folk, but I know enough to share some of it with you, ${PLAYER}. Seven centuries into the Second Age, the great Elf-smith Celebrimbor journeyed from Lindon to dwell in the holly-forests at the foot of the Misty Mountains. That realm came to be known as Eregion, and it was the birthplace of many great works and treasures of craft.

'The heart of Celebrimbor's realm lay to the south of the Loudwater, but the Elves drove out the trolls and hostile Amonedain in between, constructing outposts in the wooded hills on the border-marches. The chief of these was this stronghold, Eitheldir, built to watch the crossings of the Hoarwell and to keep open the road to Lindon.

'The governance of the keep was given to Oranoril, a Noldor companion of Celebrimbor. She had served as a captain in the wars against Morgoth, and was renowned for both her loyalty and her ferocity.''I would not have begrudged them the shelter if they came by it honestly, and turned not to robbery. But alas it was not so.'

Prestadír gazes up at the sky, peering into the unknowable heights.

'It looks like rain,' he says, and then returns his gaze to earth.

'You have heard of the War of the Elves and Sauron, ${PLAYER}, in which the Dark Lord sought mastery over the works of Eregion? Celebrimbor perceived the designs of Sauron, but there was little time to prepare for the destruction that awaited the Elves.''Oranoril took charge of the remaining soldiers following the death of Celebrimbor; only her great skill of command kept the defeat from becoming an inescapable bloodbath, and she ordered her people to retreat. At the Hollin Ridge she watched with horror as Sauron's forces marched behind the ghastly banner they had made of the once-proud Elf-smith.

'Knowing a similar fate awaited her if she was caught, Oranoril devised a scheme to save what of her people she could. She chose a small force from her own household and tricked Sauron's army into pursuing them. On a grim chase she led them across the Bruinen into the Angle, while the bulk of her kindred escaped by secret paths into the mountains and then to Elrond's young refuge. Ravaedron was one of these, and he never forgot his last sight of Oranoril's company disappearing over the ridge, pursued by Sauron's foot-soldiers.''Oranoril's company evaded the armies of the Dark Lord until they reached Eitheldir, but when they arrived below the stronghold they found the crossing of the Hoarwell already held against them. A brief siege followed, but the Orcs far outnumbered the few Elves that remained; the gates were broken and the keep razed. One by one the companions of Oranoril perished, until only she and three others remained.

'Together they performed one last act of defiance, fighting their way through the throng to Celebrimbor's body and prizing it from the clutches of the Orcs. With her last dying strength Oranoril, now the last living Elf in Eitheldir, took up the smith's corpse and leapt from the precipice into the foaming waters of the Hoarwell. And so even in his victory Sauron was robbed of his trophies, and his anger was without match.

'Furious that many of the Noldor had eluded him, and unable to find where they had fled, Sauron invaded Eriador from the Angle, intending to crush the realm of Lindon. Instead he found the Men of Númenor waiting for him, for they had answered the call of Gil-galad, and the combined forces of Elves and Men broke Sauron's strength. From whence came the final blow? Why, it came from Elrond and the folk of Imladris, for when their strength was recovered they returned with a fire in their hearts and Sauron could not stand against them. Defeated, it was his turn to flee; to Mordor he escaped.'Ravaedron has come up with another scheme he hopes will make things right with the Stagshorns.Himeinior has agreed to help Ravaedron with his latest scheme, and is waiting to speak with you.Talk to HimeiniorRavaedron has finished whatever he was doing, and is now ready to thank you for your help.Talk to RavaedronYou have come with Ravaedron to Fernbrake Cot.Talk to Ravaedron outside Fernbrake CotRavaedron has asked you to knock on the door to Fernbrake Cot.Knock on the door to Fernbrake CotYou have drawn the unenviable task of weathering the storm of the Stagshorns' displeasure with Ravaedron.Weather the storm of the Stagshorns' displeasure with RavaedronRavaedron has invited you and the Stagshorns to follow him around the side of Fernbrake Cot.Follow Ravaedron'Did Ravaedron tell you how long this would take? Because he gave me no indication, and this is not the only task I have set before me today!

'Let us hope this takes minutes and not hours.''Our next destination is Fernbrake Cot, ${PLAYER}. With your help I will set this right.''I needs must prepare, ${PLAYER}. You knock on the door while I make ready.'Ravaedron is displeased that many of the Rangers plan to depart for some mysterious purpose in the coming weeks, and Prestadír seeks to ease his mind.The Ranger Prestadír is in Tornhad, seeking to ease Ravaedron's worries.Talk to Prestadír in TornhadPrestadír asked to meet you outside the ruins of Eitheldir, to the north of Tornhad.Meet up with Prestadír outside the ruins of Eitheldir, north of TornhadPrestadír is outside the ruins of Eitheldir, north of Tornhad.Talk to Prestadír outside the ruins of EitheldirPrestadír is outside the ruins of Eitheldir, north of Tornhad.Talk to Prestadír and patrol the ruins of EitheldirPrestadír is outside the ruins of Eitheldir, north of Tornhad.Talk to Prestadír outside the ruins of EitheldirRavaedron is at Tornhad, the encampment of the Rangers, south of Eitheldir.Talk to Ravaedron in Tornhad'These Dúnedain have always gone where they will, and Prestadír tells me that they are soon to depart on such an errand. How can they protect the Stagshorns if they intend to leave the Angle and cross the mountains?

'Must I change my plans? Should I not journey to the Grey Havens, there to leave Middle-earth?''Should I stay after all? Must Híthrochon leave me behind?''I told Ravaedron that in confidence, ${PLAYER}, but yes: I and some of my folk have been tasked with looking into some of the rumours coming from Mirkwood. We are soon to depart, and cannot promise him what he seeks of us.

'We of the Dúnedain have sworn an oath to Aragorn, our chief, and none of us would break that oath, even for so dear a friend as Ravaedron. And he knows this, which tells me this performance is for your benefit, ${PLAYER}.'

Prestadír turns again to Ravaedron.

'Change none of your plans, my friend. Not all of the Dúnedain will come with me on this errand, and many of our people will remain here at Tornhad. They will keep an eye upon the safety of the woodcutter and his family. Speak to Mithrendan and Angwedh, and see if it is not so!

'But for you, ${PLAYER}, while I have your ear there is something I would like to discuss. Let us meet near Eitheldir, to the north, and we can speak while I conduct my last patrol for today.''While I have your ear, ${PLAYER}, there is something I would like to discuss. Let us meet near Eitheldir, to the north, and we can speak while I conduct my last patrol for today.''Over here, my new friend.''Thank you for coming, ${PLAYER}. The Dúnedain have long protected folk who dwell on the edge of the wilderness, but we could not do so if our hearts were troubled. Our own families reside within the Angle of Mitheithel, and if we are certain they live in safety, the obligation we have sworn is more readily-performed.

'So it is that the Dúnedain conduct patrols such as these on the outskirts of Mitheithel, and on the approaches to Tornhad. In times of danger, the village can be evacuated and we move our families to a number of caves in the outlying hills. It has happened more and more often in recent years: an unwelcome change every time, but necessary. It is a cause of sadness for the old-timers, and a pleasing lark for the children, but for me it justifies our caution.

'What say you? Will you patrol these ruins with me?''Then let us begin, and remain alert for anything that might threaten the safety of Tornhad and its people.''I thank you again for hearing me out, ${PLAYER}. I cannot begin to understand the burden that Ravaedron must feel when he walks in this land. I want him to depart these shores and find the peace his heart deserves. I hope you feel the same, and will help him accomplish it.

'Let us return now to Tornhad.''Let us return now to Tornhad.''Welcome back, ${PLAYER}. While you and Prestadír were gone, I received assurances from the others of his folk that they will keep out a watchful eye for the Stagshorns when I am gone. So that is all right.

'What did you and he speak about while you were away?'Santtu at the Lossoth outpost of Kauppa-kohta awaits a delivery of broken great swords.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver broken great swords from the sentient creatures in the woods surrounding Kauppa-khota and the frozen valleys to the north to Santtu, a Lossoth watching over the outpost.Wanted: broken great swords from the sentient creatures conspiring against the Lossoth. Deliver the items to Santtu at Kauppa-kohta.Wanted: broken great swords from the sentient creatures conspiring against the Lossoth.'It is good to know that there are fewer of these creatures about to harm us now. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Santtu at the Lossoth outpost of Kauppa-kohta awaits a delivery of great filth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver great filth from the fell spirits in the areas surrounding Kauppa-khota to Santtu, a Lossoth watching over the outpost.Wanted: Great filth from the fell spirits surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta. Deliver the items to Santtu at Kauppa-kohta.Wanted: Great filth from the fell spirits surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta.'This will prove helpful to the Lossoth. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Santtu at the Lossoth outpost of Kauppa-kohta awaits a delivery of pure black fur.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black fur from the beasts in the woods surrounding Kauppa-khota to Santtu, a Lossoth watching over the outpost.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta. Deliver the items to Santtu at Kauppa-kohta.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta.'This helps us and all of our people. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Undertake quests within Angmar.Complete quests within AngmarThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

It is said in the most ancient of tales that the world was formed in a great chorus of song, but this story is ancient and shrouded in myth even to the oldest among the living Elves, and no one in Middle-earth today could claim to know the truth of it. Towards the end of the Second Age, during the War of the Last Alliance, there was born a fair woman who sang in such wonderous tones that even the Elves would stop and listen in silence for so long as she would sing for them. Stranger still, she had no teaching in song or music, and yet could compose such melodies as to bring tears to the powerful and the Wise. She claimed that she composed no songs, and that she was simply trying to duplicate a music that she could hear -- a music that she claimed arose from the world itself, from mountains, streams, wind, and tree alike, in infinite variety.

Melodies of the Valar is the book that contains these songs as the Elves transcribed them, but most of the copies of this book have been taken away into the West, and very few remain in Middle-earth today. Alas, this one has been damaged, but an Elven minstrel of renown might well be able to shed some light on the matter.Talk to Lindir at the Last Homely House in RivendellMelodies of the Valar, page 12Melodies of the Valar, page 14Melodies of the Valar, page 23Melodies of the Valar, page 24Melodies of the Valar, page 27Melodies of the Valar, page 29Melodies of the Valar, page 30Melodies of the Valar, page 32Greetings, ${PLAYER}. I hope the day finds you well? A book? Ah, and not just any book I see! You have quite a treasure there in hand, my friend. That book is much revered among the Elves. I myself would count it among the finest works ever granted to the world by Men, but then perhaps I am a bit biased. I am sure you know some of the tale behind it? Of the woman who claimed a gift to hear the chorus of the world? It is true, or at least so I believe it to be, but there is a poignant twist to the tale as well that few remember today.

'You see, it is said that this poor girl was driven to the brink of madness by her gift, for it seemed that no matter how beautiful those around her found her voice and the melodies she crafted with it, she herself heard only broken, half-finished works from her throat -- a sound which could not compare with the grand symphony which only she could hear. In the end she sang no more and fell silent, for no matter how much those around her extolled the beauty of her song, she could stand her own voice no longer, hearing it as nothing more than a mockery of the chorus she had striven so long to compliment. She lived out the latter half of her life as mute as stone, contenting herself only to listen to the symphony of the world and abandoning her dream to draw it into the world of Men.

'Most scholars among Men believe that she was a wonderfully talented minstrel who was simply unstable -- as Men and even Elves sometimes are when true genius arises -- but among the Elves, it is believed that she was truly gifted with the ability to hear the faint echoes of the song from which the world arose, and we faithfully recorded her songs in the years before her voice fell silent. I cannot say from whence this copy came or how it came to be damaged, but it is a book worth pursuing, for no more beautiful songs exist in the tongues of Men today.Pirkka of the Suuri-lehmä, told you of a clan of Hillmen in Angmar who are friendly to them and should be made aware that the main body of the Orc-host has passed south into the North Downs.Aughaire is west of the northern pass of Ram Dúath, a valley concealed and surrounded by hills. There is also said to be a secret trail that goes there directly from the Earth-kin village.

Pirkka has asked you to take the news of the passage of the Orc-army to Crannog, chief of the Hillmen of Aughaire.'North of here, Ram Dúath opens into the dark land of Angmar. There are many evil Men and Orcs in that land, and worse things too, so we do not go there ourselves. But not all the folk of Angmar are enemies to us. There is one clan of Hillmen in those lands who have ever been our friends, sending south to trade with us when they can. Perhaps they will be friendly to you as well.

'Like us, they hate the Orcs, and they stand against the evil ones who rule in the black city. We want to let them know that the main body of Orcs has now passed south into the North Downs. This may help them in their counsels, and perhaps they can resume their trade with us.

'Find the man called Crannog. He is the Chief in Aughaire. Tell him the news I have told you. Aughaire is west from the northern pass of Ram Dúath, in a concealed valley over a steep rise in the hills. Sometimes in the past, the men of Aughaire travelled a steep trail to our village -- a trail the Jorthkyn are too big to walk, but perhaps you can find it not far from here.''Ho, Southron! The Jorthkyn sent you to us? It is long and longer still since last we had enough goods of our own to be able to trade with those strange folk.

'Your words are welcome, though little good do they do us. We are hard-pressed on all sides, even with the Orc-army no longer encamped upon our doorstep.

'Still, I will open the host-hearth of Aughaire to you. You are welcome here, for so long as we ourselves remain.''Will you bring the news of the Orc-army to Crannog?'You have assisted the Earth-kin scout, but the morroval-threat remains, endangering the Earth-kin and all else who dwell in the hills of Angmar.Morcheryn dwells in a cave on the east side of Ram Dúath, where the other merrevail can also be found.

The Earth-kin scout has asked you to seek out and destroy the leader of the Merrevail for the safety of his people. He also suggested you take friends.Slay MorcherynThe Earth-kin Scout is hidden in a valley east of Duvairë in the Ram Dúath.

With the Mistress of Shadows slain, you should return to him.'You are a proven friend to our people, but what I ask of you now may be too much. There is a cave in this very place where the merrevail make their lair, and in this cave dwells their leader, a bloody-handed creature we have heard the Orcs call Morcheryn.

'The merrevail are cruel and vicious, worse even than the Orcs and the evil Men of Carn Dûm. They will continue to prey on us if we do not fight them. I ask you to enter this cave and to destroy this 'Morcheryn'. If she falls, then perhaps the others will leave Ram Dúath and return to whatever foul place in Angmar it is that spawned them.

'These evil things have slain many of the Suuri-lehmä... I think you should take friends with you if you go there.''For the sake of my people, I ask you to slay Morcheryn.''I am pleased that you have returned safely from such a dangerous quest. In the name of my people, I thank you for your deeds.'Perform more valiant deeds among the snows of the Misty Mountains.Complete quests within the Misty Mountains.You have recovered both strongboxes for Larus Sharpshard. Now but one task remains.Heikki Goldwatcher is inside Thorin's Hall, located within the Blue Mountains.

Larus Sharpshard has sent you to deliver the two strongboxes you helped reclaim to Heikki Goldwatcher.'There's but one thing left to do, take the strongboxes to Heikki Goldwatcher in Thorin's Hall.

'I know it's a bit of a trek to go all the way there, but I think it's the proper thing to do. I wish I could do it myself, since it's my reputation that will be besmirched in Heikki's records if he's not happy, but I'm far too busy here to make such a journey.

'You'll find Heikki in Thorin's Hall in the Blue Mountains. Hopefully Heikki won't be too unhappy when you get there!''Take that box to Thorin's Hall, ${PLAYER}.''Finally, word from Larus Sharpshard. What, what's this? You've brought the strongboxes? That fool, did he even read my note to him? I wanted the name of Halmur Stoneshaper's father! I will make a note of this in my records!

'Wait, you met Halmur's descendant? Good, good! Did he tell you his father's name? Halmur? Oh, Vithurr was the first Halmur's father! Ah, very good! That is exactly what I wanted to know.

'I suppose I'll add the gold here to the treasury, although it really matters not to me. I honestly did not expect there to be any treasure left, after all these years. No, the name of Halmur Stoneshaper's father is really all I needed. Filling in the gap in the records is the real treasure!'Aurochs skulls apparently have some significant meaning to the Earth-kin of the Suuri-lehmä, and some have been stolen from them.The aurochs skulls are likely in Skathmur in the eastern part of Ram Dúath.

Santtu has asked you to recover a stolen aurochs skull.'Many years ago, we brought with us from the South some skulls of lehmä -- what you little people call aurochs. Very important skulls. The skulls honour Suuri-lehmä.

'Jorthkyn take the skulls on path through Ram Dúath to see new sights in this place. This must be done every year. This year, our brethren did not return. Scouts say that Orcs killed Jorthkyn and took the skulls. Orcs are in a strong place in the east side of Ram Dúath.

'Bring back a skull from the Orcs. Maybe they are in their strong place called Skathmur?''A skull has returned to us. Welcome home, skull! What have you seen amongst the Orcs?

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You are a friend to the Jorthkyn. All the lehmä now rejoice!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Art of the Perfect Punch was written by Brom Hawksbeard, a brawler from Bree-land.

Many of the pages in the middle of the book have gone missing.Perhaps Brom Hawksbeard can explain why this book you purchased is incomplete.Express your dissatisfaction to Brom Hawksbeard in Bree JailYou should search for missing pages from The Art of the Perfect Punch throughout Angmar and the Misty Mountains.The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 6The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 7The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 15The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 19The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 22The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 23The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 24The Art of the Perfect Punch, Page 49'Ah, the Art of the Perfect Punch! It is my masterwork, ${PLAYER}, the finest tome ever written on the art of pummeling opponents with your fists! It will require you to put in some effort of your own, of course. Did you expect otherwise?'

Brom studies you with sparkling eyes, and then laughs.

'Lesson number one: do not punch the author of the tome, for he wrote all the missing pages and no doubt can knock you down! Turn your dissatisfaction upon foes in the wild and you will complete the book you paid for in no time at all!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

This book was written as a primer in the Guardian's art some time in the middle of the Third Age, during the difficult period after the fall of Khazad-dûm. At that time, Durin's Folk were scattered and greatly reduced in number, and the Guardian's role took on a much greater importance as the threat of goblins and Orcs to dwarf-settlements beneath the roots of the world became more and more common. The dwarves often settled for building powerful strongholds designed to make the approach to their cities impervious to assault, manning them with skilled and powerful guardians to ensure that none would pass unchallenged.

While the book itself is common enough, the owner of this copy seems to have scribbled copious notes within the margins of the pages -- notes containing an experience and insight to the art that even at a glance exceeds that of the original author. Unfortunately, the copy is worn and tattered, and a number of pages are missing. Perhaps someone well-versed in the art could provide you with some insight as to the identity of the mysterious scribbler and where his remaining notes could be found?While the book itself is common enough, the owner of this copy seems to have scribbled copious note within the margins of the pages - notes containing an experience and insight to the art that even at a glance exceeds that of the original author. Unfortunately the copy is worn and battered, and a number of pages are missing. Perhaps someone well versed in the art could provide you with some insight as to the identity of the mysterious scribbler and where his remaining notes could be found?Talk to Samwise Gamgee in RivendellThe dwarves who carried with them the copy of The Best Defence which you found were scattered as they journeyed northward. It may be that some of the pages fell amidst the high crags of the Misty Mountains or perhaps were taken into Angmar by the Witch-king's minions.The Best Defence, page 2The Best Defence, page 3The Best Defence, page 5The Best Defence, page 9The Best Defence, page 12The Best Defence, page 16The Best Defence, page 17The Best Defence, page 19'Well, it's right kind of you to think so highly of me as to look me up to know more about that book you have there, but I must say I still feel rather overwhelmed by all this, and it's not much in my nature to be gallivanting about with a sword and all, if you know what I mean. Still, old Gandalf insisted that I take the time we have here in Rivendell to read up on such things and learn my duties and all before Master Frodo begins our... well, before we have to go. I guess I really shouldn't say anything more about that -- Samwise, you ninnyhammer, Gandalf will turn you into a toad for sure if you don't shape up....

'Anyways, Master Elrond suggested I read up on that book you have there -- he has a fine copy in his library, you know -- and it was an interesting read, sure enough, but that copy you've got there seems more interesting by half, as the fellow who took all those notes seems to have a fine handle on how it really goes when things heat up. Pity about the missing pages, it looks like the best parts are gone. If you manage to pull them together, I'd love to have a look at them, too... not that I'm like to have time for more reading what with us all getting ready to head off to....

'Well, never you mind that, you've got your own business to see to like as not, and don't need me bothering you with ours, I suppose.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

It is unclear who wrote this book as no author's name is given, but the clarity and wisdom with which it is written only adds further to the mystery of its apparently modest writer. The Book of Beasts speaks at great length of the bonds which may be formed between creatures of the wilds and those among the Free Peoples of Middle-earth who are willing to understand their own true natures. It further claims that the Wise might even learn the tongues of the wild creatures of the world and may coax them to greater feats of bravery and strength than would otherwise be possible for a beast lacking in its own will to strive and accomplish.

Whomever transcribed this particular copy, alas, does not appear to have been entirely in his right mind. Most of the text is clear, but some few of the pages are written in a hopeless scrawl with no meaning in any language of Elves or Men that you can discern. Indeed, it seems as if the scribe must have been taken with fits of madness during these passages. Perhaps Elrond of Rivendell might be able to tell you more about the mysterious author and help you untangle the riddle of the meaningless passages.Talk to Elrond at the Last Homely House in RivendellThe Book of Beasts, page 3The Book of Beasts, page 6The Book of Beasts, page 8The Book of Beasts, page 14The Book of Beasts, page 25The Book of Beasts, page 26The Book of Beasts, page 33The Book of Beasts, page 37'Greetings, ${PLAYER}, what can I help you with this day? The Book of Beasts is it? It has been some time since I have held a copy of this tome, and I had not thought to see another in the time remaining to me before I pass into the West. Though I do know the answer, I am afraid I cannot reveal to you the author of this book, as I must presume that he has chosen to remain anonymous for good reason, and I would not betray that faith without compelling need.

'As to the gibberish found in some of these passages, I can only imagine that the scribe who created this copy was not entirely in his right mind, as you yourself already doubtless suspected. I can find no meaning whatsoever in those passages, neither code, nor riddle, nor inspired prophecy.

'Regardless, the book has considerable value to a Lore-master such as yourself, and there is no reason for you to abandon the search for knowledge due to such a modest inconvenience. I have little doubt that you will in time be able to uncover these missing texts in other copies or pages of this book, though I am afraid I cannot tell you where to begin your search.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Book of Knives was written by a burglar of great renown -- she is even said to have retrieved the choicest gem of a hoard from beneath the grasping claw of a sleeping dragon -- but her true name remains unknown and many doubt the veracity of her most outlandish exploits. Nevertheless, she was acknowledged as one of the finest knife-fighters in Middle-earth during her time.

Sadly, The Book of Knives is incomplete; little can be gleaned from its cryptic writings. The famous burglar Bilbo Baggins is known for his love of riddles and mysteries -- perhaps he might be able to shed some light on this text.Talk to Bilbo Baggins at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.At least two copies of this book are believed to have been lost - one somewhere within Angmar and the other in the Misty Mountains.The Book of Knives, page 3The Book of Knives, page 5The Book of Knives, page 12The Book of Knives, page 17The Book of Knives, page 18The Book of Knives, page 23The Book of Knives, page 26The Book of Knives, page 31'What is that you have there? The Book of Knives? Oh my! I came into the profession quite by accident myself, but after my adventures on the Quest of Erebor, I decided it might be prudent to learn about those who were of a more professional sort, if you know what I mean.

'In my studies I came across mention of this book, but I have never held a copy of it before. This one seems to be missing several key pages -- I don't think we'll have much luck deciphering it without those.

'If you can find the remaining pages, I am sure this will prove quite a find. Unfortunately, I haven't any guidance to give you about where to look for the missing pages.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Candle's Flame is a stirring piece written by one of the great Captains of Gondor during the War of the Last Alliance. It speaks of the very personal connexion that a worthy captain forms with his followers, and the power that bond can have when a fallen warrior lies upon the threshold of Death's door. Few copies of this book exist today, most having been lost in the long years since or secreted away in the hidden libraries of the Lords of Gondor.

This copy of The Candle's Flame is missing many important pages. Rumour tells that Boromir, a Captain of Gondor, has come north. Perhaps he can help you comprehend the broken knowledge of this book.This copy of The Candle's Flame is missing many important pages. Rumour tells that Boromir, a Captain of Gondor, has come north. Perhaps he can help you comprehend the broken knowledge of this book.Talk to Boromir in RivendellThe missing pages of The Candle's Flame might be found in the Misty Mountains or Angmar.The Candle's Flame, page 4The Candle's Flame, page 7The Candle's Flame, page 13The Candle's Flame, page 16The Candle's Flame, page 25The Candle's Flame, page 26The Candle's Flame, page 31The Candle's Flame, page 35'What is this book you wish to speak of? By the Lords of the West! You have found a copy of The Candle's Flame here in the barren North? Let me see that....

'What a pity, it appears poorly worn and some of the key passages have been defaced. I had hoped you had found a complete copy -- the one in my father's library is a poorly edited sham of the original. Such a shame.

'Still, where the book has been found, so might the pages! Do not give up the search, there is still hope that you might find the missing tracts, and that would be a great boon to the Men of the North who seek the guidance and wisdom of our ancestors.'Chief Tuokki is worried that the Angmarim who command the Orcs will send them to fight the Earth-kin.Frith-vailë is in the north-east part of Ram Dúath.

Chief Tuokki, hoping to discourage the Orcs and the Angmarim, has asked you to defeat the Captain of the Angmarim in Ram Dúath.Chief Tuokki is at the Suuri-lehmä camp in Ram Dúath.

Now that you've defeated the Iron-crown Captain, return to the Earth-kin Chief and tell him of your deed.'For one so small, you are very good at killing Orcs. The Orcs are many, but we think they run through Ram Dúath because Men tell them to go. Evil Men from the North, from Angmar, have come to Ram Dúath. 

'One of the Men is the leader. We think he will send the Orcs against us soon. We cannot fight them in their camp, they are too strong, but you are strong too. Maybe you can help us fight this Man, this Captain from Angmar.

'The Angmarim have a camp north and east of here called Frith-vailë.''The captain of the Angmarim is dead? Good! Maybe the Angmarim will send another leader, but for now we are safe!''The Orcs may not come if you kill the leader of the Angmarim.'Chief Crannog has sent you to Lore-singer Arzhur to learn something of the Free Folk of Angmar.Arzhur is in Aughaire, north-east of Chief Crannog.

Chief Crannog has sent you to speak to the Lore-singer Arzhur to hear about the history of his clan.'So, another Southron comes to trouble us. Well, we are not like those who crawl before the Iron Crown, and we still hallow the guest-law. So... be welcome.

'You will not find much love amongst us for your kind, stranger. We listened to the Southron Golodir and stood against the Iron Crown, but now that he has abandoned us, we are on our last leg. The other clans have joined against us, and only the last frail vestiges of the Treaty of the Hills protects us from war. Soon enough, though, we will fall, one way or the other.

'Oh, you wish to hear more of our troubles? I have no breath to repeat the tale of woe. Ask the Lore-singer, Arzhur, if you must know the long, sad story. He is near the tents to the north.''Speak to Arzhur. I have not the breath to waste.''Ah, another stranger. Crannog has sent you, eh? Listen well, then. Some years past, when the evil men of Angmar were busily corrupting and subjugating the men of the hills, a Southron, Golodir by name, came to us. He was a noble man and a strong warrior, and we could tell he spoke truth in all things.

'Golodir warned us not to follow the Iron Crown and showed us the evil secrets of Angmar. He led us to confront our brothers in the other clans and to expel the Angmarim emissaries who whispered in the ears of our elders.

'But then Golodir disappeared. Some say he was slain, others say he has returned to the South, abandoning us to our fate. Now most of the men of Aughaire still stand against Angmar and their slave-clans, but we have lost hope for the future.'Ravaedron has finished the painting for Maddoc and Glenys Stagshorn, and he is excited to give it to them.Ravaedron has asked to meet you at Fernbrake Cot, where he will give the painting to his friends.Meet up with Ravaedron outside Fernbrake CotMaddoc Stagshorn stands outside his home, contemplating the gift.Talk to Maddoc Stagshorn outside his homeRavaedron is at Fernbrake Cot, beaming with happiness.Talk to Ravaedron at Fernbrake CotRavaedron encountered the Ranger Idhrien on his way to Fernbrake Cot, and he thinks you should aid her if you can.Defeat beasts of the Angle near Fernbrake Cot and near Glynafon (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Meet up with the Ranger Idhrien outside GlynafonThe Ranger Idhrien suggested that you could help her gather some of the wild herbs she needs for making poultices and other healing remedies for the Dúnedain.Gather wild herbs for Idhrien (${NUMBER}/5)The Ranger Idhrien suggested that you could help her gather some of the wild herbs she needs for making poultices and other healing remedies for the Dúnedain.Gather wild herbs for Idhrien (4/5)Gathering the herbs has brought you quite near to Ravaedron. He will be pleased to bring the fruit of your labours to Idhrien.Ravaedron has seen something by the side of the cottage that has quite disturbed him.Discover what has upset RavaedronRavaedron stands by the cottage of his friends, hurt by their dismissal of his gift.Talk to Ravaedron by Fernbrake Cot'Yes, this is sure to banish the memory of our quarrel. Furthermore, with the word of the Dúnedain that they will continue to make safe these lands, I can depart for the Grey Havens with an unclouded heart. But first, I must deliver my gift! I will meet you at Fernbrake Cot!''Yes, I will be able to depart for the Grey Havens with an unclouded heart. But first, I must deliver my gift! I will meet you at Fernbrake Cot!''Yes, this is... quite lovely. I have given Ravaedron our thanks for it.'

Maddoc looks at you from the corner of his eye, but he says nothing.'I hope you will forgive my not waiting for you, ${PLAYER}, but I was quite eager to present my gift to them.

'In fact, I was in such a hurry that I spared only the very briefest of greetings for the Ranger Idhrien, who I passed on the way here. She was collecting herbs for the Dúnedain, and I promised her I would help her with that task once my own was finished. Could I ask you to find her and apologize for my haste, and perhaps to defeat any dangerous beasts that you might find nearby?''Could I ask you to find the Ranger Idhrien and apologize for my haste, and perhaps to defeat any dangerous beasts that you might find nearby?''Greetings to you, ${PLAYER}. Ravaedron sent you to help me? He has a great deal on his mind, and he need not ask for any forgiveness on my part! But I would tell him this, if he took the time to hear my words: "It is not on your head to solve every problem in Middle-earth! Leave the land as best you can, and treat those who remain with as much kindness as you have to give. If you do that, you will sail into the West with an untroubled heart, deserving of the peace you have earned!"

'That is what I believe, anyway. Perhaps the Dúnedain are in no position to say such things, for our duty seems never-ending, as if it will never reach its conclusion! But I think these words will help Ravaedron, though I find them hard to follow for myself.

'But if you will help me gather the herbs I need, my work today will go twice as quickly! Keep an eye out for them after you have concluded your current business. I do not want you to run afoul of any beasts while your eyes are locked on the ground!'

Idhrien describes the particular grass-like tufts for which she is searching, and tells you she has not yet looked to the east.'I have not yet looked to the east, ${PLAYER}, if you wouldn't mind searching in that direction.''Oh, those are the herbs Idhrien needs? I was collecting entirely the wrong sort. Well, now that I know the ones she seeks, I shall have more... more...'

Ravaedron trails off, staring to the east.Ravaedron is silent, staring to the east.'I... I don't understand, ${PLAYER}. Why have the Stagshorns discarded my painting? Did they not like the gift I made for them? This is... this is very hurtful! Was I upset by our quarrel? This stings tenfold that, and makes me question our friendship entire! Do they not understand that I must leave this land, but despite my certainty of that choice, the leaving is still difficult?

'But... how could they know that? They have not had the experiences of the Eldar, or faced such things as I have. Their sorrows may be shorter-lived than my own, but... they are no less deeply-felt.

'I think I have realized something, ${PLAYER}. My gift was well-intentioned, but it did nothing to address the permanence of my choice, or to soothe the wound it could cause. I sought an easy remedy, but no such remedy exists, or ever has.'Along the road leading south from the Trollshaws into the Angle of Mitheithel, you encountered an Elf who desired your assistance.Dangerous foes stalk the rocky woodlands of the Angle, and Ravaedron desires for you to help him combat them.Defeat threatening foes in the Angle of Mitheithel (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ravaedron is along the road that runs south into the Angle from the Trollshaws.Talk to Ravaedron along the road from the TrollshawsFernbrake Cot, the home of Ravaedron's friends, lies to the south-east, beyond the village of Glynafon in the Angle of Mitheithel.Find Fernbrake Cot, the home of Ravaedron's friends, beyond the village of Glynafon to the south-eastRavaedron stands outside Fernbrake Cot, the home of his friends, south-east of the old village of Glynafon.Talk to Ravaedron outside Fernbrake Cot'Mae govannen, stranger. You are come to the Angle of Mitheithel, but in a southerly direction rather than a northerly. That is curious indeed! I have found that travellers in this land do so mainly on their way to somewhere else; so it is with me. I am not long to remain here in the Angle.'

The Elf smiles briefly.

'But I have forgotten my manners! Let me cure this lack at once: I am called Ravaedron, and I came to the Angle some time ago on an errand given me by the sons of Elrond. Ah, you are known to them? Then let us be friends as well, for we share similar purpose: to make the wilds safe for those good folk who dwell there! It will be no great burden for you to aid me in this, will it? For there is something I must do, but I have been putting it off for later. Let us first face what dangers present themselves to us here in Mitheithel, and then perhaps you will help me again.''Let us work to make safe the wilds for those good folk who dwell in this land! Perhaps afterward you will help me with the task at which I have been delaying.''Very good, my new friend. You are most adept at facing such threats as these! It is little wonder that Elladan and Elrohir trust you so. Did they send you on such a mission as they sent me? I was directed here in search of dark servants of the Enemy, riders in black that ran afoul of the defences of Imladris. Ah... perhaps I should speak no more of them. Elrohir was most clear that it was a matter of some secrecy.'

Ravaedron mimes locking his lips with a key and hanging it on an invisible chain around his neck.

'Let us say instead that I found no sign of our quarry, and leave it at that. Even so my search was not fruitless, for I did find something that is now quite dear to me. I befriended a family of woodcutters, the Stagshorns. They live not far from here, and now I cherish their friendship, the last friendship I will ever make in Middle-earth, for my steps will now take me to the Grey Havens, and I will sail into the West, and this chapter of mine will be ended.'

Ravaedron laughs suddenly.

'Perhaps the Stagshorns will not be the last friendship I make here, ${PLAYER}, for I had not counted upon meeting you! But if you will accompany me to their cottage and help me say good-bye, I would value your assistance most highly... for I have been putting it off. They live in a pleasant cottage to the south-east, beyond the old village of Glynafon.''If you will accompany me to the Stagshorn's cottage and help me say good-bye, I would value your assistance most highly, for I have been putting it off. They live in a pleasant cottage to the south-east, beyond the old village of Glynafon.''This is it: Fernbrake Cot, home of my friends the Stagshorns.''Here it is: Fernbrake Cot, home to my friends Maddoc and Glenys, and their son Bran. The Stagshorns showed me much kindness while I ventured in this land, and I will miss them when I leave.

'I appreciate you being here, ${PLAYER}. Saying good-bye is always difficult, and I would like my friends to know they will have adventurers such as you looking after them once I have departed.'Halmur Stoneshaper, a descendant of the original Halmur Stoneshaper, son of Vithurr, has been charged with guarding the strongbox you seek until all its contents are returned.The stolen ingot might be found within the walls of Gabilazan.

Halmur Stoneshaper suggested you seek the stolen ingot there.Find Hjortur DourhandThe stolen ingot might be inside Hjortur's strongbox, within the walls of Gabilazan.

You defeated Hjortur Dourhand and should now search the strongbox he guarded for the stolen ingot.Search Hjortur's strongbox for the stolen ingotHalmur Stoneshaper is in the tomb of his ancestor on the hill to the left as you approach the dwarf-keep in the pass of Gabilazan from the north.

Halmur Stoneshaper is waiting for you to return with the stolen dwarf-gold ingot you retrieved from the descendant of the thief who stole it long ages ago.The second strongbox is within the tomb of Halmur Stoneshaper in the pass of Gabilazan. Larus Sharpshard is located in a camp north of High Crag.

After helping Halmur Stoneshaper reclaim the stolen ingot and fulfil his family's oath, he has allowed you to take the strongbox to bring it to Larus Sharpshard.'I see... so you were sent by a dwarf from the Blue Mountains, were you? Perhaps it is the long years alone here, but I believe you. Now, allow me to tell you a tale.

'Long ago, the first Halmur Stoneshaper, son of Vithurr, was charged with guarding the treasures here. He stood sentry day and night, maintaining his post even on days of feasting. Yet he let his guard drop, only once, and a vile dwarf, Hjortur Dourhand, slipped in and stole a single ingot of dwarf-gold. Having failed in his duty, Halmur swore that he and his sons would keep vigil over the remaining gold until that ingot was returned. I am but one in a long line of Halmurs who have kept his oath.

'If you return the stolen ingot, the oath will be fulfilled. The strongbox will then be yours to return to the dwarf who sent you. I cannot say, though, where to find Hjortur, although the legends of Halmur's failure tells that Hjortur is somewhere within Gabilazan. Perhaps there you will be able to find the gold.''I know not where you might find the gold Hjortur stole, although the tale of Halmur's failure says Hjortur is within the walls of Gabilazan. Perhaps you will find some clue to the stolen ingot there.''Ha! You've come from one of those fool Stoneshapers, haven't you? You've heard the tale of their "oath" over that strongbox, haven't you? Well, allow me to tell you a tale!

'The first Hjortur Dourhand stole the gold not for his own treasure, but to make Halmur Stoneshaper look the fool! Upon hearing of Halmur's oath, Hjortur swore one of his own: he and his sons would forever keep the ingot and forever keep the Stoneshapers looking the fool!

'I too come from a long line... a long line of Hjortur Dourhands! And I shall keep to the oath! You shall only have the ingot if you can take the strongbox over my dead body. And I don't think that's too likely....''You defeated Hjortur, but did not search his strongbox? Return to the spot you defeated him at once and reclaim the stolen ingot from the strongbox he guarded!''My eyes must betray me, you ${RACE}, you trickster. You cannot have the stolen ingot that has kept me and my kin chained to this place for the long years.

'And yet, it is... it is! Can it be so? Has my duty at last been fulfilled?''The chest is yours, friend. My oath is fulfilled. I'm free now. Free to go where I please!''Good, good! I hope the strongbox wasn't too hard to find! Heikki Goldwatcher is sure to be pleased with this. Those records of his won't have a bad thing to say about me!

'Now, I know you've already done so much, but perhaps there is a bit more I could ask of you?'Ravaedron has had another idea, and once again he needs your help.Determined to make things right, Ravaedron needs your help once again.Talk to Ravaedron and help the chagrined artist attempt to make things rightRavaedron stands outside Fernbrake Cot.Talk to Ravaedron outside Fernbrake CotMaddoc Stagshorn has invited you inside to see where Ravaedron's new painting will be displayed.Enter Fernbrake Cot and see where the painting will be displayedMaddoc Stagshorn wishes to speak with you inside his cottage.Talk to Maddoc Stagshorn inside Fernbrake Cot'I have had another idea, ${PLAYER}, and I need your help.''I want to make things right. Let us see if the Rangers will help us once again.''I thank you once again, ${PLAYER}, for helping me to learn what I might have learned long ago: as happiness is a part of the world, so too is sadness. Seeking to avoid it can delay the pain, but it does not destroy it. There should be no shame in tears.

'I will travel to the Grey Havens with my own sadness, and I will bear it over the Sea. But so too will I bring my memories of the happy times. I will not weigh one against the other, but carry both. And in years to come, perhaps Maddoc and Glenys and young Bran will look upon this second painting, and there will be days they wish to cry, and there will be days they think to smile. It may have been among the last friendships I made in this Middle-earth, ${PLAYER}, but it will be a lasting one. And so too will mine with you!'

Ravaedron laughs, a brighter and more contented sound than you have yet heard from the Elf.

'The Stagshorns have invited you inside to see where they will set the painting, my friend. And now I say good-bye.''The Stagshorns have invited you inside to see where they will set the painting, my friend. And now I say good-bye.''I always believed Elves were supposed to be wiser than the rest of us, ${PLAYER}, but then my wife and I met Ravaedron! He is dear to us and we will cherish the time we spent with him, but I tell you truly that sometimes he could be more maddening than our Bran was when he was just a baby!

'Folk are complicated, I suppose, and in that at least the Elves are the same as the rest of us. Thank you for helping the three of us, ${PLAYER}. You are welcome at Fernbrake Cot any time.'Larus Sharpshard discovered that there was a second strongbox, which remains missing.Larus Sharpshard believes the second strongbox is inside a building on the hill to your left as you approach the Dourhand-controlled keep in the pass of Gabilazan from the north. Gabilazan is south of the camp that Larus is located.

Larus Sharpshard is very worried that Heikki Goldwatcher will record unflattering things about him if you are not able to recover a second dwarf-strongbox from the Misty Mountains.Collect the second strongbox'How could I have missed this? There was a second page to Heikki's records! It describes a second strongbox, ${PLAYER}! Heikki is not the kind of dwarf you want to cross, because he'll remember it -- he'll write it down in one of his books and never let you forget how you let him down! If I don't get that second strongbox, it wouldn't surprise me if someone found his records four hundred years from now and thought badly of Larus Sharpshard, son of Tralli!

'So we've got to find it, ${PLAYER}. Let me just look at this paper and tell you where it should be. Ah, the dwarf-keep among the High Crags, to the south. It's the keep that guards Gabilazan, and it's full of those good-for-nothing Dourhands.

'You might want to take the farthest west pass into High Crag. Look for a building on a hill to your left as you approach the keep. The strongbox should be just inside, so don't delay!''The second strongbox will be inside a building on a hill to your left as you approach the Dourhand keep in the pass of Gabilazan from the north. Journey there and bring back this strongbox, ${PLAYER}!''Who dares to enter the tomb of Halmur Stoneshaper, son of Vithurr of old? Not even a Dourhand is foolish enough to enter here! They know the tale of the Stoneshapers and quake in fear! Yet you are different....

'You are here for the strongbox, aren't you, thieving ${RACE}? You've only moments to tell me your tale before I cleave you in twain!'You have recovered the four morgul-hilts from the wraiths in the ruins of Fornost. Istuienn has asked for your help in finding a way to destroy them.Elrond can be found at Rivendell in the Trollshaws to the south and east of the North Downs.

Istuienn told you to take the morgul-hilts to Elrond Halfelven to be destroyed.Hemeldir can be found at the Forges of Rivendell outside the Halls of Elrond.

Elrond told you to take the morgul-hilts to his chief smith, Hemeldir.'So, you say you have defeated the wraiths? I fear that they were not wholly destroyed, for they are bound to this world by the spells upon those hilts.

'The malice of their spirits still remains, and they will return in time. I have read all I can from the morgul-hilts. They are twisted and black, but they bear strange devices for works of the Enemy. I would ask you to bear the hilts as quickly as may be to the vale of Rivendell to the house of Elrond Halfelven... he will know how best to deal with them.

'Rivendell lies deep in the Trollshaws, far to the south and east. Destroying the hilts should break the spell that binds their unseen sinews to this world. I caution you again: do not handle the hilts, but carry them as swiftly as possible to Rivendell.''Take these hilts at once to Master Elrond in Rivendell... he will know what to do with them.''These are old hilts and well-crafted. Their original make appears to have been by a craftsman of Eregion, for they bear many of the markings and style of their smith-work, but that craft has been twisted and corrupted into their current form.

'The morgul-runes upon the hilts bear the oaths of their owners. It is a dark oath that I will not have spoken aloud in my halls. Because of their craft, I do believe that they can be unmade... bring them to Hemeldir at the Forges of Rivendell.''Hemeldir, my chief smith, will destroy these evil things... take them to him at once.''I can unmake the morgul-hilts. It is strange that their craftsmanship is similar to that of the Elven smiths of old, though they bear marks of great pain and malice. These hilts cast a shadow upon my heart. I fear a greater evil in their making than even Master Elrond may have surmised.

'Their destruction should indeed break the spells that bind them to this world.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Written in a neatly defined Sindarin script, The Tome of Swords has long been renowned as one of the finest manuals on the art of swordsmanship and battle. Originally written in the early ages of Middle-earth, many copies exist -- but sometime shortly after the end of the Second Age, most of them either vanished or had important passages edited, meaning that most of the copies that exist today are incomplete.

The copy you have found appears to be mostly intact, but time and wear have done the duty of whomever wished to limit the knowledge contained therein. There are a few pages that are either missing or too badly weathered to read. Perhaps the dwarf Gimli, son of Glóin of the Lonely Mountain, could tell you more about this ancient tome.The copy you have found appears to be mostly intact - but time and wear have done the duty of whomever wished to limit the knowledge contained herein. There are a few pages that are either missing or too badly weathered to read. Perhaps the dwarf-champion Gimli, son of Glóin of the Lonely Mountain, could tell you more about this ancient tome.Talk to Gimli in RivendellSince the Elves collected so many copies of The Tome of Swords, it may be that some loose pages may be found in the Misty Mountains. Others may have been taken by the Enemy into the Witch-king's realm of Angmar.The Tome of Swords, page 1The Tome of Swords, page 4The Tome of Swords, page 7The Tome of Swords, page 12The Tome of Swords, page 13The Tome of Swords, page 17The Tome of Swords, page 21The Tome of Swords, page 25'Welcome and well met, ${PLAYER}. What may I help you with this fine day? A book you say? Give it here then. Ah yes, I have seen its like before: The Tome of Swords. Written by Elf-lords ages ago and still one of the finest texts a champion could wish to own. But look you here, this copy of yours is quite a bit older than any I have held, and some of the passages are different. It seems that you have stumbled upon something of great import.

'After the wars of the Second Age saw their power begin to wane, the Elves thought perhaps it was not wise to leave such fine works in the art of war lying about where any Man, dwarf, or Orc could pick them up, so they rounded up most of the copies of this book and removed some of the more important bits. Most copies you see today are from the time after that, and while they are still fine works, it is safe to say that the best parts are hard to come by.

'Now this copy of yours looks old enough to be from before that time, but it is pretty weathered and dog-eared. If you could find the missing pages, however, you would have yourself a complete copy of the original Tome of Swords, and in the hands of a seasoned champion, I can tell you that it would be worth more than its weight in gold.The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Gondolin of old was home to the most legendary Marchwardens of the Elder Days. This tome is an account told by a scribe of those brave Elf-warriors during the long wars against Morgoth. Much of it is a heroic epic, told in linnod-verse, describing multiple forays from the great city into the realms of the Enemy. As with all tales of those times, however, it soon becomes a tragedy; one by one the Wardens were lost, many slain, and some were taken and broken in the dungeons of Morgoth. Many of the tactics passed down to the Wardens of today began with this book, which ends suddenly with the tragic fall of the great city.Aragorn, at the House of Elrond in Rivendell, is known for his great store of tales of Elves and of Men, and he has oft sung in sorrowful Elvish tales of the Marchwardens of the Elder Days. This book should be shown to him if any meaning is to be gleaned from it.Talk to Aragorn in RivendellThe Watch Against the Night, page 5The Watch Against the Night, page 7The Watch Against the Night, page 13The Watch Against the Night, page 17The Watch Against the Night, page 21The Watch Against the Night, page 25The Watch Against the Night, page 41The Watch Against the Night, page 44'Where did you find this? Surely this must be a false copy of some sort...but nay, I see that it is not even as I turn the page. I know too many of these verses, for Elrond spoke the words to me when I was a child in Rivendell, for he held much of this in his memory.

'You must understand, friend, the importance of this volume. We have long thought that 'The Watch Against the Night' was as lost as the city of Gondolin itself. To have found it again just as the days darken is a grace I never expected, a blessing unguessed.

'It gives me pain to see this treasure with so many pages missing. Many accounts are missing in full, or are only partially told with their next pages all fallen out. To see this book restored would be a great thing indeed. If you happen upon any of its pages in your travels, pray bring them here as well. I would see them restored to their proper home.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Thunder and Flame was scribed by a Rune-keeper of ancient Khazad-dûm, known to have been an advisor to King Durin himself. It is said that this ancient tome held many secrets of the Angerthas, passed down from the earliest days in the First Age. It speaks at length of the subtleties of the Cirth script, and how to draw forth the powers of the elements: of fire and storm and winter's chill, for use against one's enemies. 

In the end, however, even this vastly complex might was not enough to sate the greed of the dwarves, nor prevent the destruction wrought upon Moria by the awakening of the ancient evil that slumbered in the deep.Talk to Elrond Half-elven at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.At least two copies of this book are believed to have been lost - one somewhere within Angmar and the other in the Misty-mountains.Thunder and Flame, page 3Thunder and Flame, page 8Thunder and Flame, page 12Thunder and Flame, page 19Thunder and Flame, page 23Thunder and Flame, page 26Thunder and Flame, page 34Thunder and Flame, page 42'In the name of Gil-galad, can it be? The lost book of Thunder and Flame.... Long have we thought it lost, when the darkness fell upon the Dwarrowdelf of old, now called Moria.

'Alas that it is incomplete! To have found it after so many long years but not to have the whole of its text is a grievous fate. The pages that contained its deepest mysteries are lost.

'Worry not, though; the archives of the Eldar are the richer for having this book. But the full wisdom of Durin's Folk, and the knowledge that was lost when they were driven from their homes, would be a treasure as great as the mithril they left behind.'Ravaedron has come to Tornhad for two purposes: to alert the Rangers about the danger of a large warg-pack that has descended upon the Angle, and to ask them to safe-guard his friends at Fernbrake Cot.The Ranger Himeinior stands to the west of the entrance of Tornhad.Talk to Himeinior to the west, in TornhadThe Ranger Himeinior gave you a bag of rations to distribute throughout the encampment, asking you to fill up the provision crates found throughout Tornhad.Distribute rations among the provision crates in Tornhad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Ranger Himeinior gave you a bag of rations to distribute throughout the encampment, asking you to fill up the provision crates found throughout Tornhad.Ravaedron is speaking with the Rangers within the bounds of Tornhad.Talk to Ravaedron in TornhadRanger Idhrenfair has caught your eye; he surely has a task of some kind for you to complete.Talk to Idhrenfair in TornhadThe Ranger Idhrenfair gave you a number of swords that are in need of repair, and instructed you to leave them in a barrel near the forge for that purpose. The forge is to the south-west.Bring the swords awaiting repair to a barrel by the forge in Tornhad, to the south-westThe Ranger Prestadír has a number of questions for you, and likely a task for you to perform as well.Talk to Prestadír by the forges in TornhadThe Ranger Prestadír has asked you to conduct a patrol of the outskirts of Tornhad while he finishes up his work at the forge and speaks with Ravaedron.Ravaedron is in Tornhad, speaking with a number of the Rangers who dwell there.Talk to Ravaedron in Tornhad'Now let us find some of my friends among the Dúnedain. We will tell them about the warg-pack that threatens the Angle, and I also wish for them to keep watch upon the Stagshorns when I have gone.

'Ah, there is Himeinior. Let us speak with him first.''We will tell my friends among the Dúnedain about the warg-pack that threatens the Angle, and I also wish for them to keep watch upon the Stagshorns when I have gone. Let us speak with Himeinior first.''Ravaedron has come to visit us, has he? Very nice, but he has chosen a busy time to call upon Tornhad, for some of us are preparing to depart on a mission of secrecy and some urgency. We may not have much time to spend in conversation with our artist friend.

'What did you say your name was? ${PLAYER}? It is a pleasure to meet you. If you are willing to distribute these rations among the provision crates throughout Tornhad, I will even try to remember your name in the future! Ah, I speak in jest, ${PLAYER}... but I would appreciate it.''If you distribute these rations among the provision crates here in Tornhad, I will speak briefly with Ravaedron.''I have just begun to tell the Dúnedain the reasons for our visit here, ${PLAYER}, but they insist they have too many tasks to which they must attend, and have little time to listen! I have never found them to be especially polite, but on this day it seems worse than usual.

'What say you, Idhrenfair? I believed us to be friends, but it seems you cannot spare a moment for Ravaedron, though he departs on the morrow and you will not see him again!''You wound me, Idhrenfair, truly!''I have known Ravaedron my whole life, and that means I am familiar with his old tricks: he has always had a flair for the dramatic quite unlike others of his people I have met.

'But very well. If you will do my own task for me, and bring these swords to the forge for repair, I will humour Ravaedron this once. And if this be for the last time, I will count myself fortunate!''If you will do my own task for me, and bring these swords to the forge for repair, I will humour Ravaedron this once. And if this be for the last time, I will count myself fortunate!''You are a friend of Ravaedron? ${PLAYER}? It is a pleasure to meet you. May I ask what brings you and he to Tornhad at this hour?'

You tell the Ranger that Ravaedron is saying farewell to the places he knows before journeying to the Grey Havens, where he will board a ship and leave Middle-earth.

'I see. That is his choice, and it is not a choice that is available to all of us. Can I ask you to do something for me, my new friend? While I finish up my work here at the forge and speak with Ravaedron, will you patrol the bounds of Tornhad? We are preparing our own departure, though our destination lies to the east and not the west, as his does, and if fortune is with us ours will not prove as permanent a stay as Ravaedron's.''Welcome to Tornhad, ${CLASS}. All is quiet at the ford, for the moment.'Calithil nods at you, but says nothing.'Can I ask you to patrol the bounds of Tornhad? We are preparing our own departure, though our destination lies to the east and not the west, as his does, and if fortune is with us ours will not prove as permanent as Ravaedron's.'Calithil nods at you, but says nothing.'You're back, ${PLAYER}?

'I have told the Dúnedain about the warg pack and spoken of my hope that the Rangers will watch over the Stagshorns after my departure, but they have not agreed to do so! I admit this is not the news for which I had hoped when we came to Tornhad.'The woodworkers of Bree pride themselves on their craftsmanship.Maggie Smallwood is at the Bree Hunting Lodge, west of Bree's South-gate.

Maggie Smallwood has asked you to craft viols for the woodworkers of Bree.'Have you stopped in the streets of Bree and listened, really listened, ${PLAYER}? The sound of a minstrel's instrument can usually be heard throughout Bree, though it is becoming rarer by the day.

'You see, the usual fair for the woodworkers of Bree besides hunting bows and farmcraft was wooden instruments. Clarinets, harps, and viols...ah, the beautiful viols! Those are one of the harder instruments that our woodworkers craft here in Bree. Well, what with all the commotion with the brigands hereabouts, the woodworkers have been hard-pressed by the demand for all manner of spears, bows, and staves, and they haven't the time to craft new viols!

'If you would, find five viol and return them here to me. It would be for the worst if the streets were left without music.''Ah, these are finely-crafted viols. Listen to the soft, sweet sound!

'You have done a great service to Bree, ${PLAYER}.'Defeat Wargs in Angmar.Defeat Wargs in AngmarDefeat many Wargs in Angmar.Defeat Wargs in AngmarVighar Roadwalker, a dwarf at the High Crag camp, wants to post signs warning travellers away from dangerous locations in the mountains.The pass of Gabilazan is south-west of the High Crag camp atop a slope.

Vighar Roadwalker has given you a sign to post just outside the northern entrance to the pass of Gabilazan. Evil dwarves have occupied the keep that guards the pass.Vighar Roadwalker is at the High Crag camp, north-east of Gabilazan.

Vighar will be pleased to hear that you have posted the sign he gave you in front of the pass of Gabilazan, guarded by Dourhands.'It's my job to keep these passes safe for travellers, ${PLAYER}, but especially for the trade my kin wants to reestablish through these mountains. And there are threats everywhere!

'While you were away, I prepared another sign. This one warns travellers away from the good-for-nothing, oath-breaking, false-speaking, thin-bearded Dourhands that have taken over the keep in the pass of Gabilazan. That wouldn't all fit on the sign, so I shortened it to "Beware of Evil Dwarves."

'If you don't mind helping me out again, I'd ask you to post this sign outside the northern entrance to their keep. Head south-west from here. A slope rises up to Gabilazan and the Dourhand keep. There should be a suitable post you can affix the sign to.''Evil dwarves have occupied the keep that guards the pass of Gabilazan, and you need to post that sign before the northern entrance of the pass to warn travellers of the danger.''You have returned, ${PLAYER}, and I know I need not ask if you posted the sign I gave you, for you are a trustworthy ${RACE} indeed!

'I have fashioned another sign for you to post! I see from your eye that you expected it and are eager to assist again!'Ravaedron wants to make things right with Maddoc Stagshorn and his family.Threatening beasts and other foes stalk the wilderness of Mitheithel, and Ravaedron wants you to thin their numbers for the sake of his friends.Defeat any foes in the Angle that might threaten the safety of Ravaedron's friends (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ravaedron asked to meet you outside Glynafon, the ancestral home of the Stôrs, when you finished with your task.Consumed by his memories of a bustling Glynafon, Ravaedron has asked you to look upon the places he remembers.Find the home of Meredud and AderynFind the home of WennaYou have found warg tracks in Glynafon, an unwelcome but unsurprising discovery.Examine the warg tracks in GlynafonAccording to the tracks you discovered, a large number of wargs passed through Glynafon in recent days.Talk to Ravaedron in GlynafonRavaedron has asked you to defeat any wargs you may find in the Angle, the better to ensure the safety of his friends Maddoc and Glenys Stagshorn, and that of travellers in Mitheithel.Defeat wargs or wolves in Glynafon or elsewhere in the Angle (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ravaedron has suggested that the two of you meet up at Tornhad, the Ranger settlement in Mitheithel, to alert them to the danger posed by the wargs.Meet up with Ravaedron at Tornhad, the Ranger settlement on the banks of the Hoarwell, to the westRavaedron has come to Tornhad, the Ranger settlement in the Angle.Talk to Ravaedron at Tornhad'Well, it is no matter: whether they know of it or not, their lives will not be endangered by my decision to leave Middle-earth. I hope it is not presumptuous of me to say we will make certain of that!'

Ravaedron stares for a moment longer at the closed door, and then shakes his head.

'Still, I had hoped for a friendlier parting. I have seen many farewells during my long years, and I did not envision this one going this way. But I will not dwell upon it. I have spent too long dwelling on such things! Let us make safe the lands hereabout and meet up at the old mounds of Glynafon. It stands to the west of here and was once a most pleasant village, though it has remained empty for much of the age.''Let us make safe the lands hereabout and meet up at the old mounds of Glynafon. It stands to the west of here and was once a most pleasant village, though it has remained empty for much of the age.''Let us make safe the lands hereabout and meet up at the old mounds of Glynafon. It stands to the west of here and was once a most pleasant village, though it has remained empty for much of the age.''Sights such as this are all too common these days, ${PLAYER}, and they stand in opposition with the memories I have of these places. This was once a thriving community of hardy folk toughened by the tilling of the rocky soil. I remember the pleasing sound of their voices, raised in song more often than not, and carried on the wind for miles around. They called themselves Stôrs, and it is my understanding that when they departed the Angle, they passed through many lands before finally settling in a small corner of Arthedain. I hope to see their land on my journey to the ships.

'But they have not lived here for more than a thousand years, and the wind has not borne aloft their songs for even longer, for it was the threat of war that caused them to leave. There was unrest throughout Rhudaur, and evil arose in Angmar, and the Stôrs felt no cause to sing. But when I knew them they were pleasant and full of joy.'

Ravaedron regales you with tales of the friends he once knew in Glynafon, and encourages you to see the home of Meredud and Aderyn, who delighted in staying up late, and the burrow where Wenna painted such lovely landscapes that she seemed to possess the skill of the Eldar.'You should see the home of Meredud and Aderyn, who delighted in staying up late, and the burrow where Wenna painted such lovely landscapes! Ah, she seemed to possess the skill of the Eldar.''You should inspect those tracks, my friend. There are wargs here... but I fear their numbers may have increased.''I did not expect to see friendly folk in Glynafon, and my heart was ready for their absence, but the sight of wargs among the one-time burrows of my friends is more than I can bear. How many evenings did I spend alongside Meredud and Aderyn on their nightly strolls? How many days did I spend in conversation with Wenna, who gave me such lessons in painting that my art was ever-changed? Too few, I see now.'

Ravaedron frowns, his pleasant memories scattered like leaves before a storm.

'If I am grateful for anything, let it be this: I did not quarrel with my friends among the Stôrs when they departed these lands. That, at least, is a kindness.

'Let us defeat some of the wargs here in the Angle. If a large pack of the creatures passed through Glynafon, as you say, they will be a danger to those who live nearby. Then we will continue on to Tornhad, the Dúnedain village that is our destination. I have a request to make of the Rangers there, and we can tell them about the warg pack.''Let us defeat some of the wargs here in the Angle. If a large pack of the creatures passed through Glynafon, as you say, they will be a danger to those who live nearby. Then we will continue on to Tornhad, the Dúnedain village that is our destination. I have a request to make of the Rangers there, and we can tell them about the warg pack.''Let us defeat some of the wargs here in the Angle. If a large pack of the creatures passed through Glynafon, as you say, they will be a danger to those who live nearby. Then we will continue on to Tornhad, the Dúnedain village that is our destination. I have a request to make of the Rangers there, and we can tell them about the warg pack.''We have arrived.''We have arrived, ${PLAYER}. The Dúnedain range far and wide throughout the wilderness, but Tornhad has served as their relatively permanent home for many years.'The Earth-kin Scout has been attacked by strange folk who have come into Ram Dúath from the North. He told you the women with wings are in league with the Angmarim.The bat-winged creatures who injured the Earth-kin scout are in the eastern gorges of Ram Dúath.

The Earth-kin scout's request was for you to kill the vicious merrevail so that others will not be injured or worse.Defeat merrevail (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Earth-kin Scout is hidden in a valley east of Duvairë in the Ram Dúath.

You should return to the Earth-kin Scout and tell him of your success against the merrevail.'Another thing there is now in Ram Dúath stranger than Men or Orcs, maybe worse than either of them. Evil people like women, but with wings! They are vicious, deadly creatures, while I was scouting the Orcs they attacked me.

'You must destroy these bat-women, they are fierce and kill without mercy.  Others will come to look for me, they must not hurt more of the Jorthkyn.

'I am afraid of these foul creatures. Such things do not belong in the world.''They are cruel creatures, these bat-women; it is good that you have thinned their numbers.  My wound is quickly healing, soon I will return and warn the Suuri-lehmä.''You must hurry, I do not know when my people will come in search of me.  Slay the bat-women, protect Jorthkyn.'The woodworkers of Bree pride themselves in their craftsmanship.Maggie Smallwood is at the Bree Hunting Lodge, west of Bree's South-gate.

Maggie Smallwood has asked you to find wooden figurines to sell to travelling merchants.'Welcome, traveller. I would ask what brings you to Bree, but so many strangers have been arriving from the south and east that it no longer matters what they come for.

'The Men of Bree are artful woodworkers. Our crafts are carried and traded across Eriador.  Just last season I had word from a dwarf that he had received one of our wooden figurines as a gift from a travelling merchant.

'That got me to thinking; why not sell these figurines as a novelty for travelling merchants to bring back as petty gifts? The woodworkers of Bree are worked hard enough as it is without the extra burden that I would place upon them, but maybe you would be able to find more of the figurines? You would be doing a mighty service for the Men of Bree, ${PLAYER}.''You found more wooden figurines?  I can tell that this was crafted with care ${PLAYER}, the woodworkers of Bree thank you.'The dwarf you and Bingo encountered in the Misty Mountains has recovered from his wounds and has a new adventure for you to undertake.Bingo's new friend Spalvi is in the narrow pass in the Misty Mountains north of Glóin's camp, on the way to the Northern Bruinen Source.Talk to Spalvi in the narrow passSpalvi is in the narrow pass north of Glóin's camp, in the Misty Mountains.

Spalvi and his brothers were attacked by Corcur nomads in the Misty Mountains, and the dwarf was left for dead and his valuables stolen. Bingo has volunteered to track the Corcur nomads to Helegrod to reclaim Spalvi's treasure, and the dwarf is waiting to speak with you.Talk to Spalvi in the narrow pass and travel with him to HelegrodGlóin is at his camp in the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell.

Spalvi has revealed himself to be a Dourhand, and he has hobbitnapped Bingo! You know you will need a plan if you are to rescue the hobbit.Talk to Glóin at his camp in the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell'Our dwarf has mostly recovered, and he has been telling me what happened to him. He is an adventurer, as we suspected! His name is Spalvi, and he has been wronged! He and his brothers were bringing a valuable artifact through the mountains when they were attacked by a band of Corcur nomads, and the artifact stolen!

'We should do what we can to help him recover it, ${PLAYER}. He believes the Corcur brought the artifact to an old stronghold, a place called Helegrod. We should go there and bring these fellows to justice! That will make for an exciting story, will it not? But first, you should introduce yourself to Spalvi and learn what we will be up against.''Our dwarf has mostly recovered, and he has been telling me what happened to him. He is an adventurer, as we suspected! His name is Spalvi, and he has been wronged! He and his brothers were bringing a valuable artifact through the mountains when they were attacked by a band of Corcur nomads, and the artifact stolen!

'We should do what we can to help him recover it, ${PLAYER}. He believes the Corcur brought the artifact to an old stronghold, a place called Helegrod. We should go there and bring these fellows to justice! That will make for an exciting story, will it not? But first, you should introduce yourself to Spalvi and learn what we will be up against.''I am pleased to make your acquaintance, ${PLAYER}. I have already thanked your excitable friend here, but I wish to extend my gratitude to you as well. Without your aid, I am sure to have perished.

'My brothers and I were transporting a very valuable keepsake through the mountains, a necklace of gold adorned with a beautiful ruby: the Rothstone. It is an heirloom of the dwarves and bears great value to my people, but we were ambushed by the Corcur tribesmen that haunt the snow-covered slopes. They slew several of my kin; I live only because they believed I was dead as well. They stole the Rothstone and some other valuables, and fled to the north. I believe they have gone to the stronghold of Helegrod, once abandoned.

'I ask for your help to follow these Corcur villains and recover my stolen treasure. Speak to me if you are willing to do this. It seems that your friend Bingo is a bold adventurer himself, for he has already agreed to come with me, but I would value your help as well.''I do not know very much about this Helegrod place, but it sounds very exciting!''I am ready to avenge my kin and recover our treasure. Death to the Corcur! Let us make our way to the stronghold of Helegrod!'You ask Glóin if he has seen Bingo around the camp.

'No, not for some time now. That lynx that follows him around has been missing as well. Why do you ask, ${PLAYER}?'

Glóin listens to your account, and as you relate Spalvi's treachery and the arrival of the Dourhands, his face darkens and he clenches his fists.

'Such treachery! The Dourhands have been a thorn in my side for many years, but of late they grow more brazen! We will rescue that hobbit, and they will pay for the deed! But we must not rush into action or we risk its successful result. We need a plan: a daring plan.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Daring Plan' (Recommended Level: 45)</rgb>The Elf-scout Glorwen, friend of Nogmeldir, vanished when hunting near the giant-ruins.The Wargs occupy the giant-ruins in the north-east part of the High-pass.

Nogmeldir has asked you to do battle with the Wargs to test their strength and numbers.Defeat tempest Wargs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nogmeldir is at Vindurhal, west of the giant-ruins.

You should return to Nogmeldir and tell him what you have learned of the tempest Wargs.'Glorwen had been tasked with scouting the strength of the Warg-packs in the Misty Mountains, especially those who prowl through the High Pass, near upon the gates to Goblin-town. She has failed to return, so we must continue her mission.

'The ruins to the east were once inhabited by capricious giants, but it is said that they fell at odds with the goblins of Goblin-town, and most moved south and east of here, being unable to pursue the vicious goblins into their underground kingdom. Now those ruins appear to have become the lair of a large pack of Wargs, who are likely allied with the goblins.

'Test them, if you would, and see if they fight or flee -- if they fight in the face of losses, then it is likely they are held under a strong pack-leader and remain allied with the goblins.''Defeat the Wargs, ${PLAYER}, and perhaps we will learn something of Glorwen's fate.''So, ${PLAYER}, you have returned already? It seems the Wargs remain organized in some fashion, and likely friendly to the goblins - would that there were some way to break their foul alliance.'Thorgest told you that you may be able to learn of Glorwen's fate by confronting their pack-leader, Blackfang.The totem of challenge is in the centre of the ruins of Starkhath.

Nogmeldir has asked you to find the totem and call out the Warg-leader Blackfang.Return to Nogmeldir at Vindurhal and tell him of Glorwen's capture by the goblins of Goblin-town.'I know this is asking much of you, but if Thorgest believes that the only way we might learn something from the Wargs of Starkhath is to challenge their leader, then I can see no other way forwards.

'Glorwen's life may hang in the balance -- if she yet lives at all. For her sake as well as yours, take care and arm yourself well.''Seek the Wargs' Totem of Challenge, ${PLAYER}... you may learn something of Glorwen's fate.''Who dares? You are not of the pack, and yet you would challenge me? So be it....''Your tidings chill me to the bone, ${PLAYER}. Few are those who have escaped from the dark, twisted caves of Goblin-town once they have ventured within.

'The hatred of the goblins for my kin is great, and Glorwen's torments will be long and cruel at their hands.

'We must prepare -- by stealth or force of arms -- to rescue her, for even should we lose a dozen heroes to the darts and blades of the goblins, still our suffering will be as nothing compared to hers should we leave her to their devices!'Lakhina has learned of the challenge that Crannog has set for you and knows what she would have you do.The hill-beasts of Fasach-larran often carry bloodstones they find on the ground. Many of the creatures make their home in Nád Nathair, north of Aughaire.

Lakhina has asked you to defeat hill-beasts and recover the dull bloodstones they often carry.Collect dull bloodstones (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lakhina is at the centre island of Aughaire, east of Crannog's ruling-seat.

You have collected dull bloodstones from hill-beasts in Fasach-larran and should now return them to Lakhina.'I have heard of your task, ${PLAYER}, and know exactly how you can prove your worthiness... to me, and to us all.

'This land must be coaxed to give us food, but she is more generous with gems, such as the bloodstones which we use in several crafts. But the shiny stones attract more than the Trév Gállorg who go searching for them; the hill-beasts covet bloodstones and will often fight to the death to recover them. Once the creatures have obtained such a bloodstone, the gem loses its shine! Only through the secret ways of the Trév Gállorg can the shine be returned to the stones.

'Show your mettle and recover dull bloodstones from the hill-beasts north of Aughaire! You will find many of the creatures among the chasms of Nád Nathair, though their wandering cousins may also bear the stones.''The hill-beasts of Fasach-larran often carry bloodstones. Look for them north of Aughaire, especially among the chasms of Nád Nathair.''You have done very well, ${PLAYER}. I will tell Crannog of your service and polish these dull bloodstones to the sheen they deserve. They will then be used in the crafts of my people.'Glóin told you that the Nazgûl you are pursuing can be found in the old dwarf-fortress of Gabilazan, in the company of Skorgrím Dourhand. The two entered the nearly impregnable inner keep of the fortress. Glóin believes the only way to enter the inner keep is to learn its secret entrance.The cornerstone containing the plans is located inside Gabilazan's Vault, located in one of the two passes east of Glóin's camp. Glóin's camp is north of Rivendell.

Glóin told you that to learn how to access the secret entrance to Gabilazan's inner keep, you will need to take the plans for the fortress placed in its cornerstone.Search the Gabilazan CornerstoneBring the Gabilazan plans to Glóin.'If we can but enter this fortress, we will end two problems with one fell stroke. By ending the threat of this Rider, we can ensure Rivendell's safety, and by putting an end to Skorgrím, we will crush the Dourhands. However, it won't be easy, as the scout reported that the two entered the inner keep of the fortress. That keep is nearly impregnable.

'This is what you must do, ${PLAYER}. Every dwarf-fortress has a secret entrance, and although I do not know the secret of Gabilazan, I know how to learn it. When the cornerstone for Gabilazan was laid down, a copy of the fortress' plans would have been stored within. Around that cornerstone the fortress was built, and can now only be accessed by entering Gabilazan's vault. Once we have the plans, we will be able to learn the fortress' secret entrance.

'Gabilazan can be found in the more westerly of the two passes that gate entrance to Iorbar. The easternmost pass, Rakhâs-bizar, is not guarded by dwarves and may give you a roundabout means of approaching the keep.''The plans are at Gabilazan, ${PLAYER}...with those, we may find our way in!''Ah, wonderful, you found the cornerstone, and the plans were within. I didn't mention it before you left, but there was a very good chance the plans were taken or destroyed years ago. Yet it seems fortune is smiling upon us this day!

'Oh, and look here. Here's the inner keep's secret entrance!'Glóin discovered the secret entrance to the inner keep of Gabilazan, but the way is locked and will require an artifact of the old days.The dwarf-vault that contains the key-stone is at Iskeld's Look-out, near the source of the Bruinen. Glóin is at his camp north of Rivendell.

Glóin has asked you to retrieve a key-stone from an old dwarf-vault. The key-stone will allow you entry into the Dourhand-occupied dwarf-keep.'Ah, yes, why it will be just like when Burglar Bilbo crept into Smaug's lair! We'll just need the key to open the secret door, and you'll be in the inner keep. Finding that key, though, could present a challenge.

'Now the old tales of Helegrod -- that's the great dwarf-hall to the north that fell to the dragon Thorog -- say that after it was abandoned, all the surrounding dwarf-halls were abandoned as well. The key-stones that opened the secret doors to all those halls were hidden in a small dwarf-vault near the source of the Bruinen. The building might be in ruins now, but the vault is surely still there. Once we have the key-stone, the inner keep of Gabilazan will be open to us!

'Journey north to the source of the Bruinen and look for the vault at Iskeld's Look-out. You will need to brave the snow-beasts that make their homes among the drifts, but that should not be a problem for you, I would expect!''Yes, yes, this is the key-stone of which I spoke! Good work, ${PLAYER}! Now the real business will begin.'With the Key-stone in hand, you can now enter the dwarf-keep in Gabilazan, the last refuge of the Dourhands and confront both the Nazgûl and Skorgrím.Gimli is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

When you have gathered a fellowship, seek out Gimli, and he will accompany you to the dwarf-keep where the Nazgûl and Skorgrím may be hiding.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Speak to Gimli in RivendellGlóin is at his camp north of Rivendell.

The Nazgûl left the dwarf-keep, commanding Skorgrím to slay you. Fortunately, you were able to defeat Skorgrím, but a fell spirit emerged from his corpse and cursed the Longbeards before vanishing. You should tell Glóin all that has transpired.'Now that we have the Key-stone, the back way into the Dourhand keep will be open to us! You should be able to confront both the Nazgûl and Skorgrím. However, the task will be a great one, and you will need help, so gather a company of allies. Also, since completion of this task may mean the end of Skorgrím, a great boon to Durin's Folk, I will send my son Gimli along with you to aid in the battle. I will entrust him with both the Key-stone and the secret dwarf-lore needed to open the gate.

'Now go and gather a company of allies and prepare for the battle ahead. Once you are prepared, speak to Gimli. He is staying in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House. Together you will set off for the keep, and he shall open the secret way into the inner keep.

'This will be a great day for the Free Peoples, ${PLAYER}! I am eager for your return with the tale of your victory!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Ah, hello ${PLAYER}. A glorious battle awaits us!

'Are you and your allies ready? My father sent me the Key-stone, so I am prepared. Let us go now into the Mountains to defeat Skorgrím and his Dourhands!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Speak to Gimli when your allies are ready, and he will lead you to the dwarf-keep. He is in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.''That was a good fight, ${PLAYER}! Such exercise does not come along every day!''You bring tidings both joyous and dark, ${PLAYER}. The Dourhands are done! They will never hope to threaten the Longbeards again.

'While the news of the spirit that was driven from Skorgrím is unsettling, it is not as disturbing as the news of the Rider's escape. What is worse, you tell me that he spoke of bringing men to Helegrod. What he seeks in that old dwarf-hall is a mystery to me.

'Unfortunately, there is more that must be done if Rivendell is to be secured. Know, though, that I will give you whatever help I can.'Upon hearing that the Nazgûl fled to Helegrod, Glóin told you some of the tale of that ancient fortress.Helegrod is north of Glóin's camp. The outer treasury is near Nan Gongarag.

Glóin thinks you may be able to learn what the Nazgûl's plan for Helegrod is by scouting the borders of that abandoned hall for the entrance to the treasury. He warned you not to enter Helegrod itself, for it is guarded too strongly by dark things.Scout the borders of HelegrodHelegrod is north of Glóin's camp.

At the entrance to Helegrod's treasury you encountered wights. You should defeat one and search its remains for some clue as to what brought it here.'If you have a moment, allow me to tell you some of the tale of Helegrod, the ruins of which lie north of here. The tale may shed light on what the Rider seeks to accomplish, though I cannot guess what that might be. Helegrod was delved beneath the mountain of Orod Lostol; it remained the northern-most outpost of Khazad-dûm for long years. Mithril was never found there, but a good many other stones and riches were. Those riches drew a dragon to Helegrod. Thorog, he was named; he slew all the dwarves and made his bed in Helegrod's treasury.

'In Khazad-dûm, which the Elves call Moria, Durin marshalled a company of dwarves to journey to Helegrod and slay the beast. Thorog was defeated, but Durin was slain by the dragon's dying breath. He was the fifth of that name; the next, too, had a sad end, but I will not speak of that now. Dwarves sought to reclaim Helegrod in the years that followed, but death hung heavy over that place and they abandoned the halls and removed Helegrod's riches to Khazad-dûm.

'I do not know what the Rider might seek to gain by taking to Helegrod. Perhaps he simply wants to fortify it, but I would wager there is more to this deed than we can see. Perhaps by scouting the borders of Helegrod we can learn more of his plans. Do not attempt to enter Helegrod itself--too many dark things linger there--but instead seek the outer treasury. Search near Nan Gongarag.''Helegrod lies to the north of here. Perhaps by scouting its borders, we may learn more of the Black Rider's plans for it.''Wights! Surely this is the doing of the Rider! And that sigil you found on one of them... I can tell that it is an artifact of evil, but its true meaning is beyond me. Perhaps it is time we ask for help from Master Elrond!'Troubled by the wights you discovered at the entrance to Helegrod's treasury and the sigil you found on one of the wight's bodies, Glóin suggested it was time to speak with Master Elrond.Elrond is in the library of Imladris.

Glóin has asked you to speak with Elrond about what you have discovered outside of Helegrod's treasury.'I am a master of earth, stone and axe, ${PLAYER}, not of these fell wights and evil artifacts. Go to Master Elrond in Rivendell and tell him all of what has transpired. He will be able to tell you what should be done next.''Please, go to Master Elrond in Rivendell and speak with him of the things you have seen. The hour is late indeed if we are only now seeing the true threat that festers in the north.''So you have returned and with evil tidings. This is the mark of a Gaunt-lord... perhaps you have seen one before? Sometimes Gaunt-lords use these sigils to summon the fell spirit that animates the wight.

'The sigil you have brought is a mark of Drugoth the Black, a powerful Gaunt-lord. As for what drew the Nazgûl and this Gaunt-lord to Helegrod, I fear I now know what they seek....'Elrond told you that after the dwarf-king Durin defeated Thorog the dragon, the body of the dragon was left to moulder in the empty treasury of Helegrod. He fears that the ice and cold of Orod Lostol will have preserved the body to such an extent that the arts of the Gaunt-lord, Drugoth, may be able to instill it with a fell spirit and raise it as a wight.Helegrod is north of Rivendell and Glóin's camp.

Elrond has asked you to enter the treasury of Helegrod and put a stop to the Nazgûl's schemes. He warned you to gather allies.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Enter the Helegrod treasury and end the Nazgûl's schemesElrond can be found in the library of Imladris.

In the treasury, you encountered the Nazgûl and were able to defeat him. You also interrupted the ritual being performed by the Gaunt-lord to restore the dragon Thorog, although part of the process was completed, and the dragon flew off. Elrond should be made aware of what happened.'What the Nazgûl seeks has become apparent to me, ${PLAYER}. Glóin told you of the fall of Helegrod, I expect: Durin, the fifth such dwarf to bear that name, slew the dragon Thorog but perished during the battle alongside most of his company? When it became clear that Helegrod was a place of evil and their attempts to restore it futile, the dwarves removed the riches from the treasury and left the dragon's body to moulder.

'However, I fear the cold and ice of Orod Lostol may have preserved the body of the dragon far longer than any would have thought. Even after death and the passage of so many years, the body of Thorog would possess much power. I fear that Drugoth the Gaunt-lord might use his arts to place some spirit within that corpse, raising the most terrible of wights to the service of Angmar.

'A dragon-wight within reach of Imladris is too grave a threat to be ignored. I must ask you to go into the treasury of Helegrod and put an end to the plans of Drugoth and the Nazgûl. It will be a difficult task, so gather a party of mighty warriors and go forth.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Our time may be short. Please hurry and gather a party of warriors and enter the treasury of Helegrod. There the body of the dragon, slain by Durin so many years ago, was left to moulder.''You have done a great thing today, ${PLAYER}! Few have faced the might of a Nazgûl and lived to tell the tale. Fortunately, the waters of the Bruinen and your pursuit of him weakened him to the extent that you could defeat him and send him back to Mordor.

'As for the matter of the dragon, it would appear that although the ritual was partially successful, the dragon-wight is not fully under the control of Drugoth. It still presents a threat to Rivendell, but not so great as would have been had the ritual been completed.

'For now, we can turn our attention to other matters, although someday you may wish to return to Helegrod and end this threat completely.'Eywind and Nogmeldir have formed a plan to use Gandalf's draught to wreak havoc upon the goblins.The burning cauldron is in a tall wooden structure in Ghâshru to the north-east.

Eywind told you to pour Gandalf's draught into the cauldron which the goblins use to produce their fire-pots and tip it over so the mixture will ignite the materials throughout the encampment.Tell Eywind of the destruction wrought by Gandalf's draught at the burning cauldron.'By Durin's Beard, this will be a fine day indeed! This draught of Gandalf's is precisely what we need to make a shambles of the goblin's fire-pot camps.

'I went down to take a quick peek at the encampment while you were off to Rivendell, and I found what we're after. That big thing on the lower slope, with the scaffold and the huge burning cauldron on it? That is the heart of their production, and that is where we will strike.

'They've got stored materials for their fire-pots all over the camp -- all we have to do is slip Gandalf's draught into that cauldron and give it a tip, and the mixture should do the rest of our work for us... you mark my words!''Ha! Now let's burn the goblins out of the Misties!''Haha, well done! 'Tis a fine thing indeed to watch an enemy's schemes blow up in their own faces, is it not ${PLAYER}?

'Would that we could blow the entirety of Goblin-town sky high as well, but I fear that it'll not be nearly as neat a conclusion when at last that dark warren falls....'The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of gilded sword sheaths to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver gilded sword sheaths from the Orcs and Angmarim surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Orcs and Angmarim from the lands surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Orcs and Angmarim from the lands surrounding Aughaire.'We shall sheath our weapons within these now. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Hillmen at Aughaire have requested a collection of pure black skin to be delivered to Raith.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black skin from beasts and cave-claws surrounding Aughaire to Raith, a Hillman within the settlement.Wanted: Pure black skin from beasts and cave-claws surrounding Aughaire. Deliver the items to Raith at Aughaire in the south-western reaches of Angmar.Wanted: Pure black skin from beasts and cave-claws surrounding Aughaire.'We will ensure that these are used well. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.You were sent to Crannog, the chieftain of Aughaire, to pursue a Fém for the Trév Gállorg.Torquil, Raith, and Osbail are near the ruling seat of Crannog, the chieftain of Aughaire.

Crannog has tasked you with facing the dangers of which Torquil, Raith, and Osbail will speak. Once you have done so, he expects you to return to him.Complete Torquil's tasksComplete Raith's tasksComplete Osbail's tasksCrannog is at his ruling seat in Aughaire, west of the centre island.

You have aided some of the Trév Gállorg with the dangers faced by the settlement and should now tell Crannog of your successes.'All who call themselves Trév Gállorg must have a purpose; that purpose is their Fém. If you have no Fém, you are no Trév Gállorg, and no friend to us.

'Prove yourself worthy of being called a friend to the Trév Gállorg of Aughaire. I will give you such a Fém if you do all that I ask of you.

'It is the place of a chieftain to direct his people to do great things, even in the face of danger, and many dangers are arrayed against Aughaire. Torquil, Raith, and Osbail will tell you of some of these. You will aid them in facing these evils, and you will return to me when your first challenge is met. If you are to prove yourself to me, you must prove yourself first to my people.''I have been told of your successes. You have taken the first step on this path, but the road is winding, and you have just begun to walk upon it.

'Not all need be as suspicious as I, for it is the duty of the chieftain to be guarded in all dealings with outsiders. Finnán, east of here at the centre island of Aughaire, has heard of your victories and wishes to speak with you.

'But that is Finnán. I am not yet certain of your worth.''You must prove yourself to me, and that means proving yourself to my people. Aid Torquil, Raith, and Osbail with their own concerns, and I will count this challenge completed.'At the behest of Crannog, you fought and defeated three of Aughaire's strongest warriors for the right to represent the Trév Gállorg in the Clúcath, but one more test remains.Nekhtan goes to train at a great tree east of Aughaire. Crannog told you to leave the settlement by the north, and to then turn east and follow the line of the hills until you see the tree in a basin to the north.

Crannog told you the story of Nekhtan, the greatest warrior of the Trév Gállorg, whose father was slain in the last Clúcath, where the Trév Duvárdain listened to their Angmarim masters and violated the rules of the rite. You are tasked with defeating him for the right to represent the Trév Gállorg at Clúcath.Challenge NekhtanCrannog is at his ruling-seat, west of the central island in the settlement of Aughaire.

You have defeated Nekhtan and should bring word of your success to Crannog.'Your Fém draws near to its fulfillment, Southron. There is one last warrior you must defeat, and if you prove to have the mastery, you will fight for the Trév Gállorg in Clúcath against the Trév Duvárdain.

'Your opponent is named Nekhtan, and he is the son of the last warrior to engage in Clúcath, the very Clúcath where the Trév Duvárdain listened to their Angmarim masters and violated the rules of the rite. Though he sued for mercy, the mercy that Clúcath should have granted, Nekhtan's father was slain by Avair, warrior of the Trév Duvárdain. That was the act by which we knew the Trév Duvárdain could not be redeemed and were lost to us, though we shared kinship in years gone past.

'There is a great tree to the east of Aughaire, and Nekhtan goes there to train in the skills of combat. Leave Aughaire heading north, then turn east and follow the line of the hills a short distance until you see the great tree in a basin to the north. Defeat Nekhtan and return to me for my judgement.''You have defeated Nekhtan, and earned the right to stand for the Trév Gállorg in Clúcath against the Trév Duvárdain. This is a chance for great honour, Southron, and a chance to show our foes that the Trév Gállorg are not to be dismissed so readily.

'We will stand always against them, and you will be the warrior to remind them of our opposition!''Nekhtan is filled with a great desire for vengeance on the Trév Duvárdain for the unlawful death of his father in Clúcath, and it drives him to great fury in battle.

'He trains in the skills of battle at a great tree east of Aughaire. Leave the settlement by the north road, and then turn immediately east and follow the line of the hills for a short distance. You will see the great tree in a basin to the north. Defeat Nekhtan and you will earn the right to fight on behalf of the Trév Gállorg in Clúcath.''I have been too long in training here in the wild, stranger, for I have heard naught of your deeds. You have a Fém to earn the right to do battle in Clúcath? That is foolishness. The Trév Duvárdain do not hold by the tenets of that rite; listen to one who knows of these things!

'I have honed my body to a knife's-edge so that I may exact my vengeance upon them and upon their Angmarim masters. I will not willingly stand by while you attempt to take my place in Clúcath, unless you prove to have the mastery.

'We fight! If you deserve the honour of standing against the Trév Duvárdain, you must prove to me that you are the stronger warrior!'The Fém Crannog granted you was to begin the Rite of Clúcath, an ancient Hillman ceremony of judgement. For now, though, he has other tasks for you.Raith is near Crannog's ruling seat, west of the centre island of Aughaire. Feradakh is at the Búth Luikh, south-east of Crannog's ruling-seat. Fonghala is at the Búth Sánkhas, south-east of the market of Aughaire. Moridac is at the aurochs-pens in Aughaire, north-east of Crannog's ruling-seat.

In an effort to see if you are strong enough to represent the Trév Gállorg in the upcoming Clúcath, Crannog has tasked you with fighting several of Aughaire's mightiest warriors. Raith can tell you about each of them.Defeat FeradakhDefeat FonghalaDefeat MoridacCrannog is at his ruling-seat, west of Aughaire's centre island.

You have defeated the three warriors set before you by Crannog and should tell him now of your victories.'By taking the Stone of Clúcath, you have set in motion a wave that cannot be avoided -- we move to conflict with the Trév Duvárdain, and we must prepare for the coming storm. There can be no more hiding and no more flinching from battle. We will stand or fall by the outcome of the Clúcath, and for that, we need the strongest warrior to represent the Trév Gállorg of Aughaire. Could it be you, Southron that you are? It is possible, if unlikely; your Fém becomes to find out.

'Mighty are the warriors of Aughaire. I set you now before them. Test your skills and valour against these, and we will see if your destiny leads you to the Clúcath: Feradakh of the Búth Luikh, Fonghala of the Búth Sánkhas, Moridac as strong as the aurochs he tends. Raith can tell you of them, if you desire to know what you will face.

'Prove your worth to us by defeating them.  If you succeed we will know the truth of your strength.''You have defeated three of Aughaire's mightiest warriors, ${PLAYER}. There is one more among us that may be your equal, and you must face him at last.''Speak with Raith if you need guidance in this matter I have set before you.''You have done much for us already, ${PLAYER}. Could it be that you might be the warrior we need to best the Trév Duvárdain in Clúcath? It is possible. You will need to defeat Aughaire's strongest warriors if you are to complete this Fém and prove more deserving than they to take on this burden.

'You will find Feradakh at the Búth Luikh, south-east of Crannog's ruling-seat. He is a master of fighting from a distance; you must keep him close, if you can, or he will become a most deadly foe.

'Fonghala, at the Búth Sánkhas south-east of the market of Aughaire, is a healer of great discipline. You must finish the battle quickly, or she will certainly outlast you; the tale-spinners still speak of her battle with Drostan of the Trév Duvárdain, which lasted a full day and night before he retreated in cowardice.

'Moridac is the aurochs-keeper of Aughaire, and it is said he has the strength and fortitude of the creatures with which he spends most of his days.''I know you, ${PLAYER}, and I know your Fém. If this is the fate for you, our battle will reveal it!

'Prepare to face me!''I have been waiting for you since the moment you took the Stone of Clúcath from the Cairn of Honour. Are you to be the warrior that represents the Trév Gállorg in the upcoming storm? Let our battle show us the way!''I am ready for you, Southron. I have the strength of the aurochs! If you are the one to face the Trév Duvárdain in Clúcath, you must be strong indeed!

'Let us do battle and shout our challenge to the skies!'You have completed Crannog's first challenge to prove your worth to the Trév Gállorg of Aughaire, but he does not yet trust you.Lakhina, Camran, and Rona are at the centre island of Aughaire, east of Crannog's ruling seat.

Crannog wants you to speak with Lakhina, Camran, and Rona of Aughaire and perform the tasks they ask of you.Complete Lakhina's tasksComplete Camran's tasksComplete Rona's tasksCrannog is at his ruling-seat in Aughaire, west of the centre island.

You have performed the tasks given you by Lakhina, Camran, and Rona and should now return to Crannog.'Some of my people are satisfied with your deeds, but there is still more to be done if you hope to be accepted by the Trév Gállorg of Aughaire. The short path may be stomached by the treacherous; the longer the road, the more sure I can be of your nature.

'Speak with Lakhina, Camran, and Rona here in Aughaire and perform the tasks that they ask of you. If I am satisfied with their reports of you, we may discuss your Fém.

'You will find these three near the centre island of this settlement, to the east.''Your tale grows with every challenge I set before you, ${PLAYER}. Many of my people call you friend, and I count you now among my people, Southron.

'There is always more to be done, and those of the Búth Sánkhas will welcome you to their service. The Búth Sánkhas is south-east of the market of Aughaire.

'Your path does not end here, ${PLAYER}. It begins.''Lakhina, Camran, and Rona are on the centre island of the settlement, east of here. Perform the tasks they ask of you, and we will discuss the details of your Fém.'Your accomplishments on behalf of the Trév Gállorg have earned you the recognition and trust of Crannog, their chieftain.Raith is standing by Crannog's ruling-seat, in Aughaire, west of the settlement's central island.

Crannog has granted you your Fém -- to begin the rite of the Clúcath -- and told you to speak with Raith to learn what this involves.The Cairn of Honour is north-east of Fail-á-Khro, far to the north of Aughaire. The hilltop on which the Cairn of Honour rests is marked by a line of torches.

Raith has told you that you must collect the Stone of Clúcath and bring it to Crannog.Crannog is at his ruling-seat, west of the centre island of Aughaire.

You have taken the Stone of Clúcath from the Cairn of Honour and should bring it now to Crannog.'Long have I served my people as chieftain, and I have given many dozens of Fém to those of age among my people, and to those few travellers I deem worthy of calling friend. It is a heavy burden, for a Fém is not to be granted lightly. If it is beyond the reach of the one to whom it is assigned, the blame falls upon me, as chieftain.

'I give to you now your Fém. I give to ${PLAYER}, ${RACE} from the South, this Fém: to begin the rite of Clúcath, the ancient privilege and burden of the Trév Gállorg. It has been long since the Clúcath has been called, and the memory of the last is grim; but its time has come again. Thus is ${PLAYER}'s Fém given.

'Raith will tell you what this Fém involves, ${PLAYER}. Speak with him at once and begin.''I am happy that Crannog has given you a Fém, ${PLAYER}, but I am surprised he has chosen you to begin the rite of Clúcath. In ages past, each of the tribes selected a warrior to fight on its behalf in a ceremonial combat, called a Clúcath. The victorious tribe was understood to become the leader of all the others until such time as another Clúcath was called. It has been many years since the last, during which the Trév Duvárdain violated the laws of mercy required by the ceremony and slew our champion.

'North of Aughaire is a hilltop where the Clúcath has always been held. The place is called Fail-á-Khro. Travel to Fail-á-Khro, and when you reach it, you must turn north-east. After some distance, you will see a line of torches ascending another slope. Atop this final slope lies the Cairn of Honour. Climb the Cairn and retrieve the Stone of Clúcath from atop it, then bring the Stone to Crannog.

'We must trust our chieftain. If he believes that the time has come for Clúcath, he surely has some knowledge we do not.''Speak with Raith to learn what must be done for your Fém, beginning the Clúcath.''Speak with Raith to learn what must be done to begin your Fém.''To begin the rite of Clúcath, you must retrieve a particular gemstone. The Stone of Clúcath rests atop the Cairn of Honour, north-east of Fail-á-Khro. The hill on which the Cairn of Honour sits is marked by a line of torches.

'To reach Fail-á-Khro, you must travel far to the north of Aughaire.''Give the Stone of Clúcath to Crannog, and quickly!''You have taken the Stone of Clúcath. It is now only a matter of time before the greatest warrior of the Trév Gállorg will face the champion of the Trév Duvárdain, and much will be decided.

'The warriors of Búth Luikh will now speak with you concerning tasks with which they desire your aid. Help them if you will, but know that you have another obligation.

'Your Fém is not yet complete.'Torquil's brothers were slain by Orcs, and he seeks allies to take his vengeance upon them.The cave Morfil is in Fasach-larran, east of Aughaire.

Torquil has asked you to journey to Morfil and defeat the Orcs that killed his brothers.Defeat Ongbúrz Warriors at Morfil (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Ongbúrz Skirmishers at Morfil (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Ongbúrz Captain at MorfilTorquil is at Aughaire, near Crannog's ruling-seat, west of the settlement's centre island.

You have slain many Orcs at Morfil and should return now to Torquil at Aughaire.'My brothers were great warriors, and they gained much honour in battle against the Trév Duvárdain and the Orcs of Angmar. All spoke highly of them, and both women and men called them mighty.

'But even the mightiest man can be felled by a single arrow loosed from hiding, and so it was with my brothers. Orcs beset them by the cave Morfil, and they were defeated.

'I have petitioned Crannog for vengeance, and now he sees fit to unleash you upon the Orcs in my stead! I hope you are worthy, for if your strength is found lacking, it will go poorly for you. The entrance to Morfil is in Fasach-larran, east of Aughaire. Defeat Orcs in and around the cave and make them pay for the deaths of my brothers!''My brothers would be pleased by your service in their honour, ${PLAYER}. I give you thanks in their stead for the slaying of the Orcs at Morfil.

'For my part, I believe you are trustworthy. But it is up to Crannog to rule on this matter.''Morfil is in Fasach-larran, east of Aughaire. That is where you will find the Orcs that killed my brothers, and that is where you must go.'Camran of Aughaire has prepared a task by which he will know whether you are worthy of Crannog's trust.The fortress of Tór Gailvin is north-east of Aughaire.

Camran has asked you to return to Tór Gailvin and destroy the standards that fly over the fortress.Destroy Tór Gailvin standards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Camran is at the centre island of Aughaire, east of Crannog's ruling-seat.

You have destroyed the standards that flew above Tór Gailvin and should return now to Camran.'I know of your test, ${PLAYER}, and am prepared to do my part.

'You have already been to the fortress of Tór Gailvin, the home that was given to the Trév Duvárdain by their new masters, the evil that sits in Carn Dûm. Raith believes that wresting the tokens of the Enemy from the Trév Duvárdain of Tór Gailvin is enough; I do not agree.

'Return to Tór Gailvin, north-east of Aughaire, and destroy the standards that fly above its walls. This will send a clear message to the Trév Duvárdain and their masters. I am sure of it. If you do this, I will tell Crannog the tale of your success.''You have returned alive from Tór Gailvin, and I delight to see it! The standards no longer fly above the fortress? I will tell Crannog of your victory over the Trév Duvárdain, but for my part I am satisfied and call you friend.''Return to Tór Gailvin, the fortress north-east of Aughaire, and destroy the standards that fly above it. If you are able to do this, I will report your success to Crannog.'You have survived the Clúcath, and Fail-á-Khro has been retaken by the Trév Gállorg.Cána is at Fail-á-Khro, north of Aughaire.

The Trév Gállorg have retaken Fail-á-Khro and now seek your assistance to hold it.'Your victory has driven the Trév Duvárdain into the north, and my people have retaken Fail-á-Khro, ${PLAYER}. But we cannot yet rest.

'Return to Fail-á-Khro and speak with Cána if you wish to aid us further against the Trév Duvárdain.

'Fail-á-Khro is north of Aughaire, and many of my people hold it against the enemy.''Your legend is already growing, ${PLAYER}. May you grow it further by aiding us as we drive the Trév Duvárdain even further into Fasach-falroid!''Fail-á-Khro is to the north, ${PLAYER}. Speak with Cána there if you still wish to aid the Trév Gállorg, even after all you have already done for us.'Gailthin the Elf was emissary to the giants in happier days, and she is one of the few who might understand the significance of the tusk-rings you recovered.Gailthin is in the dwarf-city of Gondamon, in the low-lands of Ered Luin.

Golhador has asked you to show the tusk-rings to emissary Gailthin.'I cannot understand the markings on these tusk-rings, ${PLAYER}, but I feel they must have some significance. I know of one who might be able to read their meaning, but she is far from here, and the threat of giants and mammoths is much nearer.

'And yet, if you were to move with haste and choose fast hooves to bear you on your mission, you could reach the one of whom I speak and obtain her counsel. I speak of Gailthin the Elf, once Master Elrond's emissary to the giants. She was one of the few they seemed to respect. She was dispatched to Ered Luin to deal with the troubles in that region and has not yet returned.

'I heard she was to meet with the captain of the dwarf-city of Gondamon in the low-lands of Ered Luin. Seek her out and show her the tusk-rings you wrested from the great mammoths! Her dealings with the giants may give her some insight into what their markings could mean.''Greetings, ${PLAYER}. It has been a long time since I was at Imladris, and I must tell you I have missed it.

'You removed these rings from the tusks of mammoths? You must be mighty indeed to have done so, a valiant ${RACE}, beyond measure! I recognize these symbols and read them for what they are: marks of ownership.

'I can tell you the names of the giants that have taken these mammoths for their allies. Only they can stir the mammoths into action against us -- I hope only that my knowledge can help you eliminate this threat before the time for stopping it is past.'Nogmeldir recalled a special draught which Gandalf the Grey mentioned using in his fireworks to enhance their power.Gandalf is in Bilbo's room on the ground floor of the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

Nogmeldir has asked you to travel into Rivendell to the west of the Misty Mountains and retrieve a special draught from Gandalf the Grey.Talk to Gandalf in RivendellNogmeldir is at Vindurhal in northern High-pass.

Gandalf the Grey gave you a special draught he had prepared, which you should take to Nogmeldir.'It would seem that there are indeed some limits to the ingenuity of dwarves, Master Eywind. Fortunately, I happen to know a Wizard who is far wiser than either of us -- particularly when it comes to matters of fire.

'I remember many years ago when Mithrandir would come to Rivendell to celebrate momentous events with us, and often his fireworks would be central to the evening festivities. It so happens that in discussing the nature of his fireworks, he mentioned a draught which he uses to enhance the virtue of his displays.

'It occurs to me now that if we happened to have a bit of this draught on hand, we might make excellent use of it when it comes time to deal with these goblins and their fire-pots once and for all.''Speak with Gandalf, ${PLAYER}. He may have a suggestion.''What is this? An Elf named Nogmeldir sent you for what? Ah, now I recall him. Bright fellow, though a bit impetuous. He wants my fire-draught does he? Whatever for?

'Goblin fire-pots? An entire camp of them? I think I see what the fellow is about. I do happen to have a vial left... where was that? Ah, here it is!

'Now, be careful with that! No more than a few drops mind you, and keep it away from the lamp oil, or you will soon find yourself lacking your eyebrows... if you are fortunate.''Take the phial to Nogmeldir, ${PLAYER}.''Were you successful? Yes, that is the draught Mithrandir mentioned. It does not look like much, does it?

'I will speak with Eywind on how we might best employ this, and then we shall see....'Nogmeldir and Eywind came to the High Pass, seeking news of an Elf-scout named Glorwen.The giant-ruins of Starkhath are well to the east.

Nogmeldir asked you to search the Warg-infested giant-ruins for signs of the Elven scout Glorwen.'Among other tasks, Eywind and I have come up into these forsaken mountains in search of a companion of mine by the name of Glorwen. She came here some weeks ago with the same duty we have: to explore the strength and purpose of the goblins of High Pass and ensure that they do not move against Rivendell without warning.

'Unfortunately, she has always been easily distracted by the hunt, and I find it likely that she has managed to while away the past weeks entertaining herself and has quite lost track of the time.

'When last we heard from her, she was going to explore a set of giant-ruins to the east, rumoured now to be inhabited by a large pack of Wargs. If you would help us find her, you should begin your search there.''What is this? Glorwen's bow? Yet you say you saw no other signs of her save a torn cloak?

'Had the Wargs slain her, it is likely they would have torn her to pieces and let the evidence lie plainly about. If there was no sign of her body, then it is possible that she yet lives. Perhaps she escaped? But I think that unlikely, for she would have gone back for her bow, or come to us in need of aid.

'I have no answer to this riddle, but perhaps the Wargs might?'Eywind was sent into the Misty Mountains to keep vigil for goblin movements from Goblin-town.The goblin-encampment is north-east of Eywind's camp, near the gates of Goblin-town.

Eywind asked you to test the strength of the goblin-encampments which lie near the gates of Goblin-town.Defeat overland guards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat overland stabbers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat overland bombers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Eywind at Vindurhal in Northern High-pass and recount to him your battles against the Goblins.'Well met ${PLAYER}, did Elrond send you up here? No matter either way... we've plenty to do before the heavy snows set in. It seems that Rivendell is getting nervous about the rumours they've been hearing about Goblin-town falling under the sway of Angmar or some-such, so they sent me and my lanky friend here to take a look around and see what's what.

'Can't say that we know for sure whether Angmar is involved, but we did find a large goblin-encampment on the surface just a short ways north-east of here. Normally the goblins prefer to stay down in their caves away from the sun, so that's a tell-tale sign that something is wrong.

'We've been making little forays now and again against the goblins, but for now we've got to keep an eye on this pass -- make sure they don't start heading west to join with the smaller tribes scattered about the Misties. Perhaps you would care to take a try at them in the meanwhile? There are plenty to go around, and it would give you a chance to determine their strength. I would also say the exercise will do you some good -- you are looking a bit winded from the heights here.''Dwarves hate goblins, but none more than those who stole the Misty Mountains from us!''Ha! Well fought ${PLAYER}. Goblins may make for poor company, but they do help keep one's skills honed.

'Now those fire-pots you mentioned, those are not weapons for little skirmishes -- they were designed as weapons of war, for burning forests and besieging fortresses. If the goblins are making so many of them, it can only mean they have plans to march beyond these mountains... and soon methinks.

'We will need to do something about that.'The goblins of Goblin-town have been producing large numbers of their fire-pots in preparation for war.Eywind believes that the goblins' fire-pots can be used against them to burn down their camps. He has sent you into their camp to steal a number of them for him to examine.Collect goblin fire-pots (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The goblin-encampments are north-east of Eywind's camp, near the gates of Goblin-town.

Eywind asked you to steal fire-pots from the encampments on the slopes and ridges near the gates of Goblin-town and bring them to him.'Well, the Elf here thinks I may have gone a bit daft, but hear me out and see what you think.

'I've got a plan in mind, and those fire-pots the goblins wield are at the heart of it. They are making them in large numbers in there, and while goblins may be industrious, they are also careless. You should be able to steal a number of those fire-pots, and with a bit of work, we ought to be able to use their own weapons against them.

'The thought of that entire camp in flames brings me a certain childish delight, so let's get to work!''Bring me some of those goblin fire-pots, ${PLAYER}!''Ha! Good work, ${PLAYER}. Now let us see those. There is not much a goblin can come up with that a dwarf can't sort out in short order.

'What's this...?'Malenhad is home to turtles of enormous size, which the Hillmen value for their edible meat and tremendous shells.The turtles live throughout the western reaches of Malenhad. Othran is at Tyrn Lhug, in the south-west corner of Malenhad.

Othran has offered to pay you for the meat and shell plates of the giant turtles of Malenhad.'Perhaps you seek more challenging prey?

'The giant turtles of Malenhad are valued by my people both for their meat, which is edible, and for their shells. They can be deadly foes at close quarters, and if you fight them, you may damage their shells before you manage to kill them.

'I will pay for both their shell plates and meat if you think you are up to the challenge. The turtles live throughout this part of the swamp.''Good shells. Meat isn't spoiled yet. I will pay for this.'Bingo is eager to help his new friend Spalvi recover the treasure stolen by the Corcur nomads and brought to the dwarf-hold of Helegrod.Bingo is standing on the snowy slope that leads down to the dwarf-hold of Helegrod.Talk to Bingo on the slope leading down to HelegrodSpalvi is on the snowy slope above Helegrod.Talk to Spalvi on the slope above HelegrodYou have defeated a handful of Corcur nomads, but Spalvi said he does not recognize among their number the foes that ambushed him.Talk to SpalviA Corcur nomad camp lies between you and the great gate to Helegrod.

Spalvi and Bingo will meet up with you once you reach the front stairs.Make your way to the stairs that lead up to the gate to HelegrodBingo is slowly making his way up the stairs to the gate to Helegrod, and you should follow him.Climb the steps to Helegrod with BingoBingo stands outside the great gate to Helegrod, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Bingo at the top of the stepsSpalvi has arrived at the top of the steps, in front of the great gate to Helegrod.Talk to Spalvi at the top of the stepsA group of Dourhands have come to Helegrod, and their leader wishes to speak to you.Talk to the leader of the Dourhand bandSpalvi has been revealed as a Dourhand, and now you are in a tricky situation.Survive the confrontation with the DourhandsBingo is being held by the Dourhands in exchange for your good behaviour.Talk to Bingo'I am not happy about this, ${PLAYER}, but it is a bad situation and this is the only way for everyone to feel safe. I will bring you to the exit, and I implore you not to try anything.''Oh my, this is exciting, ${PLAYER}! This valley is so remote... how many hobbits do you think have ever seen this snowy expanse? Not many, I'd wager!

'We have to find these Corcur nomads and get them to return Spalvi's treasure. It's not right!''Enough conversation. I hear the Corcur approaching! Let us surprise them before they see us!''We will cover more ground if we split up, ${PLAYER}. I will circle around and see if I can find the Corcur nomads who attacked me. You should press forward and bring our friend Bingo to the front stairs. I will rejoin you at that point, and if neither of us have recovered my treasure we will then consider scaling the front gate.

'Luck be with you, ${CLASS}, and with me!''So the plan is for us to meet up with Spalvi at the front stairs? I think you had better go first, ${PLAYER}. I will be right behind you. Well... perhaps not right behind you, but a goodly distance back. Still behind you, though! Good luck to us!''If you could just... give me a moment... to catch my breath...'

Bingo breathes deeply and rubs his aching legs.

'I have never climbed as many steps in all my life as I have today, ${PLAYER}! I hope Spalvi knows how to open this gate, or we will have to turn around and go back, and I am less ready to climb DOWN these steps than I was to climb UP them the first time!''I did not find the Corcur that attacked me. They must be behind this gate, and when we get through I will make them pay for their crimes against me and my brothers, and will recover the treasure they stole!''You have the look of an adventurer. Did you have business with this Corcur filth? We have already put an end to their thieving. The Rothstone, Skorgrím's Star, is ours once again.'

The Dourhand peers past you, and his face lights up with recognition.'This has become quite a pickle, hasn't it? Well, I am sure this is just a simple misunderstanding, and I will sort it all out with these Dourhand fellows.

'You should do as they say, ${PLAYER}. I do not want you to be hurt on my account. I will sort this out and meet you back at Glóin's camp.

'What an adventure! This is going to make for quite an exciting chapter!'At the behest of Master Elrond of Imladris, you have entered the abandoned treasury of Helegrod to end the Nazgûl's scheme to restore the dragon Thorog.Seek out the Nazgûl and end his schemes.End the Nazgûl's plansExamine the obelisk.

You have encountered an obelisk that was glowing faintly.Examine the obeliskSeek out the obelisks and open the way to the centre of the treasury.

Examination of the obelisk found at the entrance to the treasury revealed that the only way to enter the centre treasury is to invoke the name of Elbereth at obelisks found scattered through out the treasury.Invoke the name of Elbereth at the obelisks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Seek out the Nazgûl and end his schemes.

Now that you have invoked the name of the Elbereth at three of the obelisks in the treasury, the way to the centre of the treasury is open.End the Nazgûl's plansA plaque on the side of this obelisk is written in several tongues. It reads:

'I, Durin, lord of Khazad-dûm, have delved this vault with the aid of the Gwaith-i-Mírdain. To protect the great riches found here, there are obelisks crafted by the arts of Elves as a token of friendship.

'Only those who invoke the name of the Star-kindler may pass. Three times three are the obelisks, and three times must you speak.'With your help, Glóin was able to obtain the key-stone to the secret entrance leading to the inner keep of the dwarf fortress located in the pass of Gabilazan. Alongside his son, Gimli, he has asked you to go into this keep and put a stop to the Nazgûl and Skogrím.Speak with Gimli to begin your foray into the dwarf-keep.

The dwarf-keep in the pass of Gabilazan might be both the last hiding-place of the Dourhands led by Skorgrím and the Nazgûl you have pursued through the Trollshaws and into the Misty Mountains.Talk to GimliSpeak with Gimli to continue your foray into the dwarf-keep.

The dwarf-keep in the pass of Gabilazan might be both the last hiding-place of the Dourhands led by Skorgrím and the Nazgûl you have pursued through the Trollshaws and into the Misty Mountains.Talk to GimliSpeak with Gimli to continue your foray into the dwarf-keep.

The dwarf-keep in the pass of Gabilazan might be both the last hiding-place of the Dourhands led by Skorgrím and the Nazgûl you have pursued through the Trollshaws and into the Misty Mountains.Talk to GimliYou have made your way deep into the dwarf-keep. It is nearly time to confront the Nazgûl and Skorgrím. Speak with Gimli to begin the final battle.

The dwarf-keep in the pass of Gabilazan might be both the last hiding-place of the Dourhands led by Skorgrím and the Nazgûl you have pursued through the Trollshaws and into the Misty Mountains.Confront the Nazgûl and SkorgrímGimli is standing in the courtyard of the keep of Gabilazan.

You have successfully defeated Skorgrím Dourhand, but the significance of his death is still uncertain.Talk to Gimli'You fought well, ${PLAYER}, and we have put an end to Skorgrím's menace! I am troubled by the escape of the Nazgûl and the apparition that appeared upon Skorgrím's death, but there is nothing we can do about that right now. At least we have paid the Dourhands for their treachery! Ha ha!''It was a good battle, but I think it is time to leave. I am ready to journey back to the guest rooms at Rivendell when you are.''Skorgrím must think that the walls of his fortress will keep him safe, tall and broad as they are, but he does not know about the key-stone you recovered.

'We will fight our way through his remaining defences, and perhaps I will give him one chance to come out of the main gates of the keep to face me in combat. He has no honour, so my challenge will probably go unanswered. It is just as well, but my axe is anxious for the chance to end this fool Dourhand!

'Are you ready? The Dourhands cannot hope to stand against us!''I am ready to go on, if you are as well. I will challenge Skorgrím to come down, and if he does not, we will seek out the hidden door.''It seems these Dourhands were not expecting us to come in by the hidden door, ${PLAYER}.

'This is amusing, but I tire of dealing with lesser Dourhands. Let us find Skorgrím with no further delay!''Skorgrím has nowhere left to hide, ${PLAYER}, and I think he now realizes it. His doom stands outside his door, and he has no choice but to face it!

'Are you ready for battle?'Tasgall's plan is ready, and the two of you must attempt to end the threat of Bangrath and Murragrath.Tasgall's hunt has brought the two of you to the slopes of Nád Nathair.Talk to Tasgall'We have done very well this day, ${PLAYER}, despite the interference of the Trév Duvárdain. Tell me if you are ready to return to my camp-site; I will not speak of Drostan and his ilk until we are safely out of danger.''I have the crawler-remains you gathered, ${PLAYER}; do you smell their awful stench? To the hill-beasts, this odour will be impossible to resist!

'Let us begin our hunt, and we will lure Bangrath and Murragrath from hiding. Keep a watchful eye, for hill-beasts will be drawn to the scent of the bait I carry.'You have come to Fail-á-Khro to engage in the ceremonial rite of Clúcath on behalf of the Trév Gállorg.Crannog is waiting to walk with you to the site where the Clúcath will be performed.Talk to CrannogThe Clúcath is about to begin: Crannog of the Trév Gállorg and Domongart of the Trév Duvárdain are face-to-face.Watch the Clúcath unfoldThe Clúcath has begun, but the rules of the rite have not been followed! You must now fight to defend yourself against the enemy, for there will be no mercy.Defeat the warriors of the Trév DuvárdainCrannog is at Fail-á-Khro, waiting to congratulate you on your victory.

You have been victorious in Clúcath and should speak now with Crannog.'Are you prepared, ${PLAYER}? The rite of Clúcath will determine the fortunes of the Trév Gállorg, but we must be on guard: Domongart has been treacherous in the past, and there is small reason to expect that today will be different.

'But if you are victorious, it will mean much. It must be chanced.''The Trév Gállorg have triumphed, ${PLAYER}! You have brought victory to Fail-á-Khro, where sorrow has reigned too long.

'Speak with me when you are ready to return to Aughaire, and we will go.''Leave me be! I have no need of your sympathy! I curse the Trév Gállorg, and you most of all, fool ${RACE}!''I do not understand, ${PLAYER}. Why did you spare me? What have I to live for, now that I have been cast from the Trév Duvárdain and my name taken? I am Drostan no longer...but who shall I be now?''We will return to Aughaire in triumph, ${PLAYER}. When we left it, we left in doubt. We return now with the certainty of victory.''My heart is at ease, ${PLAYER}. My father has been avenged, and I have upheld the principles of the Trév Gállorg and of Clúcath. I owe you much.''You fought well and bravely, ${PLAYER}. It was an honour to stand with you.'Vighar Roadwalker has told you of a trio of scouts sent forth from Rivendell and passed through his camp some weeks ago to scout the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains, as they rise up into the High Pass. These three -- the Elves Nogmeldir and Glorwen and the dwarf Eywind -- have been tasked with determining the strength of Goblin-town, where it has been rumoured that a new Great Goblin has taken the throne, and may be preparing for war against settlements on either side of the Misty Mountains.Find Eywind and his companions in the High Pass, through the passage that runs past Pinnath Fenui and through Cirith Daur to the east.Talk to Eywind in the High Pass'As if we'd not enough to deal with here, it seems that a few of Rivendell's scouts have gone astray, somewhere east of here, up in the High Pass. A pair of them that passed through some few weeks ago said there were rumours that a new Great Goblin has arisen in Goblin-town and were going to see if they were true...and make trouble for them if they were.

'Well, there's trouble a-plenty up in these mountains without a pair of flighty Elves stirring up more, mark my words, but go they did, and we've not heard aught from them since. One of my folk went along with them -- a fellow by the name of Eywind -- so they ought to be fine.

'Now that I think about it, Eywind is a bit of a hot-head himself. He went tagging along with the others as he fancied he would find a bit more adventure up in the High Pass, as he put it, so maybe it would be best if you did go take a look and see if they're all right....''You should seek out my kinsman Eywind up in the High Pass. He and his companions should be encamped out past Pinnath Fenui, up through Cirith Daur.''Ho there friend! What brings one such as you into the frozen peaks of the High Pass?

'Vighar sent you, did he? Good fellow -- well, it turns out we're fine here. Plenty of goblins to go around and the weather's not so bad, once you get used to it.

'Now, we do have quite a few things on the docket, if you will -- that and it seems my skinny friend here has lost his fellow. Glorwen got up here a bit before us, and while she left signs of her safe arrival, we've seen naught of her since. He's a cool one, but I think he's starting to get a bit worried.'Santtu at the Lossoth outpost of Kauppa-kohta awaits a delivery of bright dust.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver bright dust from the fell spirits in the areas surrounding Kauppa-khota to Santtu, a Lossoth watching over the outpost.Wanted: Bright dust from the fell spirits surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta. Deliver the items to Santtu at Kauppa-kohta.Wanted: Bright dust from the fell spirits surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta.'This will prove helpful to the Lossoth. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Lossoth-scout Lauri at Pynti-peldot awaits the delivery of broken great swords.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver broken great swords from the Gauredain surrounding the encampment of Pynti-peldot to Lauri, a Lossoth-scout.Wanted: broken great swords from the Susi-väki surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot. Deliver the items to Lauri, one of the Lossoth-scouts.Wanted: broken great swords from the Susi-väki surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot.'These men who entreat with such beasts are an enemy of ours. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Santtu at the Lossoth outpost of Kauppa-kohta awaits a delivery of gilded sword sheaths.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver gilded sword sheaths from the sentient creatures north in the frozen valleys and in the woods surrounding Kauppa-khota to Santtu, a Lossoth watching over the outpost.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the sentient creatures to the north and west conspiring against the Lossoth. Deliver the items to Santtu at Kauppa-kohta.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the sentient creatures to the north and west conspiring against the Lossoth.'It is good to know that there are fewer enemies about to harm us now. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Lossoth-scout Lauri at Pynti-peldot awaits the delivery of pure black fur.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black fur from the beasts and worms surrounding the encampment of Pynti-peldot to Lauri, a Lossoth-scout.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts and worms surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot. Deliver the items to Lauri, one of the Lossoth-scouts at the encampment in Forochel.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts and worms surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot.'This will help our people much. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Santtu at the Lossoth outpost of Kauppa-kohta awaits a delivery of pure black skin.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black skin from the beasts in the woods surrounding Kauppa-khota to Santtu, a Lossoth watching over the outpost.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta. Deliver the items to Santtu at Kauppa-kohta.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts surrounding the outpost at Kauppa-kohta.'This helps us and all of our people. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Some of the Trév Gállorg live in Donnvail, a northern city now controlled by the Angmarim.Branan is by the gate of the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro, among the hills of Fasach-falroid.

Búdan has sent you to find a Man named Branan in the city of Donnvail in an attempt to cast off the oppressive yoke of Angmar.'You have done much for the Trév Gállorg, ${PLAYER}, and your name spreads swiftly among my people. There is little I can do to lift the yoke of Angmar from their backs, but perhaps together we can hope to ease the burden somewhat.

'The army of Angmar is fed by the farmers that live in the northern city of Donnvail, and almost every scrap of grain is given to the Orc-hordes. Our kin in Donnvail labour in secret to throw off this oppressive yoke. Travel to Donnvail and speak with a Man called Branan, near the gate. I have heard it said that he works tirelessly to undermine the army of Angmar. 

'Donnvail is north-east of Fail-á-Khro, among the hills of Fasach-falroid. Beware! The city is controlled by the Enemy, though some few remain loyal to the Trév Gállorg.''Keep your voice down. Unfriendly ears are everywhere in this city.

'It is true that there are some few in Donnvail who may be willing to strike against the rule of Angmar, but there are many too who heed still the empty promises of wealth and reward.''Seek out Branan by the gate of the city of Donnvail, among the hills of northern Fasach-falroid. I have heard that he knows of some few in Donnvail loyal to the Trév Gállorg.

'Donnvail is to the north-east of Fail-á-Khro. May fortune go with you!'Osbail had a dangerous escape from Wargs in Fasach-larran, and the encounter impressed upon her the threat the creatures pose to Aughaire.Wargs can be found in Fasach-larran, the foothills north of Aughaire. They can also be found in the fortress of Tór Gailvin, north-east of Aughaire.

Osbail has asked you to defeat the Wargs that threaten Aughaire from the north, and in return, she will tell Crannog of your efforts.Defeat Wargs in Fasach-larran (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Osbail is by Crannog's ruling-seat in Aughaire, west of the settlement's centre island.

You have defeated many of the Wargs that prowl Fasach-larran and should report of your success to Osbail.'Our chieftain is testing you, southron? If you are stout of heart, you will prove yourself worthy. If you are not, I do not lie when I say that coming here will be your greatest mistake.

'Some days past, I was surprised by a pair of Wargs among the foothills north of Aughaire. They are vicious, dangerous creatures, and my escape was made more difficult by my refusal to lead the pair by straight paths to the settlement; but this I would not do!

'But I am fleet of foot and know the land hereabout, and I made it back safely, if winded. But the Wargs are newly-arrived to Fasach-larran, and it will not be long before they make it here. Defeat these dangerous creatures! You will find them not only in Fasach-larran, but also within the fortress of Tór Gailvin, north-east of Aughaire. Whether they guard the Trév Duvárdain there or serve as jailors, I do not know, but I know they must be defeated, either way. If you do this, I will tell Crannog of your victory.''You have done very well against the Wargs, ${PLAYER}! I will tell Crannog of your victory and may he be pleased.''The Wargs of Fasach-larran are north of Aughaire, and the fortress Tór Gailvin to the north-east also holds their menace.'A warrior of the Trév Gállorg explained the need to prove yourself to his tribe.Crannog's ruling seat is in Aughaire, west of the settlement's centre island.

You have been told by a Trév Gállorg warrior to present yourself to Crannog to be charged with a Fém, a respected tribal duty.'I do not know you, stranger, and it is my Fém, my tribal duty, to know all those who come to these lands.

'If you come in friendship, and are strong in body and spirit, you have nothing to fear. Present yourself to our chieftain, Crannog, and he will find a Fém for you to perform. If you are weak or dull-minded, there is no place for you here. Travel south into the lands of the soft, or go north and find someplace to die.

'Crannog's ruling seat is here in Aughaire, west of the settlement's central island. I withhold my greeting until you have been accepted by Crannog and have a Fém of your own to perform.''If you wish to be counted a friend of the Trév Gállorg, you must present yourself to Crannog, chieftain of Aughaire. His ruling seat is west of the centre island.''You have come to Aughaire, but you are not one of us. You are of ${RACE}-kind. If you wish to be counted a friend of the Trév Gállorg, you must undertake a tribal duty, a Fém.

'I may have such a task for you.'Rona is very worried about her brother Tasgall, who travelled into Fasach-larran and has not yet returned.Tasgall left Aughaire travelling north and is likely somewhere in Fasach-larran.

Rona has agreed to give Crannog a favourable report of your doings if you render your aid to her brother Tasgall.'Crannog is waiting for my judgement of your worth? This is no small burden, and I will not make my decision lightly! But I thank him for your attention, for there is something you can do to earn my judgement.

'My brother Tasgall is a hunter of no small worth, but he has not returned from his latest foray into the wild. I ask you to travel north into Fasach-larran and search for my brother.

'Give him your aid, and if he deems you worthy, so too will I, and Crannog will hear of your deeds.''My sister sent you? She worries always, despite my assurances. But you are not one of the Trév Gállorg and do not have a Fém!

'I will help you test your strength, and perhaps my task here will go more quickly for it. The hill-beasts of Nád Nathair have proven a savage menace.''My brother Tasgall left Aughaire travelling north, and he has not returned for some days. Go to his aid, if he has need of it, and I will give Crannog my judgement of your worth!'Tasgall has tried to halt the spread of hill-beasts throughout Fasach-larran, but the creatures are too numerous for him to manage alone.Hill-beasts stalk the slopes of Nád Nathair to the north-west of Tasgall's camp-site, in Fasach-larran.

Tasgall has asked you to defeat many hill-beasts while he thinks of some way to resist them more effectively.Defeat hill-beasts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tasgall's camp-site is north of Aughaire and south-east of Nád Nathair.

You have defeated many hill-beasts and should now return to Tasgall.'I came forth from Aughaire intending to halt the spread of hill-beasts further into Fasach-larran, ${PLAYER}, but the creatures are too many.

'If you are deserving of the friendship of the Trév Gállorg, you will aid me with my charge. North-west of here, you will find a sloping path into Nád Nathair. It is a place of menace, and from there come the hill-beasts that prowl Fasach-larran.

'Defeat many of the hill-beasts you find there, and we will speak again when you return.''The slopes of Nád Nathair are north-west of here. Climb them and defeat as many hill-beasts as you can, and I will think of some way we can fight them more effectively.''You found my brother? That is good, but you must aid Tasgall in his campaign against the hill-beasts if you are to earn my recommendation to Crannog!''You have defeated a number of hill-beasts? I am glad to hear it, for now we may proceed with the next step in our hunt.'Taraghlan told you of a hunting-camp, Tyrn Lhuig, on the south-western edge of the great swamp of Malenhad.Othran is at Tyrn Lhuig, the hunting-camp in the south-western corner of Malenhad, east of Fasach-larran.

Taraghlan has asked you to bring the supplies to his kinsman Othran at the hunting camp in Malenhad.'You have done great deeds for the Trév Gállorg already, ${PLAYER}, and I know that you will do more still! Our distant kindred have not yet had the benefit of your friendship, and I grieve for them.

'They hunt their prey in the great swamp of Malenhad, east of Fasach-larran. It is a dangerous place, shaded by poisonous clouds and damp with foul water, home to strange beasts. Our hunters bring us much food and other things of worth from Malenhad, but we need to send them supplies so they can do their work.

'Bring these goods to Othran at Tyrn Lhuig, the hunting camp in the south-western corner of the swamp. You will find hunting-challenges there for yourself, if you are prepared.''Bring those supplies to Othran in Tyrn Lhuig, the hunting-camp in the south-western corner of Malenhad. The hunters will have need of them.

'The great swamp of Malenhad is east of Fasach-larran. You will find it without difficulty if you follow your nose.''You have come with supplies from Talaghlan? That is good, for ours were running short. Tyrn Lhuig has some of the finest hunters of the Trév Gállorg, but no Man can hunt without supplies.'A challenge has been set before Arzhur's clan, but a token of honour is required before the folk of Aughaire can fulfill their lawful obligations to the other clans of Angmar.The drake-lair is Nád Nathair, north of Aughaire. Nád Nathair can be recognized not only by the drakes but by the many hill-beasts in the area.

Lore-singer Arzhur has asked you to obtain a drake egg. The egg is needed as a token for the men of Aughaire to present to the other hill-clans. He has warned you to avoid the drakes themselves in seeking an egg.'We of Aughaire have many troubles. Too many, I think for Chief Crannog, who has lost heart and hope. But perhaps you might wish to aid us, as Golodir once did?

'We are trapped in a maze of clan-laws. Those who serve Angmar have tried to make it impossible for us to fulfill our obligations. If we fail, we will be subject to open warfare.

'Our first obligation is to obtain a drake's egg from Nád Nathair. We need this as a token of honour to meet the blood challenges of the other clans. Yet our hunters are mercilessly harrassed by our enemies when we approach the drakes. Will you go to the drake lair not far to the north and obtain an egg for us? The drakes are attended by many hill-beasts, so you will know the lair by their presence.

'Listen well: the drakes are fearsome foes, but they are not devoted parents. It would be best to take an egg from a nest which is not guarded by a drake.''Good. You have proven yourself worthy, friend ${PLAYER}.'Lore-singer Arzhur is satisfied that the drake egg you brought to him will suffice as the token of honour required to meet the rival clan's challenge.Aidan is waiting at Fail-á-Khro to the north-east of Aughaire.

Lore-singer Arzhur has asked you to take the drake's egg to Aidan and then to fight by his side against the challengers from the clans loyal to Angmar.Aidan is at Fail-á-Khro north-east of Aughaire.

Fight alongside Aidan against the challengers from a hostile clan loyal to Angmar.Speak with Aidan when you are ready to fight by his side.Arzhur is at Aughaire, south-west of Fail-á-Khro.

You should return to Arzhur and report of your victory against the first challenge.'Now we must take the egg you found to Aidan, our clan champion, who awaits it at Fail-á-Khro north-east of Aughaire.

'I am afraid we must ask you for another boon, friend from the south. Aidan is a strong fighter, but he is the last such of whom our clan can boast. We have many bold hunters, skilful hearth-keepers, and perhaps even a few wise lore-singers, but of warriors we have few; our foes have been biting away at our strength like dogs worrying a bone.

'The challenge Aidan must face is not a single combat. I ask that you fight by his side, for the honour of our clan, no less than for its survival.''You have done well, Southron. But the rival clans have more trouble for us.''Take the egg to Aidan and fight by his side against the challengers from the other clans.''Now it is time to fight. Let us see who comes against us!''Good. I thought no one would come with that egg. But you are to fight at my side? I do not know....

'Well, needs must. I hope you are stronger than you look, Southron!'Aslak of the Suuri-lehmä told you that the goblins have collected hoards of gems in the fort they share with the Angmarim on the north-eastern pass of Ram Dúath.The goblins of the Ram Dúath have stolen several treasures from the Suuri-lehmä.

Aslak has told you that the gems might be found in chests within the Angmarim fort to the North-east.Collect goblin-gems (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have recovered several semi-precious gems stolen from the Surri-lehmä by the goblins of the Ram Duath.

You must return them to Aslak at Lehmä-koti.'I know you little folk prize the glittering stones. We care little for most of these stones, but we treasure the kind we call Eyes of the Earth. I think in your tongue you call them cat's-eyes and moonstones.

'It seems that the goblins who serve the Angmarim in their fort just north-east of the Ram Dûath have stolen a great many of these stones, including eyes of the earth from our people.

'I would ask you to go to this fort and take back the goblin-treasures. They should be stored in chests filled with many gems. We will take the Eyes of the Earth, and you will take the rest, with our thanks.''Ah, yes, you have done it! Here... I will separate out the Eyes of the Earth, and the rest shall be yours!'The strange hill-beasts have become a serious problem of late. It seems that they are ruled by a secretive matriarch who serves the drakes. If she can be defeated, perhaps the threat of the hill-beasts will fade.Crawlers can be found in the general area not far north of Aughaire.

Hunt crawlers for their flesh and bring it back to Tasgall to be roasted.Tasgall has roasted the crawlers you brought him. You can return to him to learn the next step in his plan to defeat the Beast-mother.Speak with TasgallThe rock in the hill-beast lair is at the entrance to the valley, not far north of Aughaire.

Tasgall has given you some roasted crawler-flesh, which you should bring to a certain rock in the lairs of the hill-beasts.Having successfully defeated the Beast-mother, you should return to Tasgall's camp and speak with him.Speak with TasgallYou have defeated the vengeful Beast-lord and saved Tasgall's life. Speak with him.'These hill-beasts are growing more and more dangerous. We have heard that their leader is a matriarch of their kind, secretive and cunning, who in turn serves the drakes. If we can lure out this beast-mother and defeat her, the other hill-beasts may be cowed or routed.

'We know that these beasts greatly prize roasted crawler-flesh. I would ask you first to hunt crawlers in the area, bring their flesh back to me to be roasted, and then take the meat out to the hill-beast lairs where we may draw out this mother of the beasts.

'Beware though -- while the crawlers are no great threat, the matriarch may be a foe beyond the power of any lone warrior to overcome.''Revolting stuff, is it not? But keep in mind, if you have got nothing else, you can live off it. Anyway, roasted it is even worse than raw, but the hill-beasts seem to like it.

'Hold a few minutes while I cook up this lot.''These Crawlers have been roasted to a fine... something.

'In any case, take this vile stuff to the hill-beasts. You will find the old gate into the valley occupied by the hill-beasts and drakes. A short way into these valleys you will find a small cave within which the Beast-mother is believed to make her lair.

'Place the roasted crawlers on the great stone just within the entrance to draw the creature out. It is then that you must strike!''You must slay the beast-mother!''Ha! Well done, Southron! I guess those rumours of the Beast-lord were false then - I had half expected you to face a mated pair of the horrid beasts!

'Wait... do you hear something?''I am most grateful for your intervention. I had heard rumours from the hunters that there might be a mated pair of those horrible creatures up in the hills, but I had dismissed them as folly.

'Alas it was my own folly that drew the wrath of her mate down upon us and nearly cost us all our lives, please accept my sincerest apology and accept these tokens of my appreciation for saving my life.'Tasgall has devised a plan by which you and he will draw out Bangrath and Murragrath and end their menace.Tasgall's camp-site is north of Aughaire and south-east of Nád Nathair.

Tasgall is waiting to travel with you in search of Bangrath and Murragrath.Talk to Tasgall and travel to Nád NathairTasgall's camp-site is north of Aughaire and south-east of Nád Nathair.

You and Tasgall were successful in your hunt of Bangrath and Murragrath. He is waiting to speak with you.Rona is at the centre island of Aughaire, east of Crannog's ruling-seat.

Tasgall has been satisfied with the aid you lent him and wants you to tell his sister Rona that he will soon return to the settlement.'We have what we need to draw Bangrath and Murragrath out of hiding, ${PLAYER}. Let us travel into Nád Nathair and use this bait to put an end to their menace!

'Tell me when you are prepared to make this journey! We should try not to alert the creatures to our presence or the plan will be doomed at the onset.''Are you ready to go? We must rely on each other during this hunt, so be sure you are prepared!''The Trév Duvárdain and the Trév Gállorg were once one people, but over the years they grew apart, splintered as tribes sometimes do; there was always hope we would one day be reconciled, but that hope now is gone.

'Drostan spoke of Clúcath, a ceremonial combat that was once used to determine the ruling order of the various tribes. The last Clúcath was called many years ago, and Násan, the warrior of the Trév Gállorg, was slain. But this is not the time to speak of such things: our victory over the hill-beasts should not be tainted by such unhappy memories. You have aided me with my Fém, and that brings honour to both of us!

'Go now to my sister Rona, and tell her that I shall soon return to Aughaire. Where did you say she was? The centre island of Aughaire, east of Crannog's ruling-seat? That is where you will find her now, I expect. Go with my thanks and do not worry about Drostan's cruel taunts. His is an unworthy people.''Tasgall is safe, and you have helped bring his Fém to fruition? I am delighted that this is so!

'I will tell Crannog of this at once! You have my thanks and my good wishes, ${PLAYER}, and Tasgall's as well, I am sure of it. We of the Trév Gállorg do not forget such deeds as you have done.'Guirmán of the Trév Gállorg of Aughaire has returned from Fasach-falroid with troubling news of that land.Orcs of the Ongbúrz tribe and their Iron Crown allies can be found in and around the city of Dûn Covád, far to the north of Aughaire, in the western hills.

Guirmán has asked you to combat the foes of the Trév Gállorg that can be found in Fasach-falroid.Defeat Ongbúrz Orcs in Fasach-falroid (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Angmarim in Fasach-falroid (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Guirmán is at the Búth Luikh of Aughaire, far to the south of Dûn Covád.

You have defeated many Ongbúrz Orcs and their Iron Crown allies and should return now to Guirmán with word of your victories.'We of the Trév Gállorg have long maintained our watch upon the northern plains of Fasach-falroid, but we are too few in these trying times to guard the whole expanse.

'We have seen enough to know what we face: a union of the Orcs of the Ongbúrz tribe and the Angmarim. Separately, they are dangerous, but together, fighting for a common master, I fear they may be unstoppable.

'You must put an end to their alliance, ${PLAYER}. Travel north into Fasach-falroid and defeat Orcs of the Ongbúrz clan and their Iron Crown allies. The city of Dûn Covád, far to the north in the western hills, will surely have many of these foes for you to strike.''I greet you in your time of triumph, ${PLAYER}. The Orcs and the Angmarim have been taught that their union will not be permitted. For that, I thank you.''Orcs of the Ongbúrz tribe and their Iron Crown allies can be found in Fasach-falroid, far to the north of Aughaire. If you are able to brave the city of Dûn Covád, in the western hills, you will find many of these foes.'Malenhad, the vile brimstone swamp of Angmar, is rife with vile and disgusting creatures, but even the strangest of these creatures has its uses to the Hillmen of Angmar.Swamp-lurkers and swamp-flies can be found throughout the swamp near the hunters' camp.

Othran has asked you to collect Swamp-lurker legs and swamp-fly wings, which his people use to their benefit.'There are many strange beasts in this swamp. Vile creatures abound in this place -- perhaps the evil air fosters their growth -- but even the vilest of vermin has its use. 

'Swamp-fly wings are light, flexible, and tough and can be used for many crafting tasks. Swamp-lurkers are creatures of a truly strange nature, but for all their odd appearance, their legs make a tasty meal when roasted.

'Hunt these verminous beasts, and I will pay well for these trophies. You can find these creatures scattered throughout this part of the swamp.''Good, good. In Malenhad, we must make use of any beast we find.'Machan the hunter has gone missing after vowing to return with hides from the fearsome worms of Malenhad.Machan the hunter should be somewhere in the region of Sálgaitë to the south-east.

The hunter Murdaigán has asked you to locate Machan, who was hunting fire-worms in Malenhad.Find the hunter Machan'Machan is a bold hunter. Perhaps too bold. He vowed to return with the hides of fire-worms from their lairs in Sálgaitë to the south-east, and he has not returned in good time.

'Would you look for Machan, Southron? It may be he is in need of assistance. I will reward you for your efforts....'Afwald, the provisioner at the dwarf-ruin of Hrimbarg, awaits the return of broken great swords.You have accepted a task to deliver broken great swords from the Gauredain and goblins found in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains to Afwald. Afwald can be found within the ruins at Hrimbarg, also found in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Broken great swords from the Gauredain and goblins in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains. You can deliver these items to Afwald within the ruins at Hrimbarg in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Broken great swords from the Gauredain and goblins in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains.'Good to know that their number is dwindling every time that you step out into the wild, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Afwald, the provisioner at the dwarf-ruin of Hrimbarg, awaits the return of pure black fur.You have accepted a task to deliver pure black fur from the beasts found in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains to Afwald. Afwald can be found within the ruins at Hrimbarg, also found in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains. You can deliver these items to Afwald within the ruins at Hrimbarg in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains.'Supplies are supplies at the moment, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tasgall has come up with a plan by which the threat of the hill-beasts may be greatly lessened.Crawlers can be found throughout Fasach-larran.

Tasgall has asked you collect remains from the crawlers of Fasach-larran so he may create bait to lure out Bangrath and Murragrath.Collect crawler remains (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tasgall's camp-site is north of Aughaire and south-east of Nád Nathair.

You have collected the remains of several crawlers from Fasach-larran, and should bring them now to Tasgall.'The hill-beasts we are hunting in Fasach-larran are bred of two mighty creatures, Bangrath and Murragrath, too aged and cautious to be caught unawares. But that is precisely what we must do!

'Hill-beasts thrive on the large crawlers that infest Fasach-larran. With enough we might be able to lure Bangrath and Murragrath out of hiding.

'I care not where the crawlers come from, but there are many of them throughout Fasach-larran. Kill crawlers wherever you find them and bring their remains to me. I will prepare a mixture that may seem repulsive to you and me, but will be hard for our foes to resist.''Crawlers can be found all throughout Fasach-larran, ${PLAYER}. Defeat the creatures and bring me their remains so I can prepare bait to lure Bangrath and Murragrath out of hiding.''The remains of these crawlers are not pleasant to smell or look upon, but trust me when I say they are a delicacy to the creatures we hunt. I will prepare bait from this with which we can draw Bangrath and Murragrath out of hiding.'Raith has petitioned the chieftain of Aughaire for greater action against the Trév Duvárdain, and Crannog has recommended that you prove yourself by facing the dangers of Tór Gailvin.The fortress of Tór Gailvin is north-east of Aughaire, and its defenders carry the skull-amulets and bloodstone-pendants that Raith wants.

Raith of Aughaire has asked you to wrest signs of the loyalty of the Trév Duvárdain to Angmar from the defenders of Tór Gailvin.'The Trév Duvárdain press us at every opportunity, ${PLAYER}. We must oppose them with all our strength, but still Crannog insists on caution. Caution!

'The Trév Duvárdain serve the commanders of Angmar now, and they carry their tokens. In return for their bondage, the Trév Duvárdain have been given the fortress-city of Tór Gailvin. We must take these tokens from them and show that the Trév Gállorg will not stand for such betrayal!

'The fortress of Tór Gailvin stands to the north-east of Aughaire. Travel there and wrest skull-amulets and bloodstone-pendants from the traitors who now live in that place. Return to me with these tokens, and I will tell Crannog of your efforts on behalf of the Trév Gállorg.''The evil lords of Angmar must be very displeased now with the Trév Duvárdain that were given Tór Gailvin, for you have bloodied their noses with your attack!

'I will tell Crannog of your success, and he will have no doubt of it: these amulets and pendants sing of your victory!'Malenhad has long been a place of poisonous waters and vapours, corrupted by the powers of ancient Angmar during the reign of the Witch-king. There is little of value left there.The banner-marked pools are spread throughout western Malenhad.

Murdaigán has asked you to go to six springs in the swamp and collect samples of water which he can use to confirm whether those springs have not yet been poisoned by the filth of Angmar.Collect water at the banner-marked pools (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Murdaigán is back at the hunters' camp in the south-west corner of Malenhad.

Having collected several water samples, you should bring them back to Murdaigán.'There is much water in Angmar, but most of it is foul, poisoned, and deadly to drink. Even the rare springs of pure water can sometimes turn foul, when some old poison leeches from the soil.

'Of course, we must know which water is pure and which foul, but it is a deadly game to tell merely by taste. Instead we collect water from various springs and feed it to swamp-fly grubs. These creatures will sicken and die if they are given foul water.

'Banners have been placed at six of the shallow springs of the swamp where once fresh water could be found. Take these water-skins, find the banners, and collect water from the six springs, so that we may learn if the springs still run true.''Well done. I trust you remember which skin came from which spring? Very good, ${PLAYER}.'Nogmeldir seeks to learn more about the Tempest Warg-pack to the east in order to find some way to discover the whereabouts of the Elf-scout Glorwen.Eywind is at his camp with Nogmeldir, at the entrance to the High Pass.

Nogmeldir suggested you speak with Eywind concerning the Wargs and what the dwarves know of them.The dwarf brothers are said to dwell at Hrimbarg, to the east and a bit south of Vindurhal.

Eywind suggested you find the dwarf-brothers he spoke of and ask them what they know of the Wargs of the Misty Mountains.Nogmeldir is at Vindurhal, a little north and to the west of Hrimbarg.

You should return to Nogmeldir and tell him what you have learned of the Wargs.Talk to Nogmeldir'With your discovery of Glorwen's bow, we are left with little but dire tidings in the search for her, but at least you have not come upon her gnawed bones, so we can be thankful for that.

'Unfortunately, few Wargs are inclined or even able to speak the tongues of the Free Peoples, and I am afraid my own experience with their kind can be summed up entirely on the point of a yew shaft, so I have little insight to offer. I do not know how we might learn more of her fate from them.

'Eywind's people lived up here for many years before they were driven out, and some few still linger. Perhaps he could offer more insight than I?''You should speak with Eywind...he may know more about the Wargs.''Well, while it is true that I've fought and slain many a Warg in my day, I have had little cause to speak with them beforehand.

'But if my memory serves me a-right, there is a pair of brothers who have lived up in these mountains for quite some time as hunters and trappers. If anyone might have learned something about that pack, it would be them.

'The last I heard, they were holed up in the ruins of Hrimbarg -- an old dwarf-place to the east and a bit south of here -- still pursuing their trade. If you can find them -- and they still live -- they might be able to tell you more.''The brothers at Hrimbarg deal with these beasts on a regular basis...they may know more than I.''What do you want? Something about that pack up there to the north? I used to hunt the aurochs up that way, before I moved into the worm-scale business, and I learned a fair bit about the Wargs in order to stay alive. If you want to find one that will talk, that would be Blackfang, the pack-leader. A few others might know the tongue of Men, but they won't talk to you. It will be him that speaks for the pack and strikes bargains with the goblins -- but he won't speak to you either...unless you were to call him out.

'You see, Wargs are big on strength and survival of the fittest and all that -- so while there is no way a pack would ever accept YOU as a leader, they would still look to the pack-leader to kill you if you laid down a challenge to his leadership, just to prove his strength. If he backed down or refused, he would lose face -- and for a Warg, that can be fatal.

'The custom of challenge varies from pack to pack, but in this one it's simple. There is an old goblin totem in the middle of the ruins they live in -- you just go stand next to that for a few minutes and that signals you want a fight. Blackfang will have to come out and face you sooner or later or lose face with his pack. Of course, he's the pack-leader because he IS the strongest one up there, so you take care or he will tear you apart.''You should return to your friends at Vindurhal, ${PLAYER}. I have told you what I know.''So, this Thorgest told you of a means to pose a challenge to the leader of the Warg-pack?

'It sounds to me like a novel means of having one's self ripped limb from limb by ravening Wargs, but if there is some chance that we might be able to learn something of Glorwen's whereabouts from this Blackfang, then perhaps it is worth the risk!'Rodakhan the hunter has said he will pay well for worm-hides. However, worms are some of the most dangerous beasts of the swamp.Flame-worms can be found in the south-east part of Malenhad. Rodakhan has asked you to collect flame-worm hides for him and can be found in the camp of Tyrn Lhuig.'Hold... I would have a moment of your time. You seem to be capable of looking after yourself in this harsh land. We hunt the beasts of the swamp, and our most valuable and dangerous prey are the fire-worms.

'Their hides are extremely tough, proof against both water and fire, and can be worked into armour, clothes, and all manner of useful things. If you should happen to collect any hides of the local worms, please bring them here, and I will pay you well.

'But then, you are a southron... you may not have the courage or prowess it will take.''Ha! This is good. We will both profit. Here are your coins.'Defeat Worms in the swamps and caves of Angmar.Defeat Worms in AngmarDefeat many Worms in the swamps and caves of Angmar.Defeat many Worms in AngmarUna maintains her vigilance within Donnvail and wants you to take a message out of the city.Branan is by the front-gate of the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Una has told you that your allies can aid her in the struggle against Angmar, but if your allies are not near at hand, you can still aid her by bringing a message to Branan.Crannog is at his ruling-seat west of the central island in Aughaire, south of Fail-á-Khro.

Una and Branan have come to the conclusion that the time to rise against their Angmarim masters has almost arrived, and Crannog should be petitioned for aid in the effort.'There is great work to be done in Donnvail, ${PLAYER}, and we have need of many allies if we are to carry it out. Artagan, Raghnaid, and Enan have made plans to combat the evil masters that control the city, but it will take many hands and great courage.

'But all can contribute. If your allies are not near at hand, you can still aid our efforts here; bring my message to Branan at the front gate of Donnvail: tell him that we will soon move against our Angmarim masters and all must be made ready!

'We will be here when you return with allies, ${PLAYER}, but until that time you can still aid us; take my message to Branan.''Una plans to move against our Angmarim masters? We need many allies, ${PLAYER}! Travel south to Aughaire and tell Crannog of our need!''You bring word from our kindred in Donnvail, ${PLAYER}? That they will soon move against the lords that control them is no surprise to me; the Trév Gállorg will not bend forever and will strike back before they break.

'I will send all who can to await the signal, and they will move against Donnvail when the time comes. I urge you to gather allies and go to Una's aid, as well. One ${RACE} will not be enough to defeat the foes arrayed against them, even one so worthy as you.''Speak with Crannog, ${PLAYER}, far to the south in Aughaire. He must be told of our need for allies.''Branan is at the front-gate of Donnvail. Tell him that we will move against our Angmarim masters as soon as we have allies enough!'Lagordal's suspicions haven't been fully confirmed, but answers may lay hidden in an old Dúnadan cave.Lagordal has asked you to investigate the Dúnadan cave in the hills to the north-west.Find the Dúnadan caveSomeone stands in your way, blocking the entrance of the Dúnadan cave.

You should talk to him.Talk to the stranger at the Dúnadan cave'That Dunlending acted alone; his ambush might not have had anything to do with the camp-site. Still, those footprints you saw belonged to multiple people, correct? They must be the same people holed up in that old Dúnadan cave.

'Perhaps it's time I told you what led to my injury. I had been tasked with checking the cave's condition. Alas, I did not find it empty -- the cave's new occupants decided to greet me with an arrow.

'I have reason to believe these people are not to be trusted. Find out why they are here, and be careful when you go. You'll find the cave to the north-west, on the other side of this hill.

'There will be no secrets in the Angle if I have anything to say about it!''Stay your hand -- I mean no harm! I saw what you did earlier at that camp-site; you defeated that spy faster than I could get there! But you're no Ranger of the North. What purpose do you have in the Angle?'

You tell the stranger about how you're investigating the presence of "unsavoury folks" in the Angle.

'Indeed? Then I believe we may share a common goal.'A wolverine jumped out at Mainio while he was out at the lake.The snarling wolverine is on a lakeside cliff, off the north-west road from Kauppa-kohta.

A snarling wolverine jumped out at Mainio and scared him. He asked you to investigate and make sure the wolverines are not a threat.Defeat the snarling wolverineMainio is at Kauppa-kohta, south of the road.

You should return with news of your success against the snarling wolverine.Talk to Mainio'My mother told me that you gathered supplies for the kelkka, we are so happy to have your help.

'I am actually a little frightened, too. A horrible wolverine jumped out at me while I was making my way around the lake! It was big and ugly...I thought for a moment it would surely swallow me whole.  

'Maybe I ask too much of you, Sivullinen, but if you could check along the north road for me and be sure the wolverines have not found something they are protecting, I would be able to walk the lakeshore without a care!''I knew they had found something, but something as nice as a bird's nest I would not have suspected.

'It was small you say? You must be a mighty hero indeed to mock such a menace!

'Thank you again. Tomorrow I will make haste for the shore.'The Trév Gállorg gather herbs from the low-lands with which they treat wounds and season their food.Spiky mugwort, silverbell, and red lovage can be found in the lands around Fail-á-Khro and the northern city of Donnvail: spiky mugwort near the ponds south-west of Fail-á-Khro; red lovage by the farms outside Donnvail; and silverbell by the fortress of Tór Gailvin, to the east.

Cána has asked you to gather some herbs so she can make a poultice to heal the hurts of those injured in the battles sure to come.Collect red lovage (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect silverbell (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect spiky mugwort (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cána is at Fail-á-Khro, north of Aughaire along the road.

Cána has asked you to bring her the herbs she needs to make a healing poultice for her people.'You fought bravely in Clúcath, ${PLAYER}, but the Trév Duvárdain will not stop harrying us. We should have expected it: they will not obey the ancient rites, and their Angmarim masters push them still against us.

'With many battles ahead, we need to be ready to care for our wounded. I can make a poultice that will be of great aid to the healers in the Búth Sánkhas and the Búth Luikh. Pick the following herbs from the surrounding hills, and in the lands around the city of Donnvail, to the north: red lovage by the farms of Donnvail, spiky mugwort near the lakes to the south-west, and silverbell by the fortress of Tór Gailvin, to the east.

'Not all battles are fought with weapons, friend ${RACE}; some are won by the healing arts.''You will find the herbs I require growing on the hillsides near Fail-á-Khro and by the city of Donnvail, to the north: red lovage by the farms of Donnvail, silverbell by the fortress of Tór Gailvin, and spiky mugwort near the ponds to the south-west.''These herbs will do very well, ${PLAYER}. I can make a healing poultice from this with little difficulty.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

A Shield-maiden's Song was written many long years ago and tells the tale of a Shield-maiden of Rohan. While the Shield-maidens of Rohan were rarely called to war, they were nevertheless as well-trained and versed in the arts of battle as their male counterparts. This book tells the tale of one such Maiden caught in unexpected battle, who in striving to defend her Lord against a great onslaught, sang out in clearest tones a stirring song of war as she fought. Such was the power of her voice that even the Orcs whom she faced saw her not as a frail woman, but as a great warrior to be feared.

Alas, you have no idea how the story ends and several other details beside seem to be missing from this copy of the book, which has been damaged by time and neglect. Perhaps there is someone at Rivendell who would be able to make use of it.This book, though incomplete, may be of interest to someone learning the ways of the guardian. Perhaps there is someone at Rivendell who would be able to make use of it.Talk to Samwise Gamgee in RivendellThe remaining pages of A Shield-maiden's Song may be found among the blasted terrain of Angmar or among the snowy peaks of the Misty Mountains.A Shield-maiden's Song, page 1A Shield-maiden's Song, page 8A Shield-maiden's Song, page 11A Shield-maiden's Song, page 12A Shield-maiden's Song, page 15A Shield-maiden's Song, page 19A Shield-maiden's Song, page 25A Shield-maiden's Song, page 26'What is that you've got there? A Shield-maiden's Song? Why yes, indeed, that's one of the books that Master Elrond himself suggested to me, though I'm afraid his own copy went missing or was loaned out who-knows how many years ago. He's got so many books around here it's beyond me how he keeps track of them at all. Anyways, Master Elrond says that there aren't supposed to be many written copies of it, as the Horse-lords prefer to hand their tales down through their story-tellers and such.

'Oh dear, it does seem that this one is rather the worse for wear, doesn't it? What good is a tale like this without an ending -- or several bits of the beginning and middle as well, if you want to know the truth.

'Well, I still have a great deal to study before we leave, and Elrond is a bit strict about my time, so I shan't waste it trying to piece this one together. On the other hand, if you could find those missing pages, I rather expect it'd be worth your time. Elrond hinted to me that an aspiring guardian could do far worse than to find a full copy of that book.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

This curious manual seems to describe the nature of your family's ability to take the shape of animals. It is a description of skin-changing over the years, written long ago by someone with very little actual understanding of the ability. You wonder if there is any truth hidden within its exaggerated and mostly-untrue account.

Several pages are missing, but a small, much more recent, bit of parchment tucked into the front cover contains the inscription 'Ask Bilbo.'This curious manual seems to describe the nature of your family's ability to take the shape of animals. It is a description of skin-changing over the years, written long ago by someone with very little actual understanding of the ability. You wonder if there is any truth hidden within its exaggerated and mostly-untrue account.

Several pages are missing, but a small, much more recent, bit of parchment tucked into the front cover contains the inscription 'Ask Bilbo.' Is this a reference to Bilbo Baggins, currently residing in Rivendell?Talk to Bilbo Baggins at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 4A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 8A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 15A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 16A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 23A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 42A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 11A Study of the Skin-changer, Page 21Bilbo takes the book from you with scholarly interest and studies it briefly.

'This is an interesting find, ${PLAYER}, but it does not appear to be very... how can I put this? It does not seem to me that a great deal of factual study went into its preparation. I wonder who wrote it, and who placed this parchment within its pages? Did this mysterious author wish to consult with me on the nature of your family's power? I am hardly an expert, and only met Beorn on occasion.

'You should collect the missing pages. I do not think you will learn anything truthful, but you might derive some amusement from the author's interpretation and extrapolation of your family's nature.'A Dunlending named Cariad has offered to work together, citing a common goal.Cariad has suggested clearing craban spies from the wilderness north-east of the Cuan-lûth refuge.Defeat craban spies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cariad is waiting for you at the entrance of the Cuan-lûth refuge.

You should talk to him there.Talk to Cariad'I am Cariad. I'll spare you the details, but I've spotted numerous craban spies belonging to the White Hand in the wilderness north-east of here. Why don't you help me defeat a few of them? I promise I'll answer your questions afterwards.

'Oh, I'm no fool -- I don't quite trust you yet. Consider it a wager.''Well done. You deserve some of the truth now: that campsite that you investigated was ours. My clan has avoided Saruman's spies thus far, but they know we must be close.

'If the Rangers have also noticed our activity, then someone will find us eventually. I'd like for this to happen on my people's terms.'A roar echoes from the deeper caverns. A great and fearsome worm must reside within.A fearsome worm is deep within the caves.Enter the cavesA great and fearsome worm must reside deeper within these caves.

Find the worm deep in the caves.Defeat the fearsome worm in the cavesThorgest's dwelling is to the north-west of the worm-caves.

You should return to Thorgest and inform him that Ruinuan is dead, slain by your hands.Talk to ThorgestA roar echoes from the deeper caverns. A great and fearsome worm must reside within.

Find the worm deep in the caves.'So...it is done at last, and my brother's memory is avenged. Thank you, ${PLAYER}. It will be far easier to go on with business knowing that my debt to Thorkell no longer weighs so heavily upon my chest.

'What to do now, though? These mountains have grown ever more dangerous in these past few years, and Afwald is not the adventurous sort to join me on the hunt. Perhaps I will need to find a new partner, or give up the hunt at last?

'I've no patience for the comforts of civilization anymore -- I think I will stay here and take my chances with worm and wolf-man.'You may be the key to Cariad's plan.Cariad wishes to introduce you to Erlys, the woman who leads this group of Dunlending refugees.

He waits in front of the Cuan-lûth Refuge.Talk to CariadCariad waits for you in front of the Cuan-lûth Refuge.Talk to Cariad'Am I right to assume the Rangers consider you a friend? Perhaps you could help me then.

'We'd stand a better chance against Saruman with the Rangers' help. I am... hesitant to approach them alone, but with you, this mad plan of mine might be feasible.

'I should introduce you to Erlys; she has led our people out of Dunland with her wisdom and cunning. No doubt she will have her own thoughts about this.''My clan may not trust you, but you have been a staunch ally thus far. Would you come with me to Thelgarth?''Come, let's go inside.'

<rgb=#FF0000>WARNING: This is a solo instance and cannot be completed in a fellowship.</rgb>The Lossoth hunters at Kauppa-kohta practice their hunting skills on the Wargs they capture.The Warg is being held captive in the northern part of Kauppa-kohta.

Valtteri, the Lossoth Hunt-leader, has instructed you to defeat the Warg his party has captured to earn the right to hunt with the Lossoth.Defeat the Caged Red-FangValtteri is in the northern part of Kauppa-kohta, the Lossoth trading post.

You should speak with Valtteri again, now that you have defeated the Warg.Talk to Valtteri'Ha! Someone of your stature would make a great hunter...the Surmâja would never attack such light fare!

'If you ever hope to hunt with us, carrying our quivers, you will prove that you can stare the kill down and not flee at the first sight of a cruel beast like a Surmâja. I hope your luistin are cared for well...when I awaken this beast, you will no doubt be wearing them out!

'Slay the Surmâja, and you will earn the right to hunt with the Lumi-väki.''It is dead? How can that...of course, in jest did I say the Surmâja would be difficult to defeat. Why even a child could have killed such a scraggly runt.''Let us see if you can match even a lowly Surmâja like this one.'The Warg's eyes suddenly snap open as it springs up onto all fours, ready to strike.Nekhtan was upset that you were chosen to engage in Clúcath against the Trév Duvárdain, seeing it as the loss of his chance for vengeance.Raith is at Crannog's ruling-seat, west of the centre island of Aughaire.

Nekhtan has told you that he is satisfied: you have proven to be worthy of taking his place in Clúcath against the Trév Duvárdain.'What do you want, ${PLAYER}? Have you returned to gloat that you were chosen as the warrior to fight in Clúcath against the Trév Duvárdain? The vengeance for which I have trained so long has been stolen from me!

'I am sorry. I see the wisdom in it, though it be hard. It is the way of my people that the strongest warrior should take the heaviest load, and you have proven yourself the master. I must put the needs of the Trév Gállorg before my own vengeance.

'Tell Raith, at Crannog's ruling-seat, that I am at ease. Go forth to the Clúcath with my blessing, friend ${RACE}.''You have taken it upon yourself to make peace with Nekhtan? That is good, ${PLAYER}. I know now what I only believed before: Crannog was right to give you this Fém. You truly are a friend to the Trév Gállorg.''I have set my anger aside, ${PLAYER}. I no longer have any ill will toward you. Give this news to Raith, that I am satisfied with Crannog's choice of warrior for the Clúcath.

'You will find Raith at Crannog's ruling-seat in Aughaire.'Fonn has learned that Gertheryg have been seen travelling to a particular cave in Malenhad. He has sent a scout to investigate, but the scout has not returned.The Gorthorog-cave is in Malenhad, east of Aughaire.

Fonn has asked you to explore a cave in Malenhad to which Gertheryg have been seen travelling.Find Bethad in the Gorthorog-caveBethad is in the Gorthorog-cave in Malenhad, east of Aughaire.

At Fonn's request, you searched the Gorthorog-cave for Bethad. You have found him, but he appears wounded.'Gertheryg are some of Angmar's most terrible minions. Great troll-like creatures, they are cunning, skilled with arms and armour, and are not affected by the light of the Sun as other trolls are.

'We have observed parties of Gertheryg moving back and forth from a worm-infested cave in the vile Malenhad. I sent a scout, Bethad, to find out what he could of the place, but he has not returned, and I fear the worst.

'Please go and explore the cave, perhaps you can find Bethad within. I fear that it would be most unwise to explore this cave alone, so you should go with what allies you can gather.''You are a friend of Fonn? It is well that you have found me, for I need your help!''I fear that something has become of Bethad in that Gorthorog-cave. I would ask that you go there and search for him.'Fonn has learned that Gertheryg have been seen travelling to the cavern of Ologfil in Malenhad. He has sent a scout to investigate, but the scout has not returned.Fonn has asked you to explore the caves of Ologfil in Malenhad to the north of Tyrn Lhuig, to determine the fate of Bethad who was investigating the presence of gertheryg.Find Bethad in OlogfilSpeak with Fonn in Tyrn Lhuig and tell him of Bethad's rescue.Speak with Fonn in Tyrn Lhuig'Gertheryg are some of Angmar's most terrible minions. Great troll-like creatures, they are cunning, skilled with arms and armour, and are not affected by the light of the Sun as other trolls are.

'We have observed parties of gertheryg moving back and forth from a worm-infested cave named Ologfil in the vile Malenhad. I sent a scout, Bethad, to find out what he could of the place, but he has not returned, and I fear the worst.

'Please go and explore the cave, perhaps you can find Bethad within. I fear that it would be most unwise to explore this cave alone, so you should go with what allies you can gather.''I fear that something has become of Bethad in Ologfil. I would ask that you go there and search for him.''So Bethad was captured by a gorthorog captain? His lack of caution is far from commendable, but it is good to know that he is safe now. My thanks to you foreigner.'Master your understanding of battle and the space in which it takes place so that you can end battles more swiftly in the future.Strike with Battle Acuity or Enhanced Battle AcuityMaster your understanding of battle and the space in which it takes place so that you can end battles more swiftly in the future.Use Champion's Duel, Fury of Blades, or UnbreakableCuinthorn told you of the great drake Bloodwing and tasked you with defeating it.Bloodwing rules her brood of drakes from the summit of the cliffs of Nád Nathair, above the lower slopes where hill-beasts roam. Nád Nathair is north of Aughaire, in Fasach-larran.

Cuinthorn told you of Bloodwing, a mighty drake who has claimed the lives of many great heroes, and wondered if you and your allies would be the ones to end her reign.Defeat drakes in Nád Nathair (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cuinthorn is in the Búth Sánkhas of Aughaire, south-east of the market.

Cuinthorn is waiting to hear of your success against Bloodwing and her brood of drakes.'This, the Búth Sánkhas, has witnessed many tales, both tragic and triumphant, for the songs of the Trév Gállorg record all things. Many of my people have perished while testing their bravery and seeking to leave a glorious legacy, and their stories are passed from one to another here in the Búth Sánkhas.

'Bloodwing, the mightiest of the drakes of Nád Nathair, has claimed the lives of many such glory-seekers. She perches atop the great cliffs to the north of Aughaire and rules her brood of drakes with sharp teeth and cruel talons.

'The tale will be great indeed if you and your allies can best Bloodwing and her brood. Travel north to Nád Nathair and climb past the lower slopes where hill-beasts roam and seek out Bloodwing atop the cliffs. She is a mighty foe, not to be challenged lightly. Your tale will be the better for it.''Bloodwing is defeated? You have succeeded where many of my people have failed, ${PLAYER}! Crannog was right to call you friend of the Trév Gállorg, for you have brought us much good, and this only the latest!

'This reward was set aside for the one who would defeat Bloodwing, though few believed it could happen. I give it now to you.''Bloodwing rules her brood from high atop the cliffs of Nád Nathair, north of Aughaire in Fasach-larran. Climb past the slopes where hill-beasts roam, and you will come to the drake's nest.'A trained falcon arrived at Rivendell, bearing a troubling message from the hidden Ranger-camp at Esteldín for Aragorn, Chieftain of the Rangers.Esteldín lies in the North Downs, far to the north and west of Rivendell.

Aragorn has asked you to travel to Esteldín in his stead to speak with Daervunn, whom he hopes can shed light on the troubling message.'Hail and well met, ${PLAYER}. It is well that you have come. This morning, a falcon arrived, bearing a message for me from Esteldín, the hidden refuge of my people in the North Downs. It would appear that another such bird came to Esteldín from the North, gravely wounded, bearing a message from one of my kinsmen we had believed long dead.

'I know little more, for the message to me was brief, but Daervunn has asked me to come at once. My time here is short, and I cannot return now to confer with my people. My path lies to the south and to the east. Will you go to Esteldín in my stead and learn what tidings have come there? Speak to Daervunn, for it was he who sent the message to me.

'I fear that the news is grave, for Corunir was deemed lost years ago when his captain, Golodir, disobeyed my edict and took a company north into Angmar. They were never heard from again. It is a joy that Corunir lives, but what kept him from contacting us before now?''Welcome, ${PLAYER}! Did Aragorn not come with you? Alas, but that is fell news. Well, there is naught for it now. You will have to do.

'Are you prepared for a long, dark journey? It is through Ram Dúath into Angmar that I must send you.''Why do you tarry here? Go to Esteldín in the North Downs and confer with my kinsman Daervunn.'Daervunn told you of how a wounded falcon arrived, bearing a message from Corunir, a Ranger from the company of Golodir, who defied Aragorn's commands and passed into Angmar, believing that a shadow had come again unto Carn Dûm.Corunir is at Aughaire, a Hillman-village in Angmar, somewhere north of Ram Dúath.

Daervunn besought you to pass north through Ram Dúath and seek out the Ranger Corunir, who dwells now among the Hillmen of Angmar.'Perhaps I should start from the beginning. Many years ago, one of our captains -- a man by the name of Golodir -- desired to enter the realm of Angmar, for he had heard rumour that the descendants of the ancient Hillmen of Rhudaur had made for themselves a home there and began again to multiply. He suspected also that a shadow had come again unto Carn Dûm, for fell creatures began to spread southward into the North Downs.

'Aragorn forbade any to go there, for that land still had an ill-name, though it has been barren for hundreds of years. Nevertheless, Golodir gathered those who would go with him and passed out of our memory -- until a few weeks ago. A wounded falcon arrived, bearing a message from Corunir, one of Golodir's company, stating that he has been dwelling at a place called Aughaire, a village of the Hillmen somewhere north of Ram Dúath.

'Will you go there and speak with Corunir? The urgency of his message makes me fear what has come to pass. I deem we have not yet seen the darkest times.''I cannot give you better guidance than to say seek out Aughaire. But for Golodir's company, my kindred have not passed north beyond Ram Dúath.''Who are you? Nay, it matters not... all that matters is that you have come. Matters are grave, and we have need of any assistance we can muster. These good people are beset on all sides by the tyrants of Angmar, who now gather a mighty army to march south and crush the Elves and Gondor between their hammer and the anvil of Mordor. Time grows short, and we must act quickly if we are to hinder the Shadow of Angmar.

'Perhaps I should tell you somewhat of our long story. When we first came unto Aughaire, the Hillmen were suspicious of us, but not overtly hostile. Golodir quickly earned their respect, for he was a great captain among us and was able to inspire the heart. We learned that the Hillmen were under the oppression of a remnant of Angmar that had returned to Carn Dûm from over the Misty Mountains... but worse, a sorcerer much likened to the Witch-king of old had also come to the evil city.

'The Hillmen called this sorcerer "The False King", for they knew well that the Witch-king himself could never return to Angmar. Golodir determined that this Steward of Angmar had to be stopped before he could gather strength in the North. He gathered together as many of the Hillmen as would follow him and journeyed east with the rest of our companions, believing that Angmar was not yet strong enough to withstand a united front. I was left behind to take word to Esteldín in the event the expedition failed... which it did. Do you wish to hear more?'Corunir told you of how Golodir raised up an army from among the Hillmen to assail Carn Dûm. The expedition failed, and Corunir was left alone, unable to pass Rammas Deluon -- now possessed of a terrible power -- to learn the fate of his captain and companions.Lesser watching-stones litter the landscape of western Angmar, especially near Angmarim and Hillman encampments.

Corunir believes that the secret of Rammas Deluon can be revealed by investigating one of the lesser watching-stones. He has instructed you to find one of the lesser stones and take a rubbing of the runes upon it. He warned you to avoid fell and deadly watching-stones.Corunir is at the Hillman-village of Aughaire.

With the rubbings Corunir required in hand, you should return to the Ranger with your report.'So, you would know how this tragedy came to be? In truth, I know not. I do not know whether it was through some machination of the False King, or whether it was purely an evil chance, but our march was brought to an abrupt end. As we marched east, we found ourselves confronted by a vale filled with terrible statues. Rammas Deluon, the Hillmen called it. That some spirit of evil vigilance abode in the stones there was no doubt, for we could all feel the oppression of them. It was here that Golodir bade me remain.

'As the host passed through the stones, it became clear why the Hillmen feared the valley so. When the last few men crossed Rammas Deluon, the watching-stones threw forth a terrible malaise of oppression, such that those men caught within it were stricken dead by the weight of it upon their hearts. I tried to pass the stones, but could not. Golodir bid me remain, and so I did.

'Days turned to months, then years, and still Golodir did not return. I knew I should have returned at once to Esteldín, but I could not abandon my captain. I tried vainly to cross Rammas Deluon, until my very spirit was broken within me. At last, I sent the message to Esteldín, which brought you hither.

'We must learn the secret of Rammas Deluon, ${PLAYER}! Perhaps one of the many lesser stones which litter this land -- especially near the camps of the Angmarim and Hillmen -- will reveal that secret. Go to one of the lesser watching-stones and take a rubbing of the runes found upon its base. Beware the fell and deadly stones, for they have too terrible a power.''Time is of the essence, ${PLAYER}. Find one of the lesser stones which litter the landscape and take a rubbing of the runes upon it.''You have returned! Did you bring the rubbing? Good! Let me see it....'The shadow of Angmar -- Sauron's hand in the North -- grows steadily, threatening the peace and security of Eriador.Aragorn waits in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Elrond warned you of a growing evil within Angmar and asked you to speak with Aragorn.Talk to Aragorn in Rivendell'I would wish you to remain here away from the troubles of the world. But alas, the Enemy does not rest and so neither can we.

'Angmar grows in power with each passing day and without a means to stem its evil, we have no hope of winning this war.

'Speak with Aragorn. He waits for you in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.''The time has come for you to leave this place of refuge. Go now, and speak to Aragorn in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.''With matters in hand here, it is time to turn our attention to the north.'You gathered the necessary supplies for the Lossoth sled-builders, but they still need salt to cure the hides.Ofráth can be found along the road south of Kauppa-kohta.

Eija has asked you to go to the dwarves and trade a set of antlers for a satchel of salt to help in the hide-curing process.The curing barrel is next to Eija in Kauppa-kohta's shoreline sled-building camp.

Now that you have obtained a satchel of salt from the dwarves, you should return to Eija and place it in the curing barrel.Eija is in Kauppa-kohta's sled-building camp, at the lake's eastern shore.

Having started the curing process for the rawhide, you should speak with Eija once more.'Now that we have all of our raw materials, we are ready to build the sleds. However, our rawhide will never hold the wood together if it is not cured first. Curing the rawhide dries it and makes it last longer.

'First, we will need some suola -- salt, the Kivi-väki call it. The Kivi-väki bring it from a place known only in stories, where mouths rise out from the earth. These traders are the only ones who are able to gather it. Trade them a set of antlers for a satchel of suola, then place it in the curing barrel with the rawhide strips.

'If you do this for me, you will have proven yourself a friend to the kelkka-makers of the Lumi-väki.''Good day there! It's a relief to talk to someone without stumbling around in that befuddled language. You caught us right in time; we're just preparing to take the road south back towards more civilized folk.

'Salt? I don't quite know what the Lossoth do with all this salt, but as long as they've got trades for it, we're happy to haul it down here.

'What? An antler? Shame on them; I'd expect at least a few hides for this. You're lucky we've got so much work to do, or I'd send you straight back.''This will be enough suola to cure all of the hides you brought to me. Thank you, ${PLAYER}, for your help.'Machar, exhausted from his long run out of Dûn Covád, has asked for your help recovering treasure from the Ongbúrz.The city of Dûn Covád is in the western hills of Fasach-falroid, far to the north of Aughaire. The Ongbúrz have constructed a wooden palisade in front of the entrance. Machar is in the Búth Sánkhas of Aughaire, south-east of the market.

Machar, winded from his previous excursion into Dûn Covád, has asked you to travel into the city and collect silver-nuggets from the Orcs that now live there.'${PLAYER}...you said...? I've heard...of your deeds, for the people here...speak of...little else. I've just run from the city of Dûn Covád, far to the north; give me a moment to catch my breath.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. It was my Fém to recover nuggets of silver from the Orcs that now occupy Dûn Covád, and though I did gather one arm-load, it is not enough. The Ongbúrz are paid in silver for their cruelty; we must take it from them both as a remembrance of their victims and as an aid for those still with us.

'Travel to Dûn Covád, the city in the western hills, far to the north of Aughaire. The Ongbúrz have erected a great palisade in front of the city, but if you can make it past the barrier, many of the Orcs beyond will carry silver-nuggets. Collect several arm-loads of these and bring them here to me. I will rest in the meantime.''Machar runs with great speed. Any other of my people would not have escaped from Dûn Covád with all the Ongbúrz alerted. He is to be praised for this, but his recklessness displeases me.''You have returned with silver-nuggets from the Ongbúrz! You are truly a friend to the Trév Gállorg, ${PLAYER}, and you do me honour by aiding with my Fém.

'If you need to have a rest after the long journey from Dûn Covád, pull up a sleeping mat.'Fissure-worm scales look like they would make strong armour for the Elves of Rivendell.Collect scales from Fissure-wormsThe worm-caves are to the south-east of Thorgest's dwelling.

Fissure-worm scales look like they would make strong armour for the Elves of Rivendell.Fissure-worm scales look like they would make strong armour for the Elves of Rivendell.'Excellent, ${PLAYER}, that should be enough scales to satisfy my clients in Rivendell.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Chieftains of the Dúnedain was a series of annals kept by the Rangers of the North, recording the wisdom of every chief from Aranarth, the first, all the way to Arador. It has much to say of the early days of the Rangers, when they were at their most scattered after the fall of Arnor. It was lost, however, when Arador was slain by trolls in the wilds of the Ettenmoors. It is thought that he buried it before the battle that ended in his death, but none knew where. A new annal was kept beginning with his son, Arathorn II, but all the wisdom of the preceding chieftains vanished, presumably forever.Talk to Aragorn in RivendellChieftains of the Dúnedain, page 3Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 12Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 13Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 17Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 18Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 23Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 29Chieftains of the Dúnedain, page 32'I was sure that this book was gone forever, and we had no hope of ever seeing it again. How much lore of the Dúnedain was thought to have disappeared when my grandfather fell near the banks of the Hoarwell in the north.

'They are all here...Aranarth, Arahael...Aragorn my namesake...Aragost, Aravorn...but what is this? It ends halfway through the time of Arassuil, and the last three are missing -- my grandfather among them. All that remain are tattered stubs of the pages that once were there.

'Alas, I had hoped I would read at last the final words of Arador, written before he was lost, but they are gone. ${PLAYER}, you must look again, and see if the remaining leaves yet survive. I wish to join you, but a far more important errand prevents me. If you could help to restore the history of my forefathers, the hearts of my people would be much strengthened, and their anxiety perhaps lessened for a time.'Thorgest appears to have fallen terribly ill from the Gauredain's poisoned weapons.The Gauradan-camps lie to the south of Thorgest's dwelling.

Thorgest has asked you to search through the camps of the Gauredain in the hopes of finding some cure for the affliction they have placed upon him.Search Gauradan-baskets (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thorgest's dwelling is to the north of the Gauradan camps.

You should bring the Gauradan-relics you have collected to Thorgest to see if he can discover a cure among them.'It... it seems the wolf-men have gotten the best of me after all....

'My body feels cold as stone, and this roaring fire is no help at all. Their spirit-callers have laid a curse on me, or that arrow was dabbed in some kind of poison, I know not which.

'I've no healing lore beyond the setting of bones and the binding of cuts, nor are my wits about me. I hope that if it is poison or curse, you might find something among the Gauradan camps that can cure it -- please, I beg you to go see what you can find, though I can offer little advice as to what might help.''Have you found anything that might suggest a cure for me yet?''What are these? I... I do not know what to make of them.

'I have no lore to aid me. If you have not found some way to cure me among their belongings, then my last hope is gone.

'Forgive me, I have no strength left.... I must sleep now and trust to fate that I will awaken again. Would that my brother were still here to watch over me....'Lossoth guards keep watch in a sentry tent located to the north-west of Kauppa-kohta, at the edge of the forest. The guards have had frequent sightings of wolves, which have become increasingly common.The sentry post is north-west of Kauppa-kohta.

Santtu has instructed you to speak with the guards at the sentry post if you wish to gain their trust.Talk to the guard at the sentry post'Have you no sense, foolish Sivullinen? You dare come into our camp without announcing yourself? But then, you approach with the noise of a herd of norsu, so it is not hard to follow you.

'We tire of you Sivullinen. You bring the world's ills with you, sweeping unhappiness into our lands. You will have to make your true purpose clear if you expect us to accept your passage through the tundra.

'If you wish to proceed further into the northern steppe, speak with the sentry who guards our border with the forest. His post lies along the north-west road. He will be watching you closely for any signs of... questionable intentions.''If you truly wish to help us, speak with the guards upon the road to the north-west.''No! No, they are trapping me. Circling around me and... I-I am sorry, just, the terror they wrought still shudders through my mortal frame.

'It was the susi -- what you call wolves -- that did this. I thought at first that the wind had spun into terrible squall, but then they began to howl... insane wailing like a giddy group of children playing a game. Their hideous screams of delight became snarls as my tent was torn apart.

'I was sure I would be rent limb from limb, but once they got my meagre food supplies, the whole pack vanished.'Finnán believes that you are trustworthy and can help the Trév Gállorg combat the Orcs of Bûrzum Pushdug.The cave Bûrzum Pushdug is north of Tór Gailvin, in the western side of the hill on which that fortress is built. Tór Gailvin is north-east of Aughaire.

Finnán asked you to defeat the Orcs of Bûrzum Pushdug in order to send a message of reproach to the Trév Duvárdain.Defeat Tarkrîp warriors at Bûrzum Pushdug (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Tarkrîp skirmishers at Bûrzum Pushdug (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Finnán is at the centre island in the settlement of Aughaire.

You have defeated many Orcs in Bûrzum Pushdug and should now bring news of your victory to Finnán.'Crannog is the chieftain of Aughaire, and he is right to challenge outsiders to prove their worthiness. But I, Finnán, have always prided myself on my keen eye and keener judgement. I know you are to be trusted, and that you will bring glory to the Trév Gállorg of Aughaire.

'You have already ventured into the fortress of Tór Gailvin, north-east of this settlement, and taken the prizes of the Trév Duvárdain from them; the traitors will surely rue their loss, and question whether their loyalty was given too quickly to the savage rulers of this land. But their treachery runs deeper than greed, for a tribe of Orcs is quartered in the cave of Bûrzum Pushdug, just north of Tór Gailvin! The Trév Duvárdain have these Orcs for neighbours, and this is a treachery so low that even their greed cannot justify it!

'Travel to Bûrzum Pushdug with an ally or two, and slay the Orcs there to remind the Trév Duvárdain what they would do were they worthy to walk the land of their fathers. The cave is on the west side of the hill on which Tór Gailvin is built, north-east of Aughaire.''I rejoice at your victory, ${PLAYER}! I knew I was right to give you my trust. The Trév Duvárdain will surely see that you have done what they should have and will feel remorse at their failing.''The Trév Duvárdain of Tór Gailvin have been remiss, ${PLAYER}, and have allowed Orcs to muster in the cave of Bûrzum Pushdug. The two are neighbours, and it fills me with anger, as it would fill any who hear of it!

'You have agreed to defeat the Orcs of Bûrzum Pushdug, and I await news of your success. The cave is in the western side of the hill on which Tór Gailvin is built, north-east of Aughaire. An ally or two will be helpful, for the cave is dangerous and full of Orcs.'Eilig, a warrior of the Trév Gállorg, seeks to cause disarray in the ranks of the Orcs that have taken the city of Dûn Covád and to visit his vengeance upon them for his sister, as well.The Orcs Nûlthauk and Ranghâsh are in the city of Dûn Covád, far to the north of Aughaire, among the western hills.

Eilig has asked you to defeat one of the two Orcs that slew his sister and so avenge her memory.Eilig is in the Búth Luikh in Aughaire.

You should return to Eilig and tell him of your journey into Dûn Covád.'We have fought against the Trév Duvárdain and we have fought against the Orcs, and we will continue doing so until we are defeated or we drive each of them from our lands!

'Far to the north of Aughaire, the Orcs have taken the old city of Dûn Covád in the western hills. Two of their leaders are known to me: Nûlthauk and Ranghâsh. If you are able to defeat one or both of them, their ranks may fall into disarray.

'But that is a secondary consideration for me. Several months ago, these Orcs were part of the band that slew my sister Mairád. If you slay one of them, the other will know my vengeance is coming, either soon or late. That will be enough for me. Bring an ally or two, for the city is dark and full of peril.''I am glad of your victory on my behalf, ${PLAYER}. You have brought my vengeance against those who inhabit Dûn Covád. Let the other quake and wonder when my vengeance will find him!''The Orcs Nûlthauk and Ranghâsh are in the city of Dûn Covád, far to the north of Aughaire, among the western hills. Defeat one of them, and I will have vengeance upon both!

'Bring an ally or two with you, for the city is very dangerous and my foes no less so.'Eilig and his brothers Machar and Torquil are seeking vengeance against Orc-raiders who slew their kin.The Orc-cave is north of Aughaire, just east of Dûn Covád.

Eilig has asked you to slay the Ongburz Chieftain, Nûlthauk who led the Orcs who slew his kindred.Eilig is back at Aughaire.

Nûlthauk is dead, so you should return to Eilig to tell him the tale of your deeds.'My brothers and I thirst for revenge against Orcs and Uruks who slew our kin. One seeks mindless vengeance, while the other seeks restitution - neither understands our foes truly. Orcs are cowards at heart, fighting only when driven by stern and heartless leaders. If we are to know peace again, then these leaders must be felled before they drive their hordes upon us once again.

It is the ones who led the raid who must be slain. The Ongburz chieftain Nûlthauk can be found deep within their cave north of here. Destroy him and the driving power behind the Orcs will be shattered for a time.''So, you have slain Nûlthauk? This was a deed well done! But there is more yet for you to do to complete our vengeance.''You must slay Nûlthauk, the Orc who led his minions when they slew our kin!'Sorkha of the Trév Gállorg is worried about the lesser watching-stones that have been erected in the city of Dûn Covád.Three lesser watching-stones can be found somewhere within the city of Dûn Covád, far to the north of Aughaire, in the western hills of Fasach-falroid.

Sorkha wants you to find the locations of three lesser watching-stones within Dûn Covád.Sorkha is in the Búth Luikh in Aughaire.

You have located three lesser watching-stones within the city of Dûn Covád and should return now to Sorkha with the knowledge of their locations.'The Búth Luikh is a place not for the boasting of feats but for the planning of them. Guirmán talks often of storming the walls of Dûn Covád, but I have urged him to be not overzealous in this! A plan not carefully formed is doomed before it is begun.

'The Angmarim have erected lesser watching-stones throughout Dûn Covád, and if we do not learn the locations of these statues, any plan of Guirmán's to retake the city is threatened.

'The city of Dûn Covád is far to the north of Aughaire, in the western hills of Fasach-falroid. Search the city for the lesser watching-stones and return to me when you have found them. I believe there are three in the city proper, and these are the ones you must locate!''You bring me the locations of the watching-stones within Dûn Covád? This will be very helpful, ${PLAYER}, when it is time to storm the city. We will know what to expect and can plan our attack in such a way as to avoid the dreadful stones.''Somewhere within the city of Dûn Covád are three lesser watching-stones. I need you to find their locations and return to me with this information.

'The city is located far to the north of Aughaire, in the western hills of Fasach-falroid.'The dwarf-trapper Thorgest was recently ambushed by Gauradan hunters who badly injured him. He is afraid that they may yet track him.The Gauradan hunters can be found throughout the southern reaches of High-pass.

Thorgest has asked you to throw the Gauradan hunters off his trail by defeating several of them before they find his dwelling.Defeat Gauradan wind-masters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Gauradan wolf-born (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Thorgest at Hrimbarg in central High-pass and tell him that the Gauradan hunters have been defeated.'Forgive my lack of manners, but I have been in better sorts. You try playing host to a sorry lot of adventurers that barge into your quiet home up in these remote heights, while you have a Gauradan-arrow stuck in your leg....

'Ah well. I'm not much one for company, but as you can see, I have fallen on hard times, so if you are willing to lend a hand, I suppose I would be obliged. You see, I lost the hunters that ambushed me out in that blizzard, but they've been sniffing around closer and closer to my little home here, trying to figure out where I went. If they find it and tell the others... well, that will be the end for old Thorgest, I'm afraid.

'I know it's dangerous, but if you could see your way to making the lives of those hunters difficult -- and short -- it might discourage them from trying to hunt down this dwarf.''The Gauredain are a fearsome foe... be wary.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You have taken a great burden off of my mind, what with all that has happened lately. Did you happen to see my brother Thorkell while you were wandering about? No....

'Oh, that's right, he's dead isn't he?

'Sorry, I'm not in my right head... don't feel so good....'Drostan, now stripped of his name and called Soltakh among the Trév Gállorg, is anxious to redeem himself.Strongboxes can be found throughout the city of Dûn Covád, in the western hills north of Fail-á-Khro.

Soltakh has asked you to slip false orders into the strongboxes used by the Angmarim chain of command for communication of tasks.Soltakh is at Fail-á-Khro, south of Dûn Covád and north of Aughaire.

Having slipped the false orders into the Angmarim strongboxes, you should return to Soltakh at Fail-á-Khro.'You fought well and bravely in Clúcath, ${PLAYER}, and I appreciate the mercy you have shown to me; Domongart and Avair did not understand, and perhaps they never will. I am no longer Drostan; now I am Soltakh, "He Who Is Changed," and Soltakh seeks to be redeemed.

'I can help the Trév Gállorg with what I know, brave ${RACE}: I know how the Orcs receive their orders, and we can use this knowledge to our advantage. Bring an ally or two with you on this excursion, and our success will be certain.

'The city of Dûn Covád, in the western hills north of Fail-á-Khro, is crawling with Orcs; these Orcs keep their orders in strongboxes located throughout the city. Take these pages I have in my possession; they were prepared by Domongart, but I have replaced several key points -- they will sow dissension and discontent among the Orcs if you place them in the strongboxes of Dûn Covád.''Very good, ${PLAYER}! I am hopeful we will quickly reap the benefit of our cunning. The next strike must land quickly, for it is aimed at a dangerous target: Gillë Andras, the Lord of the Western Marches.''I will bring honour to the name Soltakh through you, ${PLAYER}, and the deeds of Drostan will be forgotten.

'The strongboxes into which you should place the false orders are located within the city of Dûn Covád, in the western hills north of Fail-á-Khro. Bring an ally or two with you, for the city is greatly defended.'The Lossoth of Forochel are engaged in a constant struggle against their enemies the Gauredain, against the savage northern wild-life, and against the elements themselves.

While one might expect them to receive help gratefully, this constant hardship has also bred a strong independence among their people -- it will take a great deal of effort to win them over to the cause of the Free Peoples of Eriador.Complete Quests in Forochel.Complete quests in ForochelTheir Angmarim task-masters force the people of Donnvail to mine a hot-burning type of coal from the hills north of the city.Special hot-burning coal deposits can be found throughout the canyons north of the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Ekhagan has asked you to gather the special hot-burning coal he needs to work the old black iron of Angmar.Ekhagan is at Fail-á-Khro, south-west of the city of Donnvail.

You should return to Ekhagan with the special hot-burning coal you have mined.'The food-stores you gathered will help fill the bellies of my people, ${PLAYER}, but now we must put weapons into their hands!

'The Angmarim force the people of Donnvail to mine for a special sort of hot-burning coal in the hills north of the city, and they use this ore in the production of weapons. If we had some of this coal, we could set our smiths to forging weapons at once.

'The deposits of this special coal can be found among the canyons north of Donnvail. Take this hardened pick and use it to mine some of the coal, then return to me. Donnvail is north-east of Fail-á-Khro, and the canyons are north of the city's western wall.''The deposits of this coal I seek can be found throughout the canyons north of Donnvail's western wall. The city is north-east of Fail-á-Khro.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}, once again! We will forge great weapons with the aid of this coal, and perhaps it will help us to cast off the yoke of Angmar!'Defeat giants in the Misty Mountains.Defeat giants in the Misty MountainsDefeat many giants in the Misty Mountains.Defeat giants in the Misty MountainsThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Golu o Maeth was composed, it is said, by a great scholar of ancient Doriath. It is one of the oldest translations of the fundamentals of Rune-craft, an examination of the Tengwar in particular and their relationship to the powers of combat. This lore was thought lost after the destruction of Beleriand, though rumours persisted that at least one copy was brought east of the Ered Luin before then. Age has not been kind to this volume, however, and many of its pages have crumbled to dust. Even so, Elrond of Rivendell would certainly regard it as a relic beyond compare.Talk to Elrond Half-elven at the Last Homely House in RivendellGolu o Maeth, page 7Golu o Maeth, page 8Golu o Maeth, page 12Golu o Maeth, page 18Golu o Maeth, page 19Golu o Maeth, page 22Golu o Maeth, page 24Golu o Maeth, page 31'Do you know what this is, ${PLAYER}? Do you realize what terrible loss has been undone? No, you cannot, for you were not there. You did not know the glories of Beleriand, and cannot understand what was lost when it was sundered. So much knowledge, so much lore... gone forever.

'And yet, here is a piece of what was lost, returning to us unlooked for. The Golu o Maeth: my people spoke of it with awe when I was young, and have mourned its loss ever since. We have learned much about the link between runes and combat, of course, but to have this book in my presence is as the first time one hears the sweetness of a harp, or beholds the silver light of the moon.

'It wounds me to find so many of its leaves ruined by the passing of time, though it is no great surprise. Thousands of seasons have passed, and it is a wonder that it survives at all. Yet if one copy escaped Beleriand's doom, perhaps others did as well. If the lost pages were but recovered... but that, perhaps, is too much to hope....'Machar and his brothers Torquil and Eilig are seeking vengeance against Orc-raiders who slew their kin.The Tarkrîp are just north of Tór Gailvin -- seek out the cave they call Burzum Pushdug.

Machar has asked you to collect gold from the cave of the Orcs who slew his kindred.'The silver goes to lay our kin to rest. But there is more weregild yet to collect. As Torquil no doubt told you, the Tarkrîp were also allied with the Ongbúrz during their raid.

'I would have you reclaim the gold which they stole from us during their raid, so that it may restitute those who were bereft of family. You may find the Tarkrîp at the cave they call Burzum Pushdug, north of Tór Gailvin.''Gold of the Tarkrîp! You have done very well indeed. Now the spirits of my family can rest, satisfied with our vengeance.'Cenlieg has managed to locate his fellows, who are travelling north-west towards an unknown location.Moose can be found throughout the forest surrounding Kauppa-kohta.

Cenlieg has asked you to hunt for some moose-meat to feed his bear-friend, Linden.Cenlieg is at Kauppa-kohta,  on the eastern shore of the lake.

You should bring the meat you gathered back to Cenlieg.'We are ready to trek onwards to reunite with my fellowship, but Linden is still sick. I think the poor bear is starving... his paw has been at his stomach all day. Linden will surely liven up if he has some meat in his belly. The moose in this forest should do nicely.

'If you can hunt the moose for me and bring me some meat, we should be able to get underway soon. I am so sorry for all this trouble!

'The moose graze in the forest surrounding us. Thank you, my friend!''I know it is a burden, but if you can just gather some of that moose-meat for Linden, I will pack my bags and not bother you again.''Oh thank you, ${PLAYER}! I will give this to Linden right away.'You have been encouraged to learn the ways of the desolate crags of West Angmar, so you may better find the way to Aughaire when needed.Torcall has hidden an Orc-skull somewhere within West Angmar.

You have been challenged to recover an Orc-skull that Torcall says is hidden somewhere in West Angmar. He left by the road north of Aughaire and slew an Orc at some point along his journey; if you follow his trail, you may be able to find it.Torcall is in Aughaire, in West Angmar.

You have recovered the Orc-skull Torcall asked you to find and should now return it to him as proof of your skill as a hunter.'You call yourself a hunter? We will see, ${PLAYER}. We will see.

'If you are a true hunter, you will be able to follow the trail I took when last I hunted the Orcs of Angmar. I left by the north road from Aughaire. If you can follow my trail, you will find the skull of one of the Orcs I slew.

'Return to me with this Orc-skull, and I will show you what I know of the secret ways through Angmar. You will be able to come to Aughaire with speed far-exceeding that of lesser hunters.''I left by the north road, ${PLAYER}. You will need to pick up my trail from there and follow it until you recover the Orc-skull.'A battered Orc-skull lies among the stones. It is coated with a thin layer of ash and dust.A number of tracks can be seen among the loose stones here, but it is impossible to tell what might have made them or how long ago. It does not seem likely that Torcall came this way.You have found Torcall's trail, but here it becomes confused and muddled among the stones. You cannot tell in which direction he left this place.Judging from the signs, the trail here turns to the east.Your keen eye detects that the trail here bends to the north.The trail here runs north-east among the loose stones.'This is the skull of which I spoke, ${PLAYER}. You are a skilled hunter, and I will teach you what I know of the secret paths through these lands.

'You will be able to lead your allies to Aughaire with great speed, if you pay attention to the signs and to what I have told you.'Cariad has brought you to meet the rest of his people, the Cuan-lûth of Dunland.You should talk to Cariad before approaching Erlys.Talk to CariadCariad wishes to discuss matters with Erlys.Observe the discussionA spy of Saruman's has been spotted outside the cave.

Erlys has ordered you to confront and defeat the spy.Confront the spy outsideIgrain has offered to let you leave so that she may freely spy on the Cuan-lûth refugees.

You should wait and stand your ground.Wait and stand your groundDefeating Igrain may help the Cuan-lûth trust you more.Defeat IgrainIgrain has surrendered.

You should observe her interrogation.Observe Igrain's interrogationYou should talk to Cariad about Erlys' opinion of the Rangers.Talk to Cariad'No matter. There are more important things to attend to for now.''I'm sorry you had to disarm before entering. My people don't trust you, and if I'm being honest, you'll have to prove yourself to me a bit more before I can fully trust you. I hope you understand.

'Don't worry, no harm will come to you as long as you don't do anything rash. You have my word.''Why can Erlys not see that the odds are stacked against us? Her stubbornness will be the death of her one day.

'Forgive me; that was an ugly thing to say. She wants the best for us, but she can be rather short-sighted during the worst of times.'Fonn has learned that gertheryg have been seen travelling to Ologfil in Malenhad. He has sent a scout to investigate, but the scout has not returned.The hillman Bethad must be somewhere in this foetid cave - whether he could possibly be alive or not is anyone's guess.Find Bethad in OlogfilBethad has been imprisoned by the gorthorog war-leader Ulkrank, who must be slain before the scout can be safely freed.Defeat UlkrankWith the death of Ulkrank, you should be able to free Bethad from his prison and escape from the dark lair of Ologfil.Free Bethad from his imprisonment'What valour you have shown to come so far into peril for my sake! I and my family shall be forever in your debt - now quickly, let us be away from this horrible place.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Knee-breaker's Manual is an odd tome -- it appears to be written in a rather careful and precise script, but the passages themselves describe a particularly coarse and savage individual. Whoever he was, the exploits described in this book certainly lean to the unsavoury side of the burglar's profession. Nevertheless, there are a number of intriguing points that the author of the book presents.

Frustratingly, several key pages are missing. It is said that the famous burglar Bilbo Baggins has undertaken a study of the profession since his retirement -- perhaps he could tell you something about this 'Knee-breaker' or the missing sections of his manual?Knee-breaker's Manual is an odd tome -- it appears to be written in a rather careful and precise script, but the passages themselves describe a particularly corse and savage individual. Whomever he was, the exploits described in this book certainly lean to the unsavory side of the Burglar's profession. Nevertheless, there are a number of intriguing points that the author of the book presents, but frustratingly several key pages are missing.

It's said that the famous Burglar Bilbo Baggins has undertaken a study of the profession since his retirement - perhaps he could tell you something about this 'Knee-breaker' or the missing sections of his manual?Talk to Bilbo Baggins at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.Knee-breaker was known to associate with the Hill-men of Angmar and possibly even with the orcish tribes in the Misty Mountains.Knee-breaker's Manual, page 1Knee-breaker's Manual, page 3Knee-breaker's Manual, page 4Knee-breaker's Manual, page 8Knee-breaker's Manual, page 10Knee-breaker's Manual, page 15Knee-breaker's Manual, page 16Knee-breaker's Manual, page 19'Oh dear, what is this? Knee-breaker's Manual? I can't say as I have heard of it before, I'm afraid. No wait, I'm sorry, my mind does wander a bit these days, I recall someone speaking of it once -- Glóin perhaps? Ah well, it is no matter, as I recall the story now.

'Knee-breaker was the pet name of a particularly unsavoury sort of burglar -- people nowadays suppose he was likely a good part Orc, given his disposition and appearance. Suffice it to say, he was the worst sort of knave, and a bully to boot -- but he was possessed of a devilish cunning and was known for his ability to catch his enemies off guard and set them up for the worst kind of trouble should he put his mind to it.

'Anyway, I'm not sure this book is going to be of much use to you -- a fair number of pages have been damaged or torn out, and it would be hard enough to decipher even if they were intact. I am not sure where you might find the pages, but if you do, I'm sure this will be a real help to you.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Knowledge of Kicks was written by Brom Hawksbeard, a brawler from Bree-land.

Several pages are missing.This curious manual describes a huge variety of kicks... you had no idea just how many there were.

However, several pages are missing.Ask Brom why his treatise is missing so many pagesThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.Knowledge of Kicks, Page 7Knowledge of Kicks, Page 10Knowledge of Kicks, Page 12Knowledge of Kicks, Page 18Knowledge of Kicks, Page 19Knowledge of Kicks, Page 25Knowledge of Kicks, Page 35Knowledge of Kicks, Page 37'Why are so many pages missing from this fine tome? Do you suspect that I was careless with Knowledge of Kicks, my most comprehensive guide to the deadly weapons that are your legs and feet? Do you believe the pages were lost by mistake?

'It is not so! It is another lesson, my brawling friend! Collect all the pages and you will know everything there is to know about inflicting damage with your feet!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Lore of the Blade seems to be a rather unusual tome for a Lore-master to express interest in. Rather than delving deeply into the hidden mysteries of mountains, or discussing at length the habits of wave and wind, it reads more like a rousing tale of adventure written to entertain hobbit-children. The pages of Lore of the Blade claim to follow the adventures of a brave young Lore-master who when confronted with deadly dangers is just as apt to fight her way through with flashing sword-play as to confound her opponents with the lore of the elements or the wisdom of long forgotten sages. Oddly, as you find yourself drawn into the stirring tale you begin to see the more subtle nuances of the story unfold through the eyes of its character, who displays an uncanny insight into the nature of the foes she faces, and an understanding of when the strength of arms applied at the correct moment may indeed be the least destructive course.

Unfortunately, some prior reader has handled this copy rather poorly and some of the pages are torn beyond repair. The author also proves to be a bit difficult to pin down, as he has chosen to identify himself solely by the runic letter 'G' inscribed on the binding.Talk to Gandalf in RivendellLore of the Blade, page 5Lore of the Blade, page 11Lore of the Blade, page 18Lore of the Blade, page 26Lore of the Blade, page 35Lore of the Blade, page 45Lore of the Blade, page 56Lore of the Blade, page 68'Ah, ${PLAYER}, is it? I do hope this is important, I am rather busy right now. What is that you have there... WHERE DID YOU GET THAT BOOK!?

'Ah, just as I'd hoped I'd seen the last of these, and yet here is another copy to haunt me to my dying day. Well, what is done is done. Yes well, no point denying it. I wrote that book some many years ago -- as I recall, it was for some lord or other who had done me a great service. He was a doting parent, and his children so enjoyed my tales when I dropped by to visit that he begged me to write a few of them down so he could read to them when I wasn't about. I foolishly agreed to his request, and the next thing I know the cursed fool had gone and had his scribes churn out copies for the entire nobility of the realm!

'I was the laughing stock of the White Council for a decade at the least! Finally, with the passage of time, the book faded into obscurity, and I foolishly believed that was the last of it. Now you have the gall to come here and suggest to me that the book might contain knowledge of worth to the Lore-masters of Eriador? Bah! Some days, I wonder if the lot of you combined have the sense of a stone-headed dwarf! As for the missing pages, if you are so keen to know how the story ends, then I suggest you go find them yourself -- you will get no help from me I assure you! Now be off!'The Lossoth-scout Lauri at Pynti-peldot awaits the delivery of gilded sword sheaths.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver gilded sword sheaths from the Gauredain surrounding the encampment of Pynti-peldot to Lauri, a Lossoth-scout.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Susi-väki surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot. Deliver the items to Lauri, one of the Lossoth-scouts at the encampment in Forochel.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Susi-väki surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot.'These men who entreat with such beasts are an enemy of ours. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Lossoth-scout Lauri at Pynti-peldot awaits the delivery of pure black skin.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black skin from the beasts and worms surrounding the encampment of Pynti-peldot to Lauri, a Lossoth-scout.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts and worms surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot. Deliver the items to Lauri, one of the Lossoth-scouts at the encampment in Forochel.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts and worms surrounding the encampment at Pynti-peldot.'This will help our people much. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Machan foolishly sought to hunt fire-worms alone and ended up trapped and wounded.Help Machan to get out of the fire-worm lair.Talk to Machan when you are readyTell Murdaigán that Machan is safe.'Curse my foolish pride! I should never have ventured into this worm-lair on my own. There are just too many of them, and now that I have found this safe place, I do not dare try to sneak away on my own. 

'Can you help me to escape? I would ask that you not tell the other hunters, for I would be ridiculed for the rest of my life.''Machan is safe then, is he? Good. He is an impulsive and proud man, and I had feared that he might have gotten in over his own head.

'Not that such a thing could ever happen, nor would his pride permit him to be rescued if it did.'

'I suspect that I -- and Machan -- owe you greater thanks than your tale suggests, and so I do thank you most sincerely.'Complete additional quests within Angmar.Complete quests within AngmarRaghnaid has charged you with ending the reign of Muiráthakh, the Silent Judge, who holds Donnvail in a grip of fear.Muiráthakh, the Silent Judge, holds court deep within the city of Donnvail, through the arch east of Una and her allies.

Raghnaid has tasked you and your allies with ending the reign of Muiráthakh.Defeat MuiráthakhRaghnaid is within the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

You have defeated Muiráthakh and should bring news of your triumph to Raghnaid.'The lords of Carn Dûm sent three of their servants to quell discontent in Fasach-falroid, ${PLAYER}, and we must strike them now before any lives are lost!

'Muiráthakh, the Silent Judge, keeps his court east of here, in a dangerous section of the city; there is no law to justify his misdeeds. Travel with your allies to his court and bring him to his end. But beware of Fûshath the Beast, who acts as executioner for Muiráthakh and is never far from his master.

'Do this for the family of Uthagan, ${PLAYER}, as much as for the rest of the Trév Gállorg.''I have already learned of your success, ${PLAYER}! The family of Uthagan rejoices at your vengeance, wherever they may be.

'The lords of Carn Dûm will not be pleased that Muiráthakh has been cast down. Let them shiver in their palaces, and dread to hear our footstep approach, for soon it will be their time to join him in death!''Muiráthakh must be ended if Donnvail is to cast off the shackles of Carn Dûm. Find him at his court deep within the city, through the arch to the east. Beware of Fûshath the Beast, who often patrols near to Muiráthakh's court.'You have nearly perfected your technique of wielding bees as a weapon. You only get stung once or twice!Use Bee SwarmPerform many valiant deeds within the snows of the Misty Mountains.Complete 30 Misty Mountains Quests.Complete quests within the Misty Mountains.The sled-builders have not been able to gather enough supplies because of the increasing numbers of dangerous animals in the winter forest.Moose-antlers can be gathered from the moose in the forests surrounding Kauppa-kohta. Sinew can be gathered from wolverines in the forests, and driftwood can be found along the southern shore of the lake.

Eija has asked you to collect moose-antlers, sinew, and driftwood for use in crafting her sleds.Collect moose-antlers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sinew (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect driftwood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Eija is on the lakeshore in Kauppa-kohta.

Now that you have collected the materials Eija requested, you should return to her.'These past few months have been getting more and more difficult. There are too many beasts in the forest, and there are not enough hunters to collect our supplies for the kelkka.

'You can help us by collecting moose-antlers, sinew, and driftwood. The antlers are important, you see.  They are flattened into the luisua of our sleds, keeping them light but ever so strong. Most any moose you hunt in our forests should provide you with a generous amount. The sinew comes from the wolverines all around Kauppa-kohta. Their meat is too tough for eating, but perfect for lashing the kelkka together.

'There is something strange about the driftwood, though. The lake cares for it, cradling the timbers until they emerge on the southern shore like smooth gems from a Kivi-väki trader's pouch. The wood glows like the moon, and because the kelkka are formed from it, they will always guide us back to our homes. My people depend upon the kelkka to take our goods from village to village. Your help will be greatly appreciated.''This is a hyvys -- a kindness -- you have performed. These materials will help us to make enough sleds for the hunters and for the Elders.'Cána is worried that the Trév Duvárdain and their Angmarim masters are getting fat off the land that is rightfully that of the Trév Gállorg.Pea-plants can be found in the farmland outside the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Cána has tasked you with recovering the pea-plants from the farms outside Donnvail.Collect pea-plants (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cána is at Fail-á-Khro, on the road north of Aughaire.

You have harvested pea-plants from the farms near Donnvail to keep them from the enemies of the Trév Gállorg.'The herbal poultice will help with our defence, ${PLAYER}; now we must see to the attack, and I know upon what to focus our efforts: peas!

'The farms north-east of here have been taken from my people and put to the use of the Trév Duvárdain. Now that we have retaken Fail-á-Khro, we can do something about it.

'Travel north-east to the farmland outside the city of Donnvail and harvest the pea-plants you find there. We may not have the numbers to take the farms, but we can at least take their peas!''Harvest pea-plants from the farms outside the city of Donnvail, to the north-east. Bring them to me, and we will keep them from feeding our enemies!''Thank you for these pea-plants, ${PLAYER}. Our enemies will go hungry tonight and know that we are serious about taking what is ours!'Niko, the Master of the Hunt, doubts you have the ability to move silently and not scare off game, let alone hunt well.The aurochs-herds are to the north and to the south and south-west of Jänis-leiri.

Niko instructed you to find and disable some of the Gauredain's traps to protect the aurochs-herds.Destroy Gauradan-traps (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Niko is at Jänis-leiri, northward of the aurochs-herds, near the road.

You should return to Niko and tell him of your success.Talk to Niko'Go away, Sivullinen. I have no time for the likes of you! What? Ora sent you? What for? To help me? Do you both suffer from snow-dreams? What help could you be? Go away! You can in no way help me -- I doubt you can even hunt!

'Let you prove yourself? I am not having you crash around the tundra, scaring off what little game you do not maim! Hunting in Forochel is serious business. Hunting requires patience, stealth, silence, cunning, planning. If you can prove that your feet can be silent as your mouth is not currently being, you may prove more than completely useless, and we will talk again.

'We hunt härkä. There are several herds of them nearby; look to those to the south-west and to the north. Those thrice-blasted Susi-väki have been sneaking around recently, trying to trap our härkä, and they are not too careful about how much damage those brutal traps of theirs might do to the poor beasts before they come to put the wounded out of their misery. Go and find those traps and disable them. Härkä can be somewhat skittish, so stay calm -- if you hurt one, the whole herd might run, and then you may be certain I will use your bowels for a bow-string.''We hunt härkä. There are several herds of them nearby; look to those to the south-west and to the north. Those thrice-blasted Susi-väki have been sneaking around recently, trying to trap our härkä, and they are not too careful about how much damage those brutal traps of theirs might do to the poor beasts before they come to put the wounded out of their misery. Go and find those traps and disable them. Härkä can be somewhat skittish, so stay calm -- if you hurt one, the whole herd might run, and then you may be certain I will use your bowels for a bow-string.''You destroyed the traps? And the herds have not scattered to the four winds?

'Well, I did not think a Sivullinen like you would be able to do it. Perhaps you can be of some use to me after all, seeing as my other hunters are all occupied....'Sleds -- or kelkka, as they are called by the Lossoth -- made of wood and bone are a common sight in Lossoth camps. The Lossoth use these to transport trade goods and supplies between their settlements, mainly because they can support the weight of their supplies without sinking into the snow. The Lossoth are in constant need of these sleds.Eija is in Kauppa-kohta, at the lakeshore.

Mainio suggested you speak with his mother, Eija, to learn if she could use your help.Talk to Eija'You look like you have come from a long journey. What is your land called, and what is it like? Does it have sand? I do not quite know what that is, but it sounds most interesting!

'I like to hear tales of distant places, especially after spending a whole day making kelkka. It helps to break the ikävys, the tedium, of the work.

'If you are of a mind to assist, I think my mother could use your help. We have been trying to build enough kelkka to give some to the Elders, as well as to the traders, but there are not enough workers helping. Just follow the road into Kauppa-kohta...my mother's name is Eija.''Well hello, traveller. I see my son has been making friends with all the strange peoples to the south. He should know better at his age, but that is none of your concern, I suppose.

'As long as you are here, perhaps you could be of some use to us.'The farmers of Donnvail produce as much food as they are able in this harsh land, but the Angmarim seize most of it for themselves and their army.Food-stores are kept within the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Ekhagan has asked you to steal food-stores from the city of Donnvail, where they are being kept for the use of the army of Angmar, so he can distribute them to the hungry people of his tribe.'The farms of Fasach-falroid produce as much as our people can coax from them, but the Angmarim exert pressure on the people of Donnvail and take most of the food for themselves and their army.

'The surplus food is stored in crates within the city of Donnvail until such time as the army has need of it...but the Trév Gállorg need it now! It seems that the Angmarim would rather let it rot than go to feed the hungry mouths of my people!

'Travel north-east to the city of Donnvail and return to me with the food-stores. I will make sure that it goes to those who need it. Let the army of Angmar starve!''I thank you, ${PLAYER}. My people have greater need for these food-stores than do the Orcs and Trév Duvárdain who fight for Angmar.'Foul activity may be occurring under the Rangers' noses.Lagordal has asked you to investigate a suspicious camp-site atop a cliff.

The cliff can be found to the south-east, next to the waterfall opposite of Tornhad.Find the suspicious camp-siteWhoever made this camp-site may have left something behind.

You should search the camp-site for information.Investigate camp-fireInvestigate sackYou have found a couple of signs of life at this camp-site.

You should examine the footprints for more information.Investigate footprintsThe moment you tried to examine footprints at the camp-site, a Dunlending attacked you.

You should return to Lagordal with this news.Talk to Lagordal'Do not worry about me -- 'tis a minor wound, nothing more. One of my fellow Rangers is seeking a healer as we speak.

'Do you see that cliff to the south-east, next to the waterfall? I found an unfamiliar camp-site there, devoid of any souls. But I left it alone, intent on first completing a task I had been assigned. As you can see, I haven't been able to go back to investigate.

'I suspect some rather unsavoury folks have made their way northward into this region. For what purpose, I do not know.

'Would you take up the task of investigating the camp-site? Let me know of your findings.''A Dunlending ambushed you? Then I was right to be wary, for they are a hateful people who will stop at nothing to hinder us.

'We're stretched thin enough as it is -- the last thing we needed was for Saruman to turn his gaze towards the Angle!'The brimstone swamp of Malenhad is also home to a terrible breed of beast, which the hillmen call the "Ancient Ironscale".The rare Ancient Ironscale can sometimes be found amongst its lesser brethren in Malenhad.

Othran has challenged you and your fellows to slay the dreaded Ancient Ironscale, lord of the fell turtles of Malenhad.Othran is at his camp in the western part of Malenhad.

You should return to Othran to tell him the tale of your victory against the dreaded Ancient Ironscale.'So, Southron, you have proven yourself more than worthy to hunt in the brimstone swamp of Malenhad. Perhaps you wish to gain even more glory? There is prey in this swamp that is too dangerous even for we who have hunted these swamps since time out of mind.

'I speak of the dreaded Ancient Ironscale, a monster so deadly that none of my people have ever faced it... and survived to tell the tale of it.

'I think it will be impossible for one southron, however doughty, to defeat such a foe, but even were you able, with companions, to kill this beast, I would be forced to admit you are true hunters and warriors of renown. The Ancient Ironscale is a rare beast, but it can sometimes be found amongst its lesser brethren in the swamp.''The Ancient Ironscale is too great a challenge for you, Southron? I am not surprised.''What? You have slain the Ancient Ironscale? I am astonished! I admit that I had not expected you to even make the attempt, not after telling you of its terrible menace.

'I must be honest with you, Southron, this was not quite the legendary foe I made it out to be. In fact, we hunters seek out the beast when we come of age in an ancient ritual amongst our people. Of course, not all of the youths return from these hunts....

'But still, it is a great accomplishment for one not born to the harsh, blasted hills and foetid bogs of the North. I must praise you for a worthy deed. You are a true hunter of Angmar.'Lornë only knows the Silk-lady as a name of fear, but hopes you may be able to find more clues in the Angmarim fortress of Bail Boglakh.Lornë has asked you to gather some allies and seek out any clues as to the whereabouts of the so-called 'Silk-lady' of Bail Boglakh, an Angmarim fortress to the north of Malenhad.Find a clue to the Silk-lady's presence in Bail Boglakh'The Silk-lady....I wish I knew more about her. I have heard only dark rumours of her presence in Bail Boglakh, but none among the hill-men here have seen her - or done so and lived to tell the tale at any rate.

'If you are willing to continue to aid me, I would ask you to gather your allies and scout within the fortress of Bail Boglakh to the north across the swamps, and see what clues you might find as to the whereabouts of this 'Silk-lady', if indeed she may hold my sister captive.''These tokens you have found certainly suggest that the Silk-lady does reside in Bail Boglakh as we suspected.

'But what is this? On the back of these tokens is an inscription of the high tower at Bail Boglakh... It must be there that this evil woman resides! My sister must be held within!'Lornë told you that she joined the hunting party to find her missing sister, Muirnë, only she fears that the worst has befallen her.The Angmarim camp of Bail Róva is North-west of Tyrn Lhuig, guarding the entrance to the Ram Dúath.

Lornë has asked you to look for some sign of her sister and if necessary to confront the outpost's commander for news of Muirnë.Look for MuirnëConfront the Bail Róva camp commanderLornë is at the hunters' camp in Malenhad.

Muirnë was not in Bail Rova, but the Angmarim commander mentioned a Silk-lady who may know more. You should return to Lornë and tell her what you have learned.'Listen well, stranger. To make my way here, I must do some hunting with the other men of this little camp, but in truth I am not here to live the life of a hunter. I am here for the sake of my sister, Muirnë, and if things have gone as I fear, I will take a blood price from her murderer. But I still hope that the worst has not come to pass, and if she still lives, I wish to rescue her from her captors.

'Many months past, she left our home in Aughaire to go questing with kin, but on the way I am told she was taken by the Angmarim. From what I can tell, they were encamped at the outpost of Bail Rova, but thus far I have been unable to learn more.

'Will you help me? That encampment lies to the west on the way to Ram Dúath. If the Angmarim had hoped to learn something from my sister, they would have brought her to the commander of Bail Róva.''So, she was not there after all. All my patience, wasted! But I should offer my thanks. I might have waited here much longer and still learned nothing!

'The Silk-lady...that is a name I have heard, though. Recently it has become a name of dread in these hills. Whispered tales speak of a cruel and vicious woman of Angmar who commands deadly spiders. It is said she is as dangerous to her fellow Angmarim as she is to the enemies of the Iron Crown....''Please, look for my sister, Muirnë. I must hold to the hope she lives, until it is proven otherwise.''What? Muirnë? You ask for her? Ha!

'You'll not find her here, Southron! She moved on long ago. Seek you the Silk-lady if you would know more.

'But I think the Silk-lady will not be troubled with your presence, Outlander...not if I kill you here and now and redeem the incompetence of my men!'Latharna has welcomed you as a friend of the Trév Gállorg and asks you to set right some of the injuries the people have suffered of late.The bloodstone trove is in the fortress of Tór Gailvin north-east of Aughaire, probably hidden in an out-of-the-way corner.

Latharna has asked you to recover the trove of bloodstones that was stolen by the Trév Duvárdain.Collect bloodstone troveLatharna is in the Búth Sánkhas in Aughaire, south-east of the market.

You have recovered the bloodstone trove from Tór Gailvin and should bring it now to Latharna.'Welcome, ${PLAYER}, to the Búth Sánkhas of Aughaire. This is where we pass down stories of the lost days and preserve the tales of our people. Now that you are a friend of the Trév Gállorg, it is your duty to carry on this tradition as well.

'A great trove of bloodstones was stolen from us by the Trév Duvárdain before your arrival at Aughaire; it is a tale that deserves a better ending. Go now to Tór Gailvin, north-east of Aughaire, and reclaim this trove. You have already been to this fortress and are well-suited to weaving a proper end to this tale.

'The trove will likely be kept in an out-of-the-way corner somewhere within Tór Gailvin. Use caution as you go and search in every corner for the stolen trove.''The bloodstone trove is being held somewhere within Tór Gailvin, the Trév Duvárdain fortress north-east of Aughaire. Search in out-of-the-way corners within the camp and recover what was stolen from us!''Crannog's assessment was correct, ${PLAYER}. You are a worthy friend of the Trév Gállorg! I am pleased that we may now write a more satisfactory ending to the tale of the stolen bloodstones.'Another challenge was sent to Arzhur's clan. Before the challenge can be accepted, another token of honour must be brought forth.The Cairn of Honour is in the centre of Fasach-Falroid to the north of Aughaire. It is atop a high hill, west of the north road, and at night the path to it is lit by torches.

Arzhur has asked you to obtain a stone from the Cairn of Honour. You must not spill any blood of the Hillmen who patrol the area.Obtain a stone from the Cairn of HonourArzhur is back at Aughaire.

You should bring the stone you recovered to Arzhur and prepare for the next challenge.'Another challenge has been placed before us. Again, we need a token of honour before we can fight. This time, I am afraid clan law is set against us, as there has been a dispute over your participation in the last challenge. If we are to win our way free of this morass, you must fight this time alone.

'First, we require a stone from the Cairn of Honour. The Cairn is in Fasach-Falroid to the north, in the territory of the hostile clans, and their scouts and warriors are entitled by clan-law to defend their land. Yet you must not shed any blood of these Hillmen while obtaining the stone, or the stone will be tainted with blood-debt.

'So I must ask you to journey north to the Cairn, and yet you must not kill any of our enemies, no matter if they attack you or not! You will find the Cairn atop a high hill west of the north road, and at night the path up the hill is lit by torches.''Well done. Now it is time for you to fight once more.'Enraged by their repeated defeats in duels, Chief Walraig of the hostile Hillmen has declared a feud against the men of Aughaire.Chief Walraig is with his kin in Tór Gailvin, a camp north-east of Aughaire.

Lore-singer Arzhur has asked you to raid the Hillman fort to the north-east. If you can defeat Chief Walraig, you may be able to forestall a war between the clans.Arzhur is in Aughaire, south-west of Chief Walraig's camp.

You should return to Arzhur with news of your victory over Chief Walraig.'Alas your victories have not come without cost. Walraig, Chief of the clan of our enemies, has lost all reason after your defeat of his champions, and he has declared a blood-feud against us! This may make him an outcast if he is not successful in his feud, but if he is not stopped, he will lead the full force of his clan against us here in Aughaire.

'Our one hope is to stop this feud before it begins. If we raid his camp in Tór Gailvin to the north-east, not far from Fail-á-Khro, and defeat Chief Walraig in person, we will have obeyed the law, and our enemies will be unable to proceed against us.

'But our Chief, Crannog, refuses to lead such a raid without Golodir's presence to guide him! I must ask you to do what we cannot and defeat Chief Walraig before we ourselves are overwhelmed.''You have done a noble deed for us, Southron. Something we should have done for ourselves. Are we doomed forever to wait for others to lead us?

'Well, no matter. We are in your debt. You will always find a warm welcome in our tents.''Please! You must defeat Chief Walraig!'The Lossoth hunters were impressed by your display against the Warg, though they will not freely admit it.Wargs can be found in the south-west foothills.

Valtteri has instructed you to go into the forest and hunt the Wargs, collecting the fat from their underbellies for the Lossoth to use later.Collect Warg-fat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Valtteri stands by his Warg cage in Kauppa-kohta.

You should return the Warg-fat to Valtteri as he requested.Talk to Valtteri'Strange that you could defeat a Surmâja without any training from our hunters...but it had not been fed well in our camp and was very sick last week.

'If you wish to hunt with us, then very well. Go into the woods and collect the fat from the underbellies of the Surmâja. One of our hunters will be able to use it later.

'Look to the south-west, in the hills behind the lake...that is where you will find the most Surmâja.''You are not skilled enough to collect the Surmâja-fat I require of you?''I did not think you would survive my challenge, Sivullinen. Even my hunters might have been overwhelmed by so many Surmâja.

'Now that you have proven yourself, we have one more request.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Joy of Battle is an aptly named text, written by a great dwarf-champion during the height of Durin's reign in Khazad-dûm. It is no simple treatise on the art of battle, as so many texts are. Rather, it is a philosophical tract that describes the author's outlook upon warfare and battle, and how he came eventually to seek it out as a meditative and even transcendant experience that he was unable to experience in any other setting save the midst of clashing blades and the cries of battle. It is not a text for the faint-hearted or the meek, but it seems oddly lofty and its tone does not reflect the maddened bloodlust that one might have expected given the title.

Alas, the long years have been rough on your copy, and there are a number of pages torn or missing from it. Gimli the Dwarf might have some insight into the missing knowledge of The Joy of Battle, since it was written by one of his forebears in the depths of time.Gimli the Dwarf might have some insight into the missing knowledge of The Joy of Battle, since it was written by one of his forebears in the depths of time.Talk to Gimli in RivendellThe missing pages of The Joy of Battle may be found on those who wander the darkest regions of Angmar or the blistering cold of the Misty Mountains.The Joy of Battle, page 11The Joy of Battle, page 14The Joy of Battle, page 18The Joy of Battle, page 24The Joy of Battle, page 25The Joy of Battle, page 28The Joy of Battle, page 32The Joy of Battle, page 36'Greetings ${PLAYER}, taking up some reading lately are you? Ah! What's this? The Joy of Battle! I have heard tell of this volume, but never laid eyes on it myself.

'It is such a shame that this copy is so tattered. The author was one of the finest dwarf-champions ever to walk the halls of Khazad-dûm in its grandest days. He was said to have been one of those who could stand in the midst of turmoil and battle as if it were naught but a soothing song or mother's lullaby. Most champions I know bite back the fear or cloak it in rage to hide it from their enemies, but not this one. He loved battle like his own first son, never mind the odds or the pain.

'I will tell you this and make no mistake, it will be worth your time to try and find those missing pages if they might give some insight into the heart of such a warrior.'Búdan sent you to the city of Donnvail to speak with the Trév Gállorg there who might rise up against the Angmarim.Garvan is in the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Búdan told you of the three leaders sent to Donnvail by Carn Dûm: Fûshath the Beast, Muiráthakh the Silent Judge, and Gillë Andras the Lord of the Western Marches. He also told you to speak to a Man named Garvan further within the city of Donnvail.Morven is within the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Garvan gave you the name of someone who might be sympathetic to the cause, telling you to seek Morven further into the city.Uthagan is in the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Morven has told you to speak with Uthagan, but with caution: his family was recently taken by Muiráthakh, and she does not think they have been seen since.Talk to UthaganUthagan spoke of Una, located somewhere within the city of Donnvail, north-east of Fail-á-Khro.

Though he sought to slay you, Uthagan unwittingly gave you the name of someone you might speak to: a woman named Una.'Donnvail is draped in the shadow of fear, ${PLAYER}, and the Trév Gállorg here are tormented. We keep our heads down and try not to draw attention to ourselves.

'The lords of Carn Dûm have sent cruel taskmasters to this city to stop any chance of rebellion before it begins: Fûshath the Beast, a terrible Gorthorog; Muiráthakh, the Silent Judge, doom-sayer and executioner; and Gillë Andras, the Lord of the Western Marches, cruel leader of the armies that tread upon Fasach-falroid.

'I will say no more. Seek out Garvan, further within the city. He will tell you more, if he is as fool-hardy as I, and I pray that you tell no one that I gave you his name.''I am sorry, you must have me mistaken for someone else. I am just going about my business.

'Find Morven, further into the city. I cannot be seen talking to you, but she might be willing to hear you out!''You speak boldly for a stranger newly-arrived in Donnvail. Do you not fear Muiráthakh or Fûshath the Beast?

'Uthagan, further into the city, may be able to help you, but speak with him cautiously; his family was recently "invited" to a dinner at the house of Muiráthakh, and I have not seen them since. I do not know if any have.

'We must organize if we are to strike a blow against our Angmarim masters. Uthagan may be able to guide you.''You have followed a long road to me, ${PLAYER}, and I pray that it was not too painful. Donnvail has not been a peaceful city since the lords of Carn Dûm sent their minions here.

'We few that stand against the shadow welcome your aid. This neighbourhood will be the heart of the struggle. Call allies if you have them and if they are willing to stand with you! The foes you will soon face cannot be defeated by one alone; it will take many hearts to overthrow the masters of this city.''I have nothing more to say to you, and I will repeat no names; there are too many unfriendly ears within this city.

'Perhaps you wrote down what I said to you? Consult your journal and draw no more attention to me!''I weep for Uthagan. Look for Una somewhere deep within Donnvail, and may his family not have suffered in vain.

'You must go now. The guards will become suspicious.''You will find Morven further in the city. Now leave me be!''Poor Uthagan. Would any of us been able to withstand the torment he must have suffered?

'Yet he aids us, even still: look for Una deep within Donnvail.''Uthagan is further within the city, and he may be able to help you.''That is as I feared. That Uthagan's family should suffer for his deeds is very much in keeping with what we know of Muiráthakh.

'Look for Una deep within Donnvail, and may Uthagan's mind be eased by our eventual triumph.''You speak to me of rebellion? Tell that to my family, taken by Muiráthakh on the charge that they were planning an uprising! That dog knows it was I who aided Una in that, but he took my family instead, as a warning to me!

'There was a time when I would have aided you, but Una will have to fight her battles alone!

'I will kill you myself and teach you something of despair!'Muiráthakh, the Silent Judge, commands the loyalty of many warriors sent from Carn Dûm. Artagan wants them eliminated.Iron Crown warriors and crossbowmen can be found in the neighbourhood beyond the arch east of Una's allies.

Artagan wants you to undermine Muiráthakh's rule in Donnvail by defeating his servants.Defeat Iron Crown warriors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Iron Crown crossbowmen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Artagan is in the city of Donnvail, among Una's allies.

You have defeated many of Muiráthakh's servants and should bring news of your success to Artagan.'Muiráthakh, the Silent Judge, brought a number of warriors with him from Carn Dûm; they protect him and uphold his judgements. While they remain in the city, Donnvail will never be free.

'Look for Iron Crown warriors and crossbowmen in the neighbourhood beyond the eastward arch, ${PLAYER}. Bring your allies and defeat these servants of Muiráthakh.

'We must undermine his rule if Donnvail is to break free from the bonds of Carn Dûm.''You have done much to restore the peace of Donnvail, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for it. The lords of Carn Dûm will learn not to trifle with the Trév Gállorg!''Beyond the eastward arch, the servants of Muiráthakh protect the Silent Judge and his unlawful rulings. Bring allies and make an example of these warriors and crossbowmen who serve him.'The scout Bethad has found that the worm-cave is the home to a venerated elder Gorthorog called Ulkrank.Ulkrank, the ancient Gorthorog, can be found somewhere in the depths of the cave.

Bethad has asked you to explore the cave, until you are able to find and slay Ulkrank himself.Defeat worms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Fonn is back at Tyrn Lhuig.

Having defeated Ulkrank, Bethad asked you to tell his friend Fonn of your deeds.'I am glad you are here. I was wounded by one of these cursed worms and have been forced to hide in the dark corners of this cave for some days. But now that my wound is almost healed, I have learned much by spying on the comings and goings of the Gertheryg.

'This cave is inhabited by an ancient Gorthorog called Ulkrank. He is an old one...the oldest such creature I have ever seen. The other brutes seem to treat him with the greatest reverence. He may perhaps be their ruler.

'For my own part, I am ready to leave this place, but if you would, I think you can do more here. Slay as many of these vile worms as you can, and fight your way to the depths of the cave where Ulkrank dwells. Once he is defeated, tell my friend Fonn in Tyrn Lhuig of your victory. He will surely be concerned about me.''You bring good tidings! I am very pleased to hear that Bethad is safe, for I had been cursing myself for sending him alone into such danger.

'But it is also a great deed that you have done, too, my friend. The Gertheryg are some of the most terrible fighters for the Iron Crown, and if they can be dismayed or disheartened even a little by the death of their elder, then the Enemy will be sorely weakened as a result. You have truly won a great victory!''I will rest a little longer and then return to Aughaire on my own. Have no fear for my safety -- you must go into the cave to slay Ulkrank!''My wound is healed now. Have no fear for me. I will return on my own to Tyrn Lhuig in my own time.'Cenlieg the Lore-Master has lost his party, and is now stranded at Kauppa-kohta. He sent his raven to find traces of his fellows, but the raven has not returned.Cenlieg's raven may be found near a rocky outcropping to the south-west of Kauppa-kohta

Cenlieg has asked you to find his missing raven.Find and rescue Cenlieg's ravenCenlieg is at Kauppa-kohta, north-east of the rocky outcropping where you found the raven.

You found Cenlieg's raven, but it quickly hid in panic. Perhaps you should speak again with Cenlieg.Talk to Cenlieg'Through the trees he says, just go through the line of trees. Ha! Surely we would be wandering for a week or more that way. My shortcut is the only way to...oh my, I beg your pardon!

'You see, my fellows and I were on our way to...well, that is of no consequence. In their haste, they took a road that we should not have been following, and it seems I have lost track of them.  

'I quickly dispatched my raven towards the rocky outcropping to the south-west to see if any tracks were left in the snow. So far, she has not returned. I was about to go retrieve her, thinking she got stuck in the trees, but my bear-friend, Linden, is not well and requires my help here. If you are headed in that direction, could you look for her?''How she managed to get lost among Wargs is a mystery to me, but obviously it was through no fault of my own.  

'She seems to be beckoning towards the icy cliffs to the west. I believe my fellowship must have been there within the last day or two. I think we will be able to follow their trail if Linden here is able to raise her spirits.''Please, if you can find my raven, we can learn in what direction my fellows were heading last.'The raven looks up at you with a forlorn stare, but suddenly starts flapping its wings furiously and darts off towards an opening in the rocks.The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Rising Chord is considered to be a remarkable collection of songs all of a theme that speaks to the heart of the Free Peoples of Middle-earth in one voice, be they Men, Elves, or dwarves. Drawing on the tradition of each of these peoples, the songs resound with a clarion call of freedom and hope that is unsullied by the slightest trace of sorrow, fear, or hate. There are some who claim that this work is shallow and without dimension, but to the adept minstrel, it is a description of form unparalleled by other works. Unfortunately, the book and its strident forms became 'unfashionable' after the fall of the North Kingdom, as a deep lethargy and cynicism crept over the kingdoms of Men, and the Elves withdrew ever further into their forest strongholds.

'Nowadays, The Rising Chord is quite difficult to find, and even when a copy is found -- such as this -- it is incomplete at best. Perhaps the famed Elvish minstrel Lindir might be able to tell you more about it.Talk to Lindir at the Last Homely House in RivendellDIRECTIONS TO THE PAGE DROPS.The Rising Chord, page 3The Rising Chord, page 4The Rising Chord, page 11The Rising Chord, page 14The Rising Chord, page 16The Rising Chord, page 23The Rising Chord, page 27The Rising Chord, page 28'Ah, I see you have come upon a copy of The Rising Chord -- one of my favourite works, I must say. It is not truly a work for Elves in these the autumn days of our people in Middle-earth, but for the younger races it may prove to be a beacon of light which guides you through the coming darkness. 

'Yet who am I to say? There are still some among the Elves who are young at heart and who would strive for such joy and hope as they might capture with their own two hands, even in the waning days of our glory. Seek them out then, the missing songs and melodies of The Rising Chord. No matter your destiny, they will let you approach it with your head held high and filled with the certainty that you shall prevail, come what may.

'It is at the least a fine way to die, is it not? Twas only a jest! Go and find your pages and trouble me no more, young one...your destiny is your own!'Crannog's design has been revealed: your Fém is to stand for the Trév Gállorg in Clúcath against the Trév Duvárdain and their Angmarim masters.Osbail is by Crannog's ruling-seat in Aughaire, Camran is at the centre island of the settlement, and Tasgall is still hunting hill-beasts north of Aughaire and south-east of Nád Nathair.

Crannog has advised you to speak with the people of Aughaire to learn what you may about your upcoming challenge: the Clúcath with the Trév Duvárdain.Crannog is at his ruling-seat in Aughaire, west of the settlement's central island.

You have spoken with some of the Trév Gállorg and should return to Crannog as he advised.Crannog is at his ruling-seat in Aughaire, west of the settlement's central island.

Crannog is waiting for you to speak with him so you may travel together to Fail-á-Khro for the Clúcath with the Trév Duvárdain.Talk to Crannog and travel to Fail-á-KhroCrannog is at his ruling-seat in Aughaire, west of the settlement's central island.

You have emerged victorious from the Clúcath with the Trév Duvárdain and should now speak with Crannog.'The time of Clúcath is almost upon us, and you must prepare yourself to do battle; despite your prowess against the warriors of the Trév Gállorg, it will not be enough. You need a greater ally: that of knowledge.

'Many among the Trév Gállorg witnessed the unlawful breaking of the rites of Clúcath in which Nekhtan's father was slain, and they may have some words by which you will avoid a similar fate.

'Osbail, by my ruling-seat, can tell you how Násan met his end and perhaps give you some manner of avoiding a similar fate. Camran, at the centre island of Aughaire, will tell you which of the warriors of the Trév Duvárdain you may be facing. Tasgall, hunter of the hill-beasts south-east of Nád Nathair, may have learned something during his sojourn that will be of aid to you. Speak with these three, and any others who may be able to help, and return to me before long. The Clúcath will not wait forever.''I salute you, ${PLAYER}! I have not yet returned to Aughaire, for I have been keeping a watch on Nád Nathair to ensure that the hill-beasts do not rise again. Rona will understand.

'You have been chosen as the warrior for the Clúcath? I am frozen with amazement! I believed the death of Násan and the betrayal of the Trév Duvárdain had ended that rite forever. You defeated Nekhtan in single combat? He cannot have been pleased with that; if Avair is chosen as the warrior of the Trév Duvárdain, Nekhtan will see it as a lost opportunity to avenge himself upon his father's murderer.

'I have carved a small figure from the tooth of Murragrath, and I would like you to have it. Carry it among your belongings. Perhaps it will bring you fortune in the trials ahead.''I am sure the Trév Duvárdain have not forgotten how you destroyed the standards that flew above Tór Gailvin, and neither have I. I will tell you what I can about what you may face at the Clúcath.

'The warrior who slew Násan and sullied the rite is named Avair, and she is a master of the bloodied arts. She has slain many of our people in the years since the last Clúcath, and her name strikes fear into the hearts of all but Nekhtan, who has sworn to end her. Domongart, leader of the Trév Duvárdain, surely thinks that Nekhtan will be our warrior; he may put forth Avair in the hope that Nekhtan will be blinded with rage and easily defeated. It is difficult to say, and there are others.

'Drostan of the Trév Duvárdain is fierce, but his most recent battle ended in a cowardly retreat; he fought with Fonghala for one day and one night before he escaped. He favours fighting from a distance, and fears the dangers of standing toe-to-toe with an opponent. He is surely hurting for the chance to redeem his honour. Perhaps I have been no help at all, ${PLAYER}. Even so, I hope that you can make use of what I have said.''You have taken the wisdom of my people? For your sake and the sake of the Trév Gállorg, I hope you have, Southron.

'The Clúcath will soon begin at Fail-á-Khro. Speak to me when you are prepared, and we will go to meet destiny.''I should have known, when you helped me against the Wargs that came to Fasach-larran, that you would bring great honour to the Trév Gállorg. But I fear this may be too much for you, ${PLAYER}. The Trév Duvárdain have already broken the rules of Clúcath once; who is to say they will not do so again?

'Our great warrior, Násan, met Avair of the Trév Duvárdain as the sun rose above Fail-á-Khro. For a time, everything seemed as it should: first Avair had the mastery, and then Násan took the advantage. But as the Clúcath drew on, the chieftain of the Trév Duvárdain, Domongart, became restless. Next to him sat two strangers, Men of Carn Dûm, it is thought.

'Domongart suddenly rose to his feet and gave forth a tremendous cry: a pre-arranged signal? Násan's attention was diverted, and at that moment Avair knocked him to the ground. The Clúcath was over, but Domongart was not satisfied, and he demanded that Avair slay her helpless foe. This she did, and it was clear that the Trév Duvárdain, our kin of old, were hopelessly corrupted. Násan's son, Nekhtan, swore to avenge his father, but you must do it in his place, for you have bested him, and he is not fit to stand for the Trév Gállorg in the Clúcath. Be watchful, ${PLAYER}, when the Clúcath begins. You must be prepared for anything.''Speak to the people of Aughaire to learn all that you can of the rite of Clúcath. You must use their knowledge to prevail.

'Nekhtan believed he would be the warrior to face the Trév Duvárdain in Clúcath, and he is angry to have been deprived of it. He is hot-tempered. If you care what he thinks, speak to him! But as chieftain, I believe he must come to terms with his weakness on his own time.''We go now to Clúcath, ${PLAYER}. I hope that you are ready.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest, and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'At last we have been redeemed, ${PLAYER}, and have triumphed over the Trév Duvárdain and their Angmarim masters. Násan has been avenged, and Domongart has paid for his betrayals.

'He who was called Drostan has come to join the Trév Gállorg, but his name is lost; he will be known henceforth as Soltakh, "He Who Is Changed." She who was called Avair did not accept this choice; she is now neither Trév Gállorg nor Trév Duvárdain. She has done much evil and could only see that in others; though Nekhtan might have forgiven her past, I feel she could not. She wanders now the trackless lands, a woman with neither name nor home.

'Your Fém is at an end, and I salute you not only as chieftain of the Trév Gállorg, but as Crannog, humble tribesman of Aughaire, as well. Perhaps Tasgall's gift brought you good fortune after all, and perhaps it did not. May you decide for yourself whether you believe in it, but believe always in yourself, friend ${PLAYER}. I deem that you make your own fortune.'The second token of honour, the stone from the Cairn of Honour, was more difficult to obtain, as it required entering hostile territory without engaging in battle.Donnan and his brother are at Aughaire on the west side of the Hillman town, awaiting your response to their challenge.

Arzhur has asked you once again to fight against the challengers from a hostile clan loyal to Angmar.Accept Donnan's challengeArzhur is at Aughaire

You should return to Arzhur with news of your victory against the second challenge.'Once again it is time to fight for the honour of my clan. You have proven yourself worthy, Southron, but now the rules have changed.

'This time, the two brothers Donnan and Mungan claim to have been slighted by our clan. They lie, but the Judge has permitted both of them to fight us at once. Be wary of the younger brother, Donnan, for he is the fiercer of the two. You can find them here in Aughaire, on the west side of the town, waiting for you to answer their challenge.''Again, you have triumphed, Southron, this time against unfair odds. But I fear that despite your victory, matters will soon grow more desperate.''Who are you to fight for this clan of weaklings? Well, it matters not, we will crush you, and then the Iron Crown will take Aughaire!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Shanty-singer's Compendium appears to have been penned by several different and very opinionated authors over a number of years—or perhaps centuries. This particular copy is heavily annotated, with a previous reader's thoughts scribbled into its margins. Several passages have been struck through entirely, and you notice that several shanties have been rearranged, replacing mentions of Umbar with Bree-town. Even so, it provides a thorough history of sea-shanties and contains a collection of songs sung by sailors and Mariners of Gondor and Umbar.Talk to Mick Maidenhair in the Dwarrow-ward, in Bree-townThe Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 8The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 14The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 21The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 28The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 32The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 35The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 41The Shanty-singer's Compendium, Page 44'What can I help you with, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Mick Maidenhair that 'The Shanty-singer's Compendium' seems to be missing several of its pages, including entire verses of a few of the sea-shanties transcribed by its various authors.

'They are? I knew that my copy was missing a few pages of the introduction and the historical accounts, but to be honest, I just thought that's how the shanties went! Well, that would explain a few things....'

Mick Maidenhair's face flushes, but he quickly composes himself.

'If you are somehow able to find those missing pages, I would be delighted to join you in practicing those shanties. Why, it'll be like learning whole new songs!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Treatise of Valour was written by a council of captains who once commanded the great fortresses of the ancient kingdom of Arnor during the height of its power. It was said to have been the foundation stone of the principles of leadership and warfare for the warriors of Arnor and offered knowledge and teaching that proved key to staving off the assaults of Angmar for so long as the North Kingdom stood. All the remaining copies of the Treatise were believed lost or burned in the Sack of Fornost.

Many pages of this book, The Treatise of Valour, have been torn or burned, rendering the volume nearly unreadable. Perhaps Boromir of Gondor, recently come north, will know something about it.Many pages of this book, Treatise of Valour, have been torn or burned, rendering the volume nearly unreadable. Perhaps Boromir of Gondor, recently come north, will know something about it.Talk to Boromir in RivendellIt is believed that the Angmarim and their allies carried off at least one or two copies of the Treatise of Valour at the Battle of Fornost. Perhaps you can find some of the missing pages in Angmar or on the Enemy's minions in the Misty Mountains.Treatise of Valour, page 2Treatise of Valour, page 3Treatise of Valour, page 5Treatise of Valour, page 10Treatise of Valour, page 12Treatise of Valour, page 15Treatise of Valour, page 18Treatise of Valour, page 22'What tidings do you bring me, ${PLAYER}? Not more bad news, I hope.

'Oh? Quite the contrary I see, if mine eyes do not decieve me. Is that not a copy of The Treatise of Valour? The title is well-known to me, as it should be to any who would call themselves a Captain of Men, for there are few works that stand in higher stead to those who would lead and inspire, but it is common knowledge that the last copies were destroyed with the fall of the Lords of Arthedain. How is it that you have come upon this book?

'Ah, but I see you are a bearer of poor tidings after all, for see here: many of the writings are torn away and lost to us forever. But nay, it is no use bemoaning our fortunes, for half a book is better than no book at all -- and it leaves hope that perhaps the remaining pages are not forever beyond our reach. But where to find them? That is the true question!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

You have heard of this book before -- The Way of the Hunter is believed to have been a work produced by Elven hunters who were set to the task of guarding the approaches of the hidden city of Gondolin, which fell to the forces of darkness in the First Age. While this work outlived its authors and has been copied innumerable times since, many of those copies were produced by Men from far older works and are imperfect in their translation, leaving several important points untold.

This copy of The Way of the Hunter appears to have been a much more accurate work, probably transcribed by Elven scribes in Rivendell or Lórien -- but unfortunately someone seems to have intentionally defaced the book, ripping several pages from it. Legolas of the Elves might be able to shed some light on the text.This copy of The Way of the Hunter appears to have been a much more accurate work, probably transcribed by Elven scribes in Rivendell or Lórien -- but unfortunately someone seems to have intentionally defaced the book, ripping several pages from it. Legolas of the Elves might be able to shed some light on the text.Talk to Legolas in RivendellThe missing pages may be found among the snowy peaks of the Misty Mountains or the former realm of the Witch-king, Angmar.The Way of the Hunter, page 1The Way of the Hunter, page 3The Way of the Hunter, page 4The Way of the Hunter, page 8The Way of the Hunter, page 17The Way of the Hunter, page 20The Way of the Hunter, page 21The Way of the Hunter, page 28'Ah, the Way of the Hunter. My father had an excellent copy of this text that I read in my youth. You are lucky indeed to have found one so accurately copied! Alas, few scholars of Men have a proper appreciation for the older Elven tongues in these latter ages and much is often lost in the transcriptions they create.

'If only some mischievous villain had not seen fit to tear out several of the pages, you would have a great prize in hand with this book! Do not despair though...there are other copies and pages of this tome in the world, and if you can gather them, you may well be able to complete the text after all.

'The wisdom of my brethren many ages past lies within those pages, and you would be much advised to study it should that opportunity present itself.'Thorgest has been poisoned by the Gauredain, and Afwald, Thorgest's assistant, believes the chief spirit-caller of the Gauredain may hold the key to curing him.The Gauradan-camps lie to the south of Thorgest's dwelling.

Afwald has asked you to try and find the chief spirit-caller of the Gauredain in the last desperate hope that he may hold the cure to the poison that is slowly killing Thorgest.Discover if Jakka of the Gauredain carries an antidoteAfwald is at Thorgest's dwelling, north of the Gauradan-camps.

You should bring the Ice-fang antidote to Afwald before Thorgest succumbs to the poison.Bring the Ice-fang antidote to AfwaldThorgest is at his dwelling, with Afwald.

Afwald has told you that Thorgest is fading quickly and that you must administer the antidote to the dying dwarf before it is too late.'I beseech you, ${PLAYER}! I do not think Master Thorgest has much longer to live, unless we can find some way to cure his illness. With his brother gone, I do not wish to bear sole witness to the end of his line in this harsh and lonely wilderness.

'Before he fell to this delerium, Thorgest spoke to me of the wolf-men who felled him. It seems that there is some defiler among them that was likely the culprit of his downfall... and perhaps the only one who would know its cure.

'If you can find this spirit-caller, perhaps he carries the cure to this terrible malady on his person. Whatever you do, please hurry!''Have you defeated Jakka yet? He may have the cure for Thorgest.''What is that you have there? The Gauradan spirit-caller carried it? It looks awful... but time has run out for poor Master Thorgest.

'Whatever that is, it cannot do any further harm, and if there is any chance it might save him, we must take it! Please, see to him immediately!''Hurry! There is little time...give Thorgest the antidote!''What? What is this? Are you trying to poison me further? It is horrible! Let me die rather than drink another drop of it! No! Let me be...!'Fotri, overseer of the dwarves of Forochel, awaits the delivery of broken great swords at the dwarf-home of Zigilgund.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver broken great swords from the Dourhands and Gauredain surrounding Zigilgund in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to the dwarf-overseer Fotri encamped at the dwarf-home.Wanted: Broken great swords from the Dourhands and Gauredain surrounding the dwarf-home of Zigilgund. Deliver the items to Fotri, overseer of the settlement.'Good to see that their number will dwindle. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The dwarves at Gabilshathûr await a delivery of gilded sword sheaths to be made to Avar.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver gilded sword sheaths from the Orcs surrounding Gabilshathûr to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Orcs surrounding Gabilshathûr. Delivery of the items should be made to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Orcs surrounding Gabilshathûr.'We'll make proper use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Afwald, the provisioner at the dwarf-ruin of Hrimbarg, awaits the return of gilded sword sheaths.You have accepted a task to deliver gilded sword sheaths from the Gauredain and goblins found in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains to Afwald. Afwald can be found within the ruins at Hrimbarg, also found in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Gauredain and goblins in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains. You can deliver these items to Afwald within the ruins at Hrimbarg in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Gilded sword sheaths from the Gauredain and goblins in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains.'Good to know that their number is dwindling every time that you step out into the wild, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Fotri, overseer of the dwarves of Forochel, awaits the delivery of great scales at the dwarf-home of Zigilgund.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver great scales from the worms surrounding Zigilgund in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to the dwarf-overseer Fotri encamped at the dwarf-home.Wanted: Great scales from the worms surrounding the dwarf-home of Zigilgund. Deliver the items to Fotri, overseer of the settlement.Wanted: Great scales from the worms surrounding the dwarf-home of Zigilgund.'It will take a while to cure these, so we had better get to the process. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The dwarves at Gabilshathûr await a delivery of pure black fur to be made to Avar.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black fur from the beasts surrounding Gabilshathûr to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts surrounding Gabilshathûr. Delivery of the items should be made to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Pure black fur from the beasts surrounding Gabilshathûr.'We'll make proper use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Fotri, overseer of the dwarves of Forochel, awaits the delivery of pure black fur at the dwarf-home of Zigilgund.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black fur from the bears in the areas surrounding Zigilgund in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to the dwarf-overseer Fotri encamped at the dwarf-home.Wanted: Pure black fur from the bears surrounding the dwarf-home of Zigilgund. Deliver the items to Fotri, overseer of the settlement.'We will use this wisely. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Afwald, the provisioner at the dwarf-ruin of Hrimbarg, awaits the return of pure black skins.You have accepted a task to deliver pure black skin from the beasts found in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains to Afwald. Afwald can be found within the ruins at Hrimbarg, also found in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains. You can deliver these items to Afwald within the ruins at Hrimbarg in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains.'Supplies are supplies at the moment, thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Thorgest's brother Thorkell was killed a few days earlier by worms.The worm caves are to the south-east of Thorgest's dwelling.

Thorgest has asked you to find and return Thorkell's belongings.'Ah, thank you for the help, ${PLAYER}. I will recover in time from these wounds, but nothing can help my brother, Thorkell. When last I saw him he was trapped in the jaws of a great worm. I can only hope his end was swift.

'The worms will have left naught to bury, but they do not eat dwarf-steel. If you dare venture within their caves and retrieve what remains of my brother's belongings, I shall reward you richly.

'You can find the worm caves to the south-east.''Ah, is this all that remains of my fair brother Thorkell? Would that I had fallen in his stead....

'But nay, I speak as a fool in my mourning! My dear brother was ever the warrior and adventurer -- moreso than I. He gave his life as he chose to save mine, and I'll never again gainsay his valour for doing so. Nor will I dishonour his name by failing to live as he chose that I should.

'It seems that I have much to do, ${PLAYER}, and wallowing in my grief while the merciless wind wears down these mountains will not get it done!'White wolves have destroyed the sentry hut on the forest's edge. If the wolves continue to encroach upon the trading-camp they could threaten the flow of supplies from the hunters to the North.Wolves can be found across much of Taur Orthon.
Urho has asked you to help him by hunting the wolves which destroyed his tent.Defeat wolves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Urho is on the north-west road, next to his ruined guard-post.

You have slain many wolves and should now report to Urho with news of your success.Talk to Urho'The susi have always been an evil breed, but with the onset of this frozen winter, they have become more bold and far more dangerous. They raid our guard tents and some have even assaulted Kauppa-kohta.

'If you wish to gain the trust of the Lumi-väki of Kauppa-kohta, you will hunt down the susi and kill as many as you can. They prowl all around us.

'Hurry! I do not wish to become the susi's next meal!''The susi have been pushed back into the forest? It will be long before they are bold enough to attack one of our camps again.

'Still, it is not enough. The summers have been warmer than usual, making more time for the susi to breed. We will have to kill the den-mother to keep the pack's size at bay.''You must show the susi that they are not the masters of this forest.'Glóin has an idea where the Dourhands may have taken Bingo, and thinks you should try to assault the fortress and rescue the hobbit.Dourhands can be found in the fortress of Gabilazan, in the High Crag of the Misty Mountains.

Glóin believes you can learn where the Dourhands are holding Bingo Boffin if you attack them at the fortress of Gabilazan.Defeat Dourhands at the fortress of Gabilazan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You heard some shouting from the southern reaches of Gabilazan. Is that where Bingo is being held captive?Find the courtyard in the southern reaches of GabilazanBingo was being held in a cage in Gabilazan, but somehow he has managed to escape!

You should search the slopes north of Gabilazan for any sign of Bingo's escape.Search the slopes just north of Gabilazan for any sign of Bingo's escapeSpalvi Dourhand stands by a deep, dark hole in the mountain, just north of Gabilazan. What story does HE have to tell?Talk to Spalvi Dourhand by the hole in the mountainSpalvi Dourhand has had a change of heart, and wishes to help you rescue Bingo.

Spalvi helped free Bingo from Gabilazan, but the hobbit fell down a hole during the escape. The Dourhand now wants to help you save Bingo, and thinks you can retrieve bundles of food and bottles of water from Gabilazan that you can drop down the hole to Bingo, if the hobbit survived the fall.Collect bundles of food from Gabilazan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect water-bottles from Dourhands in Gabilazan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Spalvi Dourhand is standing next to a deep, dark hole just north of Gabilazan.

You have collected supplies for Bingo, and should drop them down the hole to him, in case the hobbit survived the fall.Drop the supplies from Gabilazan down the deep, dark holeSpalvi Dourhand is standing by the deep, dark hole just north of Gabilazan, in the Misty Mountains.Talk to Spalvi Dourhand by the deep, dark hole'I have done some thinking, ${PLAYER}, and I think I know where those Dourhand villains brought your friend Bingo. They have launched a number of attacks on my people from a stronghold in the High Crag. That place is called Gabilazan, and it seems to be the Dourhand base of operations in this stretch of the Misty Mountains.

'Go to Gabilazan and defeat the Dourhands there in order to learn where they are keeping Bingo Boffin!'

You wonder if this is the extent of Glóin's daring plan, and tell him so. He laughs heartily.

'What more could a plan offer than this, ${PLAYER}? The opportunity to fight villainous Dourhands is justification enough for any such plan, if you want the opinion of this dwarf!''The Dourhands have launched a number of attacks on my people from a stronghold in the High Crag. That place is called Gabilazan, and it seems to be their base of operations in this stretch of the Misty Mountains.

'Go to Gabilazan and defeat the Dourhands there in order to learn where they are keeping Bingo Boffin!''The Dourhands have launched a number of attacks on my people from a stronghold in the High Crag. That place is called Gabilazan, and it seems to be their base of operations in this stretch of the Misty Mountains.

'You defeated many Dourhands but did not find Bingo? You should return to Gabilazan and conduct a more effective search!''Bingo has somehow escaped captivity? That is wonderful news... but where is he?

'You should search the slopes just to the north of Gabilazan for sign of the missing hobbit.''Wait! Hear me out!''Well? Where is Bingo?''Wait! Hear me out, ${PLAYER}! I am not your enemy!

'It is true that I deceived you and your hobbit friend, but I did not truly wish for either of you to come to harm. The more I dwelled upon it, the more I realized I could not stand with my brothers in this. When their guard relaxed, I freed Bingo from his cage and we fled from Gabilazan. But in our haste, that poor hobbit did not pay close attention to his footing, and the ground gave way!

'Bingo fell down this hole, and I fear he fell beyond the reach of my voice, for he has not responded to my calls. I think I heard splashing from far below, but I cannot be certain. If he did survive, he will need supplies. If you return to Gabilazan and collect bundles of food and the bottles of water the dwarves carry, we can drop them down the hole after Bingo! If he did survive, he will need any of the water and food we can provide to him.

'Please trust me, ${PLAYER}! I will do all I can to make this right!''It is true that I deceived you and your hobbit friend, but I did not truly wish for either of you to come to harm. The more I dwelled upon it, the more I realized I could not stand with my brothers in this. When their guard relaxed, I freed Bingo from his cage and we fled from Gabilazan. But in our haste, that poor hobbit did not pay close attention to his footing, and the ground gave way!

'Bingo fell down this hole, and I fear he fell beyond the reach of my voice, for he has not responded to my calls. I think I heard splashing from far below, but I cannot be certain. If he did survive, he will need supplies. If you return to Gabilazan and collect bundles of food and the bottles of water the dwarves carry, we can drop them down the hole after Bingo! If he did survive, he will need any of the water and food we can provide to him.

'Please trust me, ${PLAYER}! I will do all I can to make this right!''Drop those supplies down the hole, ${PLAYER}! If Bingo survived the fall, he will be glad to have them!''I feel really bad about this, ${PLAYER}. I swear to you that I will help you find your friend, wherever he has ended up on my account. I am certain he will be fine, and we will all laugh about this when it is done. I will do some thinking about our next step, but know that I will not abandon the search until we find him safe and sound. I will make this right!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'An Author Absent' (Recommended Level: 46)</rgb>Driftwood has washed up on the shores of the Brandywine River and is a resource worth harvesting for the League of the Axe.Driftwood can be found on the shores of the Brandywine, near Trader's Wharf.

You should collect driftwood for the League of the Axe.Collect driftwood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tegwen Spurrey can be found at Trader's Wharf.

You should return to Tegwen Spurrey with the driftwood you have collected.'We could use all the supplies we can get. I'm not about to level the whole forest just to make the mine of Writhenset safe. We need to make a home for us here in the Wildwood.

'With that said, the Brandywine River provides. A large amount of driftwood just washed up on the banks of the river this morning. Collect it for me and the League will be grateful.''Ah, this driftwood will do nicely. We should be able to fashion some of this into mining tools. Thank you for assisting the League, ${CLASS}.'Glóin recalled his experience with Thorin and Company when they faced the Great Goblin on their journey with Bilbo Baggins.The Great Goblin is in the throne room of Goblin-town, to the north-east of Glóin's camp.

Glóin spoke with a heavy heart of the evil that looms at the centre of Goblin-town. He then asked you to enter the evil place and defeat the Great Goblin before his threat grows too large to contain.Defeat the Great GoblinGlóin is at his camp, just outside the entrance to Goblin-town.

You entered Goblin-town and searched deep into the heart of the place for the throne room. There, you defeated Ashûrz, the Great Goblin. With his defeat, you have sundered the strength of the goblins in Goblin-town and lessened the threat they present against the people east and west of the Misty Mountains. You should return to Glóin with your report.Speak with Glóin at his camp in the Misty Mountains.'${PLAYER}, I do not speak often of our journeys with my Lord Thorin, but I feel I must now. Long ago, we travelled through these mountains with Burglar Baggins and became captives of the goblins of Goblin-town. Taken down into the dark for what seemed forever, we eventually came to the centre of that place and met the Great Goblin.

'We were without hope in that place, for it was so dark that even our dwarf-sight was diminished and all hope seemed lost. The Great Goblin discovered weapons upon our persons that had been used to fight his kind in ages past and became furious, and hope drew ever farther from our hearts. Thorin, my lord and our leader, remained defiant, and though he attempted to placate the goblin, nothing would calm his rage. At the moment when all seemed most bleak, Gandalf the Grey appeared and slew the Great Goblin, freeing us from Goblin-town and the clutches of the goblins there.

'This news of a new Great Goblin puts a cloud of dread over my heart. If it is true, then we have no choice but to destroy him before the goblins are organized enough to march to the east against Dáin Ironfoot and the dwarves of the Lonely Mountain, or to the west against the Men of Eriador. I ask you to make your way into the throne room with your allies and find this Great Goblin and end him before his shadow grows so large that it looms over all within it. Will you do this?''I remember the tunnels seeming to last forever. We were dragged down for days into the dark and then brought to a great hall wherein the Great Goblin sat on a large stone and from there ruled over the goblins of Goblin-town.

'Seek out this new wretched leader and end his reign before it truly begins.''This old dwarf has had hope restored by your hand, ${PLAYER}.

'All dwarves everywhere will know your name when the threat climbing across this world is quelled, you have my word on this. The word of a dwarf is stronger than the mountains upon which we stand.

'Ashûrz is no more, and we have you to thank for this.'Glóin believes that a scout sent to the north has become a prisoner of the goblins of Goblin-town.Seek out Aeglas in Goblin-town, to the North of Rivendell, in the Misty Mountains.

Glóin explained that Elrond dispatched an Elf to investigate the stirrings of goblins from their mountain stronghold at Goblin-town. Fearing that Aeglas has been captured, he hopes that you may yet find him alive within the depths of the goblin city.Find Aeglas within Goblin-town'I have concerns ${PLAYER}. Elrond asked if we had seen a scout dispatched to Goblin-town from Rivendell. He surely would have come by here, but we have had no sight of him.

'I fear he may have been captured by the goblins. If this is true, then I can only hope that he lives still and is kept somewhere in the prisons within Goblin-town. Venture within and see if you can find Aeglas.''Aeglas went to scout Goblin-town...I fear he may have been captured. Find him quickly and rescue him, if it proves he needs it!''I thank you and Master Elrond for your concern, but I am in no imminent danger.

'The goblins are strong foes, but not so strong that I cannot leave this place. Yet, there is much that we may do together.'The Woodcutter's Brotherhood has learned of a horrid crime committed in Trestlebridge.Nellie Boskins is in Trestlebridge.

You should talk to Nellie Boskins about the altercation.Talk to Nellie BoskinsThe place of the altercation can be found in Trestlebridge.

You should find the scene of the altercation.Find the scene of the altercationThe place of the altercation can be found in Trestlebridge.

You should investigate the scene of the altercation.The start of the faint trail of blood can be found in Trestlebridge.

You should follow the trail and find out where it leads.Follow the faint trail of bloodThe small shed can be found by the river, near Trestlebridge.

You should investigate the scene around the small shed.The small shed can be found by the river, near Trestlebridge.

You should search nearby for more evidence.Whoever murdered the guard must have gone down-stream on a small boat.

You should search down-stream in the Wildwood for the murder suspect.Search the Wildwood for the murder suspectThe potential murder suspect can be found in the northern part of the Wildwood.

You should confront the murder suspect.Confront the murder suspectYou should return to Trestlebridge and lock up the brigand in the pillory.Lock up the murderer in the pillory at TrestlebridgeThe weary brigand can be found in Trestlebridge.

You should talk to the weary brigand.Talk to the weary brigand in TrestlebridgeAran Greenwith can be found in Trestlebridge.

You should talk to Aran Greenwith.Talk to Aran GreenwithReconstruction work continues in Trestlebridge.

You should survey the reconstruction of Trestlebridge.Survey the reconstruction of TrestlebridgeAran Greenwith can be found in Trestlebridge.

You should talk to Aran Greenwith.Talk to Aran Greenwith'${PLAYER}, I bear grave news. A guardsman has been killed in Trestlebridge. There are rumours in the air implicating a Woodcutter's Brotherhood member in an altercation with the deceased. You know as well as I that these are outrageous accusations. Someone must be spreading lies.

'Nellie Boskins has asked for you to investigate this matter since you are both respected by the Woodcutter's Brotherhood and well liked by the community of Trestlebridge for your past heroics. Speak with Nellie Boskins as soon as you are able and please clear our name of any wrongdoing.''I called upon you because you helped us to defend Trestlebridge. You are an impartial outsider that we all trust. I do not know who is to blame here. All I know is that I have a dead guardsman in Trestlebridge and no one saw who murdered him. That is correct, ${PLAYER}. I am very certain it was murder.

'I would like you to investigate the murder and return with whomever is responsible. The longer this murder goes unsolved, the more distrust will be sown throughout the townsfolk of Trestlebridge.''Not another step! Stay, stay back! Do you hear me?! I'll cut you up real good if you come any closer! Now leave me be!''Thank you, ${PLAYER}, for clearing the names of all those who associate themselves with the Woodcutter's Brotherhood. We have you to thank for keeping the Trestlebridge townsfolk working together as we rebuild and improve our daily lives. Nellie Boskins was grateful to see the murderer brought to justice so swiftly.

'Now that you have returned, you should have a look at how the reconstruction efforts are coming along. Giles Chadwick and others have been working very hard to rebuild the town.''I am worried still about what the brigand confessed to you. We now know that an Orc is commanding an organized effort to destroy Trestlebridge, both from outside and within. We must find this Orc soon, for the sake of everyone in the town. Until then, I hope that we are able to continue to thwart the enemy's plans.''No sense hiding it now that I'm caught. Yes, I killed the guard. Got paid well for it too. Then I had to flee on my boat when you started to sniff around my hiding spot. I weren't prepared for those river rapids though! That there was my downfall.

'I should have never listened to that Orc and taken the job! What?! I don't know which Orc! We weren't chums! He didn't give me a name! Now leave me be! You already caused me enough hurt for one day!'Kaj, the son of the Lossoth-chieftain Ora, seeks to perform an ancient and time-honoured ritual.The rope is at a scout's encampment just to the south of Pynti-peldot in Forochel. The sledge needs to be borrowed from Altti the trapper at Jänis-leiri, the Master of the Hunt's encampment by the road to the west. Thick furs can be skinned from any number of beasts in and around Itä-mâ.

Kaj has asked for your help obtaining certain items he needs to prepare for his ritual.Collect a coil of ropeCollect thick furs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Borrow a sledge from AlttiKaj is in Pynti-peldot in Forochel.

You have collected the ritual components and should return to Kaj.'Greetings, Sivullinen. I am Kaj of the Lumi-väki, also named Warg-skinner, Claw-wearer, Plague of the Susi-väki, and Spearer of the Ancient Mursu. I am embarking upon a delicate endeavour: an ancient Lumi-väki ritual. I will need certain things.

'Go to Altti the trapper at Jänis-leiri, west along the road, and ask him to loan me his sledge. I will also need rope. There should be a few extra coils at the scout-camp, out on a little island on the other side of the road, south of here. And furs, thick, soft, and warm; I do not care what kind of animal they are from. Something that will feel good on soft skin... what kind of look is that to give me? It is part of the ritual! 

'I do not have time for this; I must prepare. Go find these things for me, or get out of my sight and stay out of my way!''Who are you and what do you want?

'Kaj sent you? I am not sure what that boy thinks he is doing, having me lend one of my own sledges to a Sivullinen.

'Take it. But tell Kaj it will go very hard with him if anything happens to it!''Have you gathered the items I requested yet?''We keep supplies here for the use of all Lumi-väki. As Kaj and Ora both speak for you, you may take what you need.

'Altti? He is at Niko's camp. Follow the main road to the west, and it will be on the north side.''Ah, excellent! Now we can truly begin.'The Warg Blökk lies in wait along the western shore of the lake. This beast is under the influence of Angmar's evil and seeks to destroy the Lossoth at Kauppa-kohta.Blökk can be found on the west shore of the lake.

Mirja has asked you to slay the Warg Blökk in an effort to protect Kauppa-kohta.Defeat BlökkMirja is at Kauppa-kohta.

With Blökk defeated, you should return to Mirja with news of your victory.Talk to Mirja'Sivullinen, we must talk quietly, but quickly.  There is a horrific threat along the gentle shores of our lake. I have heard Valtteri's hunters gather in the twilight at the gates of the town in hurried whispers, readying themselves for some kind of assault. I try not to be a curious person, but if Valtteri is concerned, then the likes of me should bolt for the fair fields in the South!  

'I heard them talk of a Surmâja who by night watched us from the battered peaks and spoke to Angmar in a voice we dare not try to understand. This Surmâja, whom we call Blökk, was bred long ago in that horrid land and now waits in the shadows for the right time to signal an attack upon us.' 

'I fear Valtteri is not moving quickly enough. Someone needs to strike down this craven beast. Will you do this? Blökk wanders among the other Surmâja, along the west shore.''You killed Blökk? You just...walked up and defeated such a cunning beast? If Valtteri's hunters were so careful, I find it hard to believe that you were able to eliminate the Surmâja without more deliberate planning.  

'You might think we are a foolish people, ${PLAYER}, but we do not tolerate being insulted. Take this and be on your way. I will pay a real hero far more.'Mordor has sent forth many dire Lieutenants to lead the Dead of the Barrow-downs against the Free Peoples. The time has come to put an end to them.Defeat many Lieutenants of the EnemyMordor has sent forth many dire Lieutenants to lead the Dead of the Barrow-downs against the Free Peoples. The time has come to put an end to them.Defeat many Lieutenants of the EnemyMordor has sent forth many dire Lieutenants to lead the Dead of the Barrow-downs against the Free Peoples. The time has come to put an end to them.Defeat many Lieutenants of the EnemyThe servants of the Witch-king have long made the Barrow-downs a place of terror...now the time has come to drive forth that terror.Defeat many denizens of the Barrow-downsThe servants of the Witch-king have long made the Barrow-downs a place of terror...now the time has come to drive forth that terror.Defeat many denizens of the Barrow-downsThe servants of the Witch-king have long made the Barrow-downs a place of terror...now the time has come to drive forth that terror.Defeat many denizens of the Barrow-downsInformation received from the letters you recovered in Black Speech led Glóin to offer rewards for the defeat of Aklash, the leader of the Great Goblin's elite soldiers.Aklash is in the throne room of Goblin-town, to the north-east of Glóin's camp.

Tralli Gemfinder has asked you to find and defeat the leader of the Great Goblin's soldiers within the throne room of Goblin-town.Defeat AklashTralli Gemfinder is at Glóin's camp, just outside the entrance to Goblin-town.

You entered the Great Goblin's throne room and found Aklash. After a harrowing battle, you defeated the goblin and should now return to Tralli Gemfinder.Speak with Tralli Gemfinder at Glóin's camp'The information you recovered is invaluable, ${PLAYER}. With it, we now know many of the goblin-leaders holed up in Goblin-town. Master Glóin would like you to find and kill Aklash.

'Notes on the letter suggest that Aklash is the leader of the Great Goblin's soldiers. It is likely that you will find him in the Great Goblin's throne room.

'Goblin-town is to the north-east and the throne room can be found at the end of the long tunnels of Goblin-town. Find him, defeat him, then return to me.''Make your way to the throne room of Goblin-town and defeat Aklash.''Excellent work, ${PLAYER}. With the defeat of this soldier, we have further weakened the goblins of Goblin-town.

'The weaker we can keep this enemy, the better chance we have of keeping them from joining their allies in the west.'Troubled by the Thunder-lord's mention of Skorgrím and by reports of hostile dwarves occupying a fortress in the Misty Mountains, Elrond bids you speak to Glóin to request that the dwarves and Elves work together against the forces of Angmar.Glóin is at the dwarves' mountain camp north of Rivendell.

Elrond has asked you to speak to Glóin of a new council against Angmar.'I think the time has come -- indeed it is long past time -- that the Elves and dwarves confront the enemy in Angmar together. In our past council, we discussed...other matters...and Angmar was not our main concern. 

'Until now, we Elves have not paid too much heed to the Dourhands, deeming them a problem for dwarves to deal with. But now we see that Skorgrím is a servant of Angmar, and that he has roused the giants of the Misty Mountains against us. It is time for us to think again.

'Please go to Glóin and speak to him in my name, asking him to join again in council against our foes.''Ho! So at last the Elves deign to notice our concerns, now that we have almost paid the Dourhands ourselves!

'Still, I should not be so hasty. It is true that there has not been true amity between our peoples of late, but if we do not cleave together against the real Enemy, we shall most certainly be cloven by their black blades.

'Well, I thank you and Master Elrond for his words, and I will certainly meet with him to discuss our plans to deal with these Angmarim who have been causing both our houses so much trouble.''Please go to Glóin and speak with him in my name.'Glóin's scouts have learned that a venomous brood of spiders and their wretched queen lurk now beneath Helegrod.Atli Spider-bane is in the town of Archet, in Bree-land, far to the west of the Misty Mountains.

Sigrun and Greip learned that an evil plague of spiders has taken root in caverns beneath Helegrod, and their fell queen is known to Atli Spider-bane.'Helegrod's pride has always been its mighty halls, stretching as they do for many miles into the mountains, but Greip and I have discovered that someone -- something, I should say -- has been adding to their expanse.

'As we explored near to the walls of Helegrod, the snow gave way and Greip fell into an icy cavern, a great hollow space beneath the mountains. I returned to our camp to retrieve enough rope to pull him back to the surface. I moved with all the haste I could manage, but in the few moments I was gone, Greip heard the foul language of spiders and knew the truth of where he had fallen. When I pulled him back to the surface, he related to me his brief adventure in the dark, a story of webs and evil voices.

'An evil brood of spiders have taken root in the caverns beneath Helegrod, ${PLAYER}, and the name of their queen is fell: "Zaudru," she is called, and I know one who has encountered her before. Look for Atli Spider-bane in Bree-land, in the town of Archet, for he will be most interested to hear of the spider-plague that festers beneath Helegrod.''The strangest thing happened, ${PLAYER}. I returned to the place where Greip fell through the snow -- he refused to go himself, but I wanted to see if there was anything else to see -- and the hole is gone, sealed by a thick mat of spider-webs.

'These spiders must not be underestimated, ${PLAYER}. They learned of our intrusion and resented it; they will not permit another.''I fell a good distance into the cavern, ${PLAYER}, and my fall was broken by a sheet of ice -- I am fortunate that it was so thick! The spiders of that place....

'I really do not want to talk about it. I am sorry.''You have come to me by long and weary roads, ${PLAYER}. What has brought you here with such urgency? I see from your face that it can be no small matter.

'What is this? I had not thought to hear the name "Zaudru" again in my lifetime, ${PLAYER}. She is a cruel and wretched queen among her kind, and we have been face-to-face only once, though the memory is evil. She cannot be allowed to haunt the caverns beneath Helegrod, friend ${RACE}, for she will in the span of timeless years come forth with her brood to cover all of Middle-earth with her spawn.

'If you are willing to take up arms against this wretched foe, tell me so. If not, leave my sight a coward.'The Ranger Angwedh, armour-master of the Dúnedain, is always interested in obtaining leather and hides from the creatures of the Angle with which he can fashion the defences of his people.Wolves, lynxes, and the crawling avanc can be found throughout the Angle of Mitheithel.Collect hides from wolves or wargs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect hides from lynxes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect leather from avanc (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Ranger Angwedh is in Tornhad.'All who desire friendship with the Dúnedain must accept this simple truth: there is always more work to be done than there are hands to do it. If you offer your help to a Ranger, expect him to take you up on it!

'My name is Angwedh, and I am the Armour-master of Tornhad. Rangers favour medium armour made of leather and hides over the heavier plate worn by the knights of other lands. This, then, is my request of you: bring to me leather and hides of the avanc, wolves, and lynxes that can be found throughout the Angle of Mitheithel.

'Be careful not to mar too badly the skins that you bring to me! I need relatively-intact hides if I am to make armour of suitable quality for those sworn to Aragorn, our chief.''I asked you to bring to me leather and hides of the avanc, wolves, and lynxes that can be found throughout the Angle of Mitheithel. Be careful not to mar too badly the skins that you bring to me! I need relatively-intact hides if I am to make armour of suitable quality for those sworn to Aragorn, our chief.''These will do very nicely, ${PLAYER}! I thank you for these hides on behalf of the Dúnedain, and name you a friend of Tornhad.'Your success in destroying the traps of the Gauredain without frightening away the aurochs-herds has impressed Niko, the Master of the Hunt.Moose roam all over Itä-ma, near and far from Jänis-leiri.

Niko asked you to hunt moose and bring him their antlers and meat.Collect meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect antlers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Niko is at Jänis-leiri, west along the road from Itä-ma.

You should bring the moose-meat and antlers back to Niko.'As I said, my other hunters are currently occupied with other tasks, and one lies wounded in Pynti-peldot -- not that I am ever actually given enough hunters to get everything done in any case. Anyway, it looks like another harsh winter, and it is my job to make sure it does not wind up being unbearably thin. You can help with that.

'I want you to hunt sûri-hirvi. They roam all over Itä-ma, so you ought not to have any trouble finding them. We use their antlers for building, decoration, even fires -- oh yes, bone and antler will burn if you force air the right way, and it is not like there are so many trees around here that we can burn them freely. The meat, when frozen or salted or smoked or air-cured, can last for a season or more.

'Bring me many antlers and cuts of meat. We will find a use for them.''I want you to hunt sûri-hirvi. They roam all over Itä-ma, so you ought not to have any trouble finding them. We use their antlers for building, decoration, even fires -- oh yes, bone and antler will burn if you force air the right way, and it is not like there are so many trees around here that we can burn them freely. The meat, when frozen or salted or smoked or air-cured, can last for a season or more.''Ah, excellent. These will do nicely, Sivullinen.'Mordor has sent forth many dire Lieutenants to lead the Dead of the Barrow-downs against the Free Peoples. The time has come to put an end to them.Defeat many Lieutenants of the EnemyMordor has sent forth many dire Lieutenants to lead the Dead of the Barrow-downs against the Free Peoples. The time has come to put an end to them.Defeat many Lieutenants of the EnemyMordor has sent forth many dire Lieutenants to lead the Dead of the Barrow-downs against the Free Peoples. The time has come to put an end to them.Defeat many Lieutenants of the EnemyThe servants of the Witch-king have long made the Barrow-downs a place of terror...now the time has come to drive forth that terror.Defeat many denizens of the Barrow-downsThe servants of the Witch-king have long made the Barrow-downs a place of terror...now the time has come to drive forth that terror.Defeat many denizens of the Barrow-downsThe servants of the Witch-king have long made the Barrow-downs a place of terror...now the time has come to drive forth that terror.Defeat many denizens of the Barrow-downsConstant attacks have left Trestlebridge in disrepair, and the Woodcutter's Brotherhood is doing everything they can to restore the village.Wood scraps can be found throughout Trestlebridge.

