You should talk to Enid Nettlesting.Talk to Enid Nettlesting'You are becoming a valued member of the League of the Axe, ${CLASS}. How would you like to join a hunt? Nye Crowfoot here is leading a hunting expedition into the Wildwood. Speak to him when you are ready and he will fill you in with the details.''Yup, that's me, Nye Crowfoot. You ready to go hunting? We are going to start by checking some of the traps the League has set in the Wildwood. There will be more to do later but that's all you need to know right now. Let's get to it then and we will meet up in the woods after our tasks are complete.''You found some good meat and critters there, didn't you? Ha, and look at me, empty-handed. Happens to the best of us, I suppose. You though, just beginner's luck, that's for sure! Now then, on to the next part of our hunting expedition. Hunting larger game is easier if we lure the beasts out. I want you to take those live critters you caught and put them in the bear traps, alive.'

You are taken aback by Nye Crowfoot's words and see now that he is not a very honourable man. You agree to assist Nye but form your own plan as you start to question the reputable nature of the League of the Axe.

'All right, ${PLAYER}, get to it then.''How did you fair with the hunting expedition? Good to hear! These meats will keep us fed and keep the League profitable. You have done good work for us.'

You ask Enid what her thoughts on using live animals as bait for larger game.

'Now, ${PLAYER}, I hope those words are just a test on my good character! That is both cruel and lacking in the challenge and skill of hunting! The League of the Axe is a reputable group after all.'Huorns stomp through the southern Wildwood. They must be defeated lest their corruption spreads.Shadowbark Huorns can be found in the southern Wildwood.

Thornmane has asked you to defeat Shadowbark Huorns and look for clues.Defeat Shadowbark Huorns (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Look for clues of the Huorn corruptionThornmane is located in the southern Wildwood.

You should bring the rotted bark to Thornmane.'The darkness that has warped the Huorns of the Wildwood is quite concerning, hoom. I fear the corruption will spread throughout the rest of the forest. You must put the Shadowbark Huorns to rest, ${PLAYER}. While you are doing so, look for anything that might teach us what has whipped the Huorns into such a fury.''Thank you for dealing with the violent Huorns, friend. The piece of bark you brought me is full of shadow and rot. Give me a brief moment. I will speak to the Wildwood. Perhaps the trees know something.'A Sage of Eriador has invited you to recollect your experience with Gimli in the dwarf-fortress guarding the pass of Gabilazan.The Reflecting Pool in Rivendell will allow you to revisit 'The Last Refuge.'

A Sage of Eriador has challenged you to revisit your experience with Gimli by using the Reflecting Pool in Rivendell.Peer into the Reflecting Pool and revisit 'The Last Refuge'A Sage of Eriador is by the Reflecting Pool in Rivendell.

A Sage of Eriador in Rivendell is waiting to discuss what you have learned from revisiting your experience with Gimli in the pass of Gabilazan.'Always you press forwards, ${PLAYER}, eager to accept new challenges and face new evils; but there is value in remembering your past deeds. From them, you can learn much!

'Do you remember how you and the son of Glóin stormed the fortress of the Dourhands in the pass of Gabilazan, high atop the Misty Mountains? Together you stood against Skorgrím, but the Dourhand's defeat was not the complete victory for which Gimli hoped: what was the significance of the apparition that arose from Skorgrím's body?

'Peer into the Reflecting Pool and experience this memory anew, ${PLAYER}. You will find that recalling your past may do you much good in the present.''I am pleased that you have revisited your experience with Gimli, ${PLAYER}. Only through study of our past deeds can we gain knowledge that will aid us in the future.''You should peer into the Reflecting Pool, ${PLAYER}.'A Sage of Eriador has invited you to recollect your experience deep in the treasury of Helegrod, where you sought to prevent the restoration of the dragon Thorog.The Reflecting Pool in Rivendell will allow you to revisit 'Fire and Ice.'

A Sage of Eriador has challenged you to revisit your experience deep within the treasury of Helegrod by using the Reflecting Pool in Rivendell.Peer into the Reflecting Pool and revisit 'Fire and Ice'A Sage of Eriador is by the Reflecting Pool in Rivendell.

A Sage of Eriador in Rivendell is waiting to discuss what you have learned from revisiting your experience within the treasury of Helegrod.'Always you press forwards, ${PLAYER}, eager to accept new challenges and face new evils; but there is value in remembering your past deeds. From them, you can learn much!

'Do you remember your trials deep in the treasury of Helegrod, where you sought to prevent the restoration of the dragon Thorog? Drugoth the Gaunt-lord and the missing Nazgûl proved dangerous foes, but you stood against them both with courage and resolve!

'Peer into the Reflecting Pool and experience this memory anew, ${PLAYER}. You will find that recalling your past may do you much good in the present.''I am pleased that you have revisited your experience within the Helegrod treasury, ${PLAYER}. Only through study of our past deeds can we gain knowledge that will aid us in the future.''You should peer into the Reflecting Pool, ${PLAYER}.'Glóin related to you a small portion of the history of Helegrod and asked you to speak with the scouts he sent forth to watch the Bitter Stair for signs of the threat growing now behind the walls of Helegrod.The dwarves Sigrun and Greip were sent north of Glóin's camp in the Western Bruinen Source to scout Helegrod and have likely established a small camp among the trees in sight of the Bitter Stair.

Glóin has sent you to speak with the scouts he dispatched to watch the Bitter Stair that climbs to the gates of Helegrod, far to the north of his Western Bruinen Source camp.The dwarves Sigrun and Greip have several friends they need to consult and send information to before they venture back into Helegrod.Complete Ancient Foes of the Spider-baneComplete The Extent of the ThreatComplete Old FriendshipsComplete The Worm-hunter's LamentHelegrod is north-east of Sigrun and Greip's camp.

It is now time for you to see this ancient fortress for yourself.Find the entrance to Helegrod'I charge you with seeking the scouts I sent forth to Helegrod. Hear what they have uncovered, ${PLAYER}, and we may better know how to deal with the new threat that lurks within that dark place.

'They will have taken shelter somewhere to the north, within sight of the Bitter Stair that climbs up to the walls of the keep. Ask Sigrun for the rest of Durin's sad tale when you find him, for it concerns him most directly, and he will relate it to you if you wish to hear it.

'Use caution among the snowy slopes and search among the trees in sight of the Bitter Stair to find Sigrun and Greip, my scouts. They have authority to deal with any matters concerning Helegrod.''We have seen evil things behind the walls of Helegrod. The city is once again occupied, though not by dwarves.

'Sigrun will relate to you the tale of that place, and then we must decide what can be done about the new evils within Helegrod.''Sigrun will tell you the tale of fifth Durin, for it affects him most directly, though it happened so long ago. You will find him and his brother Greip among the trees in sight of the Bitter Stair, far to the north, near to Helegrod.''You must be a messenger from Lord Glóin, friend, for no right-minded ${RACE} would climb so close to Helegrod without such an errand, and I tell you truly that evil has come to live there again! Greip and I have seen all the proof we need to know the truth of that!

'Lord Glóin would have me tell you the tale of Durin, fifth of that name? I cannot refuse, though it is a painful story. If he spoke to you of the coming of Thorog, I will tell you then of what happened when Durin learned that the dragon had taken the city.

'A dragon sleeping on the treasures of Helegrod? Durin could not abide this terrible insult! He strapped to his back Mírdanant, a gift out of Eregion to his grandsire, passed from father to son through the long years, and strode with a small company of dwarves to the gates of Helegrod. Durin bellowed a challenge, and Thorog responded in kind! The battle was joined! So fierce was the fighting between dragon and dwarf that the very gates of Helegrod were split asunder!

'After weary days and nights, Durin began at length to tire; all those in his company lay dead or dying, save Bruni who was called Yellowbeard in later years, whose wounds were too severe for him to continue. Still mighty Thorog fought on, refusing to surrender the treasures of Helegrod to their rightful masters. Thorog prepared a white-hot jet of flame with which to roast Durin, whom he judged to be near death, but too late he realized his error. Too late! For lo! Durin swung Mírdanant with all that remained of his strength, and there ended Thorog's cruel reign.

'But Durin would not live to see victory, for Thorog's final gout of flame proved too strong for Durin's stout shield, and he was ended. Mírdanant was lost with Durin; it remains to this day buried in the neck of dead Thorog.

'Woe upon the dwarves! The Elves of Eregion had spoken words of protection upon the forging of the axe: "Darkness shall not fall upon Hadhodrond while the line of Durin bears Mírdanant in friendship with the Gwaith-i-Mírdain," but those words were proved false with the death of Durin!

'I trace my lineage through many fathers to Bruni, Yellowbeard that was, the only survivor of the battle of Helegrod. That he did not die a hero's death before the gates was his lifelong shame, and it is a mark that all his descendants have borne. I bear it too, and have taken onto myself the duty of seeing that the story is not forgotten: not the treachery of the Gwaith-i-Mírdain, not the cruelty of Thorog, and certainly not the last valiant deeds of fifth Durin in defence of his people.

'Evil has crept back into Helegrod, ${PLAYER}, and we will need the help of many folk if we are to combat it. Speak with Greip and myself to learn what must be done!'Glóin looked at the set of orders recovered from the Uruks in Goblin-town and found that they were also written in the Black Speech.Elrond is at the Last Homely House in Rivendell, south-west of Glóin's camp.

Glóin realized that another set of orders written in the Black Speech would take too long to translate and sent you to Rivendell in order to speak with Elrond directly.Glóin is at his camp just outside of Goblin-town, north-east of Rivendell.

Elrond Halfelven made marks upon the orders and warned you that the goblins had a new leader, a Great Goblin who was defiant of the Uruks' intrusion in Goblin-town. He sent you back to Glóin with the translated orders.'We have no time to waste, ${PLAYER}. Take these directly to Master Elrond in Rivendell and beseech him to translate them. We have already been delayed too long.

'It is vital that we learn what the Uruks are up to. If they are able to muster the goblins of the Misty Mountains, who knows what force will be arrayed against us?

'Quickly now. Rivendell lies to the south-west, if you were not aware already.''Quickly now, show those orders to Master Elrond at Rivendell. We haven't much time!''Master Glóin was wise to send you to me. It appears that his fears regarding the goblins have proven true. Though I am loath to say it, it appears a new goblin-leader has emerged within the hollows of Goblin-town. This new Great Goblin has united the goblins in there and even now defies the emissary sent from the north.

'Glóin should be told that the emissary from the north is under orders to force the goblins to heed the call of Angmar. Warn him of this and tell him to be cautious.

'Take the orders back to him and bid him read my words.''Return to Glóin swiftly. He must be warned at once.''The Great Goblin? But this cannot be! Gandalf slew the Great Goblin with Glamdring. Another goblin has risen in his place, I am sure of it. This news is most distressing.'Accompanied by Erlys and Igrain, you set out for Tornhad to save Cariad.You should talk to Erlys before approaching Tornhad.Talk to ErlysYou should follow Erlys.Follow ErlysYou should stay hidden with Erlys and Igrain to eavesdrop.Eavesdrop on Lagordal and the spy<rgb=#FF0000>Do not stray too far from Erlys and Igrain</rgb>The spy has noticed your presence.

You should confront her and Lagordal with your allies.Confront Lagordal and the spyThe confrontation has ended violently.

You should defend Erlys and Igrain.Defend Erlys and IgrainThe spy has been defeated, but Lagordal survives.Wait for Erlys to question LagordalErlys has ordered you to release Cariad.Release Cariad from his bondsYou should wait for further commands from Erlys.Wait for further commands from ErlysA Ranger has demanded to know the reason behind the Dunlendings' presence here.

You should tell her what has happened.Talk to the RangerThere is still the question of Lagordal's fate.Wait for Lagordal's sentencingYou have helped free Cariad and end Lagordal's scheming.

You should talk to Erlys.Talk to Erlys'Onwards, then.''I may not trust the Rangers, but I'd like to do this with minimal bloodshed if possible. Follow me.''I must confess, if it were not for your presence, I would have thought the Dunlendings were here for vengeance.

'These accusations against Lagordal are grave. To see one of our own so readily aid the White Wizard's spies... it is distressing, to say the least.''I'm glad that you proved me wrong, duvodiad.

'Before you set out again, come back and share a meal with us.'Hoping to gather supplies to repair their devastated town, the people of Trestlebridge have opened the gates to the Wildwood. The forest is dangerous, however, and the way is blocked.The angry tree can be found on the road in the northern Wildwood.

Talk to the angry tree.Talk to the angry tree'Hello, friend. Welcome to the Wildwood, where even the trees themselves walk and talk and refuse to let you pass. You look like the adventuring type. Maybe you can help. There be a very angry tree blocking the road into the Wildwood.

'Maybe you can talk some sense into it, though I feel as though I have taken leave of my own senses. My grammie would tell stories of walking trees in these forests. Never thought I would see one. My wife insists it be a wood troll, but... you look like you can take care of yourself.''Well now, little ${RACE}. Do you come as well to strip these lands of their bounty?'

You explain the plight of the townsfolk of Trestlebridge.

'Hmmm, hoom. I believe there may be a way we can work together for the benefit of the Wildwood and Trestlebridge both, ta-rum.'The goblin Oghûk wants you to help him escape from his cell.Goblin-jailors can be found in the dungeons of Goblin-town.

Oghûk believes that the jailors in the Goblin-town prison have the keys to his cell. He wants you to kill the jailors and find the keys. Only after you free him will he help you.'I want out of this prison, ${RACE}. I do not belong here, locked up. I will help you drive out the nasty Orcs if you help me.

'I need the key to this cell. Look for the jailors, kill them, and take their keys. If you free me, I will help you.''No keys to my cell? Curse the name of Kaushar! Curse him by the darkest things in the earth! Curse him!

'I must think. I will gnaw the rat's guts out!'A particularly vicious and dangerous goblin has been spotted in the camps outside of Goblin-town. He is said to be hunting down warriors who dare travel too close to their stronghold.Khlârum lurks near the camp of Akûltot in the eastern Misty Mountains, far to the south and east of the North Downs.

Handarod has asked you to aid the Elves of Rivendell by defeating the goblin Khlârum.Defeat KhlârumHandarod is at Esteldín in the North Downs, far to the west and north of the Misty Mountains.

You should return to Handarod and tell him of Khlârum's defeat.Talk to Handarod'The Elves of Rivendell have spotted a particularly unpleasant goblin by the name of Khlârum. This evil creature makes a particular sport of hunting down scouts and travellers in the region, and they would like to see him chased back down into the hole from whence he came.

'Khlârum was last seen wandering in the vicinity of the goblin-camp of Akûltot, outside Goblin-town, in the Misty Mountains.''My thanks for dealing with the goblin Khlârum. Our attempts to infiltrate Goblin-town can now proceed without his interference.'Glóin received information from Rivendell that there are several leaders in the depths of Goblin-town. Vighar Roadwalker, under orders of Glóin, offers rewards for hunting down these leaders within Goblin-town.The goblin-leaders Zagh, Dâgal, and Farmak dwell within Goblin-town. Goblin-town lies to the north-east of Glóin's camp.

Vighar Roadwalker has tasked you with hunting down and defeating several leaders within the depths of Goblin-town.Find and defeat Zagh, Master of the GuardFind and defeat Farmak the Best RunnerFind and defeat Dâgal the ObserverVighar Roadwalker is at Glóin's camp in the Misty Mountains, just outside of Goblin-town.

You found the goblin leaders throughout Goblin-town. Dâgal watched over the Orcs from Angmar, Zagh travelled the tunnels yelling at the guards, and Farmak was found running through the tunnels in search of intruders. Now that thay have been defeated, you should return to Vighar.Talk to Vighar Roadwalker at Glóin's camp outside of Goblin-town'Glóin tells me that you have been working with him to discover why the goblins of Goblin-town have become so bold. I thought you would like to know that he informed me of a good number of leaders within Goblin-town that need to be found and disposed of to weaken the goblins' resolve.

'I do not know where within the depths you will find these leaders, but when you do, you should deal with them expediently.

'Goblin-town is to the north-east of this camp, and you will find these goblin-leaders throughout the place.''Find the goblin-leaders in the depths of Goblin-town. When they have been dealt with, return to me. Goblin-town is to the north-east of Glóin's camp.''There are too many goblins, if you ask me -- but then, one is too many. However, if they are this organized, then there must be someone leading them... someone with greater authority.

'We must remain vigilant, ${PLAYER}.'Wargs roam the Wildwood, but none are as fierce nor as deadly as Lhudblath, the Silver Warg.Lhudblath the Silver Warg can be found in the eastern Wildwood.

Linnet has asked you to defeat the Silver Warg, Lhudblath.Defeat Lhudblath the Silver WargLinnet is located in the south-central Wildwood.

You should talk to Linnet Redholm.Talk to Linnet'The stories of Lhudblath, the Silver Warg, speak of how the creature seems to form out of mist, attacking the unwary and leaving carnage in his wake. You would do a great service to those less able than you, if you were to defeat this monster.''Wargs are already twisted creatures, but Lhudblath was worse than any of whom I have heard tell. The Wildwood is still dangerous, but you have done a great deed this day and made these forests slightly safer.'A Lossoth, Kaleva, at Sûri-kylä is collecting great scales at the Lossoth capital.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver great scales from the worms surrounding Sûri-kylä in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Great scales from the worms surrounding Sûri-kylä. Deliver the items to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Great scales from the worms surrounding Sûri-kylä.'We will make use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.A Lossoth, Kaleva, at Sûri-kylä is collecting pure black skin at the Lossoth capital.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black skin from the beasts and worms surrounding Sûri-kylä in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts and worms surrounding Sûri-kylä. Deliver the items to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Pure black skin from the beasts and worms surrounding Sûri-kylä.'We will make use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Someone has posted a task to collect lumber in the Wildwood.Trees for harvesting lumber can be found in the Wildwood.

You should collect cuts of lumber.Collect cuts of lumber from the Wildwood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A storage crate can be found in either Trader's Wharf or Trestlebridge.

You should place the cuts of lumber in a storage crate.The bulletin notice reads:

'Needed: Lumber from the Wildwood for building and repairing purposes. You will be rewarded for your efforts.'The Ranger Mithrendan is no more concerned than usual about Mandan's absence... but he remains somewhat worried all the same.Mandan often walks far and wide throughout the Angle, but Mithrendan thinks you should look for the Herb-master somewhere by a river north-east of Tornhad.Find Mandan by one of the rivers north-east of TornhadYou found Mandan on an island in the middle of a river north-east of Tornhad, and the herb-master of the Dúnedain seems to be in no more danger than that to which he is accustomed.Talk to MandanThe Ranger Mandan has asked for your help gathering a number of the items for which he left Tornhad: luminescent gel from the glow-flies of the Angle, damp mushroom caps, and sharp reeds from the nearby river-bank.Collect luminescent gel from glow-flies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect damp mushrooms, even though Mandan doesn't want them (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sharp reeds from the river-bank (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mandan is on the island in the middle of a river north-east of Tornhad, waiting for you to return with the items he requested.The Ranger Mithrendan is at Tornhad.Talk to Mithrendan in Tornhad'It is not unusual for one of the Dúnedain to spend hours walking in the wilderness, far from home; indeed, it is how we came to be called Rangers by the folk who warm themselves by the fires we protect. This is especially true of our healers, folk such as Mandan and Idhrien, who are often gone for hours or days at a time in search of rare herbs. Idhrien is always welcomed back with smiles and laughter, and Mandan... well, Mandan's healing skills are welcomed, but the man himself can be rather abrasive. Still, he is a master healer, and we are lucky to have him.

'But he has been gone for a long time. If your travels take you to one of the rivers north-east of Tornhad, might I ask you to see if Mandan is in danger or needs assistance? He often walks the river-banks seeking ingredients of which to make use.''If your travels take you near one of the rivers north-east of Tornhad, might I ask you to see if Mandan is in danger or needs assistance?''You came in search of me? I am in no danger at all.''I am in no danger, ${PLAYER}, but I am flattered that Mithrendan sent you to assist me with the collection of the herbal remedies I seek. Unless he sent you that he might be spared coming all this way himself? Aye, that was probably it.

'Well, you're here now. I have been looking to collect the substance that gives glow-flies their luminescence as well as some sharp reeds from the river-bank I can use to stir my various concoctions. Oh, I would also appreciate it if you picked some of the mushrooms that sprout in the damp alongside the river. I don't really need the mushrooms, but gather them up all the same, if you would.''I have been looking to collect the substance that gives glow-flies their luminescence as well as some sharp reeds from the river-bank I can use to stir my various concoctions. Oh, I would also appreciate it if you picked some of the mushrooms that sprout in the damp alongside the river. I don't really need the mushrooms, but gather them up all the same, if you would.''You brought me everything for which I asked? Good. Even the mushrooms?'

Mandan inspects the underside of the mushroom caps, and points out the scarlet ribbons that lance outward from the gills.

'Some of the animals that forage along the river-banks chew the caps while they forage for food, but these are quite inedible; I like to remove any of this variety that I see, to keep the beasts from getting sick. Thank you for helping me collect them, though I have no use for them. But these other items will be of use to me and to the Dúnedain.

'If you see Mithrendan before I do, tell him I was fine and had everything handled! Surely he has other things about which he can worry!''If you see Mithrendan before I do, tell him I was fine and had everything handled! Surely he has other things about which he can worry!''Mandan is safe? Thank you for checking on him, ${PLAYER}. I expect it is true for every community, but especially-so for the Dúnedain: we may argue from time to time, and we may not always get along, but we share the same purpose and care deeply for each other.

'For all his prickly exterior, Mandan has a bigger heart than many I have known. He hides it well, but there is nothing he would not sacrifice to help those who have need.

'Thank you again.'While you were hunting trolls, Oghûk remembered where the key to his cell resides.Aeglas is in his cell in the Goblin-town prison.

Oghûk told you that the goblin-torturer Matuga has the key to his cell secured on a loop on his belt. Something in Oghûk's voice is disconcerting, however. You should return to Aeglas.Talk to Aeglas at his cell in the Goblin-town prisonMatuga is on the way to the Great Goblin's throne-room at the centre of Goblin-town.

Aeglas distrusts the goblin Oghûk's intentions, but he does not doubt that the goblin believes that the key is with the torturer. Aeglas agrees that you must search the paths on the way to the Great Goblin's throne-room for Matuga and the key.'I remembered where the key to my cell is, ${RACE}...on the belt loop of Matuga, the torturer.

'Matuga was just called to the throne room by the Great Goblin. It is at the centre of Goblin-town, ${RACE}.

'If you hurry you can catch him on the way there. Find and kill Matuga, if you are to collect the key I need.''Get the key from Matuga. He is on the way to the main hall with the Great Goblin.''It surprises me not that the goblin's tone is laced with menace. They are wicked creatures and cannot be trusted.

'Yet, I think that the goblin truly fears for his life and thinks that he might yet earn his freedom or escape his own demise. It is likely that his information of the key's location is true.

'You will need to go to the heart of Goblin-town and find this Matuga. Kill him and obtain the key. Return it to me, and we will see about freeing the others and testing Oghûk's resolve.''Get the key from Matuga. He will be on the way to the Great Goblin's main hall.''It appears that the goblin was telling the truth. We are close now to escaping this place. You have my thanks for all you have done thus far, ${PLAYER}.'Someone has posted a task to hunt for meats in the Wildwood.Meat can be found on some large game in the Wildwood.

You should collect meats.Collect meats in the Wildwood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A storage crate can be found in either Trader's Wharf or Trestlebridge.

You should place the meats in a storage crate.The bulletin notice reads:

'Needed: Meats from the Wildwood for eating and trading. You will be rewarded for your efforts.'Glóin noticed one word which he recognized among the papers you found: Warg.There are Wargs in the tunnels of Goblin-town, to the north-east of Glóin's camp.

Glóin explained that, though he cannot decipher the full meaning of the documents you found, there is something sinister within. Seeing only a name and the word for Wargs, he sent you back into the depths of Goblin-town in search of the Wargs allied with the goblins.Defeat Wargs in Goblin-town (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Glóin is at his camp outside the entrance of Goblin-town.

Entering Goblin-town, you found your way into the many twisting tunnels and defeated many Wargs. You found goblin-keepers with the Wargs, which may interest Glóin.Talk to Glóin at his camp outside of Goblin-town'Wargs are not something we want to contend with, ${PLAYER}. I remember them circling below me long ago as clear as if it were happening now. Their eyes alone could cause the hardiest dwarf to lose heart. Even Gandalf the Grey seemed afraid of such a pack arrayed against us that night. I know that you have dealt with Wargs before with all that is brewing in the wild places, but Wargs with goblins is another matter. The goblins and Wargs have long been allied, and together, they are terrible.

'Now look here... this word appears to be a name. I am not certain what it means, nor am I a scholar wise enough in this foul tongue to know what it truly says. I know only that we must deal swiftly with these Wargs.

'Return to Goblin-town and hunt them down. Kill as many as you can and then return to me. I will see about getting these writings translated.''Hunt the Wargs in the tunnels of Goblin-town. In the meanwhile, I will work on getting these papers translated.''Goblin-keepers with the Wargs already? This does indeed mean that something darker and evil lies within Goblin-town. Fortunately, I received word from our friends at Rivendell about the orders you found.

'Those orders told of visitors from the North being escorted to a specific part of Goblin-town, a place where the armies of Angmar are gathering.

'It also spoke of a goblin sent there to watch over the visitors.'Sometimes an insult may demoralize the foe, and the worst insult can be mercy....Complete Mercy of the Free Peoples -- Small FellowshipComplete Mercy of the Free Peoples -- FellowshipComplete Mercy of the Free Peoples -- RaidSometimes an insult may demoralize the foe, and the worst insult can be mercy....Survive in the Barrow-downs for five minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for ten minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for fifteen minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty-five minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for thirty minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSometimes an insult may demoralize the foe, and the worst insult can be mercy....Survive in the Barrow-downs for five minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for ten minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for fifteen minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty-five minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for thirty minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSometimes an insult may demoralize the foe, and the worst insult can be mercy....Survive in the Barrow-downs for five minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for ten minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for fifteen minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty-five minutes without defeating any LieutenantsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for thirty minutes without defeating any LieutenantsThe League of the Axe is leading a mining expedition in the Wildwood.Addy Rushtuft can be found at Trader's Wharf.

