You should talk to the lead tomb-robber.Interrogate the lead tomb-robberEnid Nettlesting can be found at Trader's Wharf.

You should talk to Enid Nettlesting.Talk to Enid Nettlesting'${PLAYER}, I have an official League task for you. We're worried about a group of ruffians who have recently come to the Wildwood from nearby Evendim. They have been spotted north, along the Brandywine River. Their purpose here in the Wildwood remains a mystery.

'I'm asking of you to seek out these ruffians, learn why they are here, and drive them out of the Wildwood. They have no claim upon this forest!''Who do you think you are, wrecking my camp?!'

You assure the tomb-robber that you are the one asking questions here.

'I'm not here looking to chop down trees or hunt boars, if that's what you're getting at! Ha, heard about the Forsaken Tomb? Didn't think so! That's what me and the boys are after. Couldn't tell you more, even if I wanted to. Don't even know where the Forsaken Tomb even is! Bah, I told the boss this venture would be worthless!

'Now get out of here and leave me be!'

The tomb-robber isn't going to be any more helpful. If he is telling the truth, there are more tomb-robbers in the Wildwood.The lead tomb-robber meets your eyes with a scowl.

'The Forsaken Tomb?! I've never heard of it! Why would I be looking for anything called Forsaken anyway?! After getting kicked out of Brychan the Bold's gang, I needed some easy pickings. The old ruins here don't have much, but they haven't been as plundered as those in Evendim, that's for sure!

'It turns out, things aren't so easy in the Wildwood after all. Thanks for nothing, ${RACE}!'

The leader doesn't seem to know what the other tomb-robber was talking about. Perhaps more than one group of tomb-robbers have descended into the Wildwood.'What have you to report? There might be multiple groups of tomb-robbers in the Wildwood? That is not great news, though I'm glad you were able to thwart one of the groups!

'Now what is this talk about a Forsaken Tomb, I wonder? I have spent a lot of time making sure the Wildwood was an ideal place for the League of the Axe to take root. In all my time, I never heard anything about a tomb here. I am sure I would have as I have stooped to dealing with a fair amount of ruffians in my past, and I'm not proud of it. Now I look ahead to better times, with honesty and prosperity for all in the League.'A young hobbit finds herself in (even farther than usual) over her head when her waggon is attacked and her goods are stolen by brigands in the Wildwood.The brigands and their leader took off up the road east of Trader's Wharf in the Wildwood.

You should follow the trail of stolen supplies and deal with any brigands along the way.Recover stolen supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Wildwood assailants (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat the waggon snatcherMinda is on a hill near her broken waggon, in the eastern Wildwood.

You should talk to Minda Heathertoes.Talk to Minda Heathertoes'Hello, friend! My name is Minda Heathertoes, and I am having a grand adventure! Well, to be truthful, it has been rather more terrifying than grand. Blasted waggon snatchers made off with my pony and my waggon full of supplies! Thankfully, these good folk found me wandering on the road and brought me here. Grateful though I am, the League of the Axe hasn't any time to go chasing after a silly hobbit’s supplies. If… if you could help me with this task, I could make it worth your while!

'The brigands went up into the Wildwood with Stomper and my supply waggon. You shoulda seen how Stomper fought them! He wasn’t going without a fight, and my supplies went flying everywhere! I bet if you follow the trail of bags that were tossed, it would lead you straight to Stomper! If you happen upon any of those brigands or their leader on the way, I encourage you to deal with them swiftly!''Poor Stomper! Please, find him and my waggon!''You did it! You found my supplies and defeated the waggon snatcher! Oh dear, but where is poor Stomper?'To keep Saruman's spies in the dark about the Cuan-lûth's whereabouts, a journey to Thelgarth is in order.Thelgarth lies to the south of the Cuan-lûth Refuge.

You should find the spies' base in Thelgarth.Find the spies' base in ThelgarthCariad would like to speak with you.Talk to CariadYou should destroy the spy reports.Destroy the spy reportsYou should discuss the next course of action with Cariad.Talk to Cariad'Thelgarth harbours those loyal to Saruman. It's been too risky for us to enter, but I have often kept an eye on it from afar. You will find it if you venture south.

'I'll meet you there. Let's find the spies' base and destroy those reports, quickly!''I can't see a path to survival without the Rangers' help. Make no mistake -- my people are strong, but we are powerless. The Rangers wield power, enough to put up a fight against the power of Saruman.

'When we hunted down Saruman's crebain, I took a risk to see if I had misplaced my faith in you. It's time to make another gamble.''I've been studying these reports. They are... very detailed. It seems that spy had been tracking us for a while.

'My clan clearly made it easy enough for her to do her work. How can we know for sure that no one else has spotted us? We can't deal with every spy we come across!

'Please, destroy the reports first. We'll talk afterwards.'The Enemy in the Barrow-downs is relentless, throwing themselves at their foes without regard to their lives...for they have no life.Complete Unvanquished -- Small FellowshipComplete Unvanquished -- FellowshipComplete Unvanquished -- RaidThe Enemy in the Barrow-downs is relentless, throwing themselves at their foes without regard to their lives...for they have no life.Survive in the Barrow-downs for five minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for ten minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for fifteen minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty-five minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for thirty minutes without being defeatsThe Enemy in the Barrow-downs is relentless, throwing themselves at their foes without regard to their lives...for they have no life.Survive in the Barrow-downs for five minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for ten minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for fifteen minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty-five minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for thirty minutes without being defeatedThe Enemy in the Barrow-downs is relentless, throwing themselves at their foes without regard to their lives...for they have no life.Survive in the Barrow-downs for five minutes without suffering any defeatsSurvive in the Barrow-downs for ten minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for fifteen minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for twenty-five minutes without being defeatedSurvive in the Barrow-downs for thirty minutes without being defeatedThe Lossoth are able to reopen their trade route to the north now that the road is safe and snow-sleds have been built.Pynti-peldot lies far to the north of Kauppa-kohta, past a great ice chasm.

You were told to speak with the chieftain at Pynti-peldot.Talk to Ora'Incredible. The northern road has not been this safe in some time. You are free to travel further into these lands, with one condition. Please speak with the chieftain in Pynti-peldot. You will be venturing out of our territory and into their hunting-grounds. We would not want you to appear unannounced.''Incredible. The northern road has not been this safe in some time. You are free to travel further into these lands, with one condition. Please speak with the chieftain in Pynti-peldot. You will be venturing out of our territory and into their hunting-grounds. We would not want you to appear unannounced.''Incredible. The northern road has not been this safe in some time. You are free to travel further into these lands, with one condition. Please speak with the chieftain in Pynti-peldot. You will be venturing out of our territory and into their hunting-grounds. We would not want you to appear unannounced.''Identify yourself at once, Sivullinen!

'${PLAYER}, I am Ora, chieftain of the Leijona-heimo Lossoth of Pynti-peldot. 

'Snow-glare and ice-dust are our constant companions; we fear no living being and depend upon no-one but ourselves for our subsistence. Can your people say the same? Of course not. None are as strong nor as brave as the cold-dwelling Lumi-väki.'The Lossoth are able to reopen their trade route to the north now that the road is safe and snow-sleds have been built.Pynti-peldot lies far to the north of Kauppa-kohta, past a great ice chasm.

You were told to speak with the chieftain at Pynti-peldot.Talk to Ora'Incredible. The northern road has not been this safe in some time. You are free to travel further into these lands, with one condition. Please speak with the chieftain in Pynti-peldot. You will be venturing out of our territory and into their hunting-grounds. We would not want you to appear unannounced.''Incredible. The northern road has not been this safe in some time. You are free to travel further into these lands, with one condition. Please speak with the chieftain in Pynti-peldot. You will be venturing out of our territory and into their hunting-grounds. We would not want you to appear unannounced.''Incredible. The northern road has not been this safe in some time. You are free to travel further into these lands, with one condition. Please speak with the chieftain in Pynti-peldot. You will be venturing out of our territory and into their hunting-grounds. We would not want you to appear unannounced.''Identify yourself at once, Sivullinen!

'${PLAYER}, I am Ora, chieftain of the Leijona-heimo Lossoth of Pynti-peldot. 

'Snow-glare and ice-dust are our constant companions; we fear no living being and depend upon no-one but ourselves for our subsistence. Can your people say the same? Of course not. None are as strong nor as brave as the cold-dwelling Lumi-väki.'Vighar Roadwalker, a dwarf at the High Crag camp, wants to post signs warning travellers away from dangerous locations in the mountains.The pass of Rakhâs-bizar is east of the pass of Gabilazan, where you posted Vighar's sign warning of the Dourhands.

Vighar Roadwalker has given you a sign to post in the pass of Rakhâs-bizar, warning of giants to the south. He advised you not to attempt this task on your own.Vighar Roadwalker is at the High Crag camp, north-east of Rivendell.

Vighar will be pleased to hear that you have posted the sign he gave you in the pass of Rakhâs-bizar, north of the land of the giants.'You have done admirably with the task of posting those signs for me, ${PLAYER}, and I am sure you will exceed my expectations with this latest one!

'This sign warns travellers to beware of giants. I realize this might seem a little obvious, but the trouble with giants is that they can usually see you from a good deal further away than you can see them. One of the Elves in Rivendell told me a story about a giant that could fell an aurochs with a single stone from a distance of two miles! I'm not worried for myself, of course, but if any traders begin passing through the mountains, we'll want them to have at least some warning before entering giant country. Two miles, at least!

'Two passes run south through the mountains. You've already seen the pass of Gabilazan, but the pass of Rakhâs-bizar is further to the east. Travel south through Rakhâs-bizar and post this sign. You should find a suitable post before leaving the pass, and beware! I won't be responsible if you run afoul of the giants south of the pass!

'In fact, these giants are so dangerous, I would recommend that you go with friends to attempt this task.''The giants can be found in great numbers south of the pass of Rakhâs-bizar, east of the Dourhand-guarded pass of Gabilazan.

'Post my sign and hurry back!''You have returned earlier than I expected, ${PLAYER}, and I have not had time to prepare any more signs!

'Perhaps I will come up with something else for you to do, friend ${RACE}, but it will take some time. In the meantime, you have my hearty thanks!'Defeat many wolves, boars, bears, deer, and moose in the Wildwood.Defeat many wolves, boars, bears, deer, and moose in the Wildwood.Defeat many of the big game animals in the WildwoodDefeat many wolves, boars, bears, deer, and moose in the Wildwood.Defeat many wolves, boars, bears, deer, and moose in the Wildwood.Defeat many of the big game animals in the WildwoodDefeat many brigands in the Wildwood.Defeat many brigands in the Wildwood.Defeat many brigands in the WildwoodDefeat many brigands in the Wildwood.Defeat many brigands in the Wildwood.Defeat many brigands in the WildwoodMinda Heathertoes needs help procuring a special ingredient for her master's feast.Wildwood croakers can be found in watery locales in central Wildwood.

Collect frog legs from Wildwood croakers.Collect Wildwood croakers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Minda can be found near a pond in the central Wildwood.

You should talk to Minda Heathertoes.Talk to Minda Heathertoes'One of my master’s feast guests loves frog legs. Buttered, grilled... you name it, he loves it! My League of the Axe friends told me where there is an abundance of the critters. So many, in fact, that they are crowding out the smaller frogs in the area. We shall be doing those little ones a favour, if you ask me!

'Unfortunately, the frogs I need like the lake and moat next to the enemy settlement! There is no way I could get close enough to gather the frogs, but you definitely could! Please, ${PLAYER}. Save the feast and bring me frog legs!''Well, will you look at that! These are the plumpest frog legs I have ever laid eyes on! My master will be thrilled, as will her honoured guest!'Defeat many Orcs, half-orcs, and goblins in the Wildwood.Defeat many Orcs, half-orcs, and goblins in the Wildwood.Defeat many Orcs, half-orcs, and goblins in the WildwoodDefeat many Orcs, half-orcs, and goblins in the Wildwood.Defeat many Orcs, half-orcs, and goblins in the Wildwood.Defeat many Orcs, half-orcs, and goblins in the WildwoodSpider cocoons found at Writhenset should be destroyed in order to weaken the brood of spiders lurking beneath the Wildwood.Spider cocoons can be found at Writhenset in the Wildwood.

You should destroy spider cocoons at Writhenset.Destroy spider cocoons at Writhenset (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Spiders lurk within the mines of Writhenset, beneath the Wildwood. Destroying their vile cocoons should weaken the brood and assist with making the mines safe for the Free Peoples.Supplies found at Writhenset should be destroyed in order to lessen the strength of your foes within the Wildwood.Supply crates can be found at Writhenset in the Wildwood.

You should destroy supply crates at Writhenset.Destroy supply crates at Writhenset (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Foes of the Free Peoples are keeping supplies at the mine of Writhenset beneath the Wildwood. Destroying these supplies should lessen the strength of foes here.Foes of the Free Peoples found at Writhenset should be defeated in order protect those within the Wildwood.Foes can be found at Writhenset in the Wildwood.

You should defeat foes at Writhenset.Defeat foes at WrithensetFoes can be found at Writhenset in the Wildwood.

You should defeat foes at Writhenset.Defeat foes at Writhenset (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Foes of the Free Peoples have claimed the mines of Writhenset beneath the Wildwood. Defeating their numbers here will assist in returning safety to the Wildwood.You have played an important part in the courtship of Kaj and Pinja.Snowdrops and white furs can be collected at Leijona-kotin, south of Pinja's homestead, which is south-west of Pynti-peldot in Itä-ma. Ella is at Pynti-peldot.

Pinja has asked that you gather the winter-blooming snowdrops and white furs for her marriage dress, and that you hand-deliver an important invitation to Ella in Pynti-peldot.Collect white furs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pick snowdrops (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pinja is at her homestead, south-west of Pynti-peldot.

You should return to Pinja with the snowdrops and furs.'The wedding will be soon, but I need two things for my marriage dress: white furs for the trim and the cape, and snowdrops for the bouquet. Also, please deliver this invitation personally to Ella in Pynti-peldot; she raised me after my mother died, and since she moved back into the village, we have not seen as much of each other as I would like.

'The highest concentration of kalpa-kita is at Leijona-kotin, a wooded area just south of here.  Their fur is the softest and the whitest, and I want the best for my marriage dress!

'Snowdrops can also be found in and around Leijona-kotin, as there they can grow in the shelter of the evergreens.''Bring me the things I asked...I have a wedding to prepare for!''Oh, my little Pinja is getting married? How lovely! Tell her that of course I will be there.

'I had my doubts that any suitor would ever even get near her, with those brothers of hers. Overprotective to a fault.

'I am somehow not surprised that it took the bravest warrior of the tribe to brave them. Ora must be so pleased!''You will find Pinja at Lumi-talletta, her home. Follow the road south-west.''Blessings of the ice-spirits upon you, Sivullinen. It is in part thanks to your efforts that Pynti-peldot will host a happy union!'Bingo Boffin fell into a deep cave during his escape from Gabilazan, and now Spalvi Dourhand wants to help you rescue the hapless hobbit.Dourhand surveyors sent by Skorgrím Dourhand never returned from their mission to map the tunnels of Goblin-town. If you can find their maps, it could be you will learn the layout of the caverns beneath the Misty Mountains into which Bingo has fallen.Dourhand surveyors sent by Skorgrím Dourhand never returned from their mission to map the tunnels of Goblin-town. If you can find their maps, it could be you will learn the layout of the caverns beneath the Misty Mountains into which Bingo has fallen.Spalvi Dourhand told you he would meet you on an overlook in the Southern High Pass area of the Misty Mountains once you recovered some useful maps from the surveyors lost in Goblin-town.Willem Whisker has gone in search of Bingo in the Erforgh Cleft in the south-east corner of the Southern High Pass, and Spalvi wants you to make sure he is all right.Find Willem Whisker somewhere within the gauredain camp at Erforgh CleftSpalvi Dourhand is on an overlook in the Southern High Pass area of the Misty Mountains, north-west of Erforgh Cleft.Talk to Spalvi in the Southern High PassSpalvi wanted to meet up with you just outside Glóin's Camp in the Western Bruinen Source area of the Misty Mountains.Talk to Spalvi just outside Glóin's camp in the Western Bruinen Source area'I feel terrible that Bingo has gotten lost on my account, ${PLAYER}. Our flight from Gabilazan was a hasty one, and I count myself fortunate that I did not stumble over the same unsteady ground as Bingo. He was not so lucky, and fell a long way. There must be a deep cavern down there, and possibly an underground river or stream. I thought I heard a splashing shortly after Bingo fell, but I cannot be certain.

'Such caverns beneath the mountains can be quite extensive. Skorgrím Dourhand ordered that a survey of such tunnels be taken, and he dispatched a number of dwarves to map the caverns of the Misty Mountains. They never returned from Goblin-town, as far as I know, but if you were to find them their maps might tell us if there are any tunnels that might take us to Bingo's location.

'I will meet you at the Southern High Pass, far to the east, after you have found any helpful maps of the underground cave system.''Skorgrím Dourhand dispatched a number of dwarves to map the caverns of the Misty Mountains. They never returned from Goblin-town, as far as I know, but if you were to find them their maps might tell us if there are any tunnels that could take us to Bingo's location.

'I will meet you at the South High Pass, far to the east, after you have found any helpful maps of the underground cave system.''Skorgrím Dourhand dispatched a number of dwarves to map the caverns of the Misty Mountains. They never returned from Goblin-town, as far as I know, but if you were to find them their maps might tell us if there are any tunnels that could take us to Bingo's location.

'I will meet you at the Southern High Pass, far to the east, after you have found any helpful maps of the underground cave system.'You give Spalvi the map and he studies it briefly before folding it again.

'I will look at this in a moment, ${PLAYER}, but first I think you should know this. That lynx pet of Bingo's... what was its name again? Willem something? He followed me here at a distance, and I did not like the look in his eye. I think he blames me for his master's disappearance, and while he does have a point on that score, I am worried he does not fully understand that I have had a change of heart and want to make that right. Well, he followed me for a while and then bolted away to the south-east. Do you think he might have caught Bingo's scent? In any case, gauredain live in the Erforgh Cleft down that way, and I wouldn't want anything to happen to him.

'Not before you assure him that I am a friend, anyway!''That lynx pet of Bingo's... what was its name again? Willem something? He followed me here at a distance, and I did not like the look in his eye. I think he blames me for his master's disappearance, and while he does have a point on that score, I am worried he does not fully understand that I have had a change of heart and want to make that right.

'Well, he followed me for awhile and then bolted away to the south-east. Do you think he might have caught Bingo's scent? In any case, gauredain live in the Erforgh Cleft down that way, and I wouldn't want anything to happen to him.'Willem Whisker whines at you plaintively, and you can see clearly his frustration at Bingo's absence, and that his search of Erforgh Cleft proved fruitless. You assure him that you will not abandon the hobbit. He snuffles in response, unconvinced.

You speak quiet words to the lynx, telling him that it will take your efforts, Spalvi's efforts, and most of all Willem's efforts, to find the cavern into which Bingo fell and to bring him back into the light.

The lynx snorts, but then grudgingly flicks his ears at you in assent. Now you should return to Spalvi and see if he has learned anything of use from the map you recovered.Willem Whisker snorts at your optimism, but grudgingly flicks his ears at you in assent. Now you should return to Spalvi and see if he has learned anything of use from the map you recovered.'You found Bingo's pet lynx, and he has returned in peace. But why does he bare his sharp teeth at me?' Spalvi asks, suddenly frightened.

You tell him that Willem Whisker is not Bingo's pet, but rather his friend, free to leave at any point.

'I suppose that must be so, ${PLAYER}, for otherwise he would be long gone by now. But look at this map you brought me! I am afraid I have both good news and bad.

'The good news is that according to this map, an underground river runs beneath the mountains and seems to travel a long distance south. It could be that Bingo was carried by the current in safety. The bad news, well...

'I think we should travel to the Longbeard camp in the Western Bruinen Source area of the Misty Mountains for the bad news. I will stay outside the camp proper... do not tell them I am a Dourhand, for I fear my reception would be quite poor.''Look at this map you brought me! I am afraid I have both good news and bad.

'The good news is that according to this map, an underground river runs beneath the mountains and seems to travel a long distance south. It could be that Bingo was carried by the current in safety. The bad news, well...

'I think we should travel to the Longbeard camp in the Western Bruinen Source area of the Misty Mountains for the bad news. I will stay outside the camp proper... do not tell them I am a Dourhand, for I fear my reception would be quite poor.''You must be wondering why I wanted to come to this Longbeard camp, ${PLAYER}. Well, I told you the good news: that Bingo may have survived his fall and been swept up in the current of an underground river. Here is the bad news. If the river runs south for as far as this map would indicate, he may have been carried into the depths of the ancient kingdom of Khazad-dûm. If that is so, we may need to seek the advice of the Longbeards, for I have heard whispers that they may be seeking to gain entrance to that place.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A New Hunt' (Recommended Level: 48)</rgb>The dwarf-ruins of Sarnúr have been discovered and occupied by the Dourhands, who plan to use them as a base from which to strike at the Longbeards -- worse yet, they have allied themselves with evil and corrupted creatures who owe their true allegiance to the powers of Angmar. You must take your battle against the Dourhands onto the crumbling streets of Sarnúr before they build their strength and are bolstered further by forces sent from Angmar.Defeat Dourhands in SarnúrThe dwarf-ruins of Sarnúr have been discovered and occupied by the Dourhands, who plan to use them as a base from which to strike at the Longbeards -- worse yet, they have allied themselves with evil and corrupted creatures who owe their true allegiance to the powers of Angmar. You must take your battle against the Dourhands onto the crumbling streets of Sarnúr before they build their strength and are bolstered further by forces sent from Angmar.Defeat Dourhands in SarnúrCorunir reacted strangely to the presence and touch of the stone-heart, recoiling from it in terror. He told you that the heart had been prepared to receive a fell spirit from an elder Age, similar to those that inhabit the wights which serve Angmar.The ancient watching-stone is near the eastern pass of Ram Dúath, near Bail Róva, east of Aughaire.

Corunir believes that the stone-heart may be able to draw forth the fell spirit from another watching-stone and has asked you to test his theory upon an ancient watching-stone which guards the passes of Ram Dúath.Present the stone-heart to the ancient watching-stoneCorunir is at the Hillman-village of Aughaire, west of Bail Róva.

With the spirit of the ancient watching-stone defeated, you should return to Corunir with the news of hope.'Nay, take this evil thing... I can bear it no longer! My apologies, friend, but the sense of this thing is too much likened to the terror of Rammas Deluon. I cannot bear its presence.

'But I know now the secret of the stones of Rammas Deluon. It is no spell that grants the statues such power, but a fell spirit of an elder Age, such as those that inhabit the wights which serve Angmar. This stone-heart was prepared to receive such a spirit.

'Perhaps this heart may be made to draw forth such a spirit from another stone. It may be a clue to breaking the will of the watching-stones! Take this stone-heart to the ancient watching-stone which stands near the eastern pass of Ram Dúath to the east of here, not far from Bail Róva, a fortress of the Angmarim.''Take the stone-heart to the ancient watching-stone at Ram Dúath. It may give you the advantage you need to defeat the spirit of the stone.''You have vanquished the spirit? How your victory heartens me, ${PLAYER}. It gives me hope that we may yet succeed!'Your defeat of the ancient watching-stone of Ram Dúath has proven that the stones can be defied.Rammas Deluon lies far to the east of Aughaire in Malenhad. Corunir suggested you look for a dwarf encampment in the south past the Rammas Deluon.

Corunir bade you pass through Rammas Deluon, for you no longer have cause to fear the deadly stones. He hopes that you may find a remnant of the force Golodir led through the stones somewhere on the other side.Search for survivors from Golodir's band'You have proven that such stones can be defeated, that their power of oppression is only as great as you allow it! Unfortunately, my spirit has been broken for far too long... I do not believe I could pass the stones and bear their fear.

'You, however, have nothing left to fear. Go east, ${PLAYER}, and pass through Rammas Deluon. I can only hope that somewhere beyond my kinsmen survive. The people here say that there are dwarves in the south past Rammas Deluon. Search there for my brethren!

'Go, and may the Valar go with you!''Go now! Cross through Rammas Deluon, for you no longer have cause to fear! Search for the dwarves that are rumoured to encamp there.''Who are you? If you are not a servant of the Shadow of Angmar, how then did you pass through Rammas Deluon and not perish?

'Corunir sent you? That is joyous news! Your coming is as a trump of victory to us!'After discovering the secret of the watching-stones of Rammas Deluon, Corunir sent you into the eastern reaches of Angmar, seeking the Rangers and Hillmen of Aughaire who had marched forth to throw down the rising power of Angmar. You found the Ranger Braigiar at the dwarf-outpost of Gabilshathûr in Malenhad.Gath Forthnír lies somewhere far to the north of Gabilshathûr.

Braigiar has sent you to find and lend aid to the survivors of Golodir's company at the encampment of Gath Forthnír.Find the Rangers of Gath ForthnírLorniel is inside the building at Gath Forthnír.

Areneth told you to speak with Lorniel, daughter of Golodir.'It is a pity that Corunir could not steel his heart against the Watchers of Rammas Deluon. The power of the watching-stones is terrible. Many of the Hillmen tried to flee back through Rammas Deluon and perished.

'We marched on, but ere we ever came unto Carn Dûm, we were beset by Orcs and creatures more terrible. Golodir was lost and our numbers were scattered. The scarce remnant that remains has barely managed to avoid the snares of the Enemy for these last few years, but I fear Angmar has grown too strong, and we will not be able to hide forever.

'I have learned that one party of my kindred is encamped at a place called Gath Forthnír, far to the north in the eastern reaches of Himbar. Go to them, if you would, and lend them what aid you may.''Your aid is needed among my brethren at Gath Forthnír. Do not delay!''You have come from Corunir by way of Braigiar? Welcome, then, and well met! Indeed, we could use your aid. These last few years have seen us nearly vanquished.

'But it is not for me to tell you of our woes. You should enter within and speak with Lorniel, Golodir's daughter.''You say Lorniel lives? You should return to Gath Forthnír at once and speak with her!''Lorniel is inside. She will wish to speak with you.''I do not believe good fortune alone brings you hither, my good ${RACE}. Come, we have much to speak about.'Lorniel told you that she believes her father still lives, held captive in the dungeons of Carn Dûm. She hopes to find a way inside the terrible city and rescue him.Meet with Raghnall south of Bail Cátharnakh, west of Gath Forthnír.

Lorniel told you that she had heard from a Hillman named Raghnall who was seeking to leave the service of Angmar and might have information that will allow her to gain access to Carn Dûm.Lorniel can be found within Gath Forthnír, east of Bail Cátharnakh.

You met with Raghnall and he gave you a scroll that explains how to forge a key for the Gates of Carn Dûm.'If you came from Braigiar, I have no doubt you heard that my father was lost. He was indeed lost to us, but I do not believe he was slain. I have heard reports from our allies that he was taken captive and is being held within the dungeons of Carn Dûm.

'One such ally, a sympathetic Hillman from the hostile eastern tribes named Raghnall, claims that he has acquired a scroll from the libraries of Carn Dûm that tells how to gain access to the city. If you would meet Raghnall and obtain the scroll from him, it would be a tremendous step towards freeing my father from the clutches of the Enemy.

'Be wary, though...he will be waiting just south of Bail Cátharnakh, a Hillman village which lies to the south-west of Gath Forthnír. Most of the Hillmen there are in the service of the Iron Crown.''You were sent here by the woman from the South? Here...take this scroll. My people are suspicious. I must go at once!''Leave me at once! My people will kill me if they see us together. Take the scroll to the woman, to your Lorniel...she will know what to do with it.''Hurry, ${PLAYER}...there is no telling how long Raghnall can stay hidden. You must go speak with him immediately! He should be hiding somewhere south of Bail Cátharnakh.''Did you find Raghnall? Ah! That must be the scroll he told of! Let me see it.'The sea-watchers have scouted a large number of fish in the ice bay, but cannot leave their post to go fishing.Tapio told you to fish where the sea bubbles and boils.Bring the icebay salmon back to Tapio.'With the Gauredain gone, we can reclaim this outpost for the Sea-watchers. We know many things about the sea: the way its currents twist and wind, the signs of its anger, and the places where it hides its fish.

'We have turned our eyes to the oceans and found a large kill of fish forced against the shore by the current. Gathering those fish would keep Barad Gaurhoth satisfied and assure us favour in the eyes of the sooth-sayers.

'The fish can be found near the shore where the sea boils and bubbles. Fish there and we will reward you with the blessings of the healers.''Ah, the winds sing lauds of our efforts. Surely the favour of those who see the truth is upon us, but take this small gift from us as well, and go in peace.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Written some two hundred years ago, "Bullroarer's Boy" was a fanciful journal kept by Haldemar Took, one of the younger sons of Bandobras Took. After the Battle of Greenfields, Haldemar became a Warden of the Shire, and told many Tall (or rather short) Tales of adventures beyond the Bounds, many of them humorous accounts of battle against goblins and trolls and other beasts, some wholly imagined. Though much of the book is whimsy, there is truth at the core that any Warden would value. Sadly, the three known copies of the book were all stolen, suspiciously, within a week of one another nearly a century ago.Talk to Aragorn in RivendellBullroarer's Boy, page 4Bullroarer's Boy, page 7Bullroarer's Boy, page 10Bullroarer's Boy, page 12Bullroarer's Boy, page 17Bullroarer's Boy, page 21Bullroarer's Boy, page 25Bullroarer's Boy, page 33'Haldemar Took...now that is a name I never expected to see on a written page outside of the family trees at the Great Smials. Bilbo Baggins will delight in seeing this book. I shall have to show it to him before my errand steers me from Rivendell.

'There was a time when every hobbit-child in the Shire was raised on Tall Tales of Haldemar, such as the story in which he slew the fabled Great Giant of the Midgewater. But he was also a skilled Warden, and though his tales were about as true as his stature was high, they are indeed great for their meaning. It is good to see them gathered under one cover again.

'It is a shame, however, that the book is in such poor condition. The pages are swollen, the ink smeared ...it appears to have been left out in the rain, or thrown in a river. Much of it is illegible. But there were other copies, once ...if another could be found, perhaps Bilbo could make a fresh copy from both together. If you should find any more to this book, be sure to bring it here.'Pynti-peldot is the primary source of cat-tusk scrimshaw in Forochel. The Lossoth carve the large tusks during the long winter nights, and sell them to south-landers.Kalpa-kita can be found in and around their den at Leijona-kotin, as well as roaming the rest of Itä-ma.

Kimmo has asked you to bring him some tusks from the kalpa-kita which roam Forochel.Collect kalpa-kita tusks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Kimmo is at Pynti-peldot. You should return to him with the kalpa-kita tusks.'You have helped me once, Sivullinen; perhaps you can help me with something else?

'We here in Pynti-peldot are the main source of tusks from the great kalpa-kita that roam the area, which are made into intricate carvings for which the area is well-known, then sent south to Kauppa-kohta to be traded or sold.

'We are running low on our supply of tusks, and I would have you bring me more. Kalpa-kita are found especially in a little wooded dell at Leijona-kotin, west along the road and then south, beyond the homestead of Lumi-talletta.''Have you enough tusks to provide some winter occupation for myself and my friends? No? Why not?

'Kalpa-kita roam all over Itä-ma, but are found especially in a wooded dell called Leijona-kotin, west along the road and then south, beyond the homestead of Lumi-talletta.''Ah! I will be able to make wonderful carvings with these... thank you, Sivullinen!'The descendants of Skorgrím Dourhand have ever been a thorn in the side of Durin's Folk, the Longbeards. Though now outcast, they remain a threat to the Free Peoples of Ered Luin.The entrance to Sarnúr is south of Thorin's Hall and west of Noglond in the ruins of Orodost.

Tórth has asked you to enter the Dourhand stronghold of Sarnúr and defeat the creatures who dwell there, claiming Dourhand crests as the proof of your valour.'At your service, ${CLASS}. The Dourhands have been removed from Thorin's Hall, yet our anger at their deeds has not been satisfied. Many of the Longbeards suffered greatly under Gormr, and his treachery must be met with revenge!

'It is rumoured that the Dourhands have found remains of their ancient home that was lost when the world was broken in the ruins of Orodost -- the Elves now call that place Sarnúr. Travel south of Thorin's Hall to this city and slay the Dourhands that you find there.

'The lesser dwarves and servants should carry the crest of the Dourhand family; bring me these crests that we may repay blood with blood!''You have returned with the Dourhand crests? That is well. The blood-price set upon the deeds of the Dourhands is high, and these crests are only a pittance of their full payment to the Dwarves of Thorin's Hall.'The Dwarves of the Blue Mountains are known best for their jewel and iron-craft.The Blue Stone Garrison is located just inside Thorin's Hall.

Ísrath has requested that you bring him crafted dwarf-jewels.'At your service, ${CLASS}. We dwarves have toiled long under these Mountains and have crafted many wonderful and beautiful things as you no doubt can see. But the skill of the Dwarves of Thorin's Hall lies not in our mastery of stone and rock, but in the cutting of gemstones and the smithy of strong iron! Though we pride ourselves most on our jewel-crafting over any of our works of baser materials.

'If you bring to me finely-crafted dwarf-jewels, you might prove yourself someone of worth to the Dwarves of Thorin's Hall.'You have returned with the dwarf-jewels. Splendid!'The Dwarves of the Blue Mountains are best known for their jewel and iron-craft.The Blue Stone Garrison is located just inside Thorin's Hall.

Ísrath has requested that you bring him a crafted dwarf-necklace.'At your service and your family's, ${PLAYER}. The finest jewellery in all of Eriador has been crafted by dwarf-hands in Thorin's Hall. But these Mountains do not give up their jewels willingly. They are filled with iron and coal, and it is seldom that precious stones or metals of great worth are found here.  

'A few veins of a very precious dwarf-stone run throughout Eriador. It is not often that even a small gem of craftable quality is found, but when one is uncovered it is a sight to behold. When crafted into a necklace and laid upon the breast, you would be unable to find an unflattering angle. The gem sparkles like Kheled-zâram in the starlight and lays an enchantment of beauty and awe upon all who behold it!

'If you were to bring to me one of these necklaces crafted with a dwarf-stone, you would hold high honour amongst the dwarves of Thorin's Hall.''Amazing! Simply amazing! You have indeed earned high honour, ${PLAYER}.'The Dwarves of the Blue Mountains are best known for their jewel and iron-craft.The Blue Stone Garrison is located just inside Thorin's Hall.

Ísrath has requested that you bring him crafted dwarf-rings.'At your service, ${PLAYER}. A fine adventurer such as yourself would appreciate the joy that I describe in the crafting of dwarf-rings. Such a thing is a wonder to behold! Aye, when you place a well-crafted dwarf-ring upon your finger, watching its gentle curves glimmer in the torch-light, it stirs even the coldest of hearts.

'I would ask you to prove your worth and bring me five dwarf-rings. Such a token would prove your alliance to the Dwarves of Thorin's Hall.'It took a skilled hand to craft these dwarf-rings. I commend you on your success, ${PLAYER}!'You have earned the respect of the Lossoth-chieftain Ora, who will now further guide your exploration of Forochel.Zigilgund is far along the road to the west of Pynti-peldot.

Ora suggested that you speak with a dwarf there by the name of Thórth.Talk to Thórth'I have been proven wrong about you, Sivullinen. Your deeds will be remembered here, and I will lend you aid. If you continue west along the main road you will reach an encampment of the Kivi-väki, which they call Zigilgund. Nearby is the fabled graveyard of the norsu, which is a sight well worth the trip.

'You will wish to speak to Thórth, the leader of these Kivi-väki; he seems to be of strong character.

'Best of luck, ${PLAYER}, and may your travels be safe.''Welcome to Zigilgund. I am Thórth.

'So you have been through some of the Lossoth lands, I gather, if Ora sent you. She does not trust outsiders easily -- when our expedition first passed through there some time ago, it took some doing to get her to so much as let us pitch tent within Pynti-peldot during a blizzard!''Follow the main road to the west if you wish to find Zigilgund.''Zigilgund? It is to the west. The easiest way is simply to follow the main road.'Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of albino fur at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver albino fur from the beasts to the south of the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Albino fur from the beasts to the south of the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Albino fur from the beasts to the south of the outpost of Gwingris.'These furs will be used wisely. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann has requested that a collection of albino fur be delivered to her.You have accepted a task to deliver albino fur from the beasts found near Echad Dúnann to the Elf-maid Tinúriel. Tinúriel can be found in Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Albino fur taken from the beasts in the areas around Echad Dúnann. Deliver the items to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann.'We will make good use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of broken wooden clubs at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver broken wooden clubs from the Men and Wood-trolls near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the Men and Wood-trolls surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the Men and Wood-trolls surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'This is a good thing you have done. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann has requested that a collection of broken wooden clubs be delivered to her.You have accepted a task to deliver broken wooden clubs from the Men and half-orcs found near Echad Dúnann to the Elf-maid Tinúriel. Tinúriel can be found in Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the Men and half-orcs in the areas around Echad Dúnann. Deliver the items to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann.'It is good to know that there are fewer defilers upon our lands. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of monstrous filth at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver monstrous filth from the insects near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Monstrous filth from the insects surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Monstrous filth from the insects surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'I will send this on to be studied by our scholars. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of powerful branches at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver powerful branches from the twisted creatures of nature near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Powerful branches from the twisted creatures of nature surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Powerful branches from the twisted creatures of nature surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'I will send this on to be studied by our scholars. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The kalpa-kita have slowly been encroaching on Lossoth territory and are now beginning to launch small raids on Lossoth-outposts virtually every night.Kalpa-kita can be found throughout the tundra of Itä-mâ.

Âtos has asked you to help purge Itä-mâ of the kalpa-kita scourge.Defeat kalpa-kita in Itä-mâ (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Âtos is at Pynti-leiri.

Now that you have defeated many kalpa-kita, you should return to Âtos.Talk to Âtos'Unbelievable! How were they able to press down on our camp yet again? For many months now, the kalpa-kita have been roaming into Itä-mâ, preying upon small creatures taking shelter near our outpost. As their prey runs low, they move closer to us. We watch for them constantly, but their hides are white like the snow, and they can be still when they lie in wait.

'Last night, they attacked us, and now it is time for them to know the cost of striking at the Lumi-väki.

'Make them flee like the wild creatures they are. Find the kalpa-kita throughout Itä-mâ and make them rue the day they came here.''It seems that for the moment our guards will be able to deal with the remaining kalpa-kita. Tonight, we will feast!

'There is no way they should be able to launch another attack for at least a few days.''The kalpa-kita must be made to fear the Lumi-väki. Be their teacher....'The descendants of Skorgrím Dourhand have ever been a thorn in the side of Durin's Folk, the Longbeards. Though now outcast, they remain a threat to the Free Peoples of Ered Luin.Sarnúr lies south of Thorin's Hall and west of Noglond in the ruins of Orodost.

Ásgeir has asked you to enter the Dourhand stronghold of Sarnúr and defeat the creatures who dwell there, claiming First Age relics for the dwarves of Thorin's Hall.'At your service and your family's, ${PLAYER}. The Dourhands have been mining in the Blue Mountains and have uncovered the remains of a lost dwarf-city, which the Elves are now calling Sarnúr.  

'Though many of them are undoubtedly destroyed, ancient relics of the First Age surely still remain in the crumbling halls and deep caverns below the Blue Mountains. Can you imagine? The ancient works of the dwarves lying in the hands of those thieving murderers, it boils the blood! The Dourhands may lay claim to the treasures, but without proof of their ancestry, any dwarf may stake his claim, and I do it thus for the Longbeards!

'Retrieve any of these relics that you may find and bring them to me here at the Blue Stone Garrison. The Longbeards would be deeply in your debt for the return of any of the ancient works, great or small.''This is a wonderful find! I will send it off to the vaults right away. I am sure that the smiths will look over every inch of the relics. We are in your debt, ${PLAYER}'The greatest danger in Forochel is the harsh environment itself. The bitter winds and ice have torn the life from more hardy folk than the claws of the kalpa-kita or spears of the Gauredain -- though those dangers come a close second.Complete Forochel Expeditionary.Complete 20 Forochel Quests.Complete quests in ForochelHeikki told you that the Gauredain placed many wards at the northern entrance of the ice-canyons that lead south to Taur Orthon.Gauradan-fetishes can be found around and within the entrance of the Jä-kuru, south of Pynti-peldot.

Heikki has asked you to destroy several Gauradan-fetishes in order to hinder their incursions against the Lossoth of Pynti-peldot.Destroy Gauradan-fetishes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Heikki is at Pynti-peldot, north of the Jä-kuru.

You should return at once to Heikki with news of your success.Talk to Heikki'Ora thinks your intentions are noble, then, does she? That remains to be seen. But if you should care to investigate something....

'You know of the Susi-väki? Yes, they who wear the pelts of their pets. It used to be that they were no real trouble, as their haunts were far from ours. Recently, however, I have had reports from Scout-master Lauri that Gauradan-fetishes have been spotted within the northern exit of the Jä-kuru, the ice-canyons that lead to Taur Orthon, south of here.

'The Susi-väki use fetishes to mark the extent of territory that they claim or intend to claim as theirs. With these, they now threaten our main route to the south. Go and destroy some of those fetishes -- that should throw them into some confusion. If it keeps happening, they shall soon enough get the message that their incursions will not be tolerated.''Excellent. If I have my way, we will never see one of those thieving, looting Susi-väki within the bounds of Itä-mâ.'Juha told you how the Lossoth are struggling to survive the attacks of the Gauredain. He asked you to defeat some of those evil Men and bring back the claws they wield as weapons as proof of your victory.Juha is at Sûri-kylä and Pynti-peldot in Forochel.

Juha has asked you to thin the numbers of the Gauredain threatening the Lossoth and to bring him the claws they wield as weapons as proof of your victory.'Ah, ${PLAYER}, you have proven to be a boon companion to the Lumi-väki in our struggles, and now I would ask you to be a further aid to us. 

'Perhaps you have heard how sorely pressed we are by the Gauredain. These terrible Men surround us on all sides and threaten our very survival. Thinning their number would be of immeasurable help to us.

'If you do defeat some of these vile creatures, bring me back the strange claws they wield as weapons as proof of your victory.''Ah, you have many claws here. Very good. Perhaps these Gauredain will be less of a challenge to us...or at least I so hope!

'If you defeat any more Gauredain, bring me their claws as proof of your victory, and you will earn more esteem in the eyes of the Lumi-väki.'At last you have come upon the homeland of the Gauredain -- and in seeing it, it becomes clear how they came to be such powerful brutes, shaped by the vicious elements and hardships of Forochel, it is hard to imagine them otherwise -- and yet the Lossoth still maintain the trappings of civilization under the same hardships. Have the Gauredain chosen their path in this fashion, or is their brutal path some long-ago contrivance of the Enemy?Defeat Gauredain in ForochelIt seems odd indeed that two people such as the Gauredain and the Lossoth should do battle to claim such a forbidding and difficult land as Forochel, and yet they strive endlessly over it. It seems that home is the place you know, and wherever that might be, Men, dwarves, hobbits, Elves, Orcs -- and whatever else -- will fight to defend it.Defeat Gauredain in ForochelThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

This book contains a number of genealogical diagrams professing to document the various lines and branches of your family, but some of the pages are missing.

You do not know who prepared this book, but perhaps Bilbo Baggins, currently staying in Rivendell, knows something about it. He has an interest in such things and knew your relative Beorn.You have found a book that purports to trace the genealogy of your family. Perhaps Bilbo Baggins, currently staying in Rivendell, will know something about it.Talk to Bilbo Baggins at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.Collect the missing pages of 'Genealogy of the Beornings' and add them to the book.Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 17Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 18Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 19Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 20Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 21Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 22Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 23Genealogy of the Beornings, Page 24You show Bilbo the book labeled 'Genealogy of the Beornings' and his face lights up.

'Ah, most interesting! I do love family trees, ${PLAYER}, when they are complete! Sadly, this one does seem to be missing a few branches. You should search around for the missing pages in order to have the complete diagrams and descriptive paragraphs!

'Your family is larger than I expected, and this could be an interesting resource for documenting its trunk and branches!'Kimmo the artist wishes to make an offering to the water-spirits on behalf of the people of Pynti-peldot.The Lossoth shrine lies along the shore of the Ice Bay, to the west and north of Pynti-peldot, past and downhill from the dwarf-make ruins known as the Lonesome Stones.

Kimmo has asked you to find the Lossoth shrine to the water spirit and place the statuette on the pedestal there before it melts.Tell Kimmo back at Pynti-peldot that you have correctly placed the statuette.'There is a place at the edge of the Ice Bay sacred to the Vesi-henki. It is traditional to leave a carved-ice statue there as a plea for the blessings of the spirits upon the weather and the hunt.

'I have carved the statue, and it is safely stored in the cold, but I am not as young as once I was. Would you take it to its proper place? You will have to be quick, as it will melt from the warmth of your body near your pack if you take too long.

'Follow the water's edge to the west and north, passing through the old ruins known as the Lonesome Stones, and you will find a path leading down from them to the water's very edge. Beware the beasts on the way, and the deadly bite of the Bay!''Excellent work. It is not an easy journey, I know, but you have done Pynti-peldot a great service.''Follow the cliff-edge of the water, to the west and north, passing through the old dwarf-ruins known as the Lonesome Stones, and you will find a path leading down from them to the water's very edge. There you will find the shrine to the Vesi-henki. Hurry! The statue will melt and make a poor offering if you take too long.'Kaj wants you to protect him as he invokes a now-disused Lossoth ritual.Talk to Kaj.Pinja has three brothers. Make sure they do not interrupt Kaj's ritual.Prevent Pinja's brothers from interrupting KajCalm the mammoth and prevent it from rampagingTalk to ManuManu has returned with a wild mammoth in tow.

You should help calm the wild beast.Tame the mammothManu demands your attention.Talk to ManuPinja seems to have been impressed by Kaj's braveryTalk to Pinja'I think we should be able to handle this matter from here on out.''You are fortunate to be able to witness this ritual, you know; few Sivullinen get to see it. It has... well, fallen out of use in recent times.

'I just need to set things up with these components.

'You will do your utmost to ensure this goes smoothly. Some folk may object to this ritual, but no-one is getting hurt! I promise.''Who are you? What are you doing here?

'That braggart Kaj? He does not deserve Pinja -- I do not care if he is the chieftain's son!

'Get out of our way. We will teach him a lesson he shall not soon forget! If you try and stop us, then you shall be taught in his place!''What is Kaj doing here, Sivullinen?

'Forget it. There is no time! That thing has gone wild and needs to be tamed!''There was to be an old ritual by which Kaj thought to win my heart? How thoughtful of him. I have always been interested in the old ways! But he has found one even older, a feat of courage that has impressed even my three brothers! I did not think such a thing could be!

'Oh, there will be a wedding. Kaj cannot back out now, and should he try he will find the mammoth was nothing compared to my displeasure! We will have to find something for our new mammoth pet to do during the ceremony... perhaps give rides to the children?

'Thank you for your part in this. We can sort out the rest ourselves, but I may call on you for help with some of the things I will need for the wedding.''I see what is going on here, outsider. You came here to help Kaj steal away my sister! Many others have tried, and always my brothers and I have shown them they are too weak to wed her. And yet only you and Kaj stood bravely before this untamed mammoth....

'If you wish to wed into our family... but no, I see that Kaj has managed to steal away something of Pinja's before you have had the chance. He has carried away her heart, somehow! Bah.

'Perhaps we will grow to appreciate this one, despite his old ways. He is at least partially-responsible for taming this mammoth, and that is no small thing... it is a mammoth thing! Forgive me my jest, Sivullinen.'Juha told you how the Lossoth protect themselves from the aggressive creatures that inhabit Forochel with their bone-tipped spears.Juha is at Sûri-kylä and Pynti-peldot in Forochel.

Juha has asked you to bring him any Lossoth spear-heads which you find during your travels.'Greetings, Etelä-vieras. I welcome you to Forochel. Would you seek to aid the Lumi-väki? If so, then I have a task for you.

'The Lumi-väki have become masters of this icy realm, using our bone-tipped spears to protect ourselves from the great beasts that threaten us in our hunts. Of course, not every spear lands true. So many spear-heads are lost. However, these spear-heads are quite difficult to make, so the loss of even one is bitter to us.

'If you find any of our spear-heads and bring them to me, you will earn the gratitude of all Lumi-väki.''Ah good! You have found some of our spear-heads! We will put these back into use soon!

'I will gladly welcome any more you find.'A Lossoth, Kaleva, at Sûri-kylä is collecting albino fur at the Lossoth capital.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver albino fur from the beasts surrounding Sûri-kylä in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Albino fur from the beasts surrounding Sûri-kylä. Deliver the items to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Albino fur from the beasts surrounding Sûri-kylä.'We will make use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.A Lossoth, Kaleva, at Sûri-kylä is collecting monstrous ears at the Lossoth capital.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver monstrous ears from the beasts surrounding Sûri-kylä in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Monstrous ears from the beasts surrounding Sûri-kylä. Deliver the items to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Monstrous ears from the beasts surrounding Sûri-kylä.'We will make use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Juha told you how the Lossoth are able to run across the ice at great speed with the bones they strap to their shoes.Juha is at Sûri-kylä and Pynti-peldot in Forochel.

Juha has asked you to keep a wary eye open for any of the luistin the Lossoth may have lost during their travels through the frozen wastes and bring them to him.'I can see that fortune smiled upon the Lumi-väki when you came to Forochel, friend, for you have done much to help us. I would ask for your aid again, but in a matter less dire than some others.

'Perhaps you have seen the Lumi-väki run across the ice? We do this with bones which we strap to our shoes -- we call them luistin. Our people have used these for centuries, travelling at great speed across the frozen land. At times though, one may stumble and fall and lose a luistin. Such a loss is a bitter one, as these luistin are difficult to make.

'If you encounter any luistin in your travels, bring them to me. I will try to reunite them with their owners.''You found one of our luistin! I will try my best to find out who the owner of it is, as I am sure they have missed it.

'Thank you, friend ${CLASS}!'Complete most quests within Angmar.Complete 30 Angmar quests.Complete quests within AngmarThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Martial Movement was written by Brom Hawksbeard, a brawler from Bree-land.You already know how to move; you have been doing it all your life. Brom had better be able to justify your purchase, or you will show him just how well you can move, to his peril!Demand an explanation from BromCollect the missing pages of 'Martial Movement' and add them to the book.Martial Movement, Page 27Martial Movement, Page 33Martial Movement, Page 34Martial Movement, Page 35Martial Movement, Page 36Martial Movement, Page 52Martial Movement, Page 56Martial Movement, Page 58You show Brom the book labelled 'Martial Movement,' and your silence speaks volumes. The brawler raises his hands defensively.

'Look, I know what you're thinking. You're thinking you purchased a book with a number of pages missing, but let me tell you why you did such a thing, and why you should be pleased!

'Are we not writing this book together, you and I? By finding the remaining pages, you are finishing the lesson I began by writing them in the first place! Is that not something special?'

Your urge to brawl rising, you decide to exercise the restraint that most would not.Many wild beasts stalk the ruins of Sarnúr, but these creatures have been enslaved or twisted to the will of Angmar through the cunning devices of the goblins, who breed and cruelly torment them. Many now act as spies and guards for the Dourhands and must be destroyed, lest they be unleashed into the lands of Ered Luin to wreak mischief and havoc upon its peoples.Defeat beasts in SarnúrMany wild beasts stalk the ruins of Sarnúr, but these creatures have been enslaved or twisted to the will of Angmar through the cunning devices of the goblins, who breed and cruelly torment them. Many now act as spies and guards for the Dourhands and must be destroyed, lest they be unleashed into the lands of Ered Luin to wreak mischief and havoc upon its peoples.Defeat beasts in SarnúrThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

This is a strange book indeed. The covers are crafted from strips of some fibrous bark supple enough to be woven together, while the pages themselves appear to be birch bark pounded carefully flat and gently inscribed with runic letters in dense rows, page after page. What magic can have allowed such a fragile-looking tome to survive time and age, you cannot guess. Nor have you ever seen a book of such length, for its author goes into each subject and sentence with such painstaking detail that you cannot imagine how long it must have taken to record it all in this fashion. The subject appears to be anything with roots and leaves. There is such an enormous breadth of knowledge concerning root, leaf, bark, and twig that you could likely study it for years without understanding it all.

To your ire, someone or something has carefully removed a few of the pages from the volume, leaving you to wonder what hidden lore they must have contained. Perhaps the master of the Last Homely House could shed some light on this mysterious tome -- you can think of few others who might.Talk to Elrond at the Last Homely House in RivendellOf Leaf and Twig, page 8Of Leaf and Twig, page 23Of Leaf and Twig, page 53Of Leaf and Twig, page 77Of Leaf and Twig, page 98Of Leaf and Twig, page 145Of Leaf and Twig, page 162Of Leaf and Twig, page 241'Ah, ${PLAYER}, that is quite a rare find you have there. Never has there been a tome containing such complete knowledge of tree and forest, nor will there be again, for as you may have already guessed it was written by the Ents themselves -- or more correctly by the Ent-wives, who were like to use the letters of the Free Peoples when the need struck them, which the Ents rarely do.

'There are few copies extant, for even the most patient of Elven scribes would be sorely tested by the patience required to transcribe that vast tract in full, while the scholars of Men might well see the end of their short lives before they completed the task.

'Unfortunately for you, my friend, the master of the White Council long ago decreed that certain elements of this text were a potential threat should they fall into the hands of the Enemy, and so some key pages were taken from each copy. Alas, as I am the master of the White Council, I will not divulge the location of the pages.'The Lossoth in the eastern tundra have a difficult time staying warm enough in the winters, because of a cold wind that whips through the canyon below. 

They have discovered a rare kind of ore, which burns longer than wood.Search for ore-bearing rocks in the area surrounding Pynti-leiri.Collect Ore (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bring the ore you have collected to Turkka in Pynti-leiri.'I am shivering just standing here!  We have dressed in as many furs as we can carry, but the winds become harsh as they whip through this canyon.  If we are to maintain our camp here we will need to rely on a more unusual source of fire.

'There is a strange kind of rock which seems to grow out of the snow which is occasionally found in the icy plains.  If we set fire to this stone it begins to burn like wood, but will keep burning for much longer.  We will keep searching for wood, but if you are able to bring us back more of the fire-stone, our camp will be much better equipped for weeks to come.''Please find the ore we need, I'm too cold to be polite at the moment.''Thank you, friend.  I will light the hearth, and soon we will sit and talk of many travels.  My, it is easy to forget how heavy this ore can be!'Ida told you that a nearby homestead is being terrorized by Fëoc, a Gauradan.Fëoc is at Voi-teltta, west of Ida's camp along the road.

Ida has charged you with defeating the Gauradan Fëoc, who has claimed Voi-teltta for himself and driven out the Lossoth scouts.Ida is at her encampment, east of Voi-teltta near the entrance to the ice canyons that lead south.

You have reclaimed Voi-teltta for the Lossoth. You should return to Ida and tell her of your success.Lauri is at Pynti-peldot, atop a hill north and west along the road from Ida's camp.

Ida has asked that you inform Lauri of your reclamation of the Voi-teltta homestead.Talk to Scout-master Lauri'Perhaps you can help, Sivullinen.

'A nearby homestead has been claimed by one of the Gauradan wolf-callers -- they are sent out by their tribes as single scouts well-adapted to silent and effective mayhem to cause as much trouble as possible, that their kinsmen may follow in their wake and take advantage of the confusion they sow.

'Go to the homestead west of here along the road, past the hill upon which Pynti-peldot is built, and make sure that the Gauradan Fëoc is defeated. I cannot wait to see the last of him and his accursed wolf.''Ida is wounded, but not badly? Thank the ice-spirits for small mercies, I suppose.

'I rejoice to hear of your success, ${PLAYER}. It is a matter that has been preying upon my mind, but I had not been able to find the time to attend to it personally.

'You may tell Ora that her scout-master believes you to be sincere in your wish to aid the Lumi-väki.''Excellent work, Sivullinen! I do not know what is going on, but it seems that every out-of-the-way Lumi-väki homestead or encampment is under attack all over Forochel!

'We receive reports daily of incursions from Gauredain, wild beasts acting unnaturally, all manner of soft-lander ruffians -- no offence -- all determined to control unattended buildings and resources. It might benefit you to investigate other such reports in the region.

'Speaking of reports -- you had best tell Lauri that I am all right!'Guard-captain Unnarr has asked that you speak with Tórth outside the Blue Stone Garrison.Tórth can be found outside the Blue Stone Garrison near the waterfall in Thorin's Hall.

Guard-captain Unnarr has asked that you speak with Tórth to aid in the fight against the Dourhands.'Hail, ${PLAYER}! If you are seeking glory among the Dwarves of Thorin's Hall, we have need of more sturdy adventurers to aid us in our struggle against the accursed Dourhands. If you are interested, seek Tórth outside of the Blue Stone Garrison.''Torth is outside of the Blue Stone Garrison barracks. Hurry along now!''I was wondering when Unnarr would send more adventurers. We have much to discuss.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Art of the Duel was penned by a scholar of Dol Amroth and begins with a prologue in which the author, Pethebir, reveals that he served Prince Imrahil's father as a loyal swan-knight in his youth. It makes careful note of many skills and techniques employed not only by the warriors of Dol Amroth and the other fiefs of Gondor, but also those of the Corsairs of Umbar, with whom they often came into conflict. In addition, it tells the tale of a great captain named Thorongil, who was a skilled warrior, practiced duellist, and trusted counsellor to the former Steward of Gondor, Ecthelion II.Talk to Mick Maidenhair in the Dwarrow-ward, in Bree-townThe Art of the Duel, Page 25The Art of the Duel, Page 29The Art of the Duel, Page 34The Art of the Duel, Page 39The Art of the Duel, Page 42The Art of the Duel, Page 45The Art of the Duel, Page 51The Art of the Duel, Page 53'Do you need something, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Mick Maidenhair that 'The Art of the Duel' seems to be missing a number of its pages, including several of those discussing a legendary warrior named Thorongil.

'I have not sold you a worthless book, if that is what you think. Mick Maidenhair is no common thief! There is still much to be gained by studying it, even if it is short a few of its pages. If you still don't believe me, then perhaps we should meet in a duel of weaponry and wits—then you will see that I have learned more than enough from it!'

Mick smirks, and after a moment, his demeanour softens.

'Ah, but it is not about that, is it? I understand, my friend. There is always more to be learned, after all. If you are able to find those missing pages, I would be curious to read them myself—once you are finished with them, of course!'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Given the title, you half expected that this book would have been written by an Elf -- and indeed the script is Sindarin -- but it seems that the author is a champion of some ancient Númenórean lineage from a time before the founding of Arnor. The text is so ancient that some of the pages threaten to crumble in your hands as you carefully peruse it. While the dialect is ancient and difficult to read, the author's approach to swordsmanship is rather unusual, placing a great deal of emphasis on grueling repetition and concentration around what appear to be the most basic motions of the form. It suggests a focus on absolute control and power, which then leads to the discovery of finesse and artistry.

If only several key passages and pages were not missing, it could be an invaluable training guide -- perhaps Gimli the Dwarf could tell you more?Talk to Gimli in RivendellThe Artisan Blade, page 2The Artisan Blade, page 4The Artisan Blade, page 6The Artisan Blade, page 9The Artisan Blade, page 14The Artisan Blade, page 15The Artisan Blade, page 17The Artisan Blade, page 26'What is that you say? The Artisan's Blade? I have never heard of it. This is very, very old...where did you come by this tome? Ah, never you mind, let us just see what it is about shall we?

'The book seems remarkably well-preserved, considering how old it must be, but it is difficult to make out. There is no doubt about one thing, though: the man who wrote it knew what he was talking about. If you could manage to fill in those empty spaces, it would be a great boon to any champion seeking to refine his art.

'I have no idea how you would go about finding these missing passages. If you are very lucky, it is possible there are a few copies left scattered about somewhere, but fool's errand or no, it is worth a try. Do let me know how it goes, won't you?'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Book of Oaths discusses the central role of Honour in true leadership and the sanctity of a King's Oath -- or a Captain's. Much of the power to guide men truly derives from the simple bond of Truth itself, and for those who lead, an Oath is a bond of commitment that cannot be broken without inviting a fate most dire. Indeed, a captain who would betray his own word risks more than his life, for it is well-known that an Oath fatefully taken binds far more than the flesh.

This book is said to be the utmost authority in such matters, but the copy you have found seems badly damaged and many important passages are missing. Perhaps Boromir of Gondor, who currently rides here in the north, may know something about this book.Talk to Boromir in RivendellThe Book of Oaths, page 6The Book of Oaths, page 8The Book of Oaths, page 9The Book of Oaths, page 14The Book of Oaths, page 17The Book of Oaths, page 19The Book of Oaths, page 23The Book of Oaths, page 28'What is this you bring me? The Book of Oaths? That is no light tome you carry there, ${PLAYER}, it is a book of great and dire portent indeed. Only a small handful of copies were ever made, and few were those who were permitted to own one, for the knowledge contained therein reaches back into the mists of time when the Dúnedain ruled the great seas to the West with fleets and armies such as we can only imagine today -- power enough to crush Sauron utterly had we such in this Age.

'The book you hold was of their writing, but it describes their downfall as well, for the ancient Kings turned from their Oaths or forgot them, and the Sea itself rose up against them wiping them away as if they had never been.

'This copy seems incomplete, but let me warn you before you embark on any quest to find its missing pages -- once you have learned what lies therin, you must not stray from the path of Truth or it will be your downfall as well.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting was written about a rather notorious hobbit-burglar mid Third Age, when the Shire was not so secure as it is today. Having found himself drafted into the Shire-muster during a particularly difficult season and pitted against Golfimbul's goblins in a number of pitched battles, he learned to fight in a most ungentlehobbitly fashion that served him well against foes considerably larger and stronger than himself.

Alas, it has been many years since its writing, and all copies of this interesting text were thought lost, until now. Perhaps the famous hobbit-burglar Bilbo Baggins will have some further insights or thoughts on the book.You've heard of this book. It was written quite a long time ago and details the exploits of an accomplished Hobbit Burglar, alas this copy seems badly weathered and damaged, rendering a number of the pages indecipherable.

Still, if you want to know more about a Hobbit Burglar, it'd make sense to ask another, wouldn't it? No one would know more about such matters than the esteemed Bilbo Baggins, if you could find a chance to speak with him and show him this text.Talk to Bilbo Baggins at the Last Homely House in Rivendell.Collect the missing pages of The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting and add them to the book.The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 4The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 5The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 7The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 12The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 15The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 19The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 24The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting, page 28'Well, bless me, look at what you have here! A copy of The Expert's Guide to Dirty Fighting! Of all the books on burgling and such, this is the one I have been most disappointed never to find a copy of, and here you walk right in with it. Wherever did you find it? Oh, never mind that, let's see here...

'Alas! It does look much the worse for wear, doesn't it? Someone has gone and torn out several of the pages. That won't do at all. I suppose you will just have to put your ear to the ground and see if you can find some other copy, won't you? I wish that I could help you, but of the books I have tried to find I have never had a good clue as to where I might find this one.

'Well, do let me know if you manage to find those missing pages, won't you? My burgling days may be over, but I do still enjoy a good read about dashing adventures and the like.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

This book is of Elvish origin, having been written so long ago that few of the Elves today were alive at the time of its writing. You cannot even guess at how old it actually is, as it seems to show few signs of age or decay. You cannot recognize the type of parchment it was written upon, nor what kind of ink could withstand the passage of so much time without fading. The main indication of its age and origin is the writing style which is in an extremely old form using ancient letters called Tengwar, which were created by the Noldor many ages ago. The main focus of the book is difficult to translate, but seems to centre around an ancient oath of battle that Elf-guardians might make in the midst of a desperate melee, in essence binding themselves and their enemies in a pact of battle and death that neither the oath-taker nor their sworn-enemies dared break.

It is clear from the text that these oaths carried power beyond that of any simple cry to infuriate or trick one's enemies -- but the secret of the technique seems lost as several key pages have been carefully cut from the book, presumably by someone hoping to safe-guard its secrets in an age long past. Perhaps someone in Rivendell will be able to help decipher the book.DIRECTION TO NPCTalk to Samwise Gamgee in RivendellThe Final Word, page 6The Final Word, page 7The Final Word, page 13The Final Word, page 14The Final Word, page 19The Final Word, page 21The Final Word, page 24The Final Word, page 27'The Final Word? Well, that's an ominous title and no mistake. I can hear Master Gandalf's voice right now -- "Samwise Gamgee, you fool, what's that you've stuck your oversized fingers into this time? A little knowledge is all very well, my dear hobbit, but I'd as soon teach a potato to wield a sword as have you nosing about in matters best left to the Wise...." That's what he'd say if he caught me fiddling about with a book like that, I warrant. But then, where poor Master Frodo's going, I reckon I ought to know everything I can to help safe-guard him as well as I may, whether I'm a fool or not for doing it.

'Now that is a mouthful and no mistake. Lady Arwen and old Bilbo have been helping me with my letters since I arrived, but this is near beyond me at that -- and with these missing pages I'm not sure what to make of it, except now I'm surer than before that this book here wasn't written for someone who doesn't mean what they say, if you know what I mean.

'Should you manage to pull those pages together, you might want to think twice before you read it cover to cover. My Gaffer would drop this one right into the Brandywine, I warrant, and I can't say for certain as that'd be a mistake.'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

The Furthest Charge was written by a Captain of Gondor who fought in a misguided battle shortly before the War of the Last Alliance. In this battle, the forces of the young kingdom of Gondor found themselves pitted against a company of Elven hunters out of Lórien, both sides having been tricked into battle by a device of the Enemy, who was determined to cripple the alliance that would eventually result in his overthrow. In this book, the Captain describes a valiant but doomed charge of the Men of Gondor against the Elves, within which they suffered a terrible onslaught of arrows raining down from the sky in such fashion as to cause brave men to cower and falter in their advance, trapped unmoving beneath an endless hail of biting wood and metal.

The Furthest Charge is considered to be a defining work on the power of the hunter's art in the grand battles that have defined the face of Middle-earth today. Unfortunately, this copy is badly damaged by time and wear. Perhaps Legolas of Mirkwood, purported to be in Eriador, could shed some light upon the text.Talk to Legolas in RivendellCollect the pages of The Furthest Charge from the goblins of the Misty Mountains, and the nomads hiding in the Trollshaws.The Furthest Charge, page 7The Furthest Charge, page 9The Furthest Charge, page 10The Furthest Charge, page 14The Furthest Charge, page 17The Furthest Charge, page 18The Furthest Charge, page 21The Furthest Charge, page 24'You have brought me a most interesting find today, have you not? The Furthest Charge is at once an excellent work on the power of the hunter to alter the tide of battle, while also describing a shameful blot upon the honour of Elves and Men alike by the ease with which it describes our falling to the base trickery of the Enemy.

'Nevertheless, the Alliance held, and the Enemy was overthrown, so perhaps this history serves as much to describe the strength of our bonds as their weakness. I only hope that we can show such strength and honour in the face of the Enemy again as the darkness continues to gain strength in this age.

'Now as to this book, time has taken a toll upon it. Even my eyes cannot make out some of these faded passages, but it is not unique. You may yet be able to find the missing or faded passages elsewhere in the world if you are tireless in your search. And as a hunter, you will find much to learn from the complete text should you succeed.'Niko has challenged you with hunting the kalpa-kita that haunt Leijona-kotin, a wooded area east of Jänis-leiri.Kalpa-kita can be found at Leijona-kotin, east of Jänis-leiri.

Niko wants you to cull the numbers of kalpa-kita in the area and especially to defeat a vicious cat named Pitkä-hammas.Defeat kalpa-kita (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Pitkä-hammasNiko is at Jänis-leiri, west from Leijona-kotin.

You should return to Niko and tell him of your success.Talk to Niko'You have proven a valuable acquaintance, Sivullinen. Perhaps you would help with one last task?

Nearby, in a wooded area we call Leijona-kotin, many kalpa-kita dwell. They are vicious beasts and kill many animals and Lumi-väki alike. I want you to go there and cull their numbers. Perhaps that will forestall any further aggression from them.

'You can find Leijona-kotin to the east of here. Be sure you kill their leader, the one we call Pitkä-hammas.''He... he is dead? Truly? Pitkä-hammas is dead?

'You may return to Ora and tell her that her Master of the Hunt has deemed you to be a skilled hunter, ${PLAYER}.''You can find Leijona-kotin to the east of here. Be sure you kill their leader, the one we call Pitkä-hammas.'Torcuil was grateful for the help you have offered his people and told you that his people needs additional allies if they are to survive under Angmar's harsh rule. He told you about a tale he has heard of dwarves living in Angmar's shadow and wonders if they could be allies for his people in the future.The dwarf-holding is east of Rammas Deluon and south of Duvuinen.

The merchant Torcuil asked you to seek the lost dwarves and obtain their allegiance.Find the hidden holding of the dwarvesThere are many dwarves within Gabilshathûr.

You have discovered hidden holding of the dwarves in Angmar, Gabilshathûr. You should speak with someone within the holding and convey Torcuil's message.Talk to the leader of Gabilshathûr'You have done much to help us here in Tyrn Lhuig. It is not easy for us to accept such aid. In fact, it was not that long ago that we would have refused such help, but in the face of Angmar's growing might, such aid is necessary if we are to survive. Even though I am not a leader among my people, I can see we need allies.

'Such allies might be found to the east, if the tales are true. It is told that past the Rammas Deluon there is a dwarf-holding. They could prove to be good allies for my people against Angmar in the future.

'If you would heed the words of a mere merchant, travel past the Rammas Deluon and look for these lost dwarves. The dwarves' hall should be somewhere south of Duvuinen.''You have come at the behest of the Hillmen to the west, have you? Good, good, someone who is not our foe, but a friend...or so I hope!

'I suppose it was foolish for we dwarves to come here those years ago, but we were chafing under the rule of the Dourhands and left Othrikar in hopes of finding gems and ores. At that time, Angmar's evil was dormant, and later we paid too little heed to its growing power. Once its Orc-armies marched forth from Carn Dûm, we were cut off.

'Now we are hard-pressed from all sides and could use your help if we are to survive in these blighted lands. We cannot give you much in return, but if we survive, perhaps one day we can lend our strength to throwing down Angmar!''What? You have come from the west to aid us? Speak to Avar immediately!''So you have crossed Rammas Deluon, have you? Friendly folk here are few and far between, so I am glad to see you. You should inform Avar that you have arrived...he will be most pleased to see you.''If you are seeking the truth behind the tales I have heard about the lost dwarves, you will want to cross Rammas Deluon and look for their holding south of Duvuinen.''The tales are true? Well then, speak to someone within this "Gabilshathûr" immediately!'Búdan told you that there are others who may need your help in Angmar. There are tales of a hidden holding of the dwarves, east of Rammas Deluon. If the tale is indeed true, the dwarves have hidden themselves well, but Búdan fears it is only a matter of time before Angmar discovers them.The dwarf-holding lies to the east of Rammas Deluon and south of Duvuinen.

Búdan spoke to you of a hidden holding of the dwarves in Angmar and suggests that the dwarves could use your aid.Find the hidden holding of the dwarvesThe leader of the dwarves is at Gabilshathûr.

You have discovered hidden holding of the dwarves in Angmar, Gabilshathûr. You should seek out and speak with the leader of the dwarves.Talk to the leader of Gabilshathûr'There is much that we owe to you, ${PLAYER}. You have given us hope that one day we will throw off Angmar's yoke. There are others, though, in these lands that need such hope as well.

'There are tales and whispers that there are dwarves to the east of Rammas Deluon. If it is true, they have hidden there long and well, but as Angmar stretches forth its hand, they may soon find they can hide no longer.

'If you wish to discover if these tales are true, travel past Rammas Deluon and look for these lost dwarves. The dwarves' hall should be somewhere south of Duvuinen.''You have come at the behest of the Men to the west, have you? Good, good, someone who is not our foe, but a friend...or so I hope!

'I suppose it was foolish for we dwarves to come here those years ago, but we were chafing under the rule of the Dourhands and left Othrikar in hopes of finding gems and ores. At that time, Angmar's evil was dormant, and later we paid too little heed to its growing power. Once its Orc-armies marched forth from Carn Dûm, we were cut off.

'Now we are hard-pressed from all sides and could use your help, if we are to survive in these blighted lands. We cannot give you much in return, but if we survive, perhaps one day we can lend our strength to throwing down Angmar!''What? You have come from the west to aid us? Speak to Avar immediately!''So you have crossed Rammas Deluon, have you? Friendly folk here are few and far between, so I am glad to see you. You should inform Avar that you have arrived...he will be most pleased to see you.''If you are seeking to find the truth behind the tales of the dwarves I told you of, you will want to cross Rammas Deluon and look for their holding south of Duvuinen.''The tales are true? Well then, speak to someone within this "Gabilshathûr" immediately!'Kaj is now prepared to embark upon the Lossoth-ritual he has vowed to complete.Kaj is in Pynti-peldot.

You should speak with Kaj when you are ready to assist him with his ritual.Talk to KajPinja is outside her house at Lumi-talletta, south-west of Pynti-peldot in Itä-ma.

You should speak with Pinja at once.Talk to Pinja'${PLAYER}, it is time for the ritual. Will you accompany me and protect me as I complete it? 

'Your task is to ensure I am interrupted by no one. Especially her three -- you know what, forget I said that.

'When you are ready, let me know, and we shall leave.''Now, I am going to go into the house and... get something. You wait here and make sure no-one else interrupts!

'No, I am not worried. There should be no trouble at all. None.

'Just keep an eye out, that's all.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest, and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Kaj means well, if only he had been forthright with me! It seems he is less afraid of mammoths than he is of speaking to a woman whose heart he prizes!'Afwald, the provisioner at the dwarf-ruin of Hrimbarg, awaits the return of albino skin.You have accepted a task to deliver albino skin from the worms found in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains to Afwald. Afwald can be found within the ruins at Hrimbarg, also found in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Albino skin from the worms in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains. You can deliver these items to Afwald within the ruins at Hrimbarg in the Misty Mountains.Wanted: Albino skin from the worms in the eastern reaches of the Misty Mountains.'I am glad to see that you are making your way through these terrible creatures. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The dwarves at Gabilshathûr await a delivery of great membranes to be made to Avar.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver great membranes from the slugs surrounding Gabilshathûr to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Great membranes from the slugs surrounding Gabilshathûr. Delivery of the items should be made to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Great membranes from the slugs surrounding Gabilshathûr.'We'll make good use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The dwarves at Gabilshathûr await a delivery of pure black skin to be made to Avar.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver pure black skin from the worms surrounding Gabilshathûr to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Pure black skin from the worms surrounding Gabilshathûr. Delivery of the items should be made to Avar at the dwarf-outpost.Wanted: Pure black skin from the worms surrounding Gabilshathûr.'We'll make good use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.It seems that the trolls inhabiting Sarnúr are of a particularly dim-witted -- if brutal -- strain. With hearts of hard-edged ice, they will destroy anything that stands in their way unless you can stop them.Defeat Snow-trolls in SarnúrIt seems that the trolls inhabiting Sarnúr are of a particularly dim-witted -- if brutal -- strain. With hearts of hard-edged ice, they will destroy anything that stands in their way unless you can stop them.Defeat Snow-trolls in SarnúrDefeat Uruks in Angmar.Defeat Uruks in AngmarDefeat many Uruks in Angmar.Defeat Uruks in AngmarThe first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

Valour's Marches -- the very title of the book you hold stirs something within your breast as you look upon it. Though its cover crumbles with age, the binding remains strong, as if infused with the same martial fervour that the melodies within it strive to invoke. This book is one of the rare few that survived the fall of the kingdom of Númenor, coming across the waves by some unlikely circumstance to Middle-earth, where it has lain hidden for ages. Though written in the Sindarin tongue, the songs are those of the noble kingdoms of Men. Nevertheless, they speak to the heart of any who would face battle, calling forth visions of endless, shining hosts on the march, thundering with the clash of steel in a thousand voices.

In the hands of a minstrel, these songs of martial eminence provide crucial insight into the working of the warrior's heart and how it might be aroused from its slumber in times of need. If only so many of the songs were not frustratingly missing from the volume you now hold. Perhaps Lindir of Rivendell may be able to help fill the voids.Talk to Lindir at the Last Homely House in RivendellValour's Marches, page 1Valour's Marches, page 5Valour's Marches, page 7Valour's Marches, page 8Valour's Marches, page 12Valour's Marches, page 16Valour's Marches, page 23Valour's Marches, page 26'Ah, my friend, now you have managed to bring me something extraordinary indeed, for I myself have never held a copy of the book you hold there. By the time of its writing, the people of Númenor and my own were sadly estranged, and after its fall, well, let us say that copies were hard to come by. It is difficult for me to imagine the long path this book has travelled to find its way to your hands today, and harder still for me to see how you will be able to find the pages that are missing from it -- but do not let my foolish words dissuade you. If fate has seen fit to deliver the book to your hands through such contrived circumstance, then why not a few simple pages?

'In any case, you will find no finer primer on how to kindle the fire of war in a band of fellows through the medium of song. The men of ancient Númenor were masters of such grand affairs in a fashion that even the Noldor perhaps were not, for few among the Elves see the mastery of warfare as an end glorious unto itself as Men do, though we were more skilled in it.

'The difference may seem semantic, but when it comes to music that is what matters, is it not?'Tero is worried; his colleague and friend Juhani has gone missing.Kissa-sari is an island in the river to the south and slightly east of Zigilgund.

Tero suggests you search an island known as Kissa-sari for his friend Juhani.Find Juhani'Your pardon, but I could really use a bit of assistance...

'I've got a good friend, a Hunter by the name of Juhani, who was due back a good little while ago. Went out looking for furs -- kalpa-kita, you know, they have right soft and pretty furs. Sell well, in the south, keep us in pipeweed and ale, as well as non-essential goods and supplies.

'It has now been too long, and I am concerned.  Please, go look for him! Kalpa-kita infest an island in the middle of the river, south and a bit east of here.''Have you not found any trace yet? Might try heading south and looking on the island called Kissa-sari....'The first four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Angmar, Eregion, and Moria. The last four pages of this book can be found on enemies scattered across Forochel, Moria, and the Misty Mountains.

"Whispers in the Dark," one of the strangest books ever written, was actually penned by a Rune-keeper of the race of Man, not an Elf or dwarf. He hailed from Rhudaur not long before its fall in the war between Arnor and the forces of the Witch-king. Its pages discuss the powers of the class no Man has ever been able to follow, much less master. The writing is mad, scribbled in wild, uneven runes in a disorganized manner upon stained pages, many of which are loose leaves simply stuffed into the book. The writer was driven to madness by seeking the secrets of Rune-craft, but rumour has it that great and terrible discoveries were recorded in this text. The book's spine is cracked and weak, and only the first half of its pages cling, yellowed and fragile, to this withered binding. Elrond would yearn to know what the lost pages contain.Elrond can usually be found dwelling in his library in the Last Homely House in Rivendell, where rare and excellent volumes line the walls in plenty. He is sure to be delighted to look upon such a treasure as "Whispers in the Dark."Talk to Elrond Half-elven at the Last Homely House in Rivendell"Whispers in the Dark," page 3"Whispers in the Dark," page 8"Whispers in the Dark," page 13"Whispers in the Dark," page 15"Whispers in the Dark," page 18"Whispers in the Dark," page 25"Whispers in the Dark," page 32"Whispers in the Dark," page 39'I have heard many tales of this book, though I scarcely believed them. It always struck me as unlikely that any Man could attain mastery of Rune-lore in his short time upon the earth.

'Terrible that knowledge must have been, for the runes upon these leaves could not have been the work of a man with a sound mind. He tried to learn too quickly that which needs long years to learn, and he paid the price. But there may still be much of value here, for it is not infrequent that the wisest of men can learn much from the fool.

'Would that it were complete, but its other half is surely gone. Rhudaur was a desolate place in the end, and no friend to lore and learning. Still, there is hope that the lost pages are out there still, waiting to be recovered that they may join their brethren in this volume once more.'The Worms of Forochel have adapted to the eternally cold environment, growing a coat of strangely-furred scales to ward off the cold, while their claws and teeth carry a strange cold of their own, often causing those who suffer wounds from them to succumb to a deadly chill.Defeat worms in ForochelIt is uncertain whether these worms are native to Forochel or whether they have come down from the northern waste-land during the winter when the Ice Bay is frozen solid enough to travel across. Of course, it no longer matters from whence they came, for they are here now and a threat to all who live in Forochel.Defeat worms in ForochelChalky salt is an essential export which the Lossoth in the Western Tundra trade to the snow sled builders in the south. The salt is used to cure rawhide strips, which are used as thread.Far to the west in Länsi-mâ there are hot springs around which salt encrusted rocks can be found. Gather salt from these rocks to bring back to Väinö in Norsu-leiri.Collect salt (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Väinö in Norsu-leiri with the salt you have collected.'Now that the outpost is secure, we can begin gathering a most valuable resource: salt.  Our post is assigned the task of watching the springs and collecting chalky salt which can be sold at a handsome price to workers in the southern forests.  They use it somehow when they build our sleds.  

'We must stay and keep watch.  If you can go and collect the salt from the vents, we will make sure to pay you well...''Collect some of the salt for us, and we will be sure to reward you.''Well, this is a fine amount of salt.  The sled builders will have quite a lot of work ahead of them!  Take with you this token of our appreciation, and our thanks.'You returned Gránung to Déornyd only to learn that he has a problem with his other blade.Gauredain roam to the north and west of Pynti-peldot, near the Lonesome Stones.

Déornyd has asked you to replace his broken sword with one stolen from one of the Gauredain.Find Déornyd a new sword from a GauradanGauredain roam to the north and west of Pynti-peldot, near the Lonesome Stones.

Déornyd has asked you to replace his broken sword with one stolen from one of the Gauredain.Find Déornyd a new sword from a GauradanDéornyd is at Pynti-peldot, south and east of where the Gauredain roam.

You should bring the Gauradan-sword to Déornyd.'Ah, ${PLAYER}... you helped me before; perhaps you can help me again. Look at this! My second blade -- apparently a cheap and weak piece of smithing, for which I will disembowel that merchant if I ever see him again -- has gone and snapped clean in two from the cold! Blasted, brittle, low-quality steel.

'I am going to need another sword -- something decent. There are more of those wild men along the shore-line, towards that pretty little shrine the Lossoth have. I will wager one of them would have a blade worth wielding! It seems only fair, as they tried to rob me of mine, that I should rob them of theirs. You had best be wary, though; the Gauredain are a fierce people.

'The shrine is north-west of here, near some dwarf-ruins, but be careful: the way is dangerous.''The ruins known as the Lonesome Stones can be found by following the water's edge north and west from Pynti-peldot, and I saw Gauradan warriors all along the way.''Ah! Let us take a look at it.

'Well, fine craftsmanship it is not, that is for certain... but it is strong, and it is sharp, and it will withstand this beastly cold. 

'It will serve admirably until I return home and can obtain a new blade. My thanks!'A terrible wind has come from Angmar, bringing with it a freezing winter.Bears can be found south of Sûri-kylä across the ice bay.

Nôra has asked you to help supply the village of Sûri-kylä for the winter by collecting bear-fur.Collect bear-fur  (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nôra is at Sûri-kylä.

You should take the furs you collected to Nôra.'It is strange to see so many strangers this far north. Sûri-kylä has not seen anything like it since the old times. At least, that is what the old tales tell. We have had so many traders, yet provisions are still short. There has been a long cold, and many have felt a change in the winds. The Elders fear that we may not see an end to this bitterness for some time.

'One supply that I am in great need of is bear-fur. It is used in many of our clothes and rituals, but the hunters have had great difficulty in obtaining any.

'The bears often wander down by the bay hunting for fish just south of Sûri-kylä. If you would go there and collect some bear-fur for me, I may find a way to reward you for your services.''Have you found any bear fur yet? It will be a cold winter if we can not obtain more soon. The bears usually hunt for fish south of Sûri-kylä across the ice bay; you should hunt them there.''You have returned with the bear-fur! Our hunters and trappers will be very grateful for your fine work.'Mika wants you to defeat the Angmarim Sorcerer who is attempting to train the Gauredain to corrupt Mammoths.The Angmarim sorcerer may be found east and north of Mika's camp.

Search near Norsu-leiri for the Angmarim sorcerer who is teaching the Gauradan the vile rites to ensorcel mammoths.Norsu-hauta is south and west of the sorcerer's camp.

You should return to Mika in Norsu-hauta and tell him of your victory.'This Angmarim sorcerer dares to try and corrupt our Norsu, using idiot Susi-väki as tools. I suggest you look to the east and slightly north, on the slopes of those peaks just there across the road. They are rarely visited by us, and so play host to many undesirable creatures and väki.

'Search the area near Norsu-leiri! You must find the sorcerer who is giving the Susi-väki these tainted and dark instructions, and defeat him!''East and north, at the foot of the hill at the top of which Norsu-leiri was built long ago. That is where I suspect the sorcerer will be.''Blessings of the Ice Spirits on you! The proud and mighty Norsu are safer through your actions.'It seems that the Angmarim may at best be searching for something, or perhaps simply meddling in the conflicts between the Lossoth and the Gauredain. A full-strength invasion does not seem to be their purpose in Forochel -- at least, not yet.Defeat Angmarim in ForochelThórth has fixed the broken key, and wants you to gather allies and find and defeat Arnmórth the Exacting within the Icereave Mines.Find the inner office within the Icereave Mines.  Bring allies.Defeat Arnmórth the ExactingReturn to Thórth at Zigilgund with the key, and report your defeat of Arnmórth the Exacting.'Right! That ought to just about do it. The key be fixed. 

'One of my cohorts caught an' interrogated a Dourhand just a little while ago, and found out that they're bein' led by a right vicious dwarf, name of Arnmórth the Exacting. He's the greediest Dourhand since Skorgrím: thinks o' nothin' but gold and how to keep it. Ye'll need help defeatin' him, just to warn ye: he's likely to have plenty o' guards.

'Now, take this key, find his quarters, open that door, and give 'im what for! ...And bring the key back when yer done -- once the Icereave Mines be ours again, we'll be wantin' to use that room.''Go find the quarters of Arnmórth the Exacting in the Icereave Mines, and defeat him! ...I've fixed the key for ye, what else're ye waitin' on?''Hah, excellent! I'll wager Arnmórth didn't even know wha' hit him!

'${PLAYER}, ye will always have a welcome by the fires an' at the tables within the Icereave Mines -- once they're ours again. Ye've more than earned a reward.'The sudden appearance of Deluches, the captain of the fell spirits, kept you from banishing the creatures from the shrine.The necromancers of Imlad Balchorth can be found at the ruins high in the southern hills of the area.

The chief sorcerer of the necromancers, the Spirit-master, no doubt controls Deluches, the spirit-captain. Gautar has asked you to defeat him, and thus banish Deluches forever.Eithel Úmfaer is in southern Imlad Balchorth, west of Myrkworth.

Deluches, captain of the fell spirits, has been summoned to Eithel Úmfaer, the master shrine in Imlad Balchorth, where he must be destroyed.Gautar is back at Myrkworth, east and a bit north of Eithel Úmfaer.

You have defeated Deluches and banished at least some of the fell spirits of Angmar. You should bring the good tidings to Gautar.'It occurs to me that this spirit you fought is after all a creature of the same ilk as those we seek to banish. He must not be controlled by the shrine, or he would not have been able to flee you so easily when he was on the brink of defeat.

'My guess is that another controls Deluches. The necromancers of Imlad Balchorth hide in a ruin high in the hills on the south side of the area. Quite possibly one of them is the protector of Deluches himself. I suspect, too, that you will find a second shrine in their lair. I was a fool to think they only had one.

'Find this master shrine. No doubt you will encounter your enemy again -- and mayhap the chief of the necromancers as well -- but this time if you are victorious, his defeat will be final...I hope''You have done very well indeed. Deluches was evidently the key to the necromancers' control over those spirits. It is unfortunate, though, that those which have been banished are but a small part of the host of Angmar.''Deluches escaped you once...he must not do so again!'The Lossoth maintain beacons to help the lost find their way and to call for aid when it is needed.The Kantâ-vilkku is east and a little south of Sûri-kylä along the snow drifts that look over the ice bay of Forochel.

Matti has asked that you go to the Kantâ-vilkku and use the parts found there to repair it.Collect banner scraps  (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect coal  (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect leg pieces (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Kantâ-vilkku is east and a little south of Sûri-kylä along the snow drifts that look over the ice bay of Forochel.

Matti has asked that you visit the Kantâ-vilkku and repair it.Matti is at Sûri-kylä, west and a little north from the Kantâ-vilkku.

You should return to Matti and tell him of your success.Talk to Matti'Welcome to Sûri-kylä. Perhaps you have seen the Kantâ-vilkku on your journey? The Kantâ-vilkku helps guide travellers and provides a means of survival in the harsh storms that too often visit us.

'Storms and harsh winds have done a good deal of damage to our beacon. If you seek to aid us, maybe you would help to fix the damage?

'The Kantâ-vilkku is east and south of Sûri-kylä along the drifts that overlook the bay, and you will find the parts scattered around the ground near at hand.''The repairs are done? That is well, ${PLAYER}. With the recent storms, the Kantâ-vilkku will see more use than it has for many long years.''How go the repairs? Many lost travellers and hunters rely upon the Kantâ-vilkku for their survival. Ensuring that it is well-maintained is very important.

'The Kantâ-vilkku is east and south of Sûri-kylä along the snow drifts that look over the ice bay of Forochel. Once your work is complete, return here so that we may speak again.''How go the repairs? Many lost travellers and hunters rely upon the Kantâ-vilkku for their survival. Ensuring that it is well-maintained is very important.

'The Kantâ-vilkku is east and south of Sûri-kylä. Once your work is complete, return here so that we may speak again.'Peikko have long terrorized the snowdrifts of Länsi-mâ. The time has come to deal with them.Peikko can be found east and up the mountains from Zigilgund, in a cave.

Keli has asked you to defeat a number of peikko.Defeat peikko in Länsi-mâ (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Keli can be found at Zigilgund, west of the peikko-cave.

You should return to Keli with news of your victory.Talk to Keli'So, do you fancy yourself a bit of a hunter? Ha! Of course you do! Few make it through the wastes of Forochel without collecting a tidy variety of trophies. Well, if you are looking for a challenge -- something a little out of the ordinary -- I've got one for you.

'Have you seen the man-shaped, white-furred, snout-faced snow-beasts? They are clever, they are devilishly strong, and they are hard to kill. The Lossoth call them peikko, and they are becoming a more and more serious threat as their numbers increase.

'Kill several of them, and your legacy will be assured. They can be found all over, but especially in a cave they have infested. Go directly east of here and up the side of the hill and look for a cave entrance.''What are you doing here? Afraid or just forgetful? Go kill those peikko! East and up the hill, I said!''Ha! I hope you gave those abominable creatures a taste of your blade, ${PLAYER}!

'That is not the end of the matter, though... there is more to do.'You retrieved the scroll from Raghnall, but it was written in Black Speech, which Lorniel is unable to translate.Laerdan is in Gath Forthnír.

Lorniel suggested you seek out an Elf named Laerdan in hopes of learning what Raghnall's scroll would reveal.Elrond Halfelven is at Rivendell in the Trollshaws.

Laerdan, also unable to translate the scroll, sent you to Rivendell to speak with Elrond Halfelven.'I fear we may have been deceived, ${PLAYER}, for this scroll is written in Black Speech and may say nothing of value. I cannot say, for Black Speech is not a tongue with which I am familiar. Even so, I would know what this says.

'Perhaps the Elf Laerdan may have some proficiency in this writing. He is an old friend of my father's and is here in Gath Forthnír.

'Take the scroll to him and ask him his opinion. If it may help us free my father, then we must learn its secrets at once!''Take the scroll to Laerdan, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps he has some knowledge of the Black Speech.''Alas, but I have never taken the time to learn the cruel mutterings of the Orcs. I have no desire to speak with them by any means save my sword.

'If this scroll did indeed come from Carn Dûm, Elrond Halfelven may have an interest in it, and he assuredly knows the Black Speech. Go to Rivendell and seek out the Master of the Last Homely House, if you would know the answer to your riddle.''I can be of no aid to you with this scroll, for the Black Speech is not my study. Take it to Elrond of Rivendell.''Elrond Halfelven? Yes, I should have thought of that myself. It is a long journey, but one you must make!''What is this? You were sent by the daughter of Golodir and by the Elf Laerdan? What a strange fate has brought you hither. The Shadow of Angmar has grown with the Shadow in the East.

'Give me the scroll... I will unravel this mystery for you.'You journeyed to Rivendell to seek the aid of Elrond Halfelven in translating Raghnall's scroll. The Master of the Last Homely House gave you a mysterious welcome, but did offer to assist you.Dwalin is at Thorin's Hall in Ered Luin.

Elrond transcribed the scroll into Westron, but told you to seek the aid of Dwalin and the dwarves of Ered Luin in forging the pass-key to Carn Dûm.'Lorniel's hopes were not wholly in vain, for this scroll does indeed reveal a way to pass the gates of Carn Dûm. The scroll tells of a special key needed, forged of a dire black iron found only in the foul pits of Angmar... and the secrets of its crafting.

'I believe such a key could be made of mithril, for the strength of Moria-silver may overcome whatever resistance the lock may give. Unfortunately, we have not mithril to use. Perhaps you should seek the aid of Lord Dwalin at Thorin's Hall in Ered Luin.

'The dwarves know best the secrets of mithril... it is they you should speak with. I have transcribed the contents of the scroll in Westron... the dwarves should have no difficulty reading it.''Seek out Lord Dwalin in Thorin's Hall and bid him greetings in my name. He is a friend of Rivendell and will help you.''Greetings, ${RACE}! You have come from Elrond of Rivendell? Then you will have the aid of Durin's Folk. Let me see that scroll....'The dwarf-holding of Gabilshathûr is in grave danger. An army of Orcs is not far away, and Orc raiding-parties are harrassing the outpost.There are Blogmal and Krahjarn Orcs throughout Eastern Malenhad, north of Gabilshathûr.

Gisur has asked you to defeat many Orcs in an attempt to give the dwarves of Gabilshathûr some breathing-room.Defeat Orc-warriors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orc-defilers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orc-skirmishers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Guard-captain Gisur is at Gabilshathûr, south of Duvuinen.

You have defeated a large number of Orcs and should return to Gisur to inform him of your success.'You came here, alone, from the west? I suppose there is more to you than one would guess at a glance.

'Here, you can indeed be of some help to us. I am charged with the protection of Gabilshathûr, and this had become quite a challenge since an Orc-scout discovered our outpost a few days back. Now, we are constantly harassed by the Orcs, and I am not sure how much longer we can hold out.

'If you can slay some of those Orcs, though, we might get a bit of breathing space. I am not picky, slaying any Blogmal or Krahjarn Orc you encounter in Eastern Malenhad is enough... although to be most helpful, you should slay some of their warriors and skirmishers. Oh, and some defilers as well....''Oh good, you have returned! You have done well, but I fear that your efforts may have come to naught. The Orcs are moving on us!''Defeat the Blogmal and Krahjarn Orcs north of Gabilshathûr. It is but a drop in the Sea in regard to their numbers, but if we can distract them from our holding, we will have bought ourselves a bit of breathing-room...at least, that is what I hope!'The Rangers at Gath Forthnír await a delivery of broken wooden clubs to be made to Areneth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver broken wooden clubs from the sentient creatures near Gath Forthnír to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-camp.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the merrevail, Orcs, and other intelligent creatures near Gath Forthnír. Deilvery of the items should be made to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-outpost.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the merrevail, Orcs, and other intelligent creatures near Gath Forthnír.'We'll make good use of these.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Rangers at Gath Forthnír await a delivery of monstrous filth to be made to Areneth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver monstrous filth from the insects near Gath Forthnír to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-camp.Wanted: Monstrous filth from the insects near Gath Forthnír. Deilvery of the items should be made to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-outpost.Wanted: Monstrous filth from the insects near Gath Forthnír.'We will find a use for this.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Gautar examined the tokens you recovered from the fell spirits and discovered that they bear signs of Angmar, sigils that may be binding the spirits to the mortal world. A name also was written upon them: Deluches.The shrine of the fell spirits is on an islet in a small pond in the southern part of Imlad Balchorth, south and a little west from Myrkworth.

Gautar has asked you to go to the shrine of the fell spirits in Imlad Balchorth. The shrine can be recognized as a large iron spike, a common feature of the works of the Angmarim which might make the shrine difficult to find.Gautar is back at Myrkworth, north and a bit east from the shrine of Deluches.

You were unable to defeat the terrible captain of the spirits, Deluches, so the fell spirits could not be banished. You should return to Gautar and report these events.'These tokens bear signs of Angmar, terrible sigils that may well be binding the fell spirits to this place. You must find the shrine used by the Angmarim in their rituals. I believe it is on an islet in a small pond in southern Imlad Balchorth. It may be difficult to find, for it appears as a large iron spike, which is not uncommon among the works of the Angmarim.

'If you call forth the spirits at the shrine, I believe they will come to that place one last time, there to be banished forever. It is a dreadful chance though...I will confess that my folk are not well-versed in dealings with evil spirits. I think the Elves would know more, and there are few enough of those in Angmar.''Alas, I had not reckoned that the spirits' master would himself be a fell spirit, able to interfere with your quest. Why, I wonder, was he able to escape you so easily?

'I have sent you into grave danger on a fool's errand. But perhaps we can still repair my error.''Have you banished the fell spirit Deluches? No? Please, you must draw him out and send him back to the Void!'Sigar has asked you to kill many wights in Imlad Balchorth in the hopes of thinning their number and stopping the nightly invasions.Wights can be found in Imlad Balchorth, west of Myrkworth.

Sigar has asked you to destroy the wights in Imlad Balchorth. You can find them wandering the eastern edges of Imlad Balchorth.Defeat wights in Imlad Balchorth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigar is at Myrkworth, east of Imlad Balchorth.

Having destroyed many of the wights as asked, return to Sigar in Myrkworth and tell him the deed is done.'They haunt my dreams! I want off of this forsaken rock and back under my own. Please help, good friend, and eliminate those shambling wights. Thin their number, and perhaps our guards will no longer have to deal with the threat of them every night.

'They lie in wait to the west in Imlad Balchorth. Destroy as many wights there as you can, then return to me. I do not know why we ever came to this abominable land -- there is no wealth in this waste -- but I want out, even if it means cutting down every walking corpse in Angmar!''So, it is done? That is marvelous news! Now we must hope that the Dead remain so!''Hurry! Destroy the wights quickly.  Another night of invasion must not happen! They are to the west, in Imlad Balchorth. You cannot miss them!'Old dwarf-ruins litter the landscape of Forochel. Being the hardiest of the Free Peoples, the dwarves claim that they were the first to settle here long ago, in hopes of finding riches beneath the frozen earth -- but most of these settlements were abandoned long ago.

Hoping to strengthen their claims as legitimate heirs of the ancient Dwarf-lords, the Dourhands seek to re-build these settlements with the aid of their Angmarim allies, but this has brought their cousins the Longbeards north as well, seeking to dispute their claims, no matter the worth (or lack thereof) of the mines here.Defeat Dourhands in ForochelThe attempts by the Dourhands to claim a legitimate descent from the Dwarf-lords of old seems futile at this point, as their dark alliance with Angmar becomes more common knowledge among the dwarves of Middle-earth -- but if anything, this turn of events has only made them more dangerous and desperate, as they now fight for the very survival of their misguided house.Defeat Dourhands in ForochelThe Snow Beasts have been forced out of the Lossoth outpost in the Western Tundra, but will return unless their matron is defeated.The Great Snow-matron can be found in the western tundra.

Defeating the Snow-matron will frighten the yeti herd and turn them against the Gauredain.Defeat the Great Snow-matron'It gets tiring, keeping the peikko away from our post.  They are persistent, but it is not because they want to harm us.

'The Gauredain are surely provoking these beasts.  If we are able to intimidate their herd, the peikko will be so confused when the Gauredain come that they will lash out at them instead of us.

'Defeat the herd's elder, and her cries will surely disrupt the whole herd.''It would seem that for the time being, the peikko will let us go about our business.  Perhaps the Gauredain will not sleep so easily tonight.

'However, much work remains to be done.'Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel has requested that albino fur be delivered to her at the Elf-ruins in the southern reaches of Eregion.You have accepted a task to deliver albino fur from the beasts far to the north and east of Echad Mirobel to Maegamiel. Maegamiel defends Echad Mirobel in the southern area of Eregion.Wanted: Albino fur from the beasts far to the north and east of Echad Mirobel. Deliver the items to Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel in the southern section of Eregion.'We will make good use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann has requested that a collection of albino skin be delivered to her.You have accepted a task to deliver albino skin from the beasts found near Echad Dúnann to the Elf-maid Tinúriel. Tinúriel can be found in Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Albino skin taken from the beasts in the areas around Echad Dúnann. Deliver the items to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Albino skin taken from the beasts in the areas around Echad Dúnann.'We will make good use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of armoured carapaces at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver armoured carapaces from the insects near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Armoured carapaces from the insects surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Armoured carapaces from the insects surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'I will send this on to be studied by our scholars. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel has requested that broken wooden clubs be delivered to her at the Elf-ruins in the southern reaches of Eregion.You have accepted a task to deliver broken wooden clubs taken from the enemies of the Free Peoples to Maegamiel. Maegamiel defends Echad Mirobel in the southern area of Eregion.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the sentient enemies of the Free Peoples. Deliver the items to Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel in the southern section of Eregion.'The enemies of the Free Peoples must continue to fall, or all will be lost. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann has requested that a collection of jewelled sword sheaths be delivered to her.You have accepted a task to deliver jewelled sword sheaths from the Men and half-orcs found near Echad Dúnann to the Elf-maid Tinúriel. Tinúriel can be found in Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Jewelled sword sheaths from the Men and half-orcs in the areas around Echad Dúnann. Deliver the items to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Jewelled sword sheaths from the Men and half-orcs in the areas around Echad Dúnann.'It is good to know that there are fewer defilers upon our lands. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of monstrous goo at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver monstrous goo from the insects near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Monstrous goo from the insects surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Monstrous goo from the insects surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'I will send this on to be studied by our scholars. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of monstrous heartwood at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver monstrous heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Monstrous heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Monstrous heartwood from the twisted creatures of nature surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'I will send this on to be studied by our scholars. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maedlathron awaits the return of a collection of monstrous wings at the Elf-encampment at Gwingris.You have accepted a task to deliver monstrous wings from the birds near and surrounding the Elf-encampment of Gwingris to the Elf Maedlathron.Wanted: Monstrous wings from the birds surrounding the outpost of Gwingris. You can return the items to Maedlathron at Gwingris in Eregion for reward.Wanted: Monstrous wings from the birds surrounding the outpost of Gwingris.'This is a good thing you have done. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The communal larder of Sûri-kylä is running short, due to the troubled times in Forochel.You will find Olavi east and slightly south of Sûri-kylä at his fishing camp along the road to Itä-mâ.

Nôra has asked you to tell Olavi to bring him as many buckets of dried fish as he is able.Talk to OlaviThe fish Nôra requires can be found along the ice bay west of Olavi's camp.

Olavi has asked you to take a fishing-pole and collect the fish Nôra requires.Catch oily fish (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nôra is at Sûri-kylä.

You caught the fish where Olavi said you would. You should now bring the fish to Nôra in Sûri-kylä.'${RACE}, do you seek to be of help to the Lumi-väki? Then you will help me.

'Thanks to these trying times, our larders are nearly out of dried fish, and we need more. Will you go and speak with Olavi and have him bring me as many buckets as he is able?

'You will find Olavi east and slightly south of Sûri-kylä at his fishing camp along the road to Itä-mâ.''Have you brought my message to Olavi yet? He is located east and slightly south of Sûri-kylä at his fishing camp along the road to Itä-mâ.''Nôra sent you for more fish? That may be difficult.

'The shoreline has been held against me, both by the Rauta-väki and by the kantâ that have been driven into the area. I have been completely unable to fish, but you look like you have a steady hand and unyielding patience... perhaps you would go and fish for me?

'Take a fishing-pole and fish down by the waters along the ice bay west of here; that is where the particular fish Nôra is looking for usually run in the largest numbers.''Have you caught the fish yet? The only place you are likely to find that particular fish is west of here along the shore of the ice bay.''These fish will do nicely. Go and bring them to Nôra. She is at Sûri-kylä, north west of here. Bring the fish to her before they spoil!''The fish! I hope it was not too much trouble. I know how Olavi can be about his fish.'Ríki is worried about low supplies for his hungry miners.Elk can be found throughout Länsi-mâ.

Ríki has asked you to help restock Zigilgund's stores with Elk-meat.'Mining, and the other sort of work we do around here, makes for hungry dwarves, as you can well imagine.

'Our stores are running low, however, especially since one of our supply lines was attacked and looted. We are low on everything -- cloth, ale, tanned leather and hides, fastenings and armour trimmings...the worst, however, is that we're very low on meat.

'Elk-meat dries, smokes, freezes, and cures nicely, as well as making for juicy roasts. Bring me some back, if you can. You can find Elk throughout Länsi-mâ.''Excellent! I can practically taste the steaks now.

'We will put a few of these onto the fire right now -- no hungry dwarf will be able to resist that sizzle once they hear it! And the rest will contribute to travel rations and our stores.

'Well done, ${PLAYER}. You will be remembered with every full belly.'The Dwarves of Gabilshathûr are unable to go out and prospect in these dark days, but they know that the armies of the Enemy have discovered some of their old mining locations and are extracting gems themselves.Gem-bearing servants of the Enemy may be found in Maethad, the arena to the east of Gabilshathûr.

Bothwar has offered to pay you for any gems you can find on the servants of the Enemy.'Greetings, ${RACE}. I heard what you did for us. We are all at your service, I'm sure. Perhaps I can point you to a good thing in the way of thanks.

'Hiding out here, we can no longer go out prospecting or mining some of the outlying gem-bearing lodes we found. We know, though, that some of the minions of the Enemy -- trolls, gertheryg, and the like -- have found those old mining locations and are gathering gems themselves.

'If you wish, you could go out hunting the Enemy in Gorothlad, in the general vicinity of Maethad. Many of those evil folk are no doubt gathering what gems they can, and you may be able to retrieve some of them. I'll give you a good price if you could bring me some.''Ha! You have done very well to gather all these stones. Have no fear... I will give you a good price!'The Lossoth blame the harsh winter on the invading Angmarim and have asked for your help in driving them away.The Angmarim tents are at the encampment south and east of Sûri-kylä, near the Forodwaith Pass.

Pasi has asked you to return to the Angmarim encampment and burn down their tents with the kindling he gave you.Burn the tents of the Angmarim (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pasi is at Sûri-kyla, north and west of the Angmarim encampment.

You should bring news of your success to Pasi.'These Rauta-väki, they carry with them a cold that only our Elders have felt before; it chills the bones and strikes the hearts of even the bravest warriors.

'You have been to their camps; no doubt you have seen the tents that house their evil kindred. If the Rauta-väki have brought upon us an unnatural cold, they can sleep in it as well! Take this sytyke and burn their tents. Maybe we can force them to retreat to their lands if they feel the bitter cold of the northlands upon them. 

'Search for their tents in the camps of the Rauta-väki to the south and east of Sûri-kylä, near the Forodwaith pass.''How goes your work, ${PLAYER}? We must show the strength of the Lossoth to these invaders!

'Their camp is to the south and east, along the path to the land of Forodwaith. Search there for the tents of the Enemy.''You have done well! Let us see how long they will endure now that they must dwell in the snow.'Bothwar has told you the location of a cache of gems he was unable to retrieve before he was assigned his current duty in Gabilshathûr.The gem-hoard is near the cave-claw nests within Dolroth in southern Gorothlad, north and east of Gabilshathûr.

The dwarf-miner Bothwar has asked you to retrieve a hoard of gems for him.'You're a good sort, ${PLAYER}, and I feel that I can trust you not to betray my confidence in another matter.

'When I was called back to Gabilshathûr for guard-duty, I did not have time to recover a hoard of gems I had mined within the canyons of Gorothlad. I left it a bit near to the cave-claw nests in southern Gorothlad, and the filthy creatures likely have it surrounded.

'Can you recover the gem-hoard for me? It does the cave-claws no good to have it, their love for shiny things notwithstanding; the Dwarves of Gabilshathûr will be able to put the gems to good use.''Well done, ${PLAYER}! I have done more good for Gabilshathûr while on guard-duty than I ever did while mining: those gem-bearing rocks mined with all due haste, and a hoard of gems retaken from the cave-claws almost as quickly!

'You deserve all of the credit, of course. Do not let me understate your accomplishments! Take this reward -- it has been well-earned!'Mika recognizes the markings on the tusk as Angmarim sorcery in a Gauradan hand. He wishes you to find and defeat the Gauradan seer who dares try and twist one of the Lossoth's mammoths.Search around Norsu-hauta for the Gauradan seer who is trying to ensorcel a mammoth.Defeat a Gauradan seer who is trying to ensorcel a mammothReturn to Mika in Norsu-hauta and tell him of your success.'This is not good. This tusk was marked by the hand of one of the Susi-väki, but the words of power used are Angmarim. This tallies with what I know of a Susi-väki seer who has been seen nearby recently. A Man of Angmar must be trying to teach the Susi-väki how to ensorcel our Norsu!

'I have heard tales of the evil fetters Angmarim place upon their beasts, twisting them into fell creatures through time and despair. This must not be permitted in Länsi-mâ. Find and stop this seer!''This is no time for hesitation. Seek out the Susi-väki seer here in Norsu-hauta.''Your task was well performed, ${PLAYER}. However, it would seem that we yet have a significant Angmarim threat to suppress.'A Lossoth, Kaleva, at Sûri-kylä is collecting albino skin at the Lossoth capital.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver albino skin from the beasts and worms surrounding Sûri-kylä in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Albino skin from beasts and worms surrounding Sûri-kylä. Deliver the items to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Albino skin from beasts and worms surrounding Sûri-kylä.'We shall find many uses for these, of this I am certain. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.A Lossoth, Kaleva, at Sûri-kylä is collecting broken wooden clubs at the Lossoth capital.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver broken wooden clubs from the Angmarim invaders surrounding Sûri-kylä in Forochel. The items are to be delivered to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the invaders from the east surrounding Sûri-kylä. Deliver the items to Kaleva at the Lossoth capital.Wanted: Broken wooden clubs from the invaders from the east surrounding Sûri-kylä.'It is good to see that the invaders are being driven away. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Warstang, a burglar currently residing in Zigilgund, has lost his marbles -- they seem to have been scattered and eaten by various beasts -- and wants your help recovering them.You may find the beasts that ate Warstang's marbles scattered throughout Länsi-mâ.

Find Warstang's lost marbles in the bellies of the beasts who ate them and return them to him at the dwarf-outpost of Zigilgund.'Pardon me, my good ${RACE}, but can you help an Eglan far from his home out? I am having the worst luck! The bag what was holding my marbles split open, and by the time I noticed it, all the marbles were gone. 

'Then, even worse, is that I saw a beast out in the snows there gobble one up! And I do not think it was the only marble what was lost that way either.

'It is a bit disgusting, I would agree, but I need those marbles back. A burglar needs his whole bag of tricks! Bring me back my marbles -- you will have to dig through more than one kind of creature's stomach, I would wager.''Ah, thank goodness! I missed these things -- want to watch me juggle them? No? Ah well.

'Unfortunately, the missing marbles have not been the worst of my troubles....'Sigrík has let you borrow the tusk you found for him, with the mysterious markings on it. He suggested that a Lossoth näkijä's apprentice by the name of Mika, who lives in Norsu-hauta, might know what to make of them.Mika is at Norsu-hauta, south of Zigilgund.

Sigrík has asked you to speak with the young näkijä Mika about the markings on the mammoth tusk you collected for him.Talk to Mika'I must admit, I've no idea what these odd markings mean. But there is a young Lossoth, an apprentice seer of some sort, apparently -- näkijä is what they call them -- who might know something.

'Mika has been on some sort of quest down in that mammoth graveyard, Norsu-hauta, just south of here.

'Would you go there and ask him about the markings.''What do you bring me? Let me see. Where did you come by this tusk?''Go talk to the Lossoth-boy! I'm no help if you are curious about the markings on that tusk. Now, if you want to learn about spear-making, I'm your dwarf....'Keli has figured out why the snow-beasts have been so aggressive recently and wants you to kill their matron.The great snow-matron can be found in the cave east and up the hill from Zigilgund.

Keli has asked you to rid Forochel of the great snow-matron who has been stirring up the peikko.Defeat the great snow-matronKeli is at Zigilgund, west of the peikko-cave.

You should return to Keli with news of your victory of the great snow-matron.Return to Keli at Zigilgund'Well, my fine ${RACE}, it would appear that there is method behind the recent madness of the snow-beasts. The peikko have been stirred up, you see, by the return of the great snow-matron.

'I thought her existence was a bit of a legend, told to us by the Lossoth as a cautionary tale, but it turns out she is quite real. Since she has come back, it is all an honest dwarf can do to make his way through Länsi-ma without having an ice-boulder hurled at his head.

'Do us all a favour, ${PLAYER}: rid Itä-mâ of this threat! I believe she is within the same cave I told you about before: east and up the hill.''What are you doing here when the snow-matron is still living comfortably in that cave? East! Up the hill! Get yourself going.''It would seem there are more and more reasons to sing your praises, ${PLAYER}! You have done well by me and mine.'Great winter-worms have been interrupting the dwarves' efforts to get hot water from nearby springs. Weth is most annoyed.You have made a dent in the population of worms around the dwarf-channels Weth described to you. You should return to him at Zigilgund.Speak to Weth in Zigilgund'Will you help me? It's the most infuriating problem!

'We are trying to run water from the springs nearby to our encampment -- I want a hot bath! I don't think that's too much to ask, do you? Not in this unrelenting cold.

'Unfortunately, the great winter-worms have other ideas. Have you seen these creatures? Rather like furred drakelings, huge, heat-loving, cold-resistant, and very territorial. There are three water-channels in particular where they have disrupted our work. Please, in the name of steaming hot bathing-chambers everywhere, put a stop to this!

'You will find them north of here, where the water steams.''Right, I've had reports and figured out where the worst of the worm attacks are happening. They seem to be concentrated at three channels -- the fourth, seventh, and tenth.

'Ridding us of the big worms at those channels would be worthwhile indeed -- then maybe you can have a nice hot bath too!''Ha hah! That'll teach 'em!

'Delighted, my dear ${RACE}, simply delighted with you, I must say. I'm one step closer to a long, hot soak. But there appears to be a bit more to do....'Juhani, reported missing by his friend Tero back in Zigilgund, is trapped on the isle of Kissa-sari.Juhani, reported missing by his friend Tero back in Zigilgund, is trapped on the isle of Kissa-sari. He wishes your help in ensuring him safe passage.Defend JuhaniYou have escorted Juhani to safety. You should tell Tero that he is now safe.Talk to Tero in Zigilgund'Thank the Ice Spirits! Every time I have tried to escape on my own, I have been turned back by the long fangs of the kalpa-kita. Will you escort me back to the road?''Thank you! You have been of great aid to my friend.'Ilmo told you somewhat of one of the greater threats to his herd: the Peikkos.Peikkos can be found north of Ilmo, north of Sûri-kylä.

Ilmo has asked you to drive off the Peikkos which threaten his herd.Defeat Peikkos  (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ilmo is outside of Sûri-kylä, north from its gates.

You should return to Ilmo and tell him of your success against the Peikkos.Talk to Ilmo'I spoke to you of the Peikkos which threaten my herd, and I would not bring danger upon someone I know so little, but my concern is urgent.

'These creatures attack my camp in the night, and often I will awake finding one or two of my herd dragged off to some unknown den of the beasts. I can not afford to lose any more elk to these Peikkos.

'Many of these beasts can be found north of here. Go and thin their numbers, and perhaps they will leave me in peace.''The task is complete? That is well...I should not be troubled by the Peikkos any longer, though I fear that I have already taken a great loss.'According to Matti, the Kantâ-vilkku must be maintained with fuel and provisions for the Lossoth who keep watch over it.The Kantâ-vilkku is east and a little south of Sûri-kylä in Jä-rannit along the snow drifts over the bay of Forochel.

Matti has asked you to visit the Kantâ-vilkku and replace the missing supplies.The Kantâ-vilkku is east and a little south of Sûri-kylä in Jä-rannit along the snow drifts over the bay of Forochel.

Besides restocking the Kantâ-vilkku, Matti asked you to search near it for signs of what damaged the crates.Search for signs of what destroyed the cratesMatti is at Sûri-kylä, west and a little north of the Kantâ-vilkku.

You should return to Matti with your report.'You did us a great favour restoring the Kantâ-vilkku, but our work is still not complete. The Kantâ-vilkku has been running low on supplies; we keep dried meats and fresh water in the crates at the base for those who find themselves lost.

'Many lives have been saved with the provisions we keep there, but only last week one of our hunters found the crates battered and half empty. Please, take these supplies and refill the crates. While you are there, I would also ask that you search for signs as to what may have happened to the missing supplies.

'The Kantâ-vilkku can be found east of Sûri-kylä along the snow drifts above the ice bay of Forochel.''Strange markings and hoof-prints? The animals have never been so desperate as to break into our provisions before. The winds from Angmar have caused many strange and unnatural things this season.''Will you take those supplies to the Kantâ-vilkku?''Will you take those supplies to the Kantâ-vilkku?'On its way to the dwarf-outpost of Zigilgund, a convoy bearing vital supplies, especially mining picks, was attacked, and its contents scattered.Collect the dwarf-picks that were scattered when the supply convoy bound for Zigilgund was attacked. Thórth said they would likely be in and around the nearby mammoth graveyard.Collect dwarf-picks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have found the dwarf-picks that Thórth sent you to recover. You should return them to him at Zigilgund.'Greetings, ${RACE}. I am Thórth, the head of the expedition to the Icereave Mines, such as it is.

'We were expecting a convoy, but recently I received news that it was attacked. Bad enough that it was carrying foodstuffs and armourer's supplies: those can be acquired locally with only a little effort. But it was also carrying mining-picks, and those cannot so easily be replaced.

'Forge new ones? Do you have any idea of the work that goes into a superior dwarf-pick? To make something that can hew the hardest stone, and extract the most precious of metals therefrom? Bah! No, if we're to support ourselves by mining, we must have our picks back. They were scattered around Norsu-hauta, the mammoth graveyard just south of here. Retrieve them and you shall be rewarded.''Dourhands? Surely not!

'${PLAYER}, this is serious. Dourhands in this remote area! They will have to be rooted out, every dwarf Jack of them.

'At least you found the picks -- that's a job well done, and doubly valuable if you prevented them from falling into the hands of stinking thin-bearded Dourhands.'There are many villainous monsters roaming Forochel, and the Free Peoples must do their part to slay them.Slay many angmarim in ForochelSlay many dourhands in ForochelSlay many gauradan in ForochelSlay many grims in ForochelSlay many sabre-teeth in ForochelSlay many worms in ForochelImlad Balchorth is the dwelling place of a host of fell spirits, servants of Angmar.There are fell spirits in Imlad Balchorth, in the southern upper passes, south and west from Myrkworth. Gautar is in Myrkworth.

Gautar has asked you to confront the fell spirits to learn if there might be a way to banish them from Middle-earth.'Angmar was avoided by my people long ago, for many things dwelled here that are better left alone. When we thought them gone, we foolishly came seeking wealth in this barren land. Unfortunately, with the coming of the False King, those fell things have been awakened.

'Perhaps the worst of the creatures which dwell here are the fell spirits, demons which have existed in Middle-earth since the First Age of this world. At the height of the Witch-realm of Angmar, the Witch-king had power to command these spirits, and it is said there are other creatures which have a similar power. It was these spirits that the Witch-king sent to the Barrow-downs after the passing of Cardolan. It is these spirits who stir the bones of the dead.

'I bid you to go west into Imlad Balchorth and confront these demons. Claim the Tokens of Angmar they carry. Perhaps we can use the tools of the Enemy to banish the fell spirits from Middle-earth.''That the sorcerers of Angmar would dare to commune with these fell spirits speaks well enough to their evil. No Man, dwarf, or Elf, pure of heart, would deign to do such... it is an abomination.'The steam-lodge is an important part of the Lossoth culture, but they have nearly spent all of their heating rocks and have been unable to search for more.The rocks Kaleva requires can be found along the Ice Bay, east of Sûri-kylä along the shore.

Kaleva has asked you to collect some rocks for the steam-lodge.Collect bay stones (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Lossoth steam-lodge is in Sûri-kylä, north and east from the Ice Bay of Forochel.

Kaleva instructed you to place the bay stones on the mounds near the fire next to the Lossoth steam-lodge.Kaleva is in Sûri-kylä.

Now that you have placed the stones, you should speak with Kaleva.Talk to Kaleva'Greetings, ${RACE}. What brings you to Sûri-kylä? Usually travellers from the south are daunted by the cold weather, but that does not bother us... we are accustomed to such things.

'The steam-lodge is used by our people to relax and refresh themselves. We usually keep a supply of extra rocks to heat and make the steam, but we have been unable to search for them in many days because of the snowstorms.

'The rocks are found all along the Ice Bay, east of Sûri-kylä along the shore. Perhaps you would go and find some? Bring them to the fire near the hut.''Have you collected the rocks yet? We must have stones if we are to have a steam-lodge!''Place the stones next to the fire, ${PLAYER}.''Thank you for collecting the stones, ${PLAYER}. You have done a great service for Sûri-kylä and the Lumi-väki!'To the north-east of Gabilshathûr is a place named "Maethad". Here, trolls and their gorthorog-masters train to fight and lead Angmar's armies.The arena of Maethad is north and east of Gabilshathûr.

Hwati has asked you to find the arena and thin the numbers of gertheryg and trolls training within.Defeat gertheryg in Maethad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat trolls in Maethad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hwati is back at Gabilshathûr, west and south of Maethad.

Having defeated a number of gertheryg and trolls, you should return to Hwati.'Ah, welcome to you! It is good to have unexpected allies in our struggle to survive! It feels that -- after many years of hiding in the shadow of Angmar -- we can now finally strike back!

'In this matter, you may be able to help. To the north and east there is a place called "Maethad." It is a great arena in which those of troll-kind, especially the gertheryg, train for battle. Those who survive the arena go on to lead and train others of Angmar. Striking at this arena would deal a blow to Angmar's ability to raise and train its army!

'To end this threat, we must first defeat those who are training within the arena's walls, then we can move on and crush those who lead the training efforts. Go and reduce the number of the gertheryg and trolls within Maethad.''Maethad lies to the north and east. If we hold any hope of ending the threat of Maethad, we must first defeat some of the gertheryg and the trolls training within.''You have thinned their numbers? Good! Ah, a strong blow against Maethad! Now we can turn our attention to those who lead the training!'Fotri has agreed to give you directions to the famed Lossoth capital, Sûri-kylä.Make your way far to the east and north to Sûri-kylä, the City in the Ice.'You have helped everyone in Zigilgund by your actions, ${PLAYER}, directly and indirectly. You have earned our trust and our gratitude.

'You mentioned to me that you wish to continue to explore the icy wonders of Forochel. To that end, I recommend that you leave Länsi-ma altogether, and head north-east, to the Lossoth capital, Sûri-kylä. There you will find an old acquaintance of mine, a Ranger by the name of Lothrandir.

'It is a long journey. Follow the road east from here, through Itä-mâ, where the road will turn northwards. Once you are in Jä-rannit you will come to several forks in the road; always stay near the edge of the Bay. Curve up north and eventually west, following the norsu-tusks.

'I wish you well, ${PLAYER}. Good-bye and good luck, wherever you fare! Hopefully when you visit again, Zigilgund will once more have been made splendid, and the Icereave Mines will echo with the ringing of picks and toasts over ale!''Well? What are you waiting around here for?

'I know that the Lossoth are prickly folk, but surely you don't fear the welcome you will receive in Sûri-kylä that much!''Well met, friend. You have found your way to Sûri-kylä, the fabled Lossoth city in the ice. I understand that there are several folk in need of aid.

'For now, rest, eat, drink, be merry, and be welcome here.'Nef lost his shield while escaping from a dangerous creature in the tundras of Forochel.Nef thinks he dropped his shield in Jä-rannit along the road to Itä-mâ at a junction in the roads.

Nef has asked you to search for the shield he lost and return it to him.Find Nef's shieldNef is in Sûri-kylä, north from Itä-mâ along the Ice Bay.

You should return Nef's shield to him at once.'Greetings, ${CLASS}! What brings you to these cold lands? I can tell you are a stranger in these parts. Perhaps you could ask the same of me, though.

'I was travelling with some companions when one of the absent-minded fools got our company turned about. I spent days wandering the tundra and would have surely perished if I had not found one of those beacons the Lossoth placed at the tops of the hills.

'While I was lost I had the most terrible encounter with one of the local creatures. The beast leapt upon me with amazing speed. It took all my cunning and strength to get away from it! In my haste, however, I seem to have dropped my shield. Maybe you can aid me in recovering it? It is hard to say, but I think I dropped it along the road south of here along the path to Itä-mâ.''Have you recovered my shield? It should be somewhere south of Sûri-kylä along the road to Itä-mâ, though you should be wary of the fearsome beast that I escaped from.''Broken? This cannot be! My shield was the finest that gold could buy, according to that Bill Ferny fellow who sold it to me.'Ilmo is a herdsman and requires some supplies for which he has already traded to care for his herd.Ilmo has asked you to collect a saddle from Rauno, reins from Matti, and a salt lick from Kaleva within Sûri-kylä.Collect the reins from MattiCollect the salt lick from KalevaCollect the saddle from RaunoIlmo is outside of Sûri-kylä with his elk-herd.

You should bring the items you collected to Ilmo at once.'I am a herder by trade, a well respected profession among the Lossoth. We provide meat, hides, and quick transportation across the harsh snow-fields for those brave enough to ride on the hirvi.

'We brought this herd to Sûri-kylä for trade, but my boy had to return on an important errand to Pynti-peldot. I cannot enter into the village with the herd here as they are. Perhaps you could retrieve some items that I have traded for?

'I need a saddle from Rauno, reins from Matti, and a salt lick from Kaleva. Return these items to me, and I would be greatly in your debt.''Ilmo's reins? Yes, I have them here. Let me find them for you.''You are here for the salt lick then?

'Tell Ilmo that the last hirvi-steaks he delivered were very good!''Have you gathered my supplies, ${PLAYER}? I cannot continue southwards without them.''Ilmo has finally arrived with the herd then?

'His new saddle is on the ground next to me. You can bring it to him and make sure to thank him for bringing my daughter to Pynti-peldot.''You have my thanks, ${RACE}. Without you, the herd could have been scattered by the Peikkos that too often attack in the night.'Thórth suspects that whoever is leading the Dourhands at the Icereave Mines is hiding in a locked area within the mines.The Icereave Mines lie to the south-west of Zigilgund.

Thórth has asked you to drive the Dourhands from the Icereave Mines.Take a key from a Dourhand within the ancient Icereave MinesThórth is at Zigilgund.

You should return to Thórth with news of your victory.'Attacks from those thin-beards is no small matter, ${PLAYER}. They are organized -- they must have supplies, and a plan, and a leader to be this far north.

'I found some old maps of the Icereave Mines, and one of them shows a room inside. If I were the wagering sort, I would lay my money on those Dourhands planning to take over the mines, and that their leader would be claiming that particular area as his own.

'You will need to find a key. Perhaps one of the other Dourhands might have one?''You will need to find the key to the locked place in the mines, ${PLAYER}. I would guess one of the Dourhands might have one....''Excellent work! Let's take a look at it....

'Oh blast, the key is bent! Ah, well. Not to worry... I think I can fix it.

'Hold on a moment....'The root of the problem with the great winter-worms which have been attacking the dwarf-canals seems to lie with a particularly vicious worm dubbed Ringdagnir.Ringdagnir can be found on the westside of the hot springs near Zigilgund.

Weth has asked you to defeat the great winter-worm Ringdagnir.Defeat RingdagnirWeth can be found in Zigilgund, south of the thermal vents.

You have defeated the great worm Ringdagnir. Weth will be pleased with you.'It would appear our efforts have stirred Ringdagnir, a very large winter-worm. Until his attacks are curbed, there is no hope for my bath.

'Will you do this one more thing for me? Will you defeat Ringdagnir and end the threat of the winter-worms?

'You can find Ringdagnir on the westside of the hot springs near Zigilgund.''What are you doing here? Ringdagnir is not going to leave on his own, you know. Go down to where the water steams and drive him out!''Bless you, ${PLAYER}! Thanks to you, I have a fighting chance of finally soaking the cold out of my old bones and the grime from off my skin!

'To the bathing-chamber!

'Oh... and please, take this as a reward.'There are many old tales long forgotten in Angmar, but Nethraw is interested in only one.The lore-master Nethraw can be found at the Krúsle Lannan, a considerable distance to the east of Gabilshathûr.

Banfuir suggested you speak with the lore-master Nethraw.'Know you aught of the lore of Angmar?

'Well, no matter. You will learn... if Nethraw will have anything to say about it. He is in a lonely vale, the Krúsle Lannan, far to the east of here, musing on the Seven Sentinels.

'I have no doubt he could use your assistance.''Well met, stranger! I have tarried here long, seeking to unravel a mystery from times past. Perhaps you can help me? Banfuir at least believes you to have some skill, else he would not have sent you.

'Look upon the seven statues behind me; doubtless you note that they each are missing the sword which they once held. This place is one of ancient evil, something that I have long studied, and I know too well the scars that have been left in its wake.

'This loathsome place may be where the doom of the northern kingdoms was wrought, but I will speak no more of it until I have recovered all seven of the swords.'You have come with Nethraw into the heart of the Krúsle Lannan.Nethraw is at the Krúsle Lannan, east of Gabilshathur.

You must place the Seven Swords in their pedestals to open the Krúsle Lannan.Search the Krúslë Lannan with Nethraw.You must escape from the Krúslë Lannan with Nethraw.Escape from Krúslë Lannan'My study is almost complete, ${PLAYER}.'You have recovered the seven swords of the Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw is at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

Nethraw has asked you to gather allies and accompany him into the Krúsle Lannan.Nethraw is at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

Nethraw has asked you to accompany him into the Krúsle Lannan.Nethraw is at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should speak again with Nethraw.'You have recovered all seven of the swords; a mighty deed, ${PLAYER}! Now they can be restored to their proper resting place, and we can finally enter into Krúslë Lannan.

'Gather what few companions you deem trustworthy and return to me when you are ready. I would not think to enter into such a place without strong allies to guard me in my study.''You have returned, ${PLAYER}, then I deem that you are ready for the task at hand.

'Hold again the Seven Swords which you gathered. You should be the one to set them in their resting place and open the door.''Shall we begin placing the swords, ${PLAYER}?  

'It will only take me a moment to get my notes in order. Ready yourself, for I do not know what we will find inside.''Give the sword-bundle back to me, ${PLAYER}, quickly!

'I will dispose of these swords and lock the Oath-breakers that remain inside of Krúslë Lannan there forever.

'You have my thanks for your aid, ${PLAYER}, I have many notes to study. Please, take one of the items that I was able to recover from Krúslë Lannan in token of my gratitude.'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should take the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with writings in Black Speech. Among the dark writings, you can make out the name Abb.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'You have brought me the sword of Abb! Abb was a great leader of the Hillmen of Rhudaur. He was one of the last to be taken under the service of the Witch-king, and it is said that he had a great gift of healing.

'Ancient text tells that he was indeed unwilling at first to heed the words of the messengers of the Witch-king, but at the urging of his people for great wealth and power, he accepted the sword that you now hold.'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should take the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with writings in Black Speech. Among the dark writings, you can make out the name Ánraig. Though the blade looks strong, it has many notches as though it has seen many battles.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'Hail, ${PLAYER}; what have you brought to me? Ah, this is a relic, the notched sword of Ánraig!  

'Ánraig was a mighty warlord of the Hillmen of Rhudaur. He was the fiercest of those that were converted to the Enemy's evil cause and was often at the head of the armies brought against the people of Arthedain.'Nethraw told you a tale of Seven Sentinels who swore to hold vigil over Angmar to ensure that the evil never returned.The Sword of Arvedui is supposedly hidden somewhere around Gabilshathûr.

Nethraw has asked you to search Gabilshathûr for some evidence that the Sword of Arvedui was hidden there.Nethraw is back at the Fane of the Seven Sentinels east, of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the Sword of Arvedui to Nethraw.'Ah yes, where was I...? Well, the Seven Sentinels had sworn to keep a watch on Angmar, but there was no more kingdom of Arthedain to support them. The Seven maintained their vigil and upheld their oaths, but after many years, the six Men died. They were not replaced, as both Gondor and the remaining Dúnedain of Arnor had forgotten them.

'In sorrow, the Elf, whose name has been lost, made a vault in the knights' honour, and then he himself sailed west across the Sea. This is the vault of the Seven Sentinels, and an old tale tells that ancient treasure, imbued with a virtue for fighting the evil of Angmar, is buried here. Yet the keys to opening it are missing. The tale says that the vault can only be opened when the seven swords of the Seven Sentinels are once again raised, but the swords of those great heroes are long lost.

'Still, I have some clues. The first sword is called the Sword of Arvedui. It was borne by a knight who served Arvedui's son Aranarth, but who took up his vigil here rather than follow the other Dúnedain into obscurity. I believe that the sword is hidden somewhere around the dwarves' outpost of Gabilshathûr, which once was the site of the knight's watch-tower. The sword may still be hidden there, someplace.''I am afraid I have let my enthusiasm for the old tales get the better of me. 

'I should probably not have asked you to search for the sword, considering it is likely that either the sword was lost long ago, or else that the old tale is nothing more than a fable.''Remarkable! So the tales are true! If this one sword has been found, perhaps the other six will reveal themselves as well! Perhaps the vault can be opened, and it may even hold some treasure that can be of use to us here!

'But I have raced ahead of myself. Recovering the other six swords will be no easy task. Indeed, it may well be an impossible quest. But we shall see....'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should take the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with writings in Black Speech. Among the dark writings, you can make out the name Guinokh.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'What have you brought to old Nethraw?  

'The sword of Guinokh? This is the sister-sword of the poisoner, Kolmak; many a wound this sword gave that too late was found to be fatal. Guinokh was longest in the service of the Enemy and had the most malice and subtlety of those that wielded a blade under the Witch-king against Cardolan.'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should take the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with faint writings in Black Speech. Among the faded writings, you can make out the name Innis.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'It is well that you brought this to me, ${PLAYER}, for few save the Wise now would have been able to discern the faded lines that speak the cold words of the Black Speech which tainted this blade.  

'This is the sword of Innis, though his sword was not as famed as his bow.  Many were caught unaware by the ambushes of Innis and the cruel Hillmen he commanded.'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with writings in Black Speech. Among the dark writings, you can make out the name Kevoka.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'Hail, ${PLAYER}, it seems that you have brought me one of the swords that I desire!

'This is the sword of Kevoka. Long, he battled against the Witch-king, and he feigned peace and offered to him a truce. In his folly, he met at the appointed place alone and was captured. 

'When he returned to his people he was barely recognizable, a shadow of his former self. He served the Witch-king, and his people were taken into thralldom.'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should take the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with writings in Black Speech. Among the dark writings, you can make out the name Kolmak. The blade's edge seems to be pitted and burned.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'This is a dark blade, ${PLAYER}; look along its edges...do you see the burn marks and the notches? Many times has this sword carried the fell poisons of the Enemy.

'I fear that this is the sword of Kolmak. He was the second of the Hillman-chieftains who took service with the Enemy. It troubles me to know that his blade was still in the service of Angmar.'You have recovered one of the seven swords of Krúslë Lannan, you should return it to Nethraw.Nethraw can be found at the Krúslë Lannan, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should take the sword you found to Nethraw.The sword-hilt appears to be of Westernesse-make, but the blade has been overlaid with writings in Black Speech. Among the dark writings, you can make out the name Mánas.

You should take the blade to Nethraw at the Krúslë Lannan to the east of Gabilshathûr.'Hail, ${PLAYER}, have you returned one of the swords that were taken from Krúslë Lannan?

'You have brought to me the sword of Mánas. Many brave warriors fell beneath this blade, though he wielded the bow stronger. Few men lived to tell tales of his brutality, for he would often put his captives to a cruel death.'You recovered the Sword of Arvedui, leading Nethraw to wonder if the other swords might not be recovered as well.The Sword of Rhudaur may be found at one of the fortified Orc-camps north of Gabilshathûr.

Nethraw has now asked you to search for the Sword of Rhudaur.Nethraw is back at the Fane of the Seven Sentinels, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the Sword of Rhudaur to Nethraw.'As I told you before, the other six swords will be much harder to recover, if indeed they still can be found at all.

'The second sword is called the Sword of Rhudaur. Evidently the Dúnedain knight who bore it was a descendant of those who fled the fall of Rhudaur long before and carried a sword from the days when Rhudaur was a kingdom of Arnor.

'According to the old tale, this knight's watch-tower seems to have once been found in the same location as one of the large Orc-camps north of Gabilshathûr. I know not which camp is the right one. But even if the sword can still be found, perhaps under such a marker as you found at Gabilshathûr, it will be no easy task to brave all those Orcs.''I hope the Sword of Rhudaur is still there to be found. For all we know, it may long since have been looted by Orcs.''The Sword of Rhudaur!

'I am greatly heartened by your success! If the first two swords can be found where the tale tells, perhaps the remaining five will be found as well. 

'The difficulty of collecting the swords grows. Make no doubt, I have asked you to find the easiest two swords first.'The Lossoth blame the harsh winter on the invading Angmarim and have asked for your help in driving them away.The camps of the Angmarim are south and east of Sûri-kylä, near the Pass of Forodwaith.

Pasi has asked you to help defeat the invading Angmarim.Collect Angmarim weapons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Pasi is at Sûri-kylä, north and west of the Angmarim encampment.

You should return at once to Pasi and show him the weapons of the Angmarim.'Hail, ${RACE}! You have come far in your travels... I can tell by your strange clothes but also the look in your eye; it is that of a true warrior!

'We have had many troubles of late -- more than the Elders would dare speak about openly -- I myself have seen the Rauta-väki from Angmar with their dark weapons, and they seek to bring war upon my people. I will not stand for it while I still draw breath!

'If you would give the warriors of Sûri-kylä aid we would be greatly in your debt. Seek out the threat from the South and aid us in crushing it! The Rauta-väki can be found in their camp along the path to the lands called Forodwaith, south and east of Sûri-kylä. Bring me their weapons as proof of your deeds.''How goes your task? Have you brought me the weapons?

'The camp of the Rauta-väki lies to the south and east of Sûri-kylä along the road to the land of Forodwaith.''Impressive. You must be a mighty warrior of your people, ${PLAYER}. Come, we have more to do.'After becoming separated from the rest of her fellowship by a storm, Burgthryth was pursued through the wilderness by a variety of hostile beasts. During the course of her flight, she lost some of her most prized sheets of music and despairs of ever finding them again.Burgthryth believes the first of her sheets was swiped from her pack by a Tundra Bear. You will have to hunt them down until you find the one that stole it -- assuming it has not already been devoured.

Tundra Bears can be found in Länsi-mâ.Find Burgthryth's first sheetBurgthryth told you that the second of her anthem sheets might have been grabbed by a curious peikko during her performance. You will have to hunt down the peikko that stole it.

Many Peikko may be found in a small cave due east of Zigilgund.Find Burgthryth's second sheetBurgthryth thinks she probably dropped the third sheet of her anthem when she was being pursued by Gauradan-hunters. You will have to hunt down the Gauredain and retrieve that sheet before they use it as a hankerchief -- or worse.

Gauredain can be found in Länsi-mâ.Find Burgthryth's third sheetBurgthryth is at Jänis-leiri.

You finally found all the sheets of Burgthryth's scattered anthem. You will need to return them to her at once.'Ah, a face from civilized lands at last! Perhaps you can help me? Our fellowship came here to Forochel a few days ago, but through folly and mishap we became separated. I have spent the last two days fleeing through this trackless wilderness trying to escape from wild beasts and wild men alike, until finally I found some refuge here.

'Imagine my dismay when I discovered that several sheets of my most prized anthem had been lost during my flight! I need to get those sheets back! Thinking back through my misadventures of the last few days, I think I know where they might have been lost. You see, there was this enormous bear, which tried to steal the rations from my pack before I managed to calm the creature with my songs and slip away -- only to stumble into a band of those horrid snow-beasts or whatever they are -- I think the locals call them peikko? In any case, I had nearly beguiled the lot of them with my finest performance yet, when a Gauradan-hunter snuck up and nearly put a spear through me!

'Needless to say, those wild men are poor patrons of the arts at best, and I took my cue to flee into the night. It was not until I later found a chance to collect my wits that I realized my prized anthem was gone! Will you please hunt down those horrid beasts and retrieve my music? I may be able to make it worth your while.''You found them? You found them all! Oh thank you so much!

'This is one of the greatest anthems of our age -- er, that is, it will be once I have finished it. You will come by to hear my performance when it is ready, won't you? I should be able to finish it once I find the rest of my fellowship, and we get out of this forsaken wilderness.

'There is just one more little problem though....'Avar, the leader of the dwarf-outpost at Gabilshathûr, told you of their desperate situation. The Orcs have discovered their hidden holding and are felling trees to the north. Avar fears they are taking the trees to construct siege-weapons, with which they can assault and destroy the outpost.The Orc-camp of Ongbishúk lies to the north-east of Gabilshathûr.

Avar asked you to take the tools of the Orcs to help slow down their efforts to construct siege-weapons.'We are fortunate that you arrived when you did. We have hidden in the shadow of Angmar for so long, but now we have been discovered. An Orc-scout stumbled into our halls a few days ago and escaped before we could slay him. Now the Orcs attack us regularly, and it is only going to get worse, if what I suspect is true.

'One of my dwarves returned and told us that the Orcs are felling the trees north of here, and that can only mean one thing: they are making siege-weapons with which to attack us. We have to slow down or halt their work!

'If you wish to help us, go to Ongbishúk, north of here and defeat the Orcs you find there. Gather their tools, such as axes, picks, and saws, and bring them here.''Good work, ${CLASS}. You have done well. I will see that these tools are melted down. At least their metal will serve us well.

'Unfortunately, it appears I was too late in sending you there to frustrate the Orcs' plans. Sadly, I have learned that they were further along than I thought!'The great white bears of Länsi-mâ often scavenge among the remains of mammoths, which can drive the herds away.Tundra bears can be found throughout Länsi-mâ.

Mika has asked you to drive off a number of tundra bears.Defeat tundra bears in Länsi-mâ (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mika is at Norsu-hauta.

Now that you have defeated many tundra bears, you should return to Mika.Talk to Mika'You have by now likely run into some of the great white kantâ that roam this area... or if you have not, you will soon. Majestic beasts, are they not? Ill-tempered, too.

'One thing that many do not know about them is that they are sometimes scavengers, when they cannot find fresh kills or are too lazy to hunt. They will eat carrion and gnaw on bones to crack them open for the marrow. Their favourite treat is norsu bone-marrow, and a norsu-corpse after a small family of white kantâ has been at it is far from a pretty sight.

'It upsets the norsu and makes them more likely to move the whole herd further away. We cannot afford to lose such a huge source of meat, bone, and fur! Drive off some of the kantâ... there is only so much competition we can afford.''The kantâ are found all over Länsi-ma, especially in areas containing norsu. Look nearby as well as far off!''Good. You have done well, ${PLAYER}. Kantâ that size are not easy prey, as I well know, but you have helped safe-guard the norsu-herds of Länsi-mâ for a little longer.

'Please, take this reward for your efforts.'A terrible wind has come from Angmar, bringing with it a freezing winter.Peikko can be found north of Sûri-kylä.

Nôra has asked you to collect Peikko hides to help supplement the bear-furs you collected for her already.Collect Peikko hides  (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nôra is at Sûri-kylä, south from where you found the Peikko.

You should return to Nôra with the hides you collected.'Have you seen the hunters and warriors of our people? They survive for long stretches out in the wilderness because of the warm clothing that they wear.

'One of the sturdiest hides is that of the Peikko. Their hides are warm and provide protection from wind, water, and ice. If we are to have a warmer winter, then we must have more Peikko hides!

'The Peikko often live north of Sûri-kylä. Bring me their hides, and I will find you a suitable reward.''Have you found Peikko yet? They can be found north of Sûri-kylä.''These are excellent hides, ${PLAYER}. I am pleased that you were able to obtain them with such ease.'Sigrík lost his spear while hunting mammoths.The mammoth should be found somewhere south of Zigilgund.

Sigrík has asked you to find his missing spear, which should be stuck in the corpse of a mammoth. He asked you to collect one of its tusks as well.Find the mammoth that was speared by SigríkSigrík is at Zigilgund, north of the mammoth's corpse.

You should return the spear and tusk to Sigrík.'I lost my spear in the side of a mammoth! Can you believe it?

'I was hunting nearby -- just south of here -- and saw a beauty of a beast, with a pair of amazing tusks, and I thought one of them would make a fine trophy for over my mantel-piece back home!

'It was a fine, straight cast, but the stupid thing just ran off with my spear sticking out of its flank, and me left behind feeling a fool. Will you look for it? I would be surprised if the beast made it very far. If you find its corpse, bring back one of its tusks, too!''It's me spear, and one of that Mammoth's tusks, that I'm wantin'! Look in the place they go to die, south of here.''My spear! I swear no other flies as true! I would have been terribly upset never to see it again. Let's take a look at that tusk, now.

'Ah, it's a beauty, isn't it? Look at the... now wait a minute. Look at this! There are markings on this tusk! I cannot make head nor tail of them, though.

'Never mind! It will still make a fine trophy.'Warstang, the Eglan-burglar staying with the dwarves at Zigilgund, has had his belt-pouch burgled from him.The Gauradan thieves can be found south-east of Zigilgund.

Warstang has asked you to recover his stolen belt-pouch from the Gauredain.Reclaim Warstang's pouch from a Gauradan thiefWarstang is currently residing in Zigilgund.

You should return the belt-pouch to Warstang.'I do not believe this, and it is a bit embarrasing, honestly, but... someone has gone and stolen my belt-pouch! A burglar burgled -- I shall not soon live this down.

'If I had to guess, I would put the money I do not have anymore on the thief being a Gauradan. There are not many beasts around here with thumbs to unhook a pouch. I wonder if they train up to burgle, too...?

'But look here, I need my pouch! Will you help me again? You can find the Gauradan thieves out in the wastes to the south-east of Zigilgund.''Have you found my pouch yet? No? Well, keep looking, then! The Gauredain are found to the south-east of here.''Wait, my pouch is here. So what is hiding in this pouch...?

'Oh dear, this is not mine: it is the Guaradan thief's pouch! I had best not look inside it. I really do not wish to know what a Guaradan keeps in his pouch.

'Thank you for your efforts anyway, ${PLAYER}! The least I can do is give you a little from my own pouch....'Defeat wights in Angmar.Defeat wights in AngmarDefeat many wights in Angmar.Defeat wights in AngmarBothwar of Gabilshathûr is aggrieved at missing an opportunity for mining some quality gems.Gem-bearing rocks of the sort Bothwar described can be found outside the entrance to Maethad. He told you to follow the road north from Gabilshathûr until you could turn east and pass through the sparse trees of Gorothlad.

Bothwar is annoyed that he was pulled from his mining efforts to undertake guard-duty and has asked you to finish his mining.Mine gem-bearing rocks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bothwar is on the bridge in the middle of Gabilshathûr, west and south of Gorothlad.

You have mined the gem-bearing rocks of which Bothwar spoke and should return to him with the fruits of your labour.'I am always glad to do my share of the work here at Gabilshathûr, as long as every other dwarf here pulls his own weight. It can be frustrating to be chosen for guard-duty when you have found a promising lode, but that is sometimes the way of it.

'If you don't mind helping out an eager miner, you could mine the gems from the rocks I have had to leave behind. You would be doing me a great service, and the wealth of Gabilshathûr will only be improved by your assistance!

'The rocks in question are north of Gabilshathûr. Follow the road northwards until you can turn east into Gorothlad. Through the sparse trees, you will come to the entrance of Maethad. Cave-claws often gather in the rocks outside Maethad, so use care while mining the gems!''You will find the gem-bearing rocks I had to leave unmined if you follow the road north from Gabilshathûr until you can turn east and pass through the sparse trees of Gorothlad. The stones are by the entrance to Maethad. Mine the gems from the stones!''You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}! I would rather mine, but I am a dwarf who knows his duty and would never gainsay my betters!

'So it is only right that we have mined those gem-bearing rocks after all...everyone is the victor! Take this as a reward with my good regards.'The Lossoth blame the harsh winter on the invading Angmarim and have asked for your help in driving them away.Ruaraikh can be found in the Angmarim encampments, south and east of Sûri-kylä, near the Ironspan in the Forodwaith pass.

Pasi has asked you to defeat Ruaraikh and throw the Angmarim into disarray.Defeat RuaraikhPasi can be found in Sûri-kylä, north and west of the Ironspan.

You should return to Pasi with news of your victory.Talk to Pasi'Our work has not gone unnoticed by the Enemy. They are assaulting all travellers along the roads. Some have gone missing, and others have seen strange shapes and heard dark voices on the wind at night.

'The Rauta-väki are being led by a mighty war-leader, but he does not make camp with his servants. Even the mightiest warrior cannot withstand the Lossoth single-handedly; we must strike at the heart of the camps and scatter them to the northern winds!

'Our scouts have learned that the Rauta-väki at the encampment are led by Ruaraikh. If he is slain, the camps will be thrown into chaos! Please, ${PLAYER}, seek out and slay Ruaraikh. He will most likely be found deep within the camp south and east of here, near to the Ironspan in the pass of Forodwaith.''You have accomplished a feat mighty even among the warriors of the Lossoth! You have our thanks, ${PLAYER}.''Have you slain Ruaraikh yet, ${PLAYER}? His death would be a great blow against the Rauta-väki.

'Ruaraikh can be found deep within the camp south and east of Sûri-kylä, near the Ironspan in the pass of Forodwaith.'Kol told you that the norboglir-legs and slug-tongues you brought back were disgusting...far too disgusting for even a desperate dwarf to eat.There are drake nests far to the east of Gabilshathûr in the valley of Dolroth.

Kol has asked you to collect drake-eggs to supplement the inedible provender you already provided.Gather drake-eggs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Kol is at Gabilshathûr, far to the east of the drake-nests.

You have counted a fair number of drake-nests and should now return to Kol with the information you have gathered.'Don't think that I do not appreciate what you have done, but these legs and tongues are horrible! Any reasonable dwarf would rather starve than eat food such as this! Bother it all! If only we could make it taste less vile.

'I hesitate to mention this, but I have heard that drake eggs are delicious. Aye, I know it sounds strange, but we are in some dire straits here.

'Far to the east, there is a valley called Dolroth which is full of drakes. If you could gather friends and raid their nests for eggs, perhaps I will have a meal that a dwarf would actually eat!''The drake nests are found to the east of Gabilshathûr, in a valley called Dolroth. Although the task will not be easy, I fear a dwarf would rather die than eat those legs and tongues. Adding those eggs may make it a palatable meal!''Ah, so you have the eggs...well give them here. I will start making a meal from what you have brought back.

'I must say, those eggs don't look like much, but maybe they will make the difference, and I will be able to make a meal a dwarf would not turn his nose up at.

'Although, I have to admit, I would just be happy if they ate it at all! A meal such as this will at least keep their strength up. They may not like it, and their stomachs may complain, but maybe it'll help us survive just long enough to get some real food!'Something foul twists the heart of the holly-forest south of Gwingris, corrupting the creatures which once dwelt there in peace.

You have been tasked with removing those holly-tenders and trees that have succumbed to the shadow and now attack those who pass through.Huorns, the great trees that move about freely, and holly-tenders can be found throughout Glâd Ereg.

Lithuilas has asked you to defeat the Huorns and holly-tenders which have been stirred to wrath. He recommends you take friends with you.Defeat fell holly-tenders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Huorns (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lithuilas is at Gwingris, north of Glâd Ereg.

Lithuilas will want to know of your success.Talk to Lithuilas at Gwingris'Greetings, ${PLAYER}. Have you of late seen the holly-forest south of Gwingris? Huorns now walk about, attacking those who pass through the wood instead of remaining docile trees as they should.

'There is something amiss in the wood. Something has awakened the trees from their usual slumber and provokes them. The Huorns and holly-tenders are proving too great a threat so close to our way-post.

'Gather your friends, ${PLAYER}, and deal with this menace. Return to me when you are through, and perhaps we can learn what has stirred them.''Return to me when you have dealt with the Huorns and holly-tenders in Glâd Ereg to the south.''It pains me to put these creatures of the forest to the sword, but the power of the Enemy has made it necessary. Against this corruption, there is little defence.

'It seems that our immediate concerns have been dealt with... let us hope that it stays that way. I fear there is more lurking beneath the surface, however.'You have found a strange axe on one of the Dunlendings near Gwingris, but you do not recognize the craftsmanship.Gwingris is at the northern end of Glâd Ereg.

You should bring the axe to the Elves in Gwingris.This axe was carried by a Dunlending, but you see markings that are clearly not of Dunlending-make. While you cannot tell the origin of the craftsmanship, perhaps one of the Elves in Gwingris will know more.'This is strange. You say this was carried by one of the Dunlendings nearby?

'This axe is of a quality not normally known to these brigands and bodes ill. It seems that what would normally be an independent force has organized under someone else's banner.'Spalvi has heard that an expedition of dwarves plans to enter the ancient halls of Khazad-dûm, and thinks you should try to follow them in case Bingo has ended up inside that kingdom.Glóin is at his camp in the Western Bruinen Source area of the Misty Mountains.

Spalvi has asked you to learn what you can from Glóin about the expedition to Khazad-dûm.Talk to Glóin at his camp in the Western Bruinen Source area of the Misty MountainsGlóin has told you of the Iron Garrison that seeks to gain entrance to Khazad-dûm, and now you should speak with Spalvi just outside the camp to form a plan.Talk to Spalvi outside Glóin's camp in the Western Bruinen Source area in the Misty MountainsSpalvi has agreed to travel with you to Gwingris, the ruins in the north-west corner of Eregion, on the next leg of your journey to find Bingo Boffin.Talk to Spalvi at Gwingris, in EregionBog-lurkers and Dunlendings can be found throughout Eregion, and they threaten the peace of the area.Defeat bog-lurkers in Eregion (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Dunlendings in Eregion (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have made the wilds of Eregion more safe for the Elves at Gwingris, and should return to Spalvi with news of your success.Talk to Spalvi in GwingrisHaley Meadowsweet has come to the ruins of Gwingris, in north-western Eregion, and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Haley Meadowsweet in GwingrisThe ruins of Pembar are to the south-east of Gwingris, in Eregion.

Haley Meadowsweet sent Theodore Gorse to Pembar to search for treasure (and learn something useful about treasure-seeking), but he has not returned and she fears he may have run afoul of the half-orcs there... or he may have not found Pembar, or he might have gotten lost, or he might have fallen asleep in the brush, or he might have mistaken something for treasure...

But if the cause of his delay is indeed half-orc related, she'd like you to handle it.Defeat half-orcs in or very near to the ruins of Pembar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Theodore Gorse is hiding from half-orcs in the middle of the ruins of Pembar, in Eregion.Talk to Theodore Gorse in the middle of the ruins of PembarHaley Meadowsweet is at Gwingris, in north-western Eregion.Spalvi is at Gwingris, in north-western Eregion, and is waiting to speak to you.Talk to Spalvi in Gwingris'Skorgrím set many plans in motion, my recent friend, and each one required watchful eyes and alert ears. As a result, I have heard a great deal about what transpires in and around these mountains. One such tale might be of use to us now.

'It is said that a group of dwarves from the Iron Hills has come this way, and seeks to enter the halls of Khazad-dûm. If we were to follow in their footsteps, they might open the way for us to continue our search for Bingo!

'I do not wish to speak to the Longbeard who runs this camp, for fear of betraying my heritage, but you should talk to him and see what you can learn of this expedition to Khazad-dûm.''It is said that a group of dwarves from the Iron Hills has come this way, and seeks to enter the halls of Khazad-dûm. If we were to follow in their footsteps, they might open the way for us to continue our search for Bingo!

'I do not wish to speak to the Longbeard who runs this camp, for fear of betraying my heritage, but you should talk to him and see what you can learn of this expedition to Khazad-dûm.''Did you teach those no-good Dourhands a lesson, ${PLAYER}?'

You give Glóin a brief account of your adventure in Gabilazan, choosing to leave out Spalvi's role in the escape. When you tell Glóin of Bingo's fall into the cave system, he shakes his head and his beard is ruffled by sighs.

'If he were a dwarf, I'd say he might even relish the chance to explore such a network of passages, but I am afraid a hobbit would not have much of a chance in those caves. Even our old burglar Bilbo Baggins much preferred to be out in the sunlight, and he was as near a dwarf as you could wish!

'You think your friend Bingo might have been washed by an underground river south to Khazad-dûm? Well, that is quite a long way, but if you seek to enter those halls you will be in good company. An expedition has just passed this way, on their way to the land of the holly-trees. If you wish to follow them into Khazad-dûm, you will need to find Bósi or his cousin Brogur. They lead the Iron Garrison.''You think your friend Bingo might have been washed by an underground river south to Khazad-dûm? Well, that is quite a long way, but if you seek to enter those halls you will be in good company. An expedition has just passed this way, on their way to the land of the holly-trees. If you wish to follow them into Khazad-dûm, you will need to find Bósi or his cousin Brogur. They lead the Iron Garrison.''The Iron Garrison, eh? Sounds to me like a lot of guards and miners. I wonder if they know what will await them inside the halls of Khazad-dûm?'

Spalvi laughs dryly.

'Perhaps they will open the doors to Khazad-dûm and Bingo will be sitting there, sopping wet, to greet them! Wouldn't that be something, ${PLAYER}?

'We have a long way to go if we are going to reach the doors to Khazad-dûm. That Longbeard spoke of 'the land of the holly-trees?' That is the land of Eregion, and while I have never seen it, I understand it to be a land of rolling hills and old ruins. We should travel to one of the ruins in the north and learn what we can do to follow the Iron Garrison.''We have a long way to go if we are going to reach the doors to Khazad-dûm. That Longbeard spoke of 'the land of the holly-trees?' That is the land of Eregion, and while I have never seen it, I understand it to be a land of rolling hills and old ruins. We should travel to one of the ruins in the north and learn what we can do to follow the Iron Garrison.''I have never been to this land before, ${PLAYER}, but I must say my first impression is not a positive one. There are so many Elves here! Weren't they leaving Middle-earth? What is taking them so long?'

Spalvi grumbles under his breath for a few moments.

'I know we are in a hurry to catch up to the Iron Garrison and see if Bingo can be found within Khazad-dûm, but I want to make a statement to some of these Elves. In between sideways glances and muttered insults cast in my direction, I have heard some of the Elves complaining about bog-lurkers and Dunlending warriors threatening the peace of this land of holly-trees.

'Let us fight these threats and prove to these Elves that we are to be taken seriously!''I know we are in a hurry to catch up to the Iron Garrison and see if Bingo can be found within Khazad-dûm, but I want to make a statement to some of these Elves. In between sideways glances and muttered insults cast in my direction, I have heard some of the Elves complaining about bog-lurkers and Dunlending warriors threatening the peace of this land of holly-trees.

'Let us fight these threats and prove to these Elves that we are to be taken seriously!''You did as I asked? Good, good! Those Elves will know we are not to be underestimated!

'While you were away, I went looking for someone I might speak with who would not sniff with distaste and treat me as a blight on the day. Just when I despaired of finding anyone but Elves, I met a lovely adventurer. And will you believe it? She says she met you some time ago!

'Her name is Haley Meadowsweet, and she is right over there. She spoke quite highly of you, ${PLAYER}, and I was pleased to say that you are my travelling companion in Eregion.''While you were away, I went looking for someone I might speak with who would not sniff with distaste and treat me as a blight on the day. Just when I despaired of finding anyone but Elves, I met a lovely adventurer. And will you believe it? She says she met you some time ago!

'Her name is Haley Meadowsweet, and she is right over there. She spoke quite highly of you, ${PLAYER}, and I was pleased to say that you are my travelling companion in Eregion.''Well, hello there, ${PLAYER}! I see that you are still at it, wandering the wilds in search of adventure! I met your dwarf-friend, Spalvi, but I do not see your companion Bingo anywhere. Does he still travel with you? He was a pleasant little fellow!'

You tell Haley that you have become separated from Bingo, but that you hope to find him again soon.

'Oh, I see. Well, good luck to you! I am sure he will turn up soon. My own companion has proven less adept at treasure-seeking than I expected, and he too has gone missing! I swear, I thought Theodore would be a better student than this!

'The two of us travelled here to Eregion because I thought the Elf-ruins were certain to bear some lost treasures, and might be used as a source of useful instruction, but Theodore seems quite unable to find or learn a single thing of value! I sent him south to Pembar to search there, but he has not yet returned. I wonder if he was even able to find Pembar in the first place?

'If you do not mind, could you go south to Pembar and scour the ruins for my student Theodore? It is possible he ran afoul of the half-orcs that have taken the place, but more likely he is simply failing to find any treasure at all.''Theodore and I travelled here to Eregion because I thought the Elf-ruins were certain to bear some lost treasures, and might be used as a source of useful instruction, but Theodore seems quite unable to find or learn a single thing of value! I sent him south to Pembar to search there, but he has not yet returned. I wonder if he was even able to find Pembar in the first place?

'If you do not mind, could you go south to Pembar and scour the ruins for my student Theodore? It is possible he ran afoul of the half-orcs that have taken the place, but more likely he is simply failing to find any treasure at all.''Theodore and I travelled here to Eregion because I thought the Elf-ruins were certain to bear some lost treasures, and might be used as a source of useful instruction, but Theodore seems quite unable to find or learn a single thing of value! I sent him south to Pembar to search there, but he has not yet returned. I wonder if he was even able to find Pembar in the first place?

'If you do not mind, could you go south to Pembar and scour the ruins for my student Theodore? It is possible he ran afoul of the half-orcs that have taken the place, but more likely he is simply failing to find any treasure at all.''${PLAYER}! Have you come to this land seeking treasure? Sadly, I am afraid there will be little for you to find in these ruins, for I have gotten here first, and their treasures are now mine! I have impressed Haley with my treasure-seeking skills already. Are you familiar with that old saying, 'The student has become the teacher?' Well, I believe that it will be true for me and for Haley any day now. Just wait and see!'

Theodore brandishes a small leather bag at you.

'Do you see this? I found it near a fallen half-orc, and I believe it to be his greatest treasure. Let us see, and remember that I found it first!'

Theodore opens the bag, fishes around inside, and then hands it to you with a shrug.

'Just a bunch of iron disks. Some of them have little white smudges painted on them, but I wouldn't consider this much of a treasure. If you'll bring the bag back to Haley, maybe she'll find some value in it. I'll stay here and look around some more, I suppose. I know there will be something worth seeking here! How could there not be?''If you'll bring that bag back to Haley, maybe she'll find some value in it. I'll stay here and look around some more, I suppose. I know there will be something worth seeking here! How could there not be?''Did you find this lovely woman's student? I trust he was not injured?'You hand the small bag of iron disks to Haley, and tell her that Theodore is safe.

'Well, that is something, I suppose. I cannot imagine he was satisfied with this bag of trinkets, but who can say what has value and what does not? To my eye, these disks bear the mark of a White Hand, and that is a symbol I have seen on many of the foes I have encountered in these lands.

'If Theodore thinks all treasure comes in strongboxes, I may have to rethink my lesson plans and return to the very beginning!'

Haley's laughter echoes in the silence, and trails away. Her smile fades, replaced by a worried expression.

'You do not think he really believes that, do you? Oh my.''I cannot imagine Theodore was satisfied with this bag of trinkets, but who can say what has value and what does not? To my eye, these disks bear the mark of a White Hand, and that is a symbol I have seen on many of the foes I have encountered in these lands. If Theodore thinks all treasure comes in strongboxes, I may have to rethink my lesson plans and return to the very beginning!'

Haley's laughter echoes in the silence, and trails away. Her smile fades, replaced by a worried expression.

'You do not think he really believes that, do you? Oh my.''We have come a long way, and Bingo is still lost. But I am confident that we will soon find our hobbit again! Do not give up hope, ${PLAYER}. Haley Meadowsweet is a Master Treasure Seeker, and if her student is not a master, well, he must be at least a novice, or an apprentice. Perhaps they can find not just treasures, but hobbits as well?'

<rgb=#00FF00>Bert Bartleby has arrived at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Dour Faces' (Recommended Level: 49)</rgb>The Angmarim, servants of the Dark Lord, threaten the Rangers of Gath Forthnír.The Angmarim are to the west of Gath Forthnír, near Carn Dûm.

Areneth said that a display of strength could break the spirits of the Angmarim loyal to Mordor. He asked you to collect the weapons of those you defeat.Collect broken swords from Angmarim warriors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect broken crossbows from Angmarim marksmen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Araneth is at Gath Forthnír, to the east of Carn Dûm.

You must return to Araneth with the weapons which you captured.'I received word of reinforcements coming from the south. If this is true, they can help us strike out against Carn Dûm. We must make a show of strength to the Angmarim, lest they become emboldened and attack us before help arrives. 

'Set out to the west, to Himbar, and defeat the Angmarim warriors and marksmen. Take their weapons and bring them back to me. We must prepare the message before we deliver it.''If we can strike fear into the black hearts of the Angmarim, they will not harry the movement of our allies to the south.''Thank you for helping us, friend. We must take these broken weapons and deliver them a message. No longer will they wreak havoc upon us.'Burgthryth is distraught over the destruction of her finest lute strings by a particularly vicious and tone-deaf sabre-tooth.Burgthryth has asked you to retrieve the guts of the kalpa-kita, Laulu-vihâja, from which she will make new strings for her lute.

Laulu-vihâja is on Kissa-sari, South-west from Jänis-leiri.Defeat Laulu-vihâja and collect his gutsBurgthryth has asked you to retrieve the guts of the kalpa-kita, Laulu-vihâja, from which she will make new strings for her lute.

Laulu-vihâja is on Kissa-sari, South-west from Jänis-leiri.Having retrieved a pile of cat-gut from the very kalpa-kita that wrecked Burgthryth's lute, you should return it to her as quickly as possible.

Burgthryth is at Jänis-leiri.'I am sorry to be so much trouble, ${PLAYER}, but I could use your help with one more thing before I set out to find the rest of my fellowship. You see, while you were out trying to retrieve my pages, I decided to give it a try myself. Unfortunately, while I was travelling along the banks of the river to the south-west, I came face-to-face with the biggest kalpa-kita I have yet seen here -- and he looked hungry!

'Well, to make a long story short, you know how they say that music soothes the savage beast? Ha! Do not wager your life on it! That horrid cat barely let me get out the first verse before he ripped the strings right out of my prized lute! I was so angry I could have bashed him senseless with my lute right then and there -- but then I would need a new lute and not just a new set of strings, so I ran instead.

'As for the strings, I have just the thing -- could you find that sabre-tooth and drag his sorry carcass back to me? I have never used cat-gut strings before, but I think now is the perfect time to give them a try! The Lossoth here say they have hunted this cat before without much luck -- they call him Laulu-vihâja...he supposedly has a den on that island in the Lune.''Have you found Laulu-vihâja yet? I'll have that cat's guts for my lute!''Have you found Laulu-vihâja yet? I'll have that cat's guts for my lute!''You got him? Wonderful! That will teach that stupid cat to disdain my talent! I hope he sounds good. Ah well, nothing but to get him strung up and give him a try....

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I will have to be off soon to try and find the rest of my fellowship -- wherever they have wandered to in this awful place. I hope I see you again someday!'A large flock of crebain out of Dunland has descended upon Eregion, spying for signs of the passing of the Company from Rivendell.The crebain may be found in the valley far to the east of Gwingris.

Thaliondir has asked you to go forth and defeat Saruman's craban-spies.Defeat crebain (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thaliondir is at Gwingris, far to the west of the craban-nests.

You should return to Thaliondir with news of your success.Talk to Thaliondir in Gwingris'Well met, friend.

'No doubt you have heard of the White Hand's many spies in this land. A favourite spy of Saruman are the crebain.  

'Our scouts report a large number of crebain along the northern edges of the valley far to the east of Gwingris. Find them and rid our valley of their menace before they discover something that should remain hidden.''Well done, ${PLAYER}. That should limit the White Hand's ability to gather knowledge of our forces here...and of other things that should remain hidden.

'With their numbers weakened by your efforts, we have an opportunity to strike a decisive blow against their tenuous foothold here in Eregion.''Crebain have been spotted to the east. Our scouts report a large number of the birds along the northern edge of the valley.'Reginald Ward has not only become separated from his Fellowship, but seems to have mislaid his herald as well. The two were separated during a battle against the Angmarim on the shelf at the edge of the Ice Bay, south-east of Sûri-kylä. Reginald is hoping that someone more familiar with the lands of Forochel can help him find his lost herald.Reginald's herald was lost somewhere on the ice-shelf, south-east of Sûri-kylä, on the eastern edge of the Ice Bay of Forochel. Reginald Ward is at Sûri-kylä, north-west of the ice-shelf.

Reginald Ward asked you search for his herald and return him to Sûri-kylä.Find Reginald Ward's missing herald'Ah! 'Tis good to see a face from the south in this harsh land. The locals are a fine enough sort but not the friendliest towards strangers, if you know what I mean. Perhaps you could see your way towards helping out a fellow countryman in need? You see, my fine young herald has gone missing! Several days ago the two of us were separated from our most excellent fellowship during a battle to the south. Afterwards, my herald and I came further north, hoping to re-group here in Sûri-kylä -- unfortunately, we stumbled upon a patrol of vile Angmarim on our way here!

'Well, though I fought valiantly, my herald somehow managed to lose his way in the confusion of battle -- you know how heralds are, no sense of direction whatsoever -- anyway, I am certain he managed to escape, but since then I have seen no sign of my faithful companion.

'I know this region poorly at best and would be most indebted to you if you could help me find my wayward herald. I cannot go off in search of the rest of my fellowship until I have him safely in tow again!''Thank goodness you are here! I was wondering if anyone would ever find me out here before I froze to death!

'Did Reginald send you? He is not here with you, is he?''Have you found my herald yet? I'm quite concerned about the young fool!'The lossoth have many ancient rites and rituals appeasing the spirits of the sky and of the water.The ritual stone is south along the Ice Bay among the Angmarim. Reodh Fuil is south east of Sûri-kylä.

Jalmari instructed you to throw the feathers into the wind at Reodh Fuil and place the fish by the Ice Bay at the ritual stone that has now been overrun by Angmarim.Jalmari is at Sûri-kylä on the north eastern edge of the Ice Bay of Forochel.

You should return to Jalmari and tell him that you have done as he asked.'At the beginning of every season, we give offerings to the spirits of the wind and the spirits of the water. For as long as our people can recall, we have made these offerings, and they have always brought fish and fair weather to our villages.

'Recently, we have not had an abundance of fish, and the storms have been sudden and terrible. This must be because of all the strangers and the Rauta-väki...their lack of offerings must be upsetting the spirits!

'You must hurry! Take this ritual fish and place it upon the ritual stone along the edge of the Ice Bay where the Angmarim have now occupied. Also, take this bag of feathers and throw them into the wind at Reodh Fuil, near to the pass into Forodwaith where the wind blows strongest.''Complete the rituals, ${PLAYER}...we need the blessings of the spirits!''The ritual has been performed. That is well. Hopefully, appeasing the spirits will bring us good fortune. We are in need of it.'So it seems that the shadowy fingers of Angmar have stretched even into desolate Forochel, sewing the seeds of conflict here as in all places they touch.

Here at least, it seems that their preparations are less ready than elsewhere, or perhaps they find little of value, for they rely mostly on their less trustworthy allies -- the Dourhands and the savage Gauredain -- rather than their well-ordered hordes of Orcs and evil Men.Defeat Angmarim in ForochelDwalin, Master of Thorin's Hall, has agreed to help you forge the key, but there are some vital components missing.Keeper Brombard Foxtail is at the Mathom-house in Michel Delving in the western part of the Shire. Jon Brackenbrook is in the town of Archet, north-east of Bree-town.

Dwalin informed you that he needs a mithril nugget, which might be in the keeping of the Mathom-house in the Shire, and a key to cast a mould from, which might be obtained from Jon Brackenbrook, the new Captain of Archet.Dwalin can be found in Thorin's Hall in Ered Luin.

Dwalin sent you to get a mithril nugget from the Mathom-house in the Shire and a key to help in casting a mould from Jon Brackenbrook in Archet.Litli can be found by the forge near the throne of Thorin's Hall.

Now that you have recovered the mithril and Éogan's key, Litli, one of Dwalin's smiths, can begin work on the key.Dwalin can be found in Thorin's Hall in Ered Luin.

Litli took the mithril from you and began heating it. However, he mentioned that shaping the mithril will be a problem, one that perhaps Dwalin can solve.'That we can forge this key, I have no doubt, but there are some items we will need before we can begin. Master Elrond's instructions say that we need mithril, but there is none to be had in the Blue Mountains. Also, we will need something to cast a mould from.

'Now, one of the gifts Bilbo took back from the Lonely Mountain was a nugget of mithril. I doubt he understood its value, so he probably left it behind when he returned to Rivendell. However, he may have given it to the Mathom-house in Michel Delving. Perhaps the Keeper of the Mathom-house can help you with that.

'As for the casting, Atli Spiderbane sent word that the Archet guards found a ruined key among the bodies of the Blackwolds. Jon Brackenbrook may still have it. If their leader was from Angmar, as I have heard, it may be that key is just what we need to cast the mould.''I will gladly help you with that key, but first you must obtain the mithril nugget and the ruined key we need.''Hello there! What can I do for you? A mithril nugget? Never heard of it.

'Oh! You mean that chunk of silver old Bilbo brought back from his adventures! I thought it was too light for real silver.

'Well, it must have little value then, and I don't see why we should keep it. You're quite welcome to take it... here.''Greetings, friend! What may I help you with? You say you have come all the way from the Blue Mountains to retrieve that old ruined key we found among the Blackwolds' belongings?

'It is true that it was found among things we believe were Calder Cob's, which is the only reason I kept it. It's quite useless.

'Here... may it assist you in your endeavours.''I am surprised you travelled so far for that key. May it serve you well.''I do hope that silvery metal is useful to you. It really wasn't all that interesting... I didn't even have it out on display.''Ah! I was right! The hobbits did indeed have Bilbo's nugget. I hope you didn't have to hurt anyone to get it....

'Foxtail didn't even know what it was? Well, it should be no surprise to me... a hobbit would not know truly precious metals if you gave them a hoard.

'And I see you retrieved the key from Jon Brackenbrook. We have all we need. Go now and take these items to Litli by the forge there. He will get started on the key.''Go on now, don't just stand about here. Take the mithril and that key you gathered to Litli. He's over by the forge near the throne.''So you're the one Dwalin was talking about. You have the mithril, I see, and that foul key from the Angmarim. All right then, give them here. I'll get started on smelting down that mithril!

'The problem is that there will be a problem shaping this metal. Perhaps you should speak with Dwalin.''Smelting that mithril will take a bit of time yet. Of course, if I don't have a shaping hammer to work it, it doesn't matter how long it takes to smelt it down.

'If getting this key is important to you, let Dwalin know that I'm going to need a mithril shaping hammer.''A mithril shaping hammer? Bah! I've seen but one of those in my entire life. And I've not seen one since... oh dear....'You obtained the mithril nugget and the ruined key as Dwalin asked, but his smith, Litli, informed you that there was still one more thing needed to work the mithril.Thrór's Hammer lies within the tomb of Skorgrím Dourhand. The tomb lies south-east of Thorin's Hall.

Dwalin told you he needed a hammer that Thrór, Thorin's grandfather and last King Under the Mountain before the coming of Smaug, took with him into exile and which Thráin his son brought to the Blue Mountains.Litli can be found by the forges near the throne of Thorin's Hall in Ered Luin.

You should take the hammer you found to Litli so that he can forge the key you need.'To work with mithril, you need a special hammer. So special, in fact, that we only ever had one here in Thorin's Hall: Thrór's Hammer, the hammer of Thorin's grandfather, which Thráin brought here! Of course, since we never had any mithril to work, we never used it.

'When most of the Longbeards moved back to the Lonely Mountain, the hammer got left behind, and -- as an insult to us, I'm sure -- the Dourhands took it and placed it in Skorgrím's tomb as tribute to him.

'At least, that is what one of them told us. We tried reclaiming that tomb, but it seems cursed. Vile things started lurking in the shadows there. I would say we should just let it lie there, but this task of yours is important enough to warrant opening the tomb again. You'll not find any dwarves willing to go in there, so you will need to retrieve the hammer yourself. The tomb lies south of here, in the Berghold.''Thrór's Hammer! I never thought to see it again! Your efforts will not be forgotten, ${PLAYER}.

'Quick now, take the hammer to Litli, and he will get to work on your key!''Bring the hammer to Litli at once, so he can begin work on your key!''Ah, you've found the hammer! Excellent, ${PLAYER}! A chance to work Moria-silver... I never thought I'd see the day!

'Come now, let's get to work!'Hwati told you that to truly end the threat of Maethad, you must defeat its master, Khurrákh. However, this Gorthorog will only fight you if you can prove yourself worthy of his attention.Ronkûrz, the champion of Maethad, can be found training Uruks within the the Orc encampment of  Mor Maudhúl, north of Gabilshathûr, on the far side of the lake in Duvuinen.

Once you have claimed one of Ronkûrz's maces, bring it back to Hwati in Gabilshathûr.'We have had a stroke of luck! While you were away, a dwarf-prospector returned and told us that he saw Ronkûrz entering the Orc-encampment of Mor Maudhúl. No doubt he is going there to train the Uruks, but being away from the arena leaves him vulnerable!

'Wait, wait, I am getting ahead of myself. Allow me to explain my plan. You see, to truly end Maethad's threat, we must defeat its master, a Gorthorog named "Khurrákh." Khurrákh rarely shows himself though, and only fights those he deems worthy of his attention. Ronkûrz, another Gorthorog and current champion of Maethad, is his prize student. If you were to defeat him and claim one of his maces, Khurrákh would have to fight you!

'Mor Maudhúl lies to the north of here, on the far side of the foul lake in Duvuinen. Ronkûrz should be found somewhere within the encampment. Hurry now!''Wonderful, you have done it! You have shown yourself to be a hardy warrior, which is good, for your hardest challenge lies ahead: defeating the master of Maethad.'Defeat the champions of Urugarth.Defeat BurzfîlDefeat SorkrankDefeat DushkâlDefeat AkrûrDefeat LhugrienDefeat the champions of Urugarth.Defeat GríshakrumDefeat AthpukhDefeat LâmkarnDefeat GruglokDefeat the champions of Urugarth.Defeat ThordraghDefeat BrízripDefeat MorthrângDefeat LagmâsThe Rangers at Gath Forthnír await a delivery of armoured carapaces to be made to Areneth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver armoured carapaces from the insects near Gath Forthnír to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-camp.Wanted: Armoured carapaces from the insects near Gath Forthnír. Deilvery of the items should be made to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-outpost.Wanted: Armoured carapaces from the insects near Gath Forthnír.'We will find a use for this.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Rangers at Gath Forthnír await a delivery of monstrous wings to be made to Areneth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver monstrous wings from the bats near Gath Forthnír to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-camp.Wanted: Monstrous wings from the bats near Gath Forthnír. Deilvery of the items should be made to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-outpost.Wanted: Monstrous wings from the bats near Gath Forthnír.'We will find a use for this.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Kol, a resident of Gabilshathûr, told you of their desperate need for food.Nerbyg and slugs can be found in Duvuinen, north of Gabilshathûr.

Kol has asked you to collect norbog-legs and slug-tongues to help supplement the provisions at Gabilshathûr.'Kol at your service! Things were looking rather hopeless around here, but with you about, perhaps we will stand a chance of surviving... if we can get enough provender, that is.

'Now, everyone here is all excited about the Orcs, and it's true that food isn't much of a problem if an Orc has lopped off your head. However, I'm not much with an axe, so I'll spend my time worrying about what I can help with, and that's food. We only have enough provisions for a few more days, and I think it is time to look for other sources of food.

'I am reluctant to bring it up, but I have heard that the Orcs eat norbog-legs and slug-tongues, and while I'm sure such provender is not much for taste, it keeps them alive. It might do the same for us as well. Could you gather some legs and... tongues from the nerbyg and slugs found in Duvuinen?''Ah, so you are back... let me see what you have there!

'Oh my, it is worse than I thought. What vile creatures those Orcs are if they live off of food such as this! And the smell... perhaps there is something we can add to it to make the taste less vile.'The Dunlendings are ravaging Glâd Ereg, cutting down trees to supply the craftsmen of Saruman with wood.The Dunlending lumber-camp is south-east of Gwingris. Inufileg is at Gwingris, west and north of the Dunlending lumber-camp

Inufileg has asked you to stop the Dunlendings' wood-cutting by taking their axes from them.'You were right to bring this to my attention, ${PLAYER}. Not only are there brigands at our very doorstep, nor are they simply taking our forest, they are part of a larger plan at work that we do not yet understand. Undoubtedly they are providing wood for the craftsmen of Saruman, but to what goal, I do not know.

'It is not for me to surmise the greater purpose behind these matters. For now let us deal with the problem at hand. Go back to the Dunlendings' lumber-camp, south-east of Gwingris, and take their axes from them.

'Return to me when you have finished. Without the proper tools, their butchery of the wood will end.''Good work, ${PLAYER}. This should slow down their efforts, while we delve deeper into their intentions.'Saruman's half-orcs have been seen stealing worm-eggs. Thus far, their full purpose remains unclear.Half-orcs can be found south-east of Gwingris.

Calelloth has instructed you to go forth and defeat the half-orcs who are stealing worm-eggs for their master.Defeat half-orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Calelloth is at Gwingris, north-west of where you found the half-orcs.

You should speak with Calelloth regarding your victory against the half-orcs.Talk to Calelloth at Gwingris'Our scouts report that a group of half-orcs have been stealing worm-eggs. This is troubling news, ${PLAYER}. I can only imagine their ultimate purpose with the worm-eggs.

'For now, though, let us deal with the immediate threat. The very presence of half-orcs in the area speaks of these evil times.

'Journey to Pend Eregion in the south-east and hunt down the servants of the White Hand. These half-orc abominations must be put to an end.''Our scouts reported seeing a small group of half-orcs in Pend Eregion to the south-east some distance away, just south of the road to Rivendell.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}. For now, the half-orcs have been thwarted.

'Rest for a while, for you will need your strength again before long.'Wights are not the only source of terror in Imlad Balchorth.Darkwater can be found in Imlad Balchorth, west of Myrkworth.

Sigar has asked you to destroy the darkwater in Imlad Balchorth.Defeat darkwater in Imlad Balchorth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigar is at Myrkworth, east of Imlad Balchorth.

Having destroyed the darkwater as asked, return to Sigar in Myrkworth and tell him the deed is done.'When we were prospecting through Imlad Balchorth, a strange and terrible thing happened: the water itself rose up and attacked us! I have never seen such a thing in my long life.

'It is a foul water indeed that houses the evil spirits of the darkwater. These terrible creatures must be banished if any peace is to be brought to my people trapped here. Please, go forth and destroy as many of the darkwater as you can.

'Come to me when the deed is done, and we will have more to speak of.''You have done us a great service, my friend. Thank you very much, indeed.  Now less work remains for me to deal with, and perhaps I can finish the task at hand.''I know not what manner of creature these darkwater are, but they must be purged from Imlad Balchorth.'Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel has requested that albino skin be delivered to her at the Elf-ruins in the southern reaches of Eregion.You have accepted a task to deliver albino skin from the boars surrounding Echad Mirobel to Maegamiel. Maegamiel defends Echad Mirobel in the southern area of Eregion.Wanted: Albino skin from the boars surrounding Echad Mirobel. Deliver the items to Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel in the southern section of Eregion.'We will make good use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel has requested that jewelled sword sheaths be delivered to her at the Elf-ruins in the southern reaches of Eregion.You have accepted a task to deliver jewelled sword sheaths taken from the enemies of the Free Peoples to Maegamiel. Maegamiel defends Echad Mirobel in the southern area of Eregion.Wanted: Jewelled sword sheaths from the enemies of the Free Peoples. Deliver the items to Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel in the southern section of Eregion.'The enemies of the Free Peoples must continue to fall, or all will be lost. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann has requested that a collection of monstrous ears be delivered to her.You have accepted a task to deliver monstrous ears from the beasts found near Echad Dúnann to the Elf-maid Tinúriel. Tinúriel can be found in Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Monstrous ears taken from the beasts in the areas around Echad Dúnann. Deliver the items to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Monstrous ears taken from the beasts in the areas around Echad Dúnann.'We will make good use of these. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann has requested that a collection of monstrous wings be delivered to her.You have accepted a task to deliver monstrous wings from the wicked craban-scouts found near Echad Dúnann to the Elf-maid Tinúriel. Tinúriel can be found in Echad Dúnann.Wanted: Monstrous wings taken from the Enemy's scouts, the crebain, in the areas around Echad Dúnann. Deliver the items to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann.'These enemies will trouble us no longer. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.You had been asked to find Reginald Ward's herald, but upon finding him, he revealed that he lost his Captain's treasured family-banner.Reginald Ward's banner is lost in one of the encampments of the Angmarim on the ice-shelf, south-east of Sûri-kylä.

Reginald's herald has asked you to retrieve his Captain's banner, which he lost during the attack by the Angmarim.Reginald Ward is at Sûri-kylä, north-west of the ice-shelf.

Reginald Ward's herald has asked you to tell his Captain that he is safe and where he can be found.Tell Reginald Ward where his wayward herald can be found'Thank you for finding me out here -- but I am afraid I cannot go back with you. You see, when the Captain led those Angmarim off as he fled, a few remained and gave me a good chase too. I was able to escape, but...

'...but you see, I dropped his banner in my flight! Oh, I am a worthless herald! The Angmarim took it and returned with it to their camp out on the ice somewhere. 

'It has been in the Captain's family for generations -- my father carried it for the Captain's father, and his father before that and so on. I cannot go back and face him without that banner. Please help me retrieve it!''A fine greetings to you again, ${PLAYER}! Any luck finding my wayward herald out there?

'Yes? Splendid! Plucky little fellow isn't he? I knew he'd come through all right. My sincere and heartfelt thanks to you for your help, friend.

'I suppose I'd best go retrieve him, shall I then? Once we've got that matter in order, then we'll need to find the rest of the Fellowship!

'Good luck to you, ${PLAYER}, should our paths cross again and you need my aid, you just let me know.''I suppose you ought to tell Captain Ward where I am....''You found it? Ah, you are a wonder, ${PLAYER}. You have saved my honour and that of my family!

'Now that I have got the banner back, I can finally make my way to Sûri-kylä and meet up with the Captain again. What a relief! No, I would never have expected him to come out here looking for me. No-one in that entire fellowship has the slightest sense of direction you know, even the hunter! 

'So, if you would be so kind as to give him detailed directions on where he can find me, I would be much obliged. It is a bit too dangerous for me to go wandering around on my own out here.'The Angmarim have driven polar bears into the fishing-grounds of the Lossoth.Olavi's fish-buckets can be found south-west of his camp along the Ice Bay.

Fearing the bears, Olavi has asked you to retrieve his fish-buckets from the fishing-grounds.Olavi is at his fishing camp east of Sûri-kylä along the road in Jä-rannit.

You should bring the fish-buckets you recovered to Olavi.'For many generations, my family has fished in the Ice Bay, and the spirits of the water have always brought to us a great bounty of fish. I am worried now, though, for my livelihood is being threatened by the Rauta-väki.  

'They have come hunting the kantâ for meat, and that has driven them closer and closer to the fishing-grounds that once were free from such danger. It has become so dangerous that I dare not even return to retrieve my fish!

'Go and retrieve my fish-buckets, which still lie where I left them by the shore. Be careful, though, for the bears will be after the fish as well.'The bucket is filled to the brim with fish.'Have you retrieved my fish-buckets yet? They lie close to the shore, south west of here.''You have brought me my fish! I doubted you would be able to handle the kantâ, but now I can see that you are truly a ${CLASS} of your word.'In his haste to escape the Angmarim, Olavi dropped his fishing-pole.There are Angmarim along the shore of the Ice Bay, south-west of Olavi's camp along the bay.

Olavi has asked you to recover his fishing-pole from the Angmarim.Collect Olavi's fishing poleOlavi is at his camp along the road in Jä-rannit.

You have recovered Olavi's fishing-pole and should return it to him at once.'In the icy waters of this land, you can be pushed to your limits and beyond. You are tested without reprieve, and the price for failure is grave indeed.

'One friend that has always been true and never failed me is my fishing-pole. It was made by my father and given to me, as I will do with my own son when he comes of age to take up the family trade.

'But darkness hangs over me. My fishing-pole has gone missing! One of those thieving Rauta-väki must have stolen it in my haste to escape from the Rauta-väki. Please, go and retrieve my fishing-pole... one of the Rauta-väki fishing by the shore will surely have it.''Those evil Men have my fishing pole... bring it to me, friend, and I will be eternally grateful.''My fishing-pole! You have done a great deed, ${PLAYER}... it will not be forgotten by my family.'The brutal wilderness of Forochel would keep all but the hardiest at bay, but for those with the spirit to endure and thrive in its environment, its call may prove irresistable at the last.Complete Forochel Survivor.Complete Forochel QuestsComplete quests in ForochelThe pools of Imlad Balchorth are noxious pits of foul water. No one knows if the corruption stems from the Enemy's industry or from a more sinister source.Imlad Balchorth's pools are to the west of Myrkworth.

The waters of the eastern Imlad Balchorth cause strange maladies, and Thorod wishes to determine the cause. He told you to take samples from the roiling patches in the pools.Thorod is in Myrkworth, east of Imlad Balchorth.

Your phials are filled with water from the roiling pools of the Imlad Balchorth.'Have you witnessed the foul waters that flow from Carn Dûm? In the past months, we have seen the waters from Urugarth become dark and rank with the smell of decay, but we cannot fathom the reason. Regardless of the cause, it is becoming ever more difficult to find drinkable water!

'We must learn more of these waters and the maladies that they cause as they flow into Imlad Balchorth. Here, take this phial and return to me with samples of the water. Please combine five drams of this foul water into a single phial.

'You should first draw water from the roiling patches in the pools in the eastern parts of Imlad Balchorth. The waters there cloud the minds and bind the tongues of those who come upon them.''Make your way to the eastern parts of Imlad Balchorth and draw water from the roiling patches of water in the pools you find there. We must find the source of the taint!''Excellent. I will study this water and attempt to glean what knowledge I can from it, though an Elf would be better suited to the task. We still have much work to do.'Guard Ansurr told you of his plan to turn the rage of the mother-turtle, Gaeruan, against the trolls and Orcs of Grishbalt. Her attack should throw the creatures there into confusion and make infiltrating the caves there easier.Gaeruan's nest is resting in the middle of the lake Duvuinen, north of Gabilshathûr. The caves of Grishbalt are north-east of Gaeruan's nest.

Take one of Gaeruan's eggs from her nest and place it near the central bonfire in the caves of Grishbalt.Find Gaeruan's nest in DuvuinenGuard Ansurr can be found in the dwarf-holding of Gabilshathûr, south-west of Grishbalt.

Guard Ansurr's plan worked perfectly. The mother-turtle was drawn into the caves, and her rage threw the camp into confusion.'Pardon me, ${PLAYER}, but I have heard you may be travelling into the caves of Grishbalt...a dangerous place, home to many trolls and Orcs. However, there is an idea I have had in my head for a while that might make your task a bit easier.

'See, the other day I saw a troll get near the nest of the great turtle-mother, Gaeruan, that rests in the middle of the lake. As the troll poked at her eggs, Gaeruan came along and sent that troll running!

'My idea is to take an egg from Gaeruan's nest and bring it in close to the large central fire in the caves. As the egg heats, the scent will bring Gaeruan, and we can turn her rage against those creatures!''Ha! So it worked, ${PLAYER}? Astounding! I didn't want to tell you this, but I was sure my plan was going to fail!

'It seems the only flaw was that I wasn't there to see the destruction that Gaeruan visited on them.

'Well done! Well done!''Have you forgotten the plan so quickly? Gaeruan's nest lies in the centre of the lake Duvuinen. Carefully gather one of her eggs and bring it to the central bonfire in Grishbalt.

'The scent of her egg heating should draw Gaeruan, and her rage should throw Grishbalt into confusion!'The Lossoth-outposts were damaged in the last Gauradan-raid. With their stores dwindling, the Lossoth must turn to bears as a source of food.Bears can be found throughout Jä-rannit. Once you have the meat, bring it to Raija in Karhu-leiri.

Raija has asked you to replenish their stores with bear-meat.'Every day, the Susi-väki raid our stores and steal our food. How are we expected to survive without food?

'Another supply must be found, and I think I have it. We must have meat from the kantâ, or we will be forced to leave this place.

'You can find kantâ -- what I believe you call bears -- throughout Jä-rannit.''This meat will do wonderfully...I thank you.'While holly-berries are generally considered to be poisonous, holly-leaves are often used in the brewing of tea.Holly-trees can be found throughout Glâd Ereg. Celebrandir is encamped at Gwingris in the northern reaches of Glâd Ereg.

You should gather more holly-leaves and bring them to the Elf Celebrandir.This branch is covered in aromatic holly-leaves. You have heard that the Elves can brew fine teas from the leaves of many different trees. Perhaps one of the Elves in Gwingris can brew something from these holly-leaves if you gather more?'What is this that you have brought to me, ${PLAYER}? Ah, holly-leaves. I can indeed brew a fine tea from these.'You have been encouraged to learn the lay of the waste that is East Angmar, so you may better find your way to Gath Forthnír when needed.Maerchiniath's brooch is somewhere in East Angmar.

The Ranger Maerchiniath has agreed to teach you how to find secret paths through East Angmar if you can follow the trail he took when he lost his brooch and bring it back to him.Maerchiniath is at the hidden camp of Gath Forthnír, in East Angmar.

You have recovered the fallen brooch and should return it now to Maerchiniath.'Welcome to Gath Forthnír, ${PLAYER}. This is a grim and cheerless place, but we do what we must to stand against the shadows that press us.

'You seek to learn the secret paths to Gath Forthnír? I could speak to you all day, and if you did not possess enough skill as a hunter, it would not matter -- anything I could teach you would flow over you like water and mean nothing.

'You must prove to me that you have the skill, ${PLAYER}. When last I scouted the eastern lands of Angmar, I let fall a brooch I carried; I did not realize it was missing until I returned here. If you can follow my trail, you might find the brooch where I dropped it. If you are able to do this, return the brooch to me, and I will teach you what I know.''I left Gath Forthnír and lost my brooch somewhere along the way. If you are a worthy hunter, you should be able to find my trail and follow it. Perhaps you will find my brooch somewhere along the way, and I will show you how to find secret paths through Angmar so you can come quickly to Gath Forthnír if the need is great.'You find a fallen brooch partially-buried in the loose stones and dirt.Something came this way, but what it might have been eludes you and the trail is long cold. Your heart tells you that Maerchiniath did not come this way.Maerchiniath certainly came this way; that much is clear. What is not clear is in what direction he set forth from this point, for the tracks are muddled and confused.From here the trail leads north-west; that much is certain.Close study reveals that the faint trail leads south.The tracks turn to the south-east at this point. Maerchiniath certainly passed this way and not long ago.'You found my brooch, ${PLAYER}, and by doing so have proven yourself a hunter worthy of reward!

'I will teach you the signs of the secret paths through this unpleasant land, so you may lead your allies to Gath Forthnír with great speed, if the need arises.'The Gauredain have been warned that their aggression would not go unmarked yet they have not relented, and so the battle shall commence in earnest.The Gauredain can be found on the great glacier across the Ice Bay to the west.

Vämmä has asked you to hunt the Gauredain stalking the glaciers.Defeat Gauradan Ravagers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Gauradan Stalkers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Gauradan Seers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Vämmä is in Sûri-kylä, far to the east of the glacier, across the Ice Bay.

With their numbers weakened, you should return to Vämmä and see what else might be done to end the Gauradan threat.'The Susi-väki are brutes, grown stronger and more savage than those who follow a civilized path. It comes as no surprise to me that their response to our warning is only to redouble their assaults in challenge. I had no true hope that it would be otherwise, for it has ever been this way with them.

'Now the war begins in earnest as their hunters roam far and wide upon the ice, searching for either man or beast to slay. They shall be the ones we hunt, for they are poorly organized and vulnerable, depending on their raw strength alone rather than the more deadly tools of cunning or numbers.

'We shall winnow their numbers now, before they form into true war-bands, which would offer a much greater threat to us. Go and seek these Susi-väki upon the glacier, west across the Ice Bay.''It is well that you have returned, for your efforts, difficult though they may have been, have not proven to be enough. While you were away, the winds brought the howls of the Susi-väki here. Our long years of struggle with these brutes have taught us some of the meaning of their howls. Few of my people have ever heard such a fierce howl as this, and it could mean but one thing: The Susi-väki have chosen a war leader.''Are the Susi-väki dead? Have you done as I have asked?'At Dwalin's direction, you have travelled to the abandoned tomb of Skorgrím Dourhand to search for mithril-shaping hammer of Thorin's grandfather, Thrór.Search Skorgrím's tomb for Thrór's hammer.Search for Thrór's hammerDefeat Faerdami, a fell spirit who has taken possession of Thrór's hammer.You have found what appears to be part of a supply delivery to the Elf-camp at Gwingris.Gwingris lies to the west of the ruined supply delivery.

You should take the satchel you found to the Elf-camp at Gwingris.This bundle is clearly part of a supply delivery. Amid the claw and bite marks the worm inflicted upon it, you see the trace of the Elven name, 'Gwingris.'

Gwingris lies to the west. You should look for someone there who might know something about the satchel.'What do you bring to me?

'It is as I feared. Our latest delivery from Rivendell fell prey to the worms of Pend Eregion. We have long known of the worms; but until now they proved little more than a hinderance.

'Increased aggression from the worms spells grave news for our way-post.'Find pages of the Orcs' marching orders scattered throughout Urugarth.Find pages of the Orcs' marching orders scattered throughout Urugarth and discover the plans of the Enemy.Find pages of the Orcs' marching orders scattered throughout Urugarth and discover the plans of the Enemy.This page tells of Angmar's plans for Dushkal and his host of trolls.This page tells of the course of training that Lagmâs is to employ in the development of Angmar's army of Orcs.This page tells of new developments with the black powder stolen from Saruman's Orcs and how it can best be used to further increase the potency of the fire-pots used by the goblins of Angmar.This page outlines the hierarchy among the Uruk-captains of Angmar.This page highlights the best practices for maintaining the health of a growing Warg-pack.This page is a rough tally of the number of slaves under the employ of Urugarth and what tasks they are directed to fulfil.Hwati has told you that now that you have Ronkûrz' mace, you can draw forth Khurrákh, the Master of Maethad, and challenge him.Maethad is north and east of Gabilshathûr.

Hwati has instructed you to enter the centre of Maethad and use the Ushum-ghâsh to draw Khurrákh forth.Defeat KhurrákhHwati is back at Gabilshathûr, west and south of Maethad.

Having defeated Khurrákh, you should return to Hwati and tell him the tale of your deeds.'We are now ready to strike at the very heart of Maethad, its master: Khurrákh. He is a great and terrible Gorthorog, having slain many to achieve and keep his position. Defeating him will not be easy, but must be done, if Maethad's threat is to be ended.

'Take Ronkûrz' mace and enter the centre of Maethad. From what we have been told of what happens there, you should find something the Gertheryg call the "Ushum-ghâsh" in the centre of Maethad. Once there, display Ronkûrz' mace near the fire. Khurrákh will have no choice but to face you. His sense of "honour," if a such a concept exists within a Gorthorog, will force him to fight you.

'It will not be an easy fight, but it must be done.''Go now to Maethad, north and east of here and draw Khurrákh out. End Maethad's threat, once and for all!''It is done? Khurrákh is dead? I cannot believe it! Maethad's master is dead!

'While some day I'm sure there will be another Master of Maethad, for now, you have thrown Maethad into confusion.

'However, there is more work to be done, further to the north....'Celebrandir in Gwingris has offered to make you more holly-leaf tea if you bring him enough leaves.Holly leaves can be found at the bases of holly-trees in Pend Eregion.

You should gather more holly leaves and bring them to Celebrandir in Gwingris.'The tea from the leaves you brought me was exquisite ${PLAYER}. Unfortunately, I have used them up and will not be able to make any more without a fresh supply.

'If you would venture out again and collect more of the leaves, I would gladly make you another batch of tea.''These leaves look perfect, ${PLAYER}. I look forward to sharing another brew with you.'Birgir was pleased with the disruption you caused among the ranks of the Angmarim already, but he feels it was not quite enough.Múl Kholuim has his camp inside a ruin in some high hills south of the large lake in Imlad Balchorth, west from Myrkworth. Birgir has asked you to find the camp of the Angmarim keeper of books.Discover the library of Múl KholuimYou have found Múl Kholuim's camp inside a ruin in the high hills south of the large lake in Imlad Balchorth.

Birgir has asked you to defeat Múl Kholuim. He also warned you to gather allies.Birgir is at Myrkworth, north-east from Múl Kholuim's camp.

Having defeated Múl Kholuim, you should return to Birgir.'Now that you are familiar with the track of the patrols of the Angmarim in Imlad Balchorth, I have another task for you... one considerably more dangerous, but at the same time could cause greater harm to the Angmarim.

'The papers you found bear the mark of a lord of Angmar. This lord, Múl Kholuim, who calls himself the Keeper of Books, seems to be responsible for managing all the trade and supplies that move to and from Angmar to the Orc-armies in the North Downs. If he were to be defeated, we may be able to stop these supply lines.

'To that end, I would ask you to gather a couple of allies and raid the camp of the Angmarim that lies some distance west of the large lake, a place of high hills and winding paths. The Keeper of Books must be defeated and the orders deciphered.''You must find Múl Kholuim's encampment!''If you plan to confront Múl Kholuim, you should take allies.''Well done. We have stopped the Angmarim for now.

'The disruption of the Angmarim's trade and supply may seem like a minor accomplishment, but in the end, it may prove more important than a military victory!'Oflár has given you a tincture of nightshade which he hopes will purify the evil from the monuments in Imlad Balchorth.The monuments are in Imlad Balchorth in front of the large lake.

Oflár has asked you to bring the tincture to Imlad Balchorth and use it on the monuments to draw out the powerful spirits that guard them.Oflár is back at the dwarf-outpost of Myrkworth.

You dispersed some fell spirits and slew the wights who came to defend the obelisk, but there was little other result. One wight boasted of something he called the Cauldron of Death. Oflár might know of these words.'The nightshade-tincture is ready. I have learned that there are three great black monuments, like huge jagged blades, placed before the lake in the centre of Imlad Balchorth. This lake can be recognized by the two vast dying trees upon a central island. The monuments are grouped together in a triangle pattern before you reach the lake. I ask you to visit the monuments and use this tincture on each one.

'If all is as I suspect, the monuments will be protected by spirits and wights that hold great power. You are encouraged to defeat these spirits.  

'This is no small task, and you should gather a few of your friends to attempt this. Report back to me when the power of the monuments is broken.''Take the tincture to the three monuments that tower before the lake west of here.''This is a strange tale you tell. It appears I was wrong about the importance of those monuments. Surely they have meaning for the wights, but this Cauldron of Death you speak of...I suppose that is the key.

'But you have done very well to learn of this and to defeat the grim wights who came to defend the monument.

'Thank you for returning the remains of the tincture. It may prove useful in the future.'The Gauredain repeatedly raid Lossoth hunting outposts, both to antagonize the Lossoth and to take their provisions.Search south of Karhu-leiri for the scattered provisions of the Lossoth.'When the Gauredain stormed into our camp, they stole our supplies: our packed fish, tinder, grease, many things that we must now shiver here without.

'We have dispatched our own parties to find these packages, but any help you could provide would be greatly appreciated.

'The Gauredain are cruel and care little for the actual provisions. Most likely they simply scattered them about. Please search south of here and see if you can reclaim some of them.''Oh, how those packed fish call to me!  All of these good things you have brought back for us, we are honoured to have had your help.'The Angmarim, servants of the Dark Lord, threaten the Rangers of Gath Forthnír.  

Having stolen their weapons, you must return their broken remains to the Angmarim altars to show them your strength.Altars of the Angmarim can be found throughout Himbar, near the road or within the Angmarim-encampments.

Areneth said that a display of strength could break the spirits of the Angarim loyal to Mordor. He asked you to place the captured weapons upon the altars of the Angmarim as a form of intimidation.The Rangers of Gath Forthnír wait for you at their camp to the east of Carn Dûm.

You broke the morale of the Angmarim with your display of strength and resolve. It will be some time before their willingness to fight returns.'Here, take back the broken weapons you brought me. Take the pieces and place them upon the altars scattered throughout Himbar. The high-priests of the Angmarim use these shrines to honour the Dark Lord of Mordor. 

'Such a display will strike a blow to their morale and allow our friends the time they need to move north. You will find the Angmarim and their altars scattered across Himbar.''Thank you for helping us, friend. Perhaps there is still hope that we can win this war? The Angmarim are routed for the moment, but I fear they will soon find their strength and rally their forces.

'We must move with all due haste. We must strike them where they reside.''You have defeated many Angmarim, and that is good. Now show the others your prowess in battle by placing their broken weapons upon their altars.'Guard Ansurr told you that in the past the drakes of Dolroth menaced Gabilshathûr. He fears that now that they have no time to thin the numbers of the drakes, they will once again threaten Gabilshathûr.Drakes can be found far to the east of Gabilshathûr, in the valley of Dolroth.

Ansurr asked you to thin the numbers of the drakes.Defeat drakes in Dolroth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Guard Ansurr is at Gabilshathûr, far to the west of the valley of Dolroth.

You have defeated many drakes and should return to Guard Ansurr with news of your success.'Is it true what I have heard? That you are travelling into the valley of Dolroth? If that is true, could you thin the numbers of the drakes found there? You would be doing a great service to Gabilshathûr!

'Although not nearly as dangerous and wicked as a dragon, drakes share at least a small part of their love for precious things. In the past, the drakes of Dolroth were drawn here, swooping in and attacking us. After one such attack, I led a band of dwarves into Dolroth and thinned their numbers.

'Yet now, seeing as we are hard-pressed by the Orcs, we have no dwarves to spare to remove the drakes, and I fear the drakes' numbers will grow once more. If you, though, could slay a number of them, we will only have one threat to deal with!''Drakes can be found far to the east of here, ${PLAYER}, in Dolroth.''Well done! I will rest easier knowing that at least the drakes will not menace us any time soon. Well, at least that would be the case if the Orcs gave us a moment's rest!

'Oh, do not think me ungrateful, ${PLAYER}. You have done us a great service.'After some time passed, Elrond Halfelven sent scouts into the fields of Eregion to follow the path of the Company which left Rivendell and make sure that no signs of their passing remained for the Enemy to find.The camp-site is nestled at the base of some large boulders, some distance to the south-east of Gwingris, beyond Sâd Rechu, south of the road to Pembar.

Maedlathron has asked you to search a place which may have been a camp-site used by the Company. Any signs of the Company's passing must be removed.Scour the company's pathMaedlathron is at Gwingris, some distance to the north-west of the abandoned camp-site.

You should bring the pouch of pipe-weed which you found to Maedlathron.'Greetings, ${PLAYER}. I and my companions are recently come from Rivendell at the behest of Master Elrond. He fears that spies of the Enemy may dog the footsteps of the Company which left Imladris some little while ago -- the Company which bears a heavy burden -- and commanded us to follow in their wake, lest some sign of their passing be left behind.

'To this end, many of my companions have gone forth to scout the lands. It is clear that Aragorn son of Arathorn journeys with the Company still, for there has been little of note to see. They have lit few fires, for fear of the Enemy, which is good; however, one of my scouts reports that he saw signs of an old camp-fire south-east of here.

'If you would, seek out that camp and learn if any other sign has been left behind which may reveal the composition of the Company. The camp-site lies far to the south-east, beyond Sâd Rechu, nestled at the base of some boulders.''Go to the camp-site to the south-east of here and search for any sign of the Company's passing. If you find anything, remove it before the Enemy descends upon the camp.''You say you found this pouch at the camp-site? It contains some small portion of the halfling's pipe-weed.

'It may be that one of the halflings or the dwarf dropped this. Such carelessness could be their undoing.'The corruption that has struck Glâd Ereg and the destruction wreaked by the minions of the Enemy have ruined many a wood.Holly-seeds can be found on the bushes growing underneath the trees in Glâd Ereg, south of Gwingris.

Celebrandir has asked you to collect holly-seeds for him, so that he may begin replanting the wood.Collect seeds (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Celebrandir is at Gwingris, north of Glâd Ereg.

Celebrandir is awaiting the seeds you collected.'I am afraid that your efforts to the south will not be enough to repel the corruption the Enemy has brought to Glâd Ereg. The evil has gone far too deep.

'In order to repair the damage that our enemies have brought about, we will need to replant the holly-trees that have been devastated by the trolls and their foul ways.

'Journey south into the forest and find holly-seeds from the bushes that grow beneath the trees there. Bring them to me, and we will still find the opportunity to ensure the forest's prosperity.''You can find holly-seeds in the bushes that grow in under the trees south of Gwingris. Bring me as many as you can, and perhaps we can heal the forest.''These seeds are the hope of the forest, ${PLAYER}. I thank you, for your efforts could very well make the difference in the future of Glâd Ereg.'This district of Carn Dûm, now called Urugarth, fell to ill-repair due to the foul waters pouring from the sewers of the seat of Angmar. Urugarth was given to the Orcs of Angmar and set as a staging point from which its terrible army could march.This district of Carn Dûm, now called Urugarth, fell to ill-repair due to the foul waters pouring from the sewers of the seat of Angmar. Urugarth was given to the Orcs of Angmar and set as a staging point from which its terrible army could march.From within a cleft nestling in the crags of Urugarth, Dushkal commands a host of trolls.For years, the drake-matron has called the southern crags of Urugarth her home despite efforts to drive her from her roost.Before the gates to Asht-Shâpol lies Lagmas'  Court. From here, the Uruk-captain Lagmas and his second-in-command Morthang rally and muster the forces of Urugarth as they prepare to march upon Eriador.Winding their way down from the sewers of Carn Dûm, the viscous Fell-waters spill into Urugarth, poisoning the air and land around them.The Warg-pack of Urugarth calls this valley home. Led by the goblin-keeper Athpukh and the Warg Lâmkarn, these Wargs are ready to hunt the hills of Angmar at a moment's notice.Asht-Shâpol is the seat of power in Urugarth. Within the remains of this castle resides the Uruk-captain Lagmâs and his flock of deadly crebain.The Elves of Eregion need the help of experienced adventurers.

An Explorer of Eregion has asked you to travel to the Elven outpost of Gwingris in Eregion, to the south of Rivendell.  Once you arrive you should look for the Elf Daegennan.Daegennan is at Gwingris in Eregion.

You were instructed to seek out the Elf Daegennan.Talk to Daegennan in Gwingris'Greetings, ${CLASS}. We have recently received word from our scouts that the outpost Gwingris in Eregion is in need of experienced adventurers like yourself.

'Head to Eregion and look to the north-west.  There you will find our outpost of Gwingris.  When you get there, look for the elf Daegennan.''Well met, friend ${CLASS}. Are you here to help the Elves of Eregion?''${PLAYER}, what are you still doing here? You should heed the call of the Elves of Eregion!''${PLAYER}, what are you still doing here? You should heed the call of the Elves of Eregion!''${PLAYER}, what are you still doing here? You should heed the call of the Elves of Eregion!'The Elves of Eregion need the help of experienced adventurers.

An Explorer of Eregion has asked you to travel to the Elven outpost of Gwingris in Eregion, to the south of Rivendell.  Once you arrive you should look for the Elf Daegennan.Daegennan is at Gwingris in Eregion.

You were instructed to seek out the Elf Daegennan.Talk to Daegennan in Gwingris'Greetings, ${CLASS}. We have recently received word from our scouts that the outpost Gwingris in Eregion is in need of experienced adventurers like yourself.

'Head to Eregion and look to the north-west. There you will find our outpost of Gwingris. When you get there, look for the Elf Daegennan.''Greetings, ${CLASS}. We have recently received word from our scouts that the outpost Gwingris in Eregion is in need of experienced adventurers like yourself.

'Head to Eregion and look to the north-west.  There you will find our outpost of Gwingris.  When you get there, look for the elf Daegennan.''Well met, friend ${CLASS}. Are you here to help the Elves of Eregion?''${PLAYER}, what are you still doing here? You should heed the call of the Elves of Eregion!''${PLAYER}, what are you still doing here? You should heed the call of the Elves of Eregion!'Found along the streets and alleys of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs' soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.Found along the streets and alleys of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs' soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.Found along the streets and alleys of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs's soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.Found along the streets and alleys of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs' soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.Found along the streets and allies of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs's soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.Found along the streets and alleys of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs' soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.Found along the streets and alleys of Urugarth, these foul idols can act as beacons to alert Lagmâs' soldiers to the presence of intruders. Destroying them may raise the ire of the Orcs in Urugarth.The Lossoth of Forochel have found themselves swept up in the rising tide of war in their northern realm, for the Angmarim have incited the ancient hatred between the Lossoth and the Gauredain. Hoping to make an alliance with the Lossoth, representatives of Men and dwarves from the south have come to Forochel to lend their aid.The Gauradan wards run in a line north of the Guaredain encampment Barad Gaurhoth, near Zigilgund. These wards are far across the bay to the south-west of Sûri-kylä.

Vämmä has asked you to make a symbolic attack upon the wards that define the borders of the Gauredain's homeland.Destroy Gauradan territory markers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tell Vämmä that the Guaradan territory markers have been destroyed. He can be found at Sûri-kylä.Speak with Vämmä at Sûri-kylä'For many long winters, we have sparred with the Susi-väki for control of these harsh lands, but we have not truly fought a war in the memories of our fathers or even our grandfathers. Now war comes upon us, unlooked for and uninvited. The Susi-väki assaults have become more vicious and frequent, and dark allies have joined with them -- the Kivi-väki from the South who name themselves Dourhands, as well as the Angmarim from the East.

'More Men and dwarves from the South have also come to us of late, seeking to make an alliance with us. To accept their offers would draw us remorselessly to war, but to reject them may well leave us at the mercy of ancient enemies. So I ask you this: Will you show us your strength in war and strike against the Susi-väki who are our enemies? If so, I would have you attack the boundaries of their ancestral homeland itself, far to the West across the Ice Bay.

'On the southern edge of the great glacier where both of our peoples have long hunted and fought, the Susi-väki have long maintained a line of wards marking the edge of their lands. Burn these wards, and in so doing, let the Susi-väki know that the Lumi-väki and their allies shall make true war upon them -- and stain every flake of snow with blood, if need be -- should they continue their aggression.''Have you destroyed the markers of the Susi-väki yet?''Well done. This act shall declare to the Susi-väki that their aggression shall not go unanswered.'Dunlendings have established a lumber-camp in the wood of Sâd Rechu, where they collect wood for the use of Saruman's craftsmen.Dunlendings can be found to the south-east of Gwingris.

Inufileg has asked you to defeat the Dunlendings who are laying waste to the forest of Sâd Rechu.Defeat Dunlendings south-east of Gwingris (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Inufileg is at Gwingris, north-west of the Dunlending camp.

You should report back to Inufileg regarding your success against the Dunlendings.Talk to Inufileg at Gwingris'While our resources have dwindled, our enemies have grown bolder. Dunlendings have established a lumber-camp to the south-east, just outside of Gwingris.

'There, they are butchering the wood to provide materials to the craftsmen of the traitor, Saruman the White. Their actions cannot go unchecked so close to our own way-post, ${PLAYER}.

'Head to the south-east and look for a small pond... our scouts report a large number of Dunlendings to be gathered there. Defeat as many of them as you can... they must be driven from this land.''There is a small pond to the south-east of Gwingris. Focus your efforts there, then return to me when you have defeated the Dunlendings.''Well done, ${PLAYER}. With the loss of so many of their labourers and guards, the Dunlendings should be thrown into disarray.'Angmarim patrols regularly carry orders and payroll through Imlad Balchorth.Angmarim patrols can be found on the roads in Imlad Balchorth, west of Myrkworth.

Birgir has asked you to waylay Angmarim patrols and recover any orders or money-boxes they may be carrying.'The main route between Carn Dûm and Ram Dúath, which leads to the southern lands, bears much Angmarim traffic. Messengers and patrols move along this path, winding through the haunted ways of Imlad Balchorth.

'Perhaps because of the dread of that place, the traffic through Imlad Balchorth is lightly-guarded. I would ask you to find the Angmarim patrols and messengers and waylay them, bearing back any orders they carry, along with any wealth in the form of pay-boxes they may be carrying.

'I know this speaks of mere brigandry, but on the one hand, the loss of those orders may delay war-like plans of the Angmarim and Orcs already in the North Downs...and on the other hand, without those funds, their trade with some of the less-reputable merchants of the south and east may be affected, and their own men may be unwilling to move if they are not paid. So in this case, to be a highwayman may not be such a bad thing.''Very good. If nothing else, this money allows me to offer you a reward! Perhaps more important are these  orders. They could possess valuable information about troop dispositions in the North Downs.

'I will make sure to look over these orders and make sure they find their way to the Rangers of Esteldín.'Menikko has told you of a lost Lossoth treasure that has now returned to light.Esko is in Pynti-peldot, just south of the great bay.

Menikko has asked you to speak with a man named Esko to learn the story of the lost Lossoth treasure.Talk to Esko in Pynti-peldot'May I have your ear for a moment, ${PLAYER}? I have a matter that I would discuss with you -- one requiring a degree of discretion. You are Sivullinen, an Outsider, and in this matter I cannot trust one of my own kin. It is an issue that runs deep in our history and would cause great turmoil if it was to become widely-known.

'One of the elder tribes of the Lossoth once held an ancient treasure, a symbol of our people's pride and vitality in this harsh land that they had been entrusted with. In time, it was lost, and this loss was a matter of great shame to that tribe -- and indeed all Lossoth. Now it seems that this treasure may at long last be within our reach again, but we must act quickly before our chance is lost.

'If you would aid me, go south and speak with Esko at Pynti-peldot -- he knows the old tales better than anyone, and it is he who learned of the re-emergence of this great treasure. He can set you upon the correct path, if you hurry.''Menikko has sent you to speak with me about our lost treasure? I know of what you speak, but lower your voice lest someone overhear. He speaks of the Jä-koru, the Jewel of Ice -- the oldest treasure of our people, long revered in story and song and lost for many long years now. The Jä-koru is a great shard of ice, but this ice never melts, nor can it be shattered by hammer or chisel. When struck by the sun's rays, it returns the light three-fold in a great array of brilliant colours.

'This was no relic of Man. Our most ancient tales say it was given to our people by a great spirit of the frozen sea to bring light and beauty to our hearts during the coldest winters. The Hylje-heimo lost the Jä-koru as their Elders travelled up from the south. Their shame was so great that never would they speak of how it was lost.

'No matter how it was lost, it may now lie in the hands of the Angmarim. My own folk say that they are promising the Hylje-heimo Elder its return, so long as they ensure that the Council of Elders makes no agreement or alliance with the Men of the South. Any fool can see that our people's days of freedom are numbered should the lands of the South fall beneath the heel of Angmar, and yet the lure of the Jä-koru is so great that the Hylje-heimo Elder does indeed speak foolishly in council. So, ${PLAYER}, for the sake of both our peoples, I think it wise that we attempt to retrieve the Jä-koru before it is too late.'The Lost Fellowship was a brave party that set off from Angmar to find a great treasure in Forochel. Unfortunately, they encountered some trouble along the way....Aid the Champion, DéornydAid the Guardian, NefAid the Burglar, WarstangAid the Captain, Reginald WardAid the Hunter, ThurmaerAid the Lore-master, CenliegAid the Minstrel, BurgthrythHwati in Gabilshathûr thinks his cousin to the north in Myrkworth may have need for your help.Myrkworth lies in Imlad Balchorth atop a high hill, north of Gabilshathûr.

Hwati has asked you to travel to Myrkworth and speak with his cousin, Eiláf.'You have been a great help to us here, ${PLAYER}, but my cousin Eiláf is facing a whole different set of problems up to the north and might have need for your help as well. He is helping to oversee a small outpost we have built there. It is called Myrkworth, and it rests within Imlad Balchorth.

'If you wish to continue to help us, travel to Myrkworth and find my cousin Eiláf. Myrkworth lies north of here atop a high hill in that treacherous land.

'Oh, and if you do make it to Myrkworth, give Eiláf my regards!''Ah, Hwati sent you, did he? A good, solid dwarf, he is! I haven't seen him in far too many days. But there is time for talk when the work is done....

'While Orcs and trolls are an immediate threat to Gabilshathûr, Angmar's Dead are a more subtle and dangerous one. We dwarves have come here to try to find a way to counter this threat.

'Speak to Gautar, Birgir, or Oflár and see if there is something they have for you to do. Perhaps in time, I may have a task for you as well.''My cousin Eiláf can be found in Myrkworth, which lies atop a high hill to the north, in Imlad Balchorth. Beware those lands, for Angmar's Dead walk the earth there!'Gabilshathûr is in danger. A raiding party of Orcs is making their way toward the dwarf-holding and may overrun it.Orcs are moving against Gabilshathûr.

Guard-captain Gisur has asked you to help defend Gabilshathûr against an Orc-attack.Talk to Guard-Captain Gisur when you are readyGuard-captain Gisur is at Gabilshathûr.

You helped stave off the Orc-attack and should speak with Gisur, now that the danger is over.'It seems that defeating those Orcs to the north was not enough. A scout sent word that a band of Blogmal and Krahjarn Orcs are approaching Gabilshathûr! They must have been angered by your assault!

'Please, join the other guards and help defend Gabilshathûr. Hurry! There is little time to prepare!''By Durin's beard! Did you see? That raiding-party was much smaller than any who have raided us in the past! Your efforts weren't for naught. And perhaps -- after the setback they suffered today -- they will think twice about coming here again. Hopefully, their pride will prevent them from telling any of their masters that a few dwarves defeated them so soundly!

'It seems that, for now, we are safe. You have our thanks, ${PLAYER}, and what little reward I can give you, I will.''Look to your weapon, friend! I fear the Orcs may be upon us in a few moments. We can only hope that they are not aware of our force here, and that they are but a small raiding-party.'Vile worms threaten the routes from Rivendell to the Elf-waypost at Gwingris.Worms can be found in the valleys east of Gwingris.

Daegennan has asked you to help clear the routes between Rivendell and Gwingris of worms.Defeat worms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Daegennan is at Gwingris, west of the worm-valleys.

You should return to Daegennan in Gwingris.Talk to Daegennan at Gwingris'Greetings, ${PLAYER}, and welcome to Gwingris.

'As you can see, our outpost is running low on supplies. I am afraid that the next shipment from Rivendell is long overdue, and that without those supplies, we will have a difficult time holding out against the forces of the Enemy.

'Prior deliveries have met with trouble in the valley to the east of here in the form of worms.  If you could take the time to thin their numbers in the valley, our efforts to resupply will proceed more smoothly in the future.''Well done, ${PLAYER}. I think that should thin their numbers enough for our supplies to make it through unharmed for the near future.

'There is still more that can be done, however.''Deliveries made in the past from Rivendell often speak of worms that dwell in the valley far to the east of here. Begin your hunt there.'Esko told you the tale of the Jewel of Ice and how the Angmarim are using it as leverage against the Lossoth.The Angmarim are encamped near the Ironspan, in the mountains of Forochel well to the east of the bay.

Esko has asked you to search the encampments of the Angmarim for some evidence of the Jewel of Ice.Search for evidence of the Jewel of IceThe Angmarim are encamped near the Ironspan, north-east of Pynti-peldot.

Esko has asked you to search the encampments of the Angmarim for some evidence of the Jewel of Ice.Search for evidence of the Jewel of IceEsko is at Pynti-peldot, south-west of the Ironspan.

You found a set of orders that tell when and where the Jewel of Ice will be brought into Forochel. You should bring the orders to Esko at once.'Now that you know the story of the Jä-koru, I think it is time that we address the matter at hand.

'A spy of mine claims that the Angmarim plan to bring the Jä-koru back into Forochel some time soon... but we do not know how or when they might do so.

'As you have likely noticed, there is an encampment of Angmarim near the Ironspan, which is the most likely route they would use to bring the Jä-koru here... assuming it was held in Angmar itself all these years. If you do battle with the Angmarim there, you may be able to find one who knows something of the Jä-koru, or at least evidence of its passage if they have already brought it through.''The Men of Angmar are evil and do us ill to withhold the Jä-koru.''The Men of Angmar are evil and do us ill to withhold the Jä-koru.''Ah! Well done, ${PLAYER}! At last we have the Jä-koru very nearly in our hands!

'According to these papers, it is already here in Forochel -- the Angmarim plan to hold it for some time yet, to hold it over the head of the Hylje-heimo and continue to manipulate the Council until such time as their servants among the Susi-väki and Kivi-väki have hurt us badly enough that we are ready to sue for peace -- and slavery to Angmar. Only then will they relinquish the Jä-koru.

'Obviously we cannot wait while this happens -- if we can capture the Jä-koru for ourselves and present it to the Hylje-heimo in secret, then we can ensure their support in the Council and join forces with the peoples of the South in time to stop this evil before it consumes us all.'The crebain in northern Eregion have established themselves in the land, finding good roosting grounds.The crebain's roosting tree lies in the valley to the east of Gwingris.

Thaliondir has asked you to burn down the roosting tree of the crebain.Destroy the roosting treeThaliondir is at Gwingris, west of the roosting tree.

You should return to Thaliondir with news of your success.Talk to Thaliondir at Gwingris'You have thinned the number of crebain in the area, but unfortunately more will return to replace the ones you killed.

'In order to drive them out entirely, you will need to remove their means of rest and shelter. They undoubtedly have found a large tree in which to roost... as much as I despise the thought, you will need to burn this tree down. Only then will the crebain retreat from Eregion.

'Return to the valley in the east and look for the roosting tree. It will likely be found near a large number of crebain. When you find it, burn it to the ground.''Have you found the roost of the crebain yet, ${PLAYER}? Try the northern edge of the valley far to the east.''Excellent work, ${PLAYER}. With their roost destroyed, the crebain in this region will likely return home to Dunland.'The dwarf Arngrim has vanished after telling some of the other miners that he was sure he knew the location of a mithril vein in the hills of Imlad Balchorth.Arngrim is seeking mithril atop a ridge on the very southern reaches of Imlad Balchorth, north of Gabilshathûr.

Bothwar is concerned that Arngrim, slightly befuddled at the best of times, might have gotten himself into trouble in the southern reaches of Imlad Balchorth.Find ArngrimBothwar is on the bridge in the middle of Gabilshathûr, south of Imlad Balchorth.

You have returned Arngrim to his senses and should report back to Bothwar.'Not everyone here does what he is told, ${PLAYER}: Arngrim is one of these. He has been acting strangely for a while now, but he has not refused direct orders until recently. He was ordered to return to Gabilshathûr for guard-duty against the Orcs, just as I was...only he didn't!

'He has long claimed that there was mithril to be found in this land, though every dwarf knows it be madness, but his delay in returning can only be explained by this cause: he thinks he has found some!

'If you are willing to help me with this one last thing, I will be very pleased. Arngrim may have abandoned his senses, but he is still a dwarf, and we owe him this much. Look for him atop the ridges on the very southern reaches of Imlad Balchorth, to the north of this outpost.''Arngrim should have returned for guard-duty instead of continuing his foolish hunt for mithril in these lands. Look for him north of here, atop the ridges on the very southern reaches of Imlad Balchorth.''You! You are here to steal it, aren't you! My mithril! My beautiful lovely mithril! The voice has told me so. The true sweet voice of the mithril! Nay, get back I say! Do not make me! Don't make me!''You have done well, ${PLAYER}! It is good that Arngrim has abandoned his mad quest...for now, at least.

'Take this reward and rejoice at a job done well. You have earned it!'With the recovery of the swords of Arvedui and Rhudaur, Nethraw has become more confident that the remaining swords may be found.The Sword of Gondor is said to be found somewhere in Maethad, north of the Fane of the Seven Sentinels.

Nethraw has now asked you to search for the Sword of Gondor, in Maethad, a place garrisoned by many Trolls and Gertheryg. For this reason, you were cautioned not to venture there on your own.Nethraw is back at the Fane of the Seven Sentinels, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the Sword of Gondor to Nethraw.'The third sword is called the Sword of Gondor. Of the host that fought the Witch-king at Fornost, many were knights and men-at-arms of Gondor, led by Eärnur, Prince of Gondor. One doughty knight, whose name has been lost, would not be outdone by the knights of Arthedain, and vowed to keep a vigil along with them.

'According to the tale, his watch-tower was at a place not far from here, called Maethad. Unfortunately, this place is infested with evil trolls, and worse, by the mighty Gertheryg, who are fearsome masters of arms.

'It would be all but impossible for you to venture into Maethad on your own, so if you would seek the third sword, I would counsel you to venture to the evil place only with stout companions.''It will be very difficult to recover the Sword of Gondor if it is still to be found in Maethad.''You have impressed me mightily. The recovery of the Sword of Gondor is a great triumph over the evil creatures of Angmar, even if none of the other swords are recovered at all!'With every sword you find, Nethraw becomes more determined to find the others.The Sword of Slaughter is said to be found in a ruin in some high hills west of the large lake in Imlad Balchorth..

Nethraw has sent you to recover the Sword of Slaughter. You have been cautioned that it will be difficult to obtain without allies.Nethraw is back at the Fane of the Seven Sentinels, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the Sword of Slaughter to Nethraw.'The fourth sword is the Sword of Slaughter. The tale is unclear on this point, but I believe the knight who bore it was sickened by the carnage of the Battle of Fornost, and named his blade such because of the many corpses it left behind.

'The tale leads me to believe that this knight kept watch in the middle of one of the most dread areas of Angmar, in some high ruins a far distance west of the central island of Imlad Balchorth. Even then this region was known as a place of dread, a suitable place for this sword to be hidden.

'Again, I must warn you that allies will probably be required to recover this sword.''I hesitate to ask you to venture into the accursed land of Imlad Balchorth merely for the sake of an old tale, but it seems there is some truth to the old story.''Four of the seven swords! Well done! But I fear the final three are in yet more dangerous parts of Angmar.'Four of the seven swords have been recovered, leaving only three.The Sword of Sorrow is said to be found in drake-infested heights east of the Fane of the Seven Sentinels.

You have been sent to recover the Sword of Sorrow from its resting-place. Nethraw again suggests you take allies.Nethraw is back at the Fane of the Seven Sentinels, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the Sword of Sorrow to Nethraw.'Next comes the Sword of Sorrow. This sword was carried by a knight who lost his lands and all his kin to the Angmarim. He swore his oath while bearing a terrible burden of sorrow and hatred.

'The tale says that this knight set up his tower not far east of here, in heights infested by evil drakes. These fell creatures are too fierce for any to fight on even terms, so yet again I must ask you to bring allies with you in the search for the sword.''The Sword of Sorrow lies to the east, so the tale tells.''We are near the end of our quest. There are but two swords left to collect!'Oflár is disturbed by the news of the wights spreading throughout Imlad Balchorth and has developed a plan to get rid of them.The nightshade plant grows north and east of Maethad, to the south-east of Myrkworth.

Oflár has asked you to collect Nightshade plants to prepare a tincture that he hopes will prove useful against the wights of Imlad Balchorth.'Greetings. I have heard your name in dispatches from Gabilshathûr. There is much work to be done here, so I must encourage you to pay attention. West of here there is a region of dread and darkness called Imlad Balchorth. It is largely inhabited, if that is the word, by terrible wights and fell spirits summoned from the world of shadow. It is most unnatural for such creatures to be seen abroad...something must be summoning them, binding them, causing them to wander in that place.

'Whatever the cause, these terrible creatures are an abomination and a terrible, growing threat to all the Free Peoples. It seems that Angmar is breeding an army of the Dead to bring against the lands of the North.

'To the north and east of the troll-infested Maethad, which is just south of here, grows the poisonous herb nightshade. It is said that this plant is a simple which has virtue to ward against the terrible wights when steeped in a certain rare liquor I have obtained from the Rangers at Gath Forthnír. Please gather nightshade-plants and bring them to me, and I will prepare a tincture for you to help with this menace.''Very good. The tincture should be easy enough to prepare. With the liquor I already possess, I will have it ready in but a moment....'Ilmo was grateful for your assistance against the peikko, but troubles seem ever ready to assail him.The wayward bull may be nearby, among the mountains north of Sûri-kylä and Ilmo's camp.

Ilmo fears that his prize bull has been taken by the peikko and has asked you to search for him.Find the wayward bullIlmo is outside of Sûri-kylä, north from its gates.

You should return to Ilmo and let him know that his bull has been returned safely.Talk to Ilmo'The most cherished bull of my herd has been missing for some time. I fear the peikko have taken him, but I cannot be sure. He is a powerful fighter and oft would put up a great call when the peikko attacked, but his call has been silent for a long while.

'I saw him grazing last to the north, past the hirvi-herd among the hills. If you would go there, perhaps you will find some sign of his passing.

'Find the hirvi-bull, even just word of him, and your reward will be great!''You have returned my bull to me unharmed! I owe you much. I will spread the word of your good deeds throughout the camps of the Lossoth. You are truly a ${RACE} of great character.''Have you found my bull? I do hope that he has simply become lost and has not fallen to the storms...or worse. He was last seen wandering north of here, near to the hills north of Sûri-kylä.'The bull looks like he is willing to follow you.Maedlathron is concerned that the hobbits or Gimli the dwarf may have dropped something else upon their journey which may divulge to the Enemy the way of their passing and the composition of the Company.Golgallon is at Echad Eregion, far to the south-east of Gwingris.

Maedlathron has asked you to warn Golgallon that the Company may have left further signs of their passing in the wild.Talk to Golgallon at Echad Eregion'${PLAYER}, I fear there is little time to lose. Go to Echad Eregion, far to the south and east, and speak with Golgallon. Warn him that there may yet be signs of the Company's passing in the wild.''If only these were new tidings you bring me, ${PLAYER}. Alas, we have ourselves seen signs of the Company's passing. I fear they have already been discovered.

'Nevertheless, we must take no chances. There is much work to be done....''Speak with my kinsman Golgallon at Echad Eregion. Warn him that the Company's path may be compromised by the carelessness of the halflings.'You found Nef's shield, but it had been broken by the beast that drove him off.The Lossoth craftsmen are in the Great Lodge in Sûri-kylä, west of Nef.

Nef has asked you to speak with the Lossoth craftsmen to learn if they are able to repair his broken shield.Talk to the Lossoth craftsmenNef is in Sûri-kylä, east from the Great Lodge.

You should return to Nef and tell him what you learned from Rauno.Talk to NefRauno is in Sûri-kylä, west from Nef in the Great Lodge of Sûri-kylä.

Nef has asked you to return to Rauno and try to convince him to craft a new shield for him.Hard bone can be found on beasts throughout Jä-rannit. Worm scale can be found on worms throughout Jä-rannit.

Rauno has asked you to collect hard bones and worm scales so that he may make a shield for Nef.Collect hard bones  (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect worm scales (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rauno is in Sûri-kylä, north from where you collected the items he requested.

You should bring the hard bones and worm scales to Rauno so he can complete Nef's shield.Rauno is in Sûri-kylä.

You should speak with Rauno to see if Nef's shield is ready.Get the shield from RaunoNef is in Sûri-kylä, east from Rauno.

Rauno told you to take the shield he made to Nef and bid him leave Sûri-kylä.'Bah! This will not do at all! What is a guardian without his shield? He is but half a dwarf, I reckon. I will not be satisfied until I have my shield repaired.

'Go and speak with the Lossoth craftsmen here in Sûri-kylä. They are strange Men, but perhaps they will be able to repair it. Otherwise, I do not know what I will do.

'The Lossoth craftsmen can be found inside the Great Lodge in Sûri-kylä. Now, run along! I will not have you waste any more time hovering about like a gnat.''I am sure it won't be as good as new, but perhaps the Snow-men will repair my shield.''I wonder that this shield remained unsundered for as long as it did, ${RACE}. Its craftsmanship is very poor and the materials from which it is made are of poor quality.

'I will not repair this worthless shield. I would bring shame upon myself and my family if I did so.''I would be shamed if I repaired this shield, for it is an unworthy piece.''Shame? Why, the gall of that Man! I would go and speak with Rauno myself, if I had not already had a falling out with him.

'I innocently inquired after the making of his craft, but he would not speak on the subject. We exchanged a number of words, and now he will not say a word to me! He is an evil-tempered lout; I would not think his silence a great loss were it not for my present need.

'Perhaps you could speak with him and see if he will give me one of the sturdy shields of the Lossoth. I will not be able to travel back to the South without a new shield!''Had I another choice, I would not ask the Snow-men for another shield, but there are no other smiths to do the work.''I do not cherish the thought of making a shield for Nef. He came to me with wild requests, and when I told him what I had at hand for materials, he said many things that I will not repeat now. I do not need to offend your ears also.

'But if he will not leave until his shield is completed, I will make it, if only to see him depart. The items that I require would be hard-bone and worm scale. Both are rare, and both are vital to the making of kilpi -- shields, as the Kivi-väki calls them.

'Hard bone can be found on many of the beasts throughout Jä-rannit, while worm scale should be found on the worms of the same region. Return to me when you have found these items, and I will craft the shield for the small outsider with the long beard and bad temper.''If I am to make a new kilpi for this Nef, I will need hard bone and worm-scales.''Is the shield finished? What did Rauno say?''You have returned with the hard bones and worm scales? I will begin work on the kilpi right away. It should not take long, as I have already completed much of the work while you were away.''The kilpi is finished. Take it to Nef, and bid him to seek his companions and return to his own home.''I hope never to see the short one ever again.''Well now...this looks like a decent piece of work.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. This should do, though I will want to replace it with something a bit more durable once I reach Bree again.'You restocked the Kantâ-vilkku, but Matti fears that there may be damage to the other beacons.The Kantâ-vilkku is south of Sûri-kylä in Jä-rannit along the snow drifts over the bay of Forochel.

Matti has told you to search the broken crates for signs of the animal which attacked the Kantâ-vilkku, then track it down and slay it.Matti is at Sûri-kylä, north of the Kantâ-vilkku.

You should return to Matti with word of your success.Talk to Matti'The animals have become so starved that they have attacked the snow-beacon of Kantâ-vilkku. I do not know what could have been gained from this behaviour, but it is done.

'You should go to Kantâ-vilkku and see what damage has been done. Search any broken crates you find for signs of the animal. You should hunt it down. It must be slain for the good of the Lumi-väki.

'Go now, and good hunting, ${PLAYER}.''Have you found the animal which attacked the Kantâ-vilkku, ${PLAYER}? We must find the beast before more supplies are lost. Search the Kantâ-vilkku south of Sûri-kylä for signs of its passing.''A great boar you say? Rumours of a terrible horned beast have been told by our camp-fires before, but never have I seen such a monster!

'Tales tell that he was driven out of the south-lands and maddened in the cold. It is well that he has been slain.'The Orcs were further along in their plans to build siege-weapons with which to assault Gabilshathûr than Avar thought. He told you that they had built two siege weapons and were hiding them within the caves of Grishbalt.The caves of Grishbalt lie north of Gabilshathûr, along the east side of Duvuinen.

Avar asked you to find and destroy the two siege-weapons the Orcs have hidden there.Find a torch to burn the siege-towerThe caves of Grishbalt lie north of Gabilshathûr, along the east side of Duvuinen.

Avar asked you to find and destroy the two siege-weapons the Orcs have hidden there.Find a torch to burn the catapultAvar is at Gabilshathûr, south of Duvuinen.

You should return to the Avar with word of your success.'A very brave dwarf-scout returned to Gabilshathûr and confirmed what I feared: the Orcs are building siege-weapons. In fact, they have already completed some of them! It is only a matter of time before they bring them to bear up on us.

'We have had two pieces of luck though. The Orcs have hidden these weapons well in the caves of Grishbalt north of here. However, the dwarf-scout saw them bring a load of lumber into the caves and carefully followed them in. The second piece of luck is that the Orcs have built only a single siege-tower and a single catapult. That makes the task easier... although only a little.

'The caves of Grishbalt are to the north of here, along the east side of Duvuinen. The caves are infested with Orcs and trolls, so bring allies to help you.'After examining the catapult, you determine that the only way to destroy it is with fire.After examining the siege-tower, you determine the only way to destroy it is with fire.'Hurry, there is no time to waste. Gather your allies and travel to the caves of Grishbalt north of here and destroy the siege-weapons found there!''Well done, ${CLASS}! You have bought us at least a temporary respite from the Orcs. It will not be long before they are at it again, I fear, but at least for the moment we are safe.'A terrible wind has come from Angmar, bringing with it a freezing winter.The bear, Kylmä-Säri can be found south east along the ice bay.  Tuima-kita, the Peikko can be found north of Sûri-kylä.Nôra is at Sûri-kylä.

You should speak with Nôra and tell her of your victory.Talk to Nôra'When I was a young girl, I would often play far afield in the early weeks of winter. Now the winds are harsh, and snowstorms spring up and ensnare those careless enough not to pay heed to the ever-shifting winds.

'These harsh winters have driven many beasts to the brink of death. The hunger and cold make them more dangerous than ever. I would ask you to go and hunt these animals, for I fear they might encroach upon Sûri-kylä and do terrible damage to my people.

'The bear, Kylmä-Säri can be found south east along the ice bay.  Tuima-kita, the Peikko can be found north of Sûri-kylä.''You have accomplished the task I gave you? Good! Perhaps now our children will once again be able to play near the village without fear of being attacked.''Have you culled Kylmä-Säri and Tuima-kita? They can be found south and north of Sûri-kylä.'Rammas Deluon prevents the brothers Eilig, Machar, and Torquil from seeing their uncle Donaith, who lives far in the east of Angmar.Donaith is somewhere far to the east of Aughaire, well beyond Rammas Deluon.

Eilig and his brothers in Aughaire wish you to find their long-lost uncle.'My brothers and I have not seen our uncle for many years. Rammas Deluon prevents safe passage to the lands beyond, and Uncle Donaith lives far to the east.

'If you find yourself in those distant lands, perhaps you may pay Uncle Donaith a visit? I am sure he would want to hear from us. It has been a long time, and much has happened in his absence.

'I do not know where he is now, only that he dwells far in the east.''It is good to meet you as well, ${PLAYER}. And you bring news from Aughaire? What a journey that must have been! And how did you pass through Rammas Deluon?

'Ah, but it matters not. Tell me, how do my nephews fare?''Our uncle, Donaig, is lost somewhere beyond Rammas Deluon...please find him.'While the worms of Pembar are likely led by a matron, there can be no offspring without a sire.Pembar is north-east of Echad Eregion and south-east of Glâd Ereg.

Faenhilith has asked you to travel to Pembar and slay the worm-sire Nethgarch.Defeat Nethgarch, Worm-sireFaenhilith is in Echad Eregion, south-west of Pembar.

You should return to Faenhilith with news of your victory against Nethgarch.Talk to Faenhilith at Echad Eregion'The ruins of Pembar are a sad place, once filled with the music of harps and the soft tap of jeweller's hammers. Now it is filled with the stench of rotten flesh and evil beasts.

'One of these monsters has been spreading the filth of his kind throughout the ruins of Pembar, a great worm: Nethgarch. Go to Pembar and seek out this worm-sire. Without him, this line of worms will fail.

'Pembar is north-east of Echad Eregion and south-east of Glâd Ereg. He will be lurking somewhere within the ruins.''The beast is slain? The worms have never encroached this far south; it is ill tidings for one who still dwells east of the Sea.

'But you have accomplished much, ${PLAYER}, and should be rewarded for your valour.''Nethgarch may be found at Pembar, north-east of Echad Eregion and south-east of Glâd Ereg.'One of the half-orcs of Barad Morlas was carrying an ornate necklace, you should return it to Ladenthir.Ladenthir is in Echad Eregion, south of High Hollin.

You should bring the necklace to Ladenthir for further inspection.This necklace is encrusted with grime and filth, but it glows with a radiance that shines through the stains.

After wiping off some of the dirt, you can see that it is an Elf-necklace made by a skilled hand. You should bring it to Ladenthir in Echad Eregion. He may be able to shed more light on its origin.'You did well bringing me this treasure. It is a relic of the Second Age of this world, made by a smith of the Noldor...its beauty could be mistaken for no other craftsman's work.

'I will send this treasure to Rivendell. Maybe there, Master Elrond can identify the exact smith who wrought it, and it can be cleaned and restored by Elves of his household; though doubtless it will never be restored to its full glory after its long neglect and mistreatment by the servants of Saruman.'You recovered many relics and trinkets from the half-orcs at Pembar, but Faenhilith is wary still.The summoning horn is inside the southern entrance to the ruins of Pembar, north-east of Echad Eregion.

Faenhilith has asked you to return to Pembar and use the Orkish summoning horn to challenge the leader of the half-orcs.Faenhilith is at Echad Eregion, south-west of Pembar.

You should return to Faenhilith with news of your victory against Fauldush.Talk to Faenhilith at Echad Eregion'${PLAYER}, while these half-orcs have a much greater cunning by far than common Orcs, they do not have the wherewithal to maintain discipline. They are greedy and unruly.

'I fear there is a powerful leader among them, who is utterly loyal to the will of Saruman. The half-orcs will not be dismayed from their task as long as this leader lives. I must ask you to return to Pembar and draw forth this leader.

'There is a horn of summoning in Pembar, within the southern entrance to the ruins, north-east of here. While it is a distasteful notion, blow upon the horn... the leader will no doubt answer your challenge.''Even among that foul breed, there is pride. If you blow upon the summoning horn at Pembar, the leader of the half-orcs will surely answer your challenge.''What is this you say? These half-orcs were led by some foul breed of Orc you have never before seen?

'Neither have I heard of the Uruk-hai. It can only be the product of some other agent of the enemy as yet unrevealed to us.

'Your efforts are appreciated, ${PLAYER}, but I fear more terrible things are yet to come....'An alliance of enemies plots against Ered Luin from within the ancient ruins of Sarnúr, as the traitorous Dourhands seek to bring an array of forces to bear against their hated cousins the Longbeards. They seek to claim legitimacy by holding the seat of an ancient dwarf-city, but their presence in the company of such evils only defiles the sanctity of their ancestors.Defeat Brúllug in the ruins of SarnúrDefeat Glúmir in the ruins of SarnúrDefeat Latub in the ruins of SarnúrDefeat Northpaw in the ruins of SarnúrDefeat Icerender in the ruins of SarnúrDefeat Gâdhûp in the ruins of SarnúrDespite the number of fell spirits you have banished from Imlad Balchorth, there never seems to be an end to them.Fell spirits may be found in Imlad Balchorth, west of Myrkworth.

Sigar has asked you to destroy the fell spirits in Imlad Balchorth. You can find them wandering Imlad Balchorth.Defeat fell spirits in Imlad Balchorth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigar is at Myrkworth, east of Imlad Balchorth.

Having destroyed the fell spirits as asked, you should return to Sigar in Myrkworth and tell him the deed is done.'It is a hard time here in Angmar. I miss the warmth of my home. The sooner we stop the Angmarim, the sooner we can leave this accursed place! I need you to help me as you are helping the others.

'There are many fell spirits in Imlad Balchorth, and they give rise to more wights for Angmar's army. Let us thin their numbers, shall we? It gives me a chill that they even exist!

'If we can reduce their number, perhaps we can make our way back to friendly lands more quickly.''You have lessened my burden and helped me to sleep better at night, I hope. Thank you, good ${RACE}. I look forward to working with you again!''The fell spirits of Morgoth dwell in Imlad Balchorth...I fear we will never escape this place unless they are banished.'Bogbereth the Spider is in league with the Iron Crown. Her progeny is kept by the Angmarim and used as many-legged foot-soldiers.The spiders are to the north-east of Himbar.

Lunathron told you of Bogbereth's spawn, the spider-broodlings that are kept by the Angmarim.Defeat Bogbereth's broodlings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat matrons, lurkers, and creepers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lunathron is at Gath Forthnír, to the east of Himbar.

You slew many spiders and their Angmarim keepers, ensuring that Bogbereth's spawn pose no threat to the Rangers. Now you should return to Lunathron.'There is a great spider that lurks in Himbar. Her eggs are prized by the Iron Crown, as the hatchlings can be trained by the Angmarim to fight for the Enemy.

'You must kill the spiders and their elder protectors north-east of Himbar, in Torech-i-Bogbereth. When facing the broodlings, beware. They can burrow into the earth and escape your attacks.

'You must exercise caution while hunting the spiders. Bogbereth stalks those lands, and she may be hunting you as well.''Well done, ${CLASS}, but there is one threat that still remains. I speak of the great spider herself, Bogbereth.''The spiders are a threat and must be stopped. Defeat them and their elder watchers.'After retrieving Thrór's Hammer and the necessary materials, Dwalin's smith, Litli, forged for you a mithril key that should allow you to pass the gates of Carn Dûm.Lorniel awaits your return at Gath Forthnír in eastern Angmar.

You should return with the key to Lorniel.'A key to Carn Dûm... who would have dreamed anyone would wish to enter that accursed city.

'Well, there is little else that I can do for you. You should return now to your task.

'Good fortune to you, ${PLAYER}.'Good fortune to you, ${PLAYER}. You and your friends in Angmar will need it.''That key should do all that you need, ${PLAYER}. You should return now to your task and make use of it!''You have returned with the key! Let us hope, my friend, that the key from which this was cast was not a false guide....'With the key to Carn Dûm in hand, the time has come to move against the forces of the False King and try to rescue Golodir.Lorniel awaits you at Gath Forthnír in eastern Angmar.

Lorniel told you to speak with her again when you were ready to begin your raid on Carn Dûm.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Talk to LornielGolodir is at Gath Forthnír.

You have rescued Golodir, but at a terrible cost. The False King, Mordirith, appeared and slew her. You were able to weaken Mordirith and escape with Golodir back to Gath Forthnír.'Your efforts have renewed my hope, ${PLAYER}. The time has come to move against the False King. Fortunately, I have just received word from Raghnall that my father was being moved outside of Carn Dûm to Rhunendin. With the key you have forged, no door will be barred to us. We will never have a better chance to free my father!

'Are you with me? If so, speak with me again when you are ready to leave.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Are you ready to leave, ${PLAYER}? Who knows how long we have before my father is moved once more.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Why did you come? Why did you allow Lorniel to come? I would sooner have dwelt there in torment for a thousand years, than to see my only daughter perish at the gates of that forsaken place....

'Here, I took this accursed key from Lorniel before we left her behind... but, no, I no longer wish it. Take it!'The assault on Carn Dûm saw the freedom of the Ranger Golodir, but proved fruitless as Lorniel perished, and Mordirith, the Steward of Angmar, withdrew into the fastness of the city.Laerdan is at Gath Forthnír.

Golodir, crushed by his daughter's death, told you to leave him be and to go speak with the Elf Laerdan.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Golodir's gear was given to emissaries from the goblins, dwarves and nomads of Angmar. These emissaries can be found in the spires surrounding Barad Gúlaran to the south of Gath Forthnír.

As a first step in his plan to renew Golodir's spirit, Laerdan has asked you to retrieve the Ranger's weapons and gear from the emissaries within the towers of Barad Gúlaran.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'I have nothing left, thanks to your carelessness. If you wish to speak with someone, speak with Laerdan. He will have to continue the fight on now, without me.

'Now leave me.' 

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Ah, ${PLAYER}. It is good that you have returned. I have known Golodir for many years, but never have I seen his spirit so broken. Though Lorniel's death was perhaps the final stone to be cast at his shattered heart, I sense there is something more dire at the root of his despair.

'We must reforge Golodir's spirit, my friend...though I know not how. I have only a dim flicker of a plan, but perhaps it will work.

Golodir was a mighty warrior, and he could be reminded of that past if he were to have his weapons again. They were taken from him, and given as gifts to the emissaries of the tribes of Angmar, as tokens of Mordirith's victory over the Free Peoples. Each of these emissaries occupies a tower at Barad Gúlaran to the south. If we are fortunate, you will find Golodir's weapons and gear on these emissaries.''Leave me be, ${RACE}. My life and my joy have been stripped from me.''Leave me be! I have no interest in your folly.''So you have returned and with success. Alas, this gear is no longer fit for use. I fear the flicker of my plan is soon to be extinguished.

'Still, there may be some hope left.'You managed to recover Golodir's equipment, but as Laerdan pointed out to you, the gear was corroded and decayed beyond use.Daervunn is at Esteldín in the North Downs, far to the south and west of Angmar.

Laerdan instructed you to take Golodir's equipment to the Ranger Daervunn to be repaired.Toram is in Esteldín.

Daervunn felt he could repair Golodir's shirt, but the sword and shield were beyond his ability. He asked you to speak to Toram in Esteldín to have those items repaired.Reclaim Golodir's sword and shield from Toram in Esteldín.

Daervunn asked you to take Golodir's sword and shield to Toram, an armour-smith in Esteldín, for repair.Daervunn is at Esteldín.

You should return to Daervunn to collect Golodir's shirt and to see if he has any thoughts on how to repair Golodir's sword.'What hope remains to Golodir must lie with his own people. It may be that they can repair this gear or somehow draw from it, something that may renew Golodir.

'Take this equipment to the hidden Ranger encampment of Esteldín in the North Downs -- I am sure you have been there before. Tell them Laerdan of Eregion sent you.

'Time is wasting for Golodir!''You should not tarry here, but make your way to Esteldín. Perhaps there Golodir's friend Daervunn may be able to repair his gear.''Golodir's equipment? I will help however I can.

'Hmm, yes. I believe I can repair the damage to his mail shirt, but repairing the shield and the sword is beyond my ability. I think though one of the armour-smiths here in Esteldín will be able to help. Go and take the sword and shield to Toram. While you do this, I will set to work on the shirt.''You should not tarry here, but make your way to Esteldín. Perhaps there Golodir's friend Daervunn may be able to repair his gear.''I will repair the shirt, but the sword and shield are beyond my ability. Take them to Toram, one of Esteldín's armour-smiths. He should be able to fix them.''So Golodir lives? It is an amazing thing. I had thought him long dead, yet another victim of Angmar. It is a shame though that his freedom was won at the price of his daughter's life. Lorniel, was a gift. Beautiful in spirit and form. What a shame.

'Ah, I have gone on too long. Let me see that sword and shield. I will do what I can to help Golodir.''You should not tarry here, but make your way to Esteldín. Perhaps there his friend Daervunn may be able to repair Golodir's gear.''I am nearly done with the shirt, but you should go see Toram and reclaim Golodir's sword and shield.''Well, the shield was not a problem, although I have never seen one quite like it. This was not Golodir's shield, it is much older and bears the markings of the South Kingdom. Very interesting... whatever the case, it will serve him well.

'The sword though, well that is another matter. It was forged by the Elves long ago and is beyond my ability to repair. I am sorry, but I have done what I can.

'Perhaps Daervunn will have another thought on how to repair Golodir's sword.''You should not tarry here, but make your way to Esteldín. Perhaps there Golodir's friend Daervunn may be able to repair his gear.''I have done all I can for you. That sword is beyond my ability to repair. I would speak to Daervunn, if I were you. He may have some thoughts on how it may yet be repaired.''Fortunately, without too much difficulty, I have been able to repair the shirt. I am disappointed though that Toram was unable to repair the sword. At least he was able to repair the shield.

'It is interesting that the shield you found was not Golodir's, but is of the South Kingdom. It is quite a mystery... but one that must be solved another time, I suppose.

'Returning to the sword, if we are to have it reforged, I can think of but one place where there are Elf-smiths of sufficient skill to repair it.'The Rangers of Esteldín were able to repair Golodir's shield and mail shirt, but the sword was of Elf-craft beyond their skill.Elrond is at Rivendell in the Trollshaws.

Daervunn suggested you take Golodir's sword to Elrond of Rivendell to be reforged.Bring Golodir's gear to ElrondHemeldir can be found near Rivendell's forges.

Elrond instructed you to take Golodir's sword to his chief smith, Hemeldir, to be reforged.Hemeldir can be found near Rivendell's forges.

You should check with Hemeldir to learn if he has finished reforging Golodir's sword.Collect Golodir's sword from HemeldirElrond is in Rivendell.

Hemeldir returned Golodir's reforged sword to you and told you to bring it to Elrond for approval.'Here, take Golodir's gear, and travel to Rivendell and speak to Master Elrond. I doubt not that he will be able to assist you. His smiths are among the greatest of the Elf-smiths of this day.

'Do not tarry here. If this will help to renew Golodir's broken spirit, then you must make haste to Rivendell!''We haven't the ability to reforge this sword. You should take it to Elrond in Rivendell.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, your tale saddens me, for I know Golodir, and he was a good man. It pleases me to know that he lives, but it grieves me that this False King was able to break him so.

'That a wraith has returned to power in Angmar troubles me even more. I have never heard of Mordirith... that is a mystery upon which I must dwell.

'Now go to Hemeldir, my chief smith, and instruct him to reforge Golodir's sword. You will find him near Imladris' forges.''We haven't the ability to reforge this sword. You should take it to Elrond in Rivendell.''Hemeldir, my chief smith, will reforge Golodir's sword.''Master Elrond requires this sword to be reforged? Very well. It is a minor essay in Elf-craft... it will not take long.

'But what is this? I daresay this shield did not belong to your friend Golodir. This shield is far older, from near the fall of the North Kingdom. It is also from Gondor, not the North. Indeed, it looks familiar... I seem to recall its like among the royal line of Gondor.

'I will speak to Master Elrond about this later. For now, I will turn my hand to the task required of me.''You left Golodir's sword in Rivendell? You go there at once and retrieve it!''I am certain Hemeldir has finished reforging Golodir's sword. You should retrieve it from him and return to me.''The work is done, ${PLAYER}. It little resembles the weapon from which it was birthed, but I believe it will better serve its master.

'Take it now to Master Elrond for his approval.''You left Golodir's sword in Rivendell? You go there at once and retrieve it!''Master Elrond will want to inspect that sword before you return it to Golodir.''As always, Hemeldir's mastery shows forth in the work of his hand. This sword shall henceforth be known as "Dúnachar," Avenger of the West, for it shall avenge the death of Lorniel, daughter of Golodir.'Elrond renamed Golodir's sword Dúnachar, Avenger of the West, and prophesied that it would one day avenge the death of Lorniel, Golodir's daughter.Laerdan is at Gath Forthnír in the eastern reaches of Himbar.

Elrond gave you the sword Dúnachar and told you to take it and the rest of Golodir's gear to Laerdan.Golodir is at Gath Forthnír in the eastern reaches of Himbar.

Laerdan instructed you to return Golodir's gear to him.'Dúnachar is a fine sword, and I foresee that one day the Steward of Angmar will rue its reforging.

'Go now. Return to Laerdan and bring him Golodir's gear. I hope that Golodir will see his despair for what it is -- the machinations of the Enemy.

'Laerdan awaits you at Gath Forthnír in Angmar.''Make haste, ${PLAYER}. Laerdan awaits your return.''You have returned, ${PLAYER}! What news? The sword has been reforged? Dúnachar it is named... yes, I can feel the virtue within it.

'And what did you say about the shield? It is not Golodir's? It shall have to make do.

'Take the gear to Golodir in his quarters.''Bring Golodir his gear... I hope that it will spark at least a small flicker of the bright flame he once was.''You should return at once to Laerdan and take him Golodir's gear.''What is this? My mail shirt? I thought it lost forever! And this shield, it is not mine, but it is a thing of wondrous beauty!

'But this sword... is this my sword? It was forged long ago by the Elves of Rivendell, an heirloom of my family. I thought it lost forever, but now it is returned as Dúnachar!

'It is well-named Avenger of the West. Indeed, it will avenge my daughter....'The priests and priestesses of the cult of Angmar have a strong influence over many of the Hillman-tribes.The priests and priestesses of Angmar can be found near the altars throughout the Bails of Himbar, which lie to the west of Gath Forthnír.

Maerchiniath has instructed you to go forth and defeat the priests of Angmar, bringing him their staves as evidence of your success.Collect Angmarim staves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gath Forthnír is to the east of Himbar.

You should return to Maerchiniath with the staves you took from the priests of Angmar.'The Hillmen of Angmar are a wild folk and prone to superstition. The priests and priestesses of the cult of the Angmarim are able to use honeyed words to exert a great influence over them.

'If we are to win the war against Angmar, we must put a halt to the confusions of the Enemy. The priests of Angmar must not be allowed to spread their evil lies.

'You will find them near the altars throughout the Bails of Himbar to the west. Bring me back five of their staves as proof of your deed.''Have you confronted all the priests and priestesses of Angmar in the Bails? If not, you should continue at once!''Well done, indeed. Hopefully, this will keep Angmar at bay for a while longer.'Esko has told you that the Hylje-heimo may be swayed to side with the Free Peoples of the South if the Jewel of Ice can be recovered.Roibeard is at the Angmarim encampments near the Ironspan, north-east of Pynti-peldot.

Esko has told you where the Angmarim are holding the Jewel of Ice. You must retrieve it before it can be returned to the vaults of Angmar.'The orders you captured from the Angmarim have made several things clear now. The Angmarim do indeed hold the Jä-koru, but they have no intention of turning it over until Forochel lies safely within their grasp.

'Nevertheless, to silence the doubts of the Hylje-heimo, they were recently obliged to bring the Jä-koru into Forochel to prove their claim. Now is the time to strike and reclaim the Jä-koru before they return it to some dark vault in Angmar. It is in the keeping of one of their most trusted captains, a man named Roibeard. He has made camp somewhere within the fortifications of the Ironspan.

'I must ask you to infiltrate that fortress by whatever means possible and wrest the Jä-koru from him.''Can it truly be? We had thought ourselves cursed by fate and never to see it again, but you have truly brought it -- the Jä-koru!'

'${PLAYER}, your name will go on in song and story for many generations to come as the one who restored to us our greatest treasure!'The Rangers at Gath Forthnír await a delivery of albino skin to be made to Areneth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver albino skin from the beasts near Gath Forthnír to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-camp.Wanted: Albino skin from the beasts and worms near Gath Forthnír. Delivery of the items should be made to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-outpost.Wanted: Albino skin from the beasts and worms near Gath Forthnír.'You have our thanks.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Rangers at Gath Forthnír await a delivery of jewelled sword sheaths to be made to Areneth.You have accepted a task to collect and deliver jewelled sword sheaths from the intelligent creatures near Gath Forthnír to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-camp.Wanted: Jewelled sword sheaths from the intelligent creatures near Gath Forthnír. Deilvery of the items should be made to Areneth at the hidden Ranger-outpost.Wanted: Jewelled sword sheaths from the intelligent creatures near Gath Forthnír.'These shall house our weapons now.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The Council of the North has need of tokens to bestow upon heroes of the war against Angmar.Hróar is at Gath Forthnír in Angmar.

Hróar has asked you to craft some Crests of Valour and bring them to him.'I have need of your help in another matter of metalsmithy. To our most esteemed heroes, we bestow a medal called the Crest of Valour, but it has been some time since we have had amongst us an artisan with the skill to craft these intricate devices. If you could find the time and resources to craft a few of these crests for us, we would be much obliged by your efforts on our behalf.''Ah, Crests of Valour, and finely made too. Our own smiths could craft no finer. It is a shame that so many of these medals will be awarded posthumously, but the honour of the fallen is as important as the honour of the living...perhaps more so, as it is all that remains to them.'The pools of Imlad Balchorth are noxious pits of foul water. No one knows if the corruption stems from the Enemy's industry or from a more sinister source.The pools of Imlad Balchorth are to the west of Myrkworth.

The waters in the western parts of Imlad Balchorth are infused with deadly poison, and Thorod wishes to determine the cause.Thorod is in Myrkworth, east of Imlad Balchorth.

Your phials are filled with the deadly water from the roiling pools of Imlad Balchorth.'This water appears to addle the senses but does little else. The Rangers have stated that the water is deadly poison. Perhaps they refer to the western pools?

'If their words are true, collecting the water from the western pools will prove most hazardous. You must be careful lest a single drop touch your skin. The stuff is deadly poison and will stop your heart in your chest.

'Venture back to Imlad Balchorth and draw water from the poisonous, roiling floods of the western pools. It may provide the answers I seek.''Draw water from the patches of roiling water that lay in the western parts of Imlad Balchorth. Be wary, for the water is quite dangerous!''Even the smell of the water is enough to cause illness, it seems. I have some thoughts about this water, but there is only one way to bear them out.'Calelloth believes news of the half-orcs' dealings with the worms of Eregion should be conveyed throughout the Elf-camps.Lengliel is at Echad Eregion, south-east of Gwingris.

Calelloth has asked you to deliver a letter to Lengliel regarding the half-orcs and worms.Talk to Lengliel at Echad Eregion'It seems that the White Hand is coming against us from all sides here in Eregion, ${PLAYER}. The note you brought me clearly mentions 'Pembar.' Pembar is near our way-post at Echad Eregion. If the half-orcs are at work in that area, then our kindred need to know.

'To find Echad Eregion, head south out of Gwingris. Look to the south-east as you follow the road... Echad Eregion will be found amid some small ruins.

'Deliver this message to Lengliel. She will know what to do.''Echad Eregion is south-east of Gwingris. Follow the road out of Gwingris, and you should find it soon enough.''Well met and welcome to Echad Eregion. What news do you bring from Gwingris?

'Half-orcs stealing worm-eggs? Yes, I know somewhat of this. Let us speak further on the matter....'Spalvi has become increasingly nervous about something, and does not seem to be his usual self. What has upset him?Spalvi is feeling nervous about something, and wanted you to check the road you took from the Trollshaws to Eregion for suspicious signs. The road leaves Gwingris to the south-east before turning north and proceeding into the Trollshaws.Visit the road that runs north from Eregion to the Trollshaws, and remain alert for anything suspiciousAn axe is embedded in a tree by the side of the road that leads from Eregion to the Trollshaws.Remove the axe from the treeSpalvi is in the ruins of Gwingris, in north-west Eregion.

You found an axe embedded in a tree not far from Gwingris, and should bring it to Spalvi.The Hollin Ridge is to the south-east of Gwingris.

Spalvi has asked that you travel with him to the Hollin Ridge, from where you will have a good view of the surrounding lands and should be able to see any Dourhands approaching your position.Talk to Spalvi on the Hollin Ridge, south-east of GwingrisSpalvi has asked you to stand near him and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> into the distance from the Hollin Ridge to see if you spy any of the Dourhands that pursue you.Spalvi is waiting to speak with you before you travel to the Redhorn Pass in search of cave entrances that might bring you closer to Bingo.Talk to Spalvi on the Hollin Ridge and climb the mountains in search of BingoSpalvi is at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.Talk to Spalvi at Gwingris, in EregionTheodore Gorse is at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.

Spalvi has given you the Rothstone to give to Theodore Gorse, since he has no more loyalty to the Dourhands and does not care to keep their treasure.Haley Meadowsweet is at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.

Convinced that it was a fake and had no value, Theodore did not wish to take the Rothstone. Perhaps Haley Meadowsweet will feel differently.Spalvi is at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.Talk to Spalvi at Gwingris, in Eregion'Can I ask you something, ${PLAYER}? Have you seen Willem Whisker anywhere? I know that he does not trust me very much, but I think in a pinch he might be useful to have around, and I would feel better if his teeth and his claws were close at hand... just in case.'

Spalvi looks around nervously.

'Do you remember the path we used to enter Eregion from the Trollshaws? Do you think you could walk back to that path and see if Willem is there, or if anything suspicious is afoot? Leave Gwingris to the south-east and the road will turn north. Do not go too far! I would appreciate having your eyes on that path, that is all.''Do you remember the path we used to enter Eregion from the Trollshaws? Do you think you could walk back to that path and see if Willem is there, or if anything suspicious is afoot? Leave Gwingris to the south-east and the road will turn north. Do not go too far! I would appreciate having your eyes on that path, that is all.''Do you remember the path we used to enter Eregion from the Trollshaws? Do you think you could walk back to that path and see if Willem is there, or if anything suspicious is afoot? Leave Gwingris to the south-east and the road will turn north. Do not go too far! I would appreciate having your eyes on that path, that is all.''${PLAYER}! Did you find anything out of the ordinary?'

You show Spalvi the axe you took from the tree, and the colour drains from his face.

'Aye, that is what I feared. This is a Dourhand axe, and more: it is a message. As we journeyed, I suspected we were being tracked, and now it seems I was right. A band of my people pursues us, and this threatening axe was left to cow us!

'Well, it will not work! We must still search for Bingo. But we must remain alert, for we could be attacked at any time. Let us travel south-east, to the Hollin Ridge. From its height, we will have a good view of the surrounds and should see any Dourhands hoping to fall upon us!''We must still search for Bingo. But we must remain alert, for we could be attacked at any time. Let us travel south-east, to the Hollin Ridge. From its height, we will have a good view of the surrounds and should see any Dourhands hoping to fall upon us!''I do not see any of my people approaching. Perhaps you should <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> as well, ${PLAYER}. Dourhand raiding parties are not known for their stealth, but I do not want them to get the jump on us!

'Once you are certain we are not being followed, speak to me and we can travel to the Redhorn Pass to look for cave entrances that might bring us closer to finding Bingo!''I do not see any of my people approaching. Perhaps you should <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> as well, ${PLAYER}. Dourhand raiding parties are not known for their stealth, but I do not want them to get the jump on us!''You did not see anyone? I do not know if that makes me feel better or worse, ${PLAYER}!

'Well, there is nothing for it. We continue with the plan: to climb the Redhorn Pass and search for cave entrances that might bring us closer to Bingo!''Thank you for your help up there, ${PLAYER}, and for Willem's! Thóvarg and the rest of them did not know with whom they tangled, and once they knew it was too late for them! It is both foolish and typical!

'Can you believe they thought I stole the Rothstone when I escaped with Bingo, ${PLAYER}? What need do I have for Skorgrím's Star?'

Spalvi smirks at you for a time, and then opens his pack. From within, he carefully withdraws a golden necklace with a large red stone in the centre.

'I have no loyalty to the Dourhands any longer, and I have no need for this treasure. Why not give it to young Theodore Gorse? He has been seeking treasure since he arrived, and now at last he can possess one!''I have no loyalty to the Dourhands any longer, and I have no need for this treasure. Why not give it to young Theodore Gorse? He has been seeking treasure since he arrived, and now at last he can possess one!''Hmm? What is this? You are giving me this shiny necklace?'

Theodore squints at it appraisingly.

'This is a test, isn't it? Haley is trying to trick me! She has been going on and on about what defines the meaning of 'treasure' and what does not, and this must be another lesson!

'It is quite a good fake, I will give you that, but Theodore Gorse has become such an astute Treasure Seeker that he will not fall for this clever ruse! Take your elaborate mockery from my sight, ${PLAYER}! I would advise you to cast it into a ditch... that is how confident I am this beautiful, shining necklace is a convincing fake!''Did you cast that elaborate mockery into a ditch yet? Nothing that impressive-looking could be real!''Oh my, ${PLAYER}. Where did you find this? I am dazzled by such a display of dwarf-craft, and by the evidence of such precious materials!'

You tell Haley that it is a gift from Spalvi, and she is welcome to have it, for Theodore did not want anything to do with it, thinking it a fake.

'He what? Ah. I see.'

Haley pockets the Rothstone and smiles.

'I thank you for the gift, ${PLAYER}, and for the opportunity to teach Theodore another valuable lesson... about valuables!''I thank you for that gift, ${PLAYER}, and for the opportunity to teach Theodore another valuable lesson... about valuables!''Well, my friend. I must say to you that I feel as if a great weight has been lifted. No, I do not speak of the Rothstone. That was gold and ruby, and its weight was purely material. That is not always the case with dwarf-made artifacts, ${PLAYER}!

'The weight of which I speak was the weight of my name. I know now what I did not know before: I am no longer a Dourhand. That name, and that life, is now behind me.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Change of Name' (Recommended Level: 50)</rgb>Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel has requested that broken long bows be delivered to her at the Elf-ruins in the southern reaches of Eregion.You have accepted a task to deliver broken long bows taken from the enemies of the Free Peoples to Maegamiel. Maegamiel defends Echad Mirobel in the southern area of Eregion.Wanted: Broken long bows from the enemies of the Free Peoples. Deliver the items to Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel in the southern section of Eregion.Wanted: Broken long bows from the enemies of the Free Peoples.'The enemies of the Free Peoples must continue to fall, or all will be lost. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel has requested that polished wings be delivered to her at the Elf-ruins in the southern reaches of Eregion.You have accepted a task to deliver polished wings from the birds surrounding Echad Mirobel to Maegamiel. Maegamiel defends Echad Mirobel in the southern area of Eregion.Wanted: Polished wings from the birds in the area surrounding Echad Mirobel. Deliver the items to Maegamiel at Echad Mirobel in the southern section of Eregion.'Let us see them learn of our plans now. Thank you.'You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.You have found Dírdrë, daughter of Éa, alive and well.Éa is at Tármunn Súrsa, near Carn Dûm.

Dírdrë has asked your aid returning to her mother, Éa.Escort Dírdrë to safetyÉa is at Tármunn Súrsa, near Carn Dûm.

You returned Dírdrë to the Hillmen. You should speak with Éa, her mother, at once.'I cannot believe that someone has come to my aid. I thought this nightmare would never end. My mother is Éa, perhaps you have heard of her? It has been such a long time without my loved ones.

'Please help me return to her. I fear that I cannot make it back on my own.''My Dírdrë is safe? Oh, thank you! Thank you, brave Southron!'Arre told you of a strange Gauradan mystic he once encountered upon the glacier. He appeared to be an exile among his people, but was wise in their lore. It is possible that he could tell more of the strange frozen amulet that you found in Kibilzahar.Nicca lives somewhere along the southern edge of the great glacier, in the hills east of Barad Gaurhoth.

Arre suggested you speak with Nicca and learn what you may of the amulets.'I have studied this amulet a bit more, but it remains a mystery to me. There is only one I know of who might be able to tell us of its true nature. He is of the Susi-väki -- a näkijä who was exiled from their midst for some crime he would not tell me.

'A few years ago, Nicca came upon me out in the  wastes and saved my life as I lay there frozen and near to death. I spoke with him for a time and learned a little of the Susi-väki and their ways before he sent me back here.

'He still lives, I think, in a hidden place near the southern end of the jätikkö. If you can find him and show him this amulet, he might be able to tell you the truth of its mâginen and purpose.''The Gauradan Nicca may be of some help to you.''You come through cold and evil to speak with me? You are not of the Gauredain. You are not Lossoth. Both would kill me. Who are you? Ah, names do not matter here in the endless ice -- you have brought something. Something made of ice and hatred. Show it to me.

'My kin who are lost to me made this, but the magic is not theirs. It comes from the dark land to the East, where the dead walk. I know its purpose, and it is very evil. Among my kin, those who show fear are exiled out into the frozen wilds to die, from the cold or the arrows of the Lossoth. My fate is the same.

'It is said that fell spirits wander the cold forever, howling against their fate, and these howls become the shriek of the wind upon the ice. Something took these spirits from the ice and trapped them in tombs of frozen flesh and bone, enslaving them. They have become warriors of the dark lords of the East -- those same lords whom I spoke against, earning exile from my kin who are enthralled by them.'Maedlathron sent you to the Elf-camp of Echad Eregion to warn Golgallon of the misfortunate signs of the Company's passing... only Golgallon was already aware of the signs.The camp-site lies east and north of Echad Eregion, beyond Torech Bornagol and Torech Carangor.

Golgallon has asked you to search another camp-site for signs of the Company's passing.Search camp-site for signs of the Company's passingGolgallon is at Echad Eregion, west and south of the camp-site, beyond Torech Carangor and Torech Bornagol.

You should return to Golgallon and warn him that the camp-site has been disturbed and something carried away.Talk to Golgallon at Echad Eregion'Our own scouts discovered another camp-site to the east and somewhat north of here, just beyond Torech Bornagol and Torech Carangor. We fear it, too, is a remnant of the Company's passing.

'Go there, ${PLAYER}, and search for any items possibly left behind. It is clear that certain members of the Company are not as careful as they should be.

'If you find anything, return at once to me.''There is little time to lose, ${PLAYER}. Go to the camp-site I told you of and search there for signs of the Company's passing.'This camp-site has been disturbed, and it appears something was taken. From the signs you have found, it would appear the ransackers were either Orcs or half-orcs.

Tracks lead away to the south. It appears that there are at least two half-orcs and one horse.

Golgallon will want to know about this.'This is worse than I thought, ${PLAYER}. Whatever the Enemy found may disclose who the members of the Company are and put their quest in jeopardy....'It appears that Saruman, too, has begun to twist and create wood-trolls. This troubling news must be delivered throughout Eregion.Faenhilith is at Echad Eregion, south-east of Gwingris.

Lithuilas suggested that you journey to Echad Eregion and speak with Faenhilith.Talk to Faenhilith at Echad Eregion'This is troubling news you bring me, ${PLAYER}. If what you say is true, then none of what has happened in Glâd Ereg is mere coincidence.

'It would seem that the Enemy is compelling all our foes against us in concert, not the least of which is Saruman the White. We must inform all of our allies and move to counter these threats at once.

'Journey south-east to the way-post of Echad Eregion and look for Faenhilith. She must know what we have learned.''There is nothing more you can do here. Journey south-east to Echad Eregion and speak with Faenhilith.''Well met, friend. What news do you bring from Gwingris? Wood-trolls in Glâd Ereg?

'That is grave news indeed. We have seen more than our usual share of trouble lately, as well. It is a sign of these dark days, I fear.

'If you wish to aid us, speak with the other members of my company. We could use someone with your talents, I am sure.'Vämmä fears that a new war-leader may have risen amongst the Gauredain who may bring them into battle in great numbers.Nadelek dwells somewhere within Barad Gaurhoth.

Vämmä has asked you to seek out the Gauradan war-leader, Nadelek, and defeat him.Defeat NadelekWith Nadelek defeated, you should return to Vämmä in Sûri-kylä and tell him of your victory.'Now the battles have begun in earnest, ${PLAYER}. The Susi-väki, stung by their losses, have begun to gather into bands where their enormous strength is multiplied. Even the hearts of our fiercest warriors may feel the cold touch of fear when facing the Susi-väki in wrathful numbers.

'These brutes do not gather gladly, however...only a strong leader can bring them together in any semblance of order in battle, and such leaders are rare among the Susi-väki.

'There is one such leader, Nadelek, of which we have heard rumour recently. He lives in their home village of Barad Gaurhoth. You have proven your own strength and bravery -- if you can find and defeat this war-leader, you will have deprived the Susi-väki of one of their greatest weapons in the battles ahead.''Good, you have returned. With their war leader dead, the Susi-väki will fight among themselves until another proves strong enough to bring them all into line. By then, perhaps our numbers will be great enough to repel such a war band.

'Or so I hope. Until then, we are at peace, and we have you to thank. We shall be forever grateful, ${PLAYER}. Thank you.''Nadelek leads the Susi-väki against us...if he can be defeated, then perhaps the war will end.'Scouts from Esteldín have been trying to break the lines and reach Gath Forthnír. So far, none have been successful.Muiladan waits in the valley to the south.

Areneth asked you to escort Muiladan, a scout from Esteldín, to the Ranger-encampment at Gath Forthnír.Find MuiladanAreneth is at Gath Forthnír to the north.

Muiladan survived the journey north and will be able to lead his companions to the hidden camp.'A scout from Esteldín, Muiladan, needs your help, and time is of the essence. If the Enemy discovers him, they will redouble their efforts to attack us before help arrives. 

'Escort him through the valley from his hiding place south of here, atop a ridge that overlooks Nan Gurth. The terrain is treacherous, and there are many dangers on the way to Gath Forthnír. 

'His companions will not be far behind, and with  their numbers, they should have little trouble fighting their way to us, if need be.''Your actions saved Muiladan from certain doom, but the Enemy is resourceful and will find a way to this safe-haven.

'We must show the Angmarim an even greater gesture of our strength. If they resume their attacks, I fear the worst.''Find Muiladan and escort him back here.''I am glad you made it through. The eyes of the merrevail are everywhere, and I feared I would be discovered before someone arrived.

'We must leave here at once.'After Sauron swept through Eregion of old, the Elves of Rivendell returned and placed statues at places where their brave kinsmen fell in battle.There are memorial statues spread all throughout Barad Morlas, south-west of Echad Eregion.

Ladenthir has asked you to preserve the memory of the Elves of Eregion by collecting the memorial statues placed in Barad Morlas by the Elves of Rivendell before the half-orcs can despoil them. He also bade you defeat Shatogtar, one of the Uruk-hai.Defeat ShatogtarCollect statues (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ladenthir is at Echad Eregion, north-east of Barad Morlas.

You should return to Ladenthir with tidings of your success.'${PLAYER}, I have a request to make of you that is of great importance to my people. As you are surely aware, Sauron long ago destroyed the realm of Eregion -- you can see it in these ruins upon which we now stand. Many of the survivors fled to the refuge of Imladris, where dwells Master Elrond.

'After the destruction of Eregion, some of our kindred returned and placed memorial statues at many locations where some of our bravest kinsmen fell. Now half-orcs have come to Barad Morlas and threaten to despoil our memorials.

'I ask that you go to Barad Morlas to the south-west and recover as many of the memorial statues as you can. While you are there, look also for a strange Orc who goes by the name of Shatogtar; defeat him, for it is he who commands the destruction of those things we hold dear.''Barad Morlas lies just to the south-west. Please bring me those statues and drive forth Shatogtar from these lands.''Shatogtar is dead and the statues have been preserved? You have my deepest gratitude, ${PLAYER}.'The Council of the North is in need of a medal to bestow upon those heroes who have fallen in the service of the Free Peoples.Hróar is at Gath Forthnír in Angmar.

Hróar has asked you to craft an Icon of Heroes and bring it to him.'Our highest award to those who have fallen in acts of unheralded bravery in the service to the Free Peoples is the Icon of Heroes. Alas, the gifted artisan who created it included some rather rare and difficult items in its design, and we have little to spare here. If you can find the needed materials and craft an Icon of Heroes, we would be greatly in your debt.

'Who knows? If you continue to prove your worth, then maybe some day such an Icon will lie upon your breast as we lower you into your tomb -- now there is a cheerful thought....''Once again, you have provided a valuable service. I am at your service and your family's!'You and Spalvi have climbed high into the Redhorn Pass in search of some way beneath the mountain.You have climbed up into the mountains with Spalvi in search of cave entrances.Talk to Spalvi within the snow-stormSpalvi has asked you to brave the snowstorm and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> or <rgb=#FF0000>look around</rgb> for cave entrances.Spalvi is high up the Redhorn Pass, in the middle of a frightening snow-storm.Talk to Spalvi within the snow-stormYou and Spalvi have climbed high up the Redhorn Pass in search of some way of reaching Bingo, but now a new danger has revealed itself: a raiding party of Dourhands!Survive the confrontation with the Dourhand raiding partyThóvarg is not willing to stand down, and Spalvi is itching for a fight.Talk to Spalvi within the snow-stormYou and Spalvi have survived the attack of a Dourhand raiding party.Talk to Spalvi within the snow-storm'Well, what are you waiting for? Let us get down from this frigid mountain-side before it proves the end of us!''The higher we climb the more difficult this gets, ${PLAYER}! Try to keep your eyes peeled for cave entrances!

'I believed we might find a way into the mountain in order to search for Bingo, but if this keeps up we will simply need to find a cave in order to survive!''No caves? Oh, ${PLAYER}, I think we may have no choice after all. This unnatural storm is too intense, and we cannot stay here to conduct a longer search! If we must enter the mountain, we will have to follow the Iron Garrison! I did not really wish to, but it seems it will be the only way!

'We may have to go back down the mountain and collect our thoughts before we can collect our hobbit!''It looks as if we will have to fight, ${PLAYER}. Thóvarg is not going to stand down, and we cannot fight his warriors and the storm both!

'I hope you are ready! He is no slouch with that axe of his!'Willem's face is inscrutable, but you think he seems quite pleased with himself.'Thóvarg will trouble me no more, and I have you to thank for it, ${PLAYER}! Not just you, I suppose, but Willem Whisker as well!

'Now let us descend this mountain! We must take the only path that remains before us. I do not want to freeze solid up here, and I feel certain it is very near to happening!'You have entered the wood-trolls' lair. At Lithuilas' instruction, you are to find the leader of the wood-trolls and defeat him.Descend into the wood-trolls' lair and defeat their leader.Find the leader of the wood-trollsDefeat Zagâfum and Logon.Defeat ZagâfumDefeat LogonYou have travelled to Rhunendin with Lorniel. Using the key you helped forge, she is going to search for her father.Protect Lorniel as she searches for her father, Golodir.Talk to LornielUse the lever on the other side of the gate.

As you progressed deeper into Rhunendin, you encountered a gate the key to Carn Dûm would not open. You should make your way to the lever and open the gate for Lorniel.Open the gate for LornielProtect Lorniel as she searches for her father, Golodir.Talk to LornielProtect Lorniel as she searches for her father, Golodir.Talk to LornielAfter you found Golodir, Lorniel was slain. Now the False King stands before you, far from his source of power. Now is the time to defeat Mordirith.Defeat Mordirith, the False KingGolodir is waiting to talk to you.

You have found Golodir and driven off Mordirith, but at a terrible cost.Talk to Golodir'Quickly, we must leave this terrible place....''Good, so far we have passed unnoticed by the Enemy. Now we must see if we can find my father.

'I have no insight into where exactly he may be... Raghnall did not tell me where in Rhunendin he would be found. We will have to make a careful search.

'Prepare yourself, who knows what lies ahead....''Hurry now, use the lever on the other side of the gate and open the way.''It is very strange that the key did not work on this gate. I hope we do not encounter any other unexpected surprises as we continue on.''My father is not here. How could Raghnall's words have proven false? I fear that we will have to go into Carn Dûm itself!'Golodir speaks through choking sobs, 'No, no... Lorniel!

'He was right... Mordirith was right. He was just playing with me... with you. He could have defeated you today if he had truly wanted to.

'All that time seeing through the palantír I thought I would see the victory... that Lorniel and you... that you would free me, would bring about the downfall of Angmar. It is all gone... all is lost....'The Angmarim, servants of the Dark Lord, threaten the Rangers of Gath Forthnír.  

The Rangers would like for you to push them back once and for all by destroying the idols in the centre of each Bail.The idols of the Angmarim can be found in the three southern bails of Himbar, west of Gath Forthnír.

Areneth has directed you into each of the three lower Bails in Himbar. At the centre of each Bail is an idol that must be destroyed to keep the Angmarim from ever attacking again.Destroy the idol in Bail DílasDestroy the idol in Bail GrundailDestroy the idol in Bail CátharnakhAraneth is at Gath Forthnír, west of Himbar.

Having destroyed the idols in Himbar, return to Gath Forthnír and tell Areneth what you have accomplished.'We have them on the run! Now we must make sure that they never come back again. Head to each Bail and destroy their idols. This is the only way to keep them from threatening us ever again.

'You will find the three Bails to the west of Gath Forthnír, scattered throughout Himbar.  

'This is no small task, ${PLAYER}. You must gather a couple of your most entrusted friends to accomplish this. Good luck to you, friend!''You have continued to impress on us the severity of your convictions, ${RACE}. If you are up for the challenge, I have one final task for you....''You must head into each of the three lower Bails in Himbar: Bail Dílas, Bail Cátharnakh, and Bail Grundail.  

'We must drive away the Angmarim once and for all if this region is to see peace.'You thinned the ranks of the half-orcs at Barad Morlas, but Lengliel warned you that would not be enough.Tattered White Hand Banners can be found throughout the ruins of Barad Morlas, south-west of Echad Eregion. Lengliel is at Echad Eregion, north-east of Barad Morlas.

Lengliel has asked you to help her determine the identity of the half-orcs' leader by collecting banners from the ruins of Barad Morlas.'We need to learn who leads the force at Barad Morlas. Until their leader is slain, the half-orcs will continue their efforts there, undaunted.

'Go to Barad Morlas, ${PLAYER}, and collect several of the banners the half-orcs have raised there. Amidst those tattered banners may be a clue as to the identity of their leader.

'The banners have been raised throughout Barad Morlas, south-west of here. Hurry now, ${PLAYER}...time is running short.''These banners will do nicely, ${PLAYER}. I should be able to learn the identity of the half-orcs' leaders with little difficulty.'Pembar is not the only Elf-ruin in Eregion to have been overrun. Barad Morlas in the south also has become a stronghold of Saruman's minions.Half-orcs can be found at Barad Morlas, south-west of Echad Eregion.

Lengliel has asked you to go to Barad Morlas and thin the ranks of the half-orcs there.Defeat half-orcs at Barad Morlas (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lengliel is at Echad Eregion, north-east of Barad Morlas.

You should return to Lengliel and tell her that you have done as she asked.Talk to Lengliel at Echad Eregion'More half-orcs have come up from the South, ${PLAYER}, encamping at Barad Morlas, south-west of here. Whatever it is that Saruman seeks here, it cannot be for the benefit of the Free Peoples.

'It is said that Saruman is a master of craft, and it may be that he seeks that forbidden Art which was lost when Sauron destroyed Eregion. His minions must be driven forth before they can uncover any shred of that ancient knowledge.

'Go to Barad Morlas, ${PLAYER}, and thin the ranks of the half-orcs there. Perhaps that will dissuade them.''Thank you for your efforts, ${PLAYER}. You are certainly a valiant ${CLASS}, but there is much more to do there....''There are half-orcs at Barad Morlas to the south-west, as well, ${PLAYER}. I fear we must deal with them as we did at Pembar.'Lithuilas has learned that there is a group of wood-trolls in the Glâd Ereg and believes that they are responsible for provoking the forest.Wood-trolls can be found near Sâd Thareg, south of Gwingris.

Lithuilas has asked you to defeat the wood-trolls of Glâd Ereg and learn their purpose there. He suggests that you take an ally or two with you.Defeat wood-trolls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lithuilas is at Gwingris, north of Sâd Thareg.

You should return to Lithuilas with word of your success.Talk to Lithuilas at Gwingris'While you were dealing with our immediate concerns in Glâd Ereg, our scouts learned that there is a large presence of wood-trolls there as well. These creatures are bred by the Dark Lord and may well be the cause for the corruption of the forest.

'From what the scouts say, the wood-trolls dwell mainly in Sâd Thareg at the southern edge of Glâd Ereg. Go and see what you can learn of their presence there.

'Be wary and take an ally or two, for these monsters are terrible foes.''Have you dealt with the trolls to the south?''Well done, ${PLAYER}. Your efforts to curtail the advance of the wood-trolls may well save the forest from utter destruction.

'I can only hope that with the wood-trolls dealt with, the forest will soon know peace again.'The leader of the wood-trolls has been discovered in Glâd Ereg. This may be an opportunity to end their threat in the holly-forest once and for all.Zagâfum, the leader of the wood-trolls can be found in the cave of Sâd Thareg, south of Gwingris.

Lithuilas has instructed you to defeat Zagâfum.Defeat ZagâfumLithuilas is at Gwingris, north of Sâd Thareg.

You should return to Lithuilas with news of your victory.Talk to Lithuilas at Gwingris'It would appear that our work in Glâd Ereg is not yet complete. Our scouts report that there is a clear leader among the wood-trolls, and that he is organizing their foul efforts against the forest.

'This abomination must not be allowed to continue. The wood-trolls' leader must be destroyed. Gather your companions and venture south into the forest once more. There you will find the cave of Sâd Thareg, ${PLAYER}.

'Inside the cave are all manner of wood-trolls, no doubt. You will also find their leader, Zagâfum. Defeat the leader of the wood-trolls, then return to me when the task is done.''Trolls, even wood-trolls, favour caves over the open air. Look for the cave of Sâd Thareg in Glâd Ereg to the south.

'It's possible the trolls have dug their own lair in the attempts to unsettle and corrupt the forest.''Excellent work, ${PLAYER}. Your efforts here will be remembered.

'Please tell me all that you have learned. I can only hope that we have rid ourselves of this menace once and for all.'The Elves in Eregion use lynx-pelts to craft their cloaks.Lynx can be found prowling in High Hollin, north-east of Echad Eregion.

Lengliel has asked you to collect lynx-pelts for her, so that she can make cloaks for the scouts.'The host of Rivendell has a number of trackers skilled in the art of subtlety and stealth in the wild, though few can rival the tawny lynx in these traits. His coat is like a fleeting shadow in the holly-trees, and he stalks his prey unseen with nary a sound.

'Go and hunt these lynx and bring me back their pelts. With a few of their hides, I will be able to fashion cloaks for our trackers that will let them stalk with the same skill as the lynx of Eregion.

'There is a saying in Rivendell among the trackers, "Where the Warg howls, there also the Orc prowls." With these cloaks we will be able to ensure that no Wargs will establish themselves in fair Eregion. Our hunters, so slyly robed, will not allow it.''These are fine pelts, ${PLAYER}. I will begin work on the cloaks at once.

'If only Aragorn were with us! He has spent much time tracking in these lands and would lend us much aid in the battles to come.'Cachúnir has expressed a desire to take as much history and lore of Middle-earth as possible when the Elves of Rivendell sail into the West.Cachúnir is at Rivendell, in the Trollshaws.

Cachúnir has asked you to pen an essay entitled 'Of Mysteries Most Profound' and bring it to him.'There are some jewels of knowledge that surpass even the most brilliant gem or bauble. It is one such that I would have you compile for us here at Rivendell, that we might bring it before our brethren in the Uttermost West, that they might gain once more from the lands that they once called home.

'Pen for me an essay entitled 'Of Mysteries Most Profound,' and your name shall be sung in the Last Homely House with reverence and gratitude.''Ah, let us see....

'Of Mysteries Most Profound' -- a title fitting for a study of one of the grand riddles of Middle-earth.

'Would that we had a hundred scholars such as yourself, ${PLAYER}, though even then it seems that countless enigmas would remain before us.'Thurmaer managed to lose his own way and that of most of his fellowship after he lost their map of Forochel! The map was lost during a battle with the Gauredain, and he believes it may have been taken by one of their hunters on the glacier of Talvi-mûri.The Gauredain can be found in Talvi-mûri, south of Kuru-leiri.

Thurmaer has asked you to hunt down the Gauredain until you find the one who stole his map.Hunt the Gauredain until you find Thurmaer's MapThe Gauredain can be found in Talvi-mûri, south of Kuru-leiri.

Thurmaer has asked you to hunt down the Gauredain until you find the one who stole his map.Hunt the Gauradan until you find Thurmaer's MapThurmaer is at Kuru-leiri, north of Talvi-mûri.

You finally found Thurmaer's map in the possession of one of the Gauradain. You should now return it to him.'All of this started to go wrong after we were separated by a blinding snow-storm. I was ambushed by the Gauredain after losing my way. It was there that I lost the map. The battle was tumultuous, and once it was over, we were scattered and the map was gone -- stolen, I think, by the retreating Gauredain.

'Well -- and I hate to admit this as a hunter and scout -- but my sense of direction is not exactly the best. I make up for it by being an excellent navigator, but without that map there was not much I could do to help find the others, and eventually I found myself all the way up here.

'I need to get that map back if I am ever going to find the rest of my fellowship! Can you help me hunt down the Gauredain until we find the one who stole it?''Those accursed Gauredain still have my map.''Those accursed Gauredain still have my map.''I see you have managed to retrieve my map at last. I owe you greatly for this!

'It seems that my luck may finally be back on track, thanks to you. Soon I shall be able to set out to find the rest of my Fellowship and reunite them at last.'

'Good luck and good hunting to you!'Commander Gisur is impressed with your performance and believes he knows of someone who can use your aid.Gath Forthnír is far to the north of Gabilshathûr.

Commander Gisur told you that Golodir's army was not completely destroyed, and the Ranger has established a camp in the north, Gath Forthnír.Talk to the gate-keeper of Gath Forthnír'You have been a help to we who keep Gabilshathûr safe, ${PLAYER}, and I am sure you can do the same for others. Some time ago, a company of Rangers and Hillmen led by a man named Golodir crossed Rammas Deluon. Many could not overcome the power of the watching-stones, but some few escaped the dreadful statues.

'The survivors established a camp, Gath Forthnír, far to the north, in Himbar. Go to them and give them the assistance you have provided us!

'You are strong and brave, ${PLAYER}, ${CLASS} of much skill. You will bring hope to those who stand watch against the evils of Carn Dûm.''You have come from Gabilshathûr? Welcome, friend. Your aid is indeed welcome here.'Your examination of the rubble at Pembar yielded some relic-fragments, which concern Faenhilith greatly.Elf-relics and Elf-trinkets are being held by the half-orcs who are encamped at Pembar, north-east of Echad Eregion.

Faenhilith has asked you to go to Pembar and defeat the half-orcs there, bringing her any relics or trinkets you find in their possession.'${PLAYER}, it is vital that these half-orcs do not bring their bounty to Saruman the White. There is no telling what mischief a Wizard might put the relics of the Elves to.

'Go to Pembar, my friend. Defeat the half-orcs and wrest from them any relics or trinkets you may find. They cannot be allowed to fulfil their task.

'Pembar lies to the north-east, if the direction eludes you.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}, for bringing these things to me. The half-orcs uncovered many whole relics and trinkets which might have been put to ill use by Saruman.'The ancient Elf-ruins of Pembar have been overrun by half-orcs in the service of Saruman. What they may be seeking there is as of yet unknown, though the worm-eggs seen there by Lengliel's scouts are quite disturbing.Half-orcs can be found in Pembar, north-east of Echad Eregion.

Lengliel has asked you to defeat a number of Saruman's half-orcs and investigate their purpose at Pembar.Defeat half-orcs at Pembar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lengliel is at Echad Eregion, south-west of Pembar.

You should return to Lengliel with news of your success.Talk to Lengliel at Echad Eregion'${PLAYER}, we are in need of your aid. To the north-east, in the ruins of Pembar, my scouts have followed the arrival of half-orcs from Isengard. What their exact purpose is, we do not know, but the scouts have also seen many worm-eggs in that place.

'Perhaps if you thin the numbers of the half-orcs, you will find some clue as to their purpose. They are newly come to Pembar, but they have come in strength.

'When you have performed this deed, return to me, and we will continue our discussion.''So the number of the worm-eggs was much greater than we first believed. And what is more, the half-orcs do not seem concerned by them.

'I fear they are part of Saruman's plans. We shall have to deal with this threat at once!''Go to Pembar, north-east of here, ${PLAYER}, and defeat the minions of Saruman the White.Celebrandir was pleased with the seeds that were brought to him, for they gave him hope that the wood may yet be renewed.There are clearings throughout Glâd Ereg, the forest south of Gwingris.

Celebrandir instructed you to plant the seeds in clear spaces where the trees will have room to grow.Plant seeds (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Celebrandir is at Gwingris, north of Glâd Ereg.

You should return to Celebrandir and let him know that you have planted the seeds as he requested.'These seeds you gathered give me hope that Glâd Ereg will yet be renewed. All that remains is to plant them where they are most likely to grow.

'Return south to the forest and look for clear spaces where these trees will have sufficient room to grow. I am sure such clearings might be found throughout Glâd Ereg, look to the light to guide you.

'When you have completed your task, return to me, and we will discuss your reward.''${PLAYER}, be sure to look for clear areas where the seeds will be able to grow unimpeded.''We have done what we may. Now it is up to the forest to bring forth its young.

'Thank you for your aid, ${PLAYER}.'You have found a scrap of paper indicating that the Dourhand dwarves and Angmar seek to strengthen their alliance.Someone in Gath Forthnír will surely be interested the information you uncovered.

You have acquired information about Angmar's allies and plans. You should immediately inform someone of this development.You have retrieved a piece of paper written by the hand of a dwarf. As you read the scrap of paper, it becomes clear that you have waylaid a messenger of Angmar.

'...it is with dire urgency that I travel to your land. The forces arrayed against us are too great. We must form an alliance, if the Dourhands and Angmar are to survive....'

Reading this grave news, it becomes clear that this must be shown to someone in Gath Forthnír immediately.'You have found what? This is dark news indeed if the Dourhands have come to pledge themselves to Angmar. We must not let this happen.

'You have taken a key first step by defeating Angmar's messenger, but we must not let the Dourhands continue to remain here. We must stop this alliance at all cost!

'I think I have a plan....'You have found a scrap of paper indicating that the goblins of the Misty Mountains and Angmar seek to strengthen their alliance.Gath Forthnír is in the eastern reaches of Angmar, beyond Rammas Deluon.

You have acquired information about Angmar's allies and plans. You should immediately inform someone at Gath Forthnír of this development.You have retrieved a piece of paper written by the hand of a goblin. As you read the scrap of paper, it becomes clear that you have waylaid a messenger of Angmar.

'...while my leader may not feel the same way, it is time that Goblin-town finds powerful friends to help us against all the nasty light-dwellers. Help us, and we will help you....'

Reading this grave news, it becomes clear that this must be shown to someone in Gath Forthnír immediately.'You have found what? This is dark news indeed if the goblins of Goblin-town have come to pledge themselves to Angmar. We must not let this happen.

'You have taken a key first step by defeating Angmar's messenger, but we must not allow the goblins to remain here. We must stop this alliance at all cost!

'I think I have a plan....'You have found a scrap of paper indicating that the Hillman-tribe called the Trév Duvárdain and Angmar seek to strengthen their alliance.Gath Forthnír lies in the eastern reaches of Angmar, beyond Rammas Deluon.

You have acquired information about Angmar's allies and plans. You should immediately inform someone at Gath Forthnír of this development.You have retrieved a piece of paper written by the hand of a Hillman. As you read the scrap of paper, it becomes clear that you have waylaid a messenger of Angmar.

'...the Trév Gállorg have become too strong for us to deal with alone. The southrons have sent heroes to help them. We humbly ask to form an alliance with Angmar and the Trév Duvárdain....'

Reading this grave news, it becomes clear that this must be shown to someone in Gath Forthnír immediately.'The Trév Duvardain have long been a thorn in the side of our allies here, the Trév Gallorg. If they have come hither to establish a stronger alliance with the Iron Crown, our efforts here may be doomed.

'You have done well by defeating the messenger, but I fear it will not be enough.

'I think I have a plan....'You have found a scrap of paper indicating that Saruman and Angmar seek to form an alliance.Gath Forthnír is in the eastern reaches of Angmar.

You have acquired information about Angmar's allies and plans. You should immediately inform someone at Gath Forthnír of this development.You have retrieved a piece of paper written by the hand of Saruman the White. As you read the scrap of paper, it becomes clear that you have waylaid a messenger of Angmar.

'...there is much more happening than even you can imagine, Mordirith. The Great Eye is upon us all, and all of Eriador will eventually fall. Perhaps it is time for the White Hand to join its power with mighty Angmar....'

Reading this grave news, it becomes clear that this must be shown to someone at Gath Forthnír immediately.'You have found what? This is dark news indeed if Saruman the White has come to assist Angmar. We must not let this happen.

'You have taken the first step by defeating Angmar's messenger, but we must not let the White Hand continue to remain here. We must stop this alliance at all cost!

'I think I have a plan....'Angered by the treachery of the Trév Dúvardain, the Hillman Artain seeks to disrupt the flow of provisions from the Trév Dúvardain and the Angmarim.Bail Cátharnakh is west of Gath Forthnír.

Artain instructed you to destroy the food crates which the Trév Dúvardain are stockpiling for the Angmarim at Bail Cátharnakh.Destroy crates in Bail Cátharnakh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Artain is at Gath Forthnír, east of Bail Cátharnakh.

You should return to Artain and tell him of your success.Talk to Artain at Gath Forthnír'You there, ${RACE}...you will assist me? I am greatly angered by the treachery of our sister-tribe, the Trév Dúvardain. They provide food to the armies of the Iron Crown and must be made to pay for their treason.

'I wish you to go to Bail Cátharnakh, west of here, and destroy the food crates you find there. They must be destroyed before the Trév Dúvardain can deliver them to the Angmarim.

'When you have completed this task, return to me, and I will reward you as I may!''The Angmarim will feel the loss of those provisions, and the Trév Dúvardain will feel the wrath of the Angmarim. I am pleased with your success!''What are you doing here still? Go to Bail Cátharnakh and destroy those food crates!'Eiláf believes that the sorcerer Ferndúr concerns himself with the fell spirits that inhabit the area not far from the Cauldron of Death.There are fell spirits in a ruined area not far from the Cauldron of Death in the central island of Imlad Balchorth.

Ferndúr must be destroyed to disperse the evil threat in this cursed land.Eiláf is back at Myrkworth, east from Ferndúr's lair.

With Ferndúr slain, you should return to Eiláf to tell him the tale of your deeds.'You have stopped the evil engine of the Cauldron of Death, you have stopped the supply of money to the North Downs, and you have ended the terror of Deluches, but still the Dead rise! I fear this is the work of the dread sorcerer Lord Ferndúr. You have gathered allies and helped us to slow the spread of the Dead. However defeating Lord Ferndûr is no small task. If you would challenge this fell master of death, you must assemble an even greater force.

'I believe that Ferndúr is concerned more with the summoning of the fell spirits that are used to animate the shambling bodies of the dead than he is with the wights. You must return to the cursed isle in Imlad Balchorth, find the place where the fell spirits throng and destroy them. Destroy them all, and perhaps Ferndúr will appear, for he must know it when his summoned spirits are banished.

'Be wary, for this threat may be the gravest yet, faced in the darkness of Angmar, a monstrous evil whose only superior in the North is in the dread citadel of Carn Dûm itself.''Find the fell spirits, and you will eventually find their master!''Truly you have accomplished a deed worthy of the great tales of past ages. It may be long before Imlad Balchorth is cleansed of its ancient evil, but in a single stroke you have broken one of the main buttresses that support Angmar's vile strength.

'I salute you.'Húriel told you of Bail Dílas and the banners which fly there as a reminder of the Iron Crown's oppression of the Hillmen of Angmar.The banners of Angmar can be found in Bail Dílas, west of Gath Forthnír.

Húriel asked you to enter Bail Dílas and remove the banners of Angmar, replacing them with the standards of the Dúnedain.Húriel is at Gath Forthnír, east of Bail Dílas.

You have replaced all of Angmar's banners at Bail Dílas and should now return with them to Húriel.'${PLAYER}, come hither, for I would speak with you. You have been of great aid to many of us here at Gath Forthnír, and now I must ask another boon of you.

'To the west you will find Bail Dílas, a place under the control of Angmar's Iron Crown. The banners of Angmar fly boldly for all the land to see; a constant reminder of the oppression of the hillmen. I would have you go there and remove the banners of Angmar, replacing them with the standards of the Dúnedain.

'Bring Angmar's banners to me, so that we can make an example of them and show the hillmen that there is yet strength and hope to resist the Iron Crown.''When our banners fly over the camps of the Enemy, they will know the Dúnedain shall resist them.''Well done, ${PLAYER}! Now, at least for a short time, the hillmen will see that there remains opposition to the Iron Crown... until our banners are discovered.

'The last days of the Iron Crown are drawing near!'Ladenthir does not know how far the reach of Saruman has gone, but his touch has been felt in Eregion.Palanaer is at Echad Dúnann, south-east of Echad Eregion.

Ladenthir has asked you to bear news of Saruman's encroachment upon Eregion to Palanaer, another of the scouts of Rivendell.Talk to Palanaer at Echad Dúnann'${PLAYER}, my friend, you have done a great service for the Elves, and I would ask one more favour of you. Go to Echad Dúnann and speak with Palanaer. He should know of Saruman's encroachment upon the ruins of Eregion.

'Echad Dúnann lies to the south-east, not far from the gates of Moria called the Doors of Durin. Go, and may the stars shine upon your road.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. You bring word from Ladenthir? It troubles me that Saruman's forces have moved so far north, and that he dares to despoil the ancient ruins of our kinsmen.

'Though your news is dire, I thank you for it, for it will allow us to prepare a defence.''Go now to Echad Dúnann...Palanaer should know of Saruman's encroachment.'The bundle of supplies you brought to Gwingris has concerned Daegennan greatly.Daegennan believes that more of the lost supplies may be found along the path from Rivendell, well to the east of Gwingris.

Daegennan has asked you to recover more of the missing supplies.'With one delivery lost and the worms on the rise, we have need of your help.

'It is unlikely that all of the delivery is completely lost. If you find any more goods from this delivery, please bring them back to me.

'I believe that you will find more of the supplies along the road from Rivendell, well to the east.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. Thank you, indeed!

'These supplies should hold us until we can arrange for another delivery from Rivendell.'You obtained some weapons from the Enemy at Urugarth, which Throst the dwarf plans to re-forge for the Rangers' use.Wargs can be found at Urugarth, west of Tármunn Súrsa. Odbjorn is at Tármunn Súrsa.

Odbjórn requires Warg hides to craft new armour for the resistance. You should take several hides, as well as the hide of the Warg-leader, and return them to Odbjórn.'Throst told me what you did. An admirable feat! It will be a fine thing when the Enemy realizes we are cutting them down with their own iron and steel. 

'If we are to go to battle, we will need more than sharp blades. You must return to Urugarth and hunt the Wargs there for their hides. We can tan their thick hides and fashion them into good quality armour.

'Skin the pelts from the largest Wargs you can find. Their leader will have the toughest hide of them all so be sure to track him down as well.''These will do well. It is a shame that we cannot get rid of the rank stench of these furs.

'Ah, well. At least the hides themselves can be saved. I will begin work on that armour right away!'Launo told you that rumour has come to him that the ancient dwarf-ruins of Kibilzahar have become haunted by fell spirits.Arre is at the camp of Kuru-leiri, in the northern reaches of the great western glacier.

Launo has asked you to speak with Arre concerning the fell spirits he reported.Talk to ArreKibilzahar lies upon the northern slope of the glacier, north-west from Kuru-leiri.

Arre asked you to explore the ruins of Kibilzahar. Perhaps you may solve the mystery behind the appearance of the fell spirits.Defeat wights in Kibilzahar (${NUMBER}/10)Kibilzahar lies upon the northern slope of the glacier, north-west from Kuru-leiri.

Arre asked you to explore the ruins of Kibilzahar. Perhaps you may solve the mystery behind the appearance of the fell spirits.Defeat wights in Kibilzahar (9/10)Kibilzahar lies upon the northern slope of the glacier, North-west from Kuru-leiri.

While exploring Kibilzahar you discovered that some of the wights wandering through its ruins were wearing strange amulets. You should retrieve one of these amulets for Arre.Collect a frozen amuletArre is in Kuru-leiri, south-east from Kibilzahar.

You should bring the frozen amulet you found in Kibilzahar. Perhaps he will be able to tell you more about it.'Greetings, Sivullinen! I am Launo of Hirvi-heimo. It has come to me that you are a warrior of some renown, and we have need of such as our battles with the Susi-väki to the west grow more frequent and deadly.

'One of my tribe has been studying the old ruins on the northern edge of the great glacier across the Ice Bay. We believe that they were built by the dwarves, but it has lain abandoned for longer than our tales recall. I received a message from my kinsman a few days ago claiming that he had seen fell spirits clambering among the ruins. Where they might have come from he could not guess, and he dared not approach too closely for he is no warrior himself.

'I ask you this, will you go to speak with Arre? His messages are disturbing, and in a time of war and strife, we can ill afford to ignore such an omen.''Speak with my kinsman Arre at Kuru-leiri.''Launo sent you? Good, good. There is something evil afoot here, and I know not what it might portend. See that fortress up on the mountainside above us? It has long stood cold and silent, built by a lost tribe of dwarves who came here hoping to find ore and riches -- to no avail. It has lain abandoned for many generations of our people.

'But no more! Lately I have seen figures moving about upon its battlements, and a deadly cold hangs over it, making the frozen blasts upon the open jätikkö seem inviting by comparison.

'The air of evil about it is palpable, and I dare not approach it myself, for I am no warrior. Will you go there and discover the secret of those strange spirits that now haunt its walls?''What manner of spirits haunt Kibilzahar, I do not know. Will you investigate for me, Sivullinen?''What manner of spirits haunt Kibilzahar, I do not know. Will you investigate for me, Sivullinen?''What manner of spirits haunt Kibilzahar, I do not know. Will you investigate for me, Sivullinen?''What is this? It is so cold that it burns flesh! You say you found this around the neck of one of the Râto you fought in Kibilzahar?

'Alas, none of my lore speaks to such evil mâginen, and I cannot divine its purpose, but I can say one thing for certain: the amulet itself is of Susi-väki craftsmanship, a work of crudely-beaten silver set with an ice-jewel.

'This leaves us with precious few means to learn where it came from or how it came to be enchanted so....'While thinning the ranks of Saruman's half-orcs at Pembar, you discovered a great many more worm-eggs than the Elves had been aware of. This has become a cause of great concern for Lengliel.Worm-eggs can be found in nests throughout the ruins of Pembar, north-east of Echad Eregion. 

Lengliel has asked you to destroy as many of the worm-eggs at Pembar as you are able. Fortkûrz the Worm-master may be found wandering in Pembar.Destroy worm-eggs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Fotkûrz, Worm-masterLengliel is at Echad Eregion, south-west of Pembar.

You should return to Lengliel and let her know that you have destroyed many worm-eggs and have defeated Fotkûrz the Worm-master, as she bade you.Talk to Lengliel at Echad Eregion'Alas, I fear the half-orcs are breeding these worms for the service of their master. What terrible strength Saruman could wield in the North with these beasts at his command....

'We must put an end to this threat, ${PLAYER}. Return to Pembar and destroy as many of the eggs as you can before they hatch. As well, find the Worm-master and defeat him. Our scouts say they have seen a half-orc in the company of a worm wandering the ruins of Pembar.

'The ruins lie to the north-east, in the event you have lost your way.''You have done well. You have dealt a great blow to Saruman's plan, I am certain, though no doubt he has many more plans for Eregion.''Pembar lies north-east of here...go there and destroy the worm-eggs before they can hatch. As well, find and defeat Fotkûrz the Worm-master.'Maerchiniath has asked you to thin out the numbers of the gertheryg in and around the Bails of Himbar.The gorthorog-flayers reside in and around the Bails of Himbar.

Maerchiniath has instructed you to thin the numbers of the gorthorog-flayers.Gath Forthnír is west of Himbar in the mountains.

Having defeated many gertheryg, you should return to Maerchiniath and tell him of your success.'The great tumult of the gertheryg can be heard even inside these caves at night. It is a fear I no longer wish to live with.

'Will you help me, brave ${RACE}? We must reduce the numbers of the terrible beasts. Gath Forthnír must be made safer, and I worry that with these beasts about, our doors will not hold.

'Thin their number and return to me. They dwell in the Bails of Himbar, calling themselves the Flayers.''Hurry to the Bails in Himbar. They are to the west of Gath Forthnír. We must defeat the gertheryg soon before they strengthen their numbers and attack!''Well done! I will inform the others that we have less to fear. But these are still dark times, and there is much to be done!'Cachúnir has expressed a desire to take as much history and lore of Middle-earth as possible when the Elves of Rivendell sail into the West.Cachúnir is at Rivendell, in the Trollshaws.

Cachúnir has asked you to pen some studies of the Unknown and bring them to him.'${PLAYER}, you are one of those who may be counted among the wise and learned of Middle-earth, and I need your help in recording the mysteries of these lands both great and small, before the time comes when I and my brethren must depart into the West.

'If you could see your way to penning some studies of the Unknown before you go, I would very much like to add your wisdom to the annals that we shall carry with us to Valinor.

'Certainly, the Elves of Rivendell will look favourably upon you should you undertake this task on our behalf.''Your studies of the Unknown are most interesting. Would that all such riddles could be explored in the time that remains to us, but with all the time in the world, that task would remain unfinished. We must simply be grateful for that which we can complete. I -- and all of Rivendell -- thank you for your efforts.'Many of the Rangers of Esteldín have fallen in the war against Angmar, and the morale of those who remain is threatened.Techeron is at Esteldín, in the North Downs.

Techeron has asked you to weave a tapestry of the deeds of the fallen, which might be used to hearten those who remain.'I have a matter of sombre import, ${PLAYER}. Many of our bravest fellows have fallen in battle against the Orcs who stream down through the passes from the North, and we would honour their deeds both for their sake, and for the morale of those who must inevitably take up their standard and march forth to die in their stead.

'Your skill is such that I would ask you to weave a tapestry of the deeds of our heroes to exemplify the life and death of those who fight under the banner of Esteldín, that they might be remembered in more peaceful ages to come.''This tapestry, finely woven and glorious in its tales of valour, shall serve our purposes well! Would that I might some day stand beside those brave spirits whose tales are chronicled herein.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. Your skill in these matters is unsurpassed, and the Rangers of Esteldín stand in your debt.'Dunlendings have established a lumber-camp in the wooded land of Sâd Rechu, where they collect wood for the use of Saruman's craftsmen.The battle-horn is in the camp east of the pond at Sâd Rechu, east of Gwingris.

Inufileg has instructed you to use the Dunlendings' battle-horn to issue a challenge to their leader, Tudur.Inufileg is at Gwingris, west of Sâd Rechu.

You should return to Gwingris and tell Inufileg of your success.Talk to Inufileg at Gwingris'Our scouts have reported that the few remaining Dunlendings have managed to reinforce their lumber-camp. Apparently, they have a battle-horn with which they can summon their leader, a man by the name of Tudur.

'Return to the Dunlending-camp, ${PLAYER}, and look to the east of the pond. Search for their battle-horn and use it to challenge Tudur. When he arrives, defeat him...perhaps then the Dunlendings will return to their own land.

'Hurry, ${PLAYER}, before Saruman can send allies to reinforce the camp.''Well done again, ${PLAYER}. With their leader gone, I do not doubt that the Dunlendings will soon return to their homes in the south.

'You have earned the gratitude of both Elf and tree this day, ${PLAYER}. Thank you.''My scouts tell me that the Dunlendings' battle-horn lies just beyond the pond to the east of Gwingris, in Sâd Rechu. Start your search there.'Oflár believes the Cauldron of Death is located near the central lake of Imlad Balchorth.The Cauldron of Death and its guardian are before the central lake of Imlad Balchorth, west from Myrkworth.

Oflár has asked you to fight your way to the Cauldron of Death and destroy its guardian. He suggested you gather allies.Find the Cauldron of DeathDefeat the Cauldron's guardianOflár is at Myrkworth, east from the Cauldron of Death.

With the Iron-bound giant destroyed, the Cauldron of Death ceased spewing forth wights, at least for now. You should return and report your success to Oflár.'I have asked you to go into danger enough ere now. But I think I must ask you to brave still a greater peril. Indeed, I think this is too much to ask of any one adventurer, no matter how doughty, so I would ask you to gather allies to your side.

'This Cauldron of Death... I have heard stories of it told by the Hillmen of the west. It is said that a corpse may be bathed therein, and a wight emerges. It must be a sort of engine of death, the place from which all these dread wights emerge to plague the land.

'I am sure that you will find many defenders at the heart of Imlad Balchorth. Fight your way through the evil throng, find the Cauldron and slay whatever monstrosity you find guarding it.''You must fight your way to the Cauldron of Death and destroy whatever fell creature guards it.''You have done very well. Very well, indeed! You deserve all the praise I can give, along with such small rewards as I can spare. But I fear that our work in Imlad Balchorth is not yet done....'The resistance at Gath Forthnír needs equipment if they are to make a stand against Carn Dûm.Urugarth is in Carn Dûm to the west of Gath Forthnír.

Throst devised a clever plan to steal supplies from the Enemy and use them to equip the resistance at Gath Forthnír.Collect weapons from weapon-crates (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Throst is at Gath Forthnír, east of Carn Dûm.

You should return to Throst with tidings of your victory.'Our stores of weapons run low, and we lack the materials to craft more. Without proper weapons, our cause seems hopeless. Unless....

'There is a possibility. We can pillage the supplies of the Enemy at Carn Dûm and bring them back here where they can be reforged. Not only will we increase our store of weapons, we will take the same out of the hands of the Enemy.

'If you will, travel west into Urugarth and spirit away their weapon crates. When you have the crates, return to me.''I cannot say it will be a pleasure to touch Uruk-forged iron, but we'll melt it down into proper swords and axes.''Excellent! I will get these to the forge, and we'll set about restocking our weapons stores.'The half-orcs at Barad Morlas are using evil wolves to scour the ruins.Half-orc wolf-keepers can be found patrolling Barad Morlas, south-west of Echad Eregion.

Dornhador has asked you to defeat the half-orc wolf-keepers at Barad Morlas to help throw their efforts into disarray.Defeat half-orc wolf-keepers at Barad Morlas (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dornhador is at Echad Eregion, north-east of Barad Morlas.

You should return to Dornhador with news of your victory.Talk to Dornhador at Echad Eregion'${PLAYER}, we know not for a certainty what the half-orcs seek at Barad Morlas, but we know that they have many evil wolves in their ranks. The foul beasts may lead them quickly to that which they seek.

'I think perhaps we should try to disrupt their efforts by slaying the half-orcs who keep watch over the wolves. Without their guidance the wolves may turn aside from their task.

'Go to Barad Morlas, south-west of here, and hunt the wolf-keepers. You must not fail!''You have done well, ${PLAYER}, but I have another task for you, if you will hear me out....''If you defeat the wolf-keepers at Barad Morlas, it may help to throw the half-orcs' plans into disarray.'Anurandir was captured by half-orcs, and it is up to you to save him.Bring Anurandir to safety.Save AnurandirReturn to Dornhador in Echad Eregion.

Inform Dornhador that you rescued Anurandir.Talk to Dornhador'I... I am the leader of the trackers of Echad Eregion... we have kept a close watch on the half-orcs of Barad Morlas. Through our vigilance we have marked the coming and going of their messengers, supplies... even intercepted orders.

'I was captured, please, help me escape. I fear that soon they will be back to torture information from me.''Anurandir returned and spoke with me, I could hardly believe his tale! You are truly a ${CLASS} of great skill.  Anurandir returned to his duties in the wild, but your rescue will not soon be forgotten.'The ruins of Pembar have become a haven of half-orcs in the service of Saruman the White, who has fallen to evil. What they seek within the ancient Elf-ruins remains a mystery.There are many rubble piles in Pembar, north-east of Echad Eregion.

Faenhilith has asked you to search the rubble piles at Pembar to see if you can learn what interest the minions of Saruman have there.Search rubble (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Faenhilith is at Echad Eregion, south-west of Pembar.

You should return to Faenhilith and let her know what you found at Pembar.Talk to Faenhilith at Echad Eregion'Greetings, ${PLAYER}. Your efforts in the North have spread your name far and wide among our kindred.

'I have a task for you, if you would accept it. Half-orcs in the service of Saruman the White have overrun the ruins of Pembar to the north-east. What they seek, I cannot say, but they defile the ancient ruin of my ancestors.

'I ask that you go there and search through the rubble piles. Perhaps you might find something that might explain Saruman's interest in Pembar.''It appears, ${PLAYER}, that there are still fragments of many relics remaining at Pembar. There may yet be record of lore that should not come into Saruman's possession....''Go to the ruins of Pembar, north-east of here, and search through any rubble piles you find there. Hopefully you will learn what it is the half-orcs are seeking.'Five of the seven swords have been recovered, leaving only two.The Sword of Twilight is in a lair of spiders far to the north, near Gath Forthnír.

Nethraw has sent you in search of the Sword of Twilight, It seems once again that allies will be required to obtain the Sword from its hiding-place.Nethraw is back at the Fane of the Seven Sentinels, east of Gabilshathûr.

You should bring the Sword of Twilight to Nethraw.'The Sword of Twilight was carried by the lone Elf in the company of the Seven Sentinels. The name of that sword perhaps derives from the feeling amongst some of the Elves that their time in Middle-earth draws to an end, and perhaps is an allusion to the direction of their retreat. Who can say?

'At any odds, the tale says the Elf's sword may be found far to the north, not all that distant from the place my kindred have made for themselves to spy upon Carn Dûm. I believe the sword's hiding place is in a lair of great and terrible spiders. No one adventurer would be able to retrieve the sword alone, so once again I must counsel you to seek allies in your quest''I hope you will be able to retrieve the Sword of Twilight.''You have done well to this point, but there is one great challenge left.'Himbar is haunted by the merrevail and their kindred, the bats. These creatures prey upon the Rangers of Gath Forthnír, showing no mercy.The merrevail and the bats hunt throughout Himbar, the lands west of Gath Forthnír.

Thoriel warned you of the merrevail, winged monsters in service to the Enemy. They prey upon the Rangers and must be destroyed, along with their lesser kindred, the bats.Defeat merrevail in Himbar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat bats in Himbar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thoriel waits for you in Gath Forthnír, the hidden Ranger camp east of Himbar.

You defeated the winged servants of the Enemy and may now return to claim victory.'These are dark days, ${CLASS}. The morroval scourge preys upon my men, snatching them from where they sleep and carrying them off to their dens.

'You will find the merrevail stalking the lands of Himbar to the west. They are the eyes of Angmar, watching our movements and preying upon us. Slay them now, lest they return to Gath Forthnír.

'Beware the spies of the merrevail, their kindred, the bats.''You alone accomplished what we could not, ${PLAYER}. We are so few in number, and without Golodir to guide us, we feared the worst.

'I can only hope you possess the spirit to face an even greater threat. There must be one that leads these Merrevail to our doorsteps.''If you desire to assist us in our plight, journey west and defeat the winged servants of the Enemy.'The egg-stealing half-orcs are being overseen by a particularly vicious creature named Arzgât.

Calelloth has asked you to head out to the northern hillside of Carchres and slay Arzgât.Arzgât dwells near a small range of mountains, on the hillside of Carchres south of the road to Rivendell, east of Gwingris.

Calelloth has instructed you to go forth and defeat the half-orc Arzgât.'You have dealt with the half-orc rabble here in northern Eregion, ${PLAYER}, but their true purpose is only beginning to become clear.

'Recently, our scouts have indicated that a half-orc with some skill in training the worms is attempting to bend the vile beasts to his will. If he succeeds, he could build a terrible army in Eregion.

'Return to the east and look for a small range of mountains south of the road to Rivendell.  Among them is Carchres.  If you find Arzgât there, put an end to his vile ambitions.''Arzgât has perished? That is welcome news, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps the evil he has wrought will soon come to an end.'The worm Brunanc and his spawn terrorize the paths leading from Rivendell to Gwingris.The worm-eggs belonging to the spawn of Brunanc lie upon the hillsides south-east of Gwingris.

Daegennan has asked you to destroy the eggs of the worms to draw forth their leader, Brunanc.Defeat BrunancDaegennan is at Gwingris, north-west of Brunanc's nest.

You should return to Daegennan with news of your victory.Talk to Daegennan at Gwingris'I cannot thank you enough for dealing with the worms that threaten our supplies from Rivendell, but I have another task for you that should prove to be a more decisive step in safe-guarding our deliveries.

'To the south of Pend Eregion in the east, there is a small clump of large hills. On the north side of these hills are a series of dens the worms use to raise their young. It is here that you will find Brunanc, lord of the worms of Pend Eregion.

'I want you to draw him out by smashing any eggs you may find on the north side of those hills. Draw out Brunanc and slay him, then return here to me.''Has Brunanc been defeated, ${PLAYER}?''Excellent work! It is unlikely that the worms will recover from their loss any time soon.

'This should give us a short respite in which we can gather our strength. The Elves of Gwingris owe you their gratitude, ${PLAYER}. Thank you.'The Hillman called Donaith is concerned about the Orcs in the vicinity of Urugarth and Carn Dûm.Urugarth and Carn Dûm lie to the west of Gath Forthnír.

Donaith asked you to support the cause of his people and defeat the Orcs which gather in Urugarth or Carn Dûm.Defeat Uruks in Urugarth and Carn Dûm (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orcs in Urugarth and Carn Dûm (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Donaith can be found at Gath Forthnír.

Return to Donaith in eastern Angmar and tell him of your deeds.'It sickens me to know that my people are threatened by the Evil Ones, and there is naught that I can do about it. In my youth, perhaps...but time and the rigours of travel across Rammas Deluon have robbed me of my vigour.

'Ah, but you are strong and brave. But are you brave enough to stand against the Orcs and thin out their accursed ranks? 

'Travel to their fortifications to the west. There, in the places known as Urugarth and Carn Dûm, you will find your share of Orcs. Any blow we strike against the False King will help my people survive to see another day.''You possess the kind of fighting spirit I recall from my younger days, but your task is not yet complete, ${PLAYER}. The Uruk-leader still lives, does he not?''Go into Urugarth or Carn Dûm and thin the Orc-hordes.'Thurmaer lost his favourite 'lucky arrow' during a recent hunt in Talvi-mûri. He was stalking a particularly cunning Snow-worm and had just loosed his arrow when he was ambushed by a band of Gauradan-hunters and was forced to flee. Though he made it to shelter, Thurmaer twisted his ankle during his flight.Beleganc wanders far and wide over the great glacier, south of Kuru-leiri.

Thurmaer has asked you to hunt down the snow-worm called Beleganc and retrieve his Lucky Arrow from it.Find the worm Beleganc and retrieve Thurmaer's arrow from itThurmaer is at Kuru-leiri, north of the glacier.

You should return the lucky arrow to Thurmaer.'Curse my luck! Everything has gone wrong of late, and now I am stuck here in this tiny camp in the middle of nowhere. Without my finest arrow, I've no hope of putting things aright again either. I need your help, if I can beg it of you!

'Since I was separated from my fellows and found myself in this farthest and coldest extent of Forochel, things have only gone from bad to worse. Now I have lost my lucky arrow -- I know it flew truly and struck that worm I was hunting, but moments after I let it fly, I was ambushed by a band of Gauradan-hunters! I escaped from their ambush and made my way back here, but I twisted my ankle badly during the flight and cannot retrieve my precious arrow. It has been with me for many a long hunt now and to lose it this way -- it is a terrible omen, I say.

'I am sure my arrow remains in the scaly hide of that worm. The hunters in this camp call it Beleganc and say it is the most cunning of its breed upon the glacier. Finding it out there will be no simple task I fear.''Will you recover my arrow from the worm Beleganc?''Aha, my lucky arrow! You found it!

'Many thanks! Perhaps my luck is beginning to turn for the better at last. After the last several days, I had begun to wonder if things would ever turn for the better.

'Now all I have to do is find my map!'Long ago, lost in an unknown battle, the Jewel of Ice was the most revered treasure of the Lossoth. Unbeknownst to all, it has been held in Angmar and was used to ransom the obedience of the tribe who was once entrusted with its protection and eternally shamed by its loss.Rikitsa is at Sûri-kylä, well north of Pynti-peldot.

Esko has asked you to bring the Jewel of Ice to Rikitsa at the Council.'One thing remains to be done: you must take the Jä-koru to the Council and present it to Rikitsa. It is important that you be seen returning the Jä-koru to its rightful place before the Council... such a gift should solidify support for an alliance against Angmar within the tribes of the Lumi-väki.

'Rikitsa and the Council are at Sûri-kylä, well to the north.

'My tribe is greatly in your debt -- as are all of us for the friendship this may yet forge between us.''The Jä-koru must be taken to Rikitsa at Sûri-kylä.''Forgive me, but I have no time to speak with one such as... it cannot be! Where did you...? The Angmarim said... no. It does not matter -- is that truly the Jä-koru?

'To finally see it returned to us -- to see the shame of our failure dissolved at last -- such has been the dream of the Hylje-heimo for many generations. To have it given to us freely, as you give it now, that goes beyond even our greatest hopes. I... I fear I have misjudged and said many foolish things in these chambers for the sake of our lost honour. I will work now to rectify that, and the nature of our true enemies -- and our friends -- will be made clear at last.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}, though words seem too simple to express our gratitude to you for this. I hope you will understand, nonetheless, what it means to us.'You recovered several of the memorial statues left in Barad Morlas by the Elves of Rivendell, but many more treasures remain lost there...or had until the half-orcs came.Half-orcs can be found in Barad Morlas, south-west of Echad Eregion. Ladenthir is at Echad Eregion, north-east of Barad Morlas.

Ladenthir believes that the half-orcs have uncovered many Elf-relics at Barad Morlas and asks that you defeat them and recover the relics.'I am grateful that you have helped to preserve the memory of those who fell at Eregion, but there is much work yet to be done. There are many relics and treasures of the Elves which the half-orcs have uncovered...these could be dangerous in more powerful hands.

'Go then into Barad Morlas, south-west of here, and recover any relics you may find in the possession of the half-orcs. Bring them back to me, and I will reward you as I may.

'Hurry, my friend, before the relics make their way to Isengard.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}...Saruman will not lay a hand upon these relics now!'As the forces of Saruman and Mordor scour the land of Hollin for the Ringbearer, evil creatures creep into Eregion.Wolves can be found throughout Eregion.

Golgallon has asked that you search out and destroy the wolves that plague Eregion.Defeat wolves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Golgallon is in Echad Eregion.

You should return to Golgallon and inform him of your success against the wolves.Talk to Golgallon at Echad Eregion'This land has seen many battles; its history is written in blood, though now it wears a veil of peace...for at least a short while. But the peace it knows is but a fleeting phantom.

'As the minions of Saruman and Mordor intrude upon Hollin, many other evil creatures have come here as well, not the least of which are the wolves. They have driven off what few birds and kindly beasts that once dwelled here, killing what does not escape them.

'The howling of the wolves in Tâl Caradhras is worse than the silence that follows in the wake of the foul beasts. Go and cull their number, then return to me when the task is complete.''You did well, ${PLAYER}. The wolves will not soon forget your fury!  Maybe Tâl Caradhras will have peace, if only for a short while.''Wolves can be found throughout Eregion but have been seen in large numbers south-east of Echad Eregion in Nan Sirannon.'No longer a Dourhand, Spalvi wishes to adopt a new surname.Now that he is no longer a Dourhand, Spalvi wants a new surname, and has asked you to speak with people at Gwingris for ideas.Ask Daegennan for a possible surname for SpalviAsk Theodore Gorse for a possible surname for SpalviAsk Thillosil for a possible surname for SpalviSpalvi is at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.Talk to Spalvi at the ruins of Gwingris, in EregionSpalvi thinks that you should brainstorm ideas for his new surname as you fight the many foes in Eregion.Spalvi is at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion.

You have fought a number of enemies throughout Eregion, and should tell Spalvi the possible titles you came up with that might fit his taste.Talk to Spalvi at the ruins of Gwingris, in Eregion'I am no longer a Dourhand, ${PLAYER}, but I want to be something! I need a new surname to replace the one that no longer fits. I could not take an existing name, for that would not be right, but perhaps I could adopt a descriptive name. A suitable epithet would do me some good, and then we could resume our search for our missing hobbit!

'Perhaps you can speak with some folk around Gwingris to get ideas for me? I do not like Elves, but there is no denying that they have a power over words and one of them might come up with a suitable name or title for me to bear.''I am no longer a Dourhand, ${PLAYER}, but I want to be something! I need a new surname to replace the one that no longer fits. I could not take an existing name, for that would not be right, but perhaps I could adopt a descriptive name. A suitable epithet would do me some good, and then we could resume our search for our missing hobbit!

'Perhaps you can speak with some folk around Gwingris to get ideas for me?''The dwarf wants a surname? How should I know what title would serve him? None of the ones I might suggest would be considered polite, ${PLAYER}, and I do not wish to insult him while he is a visitor here.''Spalvi wants a new surname? Well, I would be glad to help you come up with ideas! Let us see. He is a dwarf. And he... well... hmm...'

Theodore bites his lip, deep in thought.

'This is more difficult than I expected! Your friend Bingo was writing a book, was he not? I bet he would be good at coming up with ideas! It is a pity you haven't found him yet, ${PLAYER}. I will keep thinking, but I have only had one idea and I am afraid that Spalvi, Dwarf-like, is probably not what he had in mind!''I am no expert on dwarf-lore, ${PLAYER}, but I do know that dwarves sometimes take descriptive titles rather than the surnames of their families.

'What does Spalvi most enjoy doing? Is he a fighter, or a scholar, or a loyal friend? If you can think of a title that best describes him, you will have found an appropriate surname!'

You think for a moment, and decide that 'Bingo-dropper' or Hobbit-misplacer' are not the most complimentary of titles.'I have nothing to suggest.''Spalvi, Dwarf-like. You know, I think there might be something to that one!''How well do you know this dwarf? It should not be very difficult to come up with a title that describes him.''I don't know, ${PLAYER}. None of these suggestions seem to fit me very well.

'I have an idea! What if you bring battle to the various foes that can be found throughout Eregion? Perhaps the act of battle will inspire you, and you will come up with a fitting surname for me?

'Get fighting, and get thinking, my friend!''What if you bring battle to the various foes that can be found throughout Eregion? Perhaps the act of battle will inspire you, and you will come up with a fitting surname for me?

'Get fighting, and get thinking, my friend!'As you begin to list some possible titles for Spalvi, he holds up a hand and stops you.

'I am sorry about this, ${PLAYER}, but I was speaking with Haley while you were away, telling her about some of our adventures, and she said I sounded very stout of heart! So I have decided I will be Spalvi Stout-heart from now on!

'It suits me much better than Dourhand, wouldn't you say? Thank you for all the help, my friend, and now that I have a fitting title I am ready to resume our search for our missing hobbit!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Into the Long Dark' (Recommended Level: 51, and it is further recommended that you have gained access to Moria by completing Volume II, Book 1: 'The Walls of Moria')</rgb>The spies of the Enemy seek continually for the trail of the Company, threatening their safety and their quest.The place you should set a false trail lies to the south-west of Limlaer and west of Porth Cadlus.

Limlaer has asked you to set false trails for the scouts of the Enemy to find.Set a false trailLimlaer is south-west of Echad Dúnann, along the Nan Sirannon road.

You should return to Limlaer and inform him that the false trail has been set.Talk to Limlaer'${PLAYER}, I am glad that you have returned. We still have much to accomplish.

'The servants of the Enemy are closer at hand than even Master Elrond has feared. If we do not act quickly, they may discover the path of the Company...to the ruin of us all.

'We must devise a false trail to mislead the Enemy into believing that the Company has passed south, nearer to the Gap of Rohan. Travel south-west of here and west of Porth Cadlus, and disturb the ground there. Perhaps we can trick the foul scouts and secure more time for the Company to put this place well behind them.''The trail has been placed? Then we must continue to aid the Company as best we may.''The best place to set the trail is south-west of here and west of Porth Cadlus.'Hjór needs aid searching for his companion Thorlák upon Caradhras, which is called the Cruel.Thorlák headed into the Redhorn Gate, north-east of Echad Dúnann.

Hjór has asked that you search the Redhorn Gate for signs of Thorlák.Search for some sign of ThorlákHjór is at Echad Dúnann, south-west of the Redhorn Gate.

You should return to Hjór and tell him what you found in the Redhorn Gate.Talk to Hjór'Hjór at your service and your family's! You look like just the sort able to help me with my troubles.

'My travelling companion, Thorlák, and I journeyed with Brogur's expedition. We had long debated the existence of the famed tower of Durin that some say sits atop Zirakzigil. I did not believe Thorlák when he said he would find it or perish in the attempt, but now I fear for his life!

'Four days ago, he left camp in search of Durin's Tower, and he has not been seen since. If you could journey into the Redhorn Gate and search for any sign of him, I would be much in your debt. I last saw Thorlák heading into the snow near to the mountains.'The Redhorn-goat meat looks like it was skinned and cleaned by a skilled hand.'A skinned goat? That is good news, for Thorlák might still be alive somewhere upon the mountain!''Thorlák was last seen on the lower slopes of the Redhorn Gate, near to the mountains heading into the snow.'Sterling Proudfoot thinks that you might be able to learn something from Bilbo Baggins, whose adventure as a burglar has stirred the imaginations of hobbit-children for years.Sterling Proudfoot knows of a legendary burglar who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Articles of CunningComplete Implements of the NightSterling Proudfoot is in Buckland, west of Bree and east of the Brandywine River.

Now that you have acquired your new weapon and armour you should speak to Sterling Proudfoot.Speak to Sterling ProudfootThe whereabouts of Bilbo Baggins are unknown by Sterling Proudfoot.

Sterling Proudfoot thinks you should find the legendary burglar, Bilbo Baggins, and see what you might learn from him.'Don't take this the wrong way, my friend, but I can't help but notice that you're not exactly outfitted with the type of equipment that befits your reputation as a skilled burglar. I mean no offence, of course. In fact, I'd be willing to help fashion you a few articles that will do a better job of it!

'It won't be easy, but then again, when has anything worthwhile ever been easy? Am I right? Of course I'm right! Just let me know when you're ready to begin, and I'll tell you what you're going to need if I'm going to make you some equipment worthy of a first-class burglar!''What are you waiting for? You need those items!''I'd just like to say that I'm very impressed with what you've managed to do, ${PLAYER}. You are truly a burglar of excellence!

'Always more to learn, though, isn't there? Yes, there is, at least if you're paying attention! Now, you may have heard the tale of Bilbo Baggins... he was quite the burglar in his day, if you believe the stories. Now, he's gone away and hasn't been heard from in a long time, but I imagine he's out there somewhere.

'If you truly want to excel at burgling, you're going to want to track down old Bilbo and see what he might teach you.''I don't even know if old Bilbo is still alive, but I figure he must be. That burglar is bound to last a good long time, as crafty as he was! If you haven't heard the tale, you should find someone willing to tell it, for it's really something else!

'I mean, it's really something else if you happen to be a hobbit-child. I'm only interested in it out of professional courtesy, you understand. Yes, that's it, of course.''What's that? You're a burglar? Oh, of course, of course! I did some burgling in my day, you know. I helped Thorin and the dwarves regain their lost home in the Lonely Mountain. I'm afraid I didn't do a very good job and bungled the whole thing right up, but I suppose everything turned out all right in the end.

'A burglar needs to be light on his feet and just as quick in his own head! If I hadn't been both of those, old Smaug would have cooked me until I was well-done on several occasions!

'I wrote it all down, you know. There might be a lesson or two in there for an aspiring burglar, if you were of a mind to look. The best advice I can give to you about burgling is this: don't! It caused me only grief for a long while, and I know that Thorin would tell you the same, if he were still here. At any rate, I'm glad we had this conversation, ${PLAYER}. It's nice to recall the old days every now and then, and Rivendell is a better place than most in which to do it!'Dawn Stockard has learned that a mighty captain has come to Eriador from the south and thinks he may be willing to speak with you.Dawn Stockard knows of a legendary captain who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Implements of WarComplete Articles of CommandDawn Stockard is in the town of Archet, north of Bree, in Bree-land.Boromir is currently staying at Rivendell, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Dawn Stockard has learned that Boromir, a great captain of Gondor, has come north to Eriador and is staying with the Elves. She believes he may teach you some of his techniques if you speak with him before he begins his journey home.Talk to Boromir in Rivendell'Perhaps captains are given closer scrutiny than others. The people look to us for hope and read much in our faces beyond what may lie in our thoughts. I do not know, but your presence does bring something to my attention.

'You are deserving of equipment that reflects the brave leadership you display in battle, ${PLAYER}. I can help you fashion such articles, but I must give you a word of caution: they will not be easy to come by, for their tale must be one of danger and valour against overwhelming odds if they are to bring you strength.''You must finish the tasks set before you.''I want to take a moment to express my admiration for you, ${PLAYER}. You have matured into a captain of tremendous skill and leadership, and I stand in awe of your example.

'There is little else I can teach you, but I have learned that a Man of the South has come to Eriador and is currently staying with the Elves at Rivendell. I do not know what business has brought him to these lands, but I know that his name is Boromir, and he is a great captain of Gondor.

'Boromir may be able to teach you some of the techniques he has learned in his home-land, but you should leave for Rivendell at once. I do not know what has brought him north nor how long he will stay.''Boromir is a great captain in his homeland and might be able to teach you something of his techniques. You should speak with him at Rivendell before he returns to Gondor.''It is always a pleasure to meet a fellow captain! I must say that I have become quite overwhelmed by all of the Elves of this place. But it falls upon the men and women of Eriador and of Gondor to defend against the shadow, does it not? It does, and though we welcome the aid of the Elves and the dwarves, I would be more at ease with a captain such as you by my side and a line of solid Men stretching endlessly across the plain!

'It is up to us to defend Middle-earth from her foes, ${PLAYER}, and we will do it, though we fall in the attempt! And there is glory in that, if there is glory in anything!

'We may yet have our chance, friend captain. And on that day, we will strike fear into the hearts of our enemies and rally our friends forth to victory!'Aelindil has learned that Galdor, an Elf from the Grey Havens, has come to Rivendell. Although Galdor's master, Círdan the Shipwright, did not accompany him in his errand, Galdor himself is known to be a mariner of great repute who might help you to hone your skill.Aelindil at Tornhad in the Angle of Mitheithel knows of a legendary mariner who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete 'Implements of Navigation'Complete 'Articles of Ingenuity'Aelindil is at Tornhad in the Angle of Mitheithel.Talk to Aelindil in TornhadGaldor can be found near Elrond's Stables, in Rivendell.

You have fashioned a weapon and piece of jewellery worthy of your skill and reputation as a Mariner. Aelindil has asked you to seek out the Elf, Galdor, in Rivendell, that he might train you in the finer techniques of the Mariner.Talk to Galdor in Rivendell'It is time you learned more of the ways of the Mariner, ${PLAYER}. In my youth, I spent many years among the Elves of Imladris, and I learned much from them and from their many visitors. An Elf of the Grey Havens named Galdor has come to Imladris, and I am certain that his wisdom would be a great boon to you in your journey as a Mariner. Before such a meeting can be arranged, however, you must be suitably prepared and outfitted!

'To that end, you shall have need of equipment that better reflects your skill as a Mariner and also the good you do for the people of Middle-earth, my friend. It would be my honour to fashion for you a weapon and a fine piece of a jewellery by which to represent your prowess, but I must give to you this cautionary word: these implements and articles will not be easily earned, for I shall task you with a number of trials to collect materials of worth to aid in their creation. Only when you have finished each and every one of my trials shall you receive the rewards I have promised you, for only then shall you prove yourself a Mariner befitting them. Such was the test that was laid before me in my youth, and so too shall it be for you!

'If perhaps you do not understand how each of these materials I shall demand relate to each of the others, then allow me to explain before you ask, my friend. These materials shall serve me two-fold, for some of them shall be put to use in the craft of your weapon and jewellery, and others will prove your worth as a Mariner and as one worthy of such equipment.

'If we are understood, then let us begin.''Only when you have finished each and every one of my trials shall you receive the rewards I have promised, for only then shall you prove yourself a Mariner befitting them. Such was the test that was laid before me in my youth, and so too shall it be for you!

'If perhaps you do not understand how each of these materials I shall demand relate to each of the others, then allow me to explain before you ask, my friend. These materials shall serve me two-fold, for some of them shall be put to use in the craft of your weapon and jewellery, and others will prove your worth as a Mariner and as one worthy of such equipment.

'If we are understood, then let us begin.''You have done very well, ${PLAYER}, and none can deny your achievements—both as a ${RACE} and as a Mariner. Your skill and determination are admirable, my friend, and as promised, I have arranged for you an audience with Galdor of the Havens.

'I made the acquaintance of Galdor's master, Círdan, in my youth, and it was a meeting I shall never forget. I could spend several of my lifetimes in his tutelage, and I would still fall many centuries short of the breadth of his knowledge of the Sea. Ah, but I digress.

'It is my understanding that Galdor's errand will soon take him far afield, so if you cannot find him at the Last Homely House, perhaps he might be found nearer to Lord Elrond's stables in Imladris.''I bid you welcome, ${PLAYER}.

'The Ranger, Aelindil, is a friend of Lord Elrond, and I was told I should expect you. It is my understanding that you have come to seek guidance in the way of the Mariner. Indeed, I can offer you that, my friend—in some small measure at least.

'My lesson is this: the path of the Mariner is one of calm and hardship—of song and storms. Balance is a skill like any other, and there are many paths by which it might be achieved, but no Mariner should fear to stray from it in times of need. The Sea tasks one to perform with perfect mastery, favouring those with a sharp mind and a swift hand, and a Mariner must find harmony with its tides and currents—for those who do not will soon meet their doom. Perhaps you have spent much time upon the rivers and Seas of Middle-earth, or perhaps you have not, but consider my words, nonetheless.

'Alas, my time in Imladris must soon come to an end. I wish you good fortune in your journey as a Mariner, ${PLAYER}, and I applaud your efforts on behalf of the Free Peoples of Middle-earth! I regret that I cannot remain in Imladris to offer you more lessons, for my errand is one of great importance and urgency, but perhaps our paths shall cross again ere I return to the Grey Havens. Until we meet again!'Gwesgylliel has heard that a company of dwarves have come to Rivendell, and that among their number is a dwarf-champion, Gimli, who might be able to help you grow in power.Gwesgylliel knows of a legendary champion who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Implements of DaringComplete Articles of ValourGwesgylliel is at Lin Giliath in Meluinen in the North Downs.Talk to Gwesgylliel at MeluinenGimli is currently staying in Rivendell, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Gwesgylliel has learned that a party of dwarves came to Rivendell not long ago, and one of their company, Gimli, was a champion. This dwarf-champion might be willing to teach you some of his techniques.Talk to Gimli in Rivendell'You are deserving of equipment that reflects the good you do for the people of Middle-earth, ${PLAYER}, the better with which to serve them. It would be my privilege to help you fashion it, but I must give to you this cautionary word: these articles will not be easily come by, for their tale must be one of danger and of hardship. Only through these perils will they have strength enough.

'Speak with me when you are ready to begin, and I will tell you what must be done to fashion equipment befitting a true champion.''You must finish the task put before you.''I would like to take a moment to congratulate you on your achievements, ${PLAYER}. You have accomplished many brave deeds, and I marvel at your dedication! You are a mighty champion!

'A company of dwarves visited Imladris not long ago, and a number of champions travelled with them. They are now staying at an outpost in the Misty Mountains north of the vale. One of them might be able to advise you, for you have exceeded the teaching that I can provide. I salute you for that, friend champion!

'What was the name, again? Gimli, I think it was. Look for him in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.''I do not remember dwarf-names very well. The company at the outpost in the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell, sent a dwarf-champion to Rivendell; he is staying in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris. I believe his name was Gimli, but it might have also been Gisli. Dwarf-names are not easy, like the names of my people.''You flatter me, ${PLAYER}! There are some would say I am a champion among dwarves, but certainly I would not do so, not so boldly! Ha ha ha!

'The trick to combat, my friend, is to give it everything you've got. There are no half-measures, not in battle and not ever! If you swing your axe as though every swing were its last, you'll be a real force!

'I understand that you're a mighty champion yourself, ${PLAYER}, and I applaud you! You'll do fine in the times that lie ahead, I'm sure of it. Until we meet again!'Fenton Marshley, impressed by your dedication and your ability, has recommended that you speak with Legolas, an Elf possessed of great skill in the hunt.Fenton Marshley knows of a legendary hunter who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Articles of DiscoveryComplete Implements of the HuntFenton Marshley is outside the Hunting Lodge east of the town of Archet and north of Bree, in Bree-land.Legolas may still be in Rivendell, in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Fenton Marshley encountered a party of Elves on his journey through the Mirkwood and believes that one of that company might still have things to teach even a skilled hunter such as yourself.Talk to Legolas in Rivendell'You are deserving of equipment that reflects your well-honed skill, ${PLAYER}, and I would be delighted to help you fashion it. Be advised, however, that no path worth following is an easy one. The things I can help you prepare will only be as good as the story behind them, and the story behind these implements and articles will be one of fell dangers and the ultimate triumph over evil.

'A hunter of lesser stature would despair of this road into the dark, but you, I know, will come again into the light of the sun. Speak with me if you are ready to set upon this path.''You need to complete the tasks I set before you.''Allow me to congratulate you on your great accomplishments, ${PLAYER}. Few hunters can boast of the dangers you have braved and the dark places into which your hunts have taken you, and you should be proud of your deeds, for they are worthy of commendation!

'My home is in Dale, but the way of the hunter is one that wanders. As I passed through the Mirkwood on the journey that eventually brought me here, I chanced upon a party of Elves that were leaving Thranduil's halls on some errand to speak with Elrond of Rivendell. One of that company, a fair Elf named Legolas, seemed to be a hunter of no small skill.

'I can teach you nothing more, but I am sure this Legolas could give you some manner of instruction. If he is still at Rivendell, seek him out in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris and you may find a willing teacher.''I do not know the nature of the errand that brought Legolas and his people to Rivendell, but he seemed a hunter of no small skill. He may be able to impart to you even more hunter's wisdom. Look for him in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.''I bid you welcome, ${PLAYER}, as one hunter to another! I remember Fenton Marshley, for we were not far from my father's halls when we came upon him and offered to show him the way there to rest. He politely refused, saying he still had some miles left in him to walk that day. He seemed a hardy sort.

'There is little I need to teach you, ${PLAYER}, for you are already possessed of many skills! I can give you this, however: it is more important to be watchful, to be always aware of your surroundings, than it is to be strong. Strength of arm is important, for the bow-string must be taut before her arrow will fly, but strength of mind is more important than all! Be ever-watchful, ${PLAYER}, and your bow will never want for targets.

'I will see you again, my friend, and it is to be hoped in happier days! It is always a pleasure to speak with one who follows the same course, though the paths be different.'Baillindiel believes that you should speak with Lindir, an Elf in Imladris who might be able to give you some instruction.Baillindiel knows of a legendary minstrel who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Articles of HarmonyComplete Implements of SongBaillindiel is at Celondim, the Elf-harbour in Ered Luin.Lindir is in the Hall of Fire in Elrond's home in the vale of Imladris.

Baillindiel thinks you should speak with Lindir, for the Elf may be able to provide you with further instruction.'You are deserving of equipment that reflects the joy you bring to others, ${PLAYER}. I can help you fashion such articles, but I must give you a warning: this road is not an easy one, and you may despair of ever seeing its end. I need not tell a minstrel that the joy at the end of a long road is all the sweeter for the hardship, though.

'Let me know if you are ready, and I will tell you what must be done. The tale of these items will be difficult and dangerous, but that is what will give them their strength.''You should complete the tasks set before you.''Allow me to congratulate you on your accomplishments, ${PLAYER}! You have become a minstrel beyond compare and have exceeded that which I can teach you. I marvel at your successes and wish you only the best!

'My good friend Lindir may be able to give you more instruction, for he is very fond of song and loves to speak with minstrels of all types.

'Look for Lindir within the Hall of Fire in Elrond's home in Imladris, for I am sure he will be glad to speak with you.''Look for Lindir in the Hall of Fire in Elrond's home in the vale of Imladris, and he may be able to provide you with further instruction.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}! The song of your deeds will be quite a song indeed, when it is finally set to verse! Yes, I have already heard snippets of it, though I do not know that I am the one to set it down. Bilbo, perhaps...though it seems that every day brings to him three new ideas that crowd out what has gone before.

'Sing always, ${PLAYER}, for it is your greatest weapon. A voice raised in song is a voice that cannot succumb to despair.

'Take time for celebration and mirth, my friend, even in these dark times. Do not stay away from the Hall of Fire overlong, for you will always have friends here. I will see you again, and we will sing of happier times and of times that will come again!'Isdi believes that Master Elrond may have some words to impart to a lore-master of your great wisdom.Isdi knows of a legendary lore-master who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Articles of MysteryComplete Implements of KnowledgeIsdi is at the ruins of Edhelion, on the slopes above Thorin's Hall, in Ered Luin.Elrond is in the library of the Last Homely House, in the Vale of Imladris.

The lore-master Isdi believes that Elrond would wish to speak with you, and that you might learn something from his teachings.'I wonder at your garb, ${PLAYER}. Why have you not sought to fashion articles appropriate to our calling that reflect your desire for study? Gladly will I help you with this, if you wish it!

'But I give to you words of caution, for such articles will not come easily. Their tale will be one of hardship and sorrow, but its end will be full of triumph and victory. Do not despair, for though the road be hard, a lore-master knows its end is sweet. Speak to me if you are ready, and I will tell you what awaits beyond dark corners, down your road.''You should complete the tasks set before you.''I want to tell you that I hold you in high esteem, ${PLAYER}. You have attained a depth of wisdom that few lore-masters can hope to achieve.

'Master Elrond would likely enjoy some moments spent with you in quiet conversation, if you are interested in travelling to his home in Imladris.''Master Elrond can often be found in quiet contemplation in the library of his house in the Vale of Imladris.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. It is always a pleasure to speak with one who has turned an eye towards the mysteries of the past and an ear to the riddles of the present. There is much that can be learned from both directions, and it is a wise lore-master who learns this sooner rather than late.

'Wise, too, is one who befriends creatures of nature, as you have done. The best course to victory is the one taken by friends.

'I have enjoyed our talk. Perhaps we will speak again when these dark days are past.'Wáli heard that Samwise Gamgee is an extremely skilled guardian, peerless and without compare.Wáli knows of a legendary guardian who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete Articles of FortitudeComplete Implements of BattleThe dwarf Wáli is at the outpost of Noglond, south of Thorin's Hall and west of the dwarf-city of Gondamon in Ered Luin.The whereabouts of Samwise Gamgee are currently unknown to Wáli, but he thinks he might be in Rivendell.

Wáli thinks you should speak with Samwise Gamgee, rumoured to be a guardian of unmatched skill, with the hope that he could teach you a thing or two.Talk to Samwise Gamgee in Rivendell'You deserve to wield equipment that reflects your opposition to the forces of shadow, and your determination to fight to the last against them. I can help you prepare such equipment, but I tell you now that it will be no easy task.

'The scars we bear tell the tale of the battles we have fought, every conflict etching upon our bodies and minds the memory of all the good that we do. Your equipment should do the same, ${PLAYER}. Do not fear what lies ahead! Though your foes be fearsome, I know you have it in you to triumph. Speak with me if you are ready to begin this greatest of trials.''You must complete the tasks set before you.''I'd like to take a moment to congratulate you on your accomplishments, ${PLAYER}. You have got to be the most dedicated guardian I have ever met!

'If I am to believe the rumours, though, there is one that puts even your skills to shame! I've heard from my brother down south in a small town called Needlehole that there's an incredible guardian who calls the Shire his home. This guardian is apparently unmatched, and his name is Samwise Gamgee.

'My brother said he's not in the Shire any longer, so he didn't have a chance to meet this Samwise and learn any lessons he might have for a fellow guardian, and no one seems to know where he's gone! If you could find this Samwise, I'm sure he'd teach you a thing or two, since you're such an accomplished guardian, same as he is! You might find him in Rivendell, now that I think on it.''I don't know where this incredible guardian Samwise Gamgee is. He used to live in the Shire, but he hasn't been there in some time. If you can find him, he's sure to give a fellow guardian some pointers! Have you tried looking in Rivendell?''You were told what? You were told that I'm an incredible guardian, the finest in the Shire? Someone's pulling your leg, ${PLAYER} -- I'm no guardian! I think this dwarf is having some fun at your expense! Finest guardian....

'Actually, now that you mention it, I think I know what might have happened. You see, I'm not a guardian at all, but I am in fact a gardener, and although it's not right for me to boast, my flowers and vegetables have been quite well-received by my neighbours in the Shire on several occasions, and...

'Oh, bother! This Wáli's brother must have misheard someone, that's all! I don't know much about being a guardian, but I could give you some advice about gardening, if you were of a mind to listen! It's all about how well you watch the health of your plants and how effectively you respond to their changing conditions: by watering them, changing the amount of sunlight they receive, or re-spacing their neighbours. A guardian might learn a thing or two from a gardener, if you ask me!'Great lizards have been eating many of the small creatures of Eregion, stripping the land of much of its beauty.Lizards can be found in Ulundín in the southern tip of Emyn Naer, south-west of Echad Dúnann.

Tinúriel has asked that you slay the lizards who have been killing off the native creatures of Eregion.Defeat lizards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tinúriel is at Echad Dúnann, north-east of Ulundín.

You should return to Tinúriel and inform her that you have slain many lizards.Talk to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann'This land was once filled with the beautiful music of birds. Squirrels played in the heather, and deer wandered the hill-slopes of the mountains. But they are all silent now, driven off or killed by the invading evils.

'When the half-orcs and Uruk-hai of Saruman came, they brought with them foul creatures, none more destructive than the Lizards in Ulundín, near the southern tip of Emyn Naer. They sneak into trees and eat birds, find the unsuspecting squirrel in the holly-bush, even catch the mighty deer unaware in the mountains.

'Go south-west into Ulundín and slay these creatures. If they are driven from the land, perhaps the wild inhabitants of Eregion will return in time.''You have killed many of the lizards, ${PLAYER}, but there are many more yet to be driven forth. Perhaps someday soon, as the Elves count the days, we will hear the song of birds and the soft feet of the rabbit again in Eregion.''You can find the lizards in the southern tip of Emyn Naer in the area known as Ulundín.'A messenger has been dispatched from Barad Morlas seeking aid from the Dunlendings encamped in Eregion.The Messenger is west of Porth Cadlus, south-west of Echad Dúnann and across Nan Sirannon.

You must stop the Dunlanding Messenger from receiving reinforcements from Nan Sirannon.Defeat the messengerGlavroleth is north-east of Porth Cadlus in Echad Dúnann.

You should return to Glavroleth and inform her of your success.Talk to Glavroleth at Echad Dúnann'Once the assault on Barad Morlas was launched, the half-orcs sent out messengers in secret. Our scouts have found that the first of these messengers has not yet reached Porth Cadlus.

'The messenger must be stopped, ${PLAYER}. If Barad Morlas receives reinforcements, they may have enough strength to break our defences and rout the Free Peoples in Eregion.

'The messenger was last seen west of Porth Cadlus, south-west of Echad Dúnann, across Nan Sirannon. You must be quick if you are to stop him before he reaches their camp.''The messenger has been dispatched? Now we need not fear the Dunlending axes of Nan Sirannon coming to the aid of Barad Morlas!''The messenger was last seen west of Porth Cadlus, south-west of Echad Dúnann, across Nan Sirannon.'You have defeated Thrâng and uncovered the plot of the Ever-seer.Eimyr can be found within Norbar in The Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.

You have defeated Thrâng and hold his weapon as proof of your valour. You should go to Eimyr and tell him of the Ever-seer's evil betrayal.You defeated Thrâng, and in the process, learned that the Ghâshfra serve the Ever-seer. Eimyr, Chieftain of the Eldgang, should be told at once, for he has been deceived by the Ever-seer and unwittingly serves his evil purposes.'You have defeated Thrâng of the Ghâshfra? You have earned a great reward from the Eldgang.

'What is this you say? The Ghâshfra serve the Ever-seer? The Ever-seer seeks to unleash the Great Evil upon us?

'The Eldgang will not stand idle and allow this thing to happen. We have been betrayed!'Glavroleth spotted Sam Gamgee's pony, Bill, wandering near Echad Dúnann.Bill the Pony was last seen in Torech Andraug, west of Echad Dúnann.

Glavroleth has asked that you find Bill the Pony and clear a path to safety.Find Bill the Pony'Many of the Elves of Echad Dúnann have seen a pony wandering among the hills; it seemed an odd place for such a beast, and a wonder that it survived with such great peril close at hand.

'I went myself in search of the pony, and when I caught sight of it, I immediately recognized the animal: it was the pony that the halfling, Samwise Gamgee, had kept stabled in Imladris.

'He had a wild look and had no bit or bridle. The pony was quite wary, but seemed in good health. I last saw him beyond the hills, west of Echad Dúnann in Torech Andraug. Go and see if you can clear a path for the pony to come back safely; the wild is no place for such a beast to roam by himself.''I last saw the animal in Torech Andraug, west of here.'You have found none other than Bill, the loyal little steed of Sam Gamgee.

Bill looks like he is in a bit of a tight-spot in this Warg-infested area.Hrotha has offered to provide you with some hand-crafted gear.Hoary aurochs are said to roam in the Misty Mountains. Redtusk is said to forage near Torech Braignel in north-east Eregion.

Hrotha requires the hides of hoary aurochs and the pelt of the great boar known as Redtusk.Barghests can be found in Imlad Balchorth in Angmar. The spiders can be found throughout Angmar.

Hrotha requires the tails of barghests and the spinnerets of spiders to continue.Sálvakh dwells in Carn Dûm in Angmar. Dushkâl, Brízrip, and Thordragh can be found at Urugarth in Angmar.

Hrotha needs the third and final set of items to complete his task.'A good spear may be a Warden's best ally, ${PLAYER}, but what good will it do when an Orc-archer riddles you with arrows? Or some warrior from Angmar puts a sword in your guts? Answer that, if you can. You will need some fine gear if you are going to face the greater threats in this world.

'If you're up to the task, I will guide you as you collect the items necessary to craft items of worth to one such as you. Bring me the components I need, ${PLAYER}, and we will see about making such items of virtue as would be of benefit to you.

'The first items I will need are hides from the hoary-aurochs which dwell in the Misty Mountains and the hide of the boar Redtusk, who roams the hills of Eregion.''Well, that is a beginning, ${PLAYER}. I will get these hides tanned and boiled, and you go get the next items on the list while I stitch them together.

'I now require the barbed tails of the barghests of Imlad Balchorth and the spinnerets of the spiders of Angmar.''Two batches out of three...the end of this work is in sight now, ${PLAYER}. This last bit will be the hardest, though.

'Bring me a Rune of Evil Presence from the sorcerer Sálvakh at Carn Dûm and the teeth of a Gorthorog...perhaps from Dushkâl, Brízrip, or Thordragh at Urugarth.''Ah, and now our work is done, ${PLAYER}. Take this gear and let it protect you from the worst the Enemy has to send against you. May it serve you well, friend.'Hrotha will forge a weapon of might and glory for you, with the right components.Angmarim can be found at Annúminas in Evendim. Bryok dwells in Forochel.

Hrotha wishes you to bring the breastplates from the Angmarim invaders in Annúminas and the spear of Bryok the Gauradan.Wights can be found in Angmar, Forochel, Annúminas, and Helegrod. Cave-claws can be found in Angmar and Sarnúr in Ered Luin.

Hrotha requires the remains of wights and the talons of a like number of Gorothlad cave-claws.Gríshakrum, Kughûrz, and Dafrim may be found at Urugarth. Barashal dwells in Carn Dûm.

Hrotha needs a tally-stick from the Orcs of the Ongbúrz and the horn of Barashal the Gorthorog.'So, the spear...your weapons are good enough for a vagabond warrior, but we wardens deserve the finest of spears, do we not, ${PLAYER}? Yes, I believe we do. Otherwise we might as well be Dunlending rabble jabbing at things with pointed sticks!

'Then let us make one for you, shall we? Yes, a spear that befits someone of your repute. Then the minstrels might sing about it as well as you!

'It means work for you, of course. I cannot do it all myself, after all, and nor should I! Bring me the breastplates of the Angmarim who swarm upon Annúminas and the spear of the Gauradan Bryok in Forochel. Go on now, ${PLAYER}...the Enemy will not wait while you dawdle!''Ah! This is good. This spear will do more good in your hands than it did in Bryok's, we can be sure of that.

'Let me get started, then. No sense dallying, is there, ${PLAYER}? You go find the next items and bring them back to me when you have them.

'I now need remains from the wights that roam Angmar, Forochel, Annúminas, or Helegrod and the gem-crusted claws of the cave-claws of Angmar and Sarnur.''You have returned? You have much resolve, ${PLAYER}. Many folk would have balked, but then that is what sets us apart, is it not?

'All right, then. Just one more set of items, and the spear will be yours. Bring to me a blood-stained tally-stick from the Orcs Gríshakrum, Kughûrz, and Dafrim in Urugarth and the crimson horn of the Gorthorog Barashal, who dwells in Carn Dûm.''That is it, then! A lot of toil for one weapon, but all the great arms of legend took much sweat and blood before they were ready.

'Here is your spear, as I promised. It is yours now, so use it well. I suppose it will not truly be finished until it has been bathed in Orc-blood, will it, ${PLAYER}?'Dardúr has agreed to help you create a garment worthy of a Rune-keeper of your standing.Huorns and prowlers dwell in Glâd Ereg in Eregion. Helchuan prowls near the springs north of Zigilgund in Forochel.

Dardúr wishes you to bring him vines gathered from the creatures of nature in Eregion and the hide of the cold-worm Helchuan.Barghests can be found in Angmar. Brimstone-leeches can be found in Angmar.

Dardúr now requires barghest paws and drops of brimstone-leech ichor.Lagmâs dwells at Urugarth and Târlug may be found at Carn Dûm in Angmar.

Dardúr now needs a Badge of Command and an ashen Gorthorog-horn to complete your reward.'We rune-keepers are not a vain lot, ${PLAYER}. You and I both know that knowledge is a treasure of the mind, not of our garments. Still, it is also true that others do not give us our due respect if we do not garb ourselves as they wish to see us. Such is the way of the unenlightened.

'Now is the time for you to gain the attire that will win that respect. Seek out the materials I need, and I will make for you such equipment that befits a rune-keeper.

'First, I will need the vines that grow through the hides of the creatures which lurk in Glâd Ereg in Eregion and the hide of the cold-worm Helchuan, who dwells in Forochel. May your way be a safe one, ${PLAYER}.''Very good, ${PLAYER}. These are a fine beginning. Now you must fetch the second set of items, as soon as you can.

'These include the paws of the barghests which prowl Fornost in the North Downs and Haudh Iarchith in Bree-land and the corrosive droplets of the brimstone-leeches of Angmar.''So, ${PLAYER}, we have two sets of the necessary items. Now only the third remains, and your garment shall be complete. Go now and finish your labours.

'I need now a Badge of Command from the uruk Lagmâs at Urugarth and a horn from the Gorthorog Târlug at Carn Dûm.''This is the last of it, ${PLAYER}. Your task has been long, but now your garment is complete. Wear it well, and all shall know that you are a keeper of runes.'Invoke the name of Elendil, the Elf-friend and king of old, and let his example guide your blade.Strike with Blade of ElendilInvoke the name of Elendil, the Elf-friend and king of old, and let his example guide your blade.Strike with Blade of ElendilThe time has come to enter Agoroth. You must reach the Helcessar before the Angmarim!Complete 'Agoroth, the Narrowdelve' on any difficulty.

Use the Instance Finder to enter Agoroth, the Narrowdelve.Complete 'Agoroth, the Narrowdelve' on any difficultyThe Angmarim have been defeated and the Helcessar destroyed.

You should speak to Lothrandir. He can be found inside Agoroth, inside Jä-reitti, or at the shipwreck of the Thoroval.Talk to LothrandirThe Angmarim have been defeated and the Helcessar destroyed.

You should speak to Arvedui. He can be found inside Agoroth, inside Jä-reitti, or at the shipwreck of the Thoroval.Talk to ArveduiYou have won a great victory against Angmar and freed Arvedui from his fate.

You should return to Elrond in Rivendell and inform him of your success.Talk to Elrond'Whenever you are ready, we will enter the Narrowdelve. Not even Arvedui knows what awaits us inside, but we must reach the Oath-stone before the Angmarim. And you must not forget, you promised the Lossoth you would find a way to destroy it so they are no longer bound by its magic.'

Lothrandir crosses his arms and looks down at the ice below. A wry smile appears on his face, barely visible beneath the shadows cast by his hood.

'Elrond did say that Men might remain in the world as shades if they break their sworn promises. If we are not careful, we might share the fate of King Arvedui! Come now, let us face our destinies together!''We have done it! The combined power of Arvedui and the Sardirath was enough to destroy the Helcessar for good. We have kept our word to the Lossoth, kept the Witch-king from corrupting the relic, and freed the Sardirath from their oath, allowing them to rest. And it seems they are not the only ones who have achieved some overdue peace.

'Arvedui's spirit seems to have changed from our experiences together, as if a great weight has been lifted from his shoulders. You should speak to him and see if this be so.''${PLAYER}, we must speak. I feel my connection to this world fading, but I cannot quite move on yet.

'Glamagol's honest words, and the knowledge that my line continues still have freed me from despair. I could never have imagined that in death I would finally deal a meaningful blow to Angmar.

'My deepest thanks to thee and to Lothrandir. Were it not for thy actions, I would still be haunting the shipwreck of the Thoroval, overcome with despair until the end of days. When thou returnest to Rivendell, offer my salutations to Elrond and Aragorn, but please, do not share with my heir that I can only rest upon his coronation. He carries the weight of the world as it is. I wish him fortune in the many battles to come.

'Farewell for now, ${PLAYER}. I will linger in the shadows, unseen, until the day I may depart.''${PLAYER}, we must speak. I feel my connection to this world fading, but I cannot quite move on yet.

'Glamagol's honest words, and the knowledge that my line continues still have freed me from despair. I could never have imagined that in death I would finally deal a meaningful blow to Angmar.

'My deepest thanks to thee and to Lothrandir. Were it not for thy actions, I would still be haunting the shipwreck of the Thoroval, overcome with despair until the end of days. When thou returnest to Rivendell, offer my salutations to Elrond and Aragorn, but please, do not share with my heir that I can only rest upon his coronation. He carries the weight of the world as it is. I wish him fortune in the many battles to come.

'Farewell for now, ${PLAYER}. I will linger in the shadows, unseen, until the day I may depart.''Welcome back, ${PLAYER}. The lightness of your steps informs me of your success! Please, tell me everything that has happened.'

You tell Elrond of everything that transpired: your promise to the Lossoth in exchange for the key, the realization that the Helcessar was contained in Agoroth, the Sardirath bound for thousands of years by oath, the battles with the Angmarim and Gauredain, the vision of the moment of Arvedui's death, the Helcessar's destruction, and finally, the releasing of the Sardirath.

'Astonishing, and a tale worth recording. While I am saddened to hear of the destruction of an artifact from Númenor, it is perhaps for the best. Many of the Vandassari have already fallen into the hands of the Enemy over the years, leading to calamity. The Helcessar would no doubt have turned the tide of war in favour of Angmar. I am, however, heartened to learn that Arvedui feels he will be able to move on upon his heir's coronation. I know you will do all in your power to help Aragorn reach that goal, as will I.

'You must be rewarded for all your efforts. This tabard was recovered from Annúminas along with the log that set you on this adventure. For your service to the fallen kingdom of Arnor, you have earned it.'Lengliel fears that the half-orcs at Barad Morlas and Pembar may call reinforcements from the east.Echad Dúnann lies along the road south and east from Echad Eregion, in Nan Sirannon.

Lengliel has asked you to seek out Glavroleth at Echad Dúnann.Talk to Glavroleth at Echad Dúnann'Your efforts have been valiant, ${PLAYER}, but I fear they will come to naught. The half-orcs have brethren encamped in the Redhorn Gate, and Dunlendings in the service of Saruman are encamped not far to the east.

'I ask that you go to Echad Dúnann to the east, near the gates of Moria called the Doors of Durin, and speak with Glavroleth. Warn her of the threat of the half-orcs...perhaps she will have some insight into their presence.

'Go forth without delay, ${PLAYER}. I fear an evil tide is rising.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. You come with tidings from Lengliel?

'The half-orcs have entered the ruins of Eregion? This is old news to us, but Lengliel is right to be concerned. There is some evil at work here, against which we must prepare ourselves.''Go to Echad Dúnann, ${PLAYER}, and warn Glavroleth of the half-orc threat.'The Fire-giant city of Norbar has been invaded by Fire-orcs from Angmar.Thrâng is within The Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.

Tortrygg has asked you to find and defeat Thrâng, leader of the Ghâshfra in the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.Defeat ThrângIorelen is at Iorelen's Camp above The Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.

You should bring your report of Thrâng's defeat to Iorelen and learn how you should proceed.'Ho there, little one. Perhaps you will help with our troubles here? The Ghâshfra, the Fire-orcs from the surface world, have come to Norbar and war wih us. Perhaps you will help to drive them away?

'The name of their chieftain is Thrâng. If he is slain, the Ghâshfra may learn not to trouble the Eldgang. Asmólf sought Thrâng, but was sent instead to the intruders' mine by Eimyr at the behest of the Ever-seer.

'Perhaps you will do this for us? Thrâng may be found within The Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu. If you defeat him, your aid will not be forgotten.''Defeat Thrâng, and you will have greatly aided the Eldgang.''You have defeated Thrâng? You are a brave warrior among your folk. You will not be forgotten by Tortrygg.'

Tortrygg seems to have little more to say. It may be that Glathlírel may have more insight as to how you should proceed.'Fire-orcs have invaded the Rift? We must be wary and learn more of the situation in Nûrz Ghâshu.'Defeat Hillmen in Barad Gúlaran.Defeat Hillmen in Barad GúlaranDefeat Hillmen in Barad Gúlaran.Defeat Hillmen in Barad GúlaranMaster self-control, and many techniques will be possible that were not before.Use Devastating Strike, Exchange of Blows, or AdamantEimyr, the chieftain of the Eldgang, realized the folly of his ways and has chosen to stand against the ancient evil which lies within the halls of Nûrz Gâashu.Glathlírel stands vigilant outside of Gorthban.

Eimyr has instructed you to find Glathlírel and aid her against Thaurlach, a Balrog of Morgoth.Defeat ThaurlachIorelen is at Iorelen's Camp.

You have slain Thaurlach and should tell Iorelen of your deeds.'The Ever-seer lied to the Eldgang. We will no longer listen to his foul words.

'For many centuries, we suffered the cruelty of Thaurlach, a Balrog of the Great Enemy, and fought against him. When the Blue Wanderers came with the Elf Glathlírel and chained Thaurlach, our people were glad and helped to watch him. Now the Ever-seer wishes to free Thaurlach and use him for his own evil purposes.

'Go to Gorthban and help Glathlírel, who remains here still in vigil over Thaurlach's prison. She will know what to do.''Go to Gorthban and aid the Elf Glathlírel.''Thaurlach is dead? You should go forth and proclaim these tidings to the overworld.''What is this you tell me, ${PLAYER}? The Balrog Thaurlach has been defeated? That is a hope we dared not dream of. Your name shall be recorded in the lore of the Elves as long as time shall last.

'Take this as a reward for your dauntless courage.'Those who strike first often see more success, and you like your odds.Use the Surprise Strike skillDefeat Ancient Evil in Carn DûmDefeat Ancient Evil in Carn DûmDefeat Ancient Evil in Carn DûmDefeat Ancient Evil in Carn DûmWhile the Lossoth of Forochel are not great builders, the dwarves long ago had many mines and encampments in the North.NOT USEDIn an age when dwarves mined the crags and bluffs of Forochel, they built many fortresses. Kibilzahar is one such place, though it has been long abandoned. Rumour has it that fell spirits now dwell here, but rumours run wild among old ruins....The Ironspan was built by the dwarves long ago, as they sought precious metals and gems in the mountains of Forochel and Angmar. When the dwarves abandoned their endeavours, the Witch-realm of Angmar wrested control of the great gates.Zigilgund is an ancient dwarf-site to which the Longbeards have only recently returned.The Lonesome Stones are a weary and ruined reminder of ancient days when dwarves mined the crags of Forochel.The Icereave Mines long stood unused, but now the Dourhands seek to use them for their own benefit.Protecting oneself in battle is paramount when dealing with the monsters of Middle-earth.Use Armour of the ElementsBrúnfair called you to his aid to seek out and stop the creation of an army within the walls of Barad Gúlaran. With the discovery of Amarthiel's plan, the Council of the North has swiftly reacted to the newly discovered threat and wishes to dispatch those brave enough to face the enemies within the fortress.

Their hope is that the army Amarthiel promised to summon can be stopped when there is but one such enemy to stop the destruction of Eriador.Find the terror called to service in the halls of Barad Gúlaran and put an end to it before the sorceresses can summon more of the beasts to Angmar's service.

Brúnfair dispatched you to Barad Gúlaran to face and defeat the great evil the sorceresses call to the service of Angmar. This army must be destroyed before it grows too large for the few defenders of Eriador to face.Defeat the Terror within Barad GúlaranYou have succeeded in defeating the terror within Barad Gúlaran. Return to Brúnfair and tell him of your victory.

After a grueling battle, you successfully faced and slew the first Rogmul enslaved by the sorceresses of Barad Gúlaran.'If Amarthiel's words are true we must strike swiftly and concisely. We will not be given reprieve or quarter should we fail.

'We must rely on others to assist us against this foe while we work with Golodir to determine our best course of pursuit. If the sorcerers within Barad Gúlaran succeed at calling forth the ancient evil they mean to control, we shall face a foe we do not have the numbers to defeat.

'I ask you now, ${PLAYER}, to gather your fellows, enter the accursed halls of Barad Gúlaran, and face the Enemy called to the service of Angmar. I do not know how you will defeat this horror, I only know that you shall see great reward for your success.''We cannot discount all you have done, ${PLAYER}. Your efforts have given Eriador another breath of life and returned hope.

'I will send word south to Halbarad of your success and your name will be spoken of and written in the annals kept by the lore-masters of Esteldín. 

'You are well-deserved of this reward, my friend. Be safe in your journeys and know that we shall ever regard you as a hero for all your sacrifices.''Be swift and safe. Stand with your fellows and do not lose heart. The enemy you will face will be greater than any other you have seen. I think even the False King will pale against this enemy.'Dawn Stockard has agreed to help you fashion a piece of equipment worthy of your stature as a captain.Nerbyg can be found in the Eastern Malenhad in Angmar. Hogni Cleave-shank can be found at Gabilazan in the Misty Mountains.

Dawn Stockard has asked you to bring her norbog-carapaces and Hogni Cleave-shank's spiked helmet.Collect steam-scorched carapaces (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect Hogni's spiked helmetDawn Stockard is in the town of Archet, north of Bree.

Dawn Stockard is waiting for you to return with the first set of components.Nerbyg can be found in the Eastern Malenhad in Angmar. Bats can be found in Angmar and other dangerous places.

Dawn Stockard has asked you to bring her corroded Neekerbreeker-horns and grisly bat-talons.Collect corroded Neekerbreeker-horns (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect grisly bat-talons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dawn Stockard is in the town of Archet, north of Bree.

Dawn Stockard is waiting for you to return with the second set of components.Helchgam can be found beneath the evil city of Carn Dûm in Angmar. Lagmâs can be found at Urugarth in Angmar.

