You should return to Lengliel and tell her of your victory at Barad Morlas.Talk to Lengliel at Echad Eregion'Do you see this sign here? Perhaps your eye is not trained to see it. This banner names one Horngoth, a half-orc of a most ill repute, as the leader of these forces.

'Journey now to Barad Morlas. At the northern foot of a bridge which leads further into the ruins, you will find a summoning horn, not unlike the one at Pembar. Blow upon the horn, and surely Horngoth will answer.

'The bridge lies south-west of here. Hurry!''Find the summoning horn at Barad Morlas, south-west of here, and use it to challenge Horngoth!''It is good that you have defeated Horngoth, but his defeat may just bring more enemies down upon us. Our scouts tell us that there are more servants of the White Hand in the passes of the Redhorn Gate and Nan Sirannon.'You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the downfall of Narmeleth.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the fall of Narmeleth long ago.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete Daughter of StrifeYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the fall of Narmeleth long ago.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the fall of Narmeleth long ago.You think about what you have read and ponder the fall of Narmeleth as it was told to you from her lips.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of 'Daughter of Strife.'You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the Fall of Moria.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the Fall of Moria.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete The Fall of MoriaYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the fall of Moria and should close the book.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the fall of Moria. It is grim reading.You think about what you have read and mourn the losses of so many in the depths of Moria.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of the 'Fall of Moria.'You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the fate of Laerdan during his flight to Eregion.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the flight of Laerdan to Eregion while in possession of Narchuil.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete The Fate of LaerdanYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the flight of Laerdan to Eregion while in possession of Narchuil.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the flight of Laerdan into Eregion while in possession of Narchuil.You think about what you have read and ponder the doings of Laerdan in Eregion.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of 'The Fate of Laerdan.'You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the curious imprisonment of Laerdan in Sammath Baul.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the imprisonment of Laerdan in Sammath Baul.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete The Prisoner of Sammath BaulYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the imprisonment of Laerdan in Sammath Baul and should close the book.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the imprisonment of Laerdan in the tower of Sammath Baul. It is grim reading, drawn as it is from the account of his keepers.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of 'The Prisoner of Sammath Baul.'You think about what you have read and ponder the actions of the servants of Angmar.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the sad end of Balin's expedition to Moria.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the sad end of Balin's expedition.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete We Cannot Get OutYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the sad end of Balin's expedition and should close the book.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the sad end of Balin's expedition. It is grim reading.You think about what you have read and mourn the death of Balin and his loyal companions in the dark reaches of Moria.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of 'We Cannot Get Out.'Snow-trolls are among many evil creatures which have taken refuge in the ruins of the ancient dwarf-city called Sarnúr by the Elves.Snow-trolls can be found deep within Sarnúr, south of Thorin's Hall reached through the ruins of Orodost.

Snár has asked you to enter the ancient ruins of Sarnúr and defeat a number of the snow-trolls gathered there.Defeat snow-troll bruisers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat snow-troll hurlers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Snár is in Thorin's Hall, north of Sarnúr, just outside the Blue Stone Garrison.

You should return to Snár with news of your victory against the snow-trolls.Talk to Snár in Thorin's Hall'Greetings, brave ${PLAYER}. It is good to see you.

'Deep within Sarnúr, a large force of trolls has been gathering for some time. As my folk have explored further into the depths, these creatures have become more fearsome. Would you gather a party of friends and dispatch these trolls?

'Travel to Sarnúr, south of Thorin's Hall, deep beyond the ruins of Orodost, which can be found along the western mountains of Ered Luin.''I cannot abide trolls! Drive them out of Sarnúr, if you are able!''Greetings, ${PLAYER}. I wish to thank you for entering the caverns and dispatching those foul creatures.

'Reports from the area tell that the problem is much worse than we had known. Your accomplishments will be spread among the Dwarves of Ered Luin.

'Please seek me out if you decide to travel to Sarnúr again, as I may have more work for you.'Nicca has told you of the fell spirits which wander the glacier and the creature that draws them forth, entombing them within the frozen husks of Men to serve as dark warriors.Kibilzahar lies to the north-west of Kuru-leiri.

Nicca told you that some evil power has trapped fell spirits within the bodies of wights in Kibilzahar. Only the warmth of fire can banish them. He has told you to re-kindle the ancient fires that the dwarves once used to heat the ancient fortress. He also warned you of an evil lord who haunts that place, and to be ready to face him should you go there again.Summon and defeat DeluchelegArre is at Kuru-leiri, south-east of Kibilzahar.

Having defeated Delucheleg, the Gaunt-man responsible for raising the wights, you should return to Arre and tell him of your discoveries and battles.Talk to Arre in Kuru-leiri'The fell spirits of Kibilzahar bring cold and ice and make it a terrible place. You must bring warmth and fire to that cold, desolate place of stone.

'Once, when the Kivi-väki were there, there was warmth. They lit great fires to warm the very stones. So must you do to banish the spirits. Be warned though. There is a dark manâja hidden there. It is he who commands the spirits, and he will not like the fire. You must not take this foe lightly, or you too will die there, a frozen corpse that walks.

'Now go. This is not a place for you. It is mine, and I shall die here alone when my time comes.''Delucheleg is very old and very evil...be wary of his power.''A servant of Angmar? Here upon the very jätikkö? The Elders will be furious to hear that the Rauta-väki have already stretched their hands so far into our lands.

'Thank you for your help. If Nicca was indeed correct and that creature was the one raising the Râto, then soon perhaps the dark mâginen will fade and they will haunt Kibilzahar no more.

'Allow me to give you this gift for all you have done. I must go soon and tell the Council of Elders what has transpired here.'You must learn to make the most of personal sacrifice to be of the greatest aid to your fellows and your cause.Use the Time of Need skillYou have come to believe that confusion is only scratching the surface. With a bit of work, you could stupify them where they stand.Strike with the Confound skillMaster self-control, and many techniques will be possible that were not before.Use Controlled BurnHimbar is haunted by the merrevail and their kindred, the bats. These creatures prey upon the Rangers of Gath Forthnír, showing no mercy.The merrevail hunt throughout Himbar, the lands west of Gath Forthnír.

Thoriel has asked you to remove the lieutenant, Iarvael, from the morroval caverns.Thoriel waits for you in Gath Forthnír, the hidden Ranger-camp east of Himbar.

You have defeated Iorvael and should now return to Thoriel with news of your victory.'You have done well so far, but I have need of your services a bit further. There is a nearby cavern area where the merrevail live. They are led by one of the evil morroval-lieutenants, if such a word applies.

'Her name is Iarvael, and she haunts us even in our dreams. Please remove her threat once and for all, then return to me when the deed is done.

'This will be no small task, and you should gather one or two of your friends to help you, ${PLAYER}.''You have done the Rangers of Gath Forthnír a great service. You have allowed us to stop taking the defensive posture.

'We must take this fight to Carn Dûm!''If you desire to assist us in our plight, journey west and find the cavern and defeat Iarvael.'Develop the eye to spot and target the weak points of your foes more reliably.Achieve critical hits with your bowYour spear-thrust will weaken your foe's armour.Strike with The Dark Before Dawn<rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers.</rgb><rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers</rgb>Complete Volume I, Book 15: Daughter of StrifeYour spear-thrust will weaken your foe's armour.Strike with The Dark Before DawnDrownholtDrownholtDrownholtDrownholtBe an example to your shield-brother, and you will find your inspiration greater still.Use the Inspire skillBe an example to your shield-brother, and you will find your inspiration greater still.Use the Inspire skillThere is much to do while travelling through the lands of Eriador.Complete Deeds of Angmar deedComplete Deeds of Bree-land deedComplete Deeds of Ered Luin deedComplete Protector of Eregion deedComplete Deeds of Evendim deedComplete Deeds of Forochel deedComplete Deeds of the Lone-lands deedComplete Deeds of the Misty Mountains deedComplete Deeds of the North Downs deedComplete Deeds of the Shire deedComplete Deeds of the Trollshaws deedDardúr has sent you to Elrond Halfelven, one of the Wise, to learn the deep secrets of Rune-craft.Dardúr knows of a legendary rune-keeper who could teach you great lessons, but first you must be suitably outfitted.Complete A Worthy GarmentComplete Hewing a New StoneDardúr is at Gondamon in Ered Luin.Elrond's house lies in the vale of Rivendell in the Trollshaws.

Dardúr has bidden you seek the guidance of Elrond Halfelven so you might learn from him.Talk to Elrond in Rivendell'The secrets of our trade hark back to Daeron and Fëanor themselves, but both Elves and dwarves have added much to the depth of our craft. Many ancient secrets of the written word lie hidden in the forgotten places of Middle-earth.

'The road ahead is hard, but what thing that is worth doing is not? The gaining of wisdom is ever a struggle, but it is a fulfilling one. If you believe you are ready, then say on, and I will set your feet upon the path to greater knowledge.''You should complete the tasks I set before you.''Great is your skill with runes, ${PLAYER}. Seldom have I met one with a deeper understanding of their mysteries. And yet, can we not all learn new things, no matter how wise we may be?

'Little need be said of Elrond Halfelven. His knowledge is as broad as the Sundering Sea and deep as the pools of Celebrant. The runes are not strange to him, for he has studied them since the Elder Days.

'Go to him, ${PLAYER}, and seek his guidance. His wisdom will shed light where all is in gloom.''Oft of old I studied with Elrond, and each time was like the rising of the sun. My mind is to his as a spring's trickle is to the torrents of Bruinen. There is no better place to learn the worth of runes than by his side.

'How I envy you the chance to go to Rivendell and hear the wisdom he speaks, one more time. Ah, but my place is here now, and I fear I shall see Rivendell nevermore.''Mae govannen, ${PLAYER}, Elf-friend. It is ever a joy to meet one who studies the crafts of old, for such lore is fading in the world, and I fear will soon be lost on this side of the Sea. For three Ages now I have watched as the Rune-keepers of my youth dwindled in number. It gladdens my heart to meet one who studies them anew.

'Know, then, that the Angerthas and the Tengwar do not give up their secrets lightly. Long study awaits you, great though your power may be. Do not take the easy path of wresting the power from the runes, but consider each, both alone and in the company of other letters. I have pored over ancient tomes since the ink was fresh upon their pages, and yet I still do not know their full meaning. Each time, there is something new to be learned.

'Patience is the key... a patience that Men cannot know, and that will vanish from Middle-earth when the Elves and dwarves are gone, for the Dúnedain shall become as other Men. A light will go out of the world then, but if you treat the runes with care, ${PLAYER}, it will burn yet a while longer.'Many evil things besides the Dourhands dwell deep within Sarnúr...many which put dread even into the heart of other evil.Gâdhûp and Brúllug can be found in Sarnúr, south of Thorin's Hall, reached through the ruins of Orodost.

Snár has asked you to defeat either Gâdhûp the Snow-troll or Brúllug the drake.Snár is in Thorin's Hall, north of Sarnúr, just outside the Blue Stone Garrison.

You should return to Snár and tell him of your victory over the terrors of Sarnúr.Talk to Snár in Thorin's Hall'Greetings, good ${CLASS}! I hope that you are safe and well?

'Buried beneath the ruins of Orodost, in the depths of Sarnúr, lurk two horrible creatures. The first is a hideous Snow-troll, Gâdhûp, and the second is a wretched drake that goes by the name of Brúllug. Would you gather allies and travel to the Sarnúr to dispatch either of these vile creatures? 

'Travel to Sarnúr, which is located south of Thorin's Hall, deep beyond the ruins of Orodost, which can be found along the western mountains of Ered Luin.''Ah! It's good to see that you are well, ${CLASS}. 

'I have received word that you were able to dispatch one of the foul horrors of Sarnúr. I shall spread the word of your great deeds to the Dwarves of Ered Luin. 

'Please return to me if you choose to enter Sarnúr again, as I may have additional work for you.''There are other creatures as vile as trolls in Sarnúr.'A messenger from Barad Morlas has reached the half-orcs at Târ Stazg, warning them of the presence of the Elves in Eregion.Târ Stazg is north-east of Echad Dúnann along the mountains before the Redhorn Gate.

Palanaer has asked you to journey to Târ Stazg and defeat the Half-orcs before they can reinforce Barad Morlas.Defeat half-orcs in Târ Stazg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Palanaer is in Echad Dúnann south-west of Târ Stazg.

You should return to Palanaer and tell him of the half-orc defeat.Talk to Palanaer at Echad Dúnann'The half-orcs at Târ Stazg prepare for battle; one of the messengers out of Barad Morlas must have reached them sometime in the night.

'We cannot allow them to bring a force to bear at Barad Morlas. Assail their camp and reduce their numbers; it will be with a small host that they will issue forth to strengthen the battle.

'Târ Stazg lies north-east of Echad Dúnann along the mountains before the Redhorn Gate. Go as quickly as you can and return once their numbers have been reduced.''You have done a brave deed thinning the ranks of the half-orcs, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps now they will not be so quick to render aid to Barad Morlas.''Târ Stazg is north-east of Echad Dúnann along the mountains before the Redhorn Gate. You must hurry before they bring aid to Barad Morlas!'Advance to a high enough level for deeper delvingsYou feel ready to delve deeper and face more difficult challengesYou have fought the forces of evil and triumphed in the lesser dark.Complete a Delving Mission on Tier 1Complete a Tier 1 Delving MissionYou are called to do all that needs to be done to overthrow the fortress of Urugarth.Complete The City of OrcsComplete The Foul Idols of UrugarthComplete Marching OrdersComplete The Beasts of UrugarthComplete Champions of UrugarthComplete The Orcs of UrugarthComplete The Trolls of UrugarthYou have discovered the entrance to Askâd-mazal, the Chamber of ShadowsDiscover the entrance to Askâd-mazal, the Chamber of Shadows.Discover the entrance to Askâd-mazal, the Chamber of ShadowsSnow-trolls are among the terrible enemies that have taken refuge in the ancient dwarf-city of Sarnúr.Snow-trolls can be found deep within Sarnúr, south of Thorin's Hall, reached through the ruins of Orodost.

Snár has asked you to defeat the snow-trolls which dwell in the ancient dwarf-ruins.Defeat snow-troll rippers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Snár is in Thorin's Hall, north of Orodost, just outside the Blue Stone Garrison.

You should return to Snár with news of your success against the snow-trolls.Talk to Snár in Thorin's Hall'Greetings, ${PLAYER}, following the defeat of the Dourhands in Thorin's Hall, the Dourhands were driven to the ruins of Orodost. From there, they made their way deep into the ancient dwarf-city, which the Elves now call Sarnúr.

'We are sending warriors to rout the Dourhands, but still deeper in the ruins, there is a more sinister threat. Below the main antechamber, there is a frozen crag that has become the home to snow-trolls. Please gather a party of fellow adventurers and seek out these creatures.

'Travel to Sarnúr, south of Thorin's Hall deep beyond the ruins of Orodost, which can be found along the western mountains of Ered Luin.''The thought of those vile trolls despoiling a dwarf-hold is unbearable!''Thank you for journeying to Sarnúr and defeating the snow-trolls. Word of your bravery will be spread among the Longbeards of Thorin's Hall.

'If you plan to return to the ruins, please seek me out. I may have more tasks for you.'You are called upon to do all that needs to be done to overthrow Carn Dûm.Complete Plans of WarComplete Ancient Evil of Carn DûmComplete The Angmarim of Carn DûmComplete Champions of Carn DûmComplete The Orcs of Carn DûmComplete The Slugs of Carn DûmComplete The Trolls of Carn DûmComplete The Enemy's StrongholdLimlaer has enlisted your aid in setting false trails to lure the Enemy south.The site for the false camp is south-west of Limlaer, between Porth Cadlus and Goeolhad, in a boulder-encircled basin.

Limlaer has tasked you with setting a trap for the scouts of the Enemy.Limlaer is south-east of Echad Dúnann, along the Nan Sirannon road.

You should return to Limlaer with news of your success.'It seems that your trail worked, ${PLAYER}. A tracking-party was seen by another of my scouts close at hand to the false trail. Now we must draw the trap closed!

'Travel south-west of here between Porth Cadlus and Goeolhad. Look to the south of the ravine that runs westward to the hilltop above for some tall boulders overlooking a small clearing that lies just west of the tower on the edge of Nan Sirannon.

'There, you must set up a false camp and lie in wait for the scouts. Be careful, though... the scouts must not be allowed to return to their foul masters.

'If the Enemy is tricked into believing that the Company has gone through the Gap of Rohan or around the White Mountains, their real trail may remain hidden for good. Return to me when you have completed the task.''You should set the camp south-west of here, between Porth Cadlus and Goeolhad, in a boulder-encircled basin, but you must hurry before the scouts find the real trail.''The scout-leader lies dead? You have done a great thing, ${PLAYER}. I will report of your deeds to Lord Elrond.'Goblin-town has sent an emissary to Minas Maur to treat with Angmar.Minas Maur is one of the large towers of Barad Gúlaran, south of Gath Forthnír.

You must travel to Barad Gúlaran and defeat the emissary from Goblin-town inside Minas Maur. Brúfair suggested you take allies with you.Gath Forthnír is north of Barad Gúluran.

Having defeated Prakhum, return to Gath Forthnír and let Brúnfair know what you have done.'The text you recovered told of an alliance between Angmar and the goblins of Goblin-town.  Rumour has been told that Angmar and Goblin-town have had troubles entreating in the past. It is strange to find the goblins here. Perhaps this emissary is here without the knowledge of his leaders.

'You should investigate the matter. There have been many reports of goblins near the tower of Minas Maur near Barad Gúlaran. We must stop this alliance from happening, no matter the cost.

'Defeat the emissary and return to me when the deed is done. If you wish to succeed, you will likely want to take allies with you.''You must defeat the goblin Prakhum and stop the alliance between the Iron Crown and the goblins of the Misty Mountains.''You have helped the Free Peoples avoid the greatest of threats! An alliance between the goblins of the Misty Mountains and the Iron Crown would have been disastrous!'Having discovered a pledge from the Dourhands, you must enter Minas Angos and defeat their emissary to stop an alliance between them and the Iron Crown.Minas Angos is a tower in Barad Gúlaran, south of Gath Forthnír.

You must travel to Barad Gúlaran and defeat Laugi Goldentongue, the emissary of the Dourhands, inside Minas Angos. Brúnfair suggested you take allies.Brúnfair is at Gath Forthnír, north of Barad Gúlaran.

Having defeated Laugi Goldentongue, you should return to Brúnfair and tell him of your victory.'The text you recovered told of an alliance between Angmar and the Dourhands. Our scouts tell us that an emissary of the Dourhands has come to treat with Angmar.

'You should investigate the matter. There have been many reports of dwarves near the tower of Minas Angos near Barad Gúlaran. We must stop this alliance from happening, no matter the cost.

'Defeat the emissary, Laugi Goldentongue, then return back to me when the deed is done. If you are to succeed, you should take allies.''Laugi Goldentongue must be defeated, else the Iron Crown and the Dourhands will forge a stronger alliance.''The downfall of the Dourhands' emissary will be sure to sever the alliance between these two evil powers. Well done, ${PLAYER}.'The Trév Duvárdain have sent an emissary to Agar to treat with the minions of the Iron Crown.Minas Agar stands on the western side of Barad Gúlaran, south of Gath Forthnír.

You must travel to Barad Gúlaran and defeat Karstíona, the emissary of the Trév Duvárdain, inside Minas Agar.Brúnfair is in Gath Forthnír, north of Barad Gúlaran.

Having defeated Karstíona, you should return to Brúnfair and tell him of your success.'The text you recovered told of an alliance between Angmar and the Trév Duvárdain. I have done some research and learned that Domongart has sent an emissary to treat with Angmar. 

'You should investigate the matter. There have been many reports of Hillmen of the Trév Duvárdain near the tower of Minas Agar near Barad Gúlaran. We must stop this alliance from happening, no matter the cost.

'Defeat Karstíona, the emissary, then return to me. If you wish to enter Minas Agar, I suggest you take trustworthy allies.''Karstíona must be defeated and the alliance between the Iron Crown and the Hillmen sundered.''Of all alliances the Iron Crown could have forged, this was the most dire. If they lent greater support to the Trév Duvárdain, our efforts here would have been in vain.

'Well done!'Having intercepted a pledge from Saruman of Isengard, you must enter Minas Caul and defeat his emissary to stop an alliance between the Iron Crown and the White Hand.Minas Caul is one of the large towers of Barad Gúlaran, south of Gath Forthnír.

You must travel to Barad Gúlaran and issue a challenge to Bâlak, the emissary of the White-hand, inside Minas Caul.Brúnfair is at Gath Forthnír, north of Barad Gúlaran.

Having defeated Bâlak, you should return to Gath Forthnír and tell Brúnfair that you have accomplished the deed.'The text you recovered told of an alliance between Angmar and Saruman. I have investigated the matter and found that the White Hand has sent an emissary to treat with Angmar.

'As a result, there have been many reports of Orcs gathering near the tower of Minas Caul near Barad Gúlaran. We must stop this alliance from happening, no matter the cost.

'Defeat the emissary, the half-orc Bâlak, and return back to me when the deed is done. You will want to take allies if you are to succeed!''You must defeat Bâlak and keep the White Hand from allying itself with the Iron Crown.''You have helped the Free Peoples avoid the greatest of threats in the North! An alliance of this magnitude would certainly have spelled the downfall of Eriador.

'Thank you for helping us push back evil this day.'A plague of crawlers has been a cause of concern for the Elves of Echad Dúnann.Crawlers can be found at Emyn Naer, south-west of Echad Dúnann.

Tinúriel has asked that you slay the corrupt crawlers of Emyn Naer.Defeat crawlers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tinúriel is in Echad Dúnann, north-east of Emyn Naer.

You should return at once to Tinúriel and inform her of your success.Talk to Tinúriel at Echad Dúnann'Even the smallest worm has its place in Middle-earth, but it seems that Saruman's corruption can reach those both large and small.

'The worms of Emyn Naer have been feeding upon the corrupt flesh of the wanton slaying of the servants of Saruman. They have grown heavy with the vile flesh, and they no longer feed only upon the remains of the dead, but have been seen feasting upon the living.

'This plague must be put to an end, ${PLAYER}. The crawlers can be found throughout Emyn Naer, south-west of Echad Dúnann.''Though they will not be wholly destroyed as long as Saruman's corruption remains in Eregion, you have eased the burden upon the land.''The crawlers can be found throughout Emyn Naer, south-west of Echad Dúnann.'You defeated many Orcs in Urugarth, but your task is not yet complete. The Uruk-leader remains.The leader of the Orc-army makes his home deep within Urugarth, west of Gath Forthnír.

Donaith told you of a mighty Uruk-general that must be defeated.Donaith is at Gath Forthnír, east of Urugarth.

You should return to Gath Forthnír and tell Donaith the good news of your victory.'Uruks are loathesome, vicious creatures without a shred of honour or courage... only a mindless rage and a lust for blood.

'Their general is no different, save for the fervour of his hatred. He serves the Iron Crown without question and leads his army into battle without mercy. Defeat him, and you will prove your mettle.

'You will find him in the deepest reaches of Urugarth to the west.''Go into Urugarth and defeat their general.''So, it is done. It will not bring back the slain, but it will perhaps give me peace knowing my people have been avenged.'Your efforts in the Ettenmoors deserve acknowledgment, and the smith at Ost Ringdyr looks to provide you with a great boon. Visit Lieutenant Ovorestel to learn more.Make your way to Ost Ringdyr to speak with Lieutenant Ovorestel. She is a guard there who helps in the construction and maintenance of weaponry used in battle across the Ettenmoors.Speak with Lieutenant Ovorestel at Ost RingdyrLieutenant Ovorestel told you to go into the Ettenmoors and defeat a true enemy. Find an enemy out of Angmar that fights with fearsome tenacity and defeat them in battle to earn your reward.Defeat a monster player in the EttenmoorsThis simple voucher, penned clearly by Elvish hands, offers the bearer services from Lieutenant Ovorestel.

Lieutenant Ovorestel is a guard at Ost Ringdyr, north of Glân Vraig. She is known to be a scholarly sort who helps to maintain the weapons used by the Free People in the Ettenmoors.'${PLAYER}, for your services across the Ettenmoors you are recognized and well-regarded.

'The weapon you wield serves you well.'

She looks your weapon over and lightly touches the blade.

'Bloody this weapon by defeating a true enemy here in the Ettenmoors and you will grow its legend and yours.

'Thank you for your service in these dark times.'Your efforts in the Ettenmoors deserve acknowledgment, and the smith at Ost Ringdyr looks to provide you with a great boon. Visit Lieutenant Ovorestel to learn more.Make your way to Ost Ringdyr to speak with Lieutenant Ovorestel. She is a guard there who helps in the construction and maintenance of weaponry used in battle across the Ettenmoors.Speak with Lieutenant Ovorestel at Ost RingdyrLieutenant Ovorestel told you to go into the Ettenmoors and defeat a true enemy. Find an enemy out of Angmar that fights with fearsome tenacity and defeat them in battle to earn your reward.Defeat a monster player in the EttenmoorsThis ornate voucher, penned clearly by Elvish hands, offers the bearer services from Lieutenant Ovorestel.

Lieutenant Ovorestel is a guard at Ost Ringdyr, north of Glân Vraig. She is known to be a scholarly sort who helps to maintain the weapons used by the Free People in the Ettenmoors.'${PLAYER}, your service is well-known and oft celebrated here in the Ettenmoors.

'The enemy knows your weapon and trembles when you take the field.'

She looks your weapon over and places her hand against it.

'Bloody this weapon by defeating a true enemy here in the Ettenmoors and you will grow its legend and yours.

'Do not falter. There is always hope.'Make the most of successful coordination with your allies, be it to strike fast or ready an escape.Use the Escape Clause skillValgút has warned you about the cave-lizards that make their home within the Brackwater, and wants you to defeat some of them if you purpose to enter the cave.Cave-lizards can be found throughout the cave known as the Brackwater; its entrance is in the hill along the slope that winds up to the Hollin Gate.

Valgút has asked you to defeat several cave-lizards that dwell within the Brackwater and threaten the safety of the expedition.Defeat cave-lizards in the Brackwater (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Valgút is outside the entrance to the Brackwater, on the slope that winds up to the Hollin Gate.

You have defeated many of the dangerous cave-lizards within the Brackwater and should return now to Valgút.'A moment, if you please, ${PLAYER}. We've already lost contact with four good dwarves inside the Brackwater, and I want to be sure you know what you're in for if you dare to follow them.

'The cave seems to have many winding tunnels, and the whole network of passages is very dark, unwholesomely so. Cave-lizards crawl from rock to rock amid the damp, and are more ferocious than their small size would have you believe.

'If you mean to delve into the Brackwater on some errand, be cautious! Thin the cave-lizard horde while you are there. The creatures are a menace to any who tread too near their lair, and I do not know how long it will be before the gate is cleared and we can leave this place.''You will have no trouble finding cave-lizards within the Brackwater, ${PLAYER}. Indeed, they will likely find you!''It is good to see you return from the depths of that noxious cavern, ${PLAYER} -- too few have done so.

'Fewer cave-lizards lurk now among the stagnant pools to threaten the unaware, it would seem. I thank you for your service, and reward you with these coins. May they be of use to you!'Brogur sent four dwarves to explore the cave known as the Brackwater, but they have not yet reported back to him. He is anxious to learn their fate.The four dwarves are somewhere inside the Brackwater, the cave up the slope east of Brogur.

Brogur wants to know what happened to the four dwarves he sent to investigate the Brackwater, and has charged you with finding them.Brogur is outside the cave known as the Brackwater, down the slope to its west.

You have found the four dwarves Brogur sent into the Brackwater, and should report to him of their fates.'Hm, pardon? Can I help you? My cousin and I have a great number of things to manage here, and I do not have a moment to spare on other matters!

'You wish to help? Well, if you're willing to work, I can certainly find a use for you. I dispatched four of my finest dwarves to investigate the cavern that flows with water; it is just up the slope to the east, and they have not yet reported back. If you can find these four inside the Brackwater and bring me word of their findings, I will reward you for your trouble.

'Now that you have a task with which to busy yourself, pray do not linger about, for I find it distracting. Leave me! There is much I must consider.''The Brackwater is the cave just up the slope to the east, on your left. Four of my best dwarves went inside to explore the cave, and have not returned.''Brogur sent you to check on us, ${PLAYER}? I cannot blame him, for it is well past time that we returned to give our report!

'We were separated shortly after entering this foul dank cavern -- Brackwater indeed! I heard Ógur shouting from somewhere up above here about some sort of treasure he had found, but then his calls faded and I have heard nothing from him since. Where Súlki and Wethur ended up I do not know.

'I hope you can find my kinsmen, ${PLAYER}. We four have had poor fortune in this watery cave, and Brogur will not be pleased by our report.''These creatures are more dangerous than you might expect, ${PLAYER}; be careful as you search for the rest of my party! We were separated not long ago by the vicious beasts, and I have been unable to locate my fellows since.

'I heard Súlki calling for help not long ago, but I have not heard him in some hours. I fear he has fallen into danger.''Thank you for the tidings, ${PLAYER}, even though they are grim. I am saddened by the losses of Súlki and of Ógur. There is much work still to be done before we have cleared the Hollin Gate of the rubble that chokes it, and they were strong of arm and sturdy as the hardiest of our race.

'But your payment was not only for good news, and you have earned this reward. Take it and make yourself useful! There is still work to be done.'The dwarf Símbi has asked you to enter the cave Filgogân and obtain as much information about the half-orc camp as possible.Information about the half-orcs can be found at their camp-fires inside the cave Filgogân, south-west of Bósi's camp, across the dried-up Sirannon.

Símbi has asked you to obtain information from each of the half-orc camp-fires inside the cave Filgogân.Símbi is outside the cave Filgogân, south-west of Bósi's camp, across the dried-up Sirannon.

You have gathered a great deal of information about the half-orcs in Filgogân and should bring it all to Símbi.'Be careful, ${RACE}! This cave is home to a large encampment of half-orcs; they haven't been here long, but it seems to me that they are mustering their strength for some evil purpose. Once they are prepared, I fear that they will strike against my people!

'We cannot let that happen. If the Iron Garrison is to survive, we must ensure that the half-orcs are unable to construct an effective offence.

'Seek the camp-fires of the half-orcs inside Filgogân, and collect pieces of information from each of them. If you can retrieve any orders, maps, reports, or letters, we can surely leave these half-orcs too disorganized to pose a great threat to our expedition.''The half-orcs are sure to have the sort of information we're seeking at each of the camp-fires they've built inside the cave Filgogân. Orders, maps, plans, and letters -- all will be of use to us.''This information you have brought me paints a disturbing picture, ${PLAYER}. Someone has drawn arrows on this map in what looks to be blood, tracing a course down from the Trollshaws and into Hollin. And these orders are difficult to read, but seem to command the half-orcs to search for halflings. Could they mean Shire-folk? In Hollin? I confess I do not understand the meaning of these riddles.

'The half-orcs may not be here to stop the expedition, but they still threaten the work of the dwarves. It is to be hoped that your efforts have left them in disarray and unable to carry out a manoeuvre against my people.'Símbi wants to know who has sent the half-orcs to Filgogân, where their presence threatens the expedition of dwarves that has come to Hollin.Half-orcs inside the cave of Filgogân, south-west of Bósi's camp and across the dried-up Sirannon, surely carry some proof of their loyalty to Graug's master.

Símbi has asked you to ascertain the common thread that binds these half-orcs together, and bring evidence of it to him.Símbi is outside the entrance to the cave Filgogân, south-west of Bósi's camp, across the dried-up Sirannon.

You have recovered stones, crudely painted with the emblem of a hand, from many of the half-orcs within Filgogân, and should bring them now to Símbi.'Someone sent these half-orcs to Hollin, ${PLAYER}; it is clear to me that Graug, the chieftain who ordered the camp established inside Filgogân, has his own master. I want to know who this might be.

'I will reward you handsomely for evidence of this overseer. Enter the cave and do battle with the half-orcs, and bring me whatever common thread binds these half-orcs in loyalty to Graug's mysterious master.''Perhaps the half-orcs carry some sign of their loyalty to Graug's mysterious master. You should enter the cave and bring me proof of this common thread.''You say the half-orcs carried these stones, each of them painted with this hand symbol? This is not Graug's mark; it must certainly be the emblem of his master.

'I do not recognize it. I will show this mark to Bósi when I return to the camp; perhaps he will understand its significance.

'Thank you for your help, ${PLAYER}, and take these coins with my thanks.'Throttólf wants you to find the top of the waterfall inside the Brackwater and see if you can recover the treasure Ógur mentioned.The treasure of which Ógur spoke may be at the top of the waterfall deep within the cavern known as the Brackwater.

Throttólf has asked you to find the passages leading to the top of the waterfall so you can investigate the possibility that some treasure might be found there.Find the strongbox at the top of the waterfallThrottólf is in the cave called the Brackwater, near the entrance, down a passage from the base of the waterfall.

You found the strongbox at the top of the waterfall, but whatever treasure it might once have contained has long since fallen into dust. You should return to Throttólf with these tidings.'I am glad you're here, ${PLAYER}. My party became separated shortly after we entered the Brackwater, and I have not yet been able to regroup with them.

'I almost found my friend Ógur, however; he seems to have made it to the top of the waterfall down this passage, somehow, and I heard him shouting about some sort of strongbox that he found at the top of it. I fear that the cave-lizards must have been drawn by the sound of his shouting, for I have not heard from him since.

'If you can find the same passages he used to come to the top of the waterfall, however, you might be able to find the treasure of which he spoke! I will reward you handsomely for news of it, ${PLAYER}, and for news of Ógur's fate as well, of course.''If Ógur found some passage that could take him to the top of the waterfall down that passage, I am sure you can do the same. What treasure might the strongbox contain, I wonder?''You found the strongbox? Well, where is it? What treasure did Ógur find that can justify the losses we have suffered here?'

Throttólf's face falls as you tell him the strongbox contained only dust.

'I see. Perhaps I was a fool to expect a mighty treasure, but as we are so close to Khazad-dûm, I thought perhaps....

'It doesn't matter. Thank you for your help, ${PLAYER}. The real riches are sure to be inside the mines, rather than in these damp caves. This strongbox must have contained the belongings of some brigand long ago, and no treasure at all. It is a pity.'Trausti is worried that his friend Lofrík may have been killed by the half-orcs, and wants you to search for his distinctive gold tooth.Piles of bones can be found throughout the cave of Filgogân, and Lofrík's gold tooth might be among them.

Trausti has asked you to search the bone piles within the cave for the distinctive gold tooth of his friend Lofrík.Trausti is somewhere within the cave of Filgogân.

You have found Lofrík's distinctive gold tooth, and should return to Trausti with the sad tidings of his friend's death.'Keep it down, ${PLAYER}! I have managed to remain unseen by the half-orcs until now, but they are camped all throughout Filgogân and I do not know how much longer I can remain undetected.

'I have been searching the cavern for my friend Lofrík, who disappeared in the vicinity of Filgogân shortly after the expedition arrived in Hollin. I fear that he was killed by the half-orcs, but I must know for certain!

'The half-orcs construct grim piles containing the bones of their victims: you can see them at various locations throughout Filgogân. Lofrík had a distinctive gold tooth; this is an unpleasant business, but I will reward you well if you can search through these piles and return to me with his tooth, if you find it. I hope you do not find any such thing, but if you do, at least I will then know of Lofrík's fate.''I would be grateful to you if you were to search the skull-piles these half-orcs have made throughout Filgogân for the gold tooth of my friend Lofrík. I hope you do not find it, ${PLAYER}, but if you do, at least I will know his fate.''I had hoped you would not find anything, ${PLAYER}, but at least I know now of Lofrík's death and can mourn at last. I will weld his tooth into the haft of my axe, and in this way he shall help me cleave many a foe. We shall seek vengeance, he and I together.

'Lofrík! Alas that you will never see the halls of Khazad-dûm shining as once they did. I will avenge you upon the heads of the half-orcs who invade this land of our sires, land where their labour once shaped the stone into immeasurable splendour. May your memory burn long beneath the earth.'The dwarf Sandar has asked you to aid the expedition by defeating the half-orcs; they have set up camp inside Filgogân.The cavern Filgogân is south-west of Sandar, across the dry river-bed of the Sirannon, in the south-western corner of the surrounding hills.

Sandar has asked you to defeat the half-orcs that have encamped inside Filgogân, as well as their leader Graug.Defeat half-orcs in Filgogân (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sandar is at the small dwarf encampment north-east of Filgogân, with Bósi.

You have thinned the ranks of the half-orcs in Filgogân and defeated their leader Graug, and should now return now to Sandar.'Bósi has assured me that the Hollin Gate will be excavated soon, but I am still worried that our expedition might be left outside Khazad-dûm for some days. The hills echo with the howling of wolves, but little else can be heard; how they make us shiver in the stillness! Hollin of old was full of life, but now even the birds seem suspicious and hostile.

'These hills are riddled with natural caverns, though none approach the splendour of even the unworked passages of Khazad-dûm. Unpleasant creatures have taken refuge in some of these, and I fear they will cause trouble for my people if we remain here overlong.

'To the south-west, across the dry river-bed of the Sirannon, is the cave called Filgogân. A company of half-orcs has an encampment deep inside that place; if you care to assist the dwarves of this expedition, you will thin their numbers and defeat their leader, Graug.''Filgogân is in the south-western corner of the surrounding hills; you will need to cross the dry river-bed of the Sirannon and continue marching south-west. The half-orc encampment is likely to be extensive, and Graug is sure to be deep inside.''You have my heartfelt thanks, ${PLAYER}! The half-orcs are not welcome in Hollin so close to Khazad-dûm, and now Graug knows it. Little good it does him now, though, eh? Ha!

'You're a good ${RACE}, ${PLAYER}, and I am glad to have you on our side. May you prove as useful beyond the doors as we enter the Mines of Moria!'Extermination of GorogrodExtermination of GorogrodExtermination of GorogrodExtermination of GorogrodHandarod is concerned with the Council of the North's efforts to track the movements of the forces of Angmar within their deepest strongholds.Bail Dilas lies in Angmar, far to the north and east of Esteldín.

Handarod has asked you to scout the encampment of Bail Dílas and see what forces are arrayed near the gates of Carn Dûm.Scout Bail DílasGorthbar lies in Angmar, far to the north and east of Esteldín.

Handarod has asked you to scout the graveyard of Gorthbar and see what dread rites the Enemy is performing in that horrid place.Scout GorthbarMinas Caul is in Angmar, far to the north and east of Esteldín.

Handarod has asked you to scout the tower of Minas Caul and see what forces of the Enemy inhabit the Witch King's domain there.Scout Minas CaulHandarod is at Esteldín in the North Downs, far to the west and south of Angmar.

You should report to Handarod and tell him what you have seen in the heart of Angmar.Talk to Handarod'Greetings, ${PLAYER}. I have been asked by allies within the Council of the North to send capable scouts up into Angmar to help keep an eye on the movements of the Enemy.

'It seems that something is astir within the dark lands of Angmar, and the Council is hard-pressed to stay abreast of all that the Enemy conspires.

'In particular, I need you to scout through the lands of Himbar, Nan Gurth, and Imlad Balchorth. It will not be easy, but there are few others I would trust with this task.''You have completed your patrol in the farthest reaches of Angmar? Excellent! I await your full report... it is vital that we know what goes on before the gates of Carn Dûm.'Throst believes that the sorcerer Ferndúr concerns himself with the fell spirits that inhabit the area not far from the Cauldron of Death.There are fell spirits in a ruined area not far from the Cauldron of Death in the central island of Imlad Balchorth.

Ferndúr must be destroyed to disperse the evil threat in this cursed land.Throst is back at Gath Forthnír, east from Ferndúr's lair.

With Ferndúr slain, you should return to Throst to tell him the tale of your deeds.Talk to Throst'You have stopped the evil engine of the Cauldron of Death, you have stopped the supply of money to the North Downs, and you have ended the terror of Deluches, but still the Dead rise! I fear this is the work of the dread sorcerer Lord Ferndúr. You have gathered allies and helped us to slow the spread of the Dead. However defeating Lord Ferndûr is no small task. If you would challenge this fell master of death, you must assemble an even greater force.

'I believe that Ferndúr is concerned more with the summoning of the fell spirits that are used to animate the shambling bodies of the dead than he is with the wights. You must return to the cursed isle in Imlad Balchorth, find the place where the fell spirits throng and destroy them. Destroy them all, and perhaps Ferndúr will appear, for he must know it when his summoned spirits are banished.

'Be wary, for this threat may be the gravest yet, faced in the darkness of Angmar, a monstrous evil whose only superior in the North is in the dread citadel of Carn Dûm itself.''Find the fell spirits, and you will eventually find their master!''Truly you have accomplished a deed worthy of the great tales of past ages. It may be long before Imlad Balchorth is cleansed of its ancient evil, but in a single stroke you have broken one of the main buttresses that support Angmar's vile strength.

'I salute you.'Practice harrying your foes, encouraging them to expose their vulnerabilities.Strike with Fray the EdgePractice harrying your foes, encouraging them to expose their vulnerabilities.Strike with Hammer Down, Juggernaut, or Cataclysmic ShoutForward momentum makes the most of a thrusting sword.Use the skill Flèche.Foray Against the SorcerersForay Against the SorcerersForay Against the SorcerersForay Against the SorcerersThe Men of the West have long stood against the Shadow, and their courage is needed once again.Elrond is in the library of his house in Rivendell.

Elrond Halfelven has summoned you to speak with him on some urgent matter.Talk to Elrond in RivendellAragorn and Boromir can be found in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.

Elrond has asked that you help your fellow Men, Aragorn son of Arathorn and Boromir of Gondor, prepare to leave on their dangerous -- and secret -- mission.Help Boromir with his preparationsHelp Aragorn with his preparationsElrond is in his library within the Last Homely House, in Rivendell.

You have helped both Aragorn and Boromir with their preparations and should return now to Elrond.Talk to Elrond in Rivendell'A summons arrived here by raven this morning, ${PLAYER}, calling you to the House of Elrond, far to the east. I do not know why Elrond Halfelven requests your presence, but it must be for an errand of some urgency if he sends ravens on the wing here to Echad Garthadir.

'Does the Elf-lord have need now for the strength of the Race of Men? That friendship was never sundered, tested though it be by the passage of Time.

'You will find the House of Elrond far to the east, in the hidden vale of Rivendell, beyond the Trollshaws.''${PLAYER}, may I have a word with you? Master Elrond requests your presence in Rivendell on a matter of some urgency. His messenger did not reveal the reason for this summons, but said only that Elrond has need now of the strength of the Race of Men.

'Travel along the eastern road beyond the Trollshaws to the hidden vale of Rivendell and speak with Elrond in his house. We will do our best to stand against the Shadow in your absence, and wait eagerly for your return.''${PLAYER}, I have a message for you: Master Elrond requests your presence in Rivendell on a matter of some urgency. His messenger did not reveal the reason for this summons, but said only that Elrond has need now of the strength of the Race of Men.

'Descend from the mountains and return to the vale of Rivendell that you might speak with Elrond in his house. We will do our best to stand against the frozen dangers here and wait eagerly for your return.''Master Elrond wishes to speak with you, ${PLAYER}. He says the Elves have need of the strength of the Race of Men and wishes you to speak with him about some matter of urgency.

'Go inside and speak with Master Elrond in his library.''You represent all members of the Race of Men, ${PLAYER}. Speak to Elrond Halfelven in the library of his house in Rivendell with courtesy, but let him know too of our strength.''Travel to Rivendell and speak with Master Elrond within the library of his house. I will wait for you here.''Descend from the mountains and return to Master Elrond's house; he waits for you in the library and should not be kept waiting.''Go inside and speak with Master Elrond, ${PLAYER}. You will find him waiting for you within the library.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. I am pleased to see you well. We must be thankful for every journey that ends in safety, for these are dangerous times and the shadows grow ever long outside Rivendell.

'A small company will soon be setting forth on a mission of great secrecy to combat this growing dark; I am loath to speak in detail of their errand, but know that it will do great hurt to the Enemies of this Age if they succeed.

'The fate of the Race of Men concerns you closely, ${PLAYER}, and I know that you are trustworthy. Two doughty Men prepare to set forth on this perilous journey; lend them your assistance as they prepare to leave. I speak of Aragorn son of Arathorn and Boromir of Gondor. Look for them in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.''Though sundered by geography and the long miles, the fate of the Race of Men lies heavily upon Aragorn, Boromir, and yourself, and binds you all together.

'Speak with them in the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris and aid them in their preparations.''Thank you for helping Aragorn and Boromir with their preparations, ${PLAYER}. The fate of all your people is bound to the success of the mission they must undertake.'Frodo Baggins has been writing up some notes about his adventure for his uncle Bilbo, but has misplaced them.The Imdolen Look-out is a stone pavilion north of Rivendell that overlooks the valley.

Frodo has asked you to find the notes he left behind at the Imdolen Look-out.Look for Frodo's notes at the Imdolen Look-outFrodo is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond.

Arwen has given you the notes she found at Imdolen Look-out. You should return them now to Frodo.'Hello there, ${PLAYER}; it's always a pleasure to see a friendly face! I imagine Elrond was reluctant to let you know about the journey we are leaving on, but I think Gandalf might have had something to do with allowing it. He is very mysterious, as usual, but he generally has his reasons, even if we can't always see them!

'My uncle Bilbo wanted me to write up some notes of my adventures so far for his book, so I took a walk to a sunny spot here in Rivendell and started writing up a few things. Before long, though, I found myself getting drowsy, and when I awoke I forgot all about my papers! I seem to have left them out there.

'If you don't mind, do you think you could bring them back for me? I was resting in the stone pavilion at the Imdolen Look-out, just north of Rivendell. You will find my notes there, I'm sure of it.''Rivendell is such a relaxing place that it is easy to lose track of time. If you can bring me the notes I left behind at the Imdolen Look-out, north of Rivendell, I would really appreciate it.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. You are a long way from home. You have come for these notes? I hope Frodo is not upset at my having read them. He has weathered many adventures already, but I wonder how many roads still await him and the rest of his Company? His uncle will need more pages than this to write of them in their entirety! He has come now only from Hobbiton to Rivendell; how many more pages will it take before he sees his destination?

'We all have our parts to play, ${PLAYER}: Men such as Aragorn; dwarves; even hobbits such as you and Frodo will have an impact on the great events of our Age. Yes, even I have a role to play, though my time has not yet arrived.

'Return these notes to Frodo with my good wishes, and with my hope that he can return again to Imdolen when the errand of the Company is finished, there to sleep once again in the sun.''May all of the Company return safely to Imladris when their errand is over.

'I believe you will find Frodo in Bilbo's room on the first floor of my father's house. Return to him his notes and give him my good wishes.''You found my notes, ${PLAYER}? Thank you very much for bringing them back to me, though now that I look at them they seem very short. How very different it was to live through these few lines!

'Do the Elves feel this way, looking back on their long lives? How strange it must be! Does time pass more slowly for them, or more quickly? What must it be like to remember so much?

'I suppose we will never be able to understand that, ${PLAYER}. We are simple hobbit-folk, and that's what we will stay, I imagine. Thank you again for your help; I know that Bilbo will be glad to have these notes.'Hobbits generally stay in the Shire, never wandering far from their homes, but occasionally there are... exceptions.Elrond waits to speak with you in the library of his house in Rivendell.

Elrond Halfelven has summoned you to speak with him on some urgent matter.Frodo Baggins, Samwise Gamgee, and Peregrin Took can be found in Bilbo's room on the first floor of Elrond's house.

Elrond has asked that you aid Frodo, Sam, and Pippin with their preparations. As you are of their kind, you may be able to lend them courage for the dangerous secret journey that awaits them.Help Frodo with his preparationsHelp Pippin with his preparationsHelp Sam with his preparationsElrond is in the library of his house in Rivendell.

You have aided the hobbits with their preparations and should now return to Elrond.'A summons arrived here by raven this morning, ${PLAYER}, calling you to the House of Elrond, far to the east. I do not know why Elrond Halfelven requests the presence of a halfling, but it must be for some errand of importance if he troubles to send ravens on the wing here to Echad Garthadir.

'It is most peculiar, ${PLAYER}. Have your people had dealings with Elrond Halfelven before this? In any event, you will find the House of Elrond far to the east, in the hidden vale of Rivendell, beyond the Trollshaws.''${PLAYER}, may I have a word with you? Master Elrond requests your presence in Rivendell on a matter of some urgency. His messenger did not reveal the reason for this summons, but said only that Elrond has need now of you. It is most odd for a halfling to receive such a summons!

'Travel along the eastern road beyond the Trollshaws to the hidden vale of Rivendell and speak with Elrond in his house.''${PLAYER}, I have a message for you: Master Elrond requests your presence in Rivendell on a matter of some urgency. His messenger did not reveal the reason for this summons. It is most odd for a halfling to be called in this way, and I cannot fathom the reason for it.

'Descend from the mountains and return to the vale of Rivendell that you might speak with Elrond in his house.''Master Elrond wishes to speak with you, ${PLAYER}. Halflings truly have come to the notice of the Wise and the Great, have they not?

'Go inside and speak with Master Elrond in his library. I wonder on what business you have been summoned?''Now halflings have been called to the counsels of the Elves. What could possibly be next? The salamanders of Lake Evendim?

'Ah, I mean no offence. Seek out Elrond Halfelven at his house in Rivendell, far to the east, for he has summoned you. Perhaps he will summon a Man next, for it is strength of arms he will surely need in these dark times.''You must make the journey to Rivendell, far to the east beyond the Trollshaws, to speak with Master Elrond. What causes him to summon a halfling, I wonder?''Return to the Vale of Imladris and speak with Elrond inside his library. I wonder on what business he wishes to see a halfling, even one so unusual as yourself!''I wonder what business Elrond wishes to speak with you about? You are a worthy halfling, do not mistake my meaning, but I am curious about what matter calls you to his attention.

'You will find him inside his library.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. I am pleased to see you well. This is a safe journey for no one, not even doughty Men or dwarves or Elves, and for a halfling to come so far is commendable.

'A small company will soon be setting forth on a mission of great secrecy to combat the lengthening shadow of Evil that spreads across the land; I am loath to speak in detail of their errand, but know that it will do great hurt to the Enemies of this Age if they succeed.

'Several of your people have chosen to be a part of this company; even now they prepare to depart. I ask that you aid these halflings with their preparations: you are of their kind and may lend them courage for the task ahead. You will find them in a room on the first floor of my house: the lodgings of Bilbo Baggins. Aid them as you can, for much depends upon their courage.''You will find the hobbits in the room I gave to Bilbo on the first floor of this house. Aid them with their preparations, that they may have courage for the dangerous road that awaits them.''Thank you for helping the halflings with their preparations, ${PLAYER}. Gandalf says of your people that they have unseen reserves of strength and are stout of heart, and I hope it is true.

'Much depends on the success of the journey they will undertake.'Samwise Gamgee is trying to pack up everything he will need for the journey that lies ahead, but he is worried he might forget something.Grafinn is at the Forges of Rivendell in the north-west corner of the valley.

Trusting you as a hobbit of good sense, Samwise Gamgee has given you a small coinpurse and a list of items to buy from the provisioner at the Forges of Rivendell.Samwise Gamgee is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond.

Grafinn has given you the items Sam requested, but has refused to accept the coins he sent for their purchase.'...a small bit of flint, spare cloth for stitching patches... what am I forgetting? Bless me, I know it's something I'll be needing!

'Oh, hullo there, ${PLAYER}! You've caught me in a bit of a pickle: too much packing to do and not enough time in which to do it! Bad enough to leave something at home what you'll be needing when you're strolling through the Westfarthing, but worse when you're on a trip like the one Mr. Gandalf has planned for us!

'Do you think you could run to the Forges of Rivendell and pick up a few items from Grafinn for me? I'll write up a list, of course! Now, what am I missing?''I'm just not sure what I've forgotten! What would my old dad say? "You keep thinking, Samwise, or you'll never get it sorted out, and then Mr. Frodo will have to do without whatever it is, and he'll find someone else to do for him!"

'I need to keep thinking, ${PLAYER}! Can you visit Grafinn at the Forges of Rivendell and speak with him about the items on my list? There should be enough in that coinpurse to buy everything.''Yes, I do have all of these items, but I cannot accept these coins from Master Gamgee. I gift these items to him as a member of the Company that bears all our hopes and will brook no argument! Besides, I have little need for the monies of mortal-kind.

'I give him these items freely and would give any member of the Company all that I could, though it be in no way comparable to the burden each of them carries. Return to Master Gamgee and tell him that any items I have are his for the errand of Elrond.''It is my honour to give Master Gamgee those items, and I refuse any manner of payment for them. Return to him in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond and tell him of my gift!''Thank you very much for buying these goods for me, ${PLAYER}; I wouldn't have sent anyone but an honest hobbit to do it, I don't mind telling you. I gave you enough coins, I hope!

'He wouldn't accept my payment? Well, that's queer, and no mistake! "Always pay for your goods, Samwise, and you'll never want for them," is what my old dad always says, and I've always done right by that.

'Elves are strange folk, putting such importance on us small folk, and them so great and noble! Still, thank you for running my errand; these goods will surely help me on the next!'Gimli is anticipating the journey of the Company, but is anxious for it to begin. He has mused on what lies ahead for long enough already.Glóin is at his camp in the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell.

Gimli has asked you to speak with his father about the possibility that Gandalf might bring the company through Moria, to the halls of Balin.Gandalf is in Bilbo's room on the ground floor of the House of Elrond, in Rivendell.

Glóin has sent you to Gandalf to inquire about Moria and the ring of Thrór on behalf of Gimli.Talk to Gandalf in RivendellGimli is in one of the guest rooms down the road from Elrond's House.

Gandalf has reprimanded Gimli for inattentiveness and urged him to think only of the company's errand.Talk to Gimli in Rivendell'By my beard, ${PLAYER}, it seems like you have been entrusted with news of our errand, and why not? Elrond seems to be wise enough, for an Elf; he seems to know good character from bad. But I am ready to put an end to this waiting. Give me goblin-skulls to cleave!

'If you are as sick of waiting as I am, why not bring this message to my father at his camp in the mountains? Ask him what he thinks of the likelihood that Gandalf might let us come to the halls of Balin, in Khazad-dûm. It could be that Balin found Thrór's ring somewhere in Moria! Now would that not be something?

'My father Glóin's camp is north of Rivendell, in the Misty Mountains.''My father is at his camp in the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell. Ask him if he thinks Gandalf might let us visit Balin in Khazad-dûm. Imagine what a boon it would be if he found Thrór's ring there!''My son is not as attentive as he believes himself to be, ${PLAYER}, if he thinks Balin may have found the ring of Thrór within Khazad-dûm. Gandalf told us the fate of Thrór's ring while at the Council!

'We are dwarves, ${PLAYER}, so you understand the call of stone and gems, and in all of us there lies a slumbering desire to see the halls of Khazad-dûm alive and shining once again. It could be that Balin son of Fundin works there now, restoring the splendour of its ancient cities, but I do not know if that is a vain hope. There has been no word from Moria in many long years.

'I cannot tell my son the mind of Gandalf. You should speak with the Wizard yourself if you wish to know his plans. I believe he is in Bilbo's room on the ground floor of the House of Elrond. Speak with him to hear of Moria, for I will not speak of it more.''I do not pretend to know the minds of Wizards, ${PLAYER}. Speak with Gandalf if you wish to know more about his plans for the company. You will find him in Bilbo's room on the ground floor of the House of Elrond, I expect.''Many troubles could be avoided and much time saved if certain parties paid more attention to the things that were said to them: if Gimli son of Glóin was not interested in listening to the tales told at the council, he should have given his seat to someone else!

'His father is correct: Balin could not have found in Moria the ring of Thrór, last of the dwarf-rings forged of old, for though it was given from Thrór to his son Thráin, it was taken from Thráin in captivity.

'As for Balin himself, the lack of news from Moria is troubling, but I will not muse needlessly on his fate. Return to Gimli and tell him that the errand of the Company must be first in his thoughts. There is no room for other concern.''Gimli is in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris, with the other members of the Company.''Gandalf has that to say, ${PLAYER}? I am not to be blamed if the mind wanders during such a long discussion -- you and I are dwarves of action, given to fighting and carousing, not idle chatter!

'Thank you for your aid, my friend. It is to be hoped that Balin's work in Moria prospers, and that Khazad-dûm will shine again! Perhaps if the errand of the Company succeeds, you and I will go there someday and lend our strong arms to the effort! I am sure Balin will appreciate our help!'You have done your part to help the Company prepare to depart Rivendell on their errand, and now the time has come for them to set out.Elrond is in the library of his house.

You have earned the right to see the Company off as they depart on their secret errand.Talk to Elrond and see the Company off on their secret errandElrond is in the library of his house.

You have seen the Company off on their secret errand and should now speak with Elrond.'The time has come for the Company to depart on their errand of secrecy, ${PLAYER}. As you have been of some assistance with the preparations, you may join the members of my household as we see them on their way.

'Tell me when you are ready to wish them well.''It is time, ${PLAYER}. Let us go with my household to wish the Company well.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest, and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'It is done, ${PLAYER}. All our hopes now rest upon the nine members of the company: Nine Walkers to stand against the Nine Riders of the Enemy. May they come to no harm and return in safety, their task completed.

'There is nothing we can do now for them. We must stand tall against the Shadow and keep its attention focused upon us, far away from those who can do it the greatest harm.

'That is our task now, ${PLAYER}. Do you accept it?'Boromir of Gondor is restless waiting in Rivendell and is eager to depart on the secret errand of his company, but he is concerned that the Elves of Rivendell do not appreciate the sacrifices of his people.The guards at the southern entrance to the Vale of Imladris are atop the road that winds up the southern slope from the guest rooms.

Boromir has asked you to see if the guards at the southern entrance to the Vale of Imladris require the strength of his arm in the valley's defence.Talk to a sentinel at the southern entrance of the ValeBoromir is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

The guards at the southern entrance to the valley have politely rebuffed Boromir's offer of aid.Talk to Boromir in RivendellThe Hall of Fire is on the first floor of the House of Elrond, in Rivendell.

Boromir chafes at the lack of respect for your people's sacrifices that he sees in Rivendell and wants you to tell some tales of the great deeds of the Race of Men to Elf-singers in the Hall of Fire.Boromir is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

You have spoken of the deeds of Men in the Hall of Fire and should now return to Boromir.Talk to Boromir in Rivendell'It does my heart good to see a ${RACE} such as you, ${PLAYER}! Aragorn there seems a strong Man, but he seems to prize the friendship of Elves over that of his own kind. So many Elves in this place! Every hour we spend in this hidden valley prolongs the struggle of Gondor; how many men will fall in its defence ere we depart?

'My sword-arm itches for battle, and my horn longs to be sounded! You understand the need we have for haste, ${PLAYER} -- you are not like these others, who live for such a long span that the concerns of the present must seem trivial, or meaningless. But it is not so!

'Climb the winding road to the south entrance to the valley and ask the guards there if they have need of my vigilance. I will spend the time until we depart guarding the borders, if they have need of my strength.''Every hour we spend here is an hour of battle for Gondor and the Men of the South, and still we wait! I must work off this restlessness. Speak with the guards at the southern entrance to the Vale of Imladris and see if they have need for the strong arm of Boromir of Gondor.

'This place has its defences, but too few strong men and women, if you ask me on it.''We have things here at the entrance to the valley well in hand, ${PLAYER}. I appreciate the intention of our visitor from the South, but we have no need of his assistance: Imladris has been safe from attack for long years before either he -- or you -- walked in Eriador.

'Thank him for the offer, but tell him his concern is misplaced. The Elves can protect Imladris.''Thank our visitor from the South for his concern, but tell him the Elves can protect their own borders.''If the borders of Imladris rested on the shoulders of our visitor from the South, I do not doubt that he could be of use defending them. But the Race of Men is shortlived, and he could not defend them long.

'I mean you no disrespect, ${PLAYER}, but does the Man of Gondor really believe that the Elves cannot safe-guard their own haven?''What did you say, ${PLAYER}? The Elves have turned down my offer of aid? This is what I expected, though it grieves me to see it so clearly: the Elves believe they have no need for us, ${PLAYER}! They may change their opinion if Gondor falls, and change it quickly -- do they not see that our people are their last real defence?

'Have they forgotten the great deeds of our people that have made this land safe? We must remind them, my friend. Go to the Hall of Fire, in the House of Elrond, and remind some of the Elf-singers there of the great deeds of our people: of the Gondorian cavalry, perhaps, and of Elendil and his sons. They will not then undervalue the contributions of our people!''The Hall of Fire is on the ground floor of the House of Elrond, ${PLAYER}. Remind the Elf-singers there of the great deeds done by Men and Women throughout the years, and those that are still being done today!'At Boromir's urging, you recite the tale of the ride of the Gondorian cavalry that pursued the Witch-king from Fornost, where the forces of Angmar were routed.

'A good tale, and the deeds of Prince Eärnur are worthy of remembrance, as are those of many strong Men besides. But your tale has ended before Eärnur's horse gave way in terror of the Witch-king. Were it not for the arrival of the Elf-lord Glorfindel, things may have gone ill for the Captain of Gondor that day.

'But your point is clear: against the great evils of the Shadow, Elves and Men must stand together. It is a lesson gladly remembered.'At Boromir's urging, you recite the tale of Elendil and his sons, and the strength they brought to bear in the Battle of Dagorlad at the Black Gate of Mordor.

'I know this tale, but I know it by another name, for in Imladris it is called "The Song of the Last Alliance" and involves more characters: not just Elendil the Tall, but Gil-galad our king, and Master Elrond, and Círdan the Shipwright.

'But you have reminded me of this, and I urge you not to forget it yourself, ${PLAYER}: neither Elves alone, nor Men alone, could have withstood the power of the Enemy at the siege of Mordor.''You have eased my mind, ${PLAYER}; though it seem we struggle alone, it is not so. That is why we fight on; not just for our own people, but for all peoples.'Meriadoc Brandybuck and Peregrin Took enjoyed strolling about Rivendell, and Pippin made use of a walking-stick he found in the library of the House of Elrond.Elrond is in the library of his house.

Pippin has asked that you thank Elrond for the use of the walking-stick he borrowed and to tell him that the hobbit will return it shortly.Talk to Elrond in the Last Homely HousePippin is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond.

Pippin borrowed the Sceptre of Annúminas, thinking it a complimentary walking-stick, and now Elrond wants it returned.Talk to Pippin in RivendellPippin thinks he left the Sceptre of Annúminas atop some large grey boulders near the forges, west of the road that climbs up into the mountains.

Pippin has asked you, as a fellow hobbit, to find the Sceptre of Annúminas, the 'walking-stick' he left somewhere in Rivendell.Find the Sceptre of AnnúminasPippin is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond. Elrond is in his library.

You have recovered the Sceptre of Annúminas and should return it now to either Pippin or Elrond.'Hello there, ${PLAYER}! I'm afraid the Company has enough hobbits for now; we outnumber each of the other races, you know, although Merry says that Gandalf probably counts for more, whatever he is.

'Merry and I just got back from a nice long stroll around Rivendell, seeing the sights and keeping out of trouble. I borrowed a splendid walking-stick for our hike from Elrond's library. It was simply excellent -- I wonder if he would let me bring it with me on the journey? Probably not; he didn't want Merry or me coming along, after all, but Gandalf put in a word for us. A Wizard would know we wouldn't stand to be left behind!

'If you don't mind, maybe you could visit Elrond in his library and thank him for the use of that walking-stick? Tell him I will come along and drop it off before we leave.''Elrond knows that we would never let Frodo and Sam embark on this journey by themselves. I know they would have the help of Elves and dwarves and Men, but it's just not the same as having your friends with you.''Elrond is probably in the library, so you can just stop in and thank him for letting me use that splendid walking-stick. I'll return it before the Company sets out, of course.''You say Peregrin Took borrowed a walking-stick from this library? There is no such item kept here.

'Surely the young hobbit did not mistake the Sceptre of Annúminas for a common walking-stick? I trust Gandalf without question, but still I have reservations about Meriadoc and Peregrin joining the company. They are young and do not understand the dangers ahead.

'They will remain with the company, but I am displeased with the youngest hobbit. Peregrin should return the Sceptre; that need not be stated.''Return to young Peregrin Took and ask him to bring back the Sceptre of Annúminas. It is a relic of much importance and should not have been removed from the library.''Did you thank Elrond for the use of that complimentary walking-stick, ${PLAYER}? Rivendell really is a hospitable place, isn't it?

'The... the sceptre of Annúminas? They shouldn't keep it in a place where people will assume that it's free for anyone to use, ${PLAYER}! Well, I will just bring it back to Elrond and say there was a little misunderstanding, and... oh no! It's gone! I must have left it out there and forgotten to bring it back with me! Now that I think about it, I don't remember using it on our way back, Merry!

'You're a hobbit I can trust, ${PLAYER}! You've got to find it for me! There were some large grey stones in the northern part of the valley that we climbed on top of, west of the road that winds up into the mountains, right next to those great forges. I must have left it on top of those large boulders!''I don't remember exactly where Pippin might have left the Sceptre, but we were climbing around on some giant grey boulders in the northern part of the valley, west of the road that leads up into the mountains. I'd wager the Sceptre is on top of those boulders!''Merry and I were walking in the northern part of the valley, ${PLAYER}, and we climbed up on some really large boulders. I bet the sceptre is on top of those boulders! They were west of the road that leads up into the mountains.''Thank you for returning the Sceptre of Annúminas on behalf of your kinsman; I wonder if we should keep such relics under the guard of watchful eyes while your people are here in Imladris.

'No harm has been done, ${PLAYER}. I am not angry. You have done well.

'I hope, however, that Gandalf understands the mischief Meriadoc and Peregrin might cause as members of the Company. The errand will be perilous enough without their tendency to fall into trouble.''Just in time, ${PLAYER}! I will return this Sceptre to Elrond just as soon as I get a chance. I'm a little busy right now, but I will get to it sometime.

'I hope Elrond isn't too upset, and that he still lets me go with Frodo and Sam and the rest of the Company! Being left behind would be a terrible punishment! I wonder why no one sees it that way? Every time I say that to Strider he just smiles and agrees, but I can't tell if he is just humouring me.

'Thanks again, ${PLAYER}! You can always count on a fellow hobbit when you're in need!'Aragorn has spent the time before the company's departure planning the route to take with Gandalf.Gandalf is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond.

Aragorn has asked you to bring word of the two ways south he sees for the Company: by the Redhorn Gate over the mountains, or through the Gap of Rohan.Talk to Gandalf in RivendellAragorn is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Gandalf has asked that you return to Aragorn with his misgivings about the two ways the Ranger has considered for the Company's route and to speak to him of a third way the party might take.Talk to Aragorn in Rivendell'Elrond has entrusted you with knowledge of our errand, ${PLAYER}? I am glad. I go with Boromir to his city of Minas Tirith, a journey that lies along that of the rest of the Company for many leagues. And yet, the route to take is still undecided.

'I see two ways for the Company to take: we might make for the Redhorn Gate, the pass over the mountains that descends into the Dimrill Dale, where lie the still waters of the Mirrormere; or, we might head for the Gap of Rohan, by which Boromir came north. There are difficulties associated with each way, but one of these two must prove the better route.

'I will continue poring over the maps, ${PLAYER}; will you speak with Gandalf about these two routes? I believe he is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond.''There are two ways over the mountains our Company might take, ${PLAYER}: to the Dimrill Dale by way of the Redhorn Gate, or through the Gap of Rohan. I will keep looking at these maps, but in the meantime perhaps you can ask Gandalf which of these ways he thinks is best.

'I believe the wizard is in Bilbo's room on the first floor of the House of Elrond.''Two ways, you say? What are the two ways over the mountains that Aragorn proposes?

'The passage of the mountains could be difficult; the Redhorn Gate is very high up, and in winter, the heights may be treacherous. I do not believe the Gap of Rohan will be open to us; that way would bring the Company too close to Isengard, and Aragorn will understand why we must not go that way.

'But there is a third way through the mountains. I will not speak of it here, but Aragorn will know the way of which I speak. Return to him with my misgivings.''You will find Aragorn in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris. Give him my misgivings about the two ways he has suggested and tell him that there is indeed a third way we might take.''I do know of this third way, and Gandalf knows too that I am loath to bring the Company through the mountains by that road. It is a dark way and dangerous. I will say no more of it now, but I hope it will not come to that. My heart tells me that the Redhorn Gate is safest for our purposes.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You have done me a service.'The shadow of growing evil threatens even the safety of Rivendell, and all of the Free Peoples must come together to defend against it.Elrond waits in the library of his house in Rivendell.

Elrond Halfelven has summoned you to speak with him on some urgent matter.Talk to ElrondLegolas and Gimli are in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.

Elrond has asked you to help Legolas the Elf and Gimli the Dwarf with their preparations for the journey.Help Gimli with his preparationsHelp Legolas with his preparationsElrond is in the library of his house in Rivendell.

You have aided Legolas and Gimli with their preparations and should speak now with Elrond.'A summons arrived here by raven this morning, ${PLAYER}, calling you to the House of Elrond, far to the east. I do not know why Elrond Halfelven requests your presence, but it must be for an errand of some urgency if he sends ravens on the wing here to Echad Garthadir.

'Does the Elf-lord have need now for the strength of the dwarves? That friendship has waned over the years, has it not?

'You will find the House of Elrond far to the east, in the hidden vale of Rivendell, beyond the Trollshaws.''${PLAYER}, may I have a word with you? Master Elrond requests your presence in Rivendell on a matter of some urgency. His messenger did not reveal the reason for this summons. Perhaps Elrond has need now of the strength of the dwarves? That friendship may have waned, but let it not break forever! There are too many evils abroad for dwarves and Elves to meet in anger.

'Travel along the eastern road beyond the Trollshaws to the hidden vale of Rivendell and speak with Elrond in his house. It must be important.''${PLAYER}, I have a message for you: Master Elrond requests your presence in Rivendell on a matter of some urgency. His messenger did not reveal the reason for this summons. It must be important if Master Elrond requires the presence of a dwarf in his house. Take no offence! I merely mark it as unusual.

'Descend from the mountains and return to the vale of Rivendell that you might speak with Elrond in his house.''Master Elrond wishes to speak with you, ${PLAYER}, about a matter of some urgency. What matter concerns both Elves and dwarves, I wonder?

'Go inside and speak with Master Elrond in his library.''Travel to Rivendell, far to the east beyond the Trollshaws, and speak with Elrond Halfelven.''Travel to the House of Elrond, in Rivendell, to see what aid you can lend him.''Return to the Vale of Imladris and speak with Master Elrond in the library of his house.''Go inside the house and speak with Elrond in his library.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}, I am pleased to see you well. Be not uncomfortable in my house! The friendship of Elves and dwarves may have waned over the long years, but it must not be sundered.

'A small company will soon be setting forth on a mission of great secrecy to battle this growing darkness; I am loath to speak in detail of their errand, but know that it will do great hurt to the Enemies of this Age if they succeed. Among this company is one of your folk, a dwarf named Gimli, and an Elf of Mirkwood named Legolas; he is a prince of that folk. They must work together if their errand is to succeed, but for some time Elves and dwarves have been... reluctant to do so. I would have you aid them with their preparations. By your example, I hope they will set aside their differences.

'Look for them in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris.''You will find Legolas and Gimli in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale of Imladris. Help them with their preparations, that they may learn to cooperate with each other by your example.''Thank you for helping Legolas and Gimli with their preparations, ${PLAYER}. They must learn to work together if the errand of their company is to end in success.'The Elf-prince Legolas is worried about the harsh words he shared with some of the Elves of Rivendell following the Council of Elrond and is hopeful that he has not upset any of his fellows.Erestor is at the Haven of Orladion, north-west of the House of Elrond.

Legolas is concerned that strong words following the Council of Elrond may have caused Erestor to resent him, and he wants you to apologize on his behalf.Galdor of the Havens is at the stables in the north-west corner of Rivendell.

You have brought word of Legolas's apology to Erestor and should now do the same for Galdor.Legolas is in one of the guest rooms of Elrond's House.

Galdor has given you a white knife to give to Legolas as a token of his forgiveness.'You have the ear of Elrond, ${PLAYER}, and his trust, and that means you will have mine as well; the animosity between our peoples has been... exaggerated... over the course of the long years. In these dark times we should not focus on the distant past, but should instead strengthen the bonds of friendship, for we will have need of them against the Enemy.

'In a similar fashion I fear my bold words following the Council at which the great threat was exposed may have upset some of my fellows: the Elves Galdor and Erestor. I would not have them nurse resentment of my words overlong! They argued in favour of the westward passage; though I did not blame them for it, I spoke with some heat against that course.

'If you bring them word of my regret, perhaps they will forgive my brashness -- the errand of the company is urgent, but I was wrong to speak so harshly of its opposition. Speak with Erestor at the Haven of Orladion, north-west of the House of Elrond. If you, a dwarf, bring word of my regret, Erestor's eyes may be newly opened to the need for cooperation among all peoples. Ask Erestor if he knows where Galdor may be found, for he too deserves my apology.''I am worried that Erestor may have taken my strident words to heart, and I would like to apologize for them. Seek him out at the Haven of Orladion, north-west of the House of Elrond, and bring him my words. Ask him, too, where Galdor of the Havens might be found, for I owe him my apologies as well.''${PLAYER}, Legolas has nothing for which to apologize. His words were no more strident than my own, or Galdor's. These are difficult times and friends should not be afraid to voice their concerns with friends.

'I asked of him this: "If the instrument of our grief is unmade, as seems to be the course we must take; what will become of the majesty of the Elves? What victory is there in that?" Legolas did not waver in his reply, and I forgive him for the heat of it. Even Elrond, wisest of lore-masters, does not know what will happen if the errand of the Company is successful. But it must be undertaken; that much is clear to me now, though the sight of it came sooner to Legolas.

'Galdor of the Havens? He is at the stables in the north-west corner of the valley.''You will find Galdor at the stables in the north-west corner of the valley if Legolas has sent you to bring him similar words as those he sent me.''Thank you for bringing my words to Erestor, ${PLAYER}! Did he tell you where Galdor might be found? The stables in the north-west corner of the valley? Very good, and I hope that Galdor is receptive of my apology.''${PLAYER}, you have come on behalf of Legolas Greenleaf? Rare it is for an Elf to send a dwarf to speak his words! I jest with you, courageous ${CLASS}.

'I have already forgiven Legolas for the fire of his argument, for it is clear to me after some reflection that all other courses we might take were not real solutions, for the instrument of dread has a way of making itself known again, though you send it over the Sea or to any place thought secure.

'No, I have forgiven Legolas many times over for his frustration. Indeed, I would give him this white knife as a sign of my forgiveness. Bring it to him with my good wishes, ${PLAYER}.''Bring that knife to Legolas with my good wishes, ${PLAYER}. He is preparing to leave with the rest of his Company in one of the guest rooms along the road that winds up the southern slope of the Vale.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}! You bring me great happiness with the news that Galdor and Erestor have not borne me any resentment, and I will bear this knife in honour of their friendship.'You earned access to the Season of Destruction Portrait Frame.You earned access to the Season of Renewed Aggression Portrait Frame.You earned access to the Season of Rivals Portrait Frame.Master the Fumaroles of the Coliseum in the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.Master the Fumaroles of the Coliseum in the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.Master the Fumaroles of the ColiseumThorod is worried this is a much bigger issue and wants to enlist the help of the Elves in Gath Forthnír to solve this mystery.Fimreg is at Gath Forthnir, north and east of Myrkworth.

Thorod has asked you to speak with Fimreg in Gath Forthnír. He is worried about his findings but will not say why.Urugarth is to the west of Gath Forthnír and north of Imlad Balchorth.

Fimreg believes that a large creature of some kind is poisoning the waters. If such a beast exists, proof of it will be found upstream in Urugarth.Search Urugarth for the creature poisoning the waterFimreg is at Gath Forthnír, in eastern Himbar, east of Urugarth.

You found a curious bony hook, like a claw or beak but not resembling part of any animal you have ever encountered before.'There are many creatures in Angmar that carry venom or poisonous blood in their veins. But even so, how could all the water be affected so? Could something have spawned in the bowels of Carn Dûm? Something monstrous and poisonous to all life?

'This is more than I know of the ways of the water and such. Please, go and speak with Fimreg in Gath Forthnír. She may know more about the evils that could do such things. It is far beyond my meagre skills.

'You will find Fimreg to the north and east of here among the Rangers.''Thorod is wise to send you to the Elves. We can help you, but the rest of your journey will be much more difficult.

'You must travel upstream to discover the source of the corruption. To Urugarth you must go and find evidence of such a creature. It would have to be a large beast, for such are the amounts of poison in the water.

'The waters flow from Carn Dûm's sewers down a waterfall into Urugarth. Perhaps you will find something of note where the waters run fastest?''Go into Urugarth and search for some evidence of the creature fouling these waters. Search near where the waters run from Carn Dûm into Urugarth.''Speak with Fimreg at Gath Forthnír...she may know more of what evils could trouble the waters here so.''Fimreg wishes you to go to Urugarth to find some sign of what is fouling the waters? Well, you have gone the wrong way then....''I know not what beast that hook came from! YOu should bring it to Fimreg at Gath Forthnír.''It is worse than I feared. This is no ordinary beast we face.'There are rumours that ancient scrolls remain in Sarnúr from the days before the great cities of the Dourhands were destroyed by the great cataclysm which tore Ered Luin.Pieces of faded parchment can be found in Sarnúr, south of Thorin's Hall, reached through the ruins of Orodost. 

Rútur has asked you to recover any pieces of faded parchment you might find in Sarnúr.Collect faded parchment (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rútur is in Thorin's Hall, north of Sarnúr, just outside the Blue Stone Garrison.

Now that you possess the pieces of faded parchment that Rútur requested you gather, you should return them to him so that he may decipher them.'Greetings, ${PLAYER}! I hope that you are well.

'Within the depths of Sarnúr, there are tales of lost lore remaining still within the halls. There are those that believe that the Dourhands are searching the area for pieces of faded parchment that are scattered about. Please travel to Sarnúr yourself, recover these pieces of faded parchment, and return to me with them. 

'Sarnúr can be found south of Thorin's Hall, deep beyond the ruins of Orodost, which is located on the western mountains of Ered Luin.''I am sure you will find many scraps of ancient scrolls in the ruins of Sarnúr.''Thank you for journeying into that dark place and returning the scrolls.

'I will begin to decipher the runes that are found on this collection of parchment. 

'If you journey to Sarnúr again, I may ask that you seek out more of these parchments.'Gauntlet in Dol GuldurGauntlet in Dol GuldurGauntlet in Dol GuldurGauntlet in Dol GuldurYou aim to inflict a grave wound that your enemy will never fully recover from.Use the Grave Wound skillYour brother Sterkist has come to Rivendell, and has brought a challenge for you from Grimbeorn.Your brother Sterkist is at the Spire of Meeting, in Rivendell.Complete 'Articles of Resilience'Complete 'Implements of Ferocity'Your brother Sterkist is at the Spire of Meeting, in Rivendell.Talk to Sterkist in RivendellGrimbeorn has come to the High Pass, in the Misty Mountains. He is at the camp called Vindurhal, in the narrow space between Eastern Bruinen Source and North High Pass.

Sterkist has told you that Grimbeorn planned to travel into the High Pass if you completed the tasks set before you.Talk to Grimbeorn at Vindurhal, in the Misty MountainsYour brother Sterkist is by the Spire of Meeting, in Rivendell.Talk to Sterkist in Rivendell'Can you believe it, my sister/brother? At last Grimbeorn has relented and given me leave to cross the mountains to find adventure! He has set a task for you, and put it to me to ensure you complete it. Consider this Grimbeorn's challenge to you: to face the mightiest foes with strength and endurance, and to best them!

'When I arrived at this curious home of Elves, I did what I do best. I made a number of friends, and it seems one of them is a master craftsman. He agreed to make a weapon and other fine equipment for you, if you are able to collect the many components that will be required.

'Grimbeorn demands that you become a shining example of our kind if you are to represent our family in Eriador, so I think you should undertake this task! Indeed, I look forward to guiding you toward its completion!''Grimbeorn has challenged you to wield the finest equipment you can.''Well done, well done, my sister/brother! I must confess to you that I did not think you would be able to complete the tasks Grimbeorn set before you.

'Adventuring is difficult, tiring work! When we used to dream of going on such quests, it seems we left out the sleepless nights and the aching muscles, for this journey is the first I have thought of them. And yet you have travelled far and wide and faced terrible foes without complaint! Perhaps Grimbeorn was right to send you after the Ranger, . I am weary after a much shorter journey!

'Grimbeorn has one more message for you. He wanted me to send word if you were successful at the tasks he set before you, and I have done so. He told me he would journey into the High Pass to meet with you. You should go to him at the camp named Vindurhal, in the narrow space between the Eastern Bruinen Source and the North High Pass.''Grimbeorn wanted me to send word if you were successful at the tasks he set before you, and I have done so. He told me he would journey into the High Pass to meet with you. You should go to him at the camp named Vindurhal, in the narrow space between the Eastern Bruinen Source and the North High Pass.''It is good to see you again, young one, and strong. I would not have made this journey if I did not think it important to give you my blessing and a word or two of further guidance.

'When I sent you forth to find the Ranger Aragorn at the urging of the Wizard Radagast, I did not believe in the importance of the errand. I thought you would complete the task and return, and we could return to living in our customary way. We would collect the tolls on the river-crossing, trade for what goods we need, and otherwise remain secluded from the outside world.

'But I have come to realize this cannot be. The forces of evil that gather on our doorstep will not be content to stay there. They may respect the might of Beorn's descendants, and would be wise to leave us be for a time, but one day they would seek to stamp us out. Our ferocity and our resilience would make them pay dearly, and the cost would make me proud, but then the line of my father would be ended.

'I do not want to wait until that day, far away as it may be. I do not want to become as a cornered bear: dangerous, but frightened. I want to strike out at the enemies that surround him, and in that way keep from being cornered.

'Make yourself strong, ${PLAYER}. Strike fear into the enemies of our people, and aid good folk that lack the power of the skin-changers. You will become my weapon against the forces that assemble against us. That is all I have to say. Go with my blessing and that of your brothers and sisters. Let the memory of your mighty ancestor, my father Beorn, drive you to feats of greatness!''When I sent you forth to find the Ranger Aragorn at the urging of the Wizard Radagast, I did not believe in the importance of the errand. I thought you would complete the task and return, and we could return to living in our customary way. We would collect the tolls on the river-crossing, trade for what goods we need, and otherwise remain secluded from the outside world.

'But I have come to realize this cannot be. The forces of evil that gather on our doorstep will not be content to stay there. They may respect the might of Beorn's descendants, and would be wise to leave us be for a time, but one day they would seek to stamp us out. Our ferocity and our resilience would make them pay dearly, and the cost would make me proud, but then the line of my father would be ended.

'I do not want to wait until that day, far away as it may be. I do not want to become as a cornered bear: dangerous, but frightened. I want to strike out at the enemies that surround him,, and in that way keep from being cornered.

'Make yourself strong, ${PLAYER}. Strike fear into the enemies of our people, and aid good folk that lack the power of the skin-changers. You will become my weapon against the forces that assemble against us. That is all I have to say. Go with my blessing and that of your brothers and sisters. Let the memory of your mighty ancestor, my father Beorn, drive you to feats of greatness!''I am in amazement that Grimbeorn made the journey into the High Pass. Did his message to you justify the journey, ?'

You briefly recount Grimbeorn's words to Sterkist.

'Well, I am glad that he tasked you with this duty, my sister/brother. Real adventuring is difficult, tiring work, and this journey through the mountains to find you has already given me my fill!

'You can be the adventurer in our family, and with my blessing!'You are a guardian almost without equal, and the Free Peoples of Middle-earth are shielded by your arm and your will.The dwarf Wáli is at the outpost of Noglond, south of Thorin's Hall and west of the dwarf-city of Gondamon in Ered Luin.

You have been told to speak to Wáli, a courageous guardian who may have some words for you.Meet Wáli for further Guardian trainingThe dwarf Wáli is at the outpost of Noglond, south of Thorin's Hall and west of the dwarf-city of Gondamon in Ered Luin.

You have been told to speak to Wáli, a courageous guardian who may have some words for you.As a Guardian, you have achieved such renown as to receive a summons from Wáli of Gondamon in Ered Luin.

You should seek Wáli to receive further training in your class.'You are known to me, ${PLAYER}, as a guardian of great skill and mighty deeds, and I can see that the tales I have heard do not miss the mark! Hail, and well-met at Noglond!'Ruidhriel has asked that you seek out the source of the shadow-wargs.The leader of the shadow-wargs can be found upon the Burnt Tor, west of Ruidhriel.

Ruidhriel has tasked you with slaying the leader of the shadow-wargs.Defeat Dagronk, Warg-masterRuidhriel is east of the Burnt Tor.

You should return to Ruidhriel and tell her of your success.Talk to Ruidhriel'Though the shadow-wargs have been thinned they still plague the Burnt Tor, fighting to their last breath. There is some evil malice guiding these creatures that we have not yet faced.

'You must return to the Burnt Tor and seek out this menace. The shadow-wargs will never relent until all of Eregion lies in ruin, or they have found that which we seek to keep hidden.

'The leader of the shadow-wargs most likely stalks among them seeking for clues of the Company. I am certain you will find him upon the Burnt Tor.''Have you sought out the leader of the shadow-wargs, ${PLAYER}? He should stalk among his foul servants somewhere upon the Burnt Tor.''You have returned, ${PLAYER}! Then the deed is done?

'An Orc? This is strange news, for if your description is true this is no Orc of Saruman, this is one of the foul Orcs of Mordor!  

'Sauron's arm has grown long indeed... a report must be brought back to Rivendell.'Follow in the steps of Bard himself and the servants of evil may grow to fear your name as well.Complete Shot Through the HeartStrike with Heart SeekerIn searching for the source of the poisoned waters, you found a strange bony hook and showed it to Fimreg.Tármunn Súrsa is north-west from Gath Forthnír, past the Gates of Carn Dûm.

Fimreg has asked you to speak with Rhúnelleth in Tármunn Súrsa.Helchgam lurks in the waters of Carn Dûm, beyond the gates to the west of Himbar.

Rhúnelleth revealed the source of the poison to be a many-armed creature she called Helchgam.Defeat HelchgamRhúnelleth is in Tármunn Súrsa to the east of Carn Dûm.

You travelled far into Carn Dûm and slew the dread beast known as Helchgam.'A tooth, perhaps? Or the hooked beak of some bird? Oh, if only it were so, ${PLAYER}. Imagine a half-dozen arms, lined with hundreds of these like teeth with which to grasp its prey, and a hungry maw that spits poison and breathes decay. I cannot speak of this monstrosity any more.

'Go to Rhúnelleth in Tármunn Súrsa. She will be able to tell you more of the wicked ways of Carn Dûm.

'Tármunn Súrsa is to the north-west of Gath Forthnír. Hurry, please!''I have been waiting for you.

'There are tales of this creature, a loathesome eater of carrion. Helchgam, the Watcher. Such a beast will be hard to kill, but kill it you must, for left unchecked, it will continue to feed and grow.

'If you only found traces of its spoor in Urugarth, it must lurk in the waters north of there, somewhere within the sewers beneath Carn Dûm. This creature must be defeated. I would ask that you do this, then return to me.''The beast's corruption will take many years to abate, but with its death, we can be sure that the poisoned waters may run clean once more.''The only way to stop the poisoning of the water is to journey into Carn Dûm and defeat Helchgam itself! It is a dangerous task, but one that must be done, I fear.''You should bring the hook to Rhúnelleth at Tármunn Súrsa.''The creature Helchgam is the source of the foul water? Then you must defeat the beast!''You should let Rhúnelleth know of your victory against Helchgam.'You helped all the members of the Lost Fellowship in all of their various needs.Reginald Ward can be found in Sûri-kylä in Forochel.

You were instructed to seek out the captain of the Lost Fellowship, Reginald Ward.Talk to Reginald Ward'Can this be? You have helped me and all of my companions? I would not have believed it could happen. We were in such a state that I thought we would never get back to looking for the lost treasure. You truly are a hero to me and mine, friend.

'Please go and tell our Captain, Reginald Ward in Sûri-kylä of your great feat!''It would appear you have done a great service for myself and all my wayward companions, ${PLAYER}. All that remains for us is to regroup and begin our search for the lost treasure once more!

'Quickly now, tell our Captain, Reginald Ward, of your great feat! He can be found in Sûri-kylä here in Forochel.''What is this? You have aided my lost companions and myself? Amazing! I had nearly given up hope of all of us having our ills addressed, but thanks to your efforts, all has been set to right!

'Please now, go quickly and tell my party's Captain, Reginald Ward, of what you have done for us! He can be found in Sûri-kylä here in Forochel.''From what you tell me, it seems like you have aided all of us, myself and my wayward companions! I am greatly in your debt. I must think up a suitable reward for you, give me just a moment, please....''Now that I hear your tale, I am stunned. You have encountered all of my lost companions and helped them as best you could? What joyous news! Perhaps we will find that lost treasure yet!

'Quick now, go and tell my Captain, Reginald Ward, about your great deeds! The last that I heard was that he could be found somewhere near Sûri-kylä.''I shall sing a great song of your selflessness, ${PLAYER}. You have done all that my wayward companions and I have asked of you and have sought very little reward. Now that all our woes have been addressed, I am sure our party shall regroup and go in search of the lost treasure once more!

'Quickly now, bear this wonderful news to my Captain, Reginald Ward, in Sûri-kylä.''What a mess we were in, but from the sound of it, you have untangled all my companions' problems, ${PLAYER}. And now you have helped me as well! Soon our party will regroup, and we will find that lost treasure...I know it! I can almost feel the gold coins running through my hands now!

'Please, go and tell my party's Captain, Reginald Ward, of your success. He should be near Sûri-kylä. You never know, he may have a reward for you.''I am nearly speechless with joy, ${PLAYER}. The good you have done for my companions and me will not soon be forgotten. In fact, I still cannot get over the fact that you helped Cenlieg the Lore-master. I can hardly stand the man! Oh well, the treasure we seek will make it all worthwhile, in the end... at least, that is what I hope.

'Now, friend, do not let me trouble you further. You have done us all a great service, and perhaps if we do discover the treasure, I shall share a small portion of it with you. For now, all I can offer is my gratitude and a small token of my esteem.''Please, friend, go and tell Reginald Ward of your success in helping all of the members of my party. I am sure he would be happy to hear it!''Please, friend, go and tell Reginald Ward of your success in helping all of the members of my party. I am sure he would be happy to hear it!''Please, friend, go and tell Reginald Ward of your success in helping all of the members of my party. I am sure he would be happy to hear it!''Please, friend, go and tell Reginald Ward of your success in helping all of the members of my party. I am sure he would be happy to hear it!''Please, friend, go and tell Reginald Ward of your success in helping all of the members of my party. I am sure he would be happy to hear it!''Please, friend, go and tell Reginald Ward of your success in helping all of the members of my party. I am sure he would be happy to hear it!'Dardúr will aid you in hewing a rune-stone of great cunning.The stones can be found among the goblins of Goblin-town. The runic inscription is held by Ánraig, east of Gwaelband in the ruins of Annúminas.

Dardúr wishes you to bring the stones from the roots of the Misty Mountains and a runic inscription held by an Angmarim archivist named Ánraig.Nerbeglir can be found in Malenhad in Angmar. Wargs can be found in Angmar, the Misty Mountains, and Forochel.

Dardúr now requires Neekerbreeker-horns and hooked Warg-claws.Gruglok may be found at Urugarth in Angmar. Azgoth and Mormoz may be found at Carn Dûm in Angmar.

Dardúr now needs Gruglok's Sigil of War and a Rune of Winged Dominance.'The time has come for you to delve into the deeper secrets of rune-craft, ${PLAYER}. As with the runes themselves, the stones upon which they are graven are key to their power.

'Just as a warrior needs a sword of fine steel, so too do we need rune-stones to accompany our gifts. It is in the shaping of your stone that you will achieve wisdom.

'Here is my counsel on what materials will aid you for shaping your stone. First, bring me stone from the Misty Mountains, oft carried by the goblins there, and runes from the foul sorcerer Ánraig at Annúminas. Make haste, ${PLAYER}, if you wish to bear your weapon soon!''I will prepare the stone while you go forth and seek the second set of items. Among them are the horns of the nerbeglir of Angmar and claws from the Wargs which can be found in Angmar, Forochel, and the Misty Mountains.''Well done, ${PLAYER}. You prove your worth again and again.

'One set of components remains before your stone is complete. Follow the counsel I gave you, and though the path you walk may be dire, the reward at its end shall be great.

'Find for me a Sigil of War, borne by Gruglok in Urugarth, and a Rune of Winged Dominance from Mormoz and Azgoth in Carn Dûm.''Most impressive, ${PLAYER}. Your rune-stone is nearly complete. These items will seal its power just so.

'There. All is well. Accept this rune-stone, ${PLAYER}, and use its secrets well. The greatest craft of the Rune-keepers of old rests in your hands.'Defeat the hidden threats of Angmar.Defeat Ferndúr the VirulentDefeat BogberethBy examining the charred wood you brought her, Ruidhriel has discovered that a great battle took place upon the Burnt Tor.The remains of the battle are scattered across the Burnt Tor, west of Ruidhriel.

Ruidhriel has asked you to find any remains of the battle that occurred upon the Burnt Tor and hide them.'The burning of the Tor was the work of someone very powerful. If the Company was so pressed that they were forced to set such a blaze there will surely be other signs of the battle left upon the hilltop.

'Go and search for these signs and attempt to cover them as best you can. Be careful, ${PLAYER}, for I am sure that the threat of danger has not yet passed, and evil may yet lurk in the shadows of the Burnt Tor.''These are strange tokens, ${PLAYER}! I will keep them safe and deliver them to Master Elrond. He may be able to read the signs more clearly than I.'A well-placed strike can throw your opponent off-balance.Strike with StaggerA well-placed strike can throw your opponent off-balance.Strike with StaggerYou have braved many challenges and walked many leagues through the wilderness, and now you are counted among the greatest of hunters.Fenton Marshley is outside the Hunting Lodge east of the town of Archet and north of Bree, in Bree-land.

You have been told to speak with Fenton Marshley, a skilled hunter who may have some words for you.Meet Fenton Marshley for further Hunter trainingFenton Marshley is outside the Hunting Lodge east of the town of Archet and north of Bree, in Bree-land.

You have been told to speak with Fenton Marshley, a skilled hunter who may have some words for you.As a Hunter, you have achieved such renown as to receive a summons from Fenton Marshley of Archet.

You should seek Fenton Marshley to receive further training in your class.'It is an honour and a privilege to meet you, ${PLAYER}. Many good folk depend on us for their protection and the safety of their borders, though they may not know it. We have the respect of the Rangers of the North for a reason, and our paths often cross with theirs, to the betterment of all.'A Sage of Eriador has invited you to recollect your experience seeking the palantír deep within Barad Tironn.The Reflecting Pool on Tinnudir will allow you to revisit 'The Eye of Angmar.'

