he dwarves who lived beneath these mountains, but it was broken by some unknown calamity during the long dark years after the fall of Durin. Now the water lies here unmoving, flooding the deeps in great stagnant pools.The Forges of Khazad-dûm were without rival, greatest of all forges in the Third Age of Middle-earth. The dwarves will not rest until it has been reclaimed, for much of the priceless artistry born in that hall has been lost to the long years of Moria's abandonment.The Orcs of Fil Ghashan are suspiciously watchful, brooding ever on their hopeful assault against Lothlórien. They show no mercy to any who dare threaten their plans.The Gate of Ruin opens upon the Endless Stair of Khazad-dûm, but as its name now suggests, this doorway into the stairs has been shattered and lies in ruins.The Orc-graves in Skûmfil have bred many sickening creatures, all grown strong on the flesh of the dead, and all consumed with evil thoughts.The Dark Delvings should have been left forgotten, for they are haunted by ancient and fell creatures with no place in Middle-earth.The Sixteenth Hall is now a home to the fungal disease that poisons many Orcs and turns them to corrupted Globsnaga. Great evil brews in this hall, threatening to cover Moria in sickness and fear.Many Orcs lurk in Mudmul-charâf, serving no purpose but to endanger the free peoples who roam in Khazad-dûm.Orcs can be found in Mudmul-charâf, in the north-west passages of Zelem-melek.

