You should defeat Rûgh and burn his banner.Defeat RûghDestroy Rûgh's bannerYou have defeated the leader of Uzbad-bakân, an evil Orc named Rûgh. With his banner destroyed and his constant chanting silenced, the Orcs of Zelem-melek will be less organized in their attacks.CompletedYou have found the barracks of Uzbad-bakân, at the back of which stand a vicious Orc-leader and his rallying banner to give constant reinforcement to the soldiers of Moria.You have defeated the leader of Uzbad-bakân, an evil Orc named Rûgh. With his banner destroyed and his constant chanting silenced, the Orcs of Zelem-melek will be less organized in their attacks.The Deep-way lies upon a hidden passage between the Water-works and the Great Delving. The Iron Garrison uses this passage as a major store-house.Complete everything in the Battle of the Deep-way SkirmishDefeat LhenathronilDefeat Gâmûrz-ashDefeat EarthbreakerDefeat ForktongueDefeat FoulscumDefeat the Gorged QueenDefeat BurntailDefeat the Mad CroakerThe Twenty-first Hall of Moria is vital to the success of the Iron Garrison's expedition to reclaim Khazad-dûm.Complete everything in the Battle of the Twenty-first Hall SkirmishDefeat Swangeir GoldstealerDefeat Morûrz-kalaDefeat KalusDefeat ThroqûmDefeat FodhrovalDefeat the Shadow from the Heart of the WorldDefeat HelchnaethExtinguish the goblins' fire-potThe Way of Smiths bridges the caverns between the Redhorn Lode and the Silverdeep. Within lies the famed forge of the dwarves known as the Heart of Fire.Complete everything in the Battle of the Way of Smiths SkirmishDefeat Fire-heartDefeat Ice-heartDefeat Shadow-heartDefeat CelebarchDefeat FikûhomDefeat DurbrennilDefeat SadâukExplore Zirakzigil.NOT USEDThis gateway opens up onto the face of Zirakzigil, the mighty Silvertine.A great battle took place upon the face of Cloudyhead, a battle between a Balrog and another mighty Power. The Balrog's demise broke the mountainside here.These are the remains of the mighty fire demon who had hidden itself in Moria in the days of yore.Explore the Flaming Deeps of Moria.NOT USEDAnazârmekhem stands as the gate-house to the Flaming Deeps. While its purpose long ago was mainly a matter of governance, its purpose now is as a rallying point for the dwarf-expedition as it reaches into the lowest caverns of Moria.Travellers passing from the Water-works into the Flaming Deeps must descend the great Burning Stair. Once the flames were kept tame and well-confined within the furnaces and forges of the dwarves of Khazad-dûm, but under the brutal care of the Orcs, the waters here have become contaminated with foul oils and sulphur, and gouts of fire may leap from every chasm and by-way.The Cross-roads of Ash -- once it was a great light at the centre of the vast dwarf-forges -- now its light is overwhelmed by the ruddy burning hue of flames and the eternal black pall of soot that hangs over everything here.Dwarf-legends speak of the Seven Fathers of the Dwarves, whom they claim awoke from the stone first among all the people of Middle-earth - though the Elves claim that they were the first to walk beneath the stars. Here the Seven Fathers have been immortalized in a vast work of Stone-craft.Hadâd-mezer was renowned amongst the dwarves as one of the greatest workshops of Moria. From these forges came some of the finest works of smith-craft that the world shall ever see.Hurmulkezer once stood as the palace of an ancient dwarf-lord of Moria, though his name has now been lost from the pages of history. Now it stands as a bastion of corruption and evil from which Orc-captains oversee the labours of their minions and slaves in the hot forges of the Flaming Deeps.The gates of Hudnul-meden are said to have sealed away some of the most well-protected vaults of the dwarves in ancient days, but the old texts are not clear on whether they were meant to keep thieves and intruders out, or something else in....Orc-arrows litter the floor of the Twenty-first Hall near the east arch.Orc-arrows can be found near the East Arch of the Twenty-first Hall.

You should collect Orc-arrows to show to the dwarves of the Twenty-first Hall.Collect Orc-arrowsOrc-arrows can be found near the East Arch of the Twenty-first Hall.

You should collect Orc-arrows to show to the dwarves of the Twenty-first Hall.Collect Orc-arrows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have stowed away many Orc-arrows to show to Mögr in the Twenty-first Hall.

You should return to Mögr with the arrows you collected.Many Orc-arrows litter the floor of the Twenty-first Hall near the arch leading eastwards.

You should collect the Orc-arrows and show them to Mögr in the Twenty-first Hall as evidence that foes passed this way not long ago.'Harrumph! These arrows could be from any time, really... one year or a hundred! I do not see what your discovery signifies, but I thank you for your efforts, just the same.'Explore the Foundations of Stone in Moria.NOT USEDThe Shadowed Refuge lies in the deepest extents of Moria. Few records remain to describe these caverns, though scholars presume that it was somewhere within that Durin's Bane lay slumbering for uncounted years before the dwarves delved and awoke its wrath. This area can hardly be called a refuge now, save that a small expedition has killed or driven off the strange creatures from this section of the caverns and stands a vigilant guard against any that approach.This stair was rumoured to have been built up through a natural passage that ran from the deepest caverns of Khazad-dûm to the frozen heights of Zirak-zigil, far above. Here at the bottom, parts of it appear to have recently been shattered by some terrible force.An enormous shard of stone lies half-submerged in this gloomy lake, jutting upwards into the endless darkness. It appears to be a broken remnant of the great Bridge of Khazad-dûm, fallen from far above when the bridge was shattered.The arches of Zabad-fakâk open upon the Foundations of Stone, the deepest caverns in all of Moria. The dwarves of old long shunned these caverns out of a sense of foreboding and lingering darkness that could never be fully dispelled by lantern or torch, but when rich veins of mithril were discovered within, these concerns were set aside, and the dwarves began to dig in earnest at the very roots of the mountain.The caverns of Dalgum-ru have never been safe. Even in Durin's day, miners who entered these twisted passages would sometimes disappear without trace, and rarely would an expedition pass through without reporting strange shadows moving just beyond the light of torches, but never clearly seen.Explore the Great Delving of Moria.NOT USEDThe threshold of the western gate of Khazad-dûm, it was here that Durin completed his legendary journey through the heart of the mountains and came once again into the light of the sun, having discovered the place that would become the home of his peoples for ages to come.The Dolven-view acts as a window to the Great Delving below. From here, dwarves could once be seen bustling to and fro, visiting the markets, libraries, and workshops of this once-lively cavern. Now there is only a long, lonely view of goblin-fires and vile insects crawling through the dark.As the northern gate of the Great Delving, the Dwarf-lords' Gate provides the main passage for those seeking to reach Durin's Way.The Lonely Span lies just beyond the eastern gate of the Great Delving. It is the only direct passage to the streets of Zelem-melek, and is where the nobles of Khazad-dûm once resided.The great carvings on the walls of this ancient grotto represent the ancient dwarf-council that advised Durin and aided in the daily administration of Khazad-dûm. Many of these great carvings have been damaged by time and wear, though luckily the bridge that spans this space remains intact, even if somewhat perilous.The grand plaza of Gazatmur was once the heart of the Great Delving, surrounded by some of the most prestigious institutions and finest architecture within Khazad-dûm. Now it has been overrun by a large goblin encampment that prevents the dwarves from regaining control of the Delving.Shemeldurj was an unusual estate constructed by an eccentric but famous architect of the late Second Age. In a departure from classical dwarf-design, it features fanciful spans and buttresses that challenge the observer to seek around each turn and corner to discover where next it goes. Much of it lies in ruins, spoiled by time and the ravaging hands of goblins.After the fall of Durin, terror grasped the dwarves of Moria, now seized by panic and fear. Good Náin took his father's place and strove to quell the menace that threatened his halls, wishing to restore peace to his brethren. His short reign was a valiant one, but he too fell to the Bane of Khazad-dûm.Dómárr has found many unpleasantries during his travels through Zelem-melek.Reinn can be found in the Great Hall of Durin.

You should speak with Reinn.Listen to Dómárr's taleDómárr can be found in the Great Hall of Durin.

You should speak with Dómárr.Speak with Dómárr'I would like to hear more from Dómárr about what he has encountered in Zelem-melek. Perhaps an accurate account will bring Mögr to his senses.

'I would like to return with useful information, at any rate.''I hope Mögr has the sense to listen to Reinn this time, eh? In the meantime, I have been thinking about what to do about the Durub Orcs in Gazatu-ru....''Let us listen to Dómárr's tale, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps he will have information I can bring back to the Twenty-first Hall.'Unravel the epic story of the Mines of Moria.Complete Book 1 Chapter 13: Khazad-dûm At Last!Complete Book 2 Chapter 8: The Twenty-first HallComplete Book 3 Chapter 9: We Cannot Get OutComplete Book 4 Chapter 8: Bróin's TaleComplete Book 5 Chapter 5: The Battle of the Twenty-first HallComplete Book 6 Chapter 9: The Mirror of GaladrielUnravel the epic story of the Mines of Moria.Complete Volume II Book 7: Leaves of LórienComplete Volume II Book 8: Scourge of Khazad-dûmComplete Volume II Book 9: Fortress of the NazgûlExplore the Redhorn Lodes of Moria.NOT USEDThe Orc-watch has been founded by Brogur and Bósi's dwarf expedition as a watch-post over the Orc horde that holds the Redhorn Lodes. The dwarves hope to cut the different Orc tribes off from each other by controlling such key points throughout Moria, but the odds stand brutally against them.The Gate of Ruin opens upon the Endless Stair of Khazad-dûm, but as its name now suggests, this doorway into the stairs has been shattered and lies in ruin.Sigin-tharâkh is little more than a platform jutting up above rivers of molten fire that have broken this section of the caverns into a set of treacherous stone formations. The dwarves of old Moria tied them together with a series of stairs and pathways, but these have not been maintained for hundreds of years, and can be perilous to an unwary traveller.This vast natural pit was used for hundreds of years by the dwarves of Khazad-dûm to dispose of the tailings left behind by their enormous mining efforts within the Redhorn Lodes. It speaks to the enormous depth of this shaft that in all that time, its bottom yet remains unseen and unsounded.This important looking building must once have been an administrative centre for the mining efforts of the dwarves in the Redhorn Lodes. Now, naturally, its interior lies ruined and abandoned, for the Orcs have little use for it.Malmezel was once a street of some sort lined with small buildings. Few of the old records speak to its purpose, though one might guess that it once was filled with workshops or taverns for the dwarves who once worked these mines.In the time of ancient Moria, this building would have served as the quarters of the high-foreman of the Redhorn Lodes; now it acts as the throne of whatever Orc has risen to power over his bretheren in this vast cavern.Budkhul-beken provides a commanding view of the great cavern below. Perhaps in days long past, the mine's overseers stood here to plan out their excavations; now all one can see are the shattered ruins below, crawling with Orcs....Find important locations along the path of the Company of the Ring through Eregion.Find the Burnt TorFind the Caradhras camp-fireFind the Sirannon GateFind the High Hollin camp-siteFind the Tâl Caradhras camp-siteDiscover the ancient ruins of Eregion.Discover GwingrisDiscover Echad EregionDiscover Echad MirobelDiscover Echad DúnannDiscover PembarDiscover Barad MorlasExplore the Silvertine Lodes of Moria.NOT USEDThe Deep Descent is an old guard chamber that oversees the entrance to the mines below. The mastery of this chamber has allowed the dwarf-expedition to control the passage between the Silvertine Lodes and the Great Delving, preventing the goblins from massing together into an unstoppable horde. Nevertheless, they remain badly outnumbered and are pressed daily to hold the chambers against the vicious foes that inhabit the deeps.Once a bustling mine worked by hundreds of industrious dwarves, Gamil Filik has been overrun by goblins and is now one of the largest strongholds of their kind within Moria. If the Goblin's hold on western Moria is to be broken, the enemies in Gamil Filik must eventually be routed, but against such numbers it seems an impossible endeavour.The great pit of Menem-berej was once home to the richest lodes of mithril ever unearthed in Khazad-dûm. This enormous platform was constructed to assist the dwarves in their mighty efforts to extract the precious metal. Though this mine was believed to have been sapped of most of its resources by the time Moria fell, Brogur and Bósi's dwarves vow to find some valuable remnants of the riches that were once so abundant.The Old Silvertine City is in the south of the Silvertine Lodes, and was likely the home of all the miners that once sought mithril beneath the great mountain.Explore the Water-works of Moria.NOT USEDThe brackish damp and mould of the deep Water-works have led the dwarves of the expedition to name this place the Rotting Cellar. The air seems laden with an unhealthy mildew, though the view offered over the once-great Water-works of Moria remains awe-inspiring.This massive structure once powered great pumps that moved water about Khazad-dûm, providing drinking water for the dwarves, as well as supplying the enormous quantities of water needed to cool the grand forges. Nowhere else in Middle-earth could such feats of engineering be found, but, as they have been left unmaintained, most of these have fallen to ruin.Several underground rivers were discovered flowing throughout the great caverns that make up Khazad-dûm, and all of these were tamed by the dwarves over the centuries. Here, the greatest of these torrents have been brought together to form the great waterfall that came to be known as Durin's Beard, in honour of the Father of the Dwarves.Whether this dilapidated structure was ever truly a palace is hard to say. Time and moisture have taken their tolls, and anything of value was either stolen or rusted away long ago. It might have once housed the dwarves responsible for administrating the great Water-works that stretch out of sight from the steps of this building.Whatever this place once was, or whatever it contained, it now holds only stagnant foetid pools and debris. It may once have served as the entrance to an ancient store-house.This ancient store-house may once have held materials needed to maintain the great mechanisms of the Water-works of Khazad-dûm. Now it lies quiet, filled with still black water that does not appear to have been stirred by any living thing for years.The surviving records of Khazad-dûm mark this old store-house as a treasury. Perhaps this building once overflowed with dwarf-gold and jewels, but that was long ago, before the Orcs came and stole it all away as tribute for the Dark Lord of Mordor.Next to this great pillar, in the ceiling far, far above, one can make out an ancient shaft that seems to lead to the upper levels of Moria. Whatever use the dwarves had for it, it seems that the goblins have since taken to using it as a place to hurl their refuse.The gateway of Harâzgund leads to the source of one of the greatest ducts in all of Khazad-dûm. It once supplied water to nearly half of all the dwarves who lived beneath these mountains, but it was broken by some unknown calamity during the long dark years after the fall of Durin. Now the water lies here unmoving, flooding the deeps in great stagnant pools.Dwarf-scholars believe that the Chamber of Memory was so named because it held a great repository of books and scrolls, storing many of the more mundane records of Khazad-dûm. If it indeed ever held such records, they have either been stolen away or were destroyed when the Water-works flooded after the kingdom's fall.Defeat the numerous and varied foes within MoriaTrolls, with their thundering footsteps and mighty fists, pace the halls of the dwarves, destroying the artistry of many years with the careless thrashing of their hulking forms.Defeat trolls in MoriaIt takes a stout-hearted warrior to fell a troll who has many times the girth and might of even the doughtiest of Men. Though you have taken down enough already to earn yourself a title befitting of your righteousness, to defeat yet more would surely increase your strength in battle.Defeat trolls in MoriaDefeat the Uruk-leaders in Eregion.Defeat LogonSummon and defeat FauldushSummon and defeat ShatogtarDefeat Pizumu the Spy-masterSummon and defeat Gâmzhurm, Lord of Porth CadlusSummon and defeat Akashpug, Chief of Târ StazgSummon and defeat Râkhuga the TrackerThe dwarves of the Central Halls expeditions will need a great deal of help in overcoming the obstacles that await them in the Mines of Moria. Your assistance will be welcome, for there is much work to be done if Khazad-dûm is to be hospitable once more.Complete Quests in the Central Halls of Moria.Complete quests in the Central HallsThe silence of Moria can be terrifying, but more terrifying still is the howl that shatters the stillness. Wargs hunt in the darkness, aiding the evil purposes of the Orcs and goblins who dwell with them.Defeat Wargs in MoriaYou have done much to silence the ominous braying of the Wargs in the Mines of Moria, but their presence will remain strong unless further action is taken against them. Further hunting of the fierce, slavering beasts would be a great help to the dwarves trying to re-establish themselves in the lost halls of Khazad-dûm.Defeat Wargs in MoriaThe dwarves in the Deeps of Moria will need a great deal of help in overcoming the obstacles that await them in the Mines of Moria. Your assistance will be welcome, for there is much work to be done if Khazad-dûm is to be hospitable once more.NOT USEDComplete Quests in the Deeps of Moria.Complete quests in the Moria DeepsExplore the western area of Durin's Way in Moria.NOT USEDThis ancient guard room controls the passages leading up into Durin's Way, as well as the ones east, towards the Twenty-first Hall. It is now occupied by part of the dwarf expedition to re-take Moria from the Orcs and goblins.These stairs lead to the upper halls of Durin's Way. They were likely built before the larger road to the west was completed, and the room in which they were constructed may have been part of the original course Durin mapped through Moria as he explored its depths.These ruined workshops and dwellings once housed a family of dwarves who were famous for their skill in crafting wonderous toys in intricate designs of wood and metal. Such simple joys were lost long ago when darkness fell upon Khazad-dûm, and the Orcs came.Whatever its original purpose was, a large host of goblins has made its residence in the hall of Ninknakh Faltor. Their dark shrines and crude constructions litter the area, and their sheer numbers pose a serious threat to any hopes of retaking Durin's Way.Tharâkh Bazân is a most unusual wonder to find beneath the roots of a mountain. It is more commonly known as 'Durin's Garden', for Durin himself ordered it built so that any dwarf might come here and be reminded of the wider sun-lit world beyond the bounds of Moria. Clever constructs of polished metal and crystal guide light down from far above to grant life here, beneath the mountain. It seems that the evil things that now infest Moria likewise find this light uncomfortable, for among all the works of the dwarves, Tharâkh Bazân remains relatively unmarred by their ravages.Salab Nurjundul was once one of the busiest intersections in all of Moria, and each day hundreds if not thousands of dwarves would pass through here going about their lives and business. Now it is desolate and empty, offering only a dangerous gauntlet for would-be explorers to pass through with constant threat of ambush by the evil denizens who befoul the once-beautiful workshops and residences nearby.These chambers once acted as the gate-house of the Endless Stair. It is from here that one might once have made the ascent up thousands of steps to the heights of Zirak-zigil above and thereupon mounted the Tower of Durin, and from there look out upon the wide world below with an eye rivalling that of the Eagles themselves. Now Orcs act as the wardens of this place, and they are loath to allow any to pass uncontested.Defeat wolves and Wargs in Eregion.Defeat wolves and Wargs in EregionDefeat many wolves and Wargs in Eregion.Defeat wolves and Wargs in EregionThough one of the least intelligent forms of Dragon-kind, the Deep-worms are no less of a threat. The hunger for treasure lies heavily upon all of their kind, drawing them into the dark halls of Moria.Defeat worms in MoriaYou have struck a mighty blow against the worms of Moria, but many more continue to spawn, making it difficult to eliminate the threat.Defeat worms in MoriaExplore the region of Zelem-melek within Moria.NOT USEDIn times long past, a great market stood here where the dwarves of Moria and travellers from abroad would trade in goods from across much of Middle-earth. Fine woods, wine, and foodstuffs would be exchanged for gold, priceless gems, and finely wrought pieces of smith-craft. Anything at all could be bought or sold within this very hall, though it is hard to imagine to look upon it now.Founded as the dwarves expanded east from the halls of Old Moria to the west, the terraces of Mudmul-charâf once housed many a fine dwarf-craftsman -- rather, at least among those wealthy enough to live but a few halls from the very throne of Durin himself.Water has ever been an important aspect of dwarf-artistry, as hidden rivers and lakes are among the most vital features of any settlement beneath the hills and mountains. The Hall of Flowing Water is a fine example of this artistry, and it has remained surprisingly intact after so many years untended.The original purpose of the Chamber of Gabil-mamach cannot be found in the old texts and histories of Moria. The simple span that crosses this room does not seem to serve any vital purpose, and the wise might argue whether it is symbolic in nature, or whether something of import was once contained in the space below.It is likely that the Chamber of Mazarbul once served some great purpose in the old days of Moria, yet now it is left the tomb of Balin, son of Fundin and friend to old Bilbo, who not so many years ago led an expedition much like the current one to retake Moria and restore it to the glories of old. Alas that his efforts came to ruin so final that none ever returned to their homes to tell the tale of it, and only now has the truth been learned.Here at Gabil-hul once stood a dam constructed to capture the waters that flow through this area and so supply water to the dwarves inhabiting Zelem-melek. Now it lies in shattered ruins among stagnant puddles.Within this grand hall stood the throne of Durin the Deathless, first and greatest father of the dwarves. Though the first Durin lived a life far beyond the span of any other, he did in time pass away, but always another took up his name and ruled on as Durin. The dwarves claim that these rulers are each Durin himself, returned to them from the stone time and again to sit upon the throne of Moria and watch over them, until at last he fell before the power of Durin's Bane, leaving the throne empty, perhaps forever.The shattered plaza of Uzbad-bakân likely hosted the shops of dwarf-merchants once, long ago. Now it lies mostly silent and strewn with rubble, broken only by the occasional echoing snarl of an Orc.The mirror you found is cracked and at risk of being destroyed.Goblins and Orcs are attacking the mirror, attempting to destroy it.Kúli is at the Orc-Watch.

The dwarf Kúli will be interested in knowing the mirror here is still safe.Talk to Kúli at the Orc-watchThis mirror is cracked and about to break. It appears that the local Orcs and goblins have been using it for target practice. As you go to touch it, you hear the sounds of approaching Orcs.'You found a nearly broken mirror out there, you say? And you defended it from attack? Well my word, you are a rather honourable one. I would not have expected such heroism from someone outside of the Garrison.'The strange fungus infecting the eastern side of Redhorn Lodes seems to have affected the spiders, and even the ground has been transformed. With the many Moria and White Hand Orcs in the area, what impact is it having on them?The new form of Orc can be found to the east and south-east of Knakk's camp on the elevated walkway.Defeat Globsnaga Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Knakk is camped on the elevated walkway in the north-east corner of Redhorn Lodes.Return to KnakkThe Orcs that wandered into the infected area seem to have radically changed. These were Moria and White Hand Orcs, and now they are some new form of Fungal Orc. What form of fungus could change their body and behaviour so drastically, and so quickly?'They were once Moria and White Hand Orcs? The infection created a new form of Orc, with new behaviours and are unfriendly to their previous tribes. How curious, and frightening. They have the same mutation as the spiders?

'There must be a guiding hand to this infestation. It's working too well to be simple coincidence.'The Moria Orcs have mined the Redhorn Lodes for a long time, but more recently the White Hand moved in and have gained control of the northern region. If the White Hand gain control of all the resources in the Redhorn they could quickly become a formidable foe.White Hand raiding parties can be found on the cleared path between the two tribes of Orcs.

Ingifast wants you to find a raiding party of the White Hand and dispatch them.Ingifast is camped on the stairs leading up to the Orc-watch.

You should return to Ingifast with news of your success.Return to Ingifast'I was out scouting the movements of the Orcs, and I overheard a pair of White Hand archers that wandered by. They didn't see me, and I was able to eavesdrop on their conversation. They mentioned that a White Hand raiding party assaulted the Moria Orcs. They fended it off but took many losses.

'It sounds like the White Hand is gaining control of the Redhorn. It would bode ill for all of Moria if they gain control of the resources here. 

'${CLASS}, the only chance we have is if they maintain their struggle, while we work to weaken them both. I suggest that you begin by making sure the White Hand raiding parties cease their assault. Look for the White Hand raiding parties between the two tribes of Orc encampments.''Ah! ${PLAYER}, well done. Pushing back the aggressive White Hand may give the Moria Orcs a chance to fight back. With any luck, they will continue to blunt their swords on each other, while we quietly pick at their numbers.'You discovered a black hilt in the belt pouch of an Orc-wraith within the Forgotten Treasury.Garthar is at the Deep Descent.

Garthar may be able to afford you some level of knowledge about the strange hilt you found.Where the blade should meet the hilt there is only a thin space where once a blade fit. This hilt is heavy and cast in very dark iron, wrought not by the hand of dwarves.

Perhaps Garthar at the Deep Descent can help you discover the fell means by which this object was made.'What is this you have here? I do not like the feel of this thing.

'No blade, not made by dwarves... not made by Orc either, if my eyes do not deceive me. This blade was crafted within the darkest forges of Middle-earth, I would gather. An Elf may know more, but there is no time for sending you all about the world.

'I would venture that this is one of the blades told of that made the wraiths what they are. If there are others of these blades in Khazad-dûm, you should take great care to steer clear of them, lest you meet a terrible fate.'The Orc-tribes are driven and motivated by strong Orc-leaders. The White Hand tribe is led by a strong military tactician, while the Moria tribe is led by a brutal overseer focused on mining every last bit of ore out of the Redhorn Lodes.The Orc leaders are located somewhere within their tribe's encampment.Defeat the White Hand tacticianDefeat the Moria task-masterKnakk is located on the elevated platform just north of the White Hand Orcs' encampment, on the road toward the Grand Stair.

He has remained hidden from the Orcs so far, but their scouts have almost found him a few times. He will be happy to know they are going to be far less organized for some time, thanks to you.Find Knakk's camp-site'We have come to find these tribes of Orc are different, and this must be based on their leaders. The Moria tribes don't have much focus on their military. Their army is spread all over Moria and elsewhere, and those here are focused on the mining operation alone. I would guess their leader is a vicious task-master.

'The White Hand seem to be superior tacticians, to take over most of the Redhorn Lodes. They are pushing the Moria tribe out of Redhorn surprisingly fast. The White Hand's leader must be a brilliant tactical mind.''Defeating the leaders of the nearby Orc-tribes will help keep my location secret for a bit longer. For that, I thank you.

'Your deed is sure to stir anarchy among the remaining Orcs. The reign of Orcs in this part of Redhorn might finally come to an end!'The Moria Orcs created forges long ago to help them process and use the ore they gather from the bountiful deposits in the Redhorn Lodes. The White Hand invaded and took control of some of those forges and threaten to take the others.Moria and White Hand Orcs are hard at work mining the ore here in the Redhorn and producing vast amounts of armour and weapons for their ranks.

The White Hand Orcs have entrenched themselves in the northern half of the Redhorn Lodes, while the Moria Orcs struggle to keep the southern half.Knakk is located on the elevated platform just north of the White Hand Orcs' encampment, on the road toward the Grand Stair.

Knakk is eager for any updates on the Orcs working so close to his hidden camp-site. It is vital that they remain unaware of his being there.Find Knakk's camp-site'${PLAYER}, it is time to find the forges these Orcs have kept burning day and night in the Redhorn. They must be somewhere close-by, because the smell of burnt material and metals lingers in the air. Their forges smell like super heated furnaces.

'The two Orc-tribe camps must have a few forges of their own. I suspect they are well guarded, so keep your wits about you.''So, the forges have been found, and they really are not very far from here?'

You describe the locations to Knakk, and he scribbles notes and draws a crude map on a piece of parchment he had lying around.

'Good, I marked these on my map. I'll be sure to send this document to Róarr and Ingifast, who hope to some day use those forges for our purposes!'You have found Bingo, but he lost something of his own while he was away: his memory!Bingo Boffin is at the Fanged Pit.

Spalvi has given you the page of manuscript that Willem Whisker found within Moria, and believes that if you show it to Bingo it might help him regain his memory.Spalvi Stout-heart is at the dwarf-camp near the Fanged Pit.Talk to Spalvi at the Fanged PitSpalvi gave you a number of leads to follow that might help you collect the lost pages of Bingo's manuscript: 

'Merrevail in the Fanged Pit might have some pages. Orcs and Warg-riders cover great distances throughout Zelem-melek, and they might have found some pages. A war council was formed in Anazârmekhem, in the Flaming Deeps, and its leaders might have uncovered some pages. Dwarves in the Twenty-first Hall that see a lot of traffic for their services, such as vault-keepers or bards, might also have come into possession of pages.'Spalvi said he would meet you at the Twenty-first Hall after you followed all of his leads.Bingo is in the Twenty-first Hall, ready to read the manuscript you assembled.Bingo is reading through his own manuscript... but he doesn't know it!Wait for Bingo to finish reading the account of his journeyBingo is in the Twenty-first Hall of Moria.

Bingo has finished reading his manuscript, and you should speak with him to see if it helped him regain his memory.Talk to Bingo in the Twenty-first HallSpalvi and Willem Whisker are in the Twenty-first Hall, waiting to see if Bingo has regained his memories.Talk to Spalvi in the Twenty-first HallTalk to Willem Whisker in the Twenty-first HallBingo is in the Twenty-first Hall of Moria.Talk to Bingo in the Twenty-first Hall'You know, there is something we might try. Do you remember the page of manuscript that Willem found? It is mostly dry now, and nearly legible. Perhaps if you were to show it to Bingo it might provide just the reminder our author needs in order to regain his missing memory!

'I say it is worth the attempt, ${PLAYER}!''Do you remember the page of manuscript that Willem found? It is mostly dry now, and nearly legible. Perhaps if you were to show it to Bingo it might provide just the reminder our author needs in order to regain his missing memory!''Hello there, my new friend. I am sorry I do not remember you, and not only that: I cannot remember why I cannot remember! Yes, what is this?'

You hand Bingo the page of his manuscript that Willem Whisker fished out of the pool along Durin's Way, and he squints at it for a few moments.

'Let's see... Newbury... Buckland... I do know where those places are, of course. Every Shire-hobbit does! My, this hobbit-lass does have a lot of children. Angelica, Berilac, Camellia, Dinodas, Esmeralda, Fosco, Griffo... oh, that's the end of the page. You know something? I almost thought I remembered someone, a girl with red hair and... a sword? No, that's not quite right.

'I am sorry, but I really do not remember.''That was an interesting story, and I would not mind reading more of it! But if it was supposed to make me remember, I am sad to report that it did not.''It was not enough, but I think we might be on the right course! If we were to track down the remaining pages of Bingo's manuscript and show them to him, that might be just the reminder he needs to recapture his lost memories! We should assume that he dropped his manuscript when he fell, and the pages became scattered far and wide throughout Khazad-dûm. Anyone might have picked them up.

'But there are some places we can check:

'Merrevail in the Fanged Pit might have some pages.

'Orcs and Warg-riders cover great distances throughout Zelem-melek, and they might have found some pages.

'A war council was formed in Anazârmekhem, in the Flaming Deeps, and its leaders might have uncovered some pages.

'Dwarves in the Twenty-first Hall that see a lot of traffic for their services, such as vault-keepers or bards, might also have come into possession of pages.

'After you have followed all these leads, I will meet you in the Twenty-first Hall!''You should follow these leads and see if you can find the remaining pages of Bingo's manuscript!

'Merrevail in the Fanged Pit might have some pages. Orcs and Warg-riders cover great distances throughout Zelem-melek, and they might have found some pages. A war council was formed in Anazârmekhem, in the Flaming Deeps, and its leaders might have uncovered some pages. Dwarves in the Twenty-first Hall that see a lot of traffic for their services, such as vault-keepers or bards, might also have come into possession of pages.''You must be looking for these!' Vagun exclaims, holding up three pieces of parchment that have seen better days.

'We of this council have been conducting a campaign against the Orcs, and I must tell you that these papers have had us quite befuddled! Do you see this page? This sequence of letters is clearly a code! And all this talk of geese and ganders! We believed the Orcs were hatching some scheme, and it is a relief to learn otherwise!

'Here, you may have them. We have wasted enough time on these pages already!''You are looking for pages of a manuscript? I have one right here! I quite enjoyed hearing about the peaceful tranquility of the Trollshaws, but sadly the story was not finished: it cuts off mid-sentence. The sentence is 'I awoke refreshed in the morning, ready to face the day, as did...' And that's all of it!

'You are welcome to it, if you want it.''Someone did give me a pair of pages to hold onto, but to be honest I cannot remember who it was. Was it you? ${RACE}, yes? That sounds about right. They're all yours!''You found the rest of Bingo's manuscript? Well done, ${PLAYER}! Let me put these pages in order... there you go!

'Now give this book to Bingo and have him read it, and we will see if this does the trick!''Give that book to Bingo and have him read it, and we will see if this does the trick!''Ah, you have brought me some more pages of that story to read? I would be very glad to have some reading material, my book-loving friend!

'Hand it here, and I will settle in for a good read. It will just be a moment or three.''I hope this works!''Well, did it work?''Well, that was an interesting account, but I can't say anything in it rings any bells for me.'

Bingo stares at you intently, and suddenly breaks into a wide grin.

'I am only teasing, ${PLAYER}! To be honest, I remembered everything completely after the very first page of this account, but I figured it would be a good time to read the whole thing and see if my opinion on Bilbo's opinion of it has changed! I think it might be exciting enough after all, but now I am afraid it is too short! We will need to have more adventures now, for sure!

'But there is something else I need to tell you. This Spalvi fellow here is a good sort, and I want you to know that I completely forgive him for that misunderstanding with the Dourhands. He really did treat me kindly, and at the first opportunity he set me loose from that cage despite the fact that his people would be very cross with him about it. I hope you did not distrust him too greatly on my account!''This Spalvi fellow here is a good sort, and I want you to know that I completely forgive him for that misunderstanding with the Dourhands. He really did treat me kindly, and at the first opportunity he set me loose from that cage despite the fact that his people would be very cross with him about it. I hope you did not distrust him too greatly on my account!''He remembers! I am so glad to see that everything has turned out all right, ${PLAYER}! I apologize for the number of troubles I caused, and I do hope you forgive me for them, but you must admit that we all learned a great deal since our hobbit fell into the mountains.

'Indeed, it sounds as if his adventure is still in the beginning stages, and he plans to write many chapters after this one!'For the first time since Bingo disappeared, Willem Whisker seems genuinely happy. Listening closely, you think you might even hear thrumming in the deep: Willem is purring contentedly.'I remembered something else, ${PLAYER}! When Spalvi and I fled from the Dourhand fortress in the Misty Mountains and I fell into the caverns below, do you know what happened? I fell into an underground river! The water chilled me to the bone, and I think the sudden impact with the icy water is what drove the memories from my mind.

'But do you know what I remembered? When I hit the water, I must have also forgotten that I could not swim, because I took to the current like a fish! I suppose I just needed a little extra motivation! How about that?'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Farewell' (Recommended Level: 58)</rgb>Brogur is confident that the time is nigh for the dwarves to retake Moria, and he is not the only one: many dwarves are making dangerous excursions into deep tunnels.Rókur and Útrygg are exploring Zelem-melek. They departed from the southern passage exiting the Twenty-first Hall, but from there no one knows where they went.

Brogur has asked you to lend any assistance to the dwarves Rókur or Útrygg if you come across them while exploring Zelem-melek.Find Rókur and lend him your aidFind Útrygg and lend him your aidBrogur is in the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall, in Zelem-melek.

You have protected an overzealous dwarf from defeat at the hands of an uruk scout, and should report now to Brogur.'The time of the dwarves is truly at hand, ${PLAYER}! How ludicrous we were to fear that some curse might lie upon Moria after all these years. Had we returned before now, Moria might be thriving already!

'What's done is done. We are here now, and many of the dwarves of our expedition have ventured forth into deep tunnels, seeking great riches that must surely lie untouched within the caves of Moria.

'If you come across Rókur or Útrygg during their explorations of Zelem-melek, be sure to lend them any assistance they may require. They set out through the southern passage from the Twenty-first Hall, but where they went from there I do not know.''He owes you his life, ${PLAYER}. He has always been headstrong and impertinent, but I cannot blame him here. Desire to claim the riches of Khazad-dûm has blinded many dwarves to the minding of their own safety. Still, he is fortunate that you came along when you did; uruks are fierce and not to be underestimated.

'Tell me more of this uruk scout. There is something about your story that gives me pause.''Rókur and Útrygg declared their intention to thoroughly explore Zelem-melek, and left through the southern passage that exits the Twenty-first Hall. If you come across one of them in your own explorations, be sure to lend him your aid.''Return to Brogur in the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall, in Zelem-melek, my friend! Tell him I am fine, and there is no cause for concern!''Return to Brogur in the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall, in Zelem-melek, my friend! Tell him I am fine, and there is no cause for alarm!''Hail, ${RACE}. I have been exploring the lost halls of my fathers; it is a remarkable experience! The great history of this place fills me with wonder at every turn.'

Rókur's eyes narrow and he stares piercingly into the shadows.

'Did you hear that? There's someone there! Prepare yourself for battle!''Hello there, ${RACE}. Lord Brogur sent you to help me? I appreciate the offer, but I have no need of your assistance. In the halls of my fathers I need rely on none but the strength of my own arm!'

Útrygg cocks his head and glares into the shadows.

'I may have been too quick to speak, helpful ${RACE}. There is someone there in the shadows. Prepare for battle!'Brogur is worried that an uruk of the White Hand has been scouting near to the dwarf encampment.Orcs can be found throughout Zelem-melek and the Redhorn Lodes of Moria.

Brogur has asked you to defeat Orcs throughout Zelem-melek and the Redhorn Lodes, no matter what tribe to which they belong.Defeat Orcs in Zelem-melek or the Redhorn Lodes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brogur is in the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall in Zelem-melek.

You have defeated many of the Orcs in Moria, and should report now to Brogur of your success.'Are you certain that the uruk you fought bore the mark of the White Hand?

'That is very unusual, ${PLAYER}. What compelled these creatures to enter Moria? They certainly did not come the way we did! That means they must have gone around the mountains and come in from the east, but for what purpose? Some suspicious plot is afoot, and I do not like it.

'Ah, it is no matter. Any Orc or uruk is a threat to us, those of the White Hand no less than any other! Search out Orcs of any allegiance throughout Zelem-melek and the Redhorn Lodes, and defeat them wherever possible. The only Orc I like is a dead one!''Thank you again for your service. I don't like that there are so many Orcs in Moria, but I am particularly troubled by the presence of those of the White Hand. Either they followed us somehow, or they were here first. Neither of these possibilities bodes well for my folk.''The Orcs of the White Hand should not be here, ${PLAYER}, but all Orcs are a threat. Seek out Orcs in Zelem-melek and the Redhorn Lodes and show them how welcome they are with the bite of your weapon!'Brogur has told you that efforts to excavate the Chamber of Records are proceeding apace, despite his worries about the White Hand presence in Moria.Wárr is in the Chamber of Mazarbul, located to the right off the north hallway that leaves the Twenty-first Hall, in Zelem-melek.

Brogur has told you that Wárr has been asking for your help with something in the Chamber of Mazarbul.Debris litters the Chamber of Mazarbul, on the right side of the north hallway leaving the Twenty-first Hall in Zelem-melek.

Wárr has asked you to search the debris in the Chamber of Mazarbul for anything of interest that might be useful to the dwarves.Brogur is in the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall in Zelem-melek.

You found a mysterious book in the rubble of the Chamber of Mazarbul and should show it to Brogur at once.Bósi is in the Chamber of the Cross-roads, at the southern entrance to Durin's Way, north of the Great Delving.

Brogur has been greatly upset by the tale recorded in the Book of Mazarbul, and has asked you to bring the grim tome to Bósi.'The White Hand will have to wait, ${PLAYER}, because Wárr has sent me word that he wants your help. You remember Wárr, don't you? He supervised the opening of the Hollin Gate, and has a very high opinion of you -- as do we all, of course!

'His team of workers have been excavating the Chamber of Mazarbul, the Chamber of Records, located just north of the Twenty-first Hall and on the right.

'Walk there and speak with Wárr about the task he has for you. We will discuss the White Hand problem upon your return.''The Chamber of Mazarbul, the Chamber of Records, is located on the right side of the hallway that leaves the Twenty-first Hall to the north. Wárr has been asking for you there.''There you are, ${PLAYER}. I have some work for you, if you don't mind getting your hands dirty.

'This place was called the Chamber of Mazarbul because it once stored important records of its day. At some point during the long years that Moria stood empty, the ceiling seems to have collapsed. As you can see, the whole room has been filled with rubble and debris.

'We're trying to search through the debris for anything of interest we can find. Help my dwarves with this task and bring me anything noteworthy.''The chamber is full of debris, ${PLAYER}. Who knows what the rubble might be hiding?'Wárr flips through the damaged pages of the book and turns as white as a sheet.

'You should bring this to Brogur at once, ${PLAYER}. This... this is not good.'Brogur takes the book from you and flips gingerly through some of the brittle pages. His breath catches as comprehension appears to dawn on him.

'This is the Book of Mazarbul, and it seems to contain a record of Balin's settlement. It is grim reading, ${PLAYER}.

'If you would be so good as to bring it to my cousin Bósi, he will want to see it. He has always had more of a stomach than I for records such as this. I do not want to read of their horrific end. It is enough for me to know that they are dead.

'Bósi is still in the Chamber of the Cross-roads in Durin's Way, north of the Great Delving.''Nay, I do not wish to look upon the Book of Mazarbul again. I have seen enough.

'Bring it to my cousin Bósi in the Chamber of the Cross-roads, at the southern entrance to Durin's Way, north of the Great Delving.''What have you brought me, my friend?'

Bósi leafs through the book, and a look of deep sadness settles upon his features as he comes to understand what the brittle pages are saying.

'This is a record of Balin's expedition, and tells of their unhappy end. At least we now know what befell our kin in Moria -- Black Pit indeed! Or so it seems in my grief. I will read it from the beginning, but in the meantime I would have your help with another matter so that I may devote my attention to this.'Bósi has asked you to take care of a small matter while he reads from the Book of Mazarbul.Sweina is somewhere among the Silvertine Lodes, likely at the southernmost reach of their mining tracks, near the entrance to the Water-works.

Bósi has asked that you seek out and rescue one of his dwarves who has foolishly decided to challenge the goblins of the Silvertine Lodes. Sweina believes that the goblins of Gamil Filik have their home much deeper than the Silvertine Lodes, near the entrance to the Water-works.Talk to SweinaBósi is in the Chamber of the Cross-roads, at the southern entrance to Durin's Way, north of the Great Delving.

You found Sweina and fought alongside him against many goblins, and should return now to Bósi.'One of my dwarves, a miner by the name of Sweina, has taken it upon himself to attack the goblins of the Silvertine Lodes. He has the strength of limb to match his headstrong nature, but there are too many goblins in that place for even him to conquer alone.

'Wait! Do not leave for Gamil Filik just yet, for that is not where my dwarf has gone! Sweina believes the goblins have a home in the Water-works, and I heard him vow to follow the mining tracks that run through the Silvertine Lodes down to the flooded deeps themselves. Look for the southernmost tracks on your map; you will find Sweina searching there for goblins!

'I will continue to read from the Book of Mazarbul, ${PLAYER}, but you must journey to the southernmost reach of the Silvertine Lodes to dissuade Sweina from his foolhardy mission. I do not doubt that he will slay many goblins, but he will not know when to retreat. You must find him and rescue him from his own folly.''Sweina will be somewhere near the southernmost mining tracks of the Silvertine Lodes, near the entrance to the Water-works, seeking goblins to slay. Find him and rescue him, ${PLAYER}, for he is well over his head in this!''Bósi wants me to return to the Chamber of the Cross-roads? I cannot do that now, ${PLAYER}! There are goblin-necks to hew! Come, fight by my side!''You found Sweina? I am glad, for he is strong of arm and valiant; we will have need of him for many days to come. I hope he does not get himself into trouble before then.

'I have read more of the Book of Mazarbul.'Bósi has read more of the Book of Mazarbul, and is worried about Brogur's haste.Brogur is in the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall, in Zelem-melek.

Bósi is worried by what he has read of Balin's fate in the Book of Mazarbul, and wants you to warn his cousin to be more cautious.'It is worse than I expected, ${PLAYER}: Balin's final lot was grim. I have not yet read all, but there is much here we should take as a warning. They were greatly outnumbered, but seemed not to know this until it was too late.

'Return to the Chamber of Leadership off the Twenty-first Hall, in Zelem-melek, and tell my cousin Brogur to be more cautious. I am worried that if we do not take lessons from Balin's expedition, we may be doomed to his fate.

'Perhaps I am overreacting -- indeed I hope that is the case. Regardless, bring my warning to Brogur as quickly as you may.''My cousin has always worried too much. Balin came to a bad end, yes, but this time it will be different. Look at all the victories we have already had, and in so short a time!

'We will be fine, ${PLAYER}. Do not heed Bósi's groundless worrying. He always does this. Moria has cost us so much already; surely the worst is behind us.''Brogur is still in the Chamber of Leadership, off the Twenty-first Hall in Zelem-melek. Bring him my warning as quickly as you may, for these tidings make me uneasy.'A fellowship of dwarves from the Orc-watch set out seeking riches and adventures awaiting them in the Redhorn Lodes. They are mostly miners at heart, and were losing morale being cooped up in the Orc-watch for too long.The dwarves of the Orc-watch are fewer in number these days. Long-standing dwellers left in a fellowship seeking riches and adventure in the Redhorn Lodes below.Speak with Thithrand in Orc-watchThe dwarves of the Orc-watch are fewer in number these days. Long-standing dwellers left in a fellowship seeking riches and adventure in the Redhorn Lodes below.Speak with Ossur in the Orc-watchThe dwarves of the Orc-watch are fewer in number these days. Long-standing dwellers left in a fellowship seeking riches and adventure in the Redhorn Lodes below.Speak with Isólf in the Orc-watchThe road from Orc-watch to the Redhorn Lodes is in need of clearing, as quite a mess was left in the wake of the dwarf-fellowship.Clean up goat droppings on the road (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A few members of the dwarf-fellowship have made camp at the bottom of the stairs toward the Redhorn. They chose not to abandon their goat and waggon.Speak with IngifastThe goat seems to have made a pile of... mess in a corner of the camp. This would be a good place to put the... mess from the road.Drop the 'mess' on the pile near the goatThe dwarves of the camp will be more eager to talk to you now that you've rid yourself of the smell. But, first impressions are first impressions, and they may remember you for that.Return to Ingifast'Some of our finest dwarves are gone! Orc-watch was once a warmer place, but the day has come and gone when our old friends called this place home. It was a sad day when they parted for riches and fame in deeper parts of the Redhorn Lodes.

'You should lament with my fellow dwarves here, who may have more words to say on the matter. I need to get back to my ale....''Indeed, it feels empty here without them. Our friends set out for untold riches, but I have my doubts there is much left with all the Orcs scurrying about down below, not to mention the gredbyg....

'Ingifast hinted to me, before they left, that he believed the fellowship would part ways somewhere along the way. Ingifast wanted to stick together, with all the dangers they might face down there. Some were intent on heading to the southern cavern, while Ingifast and Róarr were interested in the rich ore found around the old mining tracks.''Róarr couldn't stop talking about the adventures he and his fellow dwarves would see down in Redhorn. He is a miner at heart, and he stayed here in Orc-watch for too long. His heart ached for the ore he knew was out there.

'If you see my dear old friend, please send my greetings.''Our old friends have departed and will be missed, but I do worry about them. They left with an ill goat, who was struggling to pull their supply waggon. I think their goat may have left a mess on the road behind them.

'Could you follow the road down toward the Redhorn cavern floor, and make sure the dwarves made out all right? Please take this shovel and help clear the road of the mess they left behind.''Woh, you smell ripe!

'Oh, did you clean up the road behind us? The goat pulling our waggon down from Orc-watch didn't seem too well. Thank you for cleaning up the mess, but please quickly take it over to the pile near the goat. You are looking a little green from the smell, and I might join you soon!''Other than our goat not feeling well, we've run into a lot of Orcs, who are in the way of the ore we came down here for!

'We didn't make it a night before the fellowship out of Orc-watch parted ways. Kúli took Auti with him to search the southern cavern for treasure and ore, and Knakk went with Kafli and headed east, through the Orcs! I worry about those two... I hope they made it wherever they were headed.'There are oddly shaped creatures wandering about to the east and south-east. Knakk swore he spotted a creature that slightly resembled a spider. But being alone way out here, he is trying to stay out of sight on the elevated walkway, and didn't get a good view.The oddly shaped shadows that Knakk referred to can be found to the east and south-east of where he is camped, up on the elevated walkway in the north-east corner of Redhorn Lodes.Defeat globsnaga spiders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Knakk is camped on the elevated walkway in the north-east corner of Redhorn Lodes.

He will be eager to hear what you found while exploring the eastern side of Redhorn Lodes.Return to Knakk'While I'm figuring out this locket, I could use your help with something. I was scouting the area on this elevated walkway, being careful to stay out of sight, and saw an odd shadow moving about down below. It moved like a large spider, but I can't be certain what it was. Its shape wasn't like other spiders around here.

'Could you find out what it was? I got shivers when I saw it crawling about, and I've had nightmares ever since! If you find more than one, can you make sure they don't get close enough to find me here? I need to concentrate on the locket!'You tell Knakk that it seems like some kind of fungus is spreading and infecting the eastern Redhorn.

'The oddly shaped creature I spotted was an infected spider? You say even the ground is infected in that area?! What are we dealing with?

'This is troubling indeed.'The infestation in the eastern Redhorn Lodes seems to be guided and encouraged by something sinister. How could it have spread so quickly from the Foundations of Stone?The cultivator of the mushrooms is likely to be near the centre of the recently corrupted area.

The corruption has spread quickly from the Foundations of Stone, and it is being helped and guided by something.Find what is cultivating the mushroomsThe cultivator of the mushrooms is a terror from deep within the Foundations of Stone.

The corruption has spread quickly from the Foundations of Stone, and it is being helped and guided by one of the nameless. This agent of corruption must have been sent here by something far more sinister....Defeat The Twisted OneKnakk is camped on the elevated walkway in the north-east corner of Redhorn Lodes.

He will be eager to know his assessment was right, and that the cultivator of the mushrooms has been found and defeated.Return to Knakk'The growth we see running rampant in Redhorn Lodes has been present in Foundations of Stone for some time, but has never been seen here before. But something has changed, and it seems as if something brought it here and is helping it grow.

'I need you to help me find the reason why it's spreading here. If we can remove that which helps it grow here, perhaps it will recede, and stay where it came from.''You found it, the one who helped to corrupt such a large area of the Redhorn? I knew there was a sinister hand behind it!

'The one who did it was a faceless creature?! You are far more brave than I, as even your description of it would give me nightmares for weeks, months perhaps. You have my thanks for saving the Redhorn Lodes, a jewel of Moria.'Crystals have been discovered in Moria, bearing marks of the Forgotten Treasury, a sealed vault hiding a substantial amount of wealth. This discovery has piqued the interest of some dwarves who now wish to uncover the vault and save its contents from the pilfering Orcs.Dark defilers can be found within the Forgotten Treasury to the south and south-east of the Deep Descent.

Falgir asked that you find and hunt down the dark defilers watching over the new inhabitants of the Forgotten Treasury.Defeat dark defilers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Deep Descent of Moria can be found north and north-west of the Forgotten Treasury.

You found and slew a fair number of the dark defilers who lurked throughout the interior of the Forgotten Treasury.Talk to Falgir at the Deep Descent'Orcs are bad news -- of that, there is no debate -- but some Orcs are worse than others. Garthar and myself ran into some trouble with a brutal sort of Orc a short time back. We thought nothing of it at the time, as we bested the foe, but they proved powerful... while alone. I cannot imagine that their power would diminish at all in a crowd.

'These Orcs use the very shadows in the mine against you. Be careful when you fight them, but fight them nonetheless, and when you have bested them in the vault, return here to me.

'The Forgotten Treasury lies a fair journey to the south and south-east of our location here in the Deep Descent. Do yourself a favour, my friend: do not travel within the place alone.''Milling about and talking to me does not thin the population of dark defilers. You had best gather friends and make your way to the Forgotten Treasury as soon as you can.''Most excellent work, ${PLAYER}! The fewer Orcs we need to deal with, the better off we are. Khazad-dûm shall be free of this dreadful infestation quickly, at this rate.

'Your efforts will be remembered by Durin's Folk.'Crystals have been discovered in Moria, bearing marks of the Forgotten Treasury, a sealed vault hiding a substantial amount of wealth. This discovery has piqued the interest of some dwarves who now wish to uncover the vault and save its contents from the pilfering Orcs.The Forgotten Treasury is located south and south-east of the Deep Descent.

Garthar asked you to search through the Forgotten Treasury for any sign of the depleted crystals within. Should you find them, he asked you to return them to him.'If you are making your way into the Forgotten Treasury, I would ask that you do me a favour and collect any of the crystals that you find within.

'They have very little value outside of this place, but their qualities were never fully researched, and history states that none who touched the things ever felt that they would be used for good purposes. 

'If you make your way to the Forgotten Treasury, south and south-east of the Deep Descent, collect as many of these as you can and return them to me.''Excellent work, friend. Though it is unclear what purpose these would be used for, I would rather we hold them in safe keeping than allow them in the hands of the Orcs now plaguing Khazad-dûm.'The sluice boxes used by both the Moria and White Hand Orcs to filter the mined ore should not be left functional. Destroying them will help slow down the Orcs' production of weapons and armour.Orcs are using sluice-boxes found at the bases of the elevated tracks throughout the Redhorn Lodes, south-east of the Orc-watch.Destroy sluice-boxes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Orcs are using sluice-boxes found at the bases of the elevated tracks throughout the Redhorn Lodes, south-east of the Orc-watch.CompletedThe Orcs of the Redhorn are busy mining ore and have built sluice-boxes to help them filter the ore from the dirt. Destroying the sluice-boxes throughout the Redhorn Lodes can only help to slow down the Orcs' mining operations.The Orcs may be able to repair these sluice-boxes over time, but hopefully they will have more to deal with before they get a chance to make the repairs. This is the first step in slowing down the Orcs' Redhorn mining operations.A dwarf named Nykr turned back during a rout because he had dropped a pretty puzzle locket, which cost him his life. His friend Gufi, distraught at this nonsensical choice, wants to know why this trinket was worth dying for.There are many dwarves at the Orc-watch.

Róarr was surprised at Nykr's passing. While he did not know why his friend valued this little hand-made locket so highly, he suggested you question the other dwarves at The Orc-Watch.Róarr is in the Orc-Watch.

Róarr will want to hear of the locket's significance, so that he can relay the information back to Gufi.Talk to Róarr at the Orc-watch'Yes, yes; I recognize that locket. It belongs by right to a dwarf by the name of Nykr. How do you come to have it, traveller?'

Roárr is shocked as you tell him all that you know regarding Nykr and the curious piece of jewellery.

'Alas for Nykr! He has fallen. So now you have come with your fell tidings, searching for answers as to why he would risk his life for so small a trinket. That is an excellent question, friend, but I fear I have not the answer. By all means, ask the other dwarves here at the Orc-watch about it.''Nay, I didn't know a Nykr. Why do you ask?''I met Nykr a few times in passing, and once he did indeed bring out this locket from inside his shirt to show me. He always kept it near his heart, so it must have meant a great deal to him. 

'I'm sorry, but I know no more about it.''Nykr? I believe that there is a Nykr staying at the Deep Descent.

'Oh. I see. My apologies.

'Nay, I do not know what that locket means. Best of luck in your search!''I have hoisted quite a few tankards with Nykr -- may the stone bring him peace -- but never have I seen this necklace. 

'My condolences, to you and to his kin.''Finna's handiwork! Explain yourself at once, ${RACE}. How did you come by that? There is only one such example east of the Blue Mountains, and it belongs to my good friend Nykr!

'...Ah. So I suppose I should say belonged. What sad news.

'Finna is one of the premier carvers back home. She is also, or rather was, Nykr's sweetheart. Their betrothal was made formal only shortly before he joined this expedition.

'Many regrets will follow us home; this one will make up part of my burden. When you next speak to Róarr, remind him that Nykr has a cousin, Kuli, who will have to also be told.''You know the story? Tell me!

'How lovely...excuse me a moment, I seem to, ahem, have a stone-chip or some rock-dust in my eye....

'I will send word to Gufi, and he will see that the news makes its way back to Nykr's loved ones, especially Finna Frostbeard.''Let Róarr know that the locket belonged to Nykr's love, Finna.''I would ask the other dwarves around here why Nykr valued this locket so highly.'The tribes of Orcs are collecting a vast amount of metal resources from the Redhorn Lodes. Their forges burn day and night, and they must have Orcs processing that metal into armour and weapons.The tribes of Orc have forges at the hearts of their camps, out among the ruins in the main cavern of the Redhorn Lodes.

The fiercest Orcs tend the forges, to process their collected metals, and fashion armour and weapons for the rest of the rabble.Defeat White Hand Metal-worker (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Moria Metal-worker (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated the Moria and White Hand Metal-workers.CompletedYou notice larger Orcs are tending the forges, and must be wicked metal-workers of the enemy.The Orcs will likely promote others to replace those metal-workers, but for now their pipeline has been slowed. The quality of their work will suffer without those experienced hands.Ingifast worries that the number of Orcs in the Redhorn Lodes could threaten the safety of everyone in Moria, if the two tribes could get along. Thankfully, they are pre-occupied with tearing each other apart than trying to work together. The amount of ore sitting under the Orcs' feet could outfit and sustain an army! There is no love lost between the old Orcs of Moria and the new arrivals of the White Hand.The Orcs can be found among the structures of the Redhorn Lodes, south-east of the Orc-watch. White Hand Orcs inhabit the northern half, and the Moria Orcs are ravaging the southern half.

Ingifast wants you to sow trouble among the Moria and White Hand Orcs in the Redhorn Lodes.Defeat White Hand Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Moria Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ingifast is camped on the stairs near Orc-watch.

Ingifast will be pleased to hear of the defeat of many of the White Hand and Moria Orcs inhabiting the Redhorn Lodes.Return to Ingifast'I'm glad our friends at the Orc-watch sent you to check on us! We didn't heed their warning of trouble down here, and they were right. You came just in time.

'We have always had trouble with the Moria Orcs lurking in dark places. But the White Hand Orcs just march in and take what they want! Moria Orcs once claimed most of the Redhorn Lodes, but they let their guard down and recently the White Hand stormed in and seized control of most of their territory. It's mayhem out there... over our precious ore!

'You should join the fray, and while they're distracted fighting each other, we will have an opportunity to weaken them both. It's time to strike while the iron is hot!''Ha! Wonderful, ${PLAYER}. A grisly task well done. We will have the realm back in hand soon enough!'Auti has noticed an unusually large number of gredbyg who seem to be protecting a doorway.The gredbyg are stirring at the Chittering Hole, south-east of the Orc-watch.

Auti is curious as to why so many gredbyg are gathering outside a door. He asked you to defeat several and see if the rest scatter or remain.Defeat gredbyg at the Chittering Hole (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Auti is at the Orc-watch, north-west of the Chittering Hole.

Auti will be intrigued to hear that the gredbyg returned and defended the doors.Talk to Auti at the Orc-watch'I have recently noticed a strangely large number of gredbyg who seem to consistently linger around a set of doors to the south-east, at a place called the Chittering Hole within the Redhorn Lodes.

'I do not know what they are protecting -- or looking for, if not protecting -- but I believe that if a giant bug wants something, I should prevent it from obtaining whatever that is. Call me queer if you like, but I maintain that this is wisdom.

'How about you go disrupt their gathering a little? Head to the Chittering Hole, south-east of here, and defeat some of them. Hopefully, they will retreat.''I take it this means there are fewer gredbyg around those doors? Excellent! But they kept returning you say? I do wonder what they want there....''I have never heard of bugs defending a door before...perhaps a nest or food, but never a door.'Auti has noticed an unusually large number of grodbog-beetles skittering around the hive. They seem to be interested in metal deposits found throughout the hive.The grodbog-beetles are found throughout the grodbog hive, but seem to collect around the various ore deposits.

Auti is curious as to why so many grodbog-beetles are gathering at the deposits. He asked you to defeat several and see if the rest scatter or remain.Defeat grodbog-beetles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Auti is camped on the northern edge of the enormous grodbog hive. 

You should return to Auti with news of your success.Return to Auti'I noticed a number of grodbog-beetles who seem to come and go from specific parts of the hive. Have you noticed that their shells seem to shimmer and shine, like finely polished ingots of Moria copper? 

'I wonder if they are attracted to the ore deposits in this tunnel, and if they have been digesting some of it? That might be why their shells are so hard!

'Why don't you find some of those beetles and dispatch them? If we can reclaim some of the metals they have ingested, all the better! In the worst case, you are simply making the hive a slightly safer place.''I take it this means there are fewer gredbyg around those ore deposits? Excellent! But they kept returning you say? I do wonder if we can ever make this area safe to mine....'The gredbyg are raising their young in the remains of an ancient dwarf-garrison. It was the home to the dwarves of old that mined mithril out of Redhorn Lodes.Grodbog-young may be found in the old barracks, inside the ancient dwarf-home of Nud-heden. 

You should destroy the grodbog-young in the ancient garrison of Nud-heden.Destroy the grodbog-larvae (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Kúli is encamped with Auti just north of the grodbog hive.

They will be pleased to know the grodbog-larvae are destroyed, and Nud-heden may some day be home to dwarves once more.Return to KúliGredbyg living where dwarves once did is unacceptable. This is an honoured site of the ancient miners of mithril! The grodbog-larvae should be destroyed in honour of the dwarves of old who called this place home.'The thought of gredbyg raising their young where dwarves of legend once slept sickens me. That was once a warm home to those honourable dwarves. They found the mithril that many treasures of Moria are made from!'Two members of the dwarf-fellowship out of Orc-watch left for the southern cavern of the Redhorn Lodes. They didn't expect this cavern to house such an impressive and imposing grodbog hive that may be more dangerous than the Orcs' mining operations.Claw Hollow, The Chittering Hole, and The Great Hive have untapped deposits which seem to attract the gredbyg.

The gredbyg have grown out of control in the south of the Redhorn Lodes. Their hive is enormous, and was built upon many untapped deposits of ore. It is likely this tunnel could be the best location for mithril prospecting in all of Moria!Kúli and Auti are camped on the northern edge of the enormous grodbog hive. 

Kúli is interested in determining good mining locations within the hive. He is hoping that some day the grodbog hive won't be as dangerous, and that mining those places will be possible.Return to Kúli'Auti and I came here despite our fellows' requests for us to stay, and I feel bad we left them behind. But, we didn't agree the area those Orcs were mining was the richest source of ore in the Redhorn. We thought the untapped veins in this cavern were more likely to produce mithril ore. I hope we find what we seek, or we may never hear the end of it!

'I need you to help me scout the hive for good prospecting locations. Knowing those locations will help, if we can someday clear the path through this hive.''You found some promising locations! I'm excited to begin our search, but we're just miners and wouldn't stand a chance out there, like you can. I'll have to keep these locations on record. If we can discover a way to reduce the size of this immense hive those gredbyg have built, we may some day get started!'The ancient garrison of Nud-heden is infested by gredbyg. The grodbog hive extends even into this old home, which is legendary among the dwarves.Gredbyg can be found within Nud-heden, found inside the grodbog hive, south of where Kúli and Auti are camped.

You should defeat the gredbyg at Nud-heden.Defeat gredbyg in Nud-heden (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Auti is camped with Kúli, just north of the grodbog hive.

Auti will be pleased to hear that the gredbyg have been defeated and Nud-heden is another step closer to again being a home of dwarves.Return to AutiThe gredbyg have converted Nud-heden into an extension of their hive. This was once an ancient home of dwarves... and should be again. While inside, defeat any gredbyg you find there.'Dead, you say? Are you sure?

'Well, that's good. Kúli and I will pack up soon and make our way there. It will be up to us to ensure those blasted insects do not try to occupy that ancient home of dwarves ever again!'The aggressive growth in the eastern Redhorn Lodes is threatening to swallow the entire area in a lichen-like covering and infest anything that lives there.The eastern edge of the Redhorn Lodes is infested with an aggressively growing fungus.Destroy fungal mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Knakk is camped on the elevated walkway in the north-east corner of the Redhorn Lodes.Return to Knakk'After you pushed back the spiders near the end of the path down to the infected area, I scouted the ground down there. Did you notice the surface is being corrupted and transformed into a lichen-like crust on the dirt floor? I have never seen anything like it.

'These mushrooms seem to be involved somehow... head back down there and destroy a few of them, and we'll see if their removal can allow the area to recover to its former state.''I think I'm almost done figuring out the puzzle in this locket.'

You explain how the mushrooms were spewing spores at an alarming rate and destroying the fungus did not affect the lichen-covered areas.

'Those mushrooms have never grown here, and they came so quickly. It shows signs of tending from a gentle hand, and it looks as if something helped to bring it here. Our only hope is that by removing the mushrooms the spreading will stop.'You have discovered the shoulder and arm piece of the Helgi Goblinbane statue from the Chamber of the Cross-roads.The statue-piece can be found in the middle of the Redhorn Lodes.

You should bring the statue-piece to someone in the Redhorn Lodes.Recover the statue-pieceKnakk is at a hidden camp, located on the elevated walkway in the north-east corner of the Redhorn Lodes.

You should bring the statue-piece to Knakk, who can send it along to Uxi in the Chamber of the Cross-roads to be reassembled.Bring the statue-piece to KnakkYou have discovered the shoulder and arm piece of the Helgi Goblinbane statue from the Chamber of the Cross-roads.

The goblins of Durin's Way destroyed the statue, and its parts can be found scattered throughout Moria. A dwarf named Uxi endeavours to reassemble this statue.'Ah, this is an interesting artifact. It appears to be the shoulder and arm of the Helgi Goblinbane statue that Uxi wishes to reassemble. It is incredible that this piece has travelled so far!

'I will have this sent to Uxi at once. He will be glad to see it safe.'The site of Kúli and Auti's camp is close to the grodbog-hive, and they are regretting setting up camp in such a dangerous location.The ancient miners' garrison is somewhere inside the grodbog-hive, south of the camp-site of Kúli and Auti.

Kúli believes the ancient miners' garrison is located in the Redhorn Lodes from an ancient dwarf-text he read in the locket. Auti and Kúli came to this cavern for mithril, but instead find themselves searching for a legend!Find the ancient miners' garrison, Nud-hedenThe ancient garrison has been found, but it has been ages since dwarves set foot inside it.

Kúli will be excited to find out the garrison does exist and is not just legend. He will want to know how dangerous the place has become and what state the legendary place is in.Kúli is camped with Auti, just north of the grodbog-hive.

Kúli will be excited to hear the ancient garrison exists as he suspected. However, it may not be the safe solution he was hoping it would be.Return to Kúli'Our camp is in a dangerous location, being so close to the enormous hive. But if we go further north, we are sure to be overrun by Orcs.

