'I must ask you to sally forth once more into Nardur-stazg and defeat the foe there. If you should find any more belongings stolen from the fallen Elves, please bring those to me as well, so that they can be returned to the families of the lost.

'Nardur-stazg lies to the north-west of here. Anything you bring back will be greatly appreciated.''The Galadhrim will be grateful for any assistance to us that you can grant against the Orcs of Moria. Any stolen belongings you can recover for us would be much appreciated as well.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}. The Orcs have suffered another defeat at your hands, and the Galadhrim will benefit from the recovery of their kinsmen's belongings.'The Dúnedain have asked many things of you. Complete all of these tasks, and you will be rewarded greatly.Complete all of the Carn Dûm DeedsComplete all of the Urugarth DeedsComplete all of the Barad Gúlaran DeedsComplete An Ancient ThreatComplete Thorog DefeatedThe Iron Garrison seeks to reclaim the Black Pit of Moria as their own, but many believe their expedition into the ancient halls may be doomed. Do all you can to help them, and you will be named the Saviour of Khazad-dûm.Complete The Watcher of MoriaComplete The Grand StairComplete Mastery Over FearComplete Persevering Against the BlightComplete Marshal of the Burning DepthsDefeat Angmarim at the Forges of Tham Mírdain.Defeat Angmarim at the Forges of Tham MírdainDefeat many Angmarim at the Forges of Tham Mírdain.Defeat many Angmarim at the Forges of Tham Mírdain.<rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers.</rgb><rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers</rgb>Complete Volume II, Book 8: Return to AzanarukârDulgurz has terrorized the dwarves of the Dimrill Dale since they first set camp.Dulgurz is at Athmadhul, south-east of Mekhem-bizru.

Ótam told you of a particulary dangerous Warg-rider, Dulgurz, who must no longer be allowed to roam the Dimrill Dale.Defeat DulgurzÓtam is at Mekhem-bizru, north-west of Athmadhul.

Ótam will want to hear of the defeat of Dulgurz.Talk to Ótam at Mekhem-bizru'We need your help, ${PLAYER}. There is a Warg-rider that comes at night, just as the sun sets. We can do naught to stop him, no trap has worked. He is slowly killing us off one-by-one.

'I think Alrekur feathered him with an arrow the last time he came, because we have not seen him for a couple of days, but I have no doubt his fury is growing while his Warg's wounds heal.

'Dulgurz, the Warg-rider, must be stopped. We cannot keep living this way, never knowing when the Warg-rider will attack next. Head south-east, to Athmadhul, he should be recovering there. But be wary, the arrow only nicked him: do not underestimate him.''Finally, a night where I can sleep sound, without the fear of hearing those feet come pounding towards me. I thank you.''Please. If Dulgurz comes at us again, I am sure it will be our last.'Slaughter the foe at Lugazag and recover it for the glory of Angmar, it is why you are here after all!Slaughter the foes of Angmar and reclaim LugazagSlaughter the foe at the Grimwood Lumber Camp and recover it for the glory of Angmar, it is why you are here after all!Slaughter the foes of Angmar and reclaim the Grimwood Lumber CampSlaughter the foe at the Isendeep Mine and recover it for the glory of Angmar, it is why you are here after all!Slaughter the foes of Angmar and reclaim the Isendeep MineSlaughter the foe at Tírith Rhaw and recover it for the glory of Angmar. It is why you are here, after all!Slaughter the foes of Angmar and reclaim Tírith RhawSlaughter the foe at Tol Ascarnen and recover it for the glory of Angmar, it is why you are here after all!Slaughter the foes of Angmar and reclaim Tol AscarnenCúvroval has received orders that every flet must be on the look-out for a sighting of one of the magnificent Great Eagles who hail from the northern Misty Mountains.Talan Brethil is to the south-east of Talan Revail, along the banks of the Anduin. Talan Gwilith and Talan Fanuidhol are well west and a bit south of Talan Revail, along the border of Fanuidhol where the Orcs have recently emerged from Moria.

Cúvroval wants you to go to three of the telain and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> into the distance from atop all three of them to search for a Great Eagle in the sky. She was not told why this look-out had to be kept, only that it was essential.Cúvroval is at Talan Revail, north-east of Cerin Amroth.

You should return to Cúvroval to report that empty skies were all you saw from the telain.Talk to Cúvroval at Talan Revail'Whence hail you and wherefore come you here, brave ${RACE}? To have come this far into the Golden Wood, you must have earned the trust of the archers who guard our borders. That is why I feel able to ask you for aid: I have orders that I fear I cannot fulfil alone. Will you help?

'Have you ever sighted one of the majestic Great Eagles who hail from eyries north of the High Pass in the Misty Mountains? Well, my orders are to keep a stringent look-out for one of them -- it is not my privilege to know why. Will you go to the nearby Telain -- Talan being our word for the platforms in the trees that you call flets -- and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> for them?

'Talan Brethil is south-east of here, along the banks of the Anduin. Talan Gwilith and Talan Fanuidhol are well west and a bit south of here, along the border of Fanuidhol, where the Orcs have recently emerged from Moria.''Have you seen the Great Eagles of the Misty Mountains? They are a sight to behold.''Nothing? You are certain? Ah well.

'The truth is, I have no idea why any of the Great Eagles should be paying us a visit at this time anyway.'Rilhathel is determined to sing songs of your efforts in defence of Lórien and has issued you a challenge in addition to simply repelling the Orcs: defeating one of the two rearward fighters that appear on the field of battle.Rilhathel has asked you to not only successfully win the battle for Lórien, but to also defeat Narshpug or Grakâmâb before they leave the field of battle.Defeat Narshpug or Grakâmâb before they leave the field, and still win the battle.Rilhathel has asked you to not only successfully win the battle for Lórien, but to also defeat Narshpug or Grakâmâb before they leave the field of battle.Successfully complete the Battle of LórienRilhathel is at Caras Galadhon, waiting to hear if you successfully completed his challenge.Talk to Rilhathel at Caras Galadhon'It is not enough simply to repel the Orcs, ${PLAYER}. If you want the minstrels to sing of your deeds, you need to do more than merely win the battle, friend ${CLASS}. You need to do something truly worthy of song.

'As you continue to fight for Lórien, I will devise additional challenges that might rightly live on in song; successfully complete one of my challenges, and I will be sure to give you a reward in addition to what Belvagor will give you.

'Two fearsome foes were seen lurking in the back ranks during the last battle: Narshpug, a foul-tempered Uruk, and Grakâmâb, a mighty troll. They were not brought to the front of the battle, which makes me think they are being saved for some purpose; at some point they left the field of battle. If you are able to defeat one of them before they leave and are also able to successfully win the battle, you will have completed my challenge to you.''Narshpug and Grakâmâb were seen in the rear ranks of the last battle, and at some point they left the field. If you can defeat one of them before they leave and still manage to win the battle, you will have completed my challenge.''Very good, ${PLAYER}! You have completed my challenge and may have hindered whatever plans the Orcs of Moria were forming around their mightiest rearward fighters!

'Take this reward with my thanks, and the knowledge that your victory was truly song-worthy!'Rilhathel is determined to sing songs of your efforts in defence of Lórien, and has issued you a challenge in addition to simply repelling the Orcs: to keep Belvagor from sustaining any serious wounds.Rilhathel has asked you to not only successfully win the battle for Lórien, but to also prevent Belvagor from sustaining any serious wounds.Prevent Belvagor from receiving any serious damage and still win the battle.Rilhathel has asked you to not only successfully win the battle for Lórien, but to also prevent Belvagor from sustaining any serious wounds.Successfully complete the Battle of LórienRilhathel is at Caras Galadhon, waiting to hear if you successfully completed his challenge.Talk to Rilhathel at Caras Galadhon'It is not enough simply to repel the Orcs, ${PLAYER}. If you want the minstrels to sing of your deeds, you need to do more than merely win the battle, friend ${CLASS}. You need to do something truly worthy of song.

'As you continue to fight for Lórien, I will devise additional challenges that might rightly live on in song; successfully complete one of my challenges, and I will be sure to give you a reward in addition to what Belvagor will give you.

'My sister Limhendeth has always been fond of Belvagor and expressed to me her concern that he might be wounded during the upcoming battles with the Orcs. My challenge to you is this: do not allow Belvagor to be so much as scratched. He is an Elf brave and true and will demand to fight at one of the passes, but for my sister's sake, ensure that he is not wounded. You must win the battle without Belvagor sustaining any serious injury to complete my challenge.''My sister Limhendeth is most fond of Belvagor and would be distraught if he were to sustain any wounds. If you can prevent him from receiving any serious injury and still manage to win the battle, you will have completed my challenge.''Very good, ${PLAYER}! You have completed my challenge, and my sister Limhendeth will be delighted to hear that her beloved Belvagor suffered no injury!

'Take this reward with my thanks, and the knowledge that your victory was truly song-worthy!'Rilhathel is determined to sing songs of your efforts in defence of Lórien, and has issued you a challenge in addition to simply repelling the Orcs: to win the battle in thirty minutes!Rilhathel has asked you to not only successfully win the battle for Lórien, but to do it quickly: within thirty minutes!Win the Battle of Lórien in under thirty minutesRilhathel has asked you to not only successfully win the battle for Lórien, but to do it quickly: within thirty minutes!Successfully complete the Battle of Lórien within thirty minutesWin the battle within thirty minutesRilhathel is at Caras Galadhon, waiting to hear if you successfully completed his challenge.Talk to Rilhathel at Caras Galadhon'It is not enough simply to repel the Orcs, ${PLAYER}. If you want the minstrels to sing of your deeds, you need to do more than merely win the battle, friend ${CLASS}. You need to do something truly worthy of song.

'We need to send a message to the Orcs, ${PLAYER}. We must show them that they are no match for the defenders of Lothlórien, and what better way to show them this than to repel them with all haste?

'If you are able to successfully win the battle in less than thirty minutes, you will have completed my challenge.''Let us send the Orcs a message, ${PLAYER}, by sending them back to Moria in disgrace! If you can successfully win the battle in less than thirty minutes, you will have completed my challenge.''Very good, ${PLAYER}! You have completed my challenge, and the Orcs must now know how overmatched they are!

'Take this reward with my thanks, and the knowledge that your victory was truly song-worthy!'Thavronwë, a statue-carver currently at Cerin Amroth in search of inspiration, would like you to help him create a stockpile of stone and wood for him and his fellow carvers.Thavronwë has asked that you gather stone and wood for him.

There is a high concentration of birch east and north of Cerin Amroth, in the grove of Cerin Brethil. Stone can be found to the east, in Gworast on the banks of the Anduin.Gather birch-wood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather stone (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thavronwë is atop the flet in Cerin Amroth.

You should return to Thavronwë and give him the raw materials you fetched for him.'May your path be ever star-lit, friend. Welcome to Cerin Amroth. Do you not find the beauty of this place inspiring? Your pardon: I am Thavronwë, a statue-carver from Caras Galadhon. I am here to listen to the trees and try to translate their lovely whispers into shapes that will last. 

'Have you time to spare? I must build up a stockpile of wood and stone to work with. If you wish to help, you might fetch me some.

'Head east into the forest and then north-east aways to the grove of Cerin Brethil. There you will find birch wood which can be gathered. Workable stone can be readily found in Gworast, well to the east of here on the banks of the Anduin.''My fellow artisans and I need more wood and stone for our projects...have you found enough for our work?''Welcome back!

'Ah, these will do, ${PLAYER}; these will do very nicely. My heartiest thanks.

'In fact -- turn your face a little towards the light, there...hold still. Perhaps I have found my inspiration after all!'Mallendathor wishes you to sing to several Malbrethil-saplings again, to aid in their healthy development, and bring her back some Malbrethril-seeds.Orthir can be found a short distance west of Cerin Amroth, near the path.

Mallendathor wishes you to speak to Orthir, the Scholar of the Trees, to see if he knows what might heal the saplings' malady.Talk to OrthirThe Malbrethil-saplings can be found around Cerin Brethil in Taur Hith, to the north-east of Cerin Amroth.

Orthir believes that the saplings, deprived of the everyday music of the now-wary forest, might be revived if you were to sing to them.The Malbrethil-saplings can be found around Cerin Brethil in Taur Hith, to the north-east of Cerin Amroth.

Orthir believes that the saplings, deprived of the everyday music of the now-wary forest, might be revived if you were to sing to them.The Malbrethil-saplings can be found around Cerin Brethil in Taur Hith, to the north-east of Cerin Amroth.

Orthir believes that the saplings, deprived of the everyday music of the now-wary forest, might be revived if you were to sing to them.The Malbrethil-saplings can be found around Cerin Brethil in Taur Hith, to the north-east of Cerin Amroth.

Orthir believes that the saplings, deprived of the everyday music of the now-wary forest, might be revived if you were to sing to them.Mallendathor has asked you to gather Malbrethil-seeds from in the area of Taur Hith, to the north-east of Cerin Amroth. The seeds are difficult to find, but are frequently collected by the hungry shrews that scamper about the forest there.Collect Malbrethil-seeds (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sendiel is in Gelirdor, south-west of the Malbrethil-trees, east of Cerin Amroth.

Orthir instructed you to tell Sendiel of your success.'If you are willing to be of further aid, ${PLAYER}, I would ask that you seek out the counsel of Orthir, a Scholar of the Trees who has made the woods of Lothlórien, and particularly the majestic trees in and around Cerin Amroth, his particular study. Describe to him the symptoms you found on the Malbrethil-saplings and follow his advice. You will find him not far west of here, near the path.''Speak with Orthir, my friend... he still needs your assistance.''Mae govannen. You were sent from Mallendathor? Very well, what assistance do you need?

'Ah! The Melbrethil! The first Malbrethil-tree was a gift from those who dwell beyond the Sea. We prize them so because they listen to the harmonies of Middle-earth itself and breathe them out to their surroundings. But you say they are suffering? This is no small matter....

'I fear that since our lands have been...  visited... by so very many of the friends and allies of Gandalf the Grey and Elrond Halfelven, the forest and its beasts have been quieter--frightened, perhaps. I think that you might remedy this were you to <rgb=#FF0000>sing</rgb> to the saplings. Any song will do. Perhaps hearing of your homeland will strengthen them. When you have sung to them, seek out Sendiel, another warden of the woods, whom I last saw in a clearing in Gelirdor, south-west of the hill of the First Malbrethil.''Have you spoken yet with Orthir? If so, I would do as he instructed you.''Raise your voice in song, ${PLAYER}... only then will the Melbrethil heal.''My thanks to you again, ${PLAYER}.

'If we are to keep this forest lush and healthy despite the depredations of time and darkness, we must work tirelessly at its renewal.''Have you collected fresh seeds for Sendiel as I have asked? I am sure the shrews have stolen many.''Song may liven the branches of the Melbrethil for a short time, but fresh trees will soon need to be planted.'In the Sixteenth Hall, defilers and other Orcs, sworn to some dark power, poison their brethren with a fungal disease. The source of this evil lies far beneath the surface of this stronghold, once inhabited by the dwarves. The dwarves in Moria wish only to drive the Orcs and the evil of the Sixteenth Hall away from their former realm.The Lost One is below the Sixteenth Hall, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall.

You have been challenged to prove your great worth by defeating the Lost One without making the battle easier by killing his summoned gredbyg.Defeat the Lost OneThe dwarf Sigurd can be found in the Twenty-first Hall, north-west of the Sixteenth Hall.

Sigurd will be very impressed to hear that you met his challenge of defeating the Lost One while the gredbyg harried your efforts all the while.Return to Sigurd at the Twenty-first Hall'Rumours abound that you are of a tougher mould than most, ${CLASS}. I will admit that you look doughty enough, and your eyes are fiercer than many I see. Perhaps you would hold up against a most difficult foe that threatens our cause.

'Beneath the Sixteenth Hall there is a terrible beast known as the Lost One. It summons wretched gredbyg to its aid when it attacks, and they can be quite the hindrance to any that oppose their master. To defeat the Lost One after the threat of gredbyg has been removed would be but a pittance to so mighty a one as yourself.

'Nay, to earn my respect and rise to the challenge I give you, you will take down the Lost One without hurting the gredbyg, however much they harry you. I would advise you to bring friends on this venture if you wish to return in victory.''I am tremendously impressed! You have done credit to your fame, great credit indeed. Something troubles me still, though. By your description of this foe, it sounds to me like evil will not be held at bay for long in the Sixteenth Hall.

'Do you think the Lost One is gone forever? You saw him bested, I know, but my heart tells me that he will be back before long. A creature so fey does not often fall after one defeat. Mark my words -- there will be trouble again soon....''Have you defeated the Lost One as I asked? It is a challenge, to be sure, but one that must be faced!'In the Sixteenth Hall, defilers and other Orcs, sworn to some dark power, poison their brethren with a fungal disease. The source of this evil lies far beneath the surface of this stronghold, once inhabited by the dwarves. The dwarves in Moria wish only to drive the Orcs and the evil of the Sixteenth Hall away from their former realm.Globsnaga spread their poison in the Sixteenth Hall, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall whence you came.

You have been asked to slay as many Globsnaga as you can to ensure the safety of the dwarves in Moria.Defeat enemies in the Sixteenth Hall (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Geirr is in the Twenty-first Hall, north-west of the Sixteenth Hall.

You should return to Geirr and tell him of your success against the Globsnaga, for he wishes to see if you are as sturdy a warrior as rumour has assured.Return to Geirr at the Twenty-first Hall'Word of your renown has spread far and wide throughout these long tunnels, ${CLASS}. Aye, don't be modest -- I can see in your eyes the bright look of a valiant adventurer. I would know your ilk even without reinforcement from the rumours.

'Are you up for a small challenge? South-east of here lies the Sixteenth Hall, utterly overrun with Orcs suffering from some plague. Healthy Orcs are terrible enough, but we cannot abide these new miscreants as they poison the halls of our fathers. They are called Globsnaga.

'An adventurer such as yourself would look forward to a little dare such as the one I am about to propose: travel to the Sixteenth Hall and slay many enemies there. The more Globsnaga you defeat, the better it will be for all the good folk of Moria and beyond.''I must be honest: I was not sure that you would be up for this challenge today. But I am glad to have been wrong on this occasion! Forgive me for not thinking better of you, ${PLAYER}, for the rumours of your courage were more than true. 

'Here is a token of my appreciation. I hope to see more of you in the following days, for you are quite the asset to our endeavours here.''Drive the Globsnaga from our halls!'In the Sixteenth Hall, defilers and other Orcs, sworn to some dark power, poison their brethren with a fungal disease. The source of this evil lies far beneath the surface of this stronghold, once inhabited by the dwarves. The dwarves in Moria wish only to drive the Orcs and the evil of the Sixteenth Hall away from their former realm.The Lost One is below the Sixteenth Hall, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall where Eilíefr stands.

You have been challenged to prove your great worth by defeating the Lost One and returning with its hide as a trophy of your victory.'So you are the mighty ${CLASS} of whom everyone has spoken so well! Word of your valour and courage has reached my ears, but seeing as I am a disbelieving sort, I would like to see if you are made for the sort of challenge I have to offer you.

'If you are truly all that they say you are, I have a challenge for you: the Lost One of the Sixteenth Hall must be defeated, and I want to see evidence of your actions! I would have you best it in battle and return here with its hide so that I can see what you have done.

'This will not be an easy task, nor is it impossible. Prove yourself in this challenge of mine, and you will receive a rich reward. Oh, and...there is no shame in bringing friends with you on this venture.''A fine trophy! This Lost being was too dangerous to be allowed to live, and I am glad to hear that it is gone once and for all.'

Eilíefr seems suddenly startled, and nearly drops the hide.

'The Lost One's flesh crawls, ${PLAYER}, even though its owner is no more. I wonder if the Lost One is truly dead....'No living Man, Elf, halfling, or dwarf will want to set foot in Skûmfil, grave of the Orcs, feeding and breeding ground of vile kergrim and slavering gredbyg. There the Orcs pile their dead, and there disgusting creatures feast on rotten waste.Brúmbereth grows strong and raises many spawn among the corpses of Orcs in Skûmfil, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall where Elíefr stands.

Elíefr has challenged you to defeat Brúmbereth in Skûmfil as proof of your worth as a warrior. Few are wilful enough to withstand the reek of that horrific place.'So you are the mighty ${CLASS} of whom everyone has spoken so well! Word of your valour and courage has reached my ears, but seeing as I am a disbelieving sort, I would like to see if you are made for the sort of challenge I have to offer you. 

'Many great and wicked things nest in the rot of Skûmfil, ${PLAYER}. One such great creature is the grodbog-queen, Brúmbereth. The corpses of the Orcs nourish her young, and she grows fat and strong on the rancid flesh of our enemies.

'If you are truly all that they say you are, you will go to Skûmfil and face Brúmbereth, matron of evil. I want you to bring me her carapace as proof that she is dead. This will not be an easy task, but it is not impossible. There is no shame in bringing friends with you on this venture.''A fine trophy! Brúmbereth was too dangerous to be allowed to live, and I am glad to hear that she is gone once and for all.'

Eilíefr then examines the hide with a scrutinising eye. He sighs when he addresses you this time.

'This carapace is not the actual hide of Brúmbereth, ${PLAYER}, but a mere layer of her skeleton. I wonder if the the grodbog-queen is truly dead....'No living Man, Elf, halfling, or dwarf will want to set foot in Skûmfil, grave of the Orcs, feeding and breeding ground of vile kergrim and slavering gredbyg. There the Orcs pile their dead, and there disgusting creatures feast on rotten waste.Grimreaver grows strong and leads many minions among the corpses of Orcs in Skûmfil, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall where Elíefr stands.

