'You may wish to visit the glades of Egladil during your stay. The Mistress of the Feast is known throughout Lothlórien for the grand banquets she prepares in honour of special occasions and important guests.''Welcome to Imlad Lalaith, ${PLAYER}! Here we prepare the great banquets of Lothlórien whenever momentous occasion arises.

'Even now, we are making ready to honour a group of important guests who have travelled a long and weary road to reach us, but we are short-handed, as many have taken up the blade and bow to guard the borders of Lothlórien in this darkening age.

'Perhaps you could aid us?'The Galadhrim of Lothlórien feel the pressing of danger upon their borders from many sides, but never so violently as the recent attacks from Moria since the arrival of a secret company in the Golden Wood. Haldir, a marchwarden of the Nimrodel, wishes to secure a friendship with the dwarves of Khazad-dûm so that the evils of Moria may be kept at bay, for the safety of both lands.Hrólfur, an important overseer, can likely be found in the Chamber of the Cross-roads in Moria, in Durin's Way.

Haldir has pressed you to bring an important message from Celeborn to Hrólfur after a previous attempt to make contact was spurned by the proud dwarf.Hrólfur is in the Chamber of the Cross-roads, in Durin's Way.

You should speak with Hrólfur and accept his two charges: the conquering of Nalâ-dûm, where the water-wheels are, and Lumul-nar, the Hall of Mirrors.Help the dwarves regain control of Lumul-narHelp the dwarves regain control of Nalâ-dûmHaldir awaits news of your good deeds towards the dwarves upon his flet near the River Nimrodel, on the western border of Lothlórien.

You should seek Haldir and inform him of all that passed while you assisted the dwarves.Talk to Haldir at Talan Haldir'We do not often welcome strangers within our borders, ${PLAYER}, but here we shall make an exception for you. Lady Galadriel is aware that you are recently come from Moria, where you have done much to help secure the dwarves' footing there. She bade me not to hinder your crossing of the Nimrodel. You are welcome to take refuge in our talan and share of our provisions, for I must speak with you at length on a serious matter.

'You may have noticed that Orcs are pressing closer and closer to Lothlórien, fairest of Elven-homes, and that there are plots and stirrings in Moria to set all the Free Peoples in this area ill at ease. It is therefore crucial that the dwarves hold fast in their newly reclaimed home; their coming was an unforeseen blessing at a most fortuitous time! Our enemies' eyes are divided with stout warriors and builders shaking the walls of their dark haven.

'But the dwarves are not secure. They need our help. In the Chamber of the Cross-roads, you will find an important dwarf who goes by the name of Hrólfur. He oversees much of what the expedition endeavours to accomplish. He did not wish to speak to our messenger, but you have reputation as a hero among the Iron Garrison. He will listen to you! Hasten to him if you will, and bring him this note. I want you to help the dwarves as a token of good will from the Galadhrim.''Time is pressing, ${PLAYER}. Make haste! Hrólfur is in the Chamber of the Cross-roads in Moria. I only hope that you will find him more friendly than our first messenger did. The letter explains all -- if he is rational, he will heed our words.''So you come from the land of the meddling Elves, do you? I am sorry you took the trouble of coming all this way for naught! I know what they seek: for their backs to be shielded by the blood of the dwarves while they wander ever in peace in their haunted eaves.'

'I will read this message, but... what? Indeed, I am a great fool. Lord Celeborn speaks of Moria flooded with rays of light and the creaking of the water-wheels. Such ideas were lost to us when Durin fell.

'I now have two great tasks to achieve. Lumul-nar and Nalâ-dûm, as Celeborn describes from his long memory, were great halls from whence the light and water flooded Khazad-dûm. They now lie dormant, surely under the sway of evil, as we find with all the once-fair halls of our great kingdom. Can you help us to restore them, ${PLAYER}? Please find some information on how this can be done!''I am relieved beyond all of my expectations that you have done so well in your task, ${PLAYER}. But do not wander away just yet -- there is someone I would have you seek.

'Please stay a moment more and hear what I have to say.'Drive evil from the Grand Stair.Complete Landings of the Grand StairComplete Letters to IgashComplete Villains of the Grand Stair (Part 2)Complete Tamers of the Vile WargsComplete Followers of IgashComplete Brutes of the Grand StairComplete Beasts of the Grand StairComplete Villains of the Grand Stair (Part 1)Once a great and beautiful cross-roads of Moria, the Grand Stair is a vile haven of many of the gathered tribes of Orcs. Igash, regarded as a leader by all of the varied groups, gathers tribute from every tribe to give to Mazog, the mightiest and greatest of Orcs in Khazad-dûm. The dwarves wish to unseat Igash's Orcs from this Grand Stair and stop Mazog from receiving valuable and unsavoury gifts.Many tribes of Orcs prowl the Grand Stair, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall.

Geirr has asked you to defeat Igash in the Grand Stair without defeating any other members of the Moria Orc tribes. In doing so, you will assist in efforts to reclaim that cross-roads for the dwarves, and prove your great worth to Sigurd.The dwarf Sigurd can be found in the Twenty-first Hall, north-west of the Grand Stair.

You have defeated Igash without defeating any other members of the Moria Orc tribes; Sigurd is waiting to hear of your foray.Return to Sigurd at the Twenty-first Hall'Rumours abound that you are of a tougher mould than most, ${CLASS}. I will admit that you look doughty enough, and your eyes are fiercer than many I see. Perhaps you would hold up against a most difficult foe that threatens our cause.

'We know that Igash sits at the top of the Grand Stair, collecting tribute from the other tribes of Moria Orcs. The White-hand Orcs, however, seem to give him no loyalty whatsoever.

'And yet, merely to confront Igash will be but a pittance to so mighty a ${CLASS} as yourself. If you can defeat him without harming the other Orc leaders except those of the White Hand, you will truly earn my respect. You cannot slay the Devoted, either. I would advise you to bring such friends as you can trust for this venture.''Igash is one of Mazog's most terrible captains... he must be defeated with due haste!''I am tremendously impressed! You have done credit to your fame, great credit indeed. Something troubles me still, though. By your description of this foe, it sounds to me like evil will not be held at bay for long in the Grand Stair.

'Do you think Igash is gone forever? You saw him bested, I know, but my heart tells me that he will be back before long. A creature so twisted and wicked does not often fall after one defeat. Mark my words -- there will be trouble again soon....'Once a great and beautiful cross-roads of Moria, the Grand Stair is a vile haven of many of the gathered tribes of Orcs. Igash, regarded as a leader by all of the varied groups, gathers tribute from every tribe to give to Mazog, the mightiest and greatest of Orcs in Khazad-dûm. The dwarves wish to unseat Igash's Orcs from this Grand Stair and stop Mazog from receiving valuable and unsavoury gifts.Igash can be found atop the Grand Stair, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall where Eilífr holds his post.

Eilífr has asked you to defeat Igash and collect his lash to assist in efforts to reclaim that cross-roads for the dwarves. He wishes to see the lash as proof of your deed.'So you are the mighty ${CLASS} of whom everyone has spoken so well! Word of your valour and courage has reached my ears, but seeing as I am a disbelieving sort, I would like to see if you are made for the sort of challenge I have to offer you.

'If you are truly all that they say you are, I have a challenge for you: Igash must be defeated upon his throne in the Grand Stair, and I want to see evidence of your actions! I would have you defeat him in battle and return here with his lash so that I can see what you have done.

'This will not be an easy task, nor is it impossible. Prove yourself in this challenge of mine, and you will receive a rich reward. Oh, and... there is no shame in bringing friends with you on this venture.''You have done well to collect this lash, friend, and I am very impressed. I did not think you had the salt to confront Igash! But I am troubled; though the Orcs fall, ever they return in full force. I hope Igash is gone for good....'Once a great and beautiful cross-roads of Moria, the Grand Stair is a vile haven of many of the gathered tribes of Orcs. Igash, regarded as a leader by all of the varied groups, gathers tribute from every tribe to give to Mazog, the mightiest and greatest of Orcs in Khazad-dûm. The dwarves wish to unseat Igash's Orcs from this Grand Stair and stop Mazog from receiving valuable and unsavoury gifts.Many tribes of Orcs prowl the Grand Stair, their leaders among them, found south-east of the Twenty-first Hall.

Eilífr has asked you to defeat Orc leaders in the Grand Stair to assist in efforts to reclaim that cross-roads for the dwarves. He wishes to see their Marks as proof of your deed.'So you are the mighty ${CLASS} of whom everyone has spoken so well! Word of your valour and courage has reached my ears, but seeing as I am a disbelieving sort, I would like to see if you are made for the sort of challenge I have to offer you.

'If you are truly all that they say you are, I have a challenge for you: Igash may control the Grand Stair, but the other leaders are a forbidding bunch as well. I would have you enter the Grand Stair and collect the Marks borne by each of these leaders, holding them as proof that they have been defeated.

'This will not be an easy task, nor is it impossible. Prove yourself in this challenge of mine, and you will receive a rich reward. Oh, and... there is no shame in bringing friends with you on this venture.''A fine haul indeed! I am glad the leaders have been brought to bay. I hope we may enjoy a little peace before their numbers swell again. Thank you for your efforts, ${PLAYER}.'Once a great and beautiful cross-roads of Moria, the Grand Stair is a vile haven of many of the gathered tribes of Orcs. Igash, regarded as a leader by all of the varied groups, gathers tribute from every tribe to give to Mazog, the mightiest and greatest of Orcs in Khazad-dûm. The dwarves wish to unseat Igash's Orcs from this Grand Stair and stop Mazog from receiving valuable and unsavoury gifts.Many tribes of Orcs prowl the Grand Stair, south-east of the Twenty-first Hall.

Geirr has asked you to defeat many Orcs in the Grand Stair to assist in efforts to reclaim that cross-roads for the dwarves.Kill enemies in the Grand Stair (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Geirr is in the Twenty-first Hall, north-west of the Grand Stair.

Geirr will wish to hear of your success among the Orcs in the once-beautiful cross-roads of the dwarves.Return to Geirr at the Twenty-first Hall'Word of your renown has spread far and wide throughout these long tunnels, ${CLASS}. Aye, don't be modest -- I can see in your eyes the bright look of a valiant adventurer. I would know your ilk even without reinforcement from the rumours.

'Are you up for a small challenge? South-east of this hall lies the Grand Stair, once a fair cross-roads of the dwarves, and now nothing but a reeking Orc-pit. Ever do we strive to remove the Orc presence from that place so that it may be reclaimed in our favour once again.

'An adventurer such as yourself would look forward to a little dare such as the one I am about to propose: travel to the Grand Stair and slay many enemies there. The more Orcs you defeat, the better it will be for all the good folk of Moria and beyond.''The Orcs of Moria must be defeated! Head to the Grand Stair and drive them out!''I must be honest: I was not sure that you would be up for this challenge today. But I am glad to have been wrong on this occasion! Forgive me for not thinking better of you, ${PLAYER}, for the rumours of your courage were more than true.

'Here is a token of my appreciation. I hope to see more of you in the following days, for you are quite the asset to our endeavours here.'Defeat gredbyg in Skûmfil.Defeat gredbyg in SkûmfilDefeat Gredbyg in SkûmfilDefeat Gredbyg in SkûmfilDefeat cave-claws and deep-claws within Skûmfil.Defeat cave-claws and deep-claws in SkûmfilDefeat cave-claws and deep-claws in Skûmfil.Defeat cave-claws and deep-claws in SkûmfilA charred and crumpled note found on the corpse of Kranklûk confirmed some suspicions held by the dwarf-agents watching over the Forges of Khazad-dûm. What is worse, it mentioned that Talug, the general of Fil Gashan, was nothing more than a puppet.Teitur is at Anazârmekhem.

You discovered the note on the corpse of the Uruk Kranklûk, and after divining what you could, it became clear that it should be brought to Teitur who watches over Fil Gashan at Anazârmekhem.The note is terribly damaged by fire, but bits of it still convey some meaning, which is frightening and clear: Igash and Kranklûk were indeed something more nefarious than they appeared. Mazog may well know what they are, but he is unaware of their allegiance to the true Enemy. General Talug of Fil Gashan is nothing more than a gift from the Black Uruks to Mazog. 

The note continued to explain that General Talug's army was growing more massive by the day and would soon be ready for some manner of transformation.

You should bring this to Teitur, Narfi's agent watching over Fil Gashan at Anazârmekhem.'Preposterous! Raf was correct...I owe him an ale. Aye, we made a wager. He was telling tales of how this Orc seems to have a strange shadow around its eyes...I should have listened.

'It means that we need to be swift about our assault against Fil Gashan, I fear. Time is now of the essence and necessity, it seems.'You have heard it whispered that the libraries of Moria yet contain many tomes, while many other volumes were brought by the dwarves seeking to reclaim their ancient realm. One such tome in their libraries is said to be the legendary "The Jolly Hunter." If you can gain enough favour with the Iron Garrison Guards, they may let you have their copy of this book.Achieve level 58Read the book, "The Jolly Hunter"Mundgeirr has told you that the quality you wish to bestow upon your weapon can only be performed at the forge within the Library of Steel.Mundgeirr has asked you to bring him an infused adamant with which he can help you to bestow a quality upon your weapon. Unfortunately, he does not know where to find such a stone.Mundgeirr can be found at the Dolven-view camp in Moria.

Mundgeirr has bidden you to seek out the Library of Steel, but warns you that a band of Fire-orcs from the depths of Moria have taken control of the library.Talk to Mundgeirr to travel to the Library of SteelMundgeirr can be found at the Dolven-view camp in Moria.

You should return to Mundeirr to complete the enhancement of your weapon.'There is but one place where a quality such as you seek might be crafted. It was once known as the Library of Steel. It contains tomes detailing many of the finest secrets of dwarf-smithcraft and is protected from theft and destruction by ancient wards.

'Since we rediscovered it, however, it has likewise been found by the Ghâsh-hai who have taken hold of it. I cannot be certain what they intend to do with it, but it can't be any good.

'If you wish to obtain your quality, you will have to free the forge; however, before you even attempt it, you will need to bring me an infused adamant, which can hold the quality.''The news grows more grim by the minute, ${PLAYER}. I've had a spy keeping an eye on the Fire-orcs in the Library...it seems that they are working upon some foul art of their own there, summoning flaming spirits from the depths of Khazad-dûm to the Library. That can only mean one thing -- they intend to break the wards protecting the library and destroy everything within it!

'You must hurry if you are to stop them, and be sure not to let them summon too many of those spirits at once, or the ward protecting the library will break, and all will be lost!

'Speak to me again when you are ready to leave.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest, and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Ah, you found an Infused Adamant! Once I have prepared it, you will need to bring it to the Library of Steel.''Is the Library still intact? Oh, fine news, indeed. It is sad that we are so few, and I can spare none to study the secrets it holds...alas the wards prevent us from removing anything from the Library, as well, but at least it is safe from the Orcs....'You have heard it whispered that the libraries of Moria yet contain many tomes, while many other volumes were brought by the dwarves seeking to reclaim their ancient home. One such tome in the libraries of the Iron Garrison is said to be the legendary "The Master of the Charge." If you can gain enough favour with the Iron Garrison Guards, they may let you have their copy of this book.Achieve level 58Read the book, "The Master of the Charge"Delve deep into the ruined halls of Moria and face the threats that lie within.Complete Book I of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book II of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book III of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book IV of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book V of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book VI of "The Mines of Moria"Delve deep into the ruined halls of Moria and face the threats that lie within.Complete Book I of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book II of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book III of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book IV of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book V of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book VI of "The Mines of Moria"Delve deep into the ruined halls of Moria and face the threats that lie within.Complete Book I of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book II of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book III of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book IV of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book V of "The Mines of Moria"Complete Book VI of "The Mines of Moria"Delve deep into the ruined halls of Moria and face the threats that lie within.Complete Book I of "The Mines of Moria"Co