You have given Radanir the message for which you sought him, and he has agreed to join the Grey Company on its journey south. You should return to Elrohir now that you have dealt with the Orc raiding-party in the Laenan caves.Talk to Elrohir at Thorenhad'The sons of Elrond have already left for Thorenhad to prepare for the coming journey. Elrohir did say that one of the Dúnedain, a Ranger named Radanir, was staying for a few days at their camp. As Thorenhad is nearest to Rivendell, you should travel there first to speak to Radanir before seeking the others.

'I have met few of Aragorn's kin, but Radanir is known to me, for he journeys oft into the trackless Ettenmoors and often stays here in Rivendell. For a time, my brethren sought to convince him to join the company in song in the Hall of Fire, as Aragorn would, but Radanir would have none of it.

'Travel to Thorenhad, north of the Bruinen Gorges in the Trollshaws, and speak to Elrohir. He will introduce you to Radanir. Once the Ranger is told of the messages from Lórien, you can seek out the others Master Elrond suggested.''You are looking for Radanir, ${PLAYER}? He left not long ago, and he took no horse; the Dúnedain will ride when they must, but he has confided to me that he is more comfortable when on foot.

'Always behind his words to me was a current of...I do not know how best to say it, other than to say that Radanir seemed always to be waiting for something, somehow. I wonder if the tidings you bring are those for which he has waited so long? I wonder too if they will send him from the Trollshaws, and for how long? I will miss his visits if this be so, for I will not remain in this Middle-earth for much longer.

'I will not delay your errand for personal reasons, ${PLAYER}. Radanir spoke to me of his plans. He learned that a war-band of Orcs from the Ettenmoors has established a camp within the Laenan Caves here in the Trollshaws, and sought to confront them. The Laenan Caves are in the north-west reaches of the Gladdalf, the wood-troll infested marsh of the North Trollshaws.''Aragorn is my chieftain, and it is my duty to go to his aid, though all the dangers of Middle-earth lie in between. This is the moment for which the Dúnedain have prepared, and I will not now abandon my kin.

'I have learned that an Orc raiding party from the Ettenmoors has established a camp within this warren of tunnels, ${PLAYER}. I cannot leave them here, unchallenged. Will you brave these caves and deal with these foes? Somewhere deep within this confusing warren is the entrance to a once-abandoned tomb of Men. If the Orcs have found it, that is where their leaders will be. Thorenhad is less than a day's march from this place, and I will not suffer these Orcs to live that they may threaten what I hold dear.

'I will make ready to depart, as you have asked, but I will be grateful if you destroy the Orcs and their leaders within the Laenan tunnels and the ruins that lie at their back. After you have done so, return to Elrohir at Thorenhad and tell him of your success. The sons of Elrond lost their mother to such a party of Orcs; they would not forgive us if we let these foes be.''I thought to give Radanir word of our meeting at my father's house, ${PLAYER}, so he would have some expectation of the road ahead, but it seems he has left Thorenhad and gone again into the wilderness of the Trollshaws.

'He has left a few parcels here, so I am sure he intends to return, but he should be told the message you bear as soon as may be. I and my brother have already begun to assemble some of the supplies the Rangers will need as they head south, but it will take some time before they are ready.

'Speak to Elweleth, by the horses, for she will know where Radanir planned to journey, if any will. Radanir is slow to laugh and to smile, but in Elweleth's company, I have observed it most frequently.''You have given Radanir the message? Good. He will be able to find some others among the Dúnedain if they are to be found within the Trollshaws.

'But now you must travel west to find others of Aragorn's kin.''Thorenhad, the camp of the sons of Elrond, is located on a hill north of the Bruinen Gorges, deep within the Trollshaws. Travel there and speak with Elrohir for an introduction to Radanir, one of the Dúnedain currently staying there. Once you have given him word that the Dúnedain must be assembled, you can seek out the rest of those suggested by Master Elrond, though it take you across much of Eriador.''Radanir planned to investigate the Laenan cave system at the north-west reaches of the Gladdalf, the wood-troll infested marshes of the North Trollshaws. You should go there and give him your tidings, even if they will draw him from the Trollshaws and into great peril far from his friends. His road has always been through the Wild, and nothing this humble Elf-maid may say will change that.''I will prepare to join the rest of my kin, ${PLAYER}, but I want you to deal with the Orc raiding-party I tracked from the Ettenmoors. You will find them among this confusing network of caves and the ruins beyond.

'After you have dealt with the Orcs, return to Thorenhad and tell Elrohir that Laenan is clear of this foe.''You have dealt a serious blow to the Orcs, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for it. I go now to prepare for the journey south. You should return to Thorenhad and speak with Elrohir.''I thought Radanir would still be here when my brother and I returned, but he had already gone again into the wild. It could be that the Elf-maid Elweleth will know where he planned to go. Speak with her by the horses here in Thorenhad.'A serious complication has arisen in regard to the quest of the Grey Company: how does the dwarf Nár have knowledge of its potential road?Frithgeir is in the library at Zudrugund.

Corunir has asked you to speak with Frithgeir about Nár, in hopes that he can reveal the source of his friend's unexpected knowledge of the Grey Company's possible road.Frithgeir has pointed out book-shelves in Zudrugund where Nár has spent time recently, at the shelves south-east of Frithgeir, surrounding an illuminated statue.

You should investigate book-shelves where Nár has shown recent interest, hoping it will prove instructive.Nár is somewhere within Zudrugund.

You have searched the book-shelves pointed out to you by Frithgeir, but have learned little that seems relevant to Nár's mysterious knowledge of the Grey Company's intentions.Corunir is in Zudrugund, the dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb.

You have spoken to Nár about what you found in the book-shelves you were pointed to by Frithgeir, but have learned little of use. You should report of your efforts to Corunir.Candaith is at a camp-site of the Grey Company in a grove of trees east of the Dunlending village of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.

Corunir has sent you to speak with Candaith to see if he has thought of a reason for Nár to have mentioned the Paths of the Dead.'I do not like this, ${PLAYER}. How has Nár come to know of our potential road? That Elrond advised his sons to remember the Paths of the Dead, for we may have need to walk them, was known to but a few, and all of them trustworthy.

'Something is not right about this. It will not be long before we must set out again, but in the time before we do we must learn what we can about Nár's recent activities. There may be some clue in them to reveal the source of his knowledge concerning our errand.

'Speak to Frithgeir about Nár. He proved helpful in returning his friend to clarity, short-lived though it was, and may help us again.''You will find Frithgeir somewhere within the library here at Zudrugund. Speak to him about Nár; it may be he will reveal the source of his friend's strange knowledge of the paths Elrond bade us keep in mind.''Yes? I am sorry he upset you. Nár means no harm.'

Frithgeir listens as you recount Corunir's request to know of Nár's recent activities. His brow creases in thought, and he is silent for a moment. When he speaks again, his words come slowly, as if he is unsure they will be of help.

'A few days before Wadu the Dunlending came to Zudrugund, I noticed Nár spending a great deal of time looking through some of the book-shelves for something. I thought it odd, because he generally takes little interest in the books and records here, but he did not speak of it and I did not ask what drove his examination.

'I can tell you which section of shelves it was, but there are a lot of books to go through. I do not know if that will be of any assistance, but you are welcome to seek a common thread if it may be of help to you and your friends. Look at the shelves to the south-east of here, around the illuminated statue.''Nár spent an unusual amount of time at the book-shelves located south-east of here, the ones surrounding the illuminated statue.'(A slim book, 'Herbs and Plant-life of the Coomb,' is resting atop several others, as if it had been recently removed and carelessly returned to a shelf.)

It is my hope that these musings on the great variety of plant-life found within the Coomb will be of help to those who, by want or need, find themselves uncertain of a herb's utility or a vegetable's properties. It is clear that such knowledge is fading in my people; Thrór has commanded the opening of a new wing in the mines beneath Zudrugund, but my own studies have been, of a necessity, self-directed. I do not complain! The gems they have taken from the old wing stir my heart as much as they do any of my fellows; but there is as much to be learned from the green as there is from the stone.

But I ramble, as I often do when called upon to defend my interest!

(This is followed by several pages of notes on various plants that grow on the slopes of Thrór's Coomb. One of the pages has been turned sideways and ear-marked, and catches your eye.)

This next herb is easily found throughout the Coomb; simply follow your nose, and on a day of calm weather, its sweet smell will guide you most easily to patches of Hulwul-harâz growing on the grassy slopes. The name, in fact, means 'Sweet Warder,' and the Dunlendings with whom I have spoken believe the smell to drive away evil beings.

When the herb is crushed and added to water containing the petals of the Nijam-mujdu and the fruit of the Kibil-luwz, steeping the solution for an hour produces a strange-smelling brew that I have noted for its ability to provide clarity of thought and heightened reasoning. It does seem that its effectiveness dulls with repeated use, and the brew should therefore not be over-used.(You found a parchment, yellowed and old, tucked away between the covers of a thin book and apparently forgotten.)

Hail, Thráin, King under the Mountain!

Preparations for leaving the Coomb are well underway, and it will not be long before our people are ready to depart for the Blue Mountains. The great wheels in the depths will turn without them; I wonder for how long?

I am writing this letter to express my concern about Nár. You know as well as anyone the things he has seen, and his service to your father cannot be questioned. He deserves much better than he has gotten. He seems not even to see me, these days, though I must endure the retelling of my own exploits two, four, or a dozen years in the past. It makes me sad to think of it now, and his excitement in each recounting, the previous one forgotten, saddens me all the more.

This is why I must ask your permission to remain, when the others go. Nár will not go to the Blue Mountains, and I wish to stay here to look out for him. My son, too, wishes to stay; he is young, and knows nothing of our real home; this to him is no exile, but the Blue Mountains would be. I do not want to deprive Frithgeir of the only home he has known.

I will do whatever you command, of course. But this is my desire, if it be your wish to grant it.

Hervin Drake-slayer(You found some torn pages, which seem to have undergone a great deal of use over the years. The words are very large, and every page has an illustration drawn in a fanciful style.)

Kári was a little dwarf.
Smaller than you or me.
And wherever Kári went
He took his axe... or three.

One axe for his left hand.
One axe for his right.
One axe to carve the roast
He ate most every night.

Now one day the Orcs did call.
They came right up to the gate.
'Come on out!' they said,
'We'll have a feast! Don't be late!'

The dwarves knew what that meant.
They knew it very well.
If they went to see the Orcs
They'd not hear the dinner bell.

Do you know what Kári said?
He said it loud and clear.
'I will go to see the Orcs,
And give them things to fear!'

'But you're so little!' said the dwarves.
They told him not to go.
But Kári went; he would not stop
'Till he faced the Orcs below.

The Orcs laughed, 'What can you do?
Just hold still, we'll get the skewers.'
They turned their backs, and that was that!
So we call him Kári Orc-hewer.'Did you find anything interesting? You can ask Nár about any of the things you saw, but he may not be able to tell you anything more than you already know.''Nothing you found among the book-shelves strikes me as being relevant to Nár's knowledge of our possible road, ${PLAYER}. Ask the dwarf and see if he can illuminate any of this for us.'Nár nods in your direction, and a wide smile creases his old face.

'You have brought the gem-stones? Good, good! These will do very nicely!'

He seems not to realize that you have brought no gem-stones. This is going about as well as you expected.'Many days I cannot unravel my friend's words. Alas that this is one of those days! Perhaps your Ranger friend, Corunir, will know what to do.''Ah, Fannar, it has been too long! Trade at Zigil-jabâl continues fine, I trust? Good, good, good.''Nár's words remain as cloudy as ever. It was worth trying, ${PLAYER}, though it has not borne fruit.

'You should go to Candaith at the camp-site of the Grey Company in a grove of trees east of the Uch-luth village of Lhanuch, and tell him that we are no closer to learning why Nár spoke of the Paths of the Dead. Candaith is a master of lore and may have thought of something.''Candaith may have thought of something. I hope so, for I am out of ideas! Look for him at a camp-site of the Grey Company in a grove of trees east of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.''You have spoken with the dwarf Nár again, to no avail? I see. Will he not tell us how he knows of the words of Elrond? Will he not tell us how he knows of the Paths of the Dead? It seems this mystery must remain unsolved for a time yet.'

Candaith sighs, thinking.

'Do you know the story of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead? Perhaps a recounting of that tale is in order, and you will be more familiar with the puzzle set before us.

'Do you wish to hear it?'The time has come for the Grey Company to travel south into Dunland, continuing its dangerous mission to reach Aragorn in time to be of help.Halbarad is in the centre of the village of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.

You have been told that Halbarad is assembling the Grey Company in preparation for its departure and should travel to Lhanuch for the last of the preparations before departing.Talk to Halbarad in LhanuchPrestadír and Braigiar, in the centre of Lhanuch, are considering the two paths the Grey Company will take to enter Dunland.

Halbarad has asked you to consult with Prestadír and Braigiar about his proposed routes into Dunland.Talk to Braigiar in LhanuchTalk to Prestadír in LhanuchHalbarad is in the centre of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.

You have spoken with Prestadír and Braigiar about the paths into Dunland.Talk to Halbarad in Lhanuch'We have delayed too long already. I am ready to go, ${PLAYER}. Return to Halbarad in Lhanuch, and we will decide how best to proceed south into Dunland.

'We will leave this place behind, and none too soon! If we had left earlier, we could have left with Candaith and my fallen brothers, and not without them.''The south-west passage into Dunland will take us through Trum Dreng, ${PLAYER}. It is a forested land, where shadows pool in the hollows between the hills.

'As I scouted ahead, I saw emblems and totems of a horned stag, but few people. They are likely to be on the edge of the conflicts in Dunland, though if it be by their choice or by necessity I did not discover.

'You are welcome to come with my group into Trum Dreng, ${PLAYER}, and we will learn the truth of it.''Corunir is right. This delay has been costly. If what you say is true, and Saruman does know of our errand, we travel from hardship into even greater peril. And yet, we must not despair or turn from our road, or our brethren will have fallen for naught.'

Halbarad sighs, his brow creased with worry.

'There are two paths we could take into Dunland, and I propose that we split into smaller groups and travel each of these paths...we will be less likely to be noticed as we cross into Dunland.

'Prestadír and Braigiar have scouted our options. Speak with them to hear of the two roads that we might use to enter Dunland.''You have heard what we know about our possible paths into Dunland. Each way could be dangerous, and we will not know what awaits us until we get there.

'Which road do you wish to take into Dunland, ${PLAYER}? Each will have its dangers. Make your choice and tell Braigiar or Prestadír which group you will follow: Braigiar if you wish to travel through Trum Dreng, or Prestadír if you wish to enter the Bonevales.''The south-east passage into Dunland will take us through the Bonevales, an inhospitable and barren land. The crags and valleys are dusty and barren, and its people are tough, like coffin nails.

'There are few souls in the Bonevales, few eyes to watch our passage. Caution must be our watch-word, stealth our constant companion. My group would welcome your presence, ${PLAYER}.''If we had not delayed, we might have left with our fallen brothers, and not without them. Go to Lhanuch and tell Halbarad that I will be there shortly. You will find him in the centre of the village.''I would be pleased if you would join my group on the journey into Trum Dreng, ${PLAYER}. Its forested hills are steeped in shadow, cold and mysterious.''There are two paths that we might take into Dunland. Speak with Prestadír and Braigiar concerning what they have learned about the way into Dunland.''I would be honoured if you travelled with my group into the Bonevales. It is an inhospitable land and barren, and it would be good to have your company.'The Grey Company has come to Rivendell, and there are only a few things left to be done before they begin their journey south.The Lady Arwen is at Imdolen, on the slope that overlooks the road into the Misty Mountains, north of Rivendell.

Elrond has asked you to speak with his daughter Arwen before you depart with the Grey Company.Talk to Arwen at ImdolenHalbarad is by the stables in Rivendell.

Arwen has given you the standard she made for Aragorn and has asked you to bring it to Halbarad in Rivendell before the Grey Company departs.'By your efforts, the summons was brought throughout Eriador, ${PLAYER}, and now the Dúnedain have responded. Many of their number have come to Imladris, and they prepare to depart for the southern lands that they may come to Aragorn in his time of need.

'You will ride with them, if you see fit to do so, but I have been made aware that my daughter desires first to speak with you. She is at Imdolen, on the slope north of Imladris, overlooking the road that climbs up into the Misty Mountains.''You have returned from your errand, ${PLAYER}, and have come to speak with me, as I asked? My delight cannot be hidden, friend ${RACE}, for my own undertaking has also come to an end, and its fruit waits now for a bearer!'

Arwen solemnly hands you a tall staff, wrapped closely with a black cloth bound with thongs.

'I have made this gift for Aragorn. The days are short, and it may be that our hopes will soon be ended. This standard I have made for him will fly aloft at a time of his choosing, and he will know for what he fights. May it bring courage to my Elfstone and those with whom he stands!

'Bring this gift and my words to Halbarad, by the stables in Imladris, for he has consented to bear them both on the journey south.''This is the moment for which the Dúnedain have waited, ${PLAYER}. It will not be long now before we ride forth to meet our destiny.''I see my sister has at last finished the gift she was making for Aragorn! How long will it be until it is unfurled, I wonder?''We are making ready to depart. It will not be long now before we take the southern road.''It was a long ride from the north-lands, ${PLAYER}, but I too have come to Rivendell to depart with the Company. They have need of my knowledge of the southern lands.''I carried the message to several other Rangers upon leaving Laenan, ${PLAYER}, and they have come to ride with the Grey Company.''Bring this gift and my words to Halbarad, by the stables in Imladris, for he has consented to bear them both on the journey south.''My daughter is at Imdolen, overlooking the road that climbs into the Misty Mountains, north of Imladris. She desired to speak with you before the Grey Company departs.''You bring both a gift and a message for my chief from the Lady Arwen? I see.'

Halbarad listens as you recount Arwen's words and gravely accepts the standard you carry.

'I will carry this burden on our journey. It will come to Aragorn, though all the perils of our road lie in between. When will this standard be unfurled, I wonder, and against what sky will it be raised aloft?'Within Gwâl Draig, there must be a leader driving his people to perform such cruel acts upon the clans of the Algraig.Heilun Brenin and his guards can be found at the centre of Gwâl Draig, east of Gronu's camp.

Gronu asked you to defeat Heilun Brenin and his personal guards.Defeat Heilun Brenin's personal guards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Heilun BreninGronu's camp lies to the west of Gwâl Draig, beneath Harndirion.

You should seek out the Gronu and inform him that Heilun Brenin and his men have been defeated.Talk to Gronu at his camp'Within the village of Gwâl Draig, there must be someone who has set his people to this course of action. It is my hope that by defeating the brenin of the Draig-lûth these folk will no longer harass the Uch-lûth. 

'Before his disappearance, my chief scout discovered Heilun Brenin and his guards standing near a large bonfire at the centre of their encampment.

'Seek out Heilun Brenin and his men and dispatch them, then return to me.''I believe that while the creatures that hunt the valley to the north-east may still be difficult to deal with, Heilun Brenin's defeat may make the Draig-lûth less of a concern.

'Thank you from the depths of my heart for all of your help. You will be remembered and spoken of well by me among the Uch-lûth.''Heilun Brenin is a vile monster and should be punished for the wicked deeds he has committed against the Uch-lûth and Turch-lûth.'Gorothúl is protected by a fell spirit called Demafaer, a foul creature from beyond the Void.Defeat DemafaerThe gates of Dol Guldur are guarded by a strong force of the Enemy's soldiers, led by a powerful captain...rumoured to possibly be one of the Nine.The stone may be found outside Fennas Guldur, east of Thangúlhad.

Rodelleth has bidden you issue a challenge at the stone before the gates of Fennas Guldur and defeat the forces of Dol Guldur which answer.Issue your challenge to join the battleRodelleth is at Thangúlhad, west of Fennas Guldur.

With the aid of your companions, you have defeated the Gloom of Nurn before the gates of Fennas Guldur and struck a mighty blow against the power of Dol Guldur. You should now return to Rodelleth with the tale of your victory.Talk to Rodelleth at Thangúlhad'The walls of Dol Guldur are beyond our power to topple unless and until the power of the Nine is broken, but that does not mean that we are powerless against them. Long has the overweening pride of the Enemy been a weakness to exploit, since days of Morgoth himself.

'The task we face now is not so different than that of old, and so shall our strategy likewise be similar, for I would have you gather a host to lay siege to the gate of Fennas Guldur and issue a challenge against those who lie within. If one of their captains -- perhaps even one of the Nine -- can be drawn into battle, then we can deliver a stinging blow against them that they will not soon forget!

'There is a stone near the gate of Fennas Guldur from which you should issue your challenge. I will give you fair warning, though: even though singly the Nazgûl are not as powerful as they are in greater numbers, they are still fell beings of greater power than you. I suggest that you keep your guard, or you may meet your fate at Fennas Guldur.''You and your companions have defeated none less than the Gloom of Nurn, one of the Nine, in a place where his strength was at its greatest! Well fought indeed!

'I fear this victory is temporary, however, for it is not in our power to destroy the Ringwraiths, but Nurn is weakened and bereft of his form for a time, forced to flee to Minas Morgul until he can gather his tattered strength and is recloaked.

'It is the Gloom that would most likely have stood at the vanguard of any strike against Lothlórien, but no longer! Unfortunately, with the fell-beasts they ride to speed his return, I do not think it will be long ere he is seen again.'From here you may issue a challenge against the forces of Dol Guldur to defend their gates...or hide behind them in shameful cowardice.Gortheron the Doom-caller threw down the gauntlet, and you rose to the challenge. He has been vanquished at the height of his power.Gortheron the Doom-caller threw down the gauntlet, and you rose to the challenge. He has been vanquished at the height of his power.Gortheron has been defeated at the height of his powerThe Gaunt-lord Ivar, aided by a band of sellswords, swept through the village of Stoneheight in his search for his helm. When his goals were reached, he abandoned the village to the mercy of the sellswords.Defeat Búbhosh-grat, and Dale Truitt within 10 seconds of each other.Those who would call themselves the Champion of the Lost Temple must overcome all of the challenges they face within.Discover the entrance to the Lost TempleComplete all the Deeds within the Lost Temple.Complete 'One Ill Turn....'Complete 'The Sick and the Dead'Complete 'Synchronized Slaughter'Complete 'Ferndúr's Defeat'Complete 'Clean Sweep'Within the quiet confines of the Northcotton Farm, Thadúr the Ravager has begun his quest to defile the Shire. In a time of peril, the Shire was in need of a hero, and you sought out Thadúr and defeated him at every turn.Complete the Thadúr's Plans Foiled DeedComplete the Cleansing of Darkthorn DeedWadu has expressed his displeasure at the slaying of his kin by other Dunlending clans and wants you to fight on his behalf.Deserters can be found north of Echad Dagoras, in Fordirith.

Wadu has asked you to recover blood-marks from the Dunlending deserters in Fordirith and to recover the weapons of his fallen kinsmen.Collect blood-marks from Dunlendings in Fordirith (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Recover weapons from fallen Uch-lûth clansmen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Wadu is at Echad Dagoras, the camp of the Grey Company in Fordirith, Enedwaith.

You have collected blood-marks and weapons from fallen Uch-lûth and should bring them to Wadu as he requested.Lothrandir is at Echad Dagoras, in Fordirith, northern Enedwaith.

You have pleased Wadu with your heroics on his behalf among the Dunlending deserters and should tell Lothrandir of your successes.Talk to Lothrandir at Echad Dagoras'The Uch-lûth will tolerate your presence, but other Dunlending lûth will be openly hostile. They have been given gold and have forgotten their true allegiances. Many of them travelled north into the land of the holly, but they have returned. They are cowards on their own, and they are cowards on behalf of their new master!

'But these lûth that fight for gold have no loyalty to their former brothers. They fight even the Uch-lûth! For every one of my people they slay, they put a mark in blood on a chip of wood. They believe their new master will pay them for every one of their kinsmen they slay. It is an abomination.

'They have already killed some of my people. Travel to the deserters' camps north of here and deprive them of the blood-marks they have already collected. Recover, too, the weapons of my fallen brothers. I do not want them taken by their foes.''Wadu at least seems pleased with you, ${PLAYER}. If his family ruled the Uch-lûth, perhaps our road south would be easier. Sadly, it appears that his father Suvulch, though respected by the brenin of the Uch-lûth, is not one of his brehur.

'Still, respect earned is not respect wasted. Perhaps Wadu's respect for you will help us as we must deal more closely with the Uch-lûth.''Deserters from the raids in the north have camps here in Fordirith. Deprive them of the blood-marks with which they seek to reward the deaths of my people! Recover too the weapons of my fallen kinsmen. It would be an insult for the weapons of the Uch-lûth to be used against them.''Tell your duvodiad friends what you have done for Wadu. They were wise to bring you, for your will is strong, like one of the Uch-lûth! They speak in riddles and seem always on the verge of despair. I do not wonder that they are not welcome in Dunland.''It brings me no joy to see these marks, nor to receive these weapons, for they are reminders of the Uch-lûth that have fallen and of the greed of these people that were once my kin. But no more! These other lûth are no longer of my stock. They are cowards, slaves to gold, and are no longer Men. They are like Orcs now, and the brave Uch-lûth will treat them like Orcs.

'You will continue to do so? Good. You are not one of us, but you will be a friend to my family. Perhaps we will compare the tallies of the slain when all of Dunland is unified again and under the strong rule of the Uch-lûth! I look forward to that time.

'Tell your duvodiad friends what you have done for Wadu. I am not certain about them, but about you I have made up my mind. Your strength of will is like to mine, and you are made of similar stuff, strong as the stones of Dunland.'It all becomes clear: if the Oath-breakers can be compelled to fulfil their oath, Aragorn's destiny is assured. The knowledge of how to do this may lie along the Forsaken Road.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Radanir is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in the grove of trees east of Lhanuch.

You should speak with Radanir when you are ready to walk the Forsaken Road in search of information that may allow Aragorn to compel the Oath-breakers to fulfil their oath.Talk to Radanir and walk the Forsaken RoadHalbarad is among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.

Halbarad is waiting to speak with you after the disaster of the Forsaken Road.Talk to Halbarad in Lhanuch'If Ergothorn is right, a number of Oath-breakers fled from the Dwimorberg after Isildur cursed them, and left Gondor. They travelled through the trackless wilderness, seeking to escape the curse, and came at last to the Lich Bluffs. Did they find what they sought, or did they come to realize there was no escaping their curse? I do not know.

'But if we can find the Forsaken Road where this group finally remained, we may learn something to our advantage. We may learn if Aragorn can compel them to fulfil their oath.

'I have spoken with Rhod, the scout who found Ergothorn's body. He knows where the entrance to the Forsaken Road is and will show us the way. Talk to me when you are ready, and I will assemble a group of Rangers to investigate.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Are you ready to depart for the Forsaken Road? Many of my brethren have volunteered to come with us; they are eager to find some information that will aid Aragorn, especially to see if there is some secret by which he can compel the Oath-breakers to fulfil their oath.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'We knew there would be hardship on our road, ${PLAYER}, but I did not think... I did not think it would happen so soon....'Devastated by the losses suffered by the Grey Company on the Forsaken Road, Halbarad wants you to tell Corunir it is time to leave.Corunir is at Zudrugund.

Halbarad wants you to tell Corunir to come back to Lhanuch.Talk to Corunir at ZudrugundNár is in Zudrugund.

Corunir, angered by the loss of his friends along the Forsaken Road, has forced Frithgeir to give Nár the strange-smelling brew once again, in order for dwarf to give clear answers about his knowledge.Talk to Nár at ZudrugundCorunir is at Zudrugund.

You have spoken with Nár about a number of topics of interest to Corunir and should tell the Ranger what you have learned.Talk to Corunir in Zudrugund'I want Corunir to come back from Zudrugund. It is time we made ready to leave once again, and I want every Ranger ready to depart. We have lost too many of our number to foolish detours! Enough!

'Go to Zudrugund and tell Corunir that it is done. We ride at first light. I do not care what Nár may know, or of what Ergothorn was afraid. I thought we could attain some secret, some new wisdom that would be of use to Aragorn when we come at last to him in Rohan, but I was wrong.

'There is no secret weapon, no easy victory. Go to Corunir and tell him we are done with Zudrugund and done with Nár.''I must follow Halbarad's orders, but I will not leave until I have some answers! After the disaster of the Forsaken Road, I strode through the Coomb and collected the ingredients for the strange-smelling brew: Kibil-luwz, Nijam-mujdu, and Hulwul-hurz. Frithgeir did not want to, but at the end I gave him no choice, and he has brewed the drink and given it to Nár.

'Hurry! Go now to Nár and ask him, once again, how he knows of the Oath-breakers! Ask him how he knows the words of Elrond concerning the Paths of the Dead! Ask him why my friends are dead!

'Go, ${PLAYER}! We will leave Zudrugund, but first I want to know why all of this had to happen!''I cannot believe this! The traitor Saruman has been influencing our errand without our knowledge, and we have played right into his hands!

'This stage of our journey, this investigation into the mystery posed by Nár, is over. Halbarad is right: the next stage of our journey will take us south into Dunland, likely towards war and danger, right beneath the shadow of our enemy, but it does not matter. What matters is that we journey. For good or ill, the Grey Company will continue on its road.

'This detour means our enemy knows we are coming. We must hope he does not know why, but if he does, perhaps that knowledge will cause him to make a mistake, to make some error he will regret.

'Our friends have perished. Our hope for the future must not die with them. The Grey Company will ride again, and soon. You will come with us, if you still wish to. I would not blame you for changing your mind, for the road will be hard.'The distant expression that Nár usually wears has been replaced with a shrewd glare. He is still under the effect of the strange-smelling brew, but that clarity may not last long.'I have given Nár the brew once again. Your Ranger friend had a strange fire in his eyes, and I did not feel I could refuse. You should hurry, for the effects will not last.''I am sorry for what has happened to your friends because of this. I did not think this could happen.''Hurry! You must ask Nár these questions before the effects of the brew fade! I want to know why this had to happen! I will not leave here without answers!'The distant expression that Nár usually wears has been replaced with a shrewd glare. He is still under the effect of the strange-smelling brew, but that clarity may not last long.'Tell Corunir to return to Lhanuch. We are done with Zudrugund and done with Nár.'The road that runs north through Dunland and Enedwaith has been troubled by hostile Dunlending clans, and the Grey Company is worried by these reports.Saeradan is at his camp, Echad Saeradan, in the Windfells, in Enedwaith. His camp can be found on a hilltop in the Windfells, south-west of Echad Dagoras.

Lothrandir has sent you into the Windfells to assist Saeradan.Talk to Saeradan at Echad SaeradanThe road north of Echad Saeradan may have been blockaded by forces of Saruman.

Saeradan has asked you to investigate the road north for signs of blockade.Investigate the road north of Echad Saeradan for evidence of a blockadeSaeradan is at his camp south of the blockaded road in Fordirith.

You found a report and a sword with ancient markings in the coffer at the blockaded road and should bring them to Saeradan.'Saeradan has gone on ahead, ${PLAYER}, and established a camp in the Windfells. He planned to investigate the state of the road that runs through Enedwaith and Dunland, for he worried that it had been blocked by hostile Dunlending clans.

'Go now to his camp far to the south-west of here, on a hilltop in the Windfells. Help him with his investigation. I know he will be happy to have your help.''Things are not right in Enedwaith, ${PLAYER}. As we have come further south, it becomes more clear to me that the plans of the Wizard Saruman, told to us by Elrond, are more advanced than even the Elf-lord believed.'

You tell Saeradan of your encounter with Wadu of the Uch-lûth, and he nods grimly.

'We have few friends here, ${PLAYER}, and I fear they are becoming fewer. Wadu may speak for us, but I am not convinced that his clan will listen. Already there are more clans in Enedwaith than just the Uch-lûth, and they are hostile to us. Travel a short way to the north and see what has become of the road. I fear it has been blockaded at the command of Saruman.''Investigate the road north of here and determine if it has been blockaded. I fear that it has.''Saeradan's camp is located in the Windfells, on a hill-top far to the south-west of here.'Saeradan takes the report from you and reads it with growing concern. He then marvels at the sword.

'This confirms what I feared, ${PLAYER}. One of the Dunlending clans has been ordered, likely by Saruman, to blockade the road north and to slay any who seek to pass that way. This sword is of ancient make; see the markings here in the shape of horses? This is a sword of the Rohirrim.'Saeradan has asked you to find the unfortunate bearer of the Rohirrim sword you recovered from the blockade of the road in the Windfells.The former bearer of the Rohirrim sword may be close to death somewhere on the road south of the blockade in the Windfells.

Saeradan wants you to search the road, starting at the blockaded north gate and working your way south, looking for the fallen Rohirrim.Find the fallen Man of Rohan along the road and try to save himSaeradan is at his camp in the Windfells.

You have found the former bearer of the sword, but he was not one of the Rohirrim at all. It was Wadu of the Uch-lûth, and when you arrived he was already dead. You should bring the news to Saeradan.Talk to Saeradan at Echad Saeradan'The former bearer of this sword may still be alive, ${PLAYER}, but injured and near death. There may be a chance that we can save him; perhaps the hostile Dunlendings who stole his blade did not finish him.

'He may have tidings of Rohan, and as that is where we hope to join Aragorn, they could be of use to us as we journey further south.

'Begin at the blockaded gate north of here and follow the road south, ${PLAYER}. You may be able to find this fallen Rohirrim and save him.'Saeradan listens to your account, and his confusion is evident.

'I am sorry indeed to hear that Wadu has been slain, but I cannot understand how he came to possess the sword that was taken from him. It is without doubt a sword of the Rohirrim, and the Men of Rohan are no friend to the clans of Dunland. There is a long history of war between the two, and I fear that if Saruman's plans continue to bear fruit there will be war again.

'Why would Wadu have borne such a sword? And what brought him to that road to die? Was he so o'ermatched in number that even such a well-crafted sword could avail him nothing?''It is a small chance, but we must take it. If this Man of Rohan is still alive, though the chance be small, we must try and save him. Begin at the blockaded gate on the road north of here and work your way south looking for him.'The sword that was taken from Wadu should be returned to his family at the village of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.Wadu's father Suvulch can be found somewhere within the Uch-lûth village of Lhanuch.

You need to find the father of Wadu and bring him the sad news of his son's death. Additionally, he should be given the ancient sword of the Rohirrim that was taken from Wadu when he was slain.Wadu's sister Nona can be found somewhere within Lhanuch, the village of the Uch-lûth, in Nan Laeglin.

Suvulch has coldly dismissed the death of his son Wadu, calling him a coward. You should bring the news of Wadu's death and the ancient Rohirrim sword to his sister Nona instead.'It is no use dwelling upon such mysteries, for we may never know the answers to them. He spoke of his family to you, did he not? They must be told of his death, and the sword returned to them.

'You will likely find them at Lhanuch, the village of the Uch-lûth, in Nan Laeglin. You should ask the people there until you find his father and bring him both the unhappy news and the sword.

'It may seem cold, but perhaps the death of his son at the hands of another Dunlending clan will soften the father's heart to the Dúnedain. Wadu may still help us in our errand, though he be slain.''You must find Wadu's father. He will likely be at the Uch-lûth village of Lhanuch, and must be given both the unhappy news and the sword that was taken from his son.''If you must speak of Wadu, you waste your time! I will not hear of him! Bring your words of him to his sister Nona, if you must, but waste my time no further!''Speak not to me of Wadu! He is a coward, and no son of mine!'

The silence following this proclamation stretches awkwardly for too long a time. Suvulch reads your expression.

'He is dead? Then his chance for courage is ended, and I mourn only that. No, I will hear no more of it! If you have words of Wadu, tell them to his sister. If the powers were just, Nona would have been the son and he the daughter, for she is like iron and could make her father proud! With the clans all around the Uch-lûth preparing for war and closing in upon us, the only good thing Wadu ever did for us is rid us of his cowering presence!'Nona listens sorrowfully as you recount your discovery of her brother's death. There is the briefest trembling within her deep eyes, then it is gone. The emotion mastered, Nona stares at you sternly.

'Wadu was not the fighter our father Suvulch wanted him to be, and when he spoke to him, which was rarely, Suvulch was cruel. I tried to give Wadu the lessons he needed, lessons in the sword and the axe and the ways of fire, but I could not teach him the courage he needed. My words to him were harsh. If they drove him to his death I would have them back!'Something Wadu saw in the abandoned dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb brought him to his death, and his sister Nona wants to learn what it was.The dwarf-hall is located among the northern mountains of Thrór's Coomb, far to the east of Lhanuch, the Uch-lûth village in Nan Laeglin.

Nona has asked you to journey to the dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb to learn what Wadu saw there that brought him to Fordirith and then to his death.Explore the dwarf-hall in Thrór's CoombTwo dwarves make their home in Zudrugund, the dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb, within Nár's Peak.

One of the dwarves in the hall seems unable to focus upon you, but the other may be able to tell you what is going on.Talk to Frithgeir at ZudrugundThe ingredients needed by Frithgeir to make the brew can be found throughout Thrór's Coomb. He gave you a list of the ingredients and their locations to study.

Frithgeir can make a brew that brings Nár back to awareness.Collect handfuls of Hulwul-harâz (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect Najâm-mujdu flowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect Kibil-lawâz (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Frithgeir is in Zudrugund, the once-abandoned dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb.

You have collected the ingredients needed by Frithgeir to make the brew that will restore Nár's awareness of his surroundings.'After our argument, Wadu went to the halls of the corrach -- the dwarves -- in Thrór's Coomb, but what he saw there I do not know; he would say nothing to me when he returned.

'Those halls have been abandoned for countless years, and have a fell reputation. No one goes there: corrach, Uch-lûth, or Draig-lûth. Did Wadu find this sword there? I cannot explain it. If you go to the dwarf-halls and learn what my brother saw there I will repay you with knowledge of these lands known only to the Uch-lûth.

'I have never been to the halls of the corrach, but they can be found far to the east of here, in the northern mountains of Thrór's Coomb.''This hall is mostly abandoned, ${PLAYER}. Two dwarves seem to live here, but I have seen no sign of the rest of their people. Nár and Frithgeir, their names are, and Frithgeir seems normal enough, but as for Nár....

'Well, see for yourself.''I do not know what my brother saw in the dwarf-halls in Thrór's Coomb, but he would not speak of it. I want to know what it was and why he went forth to his death. Go to the halls of the corrach far to the east of here, in the northern mountains of Thrór's Coomb.''Eh? Who's there? Who is it? Eh?'

The dwarf peers forward, but his eyes seem unable to fix upon you.

'The soup is cold! Hervin, the soup is cold! Set it atop the fire! Ah, yes, ah. Three and twenty!'

The dwarf wheezes, his beard fluttering slightly with the exhalation. He brings his hand up to his mouth, as if holding a ladle, and sips at the air. He pays you no more heed.'Frithgeir seems to take care of Nár, and his devotion seems to be very strong, but I have not been able to learn much about either of them.''The last one, eh? Good... good....'

The dwarf nods contentedly, but makes no sign of having seen you.'Welcome to Zudrugund, traveller. I... I should apologize for Nár. He is very old -- I know not how old -- and his mind... wanders. He has experienced a great deal of hardship and pain, but he has remained here while all others have left him. All save Frithgeir, that is.'

You explain why you have come and ask Frithgeir about Wadu. He nods with recognition.

'Yes, the Dunlending. He came to speak with Nár some time ago. Nár was more aware that night than he usually is, so I marked the visit. The two ventured into the mine beneath these halls. I'm sorry, but I do not remember anything else peculiar about the visit.

'There is a brew I can make of several ingredients found throughout Thrór's Coomb. The vapours of the brew seem to make Nár more aware of his surroundings. If you bring me these ingredients, the brew may allow you to speak with Nár directly. I will need several handfuls of the Hulwul-hurz herb, some Nijam-mujdu flowers, and the variety of nut called Kibil-luwz. Take this list of the ingredients and where they can be found.''I need a number of ingredients to make the brew that will bring Nár back to wakefulness: several handfuls of the Hulwul-hurz herb, some Nijam-mujdu flowers, and the variety of nut called Kibil-luwz. These all can be found throughout Thrór's Coomb. Consult the list of ingredients I gave to you for their locations.''Frithgeir gave you a list of ingredients he needs in order to make a brew to make Nár more lucid? Well, I see no harm in gathering them together. They will be found throughout Thrór's Coomb? That seems simple enough.''You found the various ingredients for which Frithgeir asked? Very good! Give them to him at once, and we will see if this brew has any effect at all upon Nár. I must admit I do not know what to expect, ${PLAYER}.''Ah, you have the ingredients, good. I will begin making the brew in just a moment.'You have brought the ingredients Frithgeir needs to make a brew that will restore Nár's awareness of you so you can ask him what he said to Wadu.Frithgeir is in Zudrugund, the once-abandoned dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb.

Frithgeir is preparing to make the brew that will restore Nár's clarity so you can ask him questions.Talk to Frithgeir and wait for him to make the brew for NárFrithgeir is in Zudrugund, the once-abandoned dwarf-hall in Thrór's Coomb.

You have travelled with Nár into the mine beneath Zudrugund and witnessed some peculiar things that the Dúnedain are hard-pressed to explain.Talk to Frithgeir at ZudrugundCorunir is at Zudrugund.

Frithgeir wants you to speak to Corunir and assure him that Nár means no harm.Talk to Corunir in Zudrugund'I will set about making the brew, ${PLAYER}, if you do not mind waiting. Let me know if that is fine.''I do not know, ${PLAYER}! Nár has often said peculiar things, but it is difficult sometimes to determine what he means by them. He has had an extremely long and extremely painful life, as I understand it, and only faithful Frithgeir remains now to help him continue it.

'These Men with you, these Rangers...they seem disturbed by what Nár has said. Can you ease their minds? I fear they will do him some harm, and I cannot allow that!''I am completely at a loss, ${PLAYER}. How can Nár have known what Elrond said to his sons before we departed Rivendell? And what does it mean that he does know it?'

Corunir sighs, and you realize that it has become a familiar sound. He catches your look and smiles bitterly.

'It feels like we are mired in something we do not fully understand. We cannot afford to ignore this mystery, for it may threaten the very purpose of our journey, but what will we discover?

'We must take a rest, in any event. We can talk this over later, and hope things will be more clear.''There is a Ranger by the entrance. Tell him that Nár means no harm by his words, from wherever they come.''It will be a few moments. Find a comfortable place to wait.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a solo quest and cannot be completed with a fellowship.</rgb>'Frithgeir is going to make the brew for Nár? Good. I hope it is enough to make him understandable for us. Speak with Frithgeir and see how goes the work.''What does it mean, ${PLAYER}? I do not know what the words of Nár must signify, but they fill me with dread! Speak with Frithgeir about this! Demand that he tell us what this means for the Grey Company!'The Elves of the Golden Host are prepared to mount their assault on the fortress of Dol Guldur.Goldagnir is in Thangúlhad.

You have been told to speak with Goldagnir about the details of the plan to strike the fortress of Dol Guldur.Talk to Goldagnir at ThangúlhadThe time has come to launch the strike against Dol Guldur. The first skirmish will take place at the gate of the fortress.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>Complete Skirmish: Breaching the Necromancer's GateGoldagnir is in Thangúlhad, waiting to begin the second stage of the assault upon Dol Guldur.

You have participated in the skirmish at the gate of the fortress and should speak now with Goldagnir to learn his plans for the next stage of the battle.Talk to Goldagnir at ThangúlhadThe battle for Dol Guldur continues, with the next skirmish taking place behind the walls of the city.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>Complete Skirmish: Assault on the Ringwraiths' LairGoldagnir is in Thangúlhad.

You have participated in the second skirmish at Dol Guldur and should report to Goldagnir.Talk to Goldagnir at ThangúlhadNingloril is in Thangúlhad, the camp of the Golden Host in Gathbúrz.

Ningloril has asked for you to come see her again about a secret errand.Talk to Ningloril at Thangúlhad'That Thangúlhad stands still in our control and that the Golden Host opposes still the wraiths that rule from that fortress are both testaments to your prowess and ours. Let us send a message to those who reside within yonder walls!

'The time has come to strike hard the fortress of the Enemy, ${PLAYER}! Goldagnir has devised a plan to do so, and we have been discussing it. He will tell you the specifics, if you inform him that you are willing to fight.''The fortress of Dol Guldur stands atop the rocky spire to the east, ${PLAYER}. It will be no easy task to fight our way beyond the walls, for the Enemy will see us coming from a great distance.

'Those of us who do make it to the gate will need to bear the burden of combat for the entire host, for we are certain to lack the numbers needed for easy victory.

'Seizing the gate is just the beginning, but let us concentrate on the start of the campaign before planning for the end!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>'We have taken the gate, but may not hold it for long. We must act quickly, and launch the second attack upon the fortress!

'This skirmish will happen beyond the walls of Dol Guldur; I do not know what perils we will face there, but that is the destination of the Golden Host, and we must face it now unflinching!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>'The battle goes well, ${PLAYER}! Perhaps we will see this song to the end after all and have bards willing to sing of it, and folk alive to listen!

'Ningloril was asking to see you again. Now that the Golden Host has made it so far up the rocky spire, she believes it may be possible to achieve another of our objectives here in Mirkwood, one cast aside days ago.''The Golden Host has done well in the first strike against the fortress, ${PLAYER}, but now the time has arrived to do what we came here to do.''The first skirmish in our strike against Dol Guldur will take place at the gate of the fortress. Do not waver in your resolve!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>'The next skirmish will take the Golden Host behind the walls of Dol Guldur, into the very lair of the Ringwraiths that now reside within the fortress. Fear no phantom evils! The Golden Host will be with you!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>'Ningloril is here in Thangúlhad. She has been asking for you. It is best not to keep her waiting, for when her mind is set, nothing will dissuade her from thinking on it.''Goldagnir here has the battle plan for striking the fortress of Dol Guldur. He will share it with you if you are willing to do battle. I have seen enough of your skills by now to know that you are; there can be no question on that account!'Deep within the dungeons of Dol Guldur, Bori remains captive within a prison cell. It is time to free him.Ningloril is in Thangúlhad, the camp of the Golden Host in Gathbúrz.

Ningloril is waiting to travel with you to the dungeons of Dol Guldur to rescue Bori from his imprisonment.Talk to Ningloril and infiltrate the dungeons of Dol GuldurNingloril is in Thangúlhad, the camp of the Golden Host in Gathbúrz.

You have rescued Bori from the dungeons of Dol Guldur and should speak now to Ningloril, who organized the rescue effort.Talk to Ningloril at ThangúlhadBori is inside Nothgar, the central tower of Thangúlhad.

Bori is waiting to speak to you to thank you for his rescue.Talk to Bori at Nothgar in Thangúlhad'We came here to release the dwarf Bori from his captivity, and we still have not done so. Now that we have fought beyond the walls of Dol Guldur, what can stop the Golden Host from doing what the Hidden Guard set out to do?

'We will infiltrate the dungeons after nightfall with a small force of Elves and rescue Bori. Speak with me when you are ready to depart.''${PLAYER}, I cannot thank you enough; I thought I had seen the last of you in Zabadgathol, deep in the depths of Moria. To be rescued thus...I do not have the words to tell you.

'But it is not enough to be free, while still the Enemy bears Zigilburk because of my actions! We must recover the axe of mithril before it can be used for some great evil, and we must see Gorothúl defeated!''After so much loss of life, we have at last accomplished what the Hidden Guard set out to do, ${PLAYER}. The dwarf Bori is free of the dungeons of Dol Guldur. The only task that remains now is for the Golden Host to lay siege to the fortress for as long as it can, to provide the distraction Lord Celeborn desired.

'Bori is inside Nothgar, the central tower of Thangúlhad. He desired to thank you for his rescue.''The tower Nothgar is here in Thangúlhad. Bori is inside, waiting to thank you for his rescue.''Are you prepared to embark upon the mission of rescuing Bori from the dungeons of Dol Guldur?'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>Bori has remembered Gandalf's mention, long ago, of a secret way into the foundations of Dol Guldur, and believes if it might be found it could be used to the benefit of the Golden Host.Sigileth is in Nothgar, the tower located within the camp of Thangúlhad.

Bori has remembered something that may be useful: a secret way into Dol Guldur used by Gandalf long ago.Talk to Sigileth at Nothgar in ThangúlhadThe mysterious secret way into the foundations of Dol Guldur that Gandalf must have used is surely located somewhere in the rocky spire upon which the fortress stands.Find and walk the secret passage into the foundations of Dol GuldurNingloril is in Thangúlhad.

You have discovered the secret way used by Mithrandir to enter Dol Guldur long ago and should report now to Ningloril.Talk to Ningloril at Thangúlhad'During my imprisonment, ${PLAYER}, to pass the time, I recited to myself the stories Bróin and I listened to during our childhood: tales of Khazad-dûm, mostly, and of the Quest of the Dragon upon which our grandfathers adventured.

'Through the long hours of my captivity, I came to remember details from those stories I had long since forgotten. I remembered how Gandalf brought to Thorin Oakenshield the key and the map given him by Thorin's father Thrain...who was then a captive in the dungeons of the Necromancer, in the dungeons of Dol Guldur.

'How came Gandalf into those dungeons? Why, ${PLAYER}, he must have found a secret way in!''You have done it, ${PLAYER}! This secret way into the foundations of Dol Guldur is just what the Golden Host needs to take the fortress by surprise!

'Both Raddir and Mithrandir have aided us, even in death.''The dwarf may be on to something, ${PLAYER}. I remember too the stories of Mithrandir, and though the plight of the dwarves was not one that concerned us in days past, I have heard the tale of which he speaks. More than that, a friend of ours played a part in it!

'Poor, unfortunate Raddir journeyed with Mithrandir on that long ago quest to the dungeons of the Necromancer. He spoke, on a time, of the ways they trod together, and that there was a secret way into the foundations of the fortress seems a certainty to me.

'We must find it, ${PLAYER}. It must be somewhere in the base of the rocky spire on which Dol Guldur stands, either in Gathbúrz or the Ashenslades.''Gandalf must have had some secret way into Dol Guldur by which he was able to speak with Thorin's father Thrain, mustn't he? If we can find this secret way, we might use it to sneak into the fortress and recover Zigilburk!''Gandalf must have taken a secret way into the foundations of Dol Guldur. Look for the entrance somewhere in the base of the rocky spire on which the fortress stands!''Imagine our advantage if the Golden Host could infiltrate Dol Guldur by this secret way. You must find it, ${PLAYER}, if it exists. The entrance is sure to be somewhere in the base of the rocky spire on which stands the fortress.'The Golden Host begins its final assault on Dol Guldur, and you have been asked to fight alongside the Malledhrim during the battle.Goldagnir is in Thangúlhad, the outpost of the Golden Host closest to Dol Guldur.

Ningloril has told you to speak with Goldagnir, who is organizing the last assault upon the tower.Talk to Goldagnir at ThangúlhadYou have your own part to play in the skirmish at the tower, the final assault by the Golden Host upon Dol Guldur.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>Complete Skirmish: The Battle in the TowerGoldagnir is in Thangúlhad, the closest outpost of the Golden Host to Dol Guldur.

With a small victory in hand, you should return to Goldagnir.Talk to Goldagnir at Thangúlhad'The last assault upon Dol Guldur is upon us, ${PLAYER}. We will strike against the stronghold of the Nazgûl in Mirkwood, and may the very foundations of the tower shake with the drums of battle!

'Goldagnir has drawn up a plan for the assault. Speak with him to learn of your part in it and that of your allies.''There will be no better time than this to assault the tower of the Nazgûl, ${PLAYER}. Lord Celeborn has determined that we cannot withstand the counterattack should all of the armies of Dol Guldur move on us at once; this will be our final offensive.

'We will divide our forces and lead a concentrated attack upon the tower: the main force of the Golden Host will assault the front entrance, clearing the way for a second group of adventurers, a kinship of great notoriety, to assault the tower.

'Meanwhile, you and your allies will enter the fortress by the secret way used by Mithrandir and engage in a skirmish within the tower. The Enemy will be in disarray, not knowing on what front to do battle, and we will have the day.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>'We have had victory, ${PLAYER}; at least so much victory as we had a right to expect. We cannot hold this fortress, and it may not be long before the Enemy strikes back at us with such force as it will take to push us back across the river.

'Lothlórien will be attacked, that much is certain. But this is a victory if we have delayed that attack by even a single day. My heart tells me we have, and by keeping the eyes of the Enemy drawn to the doorstep of the fortress of the Nazgûl, we have done what Lord Celeborn and Lady Galadriel wished us to do.

'The battle may be over, for now, but some stories have not had their endings. That is the way of life, is it not? It has been my pleasure to fight alongside you, ${PLAYER}, in this campaign. May your story go on and on.''You will be an important ally during the skirmish at the tower, ${PLAYER}. I hope you are prepared for this final assault upon Dol Guldur!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, Fellowship, or Raid group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.</rgb>'Goldagnir can share with you the plans of battle concerning the final assault upon the tower of the Nazgûl. He is here in Thangúlhad.'Candaith wishes you to know the story of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead, so you can appreciate why the Rangers are so disturbed by the words of Nár.Candaith is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in a grove of trees to the east of the Uch-luth village of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.

Candaith has decided to tell you the story of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.Talk to Candaith at the grove east of Lhanuch and experience 'Session: At the Stone of Erech' or read the shortened account insteadCandaith is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in a grove of trees east of the Uch-luth village of Lhanuch, in Nan Laeglin.

You have listened to Candaith relate the tale of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead and should now speak with him.Talk to Candaith at the grove east of Lhanuch'After the destruction of Númenor in the Second Age, Elendil and his sons came to Middle-earth and knew they had to start anew or perish. And so the kingdoms of Arnor and Gondor were founded, Arnor in the north and Gondor in the south.

'At the hill of Erech, in the kingdom of Gondor, Isildur set a great black stone that was brought out of Númenor before its downfall. It is said the stone stands there still, unchanged, even as it was in the latter years of the Second Age.

'That is where -- and when -- our story begins.''If you are ready to listen, I will tell you the tale of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.'In the Second Age, the Men of the Mountain swear an oath at the Stone of Erech to fight the forces of Sauron, but when they break their promise, Isildur levies a curse against them. They will never know rest until they fulfil their oath....'The Men of the Mountain sought to escape their curse, fleeing back to their mountain homes, but no word of man relates what became of them in the years since. It is said a shadow lies now on Erech, where they broke their oath, and none now take the Dimholt Road that ran through their gates and into their mountain home.

'The Rohirrim name that peak Dwimorberg, Haunted Mountain, and the passages beneath are known now as the Paths of the Dead. None would take that road, but Elrond bid his sons remember it, and that leads me to worry. I know it lies heavily upon Halbarad's mind.

'And yet Nár knows something of it as well. Have we been watched without our knowledge? But how can that be?'The Dúnadan Candaith has a camp near the foot of Weathertop, in the Lone-lands. He must be told the mission of the Grey Company.Candaith is at his camp in the Lone-lands, at the foot of Weathertop.

Elrohir has told you to bring word to the Dúnadan Candaith of the Grey Company's preparations.Talk to Candaith at WeathertopCandaith's hidden cache is north-east of his encampment, atop a small rise called Echad Sûl, in the shade of a tree.

The Ranger Candaith has asked you to recover a hidden cache of supplies he prepared some time ago, as he will have need of its contents on the journey of the Grey Company.Locate Candaith's hidden cache at Echad SûlCandaith's hidden cache at Echad Sûl seems to be gone, and an unsavoury gentleman stands there instead.

Candaith sent you to recover a cache of supplies, but it may now be missing.Talk to Orskdulug at Amon SûlCandaith is at his camp below Weathertop, in the Lone-lands.

You have recovered the supplies from the hidden cache that were taken by Orskdulug and ruined his plan to rob you with them.'The Dúnedain may be scattered across the wilds of Eriador, but my brother and I know the locations of some few, and from those few will you learn where others may be found.

'The Ranger Candaith was a help to us as we scoured the lands to the south following the rage of the Bruinen; we spoke as often as speed would allow on that journey, and he had many interesting tales to tell of the Lone-lands. You will find his camp there, at the base of Amon Sûl...of Weathertop.

'Tell him that Aragorn has need of his kin. Candaith will be certain to join the Grey Company, for his loyalty to his chieftain cannot be questioned.''So the time has come at last to stand with Aragorn son of Arathorn, my lord and the chieftain of the Dúnedain. In my heart I knew the day would come, for as the shadows have lengthened, the need for Aragorn to accept his mantle has become all the more pressing.

'I have come to love the lands between The Forsaken Inn and the Last Bridge, and it will grieve me to leave them, but I will do so; I will join the Grey Company and travel south as you have asked.

'While I make ready to leave, will you retrieve a hidden cache of supplies I prepared some time ago? I left a sword, some gloves, and a few other supplies north-east of here, atop a small rise called Echad Sûl, in the shade of a tree. Recover this cache and bring it back to me, for I will have need of it in the lands to the south.''You will find the hidden cache north-east of here, atop a small rise called Echad Sûl, in the shade of a tree. Recover its contents and bring it to me, and I will go forth to meet the Grey Company.''You say that someone may have found the hidden cache and taken its contents for himself? Confront this man, if you would, ${PLAYER}. Some of those items were of great sentimental value to me, and I would have them returned if possible. I understand that what is left in the wild is free to any who chance upon it, but perhaps this man can be persuaded to return the contents of the cache.''The Dúnadan Candaith has a camp at the foot of Weathertop, in the Lone-lands. Bring him word of the Grey Company's need for him, and he will surely pay it heed.''What are you looking at? It seems to me that these lands should be big enough for you to find a path that takes you out of my sight, little ${RACE}.'

Orskdulug follows your gaze and smirks.

'You like this sword? Found it, I did, and these gloves and a few choice other items. Sometimes you find these little caches in the wild, left by Rangers, I suspect. Easy pickings, if you ask me.

'Looks like you might be easy pickings, too. I'll be taking your gear and your coins, friend. Without a fight, or with one...it is all the same to me!''I would have been willing to part with the contents of the cache, ${PLAYER}; items hidden in the wild, once found, should go to their finders. But once Orskdulug sought to use my items to rob you, as far as I am concerned, he gave up his right to them.

'I am pleased to have them back. Thank you.'The Grey Company prepares to depart from Rivendell for the wide southern lands and needs your help to make sure that all is ready.Golodir, Lothrandir, Radanir, and Elrohir are preparing to depart in and around the stables of Rivendell.

Halbarad has asked you to check with several members of the Grey Company about their final preparations.Talk to GolodirTalk to LothrandirTalk to ElrohirTalk to RadanirCandaith is outside the gate by the stables in Rivendell.

You have spoken with several of the Rangers of the Grey Company in Rivendell and should now speak with Candaith.Talk to Candaith in Rivendell, outside the gate by the stables'All is nearly ready, ${PLAYER}. While I finish the final preparations for our departure, I ask that you speak with some of those who will accompany us south. Ensure that they are prepared for the road and rested enough for the first leg of our journey.

'I saw Golodir just to the north-east some minutes ago, speaking with Elrohir. Radanir was just to the east of the two of them, beneath the foot-bridge.

'Lothrandir is just across the way, speaking to his horse Mithúl. He knows a great deal about the lands through which we must pass. Ask him if he has any further information about the road which may help us move with haste. Once you have spoken with those four, find Candaith by the stables gate and speak to him about what lies ahead.''The road south will be long. We must ride swiftly and be wary of its dangers.''It is almost time to go, ${PLAYER}. Our road leads south.''My horse Legimbod is rested from the ride out of Angmar, and he is ready to undertake the next leg of the journey. His saddlebags are as light as I could make them, while still keeping the supplies we will need.'

Golodir takes a small pendant from one of his pockets and idly passes it from one hand to the other as he looks about the yard.

'This refuge has not changed in the many years since I last came here, but surely I, myself, must be unrecognizable. So much has happened, and so little of it good. But the ride here was a marvel, and I could feel the weight of the past falling from my shoulders with the feel of the breeze upon my face. Legimbod delighted in the speed, and I delighted with him! The southward journey will do both of us some good. I am ready to be off again.''Mithúl is ready for the next leg of the journey, ${PLAYER}. Indeed, his kind may be more prepared for what awaits us than the others of this Company! After all, Mithúl's eyes will be to the road underfoot, watching for loose stones and wind-blown branches; the eyes of the Dúnedain must be to the horizon, keeping watch for foes that may seek to slow our errand.

'I have spoken with Halbarad and the sons of Elrond about the road we will take: south through the Trollshaws and into Eregion, there to rest at Echad Eregion before passing south into Enedwaith.

'A number of Dunlending tribes called the area home when last I journeyed those lands. Some were friendly to outsiders, but most were not. The Draig-lûth, the Hebog-lûth... they are those you would be wise not to cross, and I will do my best to ensure the Grey Company does not entangle with them.''My father had some curious words for me on the eve of this, our departure for the southern lands, ${PLAYER}.'

Elrohir gazes down the hill, deep in thought.

'He said that the time is approaching when we can no longer hold back, when we must do more than defend against the actions of the Enemy. He said that the time is coming when we may need to wager everything on a final throw, and by so doing we will have done all that we can. Better a death in far off lands in the service of a chance, however small, than death at home a certainty.

'We may not return from this journey, ${PLAYER}. My brother and I are at peace with that. I hope that you and these Dúnedain are as well, for I would counsel any who are not to stay behind.''Glorengúr did not break a sweat in the short ride from Thorenhad here to Rivendell. He was ready to begin even yesterday.

'I could say the same for myself. I returned to Thorenhad to say good-bye to Elweleth, but I could not find her anywhere at the camp. I had meant to return this hairpin she had given me, but perhaps she feared the farewells. I suppose I did as well.

'We will soon set forth. I do not know what the future will bring, but I will do my duty with no complaint, and Glorengúr will do the same.''Are the words of my father correct? Is the time coming when we must no longer defend against the Enemy's moves, but strike against him with all the strength we can muster?''Legimbod is ready, and so am I. Together we will uphold our duty to Aragorn, our chief.''When will this standard fly, I wonder? And what will that day signify for the Dúnedain?''This may be a perilous road, especially once we reach the plains of Enedwaith. Many of the Dunlending tribes will be hostile to us.''It will be many days before we come to Aragorn. May we be not late!''Everyone is nearly ready to ride: the Dúnedain, the sons of Elrond, and ${PLAYER}, loyal ${RACE}.

'Are you familiar with our history, ${PLAYER}? As you are joining us on this journey, surely to go down in the annals of the Dúnedain, I would have you hear some of it, if you have not all ready.

'We have some moments. Let me tell you some of our history, that you may understand the weight of that which we are about to do.'Nona, the Dunlending girl you helped beneath Zudrugund, has been mourning the death of her brother Wadu, and Candaith wants to make sure she is comforted.Nona is in the north-eastern corner of Lhanuch.

Candaith has asked you to check on Nona to see how she is dealing with the death of her brother.Talk to Nona in LhanuchWadu's favourite tree is on a rise west of Lhanuch, overlooking the ox-pens.

Nona has asked you to recover the Rohirrim sword she left at Wadu's favourite tree. It was found with Wadu's body and she thought to leave it there as a remembrance, but has changed her mind.Recover the sword from Wadu's favourite treeNona is in the north-eastern corner of Lhanuch.

You have recovered the sword from Wadu's favourite tree and should now bring it to Nona as she requested.Halbarad is in Lhanuch, among the huts on the western side of the village.

Nona has asked to accompany the Grey Company when it departs Lhanuch and travels south.Talk to Halbarad in Lhanuch'These worries endanger our haste; we must not tarry overlong. But I would not forget the pains of those we have encountered along the way.

'The Uch-lûth girl who helped us as we came to Lhanuch: Nona. She lost her brother Wadu, ambushed by a hostile Dunlending clan, and she must be feeling the grief most keenly. Please go to her in the north-east corner of Lhanuch and lend whatever comfort you can.

'I do not want the Grey Company to wear blinders like those Erebrandir wore in his youth, before he was befriended by Saeradan. We must be aware of the hurts happening around us and do what we can to help.''We need the friendship of the Uch-lûth if we are to pass peacefully through Enedwaith into Dunland. Please check on Nona and see how she is dealing with the death of her brother. She is in the north-eastern corner of Lhanuch.''What is it you want?'

Nona laughs, a harsh and humourless sound.

'Concern? Do not be concerned for me. I was lost after the death of my brother, but that was before I learned of the glory he had attained before his death. Those who slew him were right to do so from hiding; they must have been able to sense that he had become a great warrior and would have bested them.

'There was a tree Wadu loved, atop a rise by the ox-pens, west of Lhanuch. I left his sword there, to honour his memory, but I think he would rather I used it to attain even half the glory with it he did. Go to the tree and bring me the sword.''Go to Wadu's favourite tree and recover his sword, ${PLAYER}. I have changed my mind. Wadu would want me to use the sword, as he did. It should not go unused. The tree is on a rise overlooking the ox-pens, west of Lhanuch.''Were you able to offer words of comfort to Nona? She told you to recover Wadu's sword from the tree where she left it? We all manage our grief differently, ${PLAYER}. If that is what she wants, then it should be done. She said the tree is on a rise overlooking the ox-pens, west of Lhanuch? That is where you should go, I expect.''Nona... told me... about the mighty gwiber. The one he... the one my son killed. I did not think he had the spirit for that.'

Suvulch falls silent and does not speak again.'It is very unusual for a Dunlending to have and use a sword of Rohan, ${PLAYER}, but that seems to be the case. I wonder what the story of this sword might be? Well, there is no time to find out; Nona is waiting for it in the north-eastern corner of Lhanuch, correct?'Suvulch remains downcast, lost in thought.'Thank you for bringing me my brother's sword, ${PLAYER}. It was a sword of Rohan, true, but to my eyes it is no longer the weapon of my people's ancient enemies. It is now my brother's sword, the sword with which he slew the mightiest gwiber anyone has ever seen, and I will use it in his memory.'

Nona nods sternly and looks around at the huts of Lhanuch.

'I have a request, ${PLAYER}. Go to the leader of your company, the man called Halbarad, who has spoken at length with Glynn Brenin, and tell him that I will go with your people when they leave Lhanuch. It is my desire to avenge the death of my brother on the enemies of the Uch-lûth and on the Wizard Saruman, and our road lies together for a little while. Your man Halbarad is in Lhanuch, is he not?''The man named Halbarad leads your company, does he not? Tell him I will go with your Rangers when they depart Lhanuch. He is just to the west of here in the village, I am sure of it.''Nona wishes to ride with us when we leave Lhanuch? I do not think Halbarad will allow it. You can ask him, but I feel certain he will refuse. He is among the huts on the western side of the village.''Nona wishes to join your company? It seems that she will, then. There is no man among you of sterner stuff than my daughter, and she will not be refused when she has set her mind to a thing.''What is it? The Uch-lûth woman has a request?'

Halbarad listens as you recount Nona's desire to travel with the Grey Company, but his response is quick and unyielding.

'No, absolutely not. This I must refuse. This is not open for debate.'

Halbarad shakes his head.

'But there is something urgent, ${PLAYER}. Radanir found something.'You have brought word to Candaith that the Grey Company assembles, but now Saeradan in Bree-land must be notified.Saeradan is at his cabin in Bree-land, east of Thornley's Worksite in the Northern Bree-fields.

Candaith has told you to travel next to tell Ranger Saeradan of the Grey Company's road.Talk to Saeradan in Bree-landBarliman Butterbur is the proprietor of The Prancing Pony and can be found by its bar, in the town of Bree.

Saeradan has agreed to travel south with the Grey Company, but he wants you to ask Barliman Butterbur to be careful while the Rangers are gone.Talk to Barliman Butterbur at The Prancing Pony InnCrates of foodstuffs are waiting in one of the parlours of The Prancing Pony.

Barliman has heard the warning you brought from Saeradan, but he seems to have already forgotten it, and has asked you to carry foodstuffs out to the main room of the Pony.Retrieve foodstuffs from a crate in the PonyCrates of foodstuffs are waiting in one of the parlours of The Prancing Pony.

Barliman has heard the warning you brought from Saeradan, but he seems to have already forgotten it, and has asked you to carry foodstuffs out to the main room of the Pony.Retrieve foodstuffs from a crate in the PonyCrates of foodstuffs are waiting in one of the parlours of The Prancing Pony.

Barliman has heard the warning you brought from Saeradan, but he seems to have already forgotten it, and has asked you to carry foodstuffs out to the main room of the Pony.Retrieve foodstuffs from a crate in the PonyBarliman Butterbur is in the main room of The Prancing Pony.

You have carried several loads of foodstuffs from one of the Pony's parlours out to the main room and should speak now to Barliman.Talk to Barliman Buttebur at The Prancing Pony InnUchdrud is sitting at a table in the main room of The Prancing Pony.

Barliman Butterbur has asked you to encourage Uchdrud, an unruly and coarse sort, to vacate the premises.Talk to Uchdrud at The Prancing Pony InnUchdrud is waiting somewhere by the North-gate of Bree for you to answer his challenge.

You did as Butterbur asked and told Uchdrud to finish his beer and leave the Pony, and he reacted angrily, challenging you to meet him outside the North Gate of Bree.Meet Uchdrud outside the North-gate of BreeSaeradan is at his cabin outside the town of Bree.

You have done as Saeradan asked and should now return to him at his cabin.Talk to Saeradan at his cabin'You should next bring your message to Saeradan, in Bree-land. He is more settled than some others of my kin, but no less a vital part of the Grey Company. We will have need of Saeradan's calming wisdom on the road south, for though we have need of haste, so too do we need to keep our wits about us. Too often can the hasty road lead to carelessness. Saeradan will help prevent this.

'Look for him at his cabin near the town of Bree, in Bree-land. He lives east of Thornley's Work-site, in the Northern Bree-fields.'

Candaith gives you a wistful smile.

'It is true, we don't know what we will find as we ride south. I look out over these Lone-lands and wonder what will happen to them when we have gone. But this is something we must do. Each of us has sworn an oath to Aragorn our chief, and none of us will break that oath. We know the risks. None of us would join the Grey Company if we felt its errand was not important enough to brave those risks. For my part, I will not give in to fear of the unknown. We all have our role to play, and I hope only that when I have played mine, the world will have been better for my having been in it.''You must bring your message to Saeradan, in Bree-land. Look for him at his cabin, east of Thornley's Work-site in the Northern Breefields.''I see, ${PLAYER}. I will travel south with as many of the Dúnedain as can be gathered in haste. Aragorn has need of us, and that is all that we need know.'

Saeradan looks wistfully around the yard.

'I fear for the people of this land, ${PLAYER}, without the Dúnedain to protect them. Great numbers of an unruly sort have already come to Bree, and the brigands of the countryside will have no want for members while the Rangers are not around to keep their ranks in check.

'I will make ready to depart. While I do so, will you speak with Barliman at the Prancing Pony? He does not think much of Rangers, but I would have you ask him to take especial care of his Inn and the town for the next several months, if it comes to that. He will not understand, but I want him to have at least some warning that troubles may lie in store for Bree.''Barliman will not understand, as he bears little goodwill for the Rangers, but I want him to be vigilant for any dangers that may arise while the Dúnedain are away in the south. Go now to The Prancing Pony, in Bree, and give him my warning. I hope it will put him on his guard, for dark days may be ahead for Bree.''That fellow Saeradan does come to the Pony from time to time, and we talk about the state of things in Bree. Not as often as I'd like, of course! For every moment of peace there are ten things that need doing, and no way to make them all fit in the time available! Still, I do what I can, and wouldn't trade the Pony for anything!

'Folk say Saeradan's a Ranger, but if he is, he's more respectable than most. Has a cabin just near Bree, you know, and even spares a smile for his neighbours, which is more than I've seen from some others of that kind.

'Will you look at me, going on and on like this! There's no time for idle chatter...there are foodstuffs sitting in one of the parlours that need to be brought out here for preparing. Will you go and fetch some of them? Apples, pears, mushrooms...you'll see the crate, you will, and you'll be a big help to me if you hurry!''You'll find the stack of crates in one of the parlours, ${PLAYER}. Bring me the foodstuffs you find inside: plates of apples, pears, mushrooms, and a pumpkin. You may need to take a few trips!''It's a start, but that's not all of it, ${PLAYER}! Return to the crates in the parlour and bring me some more of the foodstuffs you find within.''One more trip should do it, ${PLAYER}. There should be a pumpkin in one of those crates in the parlour, so you just run that out here and we should be all set, and a job well done it will be!''Thank you! You've been a great help to me today, and I won't forget it. So much to do, so very much to do, but you're welcome to stay as long as you like. Join the company, sing a song, warm yourself by the fire!'

Barliman pauses and his eyes pass briefly over the crowd.

'There is one more thing, actually, ${PLAYER},' he says in a hushed tone.

'There have been a few rude types come to Bree lately, and they've been scaring off some of the townsfolk who like to come to the Pony of a night; regular visitors, you know, good folk who come by for a spot of ale and some cheer.

'Do you think you might have a word with the fellow at that table there? Uchdrud, I think his name is, and he's a coarse sort. Maybe you can encourage him to finish his mug of beer and find somewhere to go for the night? Only he hasn't rented any lodgings here, and I'd just as soon he took his loud friends somewhere else. I would tell him myself, but there's so much to do....''I don't want you to make a scene, but I'd just as soon have this Uchdrud out of the Pony. He's loud and coarse and is scaring the rest of the guests! He's at that table over there.''What did you say to me? I have as much a right to this table as anyone here. More so, once the rest of my friends get here! Oh, there will be changes around here, I don't mind telling you.'

Uchdrud scowls at you.

'Are you still here? You've made me mad, and now you're going to pay for it! I will see you outside, by the North-gate of Bree, if you have any courage at all. We won't be bothered there. I will teach you a lesson about respecting Uchdrud and his boys...a lesson this whole town is going to have to learn!''I am sorry you have gotten tangled up with this, ${PLAYER}, but this sort of thing has gotten more and more common lately! What is happening to Bree these days?

'Uchdrud says he'll teach you a lesson outside the North-gate of Bree? I don't think he knows what he's gotten himself into, ${PLAYER}, but be careful. You never know how dangerous that sort of fellow might be, bark or bite!''Are you still here? I will first finish this beer, and then I will meet you outside the North-gate of Bree to teach you a lesson you won't soon forget!''I didn't think you'd show, ${PLAYER}. We're going to own this town in a couple weeks, and that fat innkeeper in there will have to beg for table-scraps from Uchdrud and his boys.

'Just like you'll need to beg for mercy in a few moments, ${RACE}. I may even grant it!''What is happening to Bree? So many coarse and unruly sorts, like that Uchdrud, are coming to the town. There will be trouble come from it, mark my words! Ah, we will weather it, we always do. The Pony will be here forever, and a Butterbur will be running it, so long as I have any say in the matter!''I was right to worry, then? I feared it was so. Very few of these people know that the safety they feel is the result of the tireless work of the Dúnedain.

'And yet our road must take us south, into lands far away from the well-meaning proprietor of the Prancing Pony. He may not trust Rangers, but my people are no less fond of him and his simple life than they are of their own sons and daughters. I hope his town and his inn will remain safe until we return...if return we can.'Candaith wishes to tell you some of the history of the Dúnedain so you may recognize the importance of the errand upon which you are about to embark.Candaith is at the stables of Rivendell.

Candaith has offered to tell you of key moments in the history of the Dúnedain.Talk to Candaith in Rivendell and experience 'Session: The House of Isildur' or read the shortened account insteadCandaith is by the stables in Rivendell.

You have listened to Candaith describe some of the history of the Dúnedain and should speak to him now for the conclusion.Talk to Candaith in Rivendell'The early years of the Third Age saw the deaths of Isildur and his eldest sons, but his line was not ended, for his youngest son was kept safely here in Rivendell. He was raised far from danger and came to rule the North Kingdom. His descendents would not know peace, for the Nazgûl were to reappear, and their chief would wage war upon the Men of the West.

'In the year Nineteen Seventy-four of the Third Age, the armies of the Witch-king seized Fornost. The North Kingdom was no more, and its ruler Arvedui fled northward, to Forochel. But there he was lost, along with the heirlooms of his house.

'Some of the Dúnedain journeyed into the icy wastes to recover them. Do you wish to hear of their quest?'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a solo quest and cannot be completed with a fellowship.</rgb>'This is the tale of the Dúnedain that travelled north to Forochel to reclaim the heirlooms of the House of Isildur following the loss of Arvedui.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a solo quest and cannot be completed with a fellowship.</rgb>The history of the House of Isildur is full of dangerous escapes, glorious triumphs, and near brushes with tragedy, but even after so long the line remains strong. Three thousand years after the death of Isildur, Aragorn, son of Arathorn, is prepared to fulfill his destiny...'And so it was that Aragorn, Heir of Isildur through unbroken line down the long years, began his friendship with Gandalf, the Grey Pilgrim. He will be known by the sword that he bears, Andúril, Flame of the West, and by the Ring of Barahir, once lost but since regained.

'It is the task of the Dúnedain, and indeed of all friends of Aragorn, to ensure he safely comes into his birth-right. We will not question if this is the time for his ascension to the kingship. It must be so.

'Ride with us, ${PLAYER}, for we ride into history. Laegened and Haerdor, Corugúr and Glangon, all are remembered for the parts they played in the great drama of the Age. So it will be with us.'Radanir found a mysterious note from a purported friend: but can Ergothorn Elf-friend be trusted?The mysterious letter is on a crate by Halbarad.

Radanir found a letter addressed 'To the Rangers' stuck in a tree with a knife, and Halbarad wants you to read it and give your opinion on its truthfulness.Read the letter on the crate by Halbarad in LhanuchHalbarad is among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.

You have read the note from Ergothorn and should now speak with Halbarad about it.Talk to HalbaradLhaid Ogo is on the right side of the road that runs through the Lich Bluffs.

Halbarad is not certain that Ergothorn can be trusted, but he wants you to travel to Lhaid Ogo to see if there is any truth to the mysterious letter.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Find the entrance to Lhaid Ogo, in the Lich BluffsSomewhere inside Lhaid Ogo, a messenger from Dunland may be in trouble.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Search Lhaid Ogo for the lost Dunlending messengerGlynn Brenin is in the Brenin's Hall, atop the hill of Lhanuch.

You have rescued the Dunlending messenger and should return to Glynn Brenin to hear his message.Talk to Glynn Brenin in Lhanuch'Radanir found this letter stuck into a nearby tree with a knife, ${PLAYER}. It is addressed 'To the Rangers,' and...well, I have left it on that crate there. Read it and tell me what you think it means.''Anirin has given me the message from Lheu Brenin of Tûr Morva, and it fills me with joy, for it means that the Uch-lûth do not stand alone against the Wizard Saruman. We have some few allies still, and that gives me hope.'To the Rangers:

I am a man of Gondor, a friend of the Elves and of all good folk. Call me Ergothorn. I see the danger growing in the East and wish to oppose it.

Lheu Brenin dispatched a messenger from Tûr Morva in Dunland, but the messenger was beset by foes outside Lhaid Ogo in the Lich Bluffs and was dragged within. If he yet lives, he will have information for you.

I cannot reveal myself to you, but trust that I am a friend. I will contact you again when it is safe to do so.

Ergothorn'Is this Ergothorn a friend? Why will he not show his face? I do not like this, ${PLAYER}. And yet, if he is telling the truth....

'We need to know, ${PLAYER}. Gather allies you can trust and go to Lhaid Ogo, far to the south of here, on the right side of the road through the Lich Bluffs. It may be that a messenger from the south is in trouble, and if so, we could use the information he brings.

'But keep your eyes open. I will not trust Ergothorn without proof. It may be a trap.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'I will be right behind you! Hurry now and lead the way to Glynn Brenin in Lhanuch.'To the Rangers:

I am a man of Gondor, a friend of the Elves and of all good folk. Call me Ergothorn. I see the danger growing in the East and wish to oppose it.

Lheu Brenin dispatched a messenger from Tûr Morva in Dunland, but the messenger was beset by foes outside Lhaid Ogo in the Lich Bluffs and was dragged within. If he yet lives, he will have information for you.

I cannot reveal myself to you, but trust that I am a friend. I will contact you again when it is safe to do so.

ErgothornTo the Rangers:

I am a man of Gondor, a friend of the Elves and of all good folk. Call me Ergothorn. I see the danger growing in the East and want to oppose it.

Lheu Brenin dispatched a messenger from Tûr Morva in Dunland, but the messenger was beset by foes outside Lhaid Ogo in the Lich Bluffs and was dragged within. If he yet lives, he will have information for you.

I cannot reveal myself to you, but trust that I am a friend. I will contact you again when it is safe to do so.

Ergothorn'Read the letter and tell me what you think, ${PLAYER}. I have left it on the crate there for you.''If you find a messenger from the south in Lhaid Ogo, we will know that Ergothorn was telling the truth...about this matter, at least.

'Gather allies you can trust and go to Lhaid Ogo, on the right side of the road that runs south through the Lich Bluffs.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'You found Lhaid Ogo? You need to go inside and search for the Dunlending messenger, if such a messenger exists. I have still not made up my mind about Ergothorn. Return at once to the entrance to Lhaid Ogo, south of here on the right side of the road that runs through the Lich Bluffs!''Ergothorn steered us true on this matter, but I do not give my trust so easily. Truth often comes before a lie, the better to ensure the lie go undetected.'Saeradan has sent you to bring word of the Grey Company's errand to Halros, a Ranger currently watching over the Shire.Halros has a camp in the Greenfields, west of Bree-land, in the Shire.

Saeradan has told you where to find the Ranger Halros and has warned you that Halros may find it difficult to leave the Shire, even for so important an errand.Talk to Halros in the GreenfieldsTrouble-makers can be found on the road through Green Hill Country, and in the village of Stock, in the Shire.

Halros has asked you to walk the road through Green Hill Country and to visit the town of Stock to deal with the bold trouble-makers that have appeared there.Deal with the trouble-maker in the Green Hill CountryDeal with the trouble-maker in StockHalros has a camp in the Greenfields, in the north-east corner of the Shire.

You have discouraged the trouble-makers in the Green Hill Country and Stock, as Halros asked, and should return now to the Ranger.Talk to Halros in the GreenfieldsHalros has a camp in the Greenfields, in the north-east corner of the Shire.

Halros has asked for your help in making the decision whether to travel south with the Grey Company or to maintain his vigil over the Shire.'I will take it upon myself to gather several others of the Dúnedain that may be found within Bree-land, but you should make your way to the Shire. At a camp by the Greenfields, you should find Halros.

'Halros loves the Shire and its people and has watched over them for many years. It may not be easy for him to leave them, even upon an errand as urgent as that of the Grey Company.''Welcome, ${PLAYER}. So long as there are goblins beneath the Greenfields, I will maintain my watch over the Shire. That is my duty, given to me by my chieftain, Aragorn.'

Halros listens intently as you tell him of the need for the Grey Company to assemble and ride south.

'I see, ${PLAYER}. I...I need time to prepare. I had been just about to deal with a particular matter when you arrived. If you can take care of it for me, that will give me time to set things in order.

'There are trouble-makers loose on the road that runs through the Green Hill Country. I fear they may do some real harm before they are stopped. Can you walk the road and visit the town of Stock, ending the threat of the trouble-makers there? I fear they may be brigands, or worse, and these are more bold than they have been in the past. Return then to me and we can talk again.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. There have been more trouble-makers on the road in recent days, and I fear they will do some harm to the peace-loving folk of the Shire if I travel south with the Grey Company.

'And yet, my loyalty is to Aragorn, and I know that if he has need of the Dúnedain, it is my duty to go to him with as much speed as can be mustered!

'I do not know what to do, ${PLAYER}. What do you advise? Can you help me make this decision?''Halros loves the Shire and its people, ${PLAYER}. He may be reluctant to join the Grey Company.

'Find him at his camp among the Greenfields, in the north-east corner of the Shire.''I am worried about this recent increase in trouble-makers in the Shire. Deal with them along the road through the Green Hill Country and in Stock, then return to me and we can discuss things.''You'd better be careful, you! I've got friends making plans in the hills around here, and these halflings aren't included in 'em! You'll be in for a fight if you mess with me and my gang!''Hey! I've got orders from Up Top that say I can do what I like here in these lands, and I plan to do just that! I fancy this house, and no ${RACE} is going to stand in my way, neither!

'It's too small to live in, but I could use it to store the haul my boys plan on getting in the next several weeks. You'd best move along if you know what's good for you!'The first leg of the Grey Company's journey will take them south through the Trollshaws and into Eregion.Halbarad is at the stables of Rivendell.

Candaith has asked you to speak with Halbarad to see if he has any final words before you join the rest of the Rangers on their ride south.Talk to Halbarad at the stables in RivendellThe Grey Company begins its ride south. Halbarad has determined that the Rangers will rest at Gwingris, in Eregion.

You should ride south to Gwingris, either on your own mount or on one engaged from Rivendell.Talk to Saeradan at Gwingris, in Eregion, after riding southLondan should still be in Eregion. He left Gwingris heading east.

Saeradan has asked you to find the farrier Londan and convince him to return to Gwingris so that he may repair Erebrandir's shoe.Find Londan the farrier and talk to himLondan the farrier will be waiting for you back at Gwingris, in Eregion.

You have rescued Londan from the Sharkúnakh uruks that he encountered and should return to Gwingris.Talk to Londan the farrier, who will be waiting for you back at GwingrisSaeradan is at Gwingris, in Eregion.

The farrier Londan has thanked you for your aid, and he has asked you to speak with Saeradan.Talk to Saeradan at Gwingris'Some of the Dúnedain have already begun to ride, ${PLAYER}. Speak with Halbarad at the stables to see if he has any advice before the rest of us depart.''I have fit a new shoe to Saeradan's horse, ${PLAYER}. He will have no problems now.

'I wonder if the same can be said for the few who live in these lands. Those uruks spoke as if more would be coming, and we have no reason to doubt them. There has been much more activity among those creatures, many more of them on the roads in recent weeks. I do not like it.

'Well, give Saeradan my thanks for sending you my way, friend ${RACE}. I would have been in a great deal of trouble if you had not come along when you had, and the price of a new horse-shoe is a small price to pay indeed!''The first leg of our journey is complete, but not without incident: my horse, Erebrandir, has drawn up lame. There is something wrong with one of his shoes, and he cannot go on unless something is done.

'Halbarad says I should just engage another horse, but I have been speaking to the Elves here and they have given me another idea. Apparently a skilled farrier has recently passed through Gwingris. This farrier, Londan by name, could certainly repair Erebrandir's shoes.

'I understand that Londan left Gwingris heading east. If you find him and convince him to return, we will have been delayed but little.''Erebrandir is ready to go once more! His new shoe fits perfectly, a testament to the skill of Londan. I am worried about these uruks, though. If it is true that many of the creatures have come north through Enedwaith into Eregion, our ride south may quickly become more difficult than it has been to this point.

'Are they solely here for plunder, or is there another purpose that drives them?

'Londan gave to me the shoe he took from Erebrandir. It is yours, if you want it; some people believe a shoe such as this brings good fortune. I do not think that is the case, but I leave you to make up your own mind.''Our preparations are complete, and some of the Dúnedain have already mounted their horses and begun to ride. You do the same or engage one of the horses at these stables, and we will be off!

'Lothrandir and I have chosen a course that will take us south in several legs, the first of which leads south through the Trollshaws and into Eregion.

'We will meet up at Gwingris, in Eregion, ${PLAYER}, and we will have a rest.''There are many more uruks in this land than there ever were before, ${PLAYER}. Tell Saeradan to use caution if he and his fellows insist on riding south.''Londan left Gwingris heading east. He is surely still in Eregion and could find time to repair Erebrandir's shoe if you are able to find him without delay.''Thank you for finding Londan, ${PLAYER}! Both I and Erebrandir thank you, for he has been able to fit my Lonely Wanderer with a new shoe. You should speak with him.''Many of our friends have already departed. Speak with Halbarad at the stables here to see if he has any final words for you.''Ride south into Eregion, ${PLAYER}, on your own mount or on one engaged from Rivendell. We will all gather again for a rest at Gwingris.''Many of our friends have already departed. I am about to go.''I am about to ride south with the rest of the Grey Company. You too should ride now!''Careful, stranger! There is a group of uruks in these parts, of great size and strength. I saw them not two hours ago.

'They were passing north, and though I could understand little of their cruel speech, it seems to me they were seeking treasure. There are ruins throughout these lands, you know. These uruks would like nothing better than a chance at plunder.'

Londan listens to your account of Saeradan's plight and agrees to tend to the needs of his horse Erebrandir. His face then freezes in a mask of fear.

'The uruks! They're coming back! Prepare to fight!'The ride of the Grey Company continues, with the Dúnedain travelling further south into Eregion.The next leg of the journey will take the Grey Company to Echad Eregion, and it is there that they will meet up with you again.Talk to Radanir at Echad EregionFúsi Gem-cutter is at Echad Eregion.

Radanir has asked you to speak with Fúsi Gem-cutter about his serious problem, a problem which has brought the Grey Company to a halt.Talk to Fúsi Gem-cutter at Echad EregionFúsi Gem-cutter's father Fúni was attacked somewhere east of Echad Eregion.

Fúsi has asked you to find the site of the attack and determine where his father has been taken by the Ninkoth uruk.Find the site of the attack on Fúsi and Fúni Gem-cutterThe tracks at the fallen pack lead east. It is in that direction that Fúni Gem-cutter must have been taken.

You have learned in what direction the Ninkoth uruks have taken Fúni Gem-cutter and should follow the tracks.Find information about the Ninkoth uruks east of the fallen pack'The rest of the Dúnedain have gone on ahead. They wait now for us!

'We will meet up with the Company for the next leg of our journey further south of here, at Echad Eregion. May the wind at your back speed you onward! Erebrandir and I will see how stands up the work of Londan.

'Until we meet again at Echad Eregion, ${PLAYER}.''Ride forth to Echad Eregion, ${PLAYER}, and we will all meet up again for the next leg of the journey.''We have called a halt, ${PLAYER}, and it may be for longer than we originally intended. We have received a call for aid from a dwarf coming north from the White Mountains, and Halbarad has determined it to be serious enough to justify a stop.

'The dwarf's name is Fúsi -- Fúsi Gem-cutter -- and he will speak to you about his problem. He will speak to anyone, it seems!''Fúsi Gem-cutter is here at Echad Eregion, and his problem is serious enough that Halbarad has extended our halt here. Talk to Fúsi and see what he needs.''Yes, who are you? You are no Ranger, I can see that. It is an outrage, I tell you, when dwarves cannot take the road north from the White Mountains without fear of harassment!

'There we were, my father and I, when out from behind one of the large stones you see scattered everywhere in these lands jumps the largest uruk you've ever seen. He beat his chest with a gloved hand, shouted the name of his clan -- I'll never forget it -- then knocked out my father Fúni and dragged him off!'

You ask him in what direction the Sharkúnakh uruk took his father, and his brow furrows with puzzlement.

'Sharkúnakh? No, no. The name he shouted was Ninkoth. In all the confusion I can't remember what direction he went, but if you can find the site of the attack, which was to the east of here, you may see some tracks or something.''If you can find this Ninkoth uruk, you may be able to find my father! We were attacked somewhere to the east of here!''Halbarad wants us to help this dwarf Fúsi. I do not know why he thinks it so important.''You found tracks leading east from the site of the attack? Why have you not yet followed them, ${PLAYER}? It is the least you can do, is it not? I say that it is! I do indeed!''I do not know why Halbarad is so intent on helping these dwarves, ${PLAYER}. I cannot begrudge it of him, even if it seems needless, given our haste. You should follow the tracks at the site of the attack to the east.''Hail to you, ${RACE}! You are in pursuit of the uruks? Your trail ends here, I am afraid: the ruins of Minas Elendúr that you see atop the cliffs here are crawling with the creatures, and with worse besides!

'Still you intend to scour the ruins? You are brave, my friend! If I cannot dissuade you, I at least will tell you what I have learned of the place. I will not myself journey there again, not for any sum! There are strange doings going on inside Minas Elendúr and its cursed cellars, and I have seen enough to last me!Now that Halros has made his decision, it is time to gather more of the Dúnedain to join the ranks of the Grey Company on its mission.The Ranger Daervunn can be found in Esteldín, the hidden Ranger encampment in the North Downs.

Halros has told you to next bring your message to Halbarad, but since the Ranger is often away seeing to the elimination of threats, you should speak first to his second-in-command, the Ranger Daervunn.Talk to Daervunn at EsteldínHalbarad is in Tham Úmdúr, in the shadow of Ost Crithlanc, far to the east of Esteldín, in Nan Amlug East.

Daervunn told you that Halbarad has gone to investigate a cave where foes fleeing from Carn Dûm following the defeat of the False King hope to create a new Angmar. If you are to enlist Halbarad's help as part of the Grey Company, you will need to travel to Tham Úmdúr.Talk to Halbarad at Tham ÚmdúrThree stone idols of great significance to the Angmarim can be found throughout Tham Úmdúr.

Halbarad has already destroyed three of the idols, but three remain, and he wants you to deal with them.Destroy statue of AmarthielDestroy statue of the Witch-kingDestroy statue of MordirithHalbarad can be found near the entrance to Tham Úmdúr, in the North Downs.

You have destroyed three of the idols prized by the Angmarim and should return now to Halbarad, as he asked.Talk to Halbarad at Tham ÚmdúrDaervunn is in Esteldín, the hidden Ranger-camp in the North Downs.

Halbarad has sent you back to tell Daervunn to gather as many Rangers for the Grey Company as can be spared without jeopardizing the safety of the people of the North Downs.Talk to Daervunn at Esteldín'My mind is made up, ${PLAYER}, and now it is time for you to continue with the task Lord Elrond of Rivendell has set before you: to gather more Dúnedain to swell the ranks of the Grey Company as it travels south to the aid of Aragorn.

'Your next journey should take you to Esteldín, the hidden encampment of the Rangers in the North Downs. Halbarad will surely wish to travel south with the Grey Company, but he is often away from Esteldín; speak with the Ranger Daervunn instead, and he will tell you where Halbarad may be found.

'Daervunn is often in the centre of Esteldín, seeing to the general defence of the people who live in the North Downs.''For long years have the Dúnedain wandered in the wilderness, ${PLAYER}. Your message confirms what I have long suspected: the hour is near at hand when my people must come forth. I will ride to the aid of my friend and chieftain, Aragorn son of Arathorn, and let no Man or beast stand in the path of the Grey Company!

'There is evil still here, ${PLAYER}, and if we Dúnedain leave Eriador to fend for itself, what then? We win little if, when all the deeds of the Age are done, the northern lands are lost to evil, their peoples destroyed.

'Daervunn told you why I have come to this place? Already I have destroyed three idols the Angmarim brought out of Carn Dûm, but I believe three remain. Destroy these statues of their most-prized leadership and the Angmarim here may abandon hope of raising Angmar into prominence again. Return to me when you have done so, and we can speak again.''You have completed what I started, ${PLAYER}, and I am grateful for that! You have my thanks, too, for bringing me word of Aragorn's need for the Dúnedain. We will ride to him with all the haste that can be mustered, through whatever perils await.

'Return now to Daervunn, in Esteldín, with this command: he is to select as many of the Rangers of that camp as he feels can be spared without jeopardizing the safety of the people of the North Downs. We ride to Aragorn, but I will not allow us to dismiss our responsibilities in Eriador.''You bring worrisome tidings, ${PLAYER}! What danger has Aragorn encountered in the distant south that makes the Dúnedain his closest allies? Perhaps I misspeak. Perhaps it is not that the Dúnedain are closest, but instead that they be the most true.

'Halbarad must hear what you have to say, but he left this morning to deal with a serious danger. Word came down to us that a number of foes fleeing from Angmar in the days following the defeat of the False King sought to establish a settlement in a cave in the North Downs.

'Halbarad travelled to this cave, now called Tham Úmdúr, to attempt to forestall or defeat these foes before they can use the relics they plundered from Carn Dûm to make the foundations of a new Angmar. Find Halbarad at Tham Úmdúr, in the shadow of Ost Crithlanc, far to the east of Esteldín, in Nan Amlug East, and give him your message. Help him end their plans before they begin.''I will do as Halbarad wishes. I too do not wish to endanger the people of the North Downs by our absence.

'I will speak with the rest of my kin here at Esteldín. Other lands wait for your message.''I have already destroyed three of the idols the Angmarim brought out of Carn Dûm, but there are more of them. I leave these to you as I rest from my efforts. Return to me when they have been destroyed.''You will find Daervunn still in Esteldín, I expect. Tell him to gather as many Rangers as can be spared without jeopardizing the safety of the people of the North Downs.

'I will return shortly.''Halbarad went to Tham Úmdúr, a cave located in the shadow of Ost Crithlanc, far to the east of Esteldín, in Nan Amlug East, where foes fleeing Carn Dûm following the defeat of the False King hope to lay the foundations for a new Angmar. You should go to him at once, for the gathering of the Grey Company is of great importance, and he will wish to be a part of it!''I know that Halbarad will wish to travel with the Grey Company, but he is often away from Esteldín. Speak with Daervunn there, his second-in-command, to learn the current location of Halbarad.'The messenger from Dunland has given hope to Glynn Brenin that other Dunlending clans are standing against Saruman.The Brehur and Brehures can be found in the Brenin's Hall in Lhanuch.

Glynn Brenin has asked you to speak with his advisors about the words of Anirin the messenger and about the events of his rescue.Halbarad is among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.

You have spoken with the Brehur and Brehures of Lhanuch and should return to Halbarad with their words as Glynn Brenin wished.Talk to Halbarad in LhanuchNona is in the north-eastern corner of the Dunlending village of Lhanuch.

Halbarad has remembered that you did not bring his refusal to Nona and wants you to tell her that he cannot allow her to travel with the Grey Company when they depart.Talk to Nona in Lhanuch'My Brehur and Brehures have heard the messenger Anirin's words. They will decide what must be done in this matter of Saruman.

'Speak with them and hear what they have to say. They will wish to know the events that led you to find this stray messenger, as well, so leave nothing out. They will decide, too, what course of action to recommend to your people.

'Once you have heard my Brehur and Brehures, bring their advice to your leader Halbarad. He and I have spoken already on this, and their council will complete the argument.''I have spoken with your leader Halbarad about these matters. Once you have heard the wisdom of my Brehur and Brehures, bring their words to him.''Your people, these Rangers, are duvodiad, but unlike others I have seen. This man Halbarad reminds me of Glynn Brenin: stern, willing to listen to the advice of others, but given to decisive action when it is necessary.

'Know that the road through Dunland will not be safe for you. It is good that Lheu Brenin still stands against the Wizard Saruman, but there are other clans in Dunland that do not have leaders as strong, and for them, the promises of the Wizard may be hard to refuse. Tell your people to be wary on the road south.''I am pleased by Anirin's message from Lheu Brenin. If Tûr Morva still stands against the Wizard, we must remain strong in our resolve as well!

'It is good to know that some of our brothers in Dunland have not believed the promises of Saruman. We are not without friends, as was my worry.''I spoke against the promises of Saruman for many days! That was the course of wisdom. How few of the clans listened to my advice? Too few.

'But Anirin brings us hope from Dunland. He will return to Tûr Morva with word that Lhanuch also stands against Saruman; let that strengthen our cousins' resolve!''Are all duvodiad so trusting? How do you know this man Ergothorn is a friend? You know nothing about him beyond what he wishes to tell you, and that not even in person!

'I say "he," but you know not even that! Ergothorn could be a woman, and that could be the least of her deceptions!

'It matters not to me that the messenger Anirin was found where Ergothorn said he would be. One truth is nothing.''The words of my Brehur and Brehures should be heeded. Bring their advice to your leader Halbarad.''Anirin's tidings are welcome, but not all of the clans of Dunland will have the strength of will to stand against the wishes of the Wizard. If Saruman seeks to dominate the Dunlending clans and command them in battle, he will not remain opposed by many.''There is glory in standing strong to the end, ${PLAYER}, though that end be a sad one. We will stand with our brothers in the south against the threats of the Wizard Saruman.''The Wizard Saruman will never command the Uch-lûth! We will stand against him, no matter the cost!''I trust no man I cannot see. If Ergothorn will not meet you duvodiad face to face, you should not listen to his words. Tear them up, unread, and you will be the happier for it.''I understand the concerns of the Brehur and Brehures, ${PLAYER}; they are fair points. When we depart for Dunland, we cannot let our caution wane for even an instant.

'The business with Ergothorn's letter drove from my mind the request of the Dunlending girl. Go now to Nona and tell her that I cannot allow her to travel with the Grey Company. She is here in Lhanuch, and here she will stay.''Nona will be disappointed, but she must understand that there is no place for her on our journey south. Speak with her here in Lhanuch and tell her this.''Your leader Halbarad does not know what he says! Too dangerous? He is a fool if he thinks I cannot stand with the best man in his company!

'A fool, then. We will see who has the greater glory, when the tally of battle is finished. It will be Nona of the Uch-lûth, sister to Wadu Gwiber-slayer!'Daervunn has sent you to bring word of the Grey Company's mission to Calenglad and the Rangers of Evendim.Calenglad is at Tinnudir, the Ranger-camp on the shores of Lake Evendim.

Daervunn has sent you to enlist the aid of Calenglad and the Rangers of Evendim for the Grey Company.Talk to Calenglad at TinnudirCalenglad is at Tinnudir, the Ranger-camp on the shores of Lake Evendim.

Calenglad is waiting to speak with you about taking one more journey into Annúminas to rescue some relics from the Angmarim and tomb-robbers that seek to plunder them.Talk to Calenglad and travel with him to the Flooded WardCalenglad is at Tinnudir, the Ranger-camp on the shores of Lake Evendim.

You have done as Calenglad asked, and in his own way, he has bade farewell to the lands of Evendim. He is ready now to travel with the Grey Company, and you should speak with him.Talk to Calenglad at Tinnudir'I do not know whether he will wish to join the ranks of the Grey Company on their southward journey, but Calenglad should be told of the mission all the same. He feels a very strong bond to the lands around Lake Evendim, and to the ruins of Annúminas, and may not be able to bring himself to leave.

'Bring him the message, in the event I misjudge his temperament. You will find him at Tinnudir, the Ranger-camp on the shores of Lake Evendim. If he does join the company, he will be a valuable ally on the southern road.''You will find Calenglad at Tinnudir, the Ranger-camp on the shores of Lake Evendim. I do not know if he will decide to join the Grey Company on their southern road, but if he does he will be a valuable ally.''You say that Aragorn has need of the Dúnedain? I wondered if this day would come. We have safe-guarded Annúminas and the lands around Lake Evendim for so long now that it seemed we would do so forever. To be told, thus, that it comes to an end, and we must travel into distant lands to the south....'

Calenglad gazes for a moment out at the clear waters of Lake Evendim.

'I will go with the Grey Company. That is my duty, and I will not shy away from it. But first I want to save some of the relics of Annúminas from being plundered. Angmarim still haunt the city, leaderless though they be, and in the past few weeks they have allied themselves with numerous tomb-robbers to strip the city of any relics they can find. Let us recover such relics from one of the wards while still we can. Speak with me when you are ready to go.''I will go with the Grey Company, but first I want to clear one of the wards of Annúminas of relics that would be plundered by the Angmarim and their tomb-robber allies if we must leave.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'That is the way of it, ${PLAYER}. Some part of my heart will remain always within the walls of Annúminas, though duty carry me far away. It will be up to others, now, to safe-guard the places... and the people... that I hold dear.

'I will ride to Aragorn, and let the future hold what it may... I will face it unblinking.'Nona is not pleased that Halbarad has refused her request to travel south with the Grey Company and is also angry with her father Suvulch.You last saw Suvulch at Wadu's favourite tree, on a rise by the ox-pens, west of Lhanuch.

Nona is angry at her father Suvulch for his cruel treatment of Wadu while Wadu was still alive, and she wants you to tell Suvulch so.Find Suvulch somewhere near LhanuchHostile Dunlendings can be found throughout Enedwaith.

Suvulch has challenged you to a competition to see who can defeat the most Dunlendings of enemy clans in honour of Wadu.Defeat hostile Dunlendings throughout EnedwaithSuvulch is by Wadu's favourite tree, on a rise by ox-pens west of Lhanuch, and hostile Dunlendings can be found throughout Enedwaith.

Suvulch has challenged you to a competition to slay hostile Dunlendings in memory of Wadu.Talk to SuvulchRadanir is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in a grove of trees to the east of the Uch-lûth village of Lhanuch.

Suvulch told you that Radanir was looking for you about an urgent matter.Find Radanir at the grove of trees east of LhanuchA second letter from Ergothorn is on the crate near Radanir, at the camp-site of the Grey Company in the grove of trees east of Lhanuch.

Radanir has found a second letter from Ergothorn and wants you to read it and give your opinion on what the mysterious correspondent has to say.Read the letter on the crate by RadanirRadanir is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in the grove of trees east of Lhanuch.

You have read Ergothorn's second letter, which calls for you to go to the defence of Zudrugund.Talk to Radanir at the grove of trees east of Lhanuch'All men are fools! Your leader Halbarad is a fool for refusing my request, the Brehur are fools for delaying so long in action against our enemies, and my father Suvulch is a fool as well!

'He has been avoiding me since Wadu died. Do you know what I think? I think he is ashamed to show his face for his cruel treatment of Wadu while he still lived. He should feel that shame! It is his alone!

'He should have been kinder to his son while Wadu lived. You find him and tell him so!''My father Suvulch should feel great shame for the way he treated Wadu while he was alive. Find him, wherever he is, and tell him so!''What do you have to say to me, duvodiad?'

Suvulch listens impatiently as you recount Nona's words to him about Wadu.

'You cannot speak to me of this matter, and neither can my daughter! Only one language will we speak: the language of battle.

'You and I will fight hostile Dunlendings of other clans, duvodiad, and we will measure which of us has the authority to judge the other. Do not return until you have slain many of our enemies!''Suvulch has challenged you to a competition to slay the foes of the Uch-lûth? He does this for Wadu?

'It is not enough. He should have done this when my brother was alive.''You have work to do! I will slay more warriors of hostile clans than you can, and I will do it for my son.''You have fought many Dunlendings of other clans. They were the enemies of Wadu, and they are my enemies. I am pleased you have done this.''How many hostile Dunlendings have you slain?'

Suvulch listens to your count and nods.

'My count is higher! Yet it does not matter, for we have done this for the memory of my son. Let his enemies rue the day they slew him from ambush and caused us to notice the stink of them.

'Shortly after I returned from battle, one of your Rangers spoke with me. He said his name was Radanir, and he wanted to speak with you. You should find him and see what he wants of you. He said he would be at the duvodiad camp among the trees to the east of my village.

'Thank you for the competition, ${PLAYER}. It is not enough to answer for my son's life, maybe, but it is something.''My father can do what he likes in memory of Wadu, but it will never be enough for me. He is simply too late.''It was a good competition, and the foes of Wadu surely regret their treachery. The Ranger named Radanir is looking for you. You should go see him. He said he would be at the duvodiad camp among the trees to the east of my village.''There you are! I have been looking everywhere for you! We have received another letter from Ergothorn.

'I left it on that crate there. If he speaks the truth, we have little time to waste!''I have left the letter on that crate there. Read what Ergothorn has to say, but read quickly! If what he says is true, we may not have much time.'To the Rangers:

I do not have much time to write this; I beg you to move with all possible haste. A group of uruks have come out of Dunland, but instead of passing further north as others have done, these have begun the climb into Thrór's Coomb. I fear they have orders involving the dwarves that still live there, especially the one called Nár.

You must defend this dwarf from the uruks! He knows much that, told to the wrong person, could be hurtful to your errand.

ErgothornTo the Rangers:

I do not have much time to write this; I beg you to move with all possible haste. A group of uruks have come out of Dunland, but instead of passing further north as others have done, these have begun the climb into Thrór's Coomb. I fear they have orders involving the dwarves that still live there, especially the one called Nár.

You must defend this dwarf from the uruks! He knows much that, told to the wrong person, could be hurtful to your errand.

Ergothorn'If we can believe the words of Ergothorn, a party of uruks marches toward Zudrugund. If they seize Nár, he could tell them of our errand!

'Or he could tell them nonsense. But I think we have no choice, ${PLAYER}. We have to do something in case Ergothorn is right.'Fúni Gem-cutter has been taken to the abandoned ruins of Minas Elendúr by the Ninkoth uruks.Fúni Gem-cutter is being held somewhere within the depths of Minas Elendúr, far to the east of Echad Eregion.

You have learned the location of Fúni Gem-cutter and should now attempt his rescue.Find Fúni Gem-cutter somewhere within Minas Elendúr'The Elves at Gwingris would not tell me much of these ruins, but they told me enough to whet my curiosity: that this was the site of a battle, long ago, and none of the people who lived here were spared the sword. But the goal of the enemy was not treasure, for the coffers were left untouched. Untouched!

'But there is something strange about the rooms and corridors of Minas Elendúr, and the cellars of Delotham, which run beneath it. You see things, sometimes, disturbing things. It is as if the horrors that fell upon that place scarred it in some way, some way that the stones have not forgotten. It would be a brave ${RACE} indeed to explore the place from top to bottom!

'Some rooms in Minas Elendúr can only be reached by stairs within Delotham, and the opposite seems also to be so! I do not know why the Ninkoth uruks have come to this place, but I feel it must be as bewildering to them as it is to me! Wherever they are keeping this dwarf for whom you are looking, it will not be easy to find!''This dwarf for whom you are looking is somewhere in Minas Elendúr? Well, you will have your hands full looking for him. These ruins are a bewildering maze crawling with uruks of the Ninkoth tribe and other dangers. It is enough to drive a man mad!''My father is somewhere in the ruins of Minas Elendúr? You need to rescue him, ${PLAYER}! The Elves say that Minas Elendúr is far to the east of here!''I have had an idea, ${PLAYER}, and we cannot leave until Fúni Gem-cutter is rescued.''I have been asking the Elves here for information about Minas Elendúr, but there is not much to tell. The ruins were the site of a terrible battle, long ago, and have remained empty ever since. They can be found far to the east of here.''By Durin, a friendly face! I had all but given up hope of rescue, and here you appear as if from nowhere! Hurry, there is not much time!'Ergothorn has left the Rangers a second letter: a band of uruks has come out of Dunland and is marching towards Zudrugund!Radanir is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in the grove of trees to the east of Lhanuch.

Radanir is waiting to speak with you, and together you will travel to Zudrugund with all haste to try and defend it from the uruks of which Ergothorn wrote.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an inspiration bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Talk to Radanir and travel with him to ZudrugundCorunir is in Zudrugund.

You have managed to stave off an attack on Zudrugund by the Grishgúk Uruks led by Núrzumgrat, and should speak now to Corunir.Talk to Corunir in Zudrugund'We have to leave at once if we are to have any hope of arriving in time to face the uruks, ${PLAYER}. We may already be too late. Let me know if you are ready!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'Are you ready, ${PLAYER}? We will travel to Zudrugund together, and at once!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>'We were able to defend Zudrugund from attack, but we are still no closer to solving the mystery of Nár. And now we know that Saruman has some interest in the dwarf as well! You should return to Zudrugund and speak with Corunir.''I am pleased that you came to the defence of Zudrugund, ${PLAYER}! Once again, Ergothorn has saved us a great deal of trouble. Why do I not feel more relieved by this?'You have found Fúni Gem-cutter, but he is not content merely to be rescued: he wants you to rescue as much gold from the Ninkoth uruks in Minas Elendúr as you can.Treasure and Ninkoth uruks can be found throughout the ruins of Minas Elendúr and the Delotham under-cellars.

Fúni Gem-cutter is waiting for you to retrieve as much treasure from the ruins as you can.Collect treasure coffers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Fúni Gem-cutter is in Minas Elendúr.

You have done what Fúni asked, and should return to him with the treasures you have gathered.Fúsi Gem-cutter is at Echad Eregion.

You have rescued his father and should bring the news to Fúsi.Talk to Fúsi Gem-cutter at Echad EregionRadanir is at Echad Eregion.

The Rangers have been discussing something with Fúni Gem-cutter, now that he has been rescued, and Radanir may know more about it.Talk to Radanir at Echad Eregion'These uruks call themselves "Ninkoth", but I care not; they are as miserable a creature as any I have seen. It seems they have come north on the heels of another breed, but these have designs of settling down in these lands. What an idea! Better up here than down near Zigil-jabâl, my home in the White Mountains, but still too close for comfort. We have had troubles there enough.

'The Ninkoth uruks kept me alive for a purpose, friend. They've been bringing golden trinkets to me from throughout the cellars of this place, with the idea that I could somehow fashion them into items of worth. Which of course I can, but would never do for these creatures!

'I cannot bear the thought of these treasures being left here for the Ninkoth. Explore these ruins, gather up the treasure, and bring it back to me! I would not mind a bit if you took some time to slay some uruks, either! Oh! You may want to make a map!''My father is back, ${PLAYER}, and he was very pleased at your performance within the ruins. He's been deep in conversation with one of the Rangers here.

'I think they want him to make something, but what it is I have no idea! That Ranger, Radanir, might be able to tell you more.''I have just been talking with Halbarad, ${PLAYER}, and now this whole affair with the Gem-cutters makes sense to me. I now know why Halbarad thought this delay to be necessary, and I agree with him.

'He has a commission for Fúni Gem-cutter, a task for which Fúni is well-suited. He wants Fúni to make an imitation of the Ring of Barahir.''My father is still within Minas Elendúr? What is taking so long?''My father has not yet returned! You must go to Minas Elendúr and show him the way!''I do not yet know what the Rangers want my father to make. That Radanir might know!''You found Fúni Gem-cutter, but he will not leave until you recover treasure from the ruins of Minas Elendúr? How long will this delay last?''You have gathered treasure from Minas Elendúr? At least you have deprived the Ninkoth uruks of it. But where is Fúni Gem-cutter?''The dwarf's father has returned from the ruins, ${PLAYER}. He has been deep in conversation with Halbarad for a while now.''There is treasure all over these ruins, ${PLAYER}. We cannot leave it in the hands of these uruks!''I will leave shortly for Echad Eregion! Tell my son I will be there soon!''Excellent, excellent! We will bring these treasures out into the sunlight with us. Let us go, ${PLAYER}! I will meet you back at, what was the name? Echad Eregion? Tell my son I am on my way.'Calenglad has sent you to speak with Lothrandir, a Ranger sent to Forochel, to bring him word of the Grey Company's mission.Lothrandir is at Sûri-kylä, the major village of the Lossoth in Forochel.

Calenglad has sent you to bring your message to Lothrandir, in Forochel, with the warning that he has lived among the Lossoth for so long that he may have taken on something of their customs and personalities.Talk to Lothrandir at Sûri-kyläLothrandir is at Sûri-kylä, in Forochel.

You have given Lothrandir the message, but he seems unsure of the Grey Company's ability to reach Aragorn in time and wants your help with a Lossoth issue.Talk to Lothrandir and help him find the screaming shadowLothrandir is at Sûri-kylä, in Forochel.

You have found the cause of the Lossoth's hunting troubles and have hopefully ended it. Lothrandir, at least, seems satisfied.Talk to Lothrandir at Sûri-kylä'I will speak with some others of the Dúnedain here in Evendim about joining with the Grey Company on its journey south, but you should travel now north to Forochel. I sent the Ranger Lothrandir to meet with the Lossoth some time ago about the growing danger of Angmar, but once his task was completed, he loved the land and its people so much that he decided to remain.

'He would be disappointed to learn the Grey Company was called without his participation. Travel north to Sûri-kylä to speak with Lothrandir, but keep this in mind: he has lived among the Lossoth for quite a while now and has surely adopted some of their customs and taken on something of their personalities.''You say it is time for the Dúnedain to ride out of the north, ${PLAYER}? It is a long way to Rohan, and even if that be where Aragorn has need of us, none know the perilous roads like I do.

'Yes, I have travelled in the southern lands. I have walked the hills and valleys of Enedwaith, been stalked by savage creatures in the mysterious wood-lands, and climbed among the rocky heights of Thror's Coomb. There are dangers on that road that must not be dismissed. There is no guarantee that even a dozen Rangers will come to Aragorn at his time of need...or at all.

'The blood of the Dúnedain flows through my veins, and they will always be my people, but I have another people now as well, and I want them to be safe. I speak of the Lossoth. Will you help me protect them from the mysterious foe that strikes from the heart of storms and under the cover of black night?''Gun Ain's life is her own, now. Whether it will be long or short will depend upon the choices she makes. If she spurns our help and continues to harass the Lossoth, it will be short indeed. Perhaps she will find a way to leave her troubled past behind. It is to be hoped she will do so, for otherwise she will perish, alone and without the name for which she grieves.

'You have done as I asked, and now the Lossoth will be able to bring in their rightful prey without interference. I will do my duty, as I said I would. I will join the Grey Company on the south road.''Lothrandir is a good man, and a fine Ranger. You will find him at Sûri-kylä, the major Lossoth village in Forochel.''The Lossoth rely in large part upon the skill of their hunters. Forochel is an inhospitable land, and though they are accomplished hunters and know every hill and plain around the Ice-bay, there can still be lean times.

'In recent days, after some of the hunters have brought down game animals, a screaming apparition has descended upon their prey from out of the darkness and borne it away amid the Lossoth's confusion and terror. I do not know what manner of beast this may be, but I have tracked it to what must be its lair.

'Come with me and let us finish this screaming shadow, that the Lossoth may bring in enough food to feed their people, and I will be satisfied. I will bring my knowledge of the south-lands with me as we ride to Aragorn.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>Halbarad has had an idea: to equip each Ranger of the Grey Company with an imitation of the ring of Barahir, in order to confuse any servants of the Enemy in the event a Ranger is captured.Halbarad is at Echad Eregion.

Radanir has told you of Halbarad's plan to have Fúni Gem-cutter craft an imitation ring of Barahir.Talk to Halbarad at Echad EregionHalbarad is at Echad Eregion.

Halbarad is waiting to travel with you to the forges Fúni will use to craft imitation Rings of Barahir for the Grey Company.Talk to Halbarad at Echad Eregion and travel to the forgesHalbarad is at Echad Eregion, waiting to speak with you.

Fúni Gem-cutter has crafted imitation rings for the Grey Company, and you should speak now with Halbarad.Talk to Halbarad in Echad Eregion'Halbarad can tell you more, but as he said to the rest of us, "What chance is it that puts a wandering jewel-crafter right in our path at this moment?" He is right. It is a small chance, surely, but a happy one.

'Talk to Halbarad, here at Echad Eregion, to learn more about his plan.''Think of this, ${PLAYER}: the line of Isildur is recognized by the heirlooms of that house: the shards of Narsil, now reforged; the Sceptre of Annúminas, long kept in Rivendell; and the Ring of Barahir, almost lost in Forochel but regained. I believe you have heard the story.

'And now, this Grey Company rides to the defence of Aragorn in the southern lands, lands increasingly under the sway of the uruk. Danger crowds around us upon every side, but our own safety must be secondary to that of Aragorn. Imagine, therefore, if a member of our company is slain or taken captive by the Enemy. If that man bore a ring of the same description as the Ring of Barahir, would that not give his captor pause?

'I have therefore made this proposal to Fúni Gem-cutter: to craft rings so like to the ring of Barahir, based on Candaith's description, that should any of this Company fall into the hands of the Enemy, it may sow confusion into his mind. This confusion may buy the true Heir precious time. Tell me when you are ready, and we will go with Fúni to the forges he will use.''It is done, ${PLAYER}. I hope that this was just a momentary delay that we will never have needed; but in the sad event that one of our friends is slain or captured by the Enemy, perhaps his Bebarahir will buy our chieftain Aragorn some precious seconds.

'All of our struggles are for him, for the return of the heir of Isildur, the return of the king of Gondor.

'We must soon be off again. Our destiny awaits us in the wide southern lands.''It is time, ${PLAYER}. Fúni Gem-cutter will craft rings for every Ranger in the Company, and we will need to be on guard for any possible danger.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a solo quest and cannot be completed with a fellowship.</rgb>'Talk to Halbarad here at Echad Eregion. He can tell you more about his plan.''Halbarad is waiting to speak with you about the plan.''Look at this, ${PLAYER}. Truly this is Bebarahir, so like as may be to the ring of Barahir that only very few might tell the difference.'You have repelled an attack on Zudrugund by the Grishgúk uruks, but Nár's curious behaviour before the attack causes Corunir concern.The book-shelves that so agitated Nár are on the upper level, on the north side of the library in Zudrugund.

Corunir has noted the book-shelves that Nár spent so much time at in the moments before the attack and wants you to search them for information that might be of use.Nár is in Zudrugund.

You have searched the book-shelves pointed out to you by Corunir, and should speak to Nár to see if he can shed any light at all on what you found there.Talk to Nár in ZudrugundCorunir is in Zudrugund.

You have spoken with Nár, but yet again he has provided very little with which you can piece together a solution.Talk to Corunir in ZudrugundHalbarad is among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.

You have defended Zudrugund from the uruks, and Corunir now wants you to report of your success to Halbarad.Talk to Halbarad in Lhanuch'Before the attack, Nár became very excited, and no amount of calming words from Frithgeir could settle him. He moved from shelf to shelf in a certain section of the library, and in his agitated state, I could not even get his attention!

'I will point out to you the shelves. Perhaps, this time, you will find some relevant information that will help us get to the bottom of our mysterious dwarf!

'The shelves are on the upper level, on the north side of the library.''I can tell you where the book-shelves that Nár spent time at before the attack are located. They are on the upper level, on the north side of the library here in Zudrugund.'(You found a stack of loose papers, yellowed with age, which seem to have been placed back onto the shelf randomly, with no regard for keeping them in order.)

...and in the last month, a great number of the supports have fallen, giving rise to much concern among the dwarves who have stalls in Zigil-jabâl. A dozen of our stone-masons were directed to repair the supports, and trade should resume shortly.

With these stone-masons went several waggons of material, chiefly unearthed stone, but also a number of fine gems: a gift to the dwarves at Kechel. We will see how hotly burn their forges, and if they can work the gem-stones into their craft as well as we are able.

(Judging from the state of the next page, it is even older, and only a few words can still be read.)

...time we lost one of our own to the Forbidden Halls. They should seal it away and forget it was ever delved.(You have found some pages, which have been hastily torn from a larger volume -- perhaps a diary or record of events -- and seem to be only a few years old.)

...surprisingly cold for March. Our friend the Ranger came by again today, and we spoke for a time about the situation in the north. His chief is on some errand to Mirkwood, but it seems to me that Túgonn knows little of it as well.

Nár has been in high spirits lately; I think he likes receiving visitors, though he does not always recognize them for who they are. Still, it seems to me he enjoys the company. He does spend a great deal of time in the mine; I only accompany him occasionally, but there are only so many times I can see Thrór's Stone-house standing empty before the melancholy is too much. Nár sees it as it was, I think, so I am content to let him visit it by himself.

These periods give me time to write in my book, which is fine by this dwarf. I think tomorrow I will walk down to the Coomb and see about picking more of the herbs I have read about. Perhaps I will make that brew after all. It will be nice to have Nár to talk to again.(You found a book, of which the cover is missing, as are many of the pages, but it has been recently read and was clearly put back carelessly.)

...but the beast returned that night, and the walls all around shook with the sound of its fury. The doors were barred, but the bars bent with each mighty crash of the creature's bulk, and cracks began to appear along their lengths. "We must return its youngling!" cried Selur over the din, and he leapt forward, knocking aside the warriors that braced the door with their bodies. The first bar gone, and then the second! "No, Selur!" they shouted, but it was too late: the third bar fell to the floor, and Selur threw open the door.

Old Stonetooth was through the doorway in seconds. Selur was trampled underfoot, along with many good dwarves that spoke against the capture of Pebblewart; it availed them nothing in the end. Stonetooth thundered down the length of the hall, braying for her young. And then, an answering bleat! It drew her down side passages and steep stairways, the flagstones cracking beneath her great weight.

The dwarves freed Pebblewart from his cage, and many of them ran; those that witnessed the reunion of mother and child did not survive to tell of it. But the next day, when the dwarves ventured back to those broken halls, all was empty. Old Stonetooth and Pebblewart were never seen in those caves again, and none know where they went. But it is still said today, when the earth shakes, that Pebblewart is running beneath the mountains, expressing joy at his freedom, carving great channels in the walls of the deepest caves with his golden horn.'I confess that I cannot piece together any solution to this riddle, ${PLAYER}. I see no reason for what you found on those shelves to drive Nár to distraction so. Perhaps he can shed some light, though I do not expect it.''That was quite a battle, was it not? Ah, it has been a long time since uruks dared to show their faces here! I must tell Thrór when he comes back.''What is it? You are speaking nonsense. You should speak more clearly if you want to be understood!''I do not know what interest Nár could have in this variety of topics, ${PLAYER}. A list of repairs at a dwarf-hold in the White Mountains, a page from Frithgeir's journal, and the story of some beast, likely more tale than true? I can think of no common thread to tie these together, just as I could not the last time we tried to make sense of the dwarf's curious behaviour.

'Return to Halbarad and tell him that, for now, Zudrugund is safe from the Uruks. You should be able to find him among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.''We have had both successes and failures at Zudrugund, ${PLAYER}. We protected Nár, but he keeps his secrets safe from us! Go tell this to Halbarad, among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.''I have bad news, ${PLAYER}. We have found Ergothorn. He is dead.'Lothrandir has sent you to speak with Corunir, a Ranger who has lived for many years in the settlement of Aughaire, in Angmar.The Ranger Corunir is at Aughaire, the settlement of the Trev Gallorg in Angmar.

Lothrandir has told you where you might find Corunir, that he may be called to the muster of the Grey Company.Talk to Corunir in AughaireThe Ranger Corunir is at Aughaire, the settlement of the Trev Gallorg in Angmar.

You must go with Corunir into danger if Golodir is to be rescued.Complete Skirmish: Rescue in Nûrz GhâshuCorunir is at Aughaire, the settlement of the Trev Gallorg, in Angmar.

You have rescued Golodir from the depths of Nûrz Ghâshu and should speak now with Corunir, your ally on that journey.Talk to Corunir at Aughaire'You should travel now into Angmar to tell the Rangers Corunir and Golodir of the Grey Company's journey.'

Lothrandir listens as you briefly tell him the reasons Elrond felt Golodir should not be troubled with the errand.

'I see. I do not agree, but if that is the will of Elrond, there is little enough I could say to dispute it. Corunir, then, should be told of the Grey Company's mission. You will find him at Aughaire, the settlement of the Trev Gallorg in Angmar.''${PLAYER}! You have come just in time, for I do not know what I would do if you had not arrived! Golodir, my captain...he is in grave peril!

'The death of his daughter, Lorniel, brought him to the very brink of despair, but I thought his triumph over the False King would bring him the peace he sought. For a time it seemed it did, but lately he has lapsed into such a pit of grief that he seems not to know even we his friends. His sorrow fuels his anger at the remnants of Angmar, and he goes alone into even the most dangerous strongholds of evil. The False King is gone, but not even Golodir can long stand against the servants that still remain in many of those places!

'But he has gone too far this time, ${PLAYER}, and I do not know what to do. I need your help, for in his grief and rage I fear that Golodir is blinded to the danger, and where he goes there can be no return. He travels alone into the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu, ${PLAYER}! We must rescue him!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, or Fellowship group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 60.

If this is your first Skirmish, you should visit one of the Skirmish Camps found throughout Middle-earth and speak with a Skirmish Captain to properly train. Skirmish Camps can be found in the following regions: Ered Luin, Bree-land, the Shire, the Lone-lands, the North Downs, the Trollshaws, Evendim, Forochel, Angmar, Eregion, Moria, and Lothlórien.</rgb>'You have my deepest thanks, ${PLAYER}, for aiding me in the rescue of Golodir from the very depths of Nûrz Ghâshu. He must have drawn the notice of Erebún during one of his excursions into the cities of Angmar following the defeat of the False King. Who knows what would have happened if the rogmul had maintained his dominance?

'We can be glad that did not happen, ${PLAYER}. I think it is best for Golodir to come with us as part of the Grey Company. It is not healthy for him to remain in this land, as marked with grief as it must be for him. The wide southern lands will do him good, I am sure of it.

'He will be kept busy, as we all will be, and I believe that to be the best means of rising above grief, as he must and will. Thank you again, my friend.''We must travel into the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu if we are to rescue Golodir!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest involves a Skirmish that can be completed at Solo, Duo, Small Fellowship, or Fellowship group sizes by using the Skirmish Join Panel. This Skirmish has a minimum level of 65.

If this is your first Skirmish, you should visit one of the Skirmish Camps found throughout Middle-earth and speak with a Skirmish Captain to properly train. Skirmish Camps can be found in the following regions: Ered Luin, Bree-land, the Shire, the Lone-lands, the North Downs, the Trollshaws, Evendim, Forochel, Angmar, Eregion, Moria, and Lothlórien.</rgb>'You will find Corunir at Aughaire, the settlement of the Trev Gallorg in Angmar.'Halbarad has given you some unexpected news: they have found Ergothorn, but he is dead.Ergothorn's body is in the Hall of Making, to the north-east of Halbarad.

Halbarad has asked you to examine Ergothorn's body to see if you can learn anything more of his fate.Examine Ergothorn's body for clues to his demiseHalbarad is among the huts on the western side of Lhanuch.

You have read Ergothorn's last letter and should return now to Halbarad to tell him what it said.Bring the letter to Halbarad in LhanuchRadanir is at the camp-site of the Grey Company in the grove of trees east of Lhanuch.

Halbarad has directed you to speak with Radanir to tell him to investigate Ergothorn's claim that the Oath-breakers who came to the Lich Bluffs might be compelled to fulfil their oath.Talk to Radanir in the grove of trees east of Lhanuch'One of the scouts of the Uch-lûth found his body this morning, hidden behind a fall of rocks on the southern edge of the Coomb. I think he was ambushed from hiding, as Wadu was, though it is strange he was not slain by sword, arrow, or bolt. He was killed by hand, the life choked out of him.

'For now, his body is being kept in the Hall of Making, to the north-east. You should examine Ergothorn's body to see if we can learn anything more of his fate.''Ergothorn's body is being kept in the Hall of Making, to the north-east. You should examine the body to see what more we can learn of Ergothorn's fate.'It is as Halbarad described: the man is wearing armour of Gondor and has no sword-wounds on his body, but he is dead.

You notice a scrap of parchment clutched in one hand, which you pry loose and read.

'To the Rangers:

'The dwarf has already told your foe much, and I fear that it will be enough to endanger not just your errand, but the delicate situation in Dunland as well. Things are not as he thought they were, but they soon will be. He has been slowed by his uncertainty concerning the errand of your company, but not stopped.

'They who swore the oath to Isildur at Erech -- you know of them and of their home beneath the Dwimorberg -- but you do not know that some number of them sought to flee their curse and came to hide in a place called the Forsaken Road, among the Lich Bluffs. If they could be made to fulfil their oath to your chieftain's forebear, all might not be lost.

'Not all remember the old ways, but I do. For Ithil that was lost -- and for all else that was forgotten -- I remain,

'Ergothorn.'The note clutched in Ergothorn's dead hand reads thus:

'To the Rangers:

'The dwarf has already told your foe much, and I fear that it will be enough to endanger not just your errand, but the delicate situation in Dunland as well. Things are not as he thought they were, but they soon will be. He has been slowed by his uncertainty concerning the errand of your company, but not stopped.

'They who swore the oath to Isildur at Erech -- you know of them and of their home beneath the Dwimorberg -- but you do not know that some number of them sought to flee their curse and came to hide in a place called the Forsaken Road, among the Lich Bluffs. If they could be made to fulfil their oath to your chieftain's forebear, all might not be lost.

'Not all remember the old ways, but I do. For Ithil that was lost -- and for all else that was forgotten -- I remain,

'Ergothorn.''He must have been coming to leave us that letter, ${PLAYER}. He was unable to deliver it in life, but has done so in death.

'I know not who set upon him in the wilderness, far from safety, but I believe it is the foe of which he speaks or an agent of that enemy. I believe now that Ergothorn's enemy is our enemy as well. But now at last it becomes clear. Whoever killed Ergothorn can move stealthily and strike without warning, but chooses to use neither sword nor bow. If this mysterious foe can move about without the knowledge of his prey, he may have overheard our conversations. If he, too, speaks with Nár, that could be the source of the dwarf's information, but twisted into a form unbeknownst to Nár himself.

'And it all comes back to the Oath-breakers. Is Ergothorn right? Could they be compelled to fulfil their oath? If we can learn that this is possible within the Forsaken Road, that will be a great boon for Aragorn! Speak to Radanir at our camp-site in the grove of trees east of Lhanuch. Tell him that I want this investigated.'The note clutched in Ergothorn's dead hand reads thus:

'To the Rangers:

'The dwarf has already told your foe much, and I fear that it will be enough to endanger not just your errand, but the delicate situation in Dunland as well. Things are not as he thought they were, but they soon will be. He has been slowed by his uncertainty concerning the errand of your company, but not stopped.

'They who swore the oath to Isildur at Erech -- you know of them and of their home beneath the Dwimorberg -- but you do not know that some number of them sought to flee their curse and came to hide in a place called the Forsaken Road, among the Lich Bluffs. If they could be made to fulfil their oath to your chieftain's forebear, all might not be lost.

'Not all remember the old ways, but I do. For Ithil that was lost -- and for all else that was forgotten -- I remain,

'Ergothorn.''Tell Radanir that I want this investigated. In his last letter, Ergothorn was right: if the Oath-breakers could be compelled to fulfil their oath, we might gain much. Will we learn that this is possible along the Forsaken Road?''Halbarad's wishes are my own as well. We will walk the Forsaken Road and see what lies within.'You have spoken with each of the Rangers whose names Master Elrond provided and should report now to the Elf-lord of your success.Master Elrond is in the library of his house, in Rivendell.

You have brought word of the Grey Company's need to the Rangers spoken of by Master Elrond and should return now to him.Talk to Elrond in Rivendell'You have come a great way, ${PLAYER}, to bring your message to the Dúnedain. Golodir and I will inform the rest of our kin here in Angmar, and we will make haste to join the Grey Company.

'You should return now to Master Elrond in Rivendell, that he may be told of your errand's success.''You have done quite well, ${PLAYER}. The Grey Company assembles with as much haste as can be taken, and they will ride to Aragorn in the wide southern lands.

'Whatever dangers have befallen him, we must believe the Dúnedain will reach him in time.

'If you desire to aid the Grey Company and Aragorn further, you may ride with them. I will tell you when all is made ready. Until that day, ${PLAYER}, go in peace and keep still the safety of these lands. The way south will be dangerous.''Master Elrond will be pleased to know of your success in this errand.'The Grey Company rides now south out of Eregion and onto the plains of Enedwaith, the northern stretches of Dunland.Lothrandir will be waiting for you at Echad Dagoras, the next stop on the Grey Company's journey south into Fordirith, in northern Enedwaith.

It is time to ride south into Enedwaith. The Grey Company will stop for rest at Echad Dagoras, in Fordirith.Find Echad Dagoras in FordirithLothrandir is at Echad Dagoras, the camp of the Grey Company in Fordirith, the northern reaches of Enedwaith.

The Grey Company has passed into Enedwaith, and you should speak now to Lothrandir to learn the lay of the land.Talk to Lothrandir at Echad DagorasWadu of the Uch-lûth has come to Echad Dagoras in Fordirith.

Lothrandir has asked you to speak with Wadu of the Uch-lûth.Talk to Wadu at Echad Dagoras'If we are fortunate, none of the rings of Bebarahir borne by the Rangers of our Company will need to serve the purpose for which they were crafted. Even so, I am glad to have them in the event they become necessary. A single moment of hesitation on the part of Aragorn's foes could make the difference.

'It is time again to ride! Let us cross south into the northern stretches of Dunland. The plains of Enedwaith will be the next stop on our journey.

'We will take our rest once we cross into the land of Fordirith, in northern Enedwaith. Dagoras will select the location, and you should speak with Lothrandir when we arrive at Echad Dagoras to learn the lay of the land.''The road south is a long one, ${PLAYER}. You should know something of this land and its people.

'The Men of Dunland are a wild people, and they care little for the Dúnedain and not at all for the Men of Rohan. They have warred with the Rohirrim and covet still their lands; it is their strongly-held belief that those lands belong to the Men of Dunland. They are proud and fierce, quick to anger and slow to forgiveness. They tolerate no weakness and are suspicious of outsiders.

'During my time here in the south-lands, it seemed to me that these Men had no identity as "Dunlendings," thinking of themselves instead as members of particular clans dominated by strong, wilful leaders. Dagoras has met already with the member of one such clan: Wadu of the Uch-lûth. He has set aside his suspicions, for the moment, and you might learn something of his people by speaking with him.''You have come with these men, but you are not one of them. The warning I gave to this man, this Dagoras, I give to you: my people will not allow you to interfere with their lives. You may pass through these lands, but you cannot stay.

'Strength only do they respect. Strength such as mine, or my father Suvulch. Even my sister Nona wields the sword, and I wager she does so with greater skill than any of these duvodiad.''Wadu is here at Echad Dagoras. He has set aside the suspicions common to so many of his people and may be of help to the Grey Company.''It is time for the Grey Company to continue its journey south, ${PLAYER}. Ride south into Enedwaith, where Dagoras will establish a camp in Fordirith, the northern reaches of that land. Lothrandir will then speak to you of the dangers in that place.'You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the story of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the history of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete At the Stone of ErechYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the history of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.You think about what you have read and ponder the history of the Oath-breakers as it was told to you by Candaith of the Dúnedain.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of 'At the Stone of Erech.'You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the story of the heirs of Isildur throughout history.You have chosen to read from the Chronicle of Events concerning the history of the heirs of Isildur.Read from the Chronicle of Events and complete The House of IsildurYou have read from the Chronicle of Events of the history of the heirs of Isildur.Close the Chronicle of EventsYou turn the pages and come to an account of the heirs of Isildur throughout history.You think about what you have read and ponder the history of the heirs of Isildur as it was told to you by Candaith of the Dúnedain.You turn the pages and become immersed in the chronicle of 'The House of Isildur.'From the innumerable beasts you have defeated in Thrór's Coomb, you have managed to obtain a great trophy: Cloben's eye.Frithgeir is at Zudrugund, which lies within Nár's Peak, north of the river.

The eye you recovered will long serve as a symbol of the defeat of the gwiber that menaced Thrór's Coomb.Talk to Frithgeir in ZudrugundWith the defeat of Cloben, the dwarves of Thrór's Coomb will no longer have to quake in fear under the threat of the powerful gwiber.

You should bring Cloben's eye to Frithgeir in Zudrugund.'This eye serves as a beacon of hope, friend. With Cloben's defeat, the dwarves have taken their first step towards the reclamation of their home. Your name will be revered among the Longbeards.

'I fear that while this creature was powerful and deadly, it may not be the same beast that has pushed the brood of gwiber out from their mountain home, however....'From the myriad beasts you have defeated in Thrór's Coomb, you have manage to obtain a great trophy: Cloben's Fang.Frithgeir is at Zudrugund, which lies within Nár's Peak, north of the river.

The fang you recovered will long serve as a symbol of the defeat of the gwiber that menaced Thrór's Coomb.Talk to Frithgeir in ZudrugundWith the defeat of Cloben, the dwarves of Thrór's Coomb will no longer have to quake in fear under the threat of the powerful gwiber.

