You should return to Himeinior with the tokens you collected from the fell spirits of Sâd Doldúr.'Scattered among the ruins below, fell spirits of Morgoth have risen and now haunt the grounds. These creatures have begun appearing in staggeringly large numbers near Sâd Doldúr.

'Could you travel to the ruins below and banish these creatures? I am sure you may find some token of their allegiance wherever they fall. Bring me some of these, as well, to prove your success.

'The spirits are found in great number outside of Sâd Doldúr below. Return to me when you have completed your task.''Dispatch several fell spirits near Sâd Doldúr and collect tokens of their allegiance, then return them to me.''The number of fell spirits in the area and the look of these coins leads me to believe that the foul sorcerer Orthadban in Sâd Doldúr may have a hand in this matter.

'Thank you for retrieving these coins... perhaps they will shed some light on the true cause of the appearance of so many fell spirits in the Ashenslades.'Sâri-surma is a place filled with many wights and many other strange things.  Complete the challenge, defeat all of the lieutenants, guardians, and bosses. Then and only then will your reward be great.

This deed is only completeable in the Tier 2 Legendary difficulty of this instance.Complete the Challenge Mode for Sâri-surma (Tier 2 only)General Tímurzgrat of the Black Uruks has emerged to force order upon the panicking Orc-troops in Gathbúrz.Tímurzgrat, the uruk-general of Gathbúrz, may be found at a tower some distance to the north of Thangúlhad.

Rodelleth has instructed you to seek out the Black Uruk General Tímurzgrat and defeat him.Defeat General TímurzgratRodelleth is at Thangúlhad in Gathbúrz, south of General Tímurzgrat's tower.

You should return at once to Rodelleth with news of your victory against General Tímurzgrat.Talk to Rodelleth at Thangúlhad'Gathbúrz lies in a state of near-anarchy before our assault, and now it is time to push it over the brink!

'General Tímurzgrat -- a Black Uruk of deadly repute -- has come to the fore in an effort to force order upon the soldiers of the Enemy. His head is ripe for the plucking, and unlike that fool Fikthrug, the masters of Dol Guldur will find Tímurzgrat far more difficult to replace.

'Take great care though: Tímurzgrat is no mean foe; he is quite skilled in combat as well as command, and you will likely need stalwart allies at your side to defeat him. When you have gathered your allies, seek him at the tower some distance to the north of here.''With Tímurzgrat's fall, so falls Gathbúrz. We should expect no further organized resistance from that front until the eastern army arrives.

'You have earned my admiration and gratitude, ${PLAYER}.''Tímurzgrat leads the forces of the Enemy here in Gathbúrz...defeat him, and the enemies will be overwhelmed.'The minions of the Enemy train in the Sword-hall of Dol Guldur, improving their skills for the inevitable invasion of Lothlórien. They must learn just how lacking they are....Complete Enter the ArenaComplete Battle RoyaleIf left unchecked, the goblins of Draugdegil will soon overrun Helethir.Goblins can be found at Draugdegil, north of Helethir and just inside the gate and Fennas Guldur.Defeat goblins (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Among the ruins, Gunnberg, chieftain of the giants, rules over his people. It is rumoured that his appetite for violence is only overshadowed by his lust for tribute.Tribute-piles can be found outside Gunnberg's lair, south of Zudrugund.

Frithgeir has asked you to seek out Gunnberg's tribute-piles and destroy them.Destroy tribute-piles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gunnberg's personal tribute-pile lies outside his home in a small mountain pass.

Frithgeir has asked you to disturb Gunnberg's personal tribute-pile and draw him forth to battle.Frithgeir is at Zudrugund, north of the giant-ruins. 

You should return to Frithgeir with news of Gunnberg's defeat.Talk to Frithgeir at Zudrugund'Gunnberg, the chieftain of the giants, sits atop the cliffs, ruling his people with an iron fist.  This monster has led his people to destroy our home, pillage our sacred resting places, and deface the beauty of our ancient craftsmanship.

'He does, however, have a weakness: it is said that the Algraig clans pay tribute to him to allay his wrath. He will likely emerge from his lair if his tribute is disturbed. 

'Gunnberg makes his home in the small mountain pass, in the southern reaches of Thrór's Coomb. This pass leads to one of the few entrances of the dwarf-halls below.''Gunnberg's defeat may serve as a lesson to those who would despoil our home.

'Perhaps the giants will return from whence they came.''Deface Gunnberg's tribute piles...that should grab his attention.''Gunnberg must be defeated and his people routed from this vale.'The Ranger Halros is having trouble deciding whether to stay in the Shire and keep watch over the hobbits or to travel south with the Grey Company.Lotho Sackville-Baggins is visiting Brockenborings and can be found inside the inn there.

Halros has decided to travel south with the Grey Company and wants you to tell his friend Lotho of his decision.Talk to Lotho Sackville-Baggins at The Plough and StarsThe Ranger Halros is at his camp in the Greenfields, in the north-east corner of the Shire.

You have delivered Halros's message to Lotho and should return now to the Ranger.Talk to Halros in the Greenfields'You think I should travel south with the Grey Company to come to the aid of Aragorn? He is my chieftain, and it is my duty to see that he comes safely to the end of his road, wherever it lies...but I fear for the folk of the Shire in my absence. How will they protect themselves from the evils that crowd their borders from all sides?'

Halros looks about his camp with new resolve.

'No, you are right, ${PLAYER}. There is strength in these hobbits yet, and if they need to repel dangers from outside, they will do so. My fate must lie on the southern road. I will stand with Aragorn, and that is the way I will protect the people of this land.

'Thank you for helping me to make up my mind, ${PLAYER}. I will go with the Grey Company. The good folk of the Shire will need to do without my help. Can you travel to the inn at Brockenborings and tell my friend Lotho Sackville-Baggins that I will be leaving? He is visiting Brockenborings for a few days and showed some interest in my comings and goings.''What's that you say? That Ranger will be leaving the Shire for a time? That's...very interesting. Thank you for letting me know, friend. That is a shame, of course. Of course, it is.''What is that you say? Lotho sounded pleased that I would be leaving? That is queer; he sought me out a few days ago and had many questions to ask: if I had any plans to travel into Bree-land, whether I thought to visit certain townships in the Shire, that sort of thing.

'Well, he and his folk will surely be fine without my protection. It pains me to leave them be, but if Aragorn has need of his kin, his kin must ride to his aid!

'Thank you for helping me to decide, ${PLAYER}.''So that Ranger will be out of the Shire for a time? That is very interesting, indeed. I will keep it in mind. He will be...missed...of course.''Lotho Sackville-Baggins showed some interest in my comings and goings in recent days, so I would like him to know that I will not be around to keep an eye on things. I believe he is currently staying at The Plough and Stars, in Brockenborings.'The Ranger Halros is having trouble deciding whether to stay in the Shire and keep watch over the hobbits or to travel south with the Grey Company.Lotho Sackville-Baggins is visiting Brockenborings and can be found inside the inn there.

Halros has decided to stay in the Shire to keep watch over its people and wants you to tell his friend Lotho of his decision.Talk to Lotho Sackville-Baggins at The Plough and StarsThe Ranger Halros is at his camp in the Greenfields, in the north-east corner of the Shire.

You have delivered Halros' message to Lotho and should return now to the Ranger.Talk to Halros in the Greenfields'You think I should stay here in the Shire? It is true that with many of the Dúnedain leaving, these lands will likely come under threat. Who will protect the Little Folk, these hobbits I have come to admire and cherish? They have a core of strength, it is true, but the shadows are lengthening and it seems to me that they creep ever closer to the Shire.

'I have walked of a time with Aragorn and the Wizard, Gandalf, at Sarn Ford, and they both spoke oft of their love for its people. I do not think they would wish the Shire to be undefended.

'Thank you for helping me to make up my mind, ${PLAYER}. I will stay here and watch over the people of the Shire. The Grey Company will need to do without my help. Can you travel to the inn at Brockenborings and tell my friend Lotho Sackville-Baggins that I have decided to stay? He is visiting Brockenborings for a few days and showed some interest in my comings and goings.''What do you want? You have a message from that Ranger?'

Lotho scowls as you tell him that Halros has decided to remain in the Shire for the foreseeable future.

'Yes, well, I'm sure you have places you need to be. I for one do not have the time to stand around talking with every ${RACE} that comes along. That Ranger can do as he likes. It is nothing to me what he does, so long as he stays out of my way.''What is that you say? Lotho sounded disappointed that I would be staying? That is odd; he sought me out a few days ago and had many questions to ask: if I had any plans to travel into Bree-land, whether I thought to visit certain townships in the Shire, that sort of thing. I assumed he would be pleased to know I would be around.

'In any event, my mind is put at ease to know that I will be able to protect the hobbits of the Shire in the coming weeks. I think Aragorn will understand. Thank you, ${PLAYER}.''So that Ranger is going to be around, eh? Well, it is nothing to me. Things can be arranged if they need to be. No Ranger is going to stand in my way.''Lotho Sackville-Baggins showed some interest in my comings and goings in recent days, so I would like him to know that I will be around to keep an eye on things. I believe he is currently staying at The Plough and Stars, in Brockenborings.'The Malledhrim have learned that the outskirts of Ânghum-maudhûl are heavily patrolled by the Orcs of Dol Guldur.Ânghum-maudhûl lies to the south-east of Mithechad.

Harchador has asked you to harass the Orcs assigned to the encampment of Ânghum-maudhûl.Defeat Hîshtgropor Marauders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Hîshtgropor Bloodbows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Harchador is at Mithechad, located on the western border of the Ashenslades, south-west of Ânghum-maudhûl.

After harassing the patrols outside of Ânghum-maudhûl, you should return to speak with Harchador.Talk to Harchador at Mithechad'Scattered among the ruins in the Southern Ashenslades, Ânghum-maudhûl stands as the central encampment for the forces of Dol Guldur in the area. These ruins have been fortified by numerous Orcs that patrol the area. We need to test to see how strong the arrayed forces are.

'Will you travel to Ânghum-maudhûl and dispatch their patrols on the outskirts of the camp? Ânghum-maudhûl can be found to the south-east of Mithechad.

'Return to me when you have completed this task, and I will find some means to reward you.''By harassing the Orc-patrols outside of Ânghum-maudhûl, it has allowed us to discern the strength of the troops in the area.

'While the Enemy is entrenched, we may be able to move in and weaken their fortifications, so that our patrols might move more easily throughout the region.''Have you travelled to Ânghum-maudhûl, to the south-east, and harrassed the patrols surrounding the encampment?'The Elves of the Golden Host have noticed a fair number of sentries along the outskirts of Ânug-stazg. These guards could prove a hinderance for the Malledhrim.Sentries can be found on the outskirts of Ânug-stazg, east of Mithechad.

Perindúr asked you to defeat the sentries of Ânug-stazg, allowing the Malledhrim to lay siege to the encampment.Defeat Hîshtgropor sentries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Perindúr is at Mithechad, west of Ânug-stazg.

You should return to Perindúr with news of your success against the sentries of Ânug-stazg.Talk to Perindúr in Mithechad'Along the outskirts of Ânug-stazg, many sentries stand vigilant, guarding the camp against incursions such as ours. These sentries could prove a hinderance to us as we press forward.

'Would you travel to the camp and defeat their sentries? Perhaps you may be able to take them by surprise and dispatch them with greater ease than would a lumbering army.

'Ânug-stazg can be found to the east of here... do not delay!''Travel east to Ânug-stazg and dispatch the setries surrounding the camp. Go now... time is of the essence!''Now that the sentries have been rendered nigh useless, our time to strike is at hand.

'Thank you for your service, ${PLAYER}. It will not be forgotten.'Shadows are lengthening throughout Eriador now that many of the Rangers have left for the south-lands, and trouble is brewing in their absence.Fenric Banks is in Oatbarton, the hobbit-town in the southern reaches of Evendim. It is one of the most northerly villages of the Shire.

Barliman Butterbur has asked you to investigate the worries of the hobbit Fenric Banks, in Oatbarton.Talk to Fenric Banks in OatbartonThe Northcotton Farm is located west of Oatbarton's town centre and up the steep hill.

Fenric Banks has told you of the hobbit disappearances and wants you to gather a couple friends and investigate the Northcotton Farm, where one of the hobbits was last seen.Talk to Bounder Boffin inside Northcotton Farm'Henric,

Strange things are afoot these days in and around Oatbarton. First there was that mysterious relic that appeared in Bungo Grubb's pipe-weed fields; killed the crop dead, it did, and left everyone else worried for their own!

But now things are even worse. Hobbits have been disappearing left and right. And these aren't Tooks I'm talking about, neither! No, these are good, honest Bolgers and Boffins, as solid and dependable gentlehobbits as you could hope to meet! Not a flaw among them, save being over-fond of their food and drink, and that's not a flaw at all in my estimation!

I don't know for sure what could have caused this recent spate of disappearances, though I have my guesses. I have not seen as many of the grim-faced Rangers lurking about in recent days; is it the same in Bree? In any event, if you know of a stalwart adventurer, be sure to send him or her to me at once. I am worried for my friends here in Oartbarton! We're within the bounds of the Shire, of course, but the rest of Evendim is a dangerous place, just to our north, and even Oatbarton has been frightful of late.

Wishing you the best, your devoted cousin,

Fenric'

<rgb=#FF0000>This Quest Arc begins with 3-Man Small Fellowship content, progresses through 6-Man Fellowship instances, and finally culminates in a 12-Man Raid. It is recommended that you complete Volume III Books 1 and 2 beforehand, but is not required.</rgb>'Things have been a mite worrisome about my fire for the past few evenings, ${PLAYER}. Talk of the surrounding lands has been grim, with little good cheer to be had, even in the common room of the Pony! That's a sure sign of trouble, isn't it?

'Why, just recently one of the good hobbits of Bree gave to me a letter he received from one of his relations out Shire way, and he talks of trouble as plain as the nose on your face, or on mine! Mysterious relics appearing out of the blue, promising crops suddenly failing, and good solid hobbits vanishing, with no reasons given!

'I was never over-fond of Rangers, but they seem to be gone now. No one has seen that Strider for weeks, and few other grim-faced Men of his stripe have been seen since either. Is it possible the Rangers really did protect the peace of the countryside? In their absence, things seem to be getting worse, that's for certain.

'If you're of a mind to, you should travel to the town of Oatbarton, in the southern reaches of Evendim but within the Shire-bounds, to speak to Fenric Banks. His cousin Henric, who showed me his letter, is worried and wants to make sure that everything is all right. You are just the ${RACE} to see it done, that is clear to me!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This Quest Arc begins with 3-Man Small Fellowship content, progresses through 6-Man Fellowship instances, and finally culminates in a 12-Man Raid. It is recommended that you complete Volume III Books 1 and 2 beforehand, but is not required.</rgb>'The letter Henric Banks showed me from his cousin Fenric is worrying, ${PLAYER}, wouldn't you say? It seems to me that troubles are increasing everywhere these days, and even the cheery common room of the Pony is not safe from these worries!

'I haven't seen hide nor hair from those Rangers recently, and if you'd asked me a couple weeks ago, I'd have been delighted! But now I'm not so sure. In their absence, the shadows seem to be lengthening and the troubles increasing! It's bad for business!

'You should travel to Oatbarton and see about helping Fenric Banks. The village is in the southern reaches of Evendim, but is within the bounds of the Shire. It is one of the most northerly of the hobbit-villages.''Ah, hello there, ${PLAYER}, it is a pleasure to meet you. I wish we met under happier circumstances, because things have not been going well here in Oatbarton, as you must know if you saw my letter to cousin Henric!

'The Bounders have been kept busy in recent weeks, but now things are happening within the Bounds: strange relics appearing in pipe-weed fields overnight, crops dying out suddenly; it's the worst I've ever seen it!

'But now hobbits are disappearing: first that thing appeared in Bungo Grubb's field, and not long after, Isenbard Bolger's up and gone. Then Crystal Bolger vanished, and afterwards Andy Boffin and Gladiola Farmer. A couple Bounders went off to Northcotton Farm, west of here and up the hill, where Gladiola Farmer was last seen. You should gather a couple friends and go investigate. I'm worried!''A couple Bounders went off to Northcotton Farm, west of here and up the hill, to look for the missing hobbits. You should gather a couple friends and go investigate. I'm worried!''What brings you to Northcotton Farm, ${PLAYER}? Something's not quite right here, and I'm glad to have your help! This is the last place Gladiola Farmer was seen before her disappearance. We'll get to the bottom of it!'Elrond's examination of the tablet he found in the Lost Temple has revealed the objective of another of the Gaunt-lords.Drugoth the Deathmonger has been commanded to raise an army of wights on Sâri-surma, in Forochel.

Elrond has translated the broken tablet he found within the Lost Temple and now knows the location of another Gaunt-lord.Travel to Sâri-surma and make your way through the fortressPirita of the Lossoth is inside the fortress on Sâri-surma.

Pirita wishes to speak with you.'I found this broken tablet here within the crypts of the Lost Temple, ${PLAYER}, and it reveals more commands of the Gaunt-lord Gortheron. The missing section seems to be addressed to Ferndúr, for I see several mentions of the Red Cloud, but most of the directives for Drugoth are intact.

'Drugoth is commanded to go to Forochel and to assemble an army of wights from Sâri-surma, the Isle of Death, with which to sweep south into the populated lands of Eriador. Gortheron's displeasure is clear: it seems that his servant Ivar was given the task of raising an army of wights, but failed to do so, and now it falls to Drugoth.

'It was Drugoth that sought to threaten Rivendell with the terror of the dragon Thorog, and though he was cast out of the dragon, somehow Drugoth has returned. He has gone to Sâri-surma in Forochel, and you must find him and end this scheme of his master.''I found this broken tablet within the crypts of the Lost Temple, ${PLAYER}, and it reveals more commands of the gaunt-lord Gortheron. The missing section seems to be addressed to Ferndúr, for I see several mentions of the Red Cloud, but most of the directives for Drugoth are intact.

'Drugoth is commanded to go to Forochel, and to assemble an army of wights from Sâri-surma, the Isle of Death, with which to sweep south into the populated lands of Eriador. Gortheron's displeasure is clear: it seems that his servant Ivar was given the task of raising an army of wights, but failed to do so, and now it falls to Drugoth.

'It was Drugoth that sought to threaten Rivendell with the terror of the dragon Thorog, and though he was cast out of the dragon, somehow Drugoth has returned. He has gone to Sâri-surma in Forochel, and you must find him and end this scheme of his master.''Sâri-surma is located in Forochel, within the Icebay. You must go there and find Drugoth the Death-monger.''Sâri-surma is located in Forochel, within the Icebay. You must go there and find Drugoth the Death-monger.''Sâri-surma is in Forochel, within the Icebay. You must hinder Drugoth's plans there!''Sâri-surma is in Forochel, within the Icebay. You must hinder Drugoth's plans there!''Things go ill with my people. Great storms descended upon our homes. The bay of ice heaved in the tumult. The words were not spoken for many days, but they could not remain unsaid forever: had the Witch-king returned to cast his deathly winter upon us again?

'It is not so. But still the land is angry. Someone has disturbed it, bends its will against my people. I do not come to this Isle of Death lightly, but Yrjänä has commanded it.'In the depths of the fortress on Sâri-surma, the Lossoth are trying to end the threat of Drugoth the Death-monger.Yrjänä is somewhere within the fortress on Sâri-surma.

Pirita has asked you to find the Lossoth chieftain, Yrjänä, within the depths of the fortress.Talk to Yrjänä in Sâri-surma'The creature within this fortress is a being of great evil. Yrjänä seems to know the name of this creature and has gone further into the fortress to confront it.

'Find Yrjänä! He cannot fight this foe by himself! We cannot lose our chieftain. You and your allies must oppose the evil here and fight in his stead.''The Lossoth cannot lose Yrjänä. We would be lost without our chieftain. You must find him and keep him from fighting the evil creature that lives now on this Isle of Death!''${PLAYER}! For what reason have you come to the Isle of Death?'

Yrjänä listens to your tale, and he nods in understanding.

'Yes, this creature that has come to Sâri-surma is the gaunt-lord Drugoth. He brings fear in his train, but there is something else: I think madness has taken him.'In the depths of the fortress on Sâri-surma, Drugoth the Death-monger threatens the safety of Forochel and the lands beyond.Drugoth the Death-monger waits in the depths of his fortress on Sâri-surma.

Yrjänä has told you that Drugoth acts impulsively, more like an animal than a thinking being.Defeat Drugoth the Death-monger in the fortress on Sâri-surmaYrjänä wants to speak with you now that you have defeated Drugoth in the fortress on Sâri-surma.Talk to Yrjänä'My people have never faced a Gaunt-lord who acts as...strangely...as this Drugoth. He pits his servants against each other; little thought seems to go into his actions beyond the sowing of discord.

'If you speak truly of his errand, his master cannot be pleased with Drugoth. His actions are almost...animal-like...and if he is raising an army, he is then turning it against itself. There is no reason there!

'Do not underestimate this creature. If he is more beast than thinking being now, he is an animal uncaged, and will fight with ferocity.''You must defeat Drugoth the Death-monger, ${PLAYER}. He is just ahead!''Drugoth has been defeated, and you have the thanks and praise of my people. Even as he succumbed to his madness, he still had teeth with which to rip and tear, and a cruel mind with which he could do harm.

'It is good he be no more.'Yrjänä picked up a tablet that seems to contain directions to the stronghold of Gortheron the Doom-caller, leader of the Gaunt-lords.The stronghold of Gortheron the Doom-caller is located in Enedwaith, in the south-western reaches of the Lich Bluffs.

Yrjänä found a tablet in the depths of the fortress on Sâri-surma that reveals the location of the leader of the Gaunt-lords.'Pirita found this tablet cast aside in a corner of the fortress. She showed it to me, sure of its significance but not its meaning.

'If I read this illustration rightly, it is a map of the southern lands spoken of by our friend Lothrandir; the lands he once lived in and now to which he has returned. See these four lines? They all come from one point and travel across the map in many directions; this one at the northern edge of the map arrives at an island. It must be Sâri-surma. Four lines, four Gaunt-lords.

'If you follow this line southward, to where all the points meet, you will find the one who commanded Drugoth to come to Sâri-surma. The line goes to the land called Enedwaith. In its south-western reaches, among the Lich Bluffs, stands the ruin of a mighty stronghold. It is in this place that the lines meet.''Pirita found this tablet cast aside in a corner of the fortress. She showed it to me, sure of its significance but not its meaning.

'If I read this illustration rightly, it is a map of the southern lands spoken of by our friend Lothrandir; the lands he once lived in, and now to which he has returned. See these four lines? They all come from one point, and travel across the map in many directions; this one at the northern edge of the map arrives at an island. It must be Sâri-surma. Four lines, four gaunt-lords.

'If you follow this line southward, to where all the points meet, you will find the one who commanded Drugoth to come here. The line goes to the land called Enedwaith. In its south-western reaches, among the Lich Bluffs, stands the ruin of a mighty stronghold. It is in this place that the lines meet.''See where the lines on the tablet begin? That is where you will find the leader of the Gaunt-lords. Travel to Enedwaith and find the stronghold in the south-western reaches of the Lich Bluffs.''See where the lines on the tablet begin? That is where you will find the leader of the Gaunt-lords. Travel to Enedwaith, to the south-western reaches of the Lich Bluffs.''It is a pleasure to meet you at last, ${PLAYER}. Yes, I know your name. The presence of my kinsmen was known to me when first they stepped into this land, and I sought out Halbarad some days ago.

'I have advised him not to bring the Grey Company too near this place; danger has been growing here recently, and I believe that Gortheron, the leader of the Gaunt-lords, is to blame. He revived his fallen allies and sent them north into Eriador on errands unknown to me.'

Túgonn listens to your account of the other Gaunt-lords you encountered.

'Impressive feats all, ${PLAYER}. But Gortheron is the greatest of the gaunt-lords, and your victories over his lesser allies will avail you nothing if you underestimate his powers.'The Ranger Túgonn has told you that the door into the central area of Ost Dunhoth is locked and can only be opened by exploring the side wings.Ost Dunhoth has two wings that must be explored before the central area can be unsealed.Survive the wages of war and conquer your fear in the east wingSurvive the spread of plague and poison in the west wingTúgonn is in the entrance of Ost Dunhoth.

You have conquered two wings of Ost Dunhoth and should return now to Túgonn.Talk to Túgonn'Each of the Gaunt-lords you defeated held mastery over a particular facet of the offensive planned by Gortheron: Thadúr the Ravager, master of poison; Ferndúr the Virulent, master of plague; Drugoth the Death-monger, master of fear. You did not encounter Ivar the Blood-hand, but he is the master of war.

'Together, with Gortheron, they are five. Five Gaunt-lords made in mockery of the five Wizards. Gortheron has corrupted these sprawling ruins, making of them a home for the servants of the Gaunt-lords.

'The door into the central area is sealed; if you are able to defeat the minions of Gortheron in each of the side wings, we may be able to open the central area and challenge Gortheron directly.''Explore the two side wings of Ost Dunhoth, and we can work to unseal the central area.''The door to the central area is open, ${PLAYER}. You will find Gortheron within.'The wings of Ost Dunhoth have been cleared, and now the way to the central area is unsealed.The central area of Ost Dunhoth is open.Fight through Ost Dunhoth and defeat GortheronTúgonn is in the entrance of Ost Dunhoth.'The way to the central area of Ost Dunhoth is open, ${PLAYER}. Gortheron awaits within.''The central area of Ost Dunhoth is unsealed, ${PLAYER}. Gortheron awaits within!''You have ended Ivar's insurrection and put an end to his master? But what is this you say of Gandalf?'

Túgonn listens as you describe the strange events surrounding the defeat of Gortheron.

'It is hope beyond hope if what you say is true, ${PLAYER}, but I dare not believe it. It may be some final illusion of Gortheron's. I cannot trust this, though my heart wish it be so. I can think of no better omen than if our Wizard be returned to us!''You have ended Ivar's insurrection and put an end to his master? But what is this you say of Gandalf?'

Túgonn listens as you describe the strange events surrounding the defeat of Gortheron.

'It is hope beyond hope if what you say is true, ${PLAYER}, but I dare not believe it. It may be some final illusion of Gortheron's. I cannot trust this, though my heart wish it be so. I can think of no better omen than if our Wizard be returned to us!'Bounder Boffin is concerned about the disappearances happening in and around the Northcotton Farm and wants you to investigate.Bounder Boffin's assistant Tulip is somewhere further inside the Northcotton Farm, and it could be the missing hobbits are as well.

Bounder Boffin has asked you to search for the missing hobbits inside the Northcotton Farm.Tulip Grubb is somewhere within the Northcotton Farm.

You have rescued the missing hobbits and should now find Bounder Boffin's assistant, Tulip Grubb, who came into the farm to investigate the disappearances.Talk to Tulip Grubb within the Northcotton Farm'My assistant, Tulip Grubb, has gone further into the farm in search of the four missing hobbits. If you do see any sign of them, be sure to tell Tulip. Keep your eyes open for any clues! Were those good folk hobbitnapped?

'There's something very strange going on here in the Northcotton Farm, that's for sure!''There are hobbits to be rescued, ${PLAYER}! Find them throughout the Northcotton Farm and save them if you can! I found several of them, but it was beyond my abilities to free them from their predicaments. But you may be able to help them, and I hope that you will!''Look for the missing hobbits inside Northcotton Farm, ${PLAYER}! Once you've found them, find my assistant, Tulip. She's gone ahead to investigate.''${PLAYER}! You've saved the hobbits! I did not believe I could free them myself, but I was able to stealthily speak with each of them before you came to their rescue, and the stories they whispered to me were very familiar! Crystal Bolger, up in her tree, spoke of a delicious smell that drew her to the farm, and Gladiola said something similar. Andy Boffin said the smell began so deliciously, but turned so foul when he started eating that it knocked him out with the power of its stench.

'I held my nose shut with one hand, and though my eyes watered furiously, I investigated the main farmhouse. There's an evil-looking creature back there, ${PLAYER}, and I think it was he who brewed that terrible poison! I think he was using it to lure hobbits into his trap, and Isenbard Bolger is still missing! That creature must still have him captive.

'He muttered to himself almost constantly. It seems that his name is Thadúr, and his poison is called Darkthorn. You've got to stop this evil creature, ${PLAYER}, you and your friends! You've just got to!'You have rescued some of the hobbitnapped hobbits from the Northcotton Farm, and now Tulip Grubb believes she knows what caused the disappearances.Tulip Grubb told you about an evil-looking creature named Thadúr at the end of the path through the doors behind Tulip. He seems to be the creature responsible for the deadly Darkthorn poison.

You and your allies need to defeat Thadúr before he can bring any more trouble to the Shire.Defeat Thadúr the Ravager on the Northcotton FarmBounder Boffin is inside the Northcotton Farm.

You have defeated Thadúr the Ravager, and Bounder Boffin is eager to speak with you about your victory on the Northcotton Farm.Talk to Bounder Boffin on the Northcotton Farm'This Thadúr seems to be a pretty terrible creature, ${PLAYER}, and he has a band of goblins to do his bidding! He seems to have given them phials of the Darkthorn poison, so it will be no easy task to make your way into the rear of the farm. Go through these doors and follow the path to Thadúr...and good luck!

'It's up to you, ${PLAYER}! Teach this ghastly fellow a lesson!''You've got to stop this Thadúr and his deadly Darkthorn poison, ${PLAYER}! He seems to be back by the main farmhouse here on the Northcotton Farm! Just go through these doors and follow the path around!''Good job, ${PLAYER}! You have rescued Isenbard, and that Thadúr fellow won't be bothering us anymore!

'I do wonder about his last words, though. What did he mean by battle, shadow, and plague returning? And where is Tirith dan Forduath? I've never heard of it and can barely even say it!'Before his defeat, Thadúr the Ravager spoke some mysterious words. You must get to the bottom of them before great evil comes to pass.Ronald Dwale lives north-east of Oatbarton, just outside the village of Dwaling.

Bounder Boffin has sent you to speak with Ronald Dwale about Thadúr's final words. If anyone knows what Tirith dan Forduath is, he will.Talk to Ronald Dwale about Thadúr's final wordsStoneheight is located on the North Downs, north-east of Amon Raith. The town of Stoneheight is located atop a sloping rise north of a deserted farm-house.

Ronald Dwale has teased apart the meaning of Thadúr's final words and has told you that Tirith dan Forduath was the ancient name for a place now called Stoneheight, on the North Downs.Talk to Benedric Jacklin in the town of Stoneheight'I get the impression that this Thadúr was an ancient fellow, which means that the mystery of his final words may be ancient as well. It could be that Tirith dan Forduath is an old name for a place that goes now by a new name! In fact, I am almost certain this is the case!

'If so, then you're not very far from solving this mystery, because a hobbit lives nearby who just loves the history and makeup of old words. If anyone knows what "Tirith dan Forduath" describes, it'll be Ronald Dwale. He is an expert at languages and words.

'Ronald lives north-east of Oatbarton, just outside the village of Dwaling. If you would solve this mystery of words, you should talk to him at once!''Look for Ronald Dwale at his home north-east of Oatbarton, just outside the village of Dwaling. He'll know the meaning of that Thadúr fellow's final words, if anyone does.''Yes? You have a philological question for me? Wonderful, wonderful! It is my favourite study of all: the study of words!'

Ronald Dwale listens eagerly as you recount the final threatening words of Thadúr the Ravager.

'Ah, very interesting, yes. Tirith dan Forduath. A bit on the easy side, but it does have a few points of note. "Tirith" is "guard", and "dan" gives us "against." So this is a location that guards against something, some threat. But what threat?

'Words, as you have certainly observed by now, sometimes behave differently together than they do apart, and such is the case here. Forduath is a construction composed of two words joined for the purpose of linguistics: in this case "forn," "northern," and "dúath," "shadow." You are looking for a place once known as "Guard against the Northern Shadow," and I think I know the place!

'It is not called that anymore, of course, and in my studies I have learned that it was also once called Orod Elon, the "Mountain of the Stars and Stones." But now it is called Stoneheight, and it is on the North Downs, north-east of Amon Raith. A road leads north of a deserted farm-house, climbing up to the heights.''You are looking for a place now called Stoneheight on the North Downs, ${PLAYER}, north-east of Amon Raith. The town of Stoneheight is located atop a sloping rise north of a deserted farm-house.''A friendly face? At last! Oh, my friend, it is terrible! War and death have come to Stoneheight! This place is lost!'You have come to the town of Stoneheight, but it seems to have been recently attacked.Benedric Jacklin has asked you to search further within Stoneheight for survivors of the attack.

The town of Stoneheight was attacked by a merciless figure who moved with a single-minded purpose, and now you must search for survivors.Search Stoneheight for survivors of the attackYou have found a survivor of the attack on Stoneheight, but his wounds seem very severe.Talk to Seward Pearse before he succumbs to his injuries'It was terrible, friend! A tall, menacing figure stalked through the streets of town while his cruel allies slew anyone that moved! I do not know what he wanted, but he moved with a single-minded purpose; I think he was looking for something.

'But why would he visit this destruction upon Stoneheight? Ours was just a town of peaceful folk whose other homes had been lost in the fighting with Orcs out of Angmar! We have done nothing wrong! We were just trying to get a fresh start!'

Benedric is on the very edge of tears.

'There may be others who were able to hide from that terrible figure. You have to search Stoneheight to see if anyone survived! But be careful... the allies of that frightful apparition may still be here!''You have to search the streets of Stoneheight for survivors of the attack, you just have to! We have done nothing to deserve this!''Have you found any survivors of the attack? You have to keep looking!''Who is it...? Are you... a friend...?'

The man is near death; his wounds seem most severe. He coughs weakly and his face contorts painfully. He gathers the little strength he has left, determined to tell you something.

'I... I think I... did this.... I think I drew the horror... to Stoneheight....'The attack on Stoneheight must be avenged, and quickly: allies of the terrible figure that led the attack still remain within its walls.Seward Pearse's home is further within the town of Stoneheight, and he wants you to see if the helm is still there.

Ivar the Blood-hand stalked through the town of Stoneheight, and Seward Pearse believes he came looking for the helm Seward purchased from a merchant.Search further within Stoneheight for sign of the helmWhile in the wreckage of the town of Stoneheight, you saw in-fighting between two of the enemies left by Ivar to finish the job.

Dale Truitt slew Búbhosh-grat and now must be defeated if the people of Stoneheight are to rebuild.Benedric Jacklin wishes to speak with you about what happened to the town of Stoneheight.Talk to Benedric Jacklin in Stoneheight'We came here...for a new life. I met a merchant...he had a helm, said it had passed through the hands of...many adventurers. Inlaid with gems, it was.... 

'He offered a fair...a fair price...and I thought I could pry the gems loose.'

Seward coughs again, and his body shakes with the pain of the effort.

'I could not...loose the gems. Woe betide me! I think...I think the helm's true owner...came to claim it! Ivar! Ivar the Blood-hand! He stalked through the town....

'My home...is further in. See if the helm...is still there. But beware the Blood-hand!''Did I...cause this...to happen...? Did the helm...draw the Blood-hand...to this place? Go to my home, further in, and...see if anything...remains....''Ivar's followers...do not get along...it seems, but do not...underestimate...them!''It feels strange to say that we have been fortunate in such circumstances, ${PLAYER}, but still it is so: for Ivar the Blood-hand has left our town behind. He must have found what he came to find and was content to leave his servants behind. They have now been dealt with!

'But now the real work -- the work of rebuilding -- must begin...if anyone will even want to rebuild this town, that is!'Benedric Jacklin found a tablet with curious markings on it in the wreckage of Stoneheight.Elrond Half-elven is in his library in Rivendell.

You have been given a mysterious tablet that was found in the town of Stoneheight. The tablet is covered in mysterious runes; perhaps Elrond can read them.The Elf-scout Ithriel is scouting the Lost Temple, located at Ost Chall in the northern reaches of Nan Tornaeth.

Elrond has read the runes on the tablet found in Stoneheight, and a mention of the Lost Temple has drawn his notice.Find Ithriel at the Lost Temple'I found this tablet on the ground on the way here, ${PLAYER}. It is covered in evil runes, and I cannot read it, but I believe it was dropped by the Gaunt-lord as he passed through Stoneheight. Perhaps if you can uncover its meaning, you will be able to make some sense of this attack.

'You know Elrond of Rivendell? It is rumoured that he is a master of lore, and he might be able to read the markings on this tablet. Perhaps he can tell us what it says.''It is said that Elrond Half-elven is most learned in the ways of lore; he may be able to read the runes on this tablet. Was it really dropped by Ivar the Blood-hand? Might it tell us where Ivar plans to go next?''These are evil runes, ${PLAYER}. I can read them, though it is not pleasing to do so. The tablet contains directives for Ivar, as you supposed, from the Gaunt-lord named Gortheron. He is instructed to raise an army of wights with which to crush the Free Peoples.

'It is curious, ${PLAYER}, that Ivar's actions in the town of Stoneheight do not seem to have been in line with these orders. Why did the Blood-hand destroy the town as he did, and then leave it? Would not following his orders be more important?

'There is more here: he is told to keep a close eye on Ferndúr, for though he was the Master of Imlad Balchorth for many years, Gortheron has not forgotten what happened to Ferndúr's people in the depths of his temple.

'I do not know what this is in reference to, ${PLAYER}, but I do know where this temple, this Lost Temple, is located, because a scout of Rivendell has observed worrisome activity there of late. Assemble a fellowship of allies and find her at Ost Chall, in the northern reaches of Nan Tornaeth.''The mention of the Lost Temple on the tablet from Stoneheight has me concerned, ${PLAYER}. Gather a fellowship of allies and join my scout Ithriel at the Lost Temple, beyond Ost Chall in the northern reaches of Nan Tornaeth.''If you are here to explore this Lost Temple, I must warn you, friends! The Gaunt-lord Ferndúr has come to this place, but to what purpose I cannot guess. There is something else, too, something that I cannot explain.'Ithriel has told you that the Gaunt-lord Ferndúr the Virulent has come to the Lost Temple for some unknown purpose.The crypts of the Lost Temple are beyond the grounds of the temple.

Ithriel has asked you to fight the mercenary band hired by Ferndúr as you make your way across the temple-grounds.Cross the temple-grounds and enter the cryptsThe mercenary Scristan is seriously injured just within the entrance to the crypts of the Lost Temple.

You have found an injured mercenary, but he makes no hostile sign.Talk to Scristan'Ferndúr the Virulent would be worrisome enough, ${PLAYER}, but he has hired a mercenary band, and they have been prowling about the grounds of the Lost Temple. It is highly unusual, and I do not know why Ferndúr would deign to work with such Men. It is my understanding that the desires of Men are beneath the notice of such creatures as the Gaunt-lords.

'But this mercenary band is certainly in the employ of the Gaunt-lord. Make your way through the grounds, and you will see it is so; enter the crypts and look for signs of what Ferndúr is planning.''Make your way to the crypts, beyond the temple-grounds, fighting the mercenary band hired by Ferndúr as you go.''You found an injured mercenary within the crypts, but he made no sign of attacking? You should see what he has to say, if he does not appear to be hostile. Perhaps he can illuminate Ferndúr's interest in this place.''I don't have the will to fight you, ${RACE}. Just leave me be. We were given good coin to come to this forsaken place, but once we descended into this crypt, something didn't feel right to me.

'I told them I would go no further. Coward, they called me, and left me here with a knife in my side to keep me company. Some payment! Didn't know Colfrith before yesterday, but me and Rimbald had done lots of jobs before this one. I guess that's what friendship is good for...these days....

'But they'll get theirs! If the screams I heard not long ago are any indication, they already have! Ha ha ha ha!'The mercenary Scristan has been left for dead by his former allies, but he wants you to avenge himself upon his employer, the Gaunt-lord Ferndúr.Ferndúr the Virulent is further within the crypts of the Lost Temple.

The mercenary Scristan believes that the Gaunt-lord intended to throw away the lives of the mercenary band he hired, and Scristan wants him to pay for it.Defeat Ferndúr the Virulent within the Lost TempleElrond wishes to speak with you about your defeat of Ferndúr the Virulent.

You have defeated the Gaunt-lord Ferndúr in the depths of the Lost Temple, and now Elrond wishes to speak with you.Talk to Elrond in the depths of the Lost Temple'It's all over now; I care not a whit for them! Let 'em suffer, I say. They should have listened to me and saved their own lives; could be they're wanting for the blade they stuck me with, eh? Ha ha ha!'

Scristan's laughter seems to cause him pain, and he wheezes with the effort of it.

'But I don't like being deceived, ${RACE}, and that gaunt fellow's coin wasn't good enough to get me to go further into this place without explanation. I want you to make him pay for tryin' to use us. His coin'd need to be better to get Scristan to throw his life away!

Scristan laughs again, painfully.

'My life's been thrown away, without my consent! That's a good deal cheaper, it seems. Find this gaunt fellow, this Ferndúr, further in and make him remember me.''I have no love for my old mates now, but Ferndúr should pay for bringing us here without telling us the risks. I don't like it. He's further within. I suspect he never intended us to leave this place alive!''I'm glad you showed that gaunt fellow not to trifle with us. Serves him right.''You and your allies have done well to dispatch Ferndúr, ${PLAYER}. He was as cruel to his allies as he was to his enemies, and my investigation of the crypts has confirmed what previously I only suspected. But now the story is clear to me.

'Long ago, the Men of Rhudaur waged battle against the other remnants of the North Kingdom. They worshipped Ferndúr, believing he could grant them the victory...and indeed, the plague he harnessed would be so great a weapon that Arthedain and Cardolan could not hope to stand against its threat.

'He gave to them the plague, the Red Cloud, but they could not control it. The Men of Rhudaur who lived and worshipped at the Lost Temple were laid low by the weapon they sought to wield. It was their undoing, but Ferndúr did not weep for them, though it cost him many servants. He merely laughed as the Red Cloud claimed its victims.

'I am glad you have ended his threat.'Thyrnir noticed that the Orcs of Gathbúrz have left their fire-oil supplies haphazardly piled about in the chaos of the Elven assault.Fire-pots and supply crates can be found in the Orc-forts throughout northern Gathbúrz.

Thyrnir has asked you to destroy the goblins' stockpiles of fire-oil by igniting them with the goblins' own fire-pots.Collect fire-pots (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Burn oil-stores (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thyrnir at Thangúlhad in Gathbúrz.

You should report your success to Thyrnir.'Hello there, ${PLAYER}. Have you had a look down there in the Orc-camps yet? It's a real mess. Orcs and goblins aren't too good at staying organized when you put a good fright into them, from what I've seen.

'Anyhow, I noticed that they have got the oil for their siege-engines piled up in great heaps of boxes and crates, just lying about. That's dangerous...you never know when it might catch fire!

'If you were to steal a few of their fire-pots and use them to set the rest ablaze, I imagine it would help add a warm glow to these dreary slopes for the evening.''The foolish goblins have left their fire-oil stores out where they can be tampered with...I trust that you will do what needs to be done....''Ha! I could see the blazes from here, ${PLAYER}! That will teach the fool goblins to leave dangerous toys lying about! Good work, my friend.'Within the depths of this cavern, several of the Uch-lûth clansmen and women have been locked away in cages awaiting a grisly fate.Seek out guards within the cavern and recover a crude key from them.Recover a crude key from the guardsDiscover and open a locked cell with the crude key that you recovered.Release the first captive.Seek out guards within the cavern and recover a crude key from them.Recover a crude key from the guardsDiscover and open a locked cell with the crude key that you recovered.Release the second captive.Seek out guards within the cavern and recover a crude key from them.Recover a crude key from the guardsDiscover and open a locked cell with the crude key that you recovered.Release the last captive.The Houndmaster Laodegan has heard that an outsider has attacked the dungeon and is guarding the entrance.Defeat Laodegan near the entrance'These duhirun attacked our hunting-party several days ago. Sadly, I have heard the screams of friends being dragged out of the cavern to a grim fate.''These duhirun attacked our hunting-party several days ago. Sadly, I have heard the screams of friends being dragged out of the cavern to a grim fate.''These duhirun attacked our hunting-party several days ago. Sadly, I have heard the screams of friends being dragged out of the cavern to a grim fate.''Thank you for dispatching these filthy creatures. I was taken several weeks ago, and I thought that I would never escape. Thank you and may the blessing of the Uch-gwirod be with you.'Thank you for dispatching these filthy creatures. I was taken several weeks ago, and I thought that I would never escape. Thank you and may the blessing of the Uch-gwirod be with you.''Thank you for dispatching these filthy creatures. I was taken several weeks ago, and I thought that I would never escape. Thank you and may the blessing of the Uch-gwirod be with you.''I do not care how you came upon this place, Duvodiad; however, your generosity shall be rewarded. May your ancestors find peace and your heirs be hearty and strong.''I do not care how you came upon this place, Duvodiad; however, your generosity shall be rewarded. May your ancestors find peace and your heirs be hearty and strong.''I do not care how you came upon this place, Duvodiad; however, your generosity shall be rewarded. May your ancestors find peace and your heirs be hearty and strong.'Nona has asked you to get to the bottom of the mystery concerning her brother Wadu and the dwarves of Zudrugund.Frithgeir is working to prepare the brew for Nár so you may speak to him without difficulty.Talk to FrithgeirFrithgeir is preparing the brew for Nár and has recommended that while you wait you speak with the members of the Grey Company who have come to Zudrugund.Halbarad wishes to speak with you while Frithgeir prepares the brew for Nár.Nona has burst into the dwarf-hall, intending to confront the dwarves about the fate of her brother Wadu.Learn what Nona wants from the dwarvesNona has demanded an explanation from you.Frithgeir has prepared the brew for Nár, and you should now see if it has the promised effect.Listen to the exchange between Nona and NárNár seems to be more aware of his surroundings, thanks to the brew supplied by Frithgeir, and may now tell you what he said to Wadu.Nár has offered to take you to the mines below Zudrugund, where he last remembers seeing Wadu. Perhaps the mystery of what happened to Nona's brother here remains below.You have travelled deep into the mine beneath Zudrugund with Nár, Elladan, and Nona. Elladan has had a curious feeling that you have been watched for some time now.Talk to ElladanNona wears a puzzled expression. She seems dissatisfied with the journey and wishes to speak with you.Elladan is thinking, pondering what to do next, now that you are deep beneath Zudrugund and Nár is passing out of awareness again.A locked gate blocks further progress into the mine, and Elladan has asked you to find the lever that should open it.Find and use the lever that unlocks the gateYou have pulled the lever to open the gate and should now rejoin your friends below.Rejoin Elladan, Nona, and Nár belowYour friends are missing. Have they already gone on past the gate that blocked passage deeper into the mine?Your friends are deep within the mine, seeking the truth of what befell Wadu in the depths when he came here with Nár.Follow Elladan to Nár and listen to the dwarf's taleNona has learned the truth: that her brother Wadu found his courage far below Zudrugund and defeated a mighty gwiber in the depths.Elladan is waiting to speak with you about Nár's mysterious and sudden change.'We are a long way from Evendim, ${PLAYER}. I wonder how our friends are managing in our absence?'

Calenglad sighs and looks suspiciously at Nár.

'I understand why we have taken this brief excursion into the hills, for if Nona is satisfied by what we learn here, our dealings with the Uch-lûth should be made easier. But what can we really learn from this dwarf? He does not seem even to notice us!''Is it the smell of the brew that has kept the bats from attacking Nár? We should hope that it was not, for he seems to have exhausted it. A few drops may remain, but the odour is much less than it was, and he seems to be falling once more out of awareness.'

Elladan shakes his head.

'There is a gate ahead, and if I have learned anything of dwarves, it is that such a gate certainly has a lever somewhere to open it. Find the lever and return to us, ${PLAYER}, and we will proceed. I fear that this journey will soon end in failure, for without a guide that can speak to us in ways we understand, what can we hope to learn here?''I am sorry we did not wait, ${PLAYER}, but Nár gave a shout and ran forward when the gate opened, and we did not want to lose him.

'He is just ahead, with Nona.''How can Nár know of the errand of the Grey Company, ${PLAYER}? And more than that: how can he know of my father's words to my brother and me? How can he know we were considering taking the Paths of the Dead once we meet with Aragorn?

'I must tell Halbarad and the other Rangers of this. I think things may have just become more complicated.''I have been listening to Nár's ramblings, ${PLAYER}, and I have managed to piece together some of his history. I believe he was once the servant or friend of Thrór, grandfather of Thorin Oakenshield, and that this was one of their homes in exile.

'Thrór did not return to Erebor, but it seems to me he made another journey, and Nár accompanied him. From his words, I believe they came into danger, and....''I do not like this. Why do the bats of this cavern not attack the dwarf? Why does he walk among them without danger?

'Wadu came into this mine full of fear and uncertainty. What happened here that he emerged from it with courage, courage that would lead him to his death?

'The longer this journey takes, the more questions it poses, ${PLAYER}! I want to be above-ground again, in the sunlight!''My brother...even after the terrible things my father said to him, and even after what I said to him, in my frustration....'

Nona trails off, staring at the body of the enormous gwiber.

'He mastered his fear, ${PLAYER}, and achieved a victory that even the bravest and strongest warriors of the Uch-lûth could not hope to match.

'And now he is gone, slain by enemies who waited in hiding for him to pass. The cowards! They were nothing compared to this great beast, but they laid him low where even it could not! My Wadu....''I do not know what to make of this dwarf, ${PLAYER}. Where did all of his people go? Why would they leave him behind? And what could he have seen that might leave him so...addled?

'Frithgeir seems to be kind-hearted, and it seems to me that Nár would be hard-pressed to survive here for any period of time without him, but there is something not quite right about these two.''So many books, ${PLAYER}, and none of them taken when Nár's people left him. It puzzles me. It is clear that many dwarves once lived here, but where they have gone and why is a mystery. I suspect that even Frithgeir's brew will not clear Nár's mind sufficiently for him to provide us the answers we seek.''The water is boiling already, and now I will add the ingredients you collected. The odour will be quite pungent, so perhaps you should speak with the rest of your friends that have come to Zudrugund while it steeps.

'The odour is rather unpleasant, though I find that it does clear the cobwebs away when I am feeling under the weather.'Nár shakes his head, breathing deeply of the steam billowing out of the mug.

'Wadu...I know that name. We went to the father-lode, where rang the sound of hundreds of dwarf-hammers, far below Zudrugund.

'It is curious. I remember walking with Wadu among the great turning water-wheels of the Old Father, but I have not seen him since. He is not still down there, is he?'

You tell Nár that Wadu has been slain, and his beard creases into a frown.

'Ah, that is a shame. He was most brave. Wasn't he? I can take you to the mine, if you'd like.''Are you ready to go to the mines below this hall? Very good. I am ready to show you the way.'Nona listens impatiently as you explain what the situation in Zudrugund seems to be.

'I want answers for myself. My brother was changed after he came here, and this Nár knows why. He will tell me why, or it will go badly for him!''There must be a lever somewhere around here that will open the locked gate. You must find it, ${PLAYER}.''We must all return to Zudrugund. I do not know what this means, but I fear for every answer we obtained in this place we have received twice as many questions.'Nár looks around in an interested fashion, but you cannot tell if he even sees you at the moment.'There must be a lever somewhere that opens the gate. Perhaps high above us? Are not the ways of dwarves strange?''Please, I need to concentrate. Speak with your friends here while I prepare the brew.''We are far beneath the earth now, ${PLAYER}, and a feeling has been growing on my mind for some time now. I think someone, or something, has watched as we descended. I cannot pinpoint it, but I believe some intelligence has observed our passage from Zudrugund.

'Something else has made me uneasy. How much of Frithgeir's brew has Nár already consumed? And how much remains? The dwarf has been alert for our descent, but what happens when the steaming brew has been exhausted?'Halbarad's plan is set into motion: for Fúni Gem-cutter to fashion imitations of Aragorn's ring of Barahir, based on Candaith's description.Halbarad is waiting to speak with you outside the ring-forges.Talk to HalbaradFúsi Gem-cutter has emerged from the ring-forges with a request for you.Talk to Fúsi Gem-cutterTreasure-coffers from Minas Elendúr can be found in the corner of the ruins.

Fúsi Gem-cutter has asked you to bring him more gold from the coffers you retrieved from Minas Elendúr.Pick up a treasure-coffer from the corner of the room where they are storedCandaith has asked to speak with you, if you have a moment to spare.Talk to CandaithElrohir is waiting for you in the courtyard north of Candaith and Halbarad.Talk to ElrohirA distant shout has broken the stillness. What has happened?Investigate the distant shoutWhat danger has come to the ring-forges, and what does it signify for the Grey Company?Golodir has remained at the entrance courtyard of the ring-forges in order to delay the attacking uruks. He needs your help.Talk to GolodirThe uruks have been defeated and the plans of the Grey Company remain intact.Talk to Halbarad'The ring-making is going well? Good. I gave the jewel-smith a description of the ring of Barahir as I remembered it, and if he has the skill he claims to have, his imitations should be suitable.

'Elrohir wanted me to send you to him, if you had a moment. He was contemplating our plans beneath a tree in the courtyard to the north of here, if you would speak with him.''The crafting of the rings sought by Halbarad goes well, ${PLAYER}? Good. If these preparations give our enemies even the slightest pause, the time they take will be counted five and seven times in value.'

Elrohir pauses for a moment, thinking, and then takes you into his confidence.

'Before we left Imladris, my father gave to me messages for Aragorn, confident that he would understand them. But I too understand their meaning, and that understanding fills me with worry. He bade me to remind Aragorn of the words of the seer Malbeth, and of the Paths of the Dead. I will tell you of...'

Suddenly, a shout breaks the silence, and Elrohir leaps to his feet!'The work goes well, ${PLAYER}! Already some of the rings have been made, and they are identical to the description with which my father was provided. But the gold is running low! Very little gold is needed for each ring, of course, but my father can use only small quantities of the material when it is melted, or imperfections will arise.

'Bring more coffers of gold to me from where they are being stored!''Fúni Gem-cutter has been working hard within, melting gold with which to fashion quality imitations of the ring borne by Aragorn our chieftain. Candaith, learned in lore as he is, described the ring to Fúni, and the dwarf has pledged that his reproductions will deceive all but the most studious observer.''According to Fúsi Gem-cutter, his father's work is going well and smoothly. With no more interruptions, I feel that we will soon see an end to this delay.'

Halbarad laughs softly, a sound that has been missing from the journey of the Grey Company, unnoticed until now.

'I say delay, but I feel better for having a secret held in reserve. Let our foes mill about in confusion, while the true heir of Isildur makes stealth his ally! It is good to be doing something.''${PLAYER}...a party of warring uruks...has climbed the path to this place. And I have seen others...in the distance. It will not be long...before they arrive....'

Halros grabs your arm, desperate to tell you something else.

'Golodir...stayed behind...to hold them back. Help...help him....''A party of...uruks! They have...climbed the path.... And there are others...in the distance. It will not be long...before they arrive....'

Radanir seeks to catch his breath, but the exertion has been too much and the urgency of his message cannot be restrained.

'Golodir...is at the entrance.... He hopes to...hold them back. Help him...help him quickly....''Elrohir did not say why he wished to speak with you, but he did seem concerned about something. I wonder what is troubling him? Something about the road ahead?''Where are you keeping the coffers of gold you brought out of those ruins, ${PLAYER}? Go get one! My father needs more high-quality gold if he is to continue making these imitations.''I hope there is enough gold for the rings your friends require. It takes more than ordinary gold, you see. Only the highest quality gold can be used if these are to be sufficiently realistic, and when melted, only very small amounts of this gold is suitable.''We have victory! The uruks will not again underestimate the Dúnedain! It is good to stand against our foes with true allies behind and beside us, is it not? I had forgotten what it felt like, ${PLAYER}. I have been bowed beneath too many shadows of late. It is good to cast them off!''His father Fúni must still be working to fashion recreations of the ring borne by Aragorn. What must Fúsi Gem-cutter require of us?''We have done well this day, ${PLAYER}. May Fúni Gem-cutter's work go as well!''Despair not, ${PLAYER}. We will defeat these uruks, or we will delay them for as long as is necessary for the remainder of our Company to complete the task for which we have come to these forges.'Candaith is telling you the tale of the Oath-breakers and the Paths of the Dead.You are Iarvund, a soldier of the young Kingdom of Gondor, come to the Hill of Erech with your friend Urcheneb to serve your master Isildur.Talk to UrchenebIsildur is standing by the Stone of Erech, waiting to speak with you.Talk to IsildurIsildur has asked you to look for the Men of the Mountain to arrive from the north slope of the hill of Erech.

Isildur and his trusted soldiers have come to Erech and now wait for the arrival of the Men of the Mountain.Wait for the Men of the Mountain to arriveThe Men of the Mountain have arrived. Will they now swear the oath of allegiance to Isildur?Witness the meeting between Isildur and the Men of the MountainThe King of the Mountain has sworn to fight against Sauron should he return to Middle-earth, and in return, his people will have the aid of Gondor.Talk to BritouTalk to BodúanTalk to King RiocUrcheneb and Isildur are on the summit of Erech.Talk to UrchenebMany years passed, and a shadow fell upon the Kingdom of Gondor.Listen to Candaith continue the telling of the taleMany years passed, and a shadow fell upon the Kingdom of Gondor.Talk to DorwëIt is as Isildur feared. Garth Lotheg has been besieged by Orcs, and its defenders slain.Talk to IsildurThe Orcs have erected a disturbing idol in the centre of Garth Lotheg.Examine the disturbing idol erected by the Orcs in Garth LothegIsildur is in the centre of Garth Lotheg, waiting to speak with you about the disturbing idol of Sauron the Orcs erected there.Talk to IsildurCandaith is continuing the story of Isildur and the oath of the Men of the Mountain.Listen to Candaith's story continueIsildur has returned to the Stone of Erech to summon the Men of the Mountain to fulfill their oath, but they have not yet arrived.Talk to DorwëIsildur is at the summit by the Stone of Erech, waiting for the Men of the Mountain to arrive and fulfill their oath.Talk to IsildurKing Rioc and the Men of the Mountain have come to Erech, summoned by Isildur to fulfill their oath.Wait for the Men of the Mountain to arriveThe Men of the Mountain have arrived, but they refuse to honour the terms of their oath.Support Isildur as he curses the Men of the Mountain for their refusalIsildur is at the summit of Erech.Talk to Isildur'There is a great deal we can learn from you and from the Kingdom of Gondor. We are now as brothers, are we not? The bond of our oath will tie us together, and let our shared enemies tremble! Gondor and the Mountain!''King Rioc has led us for these many years, and his word is hewn from stone like that of the Mountain itself, with roots as deep. Let Sauron show himself again, at his peril! He would dare it not, when confronted with we Men of the Mountain and you, the sons of Númenor and now Gondor!''Iarvund, is it? Yes, I thought so.'

Isildur looks about expansively, admiring the view from the summit.

'This has been a year of toil, Iarvund. But in moments like this, breathing the clean air and looking out over the forests of this Middle-earth, I can almost believe that this is home.''At this moment, there are none in this land more proud than I, King Rioc of the Mountain. There is so much our people can learn from each other, and by sharing our strength, we will be the stronger for it. Your lord Isildur was wise to reach out to us, and we are honoured to have been called to this place.

'Should Sauron return, he will face our combined might and be fearful!''This is the place, Iarvund: the Hill of Erech. You stayed behind at the encampment when our lord was last here, but I was in the party that wrestled the Stone to the summit. Several days of toil, true, but I can think of no better way to mark the escape of Númenor's sons from the deluge.

'Seeing the forest we came through from this height gives me a greater appreciation for its beauty, and I forgive its branches the many scratches we sustained! This new Kingdom of Gondor is a beautiful land indeed; may it stand evermore!

'This way, Iarvund! Isildur has gone ahead, and we must not let our lord wait on our arrival!''What a day this has been, Iarvund! We must learn from Isildur's example: do not undervalue the worth of our alliances! It would be easy to look upon the wonders of Gondor, even this early in its life, and think we can conquer any enemy.

'But Sauron is conniving, and if he does somehow return, as Isildur fears, we will be glad for the strong warriors of the Mountain.''It does not look good, Beriadar. The Men of the Mountain should have been here by now, but they have sent no word and made no appearance.

'I have never seen our lord Isildur like this. It may be best for King Rioc if he does not come here at all.''Our wait has ended, Beriadar. King Rioc and some of his people now climb to the summit. They are late, but their support against Sauron will excuse them their tardiness.''I did not believe the whispers, Beriadar. I thought Rioc's people would be as good as their word, but it seems the rumours were true: they worshipped Sauron in secret during the Dark Years, and now he has returned they cast their lot in with his.

'Standing here upon Erech, I look from the height and see for a great distance, even into the years ahead. Know this: my words to Rioc were not empty. This war will last for countless years, and if Gondor defies Sauron, Rioc's people will be summoned once more before the end.

'They will never know peace until they have fulfilled their oath. This I vow and let all bear witness to it!''Beriadar, this is terrible! Look what has happened to Garth Lotheg! This is the reason we have heard naught from its defenders...they are slain and the fort taken!

'How could such evil have come to Gondor without our knowledge?''We know what this means, Beriadar. It means that these Orcs are not leaderless, but were given purpose by Sauron their master.''I have called the Men of the Mountain to this place. Will they come? Gondor is young and needs her allies.

'Look for them to arrive from the north side of the Stone, Iarvund.''It is a good day, Iarvund. Gondor will be strong, but she will be stronger with allies, and this night, she will have more than she had this morning.''Look at the Stone up there, Iarvund. It will stand here long after we are gone. I feel it in my bones.

'Or perhaps I feel the aching memory of the countless men and horses used to haul it to the summit!''Examine that idol, Beriadar. It fills me with unease, for I know what it portends.''This is the work of Orcs, Beriadar. You and Dorwë come with me. We will fight our way into the fort while the rest of our band approaches Garth Lotheg from the flank. They will rue the day they spilled Gondorian blood on our soil!'If Ergothorn can be trusted, a warband of uruks marches on Zudrugund from the south, with orders involving Nár that could endanger the errand of the Grey Company.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>You have come with Radanir to Zudrugund in pursuit of the uruk-band.Talk to Radanir on the path up to ZudrugundCandaith is further up the path to Zudrugund.Fight your way further up the path and talk to CandaithNúrzumgrat has left a mighty troll to defend the span below Zudrugund. It must be defeated if you are to reach the top.Defeat the Defender of the SpanNow that the Defender of the Span is defeated, you can approach Zudrugund, where the leader of the uruks confronts the dwarves.Fight your way up the path to Zudrugund and talk to CalengladNár is outside Zudrugund, and you should speak to him now that the uruks are defeated.Talk to Nár outside Zudrugund'Ha ha ha ha! That is how you slay an uruk! But where is Thráin? He would want the honour of defeating the leader of this band, and it was wrong of us to deprive him of it!''You must defeat the Defender of the Span if we are to come to Zudrugund!''I will be well enough after a short rest, ${PLAYER}. Help me to my feet and inside Zudrugund. I have something I need to tell you about Nár. He was acting peculiarly before the attack.''We have to do something about this troll, ${PLAYER}! The Defender of the Span keeps us from Zudrugund, where Nár and Frithgeir must be in mortal danger!''Continue upwards to Zudrugund, ${PLAYER}! Find Candaith along the path further ahead and help him fight the uruks!''We are here and not much behind the uruks, if that is their foul stench I sense on the air! The other Rangers who came with us will approach from the other slope, and we will rejoin them when they have scouted the heights.

'For now let us continue and defeat whatever uruks have stayed behind to defend their way back down the mountain!''There are uruks further up the mountain! I have heard some of their talk, and they call themselves the Grishgúk. Their leader is called Núrzumgrat, and it sounds like he has come to the summit! We must fight through these uruks, ${PLAYER}!''${PLAYER}! This way! The uruks are at the summit! We have to do something, and quickly!'One of the Nine known to mortals as The Gloom of Nurn has emerged to answer your challenge at the gates of Dol Guldur. You and your allies must gather all of your strength if you have any hope of defeating him and his forces.Defeat the Gloom of Nurn and his forces before the gates of Dol Guldur.Defeat the Gloom of NurnReturn to the Stone of Challenge'You have completed the battle at the gate!'Are you prepared to leave the field of battle?Vatnar has detected a band of goblin-sappers digging a tunnel below the cellars of Thangúlhad. He and his brother Thyrnir intend to invade the goblin-tunnel and collapse it before they can undermine the tower's foundation.

<rgb=#00ff00>WARNING: Death From Below is a high difficulty solo challenge quest. Beware, all who enter therein.</rgb>Speak with Vatnar and begin your counterattack against the goblin-sappers!Talk to VatnarTalk to Vatnar to return to the surface.'Right then, let us hie ourselves from this stinking tunnel. We wouldn't want Rodelleth and her Elves getting lonesome for lack of our company, would we?''Let us be upon our task, shall we?''Aye! That did it! Well fought, ${PLAYER}! The goblins' works are broken, and the remaining sappers seem to have run off.

'It must have been that scowl of yours that did it....'You have entered the dungeons of Dol Guldur in search of the prisoner Bori with a small company of Elves.Ningloril is in the antechamber to the dungeons of Dol Guldur.

The Elves have stormed the dungeons, and Ningloril is waiting to speak to you in the main antechamber.Talk to NinglorilBori is being held somewhere in the East Wing of the dungeons.

Find Bori in the East Wing of the dungeons and free him.Find Bori and talk to himBori is in a cell in the East Wing of the dungeons of Dol Guldur.

Bori is waiting for you to leave barrels of nightshade in the hallway to knock out any guards that arrive, then to escort him out of the dungeon.Talk to BoriNingloril has run into trouble in the antechamber of the dungeons.

Talk to Ningloril when she has been freed from the cell.Talk to Ningloril'${PLAYER}, you have come for me at last! I did not dare to hope for rescue, but I cannot deny that I am pleased to see you!

'You need to make ready for our escape -- pick up some of these barrels of nightshade and bring them out into the hall. If any additional guards are on their way, the fumes from the nightshade should render them unconscious!

'Let me know when you have adequately prepared, my friend! At that time we will make our escape!''We have made it into the dungeons, ${PLAYER}, and now it is up to you to find and rescue Bori. We believe he is being held in a cell in the East Wing.

'We have managed to keep our infiltration silent thus far, and some of the guards in the East Wing may be asleep on the job. Let them sleep and rescue Bori!''We ran into some trouble without you, ${PLAYER}, but victory is ours this day! Let us leave this grim dungeon!''Let us be free of these dungeons! I am ready to see the sun again, or as much of the sun as can be seen beneath the trees of Mirkwood.''I am ready to be free of this place, ${PLAYER}! Let us leave these dungeons behind us!

'Stay close to me, for I do not know the way out, and this darkness holds great fear for me.'You have come to walk the Forsaken Road beneath the Lich Bluffs, where it is said a number of Oath-breakers from the time of Isildur still dwell, suffering from their curse.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>Radanir stands within the Forsaken Road, wishing to speak with you about the grim place.Talk to RadanirYou have not ventured far along the Forsaken Road and have already encountered one of the Oath-breakers cursed by Isildur.Talk to MaonirHalbarad has confronted Maonir along the Forsaken Road.Talk to HalbaradMaonir has given in, at last, and seems to wish to speak with you.Talk to MaonirMaonir told you that Britou wants to speak with you, and that you could find him by keeping the chasm on your right.Find and talk to BritouCandaith is in the depths of the Forsaken Road, confronting the Oath-breaker Britou.Talk to CandaithYou have been betrayed. You must escape from the Forsaken Road.Run back through the Forsaken Road and talk to Radanir to escape!'It was unwise of you to come here, warm-bloods. This place belongs to the Dead Who Do Not Rest, and all your kind who stray here perish.''Who dares disturb this place? Who dares venture where only the Dead may walk?

'The living may not pass. This road is not for you. Turn back! Turn back while you still have the power to do so!''You fight passing well, for a child. Britou will want to speak with you. Britou always wants to speak with those who brave this road. Keep the chasm on your right, and you will find him.''This is an evil place, ${PLAYER}, and the dread of it lies like a cloud upon my limbs! And so cold...I should think I would never be warm again.

'This is a place of the Dead, kept by them throughout the long years. Will they rally to Aragorn's cause? We must learn if it be so.''What has happened? All of a sudden, the Oath-breakers fell upon us, and we have only driven them off for the moment! We have to get out of here!''We will explore this side of the chasm, ${PLAYER}. You must go on ahead and search the other side of the great rift in the earth for Britou, he of whom Maonir spoke.''We will stay here, but you should find Britou on the other side of the chasm. We must learn what we may along the Forsaken Road and cover as much ground as we can.''You are correct...we must get out of here! Hurry, before it is too late for us!''Stay on your guard, ${PLAYER}. These Men are without honour. We are here to listen to their words, but we must remain cautious.''This seems to me a good sign, ${PLAYER}! If the Oath-breakers will fulfill their oath to Isildur, we will command an army the like of which has never been seen in Middle-earth. Surely victory will not be far behind!'You have come with Calenglad to the Flooded Ward to clear it of robbers so that his heart may rest easy as he travels with the Grey Company.Calenglad is in the Flooded Ward of Annúminas.Talk to CalengladCalenglad is standing in front of a tomb-entrance in the Flooded Ward.Talk to CalengladThe robbers have put their gathered plunder into a strongbox located within the crypt.Investigate the tomb-robbers' plunderCalenglad is speaking with Gwindeth, the Blue Lady of Nenuial.Wait until Calenglad is satisfied that he can join the Grey CompanyCalenglad is inside the crypt in the Flooded Ward, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Calenglad at Tinnudir'Let us leave this place, ${PLAYER}. The air has grown chill of a sudden, and I am ready to return to Tinnudir.''We have arrived, ${PLAYER}. This section of the city suffered greatly in the battle against Amarthiel's forces, and robbers have come from the surrounding lands in order to plunder it of goods.

'The tree-lined streets of this ward were quite beautiful before the fighting, deserted though they were. Now the walls are cracked and many of the buildings and tombs that are found here have been flooded, or are flooding.

'Let us save what relics we may from the undeserving pockets of robbers, ${PLAYER}.''There are more robbers inside this tomb, ${PLAYER}. Clear them out and recover whatever relics they have already stolen. While you see to that, I will investigate some of these other buildings. I will meet up with you again once the tomb is clear of these villains.''Search the crypt for the robbers' plunder, ${PLAYER}. They will have gathered it into a strongbox.''You must think me an old fool, ${PLAYER}. There is no protection I can offer Gwindeth that she cannot manage for herself. Still it is difficult to leave behind people and places we hold dear.

'I did not want to leave without saying good-bye. I have now done so, but it does not make the leaving any easier.'Somewhere inside Lhaid Ogo, a messenger from Dunland may be in trouble.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest is meant for a fellowship. If you choose to undertake this quest solo, you will receive an Inspired Greatness bonus, but the challenge will be great.</rgb>The muddy depths of Lhaid Ogo are home to many decaying horrors, and a Dunlending messenger may be lost within.Search Lhaid Ogo for the lost Dunlending messengerYou have found Anirin in the depths of Lhaid Ogo and should speak with him.Talk to Anirin in Lhaid Ogo'Who...who are you? I need to...to see the Brenin...at Lhanuch....'

You assure him that you will bring him to Glynn Brenin at once.

'This great beast attacked me as I was passing the entrance to this cave, and I could not get away. Thank you for saving me! It is fortunate you were passing by...and so far from habitation!

'I bring a message from Lheu Brenin of Tûr Morva, in the south: he urges Glynn Brenin not to believe the lies of the Wizard Saruman! The Wizard has sent agents to the Brenin to try and buy his loyalty, and he is worried that other clans may have accepted these offers. The Uch-luth have not done so? Oh, good fortune! You must bring me to Lhanuch at once!''Look out! It still lives!''We should leave at once! My leg is still hurt, but if I lean on you I can make it.'You have entered the massive cavern of Kolo-hampât with the Ranger Lothrandir in search of the 'screaming shadow' that has interfered with the Lossoth hunters.Lothrandir is in Kolo-hampât, seeking the 'screaming shadow' that has interfered with the Lossoth hunters.Talk to LothrandirLothrandir is in Kolo-hampât, waiting to speak with you.Talk to LothrandirLothrandir has gone his own way to search the depths of Kolo-hampât, and now you must seek out the mysterious 'screaming shadow' yourself.Find the screaming shadow within the massive cavernYou have found a mysterious girl crouched by a ragged camp-fire in the depths of Kolo-hampât.Talk to the mysterious girlYou have found a mysterious girl crouched by a ragged camp-fire in the depths of Kolo-hampât.Learn the history of this mysterious girlYou have found a mysterious girl crouched by a ragged camp-fire in the depths of Kolo-hampât and must now defend yourself from her.Defend yourself from Gun AinLothrandir must not be defeatedLothrandir has chosen to spare the life of Gun Ain and is waiting to speak to you.Talk to Lothrandir'Let us leave this place, ${PLAYER}. Gun Ain will have to find somewhere to make an honest living, somewhere she will not hinder the Lossoth. She will find a more forgiving place to live, or she will die in this harsh place. Others who served Angmar would not have even that chance.''Look at this, ${PLAYER}! I had no idea such an expansive cavern existed beneath the frozen tundra of Forochel.

'We must be on guard as we make our way through this cavern in search of the screaming shadow that has harried the Lossoth hunters. It has carried away much of their slain prey, and what few tracks I was able to find came to the entrance of this cave before being lost in the shifting snows of this land.

'Do not be dazzled by the reflections in the ice! We must remain alert to all threats while we search the cavern!''We need to cover more ground, ${PLAYER}. If we search different areas of this cavern, we will have a better chance of finding what we seek.''Keep searching the depths of Kolo-hampât for the mysterious shadow that steals away the prey of the Lossoth hunters.'The girl stares at you fiercely, her eyes wild with anger and fear. She shivers in the cold, and her face is thin and drawn. There is a feral air about her, and she seems tense, poised to strike.'I do not want your mercy! A pox on all ${CLASS}s and Rangers!''Stay your hand, ${PLAYER}, and I will put away my weapon as well.

'Listen to me, Gun Ain! You served Angmar, this we know, and it has gone badly for you since you were cast from your clan. You cannot steal from the Lossoth as you have done; you must find a new home, or you will die here among the ice and the snow. None would mourn you, No-name, if you perished as you are.

'We will do you no more harm. Swear to me that you will leave the lands of the Lossoth and seek an honest living elsewhere. This mercy is yours today. Do not throw it away. If you continue to trouble the people of Forochel, you will find that my heart has exhausted its pity.'You have found a cavern carved deep beneath the fortress of Dol Guldur. Could this be the same passage Gandalf used to infiltrate the fastness of the Necromancer long ago?You have found a cavern beneath Dol Guldur and must clear it of foes if it is to be used by the Golden Host.Clear Thurimen, the cavern beneath the foundations of Dol Guldur, of foesYou have cleared much of Thurimen of threats and should proceed onward and upwards.Proceed further into Thurimen, clearing it of foesYou have cleared much of Thurimen of threats and should proceed onward and upwards.Proceed further into Thurimen, clearing it of foesBróin and Sigileth have come to Thurimen and are amazed to see how well it suits their purposes.Talk to Bróin'I can hardly believe it, but this passage is exactly what we need in order to launch our final assault upon Dol Guldur! We will surprise them from below and be within the walls of the fortress before our foes know they are under attack!

'We will return here with a sizeable company and launch our final assault!''Are you ready to leave? We will return to Thangúlhad and make ready for the final battle. This secret way into the fortress gives me hope...our victory is all but assured!''So this is the secret way Mithrandir used to enter the fortress of the Necromancer. Raddir would not speak of it in any great detail, even to his friends. The experience left him greatly changed, and he was slower to laugh and to sing than he had been.

'Raddir too will be avenged when we launch the final assault upon the fortress. It lies directly above us here, and our foes do not know their danger.'You have come to Imladris in response to an urgent summons from Master Elrond.Pethelen is in the main hall of the House of Elrond.

You have entered the House of Elrond with Pethelen, and he is waiting to speak with you.Talk to PethelenMaster Elrond is waiting for you in the Hall of Fire, inside the Last Homely House.Enter the Hall of FireMaster Elrond is in the Hall of Fire inside his house in Rivendell, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Lord ElrondLord Elrond has asked to meet you in the library of his house to further discuss the charge: to gather the Dúnedain that they may ride to the aid of Aragorn in Rohan.Walk to the Library in the House of ElrondArwen is outside the library in the House of Elrond and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Lady ArwenMaster Elrond is waiting inside the library of his house to speak with you.Enter the Library in the House of ElrondLord Elrond is waiting to speak with you in the library of his house.Talk to Master ElrondMaster Elrond is waiting to speak with you in the Library of his house.Talk to Master ElrondMaster Elrond is waiting to speak with you in the Library of his house.Talk to Master ElrondElrohir is in the library of his father's house.Talk to ElrohirMaster Elrond is in the library of his house.Talk to Master Elrond'I know of the messages from my grandmother, ${PLAYER}, and I know too that my father has asked you to gather the Dúnedain that they may ride south to the aid of Aragorn.

'We knew this day would come, he and I, and have both long prepared for its arrival. It has been my part to craft an item of beauty and of use, an item Aragorn will have need of before all is done. He knows of its making, and it will be a sign to him that the hour has indeed arrived when he must seize that which is his. I hope it will be a comfort to him, too, in distant lands.

'When the Dúnedain are all assembled, I will send for you. I must be sure someone can bear this item to him. I will call you before the Rangers depart.''Golodir was a strong man and true of heart, but my father is right. He has suffered much. I did not know.

'I know very little of Corunir, beyond that he be loyal to a fault. Did he not remain in Aughaire for years, awaiting Golodir's return from beyond the Rammas Deluon?''I am pleased you received my summons, ${PLAYER}. I apologize for being so close as to the reason for its urgency, but there are dangers on the road, and I did not wish any information that might do us harm to fall into the hands of evil Men.

'Two days ago, courier-hawks arrived in Rivendell bearing messages from Lórien. The messages were short, but even in their brevity they caused me pause.

'The messages are from the Lady Galadriel. I understand some of their meaning, but I can only guess at the full import of her words.''And so it shall be, ${PLAYER}. You will walk the paths of Eriador one more time, in search of these Dúnedain. They will gather others of their kin, and when all is made ready, the Grey Company will set forth.

'It will be done as Galadriel of Lórien has asked. We respond to her messages with the haste we must, and with the messenger those messages deserve: a ${CLASS} of proven loyalty, to gather the Grey Company in time!''It will not be easy to gather the Dúnedain as quickly as it must be done, ${PLAYER}. It would be best for you to bring the message to certain of Aragorn's kin, that they may then speak to the rest of their people, while you travel to those of more distant lands.

'I have prepared the names of those to whom you must speak. Walk with me, and I will tell you where they can be found.''Once you have met Radanir in the Trollshaws and told him that the Rangers must assemble, you should travel west into the Lone-lands. At the base of Weathertop, you will find Candaith. He is sure to join the Grey Company.

'After you have spoken to Candaith, you will travel into Bree-land to speak with Saeradan at his cabin. He is not as prone to wandering as others of his kin, but he must be told of Aragorn's need.

'But there are other Rangers you must find.''Halros, Halbarad, and Calenglad will speak with others of the Dúnedain near the borders of the Shire and throughout the North Downs and Evendim, but there are two more Rangers you will need to find personally. The distances are simply too great for anyone but you to cover in time.''Very good, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps now we will have an answer for why this haste is necessary.

'Is that Elladan? It is! Perhaps he will have some answers for us.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I will send for you when this item is ready.''Go now to the library upstairs, ${PLAYER}. I will meet you there, and we will discuss how the Rangers can be assembled as quickly as can be managed.''Very good, ${PLAYER}. Many hopes rest on your journey through Eriador to gather the Dúnedain for their journey!''Unless it is forbidden, be sure to tell me what is going on, ${PLAYER}! Now, hurry into the Hall of Fire to speak with Master Elrond!''What you say fills me with foreboding, ${PLAYER}. I do not know all the details of the journey on which Aragorn set forth last December, but if Lady Galadriel says he has need of his kin, well... that can mean nothing good, can it?

'Lord Elrond said he would meet you in the library for further discussion of how to gather the Dúnedain? You should go there at once! It is just upstairs.'You have arrived at Lhan Tarren as the Feast of the Running Stag began and have been invited to join the Stag-clan for the evening.The Feast of the Running Stag is about to begin.Talk to ElainYou should perform the <rgb=#FF0000>/sit</rgb> emote near the bonfire to observe the festival.Join the Stag-clansmen and observe the ritesThe Feast of the Running Stag has begun.Observe the Feast of the Running StagYou should observe the Feast of the Running Stag.Observe the ritual as it unfoldsThe Elder's ritual crown can be found at the doorway from which he entered.

You should collect the crown and carry it carefully to the Elder.Bring the Elder what he asks forThe Elder's ritual horse-tail can be found at the doorway from which he entered.

You should collect the horse-tail and carry it carefully to the Elder when he is done speaking.Bring the Elder what he asks for when he is finished speakingThe Elder's ritual spear can be found at the doorway from which he entered.

You should collect the spear and carry it carefully to the Elder when he is done speaking.Bring the Elder what he asks for when he is finished speakingThe Feast of the Running Stag continues.Observe the ritesThe Elder has called an early end to the Feast of the Running Stag this year, due to likely danger in the south where some of his people live.Listen to the ElderElain is at the Feast of the Running Stag.

You should speak with Elain about the injured crow and the missing southern family.Talk to Elain'We should leave, ${PLAYER}. I will talk to you again before I go to scout the farm.''Good evening to you! You are just in time -- the Elder is still preparing, but the clan has gathered already.

'Come, follow me! This is the most important night of the year for my people.'The crow's wing appears twisted, as if someone attempted to break it cruelly. The message it bore is still attached to its leg.

You recognize at once that this was the crow you sent to the southern farm. Something very unfriendly intercepted this poor bird.

It is clear that the farmer's family never received this summons.'This is very unsettling. The poor bird tried to land, but friendly hands did not receive it. We will surely scout at first light.

'You heard the Elder, ${PLAYER} -- our festival is at an end. You should take some food and rest, for you have earned it.'Candaith has decided to tell you of some key events in the history of the Dúnedain.In the year 1975 of the Third Age, a party of Dúnedain led by the brothers Laegened and Haerdor travelled north into Forochel to reclaim the relics left behind in the flight from the wreck of Fornost.

Haerdor has scouted ahead and wishes to speak with you.Talk to HaerdorWorms can be found on the rise to the east of Haerdor.

Your brother Haerdor has asked you to use your stealthy abilities to clear the path ahead of worms.Defeat winter-worms in the pass to Forochel (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Haerdor is waiting to hear that the winter-worms have been cleared from the pass.Talk to Haerdor down the hill to the westIt is the year Nineteen Seventy-five of the Third Age, and the journey of Laegened and Haerdor has brought them to the shores of the Ice Bay in search of their king.

Haerdor has arrived on the shores of the Ice Bay, beholding the broken ship that was sent to rescue Arvedui. He waits to speak with you.Listen to Candaith's narrative continueIt is the year Nineteen Seventy-five of the Third Age, and the journey of Laegened and Haerdor has brought them to the shores of the Ice Bay in search of their king.

Haerdor is waiting to speak with you on the shores of the Ice Bay.Talk to HaerdorHasikkä, the chieftain of the Lossoth, is waiting to speak with you.Talk to HasikkäHaerdor is waiting to speak with you.Talk to HaerdorIt is the year Twenty-nine Thirty of the Third Age, and Arador, the Chieftain of the Dúnedain, has not returned from the Troll-height, where he adventured to maintain the safety of his people. Glangon fears the worst.Listen to Candaith's narrative continueIt is the year Twenty-nine Thirty of the Third Age, and Arador, the Chieftain of the Dúnedain, has not returned from the Troll-height, where he adventured to maintain the safety of his people. Glangon fears the worst.

Glangon is waiting to speak with you below the Troll-height.Talk to GlangonThe Hill-trolls of the Ettenmoors seized Arador, the Chieftain of the Dúnedain and must be defeated if he is to be rescued.The Hill-trolls have been defeated, but it is too late for Arador, Chieftain of the Dúnedain.Talk to GlangonIt is the year Twenty-nine Fifty-six of the Third Age, and Aragorn son of Arathorn plans to begin the great journeys by which he would come to know the world. He is waiting to speak with you, for he wishes to bid farewell before he travels into the south-lands.Listen to Candaith's narrative continueIt is the year Twenty-nine Fifty-six of the Third Age, and Aragorn son of Arathorn plans to begin the great journeys by which he would come to know the world. But first he must bid farewell to the friends of his childhood.Talk to AragornAragorn and Túgonn have encountered an old man on the road through the Dimrill Dale.Listen to the exchange between Aragorn and the old manGandalf the Grey is waiting to speak with you.Talk to Gandalf the GreyAragorn is waiting to speak with you.Talk to Aragorn'The journey has been long and will be longer. We go to the aid of our king, whatever may have befallen him!''We have come this far without incident, Laegened, but the road north is certain to become more dangerous. Little is known of the Snowmen of Forochel, but if they are opposed to the Witch-king, we will surely find them to be allies.

'Still, the ship bearing Arvedui should have returned by now, and its absence fills me with worry. We must press onward, no matter what we find. The sooner we learn what has befallen King Arvedui, the sooner we may leave this cold land!

'There are worms on the rise ahead, Laegened. Use your skills at remaining unseen and clear a path for us, that we may bring the waggon up the road safely. The trade goods we bring may loosen the tongues of the Snowmen as to the fate of our king.''We are masters at striking from hiding, Laegened, you know this! Make use of your stealthiness to take the worms unaware and clear a path for our waggon. We must learn what has happened to King Arvedui.''Very good, Laegened! Now that the worms are cleared from the pass we can resume our journey northward into Forochel to learn what we may learn.''Oh no... what can have happened?''Oh no... what can have happened?''We have come to the end of our road, Laegened! Look there! The ship that was to carry King Arvedui back home lies in ruin!

'Wish now as you never have before! Wish that he was not aboard the vessel when it broke apart!

'We cannot tarry here, though dread lies heavily upon my heart. Hurry, Laegened!''I am glad the tall king had a son to continue his rule. Long may his heirs rule their far-away peoples!

'He spoke of great treasures and valuable things. He said they were carried upon the sea-monster's back, and that they could be used against the Witch-king for the betterment of both our peoples. But now all are lost. Nothing comes back out of the frozen water.''It must be the Ring of Barahir! It possesses no power save its value as an heirloom of the House of Isildur, but its value in ancientry cannot be doubted!

'Did King Arvedui have some sense of the fate that awaited him? Surely not, but it is curious that this ring has escaped where he was unable.

'We have brought great store of provision with us. We will barter for the return of the ring, and it will go south with us, though its master cannot. We must be content with that.''We are almost there, Corugúr, for good or ill. I fear that we will not find Arador our chieftain still alive, but we cannot call off the pursuit until we know for certain.

'The Hill-trolls of the Ettenmoors make their homes here on the Troll-height, and the tracks lead into the cleft just ahead. I will climb the rise and scout the area from above. There are goblins in these parts as well; I leave their fates up to you, but know that the trolls must be our main concern.

'Good fortune, Corugúr! May we not be too late for our chief.''Alas, we are too late! Arador has been slain! Curse these vile trolls!'

Glangon wipes away a tear and composes himself as best he may.

'His son Arathorn is chieftain of the Dúnedain now, though he want it not. And him newly-married, as well! There is nothing for it, Corugúr. We must return with the sad news. I pity Arathorn for the responsibility that now falls to him, but we must console ourselves with the knowledge that the line of Isildur will continue. What a sorrow if it were to end in this world!

'May Arathorn reach a different fate than his father! Let none come to this Troll-height without remembering the fate of the Chieftain of the Dúnedain. From this point forward this height will be known as Arador's End and will be a warning to all.''We have been friends for many years, Túgonn, and we have explored the wild lands of Eriador together. Your friendship means a great deal to me. But I have said nothing for which you should be angry with me. I have told you before that I planned to adventure in the south-lands, then to return when I have done deeds worthy of my father and grandfather. You cannot come with me, my friend.

'If I am to lead the Dúnedain, needs must I perform feats worthy of that obligation. It is something I must do alone, Túgonn.''There are great things in store for Aragorn your chieftain, Túgonn, but he must arrive at them in his own time. It is the solemn duty of all the Dúnedain to protect him, for he is descended from Isildur in line unbroken, and one day he must rule in Gondor.'

Gandalf smiles with amusement, peering at you from underneath his eyebrows.

'But these are mighty words, and the time is still far off. We all have work to do before that day comes, not just Aragorn, and not just -- in our small way -- the Wizards, but you as well, Túgonn. You will not travel with Aragorn on his journey into the kingdoms of the south, for he has made up his mind, but you still have your role to play. In friendship, you will carry it out and have victory, I am certain, for that is a weapon no servant of the Enemy fully understands.''Elrond has spoken to me of Gandalf the Grey, and I am ashamed I did not recognize him at the first, Túgonn!

'Still, he has warmth, though perhaps he tries to hide it. There were many things I wanted to ask him, should we ever meet, but now I cannot think of any!

'What a chance meeting! Let us continue on to Lothlórien, and perhaps then I will remember the questions I wished to ask.'Defeat the Gaunt-lord Ferndúr in the Lost Temple discovered within the Trollshaws before he completes his plan to unleash a deadly plague upon the North!Ferndúr the Virulent has nearly completed his deadly experiments beneath the lost Rhudauran temple.

He and his servants must be defeated before his plans come to fruition!Search the temple for Ferndúr's locationYou have discovered Ferndúr the Virulent, in the  central chamber, in the depths of the lost Rhudauran temple.

He and his servants must be defeated before his plans come to fruition!Defeat Ferndúr the VirulentYou stand before an ancient spirit imbued with great wisdom. You must pass through its trial if you wish to gain the Huntsman's favour.Speak with the stag-spirit and confront its challenge.Talk to the stag-spiritAccept the stag-spirit's challenge of wisdom.Accept the stag-spirit's challengeFive spirits stand before you. One is the spirit of Wisdom, the other four are spirits of Anger, Greed, Vengeance, and Fear. You must find the spirit of Wisdom among them -- but decide carefully for your choice is perilous.

The stag-spirit told you this:

Firstly, you must know that Fear can be found directly to the right of Vengeance. Secondly, you must know that Anger is directly left of Greed. Lastly, the spirit of Wisdom cannot be found next to Greed, Anger, or Vengeance. Choose wisely.Identify the spirit of WisdomProtect the stag-spiritProtect the Spirit of WisdomAfter speaking with the Stag-spirit, you must drink from the strange pool to escape from this eerie realm of spirits.'The test of Wisdom now lies before you. Listen carefully, Mortal.

'Five spirits stand before you. One is the spirit of Wisdom, the other four are spirits of Anger, Greed, Vengeance, and Fear. You must find the spirit of Wisdom among them -- but decide carefully, for your choice is perilous.

'I can tell you but three things about these spirits: Firstly, you must know that Fear can be found directly to the right of Vengeance. Secondly, you must know that Anger is directly left of Greed. Lastly, the spirit of Wisdom cannot be found next to Greed, Anger, or Vengeance. Choose wisely....''Welcome. Thou face now the final of the Huntsman's challenges.

'Should thou prove keen of wit and wisdom, then thou shall pass -- but should thou falter now, then it may be that thy story ends here.''You have proven your wisdom, now leave whence you came before you become a part of our world forevermore.''The test of Wisdom now lies before you. Listen carefully, Mortal.

'Five spirits stand before you. One is the spirit of Wisdom, the other four are spirits of Anger, Greed, Vengeance, and Fear. You must find the spirit of Wisdom among them -- but decide carefully for your choice is perilous.

'I can tell you but three things about these spirits: Firstly, you must know that Fear can be found directly to the right of Vengeance. Secondly, you must know that Anger is directly left of Greed. Lastly, the spirit of Wisdom cannot be found next to Greed, Anger, or Vengeance. Choose wisely....'As you lean down and drink from the pool, strange visions dance before your eyes, and the odd mist that had fallen upon the forest begins to lift.Gwenaewen has bidden you challenge an ancient spirit of winds in the Wild Hunt that the Huntsman has set before you.Approach the falcon-spirit and prove your quickness in following it.Challenge Gwenaewen's spiritYou must follow Gwenaewen's spirit!Follow the spirit to the first lithYou must follow Gwenaewen's spirit!Follow the spirit to the second lithYou must follow Gwenaewen's spirit!Follow the spirit to the third lithYou must follow Gwenaewen's spirit!Follow the spirit to the fourth lithYou must follow Gwenaewen's spirit!Follow the spirit to the mystic poolAfter speaking with the Falcon-spirit, you must drink from the strange pool to escape from this eerie realm of spirits.As you lean down and drink from the pool, strange visions dance before your eyes, and the odd mist that had fallen upon the forest begins to lift.'Time is always of the essence, for ever is the world changing about us - it will not wait for us to catch up.

'Upon this hill are five points arranged as a star, and between them we shall race. Can you follow me, little ${RACE}?

Come then, and let the challenge begin! If you lose sight of me, you shall be lost....''This way....''This way....''This way....''This way....''This way....''You have run swiftly ${PLAYER}, and have proven your worth - but linger upon my words no longer!

'Those who dally here may never again awaken in mortal realms.'Lavaneth has bidden you challenge an ancient spirit of strength in the Wild Hunt that the Huntsman has set before you.Approach the ancient spirit and prove your strength in challenge against it.Challenge the boar-spiritAfter speaking with the Boar-spirit, you must drink from the strange pool to escape from this eerie realm of spirits.'Go now little one. You have proven yourself, but this is not your place. The waking world awaits you.'As you lean down and drink from the pool, strange visions dance before your eyes, and the odd mist that had fallen upon the forest begins to lift.The boar glowers at you, temperamentally pawing at the ground as you approach. Its white tusks are long and deadly, glimmering in the starlight.Munfaeril has bidden you challenge an ancient spirit as a test of endurance in the Wild Hunt that the Huntsman has set before you.The ox-spirit has set before you a challenge of endurance. You must both survive against the waves of crushing despair before you.Challenge the ox-spiritHaving faced your challenge and emerged unbowed, you may now return to the waking world through the waters of the pool.'I have seen you stand fast and endure what many could not. I cannot say that you will survive what lies before you unbowed, but you are prepared.

'Now return to your place in the world lest you become lost in dreams.'As you lean down and drink from the pool, strange visions dance before your eyes, and the odd mist that had fallen upon the forest begins to lift.'Welcome, little ${PLAYER}. You are prepared to endure the crashing waves of brutal fate?

'They come even now as a howling fury in the night. Jaws that would tear and crush, heartless as the darkest moon.

