You should speak with Doron once more.Talk to Doron at the Field of CelebrantIdmar is at his camp along the road in central Parth Celebrant.

You should bring news of your victory to Idmar.Talk to Idmar'The time has come, ${PLAYER}. We can wait no longer, as every minute we delay brings you and your allies closer to your doom.

'Make haste, travel to Ost Celebrant. Once there, call me forth with a horn of your allies' make, and I will fight by your side.

'There is no time to waste. We must strike!''Go to Ost Celebrant. When you arrive, blow your horn to summon me, and I will come.''We have work to do at Ost Celebrant.''We have work to do at Ost Celebrant.''The battle is won. You fought with such valour, and I am proud to have served at your side.

'I sense my oath remains unfulfilled as I still linger at your side. I fear that some other threat remains beyond our sight.

'You have done well, and I have yet more to speak, but now is not the time. I will return to my post among my men. When you can, come speak with me.''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}. With my ancient enemy again defeated, I feel at ease. My curse may not be lifted, but I know that along with you, I was able to stand against my foe without the fear that consumed me so long ago.

'The Horse-lords have a strong ally in you. Take the knowledge you have gleaned from our battle in the ruins and bring it to them. I sense the rumblings of a larger conflict in the near future.

'As for me, I will remain with my men. They lie in disarray, and it is my hope that I can one day rally them at my side again.

'Glory to Gondor! Farewell....''Return to your captain, Idmar.''You set siege Ost Celebrant by yourself? If it were anyone else, I would think him mad, but I believe your tale.

'The threat that Easterling posed was great, and it is with shame that I admit my blindness to his presence. The danger of more organized brigands serving under the Eye, no less, was of great concern. Nevertheless, your work here with my men and me is finished. Your contribution to our plight has been immense, and it was an honour to serve with you.

'To the east, more of my fellows are making forays into the Rushgore. If you would aid us more, I have more yet to say to you.'Following the rout at Brôntrig, Ardwyn proposes shifting his forces' focus in the Isendale.Garrun is encamped against a cliff face far to the west of Forthbrond, north of Cuvnerth.

Ardwyn has asked you to seek out the scout Garrun at the western edge of the Isendale and lend him your aid.Talk to Garrun in the Isendale'With our defeat at Brôntrig, we must refocus our efforts in the Isendale. The army at Wulf's Cleft is growing stronger with every passing day, and we must now turn our attention to weakening that force.

'Garrun, another of my scouts, has encamped near Wulf's Cleft to observe their muster and report on their movements. You should join him there and lend him what aid you may.

'Journey due west into the valleys until you reach the cliff face north of Cuvnerth, another village of the Dunlendings.''Hail, ${PLAYER}! I was told to expect you. You are highly regarded by many of our captains here in Dunland, and I am honoured to make your acquaintance.

'There have been massive movements of Dunlending forces to and from Wulf's Cleft, and we have much work ahead of us.''Find Garrun, ${PLAYER}, and lend him your aid.'After looking over the crude symbol you recovered from the brigands, Doron has decided to share how he and his men came to be cursed.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Doron is among his men on the Field of Celebrant.

Doron wishes to tell you the tale of the Oatbreakers' curse.Talk to DoronDoron is among his men on the Field of Celebrant.

You should speak with Doron regarding the tale he told you.Talk to Doron'This symbol fills me with dread and invokes in me a feeling I have not felt since the day my men and I became cursed.

'I failed Gondor at Ost Celebrant, the large fortress to the south, and condemned myself and my men to this unnatural existence. 

'You must be told what happened, else you can never understand my fate.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>'So, now you understand my past. After a time, a few of my men and I returned to the front with Cirion, but by then, word had spread of our treachery. 

'Cirion cursed all of us and condemned us to serve until the Horde no longer posed a threat to Gondor.

'I feel my guilt, but I do not understand what can be done to lift my curse. The Easterlings are just unorganized raiders...how would they ever pose a threat to Gondor now? These are puzzling times, ${PLAYER}....''Let me tell you the tale. Listen to me, and you will understand.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Cabed has asked you to find something very important to him that he hid near Ost Celebrant during the Battle of the Field of Celebrant.Cabed has sent you in search of a trinket he once carried. The trinket is located somewhere outside Ost Celebrant.Find Cabed's lost trinketCabed is atop a small hill along the southern road of Parth Celebrant.

You should bring the red-tipped arrowhead to Cabed.Talk to Cabed'To understand the doom falling upon the Field of Celebrant, you must first understand our past.

'Long ago, the kingdom of Gondor was besieged by a Horde of terrible Easterlings. They had forded the Great River at the Undeeps and sought to crush our forces here before sweeping into Gondor proper. While we fought valiantly, there was nothing we could do to stop the onslaught of Easterlings. My fellows and I were sent north to seek the aid of the North-men in desperation.

'With us, we each carried a trinket. Before I fell, I buried the trinket in hopes that my failure would be forgotten. However, having returned, I know that can never be the case. I would ask that you seek it out. I buried it just outside the walls of Ost Celebrant. Perhaps it still remains there.''Have you found my lost trinket?''So, you have found it. Thank you, I never thought I would see this again. This arrow was to be the symbol of our plight to the North-men. I was sent to deliver it at any cost, but I...I just could not do it.

'I feared death, and now see what my cowardice has brought me. I later learned that one of the messengers survived and made it to the north...my shame has only grown.

'But enough of this, there is more to be done.'Medwine has attempted to converse with several of the Oathbreakers in the newly-risen force. However, he has only been met with violence.Melethron is at the northern Oathbreaker camp among his fallen men.

Idmar asked you to speak with the shade of Melethron.Confront and defeat MelethronThinnas is at the eastern camp of Oathbreakers near the Rushgore.

Idmar has asked you to speak with the shade of Thinnas.Confront and defeat ThinnasCamlann is at the western camp of Oathbreakers near the border to Eorlsmead.

Idmar asked you to speak with the shade of Camlann.Confront and defeat CamlannIdmar is at his camp along the road in central Parth Celebrant.

You should return to Idmar with news of your dealings with the maddened shades.Talk to Idmar'Every attempt I have made to converse with these Oathbreakers has ended in violence. These men must have committed some sort of treacherous crime; elsewise, they would not have returned.

'Most have nothing coherent to say, but I have heard the whispers of three names... Melethron, Thinnas, and Camlann. Perhaps they may have insight as to why their men have returned.

'I have never been able to get close enough to speak to them, so you will probably have to fight your way to them. Be careful.''Speak with the Oathbreakers, ${PLAYER}, and learn what it is that they want....'As you acquire the Oathbreaker's attention, he glares in your direction.

'Another? Why will you not leave us be?'

Melethron's face contorts into a rage.'Speak with the Oathbreakers, ${PLAYER}, and learn what it is that they want....'Upon noticing you, the Oathbreaker appears puzzled.

'I am Thinnas, captain of the eastern front. What purpose do you have, coming here?'

You question the Oathbreaker about his curse.

'We did not think anyone would find out. But lord Cirion did... and he made certain we would never forget what we had done.

'What need have you for this? Do you only seek to torment us more?''Speak with the Oathbreakers, ${PLAYER}, and learn what it is that they want....'As you approach Camlann, he looks visibly upset.

'You have invaded our lands and slaughtered my men, and yet, you expect me to speak with you?

'The frivolity of your charge is matched only by your foolishness. The fate that befell us is ours alone to face.''You should return now to Idmar.''I take it your dealings with the Oathbreakers did not go well?

'Yet, one mentioned Cirion? These men must have fought for Gondor at the Battle of the Field of Celebrant. Long ago, our forebear, Eorl, led Cirion to victory after arriving to save his forces from an onslaught of terrible Easterlings. It was a glorious moment in our past and paved the way for the formation of Rohan.

'But how did these men come to be cursed? That battle was won, and Gondor was saved....

'Here, take this, I think we may need to approach this differently now.'Garrun reports that a number of Dunlending patrols watch the passes between the western edge of the Isendale and Wulf's Cleft.Dunlending patrols can be found in the valleys and mountain passes south of Garrun's camp.

