'It may be that their tools are worth more to Saruman than their lives? Without them, they cannot fell the trees.

'Perhaps you could gather their axes and hatchets and hurl them into the same fires upon which burn the fallen trees of Nan Curunír? Let us see these burárum cut down trees with their bare hands!''The sight of smoke rising from those fires burns at my heartwood... so many fine trees lost, and for nothing. It is scant solace to know that some small part of that smoke now carries away what is left of the hateful axes used to hew their noble trunks.'Frithgeir believes that Nár's ramblings refer to a cave in the eastern hills of Thrór's Coomb. If he is correct, there should be a great cache of weapons and armour awaiting you there.The cave entrance is believed to be in the eastern hills of Thrór's Coomb in Enedwaith.

Frithgeir has translated Nár's ramblings and believes they refer to a drake-guarded cave.Find the cave entrance'I have spent some time thinking about what Nár said and examining the map and his changes to it. I have come up with a theory by breaking down Nár's ramblings.

'In the eastern part of Thrór's Comb is a cave. The sea of gold speaks of the treasure the Draig-lûth is bringing there. Around the cave entrance you will often see drakes sunning during the day. I believe they are the teeth and scales he refers to. The fire must be part of an offering the clan makes to the dragons they worship.

'Head into the eastern hills of Thrór's Coomb and seek out this cave. If you find it, venture within and see if you find the weapons and armour you seek. If not, we can do nothing more for you. Be wary... I doubt the Draig-lûth will give up their treasures willingly.''If the cave is not there or is empty, we are at an impasse, and I doubt we will ever find where they are taking their treasures.'Saruman's servants have constructed a massive set of fell devices beneath Isengard. These engines are maintained by bands of goblin-engineers. Without their constant tinkering the whole array is likely to fall into disrepair.You may be able to get down to the device array through the network of strange pipes that seem to run through the area.

Dagoras asked you to defeat the goblin-engineers working on Saruman's great device down in the rift beneath the western ward of Isengard.Defeat goblin-engineers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dagoras is hiding within the Ring of Isengard.

You should return to Dagoras with news of your success.Talk to Dagoras within the Ring of Isengard'Do you feel that vibration in the ground, ${PLAYER}? Saruman has constructed... something down there. You can see some of the workings in the great rift in the western ward. I could not even begin to tell you what it is all for.

'He has goblins working on the engine night and day, though, so you can be certain it is important to his plans.

'If you can find a way down there and keep those goblins from finishing this great device of his, it will hopefully prevent any terrible surprises when it comes time to confront Saruman and his armies.''Most of the goblins seem to be gathered in and around that huge rift in the western quarter of Isengard.''Good work, ${PLAYER}. Let us hope that your efforts will hinder Saruman's plan for a time, or perhaps the whole accursed thing will collapse into that hole.

'I suppose that is probably too much to hope for, but hope is all we seem to have at the moment.'Quickbeam has heard musings among the Wise that trolls might have once been Ents, twisted to dark forms. He is doubtful that this is the case, but if their twisted nature is in some manner similar to that of the Ents, then he believes it might be worthwhile to try an ancient draught upon them, meant to still the stirrings of maddened or fearful Huorns.Stone-trolls patrol the uruk-encampment in eastern Nan Curunír.

Quickbeam asked you to take the strange draught he provided and try splashing it upon the Stone-trolls. It may serve to soothe them or render them sleepy, as it is meant to do with the Huorns.Defeat Stone-trolls in the uruk-encampment (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tell Quickbeam how the draught affected the trolls.Talk to Quickbeam'Hoom. I once spoke with one of the Wise who believed that trolls were indeed Ents! Ta-rum! Perhaps the Wise are not as wise as they would like to believe, hoom. But it did get me to thinking... what if they are a bit Ent-like?

'You see, we have a draught we give to the trees when they become upset. It slows them down, makes them more tree-like, less dangerous. So if the Wise are to be believed, and trolls are just a bit Entish, then perhaps it would work on them?''Slower you say? Drowsy even?

'Yes, well... hoom...

'That is unexpected, but not wholly so. There must be something of ancient wood in them after all.

'To think that trees might have been bent so, it only makes me angrier... the Entmoot must know of this. Ta-rum....'The Ent Gnarlstump desires to build a memorial to the Entwives before he leaves the Brown Lands.Troll-stone can be found throughout the Brown Lands, in the desolation of the Entwives' gardens, and at the Stone-hole, north of Gnarlstump's camp.

Gnarlstump has asked you to gather troll-stone so that he can build a memorial to the Entwives.Collect troll-stone (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gnarlstump is at his camp near the desolation of the Entwives' gardens in the Brown Lands.

You should bring the troll-stone you collected to Gnarlstump.'Hoom, little ${RACE}, I could use your help once more. I would, hoom-hom, build in this place a -- how would you say it? -- a memorial in honour of the Entwives. Yes, that is the word for it: memorial.

'I would not touch the stones of the Entwives' gardens, but there is other stone to be had in this land, ta-rum! Trolls, foul beasts made by Morgoth in mockery of Ents, dwell in this land. Occasionally, they become trapped in the sunlight and turn to stone.

'Gather this troll-stone and bring it to me. It is fitting, I think, for the foul mockeries to become a memorial.''Bring me stone from the suntouched trolls, ta-rum, and I will build a memorial for the Entwives.''Ah, yes! This shall do nicely, ta-room! You have been most helpful, little ${RACE}.'You discovered an enormous butcher's knife in the bowels of a goblin slaughter-house where the Rohirrim steeds were brought to serve as feed for the Wargs.The goblin-butcher may be found somewhere in the caves of western Nan Curunír.

You should dispatch the goblin-butcher for his foul crimes.Defeat the Butcher (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})This terrible butcher's blade has taken a deadly toll upon the noble steeds of the Rohirrim and the tithed cattle of the Dunlendings alike.

No doubt, the villainous butcher that wields it is somewhere nearby. Dispatching the fiend might be considered some form of reprisal to honour the mounts of the Rohirrim.Limthir saw something on the banks of Anduin, but speaks of it only in hushed tones: it was one of the Nine, one of the Nazgûl.Fimloth is at Haldirith, in Thinglad.

Limthir saw the shape of what he believes to be a Nazgûl on the shores of Anduin, and wants you to warn Fimloth that such a creature is loose in Thinglad.'It was dark as pitch, in the cold watches of the night, and I walked the shore of Anduin. I thought I saw... no, I did see... a darker patch of shadow in the space between the trees. When I approached, it fled. It moved with a lurching step, sliding low upon the ground, but it moved quickly, and for some reason I cannot explain I was loath to close the distance. I lost it in the dark.

'But I know the name of this apparition.'

Limthir looks from side to side, and then speaks in a voice barely above a whisper.

'Its name is Nazgûl. Bring word to Fimloth at Haldirith that one of these fell creatures is loose somewhere in Thinglad.''Are you sure? A Nazgûl?'

Fimloth stares off into the distance for a time, deep in thought. Then she shakes her head and turns back to you.

'This is disturbing news, ${PLAYER}, but I believe a single one of the Nine cannot endanger Lothlórien, not while Lady Galadriel retains her power. And she does. You need not worry on that account.

'All of the Nine together would pose a more serious threat, but our Lady still governs and her power still keeps the borders of Lórien safe. Thank you for these tidings from Limthir, but it seems to me this Nazgûl poses more danger to the Men south of here than to the Elves north of here, in the Golden Wood.''I know the name of that creature: Nazgûl. You must bring word to Fimloth, at Haldirith, of the danger that creeps somewhere within Thinglad.'The Khundolar war-camp has become a threat too great to ignore. Something must be done or the Easterlings will overrun Rohan.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Céola is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

When you are ready to begin the attack on Lashkarg, you should speak with Céola.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Talk to Céola when you are ready to assault LashkargCéola is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

You should return to Céola with news of your victory.Talk to Céola at Etheburg in the Brown Lands'Thanks in part to Dagbert's treachery, we can no longer refrain from dealing with the Khundolar. Though it may ultimately mean our deaths, we must strike at their heart before it is too late.

'This, then, is my hope: attack Lashkarg at sunset...take them off-guard, while they are resting. Perhaps then, we may hold some hope of success.

'Will you do this, ${PLAYER}? If so, speak with me when you are ready to leave.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>'Well done, ${PLAYER}. However, I fear that we must now brace ourselves for retaliation....''You are prepared to depart? Very well. May fortune go with you, ${PLAYER}.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>The Khundolar war-camp has become a threat too great to ignore. Something must be done or the Easterlings will overrun Rohan.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Céola is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

You should speak with Céola when you are ready to depart for Lashkarg.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Talk to Céola when you are ready to assault LashkargCéola is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

You should return to Céola with news of your victory at Lashkarg.Talk to Céola at Etheburg in the Brown Lands'We cannot let up, ${PLAYER}. If we are to defend ourselves and the Riddermark, we must continue to press against the Khundolar. Are you prepared to make another assault upon Lashkarg?'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>'Well done, ${PLAYER}! We may yet survive this war!''You are ready for the attack? I wish you good fortune, ${PLAYER}.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>The dwarf Armund and his companions have entered the Limlight Gorge in the hope of reopening an old sawmill on the edge of Fangorn forest. Discounting the tales of monsters and walking trees, the dwarves have begun working on the sawmill, but recently most of Armund's companions seem to have mysteriously disappeared.You should rescue the two missing dwarves, Finnvarth and Bíli, who disappeared somewhere to the east of the old sawmill in the Limlight Gorge.Complete Finnvarth is LostComplete Bíli is MissingYou should speak with Armund in the old sawmill in the Limlight Gorge.Talk to Armund in the Limlight GorgeThree more of Armund's companions remain missing within the Gorge, and morale among the band of dwarves remains low as Armund continues to drive them to complete the ill-advised venture.Complete Gerfinn Has Gone AstrayComplete Darfi Has Wandered OffComplete Dolmund Has VanishedYou should speak with Dolmund in the old sawmill in the Limlight Gorge.Talk to DolmundConvince Armund to give up the Sawmill.Talk to ArmundSpeak with Dolmund at the Sawmill in Limlight Gorge.Speak with Dolmund'We will have this mill up and running in no time and be shipping lumber up to Stangard by the waggon-load! The trees around here have fine hardwoods, but the locals are too afraid of the forest to cut it down...can you imagine that?

'The only trouble is that most of my companions seem to have...wandered off while surveying the area around the mill. I was wondering if you could help round them up for me?

'There are two in particular: Finnvarth and Bíli...they are the youngest, so I am worried that they may have gotten themselves lost in the Gorge.''Have you found my companions yet?''Well, it seems that you have managed to gather up the two young ones...that is something anyway. If we can just find the other three now, we should be able to get this old mill up and running in no time!

'They know better than to get lost like this, but Gerfinn, Darfi, and Dolmund have all disappeared on me, as well!''Have you found my companions yet?''Walking trees! The madness of the thought! Ha!''I cannot speak tactfully about it...I think my dear brother Armund may be mad.

'This venture with the sawmill was a risky one at best, and now that the very trees have turned upon us, it has gone as wrong as it can...but he will not hear anything about it!

'We have got to convince him to give up this venture, even if we have to go back to coal-mining, but I do not know if he will listen to reason....''W...what!? Give up on the Sawmill? Flee from the gorge like a dog with its tail tucked between its legs?

<Armund sighs>

'You may be right ${PLAYER}... Were it not for your help, my companions might still be wandering lost amongst the Gorge, and most of them have worked themselves into such a state that they could hardly fix a pail, much less this Sawmill. Maybe this expedition really is a waste of time...

'But wait, I have the answer! How could I have been so blind? We've had no ale here for weeks now! Our stock ran out on the way down here.

'How could any right-thinking Dwarf face the wide outdoors in this lonely gorge without a good stiffener each morn? No wonder they think the trees have been after them!

'Thank you for your help ${PLAYER}! I shall put in an order for several casks of ale up in Stangard this very day, and have these fellows right as rain in no time. You'll see!''I fear the stress of our journey may have been too much for him, or perhaps he really is just such a fool. It matters not, really.

'There's nothing more you can do, ${PLAYER}. Armund is our problem to deal with now. Thank you for rescuing us, and leave my brother to me.

'Good luck in your travels!'Andras escaped from the dungeons of Isengard. From his vantage point in the Ox-clan encampment, he saw Isengard empty and march south. The opportunity to exact his revenge on Saruman of Many Colours was beyond his ability to resist, and he sent forth a call through the Boar-clan resistance to all who would heed to come to Nan Curunír and make a stand against the White Hand.Andras has asked you to enter the Ring of Isengard, the eaves of Fangorn, and the Pits of Isengard and disrupt Saruman's war-efforts.Complete Dargnákh's Rage in the Dargnákh Unleashed 3-man instanceComplete Protecting the Forest in the Fangorn's Edge 3-man instanceComplete Into the Pit of Abomination in the Pits of Isengard 3-man instanceAndras is at the Ox-clan encampment in Nan Curunír.

You should return to Andras with news of your successes.Talk to Andras at the Ox-clan encampmentAndras has asked you to enter Saruman's Foundry, defeat the wizard's acolyte, Penrith, and destroy the forge to prevent more weapons of evil power from coming into existence.Complete Ringing of the Hammer in the Foundry 6-man instanceAndras is at the Ox-clan encampment in Nan Curunír.

You should return at once to Andras with news of your success and your discovery within Saruman's Foundry.Talk to Andras at the Ox-clan encampment in Nan CurunírAndras suggested only one course of action to remove the threat of Saruman's rings: enter Orthanc, face Saruman, take the rings, and destroy them.Complete The Rings of Isengard in the Tower of Isengard 12-man RaidAndras is at the Ox-clan encampment in Nan Curunír.

You should return to Andras and tell him that Saruman's rings of power have been destroyed.Talk to Andras at the Ox-clan encampment'I have no love for the Forgoil whom you give aid, but for now our purposes are closely entwined. Isengard has emptied -- may the Orcs and Horse-lords destroy each other -- leaving its defences weakened. There is still a Wizard within, but there may be something we can do to disrupt Saruman's plans.

'I have noted three places that might be easily and secretly accessed where we can sow the most strife: the Ring of Isengard, the eaves of Fangorn, and the Pits of Isengard.

'The Orcs and trolls in service to Isengard have begun clear-cutting the eaves of Fangorn. It is an ancient forest, and one my people fear, but I received a dream from the Uch-gwirod telling me to save the wood, for there is life there that is older than most that walk the earth. I will not forsake the Ox-spirit's command and bid you disrupt Saruman's lumber-camp.

'As well, I have learned that Saruman's forces captured a strange, white troll in Fangorn and are holding it within the Ring of Isengard. If you could free the troll and goad it into a rampage, it might wreak great destruction among the Wizard's remaining forces.

'Thirdly, Saruman has laboratories beneath Isengard, where he experiments on his hapless prisoners and twists them into terrible monsters. I want you to enter the Pits of Isengard and destroy the wretched abominations there, particularly the great troll Ironarm.

'You and a couple of friends should be able to stir up trouble in these places... they are not nearly as guarded as Orthanc. Will you aid me?''It was glorious, ${PLAYER}! I found a perch where I was able to witness your attacks both on the lumber-camp and the troll's rampage within the Ring of Isengard. The Wizard's forces will be in confusion for weeks!

'As you live, I assume you were successful within the Pits as well? Good. Some of those abominations may once have been my kinsmen, but they have been twisted into creatures wholly evil.

'Now, I have task for you that is perhaps a bit more daunting, and for which I recommend you go as a party of no less than six. When I was enslaved in Isengard, I was tasked with bringing ore to a Foundry where only Saruman and his most trusted servants were ever seen. I believe that there he is forging weapons of great magical power, obscene things that should not be permitted to see the light of day.

'I ask that you go there and destroy his evil forge, so that no more evil may come of that place. His chief acolyte, Penrith, is a kinsman who has abandoned the ways of the lûth and has embraced Saruman's evil magic called 'industry'... he must be destroyed.

'Will you do this?''Penrith is dead? Then my revenge is nearly com... what was that, you say? The Foundry was not producing magical weapons, but the wizard was in fact forging rings of power?

'Even among my people there are ancient tales of the Shadow Walkers... kings of old who took the Great Rings of Power from Sauron and became undead sorcerers of terrible power. I do not wish to see this thing come to pass in my lands, watching as my people are turned into evil shadows in the service of Saruman of Many Colours.

'I fear, ${PLAYER}, that there is only one course: to face the Wizard and take his rings. They must be destroyed, each and every one, before great evil befalls Dunland, Rohan, and the world beyond!

'I warn you, this will be no easy task, so you must go in great number... six warriors, no matter how hardy, cannot hope to enter the Tower of Orthanc and stand against a Wizard! Will you do this?''What? Y-you live? Bless the Uch-gwirod! You look like you barely made it out of that evil place alive, though. Were you routed?

'The rings are destroyed, five subservient and his master ring? All of them? Thrice blessed be the Uch-gwirod! Though I see from your look that Saruman lives and is hardly powerless, the threat I feared no longer exists: his Ring-forge is destroyed and the last of the rings with it!

'While you were gone, Gwin of the Avanc-lûth came and left gifts for any heroes who dare to stand against the Wizard... they are yours and with great thanks. We will begin our journey home as soon as my strength returns, with hope of peace in our hearts.'You found a lone white flower growing in the desolation of the Entwives' gardens...the only living thing you have seen, other than briars, growing in the Brown Lands.Gnarlstump can be found near the desolation of the Entwives' gardens in the Brown Lands.

You should bring the flower to Gnarlstump at once.Talk to GnarlstumpThis large, white flower is beautiful to behold, but most wondrous of all is that it is growing in the barren and corrupt earth of the Brown Lands.

The Ent Gnarlstump will surely want to see this.'What is this that you have found, little ${RACE}? Hoom! This flower is of a breed grown by Fimbrethil, mate of the eldest of my kind.

'While it does not speak of the passage of the Entwives, Fangorn will be pleased to know such flowers still live in this world.'Erymeldir has asked you to do battle with the various spiders in the Limlight Gorge and gather the venom-glands from them. He hopes to discover which -- if any -- of the spiders might be responsible for Greylimb's affliction.Erymeldir has asked you and your allies to try to retrieve some poison-glands from the wandering spiders hunting the northern stretch of the Limlight Gorge, in the hopes of identifying the venom that has afflicted Greylimb the Ent.Collect large venom-glands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bloated venom-glands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect glowing venom-glands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the venom-glands you collected from the spiders of the Limlight Gorge to Erymeldir at the Limlight Glade.You should ask Twistroot about spiders in Fangorn Forest.Talk to Twistroot the EntYou should ask Thickbark about spiders in Fangorn.Talk to Thickbark'The only way to be sure of the source of Greylimb's affliction would be for you to bring me samples of the spiders' venom that I could compare to the poison I found in his blood.

'Alas, I cannot defeat such a creature myself...even my companion Ordhrien would be hard-pressed against those monstrosities.''Our deepest roots sicken, and the water of the Limlight runs foul with venom. The Elves tell us it must come from near to this place, but there are no spiders here, hoom.

'Ta-rum! I have just remembered, then! Greylimb told me about it one long summer's day...of a cave he discovered nearby when he was but a slender Enting. Beneath the waters of the river, he said it was, where many of the ancient roots of the forest gathered to drink. Hoom! but he could not show it to me, because we are full-grown Ents now, and too big for the narrow ways within.

'It opens nearly onto our grove here -- over there, next to the Limlight Falls -- but we have seen no sign of spiders creeping about the entrance. Hoom-hom! If they are within, I cannot say how they came to be there.''Spiders, you say? Hoom. We have not had any spiders in these woods for many years, many long years indeed. There is bad blood, you know, between the Ents and spiders. That goes far back...hoom, far back indeed.

'Let me see, I believe it was some years after the Men of the Sea and the Elves drove back the old darkness again, and not so long after the Gardens of the Entwives were laid waste, that an old, dark-hearted spider wove her lair not far from here, in the darkest part of old Fangorn. There she waited, preying upon any so unfortunate as to wander in among her maze of webs.

'Of course, in those days, there were few who came here, for the forest had been roused to great anger after the desolation of the Gardens, and many of those who had once lived near the forest had died in the war or been driven away, so there was little prey for one such as she. I believe she grew ever more ravenous, and in time, we discovered that she had even tried to slake her endless hunger with the living sap of the trees, and so, ta-rum, we turned our anger upon her and her brood.

'We drove her out then, brushing aside her webs as a child brushes aside cobwebs, and hounded her until she fled beneath the roots of the Misty Mountains. Hoom-hom! We never heard her name again...what was it? Let me think. Hoom! Gurthúl? Yes, I think that was it.

'Do spiders live so long? I would not think so, but then, I am not a spider, so I cannot say. She would not return here though. She is not strong enough to fight us, and her webs are no hindrance to an Ent, for we can crush stone if we are so inclined. But still, Greylimb is ill from a spider's venom, and the trees around us sicken....''You gathered those samples, did you? I do hope you were not bitten. Such a bite would likely be fatal, you understand.

'This is a queer thing. The samples you brought from the larger varieties of spider are dangerous, certainly, but I do not think they would harm an Ent. The Onodrim are quite resistant to such things. But this glowing venom...you say it came from a much smaller variety of spider? It is the most potent I have seen. It is very likely the same venom that has afflicted poor Greylimb. Fortunately, with a pure sample available, I should be able to brew a draught that may help purge the venom from him, in time.

'But mysteries remain. How is it that a spider came to possess such a venom, seemingly meant to kill trees and Onodrim, and how did this venom find its way into the water and the roots of the trees here? If we cannot stop it soon, much of the forest along the river's edge will soon sicken and die. You may want to ask the Onodrim if they have seen these spiders lurking about at the forest's edge of late.'Because of their organized efforts, Céola suspects that the captain of the brigands is, in fact, a wayward member of the border-guard.The brigand captain can be found somewhere within Helstor, north of Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

Céola has asked you to confront the leader of the brigands in the Brown Lands.Defeat HafocCéola is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands, south of Helstor.

You should return to Céola with news of your victory.Talk to Céola at Etheburg in the Brown Lands'${PLAYER}, these brigands are too organized to be simple ruffians...or at least the one that leads them is not. Their tactics are all too familiar. I believe their captain may, in fact, be a wayward member of my very own border-guard.

'Hafoc, the captain of Etheburg before me, went missing several months ago...I was but a convenient replacement for him. Hafoc seemed to welcome this assignment, and he was one of the few that had come here at Wormtongue's orders not out of discipline.

'I wonder now if Hafoc has betrayed his people and joined these brigands? Would you go to Helstor and confront this captain? I expect he would be somewhere near their relic-stores.''This is welcome news, ${PLAYER}! With Hafoc out of the way, the brigands will be far less of a threat, and we may focus our attention upon the Khundolar.''I have no doubt that we have been betrayed by Hafoc, my predecessor.'The Pits of Isengard is a dangerous place filled with all kinds of Orc-kind. Defeat the Abominations...as many as you can!To earn this deed, you must defeat as many Abominations inside the Pits of Isengard.To earn this deed, you must defeat many Abominations inside the Pits of Isengard.Defeat Abominations in the Pits of Isengard (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Having watched thousands of his beloved trees felled by the axes of the Uruks, Quickbeam has determined to punish them for their transgressions... but he dares not show himself until his unnamed kindred have decided what to do about Saruman.The Uruk wood-cutters who have destroyed the trees in Nan Curunír can be found in the Vale below.

Quickbeam has asked you to defeat the Uruk wood-cutters who destroyed the trees in Nan Curunír.Defeat Uruk wood-cutters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Quickbeam is at his home on the north-eastern slopes above Nan Curunír.

You should tell Quickbeam that you have driven away the Uruk wood-cutters for at least a short time.Talk to Quickbeam east of Isengard'Those thrice-accursed burárum, those Orcs, have slain virtually every tree within the Vale of Nan Curunír. Why I knew this one beech -- barely fifty years old mind you -- down by the river that they just... they just....

'No. I shall not disturb you with the terrible details. Let us just say that I am in a mood to tear a few branches off of these burárum and see how they like it.

'Perhaps you might help? Perhaps you would punish the Orcs for me?''Have you dealt with the Uruk-hai wood-cutters yet?''I like you, little ${RACE}. You are willing to raise a weapon to aid the forest, rather than cut it down. Alas, I do not see that we have thinned their numbers much. Hoom.

'I must think upon this. We should not be too hasty, as I am often reminded.'Gnarlstump, an Ent of Fangorn, has come to the desolation that was once the gardens of the Entwives, seeking signs which might point at the direction of their long-ago departure from the Brown Lands.The gardens of the Entwives lie within the cliffs to the south-east of Gnarlstump.

Gnarlstump gave you an Ent-draught and asked you to pour it upon the ruined gardens of the Entwives.The gardens of the Entwives lie within the cliffs to the south-east of Gnarlstump.

Gnarlstump gave you an Ent-draught and asked you to pour it upon the ruined gardens of the Entwives.The gardens of the Entwives lie within the cliffs to the south-east of Gnarlstump.

Gnarlstump gave you an Ent-draught and asked you to pour it upon the ruined gardens of the Entwives.The gardens of the Entwives lie within the cliffs to the south-east of Gnarlstump.

Gnarlstump gave you an Ent-draught and asked you to pour it upon the ruined gardens of the Entwives.The gardens of the Entwives lie within the cliffs to the south-east of Gnarlstump.

Gnarlstump gave you an Ent-draught and asked you to pour it upon the ruined gardens of the Entwives.The gardens of the Entwives lie within the cliffs to the south-east of Gnarlstump.

Gnarlstump gave you an Ent-draught and asked you to pour it upon the ruined gardens of the Entwives.Gnarlstump is encamped to the north-west of the gardens of the Entwives.

You should let Gnarlstump know what you did and did not discover among the gardens.'Hello there, Little One. My old eyes are not what they once were, hoom, but in these barren places, even I can see that you are not a creature to be feared. You may call me Norndos, or perhaps Gnarlstump, if it is easier for you. Ta-rum! I will not tell you my right name, and I do not expect yours.

'I have come to this place because here were once the gardens of the Entwives. The land was ruined long ago, hom, and the gardens were lost. The Entwives went forth from this place, hoom-hom, never to be seen again. I desire to find the Entwives, if any still remain, and hope to find some sign of their passing.

'Would you aid me? Take this Ent-draught and pour it upon the desolation of the gardens...if anything grows there, ta-run, it may help to identify who the gardens belonged to.''Hoom-hom, make certain to pour the Ent-draught throughout the gardens.''Hoom-hom, make certain to pour the Ent-draught throughout the gardens.''Hoom-hom, make certain to pour the Ent-draught throughout the gardens.''Hoom-hom, make certain to pour the Ent-draught throughout the gardens.''Hoom-hom, make certain to pour the Ent-draught throughout the gardens.''Hoom-hom, make certain to pour the Ent-draught throughout the gardens.''Alas, nothing happened when you poured the draught upon the gardens? Hoom! That is disappointing. It means that the gardens are truly lost.

'But wait, you say that you saw a flower among the desolation? That is something....'Gamal has been charged with keeping an eye on the Easterling tribe that has secured a foothold in the Brown Lands, and he believes they draw inspiration from some new arrival in the area.Easterlings can be found throughout the Brown Lands, and message-shouters can be found near their established foothold at Lashkarg.

Gamal has asked you to defeat Easterlings throughout the Brown Lands, and also to defeat some of the message-shouters that use the name Khoratón.Defeat Easterlings throughout the Brown Lands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Khundolar Message-shouter (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gamal is in the Brown Lands, south of Etheburg.

You have defeated some of the Khundolar Easterlings in the Brown Lands and should return now to Gamal.'${PLAYER}, you say? I am Gamal, of Stangard. I have spied upon a camp of the Khundolar here in the Brown Lands. The Easterlings have been gathering in greater numbers for some time, but something recently has changed.

'I know very little of their language, only bits and pieces. They speak a fragmented tongue, with some words I know and some all their own, but I think they speak of some new and powerful ally. "Khoratón," they call this person that inspires them, but if that is a name or a description I do not know.

'If you are of a mind to help me, defeat many of the Easterlings here in the Brown Lands, and also confront some of the Khundolar that shout messages about this Khoratón. If hearing that name inspires the Easterlings to rise up against my people, let us make them pay for using that name!''I do not like this. The Khundolar are too near to Stangard. If this "Khoratón" continues to inspire their forces, it will not be long before they march on Stangard.'

Gamal's lips form a thin line as he considers the garrison's chance of repelling such an Easterling invasion.

'I thought of something while you were bringing justice to the Easterlings, ${PLAYER}. The word "Khoratón..." I think it is not a name. It has much in common with the word for "Black Cloak." But the Khundolar do not wear such things. Do you think they are inspired by a sorceror of some type?'

You do not voice your guess to Gamal, but the word is loud inside your head: Nazgûl.'Defeat the Khundolar here in the Brown Lands and learn what inspires them. I believe it is the name "Khoratón," but I do not know what it means.

'Unlike many of my brothers in Stangard, I have journeyed very far afield in search of adventure, ${PLAYER}. I walked in Isengard many years ago, and even spoke with Saruman the Wise. I have been to Gondor and seen the White City. There is much to learn from the peoples of distant lands.

'But these Easterlings cannot be allowed to stay here. From this foothold they endanger Stangard and my people, and for that they should be pushed into the river.'Cerdic went to some other of the battlefield sites you did not search, but he was overcome by a powerful feeling of uneasiness.The crumbling corner, the grassy river-overlook, and the stone bridge can be found in Parth Celebrant.

Cerdic went searching some more locations of the ancient battlefield, and experienced strange sensations at some of them. He has marked them on your map and wants you to see what is happening at these sites.Examine debris by the crumbling corner in Ost CelebrantExamine debris by the grassy place overlooking the fast-flowing riverExamine debris by the stone bridgeCerdic is at the watch-post on the road in Parth Celebrant.

You have investigated the sites Cerdic told you about, and should now return to him.'${PLAYER}, you have returned! I was just thinking about you, and wondering if you ever found what it was you were seeking. After you left, I went searching on my own, and... well...'

Cerdic is having some trouble meeting your eyes.

'I am not a coward, ${PLAYER}. I have stood unblinking before a shrieking mass of goblins, and not given way before a charging warband of Khundolar Easterlings. And I have even stood my ground when my wife was in one of her tempers! Well... mostly stood my ground.

'But at certain sites on the battlefield, my courage was quite drained away. I felt uneasy, and everything seemed to get cold.

'Will you go to some of these sites? I will mark them on your map. Look at the rubble of a crumbling corner in Ost Celebrant, a grassy ledge overlooking the fast-flowing river, and by one of the stone bridges over the river. I want to know what is happening out there, ${PLAYER}. I am ashamed to have felt that way. There must be a reason!''Did you experience anything strange while you explored the sites?'

You recount what you saw at each of the locations Cerdic pointed out, and he seems to be quite relieved.

'It was not all in my head, then? Good, ${PLAYER}. Thank you for setting my mind at ease! Whatever it was that instilled in me such uncommon fear may have moved on, but it is good to see it still had an effect on those sites! I knew I was no coward!'

The word "Nazgûl" dances at the forefront of your mind. If Cerdic knew the truth of what came so near to him he would not be so shamed by the fear that filled him.'What does it mean? I am no coward, ${PLAYER}. Something strange is happening at those sites I visited, and I want you to see if you experience anything similar.'Look for traces of the ancient legacy of the Limlight Gorge, when it was still a part of the Golden Wood ages ago.Look for traces of the ancient legacy of the Limlight Gorge, when it was still a part of the Golden Wood ages ago.Find Ancient Elf-ruins in the Limlight GorgeFind Mysterious Elf-ruins in the Limlight GorgeFind Tumbled Elf-ruins in the Limlight GorgeFind Hallowed Elf-ruins in the Limlight GorgeFind Engraved Elf-ruins in the Limlight GorgeFind Crumbling Elf-ruins in the Limlight GorgeDefeat wild beasts in the Great River.Defeat wild beasts in the Great RiverDefeat many wild beasts in the Great River.Defeat wild beasts in the Great RiverThe wild creatures of Dunland pose a great threat to the people who try to survive in these harsh lands.Defeat wild beasts in DunlandThe wild creatures of Dunland pose a great threat to the people who try to survive in these harsh lands.Defeat wild beasts in DunlandBíli, another dwarf-companion of Armund, has gone missing somewhere near the old sawmill in the Limlight Gorge. Armund thinks that he was exploring the south-eastern stretch of the Gorge when he disappeared.You should find Bíli the dwarf and help him get back to the mill.

Armund suggested that Bíli might have been exploring somewhere not far to the east of the old sawmill in the Limlight Gorge.You should speak with Bíli at the Limlight Gorge sawmill.Talk to Bíli'Bíli wandered off hours ago. He is a childhood friend of Finnvarth's, so I expect they went off together. I think Bíli hoped to explore the south-eastern stretch of the Gorge, so he is likely over there somewhere poking about. He just lost all track of time I suspect.''Have you found Bíli?''Bíli is a fool.''Hail, ${PLAYER}, and thank you for your help.

'Can you believe it, though? Armund thinks we are making things up, trying to slip out of work like some lazy Dourhand!

'We will see how long he manages to keep his beard upon his face when he tries to take an axe to one of those trees out there.'The Khundolar have swept into the Brown Lands in preparation to descend upon the Riddermark like a double-edged sword.Khundolar Easterlings can be found at Lashkarg, south-east of Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

Dagbert has asked you to enter Lashkarg, war-camp of the Khundolar, and defeat the Easterling warriors there.Defeat Khundolar at Lashkarg in the Brown Lands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dagbert is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

You should bring news of your victory to Dagbert.Talk to Dagbert in the Brown Lands'Céola delays too long. We must move against the Khundolar now, ${PLAYER}. They have come in great force, and my people will be unable to defend against them.

'Will you help me, ${PLAYER}? Will you enter Lashkarg, their war-camp to the south-east, and put the fear of the Rohirrim into them?

'If so, you should leave at once.''Dagbert sent you to attack the Easterlings at Lashkarg? He is an idiot! We are fortunate the Khundolar have not yet seen fit to descend upon us.

'Well, I cannot blame you for Dagbert's foolishness. You meant well.''Uh...good work, ${PLAYER}. I confess I was not sure I would see you again, but you have proven quite hardy!'The Khundolar have swept into the Brown Lands in preparation to descend upon the Riddermark like a double-edged sword.Khundolar Easterlings can be found at Lashkarg, south-east of Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

Céola has asked you to enter Lashkarg, war-camp of the Khundolar, and defeat the Easterling warriors there in hopes that they will believe Etheburg's numbers greater than they truly are.Defeat Khundolar at Lashkarg in the Brown Lands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Céola is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

You should bring news of your victory to Céola.Talk to Céola at Etheburg in the Brown Lands'Thanks to Dagbert's treason, we are forced to deal with the Khundolar sooner than expected, but perhaps there are ways to weaken or discourage them.

'Make a foray against Lashkarg and defeat some of the Easterlings there. Perhaps by making a show of strength against their encampment, they will believe our numbers are greater than they actually are.

'If you find Dagbert among them, do not hesitate to end his life. Treason is a crime punishable only by death....''As vast as you say Lashkarg is, I doubt this attack has deterred them at all.

'Thank you for your efforts nonetheless.''Uh...good work, ${PLAYER}. I confess I was not sure I would see you again, but you have proven quite hardy!'Halbarad is at the camp in the Gravenwood, trying to decide how to safely proceed with the Grey Company's mission and how to help Théodred at the same time.Saeradan is at the camp in the Gravenwood, by the fire.

Halbarad has decided that your path and the Grey Company's must now diverge, and you should say your good-byes to the Rangers.Talk to Saeradan by the fire at the camp in the Gravenwood'We have come to the cross-roads, ${PLAYER}, and sooner than I expected. I want to help the Prince of Rohan, for without his aid and that of his doughty warriors, my men and I would still be captives of the Falcon-clan. But we cannot abandon our journey; our duty lies with Aragorn, and we must find him in Rohan or die searching. We cannot stay to help Théodred with his battles.

'I want you to go with the prince, ${PLAYER}. I want you to lend him your strength and your wisdom and your courage. You can travel south more swiftly than we can; even hale and hearty, our diminished number would take some time to make ready, and we are wounded and tired.

'Make your farewells with my kin, ${PLAYER}. I know they will want to say good-bye. Saeradan is there, by the fire. He knows what I have in mind, though the others may not.''My men will want to say good-bye, ${PLAYER}. Saeradan is there, by the fire. He knows my intent, though not all the others do.'Calenglad nods at you, but keeps his peace.Corunir nods at you, but keeps his peace.Golodir nods at you, but keeps his peace.Prestadír nods at you, but keeps his peace.Radanir nods at you, but keeps his peace.'Halbarad told me his decision, and I will not speak against it. But I will miss you, ${PLAYER}, and I wish you might still travel with us. I know Erebrandir feels the same way! He has not forgotten the farrier who crafted him a new shoe in Hollin, nor the ${RACE} who saved the farrier.

'You have a skill beyond strength of arms or the reach of knowledge, ${PLAYER}. You find friends wherever you tread, and where they cannot be found you make them with kindness and generosity. Halbarad does not realize how close he came to spending the rest of his days in the prison-caves of Tûr Morva, but I do.

'Consider this, my friend, and do not forget it: Prince Théodred was at this camp for days before you escaped from Isengard and held council with me. But he did not move to rescue my kin until he spoke with you. Nay, do not console me! I am not ashamed. Your ability to rally people to your side is yours alone, and it is a power you must not forget. It has saved many lives already, and I know it will again.'Your path now diverges from that of the Grey Company, and Halbarad wants you to make your farewells before you take your leave.Corunir was last seen down the path north-west of the camp in the Gravenwood, by a large boulder.

You have spoken with Saeradan, and he told you to find Corunir and say good-bye.Talk to Corunir on the path north-west of the camp in the Gravenwood'Ah, you do not need me to tell you these things. You have seen within the Ring of Isengard and escaped to tell the tale! You will be of great help to Prince Théodred, I do not doubt it.

'I saw Corunir down the path to the north-west not long ago. You should find him and bid him farewell.''Corunir will want to say good-bye. I last saw him walking down the path from the camp, north-west of here.'Halbarad nods at you, but keeps his peace.Calenglad nods at you, but keeps his peace.Golodir nods at you, but keeps his peace.Prestadír nods at you, but keeps his peace.Radanir nods at you, but keeps his peace.'You are leaving us now? How can this be Halbarad's decision?'

Corunir stares at his feet for a short moment before he meets your gaze again.

'I suppose the Prince of Rohan has need of you. I cannot blame Halbarad for wanting you to help hold the fords of the Isen. I think your tale of the army Saruman is preparing within Isengard shook him deeply, and if that crossing is lost then the kingdom of Rohan will surely follow.

'We have been through a great deal, ${PLAYER}, have we not? It seemed we would stay within the musty library of Zudrugund with Nár and Frithgeir for the rest of our days, did it not? Even thinking now of climbing up to Nár's Peak makes my legs ache. I want you to have these spare boots of mine, in memory of our time spent in that high place.'The time has come for your path to diverge from that of the Grey Company, and you should say your farewells to the Rangers.Golodir is to the west of Corunir's location near the camp in the Gravenwood.

Corunir wants you to speak with Golodir before you go.Talk to Golodir in the shade of a boulder west of Corunir, in the Gravenwood'I am worried about Golodir, ${PLAYER}. He endured much in the depths of Carn Dûm, as you know, and he will never truly forgive himself for Lorniel's death, but this journey has done much to relieve some of his hurts. I think it is simply this: he has a purpose again. There is work to be done, and until we find Aragorn, as we are sworn to do, it remains unfinished.

'But our captivity in the prison-caves changed something within Golodir. There is a dangerous glimmer in his eye that I have not seen before. It worries me, ${PLAYER}. I am afraid for him.

'We were speaking not long ago, just to the west, but my heart was troubled, and I left him there. Will you seek him out and see if my worries are for naught? I hope they are. My mother always said I worried enough for the whole family, but the dangers through which we have come and to which we ride deserve even more worry than I can manage.''You are leaving us, ${PLAYER}? It may be Halbarad's decision, but I would not leave, even if he ordered me home again.'

He looks up at you, and you do see a hint of the hardness in his eyes that so upset Corunir.

'There are matters still left undone here. The cowards who slink in the caves beneath Tâl Methedras still live, ${PLAYER}. They spoke to us as friends, gave us shelter, took the sweat from our brows, and repaid us with treachery. I will repay them in kind. My lost brethren and my lost friends demand this of me.

'Lheu Brenin? You jest, ${PLAYER}. He is a fat man whose courage depends on a hundred of his people at his back. I have faced the evil of Mordirith...he is gone, and I still live. Lheu Brenin is nothing compared to Mordirith.'Halbarad nods at you, but keeps his peace.Calenglad nods at you, but keeps his peace.'There is some new fire in Golodir's eyes, ${PLAYER}. I am afraid for him. Seek him out, to the west, and allay my fears!'Prestadír nods at you, but keeps his peace.Radanir nods at you, but keeps his peace.The time has come for your path to diverge from that of the Grey Company, and you should say your farewells.Calenglad is at the Tree of Tribute, in the Gravenwood.

Golodir has sent you to say your good-byes to Calenglad.Talk to Calenglad by the Tree of Tribute in the Gravenwood'I thought you would come with me to take vengeance upon Lheu Brenin, ${PLAYER}, but it seems your road lies elsewhere. I am sorry you will not be there. I know you would gladly join me, if urgent matters did not drive you elsewhere.'

Golodir does not seem to be listening to you. He gestures to the south, in the direction of the Tree of Tribute.

'Calenglad is over that way, if you wish to bid farewell to him. It feels like he has been staring at that tree for hours.'Halbarad nods at you and keeps his peace.Corunir nods at you, but there is nothing left to say and he keeps his peace.'Calenglad is to the south there, staring at that tree. I think he has been there for hours.'Prestadír nods at you, but keeps his peace.Radanir nods at you but keeps his peace.'How long do you think this tree has been growing here, ${PLAYER}? It must be so old and have seen so much. Are we anything more than the flickering of a candle in the gale to something so ancient? Would it even notice if we left this place and never returned?'

Calenglad looks weary, and his face is more lined than you remember.

'You are leaving us to aid Théodred? It is no surprise to me; I overheard Halbarad and Saeradan discussing courses of action, and it seemed likely. I wish our paths did not diverge, but it seems they must, ${PLAYER}. We all have too many duties in this world.'The time has come for your path to diverge from that of the Grey Company, and for you to bid the Rangers farewell.Radanir is on the road east of the Tree of Tribute, in the Gravenwood.

Calenglad has told you to say your good-byes to Radanir, or the Rangers would never hear the end of it.Talk to Radanir on the road east of the Tree of Tribute, in the Gravenwood'I am sure Théodred will find a use for you at the Ford of Isen, ${PLAYER}. That is a crucial crossing, and it must be held against the forces of Saruman at any cost. We have seen the danger posed by the Dunlending clans, and the army of Uruks you beheld within Isengard sounds of considerable size.

'Neither of those forces must cross the river Isen.'

Calenglad's bleak expression is lightened by a wry smile.

'You should say good-bye to Radanir. If you do not, we would never hear the end of it. He is east of here, I believe, on the path.'Halbarad nods at you, and keeps his peace.'Do us all a favour and remember to say farewell to Radanir, ${PLAYER}. We would never hear the end of it if you do not. He is east of here, on the road.'Corunir nods at you, but there is nothing left to say, and he keeps his peace.Golodir nods at you, but there is nothing left to say, and he keeps his peace.Prestadír nods at you, but keeps his peace.'Sit with me a moment, ${PLAYER}. I know you are leaving us, and that Théodred has need of you at the Ford. That is the way of it, is it not? We go where we are needed. The Grey Company owes its freedom to you and to the Prince of Rohan, and this is how Halbarad seeks to repay the debt to Théodred. Perhaps he thinks our debt to you is too deep to repay so easily?'

Radanir raises an eyebrow and subjects you to close scrutiny.

'Once this is all over, if such a thing is possible, you and I will meet at some inn in some peaceful land, and I will buy you a drink and you will buy me three, and we will wonder when we got so old.'

He looks back at the Tree of Tribute, and seems to decide something.

'I think Calenglad is wrong. I think it is just a tree.'The time has come for your path to diverge from that of the Grey Company, and you should bid farewell to the Rangers.Prestadír is to the north-east of Radanir, by the path that winds up to the camp in the Gravenwood.

You should now say your good-byes to Prestadír.Talk to Prestadír by the winding road up to the camp in the Gravenwood'Prestadír is up by the camp, north-east of here. He will want to say good-bye to you before you leave us.'Halbarad nods at you, and keeps his peace.Calenglad nods at you, but there is nothing left to say and he keeps his peace.Corunir nods at you, but there is nothing left to say and he keeps his peace.Golodir nods at you, but there is nothing left to say and he keeps his peace.'Prestadír is to the north-east, near the camp. He will want to say good-bye to you next.''I heard it was time for you to leave us, ${PLAYER}. Be well on the road and give your courage to Théodred as you gave it to us.

'If you have the opportunity to rescue Lothrandir from the clutches of Saruman, I hope you will take it. It is not right for us to abandon him to the enemy. I know it does not look good for him, but there is always hope. You escaped from the pits of Isengard, and Lothrandir is made of stern stuff as well.

'I regret that we did not have the opportunity to speak more before this parting, my friend. Perhaps our paths will cross again, in happier days.'The time has come for your path to diverge from that of the Grey Company, and you should bid your farewells to the Rangers.Halbarad is in the camp in the Gravenwood.

You have made your farewells and should return now to Halbarad.Talk to Halbarad in the camp in the Gravenwood'Stay safe on the road, my friend. We will see each other again.

'Halbarad wanted to see you once you had made your farewells. He is somewhere within the camp, ${PLAYER}. You should return to him and prepare to depart.''I am not one for long farewells, my friend. Know that you are one of us, with ties not of blood but rather strengthened by blood: the blood of our foes. Our roads entwined this far, and may do so again, but for now it is the time of leave-taking.

'${PLAYER}, it has been an honour to travel with you. The Grey Company owes you much and more.'Calenglad nods at you, but there is nothing left to say and he keeps his peace.Corunir nods at you, but there is nothing left to be said and he keeps his peace.Golodir nods at you, but there is nothing left to be said and he keeps his peace.'Halbarad is in the camp over there. He wanted you to return to him once you had made your farewells.'Radanir nods at you, but there is nothing left to say and he keeps his peace.You have said your farewells to the Grey Company, and it is now time to travel south to Théodred and Grimbold's camp in the Heathfells.Grimbold's camp is in the Heathfells, south-east of the Gravenwood.

Halbarad wants you to offer your help to Grimbold, Théodred's second in command at the fords of the river Isen.Travel to Grimbold's camp and speak with GrimboldThe map table is in the south-east corner of Grimbold's camp. Trumbald is on the road north of the camp. Cynstan is south of the camp, on the western bank of the ford.

Grimbold has asked you to become acquainted with the layout of his camp and to speak with several of the scouts to learn of the situation here.Find the map table in Grimbold's campTalk to Trumbald on the road north of Grimbold's campTalk to Cynstan on the west bank of the Ford of IsenThéodred is in Grimbold's camp, overseeing the preparations of his men.

You have familiarized yourself with the situation in the Heathfells and should now find Théodred.Talk to Théodred in Grimbold's camp'I understand Prince Théodred's men have established a camp in the Heathfells, far to the south, at the fords of the river Isen. While the Prince has been scouting in the Gravenwood, leadership of the camp fell to his second, a man named Grimbold.

'Travel south to this camp and tell Grimbold you have come to help.''Grimbold's camp is in the Heathfells, south-east of the Gravenwood. Go there and help him to hold the river!''Théodred has returned to us from his study of the Dunlending clans, and his news is worrying. He tells me you are the ${RACE} who escaped from Isengard and spoke of the army you saw being trained.

'We will need your help if we are to hold this crossing against Saruman, ${PLAYER}. He commands both Dunlendings and uruks, and we must keep all of them on this side of the river, out of Rohan.

'Familiarize yourself with the situation here, friend. Find the map table south-east of here, in the camp, to see where we plan the movements of our forces. I would also like you to speak with Trumbald on the road north of the camp to learn what he has seen there today. Speak also to Cynstan, Théodred's esquire, on the western bank of the Isen, south of here. He has been running messages back and forth across the ford as needed.

'Once you have done these things, find Théodred somewhere in the camp. He is reacquainting himself with the situation here as well.''I want you to become familiar with the situation here, friend. Find the map table to the south-east; it is where we plan our movements and form our strategy. Speak also to Trumbald on the road north of the camp, and to Cynstan south of here, at the ford, to learn how things stand.''Things have been quiet on the road for several days now. If Saruman does have an army, he is keeping it inside Isengard for now. I think those must just be tales, after all.'

Trumbald looks at you curiously.

'Were you really imprisoned by Saruman? There have been some arguments among the men about it.'

You nod and tell him briefly of your stay within the Ring of Isengard and of your escape. His face falls at your tale.

'How many uruks, did you say? By the Hammerhand, he does have an army.''Hail. You must be ${PLAYER}. I am Cynstan, and I am the Prince's esquire: I help him with his armour and his steed and with anything else that needs doing.

'Right now he has me bringing messages back and forth across the Ford, getting the men on the other side organized for the defence of the bank.

'Someone has taken one of my bracers, and I think they have hidden it somewhere. It is their idea of a jest, I suppose. Nay, do not bother looking for it. It will turn up eventually.''Prince Théodred is somewhere in the camp, seeing to the preparations of the men. You should find him and see where you can help.''Things have been quiet on the road for several days now. If Saruman does have an army, he is keeping it inside Isengard for now.''Hail. You must be ${PLAYER}. I am Cynstan, and I am the Prince's esquire: I help him with his armour and his steed and with anything else that needs doing.''I am devising a plan, ${PLAYER}, and once all is made ready I will want you to be a part of it. I am glad you have come! I would have welcomed any of your Ranger friends, as well, but I understand that they have their own mission; I wish them well in it!

'Enough talk. It is time to strengthen my forces. They will have need of strength in the days to come.'The Rohirrim charged with holding the Ford of Isen are training so they will be ready when the need arises to push back Saruman's forces.Athelard is on the eyot in the middle of the river, south-east of Grimbold's camp.

Théodred wants you to help Athelard with training some of the youths who have seen less combat.Talk to Athelard on the eyot in the IsenRohirrim footmen and archers are training on the eyot in the Ford.

Athelard has asked you to help train the footmen and archers under his command.Talk to a Rohirrim footman training on the eyotTalk to a Rohirrim archer training on the eyotAthelard is on the eyot in the middle of the Isen, overseeing the training of his men.

You have practiced sparring with the footmen and archers serving with Athelard and should tell him of your experience.Rungar is just to the north of Grimbold's camp, seeing to the training of riders under his command.

Athelard has asked you to help Rungar with the training of his riders.Talk to RungarRiders are practicing their net-throwing down the hill to the east of Rungar.

Rungar has asked you to collect some of the thrown nets and bring them back to him.Collect thrown nets (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rungar is north of Grimbold's camp.

You have collected some of the nets thrown by the riders and should bring them to Rungar.Théodred is in Grimbold's camp, south of Rungar.

Rungar has sent you to see Théodred.Talk to Théodred in Grimbold's CampGrimbold is in his camp in the Heathfells.

Théodred has told you to speak with Grimbold if you wish to help the Rohirrim with their plan to take Saruman by surprise.Talk to Grimbold'From what you have said, we know that Saruman has a much larger force of uruks than we do of Men. Grimbold estimates that each one of us is worth at least twenty uruks, but he is from Grimslade, and the men of that place are not known for their mastery of numbers. I fear he underestimates the savagery of Saruman's forces. And then there are the hostile Dunlending clans to consider.

'Athelard has gone to the eyot in the middle of the river to train some of the men. They have seen less combat than others, and they have much to learn if they are to be of help during a real battle. Go to him and help train these youths.''Prince Théodred's plan is a daring one, but there is wisdom in it. He is like his father in this: he does not shy away from using his strength where it is necessary.

'I would like you by my side during this daring strike, ${PLAYER}. We will plant the seed of doubt in Saruman's mind, and he may think twice before sending his army forth.''My men are practicing a technique to help them fight from horseback, a technique that will snare their opponents. With careful aim, my men can ensnare their enemy's legs with a thrown net. It will then keep the foe-man from attacking until our footmen can close the distance.

'Gather up the nets they have already thrown and bring them to me, ${PLAYER}, and I will give them to new riders. This is something they must master if we are to be as effective as we must be.''These men need to be stronger. We are the only defence between Saruman's evil and the people of Rohan! I need to make them stronger.

'Find a warrior and an archer and fight with them, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps your example will teach them something.''Well? What do you think of their abilities? Are they made of stern enough stuff to stand against the forces of Saruman you beheld within Isengard?'

Athelard sighs heavily.

'They will have to do. You should find Rungar and help him with the training of his riders. He is just to the north of Grimbold's camp.''Your news is welcome, my friend. We need every man to be ready, for what I have in mind is dangerous. But the reward, should we prevail, could be significant.

'If we wait until Saruman's army is ready, we cannot hope to hold the ford against him. We do not have the men. Your report of the size of Saruman's force has it much larger than I feared.

'If we can catch him before he is ready, we might strike such a blow that his force will be sent into disarray. He is wise, yes, and is the wise man not cautious? He will see that we have taken him by surprise, and he will retreat behind his walls and he will continue to grow his army until he is certain we cannot oppose him. Every day that goes by is a day in our favour.

'The size of his army is of no account as long as it stays within Isengard. Let it stay there forever. We must make him uncertain enough of his success that he never moves it. That is my proposal. If you are still willing to help us, Grimbold has asked that you ride with him.''My men must master this technique, for the good of Rohan! Gather up the nets they have thrown and bring them to me. I can send another group of horsemen down to practice.''Prince Théodred is somewhere in Grimbold's camp, south of here. Find him and tell him my riders will be ready for what he has planned.''My men need to be stronger if they are to stand against the forces marshalled by Saruman. Challenge a footman and an archer to combat and see how they fare.''The training of our footmen and archers is well underway. Now you should help some of the riders with their training. Go north of Grimbold's camp and help Rungar.''Saruman has many more forces than we do. We need ours to be stronger than his, despite the numbers!

'Athelard has taken some of the men to the eyot in the middle of the river to train. Go to him and help him with this task!''The size of Saruman's army is of no account if he never moves it. I propose a strike to surprise him, and to show him he is not yet ready to march. Grimbold asked for you to ride with him, if you agree to help us with this plan.''I am ready to show you the strength of my arm, ${PLAYER}. I have sharpened my skills against many of my kinsmen. Let me now sharpen them further!''I have been the victor in many competitions, ${PLAYER}. Few can boast as swift an arrow or as true. If you believe you can best me in training, prove it is so!''Very good. Their skill throwing the nets is improving with each pass. Once they have mastered it, we will be better prepared to face any unmounted foe that comes.

'Théodred will wish to see you. He is somewhere in the camp to the south, I expect. Tell my Prince that the riders are becoming more skilled at the technique he suggested, and that I have some craftsmen working on more nets.'Prince Théodred's plan to strike at Saruman and prevent him from unleashing his armies began on the morning of the Twenty-fifth of February, but much would happen by nightfall.The map table is in Grimbold's camp.

