'I think it would be best for our Rohirrim friends if he did not arrive at his destination.'

'He should be passing by the Pillar of the Hand shortly. Deal with the sentries there before he arrives and lie in wait for him there.''Have you dealt with Saruman's messenger? Good.

'The less contact between Saruman's forces and those of the Dunlendings over the next few days, the better.''Have you dealt with Saruman's emissary yet?'Twiga is concerned about the wild cats that range the Brown Lands. They are vicious and attack the Rohirrim scouts without a second glance.Wild cats can be found throughout the Brown Lands.

Twiga asked you to cull the numbers of the wild cats which roam the Brown Lands, perhaps impressing the fear of Men upon them.Defeat wild cats in the Brown Lands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Twiga is at Etheburg in the Brown Lands.

You should return to Twiga with a report of your deeds.Talk to Twiga at Etheburg in the Brown Lands'So, Céola has asked you to lend us aid? I cannot say that your help is unwanted here, ${PLAYER}. Many of the border-guard throughout the region are genuinely roustabouts and ruffians sentenced to duty in these lands, but there are also many of us who were wrongly accused by Théoden King's counselor, Gríma Wormtongue.

'I am responsible for the scouts, ${PLAYER}, and so I am very concerned with the wild cats which range the Brown Lands. They are vicious and attack our scouts without a second glance.

'Would you hunt the cats and cull their numbers? Perhaps they will learn that Men do not provide the best meat.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}...perhaps we will not suffer so many casualties among our scouts now.''Have you dealt with the wild cats yet?'You have found a friend in Harwick, amidst all of the hostility you have encountered thus far!The friendly traders can be found outside of the Vault, Auction Hall, and Crafting Hall in Harwick.

You should speak with each of the friendly traders and acquaint yourself with the town services of Harwick. Be wary of the guards!Speak with the trader at the VaultSpeak with the trader at the Auction HallSpeak with the trader at the Crafting HallYou have found the major town services of Harwick, and found friends amongst the sea of distrustful people loyal to Harding.

You are not alone in these fair hard lands after all!Completed'Are you the legendary ${PLAYER}? I have heard much of you!

'Be wary; the guards are all keeping an eye out for you, at Aldor Harding's bidding. I will not tell, though. Some folk here count you friend for your great deeds at Langhold. Word spreads fast between our two towns.

'You have one friend here, and more. I hear only good things of you from the traders. You will not be turned away from their establishments, but be careful not to let the guards see you while you are on notice.''Ah, I have heard much of you, ${PLAYER}! I know you by reputation, though we have never met. You are a hero for what you did for Langhold, helping those women and children get out.

'Know that you are counted a friend by the vault-keepers here, brave warrior.''Oh! I have heard of your deeds. You are welcome to spend as long as you like here at the Auction Hall. The guards will not bother you here, and we will certainly not tell them you are here.''I know you! You are that ${CLASS} who gave warning to Langhold, and helped those women and children escape the cursed allies of Mordor.

'You are welcome, most welcome. No one from the Crafting Hall will alert the guards of your whereabouts.'You have found the major town services of Harwick, and found friends amongst the sea of distrustful people loyal to Harding.

You are not alone in these fair hard lands after all!The children of Langhold are extremely impressed by your bravery in facing the salamanders of the Wold.Heburga is in the centre of Langhold, surrounded by a number of admiring children.

You should speak with Heburga to find out about brigand-bashing.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest is solo-only, and cannot be completed in a fellowship.</rgb>Talk to Heburga, daughter of the ThaneThe children of Langhold have invited you to play a game of Brigand-bashing.

You should speak with Heburga to join the children in their game.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest is solo-only, and cannot be completed in a fellowship.</rgb>Play a game of Brigand-bashing with the children of LangholdDala is at the north gate of Langhold.

You should speak with Dala.Talk to Dala'...Wow. You braved the salamander den? That is the most amazing thing I have ever seen a warrior do.

'If you fought salamanders, you might be hardy enough to fight brigands! It is very dangerous, though. If you want to try, you should talk to Heburga.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest is solo-only, and cannot be completed in a fellowship.</rgb>'Hey! You're that stranger, right? I am not afraid of you. I am the daughter of the Thane, and someday I might be in charge, so I am very brave.

'Did you really kill all those salamanders, and wander right into their den to burn the nests? Even our bravest warriors don't dare go there!

'You might be courageous enough to survive our Brigand-bashing game. We'll go easy on you. Come on, let's play!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest is solo-only, and cannot be completed in a fellowship.</rgb>'Are you ready to play some Brigand-bashing? We'll try not to hurt you... much.''It would seem that trouble has arisen on the northern borders.

'You have a hero's reputation, yet you seem to have too much free time, if you are playing at brigand-bashing with the children of Langhold.'The Easterlings at the South Undeep bear the steel of Mordor, and are clearly allies of the Enemy.The Easterlings at the South Undeep bear the steel of Mordor, and are clearly allies of the Enemy.Find Mordor steel on the EasterlingsMordor steel can be found on the Easterlings who are attempting to invade the Wold by way of the South Undeep.

You should collect Mordor steel to show to the Thane.Find Mordor steel on the Easterlings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Adbert can be found at the northern-most watch-tower in the Wold.

You should bring the Mordor steel you found to the watchman and see what he makes of it.The Easterlings at the South Undeep bear the steel of Mordor, and are clearly allies of the Enemy.'This steel bears marks of Mordor! That does not bode well. I will have this sent to the Thane at once, ${PLAYER}.'Wilferth thinks the Orcs and Uruks may have different reasons for being in the East Wall, but he knows that they are all your enemies, and an advantage to any one of them is a problem for your friends.The Orc with the unusually high-quality sword fled after the raid to the north-east and is probably within the camp the Mordor Orcs have established between Mansig's encampment and the Wold.

Wilferth saw an Orc wielding a sword well above its station and wants you to reclaim it.Find an Orc wielding a stolen sword and take it from himWilferth is at the Rohirrim-camp in the East Wall.

You have recovered the stolen sword and should return with it to Wilferth so he can see it.'They may not be working together, ${PLAYER}, but the various tribes of Orcs and Uruks here in the East Wall are each our enemies. Is it possible for them to set aside their differences and work together, as Men do? I am afraid it is. We must oppose them all, wherever we can. 

'During the Orc-ambush that greeted us shortly after our arrival here, I saw an Orc from Mordor carrying a high-quality sword. It was not of Orc-make; their weapons are crude and ugly, but this blade caught the light and seemed to shine as the Orc swung it around him in deadly arcs.

'It is not right for such a weapon to languish in the hands of an Orc. I did not see this Orc after the battle, so he must have escaped into the East Wall somewhere. Find him and take the sword from him, ${PLAYER}. Many of the retreating Orcs fled to the north-east. Begin your search there, at the camp the Mordor Orcs have established between here and the Wold.''I do not like the idea of an Orc holding such a high-quality sword in his filthy hands, ${PLAYER}. Track down the Orc I saw and take it from him! If he fled with the others, he will be somewhere to the north-east, probably to the camp the Mordor Orcs established between here and the Wold.''Ah, you did it! Very good, ${PLAYER}. It brings joy to my heart to know that Orc will no longer wield this stolen sword. Now, who did he steal it from, I wonder?'

Wilferth takes the blade and examines it carefully.

'I would say it is from Gondor, though it does not have much in the way of ornamentation. I will hold onto it for now. It could use a good cleaning. Perhaps it will drink Orc-blood again, as it was intended to do!

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. By wresting it from the clutches of that Orc, you have restored a measure of this blade's dignity.'The refugees from Langhold have built a makeshift camp outside of Harwick with the permission of the Reeve, but better housing is clearly needed.Cíllan is at the refugees' camp outside of Harwick.

You should find Cíllan and see how she fares.Talk to CíllanSomeone in Harwick must deal with the acquisition of houses in the city.

You should speak with people in Harwick who may have homes for sale, so that she may begin to move her people out of the refugee camp and into more permanent homes.Cíllan is waiting in the refugee camp outside Harwick to find out if there are any houses available for purchase in Harwick.

You should tell Cíllan everything you found out.Return to Cíllan in the refugee camp'I know you are anxious to help the refugees of Langhold, ${PLAYER}. You have helped us enough -- I will not keep you anymore. I will be sure to put in a good word for you with Aldor Harding.

'He is a great man, but not a trusting one. I will not keep you from your friends, though. Be safe, ${PLAYER}, and good luck.''My home is razed, my husband and Thane dead, but... I have my children and the people of Langhold in my care, so there is much to live for. It is enough to fend off my despair.

'There are problems I must solve, but I am not quite sure what to do. We cannot stay in this camp outside Harwick for much longer with meagre supplies and no protection against the dangers of the plains.

'Harwick is a densely populated city, but surely there must be some housing for sale. Will you find out if there is any space for my people, and what we can do to acquire permanent housing quickly?''Ah, you say you are looking for a house for those refugees from Langhold? It is difficult with a city so full, but my old family manor has been vacant for over a year. It is likely large enough for all of them.

'But alas! It will be hard for me to part with it, seeing as it is so dear to me. With so many tenants, I would be willing to sell it for three hundred gold.

'Now... I hear the guards have been ordered to arrest you on sight, so I would rather not be seen with you. Three hundred is of course my final offer, since I do not wish to be spotted with the trespasser! But do deliver my offer to lovely Cíllan, with my condolences for her loss.''No, I cannot risk doing business with you. Unlike you travellers who roam about the lands, I like it here, and do not wish to get ousted from my city by an angry Reeve.''You are so brave to venture here against the wishes of the Reeve on behalf of those poor people from Langhold! It is so terrible, what happened.

'I do have a house for sale, but it is dreadfully small. I left it when I married, for it could not fit more than a single man. I am terribly sorry I could not be of more aid....''I am loyal to Harding, not to strangers of distant lands! My Reeve, whom I serve with pride, does not want you in Harwick. I am done talking to you.

'I will not report you to the guards this time, since you speak on Cíllan's behalf, but do not let me see you here again.''I admire your boldness to come into this city on a mission of charity when all the guards are looking out for you, but I wish only to go about my business, and not give myself any unnecessary trouble.

'I cannot sell you my house; I am sorry. Please leave me be.'Cíllan makes a grim face as you recount your conversations with the townspeople of Harwick.

'Only one seller that would suit our needs, and he asks for three hundred gold? That is a steep price... and we have so little.

'I must find a way to come into gold. Let me think on this matter, ${PLAYER}. I cannot thank you enough for risking Harding's wrath by venturing back into Harwick on our behalf!'Wilferth considers himself something of an expert on Orcs and thinks he knows something about the Orcs in the East Wall.Orcs and Uruks can be found throughout the East Wall, and even the warriors carry quivers of arrows.

Wilferth has asked you to defeat Orcs and Uruks throughout the East Wall and to collect arrows from them for him to study.Defeat Orcs in the East Wall and collect short arrows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Uruks in the East Wall and collect long arrows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Wilferth is at the Rohirrim-camp in the East Wall.

You have collected several arrows from the Orcs and Uruks in the East Wall and should bring them to Wilferth for study.'You are here to help? Good. I trust Mansig, of course, but he did lead us into an ambush. He is my captain, and I will not forget it, but I have seen more of war than he has. He may have underestimated the Orc-threat here in the East Wall, but I will not.

'I am something of an expert on Orcs and have fought some hundred of the creatures. They come in all shapes and sizes, and the sharp eye can distinguish other differences as well. To many of my people an Orc is an Orc, but I can see even the subtle differences.

'I think we are dealing with several distinct tribes, ${PLAYER}. Defeat Orcs and Uruks in the woods of the East Wall and collect arrows from their quivers. By studying them, I can determine what tribes we are dealing with here in the East Wall.''Orcs and Uruks carry quivers of arrows. Even those who fight with their hands carry them, if not for their use then to resupply their archer brethren. Defeat these creatures throughout the East Wall and bring me some of their arrows for study.''Let me see the arrows that you have collected. Ah, yes, this is what I suspected.'

Wilferth holds out a pair of the short arrows for you to inspect.

'The length of the shaft is a clue to its origin. This arrow was designed to fly a short distance fairly accurately. It is of the type used by Orcs of Mordor and is primarily a skirmisher's arrow. It is well-suited to combat among the trees of the East Wall and would be less of a threat upon the plains of Rohan. The danger of the Orc that fires this arrow is the danger of surprise. If you know he is there, he will be less effective.'

Wilferth presents to you a pair of much longer arrows.

'These are more troubling. The length of the arrow tells me it is to be used with a much larger bow, drawn by a stronger arm. The Uruk that fires this arrow expects it to travel much further than those fired by his Orc-brothers. This is an arrow ideal for combat on the plains of my homeland, and I fear it was designed for that purpose.

'The Orcs and Uruks in the East Wall are equipped for different purposes, ${PLAYER}. They possess different goals, but I can tell you one thing they share at least: they hate us and are all our enemies.'The leader of the brigands in the northern Wold seems overly confident despite the recent banishment of his people.The farmer whose lands were looted can be found west of Langhold, not far from the north gate.

You should speak with the farmer and hear out the story of what befell him.Question the farmer about the attackEastmund's Lair is along the western edge of the northern Wold.Find the brigand campThe foes amassed near Langhold appear to be very organized.

You should seek the leader of this rabble and encourage him to return with his unruly followers to exile.Confront the leader of the brigands about the group's aggressive positioningEastmund has asked for a message of grievances to be brought to Thane Utred of Langhold.

You should go to Thane Utred at once and deliver the message, and warn him of the forbidding brigand presence near Langhold.Bring Eastmund's message to the ThaneCíllan stands near the Thane in the Mead Hall of Langhold.

You should show Cíllan the letter.Show the letter to CíllanUtred is in the Mead Hall in Langhold.

You should speak with Thane Utred now that Cíllan has said her piece.'I see that you have some skill at fighting child-brigands. How would you like to try your hand at the real thing, eh, stranger? Why play at fighting when there are real enemies to be cowed?

'Brigands have raided a farm to the west of here, and the poor farmer has lost everything. If you want to be useful as your reputation bids, you should go and deal with the team of bandits who did this.'Cíllan looks over the letter with sharp eyes.

'Really, Utred, I do not think you should make light of this threat. They have never poised themselves to attack our town -- only to harry our lands, and steal from the farmers.

'I do not like Eastmund's language, and I think we should heed ${PLAYER}'s words of warning! We should prepare to defend ourselves.''Ah! Someone to help me. I am glad the city watch sent a strong-looking ${CLASS} to aid me.

'The brigands came just before dawn and ransacked my farm, and beat me.

'There were only four, maybe five of them that did the dirty work. Brigands would not dare return to the northern Wold in any great number, so long as Utred is Thane. I did not make out which way they ran...perhaps south-west?'Utred reads the letter with a scowl.

'Fool Eastmund! He dares offer me terms of peace if I give up Langhold and the northern Wold to his brigand scum!

'I will not send my folk away, and I will rally my riders and deal with the brigands at once. They should have been thankful for exile...it was a pardon. I will not be so forgiving next time.''If you are in need of employ, there are some real brigands in need of banishment. Soldiers grown do not waste time with children's games that mimic the deeds of Men.''We have every right to be here. Do you think you can just harass my folk, stranger, because some power-crazed Thane told you to? I will stand up for my folk!''Defend ourselves? They will never attack us. Why not, you ask? Because we will oust them first, as we always do.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}, for your diligence, but you are to leave those brigands to me. If you wish to be of service, you may perform the patrol of the River Watch.'The Undeep Watch is very concerned with the Easterling movements at the South Undeep.The Easterling camp can be found at the South Undeep.Find the source of the watchman's dread in the Easterling encampmentA fenced-in area can be found behind the Easterling camp at the South Undeep.

You should peek into the fenced-in area to see if anything is amiss.Peek into the hole in the fenceAdbert is at his post at the Undeep Watch.

You should return to Adbert with news of the Nazgûl's presence in the Easterling camp, along with the brigands poised to attack Langhold with aid from Mordor.Return at once to Adbert to warn him'We have spotted Easterlings moving along the shore, and also a great bird circling overhead. An eagle, perhaps? But... I find that I cannot bear to look at it for long, for my heart fills with dread.

'I am loath to venture down to the South Undeep to see what evil is afoot with our foes, but I know it must be something terrible. If you have a stout heart, by all means go take a look. But I will not judge you for fleeing.

'All I know is that the Thane will not react without evidence. He is too practical to risk Langhold for naught but a chill upon the air....''A Nazgûl of Mordor! Save us....

'You must ride to Langhold, ${PLAYER}, as fast as you can. Warn the Thane! We do not stand a chance against this united army of foes!'You have found an arrow in the woods. Curiously, the arrow is clearly of Elf-make, unlike the crude arrows of the Orcs in the East Wall.You need to find an Elf somewhere in the East Wall who can study the arrow of Elf-make and tell you where it may have come from.Your eye is drawn to a curious sight, an arrow delicately made, finely-crafted and prepared with obvious care. It is an arrow of Elf-make, and as you study it you wonder if there is anyone nearby who might be able to shed some light on how it came to be here.

Clearly, its owner was fighting Orcs. But where did this mysterious friend go?Corudan takes the arrow between his fingers and studies it carefully.

'This is most interesting, not at all like the arrows used by Orcs. I would know this craftsmanship anywhere: this arrow was made within the Golden Wood, within Lothlórien. I know whose arrow this is, but I will not speak his name here in the open.

'He did not loose this arrow for practice, or to test its flight: he fired it at a foe. See the blood on the point? He hit his mark. He sought to defend his companions from Orcs, that much is clear, but one thing troubles me: why did he leave this arrow behind? It is still intact, and could be used again on the road ahead. What spurred him to leave with such haste that he could not collect it, if leave he did?

'Danger, I do not doubt. But it is a danger he survived, for he is not to be found here in the East Wall, is he?'The Wargs of Fang point have grown increasingly aggressive of late, and Agelmund wishes to know what has caused this change in their behaviour.Fang Point is south of Feldburg, with two dens associated with the same pack: one to the north, and one to the south..

You should scout the north and south dens of Fang Point.Scout the northern den of Fang PointScout the southern den of Fang PointCaptain Agelmund awaits you at Feldburg in the Wold.

You should return to Captain Agelmund with your assessment of the Wargs in Fang Point.Return to Captain Agelmund in Feldburg'You defeated several of the pack-members outside of Fang Point, but I wish to know more about the beasts' movements of late. What is causing this change?

'It would be very helpful to know more; will you scout the north and south of Fang Point, and see what there is to see?''The Wargs in Fang Point are a larger pack than I expected. Thank you for the scouting report.'Utlac wants you to investigate the cave-mouth you discovered once you are done helping the other Rohirrim at the camp.The cave-mouth you discovered is south of Mansig's encampment, near where the stream flows back into the East Wall, in the western cliffs.

Utlac wants you to finish helping his friends at the Rohirrim-camp in the East Wall, then investigate the cave you discovered.Finish helping the Rohirrim at the camp, then investigate the cave-mouthYou have found a man inside the cave-system beneath the East Wall.Talk to the man in the cave'We do not know what might lie within that cave, ${PLAYER}, but that fleeing Orc wanted to get there. I think you should go back to the cave mouth and investigate.

'You should finish helping out around camp first, of course. I do not want to deprive my friends of your aid, ${PLAYER}! Go investigate the cave once everyone here is satisfied and our camp is prepared for whatever the Orcs may have in store for us.''I want you to investigate the cave-mouth you discovered, but not before you help my friends here at camp. I do not want to be responsible for depriving them of your aid prematurely! Once you are satisfied that the camp is situated well, then you should investigate the cave.

'It was near where the stream flows back into the East Wall from the western cliffs, was it not? A good distance to the south of Mansig's encampment?''Oh, I did not see you approach. But here you are!

'Well now, a ${CLASS}, is it? It is! Ah, you are a welcome change from the Orcs, I tell you true!

'There is no shortage of those creatures down here, and they are interfering with my studies and my enjoyment of this journey both! With your help, I imagine they will be less of a problem! My companions are still above-ground and are unavailable to help. I cannot imagine Rodwen even realizes I am missing. Perhaps she is the one who is missing? Yes, that sounds more reasonable!'You have found a dark stain on the large oak tree in the glade filled with the bodies of dead Orcs.If you are able to find a veteran of many battles and combats in war somewhere in the East Wall, he may be able to shed some light on what happened at the Dead Orc Glade.Find a veteran of many battles somewhere in the East Wall who can shed light on the bloodstained treeA dark stain discolours the bark of a solitary oak at the outskirts of a glade filled with the bodies of dead Orcs. The sight of blood is unmistakeable, but whose blood was it? And where did the wounded warrior go?

Someone who has seen many battles in war might be able to shed some light on what happened here.Wilferth listens as you describe the blood-stained tree at the Dead Orc Glade.

'Interesting,' he says after a moment. 

'You say there was no body near the tree? And no trail of blood leading from the base of the oak anywhere nearby? It seems to me that our unknown warrior must have rested at the base of that tree after slaying the Orcs in that glade.

'One thing still puzzles me, ${PLAYER}. Where did our mystery warrior go after his great deed? If he succumbed to his wounds, his body would remain by the tree. Did he survive? Or did he have friends to bear his body away?

'We may never know.'You need to travel into Rohan in search of what remains of the Fellowship, but for that you will need to acquire war-steeds for your companions.You have travelled with some of Mansig's riders to Langhold, in the Wold, in search of war-steeds for your companions.Talk to Adnoth outside Langhold, in the Wold'The four of you will need war-steeds if you are to have any hope of making good time on the plains of Rohan. Some of my men are returning to the Wold. You are welcome to go with them, if you like. Help my people at Langhold and Harwick, and you can have the war-steeds you need.'

<rgb=#FF0000>You will automatically travel to Langhold in the Wold when you accept this quest.</rgb>'Welcome to the Wold, ${PLAYER}. You have need of war-steeds? Perhaps I can help you.'You have come to the East Wall, where the Argonath towers on either side of the Anduin and the falls of Rauros boom unceasingly.Horn has asked you to speak with Nona and Corudan before gathering sticks to make a campfire.Talk to Nona in the shadow of the ArgonathHorn has asked you to speak with Nona and Corudan before gathering sticks to make a camp-fire.Talk to Corudan in the shadow of the ArgonathFallen sticks can be found near the trees not far from the Argonath.

Horn has asked you to collect fallen sticks near the Argonath for preparing a camp-site.Collect fallen sticks by the trees near the Argonath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Horn is by the beach near the Argonath.

You have collected fallen sticks for making a camp-fire, as Horn requested.Your boat is beached not far from the Argonath.

Horn has asked you to remove the supplies from the boat.Remove the remaining supplies from your boat near the ArgonathHorn speaks quietly, for what he has to say is meant for your ears alone.

'We spoke of a great many things on the boat, and I do not begrudge you for speaking in generalities, but you told me enough to know your errand here is important. Friends of yours came this way, friends bearing a burden of some malice. Whatever they carried, it was sought by Orcs, and perhaps others as well. We need to find what happened to these friends of yours and if their burden has been lost.'

Horn raises his voice to include the Rohirrim man standing nearby.

'This man saw our boat drifting down the river, and hailed me from the shore. His name is Framric, and he has come with a party of Rohirrim from the Wold. I would have us help his people, if they desire our help.

'But first we must prepare our own camp. Speak with Nona and Corudan for their thoughts, and then collect sticks for a fire. I will do the same.''We have several purposes here in the East Wall. Let us help Framric and his men while we search for signs of your friends and what happened to them.''I do not trust this Framric. I do not like the way he looks at me, from out of his eye's corner. If he is not careful I will teach him the way of my people with Wadu's sword.

'But Horn wants us to help him. I do not understand this loyalty to the horse-lords. They cast him out! He should hate them for that.

'I will not impose my beliefs upon him. But I do not understand his.''Framric's people rode from the Wold to investigate the Orcs crossing the river here in the East Wall. Speak with him to learn how we can help them, but of course we will keep our eyes peeled for sign of your friends, as well. That is why we are really here, after all.''Horn is free to do as he wishes, and if he is not angered by the horse-lords for casting him out, that is his choice. But I do not like it, and I will not refrain from telling him so!''You spoke in general terms on the boat to Horn and Nona, but I know why we are really here, ${PLAYER}. The Lady Galadriel entrusted me with knowledge of our true mission. I will not betray that trust.

'While we are here in the East Wall, I think it is fine for us to help Framric's people. That is clearly what Horn desires. But we must also keep alert for signs of our mutual friends. They came to this land, that we know. What happened to them then? That is a mystery, and it is one we must find the answer to and then help them, if we can.

'But first, Horn is right. We need to gather sticks to make a camp-fire, or we will have a cold night.''You will find fallen sticks near the trees by here, ${PLAYER}. Gather some sticks and we can build a fire.''Are you looking for sticks near the trees by here? I will search for some as well.''We should all be searching near the trees of this area for fallen sticks so we can make a camp-fire.''Horn is waiting for those sticks. He said he was going to gather them as well, but he has been here talking to Framric the whole time!''With those sticks we should be able to make a good camp-fire. I have gathered some as well.''You found enough sticks for our fire? Good, I will combine these with the ones Nona and Corudan have found, and we will soon have a decent camp-fire.

'Will you remove the supplies from the boat so we can assess what we have left?''Check the boat for supplies. We will need some of what we brought for thoroughly exploring the East Wall.''Horn wants to see how many supplies we have left? How long does he think we are going to stay here?''Now we see if Bordhrist has given us the amount of supplies we will need for the conclusion of our exploration. Take the supplies from the boat and see!'You reach into the boat and remove the remaining supplies for apportioning.Your exploration of the East Wall will need to be comprehensive if you are to learn what happened to the Fellowship here, and if it is still in your power to help them.Corudan is in the shadow of the Argonath.

You have divided up the supplies you brought from Lothlórien and should now speak with Corudan.Talk to Corudan in the shadow of the ArgonathThe lawn of Parth Galen is south of the Argonath, on the south-western side of the lake of Nen Hithoel.

Corudan believes you should begin your search for the Fellowship on the lawn of Parth Galen.Find the lawn of Parth Galen, in the East WallA discarded pack lies partially buried in the grass of Parth Galen.Examine the discarded pack and experience 'Session: The Breaking of the Fellowship' or read the shortened account at the Parth Galen camp-site insteadSomewhere within the East Wall, there are more signs of what happened to the Fellowship.A great battle was fought at Dead Orc Glade... but not everyone came this way. Are there clues on the beach back near Parth Galen?Scour the beach near Parth Galen for clues and experience 'Two Hobbits'Sandy footprints on the beach near Parth Galen may give some clue as to what happened to the Fellowship!Scour the beach near Parth Galen for clues and experience 'Session: Two Hobbits' or read the shortened account instead....Corudan is in the shadow of the Argonath, in the East Wall.

You have learned much about what befell the Fellowship, and should now return to tell the story to Corudan.Talk to Corudan in the shadow of the ArgonathThere are enough supplies for a short stay, and you divide them among the rest of your companions.'I think it is good to help Framric while we can, but always keep your eyes open for the real reason we are here.

'As you pass southward through the woods of the East Wall, remain alert for a grassy lawn alongside the Anduin. That peaceful space is called Parth Galen, and it may be a good place to begin our search.''The lawn of Parth Galen is south of the Argonath, on the south-western side of the lake of Nen Hithoel. That may be a good place to begin your search for our mutual friends.'You feel the rough leather of the discarded pack between two fingers, and wonder who might have left it here, unattended. Was it left out of need, or out of forgetfulness?The Fellowship of the Ring has come to the lawn of Parth Galen, but unless its eight surviving members can overcome the evils that threaten to divide them, it will go no further. Even now, the One Ring seeks to ensnare another heart....This Uruk bears the mark of the White Hand, and was slain by many desperate cuts of a blade.These tracks by the shore are very different than the booted feet that went off to the north of the camp and the discarded pack. You should investigate these sandy footprints eventually, but first you should go where the danger seems to have gone, and follow the other set of tracks that lead away from the discarded pack to the north!'You found a discarded pack? It sounds like they had to leave in a hurry! Return to the site of the pack, at Parth Galen, and look for tracks that might tell us where they went!'The Fellowship of the Ring has broken. Is it too late for Boromir of Gondor to make up for the madness that seized him near the high seat of Amon Hen? Desperate to save the hobbits from death, he fights the ferocious Uruks of the White Hand with the last of his strength....'A great battle was fought in that glade, that much is clear. But not every one of our friends is accounted for, ${PLAYER}. Return to the beach near Parth Galen and scour it for more clues.'You see sandy footprints among the stones of the beach. They are unmistakably those of a hobbit.'You found sandy footprints leading down to the water near Parth Galen? And you did not investigate them further? ${PLAYER}! This is serious!'When all else fails, good old-fashioned hobbit-sense must win the day. And so it is that Samwise Gamgee learns of the Ringbearer's plan, and the two set off for Mordor and the Mountain of Fire... alone.'It is a sad tale you weave! The burden our mutual friends bear caused some amount of discord among them, and they were splintered. It seems from the signs you read that one or more of them repented of their deeds and sought to repair the divide when the Orcs appeared.

'One halfling stayed clear of the fighting, and it seems another joined him. If they did not cross the river by themselves I cannot come up with another explanation. The two of them have now left the East Wall, heading east. You know the identity of these two, ${PLAYER}: can it be anyone but the bearer of the great burden and his loyal servant? Only Galadriel can hope to see them now.

'I am uncertain of the path of the rest of the company, but my own reading of the signs has shown me something that seems fairly clear: three of that company have left for Rohan... on foot. I believe they pursue the other two halflings of the company.'Corudan has found some sign of the Fellowship in East Wall as well, and wants you to investigate.Mansig's encampment is located south-west of the Argonath, on the border of the East Wall and the Norcrofts. A man named Ingild claims to have information of interest to you and your companions.Talk to Ingild at Mansig's encampment, in the East WallTo the west of Mansig's encampment, a ridge overlooks the Norcrofts of Rohan. Ingild thinks you should <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> out over the plains and see if you can see the Uruks that fled East Wall.Your companions stand on the ridge that overlooks the Norcrofts of Rohan, and want to speak with you concerning your next move.Mansig's encampment is east of the ridge that forms the border between the East Wall and the Norcrofts.

You should speak with Mansig about acquiring war-steeds that will allow you to follow the Uruks into Rohan.Talk to Mansig at his encampment'Horn and Nona went to the camp Framric spoke of, led by a Rohirrim man named Mansig. His encampment is located on the border of the East Wall and the Norcrofts. They were going to speak with a man there named Ingild who claims to have seen something of interest to us. You should speak with this man now and see what he knows. I will come as well.''You have urgent business within Rohan? It is not for me to ask questions. Know that my people are suspicious of outsiders, and you travel with not one but two, and are one yourself.''I do not know anything about that Dunlending girl, but Horn is one of my people and I trust him. I will tell you what I told him: a party of Uruks has fled from the East Wall on foot into the Norcrofts. If you go to the ridge west of here, you might be able to <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> over the plains and see where they went.''It is strange to look out over the beloved fields of my home and know I am not welcome there, ${PLAYER}. It is your duty to follow the rumour of your friends, and it is my desire to help you do it. So I will follow you if you must pass into Rohan.

'Corudan tells me that the Uruks are likely pursued by an Elf, a dwarf, and a Man, and though the tracks are faint and hard to read, I trust his instincts and his tracking skill. They would need to be incomparable hunters themselves to catch the Uruks if they did not depart immediately, and it seems they did not.

'And we are leaving well after both groups, so heed this warning: we will have no hope of catching up with the Uruks that fled the East Wall without war-steeds of our own. We should speak with Mansig about acquiring such steeds.''Look at the rolling fields and plains of the horse-lords. They were meant for my people, but they were taken from us.'

Nona scowls, but then her expression softens.

'Horn and I hunted for signs of your friends while you were at Parth Galen, ${PLAYER}. He is not like the rest of the horse-lords. If he wishes to go into Rohan, a land from which he is exiled, I will do so without complaint. See how much he risks for the causes of strangers? He is a most unusual man, ${PLAYER}.

'I will keep an eye on him for you.''A group of Uruks passed this way; the signs are clear about that much, at least. They trampled grass underfoot and kicked aside stones, with little care for where they placed their booted feet. They were in a hurry, and they held captives: two halflings whose identities I think we both know, ${PLAYER}.

'The Uruks are right to worry, for their run took them into Rohan, and they are several days ahead of us... and, I feel, mere hours ahead of their pursuers. For they were pursued: by three of our mutual friends, I think. But why? Did the Uruks think they had captured something more than captives?

'We must follow them. If we can lend our aid to the captives or the pursuers, we must do so. But we will need war-steeds if we are to have any hope of catching them on the great plains of Rohan.''If you go to the ridge to the west of this encampment and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> out over the plains, perhaps you will be able to see some sign of the Orcs that fled the East Wall.''Framric is from a Rohirrim encampment located to the south-west, on the border of the East Wall and the Norcrofts. Horn and Nona went to speak with a man there named Ingild who may know something of interest to us.''Stand on this ridge and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> out over the plains of Rohan. We must see if there is anything to see, ${PLAYER}!''An Elf, a dwarf, and a Man. Did those three hunters have any hope of catching the fleeing Orcs? We will need war-steeds to have any hope of catching them, let alone their prey!''From here on this ridge, who knows what you might see if you were to <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> out over the plains?''What dangers await us in Rohan? And Horn says we will need war-steeds if we are to cross the plains? What would Suvulch say of his daughter, could he see her now? What would Wadu say?''Stand on this ridge and <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> over the plains, ${PLAYER}. There may be something we can see.''Three hunters ran from this point into Rohan, pursuing the fleeing Uruks. Did they catch them? We will need war-steeds if we are to have any hope of making good time on the plains of Rohan.'You and your companions will need war-steeds if you are to travel across the plains of Rohan with any speed.In order to obtain war-steeds for yourself and your companions, you will need to accomplish two things: complete the preparations for and defence of Langhold, and learn about mounted combat in Harwick.

The defence of Langhold involves a chain of ten quests that begins with a quest bestowed by Dala called 'The Wold.' If Dala has no more quests for you, search around Langhold for other quest-givers.

You can learn about mounted combat at the Tourney Field in Harwick.Complete all quests in Langhold, in the WoldLearn how to participate in mounted combat at the Tourney Field in HarwickYour companions have obtained war-steeds of their own, and are waiting to speak with you in Harwick, by the tourney ground near the stables.Enstan is by the south-east exit of Harwick, in the Wold.

Your companions have war-steeds of their own and now you have heard that Enstan wants your help with something urgent.'We face difficult times not just in Langhold, but throughout all of the Wold as well. Théoden King has left those of us in the Wold without much support. I do not blame him! Rumour tells of great armies marshalling in the west, and we are spread too thin over too wide an area.

'If you desire to travel across the Kingdom of Rohan, you will need war-steeds for yourself and your companions. There are two things you will need to accomplish in order to obtain the privilege of such steeds. First, help in the support of Langhold. Once this city has been made safe, travel south along the road to Harwick, and you will be instructed in the skills necessary to safely and swiftly ride a war-steed.''It is good of Aldor Harding to provide these war-steeds to us. When I was last in these lands, he was a man of short temper and did not believe the King was strong enough to protect Rohan from what threatened her. I cannot imagine he has changed, but it is good to see he is making Harwick ready for whatever may come. These steeds are of good breeding, and should serve us well. Nona has not lived her life among horses, but still she has done very well to pick it up in short order. War-steeds are different from the horses she may have had occasion to ride in the past, and she has adjusted well. Corudan, too, is already at home on the back of his steed. We will make good time over the plains.''My people do not fight from horseback, so this has not been an easy adjustment for me. Still would I rather fight on foot, and let my enemies charge toward me at their peril! But I understand we have a need for the speed of hooves if we are to find your friends, and for that I will put up with it. I hope I do not shame myself in front of our companions with my poor riding. Horn rides as if he were born in the saddle, and Corudan has the natural grace of his people.

'I feel like an aurochs up here.''The horses of Rohan are renowned for their loyalty, strength, and speed, and it seems to my eye that the steeds they have given us are no exception.

'If the steeds of the others are satisfactory as well, one of the men here requested our aid. Speak to Enstan south-east of here, by the exit from Harwick.''All of us must band together if we are to survive the encroaching dangers, but there are so few of us fighting men available. And now, Floodwend has sent urgent messages calling for our aid! Mansig and most of his men are still away, investigating the East Wall. If he were here, I know he would ride at once in defence of Floodwend, but he is not!

'Can you ride those new war-steeds of yours with any skill?''Two things must you accomplish to earn the war-steeds you seek. First, help in the defence of Langhold. Second, you must travel south to Harwick to learn how to safely and swiftly engage in combat from the back of a war-steed.''Your companions have obtained war-steeds, yes? You should speak with them in Harwick.''It is good of the men of Harwick to provide us with these steeds. We must help them if we can. Enstan requested our aid. Speak with him to the south-east, by the exit from Harwick, and learn if helping him is in our power.''I hope I do not shame myself with my poor riding! If I fall from the back of this steed, I will never forgive you for making me ride, ${PLAYER}.''These war-steeds will serve us well on the plains of Rohan. Enstan requested our aid. Find him to the south-east, by the exit from Harwick.'Utlac says the greatest danger faced by the Rohirrim who have come to the East Wall is the threat posed by Orc-archers lurking among the trees.Orc and Uruk-archers can be found throughout the East Wall.

Utlac has asked you to defeat Orcs or Uruks throughout the East Wall and to take their bows so he can destroy them.Collect bows from Orcs or Uruks in the East Wall (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Utlac is at the Rohirrim-camp in the East Wall.

You have recovered many crude bows from the Orcs that inhabit the East Wall and should bring them to Utlac so he can destroy them.A camp-fire near Utlac is just the place to burn the Orc-bows you have taken.

Utlac cannot bear to handle the Orc-bows any longer and has asked you to burn them in a nearby camp-fire.Utlac is at the Rohirrim-encampment, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Utlac'Give me three good archers from Snowbourn, and I could keep a dozen Orcs at bay, even in poor light or foul weather. The bows of my people are well-made, but it is the strength of a Rohirrim archer's arm and the sharpness of his eye that truly makes him dangerous.

'Orcs are different. Their bows are crudely-fashioned, and they seek to make up for their poor training with numbers only. Still, an arrow fired poorly from hiding can pierce even the bravest man, on occasion.

'Defeat archers throughout the East Wall, ${PLAYER}, and bring me their bows for breaking. I do not want a stray arrow to fell even a single man more.''The miserable Orcs that sneak throughout the East Wall use their bows to attack the men of this company. Defeat the Orcs, seize their bows, and bring them back to me!''Look at these things, ${PLAYER}. I knew the Orcs cared little for the craft of bow-making, but I did not expect them to be this crude. It is a wonder these bows do not fall apart in their filthy hands.

'Still, an arrow fired from one of these can still kill. We had best not forget that.

'I cannot bear to handle these bows any longer, ${PLAYER}. Take them and throw them in the fire over there. The Orcs will loose no more arrows at the Rohirrim from these crude things. Oh, and one more thing! Do not burn these bows in any of the cookfires. That would simply be offensive. Make sure the fire you choose is not being used to cook someone's meal.''I gave you back the bows, ${PLAYER}. Throw them in the camp-fire over there so I do not need to see them any longer. The thought that one of my friends could be slain by a stray arrow fired from one of those crude devices has turned my stomach.''Can you smell that? Even the smoke of the burning smells foul. Thank you for helping destroy those crude bows, ${PLAYER}. Every blow to the Orcs helps our chances of surviving here in the East Wall.'Reports have come into Harwick of salamanders behaving very strangely in the bog near the stables.Crazed salamanders can be found roaming the bog near Harwick.

You should kill crazed salamanders in the bog, and observe their strange behaviour.Kill crazed salamanders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Crazed salamanders can be found roaming the bog near Harwick.

You should try to discover the source of the strange behaviour in the salamanders.Discover the source of the salamanders' strange behaviourThe source of the crazed behaviour in the salamanders seems to have something to do with draugar coming out the cave ahead....Completed'A cheeky little boy from Langhold boasted to me of your valour against the salamanders troubling that town before it was seized. Now that you have a proper steed, I have a task for you, if you wish it.

'We have had reports of salamanders behaving strangely over in the bog where the marsh-flies have settled in. The salamanders once fed on the flies, but lately we have seen flies taking over, and salamanders acting unlike themselves....

'Will you destroy the crazed salamanders? They cannot be good for the health of the plains near Harwick.'The source of the crazed behaviour in the salamanders seems to have something to do with draugar coming out the cave ahead....A farm near Langhold has been robbed, and the brigands are too many and too near for your liking.Cattle and other stolen goods can be found in the brigand encampment in the northern Wold.

You should free the cattle and collect the stolen goods from the brigands.Collect stolen goods from brigandsCattle and other stolen goods can be found in the brigand encampment in the northern Wold.

You should free the cattle and collect the stolen goods from the brigands.Free cattle from their tethers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect stolen coins from brigands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect stolen grain (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The farmer is at his farm between Langhold and the brigands' encampment.

You should return the stolen goods to the farmer, who is much poorer after the robbery by brigands.A farm near Langhold has been robbed, and the brigands are too many and too near for your liking.'My... my goods! The cattle wandered back on their own, and I've tucked them away in the barn, and now the lost money and grain? I do not know how to thank you, ${PLAYER}. Thank goodness you came to Langhold when you did.'You narrowly escaped the battle in Langhold, but you grieve for those who died defending their keep.What remains of Langhold is not far, just a little ways south along the road in the northern Wold.

You should return to Langhold and have a look around at the town that was so recently thriving -- namely the north gate, the centre of town, and the Mead Hall.Scout the north gate of LangholdScout the town's centreScout the Mead HallLanghold is empty, a ruin of what it once was.

You should seek Cíllan and the other refugees of Langhold. They headed towards Harwick, and you should ensure that they arrived safely, and bring the grievous news to the family of the Thane.Search for Cíllan in HarwickCíllan is in the stables of Harwick, tending to her late husband's war-steed.

You should speak with Cíllan in Harwick.Tell Cíllan about her husband's final stand'It is all over! If you wish to go and see if anything is left of Langhold, I will not stop you, but my heart cannot bear it. I make for Harwick, where I hope to be of use to my Thane's family.

'Perhaps I shall see you there, ${PLAYER}. I will not forget the good you did for us, while Langhold still stood strong.''Halt, stranger! What is your business here?'

You explain to the guard that you seek Cíllan, wife of the late Thane of Langhold, and bring news to the Thane of Harwick that war has begun in the Wold.

'Indeed! You may enter, then. Cíllan is at the stables, tending Utred's war-steed. You bear grievous tidings of our neighbours' fates.''Dear ${PLAYER}! I am glad another has made it here alive. We saw the smoke, and brave guards from Harwick already rode forth and scouted to return with the news. My husband...'

Cíllan does not meet your eyes; she stares only at her husband's war-steed. It is clear that she is numb from the shock of the news of her husband's death.

'This steed is for you. Utred will not need him now. Consider him a gift for all that you did for us, my valiant friend.'Cíllan hopes to make a payment on the manor to secure a place to live for her people with the savings you recovered from Langhold.Frithbéorth is in Harwick, waiting patiently for a delivery of three-hundred gold to secure the purchase of his family's large manor.

You should bring Cíllan's down payment to Frithbéorth and see if he will accept it.Bring Cíllan's first payment to FrithbéorthFrithbéorth reluctantly hands over the keys to his family's large manor.Speak to Frithbéorth to enter the manorCíllan awaits you at the refugees' camp outside of Harwick.

You should hasten to Cíllan to hand her the keys and tell her of the deal with Frithbéorth... and it would be wise to mention the rats as well.Return to Cíllan at the refugee camp'Please take this money to Frithbéorth, ${PLAYER}, and see if he will accept it as a payment to secure his family manor. Please, see if he will let us stay and pay the rest over time. The people of Langhold are honest, and true to their word.

'The guards are still on the look-out for you, so do be careful in Harwick. I will speak with the Reeve, for Harding was a friend of my husband's. I will not rest until he has released his grudge against you!

'You have helped us so much, and I endeavour now to help you.''This is not half of the price that I quoted to you. What is the meaning of this?'

You explain Cíllan's plea that this will be a down payment, and that the rest be paid over time as the people of Langhold work. After all... they have nothing. They must build it all again.

'Fine. But I expect payment every month. I will give Cíllan a year to make up the difference. But it is really a kindness on my part. Too kind. I begin to think that people take advantage.

'Here, take the keys before I change my mind!''Here are the keys. You bought it as-is, so I don't want to hear anything about what condition you find it in.''Oh, ${PLAYER}... I cannot express my thanks enough. Rats we can handle ourselves, and money we can earn in plenty, once we are settled. I will continue to seek an audience with Harding to clear his bad opinion of you.

'It is unjust that such a noble ${RACE} could receive such hostile treatment from the Reeve of the Wold, after all you did for us.

'No matter how Harding may behave, you have my thanks. I will never forget your deeds.'Easterlings have been spotted encroaching upon the South Undeep, spilling from their own lands into the Wold.Easterlings can be found at the South Undeep.

You should defeat many Easterlings in the northern Wold.Defeat Easterlings at the South UndeepEasterlings can be found at the South Undeep.

You should defeat many Easterlings in the northern Wold.Defeat Easterlings at the South Undeep (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Adbert is at the northern watch-tower at the river's edge in the northern Wold.

You should talk to Adbert at the northern watch-tower.Talk to Adbert at the northern towerEasterlings have been spotted encroaching upon the South Undeep, spilling from their own lands into the Wold.'You have shown them that the Wold will not tolerate this act of aggression, ${PLAYER}. They will not dare press forward so eagerly now. Thank you!'Marsh-flies infest the bogs around Harwick, making noise that disturbs the war-steeds. The eccentric guard of the stables spoils his steeds, and wishes for the noise to be silenced.The bog to the west of Harwick is inhabited by a swarm of Marsh-flies.

You should defeat marsh-flies for disturbing the sensitive ears of the pampered steeds in Harwick's stables.Defeat Wold marsh-flies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Céolstan awaits your return inside of the stable-gates in Harwick.

You should inform Céolstan that his horses may now sleep easier, for you have silenced the noise-makers that disturbed their rest.Talk to Céolstan at the Harwick StablesMarsh-flies infest the bogs around Harwick, making noise that disturbs the war-steeds. The eccentric guard of the stables spoils his steeds, and wishes for the noise to be silenced.'You were very good to silence the noise-makers, ${PLAYER}. The horses would surely thank you, if they could speak our tongue, but my thanks will have to do!'Mansig wants you to defeat some of the Orcs and Uruks that ambushed his men in the East Wall.Orcs and Uruks can be found throughout the East Wall.

Mansig has asked you to defeat Orcs and Uruks throughout the East Wall.Defeat Orcs and Uruks throughout the East Wall (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mansig is at his camp in the East Wall.

You have defeated many of the foes that attacked Mansig's party of Rohirrim and should now return to tell him of your success.Talk to Mansig'Look at the state of this camp, ${PLAYER}. My men had not been here for an hour when a band of screaming Orcs fell upon us. The walls were not yet up, and some of my warriors were away seeking firewood.

'The Men of Rohan are made of stern stuff indeed, as are their horses. We did not despair, though we were beset on all sides. My fighting men were able to keep the Orcs at bay until my other warriors returned. Then it became a rout.

'Many of the Orcs still lurk in the surrounding woods. If you are a friend to the Men of Rohan, I ask you to remedy this blight upon the East Wall. Wilferth tells me there are several types of Orc in this area, of different sizes, or types, or loyalties. Orc, Uruk...it is all the same to me. Remove them, ${PLAYER}, and I will count you a friend.''Very good! An enemy of the Orcs is a friend of mine, ${PLAYER}! There is more to be done, however.''The Orcs that attacked my men are still out there! Remedy this situation, ${PLAYER}, and I will count you a friend. You will find them throughout the East Wall.'The refugees of Langhold have built a makeshift camp outside the gate of Harwick.Salamanders are found just north of Harwick in the swamps along the road outside of the city.

You should collect glands from salamanders to help the refugees keep their fires lit for protection against the dark and cold.Collect glands from salamanders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cadda is at the refugee camp outside of Harwick, waiting to grow tall so that he may be a great warrior of Rohan.

You should bring the salamander glands to Cadda, but naturally keeping it a great secret, for the child wishes to take all the credit. How can you refuse?Bring Cadda the salamander glands (but do keep it a secret)'I may be small yet, but I am as stout as any warrior here! My mother tells me so. If they had let me fight in Langhold, perhaps we could have saved it from the brigand traitors....

'Well, now I will help defend this camp! Salamanders stalk about, and we scare them off, but then I recalled that Cíllan has trouble getting the fires started. Those salamanders have glands that let them spray fire from their mouths!

'I remember when I met you, ${PLAYER}... you were the only one brave enough to fight the salamanders on their own turf. Will you collect glands, and bring them directly to me? But... can you keep it a secret that you gathered them?''Oh! These are perfect! I am sure Cíllan will be happy when she finds out I got these for her, all by myself. I mean... with help, but mostly by myself.

'You will not tell anyone, ${PLAYER}... will you? Soon I will be big enough to fight salamanders on my own!'Aldor Harding of the Wold declared foreknowledge of his own death at the hands of the Khundolar, citing this as the reason he will not evacuate Harwick.Burnoth is at the refugee camp outside Harwick.

You should speak with Burnoth when you are ready to ride against the Easterlings.Talk to Burnoth to join the Riders Four in a raid against the KhundolarBurnoth is at the refugee camp outside Harwick.

You should speak with Burnoth regarding the battle against the Easterlings.Talk to Burnoth'${PLAYER}, it seems to me that we must prove the Aldor wrong, if we are to impress reason upon him. We must show him that a man can make his own fate!

'Not far from here, we came across an encampment of Easterlings. I am confident that we can overcome them, despite their greater number... if we can accomplish this, then perhaps Harding will see reason and know that he can change his fate.

'Hopefully, he will send his people to safety, though he remains to shore up his defences. Will you ride with us against the Khundolar? If so, let me know when you are ready, and we will depart.''Let us away then, and may Harding's fate change in the face of our fury!''Well done, ${PLAYER}! Our decisive victory should make it clear that the Khundolar are no match for a battle-readied éored! Aldor Harding will have no choice but to heed our warning.'Radwulf is concerned with providing for the horses of the camp and wants you to see to their needs of food and water.Radwulf gave you a water-skin to fill at the waterfall south-east of Mansig's encampment. He also wants you to search the ground between the camp and where you found the horses for their fallen saddlebags.Find fallen saddlebags in the East Wall (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Radwulf is at the Rohirrim camp in the East Wall.

You have found the fallen saddlebags and filled the water-skin given to you by Radwulf and should now return to the Rohirrim horse-master.'It is my duty to see to the needs of our horses. It is a task I relish, for I know its importance: the success of our mission depends in no small part on them!

'When Langmanu and Swithlic fled from the Orcs, their saddlebags must have fallen from their backs; they did not return with them. I kept much of their feed in those bags. Search the woods between this camp and where you found my friends, south-east of here, for their fallen saddlebags.

'Food is not enough. They will need water as well, but I do not trust the streams here; too many Orc-boots muddying the waters. You saw a waterfall on your way to rescuing my friends, I know, but there are a lot of dead Orcs lying in that stream. If you follow the river upstream, though, where the water flows from much higher ground, perhaps you can get fresh water from the waterfall before it flows past those bodies. Take this waterskin and fill it at that waterfall. That way my friends will have fresh, clean water to drink.''When Langmanu and Swithlic fled from the Orcs, their saddlebags must have fallen from their backs; they did not return with them. I kept much of their feed in those bags. Search the woods between this camp and where you found my friends, south-east of here, for their fallen saddlebags.

'Food is not enough. They will need water as well, but I do not trust the streams here; too many Orc-boots muddying the waters. You saw a waterfall on your way to rescuing my friends, I know, but there are a lot of dead Orcs lying in that stream. If you follow the river upstream, though, where the water flows from much higher ground, perhaps you can get fresh water from the waterfall before it flows past those bodies. Take this water-skin and fill it at that waterfall. That way my friends will have fresh, clean water to drink.''You have done well! I can now provide food and water for the horses under my care, for a time. I thank you. You have been a great help not only to me, but to Langmanu, Swithlic, and the other men and horses as well.'The children of Langhold are playing a violent game of Brigand-bashing, and have asked you to join them.The children of Langhold are at the farm near the town.Get the bat from HeburgaUse the bat to whomp the "brigands" at the farm.Use the bat to whomp the "brigands" (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Heburga to see if she concedes.Talk to Heburga and see if she concedesThe children are now the Rohirrim, and you're the brigand.

