'You should ride to the tower on Anduin's edge and see what they have to report. We are safe... for now.''Easterlings are upon us! They have gathered at the ford, and begin to swarm the river-banks on this side. Their numbers are still small -- I believe we still have a chance to fend them off.'The Stinging Bog is a swampy area near Harwick, and the flies that nest there annoy all who venture too near. The stings of those creatures can be violently painful.The Stinging Bog is on the far side of the road from Feldburg.

You should kill marsh-flies in the Stinging Bog.Defeat marsh-flies in the Stinging Bog (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Captain Agelmund awaits you at Feldburg.

You should return to Captain Agelmund now that you have done as he asked of you.Return to Captain Agelmund'You wish to speak with the refugees of Langhold, you say? That is for the soldiers of Harwick to deal with, not some stranger from a distant land. I suggest you mind me, lest Reeve Harding come down on you even harder, and toss you in the dungeons of Harwick.

'The Stinging Bog lies in the midst of our most travelled road in the Wold, and the marsh-flies there look upon merchants, soldiers, and farmers as a feast.

'Let us test your mettle with a small task, eh, ${PLAYER}? Kill some flies, and I may put in a good word for you to Harding.''I am glad you managed to thin the horde of marsh-flies in the Stinging Bog. I always dread going through that part of the road, myself.

'I can mark these reports as closed, and for that, I thank you. You are perhaps not so bad of a ${CLASS} as we originally suspected.'Utred, the Thane of Langhold, has invited you to eat and drink in his hall, a mark of welcome to guests of his city.Cíllan, wife of the Thane, can be found in the Mead Hall of Langhold.

You should speak with Cíllan to receive a formal Rohirrim welcome.Hear out Cíllan's gesture of welcomeYou have been offered food and drink by the kind wife of Thane Utred.

You should pledge your goodwill to the folk of Langhold by partaking of their mead and bread.Drink the mead of LangholdEat the bread of LangholdCíllan stands in the Mead Hall of Langhold.

You should <rgb=#FF0000>bow</rgb> to Cíllan to thank her for her kindness and her greeting.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest requires emotes. You must select the intended target and perform the emote.</rgb><rgb=#FF0000>Bow</rgb> to Cíllan to thank her for her hospitalityThane Utred is in the Mead Hall of Langhold.

You should return to the Thane now that you have received the proper welcome of his house.Return to Thane UtredNow that you have received the welcome of Thane Utred, you should venture out into Langhold to find something to do.

Surely some of the townsfolk or guards could use some help from a seasoned traveller such as yourself.Now that you are a guest, find a task or errand in Langhold'My wife, Cíllan, has a better temperament for welcoming guests to Langhold than I. I would sooner bid you to go find your own food, but my dear wife upholds the customs of Rohan. She has grace, despite agreeing to tolerate my bad behaviour for so many long years!

'Cíllan will give you the welcome you deserve as an honoured guest in our city, ${PLAYER}.''Ah, the traveller from Stangard! You must be weary from your long road. Do not mind my husband -- he is an unceremonious man, but he is a good Thane, and still strong.

'Come! Let us drink together, and share a meal. This custom is a pledge of honour from us that you will be safe from all harm in Langhold. It is a pledge on your part also: while here, you must respect our ways and cause no harm, and stir no trouble.

'Will you perform this gesture of goodwill?''Ah, the traveller from the western-lands. Welcome -- I am a city guardsman, but I am a little busy at the moment....''You have my wife's approval, eh? Do you? Good, good. She is a gentle soul, and she loves the old customs. I suppose there is no harm in them, especially if they make our guests behave themselves while they stay here.

'Now, you are free to roam about and do as you please. Do not let me keep you.''Don't look to me for any more welcome than "hello!" I am an abominably rude man. Find Cíllan -- go on, now.'You have received a summons to go to Rohan, and the first city you encountered here was Langhold.Thane Utred, leader of the northernmost town of the Wold, is in the Mead Hall of Langhold.

You should speak with Thane Utred at once to receive a proper welcome to the town.Receive the formal welcome of the Thane'We have had word from our kindred in the Great River of a heroic traveller who passed through Stangard. I can see that you are this same ${RACE} of whom such praise was spoken.

'The Thane of Langhold would receive you in the Mead Hall without delay. This part of the Wold welcomes guests with open arms, but enemies of the Mark beware! It is my duty to warn you thus.''You should be able to find the Mead Hall without getting too lost. It is only the largest structure in Langhold.''Ah... the famous wandering ${CLASS}! Your reputation precedes you, ${PLAYER}. I bid you welcome to Langhold. I am sure you will find plenty to keep you busy here, for we are often beset by ruffians and wild creatures.'Fang Point is a large Warg den, and the pack that lives there has been ravaging the road that runs through the centre of the Wold.Wargs can be found in and around Fang Point in the Wold.

You should search Fang Point for the Wargs that prowl the roads, making travel dangerous for all who venture into the Wold.Kill Wargs at Fang Point (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated a number of vicious Wargs at Fang Point, making the roads safer for all who travel in the Wold between Harwick and the Norcrofts.CompletedFang Point is a large Warg den, and the pack that lives there has been ravaging the road that runs through the centre of the Wold.You have defeated a number of vicious Wargs at Fang Point, making the roads safer for all who travel in the Wold between Harwick and the Norcrofts.Upon wandering into the den of salamanders in the northern Wold, a great fear has come over you. Something terrible lurks here.A cruel old salamander by the name of Venomtongue has roamed these ruins for far too long.

You should defeat Venomtongue, for he has been allowed to endure for too many long years.Defeat Venomtongue, eldest of the great salamandersA cruel old salamander by the name of Venomtongue has roamed these ruins for far too long.

You should defeat Venomtongue, for he has been allowed to endure for too many long years.Defeat Venomtongue, eldest of the great salamandersYou have defeated one of the most vicious salamanders in the Wold, old Venomtongue of the Seething Mire.CompletedUpon wandering into the den of salamanders in the northern Wold, a great fear has come over you. Something terrible lurks here.You have defeated one of the most vicious salamanders in the Wold, old Venomtongue of the Seething Mire.War is certainly at Langhold's door, and Thane Utred must choose whether to defend his city, or ensure the safety of those who serve him.Thane Utred must decide whether to defend or evacuate Langhold.Help Thane Utred make his decisionDala stands at Tunbert's farm near Langhold.

You should speak with Dala.Speak with Dala'Well, the decision is before me. Do we evacuate, and flee before those we exiled, simply because they have a new master? Or do we protect Langhold, the northern keep of the Riddermark?''It is over, ${PLAYER}. Langhold is naught but smouldering wood and ashes now. I will make for Harwick to see to the women and children there, and give aid to the survivors, for that is my duty.''Let us think on this matter together, ${PLAYER}! By your account, there isn't much time.'In your last visit with Harding, he was not sure he could trust you, and was frustrated at the timing of your meeting. Since then you have done a service to Harwick and should have a more favourable meeting this time around.Captain Agelmund is eager to take you for another meeting with the Thane of Harwick, Reeve of the Wold and Aldor of the Eastemnet.

You should speak with Captain Agelmund to travel to Harwick for a meeting with Harding.Meet Cíllan at the Mead Hall for an audience with HardingHarding has called a meeting in the Mead Hall of Harwick, and awaits your arrival.Talk to Harding'Let us end this nonsense now! I have had enough of Harding's mistrust of you, ${PLAYER}, when you have given us everything.

'Come, I will insist that a council be called. Go to the Mead Hall, and I will meet you there. If Harding permits this council, the guard will see you in.

'It is time to set things right!''You have helped make Harwick's surrounding territory safer for its people. For that, you have my thanks. I think Harwick will be able to hold its own for now.''Come with me, ${PLAYER}. No one will dare arrest you if I am by your side. I will request an audience with Harding immediately.'The Stinging Bog is home to a number of marsh-flies that carry upon them trophies -- insect-wings that are valuable to merchants and townsfolk.Wings can be collected form marsh-flies that inhabit the Stinging Bog, on the far side of the road from Feldburg.

You should collect marsh-fly wings from the Stinging Bog's large pests.Kill a marsh-flyWings can be collected form marsh-flies that inhabit the Stinging Bog, on the far side of the road from Feldburg.

You should collect marsh-fly wings from the Stinging Bog's large pests.Collect marsh-fly wings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Captain Agelmund is at Feldburg, awaiting your return.

You should bring the wings you gathered to Captain Agelmund.The Stinging Bog is home to a number of marsh-flies that carry upon them trophies -- insect-wings that are valuable to merchants and townsfolk.'Ah! What have we here? I see that you collected several perfectly intact wings of marsh-flies from that horrible bog. These are actually quite valuable, for they have beauty for those with an eye for such things; the merchants can make much of them.

'Few trappers are skilled enough to wrangle these wings from the flies without damaging them. A trophy well-earned!'Hestil wants to make a plan for rescuing her party's guardian, but first she needs her friends to prepare for the task.Cúfaron and Ramdir are on Parth Galen. Isteron and Maedal are somewhere in the East Wall.

Hestil has provided you quite the list of tasks that need to be accomplished before their guardian can be rescued from the Orcs. Help Cúfaron, Ramdir, Isteron, and Maedal, wherever they may be.Complete all quests for CúfaronComplete all quests for RamdirComplete all quests for IsteronComplete all quests for MaedalHestil is at her camp near Parth Galen.

You have aided many members of her band and should now return to Hestil to plan the rescue of Drafli Broadshield.Talk to Hestil'Drafli Broadshield has been a loyal companion and true through many dangerous days... the very best guardian I have ever known. He has saved the lives of every member of this band many times over. Well, except for Ramdir, but he joined us only recently. And perhaps Maedal, who is able to fade into the background whenever danger threatens. And Rodwen generally can take care of herself. But he has saved some of us from time to time!

'Anyway, we cannot leave Drafli to the torment of the Orcs that have imprisoned him! We need to organize a rescue, but first I need everyone to be ready for such a dangerous task. Help Cúfaron ready a new path for us to take, convince Ramdir that we are safe for now, find Isteron and get his mind back where it belongs, and... and... what am I forgetting?

'Aye! Our burglar! Find Maedal and help her come up with a plan to save Drafli! That is a lot to accomplish, but we will be in your debt, ${PLAYER}.''We need to organize the rescue of Drafli Broadshield, but first I need everyone to be ready for such a dangerous task. Help Cúfaron ready a new path for us to take, convince Ramdir that we are safe for now, find Isteron and get his mind back where it belongs, and find Maedal and help her come up with a plan to save Drafli!''Hestil wants you to help get everyone ready to rescue Drafli? Well, it is a good thought... but this is a difficult company to keep focused, ${PLAYER}.

'I wish you luck! I hope this does not take too long!''What is taking them so long? There is a dwarf to be rescued here!''What is taking them so long? There is a dwarf to be rescued here!''Everyone is happy? That is... unusual. But perhaps now they will be focused enough for us to see to the rescue of our guardian!'Ramdir told you how he came to journey with Hestil's group of adventurers-for-hire and wants your help to ensure their safety.The Tindrock is the large rock located in the water at the top of the Falls of Rauros.

Ramdir asked you to swim out to the Tindrock, looking for signs of an Orc-presence seeking a foothold on the supposedly unclimbable rock.Swim out to the Tindrock, looking for signs of Orc presenceRamdir is at Hestil's camp on Parth Galen.

During your swim out to the Tindrock, you found evidence that the Orcs will be unable to gain a foothold on the steep rock rising above Rauros.Talk to Ramdir'Well-met, friend. You are a ${CLASS}, unless I miss my guess? I thought so. It is a pleasure to meet you. My name is Ramdir, and I am a Warden of Minas Ithil. We seek to keep alive the memory of the Tower of the Moon and the virtues that it represents. There are few of us left in these dark days, but that makes our charge all the more important.

'I have travelled with this band of adventurers ever since I met them at Cair Andros, to the south. Our goals are not precisely aligned, but I could not make the journey north on my own, and they professed a need for another protector on the road. I did not know how badly they needed it: they have not done the first thing for ensuring their safety at this camp! It is common cause enough, for now.

'Do you see the great rock out there in the water? It is called Tol Brandir by the Elves, but to Men of my kind it is the Tindrock. It is said to be unclimbable, but I worry that an Orc-archer could do some hurt if he managed to make it a perch. Swim out to the Tindrock and circle it once, looking for sign of an Orc-presence. It would make me feel more secure, friend.'The water-logged body of an Orc floats here. Judging from the state of the corpse and its position lodged up against the steep cliff, this Orc sought to climb the Tindrock but failed. If he did not hit the cliff during his rapid descent, the impact with the water was enough to finish his attempt.

You are confident it also put an end to any other Orc-designs on climbing the Tindrock. It seems as unclimbable as Ramdir hoped it would be.'You say that the Orcs have not managed to climb the Tindrock? That is good! It would be a devastating perch from which they could rain arrows upon my companions.

'These are good people, ${PLAYER}, even if they care more for glory and coin than for the ideals of the Wardens of Minas Ithil. However, they are not cautious enough by half.

'I will stand by them for as long as our paths are the same, but I tell you true: I will fear for their safety once we go our separate ways.''The Tindrock is supposed to be unclimbable, but if the Orcs could get a foothold atop it they could do some real hurt to us. Swim out to the Tindrock and look for any sign that the Orcs have tried to secure a foothold.'Isteron wants you to investigate the glowing flowers that you briefly encountered while scouting the side passages in the Under-wall.Glowing flowers can be found in the corners of the side passages in the Under-wall.

Isteron wants you to collect some of the glowing flowers in the Under-wall so he can study their properties.Collect glowing flowers in the side passages of the Under-wall (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Isteron is by the northernmost exit from the Under-wall.

You have collected glowing flowers from the corners of the side passages in the Under-wall.Bring the glowing flowers to Isteron'You mentioned glowing flowers in the corners of some of those side passages, ${PLAYER}? I am very interested in those. Do you think you could pick some of them for me and bring them back? They may have useful properties.'

Isteron's attention wanders as he contemplates something. After a moment he seems to notice you again.

'Glowing flowers...glowers. Glowers, ${PLAYER}! Glowers of the Under-wall! Get me some of these glowers, friend, so I can study them! You will find them in the corners of the side passages you scouted earlier.'You hand the glowing flowers to Isteron, and he seems disappointed.

'Oh. These are just glistershade...they are as common as the dirt in which they grow! Well, maybe not as common as that, but close to it. Their only special property is that they glow. They do absolutely nothing else. I suppose they are fine if you want to attract attention, but I have collected much more interesting things than these throughout my storied past.

'Thank you anyway, ${PLAYER}. But one thing: I have noticed that you are not using the name "Under-wall" for these tunnels, and I think you should. It is a good name, and I would like you to use it. You can also call these flowers "glowers" if you would like.''Glowers! That is brilliant. I cannot wait to tell my companions about this latest naming discovery of mine. Go find me glowers in the corners of the side passages here in the Under-wall!'Rodwen lost her sword in a battle with the Orcs and would love to see it again, but she thinks it very unlikely.Wilferth is at the Rohirrim-camp in the East Wall.

You remember returning a high-quality blade of Gondorian make to Wilferth from the Orc that possessed it. Perhaps this was Rodwen's blade.Find Rodwen's swordRodwen is at Hestil's camp, near Parth Galen in the East Wall.

You have recovered Rodwen's sword and should bring it to her.'Shortly after we arrived in the East Wall, I was set upon by Orcs and barely escaped with my life. There were perhaps a dozen of the creatures, and though I gave better than I got, there were simply too many. My greatest shame is not that I retreated, ${PLAYER}, but that I lost my sword as I did so. I have borrowed this old thing from Hestil, but it is not as good a sword as my own: a high-quality blade from Gondor.

'It is probably in the hands of some Orc now. If you could find it and return it to me, I would be indebted to you. I do not even know where to begin such a search, ${PLAYER}. There are so many Orcs throughout the East Wall. It could be any of them, if the Orc that took it even remains in this land.''You say that you have found the owner of the blade? Really? How do you know?'

You recount the tale told to you by Rodwen of her encounter with the Orcs and how she left her sword behind.

'Hmm... I suppose the blade does belong to her. She does not sound like much of a champion, if you ask for my opinion. One of the Rohirrim would rather die than leave his blade behind.'

Still grumbling, Wilferth hands you the sword to return to Rodwen.'I do not know where my blade is now, ${PLAYER}. It is probably in the hands of a filthy Orc. I would be indebted to you if you could return it to me.''Return that sword to your friend and tell her not to lose it again. She is right to be shamed by its loss.''You found my sword! Thank you, ${PLAYER}, it is good to hold it once again! Where did you find it?'

You tell Rodwen the story, and though you keep some of what Wilferth said to yourself, she is still able to piece together the tone of his comments.

'Was it shameful to leave my sword behind? Perhaps, but if everyone acted as this Rohirrim man insists he would do, there would be fewer Rohirrim in this land, that is certain. There is honour in staying alive, ${PLAYER}. A noble death may lead to a multitude of songs, but a hasty retreat may lead to more dead Orcs. Your Rohirrim friend might do well to remember that.

'Thank you for finding my sword. Now she and I have work to do!'Radwig has relented to his mother Etta's desire to defend Floodwend, but you are not bound to remain in the city.Wood can be found in the Warg-infested worksite east of Floodwend.

You should collect wood from the abandoned worksite.Gather wood from the abandoned worksite (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Radwig can be found in the Mead Hall of Floodwend.

You should return to Radwig with the wood you gathered from the Worksite.'While I abide my mother's wishes, you at least are free to march into battle. Are you the sort of warrior that would wish to ride out and defeat foes on their own turf?'

You tell the Thane that you have done such feats countless times, and would welcome the opportunity to rid his lands of the evil that lurks there.

'You will go, then? I admire your boldness, ${PLAYER}! I hope to hear many stories of your might upon your return. The Wargs are our biggest concern right now, for they hold our wood-stores hostage.''You succeeded! I am so impressed, ${PLAYER}! Your deeds inspire me; while we have sat here pounding nails and sharpening swords, you actually cleared the wood of that howling menace, and took back our supplies.

'It is amazing, what one ${CLASS} can do....'Zerlúk is telling you his account of what happened at the Dead Orc Glade.Zerlúk is in the Dead Orc Glade, in the middle of giving you his account of what happened there.Get Zerlúk to resume his accountZerlúk is in the Dead Orc Glade, in the middle of giving you his account of what happened there.Get Zerlúk to resume his account'This is the tale, ${RACE}, and may it...rot your innards slowly. If you are a friend to he who...decorated this glade, it will...bring you no comfort.'

Zerlúk bares his teeth in a grin.

'I was with Uglúk's company of Isengarders, and my orders...were to capture halflings. "Alive, unspoiled, and unsearched," it was. I asked no questions, and no one with sense would have either. Orders was orders.

'We found...two of the little worms! They bit, they did, and stabbed at us with sharp knives. Killed some of us, not far from here, but some of the boys picked them up...they struggled, but they had no strength. We made it...to this glade, when....'

Zerlúk's body is wracked by another fit of coughing.Once his coughing subsides, Zerlúk continues with his account.

'He came charging out of the trees, a man possessed, his sword...his sword whirling around him in a wide arc. We had to...had to fight. He killed many of us, and we had to retreat. We doubled back on him as he made his way down the hill, and in the company of... many archers...many archers....'

Zerlúk's laughter bubbles out of him noisily.

'He gave me this gash across my belly, and I fell here...I pretended to be dead. The warrior did not last much longer than that. He was still...alive...when his friends arrived...but....'

Zerlúk's breath has gone ragged, but he still has enough life to look up at you slyly.'This warrior had friends with him...but they arrived too late to save him. Another Man...an Elf...and a dwarf. They were preparing to carry the warrior away when....'

Zerlúk grins.

'When Uglúk's boys circled around and slew them all. The Elf loosed all of his arrows...see them all around...and the dwarf killed many, but it was no use. They joined their friend...in death.

'That is my tale...now leave me...to die. I am...content.'Harwick has received urgent pleas for help from Floodwend, and cannot spare anyone to ride there but you.Floodwend is south-east of Harwick, along the road.

You have agreed to ride to the defence of Floodwend.Talk to Goldlac at the entrance to FloodwendEasterlings are crossing into the Wold south of the rapids of Sarn Gebir, and you need to defeat them and gather their scattered belongings.Defeat Easterlings in the Wold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect salvaged belongings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Goldlac is at the entrance to Floodwend, in the Wold.

You have defeated Easterlings and collected their salvaged supplies, and should return now to Goldlac.Corudan is by the entrance to Floodwend.

Corudan wants to speak with you about the bow that Elfsige took from a dead Orc near Floodwend.Talk to Corudan by the entrance to Floodwend'The road leaves Harwick and runs south-east until it reaches Floodwend. The land becomes rockier the further south you go, so your steeds will need to be sure of foot to make it there safely. Stick to the road, if you can. I do not know what threat has so worried the raven-master of Floodwend, but it was grave enough for him to send three birds with the plea for help.

'He may have sent more. I do not know him personally; he could be excitable by nature, or the keep at Floodwend could be in worse peril than ever before! Go with haste to help! We have no men to spare, and if Floodwend falls Harwick could be next!''You have come from Harwick? The Thane was hoping for more men, but I suppose you will have to do. The Easterlings have been testing the Great River, trying to find safe places at which to ford in between the two undeeps. They have a large camp to the north-east, but our people have kept an eye on them and they seek another way to strike at us. Perhaps they thought just below the rapids would be safe, or perhaps they fear their commanders more than the rushing waters: a great many perished trying to cross just below Sarn Gebir.

'Not all, however, not all by half. Many of the Easterlings survived the crossing and have been marshalling their forces for an attack on Floodwend. Many of their belongings are scattered and damaged by the waters, but still they seek to recover enough to arm themselves to attack the city. We need you to strike them along the banks of the Anduin, just to the east, below the cliffs. You should also steal from them what belongings survived the crossing.''You heard what he said, ${PLAYER}? That is almost certainly the bow I lent to Fimloth! The Orc that claimed it carried it into the Wold, and now this man Elfsige has taken it!''Easterlings have been trying to find places at which to ford the Great River. They seek to attack Floodwend! Go east of Floodwend to seize their belongings that survived the crossing, and defeat any Easterlings you find there.''If your Elf is not careful, Alwold's man Elfsige is going to challenge him to an archery contest with the bow he took from that dead Orc. And if Elfsige is not careful, he's going to win! May your Elf have mercy!'Corudan looks at you silently. He raises an eyebrow at you, and there is a strange fire in his eyes.'If Mansig were here, he would ride with his whole company to the defence of Floodwend... but he is not here. He is still in the East Wall, investigating the Orc activity. If you can ride those war-steeds of yours with any skill or swiftness, you can come to Floodwend in time to help against whatever it is they face. Ride with haste south along the road to Floodwend!''Between your skill in combat and the harsh waters of the Great River, perhaps the Easterlings will decide attacking Floodwend is too much trouble. What times we live in! Even if that were so, we would still have to worry about attacks by raiding Orcs! Will Floodwend ever see peace again?

'While you were gone, a raiding party of Orcs passed near to Floodwend. Alwold and his men rode out to fight them, and had success: all of the Orcs were defeated. The Orcs must have recently fought with some Elves, for one of Alwold's men claimed quite a trophy from the body of one of the Orcs: a bow of quality Elf-make, with the finest detailing. He has been showing off since they returned. His name is Elfsige, and he'll likely challenge your Elf to an archery contest before too long. Poor, foolish fellow.'Elfsige claimed a high-quality bow from the body of an Orc he slew: Corudan's favourite bow, loaned to Fimloth and lost in Thinglad.Elfsige is in Floodwend, north of the Mead Hall.

You have heard that Elfsige has a bow that sounds like it belongs to Corudan, and should ask him to return it.Talk to Elfsige in FloodwendCorudan is in Floodwend, north of the Mead Hall.Talk to Corudan in FloodwendAlwold is on the road outside Floodwend.

Elfsige has agreed to race you to Elthengels, with the winner of the race taking all claim to Corudan's bow. His commander, Alwold, will start the race.Talk to Alwold outside Floodwend'This will not take long. Let us have some words with this man Elfsige. He is sure to return my bow when he understands the situation.'You explain the terms of Elfsige's wager, and Alwold chuckles.

'I am not surprised. Times have been tough in the Wold of late, with Orcs and Easterlings and worse prowling around the borders. When my men are not fighting for their lives, they're engaging in fruitless wagers. I cannot stop them. As long as they, and you, abide by the agreed-upon terms, I have no complaint. But I do not want any bloodshed! If you go back on your word, you'll have me to deal with!''I am sorry that your friend lost his bow, but it seems to me that anything taken from an Orc belongs to the one strong enough to take it. If your friend wanted it back, why did he not reclaim it from the Orcs when he had the opportunity?

'I am not unreasonable. What if I give your friend the opportunity to win it back in a contest of some sort? Alwold has given me a message to deliver to Elthengels in the Norcrofts. How about this? A test of your mounted skill: whichever of us first reaches Elthengels gets to keep the bow. If I arrive there first, your friend gives up all claim to it. Does he agree to this proposal? If he does, let us speak with my commander, Alwold, outside Floodwend. He will start the race.''This is a ridiculous requirement. Why should I have to engage in a competition to determine my right to possess property that is rightfully mine? If these are the laws of Men I am grateful to be one of the Galadhrim! But I am not worried, ${PLAYER}. We will best this arrogant sapling that thinks it is a weathered oak, and my favourite bow will once again be in my possession.

'Let us speak with this Alwold outside Floodwend. The sooner this unnecessary race is begun, the sooner we will win it!''I will give your friend a chance to win this bow, but only one chance. I am not an unreasonable man! This is more than generous!''Speak to my commander, Alwold, outside Floodwend. He will begin the race!''Surely Elfsige will see reason. That is clearly an Elf-made bow, and if Corudan says it belongs to him...'

Horn's brow creases in thought.

'Of course, Elfsige did come into possession of the bow by slaying an Orc in defence of his town. He may not wish to part with it solely on the word of an Elf.''I do not like this. Elfsige will be very, very fast while mounted. He will have trained on horseback for his entire life. It will not be easy to win this wager!''That is the bow Corudan lost? No wonder he wanted it back! How many Orcs did the two of us slay in Thinglad, searching for it? And now this arrogant horse-man thinks slaying a single Orc entitles him to keep it for himself?''Is Corudan really going to accept it if we do not win this wager? Will he be willing to give up his claim to his favourite bow?''That is my bow. I would know it anywhere.''I am not worried. We will win this ridiculous wager. Let us go to speak with Elfsige's commander, Alwold. He is outside the city.'The race is on! The first person to reach the town of Elthengels, in the Norcrofts, wins claim to Corudan's bow.Elthengels is south-west of Floodwend, and you do not have much time in which to reach it before Elfsige does!Time before Elfsige arrives at Elthengels:Reach the entrance to Elthengels before Elfsige doesYou have arrived at Elthengels, but there is no sign of Elfsige.

