Maedal has given you a wooden figurine and a message to give to Drafli to help him through his imprisonment.Orcs and Uruks can be found all over Amon Hen, in the East Wall.

Drafli Broadshield wants you to defeat Orcs and Uruks on Amon Hen until you find the other wooden figurine that Maedal was talking about.Defeat Orcs on Amon Hen until you find the other wooden figurineOrcs and Uruks can be found all over Amon Hen, in the East Wall.

Drafli Broadshield wants you to defeat Orcs and Uruks on Amon Hen until you find the other wooden figurine that Maedal was talking about.Defeat Orcs on Amon Hen until you find the other wooden figurineDrafli Broadshield is in a cage on Amon Hen, in the East Wall.

You were unable to find any evidence of the existence of another wooden figurine and should return to Drafli.Maedal is on the slope of Amon Hen, in the East Wall.

Dafli told you to return to Maedal and let her know that no other figurine could be found.'Did you see the cage where those Orcs are keeping Drafli imprisoned, ${PLAYER}? It is further up the hill. Just follow the cliff wall south and east from this position until you see the Orc-camp. You can follow the large spiked posts, like these two here, until you find it. They lead right to the camp. I have something I want you to give to Drafli.'

Maedal removes a small, wooden figurine from the recesses of her clothing and presents it to you. It is a carving of a heroic figure holding aloft a sword in one hand, wearing a round shield on his other arm.

'Hand this to him through the bars and speak these words to him: "Get it back, and you will never know fear again." He will understand the message. Repeat it back to me so I know you have the words just as I told them to you.'

You speak the words back to her and Maedal smiles.

'Very good, ${PLAYER}! This figurine and the message will help Drafli through his imprisonment. I am sure of it!''Do you remember the message, ${PLAYER}? Hand that wooden figurine to Drafli through the bars and tell him "Get it back, and you will never know fear again." The message and the carving will help him through his imprisonment while we prepare our rescue attempt.

'To get to the Orc-camp, follow this cliff south and east until you see its wooden walls and the smoke from its fires. Spiked posts like these two lead the way to the camp.''Are you here to get me out of this cage? No? Then what good are you, ${RACE}?'

Drafli's annoyed expression changes into one of puzzled confusion as you pass him the wooden figure that Maedal asked you to give him, and you speak the message she gave. He looks at you in silence, then down at the figure in his hand and back up at you.

'What in the name of Durin the Deathless am I supposed to do with this? And what is Maedal talking about? I have to get it back? What?'

Thunderclouds cross his face as Drafli thinks. At last he seems to come up with something.

'I think there may have been two of these wooden figures. Maedal wants me to come back with the other one, I suppose? Maybe she's waiting to spring me from this cage until she gets her other carving back? That is probably it. She's a strange one. I have never understood burglars. Slay Orcs on this hill until you find this figure's companion! Bring it back to me when you do! I am ready to be out of this cage!''You gave Drafli my message and the wooden figure? Good, good! I saw you tangling with the Orcs. Keep it up! Every dead Orc decreases the length of time our guardian must remain in captivity!''Keep slaying those Orcs, ${PLAYER}! One of them must have the other figure Maedal wants us to get back. I am ready to be done with this cage!''You gave Drafli my message and the wooden figure? Good, good! I saw you tangling with the Orcs. Keep it up! Every dead Orc decreases the length of time our guardian must remain in captivity!

'To get to the Orc-camp, follow this cliff south and east until you see its wooden walls and the smoke from its fires.''Keep slaying those Orcs, ${PLAYER}! One of them must have the other figure Maedal wants us to get back. I am ready to be done with this cage!''You gave Drafli my message and the wooden figure? Good, good! I saw you tangling with the Orcs. Keep it up! Every dead Orc decreases the length of time our guardian must remain in captivity!

'To get to the Orc-camp, follow this cliff south and east until you see its wooden walls and the smoke from its fires.''What? No sign of the other wooden figurine? What was Maedal talking about? This is horrible! Cut my beard and call me an Elf! I will never get out of this cage!'

Drafli is now more dejected than he was before. He looks at the figurine you still hold, and its heroic pose seems to sink him further into despair.

'Look at that little hero, ${PLAYER}. He would not let himself be put into this situation! Oh, it's useless. Go back and tell Maedal we were not able to get back her other figurine, and I am going to stay in this cage forever.''It is useless! I do not deserve to be called a guardian or a dwarf! Bring that figurine back to Maedal and tell her I will simply stay here forever! This cage is my home now.'You hand the figurine back to Maedal and tell her what transpired.

'What? Oh, that is not what I wanted at all, ${PLAYER}! He was supposed to look at the heroic figure and remember how important he is to our companions! He was supposed to be reminded of his own heroic deeds! I just wanted him to gain back his confidence!'

Maedal looks glum.

'Some captain I turned out to be. I cannot inspire people the way Hestil does, or even the way that Warden of Minas Ithil seems able to inspire folk. Oh, yes, I am a captain at heart. I only began to study the art of burglary recently, but sadly it seems that I am better-suited to being a burglar than a captain.

'Thank you for trying, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps I will redeem myself when we are finally able to free Drafli. I guess this is enough for now.'Runald and his family have prepared their waggon, said their farewells, and are ready to begin their dangerous journey south to Edoras.Runald and his family await at the southern entrance to the village of Elthengels.

Runald has asked you to help escort his family safely along the first section of their journey west.Help escort Runald's family to safetyOfrith can be found within the trader's outpost, west along the road from Elthengels.

Runald has made it safely to the trader's outpost and has taken the road south. He has asked you to give his thanks to Ofrith for his support.Thank Ofrith at the trader's outpost for his help'We are at last ready to begin the dangerous march to Edoras. We have been warned that this first step of our journey may be the most dangerous to take, as those heading south from town have frequently been ambushed and assaulted by Orcs.

'You have been a welcome friend to us, ${PLAYER}, might I make one final request? Safe-guard us on this first leg of our journey, so that we may yet reach our destination without incident.''We are ready to depart whenever you are. Please keep the waggon safe... we will surely be a target for the Orcs as we take this first section of road.''There is no need to thank us. It is our pleasure to help those in need. If we cannot depend upon each other for aid, we may as well let the Orcs take us and be finished with it.

'I see that you, too, understand this. Take this for your trouble... I believe that good deeds are deserving of merit.'The trader's outpost provides a valuable service to travellers and refugees alike, but desperation has turned some of the merchants towards greed.The traders and merchants reside at the trader's outpost along the road west from Elthengels.

Ofrith has asked you to speak with the other merchants and see that their practices remain fair to both seller and buyer.Convince the first merchant to maintain fair pricesConvince the second merchant to maintain fair pricesConvince the merchant Léodrim to maintain fair pricesOfrith can be found at the trader's outpost, west of the village of Elthengels.

Ofrith awaits word of the merchants' agreement to demand only fair prices of travellers and refugees.Talk to Ofrith at the merchant-camp'Welcome to our way-post. Our place here is a beneficial one for all. We help those on the road fleeing to Snowbourn and Edoras by providing them with needed goods. In exchange, we receive enough coin to pay hired guards to keep our gathering safe... and on occasion, to help us move out of the path of the larger Orc-forces as they pass through.

'Yet, some here have begun to take advantage of these dangerous times and the desperation of some travellers by unjustly raising the costs of their wares. Would you discuss this with the other merchants? Convince them to be fair about the prices of their goods. After all, both merchant and traveller bow to the same king.''Ofrith believes we should not raise the prices of our goods? On what merit? Do I not deserve more coin for providing this service when I must risk my life to do so?

'Ah well. Perhaps Ofrith is right. Our honour to each other is more important than a few petty coins. I have never known Ofrith to be wrong about such matters, so I will agree to keep my prices fair.''Ofrith noticed that I raised some prices? Sharp as a knife, he is. I assure you it was not out of greed, but out of necessity.

'You have seen the guards patrolling here? Know that they are paid well for protecting us. But as times grow more perilous, we will need more coin to convince them to remain with us.

'I was hoping to be ready for this problem by earning more now, but I will lower my prices again if Ofrith believes it is the proper action.''Thank you for speaking with the other merchants and for listening to their concerns. I believe that it will be easier to sell goods to travellers in need, when they know they are getting a fair bargain.'You have come across a locket, that appears to be some sort of keepsake. You recognize Cyneswith's name etched in the locket. Perhaps it would be worth asking him about it.Cyneswith awaits at the farmer's estate to the east, with his son Sager.

Cyneswith may be able to tell you more about the locket you found, engraved with his name.Show the locket to CyneswithThis dull golden locket seems to have been forgotten by the house's previous inhabitants. On each side of the pendant you are able to distinguish a faintly etched name. The names read, Matfréa and Cyneswith.

This locket may have once belonged to Cyneswith. Perhaps it would be worth bringing it to him, to find out more about its history.'This locket once belonged to my wife. I gave this to Matfréa as a gift, a symbol of our love for one another. With great sorrow I recall that she was lost to us, when the first Orc attacks began in earnest. 

'After fleeing our home, I thought, perhaps, to never have something to remember her with. Thank you for bringing this to me, ${PLAYER}. It is a memento I shall treasure.'Cyneswith and his son were attacked by the invading Orcs and have found themselves separated. Cyneswith desperately needs to rescue his son before the Orcs can find him.The Orc with the cellar key is likely to be found inside of the estate that Cyneswith hides behind.

Cyneswith has asked you to search for the cellar key among the Orcs pillaging within the estate.Search the Orcs within the estate for the cellar keyCyneswith rests behind the estate, near the cellar door.

You have found the cellar door's key and should return it to Cyneswith.Return to Cyneswith near the cellar entranceThe cellar door is located behind the estate, where Cyneswith waits.

With the cellar door unlocked, you may now enter to search for Sager.Enter the cellar of the farmhouseYou should search for Sager within the cellar of the estate.

You have entered the cellar of the estate and may begin searching for Sager, hopefully before the Orcs discover him.Find Cyneswith's son, Sager, in the cellar of the farmhouseCyneswith and Sager await your return on the outside of the estate.

Sager has returned to his father's side. Cyneswith will be eager to see that you have rescued his son.Return to Cyneswith outside the farmhouse'My name is Cyneswith. I live further to the west with my son. The vile Orcs... they came upon us as we slept! We ran east towards Elthengels, while the Orcs pursued us with haste. When we reached this estate, I convinced my son to play a hiding game with me, and he hid within the cellar. I managed to lure many of the Orcs away, but was struck with an arrow and was forced to hide.

'The Orcs must know that my son is inside this home! They have gone to search for him and have locked the cellar doors. You must find the key and rescue him!

'I noticed the fattest of the Orc-fiends carrying the key, and we cannot enter without it. The front door may still be open, but be prepared for a fight if you use it. Hurry, I fear that the Orcs will find my boy soon!''Aye, that looks to be the key! I hear the Orcs searching for my son, Sager, within the cellar.

'I cannot move enough to be of help to you... you must enter without me. Be most careful... and please prevent them from harming my boy!''Are they gone now? Was I well-hidden?

'Sometimes, Father and I play games like this. He says I am the very best at hiding! He told me to find a hiding place down here, and then those horrible things came looking for me.

'I like not this scary game! I am going back up to see Father now. Farewell!''Oh, I am so relieved that Sager is safe. It is encouraging to think that his ability to remain calm and stay unseen is in part due to the games of hiding we have played together.

'Thank you for returning my son, he means the world to me. I am most fortunate to have met someone so courageous.'Ofrith wants the merchants at the trader's outpost to agree to reasonable prices for their wares, but Léodrim cannot lower his asking prices without the supplies to manufacture more goods.Greyhide wolves can be found along the stream of water leading to the woods, south-east of the trader's outpost.

Léodrim has agreed to adjust his prices, as long as you can procure him hides from the Greyhide wolves, so that he might manufacture more goods.Collect wolf-pelts from Greyhide wolves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Léodrim awaits your return at the trader's outpost along the road west of Elthengels.

You have secured Greyhide wolf-pelts for the trader Léodrim, so that he may lower the prices on his goods.Return the Greyhide-pelts to Léodrim'I am aware that my prices are too high, but my hands are tied on this matter. In order to produce these items, I require a supply of wolf-hides.

'With the recent difficulties in trading and travelling, I have been unable to secure the materials with which to work. If you could secure me a supply of pelts from the nearby Greyhide wolves, I would gladly lower my prices.''These pelts will be perfect for my work. Thank you. I shall begin treating and curing them immediately.

'With this increased stock of materials to work with, you can assure Ofrith that my prices will be much more reasonable.'A Rohirrim scout approached Cyneswith looking for someone clever and strong to aid him. Cyneswith believes it would be best for someone in better condition than himself to help this man.The Rohirrim Scout was seen heading south from the estate, across the river.

Cyneswith recommends speaking to the Rohirrim scout and inquiring as to what sort of aid he is seeking.Talk to the Rohirrim scout looking for assistance'I believe it is time for us to part. The pain of my wound regresses, and I have gathered enough strength to walk for some distance. We will not remain safe here with the Orcs about, and I must find better shelter to protect my son. 

'Before we depart, it is worth noting that a Rohirrim scout from Cliving passed this way not long ago. He travelled south, searching for someone both savvy and skilled to help him with an important task. He requested my aid, but I explained that I would be more a hinderance than help to him.

'You have both the strength and courage to help others, perhaps you would be a more capable assistant to him. May fortune follow you, friend. I can never truly repay what you have done for us.''A hearty greeting to you, stranger! I have some rather important tasks that need carrying out, and I am in need of a savvy and skilled assistant. Before you decide, just know that it will be favourable for us both to work together.'Orc-ambushers have frequently attacked travellers along the road west from Elthengels. Guthléod, a trader, hopes that confronting the ambushers directly might help to reduce the number of attacks along the roads.Orc-ambushers have been frequently seen south of the road leading west from Elthengels.

Guthléod has asked you to assault the ambushers directly in the hopes of preventing more of their attacks on unwary travellers.Defeat Orc-ambushers south of the road to Elthengels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Guthléod can be found at his tent at the trader's outpost, west of the village of Elthengels.

You have defeated many of the Orc-ambushers and should inform Guthléod that his request has been fulfilled.Return to Guthléod at the merchants' camp'You did well to make it to our encampment alive. The roads grow more dangerous with each passing day, and Théoden King's response is to send fewer Riders to protect them!

'It is of no surprise that fewer travellers have been attempting to reach our camp, and those who have dared it have been frequently ambushed by Orcs along the road to the east. If you might deal with these treacherous ambushers, we would all be in your debt.''You gave those Orcs just what they deserved. Well done! This should make things much easier on those who take the road from the east to our camp.'Baldthorne, the blacksmith, requires help filling the rather large orders he has undertaken for weapons and armour.Orcs can be found scavenging in the fields around Elthengels.

Baldthorne has asked you to recover any Rohirrim weapons or armour-pieces from the Orcs, to help him supply the guards and garrison.Reclaim Rohirrim weapons from the Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Reclaim Rohirrim armour-pieces from the Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Baldthorne is awaiting the reclaimed weapons and armour at his smith in Elthengels.

You have reclaimed the weapons and armour pieces, once taken by the Orcs from Rohirrim.Return the weapons and armour-pieces to Baldthorne'Helmets, spears, shields, pauldrons, greaves... I will never finish these orders! Elthengel's guards and its garrison have both placed urgent orders for a great deal of weapons and armour pieces. Never has my skill or speed with a smithing hammer been questioned, but this task is too great even for me!

'I have a plan that may resolve this issue, if you would be willing to aid me. I have heard that some of the Orcs besieging the Norcrofts are known to collect swords and armour pieces from our Riders as trophies of their kills. If some of these items were reclaimed, I could include them in the shipments to the garrison. What say you? Are you up to this task?''These pieces of steel are still in fine condition! May they serve their new owners better than their last. To think the Orcs would claim these as trophies from the dead angers me.

'These weapons belong in the hands of our people... thank you for helping me put them there. Thanks to you, I will have the orders ready soon.'White Hand Orcs have taken to occupying and desecrating the once-peaceful farmsteads of the noble crofters of Cliving.The abandoned farmsteads can be found south of Cliving, west of the road.

Each farmstead has been occupied by a single White Hand pillager, who has recruited others to help defile the lands.Defeat the White Hand pillagers at each farmsteadThe abandoned farmsteads can be found south of Cliving, west of the road.

Each farmstead has been occupied by a single White Hand pillager, who has recruited others to help defile the lands.Captain Garmund can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Garmund will be most appreciative of your efforts to remove the White Hand Orcs from the occupied farmhouses.Talk to Captain Garmund outside of ClivingWhite Hand Orcs have taken to occupying and desecrating the once-peaceful farmsteads of the noble crofters of Cliving.

The strongest among the Orc-infantry have taken to claiming each structure as their own personal habitat.'These pillagers are a grim indication of what the Orcs intend to accomplish. They have defiled the deserted homes of the crofters; it is a fine deed you have done in removing their presence.'Léofcetel has informed Reeve Athelward of Captain Garmund's disobedience, and Athelward must respond with fair judgement of each man.Léofcetel can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Captain Garmund wishes to have Léofcetel rejoin his men and to set aside their past differences.Talk to LéofcetelReeve Athelward resides within his Mead Hall, at the eastern edge of Cliving.

Reeve Athelward has summoned you before him to discuss the matters of Garmund and Léofcetel.Talk to Reeve Athelward at the Mead HallLéofcetel can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Reeve Athelward has judged Léofcetel worthy of the title of Captain of the Guard, receiving the role from his rival Garmund.Inform Léofcetel of his promotion to Captain of the GuardGarmund can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Athelward has judged Garmund to be a valiant protector and has granted him the duty of becoming the Reeve's personal safe-guard.Inform Garmund of his new role as Athelward's personal guardian'I am rather surprised to find Léofcetel awaiting our arrival. He has made it known that he wishes to speak with you, perhaps he has had a change of heart regarding our purpose.

'You may inform him that I hold no grudge against him for his refusal. If he wishes to assist us once more, we are more than willing to accept his return.''Rejoin Garmund? Am I taken for a fool? I have returned to deliver a formal summons by Reeve Athelward. He requests your presence at the Mead Hall, at once.

'Having disclosed the details of Garmund's betrayal, the Reeve seeks a neutral party to discuss a resolution. I hardly consider you neutral, but nonetheless, you have been chosen to discuss the matters with the Reeve.

'Do not let Garmund's arrogance go unpunished.''Thank you for heeding my summons, ${PLAYER}. Léofcetel has told that you have been assisting Captain Garmund and his riders. Please share with me the events that you have witnessed, and your opinion of Garmund's intentions.'

You describe the raid against the Orcs led by Garmund, his respect for Reeve Athelward, and his desire to protect his homeland from the invaders. Athelward listens, nods, and thinks for a moment before speaking.

'I hear truth in the words you speak, ${PLAYER}, and I will now pass judgement on these matters, with your testimony in mind. Inform Léofcetel that I am moved by his loyalty, and his adherence to my commands. As such, I have decided to bestow upon him the title of Captain of the Guard. With this rank, he shall be able to oversee the other men on guard and ensure that none shall risk their lives to attack the White Hand Orcs.

'When you have finished with Léofcetel, speak with Captain Garmund. Garmund is to pack his supplies and return to the Mead Hall. For his noble acts and his great desire to protect others, he will receive the privilege of becoming my personal protector and safe-guard. My life will be more secure with Garmund at my side.''Athelward has made me Captain of the Guard? It is an honourable title, but one wrought with peril and frustration. This feels more of a punishment than a reward! I sought to keep away from the fields of destruction, only to be thrust onto the front lines of our defences.

'I am unsure how this flawed promotion was decided, but I cannot help but think that you are to blame, ${PLAYER}. Off with you! Pester someone else with your good deeds....''If Reeve Athelward has decided that I must return to serve him, then I shall obey his judgement. I believe that my actions were just, but I must accept the outcome of disregarding his orders. Allow me time to collect my belongings.

'I regret that I will not be able to further defend the lands that I so greatly love, but by protecting the Reeve, I am in a unique position to keep safe the people I have sworn to honour.

'Perhaps, in time, I will be able to convince Athelward not to sacrifice so much of his domain in order to defend his city. Thank you for standing by me through all of this, ${PLAYER}.'Bingo knows he should travel into the kingdom of Rohan, but his fear of horses makes that prospect a frightening one and slows his steps.Sabert is at the eastern gate of Stangard, the outpost of Rohan along the banks of the Great River.

Bingo is reluctant to ride a horse in Rohan, and wants to learn from Sabert whether the kingdom is small enough to experience on foot.Talk to Sabert at the east gate of StangardBingo is in the upper courtyard of Stangard.

Sabert has made it clear that walking across the kingdom of Rohan without a horse is not a very good idea, and you should return with that news to Bingo.Talk to Bingo within StangardBingo is in the upper courtyard of Stangard.

Bingo is waiting to speak to you and begin the long walk into Rohan.Talk to Bingo in Stangard and walk with him into RohanBingo is inside the town of Langhold, in the northern area of the Wold in East Rohan.

You have entered the kingdom of Rohan with Bingo, and the hobbit wishes to do something for the people of Langhold.There are many threats in the Wold of East Rohan that threaten the safety of the people of Langhold: salamanders, brigands, and Easterlings.

Bingo wants to help the folk of Langhold by fighting dangerous foes throughout the Wold.Defeat salamanders in the Wold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat brigands in the Wold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Easterlings in the Wold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bingo is inside the town of Langhold, in the northern area of the Wold in East Rohan.

You have defeated many threats throughout the Wold to help the people of Langhold.Bingo is outside the main entrance to Harwick, on the northern side of the city, in the Wold.Talk to Bingo at the entrance to Harwick, in the Wold'I do not really know where we should go next, ${PLAYER}. We are off the edge of old Bilbo's map now, and our steps have taken us into unknown territory! We could travel into the kingdom of Rohan proper, I suppose, but... well, maybe there is another course we should take. They love their horses, you see; look around at how many of the equine figures have been carved on the buildings here in Stangard, and this is a mere outpost and beyond the bounds of the kingdom!

'For my part, I have never really liked horses very much. They are so large and frightening, always stamping around with their heavy hooves and snapping at unwary hobbits with their strong teeth! I was almost bitten by a horse when I was in my tweens, you know, and I have never managed to forgive the things.

'Do you think you could nip down to the eastern gate of Stangard and ask Sabert if we might enjoy a walking-tour of the kingdom of Rohan? It is not a very large place, is it? If our feet will do just fine, then I say that's what we should do, and leave the horses quite out of it!''For my part, I have never really liked horses very much. They are so large and frightening, always stamping around with their heavy hooves and snapping at unwary hobbits with their strong teeth! I was almost bitten by a horse when I was in my tweens, you know, and I have never managed to forgive the things.

'Do you think you could nip down to the eastern gate of Stangard and ask Sabert if we might enjoy a walking-tour of the kingdom of Rohan? It is not a very large place, is it? If our feet will do just fine, then I say that's what we should do, and leave the horses quite out of it!''Is the kingdom of Rohan suitable for experiencing on foot? You are asking if it is a good idea to walk from one end of Rohan to the other, instead of riding a horse?'

Sabert erupts into raucous laughter, and though it is a somewhat impolite response, you must admit that you rather expected it. Bingo is not going to like this.Sabert threatens to burst into laughter again at the prospect of walking across Rohan, so you decide not to say anything.'What did Sabert have to say about walking across Rohan, ${PLAYER}? Is the kingdom small enough for that to be reasonable?'

You tell Bingo that Sabert really thinks it is better to ride atop a horse in Rohan.

'He would say that, though. The Rohirrim certainly do love their horses. I do not feel the need to climb aboard one of those fearsome beasts. I say we walk! What do you think, ${PLAYER}? Is this our adventure or Sabert's? I say we can use the exercise! Speak to me when you are ready to begin our walk into Rohan!''I am ready for a good bit of walking if you are, ${PLAYER}! I would never have suggested this at the start of our journey, but now we are seasoned adventurers and I am not afraid of the prospect of such a hike!''I have heard that the first town we will encounter in the kingdom of Rohan is called Langhold. Let us go there at once!''So this is Rohan, eh? The people here seem very tense, as if they were waiting for something bad to happen. I do not know if I blame them, ${PLAYER}! This is quite a nice little town, but it seems surrounded on all sides by danger, and there are not enough guards here to protect it!

'Well, they are in luck, for three brave adventurers have strolled into Langhold: you, me, and Willem Whisker, of course! A brief chat with some of the children has given me all the information we need to help improve the state of things in this town. If we can defeat salamanders, brigands, and Easterlings throughout the Wold, I think we will have made things a good deal safer for the people of Langhold. Let's do it, my friend!''This is terrible, ${PLAYER}! We have just arrived in Rohan, and already I see that evil has truly come to this kingdom! This may once have been a nice town, but now it is abandoned, a burned shell!'

You tell Bingo that the folk of Langhold fled to the town of Harwick, and he nods.

'I am glad some of the people escaped, but if they are anything like the hobbits I have known, they will want to return to their own hearths as soon as they can. We should make the surrounding area as safe for them as we can! Let us fight the various foes that lurk nearby: salamanders, brigands, and Easterlings! Then we will be doing our part to help the people of Langhold one day return to their own homes!''If we can defeat salamanders, brigands, and Easterlings throughout the Wold, I think we will have made things a good deal safer for the people of Langhold. Let's do it, my friend!''Let us fight the various foes that lurk nearby: salamanders, brigands, and Easterlings! Then we will be doing our part to help the people of Langhold one day return to their own homes!''That was good work, ${PLAYER}! The people of Langhold still seem to be worried, but we have made things better in the Wold, even if they are not sure it is safe to be cheery!'

Bingo takes a deep breath, seemingly readying himself for something.

'You know, ${PLAYER}, you might have been right about the horse thing. Sabert and Egwylf, too. Rohan is a fair bit larger than I expected, and maybe I should see about getting a horse as well. I spoke with a man here and he said there are horses available to purchase at the town of Harwick, to the south. I suppose we should go there and see.''That was good work, ${PLAYER}! Langhold is in no condition for its people to return, and will not be for some time, but perhaps our efforts in the Wold will help its people when they do come back.'

Bingo takes a deep breath, seemingly readying himself for something.

'You know, ${PLAYER}, you might have been right about the horse thing. Sabert and Egwylf, too. Rohan is a fair bit larger than I expected, and maybe I should see about getting a horse as well. Apparently there are horses available to purchase at the town of Harwick, to the south. I suppose we should go there and see.''You know, ${PLAYER}, you might have been right about the horse thing. Sabert and Egwylf, too. Rohan is a fair bit larger than I expected, and maybe I should see about getting a horse as well. I spoke with a man here and he said there are horses available to purchase at the town of Harwick, to the south. I suppose we should go there and see.''You know, ${PLAYER}, you might have been right about the horse thing. Sabert and Egwylf, too. Rohan is a fair bit larger than I expected, and maybe I should see about getting a horse as well. Apparently there are horses available to purchase at the town of Harwick, to the south. I suppose we should go there and see.''Here we are, here we are. We can see about horses for sale very soon, ${PLAYER}. Very soon! If we must. Must we really?'

<rgb=#00FF00>After you complete this quest, Bert Bartleby will arrive near the stable-master at Harwick, in East Rohan.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Yea or Neigh?' (Recommended Level: 80)</rgb>The group of Uruks you are pursuing passed near to Elthengels, and now you should try and learn what the villagers know of them.Horn is waiting to speak with you in Elthengels.Talk to Horn in ElthengelsHorn has given you some coins of the Mark with which to buy a round of drinks for the patrons inside the Elthengels tavern. Perhaps they will have some information to tell you about the Uruks.You have bought a round of drinks with Horn's coins and should now distribute them to the patrons of the Elthengels tavern.A new arrival has come to the Elthengels tavern.Ingild is waiting to talk to you on the first floor of the Elthengels tavern.Talk to Ingild and investigate the sound he heardThane Mildrith is in the mead hall of Elthengels.

After the Orc attack, Thane Mildrith has asked you to meet with her.Talk to Thane Mildrith in the Mead Hall of Elthengels'Now that I'm here, I need to accomplish my duty. I was given a message to deliver to Thane Mildrith of Elthengels, asking for her to send some of her warriors to Floodwend to help defend against the increased number of Easterlings crossing the river. Her husband was slain by Orcs some time ago, and her son died under... unpleasant circumstances... more recently. I do not think she will respond favourably to this message, but still I must deliver it. Thank you once again, friend.''I must deliver my message to Thane Mildrith, even though she will not like it. Perhaps I will see you later.''The Uruks that left the East Wall may have passed near to Elthengels. We should learn if any of the townspeople know anything of their passage. Here, ${PLAYER}. Take these coins of the Mark, and use them to buy a round of drinks at the tavern for the patrons there. It could be one of them saw something of those Uruks.''We need to know what the people of Elthengels know about the Uruks that fled from the East Wall. Nothing helps loosen tongues quite like free-flowing ale or beer. Use the coins of the Mark I gave you to order some drinks for people in the Elthengels tavern.''I have not seen you around here before, stranger. Your coins are good, though, I will give you that. Here's a round of my finest. Pass it around as you will. There should be some of the usual folk upstairs: Wigherd, Tidwine, maybe one or two others who don't come in as frequently. They'll be glad of your generosity.''Spread those drinks around, and you'll make yourself some friends. Look for a few of my regulars upstairs.''Thank you kindly, stranger. I don't mind if I do have a sip of that.'

Wigherd drinks deeply, but shakes his head when you ask about the Uruks.

'I did not see them run by, but I heard from those who did. Running more away than toward, it seemed to them as saw it. No one wanted to ride out to challenge them, and who can blame them? We lost Thane Pendrad in the Autumn to Orcs, and his widow may be Thane now, but she is not the fighter he was. And then to lose her son! It is a wonder she can go on at all, but she's not commanding troops. The Uruks passed us by, and we should be lucky they did not turn to raid the town.''I'll have some of that, since you're buying.'

Tidwine drains the mug in one huge gulp, and at first you cannot tell if he has heard you ask about the Uruks.

'Ah! Just the stuff. What did you say? The Uruks that ran by Elthengels? Aye, I saw them. Never seen Uruks run like that before. Even if Thane Mildrith was in any state to order our riders, she would have been a fool to send men after them. And she is no fool. Suffered more than her share of grief, if you ask me, but no fool. She was Pendrad's rock, before he was slain by Orcs, and she had much to do with shaping the man Pendulf became. Pity what happened to the boy, but you'll hear no rumours from me.''If you insist!'

Dudmund finishes the mug and slams it down on the table.

'Best beer in the Norcrofts, and I don't care who hears it! You go tell Reeve Athelward that I'd rather have the brew of Elthengels than that of Cliving!'

The other patrons give Dudmund some worried looks, but it only serves to rile him up further.

'No, you listen to me! If he challenges me to trial by combat, like he did with Mildrith's son, well, I'll teach him a thing or two. I won't even let him scratch me with that blade of his. I swear he had some vile coating on it, to make Pendulf's wound fester like that. I swear it!'

He lapses into depressed, drunken silence, and has nothing more to say to you.'Ah, Ingild has returned! I have not seen him in awhile. Do you know him?''Now there is a familiar face! ${PLAYER}, I have not seen you since the East Wall. Did you find the Uruks you were seeking when we spoke at Mansig's encampment? No? A shame, I am sorry. I have returned home to... what was that noise? Did you hear that?''Something is happening outside! It sounds like trouble!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'You have my gratitude. Without your help our losses might have been more severe. I have heard Elfsige's message from Floodwend and can spare no men, but perhaps I can send a waggon of blacksmithing supplies.

'I will see you on your way with supplies of my own, but only if you will help me with a matter of crucial import for my town.'Thane Mildrith is suspicious of Reeve Athelward, and wants you to see how he is treating her daughter, newly-taken as his ward.Cliving is in the Norcrofts, to the north-west of Elthengels.

Thane Mildrith of Elthengels is suspicious of Reeve Athelward of Cliving, who took her daughter Siflád as his ward, and wants you to ensure that Siflád is being treated well.Travel to Cliving in the NorcroftsReeve Athelward is in the Mead Hall of Cliving.

Thane Mildrith wants you to ensure that her daughter Siflád is not being mistreated. The first step is to introduce yourself to Athelward.Talk to Athelward in the Mead Hall of ClivingSiflád is somewhere in Cliving.

Athelward has given you permission to speak to Siflád, his ward.Cuthred, the captain of the guard, is by a guard tower in Cliving, west of the Mead Hall.

Reeve Athelward wanted you to help the captain of his guard once you had spoken with Siflád.Talk to Cuthred in Cliving'You see before you a woman cursed, ${PLAYER}! In the Autumn my husband Pendrad was slain by Orcs while riding near to the Pillars of the Kings astride the Great River. He had requested that the Reeve send him more riders for that sojourn, but Athelward refused. O cruel fate! Even worse what happened then!'

Mildrith turns and gazes at nothing, overcome by sorrow and anger.

'My son Pendulf argued that Athelward was complicit in his father's death. Had the Reeve sent the riders he should have, Pendrad would not have been overcome by the Orcs! My son was correct, but... Athelward is proud, and would not allow this stain on his honour. He challenged my son to a sword-trial to first blood, if he did not recant his words. Pendulf sustained a slight wound, just a cut, really, but the wound festered and not long afterward he was dead.

'The cruelest cut of all: Athelward adopted my daughter Siflád as his ward, saying she would be safer and better cared-for at Cliving than with me and our grief. All I ask of you is this: go to Cliving and keep your eyes open. I want to learn if Siflád is being treated well. I cannot put my suspicions of Athelward to rest.''The Reeve sent you to me to help with our problems? Good! We can refuse no offers of help. Cliving has been less affected than some other towns in this riding, but still there are Orcs about and we need to deal with them if we are to keep our people safe.''I saw you coming to Cliving from afar, and I wondered what your story might be. And now you have come to tell it to me! How wonderful!'

You tell Siflád that you have come from her mother in Elthengels, and that Mildrith worries about her.

'My poor mother. I miss her. Some days I miss her terribly, but knowing that she is still at home eases that ache somewhat. Worse are the days when I miss my father and my brother, for they are never coming home.

'But I am as happy at Cliving as I can be. The Reeve is kind to me, if distant. I think he blames himself for all the grief my family has endured, and wants to make amends for it. I am treated well. Look at the view from the walls! There is just so much more going on here than at Elthengels!

'Yes, I am happy here. I hope my mother is not upset by that, but it is the truth. But tell her I miss her.''Welcome to Cliving, friends from afar! I am Athelward, Reeve of the Norcrofts and Thane of Cliving. My house and hospitality is yours while you stay.'

Athelward listens as you relate the reason for your visit, and a cloud crosses his face.

'Oh, Mildrith. What happened to her should not have happened to any lady of Rohan. Losing first her husband, and then her son... I should have been more understanding of the boy's youthful boldness, but he called my honour into question. The sword-trial is the customary means of resolving such disputes. I did not know his wound would fester in such a way. I would have it back, if I could. Taking young Siflád as my ward seemed the kindest thing to do, but I can understand a mother's grief may make it seem otherwise.

'I will answer any questions of yours, and you are of course free to see Siflád. If it is not too much trouble, could I ask you to help Cuthred, the captain of my guard, with his duties after you have spoken with Siflád?''I have been treated well here at Cliving. I like how bustling this city is. It is so much more exciting than my home of Elthengels!''You will find Siflád somewhere around Cliving. She likes to climb the towers and speak with the guards there. Do not forgot to introduce yourself to Cuthred, my captain of the guard, after you have spoken to Siflád.''I have lost everything but my daughter, and I want to make sure she is happy and being treated well. I do not trust Athelward, Reeve or not! Go to Cliving and ensure that Siflád is not being mistreated!''I have lost everything but my daughter, and I want to make sure she is happy and being treated well. I do not trust Athelward, Reeve or not! Go to Cliving and ensure that Siflád is not being mistreated!'Cuthred is eager to make use of your mounted skill to defend Cliving from the Orcs marauding the Norcrofts.Cuthred has sent you riding the bounds of Cliving: riding east of the city to the first marker, and then turning to your right at each new marker until you have described a great loop around Cliving. He wants you to defeat any mounted enemies you encounter on this circuit as well.Ride to the first marker, east of ClivingRide to the second marker, south of the first, outside Cliving in the Norcrofts.Ride to the second marker, south of the firstRide to the third marker, west of the second, outside Cliving in the Norcrofts.Ride to the third marker, west of the secondRide to the final marker, north of the third, outside Cliving in the Norcrofts.Ride to the final marker, north of the third oneCuthred is in Cliving, near the entrance to the city.

You have ridden the bounds, and should now speak to Cuthred, Cliving's captain of the guard.Talk to Cuthred in Cliving'An important part of the guard duty my men undertake consists of riding the bounds of Cliving, looking for threats lurking too near the city. If you have a war-steed and the skill to ride it, I would encourage you to help us with this duty, ${PLAYER}. Ride east of Cliving until you see the marker that delineates the bounds, and then turn to the south. Continue this process, turning to your right at each marker, until you have described a great loop around the city.

'Defeat any mounted enemies you encounter on this circuit, ${PLAYER}. You will be doing the people of Cliving a great service.''You have ridden the bounds? What did you encounter? My men ride that circuit many times a day, ensuring the safety of the city. Usually it is quiet, but occasionally Orcs or other foes stray within the line and must be fought or driven away.''The first marker is east of Cliving. When you encounter it, turn your war-steed to the right, and ride until you reach the next marker. Keep doing that until you have described a great loop in front of the city.

'Defeat any mounted enemies you encounter. Orcs generally do not come very near to Cliving, but they may still be encountered on the bounds.'Athelward has requested to see you about something before you leave Cliving.Reeve Athelward is in the Mead Hall of Cliving.

Athelward wanted to speak with you and your companions before you depart Cliving.Talk to Athelward in the Mead Hall of ClivingNona, Horn, and Corudan are in the Mead Hall of Cliving with Athelward.

You should speak with your companions to determine your next course of action.Talk to Nona in the Mead Hall of ClivingTalk to Horn in the Mead Hall of ClivingTalk to Corudan in the Mead Hall of ClivingReeve Athelward is in the Mead Hall of Cliving.

You have spoken with your companions and should now tell Athelward your decision.Talk to Athelward in the Mead Hall of ClivingThane Mildrith is in the Mead Hall of Elthengels, south-east of Cliving, in the Norcrofts.

Reeve Athelward wants you to bring his proposal of marriage to Thane Mildrith.Talk to Thane Mildrith in the Mead Hall of the Elthengels'I was told that Reeve Athelward wanted to speak with you before you depart Cliving. Thank you again for your help, and I hope the Reeve rewards you in suitable fashion for the aid you have graciously given. For Rohan to be safe and well-guarded, the Norcrofts must be as well. I appreciate your help in that regard, and I know the Reeve will agree.''Return to Reeve Athelward in the Mead Hall here in Cliving. He wanted to speak with you before you left Cliving.''We have been in conversation with Athelward for some time. He has been a gracious host, but we should be leaving.''I have grown weary of this city already. Too many walls, and not enough open land for my taste, ${PLAYER}.''For a city of Men, this does seem to be in a defensible place.''I have been speaking with your companions while you aided my captain of the guard, ${PLAYER}. I am inspired by their example! A man of Rohan, a girl of Dunland, and even an Elf! How is it that folk of such different backgrounds can work together?

'It pains me to see you leave. Can you not stay and help with the maintenance of Cliving? I could use each of your talents to better the state of my people! Discuss among yourselves, if you must, but do not decide to leave so quickly! A strong Cliving makes for a strong Rohan, and these are troublesome times.''I think it is time to go. The Uruks we follow did not come this close to Cliving, either because it was not on their road or they were afraid of the town's defenders.''We need not stay here any longer. Cliving has strong walls, and its defensive position is better than the other towns we have seen. Athelward has little cause to fear Orcs from this fastness. He does not need our help any longer, and we should be off.''I have seen more secure homes, but they were in the mallorns of Lórien. This city has little to fear of Orcs, if you ask for the opinion of an Elf. Athelward should see what he can do to protect the people who live elsewhere in the Norcrofts, but he can do that without our presence.''I want to help the people of Rohan, for they are my flesh and blood, but the people of Cliving are better-defended than most. I would not worry for Cliving's survival in our absence.''I do not like to stay in any one place for so long. Let us be gone!''Athelward has already done what he must to protect his people. Cliving is well-built, on solid foundations with high walls. Any Orcs that stray within the bounds will be repelled.''Ah, I understand that you need to be off. You cannot blame me for wanting to keep skilful folk at the disposal of my people. If you have business back at Elthengels, can I ask you to deliver a message for me?'

You murmur your assent, and Athelward rumbles pleasantly.

'Good, good. This may surprise you, ${PLAYER}, but I have no male heir to carry on my line. I love my daughters more than my own life, but a son would release me from the worry that my line will end with me. My wife has been dead this past year, and it is time I began thinking of the future.

'Thane Mildrith of Elthengels has had more than her share of grief these past few months. If it is in my power to heal the sorrow I have caused her, I would do so. I desire her hand in marriage. I will respect her decision in this, but please express to her how strong a union between our families and our cities would be. The Norcrofts could only benefit from such a union.

'It is, ah, not the first time I have asked for her hand. But it will be the last, no matter her decision.''Mildrith is a handsome woman, thane of an important town, and has no husband. She needs someone to support her during her time of grief, and the Norcrofts need to have unified, strong leaders if it is to survive the dangerous times ahead.'Thane Mildrith listens to your message from Reeve Athelward with growing horror and disgust.

'You cannot be serious! That scoundrel! That braggart! His inaction doomed my husband Pendrad to death by the Orcs, and he personally gave my son the wound that killed him! He has the gall to ask for my hand not once, but twice! And to use friends to do it!'

Mildrith brings herself under control, and continues in a quiet, deadly voice.

'I sent you to learn if my daughter Siflád was being treated well by the murderer of her father and brother. Is she?'Randgar has refused to leave his home, at the risk of being killed by Orcs or Uruks. His home is under continuous assault, but he perseveres for the chance to be with the woman he loves.Randgar's farm is south and east of Cliving, along the road. The gate is just outside the small home.

Randgar has asked you to secure the gates to his farmstead, but to be wary of assaults from Orcs.Defeat the Orc-tormenters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Secure the farm gateRandgar lives on a small farm, south and east of Cliving, along the road.

Randgar awaits outside his home for news that the constant Orc-menace has been put to an end.Talk to Randgar'I must endure the Orcs until I am able to prove to Inhild's father, Hwitmon, that I am worthy of his daughter.

'The fence gates around my farm need to be secured. Upon each of my attempts to leave my farm, the Orcs have harassed me and tried to ride me down. Please, help me secure the fence, but remain vigilant of the Orc-riders and their torment.''I am glad to see that you have dealt with those merciless brutes. I am fortunate to have someone with such skill in my company. Let us hope that the Orcs decide to relent their constant harassment now that they have seen our ability to defend against them.'The homes and farms of the people of Norcrofts have fallen to the savage pillagers and defilers of the White Hand force. With neither Reeve Athelward or King Théoden willing to send men to oppose them, there is little hope of reclaiming the homes without outside intervention.The farmsteads lie at the western edge of the Norcrofts.

Cyneswith has asked you to defeat many of the scavengers and defilers that have invaded and dislodged the farmers from their homes.Defeat White Hand scavengers at the Norcrofts farms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat White Hand defilers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cyneswith lies to the east, behind the large Springview Estate.

You have removed many of the Orcs from the homes and farmsteads to the west. This act should restore some hope in Cyneswith.Return to Cyneswith'The Norcrofts are home to the most beautiful and fertile farmland in all of Rohan, and they should belong to its people, not to filthy Orcs. Yet as their vile horde ravaged across these lands, they simply claimed whatever defenceless property fell within their wake.

'Reeve Athelward has abandoned these farms, Théoden King sits blindly upon his throne; my last hope falls to you. If we are ever to take these lands back, we must defeat the Orcs dwelling within our homes.''The farms and homes we once loved have been utterly destroyed by the Orcs. Even if we are able to reclaim them, it would take years of work to restore their condition... but we must have resolve.

'Thank you, friend. Even when Théoden King has closed his eyes and ears to our suffering, you have stood by us and helped fight for what is rightfully ours.'A tomb of the Men of Gondor from the Second Age has been discovered in a secluded area of the Norcrofts. It is unknown what treasures -- or dangers -- may lie within.The ancient tomb lies to the south of Elthengels, hidden in a remote corner of the Norcrofts by a waterfall.

The ancient tomb awaits your exploration.Explore the ancient tombThe ancient tomb lies to the south of Elthengels, hidden in a remote corner of the Norcrofts by a waterfall.

The ancient tomb awaits your exploration.Explore the depths of the ancient tombA tomb of the Men of Gondor from the Second Age has been revealed in a secluded area of the Norcrofts. It is unknown what treasures -- or dangers -- may lie within.

The greatest discoveries are often hidden at the bottom of such ancient tombs, waiting for some adventurers to come and seek them out.Randgar has devised a plan to win the admiration of Inhild's callous father in the hopes that the young couple might someday be together in happiness.The Uruk-hai looter has been seen by Randgar travelling between many of the deserted homes south of Randgar's farm.

Randgar has guessed that the Uruk-hai looter may be carrying enough valuables to impress Inhild's father, if Randgar were to possess them, and wishes you to obtain them.Defeat the Uruk-hai Looter and collect his stolen valuablesRandgar lives on a small farm, south and east of Cliving, along the road.

The Uruk-hai looter bore none of the valuables that Randgar had hoped for, and a new course of action must be taken. You should return to Randgar and let him know.Return to Randgar at the farmsteadRandgar lives on a small farm, south and east of Cliving, along the road.

Randgar is not to be seen. Several things at the farm seem amiss and worth inspecting.Examine the discarded spearExamine the discarded cloakInhild resides at an estate, just east of the central fountain in Cliving, with her father.

With Randgar missing and a disturbed scene found at his home, Inhild should be informed of the events that have transpired.Bring Randgar's cloak to Inhild in Cliving'Your dispatch of those Orc-ruffians has given me a rather clever idea. With your assistance, I have thought of a way to gain the respect of Inhild's father.

'There is a notorious Uruk-thief that I have witnessed looting various trinkets and valuables from the abandoned farmsteads surrounding my home. His fondness for treasure will be his downfall.

'Track down this Uruk and separate him from his ill-gotten gains. With those valuables in my possession, I might yet impress old Hwitmon and be allowed to court his daughter.''This is Randgar's cloak, is it not? I was worried that he had not been found, but it seems you have been helping him after all. You look concerned, ${PLAYER}, is something wrong?'Runald and his familly have prepared their waggon, said their farewells, and are ready to begin their dangerous journey south to Edoras.Runald and his family await you at the southern entrance to the village of Elthengels.Escort the waggon along the roadRunald and his family have arrived safely at the trader's outpost along the road.

Runald wishes to thank you for aiding his departure.Talk to Runald'We have arrived! We would not have survived the journey without your aid, ${PLAYER}. We owe you much.

'I must purchase some supplies for my family, and then we will depart the camp at once. We should find safety as we head south into Elfhelm's lands and beyond to Edoras. Farewell, and may fortune find you, friend.''May we meet again someday, ${PLAYER}.

'Feel free to introduce yourself to the traders and thank them for their service. They have remained to provide aid when our King and Reeve have turned away from us.''Stay close, ${PLAYER}. The Orcs could come upon us at any moment. We must stay upon the road and seek protection at the trader's outpost to the west.'Inhild has travelled to Randgar's farm, in order to say a final farewell to the man she loved so dearly.Inhild can be found along the road to Randgar's farm.

Inhild wishes to speak with you about Randgar.Talk to InhildRandgar has returned and is awaiting outside his farm.

Randgar has appeared, alive and well, and would like to explain things to you.Talk to RandgarInhild can be found near Randgar's farm.

Inhild wishes to discuss one final matter with you.Talk to Inhild'Greetings again, ${PLAYER}. I understand your surprise. Please, allow me to explain.''Thank you for bringing me here, ${PLAYER}. I hope that I have not been overly troublesome to you. You have been a greater friend to Randgar and myself than you know.

'Please excuse me for a moment.''My father cares for me, but he shares not my views of love. He would never allow for me to spend my life with a farmer, no matter how good that man might be. 

'Randgar and I must depart together for safer lands. You have been so helpful, but I must ask of you one final request. Please, tell my father that I have gone off with a rich and handsome Rider from Edoras, whom we encountered on our return. In this way, he will miss me, but be all together happy for my good fortune.''Good fortune to you, ${PLAYER}. We owe you our happiness together.'Bingo has decided to walk across the kingdom of Rohan, in order to avoid having to ride a horse.Bingo is ready to cross into the kingdom of Rohan.Talk to Bingo by the bridge into RohanWillem is on the southern side of the bridge, in the kingdom of Rohan.Talk to Willem on the other side of the bridgeA woman of Rohan has galloped up the road, impressed at your victory over the Orc raiding party.Talk to EgwylfYou have met a woman named Egwylf just within the border of Rohan, and she is very interested in the adventures of your small party.Listen to what Egwylf has to say of RohanBingo is on the southern side of the bridge, in the kingdom of Rohan.Talk to Bingo'Egwylf said there were towns not far from here. Let us find one of them and continue our journey... on foot!''A walk will do both of us some good, ${PLAYER}! We do not need to ride horses in Rohan. Our feet will serve just fine. If you do not believe me, just walk with me and see!

'You will be amazed at how quickly the time passes once we are moving again, my friend! Who needs horses?'Willem looks at you blandly, as if to say, 'What took you so long?'

Suddenly, his ears twitch, and the sound of guttural voices reaches your hearing as well. Orcs approach from the south!'I am Egwylf, and I am on an errand of some haste, for the message I bear must come to Stangard as quickly as it may; I will not tarry long, though it seems we four share a hatred of the Orcs and could spend some time swapping tales.'

You introduce yourself and your companions, and Egwylf looks appraisingly at Bingo.'She seemed very nice, but I think Egwylf is wrong about the necessity of riding a horse in Rohan. I have done just fine until now, have I not? And we would not have appreciated that bridge back there nearly as much if we galloped or cantered or trotted past it at speed, would we?

'No, we would not.'Ides has asked you to walk with her and to listen as she expresses her thoughts.Ides can be found at the mill, north of the training grounds.

Ides wishes to discuss her thoughts with you.Talk to IdesIdes can be found at the mill, north of the training grounds.

Ides seems to have heard a noise and wishes to inquire about it.Talk to IdesIdes can be found at the forked path along the road, west of the training grounds.

Ides has found Edwulf, injured in battle, and would like to discuss what should be done.Talk to Ides about EdwulfUruks have assaulted the Rohirrim training grounds.

Ides has asked you to help deal with the attacking Uruk-hai.Defeat the Uruk-hai at the training grounds (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ides can be found along the road, west of the training grounds.

After defeating the Uruk-hai invaders, Ides awaits your return.Return to Ides along the roadIdes can be found along the road, west of the training grounds.

Ides has spotted an Uruk, and will need your help to defeat it.Defeat the Uruk-hai warriorDo not allow Ides to become defeatedIdes can be found along the road, west of the training grounds.

After defeating the Uruk-hai invaders, Ides awaits your return.Talk to Ides along the roadway'We must allow time for the men to recover and restore the camp.

'Once again, I am honoured to be your student.''I am eager to learn more from you, ${PLAYER}. I know it will not come easily, but I will become a shield-maiden, no matter the cost.

'I am fortunate to have met someone with the same determination and will to do good that I see in myself... and in my father.''I hear the faint roar of fighting coming from the training grounds. We had better investigate with haste.''Edwulf has been wounded! It is not a fatal blow, but he will need someone to protect him.

'This must be the work of the Uruks, ${PLAYER}! No doubt they are assailing the training grounds. Go and assail the Uruk-intruders, while I stay and watch over Edwulf.''We have defeated the invaders! It is most fortunate that we were nearby... Edwulf's wounds are not fatal, and the damage to the camp will be quickly repaired.

'These men owe us their lives, ${PLAYER}. We arrived just in time.'Captain Garmund has organized an unexpected counter-attack against the Orcs who have sacked the farmlands outside of Cliving in the hopes of driving back some of the White Hand forces.Captain Garmund can be found just ahead of you, at the northern edge of the farmland.

Garmund has asked you to help him repel many of the White Hand trespassers across the farmland.Talk to Garmund'Let us return to the outpost at the watch-tower. We cannot hope to regain the fields in such short time.''I am glad to have you with me, ${PLAYER}. Stay close and be ready to strike our foe.

'Come. Let us see what these trespassers are made of!'Elthengels is under attack by Orcs! You and your companions must drive them off before they sack the town.You have run outside the tavern with Ingild, and should speak with him quickly!Talk to Ingild outside the Elthengels tavernHorn, Nona, and Corudan are each somewhere in Elthengels. Help them drive off the Orcs!Find Horn in ElthengelsFind Nona in ElthengelsFind Corudan in ElthengelsYou have driven off most of the Orcs, but some still remain within Elthengels. Defeat them and then find Ingild outside the Mead Hall at the top of the village.Defeat Orcs that remain in Elthengels and then speak with Ingild outside the Mead Hall at the top of the villageThane Mildrith is outside the Mead Hall in Elthengels.Talk to Thane Mildrith outside the Mead Hall in Elthengels'Orcs, ${PLAYER}! Orcs are attacking Elthengels!'

Ingild looks around him hurriedly, and comes to a decision.

'The companions you travelled with in the East Wall... do they still journey with you? They seemed strong and skilled, even the Dunlending. Find them throughout Elthengels and help them drive off the Orcs!

'I must find the Thane! Mildrith must be protected!''The Orcs have been driven off? That, at least, is good tidings!''Attacks by Orcs have become more frequent of late, endangering my people. Enemies are everywhere.'

Mildrith looks around suspiciously.

'It is not safe to speak here openly.''Let us go inside where we may speak more openly.''${PLAYER}! I told you that Rohan was under constant threat by the Orcs of Mordor, and now you see it for yourself! Help me drive them off! Some have gotten to the upper levels already, but let us regain this open space for the people of Elthengels!''The people of this town need our help, ${PLAYER}. They and I have no feud, though they settled in lands that belong to my people. My fight is with their leaders, with the kings and rulers who stole from and slew my ancestors.

'But these people? Few of them know by which end to hold a blade, and fewer still can do so with any effectiveness! They need a proud Dunlending woman to show them how to fight! Let them insult my people then, after they have seen what I can do! I wager they will not!''I am glad to have my bow back, ${PLAYER}, but I did not know I would have need of it so soon! My arrows will sing with my heart, truly, to rid this town of these Orcs and their Warg allies!

'Let us clear this place of them, and protect these people!'Ides has ventured into the wooded area adjacent to the training grounds, to test her skill against the wild bears. She is eager to prove herself in battle.Ides can be found waiting along the road.

Ides wishes to speak with you about hunting the wild bears.Talk to IdesIdes has ventured with you into the woods.

Ides wishes to speak with you about the hunt.Talk to IdesThe Uruk-hai scouts have invaded the woods by the training grounds.

Uruks have begun to attack Ides while searching for the location of the training grounds.Defeat the Uruk-hai scoutsIdes has ventured with you into the woods.

Ides wishes to speak with you about the Uruk-hai.Talk to Ides'Something approaches, ${PLAYER}. What seeks us here among the trees?

'Be ready to stand your ground.''That was far too close, ${PLAYER}! Hurry, let us return before others appear and overwhelm us or follow our path to the camp.''Come now, we must not risk being seen here again. Let us head back to the mill.''The wild bears are oft seen among the trees nearby. We must look only for the largest, most ferocious bears; they must offer a challenge if I am to improve my abilities.

'Let us begin the hunt. I am eager to learn through observing your skills.'Mauhúr's Uruk-hai have been attempting to establish a foothold within the eastern part of the Norcrofts from which to attack Elthengels and other Rohirrim settlements.Many Uruk-hai have attempted to establish an encampment south of Elthengels at Kufuzg.

Defeat the Uruk-hai who reside at Kufuzg and help put an end to the Uruk-leader's threat.Defeat Uruk-hai at Kufuzg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Guard-captain of the merchants can be found at the trader's outpost, along the road west of Elthengels.

The Guard-captain of the trader's outpost is said to reward those able to rid Norcrofts of its Uruk enemies.Return to the Guard-captain at the trader's outpostMauhúr's Uruk-hai have been attempting to establish a foothold within the eastern part of the Norcrofts from which to attack Elthengels and other Rohirrim settlements.

Reducing the Uruk-infantry will greatly weaken the chances of a direct assault on Elthengels.'You have done well. Each Uruk you defeat means less danger for the merchants and inhabitants of Elthengels. Their kind would do well to stay clear of the lands of the Eorlingas.'The healer of Elthengels, Elfwyn, is distraught over the death of young Pendulf, Thane Mildrith's son. He feels his supplies are inadequate and is in need of a variety of local herbs in preparation for treating future wounds and injuries.The plants desired by Elfwyn can be found growing in the Norcrofts in the areas outside the town of Elthengels.

Elfwyn has asked you to collect various plantlife, so that he may use them to treat any serious wounds that may occur to people in Elthengels.Collect Clotbur (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect Meadow-maid (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect Lamb's Ear (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Elfwyn, the healer, may be found at his home in Elthengels.

Elfwyn is awaiting your return with the various plants he has asked you to collect.Return to Elfwyn with the herbs'I am in a dire situation, friend. I am responsible for treating the wounds and injuries of the people of Elthengels, but I am without the necessary reagents to do so. Will you stay a moment and hear me out?

'We cannot allow others to suffer the same fate as poor Pendulf, but I am unprepared! I have but a meagre supply of common herbs in my possession. If you were to gather some of the herbs and reagents I am in need of, I might be able to redeem myself by saving others.

'Meadow-maid, Lamb's Ear, and Clotbur can all be found growing outside of Elthengels. These plants are most helpful in treating wounds, but I am currently lacking in their supply. Will you secure them to help bolster my reserves?''You have done well, these plants will work perfectly. I am ashamed for not having saved the noble Pendulf, but with this full set of reagents I vow not to fail Thane Mildrith again.

'I owe you much thanks.'Wuscrad, the garrison commander, has asked you to help raise a militia from the refugees and locals of Elthengels.Potential recruits can be found within the walls of the town of Elthengels.

Wuscrad has asked you to speak with refugees to Elthengels and to convince them to volunteer to join the militia.Wuscrad can be found in the town of Elthengels.

Wuscrad awaits news of the potential recruits for the Elthengels militia.Return to Wuscrad with news of the new recruitsWuscrad spoke of abandoned homes just north of the town of Elthengels, beyond the gates of town.

The Elthengels garrison commander has asked you to secure provisions from the nearby recently abandoned homes.Collect spare provisions at the abandoned homes north of Elthengels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Wuscrad can be found in the town of Elthengels.

You should return the recovered provisions to Wuscrad in the town of Elthengels.Return to Wuscrad with the provisions'Greetings to you, good ${RACE}. Grim is the fate of this land, but we will not simply lay down to die. If Théoden King will not send us aid, then we shall find the strength within our own people.

'Many of those now here in Elthengels have fled their homes seeking refuge behind our walls. Yet it is these same men that must now stand and face the dangers looming ever greater.

'I am eager to provide these refugees with weapons and training, they need only show the desire to fight. Yet few have stepped forward, and my hope of forming a militia among them quickly fades. Perhaps they could be convinced by an outsider, such as yourself. Will you aid me in finding recruits?''You have found candidates worthy of training? This is excellent news, but we must act quickly. A true militia needs more than just able bodies, it needs weapons, training equipment, food, and other provisions.'

'I will provide the equipment and the training. Would you be willing to find some rations to keep us supplied for a while longer? There are some recently abandoned homes north of town...check there for any provisions worth securing.''These provisions will help keep food and supplies available for the newly enlisted, and their families, for some time. We may yet see the people of Norcrofts defend Elthengels in the place of Théoden's Riders.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I am in your debt.''You would dare me to stand before an entire army of Orcs when I was unable to save my own home from a few pitiful savages and thieves? I am but a farmer...neither sword nor spear have I carried, but I am no coward.

'Honour bids that I must fight to repay my benefactors. The people of Elthengels have welcomed me and sheltered me, and in this way, I will repay my debt to them. Tell the militia to expect my company. If I am to fight, there is much that I must learn.''I have lost my home and my land. My family is all that matters to me now. Each day here, I have worked various odd jobs just to earn enough coin to keep my children sheltered and fed.

'Time is scarce for training and the like. Yet, I believe if this militia were to provide my family with an allotment of food each day, then I might use the time given to learn the skills to protect them. You tell this garrison commander that I accept his offer, so long as my children remain eating.''Never did I wish to become a soldier and march off towards war, but it would appear that a war for us is inevitable. Once again, the Orcs will be crashing at our door, and we will be forced to defend ourselves, ready or not.

'When that day comes, I would prefer to stand bravely with a weapon raised against my foes, and I will encourage others to do the same. The militia may consider me grateful for their training.'A company of Orcs has taken to the ruins of the ancient tower, Minas Rant, and have begun to use it as an outpost from which to attack the neighbouring homes.Discover Minas RantThe tower of Minas Rant can be found at the western end of the stream that cuts across the Norcrofts.

The Orcs inhabiting the ancient tower of Minas Rant have begun using it as a base from which to attack the neighbouring homes.Defeat Orcs within Minas Rant (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The tower of Minas Rant can be found at the western end of the stream that cuts across the Norcrofts.

The number of Orcs remaining in the area has been reduced, due to your efforts.CompletedA company of Orcs has taken to the ruins of the ancient tower, Minas Rant, and have begun to use it as an outpost from which to attack the neighbouring homes.The number of Orcs remaining at Minas Rant has been reduced, due to your efforts.Many of the Orcs that have begun encroaching on the lands in the west are carrying a strange variety of fish. Perhaps a person local to the area could reveal the significance of the fish they carry.The Orcs among the western farmsteads seem to be in possession of a strange fish.

Collecting samples of the strange fish these Orcs carry might be helpful in determining a cause.Collect unfamiliar fish from Orc scavengers and defilersThe Orcs among the western farmsteads seem to be in possession of the strange fish.

Collecting samples of the strange fish these Orcs carry might be helpful in determining a cause.Collect unfamiliar fish from Orc scavengers and defilers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cyneswith rests behind the large Springview Estate, to the east.

Perhaps showing these fish to a local, such as Cyneswith, will uncover the cause for the Orcs having them in possession.Ask Cyneswith about the presence of the unusual fishThe Orcs among the western farmsteads seem to be in possession of a type of fish that is unfamiliar.

Collecting some specimens of the fish, and showing them to a native of the area, might be helpful in determining their significance.'These fish may seem foreign to you, but they are at times caught from the stream that flows west across the Norcrofts. They have quite a poor flavour, but I suppose it matters little to the Orcs.

'I know of no farm that would have these fish stockpiled. Could it be that the Orcs have also established an outpost along the water? Thank you for bringing this knowledge to me. I must ponder the meaning of this odd new discovery.'Not everyone is willing to risk their own life and the lives of their loved ones to save the village of Elthengels. Another family plans to leave for safer lands and requires assistance in their final preparations.Runald and his family can be found with their waggon at the southern entrance to Elthengels.

Thane Mildrith has asked you to assist Runald and his family so that they may finish their final preparations in leaving Elthengels safely.Talk to Runald near the southern gate of ElthengelsHereléof can be found at the well in the western section of the village of Elthengels.

Runald would like you to ask Hereléof to watch over his home, once he departs.Talk to Hereléof outside his home in ElthengelsRunald and his family can be found at the southern gate to the village of Elthengels.

Hereléof has agreed to look after the home until the day that Runald or his kin may come to reclaim it.Talk to Runald near the south-western gateCadsterre can be found with her family at the southern gate of Elthengels.

Cadsterre would care to ask a favour of you as she prepares to leave her home.Talk to Cadsterre, wife of RunaldFrithynd can be found contemplating at the fountain in Elthengels, outside the tavern.

Cadsterre has asked you to say farewell to Frithynd, as she cannot bear to do so herself, and to inform her of the gift she has left behind.Talk to Frithnyd by the fountain in ElthengelsCadsterre can be found with her family at their waggon near the southern gate to Elthengels.

Frithynd has asked you to thank Cadsterre for her kindness and friendship and to wish her good fortune upon her journey.Return to Cadsterre near the gateMundsunu, son of Runald and Cadsterre, waits at the southern entrance to Elthengels.

Like his parents before him, Mundsunu would request a favour of you before leaving his home.Talk with Mundsúnu, son of RunaldBarcyne can be found at her home in the south-east part of the village of Elthengels.

Mundsúnu has asked that you bring his doll to his friend Barcyne.Talk to Barcyne in ElthengelsRunald and his family can be found at their waggon near the southern entrance to Elthengels.

With all of the preparations and favours completed, you should speak to Runald one last time before his departure.Return to Runald to finalize the preparations'You have done much for those who will stay and defend Elthengels. Will you offer the same aid to those who wish to flee? Do not misjudge them for their desire to escape. I lost both husband and son when they sought to protect our home, but they knew the risks of their actions. I would not force a similar fate upon those not willing to take such a risk.

'Reeve Athelward has abandoned us. The people of Elthengels are resolved to repel the invaders through their own determination and strength. I ask that you leave us to this task, with a final request before your departure.

'Runald and his family plan to set for Edoras. Runald was once an advisor to my husband and a great benefactor to the people of Elthengels, but the growing danger here has encouraged him to depart to protect his family. Search for him near the southern gate of town, and aid him with his preparations.''Greetings, ${CLASS}. Time is short for my departure. Will you lend me your aid?

'My family has sought to leave Elthengels for quite some time, yet I leave behind a fine home that should not be put to disuse. Would you speak with wise Hereléof in town and inform him that he may use the building as he sees fit until my kin are one day able to return?''A message from Runald? I understand. I will see his dwelling put to good use.

'Many refugees have come into Elthengels, their homes and farms being burned and occupied by Orcs and Uruks... I will find some trustworthy folk to inhabit and care for the home until Runald may return. His home will be in good hands.''Well done. Hereléof is a good man, and I trust he will find a good caretaker for the house.

'My wife Cadsterre has a favour to ask. Would you please speak with her?''Farewells are such difficult things. I cannot bear to speak with my friends and neighbours for a final time... the sorrow is much too great.

'I wish to give a gift to my dearest friend, Frithnyd. She has always admired my golden necklace, and I wish for her to have it. Would you please tell Frithnyd that I have placed the necklace under the small stone in her yard?

'I hope she understands why I cannot tell her myself... it is simply too difficult.''A fine day to you. Dear Cadsterre sent you? Aye, I knew she was leaving, and I understand her difficulty with saying farewell. My thoughts will be with her on her journey.'

You inform Frithnyd of the hidden gift that has been left to her by Cadsterre.

'I cannot believe that she would leave me such a beloved and beautiful gift! I will be sure to think of her whenever I look upon it. Please, give her my best wishes.''Oh, it makes me feel so much better to hear that Frithnyd enjoyed my gift. It still saddens me to say farewell to my old home and friends, but I can keep them in my mind, and they shall seem less far away.

'We must see to our final preparations here, but my son, Mundsúnu, has asked to speak with you.''Hello there! My name is Mundsúnu. Mother and Father tell me that I must say farewell to Elthengels and all my friends. I have never been so sad. I hope that Edoras is as splendid as all the stories that I have heard, but I will miss my friends.

'Mother says there is no room on our waggon for my toys. You should tell Barcyne to take care of my doll, Captain Pindle. Captain Pindle will get very lonely if no one plays with him, and Barcyne will be a good friend!''A pleasure to meet you, ${PLAYER}. A doll? I fail to understand. You seem rather big to play with dolls....

'Oh, you mean Captain Pindle! That is Mundsúnu's doll! I will take good care of the Captain for him. Tell Mundsúnu to visit when he becomes older, and I will tell him all about my adventures with Captain Pindle.''Thank you for helping us ready our final measures before leaving. Elthengels has been a fine home, but I will feel much safer once we reach the gates of the king's city.

'We will begin our journey west momentarily.'Garmund believes that the men in his éored must act for the sake of the Norcrofts, even against the will of Reeve Athelward. Not all men share his sentiments, and some refuse to disobey the Reeve.Léofcetel can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Garmund has asked you to convince the Rider Léofcetel to join him on his errand.Talk to Léofcetel and convince him to ride along with Captain GarmundCaptain Garmund can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Léofcetel has refused to ride along with Garmund and his men and has asked the Captain to consider the consequences.Talk to Captain Garmund outside ClivingCaptain Garmund can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Captain Garmund wishes to set forth at once, to prevent further destruction to the farms of the crofters of Cliving.Ride with Captain Garmund across the plainsCaptain Garmund can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Garmund wishes to convey thanks after the difficult encounter on the fields outside Cliving.Talk to Captain Garmund'Reeve Athelward has asked us to remain on watch against the Orcs and to ensure the safety of the city. We repelled the single attempted infiltration of Cliving, and in the time since, no Orcs have dared to try again. All the while, the Orcs are free to roam our lands, pillaging and raiding as they see fit.

'I mean no disrespect to Reeve Athelward, but I know that we could greatly benefit were we to ride against the Orcs in the fields. My men all agree, save Léofcetel. Please speak with him and convince him that riding against our foes is the correct course of action.''Garmund oversteps his bounds! We are under direct orders from Reeve Athelward himself; we are to guard the city walls and never to patrol beyond.

'I will not take part in this disobedience. I understand that it is a noble undertaking, but we have our commands, and we must follow them.

'I suggest you inform Garmund that I will be reporting to Athelward the moment he abandons his post. Do not take part in his foolishness, ${PLAYER}.''I cannot fault Léofcetel for his loyalty to the Reeve, but there is more to duty than simply following commands. We have all sworn oaths to protect these lands and the people that live upon them. I wish to uphold those oaths and to bring justice to the trespassers against the plains of Rohan. I will gladly face the consequences, if my deeds preserve even a yard of my homeland.

'I welcome you to ride with us, ${PLAYER}. We intend to strike fear into these brutes and pillagers.''Let us set forth. Upon your steeds, men! Ready yourself, ${PLAYER}!''It weighs heavy upon my heart to see the lands I have sworn to protect in such ruin and abandonment. Slowly may we overcome the numbers of the Orcs and their trail of wreckage, but not if we are shut behind our city walls.

'I thank you for accompanying me, ${PLAYER}. It is no small comfort to know that there are others who know the importance of protecting one's home.'The renowned huntsman Caddabrand is seeking skilled adventurers to become his apprentice and share in his profits.Norcrofts brown bears dwell among the trees and rocks east along the river.

Caddabrand has asked you to collect hides from brown bears to prove that you are capable of more profitable ventures.Collect hides from Norcrofts brown-bears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Caddabrand waits at his encampment, west and south of the river crossing.

You have collected the brown bear-hides sought by the scout Caddabrand.Return to Caddabrand with the brown bear-hides'You stand before the esteemed Caddabrand, renowned tracker, game hunter, and personal scout to Reeve Athelward. I would like to offer you a rare and wonderful opportunity: the chance to become my apprentice! Working for Athelward awards much privilege and prestige... something a modest ${RACE} such as yourself would be hard pressed to find elsewhere. Prove your abilities to me, and there will be profit in this for us all.

'Let us begin with a simple task for an aspiring tracker. The hides of the Norcrofts brown bears can be sold to the nearby merchants for a meaningful sum, but convincing a bear to part with his hide is not without considerable danger. Gather a supply of bear hides and prove that you are more than a mere novice at hunting.''Did something disrupt your hunting? I would have expected the job finished with a bit more haste, but I suppose I cannot expect you to possess the speed of a professional, such as myself.

'Nonetheless, the task is done, and you are still whole and hale. I wager you are ready for a more difficult challenge.'The men of the trader's outpost have noticed a large force of Uruk-hai establishing themselves within the region. These Uruks and their leader have broken away from the main Uruk-army to plan further assaults against Elthengels.The Guard-captain of the trader's outpost can be found outside his tent, within the camp.

Ofrith has asked you to discuss the Uruk-hai presence with the Guard-captain and to inquire as to what should be done.Talk to the Guard-captain in the merchant-campThe Uruk-hai camp lies to far to the east and south beyond the river.

The Guard-captain has asked you to infiltrate the Uruk-hai establishment and to overthrow the commander of the force.Discover the leader of the Uruk-hai camp to the south-eastThe Uruk-hai camp lies to far to the east and south beyond the river.

The Guard-captain has asked you to infiltrate the Uruk-hai encampment and to overthrow the commander of the force.Defeat MauhúrOfrith resides at the trader's outpost, along the road west of Elthengels.

Ofrith awaits at the trader outpost for news of your endeavours within the Uruk-hai camp.Return to Ofrith at the merchant-camp'There is a serious matter that I would care to discuss with you. As you may be aware, several Uruks have been encountered to the south-east across the river. Their existence has been a dangerous strain for our merchant-camp and for the people of Elthengels.

'These Uruks seem coordinated under some unknown leadership, yet I know not the purpose of their presence here. The captain of the camp-guards has been carefully tracking the manoeuvres of this force. I would ask you to speak with him and consider whether a solution to their harassment is possible.''I consider Ofrith wise for his concern. We have tracked the organized Uruk-hai force for a significant length of time. It seems to have broken off from the much larger army that passed east over the plains not long ago. Why these few have returned with haste I cannot say, but they pose a threat to the people of Elthengels while they remain. 

'I must remain behind to protect the merchant-camp; therefore, I shall entrust this task to you. We have scouted a fortified wall topped with White Hand banners to the south-east; the leader of the Uruks is sure to dwell within. He must be dealt with before his plans can be carried out.''Without a strong leader to guide them, the Uruk-hai force will be unable to execute an offensive against us for some time. I find myself indebted to you once again. Thank you, dear ${PLAYER}, for granting us this modest protection.'Ofrith has noticed that one of the men of the trader's outpost has been missing from camp for quite some time. Some have begun to worry that he has become lost or captured by Orcs.Guthléod can be found within the trader's outpost west of Elthengels.

Ofrith has suggested asking Guthléod about Dagarm's last known whereabouts.Talk to Guthléod about Dagarm's whereaboutsDagarm was last known to be heading south and east, beyond the river.

Guthléod believes that Dagarm may have headed beyond the river and towards known enemy territory. He has cautioned you to be careful as you search.Search for the missing trader, DagarmDagarm appears to have been captured by the Uruks in the camp beyond the river.

Dagarm wishes to be released from his captivity within the Uruk-hai camp. You may be able to undo the ropes that bind him.Free Dagarm from captureDagarm has returned to the trader's outpost along the road west of Elthengels.

You have freed Dagarm, and he has asked you to speak with him upon your return to the trader's outpost.Talk to Dagarm at the trader's outpost'One of the merchants in our camp has been missing for some time now. Dagarm is his name, and I have a growing concern that something may have happened to him.

'Dagarm frequently speaks with Guthléod when he is in camp. Perhaps Guthléod may know where he has gone to.''My captors told me that their leader, Mauhúr, would have plans for me. I wager that I only live now because your arrival preceded his.

'My thanks to you. You are the bravest ${RACE} I have ever known, of this I am certain.''Dagarm mentioned that he was heading south-east, beyond the river, to look for some needed supplies. He surely should have returned by now. 

'I told him to be cautious, as it seems the enemy has been hiding all around us. Dagarm may have been caught off guard...if you go looking for him, do not make the same mistake.'Thane Mildrith of Elthengels needs aid in managing the many pressing issues of her people in the wake of the Orc-assaults.Thane Mildrith's people are located within the walls of Elthengels in the eastern section of the Norcrofts.

Thane Mildrith has asked you to aid her people in their tasks of protecting the town from further attack.Help Wuscrad, the garrison commanderHelp Baldthorne, the blacksmithHelp Elfwyn, the healerHelp Adnoth, the stable-handThane Mildrith is found at the Mead Hall of Elthengels.

Thane Mildrith has promised to reward you for helping her people with their various tasks.Return to Thane Mildrith at the Mead Hall of Elthengels'My people are in need of aid, ${PLAYER}. Elthengels has survived a tremendous Orc-assault, but it has come at a great cost. My husband and son have perished, the countryside is ravaged, and many yet lay dead or dying. Without reinforcements from Théoden King or that coward Reeve Athelward, the end of our time here draws ever nearer.

'If you offer me your assistance, I will gladly take it. We must band together, and take aid from wherever it may come.

'The people of Elthengels are now my only concern. Our time for grieving must be brief. I will not let them give in to sorrow or concede defeat while they yet live...but there are many within the walls of this village that are in need of help. If you are able to resolve their troubles, return to me, and I shall see that you are rewarded.''I must commend you for helping me and my people. It is said that in the most trying of times the true worth of a person's character can be seen, and I have seen a great deal of good in these men.

'I am still learning how best to serve Elthengels as Thane. I cannot be the strong warrior that my husband was, nor can I be the fearless vindicator that my dear son Pendulf was, but you have shown me that I have the ability to garner support and to rally the people of this village. This is a most generous gift you have given me.''There are many people who seek guidance and aid. Seek out Elfwyn the healer, Baldthorne the blacksmith, Adnoth the stable-hand, and Wuscrad the garrison commander. They may all be found within the walls of Elthengels.'Many of the Orcs that marched with the main horde moving across Rohan have fallen behind to continue occupying and terrorizing the peoples of the Norcrofts and to spread the influence of the White Hand.The abandoned farms can be found south and west of Cliving.

Many of the mounted Orc-forces have continued to occupy and defile the abandoned farms.Defeat mounted Orcs in the western farmlands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Captain Garmund can be found at the western watch-tower just west of Cliving.

Captain Garmund will be pleased to see your actions dealing with the Orc-invaders.Talk to Captain Garmund outside of ClivingMany of the Orcs that marched with the main horde moving across Rohan have fallen behind to continue occupying and terrorizing the peoples of the Norcrofts and to spread the influence of the White Hand.

Defeating the White Hand's riders will help reduce their stranglehold on the territory.'Knowing that the invaders have fallen to your prowess fills me with a sense of satisfaction. Each Orc slain in the fields delays the chance of their forces amassing to attack the city.

'Keep vigilant, brave ${CLASS}.'The leader of the Orc forces pressing for control of the lands in the western plains of Norcrofts has fortified himself within the ancient Gondorian tower, Minas Rant.Minas Rant is west of Springview Estate, at the edge of the stream that runs across the Norcrofts.

Cyneswith believes that the commander of the Orcs inhabiting the farmsteads has likely established his base within the Gondorian tower of Minas Rant.Infiltrate the Gondorian tower and defeat the leader of the Orc forcesCyneswith awaits at the Springview Estate to the east of Minas Rant.

Cyneswith is eager to hear of your stirke against the commander of the invading Orc forces.Speak to Cyneswith'I have given much thought to the situation, and I believe that the Orc menace may be more coordinated than we had believed. If our foe has been poaching fish from our stream, then surely they have discovered Minas Rant, the ancient Gondorian military tower due west from where you stand. I would wager that the Orcs plan to use it as a stronghold with which to watch over their newly claimed homes, I would stake my life on it!

'It is time for us to claim a measure of retribution. The Orcs assailed my home, and now you must assault theirs. Find whichever brute commands these monsters and strike him down for the sake of all those who have fallen.''Your actions have brought me some peace, for I know that a great measure of justice has been delivered. Once again, you have proven to be a ${CLASS} without equal. We are ever in your debt, friend.'Adnoth, the stable-hand, has asked you to help him with an unruly horse that has recently come into his stable.The unruly horse is waiting at the Elthengels stable in the Norcrofts.

The stable-hand, Adnoth, has asked you to take the unruly horse for a ride, in the hopes that it might relax and slowly become more amenable to handling.Mount the unruly steed and ride until it calms downElthengels' stable-hand, Adnoth, is awaiting your return at the stables near the south-west entrance to Elthengels.

The unruly steed led you to discover the body of its former owner. It has since become calmer, and the stablemaster must be informed of this development.Return to Adnoth and talk to him about your discovery'Hail, there. Have you any skill with horses? I am a bit ashamed to admit this, but I am having trouble with one of the horses in my stable.

'This particular steed raced riderless into town, no more than a day ago. Perhaps he was abandoned and was fleeing from the Orc-raiders? I have taken him into my stable and have orders to prepare all of these steeds for the Elthengels guards... but this new horse is simply too unruly.

'Perhaps if you were to take a ride with him and see where he may take you, it could be just what he needs to relax and become more amenable to our handling.''The steed led you to a body? Aye, I imagine it was the horse's previous Rider. I will ask one of the guards to retrieve the poor fellow, so we can honour his remains.

'This seems to have calmed the steed quite thoroughly. He was trying to let us know about his owner, and we mistook it for unruly behaviour. I will make certain such a loyal steed goes to a most worthy new rider.

'Thank you for your help.'Caddabrand places value in animal-hides that can be sold to merchants and traders. Fine wolf-pelts should fetch a fair price and would likely be exactly the sort of thing that Caddabrand would offer a reward for.Norcrofts pack-wolves reside in and around Âmbal's den, west of Caddabrand's camp.

Collecting the fine hides of pack-wolves is sure to be rewarded by the opportunistic Caddabrand.Collect pelts from Norcrofts pack-wolvesNorcrofts pack-wolves reside in and around Âmbal's den, west of Caddabrand's camp.

Collecting the fine hides of pack-wolves is sure to be rewarded by the opportunistic Caddabrand.Collect pelts from Norcrofts pack-wolves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Caddabrand's camp lies east of Âmbal's den, a ways south of the river-crossing.

Caddabrand should be pleased to see the initiative you have taken in collecting him these tradeable wolf-hides.Bring the wolf-pelts to CaddabrandCaddabrand places value in animal-hides that can be sold to merchants and traders. Fine wolf-pelts should fetch a fair price and would likely be exactly the sort of thing that Caddabrand would offer a reward for.'These pack-wolf pelts should fetch a fine price at market. You are quickly proving to be more capable than you at first appear to be! 

'I certainly could have gathered these pelts myself, had I not more important tasks at hand. Yet I do feel this warrants a suitable reward. Fine thinking, on your part.'Captain Garmund is distressed by the presence of the many Orc-banners and totems being strewn across the once-peaceful farmlands outside of Cliving.Orc-totems and banners can be found around the farmsteads south of Cliving and west of the road.

Captain Garmund has asked you to destroy many of the banners and totems the Orcs have placed across their sacked territory.Destroy Orc-totems at the western farmlands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy Orc-banners at the western farmlands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Captain Garmund can be found at the western watch-tower, just west of Cliving.

Garmund awaits the results of your attempt to destroy the Orc symbols marking the precious farmlands beyond Cliving.Talk to Captain Garmund outside of Cliving'Hail, ${CLASS}. Take care as you travel in these lands. The vacant farms south-west of the city have fallen under the control of White Hand Orcs, their previous residents fleeing to Edoras or seeking sanctuary within the walls of Cliving.

'The Orcs have taken it upon themselves to mark our lands with their various brands and symbols. It may not matter to most, but I cannot bear to see our homelands so crudely desecrated. If it is not much trouble, I request that any Orc-banners or totems that you come across be destroyed. I shall see that you are compensated generously for your efforts.''I suspect that seeing the banners and totems fall disturbs the Orc-invaders as greatly as it has disturbed me to see them erected on our land.

'I praise your convictions, ${PLAYER}. Few have the bravery to risk undertaking such a task. I am pleased to reward you, as promised.'Smoke and the sounds of battle have carried to the trader's outpost from the west. Ofrith worries for the safety of the men still dwelling in the region.The farmhouses in peril are south-west of the trader's outpost.

Ofrith has asked you to carefully search among the farmhouses to the west for signs of any remaining farmers who may be in danger.Travel towards the farmhouses and search for any in need of aidThe farmhouses in peril are south-west of the trader's outpost.

You have discovered a man at one of the large farmhouse estates who seems to be in need of your aid.Talk to the frightened man'During your time away from camp, all could hear the sounds of destruction carrying from the west, and this put the guards on alert. It is known that the Orcs burned and pillaged much of the land as their armies marched westward, yet naught since had we heard the roar of carnage so close.

'We know not who may still dwell in the remaining homes and farms west of the road, but they are certainly in grave danger once more. I believe that a worthy ${CLASS}, such as yourself, ought to lend aid to any there in need.''Who approaches? You are unfamiliar to me, ${RACE}, but please, you must help me! Oh, what strength can you offer?'Mounted Orcs have been threatening the people of the Norcrofts for some time. Thinning their number would provide a great benefit for those who have remained in Elthengels, as well as bring vengeance for those who have already fallen.Orc-riders can be found in the fields north and west of Elthengels in the Norcrofts.

The mounted Orcs around Elthengels have been responsible for many casualties and much suffering.Defeat mounted Orcs around Elthengels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The people of Elthengels will greatly benefit from having fewer Orc-riders to harass them.CompletedMounted Orcs have been threatening the people of the Norcrofts for some time. Thinning their number would provide a great benefit for those who have remained in Elthengels, as well as bring vengeance for those who have already fallen.The people of Elthengels will greatly benefit from having fewer Orc-riders attacking those beyond the walls of the town.A Rohirrim helmet once belonging to the former Thane Pendrad has been recovered in the fields of Orcs near Elthengels. Pendrath's shield and sword may be found in the possession of Orc-scavengers in the area.The missing items from Thane Pendrad can likely be found among the fields of Orcs to the north and west of Elthengels.

You have found the late Thane Pendrad's helmet and are now looking for other lost weapons or armour that may have belonged to him.Retrieve Pendrad's shieldRetrieve Pendrad's swordThane Mildrith awaits you at the Elthengels Mead Hall, on the eastern edge of the Norcrofts.

You have recovered the lost items of the late Thane Pendrad and should return them to his widow, Thane Mildrith.Return Pendrad's belongings to Thane MildrithThis helmet bears the insignia of Pendrad, the former Thane of Elthengels. Many of the belongings of the deceased Thane have been scavenged and scattered by the Orc raiders. It might be comforting for Thane Mildrith if you could recover some of her late husband's belongings and return them to her.'You have something for me? Yes, I recognize these, they belonged to my husband. This sword was passed down to Pendrad from his father. I weep at the thought that he will never be able to pass it on to his own son.

'I miss both Pendrad and Pendulf dearly. Thank you for bringing these things to me. I will put them on display in my hall to preserve their memory for the people of Elthengels.'Nothwulf has been disturbed by the crofter's reports and sightings of strange creatures lurking in the woods east of Faldham.Sahard resides at his farm just east of the Faldham mead hall.

Nothwulf believes that Sahard, an elder of Faldham, may be able to shed light on the reports of mysterious beasts seen to the east.Speak to Sahard about the strange creaturesSahard resides at his farm just east of the Faldham mead hall.

Sahard wishes to discuss his plan with you.Speak to SahardThe láthbears seem to reside among the trees to the east of Sahard's farm.

Sahard has given you food to be used as bait for luring out láthbears, so that you might take a closer look.Lure láthbears with bait and defeat any that appear (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Inhere has been watching the láthbears at the northern edge of the woods.

Sahard has asked you to seek out the Rohirrim, Inhere, to see what he may have discovered.Speak to Inhere at the northern edge of the trees'Greetings, ${PLAYER}. My concern lies with a growing threat from the wooded area to the east. The crofters tell tales of strange, monstrous creatures seen lurking in the shadows.

'Of late, a drunken farmer was attacked while pacing the woods at night. Stories of the beast that came upon him have many worried.

'Speak with the elder, Sahard, about this matter. He is wise, and has been studying these events with some earnest.''Welcome, visitor. What questions have you for old Sahard?

'What I know of the beasts you seek is collected from traditional folk-tales; stories told by wives to scare children into good behaviour, or to keep fools from wandering the woods at night. Listen closely and I will recall the truth of the matter.''You must inspect these beasts for yourself. Take this meat and set it before the creatures of the woods. I know not if this will lure a láthbear, but if you manage a closer inspection be ready to defend yourself from their wrath.

'Inhere, a guard of Faldham, has gone east to explore the edge of the woods not long before you. When you are satisfied with your inspection, seek out Inhere to discover what he may have learned.''You handled those láthbears quite well. It seems that Sahard was correct. The myths of old are based in truth, but why have these creatures suddenly returned? Something must have roused them from their slumber, and now they are eagerly seeking living prey.

'Thank you for aiding in investigating our foe. Rest a moment and allow me to consider what must be done.'Feeling satisfied with the results of her training, Ides has asked you to return with a message to her father, Athelward. Reeve Athelward's attention is drawn south to another potential problem.Reeve Athelward resides in his Mead Hall at the east edge of Cliving.

Ides has asked you to return to Cliving to thank her father for his support and so that she may continue on her training without your mentoring.Talk to Reeve Athelward at the Mead Hall of ClivingThane Elfmar resides at the Mead Hall of Faldham, the southernmost town of the Norcrofts.

Athelward wishes for you deliver a message: that Elfmar's Riders are to report to the city of Cliving for duty to the Reeve.Talk to Thane Elfmar at the Mead Hall of Faldham'Thank you, for all that you have done, ${PLAYER}. I could not have come this far without you, but I must now prove my skills through my own deeds. 

'Return to my father and inform him that I am thankful for his encouragment and his aid. When next we speak, I hope to be a true sentinel of the Norcrofts.''I am grateful to hear that my daughter is enjoying her training. It seems you have a rather unique way of helping others, no matter the task.

'Your timing is uncanny, ${PLAYER}; I have another mission for you. I need a message delivered to Thane Elfmar, son of Elfhelm, at the town of Faldham to the south.

'I have given orders for all capable Riders to report to Cliving for duty, yet few men have arrived from Elfmar's lands. Elfmar may stay, if he so desires, but his Riders have orders to seek me with all haste.''Welcome. I am Elfmar, son of Elfhelm, Thane of Faldham. It is a pleasure to make your acquaintance, ${PLAYER}.

'My men have heard the summons of Reeve Athelward, but have refused to answer his call. My scouts believe that the lands of Faldham are on the precipice of being besieged by Orcs from the north, awaiting the same fate as many in Elthengels have already endured.

'My most skilled Riders have chosen to stay and defend these lands. I will not force them to abandon the people of Faldham simply out of loyalty to the Reeve, against their own will.'Edwulf has abandoned the idea of helping Ides train. You have the opportunity to take his place as Ides's mentor, but not until your talents have earned her respect.Ides can be found training at the mill, north of the Rohirrim training grounds, at the northern edge of the Norcrofts.

Edwulf has given you control of training Ides, but has counselled you about her headstrong nature.Talk to IdesOrcs can be found throughout the Norcrofts.

Ides has tasked you with dispatching Orcs in a timely fashion, in order to prove yourself to her.Defeat Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Do not allow the timer to expireIdes can be found training at the mill, north of the Rohirrim training grounds, at the northern edge of the Norcrofts.

You have surpassed the challenge set forth by Ides, and she awaits your return.Return to Ides at the training ground'Ides is quick to learn fighting techniques, but she lacks size and strength. Training is one matter, but she has absurd ideas of charging alongside the men onto a real battlefield.

'Since you seem to be quite eager to help her, I am going to relinquish her training duties to you. Athelward can speak to you when she gets herself killed... it will be none of my concern. Go speak with her at the mill to the north, above the ridge.''I have great respect for Edwulf's prowess in combat, but as a mentor, he leaves much to be desired.

'You have been sent to take his place? You would not have been chosen were you without skill, but you have yet to prove yourself worthy of my favour. I shall have you know that I intend to become a shield-maiden of great renown, and that I take my training quite seriously.

'Allow me to see how quickly you can dispatch of Orcs. Impress me, and I will eagerly follow your guidance.''That was rather impressive. Even that braggart Edwulf would be pressed to achieve such a hasty feat!

'Very well, I shall heed your counsel. Having skill and strength will not be enough. I must learn to fight with courage and honour if I am to live up to my father's reputation.'Inhild's distress over Randgar has given her an opportunity to visit his farm and to say farewell to her lost love.Hwitmon resides at his estate, just east of the central fountain in Cliving, with his daughter.

Inhild would like her father's permission to visit Randgar's farm one last time to see what yet remains and to say farewell.Convince Hwitmon to allow Inhild to visit Randgar's farmRandgar's farm lies south of Cliving, along the road to the east.

Inhild has been allowed to visit Randgar's farm in order to say farewell to the man she loved.Escort Inhild to Randgar's farm to say good-byeHwitmon resides at his estate, just east of the central fountain in Cliving.

Inhild and Randgar have asked that Hwitmon not be told the truth, as he would be happiest to believe that his daughter is with a wealthy man in Edoras.Speak to Hwitmon'Why have you brought me Randgar's cloak? Whatever concerns you, please, speak your mind.'

You describe to Inhild the scene you witnessed when you returned to Randgar's farm. She begins to quietly sob.

'Oh, no, it cannot be true. Poor Randgar, my love. Why did he not flee? I begged him not to stay on my behalf. I know that he stayed so that we might be together, but it is now doubtful that I will ever look upon him again.

'${PLAYER}, I must ask you for one final favour. You must take me to Randgar's farm, so that I may bid good-bye to him in person. I will know no peace until I see these things for myself. Please, speak with my father inside our home. Ask that he allow you to escort me to Randgar's farmstead.''Inhild yet wastes her thoughts on that foolish Randgar? He is but a worthless farm-boy! A woman of her standing should be courted by a wealthy noble or brave warrior.'

You explain to Hwitmon the events at Randgar's farm and request to escort Inhild there, so that she may know peace.

'I shed no tears for Randgar... his fate is a result of his own foolish actions. I will grant Inhild my blessing to leave if it helps her to forget about her silly affections, but you must promise to keep her safe.''Let us go out at once, ${PLAYER}, before my father changes his mind on this matter. Lead the way.''You return. I trust that Inhild has returned safely as well? Stay a moment and share with me your visit to the belated Randgar's home.'Caddabrand has tracked a notorious wolf-matron to her den. He believes that taking a trophy from the beast will increase both profit and notoriety for the ones to complete the task.Âmbal's den can be found west of Caddbrand's camp.

Caddabrand is hoping that by defeating the infamous matron, Âmbal, that you might gain fame and fortune, along with Caddabrand.Defeat Âmbal and collect a trophy from herCaddabrand's camp lies to the east of Âmbal's den.

Caddabrand awaits your return with the trophy at his camp.Return the trophy to Caddabrand'In order to truly make a name for yourself, you will need to go after a much more noteworthy kill... and I just happen to know of such prey: Âmbal the Wolf-mother. I have tracked her trail to the warrens west of here, but I will leave the rest to you.

'Âmbal and her offspring have terrorized farmers and lone travellers for years. Were you to defeat her, you would surely gain recognition even among the nobles of Cliving.

'Just by chance, I know someone who would pay dearly to have Âmbal mounted above his fireplace. Please bring back her head as a trophy... undamaged, if it is not much trouble.''This is a remarkable trophy! Your work speaks for itself, and you are indeed worthy of my trust. When I deliver this to my charitable associates, I shall proudly exclaim how the terror Âmbal was bravely bested by ${PLAYER}, Caddabrand's apprentice!

'I am happy to give you this reward for the small part you played in removing this foe to many.'Crofter Déorun, has begun arrangements in anticipation for the expected attack on Faldham. In order to protect his home and family, he is in need of several items he has lent to his neighbours over the course of time.Déorun's neighbours can be found within Faldham.

Déorun has need of the items he has lent to the other people living around Faldham, including Haligmar, Enswith, Thurgyr, and Osin.Collect Déorun's loaned hammerCollect Déorun's loaned hay-forkCollect Déorun's loaned lanternCollect Déorun's loaned hunting bowDéorun resides at his home at the southern end of Faldham, just oustide the mead hall.

You have collected Déorun's many missing items, and may now return them to the anxious farmer.Return the items to Déorun'It is said that a farmer is only as effective as his tools, and I am sorely lacking many of the tools which I now require. I have need of many of the tools I have lent to my friends and neighbours, I forsee them being invaluable in the coming days.

'Might you gather up many of the items I have loaned out, and return them to me? In particular, I am seeking my hammer, hay-fork, lantern, and an old bow. Speak to the other crofters around Faldham, and inquire about the items. These tools will be put to good use when the siege begins.''Déorun's hammer was loaned to me, but I cannot recall where I have placed it.

'Ah yes, I remember now. I was using it on the barrels by the stable. I must thank Déorun for his generosity.''Speaking with my neighbours may help you to track the loaned items. In particular, I would recommend speaking to Haligmar, Thurgyr, Osin, and Enswith. You should find them all about Faldham, busy with their own tasks.''I would be happy to return Déorun's hay-fork, I appreciate his loaning it to me.

'I last used it at Sahard's stable. If you are looking for it, I would start there.''Déorun loaned my husband his lantern, as a favour. You are more than welcome to take it, I last saw it laying near the windmill.''You are aiding Déorun, ${PLAYER}? You have reminded me, I am in possession of Déorun's hunting bow. Might you take it to him?

'I hope he enjoys the improvements to it, the grip is more secure and the limbs should have a good flex to them. I was tuning it by the stables, just outside the mead hall.''Thank you for returning all of my loaned items. My tools will be put to good use when the time is right, and the assailants will learn the folly of underestimating a prepared crofter!'Giselmon has purchased large quantities of food in order to host a banquet, in the hopes of increasing her popularity. Isenyd has taken exception to this cause and has demanded that the food be donated to the refugee farmers who have been victims of the Orc-attacks.Giselmon can be found at her estate in the western section of Cliving.

Isenyd has asked you to convince Giselmon that hosting a banquet in times of great turmoil would be distasteful and cause her to lose popularity.Convince Giselmon to donate food to the refugeesThe vendors can be found at their individual stands in the marketplace, west of the central fountain in Cliving.

Giselmon has asked you to speak with the food-vendors in Cliving, in order to divert a large portion of her purchases to help those in need.Talk to the butcher of ClivingTalk to the cheese-maker of ClivingTalk to the baker of ClivingGiselmon can be found at her estate in the western section of Cliving.

You may inform Giselmon that the vendors were pleased to help her send portions of her orders to be donated to the needy.Talk to Giselmon in Cliving'Greetings, my name is Isenyd. My home is here in Cliving, but many of the people who now occupy our city are wayward farmers who have been forced from their homes and have taken refuge within the city walls. I have asked my neighbours to donate food and shelter to those who have fled and left behind their belongings.

'Many have given to the cause, but Giselmon is ignoring my pleas. She plans to host a large feast in honour of Reeve Athelward, an action she hopes will increase her own renown, as she values her opinion in others above all else. There are people in dire need of rations and she intends to foolishly host banquets for the wealthy.

'Giselmon refuses to listen to me. She believes that I am jealous of her high esteem, and considers my request as a tactic to undermine her affairs. Perhaps you might sway her decision. Suggest to her that the nobles would view a banquet to be distasteful, and that a charitable donation would be viewed more favourably among her peers.''May I help you? I am busy with preparations for an important banquet.'

You attempt to convince Giselmon that giving to charity will increase her popularity among the nobles, more so than her feast.

'Well, I had not considered this. Oh dear, you are quite right. When others speak of me, they should be commenting on my kindness and generosity! Time is short... will you help me? Please speak to the butcher, cheese-maker, and baker of Cliving. Ask them to give much of the food intended for the banquet to Isenyd's collection instead.''More orders from Giselmon?'

You inform the butcher that Giselmon would like to donate a portion of her original purchase to the refugees.

'If she wishes to be charitable, I would be glad to help her. I will set aside a nice lot of fresh meat to be collected for that poor lot of farmers.''The finest cheese in all of the Norcrofts for sale here! May I help you?'

You explain that Giselmon wishes to donate a portion of food to charity.

'Giselmon has had a change of heart? What a kind woman! I will be sure to send a portion of fine cheeses to be donated on her behalf.''Welcome! Have you come for something fresh? I have a lot of good things for sale, stranger! Oh, you are here about Giselmon's order? Yes, I am working hastily on assembling everything.'

You explain that Giselmon has asked for a portion of her order be sent as charity to the poor.

'That is quite noble of her... I will have it done right away!''Thank you for the thoughtful suggestion, ${PLAYER}. You have saved me from making a terrible mistake. I have heard nothing but praise and admiration for my charitable efforts! I would not have guessed that such acts would be noticed and appreciated by others.

'Please accept this as a token of my appreciation.'Walnoth has decided that the time has come to apprehend the person responsible for Cliving's crimes. That person could be Eldora, the cook's assistant.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Only ONE suspect can be apprehended for these crimes. Accepting and completing this quest will not allow you to apprehend Swithorne or Wigfugol.</rgb>Walnoth will be waiting for you inside the dungeon, under the Mead Hall of Cliving.

Walnoth has gone to apprehend Eldora and will be taking her to the dungeon for holding until her crimes can be fully proven.Talk to Walnoth in the Mead Hall dungeon'So you believe Eldora to be the culprit? It is a shame to think that such a sweet girl could be responsible, but I understand your concerns. There were no witnesses to her whereabouts at the time of Malbéort's murder, and she had direct access to the kitchen where the poison phial was discovered.

'I trust your decision on this matter. I shall go now to apprehend Eldora. Let us reconvene inside the dungeon's entrance, under the Mead Hall.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Only ONE suspect can be apprehended for these crimes. Accepting and completing this quest will not allow you to apprehend Swithorne or Wigfugol.</rgb>'Eldora has admitted to nothing, but has come along quietly nonetheless. We will hold her here until more evidence can be gathered to either confirm her guilt or prove her innocence. Someone will be sent to take care of the injured soldier who was in her care.

'This is a most reasonable decision, ${PLAYER}. Eldora's lack of supporting witnesses and access to the kitchen make her the most likely fit for these crimes. Her treacherous schemes will end here.''Despite your decision, I maintain my innocence, ${PLAYER}. Athelward will not allow a suspect to remain captive for long when no solid evidence can be gathered against them. Perhaps, in time, Walnoth will see the error in this hasty decision, and the true culprit will be revealed. The truth will be my ally.

'I hope that Fréastan is well cared for until this matter is sorted out. His injuries will need proper treatment.'Walnoth has decided that the time has come to apprehend the person responsible for Cliving's crimes. The evidence against Swithorne paints him as the most likely culprit.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Only ONE suspect can be apprehended for these crimes. Accepting and completing this quest will not allow you to apprehend Eldora or Wigfugol.</rgb>Walnoth will be waiting for you inside the dungeon, under the Mead Hall of Cliving.

Walnoth has gone to apprehend Swithorne and will hold him at the dungeon until the matter can be fully resolved.Talk to Walnoth in the Mead Hall dungeon'So you believe Swithorne to be the culprit? There is good reason to suspect him. Being one of Théodmar's Riders, he would have had access to the kitchen without arousing suspicion. His brother's death can be directly attributed to Athelward's orders to withdraw from Elthengels, and perhaps this motive has fuelled his attempt to poison the Reeve.

'I trust your decision on this matter. I shall go to apprehend Swithorne now...meet us at the entrance to the dungeon, under the Mead Hall.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Only ONE suspect can be apprehended for these crimes. Accepting and completing this quest will not allow you to apprehend Eldora or Wigfugol.</rgb>'Swithorne came peacefully with me, when I explained things to him. We will hold Swithorne here as we gather more evidence...or until we receive his confession.

'I believe you have made a wise decision, ${PLAYER}. Swithorne's motive and access to the kitchens make him a logical choice. No further harm shall come to the people of Cliving at his hands.''I am not the one that should be held behind these bars, ${PLAYER}. It was a costly mistake to allow my dagger to slip from my possesion, but never would I use it against any of the people in Cliving...the very people I have sworn to protect.

'I ask that you remember all that has transpired, and all that has been revealed in your search for the truth. I will wager that the criminal will act again, in time, and that justice will be upheld.'Walnoth has decided that the time has come to apprehend the person responsible for Cliving's crimes. The evidence indicates that Wigfugol may be the man responsible.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Only ONE suspect can be apprehended for these crimes. Accepting and completing this quest will not allow you to apprehend Eldora or Swithorne.</rgb>Walnoth will be waiting for you inside the dungeon, under the Mead Hall of Cliving.

Walnoth has gone to apprehend Wigfugol and will be taking him to the dungeon for holding until the crimes can be fully resolved.Talk to Walnoth in the Mead Hall dungeon'So you believe Wigfugol to be the culprit? Yes, it cannot be understated that his grudge against Athelward gives him the strongest motive. Living alone at his estate, he has no witnesses for his whereabouts during these crimes. It takes little imagination to see how Wigfugol was the one responsible for this atrocity.

'I trust your decision on this matter. I shall go now to apprehend Wigfugol and take him to a cell in the dungeon. Meet me inside the dungeon, under the Mead Hall.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Only ONE suspect can be apprehended for these crimes. Accepting and completing this quest will not allow you to apprehend Eldora or Swithorne.</rgb>'I am ashamed to be so disrespected by a simpleton guard and his outsider lackey. I understand that you are only completing the task that has been asked of you, but I wager that you will discover that the true villain remains free. Keep your wits about you if you remain in the city.

'If disapproving of the Reeve's actions is my only crime, you will find that there are others who deserve to be locked up with me.''Wigfugol protested when I came for him, but eventually he relented and came along willingly. We will hold him here as we gather more evidence to prevent him from causing further harm.

'Thinking on it now, I believe this was the best decision, ${PLAYER}. Wigfugol's disdain for the Reeve led him to this scheme of deception and murder. Athelward's life will be safer with Wigfugol locked away.'Reeve Athelward has tasked Caddabrand with eliminating an Uruk-hai captain and has promised a large bounty for the task. Caddabrand is eager to split the reward with someone capable of doing the dirty work.The Uruk-hideout is south-west of Caddabrand's camp, beyond the entrance to the wolf-den.

Caddabrand has tracked the Uruk, Zúrsnaga, to a cave south-west of the wolf den.Enter the cave where Zúrsnaga is hidingThe Uruk-hideout is south-west of Caddabrand's camp, beyond the entrance to the wolf-den. Zúrsnaga lies hidden within the caves.

Caddabrand has asked you to find and defeat the Uruk, Zúrsnaga.Find and defeat Zúrsnaga the UrukCaddabrand's camp lies east of the Uruk-hai hideout and the wolf-den.

Caddabrand will be eager to hear the news that Zúrsnaga has been defeated.Return to Caddabrand with news of Zúrsnaga's demise'Perhaps you are curious as to why I am encamped here and not hunting Orcs near Cliving? Allow me to explain, my dear apprentice. You see, I am on a mission from Reeve Athelward himself. He has placed a rather large bounty on the head of an Uruk named Zúrsnaga, and I plan to collect on it.

'Zúrsnaga and his company unwittingly attacked the gates of Cliving in an attempt to infiltrate the city. Our Riders destroyed the attacking force and sent Zúrsnaga fleeing south. Athelward was outraged by this attack and has demanded the Uruk be slain at any cost.

'I have reason to believe that Zúrsnaga has been hiding in a cave to the south-west beyond the wolf-warrens. You have proven yourself rather skilled... I would not be opposed to letting you handle the Uruk, if you are interested in sharing a portion of the bounty reward with me.''Zúrsnaga is no more? Athelward will certainly be pleased to know this. I know not the meaning of Zúrsnaga's words, but the ramblings of a deranged Uruk are of little concern to me.

'I cannot convey how fortunate I am to have an apprentice so quick to learn! Perhaps with enough training, you could even aspire to become as skilled as myself.'Thurgyr has observed that many of the farmsteads around Faldham will be easy to breach for the attacking armies, even with locked doors. He has begun collecting planks of wood for barricading the windows of the homes.The wood pile can be found outside of Thurgyr's home.

Thurgyr has asked you to collect planks from the wood pile, in order to help barricade the windows of homes.Collect sturdy planks from a wood pileThe unprotected crofter homes can be found all around the town of Faldham.

Thurgyr has asked you to place boards across the windows of the unprotected crofter homes in Faldham.Board up windows on farmstead homes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thurgyr can be found at his home at the southern edge of Faldham, just outside the mead hall area.

Thurgyr should be pleased to hear that you have helped to protect the homes and families of his neighbours, in preparation for the siege.Speak to Thurgyr about the boarded windows'Hail to you, ${CLASS}. I imagine you are familiar with siege tactics, defensive positioning, and the like. Do our farmsteads appear ready to withstand an enemy's charge? I believe that more must be done to protect our homes.

'I beg of you, assist us in boarding the openings. A well boarded window will make it difficult for an Orc to penetrate or to easily peer inside to look for prey. Use the planks I have cut to cover the windows of unprotected homes.''Our homes are now much more difficult to infiltrate, a welcome trait once the fighting begins. Thank you for the assistance. If our advanced efforts have saved the life of even a single crofter than they were worthwhile!'The Uruk-hai rebels have stored their equipment throughout a cave they have inhabited in the Norcrofts. The weapons have been casually strewn about, and could be removed before they are weilded by their owners.The Uruk-hai rebels are located in a cave, west of Caddabrand's camp.

The Uruk weapons have been carelessly left throughout the cave, leaving them ripe for removal.Take Uruk-hai swords from the caveThe Uruk-hai rebels are located in a cave, west of Caddabrand's camp.

The Uruk weapons have been carelessly left throughout the cave, leaving them ripe for removal.Take Uruk-hai swords from the cave (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have removed several of the Uruk-hai weapons from the possession of the enemy.The Uruk-hai rebels have carelessly left their weapons strewn about the cave they have inhabited. Removing the weapons from the cavern would make things much more difficult for the enemy force.You have removed the weapons of a great many Uruk-hai, making it improbable for the forces here to ever mount an attack against the Rohirrim.Elfmar is trying to prepare the men of Faldham to stand against the Orcs crossing Rohan, and wants your help to make them ready.Elfmar is at the Mead Hall in Faldham.

Elfmar would like you to train his men in combat, and also wishes to speak with you about your travels.Talk to Elfmar and help to train his men in the defence of FaldhamElfmar is in the Mead Hall of Faldham.

You have helped to train many of Elfmar's men, and should now speak with him again.Talk to Elfmar in the Mead Hall of Faldham'I have known Thane Mildrith since I was a boy. She was not thane then, of course. It is a great shame what happened to her husband and to her son, but I am not attributing her words solely to grief. Reeve Athelward is many things, and I would never say he lacked courage, but the simple fact is that Cliving is far away. He worries first about Cliving, and second about the other towns within his riding. I cannot say I would do otherwise.'

You tell Elfmar that you spoke with his father Elfhelm at the Fords of Isen.

'I am glad to hear he still lives, ${PLAYER}! I would like to hear more of your account! While my father is away, I am trying to rule in his place as best I can. You have seen battle. Would you like to train with my men? We can discuss the state of things in Rohan, and you can tell me more of what you have seen beyond the borders.''Thank you very much, ${PLAYER}. There is something solid and reassuring about a good bout of sparring, and I know my men will be pleased to have had the opportunity when the time comes to defend their homes or those of their neighbours.''The field just outside the Mead Hall serves as the training area for my men. Will you help train them? I would be pleased to see what you can do!'Thane Mildrith refuses to either trust or marry Athelward, and wants to ensure that the young Elfmar of Faldham is not relying on Athelward.The road runs west of Elthengels for a good way before turning south. At a certain point the southward road crosses the river before it reaches Faldham.

Thane Mildrith wants you to travel to Faldham and collect emblems from Orcs in the Norcrofts to show to Elfmar.Collect Orc Emblems from the Orcs of the Norcrofts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Scout the river crossing south-west of ElthengelsElfmar is the acting thane of Faldham while his father Elfhelm is away.

Thane Mildrith wants you to tell Elfmar of the dangers that face the Norcrofts.'If Siflád says she is being treated well, I will keep my complaints to myself. Thank you for easing the worry in a mother's heart. My daughter is strong-willed, and I trust that she will have the sense to send word if her situation changes to ill.

'Turning now to the well-being of my people, I find clarity: we must not rely on the Reeve to protect us. I do not trust him, but more so: I feel in my heart he will see first to the defence of Cliving and not leave his walls to protect the rest of us. The other towns of the Norcrofts do not have Cliving's natural defences.

'You have been a good help to me, but I know you are eager to progress westward in pursuit of the Uruks you seek. Go south-west to Faldham by way of the river crossing, to scout and see if the crossing is held by foes. Also, I ask that you collect emblems from any Orcs you find in the Norcrofts. Show those emblems to Elfhelm, the Thane of Faldham, as proof that we must stand on our own and not rely on Athelward.

'Oh, wait! Elfhelm has gone to the Fords of Isen. His son, Elfmar, must have rule of Faldham in his absence. Show him the emblems.''I do not trust the Reeve. Travel south-west to the river crossing and seize emblems from any Orcs you encounter in the Norcrofts. Bring those emblems to Elfmar and tell him the danger we face is all too real, and we must not rely on Reeve Athelward to protect us.''Welcome to Faldham, friends. These are not the first such emblems I have seen. I know the Orcs move across the Norcrofts in great numbers. I am trying to do the best I can to combat them while my father is away at the Fords of Isen.'With Caddabrand's main goal completed, all that is left to be done is to collect each share of the reward.Cliving, the capital city of the Norcrofts, lies far to the north, beyond the trader's outpost along the road.

Caddabrand has informed you that your share of the bounty may be collected from the warrior Frithelm, in Cliving.Talk to Frithelm in Cliving'Your deeds have truly impressed me, ${PLAYER}. I intend to remain here a while longer, as I have some business with the merchants to the north... but you should be heading towards the capital!

'Have you forgotten the bounty we discussed? One of Athelward's men should be able to see to your payment. Travel north along the road into Cliving and seek out Frithelm. Mention that you have assisted the one and only Caddabrand with Athelward's Uruk troubles, and he will see to your share of the reward.''Greetings. What brings you to Cliving?

'Caddabrand? That braggart... I wager he convinced you to finish his tasks? The only thing greater than his sense of self-pride is his desire to profit from the labour of others.

'Here is your share... it is doubtless that you deserve it and more.'Athelward's beloved daughter, Ides, has aspirations of becoming a brave warrior, much like her father. Athelward wishes for Ides to be happy and has agreed to provide her with suitable training.Edwulf can be found at the Rohirrim training grounds, east of Cliving. The camp is a converted farmstead, hidden in a vale of trees north of the road.

Reeve Athelward has asked you to speak with Edwulf, the man in charge of instructing his daughter, Ides, in the martial arts.Talk to Edwulf at the Rohirrim training grounds'I have a personal matter that I feel can be entrusted to you. The second eldest of my daughters, Ides, has aspirations of becoming a shield-maiden, following in the reputation of her father as a great warrior. I love Ides dearly and wish her to be happy, so I have arranged for her to receive training in preparation for becoming a shield-maiden.

'I have seen your experience and talent when it comes to matters of prowess. Would you be willing to mentor Ides, oversee her training, and ensure that she is happy and free from danger? If you are able to assist her, please speak with her trainer, Edwulf. You will find him at the secluded training grounds east of Cliving, north of the vale of trees along the road.''The training grounds of the Eorlingas are no place for a young woman, such as Ides. When she becomes injured -- or possibly worse -- it will be my head that Reeve Athelward will be after! 

'If you are here to help keep her safe, then I welcome your aid, but please refrain from encouraging her foolish ambitions of becoming a shield-maiden. If the Norcrofts is to be saved, it will not be by foolhardy women.'Ides wishes to speak with you about her future as a shield-maiden and living up to her father's legacy.Ides can be found at the mill, north of the Rohirrim training grounds, at the northern edge of the Norcrofts.

Ides has important matters on her mind that she wishes to discuss with you.Allow Ides to discuss her ambitions with youIdes can be found at the mill, north of the Rohirrim training grounds, at the northern edge of the Norcrofts.

Ides wishes to speak with you regarding the events at the training grounds.Talk to Ides'I must admit that your mentoring has been invaluable, ${PLAYER}. I have learned much simply from observing you, and fighting alongside you. I know that my determination oft causes trouble for myself and for others, and I appreciate your willingness to stand by me, despite this.

'The other squires never treated me as an equal, so it is good to have someone to speak with. If you are not opposed, I have other thoughts which I would like to discuss with you.''If you are ready, I wish to discuss my thoughts on the state of things.''I am speechless, ${PLAYER}. We were able to repel the invaders and save the others. The men have begun to recognize my talents and treat me on equal footing... I can ask for no more.

'Never have I been so sure that becoming a shield-maiden is what I am meant for. I have earned great confidence in my abilities, thanks to you.'The láthbears have become disturbed by your actions in their territory and have become more active in seeking intruders. The disturbed láthbears must be defeated before they turn their aggressions towards the crofters on the outskirts of Faldham.The disturbed láthbears can be found in the woods east of Faldham.

The disturbed láthbears have grown more aggressive and must be defeated before they can expand towards the village of Faldham.Defeat the disturbed láthbears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The disturbed láthbears were unable to harm the people of Faldham in their aggression, due to your efforts in defeating them.CompletedThe láthbears have become disturbed by your actions in their territory and have become more active in seeking intruders. The disturbed láthbears must be defeated before they turn their aggressions towards the crofters on the outskirts of Faldham.The disturbed láthbears were unable to injure any of the people of Faldham in their aggression, due to your efforts to defeat them.Athelward worries for the safety of his people; many have ignored his orders to abandoned their homes and to seek shelter within Cliving.The crofters can be found upon the plains and farmsteads south of the city of Cliving.

The crofters need to be reassured that Reeve Athelward's orders must be followed, for their own safety.Reeve Athelward resides at the Mead Hall at the east end of the city of Cliving.

You have convinced several crofters to evacuate their homes and seek refuge from the White Hand invaders.Talk to Reeve Athelward at the Mead Hall of Cliving'You have already proven yourself capable of managing important and challenging tasks, so it is with confidence that I entrust you with an errand of utmost significance.

'My primary thought is always to keep safe the people of the Norcrofts, yet I simply have not the Riders at my disposal to scour each corner and crevice of my land for the threat of these invading armies. I have given orders for my people to retreat into Cliving or to flee south to Edoras. Those within the city walls will be protected.

'Many of the crofters have either not received the call for evacuation or fail to realize the seriousness of the matter. Please ride to them now and make certain that they understand the dangers of remaining at their crofts.''Thank you for providing warning to my people. Too many have fallen already, and it shames me greatly. With the crofters safely within the city walls, I shall do all in my power to keep Cliving safe.''Reeve Athelward intends to leave us unprotected if we do not flee? I cannot fathom leaving my home abandoned, but I will do so if it means safety for my family.

'Thank you, stranger. We shall make for Cliving at once.''I shall miss my home dearly, but I will not disobey a command from our noble Reeve. Athelward is a wise ruler, and if he requires us to take shelter within the city, then that is the course of action I will take.

'Pardon me, for I must begin preparations to set forth.''Thank you for the warning. It seems to me now that these lands are fit for naught but war.

'I will make haste for Edoras, in the hopes that Théoden King will still hold against our foes.''The Orcs that have spread across the fields have made it far too dangerous to remain. I will heed your warning, and leave these lands behind, yet I hope someday to return.''I cannot understand why Athelward refuses to send his riders to help defend our homes, but he has always done what is best for his people, and I will adhere to his decree for evacuation. Blessings to you, messenger.'Osin has concerned himself with the unreadiness of the crofters around Faldham for the impending siege. He prepares his body for battle, while his mind lingers on the affairs of the crofters.Farmers can be found all around the Mead Hall of Faldham, at their individual farmsteads.

Osin has asked you to seek the various farmers around Faldham and to lend them what aid you can.Osin prepares outside the Mead Hall at the centre of Faldham.

The farmer's troubles have been put to rest, and Osin may now focus on his own problems.Return to Osin in Faldham'Hail, friend. Thane Elfmar has given word that the Elfward are to prepare their steeds and equipment for what will be a dangerous assault against our home. I must keep focused upon this task, yet I cannot help but be concerned for the people of Faldham.

'The crofters are terribly unready for the coming siege. Without proper preparation, many are certain to die fighting for their land and home. May I entrust you with aiding these people of merit? Lend them your skill, and I will see that you are rewarded for your trouble.''Knowing that the people of Faldham will be ready for what comes is of great assurance to me. There are few men who would go to such great lengths to aid us. It is my pleasure to call you friend, ${PLAYER}.

'My mind will now rest solely upon thoughts of victory in the coming battle.''The crofters can be found at their farmsteads, located around Faldham. Déorun resides at the small home at the centre of the village. Thurgyr lives just south of the Mead Hall. Haligmar dwells to the east, neighbouring Sahard's farm. Lastly, Enswith can be found at the north end of town.'Haligmar has seen evidence of Orcs poaching steeds from the stables of crofters in Faldham, and is desperate to free them from enemy captivity.The Orcs have been moving the stolen horses to the south-east, beyond the edges of the town.

Haligmar has begged you to release the steeds from the pens of the Orc poachers.Haligmar awaits at his farm on the eastern edge of Faldham.

Haligmar will be most pleased with your rescue of the precious steeds of the crofter's stables.Return to Haligmar with news of the freed horses'A few men have reported horses missing, as Orc poachers have come and abducted them during the cover of night. The tracks from town lead east and to the south, towards the mountain pass.

'I beg of you, please release our steeds from the enclosures of the Orcs! No doubt, they have some vile intention for the steeds that we have taken such care of. Be most cautious, that area has grown rather dangerous of late.''It is good to know that our friends have been released to safety. We must be vigilant, night and day, to stop the Orcs from any more of this trickery.

'The people of Faldham owe you much for your help. A friend of horses will always be welcome here.'Frithelm and the other guards have been forced to waste their time settling petty squabbles and disputes between nobles, some of whom are oblivious to the true dangers that lurk beyond the city's walls.Ordwacer and Isenyd can be found within the city of Cliving.

Frithelm has asked you to speak with the nobles Ordwacer and Isenyd and help them resolve their disputes.Help Ordwacer with his problemHelp Isenyd with his problemFrithelm watches the road, just south of the fountain in the centre of Cliving.

The soldier Frithelm will be pleased to know that you have settled the disputes of Ordwacer, Trumsele, Isenyd, and Giselmon.Talk to Frithelm in Cliving'I weep for the people of the Norcrofts. You have seen the dangers outside the walls of Cliving, and you have seen the death that lingers at our gates. Yet the nobles living here are ignorant to these concerns and continue on with petty squabbles and bickering. There is no greater waste of time and effort than these fools.

'I have tried to make them understand, but they refuse to listen to any authority save the Reeve himself. Perhaps they would listen to an outsider who speaks with reason. Help resolve their disputes so that we may focus on the necessary preparations for the coming war.''There are a few nobles who are more vocal and problematic than the others.

'Ordwacer is having some dispute with one of the craftsmen. I recommend speaking with him at his estate in the eastern part of the city.

'Isenyd and Giselmon are arguing over a banquet of some sort. How frivolous! You had best speak to Isenyd, just west of the market area.''My work seems much quieter now that some of these trivial disputes have been settled. It is such a shame that no noble blood was shed.... Ha! Worry not, I merely jest.

'Perhaps now the nobles might consider the desperate situation that we find ourselves in and look at their neighbours as allies rather than enemies. The guard has more important matters to attend to than solving petty squabbles. You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.'Getric and his family plan to depart for Edoras to avoid the dangers of the coming Orc-siege. Getric fears that without bringing valuable trade-goods, he will have a difficult time bartering for food and shelter once he arrives.Pristine hides can be gathered from the black-bears and láthbears roaming east of Faldham.

Getric believes that pristine bear-hides will be useful for bartering with once he reaches the capital city of Rohan.Collect pristine hides from bears or láthbears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Getric can be found preparing to leave his home, just north-east of the Mead Hall of Faldham.

You have procured the hides that Getric will use to barter with in Edoras.Return the pristine hides to the farmer, Getric'These lands are far too dangerous. Théoden King keeps safe only those within his walls, and so, my family makes for Edoras at once!

'I worry about providing for my family once we reach the great city. If I had some pelts to sell at the market, I would feel much better about making the journey. Please, gather some hides for me, so that I might care for my children.

'Go with haste! I cannot afford to hesitate any longer, and we shall depart shortly!''What fortuitous timing! We were at the precipice of departure... you have arrived not a moment too soon.

'These hides are in pristine condition! We should be able to barter for what we need to survive, once we arrive in Edoras. Blessings upon you for your aid.'The merchants and guards at the trader's outpost outside of Elthengels have noticed many warbands of wandering enemy forces. They have offered a reward for any who have the cunning and strength to overcome the forces marching across the Norcrofts.Warbands of enemies can be found roaming across the plains of the Norcrofts.

The trader's outpost offered a reward for any adventurers capable of defeating a warband of enemies in the Norcrofts.The guard captain of the merchants can be found at the trader's outpost, west of Elthengels.

The guard captain has promised a reward for any capable of defeating a warband of the Norcrofts.Speak to the merchant guard captain at the trader's outpost'In the course of your travels, perhaps you have noticed one of the large bands of marauding enemies, charging across the plains. I have heard the guards refer to these units as warbands. 

'The warbands pose a danger to all the free people that live and travel across Rohan. If you have confidence in your skill, you may attempt to put a stop to these roaming foes. The guard captain at our camp is willing to reward any who might put a stop to these warbands.''You have managed to defeat a warband leader? A most impressive task, for they have oft managed to outmanoeuvre even the most seasoned of riders.

'I am glad to reward you for your continuing good deeds. The safety provided by removing these warbands is more than worth the coin.'Reeve Athelward has heard of your endeavours to help Walnoth investigate the crimes in Cliving.Reeve Athelward resides at the Mead Hall at the north-east end of Cliving.

Reeve Athelward has summoned you before him to discuss matters that concern him and his people.Talk to Reeve Athelward at the Mead Hall of Cliving'You have been a tremendous help, both to me and to the people of Cliving. We all owe you a debt of gratitude for your fine service.

'Reeve Athelward has sent word that he would personally like to thank you for your assistance in these matters. Go with haste! The Reeve's time is precious, and it is rare for him to pay heed to visitors of your stature.''${PLAYER}? You must be the wise ${RACE} I have heard so much about. I simply must thank you... I could not be more pleased with the results of the investigation.

'To think that a coward would threaten my life with poisons rather than face me with their grievances in person... it sickens me. We must put these matters behind us, for further turmoil yet lies ahead for me and my people.

'Receive this reward, and please consider helping us further.'Elfmar has asked for your aid in training his men, and also for a chance to discuss the situation in Rohan.Elfmar is standing outside his Mead Hall, and he wishes to speak with you about the possibility of training his men.Talk to ElfmarElfmar wishes to train with you.Talk to Elfmar to begin training with himNona and Horn are in the field outside the Faldham Mead Hall.Talk to HornTalk to NonaElfmar's men are in the field outside the Mead Hall of Faldham.

Elfmar has asked you to train with his men so they will have some additional battle experience.Elfmar wants to have another bout with you.Talk to ElfmarCorudan wishes to train with you as well.Talk to CorudanElfmar is in the field outside the Mead Hall of Faldham.Talk to Elfmar'Things go ill in Rohan, ${PLAYER}. I fear that the King's men are fewer than in days past, and less resolute. I have heard whispers from villagers and lords alike that put little faith in Théoden King.

'I stand with Éomer, the King's nephew and Third Marshal of the Riddermark. He is my shield-brother, and we have sparred in peace and fought side by side during war. If he were here, he would be the first to volunteer to train with my men. Alas, Éomer is carried hither and yon by the tides of danger, and he goes where he is most needed. For today, that is elsewhere.

'It will not always be so, and that is why I want you to train with my men. They will have need of the experience when the Orcs come to Faldham, as surely they must.''I do not quite know what to make of this, ${PLAYER}. If I did not know better, I would say that those two were a-courting. But... that would be unwise to say aloud. Perhaps I am mistaken.'

Elfmar shakes his head.

'Thank you for your help! I know that my men are better prepared to face what must surely await them now. I hope you found the training useful as well.''Well-fought, both of you. I will find someone with whom to test my own mettle!''I will try not to hurt these horse-lords too badly, ${PLAYER}, for your sake.''Are you ready to return to the Mead Hall? Nothing follows up a sweat quite like a great mug of ale!''It has been the custom in Faldham for the leader to take the first battle for himself, and I see no reason it should be different today. Let us have a good battle, ${PLAYER}, and by our example inspire the men!'This man has the rank of Ceorl.

His mood is one of trepidation.

He was born in Garsfeld, but now lives in Faldham and his loyalty is to Elfmar while Elfhelm is away.This man has the rank of Rinc.

His mood is one of eagerness.

He was born in Faldham and his loyalty has always been to Elfhelm and his heirs.This man has the rank of Guma.

His mood is one of eagerness.

He was born in Faldham and is a frequent companion of Elfmar and Éomer.This man has the rank of Ceorl.

His mood is one of slight trepidation.

He was born in Faldham, and he works daily on his combat skill but to little result. He hopes his training with you will help.This man has the rank of Ceorl.

His mood is one of extreme confidence, but you can tell it is misplaced. He is certain to yield at the first chance of injury.

He lives in Snowbourn and came to Faldham to serve Elfhelm, but he arrived after Elfhelm rode to the Fords and now seeks to impress Elfmar.This man has the rank of Ceorl.

His mood is one of quiet confidence, and you cannot tell his level of skill from his countenance.

He was born in Edoras and came to Faldham at a young age.This man has the rank of Rinc.

His mood is one of confidence.

He was born in Harwick and came to Faldham with his family very recently. He practices daily so he may better protect them.This man has the rank of Rinc.

His mood is one of weariness.

He was born in Langhold and has seen much of war. He came to Faldham because this is where his steed finally succumbed to exhaustion. He offered his sword to Elfmar as a means of regaining his honour.This man has the rank of Guma.

His mood is one of understated confidence.

He was born in Minas Tirith but is of Rohirric descent, and came to Faldham many years ago. He has already distinguished himself in battle with the Orcs.'Is it true that your friend and boon companion Horn is from Eaworth, in the Entwash Vale? If he is the son of Reeve Ingbert, then his sister is the girl Ingyth.'

Elfmar looks troubled.

'I wonder how she has taken the death of Prince Théodred? The two were betrothed, and now, alas, never will they marry. This war has already taken the happiness of many, and it is only just begun!

'I am ready to spar with you again, ${PLAYER}! And this time I will not let you have the victory so easily!''Oh my. Nona and Horn will likely be sparring like that for awhile. I have been wondering how much longer it might take. Each of their interest in the other has been quite obvious to me, though perhaps not to themselves.'

Corudan smiles.

'Would you like to spar with me? They are not likely to end anytime soon.'Discomfitted by Reeve Athelward's unusually pleasant demeanor and apparent disregard for the severity of Rohan's situation, Burnoth decides to leave Cliving by night.Speak with Burnoth.Talk to BurnothBurnoth wants you to distract the guard stationed across from his party's encampment.

<rgb=#FF0000>Help the Riders Four escape without being noticed.</rgb>Distract the guard stationed across from the Riders' encampmentLead the Riders Four to the stables.Lead the Riders Four to the stablesSpeak with Burnoth.Talk to BurnothLead the Riders Four to the stables.Lead the Riders Four to the stablesThere is a pile of rocks near the gate.

You might be able to distract the guard with a thrown rock.Throw a rock to distract the guardLead the Riders Four to the stables.Lead the Riders Four to the stablesTalk to BurnothUse the jerky Burnoth gave you to distract the stray hound.Lead the Riders Four to the stables.Lead the Riders Four to the stablesConvince the guards to leave their post.Convince the guards to leave their postLead the Riders Four to the stables.Lead the Riders Four to the stablesSpeak with Burnoth.Talk to Burnoth'Thank you for your aid, my friend. Tomorrow, you should return to Reeve Athelward and let him know that we are grateful for his invitation, but must graciously decline.''${PLAYER}, if you could distract the guard across the way, we might be able to sneak out.

'Stay with us when we move, however, for we may need your assistance along the way.'You inform the guard that you believe you saw riders approaching the city.

'I will go and look, for it is late, and there should be no Riders about.''What is it? A chicken coop?

'Aye, we should avoid it... if the hens are disturbed, the noise will alert the guards.''Do you see that stray hound ahead? Perhaps you can distract it with this jerky....'The hound sniffs warily at the meat you proffer, then takes it and wanders away.'You say Orcs are attacking? To the gate! We must defend Cliving!''We will continue from here alone... by the time word comes to Athelward of our departure, we will be well away from here.'Reeve Athelward is not content with simply giving orders. He wishes to lead by example. He has taken to the fields alongside you, in order to witness your prowess in battle firsthand.Accompany Reeve Athelward on the field of battle.Talk to AthelwardAthelward can be found along the road south of Cliving.

Athelward wishes to discuss the results of your encounter with the Orcs and Uruks.Talk to Reeve Athelward'Come, we will reassemble at the Mead Hall.''Stay with me as I patrol this area. We shall remove from these lands any of the scattered vermin that now infest them.

'Ready your weapon, friend. If your noteworthy reputation is not groundless, these Orcs should prove to be no match for us.''You have fought well, but this plague of Orcs and Uruks seems endless. In a short time, the Orcs will have taken hold of the area again, and we will have to clear them out once more. For now, we must be content with having cleared the road for those seeking refuge.

'You fight with bravery and heroism... I see now why some consider you to be such a gallant ${CLASS}. Let us return to the city.'Walnoth believes that he has narrowed the list of suspects down to three individuals. The soldier Swithorne, the cook's assistant Eldora, and an elderly man -- Wigfugol -- all must be interrogated.Each of the suspects may be found within the city of Cliving: Swithorne at the Rohirrim tents by the Mead Hall, Eldora at her home by the west gate, and Wigfugol at his estate in the north-west part of the city.

Walnoth would like you to interrogate the people he believes to be the main suspects in the crimes that have occurred.Talk to Swithorne at his tent near the Mead HallTalk to Eldora at her home near the west entrance to ClivingTalk to Wigfugol at his estate in the north-west part of ClivingWalnoth may be found near the fountain at the centre of Cliving.

Walnoth awaits the results of your interrogations.Return to Walnoth and discuss each suspect's testimony'I believe we are getting ever closer to solving this mystery, ${PLAYER}. There are three primary suspects that we must consider: the soldier, Swithorne; the cook's assistant, Eldora; and the old man, Wigfugol. Each was seen visiting Malbéort and may have killed him to cover their attempted poisoning against Reeve Athelward.

'You have been paramount to our progress in this investigation thus far. Interrogating these individuals will prove to be crucial to discovering the truth, so I must leave this task to you.

'Swithorne should be among the Rohirrim near the Mead Hall, Wigfugol resides at an estate to the north-west, and Eldora's home can be found near the west entrance to Cliving. Question each of them carefully and return to me with their responses.''Speak with each suspect alone and listen carefully to their words. Consider each person's motive, access to the places of the crimes, and any witnesses of their whereabouts.

'Swithorne may be found among the soldiers stationed around the Mead Hall. Eldora is said to live in a small home just inside the western entrance to the city. Wigfugol lives alone at his estate, north of the western gate.''Captain Théodmar told me of the circumstances involving Malbéort and my dagger. I understand your reason to suspect me, but I can assure you that I had nothing to do with his murder. My weapon was likely taken from me in order to draw suspicion towards me and away from the real killer.

'On the night that Malbéort's life was taken, I was patrolling the city perimeter with other guards. Had I left to visit his home, my absence would have been very strongly noticed.

'I have been grieving the loss of my brother, yet as I have explained to Théodmar, I do not blame the Reeve for his death. I respect Athelward greatly, and I understand his decision must have been a difficult one. I am an honourable man, and these despicable crimes are below me.''I know that you are investigating the poison found in the kitchen, and the murder of Malbéort. Yes, I have heard the rumours. I must explain my actions so that you understand why I am innocent in these matters.

'I have been taking care of the injured man you see before us, Fréastan. He was wounded in battle, and is greatly impaired. I care greatly for Fréastan, though he knows it not, and I have been overseeing his aid through medicinal herbs that Malbéort has provided for us. I have been here frequently tending to him, thus my absence from the kitchen.

'It is true that no one can account for my whereabouts when the crimes were commited, but know that I have no more reason to harm Athelward than anyone else in town. Furthermore, Malbéort was helping me to treat Fréastan, and I would never have wished harm to come upon him!''You suspect me of these rumoured crimes? How shameful! Know that I am a respected elder in this community.

'I was visiting Malbéort for treatment to the terrible nightmares I began having, shortly after my son and his family lost their lives. His croft was raided and burned in the first Orc-assaults, and still Athelward has refused to defend those helpless families outside the city walls.

'It is true, I harbour no love for the Reeve after such a heartless decision, yet I would not resort to poisoning him. Consider for a moment, how a citizen such as myself would have access to the Reeve's kitchen? Surely someone would have noticed my unexpected presence there.''Each suspect has given a different reason why he or she should be absolved. Swithorne claims to have witnesses for when Malbéort was slain, Eldora seems to be lacking a motive to harm either victim, and Wigfugol would like us to consider his lack of access to the restricted kitchen.

'The time draws near... consider each testimony and all of the evidence discovered. Action must be taken before others come to harm. We must apprehend a guilty party.'Inhild is worried about a young farmer named Randgar, whom she cares deeply for. Randgar has decided to risk his life outside of Cliving, rather than abandon the woman he loves.Randgar lives on a small farm, south and east of Cliving, along the road.

Inhild has asked you to go to the farmer Randgar and convince him to flee for her sake.Talk to Randgar at his farm'Hello, traveller. May I ask a favour of you? I am worried about a young man named Randgar, who lives on a farm outside of Cliving. I have heard countless stories of crofters coming under siege from fearsome Orcs and other beasts, and I pray that Randgar remains yet safe.

'I care deeply for Randgar, but my father will not allow me to see him. He knows that Randgar is poor and believes he is unworthy of our concern. Please, visit his farm and ensure his safety. I love Randgar, but I would rather he flee to Edoras than come to harm for my sake.''My dear Inhild... she need not worry for me. Since I first gazed upon her in the market, I have been in love, and I will do anything to be near her. I refuse to leave for Edoras, for we may never meet again.

'I appreciate your concerns, but I will not leave without seeing Inhild.'Walnoth has been asked to lead an investigation into the discovery of a phial of poison that was hidden in Athelward's kitchen. It is believed that the poison was part of a treacherous plot to murder the Reeve and has unnerved Athelward's soldiers.Cetelgat can be found working in Reeve Athelward's kitchen, just west of the Cliving Mead Hall.

Walnoth has asked you to speak with Cetelgat, the Reeve's cook, about the phial he found in the kitchen.Talk to Athelward's cook, Cetelgat, about the poisonThe phial can be found near Cetelgat in the Reeve's kitchen.

