'Th-thank you, stranger. These... these belonged to my... husband and children. My thanks to you....'Hoital, the uruk leading the Orcs of Maustazg, revealed that the siege was being orchestrated by Saruman the White and that Lady Góda was working against Ingmar on behalf of Saruman.Lady Góda stands near Ingbert in the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

Grief-stricken, Ingbert has asked you to confront Góda about her betrayal.Talk to Góda in the Mead Hall of EaworthIngbert is in the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

You should speak with Ingbert about his mother's betrayal.Talk to Ingbert in the Mead Hall of Eaworth'My heart grieves, ${PLAYER}. My mother has always been wise in her ways and her counsel... why now has she turned to folly?

'To know that Saruman, ever a friend to Rohan, has fallen to evil is nearly more than I can bear, but that he should take with him good folk, such as Góda daughter of Athelmar...?

'Nonetheless, I cannot allow this treason to go unnoticed. ${PLAYER}, I would ask that you take my mother into custody. The guards will escort her to the jail to await trial.''Ah, so my son has not the courage to confront me himself? Know this, ${CLASS}, what I did, I did for the good of the Riding and of Rohan.

'Théoden King has become feeble-minded, incapable of ruling the Mark. Saruman is strong and wise... he seeks only to bring order to the lands.''I doubt not that her intent was true, ${PLAYER}, but our allegiance is to Théoden, not Saruman. I cannot believe that there is any good in the deeds of the Wizard, and that is a great sorrow.

'We must, however, persevere, for if our hearts fail us, darkness will consume Rohan.'Éomer, the Third Marshal of the Riddermark and the nephew of King Théoden, has come to Eaworth with news of the Uruks you are pursuing.Éomer is outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

Corudan told you that Éomer, the nephew of the King, has returned from his ride north in pursuit of the Uruks.Talk to Éomer outside the Mead Hall of EaworthÉothain is outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth, with Éomer.

Éomer has told you to ask any questions you have of his man Éothain.Talk to Éothain outside the Mead Hall of EaworthÉomer is outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

You have spoken with this lieutenant Éothain, and should now speak again with Éomer.Talk to Éomer outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth'As Reeve Ingbert promised, Éomer has returned from his ride north. He is outside the Mead Hall, ${PLAYER}. Introduce yourself and see what he knows of the Uruks we pursued!''Éomer is the nephew of King Théoden. He should be outside the Mead Hall with some of his men. Speak with him about the Uruks we pursued.''I will be at your disposal if Éomer my commander wishes it. Otherwise I have naught to say to you.''I am pleased to make your acquaintance, my friend. Yours is the second party I have encountered who sought the Uruks, ${PLAYER}, and to you I say what I said before: the Uruks have all been destroyed, and I saw no sign of any other living thing at the site. The news brought great sadness to the three we encountered.

'The Man was Aragorn son of Arathorn, and his companions were a dwarf, Gimli Glóin's son, and an Elf called Legolas.

'Speak to my man Éothain if you would hear more of these travellers or their prey.''Éothain will answer any questions you have about your friends or the Uruks they pursued.''If Éomer my commander wishes me to answer your questions, I will do so. Ask what you will and hear my answers. Whether they give you joy or sadness is no concern of mine.''Did you have more questions of me? I will do whatever Éomer commands, and he has commanded that I answer you.''No Uruks escaped the circle of my riders, but it is possible your friends may have escaped before we closed in. It is a small chance, but it is what Aragorn hoped for, and perhaps his hope was more than hope, and there was truth in it.'

Éomer makes a wry face.

'Perhaps, but Éomer Éomund's son puts stock in cold steel and sunlight on the plains. Without some unlikely dwimmer-craft, I fear the halfling friends Aragorn sought met a sad end.

'The three hunters, however, lived when I left them.'Éomer has told you where to go to find the Uruks his men destroyed. It is possible you will find your lost friends at the site.Éomer's Vengeance can be found to the north-west of Eaworth, just outside the eaves of Fangorn.

Éomer has said you should look for signs of your friends at the site where he encountered and destroyed the Uruks led by Uglúk.Find Éomer's Vengeance, north-west of EaworthUglúk and Éomer fought personally at the end of the battle, but Uglúk did not walk away.Examine the remains of UglúkThe ground at Éomer's Vengeance is torn up by a confused muddle of footprints. Can anything be learned from it?Corudan wishes to speak with you at Éomer's Vengeance.

You have found a number of footprints that seem to go toward the Forest of Fangorn, and should speak to Corudan about it.Talk to Corudan at Éomer's VengeanceHorn is waiting to speak with you at Éomer's Vengeance, north-west of Eaworth.Talk to Horn at Éomer's Vengeance'My men caught up with the Uruks just outside the eaves of Fangorn, to the north-west. Their leader, a fierce Uruk whose name seemed to be Uglúk, if the panicked words of his fellows can be trusted, fought me blade to blade after I dismounted.

'I proved the better swordsman, to the pride of Éomund and the shame of whatever disgusting lout claimed to sire Uglúk.

'Ride to this site and look for signs of your missing friends or Aragorn and his two companions. If my men and I are still here when you return, you are welcome to ride south with us to Edoras.''We destroyed all of the Uruks just outside the eaves of Fangorn, to the north-west. Go there and see for yourself. If I am here on your return, you are welcome to ride with us when we depart.''Éomer did his work well. Perhaps too well, if your halfling friends were destroyed along with the Uruks.''Éomer spoke truly. The Uruks are destroyed.''Did you search the site for any sign of your friends? It is to the north-west of Eaworth.''Éomer performed his duty admirably. I fear he may have been too thorough. If your halfling friends were here, they could not have escaped the battle or the burning afterward.''Gruesome.'You stare at defeated Uglúk and wonder if Boromir rests comfortably, from wherever it is he watches, now that the leader of the band that set upon him is no more.Uglúk is beyond caring, not that he would have cared in life.'Can you tell anything from the confused muddle of footprints?''My skills do not lie with tracking. I can tell nothing from the confused footprints here. Can you?'Uglúk has no opinion about your next move.'You say some of the tracks lead toward Fangorn? That is a peculiar place, and many things are whispered about it in Eaworth, and no doubt in other places less close to the forest.''You say some of those tracks led toward Fangorn? They are likely not those of the halflings, for Éomer was certain no one left the site of the battle without his knowledge.

'They may then be the tracks of Aragorn, Legolas, or Gimli. What brought them toward Fangorn?

'What does Horn think? Will he dare the Forest of Fangorn? I will go, and gladly! But he has expressed worry about such places before.''What thinks Horn? Will he dare the Forest of Fangorn?''I see how Corudan looks at me. He thinks I fear to go into Fangorn.'

Horn looks at you with his piercing grey eyes.

'I went into another ill-omened wood with you, not long ago, ${PLAYER}, do you remember? There I took a grievous wound, an injury from which I will never recover. Nor would I wish to heal that hurt!

'I will follow you into Fangorn. What can be done to me that I should fear?'Searching for the source of the madness that has befallen the creatures of the marsh, you tracked the draugar to a cave in the Wódfen.Defeat HenmaruThe draug-chieftain probably dwells somewhere in the depths of their cave.

You should hunt for the draug-chieftain and put an end to its existence.Defeat HenmaruThere are Rohirrim at Eaworth, south of the Wódfen.

You should bring news of your victory against Henmaru to the Rohirrim.Talk to someone at EaworthSearching for the source of the madness that has befallen the creatures of the marsh, you tracked the draugar to a cave in the Wódfen.

You should enter Byrgenstow and defeat the chieftain of the draugar.'There have always been dark legends about the Wódfen, and your tale bears them out. I wonder why the Dead have awakened now?

'It is no matter. You have done us a service by destroying their chieftain.'You found a fragment of a strange obsidian talisman upon the corpse of a draug.You should break the locks on the chests within Byrgenstow and search them to find more fragments of the talisman.Collect fragments of the talisman (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected all of the fragments of the talisman. The evil it emanates is terrible. Perhaps it can be destroyed at the place of its origin.

You should find the source of the talisman within Byrgenstow. It is very likely in the deepest chambers.This shard of obsidian appears to be a fragment of some strange talisman. It may be that the talisman is the source of the evil dwelling within Byrgenstow, the burial cave of the Wódfen.

Perhaps the other fragments are locked away in chests within Byrgenstow.While exploring the Entwash Vale, you were attacked by a drake. There may be more nearby....Drakes can be found throughout the Entwash Vale.

You should hunt down the drakes of the Entwash Vale, reducing the threat they pose to the people of the Vale.Defeat drakes in the Entwash Vale (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})While exploring the Entwash Vale, you were attacked by a drake. There may be more nearby, and defeating them can only be a good thing.Wadel, the grieving father you rescued from the ruins of Thornhope, desires nothing but revenge against the monsters who murdered his family.Matumkâr will likely be found near Wadel's home in Thornhope.

Wadel has asked you to kill Matumkâr, the Orc who led the band that murdererd his family.Defeat MatumkârWadel is encamped outside the walls of Thornhope.

You should return to Wadel with news of your victory over Matumkâr.Talk to Wadel outside Thornhope'${PLAYER}, you brought me out of a pit of pity and despair, and for that I thank you. But while I no longer desire death, I do desire vengeance.

'Do you know what it is like to see your wife and children brutalized and murdered before you, and there is nothing you can do to stop it? I do. The Orcs did just that, and when they were done, I begged for death myself...only the creature which led that band -- an Orc called Matumkâr -- would not allow them to grant me my wish. I was allowed to bury what remained of my children, but my wife I never saw again.

'${PLAYER}, if naught else comes of this horror, I ask only one thing: see to it that Matumkâr perishes at Thornhope. Let my family have the justice they deserve!''You have my gratitude, ${PLAYER}. The spirits of my children can now have peace....'You found Thane Béortnoth, the last known survivor of the Orcs' assault on Thornhope, wandering half mad with injury and grief.Thornhope lies just down the road from Béortnoth's camp.

Thane Béortnoth has asked you to search Thornhope for other survivors.Thane Béortnoth is encamped outside of Thornhope.

You should return to Béortnoth with news of your findings.Talk to Béortnoth outside of Thornhope'Nay, I am sure it said it was called Blackthorn. It thought I was an Orc, but it listened to my tale and vowed to help me....'

Béortnoth shook his head.

'Nay, that cannot be right. My injury... it must be worse than I thought. So addled am I that I have forgotten my people.

'Will you enter the village and search for other survivors? I am Thane now, for I am sure I saw my father die -- indeed, by the hand of an uruk, I am certain -- but there is nothing left of Thornhope.''I am not alone then? That is well. We will drive forth the Orcs and rebuild Thornhope.'Burnoth agreed to help defend Eaworth from the Orcs and weaken the siege against the Entwash Vale.Hutha is standing near Burnoth outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

Burnoth asked you to give Hutha his instructions, then return to him.Talk to HuthaBurnoth is standing outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

You should speak with Burnoth when you are prepared to join the defence of Eaworth.Talk to Burnoth to begin preparations for the siege of EaworthBurnoth stands outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

You should return to Burnoth and discuss your victory.Talk to Burnoth outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth'Are you prepared, ${PLAYER}? Hutha will ride out into the Vale and harry the Orcs. Hopefully, he can goad them into mounting an assault on Eaworth, drawing them to the ruined southern walls.

'When they attack, we will put up a token fight, but gradually withdraw into the city, where the greater part of our forces will fall upon them. They will be trapped in our onslaught.

'Will you join us, my friend? If so, let Hutha know his task, then rejoin me to finish preparations at the southern walls.'Hutha nods as you relay Burnoth's instructions.

'That, Hafoc and I can do easily enough.

'My brothers, Hereman and Heca, would be proud, I think to know all that I have accomplished on this journey. Their little brother Elfling has grown fangs.

'Elfling, I have always been called by those who disparaged me, but it is a name I wear as a badge of respect, for in my hand, my bow Bloodhorn sings as the bows of the Elves.

'Ah well, I have a mission and must be on my way. Tell Burnoth to have everything ready when I return!''Let us go down into the city... there is much to be done.''Well done, ${PLAYER}! The Orcs never suspected the trap, and a good many of them shall no longer threaten the walls of Eaworth.

