You encountered an Ent called Baldbark, and he sent you out of Fangorn with little ceremony.Talk to Corudan at Éomer's Vengeance'My father told me his men had heard Orc axes beneath the eaves of Fangorn. There must be a camp of the creatures somewhere to the north of here, among the trees. That is the way the tracks go, are they not?

'We should go to this camp of Orcs and slay them. While we do, let us keep our eyes open for your friends. If we cannot help them directly, perhaps this will help them in an indirect fashion.''An Orc camp must lie north of here, beneath the eaves of Fangorn. Let us go there and defeat the creatures. Keep your eyes open for your friends, as well. If we cannot help them directly, slaying these Orcs may help indirectly.''If half of the stories I have heard about Fangorn are true, the Orcs that Horn thinks to find beneath the eaves will likely not be there long. But let us slay them while we may. They are Orcs, after all.''I believe you need to slay one more Orc beneath the eaves of Fangorn if you are to draw level with Corudan and myself. One Orc more, ${PLAYER}.''Did we not agree that we would each slay eight Orcs? I really believed we did. And you have slain only seven.''A voice, you say? We should investigate it! Where did you hear it? A misty dell south of the Orc camp beneath the eaves of Fangorn?''I would investigate a mysterious voice if I heard one. So would my sister. You should do the same, ${PLAYER}. You heard it coming from a misty dell to the south of the Orc camp beneath the eaves?''Hroom... boom... broom.'

The great creature stares down at you with baleful eyes. Then it speaks again.

'What are these small creatures that come among the trees? So many... so many... hroom... but Gandalf saw to the Elf, the Man, and the Dwarf...

'Treebeard has his guests... and others come among the trees. Baldbark was told not to let others disturb the meetings... hroom... boom... broom.

'You should leave... it is not a time for you... hroom...''Such marvels! When I was a boy, I heard tell of the tree-men in Eaworth, of course, but I thought they were just tales.''It is a day for wonders, ${PLAYER}! Mithrandir lives, as you said, and he has returned to where he is most needed: a kingdom beset on all sides by danger! If Aragorn, Legolas, and Gimli are not safe with Mithrandir, they will not be safe anywhere!'

Corudan's face lights up with a brilliant smile.

'And the second wonder: I did not dare to hope the tree-herders still walked in Middle-earth! But today I have met an Ent, heard his speech, and was told in no uncertain terms I was not welcome!'

Corudan is more pleased than you can ever remember seeing him.

'What a delight! Asked to leave his forest by an Ent! Did he say his name was Baldbark? Then I will call him Rúdhrif in the language of my people, and I will keep this encounter near to my heart!'You have received reassuring words from Baldbark concerning your friends, and can now turn your thoughts fully toward aiding Éomer and Rohan.Horn is already back at Eaworth, in the Entwash Vale.

You have been reassured that your friends are safe, and now you should travel back to Eaworth with Horn and Corudan.Talk to Horn in EaworthÉomer is outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth.

Éomer said you were welcome to ride south with his men when he departed Eaworth.Talk to Éomer outside the Mead Hall of EaworthÉomer is outside the Mead Hall of Eaworth, in the Entwash Vale.

Éomer is ready to ride south when you are, but he has a number of destinations he wants to visit before turning for Edoras and home.Talk to Éomer and ride south to the Stone of WyrgendeÉomer is standing by the Stone of Wyrgende.

Éomer wishes to speak with you before departing for Snowbourn.Talk to Éomer by the Stone of Wyrgende'Rúdhrif's words were reassuring, were they not? He told us, in his way, that Merry and Pippin were safely in the company of "Treebeard," who can only be an Ent. And the Man, dwarf, and Elf of which he spoke must be Aragorn, Gimli and Legolas. If they are now in the presence of Mithrandir, surely we need worry about them no longer.

'Let us return to Eaworth and see if Éomer remains there with his men. Horn wished to be off immediately. We should speak with him when we are back at Eaworth.''Horn wanted to return to Eaworth as quickly as possible to see if Éomer remained there. We should return to Eaworth at once, now that we are assured of the safety of our mutual friends.''Éomer and his men are still here, ${PLAYER}. I spoke with him briefly, and he said we were welcome to make the southward journey with him. He has a few places he would like to stop on the way, including... well, including the Stone of Wyrgende.'

Horn sighs heavily.

'Do you remember, ${PLAYER}? It was the story I told by that camp-fire on our journey by boat down the Anduin, with Corudan and... with Nona. King Fengel and his line were cursed: no heir of Fengel's line would survive to sit long upon the throne of Rohan. Many thought that the curse had been broken by Prince Théodred, but it seems it was not so.

'Éomer wishes to visit the Stone, and you must understand why: with the Prince dead, he as Théoden's nephew is now next in line for the throne. He is of King Fengel's line, and the Curse must now be ever in his thoughts. Speak to him, but gently! Who can know what it must be like to live under such a curse?''Éomer is outside the Mead Hall. Speak to him and tell him we are ready to accompany him when he departs.''Did you learn anything about your friends from the site where we destroyed the Uruks?'

Éomer listens to your account, and marvels particularly at your description of Baldbark.

'What a strange tale you weave, ${PLAYER}! To think such peculiar things persist under the light of the sun in these days!

'Tell me when you are ready to depart, and my men will be ready. There are a few sites I would have us visit on our way south to Snowbourn, and from there we will go to Edoras.''You are welcome to ride with my men and me on our southward road, but there is one place I have planned to stop before we reach Snowbourn.

'Have you heard of Wyrgende's Stone? It is involved in a tale of Rohan, and I am... quite interested in seeing it again.''Thank you for your help, friend. I am glad to have you and your companions as allies when danger strikes!

'Orcs and Wargs are the type of danger I am not afraid to face. Threats of a more otherworldly nature... those I cannot abide. Still, Éomund's son will face such dangers with as bold a heart as he can manage, the bolder still with allies such as you at his side!'Gandalf would like you to bring news of the hobbits back to Ashlimb, who first put you on their trail.Gandalf is atop Treebeard's Hill, alongside a horse you have never seen before.

Gandalf has one more thing to say to you.Ask Gandalf about the horse that stands near himAshlimb is at the eastern edge of Fangorn's eaves, waiting to hear of the hobbits' fate.

You should return to Ashlimb and convey Gandalf's tale of their meeting with Treebeard.Return to Ashlimb to tell him of the hobbits' fate <rgb=#FF00FF>you may use the horse near Gandalf for swifter travel</rgb>Ents can be found throughout Fangorn. You should search north-east of Ashlimb for other Ents you can help, as Gandalf bid.Search the surrounding woods for another sleeping Ent'Oh...and one more thing before we part ways, ${PLAYER}. You must not forget about the good Ent who sent you on this fanciful wild goose chase.

'We cannot start a flood without the first raindrop. Be sure to tell your sleepy friend all that you saw. Farewell, good ${CLASS}!'The Ent does not speak, but its eyes gleam with a sharp green spark as you relay Gandalf's tale.

You did not succeed in waking Ashlimb from his sleepy state, but from his keen gaze, you sense that you have appeased his restless curiosity.You have discovered another Ent, this one barely different from a tree.

He stands still as stone, but regards you with his deep, slow eyes.'The horse, you ask? Ah, yes -- another strange meeting in this strange wood. I do not know his tale, but he came to me.

'I believe he seeks his master, but why his master rode this way, I cannot guess. You may ride him; I am sure he will bear you wherever you need to go!'It is not a bad idea to gather food and water while you can, for the thought of being lost in the woods without such necessities is a gruesome one.It is not a bad idea to gather food and water while you can, for the thought of being lost in the woods without such necessities is a gruesome one.Find the Hale-gapTurnips and fresh water can be found along the Entwash River in the Hale-gap.

