'Just talk to the Ents you find. Learn of their memories and help them piece together the riddles that occupy their slow minds.'A vision from deep within the eyes of the Ent comes unbidden into your mind.You should pay heed to the Ent's vision.Observe the small creatures in Ashlimb's memoryYou should return to Ashlimb to wake from the memory.Return to Ashlimb to wake from the memoryAshlimb's memory goes no further, for the halflings walked out of range of his hearing.The small creatures interested Ashlimb, exciting his curiosity, for he had never laid eyes on their like before.Léofmar is ready to reveal his tale of guilt and cowardice.Léofmar is relaying the tale of his cowardice, for which he believes a curse is his punishment.Listen to Léofmar's narrationObserve Léofmar's behaviour.Listen to Léofmar's narrationLéofmar is relaying the tale of his cowardice, for which he believes a curse is his punishment.Follow Léofmar's narrationLéofmar is relaying the tale of his cowardice, for which he believes a curse is his punishment.Listen to Léofmar's narrationLéofmar's tale is ended, but to you, it is not a curse-worthy one. He is only a green young man with a farmer's blood in his veins, not a soldier's. He curses himself for running, but there is nothing evil laid upon him but his own regret.Léofmar describes a watch-tower in the settlement of Garsfeld in the Sutcrofts, and his reluctant post there.

