His eyes are wild with the madness of a sudden fear. You look upon a man who is ready to flee.Léofmar has finished the tale of how he was afraid when the armies of Orcs began to march across the field -- how he sounded his horn to alert Garsfeld, then fled in fear of wielding a sword against so many cruel foes.You have come to the Stone of Wyrgende with Éomer, the nephew of King Théoden.Éomer has come to the Stone of Wyrgende, but you have not entirely divined his purpose for doing so.

You should speak to Éomer to learn why he has come to this grim place before continuing on to Snowbourn.Talk to Éomer by the Stone of WyrgendeÉomer is at the Stone of Wyrgende.Talk to Éomer at the Stone of WyrgendeHorn and Corudan are at Wyrgende's Stone.Talk to Corudan at the Stone of WyrgendeTalk to Horn at the Stone of WyrgendeOrcs are attacking the area near Wyrgende's Stone!Defeat Vale-scouts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Éomer is at the Stone of Wyrgende.Talk to Éomer at the Stone of Wyrgende'The deeds of the past often have grave effects on the present, ${PLAYER}. Curses have power, and with the force of belief behind them they can become even more powerful. How else can the misfortune of Fengel's line be explained? And how will knowledge of the Curse affect Éomer's actions now?

'The people of Rohan need him to be strong. He cannot cower in fear of Wyrgende's memory, or his people will suffer.''There it lies, ${PLAYER}: the Stone of Wyrgende. Do you know the tale?'

Éomer seems surprised to hear that you have been told the tale of Wyrgende the Crone and King Fengel, but then realization dawns.

'Ah, of course you would know it. How could you travel with a bard such as Horn and not know it? It is a popular tale, though it be grim and unpleasant. I expect it is true in all lands: the people enjoy stories that seem to explain their poor lot or continued hardship.''It was thought for many years that Wyrgende's Curse had already expired. My uncle's father, Thengel, lived in Gondor, and the lords of Rohan called a witan and begged him to return to the Mark and take the throne. They said he was virtuous enough and would be such a good king that his reign was certain to end the curse; indeed, Thengel lived long, and his son Théoden now has many years behind him.

'It seems now, with the death of Prince Théodred, that the Curse merely slumbered. Perhaps it did not affect Thengel or my uncle because they were already born, or because they were not in the Riddermark when it was set. None but Wyrgende can know, and she is long-dead.

'The Curse that did not affect Thengel did affect his daughters, and few of their sons survived to reach adulthood.''The Orcs have unwittingly helped me, ${PLAYER}. During the battle, I had no thought at all for Wyrgende or her Curse.

'I have made up my mind. I will not allow the Curse of Wyrgende to control my actions. I am my own man, Éomund's son, and I do not fear the memory of ill deeds done or vengeance sought! I will fight for the people of Rohan until I no longer can do so, and even then I will keep fighting!

'I have given Wyrgende the time I thought she was due, and now I put the Stone behind me and will think of it no more! Onward, to Snowbourn! We are needed!''I had been so certain the Curse was broken, as had been all of Rohan. What must it be like to labour 'neath the shadow of such a thing, and never know when it will reveal its fangs?

'If Éomer out of fear refuses to fight the evils that face our land, then King Fengel's greed will have struck the death-blow for Rohan.''We are needed in Snowbourn! We will not delay!''The Stone of Wyrgende is a place of great sorrow for the people of Rohan, and for its Kings.''The Stone of Wyrgende is a place of great sorrow for the people of Rohan, and for its Kings.''The Stone of Wyrgende is a place of great sorrow for the people of Rohan, and for its Kings.''Oh my! I cannot concentrate during an Orc attack!''The Stone of Wyrgende is a place of great sorrow for the people of Rohan, and for its Kings.'You have journeyed to the horse-herd the mysterious old man directed you.Defend the herd from attacking Orcs.Defend the herd from attacking OrcsShadowfax is at the centre of the wild herd.

You should confront Shadowfax.Confront ShadowfaxShadowfax and his companions rear and gallop off in the direction of Éomer's Vengeance... where you must now return.The Lord of the Mearas whinnies and shakes his mane as you approach. When he calms, he looks upon you with regal approval.You have accompanied Thane Béortnoth and Wadel of Thornhope into the ruins of their village to wreak vengeance upon the Orcs there.Béortnoth wishes to speak with you.Talk to BéortnothFight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the first leg of the attack.Fight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the first leg of the attackFight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the second leg of the assault.Fight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the second leg of the assaultFight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the third leg of the assault.Fight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the third leg of the assaultFight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the fourth leg of the assault.Fight alongside Béortnoth and Wadel on the fourth leg of the assaultSpeak with Béortnoth.Talk to Béortnoth'Let us return to camp... we will prepare for another assault.''Let us bring death and ruin to the destroyers! Let us take blood for the blood of the fallen! Let the waters run black with the grume of the Orcs!''There are too many Orcs to dislodge from the city with but one assault... we must retreat and recuperate for the next assault.'You have come to the edge of Fangorn, a forest steeped with rumour and legend. The men camped outside to fish the Entwash seem friendly enough, but they have little to tell you, other than warnings to keep clear of the trees.The entrance to Fangorn can be found by following the edge of the trees and into a small clearing, where a light path can still be seen trailing into the forest.

You should find a way into Fangorn and see what can be seen there.Find your way into Fangorn and search for signs of life'Welcome, welcome! All the free folk are welcome, especially those handy with a weapon. The fish of the Entwash are plenty, but the dangers are great. We steer clear of the forest, but most enemies are wise enough to steer clear as well, for this is a fell place.

'Hold a moment -- you are here because you wish to go into the forest? Are you mad? I know not what spell is upon you, but I suppose it is your head to lose.

'We lost our boat downriver, so this is not the way to go. You had better go into the small clearing, which you will see gaping black at you if you follow the edge of the trees west of here. Enjoy what little is left of your life!'This is a strange tree that you have found -- rather, it is not a tree at all, but a very still, tree-like Ent.

