You should clear away the shards of ice.Remove ice shards from in front of the barn doorCyneberg is in Scylfig.

You have aided the villagers, and should now see if Cyneberg is recovered enough to speak with you.Try to speak with Cyneberg again'No, there is nothing you can do for me! Leave me. Just leave me alone.

'Help the others. I cannot bear to watch them struggle.''What... what is this ice all around us? I have never seen anything like it. I looked around when all the fighting was done, and there it was, like swords thrust from the earth.

'I have been trying to break it all morning, and my arms tire. Will you help me clear some of the debris?''There is ice everywhere, but everyone has their hands full! Please help -- we need to be able to get to the barn near the stables!''My horse is trapped! There is too much debris, and I must get to her. Please help her! She is frightened.'

The horse is not the only one who is frightened.'I saw what you did for my people, and I thank you. I... I hope they can rebuild, for I know I cannot.'Iothete believes he knows something about the relic you have been sent to investigate, but he will not tell you. He wants you to bring him a small item first.The item of which Iothete spoke can be found in his home in Dunfast.

You should collect the item when you find it.Find the item in Iothete's abandoned homeThe item of which Iothete spoke can be found in his home in Dunfast.

You should collect the item when you find it.Find the item in Iothete's abandoned homeIothete is at his small hut hidden in the woods of the northern Whitshaws.

You should bring the stone fragment you collected to Iothete as he bid.'You seek a relic, you say? I feared and hoped this day would come....

'I believe I might have some information for you, stranger, but I need you to find something first. I...I am afraid I cannot tell you without some proof in my hand, for otherwise you will not believe my story.

'It is in Dunfast, in my home. You will know when you find it.''Yes, that is it. An unmistakable relic of some terrible power. I hate it! Its power is everywhere now.

'It is the reason I live here, instead of in my true home. I cannot seem to get away from it, and it has brought evil upon us.

'Now that you feel its power, I will tell you how I came by it. I am sure that it is the reason Wildermore is falling into a wintry ruin! It is my fault....'Egwylf has asked you and Bingo to help her find one of the missing agents of the Cult of the Blood Eye.Egwylf is outside the Mead Hall of Cliving, in the Norcrofts.Talk to Egwylf outside the Mead Hall of ClivingThe missing agent of the Cult of the Blood Eye is somewhere within Wildermore.The Blood Eye cultist finally succumbed to his wounds and the elements near the Lonely Height, in Wildermore.Examine the corpse of the desperate man, near the Lonely HeightEgwylf is in the town of Forlaw, in Wildermore, north of the Stable-master.

You found the missing cultist of the Blood Eye and should bring his brooch to Egwylf with the news that he will no longer trouble Rohan.Bingo is in the town of Forlaw, in Wildermore, north of the Stable-master.Talk to Bingo in the town of Forlaw'I am ready to help Egwylf find the other member of the Cult of the Blood Eye, if you are ready as well! She said the trail has gone cold, but perhaps she has found some clue that will steer us in the right direction!''I am ready to search for another of these villains! Does Egwylf know where the other one has gone?''I am pleased the two of you will help me find this missing agent of the Blood Eye. He would sell the kingdom to its enemies, given the chance, and he must pay for his treason!

'I scouted ahead and found his trail leading west. I believe that he is making for the heights of Wildermore. You would do well to begin your search at the sloping path that leads from the Norcrofts into Wildermore.

'One more thing, ${PLAYER}. The air is heavy with some sorcery; I felt the chill in my bones as I approached. There is something unusual afoot in Wildermore.''I scouted ahead and found the trail of the Blood Eye cultist heads west. I believe that he is making for the heights of Wildermore. You would do well to begin your search at the sloping path that leads from the Norcrofts into Wildermore.

'One more thing, ${PLAYER}. The air is heavy with some sorcery; I felt the chill in my bones as I approached. There is something unusual afoot in Wildermore.''The Blood Eye cultist will not be able to evade us for long! Let us hurry to Wildermore, and put an end to his villainy!''I think the missing cultist came this way, ${PLAYER}! I heard some of those Orcs talking, and they were talking about a man with a red gem. They said they let him climb the road up into Wildermore.

'They did not sound that happy about it, and one of the Orcs said that they should have taken him prisoner instead and made a meal out of him! What a terrible thought! I suppose they must be allies, if they let him pass, but I do not think I would work with anyone I needed to worry about around dinner-time!''You are looking for a man with a red brooch? I saw him, indeed I did! He jumped out from behind this rock and knocked me down! Before I knew what was happening, he stole my axe and ran off to the north-east!

'I will be fine in a moment. I twisted my ankle when I fell; otherwise I would never have let him steal my weapon from me! Go catch him, and quickly!''You are lucky you see me in this state, ${RACE}! If I wasn't already breathing my last dozen breaths, I'd make sure to take you with me into the darkness at the end!

'This cursed man attacked me with an axe. He was one of ours, he was! What a laugh that is! He had a strange look in his eye. Desperation, I think it was. He stole my plunder, all I had: a bag of dirty coins. He will find no joy of them, if he wants to spend them. No one in this thrice-cursed land will take them from him.

'He'll be dead before the sun comes up again. I will take that knowledge with me into the blackness, and it will send me off with a smile! Now leave me to it, ${RACE}, and be glad you did not see me in my prime!''I have been speaking with the people of Forlaw, as a true solver of mysteries would do, and it seems that some people saw a man with a red gem visit the barber in town. Did he try to change his appearance? We have to know for sure!''I have been speaking to some of the townspeople, and it turns out that someone may have seen the man with the red gem arguing with a group of Orcs at the camp called Kadar-golnauk to the north-west.

'Shouldn't Orcs be his allies? I wonder what the subject of their argument might have been?'Willem Whisker stands by a number of abandoned sacks, but after a cursory sniff, he points his nose to the west. That must be where the cultist of the Blood Eye went after exhausting the contents of these empty bags.'The snow here is disturbed, and it looks as if he dropped this bag of coins in anger. I cannot say for certain, ${PLAYER}, but I think the man we are tracking is becoming increasingly desperate! Did he try to go to the city over yonder, to the west?'

Bingo looks toward the hilltop city of Byre Tor, and shrugs.'These tracks are clear, and they do not go into Byre Tor. No, I believe the man we are tracking through Wildermore would give that city a wide berth. For whatever reason, he is not eager to cross paths with Orcs or Uruks, if he can avoid it.

'The tracks continue to the west, and do not go into the city.''I see no sign of him here. I do not think he came to this place.''Yes, I saw this man you seek. He was just here, and very ill of temper! I did not like the look of him, and I liked his coins even less; they seemed to me to be of a sort used by Orcs and evil men, and I wanted nothing to do with them.

'I thought he would strike me when I refused to modify his appearance, but instead he snarled curses at me and stormed out. I would not repeat these foul words, but one sounded more like a place than an insult: "Kadar-golnauk" he said. Take this name with you if you find it useful, and beware of this man you seek! I feel certain he could be very dangerous if pressed into a corner.''You are a fool if you come here to speak with words and not weapons, ${RACE}!'

The Uruk is taken aback when you ask about a man wearing a brooch with a red gem in it, but he quickly recovers.

'I know the vermin, all right. He went to the north-east when we would not help him, but you won't be following his trail... not in this life!''You are a brazen ${RACE}, aren't you? Not many of your worthless breed would ask such questions!

'I saw this rat you seek, and he was more cowardly than you, for he turned away from Fushaum-dorám even as he approached. He must thought better of meeting my boys and me, because he turned and fled to the west at the first sight of us.

'You should have done the same!''I saw this man. He tried to buy the loyalty of my boys with a sack of dirty coins, but we refused. We might both serve the Blood Eye, but I know if our spots were reversed that maggot would not give us a second look. Let him die on his own in the High Knolls! He will have none of our protection.

'It may be that I will find his sack of coins and take it from his corpse, with no effort on my part! That would be a lark!'This member of the Cult of the Blood Eye saw his fortunes turn sour in the snowy heights of Wildermore, and the cruel sorceries at play in that region proved his undoing. This man will work no more evils, but the Blood Eye has others to work its will.

Even in death, the cultist reaches for the brooch of the Blood Eye lying next to him in the snow. You wonder if the man believed his actions had been worthwhile, in the end, but the only certainty is that he will not be able to tell you the answer.'You found him! Splendid work, ${PLAYER}!''Did you find him? Your expression seems both satisfied and a little unhappy, and I cannot puzzle out its meaning.'

You recount what you were able to discern concerning the cultist's final moments, and hand Egwylf the Blood Eye brooch. She nods grimly.

'You and Bingo did very well to track this man, ${PLAYER}. He came to a sad end: seemingly disavowed by his allies, his supplies gone, alone in the snow, there to die, but there is no sympathy in my heart for him. He chose his path, as must we all; his was with Mordor, and he was paid in full for that choice.

'I believe this brings the count of Blood Eye agents at work in Rohan to two, and I ask you and Bingo to keep your eyes open for them. One of them is without his brooch, so keep your wits about you! Farewell for now! If I see you again, may it be in time of peace for Rohan!''This brings the count of Blood Eye agents at work in Rohan to two, and I ask you and Bingo to keep your eyes open for them. One of them is without his brooch, so keep your wits about you! Farewell for now! If I see you again, may it be in time of peace for Rohan!'Bingo wears a philosophic expression.

'That Blood Eye cultist should not have gone off by himself. I think if he had friends with him things might not have gone that way for him. The Orcs were never his friends. They might be working for the same dark purpose, but they were not friends. It wasn't the cold of Wildermore that got to him. It was the cold in his heart!'

Bingo is silent for a moment, and then shakes his head. 

'Or maybe it was just the cold after all. It is so cold here, and I am exhausted! He took us on quite a journey through Wildermore, and my feet hurt. I am glad we solved the mystery, but I will be gladder still when our adventure takes us back to warmer climes!'

<rgb=#00FF00>After you complete this quest, Bert Bartleby will arrive near the stable-master at Forlaw, in Wildermore.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Bert Has a Word' (Recommended Level: 88)</rgb>The Warg-riders of Isengard pour through the Balewood daily, making the frozen plains of the Whitshaws especially deadly.Warg-riders can be found roaming the plains of the Whitshaws.

You should defeat Warg-riders in the Whitshaws.Defeat Orc Warg-riders in the Whitshaws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated a number of Warg-riders in the Whitshaws, but the forces of Isengard will soon return.CompletedThe Warg-riders of Isengard pour through the Balewood daily, making the frozen plains of the Whitshaws especially deadly.

You should defeat Warg-riders on the plains of the Whitshaws.You have defeated a number of Warg-riders in the Whitshaws, but the forces of Isengard will soon return.Something unnatural has killed a man upon the road in the northern Writhendowns.Whatever killed the man on the road in the northern Writhendowns is likely nearby.

You should search to the south, towards the Isingmere lake.Search for whatever may have killed the manA tower stands alone upon an island in the northern Isingmere lake, filling you with dread when you look upon it.

You should investigate the tower upon the island.Investigate the tower standing upon the islandA Daegul stands upon the island in the northern Isingmere lake.

You should speak with the Daegul.Speak with the DaegulSomething unnatural has killed this man upon this very road. He bears no marks or injuries. It appears that he has fallen where he stood.

An eerie mist encircles him, and a faint wailing can be heard nearby....'I need assistance from one who yet lives. Will you hear my plea?'Gárwig must decide what to do about Wildermore's dire situation.Gárwig wishes to debate the courses of action Wildermore must take regarding the recent White Hand attacks.

You should speak with Gárwig.Help GárwigGárwig is in the Mead Hall of Forlaw.

You should speak with Gárwig.Speak with Gárwig'You are good to me, ${PLAYER}. I might come to rely on you, if you continue to be so kind.

'Perhaps you can help us in our time of need. My sons worry that my judgement is clouded by grief. I worry the same for them.''Let us discuss our options, for we must do something. It will not do to sit here and let Wildermore fall to the servants of Saruman.'Gárwig says nothing, his face like stone.

Núrzum has truly crushed all that the old Reeve holds dear, and Wildermore seems as if it is ready to shatter at another blow.

The White Hand is winning in this land....Mossbeard wishes to return to Barallas and the others, but seeks your aid in investigating the Balewood in an attempt to better understand the Orcs and Núrzum.Mossbeard is standing at your side, or can be called with your whistle.

Mossbeard has requested your aid in expediting his journey through Balewood. If you aid in completing his tasks, he will be able to return to Barallas and the other Ents more swiftly.Call Mossbeard to your sideMossbeard is at your side.

Mossbeard has requested your aid in exploring the Balewood before returning to Barallas.Aid Mossbeard with Life in DarknessAid Mossbeard with Cruel BladesMossbeard is at your side.

Mossbeard has communed with the trees of Balewood, and seeks to discover if they have spoken true. You should speak to him and learn what is to be done.Aid Mossbeard with Old RootsMossbeard is at your side.

Mossbeard has communed with the trees and now wishes to tell you of the forest's past before relating the remainder of the story to his fellows.Aid Mossbeard with Shattered RootsStonecliff is in northern Balewood.

You have learned much of these woods in your time with Mossbeard. You should now return to Barallas and the others at Stonecliff.Return to Stonecliff with MossbeardMossbeard is at your side.

You have returned to Stonecliff and should now speak to Mossbeard before he tells the rest of his tale to the Ents.Talk to MossbeardBarallas can be found at Stonecliff in northern Balewood.

You have aided Mossbeard with all of his tasks. You should now return to Barallas at Stonecliff.Talk to Barallas'Hom. If Barallas wishes me to return to the others, I shall... however, I will do it in my own time. Hoom. There are many things to tend to before I leave these trees, and many grim things that inhabit this forest... in the monster's wake. I wish to know more of the monster, and if I can... bring such knowledge to the others.

'Should you wish to aid me in these errands, I may be able to return to Barallas in some matter of time that you might think... hasty.

'As we travel, I shall speak with the trees of this wood, and try to discover what has become of those I have lost in this fog.

'If I am not paying attention at times, do not be alarmed... I am simply deep in conversation.

'Well, little one? Shall we set off?''Hoom. You have returned with Mossbeard... I knew you would not disappoint us, little one.

'I am joyful to look upon him again, but in his eyes I see great distress.

'Mossbeard wishes to speak with all of us, does he? Well then, let us hear your tale!''A journey it has been, ${PLAYER}. Barallas and the others have much to learn of the monster, as do you still.

'Hoom. Allow me to gather my thoughts for this trip has been far more eventful than most things in this Ent's lifetime.

'Barallas looks as if he wants to speak to you. You should see what he has to tell, and I shall begin to take root... here.'Nindorn has sent you in search of his lost friend, Duskleaf, and hopes to have him join in his journey to Stonecliff.Duskleaf can be found somewhere near the Enting Hollow in north-eastern Balewood.

Nindorn has sent you in search of his lost friend, Duskleaf, and hopes to have him join in his journey to Stonecliff.Search for Duskleaf near the Enting HollowDuskleaf can be found at the Enting Hollow.

Duskleaf has appeared from the shadows, and you should now speak with him and try to convince him to rejoin the other Ents.Talk to DuskleafNindorn can be found in northern Balewood.

You have defeated the maddened Duskleaf. You should bring these ill tidings to Nindorn.Talk to Nindorn'This winter has been cruel to my kind, ${PLAYER}. When Núrzum came, many of the Ents remained still in slumber, but those of us that walked among the trees were faced with a great foe of our kind.

'The trees screamed to us for help. I hear their cries still... they did not understand. And Núrzum... we were so few, we could do nothing to stop him. We fled into the forest. All of us, save for Duskleaf. Hom. Such hasty decisions are unbefitting, I think, but he went forth. Núrzum struck him down, but his ending did not come. He fled into the forest and has avoided all of us since.

'I would ask that you try to convince our friend to return. Núrzum must be dealt with in time, and we will need all of our kind to stand against him. If Duskleaf is to be found, I would guess he is near the Enting Hollow, little one.''Hoom. Another little one come to hack at my branches...?

'You dare speak to me of rejoining the others? Hom. It matters not where we choose to plant ourselves if nothing is to be done. This forest is death.

'They... they are too weak to act! Hoom, but I am not! I am wont to smother any invader of these woods beneath the snow!''So he was lost... to madness. Tragic, my friend....

'Núrzum's rampage hewed his mind long ago then... hom. You made the only choice you could, little one.

'As impatient as Quickbeam that one was, but bold nonetheless... I will miss him greatly. Thank you for telling me of these happenings, ${PLAYER}... I will return to Barallas now.'In the wake of Voskor's death and the White Hand's defeat by the Ents, a moment of peace has returned to the Balewood along with the hope that the Byre Tor refugees may finally rejoin their fellows.Nindorn is at Stonecliff with the other Ents.

In the wake of the attack on the White Hand, Barallas has asked that you speak to the other Ents before returning to the refugees of Byre Tor in Balewood.Talk to NindornMossbeard is at Stonecliff in the Balewood.

In the wake of the attack on the White Hand, Barallas has asked that you speak to the other Ents before returning to the refugees of Byre Tor in the Balewood.Talk to MossbeardBarallas is at Stonecliff in the Balewood.

After speaking with the other Ents, Barallas has asked to speak with you one more time before sending you on your way out of the forest.Talk to BarallasHúna is in the hidden cave with the other refugees of Byre Tor in southern Balewood.

Barallas and the Ents have bid you farewell and offered a suggestion of how to weaken Núrzum for you to share with the Rohirrim of Wildermore. You should now return to the cave where the refugees have taken shelter and have them leave the forest while peace still holds.Talk to Húna'Hoom. A great weight has been lifted from these branches, little one. The Orcs have been pushed from our lands, and they shall never again defile this forest. Not while the others and I remain... hom. I believe the Orcs will soon come to understand how long we have wandered these woods.

'Your aid in depriving the Orcs of their flames and axes allowed us the chance to defeat them, and you have all of our thanks. You will always be welcome in these woods, little friend.

'Before you return to your fellows and lead them from the forest, the others would like to speak to you. Mossbeard and Nindorn have asked to speak with you. I do not doubt the others have much to say, but I would imagine that you shall be back in the lands you call home before they can decide on what needs to be said.''Goodness, it is you! We thought the worst when we had not seen you for so long, but what has happened? What have you learned?'

You tell Húna and the others of your adventures with the Ents and the origins of Núrzum.

'The monster may be defeated after all... I never thought we could see the day. Now then, you said that the trees themselves would hold back the Wood-trolls for us?

'I have other business for you... but the rest of you, pack your belongings as swiftly as you are able! We make for Forlaw as soon as we are ready!''I see you have spoken to the others, ${PLAYER}. They seem upset to see you leave, as am I. There is... however, the matter of Núrzum.

'As you may recall when we first met, I told you that Strongroot spoke of a horn... a horn that greatly frightened the Huorns of these woods. As Núrzum is both giant and Huorn, so may he find himself torn asunder, both in mind and body, by the blaring of such a horn. I cannot be certain if this will bode true for him, but stranger things I have seen in these past days.

'Tell the Men of these lands, and perhaps they, out of all of us, can finally stand against him. Go, my friend. Return to the Men you found lost in these woods. We shall hold back the Wood-trolls long enough for them to escape. May our paths cross in brighter days, ${PLAYER}.''You are a very strange ${RACE}, my friend. Hoom. To be reunited with the others and to have solved some mysteries of this destruction has brought me some measure of hope for what the future brings.

'Núrzum must be stopped. The others will not follow me in this, but you must see him slain and the stone destroyed. Barallas knows well of what might be used in his defeat now, but I will leave such explanations to him.

'Know this, little one. You have shaped the lands of Men and Ents for years to come through your actions, and as strange fellows as we may be, I would be glad to look upon you again. May your journey bode well, my friend.''You have done much to aid the Ents, ${PLAYER}... hoom. Yet, we must never forget the cost of these times and what Núrzum has taken from us. The monster's corruption may as well have slain Duskleaf the day he came to this forest. When you came upon him, his mind had been stripped bare and his fury over the forest's fate consumed him.

'You and the Men of these lands cannot be blinded by Núrzum's foul influence -- he must be put to an end, for such a creature stands in stark defiance of all that is good and natural in this realm.

'As with the others, you have my thanks for freeing us from the Orcs and their leader, but the greatest threat to us all still lurks beyond this wood. Go with haste, little one. Hom. Perhaps when Núrzum falls, you will find us here again.'You have come across a ragged old man on a cliff overlooking a waterfall to the south of Snowbourn. Surely, he knows it is unwise to be outside with so many Orcs about.Approach the old crofterThe old crofter is just ahead, standing atop a cliff to the south of Snowbourn.Approach the old crofterThe Orc attackers have moved to attack the elderly crofter standing atop a cliff to the south of Snowbourn.Defeat the Orc attackers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The crofter is standing alone on a cliff overlooking a waterfall to the south of Snowbourn.Talk to the old crofterYou have come across a ragged old man on a cliff overlooking a waterfall to the south of Snowbourn. Surely, he knows it is unwise to be outside with so many Orcs about.The stranger stares blankly at you, considering the situation that has just occurred. He pauses a moment, mouths something silently, and then begins to speak.

'Thank you for your aid, stranger. Your assault was opportunely timed, I must say. Those Orcs had truly taken me by surprise.

'I had come to visit a... friend of mine, but sadly, his home to the north appears to have been destroyed and I have heard naught of him in some time now...

'You need not worry about me, stranger. The Orcs will trouble me not on my journey home, of this I am sure.'Now that Gárwig knows of an unknown artifact being the cause of the fell winter in Wildermore, he wants you and Cyneberg to search for it in the Whitshaws, where the lands are most afflicted by the cold.Cyneberg can be found outside of Lornsettle, looking out at the Isingmere.

You should speak with Cyneberg.Speak with Cyneberg outside of LornsettleA summoning horn for calling Cyneberg can be found at the west gate of Forlaw.

You should go to the west gate when you are ready to depart and call Cyneberg to your side.Use the horn to call Cyneberg at the west gateCyneberg can be found with you at the west gate in Forlaw. You may use the whistle to call her to your side if you become separated.

You should speak with Cyneberg.Speak with Cyneberg at your sideCyneberg can be called by using your whistle. The cross-roads can be reached by following the road out of Forlaw into the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Cyneberg once you reach the first cross-roads.Talk to Cyneberg at the cross-roads in the WhitshawsCyneberg can be called by using your whistle. The cross-roads can be reached by following the road out of Forlaw into the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Cyneberg once you reach the first cross-roads.Ride with Cyneberg to the cross-roadsCyneberg can be called by using your whistle. The cross-roads can be reached by following the road out of Forlaw into the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Cyneberg once you reach the first cross-roads.Talk to Cyneberg at the cross-roadsCyneberg can be found at the cross-roads in the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Cyneberg.Talk to Cyneberg at the cross-roads'My greatest concern is to find out more about this mysterious artifact of which the enemy spoke, ${PLAYER}. You should begin your search in the Whitshaws, where the lands are the most cold and bitter.

'Cyneberg should accompany you, for her mind is full of Thrymm. Perhaps a quest such as this will distract her from her thoughts.''Yes, I would very much like to come with you, ${PLAYER}. I will not be able to help Thrymm while I am standing idly in Forlaw.

'Call at the west gate when you are ready to depart, and we will ride together. We should gird ourselves for swift travel and stealth if we are to avoid the enemy.''I hope we both find what we are looking for. Just imagine!

'I will find Thrymm and bring him home, and you will learn how to weaken Núrzum. Together, we will save Wildermore....

'Let us ride along the road for now.''Strange... let us pause here. Something is not right.''The lands here have been violated by some power. Perhaps you should begin your search for the artifact near here, friend ${CLASS}.'Edhild believes the goat-herd of Byre Tor can still be found nearby.Tame goats from the herd can be found in the northern High Knolls near the refugees' cave.

You should catch some of the frightened goats from the Byre Tor herd and lead them back to the cave.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: The goats will find their way if you become separated.</rgb>Catch frightened goats (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The refugees are in the cave on the peak north of Byre Tor in the High Knolls.

You should lead the goats you caught to the refugees' cave.Lead the goats to the cave (they will find their way if you become separated)Edhild is in the refugees' cave in the northern High Knolls.

You should speak with Edhild.Talk to Edhild'What we need most of all is milk for the child, or if worse comes to worst, meat for the spit. I tended a goat herd for Byre Tor, and I'm sure some are still alive.

'Go out and catch some of the goats, and bring them back. They're good creatures, and they'll follow ye.'Edhild gives the goats a fond smile.

'Poor frightened dears! We'll do our best not to eat ye, little goats. We can keep each other alive, eh?'Cyneberg believes the refugees saw Núrzum's violence on the outskirts of town and wisely decided to flee.The refugees likely fled into the woods south-west of Dunfast, away from the ice-spire where Núrzum sat and terrorized the people of town.

You should search for signs of the refugees in the trees.Search the south-western woods for signs of the refugeesCyneberg can be called by using the whistle she gave you.

You should whistle for Cyneberg and show her what you found.Whistle for Cyneberg and speak with herThe refugees seem to have fled further into the woods to the south of Dunfast.

You should search for more signs of them.Search for another sign of the refugeesCyneberg can be found at your side. You can use the whistle to summon her.

You should speak with Cyneberg.Talk to CynebergThe source of the voices can be found south of Dunfast.

You should head towards the voices in the woods.The refugees can be found at their small encampment south-west of Dunfast in the Whitshaws.

You should speak with a refugee of Dunfast.Talk to a refugee in the camp'I will tell you what I think: The people of Dunfast saw Núrzum's violence and felt the cold outside of town, and they ran, probably into the woods.

'We should find them at once! It is likely that they need our help, for it seems that they did not have enough time to prepare.

'Let us both begin searching. If you see any signs of them, give a whistle and I will join you.''Ah! A cloak snagged on a tree -- that is a good sign. It is certain they ran this way. Let us continue south-west.''Do you hear that, ${PLAYER}? Voices! Can it be they?''Ah! Here we are, then.

