The Orc force has been repelled, and Winsig and her people are free, at least for the moment, from the danger that consumed their home.Talk to Winsig'Let us speak more of this attack in a moment. For now, I must ensure my people are safe.''Our message was received! Thank goodness someone has come... we have no time to waste, we must move now or risk losing all to the Orcs!''Time and time again, ${PLAYER}, your aid has proven invaluable to me and my people.

'The Orcs cannot be allowed any more footholds in the Sutcrofts... to this day, I still mourn Hytbold. That is a matter for another time, though....

'In the wake of such destruction, I must tend to my people.'Many were lost in the battle at Scylfig, but none were more important to the people of Wildermore than their hero Thrymm Red-beard. A funeral is being planned by Gárwig and Cyneberg so that the people of Forlaw can pay their respects and say good-bye to those who lost their lives.Every living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Speak with CynebergEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Walk with Cyneberg to the funeralEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Listen to Gárwig speakEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Join Gárwig near the twins' gravesEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Listen to Gárwig speak of Edsig and GárferthEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Join the others at Cynegar's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Listen to Gárwig speak about CynegarEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Pick up the stone for Cynegar's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Place the final stone on Cynegar's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Join Gárwig near the grandson's gravesEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Listen to Gárwig speak of Humbald and GrimgarEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Join and watch those gathered for GársigEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Listen to Gárwig speak about GársigEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Pick up the stone for Gársig's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Place the final stone on Gársig's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Join and watch those gathered for ThrymmEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Listen to Gárwig speak about ThrymmEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Pick up the stone for Thrymm's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Place the final stone on Thrymm's graveEvery living member of Gárwig's family has come to the graveyard today to pay their respects and say good-bye to those they loved.Leave the funeral with Cyneberg'Let us make our way to Thrymm's home in Forlaw.''I think most of the Reeve's family has gathered, and we are about to get started.'

'Please, walk with me to the others.''It is done. The fact they are gone seems a little more real now than it did earlier....

'I need to go home and sit by a warm fire for a while.'Théoden has mustered a great host, more than a thousand spears, to ride to Isengard and bring war upon the Wizard Saruman.Théoden is outside Edoras, ready to ride to war.Talk to Théoden outside EdorasÉomer is outside Edoras, with the great host that has assembled to ride with Théoden.Talk to Éomer outside EdorasAragorn, Legolas, and Gimli are by the side of the river outside Edoras, waiting for the host to depart.Talk to Legolas outside EdorasTalk to Aragorn outside EdorasTalk to Gimli outside EdorasThéoden is outside Edoras, among the host that plans to ride to Isengard.Talk to Théoden among the hostGléowine is somewhere outside Edoras.

Théoden has asked for you to bring Gléowine before him.Find and talk to Gléowine outside EdorasThéoden has called Gléowine before him.Listen to Théoden issue commands before the hostThéoden wishes to speak with you before the host departs.Talk to Théoden before the host'Farewell, until we meet once more, ${PLAYER}. Watch over the people of Rohan while Théoden is away, and trust not too easily! There are foes hidden in plain sight. We have Saruman, and Gríma, to thank for that.''Gandalf was right, ${PLAYER}. I have sat in the shadows of my hall for too long. It is good to breathe the clean air once more, and to feel the breeze upon my face. Gríma's whisperings made me forget, but now the memory of good has returned to me, and in greater force than before!

'Walk with me a ways, ${PLAYER}. I have a request to make of you.''I know you are needed elsewhere, ${PLAYER}. Still I would feel more at ease if you rode with us.'

Éomer hesitates, and you can see him struggling with the weight of a decision.

'I do not want to add my own worries to those that already concern you, but if you find yourself in the Eastfold, perhaps you will cast an eye toward my own home? The town of Aldburg is my home, when I am not in Edoras, and it is now left in the care of my steward Arcil. It has been a long while since I returned to Aldburg, and I worry that the dangers that threaten the rest of the Mark may also affect my home. You need not go there in haste! Help my sister with her own task first, but it would ease my mind if I thought you might go to Aldburg afterward.

'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.''There you are, ${PLAYER}! Did you not recognize me? Théoden gave us raiment of war from his own hoard. I prefer my own garb, but we ride now to war and this shining mail will serve me better. Still I appreciate the gift.''I understand your path leads elsewhere, ${PLAYER}, but still we may yet meet again. There are dangers aplenty in Rohan, and not enough good-hearted folk to defeat them. If only there were two of you, or four, or six!'

Aragorn laughs, and some of the Riders nearby look at him curiously.

'Sounds of merriment are rare in these times, ${PLAYER}, and defeating Saruman will not change that. Gandalf is quick to remind me that the real foe lies East and not West, but if Rohan is to survive we must do something about the enemy that is nearest. And we will.''The armour of this land is not made for dwarves, ${PLAYER}, but the king gave to me a shield bearing his device: a white horse on a green field. I am proud to bear it, but I am less pleased at the prospect of being borne by such a creature on the journey to Isengard!

'I have had enough of bouncing around on the back of such beasts like a sack of meal! Éomer says that I may ride with him, and I have agreed to take him up on his offer, as long as Legolas will ride beside us. Together we will teach young Éomer how mistaken he has been concerning the Lady of the Golden Wood. We will be good company, assuming he learns this lesson well, and quickly!''The host is assembled and ready to ride, ${PLAYER}, but there is still one matter I would see resolved. Will you find Gléowine and bring him before me?

'There is still the matter of his punishment to be settled.''The King has chosen this moment to reveal his punishment? I am prepared, ${PLAYER}. Whatever my King decides will be just.''It is done, ${PLAYER}. Each of the three of you: Gléowine, Horn, and the ${RACE} with whom I now speak, will serve Rohan in the ways for which you are best suited, and scrub away the black mark incurred by defying my command.

'Now it is time to depart. Aid my sister-daughter Éowyn as the people journey to Dunharrow, and wish us well in the battle! With good fortune we will overcome, and put an end to Saruman's treacheries at long last!'The snows and the enemies have come to Byre Tor once more.A cold wind blows from the north-west of town.

You should speak to the Uruk in the north-west of Byre Tor.Talk to the Uruk standing in the roadA cold wind blows from the north-west of town.

You should speak to the Uruk in the north-west of Byre Tor.Defeat the Uruk scoutTwo more uruks stand in the road in north-west Byre Tor.Try to escape Byre Tor by heading north-westRun away from Núrzum!

You should approach the glowing path markers.Run along the road, away from NúrzumUruks approach from behind!

You should defeat the attacking Uruks.Defeat attackers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Núrzum is close behind!

You should run in the opposite direction.Run away from Núrzum, east!Uruks approach from behind!

You should defeat the attacking Uruks.Defeat attackers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Run east, away from Núrzum's forces!Run east!Run north, to the edge of Byre Tor.Run northJump off the edge of Byre Tor's cliff.Jump off the cliff'I hope you're ready to die.

'The boys are right behind me, and behind them... well, you'll just have to hope that you survive long enough to meet him, eh?'The fragment of stone you recovered from Iothete's home has a story behind it, and you are ready to hear it.Iothete is in his hut in the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Iothete.Listen to IotheteIothete is in the woods of the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Iothete and listen to his memory of a fateful day.Listen to Iothete's taleIothete is in the woods of the Whitshaws.

You should speak with Iothete and listen to his memory of a fateful day.Listen to Iothete's taleIothete is in the woods in the northern Whitshaws.

You should speak with Iothete.Follow IotheteIothete is in the woods in the northern Whitshaws.

You should speak with Iothete.Talk to IotheteYou should follow Iothete's memory into his home in Dunfast.Listen to IotheteYou should follow Iothete's memory into his home in Dunfast.Listen to Iothete's memoryYou should follow Iothete's memory into his home in Dunfast.Listen to Iothete's memoryYou should follow Iothete's memory into his home in Dunfast.Talk to Iothete'And now you see what the stone has wrought? The cold came over our lands not long ago, and it was as unnatural as this stone that froze my home a year ago.

'Is it my fault? Is it because I told him so much?''I will tell you everything I remember, ${PLAYER}. You will wish me dead when you hear what I did to Wildermore.'Iothete's voice paints a picture in your mind of his walk on the fateful day a year or so ago.

The woods of the Whitshaws feel very different than the bare white tines protruding from the snow today.'Yes... do come rest at my home. We can discuss whatever you'd like.'Iothete concludes his tale:

'The memory is strange, foggy, unsettling. I do not feel like I was there -- merely looking on from a distance while I spoke too much of things that do not concern strangers.

'I told my neighbours, but they dismissed me as being mad. The stone terrified me. It made me cold.

'So I left.

'Now it is cold everywhere, and it is my fault.'You have returned to Meduseld against the wishes of Théoden, and now must see if he is receptive to the warning you bring.Horn is inside Meduseld, waiting to approach the king.Talk to Horn and approach the kingInside Meduseld, Gandalf appeals to Théoden. Will the king heed the warning he brings?Witness the confrontation of Gandalf and Théoden, and allow harm to come to noneInside Meduseld, Gandalf has revealed his nature and his good intentions to Théoden King. What will the king make of the tidings he brings?Talk to Gandalf in MeduseldInside Meduseld, Gandalf appeals to Théoden. Will the king heed the warning he brings?Witness the confrontation of Gandalf and Théoden, and allow harm to come to noneGandalf is in Meduseld and has attempted to lift the veil from Théoden's eyes as it concerns the plight of his people.Talk to Gandalf in Meduseld'Rohan, and Théoden, are not free of danger yet. Still we have work to do.''We are not alone in the hall of Théoden, ${PLAYER}. Let us approach and see who has the ear of the king, but we will interfere only if it be in the interest of Rohan to do so!''Ah, ${PLAYER}. I am glad you made it here from Wildermore so quickly, but I will need to hear your report of that land at another time. All of us have work to do, for the king is not yet free of danger! Keep an eye on Gríma, if you would. He is slippery, and I fear he may intend further harm to the king. Whether he can work greater harm than he has already performed is a question for which I have no answer.

'Stay on your guard.''So that is done. I think that went rather well, considering what is at stake. We have so little time to waste, and much depends on haste! We cannot spend these precious moments squabbling among ourselves when the real foes lie without!

'Save one: Gríma, son of Gálmód, called the Wormtongue. He is an enemy of Théoden and of Rohan, and he remains within. His fate will rest with the king, as it should.'The Warg-riders at Edsig's Farm were disrupted by your efforts, and their organized raids on the farms should stop. The farmers should finally be able to repair Thrymm's Farm and begin work on the fields.The rebuilding of Thrymm's Farm is underway. Oter has brought tools to the fields, and would like your help preparing the fields to produce crops for Forlaw.Talk to OterThe rebuilding of Thrymm's Farm is underway. Oter has brought tools to the fields, and would like your help preparing the fields to produce crops for Forlaw.Follow Oter to the western fieldsThe farmers were prepared to begin working the fields, but the enemy has begun a surprise attack on Thrymm's Farm!Defeat Warg-riders from the west (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The farmers are gathering near the barn, and are concerned that Ragnold may not be safe. He was on the far side of the farm when the western side was attacked.Follow Oter to the centre of Thrymm's FarmThe enemy is attacking from the south and are trying to trap you and the farmers on Thrymm's Farm while they burn it down.Defeat Warg-riders from the south (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Oter and the others have been cornered near the barn. The enemy is burning the farm to the ground. The chances of the farmers escaping and making a run for Forlaw are slim.Talk to OterLaudh has sent the captain of this band of Warg-riders to attack you and the farmers. He seems intent on capturing the farmers for some nefarious purpose.Defend Thrymm's Farm from the final assaultLaudh is capturing the farmers and leaving you at Thrymm's Farm, to either die there or to inform Forlaw of what happened. He is convinced Núrzum will kill you eventually.Observe what Laudh has to say'A few of the tools you collected were in pretty good shape, and should help us prepare these fields. Torwulf and Siric took tools out to the far field with them to begin working. Lets hurry over there to help them!''We are in trouble. They have us surrounded! The only way we can survive is to fight. There is no way we can escape this time....'You are investigating what has happened to Dunfast, the village where Gléowine was born.Gléowine is outside the village, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Gléowine outside DunfastCorudan senses that you are being watched.Talk to Corudan in DunfastHorn, Corudan, and Gléowine are exploring Dunfast, and you should do the same.Explore Dunfast with Horn, Corudan, and GléowineHorn believes he has found Corudan's mysterious watcher, and is ready to seize the opportunity to reveal it!Talk to Horn in DunfastHorn is ready to seize the mysterious watcher in Dunfast.Be ready for anything! Horn is prepared to seize the mysterious watcher in DunfastHorn could use your help to stand up and salvage as much of his dignity as possible.You still sense a watchful presence in Dunfast, and should find who or what it is that watches.You sense someone staring at the four of youNona has returned, but she does not seem happy to see you. What will this mean for Horn?Listen to what befell Nona after she left your fellowship in the Entwash ValeHorn is down-hearted following your encounter with Nona in the empty village of Dunfast.Talk to Horn in DunfastTalk to Corudan in DunfastTalk to Gléowine in Dunfast'I feel eyes on us, ${PLAYER}, but I cannot tell if they be hostile or friendly eyes. Stay on your guard.''One look at Nona's face told me she had not been prepared to see Horn in Dunfast. She has not yet resolved her feelings for him. Caring for the injured folk she mentioned may have caused her to delay in resolving her thoughts, or perhaps it is so difficult a riddle she needs more time to untangle a solution, even without the distraction of responsibility.

'Horn may appear inconsolable, but he need not despair. I have not given up on our Dunlending fire-brand, and neither should he.''From here Dunfast looks much as I remember it, but... where are all the people? Let us walk into the village and see if anyone remains who can tell us what happened!''That is the woman who has taken Horn's heart from him? He has spoken of her a great deal to me since we have been journeying together, but not once did he mention she was a Dunlending tribes-woman!

'And she came to this town, the town of my childhood, seeking to steal its goods and provisions?'

Gléowine shakes his head.

'Ah, I remember what it was like to be young, when blood and passions flared in spring-time. I am not fit to judge them. If Horn misses her so, I hope she will reconsider her dismissal of him.''I think I have found the location of our mysterious watcher, ${PLAYER}. There is someone hiding in this bush. Be ready for anything, and I will seize this person before he can pose a threat!''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. It is better to be safe and to bruise one's dignity a small amount than to be surprised by some dangerous foe, is it not?

'This time it was a shrew. Next time it might be a hidden enemy. Perhaps Corudan can flush the next watcher out of hiding, and I will be the one laughing?''I don't understand, ${PLAYER}. It has been days since last I beheld Nona, and my affection for her has only grown during that time. The fondness she felt for me before we came to Eaworth... has it gone? Did I imagine it? I cannot have!'

Horn is greatly distressed, but you have no easy words of comfort.'I do not know what to think, ${PLAYER}. That reunion did not proceed as I might have wished.'You have travelled to the Mournshaws following the rumour of a great spirit that might know what shakes the earth in Dunland.You and Helchon have come to the Mournshaws in search of answers, but the journey is wearing on the young Ranger.Talk to HelchonHelchon is exhausted. The frequent attacks by corrupted creatures have drained his desire to proceed.Talk to HelchonA majestic stag has appeared in the forest of the Mournshaws.Slowly approach the majestic stagThe majestic stag is leading you deeper into the forest.Follow the stag deeper into the forestThe Huntsman awaits your request, Calenglad.Talk to the mighty figure of the HuntsmanHelchon is somewhere in the Hall of the Huntsman.Talk to Helchon in the Hall of the Huntsman'You say you have a feeling about this place, Calenglad, but I cannot sense anything! You say you feel a mysterious presence? I feel the chill shadows of the trees and smell old growth, but nothing else.

'Are you sure this is the place of which Daervunn spoke? There is nothing here but trees, corrupted beasts, and we two. I say we leave the trees to the miserable bears, and be done with it!''I... I do not know what to say, Calenglad. I have never seen such wonders. I do not want to leave, but I know we must. We do not belong in this place, with so much beauty around us.

'Did you learn anything of use from the Huntsman? He seems unreal to me, as if he is only partway in this world of ours... I never would have believed any of this, to hear you tell it, Calenglad, but I have seen it with my own eyes. You must forgive me my doubts.

'This journey has not reassured me, Calenglad. Now I am more worried than ever! If such a mighty figure as the Huntsman can really exist, who knows what terror the Falcon Clan could have unleashed in the caves beneath Tâl Methedras?'You speak, slowly and haltingly at first, but gradually with greater confidence and urgency. You tell the mighty figure of the Huntsman of your concern that the Falcon Clan has wrought some great evil in the caves of Tâl Methedras, and that the shaking in the earth may spell the doom of your friends.

The Huntsman is silent for a long while before speaking.'Calenglad, we have been journeying for hours, fighting the corrupted denizens of this forest, and we are no closer to learning anything! I know Daervunn spoke of seeing a great spirit in these woods, but we have seen no sign of such a lord.

'Whatever beast or magic shakes the stones of Tâl Methedras, we will learn nothing of it in these woods.'Prestadír has woken you for your turn at the watch. You have five hours to spend until the dawn.Prestadír has woken you for your turn at watch.Talk to PrestadírIt is now your turn to keep watch while the rest of the Grey Company sleeps. You will waken Halbarad at the dawn.Stand watch until dawnDid you see something move in the darkness?Investigate your uneasy feelingYou need to wake up your comrades before the attack!Halbarad has instructions for you, and you should receive them quickly! The Falcon Clan attack is imminent!Talk to Halbarad and prepare for the Falcon Clan attackHalbarad is waiting to speak with you following the attack by the Falcon Clan.Talk to Halbarad'There is no honour to be had by attacking a sleeping foe, Halros, but we should have expected nothing else from the Falcon Clan. Mair learned that tonight, to her sorrow.

'We have not repaid Lheu Brenin and the Falcon Clan for our losses, not by a wide throw, but we have shown them our claws. We are all fortunate you have such alert senses, Halros!

'I am glad you journeyed south with us, for this reason and many others. Look! Dawn breaks. Your vigil for the night is ended! Let us break our fast and see to the coming of the day.''I am sorry to wake you, Halros, but it is time for your watch to begin. These last few hours have been quiet, and it was a struggle at times to keep my eyes open. I will be glad of the sleep.

'There is a good place to watch among the trees just to the west. I will show you.''I thought this would happen one of these nights. We may not have much time before the Falcon Clan strikes!

'You three slip down the hill to the side and see if you can approach our attackers from behind.

'Halros! Prestadír! Come with me, and we will see if they can listen to reason.'You have agreed to walk with Bounder Primstone on his rounds to Oatbarton today.Bounder Primstone is in the Greenfields, waiting for you to walk with him to Oatbarton.Talk to Bounder Primstone in the GreenfieldsIt is getting late, and you are not yet at Oatbarton. Bounder Primstone wants to take a short rest.Talk to Bounder PrimstoneYou have walked with Bounder Primstone about halfway to Oatbarton, but you have heard the sound of wolves on the wind.Talk to Bounder PrimstoneYou have arrived at the outskirts of Oatbarton with Bounder Primstone.Talk to Bounder Primstone outside OatbartonBill Ferny is on the outskirts of Oatbarton, and if you know him, the Man is up to no good.Talk to Bill Ferny outside OatbartonBounder Primstone is outside Oatbarton.Talk to Bounder Primstone outside Oatbarton'Oh my, where has the time gone? Already the sun is beginning to set. Let us take a short rest and then we will walk the rest of the way to Oatbarton. What do you say, Halros?''Look up ahead. Is that one of the Big Folk? You are an exception, Halros. Usually they do not trouble to come into the Shire, and we are pleased to not be troubled by them!

'I suppose we should see what he wants, Halros.''You have my thanks for sending him away, Halros. I'm glad you were here. He was up to no good, you mark my words.

'We fought some wolves tonight, but I think maybe not every wolf we encountered wore fur. That one's teeth might prove sharpest yet.

'We should see about finding a place to stay for tonight, before we head back to the Greenfields on the morrow. Let us find an accomodating burrow, with a laden table full of supper, if we can!''You don't scare me, Ranger. I've seen plenty of your type 'round Bree-way, and it seems to me you bleed just like everybody else. Maybe easier.'

Ferny laughs an unpleasant laugh.

'I'm meeting a friend here, but I can see you're just dying to stick your nose into my business, so I'll move along. I'll be sure to tell my friend who it was what caused this inconvenience. You can count on that, Ranger.''Good afternoon to you, Halros! It is good to see you again. I do enjoy the company. The walk to Oatbarton is quite a long one, but it goes more quickly with company.

'I am always pleased when you join me for walking my rounds. I have not met very many of the Big Folk, but of those few you are as pleasant and kind a fellow as I could hope to meet. You are quite all right in my book, Halros.

'You will forgive me, as you usually do, if I share some of the news of the day? That is a relief. There is so much going on in the Shire, and keeping it all straight helps to clean the cobwebs out of my head. Relaying some of it to you serves as mental exercise of the highest order!''Is that the sound of wolves? Sometimes they come down into the Shire, but it does not happen very often. If they do come upon us, you can stand behind me, Halros. Wolves can be frightening, but I am a Bounder and have been trained to deal with the creatures.

'I am sorry, but the sandwiches will have to wait.'Halbarad has led the Grey Company through many dangers already on its journey to aid Aragorn, but many treacherous miles still lie ahead.Elrohir is waiting to speak with you on the road up the hill in Tûr Morva.Talk to Elrohir on the road through Tûr MorvaSaeradan is north-east of Elrohir, in Tûr Morva. Golodir is up the hill in Tûr Morva. Calenglad is by the entrance to the ruins.

Elrohir is pleased that you have decided to leave Tâl Methedras at last and thinks the others will be similarly pleased.Talk to Saeradan in Tûr MorvaTalk to Golodir up the hill in Tûr MorvaTalk to Calenglad by the entrance to the ruins atop the hillRadanir is among the buildings atop the hill in Tûr Morva.

Radanir has returned from his expedition in the mountains.Talk to Radanir in Tûr Morva, south of CalengladThe time has come to decide what each member of the Grey Company should do in the coming days.Await the gathering of the Dúnedain atop the hill in Tûr MorvaThe Rangers have gathered atop the hill in Tûr Morva.

You have helped Radanir fight off a small group of the Falcon Clan, and should now give the Grey Company some of the direction they seek by speaking with Prestadír.'Did you feel those rumblings in the earth, Halbarad? They are getting worse. It almost sounds as if, well...'

Calenglad trails off, embarrassed. You prompt him to continue, but his tone remains sheepish.

'Well, it sounds as if a great beast was running beneath the earth, shaking the ground with the fury of its passage. Could such a creature be?

'You may think such musings are the province of children's tales, but I have seen many strange and wondrous things in my life, Halbarad, and learning of these wonders keeps me putting one foot before the other. If there is such a beast, we must know if it poses a threat to our people and the good people of Dunland. Mustn't we?''My brother returned from his sojourn in the mountains, and he says a small group of warriors makes their way hither. "More of the Falcon Clan?" I asked him, but he was not certain. He has gone back with Radanir to cut them off before they arrive.'

Elrohir pauses, and you can tell he is struggling to find the right words. Soon he continues.

'We have been through much, my friend, and will certainly see more danger before we find Aragorn. But I feel I must ask: why do we linger in Tâl Methedras? I understand that much hurt was done to us here, and the desire to avenge our fallen is great, but delay may prove as deadly.''Corunir and I have been collecting the weapons the Falcon Clan left behind when we drove them into the caves. This accursed shaking in the earth is making it more difficult than it need be.'

Golodir listens as you explain your decision for the Grey Company to continue on its road, and his face becomes a thundercloud.

'That is an impossibility, Halbarad! We cannot leave yet! Lheu Brenin still lives, and his cronies! You cannot have forgotten the wounds he gave us, and the lives his treachery cost. We must have our vengeance upon him before we go!''This is the situation in which we find ourselves, Halbarad? The Falcon Clan harries us from the mountains and caves into which they fled. The earth shakes and rumbles more and more with each passing day. Our numbers diminish the further south we travel, brothers and friends lost to death or to urgent need in distant lands.

'You are our leader. What road or roads do we take from here? How then does the Grey Company proceed?'

Prestadír looks at you expectantly, and you know that you must dispatch some members of your group to deal with each of these situations. For now, you must think upon how best to deal with the various roads ahead.'The rumbling in the earth is getting worse, Halbarad. I do not like it.'

Saeradan listens as you explain that it is time for the Grey Company to leave Tâl Methedras.

'That is wise. I have already decided it was time to leave, and I wanted to see if you agreed with me. I wish to leave Tâl Methedras, but I wish to travel north... not south.'

Saeradan sees your puzzled expression and hurries to explain.

'Many of our brothers have fallen on this road, and it grieves me to think they must be laid to rest in these hostile southern lands. They are Dúnedain! They should be brought back to the northern lands they held dear. I propose to bring their bodies by waggon back to their homes.''You have stumbled into the middle of a surprise attack, Halbarad! Are you willing to fight alongside me?'

Radanir laughs.

'Oh, you are on the right side of the ambush, this time. We are doing the ambushing! Elladan and I have lured a small group of the Falcon Clan back here. They think they have us on the run, but they are about to be surprised! Are you ready?'You and Radanir have arranged the use of a waggon to bring your fallen brothers back to their homes in the north.Radanir has sensed something is wrong, and he is standing by the stopped waggon.Talk to Radanir by the stopped waggonShades of the oath-breakers you encountered on the Forsaken Road have appeared all around you! What evil do they pose to you now?Survive your second encounter with the oath-breakersBritou stands before you, a figure of malice that has caused you grief in the recent past. You cannot forgive him, but he appears to offer you no immediate threat.Talk to BritouThe oathbreakers fade from your sight, returning to the Mountain from which they fled.Allow the oathbreakers to passRadanir has had an idea, and wants to tell it to you.Talk to RadanirRadanir is beneath the earth along the Forsaken Road.Talk to Radanir along the Forsaken RoadYou should find the bodies of your fallen friends and pay your respects, and Radanir will begin carrying them out of the Forsaken Road.You have honoured your kinsmen, and should now speak with Radanir along the Forsaken Road.Talk to Radanir along the Forsaken Road, where Candaith fell'I am sorry I have made us stop, Saeradan, but something is not right. Do you feel it? I feel a chill in my very bones. I have not felt such a dread since... I do not wish to say.'

Radanir shudders.

'We have driven this waggon with our fallen brothers this far already, and we have much farther to go yet... but I want to set my mind at ease before we proceed. Do you agree, Saeradan? Then walk with me for a short way. We will not go far.''I offer you no words of contrition, Dúnadan. If you seek out Men such as we are, you deserve any ill that befalls you. Learn from the lesson, and put your trust in those who deserve it.

'We abandoned the Mountain, thinking to escape the chain of our suffering, but there was no escape from it. There was no end to our days, no relief from the night. Only fools stumbled upon us, and those paid with their lives. If you seek the Dead, only death awaits you.

'But my people learned from yours that our curse may yet be lifted. If an heir of Isildur's lives still, as your kin pretended, the glimmer of that hope may be rekindled. We love you not, but we will not slay you this night. We return to the Mountain. We will not come this way again.''I did not think to encounter those dead men again, Saeradan. Many of our friends and brothers were slain at their hands. How many went beneath the earth along the Forsaken Road, and did not return into the daylight? Good men, all.'

Radanir pauses, and you can read his thoughts on his face, as plain as day even in the gloom of night.

'The oath-breakers have abandoned the Forsaken Road, Saeradan. Now is the time for us to return there and rescue the bodies we were forced to leave behind. We must bring them back above-ground and to the waggon of our fallen. We must do this at once! Let us go, and quickly!''This place holds mainly painful memories, Saeradan. Let us find the bodies of our fallen kinsmen and leave as quickly as we are able.'You gaze upon the fallen form of Hodhon, and think of the delight upon his face each time he recovered a curious artifact from the ruins of Annúminas. Many were the nights you spent warming yourself by the fire while Calenglad explained to Hodhon the historical significance of the artifact he had found, and the memory brings a smile to your face even now.

This grim road should not be his tomb. You and Radanir will see to it that he rests at last along the shores of Nenuial that he so loved.Calithil kept to himself, and in that way he was much like Aragorn your chieftain. He strode often in the Trollshaws 'neath the moon, and it was he who introduced Radanir to Elweleth at Thorenhad. He used to jest that he did it only to make things difficult for Radanir.

He will rest more easily beneath the trees, perhaps a stone's throw from the sound of Loudwater.Linnor was well-known to you, for he lived on the Bree-fields and often visited your cabin to share a meal and the news. He was generally well-regarded by Man and Bree-hobbit alike, and it was Linnor who helped Burle Pierson construct the frame for his farm-house south of Bree-town.

Linnor did not range as far afield as many of your kin, and his decision to join the Grey Company was not an easy one. You hope he knows, somehow, that his service was not for nothing. You and Radanir will make sure he is laid to rest among the comfortable hills of his home.You gaze upon Himeldir's face with deep sorrow, for you remember how much he argued against coming to the Forsaken Road. He believed the words of Ergothorn should not be trusted, and thought seeking beneath the earth for the honourless ones to be a foolish journey.

He spoke of it to you in private, and shared his concerns with Candaith, but in the end he agreed to walk the Forsaken Road. From that point onward you heard no complaint from him, and he descended bravely.

'I am sorry, Himeldir,' you whisper to his fallen form. 'Be at peace, my kinsman. We will not leave you here.'Candaith was dear to you, and you still keenly feel the pain of his loss. He often came to visit your cabin on the Bree-fields, and many were the nights the two of you spent in quiet conversation about the events of the day. The conversations became more serious and more grim as the shadows deepened in Middle-earth, but your favourites will always be those that were full of smiles and laughter.

'We came back for you, my brother. We should not have left you here for even one day. Forgive me, Candaith.'

Your words hang in the still air.'I am ready for us to carry our fallen brothers outside, Saeradan. Let us not delay. They have remained in this terrible place for too long already.'Golodir can no longer resist the lure of vengeance, and has decided to return to the prison-caves of Tûr Morva in search of Lheu Brenin, chieftain of the Falcon Clan.The entrance to the prison-caves of Tûr Morva is to the north-east.Return to the entrance of the prison-cavesCorunir is waiting before the entrance to the prison-caves. He must know your mind.Talk to Corunir outside the prison-cavesCorunir refuses to let you enter the prison-caves in search of Lheu Brenin.Talk to Corunir outside the prison-caves... againCorunir has decided to accompany you into the prison-caves in search of Lheu Brenin.Talk to Corunir inside the prison-cavesMaelona has requested your help deep within the prison-caves.Talk to Maelona in the prison-cavesLheu Brenin has left Dunland and passed into Rohan with those loyal to him, but before he left he cast dissenting members of the Falcon Clan into the Pit of the Falcon at the bottom of the prison-caves.Drop into the pit at the bottom of the prison-cavesYou have dropped into the pit at the bottom of the prison-caves, where members of the Falcon Clan who displeased Lheu Brenin have been abandoned.Talk to WendaSomething lives in the bottom of the pit where Lheu Brenin throws his captives.Defeat the Beast in the PitYou have survived your encounter with the Beast in the Pit, and should see how Corunir is doing.Talk to Corunir in the pitWenda is frightened, but it has been a long time since you were adept with comforting words.Talk to Wenda'Age has not slowed you down at all, Golodir! I am lucky you were here to take the brunt of the creature's attacks!

'Do not laugh at me for this, friend, but I feel some remorse for slaying such a mighty creature. I know it sought to devour us, but I cannot help think it was a captive here as well. Lheu Brenin kept it in this pit, and he made use of its appetite. Mayhap we did it a courtesy by freeing it of its confines?

'If you will comfort Wenda and ease her fright, I will call up to Maelona. She will find us a rope and we can climb out of this pit.''Someone is just ahead, Golodir! A woman, from the sound, and she may be in trouble. She is the first living creature we have seen in all these caves today.

'We should be cautious.''I thought you might come here sooner or later, Golodir. I know what you want to do, and I am here to keep you from doing something rash!'

Corunir shrugs his shoulders, and looks up at you with a stern expression on his face.

'Lheu Brenin has so many at his command. It would be foolish for you to return to the caves in search of him. We should do as Halbarad wishes, and leave the leader of the Falcon Clan be. He wronged us, but we cannot do anything about that now.''I know you are angry, my friend, but returning to the prison-caves by yourself is simply throwing away your life!

'I am angry too, Golodir! Lheu Brenin cost us much, and I have not forgotten his treachery. Many good men lost their lives when he sided with the Wizard, friends of us both, and if the leader of the Falcon Clan had his way none of us would have survived.'

Corunir sighs.'You have to believe me, Ranger. I spoke against Lheu Brenin's decision, and many good folk did the same. Your people were betrayed by Lheu Brenin, but so were many of my own! He believed the Wizard's lies, and thought to raise the Falcon to heights undreamed-of... but in so doing he gave the Falcon a death-blow. The symbol of my clan is now little better than that of the Orcs.'

Maelona spits.

'But there is no time! Lheu Brenin has taken those loyal to him and crossed over the mountains. If you are seeking to have vengeance upon him, you are too late. He will be in the land of the horse-lords by now.'You try to ease Wenda's fears with comforting words, but it has been a long time since such words came easy to you, and your face reddens with each stumbling syllable.

'You are kind... Ranger.... Thank you...'

Wenda tests her leg, and winces gingerly.

'My arms... are fine. But I will need... some help with the rope...'

While Corunir and Maelona arrange for getting everyone out of the pit, you listen as Wenda haltingly tells you how she learned the craft of tree-climbing when she was young.

'My father will be shamed in the world of spirits if I fall from the rope... and I will blame you, Ranger!'

You swear that she will not fall, and that her father will be proud of her ascent... but if she does fall, she should make sure to fall on Corunir, who is relatively plump, for a Ranger.

She laughs at that, and you laugh with her.'Are you... here to help us? I hurt... my leg in the fall...'

The girl winces with pain, and you are stricken suddenly by the memory of Lorniel lying before the gates of Carn Dûm.

'My name... Wenda... we have to...'

Wenda falls silent, exhausted by the effort, but her darting eyes flicker to the tunnel leaving the chamber and you can see the fear in them.'I think she is telling the truth, Golodir. If Lheu Brenin cast some of his own people into this pit, and left them to die, I think they must surely help us. We cannot leave them there!''You have to believe me, Ranger! Many of us spoke against Lheu Brenin, and he repaid us for our dissent with death and violence! He cast those who still remained into this pit before he left.

'You must save them!'Lothrandir of the Dúnedain still stands strong in the dungeons beneath Isengard, resisting the torments of his captors.You have been told to report to Barkh in the kitchens for the day's orders.Talk to Barkh and receive your ordersBarkh wants you to serve some slop to the hungry uruks, but you have a better idea.Tip over the slop bucketBarkh is not pleased with your defiance.Barkh is not pleased with youBarkh has asked you one more time to comply with his orders.Tip over the other slop bucketYou have showed your defiance yet again, and the Uruks are not pleased!Survive the consequences of your defianceGun Ain has come to the kitchens underneath Isengard to bring you before Saruman.Talk to Gun Ain in the kitchens below IsengardYou have come with Gun Ain to the tower of Orthanc, there to speak with Saruman.You have been brought before the Wizard Saruman.'The Wizard wants to see you, Ranger. This could be your last day of life, and my last day without a name!

'What a fortunate day this is, if that be so! Your freedom from this life would be my release from shame. Both of us would rejoice, would we not?''We are here. Will the Wizard share his mind with his poor servant and his unworthy captive?''You're mine again today, little Ranger. And this time you will do as I say, won't you? You remember what happened the last time you refused to do your chores, don't you? Spent some time in the dungeons learning the meaning of pain, you did.

'So this time will be different! Scoop some slop from that bucket and serve it to the boys!'You have travelled with Saeradan into Enedwaith and have stopped for the night at Lhanuch on your way north with the waggon containing your fallen comrades.Saeradan is standing outside the gates of Lhanuch and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Saeradan outside the gates of LhanuchGlynn Brenin can be found near the top of the village, in front of his dwelling.Talk to Glynn Brenin at the top of the villageAnirin can be found down the hill in Lhanuch.

Anirin, the messenger of the Falcon Clan who has been recovering in Lhanuch from a leg injury, should be told what has happened in Tûr Morva.Talk to Anirin down the hill in LhanuchSuvulch of the Uch-lûth is by the gate, confronting a large and hostile force of Dunlendings.Talk to Suvulch at the west-gate of LhanuchThe Dragon Clan has offered Anirin a chance to share in the treasure taken from Lhanuch. Talk to him at the West gate of the village.Talk to AnirinGlynn Brenin is near the top of Lhanuch, fighting off warriors of the Dragon Clan.Talk to Glynn Brenin atop the villageThe Dragon Clan has released worms throughout Lhanuch, and Glynn Brenin wants you to defeat them!Saeradan is outside the gates of Lhanuch.

Now that the Dragon Clan has been driven away from Lhanuch, you should speak with Saeradan.Talk to Saeradan outside the gates of Lhanuch'Another day of our journey nears its end, Radanir. Let us see if the Uch-lûth, the Ox Clan, will consent to stabling us for the night.

'We have tidings from the south to give to Glynn Brenin, as well. Do you remember how highly he spoke of Lheu Brenin and the Falcon Clan? The news that his southern neighbours have thrown in with Saruman will not please him.'Glynn Brenin's open face grows cloudy and troubled as you speak of the Grey Company's betrayal by Lheu Brenin and the Falcon Clan.

'Alas for your fallen, and for the hopes of my people, Radanir. If Lheu Brenin has succumbed to the whispered promises of Saruman, how can we hope to persevere 'gainst him?'

Glynn Brenin snorts. 'Lheu was not as strong as I believed, Radanir. That must now be the truth. His weakness, buried deeply, has emerged and betrayed us all.''The Rangers have returned! What can you tell me of my home? Did you not travel to Tûr Morva in Tâl Methedras?'

Anirin's face falls as you describe the Falcon Clan's betrayal of the Grey Company, and despite your natural skepticism, Anirin's reaction seems genuine.

'What of my family? Do you have any news of them? My beloved is named Wenda, and she is no warrior. Is she in danger? And my mother Seren is aged and would not know to still her tongue, if she were put in a precarious position! What can you tell me of my family, Ranger? Speak!''Not now, Ranger. There are rude ones outside this gate that need a lesson in manners!''This man says the Falcon Clan and the Dragon Clan are now allies. If this is true, then my decision is made easy.''The Dragon Clan is not alone, Radanir! They brought the worms they worship down from the mountains with them, and they are loose in the village! Help my people drive them away, Ranger!'The worm hisses at its trapped victims, and lumbers in for its meal. You have to get its attention!

'Over here, worm! Over here! Let me get a look at that ugly visage of yours, and see if I can work any improvements!'

As the worm closes for battle, you wonder if you could have fashioned a better battle-cry.This worm has not noticed your approach, and though it pains you to attack unaccompanied by a shout borne of your sharp wit, you do so anyway. There will be time enough, and worms enough in Lhanuch, for you to think of something clever in the meantime.The worm snaps its fangs together as it menaces its prey.

'Oh, what is that horrible stench? Is that you, worm? It is! It is a wonder you creatures are even able to breed!''I think that is all of them, Radanir. Did I hear you shouting something about the worms smelling foul? It sounded like you, friend.'

He grimaces.

'When the fighting started, the horses bolted and the waggon rolled down the hill. You should help me find the horses and tether one of them to the waggon, and then... I think you should take the other horse and return to Halbarad.'

He raises a hand to still your protest.

'I can drive the waggon north by myself. You are of more use in a fight than I am, and if the Falcon Clan and the Dragon Clan have joined together, as it seems they have, Halbarad will have need of strong fighters. You may continue north with me if you desire, but I think you should return to Halbarad and tell him what happened here.

'This should be good-bye, my friend. For now.'Cerdic is concerned with the increased patrols of Orcs through the part of Fangorn known as the Balewood.Cerdic has asked that you speak to his men to learn more of the Balewood before venturing into the forest.Talk to FrethiTalk to AthelmanThe bunker can be found in Cerdic's camp overlooking the roadway to the east.

