 been destroyed. At night, the town is dark, and it is difficult to believe that an Orc is not hiding in the shadows. Rebuilding the roadways will go far to help the inhabitants feel safe and secure.''Hytbold is one step closer to being rebuilt and restored.'Slaughter Lore-masters in the War.Slaughter Lore-masters in the WarSlaughter Lore-masters in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Lore-masters in the WarSlaughter Lore-masters in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Lore-masters in the WarSlaughter Lore-masters in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Lore-masters in the WarSlaughter Lore-masters in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Lore-masters in the WarMost of the Reeve's family are laid to rest in the graveyard outside of Forlaw. Most of his family has been killed recently as the conflict in Wildermore escalates.There are many of Reeve Gárwig's family buried in the graveyard outside of Forlaw. Many of these graves are relatively fresh.Discover Wilnoth's GraveDiscover Gárnoth's GraveDiscover Gárferth's GraveDiscover Edsig's GraveDiscover Cuthbald's GraveDiscover Gársig's GraveDiscover Thrymm's GraveDiscover Alferth's GraveDiscover Wilsig's GraveDiscover Cuthard's GraveDiscover Grimgar's GraveDiscover Humbald's GraveDiscover Merwyn's GraveDiscover Cynegar's GraveHere lies Wilnoth, wife of Gárwig. She passed at the age of fifty-three and was survived by her five sons.  

Wilnoth was a devoted and supportive wife to the Reeve and was a great noble for her people. Gárwig took pride in instructing their sons how and when to fight, and she taught them compassion and tolerance.Here lies Gárnoth, eldest son of Gárwig. He passed at the age of twenty-nine and never married or sired children. 

Gárnoth was one of the strongest warriors amongst his brothers; and in his final act he single-handedly saved his farm and the lives of the farmers working there. He drove off the mighty Brúlk, the largest Warg ever spotted in Wildermore, and it has never been seen again.Here lies Gárferth, second eldest son of Gárwig, as was his twin Edsig. He passed at the age of forty-eight and was survived by his wife, Ebba, and their son Alferth. 

Gárferth and Edsig were always inseparable. He was the stronger of the twins and saw it as his duty to protect Edsig. As he grew older, Gárwig took notice of this quality and was proud to make him a guardian of Forlaw. He died trying to protect Forlaw from Núrzum in the Battle at Forlaw's Gate.Here lies Edsig, second eldest son of Gárwig, as was his twin Gárferth. He passed at the age of forty-eight and was survived by his wife, Hereswith, and their three children: their daughter Luba, son Wilsig, and daughter Sigburga. 

Edsig and Gárferth were always inseparable. His strengths were in wisdom and cunning, instead of skill in combat like his brothers. He distinguished himself as a capable leader of men. He frequently talked Gárferth and his brothers into doing the right thing, and helped resolve conflicts. He led the defence in the Battle at Forlaw's Gate, and died trying to protect Forlaw from Núrzum.Here lies Cuthbald, second youngest son of Gárwig. He passed at the age of thirty-two and was survived by his wife, Merwyn, and their three children: their son Cuthard, son Cuthmar, and daughter Ansith.

Cuthbald always sought to mentor their youngest brother, Gársig. Cuthbald sought to bring out the best in him, but they did not see eye-to-eye and Gársig did not appreciate his attention. One day, this got out of hand, and Cuthbald was killed by Gársig in a terrible accident while training together.Here lies Gársig, youngest son of Gárwig. He passed at the age of thirty-seven and was survived by his wife, Burghild, and their son Gárwald.

Gársig always struggled under the shadow of his brothers. Cuthbald tried to mentor Gársig, but at times they struggled to get along. After Cuthbald's death, he was deeply saddened and never fully recovered. He went to Scylfig in an attempt to redeem his name, despite Gárwig's request that he stay in Forlaw, and died in the battle with Núrzum.Here lies Thrymm Red-beard, foster-son of Gárwig, and hero to the people of Wildermore. His father was Afol, Gárwig's brother, who died while Thrymm was young which prompted Gárwig to adopt him as his son. Thrymm passed at the age of thirty-four and never married or sired children. He shared strong feelings with Cyneberg of Scylfig, and many believe they were to some day be married, but had not the chance to officially announce their intentions.

Thrymm Red-beard was the fiercest warrior in all of Wildermore. Gárwig's many sons were all good with the sword, but were no match for Thrymm. He was known for more than his prowess in battle, as he frequently travelled throughout Wildermore seeking anyone who needed help and offered his assistance. He cared not for payment, or of someone's status and was a true man of the people. He died in an attempt to defend Scylfig from the monster we now know as Núrzum.Here lies Alferth, son of Gárferth, and grandson of Gárwig. He was the only child of Gárferth and Ebba and passed away at the age of twenty. He died at the hands of Núrzum while riding with Wilsig and Cuthard to the Wold in an attempt to seek assistance from the Rohirrim there.Here lies Wilsig, son of Edsig, and grandson of Gárwig. He was the only son of Edsig and Hereswith, who had two other daughters, and he passed away at the young age of eleven. He died at the hands of Núrzum while riding with Alferth and Cuthard to the Wold in an attempt to seek assistance from the Rohirrim there.Here lies Cuthard, son of Cuthbald, and grandson of Gárwig. He was the eldest son of Cuthbald and Merwyn, and when they passed away when he was five, he was taken in by Edsig and Hereswith. He passed away at the age of sixteen. He died at the hands of Núrzum while riding with Alferth and Wilsig to the Wold in an attempt to seek assistance from the Rohirrim there.Here lies Grimgar, son of Gársig, and grandson of Gárwig. He was the eldest son of Gársig and Burghild and passed away at the age of eighteen. He died with his father and younger brother at the battle of Scylfig.Here lies Humbald, son of Gársig, and grandson of Gárwig. He was the second of three sons of Gársig and Burghild and passed away at the age of sixteen. He died with his father and older brother at the battle of Scylfig.Here lies Merwyn, wife of Cuthbald Gárwig's son. She died the same year as Cuthbald when giving birth to their third child: their daughter Ansith.Here lies Cynegar, father of Cyneberg, and Thane of Scylfig. Cynegar was a cousin of Gárwig, and was also a close friend. They frequently sought out the other for guidance on many matters. As a result, this relationship maintained a steady flow of communication and trade between Forlaw and Scylfig, more so than any other towns of Wildermore.Service to the master of Lugazag will result in the ability to find your way to the tower with less effort.Complete quests for the benefit of Lugazag!War-Tyrant Akúlhun waits your return to Gramsfoot where he will see you rewarded for your service to Lugazag!Speak with War-Tyrant Akúlhun'Another servant to Angmar! You are recognized this day with a reward long due. Use it well, maggot!'Service to the master of Lugazag will result in the ability to find your way to the tower with less effort.Complete quests for the benefit of Lugazag!War-Tyrant Akúlhun waits your return to Gramsfoot where he will see you rewarded for your service to Lugazag!Speak with War-Tyrant Akúlhun'Another servant to Angmar! You are recognized this day with a reward long due. Use it well, maggot!'Your service to the master of Lugazag is rewarded!Complete quests for the benefit of Lugazag!War-Tyrant Akúlhun waits your return to Gramsfoot where he will see you rewarded for your service to Lugazag!Speak with War-Tyrant Akúlhun'Another servant to Angmar! You are recognized this day with a reward long due. Use it well, maggot!'Mossbeard has communed with the trees and fallen of the Balewood and now wishes to tell all that he has learned of Núrzum's coming.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>Mossbeard can be found at Stonecliff in the Balewood.

Mossbeard is prepared to tell you and the Ents of the rest of what he has learned from the trees of the forest. You should listen to his tale when you are ready.Talk to MossbeardMossbeard can be found at Stonecliff in the Balewood.

Mossbeard's tale of Saruman and Núrzum has concluded. You should now speak with him of what can be done to stop the forces at work in the Balewood.Talk to Mossbeard'And so, little one, Brúndoron was taken by the Orcs. 

'Núrzum had wandered these woods some time before this happening, you see. Hoom. He was then but a Stone-giant and had not much interest in the destruction of any creature of nature. His curiosity of the Men was his only real reason for venturing into Wildermore, but when he was grievously wounded and chased from their lands, the other Ents and I thought him gone from our forest.

'Such things never come to a resolution simply with you creatures, do they? Hom. Yet, one day Núrzum returned, and this time, he was not alone.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>'I have learned much of Núrzum from these trees, my friend. When you are ready, I shall tell what I know.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This instance quest is meant to be run Solo and cannot be run in a group.</rgb>'So you see now, ${PLAYER}...Núrzum returned to the forest, his wounds healed, his might bolstered, and lashed together by what remained of Brúndoron. All he was tasked with in return for such wonders were the deaths of the Men that had wronged him. How could he see the folly of his actions in the face of such a silver-tongued man?

'I know of this Wizard, little one, for once Treebeard spoke of him with Leaflock, in days long past. Always one to take knowledge, but never share it in return. Hoom. You look as if you have met him too....

'Such times are so long past that I doubt he even remembers us amid whatever other chaos he now wreaks. Surely if the Orcs did not tell him of Brúndoron's assault, he would not have thought much of using a tree in his foul experiments. His blindness to the forest may yet prove his monster's undoing, but we shall see yet.'Defeat players of the race of Men.Defeat players of the race of MenDefeat players of the race of Men.Defeat players of the race of MenDefeat players of the race of Men.Defeat players of the race of MenDefeat players of the race of Men.Defeat players of the race of MenDefeat players of the race of Men.Defeat players of the race of MenSlaughter Mariners in the War.Slaughter Mariners in the WarSlaughter Mariners in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Mariners in the WarSlaughter Mariners in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Mariners in the WarSlaughter Mariners in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Mariners in the WarSlaughter Mariners in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Mariners in the WarSaruman has bred Wargs that are larger and more fierce than most that grow in the wild, even those found on the plains of Rohan. These Wargs are bred specifically to counter the Rohirrim's superior horses, and riding capabilities, and he strives to have riders who can match their skill. Saruman sent one of his prized breeding Warg-matrons to help establish an outpost in the Fallows.The Warg-matron's den is in the south-west corner of Edsig's Farm. She can be found lurking near the small barn.Defeat Gajat, the Warg-matronThe Warg-matron, Gajat, lurks outside a small barn that is now her den. This den is in the south-west corner of Edsig's Farm.Defeat Gajat, the Warg-matronOter is awaiting your return at Thrymm's Farm and will be happy to know of what you accomplished while at Edsig's Farm.Return to OterDefeat the ferocious Warg-matron that dwells nearby. She is of a vicious breed of Warg, and her many pups serve as mounts for Saruman's Orc army. Her death is worth that of countless Warg-riders.'You did good deeds this day, ${PLAYER}. Those of us who have lost almost everything to those Warg-riders thank you for your efforts. The sun may shine a little brighter now, for some of us.'You are adding inhabitants to the Mead Hall.Complete The Mead Hall: Inhabitants IComplete The Mead Hall: Inhabitants IIComplete The Mead Hall: Inhabitants IIIComplete The Mead Hall: Inhabitants IVComplete The Mead Hall: Inhabitants VComplete The Mead Hall: Inhabitants VIYou are upgrading the Mead Hall interior.Complete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements IComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements IIComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements IIIComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements IVComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements VComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements VIComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements VIIComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements VIIIComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements IXComplete The Mead Hall: Interior Enhancements XYou are upgrading the exterior of the Mead Hall.Complete The Mead Hall: Outdoor Enhancements IComplete The Mead Hall: Outdoor Enhancements IIComplete The Mead Hall: Outdoor Enhancements IIIComplete The Mead Hall: Outdoor Enhancements IVComplete The Mead Hall: Outdoor Enhancements VComplete The Mead Hall: Outdoor Enhancements VIA skilled metalsmith such as yourself can help the refugees of Wildermore by forging a multi-purpose toolkit for them.Hildelith has given you a recipe for a multi-purpose toolkit.

You should forge a multi-purpose toolkit for the refugees who hope to start rebuilding Wildermore.Forge a multi-purpose toolkit for the people of Wildermore (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hildelith is in Forlaw.

You have crafted a multi-purpose toolkit, and should now deliver it to Hildelith.'I hear that you are gifted at working with metals, ${PLAYER}. Would you be willing to forge something for the refugees of Wildermore?

'A set of tools would surely help the builders to repair the damage that has been done. Is this something you can make?''Ah! This is wonderful, ${PLAYER}. I have never seen such advanced, finely-crafted tools. These will work better than the rusty and broken things the people have been trying to use up 'til now.'Slaughter Minstrels in the War.Slaughter Minstrels in the WarSlaughter Minstrels in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Minstrels in the WarSlaughter Minstrels in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Minstrels in the WarSlaughter Minstrels in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Minstrels in the WarSlaughter Minstrels in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Slaughter Minstrels in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Defeat monster-players in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Defeat monster-players in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Defeat monster players in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Defeat monster-players in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Defeat monster players in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Defeat monster-players in the WarStem the tides of Darkness in the War.Complete the previous deed tier.Defeat monster-players in the WarCyneberg and the Reeve must hear the tale Iothete just told you, for you finally have a clue as to the power of Núrzum.Cyneberg can be found at the refugees' hideout south-west of Dunfast.

You should hasten to the refugees' hideout to tell Cyneberg of Iothete's strange encounter, and the stone he hid in his house.Tell Cyneberg Iothete's taleCyneberg is at the refugees' hideout.

You should tell Cyneberg that she must return to Forlaw with you.Tell Cyneberg that she must bring the refugees to ForlawGárwig is in Lornsettle, the Mead Hall of Forlaw.

You should return to Gárwig and tell him everything Iothete told you.Hasten to Forlaw to tell Gárwig all that you know'I hope my tale and that stone help you in your search, ${PLAYER}. Since I met the old wanderer, I have felt a curse of impending doom, while everyone called me a superstitious old fool.

'Now that you are here, I feel hopeful again. I believe you can lift the doom cast upon Wildermore!''That is a strange story indeed, ${PLAYER}! Gárwig will be glad to hear it. You can escort these poor folk to Forlaw, and I will head north to continue my search for Thrymm!'You gently tell Cyneberg that your errand to speak with Gárwig cannot wait, and the refugees will slow you down.

'Fine, ${PLAYER}! I will escort them so that you may make haste. But then I shall continue my search for Thrymm. Wildermore needs him!'

You bite your tongue, and hope that Cyneberg soon realizes that Thrymm is gone, so that she may confront her grief at last.'I am so glad to see you in one piece, ${PLAYER}. Did you piece together the mystery of the relic?'

You tell Gárwig everything Iothete told you, about the stone and the old man you know to be Saruman.

'The giant carries a magic stone with him, and Saruman had something to do with it. So that is it; the ice emanates from Núrzum himself, and goes where he goes....'Many dead men from Byre Tor lay strewn about the town, unburied, defiled by the Orcs even in death.Dead men lay strewn about the town of Byre Tor.

You should put the dead men to rest, for they should not be left out as trophies of the Orcs.Tend to the corpses of Byre Tor's men (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have put many of the former inhabitants of Byre Tor to rest.CompletedMany dead men from Byre Tor lie strewn about the town, unburied, defiled by the Orcs even in death.

These men are not trophies of Orcs, but brave villagers who fought to save their town. They should be given a proper respite in death.You have put many of the former inhabitants of Byre Tor to rest.

These brave men may now lie in peace, safe from the Orcs.Húna and the other refugees are trapped within a cave in the Balewood, surrounded on all sides by terrible things, and wish to be free of the forest.You should begin your search for a path out of the forest by scouting to the north, west, and east of the hidden cave in the Balewood.

Húna has asked you to scout around the cave in hopes of finding a clear route for he and the other refugees of Byre Tor to escape the Balewood.Search west of the cave for a clear routeSearch north of the cave for a clear routeSearch east of the cave for a clear routeHúna can be found in the hidden cave in the southern reaches of the Balewood.

You have discovered danger at every turn outside the cave and should now inform Húna that escape is unlikely, at least for the moment.Talk to Húna'${PLAYER}, I know not what lurks outside this cave. If it is anything like what we have seen already, I fear the worst for all of us. We lack arms, and the winter's chill bites at us even here. Nothing in this forest welcomes us... I would not be surprised if this cave collapsed upon us!

'Although the road south is overrun, perhaps there is a way for us to escape... even if only to another cave in the High Knolls or to the camp south of which you spoke. We cannot follow the road, lest their riders run us down, but perhaps we can find ways to the north, west, or east. Travel outside the cave and tell me if there is any clear path out of this forest.

'Be safe, ${PLAYER}... we would surely be lost without you.''Alas, it is as I feared....'

Húna's face sinks, and he turns to look at the others before shifting back to you.

'So, then, we are trapped in this cave... and the Enemy lurks just outside. Curse that monster! I cannot believe what he...'

Húna's eyes begin to fill with tears, but he composes himself quickly.

'Your efforts are appreciated, even if we must remain here for now. If you can aid us further, I and the others would be so grateful.'You are adding tents for the Men of the Norcrofts.Complete Norcrofts Tents: RebuildComplete Norcrofts Tents: Enhancements IComplete Norcrofts Tents: Enhancements IIComplete Norcrofts Tents: InhabitantsThere is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.'While a tent is not a house, there are still some things that can be done to add some comfort and turn it into a home. Beds, tables, camp-fires, and stools are among the things that we can add to help return a semblance of normalcy to people's lives.''Hytbold is one step closer to being rebuilt and restored.'There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.'While a tent is not a house, there are still some things that can be done to add some comfort and turn it into a home. Beds, tables, camp-fires, and stools are among the things that we can add to help return a semblance of normalcy to people's lives.''Hytbold is one step closer to being rebuilt and restored.'There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.'The tents have been fully prepared and made ready for the people of the Norcrofts to come and join your town as inhabitants and not just visitors. They will add a breath of fresh air and new life to Hytbold. Those coming are a varied sort: women, children, warriors, and craftsmen. All have been displaced by the Enemy, and all will now call this place home.''Hytbold is one step closer to being rebuilt and restored.'There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.There is much work to do to rebuild the town of Hytbold.'There are many across the Norcrofts who have been left homeless by the constant raids and assaults. These people have valuable skills and would be a be good addition to our developing town. Not to mention, they need a new place to call home, and we do not leave our countrymen behind. We should begin to set up and prepare some tents to provide these people a place to live.''Hytbold is one step closer to being rebuilt and restored.'After Núrzum attacked Forlaw from the west, he headed in the direction of Gárnoth's Farm. It was not fully understood why Núrzum spared the city, or what his business was with an abandoned farm.A trail of very large footprints has been made through Gárnoth's Farm. They seem to originate from the north, near the road coming from Forlaw....Find the beginning of the footprint trailThe path of footprints made by Núrzum start near the road from Forlaw toward Gárnoth's Farm.

