Gárwig has requested a log be put on the fire. It seems to be harder and harder to keep the mead hall warm these days.Collect a log of woodGárwig asked for the log be put on the fire-pit to his left.

Gárwig has requested a log be put on the fire. It seems to be harder and harder to keep the Mead-hall warm these days.Gárwig awaits his fate on the throne in the Mead-hall.Talk to GárwigThe messenger which Gárwig asked you to send to Dunfast is waiting near the stables in Forlaw.Send the messenger to DunfastCyneberg is keeping Gárwig company in Lornsettle, the Mead-hall of Forlaw.Talk to Cyneberg in Lornsettle'You come to me with more ill news? I suppose that is all there is to share these days. No matter... I feel bad for those men, but there is nothing I can do for them. Every last warrior is focused on keeping this city secure.

'Please put a log on the fire... a chill has crept into the room since we began talking of unfortunate situations and the poor people of Forlaw. The logs are over against the wall to your right.''I dismissed your burden, which was not right of me to do, but my mind is preoccupied by other issues. I have lost contact with most of Wildermore, and it is clear that fate has cursed me and my family.

'I know she also has a heavy heart, but Cyneberg may have room for more sympathy than I. Speak with her about what happened to those poor farmers.

'Might I ask a favour before you speak with Cyneberg? I have waited to see a report from Dunfast in Whitshaws for some time, but none has arrived. Please speak with the messenger by the stables for me and send him to Dunfast.''Ah, Gárwig is concerned about the lack of word from Dunfast? I will leave right away.''I could no longer keep myself locked in Thrymm's home, not if I am to move on from what happened. I can only imagine how Gárwig is doing. What has happened to him would break any man. Losing Thrymm is a terrible pain that I am still learning to deal with, but I could not imagine going through what he has.

