You should collect yarrow and snow for a poultice to try to save the Thane.Pick yarrow for a poultice (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect snow for a poultice (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Céolflad can be found in the refugees' cave north of Byre Tor in the High Knolls.

You should return to Céolflad with the yarrow and snow.Bring the poultice ingredients to Céolflad'This wound is no good!

'Please, stranger, find some yarrow, if ye can! I hope the snow hasn't buried it all. I'll need plenty of the leaves, and some snow to pack it on with.''Ah... very brown and brittle. Not much life left in these poor plants, but it's something. I will try.

'Now hold ye still, Thane Aldstan! We'll fix yeh right up.'Thane Edric and his men struggle to repel the Uruk invaders of Kingstead while still maintaining control of the crossing into the Eastemnet.The Uruk, Asgája, can be found somewhere nearby the stockpile south of the road to Middlemead from Entwade in Kingstead.Find and defeat AsgájaThane Edric can be found inside the Mead Hall of Entwade in eastern Kingstead.

You have slain the hardy Uruk, Asgája, and should now report your success to Thane Edric.Talk to Thane Edric'Along the road, I have seen many foes, ${PLAYER}. None were greater than the Uruk who guarded their stockpile of pillaged goods.

'He rode atop a thickly-armoured steed and bore weapons of the finest make. He has no doubt stolen these tools from our traders, caught ferrying blades of the Stonedeans back to Kingstead. I know not if this Uruk is their leader or merely one of their mightiest, but he must be slain if Entwade is to be prepared for the coming attack.

'Ride out to their stockpile along the road's edge and slay him when you can draw his attention. Thane Edric will wish to know this foe has been put to an end.''This Uruk has harried my people for weeks now, and I am glad to hear he finally met his end.

'The supplies he and his ilk had been keeping from my men can now be recovered and we can redouble our efforts to prepare the Wade.'Vicious Uruk-hai of the White Hand roam the plains of Kingstead, visiting destruction on any in their path.Uruk-hai raiders can be found roaming the fields around Bosa's Farm.Defeat Uruk-hai raidersUruk-hai raiders can be found roaming the fields around Bosa's Farm.

You have come across the burned remains of Bosa's Farm and the White Hand forces responsible. You should thin their numbers to protect the people of Kingstead.Defeat Uruk-hai raiders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many of the Uruk-hai forces at Bosa's Farm.CompletedYou have arrived at Bosa's Farm hoping to meet with Thane Mágla's lieutenant, but instead you have discovered the croftlands and homes burnt to the ground by nearby White Hand forces. Brutal Uruk-hai raiders still circle the fields, setting flame to anything that escaped their wrath thus far. 

They must be defeated if the people of Middlemead are to remain safe from the White Hand.You have defeated many of the Uruk-hai forces at Bosa's Farm and avenged the fallen crofters. With some of their strongest slain, the White Hand forces near Middlemead have been dealt a mighty blow.Éowyn has asked for your aid in rallying the people of Middlemead to Edoras as Thane Mágla has not sent word.

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: This quest will fail if you complete 'Trading Fates' before speaking to Thane Mágla.</rgb>Thane Mágla can be found in the Mead Hall of Middlemead in north-western Kingstead.

Éowyn, acting as steward of Edoras, has asked you to aid in gathering the people of Middlemead in preparation for the journey to Dunharrow.Talk to Thane Mágla in Middlemead'I have been tasked by Théoden King, my uncle, with gathering our people to Dunharrow for shelter from this brutal war.

'I have managed to keep as much order as possible in Edoras, but I cannot leave my people to attend to this matter. You have proven yourself a hardy ${CLASS}, and I would entrust you with this task should you seek to aid me.

'Far to the north of Edoras, the trading village of Middlemead lies nestled amongst the trees. Gather the people and have them seek shelter here while we make ready to travel south.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: This quest will fail if you complete 'Trading Fates' before speaking to Thane Mágla.</rgb>'Traveller, you bear tidings of Edoras? Very well then... let us speak.'Léoflad and her people have been massacred along the road to Aldburg, cutting short all hope of safety for the inhabitants of Entwade.The Entwade-slaying can be found in south-eastern Kingstead along the road through the forests of the Eastfold.

Agelman has asked that you help him bury the victims of the White Hand attack to provide them some measure of peace.Léoflad can be found amidst the wreckage along the road to the Eastfold in south-eastern Kingstead.

You have aided Agelman in burying the people of Entwade, and have discovered Léoflad's body amidst the wreckage. You should bury her as well before speaking to Agelman.Bury LéofladAgelman is at your side or can be rallied to your side by sounding your horn.

Agelman has interrupted you, calling you aside before you could finish burying Léoflad. You should speak to him and discover what he wants.Talk to Agelman'What horror... all of them, the old and young cut down.... They were innocent, ${PLAYER}, innocent and still the Uruks slew them all.'

Agelman clasps his hand to his mouth, his eyes filling with tears.

'I... I cannot bear to see my fellows like this. Entwade may yet stand, but, in truth, it has already fallen... they deserved so much better than this fate.

'I shall not allow the Wargs of the White Hand to strip what remains of their dignity. Come then, let us bury them, and perhaps then they shall find the peace they so hoped for.''Stay your hand, ${PLAYER}!

'Alas for brave Léoflad, she must have been overwhelmed by their numbers. For both her honour and for Edric, she must be returned to Entwade.

'I will handle this task myself, friend. Edric needs to see her, and he needs time to grieve for the lost, even if he thinks time short.'Théoden is now ready to ride west to Isengard... to war.Théoden King is inside the Golden Hall of Meduseld, in Edoras.

Éowyn has asked you to see if the King has any further commands before he rides west to Isengard.Talk to Théoden in MeduseldThéoden King is inside the Golden Hall of Meduseld, in Edoras.

The King is waiting to speak with you before he rides west, to Isengard and war.Talk to Théoden in Meduseld and watch his company ride to warÉowyn is outside the Golden Hall of Meduseld, in Edoras.

The King and his company have left Edoras, and Éowyn is in charge of making ready the evacuation of the city.Talk to Éowyn outside Meduseld, in Edoras'My preparations continue, but my uncle's are complete. Now he rides to Isengard and to war. I will remain behind, as he has commanded, but my thoughts will dwell upon him until his return!

'Enter the Golden Hall and see if he has further commands for those of us who will remain.''My uncle is ready to ride to war. Enter the Golden Hall and see if he has any further commands for those of us who must remain behind.''The riders are ready to depart, ${PLAYER}, as am I. We will ride to Isengard and remind Saruman of what he has forgotten: that the Riddermark is not his for taking. It belongs to the Eorlingas, who will refresh his memory with sword and spear!

'I know that Éowyn is unhappy with her charge, and she would be on the forefront of the battle if I allowed her to ride with us. It would console her little to know that it is not her safety I seek to ensure with my command, but that of my people. They need someone to lead them in my absence, someone to give them hope for better days to come. They need Éowyn to be strong, and I know she will be. Another spear at Isengard will avail us little. A shield at home may be the difference, at the last.

'I am ready to ride. Let me know when you are ready to see us off.''It is time.''So they are gone. May they ride to victory! It is now up to those of us who remain to carry out my uncle's will and the wishes of those who ride with him.

'I hope we are both up to the challenge, ${PLAYER}.'Danger threatens the kingdom of Rohan on all sides, and if nothing is done the land will be overrun by the forces of Saruman. Has the time come to defy the word of the King in order to save his people?Your companions are in Nona's Cave in the High Knolls of Wildermore.

Horn wishes to speak with you about the road ahead.Talk to Horn in Nona's Cave in the High Knolls of WildermoreCorudan is in Nona's Cave in the High Knolls, in Wildermore.

Horn has asked you to speak with Corudan and Gléowine about the course he has suggested.Talk to Corudan in Nona's CaveTalk to Gléowine in Nona's CaveHorn is in Nona's Cave in the High Knolls, in Wildermore.

You have spoken with Gléowine and Corudan, and now Horn is waiting to see if you will accompany him to Edoras.Talk to Horn in Nona's CaveHunbald is at Entwade, the Entwash Crossing in the Sutcrofts.

Horn has resolved to return to Edoras, against the commands of the King. You, Horn, and Gléowine should present yourselves to Hunbald with this decision.Talk to Hunbald at the Entwade, in the Sutcrofts'Our friends from Byre Tor have weathered the storm, ${PLAYER}. The worst is behind them, and with my help they will be well enough to travel. I have spoken with Horn about this, and he agrees that it is best if I bring them to a town in Wildermore where they can start anew. What is the name of the settlement on the eastern shore of the lake? Forlaw? That city will find a place for them, if any will. Sad news, this, but it be true: Núrzum's path of destruction has caused many houses throughout Wildermore to stand empty. My recovering friends will find a fresh start in Forlaw.

'Horn and I spoke of other matters as well, ${PLAYER}. Speak to him and see if you share his mind.''I fear my wisdom cannot illumine your course, ${PLAYER}, beyond these words. Trust in the strength of your purpose and that of your friends. They wait for you in a cave in the High Knolls of Wildermore, do they not? The treachery of Saruman struck that land deeply, and your friends have done what they could for its people, but now the greatest danger lies in the western fields of Rohan.

'Gather your friends from Nona's cave and do what you can for Rohan. That kingdom must not fall.''I am sorry, but I have received no word from Edoras that your exile has been lifted. You must remain on the eastern bank of the Entwash until I have received such a word.''I have seen fires, ${PLAYER}, fires burning in the fields of Rohan. Your friends are needed there, as are you. Return now to the cave in Wildermore where last you saw them. There are decisions you must make, and make well. Much depends upon the course you take.''Our friends from Byre Tor have weathered the storm, ${PLAYER}. The worst is behind them, and with my help they will be well enough to travel. I have spoken with Horn about this, and he agrees that it is best if I bring them to a town in Wildermore where they can start anew. What is the name of the settlement on the eastern shore of the lake? Forlaw? That city will find a place for them, if any will. Sad news, this, but it be true: Núrzum's path of destruction has caused many houses throughout Wildermore to stand empty. My recovering friends will find a fresh start in Forlaw.

'Horn and I spoke of other matters as well, ${PLAYER}. Speak to him and see if you share his mind.''The treachery of Saruman struck Wildermore very deeply. You should recover your friends from the cave in the High Knolls and see what else you can do to counter the evil of Saruman.''I have seen fires burning in the fields of Rohan. Your friends are needed there, as are you. Return now to the cave in the High Knolls of Wildermore where last you saw them, and discover your next course.''I have received no word that you may yet pass. Find your friends and find another course to take.''Thank you for coming to listen, my friend. I have put some thought into what we encountered in Wildermore, and I must confess it troubles me. The folk here are on the road to recovery, as are the survivors Nona rescued from Byre Tor, but the forces of Saruman attacked this land with much greater strength than I expected they could muster. How much might could Saruman raise to strike at the heart of Rohan if he spared a creature such as Núrzum to deal with Wildermore? Imagine what evils he might unleash upon Edoras!

'I have decided what I must do, ${PLAYER}. My King commanded me not to cross the Entwash, and I am honour-bound to obey him. And yet I cannot remain silent. I must convince Théoden King to march against Saruman before the Wizard's plans bear fruit. If it costs me my honour as a man of Rohan, so be it. I will defy the word of my King if doing so will save even one innocent. I will accept whatever justice the King deems appropriate, but he must be told of what we witnessed in Wildermore. He must be warned, even if it means my life.

'I have told Nona my decision, and she sees the need of it. She will go to Forlaw with the survivors of Byre Tor, and we will meet again once this is through. Speak now to Corudan and Gléowine and see what they think of my plan.''I will defy the word of my King, though it come at the cost of my honour. He must be told the great evil Saruman wrought in Wildermore, and what evils the Wizard intends for the rest of Rohan.''Horn plans to defy the word of his King? That cannot have been an easy decision for him to make, but I understand what has driven our friend to make it. Horn may be imprisoned, or worse, but if his tidings can break the King's reverie it might be the only action that can save the people of Rohan from their enemies.

'The survivors of Byre Tor are now well enough to travel. Nona and I will bring them to the city of Forlaw while Horn brings his message to the King, and we will rejoin him when we can. I think it would be... unwise... to enter the court of Théoden in the company of a Dunlending girl, even one proven so true as Nona. And it is not she alone who would raise the hackles of the Rohirrim. An Elf of Lórien would be as unwelcome. Am I mistaken?'Gléowine listens to your account of Horn's decision to defy the word of the King, and smiles.

'Horn can count me with him, ${PLAYER}. I will return to Edoras in defiance of Théoden King, whom I serve without question in all things save this, for I believe it is right. I am not afraid of death. If the King orders it, I will be the first to praise his justice, and will surrender to his headsman without complaint.

'The King must hear of what has happened in Wildermore. I am certain he will listen when he hears what Horn has to say.''I believe this is the right course. Horn would not forgive himself if he did not do all in his power to help his people. I would not forgive him either. The man I love fights for the people who cannot fight for themselves. I, too, do this. I will see both you and my beloved again after we have seen to our separate charges. Do not doubt it!''Nona and I will bring the survivors of Byre Tor to Forlaw, where they will be able to make a new life for themselves.''The King must be told what has transpired in Wildermore. It shows how dangerous Saruman has become! Tell Horn I will journey with him back to Edoras, though it be against the King's command!''I know now what the rest of us will do, but one path remains a mystery to me. On what path will you walk, ${PLAYER}? Will you come with me to Edoras? I do not ask you to defy Théoden King, my friend. I will understand if our paths must now separate. I would not blame you! That is, most like, the way of wisdom. It is with my heart I make this choice for myself, but I would not presume to make it for you, by head or by heart.'

His voice is casual, but you can tell from his eyes that worry lurks just beneath the surface. With a comforting smile, you tell him that you will accompany him to Edoras, for the good of Rohan's people, though it be against the command of their King. Horn's relief is tangible and sudden.

'${PLAYER}, you are as great a fool as I am! I am overjoyed to say it! Three Men and an Elf, all returning to Edoras against the command of the King, and in the company of a Dunlending girl, would be too great an insult. We defy the King, but still seek to be polite. Is this madness, ${PLAYER}?

'The two of us, and Gléowine, should now travel to Hunbald at the Entwade crossing, and tell him we will not be turned away.''The two of us, and Gléowine, should return to the Entwash crossing in the Sutcrofts. There we will present ourselves to Hunbald and declare our intention to defy the word of the King.''We must cross the Entwash. Someone must tell the King what befell in Wildermore. The threat of Saruman is greater even than we supposed.''Here we are, my friends. Now what do we do?''Nothing has changed. I have not received word from Edoras that you may pass.'

Hunbald's scowl deepens when you declare your intention to pass anyway.

'Still you defy my charge and that of my King! This cannot be. I cannot allow you across the Entwash. It is a command of Théoden King, and I will not betray my duty! The two of you have seen battle, I am certain of it, but this white-haired fellow has seen none that I can tell! Would you have me raise my sword against an old man?'Hunbald has put his honour on the line, trusting that you and your companions mean no harm to Théoden or to the people of Rohan, and now you should ride for Edoras to bring warning to the King.The barrows outside the entrance to Edoras will be a good place to meet up with your companions.

Hunbald has allowed you to cross the Entwash, and now you should ride to the entrance of Edoras.Ride to the barrows outside the entrance to EdorasHorn is by the barrows outside the entrance to Edoras.

You have arrived at Edoras, and should speak now to Horn by the barrows.Talk to Horn by the barrows outside the entrance to EdorasYou have come to Edoras and should now make your way up through the city.Make your way into Edoras and meet up with GléowineGléowine is on the road within the entrance to Edoras, by a majestic white horse.Talk to Gléowine in Edoras, along the roadThe time has come to climb the streets of Edoras to the entrance to the Golden Hall of Meduseld, there to await the judgement of Théoden.Climb the streets of Edoras to reach the entrance to MeduseldHáma stands before you, and he bars the way to the Golden Hall of Meduseld.Talk to Háma outside the Golden Hall of Meduseld'Wait, there is something familiar about that man... is it Gléowine? Gléowine, is that you? The minstrel of Théoden! On what account do you accompany these folk?'

Gléowine sheepishly steps forward, and you realize that Hunbald was never told of the minstrel's exile.

'It is you!' Hunbald continues. 'Is it true what this ${RACE} says?'

Gléowine responds quickly, and Hunbald muses in silence. After a time, he continues.

'Gléowine, if you will speak for these others, I will allow the three of you to pass. I am merely a simple man from the Eastfold, but you are held in confidence of the King, and must know his mind more thoroughly than ever I could hope to do. For the good of my King, and the Riddermark, I will allow you to pass. Do not make me regret this choice! I would cast my blade aside before I raise it against such as you.''If Gléowine will speak for you, I will allow you to pass. Ride to Edoras, and do not stray from your course. Do not make me regret this decision!''We have come to Edoras, ${PLAYER}, for good or ill.''That was a fortunate turn, was it not?''We have returned to Edoras, ${PLAYER}, and death is upon my mind. Will Théoden King heed the warning we bring, or will he answer our words of caution with admonishment and a more final decree than exile?'

Horn looks to the mounds on the left and right side of the road.

'How long has it been since Meduseld was raised, and how many Kings of Rohan sleep here in death? Ten and six mounds for ten and six kings. Will the Evermind blossoms cover another mound before this war is through, or will no good Men remain to build it? Let us continue into the city. Our errand is urgent, and we have little time for my dark musings!''We should make our way into the city, ${PLAYER}. Keep watch for anything unusual.''Will you look at that, my friend? The glory of the father of horses is returned in Shadowfax, unless my old eyes betray me with hopeful sights!''We will find Gléowine further up the road, within Edoras. Let us join him before we proceed to the Golden Hall and the judgement of Théoden.''Look at this! Unless I am very much mistaken, that horse is Shadowfax, lord of the Mearas! The King gifted him to Gandalf the Wizard some months ago, rather against his wishes, truth be known. It seems the horse has found his way back home, riderless! The King will be pleased, if he does not already know mighty Shadowfax has returned! That will brighten his countenance, for certain! He may be more willing to forgive our defiance of his commands, as well.

'Let us continue onward to the Golden Hall, ${PLAYER}. I am ready.''This is not the first time you have come before the Golden Hall of Meduseld. It is in my mind that you were sent from here in disgrace, by command of the King. What brings you hither against the word of Théoden? How many unwanted visitors will bring ill news here today?'

Háma notices your puzzled expression and continues, 'You are not the only unwelcome guests to come to Meduseld this day. Some are within the Golden Hall this very hour!'It is a day for unwelcome visitors to the Golden Hall, according to Háma, the doorward of Théoden.Háma has grudgingly decided to allow you in to see the King, and will bring you inside the Golden Hall if you speak to him once more.Talk to Háma and he will bring you before the KingYou have reunited with Gandalf within the Golden Hall of Meduseld, and should now speak to the Wizard.Talk to Gandalf within Meduseld'You are not the first to come here today, testing the word of my heart 'gainst the command of my king. Do I compound an earlier error by allowing you into the hall, or do I make all right with a single throw?'

Háma sighs heavily. 'Never have I so rued this post! The will of Théoden is clear, but I do not believe you come here with evil intent. You speak of dangers to the north, and all around, but returning here brings the greatest danger upon yourselves! Is that not the truest sign of your good hearts?

'The King must be told what you have seen. Speak to me when you are ready to continue into the hall, and I shall take your weapons as is the custom. Then you shall proceed before the King, and we will see if I have misjudged you. I hope for your sake, and for mine, that I have not.''If you are willing, I will take your weapons and bring you before the King. Know this: once you step beneath the roof of Meduseld, all chances to repent of your defiance are gone.''The error was my own, if error it was. For my part, I think my heart spoke true!''It seems your road entwines with ours for a pace or two more, ${PLAYER}. Ah, I would not celebrate so quickly were I you! War has already come to Rohan, and its drum-beat deepens with every passing hour. We are beset on all sides by evils both known and unknowable, and to be near Gandalf is to be near danger!'

Gandalf laughs, but his eyes are stern.

'Gríma would say I brought the danger hither, I do not doubt. But I say Gandalf goes where he is needed, and for this day and every day that is to where the danger is deepest!'After being apart for so long, you have reunited once again with Gandalf, Aragorn, Legolas, and Gimli, and eagerly press them for news about what befell them since last you saw them.Aragorn, Legolas, and Gimli wait to speak with you within the Golden Hall of Meduseld.Talk to Aragorn in MeduseldTalk to Gimli in MeduseldTalk to Legolas in MeduseldThéoden is more willing to listen to you, now that Gandalf has spoken words of truth to the King of Rohan.Talk to Théoden in MeduseldThéoden has asked you to bring his commands to Háma, outside the Golden Hall.Talk to Háma outside Meduseld'Much has transpired since last we met, but my companions can give you their account. My own would take too long to tell, and from any other would beggar belief. You would trust my tale, I am certain, but then only because you have experienced some things nearly as peculiar! Know this, ${PLAYER}: I bear tidings both glad and sorrowful. The task set before our mutual friends at the Last Homely House is still ongoing. Though some of that company are here with us in this golden hall, some others carry still the Burden to its destination. The errand is not yet done, but neither is it abandoned. Those be the glad tidings.

'I will let Aragorn tell you what this joyful news has cost us, for it was not bought without strife.''Speak to our friends and they will give you tidings both glad and sorrowful.''Fortune was with us on this throw, ${PLAYER}! I am pleased for our sake, but more than that I am pleased for Rohan! Now, at last, Théoden has been restored and there is hope again for his kingdom!''Théoden King has been restored! It brings joy to this old heart of mine!''I just want what is best for the king! All of you, and especially the Wizard, want to see him weak and powerless! Only Gríma loves him and can care for him!''We meet again, my friend! I am glad of it, for it seems there is a shortage of friendly faces in this land. One man of honour I met, and he was imprisoned for his kindness to me!'

You press Aragorn on the matter of Gandalf's cryptic words, and the Ranger's face falls.

'Gandalf gives you the joyful tidings, and leaves the ill news to me. It has been one week since our fellowship was sundered on Parth Galen, along the banks of the Anduin. Boromir of Gondor fell there. He gave his life protecting the halflings Meriadoc and Peregrin, but they were borne away by great Orcs of Saruman. Others went... another way. I had to make a choice, and I chose it. We could not abandon Merry and Pippin.

'I do not know if I chose aright. The Orcs were destroyed before our pursuit ended, and Gandalf tells me the hobbits found safety. Should I have done otherwise? I cannot say. But now we must do what we can for Rohan, and put behind us what regrets we may.''It has been several weeks since last we met, ${PLAYER}! What have you done with the time? I have cloven a number of Orc skulls since then, but not enough to satisfy my hatred of the beasts. Some of their ilk led us on a merry chase across much of Rohan, and my chest fills with pride to tell you I outpaced even Legolas on that race! I may have been winded, true, but Gimli, Glóin's son, did not abandon the pursuit without need. When we caught up with the Uruk band at last, they had already been slain by Men of Rohan.

'It has been two days since our pursuit ended, and I have nearly caught my breath again. I have never run so far or so fast in all my days, and I would be content to stay in one place for the rest of them, if that place were a mountain with good stone and deep roots!''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}, though we are met on the very precipice of danger. How have you fared since last we spoke?'

You tell Legolas of your adventures, and his light laughter breezily flies around the hall.

'What is it that our young friends used to say? You have gone from the frying-pan into the flames, and the frying-pan was hot enough! You have had adventures to spare, ${PLAYER}; it seems we all have done. I am glad your own journey brought you to Fangorn, as did mine. I could spend years exploring beneath its canopy, never feeling the passage of time, were it not for the pull of that which moves my feet. Once our friends are safe, perhaps I will follow that pull to where it leads.'

Legolas notices your quizzical expression, and his usual laughter melts away. 'The sea, ${PLAYER},' he responds, 'I speak of the sea.''Our errand is not ended. We must do what we can, while others pursue the true course of its completion.''No more running, I hope! Though I do not care for the beasts, I would rather be carried by a willing horse than run across the fields of Rohan once again!'Legolas smiles at you, but he is deep in thought and has nothing to say.'Théoden King has been restored, and now Rohan has a chance to fight against the evils of Saruman! My heart is filled with hope, ${PLAYER}, and I am eager to see the Wizard pay for his crimes!''The sight of Théoden King standing proudly and tall again makes me feel younger than I have done in years, ${PLAYER}.''I only want what is best for the king!''I feel as if a heavy shroud has been lifted from my sight. My days were filled with dark dreams, and my nights were no different. What foul craft of Gríma's is to blame? No, not his doing alone. It was my weakness that allowed him so to dominate my mind. No longer! I am myself again, and free of his whisperings. It is well that Gandalf arrived here when he did, and that Háma allowed him to pass the threshold. That transgression I will forgive.

'Gríma may have intended further mischief. Indeed, I am certain of it. Leave Meduseld and speak to Háma. Tell him I want Gríma's chambers searched, and my sister-son Éomer released from imprisonment. These actions were poorly-done, and must be righted.''Gríma may have intended further mischief. Indeed, I am certain of it. Leave Meduseld and speak to Háma. Tell him I want Gríma's chambers searched, and my sister-son Éomer released from imprisonment. These actions were poorly-done, and must be righted.''Only Gríma cares for Théoden King! These others want him hurt, and powerless, and weak!''Yes? You return from within on your own power, which tells me your wager was correct and you will draw breath until tomorrow, at least.'

Háma listens as you recount what befell within the Golden Hall, and his questioning face is soon aglow with smiles.

'These are better tidings than I believed possible! You are a friend both new and true to bring me such words! Théoden, my King, you have been brought from the darkness by the wisdom of Gandalf! He is the Storm-crow no longer! White Wizard, friend to Rohan and its people! I rejoice to hear it! Théoden King, restored at last!'Háma has guided you to the chambers where Gríma Wormtongue kept his bed in Edoras, so you may carry out the King's desire that the room be searched for further tendrils of Wormtongue's treachery.Gríma Wormtongue's former chambers can be found north-east of the bottom of the Meduseld steps, in Edoras.Find the entrance to Gríma Wormtongue's chambers in EdorasGríma's chambers can be found north-east of the bottom of the Meduseld steps, in Edoras.

How many secrets remain to be discovered within Wormtongue's chambers?How many secrets remain to be discovered within Wormtongue's chambers?How many secrets remain to be discovered within Wormtongue's chambers?How many secrets remain to be discovered within Wormtongue's chambers?Háma should be told what you discovered in Wormtongue's chambers.You found a number of stolen items in Gríma Wormtongue's chambers, and should confront him with them.You found a number of stolen items in Gríma Wormtongue's chambers, and should now present them to the King.'I will guide you to Gríma's chambers, as the King instructs. We must remain alert, ${PLAYER}, for Gríma may have left the seeds of some mischief in his chambers. He may still manage to work some evil, and that would be a cruel cut indeed, to know of his treachery and still succumb to it.

'Gríma's chambers are north-east of the steps that lead to Meduseld. Come, I will show you the way.''Stay alert, and I will show you the way to Gríma's chambers. Then we will search them for further seeds of treachery, as Théoden King commands.''It would seem we are not the first to search Gríma's chambers this day, ${PLAYER}.''It would seem we are not the first to search Gríma's chambers this day, ${PLAYER}.''It would seem we are not the first to search Gríma's chambers this day, ${PLAYER}.''You found the key? Well done, ${PLAYER}! See if it fits the lock!''What did you find there? That is Herugrim, the sword of Théoden! It is possible that the King asked Gríma to keep the sword safe in his chest, but such safe-keeping is no longer necessary. I will return Herugrim to its master, once I have brought Éomer word of his release.

'For now, bring these other items to Gríma in Meduseld and see what he has to say about them. These items are both too varied and too valuable to be his property, I'll warrant, and his explanation for possessing them should be interesting.''I will bring Herugrim to the King after I have released Éomer from his confinement. Meanwhile, you should present those stolen items to Gríma in Meduseld and hear his explanation for how they came to be within his chest.''What are these things? Why do you show them to me, vile ${RACE}?'

You explain that the goods were taken from the chest in his chambers, and demand an explanation.

'Every one of those items was a gift!' Gríma hisses. 'You are the thief! I demand that you return those things to my chambers, and when you return to my presence you come bearing an apology! I have suffered at the hands of these ingrates, and now my own possessions are being paraded before all the court, and my name dragged through the slime! These are the thanks I receive for my months of tireless service to the King?

'You are a liar, ${RACE}, and a thief! Your voice drips with poison and your words are all false! I curse thee, and beg that some kind heart will defend me from your insults and your slander!''Lies! All lies! Your words are false!''Where did you get these items? You say they were found within Gríma's chest? I see.'

Théoden casts a dark look in the direction of his former servant.

'I will have one of my men restore these items to their proper owners. I am of a mind to send this worm back to his proper owner, as well. Would it be a mercy, I wonder? On this day, and in sight of his continued crimes, I am more interested in justice than in mercy.'Théoden has been restored and now it is time to repair some of the other wrongs worked by Gríma Wormtongue in Edoras.Éowyn is within the Golden Hall of Meduseld.

Théoden has removed an embroidered handkerchief from the collection of stolen goods you recovered from Wormtongue's chambers, and wants you to return it to its original owner: his niece Éowyn.Éowyn has asked you to return her favour to her brother Éomer, currently imprisoned at his chambers, north of Meduseld, in Edoras.Find the entrance to Éomer's chambersÉowyn has asked you to return her favour to her brother Éomer, currently imprisoned at his chambers, north of Meduseld, in Edoras.Even imprisoned, Éomer has continued to plan for the defence of Rohan. He has asked you to study the map in his chambers so you may better understand the geography of the Kingdom.Study the map in Éomer's chambersWhen you have fully studied the map in Éomer's chambers, you should return to him to learn what he thinks of the strategic situation in Rohan.Talk to Éomer in his chambersÉowyn is in the Golden Hall of Meduseld.

Éomer has asked you to return to his sister, and to thank her for her sympathy and her worry.Talk to Éowyn in MeduseldGandalf is in the Golden Hall of Meduseld, and he will likely have some advice for you.Talk to Gandalf in Meduseld'Wait, there is one item you should return yourself, as its owner is here in the hall.'

Théoden scowls and hands you a delicate cloth. On a corner of the cloth is embroidered the name Éowyn.

'I am not so poor a judge as to think my sister-daughter Éowyn intended Gríma to have this token of her favour. Return it to her and she will tell you for whom it was intended.''This token of her favour belongs to the one who gave it: my sister-daughter Éowyn. She is here in the Golden Hall.'Éowyn accepts the embroidered cloth from you with surprise.

'I remember this token! I gave it to my brother Éomer to bring him good fortune in battle, and he wore it on his ranging throughout the Mark! It must have been taken from him when he was confined to his chambers by my uncle's command! No. The command was my lord Théoden's, but the words were Gríma's, and my brother's confinement was carried out by those men Gríma selected. One of them must have taken this token from Éomer and given it to Gríma. Had I known this, I would not have allowed it!

'Bring this token of mine to my brother, and tell him our uncle is restored! He is being confined to his chambers in Edoras, north of Meduseld.''Bring that token of mine to my brother, and tell him our uncle is restored! He is being confined to his chambers in Edoras, north of Meduseld.''${PLAYER}! I am glad to see you are well. Háma has brought me the news that I am free to leave, but that is not what lightens my heart. The most joyous tidings are that my uncle Théoden has awakened to the fact of his peril, and his treacherous counsellor has been dragged to light!'

Éomer accepts the embroidered cloth from you, and crushes it in one fist. 'This was taken from me by Forthere, one of Gríma's men. I am pleased to have it back, but I would sooner teach Forthere a lesson about respecting the belongings of others.

'I was treated well enough by the guards outside my chambers, but my inability to do anything for my family or for Rohan was the cruelest fact of my imprisonment. I spent many hours studying the map of Rohan in the room upstairs, trying to develop a strategy for the defence of the Kingdom that could not be undone by Saruman. Go! Study the map yourself and see if there is such a strategy I might have missed, for I came up with little of use.''Study the map of Rohan in the other room and see if some strategy presents itself to you, my friend.'You study the map of Rohan for a time, but few strategies present themselves to you that could stand against the armies of Saruman.'Did you find some strategy that could serve us against the armies of Saruman? Sadly, I did not, and I had more time to think on the problem than you had. There is no shame in it! Our strength lies on the open fields, but against such numbers as you say Saruman has assembled, I do not think our strength will suffice. The Mark is not as defensible as we need it to be!

'Return to my sister and give her my thanks and my love. I will see her when I return to the hall with Háma. Perhaps we will look at the map once more, and see if there is something we have missed....''Return to my sister Éowyn and tell her I will see her soon.''My brother is free of his cage? I delight to hear it, for soon I too will be free of the cage that binds me!'

Éowyn's smile brightens her face in the same way as does the sun breaking through a gap in the clouds.

'My cage is not made of metal or of wood, ${PLAYER}, but it is a cage all the same. In time of peace, it confines me to the Golden Hall and its out-buildings, but when war threatens, it cannot hope to withstand the force of our great need. I am a shield-maiden of Rohan, and I shall take up sword and spear to defend this land and its people! My uncle must surely ride now to Isengard to challenge Saruman, and I will not hesitate to take up arms and ride with him!''When my uncle rides to war, I will not refuse him. I am a shield-maiden of Rohan, and I will not shrink from the challenge of war!''All the people of Rohan will need to stand if the Kingdom is to survive, but their King must do so first.'Gandalf wishes for the King to step outside his hall and look upon the city, and to decide what can be done for the survival of Rohan.Théoden is in the Golden Hall of Meduseld.

Gandalf has asked the King to step outside the Golden Hall and look upon the city. You should speak now to Théoden and see if he is willing to do as the Wizard entreats.Talk to Théoden and see if he is willing to step outside his hallAragorn, Éomer, and Gandalf are within the Golden Hall of Meduseld.

Théoden King has determined his next course of action, and those who follow him have their orders.Talk to Aragorn in MeduseldTalk to Éomer in MeduseldTalk to Gandalf in MeduseldThéoden is in the Golden Hall of Meduseld, waiting to speak with you about his next course of action: war!Talk to Théoden in MeduseldÉowyn is outside the Golden Hall of Meduseld, in Edoras.Talk to Éowyn in Edoras, outside Meduseld'The time has come for Théoden to step beyond the confines of his hall and look out over his city. Speak to the King and see if he is willing to do this, ${PLAYER}.''Speak to Théoden and see if he is willing to step beyond the confines of his hall.''I am ready to look out upon my city with unclouded eyes, ${PLAYER}, free from the shroud of Gríma's whispers. Will you walk with me as well?''For too long have I stayed beneath the roof of my hall, ${PLAYER}. No more will I delay! I will ride forth to Isengard with my warriors, and we will remind Saruman that the Rohirrim still live!''There is strength in Théoden, and that same strength will flow into the will of his people. If its King is strong, so will be the Kingdom. Gladly will I ride to Isengard with Théoden and his men, there to demand Saruman cast down his staff! Friends of mine were endangered by the commands of that Wizard, and I have a debt to repay on that score.''I have been given my freedom once again, and now I am free to ride to war with my King! It is a far better day into which I walk than the one I left! We will show Saruman that Rohan is strong. For too long he has done as he will, but now we will remind him that this land is not his to despoil!

'My road leads west, with the King, but he has asked you to assist Éowyn at Edoras. I am certain there will be no lack of deeds that will need doing, but if you have an opportunity, perhaps you could see how fares Aldburg in the Eastfold? That town is my home, and it would be a comfort to my mind to know that you have kept an eye on it.

'There is no hurry! Assist Éowyn with her preparations here, first. But keep Aldburg in mind while I am away, if you would.''It is good that Théoden takes action to protect his people. Saruman's greatest ally for these past months has been the inaction of Théoden, sown by the whisperings of Wormtongue. How many Orcs were given free passage across the fields of Rohan? Even one would be too many, and it was much more than a single Orc.

'I do not say that Théoden rides to victory. It is good that he comes forth, but the armies of Saruman are vast and I do not know if the Rohirrim can meet them on the field.''I will collect the debt Saruman owes for endangering my friends.''I am ready to ride to war alongside my King!''This way may not lead to victory, but it may lead to a less certain defeat.''For too long have I stayed beneath the roof of my hall, ${PLAYER}. No more will I delay! I will ride forth to Isengard with my warriors, and we will remind Saruman that the Rohirrim still live!

'My sister-daughter Éowyn will chafe at my command that she remain behind and make the people ready to leave Edoras, I know. But she will carry out my wish. Speak to her outside the Golden Hall, and lend her what aid you may in this.''Éowyn will chafe against my command that she remain, but she will do her duty. Speak to her outside Meduseld and see if it be so.''There is no honour in this! Why must I remain behind while my brother and my uncle ride to war? Do they care more for Rohan than do I? No! I would fight as hard for the people of the Mark as any man, and mayhap with even greater ferocity!'

Éowyn's eyes blaze with a dangerous fire for a moment, and her fists clench and unclench.

'I will do as my King commands, ${PLAYER}. This duty has been given to me, and I will carry it out as best I may, though it be not my desire. None will say that Éowyn betrayed the word of her King. I will protect the people of Edoras and see them to safety, while the battle rages elsewhere. It may come to us, in the end, and if it does I will be ready. I will need your help in the meantime.'Éowyn has a great number of things to do before the people of Edoras can be evacuated to Dunharrow, and she needs your help to see them done.Éowyn has given you a proclamation to read at the market north of Meduseld, to prepare the people of Edoras to leave the city.Several of the people at the market north of Meduseld need to be convinced of the wisdom of Éowyn's plan.Convince Anwynd to obey the word of ThéodenConvince Baduhild to obey the word of ThéodenÉowyn has given you a proclamation to read at the top of the stairs, north-west of Meduseld, to prepare the people of Edoras to leave the city.Ethryth is atop the steps north-west of Meduseld, and has sought to catch your eye.Talk to Ethryth atop the stairs north-west of MeduseldEthryth's belongings are at the top of the stairs north-west of Meduseld. The waggon to Dunharrow is near the outside walls to the north of Ethryth.Pick up Ethryth's belongingsÉowyn has given you a proclamation to read by the entrance courtyard in the north-west, to prepare the people of Edoras to leave the city.A small child, Derláf, has sought to get your attention.Talk to Derláf at the entrance courtyard in the north-west of EdorasGrowling dissenters insulted the King at the courtyard near the north-west entrance to Edoras.Talk to a growling dissenterÉowyn has given you a proclamation to read at the two stairs south of the north-west entrance, to prepare the people of Edoras to leave the city.Several of the people at the courtyard with two stairs need to be convinced of the wisdom of Éowyn's plan.Convince Colinan to obey the word of ThéodenConvince Herith to obey the word of ThéodenÉowyn is outside Meduseld, in Edoras.

You have helped prepare the people of Edoras prepare to leave their city, and should return to Éowyn with a report of your success.'There are many things to do before the people of Edoras can leave for Dunharrow. My uncle's command must be brought to the townsfolk, waggons must be loaded with provisions, belongings gathered, and there are like to be some naysayers who refuse to follow the King's will.

'Take this proclamation and read it before the people at several locations throughout the city. Start with the market north of the Meduseld steps, and then work down through the streets of Edoras. Word will spread quickly; it has already begun to do so! But the King's command must be brought to the people so the preparations for their departure can be made. Not everyone will be pleased with this, ${PLAYER}. Be kind, if you can, but keep your resolve strong. This must be done. Théoden King has commanded it.''Read the proclamation I gave you aloud before the people of Edoras. It contains Théoden King's command to leave the city. Not all of the townsfolk will be happy to hear it, but they will need to obey the word of their king.''You read the proclamation aloud? Return and see if the people have consented to do as their king commands.''Read the proclamation I gave you aloud before the people of Edoras. It contains Théoden King's command to leave the city. Not all of the townsfolk will be happy to hear it, but they will need to obey the word of their king.''You read the proclamation aloud? Return and see if the people have consented to do as their king commands.''Do you have the time to spare an old woman, tidings-bearer? I have heard the commands of Théoden King, and would obey, but it has been many years since Ethryth felt the bloom of youth, and I do not have the strength I once did. My belongings are bundled in this crate, but I cannot carry it down the hill to the Dunharrow waggon without help. The waggon is to the north of here, if you follow the road.

'Can you bring my possessions to the waggon north of here? Just follow the road. Ethryth will be in your debt.''I hope it is not too much trouble for you to carry my belongings to the Dunharrow waggon. It is north of here, along the road.''You agreed to help an old woman carry her belongings to the Dunharrow waggon? You should do that at once!''Read the proclamation I gave you aloud before the people of Edoras. It contains Théoden King's command to leave the city. Not all of the townsfolk will be happy to hear it, but they will need to obey the word of their king.''How did the people react to the proclamation? Return to the entrance courtyard in the north-west of the city and see.''I heard you read the words of Théoden King, and I know everyone who is not going to fight needs to leave Edoras. But those men over there were laughing, and I heard them insult the name of the King!

'You are not going to let them escape justice, are you? Everyone has to obey the word of Théoden King!''They were insulting the King! They cannot be allowed to do that!''Did you encounter trouble when you read the proclamation aloud before the people?''What gives you the right to order us out of our homes? Maybe those are the commands of Théoden, and maybe they are not. Maybe they are the words of Gandalf! Maybe the Wizard is working against us, and just wants our treasures for himself! Do they not say that trouble follows him wherever he goes?

'I will teach you a lesson, ${RACE}! I will teach you what happens when you do the bidding of Wizards!''Read the proclamation I gave you aloud before the people of Edoras. It contains Théoden King's command to leave the city. Not all of the townsfolk will be happy to hear it, but they will need to obey the word of their king.''You read the proclamation aloud? Return and see if the people have consented to do as their king commands.''You have done as I asked? Good. It will be some time before all the people are ready to leave Edoras, but every journey must begin with preparation, especially one as important as this.'Éowyn has uncovered some evidence that allies of Gríma Wormtongue seek to undermine her efforts to bring the people of Edoras to safety.Éowyn has given you some writings from the search of Gríma's chambers, and wants you to find out where in the city these clues might refer.Éowyn is outside Meduseld, in Edoras.

You discovered several allies of Wormtongue working to hinder Éowyn's efforts in Edoras, and should report back to her that they will no longer be a problem.'There are still so many tasks we must see to completion before the people are ready to leave Edoras, but something worries at the back of my mind and divides my attention. When you searched Gríma's chambers with Háma, you told my uncle that the room was already in disarray, as if it had been searched before you arrived. Why would Gríma leave his chamber in such a state? If he was seeking something specific, surely he would know where to find it. No, I think his room was searched by someone entangled deeply within his schemes.

'Gríma has fled the city, but it is my belief that this ally, at least, remains. Among the papers you collected was the cryptic phrase 'No Mere Wit, He' and a number of other writings. I do not know what they mean, but mention is made of a number of locations throughout Edoras. Go to some of these and see if you can find sign of Gríma's friends or allies.''We have enough tasks before us without having to worry about the interference of Gríma Wormtongue, ${PLAYER}. His allies lurk still within Edoras, I am sure of it.''You found some of Gríma's allies, and defeated them? Good. Those snakes would have caused trouble for us, especially once my uncle rides to Isengard.

'I hope that was all of them, ${PLAYER}. I still do not understand all of the mysteries you uncovered in Gríma's chambers, but perhaps we have ended the schemes that were plotted there.

'My uncle and his riders are ready to depart.'Thane Edric and Léoflad struggle to ensure the safety of their people as the forces of the Enemy draw ever closer.Ordgar can be found near the gates into Entwade.

Thane Edric has asked that you speak to his guards inside and near the river Entwash to learn of any news of the Enemy's whereabouts in Kingstead.Talk to OrdgarInguc can be found slightly to the south-east outside the gates of Entwade.

You have spoken to Ordgar. You should continue to speak to Thane Edric's guards in hopes of discovering why the attacks have ceased in the past week.Talk to IngucAnrad can be found slightly to the north outside the gates of Entwade.

You have spoken to Inguc of more threats in Kingstead. You should speak to Anrad before returning to Thane Edric.Talk to AnradThane Edric can be found near the waggons outside the Mead Hall of Entwade.

You have spoken to Edric's men and learned much of the threats facing Kingstead. You should now return to Thane Edric.Talk to Thane Edric'Entwade has been the victim of many assaults as of late, ${PLAYER}. For my part, my men and I have kept the people from harm and our homes largely intact. 

'However, in the past week, the attacks have ceased. It does not take one well-versed in strategy to presume that a larger attack will soon fall upon us. I have been busy helping my wife prepare the people for travel to Aldburg and any new information of our foes' whereabouts has not yet been delivered to me.

'If you would, speak to my guards near the Entwash and in town. They may have more insight on the dangers we'll be left to deal with once my people depart.''Thane Edric wishes to know what has become of our foes?

'The Uruks still ride along the plains, and the King has done naught to drive them off.  He stripped us of most of our men, and soon we shall be even fewer when Léoflad and the rest depart. The Uruks circle us like vultures, ${PLAYER}, and there is no telling when they shall strike at us next or if they direct their wrath towards Middlemead.

'That is all of I know of what is to come, ${PLAYER}. The people must make ready to leave as soon as they are able.''It has been long since I have spoken with Thane Edric, and while it is  true that the relative peace has held, I fear it will soon end.

'During my post a few days ago, I spied Orcs crossing the Entwash -- with much difficulty, mind you -- passing onto the shore of Kingstead. These Orcs looked different, and they did not bear the markings of the White Hand. Moreover, they appeared to be fleeing from the farmstead nearby. Perhaps Reeve Fastred is as brutal as I have heard it told....

'I am not one to believe that an Orc can know fear, so I am certain we have not seen the last of them. Thane Edric surely knows not of this, and he must be advised about such troubles.''I cannot tell you much more than you already know, ${PLAYER}. 

'The Uruk-hai have roamed in from both east and west, and I fear that they have stolen the horses of the Broadacres in their travels. I fear that they are responsible for Middlemead's silence as of late as well, though I know Mágla would not allow such foes to come close to his people. Ah well, the troubles facing Entwade are numerous enough.

'None shall be safe until the roads are cleared of their kind, and of that, I am sure Thane Edric knows.''It is as I feared, ${PLAYER}. More foes rally around our lands even now, and with such little time before my people depart.

'We shall have to find and strike at these threats as soon as we are able, for only then will my people find safe passage.'Uruks of the White Hand encroach ever closer on the settlements of Kingstead, aiming to starve or slay any in their path.Crates of Kingstead crops can be found along the road to Middlemead in eastern Kingstead.

You have discovered a crate of crops that has survived the Uruk onslaught. You should recover as many as you can to aid the forces at Entwade.Collect crates of Kingstead crops (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have recovered many crates of Kingstead crops and should now prepare to return them to Entwade.CompletedAmid the wreckage along the road to Middlemead, you have discovered a crate of crops from Kingstead that withstood the Uruk assault. If more crates remain intact, such supplies would be of great value to the warriors of Entwade in their preparation for the coming assault.You have recovered many of the crops pillaged by the Uruks near Entwade. With these renewed supplies, Entwade will have the rations necessary to hold through the impending assault.Thane Mágla is concerned that an agent of Wormtongue lurks within Middlemead, and hopes for you to work alongside his trusted councilman to expose the traitor.Bosa's Farm can be found to the east of Middlemead in northern Kingstead.

Thane Mágla has asked you to seek out Egbalth in hopes of aiding him in exposing the agent of Wormtongue in Middlemead.Travel to Bosa's FarmBosa's Farm can be found in northern Kingstead.

You have arrived at Bosa's Farm to find it overrun by the White Hand. You should search the farm for any signs of life.Search for any sign of EgbalthEgbalth can be found near one of the burned houses at Bosa's Farm in northern Kingstead.

You have spotted a fallen soldier of Middlemead. You should investigate if this is the man Mágla meant for you to meet.Examine Egbalth'As I mentioned, ${PLAYER}, I believe Wormtongue's influence still lingers in Kingstead.

'To that end, I have kept careful watch on the people of Middlemead. Our trade routes from the Broadacres and the Stonedeans have been disrupted by the White Hand, and I sense that our fields are next. I sent one of my finest warriors to station at Bosa's Farm to the east to ensure that crops continue to flow into our settlement.

'I wish for you to rally to Egbalth's side at Bosa's Farm. Perhaps you can offer him aid in warding off the White Hand as the farmhands ready the harvest for trade, and when you both return, we can speak more of our suspicions.'Egbalth's lifeless body is slumped against the side of a burned home, and brutal wounds are visible through punctures in his armour.

Whether his horn was sounded or not, it is clear that aid did not arrive in time.The Uruks at Bosa's Farm have erected banners of the White Hand to stake their claim in Kingstead.Bosa's Farm can be found to the east of Middlemead in northern Kingstead.

You have discovered the ruined remains of Bosa's Farm and should burn the White Hand banners you discovered.Burn banners of the White HandWhite Hand banners can be found scattered about Bosa's Farm to the east of Middlemead.

Uruks of the White Hand have laid claim to Bosa's Farm. You should burn their banners to aid in loosening the White Hand's grip on Kingstead.Burn banners of the White Hand (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have burned many banners of the White Hand and erased the Uruks' claim to Kingstead's lands.CompletedYou have arrived at Bosa's Farm to find it completely overrun by Uruks of the White Hand. Already, they have worked to stake a claim to the northern reaches of Kingstead and have erected their vile banners in the charred ruins of the farm.

These banners must be burned to show the Uruks that Kingstead and Edoras will never fall into the hands of the Enemy.You have burned many banners of the White Hand in retribution for the atrocities the Uruks have carried out. Although the loss of life cannot be undone, the White Hand will no longer be able to lay claim to Kingstead's lands.Following the tragic deaths of his wife and people, Thane Edric journeys to Edoras to confront Éowyn.Thane Edric can be found inside the Mead Hall of Entwade.

Thane Edric has asked you to accompany him to Edoras to meet with Éowyn. You should speak to him when you are ready.Talk to Thane EdricÉowyn can be found outside Meduseld in Edoras.

You have incapacitated Edric, sparing the people of Edoras his rage. You should now speak with Éowyn.Talk to Éowyn outside Meduseld'I desire not to speak, ${PLAYER}. Agelman has told me of my people... and Léoflad.'

Edric stares into the distance, eyes glazing over until he suddenly turns to you.

'What use have I to remain here? We will travel to Edoras and speak with Éowyn, and that will be the end of it.

'Come, we ride now!''Let us travel, ${PLAYER}. I have little patience left to spare...''Thank you for your aid, ${PLAYER}.

'Edric's judgement was hindered greatly by his loss. I hope in time he will see the error of his ways. He shall be cared for here in Edoras, and when he is willing, I would see him returned to his post should he wish it. He has lost enough... I would not deny him this too, even in the wake of this foolishness.

'As for the attack on Entwade, we must make ready to ride. I shall fight in his place!'Amid growing dangers in Kingstead, Thane Edric and Léoflad struggle to prepare their people to journey to the Eastfold.Thane Edric and his wife, Léoflad, can be found outside the Mead Hall of Entwade.

Thane Edric has asked you to aid Léoflad and his people as they make ready to travel. You should speak to Edric and Léoflad.Thane Edric can be found outside the Mead Hall of Entwade near the waggons.

You have aided the people of Entwade greatly, and should now speak to Thane Edric of what is to be done next.Talk to Thane Edric'Well then, welcome to Entwade, traveller. I have little time to spend chatting with one so ill-acquainted with the troubles of Rohan. The King has ridden west and left me many problems and not enough men to handle them. Be him renewed or not, my people have still been forgotten.

'Look to my side, traveller. My wife, Léoflad, struggles to make ready to evacuate my people to Aldburg. Such a task would be difficult even if the Uruks were not riding our plains, but such things are commonplace under Théoden King.

'Let me speak no more of him, for my wife thinks such things bode ill for my temper. If you would help us prepare, we would be grateful for your aid, though I know not how much I can repay you in these dire times.''You have done well, ${PLAYER}. With these threats identified, and my people prepared for the journey, they can set off.

'You have saved me a great deal of time, and provided my people the safe passage they so dearly needed. Their journey will take some time, but they will find the surest of safety within Aldburg.

'As for those threats to the north and south, I have much more to ask of you before I say my farewells to my wife and people, so do not wander yet, friend.'Thane Edric and his men struggle to make ready for the coming siege of Entwade amid the numerous threats already surrounding the town.Thane Edric's men can be found inside Entwade, as well as along the road west to Middlemead and among the fields outside southern Entwade.

Thane Edric has asked that you aid his men in identifying and minimizing the threats that lurk outside Entwade, as well as helping the men of the settlement bolster its defences before the White Hand returns to siege the town.Thane Edric can be found in the Mead Hall of Entwade in eastern Kingstead.

You have aided Thane Edric's men greatly in preparing the town and eliminating the Enemy's nearest forces.Talk to Thane Edric'Hail, ${PLAYER}.  Léoflad and my people have set off to Aldburg, and my hope is that they shall arrive having encountered nary a trouble.

'Their safety weighs heavily upon me, friend. I have held the Wade for years, and I would not have sent them away, if I saw not the danger approaching from all sides. Yet, I wish Théoden had done more to aid us....he could have secured Kingstead before leaving us to fend for ourselves among so many foes. As it stands, he has left his niece to handle his affairs here, but I think her no more likely to lead than him, even spared the venomous whisperings of Wormtongue.

'This, of course, brings me to you. You have come into our lands and aided my people well. There are many matters to attend to in Entwade, and all of them of dire importance. Without a concerted effort, there can be no way to address them all before time runs out.''Few could deny the effort you have put forth, ${PLAYER}. My men and I are grateful for what you have done to aid us in preparing for the White Hand's next assault. Without the King's aid, we were ill-prepared for such a task, but should the Wade hold, he and I shall have words upon his return.

'Come then, we must talk of what is to be done with this man you have found.'Éowyn is in need of cheering up, though she hides her dire mood well for the sake of her people.Éowyn is in Edoras.

You should speak with Éowyn.Speak with ÉowynÉowyn is in Edoras.

You should follow Éowyn.Follow ÉowynÉowyn is in Edoras.

You should speak with Éowyn.Rouse Éowyn from her unhappy reverieÉowyn is in Edoras.

You should listen to Éowyn.Listen to ÉowynÉowyn is in Edoras.

You should speak with Éowyn.Listen to ÉowynÉowyn is ready to face the foe that threatens Edoras.Join Éowyn in battleFight alongside Éowyn.Fight alongside ÉowynSpeak with Éowyn.Speak with Éowyn'That was the most fun I've had in...I cannot even remember! Was that not a wonderful rush, ${PLAYER}?

'I have half a mind to ride out and find another enemy to slay...but I must return, I know. The women and children and elderly must get to Dunharrow safely, and it is my duty to make it so.

'Come, let us return to Edoras.''Come, let us talk while we walk. What is it you wished to say to me?'

You tell Éowyn that you know of the sadness she hides beneath her cool, brave exterior, and that some people are grown deeply worried for her.Éowyn's eyes are far away as you look at her, a bitterness upon her fair features that is sad to look upon.

You gently speak her name, and she looks at you once more, her reverie broken.'All right, all right! You may come with me, if you wish. I would not deny the wishes of such a worthy ${RACE}.

'Let us leave at once! I long for battle.''There they are. Look at them! My blade thirsts for their blood, ${PLAYER}.

'We ride!''We have done it!'Éowyn sits proudly in Meduseld, hearing the concerns of her people before their departure to Dunharrow.Éowyn stands in Meduseld as she presides over Edoras in King Théoden's absence.

You should speak with Éowyn.Speak with ÉowynÉowyn stands in Meduseld as she presides over Edoras in King Théoden's absence.

You should listen to Éowyn.Listen to ÉowynÉowyn stands in Meduseld as she presides over Edoras in King Théoden's absence.

You should listen to Éowyn.Listen to ÉowynÉowyn stands in Meduseld as she presides over Edoras in King Théoden's absence.

You should speak with Éowyn.Speak with ÉowynWitwyn, Éowyn's serving-woman, can be found in Meduseld.

Witwyn wishes to speak with you.Speak with Witwyn, the serving-woman'I do not know why I suddenly spilled my fears to you, ${PLAYER}. There is something trustworthy about you.

'Will you help my lady? Perhaps we can think of something that will cheer her, now that her uncle is recovered from his stupour.''I will gladly hear your concerns, my good stewards.

'Pray tell me what goes on in Edoras on the eve of our departure to Dunharrow, so that I may make this passage easier for our people.''I...I must leave. I have much to do to prepare for our departure. I beg your pardon, ${PLAYER}.

'You are welcome in Edoras, so you may feel free to go wherever you wish.''Did you chance to notice my lady's face, ${PLAYER}? Perhaps you did not see it; some sadness or gloom has taken her.

'Those who love her well see it settled on her shoulders like a rain-cloud mantle. I grow more worried for her with every day that passes....'Lady Fremedon of Underharrow finds herself at odds with Éowyn as she makes ready for the impending migration to Dunharrow.Ellen Fremedon can be found outside the Mead Hall of Underharrow.

You have arrived in Underharrow and should acquaint yourself with Ellen Fremedon and her family.Talk to Ellen FremedonEllen Fremedon can be found around Underharrow.

You have asked to speak more with Ellen Fremedon, and she is now telling you of the burdens that fall on Underharrow.Walk with Ellen FremedonEllen Fremedon can be found near the river Snowbourn in Underharrow.

You have finished speaking to Ellen Fremedon and met her daughter. You should now aid her in helping the people of Rohan.Talk to Ellen Fremedon'Come, there are many matters to be dealt with!''Come, let us talk of other things.''I am sorry, ${PLAYER}.

'No matter how I try to sway her, my daughter idolises Éowyn. While I admire her passion, she is still too young of age to follow such a charge.

'Come, let us return to the Mead Hall. There we can talk more of what is to be done.'Éowyn, Mágla, and Ellen Fremedon have made their preparations, and now, they stand ready to guard the people of Kingstead as they travel to Dunharrow.Éowyn can be found on the Firienfeld at Dunharrow.

