Arcil asked you to speak with Éothain and inform him of the decision, but also to deliver an apology.Talk to ÉothainArcil is in the Mead Hall of Aldburg.

Éothain has accepted Arcil's apology and asked you to deliver his response.Talk to Arcil'The White Hand are dangerous, but they do not have the numbers that the unknown Orcs do. While I do not believe that my original decision was misguided, I may have been too quick to dismiss Éothain's thoughts on this matter.

'What is important now is that we deal with these Orcs swiftly and with great force. Please deliver my apology to Éothain and let him know of my decision.''Arcil sent you with an apology? I...perhaps he is not as inept as I once believed. I must ponder this later, though it seems we are ready to deal with the true threat.

'Let Arcil know that his apology is accepted, but was not necessary. He has my sword when it is needed.''It appears that Éothain and I have come to terms. He is a great warrior and has served us well. We will need his sword when the time comes.

'And that time is now. ${PLAYER}, you have proven a great warrior, and I am giving you the responsibility of leading the attack on the White Hand Orcs.'Thane Arcil has invited you to dinner, where he and Éothain will discuss the Orc-threat and how best to proceed.Thane Arcil is in the Mead Hall of Aldburg.

Thane Arcil has invited you to join him for dinner. He and Éothain will attempt to resolve their differences and determine a course of action to deal with the Orcs. You should talk to Arcil when you are prepared to enter the Mead Hall of Aldburg for dinner.Talk to ArcilBoth Arcil and Éothain stated their case about which Orc-tribe poses the greatest threat.

You should speak with Arcil and let him know your thoughts.Talk to Arcil'We are about to take supper, and we would be honoured if you would join us. 

'Since Éothain and I are in disagreement on the issue of the Orcs, I will afford him the opportunity to speak his side, and I would like for you to be there to help us decide what we should do.''Let us eat, ${PLAYER}!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This is a solo quest and cannot be completed in a fellowship.</rgb>'I must admit, Éothain provided a strong case for going after the White Hand Orcs, but I cannot in good conscience approve a course of action that leaves Aldburg defenceless.

'I will leave it in your hands, ${PLAYER}. If you decide that  these new Orcs pose a greater threat, then come and speak with me again. If you decide to help Éothain against the White Hand, I will hold no ill will towards you.'You were warned that the road to Aldburg may be frought with danger.The road from Edoras to Aldburg sees much traffic, which makes it an attractive place for Orcs to attack.

You should be wary as you journey to Aldburg and deal with any threats as they arise.Assess the damage done to the overturned waggonThe road from Edoras to Aldburg sees much traffic, which makes it an attractive place for Orcs to attack.

You should be wary as you journey to Aldburg and deal with any threats as they arise.Assess the damage done to the overturned waggonWhite Hand Orcs rush toward the overturned cart ready to attack.

Defeat the White Hand attackers.Defeat White Hand Orcs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})This Orc-attack has been quelled, but it is only a matter of time before another one occurs. Nevertheless, you have made travel somewhat safer between Aldburg and Edoras.CompletedThe road from Edoras to Aldburg sees much traffic, which makes it an attractive place for Orcs to attack.

You should be wary as you journey to Aldburg and deal with any threats as they arise.This Orc-attack has been quelled, but it is only a matter of time before another one occurs. Nevertheless, you have made travel somewhat safer between Aldburg and Edoras.After a great deal of journeying, the enigmatic Bert Bartleby has finally decided to have a word with you.Bert Bartleby is by the stables in the town of Forlaw, in Wildermore.

Bingo told you that Bert Bartleby wanted to have a word with you.Talk to Bert Bartleby in the town of ForlawThe lore-master was last seen near the west-gate of Forlaw.

Bert Bartleby asked you to find the lore-master that came to Forlaw and buy some potions and scrolls from him.Find the lore-master at the west gate of ForlawOne of the guards at the west gate of Forlaw has called you over to him.Talk to the guard of Forlaw at the west gateBert Bartleby is near the stables of Forlaw, in Wildermore.Talk to Bert Bartleby in ForlawHumfrey Rumming set out from Forlaw heading west, and Bert Bartleby has asked you to track him down.Find Humfrey Rumming somewhere to the west of ForlawHumfrey Rumming caused a plague of Frozen Efts to emerge at the base of the mountains to the west of Dunfast.Defeat frozen efts to the west of Dunfast (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Humfrey Rumming is at the abandoned town of Dunfast, in Wildermore.Bert Bartleby is by the stables in the town of Forlaw, in Wildermore.

Humfrey took the money-purse given to you by Bert Bartleby and traded you some various sundries in return.Bingo is in the town of Forlaw, west of the Forlaw Feasting Hall.Talk to Bingo in the town of Forlaw'I think it is time to leave this snowy land behind us. I will be glad to do so, for it has put quite a chill in my bones! Brrrr!'

Bingo shivers dramatically, and suddenly cries out in surprise.

'Oh no! ${PLAYER}, I just remembered something I was supposed to tell you, but this cold weather drove it completely from my mind. My very thoughts must be frozen after all! You know that unusual Man who has been turning up everywhere on this journey of ours? Yes, Bert Bartleby, that is the very fellow! Well, he asked me to send you to see him about a small matter, but I forgot to pass on the message. You had better go to him at once, if it isn't too late! He's just to the south there, by the stables.''You know that unusual Man who has been turning up everywhere on this journey of ours? Yes, Bert Bartleby, that is the very fellow! Well, he asked me to send you to see him about a small matter, but I forgot to pass on the message. You had better go to him at once, if it isn't too late! He's just to the south there, by the stables.''Hullo there, my well-travelled friend! I am glad to finally be a part of this journey of yours and Bingo's, and I hope that I will not disappoint! I was quite an adventurer in the days of my youth, but those days are well past now. The merchant life suits me rather more than the questing life, these days!

'You must know what a merchant needs more than anything, surely? A cheerful disposition? Yes, that helps. Customers? Of course! A sense of humour? Well, that has never failed me, true. I am just going to tell you, ${PLAYER}.

'Goods for sale or trade! Without resupplying my stock of items, I would be a poor merchant indeed! My good friend Rattlepacks has a bit more room for goods, wouldn't you say?

'The folk of Forlaw have been murmuring about a lore-master that recently came to the town. Take this purse of coins and see if you can purchase some scrolls or tomes or potions from this lore-master, ${PLAYER}! I heard he was near the west gate of town.''The folk of Forlaw have been murmuring about a lore-master that recently came to the town. Take this purse of coins and see if you can purchase some scrolls or tomes or potions from this lore-master, ${PLAYER}! I heard he was near the west gate of town.''The folk of Forlaw have been murmuring about a lore-master that recently came to the town. Take this purse of coins and see if you can purchase some scrolls or tomes or potions from this lore-master, ${PLAYER}! I heard he was near the west gate of town.''How can I help you today, ${CLASS}?'

You tell the guard that you are seeking a lore-master that came recently to Forlaw. The guard snorts rudely.

'Aye, I've seen him. Bald fellow, big brown beard, simple cloth robe; a stout fellow. He's gone now, and I'm glad for it! He showed me some of his wares, junk mostly, and one of his blasted lizards bit my finger! See, it is still red. I never got an apology, either.''That lore-master is gone now, and I'm glad for it! He showed me some of his wares, junk mostly, and one of his blasted lizards bit my finger! See, it is still red. I never got an apology, either.''Wait a moment, my friend. Describe the missing lore-master for me once more, will you?'

You repeat the description you were told by the guard at the west gate, and Bert nods with familiarity.

'I know this man, ${PLAYER}! His name is Humfrey Rumming, and we go back a long way. You see, the Rummings and the Bartlebys were neighbours! Our homes were a bit out of the way, so weeks would go by and you'd only ever see a Rumming or a Bartleby! As a result, you might say that each family tended to wear on the other's patience.

'After I retired from the adventuring life, one of the first sales I ever conducted was to Humfrey Rumming: I sold him the key to the tumbled-down ruin of Tollobel on the island of Tyl Ruinen. I wonder if he ever used it?

'You'll have to find him and keep him out of trouble! The Rummings, and particularly Humfrey, have never been good at staying out of it. That guard watched him leave from the west gate? I expect he kept moving in that direction, then. Find Humfrey somewhere to the west of Forlaw. If there is a settlement in that direction, he is likely to have headed that way; like a curious moth to a torch at night is Humfrey, and all the Rummings!''That guard watched Humfrey leave from the west gate? I expect he kept moving in that direction, then. Find Humfrey somewhere to the west of Forlaw. If there is a settlement in that direction, he is likely to have headed that way; like a curious moth to a torch at night is Humfrey, and all the Rummings!''That lore-master went west from here, and good riddance! My finger still smarts from the bite his pet lizard gave to me.

'There is a town to the west. Could be that nuisance of a lore-master went there to trouble the people of Dunfast, if any remain.''Ah, you seem to be a fairly impressive adventurer. A ${CLASS}? I knew it! I myself am one of the world's greatest lore-masters. My name is Humfrey Rumming. You seem somewhat familiar, but since I never forget a face I will say that it is a pleasure to meet you for the very first time, ${CLASS}!

'I have a fondness for small lizards of all types, and I have a skill at nursing such creatures back to health. It is a gift I must share with the world! To that end, when I saw a clutch of eggs nestled at the base of a giant pillar of ice, I naturally warmed them up and allowed the little creatures to hatch! Naturally.

'Well, it turns out that these little efts do not take kindly to people, or to wildlife, or to plants... I think they may have scared off the people of this village, for no one is here anymore. You had better do something about those efts, ${CLASS}. They are among the woods to the west, by the base of the mountains. And please call them "efts," if you would. That is what all the great lore-masters call them.''You had better do something about those efts, ${CLASS}. They are among the woods to the west, by the base of the mountains. And please call them "efts," if you would. That is what all the great lore-masters call them.''Thank you for taking care of some of those efts. That may help encourage the people to return to this village. Either that, or the remaining efts may move in! Wouldn't that be something? Hmm... so what brought you out here anyway, ${CLASS}?'

You tell Humfrey that Bert Bartleby sent you with a coin-purse to find him and purchase some scrolls, tomes, or potions.

'Mister Bartleby! Well, imagine that. His family and mine were neighbours, you know. There was always such a racket coming from the Bartleby house. Growing up with fourteen boys will do that! How are Bert and his many, many brothers, anyway?

'I will take this purse from you and give to you these sundries. I am certain Bert will find something among them to his liking!''It was a pleasure doing business with you, ${CLASS}! I am certain Bert will find something among those sundries that is to his liking!''You found Humfrey and saved him from a predicament of his own making? That is very typical of a Rumming, ${PLAYER}, and is precisely what I expected! I see that you purchased some sundries from him. Let me see those.'

Bert looks through the small collection of goods.

'Ah, there is nothing here of note. It is a pity. Well, I will take the remaining coins from you, and... what? You gave him the entire money-purse? I expected some coins back, ${PLAYER}, especially for this bundle of nothing!

'Do not trouble yourself, my friend. I am not angry! Indeed, Humfrey may have gotten the better of me in this exchange, but many years ago I gave Humfrey a pittance in coin and he gave to me a creature that would become my dearest friend. I'd say we got the better of Mister Rumming in that exchange, wouldn't you, Rattlepacks?''Thank you for looking after Humfrey anyway, ${PLAYER}.''Were you able to help Bert with whatever he needed, ${PLAYER}? Good, I am glad!

'It is a marvel to me that we have seen Mister Bartleby at so many places on this journey, and he has not had much to say until this point. I wonder where he learned such patience? I have known one or two hobbits that were the same in my day, youngest siblings all! Perhaps Bert grew up with several older brothers, and learned patience at home. He is a unique individual, for certain!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Mistaken Identity' (Recommended Level: 90)</rgb>The Rashat-hai of the White Mountains fly crude banners marked only by skulls, and they flow menacingly in the dank air of Gurz-shâpol.You have discovered banners adorned with crude depictions of the Dead Men of Dunharrow.Burn Rashat-hai bannersRashat-hai banners can be found throughout Gurz-shâpol.

You have discovered banners adorned with crude depictions of the Dead Men of Dunharrow, and should burn them before the Orcs can fly them in Kingstead.Burn Rashat-hai banners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have burned many banners in Gurz-shâpol, and prevented the Orcs from flying their standards in Kingstead.CompletedAs you explore Gurz-shâpol, you notice grim standards depicting crude skulls and what appears to be a rendition of the Dead Men of Dunharrow. Even if their adornments are only meant to spook the young and old of Underharrow as they further their plans, these Orcs must never be allowed to fly their banners in the King's lands.You have burned many Rashat-hai banners in Gurz-shâpol, bringing a swift end to the Orcs' fool-hardy plan to disguise themselves as the Dead Men of Dunharrow.Éowyn has asked you to bring word to Ellen Fremedon of Harrowdale that the people of Edoras will soon be seeking shelter in her lands.The town of Entwade is to the north-east of Edoras.Travel to the town of Entwade, in Kingstead, and help its people with their troublesThe town of Underharrow is in Kingstead, to the south of Edoras.

Éowyn has asked you to bring the news to Ellen Fremedon in Harrowdale that the people of Edoras will soon be sheltering on her lands.After you have helped the people of Entwade, travel to the entrance of UnderharrowHorn is at the entrance to Underharrow, in Harrowdale.

Horn has come with you to Underharrow, and he wishes to speak with you by the entrance to that city.Talk to Horn at the entrance to UnderharrowEllen Fremedon is in Underharrow, in Harrowdale.Talk to Ellen FremedonOrdwald, the blacksmith of Underharrow, has a shop to the south of the Mead Hall.

Ellen Fremedon has asked you to carry a heavy barrel of swords from Ordwald's shop back to her.Pick up a barrel of swords from outside Ordwald's shopOrdwald, the blacksmith of Underharrow, has a shop to the south of the Mead Hall.

Ellen Fremedon has asked you to carry a heavy barrel of armour from Ordwald's shop back to her.Pick up a barrel of armour from outside Ordwald's shopOrdwald, the blacksmith of Underharrow, has a shop to the south of the Mead Hall.

Ellen Fremedon has asked you to carry a heavy barrel of spears from Ordwald's shop back to her.Pick up a barrel of spears from outside Ordwald's shopEllen Fremedon is outside the Mead Hall in Underharrow.Talk to Ellen Fremedon'My uncle has commanded that I bring the people of Edoras to Dunharrow, for that is a more defensible location than Edoras. Before we make our way there, we should send word to Harrowdale of the great number of people that soon will be arriving. Some have already made their way to Harrowdale, but more will be arriving soon, from all corners of Kingstead. If you have time enough to spare, I ask that you travel north-east to the town of Entwade, and ensure that the people there are prepared for what lies ahead. Afterwards, someone will need to travel to the Harrowdale.'

Éowyn pauses, and she seems to be weighing something carefully before speaking.

'The lord of Harrowdale is Thane Dúnhere, nephew of Erkenbrand, lord of Helm's Deep in the Westfold. He is a mighty man, accomplished in battle and most loyal to my uncle and the Mark, but... he rode to defend the Ford of the Isen some weeks ago, and his wife now rules in his stead. She and I have never been friendly. Ellen Fremedon is, by most accounts, a kind woman and as just as her husband, but she and I are as different as the badger is to the noble steed. She will not be pleased to hear that I and the folk of Edoras will soon be sheltering at her hall, but she would be less pleased to hear the news from me. After you have helped the people at Entwade, travel to Underharrow, to the south, and give Ellen Fremedon this unpleasant news. There is little other choice, and you seem to be the best available messenger for these tidings, ${PLAYER}.''The people of Entwade will need your assistance before they can come to Edoras. Travel north-east to their village and help them as much as you can.'

Éowyn pauses, her expression conflicted.

'The people of Entwade may be more willing to listen to you than will be the next woman with whom you speak. Ellen Fremedon and I have never agreed upon anything. After you deal with Entwade, find Ellen Fremedon in Underharrow and tell her what my uncle has commanded: that the people of Edoras find shelter in Dunharrow. She may think they have all arrived by now, but if so she is mistaken.

'I wish Thane Dúnhere had not gone to the Ford. He is more reasonable than his wife, if you ask me.''Ellen Fremedon and I have never agreed upon anything. Find her in Underharrow and tell her what my uncle has commanded: that the people of Edoras find shelter in Dunharrow. If she thinks all have arrived by now, she will not be happy to hear of her mistake.

'I wish Thane Dúnhere had not gone to the Ford. He is more reasonable than his wife, if you ask me.''We have arrived, ${PLAYER}. Now we must find Ellen Fremedon and hope that the tapestry of her that Éowyn wove for us was an exaggeration.

'I must confess that her distaste for the woman strikes me as rather strange. By all accounts, Ellen Fremedon has never spoken against the good of Rohan and has been nothing but loyal to the kingdom and to her husband Dúnhere. Indeed, she is a minstrel of no small skill herself! I heard her play the fiddle once at Eaworth several years ago, and she made it sing in a way I have never heard before nor since.

'Still, make no mistake. These are unwelcome tidings we bring. Even the most reasonable lady will not be pleased to hear the news that many scores of folk are seeking shelter in her hall.''Ellen Fremedon will not be pleased at the prospect of so many sheltering on her doorstep. Indeed, some of them have already begun to arrive.''Your tidings are over-late, bringer of ill news! Ever since early this morn, ragged folk have been pouring into Underharrow, bringing hungry mouths and sad tales! I will not close my gates to them, for the word of Théoden King is law and Dúnhere my husband would be wroth were I to disobey the Lord of the Mark. I will do my duty, but Éowyn cannot expect me to be pleased about sheltering so many.

'You, perhaps, can provide some help to me in return for this hospitality. The blacksmith Ordwald is forging weapons and armour by his home to the south. Go to him and carry the heavy barrels of swords, armour, and spears back to me.''Ordwald has been busy at his forge. Go to his shop, south of here, and bring back a barrel of the swords he has made. I have several others doing the same.''You are here for more weapons? The Lady of Harrowdale has had folk carrying off barrels of my work all morning. Take a barrel with my regards.''Ordwald has been busy at his forge. Go to his shop, south of here, and bring back a barrel of the armour he has made. I have several others doing the same.''You are here for another barrel of my work? If you can lift it, bring a barrel of my armour back to the Lady of Harrowdale. She has had folk carrying off barrels of my work all morning.''Ordwald has been busy at his forge. Go to his shop, south of here, and bring back a barrel of the spears he has made. I have several others doing the same.''You are here for more weapons? The Lady of Harrowdale has had folk carrying off barrels of my work all morning. Take another barrel with my regards.''You should return to the Lady of Harrowdale, and see what she thinks of the goods you delivered to her.''I see the question in your eyes, ${PLAYER}. Why have you toiled to bring these barrels here? Why does the sweat so sting your eyes? What good has it done to carry these weapons and armour to me, where they will stay?'

The Lady of Harrowdale's eyes are angry embers as she springs her trap.

'It has done no good, ${PLAYER}. By gift of birth Éowyn commands some little measure of respect, and I give it to her, but she has sent only trouble to Harrowdale. Do you see these swords and spears? Do you see this armour? She has sent to me townsfolk and children, the aged and the infirm. She has sent no warriors! She has sent no one to wield the bright blade, or brandish the sharp spear! These folk will die just as easily here as at Edoras. There is no purpose to this migration!

'If my husband falls, and those who ride with the King are defeated, there is no safety in all of Rohan. This is the cause of my reproach, ${PLAYER}. Éowyn plays at war, as she always has, but there are women in Rohan who truly know what is at stake. I am one of these.'Ellen Fremedon is displeased at the large numbers of people who now seek shelter in her lands, and worries about the dangers that lurk in Harrowdale.Horn is by the entrance to Underharrow.

Ellen Fremedon has made her displeasure clear to you, and has nothing else to say.Talk to Horn at the entrance to UnderharrowOrcs and Wargs can be found throughout Harrowdale.

Horn wants you to collect Orc sigils and Warg teeth from the invaders throughout Harrowdale.Collect Orc sigils in Harrowdale (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect Warg teeth in Harrowdale (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to Horn at the Dark Door, in DunharrowEllen Fremedon is outside the Mead Hall of Underharrow, in Kingstead.

You have collected Orc sigils and Warg teeth and should bring them to Ellen Fremedon to show her that she does not fight alone to defend her people.Horn is at the entrance to Underharrow, in Kingstead.

You have impressed Ellen Fremedon with your selfless actions on her behalf, and should let Horn know of the experience.Talk to Horn by the entrance to Underharrow'Enough words. I have spoken plainly, and have nothing more to say.''I have nothing more to say to you. There are too many people here, and on their way, to waste breath on trivial matters.''I did not think to find Ellen Fremedon so cold, ${PLAYER}. No one save the forces of Saruman are to blame for the unenviable situation in which we find ourselves, but I understand her frustration. We should aid her, if we can.

'I spoke with some of the folk of Underharrow while you were recovering equipment from the blacksmith, and they told me a group of Orcs and Wargs have threatened the peace of Dunharrow. The townsfolk have been used to these foes lurking in the mountains, but of late they have journeyed too near to the towns for comfort. Dealing with these creatures will allow us to send two messages, one no less important than the other. The first will serve as warning to the Orcs that Harrowdale is not theirs for the taking. The second message is for Ellen Fremedon, telling her that she does not stand alone. Her worries are ours, and she need not give up the hope of survival.

'Let us defeat Orcs and Wargs in Harrowdale, ${PLAYER}, collecting tokens from each, so we may send these two messages. I also wish to see the Dark Door of Dunharrow. I will meet you near it, for only few would speak of it and then only in whispers. It fills me with curiosity.''Yes? I am very busy and have no time for you.''My heart is heavy with dread, but so too is it filled with a sense of wonder. That is the Dark Door, ${PLAYER}, and beyond are the paths where the Dead are said to walk. You must know the story of Isildur and the Men of the Mountain, and better even than I, for you have journeyed with the Dúnedain. Aragorn is the first of that line I have met, and then only briefly. He strikes me as a most unusual man. We were not long in Edoras together, but I did mark him to be skilled with words. Perhaps in time of peace he could delight the ear with tales of his people, and may yet before all is done!

'I would ask for a different tale than that which concerns Isildur and the oath-breakers. Perhaps something with joy in it. For now, let us move away from the Dark Door. I am glad to have seen it, but I will be gladder still to leave it behind us!''Bring the items you collected to Ellen Fremedon. She may be surprised by us yet.''What are these? What have you brought me? Are these... teeth?'

Her expression changes from disgust to admiration as you recount your battles against the Orcs and Wargs in Harrowdale.

'I did not ask you to do this. I offered no reward. And yet still you campaigned against the creatures on my behalf? I do not know what to say, ${PLAYER}. After my husband Dúnhere rode to fight alongside his king, I believed no one would give a thought to the troubles of Harrowdale. And yet you did. Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I hope you did not take offence, earlier. I spoke from a place of despair, but you have given me reason to hope.

'If there are more ${CLASS}s like you in this land, perhaps Harrowdale will yet be made safe for the folk of Edoras, if the battle for Rohan ends not in victory.''I did not expect you to put yourself in danger for the good of Harrowdale, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps I have misjudged you.'Horn listens with amusement as you recount Ellen Fremedon's grateful acknowledgement of the help you provided to her people.

'Are you surprised by the Lady of Harrowdale's transformation? You should not be. There is no more certain way to attain the friendship of a ruler than by helping ease the problems of the ruled. We are, all of us, on the same side now. Only Saruman gains to profit if we squabble among ourselves.

'We have delivered the message, and we have somewhat eased its sting. It is time to move on to the next stage of our duty.'Ellen Fremedon is concerned by her daughter's jaunts into the nearby woods, fearing Dúnburg's enthusiasm for the blade will lead her to trouble.Dúnburg can be found to the south of Underharrow amidst the trees.

Ellen Fremedon has asked you to find Dúnburg and send her back to Underharrow.Find Dúnburg south of UnderharrowEllen Fremedon can be found outside the Mead Hall at Underharrow.

You have rescued Dúnburg and sent her on her way. You should return to Ellen Fremedon.Talk to Ellen Fremedon'With so much ado in Underharrow, my daughter has again slipped out of my sight!

'When will Dúnburg learn? To brazenly seek glory at her age is at best fool-hardy and at worst... I wish not to think of such things! I pray she turns to the mastery of music rather than that of the blade!

'Each time so far, I have had to drag her out of the trees to the south and sit her down within my view. She prattles on endlessly about the Dead, but I tire of such stories, especially since they might drive her into harm's way. If you would, search to the south of town, and please, send her home!''Did my mother send you?

'I cannot understand why she wishes for me to sit by in Underharrow while our people remain fearful of what lurks in the Harrowdale. I know she won't believe me, but you do, don't you?

'Wait! What is that rustling in the bushes?''Oh, thank goodness Dúnburg is safe!

'She should know better than this! No matter what she may see in Éowyn, a girl of her age does not belong on the battlefield, and I hope she realizes this soon. I know not if the life of a minstrel shall ever befit her, but the path of the shield-maiden is an arduous one, and she has much still to learn.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}.'Arcil and Éothain could not reach an agreement as to which Orc-tribe posed the greatest threat and left the decision in your hands.The Folde Farm can be found just north of Aldburg.

Thane Arcil has asked you to travel to the Folde Farm and warn them of the potential of Orc-attacks.Travel to the Folde FarmWitgil is somewhere at the Folde Farm.

Now that you have arrived at the Folde Farm, you should seek out and speak with Witgil.Talk to WitgilArcil is in the Mead Hall of Aldburg to the south of Folde Farm.

You have alerted the people at the Folde Farm about the Orc-threat and should now return to Arcil.Talk to Arcil'${PLAYER}, I must confess that I am glad you agree with me. I know that Éothain resents me because I am not his cousin Éomer, and he has every right to; however, I do not wish to lose the support of my people. They respect you, and your loyalty will encourage them.

'For now, we must alert the Folde Farm about the danger that these Orcs present. Please, go there now and warn them.''I had heard of a recent Orc-attack nearby, but I thought that it might have been an isolated incident and not something to concern myself with too much. This is very troubling news.

'I will alert everyone here; however, Athelbald is out collecting boar-meat. I hope that he makes it back safely.''Thank you for warning the Folde Farm. The food produced there is very important to Aldburg and needs to be safe-guarded.

'While I was content to stand guard here, I think it is time that we learn exactly what we are faced with. We need to find out truly how dangerous this threat is.'Arcil and Éothain could not reach an agreement as to which Orc-tribe posed the greatest threat and left the decision in your hands.The Folde Farm can be found just north of Aldburg.

Éothain has asked you to travel to the Folde Farm and warn them of the potential of Orc-attacks.Travel to the Folde FarmWitgil is somewhere at the Folde Farm.

Now that you have arrived at the Folde Farm, you should seek out and speak with Witgil.Talk to WitgilÉothain is near the entrance of Aldburg to the south of Folde Farm.

You have alerted the people at the Folde Farm about the Orc-threat and should now return to Éothain.Talk to Éothain'${PLAYER}, I must confess that I am glad you agree with me. Arcil is threatened by me because he sits as regent for my cousin, Éomer, and is but a shadow of the Reeve that he is.

