You should return to Herehasen and let him know that the Black Huorns will not threaten the Men of Rohan, and Blackthorn the Ent has pledged his allegiance to them.Talk to Herehasen at Tordag's camp'Little ${RACE}, I was already angered by the encroachment of the burárum...from my northern home, I saved a Man from his walled city when the Orcs destroyed it. Hoom, Béortnoth, I believe his name was.

'Now, I can see what evil Saruman has truly come to. Ta-rum! You may return to the Men of the lands beyond and tell them that Blackthorn of Fangorn shall fight! Look for my emissary at their village in the ruins...I will come if you send him.''Walking trees and Ents? Are you mad? No one would believe such a ridiculous tale.

'Ah well, perhaps I was wrong about the shadows in the trees....'Half-orcs are not the only servants of Saruman riding through the fields of the Broadacres. Goblin-skirmishers ravage the landscape, burning the grazing lands.You should defeat the goblin-skirmishers who scout the plains near Oserley.Defeat goblin-skirmishers near OserleyGoblin-skirmishers can be found in the fields west of Oserley.

You should defeat the goblin-skirmishers harassing Oserley.Defeat goblin-skirmishers near Oserley (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Half-orcs are not the only servants of Saruman riding through the fields of the Broadacres. Goblin-skirmishers ravage the landscape, burning the grazing lands. You should defeat the goblin-skirmishers who scout the plains near Oserley.You delivered the signet of Torsbury to Thane Tordag as Fríthild required, but you have found the people of Torsbury already routed from their town.Torferth is at Tordag's encampment north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

Tordag told you to lend your aid to his son, Torferth.Talk to Torferth in Tordag's encampmentServants of the White Hand can be found at Torsbury and in the fields south of Torsbury.

Torferth asked you to ride to Torsbury and the field to the south of the village and learn what kinds of forces are arrayed against his father's garrison.Torferth is at Tordag's encampment, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

You should return to Torferth with your report.Talk to Torferth at Tordag's encampment'Bah! Fríthild will have to wait. I have lost Torsbury! Nay, I am not disloyal, whatever it may seem. I just know Fríthild...she can hold fast without me. I should know! Look at my nose...she broke it herself, she did!

'We will attend to Stoke, do not worry! But first I must reclaim my town. I cannot bandy words any longer. My son, Torferth, is in charge of gathering information we can use to form our counterassault. Speak with him and help him as you may.

'The sooner Torsbury belongs to us, the sooner we can drive Saruman's lackeys back to Isengard!''I must ask your pardon for my father's harshness. He is a good man, but none too clever, and is embarrassed that he allowed Torsbury to fall. The Dunlendings came upon us silently in the night and had already put the town to the torch before anyone could sound the alarm. It seemed like they came from nearly every direction and without warning.

'Now he plans to retake Torsbury and has put me in charge of gathering information. I may be young -- only eighteen summers -- but I have seen my share of battles and know the habits of the Orcs.

'I need you to ride out to Torsbury and through the fields to the south to learn what kinds of forces are arrayed against us. With that information, we can lay the best plan for our counterassault.''It is as I feared. They have a great array of warriors...but your news is not all bad. It appears that most of the enemy army is comprised of wild Dunlendings -- with whom we have much experience in battle -- and half-orc raiders. If their only support is a small contingent of goblin Warg-riders, this should not be terribly difficult.'You reported the success of your duty at Ordlac's house and delivered the dagger you found to Dudsig.The brigand leader may be found in the marshes east of Oserley.

Dudsig has asked you to find and defeat the leader of the brigands who assaulted Lúfa.Defeat EldnothDudsig is near the stables in Oserley, west of the marshes.

You should return to Dudsig with news of your success.Talk to Dudsig in Oserley'This poison upon the blade of this dagger is secreted by the slugs that dwell in the marshes to the east. It is quite deadly and must be collected with much care. Perhaps that is where the leader of these brigands skulks?

'If so, he should be dealt with...perhaps if you are able to dispatch him, his followers will return to the Stonedeans and leave us in peace.''You survived the marshes? With all the poisonous slugs, I was not sure one unaccustomed to these lands would have come out alive.

'I must congratulate you!'The leaders of the half-orcs near Oserley are particularly dangerous, attacking without hesitance any who cross their paths.You should defeat the leaders of the half-orcs near Oserley.The leaders of the half-orcs near Oserley roam the field south-west of the village.

You should defeat Rakblog and Unaz, leaders of the half-orc scouts near Oserley.Defeat RakblogDefeat UnazCenulf is near the mill in Oserley.

You should bring the note you found to Cenulf at once.The leaders of the half-orcs near Oserley are particularly dangerous, attacking without hesitance any who cross their paths. You should defeat the leaders of the half-orcs near Oserley, hopefully sending the scouts into chaos.'You are mighty indeed to have faced and defeated the leaders of the half-orcs! Is there nothing you fear?

'You found a note upon one of the creatures? Let me see it....

'This is not good...not good at all....'After receiving your report regarding the forces arrayed against the Toringas, Thane Tordag has chosen to move forward with his plans to launch a counterassault against the Dunlendings who took control of Torsbury.Tordag is at his camp, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

Tordag has asked you to join his garrison as they assault the Dunlendings at Torsbury. You should speak with him when you are ready to join him.Aid Tordag and his people against the Dunlendings at TorsburyTorferth is at Tordag's camp, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

You should speak with Torferth to discuss the failure of the counterassault.Talk to Torferth at Tordag's camp'It is well, it seems. My people and my allies are prepared to stand with me and take back what belongs to Rohan!

'This day, we shall assault the Dunlendings at Torsbury and reclaim the town. Will you join my garrison, ${PLAYER}? You have proven yourself a talented ${CLASS} and would be a boon to our efforts.''Are you ready to leave? Good, then let us bring our wrath upon those barbarians!''${PLAYER}, how fare you after the battle? I knew that we moved too quickly against Torsbury. As well, we should have foreseen the treachery that befell my father. There is no honour among the half-bloods.

'I beg your pardon, ${PLAYER}, for it is not your doing, and I should not rail against you. My thanks for your services.'The note you found upon the leaders of the half-orcs seemed to distress Cenulf.Thane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Oserley.

Cenulf told you to bring the note you found to Thane Ordlac at once.'You should bring this note to Thane Ordlac at once. It contains information that he must see.

'Do not delay!''Greetings, ${PLAYER}! How goes your hunt? You found another note? Let me see it.

'Ha! These brutes think to murder my wife within my very home! These half-orcs were careless indeed to be carrying these orders upon their persons.

'Well, we shall take advantage of this, to be sure....'While scouting Torsbury, you discovered that the Dunlendings are holding several inhabitants captive.You should free the captive inhabitants of Torsbury.Captives can be found throughout the enemy camp at Torsbury.

You should free the captive inhabitants of Torsbury.Free BéowaldFree AswaldFree EthelfladFree CynegythFree AldúlfWhile scouting Torsbury, you discovered that the Dunlendings are holding several inhabitants captive. You should free the captive inhabitants of Torsbury.All you have done within the bounds of Dor-en-Ernil has made the folk who live here friendly to you.You must earn Friend standing with Dor-en-ErnilAll you have done within the bounds of Lebennin has made the folk who live here friendly to you.You must earn Friend standing with LebenninAll you have done within the bounds of Ringló Vale has made the folk who live here friendly to you.You must earn Friend standing with Ringló ValeYou discovered the badge of a great goblin-captain upon the corpse of one of your foes.You should hunt down and defeat the goblin-captain whose badge you recovered.Defeat NatulNatul roams the fields south of Torsbury.

You should hunt down and defeat the goblin-captain whose badge you recovered.Defeat NatulYou have tracked and slain Natul, a goblin-captain of the Broadacres.CompletedYou discovered the badge of a great goblin-captain upon the corpse of one of your foes. You should hunt down and defeat the goblin-captain whose badge you recovered.You have hunted down and slain the owner of the goblin-badge you discovered. The people of the Broadacres shall no longer fear his return.There are half-orc scouts ranging the fields south of Torsbury, threatening the farms and pasture-lands of the Broadacres.You should dispatch some of the half-orc scouts threatening the farms and fields of the Broadacres.Mounted half-orcs may be found throughout the fields south of Torsbury.

You should dispatch some of the half-orc scouts threatening the farms and fields of the Broadacres.Defeat half-orc warriors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat half-orc riders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})There are half-orc scouts ranging the fields south of Torsbury. You should dispatch some of them.You found a strange badge belonging to a powerful half-orc captain upon the corpse of one of his warriors.You should hunt down and defeat the captain of the half-orc riders in the western part of the Broadacres.Defeat EgurEgur may be found in the fields south of Torsbury.

You should hunt down and defeat the captain of the half-orc riders in the western part of the Broadacres.Defeat EgurYou have defeated Egur, the captain of the half-orc riders in the western part of the Broadacres.CompletedYou found a strange badge belonging to a powerful half-orc captain upon the corpse of one of his warriors. You should hunt down and defeat the captain of the half-orc riders in the western part of the Broadacres.You have defeated Egur, the captain of the half-orc riders in the western part of the Broadacres.While fighting the brigands at their camp near Oserley, you discovered a strange note upon one of their corpses.You should bring the note you found to Dudsig.Bring the note to DudsigDudsig is near the stable in Oserley, east of the brigands' camp.

You should bring the note you found to Dudsig.You should bring the note you found to Dudsig.'What is this? There are few who can read among the people of the Mark, especially among my folk...it is strange that one such as those robbers would bear such a thing.

'I do not like the tenor of this message. As you might have presumed already, these seem to be orders directing these brigands to stir trouble among us.

'Look here...it is signed with an "S." Could this be the work of Saruman?'Herehasen of Torsbury sent you into the eaves of Fangorn to uncover evidence of the evils he believes are gathering there.You have seen Black Huorns gathering in the shadows of the trees in Fangorn.

You should deal with them before they become uncontrollable.Deal with the Black Huorns before they become uncontrollableYou have seen Black Huorns gathering in the shadows of the trees in Fangorn.

You should deal with them before they become uncontrollable.Defeat Black Huorns in Fangorn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})With Huorns in the area, there may be an Ent nearby.

You should find the Ent who herds this part of Fangorn.You have seen Black Huorns gathering in the shadows of the trees in Fangorn.

You should deal with them before they become uncontrollable.'Hoom, now, little one,  I see that you have discovered the Black Huorns that have crept out of the heart of the forest. These are wilder than the wildest I have ever seen...they heed not my voice and threaten harm to all who live....'You have joined Tordag and his people for their counterassault against the Dunlendings at Torsbury.Speak with Tordag.Talk to TordagFight to the heart of Torsbury.Fight to the heart of TorsburyDefeat Bartub, Captain of the Uruk-hai at Torsbury.Defeat Bartub and his minionsSpeak with Torferth to learn his orders.Talk to Torferth'Let us go... before it is too late!''The wild men have allied themselves with foul creatures in the service of the Enemy... I do not believe there is any good in them, if ever there was.

'Let us put these dogs down!''We cannot win the day, ${PLAYER}. We must retreat and regroup at my father's camp!'As you travel across the plains of Rohan with Bingo, Egwylf, and Willem Whisker, a fierce storm has arisen and troubled your passage.You have been caught out in the storm with Bingo, Egwylf, and Willem.Talk to Bingo before the storm gets worseThe rain and the wind has become very intense, and Bingo wants to find a sheltered place to stay.Look for a place to shelter from the stormA Nazgûl has descended upon the plains of Rohan, and Bingo is in danger!Protect Bingo from the NazgûlEgwylf thinks you might be able to drive away the Nazgûl if you can set the grass afire.Pick up the torch from the ground where it has fallenThe Nazgûl hisses angrily. It does not wish to get close to the flames!Protect Egwylf and Willem from the NazgûlThe Nazgûl has fled.Talk to Egwylf'Let us search for Bingo before this storm gets any worse!''This storm sprang up most suddenly, ${PLAYER}! We should look for a place to take some shelter from the rain and the wind!

'I am quite nervous about this lightning, too!''We have to find Bingo, ${PLAYER}. That creature may come back!'Lúfa has retired for the evening, leaving you to stand guard against the anticipated attempt upon her life.Check the windows and doors to make sure they are secured.Secure the windows and doorDefend Lúfa against the White Hand's hirelings.Defend Lúfa against the White Hand's hirelingsCheck the corpse of the brigand leader for evidence.Check the corpse of the brigand leader for evidenceLúfa is at Ordlac's house in Oserley.

Speak with Lúfa and make sure that she is unharmed.Talk to Lúfa in Ordlac's house'You...you saved my life! How may I repay you?''Aye, I am unharmed...is that all of them? Are there any hiding in the shadows?'The people of Dor-en-Ernil have come to respect you as one of their own.You must earn Kindred standing with Dor-en-ErnilThe people of Lebennin have come to respect you as one of their own.You must earn Kindred standing with LebenninThe people of Ringló Vale have come to respect you as one of their own.You must earn Kindred standing with Ringló ValeBingo has had enough of Wildermore's cold heights, and is ready to return to the warmer plains of Rohan.Egwylf mapped a course out of Wildermore, to the south-west of Forlaw.

Bingo is ready to return to the warmer climate of Rohan, and asked Egwylf for the fastest route.Talk to Egwylf on the cliff-side road that runs into the Entwash ValeEgwylf asked you to <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> for signs of evil from the cliff-side path that runs from Wildermore to the Entwash Vale.Bingo is on the cliff-side path that leads between Wildermore and Entwash Vale.

You thought you might have seen a tiny shape high in the sky and far away, but now it is gone.Talk to Bingo on the cliff-side road that runs into the Entwash ValeThe town of Oserley is south-west of the road between Wildermore and the Entwash Vale.

Bingo and Egwylf want to travel next to the town of Oserley, in West Rohan. When you arrive, you should speak to the townspeople for information.Egwylf and Bingo are in the centre of the town of Oserley, in West Rohan.Talk to Egwylf in the centre of OserleyEgwylf and Bingo are in the centre of the town of Oserley, in West Rohan.Talk to Egwylf and leave Oserley by secret pathsEgwylf is in the town of Woodhurst, not far from the east gate.

Tanglemane escaped with Bingo from the attack of the Nazgûl, but now the two of them are lost somewhere in Rohan.Talk to Egwylf in the town of Woodhurst'I am ready to be warm again, ${PLAYER}! I spoke with Egwylf, and she recommended that we take the south-western road out of Wildermore into the Entwash Vale. Let us ride there at once, and hope for a thaw!''I spoke with Egwylf, and she recommended that we take the south-western road out of Wildermore into the Entwash Vale. Let us ride there at once, and hope for a thaw!''This is quite a view!''Wait a moment, ${PLAYER}. Did you feel that? For a moment I felt dread upon my heart, and sensed eyes upon our small party.

'The feeling has passed, but I do not trust that its absence means we are safe. <rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> from here and see if you spy anyone with evil intent.''For a moment I felt dread upon my heart, and sensed eyes upon our small party.

'The feeling has passed, but I do not trust that its absence means we are safe. <rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> from here and see if you spy anyone with evil intent.''This is quite a view!''Did you see anything? I thought I did, for a moment, but now there is nothing.''You saw something high in the sky and far away? Well, I don't see anything. Do you think it might be Landroval? He was quite large, I think, even for one of the Great Eagles, but from this distance even he might appear small. The eyesight of Eagles is supposed to be incredible, isn't it? I do think he would descend and say hello to us, if he recognized us.

'Egwylf told me there is a town to the south-west, across the river. The town is called Oserley, and from there we can explore West Rohan. Time to go, Tanglemane! Let's go, W.W.! Lead the way, ${PLAYER}!''Egwylf told me there is a town to the south-west, across the river. The town is called Oserley, and from there we can explore West Rohan. Time to go, Tanglemane! Let's go, W.W.! Lead the way, ${PLAYER}!''There is a town in West Rohan called Oserley. It is to the south-west, across the river. That is where we should go next.''We have come to Oserley. The people here whisper about strange things they have seen in recent days. You should ask them about the situation in West Rohan.''This is a difficult time for the people of Oserley. You might do well to set your course for elsewhere. What do I mean?

'Everyone here is on edge. Orcs have been seen on the plains of Rohan, burning and pillaging. Even the bravest warriors are seized by invisible terrors, reduced to shadows of their former selves.

'If you take my advice and leave Oserley and its troubles behind, you would be wise to leave at nightfall. The blanket of darkness may not shield you from evil eyes, but if it helps to protect the people that live here, I urge you to make use of it.''I just returned from my patrol, so I cannot tell you much of Oserley. But I can tell you that something is not right on the fields of Rohan. As I returned hither from the town of Stoke, a feeling of unease took hold of me. I saw nothing, but I would swear before my Thane that some creature watched me from afar.

'The stillness was broken by a terrible cry, bird-like and yet not so. I am glad to be back in Oserley, but the memory of that haunting cry stays with me. My blood freezes at the thought of it.

'It may have been nothing, but beware! We would all do well to remain alert when such evils are abroad in the Mark!''I agree with Éoric. We should not stay here. Let us leave beneath cover of darkness, and find a less dangerous place for Bingo to continue his journey. Speak to me when you are both ready, and we will leave Oserley by a goat-track or hidden foot-path. If we were spied upon as we descended from Wildermore, it would be best for us to slip the notice of any pursuers.''I have not often been on this side of things, ${PLAYER}. Usually I am the pursuer, not the pursued! Let us leave Oserley under cover of night, and give the slip to any of the villains who may be following us.

'I do not mean to frighten Bingo with our hasty departure, but for my part I am afraid it may be necessary. I do not like the dread that has taken hold of Rohan.''We survived the attack of that terrible creature, and I am certain that Bingo did as well. He is fortunate that Tanglemane had his pony-wits about him!

'We will find them, ${PLAYER}, have no fear of that! We will find Bingo, and then I will bid the two of you farewell. What has befallen the kingdom I love, that such frightening apparitions can haunt this land without opposition? I will not be able to journey with you for much longer. I will need to see to the hurts of my kingdom. But first we will find our friends.'

<rgb=#00FF00>After you complete this quest, Bert Bartleby will arrive near the east gate of Woodhurst, in the Stonedeans of West Rohan.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'A Horse in a Haystack' (Recommended Level: 92)</rgb>The people of Torsbury were driven out of their homes by an invasion of Dunlendings and half-orcs, among them an elderly warrior named Herehasen, rumoured among his folk to suffer from a madness of the mind.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest is designed for a raid of twelve players, though the players need not be grouped to complete it.</rgb>In the south-eastern part of the Fenmarch in the Eastfold dwells the Fastitocalon. In the north-western part of the Stonedeans dwells the Guardian of the Shrine. In the southern parts of the Westfold dwells a great and terrible dragon.

Herehasen believes that mythical creatures actually dwell in Rohan and wants you to defeat them to prove him sane.Defeat the Guardian of the ShrineDefeat the FastitocalonDefeat the Dragon of the WestfoldHerehasen is at Tordag's camp south-east of Torsbury in the Broadacres.

You should return to Herehasen and let him know that you found the legendary beasts exactly where he said they would be.Talk to Herehasen at Tordag's camp'Eh? What is that? Did you not hear what I said? Massive, it is! And I have seen it with my own eyes... well, at least the shadow of it. There are terrible creatures roaming this land!

'In the Eastfold, down in the farthest reaches of the Fenmarch, dwells the great Fastitocalon! A terrible beast... fishermen mistake it oft for a small island, and it devours them. In the north-west of the Stonedeans, there lies an ancient shrine built by the ancestors of the Dunlendings... it is rumoured that a demon guards it still, a creature with great long fangs. In the south of the Westfold, it is said there dwells a terrible dragon, if you can believe it!

'No one here believes me, but I know it is true! As you travel throughout fair Rohan, find these creatures and slay them, then no one will call me mad again!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest is designed for a raid of twelve players, though the players need not be grouped to complete it.</rgb>'Ha! Why will no one believe me? Just yesterday, I swear I saw a giant with one eye in the centre of its forehead... ghastly thing! Does anyone believe me?

'Of course not! Old Herehasen is mad, they say! Well, you have proved them wrong, to be sure!'The marshes east of Oserley are fraught with poisonous slugs, which might pose a threat to the people there.While you are hunting for the leader of the brigands, you might assist the people of Oserley by clearing the poisonous slugs out of the marshes.Defeat marsh-slugsMarsh-slugs may be found throughout the marshes, east of Oserley.

While you are hunting for the leader of the brigands, you might assist the people of Oserley by clearing the poisonous slugs out of the marshes.Defeat marsh-slugs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})While you are hunting for the leader of the brigands, you might assist the people of Oserley by clearing the poisonous slugs out of the marshes.The town of Stoke, seat of Reeve Fríthild, is under siege by the forces of Saruman. The town must be secured before they can send aid to Théoden, their king.Fríthild may be found within the Mead Hall of Stoke, within the town.

Húlac asked you to speak with Fríthild, his Reeve, and learn her wishes.Talk to Fríthild in the Mead Hall of StokeWooden planks can be found throughout the town of Stoke.

Fríthild asked you to gather wooden planks with which to repair the barricades at the town gates.Collect wooden planks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Damaged barricades can be found at the gates to the north-western, north-eastern, and southern parts of the town of Stoke.

Fríthild asked you to repair the barricades at the town gates, using the wooden planks you collected.Fríthild awaits your return in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should return to Fríthild and inform her that you have done as she asked.Talk to Fríthild at the Mead Hall of Stoke'I confess that I find it a joy to see strangers journeying through our lands! As you can see, Stoke is in dire need of assistance. You should speak at once with Fríthild, our Reeve... no doubt, she will welcome you as a friend.

'Fríthild is likely in council with her captains within the Mead Hall. You should go there at once!''You have come at an opportune time, stranger. It grieves me that I cannot yet send aid to Théoden King. I am a loyal subject, but I must consider my own people first. We have been under assault by Warg-riders for days. The grazing lands west of Stoke burn, and our town is overcrowded with refugees and livestock.

'If we can break this siege, there will be nothing to stop me from going to my king's aid. Please, help secure the gates of the town, while I consider the best course of action.

'You can find wooden planks throughout the town. Gather them and take them to the gates and repair the damaged barricades.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, you have returned. I believe I know what must be done, though I daresay I am concerned that it may not be possible....'The note you found implied that a traitor exists among Ordlac's retainers.Cenulf may be found near the mill in Oserley.

Thane Ordlac asked you to speak with Cenulf and obtain his guidance during your investigation of the brigands' note.Talk to Cenulf in OserleyMounted half-orc archers can be found throughout the fields west of Oserley.

Cenulf asked you to defeat mounted half-orc archers to discover more evidence of the traitor in Oserley.Defeat mounted half-orc archers near Oserley (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cenulf is near the mill in Oserley.

You should return to Cenulf with news of your victories against the half-orcs.Talk to Cenulf in Oserley'If indeed one of my retainers is a traitor, this could bode ill for any effort we make to bring aid to Stoke. I will continue my muster, but I must ask you to investigate this villainy.

'If you would, speak with Cenulf, one of my most trusted lieutenants. I have need of Dudsig's assistance, but Cenulf can guide you during your investigation.

'You may find him near the mill here in Oserley.''Dudsig told me of the assistance you have already wrought. I will welcome your aid now and grant you any information you need to find the traitor in our midst.

'If it is true that the orders were given by Saruman the White, then it may be that you can find more information among the half-orc scouts who harry our lands. They ride hither and thither among the plains to the west.''I confess to surprise that you live still, for the foul half-orcs are not without skill. Nevertheless, you have done some good for the Broadacres, even if you found naught upon their remains to implicate the traitor.

'I should say well done and offer you a reward.'Stoke is crowded with refugees from outlying farms and even bordering parts of Kingstead and the Stonedeans. Livestock and horses join the press. All the while, Reeve Fríthild struggles to secure her town against the White Hand's siege.Grimmar is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Fríthild told you to speak with Grimmar and obtain the signets with which to summon the Thanes of the Broadacres.Talk to Grimmar in the Mead Hall of StokeOserley lies along the road north-east from Stoke.

Fríthild has asked you to deliver a signet to Ordlac of Oserley, summoning him to her aid.Gain entrance to the Mead Hall of OserleyThane Ordlac is within the Mead Hall of Oserley.

Éoric afforded you access to the Mead Hall of Oserley to speak with Thane Ordlac.'${PLAYER}, I know what needs to be done, but I confess that I hesitate to do so. If we are to send aid to Théoden King, we must first break the siege upon Stoke...but to do that, we must muster the Thanes of the Broadacres, and I have no doubt that they, too, face the threat of assault.

'Nevertheless, it must be done. Speak with Grimmar, and he will provide you with signets to take to my thanes. Each signet bears the crest of their households, and they will know that it means we are in dire need of aid.

'Not far to the north-east lies Oserley, where dwells Ordlac. It is settled on an embankment leading down to the Entwash, so they may have not yet seen the smokes of the fires here. Ordlac is a pleasant fellow and likely will be the most ready to join our cause. You should go to him first.''So Reeve Fríthild has decided to summon the thanes? I recommended that she do so days ago, but I understand her concerns. It may well be that Ordlac and Tordag will be unable to come in time.

'Nevertheless, here are the signets of Oserley and Torsbury. Fríthild told you of Ordlac, but not of Tordag. The Thane of Torsbury is brave and a mighty warrior, but he is not the most clever man. It took much for him to accept Fríthild -- a woman -- as Reeve when her husband perished, but since she broke his nose in a brawl, he has been a faithful retainer.

'May fortune smile upon you as you deliver these tokens. Go to Oserley first.''Greetings, stranger! What news from beyond?

'What? Stoke is under siege? Aye, you must speak with Thane Ordlac at once! You have leave to enter the Mead Hall.''Welcome, stranger from afar! It is seldom that we greet travellers, even from other parts of Rohan. May I offer you some wine or ale? Indeed, I was about to sit down to a meal...would you join me?

'What is this? Ah! I did not think I would see this signet in my lifetime! My father granted it to Edmar, Fríthild's husband, to use in the event Stoke needed our aid.

'Tell me what has transpired that would bring Fríthild to such a need....'Your duties done at Oserley, it is time to continue your mission for Reeve Fríthild.Torsbury lies to the west of Oserley, near the border of the Stonedeans.

Ordlac bade you hurry by the straightest route to Torsbury to aid Tordag and bring him Fríthild's summons.Tordag is at his encampment just north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

Fríthild sent you to bring the signet of Torsbury to Tordag, summoning him to Stoke's aid.'My muster is complete. I have not a large force, but my garrison is prepared to march to Stoke's defence.

'I suggest that you make for Torsbury at once...they are likely to be in more dire straits than Oserley. I would take as straight a route as you may, for you will not want to come there too late.

'I shall send Dudsig with a handful of my finest warriors to assist at Torsbury, while I myself lead the remainder of my garrison to Stoke. May fortune go with you, ${PLAYER}!''Are you blind? Can you not see that we are in the midst of a war council? I have not the time to....

'What? The signet of Torsbury? That can only mean that Stoke is under siege. May Saruman the White burn forever in the fires he has kindled, curse his mangy hide!'The people of Torsbury were driven out of their homes by an invasion of Dunlendings and half-orcs, among them an elderly warrior named Herehasen, rumoured among his folk to suffer from a madness of the mind.Herehasen believes that there are strange and evil things dwelling beneath the eaves of Fangorn, north of Tordag's camp.

Herehasen wants you to hunt for the evils which dwell in Fangorn and bring proof of their existence so that the people of Torsbury will see that he is not mad.Find evidence of the evil things Herehasen spoke ofBlackthorn the Ent dwells in Fangorn, north of Torsbury.

You should speak with Blackthorn the Ent and learn what brings him to the eaves of Fangorn.Talk to Blackthorn the Ent in Fangorn'They move! I saw them! The trees....

'There are other things in Fangorn, as well...dark things with evil hearts. I have heard tell of winged creatures like women and walking skeletons. They are all gathering beneath the shadow of Mordor.

'No one believes me, of course. They all say I am mad. Go into that accursed wood and bring proof of the terrors within, and I will reward you as I may!''Hoom! You wander far afield. I am called Blackthorn in the tongue of Men.

'I have come here to see, hom, what evils the Orcs plan to bring to my corner of this forest.'You delivered your report to Thane Tordag, as Torferth requested, but the thane did not agree with his son's advice.Enemies of the Rohirrim can be found within the encampment at Torsbury, north-west of Tordag's encampment.

Thane Tordag wants you to test the strength of the enemy at Torsbury and patrol the village gates to see how well they are defended.Defeat enemies within Torsbury (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Scout the south-east gateScout the south-west gateTordag's encampment lies to the south-east of Torsbury, north of the road to Stoke.

You should return to Thane Tordag and let him know what you found.Talk to Thane Tordag at his encampment'I am more interested in knowing how strong the forces are at Torsbury itself. We can deal with the ranging foe later.

'I want you to enter Torsbury, if you are able, and test the strength of the enemy by defeating some of their warriors. While you are there, scout the gates to determine how well they are defended. You have but to follow the road north and west, and you will come upon the ruins of my town.

'Return to me when you have acquired a good sense of the situation there.''You say that my town is well-guarded against counterassault? Let us hope, then, that your efforts to weaken the enemy's defences there have been fruitful....'Lúfa told you her tale of woe, though she is glad of your company. Nevertheless, you must remain wary for danger.Lúfa is at Ordlac's house in Oserley.

You should speak with Lúfa when you are prepared to defend her against the servants of the White Hand.Talk to Lúfa when you are ready to defend herLúfa is at Ordlac's house in Oserley.

You should speak with Lúfa now that you have foiled the attempt upon her life.Talk to Lúfa in Ordlac's house'Do you think it true that the enemy shall attempt to take my life tonight? It would be good to know that you will remain with me, then.

'Will you stay and guard the door?''Will you check the door and windows after I retire? Perhaps if they are secured, you will have no need to risk your life on my behalf.''I cannot thank you enough, ${PLAYER}! Had you not come, I would have certainly perished!'Lady Lúfa had given you her favour to deliver to Torferth of Torsbury as a token of her faithful but forever unrequited love.When you come to Torsbury, you should deliver Lúfa's favour to Torferth.Bring Lúfa's favour to Torferth at TorsburyTorferth is in Torsbury, west of Oserley.

When you come to Torsbury, you should deliver Lúfa's favour to Torferth.Torferth is in Tordag's camp, west of Oserley.

You should deliver Lúfa's favour to Torferth.When you come to Torsbury, you should deliver Lúfa's favour to Torferth.'What is this you bring me? I recognize it! This belongs to Lúfa, daughter of Gísling...but did she not wed Ordlac of Oserley?

'You say that she did not wed him out of love? Alas, that we should be torn apart for politics and alliances. I will treasure this and swear if Ordlac does not bring her joy, he shall feel the taste of my steel....

'I am sorry...I should not speak so harshly before you.'You saved Lúfa from her would-be assassins, though how they came upon a key to Ordlac's house is yet a mystery. Perhaps the traitor is still within Oserley.Dudsig is near the stables in Oserley.

Lúfa bade you take the dagger you recovered to Dudsig and report that you were successful in your duty.'I cannot thank you enough...if you had not come, I would surely have perished. Perhaps I have misjudged Ordlac. I did not think him capable of caring for anyone other than himself, and even less capable of truly loving anyone, including himself, but perhaps I was wrong.

'Why would he send someone so bold and valiant to protect me if he did not have some spark of compassion within him? I do not -- and do not believe I ever will -- love him, but perhaps there is hope that my grief and loneliness may be lessened in the days to come.

'Return now to Dudsig and let him know that you have done your duty. The brigands are vanquished, and I am safe. He will want to see that dagger as well, I think.''What! Oh, you startled me! I thought I had seen a spirit!

'The brigands have been put down and Lady Lúfa is safe? That is well, for if she had perished we would likely have seen war with Garsfeld.

'Now let us see that dagger....'You found a note upon the corpse of one of the half-orc commanders detailing plans to murder Thane Ordlac's wife in their own home.Dudsig is near the stables in Oserley.

Ordlac has instructed you to obtain the key to his house from Dudsig.Talk to Dudsig and obtain the key to Ordlac's houseOrdlac's house is near the Oserley stable, on the road to the Mead Hall.

Dudsig gave you the key to Thane Ordlac's house and bade you fulfill your duty there.Talk to Lúfa at Ordlac's house'Ha! These oafs dare to consider the murder of my wife...and in my own home, no less? Well, we shall just have to make them rue their impudence.

'Speak with Dudsig; he shall give you the key to my home. Go to Lúfa and stay with her. When the villains come to perform their heinous act, you shall dispatch them.

'Nay, do not look at me so! Lúfa is the daughter of Gísling and granddaughter of Gísil, Thane of Garsfeld. My marriage to her sealed the alliance between our houses...how do you think it would look if I were to let her perish at the hands of ruffians? Nay, I doubt not your talents, my friend!''Aye, indeed I have the key, and here it is. It is a brilliant plan, I think, that my thane has considered. How best to deal with the situation than to have you confront the villains in his own home, where you may better protect Lady Lúfa?

'Thane Ordlac's home is just up the road leading to the Mead Hall, if you have not already taken note of it.''Who are you? Ah, my husband sent you. Is he afraid that his trophy might be lost?

'Please...do not mind my words. They are borne of sorrow. I should be glad of some company.'Thane Ordlac sent you to his house to protect his young wife Lúfa from the would-be assassins which the half-orcs intend to use against her.The wine is on the table in Thane Ordlac's house.

Lúfa has asked you to bring her a cup of wine to ease her thirst while she tells you her tale.Collect wine from the tableLúfa is at Thane Ordlac's house in Oserley.

Lúfa awaits the wine she requested from you.Lúfa is at Thane Ordlac's house in Oserley.

Lúfa has finished her tale and wishes to speak with you again.Talk to Lúfa in Ordlac's house'You wonder why I grieve so? I am recently wed to Thane Ordlac, but here I have not found love. Alas, I must call his home mine, yet I do not recall that he has ever come to this house since we were married.

'Truly, even did he show his love to me, I do not think I could be consoled, for my heart does not care for him as it should.

'Would you bring me a cup of wine from the table? If you are willing to hear my tale, then I shall need the refreshment to soothe my thirst.''Thank you. You have shown me a kindness.

'If you wish to know why I grieve so as the wife of such a young and handsome thane, it is because my heart belongs to another. I am only wed to Ordlac because my father desired an alliance with Oserley.

'I do not think my father would have promised me to Ordlac if he knew the lonely life I would lead here, but it is done now....''Now do you understand why I grieve? I will abide by my father's wishes, for I love him and would not be disobedient, even for my own sake, but I begin to feel like I will become old from sorrow and loneliness before my time.

'I do have one request to ask of you though: if you continue to Torsbury as you say you must, would you bring this, my favour, to Torferth? He thinks me unfaithful, and I wish him to know that my heart still belongs to him, even though we may never fulfill our hearts' desires.'Lady Answith, the Thane's wife, knows that he can be unpleasant, but only because he allows himself to be consumed by duty and forgets the little things in life that he enjoys.The falls of Everholt lie to the south-west of Beaconwatch.

Lady Answith wants to go to the falls in Everholt and collect several stones that would be suitable for skipping.Collect skipping stones (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lady Answith is on a porch above the vendor's area north of the Mead Hall in Beaconwatch.

You have collected skipping stones at the Everholt Falls and should now bring them back to Lady Answith.Talk to Lady Answith'So you have met my husband, Cútha? I know that he can be a bit unpleasant at times, but you should understand that he is doing what he feels is right.

'Sometimes he needs a gentle reminder to enjoy the little things in life. One of his favourite things to do is skip stones along the Mering-stream. Would you collect some stones that would be suitable for skipping?

'There is a waterfall in Everholt, south-west of Beaconwatch that always has the best stones. I would go myself, but with all the trouble lately, I confess I do not wish to leave this area.''Ah, ${PLAYER}. These stones are perfect for skipping. I imagine that you have skipped a rock or two in your lifetime, have you not?

'I will put these into Cútha's pocket sometime, and he will have a nice surprise when he finds them.'Maddened marsh-crows from the Wódfen have crossed the Entwash into the marshes east of Oserley, attacking everything they see.You should drive off the draug-maddened marsh-crows from the marshes.Defeat marsh-crowsMaddened marsh-crows can be found in the marshes, east of Oserley.

You should drive off the draug-maddened marsh-crows from the marshes.Defeat marsh-crows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Maddened marsh-crows from the Wódfen have crossed the Entwash into the marshes east of Oserley, attacking everything they see. You should drive off the draug-maddened marsh-crows from the marshes.With the defeat at Torsbury and the death of Thane Tordag, Torferth and his people regroup at Tordag's camp.The captains of the Toringas and their allies may be found at Tordag's camp, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

Torferth has asked you to confront the captains of the Toringas and their allies and obtain their permission to search their belongings for evidence of treason.Talk to DudsigTalk to WilsigTalk to TidricTalk to SidnothThe belongings of the Toringas and their allies can be found throughout Tordag's camp, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

Thane Torferth asked you to search the belongings of the Toringas and their allies for evidence of another traitor.Thane Torferth is in Tordag's camp, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

You should bring the evidence you found to Torferth for his review.'${PLAYER}, you find me in a terrible state. I wish to grieve my loss, yet I am now Thane and cannot afford that privilege. I suspect that there is more behind my father's murder than a single traitorous coward. From what I have learned, Frithmund was not a clever man...it is doubtful that he crafted the trap himself.

'I confess I have become suspicious of everyone around me, but you. You are new to Torsbury, and the one who masterminded this plan had to already know somewhat of my father's ways.

'Will you speak with my captains and our allies? You may learn something from them that they might not be willing to share with me. I would also have you obtain their permission to search their belongings...it is the nature of evil to unwittingly leave signs of its actions.''Bah! Does the boy's hatred of all who do not bear the pure blood of Rohan run so deep that he would cast aspersions upon us all? It is an insult, not only to me, but to my thane who sent me! We came to aid Torsbury, and now he seeks hostilities?

'Nevertheless, Torferth is now Thane of Torsbury, and these are his forsaken lands. I will submit...but you may inform him that I will be bringing my grievances to Thane Ordlac and Reeve Fríthild.

'I have brought no possessions of my own, save what you see upon my person, but you have my leave -- with objection -- to search my tent and those of my men.''Thane Tordag sheltered my folk when the Dunlendings came to Woodhurst, despite the counsel of Tidric and his own reservations regarding any who are not pure of blood...and yet it was one of my folk who betrayed him.

'I can understand the young thane's suspicions, and we will willingly consent to whatever questions or searches he deems necessary to allay his suspicions.

'Alas, I cannot say much more about Frithmund. I confess that he was little more than a casual acquaintance in Woodhurst, and I did not know him well.''I have ever been a faithful servant to Thane Tordag, and it is an insult to be treated with the same suspicion as the half-blood rabble! However, I cannot fault Torferth for his blind suspicion...he has just lost his father and become Thane of Torsbury in one evil stroke.

'My men and I will submit to whatever searches and inquiries Thane Torferth demands of us...and we will be found innocent, one and all.

'Alas, I warned Thane Tordag not to admit the folk from Woodhurst. The half-bloods cannot be trusted. You should look more closely into their number...or even that captain from Oserley.''The people of Torsbury loved their thane...I can think of none among us who would even consider betraying him! It was one of those Woodhurst mongrels what killed him...you should be questioning them instead.

'We are Thane Torferth's people and will submit to his will, but it is an insult! We have fought and bled with him.

'Do what you must and leave me be.''Alas, I know not what to make of these things.

'The knife could easily belong to any of the folk from Woodhurst, for many of them bear the blood of both Rohan and Dunland. The note can be read many ways; some suspicious, some not. The Falcon-clan badge is incriminating, but could just as likely be a trophy from the battle. The necklace could be just that, though its design is not familiar to me.

'The letter piques my suspicion the most, for it is signed with an "S" which could stand for Saruman and reads strangely, but it is also in a hand not well-trained in letters. I will have to think upon these things.'The people of Torsbury are shattered by the loss of their Thane, and Torferth is consumed with discovering the mastermind of his father's murder.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke, south-east of Tordag's camp.

Torferth instructed you to return to Reeve Fríthild and let her know that the Torsbury garrison will be late coming.Talk to Reeve Fríthild in the Mead Hall of Stoke'Reeve Fríthild was wise in choosing you to bring her summons. Alas, however, my people cannot come yet to Stoke. We are worn from our battle and from the grief of loss, but as well I must learn who the true mind behind my father's death is.

'You should return to Stoke at once. The Reeve will need your doughty arm in the time ahead. Let her know that we will come as soon as we may.

'I must thank you again, as well, for bringing me Lúfa's favour. My heart remains broken, for I know we shall never meet again under favourable circumstances, but the blow is softened somewhat to know that her heart remains mine.''Welcome back, ${PLAYER}!

'You say that Tordag is dead, slain by treason, and the Toringas shall come late to our defence? That is ill news, indeed, but at least we have the consolation that Ordlac has brought nearly his full garrison in good time!'You patrolled the fields to the south of Torsbury as Torferth had requested and discovered a wide array of enemies.Thane Tordag is at his encampment, north of the road between Torsbury and Stoke.

Torferth instructed you to bring your report to his father, Thane Tordag.Talk to Thane Tordag in his encampment'The news you brought me is grim, but I think there are measures we can take to even the battlefield a little. Share your report with my father and advise him to send small forays against the enemy. It may be that we can weaken their defences before initiating a full counterassault on Torsbury.''So, Torferth believes we should make small forays against the enemy to weaken their defences? While I can certainly see the value in such things, I believe a more direct approach would be better...Dudsig here agrees. He arrived shortly after you did.

'I have a task that I think better suits your talents.'The Dunleading war-leader Cledwun roams the plains of the Broadacres with his guard.The Dunleading war-leader Cledwun roams the plains of the Broadacres with his guard.Defeat CledwunThe Dunleading war-leader Cledwun roams the plains of the Broadacres with his guard.

You should defeat Cledwun and his guard, bringing some peace to the Broadacres.Defeat CledwunYou have defeated Cledwun, earning the gratitude of the people of Stoke.CompletedThe Dunleading war-leader Cledwun roams the plains of the Broadacres with his guard.You have defeated Cledwun, earning the gratitude of the people of Stoke.The Dunleading war-leader Cledwun has launched an attack against the town of Stoke in the Broadacres.The Dunleading war-leader Cledwun has launched an attack against the town of Stoke in the Broadacres.Defeat the attack on StokeCledwun's forces are attacking the western side of Stoke.

You should help defeat the attack on Stoke.Defeat the attack on StokeYou have defeated Cledwun's attack force, earning the gratitude of the people of Stoke.CompletedThe Dunleading war-leader Cledwun has launched an attack against the town of Stoke in the Broadacres.You have defeated Cledwun's attack force, earning the gratitude of the people of Stoke.While patrolling the western fields of the Broadacres for Torferth, you discovered many goblin-spearmen burning and pillaging.You should drive off the goblin-spearmen burning and pillaging in the Broadacres.Defeat goblin-spearmenGoblin-spearmen may be found throughout the fields south of Torsbury.

You should drive off the goblin-spearmen burning and pillaging in the Broadacres.Defeat goblin-spearmen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})While patrolling the western fields of the Broadacres for Torferth, you discovered many goblin-spearmen burning and pillaging. You should drive off the goblin-spearmen burning and pillaging in the Broadacres.Guard-captain Thurferth has not received funding from Woodhurst's leadership. His plea for assistance to the advisor responsible for the funds has fallen on deaf ears.Wild beasts roam the lands around Woodhurst.

Guard-captain Thurferth asked that you collect hides, bear-claws, goat-hooves, and venison to supply the garrison.Collect hides (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bear-claws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect goat-hooves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect venison (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return the supplies to Thurferth, near the centre of Woodhurst.

Guard Captain Thurferth will be thankful for these supplies.Talk to Guard-captain ThurferthHerubrand is in the cellar of the farmhouse.

You should bring the ill news of the mismanagement of the city's monies, as it may help explain why the situation in Woodhurst is growing worse.Talk to HerubrandElda has not been seen since the council-meeting, but he should be somewhere in Woodhurst.

You need to speak with Elda about the funding for the guards that has not been delivered.Talk to EldaHerubrand is in the cellar of the farmhouse.

Herubrand should know what Elda had to say about the funds for the city guards.Talk to HerubrandGuard-captain Thurferth is near the centre of the city.

You should return to Guard-captain Thurferth and inform him of the misplacement of funding that was intended for him.Talk to Guard-captain Thurferth'The guards have not received monies for some time now, and I can barely pay the men. I cannot purchase arms or supplies! Why is it that Reeve Heremond thinks a city can be protected with nothing? I have brought this to his advisors and was dismissed.

'Perhaps you can help. My men are busy with their duties to Woodhurst, but I could use someone to gather supplies from the wildlife around the city. I need hides, bear-claws, goat-hooves, and venison.''${PLAYER}, you have my thanks. Collecting these supplies was no small task, but I am afraid that I have not much to repay you with.

'I still find it difficult to believe that the Reeve would not set aside funding for the city guard. You seem to be in good standing with his advisors. Could you speak with them for me to determine why we are receiving so little support?''That is not possible. I know for certain that my father set aside a considerable portion of the city's monies to fund the guard. It is vital for any city.

'Where did the money go if not to the guards? Elda has been involved in the planning for some time, and perhaps he knows what happened. Please find him and speak with him about this.''Begone, I have nothing to say to you. Are you accusing me of thievery? You have no evidence and are fortunate that I do not dispose of you right now.

'I was instructed to fund the city's protection. I diverted funding to those who could actually protect us. The guards do naught, and I am not afraid to say it. Tell him what you want. I do not fear him... or you.''Elda said that he diverted monies elsewhere? But who else is protecting the city, if not the city guard? My father trusted this man and paid for it with his life....

'He either kept that money and is a thief, or he gave it to someone, as he said, and is a traitor.''The advisor responsible for funding the protection of the city put those funds elsewhere? He should hope that I never see him again. However, if anyone is truly to blame, it is Reeve Heremond for entrusting someone like that to handle such important affairs.

'You have my thanks for uncovering this plot.'Oter and Stithwulf will soon leave the Stonedeans. They seek a better life elsewhere in Rohan where they might find a warmer welcome...if such a place exists.Chicory can be found to the south of where Oter and Stithwulf are camped, in the shade of trees.

Stithwulf asked you to gather chicory for their supplies.Collect chicory (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Oter's waggon is at his camp to the north of the chicory patches.

Oter asked for any chicory you find to be placed in the crate on his waggon.Place the chicory in the crateOter is at his camp, north of the chicory patches.

Oter would like to thank you for helping him bolster their supply of food and medicine before he and Stithwulf leave.Talk to Oter'Stithwulf and I are glad to put the Stonedeans behind us. It is hard to leave, but it is not safe for us anymore. We left swiftly and only have a few supplies with us. We could really use more chicory, and fortunately, chicory grows around these parts. It would be a good addition to our supplies.

'You might find chicory growing in the shade of the trees to the south. Place any you collect in the crate on the waggon.''${PLAYER}, thank you for your help in gathering chicory. Speaking of which, I should start preparing the stew for tonight with a few of the fresh herbs you brought me.

'We are likely to depart in the next day or two. Perhaps tomorrow is the day we take our leave.'Ordlac sent you to put an end to the treacherous brigands who murdered Reeve Fríthild, but upon returning to Stoke, you found Lúfa, Ordlac's wife, awaiting you at the gate.Thane Torferth is at Tordag's encampment, north-west of Stoke.

Lúfa bade you seek out the aid of young Thane Torferth of Torsbury.'${PLAYER}, I have seen the token in my husband's hands many times...he used to handle it when he was anxious about something. If you found it upon one of the brigands, it can only mean one thing: Ordlac is the traitor for which you had been searching at Oserley...and the one who ordered my death!

'Do not return to Ordlac, ${PLAYER}, for I fear it would be your death. Go instead to Torferth at Torsbury and tell him what transpires. He must bring his garrison to Stoke's defence at once!''What is this? I know this token well...it was a gift of friendship from my father to Ordlac's. Now all of the pieces of evidence you brought me make sense!

'I should have seen it...Ordlac is the traitor who brought ruin to my people! Lúfa was right to send you to me. I think it likely that Ordlac will soon move against young Reeve Fríthbert. We must return to Stoke and put an end to his treachery!'When you returned from speaking with Alfbert at the inn, Thane Ordlac revealed that Engifu, the children's governess, had apparently thrown herself to her death in grief.Winegar is at the inn in Stoke.

Thane Ordlac informed you that Winegar, Reeve Fríthild's physician, asked to speak with you privately.Talk to WinegarAlfbert is at the inn in Stoke.

Perhaps Alfbert saw the stranger Winegar mentioned. You should speak with him.Talk to AlfbertThe stranger, Grimlac, was seen headed out the north-east gate of Stoke on horseback.

Alfbert directed you to follow after Grimlac.Find GrimlacYou should search Grimlac's body for evidence of foul play to bring to Thane Ordlac.Search Grimlac's bodyThane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should bring the poison you found to Thane Ordlac.'As well, Fríthild's physician, Winegar, has asked to speak with you at the inn. He seemed quite distraught, but would not tell me what concerned him...I was forced to remove him from his service, but I suppose it is understandable that he does not yet trust me.

'When you learn what he has to say, return to me, so that I may decide how best to treat with him.''Are you ${PLAYER}, the one who aided Reeve Fríthild? That is well. I wished for you to know that I inspected the wine cup from which the Reeve drank at the celebration...and found a small amount of poison therein.

'I did not wish to speak of this with Thane Ordlac, for he has been quick to dismiss the thoughts, feelings, and opinions of all around him. Perhaps you could bring him proof of this treachery?

'I have heard rumour that there have been a few strangers in town recently...perhaps one of these brought the poison?''Indeed, I did see a stranger in town recently. He was one of those half-bloods. At first, I thought he was one of Thane Ordlac's retinue -- perhaps a kinsman of Dudsig -- but he left quickly, shortly before the celebration feast.

'He departed out the north-east gate on horseback...you might be able to find him yet, if you hurry!''What do you want with me?''Why, this is the same poison that the man who tried to murder my wife used! I am truly sorry that I did not consider that Fríthild's death might have been murder!

'These treacherous brigands must be dealt with at once. We shall not brook villainy!'Alongside Thane Torferth of Torsbury, you freed Stoke from Ordlac's control and saved Reeve Fríthild's children from a terrible fate.Lúfa, Edhild, and Reeve Fríthbert are in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Thane Torferth told you that Lúfa and the children wished to say their farewells to you before you depart.Talk to LúfaTalk to Reeve FríthbertTalk to EdhildThane Torferth is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Torferth again.Talk to TorferthA herald from the Stonedeans arrived in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with the herald from the Stonedeans.Talk to LéoflacWoodhurst lies to the north-west of Stoke, in the Stonedeans.

You should find someone near Woodhurst who might give you some insight into the situation in the Stonedeans.Find someone near Woodhurst who might have some insight into the Stonedeans' travails'${PLAYER}...we cannot thank you enough for your aid. If you had not come to us, Ordlac might have usurped the leadership of the Broadacres and left an even bloodier path of destruction than he did.

'The young Reeve has appointed me, as Thane of both Torsbury and now Oserley, his regent to lead in his stead until he is come of age. Lúfa and I shall be wed, and together we will maintain the alliance her father had hoped for.

'Alas, the Dunlendings and their vile allies still threaten the Acres...we must secure the Riding before we can send a muster to Théoden King's aid. Before you leave, I know Lúfa and the children also wish to speak with you.''Greetings, ${PLAYER}. I cannot thank you enough for the aid you have given us. I knew in my heart that there was something dark within Ordlac's heart...indeed, he knew not how to love anything but power.

'I have sent word to my father of the events which have transpired here. I doubt not that he will now bless my marriage to Torferth, since he has the favour of Reeve Fríthbert.

'Go with honour, my friend!''Ah, ${PLAYER}! You have shown more kindness than we need expect from a stranger to the Acres...how can we repay you? Name it, and I will have Torferth grant it!

'Well, I suppose until I come of age, I do not have the authority to do so. I hope he rewarded you well enough for your aid.

'Thank you for your friendship! I will bear the arms you gave me with pride.''You are such a kind ${CLASS}...must you go so soon? There are enemies everywhere, and we still need your help.

'I suppose Théoden King needs your help too, so you must go. Do not worry for us. I am not scared anymore...I am going to become a shield-maiden, just like my mother.

'The Riddermark will remember Edhild, daughter of Fríthild!''Once again, I say thanks....

'What is this disturbance at the door?''We are in great need of aid! The Mead Hall of Woodhurst was attacked, and we know not the state of things within...the village council will not allow anyone inside.

'Dunlendings and their allies flood into the Stonedeans and threaten our very existence. There are many in Woodhurst who share the blood of both the Riddermark and of Dunland...some side with the White Hand and the Dunlendings, while others choose to remain neutral. Very few remain loyal to Rohan.

'Will you go to Woodhurst and lend your aid?''Hail! What brings you into this part of the Mark? It is a dangerous road you travel, but you are welcome to share our fire for a time....'Oter and Stithwulf will soon be leaving the Stonedeans. They camp along the road in relative safety while deciding where next to go and prepare by gathering supplies before they depart.Bears are hunting in the Roaring Hills to the south of the road where Oter and Stithwulf are camped.

Stithwulf asked you to hunt the bears and bring back any unblemished hides that you are able to collect.Defeat bears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bear-hides (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Stithwulf and Oter's camp lies to the north of the Roaring Hills.

Stithwulf asked you to bring any intact hides back to his camp to help bolster supplies for the long road.Place the bear-hides on the waggonStithwulf is at his camp in the Stonedeans, north of the Roaring Hills.

Stithwulf would like to thank you for ensuring the safety of their camp and returning with hides.Return to Stithwulf'We left Woodhurst and we will not look back. I expected someone to follow us, but my scouting has only revealed tracks of bears...many of them. The Roaring Hills to the south appears to be a favoured hunting ground. However, I will gladly deal with bears over backstabbing Deansmen!

'Bears can be dangerous, though, and these were not small. One might ravage our waggon or hurt us badly if it got too close. Also, we could always use a few extra hides. Could I ask you to keep them away from our camp and gather any hides you leave intact? We could always use them for blankets or trade.''My good ${RACE}, you returned with some quality hides. Those were large bears, if those hides are any evidence.

'I may keep the largest among them for a bear-rug in my new home, if we ever make it there....'The Council of Stoke determined that Reeve Fríthild would take to the field and lead her people against their aggressors, but now it must chose what front to focus upon.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should bring Thane Ordlac's recommendation to Reeve Fríthild's attention.Bring Thane Ordlac's recommendation to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Reeve Fríthild regarding the recent battle at Bardh-dorám.Talk to Reeve Fríthild'It is clear, my friend, that the foe shall not be dismayed...but all the better, for we may yet gain great glory in battle! If we wish to rout the foe, we must strike at their heart!

'Let us make an assault upon their encampment to the west, Bardh-dorám. The captains you noted there are likely the true leaders of this force. If we can lay them low, the invasion will at the very least be hindered from advancing until they can send more captains.''Indeed, ${PLAYER}, I am through with these invaders. Let us do as Ordlac suggests...these servants of evil shall know the wrath of Stoke!

'Let us away at once!''The battle was well-fought, and the foe felt our wrath...yet I am still not convinced of our victory. I am troubled, ${PLAYER}.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: Due to phasing conditions, to continue the Broadacres storyline, you must have completed Epic Volume III, Book 12, Chapter 8.</rgb>You have made good on your promise to Éomer and have provided for the continued maintenance of Aldburg. Now you should see how Éowyn's work has progressed.Horn will speak to you again by the barrows outside Edoras, as he did once before.Talk to Horn by the barrows outside Edoras, in KingsteadHorn is by the barrows outside Edoras, in Kingstead.

Horn has asked you to tell him when you are ready to walk through the streets of Edoras with him and your companions.Talk to Horn by the barrows outside Edoras, and walk with him through the streetsÉowyn is outside Meduseld, in Edoras.

You have learned that an ally of Wormtongue's attempted to sabotage the evacuation efforts, but may have fled the city.Talk to Éowyn outside Meduseld, in Edoras'I cannot thank you enough for all you have done. You have aided Aldburg and its people in ways I was unable to do myself, but I should not be surprised at your ability. After all, Éomer did send you to assist me! Éomer possesses wisdom far beyond his years, and he uses it not just on the field of battle but in times of peace, as well!

'There have been fewer of those in recent memory, alas.

'You return now to Edoras? Go with my good wishes, and may you help the folk there as you helped the people of Aldburg!''You have my thanks for all you have done. Now you and your companions travel to Edoras? Give my regards to Éowyn, and tell her that I am doing what I can to maintain her brother's home.''We have come once again to Edoras, this time in the company of both Corudan and Nona. Are you ready to walk through the streets and speak once more to Éowyn? Let me know if you are prepared. Allies of Wormtongue could be anywhere, and we must remain alert.''Are you ready? Let us remain alert. Allies of Gríma Wormtongue could be anywhere. Do not think that we cleansed the city of them when last we were here!''Éowyn waits for us outside Meduseld. We cannot abandon her to her task without aid!''A picture begins to shape itself, ${PLAYER}. The writings you recovered from Gríma's chambers spoke in vague and shrouded terms, in order to keep the identities of his allies a secret, but we have found some few of them already. Hebeca, Léofing, and Forthere have been defeated or imprisoned for their part in Gríma's schemes, but it seems there was a fourth conspirator. It seems he used several names, among them Ednoth and Góding. There may have been more that we know not.

'But now we have a common thread, ${PLAYER}. Our mysterious foe seems overly fond of a blood-red brooch, and it is by this jewellery you will know him. We cannot risk his return! Only by tracking this secret enemy can we ensure he does not return to cause deadlier mischief!'A deadly ally of Gríma Wormtongue's seeks to escape capture, and his trail leads into the Broadacres.The town of Stoke is located north of Edoras, in the Broadacres.

You have given Éowyn your word that you will travel to Stoke to find the ally of Wormtongue that fled the city. He wears a blood-red brooch, but otherwise his appearance remains unknown to you.Travel to the entrance of Stoke, in the BroadacresCorudan has called a halt just outside the entrance to the town of Stoke.

You should speak to Corudan and ask him what he has found.Talk to Corudan outside the entrance of Stoke, in the BroadacresReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You have pursued Wormtongue's secret ally to Stoke, but he escaped into the city and discarded the brooch by which you would know him. Now you must warn the Reeve of the danger he poses.Wigbald is at the southern entrance to Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild has heard your warning about the mysterious foe that may have come to Stoke, and thinks you should speak with Wigbald at the southern entrance to the city. He may have seen something.Talk to Wigbald by the southern entrance to StokeWigbald thinks you should investigate the Norcrofts, in East Rohan, but your instincts tell you that Wormtongue's ally is still somewhere in Stoke.Wigbald thinks you should investigate the Norcrofts, in East Rohan, but your instincts tell you that Wormtongue's ally is still somewhere in Stoke.You found a discarded helmet, and nearby a small child has sought to get your attention.Talk to Adgyth on the northern reaches of StokeReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke, in the Broadacres.

You have learned that a suspicious man left the town, heading west, and may have joined with the Orcs. You should report what the child Adgyth saw to the Reeve.Talk to Reeve Fríthild in the Mead Hall of Stoke'Our mysterious foe seems to have fled Edoras to the north, ${PLAYER}. There are a few settlements in that direction, but my guess is that he is making his way for Stoke, in the Broadacres. You and your companions travel with haste to Stoke, there to see if you can apprehend this ally of Gríma Wormtongue before he can wreak further mischief.

'Reeve Fríthild rules from Stoke, and she has been an inspiration to me since I was very young. Her strength is second to no man, and she has earned the respect of all who dwell in her Riding. Still, this is not the sort of danger for which she is best-suited. She faces enemies that attack over the fields and engage in open battle, not snakes in the grass that strike from in hiding. Bring news of this secret foe to her, and warn her that he may lurk within Stoke. I do not know what evil webs he might yet spin.''Reeve Fríthild has always been an inspiration for me. Travel to the town of Stoke in pursuit of our mystery foe, and warn the Reeve of the danger he could pose to her city and her Riding.''It seems our fortunes turn for the worse, ${PLAYER}. A glint of light caught my eye from afar, and my worries are now confirmed.'

Corudan holds up the small object he has found in the grass: a blood-red ruby fitted into a silver brooch shaped in the form of an eye.

'Our friend knew of our pursuit, and discarded the only thing by which we would know him. Now he vanishes into the city, free to strike at his leisure. I will give you this brooch, but I confess this is not the outcome for which I hoped. Now we must deliver a warning only to the Reeve, and not the means of ending the threat.''We will find Reeve Fríthild in the Mead Hall. Let us bring her the brooch and warn her of the secret danger that may threaten Stoke.''It is always a pleasure to meet a new ${CLASS}, ${PLAYER}! If you maintain the peace and do no harm to my people or these lands, you will be welcome to my hall at any time.'

Fríthild listens as you recount your pursuit of Wormtongue's mysterious ally and the mischief he wrought at Edoras. She smiles when you mention that Éowyn desired you to bring word of the danger he might pose for the Broadacres.

'She has always been a strong girl, and in truth she reminds me of myself at that age. When she has grown some more, and broken a nose or two, she will be quite a woman. She must chafe at remaining behind to shepherd the people to safety while others ride to war. I would do the same, had the war not come right to my own lands. There is battle enough in the Broadacres!

'Someone may have seen your secret interloper as he came to Stoke, ${PLAYER}. You should speak with Wigbald, the guard at the southern gate. If anyone saw anything, it would be Wigbald. He misses little.''Wigbald is the guard at the southern gate. He would have seen your mysterious interloper if any did.''You say you are looking for someone? Well, I've seen scores of people coming through the gate today. What can you tell me about his appearance?'

You admit to Wigbald that you do not have a good idea of your prey's appearance.

'That makes it more difficult to find someone, no doubt about it. There was one fellow I thought seemed worthy of suspicion, now that you mention it. He kept asking about the Norcrofts, so it could be he has left this village entirely and is heading east.'

You tell Wigbald that you may search around Stoke for any other clues, just to be certain, and he shrugs.

'You are welcome to do as you like, of course. It is your time you'd be wasting. The only suspicious fellow I've seen was asking about the Norcrofts.''Are you looking for the man who dropped that helmet? I saw him come back for it, but when he saw me he ran! I followed him for awhile, but he kept running west of town, and I think he might have been looking for the Orcs!

'I could tell he was trouble! Did I do the right thing? If I see him again I'll tell you or Reeve Fríthild. You had better tell her who I am so she'll let me in to the Mead Hall. My name is Adgyth, and I've lived here in Stoke my whole life! My mother says I need to stay inside the walls. That's the only reason I didn't follow that man and bring him back for the King's justice. I knew he was a villain. You tell Reeve Fríthild that I'll let her know if I see him again, all right? Promise that you will. Promise!''Tell the Reeve that I may have more information for her! If I see that man again I will be sure to let her know!''You say this child saw the man for whom you seek? I would like to meet Adgyth and speak with her myself. My hall will be open to her as well.

'If this man you pursued from Edoras has joined the Orcs, the danger he poses has turned from a secret one to a threat we here in Stoke know all too well. We have been endangered by the ravages of Orcs and their half-orc brethren for many months now.'The village of Stoke is threatened by Orcs from Bardh-dorám and the ally of Wormtongue that fled from Edoras.The camp called Bardh-dorám is to the west of Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild has asked you to defeat Orcs and half-orcs at the camp of Bardh-dorám, and to keep alert for sign of their plans.The camp called Bardh-dorám is to the west of Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild has asked you to defeat Orcs and half-orcs at the camp of Bardh-dorám, and to keep alert for sign of their plans.Defeat Orc and half-orc raiders from Bardh-dorám (7/8)You have found a torn map of Stoke, and should return it to Reeve Fríthild in the Mead Hall.Adgyth is in the Mead Hall of Stoke, with Reeve Fríthild.

You have returned to Stoke to be greeted with troubling news: Adgyth stumbled upon a dead body.Talk to Adgyth in the Mead Hall of StokeAdgyth says she found Wigbald's body on the eastern side of the town of Stoke.Examine the body of slain Wigbald, in the eastern part of StokeIf Wigbald's body lies in the tall grass on the eastern side of Stoke, who did you speak to at the south gate who claimed to be Wigbald?Return to the south gate of Stoke and look for the man who claimed to be WigbaldCorudan is outside the south gate of Stoke.Talk to Corudan outside the south gate of StokeReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Corudan has pieced together what must have happened to Wigbald, and you should relate this information to the Reeve.Talk to Reeve Fríthild in the Mead Hall of Stoke'The Orcs have launched attacks on Stoke before, but we have always fended off their assaults. If this secret foe you chased here is as canny as he seems, he might be able to help the Orcs attack more effectively. The camp is called Bardh-dorám in their filthy tongue. Wipe the land clean of its foul inhabitants, and see if you can find any sign of their plans.''The Orcs have launched attacks on Stoke before, but we have always fended off their assaults. If this secret foe you chased here is as canny as he seems, he might be able to help the Orcs attack more effectively. The camp is called Bardh-dorám in their filthy tongue. Wipe the land clean of its foul inhabitants, and see if you can find any sign of their plans.''The Orcs have launched attacks on Stoke before, but we have always fended off their assaults. If this secret foe you chased here is as canny as he seems, he might be able to help the Orcs attack more effectively. The camp is called Bardh-dorám in their filthy tongue. Wipe the land clean of its foul inhabitants, and see if you can find any sign of their plans.''The Orcs have launched attacks on Stoke before, but we have always fended off their assaults. If this secret foe you chased here is as canny as he seems, he might be able to help the Orcs attack more effectively. The camp is called Bardh-dorám in their filthy tongue. Wipe the land clean of its foul inhabitants, and see if you can find any sign of their plans.''Orcs and half-orcs from Bardh-dorám have raided Stoke before, and we have always fended off their assaults. We may not continue to be so fortunate if they are trained by the secret enemy you pursued to my city.''Bardh-dorám is still a danger to Stoke, ${PLAYER}. Return to that place, foul as it is, and strike a blow against the foes you find there.''I found something! A dead body! Can you believe it?''You say you found this map of Stoke on the body of a villain from Bardh-dorám? I am not surprised. See these markings? Someone has made notes on this map, and though I cannot read the writing, I am familiar enough with my own walls to know what these annotations represent: vulnerabilities in our defences. Your secret foe is seeking to improve the Orcs' chances of razing Stoke.

'But you return to sorrowful news. Adgyth found something, and hastened to bring the news to me at once. Wigbald has been murdered. Speak to the child and hear her account.''I will grieve for Wigbald later. For now, I want justice brought to the man who slew him.

'Adgyth says she found his body on the eastern side of the city. Go there and see if you can find any evidence of how his body came to be there.''I found a dead body! I was just telling the Reeve about it!

'I was running around on the eastern side of the town, looking for that sour-faced fellow we talked about, when I stumbled upon a body lying in the grass! I stayed just long enough to see if I knew who it was... and I did! It was Wigbald, one of the town guards! I remember his big red beard. He was always kind to me, and now I think that man you were looking for must have killed Wigbald before he fled the city. I can point out to you where I found his body, if you like. I was just telling the Reeve about it, and she says that I did the right thing to come to her at once!'

You tell Adgyth you will investigate the place she described, but you have your doubts. You do not remember Wigbald having a red beard.'Wigbald was proud and strong, a good man. Stoke is less safe now without him guarding the south entrance. Find his body and see if you can reveal what led to his death.''Wigbald was always kind to me! I liked his bushy red beard! I wish it wasn't his body I found. He's in the long grass on the eastern side of town. Maybe you can learn what killed him?'You cannot tell how long it has been since Wigbald was slain, but it seems he suffered a blow to the head from some heavy object. Dirt and mud on his clothes indicate that he was killed somewhere else and then dragged into the heavy grass.

As you stare down at the body of the slain guard, something that has bothered you since you heard the news from Adgyth slowly resolves itself.

This is not the same Wigbald with whom you spoke when you arrived at Stoke.If this is Wigbald, who was it with whom you spoke at the south gate of town?'He is gone, as we knew he must be!''We have been deceived, ${PLAYER}! The man we spoke to when we arrived was not Wigbald at all... he was our quarry instead!

'I believe I can piece together the tale, but it brings me no joy to do so. Our secret foe arrived at the gates of Stoke sometime during the night, where he was challenged by Wigbald to identify himself. What name did he give? Perhaps Ednoth, or Góding, or another we do not yet know. What story did he weave for Wigbald? I cannot say, but my guess is that it played upon the guard's sympathies. From this I draw another conclusion: our cunning foe must appear, from the outside, to be a good man of Rohan. There will be little about him to mark him as an enemy, or Wigbald would never have let him into Stoke.

'But let him in he did, and at some point Wigbald was overcome and slain. What does it reveal about our foe that he was so bold as to take Wigbald's place, and speak falsehoods to the very people in pursuit of him? That is what worries me the most, ${PLAYER}. Our foe is bold, and he is cunning, and he is unafraid. It is clear to me, now, why he spoke of the Norcrofts, though it was not at the time. He was trying to steer us in the wrong direction.

'We must give this news to the Reeve.''We must tell the Reeve of this. We have been deceived, and the entirety of her Riding is in danger.''This tale is believable, but it makes my sorrow no less. Instead, it fans the flames of my anger! This man you pursue must be made to pay for his crimes. He sought to send you to the Norcrofts? His pride will be his undoing, for by revealing his charade we have discerned his next course. He wanted you to look eastward? Turn your eyes westward, and you will find where next lead his schemes.

'After teaching the Orcs of Bardh-dorám how best to attack Stoke, this cunning snake of yours fled the Broadacres. I am certain of it. He has gone to the Stonedeans.'The Council of Stoke determined that Reeve Fríthild should take to the field and lead her people against their aggressors, but now it must choose what front to focus upon.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should bring Captain Grimmar's recommendation to Reeve Fríthild.Talk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is at her encampment to the south of Stoke, just off the road on the other side of the bridge on the road to Kingstead.

Reeve Fríthild asked you to meet her at her encampment.Talk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is at her encampment to the south of Stoke.

You should speak with Reeve Fríthild regarding the battle.Talk to Reeve Fríthild at her campThe Reeve of Stoke is in the Mead Hall.

The Reeve instructed you to meet in Stoke to plan for the next attack.'It was a waste of time and effort -- and a life -- to harry the goblins. Saruman and Mordor both breed them like flies. There is a closer threat that will eat at us like a canker.

'My scouts tell me that half-blood brigands from the Stonedeans are attempting to infiltrate Stoke and sway the people against Reeve Fríthild. This threat must be put to end, or we will see the Dunlendings marching upon this town.''These brigands which Grimmar speaks of, are they the same that troubled Oserley and infiltrated Torsbury? Then, indeed, I agree with him. This is a treachery that can only harm the people of the Acres.

'I will gather our companions and ride out to meet these villains. Join us at our encampment to the south of town.''Your timing is sound, ${PLAYER}. We have only just returned from patrolling the area.

'The enemy is indeed present and will be upon us soon. Prepare yourself... their numbers are greater than we imagined!''Yet another faithful retainer has perished. Captain Grimmar was the backbone of my garrison... he will be greatly missed.

'We must make the White Hand pay for its treachery! I will return to Stoke at once... meet me there, ${PLAYER}.''I am grateful for your assistance, ${PLAYER}, but the time has come for a greater show of strength.''I am grateful for your assistance, ${PLAYER}, but the time has come for a greater show of strength.''I am grateful for your assistance, ${PLAYER}, but the time has come for a greater show of strength.''I am grateful for your assistance, ${PLAYER}, but the time has come for a greater show of strength.''I am grateful for your assistance, ${PLAYER}, but the time has come for a greater show of strength.'During the Council of Stoke, Reeve Fríthild chose to take to the field, leading her people against their aggressors.Fríthbert and Edhild are with their nurse, Engifu, at Fríthild's house, east of the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild asked you to visit her children, Fríthbert and Edhild, and give them words of comfort and encouragement.Talk to FríthbertTalk to Edhild'${PLAYER}, my friend, I know that my children's warden objected to my bringing the children into the council, for he believed that they would be frightened by my decision to take up arms. I knew that he was right, but I still feel they needed to see their mother as strong in the face of evil.

'However, I do not wish for them to fear any longer than they should. Perhaps if you -- a proven warrior, but also a stranger -- were to speak words of comfort and encouragement to them, their hearts will be eased. Sometimes the words of a stranger may be more comforting than those of their kinsmen.

'You may find Fríthbert and Edhild with their nurse, Engifu, at my house just to the east of the Mead Hall.''I confess that I am not as afraid for my mother as my sister is, for I am nearly a man in the reckoning of the Mark, and I know that my mother is a great shield-maiden.

'She will teach the Dunlending wild-men and their Wizard that we will not be cowed easily!''Mother sent you? You are the brave warrior who helped Oserley and Torsbury, are you not?

'I do not wish to be afraid, but I cannot help it. I do not remember my mother going to battle before, and I have never heard of a woman fighting in war...aside from old stories like Engifu tells us.

'Will you keep my mother safe?'After the victory at Bardh-dorám, Reeve Fríthild fell into a fey mood, uncertain as to the true success of the foray.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Thane Ordlac asked if you would broach the subject of a feast in celebration of the victory at Bardh-dorám with Reeve Fríthild.Talk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Reeve Fríthild to join the feast.Talk to Reeve Fríthild to join the feastThane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Ordlac regarding the treachery at the feast.Talk to Thane Ordlac'Ah, ${PLAYER}...the Reeve does not seem encouraged by our success. However, I know exactly what she needs to raise her spirits...I am going to suggest a feast in celebration of our victory.

'What do you think? Perhaps she would listen if you were to broach the subject.''Will you join us for the feast, ${PLAYER}? You should be our honoured guest....''The Acres lost a great leader today, my friend, but we cannot allow grief to overwhelm us. We must show strength for the sake of the people of Stoke.

'However, the children must be given the opportunity to mourn their mother. I have ordered that a funeral be held for Fríthild.''A feast? ${PLAYER}, I am not certain that we have cause to celebrate, for I am not certain that we truly succeeded in our venture....'The Mead Hall was attacked by men from Woodhurst for an unknown cause. The Reeve's advisors have worked to keep the attack a secret to help maintain peace.Thurferth is near the centre of Woodhurst.

Herubrand told you to speak with Thurferth, Captain of the Guard, about the two men posted at the Mead Hall and whether they have seen anything unusual.Talk to Thurferth, Captain of the GuardThurferth suggested other guards posted around the city may be able to help.Thurferth is near the centre of Woodhurst.

You should tell Thurferth of what you learned from the guards around town.Talk to Thurferth, Captain of the Guard'I cannot understand why Elda will not trust you. I believe he is concerned for me. He was my father's advisor and is set in his ways. But I am a good judge of character, and you seem trustworthy.

'Arnwi's observation is troubling, and we should be careful not to divulge too much information to the guards. Please speak with Thurferth, Captain of the Guard, about the two men posted at the Mead Hall, and if they have seen anything suspicious lately.''Nay, I have seen naught out of the ordinary. With Herubrand gone for Brockbridge, the door is locked and nobody has been up here until you came. It is too quiet up here. I miss my old post.

'I once was stationed down near the mine where I could listen to some of the men talk about their distrust of Rohirrim and that Herubrand was a fool to support them.

'There are terrible rumours about the Rohirrim going around.''There has been nothing suspicious here. They go about their duty, but the miners are tense with worry about war being so close by. 

'The Rohirrim seem to be losing this war, and some believe the Stonedeans are better suited to separate from Rohan -- that it should be ruled by its own people and not some fallen king from a far-off land -- while some believe that we should throw in with the Dunlendings and ally with the White Hand.''I have noticed nothing unusual, though the lands beyond this gate can be dangerous at times. 

'Rohirrim and Dunlending roots run deep through Woodhurst, and the lands ahead have the strongest Dunlending influence. Those who live beyond here do not keep hidden their wishes that the Stonedeans were part of Dunland.

'With tensions rising, it would be wise for Rohirrim and their supporters to avoid that place.''Not many come and go through this gate these days. Occasionally, a waggon of supplies is sent to Brockbridge to aid in their defence; otherwise, it is rather quiet here.

'Smoke rises in the distance and looks to be coming from south of Brockbridge. I worry for that fair town and for our leaders, Herubrand and Herefara, who went to aid in its defence.''Nothing suspicious happens around this gate. Farmers are busy tending the fields, and two Rohirrim left recently with their waggon packed.

'You are looking for the two who once guarded the Mead Hall? I was good friends with them, but not after what they did. I never wish to speak ill of friends, but they swore off their duty to those they were pledged to protect. And if the rumours are true, good people lost their lives because of it. 

'They can usually be found at the inn nearby.''You have come just in time...you just missed a party of men that I believe are up to no good. They appeared to be following one of the stable-hands at this stable, and they followed him around the corner. He has not come back, and I am worried that they might have hurt or killed him.

'His heritage is mainly Rohirrim, and the party following him were trouble-makers from the lower part of the city. Please check on him.''The two were bribed to leave their post? That is unacceptable. I will inform the others that they should be arrested on sight.

'Loyalty is not what it once was. We are sworn to protect the people...and most of all the Reeve. They were my most trusted men and were posted at the Reeve's door. I will not soon forget this betrayal.''The two men I entrusted with the protection of the Mead Hall are gone. They have not been seen for days, and we assumed they had left for the war, for riches elsewhere, or both.

'I have seen nothing suspicious, but I have orders to give and may not have noticed. The guards around town may have more to offer.'Though Elda was an advisor to Reeve Heremond for some time, he never saw eye to eye with Herubrand. This detachment led him to seek another who shared his vision for the future of Woodhurst.Herubrand is in the cellar of the farm-house.

You should bring the letter to Herubrand.Bring the letter to HerubrandGifemund is in the ore refinery building within Woodhurst, near the centre of the city.

