Thane Ordlac wishes for you to speak with him again.Talk to Thane OrdlacYoung Reeve Fríthbert and his sister Edhild appear distraught at Thane Ordlac's pronouncement. You should speak with them.Talk to FríthbertTalk to EdhildDudsig is near the door to Reeve Fríthild's house.

Dudsig wishes to speak with you.Talk to Dudsig'I believe you were dismissed....''I am glad that you are able to join us, my friend. I am sure that Fríthild would have wished it. Let us begin....''${PLAYER}, will you bring Fríthild's sword and place it, as well, upon her in honour?''Thane Ordlac has instructed me to keep the children in seclusion. I am sure you understand that with their mother dead and Fríthbert not yet old enough to assume his title, they are in danger...you never found the traitor in Oserley, you know.

'As much as I dislike you, you have proven a trusted friend to the Broadacres; however, even you will not be allowed to speak with the children again without Thane Ordlac's leave.

'Good day to you.''My thanks to you for bringing honour to Fríthild. It means much to her children. However, before we adjourn, I must make one pronouncement, and you should be a witness to it....''Good ${CLASS}, did our mother truly proclaim Thane Ordlac as our regent? This is most horrible.

'I cannot say why, but Ordlac makes me uneasy. He always seems friendly and cheerful, but he makes light even of sad things....''I am frightened, ${PLAYER}. Thane Ordlac smiles very much, but I do not like his eyes. There is no kindness in his eyes. He looks at us strangely, and it frightens me.'Thane Torferth of Torsbury has brought his men to Stoke, where he prepares to march upon the Mead Hall and confront the traitor Ordlac.Thane Torferth stands on the road to Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Torferth before the attack.Talk to Thane TorferthThe Mead Hall of Stoke is at the end of the road.

Torferth is making for the Mead Hall of Stoke, where he intends to confront Ordlac.Fight through Ordlac's forces to the Mead Hall of StokeDudsig and his guards are blocking the way to Ordlac and the children. Defeat them and confront Ordlac.Defeat Dudsig and his guardsTalk to Thane TorferthOrdlac is near the end of the Mead Hall, threatening Reeve Fríthild's children.

Aid Torferth against Ordlac and protect the children.Defeat Ordlac and protect the childrenThane Torferth is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Torferth at once.Talk to Thane Torferth'I will wish to speak with you later...come see me when you have rested a little.''We have come to it at last, ${PLAYER}, and it is a sad day. Brother must fight brother, and much blood shall be shed. Nevertheless, Ordlac cannot be permitted to continue his treachery.

'Are you ready to march with us? I am sure that Ordlac has reinforced the road to the Mead Hall, but we cannot be delayed!

'Forth the Toringas!''Look! Ordlac is threatening the Reeve's children! We must hurry!''We are victorious, ${PLAYER}. Those of Ordlac's men who followed orders without true knowledge of their master's treachery have turned on their brethren and helped rout Oserley from Stoke.

'The battle is over....'Reeve Fríthild has summoned together the Council of Stoke in order to come to a consensus regarding what action to take against the forces of the White Hand in the Broadacres.Speak with Reeve Fríthild.Talk to Reeve FríthildObserve the Council of Stoke.Observe the Council of StokeSpeak with Reeve Fríthild.Talk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild's sword, shield, and mail are on the table below the dais.

Reeve Fríthild asked you to collect her arms and armour.Collect Fríthild's mailCollect Fríthild's shieldCollect Fríthild's swordBring the sword, shield, and mail to Reeve Fríthild.Speak with Reeve Fríthild.Talk to Reeve Fríthild'I declare this Council dismissed!''Ah, ${PLAYER}, you have arrived. Let us then begin this council....''${PLAYER}, will you honour me by assisting me with my arms and armour? You will find it on the table behind you.''Gird me, ${PLAYER}, friend of Stoke. This day I become Fríthild, shield-maiden of Rohan.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You have been of great service to the people of Stoke. Well have I named you friend.'Gifemund wishes to offer protection to those who do not feel safe. Being a wise merchant and a man of wealth and power, he seems intent on using this offer as leverage.There are many shop owners in Woodhurst.

Gifemund has asked you to speak with the healer, supplier, provisioner, and the innkeeper on his behalf.Herubrand is in the cellar of the farm-house.

Herubrand would be interested to hear of the offer being made by Gifemund to the merchants of Woodhurst.Talk to HerubrandGifemund awaits your return in the ore refinery.

Gifemund will be interested to hear the answers to his offer.Talk to Gifemund'It is important that the people of Woodhurst feel safe again. I think safety is something that I can provide. It is known that the guards are struggling to maintain control and will sometimes avert their eyes when they should not.

'I can provide protection to those who want it and have something to offer in return. Please speak with the shop-owners of Woodhurst on my behalf.''It seems Gifemund has concern for our safety, but at the same time I believe he has other intentions. He may be able to use this against me some day, but I will accept his offer. I would rather be alive and healthy and be indebted to him, than otherwise.''You are not the first Gifemund has sent here with offers. He is conniving, and I want nothing to do with it. He is a powerful man, but he is also a wealthy man with a greedy heart. How can I trust his judgement, especially with my life or shop on the line? I will trust the guards.''Are you here to threaten me too? I mean no offence to you, ${CLASS}. You seem of a better kind than the last fool Gifemund sent with this offer. I cannot trust him or the guards.

'What are we to do if we cannot afford to leave and cannot trust those who should protect us? Where is Reeve Heremond to put an end to this?''It is an interesting offer, and one I must consider. It is true that the guards do not seem able to protect us, but I wonder what price he will demand when the time comes. He is a wise merchant, and this service will not be free.

'I accept, though I may regret this decision someday. I only hope the price is not too high when he comes to collect it.''This is not only an offer of protection, it is leverage to obtain what he wants. From what we have heard, Gifemund speaks true when he says that the guards are not doing enough... and my own family was but one example of that.

'This offer of protection is dubious at best and is likely only a bid for power.''They have a right to be suspicious. I have my motives, as do you. I simply offered what I have as a courtesy, but they are right... it is not free. I would not be the man I am today if I gave anything for free.

'Now that more have seen the wisdom of my offer, I may yet be able to achieve my goals....'Oter and Stithwulf are now better prepared for the long road since you stopped to help them. They are likely to stay a few more days before departing.Someone must have survived the attack on the Mead Hall. 

An advisor of the Reeve or a family member should be somewhere inside Woodhurst, in the north-west corner of the Stonedeans.Find a member of the Reeve's familyElda is near the Mead Hall in Woodhurst.

Arnwi asked you to speak with Elda, another of the Reeve's advisors.Talk to EldaArnwi is standing near the stable-master of Woodhurst.

Elda refused to speak with you and told you to return to Arnwi.Return to Arnwi near the stableCalin is in the farm-house on the eastern side of Woodhurst.

Arnwi suggested that you speak next with the third advisor, Calin.Talk to Calin'I cannot stop you from going to Woodhurst, but it is not safe. The night we left, we spotted a party of men sneaking towards the Mead Hall with weapons drawn. They might have killed us if we had been seen. We were about to leave when we saw them and hid behind our waggon until they passed.

'I hope that Reeve Heremond and his family are still alive and safe. Perhaps someone in the mead hall survived to tell you what has happened.''Good day, ${CLASS}. How might I help you? Ah, you were sent by men leaving the town?

'They were wise to leave, for our situation is not good. They sent you to seek Heremond, but I believe you look for Herubrand, Heremond's son. I am afraid you will not find him here.'

He lowers his voice so only you can hear him.

'You did not come in vain, but I cannot say more too loudly. Eyes and ears are everywhere. Elda may tell you more. He keeps watch upon the locked Mead Hall while Herubrand is away.''You are trespassing! Why did no guard stop you? The Thane is not here, and the door is locked. Be gone, or I will call for the guards!

'Oh, Arnwi sent you.

'There is nothing to see here, so keep to your own! Herubrand is not here, and I am not certain why Arnwi said I could help you. Go back to him...or go leap in the river and rid us of your insatiable curiosity.''Hail, stranger! What might you need of me?

'I apologise for Elda, but he has been acting queer towards everyone of late. It is unusual, as he has always been a kind and caring man. Much has changed lately, and I barely recognize Woodhurst anymore.

'You seem a friend of the Mark, and so I shall speak with you. We have much to discuss.''I should have warned you that Elda can be flustered easily these days. He was not like this before and is usually a gentle soul, though his reaction was harsher than I would have expected. I believe he is taking Herubrand's departure less easily than the rest of us.

'Perhaps you will have greater fortune speaking to Calin. He can be found in the farm-house on the eastern side of Woodhurst.'War is afoot, and the town of Stoke is preparing for the inevitable siege.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Thane Ordlac asked you to return to Reeve Fríthild and tell her that he is prepared for a council.Talk to Fríthild'I am more confident than ever that we must act quickly and decisively to drive back the foe. These orders you discovered seem to indicate that the enemy is not as strong as they appear and provide information as to where they might be attacking from.

'Please, tell Reeve Fríthild that I am prepared for the council...she has but to call us together.''If my task for you seemed irrelevant, ${PLAYER}, be sure that it was not.

'Thank you for your time...we shall speak again soon, perhaps at the council.'War is afoot, and the town of Stoke is preparing for the inevitable siege.Spears, swords, shields, and armour can be collected from yards throughout Stoke.

