You should return to Grimmar and tell him that you have done all he asked.Talk to Captain Grimmar'If you truly wish to help Stoke, then post these conscription notices and gather our supplies. We need swords, shields, armour, and spears a-plenty. We have had craftsmen throughout Stoke producing them for us with instructions to leave the finished products in their yards.

'I need you to gather them and bring them to the armoury stockpile outside the Mead Hall. Once you are done with that, post the notices and meet my lieutenant, Huthric, outside.''Ah, you have done well, ${PLAYER}! Here come the louts now! I'll have a word or two for them, ere they join the garrison....''My thanks to you, ${PLAYER}! I will speak with you again when Fríthild calls the council.'The Council of Stoke determined that Reeve Fríthild should take to the field and lead her people against their aggressors, but now it must choose what front to focus upon.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Baldmund asked you to bring his advice to Reeve Fríthild in the hopes that she will see the wisdom of the plan and not be biased by his lowly station.Bring Baldmund's advice to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Reeve Fríthild regarding the recent battle against the Warg-riders.Talk to Reeve Fríthild'Ah, ${PLAYER}! If Reeve Fríthild is to choose one front to attack, let me give you my advice: attack the Warg-riders to the east. They pose a threat to our hunters, and without the provisions gathered by the hunters, Stoke will not last a siege! However, should we defeat these foes, not only would it weaken the enemy, but it would strengthen our position.

'Would you bring my advice to the Reeve? I do not know if she would accept it from me.''Baldmund believes that we should dispatch the Warg-riders to the east. His plan has merits, though he also missed the point that they are a weaker foe, as well, and thus more quickly dispatched.

'Indeed, if you are ready to ride, I am prepared as well...let us call our friends together and be off!''We were victorious, ${PLAYER}, but at a heavy loss. Poor Baldmund was not a warrior born and should have remained here to speak for the common folk.

'Alas, his presence shall be missed in this hall in the days to come, for he tempered valiance with wisdom.'Thane Ordlac seemed outraged by the poison that you found upon Grimlac's person.Grimlac's allies might be found somewhere outside the north-east gate.

Thane Ordlac asked you to ride out against Grimlac's companions.Defeat brigand captainDefeat brigands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should bring the token you discovered to Thane Ordlac.Bring the token to Thane OrdlacLúfa is at Stoke's north-east gate.

You should speak at once with Lúfa.Talk to Lúfa'${PLAYER}, I bid you ride out the north-east gate and seek this Grimlac's companions -- of which I have no doubt he has many. Strike them down, then return to me.  We shall not brook such villainy!''${PLAYER}! It is well that I saw you before you returned to the Mead Hall. Is it true that Reeve Fríthild is dead and Ordlac is regent?

'Then I have come at an opportune moment. Cenulf received word from my husband that he was to bring the entire garrison here...they left Oserley all but defenceless!

'I rode here swiftly alone to learn the truth of the matter, and now I see you bearing a token which I know well....'Reeve Fríthild fell ill and died during the feast celebrating her foray ay Bardh-dorám.Thane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Ordlac when you are prepared to attend Fríthild's funeral.Talk to Thane Ordlac to attend Reeve Fríthild's funeralThane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Ordlac regarding the funeral.Talk to Thane Ordlac'${PLAYER}, my friend, a shadow of gloom has fallen over the people of Stoke, especially Fríthild's children. We must rebuild their hope.

'Will you join me at Fríthild's house to honour her passing? It would mean much to the children, I am sure.''I believe your presence will assuage the children's hurt a little. Thank you.''Do you think it was too soon for me to declare my regency? The children seemed distressed. Surely, they must understand that someone must lead their people until such time as Fríthbert comes of age?'War is afoot, and the town of Stoke is preparing for the inevitable siege.Meat and ivory can be collected from the aurochs to the south and west and the deer to the north, south, and east. Hides can be collected from wolves, deer, and aurochs.

Baldmund has asked you to help provision the town for the coming siege.Collect aurochs meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect deer meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect hides (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect ivory (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat wolves in the Broadacres (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The provision stores are in Stoke, outside the inn.

You should bring the provisions you gathered to the provision stockpile.Baldmund is at the inn in Stoke.

You should return to Baldmund and let him know that you have done as he requested.Talk to Baldmund'While you are here, I could use your help. My people and the townsfolk of Stoke have both asked me to speak for them and make sure their needs are not being overlooked. I do not doubt Reeve Fríthild's concern, but there seems to be plenty of battle preparations and little provisioning. I would take some hunters out myself, but there are too many wolves roaming the fields.

'Would you cull the wolves' numbers and hunt up some provisions for us? We need meat, hides, and ivory. The meat and ivory can be gathered from the deer to the north, east, and south and from the aurochs to the south and the hides from the wolves, deer, and aurochs.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You have proven a friend to the common folk of Stoke and Torsbury.'The rolling hills and fertile ground of the Stonedeans supports lush vegetation which enables predators to grow quite large. There are signs of a very large predator somewhere in the area.Somewhere in the Roaring Hills roams a large and dangerous bear.

You should find and defeat the great bear Strangbera.Defeat StrangberaSomewhere in the Roaring Hills roams a large and dangerous bear.

You should find and defeat the great bear Strangbera.Defeat StrangberaThe dangerous bear that once roamed the Roaring Hills is no more.CompleteThe bears have grown less aggressive from over-gorging on the lush vegetation in the area. There are signs of a more aggressive predator roaming among them, probably the largest bear in the den. Any adventurer or hunter would be proud to defeat such a beast. 

The beast will be formidable, and may be difficult to defeat alone.The hunters of Woodhurst will now hunt these lands without fear of a predator such as Strangbera stalking the lands.After failing the assault on Torsbury, Torferth sent you back to Stoke to inform Reeve Fríthild that he and his people would be late in coming.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: The bucket skill may only be used while on a war-steed.</rgb>Fires can be found throughout the fields west of Stoke in the Broadacres.

Reeve Fríthild has asked you to ride out and extinguish the fires that the Warg-riders started in the Broadacres.Equip bucket and extinguish fires in the western fields of the Broadacres (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'The loss of Tordag grieves me, for he was a good friend and a stout warrior. However, I must look to the defence of my Riding...there will be time for sorrow later.

'Warg-riders roam to and fro among the fields to the west, burning everything they see. Will you ride out and extinguish as many of the fires that you can? I would have something left of the Acres to pass down to my son.

'Be alert for an ambush...the goblins are vicious creatures who would just as soon put you to the torch.'At Lúfa's behest, you brought Thane Ordlac's token to Thane Torferth at Torsbury. He received it with great wrath.Thane Torferth is at Tordag's encampment near Torsbury.

You should speak with Thane Torferth when you are ready to march upon Stoke.Talk to Torferth to march upon StokeThane Torferth is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Thane Torferth regarding Ordlac and his treachery.Talk to Torferth'We must ride at once, ${PLAYER}, for there is little time to lose. The lives of many are at stake, not the least of which are Lúfa's and the Reeve's children's. We must take Stoke from the traitor and bring him ruin!

'There is no time to spare, my friend...will you ride with the Toringas once more?''I am proud to fight alongside you once more...Forth the Toringas!''I am grateful for your aid, ${PLAYER}. If you had not accompanied me, I fear that Reeve Fríthbert and his sister would have perished at Ordlac's hand. This is a terrible time, however, for Fríthbert is not yet old enough to lead the people of the Broadacres....'The leaders of Stoke are at odds as to the best course of action to be taken to protect the town and drive the enemy from the Broadacres.Ordlac and Grimmar are in the Mead Hall of Stoke. Baldmund is at the inn in Stoke. Dudsig is at the Oserley encampment outside the north-east gate of Stoke. Alfbert stands outside Reeve Fríthild's house.

Fríthild has asked you to summon the leaders of Stoke to council and deliver a letter to Alfbert at her house.Talk to BaldmundTalk to GrimmarTalk to DudsigTalk to Thane OrdlacReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should return to Reeve Fríthild and let her know that all is ready for the council.Talk to Reeve Fríthild to join the Council of StokeReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should speak with Reeve Fríthild regarding the course she has chosen.Talk to Reeve Fríthild'${PLAYER}, I believe the time has come to summon my advisors to council. Return to Grimmar, Ordlac, Baldmund, and Dudsig and inform them of my decision. We must decide what action to take and how best to fulfill it. The leaders of Stoke and Oserley each have their own opinions, and they are at odds. We must come to a compromise and work together, or we shall fall to the White Hand.

'As well, bring this letter to Alfbert, the guard at my house. Return to me when your messages have been delivered.''I am honoured that Reeve Fríthild values my opinion. I have never heard of a lord or lady of Rohan calling upon the common folk for counsel.

'I will come at once.''Good! It is of utmost import that we convince the Reeve to bolster our defences!''I do not know why I should waste my time sitting in council. There are too many preparations to be made. Nevertheless, I will come.''Ah! This is welcome news, my friend! At last, we will join a battle worth a song or two! Let us join the council!''What is this? Orders from the Reeve? If this is what she truly desires....

'Let the Reeve know that I will do as she commands.''You have my thanks.

'Let the Council of Stoke begin!''Ah, ${PLAYER}! It has been long since a shield-maiden of Rohan has actually taken to the field. This is a glorious day.

'All that remains to be decided is which front to assail, for we are surrounded.'The two former guards are suspected by an old friend to have abandoned their duty for the promise of riches. You were told they frequent a nearby inn.The nearest inn is near the fountain in the centre of town.

You might find Manna and Scurfa at the inn.Enter the tavern in upper WoodhurstThe innkeeper is behind the bar.

The innkeeper may have information about the two former guards you are seeking.Talk to the innkeeperThe less-frequented inn is in lower Woodhurst.

The innkeeper you spoke with suggested looking at a less-frequented -- and likely more dangerous -- inn in town.Enter the tavern in lower WoodhurstManna and Scurfa, the two former guards, are having drinks at a table in the shady inn in the lower section of Woodhurst.

You should speak with Manna and Scurfa.Talk to MannaManna and Scurfa, the two former guards, are having drinks at a table in the shady inn in the lower section of Woodhurst.

You should speak with Manna and Scurfa.Talk to Scurfa'The two who once guarded the Mead Hall, Manna and Scurfa, are well-known at the inn nearby. If you wish to find them, you should probably start there. 

'However, they may be trying to remain unnoticed after abandoning their duty....''Aye, I know the two you are looking for, but I have not seen them come by for a few days.

'There is another inn in town, but they cater to poorer folk. That inn would put me out of business with their cheap swill, if my patrons dared to enter it. I hear only trouble goes in there, so be wary. It is by the pond in lower Woodhurst.''What do you want? Aye, you heard the truth. We abandoned our post at the Mead Hall. The captain kept going on about us being his favourites, but he posted us at that dreary hall! Nothing ever happens at the Mead Hall. He would barely even pay us! He wanted us out of the way, and now here we are.''Aye, we abandoned the captain, but not our duty to Woodhurst. The captain does not have Woodhurst's best interests at heart. We follow the man who could and will save the people. The captain distrusts Heremond and Herubrand and is more concerned about putting himself in a position of power.

'The man we now owe our allegiance to would likely want to meet you...besides, he is willing to pay us. Captain only flipped a coin our way when it suited him.'The wealthy merchant Gifemund has determined that he feels Thurferth is a direct threat to his designs for the seat of Woodhurst. A confrontation is imminent.Guard-captain Thurferth is near the fountain around the centre of Woodhurst.

You should bring the letter to Guard-captain Thurferth.Bring the letter to Guard-captain ThurferthHerubrand is in the cellar of the farmhouse.

Herubrand would probably like to see the letter Gifemund prepared for Thurferth.Bring the letter to Herubrand'The time has come to confront Guard-captain Thurferth. Since I have taken it upon myself to provide protection to the city, his services and influence are no longer needed.

'Elda and I have prepared a letter that lists the terms of his removal from his post. Please deliver this letter to him as soon as you can. If he does not leave quietly, then he must be removed by force... let us hope it does not come to that.''I assume this letter is a poor jest. That man has no leverage to threaten me and certainly cannot remove me from my post.

'He claims that I drove the Rohirrim out of this city? I will admit, I do not favour them and prefer we no longer exile the Dunlendings in lower Woodhurst. I will face his challenge for the seat in Woodhurst, as I have just as much claim as he.''This is bold and will likely result in a confrontation. It was a gamble for Gifemund to do this, and it seems that they are vying for my seat. Are they the champions Hildegard said would squabble?

'I suspect this conflict will become bloody. They now fight for the seat of my father, but I am still too weak to claim it myself. I shall wait to decide who receives my support until I hear their perspectives.'The Council of Stoke determined that Reeve Fríthild would take to the field and lead her people against their aggressors, but now it must chose what front to focus upon.Reeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should bring Dudsig's recommendation to Reeve Fríthild's attention.Talk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild's encampment lies to the west of Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild instructed you to meet her party at her encampment.Use the camp-fire to summon Reeve Fríthild and her companionsReeve Fríthild's encampment lies to the west of Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild instructed you to meet her party at her encampment.Use the camp-fire to summon Reeve Fríthild and her companionsTalk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is at her encampment, west of Stoke.

You should speak with Reeve Fríthild regarding the recent battle.Talk to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke, east of her encampment.

Reeve Fríthild has asked you to speak with her in Stoke.Talk to Reeve Fríthild in Stoke'The folk we fought in the south were of my own kind -- half of Dunlending blood, half with blood of the Mark, and likely from the Stonedeans. I find it offensive that they should be slaughtered so, and without the opportunity to explain themselves... all because they were not of pure Rohirrim blood.

'The true evil comes from the west: the Dunlendings who invade our lands and fill the minds of my people with lies and deceit. The Dunlending captain who leads the invaders at Bardh-dorám oft leads assaults against the gates of Stoke or rides the plains with his guards... slay him, and the foe shall be put to flight.''I understand Dudsig's frustrations, but I do not ask pardon for protecting my Riding. I grant, however, that he is correct in that the true responsibility for this evil belongs to this Cledwun.

'Therefore, I have decided to bring the justice of Grimmar down upon his barbarian head. Ordlac and I will set camp to the west... meet us there, and we will watch for Cledwun's appearance.''Ah, ${PLAYER}! We have found Cledwun and have drawn him to this place... on your guard! Here he comes!''Grimmar is avenged, but I fear that the invasion has not been hindered. I will return at once to Stoke and consider our next action. Meet me there.''I confess that I am distressed, ${PLAYER}. The assaults on Stoke continue, and more Uruks and half-orcs arrive at Bardh-dorám every day. We must find a way of halting their advance....'A scout has encountered Gandalf while riding in the Westemnet, and the wizard mentioned that he wishes to speak with you.Gandalf can be found near the main gate of Edoras.

You were informed that Gandalf wishes to speak with you. You should go to him at once.Speak with Gandalf in EdorasHúlac can be found in the town of Stoke in the Broadacres.

You should ride to Stoke and search for Húlac.Find Húlac in the Broadacres'You...you are ${PLAYER}, are you not? The famous ${CLASS}. I ran into Gandalf the Grey upon the plains not long ago, and he asked me to convey a message to you if I should see you.

'He said he would be in Edoras shortly to check on things there. Perhaps you will catch him if you make haste!''Well met again, ${PLAYER}! I have heard of trouble in the Broadacres, but I must continue my search for Erkenbrand, Grimbold, and Elfhelm, for I can find no trace of them since the Fords.

'Will you go to the Broadacres in my stead, and lend what aid you can? Search for Húlac in the town of Stoke -- that will be a good start.''Well met, friend. We have need for a ${CLASS} such as you....'War is afoot, and the town of Stoke is preparing for the inevitable siege.The map and orders that Ordlac desires may be found upon the mounted half-orcs to the west of Stoke.

Thane Ordlac asked you to collect maps and orders from the enemy, which might disclose the whereabouts of their encampmentCollect map to enemy encampmentCollect the enemy's ordersThe enemy encampment of Bardh-dorám lies to the west of Stoke.

Thane Ordlac wants you to proceed to Bardh-dorám.Locate Bardh-dorámBardh-dorám lies to the west of Stoke.

Thane Ordlac wants you to enter Bardh-dorám and locate the tents of the enemy leaders.Locate Mabaj's tentLocate Ushatar's tentLocate Ilska's tentLocate Cledwun's tentOrdlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should return to Ordlac and tell him of your successes.'If you need something else to do while I consider my advice for Reeve Fríthild, I do have a task for you. It would prove valuable if you could learn where the enemy's encampment lies. Perhaps one of the half-orcs roaming the fields to the west might be able to provide a map or something else indicating from whence they come.

'Search for any maps or orders you can find, and if by chance you find their encampment, look for the tents of their leaders. Any knowledge you can provide me with will help lay the groundwork for our plans to come.''Were you unable to locate the enemy encampment? Ah, but you did! Bold and doughty you continue to prove yourself.

'You say that the enemy leaders were not present there? That is queer. Nevertheless, this information will prove of great value for the council.

'Thank you!'After receiving the Dunlending token you brought her, Fríthild is concerned that the scope of the invasion from the west is far greater than she first believed.Grimmar and Ordlac are in the Mead Hall of Stoke. Baldmund is at the inn in the north-eastern part of Stoke. Dudsig is at the Oserley encampment outside the north-east gate of Stoke.

Reeve Fríthild has asked you to speak with the leaders of the forces at Stoke and learn what their advice for her would be.Talk to BaldmundTalk to GrimmarTalk to OrdlacTalk to DudsigBaldmund, Grimmar, Ordlac, and Dudsig have need of your services. You should give them your aid.Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should wait for Ordlac's advice to be heard.Complete Ordlac's AdviceFríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should return to Fríthild with the town leaders' advice.Talk to Fríthild'${PLAYER}, it is clear that Saruman the White holds sway over the Dunlendings as well. I fear they intend to sweep through the Acres and fall upon Kingstead and Edoras. We must prepare to stand as a foil to that invasion.

'If you would, speak with the leaders in the city and learn what advice they would give. Captain Grimmar and Thane Ordlac are, of course, present here, but I would have you speak with Baldmund, the leader of the refugees from Torsbury, and Ordlac's captain Dudsig. Baldmund may be found at the inn in the north-east part of town, and Dudsig is at the Oserley encampment outside the north-east gate.''I was chosen by both my people -- the refugees for Torsbury -- and the townsfolk of Stoke to speak for them. I believe we must first provision the town for siege before aught else.

'But perhaps that is a decision best left to the Reeve and her advisors in council.''Clearly, we should shore up our defences and prepare for the coming siege. However, it is not my decision to make. I would advise Reeve Fríthild to call a council of war. It may be that Thane Ordlac will have some insights that will help.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, my friend! I have heard how you fared at Torsbury and grieve Tordag's loss. However, we must not lose heart and must put aside sorrow... there are glorious battles to be fought!

'I must consider our current situation carefully. There is much that needs to be done ere we make a final decision. Return to me in a while, and I will speak my mind more openly. Good day, my friend!''It is not my place to say what the Reeve should do. If it were I, I would strike at the heart of the foe, but it is not.

'If the Reeve wishes to be advised, let her call a council.''Thank, you, ${PLAYER}. I wished to learn something of the minds of my advisors before calling a council, and you have helped me do so.

'Prepare yourself... the Council of Stoke will soon be called to session.'Thane Ordlac announced his regency of the Broadacres at Reeve Fríthild's funeral, upsetting Fríthild's children and their governess.Reeve Fríthbert is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Thane Ordlac asked you to speak with Reeve Fríthbert and learn what might be done to calm Edhild's tears.Talk to FríthbertEngifu, the children's governess, is at Reeve Fríthild's house, east of the Mead Hall.

Fríthbert asked you to bring Engifu to the Mead Hall to comfort Edhild.Find Engifu at Reeve Fríthild's houseAlfbert should be somewhere in Stoke, perhaps at the inn.

You should find Alfbert and ask him what is going on.Talk to AlfbertThane Ordlac is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

Alfbert suggested that you ask Thane Ordlac for an explanation for the replacement of the Stoke guards with his own.Talk to Thane Ordlac'Ah, ${PLAYER}, I must confess that I do not have much experience with children. Young Fríthbert seems to be bearing up fairly well -- though I daresay he has taken a disliking to me -- however, there seems no end to his sister's tears.

'Would you speak with Fríthbert and learn if there is something that might be done to comfort her? I simply do not think I can bear this weeping another moment.''Thane Ordlac only feigns concern. He cares naught for me and my sister. Look how he has treated the helm of my honoured father...it has been tossed upon the floor. He is bold to take the seat where my father's helm has sat for as long as I can remember.

'I suppose that there is naught I can do. Perhaps if you could bring Engifu to the Mead Hall, she might comfort Edhild. She was told to remain at my mother's house.''Ah, so you have begun to see Ordlac's heavy hand? Aye, Cenulf and Dudsig sent me away and replaced me with Ordlac's men.

'The Reeve's men are being displaced throughout Stoke. Many are being sent on hopeless forays against the invaders. Mark my words, ${PLAYER}, Ordlac is trouble.

'If you want an explanation, you will have to ask Ordlac yourself.''${PLAYER}! Why are you troubling me with trivial matters? Did Fríthbert not send you to retrieve the children's governess?

'Ah, I must beg your pardon for Cenulf's stiff neck. He was only following my instructions not to allow anyone into Fríthild's house to trouble the governess.

'It appears that I was mistaken. Cenulf reports that the old woman was so overcome by grief that she threw herself from the second floor and perished. It is terrible.'Warg-riders from Isengard burn and ravage the western fields of the Broadacres as they press their invasion.You found a Dunlending token upon one of the Warg-riders. You should bring it to Reeve Fríthild.You found a Dunlending token upon one of the Warg-rider defilers. You should bring it to Reeve FríthildReeve Fríthild is in the Mead Hall of Stoke.

You should bring the Dunlending token you found to Reeve Fríthild.Warg-riders from Isengard burn and ravage the western fields of the Broadacres as they press their invasion. You found a Dunlending token upon one of the Warg-riders and should bring it to Reeve Fríthild.'That the Dunlendings assaulted the Stonedeans and even Torsbury does not surprise me; however, it concerns me that a goblin would bear one of their badges...it means that these evil creatures are more closely in league than I first believed.'War is afoot, and the town of Stoke is preparing for the inevitable siege.Warg-riders and wolves haunt the fields to the east of Stoke.

Dudsig has asked you to deal with the threat of Warg-riders and wolves which plague the people of the Broadacres.Defeat wolves and Warg-riders east of Stoke (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dudsig is at the Oserley encampment outside the north-east gate of Stoke.

You should return to Stoke and tell Dudsig of your victories.Talk to Dudsig'If you wish to be of any true use to the people of Stoke, you should deal with the dangers that lie to the east. Warg-riders and wolves ravage the countryside and no one stops them. They will quickly become a threat to Oserley as well, if nothing is done.

'You are no stranger to the ways of mounted combat and so should have no difficulty dealing with this threat...that is if your courage has not already failed you. What say you? Will you attend this challenge?''You live still? That is too bad. I have nothing more to say to you, ${CLASS}...be on your way.'After he was banished from Edoras, it appears that Gríma Wormtongue went north of the road to Isengard. He is either avoiding the road, has business to attend to elsewhere, or both.You have encountered Gríma Wormtongue on the plains just north of Edoras, and he appears to be travelling west towards Isengard with a host of Orc bodyguards. You should deal with the bodyguards and confront Gríma.Defeat Gríma's bodyguards (0/6)You have encountered Gríma Wormtongue on the plains just north of Edoras, and he appears to be travelling west towards Isengard with a host of Orc bodyguards. You should deal with the bodyguards and confront Gríma.Defeat Gríma's bodyguards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gríma would like to have a word with you after you defeated his bodyguards.Talk to Gríma WormtongueYou have encountered Gríma Wormtongue on the plains just north of Edoras, and he appears to be travelling west towards Isengard with a host of Orc bodyguards. You should deal with the bodyguards and confront Gríma.'Well, are you not an annoying little ${RACE}? More like a maggot crawling in the carcass of this once great land. It is certain that you will die here...if not by my hand, then perhaps by your best friend's? A wise tongue can be stronger than the arms you bear....'

'I have need of more Orcs, since you killed mine! Should you find me again, you will not be so fortunate.'Egwylf wants to try and find where Tanglemane and Bingo fled after the attack by the Nazgûl.Egwylf wants to return to the place where you became separated from Bingo and Tanglemane. It was to the south-east of Woodhurst, in the Broadacres, just to the south-west of the overrun settlement of Bardh-dorám.Return to the location of the Nazgûl attack, in the BroadacresEgwylf is searching for sign of Tanglemane's passage in the Broadacres, at the location where the Nazgûl attacked.Talk to Egwylf near the location of the Nazgûl attackEgwylf asked you to search near the location of the Nazgûl attack for a sign of Tanglemane's passage.Search for Tanglemane's tracks near the location of the Nazgûl attackTanglemane's trail leads west of the location where you were attacked by the Nazgûl, into the Stonedeans.Look for Bingo along a straight line west of the location where the Nazgûl attackedNorth of Brockbridge, you have stumbled across Humfrey Rumming in your search for Bingo and Tanglemane.Talk to Humfrey Rumming north of BrockbridgeEgwylf is on the road north of Brockbridge, looking for tracks.The tracks lead somewhere west of the road to Brockbridge, where you encountered Humfrey Rumming on his way to the Gap of Rohan.Search to the west for more tracksWillem Whisker seems to have caught the scent, and has raced up the steep slope and towards a village on the south-west heights of the Stonedeans: Gapholt.Search Gapholt for Bingo and TanglemaneBingo and Tanglemane made their way to the Gapholt, in the Stonedeans, and now you have been reunited with them.Talk to Bingo in the Gapholt'We need to find where Tanglemane and Bingo have fled, ${PLAYER}. In order to do that, we should return to the place where we were attacked by that unnatural creature. If I can examine the soil at that location, I should be able to pick up their trail. Let us go at once to the south-east. If I remember correctly, the spot was in the Broadacres, just to the south-west of the overrun settlement of Bardh-dorám.

'We will find them before you know it. Set aside your worry!''We should return to the place where we were attacked by that unnatural creature. If I can examine the soil at that location, I should be able to pick up their trail. Let us go at once to the south-east. If I remember correctly, the spot was in the Broadacres, just to the south-west of the overrun settlement of Bardh-dorám.

'We will find them before you know it. Set aside your worry!''Hmm... I do not see any good tracks just yet, but I will keep up my search.''Hmm... I do not see any good tracks just yet, but I will keep up my search. You should study the ground for pony tracks as well, ${PLAYER}. Tanglemane may have left some clue to where he has borne Bingo.''You should study the ground for pony tracks as well, ${PLAYER}. Tanglemane may have left some clue to where he has borne Bingo.''You found tracks leading to the west? We should follow them at once!''Hullo there, friend!''I will search for new tracks.''There are a few tracks here, but they are indistinct.''Ah, hello there, ${CLASS}! It seems as if it was only yesterday that I met you in the snowy reaches of Wildermore, but business there was poor. I am bringing my wares south in search of a more eager market. I make for the Gap of Rohan. You are welcome to tag along, if you like.

'No? It is your loss, then! I wish you well on your way, but I really must be finding some customers for my marvelous wares, and there are none to be found here!

'A pony? I may have seen a pony hurrying to the west, but I did not pay it much mind. Now good day to you, ${CLASS}!''A pony? I may have seen a pony hurrying to the west, but I did not pay it much mind. Now good day to you, ${CLASS}!''These tracks are indistinct, but I think they continue to the west. I believe Tanglemane carried Bingo that way.''I may have seen a pony to the west of here. It was dark and I cannot be certain, even though my eyes are the sharpest in all the land! If I am not certain, no one would be!''The tracks lead to the north-west, I think.''I cannot make any sense of these muddled tracks, ${PLAYER}. Bingo and Tanglemane could have gone anywhere!''Hello there, my friend!''I think Willem has caught the scent! He ran up the slope! Are there any villages up that way? It could be that Tanglemane brought Bingo to one of them... we should hope that it is so!''Oh, am I glad to see you, ${PLAYER}! I am also very proud of Willem Whisker! What a good boy, and a good tracker! You could teach Egwylf a thing or two about finding the lost, couldn't you, W.W.?'

Bingo laughs happily, but then a shadow passes over his face.

'That was a very scary creature, ${PLAYER}. I have never seen anything like it! It was good of Tanglemane to carry me away as quickly as he did. I am glad you escaped from that apparition unharmed as well! I hope we do not run into any more of those things. I used to be frightened of horses, but not anymore if there are terrible winged creatures like that thing in the world. It is hard to believe!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Thoughts of Home' (Recommended Level: 93)</rgb>Gapholt has become a haven for the Rohirrim who sought a safer place to live than the dangerous streets of Woodhurst.There is a bag of horse-feed near the refugee waiting at the end of the line on the bridge trying to get into Gapholt.

You should collect horse-feed from the bag.Collect horse-feed from the containerMany horses are agitated by the wait on the bridge after the long journey from Woodhurst.

The refugee at the end of the line suggested that you feed them to help calm them down.The horses are now much less agitated thanks to your efforts.Complete'We need to ensure these horses stay calm. They all had a long journey here from Woodhurst and now grow restless waiting up on this bridge. I could use your aid, for I must keep my place in this line.

'Please gather some of the horse-feed from the bag at the end of the line and give a little to each horse. Hopefully, that will ease their spirits.'The horses seem more calm now that you have given them a little food, and it seems they are less likely to become unruly and cause injury to themselves or others. Hopefully, whatever is delaying their entry will be resolved soon.Woodhurst sends aid to Brockbridge in the form of supplies, but not many men in Woodhurst are willing to fight against their brethren or the vast armies of the White Hand.Arrow bundles can be retrieved from the market, iron bars from the miners, wood planks near the farm-house, and food from inside the farm-house.

Herubrand asked you to gather supplies for Brockbridge.Brockbridge is south and a little east of Woodhurst.

Herubrand has asked you to take the supplies to Brockbridge and support his son Herefara.Bring supplies to Herefara in Brockbridge'Herefara, my eldest son, departed to aid the defences in Brockbridge, which has been under siege. Should that bridge fall, the Stonedeans will be overrun by Saruman's armies.

'If you wish to aid them, please bring supplies with you. They could use arrow bundles from the market, iron bars from the ore-house, wood planks from near the farm-house, and food from in the farm-house.

'Herefara is a capable leader, but he would benefit from your aid...that of a veteran of battle.''Thank you, ${CLASS}. A capable warrior who also came with supplies! Your arrival is unexpected, but most welcome.'There are silhouettes of many kinds of ballistae throughout Madh-fushaum. It appears that the White Hand brought the machines with them from Isengard.You should explore Madh-fushaum in search of a torch to ignite the fuel stored near the machines.Collect a torchYou should explore Madh-fushaum in search of a torch to ignite the fuel stored near the machines.Find a torchThere are silhouettes of many types of ballistae throughout Madh-fushaum. The fuel to ignite the missiles should be somewhere near the machines.Destroy the fuel (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The stock of fuel is destroyed and will reduce the effectiveness of the White Hand ballistae.CompletedIn the distance you can see the silhouettes of many kinds of ballistae that the White Hand brought with them. They seem to be preparing them for use, likely against Brockbridge. There may not be time to destroy the machines, but you can ignite any fuel used in setting the missiles aflame.Ballistae devastate any defensive positions. It is a small victory, but you have destroyed the fuel which allowed those devastating missiles to also rain fire down upon the defenders.Reeve Fríthild believes that the man you pursue has gone westward, into the Stonedeans.Woodhurst is located in the northern stretches of the Stonedeans, west of the Broadacres.Travel to the entrance of Woodhurst, in the StonedeansNona and Horn are at the northern entrance to Woodhurst, in the Stonedeans.Talk to Nona by the entrance to WoodhurstTalk to Horn by the entrance to WoodhurstBriant is inside her son's hideout, in Woodhurst. You do not know where Reeve Heremond is. The hideout is on the outskirts of Woodhurst.

You should find Reeve Heremond or his wife Briant in Woodhurst, and convey the message from Reeve Fríthild that Woodhurst may be in danger.Talk to Briant in the hideout on the outskirts of WoodhurstHerubrand is in the hideout on the outskirts of Woodhurst.

Briant has told you to speak with her son Herubrand about the situation in Woodhurst.Talk to Herubrand in the hideout on the outskirts of WoodhurstDunlending invaders can be found throughout the Stonedeans, especially in Woodhurst.Defeat Dunlending invaders throughout the Stonedeans (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Herubrand is in the hideout on the outskirts of Woodhurst.

You have defeated many Dunlending invaders and should report of your success to Herubrand.Talk to Herubrand in the hideout on the outskirts of Woodhurst'I have no close ties with my neighbours to the west, save the oaths we share, but I wish them no harm. If this snake has entered the Stonedeans, they must be warned of the danger he poses. A secret enemy, combined with the dangers they already face, endangers the whole of my Riding. He must be stopped, and now only a few know his face. You have seen it, as has poor, departed Wigbald. Bring this knowledge to Reeve Heremond, in the Stonedeans.

'His seat is in the town of Woodhurst, located in the northern region of the Stonedeans. Heremond is very old, and there are whispers that he has been in poor health of late. If he is indisposed, you should speak to his wife Briant. She is of Dunlendish blood, but she is strong in her own right.''I have no close ties with my neighbours to the west, save the oaths we share, but I wish them no harm. If this snake has entered the Stonedeans, they must be warned of the danger he poses. A secret enemy, combined with the dangers they already face, endangers the whole of my Riding. He must be stopped, and now only a few know his face. You have seen it, as has poor, departed Wigbald. Bring this knowledge to Reeve Heremond, in the Stonedeans.

'His seat is in the town of Woodhurst, located in the northern region of the Stonedeans. Heremond is very old, and there are whispers that he has been in poor health of late. If he is indisposed, you should speak to his wife Briant. She is of Dunlendish blood, but she is strong in her own right.''I have no close ties with my neighbours to the west, save the oaths we share, but I wish them no harm. If this snake has entered the Stonedeans, they must be warned of the danger he poses. A secret enemy, combined with the dangers they already face, endangers the whole of my Riding. He must be stopped, and now only a few know his face. You have seen it, as has poor, departed Wigbald. Bring this knowledge to Reeve Heremond, in the Stonedeans.

'His seat is in the town of Woodhurst, located in the northern region of the Stonedeans. Heremond is very old, and there are whispers that he has been in poor health of late. If he is indisposed, you should speak to his wife Briant. She is of Dunlendish blood, but she is strong in her own right.''I have no close ties with my neighbours to the west, save the oaths we share, but I wish them no harm. If this snake has entered the Stonedeans, they must be warned of the danger he poses. A secret enemy, combined with the dangers they already face, endangers the whole of my Riding. He must be stopped, and now only a few know his face. You have seen it, as has poor, departed Wigbald. Bring this knowledge to Reeve Heremond, in the Stonedeans.

'His seat is in the town of Woodhurst, located in the northern region of the Stonedeans. Heremond is very old, and there are whispers that he has been in poor health of late. If he is indisposed, you should speak to his wife Briant. She is of Dunlendish blood, but she is strong in her own right.''Bring word of the danger that threatens the Stonedeans to the town of Woodhurst!''Horn tells me this town is home to many people of mixed blood-lines. Do they maintain the traditions of my people and of Horn's people? Or have they forgotten from where they came in order to survive in this land?''This is Woodhurst. I have desired to see it for some time.''Horn tells me this town is home to many people of Dunlending and Rohirric blood. Do they maintain the traditions both of my people and of Horn's people? Or have they forgotten from where they came in order to survive in this land? I do not see much I recognize of the Uch-lûth in this town. The buildings are wrong, the land is wrong. Will the people prove otherwise?''This is Woodhurst. I have desired to see it for some time. So near to Dunland, many of the folk of this town share ancestry with the Dunlending tribes. Perhaps Nona and I will be able to find a place here, when all is through?

'That is still a good while hence, I do not doubt it! But my heart tells me that Woodhurst would be more welcoming to us than my own home of Eaworth. Well, we have more pressing matters than this! Let us find Reeve Heremond and report to him of the danger his town may face!''This looks like any other village of the horse-lords to me.''I see the Dunlending influence in this place, to be certain.'Heremond has died. His wife Briant or his son Herubrand must rule Woodhurst now in his stead.'You are welcome in Woodhurst, but you do not see her at her best. My husband has finally succumbed to death, but I cannot properly mourn for him. I must mourn too for our city, for it is held in the grip of Dunlending invaders. The blood of the hill-tribes flows through my veins, but they are no longer my people. When I wed Heremond and came to live in his lands, and when I gave him a son, then I was no longer of my tribe. I was part of a new tribe.

Briant's eyes flash, and you see a fire in them that reminds you of Nona.

'Those who hold the Mead Hall and bring war to my home may share my blood, but they spill the blood of those I love. Now we are enemies. You have my thanks for this warning you bring, but Woodhurst is already imperiled. What is one more threat upon the pile of many? Speak to my son Herubrand. He rules now in place of his father. I will answer any questions of yours I am able.''Speak to my son Herubrand and help him defeat the invaders from Dunland. They are my people no longer.''You wish to help? Good. There are not enough folk willing to help with the problems of others in these trying times. My son Herefara is doing all he can, as am I, but we cannot be everywhere. There will always be somewhere we are not. You may not know much of these invaders, but I do. They are called Draig-lûth, and they worship worms and lizards of all sorts, but their loyalty has been bought by Saruman and they go where he commands.

'Defeat these Dunlending invaders, wherever you find them throughout the Stonedeans. You will find Draig-lûth aplenty in Woodhurst, to my sorrow and my mother's.''There will be no shortage of Dunlending invaders throughout the Stonedeans, friend. Fight them, and defeat them, wherever you are able to do so.''You have done well, my friend. Soon this land will be free of their clutches. My son and I will do what we can against the Draig-lûth, for they cannot defeat us.'

Herubrand smiles at your puzzled expression.

'I speak not in riddles, ${PLAYER}, and do not exaggerate my prowess or that of my heir. The Dunlendings cannot defeat us. We are invincible.'Herubrand believes that he is invincible and cannot be slain by the Dunlendings that invade the Stonedeans.Horn is at the entrance to Woodhurst.

Heruband has offered to bring you before the soothsayer Hildegard, but first you should see what Horn and Nona think of the prospect.Talk to Horn at the entrance to WoodhurstNona is at the entrance to Woodhurst.

Heruband has offered to bring you before the soothsayer Hildegard, but first you should see what Horn and Nona think of the prospect.Talk to Nona at the entrance to WoodhurstHerubrand is in the cellar of his hideout, on the outskirts of Woodhurst.

Horn and Nona have agreed to meet with the soothsayer, with varying degrees of excitement, and now you should tell Herubrand you are ready to go.Talk to Herubrand and go with him to meet the soothsayerHorn and Nona are at the entrance to Woodhurst, in the Stonedeans.

You have had a curious encounter with the soothsayer, Hildegard, and should now speak of the experience with your companions.Talk to Nona by the entrance to WoodhurstTalk to Horn by the entrance to WoodhurstHerubrand is in the cellar of his hideout, on the outskirts of Woodhurst.

You have returned from meeting the soothsayer Hildegard, and should report now to Herubrand.Talk to Herubrand in his hideout, in Woodhurst'It is true. An old soothsayer lives in a remote cave among the mounds, and she speaks words of prophecy. She has foretold that I will die at Orkish hand, as will my firstborn son.'

Herubrand laughs boisterously.

'Never before has knowledge of coming death been so welcome, ${PLAYER}! For if it be my fate to die at Orkish hand, well, no Dunlending will be able to land a hand upon me! Thus far it has proven so! Speak to your companions, and if they agree to the journey I will bring you before the soothsayer Hildegard. Perhaps she will read your fate, as well?''What is it? A soothsayer lives in this land?''A soothsayer? Such mystics never speak plain, but deal in riddles to confuse the weak-minded. Herubrand is a fool to believe the words of this Hildegard. I will go with you to this cave if Nona desires it, but I beg of you: do not put too much stock in the words of this soothsayer. There are many mysteries in the world, but I think it likely this Hildegard is merely a woman with an addled mind.

'This soothsayer will speak in riddles and lies. Mark my words well, for they tell the future. Pah!''This soothsayer will speak in riddles and lies. Mark my words well, for they tell the future. Pah!''A soothsayer lives in this land? I am filled with delight, ${PLAYER}! Such folk truly possess the power of foretelling the fates. Do you remember the dream that drew us across the mountains? We could not fathom its meaning, but a soothsayer such as the one of which Herubrand speaks is more skilled at reading such visions than the like of us.

'Return to Herubrand and humbly beg him to bring us before this soothsayer. She could tell us much!''Are you prepared to depart? Very good! Let us see what Hildegard has to say of your own future. May it be so rosy as my own, which frees my hand in battle against the Dunlending invaders!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'We have to do something! I need time to think, ${PLAYER}. Hildegard is as powerful as I had hoped, and now I wish that she was not! Her words give shape to a grim tomorrow, and I do not want to believe it will come to pass. Am I to lose Horn as I lost my brother?''Rubbish and lies. The words of that woman prey on the fears and hopes of all who listen to her. Herubrand is a fool if he believes himself to be invincible on account of Hildegard's witless ramblings. I cannot believe Nona sets any stock upon the words of this woman. She has my heart, always, but I am saddened by her belief in this nonsense.''Herubrand is a wise man to listen to the words of Hildegard. She has unusual powers, and can see much that is hidden to the rest of us. Horn denies her power at his peril!''Herubrand is a fool. His belief in Hildegard will get him killed, and perhaps not at the hands of an Orc! It will be little consolation to me, or to him, but there it is.''Now do you understand, my friend? Hildegard has unusual powers, and she speaks of the future with a convincing tongue. I have no doubt that I will cast the Dunlending invaders from Woodhurst, for none of them can hope to slay me, or my son. If my death be at Orkish hand, then no Man can hope to harm me!'As the men valiantly defend Brockbridge, they occasionally discard ineffective weapons and grab a new one to keep fighting. These dull blades now litter the ground throughout the town.Discarded dull blades can be found throughout Brockbridge.

Mula has asked you to sharpen the dulled blades that the defenders of Brockbridge have discarded.Sharpen discarded blades (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Herefara is on the northern side of Brockbridge.

Mula had instructed you to return to Herefara when you were finished.Talk to Herefara'You might be able to lend us some aid.

'We are running low on sharp swords! We have not a spare man to sharpen them, when we need more men who can swing them. If you have a moment, please take this whetstone and sharpen any dull blades you can find around the town.

'When you have done this, return to Herefara for further instructions.''I see that you have been busy helping around town. Your assistance is appreciated.  

'It is now time that we work towards breaking through this siege.'An army of this size needs a strong network of sentries to ensure communication throughout the soldiers and can respond to incoming threats.The eyes and ears of Madh-fushaum, the Uruk-hai sentry in the watchtowers around the outer wall are entrusted with the safety of the entire army.Defeat Uruk-hai sentries (0/6)The eyes and ears of Madh-fushaum, the Uruk-hai sentry in the watch-towers around the outer wall are entrusted with the safety of the entire army.Defeat Uruk-hai sentries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Uruk-hai sentries were battle-hardened warriors and a crucial aspect of the White Hand army. Removing them struck a blow to the safety and coordination of the forces within Madh-fushaum.CompleteThe wall outside of Madh-fushaum has enemies that never sleep, and are the eyes and ears of the army within. Any solid network of sentries has multiple layers, and the watch-towers along the wall make-up a strong inner layer.Their army is now blind to incoming threats, but more importantly are even less aware of your passing.The White Hand has learned it will take more than a large attack to break through Brockbridge into the northern Stonedeans and has instead sent a smaller force across a ford north of Flodgeheld.The winding Swiftwater River has a shallow ford north of Flodgeheld where the cliff walls dip down into steep but climbable banks along the river; however, they quickly rise up into a canyon just beyond that.Find the ford north of FlodgeheldA footprint can be seen in the grass on the other side of the ford. It was likely made by the White Hand that were suspected to have come through here recently.Find evidence of the White Hand's passingIt seems likely that more footprints were made which could help indicate which direction they went. You should search the land as it rises north of the river for more footprints.Find more evidenceThe grass grows thick as the ground rises from the river, and it may be harder to follow the trail of the White Hand. You will need to search for more evidence to determine which direction they went.Find more evidenceThe party of White Hand that crossed the river appeared to lead in the direction of one of Gifemund's mines, Mate Isernore.Scout Mate IsernoreHerubrand is in the cellar of the farmhouse in Woodhurst.

You have much to tell Herubrand, with your efforts in Brockbridge, the situations in Flodgeheld and Madh-fushaum, and more recently what you found in Mate Isernore.Talk to Herubrand'I noticed a party of White Hand warriors pass by here while you were at Madh-fushaum. The group was smaller than that which attacked Flodgeheld and Brockbridge not long ago. They seemed to be heading north up the river and may have crossed at a nearby ford. Their presence in the north would not bode well!

'You should follow their trail and see if you can find where they went and help those who may need it.''The Enemy has crossed the river? That is ill news indeed, but we cannot deal with Mate Isernore yet. Woodhurst has slipped further out of our grasp since you were last here. We need your help now more than ever.'The army in Madh-fushaum brought a large stock of food with them from Isengard and keep it stored in sacks throughout the camp.The food-supplies in Madh-fushaum are stored in sacks that are spread out throughout the camp. You should find them and destroy them.Find the food storesThe food-supplies in Madh-fushaum are stored in sacks that are spread out throughout the camp. You should find them and destroy them.Find the food-storesThe food-supplies in Madh-fushaum are stored in sacks that are spread out throughout the camp. You should find them and destroy them.Find a torchThe food-supplies in Madh-fushaum are stored in sacks that are spread out throughout the camp. You should find them and destroy them.Destroy food-supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroying the food-stores of this large army will have a devastating effect on its health and morale, unless the leaders of the army can find a new supply soon.CompleteAn army of this size requires a large stock of food. By destroying these stores, you could force them to find new stores, hindering their ability to attack.The leaders of the army in Madh-fushaum will be hard-pressed to replace the food-stores that you destroyed, providing an adequate distraction, while they scramble to find a new supply.The former guards, Manna and Scurfa, are now working for a wealthy merchant in Woodhurst. They claim to have the opportunity to protect people that they did not have before.Gifemund can be found in the ore-refinery, which is a wooden structure near the centre of town.

Scurfa suggested that you speak with his new employer, Gifemund.Talk to GifemundDunlending trouble-makers can be found in lower Woodhurst.

Gifemund asked you to deal with the Dunlending trouble-makers that have been hassling his mine.Defeat Dunlending trouble-makers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gifemund can be found in the ore-refinery near the centre of the city.

Gifemund will be interested to hear your progress in dealing with the trouble-makers who threaten the safety of his mining operation nearest to Woodhurst.Talk to Gifemund'The man I now work for is very influential in Woodhurst. He is the most successful merchant in the region. He owns the mines outside of the city, and his trade was unmatched until the White Hand cut off the southern routes.

'As a guard, I was seldom given the chance to truly protect people, yet that is why I became one. I am now afforded that opportunity...and I get paid for it. Gifemund may offer you a similar opportunity if you speak with him in the ore-refinery near the centre of town.''Ah, so you are the ${RACE} I have heard much about... the one who gets things done. I have need of a ${RACE} like that. Have you come to join my cause for a free Woodhurst? Free from Rohirrim demands for support, free from the Dunlendings' ill will from being exiled, and free from the war that will surely change the land?

'The guards here seem to offer little protection, and trouble from lower Woodhurst is causing problems at my mine. Would you be willing to teach them a lesson?''You did well, but bear this in mind their ill will cannot be easily contained when a people like the Dunlendings have been exiled and oppressed as long as they have. Word travels swiftly in this city, and I have heard that you handled that little problem well. You and I may be able to do great things, should you choose to join me.'The wealthy merchant Gifemund is in a strong position to make a move for the throne of Woodhurst, but is uncertain of the situation and seeks perspective from an unusual source.Hildegard the soothsayer resides in a cave on the cliff overlooking Woodhurst.

Gifemund asked you to speak with Hildegard the Soothsayer on his behalf.Talk to the soothsayerHerubrand is in the cellar of the farmhouse.

You should tell Herubrand of your meeting with Hildegard on behalf of Gifemund.Talk to HerubrandGifemund awaits your return in the ore refinery near the centre of Woodhurst.

Herubrand bade you return to Gifemund with Hildegard's words.Talk to Gifemund'The soothsayer, Hildegard, has a unique gift. She may be able to reveal things unseen to me and those of my cause. I feel my position is strong, but if I move too hastily, I might risk everything.

'Please, take this payment to her and ask her to speak of what she sees in my future.''I see many things... some things clearer than others. I saw you with Herubrand, yet you come to me now seeking my vision for another man. You come with payment from him?

'Darkness grows below, as hate rules the minds of most. The champions of Woodhurst squabble while the true enemy remains unopposed. The city will fall before it is saved.''Hildegard speaks in riddles, but I have found her words to be surprisingly truthful. Her telling was grim and does not bode well for us. I must ponder the meaning behind her words. You should return to Gifemund, since he paid handsomely for that information.''Hildegard's gifted insight is hard to understand, but seems so clear to her. That she said there is more than one champion of Woodhurst is interesting, because I must be one of them. But who is the other... or others?

'The enemy unopposed must be Guard-captain Thurferth. It is as I thought, and the time has come to oust him from this city.'The valiant defenders in Brockbridge are facing a relentless assault, and sometimes the men cannot be relieved for a warm meal.Gewis has some meals set aside for the guards in the cooking area.

Gewis has asked you to fill bowls with soup for the hungry guards.Fill the bowls with food from the potMula is keeping watch on the eastern side of Brockbridge.

Gewis asked you to bring a bowl of soup to Mula, the Sergeant of the Guard.Bring a meal to Mula, Sergeant of the GuardThere are guards posted throughout Brockbridge.

Gewis asked you to also bring meals to other hungry guards.Herefara is keeping watch on the western side of Brockbridge.

Gewis suggested that you again speak with Herefara when you are done feeding the guards.Talk to Herefara'I just finished preparing a soup for the men who are protecting the town; however, some have not come to get food for themselves. There must be guards around town who are hungry. I know for certain that Mula, the Sergeant of the Guard, must be hungry, for I have not seen him all day.

'Please fill bowls and bring soup out to the hungry guards. It will be hard to keep the town safe on an empty stomach. When you are done, I believe that Herefara wishes to speak with you again.''You brought me a warm meal! This is a luxury that we can rarely enjoy. The enemy looks for any opportunity to break through, so we have to stay alert. I will have to eat this quickly, but it will be savoured nonetheless.

'You have my thanks for bringing me this soup!''Ah, you have returned! The Thane has been met, and the guards are fed. Now it is time to help with more pressing matters.'Gifemund has made his move, and demanded Guard Captain Thurferth step down due to his failing to keep the city safe. They now try to settle their differences in front of the empty throne they have both sought.Speak to Calin before joining the confrontation. 

It appears Gifemund and Thurferth are in an argument that could get violent near the empty throne.Speak with CalinThere is a heated discussion between Gifemund and Thurferth in front of the empty throne in the mead hall.Observe the discussionNow that Thurferth has left, Gifemund and Calin have engaged in a quieter discussion.Observe Calin and Gifemund's discussionSpeak to Calin near the throne at the back of the mead hall.Speak with Calin'Herubrand needs to hear of what occurred here today. Thurferth's suggestion to side with Saruman is disturbing, and I wonder how many feel that way. Gifemund and I will meet with Herubrand immediately.

'Make your way there when you are ready.''I think we got here just in time. The argument between Gifemund and Thurferth is beginning to heat up, but I am not sure what can be done besides letting them work it out.''I believe the time has come to include Gifemund in our secrets, and share our plans with him. He can prove to be a powerful ally. I will bring him to Herubrand shortly.'The city of Brockbridge faces its greatest challenge yet as an army marches on the stalemate siege in an attempt to punch through their stalwart defences.The enemy is preparing to assault Brockbridge with renewed vigour, and the few defenders remaining will be hard-pressed to keep the city secure.Talk to HerefaraThe enemy will assault both of the barricaded entrances into Brockbridge. Herefara has asked you to use your war-steed and help the defenders that are more overwhelmed.Defend BrockbridgeHerefara would like to thank you for your assistance in keeping the enemy at bay and ensuring not too many men were lost in the battle.Talk to Herefara'${PLAYER}, we thank you for your assistance defeating everything the enemy threw at us today.''Drums announced their approach, as they were not afraid to let us know they are coming. Their confidence is unsettling. Our defenders will soon be pressed to keep up. We must keep both gates defended, and I ask that you help any of the men who become overwhelmed!

'We cannot lose too many men, or we will be overrun. I will guard this gate, but I ask that you mount your war-steed to more easily assist either gate.''They would surely have overrun our defences had you not been here. Some men were lost, but not so many that we can not recover.'You have joined Herubrand as he meets with his council to discuss what should be done in the aftermath of the attack on the Mead Hall of Woodhurst.Herubrand's council is meeting in the cellar of the farmhouse, and Herubrand has invited you to join them.Talk to HerubrandHerubrand's council is meeting in the cellar of the farmhouse, and Herubrand has invited you to join them.Observe the council meeting'We will speak again shortly, once the others have departed. I may not know you well, but you told me of my family and of the scene at the mead hall, when the others would not. I believe you have earned my trust.''The council has gathered, and the time has come to discuss our actions in response to the attack. We must attempt to secure Woodhurst and find a way to protect our people. There is something malevolent working against us here.'The Mead Hall was attacked by men from Woodhurst for an unknown cause. The Reeve's advisors have worked to keep the attack a secret to help maintain peace.Elda is outside the Mead Hall of Woodhurst.

Calin told you to return to the Mead Hall and tell Elda to let you in so that you can search the bodies of the fallen assailants for evidence of their motives.Talk to EldaThe Mead Hall door must be unlocked before you can enter and investigate the bodies of the fallen assailants.The bodies of the fallen assailants were left inside the Mead Hall.

Calin asked you to search the bodies of the fallen assailants for evidence of their motives.Enter the Mead Hall of WoodhurstThe bodies of the men who attacked the Mead Hall can be found throughout the hall.

Calin asked you to search the bodies of the fallen assailants for evidence of their motives.Calin awaits your return in the farm-house in Woodhurst.

You should return to Calin and let him know what you have found.Talk to Calin'Arnwi was right to not speak openly of the truth. We spread the rumour that Heremond left for Brockbridge, as it was the only way to ease tensions and further violence against him. Only a few know of this.

'One night a group of men forced their way into the Mead Hall and attacked everyone in sight. We are not certain who they were or why they did this, but they seemed intent upon reaching Heremond and his family. We barely escaped with Herubrand, but not everyone returned.

'Would you enter the Mead Hall and search the bodies for any evidence of their motives? Alas, we will not be able to maintain secrecy for long, as the bodies are beginning to stink.''Ah, the ${CLASS} returns! I hope it is good news that you bring me?

'Forgive me. My desire to know our enemy has clouded my reason. It was not likely that we would find much of use. However, this news of Elda's actions is troubling.

'He is no longer watching the Mead Hall? I will seek an audience with him soon, as we are apparently not of a similar mind.''I thought I made it clear that you should not be here?

'What?! Calin wants me to let you into the Mead Hall?

'This is outrageous! How dare he undermine everything we worked for and place his trust in you, whom he just met. I will speak with the others about this!'Gifemund has come forward and is demanding that Guard-captain Thurferth step down due to his inability to protect Woodhurst. They now seek to settle their differences before the empty seat of the Thane.You should speak with Calin when you are ready to go to the Mead Hall.Talk to CalinSpeak with Herubrand in the basement of the farm-house in Woodhurst.Speak with Herubrand'I have just received word from Calin that there is a gathering in the Mead Hall. This is likely the confrontation we have been expecting. From what Calin told me, most of the town has gone to the Mead Hall! 

'I need you to go with Calin and show your support for Gifemund.''I assume that Herubrand told you what I have discovered? We should hurry so as to make certain that we are not too late to stop whatever is happening at the Mead Hall!''Thurferth has left, and it appears the war around us fractured the people of Woodhurst more than I thought. How someone so close to leadership could share allegiances with the White Hand is concerning.

'His removal is important for us to rally the people under a common banner, and making this city a safer place to live. Gifemund and I will be working together toward that goal. 

'Word has arrived from Brockbridge, and they are desperate for aid.'The Brockbridge siege seems to be supported by a larger White Hand army, and there have been messengers spotted coming and going from the front lines.A messenger of the White Hand can be found among the perimeter forces outside of Brockbridge or on the road beyond it.

Herefara has asked you to intercept one of the White Hand messengers and learn what you may about their support.Find a messengerReturn the map to Herefara in Brockbridge.Bring the map to Herefara'${PLAYER}, I have a task for you.

'The enemy has a perimeter outside the town, and neither of our forces seems to make much progress against the other. I know there are many more of the White Hand out there supporting this siege. If they bolster the perimeter, we could be caught unprepared.

'What I need is more information about those supporting this siege and if more reinforcements are coming. I have seen messengers among their troops, who very likely have the information I seek. Intercept one and learn what you may from him.''This is an interesting but crudely drawn map. These Orcs are using some kind of symbolism to represent the area's features and our defences. I will need to study this to determine what it all means.

'One distinguishable marking on here represents a very large force of White Hand nearby. That must be the support behind this endless siege, and perhaps my fears are coming true; that an unstoppable force is bearing down upon us.'The siege on Brockbridge is only the tip of the spear, which is directed by a very strong and tactically wise Uruk named Nágas.Nágas can be found among the perimeter outside of Brockbridge.

