You have arrived at Brockbridge and should now speak with Herefara.Talk to Herefara in BrockbridgeThane Léoferth is in the Mead Hall of Brockbridge.

Herefara has asked that you speak with the Thane before you investigate the turmoil in the Westfold.Talk to Thane Léoferth in the Mead Hall of BrockbridgeHorn and Nona are on the southern side of the span, by Brockbridge, in the Stonedeans.Talk to Nona by BrockbridgeTalk to Horn by BrockbridgeCorudan is by the southern side of the span, in Brockbridge.Talk to Corudan by BrockbridgeHelm's Deep is in the southern reaches of the Westfold.

You and Corudan have decided to travel to Helm's Deep in search of the King, while Horn and Nona seek out the Falcon Clan raiding party.Travel to Helm's Dike, the entrance to the Deep, in the WestfoldGamling is at Helm's Dike, at the mouth of the Deep.Talk to Gamling at Helm's Dike'I sent my son Herefara south to the town of Brockbridge, for the bridge over that span is of great strategic importance. He was to help Thane Léoferth keep that point secure, for the Thane... well, some say his last days are nearly here. My son is strong and will help Léoferth defend the span.

'Travel south to Brockbridge and meet with my son. He will know how best you and your companions can help.''You will find my son Herefara at Brockbridge. Travel south to the city and offer him your aid, for I am certain he will have need of it. Thane Léoferth is in poor health, and Brockbridge has vital importance for the Stonedeans.''My father sent you to me? I am pleased he did so, but I must confess that your abilities might be more needed elsewhere! My men report that something terrible has happened at the Ford of the Isen, and no one seems able to give a clear account. Men are scattered all over the Westfold, and rumours of battle abound. Stay not at Brockbridge! Someone must learn what has befallen in the Westfold. Some say that the King himself is embroiled in the fighting, and may have ridden to Helm's Deep!

'A raiding party of Dunlendings has crossed the mountains, and they strike from cover of darkness. Their delight in killing the innocent is unrivalled. Their mark is a winged talon, and the fear of them has spread like fire in the brush. I hope to stand against them, but I have been unable yet to find them. They strike and then disappear, as if they wield some skill borne of magic.

'Before you leave, go to the Mead Hall and speak with the Thane. Léoferth is very weak, and the strain of rule has taken a heavy toll on his strength. It is the courteous thing to do. My father Herubrand believes I rule in Brockbridge, and perhaps it seems so in action, but I would not presume to tread upon the authority of the Thane.''I do not presume to rule Brockbridge. I act only at my father's request and for the good of Thane Léoferth. Speak to him in the Mead Hall, if he is able to do so. He is very weak, and I fear his last days approach.'Léoferth is very weak, and cannot speak above a whisper.

'You... come from... Edoras? What... news?'

You tell the Thane that the King has ridden to Isengard to wage war against Saruman's forces, and Léoferth's face slowly forms a rictus grin.

'Good... good! That is... the strength... the strength I remember. My son... Háma... surely rides with... with the King. He is his most... most loyal...'

Léoferth's voice fades into nothing and his eyes seem far away. You nearly turn away when his eyes close, but then he speaks once more.

'Help the... help the King. The Riddermark... must not... must not fall. The Eorlingas... will... will...'

You respectfully wait for more, but the Thane is lost in his own thoughts, and no longer sees you.'Nona and Horn plan to find the Falcon Clan raiding party in the Stonedeans. But Horn has charged me with another task.''I know the the mark of the winged talon, ${PLAYER}. That is the sign of the Hebog-lûth, the Falcon Clan. They became enemies of my people when they accepted the coin of Saruman. They are enemies of yours as well, and of Horn's people. Herefara says he has not been able to track their raiding party? That is because he is the son of a horse-lord, and does not know the ways of the wild. He is at home on the open fields, but the people of the Falcon are familiar with the high crag and the paths of the mountains. Lheu Brenin can lead his people wherever he will, and Herefara will never find him.

'But I will. I can move silently and swiftly, and I can find the tracks no horse-lord would recognize. We must part again, ${PLAYER}, for a time. I will see you again when the Falcon Clan raiders have been defeated, but I cannot allow them to roam free unopposed. I must do this while I am still able. No others can.''You spoke of the Falcon Clan to me, not long after we first met. I cannot deny their crimes, nor their evil, but I wish there was some other who could find them. Nona believes none other could track them, but surely their leader will make some error, or some mistake, that will lead to his downfall?

'No, you speak wisdom. If even one innocent life could be spared, it would be folly not to act. Nona believes she can find the Falcon Clan raiding party, and I think she is right.

'But I will not leave her again. This is a journey we will make together. I hope you understand, ${PLAYER}. I will see you again in time, but I cannot abandon Nona to this battle.''Farewell for now, my friend. I must hunt the Falcon Clan. It is my obligation.''Farewell, my friend. I have asked Corudan to look after you. You should do the same for him. Keep him away from any Ents!''Horn wishes to go with Nona on her search for the Falcon Clan, and I cannot blame him. He believed he could set his love aside while they were apart, but it seems to my eyes that proved incorrect. Rather than send his heart away with her, he would rather walk beside her on her own journey, until its conclusion. Of all the strange things that have been done for love, this is perhaps the least peculiar: a desire to not be apart.

'He charged me with one task, in his absence. He wanted me to look after you, ${PLAYER}! As we continue southward into the Westfold in search of the King, I am to ensure you do not endanger yourself unnecessarily. I told him I would do my best.

'Our farewells have been said. When you are prepared to go, let us make for Helm's Deep, in the Westfold. Horn says that this is where the King would ride if his march to war ran afoul of danger unexpected.''Horn told me that the King would make for Helm's Deep, in the Westfold, if things went ill on the ride to Isengard.''You have come to help us? I know not what help you can give, but I will turn none away. Things have gone ill for us.'An evil Orc-leader named Rípmaush has set himself upon the Thane's seat in the Mead Hall of Marton, and now mockingly titles himself Thane.Rípmaush can be found in the Mead Hall of Marton, sitting upon the Thane's chair and surrounded by his minions.

You should dethrone Rípmaush, the false Thane.Challenge: Dethrone Thane RípmaushRípmaush can be found in the Mead Hall of Marton, sitting upon the Thane's chair and surrounded by his minions.

You should dethrone Thane Rípmaush.Challenge: Dethrone Thane RípmaushRípmaush has been removed from the Thane's chair of Marton's Mead Hall.CompletedAn evil Orc-leader named Rípmaush has set himself upon the Thane's seat in the Mead Hall of Marton, and now mockingly titles himself Thane.

His presence is an insult to the real Thane of Marton.

<rgb=#FF0000>WARNING: This quest is intended to be challenging.</rgb>Rípmaush has been removed from the Thane's chair of Marton's Mead Hall.Some very strong warrior-trolls can be found in Shatorthum, and should be defeated in order to weaken this particular attack intended for Helm's Deep.Strong warrior-trolls can be found in the depths of Shatorthum.

You should defeat the strong warrior-trolls, if you are able.Challenge: Defeat warrior-trolls in Shatorthum (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated a number of the menacing warrior-trolls near Marton, weakening this particular force resting on its way to storm Helm's Deep.CompletedSome very strong warrior-trolls can be found in Shatorthum, and should be defeated in order to weaken this particular attack intended for Helm's Deep.

<rgb=#FF0000>WARNING: This quest is intended to be challenging.</rgb>You have defeated a number of the menacing warrior-trolls near Marton, weakening this particular force resting on its way to storm Helm's Deep.Herubrand has been staying in the cellar of the farmers' house, but Calin and Herubrand have not seen them in over a day and begin to worry about them.Calin is on the first floor of the farmhouse, and is looking into arranging a search party for the missing farmers.Speak with CalinThe farmers have gone missing, and are suspected to be out in the fields somewhere.Investigate the farm-fieldsThe fields are disturbed by someone who has no regard for the crops. This was clearly not the farmer's doing. It does not bode well for the missing farmers.Herubrand should be told of the farmers being murdered so close to where he is hiding.Speak with HerubrandSpeak with Gifemund about the security concerns in the city. He has taken over the protection for Woodhurst, and it is clear that things have not gotten better.Speak with GifemundReturn to Herubrand in his hideout within the farmhouse cellar and tell him of Gifemund's promise to keep the fields watched.Return to Herubrand'Most of my wounds have healed, but I am still fairly weak. This city cannot wait for me. Gifemund is a strong ally, but he does not seem able to keep control of the city. What or who is drawing such darkness and hate upon us?

'I have heard rumours from Calin that farmers have gone missing. I worry for them, and particularly for the owner of this home; my dear friend who graciously let us stay here. Please speak to Calin, and see if you can help.''The rumours are true: the farmers have all gone missing, including the man who owns this house. They have not been seen for over a day! That is very unusual for them. We need to look for them right away. I worry something terrible has happened.

'Please head out to the fields and begin looking for them. I will try to find others who can aid in the search.''I cannot believe they are gone....

'I am devastated by what is happening to this once fair city. Stuck in this cellar, I am unable to take in the beauty of Woodhurst. But, it would be hard to enjoy such beauty when surrounded by malice and the blood of my family and friends. The violence is a stark contrast to the beauty this city holds.

'Please speak with Gifemund about his security efforts here in Woodhurst. We must do something about this.''I am sad to hear that Woodhurst has lost more good men. We were not watching those fields as closely as we should have, and it seems we are too late now. The fields will be watched from now on.

'I believe Woodhurst can be a place where Rohirrim and Dunlending can co-exist, as we are simply the people of the Stonedeans. Why must there be such hatred between us, when we have been brothers here for a long time?''I believe he is sincere in providing adequate protection and in his desire to bring everyone in Woodhurst together. However, the soothsayer mentioned a growing darkness, and someone or something powerful is working against us, driving our people apart.

'I agree with his sentiment that our Rohirrim and Dunlending brothers should be able to co-exist. Most in this city have the blood of both. I believe they are who Gifemund seeks to align to his cause more than anyone else.'Bingo has learned that there may be a village of hobbits somewhere in Enedwaith, and he wants to investigate.Bingo has learned that there may be hobbits living in the Gloomglens in north-west Enedwaith, and he wants to investigate and learn if it is true.Find the settlement of hobbits in the GloomglensYou have found a settlement of hobbits in the Gloomglens.Talk to Bingo in the GloomglensRhus Cornchúthur and Iola Isbrun are keeping watch near the entrance to the hobbit-village in the Gloomglens.Iolo Brochtû, the Maer of Maur Tulhau, can be found outside his house in the north of the hobbit village.Talk to Iolo Brochtû in the north part of Maur TulhauIolo Brochtû has suggested that you speak to the villagers of Maur Tulhau and collect ingredients for a welcoming feast from the forest outside the village.Defeat wolves in the Gloomglens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect boar-meat in the Gloomglens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect stag-meat from the Gloomglens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect root vegetables south-east of Maur Tulhau (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Obtain ingredients from Violed IsbrunObtain ingredients from Iago GlennudhObtain ingredients from Caradog HenbuchCadel Balchtrod, the provisioner of Maur Tulhau, is in the central square of the village.

You have acquired numerous ingredients for the Welcoming Feast, and should bring them to the provisioner of Maur Tulhau.Bingo is ready for the Welcoming Feast, and is waiting for you by the water in the north-east corner of Maur Tulhau.Talk to Bingo at the Welcoming Feast, in north-east Maur Tulhau'I just had the strangest conversation, ${PLAYER}. I was speaking with a man in the village, and he asked what brought me out of the Gloomglens. Well, I have never heard of a place called the Gloomglens, and I have certainly never been there before! I told him so, and do you know what he said to me? He said that I must have sustained a knock on the head and forgotten!

'This hit a little close to home, to tell you the truth. After my water-logged journey through the caverns beneath the Misty Mountains, I am not altogether happy being reminded of such forgetfulness! I am quite sure I have never been to the Gloomglens!

'But... do you think there may be some other hobbits there? I do believe we should investigate and see for ourselves!''Do you think there may be some other hobbits in the Gloomglens? I do believe we should investigate and see for ourselves!''Well look at this!''Hold, stranger!''Well, who are ye? Strangers?''Well, what do you know? Hobbits do live here in the Gloomglens! How many miles have we travelled from the Shire? More than I can say without a fair bit of thought, and yet here is a settlement of hobbit-folk all the same!'

Bingo laughs suddenly.

'What would old Milo Goodbody have to say about this discovery of ours, ${PLAYER}? It seems there could be other hobbit-villages out there somewhere, not just in the Shire or in Bree but all over!''How lovely it is to meet hobbits from a new branch of the family tree, no matter how distant!''Ye look like a giant to me, but this other seems to be a friendly-enough sort! He says his name's Bingo, which does not sound like a name to me, but I suppose anything is possible.

'Are ye sure there's no giant in ye? Not even a bit? The Maer will want to speak to ye, at any rate.''I hope ye will excuse our Rhus. He seems naught but a bumpkin, but still he is dear to us, despite himself! He is a good enough fellow, if a little afeared of outsiders. He may call ye Duvodiad, but I can see ye're more than that! The Maer will be able to tell for sure.''Ye should speak to the Maer in the back of the village!''I extend ye the greetings of Maur Tulhau, ${PLAYER}. The folk of my village are most interested to hear what ye have to say of Bingo Boffin's journey. He could be one of us if he changed out of his strange garments and wore something more fit for the kinfolk of Maur Tulhau!

'We have proper traditions for the greeting of family gone long away and freshly returned. I think it would be suitable to greet Bingo with the traditional Welcoming Feast of the Stoors. It is no small thing, and we do not have the ingredients ready. If ye can acquire what we will need, we can set out the feast much more quickly!

'Speak to many of the villagers for some of the supplies, and search for others in the woods outside the village. There is much to be found before we can begin, and many dangers that ye might handle so we may dine in peace!''We have proper traditions for the greeting of family gone long away and freshly returned. I think it would be suitable to greet Bingo with the traditional Welcoming Feast of the Stoors. It is no small thing, and we do not have the ingredients ready. If ye can acquire what we will need, we can set out the feast much more quickly!

'Speak to many of the villagers for some of the supplies, and search for others in the woods outside the village. There is much to be found before we can begin, and many dangers that ye might handle so we may dine in peace!''There is to be a Welcoming Feast? What delightful news! We have had so few of them lately, and I tell ye that nothing brings the village together like a Welcoming Feast!

'Here, I will give ye some of my famous sprouted herbs for use in preparing the meal. They will add just the right kind of flavour as my folk enjoys, and I am sure Bingo will find it suitable as well!''A Welcoming Feast? I have just the thing to make it the finest such meal we have had in Maur Tulhau for some time: taters... giant ones!

'Take these fine specimens with ye, and they will be hearty enough to serve for many courses! A Welcoming Feast! I can hardly believe it!''I know why ye have come, ${PLAYER}.'

Caradog stares at you solemnly and produces a small tureen.

'The recipe for this gravy has been passed down in my family for generations, and no Welcoming Feast would be complete without it. It improves the flavour of whatever it touches: taters, meats, and crusty bread.

'Take it with my well wishes, and know that from this point forward, Bingo will measure every meal against this one... thanks to this gravy!''Our provisioner's name is Cadel Balchtrod, and he will know what to do with those ingredients!''No need to say anything more, ${PLAYER}. I have already been told about the Welcoming Feast, and preparations are already underway! These ingredients will be used for some of the later courses.

'A Welcoming Feast is no small thing, I tell ye true! The table has already been set down by the water to the east, and ye should wander yonder for its beginning!''A Welcoming Feast is no small thing, I tell ye true! The table has already been set down by the water to the east, and ye should wander yonder for its beginning!''I hope you don't mind, ${PLAYER}, but we may have started without you! I think there should be plenty to go around, though. Our new friends have said that a proper Welcoming Feast has been known to last for a full week!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Homesick and Just Sick' (Recommended Level: 93)</rgb>Gifemund is an astute merchant who has acquired a taste for collecting rare plants. With the struggles in Woodhurst coming to an end, he can once again enjoy the little things in life.Return to GifemundBingo has decided to throw a dinner party after all, but his invite list has expanded somewhat since he first had the idea.Bingo would like for you to deliver the invitations for his dinner party, if you have the patience to do so. He asked that you leave any invitations you do not deliver on the table near Postmaster Proudfoot by the Town Hole in Michel Delving.

Bert Bartleby should know where you need to bring the invitations.Deliver invitations to Myrtle Boggs, Merrimac Fairbairn, and Poppy BracegirdleDeliver invitation to Prisca BoffinDeliver invitation to Milo GoodbodyDeliver invitations to Haley and TheodoreDeliver invitation to Bilbo BagginsDeliver invitation to Spalvi Stout-heartDeliver invitation to LanghárDeliver invitation to EgwylfDeliver invitation to Humfrey RummingDeliver invitation to Iola IsbrunDeliver invitation to Willem WhiskerLeave any invitations you do not deliver on Postmaster Proudfoot's tableNow that the invites are out of your hands, it is time for Bingo to prepare for the party!Talk to Bingo inside his hobbit-hole in Michel DelvingBingo is inside his hobbit-hole in Michel Delving, north of the Town Hole.Talk to Bingo inside his hobbit-hole in Michel Delving'The invitations are all written, my adventuring friend, and now they need to be delivered! Let's see... I want to be certain I have not forgotten anyone.'

Bingo leafs through a stack of envelopes, reading aloud the name on each.

'Myrtle Boggs, Merrimac Fairbairn and Poppy Bracegirdle, my kind and caring neighbours. Cousin Prisca and her family. The talkative and friendly Milo Goodbody, of Bree. Haley and Theodore, of course! The author and literary critic Bilbo Baggins. Spalvi Stout-heart, if he can set aside his duties with the Iron Garrison for a short journey. Langhár the Beorning. Egwylf the tracker, of Rohan. Humfrey Rumming, if he promises to leave his worms and salamanders and other such creatures behind! Iola Isbrun and her family, as they are distant relatives by some reckoning or other, although I cannot figure the relation exactly. And I have an invitation here for Willem Whisker and his family as well... perhaps you could read it to him?

'I do not know exactly where you will be able to find each of my invited guests, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps you can ask Bert Bartleby? He tends to travel rather a lot, and might have seen some of these folk more recently than we have.

'If you get tired of delivering these invitations, I suppose we could rely on the Quick Post to do it. Leave any you do not deliver on the table near Postmaster Proudfoot, south of here by the Town Hole.''I want to be certain I have not forgotten anyone. Who am I inviting to my party?

'Myrtle Boggs, Merrimac Fairbairn and Poppy Bracegirdle, my kind and caring neighbours. Cousin Prisca and her family. The talkative and friendly Milo Goodbody, of Bree. Haley and Theodore, of course! The author and literary critic Bilbo Baggins. Spalvi Stout-heart, if he can set aside his duties with the Iron Garrison for a short journey. Langhár the Beorning. Egwylf the tracker, of Rohan. Humfrey Rumming, if he promises to leave his worms and salamanders and other such creatures behind! Iola Isbrun and her family, as they are distant relatives by some reckoning or other, although I cannot figure the relation exactly. And I have an invitation here for Willem Whisker and his family as well... perhaps you could read it to him?

'I do not know exactly where you will be able to find each of my invited guests, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps you can ask Bert Bartleby? He tends to travel rather a lot, and might have seen some of these folk more recently than we have.

'If you get tired of delivering these invitations, I suppose we could rely on the Quick Post to do it. Leave any you do not deliver on the table near Postmaster Proudfoot, south of here by the Town Hole.''What's this?'

You have clearly interrupted some gossip, likely about Bingo's long absence from the Shire, and Myrtle stares at you suspiciously. When you hand the invitations to Myrtle, Merrimac, and Poppy, they stare at the envelopes with surprised expressions. When they read the letters inside, their surprise increases further.

'Is this a trick? Bingo wishes to invite us to a dinner party? We have all been neighbours for some time now, and we have never attended a party at his home before! Now that I think about it, I suppose none of us have ever invited him over for tea or for dinner either.

'Do we think we will attend? I... I suppose we can try, but we are not promising anything! Good day!''Cousin Bingo is inviting my family to a dinner party? It has been ages since I visited his home, and that was many young hobbits ago! Is his home big enough to accommodate all of my children?

'We will certainly try to attend, ${PLAYER}. It is just as well, for I think Marigold was planning a secret adventure to the Shire anyway, and perhaps this will satisfy her journeying heart somewhat... for the moment!

'I could do with a fine dinner. Taking care of all these children is hungry work!''Pull up a chair, ${PLAYER}! There is room for all at the Prancing Pony!'

You hand Milo the invitation and he reads it with interest.

'Delightful! I am not one to turn down a free dinner, and I remember that Bingo was quite an amusing fellow. I hope he shows a little restraint this time, because I remember he spent some time recovering from the entertainment here among the haystacks by the stables!

'I will be happy to attend, even if folk in the Shire are a little odd... compared to the hobbits of Bree, at least!''We are back where we started, ${PLAYER}, and still trying to decide what we should do next. For now, we have mostly decided that the food here could be better and that Anlaf does not clean the mugs well enough.'

You hand the invitations to Haley and she reads it with a smile.

'A dinner party? We will certainly attend, won't we, Theodore? Yes, we will. It will be nice to see Bingo at his home.'

Theodore nods distantly, already thinking about his next adventure, but you are reasonably certain he will attend Bingo's gathering.'Oh, hullo there, ${PLAYER}! Welcome back to Rivendell!'

You hand Bilbo the invitation, and he reads it carefully.

'Bingo Boffin is having a dinner party? I am sure it will be a lovely time, but I am afraid I must decline his gracious invitation. I do not think I have as many journeys in me as once I did, and I am content to stay here in Rivendell for now. I will return to the Shire one day, I am sure of it, but I am not ready to say farewell to the Last Homely House just yet.

'Give Bingo my best wishes, and tell him that I am eagerly awaiting his book... when it is finished!''Welcome back to Khazad-dûm, ${PLAYER}! Do not tell me you have lost Bingo again, and need my help to find him?'

Spalvi laughs for a longer time than necessary. When he has finally stopped and apologized, you hand him the invitation. Ketill Map-maker, standing nearby, also takes an interest.

'I would be happy to attend Bingo's party! Ketill and I have been working to improve the Iron Garrison's foothold here in Khazad-dûm, but it is difficult work. There are many dangers still in the earth, and I am not sure we can master them. But we will try! That is the way of the dwarves!''What brings you here, ${PLAYER}? Is something wrong?'

You hand Langhár the invitation. As she reads it, her caution gives way to amusement.

'Bingo is organizing a dinner party? I hope he has not given up on his adventuring ways! I will do my best to attend, but that will depend upon whether the Easterlings seek to cross the Great River again. If they know what is best for them they will not attempt it, and I will be able to visit Bingo.''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}! How fares our friend Bingo?'

You hand Egwylf the invitation and she reads it carefully.

'A dinner party? This is not the time for parties, my friend. Rohan is at war! I will see what I can do, but I am afraid I cannot commit to such a journey. Whether I attend or not will depend upon the state of the war effort in Rohan.

'Even so, I thank Bingo for the invitation.''Bingo is hosting a dinner party? I receive so many invitations that it is hard to keep track of them all! I will do my best to attend. Perhaps I will give a speech or two before dinner. My speeches are always well-received, and is it not said that the gift of knowledge is a gift well-received? That settles it!

'Bingo will be honoured for me to attend, I am sure. You may count upon it, ${PLAYER}!''Ye have returned to Maur Tulhau, ${PLAYER}? Have ye decided to come live here? We can try and find ye a comfortable place to live! Perhaps we would see about opening a new neighbourhood for ye and Bingo!'

You shake your head with a smile and hand her the invitation.

'Well, this is exciting news as well! A dinner party? It is a long way from here, but I would like to see Bingo once again. I am also curious about the traditions of this faraway Shire! Tell him Iola will be happy to attend!'You feel a little silly, but Willem has always seemed to understand you and Bingo, so you carefully open the envelope and read the invitation to the lynx.

Willem's eyes seem to sparkle, but you can't tell for sure what it might mean.'I am so excited for this party, ${PLAYER}! I want everything to be perfect!

'I hope everyone I invited is able to attend! You did deliver all the invitations, right?'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'The Perfect Recipe' (Recommended Level: 93)</rgb>Gimli is interested in the stability of the fortifications at Helm's Gate, and wishes to hear your opinion.Fortifications can be found around the gate at Helm's Dike.

You should examine the various fortifications of the Rohirrim.Examine fortifications at Helm's Gate (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Examine the gateGimli stands at Helm's Gate in the southern Westfold.

You should speak with Gimli.Tell Gimli your observations of the fortifications at Helm's Gate'It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}! I know that you have acquired some expertise in your travels. Tell me: what do you think of these fortifications?

'The Rohirrim claim that none can break the wall, and the Hornburg will be the safest place in all of Rohan. I have my own thoughts on the matter.

'Will you have a look around and tell me what you think?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Well? What do you think, ${PLAYER}?'

You quietly relay your observations to Gimli.

'I could not have said it better myself. There is good, strong rock here, but it is not enough. I fear for these men, doughty though they are.

'Tonight I expect to make a great stand at the Hornburg, and if it is my last battle, I will take many an Uruk with me!'Legolas has seen the approach of the great host of Isengard, and wishes for you to look to the defences of the men stationed at the gate to Helm's Deep.Legolas stands at Helm's Dike, his gaze cast northward.

You should <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> to the north to get a look at the troubled plains.Legolas stands with the King's host at Helm's Gate.

You should return and speak with Legolas.Speak with Legolas about what you sawBarrels of arrows can be found lining the wall at Helm's Gate.

You should count the barrels to see if the archers at Helm's Dike have enough arrows to defend Helm's Deep.Count barrels of arrows stored at Helm's GateLegolas stands at Helm's Gate.

You should return and speak with Legolas.Return to Legolas'I am glad to see you again, ${PLAYER}, even at this dark hour. Have you seen what marches forth across the plains? This will be a fell night indeed. Will Rohan fall?'

Legolas looks out northward, his keen eyes searching long the plains of the Westfold. You should look for yourself.'Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Alas for the fair men and women of Rohan! Saruman's wrath approaches swiftly. You saw it too.

'It seems that Erkenbrand has left many archers to man the wall. I wonder how soon their arrows will be spent.''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Erkenbrand has left these men well-stocked, but it will not be enough. Perhaps the first line of enemy soldiers will fall, but more will take their place, and they will crash upon the Hornburg like the sea on a castle of sand.

'Alas for Rohan!'Aragorn has asked you to rally the despondent-looking men left by Erkenbrand to defend Helm's Gate on the eve of war.Men can be found along Helm's Dike, manning the gate to Helm's Deep.

Aragorn has asked you to cheer up the men.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: This quest requires emotes. You must select your target and type the correct "/emote" into the chat box.</rgb>Aragorn can be found at Helm's Gate in the southern Westfold.

You should speak with Aragorn.Return to Aragorn'Gamling has told us that Erkenbrand left many men to defend the dike, but that all are too old, like him, or too young.

'Will you cheer up the men? It is likely long since they have had a smile, a jest, or a sympathetic look. They know that they will likely not survive this night.''Have you ever looked out at the beauties of the plains, and known it was to be your last look at the grasses touching the sky? Nay, do not protest -- this is my last battle.

'I make my stand here, and I will not retreat. My old legs aren't much; they cannot march, and they certainly cannot run. Here I will stand until I die. I only hope to kill a hundred Uruks before I go.'

<rgb=#ffff00>You feel a swell of admiration for this brave man. You should honour him, and submit yourself to him as you would to a king.</rgb>'I -- I have never been in a battle before, but...m-my father has been in many. He is so fearsome that Orcs throw down their arms and surrender to him...so my grandmother tells me. My father is probably forcing Orcs to surrender near the fords right now!

'I do not think I am strong enough to kill many Orcs, but if I am fearsome enough, perhaps they will surrender to me. Do...do you think I am fearsome?'

<rgb=#ffff00>You should acknowledge that this young soldier is indeed fearful enough to force you to yield to him.</rgb>'However will I slay Orcs and Uruks when my hands will not stop shaking? I haven't felt calm since Gamling handed me a sword.

'I have never seen a battle before, and I am terrified. I cannot stop thinking about the blood, or the pain. Will I be able to act when I am needed? I am so restless, but I wish to be calm like my fellows!'

<rgb=#ffff00>You should try to ease this young man's fears, for tonight will be his first taste of battle.</rgb>'When I was a younger man riding out to my first war, a fellow soldier would sing before every battle, and our hearts were stronger when we rode. He had a fair voice and clear, and he sang of peace, war, life, and death.

'Nobody sings today. Everyone is silent and afraid. I long to hear a song, but I daren't raise my own voice. I am so tone-deaf that I am afraid Gamling would have me thrown off the dike!'

<rgb=#ffff00>This man longs for fair music to gladden his heart before the battle.</rgb>'Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''Yes?''You did a kind thing, to cheer up those men while your own heart probably needs cheering itself. It is weary work to give courage to others, ${PLAYER}.

'Alas that these troubled times must be ours! I only hope that we survive the night.'Bingo has perhaps enjoyed the Welcoming Feast of Maur Tulhau a little too much, and now he is feeling very poorly.Bingo has eaten too much at the Welcoming Feast, and has asked you to find a healer in Maur Tulhau who might have a remedy for him.Talk to Lili Gardhur, the healer in Maur TulhauTea plants can be found outside Maur Tulhau, in the Gloomglens.

Lili Gardhur gave you a knife and sent you to look for suitable tea plants outside Maur Tulhau.Lili Gardhur is in the central square of Maur Tulhau, in the Gloomglens.Bingo is in the north-east corner of Maur Tulhau, suffering from an unhappy stomach.

Lili Gardhur gave you some medicinal tea to bring to Bingo.Bingo and his companions are in the north-east corner of Maur Tulhau.Talk to Theodore Gorse in Maur TulhauTalk to Haley Meadowsweet in Maur TulhauTalk to Iola Isbrun in Maur TulhauBingo has finished the medicinal tea, and now you should see if he is feeling better.Talk to Bingo in Maur TulhauLili Gardhur is in the central square of Maur Tulhau.

Lili's medicinal tea did not seem to help Bingo's condition, and he asked you to see if she had any other remedies that might work instead.Talk to Lili Gardhur in Maur TulhauA hot pot of tea is brewing on the milestone in the centre of Maur Tulhau.

Lili Gardhur thinks that the other tea you brought to Bingo might have cooled too much to be effective, and she wants you to bring him another pot of tea with more haste.Pick up hot tea from the milestone in Maur TulhauBingo is in the north-east corner of Maur Tulhau, sipping the tea you brought to him.Talk to Bingo in Maur TulhauIola Isbrun is in the north-east corner of Maur Tulhau.Talk to Iola Isbrun in Maur TulhauBingo is in the north-east corner of Maur Tulhau.Talk to Bingo in Maur Tulhau'Oh... ${PLAYER}... where did the time go? It seems as if the Welcoming Feast just began, but it also feels as if it has been going on forever! The food has been so delicious, but... but...'

Bingo's face has gone quite green, and he does not look very happy.

'I fear that I may have... overindulged... rather a little. I... really do not feel very well. It's just that everything... everything looked so delicious, and there were so... so many courses. It would have been impolite not to eat my fill! Do you... do you think you could speak with... with a healer? There must be some remedy... that will help...''I fear that I may have... overindulged... rather a little. I... really do not feel very well. It's just that everything... everything looked so delicious, and there were so... so many courses. It would have been impolite not to eat my fill! Do you... do you think you could speak with... with a healer? There must be some remedy... that will help...''Yes? How can I help ye? Ah, Bingo did not understand the importance of pacing himself, did he? A Welcoming Feast is no small thing, as I am certain we told ye, time and time again!

'I can brew a tea that should help him to feel better, but my stock of tea leaves has gotten rather low. If ye will cut several flushes of tea for me, I can brew some for poor Bingo.

'Take this knife of mine and look for likely plants outside the village. Cut only from the top of the plants, about a hand-length down from the top. Three flushes of tea should be enough, the more fresh the better!''Look for likely tea plants outside the village. Cut only from the top of the plants, about a hand-length down from the top. Three flushes of tea should be enough, the more fresh the better!''Ah, ye found some! I will use these to replace the tea I brewed while ye were out. It has cooled some, but it should still serve the purpose!

'Take this to Bingo and have him drain it dry. He should start feeling better in only a little while.''Bring the tea I gave ye to Bingo and have him drain it dry. He should start feeling better in only a little while.''Oh, ${PLAYER}... I am glad you came back! I should not have eaten so much... I feel terrible, but worse: I have been a poor dinner companion for Iola and Haley and Theodore! Ooooh... I am quite filled to burst!

'Can you... handle the social pleasantries for now? I will sip the tea you have brought... and I hope it will help!''Can you... handle the social pleasantries for now? I will sip the tea you have brought... and I hope it will help!''The folk of this village have been very kind, but their Welcoming Feast has not been very kind to Bingo!

'Haley told me I needn't sample every dish that was put in front of me, and that advice has served me well! Can you imagine what might have happened if I had not gotten rid of the Rothstone? Some cruel food-related mishap, I am sure of it!''The food was very good, but I am no hobbit. I stopped eating days ago, and Bingo should have done the same!

'I think encountering that dragon might have knocked some sense into Theodore, because he actually seems to be listening to me now. He is fortunate, because otherwise he would surely be feeling as poorly as Bingo right now!''The Welcoming Feast is one of our oldest traditions, but this is the first we have had in many years. It has always served to bring my people closer together, and I am glad to share it with ye and Bingo!''I have finished the tea, but... it didn't seem to help...

'Can you go back and... see if there's anything else that might help? I know I should not have eaten so much... but it all looked so good...''Can you go back to Lili... and see if there's anything else that might help? I know I should not have eaten so much... but it all looked so good...''The tea did not make Bingo feel better? Hmm...'

Lili thinks for a moment, and then her eyes alight and she stabs the air with a triumphant finger.

'I think the tea was too cold, ${PLAYER}! I have a fresh pot of tea brewing on the milestone fire just there. If ye bring it to him and have him drink it before it cools, I think Bingo will find it the truest remedy for his ailment. But ye must be quick! The tea will cool rapidly, so ye will need to carry it swiftly to him!''Pick up that hot tea from the milestone and carry it to Bingo before it cools. I think that should cure his ailment!''Can you go back to Lili... and see if there's anything else that might help? I know I should not have eaten so much... but it all looked so good...''Did that tea help Bingo's ailment?''Ah, that is a little better. Yes, I think this tea is helping! I do not regret enjoying all those courses, but I think I am glad to see the end of the Welcoming Feast!

'It does put me in mind of my home, ${PLAYER}. I remember quiet evenings at my dinner table, setting into a modest dinner by candlelight. They were lonely meals, it's true, and I would not trade my new friends for anything, but it is comforting to think of the Shire. I wonder how the old place is managing?

'It feels as if we just arrived, and already I am thinking of leaving! I hope Iola will not find me discourteous!''It feels as if we just arrived, and already I am thinking of leaving! I hope Iola will not find me discourteous!''I am glad Bingo is feeling better! We have so few visitors to Maur Tulhau these days that I have only heard tales of Welcoming Feasts from my elder kin. Now that I have seen one for myself, I think that it is less the eating and more the recovery that brings our folk together!

'I love food, but whose idea was it to eat for a week? Some traditions might be a little excessive, if ye ask me! Could I ask ye one thing, ${PLAYER}? Please don't tell the Maer I said such to ye.

'We do not see much excitement around here. It is good to have met Bingo, and it is a pity the two of ye want to be leaving so soon.''We do not see much excitement around here. It is good to have met Bingo, and it is a pity the two of ye want to be leaving so soon.''Do you know something, ${PLAYER}? I think I may have a little more to eat before we set forth on the road again. That tea has settled my complaining belly somewhat, and this food was quite delicious, after all! Do you think perhaps the folk of Maur Tulhau have a Departing Feast to match their Welcoming Feast?

'Ah, do not look at me like that, my friend! I am only teasing! Well, mostly teasing. Mostly.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Next week: 'Through the Blockade' (Recommended Level: 93)</rgb>Agelwyn, the young wife of Thane Gladsúnu of Marton, is reluctant to lead her people in her husband's absence.Agelwyn can be found in the refugees' hiding spot north of Marton.

