<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of Helm's Dike, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'Helm's Dike' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>It is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Gamling is by the pile of straw on the lower level of the fortress, by the western wall.Talk to Gamling by the pile of straw near the western wallIt is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Gimli and Legolas are on the Deeping Wall. Gamling told you to take the eastern stairs up onto the wall in order to reach them.Talk to Gimli on the Deeping WallTalk to Legolas on the Deeping WallIt is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Aragorn wishes to speak with you atop the Deeping Wall.Talk to Aragorn on the Deeping Wall'I am no fool. Even if Saruman's forces were a quarter of the size the scouts say they are, I know we cannot hold the Dike indefinitely. But we will make them fight for it, will we not? Aye, we will! Come with me and let us stand against the vanguard!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of Helm's Dike. You can speak to Thenca for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of Helm's Dike, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'Helm's Dike' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'We cannot hold the Dike indefinitely. But we will make them fight for it, will we not? Aye, we will! Come with me and let us stand against the vanguard!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of Helm's Dike. You can speak to Thenca for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of Helm's Dike, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'Helm's Dike' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'Let us hurry to Helm's Dike! If we have victory there, perhaps we can slow or stop the advance of the Orcs!''You fought well, my new friend, and claimed the lives of many foes! The Dike is filled with the bodies of Orcs, but still others come, clambering onto the bank. We slowed them. That is what we sought to do. It was foolhardy to expect to do more. Take the eastern stairs and climb onto the Deeping Wall, ${PLAYER}. Your friends will be in the thick of the fighting before long, and they will want you by their side!''Your friends wait for you on the Deeping Wall. Go to them while there is still time! They may have need of you!''It has begun, friend! Now is the time for battle! The Orcs fill the gorge before the wall. Just look at them down there! I have never seen so many of the foul creatures in one place. There are Dunlending wild-men down there too. Every now and then I can hear one of their war-cries over the rest of the tumult.

'We must stand and fight!''The time passed slowly here atop the wall when the noise from the Dike reached us. I saw the Orc torches go out when they sought to pass the breach, and then scatter when they met resistance. You fought at the Dike? That was well-done, ${PLAYER}! It was a mere taste of what is to come on this night, but you and the men did what needed to be done: you taught our foes that we will not give them one foot more without battle.

'Now the battle is before the Deeping Wall, and it is a time for arrows and for stones! Gimli's axe thirsts for Orc-necks, but it will not thirst long!''Look at them down there! Ha! Come up here, you cowards!''Our foes are numerous, and now they are here. The next few hours will be trying indeed.''We may have a problem, my friend.'Aragorn is worried that the defenders of Helm's Deep will give in to despair now that they have seen the extent of Saruman's forces.It is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Aragorn is on the Deeping Wall, and wants your help preparing inspiring words for the beleaguered defenders.

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>Talk to Aragorn and help him inspire the defendersIt is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Aragorn is on the Deeping Wall, and wishes to speak with you now that you have done what you can to save the rapidly falling morale of the beleaguered defenders.Talk to Aragorn on the Deeping Wall'The size of the enemy force is greater even than I expected, ${PLAYER}, and I am no stranger to war. Many of the defenders here have not seen real battle, and those who have can count the number of skirmishes in which they have fought on a single hand.

'Now that the men have seen the vast extent of Saruman's armies of Orc and wild-man, will they give in to despair? Will they throw down their weapons and run from the battle, endangering their lives and all those who shelter behind them? They need to hear inspiring words, ${PLAYER}, and quickly, before their courage deserts them!

'Speak to me when you are ready to come with me to do what we may.''We must inspire the defenders to stand strong and fight against the overwhelming numbers we face, ${PLAYER}! We have little time with which to bolster their quailing hearts, but must do the best we can, and quickly!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Sometimes the right words at the right time can make all the difference, ${PLAYER}. The night is far from over, and Saruman's forces seem endless, but we have bolstered the hearts of the men that stand with us. That may prove the best weapon we wield atop this wall, my friend, stronger and sharper even than arrows or blades. We have sharpened the edges of the men who fight, the better to strike at their foes and ours!'Aragorn has decided to lead a sortie through the postern gate in the Deeping Wall, in order to strike against the forces of Saruman.It is after midnight at Helm's Deep. Rohirric Swordsmen can be found in the open space between the Hornburg and the Deeping Wall.

Aragorn has asked you to gather swordsmen to accompany him on a sortie beyond the wall.Find swordsmen and tell them to meet Aragorn at the postern gate (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})It is after midnight at Helm's Deep. Aragorn and Éomer are by the postern gate, below the north-west tower.

You have given word to several swordsmen that Aragorn plans a sortie through the postern gate, and should see if that be enough.Talk to Aragorn by the postern gate below the north-west towerIt is after midnight at Helm's Deep. Legolas is atop the Deeping Wall, accessible from ground-level by the eastern staircase.

Aragorn has sent you to watch over the battle alongside Legolas.Talk to Legolas atop the Deeping WallIt is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Deeping Wall. You can speak to Thurketel for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Deeping Wall, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'The Deeping Wall' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>Éomer is below the north-west tower, by the postern gate.

You have survived an attack on the Deeping Wall, and should see how Éomer and Aragorn fared in their sortie.Talk to Éomer by the postern gate'We need to set the forces of Saruman back on their heels, ${PLAYER}. Descend to the open space between the wall and the Hornburg, and speak with any swordsmen you see. Tell them to meet me by the postern gate. I wish to lead them on a sortie against the Orcs!''Find swordsmen willing to accompany me on a sortie against the Orcs. Tell them to meet me at the postern gate, if they are able and ready!''I will go with Aragorn! This is the hour! We will teach the enemy a lesson in steel!''These swordsmen will accompany me, ${PLAYER}, and Éomer has pledged his sword as well! But I wish for you to return to Legolas atop the wall and watch our sortie from above! There will be fighting enough for all of us, I do not doubt it!''Return to Legolas and assist him with the defence of the Deeping Wall! We will strike a blow against the Orcs from the postern gate, and may it prove a worthy distraction!''You have returned just in time, my friend! They prepare another assault!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Deeping Wall. You can speak to Thurketel for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Deeping Wall, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'The Deeping Wall' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'You have returned just in time, my friend! They prepare another assault!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Deeping Wall. You can speak to Thurketel for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Deeping Wall, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'The Deeping Wall' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'The Orcs are bringing up great ladders with which to scale the Deeping Wall! Prepare for battle!''We survived the assault, for now. Did Aragorn and Éomer fare as well as we did? Go to the postern gate in the north-west tower and see if their sortie led to victory.''Éomer is fortunate I was there!''We felt the blast from here, ${PLAYER}! I am glad you survived.

'Our own sortie did not go well. The causeway is hard-pressed, and the hinges and bars of the main gates have greatly suffered. We scattered a number of Orcs who carried rams, and the archers supporting them, but our victory was short-lived. As we attempted to return, I was tripped by Orcs lying in ambush, and it nearly spelled my end.'Saruman's attack on Helm's Deep continues, and the battle is beginning to take its toll on the fortress and on its defenders.It is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Gimli is by the postern gate, below the north-west tower.Talk to Gimli by the postern gateIt is after midnight at Helm's Deep.

Gimli has asked you to check on Gléowine by the map table west of the Hornburg.Talk to Gléowine by the map table west of the HornburgGléowine wants you to set the fallen barrel of weapons upright.

<rgb=#FF0000>Interacting with the tipped-over barrel near Gléowine will allow time to pass. Action will resume in the hours before dawn, with the next onslaught of the enemy.</rgb>Set the tipped barrel near the western wall upright, and time will passIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Théoden King is outside the Hornburg.Talk to Théoden outside the HornburgIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Deeping-coomb. You can speak to Háma for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Deep, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'The Deeping-coomb' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>In the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Aragorn is on the second level, outside the entrance to the Hornburg.Talk to Aragorn on the second level, outside the HornburgIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Aragorn wants you to hurry to the breach in the Deeping Wall, east of the Hornburg, and help the survivors in the aftermath of the blast.Find the breach in the Deeping WallDefeat attacking uruks at the breach (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to Sigferth by the breachIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Aragorn is on the second level, outside the entrance to the Hornburg.Talk to Aragorn on the second level, outside the Hornburg'The Orcs by the gate had feigned death, and as I passed by with Aragorn they leapt onto me, dragging me to the stones. I was overcome by their grasping arms, and would have perished were it not for my heroic friend here. I speak of Gimli, the staunchest dwarf I have ever met! I did not know he was with us in the battle, but at my time of need he charged from the shadows and felled the Orcs that pinned me.

'Speak to him and see if there is anything he lacks, and it will be his.''Speak to Gimli and let him know I am grateful for his assistance beyond the gate.''I followed Éomer and Aragorn not for hope of glory, but only to shake the sleep from my eyes! The wild-men seemed rather too large for me, so I sat beside a stone and watched their swordplay. When Éomer ran afoul of the Orcs I could not stand idle! If he wishes to repay me the favour, I am certain he will have opportunity enough as the night progresses!

'My count now is two! I must tell Legolas, and see how he fares. But he is a fighter. That minstrel of the King's, the one who travelled with you for a time... Gléowine? You should find him and tell him how the battle progresses. He will need to make a song of this, if we survive the night. Make certain he knows how I saved Éomer by the causeway! It would be a shame if he did not include a verse or three about that.''How does the King's minstrel fare? He is not a warrior like us, and we should keep his mood in mind.''How goes the battle? It is said that the finest warriors can tell the number and strength of an enemy force simply by listening to the sound of their cries and their weapons, but it is not so with me. I am no warrior, and to me the sound of battle is a tumult without meaning.

'The barrel of swords got knocked over in the confusion. Could you take some time to set it back upright? Then you should enter the Hornburg and speak with Théoden my master.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Interacting with the tipped-over barrel near Gléowine will allow time to pass. Action will resume in the hours before dawn, with the next onslaught of the enemy.</rgb>'Set the tipped barrel upright and gather up the fallen weapons.''The King is outside the Hornburg! Find him and see how the battle progresses from his vantage!''The night wears on, and the enemy shows no sign of relenting. We have lost many men on the wall, but for each loss we claim a dozen enemy lives. This is not a favourable exchange for us, ${PLAYER}, though it speaks well of our fighting prowess. We will run out of defenders well before Saruman's forces tire of the fight.

'We must defend the Deep! Rally our soldiers, and fight back for as long as you are able!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Deeping-coomb. You can speak to Háma for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Deep, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'The Deeping-coomb' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'We must defend the Deep! Rally our soldiers, and fight back for as long as you are able!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Deeping-coomb. You can speak to Háma for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Deep, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'The Deeping-coomb' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'I cannot leave the king's side, nor would I! But we have need of swords in the Deeping-coomb, ${PLAYER}! Go with Aragorn and keep the Orcs from passing through the hole in the wall! They may have brought more such blasting devices with them!''The Orcs brought foul sorcery of Saruman with them! The wall is breached!''That explosion destroyed much of the Deeping Wall! Many of our allies may be dazed or wounded by the blast, and even if they still survive they will not last long.

'We must get to the breach quickly! Make for the first level, and hurry east to the breach in the wall to do what you can!''Quickly, we must get to the breach! Make for the first level, and hurry east to the breach in the wall, there to do what you can!''The blast knocked me down, ${PLAYER}, even from such a distance. Béorcla and Fisc were not so fortunate. They were much closer. Foul craft of Saruman!

'We must retreat! There are too many, and they will be through the breach and upon us in no time!''You have to retreat! The Orcs have breached the wall!''I have still not found Gimli, but I heard tell that he might have been pushed back to the Glittering Caves during the fighting. He is a staunch fellow, but the fighting is very heavy around the caves.'Aragorn has heard that Gimli may have been pushed back into the Glittering Caves during the fighting, and wants you to find the dwarf and assist him.In the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Aragorn has asked you to find Gimli in the Glittering Caves. The entrance to the caves is south of the Deeping Wall, where the stream runs out of the mountains.Find Gimli in the Glittering CavesIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Gimli has been forced into the Glittering Caves during the fighting.Talk to Gimli in the Glittering CavesIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Gimli is in the Glittering Caves, and wanted to speak with you about the next phase of the battle.Talk to Gimli and secure the Glittering CavesIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Gimli is in the Glittering Caves, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Gimli in the Glittering Caves'I know that Gimli can handle himself in battle, but still I worry about him. If you can make your way to the Glittering Caves, I would ask that you do. Keep an eye on our friend. I fear that at times his desire for battle and the thirst of his axe may cloud his judgement. He may get in over his head.'

Aragorn laughs, an unusual sound over the din of battle.

'I ask that you do not repeat my unfortunate choice of words, ${PLAYER}. But if you do bring Gimli back to safety, I would be glad to relearn my manners at his request! The entrance to the caves is south of the Deeping Wall, where the stream runs down out of the mountains.''Find Gimli within the Glittering Caves. I hope my friend fares well in the battle. The entrance to the caves is south of the Deeping Wall, where the stream runs down from the mountains.''Did you find Gimli? I heard tell that he was pushed back into the Glittering Caves during the fighting, and I worry for his safety.''Look around you, my friend! Have you seen such a place? Éomer spoke at great length of the fields and grasslands of this kingdom, but he failed to speak of its greatest wonders. See how the cavern shines in the half-light? With a dozen of my people I could fashion such delights from the walls and ceilings that it would steal the breath of even Legolas, or any of his kin!

'But there is little time to admire the sights. There is fighting to be done! Those who are not warriors shelter now in the cavern, and we must protect them or none else will. Speak to me if you are ready to join the battle once again!''We must make safe these caves before the next attack begins. Come with me and we will ensure that the safety of this cavern is no phantom.''I do not like knowing that there are tunnels into the mountains through which our enemies may pass, but we cannot seal them. If the battle turns ill, we may need to bring as many folk as we can through those passages and out of the caverns. At least we have dealt with one threat, but on this night I will not be surprised if three more dangers leap to take the place now vacated by the one!'The battle has come to the Glittering Caves, and the people of Rohan that shelter there must be protected!In the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Glittering Caves. You can speak to Éomer for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Glittering Caves, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'Glittering Caves' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>In the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Gimli is in the Glittering Caves, and wishes to speak with you after pushing back the latest assault.Talk to Gimli in the Glittering CavesIn the hours before dawn, the forces of Saruman press their advantage.

Aragorn is on the second level, outside the Hornburg, and Gimli wants you to report to him of the dwarf's successes.Talk to Aragorn outside the Hornburg'The battle will soon be upon us! We must protect the folk that shelter here, or I will bring shame upon my name, that of my father, and that of my King. The Orcs and the wild-men have no place defiling this magnificent cavern, ${PLAYER}. Let us cast them out and send them howling back to their Wizard master!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Glittering Caves. You can speak to Éomer for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Glittering Caves, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'Glittering Caves' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'The battle will soon be upon us! We must protect the folk that shelter here, or I will bring shame upon my name, that of my father, and that of my King. The Orcs and the wild-men have no place defiling this magnificent cavern, ${PLAYER}. Let us cast them out and send them howling back to their Wizard master!'

<rgb=#00FF00>There are two ways to participate in the defence of the Glittering Caves. You can speak to Éomer for the Storied Tale, a story-focused experience. Or you can use the Epic Battles Panel for a more gameplay-intensive experience.</rgb>

<rgb=#FF0000>To participate in the defence of the Glittering Caves, open the Epic Battles Panel and select 'Glittering Caves' from the menu. After you successfully complete the defence, you will be returned to the location from which you entered it.</rgb>'That Dunlending chief thought to sneak into these caves with murder in his heart, but he has been repaid in kind. I am glad he is dead, but I fear other dangers may seek to ensnare the folk who shelter here.''Well done, well done! We have pushed them back for now. Return to Aragorn before we get hard-pressed again, and tell him that you found me in fine spirits, flush with the thrill of battle! I trust that you will find him the same. And keep an eye on Legolas, if you see him. I have not forgotten our little contest, and with the fighting in the caves I feel certain I must pass his tally.''Return to Aragorn before we get hard-pressed again, and tell him that you found me in fine spirits, flush with the thrill of battle! I trust that you will find him the same!''So Gimli lives and is in high spirits? That is good, but I fear that I do not share in his confidence. The fortress cannot stand much longer. We are nearly overrun.'The battle has been won, and now the defenders of Helm's Deep recover from their hurts and look to the future.Théoden King is to the south of Gandalf, on the field before the Hornburg, after sunrise.

Gandalf has given you a number of healing poultices and wants you to tend to the hurts of the King and his men.Talk to Théoden on the field of victory at Helm's Deep, after sunriseGandalf has asked you to bring healing poultices to injured men in Helm's Deep, and the King has asked you to bring his command to any men nearby who own swift horses.Issue Théoden's command to men who own swift horses (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Treat injured men with healing poultices (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Erkenbrand is on the field of battle outside the Hornburg, after sunrise.

Théoden asked you to find Erkenbrand on the field of victory and to thank him for his timely arrival.Talk to Erkenbrand on the field of victory outside the Hornburg, after sunriseErkenbrand is on the field of battle outside the Hornburg, after sunrise.

Théoden asked you to find Erkenbrand on the field of victory and to thank him for his timely arrival.Talk to Erkenbrand on the field of victory outside the Hornburg, after sunriseÉomer is on the field of victory outside the Hornburg, after sunrise.Talk to Éomer on the field of victory outside the Hornburg, after sunriseDéorwine is inside the fortress of the Hornburg, after sunrise.

Éomer thinks you should rest for a few hours inside the fortress of the Hornburg, before going with the King's company to the Ring of Isengard to confront Saruman.Talk to Déorwine inside the fortress of the Hornburg, after sunrise'The battle is won, my friend, and now we must look to the future! The design of Saruman has been ruined, not by any spell or magic, but by the stout hearts of the Rohirrim and the swift speed of Shadowfax!'

Gandalf notices your study of the curious trees beyond the Dike.

'Ah, that forest is no magic of mine, ${PLAYER}. A power much older than my own drives it to be here, and I could not have hoped for a more timely arrival than this!'

He hands you a number of healing poultices.

'Before we can proceed, we must attend to the hurts of those injured during the battle. These poultices will bring them some measure of relief. Speak with the King and see if he has need of such treatment, and then do what you can for the wounded.''You will find Théoden King to the south, enjoying the victory. Tend to his hurts, if he is injured, and heed any commands he may have.''We have seen victory this day, but our work is not ended. We have earned a brief respite only, and must take steps to ensure the safety of the Mark without much pause.'

The King sees the bundle of poultices in your hands and shakes his head.

'I am fine, ${PLAYER}. I have suffered bruises only, and the strength of my fathers has kept me from sustaining grievous injury. Many of my men will have need of that healing; bring those packets to them and see to their hurts. Look too for men with swift horses, and command them to ride for all corners of the Mark with news of what happened at the Hornburg. I want every vale in the Riddermark to have tidings of our victory, and to send their strongest fighters to Edoras by the second day of the full moon.

'After you have exhausted the poultices and the riders have set forth, find Erkenbrand and give him my thanks for his timely arrival. Without his men this sun might have risen on a different day for Rohan.''Many of our injured men have need of the poultices you carry. Bring them to those who do, and ease the sting of their wounds. Look too for men who own swift horses, and send them to every corner of the Mark with tidings of our victory here!

'I want every able-bodied warrior summoned to Edoras by the second day of the full moon. Our work here is only beginning, but it is far better to begin with a victory than from a defeat!''Find Erkenbrand on the field of victory and give him my thanks for his timely arrival. This victory would not have been possible without him.''This is a proud day for the Riders of the Mark, ${PLAYER}. We have wrested victory from the very claw of Saruman, even as he thought his triumph assured. The House of Eorl is stronger than he supposed, despite thrice-cursed Wyrgende and the whispers of frailty that have been uttered throughout the Mark. Théoden King has fight in him still, as does his sister-son Éomer!'

Erkenbrand strikes his shield with the side of his sword, and cries 'Victory for the Eorlingas!' a cry that is answered from others of the Rohirrim throughout the field. After a moment he turns again to you with a smile.

'We have put the Dunlendings to work, ${PLAYER}, hauling away the bodies of the Orcs and cleaning the field of battle. Any who take a solemn vow not to cross the Fords in war ever again will have the forgiveness of our King.''Éomer has indicated to me a desire to ride with the King to Isengard. He will wish for you to ride there as well.''Gandalf has decided to ride to Isengard to confront Saruman, and my uncle has determined he should ride with him. Some others will go as well, but first we have need of rest. It will be a few hours before we ride for the Ring of Isengard and its evil master, and all of us toiled greatly during the night.

'Find Déorwine inside the Hornburg. He has begun to arrange bedrolls for those seeking to rest for the journey.''Find Déorwine inside the fortress of the Hornburg. He will arrange a place for you to rest before the company sets forth for Isengard.''We spoke yesterday, around sunset, do you remember? It feels a lifetime ago, ${PLAYER}!'Sometimes the best allies are those that arrive when they are not expected.Déorwine is inside the keep of the Hornburg, after sunrise, at Helm's Deep.

Déorwine is waiting for your word, and then he will prepare a place for you to rest before setting out for Isengard with the King.Talk to Déorwine and rest before setting out with the companyHalbarad is on the road beyond the Fords of Isen.

You have both been through much, and Halbarad greatly wishes to hear what has transpired since last you spoke.Aragorn is beyond the Fords of Isen, and wishes to speak with you.'The King and some others of his guard plan to ride for Isengard, but first they will take some hours rest. You should do so as well, ${PLAYER}.

'Speak to me if you are ready and I will prepare a place for you.''I will show you to a quiet corner of the keep where you can rest, my friend. And then it is my understanding that you will set forth with the King for Isengard?''Hold on a moment, friend. Did you sleep too long? The company has already departed for the Fords of Isen!'''This is an unexpected joy, my friend!''I believed we would see each other again, my friend, but I did not know it would be so soon! Sending you from us was not an easy decision, but I knew Théodred had need of your aid. What sadness that he could not be saved from that fate, but perhaps that too contributed to the resolve of the Eorlingas.

'The Grey Company has done what it set out to do; we have ridden to our chieftain in Rohan, and it seems we have come in time, after all. Some of our number were lost on the way, but those of us that remain will do everything in our power to ensure that Aragorn can fulfill his destiny.

'I am glad to see you again, ${PLAYER}.''I am delighted to see you again, my friend! The Grey Company has accomplished this first leg of its journey, and now we turn to our new purpose!''This is an unexpected joy, my friend! Had I known they were journeying to find me...'

Aragorn looks at you wryly.

'When you spoke to me of the Dúnedain, I did not suspect you had travelled with them in recent days, ${PLAYER}. I do not think I would have changed my course had I known, but perhaps that is the power of hindsight. Perhaps it is the wisdom of Elrond behind your silence, my friend. He is never quick to volunteer information, which makes the word of his sons even more curious.

'I have much to think about, and little time in which to do so!'Many purposes now collide within the Ring of Isengard.Aragorn has asked you to ride along the road to Isengard, and to keep your eyes peeled as you go.Ride along the road north to Isengard, and remain alertGandalf stands by the Pillar of the Hand, on the way to Isengard.Talk to Gandalf by the Pillar of the HandGandalf has warned you that you might not recognize Isengard since last you saw it.Ride north to the gate of IsengardYou have found Merry and Pippin at the gate of Isengard.'I spoke briefly with Halbarad, and if I had business within Isengard before now I count it double. We must find if Lothrandir still lives within the Ring, ${PLAYER}. Let Gandalf speak with Saruman for weeks, if that is his desire; my thoughts are of my kinsman. I have not seen him for many years, but Lothrandir has been a friend for longer than that and I will not abandon him.

'Let us ride along the road to Isengard! I am most curious to see what it is we will find.''We have several purposes now within the Ring of Isengard. May they all come to successful conclusion!

'Ride north along the road to Isengard, and stay alert! We do not know what we will find as we approach Saruman's lair.''We have several purposes now within the Ring of Isengard. May they all come to successful conclusion!

'Ride north along the road to Isengard, and stay alert! We do not know what we will find as we approach Saruman's lair.''Isengard has been much changed since last you beheld it, ${PLAYER}. You may not recognize it. I dare say Saruman himself does not, though he brought it upon himself.''Isengard has been much changed since last you beheld it, ${PLAYER}. You may not recognize it. I dare say Saruman himself does not, though he brought it upon himself.''Well hello there!''Look who it is, Merry!''Isengard has been much changed since last you beheld it, ${PLAYER}. You may not recognize it. I dare say Saruman himself does not, though he brought it upon himself.''Hello there!''Hello there! It's been a long time, ${PLAYER}! I hope you are well.'Merry and Pippin have made themselves quite at home at Isengard, but much of their story is still a mystery to you.Merry is outside the gate of Isengard.Merry asked you to go inside the gate-house and bring out some more of the pipe-weed they found there.Enter the gate-house and look for the pipe-weed Merry mentionedMerry asked you to go inside the gate-house and bring out some more of the pipe-weed they found there.Enter the gate-house and look for the pipe-weed Merry mentionedMerry asked you to go inside the gate-house and bring out some more of the pipe-weed they found there.Aragorn is outside the gate-house at Isengard, and wears a concerned expression.'The look on your face is quite a thing to see, my confused friend! Welcome to Isengard! You have had a number of adventures, I expect, and would you believe that we have as well? We have all come a long way from Lothlórien, haven't we?

'Much of the trip was rather unpleasant, owing to our new friends Uglúk and Grishnákh. I suppose they're old friends now; we shan't be seeing them again, and that is a relief. Treebeard and Quickbeam are nice fellows, if a little odd. And I do believe I have grown several inches taller!'

Pippin laughs at your confused look.

'I don't mean metaphorically, ${PLAYER}, though perhaps I have at that. I mean that I actually have grown taller!''Merry and I have been through a great deal. And I really am taller now! Can you not tell?''I think Pip is trying to confuse you on purpose, ${PLAYER}. He gets the chance to do that so infrequently that he delights in it whenever the opportunity presents itself.

'It seems we have gone from the fire back into the frying pan, for once! Pip and I were dragged halfway across Rohan by our captors, and though we have arrived at Isengard, it was in the strong hands of a friend and not at the whips of Saruman's Orcs! It was Treebeard who brought us here, you see, and he is... well, he is Fangorn and Fangorn is he, apparently!

'This is more difficult than I expected when I began to tell it! We found some pipe-weed inside the gate-house. How about you bring some more out here and I will try to tell it properly?''We found some pipe-weed inside the gate-house. Go inside and bring some more of it out here, and I will try to tell you the tale properly.''We found some pipe-weed inside the gate-house. Go inside and bring some more of it out here, and I will try to tell you the tale properly.''Would you go into the gate-house and bring out some more of the pipe-weed we found? It reminds me of the comforts of home, even in such a place as this!'Pippin smiles at you amiably.Aragorn nods at you, but says nothing.'Ah, very good, my friend! This is a very high-quality pipe-weed of the sort they grow in the Southfarthing, back home. Allow me to refill my pipe and I can resume my story. I was going to tell you about Treebeard and the Ents, I believe. Let me see...

'But it seems that Aragorn is troubled about something. Perhaps you should speak with him first, and I will continue my story when he has a happier look about him!''Aragorn does not look pleased. To be fair, he often looks this way, but I suspect he is less pleased than usual!''Strider always looks so grim.''That pipe-weed was not grown at Isengard, and it does not bear a resemblance only to that of the Southfarthing. That weed has come from the Shire, which means that Saruman has had his eye upon that land for some while, at least. I do not like that at all.

'If our fortune holds, the Wizard will have no further chance for mischief. His interest in the Shire may have extended only to its pipe-weed.'Aragorn is concerned that Saruman may have further tricks up his sleeve.Gandalf is at the base of Orthanc, considering what to do about the Wizard waiting within.Talk to Gandalf by the base of OrthancTreebeard is by the base of Orthanc.

Gandalf has asked you to speak with Treebeard while he considers what to do about Saruman.Talk to Treebeard at the base of OrthancTreebeard believes you should search within the Ring of Isengard for any sign of your captive friend.Explore west of OrthancExplore south of OrthancExplore north of OrthancGandalf is at the base of Orthanc, waiting to hear what you discovered in your search of the flooded Ring.Talk to Gandalf at the base of Orthanc'Saruman seems to be in a tough position, but I would counsel caution in our dealings with him. I do not doubt his ability to strike even now, and a cornered beast can still do great harm.

'Gandalf went ahead to the base of Orthanc. Find him and see what he thinks of the situation.''Find Gandalf and see what he thinks we should do about Saruman. He went on ahead, to the base of Orthanc.''Hrum, hoom, hoom.''Aragorn is right to advise caution. Saruman has suffered a defeat, it is true, but we must not be over-confident or it will be no lasting defeat. He is cunning and full of tricks, and even a cornered beast can do great harm. Indeed, that is when it is most dangerous!'

You tell Gandalf that Aragorn said much the same, and the Wizard smiles briefly.

'Good! It seems he has learned something from me after all. But this is no time for idle talk. There is a Wizard to manage now. Speak to Treebeard. I must think on our next step.''For now I must think. Leave me in peace!''Hrum, hoom, hoom. The work of the Ents here is done, but Gandalf's work is only beginning. Yours too, perhaps. I do not understand this desire for... burárum... adventure. Do you not enjoy standing in a green field with the sunlight on your face? Do you not enjoy drinking deeply of cold mountain waters?'

Treebeard rumbles for a moment.

'It is no matter. The waters will cleanse this place, but some Orcs may still linger. Search the surface and see what you find. Gandalf tells me a friend of yours was imprisoned here. I hope you find some sign of him. We Ents allowed a number of Dunlendings to leave in peace, but saw no other friendly folk.''Search the surface and see what you find. Gandalf tells me a friend of yours was imprisoned here. I hope you find some sign of him. We Ents allowed a number of Dunlendings to leave in peace, but saw no other friendly folk.''For now I must think. Leave me in peace!''This sight fills me with sorrow, ${PLAYER}. Do you think Saruman understands what he has taken from this world? He must not. How could he?'

Calenglad looks around at the still waters.

'I heard Gandalf say that Ents are slow to be roused, but when they are there is little that can stand before them. I see it is true, but my heart goes out to this one, who will not return from this ring of stone.''You... filth. Come to finish the job? My time is through... curse the trees!'

The Orc coughs again, and a deathly rattle passes his lips. He will not last long. Despite the pain he feels, the Orc musters enough strength to laugh coarsely.

'You think... only my kind perished... when the flood came? There were prisoners... in the pits... and now they are drowned rats! Their lives... are on your conscience... wretched ${RACE}!''I am saddened by the passing of Beechbone. He was a handsome Ent, and took good care of his flock. He did much good in his life, and no less than on his last day, when he trumpeted victory within the stone walls of Isengard.'Gandalf listens to your account closely, especially the last words of the dying Orc.

'It is said that the last words spoken by the dying are the truest you will ever hear, and I do not doubt that this Orc believed them. But I do not.'Gandalf does not believe that Lothrandir perished beneath Isengard, and wants you to search for him.An entrance to the Isengard Depths can be found north of the tower.

Gandalf believes Lothrandir may still live, and wants you to search for news of him in the caverns below Isengard.Find the entrance to the Isengard Depths, north of OrthancGolodir also searches the Isengard Depths for news of Lothrandir.Talk to Golodir in the Isengard DepthsSurly scavengers can be found throughout the Isengard Depths.

Golodir has asked you to defeat the scavengers that lurk within the flooded depths of Isengard, for one of them might know of Lothrandir's location, if the Ranger still lives.Defeat surly scavenger (${NUMBER}/6)Surly scavengers can be found throughout the Isengard Depths.

Golodir has asked you to defeat the scavengers that lurk within the flooded depths of Isengard, for one of them might know of Lothrandir's location, if the Ranger still lives.Defeat surly scavenger (5/6)Golodir is in the flooded depths beneath Isengard, waiting to hear the results of your search.'An Orc gains nothing by being truthful to us, but may derive satisfaction by bringing us sorrow. I believe Lothrandir survives, but if he is not brought into the light soon it may become too late to save him. North of the tower you will find an entrance to depths below Isengard. Search the watery deeps for some news of Saruman's captive. He may yet live, if we hurry.''I believe Lothrandir survives, but if he is not brought into the light soon it may become too late to save him. North of the tower you will find an entrance to depths below Isengard. Search the watery deeps for some news of Saruman's captive. He may yet live, if we hurry.''I believe Lothrandir survives, but if he is not brought into the light soon it may become too late to save him. North of the tower you will find an entrance to depths below Isengard. Search the watery deeps for some news of Saruman's captive. He may yet live, if we hurry.''If Lothrandir is down here, we will find him, ${PLAYER}. This pit has seen better days, but it will see worse still if our friend has come to harm. What few of Saruman's lackeys still lurk in the depths no longer carry out his will. They seem to be busy scavenging for treasure and fighting among themselves o'er it. Fools!

'Many doors remain locked to us, and it could be one of these scavengers has picked up the key to whatever cell holds our friend. I will keep searching, as will others of the company, and you should do the same! Defeat these scavengers and see what you can find.''One of these scavengers may have picked up the key to whatever cell holds our friend, if one does. I will keep searching, as will others of the company, and you should do the same! Defeat these scavengers and see what you can find.''One of these scavengers may have picked up the key to whatever cell holds our friend, if one does. I will keep searching, as will others of the company, and you should do the same! Defeat these scavengers and see what you can find.''You found a key? Very good, very good. It is possible it will open the way to Lothrandir. I have found half a dozen keys already, and matching doors for half of those, but still no sign of my kinsman. Still, one of these may prove to be the one.'Halbarad has found a number of locked doors behind one of which Lothrandir might be held.Halbarad is in the Ring of Isengard, north-west of Orthanc, looking for Lothrandir.

Golodir has asked you to return to the surface and to help Halbarad with his search for the captive Ranger.Talk to Halbarad north-west of OrthancHalbarad is in the Ring of Isengard, north-west of Orthanc, looking for Lothrandir.

Halbarad is waiting to go with you to search further buildings in Isengard for Lothrandir.Talk to Halbarad and continue his search for LothrandirHalbarad is north-west of Orthanc.Talk to Halbarad north-west of Orthanc, within the Ring of Isengard'I have exhausted most of the locked doors here in the depths, ${PLAYER}. Return to the surface and find Halbarad. There are some buildings in the wall to which he has not yet gained entry, and these keys may unlock some of those.

'Before I left him, he said he was planning to search the buildings north-west of the tower. That is where you should seek him.''Return to the surface and find Halbarad. There are some buildings in the wall to which he has not yet gained entry, and these keys may unlock some of those.

'Before I left him, he said he was planning to search the buildings north-west of the tower. That is where you should seek him.''Golodir sends more keys? Very well. There are doors enough, that is certain. Lothrandir must be behind one of them. Speak to me when you are ready to search another building in the wall for my lost kinsman.''Are you ready to continue the search for Lothrandir? We must remain hopeful, ${PLAYER}, though the search has been fruitless to this point. There are still many buildings in Isengard where he may be held, and we will search them all if we must.''I cannot overstate my joy at Lothrandir's safe return, ${PLAYER}! I do not forgive Gun Ain her crimes, but I hope that the new woman that is Mercy finds some measure of peace. She has no place in Isengard any longer, and the path she takes from here will determine whether she ever finds another.'Lothrandir has been rescued from the clutches of Saruman, but what has become of the Wizard himself?Aragorn is on the stairs at the base of Orthanc.Talk to Aragorn at the base of OrthancGandalf, Théoden, and Pippin all witnessed the confrontation with Saruman and wish to speak with you about the Wizard's schemes.Talk to Gandalf on the steps of OrthancTalk to Théoden on the steps of OrthancTalk to Pippin by the foot of the stepsHalbarad has come to the base of Orthanc.Talk to Halbarad by the base of Orthanc'Lothrandir suffered greatly at the hands of his captors, but his will remains strong, and I am certain he will recover from their torments. But what has become of Saruman? A crowd gathers by the foot of Orthanc. Go learn from them what has happened, and I will rejoin you shortly!''A crowd has gathered at the foot of Orthanc. Go there and see what has happened, and I will rejoin you shortly.'Aragorn listens to your account of Lothrandir's rescue.

'I am pleased to hear my kinsman is safe! I wished often that my friends might be close at hand, but I did not give voice to that desire, for I would not have wanted them put in danger. It is well that Lothrandir has emerged from the pit of Isengard unharmed.

'Saruman emerged too, but not for long, and though he has returned to the tower I feel that his threat is ended! Gandalf will tell you more, if you would listen. I suspected some further mischief of Saruman, and I was not wrong to expect it! But all is ended now, and safely.''Gandalf will tell you more of what happened. I suspected some further mischief of Saruman, and I was not wrong to expect it! But all is ended now, and safely.''Saruman has come to the end of his power, ${PLAYER}. I think he will no longer be a danger to Rohan.'

Gandalf sighs heavily, and you are surprised to see a deep sadness in his eyes.

'I offered him his freedom, but after working such deceits as he has done for so long he did not see that it was an offer made in good faith. The treacherous see only treachery, and so it was with Saruman at the end. He has his life, but I fear he will not see this as a mercy. I have cast him from the order and from the White Council. His staff is broken, and he will work no further plots.

'But he has his life. He will remain within Orthanc, and think on his old designs, and mourn. But he will no longer endanger Rohan, and I think he knows that if Mordor does have victory in the days ahead, the servants of that land will not forget the treacheries he worked on his own behalf.''The Riddermark has suffered greatly at the hands of Saruman, but it will suffer no longer. He is locked in his tower, and Gandalf has cast him from the Order of the Wizards.

'The traitor used one last weapon, and it may have been his deadliest: the power of his voice. I know the extent of his crimes, and even so I nearly succumbed to the soothing persuasion of his words. We are fortunate Gandalf was here and could shatter the reverie.''The most peculiar thing happened, ${PLAYER}. Saruman and Gandalf engaged in a debate a few moments ago, and just when Gandalf seemed to get the upper hand, this object came hurtling down toward us from an upper window! It hit no one, thank goodness, but it would have quite hurt had the aim been better.

'It was a dark globe of crystal, but with a fiery core deep in its heart. I picked it up and it seemed much heavier than its size. Very odd. I carried it up the steps to give it to Gandalf, but when he saw what I bore he hurried down and took it from me quickly. He says it was thrown not by Saruman but by Gríma Wormtongue, who I had the distinct displeasure of meeting just yesterday.

'The globe is a mystery. I wonder what it was?''Saruman's power is at an end. He will do us no more harm this day.''Gandalf says that Saruman can do us no more harm, but I do not know if he is right.''I cannot help but wonder about that dark crystal globe. What was it, do you think?''Is it true? The threat of Isengard has ended? Saruman has occupied our thoughts for so long, and caused so much hardship, I never believed he would not!

'We have come so far, ${PLAYER}: from Rivendell, through Hollin, into Enedwaith and Dunland, and here at last to the stronghold of Saruman. Many of my kin fell along the way, but each of them, all of them, were allies of the king to come: Aragorn, son of Arathorn, heir of Isildur.

'You, too, have been a friend of Aragorn, and to all the Grey Company. I thank you, ${PLAYER}. Wherever our road leads now, know that you have my thanks and my friendship.'The enemy plans to breach the Hornburg through a gate-house being used as a makeshift infirmary for the wounded. The wounded men and the supplies of bandages and medicines are in danger, but the door to the Hornburg must be protected at all costs. The wounded will cry out when they begin to succumb to injuries, but you may instruct them in healing.

