'We need words of inspiration to raise the hopes of these defenders and give them the strength to fight. Gather the defenders on the wall and send them here, and I will do what I can to inspire them.''Thank you for sending them to me, friend Captain. Now we must fill them with hope and with the fire of battle!

'What should I say to them, ${PLAYER}?''Thank you for sending them to me, my friend. Now to fill them with hope and with the fire of battle!''You choose your friends well, ${PLAYER}. This Dúnadan has me believing we might survive this night, after all!

'I am ready for Saruman's armies! Indeed, I feel like I could teach the Wizard himself a lesson with my blade, a lesson so sharp he would soon regret waging this war!

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. My heart stirs for battle!'Gandalf has unsettled business with Saruman, and the company rides with him to Isengard.Aragorn has turned back to hear your opinion of the mysterious wood through which the company now travels.Talk to Aragorn on the road through the mysterious woodLegolas has dismounted and is staring into the trees.Talk to Legolas on the road through the mysterious woodThéoden is on the road through the mysterious wood. He has never seen Ents before, and the sight has filled him with wonder.Talk to Théoden on the road through the mysterious woodGandalf is on the road, waiting to proceed.Talk to Gandalf on the road through the mysterious woodThe company continues toward Isengard.Journey with the company beyond the Fords of IsenAragorn has slowed beyond the Fords. He wishes to speak with you.Talk to Aragorn beyond the Fords of IsenÉomer has heard a horseman approaching, and wants to speak with you.Talk to Éomer on the roadHalbarad and the Grey Company have accomplished their mission, and Halbarad wants to speak with you.Talk to HalbaradMany of your friends among the Grey Company wish to speak with you before continuing on.Talk to GolodirTalk to CalengladTalk to RadanirTalk to CorunirAragorn is speaking with Halbarad past the Fords of Isen.Talk to Aragorn'I feel confident that we can accomplish anything, ${PLAYER}, now that my kinsmen are here. Can it be that the tides turn in our favour this day?''I hope you found rest before we set out, ${PLAYER}. I tried to do so, but I could not quiet my roving mind, and I am still greatly fatigued. What will we find in Isengard?'

Aragorn looks from side to side at the towering trunks of the forest.

'These trees were not here when we arrived at Helm's Deep yesterday. I think we both know what brought them here, to the foot of the Dike, but that makes it no less frightening a sight, does it?''${PLAYER}, wait! Do you hear that? I hear a great sound of creaking timbers and rustling branches. It comes this way!''I never believed I would see such things, ${PLAYER}. Ents! I am filled with wonder at the sight, wonder of a kind I thought was the sole reserve of children. I am an old man, a white-beard of many winters, and still I have seen things this day I would not have believed yesterday.

'They tower above mortal men, and cover such a span with each stride! What damage they could do to the homes of my people, if that was their thought! But I do not believe that is their desire.''These Ents are herdsmen, not warriors. The people of Rohan need not fear them, as long as they cause no harm to their flock. There is one in this land that would have done well to heed the Ents before now; if he had heeded them the battle that is lately ended might have come to a different conclusion.

'It is good for Rohan, and for all free folk, that he did not take them into account. Let us proceed, ${PLAYER}. There are still many miles to go before we reach Isengard.''Many good men fell at the Fords, but their kingdom still stands. We are almost to Isengard, and will bring report of their valiant sacrifice to the Wizard who forced its need.

'Let us rejoin our companions, ${PLAYER}.''I hear the sound of swift hoof-beats approaching from behind us. Let us confront this rider before he overtakes us and offers some harm to the King!''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}! I knew in my heart we would see you once more, but I did not think to find you in the company of my chieftain Aragorn! Much has happened since we parted, and little of it good, but we will need to speak of our respective deeds when there is time to breathe.

'We have found Aragorn between perils, it seems. He had your help for the first, and he has all of us for the second. May it be enough!''We are well-met once again, ${PLAYER}, but in hardship. I sought the treacherous leader of the Falcon Clan, but he gave me the slip and escaped into the mountains. He lives still the life of a coward.'

You tell Golodir that Lheu Brenin's flight brought him into your path, and he has been slain.

'You tell me true? Then he has paid for his crimes. He abandoned some of his own people when he fled, and we left them in a place of peace, where they should be safe. I worried that their former chieftain might seek them out and visit upon them further harm, but even Saruman's magic cannot help Lheu Brenin now. I am satisfied, ${PLAYER}. Thank you, on behalf of my fallen kinsmen and also for those of his own people he betrayed.''It was not a long good-bye after all, ${PLAYER}, though the intervening days have been eventful. I think Halbarad keenly felt the burden of his duty after you left us, for he was not sure how best to proceed. But it seems he chose a-right, for we have found Aragorn as the words of Galadriel bade us.

'The sons of Elrond bear messages for him from their father, and Halbarad carries the gift of Arwen. I trust that Aragorn will know what to do with each of these gifts.''It seems you found Aragorn before we did, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps we should have gone with you.''This is truly an unexpected encounter, ${PLAYER}! I am glad to see you are well, and in the company of Aragorn, no less! Our errand has not ended, though its first goal be now accomplished. Now we must lend what aid we can to Aragorn.

'Is it true you take the road to Isengard? Whatever other objective Aragorn has with Saruman, we must add this one as well: the rescue of Lothrandir my kinsman, if he yet lives!''Halbarad has given me much to think about, ${PLAYER}. We have more reasons to converse with Saruman than I believed.'You have joined with Burnoth in remembering Léofdag's valour at the Battle of the Hornburg.The armies of the White Hand are attacking the Hornburg. You should speak at once with Burnoth.Talk to BurnothThe forces of the White Hand are attacking the Hornburg. Aid the Rohirrim against their enemy.Defend the Hornburg against the White HandThe forces of the White Hand are attacking the Hornburg. Aid the Rohirrim against their enemy.Defend the Hornburg against the White HandThe forces of the White Hand are attacking the Hornburg. Aid the Rohirrim against their enemy.Defend the Hornburg against the White HandYou should speak with Burnoth at once.Talk to Burnoth'Hurry, my friend...the wall is breached, and we must defend the Hornburg! Enemies are making for the Glittering Caves of Aglarond!''Alas, my good friend and retainer has fallen...but with his sacrifice, he proved that any accusations against his honour were without base. Let us take his body from this place, lest it be forgotten upon the field....'You must seize the Hornburg and drive out the Men!The Uruk-commander stands near the ugly gate up ahead.

You should ask the boss when the battle will be over, for you grow bored without a drop of blood to stain your sword.Ask the boss when the battle will be doneYour place in line is back a ways, near some other Orcs.

You should get back in line.Get back in line and talk to your neighbourYour place in line is back a ways, near some other Orcs.

You should watch and wait.Watch and waitYour place in line is back a ways, near some other Orcs.

You should speak with your neighbour.Talk to your neighbour againYour place in line is back a ways, near some other Orcs.

You should watch and wait.Watch and waitYou must try to escape!

Run south, towards Helm's Deep.Try to escape southYou must try to escape!

Run north-west, towards the Fords of Isen.Try to escape towards the north-westYou must try to escape!

Run east, along the dike.Try to escape towards the eastYou must try to escape!

Run north...into the strange trees.Run north!Find a resting spot at the edge of the trees.Find a spot to rest, and rest briefly at the edge of the treesTry to survive!Escape through the forest!'The night is almost over, but we'll take 'em. These horsemen have no friends to save them.

'We'll get over the walls and eat the hearts of every last one of them. Today we feast.

'Now get back in line, worm!''What do you think you're getting out of line for? It's not our turn yet. It'll never be our turn.

'The boys in front will do all the killing, and our swords don't even have a drop of blood.

'This ain't what I came for!''Let me at 'em! What do you say, you brute? Let's charge!

'Wait. What's...what's that?'You have agreed to search for Lothrandir in some more of the buildings in the wall of Isengard.The Grey Company has separated into groups in order to search more of Isengard for their captive kinsman.Search the flooded pits for some sign of the captive LothrandirHalbarad has found a large room, its floor covered with dirty water, and has called for you to join him.Talk to Halbarad in the large chamberLothrandir stands before you, a shadow of his former self.Talk to Lothrandir in the large chamberGolodir has found his way to the large chamber, and his anger is evident.Talk to Golodir in the large chamberHalbarad has determined what should be done.Listen to Halbarad determine what must be doneThe Rangers have lost a kinsman and a friend, and are sharing a moment of grief before they must return to Aragorn with the sad news.Allow the Rangers time to mournGun Ain has given voice to something remarkable. Can it be true? Does Lothrandir still live?Talk to Gun Ain in the large chamberThe woman named Mercy has told you where you might find Lothrandir: in the southernmost passage of the building in which you find yourself. You must search the building for him, and quickly!Find Lothrandir in a cell off the southernmost passage of the buildingLothrandir is in a cell unlocked by Gun Ain's key, off the southernmost passage.Talk to Lothrandir in his cellHalbarad has reunited with his lost kinsman.Talk to Halbarad outside Lothrandir's cell'Let us leave this sunken pit, ${PLAYER}. I will be glad to see daylight again, and I know Lothrandir must feel the same!''I came to this room from another passage, ${PLAYER}. It seems that the wall of Isengard is a warren of rooms and tunnels, but we will search them all for my kinsman. Saruman cannot hide him from us forever.'

Halbarad stops speaking and listens for a moment to the steady dripping of water in a distant corner.You see no recognition in Lothrandir's eyes. He shakes and convulses before you, a pitiable figure that bears little resemblance to the strong-willed man you remember. Of all Saruman's crimes, this may be the one that strikes nearest to your heart.

He seems to be trying to speak, but his words are muffled and his voice weak.'What sorcery have they worked on my kinsman? Lothrandir is a shell of himself, ${PLAYER}, and I will make those responsible for his sad state reap the reward of their crime!'You have seen anger in Gun Ain's eyes before today, and fear, but never have you seen the look she gives you now. Her expression is pained.

'All I wanted was a name, ${PLAYER}. I wanted to be someone again. I wanted a family to replace the one I had lost, friends to take the place of my emptiness... but there is no one.

'Look at them! That is what I wanted, ${PLAYER}, but no one will miss me! No one will cry when I have gone. No one will mourn.''Is that you, ${PLAYER}? I did not think I would see you again.'

Lothrandir smiles weakly.

'The Wizard was not pleased when you escaped. I almost felt sorry for Saruman. I think his Orcs are endlessly disappointing him.''Lothrandir is safe and we have accomplished our aim, ${PLAYER}. I will be glad to leave this place behind us.'For the first time, Gun Ain's eyes are alight with a fierce and sudden joy. She exults in her words, and radiates a restless energy.

'You spoke of the strength of Lothrandir's will, and you were not wrong. Saruman tried to exert control over his mind, tried with every ounce of his power, but still your friend resisted. The Wizard wove convincing illusions and portraits of despair, but still Lothrandir fought. Many and powerful were the tricks he employed to break your friend's mind, but each one failed. Lothrandir remained strong, and the Wizard could find no means of bending him to his will.

'In the end, Saruman had to abandon the effort. He took another prisoner from his cell, a Falcon Clan warrior named Kenned who had proved unable to work in the Pits, and dressed him in Lothrandir's garb. That is the poor creature you see before you; he has at last earned his freedom.'You have come with Gimli deeper into the Glittering Caves to make certain the folk who shelter here will be safe.Gimli is in the Glittering Caves, ready to proceed further into Aglarond.Talk to Gimli in the Glittering CavesYou have encountered a small child in the Glittering Caves.Talk to the Rohirrim childSome villainy has come to the Glittering Caves of Aglarond.Learn what evil has entered the Glittering CavesLheu Brenin and other members of the Falcon Clan have come to the Glittering Caves.Talk to Lheu BreninGimli is not sure what to make of Lheu Brenin.Talk to GimliLheu Brenin has commanded his warriors to kill everyone in the Glittering Caves.Survive the assault of the Falcon Clan warriorsThe child of the Westfold has excitedly asked for you to speak to her.Talk to the child of the WestfoldLheu Brenin is ready to fight, and has no fear of you or Gimli.Talk to Lheu BreninLheu Brenin has vast reserves of strength, and wishes to see you dead. You have to weaken him through battle!Weaken Lheu BreninLheu Brenin has vast reserves of strength, and wishes to see you dead. You have to weaken him through battle!Talk to Lheu BreninLheu Brenin has vast reserves of strength, and wishes to see you dead. You have to weaken him through battle!Weaken Lheu BreninThe child of the Westfold has tried to get your attention.Talk to the child of the WestfoldLheu Brenin has come to the end of his strength, and has paid for his crimes.Defeat Lheu BreninLheu Brenin has been defeated, and the people who shelter in the Glittering Caves are now safe from the Falcon Clan.Talk to Gimli in the Glittering CavesTalk to the child of the Westfold'Let us return to the folk who shelter in the Glittering Caves, ${PLAYER}. We will make sure this child reaches them safely... or perhaps she will ensure we make it safely!''The natural beauty of this place takes my breath away, ${PLAYER}, but we have an important errand here and cannot stop with our mouths agape. Let us proceed and ensure that nothing lurks here to threaten the folk that shelter in the caves.'The small girl waves off your inquiry, dismissing your questions with a toss of her hair.

'There is no time to talk! My mother is with the other folk of Westfold, hiding in the cave, but I heard something back here and came to see what it was. Do you know what I found?'

She looks around quickly, and then says in a hushed whisper, 'Villains! Villains from Dunland!''We come once more face to face, ${PLAYER}, and once more you find yourself overmatched. Before you stand the finest of my warriors, and I the strongest of them all. None know the high mountainous paths and deep tunnels as well as the Hebog-lûth, and we have put that knowledge to good use.

'I have been promised a king's reward by my ally Saruman for the death of Rohan's people, and you will not stop me. I know its women and children shelter in the caves behind you, and my force of loyal Falcons will kill any they find. You think to stop me with such allies as I see before me? A single dwarf and one child?''Who is this blustery man, ${PLAYER}? He is no friend of yours, and that makes him no friend of mine. I do not like his words or his tone. Let us teach him not to ally with Wizards who cannot tell the difference between evil and good!''You can do it, ${PLAYER}! I know that you can defeat this villain! Stand strong for Rohan, and for Théoden King!'

The girl knows nothing of Lheu Brenin's treachery in Dunland, but her belief in you, combined with the Falcon Clan's crimes, results in the restoration of your morale.'I should never have let you leave Tâl Methedras alive. Now I will remedy that mistake!'Lheu Brenin's breath is coming now in fits and gasps, but still he remains strong and defiant.

'How many of your friends have I slain, ${PLAYER}? I will not stop now. I will continue until none remain, and Saruman will pay me a sum worthy of a dragon's hoard for the favour!''This man is less than a worm, ${PLAYER}! He is no man at all, but a thrall of Saruman's, thinking only of his reward and caring nothing for anyone: not for the people of his tribe with whom he should have a bond, nor for my people, who have done him no wrong.

'I will not let him threaten my family or my king!''Even the children of Rohan fight against their enemies, when pressed! It fills my heart with pride to see it, for a child under the Mountain would do the same. I am impressed!

'Already I engage in competition with Legolas. Need I now wager with this child, as well? Ha!''No one threatens my land, my king, or my family, ${RACE}! Any villains who do had better run back to their Wizard masters!'You have come to the Paths of the Dead in search of Elfhelm's grandson Elfdag, who may have ventured beneath the mountain.Waldere is in the tunnel before you, injured and exhausted.Talk to WaldereWaldere has asked if you plan to venture deeper into the Paths of the Dead by yourself or if you have a Fellowship near at hand.Talk to Waldere and declare your intentionsWaldere told you that his men were slain outside of a passage in the south-west of a cavernous chamber, and thinks you should search that tunnel for Elfdag.Search the south-west tunnel in the Paths of the Dead for ElfdagA great city of the Dead stands beneath the mountains.

You have found Elfdag in the Paths of the Dead, and now must fight to free him from the Dead.Fight off the Dead Men and rescue ElfdagElfdag is in the empty streets of the city of the Dead beneath the mountains.Talk to Elfdag in the Paths of the Dead'We should get out of here... and at once!''Have you come here seeking Elfdag? I ventured beneath the mountain with several of my men, but we ran afoul of the Dead, and only I survived the encounter. I fled the cavernous room where they fell, but I feel no shame for it. It was folly to come here.

'There was a passage we thought to search, in the south-west wall of the cavernous room, but we were set upon before we could venture that way. If you seek Elfdag, that is a good place to begin.

'But wait! Before you go: are you journeying within by yourself, or do you have a Fellowship near at hand? Speak to me again and give me your answer.''Do you plan to venture further within by yourself, or do you have a Fellowship near at hand? Speak to me and give me your answer.''You are a great adventurer, I can tell! But I think we should leave at once... these Dead Men won't stay away for long, no matter how often they are defeated!

'We will speak once we are above-ground, yes? I overheard some things you will want to know!'Only the Oath-breakers of old know what befell Aragorn and his friends within the Paths of the Dead.Tenval has felt a curious disturbance and wishes to speak with you.Learn if Tenval shares your uneasy disquietDûgden is beneath the Mountain, in the Paths of the Dead. He shares your curse, and your feeling that something has changed.Learn if Dûgden knows what has caused this disturbanceA number of living Men have entered the paths beneath the Mountain, intruding upon your demesnes.Watch the living interlopers pass, and learn their intentionsOne of the living Men is the Heir of Isildur, and he has called your kind to the Stone of Erech. This has caused a split among your people: Tenval and Drugât argue the merits of following the Heir or ignoring his summons.Listen to the arguments of Tenval and DrugâtTenval and Dûgden were your closest friends when you walked the earth as a living man, in the days before the Curse. Now they must decide where their course lies, as must you.Talk to Tenval and learn his intentionTalk to Dûgden and learn his intentionTenval and Dûgden were your closest friends when you walked the earth as a living man, in the days before the Curse. They have decided where their paths lead, and now you must do the same.Make your choice: follow either Aragorn or DrugâtYou have made your decision, and must now deal with the consequences.Witness the actions of your potential alliesYears have no meaning beneath the Mountain for the likes of you, but even so, you hold the memory of a time when the passage of day and night mattered. A new feeling has crept into your being, but you cannot place it. Tenval senses it too.

'Something feels different in the dark, Lûtkal,' he murmurs, and you know it is true.The heavy silence beneath the Mountain remains unbroken, a held breath beneath the still waters of Time, an expectant hush. Dûgden feels it too.

'I swore I would never forget this presence,' he breathes without air or voice, giving thought to the idea that has come to all who now walk the undying paths beneath the Mountain.

'Isildur the Hated has come again, to deepen the burden of his curse.'The heavy weight of the silence before the coming of the Heir has been lifted. Now the darkness is busy with the sighing whispers of the Dead. Tenval's gaze is piercing and eager.

'What moved the Heir of Isildur to come beneath the Mountain, Lûtkal...? Has he forgiven us our treason...? I will follow him to Erech, and whither then...? I cannot see it, but I care not....'You knew Dûgden long ago, before the two of you succumbed to weakness and fell beneath the heavy weight of the Curse of Isildur. His presence is cold, but he burns with a fierce agony.

'What has brought the heir of hated Isildur to this place, Lûtkal...? Untold years we have suffered the Curse of his cruel ancestor... has it not been enough...?'

Dûgden's resolve is sudden and frightening.

'I will cast my lot in with Sauron, as we did before...! Drugât speaks the truth: only Sauron will reward us for our service. Only he can banish the chains of our curse! I will follow our true master!'The Ents have come to Isengard to remind Saruman of the danger he has risked with his plans.You have come to the ring of Isengard with as many of the Ents that responded to your call. The time has come to remind Saruman of what he has forgotten!Talk to Quickbeam outside the ring of IsengardThe Ents have torn down the gate that gives entrance to the Ring of Isengard.Talk to Quickbeam in front of the gate to IsengardThe Ents have smashed open the gate to Isengard, and now you should proceed into the ring.Pass through the ruined gate of Isengard and defeat Saruman's forcesSaruman has fought back with flame and smoke!The Ents have been driven mad by the fires of Saruman! His evil must be washed away!The Ents have diverted the river and flooded the Ring of Isengard. Their purpose: to wash it clean of Saruman's filth.Survey your handiwork and greet the newcomerSaruman's servant has gone to join his master in the tower.Saruman's servant has gone to join his master in the tower'That was a rousing march, Treebeard! We have come to the ring of Isengard sooner than I thought we would, and our business with the Wizard waits before us!'

Quickbeam peers at the hobbits clinging to your upper branches.

'Hold on, little ones! Keep a good grip, and do not fall!''What has happened here? You... you have worked some mischief...'

The little man's eyes dart left and right, taking in as much of the devastation as he can.

'What has happened to Saruman? I... bore messages for him from the King, but it seems they are no longer necessary...'

He turns to go, but you stop him. It is plain he served the Wizard and was complicit in his designs. They deserve each other's company.'I will gather the rest of our people, Fangorn, while you press forward through the main gate into Isengard! I will meet up with you beyond the gate!'Treebeard has agreed to finish the story begun by Merry, telling you of the last march of the Ents.Treebeard is near the base of Orthanc, and has agreed to finish the story begun by Merry.Talk to Treebeard and listen to his taleTreebeard is by the base of Orthanc.Talk to Treebeard by the base of Orthanc'The young hobbits told you of the moot, but did not tell you of our arrival here? You wish to hear that tale?'

Treebeard seems to give it some thought... and some thought... and some more thought. Eventually his eyes focus on you again.

'Ents do not often get asked to tell stories, little one. But I will see what I can do... hoom, hrum, hoom. I hope you have enough time to listen.''Are you ready to hear the tale, little one? Hoom, boom, hrum.''That is the tale, little one. I have not seen Saruman since Gríma Wormtongue came to the tower. His schemes may be at an end; the water seems to have dampened his spirits.

'Oh! Oh, that is very good! Hoom, hrum, boom! Dampened his spirits!

'I have enjoyed telling you the tale, little ${RACE}. I hope you will remember the Ents. It seems to me that Saruman wishes he remembered us sooner. I am glad he did not.'Gléowine is preparing a song mourning Háma, the doorward of Théoden, who fell before the Hornburg.Day has dawned, and with it comes new hope for Rohan.

Gléowine has offered to tell you the tale of Háma's heroic last stand before the gates of the Hornburg.Talk to Gléowine on the field of victory, and complete 'Interlude: The Doorward'Day has dawned, and with it comes new hope for Rohan.

You have heard his tale of Háma, and should speak now to Gléowine.Talk to Gléowine on the field of victoryDay has dawned, and with it comes new hope for Rohan.

Théoden is on the field of victory, before the walls at Helm's Deep.Talk to Théoden on the field of victory'This day brings a new dawn for Rohan, but not without cost, ${PLAYER}. Many friends and kinsmen came to their ends during the long night on the wall, and it is the minstrel's sad duty to remember their names and their tales.

'Háma, the doorward of Théoden, fell before the Hornburg. Without his valiant efforts, the gate would surely have been breached, and the King slain before he could ride to victory. I will tell you the tale of Háma's heroic stand, if you will hear it.''Listen well to the tale of Háma, who fell before the Hornburg, and gave Théoden a chance to attain his victory.''And so passed Háma, the doorward of Théoden. Without him the gate, and perhaps the kingdom itself, would now be broken. Do not forget him. I know that Théoden King will not. He served the King faithfully for many years, even with his death.''Do not forget Háma. I know that Théoden King will not. He served the King faithfully for many years, even with his death.''I know what this victory cost us, ${PLAYER}: many good men, children of Rohan all. They fought to defend their people, and I will remember their valiant deeds.

'But one loss will loom more large in my mind than the rest, for Háma, son of Léoferth, was more than the doorward of my hall. He served me faithfully for many years, but he did not do so blindly. When I succumbed to the whisperings of Gríma Wormtongue, and only Gandalf could restore me to my senses, it was Háma that weighed the Wizard's wish against my own command. And though he was sworn to uphold my word, Háma was a true man of Rohan and made the judgement he deemed necessary. It was disobedience, but in service of the kingdom, and I both forgive and am proud of him.

'He gave his life before the Hornburg, and without his sacrifice we could not have had victory this day. I will see to it that Háma will not be forgotten.'In the moments before dawn, Háma performed his greatest service for Rohan and his king.Háma stands in the gate-house. He has only one door to guard, the door that safe-guards his king.Position yourself before the door and let no enemies passHáma has only one door to guard, the door that safe-guards his king.Triumph against the Orcs that threaten the HornburgHáma must draw upon thoughts of Rohan if he is to persevere.Draw upon reserves of strength by focusing on the glory of RohanHáma has only one door to guard, the door that safe-guards his king.Stand against the Orcs that threaten the HornburgHáma must draw upon thoughts of the House of Eorl if he is to persevere.Draw upon reserves of strength by focusing on the House of EorlHáma has only one door to guard, the door that safe-guards his king.Survive against the Orcs that threaten the HornburgHáma must draw upon thoughts of his father Léoferth if he is to persevere.Draw upon reserves of strength by thinking of your father LéoferthHáma has only one door to guard, the door that safe-guards his king.Live as long as you are able, for Théoden and for RohanMerry has agreed to tell you the story of how he and Pippin met Treebeard.Merry is outside the gate of Isengard.

Merry has agreed to tell you the story of how he and Pippin met Treebeard the Ent.Talk to Merry and hear the story of how he met TreebeardMerry is outside the gate of Isengard.Talk to Merry outside the gate of Isengard'Pip and I met Treebeard after we escaped from the Orcs. He is an Ent, you know, a shepherd of the forests. I can tell you how we met him, if you like.''It is quite a long story, but I will tell it to you if you will listen! These are not the most comfortable surroundings for a bit of story-telling, but I will do the very best I can.'Pippin looks at you amiably, but says nothing.Pippin smokes contentedly, and says nothing.'Pip and I went to Quickbeam's house after that, but my hunger has quite gotten the best of me and I have lost the mood for story-telling. Perhaps I will tell you of the last march of the Ents after I have eaten. But that is enough for now, I think. Quite a tale, was it not?'Merry has agreed to fill you in on how he and Pippin met the Ent named Treebeard, and what happened afterwards.You have heard the sound of two curious creatures speaking to one another, and should investigate.Learn the nature of the curious little creaturesThe creatures seem to offer no threat, but you have not seen their like before.Learn the nature of the curious little creaturesOne of the curious creatures wishes to speak with you. How peculiar.Talk to one of the curious creaturesThe other hobbit wishes to speak with you.Talk to the other hobbitYou have decided to show these curious little creatures, these hobbits, to your home.Bring your new hobbit friends to WellinghallOne of your new hobbit friends, Pippin, wants to speak with you.Talk to Pippin and tell him your house is nearYou have decided to show the hobbits to your home.Show your new hobbit friends the way to your homeYour new hobbit friends may enjoy a sip of Ent-draught.Pick up the container of Ent-draughtYou should pick up two bowls for your hobbit friends and one for yourself.Your hobbit friends could use a sip of Ent-draught. You should bring them the bowls you picked up.Bring the bowls of Ent-draught to your new hobbit friendsThe hobbits seem to be enjoying the Ent-draught.Wait for your new hobbit-friends to finish drinking the Ent-draughtMerry wishes to speak with you about something.Talk to Merry in WellinghallYou have called a moot of your people to decide what is to be done concerning Saruman.Travel to Derndingle with the hobbitsYou have brought the hobbits to Derndingle, where the Ents will discuss the matter of Saruman. You should tell the young one what to expect.Talk to Pippin on the way to DerndingleMany of your people have come to Derndingle to discuss the matter of Saruman.Follow the hobbits to DerndingleBaldbark needs to be convinced.Convince Baldbark that something must be doneBeechbone needs to be convinced.Convince Beechbone that something must be doneQuickbeam needs to be convinced.Convince Quickbeam that something must be doneYou should tell the hobbits that Quickbeam will go with them while the moot continues.Talk to Merry'Well, it is nice to meet you, Treebeard. I have never met an Ent before! My name is Peregrin Took, but my friends call me Pippin, or Pip. I am a hobbit.''It is a pleasure to meet you, Treebeard. My name is Meriadoc Brandybuck, but most people call me Merry. We have become separated from our friends, and it seems that Saruman's Orcs would very much like to find us again and bring us to their master.''Are you certain this is the right place, Treebeard? I don't see a house anywhere.''That was delicious, Treebeard! I feel refreshed and invigorated, as if I had bountiful reserves of energy and could use them for anything at all!

'But we were going to tell you how we came to be in your forest, weren't we? I hope I have energy enough for tale-telling!''I do not know what to expect, Treebeard... are all of the Ents like you?''Greetings to you, my friend from long ago, when the forest was young and we were not full-grown. Many seasons have turned since last we spoke together in the light of either sun or moon. It is many Ent-strides from my home. Why have you called me to this place?'

You greet Baldbark and reminisce about conversations shared and absent friends. Eventually you speak of Saruman.

'I have been made aware of the Wizard's crimes. He worked great evil in the reaches of the forest near my own home. I agree that something must be done. What will the others say of this?'

You thank Baldbark for his supportive words, and wish him well in the debate to come.You greet Beechbone, a handsome Ent. His path has crossed with yours several times recently, and the memory of those pleasant conversations is still somewhat fresh. After a polite amount of time, your words turn to the matter of Saruman.

'You speak of our neighbour as if he is an enemy, and indeed, he does not seem to be the friend he was once. Fumes rise from his home and the smell gives my nose offence, but that is not a crime. Orcs, too, could come from any number of holes. What evidence marks Saruman as their master?'

Beechbone politely declines to discuss the matter with you further, but he agrees to listen to the debate and may in time come around to your point of view.You exchange pleasantries with Quickbeam, one of the younger Ents. His home is not far from Derndingle, and sometimes you have seen him walking in the sunlight among the trees. Before much time has passed, the conversation turns to Saruman. Quickbeam seems to have some idea of the Wizard's crimes already.

'Saruman has much for which to answer. He is a tree-killer, and we have been quiet neighbours for too long already. You find me over-hasty, I know, but in this there seems to be little need for discussion, Fangorn. I have quite made up my mind.'

You tell Quickbeam of the two young hobbits you met, and ask if he will watch them while you speak to the rest of the Ents at the moot. He readily agrees.'You Ents certainly do take a long time to make up your minds, Treebeard! I feel certain we could have decided this matter six or seven times over if we were still in the Shire!'

Merry and Pippin listen as you explain that Quickbeam will watch over them. You feel certain they will like the young Ent, for he is quite hasty in many respects.

'Well, if you say so, Treebeard! I think you might consider the slow drip of honey in the comb quite hasty in comparison to yourself, so Pip and I will be the judge of that, if you don't mind!'Soldier Faltúrz sent you to Hithlad, east of the southern stretch of the Hoardale River, to find the Grimwood Lumber-yard.The Grimwood Lumber-yard is found in the heart of the Grimwood within Hithlad, south and east across the Hoardale River.

