'The old man is a wise advisor, but he is not as well suited to leadership. What few of us remain have held the lands west of the river Blackroot, but our archers have grown weary. Ever since the Orcs sacked Tingobel to the east, our troubles have only grown more dire.

'Belegant seemed pleased by your arrival, so perhaps you should seek him above.''Ah, you must be the traveller that arrived today. It is true that the vale faces many problems in recent days... some much more tangible than others.

'Word from the south speaks of something stirring the beasts of the vale, but little else is known. I am unsure if he knows of your arrival, but I am certain Landiach would be pleased to speak with you on these matters as well.

'If you have a moment, I have a more personal request of you.''Greetings and well met, traveller. I did not expect any to visit during such times, much less enter the vale through the dark paths. I am Landiach, ward of Lord Duinhir and protector of Lady Rosfin.

'As you have heard, troubles loom to the south and east, and we are too few to move against them. Acting on one would leave us defenceless against the other.

'If you would lend us your might, perhaps we can begin to push back our foes after all. Please tell Malthellam I would have you fight on our behalf.''I am thankful the others had time to spare to speak with you, and I am sure you can aid them all. Landiach is a trusted advisor of Lord Duinhir and a friend to my family... if he believes you can aid us, I do not doubt him.

'It is true that I have made... mistakes since taking stewardship of Blackroot Vale, but to appear weak or unwise would undo everything Duinhir hoped for me to embody in his stead.'Celegdal thinks some great evil came out of the mountains and lingered at the Hill of Erech. He does not know the details, or how close to the truth his theory may prove...The Hill of Erech is east of Morlad, in the Blackroot Vale.

Celegdal believes that a great evil came out of the mountains and went to the Hill of Erech, and wants you to see if it is true.Journey to the summit of the Hill of Erech, east of MorladThe earth atop the Hill of Erech has been disturbed, and bears investigation.Someone has seen you, silhouetted against the sky atop the Hill of Erech!You have been seen against the sky atop the Hill of Erech!Someone saw you while you searched the summit of Erech, and called out to you. You should descend the hill and see if this person is a friend.Descend the hill and find the man who called out to you'Do you want to know what this honest man of Morlad believes? You may think me a fool, but I feel certain that some great evil came out of the mountains and sped with unnatural haste through the Vale. On moonless nights, some claim to have seen the Dead dancing atop the Hill of Erech, east of here. Is it true? I cannot speak to that, but I will tell you that no one in Morlad leaves his door unbarred when the time comes to blow out his candles.

'You are more brave than I am, ${PLAYER}, that much is certain! If you have the stomach for it, a journey to the Hill of Erech might reveal if my supposition is true. If something did come out of the dark reaches under the mountains, I feel certain that is where it would have gone. It has always been an ill-omened place.''If you have the stomach for it, a journey to the Hill of Erech might reveal if my supposition is true. If something did come out of the dark reaches under the mountains, I feel certain that is where it would have gone. It has always been an ill-omened place.''If you have the stomach for it, a journey to the Hill of Erech might reveal if my supposition is true. If something did come out of the dark reaches under the mountains, I feel certain that is where it would have gone. It has always been an ill-omened place.''If you have the stomach for it, a journey to the Hill of Erech might reveal if my supposition is true. If something did come out of the dark reaches under the mountains, I feel certain that is where it would have gone. It has always been an ill-omened place.''If you have the stomach for it, a journey to the Hill of Erech might reveal if my supposition is true. If something did come out of the dark reaches under the mountains, I feel certain that is where it would have gone. It has always been an ill-omened place.''I did not mean to startle you, but judging from your look you have seen your own share of surprises. You may be a seasoned adventurer, but I daresay what I beheld in the reaches of the night would make your blood run cold, as it did mine.

'From afar I heard the blast of a great horn, and then a multitude of horns in echoing answer. I felt then the gathering of a great company of shadows all about the Stone atop this hill. A group of horsemen stood there, and their leader was grim of face and stern of countenance. Flesh and blood treated with the shadows, and though I could not understand the words, his purpose was plain enough. The company's grim leader charged the Dead with a task, and when the dawn came they departed.

'I feel no shame when I tell you I hid myself as they passed, for the men of this company were as real as you or me, but in their train travelled countless shadows. This man commanded the Dead to follow, and follow him they did, down from Erech, through Tarlang's Neck, to where I do not know. I fear great evil has come to the kingdom I serve, Gondor that I love.'You have met Rovalang, a Swan-knight of Dol Amroth. Recognising you for a great adventurer, he has asked you to journey into Lamedon and ensure that the people there are not endangered by the movement of the Dead.Rovalang has asked you to travel south-east through Tarlang's Neck, and to help any people of Gondor who may have run afoul of the Dead in their passage.Travel south-east through Tarlang's Neck into LamedonA waggon has come to an early stop in Tarlang's Neck, and its driver could use your assistance.Talk to Bandron by his cart in Tarlang's NeckNethadan went to the town of Dínadab in Lamedon, south-east of Tarlang's Neck, to recover supplies to fix his father's broken waggon, but he never returned. Bandron asked for you to find him.Find Nethadan somewhere south of Tarlang's NeckNethadan has asked you to bring one of the barrels near him back to his father Bandron, in Tarlang's Neck.Collect barrel of nails by NethadanBandron is by his broken cart in Tarlang's Neck, between Lamedon and Blackroot Vale.

You have picked up a barrel of nails from Nethadan and he asked you to bring it to his father so he can repair the cart.Galengam is outside his shop in Calembel, south-east of Tarlang's Neck.

Bandron has asked you to speak to his employer in the town of Calembel and explain some of the reasons for the delay.Talk to Galengam outside his shop in Calembel'My name is Rovalang, and I am proud to serve Imrahil, Prince of Dol Amroth. He has marched for the White City with a company of knights and more than five hundred men at arms to defend it 'gainst all enemies.  I and several of my order he commanded to remain in the service of his daughter Lothíriel, who rules now while he is gone.

'Her safety is paramount, and I and the other swan-knights who remained would die to defend her, but we are sworn to obey her commands. She sent several of us from the city to protect her people in the Vale and in Lamedon, and we could not refuse. So I came to the Vale, and beheld the scene I have reported to you. It seems Lothíriel was right: there is danger outside the walls of Dol Amroth, and the Swan-knights are needed here too.

'Not just Swan-knights; brave adventurers too, as I deem you to be! Follow the Dead south-east through Tarlang's Neck, and help any of the people of Gondor that may have been endangered by their passage. I will remain to aid the people of the Vale, but I value your help, my new friend. May the people benefit from our friendship!''Follow the Dead south-east through Tarlang's Neck, and help any of the people of Gondor that may have been endangered by their passage. I will remain to aid the people of the Vale, but I value your help, my new friend. May the people benefit from our friendship!''The road through Tarlang's Neck is treacherous. Keep your eyes open for any danger!''Take care, traveller! You pass through Tarlang's Neck, and the road here is treacherous. Many an ankle has been turned by the rough going in this pass, riders and walkers sent to the ground by one misstep. It has claimed the axle of my cart, and now my business is halted.

'I sent my boy Nethadan to the nearest village for supplies we can use to fix it, but he has yet to return. Can you keep one eye open for him? I am afraid he may have run into trouble... or into the town's tavern for a short drink that became a long one!

'The nearest town is called Dínadab, and it is to the south-east along the road. Nethadan may be a fool, but he is my boy and I do not want him to run afoul of trouble. Not when we have a task to perform!''I sent my boy Nethadan to the nearest village for supplies we can use to fix it, but he has yet to return. Can you keep one eye open for him? The road leads south-east to the nearest village, and I hope you find him there. I need those supplies, and I need to know he has not run into trouble.''I need to return to my father's cart, up the road that way, in the Neck. Have you come from there?'

Nethadan grimaces and clutches his leg.

'We were bringing several bottles of Cemenduril liquor to sell at towns throughout the Vale, on behalf of Galengam's Divine and Delicate Draughts, but the cart succumbed to the harsh passage. My father sent me down to that village yonder to bring back supplies to fix the cart, and I did my duty. Well, as far as it went, for I was jumped by raiders and hurt my foot quite grievously. They ran off, but be warned: they may return in time!

'If you could bring one of these barrels back to my father, I will follow with the other. I think I am just about ready to walk once more.''If you could bring one of these barrels back to my father, I will follow with the other. I think I am just about ready to walk once more.''I sent my boy Nethadan to the nearest village for supplies we can use to fix it, but he has yet to return. Can you keep one eye open for him? The road leads south-east to the nearest village, and I hope you find him there. I need those supplies, and I need to know he has not run into trouble.''Bring that barrel of nails to my father, and with haste! I will be along quite soon.''Did you find my boy? Did you find Nethadan?'

You tell Bandron his son was attacked by raiders, but survived and seemed well enough to return very soon.

'That is a relief, ${PLAYER}. I have many daughters, but he is my only son. Who else will carry on the legacy of my house, as common as it is? He is my blood, and in him I see my own visage at that age. Young, but full of dreams for the road to come. He is dear to me. You have done well by my son and my family! Now, my benefactor, what supplies have you brought from him? Give them to me and I will repair this axle, the better to return to my employer's errand.'

You hand over the barrel of nails, and Bandron's eyes widen with surprise.

'What is this? An entire barrel of nails?'

You tell Bandron that his son should be by soon with another, similar barrel.

'No, that cannot be! What am I supposed to do with two barrels of nails? I need to fix an axle, not put a nail in every tree from here to the Grey Wood! This repair will take two nails, at the most. And now we have two barrels of the things! How much coin did he spend on these barrels? Every one I gave to him, I do not doubt! All of our profit on this expedition will have gone to buying nails!

'Oh, ${PLAYER}. I recommend to you a life without heirs. Every hair on my head was the colour of coal before Nethadan was born, and ever since: white hairs, each and every one!

'My employer is Galengam, of Galengam's Divine and Delicate Draughts. He runs a shop in the town of Calembel, to the south-east. Kindly go to him and tell him that we ran into some difficulty, and will return as soon as the Cemenduril liquor is delivered. If you have a kindly heart, ${PLAYER}, weave a tale of raiders and robbers... and leave the nails quite out of it!''My employer is Galengam, of Galengam's Divine and Delicate Draughts. He runs a shop in the town of Calembel, to the south-east. Kindly go to him and tell him that we ran into some difficulty, and will return as soon as the Cemenduril liquor is delivered. If you have a kindly heart, ${PLAYER}, weave a tale of raiders and robbers... and leave the nails quite out of it!''Ah, welcome to Galengam's Divine and Delicate Draughts, purveyor of healing remedies and wholesome poultices! If you are here to purchase some of my goods, I must sadly report that you have come at a poor time; my last shipment of Cemenduril liquor is already bound for the Blackroot Vale, and my remaining stock is set aside for the Houses of Healing at Minas Tirith.'

You tell Galengam of your encounter with Bandron and his son Nethadan.

'The delay is unfortunate, but not unexpected. A successful merchant accounts for such things. Theirs will be the last delivery of Cemenduril liquor to the Vale for some time, because now all my goods will be needed at Minas Tirith.'Galengam has asked for your help to preserve the Cemenduril fields that are his livelihood and perhaps that of Gondor as well.Galengam's farmers can be found west of Calembel, working the flower fields.

A wealthy merchant has asked you to protect the farmers that grow his Cemenduril crop from the attacks of any raiders that threaten.Talk to a Cemenduril farmer in the fieldsCollect Corsair weapons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Retrieve harvested Cemenduril bundles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Galengam is outside his shop in Calembel.

You have protected Galengam's farmers and collected a number of weapons wielded by the Corsair raiders. The merchant will be interested to hear how you have assisted his interests.'Yours is not the first report I have heard of raiders in Lamedon, ${PLAYER}. I am worried they may do some harm to the fields where my workers grow Cemenduril blossoms. With these crops I brew the healing liquors I sell throughout the kingdom; if they are ruined, more than my business will suffer.'

Galengam scowls. 'Prince Imrahil rode east for Minas Tirith, leaving his daughter to rule Dol Amroth. Is it any wonder that raiders have taken advantage to cause trouble while the Prince is away? I wager they would not dare do so if he remained in the city, but alas! War threatens in the east, and draws him thither. Lothíriel sits in Dol Amroth, where she is safe, but what of the people outside the walls?

'Several farmers in my employ work the fields even now. Will you protect them while they go about their business? I would greatly appreciate it, and you would be doing me, and Gondor, a favour.''Have you seen any sign of the raiders? I fear for my workers and for the Cemenduril crop, and want no harm to come to either!''I will be glad of the company, but I hope Galengam's concerns prove to be unfounded. I am no warrior. If a raiding party does threaten our work here, I will be quite helpless. Fortunately for me you are an imposing figure indeed!''Do you have news from the fields? Oh, you have more than that! These bundles of harvested flowers will allow me to brew a good quantity of my healing liquor... but what are these?'

Galengam squints critically at the Corsair weapons you have set before him.

'I do not like the look of these weapons at all. These are weapons of Umbar, I'll warrant, and that means the raiding parties that plague Lamedon come from the coast. If Prince Imrahil were here, they would not dare to land! But we have only his daughter Lothíriel to keep the coast safe, and she is not the leader that is her father.'Stirred by the Dead or some other fell force, the mountain-lions of Blackroot Vale have crept into the valley, seeking more substantial prey.Blackroot mountain-lions can be found to the east of Alagrant and as far south as Sardol.

You have encountered aggressive mountain-lions in the vale, and should cull their numbers for the good of Alagrant and Sardol.Defeat Blackroot mountain-lions (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have slain many mountain-lions in Blackroot Vale.CompletedThe mountain-lions of Blackroot Vale have grown more aggressive than usual and have begun to lurk near the villages of Alagrant and Sardol.

If the people of these two villages are to remain safe, the mountain-lions must be slain.You have defeated many mountain-lions in Blackroot Vale. Although you did not discover evidence of any fell force driving them deep into the vale, their ferocity could have easily posed a serious threat to the people of Alagrant and Sardol.In the wake of strange occurrences in the vale, the hunting village of Alagrant has come to a standstill.Dírion can be found in the village of Alagrant in western Blackroot Vale.

Calennen has asked you to speak to the other hunters in Alagrant about Malthellam's warning.Talk to DírionSarnir can be found in the village of Alagrant in western Blackroot Vale.

Calennen has asked you to speak to the other hunters in Alagrant about Malthellam's warning.Talk to Sarnir'Malthellam orders us to stay in our villages because of the Orcs and wild animals? Hah! Well, to me, there is no difference. 

'Do you hear this, Dírion? The Steward cannot be serious! You see, ${PLAYER}, we are hunters by trade...  it does not serve us well to flee from the wildlife of the vale. Sarnir and all his talk of ghosts almost seems believable now! 

'Well now, if we're stuck here, you might as well get to know us, traveller! It'll certainly provide us something to do...''What is the meaning of this, traveller? I am quite capable of looking after myself, despite what the Steward might think. I shall remain in town, but do not expect me to do so quietly.

'If Malthellam were here in your place, I would not be so polite.''The others look at me as if I have lost my mind, but I know the truth. I have seen ghastly figures wandering the hilltop at night... but I dare not approach them.

'If the Dead truly have returned to the vale, I fear for our safety and know not what ills might come of their presence here.'Rovalang beheld a frightening meeting atop the Hill of Erech, and does not know what it signifies, but you know part of its significance, at the least: Aragorn spoke with the oath-breakers in the dead of night.Rovalang stands on the slope of the Hill of Erech.

Rovalang wishes to learn more of the meeting between the Dead and Aragorn, if you are willing to speak to him.Talk to Rovalang and experience 'Interlude: Those Who Are Dead'Rovalang is on the slope of the Hill of Erech.Talk to Rovalang'I know little of the Dead, and count myself lucky. Who can say what drove them to meet with that curious company and its grim-faced leader?

'You know of this man. I can see it in your face. Are you willing to speak of him?''You may keep your secrets, my interesting friend. I do not wish to pry. Only the Dead know what drove them to the Hill of Erech to meet with this grim man, but I do believe you know more of this matter than you let on.''I am unsure what this meeting means for Gondor, but I know it will be significant. Let our own meeting be as timely, but let it signify only good fortune for the kingdom I serve!'Dírion, a hunter of Alagrant, has grown frustrated with Malthellam and finds himself confined to the village.Traps can be found outside Alagrant both east and west of the river Blackroot.

