You have gathered up a number of Corsair blades and should bring them to Mandan.Langlas is at the Camp of the Grey Host, west of the port-city of Pelargir.Talk to Langlas at the Camp of the Grey Host'Did you fight in the battle, ${PLAYER}? I was stricken with a great fear as the shades of the Dead joined the battle, and I confess to you that I felt a great urge to run from them, as the Corsairs did. And yet, before my heart betrayed me and I succumbed to the terror, I felt a hand upon my arm, and I was reassured.

'Do you know who it was? It was Celairant, the young Ranger I took under my wing in Ered Luin. He is not as young as he was, and this journey has put some steel in him. It was an honour to stand with Aragorn, and the presence of Celairant was a comfort. I am proud to call him brother.

'When the Corsairs fled, many of them cast their weapons to the ground. Mandan went ahead to gather up the fallen blades. Find him at the north-west entrance to Pelargir and help him with this task, for there are many weapons to be recovered.''When the Corsairs fled, many of them cast their weapons to the ground. Mandan went ahead to gather up the fallen blades. Find him at the north-west entrance to Pelargir and help him with this task, for there are many weapons to be recovered.''Oh! Langlas sent you to check on me? That was quite unnecessary, ${PLAYER}.'

Mandan scowls.

'Well, perhaps it was a little necessary. You see, I began to gather some of the weapons the Corsairs cast aside when they fled, but it was slow work and I decided my own skills might be better used elsewhere. You know of my talent for fashioning and applying salves, of course? There are many wounded folk who could use such treatment, and I will see to them immediately.

'Anyway, Celairant was gathering up the weapons already. You are welcome to go down to the docks and help him with that task, if you like.''Celairant was gathering up the weapons already. You are welcome to go down to the docks and help him with that task, if you like.''Ah, you had more success at the weapon-gathering than I did, but I too have done some good. These folk were fortunate I was here to see to their wounds! I know the plan is for us to leave this city, and it is important for us to do so, but we should try to help as many of the people here as we can.

'I will put these weapons into a barrel and ask Celairant where he wants it brought, but you should return to Langlas and tell him that I will be on short rations for the rest of our journey. I wish to give a portion of my provisions to some of the families the attack from Umbar hit hardest.''You should return to Langlas and tell him that I will be on short rations for the rest of our journey. I wish to give a portion of my provisions to some of the families the attack from Umbar hit hardest.''How did you find the task, ${PLAYER}? I hope it was not too onerous for you.'

You tell Langlas what Mandan asked you to say to him, and he sighs.

'I sent Mandan with Celairant in hopes that my young friend would be able to keep Mandan in line, but it seems he is still trying to peddle those salves and poultices of his. During our stay in Enedwaith, most of us looked forward to his excursions into the coomb seeking herbs... because it meant a respite from his sale-speak! He would inevitably return with more flowers and roots to add to his concoctions, but aside from worsening the smell I daresay they did not otherwise affect their properties.

'Thank you for completing his task for him, my friend.'Lothrandir is upset at his condition, still weakened from his imprisonment in Isengard, and wants to help the Grey Company ready the ships.Radanir is in the West-garth of Pelargir, by one of the bridges that leads to the Court of the Ship-kings.

Lothrandir has asked you to find Radanir and help him load the ships with supplies.Talk to Radanir in the West-garth of PelargirThere are crates that need to be carried to the Corsair flagship moored at the large dock on the south end of the Market Ward. Crates can be found by Radanir's position in the West-garth, near one of the bridges to the Market Ward.Pick up the supply crate by Radanir in the West-garthThere are barrels that need to be carried to the Corsair flagship moored at the large dock on the south end of the Market Ward. Barrels can be found by Radanir's position in the West-garth, near one of the bridges to the Market Ward.Pick up the supply barrel by Radanir in the West-garthTecheron is on the Corsair flagship moored at the large southern dock in the Market Ward, in Pelargir.

You have delivered some supplies to the flagship, and now Techeron wants to speak with you.Talk to Techeron on the Corsair flagshipLothrandir is at the Camp of the Grey Host, west of the port-city of Pelargir.Talk to Lothrandir in the Camp of the Grey Host'I do not know what I am doing, ${PLAYER}. I could do little more than stay atop the horse Dagoras provided for me on the ride from Rohan. I am still weak from my stay within Saruman's dungeons, and even lifting a sword leaves me fatigued. I am exhausted and frustrated!

'I volunteered to help load the ships with supplies, expecting to struggle through and beyond the unfair limitations of my strength, but Radanir took my place and sent me back here. My face was red with shame, but I must admit he was right. I need to rest and recover my strength. Curse the traitor-Wizard!

'Find Radanir and help him carry my share of the supplies onto the Corsair flagship. You will find him in the West-garth, by one of the bridges that leads to the Court of the Ship-kings.''Find Radanir and help him carry my share of the supplies onto the Corsair flagship. You will find him in the West-garth, by one of the bridges that leads to the Court of the Ship-kings.''Looks like Techeron owes me some coins, ${PLAYER}. We had a friendly wager concerning what would happen first: Lothrandir returning to try and carry these crates, or you showing up to carry them in his stead.

'I never doubted you for a moment, and now you've won me some coins. I thank you for being so honourable. And predictable!'

Radanir laughs.

'These need to go to the Corsair flagship, for that's the one that has the most room. Techeron is waiting for these supplies on the flagship moored at the large southern dock of the Market Ward. You can't miss it: it's the big one.''These need to go to the Corsair flagship, for that's the one that has the most room. Techeron is waiting for these supplies on the flagship moored at the large southern dock of the Market Ward. You can't miss it: it's the big one.''These need to go to the Corsair flagship, for that's the one that has the most room. Techeron is waiting for these supplies on the flagship moored at the large southern dock of the Market Ward. You can't miss it: it's the big one.''You should go back to Techeron and ask him about my coins. You know, just to make sure he has not forgotten about our little wager.''Hold a moment, ${PLAYER}. Did you know that Radanir and I had a bit of a friendly wager? It was just to amuse us during a bit of tedium, and I do not want you to take it too greatly to heart.

'You see, I wagered that Lothrandir would return to the city and finish the task of loading the ships, instead of you, but I did not mean to disparage your strength or ability! All know of your great deeds and the help you have provided to us on this journey!

'No, I based my wager on how hardy Lothrandir always appears. He chose to live in distant Forochel, after all! Such inhospitable places breed stalwart men, and Lothrandir was strong to start! He is the toughest Ranger I have ever met, and... well... perhaps that is the reason for my wager, ${PLAYER}.

'I simply could not believe that Lothrandir's strength could be taken from him, but Saruman has turned him into a shell of his former self. Such treatment in the dungeons of the Wizard would kill a lesser man, I have no doubt, but it pains me to see Lothrandir so weak, so tired. I think I wagered against you with the hope that it was not so.

'I am sorry for taking up so much of your time, my friend. I wanted you to know.''I simply could not believe that Lothrandir's strength could be taken from him, but Saruman has turned him into a shell of his former self. Such treatment in the dungeons of the Wizard would kill a lesser man, I have no doubt, but it pains me to see Lothrandir so weak, so tired. I think I wagered against you with the hope that it was not so.

'I am sorry for taking up so much of your time, my friend. I wanted you to know.''Thank you for doing my part, ${PLAYER}. Asking for help does not come naturally for me, and I beg you forgive me for it. I hope to regain my strength before long. This weakness and fatigue pains me more than any of the injuries I sustained in Saruman's care!'Daervunn sent Amlan into Pelargir to look for river-maps that will be useful for navigating the Anduin, but he has not yet returned.Amlan is in the Market Ward in the port-city of Pelargir.

Daervunn asked you to help Amlan find maps of the river Anduin to help Aragorn with his journey.Talk to Amlan in the Market Ward, in PelargirAmlan did not yet search the crates in a small area in the north-eastern corner of the Market Ward, and they may contain the river-maps you seek.Amlan is in the Market Ward, in Pelargir.

You found some maps of the Anduin and should bring them to Amlan.Orthonn is one of the lore-masters of the Grey Company, and can be found in the Court of the Ship-kings, in Pelargir.

Amlan cannot read the maps of the Anduin you recovered, but thinks that Orthonn might be able to learn some useful knowledge from them.Faeron is in the Court of the Ship-kings, in Pelargir.

Ranger Faeron heard your conversation with Orthonn and has sought your attention.Talk to Faeron in the Court of the Ship-kingsDaervunn is at the Camp of the Grey Host, west of the port-city of Pelargir.Talk to Daervunn in the Camp of the Grey Host'Aragorn hopes to sail up the Anduin on the decks of the Corsair ships, but we are not watermen, and if we are to do this we will need every advantage. To that end, I sent Amlan into the city to look for maps of the river that might aid us in this endeavour.

'If you are willing, return to the city and help Amlan find any such maps that might prove helpful. Pelargir is the great port-city of Gondor, and there must have been much use of the river for trade and passage. Surely some maps can be found that show the hazards and bends of the Anduin?

'I believe Amlan was going to begin his search in the Market Ward.''If you are willing, return to the city and help Amlan find any such maps that might prove helpful. Pelargir is the great port-city of Gondor, and there must have been much use of the river for trade and passage. Surely some maps can be found that show the hazards and bends of the Anduin?

'I believe Amlan was going to begin his search in the Market Ward.''I have been fruitlessly searching for maps ever since the battle ended, ${PLAYER}! If I never see another crate, it will be far too soon for me!

'I have searched all the crates in this area. The only place I have not yet searched is to the north-east, at the very tip of the Market Ward.

'I do not think any of those crates will have river-maps in them, but just to be safe we should search them anyway.''I have searched all the crates in this area. The only place I have not yet searched is to the north-east, at the very tip of the Market Ward.

'I do not think any of those crates will have river-maps in them, but just to be safe we should search them anyway.''You found some maps of the Anduin? I cannot believe it! It is always the last place you check, or the first place you never checked, is it not?'

Amlan takes the map from you and peers at it for a moment.

'Well, I see Pelargir here, and Osgiliath up here, which means that these four black lines here must be the Anduin... but what do these marks in the river's course mean? I cannot ascertain anything useful from these maps, ${PLAYER}. I think you should bring them to Orthonn, for he is a master of lore and should be skilled at reading maps.

'I think you will find him to the south-east, somewhere in the Court of the Ship-kings.''I think you should bring those maps to Orthonn, for he is a master of lore and should be skilled at reading them.

'I think you will find him to the south-east, somewhere in the Court of the Ship-kings.''Yes? With what can I help you, my friend? What do you have there?'

You hand Orthonn the maps of the Anduin, and with each one his smile grows.

'What a fine gift, ${PLAYER}! I did not think you knew of my interest in cartography! It is a vanishing talent these days, I am afraid, but I have done my best to craft maps of the lands through which we have travelled. This will be a great help in improving the accuracy of my own efforts in this field! Not that they are in great need of such improvement, but I believe there is always room for honing one's skills!

'Oh. This gift has a more practical purpose? I will of course study these maps of the river closely and give my findings to Aragorn, so we may travel the Anduin as safely and quickly as we can!''I will of course study these maps of the river closely and give my findings to Aragorn, so we may travel the Anduin as safely and quickly as we can!''I overheard your conversation with Orthonn, and have a suggestion of my own. I hope you will bring it to Daervunn, for I know he has the ear of Aragorn.

'I urge that he not fly the standard of the king when we sail up the Anduin. I have trained many light-fingered folk in the art of burglary, and I know the value of surprise. Sometimes it can make all the difference.

'If he keeps secret the fact of our departure and the manner of our travel, any foe we face may be put off its guard upon our arrival. My heart tells me there is value in this. Please tell Daervunn my message, ${PLAYER}.''I urge that Aragorn not fly the standard of the king when we sail up the Anduin. I have trained many light-fingered folk in the art of burglary, and I know the value of surprise. Sometimes it can make all the difference.

'If he keeps secret the fact of our departure and the manner of our travel, any foe we face may be put off its guard upon our arrival. My heart tells me there is value in this. Please tell Daervunn my message, ${PLAYER}.''You found some maps of the Anduin? Perfect!'

You tell Daervunn that Orthonn was delighted for the chance to practice his hobby of map-reading, and also relay Faeron's concerns about flying the standard of the king from atop the lead Corsair ship.

'I share Faeron's concerns, and spoke with Aragorn about this very matter while you were in the city. It has been his plan all along to keep secret the standard that Halbarad holds for him. I think he is not yet decided when to fly the standard, for it will be a message to both the Enemy and our allies that cannot be easily taken back, can it?

'Thank you for finding the maps, my friend, and for helping with all these matters. We will have many ships ready to sail, sooner and safer than I expected!'The Rangers of the Grey Company have helped deliver the port-city from danger, but their journey is not yet ended. Some of the Rangers are troubled by the things they have seen, and tensions run high among Aragorn's folk.Halbarad is located in the large courtyard just inside the north-west entrance to the port-city of Pelargir.Talk to Halbarad near the north-west entrance to PelargirCorunir is somewhere south of Halbarad's position, in Pelargir.

Halbarad has asked you to find Corunir and learn how Golodir fares after the passing of the Dead Men.Talk to Corunir in Pelargir, somewhere south of HalbaradCorunir left Golodir standing on a dock somewhere to the south, in the city of Pelargir.Talk to Golodir somewhere south of Corunir, in PelargirCalenglad is at the Camp of the Grey Host, outside the port-city of Pelargir.

Golodir was possessed of an unusually ill temper, and you should return to Calenglad and see what he thinks of the Grey Company's mood following the battle.Talk to Calenglad at the Camp of the Grey Host'I like this land, ${PLAYER}. It reminds me of the twilit wilds, far away in the North, where my heart remains. The wooded slopes encircling Lake Nenuial must be slowly shaking off the grip of winter, and in a few weeks the leaves of their trees may begin to fight through the remaining chill. No Winter lasts forever, and the coming of Spring will banish even the coldest of memories. Will this war be any different?'

Calenglad stands silently for a short while, musing on the question. Eventually he smiles, and gives you a conspiratorial wink.

'Radanir told me you agreed to treat him to a meal and drinks at a tavern of your choosing, once this is all over. If you have no objection, I would like to join the two of you there. I will look forward to that happy meeting!

'But there is still much hardship ahead of us, and with all my musings I forgot to tell you something! Halbarad wanted me to send you to him. He has returned to Pelargir already. If you leave now you should find him just inside the north-west entrance to the city.''With all my musings I forgot to tell you something! Halbarad wanted me to send you to him. He has returned to Pelargir already. If you leave now you should find him just inside the north-west entrance to the city.''I would follow Aragorn anywhere, ${PLAYER}, and standing here with the smell of smoke on the air and the memory of battle still fresh in my mind, I feel as if I have done so!

'I did not blanch when he led us through the Dimholt road, nor when we emerged from the mountain with the shades of Dead Men all around us. Still, I am glad they have departed. It is not natural for such men to linger in the world. An Elf once told me that death is a gift for all Men, but I did not understand what he meant until today. I think now he was right.

'The appearance of the Dead Men may weigh heavily upon some others of our company. Golodir in particular seemed distraught by the passage under the mountain. Find Corunir, somewhere to the south of here, and see if he knows where Golodir can be found. The two of them are rarely apart.''The appearance of the Dead Men may weigh heavily upon some others of our company. Golodir in particular seemed distraught by the passage under the mountain. Find Corunir, somewhere to the south of here, and see if he knows where Golodir can be found. The two of them are rarely apart.''I am not in the mood to talk, ${PLAYER}. This has been a trying experience for all of us. I hope you do not mind.'

As you turn to go, Corunir holds up his hand.

'I am sorry, friend. I am fine. With what can I help you?'

You tell him that you are looking for Golodir, and thought he might know where you could find the Ranger.

'Golodir is the cause of my ill temperament and I have no desire to speak with him any further. Do you remember the fell mood that seized him in Angmar, before the Grey Company was assembled? I thought he had left it behind him. Indeed, it seemed that the journey was doing him some good, but it returned again in Tûr Morva, only to be overcome by the gentle laughter of the Dunlending girl, Wenda.

'But like the rising and falling brimstone pools of Malenhad, his ill mood has returned after a short time at low ebb, and now he shouts at his own dear friends! If you seek him out, know that I left him standing on a dock to the south, looking out at the water. I will not be held responsible if he treats you unkindly, for it seems he does not know his friends.''Golodir's ill mood has returned after a short time at low ebb, and now he shouts at his own dear friends! If you seek him out, know that I left him standing on a dock to the south, looking out at the water. I will not be held responsible if he treats you unkindly, for it seems he does not know his friends.''Yes? What do you want? Are you here to chastise me for treating Corunir unkindly? He has been a sharp thorn for these past few days, constantly asking questions about my well-being. He should have expected a rebuke!'

There is an angry fire in Golodir's eyes, an unfamiliar, smouldering rage.

'I know why, ${PLAYER}. Around matters of the Dead they treat me with delicate gloves. They look at me and they see my daughter, Lorniel. My grief was strong and will never truly pass, but this treatment fills me with such an anger I would strike them all down: Corunir, Halbarad, even my own chief, Aragorn!'

He falls silent. When he speaks again, his voice is little more than a whisper, and there are tears in his eyes.

'I... I do not know from where this anger comes, ${PLAYER}. It feels as if I hear voices of the past, unflagging and unrelenting, calling my name, taunting me...

'I will master these voices. I have been troubled by these feelings ever since we began this journey, and I have managed to stifle them. It has become more difficult of late, perhaps because of the unnatural skies... the evil presence of the Dead Men...

'Do not worry about me, my friend. I will apologize to Corunir, and my outburst will remain between us, I hope?''I will master these voices. I have been troubled by these feelings ever since we began this journey, and I have managed to stifle them. It has become more difficult of late, perhaps because of the unnatural skies... the evil presence of the Dead Men...

'Do not worry about me, my friend. I will apologize to Corunir, and my outburst will remain between us, I hope?''Did you find Halbarad? Were you able to help him with whatever it was he needed?'

You ask Calenglad how he feels about the mood of the Grey Company.

'I think we are doing fine, for the most part. We have been through a great deal already, and there is more hardship ahead, but if we stay true to each other there is nothing we need fear.

'We have lost friends along the way, and brothers, but our memories of them give us the strength to go on. If I close my eyes I can still hear their voices, and they urge me to stand with Aragorn and honour my pledge. And one day, you will buy Radanir and Daervunn and me a meal, and drinks, and we will all toast the successful journey of the Grey Company.

'Oh, yes. I told Daervunn about our agreement once this is all done. I did not think you would mind.'Building a horn to summon reinforcements to Tol Ascarnen requires that several pieces be collected and assembled for an Orc to watch over and use when the Free Peoples make their assault against the fort.The Leafcull Huorns make their home in the southern woods of Hithlad. Find the Huorn amonst the trees south of the Grimwood Lumber Camp in Hithlad, to the south-west of Tol Ascarnen.

Angmar's forces at Tol Ascarnen are building a horn to call reinforcements to the fight when the Free Peoples return to assault the area. Kaasak has tasked you with collecting the heartwood of a Leafcull Huorn to act as the stand upon which the horn will rest. Damaging the heartwood will make it useless.'Listen well. In order to build a horn with which to call reinforcements to Tol Ascarnen, we need to gather some more parts, such as a strong and tall piece of heartwood to place the horn and sinew upon. Your task is to head south into Hithlad -- more importantly, the Grimwood -- to collect the heartwood of a Leafcull Huorn.

'You must be certain to collect a perfect heartwood, for no other will be able to withstand an assault from the Free Peoples. We must build a strong horn, understand?

'Go now and collect the heartwood from the Leafcull in Hithald.''A good find. When the horn and sinew are collected, we will have all that we need to build the horn.'The Haradrim and Corsairs have laid claim to Dol Brannor, and now their cruel archers use the beacon's perch to slay those who would dare to travel the roads below.Dol Brannor can be found to north-west of Linhir in Dor-en-Ernil.

Herion has asked you to patrol the beacon of Dol Brannor and defeat any of the Enemy's forces that have made camp there.Patrol Dol BrannorCorsair snipers can be found around Dol Brannor in Dor-en-Ernil.

You have discovered a group of Corsairs led by a Haradrim War-master. You should defeat them before they can slay more travellers on the roads below.Defeat Corsair snipers at Dol Brannor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat the Haradrim War-master at Dol BrannorHerion can be found in Linhir in eastern Dor-en-Ernil.

You have crippled the forces at Dol Brannor, at least for the moment, and should now return to Herion.Talk to Herion in Linhir'I have heard talk of more violence to the north-west. Our scouts lead us to believe that the Corsairs have seized Dol Brannor, one of our south-beacons, and now use it as a perch to slay our people from afar.

'They cannot be allowed to hold such an advantage within our lands, for it makes supplying our men a great challenge and travel nigh impossible.

'Travel to Dol Brannor and see if their forces are still camped at the beacon. If they have been so bold, make certain they regret their arrogance.''High-ground or not, I hope the Corsairs and Haradrim know to keep away from our beacons now.

'Should they be foolish enough to attempt to reclaim it, I shall call upon your aid once more. You have my thanks.'The Corsairs and Haradrim make ready once more for battle at Linhir as the Heirs of Castamir seek to avenge their fallen.Erchar can be found in Linhir in eastern Dor-en-Ernil.

Erchar has asked you to aid him in protecting Linhir from a renewed attack. You should speak to him when you are ready.Talk to ErcharErchar can be found in Linhir in eastern Dor-en-Ernil.

You have defeated the attacking forces from Dor-en-Ernil and Lebennin, but at the cost of Garvir's life. You should speak to Erchar.Talk to Erchar in Linhir'You have returned to us just in time, ${PLAYER}.

'We have heard the horns and shouts of the Corsairs and Haradrim in the distance. They come again for Linhir and I doubt there shall be a host of spirits to drive them off this time....

'I fear this shall be no short battle, for your efforts on the coast have surely raised their ire. When you are ready, we shall take the western gates, and my brothers shall guard the entrance to Lebennin.

'Ready your arms, my friend!''Let us face down these invaders together, my friend!''I have failed my brother, ${PLAYER}....

'He had the best mind of us all for how to approach battle, and I allowed him to fall. I should have been here... I should have -'

Erchar trails off, obviously overcome.

'You have done much for my brothers and I, my friend... I have but one more thing to ask of you.'Steward Malthellim's curiosity grows as the rumours of the Heirs of Castamir continue to spread throughout Lebennin.Malthellim's folk can be found throughout Ost Anglebed.

Steward Malthellim has asked you to speak to the people of Ost Anglebed about the various rumours surrounding the Heirs of Castamir.Nimithil can be found in the lowest section of Ost Anglebed with her guards.

You have spoken to many folk of Ost Anglebed and should now seek out Nimithil.Talk to Nimithil in Ost Anglebed'Rumours surround these men known as the Heirs of Castamir for his name was once held in great regard in Gondor... at least by some. There was a dark time in our history known as the Kin-strife, when our people fought amongst themselves to determine who was the true claimant to the throne. 

'Castamir and his followers forced the opposition from these lands and soon ruled in Pelargir, but he was killed years later. The Kin-strife's ending was a bloody one, ${PLAYER}, and as long as it has been, many of the wounds between brothers have never fully healed.

'I have heard my own folk talk of them, and I grow more curious as to what they believe these Heirs seek in Gondor.''I care not if they are of the blood-line - Castamir the Usurper was a monster who spat in the faces of his people, and these brutal men have proven to be no better than him. To grant any of them the throne would be madness!

'Were I a warrior, I would drive them from Gondor myself!''I have heard rumours of the might of the one they call the Scourge. It is said he commands a vast army and can outsmart any foe in battle.

'It has been many years since Gondor has had a King upon the throne, and should he return the throne to Pelargir, the people of Lebennin might prosper greatly. This fighting is brutal to be sure, but for this cause, I would throw my lot in with him.''I do not care for this man or the outcome of his bid for the throne. I merely wish to make it through this war with my family intact.

'If he can stop the fighting, so be it.''The folk of my line once counted themselves amongst the followers of Castamir. His dishonour tainted our family's name for years, but perhaps his descendants can restore it.

'The people of Gondor have waited long enough for the rightful Heir - let us celebrate anew!''I do not trust these rumours... Castamir's sons escaped when he was slain, but I do not believe his line could have continued for so long.

'I will follow them if it allows me to keep my life, but they are cruel, vicious men and I do not think them proper leaders for Gondor.''I am Nimithil of Pelargir. Why have you come to me?'

You introduce yourself and tell Nimithil of the Steward's curiosity.

'I do not care to know their goals, ${PLAYER}. When you bear the name of one as reviled as Castamir the Usurper, you cannot seek to do good for the people of Gondor.

'Trouble is growing in Lebennin, and it must be dealt with soon.'If Rovalang saw a Nazgûl on its winged steed, it must be prevented from learning of Aragorn's passage with the army of the Dead, at any cost.Rovalang is on the slopes of the Hill of Erech, in the Blackroot Vale.

The Nazgûl must be prevented from learning of Aragorn's passage with the army of the Dead, at any cost.Talk to Rovalang and face 'The Shadow in Morthond'Rovalang is on the slopes of the Hill of Erech, in the Blackroot Vale.

With the help of Rovalang, you have driven off the Nazgûl that threatened to learn of Aragorn's passage with the army of the Dead.Talk to Rovalang on the Hill of Erech, in Blackroot ValeLothíriel is in the Great Hall of Dol Amroth.

You faced a Nazgûl in the Blackroot Vale, and though it has been driven away formless to its master, this is a clear sign that the Great Enemy has Gondor much on his mind.Talk to Lothíriel in the Great Hall of Dol Amroth'You have heard of this winged creature, ${PLAYER}... I can see it in your eye. Will you help me find it and keep it from harming the people of the Vale?'

You nod in agreement. If the Nazgûl learns that Aragorn commands an army of the Dead, much harm might be done to the desperate hope that drives him, and to the people that depend on him.

'Well, let us go at once! If you have a Fellowship at your call, bring them along as well. There will be danger enough for all... but now let us chase this foe! I saw it fly in the direction of the haunted cave to the north-west. Perhaps we can bring it down, if we hurry!''I do not know what that creature might be, but I know it is my duty to face it. We will face it together! If you have a Fellowship at your call, bring them along as well!''I think you knew more about that creature than you let on, my friend. I am glad you were here! My tale might have ended in the shadow of the haunted mountain if you were not! But we have both faced great evil and lived, and our tales go on.

'That creature brings to mind a name from the ancient years, a name of great evil. I will not speak this name, but I have now seen with my own eyes the servant of that terrible Enemy.

'The time has come to return to Dol Amroth. Lothíriel must be told.''We should return to Dol Amroth at once. Lothíriel must be told that servants of the Great Enemy walk in Gondor!''My swan-knights have returned, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for bringing them my message. I see from your face that you have more to say to me. What is on your mind?'

You tell Lothíriel of your encounter with the Nazgûl in the Blackroot Vale. She is silent for a long time. When she speaks again, her voice is very quiet, the words not much more than a whisper.

'I remember the heat of summer on the stones of the White City, ${PLAYER}. It was late in the month of June, and my father brought me and my brothers to Minas Tirith with a dozen riders. He did not reveal the purpose of the journey until we were in the Steward's chamber: I was to marry Boromir, the son of my father's sister. He was tall, and confident, and a great warrior, and I could tell that he too did not want the match.

'But he was an obedient son, and consented to the betrothal. There would have been a wedding on that warm night of June, but Boromir had a journey of his own to make, and it was decided the wedding would occur on his return. 'I will bring you a wedding gift,' he said to me before I joined my father and brothers in the courtyard for the return to Dol Amroth. 'I will win this war and bring you the peace you deserve!' he called to me. I never saw him again.'

Lothíriel's eyes are fierce, her mouth a thin line sharp with resolve.

'I did not want to marry him, and he did not want to marry me. But we wanted the same thing. We wanted peace and happiness for our people, and we were willing to sacrifice whatever we needed to achieve it for them. Our enemies have underestimated us. They think we are soft. They think we will give in to despair and give in to grief. But we will not.

'I spent more time in study than the archivist Pethebir believed. I know the name of the Great Enemy, the master of the foe you faced in the Blackroot Vale. I am not afraid to utter his name. He is Sauron, and we will stand against him to the last man, woman, and child. Gondor is not without strength. We will face the great strength of Sauron and we will defeat him, or we will die. There are no other choices for us.

'I will call for you, ${PLAYER}. Enemies are everywhere, and we have only just begun to fight.'Lothíriel knows that further battles approach, and she wants you to judge the sea-power currently at her disposal in Dol Amroth.Maeral is the captain of the Alphroval, the northernmost of the ships docked at the wharf at Dol Amroth.

Lothíriel has asked you to visit the captains at the wharf, in order to assess the sea-worthiness and battle-readiness of their ships.Talk to Captain Maeral of the Alphroval, the northernmost ship docked at the wharfThe deck of the Alphroval, northernmost of the ships docked at the wharf in Dol Amroth, will give you a good vantage from which to look at the Sea-gate.

Captain Maeral wants you to look at the Sea-gate from where you stand on the deck of his ship.Maeral is the captain of the Alphroval, the northernmost of the ships docked at the wharf at Dol Amroth.

You have done as Captain Maeral asked, and should now speak with him about what you saw.Talk to Captain Maeral of the Alphroval, the northernmost ship docked at the wharfNoreth is the captain of the Rosigil, the easternmost of the ships docked at the wharf at Dol Amroth.

Lothíriel has asked you to visit the captains at the wharf, in order to assess the sea-worthiness and battle-readiness of their ships.Talk to Captain Noreth of the Rosigil, the easternmost ship docked at the wharfThe deck of the Rosigil, easternmost of the ships docked at the wharf in Dol Amroth, will give you a good vantage from which to look through the Sea-gate, there to see whatever it is that Captain Noreth wants you to see.

Captain Noreth wants you to look through the Sea-gate from where you stand on the deck of her ship.Noreth is the captain of the Rosigil, the easternmost of the ships docked at the wharf at Dol Amroth.

You have done as Captain Noreth asked, and should now speak with her about what you saw.Talk to Captain Noreth of the Rosigil, the easternmost ship docked at the wharfCirnaith is the captain of the Súlrohir, the southernmost of the ships docked at the wharf at Dol Amroth.

Lothíriel has asked you to visit the captains at the wharf, in order to assess the sea-worthiness and battle-readiness of their ships.Talk to Captain Cirnaith of the Súlrohir, the southernmost ship docked at the wharf'I know that further battle with the Corsairs is unavoidable, ${PLAYER}. As ruler of the city, it is my task to defend the people of Dol Amroth for as long as I am able. To do that, I must learn what I can of our weaknesses and our strengths. May our strengths outnumber our weaknesses, as unlikely as that may seem!

'If you will help me in this, go to the Wharf and speak to the captains of the ships moored there. They will know the sea-worthiness of their own ships, and whether they might be made battle-ready.

'The true sea-power of Gondor is harboured at the city of Pelargir, but that port is far to the east and we cannot depend upon its ships for aid in time.''Go to the Wharf and speak to the captains of the ships moored there. They will know the sea-worthiness of their own ships, and whether they might be made battle-ready.

'The true sea-power of Gondor is harboured at the city of Pelargir, but that port is far to the east and we cannot depend upon its ships for aid in time.''The daughter of Imrahil wishes to know how much sea-power she commands? Very little, truth be told. I am afraid the Alphroval and these few others will have to stand for her fleet, unless she can request aid from Pelargir.

'<rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> to the Sea-gate, if you wish to know the truth of it.''<rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> to the Sea-gate, if you wish to know the truth of the situation we face.''You see the truth of it? Numerous Corsair ships lie beyond the Sea-gate, and every unfriendly eye on the deck of each is trained upon that narrow space. Any Gondorian ship that tries to pass into open water would be savaged by the weapons of Umbar before reaching freedom or launching any attack.

'We are hemmed in and cannot escape. If you speak to Captain Noreth of the Rosigil, she will tell you much the same.''We are hemmed in and cannot escape. If you speak to Captain Noreth of the Rosigil, she will tell you much the same.''Before I became captain of my own ship, I served two years before the mast on the Alphroval. I know Maeral to be a wise captain and judge of the seas, but he is prone to recklessness. If he believes passage through the Sea-gate is a foolish journey, it must truly be so.

'Do you see the mighty ship in the waters outside the city? <rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> through the Sea-gate and tell me what you see.''<rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> through the Sea-gate and you will see why we cannot hope to leave the city that way.''See how that monster makes the other ships look like children's toys floating in a stream? That is the flagship of the Umbar fleet, Nûluzimir in their tongue: 'Night-jewel.'

'Many Gondorian ships have succumbed to the attacks of the Night-jewel during the years since the disappearance of Thorongil, greatest foe of the Corsairs! Would that he had returned before the Night-jewel was constructed, and might have prevented it! But none have seen Thorongil since he routed the Corsairs almost forty years ago. He is likely dead.

'I would not sail my ship against the Night-jewel unless I wanted her to lie on the sea-bottom, ${PLAYER}. None of our ships are a match for that one, not even Cirnaith's Súlrohir. Speak to him and see if he thinks otherwise.''I have only met Captain Cirnaith of the Súlrohir in passing, but I do know that he sent his crew into the city to make what merriment they might. He must believe there is nothing they can do ship-side to face the dangers that approach.''Lothíriel wants a report from the captains of the ships docked at her city? There are no good tidings, friend. If there is any good fortune here it is that very few ships are trapped at the wharf, and most of Gondor's fleet must be found at the port city of Pelargir, to the east.

'They are too far away to help us here, but at least those ships are not caught like rabbits in a trap, as we are. It is hopeless!'The ships docked at Dol Amroth are trapped at the wharf by the Umbar fleet, unable to leave.The crew of the Súlrohir are making as much merriment as they can in the Harper's Court on the west side of the city of Dol Amroth.

Captain Cirnaith, fearing embarrassment, wants you to recall his crew to the Súlrohir.Find crew of the Súlrohir and tell them to return to the ship (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have sent the crew of the Súlrohir back to their ship, and should now return to the wharf on the eastern side of Dol Amroth and see if the situation has changed.

There has been a commotion on the jetty that points north-east into the water. You should investigate and learn what has happened.Return to the Súlrohir and study the situationUmer, an emissary of Umbar, has passed through the Sea-gate under a flag of peace. He wishes to speak with you on the docks on the eastern side of Dol Amroth.Talk to Umer, the Emissary of UmbarLothíriel is in the Great Hall of Dol Amroth.

You have a message for Lothíriel from an emissary of Umbar, asking her to treat with his master on the deck of the flagship Night-jewel.Talk to Lothíriel in the Great Hall'I did not think there was much to be gained by keeping my crew here, and the sight of the fleet beyond the Sea-gate seemed likely to put my men in poor spirits. I gave them leave to find what merriment they might in the city.

'But if Lothíriel has not given up hope, I fear their absence will prove an embarrassment to me! Go to the Harper's Court, on the west side of the city, and tell my crewmen to return to the Súlrohir! We will do what we can to make her ready for battle, as unlikely a victory as that seems to be.''If Lothíriel has not given up hope, I fear the absence of my crew will prove an embarrassment to me! Go to the Harper's Court, on the west side of the city, and tell my crewmen to return to the Súlrohir! We will do what we can to make her ready for battle, as unlikely a victory as that seems to be.''We were on our way back to the Súlrohir when we beheld this sight! Noreth is not our captain, but we would not stand by while she faces down this creature of Umbar!''Be careful, friend! These Corsairs passed through the Sea-gate under a flag of peace, but I do not trust them and you would be wise not to do so either!''Be careful, friend! These Corsairs passed through the Sea-gate under a flag of peace, but I do not trust them and you would be wise not to do so either!''I bring a message for the ruler of this city. Can you take me to this man?'

Umer sees something in your eye, and smirks.

'So it is true: a woman rules this city? Bring me before her and I will deliver my message.'

You tell the emissary that you will bring the message, but not the messenger. He nods, apparently impressed with your response.

'Tell her this, then: Balakhôr the Scourge, Heir of Castamir and ruler of every Gondorian shore licked by the waves, invites her to treat with him on the deck of the mighty ship Nûluzimir. I will await your response.''This is my message: Balakhôr the Scourge, Heir of Castamir and ruler of every Gondorian shore licked by the waves, invites the ruler of this city to treat with him on the deck of the mighty ship Nûluzimir. I will await your response.''I have come to Dol Amroth to advise Lothíriel. Neither her people nor mine approve of this, but I care not.''You must bring that message to Lothíriel, ${PLAYER}. Do not worry! We will watch these treacherous snakes and ensure they do not try anything.''How do the ships fare, ${PLAYER}? Is it as grim an outlook as I have heard?'

You tell Lothíriel what you witnessed at the wharf.

'That is a fair measure of what I expected. But I see that you have more to say. What else can there be?'Balakhôr the Scourge has sent an emissary to Dol Amroth to request that Lothíriel come to treat with him on the deck of his flagship, the Night-jewel.Lothíriel needs your help to decide what should be done.

Lothíriel is in the Great Hall of Dol Amroth, considering Balakhôr's proposal.Talk to Lothíriel and help her decide what to do about Balakhôr's proposalYou were cast from the deck of the Night-jewel, and have managed to struggle back to the dock.

Captain Noreth has fished you from the water, and wants to hear what happened on the Night-jewel.Talk to Noreth on the docksLothíriel is in the Great Hall of Dol Amroth.

Balakhôr the Scourge has boasted of his plan to you, and now that you know his course you should bring word of it to Lothíriel.Talk to Lothíriel in the Great Hall of Dol AmrothThe town of Ethring is in the Ringló Vale, on the road to Pelargir.

Lothíriel has asked you to travel through the Ringló Vale and Dor-en-Ernil to help the folk of those lands and bring warning to the people of Pelargir.Travel to the town of Ethring, in the Ringló ValeTologlor in the town of Ethring has tried to catch your eye. His expression is one of frightened concern.Talk to Tologlor in EthringLothíriel listens impassively as you give her the message from the emissary of Umbar.

'Thank you for bringing this message to me. Something must be done, that much is certain! But what, I wonder?''I value your opinion and place it among the ranks of my other advisors. If you can spare the time, I could use your counsel, ${PLAYER}.''I deem you were near the end of your strength when I pulled you from the water, ${PLAYER}. Any delay and I do not know that we would have gotten to you in time.

'Curse Balakhôr and all his kind! Mark my words well: that scoundrel will pay for this betrayal. This is the sort of treachery I would expect from anyone calling himself 'Heir of Castamir!'

'Did you learn anything of his plans? You should bring word of them to Lothíriel at once!''Did you learn anything of Balakhôr's plans? You should bring word of them to Lothíriel at once!'You tell Lothíriel of Balakhôr's plan to destroy the Gondorian fleet at Pelargir, and she nods.

'That is what I suspected, but it is good to hear it directly. It seems that Balakhôr is content to keep the ships of Dol Amroth trapped in the city, and will sail with as much force as he can spare to Pelargir, where he will try to take the rest of the fleet unawares.

'I need you to ride there and warn them, ${PLAYER}! Travel through the Ringló Vale and Dor-en-Ernil, telling the people there to seek shelter from their foes, and come to Pelargir as quickly as you can.

'I have not been to the Ringló Vale for some months, but I remember the town of Ethring will be on your way. Stop there on your journey and tell them what is happening, and to be strong for my father's sake, and for mine!''I have not been to the Ringló Vale for some months, but I remember the town of Ethring will be on your way. Stop there on your journey and tell them what is happening, and to be strong for my father's sake, and for mine!''You have the look of an adventurer! Have you ever seen anything like this before? The sun never rose this morning! Some evil has been unleashed upon us!'Dark shadows cover the skies of the Ringló Vale, and the people of Ethring fear what it might mean.Orcs can be found to the north-east of Ethring, in the Ringló Vale. Suitable tinder can also be found to the north-east.

Tologlor has asked you to defeat Orcs made bold by the continuing night that blankets the Ringló Vale, and to gather suitable tinder to light the town's torches.Defeat Orcs in the Ringló Vale (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect suitable tinder (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tologlor is at the entrance of the town of Ethring, waiting for you to return with news of your successes against the Orcs. He also awaits delivery of any suitable tinder you have found.Standing torches can be found throughout Ethring. You should relight any of them that have gone out.Tologlor is at the entrance to the town of Ethring, in the Ringló Vale.

You have lit some of the torches throughout Ethring, trying to drive back the darkness that overpowers the Vale and its people.Talk to Tologlor at the entrance to Ethring'Is this some sorcery? Do our foes have the power to blanket the skies in shadow? How can we hope to fight against such evils, even with such strong men as Prince Imrahil taking up the sword?

'The continuing night has caused the Orcs to grow more bold, and they have come down out of the mountains after a respite of many years. If you truly are an adventurer, you would do a great service to my people by slaying these creatures and driving them back into the mountains. Look for the vile creatures to the north-east of town.

'We could also use a supply of suitable tinder. If the darkness does not abate soon, we will have need to keep the torches lit.''The continuing night has caused the Orcs to grow more bold, and they have come down out of the mountains after a respite of many years. If you truly are an adventurer, you would do a great service to my people by slaying these creatures and driving them back into the mountains. Look for the vile creatures to the north-east of town.

'We could also use a supply of suitable tinder. If the darkness does not abate soon, we will have need to keep the torches lit.''Thank you for teaching the Orcs we will not allow them to overrun the Vale. Whether that lesson proves more convincing than that they are given by the continuing night that blankets the sky, well... we will have to see.

'If the darkness does not abate, we will need to keep a store of tinder with which to light the torches of Ethring. Please take some of the wood you have collected and relight any of the torches that have gone out.

'The shadow may be unrelenting, but we will not cower before it. We will drive it back with light wherever we are able!''If the darkness does not abate, we will need to keep a store of tinder with which to light the torches of Ethring. Please take some of the wood you have collected and relight any of the torches that have gone out.

'The shadow may be unrelenting, but we will not cower before it. We will drive it back with light wherever we are able!''Thank you for lighting the torches around town. Will this night ever end? When will we see the sun again?'The darkness that covers the Ringló Vale flowed out of the south-east. What evil has the shadow wrought in Dor-en-Ernil?Tologlor has asked you to check on his brother Ingalad in the town of Linhir, to the south of Ethring in Dor-en-Ernil.

