Nobor was slain by the summoning of the rogmul. You should examine his body before leaving the Old Archives.Examine NoborIt has been a day filled with ill omens. Nobor's was a cruel fate - even for one of the Withered Tree.'We are nearing the proper section, ${PLAYER}. It is just ahead - up the stairs and to the left.

'It is there that we have catalogued our extensive, albeit woefully incomplete, knowledge of the various languages of Gondor, Rhûn, Harad, and beyond. If the scholars of Minas Tirith have what this scroll-thief seeks, it must be among the texts stored there.

'You may proceed at your own discretion, but I urge you to be swift. I will remain here and ensure that no one makes for the door.'You warn Nobor that he does not understand that the scroll is a tool of the Enemy.

'The words of yet another dog, happy to feast on scraps at the feet of the Steward! The Enemy shall claim all soon enough - better we position ourselves to be spared his full wrath. 

'The Steward is powerless, and his mind betrays him! If he would treat his own son so shamefully, then how lowly are we to him?

'No, I cannot surrender now. The Steward must be slain!'Nobor's body lies motionless on the floor of the Old Archives - his face frozen in horror. His body bears no wounds, but all life is gone from him.

The scroll's origin may or may not have been unknown to Nobor, but it is clear that the power it contained was beyond his imagining and control.

Mithrandir's warning was not misplaced - he must be told of what has occurred.Steward Denethor has summoned you to an audience in the Tower of Ecthelion.Peregrin Took has led you into the Tower of Ecthelion for your audience with Steward Denethor.Talk to Peregrin Took in the Tower HallSteward Denethor is in the Tower Hall, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Denethor in the Tower HallDenethor wishes to show you something, and has invited you to a secret chamber in the Tower of Ecthelion.Follow DenethorDenethor has brought you to a secret chamber high in the Tower of Ecthelion.Talk to Denethor in his secret chamberDenethor has instructed you to look into the depths of the palantír.Look into the depths of the palantírHazy images swirl before your eyes.Hazy images swirl before your eyes!Hazy images swirl before your eyes.Talk to DenethorHazy images swirl before your eyes.Hazy images swirl before your eyes!Hazy images swirl before your eyes.Talk to DenethorHazy images swirl before your eyes.Hazy images swirl before your eyes!Hazy images swirl before your eyes.Talk to DenethorHazy images swirl before your eyes.Examine the captive hobbitThe images fade, becoming more and more difficult to see clearly.The hazy images become more indistinct!You are standing by the palantír in Denethor's secret chamber, trying to shake the troubling images from your mind. Even so, they linger still.Talk to Denethor in his secret chamber'Listen to my words... hear them and know they are true, for the doom of Gondor is at hand!''This is the Tower Hall, ${PLAYER}. Do not tell anyone, but the Steward makes me nervous. I will show you where to go and then return to my posting outside, glad he does not need me at the moment!''Imrahil told me you were with my son when he fell before the city, ${PLAYER}. Would that you had drawn his foes away, or put yourself between his horse and the wraiths! He fights still, but it is an unnatural wound. It saps his strength, and his body is wasting away. My son!'

Denethor's voice breaks, and his eye is drawn back to Faramir's bed. He shakes his head, and when he speaks again his voice is full of iron and his fists are clenched.

'Do you know what it is like to live so near to Mordor, ${PLAYER}? Do you know what it is like to stand on the wall of your city and see flames on the horizon, knowing that they signal some terrible work of evil? Do you know what it is to be always in the thought of creatures so vile and so terrible that even Death cannot master them?

'I know what it is like, ${CLASS}. Every morning I wake up knowing it, and every evening I retire to my chamber knowing it. And in the darkest reaches of the night, I wake from troubled dreams knowing it.

'I have struggled long with this knowledge, and I struggle still, though my sons and heirs be taken from me. I struggle for my people.''No one comes to this chamber without my leave, ${PLAYER}, and I grant that sparingly! When I look at you I see a student of Mithrandir. The Wizard has been a friend to Gondor, so far, but he does not have the full measure of my trust. Why should he possess it? He is not of this land, and if the battle turns ill he will simply leave it, leaving my people to suffer and die!

'I am not without weapons, ${PLAYER}. I brought you here so you will see that I still fight the Enemy, though none appreciate the battle I wage within this high chamber!

'This is a seeing-stone, an heirloom of the Kings of old. I have turned it to my will.''Osgiliath was our last line of defence, and now it is taken! The armies of the Enemy spill onto the plain, and their siege engines and weapons of war eagerly follow!

'The forces arrayed against us are vast, and they hunger for our destruction!''Hirgon was to present the Red Arrow to Théoden of the Riddermark, and by doing so summon the Riders of Rohan to our defence. He has been gone for more than a week, and now I see that he is slain!

'No one is coming to help us!''Mithrandir does not share his mind with me, but I know of the dream that came to my sons, the dream that tried to snare Faramir but stole Boromir instead. This must be the Halfling, captured by the Enemy and held in his tower!

'We are undone by the folly of Mithrandir!''I have seen a great deal in the seeing-stone, ${CLASS}, but these terrible visions fill me with dread as never before!

'You have seen what I saw in the stone. Hirgon and the Red Arrow did not make it to Rohan, and the foolish one Mithrandir sent to Mordor is a captive in Cirith Ungol! Yes, I know of that errand. And I know it has failed! Whatever that fool brought with him is now gone, and if it is the weapon I perceive, it will not matter if the gates of Minas Tirith last five minutes or five hours. The end will come!

'Listen to my words, ${PLAYER}. Hear what I say, and tremble at the truth in them! There can be no turning back from this doom. It comes even now...'Now that Artamir has agreed to leave the Dead Marshes, you wonder if Frodo passed this way after all.You stare at the supplies Artamir brought to the Dead Marshes and wonder if Frodo took this treacherous route after all.Examine Artamir's supplies and contemplate 'The Passage of the Marshes'Artamir brought many supplies with him to the Dead Marshes. If Frodo did pass this way, he would not have had as many. What hardships might he have faced on this road?Examine the supplies Artamir brought to the Dead Marshes, and break from your reverie concerning FrodoArtamir has agreed to leave the Dead Marshes, but as you stare at the Gondorian man's supplies, you wonder if Frodo ever did pass this way. If he did, he was much less-equipped than Artamir. Your mind wanders as you contemplate his hardships.Your mind wanders as you contemplate the many supplies Artamir brought to the Dead Marshes. If Frodo did pass this way, what hardships might he have faced in these treacherous lands?You shake your head to dismiss the troubling thoughts of the hardships Frodo might have faced if his road truly did lead into the Dead Marshes. You are not even certain he came this way, but if he did, at least he likely had the companionship of a good friend to keep him going. The two of them would do their best to remain hidden from the eyes of any enemies, and with luck they may remain undetected.

There is nothing you can do now for Frodo and Sam, but by convincing Artamir to leave the Dead Marshes you will at least keep the Gondorian man from attracting attention to these lands. May it be enough.Arodor sends you on a mission to find Faramir in Osgiliath.The Morgul Vale lies on the north-eastern border of South Ithilien.

