Raise a final glass and drink with Forlong and company.Raise a glassThe Merry Swan Tavern is on the southern end of the Third Circle.

Hear what Forlong has to say.Talk to Forlong'Battles to come! Enemy hosts! Why, there is one perched under the very lintel of Minas Tirith, and here we have been, sitting, utterly ignorant of how they are getting on! Friend ${CLASS}, you are up and we, as you can see, are down. Would you dash outside, ascend the wall here, and gaze upon the state of the opposing force?''Mind you, we three are ready to leap up at a moment's notice and charge into battle. Even if I must do so with this blasted broken buckle! But we shall not leap spuriously, before the call, no!''They yet prepare. That is well. Let us err on the side of caution and vigilance, all the same. Will you find Húrin, Master of the Keys, and inquire as to our general state of readiness, and whether there are final preparations to which we might lend our hands?

'Húrin was down by the City Gates, last I saw him.''Go find Húrin, Warden of the Keys, by the City Gates. See if there are final preparations to which we might lend our hands.''All greater measures have been attended to. The lesser ones are well in hand. Soon we shall have naught but to watch and wait. Neither Lord Denethor nor Mithrandir can foresee or even guess at what the Enemy plans. I know, for I have sat in their councils.

'As to Lords Forlong and Hirluin... if their men are at the ready and they come swiftly when the horns blow, that is all that can be asked of them.''There would be little point in giving ready fighting men errands about the city. It would only slow their response to the Enemy's attack - and we must assume they may attack at any moment, now.'Naught to do but to wait, eh? My axe will be ready, come what may!''Have you heard there are axe-wielding Easterlings upon the field? So the scouts are saying. They have beards like dwarves and call themselves Sûhalar. Imagine you cross men such as they in battle, Forlong. Would there be confusion amongst these Sûhalar and the Men of Lossarnach?''One must wonder, Forlong, if these be ancient cousins of your kind. Neldir, do they all have beards, every one?''Cousins? Because they hold axes? What nonsense! Clearly they are dwarf-kin and no blood of mine!''I know not if they have beards to the very last man. But by the reports, Lord Forlong, they sound much like your people in Lossarnach, who have less of the blood of Númenor. That is where I cast my vote.''I shall take no sides, but only say that if every man among them has a beard, they are dwarf-kin. But if their beards vary as we do in height and the colour of our eyes, then I declare them Men.''They are dwarves, and I will have it shown! Our new friend here looks well-travelled and up for some fighting. Perhaps you could bring us back some Sûhalar-beards to settle this dispute.''You must settle this dispute for us. Slip out of the City and deprive some Sûhalar of their beards, so we may look over them and judge.''Our new friend returns! And have you beards? Let us look over them and judge.''It is just as I said, they are like unto dwarf beards.''Those seem like the beards of mortal Men to me, only braided in a foreign fashion.''Well! At least there are a handful fewer enemies to fight. That is well done, ${CLASS}, no matter who is right.''Bah! Why can you old fools not see that I am plainly right?''Do you see, Forlong, we have fallen to quarreling again. If Mithrandir were here, I think he would say it was the nearness of the Enemy, for discord and strife springs up wherever that shadow falls. Now let us take your own advice, friend, and make peace by raising a glass among well-loved friends.''That was well-spoken, Hirluin. And I hold that we must ever keep love's warmth kindled in our hearts, if we are to prevail against that which shall not be named.''Not named, but too soon joined in battle, I fear!''For this moment, I shall not dwell on that, Neldir. I am here with you, old friends and new, in a City I have loved dearly since my youth. And how could I not love it, when it was here that I found Vanyalos, my heart's desire? It was here I wooed her first, and then afterwards in the flowering fields of Lossarnach, my home.''How people talked! Terenis spurned and her sister Vanyalos wooed in her stead. To this day, I am amazed that you dared defy Lord Denethor in which of his sisters you would wed.''Oh, it was not Denethor I trembled before, but Terenis! Her wrath lingers to this day. You should have seen it while the iron was hot!''You did put Lord Denethor in a difficult position, old friend. He could not take sides between the sisters, whom he loved. That he bore it silently and did not set his heart against you speaks much of him, I think.''You broke some few hearts in that time, yourself, Hirluin the Fair. How is it none of those smitten maidens ever softened your resolve and lured you into marriage?''It was not the path for me. I have chosen another way through life, and hold no regrets.''Still, you must wonder sometimes what might have been. That maid old Authlin made so much of, who climbed the City walls to you - or didn't, as you maintain - do you never wonder what became of her? Nay, answer not, you would not confess it if you did. She was a magistrate's daughter, I think? Yes. Laranís, she was called.

'Let our new friend ask among the magistrates and councilors up in the Sixth Circle for us and learn what became of her. Then we will see if we can win a blush from that pale, fair face of yours!''Ask among the magistrates up in the Sixth Circle. See if you can learn what became of Laranís.''Nothing will come of this. It is a waste of breath.''Yes, I did know Laranís, long ago. She did not wed for many years, and some began to call her a spinster. When she did join in marriage, it was to an older man. I never met him. I do not think he frequented the Sixth Circle.''Laranís? Yes, I know her well. She is in Imloth Melui now and her sons are among Denethor's archers, posted up upon the gates. She is a widow, you know. That is what comes of marrying an older man, though the merchant's wealth he left her must console her grief to some degree. Or so I am told wealth sometimes can.''There, Hirluin, that is the path you might have had. Two fine sons, and archers under the Steward!''Did you not note that the husband is dead? If that is where the other path led, then no thank you!''And I make for the third path, I suppose, for I wed but children never came to us. Do you wish you had sons instead of daughters, Forlong?''You would have spoken better and more wisely if you had said sons as well as daughters, Neldir. No father with one strand of goodness in him has ever unwished his child, be they boy or girl. Nor could anyone unwish my two flowers.

'Why, I have their likenesses in a locket among my things. And now that we have called to mind that battle coming any hour, I dislike my locket there, so far from my breast. What if the horns should blow, and I were without it? No, this thought unsettles me. I am ill at ease.

'Friend, will you do one last thing and ease my mind? My quarters are up in the Citadel, in the Southern Great Guest-house. Will you go there, find my locket, and bring it here to me?''My locket will be among my quarters in the Southern Great Guest-house, in the Citadel. Will you bring it here and put my mind at rest?''There it is - my locket. See, friends, here are Vanyalos and our daughters. My heart warms to look on them. Oh ho ho, or is that the wine? No, no. Wine unlocks the doors of the heart, but love must light the hearth!

'Let us raise one final glass, my friends. You are so dear to me. We must hold this moment and savour it, and let tomorrow come what may!''Thank you for sharing this time with us, my good ${RACE}. I feel a quiet coming on, and I see the same upon the faces of my friends. You are welcome here among us, but I think little more shall be said. Dawn will come soon, or what passes for dawn in these strange days. Yes. We'll greet the new day quietly.'

With that, Lord Forlong falls silent, and speaks no more.You meet Dervorin and tell him of news from his home, Ethring.Dervorin will meet you upon the wall near the Wainrider's Head on the Second Circle of Minas Tirith.

You should climb up on top the wall of the Second Circle to meet with Dervorin.Speak with Dervorin upon the wall of the Second CircleDervorin can be found upon the wall near the Wainrider's Head on the Second Circle of Minas Tirith.

You should look towards Mount Mindolluin with Dervorin.<rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> towards Mount MindolluinDervorin can be found upon the wall near the Wainrider's Head on the Second Circle of Minas Tirith.

You should speak with Dervorin.Speak with Dervorin'Greetings, I am Dervorin from Ringló Vale. What brings you to Minas Tirith on the eve of battle?'

You explain that you are an acquaintance of Mithrandir, and you tell Dervorin that you have recently passed through Ringló Vale yourself.

'You have?! You must tell me of your time in the Vale. Come, meet me upon the wall and we shall talk some more.''I am to be the lord of Ringló Vale one day. I do not wish for that day to come as my father, Borhador, is a good man. He may be weary in his old age but my father still has a commanding spirit and a great judgement of character. Now tell me of your time in the Vale.'

You tell Dervorin about your adventures with his brother Arvaethor and Borhador's renewed spirits after the great darkness caused sadness and fear in all who dwelled in the Vale.'Mount Mindolluin is part of the same mountain range that looms over the Ringló Vale. When I look at the mountainside, I often think of my home in Ethring.

'I am glad my father is leading the people again. I know how much he wanted to march to Minas Tirith. I feared my leaving would sadden him since his age and health prevented him from leaving Ethring. It is good to hear that my brother and you have become friends. He will one day be a great warrior and I am sure you helped him immensely. I hope that I will be able to help Ethring by being here. Thank you for your news of Ethring and the Vale.'You meet Rossaran and tell him of news from his home, Tadrent.The brazier sits next to Rossaran.

You should look at the flames.Look at the fireRossaran can be found in the Court of Tarondor on the First Circle of Minas Tirith.

You should speak with Rossaran.Speak with Rossaran'I rode to Minas Tirith with Prince Imrahil of Dol Amroth. It has been a long journey but I am glad to be able to fight at Imrahil's side.'

You tell Rossaran that you met his wife Bruidis in Tadrent on your travels through Belfalas.

'How is Bruidis then? I bet she is keeping busy. How has Tadrent fared with the Corsair invasion I've heard tale of?'

You tell Rossaran about your adventures in Tadrent where you prevented Corsairs from sacking the town. Bruidis has turned the sleepy village of Tadrent into a prominent stop for merchants travelling between Dol Amroth and Calembel.

'This is good news that brightens my spirits, traveller. I do still long to see my home again. Come look at the fire with me.''This fire burns with embers of the signal fire for the lighting of the beacons. The flame before you connects me to each lit beacon. When I see this fire, I can see all the way to Nendath, overlooking the town of Tadrent where Bruidis goes about her daily tasks. It is good of her to keep my home a place of beauty and calm in my absence, while I am out here, protecting the prosperity of Gondor.

'Thank you for the news, traveller. It gives me hope, and it is a reminder of what I fight for. I shall one day return home.'Worried by the sudden lack of news from Minas Tirith, Lady Terenis fears for the safety of Steward Denethor and his people.The couriers of Minas Tirith are known to travel along the eastern road into Lossarnach.

Lady Terenis has asked you to search for couriers of Minas Tirith and collect any letters they may bear.Search for the missing courier along the eastern roadThe slain courier can be found off the side of the road to the south-east of Tumladen.

You have discovered a fallen courier along the road into Lossarnach, and should search him for any letters he might yet bear.Examine the fallen courierThe letters can be found near the fallen courier in eastern Upper Lebennin.

You have noticed a scattered trail of messages dropped by the courier as he fled the Haradrim. You should recover them.Lady Terenis can be found in her hall in Tumladen.

You have reclaimed the letters dropped by the slain courier, but it is unlikely that any more messages will arrive. You should return to Lady Terenis.'You know well of my interest in the war, ${PLAYER}.

'However, I am particularly curious about the affairs of my brother, Denethor, and my sons in Minas Tirith. My sons have sent me many letters here in Tumladen, but as soon as all these folk arrived, the letters stopped. I do not know if some terrible fate befell one of Minas Tirith's couriers upon the field or if the war has reached the city so soon.

'My sister... Nay, the men of Lossarnach saw a courier cross the Erui, but he has not arrived since. Search the roads east of Tumladen, and tell me if the courier still rides for Tumladen.''This is most concerning...

'One of them has been too badly smudged to read, and the other two make me fear for my brother and my sons. Lord Faramir and his rangers were sent to Osgiliath and failed, but my brother has sent them forth again... has he taken leave of his senses?

'What fate will befall Denethor's city if Lord Angbor and Aragorn do not reach it in time?

'I must think on this, ${PLAYER}.'Rat-folk living on the banks of the Hoardale, south of Dâr-gazag, are not only a succulent meal, but they are a source of replenishable food. The rat-folk are farmers, supplying the enemy forces with foodstuffs. Foodstuffs can be stolen and eaten by your army as well.Rations are portioned to enemy forces and found in locations where they hold power.

Taskmaster Gukthor charged you collecting food for Angmar's Army.'Listen well, maggot! I'm charged with feeding our forces, and though I'd have no problems throwing good for nothing sluggards like you on a spit to serve for dinner, the war-masters have other plans.

'So I give you a task that you'll complete, or I'll go about changing the minds of those who can cover up their trails better than I, and you will be dinner.

'The rat-folk in the south grow food, and we steal it to keep our army full and happy. Now listen well! Men, Elves, dwarves, and even the rat-folk that stalk through the Ettenmoors can be carrying the foodstuffs we need. If you find any bring it to me... or there'll be no dinner!''It's not meat, but it tastes better than the mash we mince from slug-meat.

'We'll eat well tonight!'Having assisted the Free Peoples in their battle against the war host of Angmar, you have earned much respect. First Marshals throughout the Ettenmoors authorized the rewards from Quartermaster Ash at Glân Vraig.Earn Tokens of Valour by assisting members of the Free Peoples and return them to Quartermaster Ash at Glân Vraig.'Apparently everything that you have done out here has not gone unnoticed. Despite protests against offering you more rewards, the First Marshals have issued a writ saying that I must offer rewards to those who have proven worthy of the honour.

