Raise a glass to friendship with Forlong and company.Raise a glassThe Merry Swan Tavern is on the southern end of the Third Circle.

Hear what Forlong has to say.Listen to Forlong'Tell me friends, were things so very grim back then, as they are now? For that is not how I recall it. Or perhaps, being less in stature and wisdom, we did not look beyond this city's walls. It is a happy thing, is it not, to see no farther than your own strong walls?

'Ah, but then wanderlust comes over you. Why, Authlin himself sang a sweet, sad song on the subject. I hardly think of a harp more fairly fingered, or words more surely put to melody. How did it go?

'When dawn over the - when over the mountain - no, no. Hirluin, help me here.''There was dawn... and restless feet... and something, something to meet. I'm afraid those are all I can muster, old friend. Neldir, can you remember? No?''Alas, I am no poet and far less a minstrel.''What? We have drunk in his memory and then forgotten his songs? Should people say Authlin was a drinker and not a master song-smith? This is an insult that cannot be endured!

'Do not look at me like that, Hirluin. I have not drunk my fill, nor am I anywhere near done with drinking! And I shall be ready with my axe, a match for any foe, now or three bottles hence! Even with the clasp on my armour un-mended.

'Now where was I? The song must be recalled. Recalled and sung! Most helpful ${CLASS}, you must find rememberers. Perhaps the barkeep here knows a line. Or Golasgil or old Aglarang. Bariel the Master Hosteller? Someone!''Authlin's song must be recalled and sung, else we do him grave injury. And I would not injure the dead.''That song about wanderlust? Why yes, I've heard it. I heard Authlin sing it many a time. How did it go?

 I'll fly away, I will not stay
 Seeking fortune, love, and war

'That much I can recall. I'm afraid the rest is, well, not even a memory!''The song Authlin used to sing? Of wandering? Now that casts me back many a year! Hm... Over a mountain, sun comes rising... find myself this home despising... no, I'm afraid I have no more of it than that!''I can indeed recall that song. The melody is as clear in my head as if he were here. Hmm hmmmm hum hum hm hm hmmmm.... But the words? Well. No, I cannot muster a single one. Ah, how memory betrays a body!''Yes, I remember Authlin, though I was but a girl, and I know the song as well.

 I'll fly away and further roam
 But I will never turn back home
 No I shall... I shall....
 
'Is that how it went? Well, I have recalled some of it, at least!'You recite and hum what you were able to gather. Lord Forlong closes his eyes and thinks.

'Yes, yes, my friend. I think it is enough. Let us see. Ahem!

When o'er mountain sun comes rising
With stirring heart and restless feet
I'll find myself this home despising
And yearn for distant lands to meet

I'll fly away, I will not stay
Seeking fortune, love, and war
I'll fly away and further roam
But I will never turn back home
No I shall wend back there no more
No more, no more, no more, no more....

'There! So sad and sweet. There were other verses of course. Love was won and lost. Battles and fortunes, too.''That was a fine song, and it was just so that we went off to war, you and I. He sang of us, too, didn't he? Standing by Thorongil's side in the Breaking of Umbar. Though now that I come to think of it, he sang mostly of Thorongil, and of Umbar's Breaking. Still, we are named.''Named? Ha ha, that makes it sound as if we merely stood about! We fought by Thorongil's side, Hirluin and I, and weathered the thick of it, far off there in the south. Now, I do not wish to be unfair. The strategem was Thorongil's, and never, I think, was there a finer swordsman - or a faster walker!

'But all the same, we had our own deeds worthy of song. Tell them, Hirluin, how I took the sorcerer's standard and with it struck him down as he stood there, chanting foul things!''Moreso than you have done, just now? You'd need to bring that battle-standard here and show them, to paint a clearer picture. You took it as a trophy, did you not?''By the seven-pointed star, I did! And left here, in Minas Tirith, within the Houses of Lore. Ah, to hold that fell thing in my hands again....'

Forlong turns to you.

'Ah, my friend, I have asked much of you. But would you do one more good turn for old Forlong? Would you go up to the Fifth Circle and fetch my trophy for me? I think you will find it in the Houses of Lore... somewhere.''The war trophy stands in the Houses of Lore, up in the Fifth Circle. Will you bring it to me?''Why, that is it! And it is just as I remembered it. You see, Hirluin and Thorongil stood about as far as... there, back to back, their swords flashing. I had made a great rush, my axe hewing all in the path, for I had seen the sorcerer across the room begin to work some foul spell. Alas, a tall half-troll stood barring my way. Not for long! He took my axe like all the rest, though it stuck in him and I could not quickly draw it out. So, thinking on my toes, I wrenched this standard from its place and struck the cruel spell-weaver down where he stood!''You should pen a song, yourself, Forlong. You do a fine job singing your own praises.''Ever the sharp wit and sharper tongue, old friend! To my mind, you are too modest in never boasting for yourself. I will right both flaws and boast for you.

'Did you know that once Hirluin held at bay two, nay three dozen corsairs, merely by throwing knives? We were surprised, and found ourselves in an Umbar tavern, weaponless but backs against the stores of cutlery. He grabbed handfuls, and like rain they flew! Why, if you gathered the knives from these tables here, I feel sure Hirluin could still show you how it was done.

'You will show us how you did it, Hirluin? Oh, come. You must!''Of all my acts of swordsmanship and cunning, you chose this to tell?''Take two fistfuls of knives off the tables. Hirluin will re-enact the feat.''Give him the knives, friend. He will show you how it was done.''I do this against all my better judgement. Let me see. We stood here and they, there. I turned and grabbed the knives and then... yes then I flung out my arm, like so.'

Two handfuls of knives clatter to the floor at Hirluin's feet.

'Bah! These knives are not weighted at all like those were. Who am I deceiving? I am an old man and I have forgotten much. I believe and hope, at least, my skill with a sword is not so far forgotten!''It amazes me to think you stood side by side with Thorongil. Now that is a name of legends and song! Tell me, friends, what was he like, in speech, and look, and action?''Names always seem thus, when they sail off and are never seen or heard from again! Ah, but Hirluin and I knew him well, back then. There was a greatness in him, and in truth, he always seemed to me as one of the men of Númenor, newly landed on Gondor's shores.  All the same, he was a man, and had all such peculiarities as men have.

'Do you remember, Hirluin, what he did with his arm when he called us over to him? It was, no, I cannot do it from my seat. You, friend. See if you can do it.''That was very convincing, friend.''That was very like unto Thorongil's beckon.''Why, that was just how Thorongil did it! Wasn't it, Hirluin? You hit it on the very first try. You have a talent for this, ${CLASS}!

'Now you really must try another. Ah, I know! Thorongil, when no one was watching, would sometimes sigh in a manner most melancholy. Can you sigh for us, friend?''I am amazed! Somehow, you have captured Thorongil. Have you ever acted upon the stage? Well, here is a challenge for you: there was a particular stern look - let us call it solemn - that would come over Thorongil's face when planning his battle strategy. Let us see you try such a solemn face.''Come now. Only one who knew Thorongil, and knew him well, could have mimicked such a face! This is true art. It is uncanny!

'Well, now, Forlong shouldn't have all the fun. Why don't you try smoking your pipe? He always did so with a stern and thoughtful look, as though the pipe-bowl were giving him grave and secret council.''Let us have a cheer for our new friend's fine acting! It casts my mind back, it does. The times of war, when Thorongil and Denethor stood side by side.''Until they did not stand side by side, if you recall. Thorongil left us and then Denethor grew cold on him... though some say the order was reversed.''Aye, then it was Denethor's turn to lead us into battle. The Taking of Osgiliath! Or one of them, at least.

'It has always seemed to me, though both men were stern and serious, that when we marched under Denethor's command, it was a graver, more tiresome affair. By the look on your face, Hirluin, I see you disagree. Or no, that is not it. It is that you will not hear ill spoken of our good steward.''You read my face too well. Denethor is not so hard or uncharitable a man as I think you take him for. He is a good man with great - nay, unbearable burdens.

'But I am forced to confess that for me, too, the memories of Umbar shine somehow brighter, on recollection, than Osgiliath. I blame it on our youth and newness to battle, and the enchantment of journeying to foreign lands.''Ha ha! Whereas Osgiliath reminded us only of what had once been great, in Gondor, and is no more. Yes, let us not quarrel over this.

'Nothing makes peace so well as food and drink, but I see that we are low on food. Will our new friend do us another kindness, and ask them to bring another course?''There is more to be said, and remembered, and sung. And for that, we shall want more food!''More food is it? I'll have it brought. You've done enough going about and carrying things for those three.''Food will be sent, presently. Do not worry. My lad will bring it up.''The feast continues, and we have you to thank. It seems to me that we owe much to our new friend, for this happy time before the coming storm.

'Here, then, let us raise a glass and welcome you into our little company. To old friends and to new!''To old friends and to new!''To old times and the roads that led us here! To battles fought and battles yet to come. Yes, battles to come.'

For a second time, Forlong falls into silence.Thurindol asks you to assist the Rangers by searching through rubble in Bâr Húrin for any buried supplies.Bâr Húrin is located in Southern Ithilien.

You should search rubble piles in Bâr Húrin for buried Ranger supplies.Collect Ranger caches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thurindol can be found in the keep at Bâr Húrin.

You should talk to Thurindol.'Years ago we buried supply caches within the rubble of Bâr Húrin and many other ruined towns in Southern Ithilien. Hiding our provisions allows us to move through Ithilien unnoticed by the enemy. Will you assist us by searching through the rubble in Bâr Húrin for any supplies we may have missed since establishing camp here?''You found some supplies. Excellent work! I shall commend you to Anborn myself.'Angered that the Corsairs have crossed the river Serni, Steward Malthellim hopes to drive them back to the coast.Corsairs can be found to the south and east of Ost Anglebed.

Steward Malthellim has asked you to defeat Corsairs that have crossed the Serni before they can invade Ost Anglebed.Defeat encroaching Corsairs outside Ost Anglebed (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Steward Malthellim can be found in the Lord's Hall in Ost Anglebed.

You have defeated many Corsairs outside Ost Anglebed and weakened their advance across the river Serni.Talk to Steward Malthellim'For days now, we have seen the advance of our foes south of the Serni, but few have been bold enough to cross over it.

'However, my men have informed me that a few Corsairs snuck across in the darkness of this morn and seemed to be making their way towards Ost Anglebed.

'I will not allow that Corsair filth to take one step into my home, much less lay claim to it. Search the fields near Ost Anglebed and make them wish they had the sense to remain across the river!''Let that be a lesson to them, ${PLAYER}. With such a paltry force, I must wonder why they even bothered...

'Here is your reward, as promised.'Arvaethor asks for you to accompany him into a cavern under the White Mountains and drive the Orkish forces back.Arvaethor asks for you to accompany him into a cavern under the White Mountains and drive the Orkish forces back.Speak to ArvaethorArvaethor can be found in the town of Ethring in Ringló Vale.

You have returned to Ethring and should speak with Arvaethor.Speak with Arvaethor'Some of the Orcs have run into the mountain. We must enter the caves and push them back, far away from the Vale. It is the only way to assure that courage never returns to them. We will enter when you are ready.''Quickly, into the cave!''This is indeed unsettling. Why are there so many of the Dead inside this mountain? I know the history of the Stone of Erech as much as any Gondorian, but the Paths of the Dead rests many miles and mountain peaks away from here. I have not heard of the Dead travelling so far away from the place that cursed them. We must find answers if we are to deter the Dead from entering the Vale.'Determined to avenge the lost Rangers of Ithilien, Halhir sends you to Meneldil's Square to perform a few executions of your own.Half-troll executioners can be found near Meneldil's Square in western Osgiliath.

Halhir has asked you to defeat half-troll executioners in western Osgiliath to avenge his fallen brothers.Defeat half-troll executioners in Osgiliath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Halhir can be found in the culverts of Osgiliath.

You have defeated many of the half-troll executioners and should now return to Halhir.Talk to Halhir'${PLAYER}, I have a task for you.

'You saved my men from the blades of the Enemy, but not all of my brothers were so fortunate. Over in the western streets, by Meneldil's Square, the half-trolls sharpen their axes and await more men to bring them down upon. Although I did not think it upon our first meeting, you are a bold ${CLASS} and I would count your skills among the finest of my men. My brothers must be avenged, and I would have the honour be yours.

'Go to the square and perform some executions of your own, my friend. The half-trolls must pay for what they have done!''I hope they suffered as much as my brothers did, ${PLAYER}.

'I shall not rest until the forces of the Enemy lay dead and broken, and I am glad to have an ally such as you.

'You have my thanks, but our work is not yet done.'Once enough contributions have been received, the tunnels beneath Emin Ernil will be re-opened. With that, aspiring town guards of Dol Amroth will be able to curry favour with the Workshop district.Tothlant has asked you to bring him Silver Tokens of Dol Amroth to help pay for unlocking Emyn Ernil.'The tunnels beneath Emyn Ernil have long been sealed by a rock slide from my father's generation. There is much worthwhile to explore and scavenge within, but it is a great feat to reopen the entrance.

'The more donations of Silver Tokens of Dol Amroth you bring me, the more progress we can make on clearing the rockslide and opening the tunnels beneath Emyn Ernil. Once that is done, there will be much to explore.''This will help aid us towards accomplishing the goal. Soon we should be able to reopen those tunnels.'Once enough contributions have been received, the lost caves of the Paths of the Dead will be opened. With that, aspiring town guards of Dol Amroth will be able to curry favour with the Swan-knights Barracks.Harniel has asked you to bring her Silver Tokens of Dol Amroth to help pay the labour for unlocking new areas of the Paths of the Dead.'There are many places within the Paths of the Dead that have not seen a living soul. With the army now having departed, there is a great deal to learn from that place.

'The more donations of Silver Tokens of Dol Amroth you bring me, the more progress we can make on opening the unexplored areas within the Paths of the Dead. Once that is done, there will be much to explore.''This will help aid us towards accomplishing the goal. Soon we should be able to open those tunnels.'The theft of a mysterious, indecipherable scroll from the Houses of Lore has left its curators worried that it might be used to some vile end.The collection of enemy trophies can be found on the second floor of the Houses of Lore.

Maenir has asked you to search the collection for signs of thievery before returning to him with the transcript of the stolen scroll.Search the collection of enemy trophies for signs of thieveryThe scrawled transcript can be found among the collection of enemy trophies in the Houses of Lore.

You have searched the collection and should now return to Maenir after collecting the transcript of the stolen scroll.Collect the scrawled transcript in the Houses of LoreMaenir can be found on the first floor of the Houses of Lore in the Fifth Circle.

You have searched the collection of enemy trophies and collected the transcript of the stolen scroll. You should now return to Maenir.Talk to MaenirMithrandir is in the Court of the Fountain on the Seventh Circle of Minas Tirith.

Maenir has asked you to seek Mithrandir's counsel in the hope that deciphering the content of the scroll might aid in capturing its thief.'Pardon my intrusion, ${CLASS}, but there is a matter I would like to discuss with you. It is one I would prefer to keep quiet, if it is possible.

'A few days ago, a warrior returned from the Pelennor with a peculiar scroll. He claimed to have wrest it from a fallen Haradrim sorcerer and sought to have it displayed among the many trophies of battle here in the Houses of Lore. We honoured his request, but so strange was the text that I endeavoured to transcribe and study it in my own time. I was able to transcribe it, but this very morning the scroll was taken from its resting place above. I hurried to cover the theft and stored my transcript in its place, but it will not be long before its loss is noticed by those with a more discerning eye.

'I would have it returned, and I care not to see the thief punished - my only concern is that of the scroll. I have little advice to offer other than to begin by searching the trophy collection - if nothing is there, return with my transcript and we can discuss a new course.''It is as I expected. If the scroll was all they wanted, then what is their aim? There are many collectors of queer trinkets in Minas Tirith and the lands beyond, but with war come to our gates, I doubt many have worried themselves with obtaining new artifacts and curiosities.

'I transcribed the text as neatly as I might, though I do not understand the very words I wrote! I thought to take it to the Archives to translate... but I do not wish to leave our collections unattended until this is resolved.

'You are a friend of Mithrandir, are you not? He knows much of old and strange languages, and perhaps he might aid us. Take my transcript to him, and see what he makes of it!''What is this, ${PLAYER}? What have you to show me?'

You tell Mithrandir that Maenir has transcribed a lost scroll without understanding its language and hopes to learn more of its origin.

'A simple translation? Certainly, he should have all the resources he requires... and within the city at that!'After finding a slain group of Corsairs in the hills of Dor-en-Ernil, the situation grows more dire as a familiar foe returns.The Corsair hideout can be found in north-central Dor-en-Ernil along the mountainside.

You have found slain Corsairs in their hideout and a sense of unease fills the air. You should investigate the cave further.Search the Corsairs' caveThe Ruthless oathbreaker can be found inside the Corsairs' cave in north-central Dor-en-Ernil.

You have discovered the Ruthless Dead are behind the deaths of the Corsairs and should endeavour to discover their purpose in Dor-en-Ernil.Confront the Ruthless DeadGarvir can be found in Linhir in eastern Dor-en-Ernil.

You have discovered that the Ruthless Dead intend to sweep eastward through Lebennin towards Mordor and should bring these ill tidings to Garvir.Return to Garvir in LinhirAs you examine the Corsair more closely, a familiar chill fills the air. Although you cannot see them, you sense that the Dead are nearby.

If they have truly escaped the Ringló Vale, the people of Dor-en-Ernil will have far more to worry about than the Corsairs and Haradrim.

You should investigate the cave further before returning to Garvir in Linhir.'The one called ${PLAYER} comes to challenge us again, brothers....

'Azruthor and the Corsairs thought they could control us, and look what ruin it has brought them! Their cause is a hopeless one - only Sauron can wield the power of the Dead!

'Nothing you do can save the Living from us... we shall sweep over these lands to Mordor!''The Dead are still in Dor-en-Ernil? Why have these spirits delayed? Aragorn and his men rode east with them to Pelargir!'

You explain the ruthless Dead to Garvir, who seems dismayed to learn of them.

'I see... perhaps our doom is unavoidable then. How can we stand against fear incarnate?'Candúr has asked you to search the High Archives for the ranger, Echil, and recover any writings of value that haven't yet been set aflame.Echil is somewhere inside the High Archives in western Osgiliath.

Candúr has asked you to seek his lost fellow, Echil, and then recover any valuable writings that survived the sack of western Osgiliath.Search for Echil in the High ArchivesRecovered writings and crumbling tomes can be found inside the High Archives in western Osgiliath.

You have found Echil and should now search for recovered writings and valuable tomes before returning to Candúr.Collect bundles of recovered writings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect crumbling tomes carefully (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Candúr can be found just outside the High Archives in western Osgiliath.

You have found Echil and recovered many valuable pieces of writing from the High Archives. You should now return to Candúr.'My ally, Echil, has delayed in his return, and I fear that the Easterlings or the flames within have overwhelmed him.

'I must keep guard here, but if you could seek him out, I would be most grateful. He was to collect some of our writings as well as a few of the crumbling works that were never moved from the city long ago for fear that they might turn to dust at a touch.

'Aid him, and then return to me for then I shall heed Mablung's call and sneak back to the culverts.''Ah, a friendly face, I hope? I am fine but for a wounded leg... alas, these Easterlings gave me some trouble. Unfortunately for them, I gave them more. They spoke of some "Lord of Pinions", but I know none who bear such a title.

'I can return to Candúr, but I have not finished collecting our writings. We keep many of our writings as well as those we have taken from the Enemy's forces within the old archives, but the Easterlings have set flame to it all.

'If you can recover those that remain, Candúr and I would be most grateful.''I am glad that Echil was not caught unawares, and that so many of the writings we collected survived. You have done me a great service, and I hope that you will fight alongside us in the battles that loom ahead.

'I shall store these away and make for the culverts at once!'Morgul-orcs and goblins have snuck into the cisterns beneath Minas Tirith, hoping to destroy it from below while the forces of Gondor are occupied upon the wall.

