You should speak with Herefara.Speak with Herefara'Ah, ${PLAYER}, I have not seen you since we fought together at Brockbridge. Your arrival is unexpected, but most welcome. Are you aware of our current troubles?

'Orc spies have been spotted in the woods, not far from here. They are searching for us, that is certain.  I have a task for you. Defeat these spying Orcs so that we Rohirrim can remain hidden. Return as soon as you can as there is much that needs doing and I need all the help I can get.''Your assistance is appreciated, as always. Hopefully, you have given us the extra time we need before we mobilize.'Angered spiders search the forest floor for prey and should be dealt with before any forest-folk fall victim to the spiders' webs.Spiders can be found in the woods of Taur Drúadan.

You should defeat spiders in Taur Drúadan.Defeat spiders in Taur Drúadan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many spiders in Taur Drúadan.CompletedSpiders, angered by invaders to the Drúadan Forest, search for prey on the forest floor. You suspect many could fall victim to the traps laid by these creatures. These spiders should be dealt with quickly.You have defeated many spiders in Taur Drúadan.In the Rohirrim bivouac, Guthláf asks you to look for a young rider named Sithgar who fell behind.Look for a young rider named Sithgar in Ost Rimmon, in the Beacon Hills.Look for SithgarYou should help Sithgar recover his horse and return to his Éored.Help SithgarWhen you return to the Rohirrim bivouac, you should let Guthláf know that you found Sithgar in Ost Rimmon and helped him to ready his horse for travel again.Return to Guthláf'We rode hard through the land to our west, and fast. Misfortune may fly swift as an arrow though, and in the briefest of rests, a young rider of my Éored, Sithgar by name, had his horse run off on him and was forced to stay behind.

'The thought pains me some. The horse he rode was not fully broken in, and, I confess, I provided her to him. He had arrived at the muster with his late father's steed, half lame and half blind, and in all honesty, I thought he would have bonded with his new steed by now. I thought he would have caught back up with us, as well, and that has not happened either!

'If you are heading that way, would you look for the young man? He will be in Ost Rimmon, most likely, unless he is still out hunting for his horse.''I feel a responsibility for the young man, as I gave him the un-broken horse that ran away. If you find him in Ost Rimmon, I would ask that you do what you can for him.''Old Guthláf sent you? Ha! Well, he was not wrong to be worried and I must confess, I am in need of some help.''Ah, there you are! I have seen Sithgar returned with my own eyes. And I understand we have you to thank. My heart is glad for what you have done, friend.''You found Sithgar? And he is well? As well as a lad may be who has lost his horse, at any rate. Ha ha! I hope he is able to return before we move on. We cannot tarry for him, I am afraid.'Sithgar's horse has run off again - this time into the Drúadan Forest.Search for Witherbreca, who has run off into the Drúadan Wood.Find WitherbrecaYou should talk to the Wose standing by Witherbreca in the Drúadan Forest.Talk to the WoseYou should return to Sithgar in the Rohirrim bivouac, in Taur Drúadan, and tell him of the horse and the Wose.Return to SithgarYou should return to Bhis-weni-Mâr with Sithgar, and help negotiate the trade of a shield for the wayward horse.Talk to Bhis-weni-MârSearch for Witherbreca deep in the Drúadan Forest.Find WitherbrecaDefeat the wood-trolls surrounding Witherbreca in the Drúadan Forest.Now that you have defeated the wood-trolls, you should lead Witherbreca back to Sithgar and Bhis-weni-Mâr.Lead Witherbreca backYou should talk to Bhis-weni-Mâr now that he, Witherbreca, and Sithgar are together in the Drúadan Forest.Talk to Bhis-weni-MârSithgar stands in the Drúadan Forest, by Witherbreca and Bhis-weni-Mâr.

You should talk to Sithgar and hear what he has to say.Talk to Sithgar'There you are, ${PLAYER}. Do not laugh at me, but my luck is as bad as ever. No sooner was I back among my Éored when Witherbreca ran off again. But now we are not among the pleasant grass-hills, we are in a forest! I'm sure the poor animal has been eaten by a bear.''I have once more lost all hope, ${PLAYER}. My horse has run off into the forest, and I will be left behind once again!''Horse,' the Wose says, looking up with kindly eyes at the great animal, 'friend.'

Through a series of short words and gestures, you make the Wose understand that you want to lead the horse back to its owner, although it is not clear that the 'owner' part is especially clear.

The Wose folds his arms across his chest.

'Shield. Bhis-weni-Mâr want shield. Good-bye horse.'

Could it be that he wants to trade a Rohirric shield for the horse?'You found my steed - and it has befriended one of these... Wose-men, you say? Well, it sounds as if he was faring better at soothing the horse than I! Ha ha.'

You explain that the Bhis-weni-Mâr seemed to want to trade a shield for the horse.

'I - I cannot do that, ${PLAYER}. I would be punished, and not lightly....'

He sighs.

'Still, I would take the punishment if it meant I could ride with the host. Let us go and meet this Wose horse-friend.''No horse, and the poor lad looks scared out of his wits!'Bhis-weni-Mâr looks at you with sad and frightened eyes. 

'Horse. Run.'

He gestures off deeper into the woods.

'Bad Ghûrdhos come. Horse run. You help?''Ghûrdhos come. Horse run.''What is a Ghûrdhos? These woods are surely full of monsters and sorcery!''You got her back unharmed. Do the wonders never end? Well, let us get on with this Wose's bargain, shall we?'Bhis-weni-Mâr looks both very serious and very sad.

'No shield. Horse-man keep shield. Horse and forest... do not go... as one. Horse is good. Very good horse. Only must be calm. Must calm horse. Or horse run.'

Bhis-weni-Mâr fixes his eyes on Sithgar, and through a sequence of words and gestures, makes it clear that he wishes to teach Sithgar how to calm and command Witherbreca, before he lets her go.'Horse is good. I will show rider how friend to make with it.''It seems to be settled then. I will be the most devoted of students, if only this Wose can teach me how to keep my horse! And I shall consider you a bearer of good fortune, ${PLAYER}. Every time you arrive, my poor luck turns to good!'You have the ransom Jarl Akhiz demanded for two of Lady Glánhar's sons.Meet Gondrad before Jarl Akhiz's camp at Bâr Nadhron.Meet Gondrad at Bâr NadhronExplore the various entrances of Jarl Akhiz's camp in Bâr Nadhron.Return to Gondrad just outside the camp at Bâr Nadhron.Return to Gondrad'We have both parts of the ransom now, so I suppose we must press on. To Bâr Nadhron, ${PLAYER}, though I have an ill feeling about this!''There is no way out of this, I am afraid. We must march right up to the camp and deliver the ransom... and if we are lucky, walk out with our hides!''Look at that! This Jarl could certainly take Ost Rimmon. I wonder if he knows it?

'Now, I have been pondering what Lady Glánhar said about caution. Call me coward and cuff me on the ear, but I would scout around the edges of the camp and see what may be seen, if I were you.

'As for myself, this is as close I have courage to go!''Lady Glánhar bade us to use caution and wit. If stealthily scouting out their camp is not caution, then I am a hapless fool. Well, most likely, I am a hapless fool all the same.'You recount all that you saw to Gondrad and his face slowly twists into a frown.

'I see two reasons to be perfectly miserable, ${PLAYER}. First and foremost is that these men are truly as numerous, ruthless, and fell as we had feared. And secondly - you found no better reason to turn back and go home without completing the deal.'A troubling message comes to Lady Glánhar of Ost Rimmon.The shepherd and Lady Glánhar stand at the centre of Ost Rimmon.

Lady Glánhar cannot make out what the flustered shepherd is trying to tell her. You should speak to him yourself and see if you can get anything out of him.Talk to the shepherdThe shepherd and Lady Glánhar stand at the centre of Ost Rimmon.

You should tell the shepherd's message to Lady Glánhar: that she is to meet the captor on the eastern slope of Calenhad to bargain for the lives of her three sons.Talk to Lady GlánharLady Glánhar asked you to find Gondrad, who should be found somewhere within Ost Rimmon.Find GondradLady Glánhar stands at the centre of Ost Rimmon.

You should go with Gondrad and learn what Lady Glánhar wishes to do about the ransomer.Talk to Lady GlánharGo to the meeting-spot, south-east of Calenhad, on the bank above the river Sírnarthan.Meet the ransomerAmbushers have jumped out of concealment on the way to meeting the ransomer. Defend yourself!Continue looking for the meeting-spot, south-east of Calenhad, on the bank above the river.Go on to meet the ransomerThe the meeting-spot is south-east of Calenhad, on the bank above the river.

Talk to the Variag Captain and learn what he wants for the lives of Glánhar's sons.Talk to the Variag CaptainLady Glánhar stand at the centre of Ost Rimmon.

You should return to Lady Glánhar with the Jarl Akhiz's demands.Return to Glánhar'I can make neither head nor tail of what this tatter-tongued shepherd is trying to say. There is a bad man it seems, and a message, and I can get no more. I wonder, is he speaking in the tongue of sheep or of men? 

'Bah! Why don't you try and see if you can get anything out of him.''Go on. See if you can get anything out of the tatter-tongued wretch.''A m-m-message the bad man b-b-bade me bear to Lady Glánhar. Only every time I think of- of- of what I've got to say to her, my heart quails and my tongue stumbles and right full tumbles over itself.'

He takes a breath and continues.

'I was standing in the shade of a tree talking to one of my sheep, when I looked up and there they were, all about me, fearsome swords in hand. And then the bad man fixed me with his eye and spoke.

'He holds the lives of three sons of this place in his hand, he said, and for a sum of gold he might let them slip through his fingers. He bade the keeper of this place - Lady Glánhar he must have meant - to meet him upon the eastern slope of Calenhad, above the river.

'I beg you, ${RACE}, to say it to the Lady here for I cannot!''You - you must tell her the message - that the bad man bade her to meet on the eastern slope of Calenhad, above the river, and that he holds the lives of her sons in his hands.'Glánhar's shrewd eyes go wide.

'What? My four sons and husband went to war after the last beacon. Unless - but he holds only three sons? What can it then mean...?'

Her face grows very sober.

'I see. If this ransomer said not my name but only 'The Keeper of Ost Rimmon', then I shall play the prudent course and send another in my stead. 

'Now, where is that blasted fool Gondrad? Hoping to weather my wrath, it seems. Will you find the rascal for me? He is skulking somewhere in Ost Rimmon, I am sure.''Once you have found that good-for-naught Gondrad, return to me and we will devise a plan.''You told her? You are braver than I, ${RACE} - or you do not fear the Lady's ire as I do!''Ah - you've found me! Wait a moment, who are you?

'No matter. You have clearly come from beyond these walls and this bodes doubly ill for me. First, because if you came from out there, then Lady Glánhar will deem it safe enough to send me out there. And second, because she has surely sent you here to summon me for just that cause. Unless she merely wishes to whip me with another thirty lashes of her tongue!  Oh, I deserve the lashings well enough. But even a guilty hound will hide his snout from his mistress' wrath.

'Well, there is no escaping it! Let us go and hear my Lady's command.''If you would be of aid to Ost Rimmon, then go with Gondrad to meet this ransomer and see he that he returns to me here in one piece.''Do you see? It is just as I said. 

'I would rejoice that my Lady does not wish to see me dead, but I know well enough that longer life means more time to punish me for my failings!''If you ask me, this fellow would cut your head off quicker than ask your name. Would and could!

'Well, let's see what he's got to say.''Am I to believe that one of you is the keeper of Ost Rimmon? You are what, a fool of a stable hand and a hired guard? You must think Jarl Akhiz dull indeed!'

Akhiz lets out a savage chuckle.

'You drew the short straw, did you not, stable hand? Ha ha! But this guard looks to be of some mettle. 

'Well, no matter. I hold two sons of the Lord of Ost Rimmon. In exchange for their lives, I demand two things: their worth in gold and, because I think you, ${CLASS}, can win it for me, a brooch that was stolen from me by a rival Jarl of the Variags. You will find his camp to the north-west, near the Firien Wood. He'll have it in some chest or another there, I am sure.

'When you have these things, you will bring them to me in the cellar in Bâr Nadhron. Now go, else I raise my price again.''Why look here, I still have my head on my shoulders! We didn't pull much wool over his eyes, did we, though?

'Still, we must count ourselves lucky. Now let us get going rather than test our luck any further!''You give the report. The less I speak, the less new trouble I can get myself into!''You will bring the gold and the brooch to me in the cellar in Bâr Nadhron, and in exchange I shall release the two brothers.

'What? The fool shepherd said three? The count has changed. Go now, or I shall grow angry."Lady Glánhar listens to your account of the meeting.

'So it is a cunning wolf we are dealing with, then. Hm! Gondrad, I am not done with you, don't go creeping off. I must think a moment. What does this man stand to lose? To gain? What does he know that he has not said?'

She falls silent with a frown.'There you are. Mark my words - this will not go well for me!''There is nothing for it. We must go see what Lady Glánhar wants. No good will come of it for you - or especially for me!''Ah, there you are. Gondrad, you craven rascal, listen to me.

'I have received grim tidings that my sons and perhaps your Lord never reached Minas Tirith, but were waylaid by some enemy captain or highwayman on the way. As you should rightfully have been with them, I am sending you to treat with the ransomer.

'${CLASS}, I ask this of you: go with Gondrad and keep him safe. He has much to answer for, but I deem a foolish death to be insufficient punishment and a general waste of penance. I have no one more capable of keeping him safe, I am afraid, and must rely on you.'After a close encounter with Jarl Akhiz and his men, it is time to reunite with the others.Find the meeting location, a tree east of Bâr Nadhron, by the Mouths of the Entwash.Find the meeting locationYou have been set upon by Variag assailants near the Mouths of the Entwash, east of Bâr Nadhron. Defeat them and do not lead them to the meeting place.Find the meeting location, a tree east of Bâr Nadhron by the Mouths of the Entwash.Find the meeting locationYou have been set upon by more Variag assailants near the Mouths of the Entwash, east of Bâr Nadhron. Defeat them and do not lead them to the meeting place.The party is under a tree east of Bâr Nadhron by the Mouths of the Entwash.

You should hear what Togbor has to say.Talk to TogborThe party has met under a tree east of Bâr Nadhron by the Mouths of the Entwash.

You should hear what Gondrad has to say.Talk to GondradThe party has met under a tree east of Bâr Nadhron by the Mouths of the Entwash.

You should hear what Togbor has to say.Talk to Togbor'So we are to meet beneath a bent tree near the Mouths of the Entwash? Let us go and not linger here one worrisome moment longer!''Beneath a tree east of Bâr Nadhron, by the wetland's edge, is where we are to meet. Let us go - and with haste!''I still say we should have fought those dishonourable dogs. Perhaps in Ost Rimmon we can muster a fighting force and come back for them!''We may be safe now, but I'm well acquainted with trouble and I believe we're in for more of it!''Well, we have made it out somehow, haven't we? Not... all of us.'

He grimaces and looks about him.

'Gondrad! Where in all Gondor had you got off to, and how is it you are here?''How glad I am to see old Gondrad. How glad I shall be to be home again!''Ah, friend! The prank your brother Narvagol played upon me has born ill fruit, indeed. 

'In revenge for failing to polish his armour, he hid my belongings far and wide about Ost Rimmon. You could not find me, for I was scouring the cellar, and then you left so Lady Glánhar has showered abuse without respite upon me ever since!''He does not know. You must tell him, brother.''Gondrad, know that Narvagol is dead. Father lies dead by his side. Your luck was not ill but a supreme blessing, for we were taken before even the sun had reached noon that day, and you, a commoner unworthy of ransom, would not have been spared. 

'They wished to ransom all of us, of course, but Father and Narvagol sought glory instead and won it. We might have too, but the curs threw nets over us and saw us disarmed before we could cause any trouble. Then you came to our rescue, and here we are.

'All, that is, except for my brother Malhar.'Lady Glánhar wishes for banners to be hung on Ost Rimmon's walls to deter enemies from an assault.Bargwen is located in the southern end of Ost Rimmon.

Go to Bargwen and tell her of Lady Glánhar's wish to restore the banners to the walls.Talk to BargwenYou should gather the stored banners from the store-house in Ost Rimmon.Gather the banners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the banners to Bargwen, who is in the southern end of Ost Rimmon.You should take the bucket and and fill it at the well in the centre of Ost Rimmon, then bring it back to Bargwen.Take the empty bucketYou should take the bucket and and fill it at the well in the centre of Ost Rimmon, then bring it back to Bargwen.Fill the bucket at the wellYou should talk to Bargwen, who is in the southern end of Ost Rimmon.Talk to BargwenYou should put the dirty banners in the bucket of water to wash them.Pick up the pile of dirty bannersYou should wash the banners in the bucket of water.Wash the bannersYou should place the cleaned, wet banners on the poles to dry.Take banners out of the bucketYou should place the cleaned, wet banners on the poles to dry.Bargwen is in the southern end of Ost Rimmon.

You should talk to Bargwen now that the banners are washed and hung on the poles.Talk to BargwenYou should return to Lady Glánhar in the centre of Ost Rimmon and tell her that the banners are ready and will be hung shortly.Return to Lady Glánhar'Our straw soldiers look fearsome, ${PLAYER}, but something is missing. What is it? Ah, I know. The banners are not hanging. True, they have not hung in many years, but I feel that they must hang now. It tells the barbarians and Orcs out there that we have not abandoned this place and are not hiding. It will make them think again about attempting an assault!

'I ask you to return to Bargwen and aid her in restoring the banners to their places. That is all.''Go back to Bargwen, please, and assist her in restoring the banners to the walls.''What does she want now? The banners, is it? I should have guessed she would choose the most laborious task short of scrubbing the stables. I have you to help me at least... do I not?

'The banners will be stored in the store-house. Bring them to me and we will see what state they are in.''The banners are stored in the store-house. When you've gathered them, bring them here.''Let me see them. Fah! These are filthy. At least by some miracle they are not moth-eaten. Still, they must be washed. There is a bucket. Take it to the well and bring some water. I will prepare the things we need.

'The well is back in the centre of Ost Rimmon, where Lady Glánhar stands.''The well is back in the centre of Ost Rimmon, where you came from. Take the bucket there, fill it, and return to me. Honestly, I did not think I would need to repeat each step to you as if you were a child!''Good. I have laid all the things out. Take the pile of dirty banners and put them in the water. Wash each one well and then place them on the poles to dry.''Go on. Put them in the water, wash them, and hang them up to dry.''You're almost done. You need but hang the cleaned banners on the poles.''Well? Wash them and hang them up to dry.''You have given me a moment's rest and not complained. Not loud enough for it to reach my ears, at any rate. I thank you. I myself could never get work done without complaining.

'Now I will rouse myself and see that these are properly hung. You may tell Lady Glánhar it is taken care of. Brag of your accomplishments if it suits you.''The banners were no trouble? Good. Bargwen is a tireless worker, is she not? Now, at last, I feel we're beginning to present a proud and fearsome face to any foe who creeps up to our door.''The well is back in the centre of Ost Rimmon, where you came from. Take the bucket there, fill it, and return to me. Honestly, I did not think I would need to repeat each step to you as if you were a child!'Ghân-buri-Ghân and the Wild Men have agreed to help Théoden and his Riders, and will show you the way to their encampment.The Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan is located west of the hill of Eilenach, along the road.Find the entrance to the Rohirrim encampment west of Eilenach, along the roadWídfara is at the entrance to the Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan.Talk to Wídfara on the edge of the Rohirrim encampment in Taur DrúadanWídfara thought you should visit many of the Riders encamped in Taur Drúadan, believing it would lift their spirits.Talk to Herubrand in the western reach of the encampment in Taur DrúadanTalk to Fastred in the north-eastern reach of the encampment in Taur DrúadanTalk to Grimbold in the centre of the encampment in Taur DrúadanTalk to Elfmar in the eastern reach of the encampment in Taur DrúadanThrymm Red-beard and his Éored are on a rise in the south-eastern reaches of the Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan.Talk to Thrymm Red-beard in the south-eastern reaches of the encampment in Taur DrúadanHarding is in the north-eastern reaches of the Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan.Talk to Harding in the north-eastern reaches of the encampment in Taur DrúadanHarding told you to speak to Ingbert at his tents to the west, in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan.Talk to Ingbert in the eastern area of the encampment in Taur Drúadan'Wild Men go through wood by secret paths. Khôr-buri-Ghân will take you to Horse-men. Go at once! Stonehouse-folk not stand long against gorgûn.'

<rgb=#FF0000>You will automatically go with Khôr-buri-Ghân when you accept this quest.</rgb>'Wild Men go through wood by secret paths. Khôr-buri-Ghân will take you to Horse-men. Go at once! Stonehouse-folk not stand long against gorgûn.''Who comes hither? ${PLAYER}? I can hardly believe it!''Horse-men camp down stone-road ahead. Wild Men count many thousand Horse-men. Go to them. You help. Wild Men help also.''You are a long way from the Northmarch, ${PLAYER}! I did not know you would respond to the muster of Théoden, but I should have, with no question! I know the King will be glad to see you have arrived!

'I came at the behest of Harding, though the injury I suffered at the hands of Núrzum still pains me. The Aldor gave me a new steed to replace poor Windfoot, and its gait is less gentle than that of my old friend. Still, my leg is on the mend.

'I could not refuse Harding's command, and would not, even were both my legs wounded as the one. For now the multitude waits for the King to decide our further course; there is some trouble in our way. While the Riders wait on his command, you should speak with some of the men. I am certain the spirits of many will be buoyed to see you again!''While the Riders wait on his command, you should speak with some of the men. I am certain the spirits of many will be buoyed to see you again!''Ah, welcome to the last journey, ${PLAYER}! The Stonedeans are behind, and Gondor lies ahead! The words of Hildegard served me as a shield in my fight with the Dunlendings, as I knew they would. The seeress spoke of my death by Orkish hand? Then let me fight the Dunlending tribes from morning until night!

'But now that Woodhurst is reclaimed and Gifemund oversees it in my name, I will not refuse the command of my King. I march to war, and I wonder if the Orc that will lay me low stands ready somewhere at the end of this road. He certainly does, but I will not let that dissuade me from facing him!

'I have prepared my son Herefara for this, if my days are soon to be ended. He knows our duty. Join us and rejoice, ${PLAYER}! Glory and honour awaits us!''I thought you would be somewhere in this assemblage, ${PLAYER}! My lands were well-represented in the muster, and my heart soared to see the gladness in my King's eye for it. Three score and three hundred valiant men of the Sutcrofts awaiting his command!

'My son Folcred will weather the storm in Edoras, with my mother.  It was the vile Crúmgam that moved her to spirit Folcred away from Snowbourn, but now I am glad that it was done. The Lady Éowyn will keep them safe, and my wife Elflád has gone after them to wait until the war be done.

'For my part, I am hoping to see Crúmgam in our path on the road to Gondor. He and I have unfinished matters to which we must attend. Insults were done to me and my house, and I would have them repaid... repaid in blood!''We meet again, ${PLAYER}, once more on the eve of battle! I have ten Éoreds of men from the Westfold with me, and we ride with Théoden King to the aid of Gondor. Some of these men fought with me at the Fords, and still others defended the Deep from Saruman's malice. They catch what rest they can before we make to move again.

'There is some trouble on the road ahead. I heard scouts tell that Orcs have blocked the straight way to the Pelennor. The danger does not worry me. Nay, I am worried that dealing with this force will slow us overmuch, and by the time we reach the White City it will be filled not with doughty men but with ravening Orcs.

'Time is our greatest enemy on this road, ${PLAYER}, as it ever is.''It is good to see you among the soldiers, ${PLAYER}! My father and I responded to the muster of Théoden King with as many men as we could find. We have eight Éoreds between us, a fine number. The Elfward will prove itself worthy of its reputation!

'I hope to fight alongside my shield-brother Éomer in the coming engagement, but that may not come to pass. The marching order is not yet set, but when we come to Pelennor he will likely ride with the King's Men and I will be among the Eorlingas. Still, the press of battle will no doubt allow us both much chance for glory!

'I am certain you will attain no small share of it for yourself, friend ${CLASS}!''Speak to Ingbert of the Entwash Vale! His tent is just to the west of here.''There is a familiar face, though it was long ago and many miles from here when last I saw it: ${PLAYER} the ${CLASS}!

'I am delighted to tell you that my son Horn has come to his senses at last! He has had a welcome change of heart and ridden with the company, as he should.

'And most welcome of all: he has left his wild-woman behind!''${PLAYER}! What adventures have brought you to join this assemblage? I have here but one Éored, but it is composed of the fiercest warriors in all of Rohan, I stake my honour upon it! We have been through much to come so to this company.

'We received the call to the weapontake on the night of March 11th, and I began to gather what men I could: two Éoreds of Wildermore's most skilled riders. At dawn, I led these men east to the Wold, but I did not know that danger was so near: under cover of darkness, Orcs and Easterlings had crossed the river at the Undeeps. They overwhelmed the watchers at Stangard and crossed into the Wold.

'The Enemy assailed Harwick, but Aldor Harding had already gone to the weapontake. His son Léodwig remained in the city, and saw that all would soon be lost. That is when my Éoreds charged right into the flank of the Enemy! Our fortunes did not hold, and we were soon routed. The city was now afire. Léodwig rode forth and fought valiantly, but he was grievously wounded. We withdrew to a defensible position on a hilltop; it was to be the location of our last stand.

'Early in the morning on March 12th, as the Enemy prepared to climb the hill and put an end to us, one of our scouts reported hearing a sighing sound on the wind. It was the rustling of branches, thousands of them, and it rushed down from Wildermore in a great flood of sound.

'Behind this great sound came the trees, dozens of trees in every variety, and among them strode great shepherds. At first I thought these were devils of Núrzum's kind, but it was not so. They were led by a strange fellow by the name of Leaflock, and he spoke in halting fashion of many unfathomable mysteries, some of which I have still not unravelled. There will be time for determining the meaning behind his words later, if we survive the war ahead. He did not consider himself or his people warriors, but I can tell you they became terror on the battlefield. The Enemy will not come back to the Wold.

'There was only one Éored left to me after this battle. We rode with haste to the Entwade and changed horses. We passed Aldburg at dusk but did not stop. Upon reaching Beaconwatch we had to stop and rest, and learned there that Théoden camped in the Firien Wood the night before!

'At dawn on the 13th, with our horses refreshed, we crossed the Mering-stream and rode hard through the Beacon Hills. We were so close, but not yet close enough: we were beset by Orcs and Variags near Amon Dîn. We broke through the line of Variags, but they did not pursue us. I think now I know the reason: they knew the road ahead would be held against us.

'In the darkness of night, we came to the western flank of the encampment in the Drúadan, and were nearly turned into pincushions by Grimbold's sentries! At the last moment, one of the archers recognized me by the spectre of my beard, crimson in the guttering light of the single torch they allowed! Can you imagine what Cyneberg would say if this great tale of mine, of riding more than five hundred miles in three days to the aid of my King, ended with me and my men put in the ground by our own sworn-brothers? I can imagine what she would say, and it makes my head hurt more than any of Núrzum's tantrums!

'That is the tale, ${PLAYER}. We have ridden hard, and through battle, to stand with Théoden King. Onward to triumph and glory! I have spoken already with Harding, and given him news of his son, but you should speak to him also. He is in the north-eastern reaches of the encampment.''You have joined this ride as well, I see. Should we get to Minas Tirith in time, there will be quite a battle. But it is not certain we will do so, for there is some trouble ahead in the road.'

You tell Harding that you spoke with Thrymm about his journey from Wildermore.

'Then you must have heard that my son Léodwig led the defence of Harwick against the creeping Orcs and Easterlings? My heart swelled to hear the Red-beard speak of my son's charge from the city! He will bear his wounds proudly, and if my own road is to end in the battles to come, I will know that the Wold is in capable hands. I did not think my boy would have the ability to lead men in battle; indeed, I sent him to investigate Wildermore in part because I thought he could not handle the dangers pressing from the east.

'But I am pleased to have been wrong. Sons have a way of growing up and becoming men, I suppose. Speak to Ingbert of Entwash Vale if you doubt me! He told me that his own son, Horn, has experienced a similar transformation!

'Ingbert's tents are to the west of here.'You have learned that Horn has joined Théoden's riders for the journey to Gondor.Horn is in the clearing where his father Ingbert's men are resting before the ride continues.

Ingbert claims that his son has finally come to his senses, and challenged you to find out for yourself.Talk to Horn in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur DrúadanHorn is in the clearing where his father Ingbert's men are resting before the ride continues.Talk to Horn in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur DrúadanGléowine is near Elfhelm's tent in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan.

Horn has asked you to speak with Gléowine if you want to hear his side of the argument.Talk to Gléowine in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur DrúadanElfhelm is not far from his tent in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur Drúadan.

Elfhelm has listened to your conversation with Gléowine, and has something of his own to add.Talk to Elfhelm in the Rohirrim encampment in Taur DrúadanElfhelm said someone with something to add to the subject of duty is just to the west, and you should find this person if you can keep the meeting a secret.Find the person of whom Elfhelm cryptically spoke'Nay, it is true! My son has finally shown some sense, and has chosen to leave the foolish loves of his youth behind him. He will do his duty as I his father command, and as his King requires. War is calling, and finally my son will prove he is a man of Rohan and worthy of my blood-line!

'Would that he had realized it sooner! I might have been spared much frustration. You do not believe that Horn has so chosen? Ask him yourself, and you will see!''My son has finally shown some sense, and has chosen to leave the foolish loves of his youth behind him. He will do his duty as I his father command, and as his King requires. War is calling, and finally my son will prove he is a man of Rohan and worthy of my blood-line!

'Would that he had realized it sooner! I might have been spared much frustration. You do not believe that Horn has so chosen? Ask him yourself, and you will see!''This accursed darkness has cast a pall upon my heart, ${PLAYER}, but it is brightened somewhat to see you again. Much has happened since we parted. Is Corudan with you? Call him over and I will tell you my tale, for he should hear of it as well.'

You tell Horn that you were separated from Corudan before the forces of Saruman fell upon Helm's Deep, and were unable to find the Elf after the fighting ended. Horn is silent for a time, and then shakes his head.

'I am sure he is fine, wherever he has gone. I do not believe he would fall prey to Saruman's evil so easily, or so quietly. That there is no trace of him should lighten our worry, not increase it.'

It seems to your ear that Horn is trying to convince himself more than you, but you do not press the issue. He is silent again for a long moment. When he finally speaks, his voice is full of sorrow.

'Nona and I did not find the Falcon Clan raiding-party, ${PLAYER}. Indeed, I think now that she did not really intend to search for Lheu Brenin at all; her plan was in service of a ruse. I blame her not for it, now, but I confess that when the shape of her mind became clear I spoke harshly.

'She wanted to get me alone to tell me to abandon Rohan. She wanted us to find a place where we might live free from the demands of kings and Wizards. 'If the forces of evil triumph, let us face them together, not for kingdom or for blood-lines but for ourselves!' she said. But I would not listen. I was angry to be made to choose in this fashion. I told her that I did not always know what it meant to be a man of Rohan, but our adventures together taught me much on the matter, and I wanted to serve my King as I should.

'There were more words, and they were hurtful. I made to leave, and Nona told me that if I rode to war with Théoden, I would die before the walls of the White City. 'How can you know this?' I asked her, but before she answered I knew the answer. Hildegard the Soothsayer spoke to Nona of my death. Nona believed it, though I do not.

'That was not her final attack, ${PLAYER}, and this last strike was to be the most deadly, though it also proved decisive in the matter. Nona said to me that she was with child. Hildegard had told her this, but Nona did not need the words of the soothsayer to believe it. I... I believed it too.''The air was still and cold, and the silence stretched between us. I was torn between two duties: service to the woman of my heart and the child our love would bring forth, and to Théoden, the king of my home-land.

'And that is when I realized that they were not different at all. If Rohan is consumed by war and falls to the Enemy's hordes, it will not matter where Nona and I and our child might prepare for our last stand. We will be alone. Together, but alone, and the Enemy will have us.

'But if Théoden defies the forces of Mordor, and finds victory, then there might be peace. Only then will there be safety for the woman I love, and for the child our love is bringing forth. Only then. She thinks I chose to ride for Théoden to seek glory, or for honour, but neither of those are the reason. I ride for Rohan for my Dunlending wife, and for my Dunlending child, that they may be safe from the evils of Mordor.

'It was the most difficult decision I have ever had to make, ${PLAYER}, and it may have been the wrong one. But still I have made it. It was the only thing I could have done, and if it is wrong and Hildegard is right, and I ride to my death in Gondor far from those I love... well, you have my permission to tell my old music-master Gléowine that he was right after all.'

You look at Horn quizzically.

'Oh, Gléowine and I have been having a disagreement about what it means to fight. Do not tell him this, but I think I have been arguing mostly to convince myself, rather than him. I have cut a most impressive figure doing so, I think: the very picture of a valiant man of Rohan. I seem to have convinced my father, at any rate. Find Gléowine if you would like to hear his side of the argument.

'It is good to see you, ${PLAYER}. I wish that we had met again in happier circumstances, but there are few enough of those in these dark days. Stay well, my friend.''Gléowine and I have been having a disagreement about what it means to fight. Do not tell him this, but I think I have been arguing mostly to convince myself, rather than him. I have cut a most impressive figure doing so, I think: the very picture of a valiant man of Rohan. I seem to have convinced my father, at any rate. Find Gléowine if you would like to hear his side of the argument.

'It is good to see you, ${PLAYER}. I wish that we had met again in happier circumstances, but there are few enough of those in these dark days. Stay well, my friend.''I bid you welcome, ${PLAYER}! Have you seen Horn? My former student is a knot of confusions, and speaking with you might help him unravel them!'

You tell Gléowine that you found Horn in the state he describes, but are unsure if your words provided him any comfort.

'I am glad you tried. I think he is being foolish, and have told him so, but he refuses to listen to me. He thinks he is doing the right thing, but I can tell you that Nona does not agree, and I do not either. What good will it do her, and the child on its way, if Horn dies now? Little or none, ${PLAYER}. Glory and honour? They will be of little consolation and no comfort to the family he leaves behind.

'I am no warrior. I record what befalls the warriors in song, and know all too well what that means. I dress it up in nice clothes, and with pretty words, but still I know it for what it is: death, and death, and death.

'Horn is full of pride, like his father Ingbert, and will not be persuaded. He is a man of Rohan, for certain: stubborn and hard-headed, thinking always of duty and honour and service! May it not be the death of him!'

You think that Gléowine may have been blinded to the truth of the matter by Horn's blustery words of justification, but you do not correct him.'Horn is full of pride, like his father Ingbert, and will not be persuaded. He is a man of Rohan, for certain: stubborn and hard-headed, thinking always of duty and honour and service! May it not be the death of him!''It is not always an easy thing to decide what duty compels us to do. I know of someone who might have something to add to this debate, ${PLAYER}. Look for him just to the west, if you can control your tongue and keep a confidence.'Dernhelm looks at you warily, but he says nothing.'Hullo there, ${PLAYER}! At last someone will speak to me! I have only had Dernhelm here for company, and he speaks very little. It is almost as if none of the men here can see me! I am almost certainly not supposed to be here; I hope that Théoden will not be cross with me when he finds out.

'But what was I supposed to do? I could not stay behind while all others have gone off to danger! That has been this hobbit's story almost from the very beginning! Frodo, and Sam, and Pip, all gone off now, braving danger in strange lands. I could not let it happen again! I am grateful that Dernhelm saw fit to help me come along.

'He has an understanding with Elfhelm, I think. The old marshal is turning a blind eye to my presence here, and I am glad of it! I just hope I am not too much of a burden... on the road or in the battle that is certain to come.'There is trouble on the road ahead. If the Rohirrim are to come to Pelennor in time, they will need another plan.Théoden meets with his advisors in a clearing at the base of Eilenach, in Taur Drúadan.Find the clearing where Théoden meets with his advisorsThéoden meets with his advisors in a clearing at the base of Eilenach, in Taur Drúadan.Talk to Théoden outside his tent in Taur DrúadanTalk to Éomer outside Théoden's tent in Taur DrúadanGhân-buri-Ghân is meeting with Théoden and his advisors in Taur Drúadan.Talk to Ghân-buri-Ghân while he meets with ThéodenThe entrance to the Stonewain Valley is far south of the Rohirrim encampment, and north-east of Drû Bhûta.Clear fallen stones near the northern entrance to Stonewain ValleyThe Stonewain Valley runs north and south, not far from Drû Bhûta, in Taur Drúadan.Clear fallen stones in the Stonewain ValleyThe Stonewain Valley runs north and south, not far from Drû Bhûta, in Taur Drúadan.Clear fallen stones in the Stonewain ValleyThe Stonewain Valley runs north and south, not far from Drû Bhûta, in Taur Drúadan.Clear fallen stones in the Stonewain ValleyThe Stonewain Valley runs north and south, not far from Drû Bhûta, in Taur Drúadan.Clear fallen stones in the Stonewain ValleyÉomer is at the southern end of the Stonewain Valley.Talk to Éomer at the southern end of Stonewain Valley'I am sick of waiting around! Let us see if we can get a look at what Théoden is doing. I will use my natural sneakiness to remain out of sight, but I cannot bear not knowing what is going on!

'The King is meeting with his advisors in a clearing to the south-west. It is not far!''Let us see if we can get a look at what Théoden is doing. I will use my natural sneakiness to remain out of sight, but I cannot bear not knowing what is going on!

'The King is meeting with his advisors in a clearing to the south-west. It is not far!''I understand we have you to thank for sending to us these Woses, these Wild Men, ${PLAYER}. Their hatred of Orcs is similar to our own, and quite useful in this case, for the Orcs have made camp in the shadow of Amon Dîn and block our road.

'My six thousand spears could scatter this Orc barricade, but it would take time, and that time cannot be spared. Ghân-buri-Ghân says there is fire on the plain before Minas Tirith, and within the walls. The defenders will not last if we do not come soon.

'Ghân-buri-Ghân says he knows a secret way around the Orc camp. He says he can show us a forgotten road that will get us near to the city without delay. We must take this road!''My uncle has spoken with the leader of these Woses, these Wild Men, and has accepted their aid. Ghân-buri-Ghân speaks of a secret way that will allow us to skirt the Orcs blocking the road beneath Amon Dîn.

'He has staked his life upon it, for he will walk beside Théoden on this secret way. If he proves false, I am to kill him where he stands. If this is a trick, I will slay him without hesitation.''Ghân-buri-Ghân make promise to you, ${PLAYER}. Now Ghân fulfill it. Wild Men will help Horse-men.

'Stonehouse-folk once very strong. Carved stones from hills. Carried stones on large wains. Built stonehouses on side of mountain. Road still there, but Stonehouse-folk forget. Wild Men not forget.

'Horse-men pass through Stonewain Valley. Entrance is north-east of Drû Bhûta, far south of here. Way is rocky. Clear stones from path to make safe for Horse-men's passage. Then Horse-men and ${PLAYER} fight gorgûn in front of great Stone-city. Wild Men live in peace.''Horse-men pass through Stonewain Valley. Entrance is north-east of Drû Bhûta, far south of here. Way is rocky. Clear stones from path to make safe for Horse-men's passage. Then Horse-men and ${PLAYER} fight gorgûn in front of great Stone-city. Wild Men live in peace.''Ghân-buri-Ghân has proven trustworthy, ${PLAYER}. We are fortunate he came to our aid!

'We may have come here just in time. Minas Tirith is not yet taken!'Aggressive láthbears have been spotted in the Firien Wood, due to the recent enemy occupation of the Beacon Hills.Láthbears can be found around the Firien Wood in the Beacon Hills.

You should defeat láthbears.Defeat Firien láthbears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many láthbears in the Beacon Hills.CompletedAggressive láthbears have been spotted in the Firien Wood and some have even ventured towards Gondorian settlements. These beasts should be dealt with for the safety of those who dwell near the Firien Wood.You have defeated many láthbears in the Beacon Hills.Narvellon has been tasked with keeping Ost Rimmon alight at night but has left his supplies at the various beacons in the region.Narvellon the Beacon-keeper has asked you to gather flint and paraffin from the beacons of the Beacon Hills.Collect paraffin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect flint (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Narvellon in Ost Rimmon with the lighting supplies that you gathered from the beacons.'If only I had looked after the lights of Ost Rimmon as I did the beacons upon their hills! Glánhar wants our lights left burning at night now but we shall go dark as a winking eye, as I left the supplies at the beacons.

'If you should venture out, ${CLASS}, will you visit the beacons and gather the flint and paraffin I left? There's no one else to do it, I'm afraid!''Explore the beacons Halifirien, Calenhad, Min-Rimmon, and Erelas along the Beacon Hills and you'll find some flint and paraffin at every one of them. Why, there might even be some at Nardol and Amon Dîn, in Taur Drúadan.''Why, that will do quite well for keeping the lamps alight. Let us hope that Glánhar's notion is a correct one, and all this light will keep the enemy at bay!'Lavandir rushed to Ost Rimmon and inadvertently left his flocks unattended and possibly in danger.You should gather the sheep on the hills due north of Erelas in the Beacon Hills.Gather sheep from the north of Erelas (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should lead the first flock of sheep back to Ost Rimmon.Lead the sheep to safetyYou should gather the sheep from the north-eastern slope of Calenhad in the Beacon Hills.Gather sheep from the slope of Calenhad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should lead the second flock of sheep back to Ost Rimmon.Lead the sheep to safetyYou should gather the sheep west of Bâr Nadhron in the Beacon Hills.Gather sheep west of Bâr Nadhron (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should lead the third flock of sheep back to Ost Rimmon.Lead the sheep to safetyNow that you have led the flocks of sheep to safety, you should return to Lavandir in Ost Rimmon.Return to Lavandir'What a bind! I ran here in a fright and left my flocks grazing out upon the hills. Now I don't dare go back out, what with those bad men and their swords roaming to and fro. Now you, you're not afraid of those men, are you, ${CLASS}? Would you go and lead my sheep back here to me? If they're left out there, they'll be eaten up by wolves and swordsmen, every last one!

'My flocks will not have strayed far. They were on the pastures north of Erelas, north-east of Calenhad, and west of Bâr Nadhron.''If you would lead my flocks to safety here, my heart would rest easy, ${CLASS}. The flocks are like as not where I left them, north of Erelas, north-east of Calenhad, and west of Bâr Nadhron.''If you would lead my flocks to safety here, my heart would rest easy, ${CLASS}. The flocks are like as not where I left them, on the hills due north and north-west of here.''You would make a fine shepherd, ${RACE}! My flocks are accounted for and unaware of the troubles surrounding them. If we are held here long, they will make food and garments for these folk too, as well they ought.''If you would lead my flocks to safety here, my heart would rest easy, ${CLASS}. The flocks are like as not where I left them, on the hills due north and north-west of here.''If you would lead my flocks to safety here, my heart would rest easy, ${CLASS}. The flocks are like as not where I left them, on the hills due north and north-west of here.'It is time to meet with Jarl Akhiz and free Glánhar's sons.Go down into the cellar in Bâr Nadhron and find Akhiz.Go down into the cellarShow Jarl Akhiz that you have brought the ransom.Show Akhiz the ransomGo past the opened gate and see that the hostages are all right.Talk to LachegTalk to TogborNow that you have seen Lacheg and Togbor, return to Jarl Akhiz to complete the ransom.Return to AkhizNow that you have seen Lacheg and Togbor, return to Jarl Akhiz for the ransom payment and choose which of the brothers to heed.Place the ransom in the chestCreate a distractionIt is time to leave the cellar in Bâr Nadhron.Leave the cellar!'You have the gold and the brooch I asked of you? Tut tut! I can see it well enough from there. We will do this after the Variag fashion. Now you will go and see that the two brothers are safe and well.

'I have only the two. Trust me, if I had the father and the other two, you would need tenfold the ransom you have brought.

'Now go. You will find them to the right, that way beyond the gate.''This is the Variag way: you go and see that the two brothers are hale and unharmed, and then you shall return and pay their ransom.''Listen!'

His voice drops to a whisper.

'Are we really to give these honourless men ransom-gold for their villainy? You look a capable fighter. Let us instead create a distraction. I have managed to get a small blade in my  hands, here. When I hear a commotion, I will cut our bonds and we will make for the door. Then we shall meet up at whatever place my brother chooses.

'Will you do it? Say you will!''So our ransom will be paid, finally? Good. 

'Listen to me. These are dangerous men. But I have come to believe that they do have their own kind of blackguard's honour. If you do as they ask, they truly will set us free. If you do aught to alarm them, they will respond swiftly and ruthlessly. You must not listen to my brother's hot-headed prattling. Let us part with our gold and take our lives with us when we go.

'They are likely to send us out one at a time. We must have an agreed upon place to meet. Hm... East of Bâr Nadhron, under the bent tree by the Mouths of the Entwash would do.  Yes, let us find each other there.''Did you heed my words? Create a distraction, and we shall cut our bonds and fly, and they will have not a single ransom-coin for their brigandry!''If you honour the agreement, so shall they. The Variags have this much of a sense of honesty, at any rate.

'I hope I shall see you east of Bâr Nadhron, under the  bent tree by the Mouths of the Entwash, ${PLAYER}.''You are satisfied? Good! Then place the ransom in the chest there and all shall be arranged. You will leave first, and the brothers will be sent out separately after you. 

'This is how it is done. No trickery. I give you my word.'Help Sithgar reshoe and saddle his unruly horse.Find the blacksmith in Ost Rimmon and see if he can provide Sithgar with shoes for Witherbreca.Talk to the blacksmithDringrod doesn't have any horseshoes or iron to make them. He suggested you gather abandoned tools from the quarry at the back of Ost Rimmon, which he can melt and forge into shoes.Gather abandoned tools (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Dringrod with the quarry tools you have gathered, so that he can forge them into horseshoes.Talk to Bargwen in Ost Rimmon about acquiring a new saddle.You should gather deer hides from the deer that can be found by the Firien Wood, in the western Beacon Hills, so that Bargwen can craft a saddle for Sithgar.Gather deer hides (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bring the deer hides back to Bargwen in Ost Rimmon, so that she can craft a saddle for Sithgar.You should go speak with Sithgar now that a saddle and shoes are being provided for his horse.Return to Sithgar'I must ask your help once more, ${CLASS}. I dare not let Witherbreca out of my sight, but as you can see, she has lost both her saddle and her shoes. Would you ask the people in Ost Rimmon and see if they have any to spare?

'There is a blacksmith here who might have horseshoes. I would begin with him.''Yes, I think the blacksmith is the best place to start. With any luck, he'll have the horseshoes at the ready.''Horseshoes? No, I have none. But if it is for that poor boy from Rohan, I could make him some. Hum! I am short of iron, as well. Why don't you go to the quarry, which is far at the back of Ost Rimmon, and gather some of the tools that were abandoned there. They are beginning to rust and we can put them to better use.''The quarry is at the far end of Ost Rimmon, opposite the stables from whence you came. Bring back a good handful of the tools that have been left around there, and I'll forge you those horseshoes.''You've got them, then? I'll forge them up and shoe the horse myself. Don't you worry.

'Now, the lad will need a saddle, too? Bargwen might help him with that. If we haven't any, she is handy with leather and will work him a new one. She's easy enough to find. You went right by her on your way to the quarry.''Bargwen should be able to set the young rider up with a saddle, one way or another. Head towards the quarry and you'll find her.''A saddle? Do I look like a stable-hand to you? Just because Dringrod never wants to do a thing but work unpaid and unbidden doesn't mean he can make others do so.

'Besides, you will find nary a saddle in Ost Rimmon, I happen to know.

'Fine! I will make you one, but I will need good leather. We have not any of that either, so you will need to hunt some of the deer found near the Firien Wood.''These hides are good. Well hunted, ${RACE}. I will get this done with as soon as I can. Now leave me to my work.''Deer aplenty can be found by the Firien Wood, west of here. Bring me some hides and I suppose I will make a saddle to be rid of this young rider of Rohan.''Look, ${PLAYER}! Witherbreca is fit to ride. Pray only that I am fit to keep her by me from here on out. I must ride - but if you should catch up with the host of Rohan before we reach the Pelennor, seek me there and I shall give you a warm welcome among my own people.'Sithgar has lost his steed and won't be able to re-join his Éored until he finds it.Search the Beacon Hills for Sithgar's runaway steed.Find the runaway horseYou found the runaway steed in the Beacon Hills. Now you should lead her back to the stables in Ost Rimmon.Lead the horse to Ost RimmonSithgar stands by the Ost Rimmon stables, in the Beacon Hills.

Now that you have led his horse back to the stables, you should talk to Sithgar.Talk to Sithgar'Fortune scowls on my every venture, ${CLASS}! I was given a horse half-broken in, and now she has run off on me. Twice I have found her again, only to have her bolt off before I could lead her to the stables!

'Like as not, she has returned to the hills west of the ford, in view of the Entwash. But what use is going after her again?

'My Éored has gone on without me and at a brisk pace at that. What hope have I of catching them? None! No hope at all.''Twice I've found my horse wandering these hills, but she has always run off again before I could lead her to the stables here.''Why, you have done it! But how? That a foreigner can handle Witherbreca better than a Man of Rohan... but never mind that. I have hope once more of rejoining my Éored, and that is enough.'Find and defeat Warg-rider message bearers in the Beacon Hills.You should defeat any Warg-rider message-bearers you find in the Beacon Hills.Defeat Warg-rider message-bearers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Déorwine in the camp in Taur Drúadan and tell him of the Warg-rider message-bearers you defeated.Return to Déorwine'If the Enemy has word of our passage, I am certain it is carried by Warg-riding message-bearers. We surprised one yesterday morn, and it bore some foul black scratching on some parchment, the meaning of which I could not fathom.

'If you should espy any of these Warg-riders behind us in the Beacon Hills, I implore you to stop them, lest our force should be found out.''You have defeated many of those cursed message-bearers, then? Good. Perhaps we shall make it to the field of battle undetected. My thanks, ${CLASS}.'To ensure the return of her sons, Glánhar requires Jarl Akhiz's brooch to complete the ransom.Find the rival Jarl's camp near the Firien Wood, in the western Beacon Hills.Meet Gondrad near the rival captain's campThe rival Jarl's camp is near the Firien Wood, west of Ost Rimmon.

Search for the stolen brooch among the camp.Find the stolen broochFound the stolen broochNow that you have the stolen brooch, you should bring it to Gondrad, who waits near the camp in the Firien Wood.Talk to Gondrad'This cunning wolf has named his price and I fear it must be paid. I ask the two of you to take this gold and to fetch the stolen trinket, and see if you can indeed buy back my sons from these men.

'It did not escape me that he first offered three sons for ransom and now counts but two. Yet I will not dwell on it. There is no time for grief or fruitless worry, now. We must act. 

'But I beseech you both - show caution! You are treating with scoundrels and must be cunning yourselves - and cautious, beside. I would give much to see my sons alive before me, but your lives are not among the things that I would give.

'Now go.''You must go with Gondrad and see the ransom paid. Tarry not. I have much work to do, and would dwell on what may become of my sons no more than I must.''Another chance to lose my head. What a fine day this is turning out to be!''That is the camp there, just as he told us it would be. You go on and steal back his brooch. I will remain here, doing my best to keep my head attached to my shoulders.''Those look very much like the men who assailed us before the meeting, do they not? I wonder if they will like you rifling through their things.''Why, there it is. And you don't look terribly bruised or bloodied, for all that. Things are going well for us so far, aren't they? It's how I know our luck must be about to turn.'Once known as the Riders Four, the company of Burnoth, Hutha, and Ulf struggle to ready and ride for war while still feeling the loss of their long-time friend, Léofdag.Caltrops can be found on and around the road outside of Ost Rimmon.

Variags have laid traps along the road to slow the forces of Rohan. You should disarm these traps before any more horses are wounded.Destroy caltrops on the road outside of Ost Rimmon (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Burnoth can be found within Ost Rimmon in the Beacon Hills.

You have destroyed the caltrops and should now return to Burnoth.Talk to Burnoth in Ost RimmonSets of horseshoes can be found within Ost Rimmon in the Beacon Hills.

Burnoth has asked you to aid him in shoeing the horses before he and his fellows set off.Collect sets of horseshoes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The steeds of the Riders Three can be found at the stables of Ost Rimmon.

You have collected enough horseshoes and should now shoe the steeds of the Riders Three.Hutha is at Ost Rimmon.

You have shoed the steeds and should now speak to Hutha as Burnoth asked.Talk to HuthaUlf is at Ost Rimmon.

You have spoken to Hutha and should now speak to Ulf as well.Talk to Ulf'${PLAYER}, can it be? In such dark times, I was unsure our paths would cross again.

'I am sorry for the manner of our parting - we knew not how to move forward for what seemed like days after the Battle of the Hornburg. Our warden and my dear friend, Léofdag, was slain in the fighting just before dawn.

'If only he had seen what we had! The ride of the Wizard and the forest that marched forth to harry our foes - such things cannot be believed if one had not been there at the battle's ending.

'Time to rest has been a rare gift as of late, but our foes have laid traps for our riders and even now, we are working to disarm them before any more of our steeds come to harm.

'Out beyond the gates of Ost Rimmon, they have laid jagged caltrops along the roadway. If you aid the others in removing them, we shall be able to resume our journey unhindered.''You have my thanks, friend. 

'It pains me to leave behind the steeds that trod upon the road before we discovered the Enemy's traps, but with fewer mounts to carry our men through the forest ahead, our journey to Gondor grows more complicated. The wounded steeds shall heal in time, but I hope we are enough to turn the battle to our favour.

'Our own steeds were fortunate enough to avoid the traps on our approach to Ost Rimmon, but they have ridden long and their shoes must be worn.

'Ost Rimmon surely bears no shortage of horseshoes, even in war-time. Gather them and shoe our steeds while I ready Hutha and Ulf for our ride ahead.''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}. These have been difficult times for the others and me, but war halts not at the wishes of a few Men. To think it has been but eleven days since we fought before the Hornburg... much has happened in so short a time! Much both good and ill...

'Alas, indeed, for poor Léofdag. His absence weighs heavily on us, but I fear Ulf has felt it most keenly of us all.

'We must soon speak of the manner of our parting, but now is not the time. It is better that you speak to Ulf - to see a familiar face might ease his pain, even if only for a moment.''Ah... ${PLAYER}, I was gladdened to see you among the men here in Ost Rimmon. 

'In truth, I have been deeply troubled since the Battle of the Hornburg. The loss of my friend tears at me, and as it does, I feel a lingering guilt for the fates of the others that fell that day. Oh, that life would allow the time for mourning, but never has that been the lot of a warrior. 

'I have more yet to say about Léofdag, but I shall speak not of it here. There are too many eyes upon us...'Lady Glánhar desires to make Ost Rimmon appear more well-defended than it is by outfitting straw men-at-arms.Lady Glánhar has asked you to go out of Ost Rimmon and gather sticks and trampled grass for her straw-man project.Gather trampled grass (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather sticks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Lady Glánhar in Ost Rimmon with the sticks and trampled grass you gathered for her straw-man project.Ask Narvellon the Beacon-keeper in Ost Rimmon for weapons, helms, and rough cloth for Lady Glánhar's straw men.Talk to NarvellonHelms and swords can be found throughout Ost Rimmon. Rough cloth can be found in Ost Rimmon's store-house.

Gather helms, swords, and rough cloth for Lady Glánhar's straw men.Collect helms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect swords (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather cloth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the helms, swords, and cloth for the straw-men to Glánhar in the centre of Ost Rimmon.Return to Lady GlánharBargwen is south-west of Lady Glánhar in Ost Rimmon.

Bring the materials for Lady Glánhar's straw men to Bargwen for assembly.You should tell Lady Glánhar that Bargwen has begun work on her straw men-at-arms.Return to Lady Glánhar'We know our enemies are out there, beyond these walls. They have let themselves be seen -- scouting, prowling, marching about. We know that their numbers are great, indeed. What I fear is that they soon shall see that our numbers are so few. The able men have marched for war and even our able women number few. We must make some display of arms or else it will be plain as day.

'Ha! The light of day would be a welcome change, would it not?

'I have a plan, but am beggared of able hands. Will you help me in this, ${CLASS}? We shall be making men of straw. You came from beyond these walls, so perhaps you can venture back out there and find me a good number of sticks and trampled grass. It grows long in some spots and the harvest there is easy.''If you would brave the lands beyond these walls, I will need a number of sticks and trampled grass with which to make my guards of straw.''There we have it! This shall be enough, I think.

'That was the most difficult leg. The rest of our work lies within Ost Rimmon. Go to Narvellon the Beacon-keeper and ask his help in finding weapons, helms, and rough cloth to dress our stand-in men.''Ask Narvellon for some swords, helms, and rough cloth. They needn't be fine. Anything that glints in the sun will do!''You want that for what? Heh heh. Well, she's always up to something, is she not? I'm afraid that when the men marched off to Minas Tirith, they tore the place apart and left things in utter disarray. Swords and helms are lying all about Ost Rimmon, inside and out. Someone will have to gather them up, and I'm afraid that, friend, would be you.

'As for cloth, I think you will find that laying about in the store-house.''You'll have to gather up the things yourself, I'm afraid. Swords and helms were scatted all across Ost Rimmon when the men were readying to march off. And the rough cloth will be found in the store-house. Might as well get that yourself, while you're about.''Let me see. Swords, helms, cloth - that will do. Narvellon was friendly and helpful, I hope?

'Now! We want these to be convincing. We would do well not to slouch in the final step. Yes, I think Bargwen must assemble them. She will do it right.

'Bring all these things to her, if you would. You will find her further south in Ost Rimmon.''Yes, it must be Bargwen who makes them. Would you bring these things to her? You will find her in the southern end of Ost Rimmon.''What is all this?'

Bargwen frowns at the things in your arms.

'She wants me to make what? Straw men? Well, if it keeps the barbarians out I suppose there's not much choice in the matter. No matter if my fingers bleed or my arms fall off!'

She takes the things from you and gets directly to work, still muttering under her breath.'She has begun? Good! That is one step taken towards our safety. We have not strength of arms, ${CLASS}, so we must rely on our wits. It is good we are possessed of wits!'Visit the tomb at Halifirien, where the Oath of Eorl established the eternal bond between Rohan and Gondor.Guthláf has asked you to visit the tomb at Halifirien in the Beacon Hills, and take note of what you see.Find the tomb at HalifirienDefend the tomb at Halifirien.You should return to Guthláf and tell him how you saw the tomb at Halifirien and defended it from pillagers.Return to Guthláf'One regret I hold is that, in our hard ride through the Beacon Hills, I could not stop and climb up Halifirien's height. For there, I am told, is a tomb. And before that place, the Oath of Eorl between Rohan and Gondor was forged so that in time of great need one would come to the other's aid, as brothers in blood and mind.

'It is thus that we ride now, though I think am not alone in feeling that we go not just to defend our brethren of Gondor, but all of Middle-earth as well.

'Well! Soon we shall move on again. But if you should find yourself there, take note for me of what you see. And if we should meet again, on this road or another, share with me what you had seen.''Now, I do not ask you to go wide of your course... but if you should happen to come near that tomb on Halifirien, would you go up it and feast your eyes for me?''You return! What news do you bring?'

You relate how you saw the tomb of Halifirien, what it looked like, and how you defended it against pillagers.

'Why, what a tale you weave, ${CLASS}! Your words have painted a picture in my mind, and so I shall carry that picture as a trinket into battle.'Déorwine has requested help in aiding his rearguard to cover the tracks left behind by the Rohirrim.Help Déorwine's scouts and cover any tracks left by the Rohirrim in the Beacon Hills.Cover Rohirrim tracks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Déorwine in the camp in Taur Drúadan and tell him that you have covered many of the Rohirrim tracks in the Beacon Hills.Return to Déorwine'While Théoden King's outriders look ahead, I am charged with leading the rearguard scouts who must look behind. We know we are being watched and that enemy forces creep up from all sides. I have sent my men out to cover what tracks they may, but I fear our marks were too many and my scouts too few.

'Will you aid them in this effort, and cover tracks where you find them in the Beacon Hills? I thank you, ${CLASS}.'Déorwine smiles widely as you describe all the Rohirrim tracks you covered. 

'I am glad to have you as a friend and not a foe, ${CLASS}! I shall rest easier knowing  how much harder a time the enemy will have in tracking our trail.'You have scouted the Variag camp at Bâr Nadhron, and now one thing remains....The time has come to enter the cellar in Bâr Nadhron, in the Beacon Hills, and pay the ransom for Glánhar's sons.Enter the cellar in Bâr NadhronYou should return to Gondrad and tell him all that took place in the cellar at Bâr Nadhron.Return to Gondrad'So go we must - and by WE let me make clear that I mean YOU. I will wait here, and you are to whistle twice if you need me. I am only being cautious as I was bade!Gondrad's eyes go wide as you relay all that took place. 

'It is well you went in and not I, at any rate. I would have made a mess of things, as I always seem to.'Poor Léofdag fell at the Battle of the Hornburg, but the treachery surrounding his death greatly troubles the Riders Three.Erelas can be found north-east of Ost Rimmon.

The Riders Three have asked you to meet them at the beacon-hill Erelas.Meet the Riders Three at ErelasBurnoth, Hutha, and Ulf are at the beacon-hill Erelas.

You have arrived at Erelas and should now speak to the Riders Three about the truth of Léofdag's demise.Talk to Burnoth at ErelasTalk to Hutha at ErelasTalk to Ulf at ErelasThe Riders Three can be found somewhere among the War-stead of the King's Men in Taur Drúadan.

The Riders Three have asked you to join them in their quest and their journey to the Pelennor Fields.Meet the Riders Three at their camp in Taur DrúadanBurnoth is at his camp within the War-stead of the King's Men.

You have arrived in Taur Drúadan and should now speak to Burnoth.Talk to Burnoth in Taur Drúadan'Indeed, ${PLAYER}, this is not the place for us to speak of such things. We are overdue to set out for Taur Drúadan, but we can spare the time to make clear these matters. 

'Will you accompany us to the beacon-hill Erelas? It lies to the north-east of Ost Rimmon and should not be difficult to spot, even with its flames quenched.

'I am sorry to be so terse, but you shall understand when we meet again. Ride!''${PLAYER}, I trust that you shall share not of what I tell you.

'Léofdag's death was no mistake. It is my opinion that it was a conspiracy of the Enemy's design. He fell just as our foes were routed - and after surviving so much else!

'I heard him fall beside me and call out, so I rushed to his side. He had been struck by two arrows, yet if he felt pain from them, he showed it not. Instead, he clutched only at his throat as if he were choking. His breathing grew laboured and in moments, he was gone.

'The arrows used to slay him were of Rohirric make, but they bore a poison that none in our company had ever encountered.

'Léofdag was murdered by one of the Men among the Riders, of this I am certain.''I confess I know less of Léofdag's fate than the others, but it pains me just the same.

'We became separated during the battle after the wall was shattered. I fell back to a higher position, but I saw Léofdag make for the breach with the Ranger and his allies.

'When our foes made for the wounded, I retreated into the tower to defend them. That was the last I saw of Léofdag...''Léofdag was slain by one of our own!

'To hold back my fury for so long... I shall not let his death go unanswered. His murderer lurks among our number in Rohan, I know it!

'How can we find one among so many? And does he dare to strike again so near to the King?

'Mark my words, ${PLAYER}... when we find him, and we shall, none will hold me back from him!

'Let us ride to Taur Drúadan, and there we can plan our next move.''I am gladdened that you found us amongst so many of our kinsmen, my friend. Although I worry that Léofdag's killer still lurks within the War-steads, I think him not enough a fool to make his presence known before we reach the battlefield in Gondor.

'We spoke much on what course to take through Taur Drúadan before your arrival, and you must be told of our purpose here in the forest.'Togbor believes his younger brother Malhar fled into the Mouths of the Entwash after escaping their captors.Look for a trail in the Mouths of Entwash that might lead you to Malhar.Search for a trailFollow the trail in the Mouths of Entwash that might lead you to Malhar.Follow the trailFollow the trail in the Mouths of Entwash that might lead you to Malhar.Follow the trailFollow the trail in the Mouths of Entwash that might lead you to Malhar.Follow the trailFollow the trail in the Mouths of Entwash that might lead you to Malhar.Follow the trailThe last clue in the trail points up a hill, deep in the Mouths of Entwash. You should see what is there.Go up the hillTalk to the young man in the clearing on the hill in the Mouths of Entwash.Talk to the young man'My brother Malhar slipped his bonds and escaped the Variags. I know not how, only that if anyone might have done so and survived, it was he. Our youngest brother is possessed of the quickest wits in a quick-witted family.

'We had agreed, while they dragged us to Bâr Nadhron, that if any of us escaped we would make for the Mouths of Entwash. I doubt not that he went there and is likely there still.

'We must return to Ost Rimmon, but I beg you, ${CLASS}, go into the marshland and find Malhar. I have every hope he yet lives and, if luck is on our side, has left some trail to find him by.''We must return to Ost Rimmon but I beg of you, head into the Mouths of Entwash and search for Malhar. He may still be alive.''In truth, I would go with you, ${PLAYER}, but I see from my brother's stern look that it is not to be.''Narvagol, dead? That is a blow. And now the fact that I never polished his armour will haunt me to the end of my days!''Who are you? You do not look to be a foe, so I judge you friend. I am Malhar, son of Lachnir.'

The young man starts to extend a hand, then winces and holds his side. He gives a wry, pained grin.

'Though if you were foe, I fear I should be somewhat at your mercy. I am starved and wounded and very ready indeed to get back out of this marsh!'Malhar is ready to leave the marshland, but first he must regain his strength before he can leave.Lomthond can be found near the Falls of Rauros, sticks among the nearby trees, and lizards can be found throughout the Mouths of Entwash.

Bring Malhar some lomthond, a walking stick, and some lizard-meat so that he has the strength to make the trek back out of the marshland.Gather lomthond (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find a walking stickGather lizard-meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Malhar with the things you gathered, on the hill in the eastern part of Mouths of Entwash.You recount the details of the ransom meeting and explain that Togbor sent you in search of him.

'Then I beg your forgiveness. My escape has caused you more trouble than if I had stayed put. I am afraid I must be more trouble to you yet, for I do not have the strength to make it back from here.

'But I believe I will, if you could bring me several things. I need a herb named lomthond, or wearywort to the shepherds, which can be found near to the Falls of Rauros and has properties that can ease the pain and grant me some vigour. I must eat, too. Of all the things in this place, the meat of the small lizards found about is most edible. And even with these in my stomach, bolstering my strength, I think I shall need a good stick to lean on. Perhaps there is one lying under one of these trees.''Find me lomthond near the Falls of the Rauros, a good walking stick, and some sustaining lizard-meat, and I think I shall be up for the journey, ${PLAYER}.''You have gathered it all! You are a fine ${RACE} to be lost in a marsh with, do you know that? Now give me a moment to gather myself up and then I shall be ready to go.'The Rohirrim have passed through the Stonewain Valley, and now they prepare for battle.Éomer is at the southern end of the Stonewain Valley, and he prepares to ride out of the vale and toward the Pelennor's encircling wall.Talk to Éomer from horseback and join Théoden's RidersThéoden's Riders have taken the field!Talk to Théoden on the Field of Onslaught'Ready your steed, my friend! The time has come for riding with haste!'

<rgb=#00FF00>Mount your travelling steed and speak to Éomer to join the Rohirrim for the final leg of their journey.</rgb>'Let us ride to the encircling wall together, ${PLAYER}!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'This day will live long in the hearts of my people! This is a day for deeds of honour and glory, ${PLAYER}!'Variag forces working for the enemy have taken to the shadows of the Firien Wood.You should defeat the Variags you find in the Firien Wood.Defeat Variags in the Firien Wood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many Variags in the Firien Wood.CompletedVariag forces working for the enemy lurk in the Firien Wood and should be rooted out.You have defeated many Variags in the Firien Wood.Root out forces of the enemy that have taken to the Mouths of Entwash.You should defeat the Orcs and Variags you find in the Mouths of Entwash.Defeat enemies in the Entwash (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Forces of the enemy now creep through the Mouths of Entwash and should be rooted out.The leaves of the Entscloak plant eases the pains of the elderly and Narvellon has requested aid in procuring some for himself and others.You should gather Entscloak in the Mouths of the Entwash for Narvellon.Gather Entscloak (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the Entscloak you gathered back to Narvellon in Ost Rimmon.Return to Narvellon'If you should find yourself out amid the Mouths of the Entwash, perhaps you might gather some Entscloak for me? It is of no great consequence, but the leaves of the plant work wonders for the aches of the old, such as myself and Lady Glánhar.

'I will find a way to thank you for your trouble, if you did bring me some.''Entscloak? Indeed it is! You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}. My gratitude and thanks!'Strike some fear into the heart of the Variag scouts who have been watching Ost Rimmon.Defeat some of the Variag scouts who linger outside the walls of Ost Rimmon.Defeat Variag scouts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should tell Lady Glánhar that you have defeated a number of scouts outside of Ost Rimmon.Talk to Glánhar'Have you stood upon our walls and looked out? I have, often, and in the last days I have seen scouts of the foe brazenly keeping watch. If they are so brazen, they may bring report that we are vulnerable to attack. If they attack us in force, we will surely fall.

'If only someone would go out there and give those scouts a lesson in humility and decorum, I feel sure they would think twice about venturing close enough to stare us in the eyes.''Struck some fear into the hearts of those brazen scouts outside our walls, have you? That is well. Perhaps, just perhaps, with all I have put in place we will weather this storm.'Malhar is ready to travel back to Ost Rimmon.Talk to Malhar when you are ready to begin the journey out of the Mouths of Entwash.Talk to MalharAccompany Malhar on the path out of the Mouths of Entwash.Go with MalharMalhar has stopped for a rest at a point in the Mouths of Entwash. Talk to him and see if you can be of any help.Talk to MalharFind fresh water near Malhar, at a point in the Mouths of Entwash, and fill his empty water-skin.Bring the filled water-skin to Malhar, where he waits at a point in the Mouths of Entwash.Accompany Malhar on the path out of the Mouths of Entwash.Go with MalharMalhar has stopped for a rest at a point in the Mouths of Entwash. Talk to him and see if you can be of any help.Talk to MalharMalhar has asked you to scout ahead, south and west of the point where he has stopped in the Mouths of Entwash.Scout ahead to the southScout ahead to the westScout ahead to the south-westReturn to Malhar where he waits at a point in the Mouths of Entwash and describe what you saw scouting ahead.Return to MalharAccompany Malhar on the path out of the Mouths of Entwash.Go with MalharMalhar has stopped on his path in the Mouths of Entwash. Talk to him to hear what he has to say.Talk to MalharInvestigate the noises Malhar thinks he heard, just south of the point he stopped in the Mouths of Entwash.Investigate the soundsThe noises Malhar heard ahead of him in the Mouths of Entwash turned out to be a Variag ambush!Return to Malhar where he waits at a point in the Mouths of Entwash and let him know that the path ahead is clear.Return to MalharAccompany Malhar on the path out of the Mouths of Entwash.Go with MalharMalhar stopped again, just as the Beacon Hills came into full view. Talk to him to hear what he has to say.Talk to MalharYou have led Malhar safely out of the Mouths of Entwash. Go with him to Glánhar in the centre of Ost Rimmon.Return to Glánhar'There. I think I am ready to begin hobbling my way home. And see, ${PLAYER}, I need not live to old age. I am already acquainted with aches and infirmity!

'Luckily, I know these wetlands well and can find the path with ease. That is why we agreed to meet here, my brothers and I. When I need to rest we will stop. How is that?''Water,' Malhar croaks. 'I am parched. It is sapping my strength.'

He proffers you an empty water-skin.

'Fresh water should... uh!... not be far off... to the west.''Water,' rasps Malhar. 'Please.''Now I must gather my wits and decide which way to go. We have come far enough that there may be enemies near. I must also think on what paths will let us make our way without being seen.'

Malhar furrows his brow and thinks for a moment.

'No, I cannot decide this just by brooding here. I must ask you scout ahead and bring word of what you see. This area stretches south and west of here. Canvas its breadth and return to me.''Scout the area south and west of here. Then return to me and tell of what you have seen.'You relay all that you encountered.

'I see, then. No path is safe, I think, but I judge our best course is to head due south. Yes, I think so.''I would swear that I heard something. Please look ahead and see if I'm correct. Use caution!''So I was right! I am glad you could handle them, friend. We are nearly out. Let us keep up our pace.''There it is, ${PLAYER}. My home soil. I cannot say how this view warms my heart. We have done it. We are out of the marsh and the going will be easy. Let us meet back at Ost Rimmon.''I have regained much strength and can find my way. Meet me in Ost Rimmon, so my mother can give you a full and proper thanks.''There you are, friend. See the family you have brought back together!''I could not say if Mother wished to slap my face or embrace me. I dare say she could manage to do both at once!''You found Malhar and you brought him home. No words of thanks could do justice to my gratitude for that.''I am but half-forgiven, it seems. But with her sons returned, she has other ears to cuff than mine in her daily rounds. And that, I well believe, is the best I could hope for!''I have much to thank you for, ${PLAYER}, although I have much to grieve for, it appears. It is a sorrowful day, indeed!

'I had understood, as all Gondor marches to war, that men of my blood might be taken from me. I never imagined, though, that cruel fate would take them so near to my door or that your kind hands would free some of them from its grip.

'But enough of such goings-on! As I began - you have my earnest thanks. You shall ever be known as friend here in the Beacon Hills, so long as I am keeper of it.''Good? Then off we go. See if you can keep up, ${CLASS}! Ha ha!'Malhar takes the water-skin in both hands and drinks greedily.

'There, that is better. How is it, ${PLAYER}, of all the things I asked you to fetch for me before setting off, water was not one?

'Well, we have set that right. Now let us be on our way.''Did you hear that? I would swear that I heard something. Humour me and check ahead. Be wary!'The Rohirrim have passed through the gap in the Rammas, and prepare to ride for the besieged city.Éomer is ready to join the line of horsemen, and he wants you to follow him.Mount your steed and talk to ÉomerThe Rohirrim have formed a line just south of the Rammas, preparing to ride for the city.Join Éomer in the lineThéoden, King of Rohan, prepares to address his people.Listen to Théoden address the RohirrimRide for battle!Ride for battle!'The men have passed ahead, through the gap in the wall. Let us join the line, and prepare for the glorious battle to come!'Cave-claws have made a home in the Rimmon Quarry after mining was recently halted.Rimmon Burrowers can be found in the Rimmon Quarry.

You should defeat Rimmon Burrowers.Defeat Rimmon Burrowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many cave-claws in the Beacon Hills.CompletedPesky cave-claws have taken up home in the Rimmon Quarry. Their burrowing is loosening the rock beneath mining platforms and should be dealt with.You have defeated many cave-claws in the Beacon Hills.If the Riders Three hope to rejoin their allies on the Pelennor Fields, they shall need to pass swiftly through the heart of the Enemy's forces in Taur Drúadan.Hutha can be found within the War-stead of the King's Men.

Burnoth has asked you to speak with Hutha about his plan for crossing through the Enemy's blockade beneath Amon Dîn.Talk to Hutha in Taur DrúadanAmon Dîn can be found far to the south-east of the War-stead of the King's Men.

Hutha has asked you to travel to the summit of Amon Dîn and light the extinguished beacon.Travel to the summit of Amon DînA suitable torch can be found on the beacon at Amon Dîn.

You have reached the summit of Amon Dîn and should now light the beacon to distract the Enemy's forces below.Light the beacon of Amon DînThe Riders Three can be found at the Field of Onslaught.

You have cleared a passage to the Pelennor Fields for the Riders Three and should now journey to their side.

<rgb=#FF0000>Note: You must complete Book 5, Chapter 8 of Volume IV to advance this objective.</rgb>Meet the Riders Three at the Field of OnslaughtBurnoth can be found somewhere on the Pelennor Fields.

You have arrived at the Pelennor Fields and should now seek out the Riders Three.Talk to Burnoth at the Field of Onslaught'There is little time to speak, ${PLAYER}. Ulf, Hutha, and I shall soon set out into Taur Drúadan and ride southward towards the Pelennor Fields.

'Hutha has mapped a swift, but deadly route to the fields, but our ride cannot succeed without your assistance.

'Aid Hutha, and then meet us on the battlefield before Minas Tirith. It is there that we shall finally unmask our foe, for better or worse.

'I regret that our paths must part for a time, but I shall count on having you at my side on the Fields!''The time has come for us to depart the War-stead, ${PLAYER}. Many of our kinsmen have already ridden south through the valley, and we must reach them by the time we arrive at the Pelennor Fields.

'Our route is dangerous, but we are without choice. We shall ride through their blockades and through the heart of their forces in Taur Drúadan!

'But, if we are to succeed in such a bold manoeuvre, we shall have need of your skill.

'Scale the beacon-hill Amon Dîn and light the beacon anew! The blaze will distract our foes and teach them well that Rohan does not surrender!''It is worse than I feared... the Enemy's forces have overrun the field! Orcs, evil Men, trolls... they number in the thousands to be certain!

'Yet... despair not, my friend. The odds may be against us, but not so long ago at all, we faced down our foes side by side in Rohan! 

'We shall stand together once more and find victory!'Defeat a number of the Variags who have made camp at Bâr Nadhron in the Beacon Hills.You should defeat Variags in Bâr Nadhron in the Beacon Hills of Far Anórien.Defeat Variags in Bâr Nadhron (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many Variags in Bâr Nadhron.CompletedA large force of Variags have sacked and then made camp at the hamlet of Bâr Nadhron in the Beacon Hills. Reducing their numbers will throw them into disarray and safe-guard nearby Ost Rimmon from assault.You have defeated many Variags in Bâr Nadhron in the Beacon Hills.In their quest to discover the agent of the Enemy embedded within the Rohirrim, the Riders Three decide to take a different route through Taur Drúadan.Ulf is at his camp within the War-stead of the King's Men.

