Aragorn sent you to read his call for more soldiers to heralds who can reach the ears of the men that have now arrived at the city.Aragorn is at his pavilion east of Minas Tirith, after the Battle of Pelennor Fields.Imrahil, Éomer, and Elfhelm have returned to Aragorn's Pavilion with a count of the soldiers they have attained for Aragorn's march to Mordor.Talk to Prince Imrahil at Aragorn's PavilionTalk to Éomer at Aragorn's PavilionTalk to Elfhelm at Aragorn's PavilionMithrandir is at Aragorn's Pavilion east of Minas Tirith, after the Battle of Pelennor Fields.Talk to Mithrandir at Aragorn's Pavilion'So it is decided, for better or ill. The men I sent marching through Lossarnach from Pelargir have drawn nigh the city; so too have the men I commanded to follow the Corsair ships in smaller watercraft up the Anduin. They loiter now, waiting for orders. Let us give them direction, and hope that they make good of their word when they find that direction is "east, toward the shadow."

'Take this call for soldiers, and read it to heralds on the field and throughout the First Circle of the city. The heralds will then bring its message to the waiting soldiers, and float the number of men that will march with us.''The men I sent marching through Lossarnach from Pelargir have drawn nigh the city; so too have the men I commanded to follow the Corsair ships in smaller watercraft up the Anduin. They loiter now, waiting for orders. Let us give them direction, and hope that they make good of their word when they find that direction is "east, toward the shadow."

'Take this call for soldiers, and read it to heralds on the field and throughout the First Circle of the city. The heralds will then bring its message to the waiting soldiers, and float the number of men that will march with us.''How many men were the heralds able to reach with their voices? More than two thousands, you say? That is very good, and matches my hopes. These other commanders have also done work to gather soldiers for the march. Speak to them and see how large a host we have assembled; it need not be enough to wage war upon Mordor, but it must be large enough to threaten battle!''My swan-knights have scoured the nearby lands of Gondor for fighting men. They report that three and a half thousands will march for Mordor with Aragorn. It is as fine a total as any for which you might hope, and proves the courage of Gondor, so fresh from battle and still hungry for more.

'But this must be the greatest jest in all the history of Gondor, ${PLAYER}! We march to assail the Black Gate and the impenetrable land of Mordor, and with fewer men than might have formed just the vanguard of its army in the mighty days.

'It brings to my mind a child threatening a mail-clad knight with a toy bow, or a buzzing fly seeking to sting a man working in the hot sun. The Dark Lord will smile and knock down the child, or squish the fly in his palm, and that will be the end of it. What else is there to do but laugh at the jest, ${PLAYER}?''Much has been asked of my men already, but Rohan will not abandon her friends. Many among the Rohirrim were unhorsed during the battle lately ended, but they are still war-worthy and will not let that stay their weapons! Five hundreds of these will follow behind, and I will lead five hundreds on horse alongside.''Éomer King has given to me the command of the main force of the Rohirrim, some three thousands still a-horse. It is to the West Road we will go, to hold it against the enemy that is still in Anórien.''The Prince of Dol Amroth believes that Aragorn's army will be as a stinging fly to the forces of the Dark Lord? He is not wrong that these seven thousands are a mere pittance to the numbers Sauron houses within the Black Land, but it seems to me that Imrahil has wrongly judged the mind of the Dark Lord.

'Yes, we go to sting the Dark Lord. But he will not squish us, for all our buzzing. No, he will seek to trap the fly and take the sting, all unaware of our deception. On that deception all of our fortunes rest. It is the secret fly that bears the most dangerous sting of all for Sauron, and let him remain blind to it until it be too late!'Mithrandir has given you a surprising directive: one of attention-seeking noise rather than the maintenance of secrecy!Trumpeters can be found on the Pelennor Fields, after the battle.

Mithrandir has asked you to find trumpeters throughout the Pelennor Fields that might be willing to announce the departure of Aragorn's army from Minas Tirith with great noise and tumult.Talk to Celebrom on the Pelennor Fields, after the battleTalk to Limlaer on the Pelennor Fields, after the battleTalk to Ruhír on the Pelennor Fields, after the battleTalk to Culang on the Pelennor Fields, after the battleMithrandir asked you to travel to the western gate of Osgiliath after you gathered four trumpeters.Go to the western entrance of OsgiliathNethadan is at the western entrance to Osgiliath.

You gathered a number of trumpeters to travel with Aragorn's army and announce the coming of King Elessar, and now should speak with some familiar faces at the entrance to Osgiliath.Talk to Nethadan at the west entrance of OsgiliathBandron is at the western entrance to Osgiliath.

You gathered a number of trumpeters to travel with Aragorn's army and announce the coming of King Elessar, and now should speak with some familiar faces at the entrance to Osgiliath.Talk to Bandron at the west entrance of OsgiliathSome enemies are still hiding in Osgiliath, waiting to jump out and attack the craftsmen responsible for repairing the city and the bridges.

Bandron asked you to defeat some of the enemies lurking in Osgiliath in order to protect his interests.Defeat enemies remaining in Osgiliath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Aragorn's army is passing through Osgiliath, and Mithrandir intended to meet you at the eastern gate of the city.Go to the eastern entrance of OsgiliathMithrandir is astride Shadowfax at the east gate of Osgiliath.Talk to Mithrandir at the eastern entrance of Osgiliath'We have moved in secrecy ever since I came to know you, ${PLAYER}. It was a secrecy borne of necessity, for we needed every advantage we might attain in our struggle against the Dark Lord. Whispered councils gave rise to hidden errands and close-guarded conspiracies.

'But no longer! While Frodo continues his quest in the shadows, Aragorn strides into the light and he must draw Sauron's gaze to him, and away from the real purpose, with every means at his disposal. Let the departure of Aragorn's army be a loud one and snare the attention of the Dark Lord!

'Wars are fought with weapons, but this battle for Sauron's Eye will be fought with noise and tumult, with musical instruments and those who wield them. Walk the Pelennor and find men who wield trumpets of silver and brass. They will announce the coming of the King as loudly and as often as they can, and perhaps this loud departure will serve as a shield for Frodo and Sam as they walk in the Black Land.

'After you gather four trumpeters, travel to the western gate of Osgiliath and Aragorn's army will begin its march. I will see you at the eastern gate of the city after the army is on the march.''Wars are fought with weapons, but this battle for Sauron's Eye will be fought with noise and tumult, with musical instruments and those who wield them. Walk the Pelennor and find men who wield trumpets of silver and brass. They will announce the coming of the King as loudly and as often as they can, and perhaps this loud departure will serve as a shield for Frodo and Sam as they walk in the Black Land.''I fought in the battle and saw more than my share of death as the afternoon wore on toward evening. Sometime during the waning hours I lost both my sword and the trumpet I carried on my hip, and suffered a knock upon the head by a heavy club.

'When I came to again, my sword was gone. It must have been taken by a foe or a friend who thought I was slain. My trumpet lay by my feet, battered and bruised even as I was, but still whole. I have taken that as a sign, and now I will be a trumpeter and not a swordsman.

'I will be honoured to wind my horn to announce the coming of King Elessar, ${PLAYER}. Let the sound strike fear into the hearts of our enemies and give them such a fright they will never again threaten Gondor!''I was inside the city when our friends from Rohan joined the battle, and my heart soared to see their charge. So inspired was I that I mounted a horse and followed the Prince of Dol Amroth and his knights through the gate and into the throng of the battle!

'I was near at hand to Théoden, King of Rohan, when the great beast descended and spelled his doom. I saw then the most beautiful woman I have ever seen, or ever will see; she stood between her fallen lord and the dreadful foe, and slew that terrible creature in defence of her king! A great evil took me then and I swooned. I think I dreamed, but I cannot remember the details of it.

'When I awoke, I was in the Houses of Healing. Friends tell me I was saved by the hands of a stranger, of curious visage and few words. I would find this man and give him my thanks, but he has left no sign. There are whispers that King Elessar may have worked his healing arts in the Houses, but who would believe such a thing? No King would trouble himself with my hurts, surely?

'In any case, I will be pleased to sound my horn on behalf of the King!''I have mastered many musical instruments, but I am most skilled, and loudest, with the trumpet! It will be my pleasure to sound mine at volume on behalf of the King! O, to think I have lived to see a king once more in Gondor!

'My heart leaps at the thought of it! You will have no problem getting me to start; getting me to stop will be another thing entirely!''You seek trumpeters to proclaim the presence of Aragorn, now that he has revealed his true claim? I am not as practiced as some others in Gondor, for I have only lately begun to learn the skill, but I would be honoured to use my meagre ability to announce the arrival of my chief and lord.

'My friend Areneth bore this trumpet on his person when he was slain in Tûr Morva. I picked it up to save it from the hands of treacherous men, and thought I might teach myself its use as a means of remembering poor Areneth.

'I will use it to proclaim the coming of King Elessar, and it will remind me of the friends that gave their lives so this day might happen.''After you gather four trumpeters, travel to the western gate of Osgiliath and Aragorn's army will begin its march.''After you gather four trumpeters, travel to the western gate of Osgiliath and Aragorn's army will begin its march.''Oh... hello, ${PLAYER}. I did not think I would see you again this soon. I had rather hoped my father would let us remain awhile longer in Imloth Melui, but when he heard that the battle was over he made us depart at once. The flowers of Imloth Melui are breath-taking and beautiful... and the roses are quite a sight as well!

'My father promised Lord Angbor that we would bring our stock of Cemenduril liquor to the Houses of Healing as soon as we were able, and we have done so, also satisfying our writ with Galengam. But now my father has that look in his eye once again, and he has made us come to broken Osgiliath over the Anduin, instead of heading for home.

'Perhaps you could suggest that he and I return home? Or maybe, if that is too far, we could turn our waggon in the direction of Imloth Melui and make sure the ladies of that place fare well in our absence?''Perhaps you could suggest to my father that he and I return home? Or maybe, if that is too far, we could turn our waggon in the direction of Imloth Melui and make sure the ladies of that place fare well in our absence?''Ah, ${PLAYER}! Any good friend of Lord Angbor would be a friend of mine, but you were my friend first, I daresay! I have not forgotten our encounter in Tarlang's Neck. We met at a low ebb in my fortunes, any man would admit that, but I am proud to say that those fortunes have changed for the better. And all because of my son! He has always made me proud, that one, and I defy any ${RACE} to doubt it!

'We did need to leave Imloth Melui in rather a hurry, but that is no fault of my Nethadan. I did not like many of the glances the fair lasses of that place gave to my boy, and I deem that should the battle have kept us there any longer I might have lost my apprentice and gained a daughter-of-the-law, and that does not bring in the coins! No, we left at the first opportunity, and now another opportunity has presented itself. Do you know what that opportunity is?'

Bandron pauses for dramatic emphasis, waiting for you to guess. At last he continues with a shout of triumph.

'Nails, ${PLAYER}! It just so happens that with tremendous foresight, my clever and enterprising son Nethadan has caused us to come into possession of two barrels full of nails, as I am sure you remember, and do you know what is in great demand following a battle? Nails of the very sort we possess in such quantities, and which we can sell to the craftsmen repairing the city and the bridges at a better than usual rate! Just doing my part as a man of Gondor, my friend, and if it allows me to set up my own shop in competition with Galengam, well, that is the merest coincidence.

'There are still enemies hiding in Osgiliath, however. If you were to defeat some of these foes, I would appreciate it!''There are still enemies hiding in Osgiliath. If you were to defeat some of these foes, I would appreciate it!''Thank you for your help, my good friend! I will need to think of a name for my shop, if I am no longer to work for Galengam. I like the sound of "Bandron and Son, Purveyors of Fine Goods." What do you think?''You have caused an impressive amount of noise, ${PLAYER}! The trumpeters will announce the arrival of King Elessar as his army marches through Ithilien. None will miss these tidings!

'Sauron will not fail to heed this march, ${PLAYER}. The Prince of Dol Amroth has rightly pointed out that the size of this army is too small to pose a serious challenge to the host that lies within Mordor, but that in itself will draw his gaze hither. He will think that Aragorn possesses the Ring, and believes that it will bring him the power he needs to overthrow Sauron. He will think this because that is the action he would take, were their positions reversed.

'The army will march slowly, but it will march loudly, and that is the best gift we can give to Frodo right now. I hope beyond hope that he is able to make use of that gift, and that it does not come too late for him.'The land of Ithilien was green and beautiful before the armies of Sauron came. Does it retain any of the loveliness it once possessed?Peregrin Took is east of Osgiliath, before the road rises into the hills of Ithilien.

Gandalf told you that Pippin is riding with the Host of the West, and might want to speak to you.Talk to Peregrin Took east of OsgiliathThe Cross-roads of the King are east of Osgiliath, in Ithilien.

Aragorn and the Host of the West are marching into Ithilien, and plan to stop at the Cross-roads.Go to the cross-roads in North IthilienAragorn is at the Cross-roads of the King, in Ithilien.Talk to Aragorn at the cross-roadsTrumpeters can be found at each of the roads that leave the Cross-roads of the King. After they have been told to sound their instruments, Aragorn asked you to defeat foes in North Ithilien and make your way to the Ranger hideout at Henneth Annûn.Defeat foes throughout North Ithilien (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tell Celebrom to announce the coming of the kingTell Limlaer to announce the coming of the kingTell Ruhír to announce the coming of the kingTell Culang to announce the coming of the kingHenneth Annûn is located north of the Cross-roads, in North Ithilien.

Aragorn asked you to travel ahead of the host and come to the Ranger hideout at Henneth Annûn.Find the Ranger encampment at Henneth Annûn, in North IthilienHenneth Annûn is located far north of the Cross-roads, in North Ithilien.

Aragorn asked you to move ahead of the host and come to the Ranger camp at Henneth Annûn.Talk to Golasdín at Henneth Annûn'Representatives from every land stand with the Host of the West: Elves, dwarves, Men, and hobbits. Young Master Brandybuck recovers still from his wounds, secure in the Houses of Healing, so it falls to Peregrin Took to ride with Aragorn as a representative of the Shirefolk.

'He rides just ahead, to the east, if you wish to speak with him on the way to the Cross-roads of the King.''The march to the Black Gate will bring Aragorn's host through Ithilien and Dagorlad. Travel first to the Cross-roads of the King, east of Osgiliath.''The armies of the Enemy have done what they could to despoil the beauty of this land, but Ithilien resists their depravations. It was once called "the garden of Gondor," and so may it be again!

'I have set trumpeters at each of the roads that leave this meeting of the ways. As Gandalf recommended, we will sound our arrival and our intentions! Speak to each of the trumpeters and they will herald our coming!

'Once this is done, you should move ahead of the host and defeat any enemies you find lurking still in the wild. Men of Gondor have told me that a Ranger encampment is not far to the north, at a place called Henneth Annûn. We will meet there.''I have set trumpeters at each of the roads that leave this meeting of the ways. As Gandalf recommended, we will sound our arrival and our intentions! Speak to each of the trumpeters and they will herald our coming!

'Once this is done, you should move ahead of the host and defeat any enemies you find lurking still in the wild. Men of Gondor have told me that a Ranger encampment is not far to the north, at a place called Henneth Annûn. We will meet there.''Men of Gondor have told me that a Ranger encampment is not far to the north, at a place called Henneth Annûn. We will meet there.''If you are a scout for King Elessar's host, you have my welcome. It is my dearest hope that he intends to make such a racket as his army moves through Ithilien, for there are no foes that could fail to notice his progress hither.''Peregrin Took rides just ahead, to the east, if you wish to speak with him on the way to the Cross-roads of the King.''Hullo, ${PLAYER}. What have I gotten myself into now, I wonder? Aragorn says that it is good for every land to be represented when we go to confront the Enemy in Mordor, but I don't feel like a fit representative for the Shire.

'I wish Merry were here, but he is still recovering in the city. He has done his share of great deeds, but what have I done? Gandalf says I might have saved Faramir's life, and I am glad that whatever small part I played might have helped, but I certainly didn't feel like much of a hero at the time!'The Rangers of Ithilien scout the wood-lands for servants of the Enemy, striking from their hidden camp at Henneth Annûn. In this way, they can help clear the way for the Host of the West as it makes its way toward Mordor.Golasdín welcomed you to explore the hidden path, the cave hideout, and the look-out at Henneth Annûn. He warned you not to enter the Forbidden Pool.Explore the descending tunnel to Henneth AnnûnExplore the main cavern of Henneth AnnûnExplore the look-out point at the highest point of Henneth Annûn<rgb=#FF0000>Do not enter the Forbidden Pool</rgb>Aragorn is outside Henneth Annûn, and wishes to speak to you.Talk to Aragorn outside Henneth AnnûnThe Rangers of Ithilien have a useful scouting location to the north-east of Henneth Annûn.

Aragorn asked you to find Orndil and to scout ahead of the host for dangers posed by lurking enemies.Orndil is north-east of Henneth Annûn, and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Orndil at the look-out spotOrndil thought he saw something to the north-east.

You should investigate further.Aragorn is outside Henneth Annûn, and wishes to speak to you.

You have learned that Orcs and Easterlings aim to ambush the Host of the West as it travels through Cirith Negen, and should report their plan to Aragorn.Talk to Aragorn at Henneth Annûn'The ways of secrecy are ending, it seems. King Elessar marches to Mordor in a great procession and with the sounding of trumpets, and so too must the Rangers of Ithilien give up some of their secrets. Still, those ways do not go easily!

'From Henneth Annûn we have harried many a foe, passing like shadows through the Window of the Sunset and back again under cover of darkness. As you are a friend to the King and to Gondor, I say that you may see what you will of our secret retreat. Find the descending tunnel, the main cavern, and the look-out point.

'But I warn you! Do not set foot into the Forbidden Pool. That is an insult to us and we have never allowed it unpunished.''From Henneth Annûn we have harried many a foe, passing like shadows through the Window of the Sunset and back again under cover of darkness. As you are a friend to the King and to Gondor, I say that you may see what you will of our secret retreat. Find the descending tunnel, the main cavern, and the look-out point.

'But I warn you! Do not set foot into the Forbidden Pool. That is an insult to us and we have never allowed it unpunished.''I have spoken with King Elessar and given him some advice. He is different than I expected, but no less wise!''Our journey through Ithilien has reassured me that it remains beautiful, but this sword cuts with both edges, for enemies can still lurk unseen among the pastoral beauty of this land. As the Rangers of Ithilien surprised their foes, so too can those foes seek to surprise us.

'Travel further ahead to the north-east, and look for signs of trouble. Golasdín tells me that there is a location to the north-east that might offer a good vantage point of the road ahead. Orndil, a Ranger of Ithilien, went to that location and might have seen something.''Do you see that? Look to the distance and see!''Travel further ahead to the north-east, and look for signs of trouble. Golasdín tells me that there is a location to the north-east that might offer a good vantage point of the road ahead. Orndil, a Ranger of Ithilien, went to that location and might have seen something.''Did you see that? I believe I saw movement over there, among the trees to the north-east. Let us make our way there and see if our eyes play tricks upon us, or if there is some foe lying in wait!

'That is the pass of Cirith Negen, and my people have long used it to surprise our foes. They may seek to turn the tables upon us, and use our own ambush spot against us!''That is what I suspected! Our enemies seek to ambush King Elessar as he marches north, and they plan to use our own ambush location for that purpose! What an insult to Gondor that would be!''Our enemies seek to ambush King Elessar as he marches north, and they plan to use our own ambush location for that purpose! What an insult to Gondor that would be!''Thank you for the report, ${PLAYER}. I wondered if our foes would seek to mount a final resistance to our march, and it seems as if they will attempt it at Cirith Negen.

'The Rangers of Ithilien have spoken to me of that pass. They have often used it to surprise unwary foes, and it was there they recently ambushed a marching force accompanied by a fearsome Mûmak and drove the beast from the bonds of its handlers. The creature stampeded from Cirith Negen and away from the war, and now there is no whisper of it.

'Will the Orcs seek to do the same to the Host of the West? They cannot be allowed to succeed at this, ${PLAYER}.'The Rangers of Ithilien have learned that Orcs and Easterlings seek to surprise the Host of the West on its march, and Aragorn has charged you with defeating the ambush before it can be sprung.Hethradir is at the look-out point above the Forbidden Pool, near Henneth Annûn.

Aragorn asked you to speak with Hethradir so he might select Rangers of Ithilien suitable for the task of ambushing the force that seeks to waylay the Host of the West at Cirith Negen.Talk to Hethradir by the look-out point over the Forbidden PoolDagoras is in the hidden cave at Henneth Annûn.

Aragorn asked you to speak with Dagoras so he might select Rangers of the Grey Company suitable for the task of ambushing the force that seeks to waylay the Host of the West at Cirith Negen.Talk to Dagoras in the hidden cave at Henneth AnnûnLegolas is in the hidden cave at Henneth Annûn.Talk to Legolas in the hidden cave at Henneth AnnûnLegolas is in the hidden cave at Henneth Annûn, ready to leave for Cirith Negen.Talk to Legolas and travel to Cirith NegenDagoras is in the hidden cave at Henneth Annûn.

You have helped to rout the intended ambush at Cirith Negen, and should now speak with Dagoras.Talk to Dagoras in the hidden cave at Henneth AnnûnAragorn is outside Henneth Annûn.

Dagoras wanted you to raise his concerns with Aragorn concerning his experience in Cirith Negen.Talk to Aragorn outside Henneth Annûn'If we are to ambush the ambushers, we will need a small force of skilled hunters and trackers who can move stealthily through the woods unseen. Hethradir, by the look-out point above the Forbidden Pool, will know which of the Rangers of Ithilien will be best suited for this purpose.

'Some of my kin will want to take part in this adventure as well. Speak to Dagoras in the cave of Henneth Annûn, and he will assemble these others.''If we are to ambush the ambushers, we will need a small force of skilled hunters and trackers who can move stealthily through the woods unseen. Hethradir, by the look-out point over the Forbidden Pool, will know which of the Rangers of Ithilien will be best suited for this purpose.

'Some of my kin will want to take part in this adventure as well. Speak to Dagoras in the cave of Henneth Annûn, and he will assemble these others.''I have heard that a force of Orcs and Easterlings seeks to waylay King Elessar's army as he proceeds northward through the pass of Cirith Negen. These monsters and their foul allies are monstrous fools if they think to use our own tricks against us!

'This is the Land of the Moon, and many are the moons that have risen and set upon my life. Still I am here, Faramir's most loyal friend, and I will aid him until I breathe my very last. Younger men you will find, quicker perhaps, but none will know this land as I do.

'It is neither my lot nor my desire to lead in anything but this: I will lead our men into the woods and against our foes, and back again. I will choose Rangers of our band that are up to the task.''I will choose Rangers of our band that are up to the task.''I am no stranger to errands of secrecy, ${PLAYER}, and I know that neither are you! There was once a time when I would say we could have handled this ambush by ourselves, but there is wisdom in being prepared. I will find others of my people who wish to help, and we will have greater chance of success if they are beside us, on secret feet.

'Battle on the open plain is very different from forest-fighting. I will not say it will be a relief to engage in the latter once again, but every man of the company has seen enough of the former by now to last him until the end of his days.''I will find others of my people who wish to help, and we will have greater chance of success if they are beside us, on secret feet.

'Battle on the open plain is very different from forest-fighting. I will not say it will be a relief to engage in the latter once again, but every man of the company has seen enough of the former by now to last him until the end of his days.''I hope you were planning to ask me to join on this endeavour, ${PLAYER}! I do not want to be left out of such an adventure. Perhaps Aragorn asked for me by name, and you thought he asked for Dagoras instead?'

Legolas laughs, a musical sound.

'I will gladly accompany you to ambush the ambushers, my friend. Tell me when you are ready and we can leave.''Let us make our plans with the Rangers of Ithilien and with Aragorn's kin, and then we will depart for Cirith Negen to surprise our foes.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Thanks to our efforts and those of our friends, the Host of the West will now be able to proceed safely through Cirith Negen. And yet, my mind turns over and over upon an idea that will not leave me in peace.

'We foiled the ambush and dispersed our foes, true, but does it seem to have been too easy? Certainly our friends in Ithilien have ambushed many foes from that spot with success, but this effort on the part of the Orcs and the Easterlings does not seem to have had any real chance of victory. Their force was too disorganized to cause us any trouble. Indeed, it seems to me that even had we stumbled unknowingly into the trap, we still might have survived to rout our foes.

'Will you mention my concerns to Aragorn? There is something peculiar in all this.''We foiled the ambush and dispersed our foes, true, but does it seem to have been too easy? Certainly our friends in Ithilien have ambushed many foes from that spot with success, but this effort on the part of the Orcs and the Easterlings does not seem to have had any real chance of victory. Their force was too disorganized to cause us any trouble. Indeed, it seems to me that even had we stumbled unknowingly into the trap, we still might have survived to rout our foes.

'Will you mention my concerns to Aragorn? There is something peculiar in all this.''Dagoras is troubled by the ease with which the ambush was upset? I think there is an answer for that, though it may not soothe his worries.

'I deem that the chief purpose of that ambush was to encourage us to keep on our course, to draw us on with a false estimate of the Enemy's weakness. If it did cause us harm, so much the better, but I think it unlikely that it was intended to do anything other than to feint at our strength and bring us on with confidence.'With the ambush of the Orcs and the Easterlings now foiled, Aragorn sets his sights upon the road ahead.Bâr Tarnaher is north-west of Henneth Annûn.

The Rangers of Bâr Tarnaher have launched many attacks upon the Orcs from that ruin, and may know of any dangers to the Host of the West on the road ahead.Travel to Bâr Tarnaher, north-west of Henneth AnnûnLegolas is at the ruin of Bâr Tarnaher, in North Ithilien.

You have come to the ruin of Bâr Tarnaher, but Legolas has found something amiss.Talk to Legolas at Bâr TarnaherBregaras is at Bâr Tarnaher, north-west of Henneth Annûn in North Ithilien.Talk to Bregaras at Bâr TarnaherRangers of Ithilien hid supply caches east and north of Bâr Tarnaher.

Bregaras asked you to retrieve Ranger supply caches and bring them back to Bâr Tarnaher.Retrieve Ranger supply caches east and north of Bâr Tarnaher (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bregaras asked you to leave the supplies you collected at Bâr Tarnaher.Gimli is at Bâr Tarnaher, in North Ithilien.Talk to Gimli at Bâr TarnaherUnréth and Maegur are at Bâr Tarnaher.

Gimli noticed that the soldiers that came with you to Bâr Tarnaher have become increasingly troubled.Talk to Maegur at Bâr TarnaherTalk to Unréth at Bâr TarnaherAragorn is at Henneth Annûn, in North Ithilien.Talk to Aragorn at Henneth AnnûnSoldiers of Aragorn's host can be found at Henneth Annûn.

Aragorn asked you to try and bolster the courage of his men.Reassure worried soldiers at Henneth Annûn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Aragorn is at Henneth Annûn, in North Ithilien.Talk to Aragorn at Henneth AnnûnAragorn is at his camp on the border between Dagorlad and North Ithilien.Talk to Aragorn at his camp on the border of Dagorlad'Do not let my assessment challenge your resolve, my friend. That the Enemy believes he has us at a disadvantage means that he prepares to seize the bait and fall into the trap we have set for him. We must not give him any sign that his real danger lies elsewhere, or we will endanger more than our own lives. All of Middle-earth will be at risk.

'And so we continue. There is a tower to the north-west of Henneth Annûn, a ruin called Bâr Tarnaher. From there the Rangers of Ithilien have launched many strikes upon the Orcs. Go with Legolas and Gimli to Bâr Tarnaher and learn what the Rangers at that place know of the road ahead.''From Bâr Tarnaher the Rangers of Ithilien have launched many strikes upon the Orcs. Go with Legolas and Gimli to Bâr Tarnaher, north-west of here, and learn what the Rangers there know of the road ahead.''From Bâr Tarnaher, the Rangers of Ithilien have launched many strikes upon the Orcs. Go with Legolas and Gimli to Bâr Tarnaher, north-west of here, and learn what the Rangers there know of the road ahead.''I thought I heard something high above us, ${PLAYER}, and I felt a shadow pass across my heart. I know this feeling. It was on the banks of the Anduin that I felled one of the flying beasts. It must have proved only an inconvenience for its terrible rider, for I know their number and all were seen on the Pelennor.

'The Nazgûl are flying above us, just out of sight. They watch the progress of Aragorn's host as it moves north toward Dagorlad. Gimli and I, and you, know of these creatures, but we know also that they can be fought. Not all of Aragorn's soldiers may believe this, and I fear that such feelings of dread as the Nazgûl inspire will grow in the breast of each man. Will their loyalty hold, or will the fear of Sauron's servants cause them to break ranks and flee as cowards?

'My father does not think highly of Man, but I have seen the courage they can wield. Will it be enough?''I hope the Nazgûl do not cause us any further trouble as we move closer to Mordor, but I fear for the resolve of Aragorn's men.''There's something up there, watching us even now? I don't like the sound of that.'

Bregaras shudders and scans the sky for some sign of the Nazgûl, but can see nothing. He sighs heavily and shakes his head.

'At Faramir's instruction, his Rangers placed hidden caches of supplies throughout Ithilien. It seems to me that we should retrieve these supplies, for the time of the Rangers is surely coming to an end. If King Elessar finds victory or defeat at the Black Gate, there will be little need for such caches in the woodlands.

'Find the hidden supply caches east and north of Bâr Tarnaher and bring them back here, and we can distribute their contents among King Elessar's soldiers.''At Faramir's instruction, his Rangers placed hidden caches of supplies throughout Ithilien. It seems to me that we should retrieve these supplies, for the time of the Rangers is surely coming to an end. If King Elessar finds victory or defeat at the Black Gate, there will be little need for such caches in the woodlands.

'Find the hidden supply caches east and north of Bâr Tarnaher and bring them back here, and we can distribute their contents among King Elessar's soldiers.''Leave the supplies you retrieved right there and we can redistribute them to King Elessar's soldiers.''The soldiers that came with us, Unréth of Rohan and Maegur of Gondor, well, they have become increasingly more and more troubled as the day wore on. I have given some thought to how I might ease their minds, but my skill is with axes, not with speech-craft!

'Perhaps you can do something to ease their worries, ${PLAYER}?''The soldiers that came with us, Unréth of Rohan and Maegur of Gondor, well, they have become increasingly more and more troubled as the day wore on. I have given some thought to how I might ease their minds, but my skill is with axes, not with speech-craft!

'Perhaps you can do something to ease their worries, ${PLAYER}?''I am no coward, ${PLAYER}, but I have to ask if there is any wisdom in this march to Mordor! I would serve King Elessar in all things and do whatever he commands, but I fear that he does not know what it is he asks of us!

'I thought his host was of a goodly size when we marched through Osgiliath, but now that we have crossed into the wild I see that it is smaller than I thought at first. It can be no match for the armies of Mordor!

'I will die for Gondor, if I must for Gondor to survive, but I do not want to lay down my life needlessly! If this march is folly, as I deem it to be, how can it be the King's command? How will six thousand fallen soldiers at the Black Gate save Gondor? It cannot! It cannot!''${PLAYER}, I am a man of Rohan and will do as Éomer King commands, but my resolve is tested with every step towards the Black Land of Mordor! Is it true that our commanders do not hope to achieve victory through battle? Why then must we throw our lives away?

'I would do anything for the Mark, and gladly, but would it not be better to take these soldiers and man a strong defence at Minas Tirith? There are strongholds in Rohan at which we could last for years if need be! I fought at the Hornburg, and am well-tested by the battle that was fought there.

'An unnatural dread seizes my heart, but there is more to this than fear! I cannot believe that Éomer King or King Elessar of Gondor would so easily throw their lives away, and ours with them. How can this be their choice, ${PLAYER}?''I think you should return to Aragorn and tell him about the worries of these men, ${PLAYER}. He must not be surprised if they abandon him when he needs them most, and he finds himself standing alone outside the Black Gate of Mordor.

'Well, alone except for you, me, and Gimli. We will not run, of course.''What did you learn at Bâr Tarnaher, my friend?'

You tell Aragorn that the Nazgûl fly high above the clouds and out of sight, but they are keeping pace with the host. You tell him too that their fearful presence, and the nature of the mission itself, is having an effect upon his soldiers, sapping them of their courage.

'I have noticed this as well, ${PLAYER}. I cannot blame these men if their hearts quail at the thought of this painful mission. I will not willingly command them to throw their lives away for the merest of chances, though I know how important that chance may be.

'Find soldiers here at Henneth Annûn and see if you can reassure them.''I cannot blame these men if their hearts quail at the thought of this painful mission. I will not willingly command them to throw their lives away for the merest of chances, though I know how important that chance may be.

'Find soldiers here at Henneth Annûn and see if you can reassure them.''Thank you for your efforts, ${PLAYER}. I have given some thought to the problem of what to do if more men prove faint of heart as we approach Mordor. It will soon be put to the test, I think.

'Let us move forward along the road, north to the border of Dagorlad! We will establish a camp at the woods' edge, and make ready for the final leg of the march to Mordor.

'I will speak with you at our camp on the border of Dagorlad, when we arrive.''Let us move forward along the road, north to the border of Dagorlad! We will establish a camp at the woods' edge, and make ready for the final leg of the march to Mordor.

'I will speak with you at our camp on the border of Dagorlad, when we arrive.''We are close to the Black Land of Mordor now, my friend. May our courage not desert us, and may our friends have the chance they need, though it cost us our lives.'Aragorn has asked you to scout the lands ahead so he will know what awaits the host on its final march to the Black Gate.Faeron and Mincham can be found at Aragorn's camp on the border of Dagorlad.

Aragorn has asked you to bring several of his Rangers on a mission to scout ahead of the host.Talk to Mincham at Aragorn's camp on the Dagorlad borderTalk to Faeron at Aragorn's camp on the Dagorlad borderLanglas is at Aragorn's camp on the Dagorlad border.

Langlas has called out to you. He has something to say before you leave to scout the plains of Dagorlad.Talk to Langlas at Aragorn's camp on the Dagorlad borderAragorn is at his camp on the border of Dagorlad.Talk to Aragorn at his camp on the Dagorlad borderAragorn is at his camp on the border of Dagorlad.Talk to Aragorn and scout the slag-hills of DagorladAragorn is at his camp on the Dagorlad border.'I do not expect us to find victory through battle, but still I want to keep Sauron's Eye focused on us for as long as we can, ${PLAYER}. To that end, it would not do for the host to be attacked before we attain the Black Gate.

'I ask that you scout ahead and learn what threats may lie in wait for us as we approach the Morannon. I suggest that you bring two of my Rangers with you: Faeron for his stealth, and Mincham for his understanding of the Black Speech. Together, the three of you may learn much that one alone could not.

'You should find them to the south and to the east of this hillock. Ask them if they will accompany you on this mission, and bear this command in mind: you are not to get too near to the Black Gate!''I ask that you scout ahead and learn what threats may lie in wait for us as we approach the Morannon. I suggest that you bring two of my Rangers with you: Faeron for his stealth, and Mincham for his understanding of the Black Speech. Together, the three of you may learn much that one alone could not.

'You should find them to the south and to the east of this hillock. Ask them if they will accompany you on this mission, and bear this command in mind: you are not to get too near to the Black Gate!''I will do as Aragorn commands, though it bring us onto the blasted plains of Dagorlad, where long ago a great battle was fought. I am no stranger to such places of evil, as you know.

'We will learn what we can and then return to tell Aragorn what lies before the host. It would be folly to do otherwise.''I will go with you onto the plains of Dagorlad and scout ahead of the host, but be warned! There will not be much cover in that place, and we may be exposed to unfriendly eyes. It will take all of my stealthy arts to keep us safe, but I will do what I can.''Wait a moment, ${PLAYER}, if you would.'

Langlas removes a small packet from within the folds of his cloak and holds it out to you.

'Mincham tells me that you and Faeron will be leaving to scout the slag-hills of Dagorlad in advance of the host? That will be dangerous, for there is little cover on the battle-plain. Take this packet of herbs with you. It was given to me by Mandan before the ships docked at the Harlond, and I have been carrying it around since then as a memento, for Mandan fell upon Pelennor.

'The herbs within are foul-smelling when burned, which was true of most of Mandan's poultices, to speak honestly. If you need to draw the attention of an enemy, it might serve you well. I want you to have it. If Mandan's gift can help you this day, he would want it as well.''Thank you for undertaking this mission, ${PLAYER}. Dagorlad is an inhospitable land, and it will be difficult to remain unseen, but I trust you, Faeron, and Mincham as I would trust no others.

'Speak to me when you are ready to depart.''Travel with care, my friend, and remember! Do not get too close to the Black Gate!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I am glad to see you have returned in safety, ${PLAYER}, and so too have Faeron and Mincham! What can you report of the slag-hills?'

Aragorn's eyes widen in disbelief when you tell him that you seized upon a chance opportunity to enter Narchost, but he nods with grim satisfaction when you report what you heard within.

'So this gambit has proven itself, ${PLAYER}. Sauron believes I have the Ring, and all of his attention is focused upon the Host of the West. He has fallen for the trap. Now everything depends upon Frodo.'

<rgb=#00FF00>To be continued in Volume IV, Book 8, 'The Black Gate'...</rgb>The Host of the West has come to the border between Ithilien and Dagorlad. There is now no other way to go but forward to the Black Gate of Mordor.Daervunn is at the northern edge of the Camp of the Host, where the road leaves the encampment and passes into Dagorlad.

Aragorn has asked you to see if Daervunn has readied the men to march.Find Daervunn at the northern edge of the Camp of the HostDaervunn and Pippin are at the edge of the Camp of the Host, where the northward road leads into Dagorlad.Talk to Daervunn at the northern edge of the Camp of the HostTalk to Pippin Took at the northern edge of the Camp of the HostAragorn is on the rise in the middle of the Camp of the Host.

Daervunn reported that the men are ready to march.Talk to Aragorn at the Camp of the HostMithrandir is at the Camp of the Host on the border of Dagorlad.

Something has worried Mithrandir and he wishes to speak to you about it.Talk to Mithrandir at the Camp of the HostMithrandir is at the Camp of the Host on the border of Dagorlad.

Mithrandir is worried about what might have befallen Frodo and Sam since they parted ways with Faramir in Ithilien.Talk to Mithrandir and experience 'Interlude: No Other Way' or read the shortened account insteadMithrandir is at the Camp of the Host, on the border of Dagorlad.Talk to Mithrandir at the Camp of the HostAragorn is on the rise in the middle of the Camp of the Host.Talk to Aragorn at the Camp of the Host'The only hope of victory lies with Frodo, but we still have a part to play in this great struggle, ${PLAYER}. I asked Daervunn to prepare the men for departure, for I believed it might take longer than expected for the Host of the West to set out upon this last march. Heavy are the feet that dread the approaching road, but still they must step one before the other!

'Look for Daervunn where the northward road departs from the camp, my friend, and ask him if the men are ready to leave.''Look for Daervunn where the northward road departs from the camp and ask him if the men are ready to leave.''Look for Daervunn where the northward road departs from the camp and ask him if the men are ready to leave.''This road will take us across the blasted plains of Dagorlad, to the very gate of Mordor. The men stir with restlessness, but they will obey their liege, whether he be Éomer or Aragorn. Others there are, too, who have joined this host in cause of allied friendship; if they have not turned back now, I believe they will never abandon us.

'I have shared words with Peregrin Took here, and he too has a courage about him.''This is the road to Mordor? No one could mistake it for anything else, ${PLAYER}! It looks as I would have expected it to look, had I ever wanted to fill my heart with fright and my mind with unease at the considering... and I did not!

'Daervunn has had some kind words to say concerning my "courage," and I did not want to offend him by disagreeing, but I think he sees what is not there. If I were truly brave, would I feel so frightened at the prospect of taking this road? Certainly not! But Aragorn believes that we have to do this, and I have smarts enough to know he is more wise than I am. If he says there is no other way, this hobbit will not gainsay him. This is the road we must take!''This road will take us across the blasted plains of Dagorlad, to the very gate of Mordor. I hope you are ready.''If Aragorn says there is no other way, I will not gainsay him. This is the road we must take!''What did Daervunn have to say? Are the men ready to march?'

You report the Ranger's words, and Aragorn nods grimly.

'It pains me that such a course is necessary, but I take satisfaction in knowing the great loyalty and courage of the men with whom I march.''Mithrandir desires to speak with you, my friend.''You were at the council of the captains, ${PLAYER}, and you heard the arguments for and against this march to the Black Gate. You must know of its importance, as I do.

'But still I am troubled. Before battle consumed Minas Tirith, I spoke with Faramir of his watch in Ithilien, and he told me that his men encountered two hobbits in the wild: Frodo and Sam. Briefly he held them, but he did not hinder their errand overlong and soon released them to their purpose.

'But a third there was, and he served our friends as their guide: Gollum. Faramir spoke against the creature, but Frodo believed he could be trusted, and intended to follow him through a secret pass into Mordor. The pass is called Cirith Ungol. It is an evil place. I know enough of that pass to know that danger lurks there in the darkness, and I fear that Frodo may trust his guide too readily. Alas that our roads ran apart!

'A fortnight has passed since Frodo and Sam departed from Faramir's care. My wondering mind worries at how little we know of their fates, ${PLAYER}, and conjures terrible scenarios of what could have befallen. What has happened to our friends? What has happened to the Ring?''Too much time has passed without word of our friends. Did their guide lead them into some danger, as I fear? We cannot know for certain, ${PLAYER}, but neither can we dispel our worries.'The worried thoughts of the Host of the West turn towards Frodo and Sam, who have passed beyond even Gandalf's sight. He knows only that the hobbits make for the dangerous pass of Cirith Ungol, their guide the creature Gollum, spared years ago by the mercy of Bilbo. Their guide possesses knowledge of this hidden way into Mordor, but another secret he keeps to himself: the identity of the terrible guardian that lurks within the tunnel through which they must pass...'In my heart I guessed that Frodo and Gollum would meet before the end, but I hope that Frodo does not trust blindly. If he remains alert to the possibility of deceit, perhaps he can prevent it before it leads our friends to harm.

'Or has it already done so? It has been two weeks since they left Henneth Annûn, and still there has been no word. If they fell prey to some treachery of Gollum's, surely we would have heard by now. Cirith Ungol...'

Mithrandir's voice trails away as his eyes search the horizon. After a moment his attention returns, and a smile briefly illuminates his features.

'I should not have given in to these fears, ${PLAYER}. The Ring remains out of Sauron's grasp. Let that keep our hearts afloat and allay some of our worries! For if Frodo were betrayed to Sauron, I feel certain that the Dark Lord would make use of the Ring at once, and we would know the end had arrived. That has not happened. The Ring, and Frodo, have not been seized by the Dark Lord. Every step that Frodo takes toward the Fire kindles our hopes.''The Ring, and Frodo, have not been seized by the Dark Lord. Every step that Frodo takes toward the Fire kindles our hopes.''My thoughts are also with Frodo and Sam, and I fear for their safety in the company of Gollum. But I agree with Mithrandir that they must still be alive, for we would know if Sauron had the Ring.

'But my reasoning is not that of the Wizard. I travelled with Frodo and Sam for almost four months, and we had many adventures and braved many dangers. And even after so many shared hardships, I think Master Gamgee had only just begun to warm up to me, and perhaps did not yet entirely trust this "Strider."

'He will not take his eyes off Gollum, even if Frodo perhaps will. I do not think Gollum will be able to take advantage of our Samwise, even if he thinks he can. They will be fine, inasmuch as any might be whose misfortune it is to journey in the Black Land.'The Host of the West prepares to set forth on the last leg of the march to the Black Gate, and Aragorn wants his friends and kin to know that he is grateful for their trust and their service.Lothrandir is south of Aragorn's position on the rise in the Camp of the Host, on the border of Dagorlad.

Aragorn wanted you to express to some of his kinsmen how grateful he is for the sacrifices they have made, and will make, in his service.Talk to Lothrandir south of the rise in the Camp of the HostLothrandir is south of Aragorn's position on the rise in the Camp of the Host, on the border of Dagorlad.

Aragorn wanted you to express to some of his kinsmen how grateful he is for the sacrifices they have made, and will make, in his service.Talk to Lothrandir south of the rise in the Camp of the HostRadanir is south-east of Lothrandir's position in the Camp of the Host on the border of Dagorlad.

