You should speak with Anborn.Return to Anborn‘One last chore remains in Haerondir before we send you out further into The Wastes. We have supplies here that would be useful to the Camp of the Host, but they need to be gathered before we continue the march. There's armour and weapons to be gathered, and water-skins to be filled. Can I ask you to make a sweep of Haerondir and attend to this? I know it is a bit menial for such a skilled ${CLASS}, but we are woefully short-handed.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. Every bit of help counts, even if it's something simple.

'The Rangers are now prepared to join the Host of the West in their march to the Black Gate. However, there is still plenty to be done in the Wastes before that. Gladhir and Maendal may have leads for you.'If not freed from the clutches of the Enemy, a cruel fate awaits those fallen captive to the Orcs of Mordor in Dagorlad.Tortured captives can be found throughout Ondoher's Folly in north-western Dagorlad.

Mincham has asked you to aid in freeing the Enemy's captives while you assault the hill of Ondoher's Folly.Mincham can be found at Tham Durlan, to the south of Ondoher's Folly in Dagorlad.

You have freed as many of the captives as you were able, and you should now return to Mincham at Tham Durlan.Talk to Mincham at Tham Durlan'The Fane of Screaming... what a vile name for a place of such sorrow for the people of Gondor.

'I have heard tales told by Aragorn of the king of old, Ondoher, and the fate that travelled far to meet him in these lands. I believe that is a tale better told by Artamir - and in less pressing times. Suffice it to say, a king of Númenórean blood laid low by the Enemy's servants is not soon to be forgotten. They have twisted the loss of Gondor into a monument to their own Master.

'It is clear that these Orcs have defiled the hill and I fear many of the Dark Lord's prisoners have met their end on its summit - and without the mercy of a swift death. Even in war-time, and with a great battle looming, I cannot abandon those that still remain to such a fate.

'I could not forgive myself for turning away from their pain, ${PLAYER}, and I believe Aragorn would consider this the proper course as well.

'Travel to Ondoher's Folly, and free all of the prisoners you can find. I shall keep watch for your return, my friend!''Ah! Let us hope we have freed the last of those held in the Wastes, ${PLAYER}! I mourn for those we were too late to save, but I hold hope that the Dark Lord shall soon suffer the fate he deserves for these atrocities.

'I do not doubt the Enemy still keeps many slaves beyond the Black Gate, but we must look to the battle ahead before we can aid them.

'Thank you, my friend.'Never before known to yield in battle, a party of Haradrim have surrendered to the Rangers of Ithilien for reasons that remain unclear.The Haradrim prisoners can be found at Aelin Veren, in North Ithilien.

Maendal has asked you to question the Haradrim prisoners and learn the reason for their surrender.Maendal can be found at Aelin Veren, in North Ithilien.

You have spoken to the prisoners and while not all of them cooperated, you feel that some measure of understanding has been reached for the moment. You should speak to Maendal.Talk to Maendal'I do not know what to make of this, my friend. To what end have the Southrons surrendered? What drove them to such a decision?

'I have separated most of them among my men, and they are kept under guard. I sought to speak with them when first we arrived at Aelin Veren, but they remained silent.  It seems that neither we nor the Southrons know how best to proceed.

'Perhaps they would think differently of you, ${PLAYER}...

'I would ask you to speak to them, question their purpose in Ithilien, and see if we cannot discover what led to their surrender.

'Would you do this for us, my friend?''Through your efforts, we have learned of the Southron's purpose in their surrender. It was a wise decision, regardless of our past encounters with their people, and I am glad their... war-master came to such a conclusion without further bloodshed.

