Artamir has spoken to the spirit of Faramir, son of Ondoher, and believes that what he has seen may be of use to the Host of the West. You should learn more of Faramir's burdens.Artamir can be found on the plains to the south of Ondoher's Folly.

You have learned much of Faramir's tragic past as well as the new foes that have risen against the Host of the West. You should now speak to Artamir and urge him to tell the Rangers of what you both have learned.Talk to Artamir outside Ondoher's Folly'As I said, I have many suspicions about the identity of this spirit... but those shall remain mine alone until I learn more of this spirit's purpose.

'If you and the Rangers would grant me leave, I shall follow where he leads and learn what I can of him and his fate in the Wastes. Think what he may have seen - such experiences are beyond the life of any mortal Man!

'Talk to Mincham and see what he makes of this. I have heard tell he is no stranger to spirits of the Dead. If he grants my request, I would ask that you - and you alone - follow us to the north after a short time.

'I shall speak well of you to him, and perhaps he shall be more willing to speak to you directly. That is my hope, ${PLAYER}, for safety seems so far away now... and I would much like to return to it.''A spirit here... in the ruins?

'I fear this shall not portend any good at all, and I would caution Artamir to put the safety of the Host before his own misguided studies. It may be true that the spirits of the Dead that followed Aragorn were honourable enough when they fulfilled their oaths, but do not forget those we lost when they felt no such duty.

'If one has been cursed by the breaking of an oath, you should not assume them a friend. We of the Grey Company have learned this all too painfully.

'I shall not stop him, but I hope he considers the great risk he would impose on all of our company.''I... I had to follow where he led, ${PLAYER}. Steward Denethor would have commanded it if he still lived, and I cannot abandon his task now.

'I sensed that the Rangers would not wish me to go, so I sent you to hold their attention as I departed. I apologise for the deception, but you must understand the significance of the man we have encountered this day. As I suspected, he is none other than Faramir, son of Ondoher!

'How capricious of fate that I should find a Faramir here amid the wastes when I so recently bid a farewell to Steward Denethor's son in the Houses of Healing...'

Artamir looks around with a sense of wonder, but soon meets your gaze again.

'Faramir, son of Ondoher, met a grim end here in the Wastes, but perhaps more tragic, he was never meant to journey to these lands at all. King Ondoher and Faramir's brother, Artamir, rode to repel the forces of the Wainriders in the Wastes, but they were caught unprepared and vastly outnumbered. They were soon aided by a company of North-men, but even still, their forces were slain on the fields and driven into the Dead Marshes. Amid all this, Faramir was bidden to remain in Minas Anor when Minas Tirith was still called as such, but he defied his father's wishes and secretly rode to join the North-men as they rode southward toward the battle. Thus, Ondoher and his two sons met their ends in the Wastes...

'As with so much of Gondor's history, there is much more to be said on these events, but I would prefer to tell it when we return to Minas Tirith. The air here has left my throat quite dry, indeed. In the meantime, Faramir has told me much of what he has seen in recent days, and I must admit... it gives me great concern for the Host of the West.

'It took some great efforts on my part, but I have convinced Faramir to trust you with his burdens. You should speak to him and learn how we might aid both him and the Host.''A man of true Númenórean blood to have survived so long in Mordor...

'This is unthinkable, ${PLAYER}. If he is responsible for the fates of those at Ondoher's Folly, he is also a danger to the Host of the West. I still am not certain what to make of Faramir's claims about the Wainriders, but I would not dismiss them so carelessly.

'We must send word at once to the Rangers at Haerondir and ready ourselves for what may come.'The Host of the West has left behind friendlier lands as banners of the Enemy fly throughout the wracked lands of Dagorlad.Banners can be found throughout Ondoher's Folly in north-western Dagorlad.

You have discovered many banners of the Eye flying at Ondoher's Folly. You should burn them and loosen the Enemy's grip on the Wastes.Burn banners at Ondoher's Folly (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have burned many of the Enemy's banners at Ondoher's Folly.CompletedAs you enter Ondoher's Folly, you hear the familiar rustling of coarse cloth in the wind. The Orcs of Ondoher's Folly have erected many banners in tribute to the Dark Lord. Standing beneath this grim heraldry, you feel the Eye of the Enemy fixed upon you and suddenly the weight of your cause at once feels more burdensome.

You think it wise to burn these banners you have discovered, so the Orcs might falter in their absence.Although burning the Enemy's banners can do little to avenge the fallen at Ondoher's Folly, it serves to loosen the Dark Lord's grip on the lands beyond Mordor.

By weakening the Enemy's resolve, the Orcs of Mordor may pose less of a threat to the Host of the West when the battle is finally joined.Thurindol asks for you to take weapons from Orcs you defeat in order to lessen the amount of weapons the forces of Mordor have at their disposal.Orcs can be found within the caverns of Skoironk.

You should defeat Orcs and gather their weapons.Gather Orc weapons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thurindol can be found at a small Ranger outpost hidden in the Slag-hills.

You should speak with Thurindol.'If the Orcs beneath the Slag-hills are still able to mobilize against our own forces, we must try to even the field.

'I ask of you to collect their crude weapons. If many of the Orcs have to fight against us with only their fists, then they will be no match to our blades.''Good work. I shall throw these in the nearby waters. The toxic filth of the Slag-hills will make quick work to brittle these crude clubs and blades.'Thurindol believes that there are even more enemies beneath the Slag-hills and asks of you to investigate.Candúr can be found at a small Ranger outpost hidden in the Slag-hills.

You should talk to Candúr before you depart.Talk to CandúrThurindol can be found at a small Ranger outpost hidden in the Slag-hills.

You should talk to Thurindol.Talk to ThurindolCarchost and Skoironk are located in the Slag-hills.

You should find the entrances and enter Carchost and Skoironk.Enter CarchostEnter SkoironkFind the entrance to CarchostFind the entrance to SkoironkYou should return to Thurindol, who can be found at a small Ranger outpost hidden in the Slag-hills.Talk to Thurindol'I tell you, ${PLAYER}, I have wondered whether I were going mad. I would track an Orc Captain climbing the nearby eastern hills, only to lose track of him. Then, as if by some sorcery, I would espy him far west, where I should surely have seen him pass.

'The riddle has evaded me until now. The maggot-holes must communicate in tunnels deep beneath these midden-heaps, and access fundaments beneath the Towers of the Teeth, as well.  It is the only answer!

'Will you investigate? Find a way into Carchost and discover what pathways lie below.

'Ah, but I forget myself. You should speak with Candúr before you go.''${PLAYER}, you honour the name Ranger with your bravery and commitment to Gondor. We shall see you again soon. This deed you undertake will not go unremembered by my order. I will see to it.''You are going beneath Carchost, then? I am glad you are willing to help us in this. It will be dangerous, but for this task I have faith in none so much as you. Fortune smile on you. We will speak again, I am sure of it.''If what you relate is true, why then an entire army hides in wait below the ground! We know full well that overwhelming odds stand locked behind the Morannon. But we shall not face those until the ordained time of battle, according to Lord Aragorn's plan. 

'What if this nether army poured forth to accost us before that time? What last gleam of hope might be lost if my Lord's plan faltered? No, it is clear. We must do something if we may... whatever we may.'Torchún, Warden of the Houses of Healing, has promised to check in on a long list of the wounded but has spent more time sending well-wishers away.The High Hall, House of Remedies, and House of Rest are on the Sixth Circle of Minas Tirith.

Visit the wounded on Torchún's list in the Houses of Healing and see how they are doing. Find Meriadoc the hobbit, Lord Faramir, Lady Éowyn of Rohan, Iorlas, and Echadon in the High Hall. Find Habdir, Bariel, Ormatar, and Thrymm of Rohan in the House of Rest. Find Gísling of Rohan,  Ulf of Rohan, and Húndil the Tavern Keep in the House of Remedies.You should return to Torchún, outside the House of Remedies, with news of everyone you visited.Return to Torchún'I am at the end of my wits, ${PLAYER}. I have succeeded in turning many of the well well-wishers away, but at a cost. I have promised I would look in on this friend and that captain, until the list has grown quite long! I am a Gondorian and a man of my word, but if I visit with every one I have promised to - well, I simply do not have time for that! I am kept out here, shooing the self-same well-wishers away, as it is!

'Would you do me a good turn, friend? Visit my promised list and bring me word of how they fare?'Torchún listens attentively to your report, nodding his head.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. I have noted what you said and I will bear word to the various well-wishers the next time they come visiting.'

He sighs.

'No doubt they'll want another report on the morrow!'After learning much from the poisons of the Enemy, Mílgamel hopes to devise a means to heal the blasted lands of the Wastes by studying the old herb-lore of Gondor.Torngroth can be found far to the north in North Ithilien, Cair Andros can be found to the south-west of Henneth Annûn, Osgiliath can be found to the east of Minas Tirith, and Ioreth can be found in the High Hall within the Masters' Tier of Minas Tirith.

Mílgamel has asked you to investigate Cair Andros, Osgiliath, and Torngroth in the hope of finding more pages from "Worts and Weeds." She also has asked you to return to Ioreth to obtain Galathir's transcription of the book.Mílgamel can be found at the Camp of the Host in the far north of North Ithilien.

You have found many pages of "Worts and Weeds" and now surely have a completed volume between them. You should seek out Mílgamel at the Camp of the Host and deliver them to her.Find Mílgamel at the Camp of the HostMílgamel can be found at the Camp of the Host in the far north of North Ithilien.

You have arrived at the Camp of the Host and should now speak to Mílgamel.Talk to Mílgamel at the Camp of the HostMílgamel's transcription of "Worts and Weeds" can be found beside her at the Camp of the Host.

Mílgamel has asked you to bind the new pages into her transcription of "Worts and Weeds."Mílgamel can be found at the Camp of the Host in the far north of North Ithilien.

You have finished binding the completed volume of "Worts and Weeds" and should now speak to Mílgamel.Talk to Mílgamel'It is unlikely to be a simple matter, but there may be a way to discover more about this lichen. Once, I recall, Master Galathir spoke of a great tome - a supposed great collection of knowledge about the plants and such-like of Middle-earth, called "Worts and Weeds." When I showed interest, he forbade me from leafing through it, for he said it contained spurious anecdotes of specimens not seen in his age, and besides, it was a damaged copy, far from complete.

'I... I have come to doubt much of my Master's wisdom. But - never mind that. This "Worts and Weeds" was penned by a Man of Gondor named Golgolas, who dwelt within Ithilien before it fell under the Shadow, and it is said it was widely read and copied by the Herbalists of that day.

'Do you think - would you visit the ruins and places of North Ithilien, and discover if any pages of that book still remain? Torngroth, Cair Andros, or Osgiliath, for example? Who can say, perhaps good, old Ioreth would share the pages Galathir would not.

'Meanwhile, I have asked the Rangers to provide me passage to the edge of the Wastes. Although they took much convincing, they have agreed. If I am to gain a better understanding of this lichen, I shall need to be closer to the lands of the Enemy - no matter how hostile they may be.

'They plan to take me to an army encampment - one with which they claim you are familiar. When you've gathered as much as you can of "Worts and Weeds," find me far to the north.''You have come all this way just to see Galathir's transcription of "Worts and Weeds?" That's enough walking for you, ${PLAYER}! Now wait here a moment....'

Ioreth walks over to a nearby book-shelf and rummages through a few heavy tomes. She finally pulls out something that more closely resembles a sheaf of crumbling pages than a book, and then walks back over to you.

'Why, you could hardly call this a volume! Why does he keep this thing about, I wonder, if he thinks so poorly of it? Here, you take it. Perhaps he will end up being of more help without it...

'Do not worry, ${PLAYER}. If he asks for it, I shall tell him that you and Mílgamel had need of it for your task.

'Oh, and tell the king I wish his campaign well when you see him next!'Amid the Orc-filth of Cair Andros, you have found a small pile of books. Most of them have various tears and tatters and a few have been burned.

You sift through the pile of books and come across what seems to be a few singed pages bound to the spine and back cover of "Worts and Weeds." The rest of the volume is missing, but you are able to collect five legible pages.While scouring the Halls of Healing in Osgiliath, you have found a decaying volume of "Worts and Weeds."

Judging by the crumbling remains of its covers, it seems likely that this could have been the original version written by Golgolas of Ithilien. The pages are largely in poor condition, and those that are still legible have sustained some water damage from the ruined cistern nearby.

You carefully pick up the volume, mark the legible pages, and stow it away.'I am glad you have come, ${PLAYER}.

'Even with so many here, there is a feeling of loneliness in the air. These men stare into the face of death... not that of a triumphant victory. I speak true, do I not?

'I can see it in your eyes. Something other than victory drives this advance - a purpose born of valour and kinship. I will not ask you of it, and I shall not tell the people of Minas Tirith of what I have learned here. Despair is a mighty force, and it can consume far more than hope alone.

'I have thought enough on that matter. Let us speak of more hopeful things.

'I see that you have brought many pages to me, but what is that under your arm?'

Mílgamel takes the crumbling volume of "Worts and Weeds" from you.

'This... this could be the original volume by Golgolas! It is ruined but for a few pages, but such a find you have made!

'Let me look at this for a time, and you should bind the other pages together into one. I'm certain you have the complete volume between them all!''I cannot thank you enough for your help with this, ${PLAYER}. Master Galathir hoped merely to survey Ithilien and gather some known herbs, but now I am on this journey, I feel my sight reaches far beyond his ken. What we can learn from this finished volume - why, for one, about this poisonous lichen and how it might heal the northern Wastes from their long blight. 

'Even with the looming battle ahead, we must take the time to tend to these lands. If they fail us, any other victory may ring hollow. Should these brave men return from this battle, they must return to a beautiful Gondor brimming with new life... in place of all that was lost.

'I shall need much time to study Golgolas's work, but I believe we can bring forth new life in these blasted lands!'In the thick of battle upon the fields of Pelennor, Bregdal, Errand-rider of Gondor, hopes to assist you in any way he can.The Black Serpent can be found in the Blood of the Black Serpent instance.Stay near Théoden.Let events transpireThéoden can be found in the Blood of the Black Serpent instance.Speak with ThéodenBregdal can be found on the Field of Onslaught.

You should speak with Bregdal.Speak with Bregdal'${PLAYER}, we meet again upon the battlefield. Through my recent missions as an errand-rider, I have been put into some perilous situations. Today, however, is indeed the most perilous!

'The Men of Rohan have fought valiantly thus far. Still, I have seen much blood shed. The Haradrim are formidable foes and are led by their king, the Black Serpent. If we were to defeat the King of Harad in combat, The Haradrim may yet back away from this battle. Are you willing to muster some fellow warriors, and take on the king of Harad?'

<rgb=#FF0000>It is recommended playing the Blood of the Black Serpent instance with a small fellowship. You can access the instance from the Instance Join panel.</rgb>'I saw your stance upon the battlefield, and I knew that it must be ${PLAYER}. Defeating the Black Serpent is the least I could do for all you have done for me and my peoples. I thank you for defeating the King of Harad's sons. It is good to fight by your side, ${RACE}.''The Black Serpent fell by Théoden King's sword? Victory is close at hand!'In the thick of battle upon the fields of Pelennor, Bregdal, Errand-rider of Gondor, hopes to assist you in any way he can.The Enemy can be found on the Field of Onslaught, upon the fields of Pelennor.

