Defeat the Caretaker.Defeat the CaretakerCompleted.CompletedIt seems you have made quite a lot of noise, with all your tinkering. There must have been a herd of goats seeking shelter in the broken machines. Now they graze the valley floor and if you do not intervene, they will make their way towards supplies at the Udûn Foothold.You have defeated the reclusive and powerful Troll, Búktavul.The spies of Ugrukhôr, Captain of the Pit, can be found throughout Udûn.Defeat spies you uncover in Udûn.Defeat spies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many of the Captain of the Pit's spies in Udûn.CompletedThe spies of Ugrukhôr, Captain of the Pit, can be found throughout Udûn. The fewer who return bearing tidings, the better!Fewer of Ugrukhôr's spies will bear him tidings of Gondor's presence in Udûn thanks to you.Captain Lachír's search party wandered the basin of Udûn, leaving men here and there to search in vain for some imagined treasure. After that, Lachír backtracked, leading his remaining company up the rising northward road.You should follow the road that winds north from western Udûn.Search the northward roadYou should continue up the road that winds north from western Udûn.Continue up the roadYou should raise the fallen Gondorian banner that you found on the road that winds north from western Udûn.Raise the Gondorian bannerYou should continue up the road that winds north from western Udûn, righting any other banners that you find.You should continue up the road that winds north from western Udûn.Continue up the road, fixing any other fallen banners you findYou should talk to the look-out on the outcropping off the road that winds north from western Udûn, and learn what he knows of Captain Lachír.Talk to the look-outThe soldier Coldan has asked you to stand beside him and look up toward the looming face of Ered Lithui.Coldan the look-out stands on the outcropping off the road that winds north from western Udûn.

You should talk to Coldan again, now that you looked and have seen the great edifice against the mountains.Talk to Coldan'You wish to know where Captain Lachír has gone? That I can say, and better than you might imagine.

'He set off east you see, after leaving us here, and was gone for some time. Then, some half a day later we saw him marching back along the road and our hearts leaped, only to sink again. They came not for us, nor were they returning to the Foothold. No, we watched them make their way down the central road, then turn up on the rising northward path. If you would find them, you must follow them there.''Have... have you come here to relieve me? Truly, I know not how long I have stood here. The winds at this height cut through my armour, and though a foul fog poisons the air, I feel as if every red, malicious eye in Udûn is fixed upon me where I stand.'

The look-out shivers.

'Why, you ask? Come stand by me, with your back to Udûn and your eyes up. What is it that you see?''That which you see is named Anglach, perhaps the greatest of forges in Mordor. I have stood and kept watch on it since yesterday, when Captain Lachír posted me here.'

The look-out seems scared and miserable. You tell him to return to the Foothold and it is as if a great weight has been lifted from his shoulders.

'Reason and duty have warred in me these many hours. Thanks to you, ${CLASS}, they may stop their contest and leave here, hand in hand.'It seems an overseer named Ushkbál is the key to this mysterious meeting, somehow.You should search for Ushkbál the Overseer in the great foundry, on the northern side of Udûn, above Maudhúl Vôr.You have not found Ushkbál the Overseer, but you have found signs of a bloody struggle. You should follow the footprints leading from the place and see where they lead.The dwarves stand behind a cart high up the path in northern Udûn, above Maudhúl Vôr.

The trail of footprints led straight from signs of a bloody struggle to two dwarf-forgeworkers standing over a corpse. You should speak to them.Talk to the dwarvesThe corpse and dwarves are behind a cart high up the path in northern Udûn, above Maudhúl Vôr.

You should inspect the overseer's corpse to verify the dwarves' claim that he bears sword-wounds, while they hold only hammers.Inspect the corpseThe dwarves are behind a cart high up the path in northern Udûn, above Maudhúl Vôr.

You should talk to the dwarves, now that you have verified that the overseer's corpse bears sword and not blunt wounds.Talk to StéthiTalk to SlárnurKurtil stands near the dwarves and corpse, behind a cart high up the path in northern Udûn, above Maudhúl Vôr.

Hear what Kurtil has to say.Talk to Kurtil'We must seek Ushkbál the Overseer, then. It is he who will accomplish what we seek.'

Paying no heed of any surprise on your face, Kurtil thinks for a long moment.

'He has agreed, agreed to talk to us at least, and there is hope for more. Surely, he is found at his post, north of here by the great foundry, which lies above Maudhúl Vôr.''The great foundry lies north of the road below, above Maudhúl Vôr. You go and seek Ushkbál the Overseer on the good legs you have. I follow behind.''Stéthi, you explain. I'll botch it up, properly. Besides, I'm still catching my breath from dragging old Ushkbál all the ways up here.''You speak to them, yes? I think that best.'''Twas not us who did this thing. I know full well how it may look. We only dragged him here, out of the sight of prying eyes.

'Hoy, you there, Kurtil! I know you by face and by name. Whatever trouble you're up to this time, you must bow to plain truth: Who in the northern 'works would touch a hair on Ushkbál's ugly pate? None, that's who! No overseer we'd more gladly work for, neither.

'Besides, you see we carry only hammers and these wounds be swords' work, plain to see. Look yourself, ${RACE}.''Look and you will see. No hammer felled Ushkbál.''If it is so, then it is so, yes? The dwarf speaks true.''Now then, that's settled, I hope! A smouldering shame about Ushkbál, though! We know not if it were one of the enforcers or some wild-frenzied soldier who stuck him through, but there you have it. He was our hope and he's gone.

'Well, and if you are here then you followed the signal flags, too, just as we. Let us work the iron together, we four, while it's got any heat left. Perhaps the work might be salvaged. If only we can think what to do now that we are on our own. What say you Kurtil? ${RACE}?''This is how I see it, and pardon my speaking thus, Stéthi. There are two impurities we've got in this slag. First-wise, there's no one to do the deed. Second, there's someone, somewhere, who knows the signal flags and maybe doesn't want the deed done.''Yes, yes, yes. The iron is hot now, but cooling, cooling...'

For a moment, Kurtil seems lost in thought.

'We need only someone to go. Who to fill the post? No, not I, Nurnhoth that I am. Who am I? No one. Will Gradrágh go? Ha ha. We know the answer to that, my friends. What of you, Slárnur? You will not. A shame!

'No Orc, nor dwarf, nor Nurnhoth here among us...'

Then a smile slowly spreads across Kurtil's face, lighting the twinkle back into his eyes, which fix on you.The soldiers of the Dark Lord's army left many of their possessions behind upon the camps in Udûn.Search abandoned soldiers' packs in the valley and camps of Udûn.Search abandoned packs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Searched many abandoned soldiers' packs.CompletedWhether from marching into battle or fleeing before their great defeat, the soldiers of the Dark Lord's army left many of their possessions behind upon the camps in Udûn. There might well be some things of value in there.While there was nothing of truly great value, the soldiers of the Dark Lord's armies left much in the packs they abandoned among the camps of Udûn.You have agreed to petition the Easterlings for a meeting with the forge-workers, so that they may beg safe passage out of Udûn.You should hail the Easterlings as Kurtil taught you before their camp, Rhûd Vorn, on the northern slopes of Udûn.You should talk to the Easterling Lord who responded to your hailing, outside Rhûd Vorn, on the northern slopes of Udûn.Talk to the Easterling LordYou should lead Yordam the Easterling to a view of the Black Gates, the Great Flag on the parade grounds, and seek the letter dispatched to Maudhúl Vôr.

If you get separated from Yordam, wind his horn to call him back to you.Find the great bannerFind the letterBring Yordam to the Black GateNow that you have found the fallen banner, the dead letter-bearer, and the Black Gates broken, you should talk to Yordam.

If you get separated from Yordam, wind his horn to call him back to you.Now that you have collected the proofs, you should return to Khôpol before Rhûd Vorn, on the northern slopes of Udûn.Having secured the pledge from the Easterling Lord Khôpol, you should return to Kurtil and the other forge-workers where they had been, on the height above Maudhúl Vôr.Return to Kurtil'Long is the tale to tell, but short is the time. Do you remember that the Captain of the Pit does not hear the signal flags' speech? He is the master of all Udûn, but many do not wish it so.

'When the ground shook and the gates fell and the Eagles crossed the skies, a light dawned upon our minds - that we might fly ourselves. Fly like the eagles and in Udûn dwell no more. But how? And then we remember the Easterlings camped in Rhûd Vorn. Perhaps, we thought, perhaps we might go back with them, to the lands far from Captains and cruel Gondorians and Elves.

'And so in flags was it written and in flags was it read by you and me and by others. Ushkbál the goodly overseer agreed to go before the Easterlings and plead our case. Now Ushkbál is gone and our hope rests on you, ${RACE}-${CLASS}. We ask you to go in Ushkbál's place and parley with the Easterlings. Only to meet! To speak awhile of what may be. 

'Stand before Rhûd Vorn high on the height and hail them with the words that I will teach you. 

Kurtil teaches you a formal military hail in the Easterling tongue.

'If fortune smiles upon us, they will agree.''The Captain of the Pit and the Men of the West are like two stones, ${PLAYER}, and we the roots who will be ground between them. We must beg the Easterlings safe passage back to their lands, and we rely on you to go before them. 

'Go then. Go to Rhûd Vorn and hail them as I showed you.'You begin to explain the case of the forge-workers, but he quickly interrupts you.

'You speak as if the Eye was fallen. You speak as if the Cause was lost. This is not so and cannot be. How could it be, for the Black Gates still stand and the great banner still waves like a lone wing above the parade grounds. Why, only this morning I sent an Orc with a letter to the Black Word's commissary in Maudhúl Vôr. As sure as the ground I stand on, it has been delivered and needed provisions are on their way.'

When you do not back down, he stares hard at you.

'Folly! There is deceit afoot, if not by you, then by someone. I will send Yordam. He will go with you so that the truth may be known to me. Go.''I will go with you, as my Lord commands. We will see what we will see.''Go with Yordam to the Great Banner, Maudhúl Vôr, and the Black Gates. You will see. The war is not lost, even if it is not yet won.''I have seen the proofs, though I hardly believed my eyes. A ${RACE} from the West has spoken the truth! I return to Rhûd Vorn, as should you.''I see. So it is as you spoke.'

The Easterling Lord thinks for a moment.

'Yes, we will meet with these forge-workers and speak to them. You may carry them this pledge, ${CLASS}.''So you are the ${CLASS}. Kurtil did not mention you were ${RACE}! If you ask me, we're in a hard place, all told. I don't know where to turn.''You are returned! And they have pledged their word? Strong as iron, an Easterling's pledge.

'See now who has also arrived. It is Gradrágh, another overseer. He wants only some persuading.'Various Gearworks in Udûn are missing levers.Elaborate gearworks can be found in Udûn,

