You have cleansed the corruption from the Vengeance of Folca and should bring it now to Elrond in Rivendell.'I have studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang. It bears a powerful corruption, but it was not made in the land of Mordor. It came from elsewhere, and the corruption that envelops it stems from its long stay in the Black Land. If this evil could be cleansed, I believe that Elrond could imbue it with a powerful and vital force for Good.

'Before that can happen, however, we must do something about the corruption that Mordor has worked upon it. There are different methods to accomplish this, though the Wise disagree upon the effectiveness of each. My preferred means for such a cure is to bring the corrupted item to the place where it was made, or to places of some significance upon its path, and to thereby diffuse the foul touch of Mordor that has, in the interim, claimed it. We must learn the story of this heirloom before it came to Mordor.'

The Wizard turns the artifact over in his hands, studying it for some clue to its origin. To your eye the heirloom is a small shard of bone or tusk, splintered smoothly from a larger piece, and you say so.

'I deem that you are right, ${PLAYER}, but I will narrow the scope of your deduction. This was once part of a porcine tusk, from a boar or similar animal. These have been known to chip or splinter during combat.

'That would not ordinarily be a cause for interest, but there are markings carved near the base of it, faded and hard to read. Perhaps an expert jeweller in Minas Tirith can use his tools to read them for you.''This was once part of a porcine tusk, from a boar or similar animal. These have been known to chip or splinter during combat.

'That would not ordinarily be a cause for interest, but there are markings carved near the base of it, faded and hard to read. Perhaps an expert jeweller in Minas Tirith can use his tools to read them for you.''What is this thing you have brought to me? It raises in me an unpleasant sensation, as if it were capable of great evil or indeed had already committed such! I will not fashion a necklace or amulet out of this, unless the price be most convincing!'

You tell the jeweller that you are here for another purpose and explain that you would like him to read the tiny markings near the base of the tusk-shard. He grunts reluctantly and takes out his glass.

'Even now they are very difficult to make out, and anyway are symbols with which I am unfamiliar. The words look Rohirric to me, but I am an expert in fashioning beautiful adornments and trinkets, not in unravelling languages! Bring to the Rohirrim that which concerns them, that is my rule! I once knew a jeweller from the Sutcrofts who learned his trade at the same time I did, and planned to repair in their city of Snowbourn when he became an expert. You should look for him and see if he can read these Rohirric words.''Welcome to Snowbourn, can I provide you with any assistance? I fashioned pieces of such beauty that Théoden King himself would marvel at my craft, and I am certain Éomer King, now elevated, will do the same!'

You explain that an expert jeweller in Minas Tirith referred you to him, believing that he might be able to read the Rohirric words that adorn the base of the tusk-shard.

'You will not be disappointed, and when next I see my friend I will be sure to thank him for sending you to me. He has always had a discerning eye, and his finest quality is knowing when to say he does not have sufficient expertise in a field. I, however, am clearly the man for the task. Give it here. My, it does possess an unpleasant air, doesn't it?'

The jeweller of Rohan squints at the tusk-shard through his glass for a moment and then snorts with disgust.

'Hooves at a gallop! Did I say my friend was a man of discernment? He is a fool, and always has been! Dress him in motley, it is all he deserves! These are not Rohirric words! If these be words at all, then they are words scrawled in a foul and Orkish tongue!

'It could be that this one here reads "Folca," if you sound it out, and that tells me that if you wish to follow the trail, such as it is, you should search in the Eastfold. Perhaps a bard can tell you of the story of Folca and the Boar of Everholt, for it seems relevant to this tusk-shard. Go to Aldburg and find a bard. The tavern seems a safe place to look.''Would you like to hear a song? I would be a traitor indeed if I did not allow you to rearrange your skills, but what would be the point of that? Aye, no point, and yet all the points! Relegated to music am I, but still I long for the adventuring life! No song for me, but all the songs of others!'

Unsure that this will prove at all fruitful, you show the bard the corrupted tusk-shard. His eyes light up when you explain that the markings near the base appear to be tiny Orkish scrawls, and they mention Folca.

'Ah, the call of adventure beckons! If you wish to unravel the mystery of this heirloom, you have come to the right place, for I know of the tale! I will bring you to the site where it is said King Folca slew the beast and was slain in turn. I will be a bard no more, but a warrior and a quester! Meet me north-east of Aldburg, my friend, and let us do deeds worthy of the great heroes of old!''I will meet you north-east of Aldburg! Adventure!''This might not be the right place, ${PLAYER}. I have sung the tale of Folca and the Boar so many times, but the layout of the land seems very different when you're actually there. Everything is so much larger than it seems in the tales; I feel as if we have been walking all day!'

Eldmath turns the corrupted tusk-shard over in his hands and shudders.

'This does have a vile, unlucky feeling about it, doesn't it? I am ready for some excitement, if only to dispel the tedium of all this walking!''You've got a look about you, ${RACE}. Seen some battle, eh? Well, you won't try nothin' if you know what's good for your friend here. You're comin' with us, and no funny business or we'll stick him before you can move.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I will meet you at the Haven of Orladion, ${PLAYER}.''I know why you have come, ${PLAYER}. If you are to journey beneath the shroud that covers Gorgoroth, you will need the aid of a light that can pierce the darkness.'

Elrond takes the Vengeance of Folca from you and dips it into the font beside him. When the artifact emerges from the glowing circle, light drips soundlessly from it and pools like water in the basin. An image rises unbidden in your mind, of the light of stars glittering in the heavens, extinguished by the coming of day but rekindled every night without fail. So it was with the vengeance of Folca, who would not live to see the rise of Rohan's star after a long night and a fresh dawn, but rise it did. Elrond smiles at the shimmering light, and his voice contains both determination and joy when he speaks again.

'With the Light of Eärendil, you will be able to walk where the shadows lie, and overcome them.'Mithrandir has studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang and believes that if it might be cleansed of its corruption it could be empowered with a force for Good.Lothrandir returned to Sûri-kylä in Forochel following the War of the Ring.

Mithrandir believes that the corrupted brooch came from Forochel and returning it there may help cleanse the corruption that befell it in Mordor.Lothrandir asked you to search east of Sûri-kylä while he showed the corrupted brooch to folk in the city.Search for the missing Lossoth warriors east of Sûri-kyläTerho is south-east of Sûri-kylä, calling for help!Talk to TerhoLothrandir returned to Sûri-kylä in Forochel following the War of the Ring.Talk to Lothrandir in Sûri-kylä, in ForochelLothrandir returned to Sûri-kylä in Forochel following the War of the Ring.

Lothrandir gave the corrupted brooch to its rightful owner, but he fears that if the corruption remains it might do her some harm.Talk to Lothrandir in Sûri-kylä and go to see the widowElrond is at the Haven of Orladion in Rivendell, north-west of the Last Homely House.

You have cleansed the corruption from the Brooch of Bone, and should bring it now to Elrond in Rivendell.'I have studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang. It bears a powerful corruption, but it was not made in the land of Mordor. It came from elsewhere, and the corruption that envelops it stems from its long stay in the Black Land. If this evil could be cleansed, I believe that Elrond could imbue it with a powerful and vital force for Good.

'Before that can happen, however, we must do something about the corruption that Mordor has worked upon it. There are different methods to accomplish this, though the Wise disagree upon the effectiveness of each. My preferred means for such a cure is to bring the corrupted item to the place where it was made, or to places of some significance upon its path, and to thereby diffuse the foul touch of Mordor that has, in the interim, claimed it. We must learn the story of this heirloom before it came to Mordor.'

The Wizard turns the artifact over in his hands, studying it for some clue to its origin. To your eye the heirloom is a small brooch made of bone, encircled by what appears to be a curved piece of antler. It reminds you of the Lossoth of Forochel. Mithrandir seems to pick up on your assessment.

'Yes, ${PLAYER}, this brooch was clearly fashioned by Lossoth hands. In material and in style this brooch of bone began its journey near the Ice-bay. That is where you should bring it, for reminding it of its origin may help to cleanse the corruption that enveloped it within Mordor.

'Go to the city of Sûri-kylä, and find Lothrandir of the Dúnedain. He returned to Forochel after the fall of Sauron, and he may be able to help you cleanse the brooch of its evil.''This brooch was clearly fashioned by Lossoth hands. In material and in style this brooch of bone began its journey near the Ice-bay. That is where you should bring it, for reminding it of its origin may help to cleanse the corruption that enveloped it within Mordor.

'Go to the city of Sûri-kylä, and find Lothrandir of the Dúnedain. He returned to Forochel after the fall of Sauron, and he may be able to help you cleanse the brooch of its evil.''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}. There are always dangers, even after the fall of Sauron, and it is our duty to face them. The hazards of Forochel are more to my liking than those we faced in the south-lands, however. What has caused you to make this long journey, my friend?'

You tell Lothrandir of the bone brooch you recovered and the corruption that grips it.

'I can sense its evil power. It puts me in mind of the dread that weighed upon me in the dungeons of Isengard; that is not a feeling I am eager to revisit! That this is a Lossoth brooch cannot be denied, for there are many like it in Sûri-kylä alone, but the real question is this: does the brooch belong to one of those who have gone missing in recent days and are currently unaccounted? If it does, this brooch may bring ill tidings to the missing man's family. That should not deter us. It should be delivered.

'Yes, several warriors have not returned from excursions among the snows, and I went with some hardy men to seek for them, to no avail. We searched to the north, but had not yet gone to the east. If you will search for them, I will show this brooch to people in Sûri-kylä and see if they recognize it.''Several warriors have not returned from excursions among the snows, and I went with some hardy men to seek for them, to no avail. We searched to the north, but had not yet gone to the east. If you will search for them in that direction, I will show this brooch to people in Sûri-kylä and see if they recognize it.''The storm came up suddenly, and I was separated from Âtos and Îkka. We did not know where we were... can we really have been so close to the city? But there is no time for that now... I am pursued! Help me!''I heard that you found the bodies of two of the missing men, ${PLAYER}, but I am glad you were able to save Terho. He spoke of a sudden storm that overtook the warriors not far from Sûri-kylä. It happens from time to time, although I did not notice such a storm since my return. The cold can work a madness on even the well-prepared, however.

'While you searched for the missing Lossoth, I discovered the rightful owner of the brooch. A woman named Pinja, recently-widowed, told me that the brooch belonged to her slain husband, Oiva.'

You ask Lothrandir if he is not concerned that the corruption in the brooch might work some harm upon the widow. He seems to consider it for the first time.

'That... that is a good point, ${PLAYER}. I do not know why I did not think of that when I gave her the brooch. Now that I think on it, I confess that I cannot remember actually handing it to her at all! That is most strange. We had better go to see her at once, ${PLAYER}! I hope I have not done ill through my desire to do right!''Quickly, my friend! Something is not right about this!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I will meet you at the Haven of Orladion, ${PLAYER}.''I know why you have come, ${PLAYER}. If you are to journey beneath the shroud that covers Gorgoroth, you will need the aid of a light that can pierce the darkness.'

Elrond takes the Brooch of Bone from you and dips it into the font beside him. When the artifact emerges from the glowing circle, light drips soundlessly from it and pools like water in the basin. You are put in mind of a blazing hearth, a beacon shining in the darkness of a crisp and clear winter's night, calling all travellers home for warmth and welcome. Elrond smiles at the sight of the glowing heirloom, and his voice contains both determination and joy when he speaks again.

'With the Light of Eärendil, you will be able to walk where the shadows lie, and overcome them.'Mithrandir has studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang and believes that if it might be cleansed of its corruption it could be empowered with a force for Good.Suvulch is in the village of Lhanuch, in Enedwaith.Talk to Suvulch in the village of LhanuchHelû is a warrior of Lhanuch on the highest rise of the village.Talk to Helû in LhanuchLhunog is on a hill outside the village of Lhanuch.Talk to Lhunog on a hill outside LhanuchHelû has come out of the village for further investigation of the bone fragment.Talk to Helû outside the villageHûdh of the Caru-lûth and Cufrus of the Avanc-lûth are in Galtrev and may know more of the bone fragment you carry.Talk to Hûdh of the Caru-lûth in GaltrevTalk to Cufrus of the Avanc-lûth, in GaltrevFrestang of the Uch-lûth is in Galtrev.Talk to Frestang of the Uch-lûth, in GaltrevAn expert tailor in the Hall of Making in Lhanuch may know something about the needle that came into your possession.Talk to the expert tailor in the Hall of Making, in LhanuchRaiglud, the oldest woman in Lhanuch, has drawn your attention. She is in the Hall of Making, and has a story to tell.Raiglud, the oldest woman in Lhanuch, has drawn your attention. She is in the Hall of Making, and has a story to tell.Talk to Raiglud and listen to her tale of long agoElrond is at the Haven of Orladion in Rivendell, north-west of the Last Homely House.

You have cleansed the corruption from the Needle of Many Leagues, and should bring it now to Elrond in Rivendell.'I have studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang. It bears a powerful corruption, but it was not made in the land of Mordor. It came from elsewhere, and the corruption that envelops it stems from its long stay in the Black Land. If this evil could be cleansed, I believe that Elrond could imbue it with a powerful and vital force for Good.

'Before that can happen, however, we must do something about the corruption that Mordor has worked upon it. There are different methods to accomplish this, though the Wise disagree upon the effectiveness of each. My preferred means for such a cure is to bring the corrupted item to the place where it was made, or to places of some significance upon its path, and to thereby diffuse the foul touch of Mordor that has, in the interim, claimed it. We must learn the story of this heirloom before it came to Mordor.'

The Wizard turns the artifact over in his hands, studying it for some clue to its origin. To your eye the heirloom is a thin shard of bone, possibly broken from the tip of a spear or an arrowhead.

'This thin piece of bone is jagged on one side, but the other is smooth and I see signs that it was carved for some purpose. The tip appears to me to have been broken off and lost. It is a pity, for if we could see the shape entire we might better know for what it was used.

'The portion of this artifact that remains tells me that it was fashioned by one of the tribes that hunts in Enedwaith or Dunland, likely for war-like purpose. Speak to a warrior in the village of Lhanuch, in Enedwaith, and perhaps he will tell you of its origin. Once that is known, you may have the knowledge you need to cleanse it of the corruption that encircles it.''This thin piece of bone is jagged on one side, but the other is smooth and I see signs that it was carved for some purpose. The tip appears to me to have been broken off and lost. It is a pity, for if we could see the shape entire we might better know for what it was used.

'The portion of this artifact that remains tells me that it was fashioned by one of the tribes that hunts in Enedwaith or Dunland, likely for war-like purpose. Speak to a warrior in the village of Lhanuch, in Enedwaith, and perhaps he will tell you of its origin. Once that is known you may have the knowledge you need to cleanse it of the corruption that encircles it.''What brings you to this village, ${PLAYER}? I will not call you duvodiad, but even Suvulch can tell an ill wind brings you here. Do not make me regret this welcome, as others would!'

You show the bone shard to the warrior, and he raises his hand as if in warding.

'What is this, a fragment of a spear-point? It must have been used in the slaughter of something vile, for the sight of it alone is enough to raise in me great feelings of dread and of sickness! Take it away!''Take it away! Whatever that fragment might be, it seems wholly evil to me!''Yes? What do you want?'

Helû takes one look at the bone fragment and reacts much as Suvulch did.

'You have brought an object of evil to Lhanuch! Take it outside the village, we do not want it here! You will draw evils to us! If you must show this spear-point to anyone, show it to warriors outside the village where these folk will not be endangered!''I give you my welcome, wanderer. I know a hunter when I see one striding among the hills and fields with such purpose! What brings you here?'

You show Lhunog the bone fragment and ask him if he can tell you anything about it.

'I see why you have come to Lhanuch, for this does seem to have been crafted by one of the tribes of Enedwaith or Dunland. I cannot say if it was made by our tribe, but this feeling of evil is so overpowering that I refuse to believe it was made by one of the Algraig!'

Lhunog suddenly straightens and he smells the breeze.

'Something is not right, wanderer. We are hunted.''I knew that it was so! Did I not say to you that the bone fragment you carry would draw evil to it? I am glad you listened to my command and quickly brought it out of the village!

'It is not welcome in Lhanuch. Perhaps one of the other tribes, such as those that gather in Galtrev, will tell you something about the cursed object you carry.''One of the other tribes in Galtrev may know more of that cursed thing, but it is not welcome in Lhanuch!''What do you have there, stranger?'

You show the bone fragment to Hûdh and he furrows his brow with thought.

'You say this might have broken off the tip of a spear? It is possible, but I would say it is not likely. The Stag-clan has used bone for weapons of various sort, but my people generally prefer stone for the greater durability on impact. If this fragment did come from a spear, I think it would have happened on the first strike, and such a spear would be of little use for a true hunter.

'No, I do not think this is from a spear-point, but I cannot tell you anything else.''You bear an artifact of evil, wanderer. I do not wish to become ensnared in anything of the sort, so I will give you a wide berth. But listen to this advice from a well-meaning stranger: do not become involved with the scheming of Wizards, even if they bear gifts! Many in my clan learned this lesson painfully, and too late.

'If you still have an ear for further advice, I say this too: whatever artifact you carry will draw evils to you. Cast it away, and save yourself some trouble!'

As Cufrus hurries away, you decide to hold onto it. After all, Mithrandir believed that it could be cleansed of the evil that surrounded it during its long stay in Mordor and might be turned to Good.'You came from Lhanuch? I was born into the Uch-lûth, but I have not been welcome there for many years. Still, I am always glad to hear news of my old home.'

You tell Frestang that you went to Lhanuch seeking information about the bone spear-point that came into your possession. He studies it for a moment and smiles.

'You went to the warriors of Lhanuch and asked them if they recognized this and of course they did not, for they are focused on weaponry and tools of war. No, they would not have known this implement, even though they rely on others like it every day.

'This is not part of a spear-point or an arrowhead. This is part of a bone needle, used by tailors in the fashioning of clothes and garments. It does seem to be very old, but it is reminiscent of the sort that are used still in Lhanuch. Go to the Hall of Making in Lhanuch, and ask the tailors what they can tell you of it.''The fragment you showed to me is part of a bone needle used by tailors in the fashioning of clothes and garments. It does seem to be very old, but it is reminiscent of the sort that are used still in Lhanuch. Go to the Hall of Making in Lhanuch, and ask the tailors what they can tell you of it.''This is similar to the needles we use here, but there is a peculiar notch at the bottom that sets it apart. I do not know why it is there, but it is distinctive. It is a mystery to me.''It has been more than fifty years since I last saw a needle such as that one! Can it be the very same? It must be, for Deilur made all her needles herself, and that mark was her own. No other tailor in all the years of my long life ever took such pains as she to add such an adornment. It served no practical purpose, but Deilur fashioned all her needles in that way.

'I am Raiglud. I am the oldest woman in the village and by my count I am the oldest member of the Uch-lûth by many years. Do you want to know the tale of that needle you carry? Speak to me and you will hear it. It is a sad tale, but to see that needle once again I think it may be worth remembering, and this old woman would enjoy the telling of it and the reminder of her youth, long ago.''Long ago, when I was just a girl, the Uch-lûth had not yet settled at Lhanuch...'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I will meet you at the Haven of Orladion, ${PLAYER}.''I know why you have come, ${PLAYER}. If you are to journey beneath the shroud that covers Gorgoroth, you will need the aid of a light that can pierce the darkness.'

Elrond takes the Needle of Many Leagues from you and dips it into the font beside him. When the artifact emerges from the glowing circle, light drips soundlessly from it and pools like water in the basin. The needle travelled many leagues before it meted out Deilur's vengeance upon the Orc that took her life, but until now it did not shine as brightly as did Deilur in the memory of a child. Elrond smiles at the sight, and his voice contains both determination and joy when he speaks again.

'With the Light of Eärendil, you will be able to walk where the shadows lie, and overcome them.'Mithrandir has studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang and believes that if it might be cleansed of its corruption it could be empowered with a force for Good.Ghân-buri-Ghân is the leader of the Drúedain and may know the origins of the corrupted carving. He is at Drû Bhûta, in Taur Drúadan.Khôr-buri-Ghân is at the Blood-cloak, east of the great waterfall in Drû Bhûta, atop a large rocky cliff. It is the location where the Wild Men perform their funerary rites.Khôr-buri-Ghân suggested that you look for Orcs at two places in Taur Drúadan, forth of Drû Bhûta and further north-east of there, in the woods.Look for Orcs north of the road by Drû Bhûta, in Taur DrúadanLook for Orcs further north-east in the woods, in Taur DrúadanKhôr-buri-Ghân suggested that you look for Orcs at another place in Taur Drúadan, north-west over a hill.Look for Orcs to the north-west, over a hill and by an island in the river, in Taur DrúadanKhôr-buri-Ghân has remembered a cave that his brother told him about and wants to find it. He is by the island in the river, waiting for you to speak with him.Talk to Khôr-buri-Ghân by the river and travel with him to the island caveElrond is at the Haven of Orladion in Rivendell, north-west of the Last Homely House.

You have cleansed the corruption from the Memento of Bhai-buri-Ghân, and should bring it now to Elrond in Rivendell.'I have studied the artifact that Ayorzén stole from the vault of Durthang. It bears a powerful corruption, but it was not made in the land of Mordor. It came from elsewhere, and the corruption that envelops it stems from its long stay in the Black Land. If this evil could be cleansed, I believe that Elrond could imbue it with a powerful and vital force for Good.

'Before that can happen, however, we must do something about the corruption that Mordor has worked upon it. There are different methods to accomplish this, though the Wise disagree upon the effectiveness of each. My preferred means for such a cure is to bring the corrupted item to the place where it was made, or to places of some significance upon its path, and to thereby diffuse the foul touch of Mordor that has, in the interim, claimed it. We must learn the story of this heirloom before it came to Mordor.'

The Wizard turns the artifact over in his hands, studying it for some clue to its origin. To your eye the heirloom is a small sculpture carved from stone, in the shape of a squat humanoid figure.

'This carving is very similar in shape to the Púkel-men that guard the road to Dunharrow, in Rohan, but I have never seen one carved so small. It is said that those figures are carved in the likenesses of the Drúedain, the Woses, the Wild Men, and the forest in Taur Drúadan is home to similar statues of enormous size.

'If this is so, perhaps the Woses will be able to tell you from where this sculpture came, and aid in the cleansing of its evil. But take heed! They hate Orcs and everything touched by Orcs, and may not receive you kindly if they deem that heirloom has been ruined by Mordor and is beyond saving.''This carving is very similar in shape to the Púkel-men that guard the road to Dunharrow, in Rohan, but I have never seen one carved so small. It is said that those figures are carved in the likenesses of the Drúedain, the Woses, the Wild Men, and the forest in Taur Drúadan is home to similar statues of enormous size.

'If this is so, perhaps the Woses will be able to tell you from where this sculpture came, and aid in the cleansing of its evil. But take heed! They hate Orcs and everything touched by Orcs, and may not receive you kindly if they deem that heirloom has been ruined by Mordor, and is beyond saving.''You return to home of Wild Men. But now bad feeling come with you! Smells of gorgûn trick!'

You show Ghân-buri-Ghân the small statue, and he wrinkles his nose with distaste.

'This statue of Wild Man, but it has evil feeling. What is meaning of this? Well-made, by hand of skill, but poisoned by gorgûn or friends of gorgûn? Ghân-buri-Ghân cannot tell you more of this, but he have idea. Sculpture may be used to trick gorgûn. They think it theirs. Maybe use it as bait. Kill many gorgûn before they know. Speak to Khôr-buri-Ghân. He at Blood-cloak. He remembers his brother. He remember Bhai-buri-Ghân.

'Khôr-buri-Ghân always ready to kill gorgûn.''This statue of Wild Man, but it has evil feeling. What is meaning of this? Well-made, by hand of skill, but poisoned by gorgûn or friends of gorgûn? Ghân-buri-Ghân cannot tell you more of this, but he have idea. Sculpture may be used to trick gorgûn. They think it theirs. Maybe use it as bait. Kill many gorgûn before they know. Speak to Khôr-buri-Ghân. He at Blood-cloak. He remembers his brother. He remember Bhai-buri-Ghân.

'Khôr-buri-Ghân always ready to kill gorgûn.''${PLAYER}, what brings you to village of Wild Men? What is that?'

You show Khôr-buri-Ghân the small stone sculpture, and he marvels at it.

'This look like statues all around village and in forest, but different. Those feel like comfort, warm presence. This one ruined by cold feeling and hate! Father Ghân-buri-Ghân wants to use this to trick gorgûn? I think idea is good one. Gorgûn killed brother Bhai-buri-Ghân. If this carving of Wild Man can bring sadness to gorgûn, Khôr-buri-Ghân will be glad.

'Let us look for gorgûn at two places, north of the road by Drû Bhûta, and further north-east of there. Then we trick them with sculpture as bait!'We have not seen enough gorgûn for our plan. Maybe too close to village? It is hidden, and they not know where, but maybe they know here dangerous.

'I keep thinking about brother Bhai-buri-Ghân. He was eldest, and seemed very picture of Wild Man to me. Maybe that is why Khôr-buri-Ghân always try to do better. Wanted to prove something, maybe.

'Now Bhai-buri-Ghân only in memory. Wild Men supposed to think of dead only at Blood-cloak, but Khôr-buri-Ghân think of brother all the time.''Not enough gorgûn here for plan. Need many to make worth it!

'Bhai-buri-Ghân used to walk in woods alone, all the time. Sometimes asked Khôr-buri-Ghân to walk too, sometimes did not. Sometimes when asked, Khôr-buri-Ghân said no. He wishes now he had not said 'no' so many times.

'Need another place to look for more gorgûn. Deeper in woods. Maybe north-west.''Not enough gorgûn here for trap, but look!'

Khôr-buri-Ghân points across the water to a wooded island, sheltered in the tree-shadows.

'There cave on island, favourite place of Bhai-buri-Ghân! He asked Khôr-buri-Ghân to come along and see, but Khôr-buri-Ghân never go. Once he asked, twice he asked. Never asked third time, and now dead and cannot ask.

'Did feet of Khôr-buri-Ghân come here on purpose, but head not know? Speak again, ${PLAYER}, and together we seek for cave of Bhai-buri-Ghân.''Ready to go? Will get wet, but that all right to see favourite place of Bhai-buri-Ghân.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I will meet you at the Haven of Orladion, ${PLAYER}.''I know why you have come, ${PLAYER}. If you are to journey beneath the shroud that covers Gorgoroth, you will need the aid of a light that can pierce the darkness.'

Elrond takes the Memento of Bhai-buri-Ghân from you and dips it into the font beside him. When the artifact emerges from the glowing circle, light drips soundlessly from it and pools like water in the basin. You are reminded of the light in Khôr-buri-Ghân's eyes when he beheld the multitude of figures his brother had fashioned and left behind, a tangible mark of his time in the world; that light will never go out, for as long as Bhai-buri-Ghân is remembered. So too is the light that now shines in Elrond's hand, and when he speaks again his voice contains both determination and joy.

'With the Light of Eärendil, you will be able to walk where the shadows lie, and overcome them.'The guards standing watch and patrolling the fastness of Durthang are its first line of defence.Defeat Orc Guards throughout Durthang in Udûn.Defeat Mordor Warriors in Durthang (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Mordor Archers in Durthang (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many Orc Guards.CompletedThe guards standing watch and patrolling the fastness of Durthang are its first line of defence. Things would surely bode better for the forces of Gondor if that line were lowered.Things will surely go better for the forces of Gondor now that the number of guards in Durthang has been lowered.Ornate coffers can be found in Durthang; the possessions of Ugrukhôr's Black Númenórean retinue.Ransack the ornate cofffers throughout Durthang, in Udûn.Search ornate coffers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Searched many ornate coffers in Durthang.CompletedOrnate coffers can be found in Durthang; the possessions of Ugrukhôr's Black Númenórean retinue. Even if there is nothing of value to you in there, it is still best that they don't have it.You ransacked a number of the ornate coffers found in Durthang. You can say at least that the Black Númenóreans in Ugrukhôr's retinue will make no use of their contents.There are strange baubles, no doubt some tool of Ugrukhôr's Sorcerers, in the fastness of Durthang.Find and break any Sorcerer's baubles you can find in Durthang, in Udûn.Break sorcerer's baubles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Broke many sorcerer's baubles.CompletedYou found a strange bauble, no doubt some tool of Ugrukhôr's Sorcerers, in the fastness of Durthang. Surely that Sorcerer will be less powerful without it - or any others found here.You have broken many of Ugrukhôr's Sorcerer's baubles in the fastness of Durthang. Surely they will be less powerful without them.The Uruks and Sorcerers within the fastness of Durthang are like sharp talons and fell venom of Ugrukhôr's might.Defeat Uruk Enforcers and Black Númenórean Sorcerers in Durthang, in Udûn.Defeat Uruk Enforcers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Sorcerers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many enforcers and sorcerers.CompletedThe Uruks and Sorcerers within the fastness of Durthang are like sharp talons and fell venom of Ugrukhôr's might. They instill fear and obedience, the one by force, the other by subtler means.Ugrukhôr now has fewer sharp talons and venomous fangs to exert his might, thanks to you.In Durthang, strict order is maintained by guard captain Magtárb the Furious, head sorcerer Dâurazar Drear-star, and Khilnazt, his Uruk assassin.Defeat Magtárb the Furious, Dâurazar Drear-star, and Khilnazt the Silencer in Durthang, in Udûn.Defeat Dâurazar Drear-starDefeat Khilnazt the SilencerDefeat Magtárb the FuriousDefeated Ugrukhôr's three trusted lieutenants.CompletedThe fastness of Durthang would hardly hold to its strict order without three of the Captain of the Pit's most trusted lieutenants: the guard captain Magtárb the Furious, head sorcerer Dâurazar Drear-star, and Khilnazt, his Uruk assassin. Gondor will face a far less formidable threat when assailing Durthang without these three.Now that guard captain Magtárb the Furious, head sorcerer Dâurazar Drear-star, and Khilnazt the assassin have been defeated, Gondor will face a far less formidable threat when assailing Durthang.You found a fallen shield outside Haudh Nogbenn. Has someone been taken inside the barrow, and now needs your help?Has someone fallen into danger in Haudh Nogbenn?You have encountered a daring hobbit lass while searching for a way to cleanse the corruption from the sceptre that came out of Mordor.There is a tomb in Haudh Nogbenn. Is this the final resting place of the sceptre's owner?Examine the tomb in Haudh NogbennYour head is pounding.Listen to the words of the slain princeMarigold Boffin is in Haudh Nogbenn.'I will return to my home, and you will go on your way. Where are you going? Rivendell? My goodness, that sounds exciting!''I say, if it isn't ${PLAYER}! It is good to see that you have not abandoned the adventuring life! Is cousin Bingo with you? What a pity that he is not!

'I have been stomping all over the barrow-downs in search of excitement. I thought I heard the barking of a dog or a hound, and came to this barrow to search for it, but there is no sign of the curious beast. I hope it is all right! What brought you to this place, other than a Guardian's general desire to face danger?'

You tell Marigold that a sceptre infused with corruption came into your possession, and you believe that bringing it to this barrow may help cleanse it of its evil.

'Well, it is worth a try, ${PLAYER}! I see you have my shield there, so I will take it back, and let's go see if we can fix this sceptre!''Hullo there, are you an adventurer? My name is Marigold, and I have been stomping all over the barrow-downs in search of excitement. I thought I heard the barking of a dog or a hound, and came to this barrow to search for it, but there is no sign of the curious beast. I hope it is all right! What brought you to this place? I can see that you are a Guardian, so is it a general desire to face danger or is there something more to it?'

You tell the unusual hobbit lass that a sceptre infused with corruption came into your possession, and you believe bringing it to this barrow may help cleanse it of its evil.

'Well, it is worth a try! I see you have my shield there, so I will take it back, and let's go see if we can fix this sceptre!''What happened to you, ${PLAYER}? You were standing there and staring at the tomb for a really long time. I tried to snap you out of it, but you didn't answer until just now!'

The sceptre has been cleansed of its corruption, and you thank Marigold for her assistance. When you ask her what she thought of the shade of the Cardolan prince, she looks at you with complete and utter disbelief.

'Are you telling me the truth? I cannot BELIEVE this! There was a mysterious shade here and I MISSED it? What a disaster! It sounds as if you accomplished what you set out to do, which is good, but I am not very happy that I missed out on seeing any shades or princes. Next time, ${PLAYER}! Next time.''What happened to you? You were standing there and staring at the tomb for a really long time. I tried to snap you out of it, but you didn't answer until just now!'

The sceptre has been cleansed of its corruption, and you thank Marigold for her assistance. When you ask her what she thought of the shade of the Cardolan prince, she looks at you with complete and utter disbelief.

'Are you telling me the truth? I cannot BELIEVE this! There was a mysterious shade here and I MISSED it? What a disaster! It sounds as if you accomplished what you set out to do, which is good, but I am not very happy that I missed out on seeing any shades or princes.'You have unlocked a door in Khazad-dûm that has remained sealed for more than a thousand years.Greith is in the central chamber, by the tremendous geode.Talk to Greith in the central chamberGreith has asked you to search the room to the north of the Chamber of Machurful.Search the northern chamberGreith is in the central chamber, just north of the geode.Talk to Greith in the central chamber, just north of the geodeGreith has asked you to search the room to the east of the Chamber of Machurful.Search the rooms to the eastGreith is in the central chamber, just east of the geode.Talk to Greith in the central chamber, just east of the geodeGreith has asked you to search the room to the south of the Chamber of Machurful.Search the southern chamber and return to GreithGrotcleaver, a tremendous deep-claw, has made its home in the Chamber of Machurful.Defeat Grotcleaver<rgb=#FF0000>Greith must not be defeated</rgb>Greith survived the battle with Grotcleaver, and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Greith'The key no longer bears the corruption of Mordor! You are welcome to keep it, ${PLAYER}, as a memento of Thrótrun and her rune-keepers. The Chamber of Machurful will be locked no longer, in memory of the wrong that was done to them. Bring the key where you will, and may it serve you well!''I cannot believe what we have found, ${PLAYER}. This must be the Chamber of Machurful, long believed forever lost! It was a place for the keeping of powerful rune-stones and the secrets of their use, but none still living knew where to find it! Before Moria fell into ruin, the most talented of rune-keepers might have known its location, but they are all long dead.

'Ah, I understand. The door locks from the outside, for the secrets contained within the Chamber might be put to deadly use. Machurful must have been sealed when Durin died, and the key lost.''I found a tablet of stone by the geode, ${PLAYER}, and it bears writing on its face. It appears to be a list of rune-keepers who served in the Chamber of Machurful. None of the names are familiar to me, save one: Thrótrun.

'She was a rune-keeper of great renown, and many lessons of the art can be traced to her teachings. Thrótrun lived in Khazad-dûm during the time of Durin, but none knew of her fate. It was believed she perished during the fall of Moria, and it seems that is the case after all.'

Greith scratches at his beard.

'This list of rune-keepers has a solemn feel about it. The carving of runes takes time, even for a skilled artisan. I think this list was made to mark their deaths.''You found the skeletons of dwarves, but no Orc or goblin remains? I begin to worry that the rune-keepers fought among themselves, ${PLAYER}. By Durin's name I hope it is not so.

'I have found more of Thrótrun's writing, and it may help us to understand what happened here. It seems that the rune-keepers were sealed away in the Chamber for their own protection. None knew the extent of what was happening. They believed that the shaking in the earth would not last; they did not know Moria would fall into darkness for a thousand years.

'She writes of a dwarf named Orthvord, who took the key to Machurful and swore to keep it safe. He vowed he would return when the trouble had passed and let the rune-keepers out. But he never came back. Thrótrun writes that some of the others despaired, but she never told them what she believed in her heart: that Orthvord was slain, the key taken.

'She was right, and eventually the key came into evil hands, but the one who took it did not know the door it unlocked. The key was taken into Mordor, and the rune-keepers were forgotten.''Thrótrun and the rune-keepers could not leave the Chamber of Machurful, but I wonder if something managed to get in: an ancestor of the great beast that attacked us?'

Greith sighs heavily, and strokes his beard.

'Perhaps it would give them some joy, if they could see it, to know that the door was eventually opened. Would they be glad to know they are remembered, even too late? I hope so, ${PLAYER}. I hope so.'Nathlad has agreed to tell you what he knows about the rigging-hook you recovered from Mordor, but he wants you to help him with his work along the river.Nathlad has brought you down to the bridges that now cross the Anduin, above which stands the city of Osgiliath.Talk to Nathlad by the bridges that cross the River AnduinNathlad has ended his patrol and now stands before you, deep in thought.Talk to NathladNathlad has ended his patrol and now stands before you, deep in thought.Listen to the words of NathladNathlad has come to a conclusion.Talk to Nathlad'Goodbye, stranger. I will count you from this day forward a friend of mine, and of Dírharn.''When those soldiers of Gondor passed over the great bridge, heading east, they did not come down here to the water's edge. I am not surprised: their eyes are ever on the horizon, dreaming about the great things they plan to accomplish for the lord that gave them their orders. It falls to common folk like me and my fellows to take care of everything else.

'In this case, that means the Orcs that hid down here when the army passed by. We are going to take care of these stragglers. Come! I will tell you about that rigging-hook while we work, and you can tell me how you came by it.''It is now your turn, stranger. How did you come to find Dírharn's rigging hook?'

You tell Nathlad that it was found within a stronghold in Mordor, and you hoped to cleanse it of the corruption that ensnared it.

'In the depths of Mordor, you say? There is a story here, to be certain, for I watched him cast that rigging-hook into the Anduin not far from this very spot. Did an Orc or some other fell creature fish it from the river and bring it on dark paths into the Black Land? That is a better answer than the one I suspected, the one for which I led you down here.

'I thought to test your loyalties, and if you proved a friend to the Orcs I would have buried my sword in your back for the murder of my friend. But I think now you are what you claim to be.''I have also given up the free-booter's life, if you must know. Shortly after my captain left, I changed the name of the ship to Valatëa, and we are now mostly honest men. Mostly.

'Thank you for hearing me out, stranger. I think it did me some good to remember my former captain, and to speak of his choice. You are welcome to keep that rigging-hook, if you would desire it. It was named Anchor of the Past by my friend Dírharn, but it need not be such a weight for you. May it be a help to you in the journeys that lie ahead.'You have entered the Rhudauran Lost Temple in an attempt to cleanse the evil from the corrupted cup that came to you out of Mordor.There must be some way to get into the innermost chamber of the Lost Temple, where Elrond believes the corrupted cup might be redeemed.Fithákh, a cultist of the Corcur, has gained entrance to the innermost chamber of the Lost Temple. She seems to be expecting someone.Talk to Fithákh in the inner chamberFithákh asked you to set the cup on the tomb in the middle of the inner chamber, saying that it will serve as an altar for a sacrifice.Set the corrupted cup on the central tombYou have done what you set out to do, and now you can either leave the chamber with haste or put an end to Fithákh's villainy.'There you are! I must confess, you are not what I expected, but the Corcur need every ally they can get if we are to recover from such defeats as we have suffered. Do you have the artifact?'

Unsure what she might be expecting, you withdraw the corrupted cup from your pack and show it to her. She gasps.

'A cup of sacrifice! I did not dare to dream I would ever see one in the flesh! I was told you had one of Ferndúr's totems, but I never expected this! Set it on the tomb. It will serve as an altar, and perhaps I can find a beast with blood to serve. It has been so long since these walls have seen a proper sacrifice, and that is worth far more to me than a gaunt-lord's totem!''You have betrayed the Corcur! You are not who you claimed to be!

'I don't know what you did, but you have spoiled a priceless artifact of Rhudaur; it can no longer be used in sacrifice. You have ruined it forever! For that you will die, betrayer!'Rattleleaf has agreed to tell you the story of what he saw by the pool beneath Kar Lak. Will hearing his tale help you to cleanse the Earth-kin pouch of its corruption?Rattleleaf is by the still pool beneath Kar Lak, waiting to tell you his tale.Talk to Rattleleaf and hear his taleRattleleaf seems to have distracted himself, and needs reminding of the story he was going to tell.Talk to Rattleleaf and get him back on trackRhothlas has begun to tell you the tale of what he saw by the still pool.Listen to Rattleleaf's taleRattleleaf has told you the story of Trotsiv, unhappy though it was.Talk to RattleleafRattleleaf is by the pool beneath Kar Lak.Talk to Rattleleaf'He who remained could have given up his task, and lived. He did not, and did not, and now it has come to you. Hrum, hoom.''This was not long ago, by the counting of Ents, but you would feel otherwise. Years? Perhaps, yes, you would say years, but it feels much shorter to me.''Hoom, oh yes, you wanted to hear the tale of the one who remained behind. I am sorry. For an Ent, every detail is important to convey, but you smaller races do not drink deeply of every flavour in the same way.''The other creatures went away, but the first remained behind, and returned to the earth. Hoom, boom, toom.'

'I saw it all, but it is not the place of the Ents to interfere in the lives of other creatures. My own charge is to tend to the trees; I am not a shepherd of anything else. She who made the earth-kin would understand, hoom, boom, toom.'

Rattleleaf's eyes are deep pools, and you cannot be certain, but they seem to hold a deep sorrow.

'I know that this is so, root and branch... but... I also know that I have done wrong, little ${RACE}.''The earthborn and the earth-kin were given similar instructions, little ${RACE}. We may not be kindred, but we tend to the same concerns and our purposes are not opposed. The divide between us is a small one only.

'The divide between the earth-kin I witnessed was much wider, and it was caused by Angmar. Ah, that is a name malignant. Hrumty bum, hroomty boom! The seed-bag went to Angmar, but it did not stay there. They could not open it. Somehow it came to you, and it was sealed tight, and there was evil all around it.

'But now the pouch has been freed from the corruption that ensnared it. It is a gift from he who remained. Go now where you can best make use of his gift. I will stay here, and remember. Hroom, broom, toom!'Aric the Stone-speaker told you that the carving seems to be of Darothild, a champion who defied the Red-maid long ago, and was lost.You have come with some Eglain warriors to the Spring of the Red-maid. Here it may be that you can remove the corruption that encircles the carving of Darothild.Talk to Walgar at the Spring of the Red-maidYou have survived the onslaught of enemies at the Spring of the Red-maid, but now a mysterious figure has revealed herself.Defend Walgar from the Woman at the SpringYou know beyond any doubt: this is the shade of Darothild, who fell at the Spring of the Red-maid.Talk to Darothild's shadeDarothild's shade stands before you at the Spring of the Red-maid, where she lost her final battle.Ease Darothild's painDarothild's shade stands before you at the Spring of the Red-maid, where she lost her final battle.

Walgar has asked you to show the corrupted sculpture to Darothild's shade.Show the corrupted sculpture to Darothild's shadeWalgar is at the Spring of the Red-maid.Talk to Walgar at the Spring of the Red-maid'Where next will you go? To Rivendell? I wish you good fortune, my friend.''These ruins are curiously empty, ${PLAYER}. It feels to me as if some presence waits for us, holding its breath! Does the Red-maid live again, thinking to seize us and pull us beneath the foetid water?

'We all listened to the Stone-speaker's tale, and in the mid-day light of Ost Guruth this seemed like an idea with merit. If the sculpture you carry is truly a carving of the warrior-woman Darothild, bringing it to the site of her last battle might cure it of the corrupting ailment that surrounds it. But now that we are at the place, I am not as certain!

'Let us finish this, then, but stay on your guard! This place ended Darothild. I do not wish for it to signal our ends as well!''Stay back! I will not hesitate to put you in the ground! My spear has tasted the blood of so many, and for the defence of my people and my land it will drink deeply of yours, villain!'

Darothild falls silent as you begin to speak. You speak calmly, with quiet words, but each one cuts deeper than the one before it, deadlier than any of your other weapons. You tell of the stories that were passed down among the Eglain, stories about Darothild, the mighty champion who faced the Red-maid in defence of her people and never returned.'Where did you find this? I would know this carving anywhere, for it was a gift from my love. He begged me not to go. He gave me presents and gifts he carved from wood and stone and bone and tusk, but I refused them all. I carried my love for him to this place, and my spear, and nothing else.

'All else is clouded to me, but I can see his face more clearly than anything else in this shadowed world. He must be long dead now, my Bemwin.'

You tell Darothild that the Eglain have found many similar sculptures in the swamp-lands; Bemwin must have lived long enough to make many such carvings. She smiles sadly at you.'The story of Darothild was a sad one, ${PLAYER}, but it was told to children of the Eglain to impress upon them the importance of standing and fighting against evil, even if it is not easy. Especially if it is not easy.

'The carving has been cleansed of its corruption, and the shade of Darothild has escaped from her long captivity. Those are both worthy goals to have accomplished this day! I think she will be glad for you to hold onto her carved image, ${PLAYER}, especially if it will help you combat evils such as the Red-maid.

'Let us leave this place, and then I will bid you farewell. May the Image of the Champion serve you well!'You have come with Techeron to the Bridge of Ariant with the hope that the corrupted badge might be cleansed of its evil.The Ranger Techeron is at the Bridge of Ariant, in Annúminas.Talk to Techeron at the Bridge of AriantThe Ranger Techeron is near the Bridge of Ariant.Talk to Techeron'Safe journey to Rivendell, my friend.''Let us cross the bridge, and remain vigilant! The forces of Angmar crept back into the city while we were in the South-lands, and they may seek to surprise us.''The badge has been cleansed of its corruption? We have made proud whatever forgotten warden wore this badge in defence of his king, ${PLAYER}!

'I have thought of something as well. The guards of the city in days of old wore these badges to signify their posts, but I am grateful that we have not maintained that tradition. If the Steadfast Sisters were consigned to their own posts, they would not have come here to Ariant when they did, and Olcránnag might have escaped to work further evil. Truthfully, there is no man save Elessar alone who might be able to command the Sisters and have them heed it, but still I am glad that wardens and Rangers go where they are needed, not where they are assigned to be.

'Where next will you go? To Rivendell, to speak with Elrond? Give him my regards, for I must stay here and hold the line against Angmar.'Umín has returned with you to the forsaken ship where the Spirit of the Necklace was defeated, hoping to aid you in cleansing the corruption from the necklace you found in Mordor, and perhaps to find some measure of peace for himself.Umín stands outside the docked ship, waiting to speak with you.Talk to UmínOthers have come to the lost ship for some unknown purpose, and you should learn what they intend.Investigate the forsaken shipMal Stackwort, former minion of Bill Ferny, leads a crew of cutthroats intending to free the forsaken ship from its trapped berth.Defeat Mal Stackwort's crew of cutthroatsMal Stackwort has no idea of the meaning behind Umín's words, and has asked you to explain them.Talk to Mal StackwortUmín's words have had an effect on Mal Stackwort, but whether they will last remains to be seen.Remain on your guardUmín has collected the necklace from where it lay, and he wishes to speak with you about it.Talk to Umín'Where next will you go? To Rivendell? I wish you good fortune, my friend.''See? It is just as I said: whatever force gave life to the traps within these caverns has fled. Was it the Spirit of the Necklace, or some other power? Whatever it might have been, it is gone now.

'But I hear voices ahead. Who else has come to this forsaken ship?''Well? Why does this fellow say I shouldn't take this ship and go a-pirating?'

You briefly describe Umín's experience with the Spirit of the Necklace, which culminated with the shade seizing control of the poor adventurer's body and turning him against his allies on this very ship's deck, before the spirit was finally defeated and Umín freed from its influence.

'He can't have been completely freed, can he?' Mal muses wryly. 'He came back here, didn't he? Are you certain this spirit was truly defeated? It sounds powerful.''I'll think about it, but I make no promise that you will not see me again, Brawler.''I know not how this necklace came to the land of Mordor, ${PLAYER}, but I am certain now that this is the very same artifact that caused me so much trouble and grief on the deck of this very ship. What servant of evil could have brought it to that place, and for what purpose? I cannot say, but this I know: the Spirit of the Necklace is well and truly gone. I feel its absence as strongly as I feel the wooden deck beneath my feet.

'Gone too is the corruption that enveloped this necklace as a result of its stay in the Black Land. I think it is fitting that you take it now, and make whatever use of it you deem right. It no longer has any hold upon me, and I am left in peace at last. I hope Mal Stackwort and his fellows will find freedom from their own controlling spirit, Man though he be.'

You thank Umín for the gift of the necklace, and tell him you believe Elrond of Rivendell will be able to imbue it with some force for Good.

'I hope so, my friend. Go to Rivendell with my good wishes.'Eldmath has run into some trouble while out in the field, and now he has been captured by Orcs. Even worse, he was holding onto the corrupted tusk-shard when he was taken!Tharbag's Orcs have captured Eldmath, and you really should free him... he was holding onto the corrupted tusk-shard when he was taken!Talk to TharbagThe Orcs that captured Eldmath belong to the Boar-tribe and are led by Tharbag.Remain alert and look for an opportunity to free EldmathSkámash has been left in charge of dealing with you. This will likely prove to be a mistake!Talk to SkámashGris-shâtroful is to the east, and you might be able to drop down from above and surprise the Orcs that captured Eldmath.Sneak into Gris-shâtroful from the backYou have found Eldmath, but the Orcs were alerted to your presence.Survive the encounter with TharbagEldmath is in Gris-shâtroful, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Eldmath'I will return to the tavern, there to add my new verse. Where will you go? Rivendell? I have never heard of it, is it far from here? Safe travels, ${PLAYER}!''The Boar-tribe has lurked in the shadows for too long! With you two as captives, we will at last be seen as the power we deserve!''Curse that Tharbag, leaving me to deal with you! He is afraid to fight you, I can tell! Pah!'

A cunning light creeps into Skámash's eyes.

'But if I slay you, that will prove I am stronger than Tharbag, and that lot will see I am fit to be the boss, and he is not! Ah, Tharbag, you fool! You have given me just what I need to take your place as leader of the Boar-tribe!''Well-done, ${PLAYER}! I would say both of us have proven to be heroes this day, wouldn't you? To think that this shard came from the tusk which gave King Folca his mortal wound... and in its own way it proved the undoing of the Boar-tribe Orcs who worshipped it! I think Folca would be very pleased about that, ${PLAYER}.

'It is an heirloom of Rohan, but you are welcome to keep it. I have had enough of adventuring, for now, and will return to Aldburg. Perhaps I will add this verse to the songs I sing about King Folca and his campaign against the Orcs. Yes, I will do just that!'Something is afoot in the the village of the Lossoth, and Lothrandir fears that the corruption that infuses the brooch may have done some harm to the widow to whom he returned it.Lothrandir is in the village of Sûri-kylä.Talk to Lothrandir in Sûri-kyläA Lossoth warrior has news of Pinja's whereabouts in Sûri-kylä.Talk to the Lossoth warrior in Sûri-kyläA Lossoth warrior claims that Pinja's dead husband Oiva has returned to life. How can this be?Find Pinja and learn what has happenedThe widow Pinja is outside her home, in Sûri-kylä.Talk to Pinja in front of her homePinja told you that whatever has taken her husband Oiva's shape is currently unconscious behind her home.Examine the unconscious body behind Pinja's homeA spirit has masqueraded as Pinja's slain husband for its own cruel jest, but you have now revealed it.Remain alert for any further treachery by HamaitoHamaito has revealed himself and seeks to avenge the spoiling of his jest.Defeat HamaitoHamaito has been defeated, and the corruption of Oiva's bone brooch has been cleansed.Talk to PinjaLothrandir is in Sûri-kylä.Talk to Lothrandir in Sûri-kylä'Farewell for now, my friend!''Quickly, ${PLAYER}! The widow Pinja lives to the south-west, and if the brooch has worked some evil upon her that is where we must go!''I have, and she has never been better! Her husband Oiva has returned! All thought him dead, but he lives still and has come back to the village! I was just going to spread the glad news!

'Pinja is no longer a widow, for her Oiva is alive!''Do you think I would not know my own husband? Oiva was mine, and when the ice claimed him it was as if a piece of me was taken too. I do not know what thing has taken his shape and pretends to be him, but it is not my husband. It is not my Oiva.

'I clubbed it over the head and left it behind my home. You can see it if you like.'You never met Oiva, but this does appear to be a warrior of the Lossoth. As you study the still form, you feel a sharp pain behind your eyes and a throbbing in your head.'The spirit Hamaito was to blame for the deaths of our missing warriors, and he would have done injury to all of the Lossoth if he could. I am glad that you and Lothrandir aided me in casting him back to nothing.

'For the aid you rendered to me, I give to you Oiva's brooch. I do not need it to remember my husband. I deem that the evil with which it attracted Hamaito attached itself to him as he carried out his jest. It went with him into the far reaches when we cast him out.

'May Oiva's brooch of bone help you as you adventure, and render your aid on behalf of those who need it. Not all people are Lossoth, and will have more need of you than we!''I should have known that Hamaito was clouding my mind, ${PLAYER}. In the dungeons of Isengard I resisted Saruman's torments, but there I knew of the Wizard's tricks. I knew to resist them! The corruption that affixed to the brooch in Mordor drew Hamaito to it, and he hid his lesser evil in that greater one, and was masked therein.

'That the corruption went with Hamaito when he was cast out is useful to us; in that way he did us a good turn! Perhaps that is the greatest jest of all.

'Farewell for now, my friend! Give my regards to Elrond in Rivendell, and I hope he is able to infuse the brooch with a force for Good, as Mithrandir believes. Until we meet again!'Raiglud has a story to tell, and perhaps hearing it will help you to cleanse the needle of the corruption that surrounds it.Raiglud, the oldest woman in Lhanuch, has begun to tell you a tale from her childhood.Talk to RaigludRaiglud is telling you a tale from her childhood.Listen to Raiglud's storyRaiglud has told you she needs to rest a moment, for the telling of this tale is taking a lot out of her.Consider Raiglud's description of DeilurConsider Raiglud's description of MaushkánConsider Raiglud's description of herself as a childRaiglud is ready to continue her tale.Talk to Raiglud and listen to the rest of her storyRaiglud is telling you a tale from her childhood.Listen to Raiglud's storyRaiglud has told her story.Talk to Raiglud'I give the needle to you. Bear it with you on your journey, and remember Deilur!''Before the Uch-lûth came to be Algraig, our home was in the south, in Dunland. I was very young then, and I spent my days climbing the hills and scaring the birds with my shouting. I was a fiery child, and the elders ran out of ways to discipline me. Then they sought to beseech my better nature, hoping I might calm somewhat and learn the art of the tailor.

'That did not work either, so they gave it up, and let me do as I would. That was the smartest thing they might have done! Ha! Ha!'You can almost see Deilur standing defiantly before the Orc raiding party, surrounded by the bodies of her friends and her family among the Uch-lûth. She has little chance of surviving this encounter, but Raiglud's description of her is so vivid that she seems almost to be standing before you, hale and hearty.

She has nothing that might be called a weapon, but she does grip a needle of bone in one hidden fist.Maushkán has already slain many members of Raiglud's tribe this day, but he is eager to kill even more. You can almost see the rage and hatred in his eyes, and smell his foul stench. He leads a raiding party of Orcs, and they follow him out of fear and a desire for treasure.It is hard to believe that Raiglud could ever have been young, but you know that it must have been true, as it is for all people. You can almost see her, hidden by a mound of grass and hay, as her fear of Maushkán and his band wars with her anger at what they have done to her people.'Yes, I am ready to continue. Sometimes words must travel through darkness to reach the light. We are quite in the darkness now.''And now you have come to my village, and you bear a needle so like those used by Deilur those many years ago. You say that it came from a place of evil, and the feeling of it is so corrupt that I do not question it. I can tell you the story of that needle, for now I know its ending.

'Maushkán was made weak by Deilur's needle, and it caused him a great deal of pain, but it was sunk deep into his flesh and he could not remove it. He returned in time to his own land, but every day was a torment, for the needle pained him so. He must have learned to live with the pain, but every sharp twinge was a reminder of Deilur's defiance. There was no day he did not think of Deilur, and in that way he was very like me.

'But while I am now grown old, the oldest woman in Lhanuch, I think Maushkán did not reach such an age. There came a day when he succumbed, at last, to the pain of Deilur's needle, and when his body was gone only the needle remained. Perhaps some villain picked it up, never knowing what it signified, or any of its history, and it came by long roads to you, and then to me.

'This needle travelled many leagues to have its vengeance upon Maushkán, but now its task is done. Farewell, Deilur! You have been avenged, and this old woman remembers you and what you did, and always will. And now ${PLAYER} will as well.'You have come with Khôr-buri-Ghân to the cave that was his brother Bhai-buri-Ghân's favourite place.Khôr-buri-Ghân is inside the cave that was his brother's favourite place.Talk to Khôr-buri-Ghân inside the caveKhôr-buri-Ghân is inside the cave that was his brother's favourite place.Talk to Khôr-buri-Ghân inside the caveAn Orc has found Bhai-buri-Ghân's cave.Descend to the lower chamber of the cave and confront the OrcThe Orc Turkalb has found Bhai-buri-Ghân's favourite cave.Confront TurkalbKhôr-buri-Ghân is in his brother's favourite cave.Help protect Khôr-buri-GhânKhôr-buri-Ghân is inside the cave that was his brother's favourite place.Talk to Khôr-buri-Ghân inside the cave'Always be friend to Wild Men. Always be friend to Khôr-buri-Ghân.''This cave favourite place of Bhai-buri-Ghân? Do not know why. Too damp for Khôr-buri-Ghân.

'What special about cave?''This more statues than in village or in forest. So many different sizes. Bhai-buri-Ghân come here often. This carving place? Brother Bhai-buri-Ghân carve sculpture here? Maybe help carve some of these, too?'

Khôr-buri-Ghân suddenly freezes, and points silently down towards the lower chamber.

'Gorgûn,' he whispers.'What a relief! It's just a ${RACE}! There are rumours about these statues. They say they come to life when you aren't looking at them, and have slain whole encampments!

'The war hasn't gone as the bosses wanted, and I thought I'd slip away and find my own place to hide out until everything blows over. And this cave would be perfect if it wasn't for these horrible statues! I think I'll do what I can to smash them to pieces, and then this will be a perfect hideout for Turkalb.''Statue carved by Bhai-buri-Ghân no longer have feel of gorgûn. Khôr-buri-Ghân give memento of brother to you, ${PLAYER}. Maybe will help on road you walk, when evil about. Khôr-buri-Ghân not need memento to remember Bhai-buri-Ghân. Will remember whenever come to favourite place.

'Always be friend to Wild Men. Always be foe to gorgûn. Wind in the trees, water in the lake. Now you take memento far away, use for good. Remember Bhai-buri-Ghân, remember Khôr-buri-Ghân.'You have found Captain Lachír. He, in turn, has found ingots of metal he believes are imbued with great power, and ordered his men to gather all they could find.Talk to Captain Lachír while you wait for his men to return.Talk to Captain LachírHelp clean off the encrusted object in front of Captain Lachír.Clean off the artifactWait with Captain Lachír for his men to return.Wait with Captain LachírSomething has come over Captain Lachír's men and they are attacking without regard.Defend yourselves from Captain Lachír's menThere is a pause in the onslaught. Captain Lachír wishes to speak.Talk to Captain LachírMore of Captain Lachír's men are attacking.Defend yourselves once more'Ah, and then there is this artifact! It is something else, entirely! It looks almost crusted over intentionally. Help me clear it off and we shall see what lies beneath.'When you meet Lachír's eyes in the light, there is a wildness behind a mask of bravery. It is fear.

'Have all my men turned on me? Can I have pushed them too far in search of something powerful, something truly magnificent? Can sullen resentment - or greed, greed and jealousy have driven them to such ends?

'But what am I saying? Did you see their faces? It was madness, madness and not mutiny written there, plain as day.'

The captain falls silent abruptly, brooding.A covert meeting between the forge-workers and the Easterling contingent might just yield a way out of Udûn.The meeting is beginning.Watch and listenThe meeting is underway. The forge-workers are presenting their case.Watch and listenIt seems that Kurtil wants to whisper something to you.Talk to KurtilThe meeting is underway. The two sides do not yet see eye to eye.Watch and listenThings are not going well. Kurtil has motioned you over.Talk to KurtilThe meeting is underway. Can Gradrágh salvage the situation?Watch and listenGradrágh has beckoned you over.Talk to GradrághGradrágh asked you to pull down the meeting flag, as it is no longer needed and only draws attention to the covert gathering.Take down the meeting flagThe meeting is underway. Can Gradrágh salvage the situation?Watch and listenWhat menacing voice spoke those words? See who approaches.See who approachesDefeat the Rogmul RúthragonDefeat Grúshat the ThrasherThe enforcer and Rogmul are defeated, but the parley is lost beyond hope.All hope is lost'It goes, it goes! You and I listen and watch, yes? Let others do the talking. That is best.''Fah! This is not the way. No, no.'

But rather than speak, Kurtil kicks Overseer Gradrágh in the foot. The two meet eyes and the overseer clears his throat.'${PLAYER}, why is the meeting flag still raised? We are all here. It draws attention. I do not like it.

'Go, pull it down.'Soot-smudged pages can be found scattered around Udûn. Perhaps you can complete the story if you find them all.Soot-smudged pages can be found scattered around Udûn.

You should collect the pages to complete the story.Collect Page 1Collect Page 2Collect Page 3Collect Page 4Collect Page 5Collect Page 6Collect Page 7Collect Page 8Collect Page 9Collect Page 10Collect Page 11Collect Page 12Collect Page 13Collect Page 14Collect Page 15Collect Page 16Completed.CompletedNarthavron can be found at the Udûn Foothold.

You should talk to Narthavron.Talk to NarthavronYou've found the page of a journal, written in an Elvish script, and ripped from its binding. It has small, sooty handprints on it. Perhaps you can find other pages around Udûn and complete the story.'Ah, yes I do know of these pages, for I am the author! One of the Captain's spies made off with this journal when we first arrived, and I was only able to recover the binding. The pages were nowhere to be found. It is truly miraculous that you were able to find them!

'Here, allow me to re-bind this tome for you. You may keep it for your efforts, and as a warning. We must never again allow evil to regain its footing here, lest it will fester and grow.'You have gathered the pages, but the binding is still missing, and the author still unknown. You recall Narthavron at the Udûn Foothold mentioned fighting in the War of the Last Alliance; perhaps he knows more about these pages.The Sorcerers in Rhûd Vorn are a corrupting and malign force, dangerous to the Easterlings and Gondor alike.Defeat Easterling Sorcerers in Rhûd Vorn.Defeat Sorcerers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Easterling Sorcerers in Rhûd Vorn.CompletedThough the Easterling Men may step back from their alliance with Evil, now that the influence of Sauron has been dispelled, their Sorcerers among them remain deadly and zealous in dark dealings. Those within Rhûd Vorn therefore pose a threat within and without.You defeated a number of sorcerers in Rhûd Vorn, ridding a corrupting and perilous threat from the Easterlings and from Gondor alike.When you have completed all you wish to do in Udûn you should speak to Gimli and Ingold about Dor Amarth.The Ruins of Díngarth is located near Udûn in Dor Amarth.

You should travel to the Ruins of Díngarth.Travel to the Ruins of Díngarth in Dor AmarthGimli and Ingold can be found at the Ruins of Díngarth in Dor Amarth.

When you have completed all you wish to do in Udûn you should speak to Gimli and Ingold.Talk to IngoldTalk to Gimli'You have been a great help, in the wake of Lachír's folly. You have helped lead us to conquer what resistance and pitfalls we have encountered here in Mordor.

'Some of our company has pressed through, past the gates of Carach Angren, and into Dor Amarth. When you have accomplished all that you wish to do here with us in Udûn, those in Dor Amarth could use your aid.''We have managed to push through Udûn with only a small resistance. Now the task before us is to see what remains of Sauron's citadel, Barad-dûr. I am glad you are with us.''It is good to be in the thick of things again, isn't it ${PLAYER}. It was odd to see dwarves in Udûn. I did not realize any lived in Mordor! Do you suppose there are more of my lost kin in these lands?'According to Coldan the look-out, Captain Lachír and his search party entered the great forge of Anglach and have not yet come out.You should approach the great forges of Anglach in Udûn, and search for a way in.Approach AnglachSearch for Captain Lachír and his men within the great forges of Anglach, in Udûn.Search for Lachír in AnglachSearch for Captain Lachír and his men within the great forges of Anglach, in Udûn.Search for Lachír in AnglachSearch for Captain Lachír and his men within the great forges of Anglach, in Udûn.Search for Lachír in AnglachSearch for Captain Lachír and his men within the great forges of Anglach, in Udûn.Search for Lachír in AnglachFollow the voices of Men within the great forges of Anglach, in Udûn.Find LachírYou should talk to Captain Lachír now that you have found him in the lower levels of Anglach, in Udûn.Talk to Captain Lachír'Have you guessed it? It was into Anglach that Captain Lachír led the search party. He felt that for certain, that miraculous treasure he had become fixated on must lie within. I have watched for the better part of a day and still they have not returned. Does that mean they have found the treasure? I am unsure. I - I do not like lingering on the thought.

'If you seek him, that is where you are bound, too. As for me, I will take your good council and make straight for the Foothold!''I have found it at last. Not, I confess, as I imagined it, a bright sword or luminous pendant. I sought high and low for those, in vain. But these ingots, why, they are heavy with power. They are hot to the touch! Think what a sword forged from this metal might be. With the Enemy's own works shall I bow and break his kingdom!'You have found Captain Lachír. He, in turn, has found ingots of metal he believes are imbued with great power, and ordered his men to gather all they could find.You should talk to Captain Lachír in Anglach, in Udûn, to learn what will happen next.Talk to Captain LachírReturn to Mulnír at the FootholdReturn to Mulnír'We found quite a store of these, tucked away. I knew the moment I laid sight on them what they were. They are heavy. I have set my men to bearing them here. They have made many trips already. When we have all of it, we will rig a pulley to get it up.

'You see? I have it all worked out. At last, everything is falling into place.''My men should be here any moment with more ingots. Wait with me. You will see!'You tell Mulnír all that took place in Anglach, of Lachír's growing fixation and of his frenzied end. 

After a long moment, Mulnír nods.

'It is worse than I feared. Worse by far. But I will tell you this, ${CLASS}: I believe that flaw was always in the Captain's character. Udûn was but the flood that showed us the weakness in the flood-wall.

'You did all you could - more than most men would be capable of. We are in a hostile land and peril is ever at a soldier's heels. Only this - this was an awful sort of peril, was it not?'The Enforcers of Ugrukhôr, Captain of the Pit, can be found throughout Udûn.Defeat Ugrukhôr's Enforcers you encounter in Udûn.Defeat Enforcers of Ugrukhôr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many of the Captain of the Pit's Enforcerers in Udûn.CompletedThe Enforcers of Ugrukhôr, Captain of the Pit, can be found throughout Udûn, striking fear and carrying out his will wherever they go.Fewer of Ugrukhôr's Enforcers will spread fear and carry out his will in Udûn thanks to you.The meeting between the discontent forge-workers and the Easterlings has been arranged, the meeting place prepared, and the flag raised. Now for the meeting to begin.You should talk to Kurtil, who has arrived at the meeting place in Maudhúl Ghûg.Talk to KurtilYou should return to Barathron in the Foothold in Udûn and tell him all about the disastrous meeting and the hopes that died there.Return to Barathron'They will all soon be here. Will it go well? We have done so much, have we not? Ah, but who can say! Still, we must wait, yes? Wait and try.

'Soon they will be here. Wait with me, ${CLASS}. Not long now. Not long.''Stand here. Wait with me. I will tell you more of the signs shared among our kind.'

Kurtil tells you more.  Time passes.'You return! You have been gone long. What do you know of the flags? What secret plans do the lurkers plan for us?'

You begin to tell him all that happened and his face goes slack.

'Captain of the Pit! And you know not what became of that Nurnhoth, Kurtil? He simply slipped off. It is all so peculiar. Nor did I ever imagine, friend, that I should feel sympathy for the bangers and the clangers here that armed the Enemy's armies against us. All suffered under the Dark Lord's reign, that much is clear! 

'Now, at least, we know the name of our true adversary in this place. Perhaps with time we may earn the trust of those who fled and lived and with their help work against him.'Scattered all over eastern Dor Amarth, survivors of the Mordor-host have built camps among the ruins of Barad-dûr.Orcs encampments can be found around Dor Amarth.

You should destroy any Orc tents that you find.Destroy Orc tents in Dor Amarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroyed many Orc tents in Dor Amarth.CompletedAfter the collapse of Barad-dûr, a torrent of debris has scattered all over eastern Dor Amarth. Those who survived the Mordor-host have built camps among these ruins and rubble. You should destroy their encampments before the Orc numbers begin to organize.You have destroyed many Orc tents in Dor Amarth on your conquests through Gorgoroth.In Dor Amarth, the toppled ruin of Barad-dûr still looms over the wracked plains of Mordor, casting its shadows over unseen foes.The causeway extends to the south-east from the Isenmouthe, eventually leading to the foot of Barad-dûr.

Legolas has asked you to scout the causeway leading to the ruins of Barad-dûr as he desires to learn what foes still lurk in the shadows of its toppled heights.Scout the causeway to ruins of Barad-dûrThe fallen dwarf and his goat can be found to the north of the causeway to Barad-dûr.

You have found a fallen dwarf and his goat. You should examine them and collect his shield.Examine the fallen dwarfExamine the fallen dwarf's goatCollect the fallen dwarf's shieldGimli can be found at the Ruins of Díngarth due south of the Isenmouthe in Dor Amarth.

You have found evidence of dwarves in Mordor and collected one of their shields. You should bring the shield to Gimli and see what he makes of it.'Gimli and I have not ventured overfar into these lands, my friend, and I am not certain I wish to. The Men of Gondor call this place Dor Amarth; the Doomfold in the Common Speech.

'Its name was wisely chosen by those Men, for I feel a deep shadow looming over us even so far from the ruins of the Dark Tower. Gimli has found old tools of dwarf-make in this ruin, and though he will not speak of it, unease has crept into his mind. If ever dwarves dwelt within Mordor, it seems their fate was not a pleasant one and that their line is long ended.

'This causeway forks some distance to the south. One path leads southward and appears less well-kept by the Orcs, while the other rises and continues east to Barad-dûr. I would ask that you follow the causeway south to the fork and then beyond to the east. Search for foes or those still held as slaves, and perhaps we shall find something to raise Gimli's spirits and draw his thoughts from those of his distant kin.

'Do not stray far from the road, my friend, for we have much still to learn of the hidden evils of Mordor.''What is this?'

You tell Gimli of the slain dwarf you found and offer him the shield.

'By your description of his wounds, the dwarf you encountered must have been caught by a stray stone thrown from the depths of Mount Doom. A poor dwarf, indeed, but not one slain by malice. The Dark Lord must not have brought my kin to ruin after all!

'Ah-ha! Look at this, my friend. This shield is newly-forged... and crafted by skilled hands!

'So dwarves still dwell in Mordor, but where have they made their home?'You have found a small stone, carved to a specific shape and with strange, grotesque symbols etched upon it.Search for more such stones and try to determine their purpose within Anglach in Udûn.Try to determine the strange stones' purpose within Anglach in Udûn.The door you opened held a great fire grim within it!Defeat Gúrflak the fire grimDefeated Gúrflak the ancient fire grim.CompletedYou have found a small stone, carved to a specific shape and with strange, grotesque symbols etched upon it. Are there others? And what is their purpose?Locked by old magic behind a great door in the very roots of Anglach was the fire grim Gúrflak, whose ages-old pent up fury roils there no longer.Zôreth, Mistress of Lûghash, has sent the Stout-axes in her service to seek treasures that once belonged to the Dark Lord.Stout-axe expeditions can be found throughout Dor Amarth. 

Spakorth has asked you to seek the remaining Stout-axe expeditions in Dor Amarth. These expeditions can be found among the rocks and crevices to the south of the road to Lûghash. 

You should tell any Stout-axes you encounter to return to Lûghash.Spakorth can be found outside the lower gates to Lûghash in Dor Amarth.

You have recalled all the Stout-axes you could find. You should meet with Spakorth outside Lûghash.Talk to Spakorth at Lûghash'The tale of the Stout-axes is not a pleasant one, friend, but I would share it with you.

'Once we were Drása's Folk—a great kingdom of dwarves, but no longer, and it is only now that Sauron is gone that I dare to utter the name of the Father of our folk. Early in this Age, Sauron ordered us to return the Great Ring of Power he granted to our folk when still we dwelt in the East. The Dark Lord sought for it some unknown purpose, and when our King of old denied him his prize, Sauron summoned forth a vast army and razed our ancestral home and stripped the golden halls of Usk-tóga. Many of our clan were slain, and those few who survived were taken as thralls to Mordor to serve the Dark Lord. We were made to forsake our clan name of old and swear ourselves to Sauron, and so we became the Khirvísa: the Stout-axes of Mordor.

'Yet, throughout our long centuries of toil, we held to the old ways and the line of our Kings of old remained unbroken. We were slaves, yes, but some measure of who we were endured and we made for ourselves a small realm of our own in secret within the lands of Mordor. But Sauron knew all too well that which we had hoped to conceal from him....

'Seventy-eight years ago, the Stout-axes were unmade. In a rage, the Dark Lord returned to Mordor from lands unknown and imprisoned our King and countless other Stout-axes in the dungeons of Barad-dûr. What little freedoms we had were taken from us, and even the lowliest Orcs came to regard us as mere vermin. In the absence of our King, I and many others remained to toil in Lûghash, and whenever Zôreth was not summoned to Barad-dûr, she too dwelt within Lûghash.

'Now Sauron is gone, but still we serve - hated as we are now in the eyes of all in Mordor. After the fall of the Dark Lord, none but the Mistress of Lûghash, Zôreth, deigned to offer the rest of our kin shelter and keep us from the Orcs. In our desperation, it would seem we have traded one master for another, but we are too far from the Black Gate and escape now seems no more possible now than it did when Sauron ruled Mordor. And to where would we even escape? Zôreth desires the weapons and trinkets of the Dark Lord above all else, but what portion of his might remains in them I cannot say. We have sought them nearly every day since his fall, but every search has come to naught.

'Days we have searched now, and with little food or rest. It is time for my kin to return to Lûghash, even if Mistress Zôreth does not wish it.

'There are four other expeditions about in this land, ${PLAYER}. Find them, and send them home to Lûghash!

'If you wish to meet my folk, you shall meet us all!''I am saddened to learn that some of my folk fell into the fires of the mountain. It seems even keeping a careful pace is no longer enough....

'The tremors have grown more powerful since Sauron's fall, and new cracks form in the earth with each passing day. It is challenging to keep a steady footing when you cannot trust that the ground shall remain whole beneath you! We shall have to be more careful the next time the Mistress sends us out in search of whatever it is we're meant to find.

'For those that have returned, they shall enjoy a long-awaited rest. You have done my folk a great service, ${PLAYER}.'Zôreth, Mistress of Lûghash, has bound foul spirits to many of the intact watching-stones in Dor Amarth and now they keep watch on her servants and foes alike.Zôreth's watching-stones can be found along the causeway to Lûghash and Barad-dûr in Dor Amarth.

Spakorth has asked you to challenge Zôreth's watching-stones and drive out the evil spirits bound to them.Spakorth can be found outside the lower gates to Lûghash in Dor Amarth.

You have forced Zôreth's fell spirits from the watching-stones, but some appear to have returned to her keeping in Lûghash. You should meet with Spakorth and tell him what has happened.Talk to Spakorth at Lûghash'Lûghash rises to the east, nestled within a rocky hill atop a deep valley. Alas, the Stout-axes are not the only ones in service of the Mistress of Lûghash....

'You see, Mistress Zôreth has grown emboldened since the fall of the Dark Lord, and she has drawn many vile spirits into the watching-stones of this land.

'Through their eyes, both servants and foes are revealed... and their designs exposed. She is distrustful, even of her own kin, and so she instead wields her spirits as servants and spies. In this way, She seeks to find the resting places of the Dark Lord's most powerful trinkets. We Stout-axes think it a foolish search and we would have no part of it... were the choice ours!

'If you wish to come to Lûghash, you must challenge Her Watching-stones and banish the spirits from them! If you do not, she shall soon know of you and Gimli!

'When it is done and Her spirits have been cast back to the shadows, I shall meet you at the lower gates of Lûghash!''Some of the spirits fled into Lûghash, you say? That is no good, no good at all....

'It is certain that Mistress Zôreth shall wish to speak with me about what her spirits saw, so it shall be my task to convince her that no threat comes to the Flaming Spire.

'I doubt she shall protest when I tell her that we drove you and your armies from Mordor! Ha-ha!'Elrond has imbued the artifact that came out of Mordor with the Light of Eärendil and gave you a powerful gift.Mithrandir is outside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir outside the Black Gate'Eärendil was a mariner, ${PLAYER}, and he braved storm and roaring wave; he bore shining light out of the darkness. He voyaged through sadness and woe, cresting the waves of his sorrow and braving the winds of wrath to bring hope from afar. He travels still upon his errand, not upon the seas of the world but those of heaven, the light of the Silmaril on his brow showing the way.'

Elrond falls silent and gazes up at the sky, remembering distant memories.

'For all the Eldar, the light of Eärendil's star is counted most precious. It is a sign of hope for all who labour against the Great Enemy or the multitude of his servants. For me it is something more.

'Eärendil was a mariner, but he was also my sire. When I look to the stars, I remember much that has now gone beyond the circles of the world, and the joy of memory is tinged with the sorrow of loss. I give you this gift so you may bear the light of Eärendil with you, and it will be a shining lamp in the darkness.''For all the Eldar, the light of Eärendil's star is counted most precious. It is a sign of hope for all who labour against the Great Enemy or the multitude of his servants. For me it something more.

'Eärendil was a mariner, but he was also my sire. When I look to the stars, I remember much that has now gone beyond the circles of the world, and the joy of memory is tinged with the sorrow of loss. I give you this gift so you may bear the light of Eärendil with you, and it will be a shining lamp in the darkness.''Ah, you have cleansed the corruption from the artifact that Ayorzén brought from Durthang. I never doubted you would be equal to the task, ${PLAYER}.

'The Light of Eärendil will serve you well within Gorgoroth. I deem that few of the commanders of which Ayorzén spoke will be able to endure its presence, and none will enjoy it. It will bring them discomfort and aid you in piercing the shadow.

'It is time to press further into Mordor.'Now that you have acquired the Light of Eärendil, Mithrandir has asked you to press further into Mordor.The Isenmouthe is the gate between Udûn and Dor Amarth, and it is built into the fortress of Carach Angren.Travel to the Isenmouthe in the fortress of Carach AngrenBrungos is on the other side of the Isenmouthe, in Dor Amarth.Pass through the Isenmouthe and find Brungos in Dor AmarthBrungos is in a small ruined settlement in Dor Amarth.Talk to Brungos in Dor AmarthFierce enemies stalk the Doomfold of Dor Amarth, and Brungos asked for your help defeating them.Defeat enemies in Dor Amarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Brungos is in a small ruined settlement in Dor Amarth.Talk to Brungos in Dor AmarthCovadil went north-east in Dor Amarth, intending to see Lûghash.Find Covadil near Lûghash, in Dor AmarthThe Gondorian scholar Covadil is near the Spire of Lûghash, in Dor Amarth.Talk to Covadil near LûghashCovadil asked you to search among the old ash piles outside Lûghash for some sign of the Gondorian presence.Dig in the ash piles outside Lûghash (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Gondorian scholar Covadil is near the Spire of Lûghash, in Dor Amarth.'Adventurers of the Free Peoples have set foot into Udûn, but the fortress of Carach Angren has hindered further passage. The eruption of Orodruin melted the doors of the Isenmouthe and they cannot be shut, but enemies still hold the channel between Udûn and Dor Amarth.

'Some few have managed the passage: Rangers, Elves, and others utilizing the arts of stealth. I believe that you can advance into the Doomfold of Dor Amarth. Pass through the guarded Isenmouthe and find Brungos on the other side. He will tell you somewhat of what he has done, and what has yet to be accomplished.''I believe that you can advance into the Doomfold of Dor Amarth. Pass through the guarded Isenmouthe and find Brungos on the other side. He will tell you somewhat of what he has done, and what has yet to be accomplished.''I believe that you can advance into the Doomfold of Dor Amarth. Pass through the guarded Isenmouthe and find Brungos on the other side. He will tell you somewhat of what he has done, and what has yet to be accomplished.''I believe that you can advance into the Doomfold of Dor Amarth. Pass through the guarded Isenmouthe and find Brungos on the other side. He will tell you somewhat of what he has done, and what has yet to be accomplished.''Welcome to the Doomfold of Dor Amarth, ${PLAYER}, but even as I bid you this greeting, the words rebel upon my lips. Welcome? Who could be welcome in this place?

'It is the way of my people to survive in the wilderness, hunting game and foraging for food even when the land seems barren. Those skills would be well-tested in this place! Luckily, it should not be necessary; I have brought supplies enough to last me, and in any event I would not trust anything that could grow here.

'Was this valley once fertile? I cannot imagine it, but mortal eyes are oft quick to judge, given how little we see of the great span of Time. Before Sauron came, was this land green and growing?'

Brungos dismisses the thought with a shrug.

'Mayhap I will consider further when we are safely away from here, ${PLAYER}. For now, the land is dead and full of enemies, and we should ask pointed questions with dagger and blade, and set aside any questions that do not fell our foes!''There is no shortage of enemies stalking Dor Amarth. Defeat any you find and the land will be made not safe, but safer at the least.''I have faced many foes here myself, but still my mind worries at the subject about which we spoke, ${PLAYER}. Can it be that this land was once much the same as any other, but the coming of Sauron laid waste to it? There are countless reasons to rejoice that he is gone, but even for one among many it must approach the top of the list.

'If he could do this to Mordor, could he not have worked such a change upon Gondor, or upon Rohan, or upon Eriador? I deem that he could. Further I contend that the transformation of Dor Amarth to this blasted plain was not accidental. I believe he did it purposefully, with malicious intent, if not for some scheme then merely for the hateful joy of it.

'I spoke about this with Covadil, who dared the passage of Isenmouthe with me; I think it was she who first planted these thoughts in my mind, and now I cannot stop thinking on them! She intended to travel north-east to Lûghash. I warned her to be careful, but she found that mirthful and laughed at my warning. She is a scholar, and a bit odd, but I do hope she is being careful all the same. Perhaps you could find her and see how she fares?''Covadil intended to travel north-east to Lûghash. I warned her to be careful, but she found that mirthful and laughed at my warning. She is a scholar, and a bit odd, but I do hope she is being careful all the same. Perhaps you could find her and see how she fares?''Will you look at that, ${PLAYER}? A fortress was built there upon the lowest slopes of Ered Lithui when Gondor established its watch upon Mordor. It was called the Peace-hold of Sídhost, and nothing moved on Orodruin without alerting the attention of its defenders.

'But the vigilance of Gondor failed, in time, and it was abandoned. The Orcs burrowed beneath its foundations, and the earth shook, and Sídhost crumbled into ruin. The Spire of Lûghash arose in its place. That is it right there! If it weren't for the scholarly pursuit of knowledge, you would think that was merely an evil tower, but now you know some of its history. Isn't that fascinating?

'But there was once a Gondorian fortress there, and I wonder if there might still be signs of it. You should do what we scholars of the House of Lore often do when we want to unearth lost knowledge. We dig about in the debris, ${PLAYER}! You never know what you might find!

'Search the old ash piles all around the Spire, and see if you can find some remnant of the Gondorian presence here. For knowledge, and for knowing!''There was once a Gondorian fortress there, the Peace-hold of Sídhost. I wonder if there might still be signs of it. You should do what we scholars of the House of Lore often do when we want to unearth lost knowledge. We dig about in the debris, ${PLAYER}! You never know what you might find!

'Search the old ash piles all around the Spire, and see if you can find some remnant of the Gondorian presence here. For knowledge, and for knowing!''Did I not tell you? Digging in the debris is never a waste... not for a scholar of history!

'This broken piece of metal is unremarkable, save for this marking right here. Do you see it? That is half of a sigil of Gondor. Nay, not even a half! It may be more of a small portion of a small portion, but even a portion's portion is a delight to one who thirsts for knowledge!

'This tiny remnant of history has emerged from the ash, a reminder to us of Gondor's resilience. My master will surely find value in this discovery, even if I was not supposed to adventure here. The House of Lore will benefit from this, I do not doubt it! This is much more interesting than anything I might have found in Osgiliath, so close to Minas Tirith. This is a land of danger, an unknown land, and the rewards are greater to match!'A tremendous feeling of evil emanates from the bones of Barad-dûr and adversely affects those unready for its touch.A number of soldiers are hurrying down the road towards Lûghash.Talk to Thorvíl on the road east of LûghashTalk to Fairanc on the road east of LûghashCovadil has come up to the group of soldiers on the road east of Lûghash.Talk to Covadil on the road east of LûghashRhogrin is near the great abyss east of the Spire of Lûghash, in Dor Amarth.Find Rhogrin on the road to the great abyss east of LûghashRhogrin stands near the great abyss in Dor Amarth.Talk to Rhogrin on the road to the great abyssRhogrin is standing on the road to the great abyss in Dor Amarth, and seems to be himself again.Talk to Rhogrin on the road to the great abyssCovadil is on the road east of Lûghash.Talk to Covadil on the road east of LûghashMithrandir is outside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir outside the Black Gate'Hold on. Do you hear that? There are people approaching from down the road, and they sound as if they are in a tremendous hurry!''There are people approaching from down the road, and they sound as if they are in a tremendous hurry!''You don't want to go this way, friend! Ours was a small company of soldiers aiming to plunder the ruins of Barad-dûr for treasure, and each man was to have a share of the loot. But one of our number turned on us, he did, and thought to slay us!

'I don't know why he attacked us. Wanted our shares of the treasure, most like, but I can't see as why Rhogrin would strike out at his fellows like that. Never known him for a turncoat, not until today!''There's something terrible about the wreckage of Sauron's tower, the ruins of Barad-dûr. Enormous pieces of it fell to the ground when the Dark Lord was destroyed, and the land all around has an unpleasant air.

'But I never thought it could work such a change on one of my fellows! Rhogrin was just as he always was on the journey out, but when we approached the terrible pit, that great abyss...'

Fairanc falls silent.

'That abyss was the site of Barad-dûr, when still it stood. Something about it infused Rhogrin with a type of madness, and he attacked us. We, his faithful friends! As if years of service and kinship meant nothing! Pah!''I can't see why Rhogrin would strike out at his fellows like that. Never known him for a turncoat, not until today!''Something about that great pit infused Rhogrin with a type of madness, and he attacked us. We, his faithful friends! As if years of service and kinship meant nothing! Pah!''I know that name! I spoke with Rhogrin at the Black Gate, and he seemed a nice enough sort. He did not seem the type to attack his friends, for treasure or for no. I think Fairanc is right: the abyss where Barad-dûr once stood is still surrounded by corruption, and it drove Rhogrin to madness.

'You have to find him before he harms any others, or himself, but do not kill him! Remember the lesson of the metal scrap from the ash pile: even good things once buried can be unearthed again!

'Find Rhogrin by the abyss, and knock some sense back into him! But kill him not, for that is not easily undone.''Find Rhogrin by the abyss, and knock some sense back into him! But kill him not, for that is not easily undone.''Find Rhogrin by the abyss, and knock some sense back into him! But kill him not, for that is not easily undone.''You won't get me! Invisible foes, flitting all about us! You can't see them, but they can see you... they're all around!

'Stay back! I'll kill you!''Did you find Rhogrin? You have to go back and make sure he is all right!''${PLAYER}? What are you doing out here? In point of fact, what am I doing here? I last remember standing on the road somewhere near Lûghash, but that is many miles away, is it not? I remember...'

The colour drains from his face.

'My friends... I think I attacked them! I didn't mean to do it, but there was such an overwhelming feeling of... of... I do not know how to describe it. My will was overmastered. Did I think I had the strength of Gondor? I was driven to madness like a common Orc, and set upon my fellows!

'I am shamed, ${PLAYER}. I did not understand the potency of Sauron's evil. Even after his fall, the lands that he touched remain marred by the memory of him. I must find my friends and apologize. I hope they will understand that I was not myself. I have to leave this place. I am not equal to it.''I am shamed, ${PLAYER}. I did not understand the potency of Sauron's evil. Even after his fall, the lands that he touched remain marred by the memory of him. I must find my friends and apologize. I hope they will understand that I was not myself. I have to leave this place. I am not equal to it.''You saved Rhogrin from himself? I am glad to hear it, and your story confirms to me something I had suspected!

'Sauron instructed his servants to build him a kingly castle, a stronghold on a hill that was called Amon Dûr. Over the long passage of years, his servants expanded the castle to cover all of the hill, and it grew with new construction until it towered into the air. It became Barad-dûr, the Dark Tower, and reached so high that only Sauron's magic could keep it aloft.

'But the tower did not only reach skyward. Beneath Barad-dûr, the Orcs delved fathomless dungeons for their master, until the Ered Lithui were riddled with pits and chasms. When the tower fell, the land gave way and a great abyss opened where Barad-dûr once stood. Sauron's dungeons were exposed to the sky: the Abyss of Mordath.'

Covadil shivers.

'Brrrrr! Knowledge can be frightening! Anyway, Sauron may be gone, but he dwelt at Barad-dûr for so long that the land has not forgotten him. It sounds to me as if he left his impression on the Abyss and the lands around it. You should find Mithrandir and tell him this, and I think it is time I returned to the House of Lore. I am not afraid of danger... in moderation. But this is more than moderately terrifying!''Sauron may be gone, but he dwelt at Barad-dûr for so long that the land has not forgotten him. It sounds to me as if he left his impression on the Abyss and the lands around it. You should find Mithrandir and tell him this, and I think it is time I returned to the House of Lore. I am not afraid of danger... in moderation. But this is more than moderately terrifying!''The tidings you bring of Barad-dûr do not surprise me, but they will need to await proper consideration. Something has happened.

'A messenger has come.'Sapakár the Cleaver confronts you on the edge of the chasm of Mordath.Sapakár can be found somewhere in Barad-dûr.

You should confront Sapakár.Defeat SapakárYou should look over the edge into the abyss of Mordath.Lômazar can be found at the Ruins of Díngarth.

You should speak with Lômazar.Talk to Lômazar'I sent down servants to bring back treasures and useful things for the Black Word. I sent them down quickly, but no one comes back! Good you are here. I can send you down real quick too. I must get something for the Black Word.'

You assure the Olog-hai that you have no intention on being flung horribly into that abyss.'What, you are going to try and stop me? Ha ha. Good luck with that, little ${RACE}!''It seems you have found the Olog and survived. Your exploits thus far surprise me.

'Moreover, you have seen the Abyss of Mordath. Indeed, that great chasm is a place of horror. I shudder to think what still lives down there.'After encountering the Stout-axe Dwarves of Mordor, Legolas and Gimli hope to establish peace with this long-lost House.Gimli can be found at the ruins of Díngarth in Dor Amarth.

Spakorth of the Stout-axes has offered to treat with Gimli at Blozronk. You should return to Gimli and Legolas.Talk to Gimli at the ruins of DíngarthGimli can be found at his camp-site within Dor Amarth.

Spakorth has told the tale of his people's enslavement by a Black Númenórean and how the House that belonged to his ancestors was slowly brought to ruin by Sauron. You should speak to Gimli about what is to be done.Talk to Gimli at BlozronkSpakorth can be found at Blozronk in Dor Amarth.

Gimli and Legolas have discussed their concerns, but Gimli appears eager to help the Dwarves of Lûghash despite Legolas's reticence. You should now speak to Spakorth and learn how best to aid his folk.Talk to Spakorth at Blozronk'It has been long since my folk last spoke to outsiders... especially those with the might to bring him low.

'For what purpose have you come to these lands? To liberate its peoples? Conquest? I suppose it does not matter now. You are here, and so that is that! The Deathless may not see it so, but their quarrels are their own!

'I must ask... in your vast armies, were there any dwarves? We have seen naught of our kin and cousins in the lands beyond Mordor since the early days of our folk in the mountains to the East, and I would be gladdened to learn that we are not the last dwarves in this realm.'

You tell Spakorth of Gimli and of the House of Durin's Folk.

'Ah! So one of my kin has come to these lands? If he so wishes it, I would much desire to meet him!

'Tell this Gimli of the Stout-axes of Mordor! If he wishes to meet with me, I would have us forge a new peace free of Sauron's influence.

'Go! I shall wait here for a time, and if you do not return, I shall honour his choice.''You found them after all, ${PLAYER}? Oh ho! Tell me of them!'

You tell Gimli of the Stout-axe Dwarves of Mordor and of their origins in the mountains to the East. You also mention that Spakorth has asked to meet with Gimli at the muddied cistern of Blozronk to the east.

'Stout-axe Dwarves? I have never heard of a House by that name in all my years. Perhaps it was a name given to them by the Dark Lord, or their line was broken...'

Gimli appears initially perplexed by this news, but a wave of merriment soon washes over his face.

'Still, they did you no harm, so I shall hear this Spakorth's words.'

Legolas lets out a stern cough.

'Oh, you think they're up to something, do you? Well, for all I know of them, they might well be. Why don't you come along too, Legolas?

'Let us go to Blozronk, ${PLAYER}. Lead the way!'


<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will swiftly travel to Blozronk when you continue this quest.</rgb>'Aye, he's a dwarf all right. Not that I doubted you, ${PLAYER}, but I am just as surprised as you to find dwarves here in Mordor!

'The Stout-axes... I must admit, I have many questions I wish to ask about the early years of their folk and how they came to dwell here. There is so much to be learned from them! Even if they toiled under the Dark Lord, I'd wager they're still a better lot than the Dourhands.

'See, Legolas? It's not like he's got evil eyes painted all over him!

'I think it's time we properly introduce ourselves, wouldn't you say?''Gimli seems a fine dwarf, and I am glad to have met him. He has many questions, and I am pleased to say that I can provide him many of the answers he seeks. If there is time enough, I have questions of my own!

'We Stout-axes hold little sway in Mordor, but we dwell in peace within the Flaming Spire under the rule of Mistress Zôreth. We are no Dwarf-kingdom of old, but we are not so few as to be forgotten in this land -- for even Sauron could not forget us!

'If you or Gimli wish it, you are welcome to visit our home in Lûghash - provided the Mistress does not find out, of course.

'Hah! But if she didn't know you were there....'Hopeful that their King will one day return, the Stout-axes of Lûghash seek to forge a new weapon from the works of the Dark Lord himself.Obsidian can be found near lava-floes around Orodruin, wood can be salvaged from ruined banner poles on the plains of Dor Amarth, and shards of Barad-dûr's steel can be found in the lands surrounding Sauron's fallen tower.

Sversting has asked you to gather obsidian, wood, and a shard of Barad-dûr's steel that he might craft a weapon of renown in honour of their lost King.Collect chunks of raw obsidian near Orodruin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect wood-scraps from ruined banner poles in Dor Amarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect steel from a ruined shard of Barad-dûrSversting can be found at the forges of Lûghash.

You have gathered much obsidian, wood, and even a shard of Barad-dûr itself. You should now deliver them to Sversting at the forges of Lûghash.'At last we meet, ${PLAYER}! Spakorth told us of the aid you brought his expedition, and I wished to look upon the outsider myself!

'You may call me Sversting, and I am the forge-master of Lûghash - and the most skilled smith of all the Stout-axes that remain in Mordor! Aye, we are not as many as we once were... but it brings me great pride nonetheless!

'My works have earned me a great deal of Mistress Zôreth's favour, and she has told me of a great rumour she learned by spying upon the Men of Barad-dûr. She claims that our imprisoned King still lives, locked away in the depths of the Dark Tower!

'It is too great a tale for me to believe at once, but what if it were true? If King Greytooth were to return to his people at last, he must have a weapon befitting a King! 

'Spakorth tells me you wish to aid us. Is that right? I would ask that you bring me wood from abandoned banner poles and chunks of sharp obsidian from the banks of the lava-floes. Also, should you encounter any of the shards of Barad-dûr that were hurled from the Dark Tower as it fell, gather as much as you can!

'It is time we Stout-axes took something from Sauron!''Yes, yes! These will do the trick!

'The Dark Lord's steel is a curious creation - it is light, yet sturdy, and I have come to believe it cannot be dulled by the passing of time.

'In the hands of any dwarf, I say it still cannot match the beauty and strength of mithril! Even so, it has no equal in Mordor. If our King should ever return, he shall bear a weapon worthy of his line. I swear it!

'I have never given up hope that the King lived, for he spared me Sauron's ire when the Dark Lord took him and the others away. To have him back again would be my greatest joy!

'I shall set my smiths to work at once. You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.'As the Orcs became more active after the fall of Sauron, they took to making camp in the ruins of Barad-dûr.Orcs can be found around Dor Amarth.

You should defeat as many Orcs as possible.Defeat Orcs in Dor Amarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many Orcs in Dor Amarth.CompletedYou notice the Orcs around Dor Amarth are quick to attack. They seem unorganized amongst the shattered ruins of Barad-dûr. You are unsure what plans they have, if any. However, something should be done to thin their numbers.You have defeated many Orcs in Dor Amarth on your conquests through Gorgoroth.When King Váskmun Greytooth was imprisoned by the Dark Lord, many of his most-trusted kin were brought to the dungeons of Barad-dûr as well.Free imprisoned Stout-axes from the dungeons of Barad-dûr (0/8)Imprisoned Stout-axes can be found throughout the dungeons of Barad-dûr.

In your search for King Váskmun Greytooth, you have found other Stout-axes imprisoned in Barad-dûr. You should free them.You have freed all the Stout-axes from the dungeons of Barad-dûr.CompletedWhile searching the ruins of Barad-dûr for King Váskmun Greytooth, you have spied several exhausted and plainly-clad Stout-axes held in the smaller cells of the dungeon.

These must be the other Stout-axes that were taken when Sauron imprisoned King Greytooth seventy-eight years ago.

You should free them from the dungeons of Barad-dûr and allow them to reunite with their kin in Lûghash.After seventy-eight long years, you have freed the Stout-axes from their imprisonment in the dungeons of Barad-dûr.

Although their kingdom of old has long laid in ruin, freedom may soon be won for all the Stout-axes of Mordor.

You have allowed them to share in this hope and to reunite with their kin in Mordor.The Order of the Eye has sent many of its followers into Dor Amarth hoping to uncover the resting places of Sauron's most powerful artifacts.Order of the Eye expedition-leaders can be found patrolling outside Lûghash, upon the broken plains of Dor Amarth, and among the ruins of Barad-dûr that have sunken into the Mordath.

Spakorth has asked you to retrieve missives from the Order of the Eye expedition-leaders searching the lands of Dor Amarth. Spakorth hopes they might reveal the whereabouts of the lost King of the Stout-axes.Collect a missive from a Dor Amarth expedition-leaderCollect a missive from a Mordath expedition-leaderCollect a missive from a Lûghash expedition-leaderYou have gathered three missives from the Order of the Eye and should now read them.Spakorth can be found at the Stout-axe barracks on the central level of Lûghash.

You have discovered the location of the King of the Stout-axes by reading the missives and should now deliver them to Spakorth.'For my part, I have but one task for you, and it is one of dire importance to my folk, ${PLAYER}.

'The Men of the Order of the Eye have been searching Dor Amarth and the ruins of Barad-dûr for Sauron's artifacts since his fall, and they have taken careful note of all they have found as has Mistress Zôreth.

'One of my folk, Sversting, once told me of a rumour shared with him by Mistress Zôreth. She told him that King Greytooth was still held within the deeper dungeons of the Dark Tower, but she would speak no more of it when the rumour spread among us.

'I must know if the Mistress spoke true - does my King still live?

'The Order has sent expeditions all across Dor Amarth - from the deepest depths of the Black Pits of Mordath to the very gates of Lûghash!

'I would ask that you seek out their leaders, and see if they bear any missives or orders that speak of King Greytooth!''King Greytooth lives? Seventy-eight years he has been gone... and for seventy-eight years we have been without hope. Perhaps those days shall soon be behind us!

'Mistress Zôreth claimed he was still imprisoned by Sauron after all these years, but one detail in this missive has left me uneasy. You see, my folk have never been sent to seek him. I... I cannot recall a single expedition to rescue him from the Dark Tower. What attempts to free him could this missive speak of?

'That is a question I shall have to ask the Mistress, I think.  Where her efforts failed, we must now succeed.

'It is time King Greytooth is freed from Barad-dûr and returns to lead his folk!'The Stout-axes have longed served the Mistress of Lûghash, Zôreth, but her rule has become only more cruel and oppressive in the absence of the Dark Lord.Spakorth and his folk can be found within Lûghash.

Spakorth has asked you to aid him and the other Stout-axes of Lûghash in easing the burdens laid upon them by Zôreth.Aid DrériAid FrekiAid SverstingAid SpakorthSpakorth can be found within Lûghash in Dor Amarth.

You have aided the Stout-axes of Lûghash in completing many of Zôreth's tasks and have also discovered the whereabouts of their imprisoned lord. You should now speak to Spakorth.Talk to Spakorth in Lûghash'It often falls to me to ensure that my folk best serve Mistress Zôreth, and as of late, that has meant tireless work. Even with so many new Stout-axes here after the fall of Barad-dûr, it seems like the work only grows more difficult. Our searches for Sauron's trinkets have not borne fruit, and the Mistress has become impatient. If we do not find something soon, I fear we shall lose her favour. And what would become of my folk then? Nothing good, I think!

'In addition to a few tasks of my own, my brethren here in the barracks and down at the forges below have quite a few tasks to finish.

'Will you aid them while I speak with Gimli?''You have done well to aid my folk, ${PLAYER}.

'As Mistress Zôreth promised, King Greytooth lives yet, but we must still wrest him from the dungeons of the Dark Tower.

'And that shall be no simple task....'After discovering the Dwarves of Mordor escaped Sauron's wrath, Gimli seeks to reunite with his long-lost kin.The Dwarves of Mordor might be found somewhere along the causeway to Barad-dûr.

Overjoyed that his kin have escaped Sauron's wrath, Gimli has asked you to search for the Dwarves of Mordor.Patrol the causeway towards Barad-dûr and search for the Dwarves of MordorThe dwarves can be found at Blozronk along the causeway to Barad-dûr to the south-east of the Ruins of Díngarth in Dor Amarth.

You have found a company of wounded dwarves in Dor Amarth. You should aid them as Gimli requested.Spakorth can be found at Blozronk in Dor Amarth.

A dwarf named Spakorth has led the dwarves to the muddied waters of Blozronk. You should speak to him.Talk to Spakorth at Blozronk'Aye, this is a tricky land. I do not know what beasts could live in such a place, but dwarves are hardy folk!

'These Dwarves of Mordor must dwell somewhere in the Doomfold, but perhaps they do not wish to be found. If they make use of dwarf-doors as the House of Durin once did in Khazad-dûm, even the most thorough search would never bear fruit. Let us hope it does not come to that!

'You found the dwarf near the causeway that leads to the Dark Tower, didn't you? If you ask me, no dwarf would be wise to travel these lands alone, and even if one did, they would soon be sought out if they did not return.

'Patrol the causeway to the east and see if you do not find others of his kin... and ones with better luck, I would hope!''What is it? Why are you bothering--'

Spakorth turns and flinches at once upon seeing you.

'I... what do you want? Why have you come here? Whatever your purpose is here, we want no part of it!'

One of Spakorth's expedition calls to him and tells him of the water you offered them.

'You do not mean us harm? Sauron told us that the folk of the West were not to be trusted, especially those from the kingdoms of Men and Elves.

'Well, then, let us speak and I shall decide if we have been misled.'Barad-dûr sentries are stationed at bridges and patrol the ruins of the vile tower.Barad-dûr sentries can be found near the bones of Barad-dûr.

You should defeat an sentries that you come across.Defeat Barad-dûr sentries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat many Barad-dûr sentries.CompletedYou notice foes guard many bridges near the ruins of Barad-dûr. If you are to move about without getting captured, you will need to defeat some of these guards.You have defeated many of the sentries that guard Barad-dûr and have made the passage through eastern Dor Amarth a bit safer.Lômazar explains to you all he knows of Dulgabêth's plans.Many lesser artifacts can be found scattered across Dor Amarth. Lômazar may have additional information about Dulgabêth's loyal.

You should find any lesser artifacts you can while searching for Dulgabêth's loyal.Search rubble for lesser artifacts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Complete The Wrath of GlúrugothComplete Edge of The AbyssComplete Treasures ForgottenLômazar is located at the Ruins of Díngarth.

You should speak with Lômazar.Talk to LômazarIngold can be found at the Ruins of Díngarth.

You should speak with Ingold.Talk to Ingold'I say again, you must give your promise to send me away from these lands. Even rotting in a dungeon until the end of days would be better than endless pain and torture at the hand of the Black Word.'

You give your word that Lômazar will be treated fairly as a prisoner.

'Good. Now listen closely, as I have not the strength nor will to repeat what I am about to say.

'I do not know why only the most important and awe-inspiring bits fell to earth while much of the city remains. Dulgabêth the Black Word, known to you as the Mouth of Sauron, has claimed what still stands of Barad-dûr. He has many followers, myself once included. Now Dulgabêth seeks to rule all of Mordor and take Sauron's place, by use of powerful relics buried within the tower's walls. None will oppose him. His faithful servants are not to be trifled with either. Glúrugoth, the Hole-warden. Sapakár the Cleaver. Krassa-phûrsa, Bringer of Tears. These are all names that strike fear upon mention.

'Do you now possess the knowledge you seek?''Have you come to take me out from this place of my certain death or have you come to tell me only that my words rung true?'

You explain to Lômazar that he will be taken out of Mordor once the situation in Dor Amarth has improved.

'It seems I may be dwelling in this company for a time then. Be warned, it should be known that some of the relics Dulgabêth pursues are much more powerful than one of your ilk could imagine. You may have thwarted his efforts thus far, but let it be known, hidden objects lie in wait below the fortress of Barad-dûr.''Lômazar has been truthful with us up to this point, ${PLAYER}. While I have my doubts, it is not impossible that Sauron would have put such thought and effort into collecting many a valuable and powerful relic. You say that you have seen some of these already? Then perhaps we must act sooner than later in preventing The Black Word from finding any more lost trinkets of Sauron.'The Stout-axes of Mordor have long dwelled within Lûghash and they have reshaped the Flaming Spire to serve their own needs.The hidden gate of Lûghash can be found in an alcove high above the main gate, accessible by a rear pathway leading onto the Spire.

Spakorth has described the path to the hidden gate of Lûghash. You should find the rear pathway, climb the tower by following the path of crystal lamps, and seek the hidden gate in a shadowed alcove.Find the rear pathway onto LûghashFind the trail of crystal lampsFind the hidden gate of Lûghash upon the parapetThe hidden gate of Lûghash can be found in an alcove high above the main gate, accessible by a rear pathway leading onto the Spire.

You have discovered the hidden gate of Lûghash and should now enter the Flaming Spire and find Spakorth.Enter the hidden gate of Lûghash and find SpakorthSpakorth can be found in the dwarf-barracks on the central level of Lûghash.

You have entered Lûghash and should now speak to Spakorth.Talk to Spakorth in Lûghash'Hail, ${PLAYER}.

'We stand before the main gate of Lûghash, but you must not enter it. The Mistress has watching-stones guarding the inside of the gate, and if you are detected, she shall bring her wrath down upon us all!

'Many years ago, she allowed us to construct a second, hidden gate leading from our barracks out to the parapet far above us. We grew weary of her spirits lingering near us and begged her to keep the watching-stones away from our barracks. We did not expect mercy, but she relented, and set them only as guards within the main gate.

'Do you understand? You must enter through the hidden gate if you are to remain beneath the Mistress's notice. It is not hidden in the ways of the dwarves, ${PLAYER}, but it is hidden from the Spire's face.

'Behind the Spire, you will find a dirt pathway leading onto Lûghash itself. You must follow our lamps of crystal to a small, shadowed alcove. It is there you shall find the hidden gate.

'Go now! I must still speak to the Mistress to calm her fears about the spirits you drove back to the Spire, but once that is done, you shall find me in the barracks on the central level of the Spire.''Ah! At last you have come!

'I spoke to Mistress Zôreth, and she was pleased to learn that the "strange invaders" were driven out. She wishes to focus on her research in the heights of the Spire, so I do not think we need worry about her for now.

'Now that that's all settled, I have a few duties to perform about the Spire. If you would aid me with some of them, perhaps Gimli shall have more time to ask me his questions!'Gondorian soldiers have intercepted a Black Númenórean fleeing from Barad-dûr.You should speak with the Black Númenórean captive.Talk to the captiveYou should intimidate the Black Númenórean captive.Intimidate the Black Númenórean captiveYou should speak with the Black Númenórean captive.Try talking to the captive againYou should intimidate the Black Númenórean captive again.Intimidate the Black Númenórean captiveYou should speak with the Black Númenórean captive.Talk to the Black Númenórean captive'As we have fought together on the Pelennor, I well know you can protect yourself. Still, the plains of Gorgoroth are filled with fires and crevasses caused by the eruption of Mount Doom. It is a dangerous place and we must tread carefully.

'Our first scouting party has just returned, and with them, they bring a captive. The man they have captured is a Black Númenórean servant. He refuses to speak to us. We must either persuade him of our cause or somehow convince him to talk. 

'I was hoping that you would try speaking to the man. See if you can find out what he knows and who he serves.'You question the captive. The man seems to have no interest in anything you tell him or any question you ask.'If I speak to thee, Dulgabêth shall have me killed. The Black Word is not one to be reckoned with.'

'My name is Lômazar. I was once a faithful servant to Dulgabêth. I have made more than one mistake and as punishment was struck blind. To escape further torment, I severed my bonds, only to be captured again and brought here.'

'Perchance, will you spare me torment inside the walls of Mordor in the exchange of knowledge?''I have nothing to say to the likes of you.'Lômazar explains of Sapakár the Cleaver, a powerful ally of Dulgabêth.Ingold can be found at the Ruins of Díngarth.

You should speak with Ingold to learn anything you can about Sapakár.Talk to IngoldYou should travel to the last known location of Sapakár.Sapakár can be found somewhere in Barad-dûr.

You should seek out Sapakár.Enter Barad-dûr and seek out SapakárSapakár can be found deep within the bones of Barad-dûr.

You should seek out Sapakár.Seek out SapakárSapakár can be found somewhere in Barad-dûr.

You should confront Sapakár.Talk to Sapakár'I would be wary in tracking Sapakár the Cleaver. The mighty Olog-hai might easily be out-smarted, though makes up for such in brute force. I know not where to find him.'You describe Sapakár as best as you can to Ingold.

'Men under my command have reported a large creature similar in description to this Sapakár that you mention. The monster was last seen near Barad-dûr. I hope this assists you on your search, my friend.''Are you here to help? We must search the pit for The Black Word.'Lômazar tells you of the ancient beast, Narchraw, that Dulgabêth wishes to treat with.Barad-dûr is located in eastern Dor Amarth.

