'I shall work to free these from their webs and uncover their heraldry, but would you examine the slain Orcs on the road? Perhaps some have met with a less gruesome fate....''It seems that the Orcs have begun to fight amongst themselves, but I wonder why Orcs of Udûn and of Durthang would flee into this land?

'To flee into a land beset by hungering foes with allegiance to none...it is unwise, to say the least.'What has brought King Thranduil of Mirkwood to the Court of Lothlórien?Legolas can be found at the Ruins of Díngarth in Dor Amarth.

You should bring word to Legolas in Dor Amarth of his father's visit to Lothlórien.Find Legolas in Dor AmarthYou should talk to Haldir at the entrance to the Court of Lothlórien.Talk to HaldirApproach the Council of War in the Court of Lothlórien.Approach the War CouncilHaving arrived at the Council of War, you should talk to the lord and lady of the court first.Talk to Lord CelebornTalk to Lady GaladrielHaving spoken to Celeborn and Galadriel, you should talk to King Thranduil as well.Talk to ThranduilHear Thranduil's tale of the events in Mirkwood and Lothlórien.Talk to Thranduil and experience 'Session: the Battle Under the Trees'.Hear what the Council of War has to say after Thranduil's recounting of the Battle Under the Trees.Talk to ThranduilTalk to GaladrielLegolas seems as if he also wishes to talk with you.Talk to Legolas'Word has reached my ears from Lothlórien, friend, and I know not what to make of it. Thranduil, Lord of Mirkwood, is there, but on what errand? In these times, the reason cannot be a light or merry one.

'Whatever the case, Legolas may wish to be by his father Thranduil's side. Will you find him in Dor Amarth and tell him all I have told to you?''Find Legolas in Dor Amarth and bear him word that his father, King Thranduil, has come to the Court of Lothlórien.''Where did we hear this news? One of the great Eagles spied Thranduil and a small host of his kindred entering Caras Galadhon. It was a fleeting glimpse, even for a beast of such keen sight, and she could tell us little more.'Legolas hears the news with a furrowed brow.

'My father would not stir so far from his domain if the occasion were not great, and like Elladan, I fear dark reasons rather than happy ones. Yes, I think I must go and learn what has passed among my cousins and people. 

'Will you travel with me, ${PLAYER}? The miles will pass swiftly with company and I find myself loath to tear Gimli away for this, just now. I will be happier knowing that I will soon rejoin him here.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Advancing will teleport you directly to the Court of Lothlórien.</rgb>'Have you come for the Council of War? If not, it is some strange chance that brings you here now. 

'Oh, I do not wish to sound unwelcoming! Go on and approach the council. Doubtless you have news and I am certain you will have their ears. They are waiting still and can do nothing yet!''We are indeed fresh from battle, as Legolas observes. Against his father's domain and our own the Enemy's forces marched, even as war waged in the place from whence you have arrived. Is it any wonder that the foe should set all his plans into motion at once?''My heart tells me you have as great a tale to tell as we do - no, I think greater. The fact that your face is not harrowed and drained of hope tells me much, but I would hear more!'

Addressing the entire council, you briefly describe all the events of war between the Pelennor and the Black Gates.

A silence falls over those around you once you are done.

'This is joyous news, then!' Galadriel pronounces, at last. 'And such as shall be sung and told of for long to come. Though even the most joyous of changes come with a tinge of sorrow to we who have dwelled long here and have parted with so much.''Greetings, ${CLASS}. My son travels in strange company, it seems, but then, these are strange times and it is through great pains that I have recently learned the difference between a stranger and a foe.

'You have told your tale uncommonly well and it fills my heart with wonder. We, too, have weathered much, and in our tale is much, I believe, that you should know.''Listen, and I will tell you of the Battle Under the Trees.''In the end, our losses were great, but not nearly so great as those of our enemy's. They have retreated to their shadowed fastnesses, wounded but not so harmed that they may not cause us grief again. That is why we must choose our actions with care and gather all wisdom to us before we do.''The time of watching and waiting has gone completely, or nearly so. I would move to retaliate swiftly and absolutely. Although this Lord and Lady do not disagree, generally, they feel that we must yet wait for the arrival of another, before we do.''We await the arrival of the Elf, Glorfindel, ${PLAYER}. Without him this council is not complete and I feel we cannot act. 

'I reached out to him as soon as I was able and he swore that he would come with all possible haste. I do not yet fear the worst, and yet still the fact that he has not arrived is troubling.

'Your arrival, friend, is some consolation, and your service to our cause eases my fears even more. There is little else we can do now, but soon, I feel certain, we shall meet again. We must hope that by then, Glorfindel is with us.''I will tarry here briefly to speak with my father. You return to Mordor. As Lady Galadriel said, we will surely see each other soon.'The hobbits Pippin and Merry draw you into a debate with Gandalf about where they can and should go.Pippin, Merry, and Gandalf stand just within Cirith Gorgor, before the ruined Black Gates of Mordor.

You should hear what Pippin has to say.Talk to PippinPippin, Merry, and Gandalf stand just within Cirith Gorgor, before the ruined Black Gates of Mordor.

You should hear Gandalf's response.Talk to GandalfPippin, Merry, and Gandalf stand just within Cirith Gorgor, before the ruined Black Gates of Mordor.

You should hear Merry's rejoinder.Talk to MerryPippin, Merry, and Gandalf stand just within Cirith Gorgor, before the ruined Black Gates of Mordor.

You should hear what Gandalf has to say in return.Talk to GandalfPippin and Merry have returned to Cirith Gorgor, before the ruined Black Gates of Mordor.

You should talk to Merry.Talk to Merry'${PLAYER}! You have come along at just the right moment. You must help us settle this argument.

'You see, our good friend Gandalf here thinks we should not be allowed to poke our noses into Mordor.'Gandalf laughs.

'Come, come! I advise, albeit sometimes strongly, but do not rule. Or had you forgotten that you had both submitted yourselves to the rule of others? If you wish to go into Mordor, it is Éomer and Aragorn you must ask.

'But let us forget this matter for a time. I have something to show you. That is why I had come looking for you both to begin with, as it happens. I cannot show it to you here, however, because it is a place and not a thing. It is a short ride out of this blasted place, and it would be best if we set off at once.''Also, do not forget that Merry and I have each missed out on some part of the excitement that everyone else got to take part in, on account of those kidnapping Orcs and getting knocked on the head and prattling stern Gondorian healers!''Dear old Gandalf! I spoke those words in a rash moment and I am ashamed of them at once. Please forgive a foolish hobbit.

'But all the same, we have done so much. You must not take us for the young innocents who first stepped into Bree those months ago.''Oh, ${PLAYER}, I hope you stay about, have some more meals with us, and help us make our way in these days we stay here. They treat us like princes, it is true, but sometimes like the pampered kind who are well fed and can do very little of their own accord. That is no life for a hobbit, if you ask me!''Really, Gandalf, I don't see why we shouldn't be allowed to go in. Plenty of others have gone in - Sam and Frodo included. Well, I'll admit, they are not the best of examples.

'But we are not proposing to march right up the slope of Mount Doom. We wish only to take a few brief strolls in the place, to be able to say that we saw it with our own eyes, find a bit of treasure perhaps to take home with us....''Old Bilbo came home with quite a lot of treasure, you know. They will be expecting us to do the same, I am sure of it.''Treasure? Ha ha! Yes, I see, now we get to the bottom of this foolish notion.

'I tell you once more: Mordor is no place for hobbits. There are things down that path beyond what either of you have ever reckoned or encountered. In truth, I should think you have had enough adventuring for three lives of hobbits put together, both of you.''More than we have reckoned or encountered you say? Yet we have encountered quite a lot between the Shire and here. Not just Moria and the Pelennor Fields, which I suppose you remember somewhat, but plenty in between when you were not around to nanny us. I say it is we who are capable of more than you imagine, thank you very much.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Thorin III is now King under the Mountain, and rules Durin's Folk from Erebor. Now that you have pledged to aid the dwarves as they adventure in Mordor, Dori believes you should meet with Thorin at the Lonely Mountain.Travel to the Hall under the Mountain, in EreborFoldgráf stands ready to welcome you to the Hall under the Mountain.Talk to Foldgráf at the entrance to the Hall under the MountainKing Thorin is currently meeting with his advisors, and is not free for an audience. Foldgráf recommended that you make yourself familiar with the Hall under the Mountain while you wait.Talk to Bróthi in the Hall under the MountainTalk to Margald in the Hall under the MountainTalk to Laurík in the Hall under the MountainKing Thorin should now be ready to meet with you.Find King Thorin III, in the Hall under the MountainThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor. You should present yourself to Hrostyr, his personal guard.Talk to Hrostyr, the personal guard of King Thorin IIIThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the MountainThree adventuring dwarves stand by the throne in the Hall under the Mountain. They have been charged by King Thorin III with the same task as you.Talk to Eirek by the throneTalk to Dómor by the throneTalk to Mogi by the throneFoldgráf is at the entrance to the Hall under the Mountain.Talk to Foldgráf'I am pleased that you have pledged to aid Durin's Folk as we adventure in Mordor, ${PLAYER}! Who knows what treasures were wrongfully stolen from us, and now wait to be reclaimed and restored to their rightful owners?

'My adventuring days are not yet done. My captivity by the Dourhands in the North Downs has taught me the value of caution, and I will exercise it smartly. See that you do as well, my valiant friend, for the land of Mordor is a place of evil, Dark Lord or no!

'But before you begin to adventure on behalf of Durin's Folk, you should return to Erebor and speak with the King under the Mountain! That title belongs now to Thorin III, for his father Dáin fell before the gates of Erebor during the Battle of Dale. He died well, but now the responsibility of leading the dwarves falls to his son. Go to the Hall under the Mountain, in Erebor, and present yourself to him. He will be glad to have your service!''Before you begin to adventure on behalf of Durin's Folk, you should return to Erebor and speak with the King under the Mountain! That title belongs now to Thorin III, for his father Dáin fell before the gates of Erebor during the Battle of Dale. He died well, but now the responsibility of leading the dwarves falls to his son. Go to the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor, and present yourself to him. He will be glad to have your service!''Before you begin to adventure on behalf of Durin's Folk, you should return to Erebor and speak with the King under the Mountain! That title belongs now to Thorin III, for his father Dáin fell before the gates of Erebor during the Battle of Dale. He died well, but now the responsibility of leading the dwarves falls to his son. Go to the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor, and present yourself to him. He will be glad to have your service!''Welcome to the Hall under the Mountain, ${PLAYER}, noble seat of Erebor and pride of Durin's Folk! I understand you have pledged to aid the dwarves as they adventure in Mordor and desire to speak with the king, and I will not stand in your way!

'And yet, King Thorin is currently speaking with his advisors and an audience will have to wait until he is free. Perhaps you can make yourself familiar with these halls while you wait?''King Thorin is currently speaking with his advisors and an audience will have to wait until he is free. Perhaps you can make yourself familiar with these halls while you wait?''I understand you fought in the battles to the south? I hope you do not think we were idle, for indeed we had battles of our own to fight! A great army of Easterlings crossed the river and fell upon our allies at Dale, and we emerged from our mountain to lend the Men our strength. The fighting continued for several days, but the enemy force was too great and we were driven back to the very gate of Erebor. It was there that King Brand of Dale was overcome. King Dáin stood in defence of his friend, but no Man or dwarf could have survived long against that assault. The King under the Mountain, Dáin Ironfoot, was slain.

'It was the command of his son, Thorin III, that we retreat into the Mountain and prepare for a siege. He is now king and we follow his commands, but there were whispers that we should not have bolted our gate, that we should have stayed and fought. I do not agree with these whispers, but I am one dwarf and there are many others who think this retreat was craven.

'During the siege, the Hall under the Mountain became a place for the recovery of hurts, where good dwarves hope to heal from their injuries, lest they succumb. It will not remain so, but the toll from the Battle of Dale is high and it will take time to set it right again.''When Dale was beset by the Dark Lord's northern army, King Dáin emerged from Erebor with his finest warriors and sought to defend our allies, the Dale-men. He fought bravely and long, but it was not enough and he fell beneath the axes of his enemies. With his death, his son Thorin III ascended to the kingship of Durin's Folk, and all looked to him to defeat the Easterlings.'

Margald snorts derisively.

'His first command? He ordered that we retreat into the mountain and bar the gates! Our friends and allies were abandoned, and we relied upon the defences of Erebor to keep us safe! Some of my fellows have taken to calling him 'Stonehelm,' for he pulled the rock of Erebor snugly down upon his ears and thought to wait out our enemies from the safety of our halls. What would Dáin Ironfoot have to say of his son if he were alive to hear of this? Nothing good, I warrant!

'Thorin is now King under the Mountain, and upon my honour as a dwarf I will not speak against him. But neither will I speak against my fellows when they name him Stonehelm, either!''Erebor was recently besieged by a great army of Easterlings, and only when word of the Dark Lord's fall came to their ears did they falter in their attack. Then did Thorin III, the new King under the Mountain, emerge from Erebor and drive them back.

'Not all are pleased with the new king, but I think he will prove a worthy successor to King Dáin, in time. He is heir to Dáin's bloodline; he will do what is best for Durin's Folk in these difficult times. I have heard that the Land of Mordor, far to the south, is now ripe for the taking. King Thorin has issued the call for great heroes to adventure in Mordor on behalf of the dwarves, and that is enough for me to put my trust in him! What treasures of the dwarves might we reclaim from that place?''King Thorin should be ready to meet with you now.''King Thorin should be ready to meet with you now.''You must be ${PLAYER} the ${CLASS}, who has sworn to adventure on behalf of Durin's Folk? King Thorin will see you now and charge you with tasks befitting your skill.''The king awaits, for he has a task with which to charge you.''Welcome to Erebor, ${PLAYER}. I hope you will forgive the state in which you find these halls, but battle was lately joined and we feared a long siege. Many supplies were brought from elsewhere in the mountain to be near at hand in the event they were needed, and they have yet to be returned.

'I have summoned adventurers of renown to Erebor in order to advance the cause of Durin's Folk in Middle-earth, but especially as it concerns the Land of Mordor. Those gates have long been shut to us, but I desire brave heroes to enter that forbidding land and retrieve for the dwarves any treasures that might be found.

'If my father had lived to see Mordor opened, I know he would advance this purpose, and fear nothing. So too will I. I have charged these dwarves with the same goal. Speak to them and they will share what they have learned of the dangers that await, and what they have learned of the great wealth that may wait as well.''If my father had lived to see Mordor opened, I know he would advance this purpose, and fear nothing. So too will I. I have charged these dwarves with the same goal. Speak to them and they will share what they have learned of the dangers that await, and what they have learned of the great wealth that may wait as well.''The Land of Mordor has been shrouded in mystery for as long as I have lived, ${PLAYER}, and it has an evil reputation. But that makes the prospect of adventuring within its bounds even more attractive to me, for Eirek Glory-seeker fears nothing! The greater the danger, the greater the honour of facing it, and that goes double for doing so in the name of one's king. I would have quested in Mordor for Dáin, and now that he has undertaken his final battle I will adventure on behalf of his son Thorin. Glory for Durin's Folk!

'We share a common purpose, but I hope beyond the Mountains of Shadow you will hunt elsewhere. The treasure I bring back, and the glory, should belong to Eirek and to his king alone!''King Thorin has no shortage of heroes and adventurers willing to brave the dangers of Mordor on his behalf, but the folk that remain behind whisper in their beards and compare him unfavorably to his father. I will return from Mordor with treasure enough to silence their whispers. They call him Stonehelm, and say he hides within the mountain? When I return they will call him Gilded-helm, and I will be richly-rewarded for my efforts!

'There are adventures enough to be had in that land, but do not expect to share in my treasure or my glory, ${PLAYER}. There will be enough for all of us, no doubt.''You say you are an adventurer? Are you certain? I have never heard of you, and I wear the dust of many distant lands on my boots. Have you faced down the terrible fire-troll Ashthrok, or the unsightly Vélnas? What about the terrible Hrimdyr, vile snow-beast of the blinding snow? I have fought them all, and lived to tell the tale.

'Now I will delve beyond the Mountains of Shadow and adventure in the Black Land of Mordor, and the tale of Mogi will grow! Stay out of my way, ${PLAYER}.''Adventure in Mordor for Durin's Folk, and return with treasure and glory!''I look forward to hearing of your great deeds on behalf of Durin's Folk, ${PLAYER}. Adventure in Mordor with caution, and know that our thoughts are with you even as you walk in the distant, dangerous wilds!

'Return in triumph, and with treasure and glory for the dwarves!'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Aragorn and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.Prince Imrahil is outside the Black Gate, waiting to return with you to Minas Tirith.Talk to Prince Imrahil and return with him to the White CityKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'The further conquest of Mordor must wait. Aragorn has business to which he must attend at the White City, and he will want to have us there at his side, ${PLAYER}. Speak to me and we will go together, all of us.''I did not mean just the two of us, ${PLAYER}. All the Host of the West will return with Aragorn. Do not worry! We shall come back to Mordor and continue our conquest, but this is something that must be done if that is to have the greatest chance of success.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'King Elessar waits for you in his throne room, ${PLAYER}, if you have matters that demand his attention. He will listen to you readily and gladly, I do not doubt!''I wish to thank you for the great help you have provided to me and to Middle-earth, ${PLAYER}, but it would be premature. I am certain you will continue to lend invaluable service to the Kingdom of Gondor, and I welcome it! Still, I thank you on this day, and will again!

'I am now king, but my thoughts before were ever for the safety of my people and that has not changed. We must preserve the safety of Minas Tirith, and of all Gondor, and to that end I ask that you keep watch on the land of Mordor. By so doing, I hope we can ensure that none of the evils remaining in Sauron's wake can be made to threaten Gondor.

'Venture forth for Gondor, my friend! I await your return with hopeful heart and glad confidence.'Merry and Pippin persist in their determination to venture into Mordor.You should talk to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion.Find Pippin in Bâr ThorenionBoth Aragorn and Éomer can be found outside of the Black Gate, in the area of the Slag Hills.

Merry and Pippin have asked you to ask Aragorn and Éomer for permission to venture into Mordor.Talk to AragornTalk to ÉomerGandalf has appeared nearby to Aragorn and Éomer, outside the Black Gate in the Slag Hills, and has made it clear that he wishes to talk to you.You should return to Merry and Pippin in Bâr Thorenion with word that they have Aragorn and Éomer's blessings to go into Mordor.Return to Merry and PippinYou should meet Merry and Pippin at the Foothold encampment in Udûn.Meet at the Udûn FootholdYou should explore down in the Udûn valley, as far as the first cross-roads, with Merry and Pippin.

If you should get separated from Pippin or Merry, use their horns to call them back to you.Explore to the cross-roadsBlow Merry's horn to call him backYou should explore further along the main road in the Udûn valley, where the mass of soldiers' tents begins.

If you should get separated from Pippin or Merry, use their horns to call them back to you.Explore to the soldiers' campsMerry has suggested going a bit further along the main road in the Udûn valley, and then look for a way that turns north.

If you should get separated from Pippin or Merry, use their horns to call them back to you.Look for a northward path aheadYou should talk to Pippin, who says his stomach is growling.

If you should get separated from Pippin or Merry, use their horns to call them back to you.Talk to PippinPippin has suggested moving to a flat spot just north of the main road in Udûn, and stopping to eat some and rest up.

If you should get separated from Pippin or Merry, use their horns to call them back to you.Stop for a bite to eatYou should <rgb=#FF00FF>sit</rgb> down with Pippin and Merry at their Mordor picnic spread, off the main road in Udûn, and share in the meal.You should talk to Merry, at the picnic spread in Udûn, and find out what startled him.Talk to MerryGo up the northern path from the hobbits' picnic in Udûn and see if you can find what Merry heard.Look for the source of the soundThree travel-worn half-orcs stand on the ledge above you, north of the main road in Udûn, talking amongst themselves. You should <rgb=#FF00FF>listen</rgb> to hear some more.Three travel-worn half-orcs stand on the ledge above you, north of the main road in Udûn, talking amongst themselves. You should continue to listen <rgb=#FF00FF>listen</rgb> to their conversation.Pippin and Merry are north of the main road in Udûn.

You should talk to Merry and Pippin about the trio of half-orcs you just overheard.Talk to MerryTalk to Pippin'The two Periain, Meriadoc and Peregrin, were here not long past, looking for you. They returned to Bâr Thorenion, but implored that you should join them as soon as you could.''There you are, ${PLAYER}! We had been looking for you. What happy luck that you are here now!''Will you ask permission for us? We would go ourselves only - somehow it would all be better if you would do it. Aragorn and Éomer are likely just outside the Black Gates, in the Slag Hills.''Pippin is joking, of course. I am not the least bit ashamed to ask, but he is. Oh, just humour him.

'And before you go, be sure to see the gardens and the baths. It is beyond remarkable, is what it is!''Poke their noses into Mordor? That is exactly how they put it, I imagine. Well, to be honest, I would mislike the thought of those two going in alone. But if you will promise to stay close by and look after them, then they have my blessing.

'To Pippin I do grant leave.''Master Merry Holdwine wishes to see Mordor? I would not deny him it, for all that he has done and been through!''A word with you, ${PLAYER}. I know well enough that I have done little to hinder Merry and Pippin an excursion into Mordor. I hope I have lent some gravity to it, however. More importantly, I would ask you to stick by them.

'On this point, I ask a favour of you. Take them into Udûn when you go, and keep a sharp eye open for things that do not belong there. I have some reason to believe you may find something of interest to you and to our two meddling friends, if you do.''Then our little plan is advanced? I knew we could count on you, ${PLAYER}.''They said yes? I knew they would! Pippin and I have packed our packs. With the help of all these friendly big folk, we are well-provisioned indeed.

'Let us meet within - what's that they call it - the Foothold in Udûn?''Imagine how it must have looked with all the Dark Lord's armies gathered there.''Pippin and I have just been enjoying the view, wondering if you'd ever arrive! We might as well start on down there, I suppose. Well, no point in loitering, now. Let's be off!

'What do you say we make our way to the first cross-roads on down into the valley? You can make it out from here, only just.''To the first cross-roads - then we shall see what we shall see and decide what to do from there.''Which do you think we shall stumble over first, adventure or treasure?''Well, what is it, Pippin?''All this wandering about in the realm of the Dark Lord is well enough, though I don't see why another troll-hole laden with Elven blades hasn't presented itself just yet. But I will not keep this up with our stomachs growling, when so many fine provisions are weighing down my pack.

'You wanted to turn northward, Merry. Why don't we head up that way and stop at that flat bit, there? It doesn't look like anyone should bother us, and we'll restore ourselves properly before moving on.''Did you hear that too? I am hardly sure of myself, but I thought I heard something... something voice-like, coming from uphill.

'Quickly and quietly, friends, let us see what it was - if it was anything at all.''Go on ahead, ${PLAYER}. If I really did hear something, it is just up the path here. Pippin and I will be just behind you.''Hush, both of you. I want to hear some more!'Merry looks uncharacteristically pale.

'I have lost my appetite for food, and adventure, and any promise of treasure or gold, Pippin. Let us call it a day and head back, shall we?''Wizard? Halfling lands? And that voice. I would have sworn it was one of Bill Ferny's friends, come all the way to Mordor.''Well! You can see what a hard time of things we have been having, Pippin and I. Still, I worry his head may fall off of his shoulders, for how swift and how often it's been turning this way and that!''We have talked it over and made up our minds, Merry and I - but say, before I go into all that, look around you. See what those big folk have done to the place! It's hard to believe we were just here, when it was quite an old ruin. It's no ruin now. Gandalf must have been quite desperate to turn our heads away from Mordor if he felt the need to drag us here before they'd gone to work.

'Ah, silly me! But Mordor is exactly why we wanted to talk to you. We have not abandoned the idea of going only... well... we were hoping you might ask Éomer and Aragorn on our behalf. You see, we've got some things to attend to here before we set off. And if you ask me, Merry couldn't bear to see the disappointed look on Éomer's face if he should disapprove the idea.

'Will you do it? They have both been carrying on their kingly business around the Black Gates. You will find them just outside, in the Slag Hills, I imagine.''No sign of either treasure or adventure here, but then I suppose we've only just started. If you ask me, we ought to head up the road and have a look at all the soldiers' tents there.''Think of it, ${PLAYER}. We are on the other side. The front porch of the Enemy, you might say, though I am awfully glad he isn't home, nor shall be!''All these empty tents only put me in mind of what horrible things war and strife are. Perhaps we should see some of the forgeworks. Let us go on a bit and look for a path heading north.''Well for one, I hardly imagined the noise - or the stench!''I say we set down on that flat patch I saw, just north of the main road and behind some tents. It would be the perfect place to have a bite to eat and restore our weary limbs.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Elessar and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.King Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne roomThe delegation of dwarves is on the north-east side of the Fifth Circle in Minas Tirith.Find the dwarf delegation in Minas TirithThe emissaries from Erebor are on the north-east side of the Fifth Circle in Minas Tirith.Talk to Hálsi of the delegation from EreborTalk to Vígmath of the delegation from EreborThe delegation of Elves are just north of the prow, in the Fourth Circle of Minas Tirith.Find the Elf delegation to Minas TirithThe delegation of Elves are just north of the prow, in the Fourth Circle of Minas Tirith.Talk to Olphadon of the delegation from LórienTalk to Lalornil of the delegation from LórienThe delegation of Haradrim is on the south-eastern side of the Third Circle of Minas Tirith.Find the Haradrim delegation to Minas TirithThe delegation of Haradrim is on the south-eastern side of the Third Circle of Minas Tirith.Talk to Hasong of the delegation from HaradTalk to Mauthoi of the delegation from HaradKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'King Elessar waits for you in his throne room, and will likely have a task for you to undertake for Gondor, ${PLAYER}. Go and see what the king has for you, and I will await your return.''King Elessar waits at the throne for you, and will be glad to have news of your adventures in Mordor.''King Elessar waits for you in his throne room, and will likely have a task for you to undertake for Gondor, ${PLAYER}. Go and see what the king has for you, and I will await your return.''King Elessar waits at the throne for you, and will be glad to have news of your adventures in Mordor.''Messages have been sent from many lands to Minas Tirith, ${PLAYER}, overtures of friendship and words of support for the kingdom. It warms my heart to hear them, and to know that we have allies both near and far.

'Some have sent delegations to the city, and they have begun to arrive. Dwarves, Elves, and Men have come to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''Delegations have come to the city to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''Delegations have come to the city to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''We bring greetings from Thorin III, King under the Mountain, to King Elessar of Gondor! There will be friendship between Durin's Folk and the Men of Gondor, and let their enemies beware!

'This is the first I have seen of this city, and while there is impressive stonework here there is much that might be improved. If King Elessar wishes it, I am sure there are dwarves among us who would be willing to refine the walls and streets of Minas Tirith.

'I would do it myself if I had a team of dwarves and my tools at the ready, but sadly we have come for diplomacy, not for masonry. Perhaps later!''The folk of this city must have seen a number of dwarves before now, for we have been treated warmly and with kindness, though we be strangers here. This is my first visit so far south, and I am pleased at the quality of the stone I have seen to this point. It could be worked with more skill and more care, but I have always been choosy about such things, as are many of Durin's Folk.

'How much higher must we climb to reach King Elessar's throne? The air thins as we ascend, and it is not to my liking.''Delegations have come to the city to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''Delegations have come to the city to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''We bring messages of friendship and alliance from the Lord and Lady of the Golden Wood! Many are the preparations, already underway, that will further advance the joined purposes of Elves and Men, and Lord Celeborn and Lady Galadriel will delight in their completion.

'I have been to this White City, but it was long ago. It is not the shining jewel it was then, but under the rule of King Elessar the Elfstone I believe it can be again.''The people of this city have been welcoming, if guarded. They do the name of their city proud in this, ${PLAYER}!

'But I must remember that Elves are not seen in Gondor as often as once they were. These are unusual days for all of us, and there is little doubt the people of Gondor have become more used to unusual sights of late!

'We will not tarry, but will continue upward to the throne room of Elessar. I am eager to speak with him, for I was a friend of his betrothed when we were young, and am still very close with the Lady Arwen.''Delegations have come to the city to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''Delegations have come to the city to meet with me and arrange for renewed ties and alliances, and I will gladly hear their proposals. There is one group that may require some delicacy, however: emissaries have come from Harad, and these men speak not of renewing friendship, for there was none. They speak of making peace where before there was only war. It must be carefully handled.

'Find the dwarf, Elf, and Haradrim delegations in the city and make them feel welcome.''I am Hasong. I come from Yâd Hakar, great kingdom to the south ruled by the tribe called Sokhûri. Great are our warriors, and greater still is Khas Rûktar, who leads the Sokhûri.

'We met on the field as enemies, for who can foretell the many paths that diverge in war? It is the way of the Sokhûri to fight fiercely when commanded, and fight we did. But in war there is always one who fails. This time, the armies that came from Yâd Hakar are the ones that failed.

'When armies fail, there are two paths. On first path, every man is slain, and army is gone. This is how tribes vanish. Sokhûri cause many tribes to vanish. Khas Rûktar cause it himself, sometimes. Second path called Kerem-thak. This happen when failed army does not want to vanish. Tribe wants to live.

'Hasong and Mauthoi sent by Khas Rûktar to speak to Gondor King. We sent here for Kerem-thak. Khas Rûktar wants peace.''I am Mauthoi, sent by Khas Rûktar for purpose of Kerem-thak. King Gondor will choose if terms of peace are acceptable.'

Mauthoi looks at you with a sly expression on his face.

'You are ${PLAYER}. Great hero, they say. I wonder what might have happened if you encountered Hasong or Mauthoi during battle. Victory... or defeat? Not know now, because of Kerem-thak. I wonder, but not enough to wish it happened. Mauthoi have family back in Yâd Hakar, and tales of ${PLAYER} very convincing.

'Peace best for all.''Thank you for meeting with the emissaries who have come to Gondor, ${PLAYER}. I have spoken with them now, and we will have further to say on the subject of peace in the future.

'I must be truthful with you, ${PLAYER}. I do not completely trust the men of Harad. It is much easier to sue for peace when your forces have lost in battle, and I do not believe that Hasong or Mauthoi would be so willing to extend peace to Gondor if our positions were reversed. Many good soldiers were slain by weapons of the Haradrim, or trampled beneath the feet of their Mûmakil. Is it a betrayal of those lives to make peace with those who slew them?

'No, I do not think it is, but it is an easier question to ask than to answer. It seems that the delegation from Harad honestly desire peace, and that is a first step. Thank you for your help in this, my friend.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Thorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the MountainKing Thorin III has asked you to deliver news of his ascension to the throne to the following dwarf-leaders throughout Eriador and the Misty Mountains:

Dwalin in Thorin's Hall, Ered Luin.
Mathi Stouthand at Gondamon, Ered Luin
Hannar at Othrikar, North Downs
Avar at Gabilshathûr, East Angmar
Bósi in the Twenty-first Hall, in Moria

After you have delivered his words to each of these dwarf-leaders, you are to return to the King under the Mountain.Talk to Dwalin in Thorin's Hall, in Ered LuinTalk to Mathi Stouthand at Gondamon, in Ered LuinTalk to Hannar at Othrikar, in the North DownsTalk to Avar at Gabilshathûr, in East AngmarTalk to Bósi in the Twenty-first Hall of Khazad-dûmThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.'It is time you reported back to King Thorin about your activities in Mordor, my friend. Tell him how your adventures are progressing, and see if he has any further requests for you.''I heard that King Thorin has a task for you, ${PLAYER}. You should report to him of your adventures in Mordor and learn what that task might be, and if it is in your power to complete.''It is time you reported back to King Thorin about your activities in Mordor, my friend. Tell him how your adventures are progressing, and see if he has any further requests for you.''I heard that King Thorin has a task for you, ${PLAYER}. You should report to him of your adventures in Mordor and learn what that task might be, and if it is in your power to complete.''I welcome your return, ${PLAYER}. Know that your journeys in Mordor on behalf of Durin's Folk are appreciated.

'But I have called you here for another purpose. It may seem trivial, but with the death of my father, Dáin Ironfoot, word that I have ascended to the throne must be sent to other cities and outposts of the dwarves. I expect the leaders of these garrisons will respect my ascension to the throne, but until I receive assurances of this I sleep uneasily.

'Take these proclamations and bring them to the leaders of the following dwarf cities and outposts in Eriador and the Misty Mountains: Thorin's Hall and Gondamon in Ered Luin, Othrikar in the North Downs, Gabilshathûr in East Angmar, and the Twenty-first Hall of Khazad-dûm. They will therefore be notified that I am now King under the Mountain, and I will await their oaths of support. I have sent messengers to other cities and outposts, so you need not concern yourself with any but these five, and I thank you for the service.''Take those proclamations and bring them to the leaders of the following dwarf cities and outposts in Eriador and the Misty Mountains: Thorin's Hall and Gondamon in Ered Luin, Othrikar in the North Downs, Gabilshathûr in East Angmar, and the Twenty-first Hall of Khazad-dûm. They will therefore be notified that I am now King under the Mountain, and I will await their oaths of support. I have sent messengers to other cities and outposts, so you need not concern yourself with any but these five, and I thank you for the service.''Word travels more swiftly in these days than it did of old, ${PLAYER}, and I know what brings you here. I lived at Erebor for many years, and I would have been there still had I not been charged with oversight of these halls. I am not boastful enough to think that would have changed the course of the battle that was there fought, and yet...'

Dwalin exhales wearily, and strokes his beard.

'I am told Dáin defied the agents of Mordor that came to Erebor seeking news of our old burglar, Bilbo. Perhaps that refusal sealed his fate, and perhaps it did not, but I know Dáin would not be glad to have died of years, in his bed. His was always going to be a battle-death.

'His son Thorin may not have proved himself the dwarf that his father was, but in time he will. He will have my support until that day, and beyond it, for he is now King under the Mountain.''The King under the Mountain has been slain before the gates of Erebor, but his son survives? We are fortunate that Thorin III did not fall in the battle.

'He will have my support and that of my dwarves, without question or complaint. That is the way of my people.''You bring tidings that there is a new King under the Mountain? Such is the way of things in war. I had great respect for King Dáin, and know little of his son, but I am a Longbeard and I will support him.

'Does he hate the Dourhands? That is all that matters to me, and to the other dwarves that live at Othrikar!''Erebor is a long way from here, and even though Gabilshathûr is no longer encircled by enemies it is still not a place of safety. The King under the Mountain will have my respect and my loyalty as he always has, old or new, but to speak truthfully matters of succession do not have much of an effect on me or the other dwarves here.

'Perhaps if this Thorin were to journey here and aid us against our enemies I would feel differently. Until that day comes he will have my loyalty, whatever it may be worth to him. I would rather he sent warriors to reinforce us.''Thank you for bringing word from Erebor, ${PLAYER}. I am filled with sadness at the death of Dáin Ironfoot, for he was my king and an inspiration to many of us that undertook this expedition. His son will have my support, of course.

'There have recently been many troubles for us here, and I hope you will put in some good words with King Thorin on our behalf, ${PLAYER}. Tremendous upheavals have shaken the earth in recent days, and many good dwarves have been lost: fallen into pits or separated from their fellows by growing chasms or crumbling ceilings. Something has shaken Khazad-dûm to its very foundations, and I worry that it portends some new evil.

'Tell the new King under the Mountain of our struggles, and remind him of the great wealth that the Iron Garrison hopes to bring to him. Here is a rock containing a small vein of mithril that may encourage him to send help to us. Give it to him with my hopes, my friend.''You have delivered the proclamations? Good, good, very good. What is this?'

You give Thorin the small rock containing mithril, and his eyes brighten when you say it is a gift from Bósi and the Iron Garrison in Khazad-dûm. You tell him also of their recent troubles, but it is difficult to say how closely he listens to that portion of your account.

'There is treasure hidden within Mordor, as you have proven, but only in Khazad-dûm can mithril be found in goodly quantity. The Iron Garrison must simply persevere and mine as much as they can. I am certain such upheavals will quiet after a time. They will need to steel themselves for such difficulties.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}, once again! Return to your adventures in Mordor on behalf of Durin's Folk, and I will call you if I have need of you again.'Pay a visit to Rivendell. Learn what has happened there, what Elrond knows of Glorfindel, and why the house prepares for travel.You should visit Elrond Half-elven. He stands in the library of his house in Rivendell.Visit Elrond in RivendellYou should talk to Elrond Half-elven. He stands in the library of his house in Rivendell.Talk to ElrondElrond has suggested you visit Bilbo, who can be found in the Hall of Fire in the Last Homely House in Rivendell.Visit Bilbo BagginsLindir can be found in the Hall of Fire in Elrond's house in Rivendell.

You should ask Lindir how you can help with the preparations.Talk to LindirYou should help Elves packing throughout Elrond's house in Rivendell.Help packing Elves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have helped a number of packing Elves and should return to Lindir in Elrond's house.Return to LindirLindir suggested you look for Pethelen outside Elrond's house in Rivendell, to help with more preparations for the journey.Talk to Pethelen outside Elrond's HouseYou should go to Rivendell's Market and gather provisions and goods requisitioned for the journey. You will need to ask among the merchants and artisans and also look for some requisitions left lying around.Talk to Ladrochan, the Stable-masterTalk to AnornanethTalk to HammadelenTalk to GaldorTalk to DúrvenelGather parcels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the parcels you gathered to Tordúril in the Rivendell Market.You should bring the pile of parcels next to Tordúril from the Market down to the Rivendell Forges.Carry the goods down to the Rivendell ForgesYou should return to Pethelen, before Elrond's house in Rivendell, now that you have gathered the parcels as he asked.Return to PethelenLady Arwen has asked that you visit her in Imlad Gelair, below the falls in Rivendell.Lady Arwen has asked that you visit her in Imlad Gelair, below the falls in Rivendell.Talk to ArwenArwen and Eliaril stand in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell.

You should talk to Eliaril, Arwen's maid-in-waiting, and see if you can calm her down.Talk to EliarilArwen and Eliaril stand in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell.

Hear Arwen's response to Eliaril.Talk to ArwenArwen has asked you to find several books in Elrond's library in Rivendell.Find the books Arwen named (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should check in on Elrond in his library in Rivendell to see if he is ready to talk to you.'Ah, ${PLAYER}! We stand here peering into the dim ruins of the Enemy's land, while leagues behind us our father Elrond, our sister, and many others of our house prepare for a journey. Can you guess to where? 

'Few travel fleet as you do, friend. Will you speed to Rivendell and see how things are going? A fear troubles me that my home was braced in battle, just as Lórien was. And another thought comes to me too: that my father Elrond may have wise words of guidance in the search for Glorfindel. Nor do I doubt that he will be hungry for news of happenings here, as well. Especially from one who has seen so much of it!''While we here plan and wait and you brave the dim ruins of the Enemy's land, Elrond, his daughter Arwen, and much of their house prepare for a journey. Can you guess to where?

'Few travel fleet as you do, friend. Will you speed to Rivendell and see how things are going? If they were braced in battle, just as we were, they may need help. Elrond, also, may have wise words of guidance in the search for Glorfindel. Whichever the case, Lord Elrond will surely be hungry for news of happenings in Gondor and beyond. Especially from you, who have seen so much of it with your own eyes!''I little thought to see you here, so soon, ${PLAYER}. It gladdens my heart, for you can only have come bearing happy tidings!'

The Elf with whom he was speaking looks meaningfully and a shadow crosses Elrond's brow.

'I fear there are several matters to which I must attend, and they cannot wait. Forgive this discourtesy, ${PLAYER}. If you will return shortly I would speak more with you.

'Why do you not pay a visit to Bilbo the Halfling? He would be as glad to see your face as I. He will be in the Hall of Fire as he always is.''All this going about and fussing and bothering! I tell Lindir here it is most un-Elvish of them, but he only asks me if I would leave home without MY pipe and handkerchief. Hmph!

'Well I'm not going, even though I shall miss seeing the old Dúnadan in all his crown and glory. Frodo and his friends I will see again, for they shall pass this way. Elrond assures me of it. 

'Ah, but all this talking has made me tired, ${PLAYER}. I am glad I have got to see YOU! But now I think I must return to being quiet, and staring into the fire, and working out the words to this new song. 

'Why don't you ask Lindir here if you can help out. Nearly everyone but me is pitching in!'Lindir laughs.

'It is true, you cannot plead infirmity and age as Master Bilbo here does! We will indeed not turn away help that is offered, for we wish to set out on our journey with some happy haste. Anywhere within these walls you see packing, you are welcome to lend a hand!''We are indebted to you, ${PLAYER}. If you are not done being helpful, you might look for Pethelen just outside the house. I believe he is charged with tasks of provisioning.''You wish to help, ${CLASS}? Such good will among the free peoples!

'I have sent down several requisitions to the market and someone is needed to carry those things down to the forges for packing in crates and barrels. You will have to ask around among the merchants and artisans. I am afraid some other goods have been left lying about. Everyone is so busy.''Here you have all the blankets and feed required for the trip. Steady now, it is a heavy load!''Here, these are all the ropes and weavings that were asked of me. May they grant a swift and easy journey.''Many hours have I laboured over these fine and resplendent raiments. See that they are handled gently and packed well. I am to travel with Lord Elrond and Lady Arwen and will see them on the other side, so I will know!''I was handed these parcels of goods to be barreled for the journey without so much as a word of where to take them. Here, they are yours!''These are table-knives and wood-axes for the travelling party. What, did you think all I ever made were swords for adventurers?''You gathered all the parcels and then delivered them at the Forges?'

Pethelen laughs.

'You have done as much as any here to prepare for this journey, I think. No more labours for you, friend! While you were in the market, Lady Arwen heard of your arrival and begged to see you. She is within Imlad Gelair.

'Do not worry if you have never been there. You shall be admitted on her word now.''${PLAYER}, it is you after all. I thought I must have heard wrong, when word came that you were in Rivendell.

'Oh, I could ask you a thousand questions - but no, I shall forbear. I know that our greatest fears are averted, and that is enough. I shall look forward to hearing the tale told in full at the journey's end.

'You know the purpose of this pilgrimage, do you not? We make for Gondor, where in time Aragorn - I mean Elessar - and I shall be wed, as we were trothed to be long ago.

'Do you see, Eliaril? I have not forgotten or neglected the wedding that lies at the end of our travels. Perhaps you can persuade her it is not a thing to waste worry or care upon, ${PLAYER}.''It is fine and well for the bride-to-be to sit and dream of the future. But how shall the dress be all fitted and altered in time, before we set off? Or if it is not, how shall it be carried in pieces? And what flowers have they in far-off Gondor? Are lilies to be stitched onto the hems of the gown when blue-bells and who knows what strange blossoms will garland the path?

'Much wisdom and lore is there in Rivendell, but how the Men of Gondor mark their nuptials is not among it, written or spoken. 

'There is nothing to be done but bring everything and make do when we are there! Meanwhile, her Ladyship worries more about books and seeds!'Arwen laughs lightly, a tinkling of bells. 

'Perhaps I am negligent, Eliaril, but I must thank you for reminding me of the seeds. Here, ${PLAYER}. Take these, seeds of the Celebég Blossom. They are a gift from Rivendell to the king-to-be. For that shall come before he is my groom-to-be.

'I would ask you to do another thing for me, ${PLAYER}. There are several books upon my father's shelves that I would not have left behind. Some pertain to matters of present import, others give guidance for the challenges my Elfstone shall face as king. Will you see that they come into my father's hands?'

'Farewell, ${PLAYER}. I will see you in Gondor, if not sooner, I hope.''There is our fleet-foot traveller! I have set aside some time for you. Come, tell me of events in the East.'

You tell Elrond of all that you witnessed in Gondor, from the Paths of the Dead, through Pelargir, the Pelennor, and on to the Black Gates themselves; of the fall of Théoden, the Last Debate, and what bits you know of Sam and Frodo's own journeys. You speak some of what you have seen within the broken land of Mordor. Finally, you relate the news of the Battle Under the Trees, and how Lothlórien awaits the arrival of Glorfindel.

Elrond is silent while he listens, and when you finish, he nods.

'Much of this I guessed at, but not the whole of it, nay, not even the half. That Glorfindel gave his word to come to Lothlórien and is not there troubles me. I do not fear the worst, and yet - I feel, somehow, that your path is intertwined with his, and that the two of you have a role to play in these events that shall soon unfold.

'Give to me these books that Arwen bade you gather. She is right to call me back to serious matters, even in this happy hour. In exchange, take these seeds. They are a gift for the king-to-be in Gondor, which shall come some while before he is Arwen's groom-to-be.

'Now farewell, ${PLAYER}. Until we meet again.''Ah, those are all the parcels. Good, now lay them down there. Let me tally them.

'Yes... that is well... indeed, they are all accounted for. 

'Excellent. Now you may pick up the pile and carry them to the forges to be boxed for transport.''Do you wish me to escort you to Lady Arwen? She awaits you, I know. Very good, I will take you now.'Something has stirred up the spiders of Lhingris, and they have begun attacking the Orcs of Cirith Ungol.Atli Spider-bane believes that you should enter Cirith Ungol while the Orcs are dealing with the newly-crazed spiders of Lhingris.Find a message addressed to Ugrukhôr somewhere in Cirith UngolAtli Spider-bane is not far from the approach to Cirith Ungol, in Lhingris.'If you are of a mind to explore the tower, now might be the best time to get on with it! Orcs are given to fighting among themselves even at the best of times, but when they must deal with spiders as well, why, that must be the best of times indeed! For us, at least. Not for them, no indeed!

'Enter the tower and remind the Orcs that spiders are not the only thing they need to fear when there's a hero or two on the loose!

'What did you say you were looking for? Information on Ugrukhôr? I wouldn't say that Orcs are my area of skill, but I expect if you're looking for news on someone high up the chain of command, you'll want to climb higher up in the tower. It stands to reason, doesn't it?''Enter the tower and remind the Orcs that spiders are not the only thing they need to fear when there's a hero or two on the loose!''What is this message you found? It speaks of loyalty, and infighting, and treachery? Hmm.

'Bah! I hope you are satisfied with what you learned, for it means nothing to me. This is why I enjoy fighting spiders. There's no intrigue, no complex loyalties. You always know what side you're on, with spiders!'Atli Spider-bane, with renewed health and vigour, has heard rumours of a spider-infested valley in Gorgoroth and wishes to see it for himself.An entrance to Lhingris can be found in Durthang or by passes in Udûn or Dor Amarth.Find Atli Spider-bane in northern Lhingris, east of the Durthang entranceAtli Spider-bane is in north-eastern Lhingris, near the pass to Dor Amarth.Talk to Atli Spider-bane in northern LhingrisSpiders can be found throughout Lhingris, to Atli's delight.Defeat spiders in Lhingris (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Atli Spider-bane is within view of the the approach to Cirith Ungol.Talk to Atli Spider-bane at the approach to Cirith UngolAtli thought you should more closely inspect the approach to Cirith Ungol.Examine the approach to Cirith UngolAtli thought you should more closely inspect the approach to Cirith Ungol, ensuring that the Orcs that lie in death before the tower are truly slain.One of the Orcs lying on the approach to Cirith Ungol lived still, but he is close to death. What might he tell you of the situation in Lhingris?Talk to the mortally-wounded Orc while he yet livesAtli Spider-bane is not far from the approach to Cirith Ungol.Talk to Atli Spider-bane at the approach to Cirith Ungol'Have we met before? You have a familiar face, for a ${RACE}, but it is hard to say for certain. Now, if you were a spider, I would remember every detail, and count the days until I could clear you from the face of this Middle-earth!

'I believed my days were soon to be over, but I have experienced a resurgence of health and vigour, ${PLAYER}. I heard that the Land of Mordor was opened and the Dark Lord defeated, and that meant little to me. But when news came to me of a valley in the shadow of the Morgai fence that was crawling with spiders, I had no choice but to take notice!

'I understand that you have business in Lhingris? I do as well, for I must introduce the crawling terrors of the Valley of Webs to the axe of Atli Spider-bane! I will not waste the renewed vigour of my twilight years staying at home. To adventure, ${PLAYER}! Let us enter Lhingris, either from the fortress of Durthang or the other passes, and we will see what waits for us there.''I understand that you have business in Lhingris? I do as well, for I must introduce the crawling terrors of the Valley of Webs to the axe of Atli Spider-bane! I will not waste the renewed vigour of my twilight years staying at home. To adventure, ${PLAYER}! Let us enter Lhingris from Dor Amarth and see what waits for us there.''Ah! Do you smell that? That is the reek of spider you smell! Every one of the creatures has a different and distinctive odour, and they are all most foul. When you have fought the terrors for as long as I have, you learn to identify such things.

'We will handle my business first, and then continue on to yours. Let us slay spiders, as many as we can! Then we will find this tower you seek. Cirith Ungol, did you say? We will go there when we have left a trail of dead spiders behind us, and when we come back this way we can tread upon them like a carpet! Ha! Ha! Ha!''We will handle my business first, and then continue on to yours. Let us slay spiders, as many as we can! Then we will find this tower you seek. Cirith Ungol, did you say? We will go there when we have left a trail of dead spiders behind us, and when we come back this way we can tread upon them like a carpet! Ha! Ha! Ha!''There your tower lies. I am sure you have your reasons for wanting to see it, but I will not stick my nose into your matters. I do not need to know why you adventure in Mordor to lend you the aid of my axe, and to speak plainly I am happier not hearing the lengthy details!

'There are many bodies on the approach, Orcs and spiders both. Spiders tend to be honest concerning whether they are dead or not, but Orcs are more given to play-acting. You may want to ensure they are really dead before you set them at your back.''There are many bodies on the approach, Orcs and spiders both. Spiders tend to be honest concerning whether they are dead or not, but Orcs are more given to play-acting. You may want to ensure they are really dead before you set them at your back.''There are many bodies on the approach, Orcs and spiders both. Spiders tend to be honest concerning when they are dead or not, but Orcs are more given to play-acting. You may want to ensure they are really dead before you set them at your back.''You found one alive? What did I tell you?

'You might as well ask him what he knows. If you wait too long you will miss the chance to do so, for he will not cling to life in order to help a ${RACE}, ${PLAYER}!''If I... if I had not suffered this wound... you would be dead... ${RACE}! I would make you regret... taunting Sárnazt!'

Sárnazt coughs weakly, and blood bubbles on his lips.

'Wretched spiders! They rarely... bothered the Orcs of the Tower.... but something changed. They used to take... one or two of the weakest... but not like this! So many... there were so many!

'When the Mountain erupted and the earth shook... many of the tunnels were exposed... and the creatures... swarmed from their pits!

'Róglarg thinks he can... keep them out...'

Sárnazt is seized by a long fit of coughing, and when he speaks again it is for the last time.

'No one knows... what happened to Shelob...''Seems like the Orcs and the spiders had an arrangement, but something's happened and they're not getting along so well these days. Ha! Serves 'em both right, don't it? That's what happens when you try to make nice with a spider. Can't keep watch on all those eyes and all those legs at once, can you?

'It sounds as if this Shelob must have been the one keeping these spiders under control. I guess it falls to me and my axe to do the job now!'Lady Galadriel wishes to speak to you.Orophin will take you from the Lothlórien Court to see Lady Galadriel in her garden.Talk to Orophin in the Court of LothlórienLegolas wishes to talk with you in the Court of Lothlórien.Talk to Legolas'It is good you are here, ${CLASS}. The Lady Galadriel wished to see you as soon as you arrived. Orophin, would you lead our friend to her Ladyship?''It is time you returned to Lothlórien, friend. I have received word that Lady Galadriel wishes to speak to you. You should return to her court as soon as you are able.''Come, I will lead you to the Lady in her garden.''You seem shaken, friend. Are you well?'

Legolas gazes into your face and seems to understand something.

'I too have spent a moment with the Lady in her garden and seen the others of The Company do the same. I have some inkling of your mind, perhaps! Best, I think, to let it pass. Let us return to Mordor. Let action replace reflection for a time!'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Thorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Go to Thorin's throne room in the Hall under the MountainGimli, son of Glóin, has come to Erebor to speak with the King under the Mountain.Talk to Gimli in the throne room in the Hall under the MountainThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the MountainDwarves of Erebor that may wish to accompany Gimli to the Glittering Caves can be found throughout the Hall under the Mountain.Gimli is near the provisioner in the Hall under the Mountain, arranging for the things he will need on his expedition.Talk to Gimli by the provisioner in the Hall under the Mountain'I daresay it is time you reported once again to King Thorin about your adventures in Mordor. Tell him how things progress, and see if he has any further requests for you.''I understand you know Gimli, son of Glóin? He has returned to Erebor after much time away, and is speaking with King Thorin.''I daresay it is time you reported once again to King Thorin about your adventures in Mordor. Tell him how things progress, and see if he has any further requests for you.''I understand you know Gimli, son of Glóin? He has returned to Erebor after much time away, and is speaking with King Thorin.''This is a welcome surprise, ${PLAYER}! I heard you had pledged to fight on behalf of Durin's folk, but I did not expect to see you within the Hall under the Mountain! I am here for only a short time, if King Thorin will favour my undertaking.

'I await now his word. He has considered my proposal, and sits now in judgement of it.''I await now the word of the King under the Mountain. He has considered my proposal, and sits now in judgement of it.''You have arrived at a useful time, ${PLAYER}, for I understand that you have stood in battle with Gimli and know somewhat of his character. Is he steadfast and loyal? Does he hold to his word?'

You assure Thorin that Gimli is honourable to his friends and shows no mercy to his enemies.

'That is good to hear, and it meets my own expectation. Glóin's son has requested permission to lead a company of dwarves far to the south, to a place he names the Glittering Caves, where he will establish a new dwarf colony. The tale he has told is one of great beauty, but even greater is the promise of the beauty he thinks to make there.

'I have arrived at my decision. Gimli, son of Glóin, by my authority as King under the Mountain I give to you all rights necessary to establish your colony and to be named the Lord of the Glittering Caves, with all the responsibilities that entails. Work for the betterment of Durin's folk, and report to me of your progress. Take such dwarves with you as you deem necessary for the success of this endeavor.''You may help Gimli select the dwarves that will accompany him to the Glittering Caves, if you are of a mind to do so.''Thank you for putting in that kind word for me, ${PLAYER}! Now we must assemble some dwarves who desire to help me establish a dwarf colony in the Glittering Caves. It is a long way from here, but I am certain there are many who are up to the task! Help me find these dwarves!''Thank you for your help finding dwarves willing to accompany me, my friend. I have found a number of willing allies as well, and together they have formed a good company. There are still many preparations to make, but the promise of the Glittering Caves has put a spring in my step and a fire in my heart!

'We will make a great work there, and fashion the Glittering Caves into a wonder even more beautiful than it began! I thank you again for your words on my behalf. You will always be welcome at the Glittering Caves, once our work there is done! Take care in Mordor, my friend. It would be a terrible pity if you were slain before you beheld my masterwork, ${PLAYER}! Ha! Ha! Ha!'Merry and Pippin have been pondering the revelations of their last foray into Mordor, and come to some conclusions.You should talk to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion to learn what serious matters they have been discussing.Talk to PippinYou should talk to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion to learn what serious matters they have been discussing.Talk to MerryPippin and Merry have asked you to visit Flooded Isengard and look in after Saruman, the Ents, and anything that will reveal what the three half-orcs were talking about.Return to Flooded IsengardYou have found Quickbeam in the waters of Flooded Isengard, who has told you that Treebeard and several other Ents are gathered up ahead on the western side of Orthanc, discussing Saruman.Find the other EntsYou have found Quickbeam in the waters of Flooded Isengard, who has told you that Treebeard and several other Ents are gathered on the western side of Orthanc, discussing Saruman.Talk to TreebeardTreebeard suggested that you search for other clues around Flooded Isengard, while you wait for Saruman to emerge from Orthanc.You discovered many more barrels of pipe-weed than Merry and Pippin had found, and packaged over the course of nearly two years, as well. You should return to Treebeard where he stands before the west face of Orthanc in Flooded Isengard.Return to TreebeardYou should approach Orthanc from where the Ents stand on the western side of Flooded Isengard and see if Saruman will speak with you.Approach OrthancSaruman stands high upon his tower on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

Saruman has asked you to <rgb=#FF00FF>listen</rgb> to his plea.Saruman stands high upon his tower on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

Saruman has said that if you free him, he will heal, regrow, and make reparations for the wrongs done in his name. You should <rgb=#FF00FF>look around</rgb> to consider the weight of his words.Saruman stands high upon his tower on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

You should <rgb=#FF00FF>listen</rgb> once more to Saruman's plea.Saruman stands high upon his tower on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

Saruman has suggested that he could aid in the subjugation and cleansing of Mordor, still covered in the corruptions, lieutenants, and shadow of Sauron as it is. You should consider the dethroned land and what Saruman might do there.Consider dethroned MordorSaruman stands high upon his tower on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

You should <rgb=#FF00FF>listen</rgb> one last time to Saruman's plea.Saruman stands high upon his tower on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

Saruman has suggested that he could employ his vast wisdom to bring prosperity to the Free Peoples. You should consider how this possibility might turn out.Consider the Free PeoplesTreebeard and another Ent stand before Orthanc on the western side of Flooded Isengard.

You have heard Saruman's three pleas and seen through each ploy. Now you should return to Treebeard and see what he has to say.Go back to TreebeardYou should return to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion with word of all that you have seen and heard.Return to Pippin and Merry'Masters Peregrin and Meriadoc have been asking after you once again, ${PLAYER}. You would do well the hasten to Bâr Thorenion, for they were grave of countenance.''It is good you are here, just when we've wanted you, as usual, ${PLAYER}. We have some grave matters to discuss.''Do you remember what those squinty fellows said? The ones we caught talking to each other in Mordor? They wanted to sell news, and that news, as best as Merry and I could tell, had to do with a Wizard and the Halfling Lands. Gandalf and the Shire, we asked ourselves? Well, if it was not the Shire, then we're both dwarves. But Gandalf? That just didn't feel right. 

'And then we tried to think of any other Wizards, and the first that came to mind was that snake, Saruman. And then it all began to fall suspiciously into place. Did we not see fellows just such as those three among Saruman's rabble? I think we did.

'Well and that brings us around to the favour we hoped to ask you. Someone has got to go back there and see what that old, white sweet-talker is up to. And poke around and see if there is anything to explain any more of what those three were talking about.''How we would love to go with you and see old Treebeard and Quickbeam again. But that should require yet more leave from Lieges, and besides, we are much in demand among the Gondorian just now. It would be quite impossible!''Why if it isn't our little friend the ${RACE}. What has brought you all the way back here, I wonder? For the last I heard of it, you and many others had gone east, far east, to deal with matters quite out of the soil of Ents. 

'Perhaps it is to some message you bear to Treebeard? Or to question that Wizard. Or... former Wizard? I am not quite sure what he is now, or how to name him. Well, as it happens you will find both of them up ahead, for Treebeard and several other Ents have just been gathered to watch him. They have only been there several days, and might be gone in several more, so you are lucky to catch them.''Hm, hoom! Here is ${PLAYER} again. Remarkable that you should show up just as we were discussing all the events that had led to this.'

Treebeard indicated the flooded waste around them.

'And what, really, should be done with Saruman. Oh, yes, I know, hm, hm, Mithrandir bade us keep him up there. And now that we have settled in, we are not in any mood to be hasty, hoom! But still the question remains, for how long? And by whose reckoning? A ${RACE}'s such as yours? That is a while, I suppose. It all, even the Elves', seems hasty to we Ents, and so we find it exceedingly difficult to judge. Hm, hm! And yet, all the same, I cannot help but feel it has been long enough and that we should release him.

'But come, I am being most inhospitable. Tell me, what has brought you to us here today, hm?'

You tell Treebeard the riddle of what you overheard in Mordor.

'Saruman? It might very well be Saruman, hoom hom! He is hiding somewhere within his rock just now, but will be out upon that high place there very soon, I believe. Why don't you look around for anything else to explain this little mystery, until he is out, and then we shall see what we shall see.''Saruman is not out yet, but he will be in a little while, hoom! Why don't you poke around this place and find what you can find, then come back here.'Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 1 March 3019Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 3 August 3018Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 11 July 3018Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 5 July 3018Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 14 May 3018Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 22 Sept 3017Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 9 August 3017Hornblower Pipe-weed, packaged 25 June 3017'The ${PLAYER} returns, and so soon! Hm, hm, but not too late, either, I think, for there is Saruman up high upon his tower. You have come all this way. Why not you go and see what he has to say? We may hope he may answer your riddle, and if he does not, perhaps you can help us decide the question of whether we have kept him locked in there too long.''Hrum, brum, hroom! You have not had such a courteous conversation with old Saruman after all, have you?'

You tell Treebeard what Saruman said and what you judge he meant by it. The old Ent's eyes grow sad.

'Yes, hrm. Well, hm. Perhaps letting him go just now would be a bit hasty, after all. I feel sorry for him at times, for I do not like to cage any creature of any temperament in stone as he is caged. And yet you have shown us that he is unbroken and might still try to do much harm if he were freed. 

'No, hm. I shall not release him. Not just now, at any rate.

'I am delighted that you have paid us this visit all the same, ${PLAYER}, and you must send my regards to the halflings Pippin and Merry, and remind them that they are always welcome in my woods!''Why, if it isn't ${PLAYER}! Have you been to Isengard? And you are back already? You must tell us all you saw.'

As you tell it, the two hobbits' eyes go wide with amazement.

'Slippery old snake, Saruman is,' Pippin says at last. 'But I'll tell you what I do not like: it is that bit about helping the Free Peoples. Something about it makes the hair stand up on the back of my neck.

'And then there is the matter of those pipe-weed barrels. There were many more than the two we found, somewhere, I suppose. It was very clever of you to take note of the dates stamped on them. We must think about that, Merry. It is quite a lot of time between early summer of two years ago and the first of this very March. What's he up to, do you suppose?''If you remember, the Ents are supposed to have kept watch on him up in his tower, where he is a prisoner of sorts. Perhaps you will see some of our Ent-friends when you are there. If you do, give them our regards and find out how they are doing for us. At this rate, it feels that it may be a long while before we might see them again.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Elessar and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.King Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne roomLegolas and Gimli have come to the throne room of King Elessar and wish to speak with you.Talk to Gimli in the throne room of King ElessarTalk to Legolas in the throne room of King ElessarThe gift of the dwarves Gimli brought to Minas Tirith is currently on the south-east side of the Second Circle.Find the gift of the dwarves on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas TirithThe gift of the Elves Legolas brought to Minas Tirith is currently on the south-east side of the Second Circle.Find the gift of the Elves on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas TirithDúnor is on the south-east side of the Second Circle in Minas Tirith, and is having trouble dealing with an unruly crowd.Talk to Dúnor on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas TirithDúnor has asked for your help in calming the unruly crowd on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas Tirith.Speak to members of the unruly crowd and try to calm them downDúnor has asked for your help in calming the unruly crowd on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas Tirith.Speak to members of the unruly crowd and try to calm them downDúnor has asked for your help in calming the unruly crowd on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas Tirith.Speak to members of the unruly crowd and try to calm them downDúnor is on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas Tirith.Talk to Dúnor on the south-east side of the Second Circle, in Minas TirithKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'King Elessar waits for you in his throne room, and will be glad to see you again.''King Elessar waits at the throne for you. He will be pleased to have your assistance.''King Elessar waits for you in his throne room, and will be glad to see you again.''King Elessar waits at the throne for you. He will be pleased to have your assistance.''You have returned at an opportune moment, ${PLAYER}, for Legolas and Gimli have come to the city and will be pleased to speak with you. They may know somewhat of your adventures in Mordor, but I am certain there is more to tell.''Legolas and Gimli will enjoy hearing of your exploits in Mordor, ${PLAYER}, as I have.''${PLAYER}, how goes the adventuring in Mordor? I hope you have found plenty of Orcs to slay!

'I have returned to the city with gifts for Aragorn and Gondor, but the finest gift will have to wait until my plan to settle the Glittering Caves has been carried out. I hope to repair and rebuild the gate of the city with mithril, but that will have to wait for a time. Still, I have already done a great deal of the planning in my head, and trust me when I tell you it will be magnificent!

'But you can see one of the gifts already! When you are finished catching up with me and Legolas here, you can see it in the Second Circle, further down in the city.''We are met in friendship, ${PLAYER}. Like Gimli with the dwarves, I have brought gifts for the city from the Elves, though our gifts are of a different sort: flowering trees and plants with which to enliven the various levels of Minas Tirith. You can see some of the things we brought in the Second Circle, once you are done conversing with Gimli and me.

'I asked Aragorn if he desired a replacement for the White Tree, since a dead tree does not seem well-suited for Minas Tirith's future, but he refused. He responded that there can be no replacement for it in all of Middle-earth, to his sorrow. He is not wrong, but I wish it were otherwise.

'Still, the many plants I have brought to the city should help to brighten the scenery and the outlook of the people who live here, and I hope they will also bring a measure of happiness to its king.''You can see the gift I brought to the city on the south-east side of the Second Circle, ${PLAYER}!''You can see the gift I brought to the city on the south-east side of the Second Circle, my friend.''What is happening in the Second Circle, ${PLAYER}? I have heard reports of unruly crowds in the city, threatening violence?''People of the city, listen to me! You must remain calm. There is no call for violence!'

Dúnor sees you and motions at the crowd.

'${PLAYER}, I need your help! These people are angry that the king has allowed the Haradrim into the city, and if he does make peace with them I don't like to think what these folk will do! This situation threatens to get out of hand. Will they listen to you?''I need your help! These people are angry that the king has allowed the Haradrim into the city, and if he does make peace with them I don't like to think what these folk will do! This situation threatens to get out of hand. Will they listen to you?''I need your help! These people are angry that the king has allowed the Haradrim into the city, and if he does make peace with them I don't like to think what these folk will do! This situation threatens to get out of hand. Will they listen to you?''I need your help! These people are angry that the king has allowed the Haradrim into the city, and if he does make peace with them I don't like to think what these folk will do! This situation threatens to get out of hand. Will they listen to you?''I think that may have defused the worst of it, but they are still angry. I cannot say I blame them, to be truthful with you. These Haradrim are only suing for peace because they lost the battle and the war. If they had won, they would have put us all to the sword and destroyed the White City stone by stone.

'But Elessar is my king, and I will do as he commands. I only hope the people of the city will do the same!''Go to the king and tell him that the Withered Tree has stirred up further trouble, but we have it mostly in hand for now.''The Withered Tree has been sowing discord since my coronation, ${PLAYER}, but my allowing the Haradrim embassy into the city has stirred them into a tempest. They are a group of malcontents who think Gondor is doomed to ruin, and they are not allowing the defeat of Sauron to change their opinion!

'I do not completely trust the Haradrim, but in my heart I know that we must make peace with them if Gondor is to maintain its safety. I understand that this is not easy for my people, but I believe that it is right.

'I will do what I can to ensure the Withered Tree do not stir the townspeople to violence. Good fortune to you, ${PLAYER}! I look forward to your return, and hope that by then this matter will be settled and we will have peace.'Merry and Pippin have grown determined to go back into Mordor once more, this time in search of facts.You should meet Merry and Pippin at the Foothold in Udûn.Talk to MerryGolodhon, Merry, and Pippin can be found in the Foothold camp in Udûn.

You should talk to Golodhon when you are ready to head back into Mordor.Talk to GolodhonYou should talk to Merry and Pippin again in Bâr Thorenion, now that you are all safely returned from Mordor.Talk to MerryTalk to Pippin'Ah, there you are, ${PLAYER}. You should seek Masters Meriadoc and Peregrin in the Udûn Foothold as soon as you are able. It seems they have resolved to venture back into Mordor.''Ah, there you are ${PLAYER}. We are going back into Mordor. We feel that we simply must. Will you come with us? We shall meet in the Udûn Foothold then? Then we are agreed!''I have other duties to which I must attend, but I can go some little way with you, and see that you are headed on the right course.''What are we waiting for? Let us be off.''When you are ready, ${PLAYER}, Golodhon will point us to where he believes those three churls have got to.''I wonder if we shall ever unravel the mystery we stumbled across out in Mordor. Somehow I do not feel much hope, although I expect we shall have to turn homewards sometime, and then we shall see what we shall see. 

'I will confess, I am quite down. Yes, I think for a spell we should try to dwell on brighter things.''There, now that I've had a bit to eat I feel half myself again. If that's all that Mordor has in store for us, you will find me elsewhere! I do hope Merry is all right.

'You seem long about the face, friend. Chin up! We will find some pleasant diversions to wash the taste of all that out, quite.''I intend to have some answers before the day is through, no matter the inconvenience.''We have found Golodhon here, who has come back from out there, and believes he knows where those disreputable fellows should be.

'When we are ready, he will point us on our way. Shall we go?'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Elessar and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.King Elessar's guard Malthengil is near the throne.Talk to Malthengil by King Elessar's throneKing Elessar is about to pronounce his judgements on a number of important matters, and his guard Malthengil has offered that you be allowed to watch.Talk to Malthengil and witness the pronouncements of King ElessarKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'King Elessar will have need of you in Minas Tirith, ${PLAYER}. You should return to him and see if you can be of service there as well as in Mordor.''King Elessar is about to pronounce his judgements in a number of important matters. Present yourself to Malthengil and you may be able to attend.''King Elessar will have need of you in Minas Tirith, ${PLAYER}. You should return to him and see if you can be of service there as well as in Mordor.''King Elessar is about to pronounce his judgements in a number of important matters. Present yourself to Malthengil and you may be able to attend.''Ah, ${PLAYER}. King Elessar was looking forward to a report from you about your further adventures in Mordor, but you have arrived in time for him to make a number of pronouncements. Speak to me if you would like to attend, and you can witness the judgements of Elessar.''It should not be long, ${PLAYER}, but there are a few matters to which the king must attend.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I thank you for your report, ${PLAYER}. It is good to know that Mordor is being explored and the evil within it combatted.

'I am not entirely comfortable remaining in the city while others adventure in dangerous lands. Perhaps one day I will join you, ${PLAYER}, but for now the city and the kingdom require more managing.

'I await your return, my friend, and the tales you will bring with you!'Travel to the places where Elves dwell, seeking word of Glorfindel and gifts for Aragorn's taking of the throne.You should talk to Lady Galadriel in the Court of Lothlórien.Talk to Lady GaladrielYou should ask Legolas about the nature of the request he has for you.Talk to LegolasYou should talk to Galadriel once more before you go.Talk to Lady GaladrielLady Galadriel suggested you begin by visiting Certhíril in Mirobel and ask her about Glorfindel and a gift of seeds.Visit Certhíril in MirobelLook for Glorfindel, or his disciple, at his accustomed spot in Rivendell, below Elrond's House and above the waterfall.Visit Glorfindel's spot in RivendellSeek Dorthaneth in the caves of the Avorrim, near Edhellond, in Belfalas, and ask her about Glorfindel and a gift of seeds.Seek Dorthaneth near EdhellondDorthaneth suggested that Dorongor, in Duillond, might know something of what Glorfindel seeks and where he has gone. You should ask him about that and also a gift of seeds.Seek Dorongur in DuillondDorongur has suggested that you travel to the library in Lin Giliath and look for Gildor Inglorion, whom you can ask about Glorfindel and a gift of seeds.Seek Gildor in Tham GiliathGildor has suggested you look around Tham Giliath for a clue as to what Glorfindel found.Check the book-shelves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should show the record that you found to Gildor in Tham Giliath.Show the record to GildorReturn to Legolas in the Court of Lothlórien with the many gifts of seed.Now you should show the record you found in Tham Giliath to Lady Galadriel.'Hail, friend ${CLASS}. I am glad you have returned to Lothlórien's court, for you are wanted here. Go, speak with her Ladyship.''Hail, friend ${CLASS}. It is time for you to return again to Lothlórien's Court, for you are wanted there.''I have pondered the things you and I saw in my pool, ${PLAYER}, and some clarity has come to my mind as to their meaning. Lord Elrond's advice was indeed true: you have a role to play in the events unfolding and, I think in bringing Glorfindel to our council.

'This I would ask of you: see places where my kind, the Elves, dwell, and ask after him. He is far-travelled and known to many. But where should you begin? Perhaps... Mirobel. Yes. Seek Certhíril there. She has seen much and knows even more.

'Ah, but before you go, speak to your friend, Legolas. He, too, has something to ask of you, I believe.''Ah, my friend. I have been thinking on the gift of seeds you were entrusted with in Imladris. I must confess I am struck with envy. Why, if I could gather some dozen of the ferns and blossoms found in Mirkwood... but this journey has taken me far enough from Mordor and my friend, Gimli.

'If you are setting out for places where Elves dwell, however, perhaps you might ask them for such gifts. We will gather them together, and what a fine garden the new king Elessar will have!''Indeed, you may certainly do both at once. Go, gather seeds, and learn what you can of Glorfindel as you go.

'Begin with Certhíril and I think your path will unfold itself before you.''It is strange! Just yesterday a pupil, or rather a disciple of Glorfindel's stood where you stood and asked what you ask: have I seen him? Alas, I have not.
 
'I sent her on to Imladris, for I have heard he has often spent time there, lately, standing in contemplation below Elrond's House and above the waterfall. I suggest you follow her, and if you do not find both, you might find one.

'Your other boon meets a more fortunate answer: these are seeds of the Gaerthond Vine, with leaves of shining silver, and morning-blooming flowers of dusky blue and gold.'The Elf woman blinks at you in disbelief.

'Is it you... ${PLAYER}? It is I, Glathlírel. Do you not recognize me? I have exchanged my fine armour for the traveller's garb.

'Does this mean that you, too, have come looking for Glorfindel? I do not know whether to take heart from the coincidence or take it as a darker sign. But tell me, why have you come?'

You tell Glathlírel of the Battle Under the Trees and war council in the Court of Lothlórien.

'Then you have better cause to find him than I, who have come looking only because of a troubling dream. Have you heard of the Elves who live hidden in a cave in Belfalas? The Avorrim, they are called. Why do you not seek one called Dorthaneth there? She is wise beyond years. I will go west, to Lindon and look for him there. Then we shall meet back at Galadriel's Court.

'Oh, and lastly, by strange coincidence, I do indeed have a seed-gift for your friend, Legolas. These will grow into Rithlas, and are from Annúminas. I know it will mean much to Gondor's new king.''Radiant Glorfindel? I have not seen his face or heard his fair words for many lives of Men. His name has come to me here, where I stand, from time to time, however.

'Here is my advice to you, young ${CLASS}. Do not attempt to find an Elf the likes of Glorfindel, for he will not be found if he does not wish to be. Follow instead what he sought. If I am not mistaken, one Dorongur in Duillond knew of the thing Glorfindel seeks, and may even know where he has gone.

'Ah ha ha. Do not go leaping off just yet. You came for a second reason and that you shall have without riddles. These seeds are Tuilloth. It grows to cover the ground, and the fine white flowers it bears make it like a mirror of the night sky beneath your feet.''Well met, ${RACE}. Let me look into your eyes. I see no deception or ill will there, so I shall tell you what I know. Glorfindel came some few days past to consult on words Gil-galad spoke very long ago, when first he set foot in the Havens of Lindon. They concerned times to come, times that he would not live to see.

'I myself knew little of this speech, nor was I certain who had heard it in its entirety. Therefore I pointed Glorfindel toward Lin Giliath. I felt that if it had been written down, some record might be there. Go to Tham Giliath and speak to Gildor Inglorian. He will know if Glorfindel came that way or no.'

Then, with a twinkle in his eye, Dorongur produces a small pouch.

'Nor could I forget these, a fair gift fairly asked for. Lodeledh will grow tall and with sweet-scented leaves cast dappled shade beneath its boughs.''Ah, ${CLASS}. You are like the gull who flies after the wind-wafted sail. Glorfindel has been here and gone again. I greeted him but in passing, but I believe he left here having found what he sought. Perhaps it was one of the texts here? Look for yourself and see if you can find it.''What is this? It is a very old record, indeed. I think it likely you have found what you sought. I would bring this to Celeborn and Galadriel in Lórien, if I were you. They will know full well what to make of it.

'But before you do, have these. They are Parthríf pods, and will grow into ferns of uncommon intricacy and form. It is a fine gift for a new king.''Why, what is this? You have quite outdone yourself, ${PLAYER}. I will find a suited vessel for this gift and bear it to Gondor, to give to our friend, the new king!''What is this you have found, ${PLAYER}? A clue to Glorfindel's whereabouts? This record is old indeed. Lord Celeborn and I shall need some time to pore over it. We will send for you when we are done. And - you have my heartfelt gratitude for all you have done.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Thorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the MountainThe dwarf heroes sent into Mordor by King Thorin have not reported back in some time, and are missing somewhere in Dor Amarth, south of Lûghash.

King Thorin has sent you to find the missing dwarf heroes in Mordor.Find the missing dwarf heroes in Dor Amarth, in MordorYou found Dómor in Dor Amarth. Does he know where the other dwarf heroes have gone?Talk to Dómor south of Lûghash, in Dor AmarthDómor asked you to search to the north, in the direction of Lûghash, for the treacherous Mogi.Search to the north for MogiSearch near the road for MogiSearch near the base of Lûghash for MogiDómor has gone to the road south of Lûghash. Did he find Mogi?Talk to Dómor south of Lûghash, near the roadThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the Mountain'I think it is time you reported back to King Thorin about your adventures, wouldn't you say? He may have another task for you.''I heard that King Thorin is worried about something, but I do not know what it might be. You should report to him of your adventures in Mordor and learn what so concerns him.''I think it is time you reported back to King Thorin about your adventures, wouldn't you say? He may have another task for you.''I heard that King Thorin is worried about something, but I do not know what it might be. You should report to him of your adventures in Mordor and learn what so concerns him.''I am pleased to see you return, ${PLAYER}, for you are the only adventurer who has properly reported back to me! I understand that Mordor is full of danger, but surely these heroes understand that their king needs updates!'

King Thorin scowls through his beard.

'They mock me with "Stonehelm," but if I did not have the responsibilities of kingship I would be among the first dwarves seeking glory in Mordor! Alas, I must stay here. You, however... yes, you must return to Mordor and see what has happened to these so-called adventurers. The last word I had from Mogi placed him in Dor Amarth, south of Lûghash, so that is where you should begin your search.''You must return to Mordor and see what has happened to these so-called adventurers. The last word I had from Mogi placed him in Dor Amarth, south of Lûghash, so that is where you should begin your search.''You must return to Mordor and see what has happened to these so-called adventurers. The last word I had from Mogi placed him in Dor Amarth, south of Lûghash, so that is where you should begin your search.''${PLAYER}! King Thorin has been betrayed! No, not him alone, all of Durin's Folk have been insulted and maligned!

'Just to the north of here, I encountered Eirek and Mogi quarrelling over treasure. It was a red gem embedded in a loose rock. Any dwarf might improve the quality of this ruby with careful labour, but a skilled dwarf could do much more! It would be a fine gift to present to King Thorin, and Eirek said as much, but then Mogi slew him where he stood! He stole the ruby and fled. I would have given chase, but a band of Orcs came near at that moment and I was forced to retreat.

'But now that you are here, we can hunt him down and bring Thorin's wrath upon the traitor! We must find Mogi and make him pay for this treachery! You search to the north, and I will search to the south and see if he snuck that way, and perhaps we can snare him. The ground is treacherous, and you may need to go out of your way to properly search for Mogi. Look for him on both sides of the chasm to the north, and to the south-east of Lûghash! One of us must find him and make him pay for his treachery!''Hmm, I do not see him anywhere, ${PLAYER}.''It seems Mogi has left no trace behind. A thousand curses upon him!

'Return to King Thorin and tell him that Dómor will keep searching for the traitor. I will come back to the Hall under the Mountain when I have avenged this insult, or when I have found treasure worthy of the king. Perhaps I will pluck the fine ruby from Mogi's cold hands, eh?''Return to King Thorin and tell him that Dómor will keep searching for the traitor. I will come back to the Hall under the Mountain when I have avenged this insult, or when I have found treasure worthy of the king. Perhaps I will pluck the fine ruby from Mogi's cold hands, eh?''Well, ${PLAYER}, have you found any word from these so-called heroes?'

You tell King Thorin what you learned from Dómor of Mogi's treachery.

'This is an insult to me, and to all dwarves! I want that traitor found! My axe will take his life, but not before his beard is shorn and he is made an object of ridicule. I will give my subjects someone else to mock, someone who deserves it!

'Eirek was on the king's business, and it is an insult to the king to interfere with such! Unacceptable, ${PLAYER}, unacceptable! May Dómor find this traitor and bring him here. I wish you good fortune on your own adventures, and if you find Mogi first I trust that you will bring him before me with all speed!'Merry and Pippin propose a very different kind of expedition.You should talk to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion to learn what sort of expedition they are setting off on now.Talk to MerryYou should talk with Merry and Pippin now that you have returned to Bâr Thorenion from your meal, and hear their thoughts on the discussion that took place.Talk to Merry'${PLAYER}! How would you feel about a very different sort of expedition?''The Periain were looking for you once more. In fact, they were here only shortly before you arrived, and very excitable about some kind of expedition, it seemed to me.''We are going down to Cormallen - to see Frodo and Sam! It has been a little while and they sent word that they are both feeling much recovered.

'Will you come and join us for a simple meal? Do not worry, you will feel just like one of the hobbits. There is always more room at a round table, as my uncle used to say!''You are coming, aren't you? Of course you are!''Oh... I have over-eaten, I am afraid. Now I am done with food, adventuring, and most other things for a good while! A good doze is the answer. And then who knows what may come up after I wake?''If you ask me, ${PLAYER}, that visit was long overdue. Now we must get the two of them up here sometime soon. 

'Don't you worry, either. We shan't break our promise to Frodo. Although... I have not quite finished puzzling over the riddles those fellows left for us. I have a queer feeling it may not be a closed book quite, when all is said and done.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Elessar and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.King Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne roomHasong is in King Elessar's throne room.Talk to Hasong in the throne roomThe Haven tavern is on the south-east side of the Third Circle of the city.Mauthoi's body is on eastern side of the Haven tavern.Examine Mauthoi's bodySeveral of the people in the Haven might have seen something when Mauthoi was murdered.A tavern-worker has begun to clean up some of the blood. Did he see anything?Talk to tavern-workerThe tavern-worker has scrubbed blood-stains near the door, but does the trail continue out of the tavern?Look for bloodstains outside the tavern and follow themYou found a body and a knife hidden a short distance from the tavern. Is this another victim, or the murderer? Or is he both?Examine the discarded knifeExamine the second victimDúnor is in the Haven tavern.King Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne roomDuinhir is in King Elessar's throne room.Talk to Duinhir in King Elessar's throne roomDuinhir may have last seen his knife at the parapet on the north side of the Fifth Circle, but he does not really remember.Find the location where Duinhir believes he last saw his knifeA Gondorian man seeks to get your attention on the parapet on the north side of the Fifth Circle.Talk to folk near the spot Duinhir describedParthadan, the Warden of the Green, is outside the Dome of the Sun on the north-east side of the Sixth Circle.Confront Parthadan outside the Dome of the SunDúnor has come with you to apprehend Parthadan.Talk to Dúnor outside the Dome of the SunKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'It is time you returned to King Elessar in Minas Tirith, ${PLAYER}. Tell him how the conquest of Mordor progresses.''You should speak to the king. Something terrible has happened; I have never seen him like this.''It is time you returned to King Elessar in Minas Tirith, ${PLAYER}. Tell him how the conquest of Mordor progresses.''You should speak to the king. Something terrible has happened; I have never seen him like this.''The unthinkable has happened, ${PLAYER}. Mauthoi, one of the Haradrim emissaries, has been murdered. He was under my protection! He was in my city! The Withered Tree have gone too far this time, and they have endangered the peace we so desperately need.

'This must be carefully handled or it will mean war. The murderer must be found. I will try to keep the situation from getting any worse, but I understand Hasong's feelings. They came here under a banner of truce, and someone betrayed that peace. This is a grave injustice, and it must be set right!

'Learn from Hasong what you can, and then go in search of the murderer. Only his blood can save the infant peace now.''This must be carefully handled or it will mean war. The murderer must be found. I will try to keep the situation from getting any worse, but I understand Hasong's feelings. They came here under a banner of truce, and someone betrayed that peace. This is a grave injustice, and it must be set right!

'Learn from Hasong what you can, and then go in search of the murderer. Only his blood can save the infant peace now.''What vile snakes live in this country! We came to this place for Kerem-thak, as was our need, and this is how you return our trust? Dhûrto! Betrayal!

'Mauthoi went by himself to tavern in your city, trusting the laws of Kerem-thak would hold. He trusted that people of Elessar's city would respect the wishes of Elessar... if those wishes are true. Or is it that Elessar sends another message, hiding his real intent behind others, and secretly laughs at death of Mauthoi, ordering it be done? Dhûrto, most foul, betrayal of the highest order!

'If ${PLAYER} came to Yâd Hakar, spoke to the Khas, and was promised safety... how would Elessar react to murder of ${RACE}? Would it mean war? Hasong asks, "Why should this be different?" Prove to me this was not done by Elessar, or I will return to Khas Rûktar with words of Gondor's true face: no different than lies... no different than Mordor! Dhûrto most vile!

'Mauthoi was killed at tavern called "Haven" in Third Circle of city.''Mauthoi went by himself to tavern in your city, trusting the laws of Kerem-thak would hold. He trusted that people of Elessar's city would respect the wishes of Elessar... if those wishes are true. Or is it that Elessar sends another message, hiding his real intent behind others, and secretly laughs at death of Mauthoi, ordering it be done? Dhûrto!

'If ${PLAYER} came to Yâd Hakar, spoke to the Khas, and was promised safety... how would Elessar react to murder of ${RACE}? Would it mean war? Hasong asks, "Why should this be different?" Prove to me that this was not done by Elessar, or I will return to Khas Rûktar with words of Gondor's true face: no different than lies... no different than Mordor! Dhûrto most vile!

'Mauthoi was killed at tavern called "Haven" in Third Circle of city.''That word, "dhûrto"... it is the word Hasong uses for "betrayal." But the Haradrim emissaries are not the only ones who stand betrayed. This treachery is a betrayal of Gondor, and of me, ${PLAYER}.

'I want the murderer found. We cannot let this crime against Gondor stand unpunished. Search the tavern where Mauthoi was killed for any trace of the murderer. It is the Haven, and it is on the south-east side of the Third Circle of the city.''Ah, ${PLAYER}. It is good to see you again. I have not forgotten how you helped me calm the unruly crowd the other day. Not enough, it seems; one of them took matters into his own hands. Did King Elessar ask you to look into the Haradrim murder?

'I have already spoken with some of the folk here, but you are welcome to see if they have remembered anything. I haven't moved his body yet, so you can check and see if there's anything I missed. He's over there.

'I have no love for the Haradrim, but this wasn't right. Curse the Withered Tree, they've gone and made things worse!''The body is right over there. I haven't moved him yet, but I don't want to leave him there for too long.'A cursory examination is enough to reveal the cause of Mauthoi's death. He was stabbed in the back by a short, bladed weapon, probably a knife or a dagger. It seems that his killer then carefully lowered him to the floor so as not to attract too much attention.

The murderer must have taken the weapon with him, because there is no sign of it.A cursory examination is enough to reveal the cause of Mauthoi's death. He was stabbed in the back by a short, bladed weapon, probably a knife or a dagger. It seems that his killer then carefully lowered him to the floor so as not to attract too much attention.

The murderer must have taken the weapon with him, because there is no sign of it.'Someone here might have seen something. If you talk to them, it could be they will remember something they forgot to tell me. Unlikely, but possible.''I didn't see anything I haven't already told Dúnor. This place is usually all a-bustle, even if the Lords of Gondor haven't been themselves since the battle on the field. First thing I noticed was that fellow lying on the ground there, and I figured he was well in his cups. When I went over to him I saw the blood, and called for the guards.

'You think the Withered Tree did this? It's not right to kill a fellow as wants peace, but you do have to wonder how many good soldiers died at his hands, or at the hands of folk like him. Not excusin' it, mind, just something to weigh, I'd say.''It was dark in here. It's always pretty dark, so that isn't unusual. I can't be sure, but I think I saw a cloaked figure moving for the door a moment before anyone noticed the dead man. He may have had a limp, or an odd way of walking? I can't say for certain, but the more I think about it the more I wonder if this fellow might be the culprit.

'He might have had a beard? Maybe not. It was dark in here! I wish I had paid more attention, but you never think of that at the time, do you?

'I can't be certain about the limp, or the beard. I think he was cloaked, but it was awful dark in here.''No one has remembered anything especially useful, ${PLAYER}. Whether there really was a cloaked or hooded figure here or not, it seems to me everyone has created one in their memory: a member of the Withered Tree brandishing a knife, slipping away before anyone thought to look at him.'A cursory examination is enough to reveal the cause of Mauthoi's death. He was stabbed in the back by a short, bladed weapon, probably a knife or a dagger. It seems that his killer then carefully lowered him to the floor so as not to attract too much attention.

The murderer must have taken the weapon with him, because there is no sign of it.'It's a terrible thing, just a terrible thing. I've been cleaning up some of the blood spatters. I figure some of the patrons might have accidentally stepped in some before anyone knew what was going on. There are bloody footprints heading for the door.''It's a terrible thing, just a terrible thing. I've been cleaning up some of the blood spatters. I figure some of the patrons might have accidentally stepped in some before anyone knew what was going on. There are bloody footprints heading for the door.''It's a terrible thing, just a terrible thing. I've been cleaning up some of the blood spatters. I figure some of the patrons might have accidentally stepped in some before anyone knew what was going on. There are bloody footprints heading for the door.''You say you found something? Well, don't dawdle here! Go examine the new evidence and bring anything you find back to me!''I hope I have not done wrong by cleaning up some of the blood! I was only trying to help.'You tell Dúnor of the second murdered man and hand him the knife. He studies it with dawning realization.

'${PLAYER}... I know this knife. It is finely decorated, and gilt front and rear. This is the weapon of a lord, not of a common man. Do you see the black circle here on the hilt? That is a symbol of the Blackroot Vale, or I am a Mûmak. This is the personal dagger of Lord Duinhir. I would know it anywhere. The Lord of the Blackroot Vale murdered the Haradrim emissary. But why would he do this?'

You think of the fate that befell his sons, Duilin and Derufin, and wonder.

'He would not have done it himself, ${PLAYER}. He must have paid yonder victim to do the deed, and once it was done he silenced the murderer and made his escape. I will take some of my best men and apprehend him. You bring word to King Elessar that the culprit has been found.''Lord Duinhir would not have done it himself, ${PLAYER}. He must have paid yonder victim to do the deed, and once it was done he silenced the murderer and made his escape. I will take some of my best men and apprehend him. You bring word to King Elessar that the culprit has been found.''${PLAYER}! You have to tell him I did not do this! You know me! I did not kill them!''Duinhir was brought before me and he claims innocence of the crime. What say you to this, ${PLAYER}? I have heard his words, but I would hear what you think as well.'

You give Elessar your opinion, and he nods.

'There is no doubt that Duinhir keenly feels the loss of his sons beneath the trampling feet of the Mûmakil, the beasts wielded in war by the Haradrim. But why would he pay a man to commit this murder, only to leave behind his dagger, distinctive as it is? If the killing was fueled by a desire for vengeance, would he not wish to carry it out himself? Further, this act has now claimed two lives, one of whom can carry no burden for the deaths of Duilin and Derufin, no matter how slight.

'I will not rush to exonerate him; neither will I render hasty judgement. Speak to him, ${PLAYER}, and hear what he has to say of this. If there is anything he knows that may point to another, he should provide it, for Hasong will not wait long before returning to his country with a call for war.'Duinhir turns his tear-stained face to you.

'I did not do this, ${PLAYER}! Tell him! You have to tell him I am not guilty of this crime! I hate the Haradrim for what they did to my boys, but I would not kill one, not like this! Let them face me in combat, man to man, and I would show no mercy, but I would not kill any among them like this, like a coward! How could I face Rosfin, having done this and brought shame to my king? I could not! You must believe me!

'My knife... my knife... someone took my knife and used it for the crime! I cannot remember when last I saw it, but I must have set it down somewhere. Yes, I remember cleaning it... was that the last time I held it in my grip? I remember looking out over the city and thinking of my sons... where was that? Maybe it was on the parapet on the north side of the... of the Fifth Circle? Go there, good friend, go there and someone may know something! I am depending on you, ${PLAYER}, and so too are Rosfin and the memory of my boys!''My knife... my knife... someone took my knife and used it for the crime! I cannot remember when last I saw it, but I must have set it down somewhere. Yes, I remember cleaning it... was that the last time I held it in my grip? I remember looking out over the city and thinking of my sons... where was that? Maybe it was on the parapet on the north side of the... of the Fifth Circle? Go there, good friend, go there and someone may know something! I am depending on you, ${PLAYER}, and so too are Rosfin and the memory of my boys!''It is a long chance, ${PLAYER}, but it is a chance. Go to the parapet on the north side of the Fifth Circle and see if anyone there saw anything.''My knife... my knife... someone took my knife and used it for the crime! I cannot remember when last I saw it, but I must have set it down somewhere. Yes, I remember cleaning it... was that the last time I held it in my grip? I remember looking out over the city and thinking of my sons... where was that? Maybe it was on the parapet on the north side of the Fifth Circle? Go there, good friend, go there and someone may know something! I am depending on you, ${PLAYER}, and so too are Rosfin and the memory of my boys!''It is a long chance, ${PLAYER}, but it is a chance. Go to the parapet on the north side of the Fifth Circle and see if anyone there saw anything.''Ho there, ${PLAYER}! I have heard of your many deeds, and it is a pleasure to meet you at last! You are a friend of Lord Duinhir of the Blackroot Vale, are you not? Did his knife ever come back to him?'

You boggle at the man, your mouth agape.

'Did I say something odd? He comes here to this spot to clean and sharpen his knife, but it has been a few days since last I saw him. He left his knife behind, an accident of his recent grief, I expect. I would have brought it to him, but before I had properly decided, another volunteered for the task.'

You ask the man who it was who volunteered to return Duinhir's knife.

'Why, it was Parthadan, the Warden of the Green. I saw him only a moment ago, outside the Dome of the Sun on the north-east side of the Sixth Circle.''It was Parthadan, the Warden of the Green, who offered to return Duinhir's knife. I saw him only a moment ago, outside the Dome of the Sun on the north-east side of the Sixth Circle.'Parthadan looks up at you with haunted eyes, and you know at once that he is guilty of the crimes.

'I know why you're here, ${PLAYER}. I have been wandering around the city, looking for reasons to go on, but I found none. It all seemed so clear to me, once, but I cannot recapture that feeling no matter how long I walk.

'I saw Duinhir cleaning his knife, and I thought of his sons, and I knew with complete certainty that the men of Harad had to die. But I serve on the Council of Gondor! I am the Warden of the Green! Who will speak for the folk of Pelennor who lost their homes during the war, if not I? So I took Duinhir's knife when he left it behind, and I paid a man in coin for the deed, and when one of the Haradrim emissaries went to the tavern, my man cut him down.

'It seemed so clear to me: if he lived he would speak my name, in time. So I took the knife from him and slew him too, but the clarity was already going. He was a man of Gondor; why did he need to die, when it was men of Gondor for whom the Warden of the Green sought to provide?'

You ask Parthadan how long he has been with the Withered Tree, and the haunted look is replaced with a confused expression.

'The Withered Tree? Oh, no, you've gotten it wrong, ${PLAYER}. I have never been one of those malcontents. Everything I did... I did for the good of Gondor. I would never join those villains. I wish I could explain to you where it went wrong, but it's just not clear to me anymore. We were at war, and then we weren't, and somewhere along the way I lost something important. I can't remember now what it might have been, but there was a time when I knew this was the right thing to do. It's gone now, though, and I don't think it's ever coming back. I wish I could tell Duinhir I was sorry. I think this is his fault, somehow.'Parthadan stares at you with haunted eyes. You have seen many unsettling things in your adventures, but this is among the worst.'I've never seen anything like it, ${PLAYER}. I've heard tales of fell spirits and shades controlling the bodies of their victims, but that's not what is happening here. I think Parthadan knew what he was doing, but once it was done he couldn't come to terms with it in his head. I think it broke him.

'I will bring him to the dungeons, but I do not expect he will be there long. Go to King Elessar and tell him what we found.''I will bring Parthadan to the dungeons. You should return to King Elessar and tell him we have found the murderer.''The guards have been and gone, ${PLAYER}. Duinhir has been released, and Parthadan will die for the murders of two men. It is not enough for Hasong, and it is not enough for me. This should never have happened.

'Hasong will return to his Khas with the knowledge that justice was meted-out upon the murderer, but it will not change this truth: they came here under a banner of peace, and Gondor betrayed them.

'Sauron is gone, but he gave us many unwanted gifts before the War of the Ring came to a close. Dhûrto, ${PLAYER}. I cannot abide the sound of the word, but I know that this day, Gondor deserves it.

'I thank you for what you have done, my friend. An innocent man was spared, and for that we should be thankful. I will await news of your return, and hope that your adventures progress well, and simply. How is it that war can be so clear, and peace be so confused?'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Thorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the MountainFoldgráf is assembling a group to come to the aid of the Iron Garrison, in Khazad-dûm.Talk to Foldgráf in the Hall under the Mountain and go with him to Khazad-dûmBósi is in the Hall under the Mountain. Khazad-dûm, and much of the Iron Garrison, is lost.Talk to Bósi in the Hall under the MountainThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the Mountain'You should return to King Thorin, ${PLAYER}. It seems there is trouble brewing, and he will want your help with it!''Messengers have come from the Iron Garrison, ${PLAYER}! There is trouble afoot in Khazad-dûm, and the king is sending me with a group of warriors to come to their aid! Quickly, speak with King Thorin and see if he wishes for you to come with us!''You should return to King Thorin as soon as you can!''Quickly now, speak with King Thorin!''I am glad you returned when you did, ${PLAYER}, for I have received worrisome news from the Iron Garrison! A tremendous upheaval in the depths has shaken Khazad-dûm to its foundations, and the Expedition's foothold is slipping in the face of constant attack by terrible foes.

'I fear Khazad-dûm will be lost if we do not reinforce them. I have sent for many fine warriors to go to their aid, and I desire for you to go as well! I have not forgotten the mithril that Bósi sent to me. If we lose Khazad-dûm, our best source of mithril will be gone!

'Talk to Foldgráf and he will arrange for your transport!''The Iron Garrison needs our help. I only hope it is not already too late!'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I do not know what happened, ${PLAYER}. I thought we had well-fortified our defences, and repelled enough foes to prove that the Iron Garrison could not be moved... and yet still it was not enough! I have never seen so many Orcs, and other foes even more terrible.

'Cousin Brogur will remain within Khazad-dûm, there to lie until dwarves once again open the doors and claim what is ours. One year hence? A hundred? Who can say how long it will be, my friend? May it bring him happiness, whenever that may be. He is with Zigilburk now.

'I have torn my beard enough for the failure of the Iron Garrison. I will do as my king commands.''I cannot believe we failed, ${PLAYER}. I thought the Iron Garrison would prove successful, in the end.''Sauron may be no more, but I see his hand in the turmoil that shook Khazad-dûm. What else could it be? The Iron Garrison had established more than a mere foothold, but still it was swept away like a clay castle on a river-bank! I smell sorcery and evil intentions!

'I want Khazad-dûm retaken! There is mithril to be mined, and Durin's Folk have the right to mine it! I cannot ask Bósi to lead another expedition there, even if he would go willingly. The death of his cousin, and so many of his fellows, will weigh heavily upon him. I do not like this, ${PLAYER}. Will my people blame me for this failure, even as they call me "Stonehelm" and whisper behind their hands?

'I need time to think. I will call for you when I have decided what is to be done. Durin himself would have trouble with these struggles, ${PLAYER}!'Learn the meaning of the ancient text you found and to where it led Glorfindel.You should ask Celeborn in the Court of Lothlórien about the ancient text.Talk to Lord CelebornYou should talk to Glathlírel in the Court of Lothlórien.Talk to GlathlírelGo with Glathlírel and return to the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu in search of Glorfindel.Go with Glathlírel to the RiftYou should talk to Lady Galadriel in the Court of Lothlórien, and tell her all that happened in the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.Talk to Lady Galadriel'${PLAYER}, there you are! Lord Celeborn has grasped the ancient text's meaning and knows where Glorfindel has gone. You should speak with him at once.''${PLAYER}, there you are! Celeborn has grasped the ancient text's meaning and knows where Glorfindel has gone. Fly there with great haste!''Know, ${CLASS}, that what Glorfindel sought is what Men or dwarves would call a prophecy: words uttered by Gil-galad in an age past, concerning both the dawn and the dusk of our time in Middle-earth. Few were there to hear it and fewer still are there who walk this earth and recall it in its entirety. Perhaps foreseeing this, the words were inscribed onto eleven scrolls and given out to trusted keepers of lore. 

'But the text you found in Tham Giliath was not one of those scrolls. No, it was a mere recounting of the texts penned by one scholar and to where they had been sent. Four of the twenty it mentions were of Gil-galad's speech, and I know well that three of those four were lost forever to calamity, folly, and fire.

'The fourth recording of the prophecy found a home among collected texts in what has come to be called the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu. That place is known to you, ${PLAYER}? It is known well indeed to you, Glathlírel.''I am ready to set out for Nûrz Ghâshu, ${PLAYER}. Give the word and we shall go.''Do you see, ${PLAYER}? The nature of the dream that stirred me from my retreat was not an idle one. I have found my way here, as you have, in time of need.

'Though I tremble at the thought of returning to that place, where the Balrog was fought and subdued, I know we must. A fear, nay, a certainty tells me that danger lurks there as it did before. If the Rift were peaceful and empty, why has Glorfindel not returned?

'Even Lady Galadriel cannot know what lies in that depth. We must go, you and I, and learn the truth of it. That much is certain.''You are returned! In your eyes I read much - much that has taken place.'

You and Glathlírel tell the assembled council all that happened.

'I see. It has ended well enough, and with time we may know whether the scroll Glorfindel sought was worth the peril you faced. Soon he will be with us and our wait will end. I will find the gift he spoke of and see that you are given it to bring to my grand-daughter, Arwen. It is a good gift, and fitting. Moreover, it is right that she receives it from you.

'Farewell for now, ${PLAYER}. We will see each other soon.'Although they promised Frodo they would not return to that land, Pippin and Merry find their thoughts turning back towards Mordor once again.You should talk to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion to hear what, exactly, they are thinking about with regard to Mordor.Talk to PippinYou should talk to Merry in Bâr Thorenion to hear his thoughts on where the two remaining half-orcs might be.Talk to MerryPippin and Merry have asked you to go in search of the two half-orcs who fled the last encounter. Merry believes they will be found somewhere in Dor Amarth, on the path to Barad-dûr.Search for the half-orcsPippin and Merry have asked you to go in search of the two half-orcs who fled the last encounter. You have found one of them, come to a bitter end, on the path to Barad-dûr, and should examine the corpse.Examine the dead half-orcPippin and Merry have asked you to go in search of the two half-orcs who fled the last encounter. You should follow the trail leading from the one half-orc, in hopes of finding the other.Pippin and Merry have asked you to go in search of the two half-orcs who fled the last encounter. Tracks leading from the corpse of the first have led at last to the second, who lives still. You should talk to him and learn what you can.Talk to the half-orcReturn to Merry and Pippin in Bâr Thorenion with news of what you learned from the dying half-orc.Return to Bâr ThorenionOn returning to Bâr Thorenion, Gandalf has appeared and beckoned you over to speak with him.Talk to GandalfYou should talk to Pippin and Merry in Bâr Thorenion, telling them at least some of the whole truth, as Gandalf counselled you.Talk to Merry'${PLAYER}! There you are. Do you have a moment? We have been discussing Mordor.''I have word that Masters Peregrin and Meriadoc wish to speak with you, ${PLAYER}. It has something to do with Mordor, though I know nothing more of the matter. They will, of course, be found in Bâr Thorenion.''Pippin, you explain.''How glad I am you are here, ${PLAYER}. If you wish to know the truth, we were just talking about you.

'You see, we could not stop thinking about the two of those fellows who fled, and what they might know, and how they are still out there in Mordor. And we thought to ourselves, we made a promise to Frodo and not for all the world would we break it. But ${PLAYER} made no such vow! Oh, but you see where this is going, don't you? You are clever enough for a ${RACE}, and no doubt about it!

'And anyway, we have heard some of the adventures you have had out there, and know you will be going back out there, anyway. While you are there, why not track down those two fellows? Oh, say you will, ${PLAYER}, won't you? 

'Merry, you say the bit about where they might be.''To begin with, they shan't be in Udûn any longer. I have looked at the maps, and they were nearly to Dor Amarth when we found them. If the wretches still think there is someone in Barad-dûr to buy their miserable tidings, then that is where they have gone.

'That, then, is where you should begin your search for them.'A look of abject misery and fear is etched across the half-orc's lifeless face. Signs of a struggle on the ground about him clearly show marks of both pursuer and pursued, bearing roughly east.'You again? I should have guessed. Didn't bring your two little friends along, did you? Too dangerous, eh? Well, no matter.'

The half-orc is seized by a painful coughing fit.

'Not going to strike me? You've only come to mock me, then. Laugh at how there's no Red Eye in that tumble-down tower out yonder. Nothing worth selling and no one a-buying. Nothing but malice *cough* *cough* and death and illusions out here in this cursed place. I have half a mind that you're an illusion, yourself. Ptah!

'I suppose you'd love to know all about your precious *cough* *hack* - precious Shire and the rest of that rot. The Wizard's got his designs on it, he does, and he's been in and out of there for some years. Oh, don't let that sour look spoil your pretty face. He's got plenty of improvements planned, he and that Lotho do. Plenty underway, even now. Why, there's even a gate on the Brandywine Bridge, now.

'Ah ha! Ha ha *cough* *cough* *hack*...ugh. Oh. Leave me, wretched ${RACE}. Leave me to die in peace.''Hold a moment, ${PLAYER}. I know where you have been and guess what you have learned, for I have known the truth of it myself for some little while, though through somewhat different means.

'You cannot, of course, withhold the truth from Pippin and Merry in any broad sense, but allow me to counsel you that they need not hear every painful detail you have learned.

'Think for a moment, of the many days of happiness that lie ahead, and the bitter challenge that lies in wait for them after the long road home. Does that foreknowledge bear any good? I do not think it does. Prepare them for the hard truth when they can see it with their own eyes, for that is an honest kindness, but perhaps, no more.

'Weigh my words and act on your own judgment. That is all I ask.''There you are! What a grave face. Tell us what you have learned. Please, I cannot take the suspense a moment more.'

Choosing your words carefully, you confirm what the two hobbits believed they knew - that the Wizard must have been Saruman and that he had designs on the Shire. You suggest more generally that the Shire they return to might not quite be the Shire as they left it, but, with the words of Gandalf ringing in your ears, can bring yourself to tell them no more.

After a long pause, Merry finally speaks.

'I see then. Those fellows worked for Saruman, then, and fled him hoping to profit from a new master. Rotten luck for rotten apples, I say! And so all those barrels of pipe-weed were for more than his own smoking habits, I take it. Still, I don't see how much harm that can do. There is only so much Hornblower farmed each season, and if too much goes missing it is sure to be noticed.

'Pippin, ${PLAYER}, can we make a pact not to burden Frodo or Sam with this? They have been through enough, and if there is trouble of some kind in the Shire, I had far rather they learn that when we get there, and not have it spoil these golden days of celebration. It shall weigh on my own heart enough, I know, though I think I shall work hard to forget about it awhile.

'And yet, I know I cannot forget about going home any longer. That I shall remember every moment from this time forward, I am afraid.'Accept the honour of delivering Glorfindel's betrothal gift to Arwen.You should ask Lady Galadriel in the Court of Lothlórien for Arwen's gift.Ask Galadriel for the giftGift in hand, it is time to visit Arwen in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell, once more.Arwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Go with Arwen to the bridge ahead.Walk with Arwen to the bridgeArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Talk to Arwen upon the bridge.Talk to ArwenArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Go with Arwen to the left past the bridge, where the statue stands.Walk with Arwen to the left statueArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Talk to Arwen by the statue.Talk to ArwenArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Go with Arwen farther down the path, through the narrow pass.Walk with Arwen some moreArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Talk to Arwen under the trees before the second bridge.Talk to ArwenArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Go with Arwen onto the second bridge.Walk with Arwen a little furtherArwen has asked you to walk with her in Imlad Gelair in Rivendell. If you get separated, ring the bell.

Talk to Arwen upon the second bridge.'The gift for Lady Arwen has been found, ${PLAYER}. Lady Galadriel herself holds it and awaits your arrival. Though it is Glorfindel's betrothal gift, the honour is yours to deliver it.''A gift for Lady Arwen awaits you in the Court of Lothlórien. It is Glorfindel's betrothal gift to her, but the honour is yours to deliver it.''A true gift is one which sings of the receiver in the voice of the giver, it is said. By that mark, Glorfindel has chosen the truest of gifts.'

Lady Galadriel produces two exquisite flowers, and only after a moment do you perceive that they are brooches, wrought of silver filament, green and violet silk, and finest cloth-of-gold, and not real flowers at all. She returns them to a silken box, which she hands to you.

'Take these to Arwen, ${PLAYER}. She will tell you something of what they mean to her, if she wishes.'Arwen meets your eyes as you approach her, and you present her with the gift directly. She opens the silken box and you explain the flowers are from Glorfindel.

'I know then what these are and what is their meaning. It is a good and a beautiful thought Glorfindel has given me in this gift. Come, ${PLAYER}. Walk with me a spell.''To the bridge up ahead. Come, friend.''Much will come to pass, ${PLAYER}. It will come soon, swift as a diving hawk. Swift as the sun's last descent into night. Much will be gained, and much, too, lost.'

Arwen laughs, softly, sweetly, and a little sadly.

'You are thinking, I am sure, that much has already happened since first your journey began. Nor are you mistaken! You have seen much, but soon all Middle-earth shall see more. And while you have been through many lands and among many peoples, I have been here and among only my own.

'To the left now, where that statue stands.''Let us go there, where the statue stands by the water.''Indeed, I have tarried in Rivendell many years now, though it is not counted overlong in the reckoning of my people. These last tens of years, I have lived here in anticipation of a promise, waiting to learn if fortune should decree it held or broken. That brief time has been both as an eternity and a single breath to me.

'Shortly, we shall set forth for Gondor, as you know, and shortly thereafter, when we reach that land, I shall become one with the treasure of my heart. He will be king and I queen. Like Lúthien, whose likeness is here before us, and Beren. Elf-maid and Man. Much gained, yes, and much lost.

'Come, let us go farther down the path.''Come, let us go on through the narrow pass ahead.''An age is ending, ${PLAYER}. It is not ended quite yet, and in the counting of Men and dwarves, it will not be over for some modest span to come. But for me, to have known centuries... centuries before my heart was given, moving freely from Imladris to Lorien, from Lorien to Imladris, like a carefree minnow between pond and stream. Then to know that nothing shall be as it was.

'Do you know what these flowers are, ${PLAYER}? They are lovers' tokens, and they are wrought with uncommon skill so that a sympathy exists between them, as if they were tied by an unseen thread no matter the distance. If anything should befall the owner of one flower - should they grow ill or die - then the other's flower will wilt, wither, and crumble. Thus may my Aragorn always know that I am well and whole, and I may know the same of him.

'It is at once a great comfort and a constant reminder of our doom and of endings-to-be.''I am going off to dwell in the lands of Men, and I shall become as they who dwell there, ${PLAYER}. I will gain my heart's desire and I will be Queen of the heirs of Númenor, at the dawn of a new age. But I will lose the home I have known. I will lose so many faces dearest to me, who I leave behind and who themselves will make even greater journeys, soon enough. I will lose the roots-deep certainty of days unending. Where I go, days will be numbered, and so also lives.'

Arwen stops and meets your gaze with level eyes that are filled with many things.

'I thank you for walking with me, ${PLAYER}. I thank you for listening kindly and without judgment. Sadness and joy are like two streams joined into a river, and the finest of Elven weavers could not part them now. But I have a journey to prepare for, and time will not tarry.

'I will see you in Gondor, friend? I hope that I do. I should like that very much!'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Bósi is in the Hall under the Mountain, grieving for the loss of his cousin and the failure of the Iron Garrison.Talk to Bósi in the Hall under the MountainBósi is in the Hall under the Mountain, grieving for the loss of his cousin and the failure of the Iron Garrison.Talk to Bósi and go with him to see King ThorinThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the MountainHis treachery revealed, you should return to Dor Amarth and search south of Lûghash, close to the slope of Orodruin, to find the dwarves slain by Dómor.Search far south of Lûghash, by the slope of Orodruin, for the missing dwarf heroesYou have found what appears to be a hasty gravesite in Dor Amarth. Was this the location of Dómor's treachery?Examine the hasty gravesiteYou have found the location where Dómor hid the bodies of the dwarves he betrayed and killed.Talk to Laurík at the gravesiteThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the Mountain'King Thorin will have need of you at Erebor, ${PLAYER}, but you should check in with Bósi first. The loss of Khazad-dûm will be treating him badly.''Have you returned to see the king? It is good that you have done so, but you should also speak to Bósi. The loss of Khazad-dûm will be treating him badly.''King Thorin will have need of you at Erebor, ${PLAYER}, but you should check in with Bósi first. The loss of Khazad-dûm will be treating him badly.''Have you returned to see the king? It is good that you have done so, but you should also speak to Bósi. The loss of Khazad-dûm will be treating him badly.''I do not know what I should be doing, ${PLAYER}. The loss of Khazad-dûm weighs heavily upon me, and dwells in my thoughts. Was there anything we could have done to be ready for such an attack? It seemed as if the mountains themselves sought to expel us!'

Bósi sighs heavily, and shakes his head.

'I must speak to King Thorin. Brogur and I were entrusted with the leadership of the Iron Garrison, and my cousin is slain and cannot present himself to the King under the Mountain. I must take the responsibility and speak for both of us. Will you come with me, my friend? I would value your support as I await the punishment that must come.''Yes, let us go to the king. I will accept responsibility for the Iron Garrison's failure.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}, and so does Bósi. Indeed, all who stood against the traitor Dómor and the terrible creature that enslaved him have the thanks of the King under the Mountain!

'It is clear to me now that the tale Dómor wove in Mordor was a lie. The ruby over which Eirek and Mogi quarreled was an invention of the traitor; the helm was the true treasure they found, and when he placed it upon his head he came under the dominion of Lachagar.

'Eirek and Mogi deserve better treatment than they received at Dómor's treacherous hands. Return to Mordor and find their final resting place. Search first at the location Dómor went after you encountered him; I believe he offered to check that spot to prevent you from seeing it yourself.''The tale Dómor wove in Mordor was a lie. The ruby over which Eirek and Mogi quarreled was an invention of the traitor; the helm was the true treasure, and when he placed it upon his head he came under the dominion of Lachagar.

'Eirek and Mogi deserve better treatment than they received at Dómor's treacherous hands. Return to Mordor and find their final resting place. Search first at the location Dómor went after you encountered him; I believe he offered to check that spot to prevent you from seeing it yourself.''The tale Dómor wove in Mordor was a lie. The ruby over which Eirek and Mogi quarreled was an invention of the traitor; the helm was the true treasure, and when he placed it upon his head he came under the dominion of Lachagar.

'Eirek and Mogi deserve better treatment than they received at Dómor's treacherous hands. Return to Mordor and find their final resting place. Search first at the location Dómor went after you encountered him; I believe he offered to check that spot to prevent you from seeing it yourself.''The tale Dómor wove in Mordor was a lie. The ruby over which Eirek and Mogi quarreled was an invention of the traitor; the helm was the true treasure, and when he placed it upon his head he came under the dominion of Lachagar.

'Eirek and Mogi deserve better treatment than they received at Dómor's treacherous hands. Return to Mordor and find their final resting place. Search first at the location Dómor went after you encountered him; I believe he offered to check that spot to prevent you from seeing it yourself.''This is the location where Eirek and Mogi were slain, ${PLAYER}. We cannot bring them out of Mordor now, but this grave is too shallow to serve. They will be burned dwarves, as is sometimes done when no tombs can be made. Let the smoke of the burning serve as a warning to traitors.

'Return to King Thorin and tell him what is being done for them. He will wish to know.''Return to King Thorin and tell him what is being done for Eirek and Mogi. He will wish to know.''You found the bodies of Eirek and Mogi? Dómor paid for his treachery, but I am still displeased that he proved corruptible. We pride ourselves on our resilience, but there are such evils in the Land of Mordor that even dwarves are not safe from their foul touch.

'I thank you for your efforts on behalf of Durin's Folk, ${PLAYER}. May your adventures continue, and bring you glory and treasure alike.'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Elessar and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.Mithrandir has come to the throne room of King Elessar, atop Minas Tirith.Talk to Mithrandir in King Elessar's throne roomMithrandir has come to the throne room of King Elessar, atop Minas Tirith.Talk to Mithrandir in the throne room and stand guardMithrandir has come to the throne room of King Elessar, atop Minas Tirith.Talk to Mithrandir in King Elessar's throne roomKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'King Elessar will be glad to hear of our conquests in Mordor, ${PLAYER}. Give him my regards too, and my wish that he will one day join us as we sojourn!''King Elessar waits for you in the throne room with Mithrandir. Perhaps you should learn what brings the Wizard here, for he is not often seen in the city these days.''King Elessar will be glad to hear of our conquests in Mordor, ${PLAYER}. Give him my regards too, and my wish that he will one day join us as we sojourn!''King Elessar waits for you in the throne room with Mithrandir. Perhaps you should learn what brings the Wizard here, for he is not often seen in the city these days.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, I expected you before this. Well, it is good that you are here now!

'There are words I must speak to Aragorn, but I do not wish to be interrupted. This room will not do; there are too many guards about! Enough safety for a king, but too many listening ears.

'I have a place in mind where Aragorn and I may speak away from his guards. Fear not! His person will be safe, for I would have you attend us and stand guard. You will prevent any interruptions, especially by members of the Withered Tree that might seek to harm the king. Speak to me when you are ready to go, and we will wait for nightfall.''At last it is time. Come now, ${PLAYER}, and tell no one where we go.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'It was from an abundance of caution that I asked you to attend us, ${PLAYER}, for I feared interruption by those calling themselves the Withered Tree. I am pleased that no such marauder sought to do harm to Aragorn or to the scion of Nimloth. Indeed, I think now that no man of Gondor could bring himself to damage the young sapling, if he knew what it portended: a sign of growing goodness in the years to come.''For how long did the fruit of the last tree lie hidden above the city, ${PLAYER}? Long years indeed, though the sapling has yet to reach seven years of growth! I will take Mithrandir's advice to heart, and when ever the fruit ripens it shall be planted, so the line of the tree will not vanish from the world. The Wizard's lesson was not subtle; it is not lost on me that the line of kings is much the same. There is time yet, and my heart is lightened by this discovery!

'Thank you for your part in this, my friend. The sapling should be planted in the Court of the Fountain, and let the folk of the city draw hope from the sight!'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Durin's Folk and fight on their behalf in Mordor.Bósi is in the Hall under the Mountain.Talk to Bósi in the Hall under the MountainBósi has been instructed to bring you before the King under the Mountain.Talk to Bósi and go with him to see the kingThorin III, King under the Mountain, is at his throne in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall under the Mountain'It has been some time since you returned to the Lonely Mountain, has it not? You should present yourself to King Thorin and see what taxing errand he has for you now! Perhaps you can speak to Bósi when you go, and learn how he fares.''Welcome back to Erebor, ${PLAYER}. Bósi wished to speak with you, and then you should attend King Thorin, who has further words for you.''It has been some time since you returned to the Lonely Mountain, has it not? You should present yourself to King Thorin and see what taxing errand he has for you now! Perhaps you can speak to Bósi when you go, and learn how he fares.''Have you returned to see the king? It is good that you have done so, but you should also speak to Bósi. The loss of Khazad-dûm will be treating him badly.''Welcome back to the Lonely Mountain, ${PLAYER}. When last I saw you, I planned to speak with the King under the Mountain and accept his judgment in the matter of the Iron Garrison, but the appearance of Lachagar quite interrupted the matter. I have since spoken with the king, and he has ruled mercifully. I still have my freedom, and my neck!

'Were there some who might see cowardice in this ruling of King Thorin? Perhaps at one time, but his bravery in the battle with Lachagar has proven to many that he is made of the same strong material as his father. His mercy is seen as wise and just by most of the dwarves here. I am grateful for his leniency, if not convinced I am deserving of it. I will grieve for my cousin Brogur and the many others who fell in Khazad-dûm, early and late, Miners and Guards, and will never forget them.

'King Thorin believes that only Durin could retake Khazad-dûm now. Perhaps he is right, but for now I cannot think so. I have been instructed to bring you to the king. Speak to me when you are ready.''The King under the Mountain waits to see you. Come with me, ${PLAYER}.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'When first you were made known to me, I did not expect to see such depth of service, ${PLAYER}. You have exceeded my expectations in every respect, and are a true friend of Durin's Folk.

'But more than that, you are a true friend to me: Thorin III, King under the Mountain. I adopt the name "Stonehelm" with pride, and will wear it as a symbol of my steadfastness and strength, and that of Durin's Folk. It will be a reminder to me that not all deeds come easily, and those for which we struggle may be the most worthy of all.

'It is with that spirit that I will not inflict a harsh punishment upon Bósi and the Iron Garrison for fleeing from the disaster of Khazad-dûm. There will come a day when the hammers of the dwarves ring again in the depths of that mighty city. It will be my duty to prepare Durin's Folk for that day, and I will undertake it as best I may.

'You are always welcome in these halls, ${PLAYER}! Go forth and seek glory and treasure worthy of your name and your tale!'You have pledged to lend your aid and your adventuring skills to Elessar and the Kingdom of Gondor, and to fight on their behalf in Mordor.King Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne roomKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar and attend the ceremonyKing Elessar is in his throne room atop Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar in his throne room'I am about to return and report to King Elessar of our campaign in Mordor. You should do so as well, ${PLAYER}.''King Elessar waits for you in the throne room.''I am about to return and report to King Elessar of our campaign in Mordor. You should do so as well, ${PLAYER}.''King Elessar waits for you in the throne room.''It is good to see you returned hale and hearty, ${PLAYER}. We will soon conduct an important ceremony, and I will be pleased to have you in attendance. Speak to me when you are ready and we will go together.''It is time. Let us go at once, and see what we will see.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'When first you pledged to adventure in Mordor on behalf of Gondor, I knew you would perform great deeds in service of the kingdom. It was not far-seeing on my part. No, I knew from your past adventures that your future deeds would be extraordinary, and so they were!

'You are a true friend of Gondor, ${PLAYER}. Go with my good wishes, and face evil wherever it rises!'Glorfindel is come to the Council of War in the Court of Lothlórien.You should talk to Orophin and join the War Council.Talk to OrophinTalk to Lady Galadriel to be released, finally, from your service.Talk to Galadriel'I hardly believed your account, ${PLAYER}, but now I must hold my eyes for liars, for there is Glorfindel himself, among the Council of War. I beg of you, speak to Orophin, who shall present you, for this is an occasion of joyous ceremony.''Urgent and wondrous tidings, ${PLAYER}! Glorfindel is arrived in the Court of Lothlórien. They await your arrival. Go. Go now!''Come with me, ${PLAYER}. There are things the council would discuss. But there is more, for you have been of great help to our cause and the Lord and Lady wish to offer thanks to you for your service.''You have done so much for us, ${PLAYER} and our gratitude is near immeasurable. By the fair stars above and the sign of the golden leaves, I free and release you from your troth of service.

'Go now and serve whom and how you please. I have every faith you will bring light and goodness wherever your journeys take you.'The hobbits have issued you a 'formal invitation' to Bâr Thorenion.You should talk to Pippin in Bâr Thorenion, and learn what sort of 'formal invitation' he and Merry are talking about.Talk to PippinYou should talk to Pippin and Merry in  Bâr Thorenion.Talk to MerryTalk to Pippin'You're too early, ${PLAYER}! You did get our formal invitation, didn't you? No? Well, you're here all the same and that is always a good thing.'The Ranger seems somewhat perplexed.

'I have a -- well, I suppose it is some manner of formal invitation for you, ${PLAYER}. You are requested to join Masters Meriadoc and Peregrin and select other personages -- that is what they said -- at Bâr Thorenion. I think you should go.''We wanted to have everyone here. You know, Aragorn, Gandalf, Gimli - the Fellowship and everyone else who has really mattered on this whole adventure. And that certainly includes you, ${PLAYER}. Since you are here early, why don't you stay around and help out?

'Well, there is no real work to be done. All these helpful Gondorian Men see to that. But you can keep me and Merry in good company until the others arrive.''Look, Pippin. ${PLAYER} has come early!''You have helped us out a great deal, ${PLAYER}, and we shall never forget it. I know you are going off to help others now, but I have a curious feeling we will see each other again -- here, or elsewhere, perhaps back in the Shire when the time comes! Until then, good speed and glad tidings.''Oh, how I am stuffed! It rarely happens, but I must confess I have somewhat over-eaten. No matter! I shall sleep it off and rise to a new day, tomorrow.

'I realize that you have adventures yet before you, ${PLAYER}, and that what Frodo said about turning our gaze homeward was meant for us and not for you. Still, I wanted you to know how much we have benefitted your company, not to mention your bravery, these days. I hope we shall still see you again from time to time. Farewell, friend!'The spiders of Lhingris have come to the rear gate of Durthang and have now set siege to the fortress in defiance of Ugrukhôr.Spiders can be found near the gates of Durthang in Lhingris.

You have encountered numerous spiders setting siege to Durthang in Lhingris and should defeat them.Defeat spiders at the gates of Durthang in Lhingris (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many spiders at the gates of Durthang in Lhingris.CompletedAs you feared, many spiders have come to the gates of Durthang in Lhingris. If they are able to breach the fortress, it is likely their numbers shall spill forth into Udûn and threaten the forces of the Free Peoples in Mordor.

You should defeat as many spiders as you can.You have defeated many of the spiders setting siege to Durthang in Lhingris and prevented them from spilling forth into Udûn.Captain Róglarg is irritated that the dead Orcs of Captain Shagrat's garrison still litter Cirith Ungol.Slain Orcs from Shagrat's garrison can be found throughout Cirith Ungol.

Disappointed in the actions of his predecessor, Captain Róglarg has asked you to burn any slain Orcs you discover in Cirith Ungol.Burn slain Orcs from Shagrat's garrison in Cirith Ungol (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have burned many of the dead Orcs from Shagrat's garrison.Completed'You see all these dead Orcs?'

You nod.

'They aren't any of mine, Silent One. Shagrat had command before me, and he let his garrison get torn apart by a single Elf-warrior! Of course, he made it out fine. Fine enough, indeed, to bring some trinkets to Lugbúrz, but that's no way for an Uruk to lead!

'We've spent days burning the bodies and there's still more left. A right mess old Shagrat left us!

'If you see a dead Orc, burn it! This place already reeks of tarks and Elves... I won't have stink of dead Orcs, too!'You have burned many of the dead Orcs from Shagrat's garrison. Perhaps it is best if Captain Róglarg continues to believe that an Elf-warrior slew Shagrat's Orcs, rather than a quarrel over Frodo's mithril-coat.The watch-tower of Lugvarg guards the pass from Magh Ashtu into Lhingris, and its Orcs keep a close watch on those who dare travel in the shadow of the Morgai.Orkish warriors and sentries can be found at the watch-tower of Lugvarg along the Morgai in Rath Cail.

You have come to Lugvarg in search of rumoured belongings of Frodo or Sam, and have found that the watch-tower guards the pass to Magh Ashtu in Dor Amarth. You should defeat many of the Orcs in Lugvarg.Defeat Lugvarg warriors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Lugvarg sentries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many of the Orcs in Lugvarg.CompletedWhile searching Lugvarg for the "evidence" the road-watchers spoke of, you have discovered that Lugvarg guards a pass from the battlement of Magh Ashtu in Dor Amarth.

If the Free Peoples intend to stage a large incursion into Lhingris in the coming days, the way must be made clear.You have defeated many of the Orcs at Lugvarg and made clear the road to Magh Ashtu in Dor Amarth.Legolas fears that remaining still in Lhingris portends an ill fate, and so he hopes to move deeper into Rath Cail to continue his search for Ungwetári.Legolas can be found atop a small ridge overlooking Rath Cail.

You have travelled into Rath Cail with Legolas and should now speak to him.Talk to Legolas in Rath Cail'As I feared, my father spoke true of Ungwetári's children. If her spiders come now to the gates of Durthang, they fear no counter-strike from Ugrukhôr or any others of the Gúrzyul. Even the deceptions of the Enemy could not sway them into his service, and now that Sauron has fallen, nothing remains to restrain them from hunting their prey.

'I think it unwise to remain still in Lhingris overlong, and so I believe we should travel into Rath Cail and seek shelter amongst the Morgai.

'When you are ready, we will depart.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will swiftly travel into Rath Cail with Legolas when you accept this quest.</rgb>'Ah, this will do. I suppose it is no worse than anywhere else in the hills of the Ephel Dúath....'

Legolas peers into the distance, silently observing Rath Cail.

'This vale is swarming with foes, but I fear we must pass through it.'Several of the Orcs of Kála-murg have fallen to a mysterious disease stemming from Shelob's children, which they refer to as "the hollowing."Hollowing Orcs can be found throughout Kála-murg in Rath Cail.

You have heard the pleas of an Orc doomed to death by "the hollowing" to spare others like him a terrible fate. You should mercifully defeat any hollowing Orcs you encounter in Kála-murg.Mercifully defeat hollowing Orcs at Kála-murg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have mercifully defeated many of the hollowing Orcs at Kála-murg.Completed'I-I cannot see! My damned eyes have gone! Is that you, Glupronk?'

The Orc sniffs frantically at the air around him.

'Nar, you don't have his scent! I'm...hollowing, and there's no way out of that!

'I'll be like the Zhavár-hai soon, and I don't want that! No Orc should want that! Old Shelob's let her children crawl around within us and feast on our insides!

'Some live, some don't... but all of them are carrying around who knows what in their guts. I think the dead ones are lucky if you ask me!

'You're probably pretty fond of Orc-slaying, stranger, so if you see any like me -- make sure you do them in!'

The Orc spits vaguely in your direction, and then draws a knife and stabs his own throat.Although the Orcs remain foes of the Free Peoples of Middle-earth, you have shown great mercy in sparing the Orcs of Kála-murg a vile fate.After finding the Silent One's home of sorts, the Orc was forced to flee deeper into Lhingris. You and Legolas must now pursue the elusive Orc if you hope to claim his armour.The trails of blood begin at the Silent Haunt and lead deeper into Lhingris.

Legolas has spotted two trails of blood leading from the Silent Haunt. It seems the Silent One is trying to lose you in the shadows of Lhingris.Follow the trail of the Silent One's blood deeper into LhingrisLegolas can be found above the swamp of Dath Nethryn in Lhingris.

The trails of blood have led both you and Legolas to the swamp of Dath Nethryn. The Silent One now hides within, hoping to tend his wounds and escape your pursuit.Talk to Legolas near Dath Nethryn'${PLAYER}, we must find the Silent One.

'As his face is mostly obscured by his helmet, his armour could be of great use to us in learning more of Shelob and her children. If we can recover his armour, you might gain entry into Cirith Ungol and go unnoticed as one of the many Orcs in its garrison. Indeed, Captain Róglarg even expects the Silent One to report to Cirith Ungol!

'The Silent One knew well enough not to remain here, and he left two trails of blood leading deeper into Lhingris to escape our pursuit. I shall follow one, and you should follow the other.

'Let us see where they lead!''Hah! A clever Orc, indeed!

'${PLAYER}, the trails of blood converge here in this swamp!

'Even now, the Silent One must seek shelter in its shallows. We must find him before we lose the path!'Many of the terrible creatures that dwell within Lhingris are fearful of light and flame and dare not stray too close to either.Slain Orcs can be found littering the road leading to Durthang in Lhingris.

You have noticed that burning the dead of Durthang has worked to repel the spiders of Lhingris. You should burn slain Orcs to fend off the spiders of Lhingris.Burn slain Orcs at Durthang in Lhingris (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have burned many of the slain Orcs at the gates of Durthang in Lhingris.CompletedAs you explore the gates of Durthang in Lhingris, you notice that the inhabitants of Durthang have begun to burn their dead as a means to repel the attacking spiders.

You should burn the slain Orcs near Durthang and repel the spiders that have come to the northern reaches of Lhingris.You have burned many of the slain Orcs at the gates of Durthang in Lhingris. The glow of the flames did well to repel some of the spiders of Lhingris, but it is likely that once the fires die out, the spiders will return in force.Legolas has ventured into Lhingris after becoming suspicious that an old foe of his father, Thranduil, still dwells in the Ephel Dúath.Legolas can be found somewhere near the road leading to Durthang in Lhingris.

Legolas has asked you to join him in his search for an old foe known to his father as the spider-queen, Ungwetári.Travel into Lhingris and join Legolas in his search for UngwetáriLegolas is standing in the remains of small encampment beside the road to Durthang in Lhingris.

You have found Legolas in a ruined Orkish encampment and should now speak to him.Talk to Legolas in the ruined encampment'${PLAYER}, I have seen something terrible along the road to Durthang. A few spiders scuttled too near the ruins from Lhingris, and I was reminded at once of Mirkwood and the tales of my father, Thranduil!

'In an Age now past, he battled against a terrible spider -- itself only a child of one even greater. The great spider-queen: Ungwetári!

'It has been over three thousand years since my father heard her terrible name spoken by one of the Nazgûl, but I fear a creature so terrible and so near to the lands of the Enemy had strength enough to persist long beyond the years of Men!

'I must go into Lhingris and seek the source of the evil within the Ephel Dúath! If you wish to join me, I shall wait for a time near the road to Durthang -- but do not tarry overlong!''I am glad you have come, ${PLAYER}....

'Be this the work of Ungwetári herself or that of her terrible children, the spiders of Lhingris are born of some terrible evil.'You have found a grave-faced Galadriel in her garden, by her Mirror, awaiting you.You should approach Galadriel.Approach GaladrielGaladriel has stepped up to her Mirror and looks ready to gaze into it.Talk to GaladrielGaladriel is gazing into her Mirror. Stand by her and she will relate all that she sees.Hear what the Mirror showsGaladriel seems to have seen all that she will see in the Mirror, tonight, and is ready to talk with you.Talk to Galadriel'I suppose this must be a taste of the mortals' Doom, to know that all we hold dear must slip from us. It is a wisdom that has been largely held from me until now.

'I will not flinch from acting, though the price be heavy. Come, ${PLAYER}, let us go back to the Court and our waiting council of war.''Stand beside me. Gaze into the Mirror with me. In truth, I do not know what I will see here, though there is a great deal I would know if I could. I feel certain, however, that now is the time and that we should not wait.

'Are you ready, friend?''Does it seem a riddle to you, what we have seen this night? The one light was Lothlórien; or rather it was myself; or rather it was this ring I bear upon my finger.'

Suddenly, you are able to see a ring upon her finger, bright as a distant star.

'And the darkness it vied with was Dol Guldur. In the Mirror we saw two possibilities of what might come. We may abandon our strife against these foes, and their power will grow and grow. Or we may snuff them out... but be ourselves ever -- diminished. I cannot say, precisely. I could not see as clearly as I have been used.

'For in truth, some of my powers are failing whether we war against Dol Guldur or not. This ring that I revealed to you just now was forged in the same days the Dark Lord forged his One. Though it was ever hidden from him and its own power kept safe, still the rings were tied. Celebrimbor, for all he did, could not sever that bond, I am afraid. Now that the One is no more, all the others fade and falter. I doubt I shall ever see clearly within my Mirror again, and much else shall be lost to me.

'Still, I would pay the price again, in a heart beat.'You have gone down with Pippin and Merry to Cormallen, to share a meal with Sam and Frodo, who have been spending their days there.You should approach the table, where the four hobbits have already begun.Approach the tablePippin wishes to speak to you.Talk to PippinYou should join in the meal and partake of the food and drink laid out on the table.Eat or drinkEnjoy the hobbits' companyYou should hear what Sam says for himself.Talk to SamEnjoy the hobbits' companyYou should help yourself to some more food and drink while the four friends talk.Eat or drink some more (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Enjoy the hobbits' companyHear Merry's reply to Frodo.Talk to MerryEnjoy the hobbits' companyListen to Frodo's answer to the complaint.Talk to FrodoEnjoy the hobbits' companyYou should help yourself to some more food and drink while the four friends talk.Eat or drink some more (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Enjoy the hobbits' companyYou should hear what Pippin says to Frodo.Talk to PippinEnjoy the hobbits' company'Don't worry, ${PLAYER}, we didn't wait to start eating. Or making merry. Still, I am awfully glad you are here to join us. I don't think we are in any danger of running short on food or drink, for that matter.

'Sit down and have some, please!''They weren't so lavish as all that, as you put it, and besides, less than half were mine, truly, but only gifts Mr. Frodo refused. He has taken not a one gift, has he, though ever so many have been laid at his feet these last days. 

'But tell us, Mr. Merry, about this hidden house they have built for us, and what a palace it is, or so I've heard tell.''No, and much has come of it, as I will tell you if you will stop frowning so sternly at me for a moment and listen. I confess, when first we set our hearts on seeing Mordor it was a foolhardy lark. We were thinking only of the treasure chest Bilbo was said to have returned with, and seeing what we had not yet seen. But that is not at all what we found.'

Merry goes on to describe the two encounters with the half-orcs, the riddles they let drop, and all that followed in the hopes of unravelling them.

'And so, you see Frodo, we have had a proper adventure of our own, in the end.''No, Pippin, I am being quite serious. Those clever arguments might work on Aragorn or Gandalf, who do not see things quite at our level, as it were -- but it is me to whom you are talking. The time of hobbits going into Mordor should be done, I think. In fact, I have been thinking a great deal about how the time of our adventuring is coming to an end.''Ah, you do see into our hearts, Frodo, as only a hobbit and an old friend could ever do. Well, you have my word then. Yours too, Merry? Good. Neither of us shall go back into Mordor during our stay in Gondor.

'But if the riddle of those squinty fellows drives me to jibbering, it will be on your conscience!'King Thorin III has called for the folk of the Lonely Mountain to assemble and hear his words.Hrostyr stands before the throne of King Thorin III.Talk to HrostyrThorin III, King Under the Mountain, addresses the folk of the Lonely Mountain.Listen to the words of Thorin III, King Under the Mountain'I could have told the king you were worthy of praise when you first arrived and saved you some time... but no one asked me, after all! Ha ha ha!

'Well-done, ${PLAYER}! You have done much for my people!''We will not need to fight side by side on this day, ${PLAYER}, as we did before. This is a day for celebration!'King Elessar has invited the folk of the city to a ceremony in the Court of the Fountain.Mithrandir is in the Court of the Fountain, high atop the city.Talk to Mithrandir in the Court of the FountainKing Elessar is in the Court of the Fountain, atop the city.Listen to the words of King Elessar in the Court of the FountainMalthengil, the guard of King Elessar, is in the Court of the Fountain.Talk to Malthengil in the Court of the FountainKing Elessar is in the Court of the Fountain, atop the city.Listen further to the words of King Elessar in the Court of the Fountain'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}, true friend of Gondor!''I am glad you have come, ${PLAYER}. You beheld the sapling growing on the side of Mindolluin, and rumour of its discovery has spread throughout Minas Tirith, but it is important for the folk of the city to see it with their own eyes. Elessar believes that one group needs to see it above all others, and I believe he is right.                               

'This ceremony is for them. I hope they hear its message clearly.

'The old tree has been removed with care. It will not be burned. It will instead be laid to rest within Rath Dínen, there to remain in dignity and in peace.''There is one more thing, ${PLAYER}. The king requested your presence for more than just that! Attend him now, and hear his words.'You have returned with Merry and Pippin to Mordor, with the sole intent of finding the three suspicious half-orcs. The Ranger Golodhon has pointed you on the way to where he believes they ought to be.Merry has brought your expedition to a stop. You should hear what he has to say.Talk to MerryYou should begin searching the area for signs of the three half-orcs.Begin searchingYou should continue searching the area for signs of the three half-orcs.Continue searchingYou should continue searching the area for signs of the three half-orcs.Stop to get your bearingsYou should resume searching the area for signs of the three half-orcs.Continue searchingYou should resume searching the area for signs of the three half-orcs.Continue searchingYou should talk to Pippin and find out what he thinks he has seen.Talk to PippinYou should follow the footprints that Pippin discovered and see where they lead.You have found the three half-orcs again. Join Merry and Pippin and try to get some answers out of them.Confront the half-orcsAn enraged troll has stumbled into the confrontation, knocking down one of the half-orcs and sending the other two fleeing. You must defend yourself against the troll!Defeat the enraged trollThe half-orc who was struck by the troll has some life yet in him. You should speak to him now and see what you can learn.Talk to the dying half-orcYou should talk to Pippin and Merry.Talk to MerryTalk to Pippin'Merry is right, of course. We are done with Mordor for now. Perhaps for good. Let us get going then, before anything else unpleasant can happen!''If they have a bone of decency among the three of them, they will be standing upon a high rock again, talking loudly enough to hear and find them by.''This is near to the place Golodhon thought they should be. They are not standing out in plain view, at any rate! I suppose the only thing is to begin looking around for them, or signs of where they have gone.''What is it, Pippin?''Just look what I've found. Over there, between those rocks. Boot marks -- and they're too soft and narrow to be Orc-feet, by my reckoning.

'Come, let us see where they lead!''Bah! That's done me in, I know it. And you lot.  So you found some barrels in Isengard, did you? That isn't half of it. Ha ha. Ha ha ha ha....'

He devolves into pained coughing for several moments.

'There's plenty more where that came from, in your precious Shire. That land is good undefended, it is. Ripe for the plucking.''Poor fellow. He will not be dearly missed by anyone, I suppose, not even his craven comrades. I cannot say that I am satisfied, but I think we have done all we could this day. It is time we headed back.''Those - those weren't answers. Those were riddles. Riddles that would drive even old Bilbo mad!'If the rumours are true, the Iron Garrison is in danger and their foothold in Khazad-dûm is threatened.You have entered Khazad-dûm from the East-gate, and the situation appears worse than you feared.Talk to Solsi inside the First HallBósi and Brogur have not yet made it back to the East-gate. Solsi asked you to find them, if they still live.Find Bósi and Brogur and escape from Khazad-dûmBrogur is on the western side of the First Hall.Talk to Brogur in the First HallBósi has made it to the First Hall.Talk to Bósi in the First HallBrogur fell behind during the retreat, and Bósi asked you to find his cousin.Find Brogur in the First HallBósi is in the First Hall, near the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.Talk to Bósi in the First Hall'Khazad-dûm is lost!''${PLAYER}! The Iron Garrison is in disarray! Great tremors shook the city to its very foundations, and Orcs and goblins boiled from every crevice and chasm in the deep! I have never seen so many!

'We were overcome! We have no choice but to escape, but Bósi and Brogur have not made it back yet! You have to find them!''${PLAYER}! We have to fight them off for as long as we can! We need to give my cousin time to catch up to us!''${PLAYER}, there is nothing we can do! We must retreat! Everyone, make for the East-gate!''${PLAYER}, Brogur has fallen behind! Can you find him?''Brogur, my cousin! How can it be? How can it be?

'We have to leave, ${PLAYER}! We have to get out of here! The Iron Garrison has failed!'There are many issues facing Gondor which require the pronouncements of King Elessar.Malthengil has asked you to deliver your report of Mordor to King Elessar, but first there are some matters on which the king must rule.Talk to MalthengilKing Elessar must weigh the issues facing Gondor and make his pronouncements.Wait until the king calls for your reportBeregond is in the hall, and has not heard that it is his time for judgement.Find Beregond in the Hall of the King and tell him it is his time for judgementKing Elessar must weigh the issues facing Gondor and make his pronouncements.Wait until the king calls for your report'Tell me what has transpired in Mordor since your last report, my friend.''There are several matters upon which the king must rule, and then he will hear your report of Mordor, ${PLAYER}. It will not be long.''${PLAYER}? Yes, I am ready.

'I left my post without permission; three men of Minas Tirith I slew in my haste to come to Faramir's aid. I will rue the deed evermore, but I did not know what else to do!'Thranduil recounts the trials Mirkwood and Lothlórien endured while Gondor marched and fought at the very gates of Mordor.You are a Lothlórien sentry, keeping watch over the Golden Wood. You should join your fellow sentry up ahead.Orc invaders have appeared in Lothlórien! Help defend the Golden Wood.Defend Lothlórien from Orcs and trollsOrc invaders have appeared in Lothlórien! Push onward to where the fighting appears worst.Push onward to the east and defeat the Orc-invadersOrc invaders have appeared in Lothlórien and now Lady Galadriel has taken to the field. Fight by her side and guard her at all costs.Head east and protect Galadriel while she readies her defencesGaladriel has used her powers to defeat the last of this Orc assault. Now she wishes to talk to you.'You are needed in Mirkwood. I fear they will need all the aid that we can spare. Go now.'Talk to GaladrielYou have arrived in Mirkwood and come to the edge of the fighting.Defeat Orc-invaders in Mirkwood by the lamp-posts in the woodYou have arrived in Mirkwood. You have crossed paths with Lord Thranduil himself, and should talk with him.Head east and talk to King ThranduilKing Thranduil asked you to follow the archers to the north-east, up the hill.Follow the archers to the north-east, up the hillYou have come to a hilltop in Mirkwood held by Elves and embattled on all sides.Defeat every attacking enemy on the hilltop. This is the last stand!You have crossed paths with Lord Thranduil once again in Mirkwood.Talk to Thranduil on the hilltop'You, however, have done your part, and I thank you. Return to your Lord and Lady and have them know that I would share in their council regarding Dol Guldur, as soon as it is safe to do so.''You are needed in Mirkwood. I fear they will need all the aid that we can spare. Go now.''From Lothlórien you come? Then hope yet glimmers. Your aid is most welcome here.

'I will move on with my retinue, but you are needed ahead, up the hill! Follow the archers to battle! Perhaps we will meet again, when the fighting is done!''We have driven them back! You fought bravely, soldier.

'Nay, do not pursue them. Too many of our kin have fallen this day, and we must restore our strength in case they strike at us yet again.'Glorfindel is come to the Council of War in the Court of Lothlórien. You, too, are expected to join them.Glorfindel waits for you to approach the council.Talk to GlorfindelIt is time to approach the Council of War.Approach the CouncilYou and Glorfindel have joined the Council of War. Talk to him to hear of the text he recovered.Talk to GlorfindelHear what Galadriel has to say of the prophecy.Talk to GaladrielGaladriel will now address the Council of War.Hear Galadriel's pronouncement'It is decided. The Elves of Lothlórien and the Elves of Mirkwood will make ready to assault Dol Guldur.''Gi suilon! They are waiting for us. Come.''As you are aware, I have recovered a text of the words uttered by Gil-galad long ago. I will speak only a little of what was written, which pertains to what is now coming to pass:

'When the light first fades from the Golden Wood, then the Shadow in the Green Wood may be torn down. But if the Shadow be torn down, faster shall the Golden Wood fade.

'I think the words are clear, and though it may give us great sorrow, still we know what course we must take.''It is just as we saw in my Mirror, ${PLAYER}. There can be no doubt in the matter now. It is decided.

'Take your place, I will address the Council of War.'After disguising themselves in Orkish armour and cloaks now kept by the Silent One, Samwise Gamgee and Frodo Baggins passed through Lhingris in their quest to reach Mount Doom.Mr. Frodo can be found somewhere in the Tower of Cirith Ungol.Rescue Mr. Frodo from the Tower of Cirith UngolThe Ring has begun to tempt you! You must resist and rescue Mr. Frodo.See what could be if you were only to claim the Ring!The Ring has tempted you with visions of yourself as Samwise the Strong. Use your hobbit-sense, Sam!Use your hobbit-sense<rgb=#FF0000>Claim the One Ring!</rgb>The gate of Cirith Ungol can be found at the easternmost point of Cirith Ungol.Pass through the gate of Cirith UngolAn unseen force seems to block your passage into Cirith Ungol.Harden your will and charge at the gate of Cirith Ungol!The phial of Galadriel! Its light might overcome whatever force bars your path!Draw the phial of Galadriel and hold it aloft!Now's your chance! Mr. Frodo needs your help!Spring past the Silent Watchers into Cirith Ungol!Mr. Frodo can be found somewhere in the Tower of Cirith Ungol.Search for Mr. Frodo in the Tower of Cirith UngolAn Orc has fled from you. You should continue higher into the Tower of Cirith Ungol in search of Mr. Frodo.Ascend the Tower of Cirith UngolYou hear Orkish voices, and the familiar voice of Shagrat. Quickly, hide!Hide behind slain Orcs and wreckage!Shagrat has chased the Orc, Snaga, onto the parapet of Cirith Ungol. You should remain hidden until the right moment.Wait for the right momentShagrat is blocking your path into the heights of Cirith Ungol. It's now or never, Sam!Confront ShagratMr. Frodo must be in the turret of the Tower of Cirith Ungol. Keep climbing!Find the topmost chamber of Cirith UngolMr. Frodo must be in the turret of the Tower of Cirith Ungol, and it sounds like he's in danger!Rescue Mr. Frodo from the Tower of Cirith UngolYou've rescued Mr. Frodo!Talk to Mr. Frodo and escape the Tower of Cirith Ungol'Am I still dreaming?'

You embrace Frodo so hard that you nearly lift him off the ground.

'You've found me, dear Sam! But where are we? And how did I end up here?'

You tell Frodo that there's no time for tales until you get far, far away from this tower!You have come with the Host of the West to the gates of the White City, where the future rule of Gondor will be determined.Prince Imrahil of Dol Amroth is before the gate of the White City, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Imrahil before the gate of Minas TirithPrince Imrahil told you to find a good place to stand among the townsfolk on the right side as you approach the city.The Host of the West has come to the gate of Minas Tirith, and Faramir, Steward of Gondor, greets Aragorn outside the city.Witness events as they transpire before the cityAfter so long, Gondor at last has a king again.<rgb=#FF0000>Cheer</rgb> for the king and for GondorAfter so long, Gondor at last has a king again.Gondor has a king again!'My heart swells to see this day, ${PLAYER}! May the reign of King Elessar be long and fruitful, and may Gondor prosper beneath his rule!''It will not be long now, ${PLAYER}. Find a place to stand among the ranks here on the right side, and I will rejoin you shortly.''Ah, have you come to see the ceremony? I have been telling my kinswoman from the country some of the finer points she might otherwise miss. Life in the city can be very different from that in the smaller villages, as I am sure you are aware!

'Why, there are so many great folk coming hither and thither on important errands! And now one is to be made king! My cousin disbelieves that I have rubbed elbows with one such as he, but I say truly that is the case, and who would doubt Ioreth in this? None who know her reputation for speaking truth would dare it!

'Now shush, for the Steward prepares to begin!'You have come with Bósi for an audience with King Thorin III.Hrostyr, the personal guard of King Thorin, will bring you to see him.Talk to HrostyrYou are waiting for an audience with King Thorin.Wait with Bósi for your audience with King ThorinSomething is not right! You have to stop Thorin from wearing that helm!Knock the helm from Dómor's hand before he gives it to ThorinA sensation of great evil emanates from the fallen helm!Remain on your guard and protect the king from dangerKing Thorin has commanded you to keep Dómor from escaping.Defeat DómorEvil has come to the Lonely Mountain, and the dwarves fight to defend their home.Remain on your guard for further danger'Thank you for your assistance, ${PLAYER}. I will question Dómor before he is brought to the dungeons, but it seems clear what I will learn: he betrayed Eirek and Mogi to their deaths.

'Do not go just yet. I will have further need of you in this matter.''You desire an audience with King Thorin? He will grant it, I am sure, but right now he is meeting with another. It should not be long. He hears a report from Dómor, one of the heroes that adventured in Mordor. He has returned with tidings for the king.'With the guidance of Glathlírel, search for Glorfindel in the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu.Glathlírel is ready to explore the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu once again, in search of Glorfindel.Talk to GlathlírelSearch the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu for clues of Glorfindel's whereabouts.Follow Glathlírel to search for clues of Glorfindel's whereaboutsFend off the attacking Pale-folk Defenders.Defeat attacking Pale-folk Defenders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Continue to look for clues of Glorfindel's whereabouts.Continue following Glathlírel to seek clues of Glorfindel's whereaboutsDefeat the Darklings.Defeat the Darklings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find a way past the locked gate.Find a way past the locked gateDefeat the troll.Defeat the trollYou have found a clue and should examine it.Examine the clueFollow the clue to Glorfindel.Follow the clue to GlorfindelStand by Glorfindel's side.Stand by Glorfindel's sideHear what Glorfindel has to say.Talk to GlorfindelWeaken the Balrog Thaurlach so Glorfindel can finish it once and for all.Weaken the Balrog Thaurlach to allow Glorfindel to defeat itYou should talk to Glorfindel, now that Thaurlach is defeated.Talk to Glorfindel'It is time to leave this place. I must seclude myself a little while and consider the prophecy of Gil-galad I have found. Then, as soon as I am able, I shall find my way to Caras Galadhon.''${PLAYER}, we must look for clues as to the whereabouts of Glorfindel.

'We know not what lurks in the Rift of Nûrz Ghâshu now, so prepare yourself.''I thought I knew all there was to know of the Balrog Thaurlach. How mistaken I was! I have much yet to learn.''I felt the dark presence brooding nearby when first I entered this place, but the prize I sought was too important and I was not to be deterred. Still, I had hoped to slip away as quietly as I came, and instead I walked nearly into its opened maw!

'Fortune smiled upon me, however, and you came upon us at just that moment. Not the mightiest warrior of Elves or Men would face a Balrog alone, willingly -- though I yet flatter myself I might have been its match.

'We should not tarry here, I think. I have found what I came here seeking, though I must take some time in secluded peace to read it over and pierce its deeper meaning. I cannot pause overlong, however, for I am expected in the Court of Lothlórien. Ah, but I see you knew that.

'Perhaps, then, you may do me a double service. First, return there forthwith and assure Galadriel and Celeborn I am on my way. Second, tell Galadriel that I wish to give the Gwedluith I entrusted to her to Arwen, as a betrothal gift. Indeed, I would have you bear the gift to her, ${PLAYER}.''Balrogs are not so easily slain. Though they may seem defeated, they may heal and even regain form with time. This lesson I have learned, painfully, in ages past.

'Fortunately, I possess the art required to fully extinguish such a foe. Come and fight by my side. Together we will put a final end to this fell servant of Morgoth.'You have come with Mithrandir and Elessar to a secret path high above the city.You have come with King Elessar and Mithrandir to a secret path high above Minas Tirith.Talk to King Elessar on the slope of MindolluinYou have come with King Elessar and Mithrandir to a secret path high above Minas Tirith, on the slopes of Mount Mindolluin.Remain alert for any sign of dangerA sapling grows by the snow line high atop Mount Mindolluin.Find the sapling amid the snows atop MindolluinKing Elessar is by the young sapling atop Mindolluin.Talk to King Elessar on MindolluinMithrandir is by the young sapling atop Mindolluin.Talk to Mithrandir on Mindolluin'I see in this discovery a sign that Gondor will once more come to prosperity, and the line of kings will survive the numbering of my own days. My heart is made glad!

'I will remove this sapling with care, and bring it to the Court of the Fountain, there to be planted. The White Tree will grow there, once again, and may the voices of the people sing with joy at the sight!''I do not know why Mithrandir wished for us to climb the slope of Mindolluin, but I am pleased you are here as well, ${PLAYER}. It will do us much good to breathe the cold, clear air of the heights!

'Remain alert for members of the Withered Tree. They have continued to speak against my rule, and while there has been no outright violence since the slaying of Mauthoi, still I fear it may come.''In shape and form this sapling is unmistakable, ${PLAYER}! This can be nothing other than young growth of the White Tree, but that has been dead for hundreds of years and this sapling can be no more than seven years old!

'How can this wonder be possible?''I see in this sapling much that identifies it as descended from the royal line of Telperion, Eldest of Trees, from which came through several generations the White Tree that grew in the Court of the Fountain. But that tree withered and died, and was left atop the city.

'But it seems now that a fruit was taken from it before it died, and was brought to this high hallow by unknown hands. It was left here on the mountainside, and the life within it lay sleeping. It remained here hidden for hundreds of years, much as the line of kings stayed hidden in the North.

'As the new age approached, the life sleeping within the fruit gave rise to this sapling, and it has grown here on the snow-line for a few years only. Remember this, King Elessar! The fruit ripens but rarely, yet if it does, one should be planted or the line might die out of the world.'Pippin and Merry have gathered 'the fellowship and everyone else who really mattered' in Bâr Thorenion for a meal and good cheer.Take your place among the guests.Take your placeAfter escaping their servitude in Dor Amarth and Talath Úrui, several Nurnhoth have become lost and endangered in the swamps of Dath Nethryn.Nurnhoth can be found in Dath Nethryn.

A Nurnhoth has asked you to find his companions, who have become lost and endangered in the swamps of Dath Nethryn.You have aided many of the Nurnhoth lost and endangered in the shallows of Dath Nethryn.Completed'No... No! I can't go back! Not after we've travelled so far!'

You try to calm the Nurnhoth and explain that you mean him no harm.

'Ah, I see. I am sorry, but when you have slaved for the Eye as long as myself and my companions, it is easy to see everyone as a foe. You see, my companions and I were on our way through a hidden pass out of Mordor, but we became separated. After all, it was not a swamp the last time we were sent here to gather ore from the caverns!

'Something broke loose in those mountains when the Master's tower fell, I think, and now there's foetid water all about!

'I cannot leave without my companions, but I have not the means to rescue them myself. These rags cannot defend against the blades of Orcs or the bites of spiders! Will you aid them and send them here to me?

'Once that is done, we can leave this sorry land behind....'You have aided many of the Nurnhoth lost and endangered in the shallows of Dath Nethryn, and you have helped them escape both the oppressive might of Sauron's servants and the terrible creatures of Lhingris.King Éomer wishes to speak with you concerning your allegiance to the Free Peoples of Middle-earth.Representatives from each of the Allegiances can be found at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the ruins of the Black Gate.King Éomer can be found at the Foothold, just inside Udûn.Talk to King Éomer at the Udûn Foothold'${PLAYER}, you have performed many great deeds for the Free Peoples of Middle-earth, but many folk still require aid. To that end, I believe it would be most beneficial for you to swear your allegiance to one of these groups. This would allow you to properly focus your efforts and your skills, thereby contributing as much as you can to their particular needs.'

<rgb=#00FF00>Choose an Allegiance by selecting the list of menus on the toolbar at the bottom of your screen and hovering over the Character option, which will allow you to select the Allegiances menu. You may then choose which of the Allegiances to aid first. Once you have finished the complete story of that Allegiance, you may then select another to complete.</rgb>'It would be most beneficial for you to swear your allegiance to one of these groups. This would allow you to properly focus your efforts and your skills, thereby contributing as much as you can to their particular needs.'

<rgb=#00FF00>Choose an Allegiance by selecting the list of menus on the toolbar at the bottom of your screen and hovering over the Character option, which will allow you to select the Allegiances menu. You may then choose which of the Allegiances to aid first. Once you have finished the complete story of that Allegiance, you may then select another to complete.</rgb>'I know you will be of great help to those folk, ${PLAYER}! May your skills and your effort on behalf of that Allegiance prove worthwhile as you adventure in Mordor!

'Perhaps one day you will return to fight on behalf of my own kingdom? Ha! We will fight together again, if that comes to pass, and let the foes of the Mark tremble! But for now, my friend, there are other folk who have need of you! Aid your chosen Allegiance to the best of your ability!'You and Legolas have tracked the Silent One into Dath Nethryn. The Silent One lurks somewhere in this foetid swamp, seeking to regain his advantage.The Silent One can be found somewhere within Dath Nethryn.

You and Legolas have tracked the Silent One to Dath Nethryn. You should now search for the Silent One in the swamp.Find and follow the Silent One's path in Dath NethrynThe Silent One can be found somewhere within Kâm Lagúrz in Dath Nethryn.

You have followed the Silent One's tracks into Kâm Lagúrz where the corpse of an enormous spider now rests. You should search for the Silent One.Find and defeat the Silent OneLegolas can be found above the swamp of Dath Nethryn in Lhingris.

You have defeated the Silent One and taken his armour. You should now return to Legolas.'${PLAYER}, we must go down into Dath Nethryn at once! Our hunt could soon be ended -- for good or ill.

'If the Silent One has left any tracks, they will soon be consumed by the mud and foul waters of the swamp.

'It shall be a contest, my friend! The first of us to find the Silent One shall wear his armour in Cirith Ungol!

'Let us go!''Ah-ha! I knew he could not elude us for much longer! I suppose the "honour" of dressing as an Orc is now yours, ${PLAYER}.

'Now then, my friend, I think it is time we test your new disguise!'After exploring much of Lhingris, Legolas has become unnerved by similarities he has noticed in the many creatures and insects of the Ephel Dúath.Ichor can be harvested from various creatures in Lhingris.

Legolas has asked you to gather ichor from the many insects and spiders of Lhingris.Collect phials of spider ichor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect phials of grodbog ichor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect phials of crawler ichor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect phials of carrion-fly ichor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Legolas can be found beside the road above the swamps of Dath Nethryn.

You have gathered a great deal of ichor from the creatures of Lhingris and should now return to Legolas.'My friend, I have a peculiar task to ask of you....

'If you would humour me, I would ask that you collect phials of ichor from the many creatures of Lhingris. Spiders, gredbyg, crawlers, and even the carrion-flies are all of interest to me.

'I have grown more unsettled the deeper we pass into these lands, and I wish to examine the ichor of its creatures more closely.

'Return to me when you have gathered as much as you can bear!''Ah, that will do. Thank you, my friend.'

Legolas begins to inspect each phial of ichor you recovered and does so for some time. All of a sudden, a wave of horror washes over his face.

'${PLAYER}... this... this cannot be!

'Each phial of ichor you have collected is of the same source... they are all of Shelob's brood! Every single spider and every single insect in Lhingris is another of her twisted children!

'Lhingris itself writhes with her essence!'After disguising themselves in Orkish armour and cloaks now kept by the Silent One, Samwise Gamgee and Frodo Baggins passed through Lhingris in their quest to reach Mount Doom.Legolas is standing in the Silent Haunt.

You have tracked the Silent One to his lair in Lhingris and discovered a tattered black cloak and Orkish armour bearing a single strand of Frodo's hair. You should talk to Legolas and think of how Frodo and Sam came to disguise themselves as Orcs of Mordor.Talk to Legolas in the Silent HauntLegolas is standing in the Silent Haunt.

You have thought long on the choices of Master Samwise, and should now speak to Legolas.Talk to Legolas in the Silent Haunt'This is very curious, indeed. A strand of Frodo's hair is caught in this armour....

'This "Silent One" is quite the clever Orc! I doubt most of his kin would have paid such a find any attention at all!

'How much did Master Samwise choose to omit from his tale of Frodo's journey through Mordor, I wonder? It seems clear that they took the guise of Orkish warriors to pass unhindered through those at Cirith Ungol that did not answer the call to the Morannon, but when did such a need first arise... and why?''I wonder what became of the hobbits when they passed into Lhingris....''Whatever befell Master Samwise as he and Frodo passed near Cirith Ungol, he does not wish to speak of it. By the sound of this Róglarg's message, it seems as if he performed heroics far beyond that of a simple gardener!

'Ah, but his choices are his own, and I will not press him to recount such a tale.

'However, I remain curious about one thing. If their disguises fooled the Orcs of Cirith Ungol, I think we may have found a way to learn more of "Her Ladyship"!'After realising that Orcs were fleeing Durthang into Lhingris, Legolas desires to learn what happened at the gate of the fortress.The gate of Durthang can be found on the road in Lhingris to the north of the ruined encampment.

Legolas has asked you to patrol Durthang and learn what has transpired between the Orcs and spiders there.Patrol the gate of Durthang in LhingrisThe dying Uruk can be found near the gate of Durthang in Lhingris.

You have arrived to find the aftermath of a bloody battle between the warriors of Durthang and the spiders of Lhingris. An Uruk nearby with grave wounds may be able to explain what happened.Talk to the dying Uruk at Durthang'This is troubling, ${PLAYER}. What could have driven some of the Orcs from Durthang?

'If it were merely the spiders of Lhingris, Ugrukhôr would have sealed the gate and thought no more of it. No, it had to be something unexpected! But what?

'${PLAYER}, patrol the road to the gate of Durthang and learn what you can of what has happened. I think there is more to this than it seems....

'Go swiftly! I shall keep watch on the road!''Come to gloat, have you? Well, you won't have long....'

The Uruk coughs hard and dark blood runs from his mouth.The Zhavár-hai of Dath Nethryn have grown mightier and possess thicker hides than their untainted, Orkish brethren.Zhavár-hai can be found in the shallows of Dath Nethryn in southern Lhingris.

Legolas has spotted numerous Zhavár-hai roaming the foetid swamp of Dath Nethryn in Lhingris. Even if the Orcs are slain, the insects and spiders within them pose a great threat, so he has advised you to burn them after defeating them.Defeat and burn Zhavár-hai in Dath Nethryn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated and burned many of the Zhavár-hai in Dath Nethryn.Completed'Although the Silent One has fled into its shallows, he is not the only Orc I have spied.

'${PLAYER}, I fear more of the hollowed Zhavár-hai lurk in the swamp-lands below. They carry with them the children of Ungwetári... or Shelob as the Orcs now seem to call her.

'Even if the Zhavár-hai are slain, the terrible things dwelling within them may not be. Thus, I ask that you burn the bodies of any Zhavár-hai you encounter in Dath Nethryn.

'It is an unfortunate task, but it is the only way to put an end to the danger they pose to us and King Elessar's armies!'It seems the longer the Zhavár-hai bond with Shelob's children, the more potent their might and the thicker their flesh.

You have done well to defeat and burn many of the Zhavár-hai in Dath Nethryn. They can no longer pose a threat to any who venture into Lhingris, and can no longer hinder Legolas's quest to find the spider-queen, Ungwetári -- now known as Shelob.An Orc known as the Silent One has betrayed the inhabitants of Durthang to the children of Shelob, casting the lands of Lhingris and Udûn into turmoil.The Silent One can likely be found off the road to the south of Durthang with the slain Orcs he took from Durthang.

A dying Uruk has asked you to kill the Silent One, an Orc who has seemingly aligned himself with the spiders of Lhingris.Legolas can be found at the ruined encampment in northern Lhingris.

You found the Silent One's victims presented as some kind of offering to the spiders of Lhingris before the Silent One attacked you and fled back into the shadows. You should return to Legolas.Talk to Legolas at the ruined encampment'Nar, not long at all...

'That little sneak... the Silent One... he betrayed us! We were holding against the spiders until he showed up, and some help he was! The Silent One killed my Orcs with his arrows... and took some of them before the spiders got to them...

'Maybe the little rat is bringing them to Shelob... or maybe he's eating them himself!

'I won't beg, but if you find him... kill him! If he finds you... well....

'Now shove off, and let me die in peace!''An Orc has slain his own kin to offer tribute to the spiders of Lhingris? These are very strange tidings, my friend. And the Uruk used the name Shelob? It seems Ungwetári is known by that name to the Orcs....

'As for the Silent One, I wonder how he gained such a curious title. Such a name would be well-earned of a skilled archer or huntsman. Ah, but these are Orcs we speak of! Perhaps his tongue was cut from his mouth for some trifle....

