Once a sufficient number of Grisgart's allies are defeated, an opportunity will arise to burn several of Zaudru's cocoons. Once burned, the spider will become enraged and enter the battle. She must fall before the final four white silk-spinners are defeated.Breosal and Zaudru are deep within the tunnels underneath Helegrod.Approach Breosal and ZaudruBreosal has brought many allies with her, they will need to be eliminated.Defeat Breosal's alliesLogs have started falling from the bonfires. They can be used to burn the cocoons and spider-nests surrounding this area.Do not defeat any silk-spinners until the cocoons are burnedLight the cocoons on fireBurning the cocoons has angered Zaudru. She must now be vanquished.Defeat ZaudruDefeat the Angmarim silk-spinners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Harthalín's quick bow-string has slain an Orc carrying word of a meeting of the Masters of Mordor, and she thinks it important to learn more.Orcs and evil Men of every variety near the Spire of Lûghash, the settlement of Fushaum Bal, the tower of Cirith Ungol, and the camp outside Naerband may carry messages from their masters to Lhaereth the Stained.Intercept a message near the Spire of LûghashIntercept a message near Fushaum BalIntercept a message from Orodir, the camp outside NaerbandIntercept a message near the Tower of Cirith Ungol<rgb=#00FF00>All intercepted messages can be read from your inventory</rgb>Harthalín is by the eastward-running road in Agarnaith.<rgb=#00FF00>All intercepted messages can be read from your inventory</rgb>'I will retain the samples we obtained from the swamp and test them with the athelas given to you by Mithrandir, and I will give to you another task. We need information, ${PLAYER}.

'The creature that gave this message to the Ghâsh-hai I slew was named Dolgrist, and the words were on behalf of Borangos, of whom we have learned little. I propose that Lhaereth has requested the Masters of Mordor to attend her in Seregost, and they have sent their responses to her in the hands of runners such as the one I killed. Other responses must be on their way to her, and we must learn of their disposition.

'Defeat Orcs and evil Men of every variety near the following sites, and you may intercept communications intended for Lhaereth. I suggest the Spire of Lûghash, the settlement of Fushaum Bal, near the tower of Cirith Ungol, and the camp outside Naerband. Good hunting to you, ${PLAYER}!''Lhaereth has requested the Masters of Mordor to attend her in Seregost, and they have sent their responses to her in the hands of runners such as the one I killed. Other responses must be on their way to her, and we must learn of their disposition.

'Defeat Orcs and evil Men of every variety near the following sites, and you may intercept communications intended for Lhaereth. I suggest the Spire of Lûghash, the settlement of Fushaum Bal, near the tower of Cirith Ungol, and the camp outside Naerband. Good hunting to you, ${PLAYER}!''Very well done, my friend! These messages shed enough light on the situation for us to determine a course of action. Mordor is tumultuous enough that each of the Masters wishes to consolidate his power; they are willing to meet, but they do not wish to do so in any of their rivals' strongholds. I cannot blame them for this lack of trust! Indeed, it seems wise for each of them to exercise an abundance of caution!

'But they each want the ascendancy of Mordor; this meeting will happen. But where will they finally agree to meet?'Harthalín believes the meeting of the Masters will take place, but if you are to make use of this information you must learn what location each of them might trust.The watching-stone inhabited by the spirit Zaghárog is on the road to the Caverns of Fire, in Talath Úrui.Talk to Zaghárog outside the Caverns of Fire, in Talath ÚruiZaghárog showed you a particular area in Lhingris and asked you to kill the creature there.The watching-stone inhabited by the spirit Zaghárog is on the road to the Caverns of Fire, in Talath Úrui.Talk to Zaghárog outside the Caverns of Fire, in Talath ÚruiZaghárog told you to walk to the north-west. Will this provide your answer?Zaghárog told you to walk to the north-westHarthalín is by the road to Nargroth, on the eastern edge of Talath Úrui.Talk to Harthalín by the road to Nargroth'I have had an idea, ${PLAYER}. Each of the Masters of Mordor has walked this land longer than either of us, and they know its out of the way corners better than we could ever hope to do. Even I, who fought during the Siege of Barad-dûr at the end of the Second Age and saw the shape of the land then, cannot pretend to the knowledge of those who seek to rule Mordor now.

'But there is one who has demonstrated such knowledge of this land to us. Do you remember? I do not trust him, for who truly could, but he has watched the movements of our enemies and knows somewhat of their motivations.

'I speak of Zaghárog, ${PLAYER}. The spirit in the stone may have seen more than we, and know of the place the Masters will settle upon for their council.''There is one who has demonstrated a familiarity of this land to us. Do you remember? I do not trust him, for who truly could, but he has watched the movements of our enemies and knows somewhat of their motivations.

'I speak of Zaghárog, ${PLAYER}. The spirit in the stone may have seen more than we, and know of the place the Masters will settle upon for their council.''I do not trust this creature, but he may have seen that which is hidden to us.'There is silence in your mind for so long you begin to doubt that Zaghárog will speak to you. Then, just as you are about to turn away, the presence returns. The questioning sea buffets you as before.

'Where will the Masters seek to meet?' you ask of the spirit. The only reply is silence.

'Have such meetings occurred before?' you try again, but Zaghárog does not reply. The questioning sea recedes, leaving behind only sand and silt. Skeletal fish wriggle madly on the dry bed, gasping and dying.

Then you notice their number: nine fish die in the water's absence. The presence in the darkness that is Zaghárog watches you closely, and the fish are gone. A sudden flash of light blinds you with its brilliance, and you recognize a flat space with sparse trees near many falls, in the southern reaches of Lhingris. Hateful chittering fills your ears, and you know there is a malevolent creature behind you, but you are unable to turn and face it. Then you hear the voice of Atli Spider-bane, and you know he hunts the creature that stalks you.

Zaghárog makes his demand, and his desire fills your mind and echoes in your ears.

'Kill,' he says.The images return. You recognize a flat space with sparse trees near many falls, in the southern reaches of Lhingris. Hateful chittering fills your ears, and you know there is a malevolent creature  behind you, but you are unable to turn and face it. Then you hear the voice of Atli Spider-bane, and you know he hunts the creature that stalks you.

Zaghárog makes his demand, and his desire fills your mind and echoes in your ears.

'Kill,' he says.'Zaghárog has proven he gives nothing for free, ${PLAYER}. What has he asked you to do this time?''Thank you for finishing the job, ${PLAYER}! That was quite a fight!''You have done as the spirit asked. Now let us see if he will do as he promised.'Unlike your previous visit, Zaghárog does not make you wait this time. He is present immediately, with a hungering and eager need.

'The spider is slain,' you say, and Zaghárog's confusion is apparent. You see the hidden gully in Lhingris once again, and feel the stalking shadow, and hear Atli Spider-bane bellow his battle-cry. A feeling of great insistence spears at you, over and over.

'The spider,' you say once again, and Zaghárog responds at once: 'The dwarf.'

'No,' you respond.

You are buffeted by disappointment and anger. The spirit's rage is a crushing ceiling that presses down on you. It is a cauldron of boiling fire that scalds you. It is a torrent of icy rain that freezes and pierces your skin. It passes, and Zaghárog's rage is replaced with a sullen resentment.

'Where will the Masters meet?' you ask once again, and Zaghárog shows you the nine dying fish, inexplicably and inexorably breathing their last.

Finally, the images fade, and your feet are compelled to walk to the north-west. Zaghárog remains, nursing his disappointment, but you are confident that he has given you what you need.'They will meet to the north-west? That is all he has given us?''Where will the Masters meet?' you ask once again, and Zaghárog shows you the nine dying fish, inexplicably and inexorably breathing their last.

Finally, the images fade, and your feet are compelled to walk to the north-west. Zaghárog remains, nursing his disappointment, but you are confident that he has given you what you need.'Of course! How did I not see it? Orodruin stands between the lands the Masters of Mordor have claimed for themselves, and on the slopes of Mount Doom they will feel most secure from the treachery of their rivals!

'That is where the meeting will take place, I am certain of it.'If you are to attend the meeting of the Masters of Mordor in secret, you will need some way to remain hidden: a disguise.Mithrandir is in the First Circle of Minas Tirith, south of the entrance to the city.Talk to Mithrandir in the First Circle of Minas Tirith, south of the entrance to the cityA group of freed Nurnhoth might be found on the north-east side of the Third Circle, in Minas Tirith.Find the group of freed Nurnhoth on the north-east side of the Third CircleHujâ is among a group of freed Nurnhoth on the north-east side of the Third Circle, in Minas Tirith.Talk to Hujâ on the north-east side of the Third Circle, in Minas TirithThe tailor on the south-eastern side of the Third Circle will be able to fashion a disguise of the appropriate size from the Nurnhoth clothes that were given to you by Hujâ.Ayorzén has followed you to the tailor, and is now on the south-eastern side of the Third Circle, seeking to get your attention.Talk to Ayorzén on the south-eastern side of the Third CircleMithrandir is in Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate'It will be easier to gain access to this meeting in the open air than it would be to pass the guards of Seregost, or any of the fortresses of Mordor, but that is not to say it will be a simple matter. You will need a disguise of some kind to get near to any of the Masters without their knowledge, but what sort of raiment will serve that purpose I cannot say.

'Mithrandir may know better than I, ${PLAYER}. Return to him in Minas Tirith and consult the Wizard, and I will continue to monitor Lhaereth's progress in plague-making.''You will need a disguise of some kind to get near to any of the Masters without their knowledge, but what sort of raiment will serve that purpose I cannot say.

'Mithrandir may know better than I, ${PLAYER}. Return to him in Minas Tirith and consult the Wizard, and I will continue to monitor Lhaereth's progress in plague-making.''Yes, ${PLAYER}? What brings you back to the city? Have you learned if Lhaereth is near to achieving her goal?'

You tell Mithrandir that Harthalín is studying the effects of athelas on the samples you collected in Agarnaith, but that you have learned a meeting of the Masters of Mordor will occur on the slope of Orodruin.

'You intend to gain access to this meeting? You might learn much that would be of use, but it will be very dangerous. You will need raiment of disguise if you are to avoid detection. Ah! I know what will serve.

'The Gúrzyul, and indeed many others in Mordor, rely upon their slaves for labour and the many menial tasks that keep the iron wheel of Gorgoroth turning. As a result, obedient slaves often pass beneath notice; I suspect that the Masters of Mordor do not even see them, though they rely upon their presence. If you were to obtain the rags worn by the freed Nurnhoth that were brought to the city, a tailor in Minas Tirith might be able to fashion a suitable disguise for you. I last saw some of the freed Nurnhoth on the north-east side of the Third Circle, trying to become comfortable in their new home.

'I will meet you at Cirith Gorgor when you have arranged for a disguise with the tailor.''The Gúrzyul, and indeed many others in Mordor, rely upon their slaves for labour and the many menial tasks that keep the iron wheel of Gorgoroth turning. As a result, obedient slaves often pass beneath notice; I suspect that the Masters of Mordor do not even see them, though they rely upon their presence. If you were to obtain the rags worn by the freed Nurnhoth that were brought to the city, a tailor in Minas Tirith might be able to fashion a suitable disguise for you.

'I last saw some of the freed Nurnhoth on the north-east side of the Third Circle, trying to become comfortable in their new home.''Yes? What is it?''We are... we are doing the best we can. It is not easy.''Every moment that comes I think we will be brought back. I cannot believe this will last.''The Gúrzyul, and indeed many others in Mordor, rely upon their slaves for labour and the many menial tasks that keep the iron wheel of Gorgoroth turning. As a result, obedient slaves often pass beneath notice; I suspect that the Masters of Mordor do not even see them, though they rely upon their presence. If you were to obtain the rags worn by the freed Nurnhoth that were brought to the city, a tailor in Minas Tirith might be able to fashion a suitable disguise for you.

'I last saw some of the freed Nurnhoth on the north-east side of the Third Circle, trying to become comfortable in their new home.''You are not here to send us back to Mordor? Promise me!'

Hujâ listens to your request, and slowly his look of fear is replaced with caution.

'You do not want to do this, ${PLAYER}. Arâm and Itli were in the service of Ugrukhôr's table, and the tales they have told of his cruelty are beyond belief. If you try to deceive him, you will be found out, and death could not come swiftly enough for you.

'I retained the clothes I wore in servitude, for some part of me believed I would not long be free of it. There was a part of me, too, that feared to dispose of them. Why? The reason must appear foolish to you, but I thought if I no longer had them at hand I would forget the time I spent in servitude, or the family and friends I lost in those years.

'I will give them to you for this reason, ${PLAYER}: that you will use them to help put an end to Ugrukhôr and the others like him, for Arâm and for Itli and for their families and mine. May they be of use to you, and I will try not to feel their loss overmuch. It will not be easy, but it will be worthwhile if this act helps free my people and puts an end to their misery and torment in Mordor.''Use the clothes I gave to you to help put an end to Ugrukhôr and the others like him. May they be of use to you, and I will try not to feel their loss overmuch. It will not be easy, but it will be worth it if this act helps free my people and put an end to their misery and torment in Mordor.''We are doing the best we can. It is not easy.''Every moment that comes I think we will be brought back. I cannot believe this will last.''Bring those clothes to the tailor on the south-eastern side of the Third Circle and he will fashion a disguise of the appropriate size for you.''You wish for me to modify these garments so you may wear them? Are you sure you would not prefer the latest fashions of the city instead? These are little more than dirty rags!

'Very well, I will do what I can. I will send them along to you when they are finished.''As I told you already, I will do what I can. I will send the clothes along to you when they are finished.''You are up to something, ${PLAYER}. I overheard your directions for the tailor, and there can be only one reason for you to wear the clothes of the Nurnhoth. You plan to sneak into one of the fortresses in Mordor! Do not try to deny it. Ayorzén knows whatever you might say would be a lie; he is often called Ayorzén the Perceptive, after all.'

Ayorzén looks from side to side and shifts his weight from one foot to the other. When he speaks again, the words come in a rush and a tumble, as if he were embarrassed to speak them.

'There are none in this city that Ayorzén would consider a friend, but you may be nearest to that lofty prize, ${PLAYER}. If you do not get yourself killed climbing the walls of Seregost, or rattling the bars of Naerband, you might hope to achieve my friendship, in time! But Ayorzén does not befriend corpses.'

Ayorzén speaks more softly now, and his words are earnest in a way you have rarely heard from the Khundolar chief.

'I think you are a fool to attempt this, but I will share something with you in the hope that it may improve your chance of survival. The bosses pay no heed to their servants or their slaves, as long as they are obedient. That will change in an instant if the servant disobeys. You must do whatever you are commanded to do, or they will see through your disguise. This should serve you well with Ugrukhôr, Dulgabêth, or any of their minions.

'But there is one you should beware, in any circumstance. Whatever Urudanî has become, I do not think she will be blinded by your appearance. She may be more sensitive to your deception. You must try and keep away from Urudanî, at any cost.

'I have done all I can do, ${PLAYER}. It is more than anyone else would do, but this city is confusing and unpleasant, and if I am stuck here I would rather have someone such as you to hear my complaints. Keep that in mind when you sojourn on your foolish errand, and let it inspire you to subtlety!''I think you are a fool to attempt this, but I will share something with you in the hope that it may improve your chance of survival. The bosses pay no heed to their servants or their slaves, as long as they are obedient. That will change in an instant if the servant disobeys. You must do whatever you are commanded to do, or they will see through your disguise. This should serve you well with Ugrukhôr, Dulgabêth, or any of their minions.

'But there is one you should beware, in any circumstance. Whatever Urudanî has become, I do not think she will be blinded by your appearance. She may be more sensitive to your deception. You must try and keep away from Urudanî, at any cost.''I bundled the garments for you. You will have them when you need them.''Did you arrange for a disguise? It is nearly time!'The Meeting of the Masters is about to commence, and you have arranged for a disguise that should allow you to attend in secret.Mithrandir is at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir and travel to the meeting of the Masters of MordorMithrandir is at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate'You have returned just in time, for it seems the meeting is soon to commence. Spies have reported large enemy groups moving toward Orodruin from different strongholds in Gorgoroth. They appear to be making for Amon Thaur, a structure on the side of Mount Doom.
 
'Are you prepared? Speak to me when you are ready to leave, but do not take too long! Time is short, and you must not miss this meeting!''Exercise caution, ${PLAYER}. You will go into danger and must remain below their notice or none will be able to save you.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'You learned much of value at Amon Thaur, but it has not eased my worries! Indeed, it has caused them to increase in potency. Where before the Masters of Mordor vied among themselves for control, and it seemed they might waste years on their infighting, now they have set aside many of their differences, at least for the time being.

'This is the worst outcome for Gondor, my friend. Mordor has been united beneath a common purpose. I... I do not know what to do, ${PLAYER}.'

His words hang in the air, unexpected and unwelcome.

'I need to think, my friend. I will call for you when I have decided what can be done. It cannot be long. It must not.

'Lhaereth and her allies will not wait for us to be ready.'Unsure of how best to combat the Masters of Mordor in their unification, Mithrandir turns to you to gather the information that will allow him to form a strategy.Gwedhir is south-west of the ruins of Díngarth, in Dor Amarth.

Mithrandir asked you to find Gwedhir in Dor Amarth and hear his report.Talk to Gwedhir south-west of the ruins of DíngarthHarthalín is west of Faltor Gâm, in Agarnaith.

Mithrandir asked you to find Harthalín in Agarnaith and hear her report.Talk to Harthalín west of Faltor Gâm, in AgarnaithThenca is south of Agarnaith, by the road in Talath Úrui.

Mithrandir asked you to find Thenca in Talath Úrui and hear his report.Talk to Thenca south of Agarnaith, along the road in Talath ÚruiMithrandir is at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate'I have given the situation some thought, but a Mordor united against us does not lend itself to easy solutions. Before the meeting of the Masters, I feared the ascendancy of Dulgabêth, the Mouth of Sauron. Now he is dead and there are others arrayed before us, wielders of powerful weapons and forgers of unexpected alliances. Lhaereth the Stained, Borangos the Horror, Ugrukhôr of the Gúrzyul... these names are to be feared individually, but when they share our destruction as a common purpose we must not fear them... we must fight them.

'Rather let the Masters of Mordor fear us. Are we not united against them? Scouts of many lands and many backgrounds adventure even now in Gorgoroth, and they pursue knowledge that might bring about the undoing of these Masters. Seek first Gwedhir of Gondor near the ruins of Díngarth in Dor Amarth, and then look for Harthalín west of Faltor Gâm, in Agarnaith. On your way back to me, pass through Talath Úrui and consult with Thenca of the Rohirrim. These three reports may help us to form an effective strategy, ${PLAYER}.''Scouts of many lands and many backgrounds adventure even now in Gorgoroth, and seek the knowledge that will bring about the undoing of these Masters. Seek first Gwedhir in Dor Amarth, and then look for Harthalín in Agarnaith. On your way back to me, pass through Talath Úrui and consult with Thenca. These three reports will help us to form an effective strategy, ${PLAYER}.''${PLAYER}! I did not expect to see you. Has this sudden movement of troops drawn your attention too? It has not? Then allow me to tell you what I have seen, and perhaps you will know what has caused it.

'Orcs and evil men have been stirred into sudden wakefulness by some directive that is unknown to me. Small groups of these foes have travelled across Udûn and Dor Amarth since I came to this inhospitable place, but never in such numbers. I believe they received urgent orders from one of their leaders, and it has compelled them to depart the Doomfold with some haste. But I cannot say where they have gone, for I do not know it.

'But this I do know: they go where they can do the most damage to Gondor or her allies. How can it be otherwise? Other scouts may know more. Go to them with speed to match that of our foes!''Orcs and evil men have been stirred into sudden wakefulness by some directive that is unknown to me. Small groups of these foes have travelled across Udûn and Dor Amarth since I came to this inhospitable place, but never in such numbers. I believe they received urgent orders from one of their leaders, and it has compelled them to depart the Doomfold with some haste.

'Perhaps other scouts will know more. Go to them with speed to match that of our foes!''Scouts of many lands and many backgrounds adventure even now in Gorgoroth, and seek the knowledge that will bring about the undoing of these Masters. Seek first Gwedhir in Dor Amarth, and then look for Harthalín in Agarnaith. On your way back to me, pass through Talath Úrui and consult with Thenca. These three reports will help us to form an effective strategy, ${PLAYER}.''I am pleased to see you, ${PLAYER}, for I encountered something peculiar and wanted to hear your thoughts! Not far from here I tracked a company of Ugrukhôr's Orcs through the swamp, intending to surprise them at the right moment and make them corpses. Before such a moment presented itself, I was robbed of my prey. Another party fell upon the Orcs and rended them all to pieces!

'The ambushers were merrevail, ${PLAYER}. They swooped down from the height and left no Orc alive. But I thought Ugrukhôr and Lhaereth were allies? Why are their minions slaying each other?'

Harthalín smiles grimly.

'I do not complain. But there is something strange in this, and if we learn what has them at odds we might turn it to our advantage.''Why are the minions of Ugrukhôr and Lhaereth fighting among themselves if their masters are united? There is something strange in this, and if we learn what has them at odds we might turn it to our advantage.''Scouts of many lands and many backgrounds adventure even now in Gorgoroth, and seek the knowledge that will bring about the undoing of these Masters. Seek first Gwedhir in Dor Amarth, and then look for Harthalín in Agarnaith. On your way back to me, pass through Talath Úrui and consult with Thenca. These three reports will help us to form an effective strategy, ${PLAYER}.''Ah, I am all right now, do not trouble yourself. I engaged in battle with a party of Orcs, but the fighting went against me and I sustained this injury. I thought my days were done, but the creatures did not stay to ensure it. Their leaders called them away before they made sure I would not recover. To think I would have names such as Plákash and Durkhlát to thank for my life, but that is now so!

'The Orcs were looking for someone. They spoke of "the Weeping Warrior" as they departed. Their language is harsh and guttural; I could not make sense of much of their talk, but I think Ugrukhôr ordered them to find this warrior at any cost. They split into two groups as they left me, and Plákash and Durkhlát planned to return to Durthang and Cirith Ungol to regroup.

'Do not worry about me, ${PLAYER}. I am mostly recovered from my injury. I give thanks to no Orc, but still I am grateful that Ugrukhôr's minions feared his commands enough to leave me carelessly behind, still alive!''The Orcs were looking for someone. They spoke of "the Weeping Warrior" as they departed. Their language is harsh and guttural; I could not make sense of much of their talk, but I think Ugrukhôr ordered them to find this warrior at any cost. They split into two groups as they left me, and Plákash and Durkhlát planned to return to Durthang and Cirith Ungol to regroup.''This is why I like you, ${PLAYER}. Before it seemed the forces arranged against us had the upper hand, but now you return with information we can use to oppose our enemies. What has caused this rift between Ugrukhôr and Lhaereth, and for what fell purpose do Ugrukhôr's Orcs seek the Weeping Warrior?'You have uncovered a course of investigation that Mithrandir hopes will lead to some means of fighting the Masters of Mordor: Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, is sought by Ugrukhôr for some unknown purpose.Plákash can be found somewhere within Durthang. Durkhlát can be found outside Cirith Ungol.

Mithrandir asked you to find one of the Orcs that seek the Weeping Warrior and compel him to divulge the purpose for his search.Find Plákash in DurthangFind Durkhlát outside Cirith UngolMithrandir is at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black Gate.Talk to Mithrandir at Cirith Gorgor, just inside the Black GateLady Galadriel and Lord Celeborn have come with the Golden Host to Dol Guldur, in preparation for the fortress's destruction. You will find them outside the fortress of Thangúlhad in the Gathbúrz area of Southern Mirkwood.Find Galadriel and Celeborn outside Thangúlhad in the Gathbúrz area of Southern MirkwoodLady Galadriel and Lord Celeborn have come with the Golden Host to Dol Guldur, in preparation for the fortress's destruction. You will find them outside the fortress of Thangúlhad in the Gathbúrz area of Southern Mirkwood.Talk to Lord Celeborn outside ThangúlhadLady Galadriel and Lord Celeborn have come with the Golden Host to Dol Guldur, in preparation for the fortress's destruction. You will find them outside the fortress of Thangúlhad in the Gathbúrz area of Southern Mirkwood.Talk to Lady Galadriel outside Thangúlhad'Yes, I know of the one called Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior. He is numbered among the Gúrzyul and has crossed my path before, though not for many years. I have known him to be active primarily in the East and in the North, but in times of great strife I have heard reports that he was seen further afield and in unexpected places. I find it curious that none have seen him in Gorgoroth of late, and you did not see him at the meeting of the Masters. He is missing, and his absence troubles me. Why did he not come to Gorgoroth when Sauron had need of him, as did others among the Gúrzyul?

'Why does Ugrukhôr seek him now, and for what purpose? Find one of the leaders of whom Thenca spoke, either Plákash or Durkhlát, and you may learn why they search for Karazgar. Thenca believed the Orcs made for Durthang or Cirith Ungol. Go to one of these towers and compel the Orc to divulge his orders.''Why does Ugrukhôr seek the Weeping Warrior now, and for what purpose? Find one of the leaders of whom Thenca spoke, either Plákash or Durkhlát, and you may learn why they search for Karazgar. Thenca believed the Orcs made for Durthang or Cirith Ungol. Go to one of these towers and compel the Orc to divulge his orders.''Ha! What did you hope to gain by coming here? A quick death? Plákash will be happy to give it to you, as he has given death to so many other fools who dared the fortress of Durthang. Speak your last words, and I will spill your guts and inspect them for both fearlessness and foolishness!'

You stand your ground and demand to know his business with the Weeping Warrior. Plákash bares his teeth and eyes you warily.

'Karazgar, eh? Why should I tell you anything, brazen ${RACE}? Ugrukhôr wants him. Weeping Warrior knows something about this artifact Bugdatish, and the Captain of the Pit is going to make him talk. My boys found out where he went: hill of sorcery in the old woods, where the Big Bosses used to tramp. My boys have already set out, and I'll follow them next, but first I'll take care of you! I'll open you up and see what makes you bold enough to ask questions of Plákash!''You made a mistake coming here. Now you will pay the price of it! Speak your last words, and may your death serve as a lesson to others of your kind not to tread too near to Cirith Ungol, or to cross paths with Durkhlát!'

You stand your ground and demand to know his business with the Weeping Warrior. Durkhlát bares his teeth and eyes you warily.

'Karazgar, eh? Why should I tell you anything? Ugrukhôr wants him. Weeping Warrior knows something about the artifact Bugdatish, and the Captain of the Pit is going to make him talk. My boys know where he went: hill of sorcery in the old woods, where the Big Bosses used to tramp. I'll go there next, but first I'll deal with you! Ready yourself for death, foolish ${RACE}!''Did you learn why Ugrukhôr seeks the Weeping Warrior, or where the Orcs have gone in search of him? I see from your expression that you learned something of use, but perhaps not the entirety of what we sought?'

You recount what you were able to learn, and Mithrandir briefly considers the Orc's words.

'It is better than I feared, ${PLAYER}. There are still gaps in our knowledge, but the Orc has given us more than I expected. It appears that Ugrukhôr expects to find Karazgar at the "hill of sorcery," a phrase used in relation to the fortress of the Necromancer. Yes, I speak of Dol Guldur. That must be where Karazgar has gone, and it would appear he searches for an artifact, something called Bugdatish. I have never before heard this name, but that means little. There is room among the dark arts of Sauron for such mysteries.

'If you are to search for Karazgar at Dol Guldur, you will need to make haste! The Lord and Lady of Lórien have entered Mirkwood and prepare to raze the fortress. The final mission of the Golden Host is underway, and if you are to find the Weeping Warrior and get some answers about this relic he seeks you must leave at once! Do not stand around gawking at me, ${PLAYER}. Go, and quickly!''If you are to search for Karazgar at Dol Guldur, you will need to make haste! The Lord and Lady of Lórien have entered Mirkwood and prepare to raze the fortress. The final mission of the Golden Host is underway, and if you are to find the Weeping Warrior and get some answers about this relic he seeks you must leave at once! Do not stand around gawking at me, ${PLAYER}. Go, and quickly! You will find them outside the fortress of Thangúlhad in the Gathbúrz area of Southern Mirkwood.''I am glad you have returned to us, ${PLAYER}. The brave advance of the Malledhrim into the forest has borne fruit, our siege proven successful. With the defeat of Sauron, the time has come to cleanse the lands that felt his foul touch. It is not enough to besiege Mirkwood and the fortress that stands atop this hill of sorcery. We must scour this place of his mark and his memory.

'This was the fortress of the Necromancer, the guise Sauron wore when he sought to recover his strength and regain the advantage while our sight was veiled. It was the mission of the Golden Host to seize the fortress, but that mission has changed. The fortress must now be destroyed.'

You tell Celeborn you have reason to believe Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, might seek a powerful artifact somewhere within Dol Guldur. He smiles grimly at you.

'He must seek it quickly, or this artifact will be forever buried and beyond his reach. So too might he, if he ignores the fury of the Elves. It comes now to the fortress and will not abate.''My patience is at an end, ${PLAYER}. For too long has this den of evil festered beneath the trees. Not content to remain behind the walls of the fortress, that evil spread throughout the forest. Mirkwood was made of it, as should never have been. If the forest is to be renewed and its beauty restored, the fortress of the Necromancer must be razed to its very foundation and its remaining corruptions brought into the light. There they will wither and die, their malice slain and their potential for evil undone.'The Lord and Lady of Lórien have joined the Golden Host for the final destruction of Dol Guldur.Elves of the Golden Host can be found within the walls of Dol Guldur.Find Elves of the Golden Host within Dol GuldurElves of the Golden Host can be found within the walls of Dol Guldur.Talk to Gladhel within Dol GuldurElves of the Golden Host can be found within the walls of Dol Guldur.Find Elves of the Golden Host deeper within Dol GuldurElves of the Golden Host can be found within the walls of Dol Guldur.Talk to Bánoth within Dol GuldurBánoth asked you to investigate the secret stairway his warriors discovered down the slope, behind the door on the left.Explore the room of shadow at the bottom of the secret stairSigileth is in her hideout deep beneath Dol Guldur.Talk to Sigileth in her hideout'I can bring down the walls of the fortress, but I will not endanger those of my people who remain within. I ask you to climb the hill and recall those of the Golden Host who remain. Tell them to leave the fortress, for soon it will no longer darken the hill upon which it stands. Their vigil is no longer needed.'

Galadriel pierces you with her bright eyes.

'Bring them all from the fortress, ${PLAYER}. All of them. Leave no one behind.''I can bring down the walls of the fortress, but I will not endanger those of my people who remain within. I ask you to climb the hill and recall those of the Golden Host who remain. Tell them to leave the fortress. Leave no one behind.''Mae govannen, friend!''The Lady has come to Dol Guldur? Then it is true. The mission of the Golden Host nears its end! These are happy tidings indeed!

'We will make our way from the fortress, as you ask. There is another sentry post ahead where our brothers and sisters stand vigil against new incursions of the enemy. Go to them and deliver your message, and we will see you again at the base of the hill!''There is another sentry post ahead where our brothers and sisters stand vigil against new incursions of the enemy. Go to them and deliver the message you brought to us, and we will see you again at the base of the hill!''${PLAYER}! I hear your message and will lead my warriors down the hill and to safety, but I wonder if you will first do something for me. This morning we found a covered passage leading to a hidden stair. We sought to explore its depths, but my warriors encountered powerful shadow and turned back.

'But you wield the Light of Eärendil, and its luminance might help you keep your spirits aloft even in the deepest darkness. Will you investigate the hidden stair and the room of shadow? Only a work of great evil could have left such a powerful mark upon this secret wing of the fortress. The Lady will soon raze Dol Guldur, but I do not want to leave this mystery unexamined. Seek out its secret and return. I will ask the Lady to delay until you have emerged safely from the dungeons!

'The secret stairway is down the slope and beyond the raised portcullis. Look to the left after you pass through, and you will see the door that leads to the secret stair.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Using the door to the secret stair will begin a Solo quest that cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>This must be the entrance of which Bánoth spoke.

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Seek out the secret of the shadowy wing and then return. I will ask the Lady to delay until you have emerged safely from the dungeons! The secret stairway is down the slope and beyond the raised portcullis. Look to the left after you pass through, and you will see the door that leads to the secret stair.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Using the door to the secret stair will begin a Solo quest that cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'This place must be the Sorcerers' Stores, but I made it my own. From here I launched my own attacks against the forces of the Dark Lord that tried to hide from the Golden Host. They could not hide from me, not even in this isolated and secret dungeon. I stalked my foes in their own domicile, bringing death to them on stealthy feet. I struck swiftly and faded again into the darkness, and kept a tally of my slain foes. Did I once belong to the Hidden Guard? Now have I truly become the Hidden Guard, ${PLAYER}. All the others are gone, but I remained behind, and I never stopped fighting.'

Sigileth laughs, but there is little amusement in it. You tell her that everyone thought she died in the Battle of the Tower. At this she does smile.

'On the stair my wounds were grievous, the pain severe enough that I dropped my knives: beloved Lanchigil and keen-edged Egnassigil. I slipped from the landing and my body fell into the depths. When I awoke, I found myself in this secret wing, cut off from the Golden Host. I sought to return to the surface, but the intense shadow of the Room of Voices prevented me from passing beyond.

'You say everyone believed I was dead? Not everyone, ${PLAYER}. I heard the voice of Lady Galadriel in my mind, and she said it was time for me to leave the fortress. I braved the Room of Voices one last time... and that is when I found you.'You have found Sigileth in a secret wing beneath Dol Guldur, and she wishes to help you complete your mission before the fortress is destroyed.Servants of Sauron and evil relics can be found throughout the halls and chambers of Gostador.Defeat Orcs within Gostador (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect hoarded relics within Gostador (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect runed tomes within Gostador (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigileth is in her hideout at the Sorcerer's Stores, in Gostador.'I have never seen such deep shadow as that which blankets the Room of Voices, and the fell whispers that still lurk there confound the mind and caused even my heart to quail. Some work of great evil must have been conducted there, and the memory of it has scarred that chamber.'

You tell Sigileth your belief that the spirit of Sauron spent the years following his defeat by the Last Alliance recovering in that chamber beneath the fortress.

'That explains the shroud that hangs upon it. Now he has fully regained his strength, his shadow will continue to grow, and fester.'

You explain, briefly, that Sauron has been defeated, and you came to Dol Guldur in search of the Bugdatish, an artifact prized by his Gúrzyul. Sigileth stares at you with wonder.

'Gurth dan i ngúdrim! I am filled with amazement, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps the end of the Dark Lord will serve to lessen the shadow that lies upon the Room of Voices, rather than increase it! It is clear you have many tales to tell since battle drew us apart, but those can wait until we escape from the fortress. I am unfamiliar with the name Gúrzyul, or with this Bugdatish, but many are the relics that lie within Gostador. Let us search for these, and lay waste to any of Sauron's remaining servants. Then we will escape together.''I am unfamiliar with the name Gúrzyul, or with this Bugdatish, but many are the relics that lie within Gostador. Let us search for these, and lay waste to any of Sauron's remaining servants. Then we will escape together.''We have gathered a number of relics and tomes, but it will be up to others to examine them. Is one of these the artifact you seek, the Bugdatish? We will not find out here.'Galadriel has laid waste to the fortress of Dol Guldur, bringing an end to its capacity to nurture the evils of Sauron.Galadriel has cast down the walls of Dol Guldur and laid bare its pits, and now she wishes to speak with you.Talk to Galadriel on Dol Dhannen, the Hill of RuinSigileth is on Dol Dhannen, the Hill of Ruin.Talk to Sigileth on Dol Dhannen, the Hill of RuinThranduil the Elvenking has come to Dol Dhannen, the Hill of Ruin.Talk to Thranduil on Dol Dhannen, the Hill of Ruin'The weeks I spent as the Hidden Guard within Gostador, levying vengeance upon the Orcs, are not pleasant to recall and neither shall I miss them. But I am proud to have done my Lady's work. Let us escape now from the fortress, and she will fulfill her own obligation and raze this place to its foundation!

'With you by my side, ${PLAYER}, even the whispers in the Room of Voices cannot stay my advance. Onward, brave ${RACE}! Our mission is fulfilled!''With you by my side, ${PLAYER}, even the whispers in the Room of Voices cannot stay my advance. Onward, brave ${RACE}! Our mission is fulfilled!''At last the fortress has been cast down. Its dark walls can no longer hide the works of Sauron. Rejoice with the Golden Host, ${PLAYER}, for a time of trouble and woe has come to its ending, and you played your part in it with grace and with skill.

'I thank you too for helping to lead Sigileth from the depths of Dol Guldur. I was not aware she still lived before we came to this hill of sorcery, but as we approached the wall I sensed her in the darkness. I could not have left her there, ${PLAYER}, though Sauron himself survive within. I thank you for bringing her forth. Many of my people died in service of the Golden Host, and I mourn for every one.

'But Dol Guldur stands no more, and that is a testament to their sacrifice.''I thank you for helping to lead Sigileth from the depths of Dol Guldur. I was not aware she still lived before we came to this hill of sorcery, but as we approached the wall I sensed her in the darkness. I could not have left her there, ${PLAYER}, though Sauron himself survive within. I thank you for bringing her forth. Many of my people died in service of the Golden Host, and I mourn for every one.'The Golden Host achieved its aim, and so too did the Hidden Guard. I am proud of our accomplishments, ${PLAYER}. My brother Corudan will be pleased to hear of them, if he is home in Lórien and not away, walking the river as is his wont.'

You tell Sigileth that you journeyed with Corudan for a time in Rohan, but he vanished while scouting outside Helm's Deep and you do not know his fate.

'My brother was always given to journey, but he was fond of the Anduin and did not stray far from its banks. Rohan? It seems there is another tale for you to tell, but it must wait. You thought me dead, ${PLAYER}, but I was not. So too might it be with Corudan. I will return to the Golden Wood for a brief time, but not to mourn.

'I go there to prepare. I will call for you when I am ready, and we will find my brother. We will find Corudan River-walker, and compare our tales of adventure.''You thought me dead, ${PLAYER}, but I was not. So too might it be with Corudan. I will return to the Golden Wood for a brief time, but not to mourn.

'I go there to prepare. I will call for you when I am ready, and we will find my brother. We will find Corudan River-walker and compare our tales of adventure.''We are well-met on this hill of ruin, ${PLAYER}. My realm in the north of the wood came under threat, but despite fire and battle I seized the victory. I came south then, and beneath the trees I met Celeborn of Lórien in friendship and in peace. He agrees, as do I, that the time of the Mirkwood is over. Together we call it by a new name, Eryn Lasgalen, and may it bear that name ever onward. From this day forth it will be the Wood of the Greenleaves, and we will work to cleanse the mark of Sauron from every corner, and heal the hurts he worked upon it by his presence.'

Thranduil surveys the ruin of the fortress, but when he turns his gaze upon you, he speaks more warmly than you expected.

'I understand you befriended my son Legolas, ${PLAYER}. In token of that friendship, I open my land and my halls to you. Tell me what you seek, and I will do what I can to help you achieve it.'Thranduil the Elvenking has invited you to visit his halls in the northern reaches of Eryn Lasgalen, where he believes you may find news of Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior.Felegoth, the Halls of the Elvenking, can be found in the north-eastern woods of Eryn Lasgalen.

The steed near King Thranduil outside the ruins of Dol Guldur can take you to Eryn Lasgalen with utmost speed.Journey to the entrance of Felegoth in the north-eastern reaches of Eryn LasgalenYou have come to Felegoth, the Halls of the Elvenking, and should make yourself known to Limlang, the guard at the entrance.Talk to Limlang at the entrance to FelegothKing Thranduil is at his throne in the north-eastern chamber of Felegoth.Talk to King Thranduil at his throne in FelegothLegolas has come to his father's throne room in Felegoth.Talk to Legolas in the throne room of FelegothLegolas invited you to visit the dining chamber, the Room of Study, and the water-way bridge in Felegoth, and to speak to the Elves you find at those locations.Talk to Gondil in the Room of Study, south-east of the throne roomTalk to Herthír in the dining chamber east of the throne roomTalk to Itheron on the river-bridge, east of the throne roomLegolas is in his father's throne room, in Felegoth.Talk to Legolas in the throne room of Felegoth'You say the Orcs sought Karazgar at Dol Guldur? Yes, the Weeping Warrior is known to me, for he has troubled my people for many years. He is a dragon-tamer, sly and cruel. I long suspected he had some part in rousing Smaug the Golden from his lair in the Ered Mithrin, but lacked the proof of it. Smaug is long dead, but Karazgar remains, a constant thorn in the side of my people and others in the North. I would be glad to see him slain, but he is elusive.

'Neither have I heard of this artifact Bugdatish. A number of relics from Dol Guldur will be brought back to Felegoth for cleansing and for study. One of these may prove the artifact you seek. You are welcome to journey north with us to Felegoth. There is a horse here on the Hill of Ruin that will bear you swiftly to Eryn Lasgalen. When you arrive at Tham Taerdol in the Green Mountains, travel north-east through the trees. You will find my halls in the caverns beneath the hill of Cerin Dorn. My son also journeys home and will be pleased to see you there.''A number of relics from Dol Guldur will be brought back to Felegoth for cleansing and for study. One of these may prove the artifact you seek. You are welcome to journey north with us to Felegoth. There is a horse here on the Hill of Ruin that will bear you swiftly to Eryn Lasgalen. When you arrive at Tham Taerdol in the Green Mountains, travel north-east through the trees. You will find my halls in the caverns beneath the hill of Cerin Dorn. My son also journeys home and will be pleased to see you there.''I have been told to expect you. Welcome to the Halls of the Elvenking.''You are welcome in Felegoth, ${PLAYER}. The king himself told us to expect you and to hasten you to him when you arrived.

'There will be time to explore the halls of Felegoth at your leisure! For now, find King Thranduil at his throne in the north-eastern chamber. And be welcome!''There will be time to explore the halls of Felegoth at your leisure! For now, find King Thranduil at his throne in the north-east chamber.''I welcome you to Felegoth, ${PLAYER}, and to the greater wood of Eryn Lasgalen. Enjoy the comforts of my halls and the conversation of my people. My scholars study the artifacts from Dol Guldur, and if any sign of this Bugdatish is found, you will be called.'

You express your concern about keeping artifacts from Dol Guldur within his halls, and Thranduil nods.

'You are wise to be wary, but my people have much experience facing the corruption of Sauron. Did they not resist its influence for the many years of darkness that gave this forest the name Mirkwood? They will be vigilant now and will learn what they safely may of these artifacts. That done, any that cannot be cured and retained will be destroyed. Worry not on that score.''Enjoy the comforts of my halls and the conversation of my people. My scholars study the artifacts from Dol Guldur, and if any sign of this Bugdatish is found, you will be called. My people have much experience facing the corruption of Sauron. They will be vigilant now, and will learn what they safely may of these artifacts. That done, any that cannot be cured and retained will be destroyed. You need not worry on that score.''I am delighted to see you in my father's halls, ${PLAYER}! I think Gimli would appreciate them as well, if he could put aside the grudges of his sire, who spent some time imprisoned in the dungeons here. Yes, I have heard the story! Glóin and the others of his company are fortunate it was not my duty to guard their cells, or they might be there still! Much mischief might have followed then, and it seems likely Smaug would still trouble the North, so let us be glad the watch on the dwarves was not a princely duty!

'In truth, I have not spent much time at Felegoth, not since I was young. These caves are home, and they remind my father of his own younger days in service to his liege Thingol, but I prefer wooded glens and sunlight. My father seldom emerges from his halls, save to hunt and feast in the nearby woods. Instead he sends me to accomplish his desires in the wider world, and I relish those errands. I would not have attended the Council of Elrond, were it not so, but that is a tale you know!

'I invite you to explore Felegoth while you are here, ${PLAYER}! Visit the dining chamber, the Room of Study, and the water-way bridge, and speak to a number of the Elves you encounter. You will find them pleasant and welcoming, no matter what Gimli and his sire might have led you to believe!''I invite you to explore Felegoth while you are here, ${PLAYER}! Visit the dining chamber, the Room of Study, and the water-way bridge, and speak to a number of the Elves you encounter. You will find them pleasant and welcoming, no matter what Gimli and his father might have led you to believe!''Our scholars study the artifacts brought from Dol Guldur, hoping to cleanse them of the evil that blankets them. I hope they prove able, for that was once our home.

'It is true. I speak not of the fortress! No, the fortress of Dol Guldur was wholly constructed by servants of the Enemy. But it was built upon the bones of the Elf-hall that once stood atop the bald hill, abandoned at the advice of Curunír the White. He believed the darkening of Mirkwood could not be slowed, and King Thranduil heeded his words and led us north beyond Agordor, the Narrows of the Forest.

'We settled at a rocky spire in the mountains of Emyn Laeg, overlooking the source of the river Lúthond. Thranduil named the hill Caras Tilion, and the hall stands still, but it stands empty. The darkness of Mirkwood spread, and our retreat from the southern reaches of the wood was not to be the last such retreat.

'I do not wish to speak of it, I am sorry. My happiest days were spent in Caras Tilion, and speaking of it now brings the pain of their loss anew.''When the Last Alliance marched through Rhovanion on its way to Mordor, none were turned away who swore to march against the Dark Lord. Men of the Long Lake, dwarves of Khazad-dûm, and Elves of the divided Greenwood, west and east, stood side by side.

'I fought with my brothers and sisters at Dagorlad, but more than half of the wood-elves did not return from the war. Lost too were our kings. Thranduil ascended to the throne, and though he is not a wood-elf, he never forgot the sacrifice of my people. He led us wisely and well during the years since the Battle of Dagorlad. He is the Elvenking, and I am proud to serve him.

'I know he will one day depart for the Undying Lands with the rest of his kin, and leave this land to be ruled by wood-elves. I will celebrate his rule when that day comes and speak much praise for Thranduil Swiftstream! That day is soon approaching.''These caverns were carefully shaped over hundreds of years, ${PLAYER}, and now their beauty is a comfort to me. So too is it a reminder that even amid darkness, Wood-elves and Grey-elves working together can achieve works of grace and splendour.

'So too can it be between Elves and dwarves? There has been trade between our peoples, to be certain, but that is a history troubled and long. There may now be friendship and peace between Elves of the Wood and dwarves of the Mountain, but it was not always so.

'In Erebor, the Lonely Mountain of the dwarves, their miners unearthed a shining white jewel of unsurpassed brilliance. King Thranduil believed it must be nothing less than one of the lost Silmarils, and demanded to see it, but Thráin of the dwarves refused. Thranduil shut the gates of Felegoth to the dwarves, and only the council of Mithrandir and his assurance that the white jewel could not be a Silmaril repaired those broken bonds of friendship. They were later strained again, but of the Necklace of Girion it is not my place to speak! Ask the prince Legolas of it if you must, but I will say naught of it now.''How did you enjoy your exploration of my father's halls, ${PLAYER}?'

You share your experience of Felegoth and ask Legolas about the Necklace of Girion, mentioned by Itheron. He stares at you in silence.

'That is a subject about which my father prefers not to speak, and I will respect his wishes. I will say only this: the Gweriamir was crafted by dwarves and gifted to the bride of Thingol, my father's liege. When he was murdered, she cast it away in disgust and it was lost for thousands of years. It came in the end to the dwarves of Erebor and seeing it there enflamed my father's anger, as you surely can understand, ${PLAYER}. When Smaug came, my father turned the dwarves away and they became a people in exile.

'I am not proud to recount this to you. The matter was resolved, in time, and I do not wish to dwell upon it. There are dangers and troubles enough to face for now, without dwelling upon old injuries and half-forgotten insults.'Legolas is eager to help you track down the Orc spies that have been seen creeping throughout Eryn Lasgalen and to learn from them what evils they might be planning.Thranduil's scouts Gardelen and Dínellas patrol the wood south-east of Torech Emel.Foes of the Elves have been stirred up by the passage of Orcs through Eryn Lasgalen, and Gardelen has asked you to defeat them if you can.Defeat foes in Eryn Lasgalen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dínellas followed a band of Orc-spies through Eryn Lasgalen. Gardelen believed the Orcs were bound for Caras Tilion, and you should seek the hunter Dínellas by the old Elf-hall there.The Orc-spy slain by Dínellas can be found by his position near Caras Tilion.Search the Orc-spy slain by DínellasDínellas is near the ruins of the Elf-hall at Caras Tilion.Legolas is near the ruins of the Elf-hall at Caras Tilion.Legolas asked you to defeat foes within his old home of Caras Tilion while he considers the meaning of the letter you discovered.Defeat foes within Caras Tilion (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Legolas is near the ruins of the Elf-hall at Caras Tilion.Talk to Legolas near Caras TilionThranduil is in his throne room in Felegoth.Talk to Thranduil in his throne room in Felegoth'Let us instead look to the dangers that await my people in Eryn Lasgalen today! It is mere hours since I returned, and already I have heard tell of Orc-spies in the forest. We should seek the scouts my father dispatched, and see what they have learned of these spies.

'Gardelen and Dínellas patrol the wood south-east of Torech Emel. Let us go to them and hear their report!''I have heard tell of Orc-spies in the forest. We should seek the scouts my father dispatched, and see what they have learned of these spies. Gardelen and Dínellas patrol the wood south-east of Torech Emel. Let us go to them and hear their report!''Gardelen is a good friend and a fine tracker. I have known him since he was young.''Any friend of the prince is friend to me also, ${PLAYER}. I bid you greetings and courtesy, and safety in the wood!

'There are Orc-spies beneath the canopy of the wood, and they have stirred other creatures to wakefulness with the tramp of their filthy boots. I do not know what has brought them hither, but any errand of theirs is certain to oppose the will of Thranduil.

'Dínellas went in search of them, in the direction of Caras Tilion. Before you follow him, will you slay some of the foes the Orcs stirred in their wake?''Orc-spies have stirred other creatures to wakefulness with the tramp of their filthy boots. Dínellas went in search of them, in the direction of Caras Tilion. Before you follow him, will you slay some of the foes the Orcs stirred in their wake?''Gladly will we slay the beasts stirred to anger by the passage of the Orcs, Gardelen!''Dínellas is a skilled hunter. Indeed, I learned much of the craft from his patient lessons!''It is my pleasure to meet you, ${PLAYER}. Tell me how the prince has fared on your journey beneath the trees, if you can spare the tale. Long ago, at the king's request, I taught Legolas much of his woodcraft. I hope he has lived up to my high expectation!'

Dínellas laughs, and although he is a new acquaintance you can tell his laughter comes easily and often.

'But there is no time for such friendly stories. You have arrived too late to aid me with the Orc-spies! I slew one of them already, and the rest fled into the trees. If you wish to see my handiwork, he lies right over there.''I slew one of the Orc-spies already, and the rest fled into the trees. If you wish to see my handiwork, he lies right over there.''Search the Orc slain by Dínellas, and see if he carries anything to shed light on his purpose here, ${PLAYER}.''The Orc carried a letter from its captain? Show it to the prince, for it may contain some information we can use against them!''This letter is very interesting, ${PLAYER}, but I do not understand what has given rise to it. Are the Gúrzyul of whom Mithrandir speaks not united? This letter, at least, speaks clearly to me: Ugrukhôr and Karazgar are at odds, and the Captain of the Pit seeks knowledge only possessed by the Weeping Warrior. Perhaps they will draw each other's blood and save us the effort!

'I know that is unlikely. If you will enter Caras Tilion and defeat the foes that lurk within, I will set my mind to considering our next steps. I once called Caras Tilion home, and seeing enemies within brings sadness to my heart, though it has been many years since any of my people lived within its walls.''If you will enter Caras Tilion and defeat the foes that lurk within, I will set my mind to considering our next steps. I once called Caras Tilion home, and seeing enemies within brings sadness to my heart, though it has been many years since any of my people lived within its walls.''I have given some more thought to the words of Ugrukhôr, but every avenue upon which my mind travels leads to no answers. This artifact called Bugdatish is not known to me. Indeed, beyond its name I know nothing of it. It seems I share that trait with the Captain of the Pit! Can you not hear the desperation underlying his threats to Karazgar? Whatever this artifact may be, he fiercely desires word of it. Very curious.

'We should return to my father and give him this news. He will be most interested in that which distresses our foes!''Legolas told me you adventured into Caras Tilion and beheld the ruin it has become. I am glad of your efforts within. Did you learn aught of the artifact Bugdatish or the Weeping Warrior?'Ugrukhôr seeks knowledge of the artifact Bugdatish from Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, and Mithrandir has come to Felegoth seeking the same.Mithrandir is meeting with the scholars of Felegoth in the Room of Study, south-east of the throne room.Find Mithrandir and the scholars of Felegoth in the Room of Study, south-east of the throne roomMithrandir is meeting with the scholars of Felegoth in the Room of Study, south-east of the throne room.Talk to Mithrandir in the Room of Study, south-east of the throne roomMithrandir asked you to inquire of Thranduil if the Bugdatish might have been part of Smaug's treasure-hoard.Return to Thranduil in the throne room of FelegothThranduil is in his throne room in Felegoth.Talk to Thranduil in his throne room in FelegothMithrandir is in the throne room of Felegoth.Talk to Mithrandir in the throne room of FelegothLoeglond, the settlement of the Raft-elves, is in the Dale-lands along the river.

Mithrandir suggested that you visit the Raft-elves on your way to Erebor, seeking news of Karazgar and the Bugdatish.Travel to Loeglond and seek tidings of Karazgar or the BugdatishLoeglond, the settlement of the Raft-elves, is in the Dale-lands along the river.

Mithrandir suggested that you visit the Raft-elves on your way to Erebor, seeking news of Karazgar and the Bugdatish.Talk to Nembadeth at Loeglond'We may have an advantage over Ugrukhôr of the Gúrzyul, ${PLAYER}, for the artifact Bugdatish might have been among those taken by my warriors when we departed Dol Guldur. The scholars of my realm are nearly finished with their study of these treasures. The Wizard Mithrandir has come to Felegoth and speaks with them even now.

'Go to him in the Room of Study south-east of the throne room and learn if any of the artifacts could be the one prized by Ugrukhôr, or if any possess a connexion of some kind to Karazgar.''Go to Mithrandir in the Room of Study and learn if any of the artifacts could be the one prized by Ugrukhôr, or if any possess a connexion of some kind to Karazgar.''I grew tired of waiting for news from you, ${PLAYER}, and prevailed upon Gwaihir to bring me hither! I spoke at once with Thranduil. His scholars have been sorting through the artifacts rescued from Dol Guldur, but they say they have not found what I seek. There is no wonder in that! How can they hope to find this artifact, this Bugdatish, while they know nothing of it? "Such things do not come labelled for easy study," they say to me. "Pah!" I said to them, and now I say it to you, ${PLAYER}! Pah!

'We may not know what form the Bugdatish might take, but if the Gúrzyul desire it so badly, I believe the thing will be enwreathed with a powerful aura. But these scholars insist they have not found such a relic in the small hoard of artifacts taken from Dol Guldur.'

The Wizard pauses.

'Hoard, did I say? That is not the only such hoard known in this part of Middle-earth. Smaug himself assembled his own hoard of treasure, when he still lived, and when he died it was divided into shares among dwarves and Men, and some came even to Thranduil's Elves. Might the Bugdatish have been a part of that treasure-hoard? Ask the Elvenking of this possibility, ${PLAYER}.''The small hoard of relics taken from Dol Guldur is not the only such hoard that has come to this part of Middle-earth. Smaug himself assembled his own hoard of treasure, when he still lived, and when he died some part of it was divided into shares between dwarves and Men, and some came even to Thranduil's Elves. Might the Bugdatish have been a part of that treasure-hoard? Ask the Elvenking of this possibility, ${PLAYER}.''Mithrandir has sent you back to me so quickly? Do the artifacts from Dol Guldur hold no answers to his questions, ${PLAYER}?'

You offer Thranduil the Wizard's suggestion that the artifact Bugdatish might have been a part of Smaug's treasure-hoard, unremarked-upon and unnoticed.

'I do not like to speak of that hoard, ${PLAYER}, as Mithrandir knows. The dragon-sickness that lay upon it caused much trouble in the hearts of dwarves; indeed, even Men and Elves became embroiled in the fighting over it. The hoard and its treasures were divided among the victors following the death of Smaug; they were many and valuable, and included the Arkenstone and the Necklace of Girion. If the artifact Bugdatish had been contained within, it surely would have been accounted in the reckoning of shares.

'The Arkenstone lies with Thorin Oakenshield in his tomb beneath the Mountain. I saw Bard lay it upon his breast myself. As for the Necklace of Girion, which I knew in elder days as the Gweriamir, it was made of gold and contained five hundred emeralds. It belonged then to the bride of my liege and disappeared for thousands of years until reappearing on the neck of King Thrór, as it should never have done. It was all I desired of the hoard. After the death of Smaug, the necklace went to Bard, but in his wisdom, the Bowman took the necklace apart and gave its gold to the dwarves and the jewels to my people, and so were those bonds repaired.

'I think the artifact sought by Ugrukhôr cannot be traced to the dragon-hoard, but dwarves and Men may tell you otherwise.''The hoard and its treasures were divided among the victors following the death of Smaug; they were many and valuable, and included the Arkenstone and the Necklace of Girion. If the artifact Bugdatish had been contained within, it surely would have been accounted in the reckoning of shares. I think the artifact sought by Ugrukhôr cannot be traced to the dragon-hoard, but dwarves and Men may tell you otherwise.''Thranduil speaks truly. From the hoard he desired the Necklace of Girion, though he knew it not by that name; he coveted no other wealth, not even the Arkenstone. I have an idea about that, ${PLAYER}, but this is not the place to recount it. Suffice it to say that if the artifact Ugrukhôr desires did end up in the dragon-hoard, it is Erebor that must be searched, not Felegoth.'

The Wizard lowers his voice so as not to be heard by Thranduil, and speaks to you in confidence.

'Elves and dwarves have a long and demanding history, and they have often bickered over their treasures and perceived slights. Thranduil said it was the wisdom of Bard the Bowman that the Necklace of Girion be separated into its component gold and gems? He said too that it was Bard's wisdom that the gold and gems be given separately to dwarves and to Elves to repair their sundered bonds of friendship? Such was the advice I gave to Bard, ${PLAYER}! How often must Wizards repair the bridges burned between these two peoples?

'But for now it seems the road leads from Felegoth. Make now for Loeglond, the settlement of the Raft-elves, and seek word of Karazgar and of the Bugdatish. I will follow shortly, and meet you at Erebor after I have concluded my own business with the Elvenking.''If the artifact Ugrukhôr desires did end up in the dragon-hoard, it is Erebor that must be searched, not Felegoth. Make now for Loeglond, the settlement of the Raft-elves, and seek word of Karazgar and of the Bugdatish. I will follow shortly, and meet you at Erebor after I have concluded my own business with the Elvenking.''Greetings to you, ${RACE}! Are you lost? Have you become like a barrel upon the Forest River, travelling from the Halls of the Elvenking downriver on your way to Lake-town? But alas! You have fetched up upon the bank, and now it falls to me to dislodge you with my long pole. Hold still and I will set you going again!'

Nembadeth laughs merrily.

'Pardon my jesting, friend, and tell me what brings you to Loeglond. I will set you going again with my words and not my staff, I promise you!'Your journey to Erebor searching for news of the Bugdatish or Karazgar the Gúrzyul continues through Lake-town.Lake-town is to the north-east of Loeglond.

Nembadeth sent you to speak with Várthrek, a guard at the entrance to Lake-town.Travel to the entrance of Lake-townLake-town is to the north-east of Loeglond.

Nembadeth sent you to speak with Várthrek, a guard at the entrance to Lake-town.Talk to Várthrek at the entrance to Lake-townVárthrek told you to ask the townsfolk of Lake-town for tidings of Karazgar.Ask around Lake-town for tidings of KarazgarVárthrek told you to ask the townsfolk of Lake-town for tidings of Karazgar.Ask around Lake-town for tidings of KarazgarVárthrek told you to ask the townsfolk of Lake-town for tidings of Karazgar.Ask around Lake-town for tidings of KarazgarKólfith is on the lower level of Lake-town, just above the water.Talk to Kólfith on the lower level of Lake-town, just above the waterKólfith wants to meet you inside the Dragonbone Inn, in the northern section of Lake-town. He asked you to speak with Fasti outside the inn to arrange a safe meeting.Talk to Fasti outside the Dragonbone Inn and meet with Kólfith in secretAuthi, the dwarf you befriended in the Dragonbone Inn, is just to the north of the tavern.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter just north of the Dragonbone Inn'These names are unfamiliar to me, friend. Karazgar? Bugdatish? Blame me for focusing on the river and my duties alone, but such curious words are the province of lords and princes, Wizards and adventurers, and not Nembadeth of the raft-elves. Trade is my charge, and in that I excel.

'Still I wish to help you, and help you I shall! Most Elves have few dealings with the Men of the Lake, but the raft-elves cannot stand aloof if the trade is to continue! It may be the Men of Lake-town know the names you seek, and I can help you gain their good graces!

'Travel to the front gate of Lake-town and introduce yourself to Várthrek. He is one of the guards there, and you will know him by his crimson hair and beard. Tell him you have come from Nembadeth of the raft-elves, and he will see you have whatever information you require. I helped him obtain from Felegoth a present for his young son, and he will help you in memory of my aid. Onward to Lake-town, friend barrel! I have dislodged you, and with my help, your course will be swift and sure!''The Men of Lake-town may know the names you seek, and I can help you gain their good graces! Travel to the front gate of Lake-town and introduce yourself to Várthrek. He is one of the guards there, and you will know him by his crimson hair and beard. Tell him you have come from Nembadeth of the raft-elves, and he will see you have whatever information you require. I helped him obtain from Felegoth a present for his young son, and he will help you in memory of my aid. Onward to Lake-town, friend barrel! I have dislodged you, and with my help your course will be swift and sure!''The Men of Lake-town may know the names you seek, and I can help you gain their good graces! Travel to the front gate of Lake-town and introduce yourself to Várthrek. He is one of the guards there, and you will know him by his crimson hair and beard. Tell him you have come from Nembadeth of the raft-elves, and he will see you have whatever information you require. I helped him obtain from Felegoth a present for his young son, and he will help you in memory of my aid. Onward to Lake-town, friend barrel! I have dislodged you, and with my help, your course will be swift and sure!''Ho there, stranger. What brings you to Lake-town? I hope you intend no trouble here, for there is turmoil aplenty without adding to it.'

You assure Várthrek you intend to cause no disruption, and Nembadeth of the raft-elves suggested you speak to Várthrek directly.

'Ah, Nembadeth is a friend, and I trust her judgement, though she is more flighty than the others of her kind I have met, and seems more a woman of the Lake than an Elf of the Wood to me! She obtained a very smart wooden carving for my son Saevarr on his past birthday, and he prizes it above all. For Nembadeth I will help you as I may. What do you need, friend?'

You ask Várthrek if he has heard tell of Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, and he grimaces.

'Too often have I heard that name. He is a constant source of trouble in these parts and seems often the well-spring from which our pains come, though he is rarely seen. Whether he buys his agents with coin or with promises I cannot say, but I am certain more hands than his own work our misfortunes. If you ask around town you may learn more. I wish you well, for Nembadeth's sake, but keep your wits about you. I do not want to tell her that the ${RACE} she sent to me met an unhappy end.''Karazgar? Too often have I heard that name. He is a constant source of trouble in these parts and seems often the well-spring from which our pains come, though he is rarely seen. Whether he buys his agents with coin or with promises I cannot say, but I am certain more hands than his own work our misfortunes. If you ask around town you may learn more. I wish you well, for Nembadeth's sake, but keep your wits about you. I do not want to tell her that the ${RACE} she sent to me met an unhappy end.''What?''Karazgar? Too often have I heard that name. He is a constant source of trouble in these parts and seems often the well-spring from which our pains come, though he is rarely seen. Whether he buys his agents with coin or with promises I cannot say, but I am certain more hands than his own work our misfortunes. If you ask around town you may learn more. I wish you well, for Nembadeth's sake, but keep your wits about you. I do not want to tell her that the ${RACE} she sent to me met an unhappy end.''What?''Karazgar? Too often have I heard that name. He is a constant source of trouble in these parts and seems often the well-spring from which our pains come, though he is rarely seen. Whether he buys his agents with coin or with promises I cannot say, but I am certain more hands than his own work our misfortunes. If you ask around town you may learn more. I wish you well, for Nembadeth's sake, but keep your wits about you. I do not want to tell her that the ${RACE} she sent to me met an unhappy end.''What?''What?''You should be more careful with that name, friend. Folk are on edge hereabout, and it's said the Weeping Warrior has ears everywhere. I might know a thing or two about him you might find of use.'

Kólfith peers into the shadows.

'We should not speak of him here. Go to the Dragonbone Inn, here in town, and tell Fasti outside that you're a friend of Kólfith. We won't be disturbed there.''You are a friend of Kólfith? I will show you to a table inside where you can wait for him.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'We should not speak of the Warrior here. Go to the Dragonbone Inn, here in town, and tell Fasti outside that you're a friend of Kólfith. We won't be disturbed there.''It was a fine brawl, but such fights are not as good for talking. We will be better able to talk here.'Authi Gem-cutter has shared some of his own problems with you and invited you to the Lonely Mountain where you may learn more about your own charge.Authi Gem-cutter invited you to go to Erebor to consult with King Thorin about the mysterious decline in the craft-skill of the dwarves. He asked you to defeat troublesome beasts on the way to the Lonely Mountain.Defeat beasts on the way to the Lonely Mountain (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Authi Gem-cutter invited you to go to Erebor to consult with King Thorin about the mysterious decline in the craft-skill of the dwarves. He said he would meet you at the Lonely Mountain.Travel to the Lonely Mountain, north of Dale, and meet with AuthiGandalf has come to Erebor, and he stands outside the entrance to the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to Gandalf at the Lonely MountainGandalf hinted that some old friends could be found on the way to the throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Find the visitors Gandalf mentioned on the way to the throne in the Hall Under the MountainAs Gandalf hinted, a number of Thorin Oakenshield's companions have come to Erebor. What has drawn them here?Talk to DwalinTalk to GlóinTalk to DoriGandalf is in the Hall Under the Mountain near several dwarves of Thorin's Company who have come to Erebor.Talk to Gandalf in the Hall Under the MountainKing Thorin III is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain'I like you, ${PLAYER}. You are good in a fight, that much is clear! Are you also good at holding things in confidence?'

You assure Authi that you will not betray his trust, and he tugs on his beard thoughtfully.

'I have worked at my craft for a long time, ${PLAYER}. My father and my brothers are jewellers of no small skill, celebrated throughout all the kingdoms of the dwarves, and I too have achieved no small success in that field. For dozens of years, the Gem-cutters had no equal, and the pieces we crafted were so fair even the most judgmental eye could be made to weep at their beauty. You should have seen them, my new friend!

'But something has been happening for the past few years. None of my efforts of late have achieved the same level of success as my earlier work. None of my pieces seem to sparkle as they once did, and others of my kind report a similar waning of their craft-skill. What is happening to Durin's Folk? There is some discussion underway at Erebor, and few can agree on the cause. I have taken to wandering, trying to come up with an explanation for this decay; I was on my way back to the Mountain when we met, ${PLAYER}. I had found no solution, but your quest has given me an idea.

'Could it be that Karazgar is responsible for this decline of our craft-skill? He is active in the area and has no love for Durin's Folk. Perhaps he has worked some evil against us? I invite you to go to Erebor and share what you have learned of the Weeping Warrior with King Thorin. It may be that our problems are related! I encourage you to slay some beasts on the way, my friend. I will as well, and see you there!''I invite you to go to Erebor and share what you have learned of the Weeping Warrior with King Thorin. It may be that our problems are related! I encourage you to slay some beasts on the way, my friend. I will as well, and see you there!''I will meet you at the Lonely Mountain, my new friend!''Ah, you know Gandalf? How fortunate for my people that he has come to investigate this decline in our craft-skill!''At last you arrive! I have been waiting here for some time now. Authi arrived before you did, and he told me all about your exploits in Lake-town. Perhaps if you spent less time carousing at taverns and more time riding, you would not have kept me waiting so long in the cold!'

The Wizard's tone is gruff, but his eyes sparkle merrily.

'You and Authi are not the only recent arrivals to the Lonely Mountain. You will find a number of old friends inside, each of them brought hither from concern. Find them on the way to the throne in the Hall Under the Mountain and you may learn their purpose matches our own.

'One more thing, ${PLAYER}. In these lands I am known as Gandalf, and not Mithrandir. Let it not confuse you!''You and Authi are not the only recent arrivals to the Lonely Mountain. You will find a number of old friends inside, each of them brought hither from concern. Find them on the way to the throne in the Hall Under the Mountain and you may learn their purpose matches our own.''Most of the surviving members of Thorin's Company have come to Erebor, driven by their concerns. Their worries may align with our own, ${PLAYER}.''You and Authi are not the only recent arrivals to the Lonely Mountain. You will find a number of old friends inside, each of them brought hither from concern. Find them on the way to the throne in the Hall Under the Mountain and you may learn their purpose matches our own.''It has been some time since we have all gathered here. It does my heart good to see my friends and distant kin, despite the worries that drew us here. I wish my brother was with us.'

Dwalin sighs heavily.

'Balin believed, as did many good dwarves, that it had been a mistake for the Arkenstone to be entombed with Thorin. He thought the Heart of the Mountain should be proudly displayed, for all to see, and its brilliance would inspire great works of craft and beauty. As the craft-skill of Durin's Folk began to wane, the research Balin conducted convinced him that sealing away the Arkenstone was to blame. He quarreled with Dáin on the matter, and this disagreement caused the King to remove him as counsellor. It was the last injury, and compelled Balin to leave. He took Ori and Óin, as well as many other dwarves, and they sought to reclaim the ancient Dwarf-home of Khazad-dûm.

'I did not side with my brother on this. I remained loyal to Dáin. He elevated me to my position as Steward of Thorin's Hall, but not a day passes where I do not miss my brother and wonder if I should have gone with him. But now Balin is gone, and Khazad-dûm is lost.''I prefer when things are simple, ${PLAYER}. Should we not have entombed the Arkenstone with Thorin? It seemed the right course back then, and no one argued against it. If the craft-skill of my people is fading, who is to say it would be any different if the Arkenstone were not sealed away? Bah! Let the wise-beards discuss it among themselves. Balin believed the Arkenstone should be removed from Thorin's tomb, and he was the wisest of us. But he is dead.

'Now, this Weeping Warrior... that is a subject more to my liking! I say we find him, let our axes and hammers do the talking, and give him something to cry about. Nice and simple. It may not solve everything, but I wager it would not hurt. Not us, anyway.''My travels do not bring me home to Erebor very often, for Dáin made me the Mountain's chief hoard-seeker. I am often away, wandering in the wilderness in search of lost dwarf-holds and treasure-caches. Many are the gold and jewels I have found to swell Erebor's treasuries, but the fading craft-skill of my people means they languish within, unimproved and unused. It is a terrible pity!

'Should the Arkenstone be taken from the tomb of Thorin? Would that restore the craft-skill of my people, and enable them to create great works such as they did of old? I once thought so, but I set aside that belief when my younger brother Ori departed with Balin. The argument filled me with sadness, and I had a job to do. I could not dwell upon the grief. Perhaps the decline of my people could not be prevented, Arkenstone or no.

'I am pleased to see my friends and kinsmen, but I do not think we can recapture our glories of old.''I do not know what has caused this decline in the craft-skill of Durin's Folk, ${PLAYER}. Neither do I know whether entombing the Arkenstone with Thorin Oakenshield is to blame for it, but my suspicion is that it is not. There is another possibility, and in it I see the workings of our enemy.

'What if the Arkenstone has been taken from the tomb of Thorin without the knowledge of the dwarves? It may be that the Arkenstone no longer lies with Thorin Oakenshield, and its departure has adversely affected the skills of Durin's Folk.

'The tomb must be opened, ${PLAYER}, if we are to ensure the Arkenstone remains within. We must ask King Thorin III for permission to do this.''The tomb must be opened, ${PLAYER}, if we are to ensure the Arkenstone remains within. We must ask King Thorin III for permission to do this.''I am as concerned as any of my people about this worrying decline in the skill of the artisans, but I do not know what can be done about it. Dáin before me also did not have a solution. Do you and Gandalf have a suggestion, ${PLAYER}? I am willing to hear any proposal, but I am not confident any solution can be found.'The King Under the Mountain has agreed to your request to open the tomb of Thorin Oakenshield and ensure the Arkenstone remains within, but he wants you to first obtain permission from Thorin's sister Dís.Gandalf wishes to speak with you by the throne of King Thorin III, in the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to Gandalf by the throne in the Hall Under the MountainDís is at Raven-hill, south-west of the Lonely Mountain.Find Dís, the sister of Thorin Oakenshield, at Raven-hillDís, the sister of Thorin Oakenshield, is at Raven-hill.Talk to Dís at Raven-hillYou offered to clear the brush and stones from the path up Raven-hill for Dís.Clear brush from the path up Raven-hill (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Clear stones from the path up Raven-hill (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dís, the sister of Thorin Oakenshield, is at Raven-hill.Talk to Dís at Raven-hillYou volunteered to bring water from the well at the base of Raven-hill to Dís.Pick up the bucket of water at the base of Raven-hillDís, the sister of Thorin Oakenshield, is at Raven-hill.Talk to Dís at Raven-hillYou volunteered to defeat foes in the vicinity of Dale for Dís.Defeat foes near Dale for Dís (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dís, the sister of Thorin Oakenshield, is at Raven-hill.Talk to Dís at Raven-hill'You wish to open the tomb of Thorin Oakenshield and ensure the Arkenstone remains within? None could have breached the tomb without our knowledge, ${PLAYER}. And yet, even as I speak these words, I am gripped with uncertainty. If the Heart of the Mountain has been taken, it might explain the diminishment of our craft-skill....'

The King Under the Mountain strokes his beard thoughtfully.

'Yes, ${PLAYER}, we should learn if the Arkenstone still lies within the tomb or if some enemy has taken it. I alone cannot give you leave to open the tomb of Thorin Oakenshield, though I am King Under the Mountain and possess lordship of all that lies within. It would not be proper to open Mazal Akrâz without the permission of Thorin's sister, Dís. Her children also lie in that place, and I would not have their rest or that of her brother disturbed without her leave.

'She is the Keeper of Raven-hill, ${PLAYER}, and that is where you will find her. Speak to her and learn if she will allow the tomb to be opened.''It would not be proper to open Mazal Akrâz without the permission of Thorin's sister, Dís. Her children also lie in that place, and I would not have their rest or that of her brother disturbed without her leave.

'She is the Keeper of Raven-hill, ${PLAYER}, and that is where you will find her. Speak to her and learn if she will allow the tomb to be opened.''Now listen here, ${PLAYER}, and listen close. Thorin Oakenshield's sister Dís is very old, and she is unlikely at first to be very accommodating to our request. She will look more favourably upon it, I expect, if she were to see the familiar faces of Thorin's old friends and companions. But not all at once! Such a crowd will set her ill at ease, and she will not allow the intrusion.

'I think there may be a way. I am reminded of an old trick that was of use to me during the Quest of Erebor, when Smaug still lived, his stolen hoard unrecovered. I will let the remaining dwarves of Thorin's Company know my plan. For your part, speak to Dís and be as friendly and as helpful as you may.''I think there may be a way by which Dís can be made most amenable to our plan. I am reminded of an old trick that was of use to me during the Quest of Erebor, when Smaug still lived, his stolen hoard unrecovered. I will let the remaining dwarves of Thorin's Company know my plan. For your part, speak to Dís and be as friendly and as helpful as you may.''I think there may be a way by which Dís can be made most amenable to our plan. I am reminded of an old trick that was of use to me during the Quest of Erebor, when Smaug still lived, his stolen hoard unrecovered. I will let the remaining dwarves of Thorin's Company know my plan. For your part, speak to Dís and be as friendly and as helpful as you may.''Yes?'

Dís stares at you, but says nothing more. Hesitantly, you begin to ask about the Arkenstone, but before you can properly word the request she interrupts you.

'The Heart of the Mountain? It lies with my brother Thorin beneath the Mountain. I have not visited him in some time.'

She looks at you slyly.

'The path down the hill is choked with brush and stones. Someone should do something.'

You offer to clear the path, and Dís grunts noncommittally.'The path down the hill is choked with brush and stones. Someone should do something.''These are my cousins, come from afar. Dwalin is the Steward of Thorin's Hall, in the Blue Mountains, and Glóin acts as emissary for the King Under the Mountain.

'The conversation has left me parched. If I am to entertain visitors, I will need more water to refresh me.'

Dís gives you a meaningful look, and you volunteer to bring up some water from the well at the base of the hill.'The conversation has left me parched. If I am to entertain visitors, I will need more water to refresh me.''This is a day for visitors! Bifur, the Keeper of Hammers, and Bofur, the Master of Chisels, have come to Raven-hill. Even Bombur, the Warden of the Larders, has paid me a visit. Most unexpected. These dwarves accompanied my brother and my sons on the Quest of Erebor.'

This seems as good an opening as any, and you ask again about the Arkenstone. As before, Dís interrupts you.

'Bombur's progress up the hill was slow, and he told me he spied a number of foes in the vicinity of Dale during the ascent. I cannot think about the Heart of the Mountain while my neighbours are endangered!'

Dís raises her eyes to you in challenge, and you volunteer to deal with the foes that lurk around Dale.'Bombur's progress up the hill was slow, and he told me he spied a number of foes in the vicinity of Dale during the ascent. I cannot think about the Heart of the Mountain while my neighbours are endangered!''More friends have arrived, and a Wizard too. As friends I count Dori, the High Hoard-seeker, and Nori, the Keeper of Coffers. They are my distant kin. Of the Wizard Gandalf I have fewer kind words to speak.'

Dís looks around her, and you brace yourself for another task.

'Ah, there is nothing else that needs doing right now, but do not think I am unaware of this plan of yours! I have so few visitors on most days that this slow trickle of friends and kin must mean something. You might as well tell me. What do the surviving members of my brother's adventuring band ask of his sister?'

Gandalf opens his mouth to speak, and Dís silences him with a look.

'I don't want to hear it from you, Wizard. I want this ${RACE} to tell me. Well? Spit it out, ${RACE}!'Dís has given her permission to the dwarves of Thorin's Company to investigate his tomb and learn if the Arkenstone remains within.Gandalf is at Raven-hill, waiting to travel with you and the surviving members of Thorin's Company to ensure the Arkenstone remains entombed with the Oakenshield.Talk to Gandalf at Raven-hill and travel to Mazal AkrâzKing Thorin III is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to King Thorin III at his throne in the Hall Under the MountainGandalf is by the throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to Gandalf by the throne in the Hall Under the Mountain'My brother Thorin was entombed in Mazal Akrâz with the Arkenstone, and with an Elven-sword beside. You think the Heart of the Mountain has been stolen? What thief would dare disturb the Oakenshield's rest or risk the guards of his tomb? No, do not try to convince me of this. There is no need.

'It has been too long since I visited my brother and my sons where they lie beneath the Mountain. I will allow you to ensure the Arkenstone remains with Thorin while I pay my respects to my brother and to Fíli and Kíli. I am old, ${RACE}. It will be good for Durin's Folk to know the fate of the Heart of the Mountain, and it will benefit my own heart to see my lost kin at least one more time before I join them.

'Tell the Wizard he has permission, but not to go alone. We will all go together.''It is good that Dís has given her permission to open the tomb of Thorin. We must know if Karazgar or his agents have worked some evil involving the Arkenstone.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'I will allow you to ensure the Arkenstone remains with Thorin while I pay my respects to my brother and to Fíli and Kíli. I am old, ${RACE}. It will be good for Durin's Folk to know the fate of the Heart of the Mountain, and it will benefit my own heart to see my lost kin at least one more time before I join them.

'Tell the Wizard he has permission, but not to go alone. We will all go together.''I am satisfied by your report, ${PLAYER}, and put at ease by the knowledge that the Arkenstone is still kept safe by Thorin Oakenshield beneath the Mountain. There is no end to the mischief Karazgar and others of his ilk seek to work upon my lands and my people, but the Heart of the Mountain remains beyond his reach.

'I am less pleased by Gandalf's supposition that the loss of the dwarf-ring Handórm has caused the diminishment of our craft-skill, for such a loss cannot be reversed. That ring is gone, along with the others to which we held a claim, and none of them are coming back. It is not our way to give up hope, and our artisans will continue to strive, but I mourn the great works that once were made and will now have no rivals.

'Thank you all the same, ${PLAYER}.''This feels right to me, ${PLAYER}. The Gúrzyul gained their continuing lives through the powers of Sauron, and now that he is gone, perhaps that power fades. Can it be their strength declines even now, just as the craft-skill of the dwarves lessens with the absence of the Dwarf-ring? Do the Gúrzyul seek some means of survival, some power to counteract this decay? It would explain Ugrukhôr's desperation, ${PLAYER}.

'If it is not a Bugdatish he seeks, not an artifact, but a Bugdash, a birth-name, perhaps he believes this information will stave off his decline. We must remain vigilant and keep the Gúrzyul from attaining the knowledge that will save them. This journey was valuable, my friend, though we defeated no Gúrzyul and found neither artifacts nor weapons. The wisdom we found is treasure enough.

'I will ask the king to grant me a room within the Mountain and will retire there to consider our next course of action. Then we will face the Gúrzyul, and bring them to an end.'Gandalf was given a residence within the Lonely Mountain and plans to retire there to study while you help the Gem-cutters with their day-to-day work.The home of Authi Gem-cutter and his family can be found in the south-western corner of the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Find the home of Authi Gem-cutter in the Dwelling-hallsAuthi Gem-cutter is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the JewelsteadMother Amma is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Mother Amma in the JewelsteadAuthi Gem-cutter is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the JewelsteadThe entrance to the New-lodes of Narathâk is located east of the Dwelling-halls, beyond the passage that runs from the entryway to the Hall Under the Mountain.Find the entrance to the New-lodes of Narathâk east of the entryway within the Lonely MountainAuthi Gem-cutter's brother Gráfar is in the New-lodes of Narathâk.Talk to Gráfar Splitstone in the New-lodes of NarathâkThe New-lodes of Narathâk are crawling with cave-claws and other pests, and Authi's brother Gráfar asked you to help make the mine safe for the miners by thinning out some of the vermin.Defeat vermin in the New-lodes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Authi's brother Gráfar Splitstone has asked you to carry a crate of precious stones back to Mother Amma in the Jewelstead. The Gem-cutter family home is located in the south-west corner of the Dwelling-halls.Pick up the crate of precious stones by Gráfar SplitstoneMother Amma has asked you to pick up the finely-crafted necklace and deliver it to Erfith, the customer who requested it. Erfith can be found on the west side of the Dwelling-halls of Erebor, on one of the lower levels.Pick up the finely-crafted necklace and deliver it to Erfith on the west side of the Dwelling-hallsErfith the Quarrelsome is on the west side of the Dwelling-halls of Erebor, east of the Jewelstead and down the stairs.Talk to Erfith on the west side of the Dwelling-hallsAuthi Gem-cutter is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Jewelstead'King Thorin has given to me a room in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor, and I will retire there to think upon what we have discovered. The efforts of the Gúrzyul to increase their power in the wake of Sauron's passing are of interest to me, particularly those of Karazgar here in the north. Durin's Folk have provided me with several leads to study, and I shall follow each of them as far as I am able.

'Such courses of study are lonely pastimes, but they are rarely fruitless. I will see you when I have learned what I may, but while I am so engaged I suggest you familiarize yourself with Erebor and its people. Perhaps you can lend some assistance to Authi Gem-cutter and his family? I am certain they would welcome your company, and will not mind showing you around.

'I believe you will find Authi's home in the Dwelling-halls, located south of the Hall Under the Mountain and to the west.''You should familiarize yourself with Erebor and its people. Perhaps you can lend some assistance to Authi Gem-cutter and his family? I am certain they would welcome your company, and will not mind showing you around.

'I believe you will find Authi's home in the Dwelling-halls, located south of the Hall Under the Mountain and to the west.''You should familiarize yourself with Erebor and its people. Perhaps you can lend some assistance to Authi Gem-cutter and his family? I am certain they would welcome your company, and will not mind showing you around.

'I believe you will find Authi's home in the Dwelling-halls, located south of the Hall Under the Mountain and to the west.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, I hoped you would find time to visit! This is Mesem-azhâr, the Jewelstead, home to my family for many years. It is a humble place, but the hearth is warm and you are welcome to stave off the chill of the heights whenever you have need!

'My father Fúni and my brother Fúsi have not yet returned from their excursion on the other side of the Misty Mountains, but I will be pleased to introduce you to my mother, and to another of my brothers who remains in Erebor.

'Mother Amma! This is ${PLAYER}, the ${RACE} I mentioned to you!''My father Fúni and my brother Fúsi have not yet returned from their excursion on the other side of the Misty Mountains, but I will be pleased to introduce you to my mother, and to another of my brothers who remains in Erebor.''Welcome to Mesem-azhâr, ${PLAYER}. If you are a friend of my son, you may come and go as you like. All I ask is that you follow the Gem-cutter family rules: when you come to the Jewelstead you never complain, you keep in good cheer, and you help us with our work. These rules are more important than ever with my husband Fúni still away, for he did not take all of the work away with him; indeed, he took almost none!

'When he returns he will have an earful from Mother Amma about the longer than expected duration of his journey, I promise you that! But for now I will follow the Gem-cutter rules: never complain, keep in good cheer, and make sure my sons and their friends do their share of the work!''Welcome to Mesem-azhâr. If you are a friend of my son, you may come and go as you like. All I ask is that you follow the Gem-cutter family rules: when you come to the Jewelstead you never complain, you keep in good cheer, and you help us with our work.''You have heard the rules, ${PLAYER}. Let us visit my brother Gráfar at Narathâk and see if he needs any assistance with his work in the New-lodes. That should satisfy Mother Amma, and I intended for you to meet my brother anyway.

'The New-lodes of Narathâk have been recently delved, and can be found east of here, beyond the passage that runs from the entryway to the Hall Under the Mountain. My brother spends most of his waking hours mining for the precious gems we need to further our craft. There has always been great demand for the jewellery my family fashions, and Gráfar works hard to ensure we have what we need to meet it!''The New-lodes of Narathâk have been recently delved, and can be found east of here, beyond the passage that runs from the entryway to the Hall Under the Mountain. My brother spends most of his waking hours mining for the precious gems we need to further our craft. There has always been great demand for the jewellery my family fashions, and Gráfar works hard to ensure we have what we need to meet it!''The New-lodes of Narathâk have been recently delved, and can be found east of here, beyond the passage that runs from the entryway to the Hall Under the Mountain. My brother spends most of his waking hours mining for the precious gems we need to further our craft. There has always been great demand for the jewellery my family fashions, and Gráfar works hard to ensure we have what we need to meet it!''Ah, you must be ${PLAYER}! Authi speaks highly of you, and tells me you can hold your own in a good old-fashioned tavern brawl. Are you also adept at clearing mines of vermin? Every new delve we dig seems to unearth all manner of unpleasant pests, and these New-lodes are no exception.

'I have a crate of precious stones ready to return to Mother Amma and I could use someone to carry it, but if you could first thin the ranks of the cave-claws and other irritations in Narathâk I would be ever at your service.''I have a crate of precious stones ready to return to Mother Amma and I could use someone to carry it, but if you could first thin the ranks of the cave-claws and other irritations in Narathâk I would be ever at your service.''You did very well to clear out some of the vermin! My brother was right to praise your skills and your helpfulness. This crate of precious stones is ready to deliver to Mother Amma back home, if you are still willing to prove my brother a good judge of character.''Erfith requested a fine necklace some weeks ago, and it is now ready to deliver to him. He paid us a tidy sum for it, but it took quite a long time to make and he had better appreciate it. Do not say this to his face, but his neighbours call him "Erfith the Quarrelsome," so I would not put it past him to find some defect or flaw with the work.

'You should be able to find Erfith on the west side of the Dwelling-halls, on one of the lower levels. Look to the east and down the stairs.''You will deliver the necklace to Erfith? Better you than me, ${PLAYER}. We all call him Erfith the Quarrelsome, and it is a well-deserved moniker, I assure you. You'll usually find him on the west side of the Dwelling-halls, down on one of the lower levels. I saw him not long ago to the east and down the stairs, but he might have moved on since then.''I had expected the delivery to come from one of the Gem-cutters themselves, but so long as I have the piece I requested I suppose it is worth no complaint. There is something about the personal touch that turns an ordinary customer into a repeat customer, if you ask me, but apparently that is not something everyone believes. Unless...'

Erfith trails off, and looks at you conspiratorially.

'I heard a rumour that the Gem-cutters might have fallen on hard times and they plan to leave Erebor in disgrace. I am not saying it is true, just that I heard of it from the tale-spinner Khîl, and if the Gem-cutters have chosen to delegate their responsibilities to others then perhaps there is some truth in it.

'Thank you for the delivery, even so. I will miss the Gem-cutters when they depart Erebor, even if their service has lost the touch of the personal that first made me interested in their pieces. Do you think this has something to do with Fúni's long departure? He brought one of the sons with him, but left the others with his wife. I heard from Khîl that they might rejoin him and start a new life away across the Misty Mountains, but I don't know that I believe that part of it.''I heard a rumour that the Gem-cutters might have fallen on hard times and they plan to leave Erebor in disgrace. I am not saying it is true, just that I heard of it from the tale-spinner Khîl, and if the Gem-cutters have chosen to delegate their responsibilities to others then perhaps there is some truth in it.''How did it go, ${PLAYER}? Did Erfith the Quarrelsome find something about which to complain?'

You reluctantly recount the rumour that Erfith mentioned to you, about the Gem-cutters leaving Erebor in disgrace, and Authi's cheerful face twists angrily.

'Leave the Lonely Mountain? And in disgrace? What rubbish! Where did Erfith hear this nonsense? Who is spreading these lies about my family?'A dwarf named Khîl has been spreading lies about the Gem-cutter family, and Authi wants you to find him and get him to stop.Khîl might be found outside the entrance to the Lonely Mountain.Find Khîl outside the entrance to the Lonely MountainKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Talk to Khîl outside the Lonely MountainKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Demonstrate to Khîl that you are ready to <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to his talesKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Talk to Khîl and hear his taleKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Khîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Talk to Khîl and hear his taleKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Valves and pipes? No one else has mentioned such a machine at the battleKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Talk to Khîl and hear his taleKhîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Khîl the Tale-spinner is outside the entrance to Erebor, regaling onlookers with his stories.Talk to Khîl and hear his taleAuthi Gem-cutter is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Jewelstead'You say Erfith mentioned the name Khîl? That does not surprise me. He is one of the Zhélruka, a clan of dwarves from the east, but he has lived here in Erebor for many years and caused no serious trouble... until now! He is a spinner of tales, and in my experience he has never had a truthful thing to say; I have found his untrue tales to be harmless, for the most part. He has never spoken ill of my family before. Why has he now begun to spread these lies about the Gem-cutters?

'Go ask him why he has chosen this course, ${PLAYER}. He tends to loiter outside, by the entrance to the Lonely Mountain, but I have not seen him today.''Khîl is one of the Zhélruka, a clan of dwarves from the east, but he has lived here in Erebor for many years and caused no serious trouble... until now! He is a spinner of tales, and in my experience he has never had a truthful thing to say; I have found his untrue tales to be harmless, for the most part. He has never spoken ill of my family before. Why has he now begun to spread these lies about the Gem-cutters?

'Go ask him why he has chosen this course, ${PLAYER}. He tends to loiter outside, by the entrance to the Lonely Mountain, but I have not seen him today.''Khîl is one of the Zhélruka, a clan of dwarves from the east, but he has lived here in Erebor for many years and caused no serious trouble... until now! He is a spinner of tales, and in my experience he has never had a truthful thing to say, but I have found his untrue tales to be harmless, for the most part. He has never spoken ill of my family before. Why has he now begun to spread these lies about the Gem-cutters?

'Go ask him why he has chosen this course, ${PLAYER}. He tends to loiter outside, by the entrance to the Lonely Mountain, but I have not seen him today.''Ah, another listener! If you would like to hear grand tales about the history of the dwarves and other peoples of the North, you have come to the right spot!''It was in March that an army of Jangovar came out of the east and crossed the Ironfold, led by their fearsome chieftains and emboldened by the power of Sauron. They threatened the people of the Dale-lands, and King Brand stood between his people and their terrible foes.

'While the Men of Dale fled before the approaching army, hoping to make for the safety of the Lonely Mountain and the protection of their dwarf-allies, King Brand stood alone in the water of the river-crossing. He was felled by arrows in the stream, and King Dáin escaped to the safety of Erebor. Should he have stood with his ally, and risked death thereby? Who can say? The dwarves had need of their king, and after the flight to the Lonely Mountain he fought against the Jangovar and died before the very gates. The Mountain was besieged, and both Dale and Erebor were saved only by the sudden defeat of Sauron in the lands far to the south.

'Had it only come sooner, both King Brand and Dáin Ironfoot might still live to lead their people.''It was in March that an army of Jangovar came out of the east and crossed the Ironfold, led by their fearsome chieftains and emboldened by the power of Sauron. They threatened the people of the Dale-lands, and King Brand stood between his people and their terrible foes.

'While the Men of Dale fled before the approaching army, hoping to make for the safety of the Lonely Mountain and the protection of their dwarf-allies, King Brand stood alone in the water of the river-crossing. He was felled by arrows in the stream, and King Dáin escaped to the safety of Erebor. Should he have stood with his ally, and risked death thereby? Who can say? The dwarves had need of their king, and after the flight to the Lonely Mountain he fought against the Jangovar and died before the very gates. The Mountain was besieged, and both Dale and Erebor were saved only by the sudden defeat of Sauron in the lands far to the south.

'Had it only come sooner, both King Brand and Dáin Ironfoot might still live to lead their people.''There is no shortage of tales from the Battle of Dale and the Siege of the Lonely Mountain, my friends. Have you heard tell of the great furnace the Jangovar built by Raven Hill? A frightening construction of pipes and valves it was, with wings of red leather extending above in mimicry of the dragon Smaug.

'None can say for certain what powered the terrible device, but I heard from some who saw it that the furnace contained a fire grim of unsurpassed heat. Metal pipes carried noxious fumes from the device to channels in the stone. The Jangovar believed they could fill the Lonely Mountain with smoke and force the dwarves to flee from their well-protected fastness.

'You may scoff, but friends of mine saw this device with their own eyes! It was destroyed by a small group of canny dwarves, who manipulated the valves and prevented too much smoke from entering the Mountain. They shut down the machine and defeated the fire grim, if such it was. Truly there was enough bravery to go around on that day, even if some do not believe this tale. I assure you it is true!''There is no shortage of tales from the Battle of Dale and the Siege of the Lonely Mountain, my friends. Have you heard tell of the great furnace the Jangovar built by Raven Hill? A frightening construction of pipes and valves it was, with wings of red leather extending above in mimicry of the dragon Smaug.

'None can say for certain what powered the terrible device, but I heard from some who saw it that the furnace contained a fire grim of unsurpassed heat. Metal pipes carried noxious fumes from the device to channels in the stone. The Jangovar believed they could fill the Lonely Mountain with smoke and force the dwarves to flee from their well-protected fastness.

'You may scoff, but friends of mine saw this device with their own eyes! It was destroyed by a small group of canny dwarves, who manipulated the valves and prevented too much smoke from entering the Mountain. They shut down the machine and defeated the fire grim, if such it was. Truly there was enough bravery to go around on that day, even if some do not believe this tale. I assure you it is true!''These stories are of recent vintage, it is true. Harken now to a tale of the distant past, a tale that grows in mystery and allure with every passing decade. More than six thousand years ago, the land shook with great calamity and the home of my people was riven with great cracks and torn asunder. The beautiful Hall of Torrents in the distant east collapsed, and thousands of my people were slain. The Zhélruka became a wandering people, and were turned away by all their neighbours.

'But King Durin II, who ruled in Khazad-dûm, heard of their plight and showed mercy to the wanderers. He bade them travel to the Grey Mountains, and promised a place would be made for them in Gundabad. King Túruz of the Zhélruka began a great march across the northern lands, and stayed for a time helping the Longbeards of the Iron Hills repair their own damaged halls. When the Zhélruka again departed for Gundabad, they parted as friends, and promised a healthy trade partnership when they came to their new home.'

Khîl pauses dramatically.

'But they never arrived at Gundabad. King Túruz and the Zhélruka disappeared in the Grey Mountains, and no trace of them was ever found. King Durin and his son Borin sent several companies of dwarves to search for them, but to no avail. After seven-times-seven years, the Longbeards gave up the search.

'And yet one of the Zhélruka survived. His name was Gúrek, and he appeared without warning at the gates of Gundabad, half-starved and raving mad. He told a confused tale of what had befallen his people: that they came across a stream that flowed out of the high mountains and gleamed like silver. The shine of the water came from tiny fragments of mithril, and when the Zhélruka followed it to its source they discovered a grand cavern full of gold, wondrous gemstones, and mithril to rival Khazad-dûm.

'They abandoned their journey at once and carved a great hall they named Thafar-gathol, the Grey Citadel, and they used all their craft and spells to hide the entrance. What became of Thafar-gathol no tale tells, and neither does history record how Gúrek alone came to Gundabad. But Zhélruka and Longbeard alike still lust for the riches of lost Thafar-gathol, and despite numerous attempts at searching the Grey Mountains, none have ever had the good fortune to find it since it vanished. But it is still out there, my friends, waiting to be found.''These stories are of recent vintage, it is true. Harken now to a tale of the distant past, a tale that grows in mystery and allure with every passing decade. More than six thousand years ago, the land shook with great calamity and the home of my people was riven with great cracks and torn asunder. The beautiful Hall of Torrents in the distant east collapsed, and thousands of my people were slain. The Zhélruka became a wandering people, and were turned away by all their neighbours.

'But King Durin II, who ruled in Khazad-dûm, heard of their plight and showed mercy to the wanderers. He bade them travel to the Grey Mountains, and promised a place would be made for them in Gundabad. King Túruz of the Zhélruka began a great march across the northern lands, and stayed for a time helping the Longbeards of the Iron Hills repair their own damaged halls. When the Zhélruka again departed for Gundabad, they parted as friends, and promised a healthy trade partnership when they came to their new home.'

Khîl pauses dramatically.

'But they never arrived at Gundabad. King Túruz and the Zhélruka disappeared in the Grey Mountains, and no trace of them was ever found. King Durin and his son Borin sent several companies of dwarves to search for them, but to no avail. After seven-times-seven years, the Longbeards gave up the search.

'And yet one of the Zhélruka survived. His name was Gúrek, and he appeared without warning at the gates of Gundabad, half-starved and raving mad. He told a confused tale of what had befallen his people: that they came across a stream that flowed out of the high mountains and gleamed like silver. The shine of the water came from tiny fragments of mithril, and when the Zhélruka followed it to its source they discovered a grand cavern full of gold, wondrous gemstones, and mithril to rival Khazad-dûm.

'They abandoned their journey at once and carved a great hall they named Thafar-gathol, the Grey Citadel, and they used all their craft and spells to hide the entrance. What became of Thafar-gathol no tale tells, and neither does history record how Gúrek alone came to Gundabad. But Zhélruka and Longbeard alike still lust for the riches of lost Thafar-gathol, and despite numerous attempts at searching the Grey Mountains, none have ever had the good fortune to find it since it vanished. But it is still out there, my friends, waiting to be found.''The promise of finding lost Thafar-gathol has long moved the hearts of Longbeards and Zhélruka to adventure among the dangerous mountains, disturbing worm and Orc alike, but none have returned from such journeys with news of its whereabouts. It has attained an air of mystery and of dread, and some dwarves have chosen less daring pursuits, if less rewarding.

'That is the case with the Gem-cutters, a Longbeard family whose jewellery has achieved some renown among the folk of the Lonely Mountain. In recent months, their craft has fallen somewhat and Fúni Gem-cutter has had to leave his wife and two of his sons behind while he seeks some means of recovering their fortunes. I heard he travelled beyond the Misty Mountains and is over-long in returning, which causes his wife to worry. If he does not return soon, and with good tidings of new fortune, she will be forced to abandon their home and set out from the Lonely Mountain in disgrace. It is a shame, but such things have been happening more and more often in these difficult days.'

You tell Khîl that you have heard these rumours concerning the Gem-cutters are untrue, and he snorts.

'I only know what I have heard, friend. It is not so hard to believe, is it? I speak only the truth, and I bristle at the suggestion that my stories are anything but honest!''I heard Fúni Gem-cutter travelled beyond the Misty Mountains and is over-long in returning, which causes his wife to worry. If he does not return soon, and with good tidings of new fortune, she will be forced to abandon their home and set out from the Lonely Mountain in disgrace. It is a shame, but such things have been happening more and more often in these difficult days.''It is as I said, is it not? Khîl Tale-spinner always speaks such nonsense. What stories did he spin for you today?'

You briefly recount Khîl's words, and Authi laughs aloud.

'Utter rubbish and nonsense! Everyone knows that King Brand and Dáin Ironfoot fought side by side before the gates of the Lonely Mountain, and when the King of Dale fell, the King Under the Mountain fought to protect his ally's body from the ravages of their foes, ceasing only when he too was slain. And a machine powered by a fire grim? How could the Jangovar have ever constructed such a device, or tamed such a spirit? It is an absurdity.

'But none of these are as unbelievable as the tale of Thafar-gathol! Bah! If such a city ever existed, it would have been found by now. Preposterous drivel!'Authi Gem-cutter is very angry about the gossip Khîl has been spreading about his family, and he wishes to petition King Thorin about the matter.Authi Gem-cutter is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Jewelstead and go with him to petition the kingAuthi Gem-cutter is in the Jewelstead, the Gem-cutter family home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Jewelstead'And yet, none of these tales are as personally offensive to me as the gossip he spreads about my family. It seems he will not stop until he is forced to stop, ${PLAYER}. I must take this matter before King Thorin. Only the King Under the Mountain can prevail upon Khîl to cease his rumourmongering.

'Will you speak on my behalf before the King? I trust you and no others to help me with this, my friend. Tell me if you are willing, and we will petition King Thorin together.''Are you ready to go? Surely King Thorin will hear my plea and command Khîl to cease speaking falsehoods about my family!''I am pleased that King Thorin ruled in my favour, but I cannot help but feel that in some respect I am being punished for bringing the matter to him! Now we have to escort Khîl to his father's home in Járnfast? Bah! Let him travel by himself!

'I suppose I should gather my belongings for the journey. At least Khîl's gossip has been stopped, and he will no longer trouble my family in Erebor. That is surely worth the trip to the Ironfold, ${PLAYER}.'You have agreed to do some chores for Captain Álarr in exchange for access to the murder location. Clean up the filthy guards' barracks.Mud can be found in the guards' barracks in Lake-town.

You should clean up mud.Clean up mud in the barracks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Captain Álarr can be found in the guards' barracks in Lake-town.

You should talk to Álarr.Talk to Álarr'You know what I really need around here? Someone to clean this mess! My guards are filthy, despite how many times I've scolded them for it! How hard is it to wipe your boots off before you come inside? Do me a favour and clean up after them.''Much better. Now, if you could only smack some sense into my men, we wouldn't need to assign someone to clean-up duty in the first place.'Radagast has grown concerned by the ailing roots of Eryn Lasgalen and hopes to heal the forest of decay and corruption.You should collect phials of the Elves' healing draught from within Felegoth.Collect phials (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should defeat corrupted roots and give water to trees in need throughout Eryn Lasgalen.Defeat corrupted roots (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Radagast, who can be found at the tree nursery just outside of Felegoth.Talk to Radagast'Ah, there is more to be done! The Elves of Felegoth have prepared more healing draughts for the ailing roots of Eryn Lasgalen, and I would ask that you nourish those in need. For those too far gone to decay and corruption, do what you must. Nature heals most of its ills, but we must help where we can.

'Take some phials with you and venture into the forest. Eryn Lasgalen shall flourish once more!''Ah, I feel the life returning to this land. That is good. Good indeed!

'I will call on you again soon!'Atli Spider-bane has requested an adventurer to enter Cirith Ungol and clear out some of the spider eggs and webs.Broodling eggs and encroaching webs can be found in Cirith Ungol's courtyard and the tower interior.

You should destroy broodling eggs and encroaching webs.Destroy broodling eggs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Burn encroaching webs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A note hangs on the board, signed by Atli Spider-bane:

'We cannot allow Cirith Ungol to become infested with spiders now that it is no longer infested with Orcs! I need someone to get in there and clear some of the eggs and webs, quickly!'Lorndúr has told you of several problems plaguing trade on the Forest River. Help the raft-elves so they can return to trade upon the water!Lorndúr can be found in Loeglond.

You should complete quests for Lorndúr.Complete: Trouble Comes Down the MountainComplete: Thievery on the RiverComplete: Victims of the AmbushersLorndúr can be found in Loeglond.

You should talk to Lorndúr.Talk to Lorndúr'We should begin with troubles along the Forest River. Shipments are sent downstream from Felegoth, and then picked up here in Loeglond before being sent off to the Long Lake. Not long ago, shipments began going missing before even reaching us. I suspect Orcs are involved. Please do what you can to make the Forest River safer for us.''Thank you for all you have done for us, ${PLAYER}. There is one more danger that I am aware of: Esgaroth.'Despite your efforts, Orcs from the Green Mountains continue to disrupt trade and harass the Raft-elves of Loeglond.Orcs can be found along the Forest River, between Felegoth and Loeglond.

You should defeat the Orc ambushers and hunters that have come down from the Green Mountains.Defeat Orc hunters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orc ambushers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lorndúr can be found in Loeglond.

You should talk to Lorndúr.Talk to Lorndúr'I am glad you have come, ${PLAYER}! I have received more reports of Orcs venturing down from the Green Mountains this very morning. We cannot allow them to continue to disrupt trade along the Forest River, but I have none to spare for this task.

'Will you aid us and drive the Orcs back into the mountains?''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps now that the war is over, these occurrences will be less frequent.

'Nevertheless, I would appreciate it if you returned tomorrow.'The dwarves have a long history with dragons. You may be able to learn something of the riddle of the dragon bones and Karazgar from the lore-masters of the Lonely Mountain.Erebor is in the northern end of the Dale-lands.

You should travel to Erebor to speak with the dwarves there.Travel to the gates of EreborVíglund and the crowd stand before the gates of Erebor, in the northern end of the Dale-lands.

You should talk to Víglund about the dwarves gathered around the statue.Talk to VíglundYou should examine the axe embedded in the statue at the gates of Erebor.Examine the axeYou should examine the rust spot found at the gates of Erebor.Examine the rust spotVíglund the guard can be found at the gates of Erebor.

You should tell Víglund your conclusion about the axe.Talk to VíglundYou should continue assisting the Dwarves of Erebor.

<rgb=#FF0000>The Trail of Rust quest arc will continue after completing the Under The Mountain quest arc which begins with Órug in Erebor.</rgb><rgb=#FF0000>Complete the Under The Mountain quest arc to continue</rgb>Talk to ÓrugYou should speak with Víglund, the guard at the gates of Erebor.Talk to VíglundYou should speak with Lore-masters Rúni, Spall, Fróthi, Mágsal, and Belgur in Erebor.Talk to RúniTalk to SpallTalk to FróthiTalk to MágsalTalk to BelgurGórsim can be found in Erebor.

You should speak with Górsim about viewing the teeth of the dragon Scatha.Talk to Górsim'Why is it, then, that I cannot stop thinking of the riddle of the dragon-bones you told me of, in the Long Lake? And now you tell me this Karazgar appeared holding a dragon's tooth.

'Although dragons play no small part in the history of the Dale-lands, we know remarkably little about the beasts. The dwarves, however... they have long memories, and I know that dragons figure largely into their own histories. I think you should make your way to Erebor, ${PLAYER}, and learn what you can about dragon's bones and teeth, and what this man-of-rust might be after.''You there, hold. Do you know anything about this, this - mischief?'

Víglund gestures at the axe lodged in the statue.

'No? If that is the doing of those Stout-axes, then King Thorin shall hear a piece of my mind, and then some! He may trust them, but I do not. Take a look at it for yourself. I am right, am I not?''A tamer of dragons, this Karazgar? Why, that reminds me of an old tale. It is said that Smaug's grudge against Durin's folk began when he woke, far in the North, to a dead worm's head, with an axe left in it - much like what we see before us.

'Could that have been this Weeping Warrior? These servants of Sauron are deathless, are they not?

'But what is it he wants with old dragon bones? Bah! I am no expert in dragons, but there are dwarves in Erebor who are. Why don't you speak to Rúni, Spall, Fróthi, Mágsal, and Belgur? They are counted among our most learned lore-masters.''Smaug? Why yes, of course. It is common knowledge the cunning worm seized this very mountain from the dwarves and made it his lair for many long years.

'What fewer know is from whence Smaug the Golden came. The answer, of course, is the North, where the great dragons dwelled. Why he came south, I cannot say, but it is written that his hatred of the dwarves runs old and deep, ever since discovering five young worms slain, and wrongly blaming Durin's Folk.''Dragons? Hrm! Dragons... well then, have you ever heard the name of Hrímil Frost-heart? Aye, one of the eldest and most feared of her kind, mother of many cold-drakes who have filled the world with terror.

'One of these Stout-axes told me another tale, claiming that Hrímil had somehow been captured by Sauron and was held in Barad-dûr! I cannot quite decide whether to believe the fellow, but all the same, I find myself wondering if the old cold-drake was crushed beneath Barad-dûr when it fell.''I shall speak of Scatha the Worm, which is of great interest to me, and, indeed, touches in many ways on the lives of all of us who have come to dwell here under the mountain.

'It was long ago and far to the north, in the Grey Mountains, that Durin's Folk disturbed the slumber of the great dragons, Scatha among them. Many dwarves and their friends paid for that mistake with their lives, and a few dragons as well - but not Scatha. Swift and cunning was he, and fled that place to trouble the world above.

'Nor did trouble end with his death, for Men fought with dwarves over Scatha's hoard. And though the greater portion of it was stolen from us, all we ever had of the treasure was a string of Scatha's teeth! They are kept, even now, here in the Lonely Mountain.

'If you wished to see them, you might ask Górsim, keeper of the vaults.''Fingar, the wingless worm, lay hidden in the Grey Mountains long, long after the other great dragons were fled or slain. From hidden places he watched the Dwarf-kingdoms rise and grow in wealth. And so, too, grew his greed until at last he came forth from hiding and laid waste to the dwarf-holds, claiming their coin and craft for his hoard.

'That is an old telling, and little else is known... although I have also heard that greed was Fingar's undoing, and through it he became a captive of Sauron, in Mordor.

'Then, if the Stout-axes are to be believed, he was fought and slain shortly after Mordor fell. Bah, what nonsense! I believe it not.''The Stout-axes have filled my ears with tales of Thostír, a fire-drake whom they say escaped the Abyss of Mordath upon the plain of Gorgoroth. I have done some reading of old texts, and I believe this Thostír may just be the dragon who laid waste to Dwarf-kingdoms in the eastern mountains, in times of old. Of course, we may never know for certain.'You tell Víglund that the axe is the work of Karazgar and what little else you know.

'Never heard that name. I know I would remember it if I had! This Karazgar would be from Mordor, or I'm a wren's egg - and so are those Stout-axes. Perhaps they know something of this mysterious figure.

'Why don't you speak to Órug and learn what you may?''What? What is it, ${RACE}? Can you not see that I am taking a well-deserved rest?''Ah, Víglund sent you, did he?'

You tell what you have learned of Karazgar.

'Ah, the Weeping Warrior, yes. That he is called. One of the Gúrzyul I never set eyes on, but tales of his wicked deeds are told by the Stout-axes. A cruel assassin, a spy, and a tamer of beasts. He enslaved dragons for the Dark Lord, it is said.'The spiders have vanished from all over Mirkwood, and King Thranduil wishes to know why.Known spider dens can be found throughout Eryn Lasgalen.

You should search the woods and remaining spider-dens for spiders.Search GwígarSearch Glad ThawSearch ImnagathClear spider webs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat spiders in Eryn Lasgalen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thranduil can be found in the halls of Felegoth.

You should return to Thranduil.Return to Thranduil'Upon returning to these halls after the trials of battle, I learned of something surpassingly strange: the spiders of these woods are no more. Not by us were they slain, nor by any hand, from all we can tell. They have simply vanished, it seems, leaving only their web-covered dens.

'Any person in my kingdom, be they Elf or not, who unravels this mystery shall earn my regard and generosity.''You have returned. Have you any news of the spiders' vanishing?'

You relate all you found.

'I see. Then they have truly left. Perhaps with Sauron's fall and the greening of these woods, they seek refuge in darker places?

'If there be naught left but broodling stragglers, we should concern ourselves with more pressing matters.'<rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers.</rgb><rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers</rgb>Complete Weekly Conquest: Allegiance QuestsComplete Weekly Conquest: ScourgesComplete Weekly Conquest: Resource InstancesComplete Weekly Conquest: Group InstancesThere is still much work to be done in Gorgoroth! Report to your Allegiance Hall to find out how you can help.Expedition Organizers can be found in Allegiance Halls. They are Vórthi, Glórellas, Tancram, and Aster Hayward.

You should talk to your Expedition Organizer.Reach Level 115Travel to the Udûn FootholdTravel to the Ruins of DíngarthTravel to Magh AshtuTravel to an Allegiance HallExpedition Organizers can be found in Allegiance Halls. They are Vórthi, Glórellas, Tancram, and Aster Hayward.

You should talk to your Expedition Organizer.Talk to your Expedition OrganizerThere is still much work to be done in Gorgoroth! The Allegiances of the Free-peoples seek adventurers to continue the work of gaining control of the region. Report to your Allegiance's Expedition Organizer to find out how you can help.'Greetings. There is still much work ahead of us, and I have been assigned to determine areas most in need of your assistance. Report to me each day for a new expedition assignment. You can then visit the Bounty Boards for specific tasks, though the boards here will only have tasks for that day's Expedition. If you wish to do more, you can find Bounty Boards in each region of Gorgoroth with additional threats.'While Sírithon sends news of the theft to Felegoth and awaits assistance, there is much else to be done in Loeglond.You should assist the Raft-elves of Loeglond.

<rgb=#FF0000>The Trail of Rust quest arc will continue after completing the Reopening the Realm quest arc which begins with Thranduil in Felegoth.</rgb><rgb=#FF0000>Complete the Reopening the Realm quest arc to continue</rgb>You should talk to Thorlind, who can be found in Loeglond.Talk to Thorlind in LoeglondYou should travel to Esgaroth-of-old, which is located east of Loeglond.Travel to Esgaroth-of-oldThe voices were coming from somewhere in the ruins of Esgaroth-of-old, upon the Long Lake.

You should investigate the voices, listening when you can and defeating any foes to protect yourself and keep the way safe.Defeat foes at Esgaroth-of-old (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Thorlind, who can be found just outside of Esgaroth-of-old.Talk to Thorlind at Esgaroth'I will send word to Felegoth. Truly, I know not whether aid will come. As of late, what with the closures in trade and whisperings among the rafts of this and that - yet I will not stoop to gossip.

'I must send word and wait. While I do, perhaps you might continue assisting Lorndúr?''Greetings - ${PLAYER}, is it not? Word reached Felegoth of a thief or thieves troubling Loeglond and the strange trail you uncovered. Though many cares and duties attend the chief hunter of the Woodland Realm, I set them all aside to look into this myself.

'Has any new revelation come to light regarding the stolen items or the shadows who fled into Esgaroth? You have driven some thieves from out of the Esgaroth ruins?'

You tell him that the stolen supplies have yet to be recovered.

'Then am I still needed here? I see.

'Will you be my fellow tracker in this case? I will survey the ground from here to Esgaroth-of-old for signs and you will head directly there and learn all you can of what goes on in that place.''The remnants of the trail I found confirm your and Sírithon's tale, but tell no new ones. And what have you learned?'

Thorlind listens attentively.

'It would seem those in the ruins are hired hands and not the minds behind all this. You may have driven them out, only to be forced to return once again. Do they truly intend to dredge up Smaug's bones? Whatever for, I cannot imagine, but it is for some ill design, surely!'Exchange crafted items for Motes.Craft worthy items and exchange them for Motes of Enchantment at a Mote and Ember Trader in Bree, Galtrev, the Twenty-first Hall, or Mózhek near the Stable-master in Skarháld.'Are you handy at crafting things, ${RACE}? I will trade you Motes of Enchantment for them, as my rations allow.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest can be turned in once per week.</rgb>'Are you handy at crafting things, ${RACE}? I will trade you Motes of Enchantment for them, as my rations allow.'

