You should talk to Dagrun.Talk to Dagrun'Let us give them some space to take the victims away.''It seems everyone else has already arrived! Let us join them at the dinner table. And do be on your best behaviour.''Oh my, he did not seem well. Will you go check on him?'Hoskuld lies on the floor, unmoving, his face a deep maroon colour. You lean in closer and realize he is not breathing!Háta lies on the floor. You see red marks around her neck. She has been strangled!'He is dead? How could this have happened?''Háta has been strangled?! Hoskuld's death could not have been an accident then. We are locked inside this manor with a murderer....'Maeriel lies slumped on the floor. She has been stabbed through the back with a carving knife!'Maeriel too... we must stay together now, ${PLAYER}. It is too easy for the fiend to end us one by one if we separate.'Thewár looks to have several broken bones from the fall, but the back of his head is bloody, as if struck by a heavy object. He is dead!'I fear this will only become easier for the murderer as our numbers thin. Where is Orgrand? I realize I have not seen him in quite a while.'Orgrand seems disoriented and barely notices you approach.

'What... what happened? I think someone struck me... Sútvif?'You tell Captain Álarr all that has transpired.

'The murderer must be someone present. There was no way into the manor from the outside. I no longer believe Skótmun was responsible for the Master's death either. Who had the most to gain by these deaths? It must be someone who would be in the running for the position; one of these four. I know it was not you, ${PLAYER}. You have been involved in the investigation from the beginning! Tell me who did this.''Tell me, ${PLAYER}, how did these three behave in this time of crisis?'

You tell the Elder of Orgrand's cowardice, Frekur's belligerence, and how Dagrun remained calm and collected while taking leadership.

'I see. It is just as I thought. I will go and speak to the other Elders. Wait here.''Master! I've been named Master! I wish it had not been in such gruesome circumstances. I think I'm in shock.''Fools, all of them! Panicking is not the solution here. Maeriel is a friend, however, and I do worry about her. Will you please go check on her?'A man of Lake-town has offered you information on Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, and agreed to meet you inside the Dragonbone Inn.Fasti is inside the Dragonbone Inn, waiting to show you to a table where you can wait for Kólfith.Talk to Fasti inside the Dragonbone InnFasti is speaking with the owner of the tavern in order to arrange a table for you.Follow Fasti to your tableFasti told you to wait for Kólfith to arrive.Wait inside the Dragonbone Inn for Kólfith to arriveYou should return to your table in the Dragonbone Inn.Return to your table by the fireA dwarf has come to your table in the Dragonbone Inn.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Dragonbone InnKólfith has come to the Dragonbone Inn, but his friendly demeanor has fallen away and he seems eager for a fight.Remain alert for signs of troubleKólfith has come to the Dragonbone Inn, but his friendly demeanor has fallen away and he seems eager for a fight.Talk to Kólfith in the Dragonbone InnKólfith has come to the Dragonbone Inn, seeking to punish you for asking about Karazgar.Survive the confrontation in the Dragonbone Inn<rgb=#FF0000>Authi Gem-cutter must not be defeated</rgb>Ránnvig, the proprietor of the Dragonbone Inn, has some words for you.Talk to Ránnvig in the Dragonbone InnAuthi Gem-cutter is in the Dragonbone Inn, and he wishes to speak to you.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Dragonbone Inn'I always have time for a good brawl and a refreshing ale! Let us speak outside, ${PLAYER}. I have something I'd like to ask you.''I am sure Kólfith will be along to speak with you shortly, ${PLAYER}. There are two taverns in Lake-town, but the Dragonbone Inn is always full of patrons; the noise makes it most well-suited to conducting discreet business.'This patron of the Dragonbone Inn has had more than his share and pays you no mind.This man of Lake-town spends most of his days in the Dragonbone Inn, deep in his cups. He has no information for you, or if he does he is in no condition to share it with you.'You aren't from around here, are you? Keep to yourself and cause no trouble, and you should have no problems in Lake-town. I have lived here all my life, and I come often to the Dragonbone Inn for Ránnvig's finest ale.

'Why, it has been almost an entire week since the last brawl! Time passes swiftly in safety, I suppose.''Lake-town has always been good to me. It is not an easy life, but if you work hard the Lake will provide. Years ago it was different. You know when I mean: the days of the Dragon. But he is long gone, and only his bones remain.

'I raise a mug to his ferocity, and to the memory of those good folk who lost their lives to it!'The dwarf ignores you. He seems content to sit and take in the scenery, keeping to himself.'There are many forces at work in Lake-town, friend. You had best keep to yourself or you might get hurt. Most of the folk here are good people.'

The man shares a look with his companion before turning back to face you again.

'Most,' he says.'You should keep moving. We don't need your company today.''Have you seen the bones of the Dragon, stranger? They are hard to miss, for even a cursory glance will reveal their blanched horror, and none could mistake them for anything but the legacy of their cruel owner, no longer living.

'We were just discussing what has caused their emergence from the Lake. My wife believes that soil and other deposits washed downstream have collected beneath the buildings of Old Lake-town, raising both the pilings and the Dragon's bones above the water.

'I think the Lake cannot bear to hold Smaug's malice any longer, and belches him forth, revealing him to the sun and to the stars. What say you?''Smaug has been dead these many years! I say let him lie, and forget him, even if the Lake will not!''Bah, nothing but fanciful imaginings. There are no more dragons about, no matter what the old folk may say.

'Drink up, stranger, and pay no heed to such stories!''Ránnvig has been a good friend to my family for many years. Do not cause any trouble for her and we will get along fine, stranger.''Hello there, friend. I hope you do not mind my intrusion, but I have not seen you at the Dragonbone Inn before today, and it seems to me you must have an interesting story or two to tell! This is my third-favourite drinking spot, and I never miss an opportunity to sample the local brew whenever chance brings me through Lake-town, as it has today. Another opportunity I never miss: learning more about the folk who cross my path, and the adventures that led them to cross it!'

The dwarf laughs heartily and strokes his beard.

'I am called Authi Gem-cutter, and I am at your service, of course! What brings you to the Dragonbone Inn? Men and women are no rarity in Dale and Lake-town, but I can tell from your dress and bearing that you have passed through many lands to come here. Which is the furthest, would you say?'

You consider your travels and hazard a guess. Authi nods wisely.

'That is quite a ways, friend! I am something of a traveller myself, for it runs in my family, but I take most joy in the fashioning of complex trinkets and the improvement of my handiwork. Of late I have been less successful, I'm afraid, and it is not just my own craft that suffers!''This town is built upon the Lake, ${PLAYER}, and the water rises and falls around it. There is an order to this place. If that balance is upset, Lake-town suffers. You should know your place here, ${PLAYER}. It's dangerous to ask questions about Karazgar here. You might not like the answers.''It is long past time someone stood up to Kólfith. He has been a source of much trouble for the patrons of my inn, and I am glad he has been put in his place. He will not dare to show his face around here for some time, and when he does I wager I will have no trouble finding folk to show him the door.

'Is it true he worked for Karazgar? That explains much about his dealings and about the company he has kept. Good riddance to him!

'You, however, will always be welcome at the Dragonbone Inn, ${PLAYER}!''That was an invigorating fight, ${PLAYER}, and all the better because I did not expect it! Perhaps I must elevate this tavern one place in my personal ranking of drinking spots; now it is my second-favourite, and not my third!'King Thorin III has planned a great celebration for the Dwarves of Durin's Folk.King Thorin III is standing before his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.

