She waves a piece of paper in front of you, with the claims of her kinship with Othur written out on it.

'Please, I ask you to track down those who seek to wrongly discredit me and tear down as many of these awful notices as you can find!'You have defeated Utûkel and put a stop to the slanderous gossip in Lake-town. Seeing the same pile of rust in both Esgaroth-of-old and the underbelly of Lake-town has made you wonder if there is some connexion between the two groups of evil men operating in the Dale-lands. It would seem this Akshir, whoever he is, may be behind it all!There are many doors in Udûn, most of them locked tight. Reports indicate there might be powerful foes behind some of them.Locked doors can be found in many places in the valley of Udûn.

You should attempt to open these doors and defeat whatever creatures lurk inside.Attempt to open Locked Doors (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A notice is pinned to the board, stamped with the royal seal of Gondor:

'Our scouts have discovered many locked doors in the valley of Udûn. They have reported hearing Orkish-sounding creatures talking inside. An adventurer is needed to clear out the creatures hiding out inside these doors. Beware: Some of Ugrukhôr's lieutenants are still unaccounted for.'Scout the major routes and vantage points of Mordor's antechamber, Udûn.Scout the major routes and vantage points of Mordor's antechamber, Udûn.Discover the Iron RoadDiscover the west road to AnglachDiscover the parade groundsDiscover the road to MornaurDiscover the west road to DurthangDiscover the Durthang tower-topDiscover the Durthang look-outDiscover Durthang's eastern stairsDiscover the path to Rhûd VornDiscover the Rhûd Vorn watch pointDiscover the Rogkalúr overlookDiscover the Anglach vantage pointDiscover the overseer's perchDiscover the gates of the IsenmoutheDiscover the foundry deckErna is worried about her son Bryn, who has become increasingly distant and sullen.Bryn can be found in the Great-keep in Dale.

You should tell Prince Bryn stories of your adventures.Tell Bryn a <rgb=#FF0000>Story</rgb> of your adventuresBryn fled to the south in Dale.

Look for a guard on the southern bridge of Dale to see if he has stopped Bryn.Look for a guard on the southern bridgeTalk to the guard on the southern bridge out of Dale and find out if he has seen Bryn.Talk to the southern bridge guard in DaleTalk to the guard on the southern bridge out of Dale.Talk to the southern bridge guard in DaleYou should speak with both King Bard II and Prince Bryn, who can be found in the Great-keep in Dale.Talk to King Bard IITalk to Prince BrynYou should speak with Queen Regent Erna, who can be found in the Great-keep in Dale.Talk to ErnaErna draws you near and speaks in a hushed tone.

'I would speak with you of Bryn, ${PLAYER}. You have seen that he is sullen and distant, but this was not always so. The death of his father and, I doubt not, kingship of his brother have been hard upon the boy.

'Do you recall when I asked you to gather the items for my lecture? I found Bryn where I knew I would find him - outside the walls of Dale. He has gone out much of late, and the danger in doing so is grave. I think, perhaps, that he dreams of the wider world and adventures a young person might have there.

'Would you talk to him of your own adventures? I do not know, yet I hope that may allay some of his restlessness.''He crossed the bridge just now. If you hurry, I am sure you will be able to catch up with him.''I am glad to hear that my brother is no longer in danger. It will... take longer to forgive him, but I am sure that I shall. Much like the rebuilding of Dale and of Lake-town. It is hard work, and long, but we know it must be done.''It is hard to believe that my brother has forgiven me... or that he ever shall. I even told him about the knife. Now I must do all I can to make it up to him. Ah, what a fool I was! It is bitter, and I am ashamed, but I must hide from unpleasant truths no longer.''The prince? No, I...'

The guard sighs.

'No, I speak the lie. He did come this way, and paid me not to tell a soul. I was a fool to accept the bribe, and will pay a steeper price, I know. But you must go after the prince, and see that no harm comes to him. That is the greater matter!''You have unravelled one more troubling scheme, ${PLAYER} - one that ran perilously close to my own blood. For that, I thank you.

'And yet... and yet...'Learn how the folk of Dale feel about newly-arrived Easterling refugees.Talk to folk in Dale about the Easterling refugees.Return to Dale.Hear what's going on in DaleYou should go to the location where you heard shouting, in the lower markets, in the south-eastern quarter of Dale.Find the cause of the shoutingThe Dale-men have encircled the unarmed Easterling man in the lower markets, in the south-east of Dale.

You should persuade the Dale-men to leave the Easterling man alone.You should talk to the Easterling refugee, whom you rescued in the south-eastern quarter of Dale.Talk to the Easterling ManYou should return to King Bard II, who can be found in the Great-keep in the north of Dale, and bring word of all you saw and heard among the Dale-folk.Return to King Bard II'We are most pleased to welcome you to Dale, ${PLAYER}. Many roads and many peoples find their way hither, and with them, of late, come the most remarkable rumours. It is said that the Dark Lord has fallen in Mordor and that Gondor has a King!'

The young man glances hastily back at the woman beside him, as if afraid he has said too much. You assure him that the rumours are true, and his eyes widen.

'You are well-spoken and worldly-wise. There is a goodness in your eyes. Might I... ask you to help serve Dale in a matter most suited to your virtues?

'We have lately weathered our own assault from the Dark Lord's forces, in the form of the warlike Jangovar, coming out of the east. Their strength was broken, though our losses were great and a remnant clings to this valley, intent on harrying what they cannot conquer. But this is not the matter of which I spoke.

'After the Jangovar, other Easterlings appeared. These bore not armour but rags, not arms or battle-standards but meagre satchels with scant belongings. They came scared, and hungry, and speaking of strange and terrible tidings to the east. They have gathered in some small camps in the wilderness outside Dale and I wish to welcome them into our gates... but the Folk of Dale have endured much in recent days, and I would know their hearts before I act. I fear that if I or my known agents asked among them, they would tell us what we wished to hear, not the truth of their hearts.

'Would you go among the Dale-folk and hear their true thoughts on these new Easterlings who have come to our doors, seeking refuge?''Please, will you go among the Dale-folk and hear their thoughts about the refugees?''Asking a foreigner to talk to our townsfolk about other foreigners? How does that help, brother?''Those poor bedraggled souls? I have seen them and seen the sorrow on their faces. My grandmam held me on her knee and told of the sorrow on the faces of our own people during the Desolation of Smaug... and the hope we felt when first we returned here to Dale.

'If you ask me, we ought to do all we can to offer them comfort and shelter.''Do you know how our battle against the Jangovar was won? I do, for I was there.

'In truth, we thought ourselves outmatched. Dale was taken and our dear king, Brand, slain, and the King of Dwarves as well. And yet we fought on, for what else could we do? So there we were, readying ourselves for death with our swords in our hands, when the ground began shaking and the sky changed, and lo! An Eagle came and it spoke glad tidings to the Good Peoples of Middle-earth.

'The enemy then quailed before us and we pressed our advantage, driving them before us in a great rout.

'What do I think of these refugees? That if we treat our guests and supplicants poorly, we are no better than evil Men, and I had rather belong to the good!''The refugees? I do not know whether they mean well or ill, but many among us Dale-folk trust not these new Easterlings. Not in such numbers, nor so soon after the Jangovar assault!''I have travelled far and wide, and traded many times with the Men of Dorwinion. Many of those refugees who arrived in rags at our gates are from that Eastern land, and I know them to be Women and Men of principles, learning, and laws just as we are.

'But among the refuge-seekers are others, the Chayasír, and I have never done trade with these. They are wanderers, I am told, with no writing, building, or craft.''What do you know of the Chayasír, who are among these new-arrived Easterlings? Here is what I have heard: that they themselves claim they dwelled here by the Long Lake once, and have returned to claim it as their own.

'Even if their tales be true, have we not held this place, built it and rebuilt it again through plagues, dragons, and Jangovar swords?

'Let them try and claim it. They'll get nought so long as Dale stands and thrives!''These Easterlings come seeking shelter and aid, yet why have they come to us? Are we not ourselves beleaguered from war and strife?

'We have hardly patched our walls and gates, which the Jangovar shattered. Jangovar raiders linger yet in the wilder corners of the valley, sapping off our trade and slowing the commerce we'll have to rebuild.

'You cannot help someone up, I say, while you yourself are on your hands and knees!''These refugees who have come out of the East speak of ruin in their homelands, but I say it is retribution for siding with the Dark Lord against the Free Peoples.

'Let them go back from whence they came and suffer their due!''They say they come in peace, but I know what these Easterlings are: nothing but a new Jangovar ruse! Behind every tattered cloak you will find sharpened swords and shining mail.

'They wait only 'til we welcome them into Dale, when they will draw their blades and cut our throats!''Please, I mean no harm! Can you persuade them to leave me be?''I thank you, stranger. I knew that I took a risk upon myself when I ventured into Dale, but the need was great.

'I thought to myself, is this not known to the ends of all roads as a place of trade, yes even as far as my land of Dorwinion? And am I not myself a man of trade and tallies? There is hunger and need among the camps, I thought, and so I will find what has value that cannot be eaten or worn, and trade it for what can.

'Yet alas, I have found no fellow merchants, only ill-will. Now I fear I must turn back and find some other way. There will be another way, I am certain. We have come leagues upon leagues from our lands and survived greater travails than these.'The young King's face darkens and emotions struggle unconcealed across it as you describe all you saw and heard among his folk. He looks to you, then back to his mother in doubt.

Before he can speak, a messenger appears, bowing low.The saplings Radagast has been cultivating are ready for replanting.Saplings can be found just south of Felegoth.

You should gather saplings that are to be planted around the forest.Gather saplings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Fertile soil and dead trees can both be found throughout Eryn Lasgalen.

You should clear away the dead trees and plant the saplings in fertile soil around the forest.Clear dead trees (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Radagast, who can be found at the tree nursery just outside of Felegoth.Return to Radagast'The time has come to plant these saplings which have been growing strong under my care. Will you help me by gathering the saplings and planting them within the forest? You may need to clear some of the dead trees, which are too far gone. Such is nature's way. 

'A wood of greenleaves is upon us, my friend. We need but put our elbows into it to see it through.''The trees were planted well? Good, good! I knew I could count on you. Really, you must come and visit me in Rhosgobel one of these days!'The Taurogrim are a destructive force, poisoning wildlife and destroying plants as they spread.Scarred trees can be found near Tholkát in Eryn Lasgalen.

You should heal scarred trees with the salve given to you by Thranduil.Spread healing salve on scarred trees (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thranduil can be found in Felegoth.

You should talk to Thranduil.Talk to Thranduil'With each new foray into Eryn Lasgalen, the Taurogrim work to ravage the land. They slaughter the beasts of the forest and defile the trees. 

'If you discover any trees bearing the scars of their wrath, spread this salve upon them. It will not undo the hurts of the Taurogrim, but they will heal in time.''I have experienced great loss in my time, but such needless destruction cannot go unanswered. The Taurogrim must know that my people are the true masters of Eryn Lasgalen.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}.'Shipments from Felegoth have not been making their way down the Forest River to Loeglond. Lorndúr believes someone is stealing them and would like you to investigate.River nets can be found along the Forest River between Loeglond and Felegoth.

You should destroy river nets.Destroy river nets (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lorndúr can be found in Loeglond.

You should talk to Lorndúr.Talk to Lorndúr'Shipments from Felegoth have not been making it to Loeglond. I believe someone may be stealing them out of the Forest River before they reach us. Can I ask you to investigate? Travel along the Forest River from here to Felegoth and see what you find.'Lorndúr listens as you describe the nets you found along the river.

'Those do not sound like the crude nets I would expect Orcs to make, ${PLAYER}. Someone else is behind this. Thank you for allowing trade to continue to flow down the river.'The fate of Thikil-gundu remains uncertain, but your timely intervention has banished the evils that sought shelter in the halls of the Steel Keep.The fate of Thikil-gundu remains uncertain, but your timely intervention has banished the evils that sought shelter in the halls of the Steel Keep.Lead the charge in defeating the evils lingering in Thikil-gunduIn the wake of Vethúg Wintermind's defeat at Erebor, Nori set forth with a small company to reclaim the lost hoards of Thikil-gundu. However, great wealth is not all that remains hidden in its frozen halls, and the Dwarves of the North shall soon have to contend with the true master of Thikil-gundu.Complete 'Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep (Tier 1)'In the wake of Vethúg Wintermind's defeat at Erebor, Nori set forth with a small company to reclaim the lost hoards of Thikil-gundu. However, great wealth is not all that remains hidden in its frozen halls, and the Dwarves of the North shall soon have to contend with the true master of Thikil-gundu.Complete 'Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep (Tier 2)'In the wake of Vethúg Wintermind's defeat at Erebor, Nori set forth with a small company to reclaim the lost hoards of Thikil-gundu. However, great wealth is not all that remains hidden in its frozen halls, and the Dwarves of the North shall soon have to contend with the true master of Thikil-gundu.Complete 'Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep (Tier 3)'Sigrun knows of Thorog and will lead the way to examine the dragon's corpse.You should speak with Sigrun, who can be found in the Misty Mountains.Talk to SigrunYou should speak with Sigrun, who can be found in the Misty Mountains.Talk to Sigrun'You seek the dragon Thorog? Ah ha ha ha ha! You are a bit late, my friend. The undead worm was soundly defeated by a group of adventurers. If you wish to see where they won their glory and Thorog fell, I will show you.

'And you have come from the Lonely Mountain, have you not? In return for this favour, you will answer the many questions I have of the dwarves who dwell there, as we go.''Come, we climb the slopes to see some dragon bones!''I hope your journey here was worth the effort. It would seem you obtained what you were looking for, did you not?'The Taurogrim have taken over an area of Eryn Lasgalen called Tholkát. Thorlind has asked you to defeat them so their evil cannot spread.Taurogrim wood-trolls can be found in Tholkát, in northern Eryn Lasgalen.

You should defeat wood-trolls in Tholkát.Defeat Taurogrim in Tholkát (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thorlind can be found near Tholkát.

You should talk to Thorlind.Talk to Thorlind'Though we have defeated one of their leaders, the Taurogrim still swarm these pine barrens. We have truly come to the heart of this evil and must purge these abominations before they threaten Felegoth. Enter Tholkát and defeat as many Taurogrim as you can before their numbers can overwhelm us.''Thank you for your help, ${CLASS}. I will be more vigilant in my watch of our realm so the Taurogrim can never again return.'Arcah, the raven-chief, has summoned her kin back to Ravenhill, but a few of her ravens have not answered her call.Arcah can be found at Ravenhill.

After learning that some of Arcah's ravens have begun to return from their spying, King Thorin III has asked you to seek the counsel of Arcah at Ravenhill.Talk to ArcahThe missing ravens can likely be found in the heights of Eryn Lasgalen and Dale or somewhere near the banks of the River Running.

After they each failed to answer her call, Arcah has asked you to seek three ravens in the lands of the North and send them to Ravenhill.Arcah can be found at Ravenhill.

You found the ravens who failed to answer Arcah's call and should now return to her with these tidings.Talk to Arcah at RavenhillThe missing raven would have flown from the Grey Mountains, so it can likely be found somewhere among the north-western slopes of Erebor.

After it failed to return from spying in the Grey Mountains, Arcah has asked you to seek a lost raven on the slopes of Erebor.Find the raven that spied upon the Grey MountainsThe raven who spied the Grey Mountains can be found upon the western slopes of Erebor.

You have found the lost raven and should now examine it.Examine the wounded ravenArcah can be found at Ravenhill.

You were able to rescue the raven that spied upon the Grey Mountains. You should now return to Arcah.Talk to Arcah at RavenhillKing Thorin III can be found before his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.

You have learned that Vethúg Wintermind has stirred in the Grey Mountains and now prepares to fly for Erebor. You should inform King Thorin III at once.Talk to King Thorin III'I am glad you have returned, ${PLAYER}.

'I received word from Dís that Arcah's ravens have begun to return to Ravenhill, and I would like to know what they have learned concerning the plots of Karazgar and Vethúg Wintermind. Alas, there are matters I must attend to here in Erebor or I would make the journey myself!

'Will you go to Ravenhill and seek the counsel of Arcah on my behalf? I trust you will recount every detail to me upon your return!''Three of my people were lost, and three are now found.

'If you would hear my counsel, you must now seek the fourth. I sent him to spy upon the Grey Mountains, and it will be his account that determines the course of the folk of Durin.

'You should begin your search on the slopes of Erebor. If some trouble has befallen him, he will have flown there.'To your relief, it appears the raven was only left stunned by a recent encounter with a foe, but escaped before a more serious wound could be inflicted.

As you move to aid the raven, it lets out a bubbling croak in fear!'You have done well to rescue my people, ${PLAYER}, but now I ask that you hear my counsel.

'I shall tell what I have learned, and you would be wise to listen. There are strange faces come to the Iron Hills; alike yet different. I do not think they shall play a role in these dealings, but that remains to be seen. Karazgar moves silently under the eaves of the forest that was Mirkwood, but he has not travelled far. Whatever wound he once bore no longer burdens him. The cold-drake named Vethúg has stirred in the old halls of the great folk of Durin. The gates of the Steel Keep are open, and a bitter cold has swept over the Grey Mountains. He shall soon fly for Erebor, and it is then Durin's Folk shall be tested. The Mountain is wide enough to shelter all, but there must remain those who will take up arms.

'The wisdom of King Thorin and those he trusts must now decide your course. I have spoken.''Ah, ${PLAYER}. I did not expect to see you this day, but it has proven to be one of many curiosities.

'I called my people to return to the hill, but four have not yet returned from their spying. One among them flew the furthest abroad, but the other three did not have such a journey ahead or behind them. We are fewer in number still than we were in the days of the last king to bear Thorin's name, and I grow concerned on their behalf.

'Perhaps they did not hear my call, or perhaps they were discovered by unfriendly eyes. One flew into the forest that was Mirkwood, another to the Iron Hills, and another among the Dale-lands.

'If King Thorin seeks the sight and wisdom of the ravens, you must do this. Seek out my people and send them to the hill. It is then you shall learn what you seek!''It has been quite some time, ${PLAYER}. What tidings do you bring? What did Arcah's ravens discover?'

You recount Arcah's telling of what her people spied in Eryn Lasgalen, Dale, the Iron Hills, and the Grey Mountains. You warn King Thorin that Vethúg Wintermind prepares to fly from the Steel Keep in the Grey Mountains, and that Karazgar still plots some evil from the shadows of Eryn Lasgalen.

'Arcah is of the great ravens, and so, I do not doubt her wisdom. Vethúg will soon come to Erebor, and I shall make certain that the other folk of this realm are protected from his wrath.'


