You should go inform Spall the Lore-master that King Thorin wishes to speak with him.Fetch Spall the Lore-masterYou should return to King Thorin in Erebor now that you have summoned Spall the Lore-master.Return to King Thorin'How glad I am you are here, ${PLAYER}. The war is done and the dust settled, yet there is such a wagging of tongues in my halls that you would think the mountain was falling in on itself!

'The talk runs eastward to the Iron Hills, mostly, but that is as much as I can make of it. My displeasure at such gossip is known in this court, and so they hide it from me.

'They hold no such fear of you, however, and would be eager to pour their rubbish into a fresh ear. Will you go about my hall and learn what you can? This tangle of tongues must be settled, and I cannot see how to do so if I cannot even learn what they are saying!''Will you go about my hall and learn what you can? I must settle these rampant rumours before they drive me mad.''The latest word from the Iron Hills is this: that the Easterlings only feigned retreat, gathering their forces anew just beyond our ken. Then, not two days ago, under cloak of night they flooded the Ironfold and hold it in complete blockade.

'That is why we have had no other word from our brethren there. Dale is all but lost and we ourselves must prepare to seal our gates.''Have you heard of the wounds Lord Náin took in the battle? He bears an agate eye now, and a gold ear beside. They say he sits in the Járnfast keep in the Iron Hills now, mired in despair, mourning his wounds and his lost comrades, and that he has ordered the gates sealed and no dwarves to come or go.

'Now strangers from the south, Men, yea, and dwarves too, wander the Ironfold as they will.''Lord Náin's new steel teeth, it is said, bedevil him with phantom pains. His false eye, too, aches in the dead of night and he cannot sleep. He has fallen into an eternal black mood, or so I have heard, and has ordered the north-eastern road in the Iron Hills walled off, and the stair at Skald's Drop broken so that it may no longer be traversed.''Did you feel the earth tremble the day the fighting ended? I can feel it as if it were moments ago, though more than a month has passed.

'Well, and some say the shaking was far more severe to the east in the Iron Hills. Some even say that Járnfast keep fell to the ground, if you can believe it!''If any dwarf tries to tell you that Járnfast fell when the ground shook the Iron Hills, it is nothing but a vicious lie. I have the truth, on good authority, too, and it is this: the keep stands, but something below that was sealed away has been freed, and the Dwarves of the Ironfold have all fled in terror, every one.''In the land before the Iron Hills lies the small Dalish township of Utterby. If rumours are to be believed, Easterlings of all stripes have taken up there, pillaging and striking terror into all who travel through.''There are strange whispers these days... whispers of strange dwarves arriving in the Iron Hills. They come from the east, it is said, and have neither beard nor hair on the tops of their heads. Their ears face behind instead of ahead.

'Furthermore, when asked about good news, they always lie - but on subjects of bad news, tell ever the truth. Or so the whispers say. I know not. I have not set foot beyond the Lonely Mountain since the fighting began.''Rumours? Yes, I have heard rumours, but I am beyond my wits knowing which to believe. The Iron Hills are overrun, they say. The Iron Hills are blockaded. Lord Náin is dead - or he is mad - or he has second sight through his agate eye!

'Or the lot of it is nonsense and idle talk. Unless it is not.

'What someone really ought to do is go out there and settle all these rumours for once and for all. Then we should have some peace. Or at least a little certainty!'You relate the rumours you heard and Thorin listens intently. After you finish, he thinks for a long moment and then speaks.

'From my own people, I have heard seven wastes of breath and one single, half-sensible thought. Would you be a good ${CLASS} once more and go tell Spall the Lore-master that he should come and stand before me?''Find Spall the Lore-master, if you will, and tell him to come before me. He can be found midway down the court, I believe.''Me? I - before King Thorin? Oh, I must surely have stuck my foot in it now. Stuck my foot in what? ${PLAYER}, I cannot imagine. Some rumour about me has spread. Surely that is it. I would be the last to hear. Well, I suppose I shall hear it now.

'Gather your wits, Spall! You are a proud Lore-master. Not to mention, you have hardly done anything wrong.

'Ahem! Very well. I - I shall be there directly, I suppose.''What rumours do you think they have told about me, ${PLAYER}? If I am blamed for the ale spilled on that scroll, why, I shall have no choice but to out Mágsal as the true cause.

'But what am I doing? We are before the king. Look proud, Spall, and wear a grave face. There we have it.''You may tell King Thorin I will be there directly. And face - whatever slander has been spoken of me with my head up.''Ah, there you  are Spall, ${PLAYER}. Please, be at your ease. There is a matter about which I wish to speak.'With the Jangovar plot ended, the Easterling refugees need somewhere to go. You must seek an audience with King Bard and Queen Erna to solve their plight.Tuhâl is travelling to Dale to meet with Queen Erna and King Bard.

You should meet Tuhâl in Dale.Meet Tuhâl in DaleQueen Erna and King Bard can be found in Dale.

You should talk to Queen Erna.Talk to Queen ErnaQueen Erna and King Bard can be found in Dale.

You should talk to Queen Erna.Talk to King BardThe King has decided to set aside land for the Easterling refugees!

You should discuss the good news with Tuhâl!Talk to Tuhâl'If Lord Náin cannot help us, we must make a plea to the King in Dale. Do you know this King Bard?'

You tell Tuhâl about the young King Bard and his mother Queen Regent Erna.

'A boy King? Interesting. Perhaps they will be more willing to hear my plea. Let us travel to Dale, and speak with this King and his mother. I will gather what refugees I can and meet you there.''Hello again, ${PLAYER}. You are always welcome in Dale. What can I do for you?'

You tell Erna of the situation in the Ironfold: of how the Easterling refugees who are no longer under the rule of the Jangovar would like to stay in the village of Utterby, but need permission from the Kingdom of Dale. They have no home to return to, and few options left. Erna sees Tuhâl standing behind you, and studies his face carefully. She sees the desperation and exhaustion in his eyes. He carries a heavy burden.

'I feel for their plight, ${PLAYER}. Whatever has happened in Rhûn, Dale will welcome those who come to us hospitably. However, there are Dalish families waiting to return to their homes in Utterby. It would be unjust, nay, impossible for us to simply hand over the village to the Easterlings. Unfortunately, we have already taken in many Dorwinion and Chayasír refugees here in Dale, and I am unsure we can accept any more. I will leave this decision to my son, the King.''I have listened well to your conversation with Moth-, I mean, the Queen Regent. As she said, we cannot justly offer Utterby to the refugees when there are Dale-folk waiting to return to their homes, but I also cannot bear to turn away those that need our aid. There must be a solution here....'

You notice Queen Erna watching over her son, smiling proudly.

'I have made my decision. Utterby will be returned to the Men and Women of Dale who used to call it home. We cannot support any more refugees here in the town of Dale, however, land in the hills west of here will be set aside for their use. They are welcome to stay in our lands so long as they obey our laws and keep the peace. We will provide aid to our friends from the east until they can establish themselves. That is the best we can do.'Tuhâl looks to be on the verge of tears.

'A new home in this beautiful land! The living will be rough, at least for a while, and much hard work lies ahead of us, but this is the hope my people needed! I will discuss the specifics with the King and Queen, and then return to the Ironfold to gather the refugees and spread the word.

'I cannot thank you enough, ${PLAYER}. Had it not been for your kindness, we would either still be slaves to the Jangovar, or slain at the hands of the dwarves. I will make sure the Chayasír tell your story for years to come!''I am sorry, ${PLAYER}, but my courage fails me! This land; the cobblestone streets, the river flowing into the glittering lake below, the majesty of Erebor! I am overwhelmed by the beauty of this place! Please, will you speak to the King and Queen on my behalf? You are known to them, yes?'When the earth shook following Sauron's defeat, great tremors came to the Emyn Engrin and now the River Carnen spills into the halls of Járnfast.Leaks can be found throughout Járnfast.

Holkur has asked you to aid the Dwarves of the Iron Hills in sealing leaks from the underground source of the River Carnen in Járnfast.Seal leaks in Járnfast (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have sealed many leaks within Járnfast.Completed'Do you hear that, ${CLASS}?

'It is the sound of the River Carnen... spouting into our halls! Ever since those tremors shook Járnfast, we have had leaks. They have appeared all about, and the water is red with rust. I have worked to seal them, but it is too great a task for one dwarf, I think!

'Will you aid me?'You have sealed many leaks and prevented the River Carnen from flooding Járnfast. As a result, the Dwarves of the Ironfold will be able to work unhindered to repair the halls of Járnfast.King Thorin has given you a task to complete in the Ironfold, and you will accompany Authi Gem-cutter with his own.Gandalf is in the room that was given to him in the Dwelling-halls, east of the Jewelstead and up several sets of stairs.Gandalf is in the Wizard-home, east of the Jewelstead and up several stairsAuthi planned to meet you outside the entrance to the Lonely Mountain after you prepared for the journey and said good-bye to Gandalf.Meet up with Authi and Khîl outside the entrance to the Lonely MountainKhîl Tale-spinner is at the entrance to the Lonely Mountain.Talk to Khîl outside the Lonely MountainAuthi Gem-cutter is at the entrance to the Lonely Mountain.Talk to Authi outside the Lonely MountainAuthi has suggested you help the people of the Ironfold as you travel to Járnfast.

<rgb=#00FF00>Complete any four of the objectives Authi has suggested as you travel through the Ironfold to Járnfast.</rgb>Visit Méthur, a friend of the Gem-cutters who lives in HammersteadFind an entrance to the town of UtterbyDefeat insects throughout the Ironfold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat beasts throughout the Ironfold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Easterlings throughout the Ironfold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Visit Arin, a friend of the Gem-cutters who lives in Járnfast but is likely fishing in the RedwaterThe entrance to Járnfast is in the north-east corner of the Ironfold.Travel to the entrance of Járnfast, in the IronfoldYou have successfully escorted Khîl to Járnfast and should now speak to him.Talk to Khîl at the entrance to JárnfastAuthi is at the entrance to Járnfast.Talk to Authi at the entrance to Járnfast'I will meet you outside the entrance to the Lonely Mountain after I have gathered the things I will need for the journey and said good-bye to my mother and my brother. It will not be long; we Gem-cutters are not especially sentimental.

'If you have any preparations to make before we set out for the Ironfold, I suggest that you make them! You might want to check in with Gandalf before we leave. I do not know him very well, but I know enough about Wizards to tell you they do not like surprises! Tell him we will be away for a few days, so if he has need of you he will know where you might be found.

'King Thorin ordered that a room be set aside for the Wizard somewhere in the Dwelling-halls. I believe you will find it east of here and up several stairs. I expect it was chosen to satisfy the well-known predilection of Wizards for tall towers, and will be quite high up!''If you have any preparations to make before we set out for the Ironfold, I suggest that you make them! You might want to check in with Gandalf before we leave. I do not know him very well, but I know enough about Wizards to tell you they do not like surprises! Tell him we will be away for a few days, so if he has need of you he will know where you might be found.

'King Thorin ordered that a room be set aside for the Wizard somewhere in the Dwelling-halls. I believe you will find it east of here and up several stairs. I expect it was chosen to satisfy the well-known predilection of Wizards for tall towers, and will be quite high up! I will meet you outside once you have spoken with Gandalf.''Ah, ${PLAYER}, there you are. I have been studying several historical accounts concerning the struggles of Durin's Folk. Many are the hardships they have faced, more than a few of their own making, but these accounts do not agree on all the particulars. Dragons feature prominently, as you might expect, and I see the hand of Karazgar at work in some of the events that befell the dwarves in this area.'

You tell Gandalf that King Thorin has requested you travel to Járnfast, in the Ironfold, and return to the Lonely Mountain with his son. Gandalf strokes his beard.

'He believes it is time for Durin to learn the ways of the king, does he? That is new. Thorin is stubborn, as are most of his family, and I thought his decision to keep Durin from harm or difficulty would last longer than this. Why the change, I wonder?

'Safe travels to you, ${PLAYER}. Keep one eye open for Karazgar or his agents while you travel in the Ironfold. I will see you when you return.''King Thorin believes it is time for his son to learn the ways of the king, does he? That is new. Thorin is stubborn, as are most of his family, and I thought his decision to keep Durin from harm or difficulty would last longer than this. Why the change, I wonder?

'Safe travels to you, ${PLAYER}. Keep one eye open for Karazgar or his agents while you travel in the Ironfold. I will see you when you return.''I have not been back to my father's home in Járnfast for many years. Were it not for the command of King Thorin, I would not return now! But I cannot remain here if the King Under the Mountain orders me to leave. Zhélruka do not answer to Longbeards, but I will not gainsay the word of a king.

'So we will be travelling companions for a short while, ${PLAYER}. I suppose I welcome the company, even if the son of Fúni Gem-cutter does not.''Well, I am here, despite my wish to be elsewhere. Surely Khîl could find his own way back to his father's old home, but you will not hear me speak crosswise to King Thorin's commands. We are to escort Khîl to Járnfast, and that we will do. I will only grumble about it a little, and beneath my beard.

'Mother Amma suggested that we make the most of the journey into the Ironfold, since the Gem-cutters do not often travel out that way. She has made a list of suggested activities for us to pursue on our way. "A Gem-cutter always seeks to make things better for those he meets!" she says, and I could not possibly argue with that. So her list consists of a few tasks she recommended we complete to keep the land and its people safe, as well as a number of family friends in the Ironfold I have not seen in some time that we might visit. I do not feel particularly strongly about which of these tasks we complete, so I leave the decision-making up to you, ${PLAYER}. Why not choose four of these errands to carry out on our way to Járnfast?

'No, Khîl, you do not get a say in the matter! This is your fault, and you're lucky we let you speak on this journey at all. You leave the decisions to me and to ${PLAYER}.'

<rgb=#00FF00>Complete any four of the objectives Authi has suggested as you travel through the Ironfold to Járnfast.</rgb>'Mother Amma suggested that we make the most of the journey into the Ironfold, since the Gem-cutters do not often travel out that way. She has made a list of suggested activities for us to pursue on our way. "A Gem-cutter always seeks to make things better for those he meets!" she says, and I could not possibly argue with that. So her list consists of a few tasks she recommended we complete to keep the land and its people safe, as well as a number of family friends in the Ironfold I have not seen in some time that we might visit. I do not feel particularly strongly about which of these tasks we complete, so I leave the decision-making up to you, ${PLAYER}. Why not choose four of these errands to carry out on our way to Járnfast?''It has been a long time since I have seen Authi! Why, it has been five years at the very least since I ventured to the Lonely Mountain. How fares your mother? When last I visited, she put me to work sifting through buckets of rock, separating tiny flakes of copper from the stones. "Gem-cutter house-guest rules!" she said. Perhaps that is why I have not visited since then!

'Ah, but it is good to catch up with friends. We have had a fair amount of trouble here in the Ironfold, but I understand the fighting was thickest in Dale and at the foot of the Mountain. The Easterling armies passed by Járnfast just after the gates were shut, but the Men of Utterby had no such sturdy door to close; the Jangovar sacked the town and some of them remain there, as far as I have heard.

'You keep Authi out of trouble, my friend. The worst of the fighting may be over, but nowhere is really safe, not these days.''You are fortunate you caught me, Authi! I was about to head back to the city, empty-handed I'm afraid. The fishing has been poor for a fortnight. I suspect the Easterlings are to blame in some measure, for a portion of the Jangovar came by here on their way west. I understand they joined more of their number who were coming up the Drop, but my work does not take me out by Utterby so I didn't see that with my own eyes.

'What brings you to the Ironfold, Authi? Your family is well, I hope!'

Authi and Arin spend a few minutes regaling each other with news of their families, and Arin promises his friend the pick of his next catch.

'I am sure I will have something for you too, ${PLAYER}, if the fish ever resume biting. Until next we meet, friends!''You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}, for coming with me to Járnfast. It has been many years since last I was here, before my father Bránit and I fell out of friendship. He died some time after that, before we were ever reconciled, and I saw no reason to return.

'I suppose now I should put his house in order. You and Authi are welcome to visit me there at any time; I would appreciate the company! My father's house is up a ramp opposite the entrance.''It has been many years since last I was here, before my father Bránit and I fell out of friendship. He died some time after that, before we were ever reconciled, and I saw no reason to return.

'I suppose now I should put his house in order. You and Authi are welcome to visit me there at any time; I would appreciate the company! My father's house is up a ramp opposite the entrance.''You may do as you like, ${PLAYER}, but I shall not be visiting Khîl... not unless my king commands me to do so!'You have completed the first part of your commitment to King Thorin, and now it is time to undertake the second portion of your responsibility: bringing word to his son Durin that it is time to return to the Lonely Mountain.Náin the Slakeless, Lord of the Ironfort, can be found at his throne atop the northern ramps.Find Náin the Slakeless, Lord of the Ironfort, at his throneNáin the Slakeless, Lord of the Ironfort, can be found at his throne atop the northern ramps.Talk to Náin the Slakeless in JárnfastThe Quenching Trough is a low-ceilinged tavern opposite the entrance to Járnfast.Find Durin in the Quenching Trough opposite the entrance to JárnfastDurin is in the Quenching Trough, a low-ceiling tavern opposite the entrance to Járnfast.Talk to Durin in the Quenching TroughDurin is in the Quenching Trough, a low-ceilinged tavern opposite the entrance to Járnfast.Talk to Durin (again) in the Quenching TroughNáin the Slakeless, Lord of the Ironfort, can be found at his throne atop the northern ramps.Talk to Náin the Slakeless in Járnfast'I am going to find a tavern and spend some coin and some time while you go about the second part of our errand, ${PLAYER}. I have heard the Quenching Trough is quite good; look for me there if you need me.

'King Thorin's younger brother Náin is Lord of the Ironfort, and you should find him at his throne, atop the northern ramps. They call him Náin the Slakeless now, for his appetite for battle cannot be quenched! I heard his deeds during the Siege of Erebor were quite extraordinary; no doubt he will tell you of them. Once that is done, it is to be hoped he will consent to his nephew Durin travelling with us back through the Ironfold.

'Come find me when you have Durin in your company and are ready to return to the Lonely Mountain.''King Thorin's younger brother Náin is Lord of the Ironfort, and you should find him at his throne, atop the northern ramps. They call him Náin the Slakeless now, for his appetite for battle cannot be quenched! I heard his deeds during the Siege of Erebor were quite extraordinary; no doubt he will tell you of them. Once that is done, it is to be hoped he will consent to his nephew Durin travelling with us back through the Ironfold.

'Come find me when you have Durin in your company and are ready to return to the Lonely Mountain.''You come from the Lonely Mountain, and from my brother? I bid you welcome, but I do not know why he sent you to me. I saw him short weeks ago, when the siege was lifted. Aye, I did my part in lifting it! I cannot imagine there have been events of import enough to send a messenger so soon.

'My older brother Thorin did little in the way of fighting during the war. We have always been different, and ladies of our mutual acquaintance will happily tell you that my blood runs hotter than his, and my temper too! It is not a slight, do not take it as such! He is the King Under the Mountain and I am the Lord of the Ironfort and Iron Hills, both stations of importance, but I am sworn to serve him. I would serve him regardless, for he is my elder and dear to me, but he is also my king, and that alone merits respect.

'You are here for his son? When the Easterlings brought their war through the Ironfold, Thorin sent word to me that Durin was to remain within the Ironfort, and I saw it done. Afterward I marched to the Lonely Mountain and there engaged in such battle as you would hardly believe! Stranded atop one of the spurs of the Mountain I was, wielding the Black Mattock with unheralded ferocity. Well, I have heralded it aplenty now to my minstrels, so in time folk from all about will hear the tale, or my minstrels will have an earful to match this golden one I earned following the battle!

'If it had been up to me, Durin would have come with me to the fighting. Alas it was not. His father has always been a cautious sort. The boy was not happy about staying behind, and he spends most of his days deep in his cups, drowning his resentment and his boredom. Go to the Quenching Trough, his preferred tavern, and give him the news that his father seeks him. He will surely be pleased.''You are here for Thorin's son? When the Easterlings brought their war through the Ironfold, my brother sent word to me that Durin was to remain within the Ironfort, and I saw it done. Afterward I marched to the Lonely Mountain and there engaged in such battle as you would hardly believe! Stranded atop one of the spurs of the Mountain I was, wielding the Black Mattock with unheralded ferocity. Well, I have heralded it aplenty now to my minstrels, so in time folk from all about will hear the tale, or my minstrels will have an earful to match this golden one I earned following the battle!

'If it had been up to me, Durin would have come with me to the fighting. Alas it was not. His father has always been a cautious sort. The boy was not happy about staying behind, and he spends most of his days deep in his cups, drowning his resentment and his boredom. Go to the Quenching Trough, his preferred tavern, and give him the news that his father seeks him. He will surely be pleased.''What d'you want? Leave m'alone, stranger. Don't want to talk to you. M'busy.'

Durin takes a long pull from his beer, spilling quite a lot of it, and wipes his beard with the back of his hand. He peers at you woozily, apparently trying to decide why you haven't left yet. You introduce yourself as ${PLAYER}, an emissary from King Thorin, and describe your charge. Durin's eyes widen and he sets his mug down on the table too heavily.'My father wants me to return to Erebor? These are joyous tidings indeed! I thought I would never be permitted to leave the Ironfort ever again, neither in peace or in war. More the pity that the war is ended, and I never had the chance to prove myself in it!'

His drink-bred surliness is gone, but now it is replaced with a sudden flash of uncertainty.

'Am I ready to prove myself? My father did not think me strong enough to fight, commanding me to remain behind the sealed doors of the Ironfort. How can I know I will make him proud now, untested as I am?

'What did you say your name was again? ${PLAYER}? Let us go now to my uncle Náin and tell him we leave at once. I... I am ready to prove myself worthy, and let none doubt my resolve!''Let us go now to my uncle Náin and tell him we leave at once, ${PLAYER}. I am ready to prove myself worthy, and let none doubt my resolve!''I see you found my nephew, ${PLAYER}. Very good! I had a task in mind for him to complete, but now that he is needed at the Lonely Mountain I shall find someone else to take care of it.

'What is that, Durin? Speak up, boy, my recent exploits have done something to my hearing, I can't imagine what. You want to handle this task before you set out for the Mountain? Ah, I am proud of you! Truly you will be a great king someday. Why, dare I say it...

'Nay, I will not say it. You will grow into your name, of that I have no doubt. Let me tell you the details about this task I have for you and for ${PLAYER}. I am sure ${PLAYER} does not wish to be left out? No, of course not!'Náin the Slakeless hopes that you and his nephew Durin will be able to combat some of the Easterling threat that remains within the Ironfold.Durin is at his uncle's throne in Járnfast.Talk to Durin in JárnfastDurin is east of Utterby, in the Ironfold.Meet up with Durin east of Utterby, in the IronfoldDurin is east of Utterby, in the Ironfold.Talk to Durin east of UtterbyDurin suggested using dwarf-phrases that will be unfamiliar to the Jangovar in order to confuse your foes and to attain strategic advantage when you move against them:

Biraisbêj tharâkh, "scouting the approaches." 
Bini'zêg utrâd, "blinding the watchers." 
Mahinsês mamahkhafdân, "rescuing the prisoners." 
Isgêr narakakâ, "defeating the reinforcements." 
Ensêkh, "setting a trap."Biraisbij tharâkh: Jangovar tents on the eastern side of UtterbyBiraisbij tharâkh: Jangovar tents on the southern side of UtterbyBini'zig utrâd (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Durin suggested using dwarf-phrases that will be unfamiliar to the Jangovar in order to confuse your foes and to attain strategic advantage when you move against them:

Biraisbêj tharâkh, "scouting the approaches." 
Bini'zêg utrâd, "blinding the watchers." 
Mahinsês mamahkhafdân, "rescuing the prisoners." 
Isgêr narakakâ, "defeating the reinforcements." 
Ensêkh, "setting a trap."Durin suggested using dwarf-phrases that will be unfamiliar to the Jangovar in order to confuse your foes and to attain strategic advantage when you move against them:

Biraisbêj tharâkh, "scouting the approaches." 
Bini'zêg utrâd, "blinding the watchers." 
Mahinsês mamahkhafdân, "rescuing the prisoners." 
Isgêr narakakâ, "defeating the reinforcements." 
Ensêkh, "setting a trap."Isgir narakakâ (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ensikh to waylay the Jangovar at the cliff-face near Skald's Drop (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Durin is beneath the rising cliff-face at Skald's Drop.Talk to Durin at Skald's DropNáin the Slakeless, Lord of the Ironfort, can be found at his throne atop the ramps.Talk to Náin the Slakeless in JárnfastDurin can be found by his uncle's throne in Járnfast.Talk to Durin in Járnfast'Durin has a fine mind for military tactics and strategy, but he has little in the way of true experience, ${PLAYER}. Once he returns to his father in the Lonely Mountain, it will be more difficult for him to attain such valuable seasoning. I must impart to him what I can before he sets forth, and goes beyond the reach of my training.

'As it happens, the state of affairs in the Ironfold could use some adjustment. My nephew may be just the one to do it, all while getting some good experience of his own. The town of Utterby was occupied by the Jangovar on their way to the Lonely Mountain, and I hear reports that Easterling reinforcements climb Skald's Drop every day. Many of them gather in tents around the city, and they ravage the lands thereabout.

'Durin, I ask that you go to Utterby with ${PLAYER} and do what you can to combat our foes. It will be a good opportunity for you to accomplish two goals at once: to show what you have learned and to aid the people of the Ironfold. Do you agree to this plan?''The state of affairs in the Ironfold could use some adjustment, and my nephew may be just the one to do it! The town of Utterby was occupied by the Jangovar on their way to the Lonely Mountain, and I hear reports that Easterling reinforcements climb Skald's Drop every day. Many of them gather in tents around the city, and they ravage the lands thereabout.

'Durin, I ask that you go to Utterby with ${PLAYER} and do what you can to combat our foes. It will be a good opportunity for you to accomplish two goals at once: to show what you have learned and to aid the people of the Ironfold. Do you agree to this plan?''I am ready for the challenge if you are, ${PLAYER}! Let us go at once to Utterby and assess the situation. I know it is foolish to rush into battle without a plan, so meet me outside the city and we will gather the information we need to attain victory!

'You will not be disappointed, Uncle Náin! Let us go, my friend. Utterby awaits!''Let us go at once to Utterby and assess the situation. I know it is foolish to rush into battle without a plan, so meet me outside the city and we will gather the information we need to attain victory!''There lies Utterby. It was once the home of Dale-folk, but now it is occupied by the Jangovar.''There lies the town of Utterby, surrounded by the tents of the Jangovar. I do not think all who remain within are warriors, ${PLAYER}. We have no quarrel with the refugees, but those of the Jangovar who bear weapons wish us ill, and I mark them our enemies.

'My uncle spoke true when he said I have little combat experience, but I have studied the art of war and am no novice in battlefield understanding! I was taught that the efficient commander finds the victory, and that is what I want to become. Let us use the words of my people to communicate on the battlefield, ${PLAYER}. The Jangovar will not understand us if we speak in this way, and that will give us the advantage.

'First comes biraisbêj tharâkh, "scouting the approaches." If I shout "biraisbij tharâkh!" you will know we need to scout the locations to which our efforts turn. Next comes bini'zêg utrâd, "blinding the watchers." Your objective in that case will be to defeat any look-outs that threaten to raise the alarm. Our next aim will be mahinsês mamahkhafdân, "rescuing the prisoners," if any Dale-folk have been taken captive. By that point we should see about isgêr narakakâ, "defeating the reinforcements" that are likely to come from Skald's Drop. I am still working out the kernel of a plan, but it may involve ensêkh, "setting a trap," if all goes well.

'Do you understand all this, ${PLAYER}? Good! We cannot fail!''First comes biraisbêj tharâkh, "scouting the approaches." If I shout "biraisbij tharâkh!" you will know we need to scout the locations to which our efforts turn. Next comes bini'zêg utrâd, "blinding the watchers." Your objective in that case will be to defeat any look-outs that threaten to raise the alarm. Our next aim will be mahinsês mamahkhafdân, "rescuing the prisoners," if any Dale-folk have been taken captive. By that point we should see about isgêr narakakâ, "defeating the reinforcements" that are likely to come from Skald's Drop. I am still working out the kernel of a plan, but it may involve ensêkh, "setting a trap," if all goes well.''Mahinsis mamahkhafdân, ${PLAYER}! And then isgir narakakâ!''I have been ensêkh, ${PLAYER}! Weaken the cliff-face and we may be able to stall the Jangovar passage up the Drop!''I think we have done very well, ${PLAYER}! My uncle is sure to be impressed by the work we have done to combat the Jangovar here in the Ironfold. And better: by weakening the cliff-face we have made it possible to hinder the flow of the Jangovar reinforcements climbing Skald's Drop. We will not collapse the path now, but dwarves will be able to do so at my uncle's command, should he deem it necessary.

'Let us return to him in Járnfast with these good tidings!''I think we have done very well, ${PLAYER}! My uncle is sure to be impressed by the work we have done to combat the Jangovar here in the Ironfold. And better: by weakening the cliff-face we have made it possible to hinder the flow of the Jangovar reinforcements climbing Skald's Drop. We will not collapse the path now, but dwarves will be able to do so at my uncle's command, should he deem it necessary.

'Let us return to him in Járnfast with these good tidings!''We have done a great deal to hinder the efforts of the Jangovar Easterlings in the Ironfold, Uncle Náin.''Durin told me of the work you two did near Utterby and Skald's Drop, and I must commend you for it. I do not think the Jangovar will long remain in the Ironfold to trouble us!

'Part of me wishes my nephew would remain here to assist me with finishing the job. As Lord of the Iron Hills, I command much respect and bear a great deal of responsibility for maintaining their peace, but my brother Thorin outranks me in age and in authority. He calls for his son, and I will not stand in the way!

'It is time for my nephew to learn what it takes to lead, and to grow into the greatness of his name. Was it not for that very purpose that his father named him Durin?''Part of me wishes my nephew would remain here to assist me with finishing the job. As Lord of the Iron Hills I command much respect and bear a great deal of responsibility for maintaining their peace, but my brother Thorin outranks me in age and in authority. He calls for his son, and I will not stand in the way!

'It is time for my nephew to learn what it takes to lead, and to grow into the greatness of his name. Was it not for that very purpose that his father named him Durin?''Ah, I hate when he talks like this, ${PLAYER}. I love my uncle, but not a day goes by without him telling me that I must be Durin the Deathless come again. Can you imagine it? It is quite frustrating.

'It is not an uncommon name among my people, and no one forbade my father from naming me thus! It is not fair to compare me to such a measure... it would not be fair to anyone!'Many streams and rivers flow from the Iron Hills, and in their shallows hidden riches may be found.The rivers of the Ironfold can be found flowing from the Iron Hills.

Kári hopes to impress Foreman Brumbur by proving that panning the rivers of the Iron Hills might prove a substantial source of iron and other minerals.Kári can be found among the ruined mines of Járnfast.

After panning in the rivers and streams of the Iron Hills, you have collected a small amount of glimmering fragments. You should deliver them to Kári in Járnfast.'O! Have you a moment?

'I believe there may be a way to bolster our yields of iron and gemstones while the mines of Járnfast remain in disrepair.

'I have had some success in panning the rivers of the Ironfold, and the tremors have not obstructed their flow as they have the tunnels of Járnfast.

'Here, take this! I would ask that you visit the six sources of the rivers of the Iron Hills, and see what you can pan from them.

'When you have travelled to all of them, bring what you have found to me!''Ah! Look at those! When you go to the other five sources, you are certain to come back with even more!'

You inform Kári that the glimmering fragments you have collected were panned from all six sources.

'Oh... ah. You were gone for quite some time, weren't you? Bah, Brumbur was right. Ruined as the mines of Járnfast are, much more can be delved from them... and with much less walking about!

'I am sorry to have sent you on such a fruitless errand, ${PLAYER}.

'Take this for your troubles.'Much to Grámi Hornhelm's dismay, a newly-arrived company of Zhélruka has taken an uncomfortable interest in the works of Hammerstead.Leering Zhélruka can be found throughout Hammerstead.

Concerned that ill-meaning glances shall soon turn to theft, Grámi Hornhelm has asked you to politely urge the visiting Zhélruka to depart Hammerstead.Grámi Hornhelm can be found at Hammerstead.

Although not all of them took your words to heart, you have urged many of the visiting Zhélruka to depart Hammerstead. You should now speak to Grámi Hornhelm.Talk to Grámi Hornhelm'Ah, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps you can help me with another matter....'

Grámi Hornhelm pauses and glances around.

'You have seen them, haven't you? A company of Zhélruka has come to Hammerstead, and I do not know how long they intend to remain. All I know is that I am not fond of the gazes of some of these Zhélruka lingering upon the works of my kinsfolk. These are not glances of admiration or curiosity, I think. These seem to me the leers of thieves laying in wait!

'There are already more than enough matters that demand my attention in Hammerstead, and I would not burden myself further by keeping watch on the works of my smiths. Would you politely urge the Zhélruka to continue on their journey?''I do not mind the occasional guest, but it seemed to me those Zhélruka had hidden intentions. I shall keep a watchful eye on those that still remain, but that should not prove a difficult task.

'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.'The Dalish village of Utterby is currently under control of the Easterlings, who call it Er Shamol. Soon, the dwarves will be pushed to reclaim this territory for their allies.Tuhâl is waiting to speak to Lord Náin on behalf of his people.

Speak with Tuhâl to enter the instance 'Lord of Iron'.Speak with Tuhâl and enter Instance: The Lord of IronTuhâl has convinced Lord Náin to give him time to evacuate the innocent Easterlings from Utterby.

You should talk to Tuhâl.Talk to TuhâlTuhâl has fashioned you a disguise!

You should talk to Tuhâl.Talk to TuhâlNow that you have a disguise, you need to infiltrate Er Shamol.

You should travel to Utterby with Tuhâl.Travel to UtterbyTuhâl is ready to infiltrate Er Shamol.

You should talk to Tuhâl.Talk to TuhâlKunchat, who oversees the Jangovar's operations, will put you to work.

You should talk to Kunchat.Talk to KunchatUtterby is currently occupied by Jangovar forces.

You should complete quests in Utterby to undermine the Jangovar.Undermine the Jangovar by completing quests in UtterbyTuhâl's family is somewhere in Utterby.

You should find and free Tuhâl's family.Find and free Tuhâl's familyKunchat, who oversees the Jangovar's forces, would like to speak with you.

You should talk to Kunchat.Talk to Kunchat'The time has come. I must explain to Lord Náin what is happening in Er Shamol. I know you have no reason to help us, but will you stand by my side, ${PLAYER}? Forgive me for assuming, but you seem to have a kind heart.''How my heart beats within me! Lord Náin truly lives up to his reputation.

'We are going to have to come up with a plan. Many lives weigh on our shoulders now. If you are going to pass as one of my people, you will need a disguise. I have some extra clothes; let us see what I can do.''I must do this for my people.''Hmm... good enough, I think! Just keep your face covered, and let me do the talking. Let us travel to Er Shamol. And watch you do not get too close to the Jangovar outside the village. They are cruel, even to us.''Here we are: Er Shamol. It means "Windy-home" in your tongue. If only it were really a home, and not the work camp it has become.

'We must do as Lord Náin asked, and find a way to undermine the Jangovar from within. I see no other way than to offer ourselves to Kunchat for work, and see what opportunities present themselves. Perhaps if we do well, we can meet their leader, Akh-gulûkh, and discover more of their plans.

'My family is also here somewhere, being forced to work. I must find them and warn them of the dwarf attack, before it is too late.''Tuhâl! We assumed you fell at Hammerstead when you did not return with the rest. Akh-gulûkh will be pleased to hear you survived your first taste of combat. And who is this with you? Fresh meat?'

The man smirks and looks you up and down

'Take her/him to Kúlkhur. He will put her/him to work.''Good work, Chayasír. You have proven yourself a skilled warrior and worthy of our attention. Perhaps one day we will make you a proper Jangovar!''I told you we are working as fast as we can! Wait... you are not one of them. And who is that with you? Tuhâl? Is that truly you?'The time has come for Tuhâl of Chayasír to stand before Lord Náin and speak for the Easterling refugees.The time has come for Tuhâl of Chayasír to stand before Lord Náin and speak for the Easterling refugees.Talk to TuhâlTuhâl is speaking to Lord Náin.

You should listen to the conversation.Listen to the conversationYou have been tasked with infiltrating Utterby!

You should talk to Náin.Talk to Náin'I am so relieved that Lord Náin heard my words. Now, we must come up with a plan.''I apologize for putting this on your shoulders, ${PLAYER}, but from what I have heard, you are more than capable of this task. Keep an eye on our "friend" here. His words are convincing, but I am not quick to trust any foreigner. Do what you can from within to clear the innocents out and undermine their operation. I will come to finish the task when the time is right.''Wish me luck, friend.'King Thorin has charged Spall with visiting the Iron Hills to determine whether any of the rumours in his court are the least bit true.Spall can be found before Thorin's throne in the court of Erebor.

You should hear Spall's reply to King Thorin Stonehelm.Talk to SpallThorin stands before his throne in the court of Erebor.

You should hear King Thorin's Stonehelm reply.Talk to King ThorinYou should meet Spall the Lore-master in the Ironfold, which lies down the road that leads from the south-eastern gates of Dale.Meet Spall in the IronfoldSkald's Drop lies along the southern edge of the Ironfold, Utterby right in the middle, and Járnfast at its eastern edge.

You should investigate the rumours about the Iron Hills near the stair at Skald's Drop, in the Dalish town of Utterby, and at the keep of Járnfast where Lord Náin dwells.

If you get separated from Spall, use the horn to call him back.Investigate Skald's DropInvestigate UtterbyInvestigate Járnfast's foundationsInvestigate the Dwarves of JárnfastUse this to call Spall back to youYou should talk to Spall the Lore-master.Talk to SpallYou should talk to Lord Náin again, at his seat in Járnfast in the Iron Hills.Talk to Lord Náin again'As you may be aware, a plague of injudicious rumours have come into this court, nearly all concerning affairs in the Iron Hills.

'In truth, we have heard little from my brother Náin in the month since the fighting ended, and I must grudgingly concede that a desire to hear how things go out there is reasonable, even if such wanton gossip is not.

'Now, I have heard a very wise and reasonable suggestion from one of my trusted Lore-masters. And who better to act on such a sensible sooth than to dispatch its author into the Ironfold, where he can determine the truth of each and every rumour, then return here and set the record straight.

'I am speaking of you Spall. You needn't look around as if it were someone else.''Mm - me? Well, in truth, I did make just such a suggestion to ${PLAYER} here, for I abhor these rumours just as you do, my lord. I cannot deny that, no matter my humility.

'And I will stand by what I have said. You should dispatch some dwarf keen of eye and stout of frame, fair of mind and with a good memory. And while many of those attributes this Lore-master would humbly claim to possess, surely King Thorin, you cannot mean to send me out there. I am no adventurer, or guard, or soldier.

'If half the rumours whispered in this court are true - and indeed, more than half would be most contradictory and impossible - then the Ironfold might be a truly dangerous place!'King Thorin laughs unexpectedly.

'Well, Spall! You have talked yourself nearly in a circle, between worthiness and unworthiness and worthiness again. You have made a fair point, however, that the Iron Hills may yet hold peril. Jangovar yet linger by the Long Lake to the south, after all.

'I maintain that no dwarf in all the Lonely Mountain is more suited to this endeavour than you. However, perhaps what is called for is company. ${PLAYER}, would you do Erebor one more service, and go with Spall into the Ironfold? I dare say there is much there of interest for an adventurer like yourself.''We are in this together it seems. I must gather some effects both here and down in Dale. Let us meet just where the road enters the Ironfold, then. If you leave Dale through the south-eastern gate, you will find the way.''Do see that Spall accomplishes his task safely, ${PLAYER}. And bear my greetings to my brother Náin in Járnfast, when you see him there.''There you are! What a long stroll this was. Longer than I am accustomed to, I confess. Spall, I said to myself, you are a proud dwarf and you must not tire so easily. Still, I have rarely gone so far in one day!

'Now then, we have work to do, do we not? One cannot see much from here, can one? Before we go on, however, let us review our task.

'First, many rumours circled around the stair at Skald's Drop, on the Ironfold's south. We should visit there and see for ourselves. Second, at least as many rumours circle the Dalish town of Utterby, due east of here. Third and hopefully last, many were the rumours of Járnfast and of Lord Náin who sits within.

'If we visit these three locations, we will have settled the majority of the claims.''Let us see then... first, the stair at Skald's Drop, which lies along the southern edge of the Ironfold. Second, the Dalish town of Utterby right in the middle of the Ironfold. Third, eastward built into the Hills, Járnfast where Lord Náin sits.''There you are, ${PLAYER}. I must thank you for being my travelling companion on this expedition. I think - I think perhaps I shall get out more often.

'Ah, but who are you trying to fool, Spall? You will go back to your scrolls and runes and objects of lore and forget the sky-topped lands.''There you are. I have given Spall room to finish writing down his findings before he returns to the Lonely Mountain. I will send him back with an escort of my guards. I thank you looking after him, ${PLAYER}.

'You are most welcome here. As I mentioned before, I fear no such storm as we weathered in battle, yet troubles have a way of creeping in, and lingering. An adventurer such as yourself could do much good among the simple smiths of the Iron Hills. If you remain and lend your hand, you shall be rewarded richly and gratefully.''We have done well and fully the task we were charged with, ${PLAYER}. Only, Lord Náin asked us to return to him once we were done. I will meet you there.'The Dwarves of Járnfast will be able to confirm or deny the remaining rumours about the Iron Hills.You should talk to the Dwarves of Járnfast, including Lord Náin.Now that you have talked to many dwarves in Járnfast, you should talk once more to Spall.Talk to Spall'We should not attend only to the stonework while in Járnfast, ${PLAYER}. Good dwarves, kinfolk of mine, still dwell here, too - I hope!

'They will settle any last rumours we have been charged to test. Not only that, but I shall be glad to see the faces of my kin.

'Let us not forget, as well, King Thorin specially bade us bring his greeting to his brother Náin.''My kin in Járnfast will set to rest any rumours that remain. We must not forget to bear King Thorin's greetings to his brother Náin.''If we have been quiet, it is because we have been hard at work repairing, reforging, and clearing the dust from our home. Yet we have ever been quieter and more industrious than our kin to the west.''I heard that a dragon had returned to Erebor, and that Jangovar roam freely through Dale, which burns both night and day. Is any of it true?'

When you tell the dwarf it is not, his face darkens for a moment, then brightens again.

'Then my cousins are well. I am happy to hear it!''Yes, the ground shook here. Many of us were in the midst of battle. When our foes fell back, we turned homeward fearing the worst. To our great relief, Járnfast still stood. It was days more before Lord Náin's face was seen. And how he has changed! We stood in awe of him before. Now... well, I will say no more. Have you seen him for yourself?''So there are rumours in my brother's court?'

A wry smile shows in the one living eye in Náin's face.

'There are rumours in Járnfast as well, I am certain, though a stern rebuke would meet the dwarf who brought them before me. It is a symptom of the quiet that follows the storm, no doubt, that many fear more storm. 

'But I think we will have no more storms, not such as we have weathered, though other troubles may come to us, from other directions.

'But I wander in my thought. I grant you full leave to satisfy your curiosities. Or have you already done so? I would speak with you when your errand is at an end.''It was good to see my kinfolk, but better still to hear their voices, hearty and hale. I will return to Erebor with more news than just the task with which I was charged.'Investigate the rumours about the structural integrity of Járnfast.Investigate the rumours about the structural integrity of Járnfast.Investigate the foundations of JárnfastYou should talk to Spall about the foundations of Járnfast.Talk to SpallYou should examine cracks in the stonework of Járnfast, both inside and out.Inspect cracks around Járnfast (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Now that you have inspected a great number of the cracks apparent in Járnfast's stonework, you should talk to Spall.Talk to SpallInvestigate the rumours about the structural integrity of Járnfast.'Járnfast! This much we can say without any inspection: it has not been shaken to the ground. To be proper record-keepers, however, we must get closer and inspect any cracks in the stone from without and from within.''Many of the flaws in Járnfast's stone are older than you or I, ${PLAYER}, though a few are clearly new. Stone-masonry is among the lore I have studied in my days, and I can state with good authority that there is no fear of Járnfast tumbling today, or in a hundred years, barring some fresh calamity.'Investigate the rumours about Skald's Drop and the Stair with Spall.Investigate the rumours about Skald's Drop and the Stair with Spall.Investigate Skald's DropYou should talk to Spall about the rumours regarding Skald's Drop.Talk to SpallYou should view the Stair in the cliff's edge, at Skald's Drop in the Ironfold.Find the StairYou should talk to Spall, now that you have seen the Stair at Skald's Drop.Talk to SpallYou should look for a camp-fire inland near Skald's Drop, and see if there are any of the rumoured travellers there.Investigate the small campYou should talk to the strange dwarves you found at a camp-fire near to Skald's Drop in the Ironfold.Talk to the strange dwarvesThe possessions are scattered near to Skald's Drop in the Ironfold.

You should help the strange dwarves gather their scattered possessions, including small sacks, big parcels, and water-skins.Collect small sacks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect larger parcels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect water-skins (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the belongings you gathered to Okuz and Túmo at their camp, near Skald's Drop.You should search for Okuz and Túmo's axe, which they say 'ran' off away from their camp near Skald's Drop, toward the river.Find the axeYou should return the axe to the strange dwarves at their camp-site near Skald's Drop. You should also ask them why the axe was tied to an ox.You should hear what Spall has to say in response to Okuz's claims.Talk to SpallOkuz and Túmo stand by their camp-fire, near Skald's Drop in the Ironfold.

Hear Okuz's answer to Spall's charge.Talk to OkuzYou should talk to Spall and hear his thoughts on what you have found in the Ironfold.Talk to SpallInvestigate the rumours about Skald's Drop and the Stair with Spall.'Well now, we have arrived at Skald's Drop. Properly speaking of course, the name refers to the cliff and, well, the drop. We had better get right up to it, enjoy the view, and see whether or not the Stair has been broken.''We had better get right up to the cliff's edge and see whether or not the Stair has been broken.''Can't you see I'm busy? Off with you.''It may not look it to you, but the Stair is as intact now as it was when last I came this way. Which... hm... I believe was the early days of my youth!

'There is a fair stream of travellers coming from the Stair. What can we say of them? I do believe I espied the smoke of a camp-fire as we approached, earlier. Let us make our way back and find what there is to be found.''You talk to them, ${PLAYER}. You are the adventurer, after all!''What do you want?'

The dwarf eyes you suspiciously.

'I don't have to tell you anything. And besides, I have not time. As you can see, our possessions have become scattered - here, there, all about.'

The dwarf stands, waiting for you to move on. When you do not, he shakes his head.

'First, kízinek, help us, then talk. There - the small bags, the big parcels, the water-skins.''Small bags, big parcels, water-skins - they are scattered all down there.''I suppose we'd better help them. Perhaps they'll be inclined to speak with us if we do.''The axe? Where is the axe? Ah, Túmo, you drak. You did not tell me that ran off, too. And how many thousands of leagues have we lugged that piece of iron?

'Ah, kízinek, this is a very sour thing to have lost. If you can return to us our axe, you will be owed a debt, I assure you.

'It ran off that way, across the river to I know not where.''Am I much mistaken, or did he say their axe ran off toward the river?''It ran off that way, across the river to I know not where.''Look, Túmo! The kízinek has brought us back your axe. I told you it was a terrible idea to -'

The dwarf looks up at you and falls silent.

'You wish to know who we are? We are dwarves, just like your friend here. Only he is - what is that word? -  Longbeard, while we are Zhélruka. Many thousands of leagues, as I have said to you, we have come from our home. Our home - it is no home. It is Dôm Fálesh.''Well, if you ask me, there is little doubt that these dwarves attempted to saddle that ox with all they owned and ease the load upon their own two backs. No doubt it belonged to some Man or dwarf of the Ironfold, though after all the war this place has seen, who can say if its master yet lives.

'My ire was up when I began my thoughts, ${PLAYER}, but on further reflection, the pathetic state of these dwarves, and the innocence of the crime, softens my heart.

'They called themselves Zhélruka, and indeed this is a name known in lore, belonging to another proud line of dwarves. A line that has dealings with Durin's Folk in past times. I wonder what these two fled, and if others of their kin also come this way.''And what of that ox your axe was tied to? What of that, I should like to know? Did that belong to you? And how, now that I think of it, did your belongings become scattered in the first place?''Túmo. What is this word - ox? No? We do not know it. We have never seen such a beast. We are poor and weary travellers. Many, many, many leagues we have gone on foot, and without our few possessions we would be done for.

'Túmo and I, we thank you for helping recover them.'Investigate the rumours about the Dalish town of Utterby.Investigate the rumours about the Dalish town of Utterby.Investigate UtterbyYou should talk to Spall about investigating the rumours regarding Utterby.Talk to SpallYou should scout Utterby with Spall, making sure to get a view of the south and east gates, and looking out for signs of who controls the town now and how that came to pass.View Utterby's south gateView Utterby's east gateLearn who controls Utterby nowFind signs of what happenedNow that you have scouted the perimeter of Utterby, you should talk to Spall.Talk to SpallInvestigate the rumours about the Dalish town of Utterby.'Behold, ${PLAYER}: Utterby, a trading town of the Dale-men. At least, that is what it had been before the battles upon the Ironfold. Viewed from a distance, one cannot help but suspect some of the rumours we have heard are true.

'I am not a warrior and I have no desire to march into the lion's mouth, yet all the same, we must get a bit closer to be sure. Let us tour both the southern gate and the east gate, and see if we may learn who controls Utterby now, and how that has come to pass.''Though we cannot be sure without, as I said, marching straight into the lion's mouth, I fear the rumours we had heard in court hold some truth here. Jangovar tents at both gates, their flags raised and the Dalish ones thrown down? That is truly an ill sign carved in plain runes, if you ask me.'The Eymn Engrin, the Iron Hills, are worthy of their name, and there is iron to be found in even the most unexpected of places.Rust-pocked deposits can be found throughout the Ironfold.

You have discovered an unfamiliar deposit in the Iron Hills. You should mine the deposit and deliver the ore you collect to the Dwarves of Járnfast.Collect chunks of ore from rust-pocked deposits (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Foreman Brumbur can be found among the ruined mines of Járnfast.

You have gathered a substantial amount of crude ore and should now deliver it to Foreman Brumbur in Járnfast.While exploring the Ironfold, you have discovered a peculiar deposit. It glimmers with a hint of iron, but it bears many pocks of rust. Perhaps this could be of use to the Dwarves of Járnfast while they work to unearth the collapsed tunnels of their mines.

You should collect some crude rust-stone ore and deliver it to Foreman Brumbur in Járnfast.'What is this?

'Rust-pock, you say? I have never known an ore by that name. Is that the name you have given it? 

'It looks to be of a very crude deposit, and much of it is not good stone. Ah, but I can see hints of iron within it! It may be of use to us after all!

'Thank you for delivering this to me, ${PLAYER}.'The Dwarf-holds Rewards Quartermaster has a small quantity of Motes available.Talk to the Dwarf-holds Rewards Quartermaster to trade Longbeard Marks for Motes.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest can be completed weekly.</rgb>'If you are looking for Motes, I have just a few. I would be willing to part with them if you have any Marks of the Longbeards.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest can be completed weekly.</rgb>'It is a worthwhile trade! I am glad you agree. This is all I have though. Perhaps I will acquire more Motes soon!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Completing this quest will remove 15 Marks of the Longbeards.</rgb>Despite its wealth of gold and other valuable gemstones, Erebor does not produce enough iron to serve the needs of Durin's Folk.Iron rods, barrel-hoops, and pick-axes can be found throughout Járnfast.

Foreman Brumbur has asked you to aid in preparing a shipment of iron tools and materials to Erebor.Collect bundles of iron rods (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sets of barrel-hoops (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect iron pick-axes (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Foreman Brumbur can be found among the ruined mines of Járnfast.

You have gathered a substantial amount of iron tools and materials. You should now deliver them to Foreman Brumbur.'Well met, ${PLAYER}. I am Brumbur, foreman of the mines of Járnfast.

'I have heard tell of the many tasks you have performed in service of the King under the Mountain, but Erebor remains in need. Of what, you ask? Iron!

'Indeed, Erebor is rich in many gemstones and ores, but it is nearly barren of iron. Ah, but these are the Iron Hills! We provide Erebor all the iron our kinsfolk require for their craft, and I intend to send more to the Lonely Mountain this very day.

'However, Járnfast is in a state of disrepair and even now many of my kinsfolk work to restore it. If you would aid me, I would ask that you gather iron rods, sets of barrel-hoops, and pick-axes left about Járnfast. The tremors may have shifted them from where they were first placed, but I do not think they will be in short supply!

'Deliver them to me, and it would be a great boon to our efforts to supply Erebor and restore Járnfast!''Very good!

'Once Járnfast is restored, I shall ensure that we send greater shipments of iron to Erebor.

'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.'As they find them useful in clearing less valuable stones, the Dwarves of the Ironfold have begun to share their mines with the cave-claws of the Emyn Engrin.Harnessed stone-biters can be found along the Emyn Engrin in the Ironfold.

Foreman Brumbur has asked you to defeat and collect harnessed stone-biters that have inadvertently burrowed out of Járnfast.Defeat and collect harnessed stone-biters in the Ironfold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Foreman Brumbur can be found among the ruined mines of Járnfast.

You have collected a number of harnessed stone-biters in the Ironfold and should deliver them to Foreman Brumbur in Járnfast.Deliver the harnessed stone-biters to Foreman Brumbur in Járnfast'Have you a moment to aid me with another matter, ${PLAYER}?

'I have no doubt you have already noticed, but Járnfast has shaken itself into a bit of ruin. The tunnels of our delving have collapsed, and we have lost access to nearly a third of our richest lodes. If we intend to clear them out, we will have need of many more stone-biters. Stone-biters, you ask? I believe they are known as cave-claws or whisperbacks to other folk, or so I have been told. 

'We have long used their knack for burrowing to clear less valuable stones from our mines, but after the tremors, many of them burrowed beyond our reach. They have done similarly before... but not for thought of escaping, mind you! They simply become too excited in their burrowing and just keep digging. Sooner or later, they will burrow back to the surface, but never quite where you lost them!

'If you encounter a stone-biter in a harness of sorts as you travel among the Iron Hills, they are one of ours! You may need to convince them to come along, if you understand my meaning. Ah, but do not worry about being too rough with them. They may as well be made of stone!

'Deliver them to me, and I shall get them back on their feet!''Hah! It was even more than I thought!

'Do not worry for their well-being, ${PLAYER}. I have found a nice hunk of garnet often rouses them. If they become too excited in their burrowing again, I shall call on you!

'You have my thanks.'The vicious battle-cats of the Jangovar have begun to stray from their masters in Utterby, threatening the peoples and wild beasts of the Ironfold.Prowling battle-cats can be found roaming outside Utterby in the Ironfold.

You have encountered a number of prowling battle-cats and should defeat them.Defeat prowling battle-cats in Ironfold (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many prowling battle-cats.CompletedWhile exploring the foothills of the Ironfold, you have encountered a great number of the battle-cats used in battle by the Jangovar. With Utterby seized by the Jangovar, the battle-cats now prowl the Ironfold and threaten its inhabitants. You should defeat the battle-cats of the Jangovar before they can bring greater harm to the peoples and beasts of the Ironfold.You have defeated many of the prowling battle-cats of the Jangovar. While more still remain in Utterby, you have done well to protect the peoples and beasts of the Ironfold.The dwarf attack on Utterby is imminent. Through your efforts, the innocent Easterlings have fled. The time has come to take on the Jangovar.Akh-gulûkh has asked you to join him and the other Jangovar leaders inside the Utterby Town Hall.

You should speak with Kunchat and enter Instance: The Lone Dwarf.Speak with Kunchat to enter Instance: The Lone DwarfLord Náin is outside Utterby.

You should talk to Lord Náin.Talk to Lord Náin'Akh-gulûkh has asked to see you both. It seems all your hard work has been noticed! Or you have done something wrong, ha ha! Go inside, and mind yourselves.''What are you waiting for? Go inside!''Now that their leadership is eliminated, the rest of the Jangovar soldiers will be easily dispatched. Thank you for fighting by my side. And thank Tuhâl for his part in this. Perhaps some Easterlings are worthy of our trust.

'As for Utterby, I am afraid it is not my decision whether or not the peaceful Easterlings stay. This village belongs to the Kingdom of Dale, so you must take up the matter with King Bard. Good luck. I truly do hold sympathy for their plight.'Hammerstead, a small community of miners and smiths in the Ironfold, is under attack. Help the dwarves defend it from the Jangovar!Grámi Hornhelm can be found in Olin's Forge, in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Grámi Hornhelm.Talk to Grámi HornhelmThere is work to do in defence of Hammerstead.

You should complete quests in Hammerstead.Help Lóssi with 'Withstanding the Marauders'Help Lóssi with 'The Head of the Snake'Help Lóssi with 'Relieving the Guard'Help Grámi Hornhelm with 'Weapon Repossession'Grámi Hornhelm can be found in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Grámi Hornhelm.Talk to Grámi HornhelmAn Easterling is being held prisoner in Hammerstead.

You should talk to the Easterling prisoner.Talk to the Easterling prisonerYou should talk to Grámi Hornhelm, who can be found in Hammerstead.Talk to Grámi HornhelmLord Náin needs to know about the Jangovar threat.

You should return to Járnfast with Tuhâl.Return to Járnfast with TuhâlLord Náin needs to know about the Jangovar threat.

You should bring Tuhâl before Lord Náin in Járnfast.Bring Tuhâl before Lord NáinTuhâl is preparing to stand before Lord Náin and speak his piece.

You should talk to Tuhâl.Talk to TuhâlYou tell Lóssi that the convoy was destroyed and the weapons stolen. Only Vílur Strayfoot survived.

'Curse these Easterlings! There is not time to grieve. Go up the road to Olin's Forge and speak with the Master Smith, Grámi Hornhelm. We can hold them off for now.''I am Grámi Hornhelm, son of Grárin, Master smith of Hammerstead. Are you here to help?'

You tell Grámi about the request from Lord Náin, and the destroyed convoy.

'Aye, we knew it was only a matter of time before the Easterlings made their move. We did not expect these tactics, however. They are almost certainly after the weapons we craft here, so that they may arm the refugees coming in from the Stair. Ha! But they will not have them so easily! There is work to do, ${CLASS}. I will not allow this rabble to hobble our production. Report back to Lóssi, and help him however you can.''Thanks to your aid, we should be able to repel the rest of these Easterlings. Before you return to Járnfast, you should see this.'

He points at the bound Easterling in the corner. He does not look like a Jangovar.

'This one actually surrendered! He has been blabbering on about being forced into this and wanting to speak to someone of authority. I have no time for such things, so I give you, ${CLASS}, that authority! Go learn what he is going on about!''I never wanted any of this, you must believe me! The Jangovar, they have my family!'

You ask the Man to calm down and explain himself.

'I am Tuhâl, of the Chayasír. My family and I came from the east. We are refugees, fleeing for our lives! The devastation... it was horrible... 

'We came up the stair, following word that there was safe haven among the vales. A man with a kind face told us that we could have a new life in Er Shamol, which you call Utterby. There was plenty of food, and good shelter, he said, but it was a lie. The Jangovar are luring refugees in, and then forcing the strong to fight, under threat to their families! The ones who cannot fight are chained and made to work. The convoy raid and these small strikes are only the beginning. They are planning to attack Dale again when they can!'

You tell Tuhâl that Lord Náin is considering collapsing the stair to prevent the Easterlings from entering the Ironfold.

'He cannot! My people will have nowhere to go! More are coming. Does no one know what took place in Rhûn?''Lord Náin's reputation precedes him, but he is even more intimidating in person! Despite my fears, I must find the words to convince him, for the welfare of my people.'You tell Grámi all that the Easterling has said. 

'Just as I thought; they are arming themselves for another attack. Return to Járnfast with haste and inform Lord Náin! And take that prisoner with you. The last thing I need is some useless oaf taking up space!'Náin the Slakeless, Lord of Járnfast, has many decisions to make regarding the Easterling host that occupies his lands.Lord Náin has asked you to speak to his advisors in Járnfast.

You should talk to Aldin, Hógur, and Féli.Talk to AldinTalk to HógurTalk to FéliLord Náin has asked you to gauge the opinions of the citizens of Járnfast.

You should talk to Járnfast citizens.You should talk to Lord Náin, who can be found at his seat in Járnfast.Talk to Lord NáinThe convoy of weapons was coming from Hammerstead, north-west of Járnfast.

You should find some sign of the convoy on the path from Hammerstead.Find any sign of the convoyYou have found the weapons convoy sacked, and many dwarves dead.

You should talk to the survivor, Vílur Strayfoot.Talk to Vílur StrayfootVílur Strayfoot has warned you that an attack on Hammerstead is imminent!

You should travel to Hammerstead!Travel to HammersteadHammerstead is under attack by the Easterlings!

You should find the captain of the guard, Lóssi.Talk to Lóssi'Aye, I am still alive. Had someone told you otherwise? I fought at Erebor, and though my father and King Brand both fell, I survived... mostly intact.'

He gestures to his metal ear and agate eye.

'The ones responsible for these paid dearly. 

'Though we suffered heavy losses, we were victorious. The Easterlings failed to sack either Járnfast or Erebor. However, instead of fleeing back East with their tails between their legs like the dogs they are, they stayed put!

'They are holed up in the Dalish village, Utterby. You no doubt passed it on the way here. Perhaps we could easily deal with just the small remaining host from the battle, but more Easterlings are constantly streaming up the stair at Skald's Drop and adding to that host! It is only a matter of time before their numbers overwhelm what we have here in the Ironfold. More bloodshed seems unavoidable.

'I have decisions to make. Difficult decisions. We could collapse the stair, and prevent more Easterlings from entering the Ironfold, but it would also cut off legitimate trade into the Dale-lands, and the route of the Zhélruka clan. And, if rumours are to be believed, the Easterlings are fleeing something terrible and they no longer have a home to which they may return. Aye, the Longbeards would be sympathetic to that plight, or at least they should be.

'Please speak with my advisors, and the citizens of Járnfast. Hear their words, and relay them back to me. This matter is one that must be carefully considered.''Collapsing the stair? He cannot! There are Zhélruka Dwarves, distant cousins of ours, passing that way. We could not do something so low to other dwarves, even under the circumstances!''Aye, collapsing the stair might be the best way to prevent the Easterlings from bolstering their numbers. More are entering the Ironfold every day. 

'Please tell Lord Náin that a convoy from Hammerstead never arrived. I fear the Easterlings ambushed them along the road.''Perhaps collapsing the stair is the right answer, but I caution Lord Náin to do nothing without the council of Thorin Stonehelm, our King in Erebor, and our allies in Dale. The Stair is the main road into the East, and any action we take will surely affect them as well. This is much to consider before acting.''I have heard that the Easterling refugees no longer have a home to return to. If we collapse the stair, would not that leave them stranded?''Those Easterlings are nothing but trouble, and bad for business! Throw them all out, I say!''We cannot collapse the stair. There are Zhélruka Dwarves coming that way. I would not have it said we treated fellow dwarves this way!''Aye, collapsing the stair may be the only solution. We must protect ourselves from the Easterlings.''I am unsure, ${CLASS}. It seems there are reasons for and against collapsing the stair. I trust Lord Náin to make the right decision.''I agree something should be done about Utterby, but collapsing the stair seems rather rash!''Hmph. Let us collapse the stair and keep those Easterlings out of our land!''It seems the Dwarves of the Ironfold are torn on what to do, as am I. I will have to think on this in solitude. In the meantime, I have work for you. 

'That convoy from Hammerstead was a shipment of weapons. Hógur's concern is justified. If it is as he fears, and the Easterlings have attacked it, my hand may be forced. I ask that you discover what became of this convoy by visiting the path it should have taken, and return to me as swiftly as you can.''The Easterlings... ambushed us! They stole the weapons! Hammerstead! You must warn Hammerstead! I will live, just go! It is Lóssi, Captain of the Guard you want. He must be told!''Vílur Strayfoot sent you? Yes, we could use some help! These Easterlings have been harassing us since the convoy left, and we are pinned down!'Determined to follow the dark road to the Grey Mountains, the Zhélruka seek to clear a massive rockslide from the northern pass leading to the Men Angrún.Loose stones can be found upon the northern pass to the Men Angrún in the Ironfold.

Wishing to avoid passing through the lands of the King under the Mountain, Ingór has asked you to aid his company in clearing the way to the Men Angrún.Clear loose stones from the pass to the Men Angrún (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Fallen boulders can be found on the pass to the Men Angrún.

After clearing a number of loose stones, you should now attempt to break the boulders that have fallen upon the pass to the Men Angrún.Attempt to break boulders that have fallen upon the pass to the Men AngrúnIngór can be found on the pass to the Men Angrún.

You have attempted to break the fallen boulders upon the pass to the Men Angrún, but they resisted your efforts. You should talk to Ingór.Talk to Ingór'Have we not travelled far enough to satisfy that stuffy Longbeard smith?'

Ingór studies your face and notes your confusion.

'Do you not remember me? You sent my company away to continue on our journey, and so we have. Indeed, we would have travelled further, but the northern pass to the Men Angrún is sealed by stone and debris!

'To speak plainly, I do not wish to travel through the lands of the King under the Mountain. We of the Zhélruka seek to make our pilgrimage to Dómekh in the old ways, unhindered by politics. There are old wounds between our peoples best forgotten, and I would not see them renewed by the manner of our passage to Dómekh.

'Will you aid us in clearing the loose stones and boulders from the way to the Men Angrún?''Bah!

'The dark road is the swiftest course to Dómekh, and I am not prepared to give up so easily. If I must scale this rockslide to walk the Men Angrún, I shall do it!

'You may go, ${RACE}. I will find a way through.'King Thorin III Stonehelm has granted Dori leave to pursue his brother into the Ered Mithrin, but now Dori must now form a company of his own.Coils of rope, waterskins, and bedrolls can be found throughout the Hall under the Mountain, including the New-lodes of Narathâk.

Dori has asked you to collect coils of rope, waterskins, and bedrolls in preparation for the coming journey to the Grey Mountains.Collect coils of rope (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect water-skins (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bedrolls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Dori can be found inside Nori's home in Tumnuguhûd, the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.

You have gathered several coils of rope, water-skins, and bedrolls and should now deliver them to Dori at Nori's home.Deliver the coils of rope, water-skins, and bedrolls to Dori in Nori's homePacks can be found throughout Nori's home in Tumnuguhûd, the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.

Despite finding only four willing companions to join him on the journey to Thikil-gundu, Dori has asked you to prepare six packs in the event that Nori is found before he reaches the Steel Keep.Dori can be found inside Nori's home in Tumnuguhûd, the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.

You have prepared all of the packs for Dori and Company. You should now speak to Dori.Talk to Dori in Nori's homeOnce night has fallen, Dori and Company can be found outside the gates of Erebor.

Hoping to preserve the secrecy of their journey, Dori has asked the company to join him outside the gates of Erebor after dusk.Meet Dori and Company outside Erebor after duskOnce night has fallen, Dori and Company can be found outside the gates of Erebor.

You have arrived outside the gates of Erebor to find Dori and Company assembled under a tree beside the River Running. You should speak to Dori.Talk to DoriOnce night has fallen, Dori and Company can be found outside the gates of Erebor.

While Dori and the others begin their journey into the Ered Mithrin, Dori has asked you to deliver a share of his treasure to the ravens of Ravenhill in the hope that he might sooner learn of Nori's whereabouts. You should collect the share of Dori's treasure and prepare to travel to Ravenhill.Pick up the share of Dori's treasureArcah can be found at Ravenhill to the south-west of Erebor.

Dori has bidden you deliver a share of his treasure to the ravens at Ravenhill. You should ride swiftly and deliver the treasure to the raven-chief, Arcah.Talk to Arcah at RavenhillRavens can be found at Ravenhill to the south-west of Erebor.

Arcah has accepted the share of Dori's shiny treasure in exchange for sending a few of her unkindness into the Ered Mithrin to seek for Nori. You should direct ravens to take flight and make for the Ered Mithrin.Dori and Company can be found along the road into the Ered Mithrin.

You have earned the aid of Arcah's conspiracy and should now follow the road into the Ered Mithrin to rejoin Dori and Company.Find Dori and Company on the road into the Ered MithrinDori and Company can be found along the road into the Ered Mithrin.

You have discovered Dori and Company resting before a large stone structure along the road into the Ered Mithrin. You should talk to Dori.Talk to Dori at Furuk-mekhem, the Southgate'Very well!

'I am not the young dwarf I once was, ${PLAYER}, but I am hale still in my old age, so I will lead the Company. However, I must find those willing to follow me into the Ered Mithrin for no promise of wealth or glory beyond my own gratitude. I know a few dwarves whom I can trust with such a task, and so I will speak to them myself.

'Ah, but such a company need not only be comprised of dwarves. After all, old Mr. Baggins was not a dwarf and he proved an invaluable ally of Durin's Folk. You are a strong ${RACE}, well-travelled and skilled in the ways of battle. Will you join me?'

You nod in assent.

'Good, good! We shall have need of a great many things in our travels, and so I would ask that you gather as many coils of rope, water-skins, and bedrolls as you can bear. Once you have done that, you should meet me and the others in Nori's home. Let us hope it is enough!

'Are we understood, ${PLAYER}? Swiftly now!''Very good, ${PLAYER}.

'After dusk, we will gather before the gates of Erebor and begin our journey into the Ered Mithrin under the cover of darkness.

'If you have any unfinished business in Erebor, I would see to it. The hour soon approaches!''It is time, ${PLAYER}.

'Our preparations are made, our goats are watered and well-rested, but one matter still remains.'

Dori gestures at the massive chest of gold and gem-stones before him.

'This is but a small measure of my share of the treasure reclaimed from Smaug the Golden, and it is still greater wealth than most dwarves will ever know.

'I want you to deliver it to Ravenhill, ${PLAYER}. The raven-chief, Arcah, and her people are fond of all that glimmers, and if her people would aid me in the search for my brother, I offer it all. I do not know if I could deliver it by my own hand, and so, I ask this of you.

'Once you have finished this business at Ravenhill, follow the long road beneath its heights into the Ered Mithrin. The Company shall await you along the road.''Ah, ${PLAYER}! It is done, then? Very good.

'I have known the raven-chiefs to be wise counselors, but whether their counsel is good or ill, they speak with sharp tongues. Has the raven-chief offered you any counsel on our task?'

You inform Dori that the raven-chief warned that Thikil-gundu was an accursed place, and that she would not send her ravens there.

'Accursed? I do not think so, ${PLAYER}. Thikil-gundu has been the doom of many Longbeards, but there is no curse upon it! A curse of dragon-kind, perhaps... but nothing more.

'That is enough talk of Thikil-gundu for now, I think. Let us hope the ravens find some sign of my brother's passage through the Ered Mithrin.''Why have you come, ${PLAYER}?''Ah, why have you come to Ravenhill?'

You open the chest of gold and gem-stones before Arcah, and explain that Dori has offered a share of his treasure in exchange for the aid of the ravens.

'Aid of what sort, ${RACE}? These glimmering trinkets and baubles please me, but I would know what is to be promised if I offer our aid.'

You explain that Dori's brother has taken an expedition to the Ered Mithrin in secret and seeks for Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep.

'That is an accursed place, ${PLAYER}. You may send three of my conspiracy into the Grey Mountains to search for Nori and his kinsfolk, but I will allow none to fly too near the Steel Keep.

'I have spoken.''Humph!

'Of all the dwarves I hoped to gather, I have found only four. I spoke first to the dwarves I travelled with on the quest of old to the Lonely Mountain, but only Bofur and Glóin have agreed to join us. Bifur offered no reason for his refusal, but I do not doubt he has seen enough of battle. As for the others, Dwalin must tend to his duties at Thorin's Hall in the Ered Luin, and I would not ask Bombur or the fine dwarves who bear him about to make such a journey.

'Ah, but the elder of the Stout-axes, the one named Spakorth... he is a keen dwarf! I spoke to him of our task in the Ered Mithrin, and he offered two of his finest warriors to me. Náth and Dréri are young in the years of dwarves, but if the wisdom of my kinsfolk should fail us, the might of the Stout-axes will deliver us from trouble.

'I have spoken enough of such things! You have gathered what I asked, and now you should prepare six packs. One for each of the dwarves of the Company, and one for my brother, Nori, for when he is found.

'Once that is done, return to me.'Although many weapons were stolen by the Easterlings, Grámi Hornhelm has asked you to deliver what weapons they can spare to Járnfast.The Hammerstead weapons have been crated and are ready for delivery.

You should collect the weapon crate.Collect the weapon crateHógur is expecting this weapon shipment in Járnfast.

You should talk to Hógur.Talk to Hógur'Since you are heading to Járnfast, please take this crate of weapons with you. They were expecting a much larger convoy, but all we have to spare now are the ones you successfully recovered from the Easterlings. And of course, the dagger you smithed yourself. It will have to do. Bring them to Hógur, one of Lord Náin's advisors.''This is all that is left after the Easterling attack? Lord Náin must act soon, before we are overwhelmed by these curs. Thank you for your assistance in this matter, ${PLAYER}.'Some of the Easterlings in Utterby are too sick to get out of the village before the dwarves attack. Find medicine and heal them before it is too late.Dalish medicine can be found inside Utterby.

You should find the Dalish medicine.Acquire Dalish medicineIll refugees can be found inside Utterby.

You should offer medicine to ill refugees.Treat ill refugees (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Tuhâl, who can be found in Utterby.Talk to Tuhâl'Look around, friend. Some of my people are too sick to leave the village. They are in grave danger if this place becomes a battlefield. I know for a fact that the Dale-folk left medicine behind, but the Jangovar are not letting anyone else have access to it! Hmm. If we are careful, we may steal it out from under their noses.''Thank you helping my people, ${PLAYER}. Once all of this is over, we can have another chance at a new life, hopefully without wolves like the Jangovar feeding upon us.'A bounty notice asks you to seek out Blackhorn, a maddened wild beast in Ered Mithrin.Blackhorn and other mountain rams can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should defeat rams.Defeat BlackhornDefeat mountain rams (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Blackhorn.CompletedThe bounty notice is written in a messy and hasty scrawl. It reads:

'He stood atop a great cliff, and I knew he spied me from afar. Blackhorn, mightiest of the rams that rule the Ered Mithrin. Stalked me for days, he did; I can still hear the sound of his hooves on the stone behind me! Clop. Clop. Clop! When will it end?

'I offer a reward to any who can rid these mountains of Blackhorn and the rest of his kin. I never want to hear the sound of hooves on stone ever again! Clop! Clop! Clop!'You have defeated Blackhorn and assisted the expedition.A bounty notice asks you to seek out Grazadhrú, a terrible creature in Ered Mithrin.Grazadhrú and wood-trolls can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should defeat wood-trolls.Defeat GrazadhrúDefeat Ered Mithrin wood-trolls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Grazadhrú.CompletedThe bounty notice is written on coarse paper. It reads:

'I thought it was a tree at first, but what tree could exude such menace? It was more troll than tree, if you want this dwarf's opinion. What do you do with a thing like that? Chop it down? Stack it for lumber? I don't know.

'What I do know is that something should be done about it. I offer a reward for anyone who can uproot some of these wood-trolls from the Ered Mithrin. If you see the worst of the lot, the one that folk of these high places call Grazadhrú, be sure to give him your special attention. I don't like trees at the best of times, but these wood-trolls make for the worst of times, if you ask me!'You have defeated Grazadhrú and assisted the expedition.A bounty notice asks you to seek out Gristlefell, a ravenous wild beast in Ered Mithrin.Gristlefell and Mithrin láthbears can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should defeat láthbears.Defeat GristlefellDefeat Mithrin láthbears (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Gristlefell.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'Who among you has not known the fear of a bear's call in the woods? It is a warning to keep away, a distant threat. But it is different here in the Ered Mithrin. Here, the láthbear will give you little notice of its ire; before you know you are hunted, it is upon you, its red eyes full of hate, its sharp teeth slavering!

'Of all the láthbears that prowl these mountains, none is more dangerous than the one named Gristlefell. A reward to any who can bring him down, along with others of his sloth!'You have defeated Gristlefell and assisted the expedition.A bounty notice asks you to seek out Holkorm, a ravenous wild beast in Ered Mithrin.Holkorm and Mithrin worms can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should defeat worms.Defeat HolkormDefeat Mithrin worms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Holkorm.CompletedThe bounty notice is written in a flowery script. Its author must have taken awhile to set down his message. It reads:

'We Zhélruka prize our claim to these mountains, but the rocky cliffs are home to other less noble denizens. The Mithrin worms claim them as well, and do not willingly surrender their hold! Strike them down and show we are, as ever, to be feared!

'But I urge caution in one respect. Worms of a reddish, coppery hue will put up a fight but can be bested. But if you see a worm with ice-blue scales, you may have found Holkorm, more ferocious than these others. He may be a worm of a different brood; I cannot say for certain. I offer a reward to any who can rid these mountains of Holkorm and thin the ranks of the Mithrin worms that threaten our expedition.'You have defeated Holkorm and assisted the expedition.A bounty notice asks you to seek out Keenclaw, a ravenous wild beast in Ered Mithrin.Keenclaw and grey hawks can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should defeat hawks.Defeat KeenclawDefeat grey hawks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Keenclaw.CompletedThe bounty notice is written in a shaky hand, as if the author feared he might be interrupted. It reads:

'The air is thin up here, they all said it. You might be able to see further than in the low-lands, but the thin air makes it harder to hear sounds from far away, especially with the wind whistling among the rocks the way it does. I didn't hear the hawks until it was nearly too late. They flew down from the heights, clawing and scratching, trying to get at my eyes. My hands are covered in scratches, but at least I kept my eyes! For now, anyway, for now! Cursed birds!

'I will give a reward to any who slay the grey hawks of the Ered Mithrin, especially the largest and foulest of them. Keenclaw I have named him. He will never have my eyes, no matter how much he desires to dine upon them!'You have defeated Keenclaw and assisted the expedition.A bounty notice asks you to seek out Moorstalker, a ravenous wild beast in Ered Mithrin.Moorstalker and Mithrin wolves can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should defeat wolves.Defeat MoorstalkerDefeat Mithrin wolves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Moorstalker.CompletedThe bounty notice is written in a messy scrawl. It reads:

'I heard a howling among the high cliffs, and it echoed from rock to rock until I could no longer remember the silence beforehand! It was not the howl of one wolf, no, it could not be; it seemed to be the howling of every wolf that ever stalked the high crags of the mountains. But one was first. One set them all going, and I named him Moorstalker, larger and more frightening than the others.

'My brethren call me mad, but I know what I heard. I offer a reward for any who can bring down Moorstalker and the others of his pack. Let the Ered Mithrin no longer echo with the sound of their call, and I will know my reward was well-earned!'You have defeated Moorstalker and assisted the expedition.Satisfied with his efforts against the Jangovar in the Ironfold, Durin is now ready to return to the Lonely Mountain with you, but first you need to find Authi Gem-cutter and make sure he is ready to go home.Authi Gem-cutter is in the Quenching Trough.Talk to Authi Gem-cutter in the Quenching TroughKhîl is in the Quenching Trough, quite distraught.Talk to Khîl in the Quenching TroughKhîl is in the Quenching Trough, quite distraught.Talk to Khîl and travel to his father's homeNáin is in the hidden chamber in Khîl's home in Járnfast.Talk to Náin in the hidden chamber'I am ready to return to the Lonely Mountain with you, ${PLAYER}. I understand there is to be another dwarf travelling with us? Yes, Authi Gem-cutter. Where is he to be found?

'The Quenching Trough? You are welcome to go there and retrieve him, friend. I have spent more than enough time within those walls, and do not desire to spend any more. Now my thirst can only be quenched by battle and glory, and that lies beyond Járnfast!

'I will meet up with you after you have found Authi.''You are welcome to go to the Quenching Trough and retrieve Authi Gem-cutter, friend. I have spent more than enough time within those walls, and do not desire to spend any more. Now my thirst can only be quenched by battle and glory, and that lies beyond Járnfast!

'I will meet up with you after you have found Authi.''Oh! ${PLAYER}! My friend! I have discovered something most unusual!''Have you come to save me, ${PLAYER}? I simply wanted to drink in peace, but Khîl has come here and will not stop bothering me! Will you help him with whatever nonsense he is chattering about?''Khîl has come here and will not stop bothering me! Will you help him with whatever nonsense he is chattering about?''I have been cleaning out my father's house, ${PLAYER}, and I... well, I found something unusual. I have no friends here in Járnfast save for you, so there is nowhere else for me to turn!

'Will you come help me make sense of this? Please tell me you will!''You might as well go with him, ${PLAYER}. After you see whatever has him so worked up we can set out for our return to the Lonely Mountain.''You'll come see? Good, good! I really appreciate this, my friend!''What does this mean? For how long has this chamber remained hidden right beneath our very noses? I do not like secrets in my city, ${PLAYER}, especially when those secrets come from the land of Mordor.'Within the rune-covered walls of the secret chamber in the home of Khîl Tale-spinner you found a black-bound book written by his thrice-great grandfather Vóin and hidden long ago.Khîl Tale-spinner is in the hidden chamber in his father's house where you discovered the Black Book of Mordor.Talk to Khîl in the hidden chamberThe Black Book of Mordor is on the table in the hidden chamber in Khîl's family home in Járnfast.Pick up the Black Book of Mordor from the table where it liesDurin is in the hidden chamber in Khîl's family home in Járnfast.Talk to Durin in the hidden chamberDurin wanted to meet up on the road west of Utterby to admire his handiwork.Durin has spied a large party heading west on the road to the Lonely Mountain, and he wants to investigate.Catch up to the large party heading westBótuz Frostblood leads a group of Zhélruka toward the Dale-lands.Talk to Bótuz Frostblood among the large Zhélruka forceDurin stands among the group of Zhélruka that intends to cross the Dale-lands.Talk to Durin among the large Zhélruka forceKing Thorin III is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Hurry to King Thorin in the Hall Under the MountainKing Thorin III is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the MountainGandalf is at his chambers on the upper level of the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.'My people have had a friendly relationship with the Zhélruka for thousands of years, ${PLAYER}. When our halls were cracked and damaged in the devastation that followed the fall of Thangorodrim, it is recorded that the Zhélruka stayed for a time in the Iron Hills and participated in the Great Mending. Most of that people went on to Gundabad, though they never arrived and their fate is shrouded in mystery. Over the centuries since, small families of Zhélruka came to live in the Ironfold and here in Járnfast, and rarely caused trouble.

'But look at this chamber, ${PLAYER}! What evils might have been worked within its walls? What foul sorcery was recorded within that book? The sigil on its cover is known to me, indeed it is known to all of Durin's Folk: the Eye of Sauron, feared and despised! Did Khîl's forebears worship Sauron at the height of his powers? Do the Zhélruka worship him still, even following his defeat? Were you privy to this knowledge, Khîl? What have you to say for yourself, villain?

'This book cannot remain in Járnfast! I will not allow it! It must be taken from the Iron Hills! It remained hidden near my people for too long, and now that I know of its presence I will no longer tolerate it! Take it to your Wizard friend, ${PLAYER}. Take it to Gandalf, and let him decide what to do about the evil thing!''This book cannot remain in Járnfast! I will not allow it! It must be taken from the Iron Hills! It remained hidden near my people for too long, and now that I know of its presence I will no longer tolerate it! Take it to your Wizard friend, ${PLAYER}. Take it to Gandalf, and let him decide what to do about the evil thing!''Lord Náin may not believe me, but you must, ${PLAYER}! Don't you? I swear to you I had no knowledge of either chamber or tome! My thrice-great grandfather Vóin lived more than a thousand years ago, and of his line I knew only my father Bránit; each of the others were names only, names in a family tree I did not see the need to study!

'Do you think my father knew of this room? He cannot have! Certainly my mother Zéma would not have permitted such an artifact as that tome of Mordor to remain within her walls, so near to her child! It is preposterous, ${PLAYER}. And what of these runes? I have not studied the language of my people in many years, and the words are quite difficult for me to read. I think they might speak of Thafar-gathol, the lost Grey Citadel sought by both Zhélruka and Longbeard alike, but I cannot say for certain. That is not an evil place, though it be shrouded in mystery. The book, however, I cannot explain. What would Vóin have to do with artifacts of Mordor?

'I like it no more than Lord Náin, ${PLAYER}. Pick up the book and carry it to Gandalf with my approval. Present it to him for study, and if he determines that it poses no danger, I ask that you return it to me. Unpleasant as it seems, this tome is an artifact of my house and I would not have it lost, unless the Wizard confirms that it be wholly evil. Even so, I would want to have some form of compensation in that case. The book is more than a thousand years old and of great historical interest for my people and for me personally.''For this chamber to remain undiscovered for so long fills me with amazement, ${PLAYER}, but I do not fear the room or its contents. The black-bound book has some connexion to Sauron? What is that to me? Sauron is defeated and can trouble us no more. Any dread I would feel at the mention of his name or his works has gone and cannot stir my heart to worry.

'Long did I nurse the resentment of being left behind while my uncle fought in the war, but now we are to return to the Lonely Mountain and I find a spring in my step that was not there before. I aim to prove myself to my people and to the world, and the memory of Sauron cannot diminish my eagerness!

'Let us set forth, ${PLAYER}. Let us go to Utterby and admire our handiwork there before we continue on to the Lonely Mountain and King Thorin. You can deliver the black book to Gandalf while I speak with my father.''Long did I nurse the resentment of being left behind while my uncle fought in the war, but now we are to return to the Lonely Mountain and I find a spring in my step that was not there before. I aim to prove myself to my people and to the world, and the memory of Sauron cannot diminish my eagerness!

'Let us set forth, ${PLAYER}. Let us go to Utterby and admire our handiwork there before we continue on to the Lonely Mountain and King Thorin. You can deliver the black book to Gandalf while I speak with my father.''Did you see? The Jangovar surrounding Utterby remain in disarray, like as not a result of our efforts there.

'But that is not what has drawn my attention, ${PLAYER}. A large party has taken this road westward; I spy dust on the horizon, kicked up from the road as they passed. Could it be the Easterlings attempt another attack on the Lonely Mountain? Let us follow them and ensure they do not!''A large party has taken this road westward; I spy dust on the horizon, kicked up from the road as they passed. Could it be the Easterlings attempt another attack on the Lonely Mountain? Let us follow them and ensure they do not!''What brings this Zhélruka force here? Who leads this band, and for what purpose?''Why are these Zhélruka here?''What brings this Zhélruka force here? Who leads this band, and for what purpose?''Why are these Zhélruka here?''You are with these Longbeards, ${RACE}? I am Bótuz Frostblood, leader of this Zhélruka band. I hope you have answers for me, for my patience is nearly spent. It has been days since my axe has drawn blood, and she is thirsty! Tell me what transpires in Erebor, and tell me quickly!

'You came from the east and not the west? Bah! We have been waiting here for our messenger to return, a dwarf of good speed and loyal temperament, but he is hours overdue. He was to speak with the King Under the Mountain and obtain permission for us to cross the Dale-lands on our way to the Grey Mountains. Why has he not returned? Why has he not sent word? I smell treachery, ${RACE}! Has King Thorin imprisoned him? This insult cannot stand!

'We will wait for a little while longer, but my Zhélruka will not wait long! We sent word to the king as a matter of courtesy, but I begin to question whether such courtesy was deserved! No one, not even a king, can get away with insulting Bótuz Frostblood! Perhaps I and my Zhélruka will force our way through the Dale-lands to our promised lands among the Grey Mountains, and spit upon Thorin's permission!''My people have the right to cross to our lands in the Grey Mountains, no matter what King Thorin says about it! I sent my messenger to be polite, but now I begin to think the king did not deserve even that measure of courtesy!''I do not like the tone of this Zhélruka! He should show more respect to King Thorin and to the Longbeards!''These Zhélruka are getting impatient, and I fear what might happen if they seek to force their way across the Dale-lands. If it comes to violence...

'I will remain here with Authi, ${PLAYER}, and we will do our best to calm them. You hurry on to the Lonely Mountain and tell my father that Bótuz Frostblood and his band grow tired of waiting. Perhaps the king had a good reason for keeping the Zhélruka messenger from returning?

'Go, ${PLAYER}, and with haste! I fear the Zhélruka will not wait much longer, and the situation here will get worse if it is not resolved quickly.''I will remain here with Authi, ${PLAYER}, and we will do our best to calm them. You hurry on to the Lonely Mountain and tell my father that Bótuz Frostblood and his band grow tired of waiting. Perhaps the king had a good reason for keeping the Zhélruka messenger from returning?

'Go, ${PLAYER}, and with haste! I fear the Zhélruka will not wait much longer, and the situation here will get worse if it is not resolved quickly.''My people have the right to cross to our lands in the Grey Mountains, no matter what King Thorin says about it! I sent my messenger to be polite, but now I begin to think the king did not deserve even that measure of courtesy!''Where is Durin? Did I not send you to bring my son back from Járnfast? How can it be you have not fulfilled this request, ${PLAYER}?

'What? He came with you as far as the edge of the Dale-lands, but has stopped to treat with a band of Zhélruka? I have heard nothing of this, ${PLAYER}! Explain this business!'

You quickly describe your encounter with Bótuz Frostblood and the Zhélruka he leads, and recount Durin's decision to remain behind to keep the situation from worsening.

'These dwarves are liars, ${PLAYER}. I have seen no messenger, and even if I had I would not listen to any request of his! I am not convinced the Zhélruka have a claim to land in the Grey Mountains, and at any rate I have no obligation to allow them to pass by the foot of the Lonely Mountain. Let them go around, if they must, and take the long road that passes well north of here. They shall not pass through my lands and trouble the folk of Dale.

'I will dispatch some warriors to reinforce my son, and they will ensure the Zhélruka stay put. ${PLAYER}, go find Gandalf at his chambers in the Dwelling-halls. Perhaps the threat of Wizard-magic will encourage these Zhélruka to remain peaceable!''These dwarves are liars, ${PLAYER}. I have seen no messenger, and even if I had I would not listen to any request of his! I am not convinced the Zhélruka have a claim to land in the Grey Mountains, and at any rate I have no obligation to allow them to pass by the foot of the Lonely Mountain. Let them go around, if they must, and take the long road that passes well north of here. They shall not pass through my lands and trouble the folk of Dale.

'I will dispatch some warriors to reinforce my son, and they will ensure the Zhélruka stay put. ${PLAYER}, go find Gandalf at his chambers in the Dwelling-halls. Perhaps the threat of Wizard-magic will encourage these Zhélruka to remain peaceable!''What is this you bring to me, ${PLAYER}?'

Gandalf takes the black book, warily eyeing the sigil on the cover. You describe the hidden chamber in which the book was found, and the Wizard strokes his beard thoughtfully. He carefully opens the book and turns several of its leaves.

'Some of these pages contain words written in the Black Speech, but there are also dwarf-runes of several varieties, and many blank pages. I suspect these gaps are not truly empty, but contain words written in the hidden fashion used by dwarves to keep secrets. Most interesting! This book clearly has its origin in Mordor, but the Mordor of a thousand years ago is not the same as it is today. Truly, even in a month's time Mordor has greatly changed!

'I will study this book with interest, but I see from your expression that more than one purpose has drawn you hither.''Do you think my father knew of this room? He cannot have! Certainly my mother Zéma would not have permitted such an artifact as that tome of Mordor to remain within her walls, so near to her child! It is preposterous, ${PLAYER}. And what of these runes? I have not studied the language of my people in many years, and the words are quite difficult for me to read. I think this one might speak of Thafar-gathol, the lost Grey Citadel sought by both Zhélruka and Longbeard alike, but I cannot say for certain. That is not an evil place, though it be shrouded in mystery. The book, however, I cannot explain. What would Vóin have to do with artifacts of Mordor?

'I like it no more than Lord Náin, ${PLAYER}. Pick up the book and carry it to Gandalf with my approval. Present it to him for study, and if he determines that it poses no danger, I ask that you return it to me. Unpleasant as it seems, this tome is an artifact of my house and I would not have it lost, unless the Wizard confirms that it be wholly evil. Even so, I would want to have some form of compensation in that case. The book is more than a thousand years old and of great historical interest for my people and for me personally.'The Jangovar climb ranks by beating their betters in combat. Perhaps if you are able to defeat some of them yourself, the Jangovar will look upon you favourably.Jáldor can be found somewhere inside Utterby.

