King Thorin III Stonehelm has grown weary of rising tensions among the Dwarves of Erebor, and has asked you to listen to the grievances of his people that he might better understand them.King Thorin III can be found before his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.

You have heard the grievances of the dwarves of Erebor, and you should now return to King Thorin III.Talk to King Thorin IIIDori can be found in the Hall under the Mountain.

King Thorin III has asked you to deliver the missive Nori left behind before he departed Erebor to Dori in the Hall under the Mountain.'Hail, ${PLAYER}. I was sent to deliver a message to you by the King under the Mountain!

'King Thorin requests an audience in the Hall under the Mountain. There are troubling matters afoot in Erebor, and he seeks your counsel and skill to aid him in settling them.''Hail, ${PLAYER}.

'I am pleased that you have returned to Erebor, but I wish it were under better circumstances. It seems only more troubles have come to Erebor since the defeat of the great cold-drake, Vethúg Wintermind.

'There is much to be said of such things, and I would hear your counsel. Where shall I begin?''Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior... not since the days of Azog has a name been so reviled by my people! 

'His cunning drew our foe of old, a great cold-drake, to Erebor. Dwarves, Elves, and Men battled against Vethúg Wintermind, but it seems our defeat of the great cold-drake only served to further his designs for the North.

'You see, after Vethúg fled the battle, Karazgar followed him into the Ered Mithrin. By Durin's beard, nothing good can come of that!

'Karazgar has left us in peace, but he will return. I am certain of it!''The great cold-drake, Vethúg Wintermind, has long been known to my people.

'When the Longbeards still dwelled among the Grey Mountains in the Dwarf-kingdom of Grárik, my people discovered a great delving beneath the slopes of the Wrathhorn. They restored it and built upon it, naming it Thikil-gundu, or the Steel Keep in the Common Tongue.

'It is true that the first mines and outposts in the Grey Mountains were delved by the Zhélruka, but the halls of the Steel Keep were shaped by the craft of the Longbeards. I will not speak of rightful claims or of the old conflict between our peoples -- they matter no longer. It was a great cold-drake that stole Thikil-gundu from us! Vethúg Wintermind!

'Vethúg devoured Dáin I, and slew his son, Frór. Grárik was ended, and if not for the flight of Thrór and Grór, the Longbeards would not have survived.

'Mere weeks after the War had ended, Karazgar drew Vethúg Wintermind to Erebor, and though my people suffered great losses, the cold-drake was defeated... but not slain.

'Whether he seeks to tend his wounds or die atop our hoards of old, I am certain he has returned to the halls of Thikil-gundu.''After Vethúg was defeated and Karazgar travelled into the North, I issued an edict forbidding any expeditions to the lost halls of Thikil-gundu.

'It seemed that my people had come to understand my wisdom, or so I thought. 

'It was then I was betrayed by one of my father's oldest and closest friends. Nori, hero of Erebor and Keeper of the Coffers, had gathered an expedition in secret and set out in the night.

'In anger, I thought to leave him to his fate, but that is not the way of a King under the Mountain.''And now you understand, ${PLAYER}.

'It is true that Vethúg Wintermind was defeated, but that does not mean that Thikil-gundu has been left unguarded. Karazgar has pursued Vethúg into the Grey Mountains, and there are Orcs of Gundabad lurking in the foothills. If the Zhélruka hope to reclaim the Steel Keep, they will have to contend with the dangers of the Grey Mountains... and I am inclined to let them.

'However, their passage into the Ered Mithrin has not gone unnoticed by my people. Even now, they gather before my throne in anger and restlessness.

'Listen well to their grievances, ${PLAYER}, and then we will see if I cannot put their minds at ease.''It is as I expected, but not as I had hoped, ${PLAYER}.

'My edict was not firm enough to withstand the passage of the Zhélruka... and my people will not heed it. Nori has proven that much to be true. I may hold the rest of them back for days, weeks, or even years, but it will not last. There must be another way....'

King Thorin produces a small missive and glares at it.

'Nori left a missive behind when he made for Thikil-gundu with his expedition. I would ask that you deliver it to Dori.

'I think now it is time that he learns what his brother has done!''What is this?'

Dori begins to read the missive and his face sinks.

'This is some trick, ${PLAYER}. It must be!

'Nori would not have done this!'The Jangovar have been holding what food there is for themselves, leaving many to starve. Give food to the hungry refugees so they can flee the village.Many of the refugees are starving, as the Jangovar hold all the food for themselves.

You should offer food to hungry refugees.Offer food to hungry refugees (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Tuhâl, who can be found in Utterby.Talk to Tuhâl'The Jangovar have been holding what food there is for themselves, leaving many to starve. Now that we have some food, we should give it to those most in need. It should give them strength enough to flee this terrible place.''They were so grateful just to receive a few scraps of food.... The sooner we get out of here, the better.'The food stores of Utterby are beginning to run out, so the Easterlings must find a local edible flora to get by.You should talk to Tuhâl, who can be found in Utterby.Talk to TuhâlEdible plants can be found in the scrublands near Utterby.Collect edible plants (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the edible plants to Kúlkhur, who can be found in Utterby.Bring the plants to Kúlkhur'We only have a small amount of grain left in the stores, so we must find something else to eat. There must be edible plants out there somewhere, or else there would be no animals here, either. Well? What are you waiting for? Go find me some plants!''These foul cabbages are all you could find? Perhaps the taste can be boiled off... as for you, well, at least you can follow instructions.''He is right, there must be edible plants out there. Whether or not they are palatable is another matter! Let us also keep some of these plants for ourselves, to help feed my people.'Help Rúzat gather way-food to be eaten on the trail.Nuts and berries can be found in eastern Ered Mithrin near Erebor.

You should collect pine nuts and wild berries.Gather pine nuts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather wild berries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the nuts and berries to Rúzat at Dôm Goru in Ered Mithrin.'Humble Rúzat, at your service. True, true, you will not find all Zhélruka so open to kízinek - pardon me, to outsiders - as I. The world has taught us no such lessons, I am afraid. I, myself, might have turned my head away and spoken no words to you on the weary, dusty roads that lie behind. Yet the moment I set my eyes upon these grand peaks, these open spaces, something in my heart opened, too.

'No, friend, I will be truly honest with you. We are in need of way-food. We cannot tarry to cook, for we must keep moving and join  with our národ, up ahead. We have stumbled upon some nuts and berries already, but could use more. 

'Would you, who are well-rested and full of vigour, gather some as you go, and meet me at Dôm Goru, a small camp up the road?''Find what nuts and berries you may, my friend, and then meet me at Dôm Goru, a camp along the road.''Ah, this mixture will do. We have grown hungry from so much walking. All my brethren will be grateful for your efforts!'The food stores of Utterby are beginning to run out, so the Easterlings must turn to the local fauna for meat.Tuhâl seems to have something to say about the Jangovar's orders.

You should talk to Tuhâl, who can be found in Utterby.Talk to TuhâlAnimals can be found among the scrublands of the Ironfold.

You should hunt animals for their meat.Collect animal meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the meat to Kúlkhur, who can be found inside Utterby.Bring the meat to Kúlkhur'Come here, Chayasír. If you really want to be useful, go hunting for us. The food left by the Dalish is running low, and we must find more. It seems there are few game animals here to hunt, but that is your problem to figure out!''What is this meat? You got it from what? Ugh, it smells foul! I only hope we will soon move on to the Dale-lands, where we can eat meat fit for Men again!''What do they expect? I have not seen any deer or boar here, have you? I suppose we will just have to hunt what we can find out in the scrub and make due. We should save some of what we find, as well. The Jangovar have been keeping all the food for themselves, leaving everyone else to go hungry.'Desperate to learn more of Nori's passage into the Ered Mithrin, Dori hopes to parley with the Zhélruka that have come seeking their ancestral home of Dómekh.Dôm Goru can be found somewhere to the north-west of Furuk-mekhem, the Southgate.

