After returning to Zádokh Flint-eye with the dragon-stone you discovered, he informed you that Dori and Company have come to Dôm Goru. You should speak to Dori and tell him what you have learned.Talk to Dori at Dôm Goru''There is another matter to discuss, ${PLAYER}.

'While we waited on your return, a small company of Zhélruka passed through the Southgate. They spoke in hushed tones, but I heard enough. It seems they make for a small encampment named Dôm Goru in the foothills of the Ered Mithrin.

'The Zhélruka are not the most cooperative of dwarves, but I have known them to be keen observers. Either this company or their kinsfolk might know of Nori's passage into the Ered Mithrin... or perhaps where Vethúg Wintermind has fallen. Ask what you must, but do not speak of Thikil-gundu. The rightful claim to the Steel Keep is still a matter of contention between our peoples, and it would be unwise to provoke them needlessly.

'It seemed from their tone that they did not intend to remain there long, and so, I must ask you to ride ahead of our company.

'Once we have readied ourselves, we will join you at Dôm Goru.''Ah, what brings a ${RACE} like you so far into the Grey Mountains?'

You explain to Zádokh Flint-eye that you seek a company of Longbeards that passed through the Grey Mountains and that you have also come in search of the remains of the cold-drake, Vethúg Wintermind.

'Zádokh Flint-eye is my name, and if you are in search of something, I will know where to find it. Indeed, the fates of those Longbeards and the cold-drake are known to me, but information of that sort is valuable... very valuable.

'I will offer it to you... for a price. The worms, drakes, and Dragons of the Grey Mountains have slowed my journey. If you were to remove some of them from my path, I could be convinced to share what I know.

'Do we have a deal?''Drakes and worms? I think you misheard me, ${RACE}.

'I asked you to defeat dragons... not their feeble offspring! Do you expect me to part with any worthwhile information, and information about Longbeards no less, for such a pittance?

'You would have saved the both of us time if you had just handed me a bag of gold. In fact, why don't you do that?

'Ten gold coins, and I'll tell you what I know. It is a fair price for such knowledge!''Very good. 

'Listen well, ${RACE}. You'll have to scrounge around for ten more gold coins if you want me to repeat myself.

'You spoke of the cold-drake known to the Longbeards as Vethúg Wintermind. I know where he has gone! Such was the weight of his fall into the Grey Mountains that the ground was torn asunder. He fell broken into the foothills of Winterstith and left the Bitter Scar in his wake. Neither the Zhélruka nor the Longbeards knew such a ruin in the earlier years of the Grey Mountains, but it is here now, I assure you.

'Ah, and if you wish to learn the destination of your Longbeard friends, you'll see it soon enough.

'Have you heard of dragon-stone? It is normally found deep beneath the earth, but the creation of that black cleft by the Longbeards' cold-drake might have revealed some long-hidden deposits.

'If you find even the smallest fragment of dragon-stone in the Bitter Scar, give it to me and I will tell you where the Longbeards have gone.''Do you have it? The dragon-stone?'

You produce a small fragment of dragon-stone and show it to Zádokh Flint-eye. His eyes widen in awe.

'I did not even believe it to be true! It is beautiful!'

Zádokh Flint-eye pockets the dragon-stone fragment and appears to collect himself.

'You have done as I asked, and I am nothing if not a dwarf of my word. The Longbeards have gone to Brúgrad, but they do not name it as we do. It is known to them as Tharrazhâr, and to those who speak in the Common Tongue: Skarháld.

'If they have remained there, they will not be as welcome as they may think. The great warriors of my people have travelled for many miles, and they are not eager to share in the lost wealth of the Grey Mountains.

'That is all I have to tell. Leave me to my gold!''I had begun to think you continued to Thikil-gundu without us, ${PLAYER}! Did you learn anything of my brother or Vethúg Wintermind?'

You explain that Vethúg Wintermind crashed onto the foothills of the mountain, Winterstith, and left the Bitter Scar in his wake. You also mention that Nori's expedition passed through the old dwarf-hold of Skarháld in the Ered Mithrin.

'Skarháld lies upon the Men Angrún, but the matter of Vethúg Wintermind cannot be put off any longer.'Khîl has found something in his father's house that fills him with worry, and he has asked for your help to investigate it.Khîl has brought you to his father's house. He is consumed with worry about something he discovered there.Talk to Khîl in his father's houseKhîl has brought you to his father's house. He is consumed with worry about something he discovered there.Listen to Khîl's explanationKhîl has asked you to search his father's house for some means of opening the secret door he discovered.Khîl has asked you to search his father's house for some means of opening the secret door he discovered.Khîl has asked you to search his father's house for some means of opening the secret door he discovered.Return to KhîlKhîl is standing in front of the secret door, but it remains closed.Talk to Khîl by the secret doorKhîl has asked you to search his father's house for some means of opening the secret door he discovered.Khîl stands by the secret door, studying the runes that appeared on its face.Talk to Khîl by the secret doorKhîl is standing in front of the secret door, trying to learn the words that will open it.Help Khîl discover the words that will open the doorKhîl has discovered the word that opens the secret door: Vóin, the name of his great-great-great-grandfather.Talk to Khîl outside the secret doorKhîl has discovered the word that opens the secret door: Vóin, the name of his great-great-great-grandfather. Now he wishes to explore the chamber.Explore the hidden chamber with KhîlKhîl is inside the hidden chamber in his father's house.Talk to Khîl inside the hidden chamber'I have not been back here for many years. My father Bránit and I had a falling out and he told me never to return. He died some time after that, neither of us having apologised, and I could not bring myself to come back.

'But when I did live in this house, I never knew of anything like this!'It is impossible to tell exactly when this illustration might have been painted. An armoured dwarf commands his warriors to assault a forest encampment of Orcs against a backdrop of flames. Is this a painting of Mirkwood, or other woods entirely?

You feel around the edges of the frame and behind the painting for a hidden lever or other device, but find nothing.Thinking the ore-filled barrow might contain a hidden lever or other mechanism, you search it closely but find nothing out of the ordinary. It rolls on its front wheel and is not rooted to the floor as you might have expected.

At last you dig around in the contents of the barrow, but your search reveals nothing suspicious.You inspect the desk Khîl moved away from the wall and note the scrapes on the floor caused by the movement. The drawer contains nothing of interest, and although you search beneath and behind the heavy stone of the desk, you find no apparatus that might connect it to the secret door.

The desk does not appear to be involved with opening the door, and served only to keep its location a secret from idle eyes.You cannot move the heavy stone of the bed by yourself, but you inspect its sides and back for hidden sigils or devices and find none. Reaching under the mattress, you disturb only dust and long-dead insects.This bed is too heavy to move. You search all around its sides and back but find only cobwebs and dust, and nothing suspicious.You reach under this corner of the table and find it covered in a film of ancient dust. The dirt smears and you wipe it off on your clothes.You reach under this corner of the table and feel a series of small scratches that merit closer inspection. You study the scratches and discover a crude picture of what seems to be a bed, with a line emanating from its very centre.You reach under the corner of the table and disturb an insect that flies away, buzzing angrily. You doubt the insect knows anything about the secret door so you let it escape.You reach under the corner of the table and find only dust. Even so, it does not dissuade you from your search and you only relent when you are certain this corner of the table holds no secrets.It is impossible to tell exactly when this illustration might have been painted. An armoured dwarf commands his warriors to assault a forest encampment of Orcs against a backdrop of flames. Is this a painting of Mirkwood, or other woods entirely?

