Dori has begged King Thorin III to grant him leave to pursue his brother into the Ered Mithrin. You should now speak to King Thorin III.Talk to King Thorin IIIDori can be found before the throne of King Thorin III in the Hall under the Mountain.

King Thorin III has granted Dori leave to pursue Nori into the Ered Mithrin, but Dori must now meet his terms. You should speak to Dori.Talk to Dori'I do not understand, ${PLAYER}.

'What has compelled my brother to act so foolishly? He has lived a life of wealth in Erebor! He is the Keeper of the Coffers! Or he was....

'Bah! I should have confronted him. I saw dwarves speaking with him in hushed tones, but I thought nothing of it. Nori is a dwarf of some repute, after all.

'If only I knew the names of those dwarves, but... wait. I do recall one of them. This dwarf seemed to be of a line descended from both the Longbeards and the Zhélruka of the East. He was thin in the beard, but any dwarf would be proud to have such a moustache.

'He is a miner, I think. Search for him in the New-lodes of Narathâk, and perhaps he will be better able to explain why my brother has left for Thikil-gundu.''I do not appreciate your gaze. What business have you in the New-lodes?'

You explain to the dwarf that he matches the description of a dwarf Dori believed to be in league with Nori's secret expedition. The dwarf lets out a long sigh.

'Very well... I am Débel. I am not so deeply involved as you might believe, but yes, I know of these things. I suppose the truth would come out sooner or later, but I had hoped it would be after Nori returned to Erebor.

'I cannot give such information freely, but I can offer it for a trifle. If you were to clear out some of the deep-claws that have burrowed into the New-lodes, I give my word that I will share all I know of Nori and his expedition.

'What do you say?''Did I not say I was a dwarf of my word?

'I knew very little of the plans of the Keeper of the Coffers, but I knew enough. He made for the Steel Keep of old... Thikil-gundu as it was once named in the tongue of the dwarves.

'I could not follow him as he wished, but I offered to keep his home in order until his return. I have borne Nori's secret faithfully, but I do not wish to lose the favour of the King under the Mountain.

'Here, take this key. Perhaps Nori left behind some clue for his brother to find....''You found the dwarf, did you? Very good! What did he have to say of my brother?'

You explain to Dori that Débel did not offer much in the way of new information, but he did offer up the key to Nori's home.

'A key? My brother entrusted his key to this Débel before me? We shall have much to discuss when next I see him!

'My brother was not one to leave things unorganised... he is the Keeper of the Coffers after all! However, if Nori has travelled to Thikil-gundu, I must know his course through the Ered Mithrin. They are unfriendly lands, now home only to Orcs and dragons.

'It is to Tumnu-dûm we must go. Those are the dwelling-halls of Erebor, and it is there Nori makes his home.

'I will await you outside his home. After all, you have the key!''At least Débel did not allow my brother's home to fall into disrepair....'

Dori glances around Nori's home and pauses upon a stack of papers piled on the dining table.

'I think my brother left more hastily than he expected to, ${PLAYER}. Look!

'There are papers piled upon the table, and I see other curiosities left about that I would like to examine. Take a look at those papers and see if my brother has left any hint of his course or the names of other dwarves that we might question.

'Once you have done that, let us meet back here.''My brother believes that because the great cold-drake was defeated that our old hoards lay unguarded? By dragons, perhaps, but the Orcs will not have abandoned them.

'As the King under the Mountain said, Thikil-gundu is best forgotten. The Longbeards have found only misfortune and death in the Steel Keep. If I do not aid my brother, this expedition will be his doom.

'We are forbidden from travelling to Thikil-gundu, but perhaps King Thorin will be merciful upon my family and allow me to pursue him into the Ered Mithrin. It is the only course!

'Come, we must go at once!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: When you continue this quest, you will swiftly travel to the Hall under the Mountain.</rgb>'You have my truest gratitude, King under the Mountain. I will do as you have commanded.'

Dori turns to you.

'What do you say, ${PLAYER}? Let us begin our preparations!'Dori seems fixated upon the King under the Mountain and barely seems to notice your approach.

'King Thorin III Stonehelm, King under the Mountain, I beg leave to travel into the Ered Mithrin. You have forbidden Durin's Folk to return to the Steel Keep, Thikil-gundu, but that is not my purpose. I go in search of my brother, Nori!

'I am a loyal dwarf of Erebor. I have fought and bled in its defence... from the Easterlings... from Bolg! All I ask is the chance to save Nori from his folly!

'Will you grant me leave, King under the Mountain?''I hear your words, Dori of the Company.

'Your deeds and those of your kin are not forgotten to any who dwell in Erebor, but Nori has acted in defiance of my edict. Whether his expedition is successful or not, there will be consequences for him.

'I know well that your brother, Ori, was among those lost in Balin's expedition, and I know as well as you do that this bears a resemblance to our efforts to reclaim Khazad-dûm. Both Balin's expedition and the Iron Garrison have failed, and if future losses may still be prevented, they must be. And so, Dori, I grant you leave to follow your brother and put an end to his expedition.

'You will travel on my behalf, but you will go in secret as your brother did. You may form a company of your own choosing, but do not spread word of this to any untrustworthy ears. In addition, I ask that you find the remains of Vethúg Wintermind. He flew from battle gravely wounded and passed into the Ered Mithrin, but I desire proof of his death.

'Heed my words. Deliver Nori from his folly and retrieve for me a bone of Vethúg Wintermind. Only then will we speak of forgiveness....''You have the spare key, yes?

'Let us see what my brother left behind, ${PLAYER}. I hope it is of some use to us....'Help Rúzat gather warm pelts for the Zhélruka so they may stay warm up in the mountain paths.You should collect wolf and láthbear pelts in the valleys of Ered Mithrin.Collect wolf pelts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect láthbear pelts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the pelts to Rúzat, who can be found at Dôm Goru in Ered Mithrin.'Ah, but it is cold out here! Refreshing - and yet even a dwarf might shiver under his whiskers! We have food now, but we cannot carry a fire with us as we go. What is needed is something warm to wear. Pelts!

'You might win some off the beasts that hide in the valleys. Wolf and láthbear pelts would be warm indeed!  Will you gather the pelts and meet me at the Dôm Goru?''Gather pelts from the wolves and láthbears around here and then meet me in Dôm Goru, a way-camp we have made near to the road.''How vúchut! These pelts are just the thing! You have done much. I will make sure these pelts get to only the coldest dwarves!'Many of Hammerstead's guards have been injured by the Jangovar Easterlings. Lóssi has asked you to do what you can to help them.Guards have become injured in the fight against the Jangovar, outside Hammerstead.

You should aid injured guards.Aid injured guards (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Lóssi, who can be found defending Hammerstead.Talk to Lóssi'Many of my guards have been injured at the hands of these Easterling dogs! Do what you can to patch them up and get them back into the fight!''Good to see them back in fighting shape! Thank you, ${CLASS}.'The Jangovar are harassing Hammerstead, attempting to steal the dwarves' weapons. Defeat their leaders to throw them into disarray!The Jangovar are attacking Hammerstead!

You should defeat the Jangovar leaders.Defeat Boskhín WolfseyeDefeat VulkhósDefeat IltishYou should talk to Lóssi, who can be found defending Hammerstead.Talk to Lóssi'I have seen the Easterlings' leaders out there, barking orders at the rest of these louts. We are pinned down here, but perhaps you can make your way to them and cut the head off this snake!''Good work! With the leaders defeated, the rest will fall into disarray and scatter before us!'Help Rúzat polish the road markers so that the Zhélruka travellers can more easily find their way.You should polish road markers that can be found along the path between Erebor and Dôm Goru in Ered Mithrin.Polish road markers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Rúzat, who can be found at Dôm Goru in Ered Mithrin.Talk to Rúzat at Dôm Goru'I know not how many centuries have passed since dwarf-feet trode upon these paths, yet many Zhélruka do so now! They say upon the well-marked path you cannot go astray.

'Let us do our part to help them and polish up old road markers. I will polish the large ones. Will you look after the smaller ones?''We poor Zhélruka have travelled far, farther than you can imagine, only to reach Erebor. Here the way is harder and harsher than ever. I will linger here and tend to my brethren as they come through.

'You will not linger but go on, I know. Yet I will be ever thankful for your friendly help and the road we shared.'There are still many Jangovar stubbornly holding onto Utterby. Will you help the Ironfold Dwarves clear out the village?Many Jangovar can be found inside Utterby.

You should defeat Jangovar outside Utterby and inside Utterby: Continue the Fight.Defeat Jangovar in Utterby (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat stubborn Jangovar lords (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lord Náin can be found inside of the gates in Utterby.

You should talk to Lord Náin.Talk to Náin'You want to go back inside Utterby? Hah! Don't let me stop you! There are plenty more Jangovar heads to bash together.

'Just speak to me when you are ready and my guards will allow you back inside the gates.''Just let me know when you are ready.''Ready to go? I will escort you back to Lord Náin.''My guards have spoken highly of your skills in combat, ${PLAYER}. They are not easy to impress, believe me! Should you ever wish to help us further, we will be here.'Grámi Hornhelm has asked you to recover what stolen weapons you can, before the Jangovar retreat back to Utterby.The dwarf-made weapons stolen by the Jangovar may still be nearby!

You should recover stolen weapons.Recover stolen weapons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Grámi Hornhelm, who can be found inside Hammerstead, in the northern Ironfold.Talk to Grámi Hornhelm'The weapons from the convoy are probably already in the hands of the Easterlings at Utterby, but you may be able to recover the ones stolen by the soldiers outside. Hurry, before they decide to cut their losses and retreat!'Grámi sighs deeply.

'At least some of our work was recovered. Thank you, ${PLAYER}.'Grámi Hornhelm is grateful for your aid with the Easterlings, and has offered to give you a small taste of the dwarf craft of weaponsmithing.Grámi Hornhelm has requested that you help craft a weapon.

You should talk to Belg Thickshanks to proceed.Talk to Belg ThickshanksBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should use the forge to heat steel.Heat steel in the forgeBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Belg Thickshanks.Talk to Belg ThickshanksBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should hammer the steel on the anvil to shape it.Hammer the steel on the anvilBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Belg Thickshanks.Talk to Belg ThickshanksBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should hone the edge of the blade on the grindstone.Hone the blade on the grindstoneBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Belg Thickshanks.Talk to Belg ThickshanksBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should re-heat the blade in the forge.Re-heat the blade in the forgeBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should quench the red-hot blade in the water tank.Quench the blade in the water tankBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Belg Thickshanks.Talk to Belg ThickshanksBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should assemble the dagger at the assembly table.Assemble the dagger at the assembly tableBelg Thickshanks has asked you to help craft a dagger in Hammerstead.

You should talk to Belg Thickshanks.Talk to Belg ThickshanksYou should talk to Grámi Hornhelm, who  can be found in Hammerstead.Talk to Grámi Hornhelm'Since you have been so helpful, I have decided to let you assist with smithing our next shipment of weapons. Pay attention, and you might just learn something about superior dwarf craftsmanship! Speak to my assistant, Belg Thickshanks. He will show you what to do.'Grámi examines your work with a discerning eye.

'Acceptable work. We will put it with the others.''Master Grámi sent you? Excellent! You are going to help me make a dagger. I will walk you through the process. Take some steel over to the forge. First step, heating it so we can shape it.''Now that the steel is good and hot, you can hammer it into shape. Place it on the anvil, and hammer it into a basic blade. You know what a dagger looks like, don't you?''Good, good, that is the right shape. Now, you need to hone an edge, or that dagger may as well be a club! Take it over to the grindstone.''Nice and sharp? Now, we need to re-heat the metal and quickly quench it in the water tank. Why? Because right now, the blade is brittle. Wouldn't want that dagger to shatter while you're toe-to-toe with an Orc, eh?''Good, good. Now, all that is left is polish and assembly. Take it over to the table there. Attach the guard, grip, and pommel. And then, give that blade a good polish, so it shines like a mirror.''All done? Give it here.'

Belg examines your work for any flaws.

'Not half-bad! The only thing left to do is show your work to Master Grámi. Good luck! He has very high standards.'The Jangovar are harassing Hammerstead, attempting to steal the dwarves' weapons. Defeat as many as you can to give the dwarves time to recover.The Jangovar are attacking Hammerstead!

You should defeat the Jangovar forces.Defeat Jangovar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Lóssi, who can be found defending Hammerstead.Talk to Lóssi'You wish to help? Go out there and cleave some Easterling helms! Bash some sense into their thick heads, so they finally leave us in peace.''Hah! I saw you out there, ${CLASS}! Excellent fighting!'Hórin has sent the surveyors back to Skarháld, while you and he investigate the Orcs with greater stealth.You should talk to Hórin on the path to Glimmerdeep.Talk to HórinUse the whistle to summon Hórin if you become separatedYou should defeat any Orcs you come across on the path to Glimmerdeep.Defeat Orcs on the path (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Use the whistle to summon Hórin if you become separatedYou should search for the entranceway to the Glimmerdeep, north of Skarháld.Find the entrance to the GlimmerdeepDefeat Grísblog before GlimmerdeepUse the whistle to summon Hórin if you become separatedGlimmerdeep lies upon Mount Thrumfall, north of Skarháld.

You should complete a full survey of the Glimmerdeep mines and whatever must be done to that end.Help Hórin with 'The Depth of the Problem'Use the whistle to summon Hórin if you become separatedYou should talk to Hórin.Use the whistle to summon Hórin if you become separatedYou should meet Hórin back at Skarháld.Meet Hórin at Skarháld'Hm... yes, it must be so. ${PLAYER}, the survey expedition cannot continue. The risk is too great. I am sending the surveyors back. 

'But you and I? Well. A few may do what many may not. These Orcs spoke of a deal made, you said? I wonder, what could it be? 

'Stay close. If we become separated, use this whistle. To the Orcs it will sound like the call of a bird.''Come, we climb the path. That is the way to Glimmerdeep, after all! Keep two eyes open for Orcs.''We are thwarted, ${PLAYER}, but not for lack of trying. You have stood at my side like true národ, and for this I thank you sincerely.

'Now let us return to Skarháld, regroup, and try again!''Take great care. Surely there are many hazards in a mine so long abandoned.''I keep an eye on you, ${CLASS}. You have worked hard, but the promise of some of our wealth is yet beyond reaching. Do you sneak away, to seek at fortunes elsewhere, I wonder? Or do you remain at my side?

'Stay or go, my troubles are the same. Something must be done about these strange and wild Orcs.'Hórin believes you have seen the true threat of Glimmerdeep across the bridgeless chasm.Join Hórin in 'Glimmerdeep'.

You will find the instance on the Specific tab of the Instance Finder panel, under the Grey Mountains section.You should talk to Hórin at Skarháld.Talk to Hórin'Do you recall the chasm we found in the mine, ${PLAYER}? The bridge was out and we could not cross, yet on that far side, I thought I saw... no, I could not tell. Some great form. Far greater than an Orc, but up upon two legs, arms at its sides. Did you see it too?

'I know not what it was, and I fear to know the truth, but within my heart know that is what we must face, if we are to claim those mines.''We return to Glimmerdeep. Now is the time to fight. Is your weapon ready, ${PLAYER}?''Two heads! Old tales are told of such trolls who lurk in the shadows of the northern mountains. Never have I dreamt to see one. But then, never had I dreamt the Zhélruka would come back here.

'And yet, it is clear, we alone do not seek to claim this place. These Orcs, these Hobgoblins, this two-headed troll sought the mine for wealth and not shelter. Treasure for some master or cause we know not of. It is troubling, ${PLAYER}! We must guard ourselves well. We must watch our backs.'While Radagast tends to the poisoned animals, some nearby Beornings are looking into the cause, which is a mystery considering all the Mirkwood spiders seem to have fled the area.You should collect honey to present to the Beornings as a gift.Collect honey from beehives (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should travel due south of Radagast, in Thornholt, to Skoghús, the Beorning camp.Seek Threkmóth in SkoghúsThrekmóth can be found in Skoghús, deep in the Thornholt woods.

You should introduce yourself to Threkmóth, the leader of the Beorning settlement, and deliver your gift to him.You should go speak with Vaskorn, who can be found in Threkmóth, deep in the Thornholt woods.Talk to Vaskorn'Now that you are here, ${PLAYER}, I have little need for odd and flighty dwarves! Many goodly beasts in this most northern edge of Lasgalen have been sickened with poison. I would have sworn it was spiders, yet of course it could not be, for they have all gone missing.

'I have spoken with Beornings who dwell nearby to the west, and they, it seems, have a very similar tale to tell. Will you go to them and learn what you may, while I tend to my sickened friends? Bear with you a gift of honey. It will sweeten your dealings, I think you will find.

'Once you have gathered some honey from over there, you will want to seek out Threkmóth, the Beorning elder, at Skoghús, due south of here.''Is that honey I smell? You must be a friend of our friend Radagast. He said he would send help.

'Now hand that honey over, and go talk to Vaskorn while I prepare the honey cakes! He has been leading the efforts to track down the stingers.''Ah yes, as usual Threkmóth is more concerned with honey cakes than graver matters. Well met, ${RACE}, we have much to do.'After discovering one of Arcah's ravens near to Óinsbridge, Dori and Company must now deal with unexpected threats if they are to make it to Thikil-gundu.Gundabad black-arrows can be found along the Men Angrún, especially as it nears the Rushgate.

The raven told Dori that cruel archers of Gundabad have been taking ravens' feathers for their arrows. You should defeat them and take their arrows.Find and defeat Gundabad black-arrows (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})One of Arcah's ravens can be found at the Rushgate.

After speaking with the raven, Dori has learned that the other two ravens sent from Ravenhill have not returned. You should search for one of the ravens near the Rushgate.Search for the lost raven near the RushgateOne of Arcah's ravens can be found at the Rushgate.

You have found the raven at the Rushgate. You should now follow the road to Thikil-gundu to the east and rejoin Dori and Company.Direct the raven to fly to Dori and Company along the road to Thikil-gunduDori can be found somewhere along the road to Thikil-gundu.

The raven has flown from the Rushgate. You should now find Dori and Company along the road to Thikil-gundu.Follow the road to Thikil-gundu and and find Dori and CompanyDori can be found on a hilltop overlooking Stánbalk, the Stone Wall of Thikil-gundu.

You have found Dori and Company along the road to Thikil-gundu. You should now speak to Dori.The lost raven can be found somewhere near Stánbalk, the Stone Wall.

Dori has asked you to seek the raven that flew near to Thikil-gundu.Find the lost raven near Thikil-gunduThe slain, frozen raven can be found on the ground before Stánbalk, the Stone Wall.

You have found the frozen remains of a raven before Stánbalk, the Stone Wall. You should examine it.Examine the frozen raven, but remain on guardDori can be found on a hilltop overlooking Stánbalk, the Stone Wall of Thikil-gundu.

You have learned that one of Arcah's ravens was slain by a cold-drake. You should return to Dori.Talk to Dori on the road to Thikil-gundu'Just a moment, ${PLAYER}!'

Dori asks the raven if it has seen any sign of Nori or his expedition. The raven croaks and chitters in answer. All of a sudden, the raven spreads its wings and looses a shrill cry.

'I understand, friend.'

Dori turns his gaze to you.

'These are not glad tidings, ${PLAYER}. Nori is nowhere to be found, and two of the three ravens sent to search for my brother have become lost in the Grey Mountains. One flew for the Rushgate far to the north-west, and the other... the other may have gone to Thikil-gundu. And that is not the half of it! The ravens have spied black-arrows of Gundabad lying in wait beyond the forest, and they have fletched their arrows with the feathers of ravens! It is also said they are practised in the art of stealth, but I have not known many Orcs to possess such patience. If we are to reach Thikil-gundu, we shall have to remain on guard.

'Ride to the Rushgate along the Men Angrún and search for the lost raven. If you encounter any of those black-arrows, defeat them and reclaim the feathers they have stolen. Such an insult cannot go unanswered!

'When you have reached the Rushgate, turn back and ride to meet us along the road to Thikil-gundu. Our journey draws near to its end, ${PLAYER}. Let us not falter at so late an hour!'The raven perches silently....'Ah, ${PLAYER}!

'Did you encounter the Orcs of Gundabad as well? Several harried us in our travels, but they were soon defeated. Give me the feathers you have recovered, and when at last we return to Erebor, I shall deliver them to Arcah myself!

'Two of the ravens are now found, but one remains lost. Though it is now broken and ruined, the great Stone Wall before us, Stánbalk, long stood in defence of Thikil-gundu. If Arcah forbade her people from venturing too near to the Steel Keep, they would have flown no further than Stánbalk.

'While the others and I take rest, I would ask that you search for the lost raven among the ruins of Stánbalk. 

'Be careful, ${PLAYER}. I fear Vethúg was not the last of his kind in the Grey Mountains!''Are you all right, ${PLAYER}? Even a cold-drake as young as that one poses a great threat!

'Did you find the lost raven?'

You tell Dori that the lost raven was seemingly slain by a cold-drake.

'When the raven did not fly to answer the call of its kin, I feared it so. Though I did not wish to believe it, I knew in my heart what fate must have befallen it.'

Dori casts a sorrowful glance to the ravens, and shakes his head.

'So this is the way of the Grey Wrack....'A band of Zhélruka desires to travel to the Grey Mountains, but its presence near the Lonely Mountain has threatened to spark an incident between the Zhélruka and the Longbeards.King Thorin is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Return to King Thorin with Gandalf's warning about the ZhélrukaKing Thorin is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the MountainDurin has followed the Zhélruka into the Grey Mountains, and at King Thorin's request you should look for him on the road that climbs into Ered Mithrin.Find Durin on the road that climbs into the Grey MountainsYou have found Durin on the road that climbs into the Grey Mountains.Talk to Durin on the road that climbs into the Grey Mountains'Ah, I see. The relationship of the Zhélruka and the Longbeards has been tumultuous during the years of their interaction, changing from peace to outright war and then to uneasy truce. The passage of the years and the waning of memory finally caused friendship to once again emerge between them... and now this happens to cast that renewal into doubt.

'With the devastation that heralded the end of the First Age, much of Middle-earth was shaken and sundered. In their land far to the east, the stronghold of the Zhélruka was destroyed and they became a wandering people. Their neighbours turned them aside and wished them ill, but King Durin II of Khazad-dûm took pity upon them and bade his folk make a home for the displaced people at Mount Gundabad. They disappeared in the mountains and never arrived, but a single survivor emerged centuries later. He spoke of the vanished Zhélruka and Thafar-gathol, a citadel he claimed they delved in the mountains. He insisted too that its riches could match those of mighty Khazad-dûm. Mad ravings, surely. None of the many to seek it over the years, Longbeard and Zhélruka alike, have ever found it. I do not believe it ever existed.

'Dwarves from Khazad-dûm did uncover the remains of an ancient delving in the Grey Mountains, and although they found a great deal of iron ore they found no mithril; this could not be Thafar-gathol. They called it Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep, and soon their iron production threatened to rival that of the Iron Hills. Scholars whisper that this threat to their prosperity caused the Dwarves of Járnfast to send word of the Steel Keep to the Zhélruka, who demanded it was theirs by right. If the Dwarves of the Iron Hills did take part in this none have proven it, but it seems likely to me. Ever since, the Zhélruka have jealously guarded their claim to holdings in the Grey Mountains, and that resentment has coloured their dealings with all other people. If Bótuz Frostblood and others of the Zhélruka believe their property is being kept from them they will not hesitate to spill blood to regain it.

'I will not involve myself in this matter. Return to King Thorin and warn him not to trifle with the Zhélruka. They will become a dangerous foe if they feel they are being treated unfairly, and the Dwarves of the Lonely Mountain need more friends, not new enemies.''With the devastation that heralded the end of the First Age, much of Middle-earth was shaken and sundered. In their land far to the east, the stronghold of the Zhélruka was destroyed and they became a wandering people. Their neighbours turned them aside and wished them ill, but King Durin II of Khazad-dûm took pity upon them and bade his folk make a home for the displaced people at Mount Gundabad. They disappeared in the mountains and never arrived, but a single survivor emerged centuries later. He spoke of the vanished Zhélruka and Thafar-gathol, a citadel he claimed they delved in the mountains. He insisted too that its riches could match those of mighty Khazad-dûm. Mad ravings, surely. None of the many to seek it over the years, Longbeard and Zhélruka alike, have ever found it. I do not believe it ever existed.

'Dwarves from Khazad-dûm did uncover the remains of an ancient delving in the Grey Mountains, and although they found a great deal of iron ore they found no mithril; this could not be Thafar-gathol. They called it Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep, and soon their iron production threatened to rival that of the Iron Hills. Scholars whisper that this threat to their prosperity caused the Dwarves of Járnfast to send word of the Steel Keep to the Zhélruka, who demanded it was theirs by right. If the Dwarves of the Iron Hills did take part in this none have proven it, but it seems likely to me. Ever since, the Zhélruka have jealously guarded their claim to holdings in the Grey Mountains, and that resentment has coloured their dealings with all other people. If Bótuz Frostblood and others of the Zhélruka believe their property is being kept from them they will not hesitate to spill blood to regain it.

'I will not involve myself in this matter. Return to King Thorin and warn him not to trifle with the Zhélruka. They will become a dangerous foe if they feel they are being treated unfairly, and the Dwarves of the Lonely Mountain need more friends, not new enemies.''This is bad, ${PLAYER}! This is very bad!''This is bad, ${PLAYER}! This is very bad!''Authi Gem-cutter has come from the edge of the Dale-lands with urgent news, ${PLAYER}! He says that Bótuz Frostblood and his band of Zhélruka forced their way into the Dale-lands against my wishes and have marched for the Grey Mountains! At first my son Durin barred their way, but he refused to spill dwarf-blood and allowed them to pass! It seems he followed them, intending to watch them and prevent the Zhélruka from doing any harm to the people of Dale or the lands around the Lonely Mountain. He sent Authi back to bring me word of their movements.

'This is an outrage, ${PLAYER}! These Zhélruka have proven themselves to be impolite and no friend to me or to my people. I am insulted, and demand satisfaction! I do not say my son should have stood alone against dozens of these traitors, but I wish the Zhélruka had been made to pay for this slight in some fashion! Had they waited for my warriors to arrive, they would not have dared to cross, or they would have paid a good sum in blood and in pain! They are brazen enough to disregard my commands, but too cowardly to engage in outright battle for it!

'Go to the Grey Mountains and tell my son to return. These Zhélruka will pay for this outrage, but it will not be for Durin to exact the price. He belongs here with me, and will learn the responsibilities of kingship at my feet.''My father must have sent you to find me.''These Zhélruka have proven themselves to be impolite and no friend to me or to my people. I am insulted, and demand satisfaction! I do not say my son should have stood alone against dozens of these traitors, but I wish the Zhélruka had been made to pay for this slight in some fashion! Had they waited for my warriors to arrive, they would not have dared to cross, or they would have paid a good sum in blood and in pain! They are brazen enough to disregard my commands, but too cowardly to engage in outright battle for it!

'Go to the Grey Mountains and tell my son to return. These Zhélruka will pay for this outrage, but it will not be for Durin to exact the price. He belongs here with me, and will learn the responsibilities of kingship at my feet.''This is bad, ${PLAYER}! Durin followed those traitors all by himself, and he might be in danger!''Did my father send you to bring me back to the Lonely Mountain, ${PLAYER}? Bótuz Frostblood and his band of Zhélruka have gone on ahead, but now that I behold this place with my own eyes I see some of what has driven them to come here, against my father's command.'

Durin looks around at the rocky highlands and the towering peaks of the Grey Mountains.

'Do you see it too? The vast mountains, the untouched valleys, the hidden recesses beneath them... each holds the promise of adventure! My father's throne-room is no place for me. I should not waste my years there in study and in idleness, as he did. No, I belong here in the unexplored wilderness! I should seek its lost treasures, just as my people have done for thousands of years! Only then will I find the glory that is to be mine! Uncle Náin would understand, even if my father does not.

'I am not going back until I have proven I am worthy of my name, ${PLAYER}. Remember our deeds in the Ironfold? You were a good companion and a reliable friend to me. I ask you to adventure with me here, and together we will achieve great things!'Now that Durin has seen the Grey Mountains for himself, he has decided he wants to adventure among them for glory and for treasure, rather than remaining at the Lonely Mountain with his father.Durin asked you to defeat enemies in the Wyrmgap on your way to the Anvil on the Withered Heath.Defeat enemies in the Wyrmgap (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Travel with Durin to the Anvil on the Withered HeathDurin stands by the Anvil on the Withered Heath.Talk to Durin by the Anvil on the Withered HeathDurin has asked you to defeat some of the worms and drakes that stalk the Withered Heath and Ered Mithrin, and collect their teeth and hides.Collect drake-teeth on the Withered Heath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect worm-hides throughout Ered Mithrin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Durin stands by the Anvil on the Withered Heath.Talk to Durin by the Anvil on the Withered HeathDurin asked you to defeat enemies in the Vales of Mithrin.Collect hatchling-wings in Ered Mithrin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect burning scales in Ered Mithrin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Travel with Durin to the canyons south of StormwallDurin is in the canyons south of Stormwall.Talk to Durin in the canyons south of StormwallThe old fortress of Skarháld is south and west of the path to Stormwall.Travel to the old fortress of Skarháld, where the Zhélruka planned to encampThe old fortress of Skarháld is south and west of the path to Stormwall. Durin is at the eastern entrance of the city, waiting to speak with you.'There will be adventures enough for both of us, ${PLAYER}, I am certain of that! I will not look back fondly upon the time I spent in Járnfast, kept from honour and glory, but something at least came from the captivity of my youth. Many were the tales I heard, and many were the maps I studied, and now that we are here among the Grey Mountains I will see these subjects with my own eyes, and thereby make them real!

'There should be a narrow passage to the north-west of here, the Wyrmgap, bored through the very stone by the dragon-kind that came down from the north. Let us see if any of their kin still inhabit the gap, and bring battle to them if they do! I would like to pass through the Wyrmgap to the Withered Heath beyond and see the great Anvil for myself. Many tales tell of the Anvil, and dreams of it have fired my imagination since I was small!''What a sight! The Anvil is even larger and more magnificent than I imagined it to be!''There should be a narrow passage to the north-west of here, the Wyrmgap, bored through the very stone by the dragon-kind that came down from the north. Let us see if any of their kin still inhabit the gap, and bring battle to them if they do! I would like to pass through the Wyrmgap to the Withered Heath beyond and see the great Anvil for myself. Many tales tell of the Anvil, and dreams of it have fired my imagination since I was small!''Behold the Anvil of Winterstith, ${PLAYER}! How many dwarves have looked upon its smooth surface and questioned the reflections that gazed back, asking of themselves what their futures were to be? Did they believe the answers in their hearts? Longbeards have come here in search of treasure or knowledge, and it is said that more than one Zhélruka king perished on the Heath while seeking the Citadel. Dóbruz? Ilkún? I cannot remember their names. My teachers would be shamed and disappointed!

'But whatever their names may have been, I do remember their fates. How could a young dwarf forget their stories? For one of these kings sought the remains of his lost forebear and the way to Thafar-gathol, and while digging beneath the Anvil his dwarves accidentally caused a great collapse. The glacier was split in twain, and hundreds of cold-drakes awoke within and boiled from the crack in the ice. The few Zhélruka that survived the initial attack fled back to the Steel Keep, but the rest of the dwarves were slain. My teachers say it was dwarf-made fire that eventually melted the ice, sealing most of the cold-drakes within again, but I heard from somewhere that it was one or another of the Wizards that closed the crack and mended the Anvil. I wonder which is the truth?

'Now that I look upon the Anvil for myself, I find its beauty impossible to describe, but that beauty is matched by a terrible feeling of watchfulness. Let us not forget the horrors that slept behind its shimmering surface, ${PLAYER}, and take care not to disturb them! Instead let us put the Anvil to our backs and collect a number of trophies from the drakes and worms that stalk among the steaming geysers of the Withered Heath and elsewhere throughout Ered Mithrin. I have a plan that requires them, but let us also collect them in memory of the dwarves that perished in sight of the Anvil.''Now that I look upon the Anvil for myself, I find its beauty impossible to describe, but that beauty is matched by a terrible feeling of watchfulness. Let us not forget the horrors that slept behind its shimmering surface, ${PLAYER}, and take care not to disturb them! Instead let us put the Anvil to our backs and collect a number of trophies from the drakes and worms that stalk among the steaming geysers of the Withered Heath and throughout Ered Mithrin. I have a plan that requires them, but let us also collect them in memory of the dwarves that perished in sight of the Anvil.''Ah, those teeth and hides will serve my purposes quite well. Thank you, my friend!

'We should depart now, for I find the more I gaze upon the smooth ice of the Anvil the more reluctant I am to leave it behind, as if it carries upon its surface some strange power of bewitchment! Do you feel it too?

'Let us go, ${PLAYER}. If we travel south back through the Wyrmgap and a little ways to the west, we should find another canyon that leads north to Stormwall. I wish to survey the path to Stormwall before we seek the Zhélruka, and while we explore the Vales of Mithrin we should collect some further trophies from the creatures there: drake-hatchling wings and burning scales, if you please.''We should exercise caution, for I sense the presence of dragon-kind very near to us. They may have roosted within Stormwall and increased in number. I am still waiting for hatchling-wings and burning scales, but these canyons seem to have more than enough of each for our purposes.''If we travel south back through the Wyrmgap and a little ways to the west, we should find another canyon that leads north to Stormwall. I wish to survey the path to Stormwall before we seek the Zhélruka, and while we explore the Vales of Mithrin we should collect some further trophies from the creatures there: drake-hatchling wings and burning scales, if you please.''The fortress of Stormwall has been abandoned for an age, but at its height it was known for its riches. The dwarves who dwelt here made fine work in gold and silver, but it was that very reputation that brought about their downfall; rumour of their treasure drew the attention of dragons, and the dwarves were driven out.

'This would be the straight way to Stormwall, but I see that the stairs have collapsed. To reach the old fortress we would need to brave the canyons, and if I remember my maps aright the cliffs and gullies on the approach to the stronghold are certain to be quite confusing. Perhaps we will return here when we are better supplied. Let us leave this canyon and find the Zhélruka; they intended to establish a camp in the old stronghold of Skarháld, south of here and to the west.''This would be the straight way to Stormwall, but I see that the stairs have collapsed. To reach the old fortress we would need to brave the canyons, and if I remember my maps aright the cliffs and gullies on the approach to the stronghold are certain to be quite confusing. Perhaps we will return here when we are better supplied. Let us leave this canyon and find the Zhélruka; they intended to establish a camp in the old stronghold of Skarháld, south of here and to the west.''We have found them.''We found the Zhélruka, ${PLAYER}, and it also seems some Longbeards from the Lonely Mountain have made their way to Skarháld! I wonder if they attained permission from my father to come here, or if they too dismissed his command? A small part of me regrets that it was necessary for me to do so, but I believe it truly was. He will understand when I return in glory.

'I will take the various trophies we obtained from the dragon-kind in the vales and present them to the Zhélruka as proof of our intentions here. They will see that their goals are ours, and we should work together if we are all to thrive in this land.

'It is a good plan, is it not? It is.'You and Durin have come to the old fortress of Skarháld, where Longbeards and Zhélruka both attempt to survive in the dangerous Grey Mountains.Bótuz Frostblood is near the top of the fortress of Skarháld.Talk to Bótuz Frostblood at SkarháldBótuz Frostblood told you to look for his chieftains Inek, Kópat, and Dóbrokh on the upper levels of Skarháld and in the keep.Find Dóbrokh at the view from the high eastern archFind Inek the Brash at the siege defencesFind Kópat inside Tharrazhâr, the keep of SkarháldTalk to DóbrokhTalk to Inek the BrashTalk to KópatDurin is inside Tharrazhâr, the keep of Skarháld.Talk to Durin inside Tharrazhâr, the keep of SkarháldYou heard raised voices outside the keep. What is happening?Leave the keep of Skarháld and learn what transpired outsideA crowd of dwarves surround a wounded Zhélruka warrior on the upper level of Skarháld.Talk to Vútro the West-wind and learn how he was injured'Like Stormwall, this fortress was abandoned for centuries. Once it climbed proudly up the mountain's side, but time and the repeated onslaughts of dragon-kind have worn away some of its majesty. Much like my people, however, it perseveres, no matter the hardship it endures!

'Bótuz Frostblood will be near the top of the stronghold, ${PLAYER}. Present yourself to him and say that we are at his service here in Skarháld. Longbeard and Zhélruka alike have much to gain as friends and allies, and the mountains can only be conquered if we work together, despite the differences we may have had in the past.''Bótuz Frostblood will be near the top of the stronghold, ${PLAYER}. Present yourself to him and say that we are at his service here in Skarháld. Longbeard and Zhélruka alike have much to gain as friends and allies, and the mountains can only be conquered if we work together, despite the differences we may have had in the past.''Oh, it's you. I heard that the son of Thorin had come to Skarháld bearing drake-teeth and burning scales, hatchling-wings and worm-hides, as if that proves anything to me. Did he slay these creatures or did you, ${PLAYER}? He is young and brash, and I know his sort: a king's son, dreaming of glory but unable to attain it. He is soft! The lands of my people are harsh and cruel, and he would not long have lasted in the hard lands.'

Bótuz snorts, delivering his taunts with relish.

'Durin! I see the arrogance of the Longbeards in that name. Thorin is a fool to aim so high!

'But you are different, ${PLAYER}, and I welcome you to Skarháld without reservation. Make yourself at home here, and meet with my chieftains. Look for Inek, Kópat, and Dóbrokh on the upper levels of Skarháld and in the keep.''I welcome you to Skarháld, ${PLAYER}. Make yourself at home here, and meet with my chieftains. Look for Inek, Kópat, and Dóbrokh on the upper levels of Skarháld and in the keep.''Watch your footing, ${RACE}. It is a long way to the valley floor below, and if you are not careful you might find yourself taking the fast way down. Most are not as surefooted as Dóbrokh!

'Far away to the east are the lands from which my people came, past the mountains and to others beyond. Many were the hardships we endured there, and the sum of them made the Zhélruka slow to smile and wary of strangers, but it also made us hardy and tough, and protective of the things we hold dear.

'I say we came from the East, and that is true, but that is not the home for which we have spent so many lifetimes searching. How many kings of the Zhélruka have sought for such a home in these very mountains? How many more will follow us in that purpose? Dóbrokh cannot say.''Are you here for sight-seeing or for helping us, ${RACE}? Yes, I remember you and your companion speaking with Bótuz before we crossed to the Grey Mountains. What can you tell me of this Durin? He tried to stop us from entering the Dale-lands, but when Bótuz refused to heed his warning he did nothing. That is a sign of weakness. His father must be very disappointed in him.

'I am proud to count myself among the Zhélruka, for we reinforce our position with actions... not with empty words! Let other dwarves waggle their beards in discussion, and while they speak the Zhélruka will take what they want!

'I will tell you no false-hoods: sometimes this creed causes others to resent the Zhélruka. Bótuz knows this, and he commanded me to inspect the defences of this fortress. Sadly, I must report that this equipment is not satisfactory, and will require some work if it is to be made war-worthy.''It has been many hundreds of years since any dwarves lived in this place, but the signs of each previous habitation are there if you know where to look. To the Longbeards this was Tharrazhâr, and they stocked the keep with the metals they prized and the equipment they needed to defend it. When my people first dwelt here they gave it the name Brúgrad, but they had their eye set upon larger and more wealthy cities, and did not intend to remain long.

'It was a useful foothold, but it proved a tenuous one. Many Zhélruka set out from Brúgrad in search of fabled Thafar-gathol, but those who returned did so only with disappointment. And at last there came a day when the returners came back to a fallen fortress. None can say how long the dwarves fought against the dragons, but in the end none called it Brúgrad any longer. Now the ruins are named only in the Westron Speech, and the heart of every true Zhélruka scholar mourns, knowing what was lost when the name became Skarháld.''I delivered the trophies we collected to Bótuz Frostblood, and he seemed quite impressed by our valour, ${PLAYER}. He has charged me with cataloguing the supplies here in the keep, and I have agreed. This work is not well-suited to one of my lineage, but I understand the need for it and want to encourage cooperation between the Longbeards and the Zhélruka here in Skarháld. This work will bear fruit in the long run, I am sure.

'Wait. Did you hear that? There is a commotion of some sort outside. We should go see what is happening!''I heard raised voices! Let us see what has happened outside! The cataloguing of the supplies can wait!''I thought I was not observed... but he must have seen me. The blades flew faster than I... could have expected....'

The scout clutches his side, where a sharp weapon has cut through his armour. One of the nearby dwarves asks who it was that did this to him, and he grimaces in pain.'I heard raised voices! Let us see what has happened outside! The cataloguing of the supplies can wait!'The Ungoledain have taken the old ruins at Kêr Bréin as their own.You should defeat Ungoledain in the old ruins at Kêr Bréin.Defeat Ungoledain (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many Ungoledain in the old ruins at Kêr Bréin.CompletedThe Ungoledain have taken the old ruins at Kêr Bréin as their own and need to be defeated.Many of the Ungoledain at Kêr Bréin have been defeated.Crude traps have been hidden around Óinsbridge. These could prove dangerous for any dwarves travelling in Ered Mithrin.Crude traps have been hidden around Óinsbridge.Disarm Ungoladan-trapsCrude traps have been hidden around Óinsbridge by the Ungoledain. They should be disarmed in order to allow safe passage for travelling dwarves.Disarm Ungoladan-traps (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have disarmed many traps at Óinsbridge.CompletedCrude traps have been hidden around Óinsbridge. These could prove dangerous for any dwarves travelling in Ered Mithrin. You should disarm any that you come across.You have disarmed many traps at Óinsbridge. The Dwarves of Ered Mithrin will surely be thankful for your efforts.It is well known that the Beorning have a strong craving for honey. Gifting more of it is a sure way to get in their good graces.You should collect honey to bring to the Beornings. It can be found in beehives along the road to Óinsbridge.Gather honey (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should collect honey to bring to the Beornings.It is well known that the Beorning have a strong craving for honey. Gifting more of it is a sure way to get in their good graces.'This honey looks delicious and will certainly come in handy! Thank you, ${PLAYER}'After learning that Vethúg Wintermind fell upon the foothills of Winterstith, Dori and Company have decided to seek the fallen cold-drake and discover some final proof of his demise.Náth can be found at the Bitter Scar.

You have travelled to the end of the Bitter Scar with Dori and Company, you now prepare to search for the remains of Vethúg Wintermind. You should speak to Náth.Talk to NáthDori, Bofur, and Glóin have ventured into the Bitter Scar.

You should follow Dori, Bofur, and Glóin into the Bitter Scar.Find Dori, Glóin, and Bofur in the Bitter ScarDori can be found in the Bitter Scar.

You have travelled to the end of the Bitter Scar and rejoined Dori and Company, but there is no sign of the remains of Vethúg Wintermind.Talk to Dori in the Bitter ScarSigns of battle can be found at the end of the Bitter Scar.

You have come upon the aftermath of a struggle between Vethúg Wintermind and an unknown foe. You should examine the signs of battle to determine what has happened.Dori can be found in the Bitter Scar.

You have discovered that a great struggle occurred between Karazgar and Vethúg Wintermind, and it seems Karazgar was the victor. You should speak to Dori at once.Dori can be found in the Bitter Scar.

You have discovered concerning evidence that Karazgar may have slain or taken Vethúg Wintermind from the Bitter Scar. You should leave at once with Dori and Company.Leave the Bitter Scar with Dori and CompanyIngór, son of Vékun, has come to the Bitter Scar with a company of his own and appears eager to speak to Dori and Company.

You should remain on guard and defend Dori and Company if the need arises.Witness the meeting of the companies of Dori and IngórDori can be found in the Bitter Scar.

Ingór has revealed himself to be a Prince of the Zhélruka, and he has offered Dori and Company safe passage to Skarháld. You should speak to Dori and see if he wishes to accept.Talk to Dori'Let us follow these Zhélruka to Skarháld, ${PLAYER}, but speak not of our true purpose.''${PLAYER}!

'Dori, Glóin, and Bofur have travelled deeper into the rift on foot. It grows narrower and more treacherous ahead, and so, I offered to tend to the goats.

'Of course, that was not the only reason Dréri and I have remained behind! We Stout-axes are hardy dwarves, but we are not accustomed to such cold weather. Ah, but I think these Longbeards would not feel so at home on the plains of Dor Amarth!

'In truth, I feel a sense of dread in the shadows of the Grey Mountains. It is nothing more than a feeling, I think, but there is something about this place that has left me uneasy.

'It has been some time. You should go ahead and see what is keeping the others.''Rust... and rime. It is as I feared, ${PLAYER}.

'Was Vethúg slain by Karazgar's hand or did the Weeping Warrior find some crueler purpose for the great cold-drake?'

Dori glances at the damaged remains of the bloodied, black arrow.

'If Vethúg Wintermind lives still, he will not have forgotten the hurts done to him by the Longbeards and Bardings....

'We must go.''Listen to me, ${PLAYER}.'

Dori's voice drops to a whisper.

'Do not think I chose my words to make a fool of myself. This Prince seems wiser than his kinsfolk, but I do not trust these Zhélruka.

'If they make for Dómekh then they make for Thikil-gundu, and the Zhélruka do not share the spoils of delving and war. If Ingór was to learn that our path also leads to the Steel Keep, I do not think we would find him so friendly much longer.

'I believe the only way this can be settled without the spilling of dwarf-blood is to accept Ingór's offer of passage to Skarháld.''I do not like the look of this....

'Either we have been deceived by the Zhélruka or Vethúg was not as wounded as he appeared to be! There is no mistaking that Vethúg tore this rift in the foothills of Winterstith, but it is where he has gone that concerns me!

'Do you see these marks in the earth? I first thought nothing of them, but there are signs of battle here among the fallen stones of Winterstith! Unless the great cold-drake battled only against rocks and ice as they fell, some other foe must have come to this place. I know few who could withstand the might of Vethúg Wintermind, and so, I fear this is the work of Karazgar!

'Perhaps you can uncover the truth of what has happened here, ${PLAYER}.'Pages of a tome can be found scattered around Ered Mithrin. Perhaps you can complete the story if you find them all.Frozen pages can be found scattered around Ered Mithrin.

You should collect the pages to complete the story.Collect page 1Collect page 2Collect page 3Collect page 4Collect page 5Collect page 6Collect page 7Collect page 8Completed.CompletedYou've found a torn page of a tome, frozen from the harsh weather of the mountains. Perhaps you can find other pages around Ered Mithrin and complete the story.The story still seems incomplete.  This appears to be the first half of a poem written by the Zhélruka Dwarves. Perhaps the second half still exists somewhere, in some form.Pages of a tome can be found scattered around Stormwall and Glimmerdeep. Perhaps you can complete the story if you find them all.Claw-mark writing can be found scattered around Glimmerdeep and Stormwall.

You should record the writing to complete the story.Record writing 1Record writing 2Record writing 3Record writing 4Record writing 5Record writing 6Record writing 7Record writing 8Record writing 9Record writing 10Record writing 11Completed.CompletedYou've found writing scratched into the stone, left by the claws of a large creature. Perhaps you can find more in Stormwall and Glimmerdeep to complete the story.The story is complete. The Dwarves of Erebor certainly have a long history of battling dragons. You have a feeling that, despite Thorin III's wishes, the remaining dragons will continue to be a danger to his people in the future.Hórin, a Zhélruka Dwarf, says his people do not have clothing warm enough to go up into the mountains.Deer can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should collect hides from harts and hinds around Ered Mithrin.Collect deer hides (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hórin can be found in Skarháld.

You should return to Hórin with the hides.'You, kízinek, stranger. You wish to be useful? We are cold. Too cold for the mountain heights. No such cold where we have come from, and besides, travel has worn our clothing bare.

'Nor are we swift at hunting like you, ${CLASS}. Will you gather hides from deer to keep us warm?''Well now, you have done well on your hunt. I will give you that. Hides for the národ. Hides to keep my people warm. That is good!'Wolverines have taken refuge at Óinsbridge.Wolverines have taken refuge at Óinsbridge.Defeat Mithrin wolverinesWolverines have taken refuge at Óinsbridge.Defeat Mithrin wolverines (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many wolverines at Óinsbridge.CompletedWolverines have taken refuge at Óinsbridge and should be evicted from the old dwarf ruins.You have defeated many wolverines at Óinsbridge. The Dwarves of Ered Mithrin will surely be thankful for your efforts.Hórin and Rúni have realized that the Stave of Veluk-zámok may have been among the hoard of Etterfang, in Glimmerdeep.Rúni can be found at Skarháld.

You should talk to Rúni and hear his thoughts on how to track the dragon Etterfang.Talk to RúniYou should search Rúni's belongings in Skarháld for any scroll mentioning Etterfang.Search Rúni's belongingsYou should continue searching Rúni's belongings in Skarháld for any scroll mentioning Etterfang.Continue searching Rúni's belongingsYou should bring the scroll to Rúni in Skarháld.'${PLAYER}, I have been speaking to Hórin about the Stave of Veluk-zámok. We are both in agreement that the tales of this artifact and of Borin's battle with Etterfang are closely intertwined. We feel certain that to find the Stave, we must find the beast. And yet - we cannot simply follow the path she took deep into the mountain!''I have been speaking to the Longbeard Rúni about his dragon, Etterfang. We are both in agreement that the Stave and the tales of this dragon are closely intertwined. We feel certain that to find the Stave, we must find the beast. Will you ask Rúni if he has an idea of how to follow the trail of the dragon?''No, ${PLAYER}, I am at a loss. Nothing we have before us points the way. Aha! Yet there is a source of knowledge that is not before us. For I took with me many scrolls on the old keeps, mountains, and yes, dragons from our lore.

'Will you you go into my belongings and search for any scroll bearing the word Etterfang upon it?''What have you found? Let me see...

'Yes, yes. Etterfang Foulmaw, a fierce worm of smaller proportion, last seen in the Glimmerdeep... aha! Now here we have something. She was a beast of both venom and flame... and sister of Smaug! Known to burrow deep within the stone, feasting on what may be found there, including spiders. Some hold it is from these she gains her venom....

'A smaller dragon, given to crawling through deep stone? This strikes me as the beginning of an answer, ${PLAYER}. Yes... let me ponder a moment.'Hórin, a Zhélruka Dwarf, claims rivers and streams can be panned to learn what is hidden under stone.The Greylin, Forest, and Heath Rivers can all be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should pan for precious minerals at all three rivers.You should return to Hórin in Skarháld with the minerals you have panned.Return to SkarháldYou should sift through the contents of your panning bag in a rinsing cask, found in Skarháld.Sift through contents of your panning bag in a rinsing caskYou should bring the mithril flake to Hórin in Skarháld.'Did you know, kízinek, that river and stream hold secrets to what lies hidden under stone?

'Do you not believe me? Take these panning things and see for yourself! You will not find the fabled realm of Thafar-gathol, but then, if you are not an utter drak, you may find something of value or use.

'Three rivers you will find here, or so the old tales tell: Greylin, Forest, and Heath. Go then, and see what you can find.''What? You did not find this panning. You could not. And yet, where else could you have? Are you a drak - or are you vúchut? No, you are no fool. Tell me then, from which river did this come?'

You confess that you only found it rinsing your panning bag in Skarháld.

'Ah, a drak, a fool then! But a luck-touched fool. You have brought us a sign of fabled Thafar-gathol. A flake, no more. It is all we are accustomed to going on, in truth.'Vaskorn advises seeking the council of Radagast in the fight against the poisoners of the woods.You should return to Radagast, located at the highest point of Thornholt.Talk to RadagastAll the items can be found in and around the Thornholt woods.

You should collect the ingredients Radagast requires for the potion of protection against the Ungoladan-poison: poison from discarded Ungoladan weapons, fresh water, livers of animals sickened from the poison, and the mushrooms common to Thornholt.Collect Ungoladan-poison (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect fresh water (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect animal livers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should return to Radagast in Thornholt with all the ingredients you have collected.Bring the ingredients to RadagastYou should place all the ingredients in the nearby mortar and pestle for Radagast, in Thornholt woods.Radagast can be found in the Thornholt woods.

You should return to Radagast with the mixed concoction.You should return to Vaskorn in Skoghús, in the Thornholt woods, and tell him of Radagast's potion.Talk to Vaskorn in SkoghúsYou should go to Threkmóth in Skoghús, in the Thornholt woods, and find out where Vaskorn is.Talk to Threkmóth'For all the tireless scouring of Thornholt I have done, and you stumbled across the trail quick as could be! But that is no matter, as long as the vile poisoners are stopped. I am a simple man. I seek not glory but peace.

'You had better return to the brown wizard and seek his council on this whole affair. I will stay put and speak with Threkmóth on what actions we shall take.''Very good. This should suffice, ${PLAYER}! I need to do one more test to be certain that it is safe for your kind. Could you combine all those ingredients in the mortar and pestle for me?''I know full well that Vaskorn is not at his post. It is I, my very self, who told him to take care of these spider-folk and drive them away while I finish baking the honey cakes. I told him he should have two honey cakes for himself, when he returned!''These Ungoledain are indeed a threat. Many of my friends have perished to their poison. While you were away I began experimenting with a potion to protect against it. I think I have perfected the concoction.

'If you collect the ingredients, I will be able to make plenty to protect you, the Beornings, and most importantly, my friends.

'Here is what you must find: some poison of the Ungoledain, which is likely obtained most easily from their discarded weapons; clean water from a good source; the livers of animals sickened from the poison; and some of the common mushrooms that may be found all around Thornholt.''Well, I never said it would smell nice. The odour is how you know it will work!

'I will test this batch, and if all goes well, I will be able to make enough for you and the Beornings to protect against the Ungoladan weapons.

'Return to the Beornings now and make our work here known to them. Before you proceed with the assault on the Ungoledain, return to me and I will provide you with an antidote sufficient for the task.'The Zhélruka are preparing a survey of the ancient Glimmerdeep mine.You should collect surveying equipment and expedition supplies in and around Skarháld.Collect surveying equipment (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect expedition supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Empty packs and Zhélruka surveyors can both be found in Skarháld.

You should deliver the surveying equipment to Zhélruka surveyors and pack the expedition supplies in empty packs.Now that you have made the preparations he requested, you should return to Hórin in Skarháld.Return to Hórin'Why have you come here, outsider? You are no Zhélruka. Surely you have some home. If you mean us harm, though you be mighty, the národ will turn their axes on you as one. You will be outnumbered. You will regret the day.

'Or are you hoping to win some wealth? Long-lost dwarf treasure? Know that we have ancient claim to this land and the treasures here are ours. However... perhaps you might win some small share by lending a hand.

'Even now, I am putting together a survey of the Glimmerdeep, a delve we mined once, long ago. Help in our preparations and perhaps you will be permitted to join the expedition.

'Go collect surveying equipment and expeditionary supplies. The surveying equipment is given to the surveyors, the supplies packed in bags. Then we will talk.''What is it, kízinek? The expedition is prepared to set off? Truly? You work swiftly, ${CLASS}, I must grant you that.'Where there is honey there are sure to be bees. In order to collect honey, the bees will need to be dealt with.Take care of any bees that get in your way while collecting honey.Defeat bees while collecting honey (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Take care of any bees that get in your way while collecting honey.CompletedWhere there is honey there are sure to be bees. In order to collect honey, the bees will need to be removed.Several swarms of bees were merely stumbling blocks on the way to collecting their honey. Their sacrifice will be delicious.After learning that Vethúg Wintermind fell upon the foothills of Winterstith, Dori and Company have decided to seek the fallen cold-drake and discover some final proof of his demise.Dori can be found at Dôm Goru.

You have learned that Vethúg Wintermind crashed upon the slopes of Winterstith, one of the Grey Mountains. You should follow Dori to the end of the Bitter Scar to learn the fate of Vethúg Wintermind.Talk to Dori at Dôm GoruDori can be found in Skarháld.

You have travelled to Skarháld with Dori and Company alongside Ingór, son of Vékun, and the Zhélruka. You should now speak to Dori.Talk to Dori at Skarháld'So Vethúg has fallen upon Winterstith....

'If what the Zhélruka say is true, Nori has already passed beyond the easternmost mountains to Skarháld, but Winterstith lies not far to the north. From what you have told me, Vethúg must have carved a path of frozen ruin through the foothills of the Ered Mithrin as he fell. Ah, but where does it lead? To Winterstith? To the heart of the Anvil itself? We must find out!

'Come, ${PLAYER}. Let us follow the Bitter Scar to its end and see what we discover!''It is time, ${PLAYER}. Let us see where the old cold-drake has come to rest.''We have arrived, ${PLAYER}. It may be named Skarháld in the Common Tongue, but I know it as Tharrazhâr.

'I know well the history of Tharrazhâr, but I must appear to be an old dwarf, frail of mind, to Ingór and his kinsfolk... at least for now. These walls of stone and azurite... they were wrought by Longbeards! Though I cannot contest it, these Zhélruka have no claim to this fortress.

'I have seen more unfriendly glances fall upon our Stout-axe companions, and so, I think we should press on once we have rested.

'Yet, I have not forgotten that Zádokh Flint-eye spoke of a company of Longbeards in Tharrazhâr. He may have spoken half-truths of Vethúg Wintermind, but who can say what will we find here?'Agátur, sent by King Thorin to watch the Zhélruka, fears that Skarháld is ill-defended and unsafe.You should patrol look-out spots in Skarháld.You should return to Agátur in Skarháld and tell him of the drake attacks.Return to Agátur'Welcome, ${CLASS}. We are strangers here, among these strange dwarves. I have followed them at King Thorin's behest, as perhaps you have, too, to keep a wary eye upon all they do.

'Only now I have come not so much to fear what they do as to fear for them. They have stubbornly insisted on making camp among these ruins. They busy themselves with building anew, and seem little to fear for the many, many things around, above, and below that might do them harm. Have they no memory? No tales of what once dwelled here and made war with dwarves of their line and ours?

'Will you go about and patrol the look-outs of Skarháld, ${PLAYER}? It would put me greatly at ease.''Cold-drakes! Have they come here errantly, drawn to our light and noise? Or are we being tested for weaknesses in our defences? I know not, but if you ask me, it bodes ill!'Rúni, a Lore-master from Erebor, has journeyed to the Grey Mountains seeking lost history and knowledge. He suspects that even the fortress of Skarháld may hold secrets.Old chests may be hidden in Skarháld.

You should search any old chests that you find in Skarháld.Rúni can be found in Skarháld.

You should bring the old maps to Rúni.'Well now, I remember you! Did we briefly speak of dragons in Erebor not too long ago? Ah yes, that is what I thought!

'I have come to Ered Mithrin seeking lost history and knowledge. There is much of dwarf culture buried here in the mountains. I suspect that even this fortress, Skarháld, may hold some secrets yet. Have a look around and perhaps we will find something that the Zhélruka have not!'Rúni pores over the old maps.

'Oh, this is too good not to share. I will have to show the Zhélruka as well! Do you know what these maps contain? One map is of Thikil-gundu, an old keep north-west of here.  Another map is of Glitgróf, a mine to the north. The third map is of Stormlind, a crafting hall north-east of here. These maps will assist in planning expeditions. You have found something very helpful, very helpful indeed!'A particularly mischievous wolverine has stolen honey cakes from Hunulf on several occasions.You should find and defeat the wolverine.Defeat Hunulf's BaneYou should find and defeat the wolverine.Defeat Hunulf's BaneYou should return to Hunulf, who can be found in Skoghús, in the Thornholt woods.Return to HunulfA particularly mischievous wolverine has stolen honey cakes from Hunulf on several occasions. He hopes for a just resolution to his problem.'The mischievous creature has met its end? While I do not celebrate its demise, I am thankful for a just resolution.'Glimmerdeep is overrun with Orcs.You should enter the Glimmerdeep, which lies on Mount Thrumfall north of Skarháld.Enter the GlimmerdeepYou should talk to Hórin.Talk to HórinYou should survey the Glimmerdeep and defeat foes you find there.Defeat foes in the Glimmerdeep (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should continue surveying the Glimmerdeep and defeating foes you find.Defeat more foes in the Glimmerdeep (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should continue surveying the Glimmerdeep and defeating foes you find.Defeat even more foes in the Glimmerdeep (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should talk to Hórin.Talk to Hórin'The time has come, ${PLAYER}. We must enter the mine and see what lurks within. Come, kízinek. We go.''Ah, ${PLAYER}. I fear there are indeed too many foes here for us to fend off alone.''The Glitgróf, or Glimmerdeep, is like no mine I have ever seen! There is some exploring to be done if we are to drive out these Orcs.'Hórin, a Zhélruka Dwarf tasked to dealing with outsiders, seeks assistance in deciphering an ancient moon-letter.You should inspect the parchment that Hórin gave you.Inspect the parchmentYou should search in the Grey Mountains for the location hinted at in Hórin's parchment.You should read the moon-runes on the parchment.Read the moon-runesYou should search in the Grey Mountains for the second location, described in moon-runes, on Hórin's parchment.You should read the moon-runes on the parchment.Read the moon-runesYou should search in the Grey Mountains for the third location, described in moon-runes, on Hórin's parchment.You should read the moon-runes on the parchment.Read the moon-runesThe Glimmerdeep can be found north of Skarháld in the Grey Mountains.

You should examine the location in the Glimmerdeep mentioned on Hórin's parchment.Look for a clue in the GlimmerdeepHórin may be in Skarháld or adventuring with you.

You should talk to Hórin.'Have you heard of the fabled underground realm, Thafar-gathol, the Grey Citadel? No dwarf has ever found it, but the lost realm is said to exist somewhere in these northern mountains. I have brought a moon-letter from the east with me. It is said this letter contains a clue as to the location of Thafar-gathol. The tale goes, if a great dwarf finds the Stave of Veluk-zámok, the path to Thafar-gathol will become clear.

'Here, you may borrow the moon-letter, if you like. I am at an end with this riddle. I am no closer to finding the Stave now than I was before I came to Ered Mithrin.'You tell Hórin how you followed the clues and learned the location of the Stave of Veluk-zámok. His eyes widen.

'In Glimmerdeep, of all places, among the treasure of a dragon! Yet dragon and treasure are not to be found? And why not? Centuries upon centuries have passed. And yet dragons live longer than centuries....

'Perhaps, perhaps not all hope is lost.'You tell Hórin how you followed the clues and learned the location of the Stave of Veluk-zámok. His eyes widen.

'In Glimmerdeep, of all places, among the treasure of a dragon! Yet dragon and treasure are not to be found? And why not? Centuries upon centuries have passed. And yet dragons live longer than centuries....

