With the smithies clear of foes, you should now search the smithies for a way into the lore-trove of Afhân-binnîn.The feast-house of Amrâg-zahar can be found through the northernmost doors of Durûz-mazal, the Chamber of Faces.

With the heat of the smithies now spreading through Thikil-gundu, Dori hopes to find another way to reach Afhân-binnîn and continue the search for Nori.Find a way to Afhân-binnîn through the feast-house of Amrâg-zaharNori might be found somewhere within Afhân-binnîn. Afhân-binnîn can be found through Amrâg-zahar.

You have discovered a passage into Afhân-binnîn, and you should now search for Nori within the lore-troves of Thikil-gundu.Frór the Lost can be found in the heights of Afhân-binnîn.

You have discovered the remains of Frór, son of Dáin I, brought to life by a fell spirit in Afhân-binnîn. You should defeat him and let him at last be laid to rest.Defeat Frór the Lost and banish the fell spirit inhabiting his remainsRecover the crown of DáinDori can be found in Thikil-gundu.

You have defeated the fell spirit inhabiting the remains of Frór and recovered the long-lost crown of Dáin. You should offer the crown to Dori and learn what is to be done.Thikil-nesad, the Steel Seat, can be found deep within Thikil-gundu.

Fearing that Nori has been discovered by the inhabitants of the Steel Keep, Dori now hopes to find him before the old throne of Thikil-gundu.Search for Nori at Thikil-nesad, the Steel SeatAthramóth and her brood can be found in Thikil-nesad.

You have discovered a great cold-drake resting among her brood. You should defeat Athramóth and make safe the old throne of the Steel Keep.Defeat AthramóthNori can be found at Thikil-nesad.

Nori has fled into Thikil-nesad and unwittingly reunited with Dori. You should talk to Nori.Talk to Nori at Thikil-nesadDori can be found in Thikil-gundu.

You have reunited with Nori. You should now speak to Dori and learn what is to be done.Zashar-tûmun, the ruin-dwellings of Thikil-gundu, can be found to the west of the feast-house of Amrâg-zahar.

Nori has warned you that Karazgar now prepares a great force of dragon-kind in Zashar-tûmun. You should find a way to Zashar-tûmun and confront Karazgar.Find a way through Amrâg-zahar to Zashar-tûmunKarazgar can be found somewhere within Zashar-tûmun.

Nori has warned you that Karazgar now prepares a great force of dragon-kind in Zashar-tûmun. You should find and confront Karazgar.Find and confront Karazgar in Zashar-tûmunKarazgar can be found in the western reaches of Zashar-tûmun.

You have found Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, and you should now attempt to defeat him.Defeat Karazgar, the Weeping WarriorDori can be found in Thikil-gundu. If you have become separated, you may call him and the rest of the company by using your horn.

You have repelled Karazgar and rescued Nori from the depths of Thikil-gundu. You should now speak to Dori.Talk to Dori in Thikil-gundu'Listen well, ${PLAYER}.

'If these old maps are to be believed, Durûz-mazal should lie beyond these doors. It was a great chamber wrought in honour of the Seven Dwarf-kings and in its centre, a great hoarding-vault was delved. It became known as Immêsh-khalt, and it held the whole of the wealth of Grárik in its depths.

'If my brother came in search of the treasures of Thikil-gundu, he and his expedition would not have had to look any further than Immêsh-khalt. And so, it is there we will begin our search!

'Let us hope these old gears still turn!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: If you become separated from Dori and Company, you can summon them to your side using the Horn of Erebor. In addition, you can access the map of Thikil-gundu by pressing 'M'.</rgb>'It has been frozen solid!

'Is this some final insult by Vethúg Wintermind? If this is how the great cold-drake has chosen to guard his stolen gold, there will be no recovering it... not until all the halls of the Steel Keep are reclaimed. And many still lie ahead of us, ${PLAYER}!

'Many of these old gears are frozen or ruined, but we must find a way to travel deeper into Thikil-gundu. Unless my eyes deceive me, it looks like the gears to some of the eastern halls are intact. If we can reach the old lore-trove of Afhân-binnîn by some path, it would allow us to reach many of the deepest chambers of Thikil-gundu. We must find a way there!

'According to the notes Nori left behind, Afhân-binnîn can be reached through a small hall at the northern end of the smithies of Kall-khebâb.

'Let us find a way into Kall-khebâb, and then we will see if we can find the way into Afhân-binnîn. Besides, it would do us all well to warm ourselves by the forges, I think!

'But... we would be wise to remain on guard. Such silence brings me no comfort.''It is worse than I feared....

'The Dead walk the halls of Thikil-gundu, and through some vile means, Karazgar has mastered the will of Vethúg Wintermind!

'There is little to celebrate this day, ${PLAYER}, but we have found my brother... and that is enough!

'Let us return to Tharrazhâr, and leave this sorrowful place behind.'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: When you complete this quest, you will swiftly travel to the Keep of Tharrazhâr in Skarháld. If you have not finished exploring or seeking treasures in Thikil-gundu, it is advised that you wait to talk to Dori.</rgb>'Bah! Orcs? Hobgoblins? This was supposed to be the swiftest path to Afhân-binnîn!'

Dori studies his map of Thikil-gundu and turns it about a few times.

'It seems that there is another way to the lore-trove, but to reach it, we must pass through the old feast-house of Amrâg-zahar! The gears outside its doors appeared to be frozen when I stood atop the hoarding-vault, but perhaps the heat of the smithies might loosen them.

'We have been lucky to find only a few Orcs so far, but I fear that Karazgar and the Dragon-kind dwelling in Thikil-gundu may soon be aware of us. It is some consolation that those dwarf-wights are not fond of the dragons, but they are no friends of ours either!

'The swifter we find my brother, the swifter we can leave this place. Let us go!''According to my map, there is a way to Afhân-binnîn through the feast-house of Amrâg-zahar. Let us search for it!''How could so many vile spirits be drawn to this place?

'Dwalin once told me of the fate of Skorgrím Dourhand. Have you heard the tale?

'He spoke much of spirits, Gaunt-lords, bloody hands, and other things of which I knew little, but perhaps that was for the best! What a terrible fate for a dwarf... even for one as villainous as Skorgrím!

'What has happened? Has a curse fallen upon Thikil-gundu? So many dwarves have been roused from their rest to serve whatever evil now lurks here....'

Dori pauses and looks at the company of dwarves staring at a great geode in Afhân-binnîn.

'Wait a moment, ${PLAYER}. There is something familiar about that dwarf....

'No... it cannot be! That is Frór, the lost son of Dáin I. After Dáin fell to Vethúg Wintermind, Frór sacrificed himself so that his brothers and kinsfolk could escape from Thikil-gundu. It can be no other... the crown of Dáin I rests upon his head!

'We must banish whatever evil has taken hold of him!''It is with great regret that we cannot bear Frór's remains from the Steel Keep. Thikil-gundu itself will have to serve as his tomb!

'The King under the Mountain demanded that we return bearing a bone of Vethúg Wintermind, but to instead return with the crown of Dáin might speak better of our deeds in the ruins of Grárik!'

Dori places the crown in a pack, and begins to study his map of Thikil-gundu again.

'We should descend into the lore-trove and continue to the northern halls. If we follow that path, we will come to Thikil-nesad! It was once the seat of all of Grárik, and it was there that King Dáin I ruled before he fell.

'Perhaps Nori has hidden himself away before the old throne of Thikil-gundu, but I fear many more foes lurk in these halls.

'Even so, we must press on!''What a terrible cold-drake! We must slay it before it can lay more of its eggs in Thikil-gundu!

'What has become of my brother?''Dori! I cannot believe my eyes!'

Nori rushes forward and embraces his brother.

'It has gone all wrong, brother. I am the last of my expedition. The Weeping Warrior ambushed us before the gates of Thikil-gundu, and he slew the few of my company that survived the Wrack.

'In vengeance, I faced Karazgar in battle. Though I landed many blows, he managed to wrest my axe from my hand! It is a coward's way out of a fight, I think! All I have now is this torch. It has kept me warm, but it is no weapon!'

Dori hands Nori the axe you found before Thikil-gundu.

'My axe! Karazgar will know its bite once more!

'You have found me, brother, and for that I am grateful... but I cannot let Karazgar escape Thikil-gundu! Let us seek our vengeance against Karazgar together!''${PLAYER}....

'What Karazgar has done cannot go unanswered. He has slain our kinsfolk, he has set the Dragons of the North upon the Lonely Mountain, and he has stolen and defiled the halls of Thikil-gundu! We seven must stand against him!'

Dori turns to Nori.

'Where has he gone, brother?'

Nori explains that Karazgar travelled into Zashar-tûmun, but he warns you that many Dragon-kind now lurk in the ruin-dwellings of Thikil-gundu.

'Karazgar must answer for the evil he has worked against Durin's Folk! Baruk Khazâd!''Karazgar must pay for the evil he has wrought!'Orcs have been troubling the road between the Gladden Fields and Duskenvale, preventing trade and travel. The trader Lúfred has asked you to take care of the issue.Lúfred has asked you to stop the Orc activity in Duskenvale so trade can resume.

You should find the Orc camp in the hills in Duskenvale.Find the Orc camp of BrafulugYou have discovered a camp of Orcs in northern Duskenvale.

You should defeat the Orcs and their leader in Brafulug.Defeat Orcs in Brafulug (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Captain UgúrzYou have defeated the Orcs in the hills of Duskenvale.

You should return to Lúfred at the Beorning Watchpost to inform him.Talk to Lúfred'The Beornings tell me that I can't deliver these goods because of Orc activity, but they also will not do anything about it! Why? Because then the post would be undermanned! You look to be skilled in combat, ${CLASS}. Would you please do something about these Orcs? I will pay you, of course!'You inform Lúfred that the Orcs have been defeated.

'Wonderful! I cannot thank you enough, ${PLAYER}. A trader is nothing if he cannot freely trade!

'Now I have to convince these stubborn Beornings that the road can be opened again!'If the Great Goblin thinks that these drawings will help sway more of Goblin-town to his side, it might be worth trying to deliver them yourself.Ideal drawing locations can be found inside Goblin-town.

You should place goblin drawings in ideal drawing locations.Place goblin drawings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You left some of the crude drawings at ideal locations within Goblin-town.Completed'Do you remember those crude drawings we found at Ainhard's homestead? I believe the goblins were going to spread them in Goblin-town in an attempt to sway more of his former followers to the Great Goblin's side. I have them right here. I cannot believe I am entertaining this idea, but perhaps it is worth it to continue the Great Goblin's work?

'Ugh, even the words taste foul to me. But this is one case where it truly seems like we must help the Great Goblin, for he is the lesser of our foes today!'You have placed many of the goblin drawings inside Goblin-town. Perhaps they will convince some of the goblins to return to the side of the Great Goblin.Grimbeorn invites many family members and guests to a feast.Talk to Gírta.Talk to GírtaHelp set the table.Enjoy a moment's rest.Enjoy a moment's rest<rgb=#FF0000>Sit</rgb> down for a meal.Talk to Grimbeorn.Talk to GrimbeornEnjoy a meal.Enjoy a meal'${PLAYER}, I am glad that you have visited my home. You have been a great help and voice of reason. You are always welcome here, my friend.''A Beorninghús feast is one of my favourite things! It is kind of you to volunteer to help us prepare for the feast.

'${PLAYER}, I must finish preparing the food. If you would please help set the table, we will be ready for the guests. Do not worry about the time! Rose, Star, Bud, Sprout, Lily, Violet, Cream, Lady, Clover, Honey-bun, Honey-pie, and Leaf will help you out! Thank you kindly, ${RACE}.

'Oh and some of the food will be out shortly to put on the table.''Eat, eat. There is food enough for all of us.'The tunnel behind Skarháld has been cleared by an unknown interloper. Can it be that Karazgar has come to fulfil his promise?The Dwarves of Skarháld are in danger! You must hurry through the escape tunnel carved behind the fortress if you are to reach them before it is too late!Make your way through the tunnel to the Skarháld throne roomKámat has been seriously wounded and is near death.Talk to Kámat in the throne roomKarazgar has left the keep. If you hurry you might still be able to save some of the Dwarves of Skarháld.Hurry to the exit of the keep and you may still save some livesKarazgar has cut a swath of destruction through the Dwarves of Skarháld, but you may still have a chance to stop him.Find and confront KarazgarKarazgar has come to Skarháld in fulfilment of his second promise.Stand with Gandalf in defence of the Dwarves of SkarháldKarazgar has escaped, but he left his mask behind.Pick up the mask of KarazgarGandalf awaits the mask of Karazgar.Bring the mask of Karazgar to Gandalf'Karazgar is wounded, but still... he overmatched us....

'He left... the keep....''This mask is a relic of power, an artifact of great evil. It is not safe for any others to hold it for long. In my care, however, it will be safe.

'Karazgar has been defeated, for now. We must discuss what should be done next, for he has revealed to us something most peculiar: his desire to possess the Black Book of Mordor.'Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, has declared a truce and desires to speak with you.Karazgar has confronted you at the Old Ford, but for the moment he has declared a truce. He wishes only to speak with you.Approach Karazgar and hear what he has to sayKarazgar has confronted you at the Old Ford, but for the moment he has declared a truce. He wishes only to speak with you.

He waits for you to respond.Give your response to KarazgarKarazgar has confronted you at the Old Ford, but for the moment he has declared a truce. He wishes only to speak with you.

He has given you the chance to ask the questions you wish.Ask Karazgar the questions you wishKarazgar has confronted you at the Old Ford, but for the moment he has declared a truce. He wishes only to speak with you.Listen to what Karazgar has to sayKarazgar has made his request of you: he wants you to give him the Black Book of Mordor.Give Karazgar your response to his requestRemain alert for the reaction of Karazgar, and stay on your guard.Remain on your guardKarazgar has fled from the new arrivals on the road.Wait for the newcomers to arriveElrond and much of his household have made the passage of the mountains.Talk to Elrond on the road near the Old Ford'Lead on, ${PLAYER}. I trust we will find the son of Beorn a hospitable host.''I have come with much of my household across the mountains, by way of the High Pass, for the marriage of my daughter awaits us in Gondor. Aragorn Elessar has claimed the crown of that land, and it is Arwen's wish, and his, that they marry. She will remain by his side forever. I know the way of hard choices, and this is hers to make.

'Glorfindel accompanies us, as do many others of my folk. Some will attend Arwen at the wedding, and others there are who desired to see the lands beyond Imladris one last time before departing this Middle-earth. All are welcome, for such an occasion should be made as happy as can be, and more hearts make for merrier times. We travel light, but not without goods and gifts. I myself bring the Sceptre of Annúminas. It will be a gift for King Elessar, for it is his by right.

'It is my own desire that this journey be full of cheer, for my daughter's benefit, but this land holds only sorrow for me; I will be happy to leave it. I understand there is a pleasant house nearby at which we might find lodging. It was the home of Beorn the Skin-changer, and is kept now by his son, is it not?'You have found an ancient chamber deep beneath Moria. What secrets lie within?Frethi is ready to begin exploring the chamber.

You should talk to Frethi.Talk to FrethiAre you... imagining this?Look around with FrethiYou ask if Frethi saw the strange figure.

'No, I saw nothing. Are you feeling all right, friend?''What in Durin's name have we found here? There are no records of this place! I had thought the only thing we might find down this far was the Stair itself, but... perhaps I was wrong! Let us look around!'A dwarf named Grímyr has asked to speak with you privately about some urgent matter.Grímyr is waiting to speak with you.Talk to GrímyrGrímyr has asked that you follow him to a more discreet location.Follow GrímyrGrímyr has suddenly attacked you!

You should defend yourself!Defend yourself'I do not want to hear your excuses! You must stand before the King! Now!''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. What I must say is very important. Come, let us move somewhere more private.'Despite our efforts, evil still lingers in the world. It has crawled into dark corners and deep places to hide from the light. I will not allow it to threaten one of our own. It will not hide from me anymore.Baradal needs to talk to you.

You should talk to Baradal.Talk to BaradalRánulur has come to finish what he started.

You should defeat Ránulur!Defeat RánulurGaladriel and Arwen are conversing.

You should listen to the conversation.Listen to the conversationElrond would like to speak with you about what has transpired.

You should talk to Elrond.Talk to Elrond'Come, let us be on our way.''He is coming! I am injured, but I will do what I can. Stand behind me!''Being in this place rekindles the pain I felt at my wife's departure. But I will see Celebrían again in the Undying Lands; Arwen will not. I hope this has brought her some peace. She follows in my brother's footsteps, for the love of one of his line. My heart breaks and wells with pride all at once.

'I must thank you for the part you played in this, ${PLAYER}. We were unaware such a powerful foe resided in these ruins, and Baradal might have perished had you not been here. Arwen would have blamed herself had that happened.

'Baradal has been eager to prove herself. I fear she will consider this incident a failure and lose faith in her abilities. Many of those in her position would have seen their valour fail; reassure her of that, if you can. She is dear to our family, and I do not wish her to be mired in self-doubt.

'I believe we are done here. Let us find a place to heal Baradal's injuries.'The Carrock is under attack!Talk to Anórohir.Talk to AnórohirProtect the Carrock and defeat the attacking drakes.Protect the Carrock and defeat the attacking drakesDefeat Ormoth, the drake assault leader.Defeat Ormoth, the drake assault leader.Find Varthmath.Find Varthmath'We must tell Grimbeorn at once. He will be grateful to know that the Eagles gave you warning of this attack.''There you are, ${PLAYER}. Luck would have it that I decided to enter Beorn's cave at the Carrock's base, precisely before these drakes attacked. A group entered the cave, but I fought them off. It would seem they tried to desecrate this sacred cave and the Carrock!''The drakes, they approach. I must take leave, for Lord Gwaihir has bade me not to interfere. I have faith in your skills, ${CLASS}. Farewell!'Long ago, many hobbits dwelt in Tûn Melen, the Yellowbank, but none suffered such a fate as one named Sméagol.You are at home in Tûn Melen, and you have much on your mind.

You should find Déagol.Find Déagol in Tûn MelenRadagast is concerned that the Ungoledain may have come to Laerlad because they are searching for Rhosgobel.Defeat Ungoledain at Rhosgobel.Defeat Ungoledain at RhosgobelListen to RadagastTalk to Radagast outside his cottage.Talk to Radagast outside his cottageDefend Rhosgobel from the Ungoledain.Defend Rhosgobel from the UngoledainDefeat Mengris Tásakh.Defeat Mengris TásakhLet events transpire.Let events transpire'It is done. The Ungoledain will no longer linger here.''Good work, ${PLAYER}. We have secured Rhosgobel, for now.

'I fear our time to rest will be cut short, my friend. More of the Ungoledain approach as we speak! Prepare yourself, ${RACE}. I will do what I can to assist you.'You have earned the trust of Gultháva, the Maiden of the Gladden, and she has brought you into Kidzul-kâlah with the hope that you will be a comfort to her friend.The Maiden of the Gladden is inside Kidzul-kâlah.Talk to Gultháva, the Maiden of the GladdenGultháva has released the enchantment that sealed the chamber in which her friend resides, deep within Kidzul-kâlah.Make your way through the torrential passage to the chamber where Gultháva keeps her friendSmooth paths of crushed stones climb to a rocky platform in the centre of the odd chamber.Climb one of the paths up to the centre of the grottoIn appearance, Gultháva's friend puts you in mind of Vágari the Wanderer, but whereas that shade filled you with a sense of dread, this one does not.Talk to Gultháva's friendA piercing scream shattered the stillness! Has something happened to Gultháva?Hurry back down the torrential passage and find GulthávaGandalf the Wizard and the Elf-lords Elrond and Glorfindel have come to the flooded passages of Kidzul-kâlah.Talk to Elrond in the torrential passageGandalf, Elrond, and Glorfindel have entered Kidzul-kâlah and wants you to lead them to Gultháva's friend.Lead Gandalf and the Elves to Gultháva's friendGultháva's friend has been revealed to be the shade of Isildur, long kept imprisoned in a secret grotto behind Kidzul-kâlah.Talk to the shade of Isildur in Gultháva's secret grottoGultháva's friend has been revealed to be the shade of Isildur, long kept imprisoned in a secret grotto behind Kidzul-kâlah.Remain alert in the secret grottoGultháva mourns the disappearance of her friend, the shade she held captive for so long.Talk to Gultháva in her secret grotto'Gandalf's suspicion proved correct, ${PLAYER}. I did not think it could be so, but when I beheld him with my own eyes I could not deny it. The shade of Isildur remained after his death, and Gultháva kept him hidden from friend and foe alike. But what can have bound him here beyond the span of his life? There is more to learn of this matter.

'He spoke of an island in the river. He must have returned to Tol Send. Go there and speak with him, and then return to the Beorninghús with what you have learned.''I have not allowed anyone to see my friend for many a span of years. Perhaps your visit will be a comfort to him.''Who... are you? Have you come to let me out? I have been here... for a long time.'

The shade looks around the grotto with evident confusion.

'I think I meant to leave, but whenever I set my mind to it I am overcome by such drowsiness. I should ask Gultháva about it....''I have spent some time in discussion with Gandalf, and he persuaded me to join him as he unravels this mystery. I do not believe we will find that for which he hopes, but I have brought the sceptre of Annúminas with me at his request.

'The keeper of this place sought to bar our passage. I grieve to cause her such pain, but we must know what she has sealed within Kidzul-kâlah.''I see the faces of those I knew... but that was so long ago. There was war, and it seemed it would never end. And yet it did... at last there was an ending, but...

'Peace? No... it did not last. I remember... I remember...

'I remember death! Treachery and death! I swam... there was an island in the river... an island....''You have done me a cruelty! In return I demand this of you: harm him not! If you can bring him peace, I charge you with the accomplishment of it!

'There are those who seek to do him ill: forces of evil, servants of a powerful darkness. I brought him here to give him safety from them, and I sang my slumbering song so he might find rest. I knew it could not last, and I wept, but I wanted only to keep him safe. That is all I ever wanted.

'Beware his foes, cruel stranger. A shade stalks among the foetid pools, and dread follows in his train; he seeks my friend, and I have long kept him away. Now that you have broken my protections, you must take up the mantle in his defence. If you should fail, my wrath shall be as the deluge, and will wash you away in the torrent!'Gandalf desires to speak to you concerning what he has learned from his study of the Black Book of Mordor.Gandalf stands within the Halls of Memory woven by the words of Vóin the Zhélruka, long ago.Talk to Gandalf in the Halls of MemoryGandalf speaks to you of the Halls of Memory, a representation of the contents of the Black Book of Mordor given shape by his words and those of the dwarf Vóin.Listen to Gandalf's description of Vóin's accountVóin wrote of a journey he took to a "cold, hard land," where great evil dwelt. Further information about this journey lies ahead, beyond the unformed passage that joins the several Halls of Memory.Move through the twisting passage until you reach another of the Halls of MemoryGandalf has found the place he sought in the Black Book, and is ready to speak to you of Vóin's journey in Mordor.Talk to Gandalf in Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryGandalf is speaking to you of Vóin's journey in Mordor, where he apparently obtained the Black Book, but the Wizard remains confused about a number of obscured events in the Zhélruka's account.Listen to the musings of GandalfSomething befell Vóin among the rocky crags of Mordor, but his account remains vague, the details obscured.

Gandalf has asked you to consider the cryptic figures he has found in Vóin's account of his journey in Mordor.Consider the obscured figures in Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryGandalf informed you, regretfully, that this branch of his study has come to naught. Instead, the Wizard desires for you to return to the entrance of the Halls of Memory, there to follow a different avenue of inquiry.Return to the entrance of the Halls of Memory, there to pursue a different avenue of inquiryGandalf waits at the entrance to the Halls of Memory, prepared to follow a different avenue of inquiry.Talk to Gandalf in the Halls of MemoryVóin wrote of a journey he took in search of Thafar-gathol in the company of King Thráin I, who led the Longbeards prior to the founding of Erebor beneath the Lonely Mountain. Further information about this journey lies ahead, beyond the unformed passage that joins the several Halls of Memory.Move through the twisting passage until you reach another of the Halls of MemoryGandalf has found the place he sought in the Black Book, and is ready to speak to you of Vóin's journey in the Grey Mountains.Talk to Gandalf in the Grey Mountains, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryGandalf has told you that despite failing to find Thafar-gathol, Vóin found something of interest in the Grey Mountains, and he has asked you to consider what it might have been.Consider the obscured figures in the Grey Mountains, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryWhile adventuring in the Grey Mountains, Vóin encountered an Elf named Magoldir, who claimed to oppose evil in all of its forms. According to the Wizard, the name "Magoldir" occurs frequently among the pages of the Black Book of Mordor.Listen to Gandalf's thoughts concerning Vóin's encounter with MagoldirGandalf has told you that the name Magoldir does not appear only within the pages of the Black Book of Mordor, and he desires to tell you what else he knows of the Elf.Move through the twisting passage and Gandalf will tell you what he knows of MagoldirGandalf has told you that the name Magoldir does not appear only within the pages of the Black Book of Mordor, and he desires to tell you what else he knows of the Elf.Talk to Gandalf in Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryGandalf's study of the Black Book of Mordor has raised as many questions as it has answered.Many questions remain'That is all I have learned so far. It will take some days to learn more, but I am confident Vóin and Magoldir will not keep their secrets from me for long.''Much of the writing within the Black Book of Mordor remains a mystery to me, ${PLAYER}, but I know enough of ancient dwarvish to make a beginning. The tome was written by a dwarf named Vóin, the thrice-great grandfather of Khîl Tale-spinner. Like Khîl himself, Vóin was one of the Zhélruka. He kept a home in Járnfast more than a thousand years ago, but the Black Book you found within the hidden chamber there is all that remains of his life. Vóin himself appears in no other record that I have found.

'The writing of dwarves, or at least that of the Longbeards I have known, is generally straightforward and to the point. Not so that of the Zhélruka! Indeed, while I have not had occasion to study many works of the dwarves of the East, Vóin's writing in the Black Book seems more convoluted than others of his kind. He writes in multiple languages, using many different runic alphabets. Furthermore, some of these scrawlings are clearly written using a cipher, or I am not a Wizard! There are curious gaps between some of these lines and on some of these pages. Khîl's cryptic ancestor may have written some of his account with hidden letters, ${PLAYER}, but unless we learn their trick they will remain invisible to us. That is a pity, but I warrant the visible writing will keep us busy enough!

'He writes often of the lost city of Thafar-gathol. It seems to have been a preoccupation of his.''Vóin writes of Mordor in many different pages of his tome, but I cannot tell if he speaks of one journey or several. The writing on these pages is confused, and many of the details are obscured; I cannot say when he adventured there or for what purpose. I believe when he reached the end of the book, he continued to write on the overleaf pages, seemingly at random. This makes a true accounting of the chronology difficult.

'And yet, there are some conclusions to be made. This black-bound tome bears the mark of Sauron, and that is not a symbol commonly found beyond the borders of his land.''Spare me this look of disappointment, ${PLAYER}! I have not been idle, and while that course of study has come to an early end, there is another avenue upon which I have had some success.

'I told you that Vóin filled the Black Book with much talk of Thafar-gathol; indeed, it seems to have been an obsession of his. The reading was slow, and for a time I paid it little mind, for I could not see how it might relate to the rest of his writings. But I am a Wizard! I am Gandalf! I was seized with sudden inspiration and knew there must be some element I missed, a kernel of useful information buried within his constant talk of Thafar-gathol. And so there was!''Why did King Thráin agree to lead an expedition in search of Thafar-gathol? His father Náin had been slain in Moria, along with many of his people, and the survivors gathered in the Grey Mountains. They dwelled for a time in the Steel Keep and reopened several of their mines, but it was not enough. If he could make use of the Zhélruka to find a more suitable home, with wealth to rival lost Khazad-dûm, he would do so.''Almost two thousand years before this encounter with Vóin in the Ered Mithrin, an Elf named Magoldir served beneath High King Gil-galad during the Siege of Barad-dûr. His name appears in a list of Elves who maintained the fortifications of an encampment in Mordor called Echad-in-Edhil, but that is all. If he performed any heroic deeds on behalf of the Last Alliance, they are not recorded.

'Indeed, if it were not for the frequent mentions of his name in the Black Book, I should think he perished during the Siege.'The Great Goblin's forces are approaching. The Warlord's host lies in wait. Battle will soon erupt. All we can do now is watch, and hope they fall upon each other's spears.The Great Goblin's forces are approaching.

You should talk to Skalla.Talk to SkallaThe Great Goblin's forces are approaching, and will soon clash with those of the hobgoblin warlord.Witness the battleBattle has been joined!Witness the battleThe battle has been won!

You should talk to Langhár.Talk to Langhár'Coming, ${PLAYER}?''We received word that the Great Goblin and his host are on the move and will arrive soon! Ready yourself; this may be messy.''What a battle! Those goblin fools did not expect a visit from a Great Eagle! Haha!

'Let us return to Vegbár. I could use a hot meal and a stiff drink!'The time has come to speak the password to the Emissary.You should talk to the Emissary in the Mountains of Shadow Chamber.Talk to the EmissaryThe Emissary has asked you to speak the pass code in "the King's chosen language".

You should speak the words to the Emissary.Speak the WordsTalk to the EmissaryYou have the key!

You should unlock the door and retrieve the second stone!Unlock the doorThe door is open!

You should retrieve the second stone!Take the second stone'You are a worthy servant of the Master. Go, and complete your mission.''The time has come to prove yourself worthy. Speak the words in the King's chosen language.''You are worthy! Take this. By your own hands the door may be unlocked. Reunite the stones. All will serve the Master!'

He reaches into the gaping maw that comprises most of his body, produces an ancient key, and hands it to you.Sharkey, the vicious leader of a band of half-orcs and ruffians, has big plans for the Gladden Fields.Tautila can be found in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

You have returned after nightfall as Tautila asked. You should speak to Tautila.Talk to TautilaVulfila can be found in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

Tautila has been taken from his cage and Vulfila appears to have been waiting for you to return. You should confront Vulfila.Talk to VulfilaTautila can be found somewhere in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

Sharkey has taken Tautila and plans to hang him as punishment. You should try to rescue Tautila from Sharkey.Find and rescue Tautila from SharkeyTautila can be found deep within the warrens of Tûn Melen.

You have found Tautila. You should loosen his restraints and free him.Free TautilaTautila can be found deep within the warrens of Tûn Melen.

You have found and freed Tautila, but he is unable to walk. You should talk to Tautila and try to help him to his feet.Talk to TautilaSharkey is in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

Sharkey has revealed himself. You should confront Sharkey.Confront SharkeySharkey is in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

After you defeated his guards, Sharkey has surrendered to you and Tautila. You should talk to Sharkey.Talk to SharkeyTautila can be found in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

You have defeated Garmog. After he surrendered and bartered the true identity of Sharkey for his life, Garmog fled the warrens of Tûn Melen. You should talk to Tautila.Talk to Tautila'Let's... get out of here, ${PLAYER}.''Surprised to see me, eh?

'So was Sharkey... but I told him all about what you and Tautila got up to down here in the warrens.

'Sharkey says whoever captures you gets to keep half the spoils of the next dig. Don't get any ideas about turning back, though, or Sharkey's going to string that little sneak up before the night's out!

'What do you say, ${PLAYER}? Care to make me a richer man?''I did not... expect you to come, ${PLAYER}. 

'My wounds... are not deep. I just need... a moment to catch my breath.'

Tautila's eyes widen as he looks past you.Contrary to Radagast's belief, Sharkey seems to be a pale half-orc. His hair is matted with some sort of white clay, but darker patches are still visible around it. You would never describe a half-orc as handsome, but it seems that Sharkey has taken a great deal of care in crafting his appearance. His brow is furrowed in frustration as he looks you up and down.

'Not one of them could stop you?'

Sharkey glares at you. Behind his stern gaze, you detect a hint of shock.'Wait... please! I can explain! I'm not Sharkey!'

The half-orc lowers his sword.

'My name is Garmog, and I once served Saruman....''What need would a wizard have to lead a band of ruffians for petty thievery? Could a wizard not do such things himself if he so desired?

'How can we trust that Garmog was not... deceiving us?'

You tell Tautila that you can't be certain, but the name Sharkey has long been known to you. From the fields of Bree-land to Andrath, his outlaws have long troubled the Free Peoples. Your mind turns to the pipe-weed discovered in the ruins of Isengard. Could Garmog have spoken true? Is Sharkey the 'old man' truly Saruman?

'Whatever plans Garmog had are undone, and I do not think the Wilderfolk shall need to fear the return of his ruffians.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}... and thank you for saving my life.'While waiting for Grimbeorn's answer, Legolas recalls a recent story told to him by King Thranduil.<rgb=#FF0000>Listen</rgb> to Legolas.<rgb=#FF0000>Listen</rgb> to LegolasLet Legolas tell his story.Let Legolas tell his story'Now you too know how the Woodmen were granted the middle woods of Eryn Lasgalen. I hope this story will be helpful in your dealings with the Woodmen in the Vales of Anduin.'A horde of goblins has departed Goblin-town, wreaking chaos in their path. We have followed, and come to their new hiding place. Now, we have them cornered. It is time we found out exactly what is going on here.Langhár is ready to explain her plan to enter the goblin hideout.

You should talk to Langhár.Talk to LanghárLanghár has asked you to get closer to the cave entrance before she alerts the goblins.

You should get into position, and then <rgb=#FF00FF>wave</rgb> at Langhár to give the signal.Langhár has drawn the attention of the goblins in Waldfast.

You should sneak behind them and find the cave she mentioned.Enter the cave behind the villageYou should investigate the cave to find evidence of why the goblins are here and how many they number.Investigate the caveYou have found the Great Goblin and a large number of goblins loyal to him.

You should listen to him speak to his followers to see what you can learn.Eavesdrop upon the Great GoblinYou have learned much about the Great Goblin's situation.

You should return to Langhár and tell her this new information.Return to Langhár'Come, let's move.''There are a few goblins milling around out here, but this cannot be all of them. There must be more, and I would bet a crate of honeycakes they must be in that cave behind the village.

'Here is what we should do. You go closer, and when you are ready, give the signal. I will shout and roar and draw them from their hiding holes. You then sneak inside the cave, and find out what is really going on here.

'That sounds like a plan to me. Does it sound good to you?''Hah! A great battle, but these tiny things are no match for a Beorning! Now, tell me what you have learned!'

You tell Langhár of the large number of goblins inside, the takeover of Goblin-town by hobgoblins from Gundabad, and the exile of the Great Goblin.

'That has given me much to think about, ${PLAYER}. Let us move away from the goblins before more come to investigate, and we will find somewhere to think this through.'The time has come to enter the dwarf ruin, Shathur-munzu. The woodmen warned us of 'monsters' and 'The Shadow Hunter', but I remain unconvinced. Whatever lies inside, we are capable of defeating it.Baradal is standing by outside Shathur-munzu.

You should talk to Baradal.Talk to BaradalYou find yourself inside a cage in Shathur-munzu.

