You should destroy shadow sources and defeat any foes you find at Kidzul-kâlah.Defeat foes at Kidzul-kâlah (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy sources of shadow at Kidzul-kâlah (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many foes at Kidzul-kâlah.CompletedAn unpleasant darkness has settled upon the Vales of Anduin. Spirits drift upon the Gladdenmere and through the old dwarf ruins of Kidzul-kâlah. These fell beings should be defeated if the Vales are to remain safe.You have defeated many foes at Kidzul-kâlah, making the Vales of Anduin safer.Grimbeorn has tasked you with investigating the increased presence of ghostly figures within the Gladden Fields, for his neighbours among the Woodmen may be threatened by these apparitions.One of the hunters from the village of Hultvís may know what has disturbed the shades in the Gladden Fields.Talk to one of the hunters in HultvísEurik asked you to defeat the Orcs he encountered at Avabárg while you search for his son.Defeat Orcs at Avabárg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Eurik believed that you might find his son among the shades of the Gladden Fields if you did not find him with the Orcs of Avabárg.Defeat shades on the Gladden Fields (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You hear a faint voice calling from the north-western reaches of the Gladden Fields!What is that noise?Badwila disappeared somewhere in the north-western reaches of the Gladden Fields.Look for Badwila in the north-west of the Gladden FieldsYou have found Eurik's son Badwila. He has been injured, but he is surrounded by the corpses of Orcs much less fortunate than he.Talk to Badwila among the Orc corpses'The Woodmen live nigh to the Gladden Fields, and if some evil has stirred up the shades there they will be among the first affected. I did not fight to defend them so recently at the Old Ford for them to succumb to a new danger so soon afterward, ${PLAYER}! Go to Hultvís at Woodsedge and find someone who might know what has happened to disturb shades and spirits formerly at rest.

'One of their hunters may best know, ${PLAYER}. Speak with one of them.''If some evil has stirred up the shades in the Gladden Fields, the Woodmen will be among the first affected. I did not fight to defend them so recently at the Old Ford for them to succumb to a new danger so soon afterward, ${PLAYER}! Go to Hultvís at Woodsedge and find someone who might know what has happened to disturb shades and spirits formerly at rest.

'One of their hunters may best know, ${PLAYER}. Speak with one of them.''Ah, ${PLAYER}. You should speak to Eurik. He and his son went hunting to fill the larders of Hultvís... but only Eurik returned.''${PLAYER}! Can it be? In my heart I hoped you would return, but I feared to send word, and in any event I did not know how to reach you. But for you to arrive at this moment... I dared not hope for it, even at my lowest point!

'My son Badwila is a hunter, as I am, ${PLAYER}. We were hunting on the west bank of the river, not far from Avabárg, when we were surprised by a party of Orcs. That is not unusual in these dark days, and as is our custom we split up in order to frustrate their pursuit. Badwila knows to return to Hultvís when it is safe, but I have not yet seen him!

'Do you think you could look for my son? It may be that the Orcs of Avabárg proved too wily for him to escape, and he may be held in their fort on the island! Slay the Orcs there, and if you still do not see sign of him proceed further south into the Gladden Fields. We heard tell of shades and coloured lights among the pools, and while I forbade him from seeking them he may have been drawn in that direction after escaping from the Orcs! Please, ${PLAYER}, there is no one else to whom I can turn!''Do you think you could look for my son? It may be that the Orcs of Avabárg proved too wily for him to escape, and he may be held in their fort on the island! Slay the Orcs there, and if you still do not see sign of him proceed further south into the Gladden Fields. We heard tell of shades and coloured lights among the pools, and while I forbade him from seeking them he may have been drawn in that direction after escaping from the Orcs! Please, ${PLAYER}, there is no one else to whom I can turn!''Do you think you could look for my son? We heard tell of shades and coloured lights among the pools, and while I forbade him from seeking them he may have been drawn in that direction after escaping from the Orcs! Please, ${PLAYER}, there is no one else to whom I can turn!''Do you think you could look for my son? We heard tell of shades and coloured lights among the pools, and while I forbade him from seeking them he may have been drawn in that direction after escaping from the Orcs! Please, ${PLAYER}, there is no one else to whom I can turn!''Are you... are you a friend?''Did my father... send you to me...? We were separated... and I could not get back to the village. I think the Orcs were trying... to take me alive. That is the only... the only reason I still live!'

Badwila pauses to catch his breath, but when he speaks again his voice is still full of fear and his face is pale.

'Look what the Orcs got for their trouble! Death! Death came for them! It was a terrifying spirit, a shade of evil countenance! It descended upon them and killed them all! It is a wonder it did not kill me too!'You have done as the Maiden of the Gladden asked. Will she now help you uncover the cause of the darkness that has settled upon the Gladden Fields?The Maiden of the Gladden waits for you by the flooded dwarf-hold of Kidzul-kâlah.Talk to Gultháva and follow her into the flooded dwarf-hold of Kidzul-kâlah'Tell me when you are ready, and I will bring you to see my friend. Step with care, for the way may be treacherous for mortals.''My dear friend has suffered for too long. My song keeps him at peace. You have my trust, and may follow me to him.'You found the Maiden of the Gladden, a figure out of Woodmen legend, and followed her into the flooded wilderness. Does she have something to do with the darkness that has settled upon the Gladden Fields?The Maiden of the Gladden has reappeared further to the west along the path that runs through Gladdenmere.Catch up to the Maiden of the Gladden further to the west in GladdenmereThe Maiden of the Gladden stares at you cryptically.Talk to Gultháva, the Maiden of the GladdenThe Maiden of the Gladden has reappeared further along the western path through Gladdenmere.Catch up to the Maiden of the Gladden further to the west along the path through GladdenmereThe Maiden of the Gladden stares at you with enigmatic eyes.Talk to Gultháva, the Maiden of the GladdenThe Maiden of the Gladden asked you to pick up the soil of purity and sprinkle it on flowers planted by her friends throughout the Vales of Anduin.Pick up the soil of purityThe Maiden of the Gladden asked you to find the flowers planted by her long-lost friends throughout the Vales of the Anduin and sprinkle the soil of purity on them. You have marked the locations she gave to you on your map:

Merillif's Flower grows within the camp of Lúlstrok.
Braiglad's Flower grows behind a house in Waldfast.
Silloth's Flower grows on the sloping path that climbs up through Brafulug.
Lenhwest's Flower grows in the camp outside the cave of Fil Sulmog.Sprinkle soil of purity on Merillif's flowerSprinkle soil of purity on Braiglad's flowerSprinkle soil of purity on Silloth's flowerSprinkle soil of purity on Lenhwest's flowerDefeat Orcs or goblins in the Vales of Anduin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Maiden of the Gladden waits for you by the flooded dwarf-hold of Kidzul-kâlah.The Maiden's eyes are deep, still wells. She regards you silently, and disappears.'Few only come this way.'The Maiden regards you with distrust.

'Few only come this way,' she says, and her voice puts you in mind of a sighing breeze among the reeds. 'My friend is not to be disturbed. Go, and let him remain at peace.''What do you seek?'The Maiden motions at the encircling hills with one slender arm.

'Many are they who seek to harm my friend. Long have I kept him safe, granting him the peaceful slumber he needs. How am I to know you wish him well?'

The Maiden falls silent, but her words hang in the air like ripples on the surface of a pond. Then she crouches and picks up a handful of soil, letting the dirt fall back to the ground between delicate fingers.

'So many of my friends have gone away. Merillif and Braiglad, Silloth and Lenhwest... they planted flowers among the vales, and now their flowers are troubled by the encroaching darkness. Sprinkle this pure soil upon their flowers, in memory of my lost friends, and I will know you can be trusted.'

As the Maiden gazes off to the north, beyond the still waters, images rise unbidden in your mind's eye. You see flowers growing among the camps of your foes in the Vales of Anduin, and hastily mark them on your map.'Merillif and Braiglad, Silloth and Lenhwest... they planted flowers among the vales, and now their flowers are troubled by the encroaching darkness. Sprinkle this pure soil upon their flowers, in memory of my lost friends, and I will know you can be trusted.''So many of my friends have gone away. Merillif and Braiglad, Silloth and Lenhwest... they planted flowers among the vales, and now their flowers are troubled by the encroaching darkness. Sprinkle this pure soil upon their flowers, in memory of my lost friends, and I will know you can be trusted.'

As the Maiden gazes off to the north, beyond the still waters, images rise unbidden in your mind's eye. You see flowers growing among the camps of your foes in the Vales of Anduin, and hastily mark them on your map.The Maiden's former distrust is gone. Now she gazes at you with fondness, and her smile sparkles like sunshine on the water.

'I delight to know you have honoured my friends, though they went away so long ago. The passions of Lenhwest could not be quenched, and were not; she was the first to leave, and I missed her most of all. Merillif had a beauty all her own, and as she walked among her gardens there were none who did not love her. Silloth's laughter echoed in the dell, leaping from hearer to hearer, arousing happiness in each one. Wild Braiglad was the last to leave; she drew pleasure from even the most untamed of regions, and delighted in ferocity.

'You have the trust of Gultháva. Perhaps you can help my friend.'Gandalf believes that the reappearance of Vagári the Wanderer may be related in some way to the darkness that has fallen on the Gladden Fields, and he has sent you to find the mysterious shade.Gandalf has asked you to explore several locations in the Gladden Fields, defeating shades you find, and to search for Vagári the Wanderer.Defeat shades in the Gladden Fields (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Search Tol Send for Vagári the WandererSearch Sharkey's Slough for Vagári the WandererSearch Lúlstrok for Vagári the WandererSearch Tûn Melen for Vagári the WandererGandalf has asked you to explore several locations in the Gladden Fields, defeating shades you find, and to search for Vagári the Wanderer.You feel a watchful presence to the westYou felt something watching you from the western side of the Gladden Fields. Could it be the shade you seek?Search for Vagári the Wanderer on the western side of the Gladden FieldsYou have found a watchful presence that seems to match Badwila's description of Vagári the Wanderer on the western side of the Gladden Fields.Talk to the shadeThe shade of Vagári the Wanderer begins to fade from view.The shade of Vagári the Wanderer begins to fade from viewYou felt another watchful presence up the cliff path to the north-west of where you encountered Vagári the Wanderer.Search atop the cliff path to the north-west for the new presenceThe Maiden of the Gladden stands by the rocky path in Gladdenmere, watching you carefully.Talk to Gultháva, the Maiden of the Gladden'That is enough for now, ${PLAYER}. I will continue to think on it, but I ask you to conduct a search throughout the Gladden Fields for signs of this mysterious presence. If Badwila's account can be trusted, as I think it can, you may find some clues about the shade's motivations at places where the Orcs and half-orcs gather. Search too on Tol Send, the Isle of the Flowers, and among the mounds of Tûn Melen.

'You are certain to find lesser shades among the fields as you search these places. Whether they are drawn here by the presence of the Wanderer or not, they pose a danger for the warm-blooded folk of the vales and you should defeat them if you can. It is my hope that your search will reveal why Vagári has returned to this place. If we were to learn that, we might learn much else of use to us.''I ask you to conduct a search throughout the Gladden Fields for signs of this mysterious presence. If Badwila's account can be trusted, as I think it can, you may find some clues about the shade's motivations at places where the Orcs and half-orcs gather. Search too on Tol Send, the Isle of the Flowers, and among the mounds of Tûn Melen.

'You are certain to find lesser shades among the fields as you search these places. Whether they are drawn here by the presence of the Wanderer or not, they pose a danger for the warm-blooded folk of the vales and you should defeat them if you can. It is my hope that your search will reveal why Vagári has returned to this place. If we were to learn that, we might learn much else of use to us.''You felt a watchful presence to the west of the Gladden Fields? Why then have you come here, ${PLAYER}? That way may lie the answers we seek!''Son-of-no-blood! Son-of-no-blood!''Son-of-no-blood! Son-of-no-blood!'

The shade's voice is cruel and cold. It stares past you with wild eyes, seeming to address someone you cannot see, and you are overwhelmed by a feeling of dread that causes the memory of the Nazgûl to rise unbidden in your mind. But the Nazgûl are gone, destroyed alongside the Dark Lord. Your musings are cut short by an unexpected realization. The shade is weeping.

'I ask for only three things!" it cries, its hollow voice thick with despair.
'I ask for my life!' it pleads.
'I ask for escape!' the shade begs, tearing at its face with spectral hands.

The ghostly figure suddenly writhes and shakes. Instead of asking its unseen tormentors for a third boon, it seems to be somewhere else in its memory. 'I am sorry, I am sorry!' it cries.'What brings you to this place?'The Maiden stares at you, her face betraying no emotion. At last she speaks, and her voice is like the sound of water falling over and among stones.

'What has brought you to this place?' she asks quietly.Badwila had a frightening experience in the Gladden Fields, and he related to you that something about the shade he encountered seemed different than Marhairu Winter-hair's encounter with Vagári the Wanderer.Gandalf is inside the Beorninghús, studying the Black Book of Mordor.Talk to Gandalf inside the Beorninghús and tell him of Badwila's frightening encounterElrond is outside the Beorninghús.Talk to Elrond outside the BeorninghúsGandalf is inside the Beorninghús.Talk to Gandalf inside the Beorninghús'It was a man, or so it must have been in life. It put me in mind of the tales my people tell of Vagári the Wanderer, the spectre that stalks the vales; do you know the story? It is said he appears in the mist from time to time, but few have seen him with certainty and none have gotten near enough to hear him speak. Marhairu Winter-hair of the Northmen followed Vagári for a time, but he was missing for a year and when he returned he remembered little of the experience.'

Badwila frowns at you and tests his injured leg.

'Nay, it is not just this Orc-wound that troubles me, ${PLAYER}. I said the shade reminded me of the tales of Vagári the Wanderer, and it does, save one difference. As it approached I felt an overwhelming sensation of hatred, not just for the Orcs but for all living things, and it spoke without breath. It slew every Orc, chanting in its terrible voice, and I feared it would slay me as well were it to pay me any heed. But I feigned death and it did not notice me. Had it done so this Orc-wound would be the least of my troubles, and you would have only a corpse to bear back to my father!

'The words it spoke were not addressed to me, nor I think to the Orcs, but as it set about dispatching its foes it muttered these words without breath: "son-of-no-blood," it said, "son-of-no-blood," over and over. I am chilled to remember it!

'The tales my people tell of Vagári the Wanderer are different. That shade seemed to pose no ill will toward Marhairu Winter-hair, and his account contained no report of such malicious intent or frightening chant. What can it mean, ${PLAYER}? I think my leg is well-enough to carry me home now, but the memory of this encounter will haunt me until the end of my days! Will I become as Marhairu Winter-hair did, scorned by my people? But I know what I saw, and I know it is true!''I said the shade reminded me of the tales of Vagári the Wanderer, and it does, save one difference. As it approached I felt an overwhelming sensation of hatred, not just for the Orcs but for all living things, and it spoke without breath. It slew every Orc, chanting in its terrible voice, and I feared it would slay me as well were it to pay me any heed. But I feigned death and it did not notice me. Had it done so this Orc-wound would be the least of my troubles, and you would have only a corpse to bear back to my father!

'The words it spoke were not addressed to me, nor I think to the Orcs, but as it set about dispatching its foes it muttered these words without breath: "son-of-no-blood," it said, "son-of-no-blood," over and over. I am chilled to remember it!

'The tales my people tell of Vagári the Wanderer are different. That shade seemed to pose no ill will toward Marhairu Winterhair, and his account contained no report of such malicious intent or frightening chant. What can it mean, ${PLAYER}? I think my leg is well-enough to carry me home now, but the memory of this encounter will haunt me until the end of my days! Will I become as Marhairu Winterhair did, scorned by my people? But I know what I saw, and I know it is true!''Have you learned more about the darkness that has settled upon the vales, ${PLAYER}?'

You recount to Gandalf the tale of Badwila the Hunter and his encounter with the hate-filled shade. Gandalf frowns.

'I cannot tell what you think of this story, ${PLAYER}. Is it your belief or Badwila's that this shade he encountered is the same as that encountered by Marhairu Winter-hair, the spirit called Vagári by the Woodmen? The Gladden Fields have seen much death and tragedy over the years, and the Woodmen have long reported mysterious lights and unsettling voices in the marshes. It would not surprise me to learn that this is a second shade, as filled with rage as the first seemed benign. The aura of hatred that Badwila described reminds me of the dread that accompanied Sauron's most powerful servants: the Nazgûl.

'But Sauron is gone, and the Nazgûl with him. This spectre must be something else, and indeed let us be thankful for that! In point of fact, I believe I chanced upon this very shade during my travels, long ago, and thought it gone, never to return. I will tell you of that encounter, but first I ask that you go outside and speak with Master Elrond. I would like to know what he thinks of Badwila's story, for he is counted highly among the Wise and I greatly value his opinion and the reach of his knowledge.''I believe I chanced upon this very shade during my travels, long ago, and thought it gone, never to return. I will tell you of that encounter, but first I ask that you go outside and speak with Elrond. I would like to know what he thinks of Badwila's story, for he is counted highly among the Wise and I greatly value his opinion and the reach of his knowledge.''This tale you weave is of great interest to me, ${PLAYER}, but I am afraid I can provide little insight that Gandalf does not already possess. Save this, perhaps: shades linger most often in furtherance of an oath they swore in life. For a shade of such hatred to remain in this world may indicate that it served some terrible power. The Enemy is gone, but the things He set in motion may not yet have dissipated.

'Whether there be one shade or two at work in the Vales of Anduin, I advise you exercise caution. Whatever may be the truth of it, stories can change over the years; who can say what the original kernel may have been? Vagári the Wanderer may mean no harm to Woodmen or to Beornings, or to those who travel in their lands, but remember that even in the original story, Marhairu Winter-hair was taken from his home for a year, and returned greatly changed. Ill intent or no, his life was altered by that encounter, and I should judge not for the better.

'I miss my library, ${PLAYER}. If only I had access to my books, I might say more! But this will have to do. Return to Gandalf and tell him this: I know what the Wizard believes Vagári the Wanderer to be, and I caution him that it may not be so. Beware!''Whether there be one shade or two at work in the Vales of Anduin, I advise you exercise caution. Whatever may be the truth of it, stories can change over the years; who can say what the original kernel may have been? Vagári the Wanderer may mean no harm to Woodmen or to Beornings, or to those who travel in their lands, but remember that even in the original story, Marhairu Winter-hair was taken from his home for a year, and returned greatly changed.

'I miss my library, ${PLAYER}. If only I had access to my books, I might say more! But this will have to do. Return to Gandalf and tell him this: I know what the Wizard believes Vagári the Wanderer to be, and I caution him that it may not be so. Beware!''I accept Master Elrond's warning, but it does not dissuade me from wishing to learn more about Vagári the Wanderer, and neither should it discourage you!

'I told you I encountered this shade long ago, and it is true. I walked these lands for many years, watching the trials of Men and Elves and lending my aid whenever I deemed it necessary. It was some time after the Battle of Parth Celebrant and the departure of the Éothéod that I walked by the side of the Anduin, pondering who would now defend the Woodmen against the ravages of the Orcs of the Misty Mountains. Then it was that I saw this very shade.

'I would have let it be, ${PLAYER}, had it not spoken. But it called me by a curious name, a name I had never heard before, and it drew me on. I pursued the shade into the swamp, and even from so great a distance I could feel the intense aura of its hatred. This spirit should not have been, and I knew that in the Éothéod's absence, such a hateful presence would endanger the Woodmen.

'I cast a spell of banishment upon the shade, and it vanished. I believed it had been gone for good, but now it has returned. I know not why.'Shákoth poses a great threat to all that enter Glimmerdeep to remove resources while under his watch.Shákoth poses a great threat to all that attempt to remove resources while under his watch in Glimmerdeep.Defeat Shákoth, Scourge of GlimmerdeepShákoth poses a great threat to all that attempt to remove resources while under his watch in Glimmerdeep.Defeat Shákoth, Scourge of GlimmerdeepShákoth poses a great threat to all that attempt to remove resources while under his watch in Glimmerdeep.CompletedShákoth poses a great threat to all that attempt to remove resources while under his watch in Glimmerdeep.Shákoth, Scourge of Glimmerdeep has been defeated. You have removed a significant threat.Sharkey, the vicious leader of a band of half-orcs and ruffians, has big plans for the Gladden Fields.Tautila can be found in the warrens of Tûn Melen.

Tautila has asked for your aid in escaping before Sharkey returns to the warrens of Tûn Melen.Talk to Tautila in the WarrensTautila can be found just outside the Warrens in the Gladden Fields.

You saved Tautila and defeated a half-orc, Garmog, who sought to deceive Sharkey's Men. You should talk to Tautila.Talk to Tautila'I am thankful for what you have done, ${PLAYER}, but still I fear for the vales.

'Even if I am freed from Sharkey's keeping, he will continue to pillage the Gladden Fields... and I do not think it will end there. With each passing day, more ruffians are lured into his service. Sharkey is building an army, and the vales cannot endure another war... not so soon after the last! The skin-changers are a mighty folk, but Sharkey might one day be able to overcome them.

'Every morning Sharkey leaves the warrens, and deep in the night, he returns. Many of Sharkey's demons and ruffians have taken rest by so late an hour, so we might then catch him unawares!

'Leave me, ${PLAYER}. Return in the night and we will put an end to Sharkey... together!'Tautila attempts to get comfortable within the cage.

'I'll see you tonight, ${PLAYER}.''How wonderful it is to see the vales again!'

Tautila breathes deeply and looks around, pausing on the ruined dredging machine next to him.

'Well, perhaps this isn't the most beautiful spot, but it's certainly better than a cage!'The Beorning Ragnild is concerned about the threat posed by the wargs of Duskenvale.Somehow, the wargs have built cages to hold captive animals, and they must be destroyed.Destroy cages to save the animals (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'Many animals have gone missing since the wargs Kasak and Dúlkoth came down from the mountains. Many are lost to the ferocity of the wargs, their sharp teeth and their biting jaws, but several others are... caged? I am puzzled, ${PLAYER}. Who built these cages? Do the wargs work with some allies, cage-builders that work in tandem with them to save their prey for later? These are patient wargs indeed, if that be so, and if not, does that mean they built the cages themselves? And how could this be done?

'It matters not, though the question bothers me tremendously! I will put it from my mind, and ask that you destroy the cages and save the captive animals, no matter who built the cages that confine them. But I cannot banish the thought of the wargs pulling twine with their teeth and assembling wooden poles and boards for the cage-walls, and it quite upsets me! Bah!'The Beorning Ragnild is concerned about the threat posed by the wargs of Duskenvale.Wargs have become a threat to the good folk of the Vales of Anduin, and must be defeated.Defeat wargs in Shathur-munzu (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Brafulug wranglers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'The old dwarf ruins of Shathur-munzu have been quiet for years and years, but no longer. Wargs dwell there now, and they pose a very real threat to those travelling on the road, and to those who herd sheep in Duskenvale. Make war upon the wargs, and make this land safe from them once again.'The Beorning Ragnild is concerned about the threat posed by the wargs of Duskenvale.Defeat Kasak and Dúlkoth, the kin of Drokstaz.Defeat KasakDefeat Dúlkoth'The vile chieftain of the wargs, Drokstaz, may be banished, but his kin remain and they are no less a threat than he was! Kasak and Dúlkoth were born of the same litter, and now they seek to avenge their sire. They have come down from the mountains and will stop at nothing less than to feast upon any of Duskenvale's folk that might smile at the banishment of Drokstaz. Defeat these two brother-wargs, and quickly, for their ranging is swift and their anger deep.'The Beorning Ragnild is concerned about the threat posed by the wargs of Duskenvale.Some sheep have been captured by the wargs of Duskenvale. If you can free the captive sheep, you will have deprived the wargs of a future meal.Free captive sheep from the wargs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'There is something very peculiar about the wargs of Duskenvale, ${PLAYER}. They feed upon whatever animals they can seize in their powerful jaws, but it seems that sometimes they save their prey for later. They drag their victims back to their dens, and when their appetite returns, their meal is ready and waiting to be devoured!

'Many sheep once grazed in Duskenvale, cared for and tended to by my kin. But the wargs have made this a dangerous task! If you can rescue sheep from the wargs, I would greatly appreciate it. Let us show these wargs that we mean to protect our animals!'After reclaiming Moria, the Shapag-hai have taken to wearing the shorn beards of dwarves as trophies.Collect shorn beards from the Shapag-hai at LúlstrokShorn dwarf-beards are worn by the Shapag-hai at Lúlstrok in the north-western reaches of the Gladden Fields.

You have noticed a number of Shapag-hai wearing the beards of slain dwarves as trophies on their armour. You should defeat them and take the dwarf-beards to be burned.Collect shorn beards from the Shapag-hai at Lúlstrok (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Camp-fires can be found in Lúlstrok in the north-western reaches of the Gladden Fields.

You have collected a number of shorn dwarf-beards from the Shapag-hai. To keep them out of the hands of the Orcs, you should burn them in a camp-fire at Lúlstrok.You have burned the dwarf-beards kept as trophies by the Shapag-hai.CompletedAfter defeating one of the Shapag-hai at Lúlstrok, you notice a grim trinket hanging loosely from a band around its neck: a shorn dwarf-beard.

The Shapag-hai must have taken trophies from the dwarves they slew in Moria, and they now wear them as a cruel reminder of their victory over the Iron Garrison.

You should reclaim the shorn dwarf-beards from the Shapag-hai and burn them in one of their camp-fires. No dwarf should be forced to endure such an insult!Although Moria remains lost, you have done much to avenge the dwarves slain by the Shapag-hai.Explore the many interesting locations to be found within the Vales of Anduin.Explore the many interesting locations to be found within the Vales of Anduin.Explore the EyrieExplore High PassExplore HadhodiantExplore AvabárgExplore RhosgobelExplore Gladden FallsAfter his tale was met with disbelief by the people of Hultvís, Atta became mistrustful and withdrawn.Traps can be set outside Hultvís in Woodsedge.

