<rgb=#FF0000>You may enter this instance by using the Instance Join panel. </rgb>Complete The Depths of Kidzul-kâlahGultháva can be found either outside Kidzul-kâlah or inside after defeating the Grasping Maw.

You should talk to Gultháva.Talk to Gultháva'I would ask you to speak with Lord Elrond when you are able, ${PLAYER}. Though we have unraveled one mystery, something still troubles the Master of Rivendell and draws his attention from present matters. With your help he may be able to focus his attention on the here and now.'You approach Gultháva and ask if she might answer the questions of Lord Elrond. She seems to ignore you, barely acknowledging your presence, but then turns to you and speaks.

'We meet again, ${PLAYER}. I have need of your aid.'

She seems completely untroubled by the questioning of two very powerful Elf-lords. She turns to gaze at the ruins of what must have been a grand dwarf-fortress before its ruin. She does not hide her melancholy as she reminisces.

'Once, the dwarves dwelled here in great numbers. They busily toiled below, sang songs, feasted, lived their lives. We had an understanding, and though I did not appreciate it then, I enjoyed their company. But now they are gone, at my bidding. It was I who flooded this place.'

She notices your shock and confusion at this admission. You feel Elrond and Glorfindel's stances change, and see their faces harden. It appears Elrond's suspicions were justified.

'Judge me if you wish, for I failed. I did not achieve what sought. On that day, a great darkness stirred beneath the mountains. The ground shook, and new paths opened deep below. I felt the taint of evil seep ever closer. I could not allow it to corrupt the Gladden. I bade the dwarves leave, and called forth the flood-waters. Dark, twisted things lurk in the depths now. They did not drown, as I had hoped. I merely halted their spread. I am not able to defeat them on my own. Perhaps with your help, I may finally be able to set things right.'

You ask what became of the dwarves that used to occupy this place. Indignation briefly flashes across her face.

'I would never have let them drown by my hand. Whatever befell them after they left the shores of the Gladden was beyond my control. My wish was for them to live their lives happily in other halls.'Elrond appears lost in thought as you approach. He turns to greet you.

'Gandalf was kind to send you. Indeed, something troubles me still, and my thoughts dwell on the years following the War of the Last Alliance.

'In the early years of the Third Age, Khazad-dûm was still a thriving kingdom. Durin's Bane had not yet awoken. Despite this, I have memories of dwarves seeking refuge fleeing from a mining colony in the mountains at a time when all should have been well. It was known as Kidzul-kâlah. Many dwarves were waylaid on the road by Orcs and goblins as they attempted to reach Durin's halls or other nearby forts. They told us of a great flood, perhaps the same flood that caused the Gladden Fields to become the marsh it is today. Perhaps it is as simple as that, an earthquake and a flood....

'And yet, I cannot help but think the Maiden of the Gladden may be involved. The Gladden is her domain, and the waters would answer her call. I would like to travel to Kidzul-kâlah and question Gultháva myself. I would ask you and Glorfindel to accompany me. We will travel to the Gladdenmere and seek her. Find us there.''Gultháva presents a pleasant form, but her answers to Lord Elrond's questions are evasive. Even the patience of the Eldar is not infinite; not while we suspect her of some misdeed. Perhaps she will answer if the inquiry is from someone she trusts. We need to know if she caused the flood, and if she did, if it was due to some dispute with the dwarves, or another petty cause.'Elrond's stance has relaxed considerably after hearing Gultháva's story.

'It appears my suspicions were correct, but her reasoning was sound. She was benevolent enough to give the dwarves time to escape before acting. If she requires assistance clearing this place of evil, she will have it.'You tell the Maiden that you and the Elf-lords will assist her in Kidzul-kâlah.

'Very well, ${PLAYER}. When you are ready, we shall begin. But know this: I must test your strength before I allow you into harm's way.''The darkness has been defeated, and the waters of the Gladden made safe. Thank you for your valour, heroes.'In the depths of Kidzul-kâlah something evil has taken root. The Maiden of the Gladden has asked for your help in removing the corruption.Elrond and Glorfindel are standing by.

You should talk to Elrond.Talk to ElrondThe Maid wishes to test your strength before allowing you passage into the mines of Kidzul-kâlah.

<rgb=#FF0000> If Elrond or Glorfindel will not answer the call of the Beacon of Imladris, try using the chat command /pet release twice to dismiss them, and then re-summon them with the Beacon. </rgb>Pass the Test of the MaidenNow that you have passed the Maid's test, it is time to confront the darkness within the mines.

<rgb=#FF0000> If Elrond or Glorfindel will not answer the call of the Beacon of Imladris, try using the chat command /pet release twice to dismiss them, and then re-summon them with the Beacon. </rgb>Defeat the Grasping MawElrond would like to speak with you.

If he is no longer with you, you may call him to your side with the Beacon of Imladris.

<rgb=#FF0000> If Elrond or Glorfindel will not answer the call of the Beacon of Imladris, try using the chat command /pet release twice to dismiss them, and then re-summon them with the Beacon. </rgb>Talk to Elrond'Glorfindel and I are prepared to assist you with the trial of the Maiden and whatever dangers lie beyond. Be wary; she is more powerful than she appears. If, despite her abilities, she is unable to defeat the foul creatures within the flooded mine, that is all the more reason to be on guard.''Our task here is done. Now that the corruption is purged and the water receded, perhaps life can one day return to this place. Thank you for your part in finding the truth of this matter. Without your presence, we might have punished Gultháva for a crime she did not commit!

'With this mystery solved we will take our leave, and return to the Beorninghús. There is still a long road before us, and we should take advantage of Grimbeorn's hospitality while it is available to us.'In the depths of Kidzul-kâlah, something evil has taken root. The Maiden of the Gladden has asked for your help in removing the corruption.The Maid wishes to test your strength before allowing you passage into the mines of Kidzul-kâlah.Pass the Test of the MaidenNow that you have passed the Maid's test, it is time to confront the darkness within the mines.Defeat the Grasping MawIn the depths of Kidzul-kâlah, something evil has taken root. The Maiden of the Gladden has asked for your help in removing the corruption.The Maid wishes to test your strength before allowing you passage into the mines of Kidzul-kâlah.Pass the Test of the MaidenNow that you have passed the Maid's test, it is time to confront the darkness within the mines.Defeat the Grasping MawIn the depths of Kidzul-kâlah, something evil has taken root. The Maiden of the Gladden has asked for your help in removing the corruption.The Maid wishes to test your strength before allowing you passage into the mines of Kidzul-kâlah.Pass the Test of the MaidenNow that you have passed the Maid's test, it is time to confront the darkness within the mines.Defeat the Grasping MawIn the depths of Kidzul-kâlah something evil has taken root. The Maiden of the Gladden has asked for your help in removing the corruption.Complete The Depths of Kidzul-kâlah (Tier 3)Sterkist has been tasked with feeding the cows, but he has run into a bit of a snag: one of them is missing.Honey-bell is probably somewhere in the Beorning-lands, beyond Sterkist's sight.

You should find some sign of Honey-bell's wanderings.Find some sign of Honey-bell in the Beorning-landsSterkist can be found at the Beorninghús.

You should show Sterkist the bell you found.Bring Honey-bell's bell to SterkistHaldis can be found at the Beorninghús.

You should talk to Haldis about where she found Honey-bell.Honey-bell can be found at the Beorninghús.

You should give Honey-bell a good pat.Give Honey-bell a good <rgb=#00FF00>pat</rgb>Sterkist can be found at the Beorninghús.

You should talk to Sterkist.Talk to Sterkist'I have run into something of a snag with one of my tasks, and I tell you it may be a sign of something much worse! I have been keeping to the safety of the Beorninghús ever since the recent troubles, and feeding the cows seemed enough to keep me here. All of the animals have now been fed, save one, for Honey-bell is gone! Ha. It may be time to save one, indeed.

'Honey-bell may just have wandered off, but if she has, she has gone further than I would like to be searching. Help me find her!''Oh no, that is Honey-bell's bell! Where did you find it? In a warg-den? Tragedy indeed, ${PLAYER}! She was such a good girl, faithful bovine companion, supplier of milk and cheese! We will mourn, not just for the products with which she supplied us but also for her dear loving and sweet demeanour. Poor Honey-bell, to perish in this way!'

Sterkist squints into the distance, looking at the far gate.

'One moment. Is that Haldis I see? And who is that with her?''You are a friend of Sterkist? I see that you found Honey-bell's bell! I took her out for a walk in the sunshine earlier today, for she seemed quite agitated about something and I thought a walk might do her some good. Her bell must have fallen off sometime while we were away and I did not notice it was missing until we returned.

'You found her bell in a warg den? I must say I am grateful you found the bell only, with no cow attached, and that Honey-bell was safe with me, and I with her! I am surprised Sterkist did not see us. We must have just missed one another.''Haldis took Honey-bell for a walk? It seems I sent you chasing for wild goose and worried myself for nothing! I am sorry, but the darkness that has settled upon the vales has me all on edge. I shall focus on my next task, which must surely cause me no alarm. How worrisome could mushrooms possibly be?'Concerned by new threats and unfamiliar names come to the Vales of Anduin, Radagast has enlisted your aid to help mend the hurts done to the Gladden Fields.Radagast can be found in the Gladden Fields.

After learning of several concerning matters, Radagast has asked you to meet him in the Gladden Fields.Meet Radagast at the Gladden FieldsRadagast can be found in the Gladden Fields.

You have found Radagast standing on a small bluff on the marshy banks of the Anduin.Talk to Radagast in the Gladden Fields'Ah, yes! The reason I've come to find you, ${PLAYER}....'

Radagast produces a small diary from his robes. He opens it, and you notice that most of the pages have been torn out. Appearing unconcerned by this, Radagast continues.

'Yes, yes! I have written it all down. It's just not here is all. You would do the same if you lost your writing to a bunch of Orcs! But I digress... I have heard a number of troubling tales about what has happened to the Gladden Fields in my absence.

'You have been of great aid to me in the past, ${PLAYER}, so I would ask that you ride far to the south and meet me on the eastern banks of the Anduin.

'Let us hope whatever hurts have been done to the Gladden Fields might still be mended.''I am glad you have come, ${PLAYER}.

'It has been too long since last I looked upon the Gladden Fields, but seeing them in such a state weighs heavily on my heart.'The stones must be returned to their rightful places in the Crown Chamber.An illusion has appeared before you in the Crown Chamber.

