You should talk to Odahild.Talk to OdahildRadagast can be found in Blómgard.

You should talk to Radagast.Talk to Radagast'Once there was a group of Men who respected the land and nature, and saw the beauty of the mountains and the vales between them. I was unlike them, of course, being a Wizard, but I did see some of myself in them. More than a little! I gave them a gift: the gift of skin-changing. I taught them how to use it, and as they practiced the art they became closer to nature, and I was most glad for them. They were a good people, and they remained so to this day: the Beornings of the Vales of Anduin.

'But other Men became jealous of the gift. They captured one of the skin-changers, and tortured him, and in the end he gave away the secret art. These evil ones were driven mad by this new power, a power they were never intended to wield. These were the Gauredain, men of the wolf, and the Ungoledain, men of the spider.

'I should have been more careful, ${PLAYER}. I repented of the gift, for once it was stolen I could do nothing. Now the Woodmen face a peril of my own creation. We must find their encampment and protect them from the leader of the Ungoledain. Mengris Tásakh, did they call it?''I am glad you have returned, but Odahild wishes to speak with you first. I cannot blame her, for she has become impatient with us. It seems that speaking in hushed tones and whispers does not cause people to trust you, ${PLAYER}, did you know that?

'Well, tell her what you have accomplished for the people of Blómgard. I trust she will be more satisfied if she is kept up to date with our progress.''So this Ungoledan encampment has been destroyed? Good. I felt the air change when you first entered the village, and although I did not know it at the time it truly must have been a good omen. I hope that you have done enough to persuade these beasts to never set foot in our woods again, and if you have not, that you will continue to persuade them until they do!''Odahild believes your arrival was a good portent? Ha! Perhaps it was. I am glad she is pleased with our efforts. Now I have some pressing matters to discuss with you concerning my home. I speak of Rhosgobel.'The stones must be returned to their rightful places in the Crown Chamber.An illusion has appeared before you in the Crown Chamber.

You should inspect the illusion of the third stone.Inspect the illusion of the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Inspect the illusion of the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stone.The missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stone.The missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone is out there somewhere, waiting to be found.Find the third stoneThe missing stone was found by Mordirith, and must be somewhere in Angmar.Find the third stone somewhere in AngmarYou have found one of the missing stones!

You should return it to its rightful place in the Crown Chamber.Return the stone to its pedestalThe vision shows you a triangular stone on this pedestal. This third stone must be out there somewhere, waiting for you to find it. You swear you can hear faint whispers, bidding you to look closer at the illusion.This must be where the third stone was kept, but it is missing. The door was broken down and the stone stolen, but it is unclear how long ago this happened.

Suddenly, the Secret Stone begins to react in its pouch. A new illusion appears before you: the third stone as it had been on its pedestal. You can just make out whispers on the edge of your hearing. They bid you to look closely.As you peer into the illusion of the third stone, vivid imagery appears in your mind.

You see a cave troll entering the chamber and breaking down the door with a massive club. He sees the stone on the pedestal and pauses, becoming transfixed. It entrances him, and he takes the stone and leaves.

It is clear you must track down this troll in order to find the stone.You peer into the illusion of the stone within the hand of the troll. Vivid imagery appears in your mind once more.

The troll, stumbling around in a stupor induced by the stone, broke through the ice and became trapped. Come morning's light, he was turned to stone. There the Third Stone waited for some time, until a Dourhand Dwarf chanced upon the statue. You see the Dourhand climbing the troll, chiselling the precious artifact from its stone hand. He grins wildly as he looks deep into its black surface, and runs off with it under his arm.

It is clear you must track down this Dourhand in order to find the stone.You have found the decayed corpse of a Dourhand Dwarf. You are unsure how long it has been here, but the illusion of the third stone in its hand clearly indicates this is the dwarf for which you were looking.

You peer into the illusion of the stone within the hand of the dead dwarf. Vivid imagery appears in your mind once more.

You see him running along the road alone at night. He is trying to get the stone somewhere in a hurry. Suddenly, he is ambushed by a trio of Blue Crag goblins! He evades them, running north toward the mountains. Eventually, he is cornered against the cliff. The goblins kill him, and steal the stone.

It is clear you must track down these goblins in order to find the stone.The illusions have become more vivid. When you peer into the illusion of the stone, the frozen moment in time springs to life. This time, you can hear it as well.

You see the Blue Crag goblins emerging from the mountains. They look around nervously, muttering to each other. You hear a twig snap somewhere nearby. They pause, and pull out their weapons. A gauradan howls and charges at them from a higher slope. He bellows something at the goblins, but you cannot make out the words. The goblins try to fight back, but are quickly overpowered by the hulking beast-man. The gauradan notices the strange stone still clutched in the hand of the dead goblin. He begins to peer into it, but then winces, and looks away, disgusted by the foul magic attempting to enter his mind. Still careful to look away, he picks up the stone, and hides it in a pouch under his furs. He then runs off toward his village.

It is clear you must track down the gauradan in order to find the stone.The illusions next show you a different gauradan tribesman negotiating with two Angmarim cultists. You are now able to hear their conversation with surprising clarity.

'You were wise to inform us of this, wolf-man. This will bring you great favour with Angmar.'
'I care little for your favours, cultist of Angmar. Take that dark magic far from here!'

The Angmarim turn and leave. Surprisingly, this time when the stone changed hands, no bloodshed occurred. It is clear you must track down the Angmarim in order to find the stone.The illusion now shows you the moment that the Angmarim cultists were ambushed at the cross-roads by Rangers of Eriador. They were both easily brought down by their arrows. The Rangers carefully look through the cultists' belongings, and find the stone.

'Oh ho, what is this? Some hidden treasure?'
'I've never seen anything like it. Let me look at it more closely....'
'Snap out of it! This thing had you completely entranced! Put it away, quickly!'
'This is clearly some artifact of immense evil. Just being in its presence puts me on edge.'
'What should we do? This may be too dangerous to bring back to camp.'
'It must be brought to Aragorn, right away.'
'But he is in the field! You would have to travel past Fornost!'
'It must be done. I will ride as swiftly as I can. You two must return to camp.'

It is clear you must track down the mounted Ranger in order to find the stone.The illusion shows you the Ranger's end. He rode through the Fields of Fornost as fast as his mount would carry him.

'If I can make it to the Greenway, I should be safe!', he mutters urgently. But then a pack of barghests began to close in on him, barking and snapping at his heels. When they reached the shallow pond, the moon's reflection spooked the horse, who reared up, knocking the Ranger to the ground. The Ranger tried to fend off the beasts, but it was the end of them both. As a final act, the Ranger hurled the stone into the pond, hoping no one would find it.Unfortunately, it WAS found. Mordirith, Steward of Angmar, came looking for the missing cultists carrying a powerful artifact. The water did not obscure the stone to one such as he. The dark powers of the Nameless glow like a beacon to the eyes of a wraith.

If the stone was taken by Mordirith, it may be somewhere in Angmar.Haunted by a massive warg that slew her husband at Avabárg, Fara seeks to put an end to the demon-beast that lurks in the old keep of the North-men.Drokstaz the Undying can be found somewhere in the depths of Avabárg.

Desperate to avenge her husband, Fara has begged you to defeat the terrible warg known as Drokstaz the Undying.Find and defeat Drokstaz the Undying in AvabárgFara can be found in Hultvís.

You have slain Drokstaz the Undying and should now return to Fara.Talk to Fara'Will you lend me your ear, ${PLAYER}?

'I have suffered in silence since the ending of the War, but I cannot bear it any longer. My husband Beremud fell at Avabárg in the final days of the War. We had pushed our foes from the walls of Hultvís to the islet, and victory seemed certain... until Drokstaz came. Before either of us could loose an arrow at him, that terrible warg leapt forth and tore into Beremud's throat.  With his last strength, my husband slashed Drokstaz's flank and loosed his entrails. The beast withdrew into the catacombs of Avabárg to die... or so I thought.

'But I have seen his eyes glowing in the night. They stare at me from across the waters of Anduin... from Avabárg. You must believe me! I promised Beremud that I would avenge him, but I cannot go back to that place... I cannot face that creature again, and Drokstaz knows my shame, and taunts me with it.

'I beg you, ${PLAYER}. Slay Drokstaz and put out those terrible eyes!''You have done what I could not....

'I could not bear to look upon that monstrous creature again, but thanks to you, my dear Beremud is avenged.'Hertha, Keeper of the Pass, has asked you to clear out a pack of wargs that plague the road into the Beorning-lands.The Warg Clearing can be found north-west of Vegbár.

You should defeat the wargs and their leader.Defeat wargs at the clearing (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat ShárahaHertha can be found along the road to the High Pass in the Wolf-denes.

You should talk to Hertha.Talk to Hertha'I have a favour to ask of you, ${CLASS}. The Wargs of the Wolf-denes have long been a thorn in the side of the people in the Vales. They are a great enemy of my kin. I have watched most of the warg host move south, but a small pack of them remains to reinforce their allies, the goblins in the Misty Mountains.

'I cannot leave my post to deal with them myself, but they are a plague upon travellers. Please, will you go and clear them out?''Excellent work, ${PLAYER}. I could see your fighting skills from here! You are a true ally of the Beornings.'You have travelled with Baradal to the village of Arhaim. The Woodfolk of the village are distrustful of strangers, and will need to be convinced of your intentions.Baradal has asked you to gather information from the villagers in Arhaim.

You should attempt to talk to the villagers.Talk to the frightened villagerTalk to the suspicious villagerTalk to the superstitious villagerOne of the villagers suggested that you speak to Arhaim's chieftain for information.

You should talk to Garsind.Talk to GarsindBaradal can be found in Arhaim, the woodmen village in Duskenvale.

You should tell Baradal that the woodmen need to be convinced of your trustworthiness.Talk to BaradalThe chieftain of Arhaim, Garsind, has asked you to help the village.

You should complete quests in Duskenvale to earn the villagers' trust. Lúfred, Ingelfled, Goslim and Tassila may have quests for you.Complete quests in Duskenvale (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Garsind can be found in Arhaim, the woodmen village in Duskenvale.

You should talk to Garsind.Talk to GarsindGarsind has told you of Shathur-munzu, the dwarf ruin to the west, and of the monster known as the Shadow Hunter.

You should relay this information to Baradal.Talk to Baradal'I was told that Arwen's brothers found Lady Celebrían in a ruined dwarf fort south of the Gladden Fields, and little else. It would be wise to gather more information before we venture into the wilderness in search of it. We should try speaking with the villagers here. They may be willing to share what they know.''A dwarf fort? There is one to the west, but you must not go near! The wilds are filled with terrible demons! Many have lost their lives seeking treasures of dwarf-make!''Who are you, outsider? Why does that Elf look at me like that? I do not trust you. Go talk to the chieftain if you need something.''Don't go into the forest! The Shadow Hunter will steal you away, and you'll never be seen again!''I am Garsind, Chieftain of Arhaim. You come seeking information, but we have no reason to trust you. Help the village, and we will help you. That is a fair exchange.''Very well, if they require proof of our noble intentions, they shall have it. See if anyone in the village requires our aid. I will do my best to explain to the chieftain why I have come.''You have helped my people, so I will give you the information you seek. The dwarf ruin the Elf speaks of must be Shathur-munzu. It lies to the west. To find it, you must cross the territory of the Shadow Hunter.

'No one has seen his true form and lived. The few that have returned to us say he is shadow itself, slinking among the trees. They describe huge glinting fangs and glowing eyes, and his children stalk the woods as well, terrible demons all.'

'I understand the Elf's mission, but be warned: you continue at your own peril.''Demons, indeed. Superstitions will get us nowhere. Perhaps the dwarf ruin is inhabited by Orcs or the like, but it will not sway me from my charge. Prepare yourself, we will be heading west at once.'Even after his defeat at Dol Guldur, the word of Mazog has not gone unheeded by the Pûlpum and Shapag-hai who now seek to slay the dwarves of Middle-earth.Shapag-hai can be found at Lúlstrok in the north-western reaches of the Gladden Fields.

You have encountered more of the Shapag-hai in the Gladden Fields. You should defeat them and drive them back into Moria.Defeat Shapag-hai at Lúlstrok (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many of the Shapag-hai at Lúlstrok.CompletedAfter discovering an encampment of Shapag-hai in the Gladden Fields, you recall Mazog's last words in the Tower of Dol Guldur:

'Wherever your people catch a glint of gold, or silver, or gems beneath the mountains there will be an Orc to slay him, every one. That is my vow! That is the word of Mazog!'

It seems that Mazog's words have not gone unheeded by the Orcs of Moria. You should defeat them and drive them from the Vales of Anduin.With many of the Shapag-hai slain or driven back into Moria, you have helped to make safe the Vales of Anduin.In the wake of Vethúg Wintermind's defeat at Erebor, Nori set forth with a small company to reclaim the lost hoards of Thikil-gundu. However, great wealth is not all that remains hidden in its frozen halls, and the Dwarves of the North shall soon have to contend with the true master of Thikil-gundu.Defeat the inhabitants of Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep.Defeat Karazgar, the Weeping WarriorDefeat AthramóthDefeat Frór the LostDefeat Karkasht Crow-feederIn the wake of Vethúg Wintermind's defeat at Erebor, Nori set forth with a small company to reclaim the lost hoards of Thikil-gundu. However, great wealth is not all that remains hidden in its frozen halls, and the Dwarves of the North shall soon have to contend with the true master of Thikil-gundu.Defeat the inhabitants of Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep.Defeat Karazgar, the Weeping WarriorDefeat AthramóthDefeat Frór the LostDefeat Karkasht Crow-feederIn the wake of Vethúg Wintermind's defeat at Erebor, Nori set forth with a small company to reclaim the lost hoards of Thikil-gundu. However, great wealth is not all that remains hidden in its frozen halls, and the Dwarves of the North shall soon have to contend with the true master of Thikil-gundu.Defeat the inhabitants of Thikil-gundu, the Steel Keep.Defeat Karazgar, the Weeping WarriorDefeat AthramóthDefeat Frór the LostDefeat Karkasht Crow-feederDeep within the Grey Mountains lies Thikil-gundu. It is home to tomes of knowledge, long thought lost.

