<rgb=#FF0000>Warning: This quest has a one hour time limit. The timer will begin upon accepting the quest.</rgb>Defeat Jókull in the Withered Heath.Defeat JókullYou should return to to Stráhek the Stout in Skarháld and let him know of your success in the Withered Heath.Talk to Stráhek the Stout'You guessed it, there is no possible way I'm setting foot to ground in the Withered Heath! Cold-drakes, blizzards, freezing temperatures? No thank you, this dwarf will stay in the quiet and relative safety of Skarháld.

'You on the other hand have proven time and time again that you don't run from danger or impossible odds! Could you do this one last thing for me?'

<rgb=#FF0000>Warning: This quest has a one hour time limit. The timer will begin upon accepting the quest.</rgb>'I hesitate to ask you to hurry... but could you? Make sure to bundle up!''I commend you for your bravery, ${PLAYER}. The cold-drakes have been weakened greatly... for now.'The Beornings of Vegbár must keep constant watch on both the High Pass and Goblin-town, but can do neither with their buildings and walls in disrepair.The forester Ainhard lives south of Vegbár.

You should talk to Ainhard.

If Ainhard is not at his home, he may have already headed to Vegbár.Talk to AinhardThe wood planks can be found outside Ainhard's home.

You should collect the wood planks.Collect the wood planksWith the new planks, you can now repair some of the damaged walls in Vegbár.

You should repair damaged walls in Vegbár.Repair damaged walls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Skalla can be found in Vegbár, the Beorning outpost in the Wolf-denes.

You should talk to Skalla.Talk to Skalla'Now that you have the materials, they must be shaped. Take the timber to the forester Ainhard south of here. He will cut the wood into usable planks.''You want planks? I have no time to shape these now! Those goblins, or whatever they were, could be back at any moment! Just take the planks over there and leave me be!''You want planks? Ah... I do not have the tools with me to shape them here, but I believe there are some at my homestead. You may leave the untreated timber and take the planks. No charge, of course. I owe you my life!''Good work, ${PLAYER}. Vegbár is now more prepared for the next time the goblins decide to measure their bravery against our blades and our claws.'Ohtar, Esquire of Isildur, wishes to deliver a missive of tactical importance to Eärtur in Adambel.The missive can be found near Ohtar in Díngarth.

You should collect the missive.Collect the missiveEärtur can be found at Adambel.

You should bring the missive to Eärtur.'Thinking about those steaks quite drove another important matter from my mind! What would Isildur say? He would not be happy about it, I will tell you that much!

'Scouts have learned something of great importance for the men at Adambel, and the details of it are contained in this missive. See that it is delivered to Eärtur in Adambel, as quickly as you can! I am counting on you!''Your timing is impeccable, for this missive contains information concerning the plans of our gathered foes, especially those in encampments nearby. These details should enable us to launch assaults upon them before they do the same to us!

'You have my thanks for the timely delivery.'King Amroth wishes to deliver a missive of tactical importance to Nithi at the small outpost of Barthost.The missive can be found near Amroth in Echad-in-Edhil.

You should collect the missive.Collect the missiveNithi can be found at Barthost.

You should bring the missive to Nithi.'I have received information from my scouts that must go to the front line. The dwarf Nithi has secured the ruin of Barthost, located between the Corpse-fields of Parth Daenath and the Shadowfells of Emyn Duir. Bring him this missive and aid the efforts of the Great Alliance.''What is this? I am not sure how helpful the information upon this missive is going to be. The creatures of the Shadowfells move about silently and play tricks on the mind! But I am glad you are here all the same. I could use your assistance!'Eärtur, the Prince of Belfalas, wishes to deliver a missive of tactical importance to Celeborn in Echad-in-Edhil.The missive can be found near Eärtur in Adambel.

You should collect the missive.Collect the missiveCeleborn can be found at Echad-in-Edhil.

You should bring the missive to Celeborn.'The Great Alliance is not just a name, my friend. It is a symbol of the friendship between Men and Elves, and as such we must do whatever we can to maintain it! I have here a missive that must go to Celeborn of the Elves at Echad-in-Edhil. Pick it up and bring it to him, for we must keep the lines of communication open or Sauron will have the victory!''Thank you for bringing this information to me. What does it concern? Does every messenger seek to know the contents of the message upon its delivery?

'I mean no harm by the question. Indeed, I will tell you, for it is only by open communication that we can hope to bring this siege to an end. There are forces gathering at Amon Fuin, and this letter contains information about the fortress built upon that hill that will help us oppose them. This missive may help turn the tide of this war.'The fortress of Amon Fuin houses the Mordorrim, a cult of evil Men who worship Sauron.Statues and shrines to Sauron can be found at Amon Fuin in Parth Daenath.

You should destroy the statues and shrines to Sauron.Destroy the statues and shrines to SauronStatues and shrines to Sauron can be found at Amon Fuin in Parth Daenath.

You should destroy the statues and shrines to Sauron.Destroy statues of the Dark Lord (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy shrines to Sauron (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroyed many statues and shrines of Sauron's worship at Amon Fuin.CompletedThe fortress of Amon Fuin houses the Mordorrim, a cult of evil Men who worship Sauron. Behind the walls of the fortress stand terrible statues and vile shrines at which are performed ritual sacrifices to the Dark Lord. You should destroy these statues and shrines, helping to cleanse some of the evils at Amon Fuin.You have destroyed many statues of the Dark Lord and shrines to Sauron at the fortress of Amon Fuin. The evils of that place are deeply-rooted, but these efforts are a good start on behalf of the Great Alliance.The fortress of Amon Fuin houses the Mordorrim, a cult of evil Men who worship Sauron.Coffers can be found at Amon Fuin in Parth Daenath.

You should search the coffers for supplies.Search coffers for suppliesCoffers can be found at Amon Fuin in Parth Daenath.

You should search the coffers for supplies.Search coffers for supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The supplies that you have found should be brought back to Echad-in-Edhil and stored in a chest.The fortress of Amon Fuin houses the Mordorrim, a cult of evil Men who worship Sauron. The fortress may contain supplies that could be useful for the war effort.You deposit the supplies you found into the chest at Echad-in-Edhil. It is your hope that these provisions will be of some use to the war effort of the Great Alliance.The fortress of Amon Fuin houses the Mordorrim, a cult of evil Men who worship Sauron.Many foes gather at Amon Fuin in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat Amon Fuin's forces.Defeat Amon Fuin's forcesMany foes gather at Amon Fuin in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat Amon Fuin's forces.Defeat Mordorrim at Amon Fuin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Black Númenóreans at Amon Fuin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat MátrothogDefeated many foes at Amon Fuin.CompletedThe fortress of Amon Fuin houses the Mordorrim, a cult of evil Men who worship Sauron. Behind the fortress walls wait many who know only loyalty to the Dark Lord and will die for Sauron if necessary. One such powerful foe is the rogmul named Mátrothog. You should defeat this dangerous foe and any others who perform unspeakable rituals at Amon Fuin.You defeated many foes at the fortress of Amon Fuin, including the vile rogmul Mátrothog, and put an end to some of the unspeakable rituals being performed at that fortress.The Warg dens of Baur Kasak are a dangerous place to venture, but if the Great Alliance is to have victory they must be dared.Vile totems can be found at Baur Kasak in Arandor.

You should burn vile totems at Baur Kasak.Burn vile totems at Baur KasakVile totems can be found at Baur Kasak in Arandor.

You should burn vile totems at Baur Kasak.Burn vile totems at Baur Kasak (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Burned vile totems at Baur Kasak.CompletedThe hungry inhabitants of Baur Kasak allow no intrusion into their domain. Vile totems stand here and there on the hillside, their presence seeming to empower the Wargs with an unnatural ferocity. You should destroy these totems.You destroyed many of the vile totems dotting the hillside at the Warg dens of Baur Kasak, and it is to be hoped this will put an end to the unnatural ferocity the totems seemed to bequeath to the creatures.The Warg dens of Baur Kasak are a dangerous place to venture, but if the Great Alliance is to have victory, they must be dared.Slop piles can be found at Baur Kasak in Arandor.

You should burn slop piles at Baur Kasak.Burn slop piles at Baur KasakSlop piles can be found at Baur Kasak in Arandor.

You should burn slop piles at Baur Kasak.Burn slop piles at Baur Kasak (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Burned slop piles at Baur Kasak.CompletedWithin each crack and crevasse of the hills at Baur Kasak, Wargs feast on disease-ridden piles of mangled flesh. You should burn this vile food, if food it can be called.You burned many piles of the disgusting slop found among the Warg dens of Baur Kasak, depriving the creatures of many stomach-wrenching meals. To your disgust, you find that you can still smell the burning slop, and you hope only that the odour will have faded by dinnertime.A bounty notice asks you to find and defeat Agan-khô, the Mordorrim cult leader in Parth Daenath.Mordorrim can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat Mordorrim cultists and their leader, Agan-khô.Defeat Mordorrim cultists in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Agan-khô in Parth DaenathDefeated Mordorrim cultists.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'You have heard the rumours, of course. Men disappearing in the middle of the night, vanishing from encampments we have safely held for many years of siege? People say the Mordorrim are to blame, but if that is true, why have none managed to catch the cultists responsible?

'I am not saying it is true, but there is no question the Mordorrim conduct evil, corrupting rituals. One of their leaders seems to be Agan-khô, a dangerous user of dark magic. He needs to be slain, but be wary; we do not know the full extent of his powers. Discover them, and then put an end to him so he can never make use of them again, and you will be rewarded!'You defeated Mordorrim cultists and their leader, Agan-khô. Perhaps this will put an end to the disappearances, although the rumours will likely persist.A bounty notice asks you to push back the Black Númenórean's advance in Parth Daenath.Black Númenóreans can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat Black Númenórean soldiers and their captains.Defeat Black Númenórean soldiers in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Black Númenórean captains in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Black Númenórean soldiers.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'After so many years of siege, our forces are spread thin, and every now and again there are fresh assaults at the locations we are weakest! I would wager all of my coin that it's these Black Númenóreans who are responsible. Take up the sword and put a stop to these attacks, because my purse runs low. I need to stop making these wagers!'You have defeated Black Númenórean soldiers and their captains, successfully pushing back their assaults in Parth Daenath. The man who posted the bounty will be pleased that the Great Alliance has been made safer, and if he truly manages to refrain from gambling on the makeup of the enemy forces, his coin-purse should not get much lighter.A bounty notice asks you to take an offensive stance and defeat Easterling soldiers in Parth Daenath.Easterlings can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat Easterling beast-masters.Defeat Easterling beast-masters in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You defeated a number of Easterlings and satisfied the bounty.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'How much of our time is spent making plans, or hiding behind crumbling fortifications? Too much, if you ask me! What we need to do is take the fight to the enemy. It is time to attack! If you share my convictions, you will pick up sword or axe and take action!

'Easterling soldiers have caused us no end of trouble during these years of siege. Attack them directly and you will achieve these two purposes: you will show them our strength, and you will earn yourself a good amount of coin!'You satisfied the bounty on Easterlings in Parth Daenath. The frustrated soldier who posted it will be pleased that you went on the offensive.A bounty notice asks you to find and defeat the gorthorog triplets that control Arandor.Gertheryg can be found in Arandor.

You should defeat the gorthorog triplets.Defeat Urrog in ArandorDefeat Khahún in ArandorDefeat Shumauk in ArandorDefeated the gorthorog triplets.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'Accustomed are we to the foul Orcs and trolls that plague these lands, but the gertheryg are something else, great and terrible! Three such creatures seem to share some form of kinship, and together they maintain control over a large swath of territory.

'Any who can slay these gorthorog monstrosities will be rewarded.'You defeated the three gertheryg that wrought havoc throughout Arandor. The man who posted the bounty will be pleased.A bounty asks you to thin the Orc numbers and slay their lieutenants in Arandor.Orc soldiers can be found in Arandor.

You should defeat Orc soldiers and lieutenants.Defeat Orcs in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orc lieutenants in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated Orc soldiers.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'Pay attention to this notice! Do not assume one of your brethren will heed it! Yes, I am writing this to you! The Orcs' numbers are getting out of control in Arandor, and a group of their lieutenants has been rallying for an assault on our camp. Put an end to it, and you will be handsomely rewarded. Yes, you!'You defeated Orc lieutenants and successfully thinned the ranks of the Orc soldiers. The man who posted the bounty must be pleased, but you do wonder how many potential bounty-seekers his notice may have sent into danger.A bounty notice asks you to defeat trolls in Parth Daenath.Trolls can be found in Parth Daenath, as can the fearsome beast Fuluk.

You should defeat trolls.Defeat trolls in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Fuluk in Parth DaenathYou defeated trolls, as well as the fearsome Fuluk.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'I tell you there is naught more frightening than the lumbering visage of a troll towering over you, weapon raised high and poised to strike. Few of our soldiers are equipped to take down such monstrosities on their own, let alone the great hulking troll Fuluk. He is stronger than the others, and he crushes soldiers beneath his fists just for the amusement of it! He is a formidable foe.

'If you think you can help with our troll problem, slay a few of them and be rewarded, but do not expect a single coin while Fuluk still lives. We need our soldiers uncrushed, and while he stalks the battlefield that is no certain thing.'The man who posted the bounty will be pleased to learn you defeated trolls and put an end to Fuluk's crushing days.A bounty notice asks you to hunt the wargs patrolling around Arandor.Wargs can be found patrolling the waste-lands in Arandor.

You should defeat wargs and pack-masters.Defeat wargs in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat pack-masters in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated wargs.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'Orcs and trolls are bad enough, but now the wargs have become emboldened and run down my soldiers even in broad daylight! Their leaders have no fear of Man, and by their example this new aggression spreads to the rest of the wargs in their pack.

'I'm asking for able-bodied fighters to hunt down and slay some of these stronger wargs. Let the pack know that Man still rules in Mordor!'You have defeated pack-masters and other wargs in Arandor, fulfilling the bounty and ensuring safer travels on the road. The one who posted it should be pleased, but inwardly you wonder if Man will ever truly rule in Mordor, as he desired.Celeborn has learned that a group of soldiers was recently captured by the Enemy.Captured soldiers are being held by the Enemy in Parth Daenath.

You should free any captured soldiers that you find.Celeborn can be found at Echad-in-Edhil.

You should talk to Celeborn.Talk to Celeborn'A group of soldiers was recently ambushed in Parth Daenath, but they were not slain. No, they were taken prisoner instead. Some were taken to the Orc encampment of Mokh Darga, and the others were taken to the fortress of Amon Fuin. I know not why, but their capture must be for the purpose of gaining information. Even were that not so, we must try to bring them back from darkness. We cannot leave them to this torment.''All of the captured soldiers were rescued and brought safely back to Adambel? You have done well indeed, bringing joy to my heart and dealing a blow to the efforts of the Dark Lord. He will learn nothing of our plans, not on this day.'Ontamo is worried about the imminent inspection of the fortifications of Díngarth by Isildur, King of Gondor, and has requested your aid in their improvement.Ontamo asked you to collect wood and stone for patching up the fortifications at Díngarth and to keep resting soldiers from giving Isildur the impression of laziness when he comes to inspect the defences.Collect wood for patching the defences of Díngarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect stone for patching the defences of Díngarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Get resting soldiers at Díngarth moving again (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ontamo has asked you to improve the fortifications at spots in need of repair in and around Díngarth.Ontamo awaits your return in Díngarth.Talk to Ontamo at DíngarthAn Orc raiding party was seen patrolling south of the encampment of Díngarth, and Ontamo asked you to take care of it before King Isildur arrived for his inspection of the defences. Orcs can be found leaving their encampment of Mokh Shapat, far to the south of Díngarth.Defeat Orcs south of Díngarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The guttural voices of Orcs can be heard among the rocks far to the south of Díngarth.Investigate the sound of Orc voices far to the south of DíngarthYou have stumbled upon an Elf surrounded by the bodies of a slain Orc raiding party.Talk to Magoldir among the bodies of the slain OrcsOntamo is at Díngarth, awaiting word of your success against the Orcs.Talk to Ontamo at Díngarth'I have been informed that King Isildur of Gondor plans to return to Díngarth in order to inspect the defences! As the master of fortifications, the responsibility falls to me to ensure that the king is satisfied by the state of them. But that is no easy task, for Díngarth was the first encampment we constructed after the Alliance pushed through the ruins of the Gate, seven years gone. It has been repeatedly assailed by the forces of Sauron, and rebuilt anew following the worst of the assaults.

'Isildur rules Gondor with his brother Anárion, at the behest of their father, the High King, and there is no end to the worries upon which they must dwell as the siege drags on. King Isildur would not trouble to return to Díngarth if he thought our business here unimportant. He must know, as I do, that if the forward positions of the Alliance are pushed back, Díngarth will become our only foothold in the Black Land. To face defeat after such cost of lives and years would be impossible to bear!

'So I ask you to gather wood and stone from around Díngarth and patch up the fortifications. You will impress King Isildur with the sweat of your brow and I shall impress him with my foresight and my leadership. Look too for resting soldiers and get them moving. To see such sluggards lazing about would surely offend the king's eyes. Go then, and see these tasks done!''So I ask you to gather wood and stone from around Díngarth and patch up the fortifications. You will impress King Isildur with the sweat of your brow and I shall impress him with my foresight and my leadership. Look too for resting soldiers and get them moving. To see such sluggards lazing about would surely offend the king's eyes. Go then, and see these tasks done!''The fortifications of Díngarth need to be repaired before King Isildur conducts his inspection! Use the wood and the stone you collected to improve the weaknesses in our defences!''You improved the defences here at Díngarth? Good, good, and just in time! I have received word that a patrol of Orcs has been spotted to the south, leaving the encampment of Mokh Shapat. If we don't do something about them, and at once, King Isildur will be most aggrieved!

'Your work here is not yet done! Hurry to the south and slay the Orc band before the king comes to Díngarth!''I have received word that a patrol of Orcs has been spotted to the south, leaving the encampment of Mokh Shapat. If we don't do something about them, and at once, King Isildur will be most aggrieved! Hurry to the south and slay the Orc band before the king comes to Díngarth!''You heard Orc-voices far to the south of Díngarth? You need to investigate, and at once! The king might be here any moment!''Mae govannen, friend. I am sorry, but you have come too late to join in the festivities!''I am afraid you have come too late to join the festivities, friend. These Orcs thought to do some mischief to the Alliance, but they were neither stealthy nor clever enough to escape my notice. They felt the bite of my blade and did not like it!

'I am called Magoldir, and my sword is Orthadel, the Rising Star. I wield it at the command of Gil-galad, and it has proven the undoing of Orcs and evil Men for the long years of this siege. My errand today was to scout the southern reaches and report discovery of anything suspicious, but if I suspect anything now it is that these Orcs should have known better than to come this far west! Even after these long years of siege, they still test the encampments of both Men and Elves, probing for weaknesses. If they think we will relax our guard, they are mistaken, and we will prove it ever so.

'It was a pleasure to meet you, friend. Return now to Díngarth and relay word of this Orc raiding-party to whichever leader there has the command. These Orcs have come to the end of their raiding days, and will trouble us no further.''You came today from Díngarth? Return there now and relay word of this Orc raiding-party to whichever leader there has the command. These Orcs have come to the end of their raiding days, and will trouble us no further.''What can you say of the Orcs? Dead? Good, but it does not matter.'

Ontamo scowls and clenches his fist.

'Isildur has chosen not to come to Díngarth! This was my opportunity to impress the King of Gondor with my maintenance of the defences, and now it has come to naught! I spoke of Díngarth's importance to the Great Alliance, but it seems Isildur does not agree after all! Instead of a personal visit, he has sent his sons to assess the defences! His sons only! They can bestow neither land nor title, not while their father lives! Bah!'Ontamo believes that he has been personally insulted by Isildur's decision to send his sons to inspect the defences at Díngarth, rather than come himself, and he has decided that you should meet with them instead.Ontamo feels insulted by Isildur's decision to send his sons to conduct the inspection of Díngarth's defences, and has asked you to speak with them instead.Talk to one of Isildur's sons at DíngarthVëamacil is at Díngarth, waiting to take a meal with you before he conducts the inspection of the encampment's defences.Talk to Vëamacil and complete 'Instance: The Sons of Isildur'The sons of Isildur have conducted their inspection, and now Vëamacil wishes to speak with you.Talk to Vëamacil at DíngarthTárandil is at Díngarth.

Vëamacil has decided that you should come with them to Adambel, but that you should first ask his foster-brother Tárandil what he wants as a present for his birthday.Talk to Tárandil at DíngarthVëamacil is waiting to speak to you, now that Tárandil has given you no suggestions for his birthday present.Talk to Vëamacil at DíngarthOrcs and wargs can be found throughout Arandor in Mordor Besieged.

Vëamacil asked you to collect trophies from Orcs and wargs to give to Tárandil for a birthday present.Collect trophies from Orcs in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect trophies from wargs in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tárandil has gone to the primary Gondorian encampment of Adambel.

Vëamacil asked you to slay Orcs and wargs and bring trophies of your victories to his foster-brother Tárandil as a birthday present.Find Tárandil at the encampment of AdambelTárandil is in the primary Gondorian encampment of Adambel.Isildur is at the Gondorian encampment of Adambel.Talk to King Isildur at Adambel'This disrespect cannot stand. If the king chooses not to meet with me himself, well, I shall extend him the same discourtesy by not meeting with the sons he sent in his stead! You meet with them, ${CLASS}. I find myself otherwise engaged and cannot spare the time to meet with three unproven striplings, sons of the king though they be.

'One of them stands yonder, do you see him? He is to the north-east, dressed in green. See what he wants, and why his father could not deign to visit us himself!''One of Isildur's sons stands yonder, do you see him? He is to the north-east, dressed in green. See what he wants, and why his father could not deign to visit us himself!''Yes? Ah, thank you for the welcome, but I must ask you not to use that name. At our uncle's request, my brothers and I have agreed to take on false names in order to hide that we are kindred with the kings. You may call me Vëamacil, and if I do not respond to it, keep trying until I do! One day I will no doubt be used to the name... and when that day comes the war will be over and there will be no further need to take these precautions!'

Vëamacil laughs and nods in the direction of Ontamo, who is poorly attempting to hide his scowls.

'When the siege was still young, a few years back, one of my brothers met with Ontamo. The description he conveyed of him was... not kind. "He is a fawning, little man, more concerned with his own elevation than with the good of the kingdom," he told us. From that day forth, it has amused me to ensure he never gets the ear of my father or my uncle. See how he stews! This siege seems endless, and jests are in short supply; we must find them where we can.

'I like you, my new friend. Let us take a meal first, and then my brothers and I will conduct our inspection. Honestly, there is little need for it. If the Alliance is pushed back from its forward encampments, we will have more problems than a well-defended Díngarth could solve!

'Let me know when you are ready to take a meal and sit with my brothers, friend.''Are you ready? Then let us find my brothers and sit for a time before we conduct the inspection. The siege has gone on for years, and it can wait for the span of one meal, can it not? At any rate, I say we will fight better with full bellies!''As much as I would enjoy reporting the defences insufficient, if only to see Ontamo squirm, I cannot. Díngarth is as prepared as it needs to be. I presume that is your doing? Of course it is!

'I have taken a liking to you, friend! What say you to returning to Adambel with us? You will find the company much more pleasing than here at Díngarth, and I know my brothers will not object. There is one thing, however. You should think of a birthday present to give to Tárandil, and bring it with you when you come to Adambel. Ask him what he wants!''You should think of a birthday present to give to Tárandil, and bring it with you when you come to Adambel. Ask him what he wants!''I don't want anything. Really.

'Actually, if you can get Vëamacil to remain silent for even five minutes, I would consider that the worthiest of birthday presents!''I don't want anything. Actually, if you can get Vëamacil to remain silent for even five minutes, I would consider that the worthiest of birthday presents!''Ah, he's always like this. Tárandil never wants anything. Well, I won't allow him to get away with it this time! He is my foster-brother, and he is going to have the finest of birthdays, with the very best presents. What do you think, my new friend? Give me your hand on this, and we will see it done!'

Vëamacil stands up straight and gives a shout, which causes nearby soldiers to look up in alarm.

'I've got it! We're going to give my foster-brother the only thing he truly wants: we're going to give him an end to the war! Let us do what we can to bring an end to this endless siege of Mordor. That will make for the very best present, will it not?

'Like every good plan, this will take some doing. Go slay some Orcs and some wargs, and collect trophies from them. We can deliver them to Tárandil at the encampment of Adambel, and tell him they are just the first part of his birthday present. He will love it, friend, trust me.''Like every good plan, this will take some doing. Go slay some Orcs and some wargs, and collect trophies from them. We can deliver them to Tárandil at the encampment of Adambel, and tell him they are just the first part of his birthday present. He will love it, friend, trust me.''I bid you welcome, ${CLASS}.''I told you I wanted for nothing, friend, or at least nothing in your power to give! Why should Tárandil ask for aught when the war shows no sign of stopping? Give me an end to the siege, and I will measure it my finest birthday since the war began! But even that would bring little comfort to my heart. These years have been hard, and one good day cannot balance out the rest.

'Vëamacil thought I would want these trophies for a present? They smell terribly, but I tell you true that my brother usually smells much worse! Be not offended if I cast them into a fire and rid the soldiers here of their stench. It is a pity that Vëamacil's odour cannot be cleansed so easily!''My foster-father Isildur does not approve of Vëamacil's jests. It is a trait we share!''What is the meaning of this foolishness? I pray you do not encourage my sons in this behaviour, ${CLASS}, or you will risk my anger. I have taught them the importance of an even temperament and a steady hand, for my sons are of a king's lineage and a ruler has no time for jest. If you are a friend to them, and to me, you will remind them of this from time to time.

'Tárandil is mine to foster, and while he is not in the line of kings, he is still dear to me. I deal with him as I deal with my sons, and it is my hope he will prove a good example to them, and guide them on their path, wherever it leads.'King Isildur of Gondor worries that his sons have allowed the length of the siege to dull their wits and their weapons, and wants them each made sharp again.The shade of Isildur has interrupted the telling of his tale with a brief aside.Talk to the shade of Isildur at AdambelKing Isildur of Gondor is at the encampment of Adambel.Talk to Isildur at AdambelA crate of whetstones can be found by the camp-fires to the north in Adambel, the primary Gondorian encampment in Mordor Besieged.Find a crate of whetstones in AdambelIdle soldiers can be found throughout Adambel.

Isildur asked you to deliver whetstones to idle soldiers of the Alliance, as well as to his sons.Idle soldiers can be found throughout Adambel.

Isildur asked you to deliver whetstones to idle soldiers of the Alliance, as well as to his sons.Deliver whetstones to idle soldiers and the sons of Isildur (5/6)High King Elendil can be found somewhere within Adambel, the primary Gondorian encampment in Mordor Besieged.Deliver a whetstone and a warning to High King Elendil at AdambelHigh King Elendil has commanded that you train with soldiers at the encampment of Adambel and remind them not to relax their guard despite the length of the siege.Vëamacil is at Adambel.

Now that you have trained with the soldiers, High King Elendil expects you to convey his reprimand to his grandson Vëamacil.Talk to Vëamacil at AdambelMagoldir is at Adambel, the primary Gondorian encampment in Mordor Besieged.'Ah, I cannot blame them for relaxing their guard. When first we pushed through the ruins of the Black Gate with the vanguard, it seemed victory was near, and Sauron must surely abandon the fight. Yet it was not so. He hid within his tower, sealing the door against us, and he has not been seen again. Days turned to weeks, weeks turned to months, and then at last we measured the time in years. The siege seems without end. Sauron is trapped in his tower, and though his servants test us from time to time, the Dark Lord seems content to outlast us, or at least to make the attempt. I would rather he face us in battle, and put an end to this endless waiting!

'Is it any wonder that the idleness causes my sons to chafe, sharpening their tongues and not their weapons? It is difficult to remain alert when combat with the enemy is merely a memory; our swords untested, our shields unbattered. Perhaps Sauron makes us wait in the hope that we will grow tired and abandon the cause. If that be so, he is more fool than we thought! We will wait as long as is necessary!''Alas! It is with the benefit of looking back that I know now what I did not know then. Sauron did not hide from us out of fear, and he did not think to outlast us with time alone. Nay, it was with purpose that he waited in Barad-dûr, biding his time. He knew what we did not: the Ghâzab Bôron had won a decisive battle against the armies that had gone to face it. Even now, the Storm of Wrath moved in the direction of Mordor, chasing the defeated Thíuda and Longbeards before it.

'Sauron needed do nothing but wait for the Rhúnhoth hordes to arrive, and wait he did, secure in his tower and confident his victory would arrive with the Storm. We knew not what approached, and we let the long years of the siege lull us into complacency. We would learn our mistake before long, but that is further ahead in my tale.''It has been some time since Sauron sent his Orcs against us, but they may return at any moment. We must be ready, with sharp blades and sturdy armour prepared to turn aside their attacks. Find a crate of whetstones somewhere here at Adambel, by the camp-fires to the north, and deliver its contents to idle soldiers throughout the encampment. Remind them of the need for weapons clean and sharp, and see that they heed my warning! Do not forget to give whetstones to my sons, as well; they may need the reminder most of all. False names will only protect them for so long, and in the end they must rely upon their weapons. That is the way of war.

'I must now attend my brother Anárion, but I will leave you with these tasks and my thanks for their successful completion.''Find a crate of whetstones somewhere here at Adambel and deliver its contents to idle soldiers throughout the encampment. Remind them of the need for weapons clean and sharp, and see that they heed my warning! Do not forget to give whetstones to my sons, as well; they may need the reminder most of all. False names will only protect them for so long.''Thank you, friend. I will make use of this whetstone, and take heed of the warning, as well.''My blade is as sharp as it was both yesterday, last week, and a month past, but all the same I will not spurn the gift. I do not call for the servants of Sauron to attack, but neither do I enjoy the tedium of this endless calm. I will spend some of today sharpening my sword. Pointless shall be neither the exercise nor the blade, my new friend!''Thank you for the whetstone and for father's warning, though I really needed neither. Every day seems much like the one before, and the Orcs have not dared tangle with our warriors for some time!

'Still, there may be some wisdom in it. Aye, I think it may be worthwhile to ensure that everyone who bears a weapon has what he needs to keep it in pristine shape. Yes, my friend, everyone, from the lowest soldier to the very highest!

'Indeed, even my grandsire should not neglect his own weapon. If we need sharpen our blades every morn, I say that even High King Elendil should benefit from his son's advice. Bring a whetstone and my father's warning to my grandsire King Elendil, friend. You will find him somewhere around Adambel, I warrant, and spare not the lecture!''Even my grandsire should not neglect his own weapon, should he? If we need sharpen our blades every morn, I say that even High King Elendil should benefit from his son's advice. Bring a whetstone and my father's warning to my grandsire King Elendil, friend. You will find him somewhere around Adambel, I warrant, and spare not the lecture!''I would not need three guesses to know which of my grandsons sent you to me thus, ${CLASS}. He has spent too long in idleness, and forgotten the danger. The tedium of the hours is a dangerous foe for an army at rest, but a forgotten enemy long-accepted is even more of a threat!

'I will take this whetstone, and thank you for it, but know this: my blade Narsil needs sharpening but rarely, and I am not lax with its keeping. Let my grandson take these words to heart: a soldier needs sharpening just as a weapon does, and if one is neglected neither can carry out its work. He should treat the business of war more seriously, and leave the jesting for tavern-keepers and stable-boys.

'Find some soldiers with which to train, as a reminder not to let down their guard. Afterwards, convey the words I spoke to my grandson, as well as my displeasure. He has the blood of kings, and should act such.''Find some soldiers with which to train as a reminder not to let down their guard. Afterwards, convey the words I spoke to my grandson, as well as my displeasure. He has the blood of kings, and should act such.''It is the command of the High King? Let us begin, and without delay!''I am ready for you! Hold nothing back, for I will not either!''My muscles ache already, but if this is the High King's command I will not cry off. Let us begin!''It was only a bit of fun, my friend. Do not let my grandfather's words sour your day. But look! Did you not say you met this Elf earlier, among the corpses of our enemies and his? He has come now to Adambel on some errand, and will be pleased to speak with you.

'Give him a whetstone!''He has a sword. Why not give him a whetstone and remind him that we are at war?''Hello again. I will take one of these whetstones, and gladly, for my own sword could use the sharpening. Let it not be said that I took poor care of Orthadel, the Rising Star, for in return he might take poor care of me!

'Elf-soldiers, like those of other races, must keep their weapons and their wits as sharp as they can. That has always been my experience, and I wonder if it has been yours?'Magoldir has come to Adambel on behalf of the Bright Company, and seeks your help.Magoldir desires to scout a location to the east of Adambel for Glorfindel of the Bright Company.Find the location east of Adambel that Magoldir wishes to scoutDefeat foes of the Great Alliance in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to Magoldir on the northern slope of Amon AmarthMagoldir wishes to report to Glorfindel of the Bright Company at the encampment of Echad-in-Edhil, north-west of Adambel in Mordor Besieged.Find the entrance to Echad-in-EdhilTalk to Magoldir at the entrance to Echad-in-EdhilMagoldir stands at the entrance of Echad-in-Edhil, waiting for your readiness to report to Glorfindel, leader of the Bright Company.Talk to Magoldir and complete 'Instance: Tales of the Bright Company'Glorfindel, leader of the Bright Company, is waiting to speak with you in the Elf-camp of Echad-in-Edhil.Talk to Glorfindel at Echad-in-Edhil'It is no accident that brought me hither, friend. No indeed! I came here seeking you, for I have been given an errand for which you might be well-suited. These two eyes have the keen sight of my people, but the eyes of one such as you might pick up something I miss, and your skills turn aside the danger of whatever we find!

'I was sent to scout the southern reaches at the behest of Glorfindel, leader of the Glánnothrim, the Bright Company. They have devoted themselves to combating the evils of old that linger still in Middle-earth. Oh, do not mistake me! I do not belong to that esteemed group, but I tell you true: if an Elf of the Bright Company asks you to do something, only a fool or a coward would refuse, and I deem myself neither. It is my belief that Glorfindel plans their next step, and the information I return to him could prove crucial! Will you aid me in this?'

Magoldir smiles and raises Orthadel in salute.

'It shall be a fine hunt, my friend. Let us patrol to the east of this encampment, skirting the northern face of Amon Amarth, and slay whatever foes mischance to cross our path. There is a location I would have us scout, and once I am satisfied we have not missed anything, I will make my report to Glorfindel.''Let us patrol to the east of this encampment, skirting the northern face of Amon Amarth, and slay whatever foes mischance to cross our path. There is a location I would have us scout, and once I am satisfied we have not missed anything, I will make my report to Glorfindel.''Now let us see what we might spy from the height.'

Magoldir peers into the distance, looking west, north, and east from the slope of Amon Amarth.

'I do not know if this information will aid the Bright Company after all, for all is quiet, and there is little movement upon the plain. Is there some fell reason for this calm, or does Sauron tire of his war as we do? I do not like this uncertainty, friend. See you there the great fortress across the plain? That is Amon Fuin, the Gloom-hill, and it is a den of evil. I do not like it, but I tell you true that I like the quiet of Parth Daenath even less, if that were possible!

'If you are willing, I would ask you to come with me to Echad-in-Edhil, where we can report of this to Glorfindel. It may be he will know what to make of this unpleasant stillness.''We have arrived. Are you ready to go with me to deliver my report? I do wish we had something of substance to tell Glorfindel, but no scout can decide what it is he finds.

'Let me know when you are ready to meet with the leader of the Bright Company.''This way, my friend. May Glorfindel know what to make of this unpleasant stillness.''I welcome any assistance you can provide, ${CLASS}, not just to the Bright Company but to the Great Alliance as well. We must not let Sauron's inaction lull us to sleep. Indeed, let us renew our attention and maintain our guard, and perhaps we will catch unawares the Dark Lord himself!

'His is a singular evil, for every command comes from Barad-dûr: when to attack, when to retreat, and when to mask his intention. Against this stands our Alliance, and its strength comes from the unity of our separate peoples. It is Gil-galad's belief that Sauron will try to sever those bonds, and in so doing he will reveal our greatest weakness, and strike at our vulnerability.

'But instead he has pulled back his hand, abandoning all pretense of engaging us in serious combat. It puzzles me that the Dark Lord has drawn his forces back in this fashion. What is he planning?'Soldiers who fell in battle have been brought back as horrifying wights to fight against their fellows.Elf-wights and fallen soldier wights can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should lay to rest your slain fellows.Defeat Elf-wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat fallen soldier wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thranduil can be found at Echad-in-Edhil.

You should talk to Thranduil.Talk to Thranduil'Must we now fight our own? By some dark necromancy, Sauron has infused the bodies of our fallen with vile spirits and brought them back to fight their former brethren. These were once good Men and Elves, but now they move without thought, animated by vile spirits. We must slay these wights, and put an end to this worst affront of Sauron.''The wights are no more? Then the bodies of our fallen no longer move in anguish, and may return to their rest. Should the Dark Lord send these spirits against us once again, we will be ready. I thank you for what you have done this day.'Celeborn seeks to raise the heraldry of the Elves throughout Parth Daenath.You should raise heraldry of Gil-galad, Eryn Galen, and Lothlórien in Parth Daenath to intimidate the Enemy and boost the spirits of the soldiers.Raise heraldry of Gil-galad in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Raise heraldry of Eryn Galen in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Raise heraldry of Lothlórien in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Celeborn can be found in Echad-in-Edhil.

You should talk to Celeborn.Talk to Celeborn'The banners of the Elves have oft struck fear into the hearts of our enemies, if they indeed have any! Take these and raise them throughout the Corpse-fields of Parth Daenath. Doing so will shake the courage of our foes and inspire the spirits of our friends.''The banners now surround the lands of our enemies? It foreshadows the future, for soon it will be our armies that surround the foe and end the siege.'Eärtur, the Prince of Belfalas, seeks to plant the heraldry of the High King throughout Arandor.You should place heraldry of the High King in Arandor to intimidate the Enemy and boost the spirits of the soldiers.Raise heraldry of the High King in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Eärtur can be found at Adambel.

You should talk to Eärtur.Talk to Eärtur'This land will never possess the beauty of Belfalas, my home. Still, I have lived here for...'

Eärtur pauses a moment.

'...seven years now? Can it really be so? Perhaps a boost of our spirits is needed, and even better if we might strike fear into the hearts of our enemies at the same time! Take this heraldry of High King Elendil and plant it throughout the Kingsland of Arandor. May it bring strength to our fellows, and drain the courage from our foes!''I see the banners of the High King waving, even from afar. Perhaps the winds are changing, and the siege will soon be over. Can you feel it too?'Ohtar, Esquire of Isildur, is in need of additional food to send to the front lines.Sûlokil can be found throughout Arandor.

You should hunt sûlokil in order to gather meat for the soldiers of the Great Alliance.Gather sûlok meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ohtar can be found at Díngarth.

You should bring the sûlok meat to Ohtar.'Our store of provisions is now quite low, and we will need additional food here at Díngarth as well as on the front line. In order to meet these demands, we must hunt for food, but the small animals of Gondor and Ithilien are not large enough to provide enough meat for our armies.

'We need an animal with more meat on its bones, and the great beast that is the sûlok of Mordor will provide the nourishment our soldiers require. Go forth and hunt the sûlokil in the Kingsland of Arandor.''Yes, this meat will do nicely, and there is certainly a great deal of it! Your hunting skills are commendable. I shall have the meat cured and brought to the front line as soon as possible, and the soldiers will dine on sûlok steak! It will be a fine change for them, and they will toast our names in thanks!'The High Elves of the Bright Company undertake the most dangerous missions required by the Great Alliance.Magoldir is at Echad-in-Edhil.Talk to MagoldirGlorfindel is at Echad-in-Edhil.Talk to GlorfindelLendelen is at Echad-in-Edhil.Talk to LendelenGandalf has interrupted the shade of Isildur's story-telling, bringing you back to the present situation on Tol Send.Listen to the tale told by the shade of IsildurThe shade of Isildur has been interrupted by Gandalf, and he is not happy about it.Talk to the shade of IsildurGandalf has interrupted the telling of Isildur's tale with a number of questions.Listen to the concerns of GandalfThe shade of Isildur is on Tol Send.Talk to the shade of IsildurGandalf is on Tol Send.Talk to Gandalf'Was this the last of the interruptions I must endure? I will continue my tale, if all agree to still their tongues!''This way, my friend. Glorfindel will be glad to meet you, and he will hear my report. Whether this strange inaction of Sauron's servants will be as pleasing, I cannot say. I myself am troubled by it, but I do not lead the Bright Company. Glorfindel will know more than either of us, I expect!''It is good to meet you, friend ${CLASS}, for in war all friendships must be prized. I am the captain of the Glánnothrim, the Bright Company, and we are tasked by High King Gil-galad to undertake some of the most dangerous missions on behalf of Elvenkind. You have not heard of us, I imagine? That is good, for none of us expect or desire glory. All we seek is the defeat of evil, wherever it lies. It lies now in Mordor.

'We are few in number, but that does not cause us to shrink from danger. Rather does it cause us to rely upon our fellows, and draw courage from their strength. Meet you now Lendelen, closest and wisest of my counsellors. Watch him closely, for he shares our purpose but possesses more wisdom than any Elf I have known! He is my trusted confidant, and a dear friend.''Glorfindel speaks glowingly of my wisdom, but he boasts overmuch. I seek only the destruction of evil, and take no praise for myself. If there is glory to be had, divide it among every member of the Bright Company and give no greater a portion to one Elf or another. Yes, at the light-house of Calatirion I spoke the words that shattered Sauron's control over the Vandassar perched atop the tower, but I could not have reached it without the others of the company.

'Give the praise to all or to none, dear Glorfindel. I think you seek to avoid your own share and give too much of it to others.''Why has the old man interrupted the telling of my tale? Does he desire to hear no more of it? It was only at your behest and his that I began to speak of this sorrowful time, and if he did not intend to let me finish it he should never have had me start!''I am not surprised you do not know of the Vandassari, for they were treasures brought from Númenor to Middle-earth by my father and brother, long ago. Seven only survived the realm's fall, but seven was enough, and indeed would prove to be too many. They were the Oath-stones, and they were set upon the borders of the kingdoms we founded, three in Arnor and four in Gondor, to make peace with the tribes that lived nearby.

'These were the Oath-stones of Arnor: Helcessar of Forochel, pale blue. Tauressar of Rhudaur, pale green. Ambossar of Cardolan, red-brown.

'These were the Oath-stones of Gondor: Landassar of Rhovanion, golden brown. Orossar of Erech, deep black. Airessar of Harad, clear crystal. Naldassar of Rhûn, white-silver.

'There was a power in them, but it was Sauron's malice that turned vows sworn upon an Oath-stone into bonds of enslavement. He searched for the Vandassari, attempting to weaken our alliances and subvert them for his own ends. He found Airessar, the crystal Oath-stone my brother Anárion set atop the lighthouse of Calatirion at the harbour of Vëalondë, and with his dark arts he used it to compel the service of the Haradrim.

'While we worked to assemble the Great Alliance, it was the Bright Company that journeyed to Umbar and broke Sauron's control over Airessar and the enslaved Haradrim. This ensured the Great Alliance need not contend with an army from the south when it reached Dagorlad, which might have proven insurmountable.''What brought they from the foundered land,' Gandalf murmurs quietly, 'over the flowing sea? Seven stars and seven stones and one white tree.'

The Wizard shakes his head, and fixes you with his stern eyes.

'I always believed the seven stones referred to by this Rhyme of Lore were the palantíri, the seeing-stones that Elendil brought from Númenor to these shores. Can it be that the palantíri were the stars of the rhyme, and the Vandassari were the stones? How can I not have known this, ${PLAYER}? What strange fog has clouded my knowledge on their account? And yet these Oath-stones have no place in my memory.'Vëamacil has suggested that you take a meal with his brothers before the sons of Isildur conduct their inspection of Díngarth's defences.Vëamacil is at Díngarth.Talk to VëamacilOrnaher is at Díngarth.Talk to OrnaherTárandil is at Díngarth.Talk to TárandilThe sons of Isildur wait for you to sit by their camp-fire and share a meal.The sons of Isildur are sharing a meal and conversation around a camp-fire at the encampment of Díngarth.Listen to the sons of Isildur discuss the war's progressTárandil has returned with the wine, but he is displeased to be the subject of his foster-brothers' gibes.Talk to TárandilVëamacil and Ornaher are at Díngarth, relating stories told by their eldest brother Meldaran, who remained at the Black Gate.Listen to the conversation of the sons of IsildurOrnaher is at Díngarth.Talk to OrnaherA Gondorian soldier has interrupted the merry-making to ask a concerning question of the sons of Isildur.Talk to the Gondorian soldierA soldier of Gondor has asked the sons of Isildur for tidings of the Ghâzab Bôron.Listen to the sons of Isildur discuss the Ghâzab Bôron, the Storm of WrathOrnaher is at Díngarth.Talk to Ornaher'Ah, it is probably time for us to begin the inspection. Let us see if Díngarth's defences remain secure.''My youngest brother, Valandil, was born in Rivendell, and he remained there with my mother when we returned to Gondor and the war. I have not seen him since we departed, and he was a lad of just four years. Why, he must be nearly eleven now! A pox on this everlasting siege! He will be a man grown by the time I see him again!

'The rest of my brothers have each seen battle during the long years of the siege, and they have acquitted themselves most admirably. Two of them have come with me to Díngarth, and will be happy to meet you. The third will still be at the Black Gate, I expect.''My brother told you I am to be called Ornaher? Very good, very good. He cannot always be trusted with serious matters, for he is prone to jests and flights of fancy, but it is good to find he has not endangered our well-being. Not on this day, at least.

'This siege has dragged on for so many years, it becomes difficult to remember what life was like before we came to be encamped within Mordor. Every semblance of a normal life fades, replaced with the deeds we accomplished in the war. Hearken to this: while our brothers fought at Dagorlad, Vëamacil and I marched to Imlad Ithil alongside other soldiers of Gondor and fought to drive the Orcs from that valley. And yet, if you ask me of my life before the war, I would find myself quite tongue-tied! Now there is only battle, and waiting.

'To speak true, there is more waiting now than battle. I do not have the stomach for sieges.''Ornaher says he cannot remember life before the siege? I know it was years ago, but that still seems a foolish thing to say; I cannot agree with him. Were he my brother by blood, perhaps it would be different, but it is not so. He and the other sons of Isildur are my foster-brothers, for the king took me to foster following our arrival on the shores of Middle-earth.

'But I did not come here to argue with them or with you. I came here to eat, and I suggest we commence that undertaking with no further delay!''They do not make merry at Meldaran's expense. It seems I am always to be the subject of their taunts. Were I their true brother...''Vëamacil exaggerates the story somewhat, but only somewhat... in its broad strokes, that is much as Meldaran conveyed it to the two of us. Likely it was some feat of Elf-magic that destroyed the gate. And has Tárandil not affirmed that was the way of it?''I beg you will take no offence, for I do not mean to disturb the noble ones, but the boys of my company were hoping to find out if they knew anything recent of the Ghâzab Bôron.

'Men such as they are can sometimes hear things sooner than the rest of us, is all. Do they know what has happened to the men and dwarves that went to face the Ghâzab Bôron?''I give you the advice I gave him: do not despair! If the Rhúnhoth that make up the Ghâzab Bôron were to be a threat to us, they would have arrived by now. The Longbeards and the Thíuda will defeat the Storm of Wrath, and return in victory. Their fighting prowess is well-known, and their foes cannot match them. It is not the fate of King Durin to be slain by Easterling warriors, and I daresay Huneric Hundred-slayer will need a new epithet once his victory against the horde is complete!

'Fear not. Word must soon arrive of the Storm's defeat. It will be any day now... perhaps in time for Tárandil's birthday!'The Orc encampment of Mokh Darga is a place of danger that has long troubled the Great Alliance.Mokh Darga can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should destroy cages and supplies at Mokh Darga.Destroy cages and supplies at Mokh DargaMokh Darga can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should destroy cages and supplies at Mokh Darga.Destroy cages at Mokh Darga (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy supplies at Mokh Darga (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroyed many cages and supplies at Mokh Darga.CompletedThe Orc encampment of Mokh Darga is full of cages which have been used to trap soldiers before sending them on to Amon Fuin or Barad-dûr for unspeakable rituals and torment. You should destroy the cages in the encampment as well as the supplies that keep it functioning.You destroyed many cages and supplies in the Orc encampment of Mokh Darga. Your efforts will not go unnoticed, not by the leaders of the Great Alliance nor also by the Dark Lord and his fearsome lieutenants.The Orc encampment of Mokh Darga is a place of danger that has long troubled the Great Alliance.Mokh Darga can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat enemies at Mokh Darga.Defeat enemies at Mokh DargaMokh Darga can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should defeat enemies at Mokh Darga.Defeat goblins at Mokh Darga (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orcs at Mokh Darga (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat trolls or gertheryg at Mokh Darga (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat many foes at Mokh Darga.CompletedThe Orc encampment of Mokh Darga is not well fortified, but this speaks all the louder of the ferocity of its inhabitants. You should defeat any foes you come across while in Mokh Darga, and quiet the whispers.You have defeated many foes at Mokh Darga and helped to quiet the hushed whispers that speak of its inhabitants' ferocity.Many Orcs and beasts under the command of Sauron gather at the encampment of Mokh Shapat.Orc barricades and supplies for battle can be found at Mokh Shapat in Arandor.

You should destroy supplies and Orc barricades at Mokh Shapat.Destroy supplies and Orc barricades at Mokh ShapatOrc barricades and supplies for battle can be found at Mokh Shapat in Arandor.

You should destroy supplies and Orc barricades at Mokh Shapat.Destroy Orc barricades (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy Orc supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroyed many barricades and supplies at Mokh Shapat.CompletedThe Orc encampment of Mokh Shapat is protected by crude barricades. Within, foes of the Great Alliance have stocked the camp with supplies and weapons that will be put to evil use. These barricades and provisions must be destroyed.You destroyed many barricades and supplies at the Orc encampment of Mokh Shapat, which will weaken their ability to do battle with the soldiers of the Great Alliance.Many Orcs and beasts under the command of Sauron gather at the encampment of Mokh Shapat.Mokh Shapat can be found in Arandor.

You should defeat the champion of Mokh Shapat and the encampment's raiders.Defeat enemies at Mokh ShapatMokh Shapat can be found in Arandor.

You should defeat the champion of Mokh Shapat and the encampment's raiders.Defeat the Champion of Mokh Shapat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Mokh Shapat's raiders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many foes at Mokh Shapat.CompletedThe bellowing voice of Mokh Shapat's champion echoes across the encampment as he orders the raiders under his command to descend upon Díngarth. Both raiders and champion must be stopped.The champion of Mokh Shapat has been silenced, and his raiders will no longer menace Díngarth or Adambel.King Amroth requires knowledge of enemy movements in order to plan his next attack.Orders might drop from many enemies found throughout Parth Daenath.

You should intercept Enemy orders for Amroth.Intercept Enemy orders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Amroth can be found at Echad-in-Edhil.

You should bring the Enemy orders to Amroth.'We know the broad strokes of our enemies' movements, but we need more detailed information about their plans. If you can find the orders sent between their camps, we might fashion a more complete picture of their upcoming strategies. Find such a thing and bring it to me.''So it is true! You have brought me just what I sought, and I thank you for doing so! Let us now organize our attacks to coincide with their movements. Perhaps we can sow confusion within their ranks, and improve the position of our own soldiers.'Habhûk is worried that Adambel may not be properly patrolled.Habhûk has asked you to patrol the wall surrounding Adambel.Habhûk can be found at Adambel.

You should talk to Habhûk.Talk to Habhûk'When we wait, our guard is down. Soldiers relax and forget they are in a great war. I fear that our eyes do not watch Adambel as well as they could, or as well as they should! Would you put me at ease and patrol the wall of Adambel? It should not take very long, and I would feel much better!''Nothing to report? I am relieved, my helpful friend! Oh, my mind does wander when I have nothing to do. I am glad that Adambel is safe. Let us keep it that way!'Heraldry of Sauron still waves above the Kingsland of Arandor.Heraldry of Sauron can be found in Arandor.

You should destroy any heraldry of Sauron that you find in Arandor.Destroy heraldry of Sauron in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroyed the heraldry of Sauron in Arandor.Completed'Have you seen the banners of the Dark Lord that still stand upon the Kingsland? Even one would be too many! Sauron thinks to intimidate our soldiers? Bah! Seek out the banners and remove them at once!'You destroyed the heraldry of Sauron that flew above Arandor, and Prince Eärtur will surely be pleased.Thranduil requires more knowledge of the Enemies' movements throughout the Corpse-fields of Parth Daenath.Thranduil has asked you to scout Parth Daenath.Thranduil can be found at Echad-in-Edhil.

You should talk to Thranduil.Talk to Thranduil'We have secured much of Parth Daenath, but Sauron's forces still grasp at the edge of the Corpse-fields. I require more men to patrol and scout Parth Daenath. Once you have done this, report back to me. We must make certain our presence is not forgotten if we are to win this war. Let us remind Sauron that he remains opposed, even after so many years of siege.''You did well in this task, but it was only a start. We are known to the enemy. Now we will take that reminder and turn it into a weapon of fear.'Eärtur, the Prince of Belfalas, has noted that the siege equipment in Adambel has fallen into disrepair from lack of use.Siege equipment in disrepair can be found at Adambel.

You should repair the siege equipment.Repair siege equipment at Adambel (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Eärtur can be found at Adambel.

You should talk to Eärtur.Talk to Eärtur'It has been a long while since the Enemy attacked us directly at Adambel, and the siege equipment has fallen into disrepair. If you are able to do so, inspect the siege equipment here at the encampment and see if any of it is salvageable.''The siege equipment is in working order again? These are happy tidings! It is to be hoped we do not have need of them, but in the event we do we will have you to thank for this repair. What further tasks might be solved by sending you in their direction, my capable friend? Find something and do it, for the Great Alliance is always in need of assistance, as you well know.'Habhûk does not like to remain idle for too long. The lull in battle has him on edge.Fire pits can be found at Adambel.

You should stoke the fires.Stoke the fires (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Soldiers can be found at Adambel.

You should talk with the soldiers to pass the time.Habhûk can be found at Adambel.

You should talk to Habhûk.Talk to Habhûk'We wait for battle, and the wait is long. It seems longer still when the air is quiet and nothing moves! I must have patience, but I am not the only one who is restless. Look around you, and you will see many others grow weary of this endless waiting!

'Let us brighten their spirits and take the stillness from their minds. How, you ask? Why, stoke the fires and share some stories. It will make the wait feel shorter.''For the first time in many days, I heard the low rumble of pleasant talk! There were tales of battles gone, happy memories of children, wives, and husbands waiting at home, loved ones all. Good memories were shared. Did you hear the same?

'We have lasted this long, friend. One day this siege will end, but if it is not to be today, we will not let the passage of another restless day drive us to madness, and give the lie to those already spent. We will wait. One day we will win, and this waiting will be worth it.'Terna has devised a plan to trap enemy forces within their own encampments.Enemy encampments can be found throughout Emyn Duir.

You should hide traps at these encampments to confuse the enemy forces within.Set many traps in encampments throughout Emyn Duir.Completed'I have scouted the Emyn Duir, and I have decided if we are to remain in secret here at Halroth, we must do something about the enemy forces that inhabit the lands nearby. I thought we might set traps around the mountain passes near Halroth, but I fear that might alert these very foes to our presence!

'And what happened then? Why, I had a brilliant thought! What about laying traps within their own encampments, all in secret? This would certainly confuse them, and they would not even think to look for us here. I have already made the traps. Would you be willing to sneak into several enemy encampments in order to set them in place? I am sure you would enjoy it!'You have set all of Terna's traps in the Emyn Duir encampments. It would be best not to return to Halroth unless absolutely necessary, for if you were followed you might endanger the secrecy of the mission. But you know Terna would be satisfied by the thought of enemies stumbling into her traps within their own camps, and you can't help but smile.The Dead swarm in great numbers throughout the sunken ravine of Angathrai.Angathrai can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should defeat foes at Angathrai.Defeat foes at AngathraiAngathrai can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should defeat foes at Angathrai.Defeat shades at Angathrai (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat fell summoners at Angathrai (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many foes at Angathrai.CompletedBeneath the tower of Barad-dûr, a ravine of death and decay simmers and stagnates. There, the Dead swarm in great numbers throughout the pools of Angathrai. Fell summoners use cursed runes to call forth the Dead to do their bidding. You should defeat the creatures that wander among the pools of Angathrai.You defeated many foes at Angathrai and halted the summoning of fell spirits. Your efforts upon the Shadowfells will not go unnoticed, and must surely hinder the plans of Sauron.The Dead swarm in great numbers throughout the sunken ravine of Angathrai.Angathrai can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should destroy cursed runes at Angathrai.Destroy cursed runes at AngathraiAngathrai can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should destroy cursed runes at Angathrai.Destroy cursed runes at Angathrai (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy many cursed runes at Angathrai.CompletedBelow the tower of Barad-dûr, a ravine of death and decay simmers and stagnates. There, the Dead swarm in great numbers throughout the pools of Angathrai. Cursed runes and shrines line the foetid ravine and should be destroyed.You destroyed many of the cursed runes at Angathrai. Those who must venture into the ravine will be grateful for your efforts here.Radigond, daughter of Huneric Hundred-slayer, has been tasked with maintaining Díngarth's weapon stores.Blunt swords can be found in Díngarth.

You should gather blunt swords.Gather blunt swords (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A grindstone can be found in Díngarth.

You should sharpen the swords on the grindstone.Radigond can be found in Díngarth.

You should give the sharpened swords to Radigond.'Do you know aught about maintaining weapons? Do not lie to me! If you do not know of such things, simply say so! It is not as easy as you might think, unless you have given it study! Ah, you do know the art? Why did you not simply say so?

'My father the Hundred-slayer taught me everything there is to know about keeping swords balanced and sharp, and those are both traits that our Alliance will need in the days to come! Take some of the blunt swords in Díngarth and give them a sharpen on the grindstone. Show me what you know about maintaining weapons.''Let me see those weapons. Hmm...

'Yes, these are not bad at all. You show promise, and lack only the practice. Oh, it is not an insult! Not even I have mastered the art, and I have spent all the days of my life training in it with my father!

'There are many more weapons that need inspection and maintenance, but these will see use soon, for the Storm of Wrath is coming. Perhaps these blades will be sharp enough to give that horde something to think upon.'A bounty notice asks you to quell the fell spirits around Emyn Duir.Fell spirits can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should defeat Mangath and other fell spirits that roam Emyn Duir.Defeat fell spirits in Emyn Duir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Mangath in Emyn DuirDefeated fell spirits.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'Have you noticed the dangerous spirits that wander these lands, particularly at night? Of course you have. A wise soldier would not tangle with these corrupted ones... unless he was commanded to do so for the good of the Great Alliance, or if there was coin in it. Well, today is a fortunate day for you, for both are true!

'These spirits would be dangerous enough, but they are led by a particularly ruthless spirit the boys name Mangath. Put an end to Mangath and the spirits he commands, and you will be rewarded.'You defeated Mangath and the spirits he commanded, helping to make safe the lands held by the Great Alliance. The one who posted the bounty will be pleased.A bounty notice has asked you to drive the wights out of Emyn Duir.Wights can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should defeat wights.Defeat wights in Emyn Duir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat banner-wights in Emyn Duir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You defeated wights.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'These lands have no shortage of foul creatures, but if you ask me the wight is the worst, bedraggled and disgusting. They are beasts of horror, and I hesitate to call them something so common as pests, but they harass our gravesites and bother our dead. It turns my stomach to think of them!

'If you wish to earn some coin and have the time to spare, I implore you to hunt as many of the vile creatures as you can. I am sick of the things!'You have defeated many wights in Emyn Duir, and the one who posted the bounty is certain to be pleased.A bounty notice asks you to clear wraiths from around Emyn Duir.Wraiths can be found in Emyn Duir.

You should defeat wraiths, vengeful and otherwise, that plague the land.Defeat vengeful wraiths in Emyn Duir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated wraiths.CompletedThe bounty notice reads:

'Shivers and screams in the night! A terrible, lingering presence! Cold, chilling wind, blowing suddenly from nowhere! These are only some of the stories my men have told to me, trying to convince me that these lands are haunted. It begins to interfere with their patrols, which means it must stop, and now!

'Put an end to the wraiths, and you will put an end to the stories, and earn some coin beside.'You have defeated wraiths around Emyn Duir hopefully putting an end to the haunting of the land. Whether this will end the stories that so bothered the man who posted the bounty remains to be seen.Glorfindel of the Bright Company has collected a number of scouting reports from his Elves in the field, and wishes for you to investigate what they might signify.Glorfindel asked you to deliver the scouting reports to either Isildur or Anárion at the Gondorian encampment of Adambel.Anárion asked you to bring his message to soldiers in the field east of Adambel, nearer to the Dark Tower. He also charged you with defeating servants of Sauron and the beasts that stalk among the crags of Mordor.Bring Anárion's message to MartoreBring Anárion's message to TumbomehtarBring Anárion's message to AstondorDefeat beasts and servants of Sauron in Mordor Besieged (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have done as Anárion asked, and should return to him at Adambel.Return to Anárion at AdambelAnárion has been summoned to an urgent council and is unavailable. Isildur's foster-son Tárandil has called you over and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Tárandil at AdambelVëamacil is at Díngarth.

Tárandil told you that his brothers may have found some clue to the mystery of the missing soldiers.Talk to Vëamacil at Díngarth'It is a mystery to me, and to Gil-galad, and to Lendelen my closest advisor. Rare is the puzzle we three cannot solve, ${CLASS}, and that by itself is worrisome.

'Magoldir is not the only scout who has reported stillness on the plateau of Gorgoroth. I possess a number of such reports now, and each tells a similar tale: fewer enemy patrols than before, and a reduction in the number of foes assaulting the encampments of Men or Elves. I will inform Gil-galad of these reports, but I ask that you bring word to the leaders of Gondor. I understand Elendil is in council, and I would not have him disturbed, but if you bring these reports to one of his sons they will deliver word to him when he is free.

'Seek out Isildur or Anárion at Adambel. I am troubled by these reports, but cannot say precisely why. Perhaps the sons of Elendil can pinpoint the reason behind my worry, and speak such words as will abate it.''Seek out Isildur or Anárion at Adambel. I am troubled by these reports, but cannot say precisely why. Perhaps the sons of Elendil can pinpoint the reason behind my worry, and speak such words as will abate it.''Yes? How came a ${RACE} of Gondor to be in possession of reports from Glorfindel of the Elves? Have you not your own duties to perform, your own captain to follow?'

Anárion's stern visage suddenly melts away, and his expression becomes merry.

'Nay, friend, I mean rather that you might serve as an example to some who might forget: only together can we defeat the will of Sauron. I see frustration in the faces of my men; how could I not, after seven years of siege? But your coming today is a reminder that, unlike Sauron, we are not alone. We stand together against him: Men, Elves, dwarves. Aye, all the peoples of Middle-earth have joined hands to oppose his evil. This endless waiting cannot last, no matter how certain it seems it must. Tell me, ${CLASS}, what word you bring from Glorfindel of the Elves.'

You relay the reports of Glorfindel's scouts, and Anárion listens to your words with increasing excitement.

'I too have noticed fewer attacks of late, but I thought little of it. Now I wonder. Can it be that Sauron's resolve has been tested by the long years of siege? His allies are not allies at all, but servants only! His domination of their spirit can be no substitute for the bonds of friendship formed in battle, like those we formed with the Elves and the others of our Alliance. I draw hope from these reports, and see in them a sign that our victory must soon be at hand!

'There are soldiers in the field of battle east of Adambel, nearer to the Dark Tower. Find them and relay to them this message: "Sauron nears the end of his resolve, and a renewed assault may break the morale of his defenders!" I ask also that you slay whatever servants of Sauron and vile beasts you find on your way. Let every success sing the tale of the victory that surely approaches! It will not be long now, friend!''There are soldiers in the field of battle east of Adambel, nearer to the Dark Tower. Find them and relay to them this message: "Sauron nears the end of his resolve, and a renewed assault may break the morale of his defenders!" I ask also that you slay whatever servants of Sauron and vile beasts you find on your way. Let every success sing the tale of the victory that surely approaches! It will not be long now, friend!''You say you bring a message from King Anárion? Deliver it, friend, for I am his to command!'

You tell Martore that Anárion believes Sauron nears the end of his resolve, and a renewed assault on the part of the Great Alliance may bring an end to the war.

'It is my heart's wish that the king guesses aright, but this is not the first day I have heard this hope given voice. I think it more likely that Sauron prepares some new weapon to unleash upon us. Still, I will not abandon my duty. I will fight on!

'For Anárion and for Isildur! For Gondor!''What message brings you to this blasted plain, friend? Good tidings or ill?'

You tell Tumbomehtar that Anárion believes the war may soon be ended if the soldiers of the Alliance remain steadfast. He grimaces.

'So neither good nor ill, then, for I hear nothing new in the king's words. He has my love and my respect, for my loyalty is to both of Elendil's sons and the kingdom they made on these shores, but I have seen no sign the end of the siege is any nearer now than it was these past few years. King Anárion is a good man, but I see in him the desire of all soldiers to welcome the onset of peace at the slimmest of portents. It fell to him to defend the borders of Gondor while his brother travelled north to gather the Great Alliance; Anárion has been fighting this war longer than many of us, and he must feel the weariness of it as keenly as any soldier of Gondor.

'I hope he is right that it will soon be ended, but I will lay no wager upon it.'You came too late to deliver Anárion's hopeful message, for Astondor has been slain.'My foster-father and King Anárion have been summoned to an urgent council, and they would not tell me the reason. It may be nothing, or it may not. But that is not the matter that brought me here. I came here to find you, and bring you back with me!

'Do you remember what my brothers and I discussed with you when we met at Díngarth? A curious number of soldiers had vanished from their posts in recent weeks, not simply while on patrol but also from within the safety of our own encampments. Deserters, Ornaher thought, but the rest of us were not so sure. Well, now we have discovered evidence that these vanished soldiers might not have succumbed to cowardice at all!

'Let us go to Díngarth at once to meet with Vëamacil, and you will hear what he told me. It may be that together we can solve this mystery, and if we act quickly we may save some lives as well!''Let us go to Díngarth at once to meet with Vëamacil, and you will hear what he told me. It may be that together we can solve this mystery, and if we act quickly we may save some lives as well!''Welcome back, my friend! I trust Tárandil told you we might be near to solving the mystery of our vanished soldiers? It is true. How can I say these were no mere deserters?'

Vëamacil raises an eyebrow, barely able to mask a grin.

'We have an onlooker, ${CLASS}, someone who could spy upon the latest disappearance without being seen. Someone who was beneath notice: one of the Hól-budlan. She saw everything.'Tûka, matriarch of the Hól-budlan, has made a discovery concerning the missing soldiers, and the sons of Isildur want your help to track down a solution to the mystery.Tûka, leader of the Hól-budlan, may have some clue as to what befell the missing soldiers. She can be found in Díngarth.Talk to Tûka, leader of the Hól-budlan, on the outskirts of DíngarthIsildur's son Ornaher can be found in Díngarth with the Hól-budlan matriarch Tûka.Talk to Ornaher at DíngarthIsildur's son Vëamacil can be found at Díngarth.Talk to Vëamacil at Díngarth'Who is this secret onlooker? Her name is Tûka, and she is the matriarch of the Hól-budlan. Her folk are known for their skill at the art of stealth, and in this case she has seen something of great interest to us and to the Alliance! Find her here in Díngarth and hear her story complete. She told me but a portion of it, and I tell you it was enough for me to know we need hear it all!''Find Tûka in Díngarth and hear her story complete!''You are friend to the sons of Isildur? I will tell you what I told them, and may the information be of some use to you!

'My people are often underestimated by the Big Folk, and for that reason we can go where they might not, and remain below the notice of unobservant eyes. It is a useful skill, and one we have put to use in the fight against Sauron and his armies. Let no one say the Hól-budlan have not lived up to their word, or carried their share of the war's burden!

'I was walking in the night air, looking forward to the comfort of sleep, when I heard the voice of a man of Gondor. His words were low and indistinct; it took me some time to find the speaker. He was stretched out upon his bedroll, like many other soldiers sleeping nearby, but unlike these others he muttered in his sleep. He sat up all of a sudden, giving me quite a fright, and I greeted him but he saw me not.

'Judging from his speech, he thought he heard a woman's voice he recognized: that of a sweetheart from home, seemingly. He walked out of the camp, stepping over the bodies of his sleeping fellows. By now my curiosity was well and truly wakened, and I followed him at a safe distance. Outside the camp, far from the guards or the light of the torches, he was attacked and subdued by a pair of Mordorrim! They were well-armed with dangerous-looking knives; I dared not risk making myself known, so I could not rescue him, but I did follow them for a time. They got too near to Amon Fuin and I had to give up the pursuit, but I knew I needed to tell someone what I saw. These Mordorrim used some trickery to lure him from the safety of the camp; who can say what evils awaited him within Amon Fuin?

'That is all I know. But Amon Fuin is well-defended, and even the sons of Isildur should beware of that place!''I have told you what I know. But Amon Fuin is well-defended, and even the sons of Isildur should beware of that place!''Tûka's words make it clear: the missing soldiers are not cowardly deserters. Nay, they were ensorcelled by the cultists of Sauron we call the Mordorrim, and brought to Amon Fuin, the Gloom-hill, for some fell purpose. They may still be alive! My father is in council, and there is no one else to attempt a rescue! You must help me and my brothers save these missing men!'

Ornaher pauses, his expression conflicted.

'I must tell you something, friend, and I beg you to keep it in confidence. It is this: we should not ask my foster-brother Tárandil to accompany us on this errand. Like my older brother Meldaran and my cousin Meneldil, Tárandil was born in Númenor, where he was given the birth-name Adûnzagar. His father... his real father, mind you... was a cruel man named Ûrizagar, the Sword of the Sun. He was a captain-general of the fleet, and he worshipped at Sauron's altar. He wished for his son Adûnzagar to follow him on his evil path, but his wife wanted only happiness for her son; Abrazâni she was called, and she was a friend in childhood to Isildur and Anárion. She pleaded for them to take her son with them on the ships, and escape that doom. But Abrazâni was betrayed by the servants of Ûrizagar, and when the great wave sank Númenor beneath the Sea, Abrazâni perished.

'But in this, at least, let the wife of cruel Ûrizagar find happiness. For her son Adûnzagar escaped on the ships to Middle-earth, and when they arrived safely on these shores, my father took him as his ward. To protect the boy from the suspicions of good folk and Sauron's servants alike, he set aside Adûnzagar's birth-name. Henceforth he was called Tárandil, foster-son to the king, and for all my life I knew him as an older brother. He has my love, and always will.'

Ornaher looks away, sorrow etched upon his face. He drags his booted toe in the dirt. When he faces you again his expression has hardened.

'He is my brother in all things, friend, save blood. It is not Isildur's blood that runs through his veins, but that of Ûrizagar. His father worshipped at the altar of Sauron. What if that weakness lies within Tárandil, even today? These Mordorrim are cultists of Sauron, and I fear my foster-brother might succumb to their whispers, their temptations, where the rest of us would not.

'Keep this in confidence, and tell Tárandil none of this. But return to Vëamacil with Tûka's wisdom, and my own. We must rescue these soldiers of Gondor, but if the Mordorrim are involved, we should not involve my foster-brother in this adventure.''Keep this in confidence, and tell Tárandil none of this. But return to Vëamacil with Tûka's wisdom, and my own. We must rescue these soldiers of Gondor, but if the Mordorrim are involved, we should not involve my foster-brother in this adventure.''You see? It is as I thought all along! Soldiers of Gondor have been ensorcelled by the Mordorrim, and it is up to us to save them, if we can. You and I will do this, with Ornaher at our side. We will leave Tárandil behind; there is no need to wake him, not so near to his birthday.

'What's that? Tûka thinks it would be foolhardy for us to brave Amon Fuin? Well, I thank her for her stealth, but her wisdom she can keep. You need not fear to go with us! Ornaher and I are of Isildur's blood-line, friend, and we will protect you.'Stirred to action by the discovery that the missing soldiers were abducted by Mordorrim cultists, Vëamacil and Ornaher are eager to attempt a dangerous rescue in the heart of Amon Fuin.Isildur's son Vëamacil is at Díngarth.Talk to Vëamacil and complete 'Instance: All Is Quiet'Isildur's foster-son Tárandil is at Ram Raedas.Talk to Tárandil at Ram RaedasIsildur's son Meldaran has come to Ram Raedas with worrying tidings.Talk to Meldaran at Ram Raedas'Make your preparations and tell me when you are ready to depart. Outside of Barad-dûr, the Gloom-hill of Amon Fuin is one of the last places still held by Sauron. We must not fear it, but we should be prepared for anything!''You are ready, as I am? Then let us be off, and quietly. We can be as loud as we like when we return victorious, but for departure silence is our ally.''Where have you been? I have been looking everywhere for you! It is quite unlike my brothers to disappear without saying a word to me... I feared some evil had come for them!'

Tárandil shakes his head.

'I should choose my words more carefully. The evil comes not just for them, but for all of us. My eldest brother has ridden from the gates of Mordor with fell tidings. To protect his kinship with the kings during the war, he has taken the name Meldaran, but I fear the safety that has given him might be soon exhausted. Find him at Ram Raedas and learn what has happened.

'Did we hope the war would soon be ended? What fools we were! I think the end does come, but not in the form we wished. Alas for the Great Alliance!''My eldest brother Meldaran has ridden from the gates of Mordor with fell tidings. Find him at Ram Raedas and learn what has happened, if you have the heart for it.''You are friend to my brothers? You are friend then to me, though we have no time to spend in greeting. I rode hard from the gate, but my errand is not yet finished. Need drives me, and will not let me rest, for I must bring my report to my father the king.

'This is my message: the Longbeards and the Thíuda of the north that stood against the Rhúnhoth have suffered a crushing defeat. The Easterling horde was greater by far than we thought; our allies have been utterly defeated. They were to be our shield against this second army, but now the shield has fallen, and we lie exposed to the Storm of Wrath. The Ghâzab Bôron is on its way.'The Longbeard and Thíuda armies that engaged with the Ghâzab Bôron, the Rhúnhoth Storm of Wrath, have been utterly defeated, and now nothing stands between the flank of the Great Alliance and the onrushing horde.Isildur's son Ornaher is at Díngarth.Talk to Ornaher at DíngarthFearful soldiers can be found at Díngarth, despairing at the news of the approaching Rhúnhoth horde.Speak inspiring words to fearful soldiers at Ram Raedas (0/8)Words are no more use to the despairing soldiers of the Great Alliance. Better now to collect the weapons they cast aside with the hope that they will still be of use to those who remain to fight.Collect abandoned weapons at Díngarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered many weapons cast aside by despairing soldiers at Díngarth, and should bring them to Ornaher for distribution to those who may still wield them.Frightened Men and Elves can be found at Díngarth.

Ornaher believes tales of Elendil's sword Narsil and Gil-galad's spear Aiglos may inspire the frightened Men and Elves to stand against the Ghâzab Bôron.Remind Men of the Alliance about Narsil, the sword of Elendil (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Remind Elves of the Alliance about Aiglos, the spear of Gil-galad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tárandil is at Díngarth, and he desires to speak with you.Talk to Tárandil at Díngarth'I was at the Morannon when the wounded began to arrive: weary Men and dwarves, bloody and battered, the tale of their defeat writ large upon mournful faces. I saw King Durin from afar, but even from such a distance I could see his face was ashen, and the fire of battle had gone out of him. I spoke with Huneric Hundred-slayer, lord of the Thíuda, and my heart wept to hear his account of the battle on the field of Khôn-khaigu.

'"We had so many victories!" he said to me, "But it was a deception. We were drawn forward by the promise of those successes, and only when we thought the end was near did Sauron's commanders reveal the true size of the Easterling horde. We fought valiantly, Man and dwarf alike... but we could not fight for long against so many. Any who survived the massacre fled, and I fled with them. Those who could not were captured, cut to pieces or burned on altars to Sauron. A few score can move faster than a horde, and we outpaced the Rhúnhoth, but still they come. They will be here before long."

'So said Huneric to me, and I must bring word to my father. Tell my brothers I will see them after I deliver my message.''I must bring word of the Ghâzab Bôron to my father. Tell my brothers I will see them after I deliver my message.''Sauron has bested us! This endless waiting did not signify a beaten enemy at all! He waited for the Ghâzab Bôron to swell to such size as it could not be opposed, and now we will be crushed between the Rhúnhoth and the Dark Tower!

'We cannot despair. We must not. Already I see men casting down their weapons! We must stop them. Quickly, tell the men it is not over! Tell them we must still fight!''We cannot despair. We must not. Already I see men casting down their weapons! We must stop them. Quickly, tell the men it is not over! Tell them we must still fight!''It is no use. Words will no longer avail us! Better to collect the weapons they have cast aside, for those at least may still be of some use to those soldiers who remain to fight!''I will see these weapons go to those who remain. Those soldiers with the heart to fight may still make use of them.

'But wait, friend. As I look upon these weapons, I am filled with newfound courage. These swords have seen battle, even during the long, slow years of our siege. Their tale is one of standing strong, though today their owners do not. But there are weapons in our Alliance of whom the minstrels sing: Narsil, the blade of my grandfather Elendil, and Aiglos, the spear of Gil-galad. The tale of these weapons is long, and will be longer still! They will not be cast aside. Nay, they will be wielded to the last, and if their song is to end in battle with the Ghâzab Bôron, is that not a glorious end?

'Go now, ${CLASS}, and speak of Narsil and of Aiglos to the frightened Men and Elves who have forgotten the vows they made when they joined the Great Alliance. Will they join their own tales to those of proud Elendil and noble Gil-galad? Will they wield their own weapons alongside Narsil, sharp and deadly, and alongside Aiglos, bright and cold? Tell them, my friend. This may be the end, but let it be an end that shall be worthy of song!''Go now, my friend, and speak of Narsil and of Aiglos to the frightened Men and Elves who have forgotten the vows they made when they joined the Great Alliance. Will they join their own tales to those of proud Elendil and noble Gil-galad? Will they wield their own weapons alongside Narsil, sharp and deadly, and alongside Aiglos, bright and cold? Tell them! This may be the end, but let it be an end that shall be worthy of song!''I have learned there is to be a council of the kings, at which they will discuss what can be done about the Ghâzab Bôron. My brothers will likely be permitted to attend, but I will not.

'You asked what I wanted for my birthday, and I told you I wanted nothing. But now I know what I want for my birthday present. I want this. I want to attend the council.'Tárandil wants to attend the council of the kings, but he fears it will not be permitted without some evidence that he is deserving.Patrol leaders, and others who intend to lead such excursions, can be found throughout Parth Daenath. Mordorrim hopefuls gather west of the entrance to Amon Fuin, and hopeful Orcs can be found in and around Mokh Darga. Look for Easterling hopefuls south of the road halfway between Mokh Darga and Barthost.

Tárandil believes that proof of your value to the Great Alliance will gain the two of you entrance to the council of the kings, and he wants you to collect badges of rank belonging to hopeful patrol leaders as proof of that value.Collect a badge from a hopeful Orc patrol-leaderCollect a badge from a Mordorrim hopefulCollect a badge from an Easterling hopefulTárandil is at Adambel, hoping to attend the council of the kings.Tárandil is at Adambel, hoping to attend the council of the kings.Talk to Tárandil and complete 'Instance: The Council of Kings'Vëamacil is at Adambel, dismayed by the result of the council of the kings.Talk to Vëamacil at AdambelHigh King Elendil is at Adambel, and has asked to speak with you.Talk to Elendil at Adambel'Isildur generally treats me the same as he treats my foster-brothers, but there are limits to his even-handedness. I fear this will be one such case, for there are still many who consider me no true son to the king. I too... there are times I am reminded that he is not truly my own father, and I wish he would listen to my words, pay heed to the advice I give... but he does not.

'Do not misunderstand me! I consider Isildur my true father, for he raised me as his own and I would have it no other way. There is a reason I count my years from the day I was fostered by the king and not from the day of my birth! But there is a sadness about my early years and you will understand if I do not wish to speak of it to you.'

Tárandil falls silent, pondering solutions.

'Yes, I think I have it! Perhaps we can prove we both deserve to attend this council, my friend. There are those in the Dark Lord's service hopeful of attaining glory, and they seek the honour of leading their own patrols. Mordorrim hopefuls gather west of the entrance to Amon Fuin, and hopeful Orcs can be found in and around Mokh Darga. Look for Easterling hopefuls south of the road halfway between Mokh Darga and Barthost. Let us each find some of these hopeful leaders and seize their badges of rank. If we present these trophies we may prove ourselves deserving of attendance at the council!''Perhaps we can prove we both deserve to attend this council, my friend. There are those in the Dark Lord's service hopeful of attaining glory, and they seek the honour of leading their own patrols. Mordorrim hopefuls gather west of the entrance to Amon Fuin, and hopeful Orcs can be found in and around Mokh Darga. Look for Easterling hopefuls south of the road halfway between Mokh Darga and Barthost. Let us each find some of these hopeful leaders and seize their badges of rank. If we present these trophies we may prove ourselves deserving of attendance at the council!''I am impressed! You managed to acquire three badges? I only obtained one! Will you allow us to combine our trove? Four is better than both three or one, friend. And it is my birthday, after all.

'Let me know if you are ready, and we will see if we can attend the council.''I hope this works. It must!''I cannot believe you get to go on the daring mission and I do not! You are just a ${CLASS}, and I am the son of a king!

'I am sorry, I did not mean that. But you must see that it is unjust! My brothers and I are not afraid! All of us save Tárandil volunteered to accompany the Bright Company, and yet we must remain behind with him, as if his cowardice was our own! If we are going to die when the Ghâzab Bôron arrives, I would much rather die fighting on the offensive within the Dark Tower than back on my heels on this blasted plain!

'If it is a jest, it is a poor one! I cannot believe my father would treat us so. It is not your fault, friend, it is his. I apologize for my words, but my blood is hot. My grandfather wishes to speak with you. Perhaps when you go to him you will ask him to reverse my father's decision and allow my brothers and I to join with the Bright Company on this mission?''Go to my grandfather, then. I have nothing more to say to you.''I see the weariness in you, though you seek to hide it. Let us speak about the task to come, and why you were selected to join with the Bright Company on this mission, and my grandsons were not.'Lendelen of the Bright Company has suggested a dangerous mission to overcome Sauron's domination of the Ghâzab Bôron, and a number of preparations must be made before it can be attempted.Tûka is outside the tunnel of Halroth, which the Bright Company hopes to use to infiltrate Barad-dûr.Find Tûka outside the entrance to the tunnel of HalrothTûka is outside the entrance of Halroth, a tunnel carved in the rocks of Mordor.Talk to Tûka outside the entrance to HalrothDefeat enemies in the vicinity of the entrance to the tunnel of Halroth, in Emyn Duir.

Tûka thinks you should defeat watchful eyes near the entrance to Halroth, not too many and not too few.Defeat just the right number of enemies outside Halroth, in Emyn DuirDefeat enemies in the vicinity of the entrance to the tunnel of Halroth, in Emyn Duir.

Tûka thinks you should defeat watchful eyes near the entrance to Halroth, not too many and not too few.Defeat just the right number of enemies outside Halroth, in Emyn DuirTûka is outside the entrance of Halroth, a tunnel carved in the rocks of Mordor.Talk to Tûka outside the entrance to HalrothVëamacil is at Adambel, and he wishes to speak with you.Talk to Vëamacil at AdambelVëamacil is at Adambel, and he wishes to speak with you.Talk to Vëamacil and complete 'Instance: A Bad Birthday'The shade of Isildur is on Tol Send, waiting to tell you more of the tale of the Great Alliance.Talk to the shade of Isildur on Tol SendGlorfindel is on Tol Send.

Glorfindel has interrupted the shade of Isildur in his telling of the tale, and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Glorfindel on Tol SendGandalf is on Tol Send. He has listened with interest to the tale told by the shade of Isildur, but does not understand the need for Glorfindel's interruption.Talk to Gandalf on Tol SendGlorfindel is on Tol Send, and he has volunteered to complete the tale begun by the shade of Isildur, despite his misgivings.Talk to Glorfindel on Tol Send'Our Alliance is composed of the mightiest heroes of the age, ${CLASS}, and together we have achieved many victories. But here, after seven years of siege, our triumphs are mere memory. Sauron has outmaneuvered us. If we cannot overcome his domination of the Ghâzab Bôron, it will not matter how many great heroes stand with us. Our defeat will be certain, and it will be forever.

'If Lendelen is trustworthy, as I am told he is, there are few only who can break the spells enwreathing the Oath-stone. They must reach the chamber in which it lies, though it be kept within Barad-dûr itself. Their purpose must be hidden from Sauron, for if he learns what they seek to do he will turn his every thought upon preventing it. If he sees a ${CLASS} among the most powerful High Elves that walk in Middle-earth, he may hesitate. "What purpose do you serve?" he may wonder, and in that briefest of delays the mission of the Bright Company might be accomplished. It is a hard thing to ask, ${CLASS}, but we must seek every advantage we can. It is likely you will not return.

'But there are still preparations to be made before the Bright Company departs. You should find the Hól-budlan matriarch Tûka, somewhere to the east of our encampments, for she intended to scout the entrance to the tunnel discovered by her people.''But there are still preparations to be made before the Bright Company departs. You should find the Hól-budlan matriarch Tûka, somewhere to the east of our encampments, for she intended to scout the entrance to the tunnel upon which our hope rests.''This way... and be as quiet as you can.''But there are still preparations to be made before the Bright Company departs. You should find the Hól-budlan matriarch Tûka, somewhere to the east of our encampments, for she intended to scout the entrance to the tunnel upon which our hope rests.''Over here... as quiet as you can!

'This is the tunnel about which I spoke at the council. Some of my people followed it a goodly way, and it seemed to them that it did curve in the direction of the Dark Tower. We all agree that it must empty into the dungeons, and if the Elves are as powerful as they say they will be able to make it at least as far as the tower. Beyond that, well...'

Tûka shudders.

'There are a lot of eyes out here. We should thin out some of the foes this near to Halroth, but not so many as to be suspicious. You had better get to it!''There are a lot of eyes out here. We should thin out some of the foes this near to Halroth, but not so many as to be suspicious. You had better get to it!''Well done! You defeated just the right number, I would say: not too many nor too few. The passage of the Bright Company should be unremarked upon, which is better than we might have hoped!

'There is nothing else you need help me with at Halroth. You may as well return to Adambel, and relay the news that the tunnel entrance is less-watched than it might have been.''There is nothing else you need help me with at Halroth. You may as well return to Adambel, and relay the news that the tunnel entrance is less-watched than it might have been.''I am glad you have returned, for I... I wish to apologize to you for my words earlier. I repented of them not long after you left, and would have them unsaid. Alas, that is beyond my power. Still, I beg you will forgive me. The war must soon end, one way or another, and I would not have us part with such a cloud of ill feeling between us.

'My brothers and I plan to take a last meal together at Díngarth, for it seems clear the mission of the Bright Company is the final hope of the Great Alliance, and we may not have another opportunity. I hope that you will join us, my friend. Let me know if you will.''Thank you, my friend. Let us go together to Díngarth. It is Tárandil's birthday today, and we are celebrating as best we can.''The sun dawned sickly on the day the mission of the Bright Company was to take place. They gathered at Echad-in-Edhil and prepared to depart for the secret way of Halroth.''Glorfindel of the Elves desires to speak? If he wishes to continue the tale he may do so, though he seems ill at ease.''It is my turn to take up the tale, ${PLAYER}. Well do I remember the glory of the Bright Company, but if there was valour so too was there tragedy. I hoped it would not be necessary to recall that dark day, but I see now it cannot be avoided.'

Glorfindel turns to address Gandalf, sorrow etched on his face and regret in his voice.

'I beg your forgiveness, Olórin, Mithrandir, Gandalf. I wished it were not necessary to speak of it, but speak of it I will, if there is no other way.''I hoped it would not be necessary to recall that dark day, but I see now it cannot be avoided.''I do not understand Glorfindel's reluctance, ${PLAYER}. I have studied the records of the Siege of Barad-dûr and understand well the price of victory over Sauron. I know too that the victory did not last, and it would be many thousands of years before Sauron's ultimate defeat.

'There is nothing Glorfindel need keep from me, and his belief that I will be disturbed by this tale of the Bright Company is most puzzling to me. I say he should speak on, and delay no further.''I say Glorfindel should speak on, and delay no further.''Very well. I will conclude the tale, despite my misgivings.''I think you've almost got it! But not quite!'Glorfindel has volunteered to finish the tale begun by the shade of Isildur, for he fought alongside the Bright Company and remembers both the triumph and the sorrow they encountered within the Dark Tower.Glorfindel is on Tol Send. He has agreed to finish the tale begun by the shade of Isildur, despite his misgivings.Talk to Glorfindel on Tol Send and complete 'Instance: The Glory of the Bright Company'Glorfindel is on Tol Send.Talk to Glorfindel on Tol SendGandalf is on Tol Send.Talk to Gandalf on Tol Send'Will you hear me, then?''Listen now to the last glory of the Bright Company, and may it prove worth the sorrow I foresee in its telling.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'And so it is done, ${PLAYER}. When I beheld Olórin in a new guise in this Third Age, I knew him to be Lendelen come again. And yet he knew nothing of his former life, and remembered nothing of the trials he endured within Barad-dûr.

'I could think of one reason only for that: the Valar had kept the memory of that experience from him as a mercy and a kindness. For if he remembered the cruel tortures he suffered at the hands of Sauron, how could he oppose the Dark Lord upon his return, knowing the extent of Sauron's malice?

'And so we chose not to speak of Lendelen or his sad fate within the Dark Tower during the final days of the Second Age. I did not wish to inflict this harm upon Olórin, Mithrandir, or Gandalf... and for that I beg his forgiveness. Lendelen was my closest advisor, my dearest friend, and I failed him. I thought it best to leave his memory behind, for if that was the purpose of the Valar I would not wish to do otherwise.''Forgive me, Olórin, Mithrandir, Gandalf. Forgive me, Lendelen.''Do not trouble yourself, Glorfindel, on my account. All that was kept from me has been restored, and I do not begrudge you for keeping secret that which the Valar hid from me. Indeed, in this knowledge I find reassurance, for I see now that my reluctance to oppose Sauron directly was borne from Lendelen's sad fate. And is it not true that Frodo's quest proved successful, and the Ring and the Dark Lord both defeated at one stroke? Indirect opposition found success where direct opposition could not, and in that I may say the Bright Company came at last to have its greatest triumph.

'But I remember now that which I did not before. I know the end of the tale.'His memory restored by the words of Glorfindel, much that was hidden from Gandalf has been returned to him: pain, loss, and suffering, but knowledge also.Elrond is on Tol Send, and he wishes to pick up the tale where Gandalf has left it.Talk to Elrond on Tol Send'While Sauron tormented Lendelen within Barad-dûr, the leaders of the Great Alliance met once more in secret. A trap was decided upon, a trap with which to ensnare Sauron and draw him forth from his tower.

'The Lady Galadriel had remained at Minas Ithil after she and Lord Celeborn drove the Nazgûl from the valley. Now she sent her thought to Lendelen, and she spoke directly through Sauron's prisoner to the Dark Lord. "One broke the gates of Ithil," Lendelen said, his last breaths upon him, and Sauron laughed, for he had the mastery.

'"Two sundered the ramparts of Morannon," Lendelen continued, and doubt began to gnaw at the Dark Lord. He had long feared the Elven-rings, and in his secret heart he dreaded to think they could be used against him.

'"But Three..." Lendelen said as the last of his strength departed. "The power of the Three, wielded together, shall cast you down at last."

'And so Lendelen perished, and his final words remained hidden to me for this past Age. But now I know what they wrought, for it was by this message that the Deceiver was himself deceived. Sauron climbed to the pinnacle of Barad-dûr, and looking from that vast height he beheld a shining figure ascending the slopes of Mount Doom. Fear seized him, and he imagined that Gil-galad possessed all three Elven-rings, and would use them together to destroy him. For the Three were hidden from his sight, but he thought he could sense they had been united at last.''I remember this day. It is burned upon my memory, and will not fade.''I was with my king on this day. I know what then befell.

'The Dark Lord opened his gates and emptied Barad-dûr, unleashing the last of his armies in a final bid to destroy those who besieged him. On the slopes of Mount Doom he brandished his black mace Mordagnir, wading through friend and foe alike, until at last he faced the High Kings of the Alliance. The One Ring shone on his finger, and he sent forth his evil thought, seeking to dominate Gil-galad's mind when he donned the Three.

'But he did not see it. Gil-galad threw back his head and laughed at Sauron, and the Dark Lord was filled with rage. "The rings of my people may be together," my liege declared, "but they are far from here. Far from you." And Sauron knew he had been deceived, lured from the safety of his tower, and he knew his peril. But above all else he desired to inflict anguish upon those arrayed before him. He sent his will into the One Ring, and his foes were held fast by its spell. Sauron fell upon them in deadly combat, and great gouts of ash and flame roared from the peak of Mount Doom.

'I stood on the slope, too far to aid my king, but near enough to feel the terrible heat of that battle. Even now I can feel it, and I know the pain of that day anew.'Gelvira, daughter of Huneric Hundred-slayer, has been tasked with fortifying Díngarth.Díngarth's crumbling ramparts are in need of repair.Repair crumbling ramparts in Díngarth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gelvira can be found in Díngarth.

You should talk to Gelvira.Talk to Gelvira'I am Gelvira, daughter of Huneric, the Lord of the Thíuda. I have sent for you to assist in the fortification of Díngarth, for the man I spoke with first is a fool and seems not to know the first thing about maintaining the defences! He spoke most rudely to me; I have already forgotten his name. Ontamo, was it? If it were up to me I would give him a sword and send him alone to be the first defender to face the Ghâzab Bôron. Luckily for him, it is not up to me.

'But my father has tasked me with fortifying this fortress before the Storm of Wrath arrives, and that is what I will do! Go and repair the crumbling ramparts along the walls, for there is little time to spare!''These walls will have to do for now. I will continue to look for weak points that the Storm of Wrath may use to breach our defences. Our efforts must not be in vain.'Wight archers patrol Dol Naeg, watching for attacks from the Great Alliance.Mordor dread-archers can be found at Dol Naeg.

You should defeat Mordor dread-archers.Defeat Mordor dread-archersMordor dread-archers can be found at Dol Naeg.

You should defeat Mordor dread-archers.Defeat Mordor dread-archers at Dol Naeg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeated many Mordor dread-archers at Dol Naeg.CompletedWight archers patrol the hill of Dol Naeg. They wait patiently, diligently, watching for approaching attacks from the Great Alliance. Their defeat will greatly assist all free folk in the war effort.You have defeated many Mordor dread-archers at Dol Naeg. Your efforts upon the battle-worn hill greatly assist all those who wish to bring freedom to the very steps of Barad-dûr.Nithi wishes to sow confusion among the ranks of Sauron's forces so soldiers of the Great Alliance can carry out their attacks in the Shadowfells of Emyn Duir.Flammable debris can be found throughout Emyn Duir.

You should start fires and defeat enemies throughout Emyn Duir.Start fires throughout Emyn Duir to distract the Enemy (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat foes in Emyn Duir (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nithi can be found at Barthost.

You should return to Nithi with news of your successes in Emyn Duir.Talk to Nithi'If we are to press the attack upon the tower of Barad-dûr, we will need to have a distraction. Otherwise the attack will fail before it is barely begun! I have spent many years in the mines, and the darkness of the Shadowfells is not unlike the caverns with which I am familiar.

'What is the best way to distract the darkness, you ask? Why, with fire, of course! I ask you to light fires throughout Emyn Duir, for the sorcerous creatures of the Shadowfells will not be able to abide their light, and the flames will draw the attention of anyone hereabout.

'By Durin's beard, I am certain this plan will work! It just makes sense, my friend, does it not?''My plan progresses apace! Even from here I saw the fires burning brightly across the Shadowfells! You have done better than I hoped. I shall alert the others.

'Those fires will not burn forever, and I fear the flame of my desire for battle might outlast them! May we attack soon, before they go out!'Durin IV seeks to raise the heraldry of his people above the Corpse-fields of Parth Daenath.Good places to raise the heraldry of Durin IV can be found in Parth Daenath.

You should raise the heraldry of Durin IV in Parth Daenath.Raise heraldry of Durin IV in Parth Daenath (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Durin IV can be found at Echad-in-Edhil.

You should talk to Durin IV.Talk to Durin IV'I have come to assist in the war effort, though all else seems lost. Still would my people stand, fighting to the very end, even to the last dwarf. We will defy the Storm of Wrath for as long as we are able! But that horde is not yet here, and there are Orcs enough to deal with before it arrives. Know this: the Orcs of Sauron still fear my name and the axes of the dwarves!

'Take the heraldry of my people and raise it above the Corpse-fields of Parth Daenath. Our enemies will cower and our fellows will have strength in the coming days! Baruk Khazâd!''I see the great banners of my people above the battle-plain. The Orcs are surely running in fear back to Barad-dûr, the cowards!'Huneric, Lord of the Thíuda, seeks to plant the heraldry of the Thíuda throughout Arandor.Good places to raise the heraldry of the Thíuda can be found in Arandor.

You should raise the heraldry of the Thíuda for Huneric.Raise heraldry of the Thíuda in Arandor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Huneric can be found in Adambel.

You should talk to Huneric.Talk to Huneric'The Thíuda are a proud people. When I see one of our banners proudly riding the wind, I take on the courage of ten Men! Take this heraldry of my people and plant it throughout the Kingsland of Arandor. May it lessen the courage of our foes and bring strength to our fellows!''The banners of my people will lift the hearts of all who fight for the Great Alliance, and lend them the courage they will need to face the Storm of Wrath!'The sons of Isildur are spending a final night by the fire before the day dawns upon the Bright Company's desperate mission to rob Sauron of his domination over the Ghâzab Bôron.Vëamacil and his brothers are at Díngarth.Talk to VëamacilMeldaran is at Díngarth.Talk to MeldaranThe sons of Isildur are at Díngarth.Listen to the conversation of the sons of IsildurTárandil is at Díngarth.Talk to TárandilThe sons of Isildur are at Díngarth.Listen to the conversation of the sons of IsildurVëamacil is at Díngarth.Talk to VëamacilThe sons of Isildur are at Díngarth, waiting for you to sit by their camp-fire.The sons of Isildur are at Díngarth.Listen to the conversation of the sons of Isildur'It is strange to me that after so many years spent in siege, tomorrow the sun may dawn upon the Great Alliance for the last time. The council was most clear: if the Bright Company fails to break Sauron's control over the Oath-stone, all hope of our victory is fled.

'No! Banish the thought, banish it! We will think of something.''I will not lie to you, friend. Your mission tomorrow will not be easy, for you go to the most dangerous of all places in this Black Land. I envy you not the chance; I am no fool! But in this one thing I am envious: tomorrow you will be given the chance to do something, stand or fall, and you need not wait for fate to arrive; you will go to meet it! Not every man gets the opportunity for that.

'The Elves of the Bright Company are renowned for their courage. If I had any advice to give you, it would be this: stay near to Glorfindel! Some measure of inspiration will serve you in good stead, and I warrant he will provide any you might require! Who can tell what we might say on the morrow? The glory of the Bright Company may be joined with yours, and Men may speak in reverent tones of your own contribution. Victory might not be yours, but at the least none will speak of cowardice!''If we do survive... if the Bright Company does complete its mission, and Sauron's hold over the Ghâzab Bôron is broken, I wish to ask my foster-father for a boon. I do not seek treasure, or reward, or glory. I want only one thing.'

Tárandil clenches his hands into fists.

'I want Isildur to send me to Morthond. I want to find Rioc of the Mountain, in whatever hole he hides, and make him pay for his oath-breaking. I want to slay him myself. If we survive the days to come, I will take no rest until I have paid Rioc for his treachery.''I have seen brighter birthdays, but if we do survive, let us look back upon this one more fondly! Tárandil, I am certain father will grant you the boon you seek; did he not rage for a week and a day at the betrayal of the Men of the Mountain? If you desire the death of Rioc, father is certain to grant it.

'But let us speak of lighter matters! Among the Thíuda recently come through Ram Raedas, I saw a woman of such loveliness she could melt even your own sturdy armour, Tárandil! The blade she carried was sharp, even deadlier than your own, and with words of love you might win her heart. Why, she might join her weapon to yours, and let Rioc pay double for his treachery!'Vëamacil and Ornaher, two of the sons of Isildur, are eager to conduct a dangerous rescue mission into the enemy-held fortress of Amon Fuin.Vëamacil and Ornaher are outside the walls of Amon Fuin.Talk to VëamacilYou and the sons of Isildur have encountered Magoldir the Elf outside Amon Fuin.Talk to Magoldir outside Amon FuinMagoldir wants you to advise Vëamacil and Ornaher to abandon their mission to Amon Fuin.The sons of Isildur have charged forward into Amon Fuin, dismissing the warnings of Magoldir.Talk to Magoldir outside Amon FuinUrudanî Stonemaiden commands the Mordorrim cultists within Amon Fuin.Defeat the guards that defend Urudanî StonemaidenUrudanî's cultists have been defeated, and her contingent of Orcs has abandoned her, but she still intends to stand and fight.Talk to Urudanî Stonemaiden in Amon FuinThe sons of Isildur weigh what should be done with Urudanî Stonemaiden. She has been defeated, but while she lives she remains a threat to the Great Alliance.Stand alongside Magoldir and the sons of Isildur in Amon Fuin'I hate to leave without the success for which we hoped, but I see no other choice. There is no sign of our missing soldiers, and from the words of Urudanî Stonemaiden I fear we are not likely to see them again.

'Magoldir should not have had us spare her. If she works some mischief upon the works of the Great Alliance it will be upon his head, and no other.

'Bah! Let us leave this place, friend. It is not safe to remain here, no matter its unusual quiet.''All is quiet, but do not trust the stillness! There will be look-outs up ahead, and we should dispatch them quickly before they can raise the alarm. Are you ready?''I saw you from afar, my friend, and came to lend you any assistance you might require. And this too: a warning 'gainst this course! Was this excursion to Amon Fuin your idea, or that of these sons of Isildur?

'It is true that there is less activity here than there was of late, but Amon Fuin is still a place of evil and will not be undefended. This seems to me an unwise course, and I beg you convince these two to turn back, for the Great Alliance will be the worse for it should something befall them.''Yes, I hear Magoldir's warning, but the evil of the Mordorrim must be opposed, and who better to do so than the sons of Isildur? Good soldiers have been spirited away by some evil ruse, and we must find them if we can!''Magoldir's warning is well-intentioned, but you must understand that we cannot allow good soldiers of the Alliance to remain in torment within Amon Fuin. We have no choice! It is up to Isildur's sons to save them, and we will not shrink from that purpose!''That was... unexpected.'

Magoldir hefts his sword.

'I suppose we need to follow them. Their father will not look kindly upon us were we to abandon them now, although it seems to this observer that they were the ones who abandoned us. Even so, this is our duty.''A pox on them!

'I did not need those cowards before, and I do not need them now! I will slay you myself, and exult in your demise!'With the triumphant Rhúnhoth horde of the Ghâzab Bôron on its way, the lords of the Great Alliance have called a council to determine what can be done to stave off defeat.Tárandil is outside the pavilion where the council of the kings will take place.Talk to TárandilThe council of the kings will soon begin, and the sons of Isildur are hopeful that their presence and yours will go unremarked.Wait for the council of the kings to beginHigh King Gil-galad has reservations about your attendance and that of Isildur's sons.Talk to High King Gil-galadHigh King Gil-galad has called into question your presence, and that of the sons of Isildur, at the council of kings.Observe the council of the kingsVëamacil is in the tent where the council of kings is underway.Talk to VëamacilThe council of kings is underway.Observe the council of the kingsLendelen of the Bright Company has proposed a dangerous plan by which Sauron's hold over the Ghâzab Bôron might be broken.Talk to VëamacilThe council of the kings continues, but not all in attendance are satisfied with its pronouncements.Observe the council of the kingsHigh King Elendil is inside the pavilion for the council of kings.Talk to High King Elendil'I cannot believe this!''Let us enter the tent, but do not walk with fear or nervousness! Stride, rather, as if we were invited and indeed the council cannot begin until we are in attendance.

'That is always the advice of Vëamacil, and to speak truth I have yet to see it fail him.''This pavilion seems more crowded already than it need be for such a council. Is it not better to limit those in attendance? I mean no disrespect to the sons of Isildur or their sire, but what is to be gained by their presence here, or that of a ${CLASS}? Can they, and you, not be told the decisions of this council once they are made?''Durin is right. Sauron will hide in his tower until the Rhúnhoth horde arrives and destroys us! Why would he dare come out when his victory is all but assured? He has only to wait for it!

'We have to think of something!''This is our opportunity! Now is our chance for glory!''You will fight alongside the Bright Company for this undertaking, ${CLASS}, if that is the wish of Glorfindel and his counsellor Lendelen. It will be dangerous, but what matters of war are not? If they are Men, my grandsons will forgive you for being so-selected, and resent it not.

'If the Bright Company fails in this, the Storm of Wrath will come for us all, and they will then have ample opportunity to die in glorious battle. If that is a comfort to them, let it comfort them. I will coddle them not.'Glorfindel relates to you the last glorious mission of the Bright Company, but he seems to harbour some hesitation, reluctant to share the tale with you.Glorfindel is in the tunnel of Halroth, prepared to fight through the dark passage to Barad-dûr itself.Talk to Glorfindel in the tunnel of HalrothGlorfindel is in the tunnel of Halroth, prepared to fight through the dark passage to Barad-dûr itself.Talk to Glorfindel in the tunnel of HalrothGlorfindel is in the tunnel of Halroth, prepared to fight through the dark passage to Barad-dûr itself.Talk to Glorfindel in the tunnel of HalrothNow that you have passed beyond the wall of Barad-dûr with the Bright Company, Lendelen wishes to speak to you.Talk to Lendelen inside Barad-dûrLendelen wants you to climb the spiral stair to the upper floors of the tower, where other Elves of the Bright Company will join you.Climb the spiral stair and speak with an Elf of the Bright Company outside the upper chambersLendelen and Glorfindel have rejoined you atop the stair, and now their mission, and yours, must be completed.Follow the Bright Company into the upper chambers of the towerThe Bright Company stands against the Nazgûl in a high chamber of Barad-dûr, but the mission of Glorfindel and Lendelen remains higher still.Talk to Glorfindel in Barad-dûrThe Bright Company stands against the Nazgûl in a high chamber of Barad-dûr, but the mission of Glorfindel and Lendelen remains higher still.Follow Glorfindel and Lendelen to the chamber of the Vandassar, and stand with themYou have come face to face with Sauron in an upper chamber of Barad-dûr, and must distract the Dark Lord from his real danger.Talk to SauronThe mission of the Bright Company all comes down to this moment, high in the chamber of the Vandassar.Stand with Glorfindel and LendelenLendelen has successfully broken Sauron's hold upon the Vandassar, and now you must escape from the tower!Follow Glorfindel and escape from the tower!<rgb=#FF0000>If you do not escape in time you will fail the quest.</rgb>Glorfindel has halted, waiting for Lendelen.Talk to Glorfindel in Barad-dûrGlorfindel has concluded his tale of the last glorious mission of the Bright Company, in which Sauron's control over the Vandassar was broken.Listen to the words of GlorfindelGlorfindel stands on Tol Send, ready to conclude his tale.Talk to Glorfindel on Tol SendGlorfindel stands on Tol Send, ready to conclude his tale.Listen to the words of GlorfindelGandalf stands on Tol Send, the events of the past newly-awakened in him.Talk to Gandalf on Tol Send'It is time. Lendelen and the other Elves of the Bright Company have gone ahead to secure the tunnel, but we must still remain alert for any threats that might have arisen behind them.

'Stay near to me.''Lendelen has never been one to exercise caution, not when he knows his fellows could be endangered by some approaching threat. On any other day I would appreciate that trait, but on this day Lendelen is the only Elf among us with the ability to break Sauron's hold upon the Vandassar! His role is crucial, and he must be protected!''The passage begins to rise. We must be nearing the mountainside well-nigh to the side of the tower.''Listen to me very carefully, friend ${CLASS}. Ahead, a stair spirals to the upper chambers of the tower, where the Vandassar awaits me. That is our destination.

'But we will be exposed on the stairs, and I fear the hateful eyes that may watch our ascent. This I suggest: that you go first.

'Nay, do not protest! I do not jest. I wish for you to climb first so I may cover your ascent with my bow. I will slay any foes that think to threaten you from afar, and then the rest of us will follow. We will meet you atop the stair.''We cannot stay here, though it grieves me deeply to leave my friends to their doom.

'Our goal lies above! Lendelen must reach the Vandassar!''Your people believe they are strong, gathered beneath their banners and behind their kings. For how long has this "Great Alliance" lasted? No more than a handful of years. It needs but one hammer-stroke, well-aimed, and it will crumble into dust. So it always is with Men.

'I have called into being a Storm, and it is on its way. Your Alliance will be swept away by the wrath of it.''I thought he was right behind us, but it is not so!

'Lendelen, forgive me, my friend! Alas that we must bring word of both your triumph and your loss to the Great Alliance! By your hand the Vandassar is broken, and Sauron's control over the Ghâzab Bôron must begin to wane, and you should celebrate with us! But it is not to be.

'Quickly, mellon. There is no time to spare.''I remember little of our last desperate flight down the stairs and through the tunnel of Halroth. When we returned to the relative safety of Adambel, we were greeted with more sorrow than we brought with us. Men spoke in hushed tones of the last ride of noble Anárion, son of their High King, slain on the field before the Dark Tower. It was a great stone, cast from the heights, that laid him low and ended his life. But I will never forget that it was his riding that granted us access to Halroth, and enabled Lendelen to break Sauron's hold upon the Vandassar.

'For so it was. Without the power of the Oath-stone, the Ghâzab Bôron splintered. No longer unified, the Storm of Wrath dissipated, falling apart into its component tribes. The Easterlings fled, no longer dominated by Sauron's will, and they turned instead for their own lands. Their appetite for conquering was sated, and it would be many years before their return. Even so, Middle-earth has never seen so great a horde as the Ghâzab Bôron in all the time since, and we have Lendelen to thank for that.''I have walked this Middle-earth for the long years of this Third Age, sent by the Powers to oppose Sauron. But this was not the first time the Valar sent me hither, though they hid the truth of it from my memory's sight! For what purpose?

'It is true. I remember the Bright Company, not only from the tales of Glorfindel but as if I were there... for so I was, in different form. I know the torments Lendelen suffered within the dungeons of Barad-dûr, for it was I who suffered them, and though Sauron be gone the memory of those days fills me with a terror I thought never to know.

'But this cut is the worst: I have known these Elf-lords for so long, and this secret they kept from me. Why? Why did you keep this truth from me, Glorfindel? Have I not been a friend to you, no matter the name I wore? You knew me first as Olórin, and now you know me as Gandalf, or Mithrandir. Why would you keep the knowledge of Lendelen from me?

'No, I am not angry. Perhaps it was a kindness to keep this from me. What say you to this?''I will think no worse of you if you choose to turn back.

'No? You wish to stay? Then you are truly one of us, mellon.'Huneric, Lord of the Thíuda, has noticed forces gathering at Mokh Darga and wishes to halt a raid before it begins.Talk to Huneric.Talk to HunericDefeat the raiding party.Defeat the raiding party<rgb=#FF0000>Huneric must survive</rgb>'You have fought well. Come, let us return to Adambel.''The raiding party prepares their assault! Now is the time to attack. Follow me into battle!'Pages of a journal can be found scattered across Mordor Besieged. Perhaps you can complete it if you find them all.Stark-white pages can be found scattered throughout Mordor Besieged.

You should collect the pages to complete the story.Collect Page 1Collect Page 2Collect Page 3Collect Page 4Collect Page 5Collect Page 6Collect Page 7Collect Page 8Collect Page 9Collect Page 10Collect Page 11Collect Page 12Collect Page 13Collect Page 14Collect Page 15Collect Page 16Collect Page 17Collect Page 18Collect Page 19Collect Page 20Collect Page 21Collect Page 22Collect Page 23Collect Page 24Collect Page 25Collect Page 26Collect Page 27Completed.CompletedYou have found lost pages of a journal authored by Elendil.

You should continue adventuring in Mordor Besieged.Continue adventuring in Mordor BesiegedHigh King Elendil can be found in Adambel.

If he is missing, he will return after the Council of the Kings.Talk to ElendilYou have found the page of a journal, stark-white in contrast to the battle-scarred plain of Gorgoroth. Perhaps you can find other pages in Mordor Besieged and complete the story.The story is complete. You believe this is an account of Gil-galad's shattering of the Morannon written by High King Elendil himself. Perhaps if you bring it to him, he will be able to comment further.'Hail, friend, what have you there?'

You show the High King the pages you have collected.

'Ah, I had thought this journal lost! You are quite resourceful to have reconstructed this account.

'Indeed, the words here-in are mine. All who were present were shaken to the core by the storm Gil-galad brought forth that day. I am sure you feel the same. Does that surprise you? Even Kings feel fear in the face of real power. And still, the knowledge that the Dark Lord has a ring of power all his own brings me pause. We must bury that fear, and hold on to our courage. This is our last stand against the darkness; Courage is our greatest weapon.'

He hands you the spine from which the journal pages originated.

'Keep this journal for yourself. Let it be a constant reminder of the power of our Great Alliance, and give you hope in the battles to come. The Enemy may have great power, but so do we.'The watch-post of Matum Graz overlooks the path to Barad-dûr. Within its walls lurk many shadowy dangers.Matum Graz can be found in Emyn Duir.

