You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to Rhadrog'${PLAYER}, after claiming this tower, I expected all of the Rangers to check in, but I have not seen or heard from several of them. They could be in danger! Please, search for them. They must still be in the Circle of Madness.''The missing Rangers are all accounted for, thanks to you! I think they learned their lesson around the merrevail: always keep an eye on the skies!'Mithrandir has a plan for you to deceive the guardians of Barad Cúron, but it will require some outside help.The gossipmonger Meldir is just inside the entrance to Minas Tirith.

Mithrandir has sent you to Minas Tirith seeking the assistance of Ayorzén, but first you need to find him.Talk to Meldir just inside the entrance to Minas TirithMeldir told you that Ayorzén might be found at the Eagle and Star, a tavern south-west of the entrance to Minas Tirith.Find Ayorzén at the Eagle and Star, in Minas TirithAyorzén the Wily is at the Eagle and Star in Minas Tirith.Talk to Ayorzén the Wily at the Eagle and StarYou should place the mask of Karazgar on the table in front of Ayorzén, and enlist him to help you.Ayorzén is at the Eagle and Star in Minas Tirith.Talk to Ayorzén at the Eagle and StarMithrandir has come to the Eagle and Star.Talk to Mithrandir at the Eagle and StarMithrandir has directed your attention to a darkened corner in the room furthest from the entrance.Investigate the mysterious figure in the darkened corner of the tavernAragorn has come to the Eagle and Star to meet with you, wearing a disguise that hides his identity.Talk to Aragorn at the Eagle and StarAragorn is at the Eagle and Star, and he wishes to tell you the story of Eärnur's last days.Talk to Aragorn and complete 'Instance: The Warrior Remade'Aragorn is at the Eagle and Star.Talk to Aragorn at the Eagle and Star'Nay, my friend, do not look at me with such horror! I do not speak of performing Sauron's dread ritual! The act of bleeding the men and women that became the Gúrzyul required more than just Sauron's morgul-knife Gulthauk. It needed also a will of evil, and be glad that is not a thing we possess. But we do have in hand something else.'

The Wizard opens his satchel of possessions and removes an unpleasant artifact: the Mask of Karazgar.

'The Weeping Warrior dogged our steps in the North, ${PLAYER}, and he inflicted much pain and sorrow upon dwarves and Men. But he does possess two useful traits: his reputation, and his absence. Of all the Gúrzyul, he may be the most feared: a Black Númenórean who moved with stealth, appearing out of nowhere to slay and torment before vanishing, leaving only the memory of a frightening visage, a masked evil. He captured dragons and brought them to Sauron, but it is long since he was seen in Mordor. Rumour of his cruelties in far-off Rhûn will have spread even here.

'I think the spirit of this Watching-stone will know of the Weeping Warrior and his exploits in the East and in the North, and I think too that it will not have seen him in long centuries, if at all. Were you to wear the mask, the spirit would no doubt see through our deception. But if another were to wear it, perhaps one of the Khundolar who rarely come to the Vale, who is to say what the spirit might think? And if Karazgar were to bring a powerful prisoner to Gothmog, the very ${RACE} who has proven so troublesome to Gothmog's schemes, would the spirit not allow both of them access to the inner sanctum?

'What has your friend Ayorzén gotten up to of late, ${PLAYER}? Is he still as Wily as he ever was? Go to Minas Tirith and speak with the gossipmonger Meldir just inside the entrance of the city. He will know where the Easterlings who came out of Gorgoroth have gone.''What has your friend Ayorzén gotten up to of late, ${PLAYER}? Is he still as Wily as he ever was? Go to Minas Tirith and speak with the gossipmonger Meldir, just inside the entrance to the city. He will know where the Easterlings who came out of Gorgoroth have gone.''You are looking for Easterlings? Aye, King Elessar has called for them to be given places to stay in Gondor, and the kingdom will follow his command. But he cannot bid every man or woman of Gondor to open their hearts to these newcomers, and were he to try he would likely face his first failure as king. Many here lost loved ones to Khundolar swords within view of these walls, and they can neither forget nor forgive it.

'But if you seek Easterlings, you may find them at the Eagle and Star, to the south-west. The barkeep there has welcomed their coin, at least.''If you seek Easterlings, you may find them at the Eagle and Star, to the south-west. The barkeep there accepts their coin, at least.''If you seek Easterlings, you may find them at the Eagle and Star, to the south-west.''So you are still alive, ${PLAYER}? Good for you. When last I saw you, you planned a foolish raid upon some stronghold in Mordor, thinking a disguise might keep you safe. It seems the advice I gave you proved helpful, for you live still! Are you here to thank me for your survival? Know that I am Ayorzén the Unrewarded, and will gracefully accept whatever coin or treasure you balance against your life!'

Ayorzén's eyes narrow when no reward is forthcoming, and they narrow further when you tell him you need his help gaining access to the tower atop Minas Morgul.

'You only come to me when you need something, ${PLAYER}. Am I to dress up as one of the Nurnhoth, as you did before? For I will not. I led the Khundolar into battle! Men followed me to war, even to death, and if my star has fallen low it will rise again. I wear these garments now, but in time I will again don my shining armour, and lead Men to victory! Keep your base disguise. Ayorzén the Proud will have none of it.''I will never again wear the clothes of a prisoner. Indeed, the garment I wear now seems too near to that for comfort!''Did you kill him, then? There is only one mask such as this in all the world, ${PLAYER}, and the Weeping Warrior would never part with it. Long did he stalk the lands of my people, bringing death without mercy to all. For all my years, the mention of his name drained the courage of even the bravest soldier, and set to weeping mothers and fathers both, remembering children slain by his blades for no reason at all. The sight of his mask commanded...'

Ayorzén falls silent.Ayorzén says nothing, his eyes still fixed upon the mask of Karazgar.'Has he agreed to our scheme, ${PLAYER}? Ah, I see he considers it. May he hasten his decision! How much time does he think we have?

'But there is another here with whom I would have you speak. Did you not notice him enter? I see his ascension has not diminished his former skill at moving without being seen, nor his desire to make use of it!

'Unless I miss my guess he will be in the furthest room, ${PLAYER}, in a darkened corner.''He is further inside, in the corner. Speak to him while Ayorzén considers our proposal.''Old habits do not always vanish easily, my friend. It remains a wonder to me how much a difference one's garb can make in the eyes of the people. What would Barliman have to say if he saw me in my king's raiment? No, I should prefer to wear this to the Pony. That is Strider's place, not Elessar's.

'Mithrandir has told me some of what he has learned from that tome you discovered in Járnfast, and he entreated me to tell you what I know of King Eärnur's last days. Some of this you may have heard already, but the true story was never intended for the ears of the common folk, and some of it was passed down only by the Wise and to those of Isildur's line, kept secret in the wilderness.

'I will tell you what I know of it, if that is your wish.''I will tell you the tale of Eärnur's last days, as it was told to me.''That is the tale, as it is known to the Wise and to those of Isildur's line. But I would have you remember this, ${PLAYER}: whatever Eärnur may have been, that man is no more. Do not let the knowledge of who he once was stay the punishment that is due. He is now wholly evil, and all of Middle-earth has suffered at his hands and those of his masters.'The Orcs of Ugrukhôr's forces have begun to construct ladder-bridges over the Circle of Despair.Orkish ladder-bridges can be found in the Circle of Despair.Destroy Orkish ladder-bridges in the Circle of DespairOrkish ladder-bridges can be found in the Circle of Despair.

While working to secure the Circle of Despair, you encountered a number of Orkish ladder-bridges constructed by Ugrukhôr's Orcs to climb higher into Minas Morgul. You should destroy them.Destroy Orkish ladder-bridges in the Circle of Despair (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})While working to secure the Circle of Despair alongside Dornach, you noticed several crude ladder-bridges erected by Ugrukhôr's Orcs.

Presumably, the Orcs intend to avoid the perils of the Circle of Despair and gain an advantage over the Rangers now seeking shelter within Barad Arthir.

You should destroy the ladder-bridges before more of Ugrukhôr's forces are allowed to climb higher into the Dead City.Faeron has decided the time is right to enter the Halls of Black Lore to seek the recipe for Lhaereth's Great Plague.Complete 'Leave No One Behind'Faeron and Rhadrog are stationed in the Stargazer's Spire in Minas Morgul.

You should report to Faeron.Talk to FaeronRhadrog can be found in the Stargazer's Spire.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to RhadrogComplete Ghashan-kútot, the Halls of Black Lore on any difficulty mode.

<rgb=#FF0000>You may enter this instance by using the Instance Join panel. </rgb>Complete Ghashan-kútot, the Halls of Black LoreYou have defeated Dolguzigir, and been granted an opportunity to take one tome from the Halls of Black Lore.

You should ask Dolguzigir for the Great Plague recipe.Talk to DolguzigirYou have completed your mission and acquired the plague recipe.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron in the Stargazer's SpireWord has reached you that the Rangers Faeron and Rhadrog are ready to infiltrate the Halls of Black Lore. You should return to the Stargazer's Spire to assist them.'Welcome back, ${PLAYER}. The time has come to return to the Halls of Black Lore. I hope you are ready.

'I have kept a vigilant watch on the Circle of Madness since you departed. Lhaereth's forces have ceased their frenzied attacks, as it seems whatever she seeks is not within the city. The Dark Archivist, Dolguzigir, has never left the Halls, but I have seen his forces, mostly some variety of Morgul Cultists, coming and going. They have finally let their guard down, if only slightly. If we wish to sneak in, this is our best chance! To that end, I have decided that you should take the lead on this mission.

'You are renowned for your skill, ${PLAYER}. I have no doubt that you have many allies whom you may call upon. If you deem it our best chance, you may muster a force to storm the Halls and take the plague recipe by force. Otherwise, Rhadrog and I will accompany you.

'Speak with Rhadrog, and let him know what you decide. And remember, we know not what strength the Dark Archivist possesses, but we should not underestimate him. He did dare to stand up to Lhaereth herself.'You ask Dolguzigir for Lhaereth's recipe for the Great Plague. He cackles, and snaps his fingers. A blood-red tome materializes directly into your hands.

'As you wish, mortal! I look forward to seeing the fallout of Men gaining this knowledge!'You hand Faeron the red tome given to you by Dolguzigir. His expression turns grim as he takes it. He opens it, and flips through the pages. The contents appear to disgust him, and he slams it shut. 

'He did not deceive you; this is indeed the Great Plague recipe, penned by Lhaereth herself. I will make sure this is delivered to the scholars in Minas Tirith. With this, as distasteful as this knowledge is, we will have a chance. If Lhaereth deploys her plague again, the Free Peoples will endure.''${PLAYER}! Ah, if you have returned, that must mean it is time for Faeron's plan... Not that I'm not happy to see you! I suppose I'm just a bit nervous to be heading back to that place. Something about that Dark Archivist unnerved me. He barely reacted when Lhaereth struck him down!

'I will of course be ready to accompany you if that is your choice! The three of us conquered Seregost together, after all. What trouble could a library be?'The tower of Barad Elenath, otherwise known as the Star-gazers' Spire, must be captured in order to enter the Circle of Madness and reclaim Minas Morgul.Secure the tower.

<rgb=#FF0000>It is recommended to have the quest The Circle of Sorrow underway.</rgb>Secure the towerTalk to one of the Rangers.Talk to one of the RangersThe tower of Barad Elenath, otherwise known as the Star-gazers' Spire, must be captured in order to enter the Circle of Madness and reclaim Minas Morgul.'We have secured the tower!'You have entered the Halls of Black Lore to uncover what Lhaereth seeks, but danger is near.You have entered the Halls of Black Lore.

You should speak to Faeron.Talk to FaeronYou should follow Faeron and keep to the shadows.Follow FaeronLhaereth has entered and is speaking to one who can only be the keeper of the Halls of Black Lore.

You should listen to their conversation.Listen to the conversationSomeone, or something, has created a diversion for you!

You should escape the Halls of Black Lore!Escape the Halls'I do not think we were seen. We should return to the tower.''Stay together, stay quiet. Remember: if Lhaereth is already here, do not engage.'Rhadrog has a tale to tell about his departure from Agarnaith.Rhadrog is telling you the tale of why he left Agarnaith with Faeron.

You should talk to Rhadrog to begin.Talk to RhadrogRhadrog is telling you the tale of why he left Agarnaith with Faeron.

You should listen to Rhadrog's story.Listen to Rhadrog's story'We then left Agarnaith and followed the merrevail to the Morgul Vale.''We were tasked with keeping a watch on Seregost. Things had been mostly quiet for a while, though I was beginning to grow tired of the colour red!'Aragorn has agreed to tell you the tale of Eärnur's last days, known only to the wise and to those of Isildur's line.Aragorn has agreed to tell you what he knows of Eärnur's last days.Talk to AragornAragorn has agreed to tell you what he knows of Eärnur's last days.Listen to the words of Aragorn concerning EärnurAragorn has agreed to tell you what he knows of Eärnur's last days.Talk to AragornAragorn has agreed to tell you what he knows of King Eärnur's last days.Listen to the words of AragornAragorn has agreed to tell you what he knows of King Eärnur's last days.Talk to Aragorn'My tale is at its end, ${PLAYER}, but the wraith that was once Eärnur troubles Gondor still.''You have heard the story of Egalmoth's Onslaught against the forces of Mordor that came to seize Minas Ithil? It is a popular tale, oft-told in Gondor even today, for it is heroic and tragic in equal measure. Lord Egalmoth had the command of Barad Cúron, and his charge through the city into the invading Orcs is now the province of legend. He and all his men died in that sally, but when the last defender was dead and the Witch-king entered the tower, he learned of Egalmoth's other purpose. The palantír was missing. One of Egalmoth's riders had carried it from Barad Cúron, casting it into the Ithilduin lest it be claimed by the Nazgûl.

'Like many victories of the Dúnedain over the years, this one was short-lived. Servants of the Witch-king found the Ithil-stone fetched up upon a rock in the current, and they brought it to their master. Gloating over his victory, the Lord of the Nazgûl used the palantír only once before bringing it personally to Sauron, as he was commanded. The Witch-king made contact with the Anor-stone wielded by King Eärnil, showing him that Egalmoth had failed and the city belonged now to him. "Minas Ithil has fallen," the Nazgûl sneered. "You look now upon Minas Morgul."

'Eärnil despaired, and he forbade any of his subjects or his line from using the Anor-stone. "It is no longer safe," he declared. The Ban of Eärnil would last for nearly a thousand years, unbroken until the time of Steward Denethor. It may be that the Ban prevented Sauron from achieving his goal of a corrupted Gondor centuries before he nearly succeeded... but I cannot say for certain.''Eärnur was hot of temper, and surely would have ridden forth at once, were it not for the intervention of his advisor Mardil. A wise man and studied, Gondor remembers him as Mardil the Good, and his actions on this day forestalled some measure of doom. Not forever, alas! But for a time.

'Mardil stepped forward. "We know who your master is," he declared. "We know how much he can be trusted, or how little. If he is so eager for this combat, let him ride here, to the Tower of Guard, and show his true worth before all."

'The emissary bridled at this response and departed, but his anger was as nothing compared to the wrath of Eärnur. He brooded and raged in the time that followed, but whenever he thought to dare the shadowed vale, his Steward was on hand with soothing words and reminders of his duty. The Morgul Vale remained silent.

'So passed seven grim years. Seven years following the day that he first came to Minas Tirith, the messenger returned.

'This time he was not alone.''Before his blood had cooled, Eärnur and a company of his bravest knights rode from Minas Tirith for the last time. They entered the Morgul Vale and were never seen again.

'The common belief in the years that followed was that the Witch-king treacherously trapped the king and his knights in Minas Morgul, and they perished there in torment. No proper claimant to the throne could be found, and the Line of Kings was ended. Mardil did not don the crown himself; instead, he became the first of the Ruling Stewards, swearing to govern Gondor only until the king returned. And you know how that tradition reached its ending, ${PLAYER}.

'But when Eärnur succumbed to the taunts of the Witch-king and rode to Minas Morgul, we know now what was hidden for those many centuries. Through the dark arts of the Lord of the Nazgûl, Eärnur was corrupted and changed into the fell wraith Mordirith. Bound to the Witch-king, he served Mordor as the False King of Angmar until his defeat last year. When next he crossed our paths, he had been given a new form with which to wage Sauron's war: Gothmog, commander of Mordor's armies.

'Better to have died as Eärnur, ${PLAYER}, than be reborn as a tool of evil, again and again. It saddens me to think of that fate.'Faeron and Viznak are about to be overrun by merrevail and need your help!With Rhadrog and Dolenthol in tow, you should return to Faeron and see how you can help.Return to FaeronDolenthol has just learned that Viznak is actually a goblin.

You should listen to the conversation.Listen to the conversationYou are under attack!

Defeat the merrevail attackers!Defeat the merrevailViznak has regained consciousness and is speaking with the Rangers.

You should listen to the conversation.Listen to the conversation'The more Rangers there are that know my true identity, the harder it will be to maintain my deception. Alas, it was necessary to reveal myself to them. Let us return to the tower. The city continues higher still.'The merrevail have also brought their pets and plague-carriers, the great-bats, with them to Minas Morgul.Great-bats from Agarnaith have invaded the third tier in Minas Morgul, the Circle of Madness.

You should defeat great-bats.Defeat great-bats (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Faeron is stationed inside Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron'The merrevail have not come alone; the great-bats have also joined in the invasion of the city. If I were to hazard a guess, Lhaereth plans to use them as plague-carriers to attack Gothmog's living forces in the heights of the city. We know first-hand how dangerous they are. They must be eliminated.''With their plague-carriers defeated, they will be unable to deploy the plague on any Ranger encampments in the city. Thank you for your efforts, ${PLAYER}.'You have escaped from the Halls of Black Lore, but without Faeron. You must find him and discover if it was indeed Viznak you heard inside.Faeron did not return to the Stargazers' Spire with the others, so he must still be somewhere in the Circle of Madness.

You should find Faeron!Find FaeronFaeron has rescued Viznak, but the merrevail are in quick pursuit.

You should return to the Barad Elenath and inform Rhadrog!Talk to RhadrogFaeron has rescued Viznak, but the merrevail are hot on their heels!

You should inform Dolenthol!Talk to DolentholDolenthol is ready to help Faeron and Viznak.

You should let Rhadrog know you are ready to go.Talk to RhadrogViznak and Faeron need your help!

You should talk to Rhadrog to begin 'Instance: Viznak Lives'.Talk to Rhadrog to begin 'Instance: Viznak Lives'Viznak has survived the attack, and is now resting outside Barad Elenath.

You should talk to Viznak.Talk to ViznakFaeron has returned to Barad Elenath, the Stargazers' Spire.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron'You must search for Faeron! I will stay here in case he comes back. He must be somewhere in the Circle of Madness!''${PLAYER}! I lingered outside the Halls to see if Viznak escaped, but the merrevail were in close pursuit! I took out as many as I could and carried him here, but he is gravely injured. I do not know if more are coming. You must bring help! Hurry!'You quickly tell Rhadrog that Faeron is nearby with a gravely-wounded Viznak.

'They're alive! We must go at once, but I admit I know only the basics of field medicine. We need a proper medic!''What is wrong, ${PLAYER}? Is this about your friend Viznak? Who is he?'

You avoid answering the question outright, but you tell Éowyn that Viznak is wounded and Faeron needs help.

'Of course I will help! I should be able to patch him up so we can get him to safety.'You inform Rhadrog that Dolenthol will suffice as a medic and ask if he is ready to depart.

'You told him? But, but... can we trust him with Viznak's secret?'

You nod and assure Rhadrog that Dolenthol is trustworthy.

'Good enough. We must leave at once!''Quickly! They may be under attack!''Hai, ${PLAYER}! How's my favourite bodyguard?'

Viznak is not quite his usual self due to his injuries, but he seems chipper enough. You ask him how he is feeling.

'Viznak won't let some bat bites slow him down, no way! Not when my bodyguards need me! I'll stay here with Faeron and Rhadrog until Blight Lady goes away. Stupid Blight Lady. She is the worst!

'You go inside, I'm sure Faeron wants to order you around some more! Haha! I'll stay out here, out of sight. Pretty sure the other Rangers aren't so nice to goblins.''Now that the crisis is averted, I will return my focus to the Halls of Black Lore. It is undoubtedly in the best interests of the Free Peoples that everything inside is removed from the world. However, we witnessed Lhaereth adding the recipe for her perfected plague to the archive.

'Do you know what a boon it would be to understand how it is made? Our scholars can study it, develop a cure, possibly even find a way to counteract it before it is even released! We must find a safe way inside the archive, and we must find that plague recipe!

'I will put all of my efforts into this task, but caution requires patience. I will find a way to contact you when the time is right, and then we will return to the Halls of Black Lore.'Ballistae along the Circle of Madness were abandoned when Gothmog's forces retreated higher into the city.Loaded ballistae can be found in the Circle of Madness.

You should fire loaded ballistae at flying merrevail.Fire loaded ballistae (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rhadrog is stationed inside Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to RhadrogYou have found a ballista, unmanned but loaded and ready to fire. You guess that these were prepared by Gothmog's forces for use against the merrevail, but Gothmog's men were forced to retreat before this one could be fired. You decide you may as well use them to shoot merrevail and great-bats out of the sky yourself!You tell Rhadrog of the ballistae and the merrevail you shot down.

'Oh ho! I did not know you were so comfortable with siegecraft, ${PLAYER}. I wonder if there are any more bolts lying around. I wouldn't mind taking a few shots at them myself!'Pages of a letter can be found scattered across Minas Morgul. Perhaps you can complete it if you find them all.Glowing Pages can be found in Minas Morgul.

You should collect the pages to complete the story.Collect Page 1Collect Page 2Collect Page 3Collect Page 4Collect Page 5Collect Page 6Collect Page 7Collect Page 8Collect Page 9Collect Page 10Collect Page 11Collect Page 12Collect Page 13Collect Page 14Collect Page 15Collect Page 16Collect Page 17Collect Page 18Collect Page 19Completed.CompletedYou have found what appears to be a page of a letter. The paper glows softly under the light of the moon, its words written with beautiful penmanship. Perhaps you can find other pages in Minas Morgul and complete the story.The story is complete. The letter appears to be from Lady Galadriel to Queen Valardis of Gondor shortly after the fall of Sauron at the end of the Second Age. It is clear that Galadriel had affection for both the Ithil Vale and the Men within, and from what history you know, she did achieve her aim of restoring it to glory before it once again fell into the hands of evil. Perhaps, once Gothmog is defeated, she will have another opportunity to restore this place to beauty.Rhadrog has a tale to share with you about Lhaereth's forces in Agarnaith, and the fate of Seregost.Rhadrog can be found in Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to RhadrogRhadrog would like to tell you the tale of how he left Agarnaith with Faeron.

You should complete 'The Red Sky Darkens'.Listen to Rhadrog's story in 'The Red Sky Darkens'Rhadrog can be found in Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to RhadrogFaeron can be found in Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to FaeronDolenthol can be found in Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Dolenthol.Talk to Dolenthol'We have reached another tier of the city, my friend! Let us talk to the Rangers here and see how we can help.''Are you comfortable? Let me begin my tale.''We caught up with the rest of the Rangers, entered the Morgul Vale with Captain Faramir, and were sent here as we, apparently, are the foremost experts on Lhaereth and the merrevail! I am unsure if that is really a title I should wear with pride.''Hail, ${PLAYER}. Has Rhadrog apprised you of the situation? Excellent.

'It is clear that Lhaereth is using this tier of the city as a base of operations to launch aerial assaults against the Tower of the Moon, where we believe Gothmog to be. But, I have a hunch she is also looking for something. This is the Circle of Madness today, but before the city fell to evil, this was the Circle of Wisdom, the center of lore and learning in Minas Ithil. There is certainly still wisdom to be found here.''${PLAYER}! I am quite happy to see you, my friend! Much has happened since we last saw you in Agarnaith. Faeron and I have come quite a long way in pursuit of Lhaereth's forces. Have a seat. I have quite a story to tell!''These Rangers seem very familiar with you, ${PLAYER}. Have you worked together before?'

You briefly describe your adventures with Faeron and Rhadrog in Agarnaith, but do not mention Viznak.

'So the three of you have already faced Lhaereth the Stained! It is reassuring that our enemy is not completely unknown to us. Let us begin our preparations to venture out into the Circle of Madness.'You have found strange flowers in the planters in the Circle of Madness. Who planted them here?Strange flowers can be found in the planters in the Circle of Madness.

You should collect strange flowers.Collect strange flowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Faeron is stationed inside Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to FaeronYou have found a strange flower growing among the planters in the Circle of Madness. They look slightly out of place. Were these planted by Gothmog's cultists or by the merrevail? It would be prudent to collect more and show them to the Rangers.You show Faeron the strange flowers you have gathered from the Circle of Madness.

'You were right to note these as out-of-place, ${PLAYER}. These are some of the same strange plants we discovered in Agarnaith. It can be no accident they have found their way here; the merrevail may have brought them and transplanted them on purpose. For what reason, I am unsure. Perhaps they are a component of the plague. We will be sure to destroy any more we find in the Circle of Madness.'Lhaereth's forces have set up plague cauldrons in the Circle of Madness. The cauldrons should be destroyed to protect the Rangers of Ithilien.Plague cauldrons have been setup in the Circle of Madness.

You should destroy plague cauldrons.Destroy plague cauldrons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rhadrog is stationed inside Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to Rhadrog'${PLAYER}, I fear that Lhaereth has brought her plague with her to Minas Morgul. I do not know if it is the same Great Plague you destroyed in Seregost, or some weaker strain she could concoct quickly, but we cannot take any chances! Destroy her cauldrons before the winged discover our encampments and use it on us instead of on Gothmog!''Did you destroy the cauldrons? Oh, that is a relief. I was not looking forward to contracting a deathly illness again!'A powerful and stealthy morvul stalks the Circle of Madness.Stalker Thimmoth can be found scouting the Circle of Madness.

You should defeat Stalker ThimmothDefeat Stalker ThimmothCompletedYou have found a powerful morvul scouting the Circle of Madness. Stalker Thimmoth should be defeated before he discovers the Ranger encampment in the Stargazers' Spire.Stalker Thimmoth has fallen by your hand, further protecting the Star-gazers' Spire from the eyes of your enemies.Weeping in endless torment, shades of the Dead wander the Circle of Despair.Sorrowful shades can be found throughout the Circle of Despair.

Dornach has asked you to try comforting the sorrowful spirits of Minas Morgul so they might reveal the fate which befell them.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: If you lose sight of Dornach, you can summon him to your side with his horn.</rgb>Dornach can be found in the Circle of Despair.

You have tried to comfort the sorrowful spirits of Minas Morgul. You should talk to Dornach.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: If you lose sight of Dornach, you can summon him to your side with his horn.</rgb>Talk to Dornach'One of the shades I saw sobbing in the Circle of Despair was a boy no older than seven years of age. What crime had that poor child committed in the eyes of the Enemy? What threat did he pose to the master of Mordor?

'What of the others like him? I have seen many sorrowful shades weeping in the dark corners of the Circle.

'Are they even real or are they just some cruel trick of the Dead City?

'Let us see if we can offer them even the slightest comfort, and perhaps we shall learn the truth of it.''The words of the Dead trouble me greatly, ${PLAYER}.

'There was once a great bath-house in the Circle of Mirth. Could this be where they met their ends?

'I can lead us there, but I fear what we will find.'Standing upon the Circle of Sorrow, Damrod believes that there is much to do in this cursed section of the city before it is safe for the Rangers to continue their infiltration of Minas Morgul.You should complete quests in the Circle of Sorrow in Minas Morgul.Complete quests in the Circle of Sorrow (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Damrod can be found in Barad Orhant in Minas Morgul.

You should talk to Damrod.Talk to Damrod in Barad OrhantThe entrance to Barad Elenath can be found in the Circle of Sorrow.

You should enter the tower of Barad Elenath.Complete 'Instance: Into the Circle of Madness'Enter Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' SpireRhadrog can be found in Barad Elenath in Minas Morgul.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to Rhadrog in Barad Elenath'The Circle of Sorrow is nothing like what we just encountered upon the Circle of Wrath. This place is not for the living. No, it is the realm of the Dead. Streams of spirits move through the streets and strange glowing lights flicker among the old buildings. There is much here that we need accomplish before it is safe to delve further into the city.

'You should assist those you can in the Circle of Sorrow. When you have done this, return to me and we shall continue our efforts.''It is good to see you, ${PLAYER}. Thank you for helping us secure the tower of Barad Elenath.

'Damrod believes my time in Agarnaith has best qualified me to deal with the threat of Lhaereth the Stained and her minions. I am glad you have also come to this Circle of Madness, though the name is ill-starred.''You have done well to combat the Dead upon the Circle of Sorrow. I ask you now to continue upwards, further into the city. I will stay here and continue to command the Rangers in these first two circles. Our scouts have found a nearby tower that should bring us to the next level: the Circle of Madness.

'Seek out the tower of Barad Elenath. We have all seen the merrevail flying above. It is certain they have already taken the next circle and the towers that stand upon it! When you are ready, prepare to take hold of Barad Elenath!'You have discovered evidence that Lhaereth is searching for a place called The Halls of Black Lore within the Circle of Madness.Faeron has asked for you to search for the Halls of Black Lore. They are somewhere in the Circle of Madness in Minas Morgul.

You should find the Halls and then signal the Rangers by <rgb=#FF00FF>whistling</rgb>.Faeron and the rest of the Rangers have moved to the Halls of Black Lore.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to FaeronThe time has come to enter the Halls of Black Lore.

Speak to Rhadrog near the Halls to begin 'Instance: Nothing for Nothing'.Speak to Rhadrog to complete 'Instance: Nothing for Nothing'Rhadrog has returned to Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Rhadrog.Talk to Rhadrog'We must seek the Halls of Black Lore, but I know not where they lie within the Circle of Madness. The four of us must search for it, and then enter together when it is found. I will be sending Dolenthol on this mission as well. I have only worked with him for a short time, but his ingenuity and enthusiasm have impressed me.

'Should you find the Halls, give the signal, and we will find you. Good luck out there.''Excellent work, ${PLAYER}. Steel yourself; we know not what lies inside, but we will soon discover what Lhaereth seeks.

'A word of warning: We know of what the Lady of Blight is capable. The four of us are no match for her. If she has arrived before us, do not engage.''It is time, then? Ugh... I do hope Lhaereth is not inside....''${PLAYER}! You made it back! Was that really Viznak? If it was, we have left him to die! Oh no, I will never be able to live with myself if Lhaereth got him. You know, I really am quite fond of that goblin. Don't tell Faeron I said that.

'Wait, Faeron is not with you? Where is he?!'Morvul stalkers prowl the Circle of Madness, hidden from sight and extremely dangerous.Morvul stalkers are hidden somewhere in the Circle of Madness.

You should defeat morvul stalkers.Defeat morvul stalkers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The morvul stalkers have been defeated, eliminating one more threat to the Rangers of Ithilien.Completed.You have encountered a morvul stalker, a hidden and deadly foe, among the rest of the merrevail in the Circle of Madness.  There are surely others, and as long as they persist, they are a threat to the Rangers.The morvul stalkers have been defeated, eliminating one more threat to the Rangers of Ithilien. You hope, but are somewhat certain they will not be, the last unseen enemy you face in the Morgul Vale.The Circle of Madness has been invaded by Lhaereth's forces. Faeron thinks it prudent to discover what it is the winged-folk seek.Dolenthol can be found in Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Dolenthol.Talk to DolentholFaeron has asked you to find merrevail lieutenants and uncover orders from Lhaereth.

You should find and defeat Ogolúm, Durthúl, and Rhanarch within the Circle of Madness.Collect Orders from OgolúmCollect Orders from DurthúlCollect Orders from RhanarchFaeron can be found in Barad Elenath, the Star-gazers' Spire.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron'I have a task for you, if you are prepared to venture forth.

'We are few here, and are lacking in information more than anything else. In enemy territory, information is the most valuable commodity. I need someone to find and remove lieutenants of the enemy and search them for any orders from Lhaereth. I believe you are best suited to this task as you have experience fighting the merrevail, and you are among the most skilled in our forces.'

You ask Faeron if he has a task in mind for Dolenthol, as you can vouch for his skill in combat.

'Dolenthol? I do not believe I am acquainted with him. Is he the Ithilien Ranger who arrived with you? If you say he is skilled, I will have him assist me with establishing message and supply lines with the lower tiers and Faramir's camp. Worry not, I will allow no harm to come to your friend.''Faeron will have me assist with establishing supply lines? Not quite the mission I was hoping for, but I understand the need. Be safe out there, ${PLAYER}. I will see you when you return.''Welcome back, ${PLAYER}. What have you discovered?'

You show Faeron the orders Lhaereth issued to her lieutenants. He reads them with a puzzled expression.

'I was correct, she is looking for something, but I do not know who or what Morloth is. The Halls of Black Lore, however... I have heard rumours of a great repository of dark knowledge held by the Witch-king somewhere in the city; this must be the same hall. If Lhaereth seeks this place, we must seek it as well.'Ugrukhôr's Orcs have made a series of unsuccessful forays into the Circle of Despair, and Dornach hopes they might still hold orders from the Captain of Pit.Slain Orcs and Uruk-hai can be found in the Circle of Despair.

You should search fallen Orcs and Uruk-hai for orders from Ugrukhôr.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: If you lose sight of Dornach, you can summon him to your side with his horn.</rgb>Search slain Orcs and uruks for orders from UgrukhôrSlain Orcs and Uruk-hai can be found in the Circle of Despair.

You should search fallen Orcs and Uruk-hai for orders from Ugrukhôr.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: If you lose sight of Dornach, you can summon him to your side with his horn.</rgb>Search slain Orcs and Uruk-hai for orders from UgrukhôrDornach can be found in the Circle of Despair.

You have found orders from Ugrukhôr carried by a slain Uruk-hai. You should deliver them to Dornach.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: If you lose sight of Dornach, you can summon him to your side with his horn.</rgb>'It seems Ugrukhôr's forces do not know what to make of the Circle of Despair.

'Many of them have fallen to the cruel shades that linger here, and it is likely that some among those that have fallen might still carry orders from Ugrukhôr. If we can learn more of what he has planned in his war against Gothmog, we might better counter the both of them.

'We should search the slain while we explore the Circle.''According to these orders, Ugrukhôr's forces make for Barad Cúron at all cost.

'Lhaereth and her merrevail shall serve to harry Gothmog's forces, and Ugrukhôr's forces will shatter the doors of the Tower of the Crescent Moon. When that is done, there is little hope of escape for Gothmog, but it seems foolhardy to treat his downfall as a certainty.

'There is little mention of the Rangers in these orders... so perhaps they do not think us a threat. That will prove to be a grave mistake, I think!'You have received an urgent summons from Dolenthal to aid Prince Faramir in the heights of the Dead City.Dolenthol can be found somewhere in the Circle of Death.

Candúr has directed you to find Dolenthol in the Circle of Death and join Prince Faramir's company.Find Dolenthol in the Circle of DeathDolenthol can be found in the Circle of Death.

You should talk to Dolenthol.Talk to Dolenthol in the Circle of Death'As soon as we entered the Circle, Gothmog's Orcs were upon us. Prince Faramir and a few of his men were able to press deeper into the city, but many of our number were separated in the fighting.

'I do not know how many we have lost... and I do not know if the Prince still lives. If you would help me to find the others, I can lead the search for Faramir myself.

'If you find any lost bands of Rangers in the Circle of Death, tell them to meet us before the Tower of the Crescent Moon. If you don't... that is where you will find us.''I take it Candúr delivered my message, ${PLAYER}?'Ayorzén has agreed to help you gain access to Barad Cúron, but even with the Easterling's aid this mission will not be free of danger.Ayorzén is at the Eagle and Star in Minas Tirith.Talk to Ayorzén at the Eagle and StarBeregond is at Echad Uial, in Imlad Morgul.Talk to Beregond in Echad UialAyorzén has come to Echad Uial, in Imlad Morgul.Talk to Ayorzén at Echad UialMithrandir has returned to Echad Uial, in Imlad Morgul.Talk to Mithrandir in Echad UialOrcs and other foes remain a danger within Minas Morgul, and you should thin their numbers before setting Mithrandir's plan into motion.Defeat Orcs in Minas Morgul (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat the Dead in Minas Morgul (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat merrevail in Minas Morgul (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Culang is standing watch near the entrance to Barad Cúron on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul.Talk to Culang on the uppermost tier of Minas MorgulAyorzén is on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul.Talk to Ayorzén on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul'Mithrandir has told me of his plan, and I will not speak against it. But I urge you to remain alert, ${PLAYER}, for many centuries have passed since the Tower of the Crescent Moon last fell into the grasp of evil. Even Mithrandir cannot say what you might find within. Gothmog alone would be threat enough, but while Sauron held our attention Gothmog had time enough to ransack Minas Morgul for its secrets; he may have prepared some defence or trap we cannot anticipate.

'I will give you one further warning. If the Khundolar Ayorzén does agree to help you with this, remain alert for any sign of treachery. I believe he will aid you for as long as his interests align with yours; see that they do and he may be worthy of trust. But if that becomes otherwise...

'Good fortune to you, ${PLAYER}. I think you will need it.''Good fortune to you, ${PLAYER}. I think you will need it.''I will play the part of Karazgar, ${PLAYER}, but Ayorzén the Helpful has a number of conditions.

'Know this: I will not endanger myself to save you! If this deception appears ready to fail, I will not maintain it. You may be a friend to me in this city, but in the Morgul Vale the only men who still have friends are the dead ones. The living cannot stay living if they hold friendship more dearly than their own skin. Ayorzén must come first. Are we agreed?

'Yes? Very well. I did not escape the end of the war and the dungeons of the Dark Lord to die in the Morgul Vale. If that is understood, then I will help you. Where do we need to go?''Let us go to this camp, then. Ayorzén is not afraid.''I do not like this Easterling, ${PLAYER}. Are you certain we have no other choice?''I do not like this Easterling. I do not want him troubling Prince Faramir.''The men here do not like me, ${PLAYER}. It brings a smile to my face to hear their whispers, to see the way they look at me with fear in their eyes. Why? Because it means they remember. Even fallen so low, Ayorzén is not forgotten. If the cards had fallen differently, these others might be unwelcome guests in my own war-camp, and they would not be free to whisper then. This man, this Beregond? He is a mere underling. But Ayorzén was no man's underling.

'I remember leading my own band, strong Khundolar warriors who would live or die at my command. Perhaps that time will come again, for I feel my star is once again on the rise! After all, you will soon be indebted to me for this favour, as will many other powerful men. Why, Beregond's own prince will be in my debt, and perhaps then he will speak to me with more respect.

'Call me Ayorzén the Prepared, ${PLAYER}, for I am ready; ready for this plan of yours, and for a hopeful future.''I am ready. Are you not?''Something has happened on the upper levels of the city, ${PLAYER}, but I know not what. Merrevail were seen clinging to the upper reaches of Barad Cúron, illuminated in the light of the tower's beacon, and something has stirred the Orcs in the lower levels to greater activity. Of the risen Dead in the middle tiers there are few reports, but that in itself gives me concern.

'Go with Ayorzén to speak with the Ranger Culang on the uppermost level of the city, outside the entrance to the tower, but first investigate each of these threats as you climb up each tier of the city. If Gothmog or the other Masters of Mordor have called for this increased activity on the part of their minions, they must be opposed.

'Have they enacted the final stages of some scheme, even as we prepare to undertake our own? I hope it is not so, but an air of dread and mystery lies upon Minas Morgul and it makes me wonder.''Go with Ayorzén to speak with the Ranger Culang on the uppermost tier of the city, outside the entrance to the tower, but first investigate each of these threats as you climb up each level of the city. If Gothmog or the other Masters of Mordor have called for this increased activity on the part of their minions, they must be opposed.''Kill them all, kill every one!'

Beneath the mask of Karazgar, you hear Ayorzén giggling to himself.

'Do you not find me more convincing than the Weeping Warrior himself?''Something has been happening in the upper levels of the tower, ${PLAYER}. A short time ago a flight of merrevail descended upon the beacon. I could not see from here what they tried to do, but whatever it was they did not achieve it; there was a great flash and a sound as of thunder, and dozens of them fell in torment.

'It has been quiet since then, but I do not trust the silence. Be careful if you still intend to carry out Mithrandir's plan.''It has been quiet ever since the merrevail attacked, but I do not trust the silence. Be careful if you still intend to carry out Mithrandir's plan.''I have never seen the inside of this city before now. It is not as frightening as your people led me to expect, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they are all cowards after all?'The time has come to deal with Gothmog. With Ayorzén's help, you hope to gain access to the Tower of the Crescent Moon and your long-time foe hiding within.Ayorzén is on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul, willing to help you gain access to the Tower of the Crescent Moon.Talk to Ayorzén on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul and complete 'Instance: Gothmog's Rage'The nearby presence of Isildur's bones has allowed his shade to assume physical form in Barad Cúron.Talk to the shade of Isildur in Barad Cúron'But I am no coward, and I am ready to play my part in this foolhardy scheme. Are you as prepared as Ayorzén the Courageous?''I will do my best to get you beyond the guardians of the tower, ${PLAYER}, but I will not die for you.

'We will meet back here if we get separated.''I sense the presence of my bones, ${PLAYER}! They are nearby!'The shade of Isildur can feel the nearby presence of his bones, and wants you to find them.The shade of Isildur believes his bones can be found in the room beneath the beacon atop Barad Cúron.Find the room of the beacon in Barad CúronThe shade of Isildur believes his bones can be found in the room beneath the beacon atop Barad Cúron.Examine the Oath-stone of DúathDefeat invading merrevail in the room of the Oath-stone (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The shade of Isildur believes his bones can be found in the room beneath the beacon atop Barad Cúron.Find the casket containing Isildur's bonesThe shade of Isildur waits for you to bring the casket containing his bones to the throne room in the tower.The shade of Isildur waits in the throne room of Barad Cúron.Talk to the shade of Isildur in the throne room of Barad CúronGothmog slumps against the wall in the throne room of Barad Cúron.Talk to Gothmog in the throne room of Barad Cúron'We are so near! I can see the chamber where my bones must lie, for the Oath-stone stands there too, 'neath the beacon! Find the casket containing my bones, ${PLAYER}, and bring it to me!

'Too long have I remained bound to this place. The rest I seek will elude me no longer!''The casket containing my bones is somewhere within the tower! Find it and bring it to me!''Just dust, now, but unmistakable. I thank you for this, my friend.

'The enchantments of Gultháva, the Maiden of the Gladden, kept me restrained for the long years following my death. The slumber with which she held me gave me no rest, and it did not ease the torment of the vow I left unfulfilled. There was no peace in that sleep, though the blanket with which she encircled me was woven in love.

'I swore to bring Sauron's Ring to Rivendell, and though that weapon came in the end to that valley, I did not. But now... this casket of dust gives me hope. Bring my earthly remains to Rivendell and I shall at last know peace.''What so amuses this coward?''Peace? What peace does this shade think to find? What peace does he deserve? I remember... I remember the tales of Elendil, and of Isildur and Anárion his sons. We were told they were great men, valiant warriors from an age of heroes. But what victories did they ever know? What lasting triumph was ever theirs?

'None. There was no enduring triumph. Only evil lasts. Only evil can return... again and again. No good man can stand against it. In the end, even the greatest of heroes will succumb. What was Isildur's reward? A casket of dust, and regret unending.

'But that is not to be my own reward. My Master gave me the greatest of gifts, and it is from that gift I draw strength even as my life's end draws near. It was this: I feel no regret. Everything I have done, I would do again. If I am to become dust, as did this poor excuse for a king, let it choke the air from all who breathe it in! As they fall, gasping for breath, it will serve as one final reminder of Gothmog, one last remembrance of Mordirith, and I will count my own victory complete!'Gothmog believes he is most deserving of Thuringwath's secrets, but his enemies encircle the tower and wish to challenge him for them.Culang is in the throne room of Barad Cúron.Talk to Culang in the throne room of Barad CúronCulang is in the throne room of Barad Cúron, concerned about the sound of newcomers approaching.Talk to Culang in the throne room and complete 'Instance: A Vengeful Heart'Culang is in the throne room of Barad Cúron.Talk to Culang in the throne room of Barad Cúron'My enemies seek access to the gorge of Thuringwath, but they are unworthy of the secrets within. That place was enwreathed with spells, kept from the eyes of all but Sauron's most trusted servants. My Master could claim the right, as could the others of his order, but the Nazgûl are gone to the void and will not return. None but Gothmog now has the right. Have I not paid the greatest price of all?''I paid the greatest price. Only I am worthy to possess Thuringwath's secrets.''I cannot tell from his words if he found aught of use in that place, ${PLAYER}. His wound looks to be mortal, but he was kept alive by dark arts, and I cannot say for certain if it be so. If he is dying, let him die! The sooner his evil is gone from this world, the better!

'He claims to deserve whatever weapon lies within Thuringwath. Did he find it or no?'

Culang draws a sharp breath.

'Did you hear that?''Did you hear that? Someone comes!''${PLAYER}, have you escaped serious injury? Annoth was not so fortunate. He has achieved the vengeance he sought, though it arrived not in the manner he sought. Let him rest now, and may the Thandrim for whom he grieved find peace with the death of Ugrukhôr.

'And so the Captain of the Pit is undone by the sword of Annoth, wielded by Gothmog, and now both Ugrukhôr and Gothmog are undone. Is that not the nature of evil? Treacherous even to its own. None shall mourn for either of the slain.'Death has come to Barad Cúron and claimed two of the masters of Mordor. How many countless others have perished in that cruel throne room over the years?Gothmog is in the throne room of Barad Cúron, trying to speak despite his wounds.Talk to Gothmog in the throne room of Barad CúronThe shade of Isildur is in the throne room of Barad Cúron.Talk to the shade of Isildur in the throne room of Barad CúronThe shade of Isildur has asked you to bring the casket containing the dust of his bones out of Barad Cúron.Pick up the casket of dust in the throne roomAyorzén is waiting for you on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul.Meet up with Ayorzén outside Barad Cúron, on the uppermost tier of Minas MorgulYou did not find Ayorzén at the meeting-place, and should look for him at Echad Uial.Look for Ayorzén at Echad UialMithrandir is at Echad Uial.Talk to Mithrandir at Echad UialPrince Faramir is at Echad Uial.Talk to Faramir at Echad UialMithrandir is at Echad Uial.'By the Tree of Gondor! Gothmog clings to life. He tries to speak, ${PLAYER}, and we should listen. Is it not said that dying men rarely speak falsely with their last breath?''Mordor should have been... should have been mine. No one endured... what I endured. Not Ugrukhôr... not Dulgabêth... not the pestilential queen of... Agarnaith. A curse on them all... but I levy my worst upon Isildur who could have prevented it and did not! He calls me coward? He knows nothing of the torments that made me! From that crucible of evil I was born anew, the greatest creation of Angmar! I was to usher in a new age!'

The fire in Gothmog's eyes flickers and burns low.

'Why could I not, ${PLAYER}? There were so many chances, but... the Ranger and the Elf-maid...'

Gothmog stares at the floor, his life's essence departing.

'Narmeleth. I think Narmeleth knew the same torments as I. How could she fight... longer than...?'

He falls silent, and it seems as if he will speak no more. As you are about to turn and leave, he reaches out to you, a trinket held in his clenched fist.

'Listen to me, ${PLAYER}. There was a Morgul-slave who knew the secrets of this place. At my Master's command... he forged a key. "Only this weapon cannot be overcome," the dwarf said to me. I spent the time I could... seeking it... but it eluded me. I give it to you, ${PLAYER}. If Mordor cannot be mine... let it be no one's! Find this weapon and use it... against all who seek to master the realm that was denied me!''Even with his last breath he raged against Gondor, and shamed his people! He did not repent of his evil misdeeds. He claimed to regret nothing? His words speak falsely to me; it is clear he felt regret only at his failure to fulfil Sauron's commands and achieve the victory of Mordor!

'He should have resisted, ${PLAYER}, and died as Eärnur. My brother Anárion would have fought the torments of the Lord of the Nazgûl, and embraced death rather than succumb to such evil. So too would I. But instead, he became a tool for evil, and he dies as Gothmog. Let him rot where he lies.

'I do not want my bones to remain here any longer. Pick up the casket of dust and bring it from the tower.''Let Gothmog rot where he lies. He shamed his people.''We should leave the tower and meet up with Ayorzén at the meeting-place. It was north of the tower entrance, on the uppermost tier of Minas Morgul, wasn't it?''You have returned, presumably in victory? Tell me what transpired in Barad Cúron, ${PLAYER}.'

You briefly relate the events within the tower to Mithrandir, and the Wizard nods with understanding at the death of Annoth.

'That is all too often the result of a vengeful heart, my friend. It did not take a Wizard's sight to see that Annoth might not walk back down that road. But what of Ugrukhôr and Gothmog? And did Ayorzén prove able in the battle? Our Khundolar friend has not entered the tale since you passed beyond the guardians at the entrance of the tower.'

You conclude your story, and tell Mithrandir that Ayorzén did not meet you at the place you arranged.

'Hmm. I have not seen him here either. Perhaps Faramir or his scouts know something.''Perhaps Faramir or his scouts know something of Ayorzén.''Ayorzén? My scouts did see him, ${PLAYER}, but they had no good tidings to relate. They last saw him moving eastward at great speed, bearing the mask of Karazgar about his person. He passed into Gorgoroth and has not been seen since.

'I spoke with Beregond. He believes that Ayorzén will use the mask of Karazgar to rally Men of the East to his banner, becoming once again the commander he wished to be. Did he say his star was on the rise? Perhaps so, and perhaps not. I should not envy Ayorzén his position if the true Weeping Warrior should come seeking the pretender!''I should not envy Ayorzén his position if the true Weeping Warrior should come seeking the pretender!''Ayorzén has gone beyond our reach, for now. His deception was not entirely unexpected to me, but I wished he might act otherwise. The price of his aid is clearer now, but I deem his help to be worthwhile, all the same.

'I will take this casket containing the dust of Isildur's bones and arrange with King Elessar for a waggon to bring it north. I believe its arrival at Rivendell will at last fulfil Isildur's oath, and grant him rest.

'I will also examine this broken key. If it truly unlocks the door to "a weapon that cannot be overcome" we will need to treat it with care. Even at the end, Gothmog sought death and despair for his enemies, and we must not forget that he counted us in that group. This broken key could be one last trap, one final act of vengeance against those who served him his ultimate defeat.

'I have exhausted my study of the Black Book. Vóin has told us what he can, and it is with a measure of sorrow that I close it for good. I will turn my attention now to this broken key and Gothmog's final words, but you should take a moment to rest and recover your strength. Much of the evil within Minas Morgul has been opposed this day, and defeated! For that you should celebrate!'

<rgb=#00FF00>The Epilogue will become available from Mithrandir at Echad Uial once you have completed Chapters 1-14 of the Black Book of Mordor</rgb>Gothmog gave you a broken key as he died, but you do not know what it might have unlocked when whole. Unless your guess is wrong, it seems as if Gothmog himself did not know.You must find someone who knows what the broken key might once have unlocked. But who could hold that secret?Find someone who knows about the broken keyIs it possible that Khîl Tale-spinner might know something about the broken key given to you by Gothmog?Metalworkers can be found on the lower level of Járnfast.Talk to a metalworker by the forges on the lower level of JárnfastKhîl Tale-spinner is by the forges in Járnfast.Talk to Khîl Tale-spinner by the forges on the lower level of JárnfastThe metalworkers are by the forges on the lower level of Járnfast.The metalworker needs some time to fashion a new key using the mould provided by Khîl Tale-spinner.Wait for the metalworker to forge a key using the mouldThe metalworker is by the forges on the lower level of Járnfast. He should have finished forging the key by now.Talk to the metalworker by the forges in JárnfastKhîl Tale-spinner has returned to the hidden chamber built by his thrice-great grandfather Vóin.Talk to Khîl in the hidden chamber in his homeKhîl told you about a tiny locked box that he thought his mother used to store jewels, but perhaps it is far older than that...

He has placed it on the table in the hidden chamber, and waits for you to try the newly-forged key in its lock.Unlock the tiny locked box in the hidden chamberKhîl Tale-spinner is in the hidden chamber built by his thrice-great grandfather Vóin.Talk to Khîl in the hidden chamber in his homeGandalf intended to travel north with the casket containing the bones of Isildur, and should have arrived at the Beorninghús by now. You should bring him the glass shard you found within the tiny locked box from Khîl's home.'I see the matter of the broken key has captured your interest, as it has mine. I have studied it closely, but that examination revealed no distinguishing marks upon its surface, and no clue to its origin. The top of the key appears to have been broken off, but several teeth remain. Not enough to match the key with a particular lock, however, and unlikely to open it should we chance upon the correct one. Pity.

'I will give it back to you, with the hope that you may find someone who knows about this key, or others like it.'

Gandalf leans on his staff and looks away over the encampment.

'I have been thinking about Gothmog's words. He said he was given this key by a Morgul-slave, and it would unlock a weapon that could not be overcome? I cannot imagine what this weapon might be, but we must not let it fall into the hands of evil. And there is another thing, ${PLAYER}. I get the sense that Gothmog himself did not know what this key might unlock. If he knew the location of such a weapon, would he not claim it at once? But he told you he searched in vain.

'There is something peculiar about all this. Good luck, friend.''Gothmog said he was given this key by a Morgul-slave, and it would unlock a weapon that could not be overcome? I cannot imagine what this weapon might be, but we must not let it fall into the hands of evil. And there is another thing, ${PLAYER}. I get the sense that Gothmog himself did not know what this key might unlock. If he knew the location of such a weapon, would he not claim it at once? But he told you he searched in vain.

'There is something peculiar about all this. Good luck, friend.''Have you learned anything about the broken key?''What a surprise, my friend! I did not expect to see you again, and because we are so close I will forgive your unheralded intrusion into my home! Ah, just a jest, a jest! You are welcome here at any time. How goes the examination of the Black Book written by my thrice-great grandfather Vóin? Do not forget that it belongs to me, and if it proves valuable I would welcome payment equivalent to its value.'

Khîl gestures at the runes on the walls.

'When we first discovered this place it frightened me, but I have found myself coming in here more and more often. I find that the aura here calms and relaxes me. It is not so for Lord Náin! He is still not happy about this chamber, but it is so old and my claim to this home of my forebears is so clear that he dares not do anything about it. For that the Lord of the Iron Hills has my respect, and the stillness of my tongue. Many are the stories I might tell of him were it otherwise, true tales all, though he might not agree. So everyone is happy!

'But what brings you here, ${PLAYER}?'

You show Khîl the broken key, and he studies it carefully.

'Do you know something? I think I have seen this shape before. I cannot be certain, but... yes, it is worth a try! It will take me some time to dig it out again, but if you go to one of the metalworkers of Járnfast, I will meet you once I find it. Wait for me by the forges on the lower level of Járnfast, ${PLAYER}. There you will find the best, and the fastest, of Járnfast's metalworkers.''I think I have seen this shape before. I cannot be certain, but... yes, it is worth a try! It will take me some time to dig it out again, but if you go to one of the metalworkers of Járnfast, I will meet you once I find it. Wait for me by the forges on the lower level of Járnfast. There you will find the best, and the fastest, of Járnfast's metalworkers.''Yes, what can I do for you?'

The metalworker looks at you with surprise.

'You mean you don't know? Oh, you're waiting for someone. Khîl Tale-spinner? This suddenly makes a great deal of sense. That Zhélruka...''I found it, ${PLAYER}! When I was cleaning out my father's home, I chanced upon this mould and a small locked box. I thought little of these artifacts, and put them away with the rest of the odds and ends. But look at this!'

Khîl holds out a small casting mould with a hollow in the shape of a key. The broken key given to you by Gothmog rests comfortably within the indentation.

'Using this mould, the metalworkers here could fashion a duplicate of this key, and I warrant it will unlock the box I found, or I am no Zhélruka! Give the mould and these instructions to this fine dwarf, and once the key is forged I will meet you back in the hidden chamber!''I will meet you back at the hidden chamber when the key is forged!''This should not take long. I will have a new key forged for you in just a short while.''Disturb me not while I work! This may look easy, but I assure you it is not!''Here you go. May this key unlock whatever it is you seek to unlock.

'If the container be of Zhélruka make, perhaps it is better for me to hope that this key will properly lock it away, and rid us of whatever madness Khîl Tale-spinner weaves now!''My work is finished. I suppose you should bring the newly-forged key to Khîl Tale-spinner.''The key is forged? Then let us see if it opens the box I found in the same place as the mould. I assumed the box belonged to my mother and contained her decorative jewels, but perhaps it is not so.

'I have set it on the table there.''Well? Does the key fit in the lock or not?''A shard of glass, no more than a hand's breadth in size? This is the treasure? I do not know what I expected, ${PLAYER}, but I expected more than this! It seems quite ordinary. Why would anyone lock this away, and then break the key?

'You told me you were acquainted with Gandalf the Wizard. Perhaps he will know what to make of this broken glass, if there is anything to make of it. I fear you will be wasting his time, but if you think he will not mind it you should ask him anyway. Where has he gone?'

You tell Khîl that Gandalf recently spent some time in the lands far to the south, but the Wizard intended to journey north once again alongside a waggon. He is likely to be at the Beorninghús by now.'I think you will be wasting the Wizard's time with this. It is just a piece of broken glass. How disappointing!''The bones of Isildur have come part of the way, but I travelled ahead of the waggon to once again test Grimbeorn's hospitality. He is prone to grumbling, but the meals he serves are without compare on this side of the Misty Mountains. Did you learn anything about the broken key given to you by Gothmog?'

You tell Gandalf what transpired in Járnfast and show him the glass shard you obtained from the locked box in Khîl's home. He studies it closely.

'Hmm... a broken piece of glass? This does not appear to be a weapon of any kind. Is it possible Gothmog's key might have unlocked more than one container? Unless...'

Gandalf holds the shard up to his eye and squints through it.

'The glass is cloudy, as if it contains something which obscures my vision. It moves from side to side as I stare at it, almost like...'

Gandalf looks up suddenly, and clutches at the Black Book hanging from his belt. He flips it open and peers through the shard of glass at one weathered page.

'There is hidden writing here, ${PLAYER}! It is as I suspected from the very outset! I did not think we could find the means to reveal it, but you have done so!'Together, you and Gandalf have managed to reveal the last of the secrets hidden within the Black Book of Mordor, and the only remaining question concerns the matter of its final disposition.Gandalf has presented you with a choice concerning the ultimate disposition of the Black Book of Mordor.Deliver the Black Book to Khîl Tale-spinner in his homeDeliver the Black Book to Covadil in the Houses of LoreDeliver the Black Book to Karazgar by the flat stone at the Old FordGandalf gave you the choice of where to bring the Black Book and you have now performed that duty.'Yes, ${PLAYER}, I have been expecting you. Together we have unearthed every secret hidden within the Black Book of Mordor by Vóin and Bozhéna, the long-distant dwarves who authored it. Only one question remains: what is to be done with the book now?

'I see a number of possibilities, my friend. It was Khîl Tale-spinner who gave the book into our keeping, and his claim is perhaps the strongest. Vóin and Bozhéna were his thrice-great grandparents, and if the book is to be kept by anyone still living today in Middle-earth, we might do worse than a Zhélruka relative of its authors.

'But there is another interest to be considered. The Black Book of Mordor contains a great deal of information that would be prized by scholars concerning the history of Gondor and the fate of King Eärnur. The book is truly deserving of a place of honour in the Houses of Lore in Minas Tirith. The scholar Covadil would gratefully accept the donation of it, and in that way Vóin's wish for Eärnur's struggle to be remembered in the future will truly be fulfilled.

'There is one other claim to the Black Book, ${PLAYER}. Indeed, the nobility of this claim might be doubted, but I believe it is still worth considering. I speak of the claim of Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, who asked that you steal the book and deliver it to him. Yes, I knew of your encounter with him at the Old Ford. I chose not to say anything, but I am pleased my trust in you was not misplaced, and we were able to decipher the last of the book's mysteries.

'Why then might we entertain Karazgar's request, ${PLAYER}? Why, it is for that very reason: we know what the book says, and he does not. He believes it contains the secret to preventing his decline, or securing his power... but it does not. It does contain a great deal of confusion and mystery for one who does not know how to read it, and that is to say nothing of the trick with the shard of beacon-glass. What might he give up in exchange for a book he thinks will be of use to him, but is not, and cannot be? It may be an academic question, but those are always of great interest to me.

'So I leave the disposition of the Black Book of Mordor to you, my friend. Choose who should have it, and deliver it either to Khîl Tale-spinner in Járnfast, Covadil in the Houses of Lore in Minas Tirith, or Karazgar at the Old Ford. I leave the decision up to you, and with this choice we will close the Black Book of Mordor for good. I thank you again, ${PLAYER}.''I leave the disposition of the Black Book of Mordor to you, my friend. Choose who should have it, and deliver it either to Khîl Tale-spinner in Járnfast, Covadil in the Houses of Lore in Minas Tirith, or Karazgar at the Old Ford. I leave the decision up to you, and with this choice we will close the Black Book of Mordor for good. I thank you again, ${PLAYER}.'You stand at the Old Ford and resolve to wait for Karazgar to appear.

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Welcome back, my friend! Were you able to solve the mystery of the glass-shard and Vóin's book? Did Gandalf have any ideas?'

For a number of hours you describe what you learned from the secret writing in the Black Book, and conclude at last that your need for the tome has ended.

'You are giving it back to me? Well, I thank you for this! It seems that it properly belongs here in the chamber Vóin built, and with your assurance that it is not truly a tome of evil, I should think it will be quite safe to keep it here!

'Like my thrice-great grandmother Bozhéna, I too have found the aura of this hidden chamber to be one of peace and comfort. I am quite pleased to know more of these relatives of mine, who once were mere names, but now are much more than that! And to think my thrice-great grandfather Vóin defied the Witch-king himself, and not only that, but he may have at last ended his days in the halls of Thafar-gathol itself!

'Now that is a tale, ${PLAYER}. That is a tale indeed! Thank you for upholding your end of the bargain and delivering the Black Book back into my keeping, where it belongs. You will be my friend for always, good ${RACE}.''Hello again, my friend! Did the kingsfoil I give to you cure your headaches? I have some more here if you... oh, you are not here for kingsfoil? What brings you then to the Houses of Lore?'

You explain to Covadil that you have come into possession of a book that has much to say concerning the history of Gondor and you would like it to find a place on the shelves among other such tomes. You hand her the glass shard and begin to describe the account contained within the pages of the Black Book, but before you have gone very far Covadil interrupts with an apologetic smile.

'It isn't really up to me, but I can give the book to the masters, and they will study it for acceptability. If they deem it merits inclusion, and truly does contain knowledge of use to Gondor, they will add it to the collection.'

You wonder aloud whether any of the scholars in the Houses of Lore can read ancient Zhélruka runes.

'Oh, I doubt it,' she responds. 'I think I will just add it to the shelves myself. If you say this contains important historical knowledge, I believe you! None today can say what might be important to know for tomorrow! I say we preserve this tome for future scholars to mull over!'

Covadil takes the Black Book from you and carefully tucks the glass shard inside the cover.

'I look forward to reading it, ${PLAYER}! I have been trying to decide which language I should next set my sights upon, and it seems I have received my course: the runes of the ancient Zhélruka! Thank you once again, my friend! The Black Book will remain here in the Houses of Lore, waiting to enrich the knowledge of future interested scholars, and I am glad to have played a part in it!'Through the telling of his tale, the shade of Tárandil has achieved the clarity he had lost. It is time for another of the shades that has remained overlong in Middle-earth to find his own respite.The shade of Isildur is by the waggon bearing his bones northward, in Laerlad.Talk to the shade of Isildur by the waggon in LaerladThe shade of Tárandil has made his decision: he will seek out Bravantel and Héol, no matter the torment it will cause him.The shade of Tárandil fades from viewGandalf is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward, in Laerlad.Talk to Gandalf by the waggon in LaerladGandalf suggested you catch up with the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur when it arrives at the entrance to the valley of Rivendell.Meet up with Gandalf and the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur at the entrance to the valley of RivendellThe shade of Isildur has at last finished the journey he swore in life to undertake more than three thousand years ago.Talk to the shade of Isildur at the entrance to RivendellThe shade of Isildur has at last finished the journey he swore in life to undertake more than three thousand years ago.The shade of Isildur fades from viewGandalf is by the waggon bearing Isildur's bones to Rivendell, by the entrance to that secret valley.Talk to Gandalf by the waggon near the entrance to RivendellGandalf is by the waggon bearing Isildur's bones to Rivendell, by the entrance to that secret valley. He has finished reading the secret writing in the Black Book of Mordor, and wishes to tell you the end of that tale.Talk to Gandalf by the entrance to Rivendell and complete 'Instance: Freed of the Burden'Gandalf is by the waggon bearing Isildur's bones to Rivendell, by the entrance to that secret valley.Talk to Gandalf by the waggon near the entrance to Rivendell'I desired to confess my treachery, and I have done so. There remains but one thing for me.

'I will seek for my wife and child, for Bravantel and Héol, no matter where they may be. I would suffer any hurt to see them again, and to know their fate, whether it be doom undeterred or rest finally achieved. Do they linger still in this world? I have to know. If I must consign myself to mindless oblivion in the effort, so be it. I face it without fear.'

Tárandil's form wavers uncertainly, and he addresses Isildur for the last time.

'Father, I do not seek your pardon. I know it could never be granted, and I would not ask it. But I wanted you to know how sorry I am. I have forgotten so much over the centuries, but that I never lost. That sorrow was always with me, even when all else was emptiness.''I do not forgive you, Tárandil, my son not of blood but of choice. But I think there was a time when these sorrows might have been avoided, had you spoken freely to me in life.

'I set aside my vengeance. There is no suffering I could impose that you have not inflicted upon yourself. Go then, and endure the punishment you earned, the punishment you created. Begone.''Hearing the tale of Tárandil in its fullness fills me with both satisfaction and regret, ${PLAYER}. The first for the responses in it to questions long unanswered, but the second for my own part in the tale. The evil deeds he performed cannot be excused, but if I had known the full extent of the shade's story I might have stayed my hand. Farewell to Tárandil, and to Bravantel and Héol, whatever their fates.

'Our journey must continue, ${PLAYER}. One oath may yet be fulfilled. We must continue on to Rivendell.''Our journey must continue to Rivendell, ${PLAYER}. One oath may yet be fulfilled.''We have arrived.''At last.''After the passage of so many years I have come at last to Rivendell, ${PLAYER}, as was my vow. I can now know rest, these long centuries later. Never did I think this day would come!'

The shade of Isildur wavers before you, shimmering in and out of view. With a great effort, he remains to give you his final words.

'The tale of Tárandil has stirred something in me, and I can dismiss it no longer. I know the struggle he faced, the conflict that caused him such distress, and I find I can understand. I do not forgive him! But I understand what caused him to fall. He was my son, not of blood, but a son all the same. Some measure of his wickedness must be traced to me, for was I not the man he would strive to be?'

The shade of Isildur wavers again, his attachment to Middle-earth made tenuous by fulfilment of his ancient vow.

'My thoughts turn to Eärnur, ${PLAYER}. Tárandil fell to darkness through the arts of the Blade of Lebennin, one of the Nazgûl, and not the greatest nor the most evil. But Eärnur fell to the Lord of the Nazgûl himself. Eärnur spoke of the torments he endured in that dark place, and I dismissed them! The blood of my brother Anárion ran through his veins, and I chastened him for not resisting! I cursed him for failure... I, who might have ended the reign of Sauron forever, and could not!

'I regret my words. Eärnur should not be left to rot within Minas Morgul, ${PLAYER}. Did I say he died as Gothmog? Nay. He died as Eärnur, descended through many generations from my brother Anárion, who perished on the fields before Barad-dûr. It is my hope that the body of Eärnur will be recovered, and given a king's rest. This is my final request, ${PLAYER}. Farewell.''Both Isildur and the Ring came to Rivendell, long years after he so vowed, and now both are gone from the world. It has been an eventful journey. But there is one more thing I must say to you, my friend.'

Gandalf's eye twinkles merrily, and he lifts the Black Book of Mordor from its leather strap.

'I spent the latter half of the ride to Rivendell considering what I might have missed within the Black Book, ${PLAYER}. The glass shard from the beacon of Barad Cúron revealed no further secrets to me, but I felt certain there was more to learn. Had I overlooked some trick? At last I have determined my error, and while I am ashamed to admit it, even the very wise will sometimes make mistakes of this fashion. I was so focused upon the writing of Vóin that all else was obscured, as if I were blindfolded. That is how I missed it!

'Vóin's hand-writing is very distinctive, and as I scoured it for any further clue, I quite ignored the drawings which decorate the margins of several pages near the end of the tome. I saw no meaning in these pictures, for I thought them mere embellishment. And yet... hidden among these illustrations I finally recognized further dwarf-runes! I did not see them during my previous studies because they were not written in Vóin's hand. Someone else wrote these runes, hiding them in plain sight!

'I have now translated them, ${PLAYER}, and can properly tell you the ending to Vóin's tale. Are you ready to finish our study of the Black Book of Mordor, and close it for good?''The runes hidden within the embellishments on some of these pages are written in the Zhélruka mode, but not in Vóin's distinctive hand.

'No, these letters were made by another: Vóin's wife Bozhéna. It was she who finished the Black Book of Mordor. Hear now her words, and you will know the end of the tale herein contained.''That is all Bozhéna has to say to us, ${PLAYER}. I am pleased to know the end of the tale. Now with the departure of Isildur our business in Rivendell is concluded; Aragorn will have need of us in Gondor, for evil still lurks within Minas Morgul.'

Reminded of Isildur's last request, you tell Gandalf that the son of Elendil repented of his words concerning Eärnur, and hoped his body might be recovered from Barad Cúron. Gandalf mirthfully raises an eyebrow.

'It has already been done, ${PLAYER}. Aragorn, that is to say King Elessar, commanded that Eärnur's body be recovered from Minas Morgul before I began the northward journey to Rivendell.'

You ask Gandalf the reason behind Elessar's command.

'Actually, it was quite simple. "Eärnur was a king of Gondor," he said to me. "He should return to the land he loved, and that loved him."

'I saw the wisdom in it, and did not gainsay his words. Within the hour, guards of the Citadel were dispatched to Barad Cúron to carry out the king's command. They found the watching-stones outside the tower abandoned, and passed within without objection. Eärnur's body was borne from the city and returned to Minas Tirith. He was laid to rest in the tomb of his fathers on the Silent Street, and at last he knew freedom from the shadows of Mordor and the long reach of Angmar.'

Gandalf nods thoughtfully, and raps his staff upon the ground.

'Well! I think that is story enough for one journey! I thank you once again for your company on this long road, my friend, and I shall seek for you when next I have cause. Until then, farewell! Should you have need of me before then, look for me at the lodge of our good friend Grimbeorn, whose hospitality cannot be measured!'The shade of Tárandil has manifested for a single purpose: he wishes to make his confession.The shade of Tárandil is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward.Talk to the shade of Tárandil and complete 'Instance: His Father's Son'Gandalf is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward.Talk to Gandalf by the waggon in Laerlad'Tárandil's confession has not yet ended. If he will say more, we must listen.''I have more to tell, though it cause me pain. I heed it not, for the wounds run deep; there is no part of me that feels them not.''What says Tárandil now? His confession is not complete.'The shade of Isildur stares impassively at Tárandil, and says nothing.The shade of Isildur stares impassively at Tárandil, and says nothing.'Much has been made clear to me by Tárandil's confession.'

Gandalf leans wearily on his staff.

'The shade of Isildur spoke true. The Disaster of the Gladden Fields was no accident.'Gandalf journeyed north ahead of the waggon bearing Isildur's bones, and he thinks it is time to check on its progress.The waggon bearing Isildur's bones is in Laerlad, on the road beside the Gladden Fields.Find the waggon carrying Isildur's bones on the road through Laerlad, beside the Gladden FieldsThe shade of Isildur feels the presence of his bones, and follows with interest their northward course along the river.Talk to the shade of Isildur beside the waggon in LaerladGandalf is by the waggon bearing Isildur's bones through Laerlad.Talk to Gandalf beside the waggon in LaerladGandalf has directed you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for the source of the dread near the waggon bearing Isildur's bones.Vágari the Wanderer has appeared by the waggon. But why has he chosen to appear now?Talk to Vágari the WandererGandalf is by the waggon bearing Isildur's bones, near to the Gladden Fields.Talk to Gandalf by the waggon in Laerlad'Have you forgotten why I came this way, ${PLAYER}? I am here in the Vales of Anduin as escort for the waggon bearing Isildur's bones northward! Yes, the son of Elendil will at last complete the journey he began at the start of the Age, and by so doing see the fulfilment of his oath! I left the waggon to partake of Grimbeorn's hospitality, for his dogs serve a merry feast, but it is past time for this Wizard to return to service. And you should come with me! Are you not tied up in this business as well?

'Do not give me that look. I had very little to do with your getting involved, if you remember. I daresay I could not have stopped you, had I the mind to try. I will see you alongside the waggon. The driver should have reached Laerlad by now. Look for him along the road beside the Gladden Fields.''I will see you alongside the waggon. The driver should have reached the road through Laerlad by now, beside the Gladden Fields.''Something is not right. Something here is amiss.''There is a dreadful presence here, ${PLAYER}, but I cannot see the source of it. I advise you to stand by the waggon and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for the source of this dread. It may be nothing, but it also may not.''Stand by the waggon and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for the source of this dread. It may be nothing, but it also may not.''The source of the dread has become visible to us. How curious.''I know. I know. I know. Son-of-no-blood, son-of-no-blood!'

The shade stares past you, glaring at the waggon, and the dread sensation intensifies. Then it abates, with only emptiness left in its wake.

'I remember now,' the shade murmurs. 'It was lost to me, but now it has returned. I know what I saw. I know what I did.'

The shade grows indistinct, wavering in your vision, and then reforms again. It seems to take a great deal of effort, and leaves Vágari weary.

'I want to confess to you what I saw, and what I did. I want one among the living to know of my misdeeds. And then there is something I must do. Will you heed me?''The source of the dread has become visible to us, and wishes to confess its crimes? I find this most peculiar, ${PLAYER}, and I urge you to remain on guard. We must not forget this is a creature of evil.

'But I think we should hear what it has to say.''Something is not right. Something is amiss.

'I sense the presence of evil. I am beyond harm... have I not suffered harm enough in these lands? But I would have no further injury come to my remains! Whatever evil comes here, begone! Take thee from these lands, and leave us be!'The shade of Tárandil has manifested for a single purpose: he wishes to make his confession.The shade of Tárandil is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward.Talk to the shade of Tárandil and complete 'Instance: The Disaster'The shade of Tárandil is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward.Talk to the shade of Tárandil by the waggon in Laerlad'Tárandil's confession draws to its close, ${PLAYER}. We must hear what he has to say, though the ending is known to us.''As my foster-father's company followed the northward road between marsh and forest, I saw the signs of Gurnákh's raiding-party everywhere. Each snapping twig, every bird taken flight, all seemed to herald the missteps of the Orcs. "The maps are changed," I remember thinking as the company neared my scouted location. "The course of the river is different than it was when we came south, and the lay of the land may not be as I remembered. It is not going to work."

'Then there was no time left for doubt. If Adûnzagar could have repented of his treachery and stayed Tárandil, that time was gone when the Orcs attacked. I did not notice its passing, for at first the plan I had given the Orcs seemed without flaw. At first.

'The darkness shielded their numbers, and enlarged the attacking force in the minds of Isildur's men. Still, they were valiant, and tempered by their travails during the war they had so recently fought. When there came an opening, my foster-father commanded they press further north.

'But it was not the harbinger of victory it seemed, and the Orcs returned for the second of the attacks I had suggested.''Speak on, shade. You have brought us this far along your sorrowful recollection, and should not leave us here.'The shade of Isildur stares impassively at Tárandil, and says nothing.The shade of Isildur stares impassively at Tárandil, and says nothing.'My dagger cut deeply, and I perished. But although the line of my life had reached its end, I was to have no rest. The oath I swore was to a power far greater and darker than that sworn by Rioc, and it bound me to these cursed fields long past the night my blood was spilt. I searched in vain for Sauron's Ring, but my prize was lost, and with it all my hopes.

'I yielded to my misery, and rage consumed me. So great was my ire that all fled before me, and as the centuries passed I left behind all memory of my life. All I knew was anger and resentment, and I gave the death that was denied me to all who entered my domain. Orc, Elf, or Man... no creature was safe from my wrath.

'But then, amid the confused tatters of my memory, one crossed my path who I knew of old. He wore the appearance of an old pilgrim, cloaked in grey, but I knew this vagabond's form hid a greater power. How could he have returned? I confronted him, and my spirit was seared by the heat of the power he kept hidden. Then I was afraid, and fled; but he followed close behind, and cast a spell most cruel. I was banished, but the force of my oath prevented my leaving; in distress, my very being was torn asunder, and I knew no more for long years.

'But something changed, something far away and yet ever-present, and my form coalesced in agony out of the mist. The pain I felt in that moment was worse than every torment I ever experienced, save one: the anguish of losing Bravantel and Héol forevermore. That pain I feel still, and it will never abate.'Vágari has manifested for a single purpose: he wishes to make his confession.Vágari the Wanderer is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward.Talk to Vágari and complete 'Instance: The Wanderer's Confession'Gandalf is by the waggon bearing the bones of Isildur northward.Talk to Gandalf by the waggon in Laerlad'Let us hear what Vágari has to say.''My story begins long ago... so very long ago...''Speak to Vágari, and we will judge him by the content of his confession.'The shade of Isildur stares impassively at Vágari, and says nothing.The shade of Isildur stares impassively at Tárandil, and says nothing.'The pieces fall into place, and I begin to see the sad road that brought the shade of Tárandil to this place, and this state. Could he have turned away from this course, and prevented the evil he would cause? For I guess at the ending, and it is sorrowful. Still we must hear it.'You have given Gandalf the glass shard you found locked in Khîl's home, and the Wizard believes it may prove crucial to revealing the secret writing in the Black Book of Mordor.Gandalf is inside the Beorninghús, eager to decipher the secret writing in the Black Book of Mordor.Talk to Gandalf inside the Beorninghús and complete 'Instance: Vóin's Tale'Gandalf is inside the Beorninghús.Talk to Gandalf inside the Beorninghús'There is a moving radiance held within this glass, ${PLAYER}, and it reminds me of something: the rotating beacon atop Barad Cúron, Tower of the Crescent Moon, in Minas Morgul. Is this a shard of glass from the beacon in the tower's topmost chamber?

'You have seen moon-letters before, and so have I. Can it be that Vóin wrote these secret words in the light of that beacon, knowing they could only be revealed by the same light? But what did he seek to hide? Did he record the location of the weapon sought by Gothmog?

'We must read the secret writing in the Black Book, ${PLAYER}. Let me know if you are ready to do so.''Are you ready? I do not know what we will learn from Vóin's secret writing, but whatever it may be we will discover it together.

'It is time to hear the rest of Vóin's tale.''Much that was obscured from us now seems clear, my friend. When the sword Dúnachar pierced Mordirith's form in the throne room of Carn Dûm, the purpose of the Witch-king was fulfilled, a thousand years later, but not in the manner for which he planned. Bonds were made that day, and months later, on the Pelennor Fields before Minas Tirith, they would be broken. He could not remember it, but one thousand years later, Eärnur's final act of resistance freed him from the domination of Mordor.'

Gandalf closes the Black Book and looks up at you.

'That is enough for now, my friend. We have come to the end of the moon-writing, but the discovery has stirred in me the certainty that I missed something in my earlier perusal of this tome. I do not know what it may have been, but I can sense its presence still.

'Ah, mysteries upon mysteries. Perhaps I will return to this matter when we have satisfied our other charge. Yes, I fear we have spent too many hours in study and neglected our other tasks.'<rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers.</rgb><rgb=#00e4ff>This deed is for Legendary Servers</rgb>Complete The Black Book of Mordor: Chapter 14Cannoth was sent to investigate the disappearances of several allies, but now Cannoth himself has also vanished.Locate Cannoth east of Barad Arthir, the Lofty Watch-fort, and learn what happened.Find Cannoth east of Barad Arthir'There have been many worrying disappearances near Achathras, east of this location. Cannoth journeyed in that direction to scout a nearby pass, and we are waiting for word. Aye, we wait still, and longer than we expected. Has he run into trouble, or has some trouble run into him? Find him and learn what happened, if you can.''Your arrival is well-timed, for things are worse here than I feared! Through this archway, a path winds upward along the mountainside. There lies Gath Daeroval, the Shadow-roost. This place is home to Khatlob, the massive, voracious brood-mother of the roost. At least, so say the Orcs; it sounds from their talk that she laid the eggs from which sprang the terrible flying mounts ridden by the Witch-king and the rest of the Nazgûl.

'She is not the only danger within. The lower reaches are said to be guarded by Khúrthak the Rent, a great Olog that has long stood guard over this area... and over Khatlob. It sounds to me as if he is both her jailor and her protector. The Orcs say he suffered a grievous injury from her claws, and now fears to get too near.

'Such beasts that Khatlob bears can work great evil upon our forces in Imlad Morgul, and elsewhere. When you are prepared, you should follow the path and put an end to the brood-mother and her terrible spawn!'Gothmog has been defeated, but he is not the only Master of Mordor who has come to Barad Cúron.The Ranger Culang is in the throne room inside Barad Cúron.Talk to Culang inside the throne room of Barad CúronUgrukhôr, the Captain of the Pit, has captured the Ranger Annoth. He comes now to Barad Cúron to challenge Gothmog for supremacy of Imlad Morgul.Remain on guardUgrukhôr has asked you a question.Talk to UgrukhôrUgrukhôr does not have the capacity for mercy. He toys with you and with the lives of your friends.Remain on guardUgrukhôr does not have the capacity for mercy. He toys with you and with the lives of your friends. He must pay for his cruelty.Talk to UgrukhôrUgrukhôr has commanded one of his guards, an Orc named Lûrkh, to take you on a search of the tower for Karazgar.Talk to LûrkhLûrkh wants you to clear the room of merrevail while he studies the Oath-stone, pondering if some valuable piece of it might be chipped away and kept as treasure.Clear the room atop Barad Cúron of merrevailLûrkh has given all his attention to the Oath-stone in the centre of the chamber. Now is your chance to overpower him!Defeat Lûrkh, and do it quicklyWith the advantage of surprise on your side, you should descend the tower of Barad Cúron and return to the throne room.Descend Barad Cúron and return to the throne room without alerting Ugrukhôr to your presenceCulang has noticed your return, and now you must wait for his signal to attack. Do not let Ugrukhôr see you before that time!Wait for Culang's signal to attackCulang has noticed your return, and now you must wait for his signal to attack. Do not let Ugrukhôr see you before that time!Wait for Culang's signal to attackUgrukhôr is ready to face you in single combat.Talk to UgrukhôrThe time has come to face Ugrukhôr, the Captain of the Pit, and avenge the lives he has taken.Defeat Ugrukhôr, the Captain of the Pit'${PLAYER}... I am all right...

'I will survive... and Ugrukhôr will not!''Be on your guard, my friend. We have few friends in this dark city, and fewer still who might gain access to this tower!''Ah, now you speak swiftly. It is a good lesson, ${RACE}.

'But you will still not tell me if Karazgar remains in the tower? This defiance does not suit you, and it will avail you nothing. Indeed, it may cost you your life, or the lives of more friends. Is that what you want?''You'd better come with me, slime! You're going to show me where your friend Karazgar is hiding, and if you so much as look at me strangely I'll kill you first!''You are alone, ${PLAYER}. You came to this tower with allies, but they have abandoned you.

'Your Rangers may find success in the woods, but in towers of stone they die the same as any others. They cannot save you.

'The Thandrim crossed me, long ago, and they are all dead. The only man who remembered them will have no vengeance. I gave him death instead, and none now will wield his sad sword or bear his broken shield.

'And Karazgar? Long have I thought to test my might against the Weeping Warrior, but he is a sneaking coward. When I have secured my position here, he will be the next to fall. But of this I am now convinced: he is no friend to you. Perhaps he and I will strike a bargain, for we each may have information of use to the other.

'${CLASS}! Do you dare test your will against Ugrukhôr, the Captain of the Pit? Did I say we were equal? I see now I was wrong! You are no equal of mine, for I am Gúrzyul... and I am your ending!'You have decided to deliver the Black Book of Mordor to Karazgar, as he requested.You have chosen to deliver the Black Book of Mordor to Karazgar, and should wait by the flat stone in the Old Ford until he arrives to claim his prize.Wait by the flat stone for Karazgar to arrive and claim his prizeKarazgar has come to meet with you at the Old Ford.Remain on your guardKarazgar has come to meet with you at the Old Ford.Talk to Karazgar at the Old FordKarazgar has asked you a question, and awaits your response.Answer KarazgarKarazgar has come to meet with you at the Old Ford.Talk to Karazgar at the Old FordKarazgar waits to hear your conditions.Give Karazgar your conditionsKarazgar has agreed to your conditions, and waits for you to set the Black Book on the flat stone.Place the Black Book of Mordor on the flat stoneKarazgar has taken the Black Book of Mordor.Remain on your guard'I see you have brought the tome with you, ${PLAYER}. Do you intend to honour our agreement?'

You tell Karazgar that you will give him the Black Book of Mordor, and he smiles.

'I am pleased you were willing to listen to reason, ${RACE}.''Give to me this artifact and I will leave you be.'

Karazgar holds out his hand, and when you do not immediately give him the Black Book he scowls.

'Why do you hesitate? You risk the wrath of Karazgar, ${PLAYER}.'

You tell him the Book is not without cost, and there are conditions attached to this gift.It was Vóin's wife Bozhéna who completed the Black Book of Mordor, hiding her contributions among the illustrations in the margins towards the end of the tome.Bozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Listen to the words of Bozhéna in the Halls of MemoryBozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Listen to the words of Bozhéna on the slopes of Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryBozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Bozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Listen to the words of Vóin, as remembered by BozhénaBozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Listen to the words of BozhénaBozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Listen to the words of Vóin, as remembered by BozhénaBozhéna will pick up the tale on the slopes of Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of Memory.Talk to Bozhéna on the slopes of Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of MemoryBozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Bozhéna has made her own contributions to the Halls of Memory.Listen to the words of BozhénaAs the end of his years approached, Vóin took one last journey to the Grey Mountains.Hear the words of Vóin, as recorded by BozhénaAs the end of his years approached, Vóin took one last journey to the Grey Mountains.Hear the words of Vóin, as recorded by BozhénaAs the end of his years approached, Vóin took one last journey to the Grey Mountains.Hear the conclusion of Bozhéna's account, secretly recorded in the Black Book'Farewell, unknown friend, farewell!''I am named Bozhéna, and I am of the Zhélruka. I met Vóin who would be my husband when I was young and my beard was but a shadow of what it is today, and I did not count him much of a dwarf. It was only with the passage of years that he earned my love, and now we have a son. Tázko is his name, and already I have filled his ears with the stories of his father's adventures. But he is young, and to him tales are just tales; ask an old dwarf the tales of her youth, and she may not remember them.

'So I do not entrust this story to Tázko, though he is dearest to me in all this world and I will raise him to be all a Zhélruka should be. It was Vóin's wish that I record the end of his tale in the tome he brought out of Mordor, and this I now do.''For twelve years he remained in Mordor, and when he finally returned he was not the same. The time he spent among such wickedness in the Black Land and the citadel of Minas Morgul took its toll upon him.

'No one could have survived such an experience unchanged, but by my beard I say none could have done better than my Vóin.''But it was not to be. The torments of the Witch-king proved too severe, and in time Eärnur's resistances fell, as he knew they would. He had told Vóin this day would come, but my husband refused to believe it.

'Alas, the depth of the evil in that place overmastered Vóin's hopeful heart, and the words of his friend proved true.''Vóin knew the time had come. He ascended the stairs in the tower and came to the very highest chamber, where resided the great beacon. He broke a shard from its reflective surface, and using the secret arts of our kind he trapped a ray of the moon's light in that glass.

'Sitting in that high place, my husband took from his satchel an empty tome he had taken from an archive in the city; the accursed sage that governed it never knew the book to be missing. In the light of that moon, Vóin wrote the tale of his years, and he left nothing out. He wrote of Magoldir the Elf, into whose life my husband came only near the end, and wept to not have known him longer. He wrote of the knights of Gondor with whom he adventured in Gundabad and elsewhere: hopeful Silmahtar, courageous Calatúr, swift Macilnis, and loyal Orolang, whose death grieved him for all the rest of his days.

'But most of all, he wrote of Eärnur, the lost king of Gondor and his dearest friend. It would be the only record of the man Eärnur had been before he fell into the net of the Witch-king. When Vóin could find no more words to write, he found a plain coffer in the city and fashioned two keys to open it. One of the keys he kept; the other he set beneath hammer, and broke the end.''Vóin returned home, walking through Mordor as if he were the master of that land. He had no more to fear from it.''He did not stay in Mordor. After twelve years, my husband returned to me.'

Even hidden among the margin illustrations in the Black Book, the tone of Bozhéna's writing is as clear as the crystal surface of a mountain lake.

'I had some questions,' she writes.'The chamber took many years to finish. It was his life's work, and into its construction he poured every part of himself. On the walls he carved the song of Thafar-gathol, for that was the dream he set aside for the love of his friends and the memory of their struggles, but it still captured his imagination. Often I would enter the hidden chamber unnoticed and find him staring at the song, humming to himself. More than once I would interrupt his musings and hear him speaking softly in the dim light. "One day," he would say from time to time. "One day."

'Our son Tázko was born in the years following his father's return from Mordor, and he delighted to play in the hidden room, but eventually Vóin grew cross and forbade his son from entering. "It is disrespectful, Tázko!" he chided the boy. "This room is a crypt. It is a tomb for the dwarf I was, and for my friends who have died, and worse than died."

'"What could be worse than death?" Tázko asked him once, but that was the end of the discussion. Vóin would no longer speak openly of the hidden chamber or the tome within.

'In time, my husband felt the onset of his years, and knew his end approached. There was one last journey for us to make, and then he would be satisfied with the life he had lived.''It has been a long journey, but I have a few steps more to take. I trust you both know you have all my love? Good, good!

'Keep the deeds of the Zhélruka close to your heart, Tázko, and live by their example! You will be a strong dwarf and brave, stronger and braver than I have been, I expect! I am proud of you, my son. Take care of your mother, and see that she wants for nothing.

'Bozhéna, you are more dear to me than you know, and these years have been better than I deserve. Take care of Tázko, and know that I will bear the memories of our time together wherever these steps take me.''We returned to Járnfast. My husband was right: Tázko grew to be a fine, strong dwarf, and as the years passed he proved an able help to his mother and to the rulers of the Iron Hills. He spoke often of the great deeds of Zhélruka past and present; Vóin would have been very proud of his son.

'I placed the tome my husband had written in the hidden chamber, and sealed the door. I entered that secret room less and less often as the years went on, and in time I stopped going inside at all. Tázko does not remember the journey we took to the Grey Mountains with his father, but every now and then he asks what I think happened to him. "He found the lost city of Thafar-gathol," I tell him, and he smiles. And why should he not? Vóin survived Mordor, and Minas Morgul, and defied the edicts of the Witch-king himself. I think if anyone could find that lost city, it would be my husband.

'Now I feel the passing of the years, as Vóin did, and I know my time is nearly ended. I have come into the hidden chamber one last time, and I will write the conclusion of my husband's story in the manner he wanted. "My friends defied evil," he said to me, "and I do not want them forgotten." I promised to conclude the tale, and now that I have done so I will set this book down upon its table, and sing the song my husband carved upon the walls. I will leave the hidden chamber and seal the door, and then I think I will sit for a time and think of Vóin.

'If any should find this book after a year, or two, or ten, know that Vóin and Bozhéna and their son Tázko were happy with their lot, and despite the difficulties of their time they knew that even a hopeless night must come to an end, and there would always be good-hearted folk ready and eager to greet the dawn.'Inside Barad Cúron, the Tower of the Crescent Moon, the villain Gothmog awaits.Ayorzén has disguised himself as Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, and plans to bring you inside Barad Cúron as his prisoner.Talk to Ayorzén outside Barad CúronAyorzén has disguised himself as Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior, and now he attempts to bring you and the Ranger Culang into Barad Cúron as his prisoners.Go with the disguised Ayorzén to Barad Cúron, pretending to be his prisonerThe watchful presence that guards the entrance to Barad Cúron has given you leave to enter the tower.Enter Barad Cúron and confront GothmogInside Barad Cúron, the wraith Gothmog attempts to demoralize you with painful memories of the past.Inside Barad Cúron, the wraith Gothmog attempts to demoralize you with painful memories of the past.Gothmog has not yet exhausted his powers. With his illusions defeated he redoubles his attack!Survive the further assaults of GothmogCulang believes you should use Gothmog's own weapons against him, reminding the wraith of those heroes who stood against darkness despite the evils arrayed against them. It is Culang's hope that the light of their inspiration will serve to weaken the wraith, sapping him of his strength.Draw upon the memory of Narmeleth for inspirationDraw upon the memory of Golodir for inspirationDraw upon the memory of Isildur for inspirationDraw upon the memory of Anárion for inspirationDraw upon the memory of Elendil for inspirationBoth weakened and enraged by your defiance, Gothmog presses the attack!Defeat Gothmog'I hope you are ready for this, ${PLAYER}. Remember what I said! I will not die for you!'The shade that came to be called Vágari the Wanderer desires to confess to you the misdeeds of his past.Vágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Hear the words of Isildur as recounted by VágariVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Vágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to GurnákhVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererThe shade of Tárandil says nothing.'I remember little of the days and weeks that followed. I wandered in the wilderness, mad with grief for my wife and son, lamenting their doom: an eternity of pitiless unrest. I never came to the halls that awaited me; Belfalas was not to be mine, and I cared not. I dreamt my family called to me, their voices drawing me deeper into the wild lands. I know not where trod my feet; uncaring, I grew gaunt and hungry.

'Somewhere in the wilderness, not far from the site of a past victory that was now wholly meaningless, I found a creature that was known to me, one of Nine. It was a Nazgûl of Sauron, and I could not escape it. Do I say "could not?" That is not the right of it.

'I did not try to escape. Aye, perhaps this Nazgûl could give me the only escape I wanted. I welcomed the cold touch of death, and feared it not.''And so I became my father's son, after all.

'By how many twisting paths had Adûnzagar, son of Ûrizagar, come to this place? The name Tárandil no longer suited me, but I knew I needs must wear it a little longer if I was to fulfil my bargain with the Blade of Lebennin and his master.

'I returned from the wilderness gaunt and haggard, but with new-found purpose. I returned to Minas Ithil just as my foster-father Isildur prepared to depart for Annúminas to claim his father Elendil's throne.''The falsehoods came more easily to me than they once did. I told Isildur and my brothers that I had been waylaid and wounded by Orcs on the road, left to die by one of their raiding-parties. "That would have been the end," I said to them, "but for the Orodhoth, the Men of the Mountain. They rescued me and nursed me back to health; for that they have my gratitude. I tell you true, I would count their broken oaths repaired for this deed."''I am glad of their service to you, Tárandil, son-of-no-blood, but their betrayal remains beyond pardon. This treachery cannot now be redeemed, no matter how dear to me the subject of their aid.

'But let us not dwell upon the oath-breakers! It brings me no happiness to speak of such faithless traitors, not on such a joyful occasion as this, your return from the wild, and from the dead!

'I understand if you do not wish to take the western road again so soon. The change of heart that brought you hither to Minas Ithil is understandable, and if it is your wish I will not push for you to return to Belfalas. Come with us instead on the northern road, if you are hale and hearty enough for the journey.''You are more reckless than others of your kind I have slain, but there is something else, too. A shadow lies upon you, deeper and darker than that on any Orc. Is that why you have given us the marching orders for your band, and suggested this plan of attack? It is more detailed than any Orkish plan of battle: a feint and a retreat, followed by a finishing attack when their guard is down. I like it.

'I could kill you right now, foolish one. You have given us the perfect location to surprise your band, trapped between the marshes to their left and the forest to their right. What need do I still have for you?

'Ah, I can name the reason you still live, and it is this only: my curiosity. I want to know why you do this.''So be it. I will give you these three things for which you have asked. My Crow-feeders will get first crack at your marching men, but I will send word to the other raiding-parties within range of us. Bloody Moon and Twisted Neck will want to be included, but my Crow-feeders deserve the greatest share of the treasure, and they will get it.

'But I warn you, traitor. Cross me, and you will keep your life, but only so you will know torments the like of which you have never dreamed.''I resolved to stay as near to Isildur as I could without arousing suspicion. When he fell beneath the blades of the Orcs, I needed to ensure I could take the Ring from his body before the Orcs seized it for themselves. A traitor sees treachery everywhere, and I did not trust Gurnákh.

'One hour after meeting with the Crow-feeders, I was back at the camp with my sword sheathed at my side. No one had missed me. My treason remained a secret.'The tower of Barad Arthir, otherwise known as the Lofty Watch-fort, must be captured in order to enter the Circle of Despair and reclaim Minas Morgul.Secure the tower.

<rgb=#FF0000>It is recommended to have the quest The Circle of Madness underway.</rgb>Secure the towerTalk to one of the Rangers.Talk to one of the RangersThe tower of Barad Arthir, otherwise known as the Lofty Watch-fort, must be captured in order to enter the Circle of Despair and reclaim Minas Morgul.'We have secured the tower!'The shade that came to be called Vágari the Wanderer desires to confess to you the misdeeds of his past.Vágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Hear the words of Vëamacil as remembered by VágariVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Hear the words of Meldaran as remembered by VágariVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari believed Isildur ran for the river-bank, and he desperately searched for some sign of the king's escape.Find some sign that Isildur made for the river-bankVágari believed Isildur attempted to swim across the river in order to escape.Swim across the river to find IsildurVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to TárandilVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the Wanderer'I became separated from Isildur during the fighting, and while I was engaged with the Orcs on one flank of our diminishing circle, he gave something to his esquire Ohtar.

'Ohtar vanished during the fighting. Did he escape? And more importantly: what was he given? What was he given?''Tárandil! You have to keep fighting! We are going to get through this!

'What did father give to Ohtar? This is not the time for this, brother! Keep fighting!''Tárandil, you are alive! Thank the Valar!

'Things go ill for us, brother. Father sent his esquire away before the fighting became too thick, entrusting Ohtar to bear the shards of Narsil away from the battle. But I fear he did not give him the object of the Orc's desire, and if we do not do something they will seize it!''During the confusion, I lost sight of Isildur. Where had he gone, and did he still have the Ring? If he escaped now, my bargain with the Nazgûl would come to naught!''He discarded his armour on the bank of the river.''All is lost, all is ashes! A thousand curses upon you!

'Without the Ring I cannot fulfill my bond... I cannot save my family! Bravantel, Héol, I tried! I tried, I tried!''My muscles ached. The swim had taken its toll on me, and I knew I must rest before attempting the return journey. I would hide among the reeds and wait until the Orcs prowling on the distant shore had abandoned their vigil; then I would swim back with my prize.

'Despite my exhaustion, I had to complete the task I had set for myself. It was time.''But for men such as me, death was no escape.'The shade that came to be called Vágari the Wanderer desires to confess to you the misdeeds of his past.Vágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to the shade's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to Vágari the WandererVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Listen to Vágari's accountVágari the Wanderer desires to speak to you of his past misdeeds.Talk to RiocThe shade of Tárandil says nothing, dwelling upon his old sorrows.'I was born in Númenor, two scant years before its fall, but I cannot trust my memories of that land. Are they true recollections, or the fleeting shadows of stories I was told, the impressions of others? I cannot say. But I know the name I was given. I was born Adûnzagar. It was only the first of my names, and not the last.

'My father was Ûrizagar, the captain-general of Númenor's fleets, and his title was Sword of the Sun. Do I remember his cruelty, or his worship at the temples of Sauron? I thought I did. Do I remember the warmth of my mother Abrazâni, or is that memory a creation too, balm for the hurts of my heart?

'This I do know: my mother pleaded with the sons of Elendil to make a place for me on their ships when the deluge came to Númenor. In later years, when I was older, Isildur told me that my father's spies prevented her from reaching the ships in time. Was it so? I do not know.

'But that is how I came without father or mother to the shores of Middle-earth when Númenor sank beneath the waves.''King Rioc was enraged, for he was certain that his grandson had been born a bastard, but Bravantel soothed his ire with reassuring words. She told the king she had been married in secret, not to an invading Númenórean but to one of his own warriors, who had recently been slain in battle with Orcs. Héol was born of that union, she said, and therefore the stain of dishonour could not cling to him. Rioc's anger cooled, and he agreed to raise the boy as his own grandchild.

'I grieved that my son would not know his true father, but there was nothing I could do. I stole gold from the treasury of Prince Eärtur of Belfalas and sent as much as could be done secretly to Bravantel to provide for the boy. But then I was summoned back east by my foster-father Isildur, for the host of Mordor had come down the valley and overthrown Minas Ithil. Sauron's war had begun, and the kings sent word to all their allies that it was time to honour their vows.

'It was then that Rioc learned the truth.''I pleaded with Isildur to show mercy, to forgive Rioc and his people of their oath-breaking, but my foster-father's wrath at their treachery was so great I feared for my life. I quailed before the storm of his rage, and told him not of Bravantel my wife or Héol my son. Would that truth-telling have changed his mind? Would it cause him to repent of his curse? My mind dwells ever on this, not the earliest of my wrongs but the first I regret.

'I went north with Isildur and my foster-brothers to summon aid from Arnor and from the Elves, and every night my heart pleaded with me to speak the truth, and every day the guilt pressed heavily upon me and weighed my lips shut. For all my time with the Great Alliance, I kept secret my marriage to Bravantel and the duty I owed my son; my brothers goaded me with the thought I might never marry, and each of their barbs pricked more deadly than they intended or imagined. I feared for Bravantel and for Héol, and when the war finally ended I was frenzied with worry for them.

'Isildur offered me the chance to be the captain-general I had dreamed of becoming, commander of all the armies of the Dúnedain, but no longer did my heart desire it. I longed only to return to the White Mountains so I could find my wife and son. "Then I will make you more than a mere lord, my son-of-no-blood," Isildur said to me, "but rather a prince, for Ëartur and all his sons perished during the war. Belfalas needs a new master. Go, and take my blessing with you."

“I will take your blessing,” I said to him, “but not for me. Show mercy instead for the Men of the Mountain, and lift the curse you have placed upon them. I will bring you their false king’s head, and his people shall swear their oath anew."

'But he refused. Always did Isildur refuse me this one request.''I know now who despoiled my daughter, faithless invader from beyond the Sea!

'My rage will follow you forever, though I remain bound to pass no further than Erech! You will never be free of it, for my howling anger will pursue you wherever you think to go!

'But that is the least of the daggers with which I cut thee. This will be the sharpest: there can be no escape for my daughter and her child. You will share my sorrow, detestable thief, for she and the child will remain with me forever!'Gandalf believes he can reveal the rest of Vóin's tale using the shard of glass you discovered in the locked box inside Khîl's home.Gandalf is in the Halls of Memory, ready to read the secret writing of Vóin's tale.Talk to Gandalf in the Halls of MemoryHidden within the secret runes, Vóin speaks of his journey to Mordor.Vóin joined a group of wicked dwarves bound for service in Minas Morgul.The hidden runes left by Vóin in the Black Book of Mordor describe the time he spent within Minas Morgul, seeking to free Eärnur.Listen to Vóin's tale as recorded in the secret runesThe hidden runes left by Vóin in the Black Book of Mordor describe the time he spent within Minas Morgul, seeking to free Eärnur.Vóin recorded the words of Eärnur in hidden runes within the Black Book of Mordor.Hear the words of EärnurVóin recorded the words of Eärnur in hidden runes within the Black Book of Mordor.Listen to Eärnur's plan as recorded by Vóin in the secret runesHidden within the secret runes, Vóin speaks of the dungeons of Minas Morgul.The hidden runes left by Vóin in the Black Book of Mordor describe a difficult choice made within the dungeons of Minas Morgul.Listen to Vóin's tale as recorded in the secret runesVóin writes of his difficult choice in the dungeons of Minas Morgul, and his words return to the slopes of Mordor.Gandalf has finished reading the hidden runes Vóin left within the Black Book of Mordor.Talk to Gandalf on the slopes of Mordor, as depicted by the Halls of Memory'Yes, it is conjecture only... but I believe it is true.''I believe this will be our last delve into the words of our distant friend Vóin, ${PLAYER}. Yes, I say friend, for though the tome he left us comes from Mordor and bears the sigil of Sauron upon its cover, I have detected little malice on the part of the Zhélruka who wrote it. He has expanded our knowledge of the past, and for that I call him friend!

'The shard of glass reveals hidden writing that speaks of Mordor. That is where Vóin picks up the tale, and that is where we should follow in these Halls of Memory.''My mind wandered on the hard journey. I remembered our adventures together, and they brought a smile to my face even in that dark land. But there were other memories, too, and regrets, and those were less welcome.

'He had just become king of Gondor, and the crown was an ill fit. The taunts of the Witch-king at Fornost ate away at him. "If my horse had not bolted..." he often began, but one of us was always there to keep him from dwelling upon it. If Magoldir had lived, his words might have kept Eärnur from going to Minas Morgul, but Magoldir was dead, and would never return from Durthang.

'Could I have stopped him? This is the thought that comes back to me again and again, as I write these passages in the dim light of the accursed beacon. He had just become king, and I trusted that his knights would keep him safe. Gondor was no land for Zhélruka, and I had deeds of my own to perform. I remember his words to me before I returned to the northern mountains. "Go find your city, Vóin. Go find Thafar-gathol!" I embraced him then, and bid him farewell. "I will!" I told him.

'"Good!" he responded. "Perhaps then you will stop talking about it morning and evening!"

'Seven years after that parting, I learned from travellers upon the road that King Eärnur went to Imlad Morgul with a band of knights, and vanished. I set out at sunrise the following morning, bid farewell to my wife, and resolved to find Eärnur.''It was in the city of Minas Morgul that I came to live, hiding in plain sight among the other wicked dwarves that served the Orcs and their foul masters. It was unpleasant work and cruel, but every disagreeable task elevated my position in the city and brought me nearer to my goal. Other dwarves claim the Zhélruka have no patience? I proved them all wrong, and was rewarded. In time the bosses gave me a new post: steward of the chambers within the tower atop the city.

'It was there I at last found Eärnur, and the few of his knights who yet survived.''We will think of something. There is still time.

'The Witch-king's pride will be his downfall. He thinks to rob you of the spark that drives you, Eärnur, the fire that so infuriates him. As long as that passion to resist him burns within you, he will not know victory over you.

'I have stayed here too long. I will be back tomorrow, my friend.''Think, Eärnur, think! There is still time, but... there is less now than there was. I have devised a hundred plans, each of them less likely to succeed than the previous one. But that does not mean escape is impossible. Never think that!

'I do not want to worry you, but... you should know. Silmahtar is... no more. The Houses of Lamentation claim another of our friends.

'No, we should not weep for him. Let his fate inspire us to escape, and we can mourn him when we are both free of the Black Land! Not just he, but Silmahtar and Calatúr both. Think!''I am beginning to worry, my friend. There must be some means of escape, some way to free you from the Nazgûl's grasp. I have searched this tower high and low, and delved into every corner of the vale, but... there is nothing.

'What is that? What did you say? You have thought of something? At last, Eärnur! Speak! Tell me what you have devised, and I will carry it out!

'What? Thafar-gathol? Why do you speak its name, here in the darkness of Morgul? That is of no use to us now, friend. We must think only of escape!''For all the time I have known you, Vóin, you have dreamed of finding your lost city. I do not want you to waste your years here in this tower seeking to prevent something I know to be unpreventable.

'Yes, I know my doom. Here in the heart of his power, I cannot hope to resist the Lord of the Nazgûl for much longer. He knows this. But he seeks not only a thrall. He wants something else: he wants to destroy my spirit, the fire inside me that so infuriates him. He wants me to despair, to fall upon my own sword, to say with word and deed "you have the victory, and I am bested." You were right, Vóin. That would be no escape, and would bind me to his will.

'Know you of Egalmoth? He was the lord of this city when it fell, but he did not wait in the tower for the Nazgûl to slay him. He rode out, defying the armies of Orc and wraith, and though he came in the end to death, I think his Onslaught will be long-remembered. I cannot do as he did, and I cannot keep the Lord of the Nazgûl from dominating my mind. But I can hinder the scheme he has devised for me, and cheat him of the total victory he seeks.

'None will know of this save you, Vóin. My own Onslaught will not become song or tale or legend. But it will be the deed of Eärnur, who resisted evil to the last.''Vóin! What news of Eärnur? Tell me he still resists the torments of the Nazgûl!

'What? Escape? What has changed? I will not leave without Eärnur! You say he is already fled? Happy news! If you will open my cell, I will follow him to freedom! Dear, sweet Vóin!

'What of Macilnis? You go to free her now? Then I will away, and let us meet again when we are safely outside the Black Land! Farewell, Vóin, bearer of happy tidings and welcome escape!''Only one of Eärnur's knights could escape, but even that would require the guardians in the Vale to be occupied with some distracting mission. Something was needed to draw their attention away from the bearer of the sword.

'I knew Macilnis was more fleet of foot than Orolang, and would have the greatest chance of escaping the valley, so I gave to her the sword Orthadel and set her free. Only after she was fled did I go to release Orolang, but there was little time to waste: I sent the tower's guardians after him almost immediately, and he led them on a desperate chase... away from Macilnis. Alas, my friend! I had no choice! Anyone could see that.''And so ends the hidden runes Vóin left within the Black Book of Mordor, ${PLAYER}. If the knight Macilnis escaped Imlad Morgul and came to free lands again, Vóin does not tell. And yet... I think we have learned enough from this Black Book for me to suggest some possibilities.

'I believe Macilnis escaped the bounds of Mordor still in possession of Orthadel, the Rising Star, but not without injury. For how long did she journey, and to where did she go? We cannot know for certain, but it seems she did not make for Minas Tirith or word of her would appear once again, and it does not. I think she turned northward in an attempt to put as much distance between herself and the Witch-king as she was able. Did she survive the journey, and live out her days in peace? Did she succumb to wounds freshly-inflicted during her escape, or those she endured during the years of her torment in the dungeons? Was she recaptured by hunting-parties sent by the Witch-king? Vóin never learned which of these came to pass, and I cannot say.

'But of this I am quite certain. Whatever may have been the fate of this knight of Gondor, the blade she carried out of the Morgul Vale survived. Orthadel was of Elf-make, wielded first by Magoldir the Elf during the Siege of Barad-dûr and later by King Eärnur of Gondor, but in time its history was forgotten, and its name was lost. It became just a sword, handed down from bearer to bearer for many years. Somehow it found its way into the hands of a Dúnadan, and became an heirloom of his house.

'That is how it came to the Ranger Golodir, ${PLAYER}. Yes, though it endured much over the years, I think the sword that would be remade in the forges of Rivendell and renamed Dúnachar was once known as Orthadel. And so the Rising Star became the Avenger of the West.'A strange seal bearing the mark of death points towards the Dead City of Minas Morgul.The Kolos Gurz socket can be found somewhere in Minas Morgul.

