You should talk to Skarding.Talk to Skarding Wargsbane at Thokvist'Now then, the time has come to attack the Gundabad blockade. We know that Lumrog is too well-guarded to attack directly. We will need to even the odds in our favour. Fanglind and I have a plan that should leave Lumrog unprotected.

'If we can confuse the brutes and make some of their numbers leave the outpost, you should be able to strike at Gúrbarash. Defeat worms or avanc in Misthallow and lay their carcasses upon the stone outcroppings near Lumrog. Fanglind and I will take care of the rest.''${PLAYER}, I was able to convince some Eagles, including my brother, that the fresh kills you supplied were not a trap. No, not a trap at all: a free meal! When the forces of Lumrog come to investigate the Eagles surrounding their blockade, we shall rise above the mist! That is when Skarding will ambush those unfortunate enough to observe an Eagle feast at close range.

'Quickly now, you must infiltrate Lumrog and defeat Gúrbarash!''It is over, and Gúrbarash flees for the distant hills. Lumrog remains only the ruins of a blockade. We have all fought well!''Eagles, and one such as you? Har, now I've seen it all! You think you can brazenly walk right in here and I'll set down my weapons without a fight? You've read me all wrong, worm! Now then, I'll be nice and let you prepare for a moment before I kill you!'Word has spread that a mysterious enemy has arrived at Caivád Sâr, threatening to turn the tide of the War of Three Peaks.Múta can be found in Tûr Ágal, the tower at the centre of Caivád Sâr.

After learning that a ruthless dwarf-lord named Brathar leads the Dourhands at Caivád Sâr, Múta has resolved to defeat this mysterious, new leader for the good of the Gabil'akkâ.Talk to MútaMúta can be found outside of Caivád Sâr in the War of Three Peaks.

You were able to escape Tûr Ágal after learning of Brathar Crack-helm and the Dourhands' plans. You should talk to Múta.Talk to Múta outside Caivád Sâr'Brathar is here, ${PLAYER}. I am sure of it!

'Before you arrived, I discovered a passage that I believe might lead to the upper chambers of Tûr Ágal. If Brathar had already left Tûr Ágal, I don't think his Dourhands would be keeping such a close watch on Caivád Sâr. Whatever his plans, he must be overseeing them from the highest spire of Tûr Ágal... and it is there we must go!

'It pains me to admit it, but the time for sneaking is over. Let us seek out Brathar and put an end to him before his Dourhands can hinder the Gabil'akkâ any further!''Let us see what all this is about, ${PLAYER}.''I think we're out of sight.

'Although Brathar escaped, at least Bandrauth Adder-fang and his servants will no longer trouble the Gabil'akkâ. Whatever business Brathar had at Caivád Sâr appears to be finished, so he and his followers must now return to Câr Bronach. Unfortunately, I know no place on our maps by that name!

'I shall return to Zarkul-sulûn and bring word to Prince Durin at once. Perhaps he and commander Glóin can make some sense of this!

'You have my thanks, ${PLAYER}.'Mekhnet harbours a concern that she'd like to put to rest.Mekhnet has been keeping an eye on the opposite shoreline to the east of the the Kambráda encampment in Amdân.

You should check to see if anything strange is happening there.Check the areaYou have found nothing of note other than an inert sapper-bomb.

Perhaps inspecting it will unlock more clues.Examine the bombMekhnet waits near the Kambráda encampment in the eastern part of Amdân.

She will want to hear about the goblin-sapper and the bomb you found.'Do you see the shoreline to the east of us, across the river? Sometimes, I catch strange movement among the trees there. The guards dismiss it as nothing more than animals in the foliage, but I'm not so sure. Perhaps I'm being too cautious. Perhaps not.

'If you could go out there and check that nothing seems suspicious, I'd appreciate it.''Ugh. No matter where I go, those sappers end up haunting me. We don't even know where they're crafting these bombs. All I have is a name -- Thraunákh.

'But you've managed to get your hands on a bomb... that's valuable knowledge that you're holding. And I know folks who would appreciate that knowledge.'Trygvar, son of Runerik and Yrsa, has not eaten honey in over a day.Honey can be found at the forest's edge in the eastern reaches of the Floodfells.

You should gather honey for Limlók.Gather honey (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat frenzied bees (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Honey jars can be found in Limlók.

You should fill up the empty honey jars.'It has been a whole day! It has been well over a whole day since I last ate some honey! It is my favourite food, after all. I am not sure I can go much longer without that sweet nectar. I am not allowed to venture too far from Limlók right now or I'd head to the forest's edge to retrieve some. Perhaps you would be kind enough to gather some honey for me? Some extra for the village would certainly raise spirits in Limlók! I would watch out for the bees though. The Taurogrim have angered most every creature living at the forest's edge.'Mekhnet has plans to send a gift and a "gift," but can't make the trip herself.'Have you visited the soldiers at Ashmargathâl? I miss them dearly, even if I do not miss trudging through piles of snow. I'll return to my post soon enough, but before that happens, I'd like to deliver one of these cups to my wife who's stationed there.

'Of course, my gift isn't important enough to warrant official resources for its delivery, so I've been hesitant to ask. But you have a sapper-bomb, and Lúka would be interested in studying it. If you find yourself heading north at some point, you could give my cup to her too.

'Let her know that I'm on the mend, and please, please tell her not to worry. I'm sure she's already beside herself with grief.''Mekhnet sent you? Is she well? Have her wounds healed? I hope she is keeping warm.

'I'm sorry -- I haven't been able to see her since... well, it's been maddening. Though, if she's wood-carving for fun, then she must be fine.

'We've been managing, but we'd welcome the extra help.'Dursh Hûl occupies a strategic position, blocking the path to Tharkh Shathur.Destroying the Orcs' armour and weapons at Dursh Hûl will weaken the forces here.Destroy Orkish armour and weapons at Dursh HûlDestroying the Orcs' armour and weapons at Dursh Hûl will weaken the forces here.Destroy armour barrels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy weapon barrels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have sabotaged as much of the Orcs' equipment as you could find.CompletedDursh Hûl occupies a strategic position, blocking the path to Tharkh Shathur. Destroying the Orcs' armour and weapons will weaken the forces here.Without the necessary equipment, the Orcs of Dursh Hûl cannot hold out for long. Perhaps now the Haban'akkâ will have the chance to ascend the mountain.The Battle of Azanulbizar has ended, but at unimaginable cost.King Thráin can be found outside Muru Azog at Azanulbizar.

After the defeat of Azog and the clearing of Orkish filth from Azanulbizar, King Thráin wishes to honour his father, Thrór. You should talk to King Thráin.Talk to King ThráinKing Thráin can be found at Amdân.

After the burning of the dead at Azanulbizar, King Thráin and the survivors of the Sixth War of the Dwarves and Orcs have returned to Amdân. You should talk to King Thráin.Talk to King Thráin at AmdânDáin can be found at Amdân.

After the burning of the dead at Azanulbizar, King Thráin and the survivors of the Sixth War of the Dwarves and Orcs have returned to Amdân. You should talk to Dáin.'At last, Azog is slain and those of his kin who survived have been driven back into Khazad-dûm. Alas, we have borne many losses of our own....

'For nine years, I desired only to avenge my father, Thrór, and now that is done. Young Dáin took Azog's head in retribution, and now I would see it set upon a stake as a warning to Bolg and all those who would dare to challenge the Longbeards—nay, any of the Dwarves of the Seven Houses!

'Let us return to the gates of Khazad-dûm, son of the Iron Hills.''Let us go, Hermáth.''Although I wish it had gone elsewise, the war is ended.

'Too many were the omens that followed the death of Azog... and I must heed them.

'You fought admirably, Hermáth, as did so many of our kin. But they are now Marasrân—Burned Dwarves, and we remain to reckon with our fortunes, both good and ill.

'I hold hope that Khazad-dûm will yet be ours once more, but such a prize will not be easily won.''Alas for Azanulbizar, Hermáth. Better it to remain in our memory as it was in the time of Khazad-dûm!

'It will soon be time to return to the Iron Hills, my friend. I, for one, will be glad to leave this place behind.'Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, recalls a curious story about Fram, and wishes to investigate.Grazadh-gropor can be found in the south-east part of Misthallow.

