You should talk to Léothred.Talk to Léothred at LyndelbyLéothred can be found at Thokvist.

You should talk to Léothred.Talk to Léothred at Thokvist'Fram's friend! I just recalled an old children's song, which I believe mentions a hobbit? No, not a hobbit, but one perhaps of the little folk, or river folk, as they were sometimes called. Yes, there were some songs that told of Fram's friendship with a strange, Elfin folk in those lands who only revealed themselves to children; perhaps these songs were nonsense, as many such rhymes tend to be, but the resemblance between the Elfin folk and the Hol-búdlan of old cannot be ignored. You have heard tales of the Hol-búdlan too? Perhaps this is worth investigating!

'If there is any such remnant of the Hol-búdlan left in Misthallow, it will certainly not be in this valley. No, they most likely hid in the hills. There seems to be a path following the river, just east of here. Let us venture up it and see what we can find!''${PLAYER}, the Cabed Rhimdath can be a treacherous path. I would recommend that you do not traverse much further.'

You assure Fanglind that you will be fine.'Why do you continue upon the Cabed Rhimdath? What purpose could walking this dangerous path accomplish?'

You explain you are only exploring, acting as a guide to your Rohirrim companion, Léothred.

'Please heed my warnings, ${PLAYER}.'

You decide to continue.'I ask only one last time: turn around. You will not find anything that could possibly interest your Rohirrim friend at the end of the Cabed Rhimdath. All you will find is brambles.'

You decide to continue until the path becomes too difficult to traverse.'As I have said, this valley has nothing to do with the lost history of the Rohirrim people. Leave the village be, ${RACE}. The Holbytlan of Lyndelby will not show themselves to you.''Not much more is known about the Ai-thúda and the Holbytlan. It is said that late during Lord Thíudric’s time, the Holbytlan reappeared. Even then, they were considered nothing more than children’s stories. We know that Thíudric died at seventy-nine, and his son Gunthar became lord when already old and ruled only a short time. Lóthar followed, and ruled well. Sightings of the Holbytlan proliferated during his time, as the songs say. Lóthar’s reign was also long, and saw little strife, though it was not unmarred by sorrow.

'Come, let us return to Thokvist. I have acquired all that I need for my writings and I do not wish to overstay my welcome here... if welcome it can be called.''Taurogrim? That is not what I was hoping to find. Hmm, I did notice a possible path west of here, across the Langflood. Perhaps that might be worth investigating as well? It could be more of the Taurogrim too, but we shall never know if we do not explore! Come, let us be off!'

You notice Fanglind listening closely to your conversation.'Ah, right then, Thíudric, Gunthar, Lóthar. I see there in my writings a line of rulers where history becomes only lineage. Nothing more, nothing less. Those must have been peaceful times indeed.'A terrible stone-giant walks among the hills of Misthallow. Rumour says he is called Hregmód, but none can remember who might have given him that name, for no one to see him up close has survived.A terrible stone-giant walks among the hills of Misthallow. Rumour says that he is called Hregmód, and ending this creature would bring some amount of peace throughout the region.Defeat Hregmód, if that is his nameSearch among the hills of Misthallow, and see if you can find the creature called Hregmód. Be wary none can remember who might have given him that name, as no one to see him up close has survived.Defeat Hregmód, if that is his nameCompleted.CompletedYou have heard rumours of a terrible stone-giant living among the hills of Misthallow who goes by the name of Hregmód, The rumours also include stories of the damage he's caused to the homes and lives of those dwelling here, person and creature alike. Putting an end to Hregmód, if that is truly his name, will be of little relief to his countless victims, but Misthallow will be made safer without him in it.You have slain the stone-giant who stalked the hills of Misthallow and fed upon countless victims over the years. By doing so you have put an end to his reign of terror and learned that his name was in fact Hregmód, but now that he is slain you think it is best for that name to fade into obscurity, disappearing into the mists along this stretch of the Langflood.Madelgard Túcca's dusty home is in need of a good cleaning.Madelgard Túcca's home can be found in Lyndelby.

You should give Madelgard Túcca's home a good dusting.Give Madelgard Túcca's home a good dusting (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Madelgard Túcca can be found in Lyndelby.

You should talk to Madelgard Túcca.Talk to Madelgard Túcca in Lyndelby'Before you go, I have one more favour to ask of you. You see, my home is quite dusty, and quite big. Might you give it a good dusting for me? I would reward you for your efforts, of course!''Oh thank you! Not only did you sweep up all of that dust, you found all of those hiding rabbits in my home too!'Nestled in the foothills along the Greylin, a curious company of dwarves has built a small encampment in Elderslade.You have discovered a small company of dwarves at an encampment in the foothills of Elderslade.Discover the dwarf encampment in EldersladeYou have discovered a small company of dwarves at an encampment in the foothills of Elderslade. You should investigate the encampment and learn why these dwarves have settled in Elderslade.Investigate the dwarf-encampment in EldersladeThe dwarf encampment can be found in the foothills of eastern Elderslade.

After investigating the dwarf-encampment in Elderslade, your presence has caught the attention of one of the dwarves. You should speak to Nagli Want-kin.Talk to Nagli Want-kinWhile travelling through Elderslade, you have caught a glimpse of a strange sight. There appears to be a small dwarf-encampment in Elderslade, nestled in the foothills along the Greylin.

None of your maps have such a location marked on them, and it appears to have been built much more recently than any of the ruins that remain in Elderslade.

You should investigate the dwarf-encampment and attempt to learn why these dwarves have settled in Elderslade and so near to Mount Gundabad.'Well now... what brings you to the Slade, traveller?

'For my part, I am Nagli Want-kin... a free dwarf like all of the others who dwell here at Khadâsh-gâra.

'Come, let us talk.'Determined to outlast even the most concerted assaults of the Gabil'akkâ, Gorgar has ordered provisions be brought forth from the depths of Gundabad to maintain his hold on Bagûd-mekhem.Orkish provisions can be found at Bagûd-mekhem.

It appears that Gorgar has ordered that provisions be brought to Bagûd-mekhem in an attempt to draw out the War of Three Peaks. You should destroy Orkish provisions at Bagûd-mekhem.Destroy Orkish provisions at Bagûd-mekhem (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have destroyed many Orkish provisions at Bagûd-mekhem.CompletedDetermined to outlast even the most concerted assaults of the Gabil'akkâ, Gorgar has ordered provisions be brought forth from the depths of Gundabad. In addition to aiding his forces in maintaining their hold on Bagûd-mekhem, it seems Gorgar intends to draw out the War of Three Peaks.

You should destroy these Orkish provisions and put an end to Gorgar's plot.You have destroyed many Orkish provisions at Bagûd-mekhem, depriving Gorgar's forces of much-needed rations during the War of Three Peaks.After brokering an uneasy alliance between the forces of Gorgar the Ruthless and Hrímil Frost-heart, a powerful two-headed troll, Narg-and-Mauz, has taken command of the forces at Bagûd-mekhem.Narg-and-Mauz can be found deep within Bagûd-mekhem.

Náin has commanded you to defeat Narg-and-Mauz, the powerful two-headed troll that leads the forces at Bagûd-mekhem.Defeat Narg-and-MauzNáin the Slakeless can be found at Drenghól in the War of Three Peaks.

You have defeated Narg-and-Mauz. You should report to Náin with news of this victory.Talk to Náin at Drenghól'Now then, onto the matter at hand!

'My scouts have discovered that the forces at Bagûd-mekhem are being led by none other than a two-headed troll known as Narg-and-Mauz. It is our assumption that his... their... considerable stature allowed them to broker the uneasy alliance we've observed between Gorgar's Orcs and the Frost-horde at Bagûd-mekhem. However, I believe that if Narg-and-Mauz were not keeping their smaller subjects in line, that delicate peace would shatter like Orc-skulls beneath our axes!

'${PLAYER}, I need you to gather your allies and make for the deepest reaches of Bagûd-mekhem. Put an end to Narg-and-Mauz for the glory of the Gabil'akkâ!'Ha-ha! Well done, ${PLAYER}!

'Without Narg-and-Mauz to keep the peace at Bagûd-mekhem, it is likely that the Orcs' in-fighting will soon do our work for us! I only regret I could not join you in battle.

'Such are the demands of leadership... but I will have many words for King Thorin if Prince Durin thinks to keep me from the battle at the gates of Mount Gundabad!'As Prince Durin contemplates the course of the Gabil'akkâ, Glóin, father of Gimli and hero of the Lonely Mountain, has sent a letter requesting your aid at Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.Glóin has sent you a letter requesting your presence at Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.Glóin wishes to meet you in the war-room at Annâk-khurfuAs long as you are travelling with Imák, you might as well try to get along with the bristly Stout-axe.Imák has asked you to help him unpack some of the belongings he brought with him from Skarháld.Help Imák unpack his belongingsA small coffer among Imák's belongings has drawn your attention.Open the small cofferImák is very cross with you.Talk to Imák at your camp-site in MisthallowImák says he can smell wargs prowling about to the south-east, and the bristly Stout-axe has sent you to slay one or more of the creatures before they attain the numbers to brave your camp-site.Imák is at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Imák at your camp-site in MisthallowImák has fallen silent, and perhaps you should eat in silence and leave him be for a time.Eat in silence, leaving Imák to his thoughtsGlóin and Venko are at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Glóin at your camp-site in MisthallowTalk to Venko at your camp-site in Misthallow'Yes, very well, if you wish to make yourself helpful, do not let me stop you. We will not rest here for long, but I would welcome the chance to adjust the contents of these satchels. They have settled uncomfortably and caused me some discomfort while we walked.

'If you unpack that bag for me I will rearrange the contents while we rest. But expect no thanks for it, ${RACE}.''If you unpack that bag for me I will rearrange the contents while we rest.''If you unpack that bag for me I will rearrange the contents while we rest.''Not that one! Can you not follow instructions? Leave that coffer be, ${RACE}, for that is no business of yours! Poking around in my things like a common brigand! Bah!'

Imák throws his hands into the air.

'No, it is fine, ${PLAYER}. I should have said something first. That coffer contains a number of keepsakes, mere curios, really, but they are objects of some sentimentality for me. They remind me of friends and family less fortunate than I am. Some were not able to escape from Mordor, and others knew freedom for only a short time.

'Listen, ${PLAYER}, I am sorry I yelled at you. I am a little on edge because my nose is telling me there are wargs about. No, it is true; my sense of smell is very acute. If my nose says there are wargs a-hunting nearby, then wargs nearby are a-hunting! Search for tracks to the south-east and tell me it is not so! It is the least you can do after disturbing my privacy as you have done.''I am a little on edge because my nose is telling me there are wargs about. No, it is true; my sense of smell is very acute. If my nose says there are wargs a-hunting nearby, then wargs nearby are a-hunting! Search for tracks to the south-east and tell me it is not so! It is the least you can do after disturbing my privacy as you have done.''You found a hunting warg, didn't you? No need to say a word, ${PLAYER}, I know it is true!'

Imák nods with satisfaction, a pleased smile creeping onto his face.

'I have always had a heightened sense of smell, but in Mordor it became a matter of survival. I developed a nose for danger. Nowhere was ever truly safe, but I was able to sniff out the worst of the dangers, and steer clear of them! That skill is still with me; I could not ignore it if I tried.

'Like the keepsakes I brought out of Mordor, it is a constant reminder of my time toiling in fear of Sauron. I cannot forget, and will not.''Imák endured much during his captivity in Mordor. Never have I been within the borders of that land, and never have I heard anything pleasant about it. I do know this: I would not wish to have a heightened sense of smell in THAT place! For that Imák has my sympathy!''Of all my senses, my sense of smell has never been especially potent. Now, my sense of taste is another thing entirely! I have a taste for beer and for roasted mutton, although we may not have much of either on this journey.

'But Imák was right about the hunting warg. Perhaps he should take the first watch, and let his nose alert us to any approaching dangers!'Foreman Brumbur knows that more dwarves will cross the Rushgate soon and eyes the crumbling hills as a hazard to those making the journey through Elderslade.Rockslides can be found on the path west of the Rushgate in Elderslade.

You should remove the rockslides from the path for Foreman Brumbur.Remove the rockslides from the path (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rockslides can be found on the path west of the Rushgate in Elderslade.

You should search the hill for the source of the rockslides.Search the hill for the source of the rockslidesStone-claws and deep-claws can be found within the burrows of Klórgren in Elderslade.

You should defeat some of the stone-claws and deep-claws within Klórgren.Defeat mountain stone-claws within Klórgren (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat mountain deep-claws within Klórgren (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Put a stop to the rockslides troubling the roads.Completed'Well met, ${PLAYER}. I hope that your travels since Járnfast have been prosperous. Yes, I have decided to leave the iron mines for once and help my kin who journey towards Gundabad. You see, digging the mines in Járnfast is not that different from building a bridge. In fact I build bridges over chasms in the mines all of the time!

'Indeed, I consider it an honour to oversee the building of these temporary bridges and put my skills to good use! Now that we are able to safely set foot in Elderslade, there is another matter that needs attending. The road ahead is dangerous, for landslides seem numerous around here! Would you be able to tend to the road, and clear it of debris so that the path is once again safe for those who travel further west? If I had more time, I would investigate these landslides, for I believe something is causing them!'You have successfully cleared the road and put a halt to the rock slides that have been threatening all those who enter Elderslade. Tales of your deeds will surely reach Foreman Brumbur, who will certainly be pleased with your efforts. It concerns you, however, that the forces of Gundabad have done much to scar this sacred landscape.Léothred, a Rohirric scholar, has arrived at the Beorninghús, seeking permission to travel the through the Vales.Léothred can be found at the front gate of the Beorninghús.

You should talk to Léothred.Talk to Léothred at the front gate of the BeorninghúsGrimbeorn can be found at the Beorninghús.

You should talk to Grimbeorn.Talk to GrimbeornLéothred can be found at the front gate of the Beorninghús.

You should talk to Léothred.Talk to Léothred'Ah, it seems this Léothred you speak of has finally arrived. If he is merely a scholar seeking passage, I will hear his request. If he has any other purpose here, I ask you to send him on his way.''Ah, you must be ${PLAYER}. I am glad that you received my letter, and I am honoured that you would meet with me, great ${CLASS}. As I have told you in my letter, I came north to study the legacy of my people. The Rohirrim are rich with history, and I feel that I have an obligation to preserve their tale in writing. I wish not to trespass upon the wilds of the Beornings. If Grimbeorn would approve my short stay in his lands, I would be most grateful and repay him in kindness.''As I have told you, I am very busy, ${PLAYER}. If you wish to see Léothred to where he wants to go, I have no objection. All I ask is that he pays the toll for travel that all must pay. I am glad that you are here, ${RACE}. I do not wish for, nor have the time for guests!''You will guide me then? Splendid! What is this, a toll? Of course, I will pay it. It is the least I can do for the kindness that Grimbeorn has shown me!'Náin the Slakeless, Lord of the Iron Hills, has taken charge of Zarkul-sulûn with hopes to steer the dwarves to victory.Supplies can be found at Zarkul-sulûn.

You should take inventory of supplies for Náin the Slakeless.Take inventory (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Náin the Slakeless can be found at Zarkul-sulûn.

You should report back to Náin the Slakeless.Report back to Náin the Slakeless'Bah, there is nothing but rushed preparations here! There needs to be order in war, or the enemy will find cracks and weaknesses. I do not even know what supplies we have available here at Zarkul-sulûn. I am tasking you with taking inventory. This is important, and I know that I can trust you.''I thank you for taking this detailed inventory. Our stores seem to be in better shape than I hoped. Indeed, we can work with this.'A bat has delivered you a cryptic note. Where could this lead?The note contains directions to a specific location.

Asking the scouts around Annâk-khurfu about this bat may give you some much-needed context.All of the scouts you talked to about the bat have mentioned Rékkur, a dwarf who may be interested in what you've found.

He stands guard in front of Annâk-khurfu.Talk to RékkurThe note describes an overlook west of the path leading up to Zarkul-sulûn, a ruin west of Annâk-khurfu.

Rékkur has suggested that you venture to that location to see what's there.Go to the overlookThe note has brought you to the body of a dwarf scout near Zarkul-sulûn.

You should search the body and the immediate area for clues to what has transpired.Search the bodyRead the noteExamine the footprintsYou have found a new note, as well as possible clues to this scout's death.

Rékkur will want to hear about your discoveries back at Annâk-khurfu.The bat carries a piece of paper in its claws. As you approach, it recoils but then tentatively chirps, dropping the paper at your feet.

Scrawled onto the paper are directions to an unnamed location, but nothing more.

Perhaps you should ask scouts around Annâk-khurfu about this strange bat.'The bat carries useful information, to be sure, but it refuses to deliver messages to anyone other than Rékkur.'

The dwarf shudders.

'Fine by me. I dislike the look of those creatures.''Only a fool would trust everything the bat sends, and Rékkur is one such fool. I say this is a trick of the enemy's, to lure us in before striking!''You should talk to Rékkur, the dwarf standing guard in front of Annâk-khurfu. He has a keen interest in that bat, though I doubt we'll have the resources to uncover the identity of its owner soon.''Aye, the scouts have their doubts, but I know I've met that bat before.

'Not long ago, Orcs ambushed our supply train. We escaped and made camp. The next morning, our wounds were treated and bandaged! Though I never saw the kind soul who had saved us, I noticed that bat hanging overhead.

'The little one has gotten courageous, if it approached you and not me! What has it dropped off this time?'

He frowns at the note you show him.

'The messenger wants you to travel to the ruins directly west of here -- Zarkul-sulûn. Find an overlook to the west of the path leading up to the ruins. Whatever is there may be important.''What news, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell him of your discovery. His face falls when you describe the scout's appearance.

'Alas, I knew him. Gáfin is -- was, I mean -- a dear friend. I'd been waiting for him to return. 'Tis a dark day indeed, but I'll mourn later.'Goblins riding fierce Wargs pose a threat to the Haban'akkâ.Moria Warg-riders can be found roaming around Târ-kútoz.

Defeating these Warg-riders will help the dwarves at Ashmargathâl.Defeat Moria Warg-riders at Târ-kútozMoria Warg-riders can be found roaming around Târ-kútoz.

Defeating these Warg-riders will help the dwarves at Ashmargathâl.Defeat Moria Warg-riders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have weakened the Warg-riders of Târ-kútoz.CompletedGoblins riding fierce Wargs pose a threat to the Haban'akkâ, especially in the mountains where footing is treacherous. Defeating Warg-riders in Târ-kútoz will help the dwarves at Ashmargathâl.Your efforts have lessened the amount of Warg-riders in Târ-kútoz, and for that, the dwarves at Ashmargathâl are grateful.Although Edash discovered a cure for the Orkish poisons used by Azog and his clan, it has proven difficult to maintain a supply of twistbeard.Twistbeard can found on rocks in Azanulbizar.

Thorin has asked you to gather twistbeard for the Dwarves of the Haban'akkâ.Collect twistbeard (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thorin can be found at Amdân.

You have collected a great deal of twistbeard and should now deliver it to Thorin.'As you know, Edash has devised a clever cure for the Orkish poisons used by Azog's kin. However, his supply has again run short.

'You are familiar with twistbeard, yes? Gather as much as you can find in the dale, bring it to me, and I will see that Edash receives it at once!

'Be swift, my friend. Several of our warriors are in dire need of the antidote!''Ah, let us hope this will suffice!

'You have my thanks.'Eyes are set on the goblin Thraunákh in Atrad-zarakh, but the Orc camp of Târ-kútoz remains an obstacle.A recently-arrived Mekhnet wishes to speak with you.Talk to MekhnetOrc-sentries can be found in Târ-kútoz.

You should defeat them to ensure easier access to Atrad-zarakh later.Defeat Orc-sentries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated as many Orc-sentries as you could find.

You should report back to Lúka in Ashmargathâl.Talk to Lúka'The goblin Thraunákh must be in Târ-kútoz, holed up with all the bats he needs to create his sapper-bombs. We won't find him outside; I doubt the bats would willingly suffer the elements. Which means... they've made the watch-tower Atrad-zarakh their new home.

'If we are to stop Thraunákh, we must first clear out a path through Târ-kútoz. We ought to target the Orc-sentries -- they are the eyes and ears of the camp.

'I'll come with you. I have no desire to see another dwarf get hurt --

'Ah, wait a moment. It seems that Mekhnet has something to say to you.''I am putting my foot down -- Lúka needs her rest! I know you can handle yourself, maybe even better alone than accompanied by a sleep-deprived dwarf.

'Quickly, let me distract her. Head to Târ-kútoz, defeat their sentries, and come straight back. I wouldn't want you entering that watch-tower without help!''I should have known better than to trust Mekhnet the moment you disappeared. But what's done is done -- getting into Atrad-zarakh will be safer now. 'Disturbed by the presence of Angmarim still loyal to the Iron Crown in Elderslade, Glóin has asked you to rally with one of his scouts, Múta, and investigate the settlement of Caivád Sâr.Múta can be found somewhere near Caivád Sâr in northern Elderslade.

Glóin has asked you to rally with one of his scouts, Múta, near the Angmarim settlement of  Caivád Sâr. You should travel north and find Múta.Find Múta at Caivád SârMúta can be found outside Caivád Sâr in northern Elderslade.

You have found Múta and you should now talk to her.Talk to Múta at Caivád Sâr'One of my scouts, Múta, has travelled north to the Angmarim settlement at Caivád Sâr.

'She is a cunning Stout-axe, and I would have you aid her in repelling the forces of the Iron Crown that now dwell in Elderslade. Chief among them is a sorcerer of some infamy, Bandrauth Adder-fang. In addition, we know little of why so many Angmarim have migrated to Elderslade... aside from recent happenings in Angmar.

'Múta can tell you more of each of these, ${PLAYER}. As for me, I must return to my duties.

'Be swift, my friend!''Ah! Are you the one Commander Glóin promised to send?

'I am Múta, and that...'

Múta gestures toward the Angmarim village nearby.

'...is Caivád Sâr.'Deep in the frozen wilds of Forochel, the Arnorian fortress Agoroth lies hidden. Although no living souls have set foot there for thousands of years, you now find yourself in a race to reach its vault before the forces of Angmar.You have arrived inside the Narrowdelve.

