You should distribute bone combs to bedraggled dwarves.Elgur is located in Amdân.

You should return to Elgur.'The army has been on the move and on the attack for so long that I fear some of our brethren have forgotten better days. What seems a small thing may in truth be a large comfort. I have carved these combs from bone and horn. Please, bring them to those in need. Our beards are our pride, and our bedraggled friends will appreciate freeing their locks from the tangles and knots travel and fighting have brought on.''A beard comb? What are you implying! Well, give it here. Might as well put it to use.''What's this now? A comb? I was going to get around to that... give it here, then.''Ah, a comb? Thank you, friend. My thick fingers are no match for these snarls.''For me? You have my thanks. I will be sure to pass this along to others in need as well.''What now? No, I haven't got a comb for you. Oh! You offer it to me! Well isn't that finely made. Give Elgur my thanks.''Oh! Give it here, will you? My own comb broke weeks ago. Well, I suppose you could tell that.''Excellently done, friend. This was a small thing, but is one that has the potential to impact our brethren greatly. No dwarf should go to war with his beard a tangled mess.'Happy memories are hard to come by during war. Mekhnet, an injured soldier, has a favour to ask.Mekhnet has requested that you spend some time with soldiers who are resting around Amdân.Mekhnet sits near the Kambráda encampment in the eastern part of Amdân. She might like to hear about how her fellow soldiers are doing.Talk to Mekhnet'Well met, stranger. Come to join this lonely soul? I can't say there's much to report. I've spent the last few months waiting for my wounds to heal, so I've been carving.' 

The dwarf holds up twin wooden cups. 

'It passes the time well enough. I mean to keep one of these cups for myself. The other is for my wife.

'Hmm... you seem like a good listener, and I think our comrades would appreciate that. Why not seek out those who are resting here and talk with them a bit? Surely anything must be better than being alone with their thoughts.''I have nothing to talk about, no family or friends left to speak of, but... come, sit with me.'

You sit next to him for several minutes. No words are exchanged, but the dwarf seems to find comfort in the relative silence that follows.

Before you get up to leave, he nods in farewell.'Share a drink? It's just water, but I mustn't drink anything heavier.

'So many soldiers miss their loved ones, but I miss the taste of ale. Not the ale we get here, but the ale from taverns. Tied to good memories, mostly. Drinking with friends.'

She sips some water.

'Ah well. Can't get everything we want.''These years have not been kind to me, but I hope they have been kinder to my family.'

The dwarf shows you a piece of paper he's been sketching on. The rough drawing of a bright-eyed child smiles from the page.

'My daughter, or at least, how I remember her. I'll survive to see her again, mark my words.''That was kind of you. There is nothing wrong with reminiscing about better days during dark times.

'Say -- I think there's something you could help me with.'After gaining a foothold at the dwarf-ruins of Zudramdân, Glóin hopes to expand the reach of the Gabil'akkâ in Elderslade.Zudramdân can be found to the north-west of Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.

Glóin has asked you to travel to Zudramdân and rally with one of his scouts, Gárjan. You should travel to Zudramdân.Travel to ZudramdânGárjan can be found at Zudramdân.

