Durin has asked you to return to the war-front and seek the counsel of his uncle, Náin, at Zarkul-sulûn.Talk to Náin the Slakeless at Zarkul-sulûnEnemies can be found throughout the War of Three Peaks. Agnar Brokentooth, Lági Helmbiter, and Reithvald Red-ember of the Kharum-ubnâr can be found at Zarkul-sulûn and nearby encampments during the War of Three Peaks.

Náin the Slakeless, uncle of Durin, has asked you to present Durin's plan to reclaim Mount Gundabad to Agnar Brokentooth, Lági Helmbiter, and Reithvald Red-ember. He has also asked you to slay enemies of the Gabil'akkâ in the War of Three Peaks.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You may access the War of Three Peaks by using the Blazon of the Gabil'akkâ behind Durin in Annâk-khurfu or by taking a travel route from the stable-master outside Annâk-khurfu.</rgb>Defeat enemies in the War of Three Peaks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to Agnar BrokentoothTalk to Lági HelmbiterTalk to Reithvald Red-emberNáin the Slakeless can be found at Zarkul-sulûn in the War of Three Peaks.

You have slain many foes in the War of Three Peaks, and you have heard the guidance of Agnar, Lági, and Reithvald in regard to Prince Durin's plan to reclaim Gundabad. You should return to Náin.Talk to Náin the Slakeless at Zarkul-sulûnDurin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu.

Náin the Slakeless has advised you to bring word to Prince Durin that he should stay his hand in the war and consult his father, Thorin, at the Lonely Mountain.Talk to Durin at Annâk-khurfu'Before this war began, King Thorin sent three elder warriors to aid his son: Agnar Brokentooth, Lági Helmbiter, and Reithvald Red-ember. They are Dwarves of the Kharum-ubnâr, the Brethren of Survivors. It is an order little known outside the line of Durin's Folk, but its members hold a place of high esteem in the eyes of King Thorin, and more importantly, those of his brother, Náin.

'I would wager there is little that might temper his fervour for battle as much as the word of one of the Kharum-ubnâr — let alone three of them!

'I believe Prince Durin intends to send word to Zarkul-sulûn that his uncle might be apprised of the plan to reclaim Mount Gundabad. It so happens that Agnar, Lági, and Reithvald are among his garrison. Need I explain myself further, ${PLAYER}? This must be done delicately, that the Prince is not undermined in the eyes of the Gabil'akkâ.

'You are a trusted friend, ${PLAYER}, and I am certain the Prince would send you on this errand should you ask.''Let us hope the guidance of the Kharum-ubnâr is enough!''Ah, ${PLAYER}! It is good to see you here once again, my friend.

'For my part, I have not sat idle since the battle at the gates of Mount Gundabad! After the death of Ránulur, Gorgar fled in fear from the Gabil'akkâ! And yes, I am quite aware that Hrímil Frost-heart may have influenced his actions, but it was a victory nonetheless. After so much hardship, a mere wall of ice cannot keep me from my prize!

'I saw you speaking with Glóin. All is well, I hope?'

You tell Durin that Glóin told you of a new plan to reclaim the Mountain-home and you request that you might be the one to bring word of it to Náin.

'Indeed! It is my belief that we should breach the ice on the morrow and hunt down Gorgar within the halls of Gundabad. Dwarves are hardier than Orcs, after all, and if we grant our enemies too much rest, all of our efforts in this war would be for naught. We are so near to victory, my friend! We mustn't let up now!

'As for your request, it is granted. Deliver word of my plans to Náin, and see if he finds it to his liking!''If my uncle and I are in agreement, we will press the attack at dawn!''How fares my nephew at Annâk-khurfu, ${PLAYER}?'

You exchange welcomes with Náin, and then tell him of Prince Durin's plans to reclaim Gundabad. After a moment, you ask Náin what the Kharum-ubnâr in his garrison might make of it.

'It is a sound plan to my ear, but my eagerness for battle is well-known. I would not even wait for the morrow, were I a younger dwarf!'

Náin pauses.

'It may be that Thorin is not as well-suited to battle as I am, but he is keen in judgement. It was not by a mere whim that my brother sent three of the Kharum-ubnâr to aid the Prince. Beyond those three warriors, none of the Longbeards in the Gabil'akkâ, even old Glóin, have seen so much of battle and its costs.

'I will honour your request, if you would honour one of my own. I have seen Gorgar's ilk, all manner of evil beasts, and even some of those wicked Men of Angmar drawing nearer to Zarkul-sulûn since the battle at the gates, and I would not have them doubt our mettle. Slay them to the last!

'Let us see how the Kharum-ubnâr judge Durin's plan, ${PLAYER}.''Well, what had the elders of the Kharum-ubnâr to say of the Prince's plan?''What does Lord Náin ask of me?'

You tell Agnar about Prince Durin's plan to reclaim Mount Gundabad and ask what he thinks of it.

'We did what we could to hinder Gorgar and his army of Orcs on the field, but these old bones are not as strong as they were when I was a younger dwarf. I am as eager as Prince Durin to return to Gundabad, but it is a difficult prize to grasp — and I should know!

'After all, I was there when Komog, son of Azog, fell broken in its halls, and I remember how it fell just as quickly back into the hands of the Orcs after the war!

'Do not misunderstand me, ${PLAYER}. I think much better of our chances this time, but I would not press the attack until we are better prepared for what awaits us within.''It is good to see you, ${PLAYER}. But what has brought you here?'

You present Prince Durin's plan to reclaim Mount Gundabad to Lági and ask his thoughts on it.

'Although he has led the Gabil'akkâ admirably through this war, I have many misgivings in regard to the Prince's plan.

'Just look at the soldiers here in Zarkul-sulûn. They are tired.

'We spent the lives of many dwarves to win the gates of Mount Gundabad, but for those who survived, it is now a matter of will — and not all of the dwarves in the Gabil'akkâ can bear such a burden. Hardship is to be expected in war, but many of these soldiers do not know it as I do.

'In my youth, we spent six years of bitter war fighting our enemies, but even in the height of his fury, King Thráin knew the value of respite from battle. Many look back at that war as a single, great battle at Azanulbizar, but had we fought those years without rest, we would never have made it to the gates of Khazad-dûm.

'If Prince Durin would hear my advice, it is that our soldiers need rest and the time to heal for the coming battles. We have been fortunate thus far in the War of Three Peaks, but winning a battle does not win a war!''Lord Náin has sent you? I have much to report, ${PLAYER}, some for good and some for ill.

'Of the good, our defences have held against the Orcs and their numbers have dwindled since our encounter at the gates of Mount Gundabad. Those few that make their way to our line are unorganized and easy prey for the Zhélruka. In the past several days, I am proud to report that we have not lost a single dwarf!

'Ah, but of the bad, it should come as little surprise. We are still engaged in war, my friend. There is no time to simply take shelter and rest while Gorgar hides within the Mountain-home. After all, there are other ways out of that mountain and the Orcs must know all of them.'

You interject to ask Reithvald about Prince Durin's plan to reclaim Gundabad.

'Ah, that is not so simple a matter. My soldiers are in need of rest. I have seen enough of battle to know the signs of fatigue, even if they are too proud to admit it.

'Durin holds some of his army in reserve at Annâk-khurfu, and I would urge him to send as many soldiers as he can spare to relieve us. Even if only for a short time, it would be of great help to us for the coming battles!''So you return, ${PLAYER}. What did they have to say of Prince Durin's plans?'

You inform Náin of what Agnar, Lági, and Reithvald told you.

'I should have expected such wisdom from the Dwarves of the Kharum-ubnâr! In truth, I find myself in agreement with them, but a dwarf of my reputation could hardly deliver such news to Durin. I would be called Náin the Slaked, and that is no title for the Lord of the Iron Hills!

'Were it my army, I would muster more forces to Elderslade and call upon distant cousins and allies, but that would be a matter to bring before my brother, Thorin. Ah, for once, I am pleased such a decision is not left to me!

'You must return to Annâk-khurfu on my behalf, ${PLAYER}. Let this be my guidance to the Prince: seek the wisdom of your father and stay your hand for the moment that it might soon swing with renewed strength.''Ah, for once, I am pleased such a decision is not left to me!''${PLAYER}, what did Náin have to say of my plan?'

You deliver Náin's guidance to Prince Durin. The Prince's face becomes stern, betraying the faintest hint of embarrassment.

'I must admit, my friend, that is not the response I expected. But if uncle Náin believes I should send word to my father that the Gabil'akkâ might be the better for it, I have no reason to doubt his judgment. After all, it was not so long ago that I spent my days at the Quenching Trough in Járnfast, spared the battle before the Lonely Mountain by the will of my father. Ah, but when it is my uncle who urges restraint, only a fool would ignore his counsel!

'Perhaps I was too swift to dismiss Glóin's concerns. I shall have to apologize to him for my imprudence, if I might.

'It seems I have much still to learn, despite my station.'After resolving to aid King Thorin Stonehelm and Prince Durin, Hermáth Stormhammer and Hersegg prepare to join the Gabil'akkâ at Elderslade.King Thorin can be found in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor.

Hoping to set out for Elderslade, Hermáth Stormhammer has urged you to bid your farewells to King Thorin. You should find King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain.Find King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in EreborKing Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

After preparing for the journey to Elderslade, Hermáth Stormhammer has suggested the three of you speak to King Thorin. You should talk to King Thorin.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in EreborHermáth Stormhammer can be found before King Thorin's throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

After bidding farewell to King Thorin, Hermáth and Hersegg appear ready to begin the journey to Elderslade. You should talk to Hermáth Stormhammer.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer in the Hall Under the Mountain, in EreborAnnâk-khurfu can be found in Elderslade.

You should travel to Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade with Hermáth and Hersegg.Travel to Annâk-khurfu in EldersladeHermáth Stormhammer can be found outside Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.

You have arrived at Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade. You should talk to Hermáth Stormhammer.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer at Annâk-khurfuHersegg can be found outside Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.

You have arrived at Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade and Hermáth Stormhammer has gone to speak to Prince Durin. You should talk to Hersegg.Talk to Hersegg at Annâk-khurfuDurin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu.

You have returned to Annâk-khurfu at last. You should find and talk to Durin.Find Prince Durin and Hermáth Stormhammer in the war-room at Annâk-khurfuDurin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu.

After Hermáth Stormhammer's arrival at Annâk-khurfu, Durin appears equally confused and awed. You should find and talk to Durin.Talk to Durin at Annâk-khurfu'It's time we set out, I think!

'I am not the young dwarf I once was, so better we travel sooner than later. Ah, but before we go, you should speak to King Thorin. After all, he will wish to know that another dwarf is coming with us!'

Hermáth gestures to Hersegg.

'Come along, ${PLAYER}. We ought to find him in the Hall Under the Mountain!''Ah, ${PLAYER}!

'It is high time you returned to my son, I think. After all, there is a war to be won and the Mountain-home awaits!

'Although I am certain my father Dáin would have been just as eager to join in the reclamation of Mount Gundabad, I am pleased that Hermáth will be there to guide Durin in the coming days of his campaign.

'You have done much to aid my son. For that, I and all of Durin's Folk thank you. May the Gabil'akkâ triumph!''A moment, ${PLAYER}....'

Hermáth Stormhammer looks to King Thorin and speaks.

'Do not fear for your son, Thorin. Your father, Dáin, was so proud when he was born, and rightly so! Each of us hoped that our sons would be Durin reborn... and though it was not fated to be for my son, I still hold hope that Durin will embody his namesake when the time is right.'

Hermáth Stormhammer turns back to you.

'Lead the way, my new friend... the Prince awaits us!'


<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will swiftly travel to Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade when you advance this quest.</rgb>'It has been a long time... but the Slade is still as I remember it.

'This old fortress had a different name during the Sixth War, but Annâk-khurfu is more fitting, I think.

'I am glad that our paths have crossed, ${PLAYER}. When the time comes to reclaim Gundabad, I will consider myself fortunate to have you at my side—and I am sure Durin will think much the same!

'I ought to not keep Prince Durin waiting, so I will meet you inside, my friend.''So this is Elderslade? Hmmph, it is smaller than it seemed in grandfather's stories!'

Hersegg pauses.

'It is beautiful, though, and Mount Gundabad is greater than I could have ever imagined. Ah, but this is not a time for tours. I should introduce myself to the Dwarves of the Gabil'akkâ and see what needs to be done!

'I'm sure I'll see you around, ${PLAYER}. After all, this won't be the last time you hear my name!''When I asked my father to send his aid, I did not expect you to return with Hermáth Stormhammer!'

Durin appears overwhelmed.

'Hermáth and I have spoken since his arrival, and we have agreed that the Gabil'akkâ will march on Mount Gundabad at dawn—in seven days, of course! Those that would answer the muster will need time to travel to Elderslade, and I eagerly await their arrival. Every dwarf shall have a role to play in the reclamation of Mount Gundabad!'As King Thorin prepares to call upon the Longbeards and their allies to muster to Mount Gundabad, his thoughts turn to the Kharum-ubnâr of the Lonely Mountain.Akrâz-zahar can be found in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in Erebor.

King Thorin has asked you to deliver a missive to Hersegg, granddaughter of Hermáth Stormhammer, at Akrâz-zahar that the wisdom of the Kharum-ubnâr might help guide Durin to victory. You should travel to Akrâz-zahar.Travel to Akrâz-zahar in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in EreborFrithgeir can be found at Akrâz-zahar in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in Erebor.

Upon entering Akrâz-zahar, you have come across the dwarf, Frithgeir, and his companion, Nár. You should talk to Frithgeir.Talk to Frithgeir at Akrâz-zaharNár can be found at Akrâz-zahar in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in Erebor.

Frithgeir hopes Nár might bid you well in your quest before he returns home to rest. You should hail Nár, and see if he wishes to say anything.<rgb=#FF00FF>Hail</rgb> Nár at Akrâz-zaharFrithgeir can be found at Akrâz-zahar in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in Erebor.

Nár's mind appears clouded, but he seemed to wish you well. You should talk to Frithgeir.Talk to Frithgeir at Akrâz-zaharHersegg can be found at Akrâz-zahar in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in Erebor.

After seeing Frithgeir and Nár off, you should return to your task and find Hersegg at Akrâz-zahar.Find Hersegg in Akrâz-zaharHersegg can be found at Akrâz-zahar in the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, in Erebor.

You have found Hersegg at Akrâz-zahar. You should deliver King Thorin's missive to Hersegg.'My son asks my guidance in winning a war.'

Thorin chuckles to himself.

'I may not be a warrior in the manner of my father, ${PLAYER}, but I know well enough that those of Kharum-ubnâr learned such lessons long ago.

'After all, it was Dáin who organized the Kharum-ubnâr after the war's end. It was to be comprised of those who survived the Sixth War, and those descended from Marasrân—the Burned Dwarves of Azanulbizar. Agnar, Lági, and Reithvald are not the only dwarves of that order who still dwell within the Lonely Mountain, my friend. Perhaps their wisdom might be of use to my son....'

Thorin thinks for a moment, stroking his beard. After a moment, he produces a small scrap of paper and writes upon it a short message.

'Within the Dwelling-halls of Tumnu-dûm, there is an order-hall of the Kharum-ubnâr. It is known among Durin's Folk as Akrâz-zahar, and there you might find a dwarf named Hersegg. She is barely beyond the age of a stripling, but she is the granddaughter of one of the greatest heroes of Azanulbizar, and more importantly, one of the dearest friends of my father, Dáin.

'I have written her a note asking that she might introduce you to the works of her grandfather, Hermáth Stormhammer. She, too, is well-studied in the lore of Durin's Folk, possessing wisdom beyond her years.

'Deliver this missive to her, and she will understand what I ask.''Deliver this missive to Hersegg at Akrâz-zahar, my friend.''${PLAYER}! What brings you back to the Lonely Mountain?

'I heard you followed Prince Durin in his quest. Was that not so?'

You explain to Frithgeir that you have returned at Durin's request to seek the aid of King Thorin.

'I see. Well, do not let me keep you, my friend. For my part, I was just about to take Nár home. It has been a long day, and although he was more himself this morning, his mind... has wandered. Perhaps Nár might wish you well in your quest, but I can make no promise.

'Nár?'Nár smiles happily, but he does not appear to notice you.'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. It means a great deal to me, and I know it does Nár good to see his friends.

'Wherever Prince Durin's quest might lead you, I hope you take care of yourself, my friend. Perhaps we will see you when next you return to the Lonely Mountain!'Hersegg stands behind a bar within Akrâz-zahar, dutifully cleaning plates and mugs.'What business have you with the Kharum-ubnâr?'

Hersegg appears to tighten her grip on a weapon beneath your view. You swiftly explain that you have been sent by King Thorin to deliver a missive to Hersegg.

'A missive from King Thorin? I do hope this isn't about the cave-claws again. Last time was an accident, I swear it!'After seeking the counsel of his uncle, Náin, Durin has chosen to send word to his father, King Thorin III Stonehelm, at the Lonely Mountain.Letters and parcels can be found within and without at Annâk-khurfu.

Durin has asked you to collect the letters and parcels of Longbeards fighting in the War of Three Peaks before you depart for the Lonely Mountain.Collect letters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect parcels (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Durin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu.

You have collected all of the letters and parcels prepared by the Longbeards of the Gabil'akkâ. You should return to Durin.Talk to Durin at Annâk-khurfuKing Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

Durin has written a missive to be delivered to his father, Thorin, at Erebor. You should deliver the letters, parcels, and Durin's missive to King Thorin at Erebor.King Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

You should talk to King Thorin.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in EreborKing Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

King Thorin is musing on thoughts of his son, Durin, and his father, Dáin.Listen to King Thorin's thoughts of his father, DáinListen to King Thorin's thoughts of his son, DurinKing Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

You should talk to King Thorin.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor'So be it, ${PLAYER}.

'I must admit, I had not expected to send another messenger to the Lonely Mountain until this war was ended. I had hoped to send tidings of victory to my father—that I might finally prove myself a dwarf worthy of the name "Durin"!

'Bah, perhaps it was folly to entertain such stubbornness! If my uncle believes the Gabil'akkâ would be helped by again swelling its numbers, I see no reason to argue with him.'

Durin looks across the upper hall of Annâk-khurfu.

'I will need some time to draft a missive to my father, but I have had an idea!

'Many of the Longbeards here at Annâk-khurfu have written letters and prepared parcels to be delivered to their kin at the Lonely Mountain. Unfortunately, a great deal of them have gone unsent in recent days. It would please me to correct this oversight at the same time!

'I will set to writing my father at once. After you have gathered all of the letters and parcels, return to me.''I will need some time to draft a missive to my father, so gather the letters and parcels here at Annâk-khurfu.''Deliver the missive to my father as swiftly as you are able, ${PLAYER}!''Welcome, ${PLAYER}.

'It has been some time since Glóin's message-raven arrived, bearing a warning that the line of Azog remains unbroken in Gundabad. What news do you bring of my son? How fares his campaign?'

You inform King Thorin of the events that have transpired since he sent the remnants of the Iron Garrison to Annâk-khurfu. You tell the tale of the battle on the threshold of Gundabad—of Gorgar's near-defeat and of the sudden danger of Hrímil Frost-heart, returned from the Anvil of Winterstith. You also tell King Thorin of Durin's desire to press the attack and how he was persuaded against it, at least for the moment, by Náin and dwarves of the Kharum-ubnâr.

'I feared you had come bearing ill tidings, my friend. So long as my son is well, it puts my heart at ease.'

Thorin glances at the mass of letters and parcels you have brought to Erebor.

'I appreciate that you answered me as swiftly as you did, but please, set all of that down. My subjects will see that they are delivered to the proper dwarves here in the Lonely Mountain.

'As for his missive, let us see what he has written.''A moment, my friend.'

King Thorin reads the missive from Durin.

'I see. Tell me, ${PLAYER}, how did my son come to change his mind, truly?'

You tell King Thorin of Glóin's concern and of his suggestion to speak to the Dwarves of the Kharum-ubnâr among the Gabil'akkâ.

'It was for that reason I sent them, and I am heartened that Glóin warned him against plans borne of vengeance. I only wish my son had heeded his words. Durin leads the greatest Longbeard army we have brought to bear in seventy years, but he is still young and, in truth, untested. Yes, he has found victory at many stops along his journey from the Iron Hills, but it is hardship that shapes a dwarf!

'Durin was fortunate to know my father, Dáin, in his time. He, too, was young when he answered the call of war, and I often wonder what he would think of how swiftly Durin has grown into his name.''I have thought often both of my son, and of my father, Dáin, since Durin set off into the Grey Mountains.'As he reminisces about Dáin, Thorin's voice softens:

'What can be said of my father, Dáin, that has not been etched into the very history of Durin's Folk?

'He was but a stripling when he slew the Great-orc, Azog, at Azanulbizar. It was he who answered the call of Thorin Oakenshield and defended the Lonely Mountain against Bolg and the goblins of the North, and in so doing, ushered in a time of peace between Men, Elves, and dwarves. And again, when the Enemy sent his servants to the gates of the Lonely Mountain, he died in defence of his people—a hero to his last breath!

'But despite all of his great deeds, I knew him as my father. I was his son, and Durin, his beloved grandson.

'I must confess that I often saw more of Dáin in Durin than I did in myself, but what greater dwarf could one aspire to be?'As his thoughts turn to Durin, Thorin's voice swells:

'I have always been the more cautious of Dáin's sons, and it has served me well. But for too long, I believed it would suit Durin in much the same manner.

'It was not so long ago that I sequestered Durin in the Iron Hills while battle raged in the Dale-lands, but now my son leads an army to reclaim the Mountain-home. I dared not dream that Gundabad might be within reach, especially after the failure of the Iron Garrison, and yet, my son has proven its possibility, both to me and to all of Durin's Folk.

'Durin is not as hot-tempered as my brother, nor is he as measured as his father—he is himself, and that is enough. Durin will err, as any dwarf will in his time, but I did not name him Durin that he would earn the name in his life—the name will earn him through his deeds.''It is true that many Dwarves of the Iron Garrison pledged themselves into the service of my son, and more still journeyed from our holdings in the Misty Mountains to aid the Gabil'akkâ. Even so, there are many Longbeards and trustworthy Dwarves of the Landorrim and Narfanghoth that might still be called upon.

'Durin has requested that I send a new muster to the Mountain-home. It will be done!'

Thorin pauses for a moment and strokes his beard.

'But so too does my son ask for my guidance, and that has given me much to consider.''Ah, very good!

'I have written my father a formal request for a new muster to Elderslade—one that comprises all of our trusted allies, even those unskilled in combat. When the time comes to reclaim Gundabad, we will have need for dwarves of every vocation!

'I have also made special note that I will not begin the assault on Mount Gundabad until the time of your return. If my father wishes to offer any guidance, I would prefer to hear it before the battle is upon us!'

Durin seals the missive he has written to his father and hands it to you.

'By entrusting you with this missive, I am entrusting you with the success of the Gabil'akkâ, my friend.

'Be swift and let nothing turn you from your path to the Lonely Mountain!'After you delivered a missive to Hersegg, granddaughter of Hermáth Stormhammer, and learned of the Sixth War of the Dwarves and Orcs, King Thorin has tasked you with guiding his swiftest riders to send word of the muster to the Mountain-home.King Thorin's riders can be found at the Lonely Mountain, Erebor.

King Thorin has asked you to deliver missives to his riders at Erebor. You should deliver missives to King Thorin's riders.Deliver a missive to Fregi at EreborDeliver a missive to Garni at EreborDeliver a missive to Skalla at EreborDeliver a missive to Uddvar at EreborDeliver a missive to Vísbur at EreborKing Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

You have delivered all of the missives to King Thorin's riders at Erebor. You should return to King Thorin.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor'Let us make certain that Durin receives his reinforcements, ${PLAYER}. The Gabil'akkâ must be well-prepared for the conquest of the Mountain-home!

'To that end, I have written a number of missives to be delivered to other Longbeard holdings across the world as well as those of our cousins, the Landorrim and Narfanghoth. Among several others, I have called upon all the dwarves of Thorin's Hall, Gabilazan, and even Gabilshathûr in the realm of old Angmar. None shall be excluded from joining the Gabil'akkâ, for as my son rightly said, there will be need for dwarves of all vocations at Gundabad in the days ahead.

In addition, I have drafted missives to the Kings of the Temámir, the Abnúzhu, and... the Kámbrada. After all, it is high time they learned of their cousins from Mordor! I do not know if any among them will send word in return, but I hold hope that the old friendships between our peoples have not been forgotten.

'I ask that you seek out Skalla, Vísbur, Fregi, Garni, and Uddvar here at Erebor, and see that they bring word of the muster to the Mountain-home!

'Will you deliver these missives to my riders, ${PLAYER}?''Deliver these missives to my riders at once!''It is done? Very good, my friend.

'Let us hope their errands are done swiftly and without incident. If the reclamation is to succeed, a great many dwarves must answer the call to Elderslade!'To the surprise of both you and the dwarf Hersegg, King Thorin's missive directed Hersegg to share with you her grandfather's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar.Hersegg can be found at Akrâz-zahar in Erebor, the Lonely Mountain.

Hersegg has offered to tell you Nár's tale as transcribed by her grandfather, Hermáth Stormhammer. You should talk to Hersegg.Talk to Hersegg and learn of 'Nár's Tale'Hersegg can be found at Akrâz-zahar in Erebor, the Lonely Mountain.

You have listened to Hersegg's telling of Nár's tale as transcribed by her grandfather, Hermáth Stormhammer. You should talk to Hersegg.Talk to Hersegg at Akrâz-zahar'Tales of Yore: Azanulbizar' can be found at Akrâz-zahar within the Lonely Mountain.

After learning that King Thorin wishes for you to learn more of the Battle of Azanulbizar, Hersegg has directed you to the works of her grandfather, Hermáth Stormhammer, collected in a volume known as 'Tales of Yore: Azanulbizar'.Begin your study of Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar
<rgb=#FF0000>(Use 'Tales of Yore: Azanulbizar' in Akrâz-zahar to travel to Azanulbizar, T.A. 2799)</rgb>'Tales of Yore: Azanulbizar' can be found at Akrâz-zahar within the Lonely Mountain.

You have begun your study of Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar. You should complete your study before returning to King Thorin.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You must complete the deed 'The Battle of Azanulbizar' to advance this quest.</rgb>Complete your study of Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of AzanulbizarKing Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

You have finished your study of Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar. You should return to King Thorin.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor'Let me see what all this is about!'

Hersegg looks over the missive from King Thorin. Her bushy eyebrows raise and lower a few times until finally she lets out what seems to be a sigh of relief.

'Well, that wasn't what I expected....

'I must apologize for my curt introduction, ${PLAYER}. I am Hersegg, and I tend to Akrâz-zahar on behalf of the Kharum-ubnâr.

'As for that missive, it seems King Thorin wishes for me to introduce you to the works of my grandfather, Hermáth Stormhammer. He kept a detailed account of his experiences in the Sixth War of the Dwarves and Orcs, especially the Battle of Azanulbizar. In fact, a collection of his volumes are right over there! Although I am sure Prince Durin has heard tales of that war before, the King insists your study of it will be of use to his son and the Gabil'akkâ.

'But it's no use studying the battle without hearing the beginning of the tale, I say. King Thorin has given me a task of my own, but since I have a little time, I'd be honoured to read it to you.

'So, what do you say?''As for Nár, I think it's good that he has finally left Zudrugund behind.

'Since his return, Nár has made many friends in the Lonely Mountain. When he feels well enough to visit us here in Akrâz-zahar, I have heard his laugh and seen the light in his eye when he smiles. Although it pains me to even think such a thing, perhaps his addlement has been something of a mercy — some small kindness bestowed upon him by the First Crafter.

'Let us hope that Nár has found some measure of happiness again, even at so old an age. It is the very least he deserves!

'Now then, I must tend to the task King Thorin gave to me, and you ought to begin your studies of my grandfather's work!'

Hersegg gestures to a stack of books atop the nearby table.

'Those will tell you all you need to know about the Battle of Azanulbizar, ${PLAYER}. When you've studied it enough for your liking, you are to return to the King Under the Mountain.

'With any luck, I'll see you again soon.''What did you make of Hermáth's account, my friend?'

You talk to King Thorin about Hermáth's account of the battle, making note of certain moments.

'Ah, very good. I am told it is quite a faithful retelling of the battle... speaking as a dwarf who wasn't there, of course!

'While you were off with the Kharum-ubnâr, I had time to consider our next course of action. My son awaits his reinforcements!'Determined to aid his son, Durin, and the Dwarves of the Gabil'akkâ to reclaim Mount Gundabad, King Thorin Stonehelm hopes to enlist the aid of an old friend.King Thorin can be found at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.

King Thorin has asked you to accompany him to Vosh Torú for a meeting with one of the Kharum-ubnâr. You should talk to King Thorin.Talk to King ThorinKing Thorin can be found at Vosh Torú in the Lonely Mountain.

After talking with King Thorin, Hermáth Stormhammer has agreed to aid Prince Durin and the Gabil'akkâ. You should talk to King Thorin.Talk to King Thorin at Vosh TorúHersegg can be found at Vosh Torú in the Lonely Mountain.

As King Thorin left Vosh Torú, Hersegg called you over. You should talk to Hersegg.Talk to Hersegg at Vosh TorúHermáth Stormhammer can be found at Vosh Torú in the Lonely Mountain.

In an effort to aid King Thorin, Hermáth Stormhammer has agreed to travel to Elderslade and join Prince Durin and the Gabil'akkâ in the reclamation of Mount Gundabad. You should talk to Hermáth Stormhammer.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer at Vosh TorúVosh Torú can be found in the Lonely Mountain.

Hermáth Stormhammer has asked you to wait outside Vosh Torú as he equips himself. You should leave Vosh Torú and wait outside.Leave Vosh Torú and await Hermáth Stormhammer in the Dwelling-hallsYou should wait for Hermáth Stormhammer outside Vosh Torú.Wait for Hermáth Stormhammer outside Vosh TorúHermáth Stormhammer can be found outside Vosh Torú in the Lonely Mountain.

Hermáth Stormhammer has equipped himself, and it appears Hersegg has convinced him to let her join the Gabil'akkâ. You should talk to Hermáth Stormhammer.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer'It is time we dealt with the matter at hand, my friend.

'When I sent you to seek out Hersegg at Akrâz-zahar, I also gave to her an errand. Although I sent Agnar, Lági, and Reithvald to aid my son in his campaign, I must admit I hesitated to enlist another of my father's old friends in the Kharum-ubnâr. Not because I did not think him suited to the task, but for reasons I am ashamed to give voice. But I have shrunken from my duty for long enough!

'As I had hoped, Hersegg has sent word that I have been invited to visit her family's home at Vosh Torú. Whether this meeting goes well or ill, Durin awaits your return.

'What say you, ${PLAYER}? Will you accompany me?''I will show you the way to Vosh Torú, my friend.''You must understand, ${PLAYER}. Hermáth was my father's friend, not mine.

'After what happened at the end of the War, I feared he would want nothing to do with me. I know how dear Durin was to him, and my heart aches that his son fell in the defence of the Lonely Mountain.

'Yet, young Hersegg has her father's spirit. If Hermáth honours my request as he says, I think he will have a difficult time convincing Hersegg to stay behind!

'Ah, I must return to the Hall Under the Mountain, my friend. But, thank you for helping me to clear the air between us!''If my grandfather is going to join the Gabil'akkâ, then so am I!'

From the other room, you hear Hermáth chuckle to himself.

'I'm no younger than your old friend, Dáin, was when he went to war! Who knows what sort of title I might win for myself?

'I'm going to gather my things, ${PLAYER}. My grandfather can try to stop me if he wishes!''I couldn't keep her here even if I wanted to, ${PLAYER}....'

Hermáth smiles to himself. After a moment, he looks around his room, his gaze landing upon his old armour and hammer.

'Wait outside a moment, and I will meet you in the Dwelling-halls.'

'Would you please give this old dwarf a moment to equip himself? Go on!''There! Now that is more like it!'By completing this quest, you will automatically complete the Amdân Dammul, the Bloody Threshold, tiers 1-5 raid deeds.'Deeds! Get your Bloody Threshold Raid Deeds here!

'Talk to me one more time, and they're all yours!''Deeds, incoming!'After King Thráin and his vanguard set out to assault the gates of Khazad-dûm, a sudden revelation threatens to raise tensions between the Longbeards and the Zhélruka at Amdân.Thromur can be found at Amdân.

Thromur, esquire of Frerin, has asked you to accompany him to confront Shálun. You should talk to Thromur.Talk to ThromurThromur can be found at Durin's Stone.

You have aided in the defeat of Urro One-hand and routed Bolg and the other Orcs at Durin's Stone. You should talk to Thromur.Talk to Thromur at Durin's Stone'Shálun? I know him well indeed. This would not be the first time he has disrespected Frerin, but even I did not think he would stoop so low!

'Let us see what excuse he makes for this insult... this theft!

'Will you come with me, Hermáth? If you would bring your hammer, I would feel more at ease... though I should hope you need not use it!''Let us confront this thief!''I misjudged him... Shálun, I mean. My master is lost, but the King and Thorin live because of his sacrifice.'

Thromur lets out a sorrowful sigh.

'The Iron-folk have come at last... I only hope it is not too late!'The Red-maid is lost -- or so it seems -- to the corruption wrought upon her land at the hands of corrupt Men. These Men stand before her, ready to sacrifice themselves for the Red-maid, and they deserve this fate. However, the nobility of your blood will be a greater reward for her, and when these Men fall before her willingly, she will see that the noble are attempting to destroy her and ignoble keeping her this way.The Créoth will sacrifice themselves to the Red-maid to restore her strength and connexion to the corruption within the swamp. These Men deserve nothing better than death, and your presence in standing against the Red-maid may alert some part of her to the folly of her ways. You should let these Men die and strengthen the Red-maid, then defeat her. Perhaps a piece of her will understand that the corruption has grown too great, and she must return to the natural world of which she was once a part.Aid in the cleansing of the Red-maid'My sisters are far from me.... I cannot hear their songs or feel their gentle breath among the trees. Barren are those trees, nearly lifeless, choked out by roots bloated with something that has a bitter taste of iron. Where are you my sisters, and what is before me?

'I cannot see beyond the red of this land, but I sense something sweet among the bitter ferns and swollen roots. Are you real? Am I still imprisoned in the land?

'There is life in you -- I feel this. There must be life elsewhere in these lands. Perhaps I can find this life anew and nurture it back to health.'The Red-maid is lost -- or so it seems -- to the corruption wrought upon her land at the hands of corrupt Men. These Men stand before her, ready to sacrifice themselves for the Red-maid, and they deserve this fate. However, the nobility of your blood will be a greater reward for her, and when these Men fall before her willingly, she will see that the noble are attempting to destroy her and ignoble keeping her this way.You have returned to Garth Agarwen to defeat Naruhel, the Red-maid.<rgb=#66b3ff>Daily Reward -- Tier 2</rgb>The Red-maid is lost -- or so it seems -- to the corruption wrought upon her land at the hands of corrupt Men. These Men stand before her, ready to sacrifice themselves for the Red-maid, and they deserve this fate. However, the nobility of your blood will be a greater reward for her, and when these Men fall before her willingly, she will see that the noble are attempting to destroy her and ignoble keeping her this way.You have returned to Garth Agarwen to defeat Naruhel, the Red-maid.<rgb=#66b3ff>Daily Reward -- Tier 3</rgb>A bounty notice asks you to seek out a goblin that has been seen lurking around the cavern of Scraper's Clough in the Floodfells.

