You should bring the necklace to Elgun.Bring the necklace to Elgun'Have you seen Elskur? He was asking after you. Did he find you? No? Perhaps you could go and see what he's about? He was due back some time ago.''Ah, Hermáth. There you are. Listen, friend. I have a favour to ask of you. I have buried a necklace here for my wife. If... if something were to happen to me, would you dig it up and bring it to her? Tell her that I am sorry for the words I said when we parted?'

'Of course,' you say.

'Thank you, my friend. I should have given her the necklace before I left. She would have loved this view. Perhaps I shall return here with her one day, and this promise I ask of you will have been for naught.

'Tell Sárgo I shall return shortly, will you? I think I will take in this view for a bit longer.''Elskur, Elskur... let me think. Oh! Oh yes, I do remember now. Grandfather often spoke of him. He was one of the Burned, sadly.'

You ask her about the necklace.

'Why no, grandfather never mentioned going back for that. I imagine that it could still be there!''How lovely this is! His wife was a woman of fine tastes and she would have loved this dearly. She joined her husband years ago, however. Their daughter Elgun, however, lives here in Erebor.''Well met. I am indeed Elgun.'

You hand her the necklace and tell her its story. She holds it gently, a look of sad wistfulness on her face.

'Thank you for bringing this back to our family. My mother never doubted he loved her. I never got to meet my father, but now I have a piece of him, and of their love.'A sealed letter, delivered in haste, contains a request to travel north.Grimbeorn can be found at the Beorninghús in the Vales of Anduin.

You should talk to Grimbeorn.Talk to Grimbeorn at the BeorninghúsWhile we both fought for the Rohirrim during the Battle of Pelennor, we have not met before. My name is Léothred. I am not much of a warrior and I am not completely sure how I survived the battle without injury. Was it luck? Nay, it was heroes such as yourself. Those with the strength of ten steeds!

I am much more of a scholar than a hero. After the war I stayed at Minas Tirith, where I learned to write. I have made it my life's work to record the history of my people. The earliest account of the Rohirrim places our origins in the Vales of Anduin and the Wells of Langflood.

I have been told that you recently visited the Vales and are in good graces with their protector, Grimbeorn. I humbly request to accompany you on this journey so that I may further catalogue and preserve the history of my people. I shall meet you at Grimbeorn's lodgings.

- Léothred'${PLAYER}, what brings you to my home? You have not brought another parade of Elves with you, I hope?! I do not have time for hospitality! Forgive me, ${RACE}. You are always welcome here.'

You tell Grimbeorn that you have come to meet Léothred, a scholar who seeks permission to study the history of his people in these lands.

'Hmm, I have not seen this Léothred yet. Since you are here, I should inform you that all is not well in the Vales. The Eagles have recently warned me that trouble is brewing north of here, in the Langflood.'The War of Three Peaks rages at Azgh-buzru where clashes between the forces of Gorgar the Ruthless and the Gabil'akkâ have grown ever more bloody and desperate.Náin the Slakeless can be found at the hidden refuge of Drenghól in the northern reaches of Elderslade during the War of Three Peaks.

Náin the Slakeless has led a company north toward Bagûd-mekhem in attempt to secure a forward encampment outside Mount Gundabad. You should muster at Drenghól and report to Náin the Slakeless.Talk to Náin the Slakeless at Drenghól'The road to Caivád Sâr has been claimed for the Gabil'akkâ, ${PLAYER}, but a great battle awaits us before Gundabad!

'Náin the Slakeless, Lord of the Iron Hills, has set out with a great company and sent word that all able warriors are to muster to the battle at Azgh-buzru. It is Lord Náin's mission to overcome Gorgar's garrison at Bagûd-mekhem and win a final foothold for the Gabil'akkâ outside the gates of Mount Gundabad.

'The war-fell remains contested between the Gabil'akkâ and Gorgar's forces of Gundabad, so all who wish to answer the call are to report to Lord Náin at Drenghól. Be certain that you are not followed!

'That is all I have been told! Will you answer the call to arms?''The road to Caivád Sâr has been claimed for the Gabil'akkâ, ${PLAYER}, but a great battle awaits us before Gundabad!

'Náin the Slakeless, Lord of the Iron Hills, has set out with a great company and sent word that all able warriors are to muster to the battle at Azgh-buzru. It is Lord Náin's mission to overcome Gorgar's garrison at Bagûd-mekhem and win a final foothold for the Gabil'akkâ outside the gates of Mount Gundabad.

'The war-fell remains contested between the Gabil'akkâ and Gorgar's forces, so all who wish to answer the call are to report to Lord Náin at Drenghól. Be certain that you are not followed!

'That is all I have been told! Will you answer the call to arms?''I had hoped you would come, ${PLAYER}.

'The battle at Azgh-buzru has grown bloodier with each passing hour, and there have been enormous casualties on both sides. For my part, I shall make certain that Gorgar suffers the greater loss! If we are to emerge victorious, we shall have need of more warriors like you.

'There is much to discuss if you are to aid us!'The burrows of Klórgren are the cause of unstable earth in the eastern edges of Elderslade.The burrows of Klórgren can be found on the eastern edges of Elderslade.

You should burn the stone-claw corpses within Klórgren.Burn the stone-claw corpses within KlórgrenThe burrows of Klórgren can be found on the eastern edges of Elderslade.

You should burn the stone-claw corpses within Klórgren.Burn the stone-claw corpses within Klórgren (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Burn many stone-claw corpses within Klórgren.CompletedKlórgren, the massive network of stone-claw tunnels and burrows, cracks the earth as the passages inside weave and twist through the hills. Within Klórgren, the stone-claws are frenzied and disease-ridden. Dead stone-claws line the tunnels ahead. If the corpses are not removed from Klórgren, it is possible that the rock slides will increase and the stone-claws will become even more frenzied as the disease spreads.You have successfully burned many of the diseased stone-claw corpses found in Klórgren. The dwarves coming to Elderslade will thank you for your efforts. This disease that the stone-claws struggle with is not natural, however. You wonder what else in Elderslade has been affected and defiled by the forces of Gundabad, and to what purpose.There is always work to be done at the Beorninghús.Lost chickens can be found in the Beorninglands near the Beorninghús.

You should catch the lost chickens for Grimbeorn.Catch lost chickens (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should bring the lost chickens back to the Beorninghús.Return to the BeorninghúsYou catch one of the chickens that Grimbeorn is worried about. You had better find the other three!Durin is pleased you have returned to Skarháld, for he has had some difficulty forging a workable alliance between the dwarves who have come to the Grey Mountains and could use your help.Durin asked you to climb the stairs at Skarháld in search of Longbeards, Zhélruka, and Stout-axes who might share their opinions on what he should do for them.Talk to Kóvo of the Stout-axes in SkarháldTalk to Bórnek of the Zhélruka in SkarháldTalk to Glóin of the Longbeards in SkarháldDurin is on the lowest level of Skarháld, near the stable-master, waiting for your report from the three dwarf-clans who inhabit the mountain fortress.Talk to Durin by the stable-master in SkarháldDurin's father, King Thorin Stonehelm, is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin Stonehelm in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor<rgb=#00FF00>It is recommended, but not required, that you complete 'The Black Book of Mordor' before beginning 'The Legacy of Durin and the Trials of the Dwarves.'</rgb>

'You have returned, my friend! I did not know when your adventures to the south might release you, but I am pleased it was sooner and not later! It is good to see you!'

Durin claps you heartily on the back. Then he lowers his voice and warily eyes the courtyard.

'Truthfully, things have not been going as well as I might have hoped. I have been trying to ease tensions among the dwarves that have come to Skarháld, but it has not been easy. The Longbeards, Zhélruka, and Stout-axes here all take exception with each other, even on trifling matters of little importance. All three of these dwarf-companies suffered grievous losses at the teeth and claws of the Frost-horde, under Karazgar's direction, but we have seen no sign of the worms since you departed. The Weeping Warrior himself has not reappeared in that time... do you know anything about that, ${PLAYER}?

'But now it falls to me to get these dwarf-clans working together, and that is no easy thing. Perhaps you can ask members of each family what they think I should do on their behalf? Stout-axes, Zhélruka, and Longbeards up the stairs here at Skarháld are sure to give you their opinions on this subject, and it may help me decide how best to proceed.''Things have not been going as well here as I might have hoped. I have been trying to ease tensions among the dwarves that have come to Skarháld, but it has not been easy. The Longbeards, Zhélruka, and Stout-axes here all take exception with each other, mostly for trifling matters. All three of these dwarf-companies suffered grievous losses at the teeth and claws of the Frost-horde, under Karazgar's direction, but we have seen no sign of the worms since you departed. The Weeping Warrior himself has not reappeared in that time... do you know anything about that, ${PLAYER}?

'But now it falls to me to get these dwarf-clans working together, and that is no easy thing. Perhaps you can ask members of each family what they think I should do on their behalf? Stout-axes, Zhélruka, and Longbeards up the stairs here at Skarháld are sure to give you their opinions on this subject, and it may help me decide how best to proceed.''Durin wants to know what is to be done? Ha! This Stout-axe has a recommendation or two, but I do not think he is prepared to hear them!

'I am a Stout-axe, ${PLAYER}. Of all the dwarves in this Middle-earth, we have endured the greatest trials; I dare you to say otherwise. Many were the torments Sauron devised for our great king when Drása did not submit, and his people were enslaved. But we kept our honour, though we toiled without end in Mordor, and now that the Dark Lord is gone the Stout-axes have emerged to take what we will, and what we deserve. So I say again to this dwarf-princeling: ha! What authority has he to command any Stout-axe? He fought against the Frost-horde? So did all of us at Skarháld. I count his deeds there no better than any of ours.

'He will need a dozen such victories before he has matched even the least of my own.''Durin sent you to ask what should be done? He is no Zhélruka, that much is clear. If Bótuz Frostblood were still alive, he would not ask such a thing. He would simply tell us, and in no uncertain terms; woe be to the dwarf who did not comply with his command!

'The Longbeard prince is still as green as Spring in the low-lands. He would do well to keep to himself, and leave the Zhélruka alone. We are not his to command, though we must inhabit Skarháld together for now. That will be for a short time only. Durin will soon tire of adventure and return to his father's halls. Beneath the Lonely Mountain he will live well, while we Zhélruka remain here, sleeping on the cold stone and dreaming of Gundabad, the home that has been kept from us.

'He knows little of the Zhélruka, and cares for us less. I find it distasteful for him to pretend otherwise.''It seems we share the same affliction, my questing friend! Most of my companions have gone home, satisfied with their adventures, but you and I belong here in the mountains, courting danger and unafraid! I know there is a place of honour set for me beneath the Lonely Mountain, but no matter how great the feast I would still feel the emptiness in my belly. Only adventure can sate my appetite now. My son Gimli will discover this hunger runs in his blood, if he has not already! But from your recent tales I know he has!'

You tell Glóin that Durin is seeking advice from the dwarves at Skarháld.

'I would tell him to treat carefully with the Zhélruka and Stout-axes who have come here, for they are not his people and care little for Longbeards. Each of these kindreds defended Skarháld when the Frost-horde attacked, and each suffered losses. But there are fewer Longbeards here than Zhélruka, and although the Stout-axes were few in number to begin with, it seems to me that more arrive everyday. Durin must be aware of this. Let him keep the Red Axe near at hand.''I have said my say. If Durin does not like the truth I speak, he is welcome to tell me himself.''Durin does not belong here. He is untested and unproven, and should go back to the Lonely Mountain with the rest of the Longbeards.''Durin should keep the Red Axe close. There are too few Longbeards at Skarháld for me to sleep comfortably, and he should not either.''What have you heard, my friend? Are your tidings as grim as I fear they will be?'

Durin listens to your report of growing unrest among the Stout-axes and Zhélruka, and of Glóin's concerns, and his face falls. He hefts the Red Axe thoughtfully.

'I came here too hastily, ${PLAYER}. Each of our peoples suffered losses when the Frost-horde attacked, but there were never that many loyal Longbeards at Skarháld to begin with. I only assumed command because there was no one else! Without a strong leader to unify each of the dwarf-clans here, I feared the Grey Mountains would prove a grave-cairn for all of us. They might still, but now it is not drakes of the Frost-horde I fear. I fear the disloyalty of the Zhélruka and the Stout-axes. Is there not betrayal in the history of both clans?

'I need more loyal warriors to reinforce me! I ask you to return to my father at the Lonely Mountain and request that he send as many Longbeard warriors as he can spare. Too many were lost when the Frost-horde attacked, and I would not have us outnumbered by... by foes.''I need more loyal warriors to reinforce me! I ask you to return to my father at the Lonely Mountain and request that he send as many Longbeard warriors as he can spare. Too many were lost when the Frost-horde attacked, and I would not have us outnumbered by... by foes.''Welcome back, my adventuring friend! What news of my son? What news do you bring of Durin? Has he brought to heel the Zhélruka that travelled without leave across my lands?'King Thorin Stonehelm is displeased to hear of the situation in the Grey Mountains and worries for his son Durin.King Thorin Stonehelm has tasked you with going to Nabgûn-mazaru, the home of Stáli the Hewer, south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls of Erebor, and asking him about 'the Greymauls.'Find Nabgûn-mazaru, the home of Stáli the Hewer, south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls of EreborStáli the Hewer, a Longbeard artist and sculptor of some renown, lives at Nabgûn-mazaru, south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Stáli the Hewer in Nabgûn-mazaruStáli the Hewer has been restoring a mosaic depicting the death of Mótsog and the end of the Greymaul Rebellion, and he has invited you to admire it. The mosaic is currently in his workshop of Nabgûn-mazaru, but it will be returned to the hall where it came from once his work is finished.Examine the mosaic depicting the death of MótsogStáli the Hewer, a Longbeard artist and sculptor of some renown, lives south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Stáli the Hewer in Nabgûn-mazaru'I do not like this, ${PLAYER}. I do not like this at all! Not every dwarf-clan is good-hearted, and treacherous, wicked dwarves have troubled the Longbeards throughout history. Our cousins in the Blue Mountains could speak at length about the exploits of the villainous Dourhands, and theirs is not the only name of ill repute. Durin will have his reinforcements! I will command the assemblage of a Longbeard army the like of which has not been seen in seventy years!

'Do you think this unnecessary? I assure you it is not. I will not allow my son to be outnumbered by Zhélruka or Stout-axes! The Zhélruka have long quarreled with my people, and the Stout-axes could not have remained thralls to Sauron for so long without it affecting them deeply.

'You remain unconvinced? Go then to Nabgûn-mazaru, the home of Stáli the Hewer, south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls, and ask him about the Greymauls. Then you will see the importance of caution, even among dwarves. Especially among dwarves.''I will not allow my son to be outnumbered by his foes! Go to Nabgûn-mazaru, the home of Stáli the Hewer, south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls, and ask him about the Greymauls. Then you will see the importance of caution, even among dwarves.''I will not allow my son to be outnumbered by his foes! Go to the home of Stáli the Hewer, south-west of the entrance to the Dwelling-halls, and ask him about the Greymauls. Then you will see the importance of caution, even among dwarves.''Welcome to Nabgûn-mazaru, stranger. Are you a patron of the arts? I must confess to you that my hours are quite full, with a number of half-completed works already. If you desire a painting or statue, perhaps to decorate your home, it will be some time before I am able to get to it. With that understanding set forth, what would you like?'

You tell Stáli that King Thorin did not send you to him with a task, but for information on the Greymaul Dwarves.

'The Greymauls? Why should they occupy his thoughts? Better to forget they ever existed, lest you doubt the decency of your closest friends and your family. But I will not disobey the King Under the Mountain, and will speak of them to you.

'They were ancient dwarf traitors in the First Age who rebelled against Durin the Deathless, Father of the Longbeards. Their leader was named Mótsog, and he led the Greymauls in a bloody uprising against Durin. For a time the Greymauls ruled Durin's holdings, but it did not last. Durin returned and put Mótsog to death, and the rebellion was quelled.

'I have been restoring a mosaic of this very event, if you would like to see it. It is over there, against the wall. It was damaged during the residency of Smaug, and once I have finished repairing the stones I will return it to the hall where once it stood. It will once again serve as a reminder that treachery is always rewarded with justice.''The Greymauls were ancient dwarf traitors in the First Age who rebelled against Durin the Deathless, Father of the Dwarves. Their leader was named Mótsog, and he led the Greymauls in a bloody uprising against Durin. For a time they ruled Durin's holdings, but it did not last. Durin returned and put Mótsog to death, and the rebellion was quelled.

'I have been restoring a mosaic of this very event, if you would like to see it. It is over there, against the wall. It was damaged during the residency of Smaug, and once I have finished repairing the stones I will return it to the hall where once it stood. It will once again serve as a reminder that treachery is always rewarded with justice.''That mosaic stood in the Hall Under the Mountain with many others for hundreds of years, but the coming of Smaug was not kind to any of them. Many of the stones were lost, and it has been a time-consuming process to recreate the mosaic as it was.

'But when Stáli the Hewer commits to such a project, he sees it through to the end!'King Thorin Stonehelm sent you to consult with Stáli the Hewer, a Longbeard artist of some renown who might tell you of the treacherous Greymaul dwarves and their rebellion against Durin the Deathless in the First Age.Stáli the Hewer has invited you to look at the statue he carved of Durin the Deathless, located in his home and workshop of Nabgûn-mazaru.Examine the statue of Durin the Deathless carved by Stáli the HewerStáli the Hewer is in Nabgûn-mazaru, his workshop and home in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Talk to Stáli the Hewer in Nabgûn-mazaruKing Thorin is in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor'Long, long ago, before the mountains were shaped as they are now, Durin awoke in the caverns beneath a mighty peak that joined two great mountain-ranges of the north. He looked to the peak and gave it the name we have revered ever since: Gundabad, the Mountain-home.

'The fathers of other dwarf-clans began to awake elsewhere in Middle-earth after that, in pairs, but he had awoken first of all the dwarves, and alone, and we revere him as the father of the Longbeards. We are Durin's Folk, and our claim to these lands springs from he who first awoke beneath Gundabad. In time, other dwarves awoke to accompany Durin, and to serve him, but his closest friend was a mighty warrior who helped delve the great halls beneath Gundabad. Legend tells us this friend was also the first dwarf to brew mead, and later ale, but do not think so well of him! He it was who would take the name Mótsog, and lead the Greymauls in rebellion. But that was still some years away.

'The servants of Morgoth in those days were a constant threat, and together Durin and Mótsog fought bravely to defend the Mountain-home from them, but Durin grew restless. He desired a place where the Longbeards might know peace, and so he set forth in search of such a haven. He named his dear friend Mótsog the Lord of Gundabad, a decision he would come to regret, and departed the sacred mountain.

'Do you see the statue there? It was one of my first pieces, and dare I say one of my finest. I hoped to capture some small portion of Durin's majesty as he set forth on his journey to what would become the site of treasured Khazad-dûm.''Go on, feel free to admire the statue of Durin. I am quite proud of it! See his noble visage? I like to imagine he has just looked into the lake of Kheled-zâram, and has realized he is near to finding a place of great wealth and safety for his people.''Ah, thank you very much, my friend! I can see you are an astute patron of the arts and have a fine eye for detail. I agree that I have done an excellent job of capturing Durin's heroism and majesty.'

Stáli beams at you. You have clearly won him over.

'Durin the Deathless he was called, for he possessed a great span of years and it seemed he would not die. It was not so, for death comes to all, even to the very greatest of dwarves. And yet: we Longbeards believe that Durin comes back whenever the dwarves have need of him, reborn into a new form. It is true! He has come back to us five times during our long history, and we therefore count each Durin in a single line, from Durin I who awoke beneath Gundabad all the way to Durin VI, who perished in Khazad-dûm to shadow and flame. Some folk believe that the son of King Thorin Stonehelm may prove to be Durin the Deathless reborn, a seventh Durin to lead us into the future.

'I hope it is true, ${PLAYER}. I have been planning a statue of him, and it would be a shame for that work to come to naught. But I have spoken too long already, and should get back to my endeavours. Return to King Thorin with my good wishes.''Can King Thorin's son really be Durin the Deathless reborn? I hope so! I have been planning a statue of him, and it would be a shame for that work to come to naught. But I have spoken too long already, and should get back to my endeavours. Return to King Thorin with my good wishes.''Stáli spoke to you of Mótsog and the Greymaul Rebellion? Good. Now you see how even good-seeming friends can turn traitor, and why it is so important that my son be surrounded by Longbeards he can trust.

'I have commanded a great force of loyal warriors to go with you to join my son in the Grey Mountains. Durin will have his reinforcements!'King Thorin Stonehelm has ordered a great force of Longbeard warriors to go with you to reinforce Durin in the Grey Mountains, but first he wants you to test the mettle of some of the eldest.King Thorin Stonehelm asked you to test the combat readiness of three survivors of the Sixth War of Dwarves and Orcs, which occurred more than two hundred and twenty years ago. These three heroes can be found in the passages leading to the Dwelling-halls and the New-lodes, in Erebor.Talk to Agnar Brokentooth and measure his combat prowessTalk to Lági Helmbiter and measure his combat prowessTalk to Reithvald Red-ember and measure his combat prowessKing Thorin Stonehelm is at his throne in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor.Talk to King Thorin in the Hall Under the Mountain, in Erebor'You have proven yourself a ${CLASS} of great courage, ${PLAYER}, and I judge that you have an eye for strength and capability in battle. Will you measure your fighting prowess against some of the dwarves I have chosen to join this Longbeard army? I do not question their desire for glory; indeed, these dwarves fought bravely and well in time of war, and achieved much honour thereby.

'But their greatest deeds are many years distant now. These three heroes fought in the Sixth War of Dwarves and Orcs, but that was more than two hundred and twenty years ago, when Durin's Folk last set foot within Gundabad. The Battle of Azanulbizar ended that war, and our losses were too great to pursue the Orcs into Khazad-dûm. I do not doubt the bravery of these three! But I want to know they still possess sufficient weapon-skill to aid my son.

'Seek out Agnar Brokentooth, Lági Helmbiter, and Reithvald Red-ember in the passages to the Dwelling-halls and to the New-lodes, and challenge them to battle. If they still desire to join my son in the Grey Mountains after testing their skills against you, then they shall.''Seek out Agnar Brokentooth, Lági Helmbiter, and Reithvald Red-ember in the passages to the Dwelling-halls and to the New-lodes, and challenge them to battle. If they still desire to join my son in the Grey Mountains after testing their skills against you, then they shall.''A chance to test my skills? I welcome it, and promise I will not go easy on you!''You had best prepare for a real fight, stranger! I have not lived so long in idleness! Indeed, I test my combat skill every day without exception!''You shall have a fight, then! I may be old, but I remain fierce and eager for battle!''Those three remain eager for battle? I did not really expect otherwise, but I thought it best to make certain. Thank you for indulging me in this, ${PLAYER}. May their greatest heroics remain ahead of them, in service to my son Durin!'King Thorin Stonehelm has commanded a large force of Longbeards to travel with you to reinforce his son Durin in the Grey Mountains.King Thorin has asked you to return to Skarháld with the great force of Longbeards he commanded to reinforce his son Durin.Return to Skarháld with the Longbeard forceMírkuz eyes the Longbeard force with great suspicion.Talk to Mírkuz at SkarháldGlóin stands near the stable-master at Skarháld, pleased to see the Longbeard reinforcements from the Lonely Mountain.Talk to Glóin by the stable-master in SkarháldGlóin stands near the stable-master at Skarháld. He is worried that Durin left the fortress some time ago and has not returned.Talk to Glóin and go with him to search for DurinDurin has returned to Skarháld with renewed purpose, his eyes alight with hopeful flame.Talk to Durin by the stable-master in Skarháld<rgb=#FF0000>You will automatically travel to Skarháld with a large force of Longbeards when you accept this quest.</rgb>

'Go now to the mountain-fortress where waits my son! Bring him this mighty force, and tell him his father knows he will bring glory for the Longbeards for so long as there is glory there to be had!''Bring Durin this mighty force, and tell him his father knows he will bring glory for the Longbeards for so long as there is glory there to be had!

'Skarháld awaits your return, ${PLAYER}!''Why have you brought all these Longbeards here, ${PLAYER}? I am offended to see so many! The Zhélruka were offered a home in these mountains, but now it seems Durin's Folk think to once again stand in the way of that offer.

'What other meaning can there be for this great force? The son of Thorin Stonehelm thinks to cheat my people of the lands that were promised!''If the son of Thorin Stonehelm thinks to cheat the Zhélruka of the lands that were promised, he will be sorry. Surely he must know this!''Now this is a fighting force! I do not suppose my son Gimli joined the company? No, I do not see him. He is far away, having further adventures of his own. A pity.

'But look at these warriors! Why, even Agnar Brokentooth has come! And there is Lági Helmbiter! These are truly great heroes of my people, ${PLAYER}. I fought alongside them at Azanulbizar, you know; I was among the youngest to stand at that battle. I am not so young now, am I?

'What a wonder it is that they have come to aid the prince here in the Grey Mountains! Where is Durin? He will be pleased to see so many fighters. I saw him speaking with Heilar some time ago, and the two of them went away to the north-east. Have they not returned?''I do not like that Durin has not yet returned. He did not say where he and Heilar were going, but they were heading north-east when last I saw them, and there is not much in that direction, unless....'

A dark shadow passes across Glóin's face.

'That is the way to the Anvil of Winterstith, ${PLAYER}. You do not think they would be going there, do you? We must hurry!'

Glóin looks around grimly.

'And bring the army, ${PLAYER}. We cannot be too careful.''Finally, my friend! At last I know my purpose, and with this army of Longbeards at my back, I cannot hope but to achieve it! Gundabad awaits, ${PLAYER}!''Bring Durin this mighty force, and tell him his father knows he will bring glory for the Longbeards for so long as there is glory there to be had!

'Skarháld awaits your return, ${PLAYER}!'Reinvigourated by the arrival of the Longbeard reinforcements from the Lonely Mountain, Durin wishes to prepare for an assault upon Gundabad, the Mountain-home of Durin's Folk.The western armoury is up the stairs inside the Skarháld keep.Collect weapons from the western armoury up the stairs inside the Skarháld keepThe first armoury was empty of weapons. Perhaps some implements of war remain in the other armoury on the eastern side of the Skarháld keep.Collect weapons from the eastern armoury inside the Skarháld keepTomókh has called for your attention inside the eastern armoury inside the Skarháld keep.Talk to Tomókh in the eastern armoury inside the Skarháld keepDurin is waiting by the Skarháld stable-master for you to return with weapons from the armouries.Talk to Durin by the stable-master in SkarháldOne of the Zhélruka hunting parties went to the east of Skarháld.One of the Zhélruka hunting parties went east of Skarháld.Talk to the Zhélruka drake-hunter east of SkarháldThe second Zhélruka hunting-party continued their hunt to the north-east of the first.Find the second Zhélruka hunting-party somewhere to the north-eastThe second Zhélruka hunting-party ran afoul of the drakes they sought, and now you should collect the weapons they took from Skarháld.Collect the abandoned weapons from the slain hunting-party (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Durin is waiting by the stable-master in Skarháld for you to return with the weapons the Zhélruka "borrowed."'My father's reinforcements from the Lonely Mountain have brought their own weapons, but those will not be enough if we are to truly assault Gundabad. Can I ask you to climb the stairs and find the armoury in the Skarháld keep? Bring back some of the swords and axes that are stored there, and we can better supply this fighting force.''My father's reinforcements from the Lonely Mountain have brought their own weapons, but those will not be enough if we are to truly assault Gundabad. Can I ask you to climb the stairs and find the armoury in the Skarháld keep? Bring back some of the swords and axes that are stored there, and we can better supply this fighting force.''There were no weapons in the armoury? Did you try the other one?''Yes? What do you want?''You are looking for weapons? You will find none here. Several parties of my people armed themselves with swords and axes from the armouries here and set out into the mountains in search of the remaining drakes of the Frost-horde. It is a proud tradition of the Zhélruka to hunt such creatures... but I might have advised against it if I had known an army of Longbeards was soon to arrive!

'Tell the Longbeard prince that if he wants those weapons he will have to wait for my Zhélruka brothers to return victorious from their hunt.''Tell the Longbeard prince that if he wants those weapons he will have to wait for my Zhélruka brothers to return victorious from their hunt.''Some of the Zhélruka have gone into the mountains to hunt the remaining drakes of the Frost-horde? I would say to let them go, and good riddance, but we need those weapons!

'You had better go after them, ${PLAYER}, and save them from their own foolishness. I want them alive so they can hear my reprimands, but more importantly I want the weapons they stole. I have spoken with some of the reinforcements from the Lonely Mountain, and many of their weapons are of poor quality, damaged either from disuse or entirely too much use; there is no middle ground.

'Find the Zhélruka hunting-parties outside Skarháld and bring back those weapons!''Did you hear that horn blowing? It ended suddenly. I think the Zhélruka hunting parties may have run into some trouble after all, ${PLAYER}. You had better go and bring back their weapons, for it seems they may not need them any longer!

'The horn sounded to the east of Skarháld. I think that is where you will find them.''Sound the traditional drake-hunting horn and help us slay whatever answers the call, ${PLAYER}. It waits there for you.''Thank you for your help, friend. We will return to Skarháld, but there was another party of drake-hunters with us, and they have not yet come back. We heard their horn blowing some time ago, but it stopped abruptly.

'They continued on to the north-east, if you wish to search in that direction.''The second party continued on to the north-east, but I fear they are dead. You had better find out for certain.''Yes, drake-hunting is a dangerous endeavour. They knew the risks when they set out from Skarháld, the same as us.

'Where did you find them? To the north-east?''Thank you for these, ${PLAYER}. My Longbeards are going to need every weapon on which they can get their hands, and I do not appreciate these Zhélruka raiding the armouries without notice. That practice must end, or they will face my wrath and that of my Longbeards.

'But there is trouble brewing in Skarháld, and I need my most trusted ${CLASS} to help deal with it before the situation gets worse.'The arrival of the large Longbeard force has escalated the tensions between the dwarf-clans at Skarháld, and Durin has asked you to try and resolve the brewing conflict.The brawling dwarves can be found to the north-west and up two levels from the stable-master in Skarháld.Find Imák and calm him downFind Gílpi and calm him downTensions run high as Stout-axes, Zhélruka, and Longbeards quarrel at Skarháld.Help Heilar to his feet and find out what happenedTensions run high as Stout-axes, Zhélruka, and Longbeards quarrel at Skarháld. Durin tasked you with easing these tensions, if such a thing is possible.Talk to HeilarTalk to VenkoImák the Stout-axe still glowers at Heilar the Longbeard. Durin asked you to ease the tensions among these dwarves, if such a thing is possible.Talk to ImákIn order to help ease the tensions among the quarrelling dwarves, you have decided to fetch four clean mugs and fill them with ale from one of the kegs at Skarháld.Collect clean mugs at Skarháld (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A keg of ale stands south of the quarrelling dwarves in Skarháld. You have gathered four (mostly) clean mugs and should now fill them from the ale-keg.A keg of ale stands south of the quarrelling dwarves in Skarháld. You have gathered four (mostly) clean mugs and should now fill them from an ale-keg.The ale-keg ran dry after filling two of the mugs, and now you need to find another keg or risk making the dwarves' quarrel worse instead of better!You found another ale-keg in Skarháld and can now fill the last of the mugs you collected.The quarrelling dwarves are two levels up from the stable-master at Skarháld, in the north-west corner, waiting for you to return with the mugs of ale you promised.Bring the mugs of ale to the quarrelling dwarves in the north-west cornerDurin is waiting by the stable-master in Skarháld for you to return.Talk to Durin by the stable-master in Skarháld'While you were away, a brawl broke out on the upper level. I do not know the precise cause of it, but it seems there is some resentment on the part of the Zhélruka and the Stout-axes here at Skarháld. When I climbed up to investigate, the matter had been resolved, but....'

Even as Durin speaks, you hear shouting from up the stairs.

'There! You hear them? It appears nothing was settled after all! You go find these unruly dwarves and get to the bottom of this, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they will listen to you. If they do not, well... we will swing that axe when we get there!''It appears nothing was settled after all! You go find these unruly dwarves and get to the bottom of this, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they will listen to you. If they do not, well... we will swing that axe when we get there!''Find these unruly dwarves and get to the bottom of this, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they will listen to you. If they do not, well... we will swing that axe when we get there!''Find these unruly dwarves and get to the bottom of this, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they will listen to you. If they do not, well... we will swing that axe when we get there!''You want to know what happened? I said two words, little more than that, and turned to go. That treacherous slime knocked me down when my back was turned! He calls himself a Stout-axe, but I know what he really is! He spent so long in Mordor... he was a willing servant of Sauron, or I am an Elf!

'I called him a Greymaul, what of it? He shows his true nature with such a cowardly strike! I wager all the Stout-axes have some Greymaul blood in them, and it wouldn't surprise me if the Zhélruka did too! Yes, some of the Greymauls must have escaped Durin's judgement, and their descendants probably intermingled with the other dwarf families. It would not surprise me to learn that Greymaul blood might have led to the crimes and failings of even the Dourhands!''I saw it all. They were grumbling at each other for a good while, mostly about imagined slights, I expect, but before long they were each at a full bellow. Heilar called Imák "Greymaul filth" as he turned to go, and that is when Imák knocked him down.

'I do not know what he meant by that, but he is fortunate Imák used his fists and not his axe. I might have done worse.''Find these unruly dwarves and get to the bottom of this, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they will listen to you. If they do not, well... we will swing that axe when we get there!''I do not know anything about these Greymauls he mentioned, but his tone of voice was clear. He intended for it to be insulting, so I was insulted. This story of treacherous dwarves must be some fable of the Longbeards. We Stout-axes have our own tales, and no shortage of villains to which I might compare such as he, who speak without thinking!

'He would do well to remember the force of my punch, for if he says anything of the sort to me again he will face my fists once more, and this time I will hold nothing back!'

You ask Imák if there is anything that might be done to put this quarrel behind them, and he nods in the direction of Heilar and the interested onlookers.

'I would accept an apology over a mug of ale, if you were to fetch enough for the four of us. I find that usually helps.''You say you have it well in hand? I expected nothing else, my friend!

'If you cannot make these dwarves forget their quarrel, I fear no one can.''Mugs of ale? Aye, that should help them set aside their differences... while they are drinking, anyway.''You were able to smooth things over with the quarrelling dwarves? I expected no less, ${PLAYER}.

'But now there is something else. Isn't there always?'Durin's scouts have reported the presence of a raiding-party from Gundabad near the Wells of Langflood, and he thinks he may have found a solution to more than one of the problems that troubles him.Imák and Venko can be found two levels up from the stable-master in Skarháld, in the north-west corner.Return to Imák and Venko in the north-west corner, two levels up from the Skarháld stable-masterImák of the Stout-axes is in the north-west corner, two levels up from the stable-master in Skarháld.Talk to Imák of the Stout-axes in SkarháldVenko of the Zhélruka is in the north-west corner, two levels up from the stable-master in Skarháld.Talk to Venko of the Zhélruka in SkarháldDurin is waiting by the Skarháld stable-master to hear that Imák and Venko will go in search of the raiding-party in the Wells of Langflood.Talk to Durin by the stable-master in SkarháldGlóin has made it known he wishes to speak with you and Durin at the stable-master in Skarháld.Talk to Glóin by the stable-master in SkarháldA bag of foodstuffs can be found two levels above the Skarháld stable-master.
Climbing gear can be found three levels above the Skarháld stable-master
Flint and camping supplies can be found two levels above the Skarháld stable-master.Collect the bag of foodstuffs in Skarháld, two levels upCollect the bag of climbing gear in Skarháld, three levels upCollect the bag of camping gear in Skarháld, two levels upGlóin is by the stable-master in Skarháld.Durin is by the stable-master in Skarháld.Talk to Durin by the stable-master in SkarháldThe way to the Wells of Langflood from the Grey Mountains is west of Skarháld, accessible by a narrow pass in the southern cliffs east of the broken Rushgate.Find your travelling companions to the west of Skarháld, at the pass to the Wells of Langflood east and south of the broken RushgateGlóin is at the entrance to the pass that leads to the Wells of Langflood from the Grey Mountains, east and south of the broken Rushgate.Talk to Glóin by the path leading into Misthallow'You know I desire to take back Gundabad for my people, and it will not be long before I drive the Orcs from that sacred mountain. But I am no fool, ${PLAYER}! Too often the hasty stroke goes astray, or so they say, and I know any assault upon the Mountain-home must be carefully planned. To that end, I sent a number of scouts to the Wells of the Langflood, swift and true, and they have now returned.

'But they return with news not from the foot of Gundabad, but from the lands to the south! They bring word of a raiding-party set loose from Gundabad upon the inhabitants of Misthallow and the Floodfells. There are few enough of those, but if the Orcs move in those lands they will trouble any army that seeks to advance upon Gundabad. Someone must deal with this raiding-party.

'Do you know something, ${PLAYER}? I believe we might solve two problems with a single stroke! Why not send some of the troublesome Zhélruka or Stout-axes to confront this raiding-party? They will realize their true quarrel is with the Orcs, and during their absence some measure of peace may return to Skarháld. What were their names? Imák? Venko? Bring them the news of this task, and invite them to adventure on behalf of their people.''My scouts bring word of a raiding-party set loose from Gundabad upon the inhabitants of Misthallow and the Floodfells. There are few enough of those, but if the Orcs move in those lands they will trouble any army that seeks to advance upon Gundabad. Someone must deal with this raiding-party... and I believe we might solve two problems with a single stroke! Why not send some of the troublesome Zhélruka or Stout-axes to confront this raiding-party? They will realize their true quarrel is with the Orcs, and during their absence some measure of peace may return to Skarháld. What were their names? Imák? Venko? Bring them this news of this task, and invite them to adventure on behalf of their people.''My scouts bring word of a raiding-party set loose from Gundabad upon the inhabitants of Misthallow and the Floodfells. There are few enough of those, but if the Orcs move in those lands they will trouble any army that seeks to advance upon Gundabad. Someone must deal with this raiding-party... and I believe we might solve two problems with a single stroke! Why not send some of the troublesome Zhélruka or Stout-axes to confront this raiding-party? They will realize their true quarrel is with the Orcs, and during their absence some measure of peace may return to Skarháld. What were their names? Imák? Venko? Bring them this news of this task, and invite them to adventure on behalf of their people.''Durin expects the two of us to go in search of a raiding-party from Gundabad? Is that what you are saying, ${PLAYER}? I cannot fail to notice that he has charged a Stout-axe and a Zhélruka with this mission, rather than any of the large number of his own folk that have come to Skarháld. I find that most curious, Venko, do you not?

'I believe the Longbeard prince is seeking to get rid of us!''Why has the Longbeard prince not charged any of his people with this mission, Venko? That is what I want to know.''Durin wants us to go face a raiding-party from Gundabad? I see the workings of his mind, and I tell you they are not as subtle as he thinks them to be! If Imák and I prove victorious against these Orcs, then the few folk who live along the Langflood will be made safer, and if we do not, well, Durin has only lost a Stout-axe and a Zhélruka in the effort. He will not mourn us overmuch, Imák, will he?

'In the interest of setting aside our differences, I will do as Durin requests, if Imák will do the same. I will venture to the Wells of the Langflood in pursuit of this raiding party... with two conditions only.

'The first is this: you must come with us, ${PLAYER}. You are a close friend of the Longbeard prince, and he would not send you into danger without reason. If you were to perish, I believe he would be aggrieved. So I ask that you come with us in search of this raiding-party. My second condition is that Durin send a Longbeard as well. Why should Stout-axes and Zhélruka go into danger when Durin's Folk remain behind in safety?

'Those are my conditions. Bring them to Durin with haste, ${PLAYER}, and see what this Longbeard prince has to say.''In the interest of setting aside our differences, I will do as Durin requests, if Imák desires to do the same. I will venture to the Wells of the Langflood in pursuit of this raiding party... but only if you come with us as well, ${PLAYER}. Let Durin's trusted friend be a part of this errand too, and perhaps he will be more interested in its successful outcome, and our survival.''They will only undertake this errand if you go with them? This is outrageous, ${PLAYER}! No one else has assumed the mantle of leadership here at Skarháld, and I do so only unwillingly, but if I make such a command as this it should be followed, and without question! These dwarves will cause only trouble for me and mine, and if I want them well away from Skarháld then away from it they shall be, without negotiation!

'But what is it, Glóin? You have something to say?''What is it, Glóin? You have my respect and for that I give you my ear.''Yes, Durin, I have something to say. I have seen a thing or two during my long life, and it has taught me much. I was among the youngest of the dwarves to fight at Azanulbizar... did you know that? Every dwarf-clan sent representatives to fight in that war, Durin, not just the Longbeards. Even so youthful as I was, I knew that each family fought for more than just their own glory; they fought for the survival of all dwarves.

'You should not send Zhélruka only, or Stout-axes only, in pursuit of this Orc raiding-party. Neither should you keep your Longbeards in reserve, for that speaks poorly both of you and of them! A Longbeard should go, and I volunteer myself. Let ${PLAYER} adventure there too, not just because Imák and Venko request it but because ${PLAYER} is a loyal friend and true, and will see this raiding-party destroyed. These are words of wisdom, Durin, and with age and experience they will come naturally to you. For now let them come from your friends and allies, for they want the same as you and will not lead you astray.

'${PLAYER}, will you gather the supplies we will need for an excursion into the Wells of Langflood? You will find satchels of foodstuffs, climbing gear, and assorted other supplies two or three levels up in Skarháld. While you do that I will tell Imák and Venko that we will be travelling companions, for the good of Skarháld and all the dwarves.''Glóin is right. The two of you have leave to go with them in search of the Gundabad raiding-party. But you will need to collect some supplies before you depart.''You found the supplies for our expedition? Very good. At a young age I learned the importance of preparing for a journey, and my appreciation of such preparation has only increased with every passing year. You do not want to lack some crucial tool in the middle of the wilderness, I can tell you that! But you know that as well as I, my friend.

'I spoke to Imák and Venko while you were gathering up the supplies, and they said they will meet us at the pass that leads to the Wells of Langflood, far to the west of Skarháld, east and south of the broken Rushgate. They were not very happy about it; they believed their conditions would change Durin's mind and keep anyone from seeking out this raiding-party, but it was not so. Speak to Durin and then we will join our new companions.''Speak first to Durin, and then we will depart for the Wells of the Langflood.''So you are ready to depart for the Wells of the Langflood? I know very little of that region, but I understand Glóin journeyed there during the Sixth War of Dwarves and Orcs, when he was very young. I am sure he will tell you anything you may need to know of that land.

'I will await word of the raiding-party when you find it, and then we will turn our attention to the place from which it came: Gundabad, the Mountain-home of my people. For too long has it been kept by the Orcs, but their residency will soon come to an end!

'One more thing, ${PLAYER}. Keep a watchful eye on the Stout-axe and Zhélruka with whom you travel. They may not wish you ill, not directly, but they may treat you dishonestly if it serves their purposes. The Wells of Langflood are west of here, accessible by a narrow pass in the southern cliffs, east of the broken Rushgate. Journey there boldly, but with caution in your eye, and find this raiding-party before it can do more harm.''I will await word of the raiding-party when you find it, and then we will turn our attention to the place from which it came: Gundabad, the Mountain-home of my people. For too long has it been kept by the Orcs, but their residency will soon come to an end!

'One more thing, ${PLAYER}. Keep a watchful eye on the Stout-axe and Zhélruka with whom you travel. They may not wish you ill, not directly, but they may treat you dishonestly if it serves their purposes. The Wells of Langflood are west of here, accessible by a narrow pass in the southern cliffs, east of the broken Rushgate. Journey there boldly, but with caution in your eye, and find this raiding-party before it can do more harm.''Ah, there you are.''We are all arrived, my friend. This is the path that will take us to the foggy basin of Misthallow, through which runs the tributaries of a familiar river. The folk of these northern lands named it Langflood, but you would call it the Great River, or Anduin.

'The raiding-party from Gundabad was spotted somewhere near the Wells of Langflood. That is where we must go.'As you leaf through the pages of Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Sixth War of the Dwarves and Orcs, you pause at the volume containing a description of the Battle of Azanulbizar.Hermáth Stormhammer steps aside, allowing you to experience the Battle of Azanulbizar as he once did.Become Hermáth Stormhammer!The Iron-folk can be found in the centre of Amdân at Azanulbizar.Find and rally with the Iron-folk in AmdânDáin can be found among the Iron-folk at Amdân.

You should talk to Dáin.Talk to Dáin at AmdânDáin can be found among the Iron-folk at Amdân.

You should talk to Dáin.Talk to Dáin and attend the 'Council of the Seven'Dáin can be found among the Iron-folk at Amdân.

You should talk to Dáin.Talk to Dáin at Amdân'By the winter of T.A. 2799, King Thráin and his great reckoning-host, the Haban'akkâ, had made camp at Amdân, the ancient threshold of the kingdom of Khazad-dûm. It had been a long journey, for they had made a wide march around Lórien and come up from the south, treading the old stones of the Great-way.

'All through Amdân, Dwarves of the Seven Houses grew restless. I, like all of the Iron-folk, were camped near to the House of Thráin.

'Although few of us knew it at the time, it was to be the eve of battle.''Hermáth, my friend! I was beginning to think we lost you on the Great-way!'

Dáin embraces you warmly.

'It is hard to believe that we are here at last. I have read the stories of old Azanulbizar, but none could describe it in such detail.

'It seems fitting that this war should end where it began, don't you think?

'And if what I have heard is true, we will not have to sit idle for much longer!''Alas, such are the disadvantages of being the grandson of the Lord of the Iron Hills!

'But you, my friend... I suspect you could pass among the others in this great Haban’akkâ without a second glance, even with that hammer of yours!'You have been given a task to complete, and although your companions are not pleased to be travelling together, the four of you intend to see it successfully done.Imák and Venko are at the entrance to the pass that leads to Misthallow, east and south of the broken Rushgate.Talk to Imák at the entrance of the pass to MisthallowTalk to Venko at the entrance of the pass to MisthallowIt is time for you and your travelling companions to cross into Misthallow, the foggy basin in the northern reaches of the Wells of Langflood.Cross into Misthallow, the northern reaches of the Wells of LangfloodIn order to find a safe camp-site, you will need to slay aggressive beasts in Misthallow and scout for possible places to shelter out of the wind.Defeat beasts or other dangers in Misthallow (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Explore potential camp-site location east of HlithseldExplore potential camp-site location south-east of HlithseldImák has pointed out a spot that might make for a suitable camp-site.Explore potential camp-site location beside the rocks south-east of HlithseldYour travelling companions have found a suitable camp-site south-east of Hlithseld in Misthallow.'We will need to work together if we are to see this task accomplished. Imák? Venko? Do you not agree?''What say you? Can we work together in this matter, or will your squabbling continue?''Do not speak to me as if I were a mere child, Glóin. You are my elder in years, it is true, and your beard is white, but you spent no time in the dungeons of Sauron, as I have. Doubt not that I will match both you and ${PLAYER} in courage!

'A raiding-party of Orcs? I have proven far better against much worse. So do not treat me as some youthful stripling!

'Once we arrive in Misthallow we will need to find a place to camp, and that means fighting whatever aggressive beasts stray near our prospective camp-site. I will slay my share of the creatures, so long as you pull your own weight.''None of us want to be travelling together, Glóin, but each of us has reason to hate the Orcs. Let that purpose unify us, and in a day or two, once the raiding-party is destroyed we need not remain companions.

'Let us look forward to that, and we should have little reason to complain. The Wells of Langflood await us, and after we can return to our own concerns.

'I have never seen the lands along the Langflood, but we should keep watch for places at which we might camp, sheltered from the wind and from threats that might roam near.''Onward to Misthallow, then. The sooner we accomplish this task, the better: not only because it was commanded of us, but because my patience may not survive these dwarves for long!''Aye, this place will do.''Aye, this will do. Let me find my tinder-box and I will get a fire started.'You have come to Misthallow with your travelling companions in search of a raiding-party from Gundabad, and now the four of you need to set up camp.Imák and Venko need your help setting up the camp-site.Help Venko with 'Promises Unkept'Help Imák with 'A Nose for Danger'Glóin is at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Glóin at your camp-site in Misthallow'You have no doubt heard of the Quest for the Lonely Mountain, have you not? I understand our Burglar wrote quite a tale of our adventure! I mean no disrespect to his skill with a pen when I say it likely cannot compare to the real experience of the thing. Of course, we spent most of the time being either wet or cold, or both at once, and it is the nature of memory to sand off the rough edges until only the glory and excitement remains. Still, that was an adventure!

'I do not know if Bilbo recorded it in his book, but my brother Óin and I were responsible for getting the fires going whenever we stopped to camp; our tinder-boxes saw much use, and in far bleaker places than this one! While I see if I still have the skill, I suggest you help our companions unpack their belongings. We will rest here for awhile before we set out in search of the raiding-party in earnest.''While I see to this fire, I suggest you help our companions unpack their belongings. We will rest here for awhile before we set out in search of the raiding-party in earnest.''I told you I still possessed the fire-making skill! Ah, if Óin were still alive he would have had this fire going in half the time. My brother was the only one of Thorin's Company who could match me at campfire-lighting.

'Well, I suppose Gandalf could have, if he set his mind to it, but in my recollection he never offered to light a camp-fire, no matter how wet or cold we might have been. He would only set things ablaze when there were wargs about, and never only for convenience! I suppose that is the way of Wizards: they feel the cold less than dwarves, and they are less considerate as well!

'But you did not hear that from me, ${PLAYER}, I beg you.'No stranger to the adventuring life, Gimli's father Glóin reflects upon his past campaigns and eagerly awaits the next.Imák and Venko are at your camp-site in Misthallow, and they have some thoughts about Glóin's tale.Talk to Venko at your camp-site in MisthallowTalk to Imák at your camp-site in MisthallowGlóin is at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Glóin at your camp-site in MisthallowSilence has fallen upon your camp-site, and your companions eat and drink in silence.Eat in silence, leaving your companions to their thoughtsDrink in silence, leaving your companions to their thoughtsGlóin is at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Glóin at your camp-site in Misthallow'I understand you fought alongside my son Gimli at a number of recent battles, ${PLAYER}, and I look forward to hearing about them! Every people has its own way of recording the history of war, but there is none so detailed or pleasing to the ear as that of the dwarves; my people have had a great deal of practice at both the conduct of war and its description! Yes, I have lived a life of battle, and would prefer no other!

'I told you before that I fought at the Battle of Azanulbizar. Others of my kind earned glorious epithets like "Oakenshield" or "Ironfoot," and the dead were so numerous they could not be entombed; instead they were burned, and the honour of the Burned Dwarves of Azanulbizar will not be forgotten. I slew my share of Orcs in the Dimrill Dale, and I dare say I made my father proud. I expect it will be the same with Gimli when he shares his account of his own fighting in the south!

'Azanulbizar closed that war, but it was not the only such war between Dwarves and Orcs; nor would it be the last. It was the sixth, sometimes called by my people the Great Orc War. We held Gundabad again during that war, but only briefly, and after the losses at Azanulbizar King Thráin had neither the heart nor the warriors to reclaim it. The Longbeards became a people in exile.''Azanulbizar closed that war, but it was not the only such war between Dwarves and Orcs; nor would it be the last. It was the sixth, sometimes called by my people the Great Orc War. We held Gundabad again during that war, but only briefly, and after the losses at Azanulbizar King Thráin had neither the heart nor the warriors to reclaim it. The Longbeards became a people in exile.''Glóin speaks as if only Longbeards fought at Azanulbizar, but it was not so. Warriors from every dwarf-clan came together to fight the Orcs, and my people too numbered among the Burned Dwarves.

'Indeed, the Zhélruka know the pain of a life in exile as well as the Longbeards ever did, and we still do.''I know that tale, for dwarves of my clan fought in that war as well. Not so many as the Longbeards or the other dwarf-clans, perhaps, but there was glory earned by Stout-axes at Azanulbizar. Indeed, I would claim a greater share of the glory, for what was Khazad-dûm to the Stout-axes? Theirs was a selfless battle, borne out of friendship with the other clans and hatred for their shared, ancient enemies.

'The Longbeards might do well to remember the heroism of my people in those years, and treat them with greater respect!''I meant no offence, my companions. I did not forget the contributions of the other clans at Azanulbizar! ${PLAYER} will vouch for me: did I not speak of these very contributions to Durin before we departed from Skarháld? I did!

'Indeed, the other clans are not descended from Durin the Deathless, but they revere him still as the eldest of dwarves, the first to awake. Any insult to his heirs is an insult to all dwarves, and no dwarf-clan would permit such a cruelty as that done by Azog, the fearsome Orc that slew Thrór, the father of Thráin. Each of the clans responded, and so it was that Azog met his doom.

'There was glory enough for all, of course there was, and triumph not just for Durin's Folk but for all! You know that to be true. I did not mean to offend.''Bah! I spoke poorly, and without thought. I know the other clans were crucial to our victory, of course I know that.''Pah! I spoke over-hastily, for I am skilled with my axe but less so with words. If Gimli were here he would speak with more polish. He has always been more well-spoken than I, and he has a better grasp of the niceties than his father ever has.

'By my beard, ${PLAYER}, what is this expression you wear? Have you not found that to be true?'Glóin thinks the raiding-party from Gundabad may have passed near Framsburg, and the old dwarf believes you should begin your search near its abandoned ruins for the Orkish threat.Glóin directed you to meet up with him on the rocky spur to the west, on which stands the old town of Framsburg, from where the forebears of the Rohirrim once hailed.Meet up with Glóin at the rocky spur on which stands the ruins of FramsburgGlóin is on the rocky spur on which stands the ruins of Framsburg.Talk to Glóin on the Framsburg side of the bridgeDrakes, worms, and other creatures have taken up residence within the ruins of Framsburg, and Glóin thinks you should defeat the creatures while you search within Framsburg for some sign of the Gundabad raiding-party.Defeat creatures near the ruins of Framsburg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Explore the north-east side of the Framsburg promontoryExplore the northern ruins on the Framsburg promontoryFind some disturbed earth among the ruins of FramsburgGlóin is on the rocky spur on which stands the ruins of Framsburg. The old dwarf will be interested in the metal badge you discovered.Glóin believes the Orc raiding-party scouted its path from the Framsburg promontory and continued south, following the river.Find more tracks somewhere to the south, along the riverImák stands by the tracks he found south of Framsburg.Talk to Imák by the tracks south of Framsburg'I think that is enough rest for now. Let us find the raiding-party before this old dwarf trips upon his own beard and offends our companions further!

'Few people live in these lands, but I understand the forebears of the Rohirrim once lived alongside the Anduin somewhere in Misthallow. Their town was named Framsburg, and I expect it lies on the rocky spur to the west of us. Let us see if anyone lives there now, and find out what they may know of the raiding-party from Gundabad.

'I will help put away our camp-site and meet you there, ${PLAYER}.''Few people live in these lands, but I understand the forebears of the Rohirrim once lived alongside the Anduin somewhere in Misthallow. Their town was named Framsburg, and I expect it lies on the rocky spur to the west of here. Let us see if anyone lives there now, and find out what they may know of the raiding-party from Gundabad.

'I will help put away our camp-site and meet you there, ${PLAYER}.''Framsburg fell into ruin long ago, but that does not mean it will be empty. Be wary!''When I hoped this town might be inhabited, I was thinking of Men, not worms, drakes, or beasts! These creatures may not have willingly allowed the raiding-party to pass, but neither will they tell us anything of use. A few Orcs may wander among the ruins, but we seek not one or two; we are looking for a more substantial force!

'We did not come here for nothing. The town may still speak to us of the Orcs' passage. Search for signs of the raiding-party within the ruins of Framsburg, ${PLAYER}, and slay these other foes while you do. Men may not live in Framsburg now, but Durin has his sights set upon Gundabad to the north, and these creatures will pose a threat to any army passing nearby. Thinning their number can only be a boon for our future course.''The town may still speak to us of the Orcs' passage. Search for signs of the raiding-party within the ruins of Framsburg, ${PLAYER}, and slay these crawling terrors while you do. Men may not live in Framsburg now, but Durin has his sights set upon Gundabad to the north, and these creatures will pose a threat to any army passing nearby. Thinning their number can only be a boon for our future course.''What is this? Hmm... I have seen metal badges of this type before. The Orcs carry them, most likely to identify the tribe to which they belong. No dwarf would need such a thing! Can you imagine a Longbeard needing to carry a marker to remind himself he is a Longbeard?'

You consider pointing out the numerous symbols engraved on Glóin's weapons and clothes, but decide against it.

'I confess to you this symbol is not one I have encountered,' Glóin continues. 'It looks like a thundercloud to me. Perhaps that is the identity of the band to which our raiding Orcs belong: the Downpour of Woe, or the Deluge of Pain, or some other Orkish nonsense.

'There is only one way onto or off of this rocky spur, ${PLAYER}, and the raiding-party is no longer here. You found this badge close to the southern cliff's edge? It seems to me the Orcs stood there for a time, scouting their course, and this badge fell unnoticed as they did. I think they must have followed the river south. That is where our path leads!''Look at this, ${PLAYER}! Tracks... and these were made by creatures walking on two legs, not on four. Orcs!''There is only one way onto or off of this rocky spur, ${PLAYER}, and the raiding-party is no longer here. You found this badge close to the southern cliff's edge? It seems to me the Orcs stood there for a time, scouting their course, and this badge fell unnoticed as they did. I think they must have followed the river south. That is where our path leads!''These tracks were definitely made by the raiding-party. They scouted a course from high above, by the ruins of Framsburg, and they followed it.'You and your companions discovered evidence that the raiding-party travelled south from the ruins of Framsburg. Where has it now gone?Imák wants you to follow the river southward in search of the raiding-party.By Hengi's Leap you found more tracks left by the raiding-party, but the broken bridge likely prevented them from crossing the river. They may have continued south instead.Bloody tracks led eastward, away from the river.Find the source of the blood-spattered tracksThrúgord spoke of a village of 'Bear-folk' to which the raiding-party planned to go. He must have meant the town of Limlók, to the north-east.Travel toward the town of Limlók, to the north-eastVenko looks out upon the Beorning village of Limlók, the obvious question on his lips: where is the raiding-party?Talk to Venko in view of Limlók'Nothing about these tracks convinces me that the raiding-party changed its chosen course, ${PLAYER}. We should continue following the river south, but not without caution! Let us remain alert for further sign of the Orcs!''Follow the river south, and remain alert for further sign of the Orcs!''Keep following those tracks! I will be right behind you.''Stay back, vermin! You are no better than the treacherous slime that stabbed me, but Natsatúg was surrounded by a company of his fiercest warriors, and you are not!

'They left me here to die, but I have strength enough to cut your throat before my own life ends!'

Thrúgord's head sinks to his chest, and his sudden, barking laugh seems to cause him a great deal of pain. Even so, he bares his teeth in a smile.

'If you are able to slay me, do me this favour: go to the village of the Bear-folk and slay Natsatúg, the wretched villain!''The village seems safe, for now.''There lies the village, but it seems peaceful. We know the raiding-party came this way... but where has it gone?'You have come to the Beorning village of Limlók with your travelling companions, but there is no sign of the raiding-party from Gundabad.Venko does not believe that Limlók is safe, and the Zhélruka warrior wants to investigate.Talk to Venko and search Limlók for the vanished raiding-partyImák stands in the centre of Limlók, satisfied with the day's fighting.Talk to Imák in Limlók'Do you remember the metal badge marked with the thundercloud symbol? The one you found among the ruins of Framsburg that the Orcs must have dropped? Glóin showed it to me while we journeyed south. I do not know about you, ${PLAYER}, but I do not generally think thunderclouds are known for being stealthy.

'No, Thrúgord's fellows are around here somewhere. That they have not shown themselves reveals a more worrying purpose than I believed they could possess. If they are not mere pillagers... what are they?

'We should go down into the town. Is it too much to hope that they have already turned aside these raiders?''Why has this raiding-party not shown itself? Perhaps the villagers have seen something of use to us.''And so our mission is at an end, ${PLAYER}! Had I known at the outset how little time we would need to spend together before its successful conclusion, I might not have complained so loudly! Ah, it is a jest only.

'You performed admirably during the attack, ${RACE}. Indeed, Venko and Glóin also proved themselves most able in battle. These folk are fortunate that we were here.'The Blade-feast raiding-party has been defeated and the village of Limlók is safe, but the news that Gundabad has a new lord can only mean further troubles ahead for the region washed by the Langflood.Glóin has walked away from your companions and stands now at the edge of Limlók, deep in thought. What troubles the old dwarf?Join Glóin at the edge of LimlókGlóin has walked away from your companions and stands now at the edge of Limlók, deep in thought.Talk to Glóin at the edge of LimlókRunerik Knotbeard stands in the village of Limlók.Return to Runerik Knotbeard in the village of LimlókRunerik Knotbeard stands in the village of Limlók.Talk to Runerik Knotbeard in Limlók.Imák stands in the village of Limlók.Talk to Venko in LimlókTalk to Imák in LimlókGlóin has returned to Limlók.Talk to Glóin in Limlók'But where has the old Longbeard gone? Does he not wish to celebrate this victory with the rest of us, and with the people of the village?''Where has the old Longbeard gone?''I do not like it... no, not at all...''Where has the old Longbeard gone?''I am sorry, my friend. I should celebrate with the others, for the raiding-party is destroyed and Limlók is safe, but the words of Natsatúg as he lay dying have embedded themselves in my heart like a knife.

'The name he spoke is known to me, for his lineage is long and cruel and has troubled Durin's Folk for all the years of my life. I spoke to you of Azog, who led the Orcs against us during the Great Orc War, and perished beneath Dáin's heel at Azanulbizar? A son he had, Bolg, who commanded the Orcs at the Battle of Five Armies. Bolg died before the Lonely Mountain, but his line did not end there. Two were the sons of Bolg. One was named Gorgar, and if the words of Natsatúg can be trusted he calls himself now the Lord of Gundabad.

'Bolg's other son was Mazog, ${PLAYER}, the same Mazog who was slain by my old companion Bofur's grandson Broín, wielding the mithril axe Zigilburk. This new Lord of Gundabad possesses no shortage of grievance against the dwarves, but the death of his brother Mazog can only fuel that most powerful of desires: a thirst for blood, and vengeance.'Glóin remains silent, contemplating the words of Natsatúg.'Why do your companions wear these solemn expressions? Nothing has changed: Orcs have long been a threat by the Wells of Langflood, and this is not the first raiding-party whose blood has watered the ground near Limlók. A new Orc-lord in Gundabad means little to me or my people.

'Should he send another batch of raiders, they too will be slain. I do not see why this need be any different.''Should this new Lord of Gundabad send another batch of raiders, they too will be slain. I do not see why this need be any different.''I understand Runerik's position, for Orcs have always threatened his lands and he sees no difference here. Would you ask me to be newly-afraid of the worms and drakes of the Grey Mountains, or would I respond as he does?''I think Runerik is twice-wrong about this. Something about this new Lord of Gundabad smells wrong to me, and my nose is never wrong. Second, he says his people will simply stand against whatever raiders come his way? I say he is wrong! Without our assistance Limlók would be a smoking ruin by now, and he had best not forget it!''I have made up my mind, ${PLAYER}. Gorgar the Ruthless prepares for war. Whether he is motivated by vengeance or traditional Orkish hatred does not matter. It cannot matter. Whatever his reasons, this new Lord of Gundabad must be stopped before he is ready to strike.

'I will send messages back to Durin at Skarháld and to King Thorin at the Lonely Mountain. We cannot wait for Gorgar to attack. We must assemble our armies before he is made ready. Durin was right, ${PLAYER}. We must retake Gundabad from its new master. It is no longer a mere dream. It is now a matter of survival.'

<rgb=#00FF00>The story continues in future updates!</rgb>As you continue to read Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar, you begin the second chapter in which the Dwarves of the Haban'akkâ of Thráin wake to a dark winter's day and the battle is joined.Dwarves of the Landorrim, Narfanghoth, and Temámir can be found at Zirakazhâr on the slopes of Zirakzigil to the north-west of Amdân. Dwarves of the Kámbrada, Zhélruka, and Abnúzhu can be found at Ashmargathâl, far to the north of Amdân on the slopes of Bundushâthur.

Dáin has asked you to aid the other Dwarves of the Haban'akkâ. You should aid the dwarves in Amdân, as well as those who have battled to Zirakazhâr and Ashmargathâl.Aid the Dwarves of the Landorrim, Narfanghoth, and Temámir at ZirakazhârAid the Dwarves of the Kámbrada, Zhélruka, and Abnúzhu at AshmargathâlAn Iron-folk messenger can be found at Zirakazhâr or Ashmargathâl.

After aiding the Dwarves of the Haban'akkâ, a messenger of the Iron-folk has arrived bearing urgent news for you.Talk to the Iron-folk messenger at ZirakazhârTalk to the Iron-folk messenger at AshmargathâlHermáth Stormhammer can be found at Khur Azan, the Dim-crossing.

After learning of how Hermáth Stormhammer aided the dwarves who battled upon the slopes of Zirakzigil and Bundushâthur, Hermáth Stormhammer interjects and calls your attention to events at Khur Azan, the Dim-crossing.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer at Khur Azan'As we heard at the council last night, King Thráin has ordered the Seven Houses to aid upon the fringes of the dale.

'Among them, the Landorrim, Narfanghoth, and Temámir have travelled to Zirakazhâr on the slopes of Zirakzigil. Once it is reclaimed from the Orcs, I fear they shall have to contend with the trolls we spied from afar.

'As for the Kámbrada, Zhélruka, and Abnúzhu... they have a harder path to take. If they can break through the Orkish outpost blocking the way to Tharkh Shathur, they intend to claim Ashmargathâl and set siege to Atrad-zarakh, the old warding-spire of Khazad-dûm.

'It is far too much, indeed, to ask of any one of the Seven Houses, but together, we will triumph! By Durin's beard, Azog will not live to see the morrow!''As the battle continued on the slopes of Zirakzigil and Bundushâthur, I was recalled to Khur Azan. It seemed that Dáin's ruse had not gone as unnoticed as he thought, but more pressing matters were at hand.

'Indeed, the Iron-folk had marched forth from Amdân at last—not yet to join the battle, but to stand closer in reserve after King Thráin's vanguard burst through the Orkish encampment of Kamaz-kala, the furthest border of Azog's would-be realm.''Lord Náin suspected you would be here, Hermáth. I am certain our cousins from the Blue Mountains and those of the East have been grateful for your aid, but I come with orders from King Thráin himself!

'King Thráin and his vanguard make ready to set out, and we of the Iron-folk have been commanded to rally to Khur Azan.

'There is no time to waste! Follow me!'

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will swiftly travel to Khur Azan when you advance this quest.</rgb>'Lord Náin suspected you would be here, Hermáth. I am certain our cousins from the East have been grateful for your aid, but I come with orders from King Thráin himself!

