'When the great mass of goblins and Orcs erupted from the deep places beneath Moria in that last great flood of evil that spelled defeat for the Iron Garrison, I was separated from my comrades. I was lost and alone in the darkness, cut off from my friends, and it seemed my end was truly at hand. But wait! I still had the map you helped me to prepare of the Winding Way, and by making use of it I was able to find my way back to my retreating allies... so I thank you once again!

'Prince Durin has need of our mapping skill in the war effort, ${PLAYER}. Take this parchment and scout the ruins and camps nearby: two old dwarf ruins, one to the north-west and one to the south-west. When you arrive at these, I ask that you draw them on the map. I think there must also be a warg den further north-west, so map that too! In this way, Durin can fashion a plan of attack. Good fortune to you, and when you have finished I will meet you further ahead at Zudramdân.''Prince Durin has need of our mapping skill in the war effort, ${PLAYER}. Take this parchment and scout the ruins and camps nearby: two old dwarf ruins, one to the north-west and one to the south-west. When you arrive at these, I ask that you draw them on the map. I think there must also be a warg den further north-west, so map that too! In this way, Durin can fashion a plan of attack. Good fortune to you, and when you have finished I will meet you at Zudramdân, the site that will be our foothold further ahead!''Oh, this map is quite well-done, ${PLAYER}, in both artistry and knowledge.

'I heard some troubling news, however. It seems there may be some Angmarim cultists readying for one of their loathsome rituals north of here, at Caivád Sâr. Can I ask you to go there to investigate, defeating any of the cultists you find and adding their camp to your map? Prince Durin will want to know the extent of the Angmarim force allied with the Orcs of Gundabad.''It seems there may be some Angmarim cultists readying for one of their loathsome rituals at Caivád Sâr, to the north. Can I ask you to go there to investigate, defeating any of the cultists you find and adding their camp to your map? Prince Durin will want to know the extent of the Angmarim force allied with the Orcs of Gundabad.''Hmm. I am worried that both the Orc and Angmarim presence may be larger than my initial scouting indicated, ${PLAYER}. You are certain these numbers are accurate?

'And where did all these cultists come from? I thought their kingdom was ended!'Thálfi is worried that the forces defending Gundabad need a more thorough scouting, but they will not give you the benefit of leisurely adding their camps to your map.Thálfi wants you to scout enemy camps nearer to Gundabad and to defeat any Orcs and Angmarim you find on the way. This time he has not asked you to draw the locations on a map, and will rely instead upon your report. Crossing the bridge in front of Gundabad and exploring past the central courtyard on your way to the buildings beyond the entrance should be sufficient.Scout the bridge in front of GundabadScout the central courtyard in front of GundabadScout the settlement of Dûn Traikh beyond the entrance to GundabadDefeat Orc-kind in Elderslade (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Angmarim in Elderslade (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thálfi is at Zudramdân in Elderslade.Talk to Thálfi at Zudramdân in EldersladeImák is at Zudramdân in Elderslade, and he wishes to speak with you.Talk to Imák at Zudramdân in Elderslade'I need you to scout the camps nearer to Gundabad now, ${PLAYER}. Trouble not to draw these on the map, for I suspect the enemy presence at these locations will afford you no time to draw! No, this time I ask you simply to observe the situation nearer to Gundabad, and then report back to me what you saw.

'Perhaps it goes without saying, but please be careful. And this too: defeat any Orcs and Angmarim in your way!''Find those camps nearer to Gundabad and see what there is to see, slaying whatever Orcs are foolish enough to cross your path. Trouble not to draw these camps on the map; I will rely upon your report and not your artist's hand!''Thank you, my friend! What did you learn of the enemy presence nearer to the gates of Gundabad?'

You give Thálfi your report, and he makes some notations on the map.

'Yes, I see. We were both right: there is no shortage of foes! Perhaps by studying this information Prince Durin will find some weakness in the line. Hmm... no such vulnerability makes itself known to me, but I am only a scout, and not a master strategist. I have faith in the prince and his advisors. One of them will surely see something.''What is this Stout-axe trying to say?''Are all Longbeards so trusting, so willing to rely upon blind faith? A Stout-axe does not sit idly by, waiting for the tide of fortune to turn. He takes action!'Imák does not believe that the Longbeards are adequately prepared for the assault on Gundabad, and he wants to adopt a different approach.Goblins carry sapper-bombs that Imák wants for his plan. He also wants you to collect some loose, heavy rocks from the base of cliffs north of Zudramdân in Elderslade.Collect sapper-bombs from goblins in Elderslade (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect loose rocks from the base of cliffs in Elderslade (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Imák is at Zudramdân in Elderslade.

You have collected a number of sapper-bombs and loose rocks and should bring them back to Imák.Thálfi is at Zudramdân in Elderslade.Talk to Thálfi at ZudramdânDurin is on the upper level of Annâk-khurfu.Talk to Durin on the upper level of Annâk-khurfuPrince Ingór is on the lower level of Annâk-khurfu.Talk to Prince Ingór on the lower level of Annâk-khurfuDurin is on the upper level of Annâk-khurfu.'Durin's Longbeards, like this scout here, must be willing to do whatever it takes if they are to have victory in Gundabad. Their combat prowess will not be enough, not by itself. Do you think the Orcs will face the dwarves in a fair fight? No! They will do whatever they can to gain an advantage, and so too should we.

'It is a lesson I learned in Mordor, and I learned it well: Durin must think like his foes if he is to have the greatest chance of success. If he wants to have the victory, he will need to think like a goblin!

'I have a plan, ${PLAYER}. I want you to gather heavy rocks from the base of cliffs and some of the explosives carried by goblins throughout Elderslade. Depriving our enemies of these items will surely help to tilt the field in our favour.''I want you to gather heavy rocks from the base of cliffs and some of the explosives carried by goblins throughout Elderslade. Depriving our enemies of these items will surely help to tilt the field in our favour.''These will do nicely, ${PLAYER}. I have in mind a number of traps that will prove most harmful for our enemies, and surely turn the tide in our favour.

'Victory is a step closer now.''Victory is a step closer now. Even this Longbeard should see it. Or does he not wish to reclaim Gundabad after all?''I don't know about this, ${PLAYER}. Setting traps? Trying to win a battle through deceit? Imák is not wrong about this, at least: these strategies belong to the Orcs! If it is up to me, I say they can keep them.

'I have finished the scouting map. Here, I give it you so you can deliver it to Durin at Annâk-khurfu. Will you also bring him word of my discomfort with Imák's plan? I do not think dwarves should rely upon traps in this way, and I am sure the prince will agree.''Deliver that map to Durin at Annâk-khurfu. Will you also bring him word of my discomfort with Imák's plan? I do not think dwarves should rely upon traps in this way, and I am sure the prince will agree.''You have returned with tidings from Thálfi?'

You hand Durin your map of Elderslade with Thálfi's annotations. While he studies the defensive layout before Gundabad, you tell him about Imák's plan to use traps against the enemy forces.

'The Stout-axe is not wrong: given the opportunity, the Orcs would prefer not to face us in a fair fight. That is not the Longbeard way. It is not my way.'

He draws some lines on the map with a bit of charcoal and hands it back to you.

'But we must reclaim Gundabad, at any cost. If the traps Imák has devised will help us in this, I will not command him otherwise. Do not think the battle will be won with deception alone! The greatest share of the burden will fall upon the swords and axes of my Gabil'akkâ!

'Show that map to Prince Ingór downstairs, and have him agree that his Zhélruka will move in these marked formations when the fighting begins. It is nearly time to begin the assault!''Show that map to Prince Ingór below, and have him agree that his Zhélruka will move in these marked formations when the fighting begins. It is nearly time to begin the assault!''Durin sends you to ME with a plan for the first attack? Why should he not wait for my plan? I have my own scouts, and I trust their word more than any of his!'

Prince Ingór takes the map from you and studies it with distaste. Then he produces his own piece of charcoal and obscures Durin's notes with his own.

'I have some reservations with this plan. Take this map back to the Longbeard and tell him I have improved it.''Bring my misgivings back to Durin.''We are out of time, ${PLAYER}! I have received word that an army of Orcs has emerged from Gundabad, and they march now on our position!'

You give him the map marked with Prince Ingór's annotations and he hands it to one of his lieutenants.

'The time for planning is over! The war is upon us!'The great armies of Durin and Prince Ingór begin their assault upon Gundabad, but the forces that defend it will not relinquish the Mountain-home without a fight.Durin is on the upper level of Annâk-khurfu, waiting to give the word for the assault to begin.Talk to Durin and begin the assault upon GundabadDurin is inside Zarkul-sulûn, commanding his forces amid the chaos of the War of Three Peaks.Talk to Durin inside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three PeaksPrince Ingór is north-east of Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks, standing atop a barricade and commanding his Zhélruka.Talk to Prince Ingór north-east of Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks'For too long have Orcs dwelt within the sacred mountain of my people! Gundabad was home to the first of us! It was home to the best of us! Let us take it back!'

Durin bares his teeth and gives the assembled crowd a ferocious smile. Then he breathes deeply, and in a quieter tone he addresses you.

'Are you prepared, ${PLAYER}? Shall I give the order to march?''Then it begins: the War of Three Peaks is upon us! For Gundabad!''What is the meaning of this? Ingór was supposed to lead his Zhélruka at the head of the charge! Why then did the dwarves under his command dawdle in the rear, allowing my Longbeards to bear the brunt of the first impact? What can it be but cowardice?

'${PLAYER}! Find this Zhélruka coward! I demand answers! Does he still dawdle outside?''Does Ingór have an excuse for this cowardice? Where is he? Does he still dawdle outside?''Speak not so to me, ${RACE}! I know why Durin wanted the Zhélruka to lead the charge... he thought to expend us at the tip of the spear, throwing away our lives and holding his own people in reserve! He thought to spend Zhélruka blood to gain Gundabad... and reward it to his Longbeards!

'But he is not as canny as he believes, and I did not fall for his scheme!'Ingór is furious at Durin's suggestion that the Zhélruka prince kept his forces in reserve during the initial assault of the War of Three Peaks.Prince Ingór wants you to pick up weapons dropped by his slain warriors as proof that the Zhélruka are shouldering their own share of the war's burden. You should also slay any enemies that break through the line.Collect fallen Zhélruka weapons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat enemy forces during the War of Three Peaks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})A crate for storing spare weapons can be found in a trench north-west of Prince Ingór's defensive position during the War of Three Peaks.Bori is in the trench north-west of Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks.Talk to Bori in the trench north-west of Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three PeaksBori wanted you to look for his cousin Bróin or his father Bósi north-west of his trench. He has not seen them since the fighting began, and he is worried for their safety.Find Bróin or Bósi north-west of Bori's trenchBósi told you he saw dwarves retreating from thick fighting to the north. You should search there for Bróin.Find the retreating dwarves Bósi saw near the trench to the northYou found Trausti in a trench not far from the lakeside while the fighting rages all about him and the War of Three Peaks continues.Talk to Trausti in the trench not far from the lakeside during the War of Three Peaks'I bristle at these accusations, and refute them! My Zhélruka have fought and died as bravely as any two Longbeards, and if Durin calls me coward he will answer to my axe! He seeks proof that my Zhélruka have paid the price? Look then upon the corpses of my slain, and gather their fallen weapons! Then you will see that my own folk have done their share... nay, more than their share!

'The Zhélruka release their weapons only when slain! Collect the weapons by their corpses and store them in a barrel at the trench to the north-west so they can be wielded by Longbeards who cast aside their own weapons when the fighting turned fierce! Durin speaks to me of cowardice? He should look to his own!''The Zhélruka release their weapons only when slain! Collect the weapons by their corpses and store them in a barrel at the trench to the north-west so they can be wielded by Longbeards who cast aside their own weapons when the fighting turned fierce!''The Zhélruka release their weapons only when slain! Collect the weapons by their corpses and store them in a barrel at the trench to the north-west so they can be wielded by Longbeards who cast aside their own weapons when the fighting turned fierce!''${PLAYER}, have you seen my father or cousin? I lost track of them after the first assault, and I have not been able to find them amid the chaos. Can you look for them? I want to make sure they are still all right.

'I have not been able to search beyond this trench to the north-west. Perhaps they were pushed that way during the fighting!''I have not been able to get north-west of this trench. Perhaps my father or cousin were pushed that way during the fighting!''Ready yourself, ${PLAYER}! They prepare for another assault!''There was a great sound of shouting to the north-west! Perhaps my cousin is there?''There was a great sound of shouting from the north-west! Perhaps my cousin is there?''I thought Gundabad had unleashed all of its defenders upon us, but it was not so! Another wave of enemies emerged from the mountain, forcing us to give ground!'Fighting during the War of Three Peaks has turned against the great force of Durin and Prince Ingór, and their warriors will need to stand together if they are to have the victory. Can they set aside their differences and achieve the triumph they seek?Trausti last saw Bróin and Slagvi fighting side-by-side to the north, while battle raged on all around them.Find Bróin somewhere to the north, where Trausti last saw himSlagvi has been trapped by a fall of stones, and Bróin is trying to free him from the heavy rocks.Talk to Bróin as he works to free Slagvi from the rock trapSlagvi has been trapped by a fall of stones, and Bróin is trying to free him from the heavy rocks.

Bróin wants you to help him free Slagvi from the rocks, and he will need you to defend them while he works!Bróin helps Slagvi to safety!Bróin helps Slagvi to safety!Meet up with Slagvi in a trench to the south while the War of Three Peaks continues.Meet up with Slagvi several trenches to the south during the War of Three PeaksSlagvi cannot believe his ears: the trap in which he was caught had been set by the Stout-axe Imák? He demands that you find Imák and ask why he has chosen to fight dwarves and not Orcs.Find Imák outside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three PeaksImák is outside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks, surrounded by an angry group of Longbeards.Talk to Imák outside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three PeaksImák has reluctantly agreed not to use the goblin-bombs you collected. He sent you to collect the bombs from the crate where he stored them, inside Zarkul-sulûn, and render them unusable.Collect and defuse the goblin-bombs from the crate where Imák stored them, inside Zarkul-sulûnThe dwarf Afwald stands near the empty crate where Imák stored the goblin-bombs, in Zarkul-sulûn. You should ask him what happened to the explosive devices.Talk to Afwald by the empty crate in Zarkul-sulûn, during the War of Three Peaks'The tide of battle turned against us, and we were forced back! This is not a defeat, nay, but we need time to regroup. We must steel ourselves and return to the fray!

'You are looking for Bróin? I did not see him during the retreat. When last I saw him he fought alongside Slagvi, north of here. Did he not escape with the rest of us?''You are looking for Bróin? I did not see him during the retreat. When last I saw him he fought alongside Slagvi, north of here. Did he not escape with the rest of us?''${PLAYER}! By Durin's beard, I am glad to see you!

'Slagvi and I fought against wave after wave of Orcs, but we were forced back by the sheer ferocity of their attack. During a break in the onslaught, as we prepared to rejoin the others, Slagvi tripped over some hidden cord and was partially buried beneath this fall of stones! It must be some goblin-trick, set here to ensnare us!

'Help me lift these stones from him, ${PLAYER}!''Help me lift these stones from him, ${PLAYER}!''Did I hear you rightly? That trap was set not by the Orcs, but by Imák the Stout-axe? Is this true? Is he a fool, or has he been stricken with some madness? We have difficulty enough fighting the Orcs without having to worry about running afoul of traps set by our supposed allies!

'I cannot believe this! A pox on the Stout-axes! You must make this right! Find Imák and ask why he has chosen to fight dwarves and not Orcs! Does he not have enough enemies from which to choose that he must attack his supposed allies too?''You must make this right! Find Imák and ask why he has chosen to fight dwarves and not Orcs! Does he not have enough enemies from which to choose that he must attack his supposed allies too?''These Longbeards should save their breath! They will not cease hurling insults and threats at me, but I have weathered worse winds than these! What is the Greymaul Rebellion to me? They think I care about the stories of ancient dwarves? Bah. They are nothing to me!

'They think I was wrong to set traps for the Orcs? If these Longbeards paid more attention to their surroundings they would not blunder into one of my devices! You would not think they wanted to reclaim Gundabad at all, so unwilling are they to dirty their hands!

'Well, they will be happy to know this: I did not have time to put the rest of my plan into motion before the defenders emerged from Gundabad and marched on our position. I lacked the time to set the goblin-bombs you collected, so I stored them in a crate inside Zarkul-sulûn. It is a shame, for those explosives could have claimed the lives of many Orcs if I had placed them as I wanted.''I lacked the time to set the goblin-bombs you collected, so I stored them in a crate inside Zarkul-sulûn. It is a shame, for those explosives could have claimed the lives of many Orcs if I had placed them as I wanted.''Are you looking for something, ${PLAYER}? I heard the battle might be turning against us, but as long as you are still fighting I think victory must still be within reach!'

You tell Afwald that you are looking for a number of items that Imák stored in this crate. He nods.

'Oh, the goblin-bombs? I did wonder why they were being saved. Eywind came by and gathered them up. He seemed very excited about them, now that I think about it.'

Afwald sees something in your expression.

'Is that a problem?' he asks.The dwarf Eywind has a long history of setting goblins on fire. But will he prove able to distinguish friend from foe amid the chaos of the War of Three Peaks?Eywind is somewhere near Caivád Sâr as the War of Three Peaks continues.Talk to Eywind near the Angmarim tower of Caivád Sâr during the War of Three PeaksSome dwarves remain dangerously close to the path where Eywind has set his stolen goblin-bombs! Eywind will not evacuate them, so it falls to you.Convince dwarves to evacuate the north-eastern road targeted by Eywind (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Eywind is south of the Angmarim tower of Caivád Sâr, and you should return to him quickly. You do not know how long his goblin-bombs will remain undetonated.Return to Eywind south of Caivád Sâr during the War of Three PeaksEywind is south of Caivád Sâr, admiring his handiwork.Talk to Eywind south of Caivád Sâr during the War of Three PeaksEywind's satisfaction has turned to displeasure: for some reason not all of the bombs exploded. Did goblin-sappers recover some of the bombs, and do they now plan to use them against the dwarves? You should try and recover these twice-stolen goblin-bombs, while there is still time. Goblin-sneaks can be found in Eywind's vicinity near Caivád Sâr during the War of Three Peaks.Eywind's satisfaction has turned to displeasure: for some reason not all of the bombs exploded. Did goblin-sappers recover some of the bombs, and do they now plan to use them against the dwarves? You should try and recover these twice-stolen goblin-bombs, while there is still time. Goblin-sneaks can be found in Eywind's vicinity near Caivád Sâr during the War of Three Peaks.Recover twice-stolen goblin-bombs from goblin sneaks (2/3)The goblin-sneak probably set his stolen bomb somewhere near the lakeside trench to the south-west, hoping to do as much damage to the dwarves as possible. You have to find and defuse it as quickly as you can!Find and quench the hidden goblin-bomb somewhere near the lakeside trenchEywind is in the lakeside trench during the War of Three Peaks.Talk to Eywind in the lakeside trench during the War of Three PeaksDurin is inside Zarkul-sulûn while the War of Three Peaks continues.Talk to Durin inside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks'Oh, he does like setting things aflame, doesn't he? As long as he is careful and vigilant, perhaps he will make good use of those goblin-bombs against our foes.

'It may be a good idea for you to find him and ensure he is using caution, ${PLAYER}. He mentioned something about the Angmarim tower of Caivád Sâr, so you should begin your search near there.''It may be a good idea for you to find Eywind and ensure he is using caution, ${PLAYER}. He said something about the Angmarim tower of Caivád Sâr, so you should begin your search near there.''Yes, my friend, yes! This war will soon be ended, and Durin will have the victory! I have set a number of goblin-bombs on the path to the north-east, and in mere moments the Orcs and their Angmarim allies will be consumed by a conflagration of their own devising! Reinforcements will no longer be able to use that path to come to the defence of Caivád Sâr, and it is all thanks to my foresight and ingenuity! This conflagration may not be as impressive as the fire we set at Ghâshru, but it will serve. Ah, if only we had some of Gandalf's draught left! That made for a truly spectacular inferno!

'What's that? How close will the fires be to our fighting dwarves? Hmm... good question, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps you should tell them to back up a bit!''How close will the fires be to our fighting dwarves? Hmm... good question. Perhaps you should tell them to back up a bit!''Well? How did it go?''Ha ha! Tremendous! Did you see that? What a beautiful sight! It serves those goblins right for trying to use such weapons against us... how do you think they like the power of the devices they fashioned?

'But... there were not as many explosions as there should have been, friend. Do you think it is possible some goblin-sneaks managed to retrieve some of the bombs I set and think now to use them against us?

'There are three goblin-bombs for which I cannot account, ${PLAYER}! Steal back the bombs they stole from us! Yes, the ones we stole from them first!''Do you think it is possible some goblin-sneaks managed to retrieve some of the bombs I set and think now to use them against us?

'There are three goblin-bombs for which I cannot account, ${PLAYER}! Steal back the bombs they stole from us! Yes, the ones we stole from them first!''Do you think it is possible some goblin-sneaks managed to retrieve some of the bombs I set and think now to use them against us?

'There are three goblin-bombs for which I cannot account, ${PLAYER}! Go find them, and steal back the stolen bombs they stole from us!''The last goblin-bomb must have already been placed, but where? The goblin's boots were wet? That tells me he must have left the bomb somewhere near the lake to the south-west...

'Check the trenches near the lake, ${PLAYER}!''You quenched the last goblin-bomb? Good. It is a pity we were not able to turn it against its creators, but at least we made effective use of the others!

'Return to Durin inside Zarkul-sulûn and learn what is to be our next plan of attack. The fighting turns once again in our favour!''Return to Durin inside Zarkul-sulûn and learn what is to be our next plan of attack. The fighting turns once again in our favour!''We were pushed back on our heels, ${PLAYER}, but we did not give up hope, and now we are rewarded: the tide of battle has once again turned in our favour!

'Victory is at hand!'Durin believes the dwarves are beginning to turn the tide of battle in their favour, and he wishes to lead a charge up the steps to face the Lord of Gundabad and claim the fortress.Durin asked you to use the signal horn outside Zarkul-sulûn to rally the dwarves for his assault upon the threshold of Gundabad.Blow the signal horn outside Zarkul-sulûnDurin asked you to use the signal horn at one of the trenches to the north-west of Zarkul-sulûn to rally the dwarves for his assault upon the threshold of Gundabad.Blow the signal horn in one of the trenches to the north-west of Zarkul-sulûnDurin asked you to use the signal horn at the hidden cave of Drenghól, nearest encampment to the Mountain-home, to rally the dwarves for his assault upon the threshold of Gundabad.Blow the signal horn in the hidden cave of Drenghól, the nearest encampment to GundabadBróin is in the hidden cave of Drenghól, nearest dwarf-encampment to Gundabad.Talk to Bróin inside the hidden cave of DrenghólBori is outside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks.Talk to Bori outside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks'One obstacle remains, my friend. We must do something about Gorgar the Ruthless, the Orc who rules Gundabad. His Orcs will defend the Mountain-home from us for as long as he lives, but if I can put an end to him they will scatter. They will learn first-hand that only a dwarf can lay claim to the title Lord of Gundabad!

'Blow the signal horns to rally dwarves to my banner: outside Zarkul-sulûn, in the field of battle, and at our forward encampment nearest to the Mountain-home! I will lead my warriors up the steps on this last offensive, and on the very threshold of the fortress I will challenge Gorgar for the rule of Gundabad!''Blow the signal horns to rally dwarves to my banner: outside Zarkul-sulûn, in the field of battle, and at our forward encampment nearest to the Mountain-home! I will lead my warriors up the steps on this last offensive, and on the very threshold of the fortress I will challenge Gorgar for the rule of Gundabad!''Blow the signal horns to rally dwarves to my banner! I will lead my warriors up the steps on this last offensive, and on the very threshold of the fortress I will challenge Gorgar for the rule of Gundabad!''Blow the signal horns to rally dwarves to my banner! I will lead my warriors up the steps on this last offensive, and on the very threshold of the fortress I will challenge Gorgar for the rule of Gundabad!''I am sorry, ${PLAYER}. We are right on the threshold of victory, but my heart is heavy.'

You ask Bróin what you can do to lighten his burden, and he frowns.

'There is nothing, my friend. These worries are my own, and...'

He trails away, engaged in some internal debate. At last he nods decisively.

'You have always stood by me and my kin, ${PLAYER}, no matter the mistakes we have made; I will forget neither that loyalty nor those kindnesses. Neither will I keep these troubles from you, though I am shamed by them. This then is my confession, friend.

'I fear my decision to seal away the mithril axe may have cost my father his life. Would Brogur still live today if we had wielded Zigilburk in defence of the Iron Garrison? When the horde emerged from the depths of Moria, could I have repelled them if I still possessed the truest reflection of Durin's own axe in this Age of the world? I chose to seal Zigilburk away because I thought it was cursed, but can you truly say that the Iron Garrison's fate in Moria could have been worse than it was? I find that hard to believe.

'How many lives might I have saved if I kept Zigilburk, ${PLAYER}? How many lives in Moria, and how many lives here, on the steps of Gundabad?'

You speak what reassuring words you can, and Bróin thanks you for them, but you can tell his heart remains heavy.

'Tell my cousin Bori I am well... or well enough. He does worry.''Tell my cousin Bori I am well... or well enough. He does worry.''Did you hear the signal horns? Durin prepares to lead an assault upon the threshold of the Mountain-home, where he will strike down Gorgar the Ruthless and become the Lord of Gundabad!'

<rgb=#00FF00>If you wish to participate in the raid, this is when 'Amdân Dammul, the Bloody Threshold' takes place during the story.</rgb>Durin's assault up the steps of Gundabad intends to reclaim the Mountain-home from the new Lord of Gundabad, but such a prize cannot be claimed so easily.Bori is outside Zarkul-sulûn, eager to follow after Durin's assault up the steps. Can victory be close at hand?Talk to Bori outside Zarkul-sulûn and go with him to confront the Lord of GundabadDurin is inside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks, still reeling from the events of his assault on the steps before Gundabad.Talk to Durin inside Zarkul-sulûn during the War of Three Peaks'Let us follow after Durin, my friend! We will lend what aid we can, and be among the first to venture within the Mountain-home in victory!

'I have an idea. Let us take another path to the steps of Gundabad. In that way we will obtain the element of surprise, and may be of greater help to the prince and to his warriors!''Quickly! We must follow Durin and his warriors! Thálfi told me of a path near Dûn Traikh that will allow us to gain access to the threshold of Gundabad from the other side!''We were so near to victory, ${PLAYER}! What cruel chance has brought Hrímil Frost-heart here to rob us of our triumph? How came she to escape her icy prison?

'This is not a defeat. We will not stray from our course! We will regroup and mount one last offensive, and no Orc or dragon will stand before the might of my armies! I am descended from the line of Durin, and I will prove the glory of my name and the rightness of my cause!

'Rally my forces. When all is ready, we will storm the gates, and Gundabad will once again be ours, at last!'

<rgb=#00FF00>The story continues in the Gundabad expansion!</rgb>As you continue to read Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar, you begin the fourth chapter in which the Dwarves of the Haban’akkâ of Thráin turn the tide of battle and the Iron-folk charge to meet Azog at the gates of Khazad-dûm.Iron-folk can be found on the field near Durin's Stone.

Dáin has asked you to command the Iron-folk and send them to aid his father at the gates of Khazad-dûm.Dáin can be found at Durin's Stone.

You have ordered the Iron-folk to aid Náin Orcsfoe at the gates of Khazad-dûm. You should talk to Dáin.Talk to Dáin and confront 'The Lord of Moria'Hermáth Stormhammer can be found at Muru Azog at Azanulbizar.

After the defeat of Azog, Hermáth Stormhammer interjects and calls your attention to events at Muru Azog. You should talk to Hermáth Stormhammer.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer at Muru Azog'Urro is slain? That is no small feat, my friend, and all the better for us!

'When Bolg fled the field, my father and his guard pursued him towards the gates of Khazad-dûm. Many of the Iron-folk battle on the field here at Durin's Stone, but if ever there was a chance to slay Azog, the time has come!

'Before we set out, order as many of our kin as you can to rally to the Great Gates!

'Go! There is no time to waste, my friend!'And so, Azog was slain, crushed beneath the boots of Dáin and beheaded. A fitting fate!

'But something had troubled my friend ever since he passed beyond the gates of Khazad-dûm, and I dared not ask what it was. The battle was not yet ended, after all, nor would it be until every last Orc was banished from Azanulbizar.'Durin will not be dissuaded from reclaiming the Mountain-home. He commands the Dwarves of the Gabil'akkâ to break through the frozen wall that now blocks the gates of Gundabad.Glóin stands outside Zarkul-sulûn, watching as more dwarves arrive for the conquest of the Mountain-home.Talk to Glóin outside Zarkul-sulûnGlóin expressed his surprise and dismay at the artists and craftsmen that have answered Durin's call and come to Zarkul-sulûn. You should speak with these new arrivals and learn why they have come to such a place of war.Meet with the new arrivals outside Zarkul-sulûnStáli the Hewer stands outside Zarkul-sulûn. Why has the sculptor come to such a dangerous place in time of war?Talk to Stáli the Hewer outside Zarkul-sulûnStáli has directed you to speak to his apprentice, Eskil Bloodthumb, outside Zarkul-sulûn.Talk to Eskil Bloodthumb outside Zarkul-sulûnStáli the Hewer has ordered that Eskil Bloodthumb carry his master's luggage to the upper floor inside Zarkul-sulûn. You have decided to help Eskil in this task, so the apprentice need not carry everything by himself.Pick up some of Stáli's luggageStáli the Hewer has arrived on the upper floor of Zarkul-sulûn.Talk to Stáli the Hewer on the upper floor of Zarkul-sulûnPrince Ingór is downstairs in Zarkul-sulûn, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Prince Ingór downstairs in Zarkul-sulûn<rgb=#FF0000>You will automatically travel to Glóin during the War of Three Peaks when you accept this quest.</rgb>

'We are so near to achieving our goal, ${PLAYER}! Gundabad belongs to my people, and neither Orc nor dragon will keep it from them any longer! Should Gorgar stand before us, he will be cut down! Should Hrímil Frost-heart seal the entrance with ice, as she has done, we will break through it... and then we will take her head and ensure she can never hinder us again!

'Every day, more dwarves arrive to swell this great force, this Gabil'akkâ of mine. Speak with Glóin and assess these new arrivals, and we will set them on tasks with which the Mountain-home shall be reclaimed!'<rgb=#FF0000>You will automatically travel to Glóin during the War of Three Peaks when you accept this quest.</rgb>

'We are so near to achieving our goal, ${PLAYER}! Gundabad belongs to my people, and neither Orc nor dragon will keep it from them any longer! Should Gorgar stand before us, he will be cut down! Should Hrímil Frost-heart seal the entrance with ice, as she has done, we will break through it... and then we will take her head and ensure she can never hinder us again!

'Every day, more dwarves arrive to swell this great force, this Gabil'akkâ of mine. Speak with Glóin and assess these new arrivals, and we will set them on tasks with which the Mountain-home shall be reclaimed!''The prince is right: more dwarves arrive every day. But I daresay he has not looked closely upon these new arrivals, or he should not speak so confidently of the help they will provide to us.

'These are not warriors! Most of the dwarves arriving now are peaceable folk: artists, craftsmen, and traders. They were lured here by the promise that Gundabad will once again be ours, and aye, so shall it be, but they seem to believe the war is done and the mountain is already ripe for the taking!

'Do you see there? More dwarves arrive, and I recognize one of their number from the halls of Erebor. Stáli the Hewer is so-named for his skill at sculpting, not for severing limbs from his foes! He is no fighter. Go tell him he has come too early, and should depart.''Tell Stáli to return to Erebor. He is an artist, not a warrior, and there are still battles to be won before he will do us any good here.''Tell Stáli to return to Erebor. He is an artist, not a warrior, and there are still battles to be won before he will do us any good here.''What's that? The mountain is not yet taken? What has Prince Durin's army been up to, then? I should have thought Gundabad would be well in hand by now!

'But you will not dissuade me. I have come all this way and will not return empty-handed. The Mountain-home has been held by the Orcs for too long, and it will take a sculptor's careful hand to restore the beauty it once possessed. Imagine the great works of art that must lie within, covered by the dust of years and ignored by the dismissive Orcs! Yes, the prince will need warriors... but he will need artists too, and I have answered the call he did not think to make. He will thank me, eventually.

'For now I need my luggage brought inside the fortress. You can help my apprentice with this task. Eskil! Carry my things! On the double, lad!''I need my luggage brought inside the fortress. You can help my apprentice with this task. Eskil! Carry my things! On the double, lad!''Greetings to you. Did you fight in the war? It does seem to still be going on!'

Fastrith looks at Eskil Bloodthumb, currently puzzling how best to carry his master's luggage, and sighs.

'Sometimes I wonder why my brother Eskil agreed to become Stáli's apprentice. He does not seem to enjoy the experience very much.''Oh, you will help me carry some of my master's luggage? Thank you, friend. That will save me a trip or two up the stairs.

'When I agreed to become Stáli's apprentice, I thought there would be rather more instruction in the craft than such labour as this....'

Eskil looks around nervously.

'I am not complaining! No, not at all! I had better get going. I will find a place for the luggage upstairs in the fortress, if you truly wish to help.''My luggage needs to be brought inside the fortress. My apprentice will show you where on the upper floor to leave it.''Thank you again for agreeing to help me carry my master's luggage. I will find a place for it inside the fortress, somewhere on the upper floor.''Sometimes I wonder why my brother Eskil agreed to become Stáli's apprentice. He does not seem to enjoy the experience.''I hope you and my apprentice were careful with these! I have brought a number of pieces from Erebor, some which are quite fragile, and if any of them have suffered cracks or scratches I shall see that the prince hears about it.''It is so difficult to find good help these days, no matter where I look!''There you are, ${PLAYER}.

'I wanted to remind you that my people have a claim upon Gundabad, and I expect Prince Durin to respect this claim when the mountain is won. I want you and Durin to understand that my Zhélruka are not fighting for nothing.'As you continue to read Hermáth Stormhammer's account of the Battle of Azanulbizar, you begin the fifth and final chapter in which the Dwarves of the Haban’akkâ of Thráin must reckon with the cost of victory over Azog.King Thráin can be found at Muru Azog.

After the defeat of Azog, King Thráin appears intent on routing the remaining Orcs at Azanulbizar. You should talk to King Thráin.Talk to King Thráin at Muru AzogNud-mekhem, the east-gate, can be found to the west of Muru Azog.

Hoping to rout the last of the Orcs at Azanulbizar, King Thráin has asked you to investigate the gates of Khazad-dûm and defeat Orcs of the Ufúrzrak clan.Investigate the gates of Khazad-dûmDefeat Ufúrzrak Orcs at Muru Azog (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Ufúrzrak thrug-hai at Muru Azog (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})King Thráin can be found at Muru Azog.

You have driven back the Orcs at Muru Azog and found the remains of Azog being picked by crows. You should talk to King Thráin.Talk to King Thráin and aid in 'A Final Reckoning'Hermáth Stormhammer can be found at Amdân.

You have witnessed the end of the Battle of Azanulbizar. You should talk to Hermáth Stormhammer.Talk to Hermáth Stormhammer at Amdân'Before the pass leading to the gates of Khazad-dûm, the survivors of the confrontation against Azog gathered anew. After the slaying of Azog, they had withdrawn from the encampment, anticipating another assault from the Orcs who still dwelt within Khazad-dûm. With them, they bore away only the head of Azog and the body of Náin Orcsfoe. Indeed, Náin's mail-collar had withstood Azog's blow, but the same could not be said for his neck. It snapped, and by all accounts, Náin had died in an instant.

'Little comfort this was to Dáin, who had remained silent ever since he ventured beyond the Great Gates. Even so, the head of Azog was the prize King Thráin had sought ever since Nár brought word of Thrór's death to the exiles at Zudrugund. And it had been won!

'Left nearly halt after Urro wounded him at Durin's Stone, King Thráin knelt among his guard, but his eyes would not be turned from Khazad-dûm.''And so, the pyres burned away and so the ashen dale of Azanulbizar was abandoned. Although none beheld them at the battle's end, we later learned the Elves of the Golden Wood had borne witness to our deeds, spying the smoke from afar as it rose into the sky above the dale. As King Thráin had named them at Durin's Stone, the Marasrân—the Burned Dwarves came to occupy a place of great honour and reverence among Durin's Folk. Long after, among each of the Seven Houses, all a Dwarf needed to say about his ancestor was 'he was Marasrûn' and it would elicit grave nods and murmurs of respect.

'In time, the other Houses departed back to their homes, bearing the burdens of the arms and amour collected from their dead. As for the Iron-folk, we too departed, making the long journey back to Járnfast. Dáin became the Lord of Iron Hills in all but title, for Grór had become lost in his sorrows, surviving his father, brothers, and now his son alike with not one among them whose bones he could honour.

'Last of all, King Thráin and the exiles departed. They were few now and weary, having lost much and won little in the war, but south they went along the Great-way, until at last they returned to Zudrugund.

'The Sixth War of the Dwarves and Orcs was ended—the cost too great, the prize too small to hold.''Azog is slain, and the Ironfoot has taken his head. The Orcs should know the battle has turned against them!

'And yet, some of Azog's own clan still cling to their meagre holdings outside the Great Gates. It cannot stand!

'Slay these... Ufúrzrak, and drive the rest of his ilk back beyond the gates of Khazad-dûm! Azanulbizar will be ours!'Scattered across Elderslade, the sun-bleached bones of great worms are a harsh reminder of the dragons that once prowled these mountains. What secrets might these skeletons hold?Dragon skeletons can be found in Malkorlash, in northern Elderslade.

Lore-master Rúni has asked you to get samples of dragon bones.Gather a sample of dragon clawGather a sample of dragon hornGather a sample of dragon ribGather a sample of dragon spineGather a sample of dragon toothLore-master Rúni is at Zudramdân in Elderslade.

You should bring the dragon samples to Lore-master Rúni.Return to Rúni'Ah, ${PLAYER}! I see you made it all this way in one piece. I could not resist the opportunity to come to Elderslade, a landscape full of cold-drakes and even better – dragon bones!

'Though we dwarves have a long history with the beasts, there is still much that we don’t know. While you are out on the field, it would really help my studies if you could gather samples from the various remains that are scattered about.''Yes, yes. These will do quite well! I shall begin to study them immediately!'There is always work to be done at the Beorninghús.Wood that needs to be chopped can be found at the Beorninghús.

You should chop some wood for Grimbeorn.Chop wood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You find the wood that Grimbeorn needs chopped. You had better get to it!Through your many forays into the Halls of Black Lore, you have seen much, and recalled many past adventures through the magic of the Dark Archivist.Through your many forays into the Halls of Black Lore, you have seen much, and recalled many past adventures through the magic of the Dark Archivist.Those of Mordor seek to remain in control of the dead city of Minas Morgul.Reclaim the courtyards in Minas Morgul.Complete The Court of SongComplete The Silver CourtComplete The Joyous CourtComplete The Guarded CourtCave-claws have long inhabited the area known as Scraper's Clough, scraping their horns upon the sides of that rocky crag. They pose a deadly threat to the unwary unfortunates who might enter the Clough.Cave-claws have long inhabited the area known as Scraper's Clough, scraping their horns upon the sides of that rocky crag. You should put an end to the danger they pose.Defeat cave-claws in Scraper's CloughCave-claws have long inhabited the area known as Scraper's Clough, scraping their horns upon the sides of that rocky crag. They pose a deadly threat to the unwary unfortunates who might enter the Clough. You should put an end to the danger they pose.Defeat cave-claws in Scraper's Clough (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Completed.CompletedCave-claws have long inhabited the area known as Scraper's Clough, scraping their horns upon the sides of that rocky crag. They pose a deadly threat to the unwary unfortunates who might enter the Clough. You should put an end to the danger they pose.Thanks to your efforts, Scraper's Clough is clear of the cave-claw threat. The creatures will not remain away forever, but for now what few folk find themselves adventuring in that place need not fear the sharp claws or stabbing horns of those creatures.Deep in the darkness of Thuringwath lies Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation. There, within that great fane where the Nazgûl once worshipped and made sacrifice to Sauron, one of the Dark Lord's most mysterious servants prepares Dhak Thrakrut, the Rite of Returning.Deep in the darkness of Thuringwath lies Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation. There, within that great fane where the Nazgûl once worshipped and made sacrifice to Sauron, one of the Dark Lord's most mysterious servants prepares Dhak Thrakrut, the Rite of Returning.Complete Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation -- Tier 1Complete Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation -- Tier 2Complete Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation -- Tier 3Complete Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation: Slayer of Acolytes of LamentationComplete Bâr Nírnaeth, the Houses of Lamentation: Slayer of the UnhousedThe rivers of Elderslade are polluted, fouling the sparkling waters of the Dvergvát.Piles of Orc-filth can be found along river-beds in Elderslade.

Mother Amma has tasked you with cleansing the rivers that flow into the Dvergvát.Burn piles of Orc-filth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mother Amma is in Annâk-khurfu, in eastern Elderslade.

You should talk to Mother Amma.Talk to Mother Amma'Water is essential, and whoever controls the waterways will have an advantage in this war. Our forces also need clean water to drink, but the rivers have been fouled by filth that the Orcs have strewn about. Their waters flow into the Dvergvát, whose sapphire waters are famed among the dwarves.

'Find the piles the Orcs have left behind and burn them. The Dvergvát will return to its former glory and our troops may partake of its waters without harm.''Excellently done! A grisly task, but necessary.'Malkorlash is crawling with enemies who need to be disposed.Malkorlash is in northern Elderslade.

You should defeat enemies at the hobgoblin camp, Malkorlash.Defeat hobgoblins in Malkorlash (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Borghild is north-east of Malkorlash in eastern Elderslade.

Talk to Borghild.Talk to Borghild'Gunvald's missive sends word that reinforcements are on the way. I must move on to my next scouting location, but if you are as resourceful as Gunvald says, you could be of great help.

'At the camp up the road there is a swarming nest of hobgoblins. Yes, that seems to be the source of that wretched stench.

'The hobgoblins are squatting in a gap between this mountain's two peaks. From there, they control the nearby river and the pass. Anything you can do to put a dent in their ranks would benefit our forces immensely. Take care when you move into their camp, friend, for they are a deadly foe. I shall circle 'round and meet with you when you reach the other side.''Well done, friend. Gunvald was right about your prowess. I suspect many will be the songs that sing of your glory when this is all over.'Zarkul-sulûn is crawling with enemies who need to be removed.Zarkul-sulûn can be found in southern Elderslade.

