You should talk to Léothred at Framsburg.Talk to Léothred at Framsburg'The Ai-thúda were badly hurt by Scatha’s onslaught, and Fram knew another attack might be the end for his people.

'Come, let us search the ruins of Framsburg once more and learn what we can about Fram.''Frumgar’s mood grew blacker and blacker. He had led his people out of danger and into death, it seemed. Now it looked as it might have cost him the son he had hoped to spare. Fasthálla remained a broken keep, haunt of a fading people. Hope was not lost, however.'Léothred and Fróthi have finally uncovered the truth of how Fram died and who killed him.Fróthi can be found next to Wyrmsgráf.

You should talk to Fróthi.Talk to FróthiLéothred can be found next to Wyrmsgráf.

You should talk to Léothred.Talk to LéothredFróthi can be found next to Wyrmsgráf.

You should talk to Fróthi.Talk to Fróthi'Fróthi and I have discussed everything that we have learned from our journey together. Yes, I believe that the map on Scatha's tooth was indeed carved by Fram. The visions you had help to answer the questions for which we have come searching. The truth that Fróthi and I agree upon is this:

'In Fram's dealings with Thráin, he very much wanted to share the treasures and location of Scatha's hoard. The blood pact Fram made with Karazgar certainly prevented this. Remember, ${PLAYER}, this is the same Fram who befriended the Holbytlan! The Holbytlan are a cautious people and would only befriend those with kindness in them! Yes, something about the blood pact clouded Fram's judgement and made him grow ever suspicious towards the dwarves. We believe, in a moment of clarity, Fram carved the route to Scatha's hoard on one of the Scatha's teeth.''Unavoidably, the Ai-thúda blamed the dwarves, though King Thráin disavowed the deed and mourned his one-time friend. He made no more claim on Scatha's hoard, much of which remained lost, for Fram alone knew the exact whereabouts of the Worm's lair. The bond between the Ai-thúda and the Longbeards was not mended until this most recent war, the War of the Ring.''Fram the Foremost never got the chance to make proper amends with Thráin. He set forth from his hall on a journey to Scatha's hoard, but never returned. When his sons went forth to seek him, they found him slain, pierced by dwarf-arrows and surrounded by the bodies of several dwarf warriors.''Yes, Karazgar knew that something was amiss when word reached him that Scatha's teeth, a quite offensive gift if I do say, had been given to dwarves. We can agree that Fram knew enough about the dwarves' disdain of dragons to know that a necklace of dragon's teeth would not be sufficient for Thráin. Scatha's hoard was entirely stolen dwarf riches after all! At this moment, Fram was no longer useful to Karazgar and so, in Karazgar's mind, Fram had to die.'Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, wishes to learn more about the history of his people on the isle of Avabárg.Orcs can be found at Avabárg.

You should defeat Orcs at Avabárg.Defeat Orcs at Avabárg (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Avabárg can be found in Woodsedge in the Vales of Anduin.

You should learn the history of the Rohirrim with Léothred.Learn the history of the Rohirrim with LéothredAvabárg can be found in Woodsedge in the Vales of Anduin.

You should learn the history of the Rohirrim with Léothred.Continue learning the history of the Rohirrim with LéothredLéothred can be found at Avabárg.

You should talk to Léothred.Talk to Léothred at Avabárg'To see these Orcs upon Avabárg.... No, this is a great injustice to the legacy of the Rohirrim. We must show these foul marauders that Avabárg is no home for Orcs!''Go forth and defeat those Orcs, just as Frumgar did so many years ago.''Frumgar named his son Fram, and swore he would not know the same sorrow when he was grown. In my research, I learned that, six years after Fram's birth, Angmar fell. The kingdom's demise was sudden, coming only a year after the Witch-king’s final victory over Arthedain and the fall of Fornost. The chronicles of this time say an army of Elves and Men of Gondor, having come too late to save the northern Dúnedain, put the Angmarim to the sword and sent the Witch-king flying back to Mordor.

'Why do I relate this story to you? Angmar's fall had an important connexion with my people. At this time, Gundabad no longer had the Iron Kingdom at its back, and the moors and glens of the Welling-folds no longer seemed so daunting as they had to Marhwini a hundred years before. Fram saw this as an opportunity to lead his people to great things.'Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, wishes to learn more about Fram's history and the decisions that he made in life.Léothred can be found at Framsburg.

