You should talk to Hersegg.Talk to Hersegg'And that is all there is to tell of the doom of Thrór, ${PLAYER}.''...but whatever the case he summoned Thráin and gave to him what heirlooms of their house remained – chief among them his Ring, last of the Seven that had been given to the Dwarf-kings.

'He said to Thráin, "Riches may yet spring anew from this bauble, my son. Or perhaps not. Yet one cannot breed gold without gold." When Thráin asked if his intent was to return to Erebor, Thrór remained silent. After a long moment, he said, "These bones are too old. I will not be avenged on Smaug - that fate, perchance, shall be yours, or your sons’. I have known too much of loss and of Men’s derision. I go to seek better fortunes, if they may yet be found."

'He did not say where he meant to go, and Thráin did not ask, but merely wished him fortune in his quest.''Thrór had bidden Nár to stay and await his return, but it was not merely the word of his king that kept him from daring to enter Moria. In truth, and to his shame, Nár was afraid.

'For many days Nár hid among the trees outside the gates, lonely and troubled. When he dared to draw nearer to the gate and peer into the gloom, neither sound nor the faintest flicker of light emerged from within.

'More troublingly still, neither had Thrór.''Although both Thrór and Nár saw many signs of Orcs in the dale, they were old and overtrodden. Outside Khazad-dûm, all was still and silent. Before them, the East-gate yawned open, as if to beckon the two of them forth.

'But the journey had been long, and Nár thought better of seeking shelter within Moria.''Hidden beneath the eaves of trees and stones at the foot of Zirakzigil, Nár built a fire and prepared what meagre meal he could that might befit a king and one road-weary traveller.

'When the supper was ended, Thrór stood at once. As if in answer, Nár knelt before the old king and helped him to fasten a shield to his arm.''And waited... until he thought he could bear it no longer.

'Despite every doubt, though, his resolve did not falter. Even if he were fated to live the rest of his days huddled beside a fire at Azanulbizar, Nár would not disobey his king — it was his duty to await Thrór's return.''At first, Nár again made for the Redhorn Pass, but Orcs beyond counting had gathered there and watched him with their red eyes. And so, Nár fled back the way he had come. As he passed by the gates of Khazad-dûm again, more great Orcs, including some larger than any he had heard of before, had surrounded Thrór’s corpse and were hacking it to pieces. Into that hateful frenzy, a great mass of crows descended, and to them, the Orcs began to fling the scraps of the Dwarf-king. Nár dared not look again.

'Tear-blind, Nár ran on along the banks of Kheled-zâram, fleeing the Orcs and wolves that now hunted him in cruel sport. When at last he came to the ruins of Amdân, he found them barred, and black arrows hounded his every step. Desperate to escape his own doom, however, Nár thought of Kibil-nâla and leapt down the paths alongside the stream. By some stroke of fortune, Nár had won his escape, but behind him the laughter of Azog and the Orcs rang in his ears.

'For many months, Nár wandered in the wilderness, wracked with sorrow, the sight of his King’s defilement branded in his heart. At long last, he found his way north to the High Pass across the Misty Mountains, then made his way back to Thrór’s Coomb. By the time he entered Zudrugund the guards scarcely recognized him, so haggard and wild he had grown.

'Nár was never well in his mind again.''When Nár awoke, his clothing was cold and heavy with rain. Nár's rest had ended abruptly, and in a daze, he tried to regain his bearings.

'Above him the sky loomed dark, its hue a sickly purple, and he could not tell if it was day or night. Aside from the sound of trickling rainfall, all was still and silent.

'Alas, Nár would have little time to ponder his situation.''Nár had known the truth at once, but wished not to believe it. It was Thrór.

'As Nár crept closer, there came a chittering laughter from beyond the darkness of the East-gate.''Long after the realm of Grárik had fallen to the cold-drake Vethúg, and twenty years after the Lonely Mountain fell to Smaug, King Thrór and his folk came to dwell in a coomb near the wilds of Dunland. From the old stronghold of Zudrugund, Thrór ruled what remained of the two kingdoms he had fled in his life. His son was Thráin, a formidable warrior; his grandsons Thorin and Frerin, and granddaughter Dís, were yet young. His people were few, poor, and hungry, and they knew little joy since the loss of their homes in the North.

'Dotage and gloom took their toll on King Thrór, and he thought long of regaining the splendour of his fathers’ halls. But it was not in the halls of Erebor where Smaug now slumbered upon the heirlooms of his people, nor the frozen wastes in the north that called to him: it was Khazad-dûm itself! It had been more than eight hundred years since doom befell it - since the Elves named it Moria, but it mattered not. Thrór determined that he would reclaim it, and in so doing, restore the glory of his kingdom and of Durin's Folk. What true reason had he, none know for certain. 

'Perhaps he had gone mad, or perhaps not....'Long ago and high above Sundergrót, the Greymaul Rebellion came to an end. It is now a place of great import for Durin's Folk, and Longbeards have been known to visit the high crags for contemplation and reflection.The climb to High Sundergrót has left Glóin quite exhausted, and he wishes to speak with you before you proceed.Talk to Glóin among the crags of High SundergrótGlóin suggested that you continue your northward climb among the crags of High Sundergrót.Continue climbing northward among the crags of High SundergrótGlóin suggested that you continue your northward climb among the crags of High Sundergrót.Search the drift of snow to the north, where a glint of metal has caught your eyeYour companions have rested from their climb and now look around the snowy heights of High Sundergrót.Wait with your companions while they discuss your find and the history of High SundergrótPrince Durin has come to High Sundergrót and waits to speak with you.Talk to Durin at High SundergrótDurin has asked you to continue your climb to the upper levels of High Sundergrót, among the ruins to the north-east. A sloping pillar lies across the broken stairs that will help you gain access to the upper levels.Continue north-east and climb the fallen pillar lying over the stairs to reach the upper level of the ruins in High SundergrótHill-beasts and bats haunt the upper reaches of the ruins perched atop High Sundergrót.

You should clear the path of these dangers to make the way safe for Durin and your companions.A broken dwarf-blade catches your eye by the overlook on the upper level of the ruins perched atop High Sundergrót.Examine the broken dwarf-blade lying near the overlook on the upper levelDurin and your companions have safely arrived on the upper level of the ruins perched atop High Sundergrót.Listen to the words of Durin by the overlook in High SundergrótDurin and your companions have safely arrived on the upper level of the ruins perched atop High Sundergrót.Talk to Durin by the overlook in the ruins atop High SundergrótDurin is ready to turn once more for the north, and to war.Accompany Durin on the northward road to war'To Elderslade, then! May the journey be quick, and the road uneventful. Victory lies at the end of it, at the very gates of Gundabad!''Just a... just a moment, my friend. The climb to this high place has quite exhausted me, and we are not yet at the top. I must rest for a moment.''The climb to this high place has quite exhausted me, and we are not yet at the top. I must rest for a moment. You go on ahead, and keep your eyes open.''You and your travelling companions have done well, ${PLAYER}! I received Glóin's message at Skarháld not long after it was sent, for his messenger-ravens are renowned for their speed, and the bird he selected was no exception. His tidings of a new Lord of Gundabad fill me not with fear but with anticipation, for no Orc will stand between me and my chosen path, no matter his lineage. I had the message copied and sent on to the Lonely Mountain, the Iron Hills, and other dwarf-holdings in lands both near and far. My armies gather to march on Gundabad, and all will be forewarned with the tidings uncovered on your scouting expedition.

'I am eager to turn my gaze upon the Mountain-home, and sought no delay, but I wanted to first make the journey here to Sundergrót. It was here that the very first Durin to bear that name put an end to the usurper Mótsog, solidifying his power and his claim to lead the dwarves with that victory. By making this journey I hoped to become closer to my ancient forebear, that I may draw strength for the battles to come from the memory of he whose name I bear.

'I confess that I did not expect so much climbing.''The path continues to the north-east. I ask you to keep climbing, my friend, and to clear the way for the rest of us.''This is a matter of some delicacy, my friend, and I urge you to exercise discretion. I will not belabour the point, for I trust you will use such prudence as this subject requires.

'I tasked you with finding and defeating the raiding-party from Gundabad that endangered the folk living among the Wells of Langflood, and from Glóin's message-raven I know that you did so successfully. But there is one matter of concern that was not made clear to me in the message.

'I ask you this, then: were Imák and Venko effective on this errand? Did you have any reason to question their loyalties? Did they strike you as good-hearted allies of the Longbeards, or did you detect veins of disloyalty or deception running beneath their surface appearances?

'I do not know if you are familiar with the Greymaul Rebellion, but all my people know the lesson of High Sundergrót: even our closest friends can be tempted into treachery, as was Mótsog the Traitor. He was once the dearest friend of Durin, Father of the Dwarves, and still he raised the Greymaul army in rebellion, claiming a lordship that was not his. Durin the First repaid that treachery with death, though Mótsog was once his closest friend. I would not say this Zhélruka or this Stout-axe are as dear to us as that, ${PLAYER}!'

You assure Durin that you have few complaints about the trustworthiness or effectiveness of your companions.

'Then I do not either. Still, I advise you to keep an eye upon the Zhélruka especially, for his people believe they have a claim upon Gundabad, and I do not want to win it back from the Orcs only to lose it to these dwarves from the east!'Gori Stonesetter needs protection so that he might see for himself the ruins of the ancient buildings and bridges his ancestors helped build.Gori waits for you by the side of the River Kibil-nâla.Talk to GoriGori continues his way through the ruins, too distracted to notice any imminent danger.Talk to Gori on the bridgeGori has paused to speak with you before the next set of stairs.Talk to Gori by the plaqueGori is catching his breath after the battle and motions you over to talk with him.Talk to Gori'I do believe I could spend decades just marvelling at the details here. But come, let us return to Amdân before more of the enemy comes looking for their friends.''See those pillars there? An ancestor of mine built them ages past! She left detailed journals of her time here. Sadly, the pages of the journal are as crumbled as the ruins, but I hope to learn more here. The stone of this valley has stood for many thousands of years.''Khur Azan - the Dim-crossing. Built over two thousand years ago, and now here we are! A testament to our mastery over the valley. Look at these arches, and the detailing. Our ancestors were comfortable enough and had time enough to build grandly. This is no simple bridge over the Kibil-nâla, but a grand testament to the power and abilities of the builders.''Fundin's Steps. My ancestor spent much time working on this staircase. She wrote that she worked under the great stoneworker, King Fundin II. Landslides left the already ancient paths to Atrad-zarakh too perilous to safely traverse. They carved the stairs from the very bedrock itself!''My ancestor was close with Fundin’s widow, Vigdis. She wrote that the queen’s beard turned white when news of the king’s death reached her. Perhaps it was from grief, perhaps it was from having to take over the duties of ruling until their son, Kalin VI the Young, came of age in 501.'A celebration is in order after the fall of Lumrog and Gúrbarash's cowardly flight.Approach the gathering in the woods.Approach the gathering in the woodsLet events transpire.Let events transpireTalk to Runerik.Talk to RunerikRun wild!Run wild!<rgb=#FF0000>Roar</rgb> to the wilds!'I have heard from Skarding of your deeds in Misthallow. You have my thanks.

'Now, let us celebrate!'Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, wishes to learn more about the great worm, Scatha, whose history weaves closely with that of the great warrior, Fram.Talk to Léothred.Talk to LéothredListen to Léothred's tale.Listen to Léothred's tale<rgb=#FF0000>Fram must survive this tale</rgb>'Frumgar's arm had been horribly gnawed by Scatha's sharp teeth. While Fram saved his father's life, Frumgar would never fully recover from that day.''Frumgar faced the dragon among his slaughtered household, Wrothbláda in hand...'For Léothred and Fróthi, the truth of how Fram slayed Scatha is finally revealed.Approach the vision of Karazgar and Fram.Approach the vision of Karazgar and FramExperience the vision.Experience the vision<rgb=#FF0000>Fram must survive this tale</rgb><rgb=#FF0000>Karazgar must survive this tale</rgb>You can hear Karazgar's thoughts.

'Seek your fame and riches, petty Fram. You are now a servant of Karazgar, whether you know it or not.'Within the Dead City of Minas Morgul, a locked chamber of the Witch-king has opened.Enter further into Roz Dagalur, the Fiend-houseEnter further into Roz Dagalur, the Fiend-houseDefeat the gaunt-men.Defeat BortharakDefeat ManargothDefeat GámgrishDefeat ÚghurnRead the notes on the altar.Read the notes on the altarYou inspect the book. It seems to be a journal, written in Black Speech. Each page lists the names of the four gaunt-men you have bested, as well as various indecipherable notes. The first four pages also reference Ivar, Thadúr, Ferndúr, and Drugoth, four of the gaunt-lords. There is a fifth and final page of writing that lists the name, Gortheron. No notes have been taken on this page. You believe the Witch-king may have been attempting to create a new set of gaunt-lords to command and locked it away here. Luckily his work seems to have gone unfinished. It is good that you were able to defeat these gaunt-men before the notes were discovered by one of Sauron's former servants.Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, wishes to learn more of the political tensions that arose during Fram's reign over the Ai-thúda.Talk to Léothred.Talk to LéothredListen to Léothred's tale.Listen to Léothred's tale'What began in celebration might have ended in a declaration of war, had it not been for Wandalb who was able to steer the Men and dwarves back from the brink of battle.''All the guests arrived, excited to meet the one who had slain Scatha, the Worm. The feast began with lively song and joyous laughter....'Bori is eager to follow Durin to the threshold of Gundabad, where he hopes to observe the prince's defeat of Gorgar the Ruthless and acquisition of the title 'Lord of Gundabad.'You have crossed the lake with Bori, and now he waits to speak with you before you climb to the threshold of Gundabad to meet up with Durin's fighting force.Talk to Bori on the path leading to the threshold of GundabadThe gate to the threshold of Gundabad lies ahead of you, where Gorgar has devised an arena to challenge Prince Durin and his allies.

Bori needs your help to push open the gate that lies between you and Durin's fighting force.Push open the gate to Gorgar's arenaPrince Durin and his allies stand bravely in the arena of Gorgar the Ruthless on the very threshold of Gundabad.Enter Gorgar's arena and join Durin and his alliesPrince Durin and his allies stand bravely in the arena of Gorgar the Ruthless on the very threshold of Gundabad.Remain on your guard!'We'll have to fight our way up the slope and to the threshold of Gundabad if we do not wish to miss Prince Durin's victory. Let's go!'After the horn-call of Grór sounded, Náin and the Dwarves of the Iron Hills charged forth to challenge Azog and put an end to the battle.Dáin can be found just outside Muru Azog at Azanulbizar.

After overcoming Azog's forces at Durin's Stone, Náin Orcsfoe and the Iron-folk have begun their assault on the gates of Khazad-dûm. You should talk to Dáin.Talk to DáinDáin can be found at Muru Azog.

You have joined in the assault on the gates of Khazad-dûm. You should follow Dáin and aid the soldiers of the Haban’akkâ.Follow Dáin and join in the assault on the gates of Khazad-dûmNáin Orcsfoe and the Iron-folk have broken through Azog's defences at Muru Azog.

You should aid Náin and the Iron-folk as they approach the gates of Khazad-dûm.Witness as Náin confronts Bolg outside Khazad-dûmNáin can be found outside the gates of Khazad-dûm at Muru Azog.

Náin Orcsfoe has driven off the coward Bolg, leaving Azog to face the Haban’akkâ. You should talk to Náin.Talk to NáinAzog can be found outside the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

In answer to Náin's taunts, Azog has come out of the shadows of Khazad-dûm. You should confront Azog with Náin and the Iron-folk.Confront AzogNáin and Azog can be found outside the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

Azog has answered Náin's challenge. You should witness the battle between Náin and Azog.Witness Náin as he battles against AzogAzog's bodyguards can be found outside the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

After slaying Náin, Azog has set his bodyguards upon the Iron-folk. You should defeat Azog's bodyguards.Defeat Azog's bodyguardsAzog can be found outside the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

After slaying Náin, Azog hopes to escape the wrath of the Iron-folk. You should aid Dáin and defeat Azog.Defeat AzogDáin can be found outside the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

Dáin has slain Azog. You should talk to Dáin.Talk to DáinDáin can be found at the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

After slaying Azog, Dáin has chosen to enter Khazad-dûm. You should watch as Dáin enters Khazad-dûm.Witness as Dáin enters Khazad-dûmDáin has walked into Khazad-dûm.

You should await Dáin's return.Wait for DáinDáin can be found outside the East-gate of Khazad-dûm.

After entering Khazad-dûm, Dáin has returned deeply troubled. You should talk to Dáin.Talk to DáinDáin remains silent.'Here we are at last!

'Owing to his wounds, King Thráin and his guard were forced to remain behind at Durin's Stone, but his son, Thorin, has come to aid us. In so short a time, he has made a name for himself by wielding that oak branch—the Oakenshield, they call him. Hah!

'Ever since Bolg fled our arrival at Durin's Stone, my father, Náin, has pursued him... now all the way to the gates of Khazad-dûm! When we catch him, he will fare no better than his other brothers... or his father for that matter!

'It's time, Hermáth. Let us join in the final attack!''Hah! See what the line of Azog has been reduced to!'

'I'm glad to see you here at the end of this war, Hermáth. I trust you and my son have slain your fair share of Orcs!'

Náin glances into the shadows beyond the gates of Khazad-dûm, gripping his mattock tightly.

'We have waited long enough for our vengeance. Let us see if this coward has found his spine!''Thrór is avenged.'

Dain's face is fraught with sorrow as he glances to where his father lays slain outside Khazad-dûm.

'Alas, there is one more thing to be done. Wait here, my friend.'As you approach Dáin, he appears to hardly notice you.

After a moment, he opens his mouth as if to say something, but remains silent.For Léothred and Fróthi, the truth of how Fram slayed Scatha begins to take form.Approach the vision of Fram.Approach the vision of FramExperience the vision.Experience the visionYou can hear the thoughts of Karazgar.

'Oh the pieces, they fall right into place. I have Fram right where I want him.'Thuringwath, the Valley of Secret Shadow, has remained hidden among the slopes of Ephel Dúath since the Second Age of the world.Mithrandir can be found in Falch Gurth.Talk to MithrandirYou and Mithrandir are walking through Falch Gurth.Follow MithrandirYou are following Mithrandir.Allow Mithrandir to banish the shadows in Falch GurthYou should talk to Mithrandir.Talk to MithrandirYou are following Mithrandir.Follow MithrandirYou are following Mithrandir.Allow Mithrandir to banish the shadows in Falch GurthYou should talk to Mithrandir.Talk to MithrandirYou should follow Mithrandir.Follow MithrandirYou are following Mithrandir.Allow Mithrandir to banish the shadows of ThuringwathYou should talk to Mithrandir.Talk to MithrandirMithrandir falls silent.'Walk with me, ${PLAYER}.''I have not seen the like of this enchantment before in Middle-earth. Perhaps you might wish that I not say so... but I find myself uneasy, ${PLAYER}.

'Let us continue, but not in haste.''It... should not be far now to the end of the pass.'

Mithrandir appears to be tiring in a way you have never seen.

'Come, ${PLAYER}.''It... cannot be.

'I thought I had seen the last of the Enemy's great works brought to ruin, but I see now I was mistaken.'

Mithrandir leans on his staff, seemingly barely able to support himself. You try to aid him.

'No! No... there is no time for rest, ${PLAYER}.'The goblins of Sundergrót have set up a secret meeting within the old dwarf ruins.Approach the dwarves.Approach the dwarvesLet events transpire.Let events transpireDefeat Rákhrum and his forces.Defeat Rákhrum and his forces<rgb=#FF0000>Agátur must survive</rgb><rgb=#FF0000>Náth must survive</rgb><rgb=#FF0000>Rúzat must survive</rgb>'Rákhrum is of no concern. I say leave the goblin to sulk in the hills.

'Come, let us leave Sundergrót.'Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, seeks to learn more of his people history in Misthallow.Talk to Léothred.Talk to LéothredListen to Léothred's tale.Listen to Léothred's taleTalk to Léothred.Talk to LéothredListen to Léothred's tale.Listen to Léothred's tale'And that is the end of Eorl's tale.''Eorl, with bow in hand, ran into the woods....''Where before, the lords of the Éothéod, even mighty Léod, had sought to break the mearas, Eorl had treated his father’s animal as a friend and it had met him in kind.'For those reclaiming Minas Ithil from the grasp of darkness, a trial of madness has begun.Find the artifact.Find the artifactDestroy the Orb of Madness.Destroy the Orb of MadnessOvercome the Trial of Madness.Overcome the Trial of Madness'${PLAYER}, are you alight?! What is wrong?!'For those reclaiming Minas Ithil from the grasp of darkness, a trial of wrath has begun.Defeat the Ghâsh-hai.Defeat the Ghâsh-haiDestroy the Orb of Wrath.Destroy of the Orb of WrathOvercome the Trial of Wrath.Overcome the Trial of WrathRunerik Knotbeard fears an assault upon Limlók may be imminent.Talk to Vaskorn.Talk to VaskornFind and defeat Khilbakh and the Warg raiding party.Find and defeat KhilbakhDefeat the raiding party<rgb=#FF0000>Vaskorn must survive</rgb>'We are victorious and Limlók remains safe, for now.''Quickly now, follow me. We attack!'Neither Longbeards, Zhélruka, nor Stout-axes, a company of clanless dwarves known only as the Want-kin have made camp in Elderslade.Nagli can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

You should talk to Nagli.Talk to NagliNagli can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

You should follow Nagli.Follow NagliA suitable log for sitting can be found beside Nagli at Khadâsh-gâra.

Nagli has asked you to join him and the other Want-kin at the camp-fire at Khadâsh-gâra.The Want-kin are preparing to have supper.Listen to Nagli and the Want-kinNagli set your supper beside you at Khadâsh-gâra.

The Want-kin have begun their meal. You should share a supper with them.Share a hearty supper with the Want-kin at Khadâsh-gâraNagli can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

Nagli has asked to speak with you. You should talk to Nagli.Talk to NagliFirewood can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

Nagli has asked you to fetch some firewood to stoke the fire at Khadâsh-gâra. You should collect the firewood.Collect firewood to stoke the fire at Khadâsh-gâraNagli can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

You have brought more firewood to the fire at Khadâsh-gâra as Nagli asked. You should talk to Nagli.Talk to NagliNagli can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

Nagli has asked to hear more tales of your adventures before coming to Elderslade. You should tell Nagli and the Want-kin of your adventures in Middle-earth.

<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You will need to select Nagli before emoting to advance this quest.</rgb>Tell Nagli and the Want-kin the <rgb=#00ff00>story</rgb> of your adventures before coming to EldersladeNagli and Stórath are talking about the tales you told.Listen to Nagli and StórathNagli can be found at Khadâsh-gâra.

After Stórath expressed his displeasure in hosting you, Nagli and the other Want-kin defended your presence at Khadâsh-gâra. You should talk to Nagli.Talk to Nagli'It has been a long day, ${PLAYER}. Let us take our rest!''Thank you for staying for the night, ${PLAYER}. You will not regret it!

'We've prepared a fine supper, and it would be our honour to share it with you.

'I've prepared a place for you at the fire. Come!''Ah! While you are standing, could you fetch some more wood for the fire?

'It's just over there. But do try to be quiet, Lapis doesn't like to be startled in her sleep!''Ah, that warms my bones! Thank you for fetching that firewood, ${PLAYER}.

'It is true enough that you've come to aid Durin in his quest, but what else brought you here to the Slade? Where have your past adventures led you?

'I know my companions and I are eager to hear more about you. What do you say?''Do not let Stórath's words bother you, ${PLAYER}. I think perhaps the ale has gone to his head!

'I know the others and I were glad to hear your tales of adventure. I have a feeling we've not even heard the half of it, but that's enough for one night. It was a fine evening, and I am glad to have shared it with you.

'It is late. Your adventures with Durin can wait until morning, I think.

'Come, I've prepared a bedroll for you.'Durin has commanded that the assault on Gundabad begin by seizing a foothold further ahead in the foothills of Elderslade: the ruin of Zarkul-sulûn will become the base of operations for the Gabil'akkâ!Prince Durin has commanded that the Gabil'akkâ seize the ruin of Zarkul-sulûn from the Orcs.Talk to Durin during the assault on Zarkul-sulûn'All around me I see only Longbeards! Where are the Zhélruka? Where is Ingór?''We need to seize Zarkul-sulûn if we are to push through Elderslade toward Gundabad! The Zhélruka will lead the way behind their prince, and my Longbeards will follow them!

'We have Orcs to slay, ${PLAYER}! Stay close to me!'Determined to break a bloody stalemate, Agnar Brokentooth has devised a plan to infiltrate the Orkish trench-tunnel of Ushkútoz.Agnar Brokentooth can be found in Ushkútoz.

Agnar Brokentooth has led you to Ushkútoz to aid in sabotaging the Orcs' trench-tunnel and the defeat of Kraznákh, a lieutenant of Gorgar the Ruthless.Talk to Agnar BrokentoothAgnar Brokentooth is leading the attack on Ushkútoz.

You should follow Agnar Brokentooth and defeat the Orcs inhabiting Ushkútoz as you search for Kraznákh.Fight through Ushkútoz and find KraznákhThe central chamber can be found deep within Ushkútoz.

You have found no sign of Kraznákh, but the central chamber of Ushkútoz is filled with Orcs. You should defeat them before continuing your search for Kraznákh.Clear the central chamber of UshkútozAgnar Brokentooth can be found in Ushkútoz.

You have defeated the Orcs in the central chamber of Ushkútoz, but you have found no sign of Kraznákh. You should talk to Agnar.Talk to Agnar BrokentoothAgnar Brokentooth can be found in Ushkútoz.

Agnar fears Kraznákh has lured you into an ambush. You should remain alert.Remain alert!Kraznákh can be found in Ushkútoz.

You have been trapped in the central chamber of Ushkútoz by Lieutenant Kraznákh and his Orcs. You should confront Kraznákh.Confront KraznákhKraznákh's Orcs can be found in Ushkútoz.

After trapping you in the central chamber of Ushkútoz, Kraznákh has set his Orcs upon you. You must defeat them.Defeat Kraznákh's Orcs and survive the ambushKraznákh can be found in Ushkútoz.

After you and Agnar defeated his retinue, Kraznákh has entered the fray in a fury. You must defeat Lieutenant Kraznákh.Defeat Lieutenant KraznákhAgnar Brokentooth can be found in Ushkútoz.

With the sudden aid of Lági Helmbiter and Reithvald Red-ember, you and Agnar Brokentooth have defeated Lieutenant Kraznákh. You should talk to Agnar Brokentooth.Talk to Agnar Brokentooth'Let's get out of here!''It is time, ${PLAYER}.

'I hear Orcs ahead. It will not be long before they discover us here. I hope that Lági and Reithvald have reached the western entrance without incident or our mission may well be over before it begins!

'Let us fight our way through Ushkútoz and put an end to Kraznákh!''Bah!

'All of our reports led us to believe Kraznákh would be here! I do not like this, ${PLAYER}.

'I need a moment to think. Keep your wits about you!'Lieutenant Kraznákh eyes you with disdain.'You were a great help in battle, ${PLAYER}... but if it's all the same, I am glad my old companions came to our aid when they did!

'Kraznákh is dead, and his little trap did half the work of destroying Ushkútoz for us.

'We've... done it.'With Atrad-zarakh finally breached, it is up to you and Mekhnet to stop Thraunákh and his sapper-bombs.Before delving deeper into Atrad-zarakh, you should talk to Mekhnet.Talk to MekhnetYou should help defend Mekhnet.Defend MekhnetYou have found Thraunákh, though Mekhnet wishes to speak with you before facing him.Talk to MekhnetYou should defend Mekhnet as you both face Thraunákh.Defend MekhnetThraunákh has been defeated.Talk to Mekhnet'Lead the way.''Look at us; we're in Atrad-zarakh and the Orcs outside are none the wiser! You made short work of those sentries earlier.

'Thraunákh's creations have caused no small amount of woe for us. With him out of the way, we stand a chance to claim Atrad-zarakh for good.

'Of course, we can't claim the watch-tower tonight with just the two of us, but let's find Thraunákh and leave our mark. Shall we?''That must be him. He seems like a nervous worm, but putting together these sapper-bombs requires careful planning and thought. If only that intelligence was not used for such a destructive purpose.

'Well, enough talk. Let's finish what we set out to do, yes?''Good riddance. I can never get back the months I spent recovering, but at least this will prevent more of our comrades from experiencing the same pain.

'Now, let's head back. I'm sure Lúka is worried sick. Heh, sometimes I think she'll never relax.'After the escape of Gorgar, son of Bolg, and the sealing of the gates of Mount Gundabad by Hrímil Frost-heart, Prince Durin faces his first true test. As Durin weighs whether it will be vengeance or wisdom that guides his hand, Lord Glóin's thoughts turn to conflicts of old. All too familiar with the triumphs and sorrows of Durin's Folk, Glóin hopes that a lesson in history might temper Prince Durin's desire to bring the War of Three Peaks to an end at all cost.Glóin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.Aid Glóin with 'Blood of Azog: A Lesson Hard-learned'Prince Durin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu in Elderslade.Aid Prince Durin with 'Blood of Azog: Fathers and Sons'King Thorin can be found in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.Aid King Thorin with 'Blood of Azog: Brethren of Past and Present'Hersegg can be found in Akrâz-zahar at Erebor.Aid Hersegg with 'Blood of Azog: The Sixth War'King Thorin can be found in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.Aid King Thorin with 'Blood of Azog: Muster to the Mountain-home'King Thorin can be found in the Hall Under the Mountain at Erebor.Aid King Thorin with 'Blood of Azog: Will of the Stormhammer'Hermáth Stormhammer can be found at Vosh Torú, in the Dwelling-halls of Erebor.Aid Hermáth Stormhammer with 'Blood of Azog: At Seventh Light'Durin can be found in the war-room at Annâk-khurfu.

After speaking with Hermáth Stormhammer, Durin has decided that the Gabil'akkâ will march on Mount Gundabad in seven days. You should talk to Durin.Talk to Durin at Annâk-khurfu<rgb=#00FF00>It is recommended, but not required, that you complete The Legacy of Durin and the Trials of the Dwarves, Chapters 1-4 before playing 'Blood of Azog.'</rgb>

'It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}. This is not the manner of reunion I would have preferred, but I'm afraid it could not be helped.

'Though I have tried to convince him otherwise, Prince Durin blames himself for the setback at the gates of Mount Gundabad. He considers it a defeat, a failure, and perhaps most of all, a rebuke to his worthiness of the name Durin. Even though that is not so, I fear he believes that vengeance might be his only path to redemption in the eyes of the Longbeards.

'Prince Durin has heard many tales of the deeds of his grandfather, but neither he nor even King Thorin know them as I do. In my youth, I learned the cost of vengeance first-hand. I was at Azanulbizar! I know well the price that was paid to see Azog's head set upon a pike!

'It is true that Gorgar and Hrímil await us beyond the gates of Mount Gundabad, but within the Mountain-home, the advantage belongs to them. If we are to win this war and reclaim the sacred mountain, our soldiers must be given the time to tend their wounds and prepare for the battles ahead.

'Let us help Prince Durin to lead the Gabil'akkâ to victory, my friend. That is all I ask.''I can hardly believe how much has changed since you departed Elderlsade. I am told you studied Hermáth's account of the Sixth War, and perhaps I should as well when at last I return to the Lonely Mountain. In the meantime, I am fortunate enough to have the Stormhammer at my side!

'The last blood of Azog still dwells within Mount Gundabad, but when the final test comes in those hallowed halls, we shall be victorious! Neither Gorgar nor Hrímil shall keep us from our prize!

'Were it not for you and Glóin, I... thank you, my friend.'As the War of Three Peaks overtakes Elderslade, Glóin has need of hale warriors to see the battle to its end.Órug, Zhúdo, and Gárjan can be found in the trenches or on the battlefield in the War of Three Peaks.

Glóin has sent you on a mission to join the forces of the Gabil'akkâ on the battlefield and receive reports from Órug, Zhúdo, and Gárjan before delivering his orders to Agnar Brokentooth at Ifbar-athrâkh.Talk to ÓrugTalk to ZhúdoTalk to GárjanThe forward trenches can be found to the south of the ruins of Zudramdân in the War of Three Peaks.

You have received reports from Órug, Zhúdo, and Gárjan. You should continue on to the forward trenches and deliver Glóin's orders to Agnar Brokentooth in Ifbar-athrâkh.Advance to the forward trenchesIfbar-athrâkh can be found beneath the hillside to the south-east of the forward trenches.

You have arrived at the forward trenches and should now seek out Agnar Brokentooth in Ifbar-athrâkh.Find Agnar Brokentooth at Ifbar-athrâkhAgnar Brokentooth can be found at Ifbar-athrâkh.

You have found Agnar Brokentooth in Ifbar-athrâkh. You should deliver Glóin's orders to Agnar Brokentooth at once.'Hail, ${PLAYER}!

'The War of Three Peaks has begun, and we of the Gabil'akkâ have delved our trenches in answer to the Orcs of Gundabad. Indeed, the lines of battle are drawn in the very earth of Elderslade! 

We may have secured a foothold at Zarkul-sulûn, but it will take more than one victory to triumph against Gorgar the Ruthless. To that end, I have a mission for you.

First, I ask that you gather reports from the dwarves Órug, Zhúdo, and Gárjan. I must know more of how our forces fare in the field, and I trust you to hear their words.

'Second and of most import, I have sent three of Prince Durin's most trusted veterans of the Sixth War of Orcs and Dwarves to establish a forward command at Ifbar-athrâkh. You are to deliver these orders to Agnar Brokentooth and aid him and the others as best you can.

'I trust you alone with this mission. Be swift!''${PLAYER}!

'I did not expect to see you here in the trenches. A surprise, to be sure, but a welcome one!

'We have held the line here for some time now, and I am pleased to report that the approach to Zarkul-sulûn remains safe... as safe as it can be in a time of war! Why have you come this way?'

You tell Órug that Glóin has sent you on a mission to Ifbar-athrâkh.

'Glóin has ordered you to the forward trenches? Then you'd best be on your way, my friend!

'Keep your head down and do not let those terrible fumes take hold of you!''Things do not go well, ${PLAYER}.

'We thought to sail beneath the notice of Gorgar's forces on the field, but when we were beset by ballista-fire when we drew nearer to Gundabad.

'Many of my kinsfolk were lost in the burning of their ships, and it is only by some chance of fate that ours was not lost as well.

'We plan to return to Zarkul-sulûn and tend our wounds. I plan to rejoin the battle, but this time, it shall be on foot.''I have nothing of note to report, ${PLAYER}, except that not once in all my years in Mordor have I seen a troll with two heads!

'Shakal-atrâd is ours, and we have rallied to its defence. For the Gabil'akkâ!''Have you brought orders from commander Glóin? Give them here!'

Agnar unfurls Glóin's orders and begins to read them.

'It is as I feared, my friends.'You have been summoned to Skarháld in Ered Mithrin, for dwarves gathered there have need of your aid in their journey through Elderslade.The fortress of Skarháld can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should travel to Skarháld.The fortress of Skarháld can be found in Ered Mithrin.

You should talk to Agátur the Boastful in Skarháld.Talk to Agátur the Boastful in SkarháldThe Rushgate can be found on the border between Ered Mithrin and Elderslade.

You should talk to Rúzat at the Rushgate.Talk to Rúzat at the RushgateYou have received a summons from Agátur the Boastful to join him at the fortress of Skarháld in Ered Mithrin. The Longbeard Dwarves have called for all their friends and allies to proceed to Skarháld as soon as they are able. The time to retake Gundabad is at hand.'${PLAYER}, I am glad to see you! The march to Gundabad has begun! Many of us have set forth from Ered Mithin with our paths taking us through Elderslade. All sights are on Gundabad! I knew that you, one of our greatest allies, would march by our side. When you are ready, travel west to the Rushgate. I will meet you in Elderslade at Annâk-khurfu.''Why the great ${CLASS} comes to the Rushgate! Humble Rúzat is very happy to see you again. You have done so much for the Zhélruka already! Now that you are here, Gundabad will certainly belong to the Zhélruka once more! Oh do not worry, other brave heroes are here too. I did not mean to put the weight of the entire march on your shoulders, my friend!'Explore the enemy encampments and strongholds within Gloomingtarn.Explore the enemy encampments and strongholds within Gloomingtarn.Explore DrúkalaExplore OrmgrenExplore GlittengrofExplore SkelstrandExplore Mîn LorgExplore Lochan DúvDefeat many Angmarim, Dourhands, and Hill-men sworn to the Iron Crown in Câr Bronach.Defeat many Angmarim, Dourhands, and Hill-men sworn to the Iron Crown in Câr Bronach.Defeat servants of the Iron Crown in Câr BronachDefeat many Angmarim, Dourhands, and Hill-men sworn to the Iron Crown in Câr Bronach.Defeat many Angmarim, Dourhands, and Hill-men sworn to the Iron Crown in Câr Bronach.Defeat servants of the Iron Crown in Câr BronachDefeat many Angmarim, Dourhands, and Hill-men sworn to the Iron Crown in Câr Bronach.Defeat many Angmarim, Dourhands, and Hill-men sworn to the Iron Crown in Câr Bronach.Defeat servants of the Iron Crown in Câr BronachSkarding Wargsbane, the great Beorning hunter, tracks a deadly beast upon the battlefield.Skarding Wargsbane can be found on the battlefield.

