You should talk to Léothred at Hlithseld.Talk to Léothred at Hlithseld'I have learned much from our time in Misthallow thus far, ${PLAYER}. Now comes the time to make the trek to the great keep of my people's past. It is a relic and a keeper of memories both. There we will most certainly uncover many a hidden secret, and reaffirm many of the stories of old. Come, let us travel and establish a safe encampment near Framsburg. I would imagine that we will stay there to study the keep for quite some time.''This encampment will do nicely. I thank you for your help. Framsburg is but a bridge away, not far at all!

'Yes, I did call this keep Fasthálla earlier. I do apologize. The keep was renamed Framsburg after the great Fram!

'Now then, should we begin studying the ruins?''We learned quite a lot upon our first day at Framsburg. I do say, we shall learn even more tomorrow! Until then I will write down my findings and get some well-deserved rest. I suggest you do the same.''At last, we reach Framsburg! Now where to begin? Ah yes, Lóthar.

'Now it was Lóthar’s son Gundric who brought the most grief to his father’s rule, for he dared seek to ride one of the mearas. Like his longfather Gisilhári he failed, perishing beneath the angry hooves when the beast trampled him.''Grieving, Lóthar took in his one-year-old grandson, Bóra, and raised him as his own. During this time, a change befell the Ai-thúda. Due to close contact with both the dwarves and the Hól-budlan, their language underwent a series of changes, so that by the end of Lord Lóthar’s rule, it mostly resembled the later tongue of my people.

'Framabárg became Framsburg in their language; Hól-budla became holbytla, and their own name of Ai-thúda now was spoken as Éothéod. It also became tradition, after the fashion of Frumgar long ago, for the lords to name their heirs with words that meant lord or some variant thereof.''Lóthar’s grandson Bóra succeeded him, and during Bóra's rule, King Thorin I of the Longbeards was slain by Orcs in Ered Mithrin. This sparked the Fifth War of Dwarves and Orcs. The Éothéod found themselves pulled into the fighting that raged in the mountains around them, for the Orcs threatened them as well. This unrest lasted through Bóra’s rule and that of his son Maera, ending at last in the time of Théodric, Maera’s son.''With the Éothéod’s aid, the Longbeards once again drove the last Orcs back to Gundabad. Théodric would not break his namesake Thíudric’s ban upon entering the Mountain-home though. His reluctance played a part in King Glóin’s decision not to enter Gundabad either.''Lord Théodric was in turn succeeded by his son Haldend, a grim and rude-mannered man who urged his father to break the Ban of Thíudric and enter Gundabad. He yearned for the glory he had found fighting the Orcs, and at the age of fifty, only two years into his reign, he foolishly chose to attempt to ride a mearh.''Haldend was no more fortunate than Gundric and Gisilhári. The steed he chose kicked him in the head, cracking his skull. He survived, but the wound robbed him of his wits, leaving him unable to speak more than a few words or lift a sword.'Léothred, the Rohirric scholar, wishes to learn more about the fabled white horses, the mearas, who once roamed wild in Misthallow.Evidence that the mearas still exist may be nearby in Misthallow.

You should search for evidence that the mearas still exist.Find evidence that the mearas still existThe hoofprints may lead to a mearh.

