Return to Dráttur.'Our enemies have learned to fear boiling and burning pitch that tumble from our walls. Our pitch buckets have a sad lack of the black tar now, however. Go and gather resin from the pine trees.

'I will meet you north, in Zirakazhâr. Bring the resin there, that we might melt it down. Let us make it rain black ichor once more.''I will meet you north, in Zirakazhâr. Bring the resin there, that we might melt it down.''I will meet you north, in Zirakazhâr. Bring the resin there, that we might melt it down.''Excellent! Now we shall melt it down. Fill that cauldron there. It shall take some time to melt, so you can take this bucket I just filled. Bring it to the walls and replenish the supplies there.

'When you have finished, meet me once more in Amdân.''Well done, friend. The enemy trembles when they approach our walls.'As long as you are travelling with Venko, you should try to get along with the moody Zhélruka.Venko has unpacked a number of weapons from his belongings, and they have attracted your curiosity.Examine the axe Venko unpackedExamine the sword Venko unpackedVenko is at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Venko at your camp-site in MisthallowVenko has asked you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the Reikfoss of Greylin to the north, in memory of his fallen brothers.Venko is at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Venko at your camp-site in MisthallowVenko has fallen silent. Perhaps you should drink in silence and leave him be for a time.Drink in silence, leaving Venko to his thoughtsGlóin and Imák are at your camp-site in Misthallow.Talk to Glóin at your camp-site in MisthallowTalk to Imák at your camp-site in Misthallow'We should not spend too long here in idleness. I understand few folk live alongside the river this far north, but those who do will be in greater danger from such a raiding-party as the one we seek.'

You ask Venko if he needs any help unpacking his belongings.

'No, ${PLAYER}, thank you. We Zhélruka learned long ago the value of unencumbered travel, and carry very little on our mountain journeys.''I do not need your help, but I thank you for offering it.''Leave those alone, ${PLAYER}. They belonged to my brothers, and I would have no other hands touch them!'

Venko scowls at you, but after a moment's glower he relents with a sigh.

'Ah, I am sorry. Do not think me rude. The three of us came west to seek our rightful home: me, my younger brother Dúsek, and my eldest brother Lékhun. I wish now we had stayed in the East, but what sense is there in dwelling upon it? My brothers were both slain when the Frost-horde attacked, and now it falls on me alone to fulfil the purpose with which we set out. If the Zhélruka are ever given a home to call our own, I will hang my brothers' weapons above my mantlepiece and at last know peace. Until that day, I will bear Krúvzhar and Ránkova with me, but they will not see battle.

'The Zhélruka were promised a place in Gundabad, long ago, but they never reached it. Do you think your friend Durin will honour this ancient offer if he is able to retake the mountain from the Orcs? I do not mind telling you I have my doubts, ${PLAYER}.

'But that is a worry for the future. For now I think of my fallen brothers and mourn that they will not see the gates of Gundabad, or the crags of Elderslade, or the famed Reikfoss of Greylin. Ah! I have had an idea. We are not that far from the roaring waterfalls of Misthallow, ${PLAYER}. I charge you to find a good vantage point to the north and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the nearest of the Three Sisters, the Reikfoss of Greylin, in honour of my brothers. Return then to me and I would be grateful to hear your description of it.''We are not that far from the roaring waterfalls of Misthallow, ${PLAYER}. I charge you to find a good vantage point to the north where you can <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the nearest of the Three Sisters, the Reikfoss of Greylin, in honour of my brothers. Return then to me and I would be grateful to hear your description of it.''You have returned from the Reikfoss? Tell me, ${PLAYER}. What did you make of this mighty waterfall of Misthallow?'

You give Venko your impression of the Reikfoss from the vantage point you found, and the Zhélruka nods solemnly.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}. My brothers and I promised to look upon the Three Sisters on our approach to Gundabad, but the Frost-horde ensured that promise was not to be kept. I still look forward to my first view of the falls, but I am pleased you have done so in memory of Dúsek and Lékhun.''It seems that Longbeards and Zhélruka know the same sorrows, ${PLAYER}, and Venko has my sympathy. I too have lost a brother. Óin went to Moria with Balin, and died there. Some dwarves would be content to live out their lives in safety and in peace, but not my brother. Nor would I, before you say it!

'Neither would our Zhélruka companion, it seems. He still intends to see the sights that were denied his brothers, and plans no defeated return to the eastern lands. I find that commendable.''We have all lost people. The Zhélruka has my sympathy, but no more and no less than anyone who has suffered the same. Let the memory of his brothers strengthen his resolve and toughen his spirit.'Yrsa Spearwife, wife of Runerik and chief warrior of Limlók, seeks to repair the broken palisades ruined by recent assaults upon the settlement.Dead trees can be found in the Floodfells.

