You should talk to Molkha.Talk to Molkha in the valleyNow that you have Molkha's report, it is time to move further up the mountain.

You should talk to Hórin and decide how to proceed.'Now that the camp is ready to receive our reinforcements and provide us adequate shelter in this harsh place, it is time we venture out in search of our enemies. Ausma's apprentices Molkha, Kyrstig, and Klúso are out there in the wild somewhere, spying on the hobgoblin host. They are to signal us when they see us approaching and give to us their report. If we're lucky, they will have some information on why Dushtalbúk is all the way up here in the freezing cold!

'Let us begin by heading south up the Father's Path. It should wind all the way up the peaks, but who can say what condition the ancient road might be in? Lead the way, ${PLAYER}.''Hello again, ${PLAYER}. Keep an eye on the sky, because this area is a drake hunting ground! A big one flies overhead quite often, hunting for goats. I've seen him pluck one right off the side of the mountain and carry it up to the cliff tops there, where I wager he has a nest of whelplings to feed!

'As for my report, I have both good news and bad. The good news is that I was able to observe the hobgoblins passing through here. The bad news is... well, I should just start from the beginning.''Let me start from the beginning. Here's what I saw....''That is all I saw. Although the Orcs and hobgoblins are not on friendly terms, Dushtalbúk and Loknashra are forming alliances of their own. I expect that is bad news for us, yes?

'The Angmarim have set up camp to the south-east. They call it Mullach Cagar. I am afraid I do not know their aims, but that camp must be of some importance to them. They have poured no small effort into proving themselves valuable allies to the hobgoblins, seemingly to gain safe passage from the drakes. I hope you and Hórin can find out more. I have a very bad feeling about this.''It seems our enemies are forming alliances right in front of our faces! My blood boils to hear how they use fallen dwarves as bargaining chips in their games of state-craft! Even with those "gifts", I did not foresee the Frost-horde accommodating the Angmarim in this fashion. From what I understood, Sauron's torture of Hrímil had made his former servants her hated enemies. I remember what she did to Karazgar, the Weeping Warrior! It may be that times change, or perhaps she has some nefarious plan in mind for her former foes? Bah, trying to understand the motivations of a Dragon feels like a waste of time. She means to use whatever is at her disposal to exterminate the dwarves, I'm sure!

'But I do think this news merits some investigation before we continue. We need to know why the Angmarim are up here in the first place, and we need to do something about this nest of drakes before they threaten Fellgát and our approaching reinforcements.''Bah, the bridge is out! I suppose I should not have expected the road to be in good condition after all these years. The Orcs certainly wouldn't maintain it! Let us see if we can find a way around by walking across that frozen lake down there. Hmm. Molkha should be around here somewhere. Keep an eye out for her signal.'You have discovered some very old dwarf-made equipment inside Draurákh.You have discovered ancient dwarf-made equipment inside Draurákh.

You should collect ancient dwarf armour and ancient dwarf weapons and return them to Fellgát.Collect ancient dwarf weapons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect ancient dwarf armour (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected several pieces of ancient dwarf-made equipment.

You should return to Fellgát and show it to Dréri.Talk to Dréri at FellgátAmong the scattered bones and scraps of hide you find a selection of very old dwarf-made equipment, certainly not from the current expedition. Whether beasts dragged fallen dwarves into this cave and only their gear remains, or long ago dwarves were sheltering from something in this cave, you cannot say. If you can find more of it in Draurákh, the dwarves back at Fellgát will be interested to see it.'What have you found, friend? I see by the glint in your eye that you have found something valuable! Quickly, show me!'

You show Dréri the ancient dwarf-make weapons and armour you discovered resting in the wolf den.

'By the haft of Drása's axe! These seem to be from the Second Age, and still in reasonable condition considering the passage of so many years! Our Longbeard friends will be most pleased to see these treasures recovered. Thank you, ${PLAYER}.'Some of the ice-giants did not make it inside before the battle in Thyrstáth. They may be grateful if you come to their rescue.Several ice-giants have been caught between the battle between the Orcs and hobgoblins.

You should rescue harassed ice-giants in Thyrstáth.Rescue harassed ice-giants (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Use the summoning horn to call Hórin to your side.

You should talk to Hórin.Call Hórin and talk to him'Some of the ice-giants did not make it inside before the battle began, and are now trapped between the Orcs and the hobgoblins. We should come to their rescue, and earn their favour!''I think we may have made a good first impression! If at least one of those giants informs their leader that we helped them, that may prove sufficient for him to grant us an audience.'With the mithril hammer in his possession, Prince Durin is interested to learn from where it came, and what significance it might hold for his conquest of the Mountain-home.Zul-mazal is by the upper crypts on the western side of the Pit of Stonejaws.Return to Zul-mazal by the upper crypts on the western side of the Pit of StonejawsA number of Longbeards stand in Zul-mazal, studying the broken mosaic and the curious stone block in the centre of the chamber.Talk to Stáli the Hewer in Zul-mazalTalk to Fastrith in Zul-mazalTalk to Bróin in Zul-mazalDurin stands in Zul-mazal, deep in thought.Talk to Durin in Zul-mazal'It was with the help of Glúrkub that Gorgar first acquired this hammer from Zul-mazal. If Hrímil fears it, I would see the chamber from where it came. Some clue to her fear might remain within, and this understanding may help us use it against her.

'Lead on, ${PLAYER}. I have not yet seen Zul-mazal, but you know where it lies.''Lead on, ${PLAYER}. I would see the chamber where the hammer was found.''Oh, it's you again. Do not forget that you owe me a favour! I did not have to release Eskil from his agreement with me, and chose to do so from benevolence only! You have surely noticed that my new apprentice has not retrieved the Zabad'ibîn for me, as you promised she would! You did not promise that? Well, someone did.

'But I do confess she has a better eye for artistry than her brother ever possessed. See this mosaic Fastrith brought to my attention? It is the perfect match to one that decorated the Hall Under the Mountain in Erebor, and which I was restoring before departing for Gundabad.

'My apprentice has somehow come into possession of numerous colourful stones with which I may be able to complete the mosaic. It will be another triumph by Stáli the Hewer, master sculptor!''Welcome back to Zul-mazal, ${PLAYER}! I understand that you helped dear Bróin collect stones and pebbles for me? Thank you for taking the time to do so... it can't have been easy!

'My master Stáli is very pleased that he may be able to complete this mosaic, although he tries not to show it. When I presented to him the pouch of colourful pebbles that Bróin gifted to me, he said he might begin my instruction in sculpture-carving sometime in the next ten years.

'That is quite soon, ${PLAYER}! Yesterday he said he would teach me sometime in the next hundred!''Fastrith was delighted by the gift, ${PLAYER}! Thank you again for collecting those stones!'

Reminded of the pouch of stones you found on an Orc in the Welkin-lofts, you tell Bróin of your discovery and hand it over. He beams happily at you.

'Perfect, my friend! This will surely be enough stones to complete the mosaic in this chamber!''For what purpose was this room constructed, ${PLAYER}? Is it a tomb or memorial of some kind? And yet it shares little with the crypts outside. I do feel the presence of an enchantment of some sort... it puts me in mind of a net draped upon stone.'

You tell Durin that Imák sensed something similar in the chamber, but dismissed it as a product of the Angmarim cultists in Gundabad. Durin shakes his head.

'I do not think this is the work of the Angmarim: this enchantment feels dwarf-made to me. There is a secret in this chamber, ${PLAYER}. A dwarf secret.'Durin believes that Zul-mazal holds some secret, and he is determined to learn what it may be.Durin is in Zul-mazal, determined to unravel whatever mystery lies within the curious chamber's walls.Talk to Durin and help him uncover the secret of Zul-mazalDurin stands beside a secret stair deep beneath Gundabad, marvelling at the long-hidden passage.Talk to Durin deep beneath Gundabad'Will you help me uncover this secret, ${PLAYER}? According to Glúrkub, this is the room from which Gorgar acquired the mithril hammer. Revealing this secret may help us understand why Hrímil Frost-heart so fears this weapon, and how we may use it against her.''Then let us begin. This chamber will reveal its secrets to me!''I can hardly believe it, ${PLAYER}. For how long has this secret stair remained hidden? And where does it lead?'Durin believes that none have travelled these secret stairs for an age, but he will not allow that to dissuade him from seeking whatever may lie at the bottom.The secret stairs have not been travelled for an age, descending far below Gundabad.

What lies beyond their lowest step?Descend the secret stairs far below Gundabad and explore the deepest cavernsDurin runs into the long-forgotten chamber.Wait for Durin to catch up with youDurin stands in the long-forgotten chamber deep below Gundabad.Talk to Durin in the long-forgotten chamberA statue of Mótsog stands in the long-forgotten chamber.Examine the statue of MótsogDurin stands in the long-forgotten chamber beneath Gundabad.Talk to Durin in the long-forgotten chamberDurin has asked you to meet him on the stairs outside the long-forgotten chamber.Meet up with Durin at the bottom of the stairs outsideSomething about the air feels different. But what?Return to Durin and learn what has disturbed him'My breath is stolen away by the sight of this cavern, and by the knowledge that none have traversed these steps for an age. The stair snakes along the wall, descending out of sight; how far below Gundabad will we find its lowest step?

'Tread carefully, ${PLAYER}. I will meet you at the bottom of the stairs... for surely there must be an end to them. Is it not so?''Tread carefully, ${PLAYER}. I will meet you at the bottom of the stairs... for surely there must be an end to them. Is it not so?''Even these stairs have an end, ${PLAYER}. But look there: a path leads to a chamber of some kind!''What is this place?'

Durin stares in disbelief at the statue on the opposite side of the chamber.

'That is a statue of Mótsog the Traitor, who led the Greymauls against Durin the Deathless in the First Age! He was slain at the conclusion of his rebellion... why would he be given the dignity of a tomb?'

Durin fumes, his eyes flashing angrily and his fists clenched.

'What is the meaning of this?''What is the meaning of this? Mótsog was slain for his treachery. He should never have been given the dignity of such a tomb!''I cannot believe that a traitor such as Mótsog would be given the dignity of a tomb beneath the Mountain-home, ${PLAYER}. One of his followers must have built this place in secret after Durin's judgement. Did they spirit his body here? Does it lie behind one of these walls, enjoying the undeserved rest of a traitorous worm?

'I am disgusted. Such a place should not be. I thought we would find some secret to opposing Hrímil Frost-heart? There is no such secret here. Let us meet outside this shameful crypt and decide what next can be done.''Let us meet outside this shameful crypt and decide what next can be done.''What was that? Something changed, ${PLAYER}! What enchantment is this?'The statue of Mótsog has changed. Where once it was worked in grey stone, now the statue is given the colour he might have had in life. So too is there another change: the statue is now shorn of any beard.

Despite its lifelike colour, the statue remains unmoving and bereft of life.There is something strange about this chamber and the statue of Mótsog it contains, but Durin has vowed not to rest until he unravels their mysteries.Durin contemplates the lifelike statue and the strange chamber in which it stands.Talk to Durin and unravel the mystery of the long-forgotten chamberDurin stands in Mótsog's Tomb, shaken by what he has seen and experienced.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombSomething about the lifelike visage compels your eye, but the strange enchantment that encircles the statue of Mótsog resists your study. The feeling that you are being watched by an invisible presence grows stronger and more unwelcome.'Something is not right, ${PLAYER}. We are being watched. Can you sense it?

'Show yourself, villain!''I am... shaken, I confess it. Why does Mótsog show these visions to us, ${PLAYER}? For what purpose would such a foe of my great ancestor give this knowledge to me? What does Mótsog hope to gain from aiding me thus?'Durin has demanded that Mótsog account for himself, and deny his treachery if he can.Mótsog stands in a long-forgotten chamber deep below Gundabad.Talk to Mótsog and hear his accountMótsog stands in a long-forgotten chamber deep below Gundabad.Talk to MótsogDurin stands in Mótsog's Tomb deep beneath Gundabad.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's Tomb'I will not be swayed by honeyed words from an enemy's tongue, ${PLAYER}. Did you mark how often he called me his friend? But it is not so. Remain on your guard, and I will do the same.'

Durin stares coldly at the beardless dwarf standing in the ancient crypt.

'I know you for an enemy, Mótsog, and I demand that you account for yourself! Do you deny your treachery?''I do not deny it, Durin who was once my friend.'

Behind you Durin snorts loudly with disgust.

'It is true,' Mótsog continues. 'Once we were great friends, and you trusted me with your kingdom. But I do not deny my treachery. Hear how it was, and you may remember it anew.''Deny your treachery, if you can!''See you now that I deny nothing?

'You who were Durin: you were my friend, and my lord, but I coveted your title and your lands, the respect and the love that was given you. I listened too closely to those of my people who whispered that I might truly be an Eighth Father of the Dwarves.'

Mótsog turns his attention to you.

'Aye, an Eighth Father to join the other Seven. "If there were eight, and not seven," my people whispered, "about what else might they deceive us?" Some suggested, falsely, that perhaps I awoke first beneath the mountains, and it was Durin who usurped my rights and privileges! Durin was the usurper!

'They were false whispers, but they echoed loudly in the halls of Gundabad while Durin was away, and I did not speak against them as I should have done. That failure led in direct line to the Greymaul Rebellion.'

Mótsog once again addresses Durin.

'No, my friend. I do not deny my treachery. Neither do I deny the blood spilt by those who followed me.''I do not deny my treachery.'Durin wears an impassive expression as he considers Mótsog.

'These crimes are unforgiveable, and they match my understanding of the Greymaul Rebellion. You deserved the death given to you by my ancestor Durin, to whom you owed fealty.

'You claim you were his friend? You say you speak the truth about the Greymaul Rebellion, but I deem you speak falsely on this: you were no friend of Durin. Nor are you a friend of mine.'Mótsog's tale is not yet complete, and Durin is determined to hear more.Mótsog stands in a chamber far below Gundabad.Talk to Mótsog and hear the rest of his taleMótsog stands in a long-forgotten chamber deep beneath Gundabad.Talk to Mótsog'Finish your tale, Mótsog, and be done with it. I care not if you repent of the evils you wrought. Speak of Durin's judgement, and know that you deserved it.'Mótsog watches Durin, his eyes glinting in the dim light of the chamber.

'I speak not of "deserving," my friend. I speak only of what occurred, and leave the judging of what was "deserved" to others.'

When Durin does not reply, Mótsog addresses you once again.

'My rebellion led to ten years of war. Ten years of fighting levied a bloody toll on Greymaul and Longbeard alike, but still there was no sign that an end could ever come. At last, after ten years, Durin devised a plan to bring the conflict to an end.''Much I have seen in the years since the Sunder-battle might have transpired differently had I slain Hrímil Frost-heart in Biriz-zahar, but it was not to be. And yet I know this: the memory of the wounds she sustained from the mithril hammer pains her still, even to this day.'

Mótsog falls silent and turns his piercing gaze upon Durin.Mótsog has finished his account, and he has told you that the conclusion is for someone else to tell.Durin stands in a long-forgotten chamber beneath Gundabad, clearly confused by the words of Mótsog.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombDurin looks at you with realization dawning in his eyes.Talk to Durin and hear his remembranceMótsog stands in a long-forgotten chamber deep beneath Gundabad.Talk to Mótsog'Durin knows the rest of the tale. It is not for me to tell.''Durin knows the rest of the tale. It is not for me to tell.''Is this some trick? How do you expect me to know the conclusion of your tale, Mótsog? It is an impossibility.'

Durin turns to you, irritation on his face, but before he can voice his dismissal his brow furrows with confusion.

'And yet... there is something strange, ${PLAYER}. As I cast my memory to Sundergrót, I find that I see it not as it was mere weeks ago, but as Mótsog described it during the First Age, when it was the site of Biriz-zahar! How can this be? I... I do know what happened following the Sunder-battle. I was...'

Durin stares at you with amazement in his eyes.

'I was there, ${PLAYER}, as I was in Moria, and at Helegrod, and in Mordor. I am Durin now as I was then, six times before. I will finish your tale, Mótsog. I am the only one still in this world who can.''Hear then the end of the tale, as I know it to be.

'For I am Durin, Father of Dwarves, first to awaken, and I was there.''Aye, Durin gave me my life, both mercy and punishment. I have remained in this chamber ever since, casting my dreaming gaze over the mountains, watching from afar the trials of the dwarves. I say it was mercy, for truly I deserved a usurper's death, but so too do I call it punishment, for I was unable to intervene or aid them in their struggles.

'On rare occasions one of those I watched would seem to catch a glimpse of me, but never in my own form. Was this a cruelty of the First Crafter, or some confounding jest? I cannot say. But this I know: only once did he to whom I spoke hear my words.

'It was Durin who heard me, Durin who returned again and again with new visage but the same spirit in his breast. Only Durin heard my words, my friend of old who showed me the mercy I did not deserve. So often had I sought to aid the dwarves in their trials, and so often failed. But when Durin stood before the Anvil of Winterstith I spoke to him of Gundabad and his right to rule the Mountain-home for all dwarves, and he listened. It must mean something. My long penance nears its end, and the prolonged hours of my dream must soon pass away. Soon I will wake, and Mótsog will be no more.'You have discovered fell spirits contained in Angmarim crystals in Mullach Cagar.Glowing crystals can be found in Mullach Cagar.

You should destroy these crystals and defeat the released fell spirits.Destroy glowing crystals to release fell spiritsDefeat released fell spirits (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many fell spirits in Mullach Cagar, preventing them from being used to make wights.

You should report to Molkha in the valley.Talk to Molkha in the valleyYou found a crystal apparently left behind by one of the Angmarim, possessed of a foreboding aura. As you pick it up to examine it more closely, you think you can hear a tapping sound emanating from inside, and a quiet whispering. It seems likely the Angmarim priests use these crystals to hold fell spirits before they find suitable corpses for creating wights.

If you destroy these crystals, the fell spirit might be more easily banished.'What did you find in Mullach Cagar?'

You describe the crystals you found and the spirits they contained.

