Nekhtan has told you to tale of the rite of Clúcath between the Trév Gállorg and the Trév Duvárdain. You should talk to Nekhtan.Talk to Nekhtan at Bail AvarcNekhtan has offered to tell you what he knows of Crannog's fate after his defeat at Clúcath.Hear what Nekhtan knows of Crannog's fateNekhtan can be found at Bail Avarc in Angmar.

Nekhtan has told you to tale of the fate that befell Crannog and the Trév Gállorg after the Iron Crown returned to Carn Dûm. You should talk to Nekhtan.Talk to Nekhtan at Bail AvarcSoltakh can be found at Bail Avarc in Angmar.

After informing you that Crannog was taken by the Iron Crown, Nekhtan has suggested that Soltakh might know of secret ways into the city of Carn Dûm. You should return to Soltakh.Talk to Soltakh at Bail Avarc'Much has changed since last you came to Angmar, my friend. In truth, even to my own eyes, it has changed since I returned.

'Not long ago, Crannog gave to me a Fém—a purpose—that led me into the east. I am ashamed to even think it, but I wish I had never left. After Carn Dûm was abandoned, I was to follow the remnants of the Iron Crown to the lands of Mount Gundabad. Many of the Trév Duvárdain travelled with them, despite the countless divisions that had grown between their tribesmen and the Iron Crown after the False King was deposed. The Iron Crown sought for something in the lands of Câr Bronach, but as their search grew more desperate, more and more of the Trév Duvárdain forsook the Iron Crown and fled into the hills.

'It was there I greeted them, neither as friend or foe, but as one who also sought to walk a new path. I gave shelter to them at Eiricháv, and for a time, it seemed that we might soon return to Angmar in friendship with nothing more to fear of the Iron Crown.

'Indeed, I heard that the High Priestess of the Iron Crown, a woman named Ásachal, had been slain in the heights of Tûr Fúar. Imagine my surprise, then, when I saw her lead her forces out of the gates of Agal Dûn some days after that!

'I tried to follow them as they crossed Bálach Iaran, but I was discovered. I managed to slay my attackers, but one of their crossbow bolts pierced my shin. It was only with the help of Aighrig that I was able to make it back to Eiricháv and tend to my wounds. Even so, I could not follow the Iron Crown until I could stand on my own again.

'By the time I got to Aughaire, it was too late. Crannog was nowhere to be found, and I learned from Nekhtan that another Clúcath had been called between the Trév Gállorg and the Trév Duvárdain.

'Alas, I cannot speak to what I did not witness. If you wish to know what happened, better you hear it from Nekhtan!''I have said enough and done too little. Nekhtan knows the truth of what befell the Trév Gállorg at Clúcath.''Yes?'

You ask Nekhtan what has happened in Angmar since the return of the Iron Crown. You mention that Soltakh spoke of another Clúcath—one called by the Trév Duvárdain.

'That much is true, ${PLAYER}. A great deal happened after Soltakh travelled into the east, and though he may not believe it, I do not begrudge him his absence when the Iron Crown returned.

'It seems so long ago now, but for a time Aughaire knew peace, or so we thought. The Iron Crown had crept back into the shadows, but from within the darkness, their gaze was still fixed upon us. I sensed something, and always I felt uneasy in the new way of things after the fall of the False King, but there was no danger to be found. The Iron Crown had gone. Carn Dûm was abandoned, and the Angmarim made for Bálach Iaran. After they crossed into the Iron Pass, we saw no more of them and they fled into the East.

'When many of the Trév Duvárdain returned from the East in the wake of their masters in the Iron Crown, I feared what might become of Angmar. And I was right to!

'It was not long after that the Trév Duvárdain began to trouble us once more, and though I did not know it then, everything we had fought for was about to be taken from us.''Do you wish to hear the tale, my friend?''You wish to know what became of Crannog, do you? I thought as much.

'If that is what you truly wish, I will tell you.'Nekhtan shakes his head.

'With their victory, Claghórd and the Trév Duvárdain had won the right to govern the affairs of the tribes of Angmar until another Clúcath was called. But Claghórd, guided by the hand of the Iron Crown, intended to make certain it never happened again. After a few days, Claghórd and emissaries of the Iron Crown came to Aughaire. They demanded that five of us come willingly to Carn Dûm or the Iron Crown would send their servants to burn our village in the night. Crannog pleaded with them to take fewer, and though it did not appear to please the emissaries, Claghórd relented, and asked only for one of the Trév Gállorg.

'Fearing what awaited our people in Carn Dûm, Crannog offered himself to the Iron Crown that others might be spared.'

Nekhtan's voice wavers.

'The old fool...

'His bargain had been for nothing, for the emissaries of Iron Crown returned the very next day and they were no longer so eager to negotiate. They took more of our people as each day passed, and soon, many of us fled Aughaire that they might not find us upon their return. Crannog had urged us to shelter at Bail Avarc, and so that is where we went.

'Fifty-eight of the Trév Gállorg have been taken, and we have heard naught of any of them. I only wish that Crannog were still here. He would know what to do....''Twice now Clúcath has claimed those dear to me: first, my father Násan and now, Crannog. I will not suffer another loss!

'These pretenders who still call themselves the Iron Crown hide behind the walls of Carn Dûm. I know that ways into the city still remain, and I think that Soltakh knows them better than he wishes to admit.

'Soltakh—or He-Who-Is-Changed as Crannog perhaps too generously named him—was once of the Trév Duvárdain, after all. His Fém remains unfulfilled, and he has much still to do to prove himself to the Trév Gállorg.

'If there are ways into the city, we must strike at the Iron Crown before their grip upon Angmar is too strong to be broken!''He-Who-Was-Drostan knows well the ways of the Iron Crown.''Do not think ill of Nekhtan, my friend. He is right—I have not fulfilled my Fém.

'Neither have I forgotten the man I was not so long ago: Drostan of the Trév Duvárdain. Deeds alone cannot atone for my past words and actions, and I am well aware of that.

'However, I say there is still worth to be found in the life I once lived!Under the direction of the seemingly-slain High Priestess of the Iron Crown, Ásachal, the servants of the Iron Crown have spread throughout Carn Dûm, seeking a means to reclaim some measure of their former glory at Sant Lhoer and Thaurisgar.Rhúnelleth and Indagir can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

Soltakh has asked you to aid Rhúnelleth and Indagir in their efforts at Sant Lhoer and Thaurisgar as it will advance the fight against the Iron Crown in Carn Dûm. You should help Rhúnelleth and Indagir.Help Rhúnelleth with 'Into Sant Lhoer, the Poison Gardens'Help Indagir with 'Remedies for Maladies'Soltakh can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

You have helped Rhúnelleth and Indagir with their tasks at Sant Lhoer and Thaurisgar—for better and worse. You should return to Bail Avarc and talk to Soltakh.Talk to Soltakh at Bail AvarcLágsaith can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

Soltakh has heard word that a company of Trév Duvárdain battled against the Iron Crown in the sewers of Carn Dûm. You should talk to Lágsaith and learn of what has happened.Talk to Lágsaith and listen to her tale of 'The Lair of Vermin'Soltakh can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

You have heard Lágsaith's tale of the Trév Duvárdain's fight against the Iron Crown in Sagroth. You should talk to Soltakh.Talk to Soltakh at Bail AvarcFinrúth can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

Soltakh has asked you to speak to a woman of the Trév Duvárdain named Finrúth, who came to Bail Avarc seeking shelter from the Iron Crown. You should talk to Finrúth.Talk to Finrúth at Bail Avarc'Like many of the Trév Duvárdain, I once lived in Carn Dûm. Before the downfall of the False King, we even had our own quarter in the city.

'When I was Drostan, I was given a small house by the Iron Crown. It is a strange thing to be gifted something that was stolen from your ancestors. The fools we were, and that too many of those I once called my brothers still are!'

Soltakh pauses for a moment, appearing to calm himself.

'Although many of them have been sealed, a number of hidden paths into Carn Dûm still exist. I can mark them on your map, my friend. I am told that the Iron Crown has taken up in Sant Lhoer and Thaurisgar, but I doubt they mean to tarry in the latter. After all, the old apothecary was already a dangerous ruin before I was cast out of the Trév Duvárdain!

'The Elf, Rhúnelleth, and a man named Indagir are seeking help in venturing into Carn Dûm. Would you speak to them and help them if you are able?''We need to find out what the Iron Crown is doing at Sant Lhoer and Thaurisgar. If we can aid the others in the course of our efforts, all the better!''Even with all I know of the Iron Crown, it is hard to believe what you've told me, ${PLAYER}.

'I am glad that Oganuin will not trouble us any longer, but if the creations of Aniochán outlived him, I fear that our work might not be finished. Especially if what Indagir stole from Thaurisgar falls into unfriendly hands!

'However, it seems we're not the only ones who have been sneaking into Carn Dûm. I have heard that a company of the Trév Duvárdain journeyed into the depths of the city to hunt down a sorcerer of the Angmarim. If that is true, some of our work might have already been done for us!

'I am told that Lágsaith has been telling that tale to all willing to hear it. Would you speak to her and learn what has happened?''You ought to speak to Lágsaith. It sounds like some of our work has already been done—and by a few of these Trév Duvárdain no less!''Well, what did you make of that? It's quite the story, but I wouldn't put it past the Angmarim to sink so low!

'While you were listening to Lágsaith's tale, a woman of the Trév Duvárdain named Finrúth came to Bail Avarc begging for shelter from the Iron Crown and rambling about something she called "bódraillan". It is of our language, but I have never heard it spoken. It means something akin to "oath-thralls", but as I said, I don't know what to make of it.

'As curious as I am to know more, she looked unwell. Perhaps her journey out of Carn Dûm was not a pleasant one, my friend. Knowing what I do of that city, it wouldn't surprise me!

'Once I was able to get a word in, I asked her to take rest in the tent over there until you returned. Would you check on Finrúth and see if she can explain what has happened?''Please, hear me out! I swear I do not mean to deceive you!'After the fall of Mordirith and abandonment of Carn Dûm, the tribesmen of the Trév Duvárdain grew increasingly divided, leading many to forsake the Iron Crown. However, many still remained among the Trév Duvárdain, and either ignorant or wilfully blind to the true wickedness of the Iron Crown, they followed them to Mount Gundabad.A hearty meal and a waterskin can be found at Bail Avarc.

Before Finrúth recounts the tale of how she came to turn on the Iron Crown, she has asked for water and a meal. You should collect a hearty meal and a waterskin for Finrúth.Collect a hearty meal for FinrúthCollect a waterskin for FinrúthFinrúth can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

You have collected a hearty meal and a waterskin for Finrúth. You should return to Finrúth.Finrúth can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

Finrúth has offered to tell you the tale of how she came to abandon the Iron Crown and the true nature of the "bódraillan" that now dwell in Carn Dûm. You should talk to Finrúth.Talk to FinrúthFinrúth can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

Finrúth has told you the tale of how she came to abandon the Iron Crown and you now know the true nature of the "bódraillan" that now dwell in Carn Dûm. You should talk to Finrúth.Talk to Finrúth at Bail AvarcSoltakh can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

You have listened to Finrúth's tale of the shard of Tauressar's power and learned that Crannog may still live within Carn Dûm. You should talk to Soltakh.Talk to Soltakh at Bail Avarc'You must listen to me... before it is too late!

'This land has known too much sorrow, and I fear that darker days lie ahead! I spoke to Soltakh and warned him that the Iron Crown means to make bódraillan of us all. He may not remember me, but I remember him. All among the Trév Duvárdain know of Drostan the exile... and of the one who was called Avair. I will speak no more of her—her deeds have not been forgotten to the Trév Gállorg and I doubt those of hers or Domongart's ilk would find welcome here among them.

'Do you know what I mean by bódraillan, Southron? It means "oath-thralls" in the tongue of my people, but it is worse than it sounds. The Trév Gállorg taken captive by the Iron Crown were the first... and their minds are no longer their own. I thought that was the worst of it, but then they took some of us! It won't be long now... until the rest are... taken and... changed.'

Finrúth appears to be exhausted, and she slumps against the nearby crates.

'I didn't have time to gather many of my belongings when I fled Carn Dûm and I am tired from the journey. Might... I beg for a meal and some water?''You have my thanks, Southron.'

Finrúth eats, perhaps more swiftly than you or she expected. In between bites of rib and potato, she speaks.

'I must admit, I doubted the rumours that the free-folk of Angmar had taken to welcoming the outcasts of the Trév Duvárdain. When I arrived, though, I saw others of my tribe here among you and I knew that I had found shelter—at least for the moment. I have never been more grateful to have been proven wrong!'

Finrúth pauses, setting the plate on the ground beside her. After a moment, her demeanour sharpens.

'Do you know why I have come? Why I would turn my back upon my people all of a sudden? It is not that I have had a change of heart. The Trév Duvárdain were victorious at Clúcath and to govern Angmar is our right. I say to you that the Iron Crown means to cast us aside—and that those who lead the Trév Duvárdain are all too eager to let them!

'I saw things... things that I was not meant to see when the Iron Crown travelled to Mount Gundabad. Beneath Tûr Fúar, the Drearspire, I spied one of the lost Oath-stones of legend: Tauressar. I saw what becomes of those who swear oaths upon it, whether they wish to or not! If the Iron Crown is not stopped, we will all become bódraillan: oath-thralls! Our tribes will mean nothing to us!

'If you will listen, I will tell you everything I know. What say you?''Listen to me, ${PLAYER}, and I will tell you what I know of Tauressar and the bódraillan.''For too long, I would not allow myself to believe what I knew to be true, but when I saw what became of the bódraillan... I...'

Finrúth buries her face in her hands for a moment.

'I am ashamed that I did not abandon the tribe when the others did. I held hope that the Iron Crown might still deliver us to some better future, but I see now that I was a fool. You are a friend of the Trév Gállorg, are you not? The Trév Duvárdain were not always like this, despite what you might think. We are a strong people, and as we always have, we did what was necessary to preserve our tribe. But Claghórd and Domongart before him have betrayed us to the Iron Crown for their own gain—to masters who care nothing for us.

'With that accursed shard, High Priestess Ásachal will take everything from us. There will be no Trév Gállorg, Trév Duvárdain, or even the Iron Crown—only the bódraillan and their terrible Witch-queen. And we won't even remember what we have lost!

'Before I left Carn Dûm, I heard that Crannog was being kept in the Witch-king's tower—Gwathrenost, they call it. The chieftain of the Trév Gállorg lives, but only barely, and I do not think the Iron Crown intend to free him. He has witnessed his people become bódraillan, and I think it is only some mercy or cruelty by High Chieftain Claghórd that spared him from that fate. Perhaps he has become one of them by now, perhaps not. That is all I know, Southron.

'I only hope that what I have told you can help to put an end to this before it is too late!''A stone that can bend wills...

'Such a thing was long considered a legend among the Trév Gállorg, but for it to truly exist? It is unthinkable!'After learning that High Priestess Ásachal possesses the power to dominate the wills of the Trév Gállorg, the Trév Duvárdain, and even those among the Iron Crown, the Hill-men of Angmar plot a desperate siege upon Gwathrenost, the Witch-king's citadel of old.Nekhtan can be found at Bail Avarc in the foothills of the Ephel Angren in Angmar.

Soltakh has told you to prepare to set siege to Gwathrenost, the Witch-king's Citadel. You should talk to Nekhtan and inform him that Crannog still lives before you set out.Talk to Nekhtan at Bail AvarcGwathrenost, the citadel of the Witch-king of Angmar can be found at the heart of Carn Dûm.

You should travel to Gwathrenost and put an end to the Iron Crown's plot to use the shard of Tauressar against the free-folk of the North.


<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You can advance this quest on any difficulty Tier of the Raid: 'Gwathrenost, the Witch-king's Citadel'.</rgb>Travel to Gwathrenost, the Witch-king's CitadelCrannog can be found somewhere high in Gwathrenost, the Witch-king's Citadel.

You must continue your search for Crannog as you battle the Iron Crown at Gwathrenost.Find Crannog in GwathrenostCrannog can be found high in Gwathrenost.

