<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You must complete the quest 'The Fight for Carn Dûm (Daily)' to advance this quest.</rgb>'You have done us a great service, ${PLAYER}.

'I pray the evils that haunt Carn Dûm will at last be banished from our land!'Zóva, advisor to Prince Ingór, has requested your aid in the continued efforts to reclaim Gundabad for all dwarves.<rgb=#FF0000>Complete instances in the Gundabad Instance Cluster for Zóva. Once discovered, use the Instance Finder to enter Gundabad Instances.</rgb>Complete Gundabad instances (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Completed many Gundabad instances.Completed'We must reclaim Gundabad for all dwarves!

'I ask for your help to aid in this effort. If you are able to delve into the dungeons of Gundabad, we will be able to reward your accomplishments.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Complete Instances in the Gundabad Instance Cluster for Zóva. Once discovered, use the Instance Finder to enter Gundabad Instances.</rgb>Your efforts have truly assisted those who work to reclaim the mountain-home of Gundabad. You will certainly be rewarded for your accomplishments.You learned from a gossipmonger in Linhir that King Elessar has begun to tour his kingdom, and indeed has already departed Minas Tirith!According to Pethanar, the gossipmonger of Linhir, King Elessar's travelling party will have reached Imloth Melui by now.Travel to the entrance of Imloth Melui in King's GondorLegolas and Gimli have come with King Elessar's travelling party to Imloth Melui.Talk to Legolas at the entrance to Imloth MeluiTalk to Gimli at the entrance to Imloth MeluiKing Elessar is inside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar inside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth MeluiKing Elessar told you to find his trusted bodyguard somewhere outside the Hall of Lady Vanyalos, in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Find King Elessar's trusted bodyguard somewhere outside the Hall of Lady VanyalosKing Elessar's most trusted bodyguard greets you with a warm smile.Talk to King Elessar's most trusted bodyguardLíniel is outside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui.Talk to Líniel outside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui'I have heard his travelling party will be quite large, so it will take him some time to arrive. The person with whom I spoke said the King intended to first visit Imloth Melui, if you cannot wait for him to reach Linhir.''I have heard his travelling party will be quite large, so it will take him some time to arrive. The person with whom I spoke said the King intended to first visit Imloth Melui, if you cannot wait for him to reach Linhir.''You have returned from your mission of secrecy, ${PLAYER}! What befell you while you walked among the ranks of the enemy?'

You tell Legolas about your experience with the supporters of the Heirs of Castamir and about their conflict with Nakási and Tumúldo, emissaries from the Kindred of the Coins.

'I do not grieve to hear that supporters of the Heirs perished as a result of their own treachery, but they were a known danger and I feel we could manage them easily. We know little or nothing about this Kindred of the Coins, and that does worry me.''We returned to Minas Tirith and told the King what we discovered, ${PLAYER}: that the Heirs of Castamir still have their supporters, but Balakhôr himself is likely dead and no leader has the strength to replace him. Those tidings were enough to convince Elessar that his tour of the kingdom could safely proceed.

'But now you tell us there is another group who wishes Gondor ill? He is not going to turn around now, ${PLAYER}.''We know little or nothing about this Kindred of the Coins, and that does worry me.''Now you tell us there is another group who wishes Gondor ill? The King is not going to turn around now.''My friend! I want to thank you for accompanying Legolas and Gimli on their scouting mission. As you can see, I have at last prevailed at court and now my tour of the kingdom can begin.'

You lower your voice and tell the King what you learned about the Heirs of Castamir and the Kindred of the Coins, concluding at last with the worrisome news that someone near to the King may secretly support the Heirs. He dismisses your worry with a wave.

'I am not troubled by this news, ${PLAYER}. I have brought only those I trust with me on this tour, including my most capable bodyguard.'

You tell Elessar that you already spoke with Legolas and Gimli outside, and he smiles.

'I speak of another, my friend. You will find my fiercest bodyguard somewhere outside the Hall of Lady Vanyalos, near the top of Imloth Melui.''I do not speak of Legolas or Gimli. You will find my fiercest bodyguard somewhere outside the Hall of Lady Vanyalos, near the top of Imloth Melui.''The riddle proved quite easy in the end, ${PLAYER}. Why should I remain behind in Minas Tirith while my husband sees his kingdom? Is it not now my kingdom also? Any who think to do him harm must first consider the daughter of Elrond, and to those who believe her to be defenceless, I say only this: beware!'

You ask Arwen if she knows anything about a "Song of Waves and Wind," and the Queen thinks for a moment.

'I have not heard a song by that name, ${PLAYER}, but songs of every sort have a mysterious power, both upon the singer and upon those who hear them. One of the heralds who travels with the company is a minstrel of some repute; perhaps she has heard this song. Líniel is her name, and she should be outside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui.''I have not heard a song by that name, but songs of every sort have a mysterious power, both upon the singer and upon those who hear them. One of the heralds who travels with the company is a minstrel of some repute; perhaps she has heard this song. Líniel is her name, and she should be outside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui.''The Song of Waves and Wind? I suppose there are a number of melodies to which that name could apply, but I confess to you that nothing comes at once to mind. I hope I have not disappointed Queen Arwen!

'I will keep my ears attentive as the King tours Gondor, ${PLAYER}. Will you be joining us? I hope you will. The travelling company is a large one, but there will always be room for a friend of the King and Queen such as you, and you a hero in your own right, besides! None will think to threaten them while you stand near.'

You thank Líniel for her time, and wonder if she is right.'I do not speak of Legolas or Gimli. You will find my fiercest bodyguard somewhere outside the Hall of Lady Vanyalos, near the top of Imloth Melui.'King Elessar, recently crowned and newly wedded, is eager to heal the hurts of his kingdom and to move Gondor into an age of prosperity.Glindor, King Elessar's herald, is outside the stables in Minas Tirith (Midsummer).Talk to Glindor in Minas Tirith (Midsummer) and go with him to see King ElessarGimli is north-east of the Harlond, in King's Gondor.Talk to Gimli north-east of the Harlond, in King's GondorLegolas is north-east of the Harlond, in King's Gondor.Talk to Legolas north-east of the Harlond, in King's Gondor'A king has returned to Gondor, ${PLAYER}, and all the kingdom celebrates! And yet he is not content to rest on the laurels of his deserved victory over Sauron.

'I was instructed to bring you to see him. Will you come with me?''It is to the Tower of Ecthelion we go, there to audience with King Elessar and Queen Arwen, the rulers of victorious Gondor.''My feet are sore already, ${PLAYER}, and we have come to just the first of the places Aragorn told us to visit. His kingdom is larger than it looks on the maps, and Legolas and I have already crossed it once!'

Gimli falls silent.

'I named him Aragorn again, didn't I? He will have as many names as a Wizard next, but he has been a trusted companion through many adventures, and for that I will learn them all.

'He is not the king of any other kingdoms, is he?''There will be fewer Orcs to fight inside the Harlond this time, I wager.''We have come to the South-gate of the Rammas Echor, through which lies the Harlond, the south-harbour of Minas Tirith. It was held by Orcs of Mordor during the war, and they did not expect the arrival of Elessar aboard the ships of the Corsairs!'

Legolas smiles.

'They did not expect Legolas Greenleaf or Gimli, son of Glóin, either. That much was clear to all!'The War of the Ring left much of Gondor in ruin, and now its people work to restore their lands.Legolas suggested that someone to the south-west must be overseeing the repairs of the Harlond.Talk to Othanar inside the HarlondOthanar asked you to clean up rubble piles, collect wooden planks, and defeat the rats that now infest the Harlond.Clean up rubble piles inside the Harlond (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect wooden planks in the Harlond (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat rats throughout the Harlond (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Othanar oversees the rebuilding of the Harlond by the north-eastern entrance.Legolas is working along the docks south-east of Othanar in the Harlond.Talk to Legolas in the HarlondGimli is in the Harlond, helping Legolas with the rebuilding effort.Talk to Gimli in the HarlondA small crowd of Gondorians speaks in hushed tones to the east of Legolas and Gimli. You should get closer to them and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to their conversation.Berthon speaks to a small crowd of Gondorians by the docks of the Harlond.Talk to Berthon in the HarlondLegolas stands to the west of the whispering Gondorians by the docks of the Harlond.Talk to Legolas in the Harlond'I do not think this expedition of ours will take very long, ${PLAYER}. If there are folk here who believe the Heirs of Castamir will return, they will soon see that King Elessar holds their fortunes dear and seeks to repair the damage done during the war. They will leave the Heirs of Castamir in the past, where they belong, and Gondor will move forward.

'Let us find someone inside the Harlond, to the south-west, who will tell us how we can help repair the damage done to the South-harbour.''Let us find someone inside the Harlond, to the south-west, who will tell us how we can help repair the damage done to the South-harbour.''You are here to help? That is good. We turn none away who desire to help cleanse the South-harbour of the filth left behind by the Orcs of Mordor! The land remembers the scars, but it is up to its people to stitch them up and allow them to heal. Despite many offers of help, there is still plenty to be done.

'Begin by cleaning up rubble piles throughout the Harlond. Earlier today some folk offered to pick up loose wooden planks around the port, and left them leaning upright at several locations to the south-west; if you can gather those for me I would appreciate it.

'There has also been an increase in rats here of late... I think they fled the Corsair ships for new homes, which I would not ordinarily begrudge them, but now the creatures trouble the food-stores we keep here for the workers, and I need them gone. Will you and your fellows complete these tasks for me? You will on behalf of the King? I do not see him, but very well. I care not for whom you do these tasks, so long as they are done.''Begin by cleaning up rubble piles throughout the Harlond. Earlier today some folk offered to pick up loose wooden planks around the port, and left them leaning upright at several locations to the south-west; if you can gather those for me I would appreciate it. And also take care of the rat problem, if you would.''You almost had me believing that King Elessar himself would bring these wooden planks to me, stranger. I am disappointed to see that is not the case.

'But you have my thanks for this work, all the same. Your friends work still, by the docks to the south-east.''Your friends work still, by the docks to the south-east.''How goes the work, ${PLAYER}? Gimli and I have made ourselves useful.

'Some of the folk here seem... distrusting... of Elves and dwarves. It may be caused by fear or by superstition, but most have given us a wide berth even as they keep one eye upon us. It is nothing to me, but Gimli is bothered by it.''Most of these folk have given us a wide berth even as they keep one eye upon us. It is nothing to me, but Gimli is bothered by it.''Bothered by it? You wound me, Legolas! I am no delicate crystal, and can give insult better than any of these cowards... should the need arise, which King Elessar should hope it does not, since we are here on an errand of peace and restoration.

'But has the Elf-hearing of which you often boast abandoned you? For I heard those men speak the name "Balakhôr," and it is not so common a name that it could belong to anyone but the leader of the Heirs of Castamir, about whom we seek tidings!''See that gathering of Gondorians to the east? Approach them and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to their conversation, and you will see I have not invented this worry!''Is this true? Do they really speak of Balakhôr the Scourge?''What do you want, stranger? Don't like what you hear, do you? It doesn't matter to me what you like and what you don't. Gondor changed once, and it can change again; folk like you might want to watch where you stick your noses.

'Friend of mine in Arnach heard the Heirs of Castamir are gathering in strength and will soon return. Their leader Balakhôr survived the fighting and slipped away to safety before he could be executed... such a pity for the upstart king! Now Balakhôr plans to overthrow the new king and his foreign bride. It was them, and people like them, that brought the war upon us: the land remembers it all. Won't be long before it has its revenge upon them, using Balakhôr as its instrument.'

You insist that Balakhôr is very dead, and Berthon snorts.

'That's not what my friend in Arnach says, and I think I'll be listening to him and not to you, stranger.''Now move on. I don't like the smell of you.''That went well, ${PLAYER}.'Some folk of Gondor whisper that Balakhôr the Scourge survived the war and will yet trouble Elessar's kingdom.Legolas believes the three of you should investigate these rumours in the village of Arnach, to the south-west.Travel to the village of Arnach, south-west of the Harlond, in King's GondorBaranor stands in the centre of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Baranor in the centre of ArnachWild beasts and other dangers to Arnach can be found throughout Lossarnach.Defeat wild beasts and other foes of Arnach throughout Lossarnach, in King's Gondor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Baranor stands in the centre of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Baranor in the centre of ArnachAccording to Baranor, some folk of Arnach believe Balakhôr survived the fighting at Pelargir and came ashore south of Arnach.Search for some sign of Balakhôr by the shore south of Arnach, in King's GondorYou found the remnants of an old camp... but how old is it, and who made it?Examine the abandoned camp-fireExamine the bag of dirty clothesExamine the boat among the reeds not far from the camp-fireGimli has joined you by the camp-fire south of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Gimli by the abandoned camp-fire south of ArnachLegolas stands deep in thought near the abandoned camp-fire south of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Legolas by the abandoned camp-fire south of Arnach'Balakhôr died on the deck of his banner-ship, the Night-jewel, at the hands of the Army of the Dead. We saw him die. About whom, then, do these rumours speak? Do the Heirs of Castamir have a new leader?

'We should follow these whispers to Arnach. Perhaps there we will learn from where they began. Arnach is to the south-west of here, in Lossarnach.''We should follow these whispers of Balakhôr to Arnach. Perhaps there we will learn from where they began. Arnach is to the south-west of here, in Lossarnach.''Welcome, my friend, to Arnach! What brings you hither?'

You explain that you seek word of Corsair activity along the coast of Lossarnach, and that some folk of Gondor believe Balakhôr and the Heirs of Castamir still threaten the King's peace. Baranor rubs his chin.

'I do not doubt that some Corsairs fled into the wild when the fighting turned against them, but their numbers must be quite small, shouldn't they? I will ask around the town and see what I can learn. Perhaps while I do, you will take care of some of the foes that do endanger Arnach? There may be some few brigands, as ever, but I speak mainly of wild threats: wolves, avanc, and any beasts that grow bold when unopposed.''I will ask around town and see what I can learn of the Corsairs and about Balakhôr. Perhaps while I do, you will take care of some of the foes that do endanger Arnach? There may be some few brigands, as ever, but I speak mainly of wild threats: wolves, avanc, and any beasts that grow bold when unopposed.''You upheld your side of the bargain? I have done the same. I asked around Arnach, and... well, it seems that a few of the people here have heard that Balakhôr the Scourge survived the fighting at Pelargir and escaped into the wilderness. Some folk believe they saw him personally, though from a distance.

'He supposedly made camp to the south of Arnach, in view of the river. If you wish to learn the truth of it, I suggest you there begin your search.''Some folk believe Balakhôr survived the fighting at Pelargir and made camp to the south of Arnach, in view of the river. If you wish to learn the truth of it, I suggest you there begin your search.''This camp-site might have belonged to anyone. Just because it bears the signs of being a Corsair camp does not mean it was made by Balakhôr. Anyone could have come ashore here.

'Do I need to remind you that we saw him die aboard the Night-jewel? He could not escape from the Dead, and did not. It beggars belief, ${PLAYER}!''Balakhôr is dead. Why do the folk of Gondor have such difficulty believing it?''Gimli is right: this camp might have been made by anyone. Balakhôr did not leave the deck of the Night-jewel alive.

'But this too can be true, ${PLAYER}: it may not matter.'Legolas is concerned that the legacy of Balakhôr the Scourge may still threaten King's Gondor.Baranor is the Captain of the Guardsmen of Arnach, north of the abandoned camp-site in King's Gondor.Talk to Baranor in the centre of ArnachThe Crossings of Erui are located to the south-west of the village of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Travel to the Crossings of Erui, south-west of Arnach, in King's GondorAt the Crossings of Erui you encountered the woman Glassiel, floating baskets on the river.Talk to Glassiel at the Crossings of Erui, in King's GondorGlassiel speaks to you of the Five Sisters of Lebennin.Listen to Glassiel speak of the Five SistersGlassiel speaks to you of the Five Sisters of Lebennin.Listen to Glassiel speak of the Five SistersGlassiel speaks to you of the Five Sisters of Lebennin.Listen to Glassiel speak of the Five SistersGlassiel speaks to you of the Five Sisters of Lebennin.Listen to Glassiel speak of the Five SistersGlassiel speaks to you of the Five Sisters of Lebennin.Talk to Glassiel at the Crossings of Erui, in King's GondorGimli stands at the Crossings of Erui, in King's Gondor.Talk to Gimli at the Crossings of Erui, in King's GondorLegolas stands at the Crossings of Erui, in King's Gondor.Talk to Legolas at the Crossings of Erui, in King's GondorGlassiel is at the Crossings of Erui, in King's Gondor.Talk to Glassiel at the Crossings of Erui, in King's GondorYou agreed to aid the folk of Lebennin by defeating foes between the Crossings of Erui and Halach.Defeat foes on the way to Halach, in King's Gondor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You agreed to make safe the lands between the Crossings of Erui and Glassiel's home of Halach, in King's Gondor. Now you should ensure she arrived safely.Travel to the town of Halach in King's GondorGlassiel stands inside the entrance to Halach.Talk to Glassiel in her home-town of HalachBreglang, a sailor at Halach, has called for your attention.Talk to Breglang in HalachFishing nets can be drawn in along the docks of Halach.Retrieve fishing nets from near the docks of Halach (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect building supply crates within Halach (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Breglang is in the town of Halach, waiting for you to return from your errands.Return to Breglang in the town of Halach, in King's GondorIt is time for you to meet up with Legolas and Gimli in the town of Glaniath, to the north-west.Travel to the town of Glaniath, north-west of Halach in King's GondorLegolas sits on a bench by the entrance to Glaniath, in King's Gondor.Talk to Legolas by the entrance to Glaniath, in King's Gondor'Balakhôr need not have survived to inspire those who believed in his purpose. Is it not possible he might have become, in death, more of a symbol to his people than he could have remained in life? A man can be slain; it is more difficult to fully destroy that for which he stood.

'What if someone has taken his place, ${PLAYER}? What if someone has adopted his name and his appearance in an attempt to rally the Heirs of Castamir? We should tell Baranor of this possibility, so he can keep watch and guard against it.''What if someone has taken Balakhôr's place, adopting his name and appearance in an attempt to rally the Heirs of Castamir? We should tell Baranor of this possibility, so he can keep watch and guard against it.''What have you discovered, ${PLAYER}? Was there any truth to the rumours spoken among the people of Arnach? Did Balakhôr survive the war?'

You recount to Baranor what you discovered, and what Legolas had to say about the fallen leader of the Heirs of Castamir. The guardsman nods grimly.

'Aye, an imposter could cause trouble for King Elessar and for Gondor just as easily as Balakhôr himself might have done. We will remain alert for any who appear, and I trust you will do the same as you continue south and westward, to the Crossings of Erui. There are several small villages beyond the Erui that may foster similar sentiments as those here in Arnach. Good fortune go with you.''We will remain alert for any supporters of the Heirs who appear, and I trust you will do the same as you continue south and westward, to the Crossings of Erui. There are several small villages beyond the Erui that may foster similar sentiments as those here in Arnach. Good fortune go with you.''These are the Crossings of Erui, where Castamir the Usurper perished long ago during the Kin-strife of Gondor, when his rebellion was at last ended, and the rule of the kingdom was restored. But Kings and Stewards rule from distant castles and towers, and our people need the blessing of nearer protectors.

'And so I and other good people of Lebennin float baskets on the river in memory of the Five Sisters, who protected this land in days gone. Some folk believed they were only a myth, but it is not so!''I speak of the Five Sisters of Lebennin, who protect these lands even now.''I speak of the Five Sisters of Lebennin, who protect these lands even now.''The blood spilt in these waters during the Kin-strife of Gondor so changed the Lady of Erui that she became estranged from her sisters, and turned her anger upon the folk who dwelt nearby. Not long ago she raised the Armour of the Usurper from these waters and did battle against her sisters, but at last they were able to soothe her rage and she was healed of her hurts.

'So I float these baskets upon her waters as tokens of both forgiveness and love, and I hope that she takes notice of them.''I hope the Lone Lady of Erui knows she now has the love and belief of all of Lebennin.''It is a good story, so far as it goes. A little unbelievable, I think.'

Gimli stares at you with amazement when you tell him it is true, and you witnessed the end of the tale as you passed through Lebennin during the war.

'Really? I missed more than I thought when I agreed to take the ship from Pelargir with Legolas! What happened to the Armour of the Usurper after the River-maidens calmed their sister of her anger?''I hope I did not miss many other such adventures while I was aboard the ship!''You say Angbor, the Lord of Lamedon, directed that the armour of the Usurper be sent to Pelargir for safekeeping? That was a wise decision, for Castamir's armour has already proven unable to remain lost. Better to keep it under the watchful eye of Sirgon, Lord of Lebennin.

'But a thought occurs to me. If someone were trying to convince others that he was Castamir's successor, or pretending to be Balakhôr himself, he might make use of artifacts such as armour or weapons that once belonged to the Usurper. We should make sure the armour came to Sirgon as Angbor intended.

'But Elessar also wanted us to help the people. Will you see Glassiel safely back to her home? Gimli and I will meet you at Glaniath when you return, and we will proceed together on to Pelargir.''Will you see Glassiel safely back to her home? Gimli and I will meet you at Glaniath when you return, and we will proceed together on to Pelargir.''It is good of you to offer to bring me to my home, but I can find my own way.

'Still, if you wish to be of use to the folk of Lebennin, you can oppose some of the dangers to be found between here and my home of Halach. Wild creatures and the occasional hold-out from the war are still to be found prowling the lands between Erui and Halach.

'Meet me at my home when I have safely arrived and I can put you further to work. I understand you have been given a duty to help us by the King himself?''If you wish to be of use to the folk of Lebennin, you can oppose some of the dangers to be found between here and my home of Halach. Wild creatures and the occasional hold-out from the war are still to be found prowling the lands between Erui and Halach.

'Meet me at my home when I have safely arrived and I can put you further to work. I understand you have been given a duty to help us by the King himself?''You helped make the lands between the Crossings and Halach more safe? I thank you for it! I was not in any danger, for the blessings of the Five Sisters are upon me, and their influence extends into the lands washed by their rivers. Still, it is a welcome deed.

'King Elessar was good to send you to us.''The King will be pleased to hear how you helped the people of Halach, if such is your intent.''Mercy upon me for listening, stranger, but I understand you're here on the King's business? That is a joy to me and mine, for certain, but you might want to know that some of the folk hereabout aren't as true as my family. I've a-heard some talk that the Heirs of Castamir never surrendered, but lie in wait, fostering their strength.

'Seems to me a good way to fight some of these rumours might be to show the King is nearer to us than distance would indicate. Maybe you're here to do just that? If'n you bring in some fishing nets down by the piers, and gather up a couple crates of building materials around town, you might fill the King's sails with wind, and leave the Heirs of Castamir without? Do these tasks for me and I'll share what else I know, and that would be a help to the King.''Bring in some fishing nets down by the piers, and gather up a couple crates of building materials around town, and you might fill the King's sails with wind, and leave the Heirs of Castamir without. Do these tasks for me and I'll share what else I know, and that would be a help to the King.''I thank you for doing the tasks I had set before me, friend! Do not be cross with me, for I put the time you spared me to good use!

'My loyalty is to the King, but some of the men I work beside are not as true to Gondor as I am. I have learned from their speech that the Heirs of Castamir have taken to lighting the beacons of Gondor when they find them unguarded, as a sign to their supporters. I do not know if it is true, but that is what I heard. Look for lit beacons and there will be serpents nearby, snakes who endanger the King and his kingdom.

'At least, that is what I was told. Go from Halach with my thanks, and with the warning I give you!''They say the Heirs of Castamir are still around. If you are loyal to the King, as I am, you will remain on your guard in these lands.''How went your visit to Halach, ${PLAYER}?

'The Heirs of Castamir may never truly disappear, but I think it is becoming clear that their power wanes. An imposter is not as great a threat to Gondor as Balakhôr himself would have been.

'The time approaches when we can return to King Elessar and tell him his tour can proceed.'Legolas and Gimli wish to return soon to Minas Tirith with word of what you discovered, but first they wish to consult with Sirgon, Lord of Lebennin.Arvellon is in the centre of Glaniath, and Legolas believes you should speak with him.Talk to Arvellon in the centre of Glaniath, in King's GondorCollection boxes can be found beside stone buildings on the outskirts of Glaniath.

Arvellon asked you to gather the tokens cast by townspeople of Glaniath in support of his proposal.Arvellon is in the centre of Glaniath, waiting for you to return with the tokens cast in support of his proposal.Talk to Arvellon in the centre of Glaniath, in King's GondorGimli is by the entrance to Glaniath.Talk to Gimli by the entrance to Glaniath, in King's GondorThe port city of Pelargir is to the south-west of Glaniath, in King's Gondor.Travel to the eastern entrance of Pelargir, south-west of Glaniath, in King's GondorCaptain Thandor is in the square by the eastern entrance to Pelargir, in King's Gondor.Talk to Captain Thandor by the eastern entrance to PelargirSirgon's Manor is located down the street to the west of the north-east entrance of Pelargir, in King's Gondor.Enter Sirgon's Manor in Pelargir and find its lordSirgon rests on a bench inside the entrance to his manor.Sirgon rests on a bench inside his manor, thinking about the tidings you brought to him.Wait for Sirgon to speakSirgon has come to a decision, and is ready to speak with you.Talk to Sirgon inside his manorSirgon has asked to meet you in his room of keepsakes, to the north, where he will speak with you further.Meet Sirgon in his room of keepsakes, to the northSirgon stands in his room of keepsakes, appraising his treasured memories.Talk to Sirgon in his room of keepsakesSirgon has suggested that you examine the keepsakes he acquired over a long life at sea.Sirgon considers the treasured memories in his room of keepsakes.Talk to Sirgon inside his room of keepsakesFaltharan stands within his father's room of keepsakes.Talk to Faltharan inside Sirgon's room of keepsakes'While Gimli and I waited for you to arrive, a resident of this town came to speak with us. Arvellon, he is called, and he was most interested to learn we plan to continue on to Pelargir. The matter that occupies his thought may be of interest to King Elessar.

'Speak with Arvellon and see what you think. He should be near the centre of town.''Speak with Arvellon and see what you think. He should be near the centre of town.''You are welcome to Glaniath, my friend, and on the King's business, no less! It is remarkable how much can change in the matter of mere months!

'The war left its mark upon Lebennin, and I expect it has done the same in other areas of Gondor. But here it marked us deep, for Sirgon our lord was captured by the Haradrim, and while he was freed from their bonds the experience changed him. I fear he is no longer suited for rule, and it would be just for the King to permit him to step down. Faltharan, his son, is fully capable of following his father as Lord of Lebennin.

'I have called for families of Glaniath to cast tokens in favour of this proposal. Will you gather these tokens from the collection boxes around town so Lord Sirgon will see how many families support my proposal?''Will you gather tokens from the collection boxes around town so Lord Sirgon will see how many families support my proposal?''So many tokens have been cast in support of my proposal! This is nearly all!

'I want you to understand that folk of Glaniath hold no ill will toward Sirgon in their hearts. His experience was a mournful one, and it is wrong to expect him to carry on without harm. Let him cede the rule of Lebennin to his son Faltharan. That will be best for him, and it will be best for the lands he must govern. I am certain King Elessar will approve, but we must take single steps first. Sirgon served Gondor, and with the war finished he has earned a peaceful rest, and should take it with our gratitude.

'As you are going soon to Pelargir, you will bring these tokens to him and show him the mind of Glaniath's families, yes?''As you are going soon to Pelargir, you will bring these tokens to him and show him the mind of Glaniath's families, yes?''I did not understand this proposal when Arvellon told it to us, and I still do not. He expects Sirgon to simply abandon the lordship of Lebennin because a few families thought he should? No dwarf would do this.

'At any rate, the sooner we go to Pelargir the sooner we will return to Minas Tirith with the tidings we sought. We have heard plenty of whispers and rumours, but little in the way of real danger. I suppose that is good, but it is also a pity, for my axe needs swinging!''At any rate, the sooner we go to Pelargir the sooner we will return to Minas Tirith with the tidings we sought. We have heard plenty of whispers and rumours, but little in the way of real danger. I suppose that is good, but it is also a pity, for my axe needs swinging!''It brings me joy to see you return to Pelargir, my friend, with the spectre of war behind us!

'You wish to see Sirgon, Lord of Lebennin? His manor is down the street to the west. He will be pleased to see you, I imagine, but be warned that his captivity during the war left its mark upon him and his spirit is not as vital as once it was.

'He is still strong and stern, do not mistake me! But he is changed, and weariness lies upon his brow.''You wish to see Sirgon, Lord of Lebennin? His manor is down the street to the west.''Welcome to my home, ${PLAYER}. I offer you what hospitality I can, though it cannot hope to match that of the King in Minas Tirith. What brings you so far from the White City?'

You tell Sirgon that King Elessar has sent you to help heal the hurts of the war and to prepare the way for his own tour of the kingdom.

'That is a worthy purpose. The war... the war may be ended, but the scars it left behind cut deep. How fare the towns within my purview?'

Reluctantly, you tell Sirgon the people of Glaniath have proposed that he step down as Lord of Lebennin and leave the lordship to his son Faltharan, and show him the tokens they cast for that purpose.'Maybe the people of Glaniath are right, ${PLAYER}.

'The days of my youth are far behind me now. Who will follow a leader so easily captured in war, as I was by the Men of Harad? Respect is hard to gain, but easy to lose. There were moments I despaired, and thought Lebennin would be better served if I never returned.'

Sirgon sighs heavily and looks you in the eye.

'This was not supposed to be my life. Meet me in my room of keepsakes, to the north. I want to show you something.''Meet me in my room of keepsakes, to the north. I want to show you something.''I was once the master of the fleets of Gondor, ${PLAYER}. I sailed across the Bay of Belfalas at the direction of the Steward, and brought the might of Gondor to bear wherever it was needed. I remember the smell of the salt on the spray, the cry of the gulls overhead, the wind in my hair, the rocking of the ship's deck beneath my feet.

'When I was held captive during the war, I feared for my family and for the people in my charge... but I am shamed to admit that I also feared I would die on land. I was a man of the sea, ${PLAYER}, but in recent days I have felt penned up in this city, in this kingdom, in my very title. "Lord of Lebennin!" Rather would I be once again the Lord of Ships, the Lord of Tide and Wave!

'See the keepsakes I have gathered in my life, and know what I have come to miss.''See the keepsakes I have gathered in my life, and know what I have come to miss.''So many memories.

'Perhaps my son should succeed me. It took the folk of Glaniath to make me realize something I think I have known for these past few months, but did not express. My heart does not belong on land. I wish to sail again, ${PLAYER}!

'I can better serve King Elessar from the deck of a ship, as I did in prior years. Faltharan should rule in Lebennin.''Faltharan should rule in my stead, if the King will have him.''My father has been seized by one of his moods again, I see. He has always suffered from the call of the gulls, the pull of the sea, but it has been worse in recent days.

'I do not desire to rule in Lebennin, but I will do so if that is my father's wish, and the will of the King.'Faltharan agrees that the Heirs of Castamir might try to steal the Armour of the Usurper, but believes the relic is safe.The Pelargir Armoury is to the south of Sirgon's Manor.Find the Pelargir Armoury south of Sirgon's Manor, in King's GondorThe guardswoman Cuguril stands watch outside the Pelargir Armoury.Talk to Cuguril outside the Pelargir ArmouryCuguril invited you to check on the Armour of the Usurper inside the Pelargir Armoury, as long as you also deal with any of the pests you might find inside.Check that the Armour of the Usurper remains inside the Pelargir ArmouryDefeat pests within the Pelargir Armoury (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cuguril stands watch outside the Pelargir Armoury.Talk to Cuguril outside the Pelargir ArmouryLegolas has had an idea.Talk to Legolas outside the Pelargir ArmouryLegolas has begun a dramatic argument for the benefit of the nearby onlooker, and he needs your help to show that you are truly <rgb=#00FF00>angry</rgb>.

You will be most convincing if you target the Elf and use the <rgb=#00FF00>Angry</rgb> emote.Convince Legolas that you are truly <rgb=#00FF00>angry</rgb>Legolas stands outside the Pelargir Armoury, pretending to be angry with you.Talk to Legolas outside the Pelargir ArmouryThe suspicious onlooker is to the south-east of the Pelargir Armoury, in King's Gondor.Walk by the suspicious onlooker to the south-east and see if she speaks to youThe woman Aithil is south-east of the Pelargir Armoury, in King's Gondor.Talk to Aithil south-east of the Pelargir Armoury, in King's Gondor'That day is not yet here, but still you must tell me what brings you to Pelargir. The Armour of the Usurper? Aye, I know it. Angbor of Lamedon sent it to us several moons ago, charging my father with its safekeeping, and so it is kept safe.

'You are welcome to see for yourself, ${PLAYER}. The armoury of Pelargir is to the south of the manor; with my permission the guards will allow you to pass. They are men and women of true hearts and proven loyalty, and they guard the armoury day and night.''You are welcome to see for yourself. The Armoury of Pelargir is to the south of the manor, and is guarded day and night, but with my permission the guards will allow you to pass.''Ho there, halt.''You have come at the request of Lord Sirgon and his son? What brings three such as you here?'

You tell Cuguril that supporters of the Heirs of Castamir may seek to steal his armour for their own purposes, and the Lord of Lebennin wants to ensure it is kept safe.

'He need not fear on that account, friend! We take great care to keep unknown folk from entering the armoury. Why, a suspicious woman has been watching us from a distance for the past few days; I have yet to see her today. Rest assured we keep an eye upon her when we do see her skulking about.

'You are welcome to go inside and check on the armour yourself. You and your companions would stick in my memory well enough that I do not think you are the sort we need suspect! In gratitude perhaps you will deal with some of the pests within? We have had something of a rat and crawler problem in recent days.''You are welcome to go inside and check on the armour yourself. Perhaps you will deal with some of the pests within? We have had something of a rat and crawler problem in recent days.''Castamir's armour remains where it was left? I told you it was so. But look here!

'The skulking woman is back. She watches us from yonder, pretending that we are not the object of her attention... but it is clear to me she spies upon us even now.''The skulking woman watches us from afar.''I have had an idea, ${PLAYER}. If we stage a performance for the benefit of this spy, we might be able to learn if she works for the Heirs of Castamir, and turn that knowledge to our benefit.'

Legolas smiles at you briefly before the Elf adopts a pained expression.Legolas stares at you with his eyes aflame and his fists clenched. He needs your help to show that you are truly <rgb=#00FF00>angry</rgb>.

You will be most convincing if you target the Elf and use the <rgb=#00FF00>Angry</rgb> emote.'I should have expected no less from such as you!' Legolas exclaims. Then he lowers his voice.

'Now you should stroll most casually in the direction of the skulking spy, my friend. Perhaps she will be interested to speak to you, and if you can draw from her some knowledge of the Heirs of Castamir or their supporters, we will be aided.

'And let there be no hard feelings between us, ${PLAYER}! Such a performance did not come easily for me, but if it serves as effective bait for this suspicious fish it will prove worthwhile. Gimli and I will meet you back at Sirgon's manor after you have spoken with this onlooker. Good fortune go with you!''Gimli and I will meet you back at Sirgon's manor after you have spoken with the spy. Good fortune go with you!''I beg your pardon, stranger, but I could not help but notice your argument with that Elf over the way. Did he give you insult? I have seen such as he treat others with disrespect, and often enough that it has become to me a sad indicator that all of that folk believe themselves to be our betters.'

You tell the woman that he took issue with some statements you made about the new Queen of Gondor, and she studies you closely.

'Is that so?' she asks. 'Perhaps you would like to hear my own thoughts on the subject?'Convinced by your argument with Legolas, the woman Aithil has welcomed you as a supporter of the Heirs of Castamir.The woman Aithil, south-east of the Pelargir Armoury, is convinced that you <rgb=#00FF00>agree</rgb> with the Heirs of Castamir. You should ensure she continues to believe it.Sirgon's Manor is located to the north of the Pelargir Armoury, in King's Gondor.Return to Sirgon's Manor with what you have learnedLegolas and Gimli have come to Sirgon's Manor to await your tidings.Talk to Legolas inside Sirgon's ManorTalk to Gimli inside Sirgon's ManorFaltharan is inside his father's manor.Talk to Faltharan in his father's manorFaltharan told you that Amon Anglebed has been kept burning, and may be the beacon about which the skulking woman spoke.Travel to Amon Anglebed, the beacon outside Ost Anglebed, in King's GondorA man stands by the beacon. Perhaps he is the contact about whom you were told, and if you <rgb=#00FF00>agree</rgb> with him he will reveal himself.<rgb=#00FF00>Agree</rgb> with the man and determine his loyaltiesMaethon stands by the burning beacon of Amon Anglebed, in King's Gondor.Talk to Maethon by the lit beacon, in King's Gondor'I do not begrudge Elessar his choice of wife! Were he not King, he could marry anyone he wished, and he would have my blessing. But if he is to be King, he should put the needs of Gondor ahead of his heart's call. If Gondor is to have a Queen, she should be a woman of Gondor. Does he not know the history of his own kingdom? Eldacar married a north-woman and caused much bloodshed. Does Elessar desire another Kin-strife?

'The scribes named Castamir "the Usurper" for trying to restore Gondor, and he perished for it. But even today there are some who believe in that for which he stood. If you join with them, you may be well-rewarded, and see the return of a King who prizes what Gondor should be.

'One of the beacons of Gondor remains lit, and it casts Castamir's shadow long and far. If you are a friend to those who wish to see it remain so, you will <rgb=#00FF00>agree</rgb> with us. We must act in the shadows, for now, and keep down our voices; you never know who might be watching us.''One of the beacons of Gondor remains lit, and it casts Castamir's shadow long and far. If you are a friend to those who wish to see it remain so, you will <rgb=#00FF00>agree</rgb> with us.''I have two questions for you, my friend. First, was she convinced by our display, and second, is she a supporter of the Heirs of Castamir, as we supposed?

'Yes to both? Very good, ${PLAYER}. I am sorry it fell to you to play this part, but if she believes your interests are aligned with hers, we may be able to follow this thread to its very source, and learn who now leads the Heirs of Castamir.

'You must keep these folk convinced, my friend, for as long as you are able to do so.''If it were up to me, I would have shown this woman the blade of my axe and gotten just as many answers, if not more! But Legolas says that would not be wise. Hmmph. Does it matter where the wisdom comes from, if it might be gotten more swiftly one way than by another?

'If you must be the play-acting spy today, so be it! But when it comes to fighting, as it always does, don't be too slow to set aside the lies and pick up your weapon!

'Legolas and I will return to Minas Tirith to tell the King what we learned on this journey, but you won't be coming with us. You have somewhere else to go, I understand!''Gimli and I will return to Minas Tirith with news of what we have learned, but you still have work to do here.''We will see you after you have learned what you can of these folk, ${PLAYER}.''Legolas and Gimli told me about the woman you met. What were her words? "One of the beacons of Gondor remains lit, and it casts Castamir's shadow long and far?" She may have been speaking in riddles, but I think it is more likely she meant her people keep one of the beacons lit even in the aftermath of the war.

'My scouts have reported smoke on the horizon to the north-west. It is possible Amon Anglebed could be the source of it. That is where you should go if you think to find more supporters of the Heirs.

'But use caution! These could well prove to be dangerous folk, if they suspect your beliefs do not align with theirs. You should continue to convince them you stand beside them, until you have learned what you need.''My scouts have reported smoke on the horizon to the north-west. It is possible Amon Anglebed could be the source of it. That is where you should go if you think to find more supporters of the Heirs. But use caution! These could well prove to be dangerous folk, if they suspect your beliefs do not align with theirs. You should continue to convince them you stand beside them, until you have learned what you need.''Can I help you with something?' he asks carefully, and you respond by noting that the beacon casts a long shadow, like Castamir did in years past. The man smiles broadly.

'You must be the kindred spirit I was told to expect! What a pleasant surprise! Aithil described you to me, but I did not foresee that one of the King's close personal friends could possibly support our mission... and yet here you are! I need not tell you how useful it would be to have you with us, especially if you still have the ear of the King! I hope you have not aired your true feelings and endangered your friendship with him?

'My name is Maethon, and I have been keeping this beacon lit. It is to be a sign that although the war is over, the fighting is not yet done. The guards at Ost Anglebed try to extinguish it as often as they can, but my friends and I re-light it when their attention wanes; they cannot guard it at all hours.'Eager to make use of the help you have offered, Maethon has a number of tasks for you to complete on behalf of the Heirs of Castamir.Maethon stashed a number of parchments to distribute on behalf of the Heirs of Castamir in a crate outside the Hall of Ost Anglebed, to the north-east.Collect Maethon's parchments from the crate outside the Hall of Ost AnglebedResidents of Ost Anglebed can be found throughout the city if you wish to hand out the parchments, <rgb=#00FF00>or</rgb> you can burn Maethon's parchments in the nearby brazier.Residents of Ost Anglebed can be found throughout the city if you wish to hand out the parchments, or you can burn Maethon's parchments in the nearby brazier.Distribute Maethon's parchments in support of the Heirs of Castamir to residents of Ost Anglebed, or burn the parchments in the nearby brazierMaethon will want to hear about your efforts on behalf of the Heirs of Castamir. He should still be at the lit beacon of Amon Anglebed.Return to Maethon at the lit beacon of Amon AnglebedA guardsman of Ost Anglebed, Ladben, stands by the lit beacon.Talk to Ladben at the lit beacon of Amon AnglebedLadben asked you to bring the container of beacon oil to Halwen for safekeeping. Halwen keeps watch over the supplies near the north-east fountain in Ost Anglebed.Pick up the beacon oil at Amon AnglebedMaethon stands beside the stairs to the Hall of Ost Anglebed, calling for you in hushed tones.Talk to Maethon by the stairsMaethon makes himself scarce so you can accomplish his goal.Wait for Maethon to leave before you begin his errandHalwen stands by the supplies near the north-east fountain in Ost Anglebed.Talk to Halwen by the supplies near the north-east fountain in Ost AnglebedHalwen believed your story that Ladben needs a barrel of weapons, and she told you to take one from the other side of the fountain. She warned you that there is a barrel of rusty weapons there as well, so be careful not to bring Ladben weapons of poor quality.Maethon stands below the cliff south of the eastern entrance to Ost Anglebed.Talk to Maethon below the cliff south of the eastern entrance of Ost Anglebed'If we are to advance this cause, we need people to know that Elessar does not speak for all of Gondor. I have written a number of parchments proclaiming that Balakhôr survived the war, and he marshals a force that will rise up against Elessar and his Elf-queen; the people of Gondor need to ready themselves for the coming conflict.'

You ask Maethon where Balakhôr has hidden himself to prepare, and he waves away your question.

'There will be time later to discuss it. For now we need to distribute those papers! I stashed them in a crate outside the steps to the Hall of Ost Anglebed. Retrieve them and hand them out to the people of the city!''We need to distribute those papers! I stashed them in a crate outside the steps to the Hall of Ost Anglebed. Retrieve them and hand them out to the people of the city!''I know you! You performed a great many deeds throughout the kingdom during the war, and your name is spoken of with reverence. It's... that ${RACE}... something. ${PLAYER}? Yes, that's right.

'You don't know anything about the lighting of this beacon, do you? No, you wouldn't. It's just that these troublemakers keep setting it afire whenever we extinguish it, and it's not supposed to be lit except in dire need. Has no one told these miscreants the war is over?

'Maybe you can do me a favour? Bring this beacon oil to Halwen for safekeeping. If we store the oil further away, that may prevent these villains from lighting the beacon every time we douse it. At least it will be made more inconvenient for them.

'Halwen guards the stores by the north-east fountain, in Ost Anglebed.''Maybe you can do me a favour? Bring this beacon oil to Halwen for safekeeping. If we store the oil further away, that may prevent these villains from lighting the beacon every time we douse it. At least it will be made more inconvenient for them.

'Halwen guards the stores by the north-east fountain, in Ost Anglebed.''Sorry I didn't meet you at the beacon, but Ladben was sniffing around.

'The guards really trust you, don't they? It was truly a stroke of good fortune that sent you to us! If you tell Halwen you need a barrel of weapons, I am certain she would give it to you, and happily! Delivering papers is one thing, but we will need weapons if we are to carry out our plans; eventually it will come to fighting. It always does! At least we will know we are in the right, when it comes.

'When you have the barrel of weapons, bring it to me outside the eastern entrance of the city, below the cliffs to the south. Hold on! Wait for me to leave before you speak to Halwen. We do not want to be too suspicious.''What do you need, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Halwen that Ladben asked for you to bring him a barrel of weapons so he could rearm the guards of Ost Anglebed and keep troublemakers from re-lighting the beacon. Halwen nods.

'That has been a real problem of late, and they're using so much oil! What if we had need of it? You can of course bring him the weapons, my friend. There's a barrel of high quality weapons just on the other side of the fountain. Wait, don't go just yet! There's also a barrel of rusty old implements right next to it. Be careful not to bring the wrong one!''Of course you can bring him the weapons, my friend. There's a barrel of high quality weapons just on the other side of the fountain. Wait, don't go just yet! There's also a barrel of rusty old implements right next to it. Be careful not to bring the wrong one!''Over here!''I am grateful for your assistance, my friend, as we all are, or will be, when you meet the rest of us. And I think it will be soon, for there is to be a meeting!

'Our plans have accelerated, and the time to act is approaching!'Maethon told you supporters of the Heirs of Castamir are to meet soon in a secret place in Linhir, and he invited you to attend. This may be a chance to learn more of their plans!Maethon described the location where the supporters of the Heirs of Castamir intend to meet. It is a dwelling in the northern half of the city of Linhir, opposite a fountain and with a bronze door.Find the entrance to the dwelling in the northern half of Linhir where supporters of the Heirs of Castamir intend to meetYou should enter the building by the fountain in the northern half of Linhir and attend the meeting.Enter the building by the fountain in the northern half of Linhir and attend the meetingNakási is outside Linhir, south of the eastern entrance.Talk to Nakási outside LinhirThe Linhir gossipmonger has news, and can be found just inside the eastern entrance to Linhir, in King's Gondor.Talk to the gossipmonger inside the eastern entrance to Linhir'You are welcome to attend the meeting, my new friend. Many of those who share our way of thinking will be in attendance, and they will be eager to meet you!

'Go to the northern half of the town of Linhir and knock on the bronze door opposite the fountain. My name will get you inside. I will see you there!''Go to the northern half of the town of Linhir and knock on the bronze door opposite the fountain. My name will get you inside. I will see you there!''My husband and I were at sea when Balakhôr summoned us to join his fleet as he besieged Gondor. Instead we quit the war and sailed for home, and so were we robbed of our chance to spit upon his corpse; but not before he took something else from me, for my youngest brother died there in service to the Scourge. It meant we were near at hand when the uprising began, and when the waves receded, the Kindred of the Coins ruled in Umbar, and the Heirs of Castamir were only a foetid memory.

'Tumúldo and I swore to fight for the Kindred, and fight we have, but I was not certain that Gondor would have to die until this day. Now, however... I am sure of it. The Sea Shadow hates Gondor, and he has chosen the manner of its doom: the Song of Waves and Wind will spell the end of Elessar's Gondor, and you would be wise to leave the kingdom before it does.'

Nakási falls briefly silent, as if waiting for Tumúldo to speak, but he is dead. She turns cold eyes upon you.

'Today we were allies, ${PLAYER}. The next time we meet it will not be so. This is the only warning I will give to you. Gondor will pay for the death of Tumúldo. A thousand times over it will pay. Do not stand in my way.''Today we were allies, ${PLAYER}. The next time we meet it will not be so. This is the only warning I will give to you. Gondor will pay for the death of Tumúldo. A thousand times over it will pay. Do not stand in my way.''What tidings have I of King Elessar? Can it be you have not already heard?

'He has departed the White City and begins a tour of his kingdom. It may be a few weeks, but he will be coming here if you wish to see him!'Mótsog has told you that he will walk in the world for only one more day before his long life is spent, and he wants to do what he can for the friend he once betrayed.Durin stands in Mótsog's Tomb far below Gundabad, trying to decide what is to be done.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombDurin stands in Mótsog's Tomb far below Gundabad, trying to decide what is to be done.Wait for Durin to come to a decisionDurin stands in Mótsog's Tomb far below Gundabad.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombDurin waits for Mótsog's answer.Talk to Mótsog and learn if he will accept Durin's decisionDurin stands in Mótsog's Tomb.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombMótsog addresses you once again.

'I deem you are his close advisor. Advise him then of this: I will walk in the world for only one more day before my long life is spent. When the sun sets on this day, my breath will be stilled and I will disappear from this place, never to return. In that time I have but one desire. I wish to do whatever my friend commands, for the vigil of so many years has given me this knowledge: Durin is the rightful king of the dwarves.

'He was my first friend, my dear companion, and having made him an enemy I mourned the brother I lost. Now in the span of the single day I have remaining to me I will do what I can to deliver him his kingdom.'Mótsog waits quietly for Durin's decision, and says nothing.'I am conflicted, ${PLAYER}. I am grateful to know now without doubt that I am he who I wished to be... but Mótsog's part in this discovery does not erase his treachery, nor will it cause me to forgive his misdeeds.

'I must think upon what is to be done.'Mótsog waits quietly for Durin's decision, and says nothing.'I have decided, ${PLAYER}.

'Were I not to be king when my father leaves the throne, I could say to Mótsog, "Be gone. Let the sun set upon you, and trouble no longer the dwarves." That would even be just, for the wrongs of the Greymaul Rebellion did not abate when the Sunder-battle ended. No, the injuries left by that conflict remain with us. Do Zhélruka and Longbeard not bristle at the perceived slights of the other, and ponder if something of the Greymaul spirit lives on behind their rival's clenched teeth? Do they not look upon the Stout-axe with distrust, brooding upon his long habitation in Mordor? The lesson of Mótsog was taught to the dwarves early, and did not diminish with the years.

'Were I not to be king when my father releases the crown, I could climb the secret stair to the known levels of Gundabad, leaving Mótsog and what I learned here behind. But I cannot, for I will have the concerns of a king, as I did six times before. Mótsog is a tool to be used, ${PLAYER}, and I will wield this tool against our foes. I will use him to wrest control of the Mountain-home from both Orc and Dragon.

'Surely Hrímil Frost-heart will remember the Greymaul leader who injured her during the Sunder-battle, and without the power of the dragon behind him, the Gabil'akkâ will be able to deal with Gorgar.'

Durin once again addresses the impassive figure in the hidden chamber.

'What say you, Mótsog? Will you spend your last day fighting for the dwarves you once betrayed? I do not command you; I only ask it of you.''What says Mótsog to this?'Mótsog nods solemnly at Durin.

'I swear to fight for you, my friend of old, my king, for as long as I am able. I will deliver to you control of the kingdom I once stole, and if the last rays of the sun vanish behind the mountain before I seize your victory, I will perish with sorrow in my heart, and none before or since will know such grief as I know in my failure.'Mótsog stands silently, and says nothing more.'Well, ${PLAYER}, it seems the conquest of Gundabad will succeed or fail by nightfall. Let us hope we will celebrate the victory before the sun sets on this day!

'I know what I must now do.'Durin has decided to call for Dwarrowmoot, a meeting of all the dwarves, where it will be decided what should be done to secure Gundabad.Durin has asked you to climb from the depths and return to Zul-mazal, where you can begin gathering the dwarves for Dwarrowmoot.Climb the secret stair out of the depths and return to Zul-mazalDurin asked you to bring word to a number of dwarves about the upcoming Dwarrowmoot:

Bósi, Bróin, or Bori at Zul-mazal.
Prince Ingór at Maergrind in Máttugard.
Glóin at the Steepset in Máttugard.
Imák on the western shore of the lake in Gloomingtarn.

These dwarves will select others of their kind to attend the Dwarrowmoot. Time is short!Talk to Bósi, Bróin, or Bori in Zul-mazalTalk to Prince Ingór at Maergrind in MáttugardTalk to Glóin at the Steepset in MáttugardTalk to Imák on the western shore of the lake in GloomingtarnDurin has called for Dwarrowmoot on the south-eastern side of the Thing-fold, in the Welkin-lofts.Meet up with the dwarves on the south-eastern side of the Thing-fold for DwarrowmootDurin is at the Thing-fold in the Welkin-lofts for Dwarrowmoot, but is disappointed that more dwarves have not already arrived.Talk to Durin at the Thing-fold in the Welkin-loftsDurin fears that the foes prowling the Welkin-lofts may have hindered the arrival of his dwarves, and wants you to make the high passes as safe as possible.Defeat Orc-kind in the Welkin-lofts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Angmarim in the Welkin-lofts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Your efforts have enabled more dwarves to arrive for the Dwarrowmoot. Durin is eager to begin.Talk to Durin at the Thing-fold'The time has come for me to call for Dwarrowmoot, a meeting of all the dwarves here in the Mountain-home. It was held at the Thing-fold in the Welkin-lofts whenever dwarves had grave matters to discuss or a need that could not be addressed without a consensus of many dwarf-lords. It was a ritual for the martialing of war or the making of peace, and was common at one time. When Gundabad was briefly reclaimed at the outset of the Sixth War, the first Dwarrowmoot in four thousand years was called at the Fold, and the course of that conflict was decided.

'I will call for Dwarrowmoot once again, ${PLAYER}. There I will explain the final steps we must take to drive Hrímil Frost-heart and her thrall Gorgar from Gundabad. Only then will the Mountain-home be ours again.

'Climb the secret stair back to Zul-mazal. Seek out a leader of the Iron Garrison such as Bósi or his kin, Glóin of the Longbeards, Prince Ingór of the Zhélruka, and Imák the Stout-axe, and tell them to bring their companies of dwarves to the Thing-fold for Dwarrowmoot.''I will call for Dwarrowmoot once again, ${PLAYER}. There I will explain the final steps we must take to drive Hrímil Frost-heart and her thrall Gorgar from Gundabad. Only then will the Mountain-home be ours again.

'Climb the secret stair back to Zul-mazal, ${PLAYER}. Then I ask you to seek out a leader of the Iron Garrison such as Bósi or his kin, Glóin of the Longbeards, Prince Ingór of the Zhélruka, and Imák the Stout-axe, and tell them to bring their companies of dwarves to the Thing-fold for Dwarrowmoot.''I will call for Dwarrowmoot once again, ${PLAYER}. There I will explain the final steps we must take to drive Hrímil Frost-heart and her thrall Gorgar from Gundabad. Only then will the Mountain-home will be ours again.

'Climb the secret stair back to Zul-mazal, ${PLAYER}. Then I ask you to seek out a leader of the Iron Garrison such as Bósi or his kin, Glóin of the Longbeards, Prince Ingór of the Zhélruka, and Imák the Stout-axe, and tell them to bring their companies of dwarves to the Thing-fold for Dwarrowmoot.''${PLAYER}! What did you discover beyond those secret stairs? How fares the prince? Has he need of us? We are ready!'

You tell Bróin that Durin does have need of him, and indeed the prince calls for the rest of his kin to answer his call.

'Dwarrowmoot? Can it be? I will send word to the others, and at once! We shall assemble at the Thing-fold and there await Prince Durin!''Dwarrowmoot, eh? So we come to it at last. Good! The Zhélruka will hear what the Longbeard prince has to say, and we will await our reward when the fighting is done.

'Aye, there will be fighting, make no mistake of that. Durin calls for the dwarves to assemble at the Thing-fold in the Welkin-lofts, after all. Has he not considered how exposed that hillside is to the air? We will be meeting in full view of the peak, from which Hrímil watches over the lands all about.

'One thing is clear to me, ${PLAYER}. Dwarrowmoot will decide the fate of Gundabad, and of us all... one way or another.''Dwarrowmoot! To think I should live to see another! I remember the glory of the last, though I was just a lad. Thráin led a great attack upon the Fold, recovering it from the Orcs for the first time in thousands of years. He called for the lords to determine where the hammer stroke of our war should fall; we left Gundabad when it was decided, and only a small garrison remained. Much might have been different had the Moot chosen otherwise.'

Glóin shakes his head.

'But that was long ago, and now our concerns are with the Mountain-home and not at Khazad-dûm; we need not have our attention divided. I will gather the Longbeards, ${PLAYER}. My prince will have his Moot!''Dwarrowmoot? Is that what it sounds like?

'Well, you must forgive me, but I was not present at the last one! The Stout-axes had other things on their minds, as you might imagine! But I will attend this one. My people should be represented, for this Moot may determine the success of the conquest. There are few of my people here, but they will answer Durin's call.

'Durin can count on my attendance, and on my axe, if it comes to that.''Dwarrowmoot is almost upon us. I will meet you at the Thing-fold in the Welkin-lofts.''Some dwarves have arrived, but not enough... not nearly enough!

'What is taking them so long, ${PLAYER}? Mótsog has less than a day remaining to him, and I am relying upon him to strike fear into the dragon's heart. If the Greymaul is unable to stand with us...

'Perhaps the dwarves are stopping to fight Orcs and Angmarim on the way? Thin their numbers in the Welkin-lofts and diminish the potential distractions for dwarves coming to the Fold, ${PLAYER}!''Thin the numbers of Orcs and Angmarim in the Welkin-lofts and diminish the potential distractions for dwarves coming to the Fold, ${PLAYER}!''That was all we needed, ${PLAYER}, and I thank you for your efforts on behalf of the Gabil'akkâ. More dwarves have arrived!

'It is time for Dwarrowmoot.'It is time at last for the Dwarrowmoot to begin.Durin stands at the Thing-fold, prepared to begin the Dwarrowmoot.Talk to Durin and participate in the DwarrowmootDurin stands near the Thing-fold, readying himself and his people for battle.Talk to Durin before the battle is joined'Are you ready, my friend?''Longbeards! Zhélruka! Stout-axes!

'Hear me now! I call for Dwarrow-moot!''Hrímil's armies approach, ${PLAYER}! Now the fate of Gundabad will be decided!'Durin has entrusted you with accompanying Mótsog to the peak, there to face Hrímil Frost-heart. The prince and all of the Gabil'akkâ will create a path through the enemy forces so you can achieve your mission!At the Thing-fold in the Welkin-lofts, Durin prepares for the battle to come.Talk to Durin and accompany Mótsog to the peak... and to Hrímil Frost-heartGorgar has been defeated and Hrímil Frost-heart has fled on injured wings, abandoning the high places to Durin's army. You have achieved victory!Talk to Durin on the mountain-side'Mótsog must reach the peak, ${PLAYER}. I need you to get him near enough that he can strike the killing blow upon the Dragon. Can you do this?'

You assure Durin that you will see it done.
 
'I entrust no one else with this, my friend. All now depends upon you and the last of the Greymauls. If we do not see each other again, know that it has been an honour to fight alongside you, ${PLAYER}.'

He clasps your hand briefly and raises the Red Axe in quick salute.

'Are you ready?''Go then, and hurry! Our time is running short!

'The Gabil'akkâ will clear a path for you.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'The Dragon's cowardly escape has broken the spirit of her armies! They flee and scatter, seeking for Gorgar to lead them; they know not that he is dead, but the rumour will spread.

'Injured as she is, Hrímil will not prevent us from reclaiming the Mountain-home! Gundabad is ours!'The dwarves have achieved victory in Gundabad, and the Gabil'akkâ celebrates on the mountainside.The Gabil'akkâ celebrates its victory in Gundabad.Talk to Prince IngórTalk to GlóinTalk to BósiThe Gabil'akkâ celebrates its victory in Gundabad.Talk to VenkoTalk to ImákTalk to BoriThe Gabil'akkâ celebrates its victory in Gundabad.Talk to FastrithTalk to BróinThe Gabil'akkâ celebrates its victory in Gundabad.Talk to Stáli the HewerMótsog has stepped away, unnoticed, from the celebrating throng.Find Mótsog down the slope to the westMótsog stands on the slope, watching the setting sun.Talk to Mótsog high above GundabadDurin is up the slope to the east, celebrating the victory of the dwarves.Talk to Durin high above Gundabad'Hrímil Frost-heart is wounded, and we will need to deal with her, but for now let us enjoy the victory!

'What say my dwarves? What say my courageous allies?

'Let all take up the cheer: Gundabad is ours!''It is done, ${PLAYER}. Durin has agreed that the Zhélruka will have a place in Gundabad, and I am satisfied. Let the old rivalries wash away in the torrent of our shared hardships, and let new bonds be forged by the blood we spilled together.

'There will still be work to do to make a home for my people in Gundabad, but I welcome it. If you help us the work will be easier! What say you to this, ${PLAYER}? Good, good. The Zhélruka welcome your assistance!''We have done it, ${PLAYER}! Prince Durin has achieved what he swore to do, and the Mountain-home has been reclaimed!

'Aye, there remains a dragon to manage, but I saw her flight as she flew away, wounded, and I deem more than her pride was injured here. And without Gorgar to command, her grip over Orc-kind will surely be loosened.

'Enjoy the victory, but prepare for the next! It is a Longbeard saying, but all of the Gabil'akkâ would do well to keep it in mind!''We have the victory, ${PLAYER}! Gundabad is ours.'

Bósi nods with satisfaction despite his own injury.

'Brogur would be pleased at our success, but if I know my cousin he would be disappointed that he missed all the fighting. And yet, his son Bróin fought well when the Dwarrowmoot was interrupted, so perhaps fatherly pride would prove a balm for Brogur's heart.'

Bósi peers at you closely.

'Do you know something, my friend? During the battle, while you made your way to the peak, I thought I saw my cousin fighting alongside you. It must have been only a trick of the light, but for a moment I would have sworn it was Brogur. Well, it is a day for battle-shouts and tales of glory, and those were always my cousin's favourite days. Perhaps he could not resist one more.''When we set out for the Wells of Langflood from Skarháld, did you think this is where our journey would lead? I tell you now that I did, for the Zhélruka are noted optimists!'

Venko laughs long and loudly.

'Perhaps not, my friend. But both Durin and Prince Ingór have reached an understanding, and their agreement should end years of tension and conflict. A place will be made for my people in Gundabad, and we will work together with the Longbeards to defend it from any who would seek to take it from us, whether they be Orc or Dragon or any other fearsome foe!

'I will yet have a hearth above which to hang my brothers' swords, and I know both Lékhun and Dúsek would be pleased.''Are all such Dwarrowmoots so eventful, ${PLAYER}? You should have told me you planned to invite Hrímil Frost-heart and her armies!

'But the day belongs to us, and the Dragon has escaped to nurse her wounds. If Durin and the Zhélruka prince know what they are doing they will dispatch courageous warriors to put an end to the threat of her, and fast! Still, this is a great victory, and I intend to celebrate it. I advise you do the same!''You should have seen it, ${PLAYER}! The Orcs pressed in around us and I thought for sure it was the end. But then Fastrith and Bróin came rushing by, and they swung their axes so swiftly and in such concert they seemed to be one dwarf and not two! I have never seen anything like it!

'Their battle-dance was really something. You don't suppose Fastrith has a sister she hasn't mentioned? I never cared much for wooing before, but the sight of their spirited scuffle has made me want to find a deadly dwarf-woman with which to share such joyful combat, and let our enemies beware!

'You let me know if you find one, ${PLAYER}, and I shall tidy my beard and sharpen my axe-blade, the better to impress her!''You made it, ${PLAYER}! We did not have a good view of the peak, so I could not tell exactly what befell you once you departed up the slope, but Bróin assured me that you knew what you were doing.

'As for us, it turns out that Bróin and I complement each other in battle fairly well! I knew what his axe would do even before he did, and he knew the same for mine! I would prefer not to boast, but I daresay the Orcs will not choose to tangle with the two of us for some time! And if they do: beware!

'Once my master Stáli begins to teach me the art of sculpting, I should like to immortalize this battle in stone! Picture this, ${PLAYER}: me and dear Bróin back to back, axes at the ready, while a whirlwind of Orcs and hobgoblins encircle us. Our foes are carved from a single sweep of the rock, with no telling where one enemy ends and another begins! And for a name? Hmmm... perhaps I will call it "The Courtship!'

Fastrith blushes suddenly.

'Well, perhaps. We will see! Names are very difficult, aren't they?''When first I came to Elderslade, I asked you to keep a secret for me, ${PLAYER}. Do you remember? I feared I did not have the courage to fight another war like the one that claimed my father Brogur's life, in dark Khazad-dûm. And I did not. When the Dragon appeared above the Dwarrowmoot, it was as if I was back in the depths of that drowned treasury, scared and alone, but this time there was no Zigilburk near at hand, no hope of survival. I despaired, ${PLAYER}, and I thought to run.'

Bróin stares at his feet. When he looks up at you again, a fire blazes in his eyes.

'But then I saw Fastrith standing near, and a great courage rushed through me, a courage I never thought I could have again. I raised my axe, and without a word the two of us set upon the approaching Orcs, and our battle-dance was like nothing I had ever experienced. I would not have thought it possible! But so it was, and now Gundabad is ours!''This was a great victory for the Longbeards, and for Prince Durin! You know what that means: there will be a tremendous call for me to make statues of everyone involved, heroes in mighty pose with their weapons aloft! I will be kept busy for years, and will need to hollow out the mountain entire to have stone enough! Perhaps I should accelerate the training of my apprentice after all....

'But one thing still confuses me, ${PLAYER}. I am perplexed by the identity of the mysterious dwarf you accompanied to the peak. Do you know who he was?'

You stare at Stáli in disbelief, for surely the sculptor has spent hours working on mosaics depicting the likeness of Mótsog.

'You don't know either, I suppose,' Stáli continues. 'He did seem a regal and powerful figure. I wonder if he knows anything about the Zabad'ibîn?'Mótsog gazes toward the western horizon.'My day is nearly up, friend of my friend. The sun is setting.

'I will rest well with the knowledge that Durin is once again at home in Gundabad, as he should have been. And when he is king, he will lead all the dwarves into a new age of peace and prosperity, as was always his desire.'

Mótsog stands quietly for a moment. When next he speaks, his voice is low, as if he addresses someone you cannot see.

'In all the long years of my sleep, through which I watched all the trials of the dwarves, I have seen the end of countless days. But I have never seen a sunset so beautiful as this.'

Lost in his thoughts, he has forgotten you stand near. You decide to leave him in peace.'I must thank you again, my friend, for all your help. We could not have retaken the Mountain-home without you!'King Elessar and Queen Arwen have begun to tour their kingdom so the wounds inflicted during the War of the Ring might be healed.King Elessar is inside the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in the Imloth Melui guard-houseThe Hall of Lady Vanyalos is to the east of the tower guard-house in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Enter the Hall of Lady Vanyalos in Imloth Melui, King's GondorThe Hall of Lady Vanyalos is to the east of the tower guard-house in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Talk to Ioreth in the Hall of Lady VanyalosThe Hall of Lady Vanyalos is to the east of the tower guard-house in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Talk to Lady Vanyalos in her hallThe Hall of Lady Vanyalos is to the east of the tower guard-house in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Talk to Queen Arwen in the Hall of Lady VanyalosArwen asked you to fill the ceramic bowl she gave you with water from one of the fountains outside the Hall of Lady Vanyalos and with water from one of the falls in the north-western reaches of Imloth Melui.Queen Arwen is in the Hall of Lady Vanyalos, in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Lady Vanyalos is in her hall in Imloth Melui, King's Gondor.Talk to Lady Vanyalos in her hall, in King's GondorKing Elessar is in the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in the tower guard-house in the centre of Imloth Melui, in King's Gondor'Gondor is wide, and the King and Queen will not travel swiftly. They will have reached Imloth Melui by now. Let me speed you upon your way, so you can aid them with utmost haste.''Even in time of peace, Gondor is wide and travel will take some time! After all, the King desires to speak with as many of his folk as he can, doing whatever might be done to ease their burdens after the war. Speak with him inside and learn what he wishes to accomplish in Imloth Melui before continuing his journey.''I too am concerned about the Kindred of the Coins, ${PLAYER}, but whatever power now controls Umbar is beyond the bay; we must first see to the well-being of our people, while we have the time to do so.

'Already we have come to some of the deepest wounds inflicted by the War of the Ring. The Lady Vanyalos dwells here in sorrow; she is the widow of my fighting companion Forlong, who perished on the Pelennor. Her grief is two-fold, for she also survives another: her brother Denethor, victim of Sauron's manipulations.

'Arwen has taken one of the healers who accompanies us, and the two of them have gone to speak with the Lady Vanyalos. Will you attend them and learn if my Queen's administrations have eased her sorrow? The Hall of Lady Vanyalos is to the east.''Arwen has taken one of the healers who accompanies us, and the two of them have gone to speak with the Lady Vanyalos. Will you attend them and learn if my Queen's administrations have eased her sorrow? The Hall of Lady Vanyalos is to the east.''Oh! ${PLAYER}! I was just telling the Lady Vanyalos that all clouds will surely pass, and even in time of grief there will be a new dawning and the sun will shine out in the morn such as you could never expect in the night-time, when all seemed darkest! It is true that these have been sorrowful days, and many tears have been shed, but it is like I always say: there's a remedy for it, you just have to find the right one! And I learned a great deal about healing remedies when I was just a child in Imloth Melui.

'Yes, I grew up here, among all these flowers. Did I ever tell you that? It is true! Why, when I was just a girl, I never expected to see much further than just down the hill or past that curve in the road, and wouldn't you know that I went on to dwell in Minas Tirith, and in the Houses of Healing there would be a great many ailments that would take more than medicine, but also a soothing word or three! It is like I always say...''I am sure the Queen will do whatever she can to comfort Lady Vanyalos. I will do my best, as well! Perhaps a story of some sort to take her mind from her troubles? I know many such tales! But which one will suffice? Oh, that is a difficult decision to make...''The Queen and her healer Ioreth mean well, ${PLAYER}, but they can provide no healing for me. Mine is no ordinary grief! This is not the sorrow of a woman whose husband marched to war and perished there, or that of a woman whose brother ruled a city and fell when it burned.'

The eyes of the Lady Vanyalos shine with tears, but sadness alone does not line her face. Her expression is haunted too by guilt.

'Would that I had no part in this! Then might I grieve as does the rest of Gondor! But I have done as they did not, for I dreamed of what would transpire, and I did nothing to prevent it! It is as if I put the axe in the hand of he who slew my Forlong, or cast the torch that set aflame the city walls behind which my brother Denethor perished! It is as if I, Vanyalos, did these things, by failing to prevent them!''Would that I had no part in this! Then might I grieve as does the rest of Gondor! But I have done as they did not, for I dreamed of what would transpire, and I did nothing to prevent it! It is as if I put the axe in the hand of he who slew my Forlong, or cast the torch that set aflame the city walls behind which my brother Denethor perished! It is as if I, Vanyalos, did these things, by failing to prevent them!''Not all dreams have the meaning you give to them, Vanyalos, but my people know well that some there are to whom the Valar grant the gift of foresight. "Gift," I say, but if so it must be an unwelcome one.'

Vanyalos nods unhappily, and Arwen briefly takes her hand.

'None disbelieve you, sweet woman. Your dreams came to pass in the way you foresaw, and great is the pain to grieve, especially when the grieving must begin before the loss is suffered. But to think you might have prevented what the Valar showed to you... can you really believe it might be so? Does the Lady of Imloth Melui stand among the ranks of such as they?'

The expression of guilt on Vanyalos's face disappears, replaced with shock. Arwen continues.

'Of course not, my dear. Let me help you ease this burden. ${PLAYER}, take this bowl and fill it with water from one of the fountains outside the hall. Then add to it some of the water that falls from the heights in the north-western reaches of Imloth Melui. This will help to soothe the mind of Lady Vanyalos.''Fill the bowl I gave you with water from one of the fountains outside the hall. Then add to it some of the water that falls from the heights in the north-western reaches of Imloth Melui. This will help to soothe the mind of Lady Vanyalos.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}.'

Arwen accepts the ceramic bowl from you, taking care not to spill any of the liquid within. She passes her hand over the water and speaks quietly.

'Idhido i nin hin, a caetho hend,' she says, and hands the bowl to Lady Vanyalos. She indicates the Lady of Lossarnach should take a sip of the cool waters. When Vanyalos has done so, Arwen smiles.

'My friend Ioreth would tell you no remedy acts as quickly as we might wish, but I hope you will find the comfort you deserve. Grieve for your husband Forlong, and for your brother Denethor, but think not to carry the blame of what befell them! In that you are blameless, and the fault lies with Sauron who wove it, or with the Valar who thought such dreams might be of use to you.''My friend Ioreth would tell you no remedy acts as quickly as we might wish, but I hope you will find the comfort you deserve, Vanyalos. Grieve for your husband Forlong, and for your brother Denethor, but think not to carry the blame of what befell them! In that you are blameless, and the fault lies with Sauron who wove it, or with the Valar who thought such dreams might be of use to you.''Is this Elf-magic, my Queen? The water is cool and refreshing, and even as it passed my lips I confess I felt my burdens lift!

'I cannot thank you enough for your kind words, and for this remedy. I will grieve for my family, and keenly, but you are right: if the dreams of Vanyalos were sent by the Powers, none would think her able to turn aside from them, or prevent the horrors so revealed. That would be arrogance unbefitting the Lady of Lossarnach, or indeed any woman of Gondor.

'Thank you for aiding Queen Arwen with this ritual of Elf-magic, ${PLAYER}. I appreciate what you have done, and will not forget it.''I appreciate what you have done, and will not forget it.''I am pleased we were able to help.''How delightful to have been of service! When my Queen asked me to attend her on this tour of the kingdom, I never suspected we would be able to do such good, and so early on the journey! Of course, I never doubted her intent or her ability, but it does remind me of a saying from my youth, when I was just a young girl growing up among the flowers of Imloth Melui. It feels so long ago now, but of course that is natural, is it not? Anyway, this is the saying...'

You should likely return to the King, for once Ioreth begins a story it can often take her a long time to finish it.'Arwen was able to provide some comfort to the Lady Vanyalos? I am pleased to hear it, but it is no surprise to me. She has always had a rare talent for putting folk at ease, which is only one of the reasons for which I am glad she accompanies me on this tour of our kingdom.

'She left me little choice, of course. But it will do Gondor much good to see its Queen.'King Elessar has decided to travel next to the town of Arnach, south of Imloth Melui.The town of Arnach is located to the south of Imloth Melui, in King's Gondor.Travel to the main square of Arnach, south of Imloth Melui, in King's GondorFolk of Arnach gather in the main square of the village, staring at the King and his party.King Elessar is in the main square of the village of Arnach.Talk to Elessar in the main square of ArnachBerry bushes can be found to the south-east of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Pick berries from bushes to the south-east of Arnach (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Elladan stands picking berries to the south-east of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Elladan south-east of Arnach, in King's GondorElladan asked you to begin preparing the meat of the slain deer just to the south-east.Prepare meat of the slain deer to the south-east of ElladanQueen Arwen is hunting south-east of Arnach.Talk to Queen Arwen south-east of Arnach, in King's GondorElrohir is working alongside King Elessar in the village of Arnach, in King's Gondor.King Elessar is working in the village of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in Arnach, King's GondorBaranor is in the centre of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Baranor in the centre of Arnach, in King's GondorKing Elessar is working in the village of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in Arnach, King's Gondor'Lady Vanyalos is not the only resident of Lossarnach who grieves the loss of Forlong; he was a most popular figure, and treated all the folk of the lands within his governance as if they were his own children. Sometimes he treated me in this way, despite the difference in our ages!

'"Good old Forlong," they used to cheer. We fought together on more than one battlefield, ${PLAYER}, and he was always a true friend, and big-hearted. Aye, and his frame grew to match! Good old Forlong. Alas for war!

'The people of Lossarnach will need to provide for themselves until another lord is raised to the station, and I doubt not they will; but it will take time to replace such a hole as Forlong leaves. Let us travel south to Arnach and see what the people there need.''The people of Lossarnach will need to provide for themselves until another lord is raised to the station, and I doubt not they will; but it will take time to replace such a hole as Forlong leaves. Let us travel south to Arnach and see what the people there need.''Is that... is that the King? What is he doing here?

'Have we done something wrong, stranger? What could bring one such as he to our little village? Is he here to punish us? Have we not suffered enough?

'Oh, if only Lord Forlong had returned from the fighting! He would speak to the King and tell him we have done all we should!''That is King Elessar! I would know him anywhere!

'My family attended the royal wedding, stranger. Well, we were among the crowd, but even at so great a distance I could tell the King and his Elf-bride would be so happy in each other's arms, and would rule Gondor fairly and well.

'But I did not expect either of them to come to Arnach! He's going to get mud all over his fine clothes! Oh dear, I am so ashamed!''That is the King? Doesn't he know he is supposed to stay in his tower? What's he doing here in Arnach? I don't think he's a very good King if he doesn't know what he's supposed to be doing.

'We're supposed to be picking berries and hunting deer to fill the store-houses, now that the men have come back from the war. He's not going to do that, surely?''What do the folk of Arnach need us to do, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Elessar that some of the folk believe he is here to punish them, as Kings and Stewards rarely came to their village before, but that others planned to pick berries and hunt for game to fill the village store-house.

'I am not going to punish them! They and their lord did more than could be expected of anyone during these past few months! We will help them with their tasks, my friend, and when we move on we will leave their store-house filled. Is that clear enough?''There should be some berry bushes south-east of the village.''This was my berry bush, ${PLAYER}. No, it is fine. The bounty of all these bushes will end up in the Arnach store-house. Mind not that I picked the finest berries from this bush before you arrived.

'While I chose to pick these berries, my sibling is more of a hunter, and brought down a deer not far from here. Perhaps you can start preparing the meat to bring back to Arnach? The deer should be just to the south-east of here.''My sibling is more of a hunter, and brought down a deer not far from here. Perhaps you can start preparing the meat to bring back to Arnach? The deer should be just to the south-east of here.''I borrowed this bow from my brother Elrohir. I may be Queen, but I think it is important for the folk of Gondor to see that I work to provide for them, as does my husband. Will you return his bow and quiver to him while I finish preparing this meat? It has been some time since I had such a task, but I do know how it is done.

'Elrohir will be somewhere within Arnach, working alongside my husband.''Will you return Elrohir's bow and quiver while I finish preparing this meat? It has been some time since I had such a task, but I do know how it is done. He will be somewhere within Arnach, working alongside my husband.''Ah, I see my sister has finished with the bow and quiver I lent to her. I hope she was not too disappointed not to bring down any prey.'

You tell Elrohir that Arwen is even now preparing the meat from her fruitful hunt, and he laughs.

'Even after so many years she has the skill for it? I had thought residing in Imladris these past years might have driven how it is done from her memory, but I suppose it is not so! She need not prove it to me, though perhaps it is the folk of Gondor she thinks need convincing? Anyway, thank you for the return of my bow and quiver.''She need not prove her hunting skill to me, though perhaps it is the folk of Gondor she thinks need convincing? Thank you once again for the return of my bow and quiver.''Thanks to our efforts, the folk of Arnach will be well provided for in the weeks to come. I hope this will be a comfort to them, and to the memory of Forlong.

'Will you speak with Baranor and learn if there is aught else we can do before departing? Perhaps he will have news of villages in Upper Lebennin that can guide our course?''Will you speak with Baranor and learn if there is aught else we can do before departing? Perhaps he will have news of villages in Upper Lebennin that can guide our course?''We do appreciate the work you have all done for us, of course we do. It is just that... well, the folk of Arnach are a little uncomfortable with their King doing such labour. It is not unwelcome! They are just unused to such things.

'They have taken hard the death of Forlong. If you encountered him in a tavern, he might buy ales for everyone in the place, and spin tales you couldn't help but believe, no matter how incredible they seemed! But he was one of us. Good old Forlong. This King and his Elf-queen... they seem well-intentioned, but it will take time for the people to warm to them. You understand.

'The King wishes to know of news from Upper Lebennin? I have been in contact with Captain Imladir of Tumladen, and he says now the war is over the refuge is emptying, but slowly. Other than that I have heard little.''The King wishes to know of news from Upper Lebennin? I have been in contact with Captain Imladir of Tumladen, and he says now the war is over the refuge is emptying, but slowly. Other than that I have heard little.''Aye, Baranor surely speaks truth: Tumladen must be emptying of refugees now that the War of the Ring has ended and the people of Gondor return to their homes. I would like to hear how that proceeds, but perhaps we need not bring the company there in its entirety.'King Elessar has asked you to proceed on to Tumladen in order to learn of the situation there before meeting up with the rest of the travelling party in Pelargir.Captain Imladir is in the centre of the refuge of Tumladen, located to the west of Arnach, in King's Gondor.Talk to Captain Imladir in the centre of Tumladen, in King's GondorImladir asked you to find the dwarves from Furukzahar where he saw them last, to the south-east.Find the dwarves from Furukzahar to the south-east of Captain Imladir, in TumladenThe dwarves from Furukzahar stand in Tumladen, waiting for a friendly <rgb=#00FF00>bow</rgb> of greeting.An expedition of dwarves has come from Furukzahar with a proposal for the people of Tumladen.Talk to Asitám in Tumladen, King's GondorThe dwarves from Furukzahar intend to survey the debris within Tumladen, up the slope to the north-east.Survey debris within Tumladen up the slope to the north-eastThe dwarves from Furukzahar intend to survey the debris within Tumladen, further up the slope to the north-west.Survey debris within Tumladen further up the slope to the north-westThe dwarves from Furukzahar intend to survey the debris deeper within Tumladen to the north-east.Survey debris deeper within Tumladen to the north-eastA nearby resident of Tumladen stares at the dwarves with anger in his eyes.Survey debris deeper within Tumladen to the north-eastKing Elessar would not appreciate any violence between the Dwarves of Furukzahar and the residents of Tumladen.You have prevented violence between the dwarves from Furukzahar and residents of Tumladen, and should now speak to Asitám.Talk to Asitám in TumladenCaptain Imladir is in the centre of Tumladen, in King's Gondor.Talk to Captain Imladir in Tumladen, King's GondorKing Elessar stands by the eastern docks of Pelargir, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar on the eastern docks of Pelargir, in King's Gondor'I will go with the rest of the travelling party to Pelargir, but I ask that you journey first to Tumladen and speak with Captain Imladir. If he has need of you, do what you can to assist him in my name; when he can assure you that the situation at the refuge is well in hand, then I will see you again at Pelargir. I have much to discuss with Sirgon, as you know, and I would prefer to speak with him sooner instead of later.''I ask that you journey first to Tumladen and speak with Captain Imladir. If he has need of you, do what you can to assist him in my name; when he can assure you that the situation at the refuge is well in hand, then I will see you again at Pelargir. I have much to discuss with Sirgon, as you know, and I would prefer to speak with him sooner instead of later.''I cannot tell you how pleased I am that Lady Terenis has returned to Minas Tirith, now that the need for safe refuge from the war has passed... but there are fresh difficulties to replace the old ones, ${PLAYER}.

'A group of dwarves has come from Furukzahar with a proposal, but I am not certain I have the authority to grant it. Perhaps you will speak with them, since you have come lately from the King? You may better know his mind and what he would think of their proposal.

'They are to the south-east, and intended to survey the buildings of Tumladen.''Perhaps you will speak with the dwarves from Furukzahar, since you have come lately from the King? You may better know his mind and what he would think of their proposal.

'They are to the south-east, and intended to survey the buildings of Tumladen.'The dwarves stare at you warily.'It is a pleasure to make your acquaintance, and I trust you speak with the king's own authority? The captain with whom we spoke earlier did not, and he spent many words telling us so, wasting his time and our own. I am pleased you have taken his place.

'My fellows and I were sent here by Hámisar, ruler of the Temámir folk who dwell in Furukzahar, stronghold of the White Mountains. Long have we worked alongside the people of Gondor, helping to repair and rebuild their cities, but the recent devastation is worse than any in our memory, and requires a good deal more effort and resources if it is to be made right.

'To that end, Hámisar wishes to send more crafters and dedicate more blocks of stone to the work. Defári, Setényil, and I have come to survey the needs of this refuge. Can you assure us that the King of Gondor will give us appropriate payment for any work we here perform?'

You tell Asitám that King Elessar is known for his fair dealings, and he greatly desires that Gondor be restored. The dwarf smiles broadly.

'Then he will not regret hiring the Temámir Dwarves of Furukzahar, my new friend! Let us continue deeper into the refuge and see how much work will be required.''I hope the King of Gondor appreciates your work on his behalf, ${PLAYER}, for most of his folk may not. Would they think to aid us, if our stronghold was directly threatened while their own towns and villages were not? I know the answer, though they believe otherwise. Thank you for helping us here keep the peace.

'I will bring my report back to Hámisar, and will tell him that the King has, for now, agreed to these repairs. Our price will be fair, and the friendship between our folk... well, there is more to be repaired than just stone, it seems. Will you convey an invitation to your King on Hámisar's behalf? He is welcome to visit us at Furukzahar at his earliest convenience. After all, it is one thing to do business between reliable intermediaries such as you and I, but when rulers can meet face to face it seems to me much more can be accomplished, better and more quickly.

'Good day to you, my friend.''Good day to you, my friend.''I am not surprised to hear your report of the dwarves from Furukzahar, for I have heard the mutterings around Tumladen. The folk here wonder why the doors of the dwarves remained shut during the war and only opened when the danger had passed. I say they wonder, but that is not the truth; they know the reason, and resent the dwarves for it. Where was this help when it might have saved lives?

'I know each folk must look first after its own, but we could have used the assistance of Furukzahar before now. It is good you prevented the insults from turning to bloodshed; we have seen enough of it this year, at least, even if the Temámir stayed safely in their mountains and saw none.

'You go now to see King Elessar in Pelargir? Tell him of the agreement you brokered with Furukzahar, and also that the refuge of Tumladen stands strong, its purpose fulfilled, and with good fortune it will not need to be used again for many years.''You go now to see King Elessar in Pelargir? Tell him of the agreement you brokered with Furukzahar, and also that the refuge of Tumladen stands strong, its purpose fulfilled, and with good fortune it will not need to be used again for many years.''It is good to see you back, my friend! How stands the refuge of Tumladen?'

You tell Elessar about the situation in Tumladen and of the expedition of Temámir Dwarves that came there to survey the state of the buildings, as well as the resentment felt toward them by some of the Gondorians.

'I understand their feelings, even as I wish it was not so. Perhaps I should accept the invitation of Furukzahar, ${PLAYER}, in order to repair the relations between my kingdom and theirs. But first there is work to attend to in Pelargir.'King Elessar and Sirgon, Lord of Lebennin, consider the poor state of the Gondorian navy following the events of the War of the Ring.Sirgon stands along the eastern docks of Pelargir, in King's Gondor.Talk to Sirgon on the eastern docks of Pelargir, in King's GondorBrigand toughs on the western side of Pelargir have made problems for Sirgon's shipbuilders, and he wants you to help his son Faltharan deal with them.Defeat brigand toughs on the western side of Pelargir, King's Gondor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Faltharan is to the north-west of the auction house in Pelargir, King's Gondor.Talk to Faltharan north-west of the auction house in Pelargir, King's GondorKing Elessar and Sirgon are overseeing ship repairs in south-eastern Pelargir, King's Gondor.Find King Elessar and Sirgon at the dry dock in south-eastern Pelargir, King's GondorKing Elessar is at the dry dock in south-eastern Pelargir, King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar at the dry dock in Pelargir, King's Gondor'Sirgon has been showing me the state of the Gondorian navy, ${PLAYER}, and I confess to you that it is worse than I feared. The Corsairs of Umbar knew where to strike and with precisely how much force, and the ships that were once the pride of Gondor lie now at the bottom of the bay, or burned along the coast. It is true we seized some of their own vessels, without which we could not have arrived at Minas Tirith in time, but most of those were in poor shape, or are now.

'We do not know how much of Umbar's sea-power has been inherited by the Kindred of the Coins, but unless it is very little we have scant hope of repelling it, should the Kindred cross the bay with ships of their own. I have accepted Sirgon's suggestion that he step down as the Lord of Lebennin in favour of his son Faltharan, but there is a condition: he must supervise the rebuilding of Gondor's ships, and he must do it with utmost haste.''I have accepted Sirgon's suggestion that he step down as the Lord of Lebennin in favour of his son Faltharan, but there is a condition: he must supervise the rebuilding of Gondor's ships, and he must do it with utmost haste.''My King has done me the great honour of allowing me to step down, and I will do the best I can to repay him for it tenfold, as will my son Faltharan! Gondor's fleet is at its lowest ebb now, but I hope to restore its glory to such a height as has not been seen in hundreds of years! Perhaps then I shall set sail once again, as I do in my dreams....

'But I have a task, and it will occupy my every waking moment. My son has his own task, and it involves clearing out some of the unsavoury folk that have taken to preying upon the folk of Pelargir. Ships cannot be built without craftsmen, and craftsmen cannot work if they are being burgled and harassed whenever they cross the city!

'Will you aid my son with his task, and make the port-city more safe for my workers and for their families?''My son has his own task: clearing out the unsavoury folk that have taken to preying upon the folk of Pelargir. Ships cannot be built without craftsmen, and craftsmen cannot work if they are being burgled and harassed whenever they cross the city! Will you aid my son with his task, and make the port-city more safe for my workers and for their families?''There are a lot of brigand toughs on the western side of the city, if you're willing to help us thin their numbers.''I do not know if I have the fortitude to follow my father as the Lord of Lebennin, but I will do my duty as best I can, ${PLAYER}. I have begun by trying to stamp out the brigands with whom my brother Lorvegil sought to seize the city, but they are many and well-hidden. Some may be allies of the Heirs of Castamir, and I have heard whispers that a new power rises in Umbar... I think they might seek to court it, if they can.

'But my father is right. If we are to resist foes from Umbar, we must repair and rebuild our ships. He planned to show the King how the work progresses, and you should catch up with them at the dry dock to the south-east.''If we are to resist foes from Umbar, we must repair and rebuild our ships. My father planned to show the King how the work progresses, and you should catch up with them by the dry dock to the south-east.''The work progresses, but it progresses slowly, and I fear not as quickly as does the situation in Umbar.'Precious timber awaits those who brave the hidden dangers of the ethereal crevasse of Clovengap.

<rgb=#FF0000>Warning: This quest has a one hour time limit. The timer will begin upon accepting the quest.</rgb>Defeat Ilvind or Muzhudh.Defeat IlvindDefeat MuzhudhYou should return to Zádokh Flint-eye and apprise him of your efforts.Talk to Zádokh Flint-eye'Hidden within the crevasse of Clovengap is the precious resource of lumber. The journey there is not an easy one, however, we need the lumber there for our restoration efforts. Are you willing to take on this task?'

