Find a place to hide the map.Find a good place to hide the false mapYou have successfully hidden the map, and should inform Aerin.

Talk to Aerin in Aerthir.Talk to AerinAs you approach, Aerin holds up a finger, bidding you pause to let her finish a passage in the book she's reading. She scans the next few lines and laughs quietly to herself before bending a corner of the page and closing the book shut.

She spends a moment studying your face. You're not readily familiar to her. Maybe you've changed your appearance since your last meeting.

'${PLAYER}!' she recognizes you at long last. 'Have you been well? I certainly have. Though there has been some trouble brewing.'

It doesn't take much to get her to say more.

'Hata Kebir. It's been abandoned these past few weeks, but a few days ago some louts came scrounging around, no doubt hired by the Heirs of Castamir. Firiondur approached them and asked what they were doing here. They said they were here for a map of some kind, and that they weren't leaving until they found it. They even offered him coin if he were willin' to help them!'

She produces a map of Pelargir from her pocket. It's marked with various symbols whose meanings you can only begin to guess at.

'I assume this is the map they were lookin' for. What? Don't give me that look. I went searching through the place after it were abandoned. Anyway, the man says he won't leave until he finds the map, so I say let's give him what he wants.'

She produces another map, remarkably similar to the original, but the symbols are drawn in completely different locations.

'What do you think? I made it myself. If you're inclined to help us, go plant this fake map somewhere in Hata Kebir. If you happen to thrash some of those ruffians while you're at it, I certainly won't be complainin'.'Reluctantly, Aerin tears her gaze from her book and smiles at you.

'Did you do it? Did you hide the map? Oh how delightful! I'm not entirely sure what the purpose of the original map is, but I can tell you that the map that I made points to nothing but sewer entrances! I hope they have fun rooting around in the muck.

'Now, if you'll excuse me, I'd like to see how this duel ends.'

She returns to her reading, quickly forgetting you're there.The Dwarves of the Gabil'akkâ are fighting the Battle of Uzdarulzahar and could use your aid!The Dwarves of the Gabil'akkâ are fighting the Battle of Uzdarulzahar.

You should assist fighting dwarves in Uzdarulzahar by helping them defeat their opponents and then giving them new orders.Assist fighting dwarves by defeating their opponents and delivering new orders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The battle of Uzdarulzahar proceeds!Complete'The Dwarves of the Gabil'akkâ are in the thick of the fighting! Go and aid them wherever you can! We have a battle to win!'You have aided many dwarves. The Battle of Uzdarulzahar is almost won!Explore the many enemy locations to be found within Máttugard.Explore the many enemy locations to be found within Máttugard.Explore BúshatapExplore KhorrozExplore RonglagamExplore Sadauk-kalaExplore BrúvraumExplore TholmuzogExplore Vâr NuchurDurin wants to speak with Mótsog before the day is ended.The entrance to the secret stair that leads to Mótsog's Tomb, deep below Gundabad, is in Zul-mazal.Return to Mótsog's Tomb at the bottom of the secret stair beneath Zul-mazalDurin hurries to Mótsog's Tomb to speak with the last of the Greymauls before the day comes to an end.Wait for Durin to arriveDurin has come to Mótsog's Tomb to speak with the last of the Greymauls before the day is ended.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombDurin has come to Mótsog's Tomb to speak with the last of the Greymauls before the day is ended.Talk to Mótsog and hear his responseDurin is in Mótsog's Tomb, thinking about the words of the last Greymaul.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's TombMótsog is gone, and only a statue remains.Examine the statue of MótsogDurin is in Mótsog's Tomb, thinking about the road that brought him here... and the road that still lies ahead.Talk to Durin in Mótsog's Tomb'Where has Mótsog gone? I wished to speak with him before the day was ended.

'It is not too late, is it? Will you come with me, ${PLAYER}? Let us find him in the chamber beneath Gundabad, if it is not already too late. There is something I wished to ask him, and I will regret it if I do not!''Where has Mótsog gone? I wished to speak with him before the day was ended.

'It is not too late, is it? Will you come with me, ${PLAYER}? Let us find him in the chamber beneath Gundabad, if it is not already too late. There is something I wished to ask him, and I will regret it if I do not!''We are not too late, ${PLAYER}, but I deem his time is short.'

Durin addresses the silent figure.

'Mótsog, I cannot forgive you for the treachery in your past, but I acknowledge the aid you provided to the Gabil'akkâ in the reclamation of Gundabad. For that you have not only my gratitude but that of every dwarf whose heart longed for the Mountain-home to be restored. So too do you have the thanks of dwarves not yet born, who will now know a world where Gundabad will never again be held by the Orcs.'

Durin nods, that business accomplished. When next he speaks his voice is tinged with curiosity and worry.

'This question is for me, Mótsog, not as a prince or a king to be, but as a dwarf only. Did you see in your dream whether I would one day have victory in Khazad-dûm? I would have us reclaim it, as we have with Gundabad... but I cannot tell if only failure awaits us as it did the Iron Garrison, and Balin before.'Durin awaits Mótsog's reply, and does not speak.'My dream is ending, Durin. I will watch no more.

'Your fate is your own, and will be the result of the choices you make. Whether those may lead to good ends or ill will be up to you, and to those who stand with you.

'But I have seen our kind face innumerable trials during the long years of my vigil. It is the belief of this dwarf that you will have success, in this age or the next, though it seem far away and nearly impossible to achieve.

'Farewell, Durin, both my oldest friend and my newest. Farewell.''Good-bye, Mótsog. Once I called you friend, and once enemy. Let us part now as friends, for there will be no chance more to make it otherwise.

'Take your rest, last of the Greymauls, and know that on this, your last day, you were a son of Gundabad, and a proud dwarf.'Durin studies the statue where Mótsog once stood.'He is gone.'

Durin sighs deeply and peers around the now-empty chamber.

'Whatever distant sight was given to Mótsog by the First Crafter is gone with him, ${PLAYER}. I thought he might have advised me one way or the other, and so help me make this choice: should Khazad-dûm stay lost to us, or is it to be my task to reclaim it? But he guided me not.

'And yet, I still feel comforted by his words. Why should that be so? Perhaps it is because it is as he said: those who stand with me will lend me their courage. One day, perhaps, it will fall to me to lead another union of dwarves to the Doors of Durin, and on that day Khazad-dûm will belong to dwarves forevermore.

'In this age or the next, did he say? Let us work together, then, and see that the age that comes will be one of peace and prosperity for all the good folk of this Middle-earth!'

Durin takes one more look around the chamber and hefts the Red Axe in his hand.

'And in the end, perhaps the lesson of Mótsog will be this: not all stories end when they seem to, but rather they continue, and you cannot fully judge one's part in it during the telling! Both good acts and evil shape the world, in Gundabad no more nor less than anywhere else, and it is the duty of good-hearted folk to care for those who dwell in it. Will you take up this duty with me, my friend, and make that the legacy we leave behind?'

You assure Durin that he can rely on you.

