'Eat up!''Eat up! This one is yours!''I assume Zadók asked you to return to him with your compliments? Can I give you some advice?'

You nod, resisting the urge to pat your belly. That meal was really... something.

'Zadók does not take criticism well, and if you did not enjoy that meal, telling him so is going to interfere with the running of the fortress for at least a week, as the dwarves who work in the kitchens try to cheer him up. It has happened before, and it is not a pleasant thing!

'I am not asking you to lie! No, it is simple: if you enjoyed the meal, feel free to return to Zadók with your positive words! But if you did not... can I ask you to share your sentiments with Alem instead? He is usually outside the fortress, looking out over the valley. He taught Zadók the cooking profession, and therefore if he conveys your displeasure it will not be received as poorly. That is not too much trouble, is it, friend? The stomachs of the Temámir of Furukzahar thank you for your discretion!''I am not asking you to lie! No, it is simple: if you enjoyed the meal, feel free to return to Zadók with your positive words! But if you did not... can I ask you to share your sentiments with Alem instead? He is usually outside the fortress, looking out over the valley. He taught Zadók the cooking profession, and therefore if he conveys your displeasure it will not be received as poorly. That is not too much trouble, is it, friend? The stomachs of the Temámir of Furukzahar thank you for your discretion!''You liked it! Oh, I knew you would: folk are always coming in here to tell me how much they enjoyed the meals I prepared for them! I suppose Alem knew what he was doing when he taught me the finer points of cooking! Sometimes I think I cannot possibly live up to his example, so it is nice to hear such kind words as yours. Thank you for them!'

Zadók has a spring in his step. It seems the Kitchens of Furukzahar will continue to bubble along for another week.'Well? How bad was it? Gírma only sends folk to me when Zadók has really messed up the flavours of a dish. You would not think a student of mine could so badly bungle a meal, but Zadók has managed it, from time to time.

'I will do my best to soften the blow, but still he must keep practicing. That is the only way to improve, you must agree. I do not know if Gírma told you, but we cannot allow the work in the Kitchens of Furukzahar to slow to a stop while my student overcomes his self-doubt. He is a skilled cook! It is just that sometimes he leaves the meat on the fire too long, or uses too dull a knife, or adds the wrong blend of spices. It happens to all of us! Yes, even to me. Very, very rarely, but the point stands.

'Thank you for bringing these words to me. I hope your stomach is not too upset by the meal.'A villager wants to know if there are salvageable materials in the nearby bandit camp.A villager wants to know what might be found at the nearby bandit camp.

Investigate the bandit camp west of Dínadab.Examine the lumber pileExamine the clothExamine the barricadeTell Peran that you have examined the supplies at the camp.Talk to Peran'Some bandits have made camp west of here. They have not bothered us here in Dínadab in some time. Could you check on the camp for me? Tell me what you find.''Just as I thought: they have barely anything to their name but the clothes on their backs. Why continue to live out there when they could come home? Lamedon has no shortage of honest work that needs doing.'Corsairs linger in Tathrendalf.Corsairs linger in Tathrendalf.Discover TathrendalfCorsairs linger in Tathrendalf.

Defeat Corsairs.Defeat Corsairs in Tathrendalf (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many Corsairs in Tathrendalf.CompletedThe woman in hand-me-down armour and threadbare boots almost felt bad for the flock of Corsairs laid out on the ground.

Almost.

Her travelling companion, a woman in a grey cloak, got up from the rock she was sitting on and came to stand next to her.

'What was their first mistake?' she asked.

Threadbare Boots pondered the question. "They underestimated us!" she declared. "They saw two travellers and thought six people would be enough to best us." She smiled triumphantly.

'Close, but not quite correct. They did underestimate our strength, yes, but they made a bigger mistake first.' She gave Threadbare Boots a few moments to consider, but the silence stretched a little too long.

'Look at us,' Grey Cloak said. 'Do we look like we are worth robbing? I wear a dull cloak and your armour is scuffed and dented. We travel with light packs that could not hold more than two days' worth of supplies. What could we possibly have that is worth risking a confrontation? It would have been better to save their strength for a better target like a merchant with a cart full of goods.'

'But a merchant would employ an escort," Threadbare Boots retorted.

'And? A beast is only as hale as the prey it devours. A hard fight is worth the risk if the spoils are commensurate. If you are to make it as one of us, you would do well to remember that.'You have defeated many Corsairs in Tathrendalf, making it safer for nearby Lothgobel.Gonodil wants to keep the building efforts organized around Barad Rill.Gonodil has asked you to collect all the worker's tools that have been left abandoned.Collect worker's tools (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should tell Gonodil that you have collected all the tools.

Gonodil can be found in Barad Rill.'What a mess. Ever since the workers were scared off by the hauntings in Gaerlond, their tools have been left scattered about.

