'Hopefully, none are lost.''That takes care of that. Now I can be free of all that clutter. Hopefully the workers return yet to see work on the lighthouse completed.'Dervorin is frustrated with the lack of output from the nearby lumber camp.Dervorin wants to know why lumber production is so slow.

Talk to Dravron about why his camp is not putting out lumber.Talk to DravronDravron mentioned that his leadership of the camp stems from a land claim passed down through his family.

Report this finding to Dervorin in Ethring.Talk to Dervorin'My friend! I have not set eyes upon you since our time together at Minas Tirith. I feared you dead, but I am glad to have been proven wrong.

'I returned home safe some weeks ago, and have been dealing with a frustration ever since. I don't know if you are aware that the Ringló Vale produces much of the timber used in Gondor. Yet I arrived home only to find that the nearby lumber camp has been laying idle! I tried to speak to the owner of the camp, a surly man named Dravron, but he's only offered middling excuses. I could step in as acting Lord of the Vale, but I am loath to wield my power so heavy handedly, at least not yet.

'Say, you are a fine conversationalist. Perhaps he would be more open with you than he would me. Find the camp up the hill, north-east from Ethring. See if he'll tell you why he's dragging his feet up there!''Hmph. Dervorin wishes to know why lumber has not been leaving this camp? It is because it's not profitable enough for me to send any!

'The longer I withhold the product of these forests, the more the price of it will increase. Only when it is guaranteed to make me wealthier than the Lord of the Vale himself will I begin to sell!

'What gives me the right? My family holds a claim to this land per an agreement with the Lords of the Vale from generations ago. Dervorin is welcome to revoke it but without cause, he will lose face. Go tell him that I will operate my lumber camp as I see fit.''He claims to own the land itself per an agreement with my forebears? I find this suspicious....'Erchar and his brothers have turned their efforts to the restoration of Barad Rill.Erchar is worried that a camp of lingering Corsairs are stealing supplies meant for Parth Rest.

The Corsair camp can be found west of Dol Brannor.Defeat Corsairs in Tungobel (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect pillaged supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should deliver the pillaged supplies to Herion at Parth Rest.

Parth Rest can be found to the south-west of Dol Brannor.'Ah, ${PLAYER}!

'It is you! Well met! The restoration efforts are well at hand in Linhir, and so my attention is needed elsewhere.

'Not since I was ambushed here, did I really see the devastation brought upon by the war. My brothers and I have begun relief efforts and plan to restore Dor-en-Ernil.

'As fate would have it, your appearance before me is quite fortuitous. The Corsairs have been pushed back, but some still linger in these lands hindering our ability to transport supplies.

'Scouts have reported a nearby encampment west of the beacon full of these lowly thieves. They are the culprits, I'm sure of it.

'Follow the road west and you'll find them. Take care of any lingering Corsairs and find any supplies that may be useful to us.

'Herion will be expecting them at Parth Rest, his relief camp to the south-west.''${PLAYER}! Erchar sent you?

'That is a blessing. After the battle raged here, and Linhir was made safe, I established this relief camp to organize and route supplies through Dor-en-Ernil all the way to Ringlo Vale.

'Please, if you have it in you to help with relief efforts, you would be most welcome.'Yared is engaged in a contest with some others of his folk, and he is very worried that without your help he will lose.Yared has asked you to pick up the pivotal crate and attempt to win The Stacking Game.Collect the pivotal crateMake your way to the top of Yared's stack of crates and set the pivotal crate there to win The Stacking Game.Yared is waiting for you below.Talk to Yared'You! You there! You look like you might be the sort who hungers for adventure! You must always be climbing about the wilderness, scaling cliffs and leaping from dangerous embankments, yes?'

You tell the dwarf that you have been known to do some of those things from time to time.

'I need your help! You see, we are engaged in a little friendly competition to see which team of dwarves can stack their pile of crates the highest. We call it The Stacking Game, and it is something we came to play in the weeks following the outbreak of war in Gondor, when Hámisar ordered shut the gates of Furukzahar. My team only has two members on it, and we are losing... just as we lost the game before, and the game before that! But since the other team has three... you are free to join us!

'This is the pivotal crate, my friend. I need it placed atop the northernmost stack, and if you can manage it then we win! Otherwise, the height of the stacks remains unchanged and we lose... as usual!''This is the pivotal crate, my friend. I need it placed atop the northernmost stack, and if you can manage it then we win! Otherwise, the height of the stacks remains unchanged and we lose... as usual!''Now climb up there and win The Stacking Game for us!''Very nicely done, my friend! You must tell me your name. ${PLAYER}? I see. It is good to meet you, ${PLAYER}!

'I know this is just a silly game, and we should not let it affect us so much as it does... but there was little else for us to do once Hámisar shut the gates. Now that visitors are coming once again to Furukzahar, perhaps there will be less need for us to play the Stacking Game.

'Thank you again for helping us to win, my new friend! Winning is not everything, of course, but it happens to me so rarely that I must cherish it when it does!'Dejeni, historian of the Temámir, is eager for you to learn more about his people during your stay in Furukzahar.Dejeni's father Mulúgu fought in the Battle of Azanulbizar and will tell you about it if you find him by the throne of Furukzahar.Find Dejeni's father Mulúgu by the throne of FurukzaharMulúgu was sleeping, and you chose not to bother him. Perhaps Dejeni will tell you about the Battle of Azanulbizar instead.Talk to Dejeni in the feast-hall of Furukzahar'Welcome to Furukzahar! The fortress has not had many visitors of late, due to the war in Gondor, but I am pleased to see that Hámisar has ordered the gates open once again. A number of noteworthy visitors have come to the fortress since then, friends from near lands and distant. This is a city of the Temámir, wisest of the dwarves, and we have made this home for ourselves far from our ancestral lands.

'It was not always home to the Temámir. Are you familiar with the Battle of Azanulbizar? Yes, the last great conflict in the War of Dwarves and Orcs. Two hundred years ago it was, but it was so momentous a battle that it is still spoken of by all the clans of the dwarves, including my people! My own father Mulúgu fought in it and achieved glory, when he was young... I am fortunate he survived the fighting, or I would not be here today!

'Do you have an interest in history? You should hear my father's account of the battle from one who fought in it! Seek him by the throne of Furukzahar, for he often goes there to advise Hámisar about matters of importance to our folk.''Do you have an interest in history? You should hear my father Mulúgu's account of the battle from one who fought in it! Seek him by the throne of Furukzahar, for he often goes there to advise Hámisar about matters of importance to our folk.''My father was sleeping? Yes, I should have realized. He is old, and while I treasure his wisdom and the tales he tells, he does spend much of his day sleeping by the throne. Of all the places in Furukzahar, that is his favourite chamber; he told me once it reminds him of the Court of King Jegín the Enduring, ruler of the Temámir Dwarves.

'But I promised you would learn of the Battle of Azanulbizar! More than two hundred years ago, all the dwarf clans answered the summons of King Thráin of the Longbeards, and assembled on the very threshold of Khazad-dûm to answer the violence of the Orcs with vengeance of our own. My father Mulúgu, very young in those days, served King Jegín and fought bravely among the ranks of her Temámir in skirmish after skirmish. Many of those who followed her have now passed on, but my father Mulúgu still lives, as does the King herself; Jegín remains King of the Temámir in our lands far to the south-east.

'Every clan of the dwarves suffered terribly during the war and at Azanulbizar, and King Jegín was not alone in demanding some recompense for the losses her people had suffered. For their service during the War of Dwarves and Orcs, the Longbeards granted to the Temámir some of the mountain strongholds they could no longer maintain, including this fortress. And so Furukzahar became a home for the Temámir. We have gotten along with the neighbouring kingdom of Gondor for many years, and I hope that will continue for years to come.

'The Temámir have always been the wise voyagers of the dwarves, walking long roads and sailing the vast seas in search of opportunity, quite unlike those of other dwarf-families. Like them, however, we are also loyal to our friends and deadly to our enemies. It has been a pleasure to speak with you about my people, and perhaps some day I will introduce you properly to my father. Until then farewell, my fellow student of history!'Lumber is beginning to move out of the Ringló Vale. Best inform the neighbouring regions of this development.Dornach asked you to tell Rusgwen in Calembel that the lumber camp is up and running again.

Talk to Rusgwen in Calembel.Talk to Rusgwen'I have sent word to some of our neighbours that we are resuming production.

