Lord Angbor is in Calembel.'You there! I charge you with assisting Calembel!'

As you draw near, the guard straightens and looks around to see if anyone is listening.

'I recognize you - word of your deeds has even reached those of us who spend most of our days underground. Dúnach, the other guard who works at the prison with me, has gone missing. I cannot leave my post any longer to search for him, but perhaps you could keep an eye out for him?''Greetings, ${PLAYER}. What have you there for me?'

You hand him the accounts book and explain what happened. Angbor's face grows grave and he is silent for a few moments.

'Her methods were wrong, but I must think about how to deal with the situation. In the meantime, I will review the accounts you brought me to see what other rotten apples may be in this barrel. I thank you for your assistance with this.''Relax, I shan't try to run from you. Yes, I killed Dúnach, and I would do it again, even knowing he'd wound me in return. But this cut across my flesh is as to nothing compared to that which he dealt my heart. Dúnach is the reason that the foul murderer Candúr was allowed to break free of Calembel's prison. That cur slayed my husband Horthir on his way to escape towards the mountains! I vowed to avenge Horthir, and so have I done. I will stand trial if I must, but I could not let Dúnach set any more prisoners free. Here, take this.'

She reaches a shaking hand into a pocket and withdraws a small book.

'I searched Dúnach's house one day while he was on duty and found this. It's an accounting of prisoners he has let free in return for bribes. How many other murderers, thieves, and traitors did he allow to slip their bonds?! If we cannot trust the law, we must take what measures we must.

'I will accept judgement, but I beg you bring this book to Lord Angbor and let him be the one who decides my fate. Go now. Eliaril is about to sew me shut and I do not wish for an audience.'Shegai has asked you to retrieve the weapons that were left lying around Furukzahar.Forgotten weapons have been discarded throughout Furukzahar and should be returned to the Armoury.Collect forgotten weapons throughout Furukzahar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Shegai is in the Armoury of Furukzahar.'You are a visitor to Furukzahar, are you not? You could have come little earlier than this, of course, for it was the command of Hámisar that we shut the gates and not allow outsiders within the fortress while Gondor was at war. It was a wise decision, to be sure, but it has left me with something of a problem.

'You see, when Hámisar decreed that no Temámir was to depart the fortress, many of my folk left their weapons lying about. There was to be no need for them, you understand? But now they are scattered all over Furukzahar, and whose job is it to retrieve them?'

Shegai looks at you with a twinkle in his eye.

'Why yes, my new friend. You guessed it on the first try! You will be rewarded for this work, of course.''When Hámisar decreed that no Temámir was to depart the fortress, many of my folk left their weapons lying about. There was to be no need for them, you understand? But now they are scattered all over Furukzahar... waiting for you to retrieve them. I did promise you a reward, of course.''Thank you for bringing me these weapons. You know... not all of the Temámir agreed with Hámisar's decision to shut the gates of Furukzahar during the war. Some of us believed that it was wrong to leave Gondor to stand alone.

'But it was Hámisar's decision to make, and he made it. That is how things have been done for hundreds of years, and our King is far away. Hámisar has the authority in these mountains, and in this fortress.'Defeat many wolves in western King's Gondor.Defeat many wolves in western King's Gondor.Defeat many wolves in western King's GondorDefeat many wolves in western King's Gondor.Defeat many wolves in western King's Gondor.Defeat many wolves in western King's GondorWhile the lumber camp lay dormant, wolves moved into the surrounding woods.Dornach has asked you to thin the number of wolves sharing the forest with his people.

