You tell Berthalend that the Ruthless Dead appeared at the Stone of Erech.

'I see. I hoped they would not, but I feared as much. We still tell tales of the Shadow-men because there is truth to them, but too often the children think they are just that—stories! I am just grateful that you were there to protect her, ${PLAYER}. Now that she is safe, I hope Randis will take this lesson to heart.

'The other children look up to her, after all!'Little Pip has a plan to exact his revenge upon Vexed Meldis.A supplier sells goods in the south-west corner of Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Talk to the supplier in south-west TadrentLittle Pip is by the haystack in question near the northern entrance to Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Bring the shiny golden needle to Little Pip in Tadrent, King's GondorVexed Meldis stands by her home in western Tadrent, King's Gondor.Vexed Meldis stands in shock.Wait for her responseLittle Pip is by the northern entrance of Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Talk to Little Pip by the haystack in Tadrent, King's Gondor'Do you think you can help me play a trick on Vexed Meldis? It's only fair: she lied to us and wasted our time! But what if we tell her that we actually DID find a golden needle in this haystack? Ah-ha, I think we're on to something here!

'Will you go to the supplier here in Tadrent and ask her for a needle? I know that she sells them to tailors, but if you tell her about the trick we want to play on Vexed Meldis, it could be she'll give it to you for free. Bring it back here and I'll fill you in on the next step.''Will you go to the supplier here in Tadrent and ask her for a needle? I know that she sells them to tailors, but if you tell her about the trick we want to play on Vexed Meldis, it could be she'll give it to you for free. Bring it back here and I'll fill you in on the next step.''What can I get you? Vengeance? Oh my!'

The supplier giggles as you explain the situation. When you have finished, she produces a golden needle from among her wares.

'This is not real gold, of course, and it is actually not very suitable for sewing... that is why I haven't sold it. But it does look pretty, and I think it will serve your purpose nicely.''This is perfect!

'Now, here's the important part, friend. Bring this shiny golden needle to Vexed Meldis, and tell her that you found it in this very haystack, and it seems like there are even more just like it hidden inside! We'll turn the tables on her for sure!'

Little Pip doubles over with laughter, recovering just enough to give you back the golden needle.'Bring that shiny golden needle to Vexed Meldis, and tell her that you found it in this very haystack, and it seems like there are even more just like it hidden inside! We'll turn the tables on her for sure!''What? This golden needle... you mean... you and Little Pip actually found this inside that haystack? And there may be others there?

'But... but how can this be?''Thanks for your help, friend! I think today we can count ourselves the victors... unless Vexed Meldis really does find a golden needle in there! If that happens, well... let's just hope that doesn't happen!'

A worried expression crosses Little Pip's face.

'You don't think she will find a needle in there, do you? It is a pretty big haystack, and that supplier did seem to have a number of golden needles available... and she did know what we were planning...

'Oh, it will be fine. Nothing to worry about. I hope! Good day to you, friend.'A young boy named Orthor has disappeared from Ost Lontir.Cenedel has asked you to check Amon Lontir and see whether Orthor is there.

Amon Lontir is located just outside of Ost Lontir.Look for Orthor at Amon LontirYou have found Orthor at Amon Lontir.

You should talk to him.Talk to OrthorOrthor has asked you to deliver an apology note to Mildir.

Mildir can be found in Ost Lontir.'Excuse me -- have you seen my younger brother Orthor? He has brown hair, wears red... no? I swear I've searched for him everywhere!

'Oh, I hope he's not upset with me. Just earlier, I scolded him for playing so many pranks on everyone, especially poor old Mildir.

'I'll keep looking around Ost Lontir but... could you please make sure he isn't at the beacon? If he isn't here or there, I don't know what I'll do...''I'm not upset!'

The boy's puffy, red eyes tell you otherwise.

'I'm sorry, I didn't mean to yell. And I didn't mean to make Cenedel worry.

'I was just... writing a letter to Mr. Mildir. I wanted to say sorry. But he might think I'm trying to bother him. Or that I'm trying to play more pranks.

'Please, could you go back and give my letter to him? It's very important.''This letter is... completely illegible.'

The man cracks a slight smile.

'Thank you for delivering this very important message. I suppose I'll have to go up to the beacon myself to ask Orthor what he wrote. Worry not; I'll make sure to let Cenedel know of his whereabouts.'Mousy Othwen is trying to relax, but the sound of insects is driving her mad.Sickle-flies and angry gnats are a constant irritation for Mousy Othwen, a somewhat eccentric resident of Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Defeat sickle-flies and angry gnats for Mousy Othwen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mousy Othwen is in southern Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Talk to Mousy Othwen in southern Tadrent, in King's Gondor'I wish they would be silent,' the woman says quietly, her voice little more than a whisper. You look around, wondering if she is perhaps referring to the children of Tadrent. She shakes her head, but does not raise her voice.

'It is the insects: the sickle-flies and the angry gnats. Their buzzing is so loud. How can anyone think with that constant racket going on? I have asked the guard to do something, but they have barely lifted a finger to help. Perhaps you can do something about them?''The buzzing of the sickle-flies and angry gnats near Tadrent is so loud. How can anyone think with that constant racket going on? I have asked the guard to do something, but they have barely lifted a finger to help. Perhaps you can do something about them?''You have done so well!' she says, happily but quietly. 'I barely hear any buzzing at all!'

The woman closes her eyes and for a moment it seems she will drift off to sleep. Then she opens one eye and fixes it upon your face.

'It is very quiet now. How will I ever be able to think with this dreadful silence echoing in my head?'

You politely take your leave of Mousy Othwen before she can ask you to release more sickle-flies or angry gnats outside Tadrent.Talk to MalthellamTalk to Malthellam in MorladThe eldest of the wolves invading the Blackroot Vale, Dokhvor and Húlthak, threaten the people of Lancrath and have made the pass of Tarlang's Neck unsafe for travellers.Deer can be found throughout the Blackroot Vale.

Gúrion of Lancrath has posted a bounty seeking skilled hunters to defeat the wolf elders, Dokhvor and Húlthak, who now dwell in Eryn Asgath. You should collect a deer carcass to use as bait.Collect a deer carcassDokhvor and Húlthak can be found in Eryn Asgath in the Blackroot Vale.

You have obtained a deer carcass from one of the many deer of the Blackroot Vale. You should use it as bait to draw out Dokhvor and Húlthak in Eryn Asgath.Place the deer carcass in Eryn Asgath to draw out Dokhvor and HúlthakGúrion can be found in Lancrath in the Blackroot Vale.

You have defeated Dokhvor and Húlthak and taken their pelts. You should deliver the pelts to Gúrion in Lancrath.The posted note advertises a bounty on a pair of elder wolves that have taken up in the Blackroot Vale known as Dokhvor and Húlthak, describing them as 'vicious and bloodthirsty pack-leaders and mates, and the foul parentage of many of the most aggressive wolves in the western reaches of Gondor'. It suggests that those who would seek to hunt Dokhvor and Húlthak should collect a carcass from one of the deer in the Blackroot Vale, as the wolves are notoriously reclusive and will only appear to feast upon a fresh kill.

You notice that the note demands the pelts of Dokhvor and Húlthak as proof of their demise, and it invites those who have fulfilled the bounty to speak to Gúrion in the hamlet of Lancrath.'Hail, my friend. What can I do for you?'

Gúrion notices the pelts you carry, and quickly offers to take them from you.

'I had a feeling you might be the one to fulfil that bounty, ${PLAYER}. Blackroot Vale has no shortage of hunters, but there is far easier prey than wolves and prey that is of greater use to our people... and to our stomachs! Even so, you have done us a great service, and I think we shall all sleep more soundly knowing that Dokhvor and Húlthak no longer lurk just beyond the light of our lanterns in the night.

'You have my thanks!'The terrible spider known as Kurzglok poses a grave threat to the hunters of Sardol.Kurzglok can be found somewhere in the caverns of Lhingorth in the Blackroot Vale.

A bounty posted by Melril of Sardol seeks for a skilled hunter to seek out and defeat the spider, Kurzglok, in the caverns of Lhingroth.Find and defeat Kurzglok in LhingrothMelril can be found in Sardol in the Blackroot Vale.

You have defeated Kurzglok and taken its mandibles. You should deliver them to Melril in Sardol.The posted note advertises a bounty on a spider of Blackroot Vale known as Kurzglok, described as 'a terrifying great spider and devourer of its own kind lurking in the caverns of Lhingroth', and it invites any who claim to have slain Kurzglok to speak with Melril in the hamlet of Sardol.

You notice that a hasty addition has been scrawled in different hand-writing at the bottom of the note, demanding that Kurzglok's mandibles be collected as proof of its defeat.'Hello, ${PLAYER}. What is that you've got there?'

Melril raises an eyebrow with some measure of concern growing on her face. You present Kurzglok's mandibles to her, and her face contorts into an annoyed revulsion.

'I absolutely did not ask for these! But you say the bounty demanded them as proof? Nonsense! One of the other hunters must be playing a prank on me again. They know how much I hate spiders, after all, and if it had been anything else, I'd have dealt with it myself. Well, we'll see who's laughing when I find out who it was!

'Nevertheless, I would have taken you at your word, friend. And so long as Kurzglok won't trouble us any longer, that is enough for me. You have my thanks!'Not all of the Corsairs in Abadec's Watch are willing to continue fighting.Dolvir has asked you to convince war-weary Corsairs to seek him out for help.

You can find weary Corsairs in Abadec's Watch in the south-west corner of Belfalas.You have successfully convinced many weary Corsairs to leave Abadec's Watch.

Dolvir waits for you amongst the trees to the north of the camp. You should talk to him.Talk to Dolvir outside Abadec's Watch'Some of the Corsairs who have flocked to Abadec's Watch seek safety rather than a cause to continue fighting. If you find any Corsairs who seem hesitant to draw their weapons, please convince them to find me. I promise to help them find peace in Gondor, or a safe voyage home to Umbar.''Thank you for helping me convince them, ${PLAYER}. I hope we can put the days of war behind us, for the sake of both of our peoples.'King Elessar and Queen Arwen have crossed into Lamedon, and now their sights are set upon Dol Amroth and the Palace of the Swan.Queen Arwen is inside Galengam's Divine and Delicate Draughts, in Calembel, King's Gondor.Talk to Queen Arwen in Galengam's Divine and Delicate DraughtsKing Elessar is inside Galengam's Divine and Delicate Draughts, in Calembel, King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in Galengam's Divine and Delicate DraughtsRossaran will meet you and the rest of the company at the western entrance south of Dol Amroth, in King's Gondor.Travel to the western entrance south of Dol Amroth, in King's GondorThe Swan-knight Rossaran stands at the western entrance south of Dol Amroth, in King's Gondor, waiting to bring you to Prince Imrahil.Talk to Rossaran at the western entrance south of Dol Amroth and travel with him to see Prince ImrahilPrince Imrahil is inside the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, in King's Gondor.Talk to Prince Imrahil in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor'When I awoke this morning, I did not think I should be made the King's Own Merchant by the afternoon, and yet so it is! Let the passing of the war lead to great wealth and riches for Galengam, personal merchant of the King and Queen of Gondor!''I can hardly believe my good fortune!''Galengam has been promised nothing of the kind by either my husband or myself, but he seems so happy that neither of us have the heart to correct him. He is an excitable man, is he not?''Galengam has been promised nothing of the kind by either my husband or myself, but he seems so happy that neither of us have the heart to correct him. He is an excitable man, is he not?''How were your travels with Caebar, ${PLAYER}? Were you able to help the folk of Dor-en-Ernil and the Ringló Vale?'

You tell Elessar about Caebar's unfortunate homecoming, and he frowns.

'I am sorry to hear that. The war was not easy for anyone, and I sympathize with the plight of Caebar's family even as I believe they treat him unfairly. I can only hope that in time they will open their hearts to him again.

'Our course now is to speak with Prince Imrahil of Dol Amroth. I have sent Rossaran ahead to prepare our way.''Our course now is to speak with Prince Imrahil of Dol Amroth. I will send Rossaran ahead to prepare our way.''Let us go to speak with Prince Imrahil, my friend. I will bring us by the quickest way. After all, I spent many years in service to this city!''I have seen many surprising things in my life, ${PLAYER}, but never did I expect such figures as these to step from the mists of legend into my hall. These are truly days of wonder.

'I have some matters to discuss with King Elessar, but I am pleased you accompanied him to Dol Amroth. I have heard your name recently, and I need you to deal with something personally.'Prince Imrahil wants you to keep an eye on an apparent troublemaker who named you a friend, ensuring he causes no trouble for the city.Beriaudur, leader of the city's guard, can be found south-east of the Palace of the Swan, in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Beriaudur by the steps south-east of the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's GondorA guard keeping watch on Jajax's movements in Dol Amroth is to the east, beyond the market stalls.Find the guard keeping an eye on Jajax to the east, beyond the market stallsThe guard Tholion is distressed about something.Talk to Tholion by the market stallsTholion sent you to check with Lindor, another of his company, in the courtyard to the north.Seek Jajax in the vicinity of Lindor in the courtyard to the northLindor seems to have also misplaced Jajax.Talk to Lindor in the courtyardThe gull-call you heard came from the street to the east and seemed rather out-of-place.Follow the sound of the unusual gull-call down the street to the eastThe gull-call you heard came from this tree-shaded street and seemed quite out-of-place.Look for the source of the strange gull-callJajax stands in a tree-shaded street in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Jajax in the tree-shaded street in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor'Just a few days ago, the City Watch apprehended a man lurking outside the city. He was dressed as one of the Corsairs of Umbar and made no apology for it, but when he named you as a friend the Watch was forced to let him go. I might have commanded otherwise, but the guards had to make a decision and they made it. Holding prisoner a friend of the hero ${PLAYER}... they did not wish to make a mistake such as that.

'I have had them keep a wary eye on this man ever since, just to ensure he causes no disturbance in Dol Amroth, but perhaps it would be best if you speak to him and learn his intentions here. Find Beriaudur, the leader of the Watch, and ask him for the latest report on this Jajax's whereabouts and behaviour. Beriaudur will be south-east of the Palace of the Swan, at the bottom of the steps.''I have had them keep a wary eye on this man ever since, just to ensure he causes no disturbance in Dol Amroth, but perhaps it would be best if you speak to him and learn his intentions here. Find Beriaudur, the leader of the Watch, and ask him for the latest report on this Jajax's whereabouts and behaviour. Beriaudur will be south-east of the Palace of the Swan, by the bottom of the steps.''You have just missed the report from one of my guards concerning this man! If you hurry, you might catch up to the pair of them. Look for Tholion to the east and beyond the market stalls, where he said this Jajax lingered.''You have just missed the report from one of my guards concerning this man! If you hurry, you might catch up to the pair of them. Look for Tholion to the east beyond the market stalls, where he said this Jajax lingered.''He was right here! I don't know how he gave me the slip!

'What if you check in the courtyard to the north? Perhaps Lindor saw which way he went? Captain Beriaudur is not going to be happy about this...''What if you check in the courtyard to the north? Perhaps Lindor saw which way he went? Captain Beriaudur is not going to be happy about this...''Nothing!''I am at a loss. We watched this fellow from a goodly distance for the better part of three hours, and he made no suspicious movements or gave any sign that he knew he was observed. And then in a snap he just vanished! Are we certain he is a reformed Corsair, and not some irksome water spirit instead?'

Knowing what you know of Jajax, you hesitate to describe him as "reformed." Instead you commiserate with Lindor about having lost him.

'Aye. It is to be hoped Captain Beriaudur will not be too cross with us. I will go to him now with the hope... no, the certainty... that we will find this Jajax again before he causes any serious trouble.''It is to be hoped Captain Beriaudur will not be too cross with us. I will go to him now with the hope... no, the certainty... that we will find this Jajax again before he causes any serious trouble.''Those guards are fortunate I chose to do no more than merely embarrass them... I could have slain them very easily, if I wanted. But you are a friend, so they can live for today.

'To think you believed our paths would never cross again, ${PLAYER}. It seems you have the pleasure of entering my story at least once more!'You have encountered the Corsair Jajax in Dol Amroth and he has a tale to tell.Jajax stands on a tree-shaded street in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Jajax in Dol Amroth and hear his storyJajax stands on a tree-shaded street in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Jajax in Dol Amroth, in King's GondorPrince Imrahil is in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Prince Imrahil in the Palace of the Swan, King's Gondor'Much has happened to me since I saw you last. Do you want to hear of my adventures?''My brother Daxamat and I believed we were done with Gondor when we bid you farewell, ${PLAYER}. I thought we would return briefly home, to Umbar, and after finding a new ship and a new crew we would set sail once again in search of treasure.

'It was a fine plan, but... well, that is the story, is it not?''It was oar-work alone that allowed us to escape, but if good fortune permitted it, ill fortune was soon to follow. A sudden storm rose up and swamped the boat, and when at last I came to shore it was back to Gondor I had been delivered. Of Dax there was no sign. I am certain my brother lives, for he has survived much worse than that, sturdy crab that he is.'

You tell Jajax that Prince Imrahil and King Elessar should be told of the situation he encountered in Umbar, and the Corsair tilts his head, weighing your suggestion against the potential for reward.

'It is good to be owed favours and coin by Kings and Princes, ${PLAYER}. I accept your offer. I have not been the guest of a royal court such as this before. Well, not an invited one, anyway.''It is good to be owed favours and coin by Kings and Princes, ${PLAYER}. I accept your offer. I have not been the guest of a royal court such as this before. Well, not an invited one, anyway.''I said to keep an eye on him, not to bring him to my court, ${PLAYER}. Why should I trust these things he says of Umbar, or of the Kindred of the Coins? How do we know he is not one of them?

'Why should I trust one of the Corsairs who so troubled our coasts during the war, and who continues to do so even now? Have you taken leave of your senses?'A tense situation arises in the Palace of the Swan with the arrival of Jajax.Captain Maeral is in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Captain Maeral in the Palace of the Swan, King's GondorJajax is in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Jajax in the Palace of the Swan, King's GondorPrince Imrahil is in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Prince Imrahil in the Palace of the Swan, King's GondorKing Elessar is in the Palace of the Swan, in King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in the Palace of the Swan, King's GondorJajax is in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Jajax in the Palace of the Swan, King's GondorJajax has decided to leave.Wait for Jajax to leavePrince Imrahil is in the Palace of the Swan in Dol Amroth, King's Gondor.Talk to Prince Imrahil in the Palace of the Swan, King's Gondor'Evil tidings have come to Dol Amroth from the coastline of Anfalas, tidings that harden my heart against such men as this Jajax. But you need not hear of them from me. Speak instead to Captain Maeral, for he left Outer Gondor just days ago... afoot and without ship. Tell ${PLAYER} why this was your manner of travel, Captain.''You need not hear these tidings from me. Speak instead to Captain Maeral, for he left Outer Gondor just days ago... afoot and without ship. Tell ${PLAYER} why this was your manner of travel, Captain.''If you command me to speak, my Prince, I will, but the pain is fresh and the losses I suffered there will haunt me to the end of my days. I came afoot because my ship, the Alphroval, was burned off the coast of Anfalas by Corsairs of Umbar just days ago. Some of my crew survived and made the journey back to Dol Amroth overland with me, but too many good sailors died to fire and water and Corsair blade.

'I demand to know why you now host in this palace one of the monsters who set my ship aflame!''What says this villain to that?''You can tell this weepy old sailor that I was not involved with the burning of his precious ship, ${PLAYER}, and my proof is that he still lives. If I had been there, he and his crew would have been spared...'

Jajax grins.

'Aye, they would have been spared... spared the walk back to this city! Tell him this, too: I did not burn his ship, but if he gets another he would be wise to steer it far from Jajax in the future!'Jajax says nothing further, but he looks very satisfied with himself.'A pox on all Corsairs... but especially this grinning villain!''Silence! I will allow no fighting in the Palace of the Swan!

'King Elessar, these tidings of Anfalas make it clear to me that you should make a change in the plan we discussed. If the Corsairs of Umbar remain active along the coast of Langstrand, it is too dangerous for you to continue that way. Instead you should make preparations to return to Minas Tirith. I will allocate a number of swan-knights to attend you on the journey, if you so desire.''The King should make preparations to return to Minas Tirith.''I value your words, Prince Imrahil, but I cannot heed the warning they contain. The suffering of Anfalas makes it a more necessary destination for me, not a lesser one. I will see the state of things in Outer Gondor with my own eyes, and I will aid the people there as best I can, despite the danger... or perhaps because of it.''I will not change my plan. I will go to see the state of things in Anfalas.''Halt! Someone stop that man.''The Prince wants to know where I am going? That is my own concern. Being his guest has already grown tiresome.

'Farewell, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps I will see you again. Perhaps not.''I will not change my plan. I will go to see the state of things in Anfalas.''He may not have been responsible for the burning of the Alphroval, ${PLAYER}, but I think it need not matter. How many good men do you think he has slain during his life as a Corsair? Whether he fights on behalf of the Heirs of Castamir or the Kindred of the Coins or for himself, does it really matter to the men whose lives he takes, or whose ships he burns?

'Watch that one carefully if your paths cross again.'Hearing about Corsair activity along the coast of Anfalas, King Elessar is more determined than ever that his journey across Gondor should continue.King Elessar is in the Palace of the Swan, King's Gondor.Talk to King Elessar in the Palace of the Swan, King's GondorKing Elessar has given you a choice of road north into the Blackroot Vale: either to fight your way through Tarlang's Crown, or to take the longer, safer road through Lamedon.Defeat enemies in Lamedon and Blackroot Vale, King's Gondor West (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Travel through Calembel in King's GondorTravel through Lothgobel in King's GondorTravel through Dínadab in King's GondorKing Elessar suggested that you travel to the Hill of Erech in the Blackroot Vale, in King's Gondor.Travel to the base of the Hill of Erech in the Blackroot Vale, King's GondorLegolas stands at the base of the Hill of Erech.Talk to Legolas at the base of the Hill of Erech, in King's Gondor'If I cannot convince you not to travel to Anfalas while it remains threatened by Corsairs of unknown loyalties, my King, I beg that you will consider delaying long enough to prepare for the journey. The store-houses and armouries of Dol Amroth are yours to make use of as you will. Take the time to prepare, for I will not have it said that I sent you on to the outer fiefs without adequate supplies or warning!''If I cannot convince you not to travel to Anfalas while it remains threatened by Corsairs of unknown loyalties, my King, I beg that you will consider delaying long enough to prepare for the journey. The store-houses and armouries of Dol Amroth are yours to make use of as you will. Take the time to prepare, for I will not have it said that I sent you on to the outer fiefs without adequate supplies or warning!''I understand Prince Imrahil's concerns. Let this, then, be the compromise.

'I will remain for a few days' time in Dol Amroth, preparing to travel to Anfalas, but some of the company will instead journey north to the Blackroot Vale. The people there, and their lord Duinhir, lost much in the defence of Gondor. After the folk of the Vale have had some of their needs met and their concerns heard, this smaller group will rejoin the larger travelling party outside Anfalas, and we will together visit the Langstrand.

'You will go to Blackroot Vale, ${PLAYER}? With you I send Legolas, Gimli, and my scribe Caebar. There are two roads by which you may reach the Vale, but I leave to you the choice of which to take: the shorter but more dangerous road through Tarlang's Crown, or the longer but safer road through Lamedon to the east, through Calembel, Lothgobel, and Dínadab. Whichever road you choose, your destination will be easily seen from afar: the Hill of Erech towers above the Blackroot Vale.''You will go to Blackroot Vale, ${PLAYER}? With you I send Legolas, Gimli, and my scribe Caebar. There are two roads by which you may reach the Vale, but I leave to you the choice of which to take: the shorter but more dangerous road through Tarlang's Crown, or the longer but safer road through Lamedon to the east, through Calembel, Lothgobel, and Dínadab. Whichever road you choose, your destination will be easily seen from afar: the Hill of Erech towers above the Blackroot Vale.''I have said what I can. At least the King will take the time to prepare before he comes within reach of the Corsairs that pillage Anfalas.''Whichever road you choose, your destination will be easily seen from afar: the Hill of Erech towers above the Blackroot Vale.''The Hill of Erech. We have seen it before, but in different circumstance.''The Hill of Erech. We have seen it before, but in different circumstance.'The memory of losses suffered during the War of the Ring remains to trouble the people of the Blackroot Vale.Caebar stands at the base of the Hill of Erech, in King's Gondor.Talk to Caebar at the base of the Hill of Erech, in King's GondorGimli stands at the base of the Hill of Erech, in King's Gondor.Talk to Gimli at the base of the Hill of Erech, in King's GondorTheir oaths forever broken, the Ruthless Dead now haunt the Blackroot Vale with renewed hatred for the living. Gimli thinks you should inconvenience them, since he isn't certain they can be slain. Ruthless Dead can be found atop the Hill of Erech at night, and on the road to the Paths of the Dead at any time.Inconvenience Ruthless Dead in the Blackroot Vale, in King's Gondor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rosfin, wife of Duinhir, stands outside their home at the highest point of Morlad, in King's Gondor.Talk to Rosfin outside her home in Morlad, in King's GondorDuinhir stands by his wife Rosfin in Morlad, in King's Gondor.Talk to Duinhir in Morlad, in King's GondorYou should hear the concerns of the folk of Morlad, in King's Gondor.Talk to the surly Morlad resident down the hill to the northYou should hear the concerns of the folk of Morlad, in King's Gondor.Talk to the angry Morlad resident down the hill to the eastCaebar is south-west of the entrance to Morlad, in King's Gondor.Talk to Caebar south-west of the entrance to Morlad in King's GondorLegolas and Gimli are to the west of the entrance to Morlad, in King's Gondor.Find Legolas and Gimli to the west of Morlad in King's GondorLegolas and Gimli stand at the ready to the west of Morlad.Examine the arrow embedded in the treeTalk to Legolas west of Morlad, in King's GondorTalk to Gimli west of Morlad in King's GondorDuinhir stands outside his home at the highest point of Morlad, in King's Gondor.Talk to Duinhir outside his home in Morlad, in King's Gondor'I do not wish to climb this hill again, ${PLAYER}; the memory is grim. When last we stood atop the summit, a great host gathered about King Elessar. They were those who broke their oath to Isildur, and they came to his descendant that they might fulfil it, and have peace.

'Many were the shades of dead Men that followed in our train when we departed Erech, and they were the fighters who wrested control of Pelargir for the Grey Host. Without this army of the Dead, we could not have seized the Corsair ships that bore us to the Harlond, and Minas Tirith.

'The shades who gathered about King Elessar atop this Hill redeemed their oath, and recovered their honour. Should that make it pleasing to recall for we who were there? I do not believe it does. Speak with Caebar and Gimli, and afterwards we will proceed to Morlad on King Elessar's business.''The shades who gathered about King Elessar atop this Hill redeemed their oath, and recovered their honour. Should that make it pleasing to recall for we who were there? I do not believe it does. Speak with Caebar and Gimli, and afterwards we will proceed to Morlad on King Elessar's business.''This story that Legolas and Gimli speak about... it fills me with fright, ${PLAYER}! I am gratified to learn that the King made use of the shades that betrayed his mighty forebear, and so gave them the redemption that was once denied them, but...'

Caebar frowns deeply and casts nervous glances toward the summit of the Hill of Erech.

'You say that some of the shades did not fight on behalf of the King? How ruthless must those dead Men now be, knowing there is to be no redemption for them? How much must they hate the living, now that the chance for peace has come and passed them by?

'I do not want to go up there. King Elessar would understand if we do not, surely!''I do not want to go up there. King Elessar would understand if we do not, surely!''I am not afraid, you understand, but there is little reason for us to climb again to the summit.

'The King would expect us to slay any such members of the Ruthless Dead that we encounter in the Vale... or whatever passes for slaying such spirits. I am not entirely clear on that point. Legolas has tried to explain it to me, but it all seems rather complicated.

'What do I know? I know that if any of the Ruthless Dead think to trouble us or the folk who live nearby, we will inconvenience them. We will inconvenience them with sword or bow or axe, and let them return if they dare!

'They are sure to haunt the Hill of Erech at night, but they will be along the road to the Paths of the Dead at all times, I wager!''The King would expect us to slay any such members of the Ruthless Dead that we encounter in the Vale... or whatever passes for slaying such spirits. I am not entirely clear on that point. Legolas has tried to explain it to me, but it all seems rather complicated.

'What do I know? I know that if any of the Ruthless Dead think to trouble us or the folk who live nearby, we will inconvenience them. We will inconvenience them with sword or bow or axe, and let them return if they dare!

'They are sure to haunt the Hill of Erech at night, but they will be along the road to the Paths of the Dead at all times, I wager!''We should now go to Morlad. That is the main settlement in the Blackroot Vale, is it not?''Oh, ${PLAYER}, a pall lies upon the Blackroot Vale, for it was caught in the grip of war, and all her young men were drawn away eastward. My sons, too, were caught in the whirlpool, and Duilin and Derufin will not come home again. My husband Duinhir thinks to spare me the details of what befell my boys... but there is evil in not knowing! If only I knew they perished quickly, and without pain...'

Lady Rosfin entreats you through eyes filled with tears, but when you hesitate she looks away.

'No, I will not ask it of you. I know Duinhir thinks to spare my feelings, the hard-headed man! I am stronger than he knows, and have always been so! It is not lost on me that he was not able to save our sons from death, he with his strong sword-arm and his thick shield, and I was forced to remain behind in Morlad; I, who might have been able to keep them safe!

'Life can be cruel, ${PLAYER}, but it is crueller still for a mother in time of war. I keep thinking I will hear the sound of my sons boasting and laughing on the wind, and then I will see Duilin and Derufin climbing the path through the village, alive and well and happy. But it is not to be so.'Lady Rosfin looks at you with sad, cold eyes, and says nothing.'I have told Rosfin over and over again that there was nothing I could do to save our sons, but she will not listen.'

Duinhir lowers his voice so he can speak only to you, out of his wife's hearing.

'Sometimes when I lie awake in the silent watches of the night, ${PLAYER}, I wonder if it is the truth I speak to my wife. I raised Duilin and Derufin to be bold and to be valiant, and they made me proud every day of their lives. But if I had raised them to be... less bold... to be less valiant...'

Duinhir chokes on his words and stifles a sob, turning his back on Rosfin to hide his face.

'Would my boys be alive today, ${PLAYER}, if I had not raised them to hold dear the same things as I? No, stop. Enough. You are here on the King's business. You are not here to listen to the ramblings of a sad old man. Speak to the people of Morlad. Do what you came to do.''You are here on the King's business. You are not here to listen to the ramblings of a sad old man. Speak to the people of Morlad. Do what you came to do.''I've got nothing to say to you. Move along.''Do what you came here to do.''You dare speak to me, after bringing such death to Morlad? I will not stand for this!''Do what you came here to do, then.''Oh, am I glad to see you, ${PLAYER}! A gang of toughs surprised me a few minutes ago and I had to run and hide. What if they come back?''Do what you came here to do, then.''Someone loosed an arrow at me from hiding, ${PLAYER}. I am fortunate indeed that this is a town of Men and not of Elves, or it might have hit its mark.

'These folk are not happy to see us.''What is wrong with these people, ${PLAYER}? Do they not know we are here to help them? Do you think the Heirs of Castamir are to blame?''Supporters of the Heirs of Castamir? No, I do not think they are responsible for this; not in the Blackroot Vale.'

Duinhir looks directly into your eyes and holds your attention.

'My people have suffered much, as have we all, and King Elessar did not take the time to visit Morlad himself. There is no conspiracy here, ${PLAYER}. The folk of the Vale grieve, and their grief has made them angry. I understand it, for I am angry too.'Bruidis has asked you to deliver supplies to the people of Ost Lontir.Gilanor can be found in Ost Lontir, to the west of Tadrent.

You should deliver these supplies to him.'If you plan to travel west to Ost Lontir, could you bring this crate to Gilanor? The townspeople have been waiting for these supplies.

'He'll be sure to pay you for your work.''I am glad to see your face again, ${CLASS}, and to see that you continue to do good work. Thank you for these supplies.'The barricades in Abadec's Watch have granted the Corsairs a sense of security.Dolvir has asked you to destroy barricades at Abadec's Watch.

Abadec's Watch is located in the south-west corner of Belfalas.Destroy barricades at Abedec's Watch (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have destroyed many barricades in Abadec's Watch.

Dolvir waits for you amongst the trees to the north of the camp. You should talk to him.Talk to Dolvir outside Abadec's Watch'While Abadec's Watch will never return to its former strength, we cannot allow the Corsairs to continue like this.

'Destroy the barricades that they have constructed, and those crumbling ruin walls will seem a little less sturdy than they once appeared.''Well done. Now we shall have an easier time chasing the Corsairs out.'The structures at the former Corsair camp contain plenty of useful materials.Bruidis has asked you to collect wood and cloth from wood piles and banners in Makham Mijann.

Makham Mijann lies north of Tadrent.Collect wood from wood piles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect cloth from banners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected a suitable amount of wood and cloth.

You should give these materials to Bruidis in Tadrent.'There is an abandoned Corsair camp to the north of Tadrent -- Makham Mijann, it was called. We tried to dismantle the camp to reuse materials, but efforts have halted for the time being.

'If you venture there, could you collect as many pieces of wood and cloth as you can? There ought to be wood piles and untouched Corsair banners that we left behind.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. We shall put these to good use.'Anírwen is concerned about the quality of the fish that is being sent up to the king's hall.Anírwen has asked you to inspect crates of fish on the docks of the bay.

The docks are in the Dol Amroth bay.Examine crates of fish (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You should let Anírwen know that you examined many crates of fish.

Anírwen is at her shop in Dol Amroth.Return to Anírwen'Oh, hello, my dear. I wonder if you might help me with a small task. I am worried that some of the fish being sent up to the king's hall is not as fresh as the royal table deserves. The crates of fish need to be examined, but if the fishermen see me coming, they're quick to swap out the crates they intended with fresh fish, only to later send up fish of poor quality.

'Tour the docks and inspect the crates and report back to me. Together we can identify who has been passing along subpar catches.''Welcome back, dear! Tell me, how were the crates? Did you find fish unworthy of the prince's table?'

You inform her of your findings. 

'Is that so? I knew it. I shall reprimand those swindlers immediately!'Dírion's dog, Draug, is disheartened after losing his favourite toy, a doll called 'Blue', somewhere in the woods around Alagrant.Traps can be found outside the hamlet of Alagrant, in the Blackroot Vale.

Dírion has asked you to search the traps outside Alagrant for any sign of 'Blue', a beloved toy of his dog, Draug. You should search the traps in the woods around Alagrant.The sprung trap can be found outside Alagrant, in the Blackroot Vale.

You have found a small blue object caught in one the traps. You should free 'Blue' from the trap.Free 'Blue' from the sprung trapDraug can be found in Alagrant, in the Blackroot Vale.

You have found 'Blue' in one of the traps outside Alagrant. You should return 'Blue' to Draug in Alagrant.Dírion can be found in Alagrant, in the Blackroot Vale.

You have returned 'Blue' to Draug and he appears pleased. You should talk to Dírion.Talk to Dírion'${PLAYER}? I had not heard that you'd returned to the Vale. What business brings you here?'

You inform Dírion that you are one of King Elessar's emissaries in Gondor, and that you have come to aid the people of the Blackroot Vale.

'Oh, I—you must excuse the manner of my behaviour when last you visited the Vale. I was perhaps too vocal in my displeasure with the Steward's leadership, and you did not deserve such a harsh welcome to Alagrant. But, if you are here to aid us...'

Dírion appears embarrassed, and his face flushes red.

'You see, Draug has lost his favourite toy somewhere in the woods, and try as I may, I haven't been able to find it. Draug could likely find it himself, but there are traps about, and I would not risk him stepping into one of them. I call his toy 'Blue' because... it is blue, and well, you'll understand.

'Would you please take a look in the traps outside Alagrant? If it's not in one of those, it's probably lost, but I would appreciate your help just the same, my friend.''Oh, wonderful! Look how happy Draug is!

'I must admit, I'd begun to worry that 'Blue' was lost for good. I could have sewn another, of course, but it would never have been quite the same. And besides, Draug would have figured it out!

'I cannot thank you enough, ${PLAYER}.'Somewhere in the ruins of Hashadir lies a precious heirloom.Nella has asked you to you look for her missing way-stone.

The way-stone went missing in Hashadir.Search for the missing way-stoneYou should bring the way-stone back to Nella.

Nella is in Dol Amroth outside of the Swan-knight's Keep.'Excuse me, I couldn't help but hear that you're going to Hashadir. Oh, pardon me, where are my manners. I'm Nellagwen, but you can call me Nella, and I really need your help. The thing is, I went wandering down to Hashadir a few days ago without knowing it'd been infested with rats. I was horribly surprised when a group of them swarmed me, a terrifying wave of fur and fleas, and I lost my footing. In doing so, I also seem to have dropped the way-stone that I was using to navigate my way around.

'That way-stone belonged to my great, great, great... well, a very long ago ancestor, and it's been passed down in my family for generations. I came here to ask for help getting it back, but as soon as the word "rats" was mentioned, everyone turned their backs on me.

'Please, I beg you - if you're going to Hashadir, can you look for my way-stone? I need to return it to its safe box before mother finds out that it's missing....''Did you - oh, you found it! Thank you, thank you, thank you! I need to get this back to its safe box right away. Oh, thank you!'You have come to the Palace of the Swan, where Imrahil, Prince of Dol Amroth, considers how to deal with the threats that still endanger Gondor in the aftermath of the War of the Ring.The Swan-knight Rossaran has brought you before the Prince of Dol Amroth, as King Elessar desired.Talk to Rossaran in the Palace of the SwanKing Elessar and Queen Arwen wait to discuss important matters with Imrahil, Prince of Dol Amroth.Wait for your audience with the Prince of Dol AmrothQueen Arwen has asked to speak with you.Talk to Queen Arwen in the Palace of the SwanQueen Arwen has indicated a place on the floor where she wants you to set down the bowl of water.Set the bowl of water down on the floor where Queen Arwen indicatedWait until Queen Arwen explains her peculiar words and deeds.Wait for Queen Arwen to explain herselfRoamingstar has appeared in the Palace of the Swan.Talk to Roamingstar in the Palace of the SwanDorthaneth stands in the Palace of the Swan.Talk to Dorthaneth in the Palace of the SwanDorthaneth stands in the Palace of the Swan.Listen to the words of DorthanethKing Elessar waits patiently for his audience with Prince Imrahil to begin.Talk to King Elessar in the Palace of the Swan'Many of the Prince's court and the greatest part of his swan-knights went with Imrahil to Minas Tirith for the waging of the war, but his daughter Lothíriel managed the rule of the city while he was away. That Dol Amroth still stands is a testament to her ability, and tells me she has learned much from her father!''Just one moment,' the Queen says to you seriously, retrieving a finely-crafted bowl and a glass jar from the recesses of her cloak. She removes the corked stopper from the mouth of the jar and slowly pours the crystal-clear water contained within into the bowl, spilling not a drop.

'Will you set this down on the floor just there?' she asks you.'This hall is so cold... but the Queen wants me to speak, so I shall speak.

'Long ago, and for always, I dwelt within and beside the River Gilrain, and my music was the song of sunlight on the water, and of wind in the leaves. But it was a lonely song, for my sisters had gone away and I did not think I would see them again. Animals came to the stream to drink, and birds to bathe, but except for such creatures I was alone and felt the pain of it... until one day.

'An Elf-maid tarried by the river as she journeyed southward from a distant land. She was more beautiful and radiant than anyone I had ever seen. I sang to her and she delighted in my song, for it reminded her of home. She danced beside my stream, and I was so gladdened by her presence that the music I made held more enchantment than ever it had before. She slept on the bank of Gilrain, comfortable and safe, and I was no longer alone. I dreamt that once she awoke she would fashion a home for herself in my wood, and I would teach her my song and she would call me sister. She would have children of her own, Elf-children of merry eyes and high voices, and I would show them the flower-fields and the secret springs, and they would fashion brightly-coloured petal-crowns to wear, and their mother would delight in them.

'It was a happy dream, but it lasted too long, and when she at last awoke many seasons had passed. The Elf-ship that waited in distant harbour for her to arrive could wait no longer. With selfish hands I stole that life from her, and I grieve every day at the sorrow I caused her. I did not intend such sadness to befall, and I would take it back if I could! And yet it is my doing, and the guilt weighs heavily upon my every moment.''I know what cruelty is, Maiden of the Gilrain. The world is cruel, and has always been. Can you know my story and say it is not so?

'Amroth was a king among Elves, but to me he was more; he was my love, and the brightest star of my night sky. "We must depart, Nimrodel," he said to me, "for the dwarves have awoken terrible evil, and if we stay it will endanger our folk too," and I believed his words. I believe them still, for I know what befell in the centuries since, as he cannot.

'Amroth went ahead to make ready the ship. I travelled with a small company to join him at that distant harbour, but the passage of the mountains was hard. The high crags filled me with unease; the rocky way troubled my heart. When at last we descended to the low-lands, I danced in the woods and became separated from my folk, so grateful was I to leave the mountains behind. The sound of the River Gilrain was beautiful, and its music had magic in it, and whatever else befell I can still remember how it was, and know that I loved it. I slept in peace for too long, and by the time I awoke a fell wind had come from the north and driven my love's ship from the shore, never to return. It is said Amroth leapt from the ship and braved the waves that he might wait for me... but never again was he seen by Elf or by Man, though I walked the shore for uncounted days and innumerable nights.

'I dwelled in sorrow from that day forth, never far from the Bay where I lost my love, and I have worn this mourning veil for so long I cannot remember the sight of the sun without it. I have held my grief close, but so too has it held me. Until now I believed the weight of those heavy chains was only mine to carry. I did not know you sorrowed too, Maiden of the Gilrain. It was not for you to feel this way. Do not burden yourself with the grief that is mine. I spoke of cruelty? The greatest cruelty is that this sadness must snare more than one heart, and for so many years!''And so concludes my Queen's greatest purpose in this journey, ${PLAYER}. She desired to ease Roamingstar's burden, and I believed we would need to visit the Cave of the Avorrim to make the attempt. But we were fortunate that Nimrodel was here in the Palace of the Swan, and now we have saved some time.

'I said Arwen's purpose on this journey was ended, but of course mine is only beginning! We must speak with Prince Imrahil about the continued presence of the Corsairs that threaten Gondor's shoreline, and also about the rise of the Kindred of the Coins in Umbar, across the Bay of Belfalas! But we have already achieved some good today, for Roamingstar and Nimrodel each deserve the peace and the happiness that their sorrows stole from them. I hope we have helped lighten their burdens today. I think we have.'Jajax has agreed to recount to you what happened to him and his brother Daxamat when the two of them sailed from Gondor to Umbar after the conclusion of the war.You have returned to Umbar with your brother Jajax.Talk to Jajax in the Sail-haven of Umbar BaharbêlYour brother Jajax has asked you to follow him through the city.Follow JajaxJajax stands in the courtyard of the Teeming Net Tavern.Talk to Jajax in the courtyard of the Teeming Net TavernJajax has decided to look for information about what has happened in Umbar.Wait in the courtyard for Jajax to returnYour friend Itûr has recognized you and called you over to him.Talk to Itûr in the courtyard of the Teeming Net TavernRemain in the courtyard as your brother Jajax requested, and cause as little trouble as possible.Wait for Jajax to return... and cause no troubleKhôltekh has asked you for your name.Talk to Khôltekh in the courtyard of the Teeming Net TavernRemain in the courtyard as your brother Jajax requested, and cause as little trouble as possible.Wait for Jajax to return... and cause no troubleItûr has quietly caught your attention.Talk to Itûr... quietlyItûr has asked you to discreetly escape from Khôltekh through the northern exit of the courtyard.Escape through the northern exit of the courtyardJajax has returned to you at great speed. You are not the only sibling to have gotten into trouble!Talk to Jajax outside the courtyard of the Teeming Net TavernJajax told you the city is not safe for the two of you, and you have to hurry back to your ship and escape!Follow Jajax back to the ship... and quickly!'Ah, it is good to be home! The sea-breeze welcomes us back to Umbar! My name may be spoken of all across the world already, but it will mean a little more to hear it spoken in the streets of our city. No doubt my exploits will earn us a tankard or two of ale in the Crab and Cutlass, or in the Teeming Net Tavern. You were always partial to the Hungry Deep, weren't you, Dax?

'But we have work to do! The drinking will have to wait. But just a little while!''Something about the city is different, Dax. Do you feel it too?

'There are too many guards here. The Haradrim of Ordâkh seem to be gone, but in their place the guardsmen appear... more vigilant. What has happened to my City of Sails? I do not like this. Someone must answer for it... and they will answer to Jajax!''Dax! I haven't seen you in an age! I thought you perished in the war... but you must have escaped that fate, somehow! I take it some portion of your brother's good fortune must have transferred to you? We could have used some of that here!

'Were you in the battle? I heard the Corsairs who followed Balakhôr were slain by an army of Dead Men... and that they were led by the hated warrior Thorongil, who despised the Umbari so much he returned to life that he may slay even more! But that cannot be so. Still, there have been strange occurrences here in recent days, ever since the great earthquake that collapsed the Temple of the All Seeing. People say he is dead... if such a thing is possible. But the Empire of Ordâkh did not stand long against the uprising, and when the fighting ended the Kindred of the Coins announced that neither Sauron nor Ordâkh would rule again in Umbar.

'The Kindred claim they have ushered in a new age, but for people like you and me, Dax, and like your brother... I do not know if they mean it. The gaols are full of thieves and Corsairs alike; no opponent of the Kindred lives long after voicing it. If they learn that you sailed with any Heir of Castamir...''What is your name? Daxamat?'

Khôltekh nods idly, but behind his eyes you sense that he is paying very close attention to your face, and filing away your name for later consideration.

'Some there are who lead, and some there are who follow. Even the Kindred of the Coins is subject to this rule, my new friend. Azagath the Sea-shadow prides himself on leading; he does so with aplomb. Rothog the Storm carries out the wishes of those who give the commands, and they are made stronger with every swing of his mighty fists, and every bite of his sharp teeth. Ask the Heirs of Castamir who attended the half-troll's last bout before the Sea-stone Mutiny about those teeth...'

Khôltekh grins.

'A private joke, Daxamat. But heed these words, for they are also true: the wisest of all is he who knows when to lead and when to follow. The wisest is he who knows when to speak, and when to listen. Which are you, my new friend? And who do you follow? The Heirs of Castamir? Or perhaps the Church of the All Seeing? This city has many foes, and sometimes they require some... rooting out. The uprising in Umbar was thorough... but Azagath has mused aloud that he sometimes wonders if it was thorough enough.''You have to go, Dax, before Khôltekh remembers you are here. Quietly, through the northern exit!''Dax! This place is no longer safe for us! This Kindred of the Coins intends to imprison or put to death any freebooter and pirate that does not sail in support of them!

'There's no time... we have to leave this city! Quickly, back to the ship!'Many fish dwell in the waters of the River Morthond and provide a rich bounty for the fishermen of the Blackroot Vale.Fishing nets can be found along the River Morthond near Sardol in the Blackroot Vale.

Gordir has asked you to search for fish in the nearby fishing nets and bring any fish you find to him. You should search the nets.Gordir can be found along the River Morthond in Sardol.

You have searched the nets as Gordir asked and found a few fish caught in them. You should deliver the fish to Gordir.Gordir can be found on a pier along the River Morthond in Sardol.

You have delivered the fish caught in the nets to Gordir, and he has asked you to fish with him for a time. You should fish with Gordir.Fish with Gordir along the River Morthond (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gordir can be found along the River Morthond in Sardol.

You have fished with Gordir and caught three pike out of the River Morthond. You should talk to Gordir.'It has been some time, ${PLAYER}!

'I, for one, have not forgotten how you helped the people of the Blackroot Vale. If it were not for you, we might still be troubled by those terrible water-spirits, and worse yet, we might still be beset by the Corsairs and that so-called Heir of Castamir who took up in Tarlang's Crown. And now, I hear tell that you've become one of King Elessar's emissaries after serving in the War. I say it is an honour and one well-deserved, no matter what some others in the Vale might think of the new King of Gondor. A fine honour, indeed!

'I'm afraid there isn't much trouble about at Sardol—at least not like there was during the War. I've heard that some of the Corsairs and some deserters have made their way to Tarlang's Crown, but it'll come to nothing, I think. 

'It may not be much, but I could use a little help today, if you can spare the time in the course of your errands for King Elessar. Would you check the nets near the pier for fish and bring me whatever you find? I've been coming up with small fish all day, so I don't want to let the big one swim by while I'm not paying attention!''Ah, not a bad catch for today then. You can toss those in the bucket there, ${PLAYER}.

'I don't have anything else to ask of you, but would you stay and fish with me for a time? The nets work well enough, of course, but I find that taking the time to fish can ease my mind. There is just something right about it, I think. And, to be honest, I would welcome the company. All of these bowmen back from the War, and it seems not one of them is a fisherman!'

Gordir laughs to himself, but it seems half-hearted. He looks out at the River Morthond for a moment, and then looks back to you.

'What do you say, my friend?''Three pike is a fine bounty, I'd say. Very good!

'Thank you for taking the time to fish with me, ${PLAYER}. It has been too long since I last had company on the pier, so our time together has done much to raise my spirits. And I know that you have many other errands upon which to spend your time, so I appreciate it all the more, my friend.

'If you ever find yourself back in Sardol, you are always welcome to fish the Morthond with me!'Bruidis wishes to surprise her husband with a gift.Bruidis has asked you to help her collect lilies from the marsh north of Tadrent.Collect lilies in the marsh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected plenty of lilies.

You should bring them to Bruidis in Tadrent.'If you are able, ${PLAYER}, would you help me gather lilies from the marsh north of here? It has been a busy time for both my husband Rossaran and me, and I wish to surprise him with something nice. Of course, I will reward you for your efforts.''Oh, these lilies are beautiful. Thank you. Perhaps such a request seemed silly in the face of everything that is happening... but I am grateful that you went out of your way to help me.'Bog-lurkers have been creeping into Makham Mijann.You should clear some bog-lurkers out of Makham Mijann to make their numbers more manageable.

Makham Mijann is located to the north of Tadrent.Defeat bog-lurkers at Makham MijannYou should clear some bog-lurkers out of Makham Mijann to make their numbers more manageable.

Makham Mijann is located to the north of Tadrent.Defeat bog-lurkers at Makham Mijann (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many bog-lurkers at Makham Mijann.CompletedIt seems that once dismantling efforts in Makham Mijann ground to a halt, bog-lurkers began creeping in.

You should clear some out of the area to make their numbers more manageable.You have defeated many bog-lurkers at Makham Mijann. When the residents of Tadrent return to dismantle the rest of the camp, they should have no problem with the bog-lurkers.Osbenn, an exhausted guard at Tadrent, has asked you to make his rounds for him.Osbenn asked you to leave Tadrent by the northern entrance and circle the town to the left.Leave Tadrent by the northern entrance and circle around the town to the leftYou have have begun making Osbenn's rounds for him, and should continue to circle the town of Tadrent on his behalf.Continue circling the town of Tadrent for Guard OsbennYou have reached the halfway point of Osbenn's rounds, and should continue to circle the town of Tadrent on his behalf.Continue circling the town of Tadrent for Guard OsbennWhile making his rounds for Guard Osbenn, you have found the corpse of a Corsair.Guard Osbenn is in the centre of Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Talk to Guard Osbenn in Tadrent, King's Gondor'You look like a reliable sort. Can I ask you to make my rounds for me? I am just exhausted.

'You see, villagers reported hearing a howling in the hills, and I have spent all day tracking down the source of it... but I have nothing to show for it except my weary, aching bones. If you will leave Tadrent by the northern entrance and then circle around the town to the left, keeping an eye out for anything suspicious, you will be doing me a favour.''If you will leave Tadrent by the northern entrance and then circle around the town to the left, keeping an eye out for anything suspicious, you will be doing me a favour.''A dead Corsair? And a deadly warg? I am tired just thinking about the adventure I narrowly avoided, ${PLAYER}!

'Thank you for helping me with this, and for defeating the warg for which I spent most of the day searching! All's well that ends well, I suppose.'Little Pip has been put to work searching a particular haystack in King's Gondor... for something.You have stumbled into the affairs of Little Pip and Vexed Meldis. Does one of Tadrent's haystacks hold a valuable treasure?There is no needle in that haystack after all.Wait...Little Pip stands by the haystack in question at the northern entrance of Tadrent, in King's Gondor.Talk to Little Pip by the haystack in question'You there! Do you think you might help me with a most exhausting task? My friends were helping me with it until a few minutes ago, but they all gave up and went home. They'll be sorry, they will, when I find the treasure! Vexed Meldis told me she lost a golden needle in this haystack... somehow... and if I found it she'd allow me to keep it!'

You ask Little Pip how someone could come to lose a needle in something like a haystack, and he rolls his eyes.

'You might as well ask why she's called Vexed Meldis, stranger!'

As it seems to be expected of you, you ask why she's called Vexed Meldis.

'Because she's always upset about something... usually about how loud me and my friends are being! Are you going to help me look for this needle?''Are you going to help me search? Vexed Meldis said there's a needle in this haystack!''Yes? Can I help you?

'I did tell that noisy child and his friends that I lost a golden needle in that haystack, but it was a necessary deception. They were driving me quite mad with their incessant shouting! They are still looking for it? What a brilliant ruse... I should have thought of it sooner!

'No, there's no golden needle in that haystack. Imagine what sort of fool would lose a needle there... and what sort of fool would spend time searching for such a thing!''There is no golden needle in this haystack at all, is there? Vexed Meldis lied to me! Now I am the one who is vexed!'

The boy stamps his foot.

'We have to do something about this! I don't like feeling this irritable, stranger! Is this what Vexed Meldis feels like ALL THE TIME?'Amon Lontir's use is not limited to times of war and strife.Boradan has suggested that you look out north towards Edhellond from Amon Lontir.You have taken in the sight of Edhellond at peace.

