'You have my thanks. Here is something for the trouble and I will pass on the news of Rakhûl's defeat to the bounty poster.'The great plague boar Suginzi is spreading disease and mayhem throughout Imhûlar. You are to defeat him and return to Numûru with his great tusk.Mizâdi Shâra, Queen of Renewed Hamât, has posted a bounty seeking skilled hunters to find and defeat the great plague boar, Suginzi. You are to defeat him and return to her Hand, Numûru, with one of his great tusks. He was last seen south-west of Urmâkh, near Shakhdag.Defeat Suginzi and collect one of his great tusksBring Suginzi's great tusk to Numûru in Urmâkh to collect your reward.Bring Suginzi's great tusk to Numûru in UrmâkhThe bounty notice reads:

On behalf of the Queen of Renewed Hamât, Mizâdi Shâra, you are empowered to seek out and defeat the great plague boar Suginzi. Suginzi has spread disease and mayhem throughout Imhûlar, and his reign of fear must end.

The proof of his death shall be one of his great tusks, hewn from his head. Bring the tusk to Numûru, Hand of the Queen in Urmâkh for a fitting reward.'You defeated Suginzi? That is quite the feat, you must be quite the fighter. He has eluded us for years. You have my gratitude. Citizens of Urmâkh no longer have to fear travelling south and being gored by those great tusks. Here, this should be a suitable reward for such a feat.'The dread scorpion Tamâhu is harassing citizens and growing her brood west of Urmâkh. You are to defeat her and return to Numûru in Urmâkh with her mighty stinger.Mizâdi Shâra, Queen of Renewed Hamât, has posted a bounty seeking skilled hunters to defeat the dread scorpion Tamâhu and return with her mighty stinger. She has last been seen west of Urmâkh, near Dûlagig.Defeat Tamâhu and collect her mighty stingerBring Tamâhu's stinger to Numûru in Urmâkh to collect your reward.Bring Tamâhu's stinger to Numûru in UrmâkhThe bounty notice reads:

On behalf of the Queen of Renewed Hamât, Mizâdi Shâra, brave citizens amongst you are asked to seek out and defeat the dread scorpion Tamâhu. Tamâhu has long plagued the lands west of Urmâkh, endangering all the peoples of Imhûlar. Defeat her before her brood spreads and more harm comes to innocent citizens of Urmâkh.

The proof of your triumph shall be her oversized stinger. Bring the twisted weapon to Numûru, Hand of the Queen in Urmâkh for a proper reward.'That is quite the stinger. I wouldn't want to tangle with the beast to whom it was attached. Clearly you know your way around a fight. Please know that the people of Urmâkh will sleep easier tonight because of your valourous deeds. Please accept this reward, you've more than earned it.'A bounty notice asks you to defeat a vicious kergrim known as Tukûrga in Tûkurdul, to the north-west near Ashâgir.A bounty notice in Ub Nishir has asked for someone to defeat the kergrim Tukûrga in Tûkurdul to the north-west, near Ashâgir.Defeat TukûrgaReturn to Neshurga in Ub Nishir north of Maurûsh and inform him that you have completed the bounty and defeated Tukûrga.Return to Neshurga and inform him of Tukûrga's defeatThe bounty notice reads:

'Tukûrga is a vicious and dangerous kergrim who can be found in Tûkurdul to the north-west of here. This monster must be stopped.

'Neshurga in Ub Nishir will reward you if you slay Tukûrga.''So Tukûrga will no longer be a terror to these lands.

'Take this as a sign of my thanks, ${CLASS}.'A bounty notice asks you to defeat Uzukar, a ferocious jackal that has been spotted roaming near Urgudag to the west.A bounty notice in Ub Nishir has asked for someone to defeat a ferocious jackal known as Uzukar who has been spotted near Urgudag to the west.Defeat UzukarReturn to Neshurga in Ub Nishir north of Maurûsh and inform him that you have completed the bounty and defeated Uzukar.Return to Neshurga and inform him of Uzukar's defeatThe bounty notice reads:

'A ferocious jackal called Uzukar has been spotted near Urgudag to the west. Traders in the area are in need of someone to get rid of this beast.

'Inform Neshurga in Ub Nishir if you complete this request.''The traders will be relieved to hear of Uzukar's defeat.

'You have their thanks and mine as well. The reward they provided is yours to claim.'Defeat many brigands in Ambarûl.Defeat many brigands in Ambarûl.Defeat many brigands in AmbarûlDefeat a great many brigands in Ambarûl.Defeat many brigands in Ambarûl.Defeat a great many brigands in Ambarûl.Defeat a great many brigands in AmbarûlSlay many brigand enemies throughout Mûr Ghala.Slay many brigand enemies throughout Mûr Ghala.Complete Brigand-bane of Kighân (Advanced)Complete Brigand-bane of An Shêru (Advanced)Complete Brigand-bane of Idagâl (Advanced)Defeat many brigands in Urash Dâr.Defeat many brigands in Urash Dâr.Defeat many brigands in Urash DârDefeat a great many brigands in Urash Dâr.Defeat many brigands in Urash Dâr.Defeat a great many brigands in Urash Dâr.Defeat a great many brigands in Urash DârYou have been asked to save unburned scrolls you find as you move throughout Kûr Anzar before they are set ablaze.Zuhûl, a Hamâti scholar, has asked you to save any unburned scrolls you find as you move through Kûr Anzar.Save unburned scrolls in Kûr Anzar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Zuhûl in Urmâkh with the unburned scrolls.Return to Zuhûl in Urmâkh with the unburned scrolls'I have a small favour to ask, if it's not too much trouble that is. If it is too much trouble, well...'

You see the young man make a great effort to collect himself.

'Sorry. I'd rather spend my time in books than in war, so I feel very out of sorts, but here we are. I have a favour to ask of you, let's just get right to it. The Barkhûshim are horrible for a variety of reasons, but first among them in my estimation is their hatred for history. They regularly destroy and burn historical documents, books, and scrolls for fear of knowledge that does not conform with their Fane.

'I am afraid that with our assault on Kûr Anzar, any historical scrolls or works they haven't burned they will quickly set to the fire, in a senseless desire to prevent us from recapturing them. If you see any unburned scrolls as you move through Kûr Anzar, please collect them. There are incalculably valuable historical anecdotes and treasures here. It would be a tragedy of epic proportions if they were all lost. Please save them.''You found some! So many! This is excellent, there is so much we can learn from these! Oh thank you, truly your bravery is only matched by your kindness. With this, we can learn so much more about our past and that of Kûr Anzar. Thank you for this.'Zidemel, an elder of Jirush and zealot who hears the desert's voice, Ishími, tends a flock of followers in the outpost. He asks you to aid his followers in return for his respect.Zidemel asked you to assist three adherents to appease the spirit of the desert, Ishími. Assist Meliri, Zidek, and Andur. They are all in attendance Zidemel preaches.Assist Andur in seeing his bloodline survives beyond himAssist Meliri in putting her husband's spirit to restHelp Zidek achieve vengeance for all he lostYou aided Zidemel's followers to complete the appeasement of the spirits. You should return to Zidemel in Jirush, located in central Khûd Zagin, to tell him that have aided his adherents.Return to Zidemel to tell him you aided his flock'Nangar is a good man. His refusal to understand the signs disconcerts me, but that is of little matter.

'He sends you to aid my growing flock, and so I thank him, and I thank you.

'The faithful understand the meaning of the Ishími as it wails across the land. I interpret Ishími's wails and guide the flock to their absolution. We faithful will usher our age of glory back into Khûd Zagin.

'Speak with the infirm among my flock. Actions like those will earn you my respect.''Each of the adherents you can help listen to me here. Meliri is seated, as is Zidek, and Andyr leans there against the wall.''I heard the cries of joy from the faithful. I hear Ishími's joyous laughter. Soon, all will see as we do. The prophecy of his return is nigh. We shall, again, know our greatest leader. Like he, we shall rise from the ashes and be a gem of the world once more.'Entârshul has asked you to call the children of Urmâkh home for supper.Call the children Ninda, Shûbar, Zâli, and Ladûl home to Entârshul in Urmâkh for supper. Entârshul suggests telling them Shangarûd will find them if they are reticent to come home. They can be found inside and just outside the gates of Urmâkh.Find and call Ninda home for supperFind and call Shûbar home for supperFind and call Zâli home for supperFind and call Ladûl home for supperSpeak with Entârshul in Urmâkh.Speak with Entârshul in Urmâkh'You are sweet to ask if you can help. It's a small thing, but you can round up the children for their supper. They are out playing around Urmâkh right now.

'If they need convincing, just remind them Shangarûd will get them if they are out past dark. It's a local legend we tell the children. My mother told it to me when I was a little girl, about a hungry storm in the guise of a man who would gobble up men, children, and animals alike. It's all very silly, but it gets the children to come in every time. They've even invented a little rhyme about Shangarûd. If you ask them, they might tell you.

'There are four children under my care, they should each be playing in or right outside Urmâkh somewhere.''Thank you for calling them home. It may seem silly to try to scare them a bit, but it keeps them out of trouble. My mother told me the legend of Shangarûd, the Hungry Storm and I was always home before dark.

'Especially now with all the dangers beyond the gates, we do everything we can to keep the children safe.'Your continued efforts to support Shâra Mizâdi and her forces in Idagâl and Pahar Hatokáli earn you celebrated status with Hamât Renewed.You must earn Celebrated standing with Hamât RenewedThe Quartermaster has requested your aid in the continued efforts to help the people of the Ikorbân Valley.<rgb=#FF0000>Complete Instances in the Ikorbân Valley Cluster for the Quartermaster. Use the Instance Finder to enter Ikorbân Valley Instances.</rgb>Complete Ikorbân Valley instances (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Completed many Ikorbân Valley instances.Completed'We must assist the people of the Ikorbân Valley!

'I ask for your help to aid in our efforts. There are many challenges still to overcome in Shagâna, and much evil still to be met in equal combat and defeated.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Complete Instances in the Ikorbân Valley Instance Cluster for the Quartermaster. Use the Instance Finder to enter Ikorbân Valley Instances.</rgb>Your efforts have truly assisted those who work to forge a new path for the people of the Ikorbân Valley. You will certainly be rewarded for your accomplishments.The Quartermaster has requested your aid in the continued efforts to help the people of Umbar.<rgb=#FF0000>Complete Instances in the Umbar Instance Cluster for the Quartermaster. Use the Instance Finder to enter Umbar Instances.</rgb>Complete Umbar instances (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Completed many Umbar instances.Completed'We must assist the people of Umbar!

'I ask for your help to aid in this effort. If you are able to take on the ills of Umbar, and bring justice to our home, we would be most indebted to you.'

<rgb=#FF0000>Complete Instances in the Umbar Instance Cluster for the Quartermaster. Use the Instance Finder to enter Umbar Instances.</rgb>Your efforts have truly assisted those who work to secure Umbar for the people. You will certainly be rewarded for your accomplishments.Satisfied with her victory over the Ordâkhai remnants, Rahâzi is ready to return to Iridír.Rahâzi is outside the grove of Arwáth, preparing to return to Iridír.Talk to Rahâzi outside the grove of Arwáth and return with her to IridírKhôltekh stands within the village of Iridír, studying the people nearby with interest.Talk to Khôltekh within IridírGirhâzi is inside her lodge within Iridír.Return to Girhâzi in her lodge within IridírGirhâzi is in her lodge within Iridír.Talk to Girhâzi in her lodge within Iridír'We have done well today, ${PLAYER}. With our actions and those of Surdûsha, we have protected my home and the Elf of this grove from the Ordâkhai remnant in Ambarûl.

'Are you ready to return to my mother with word of our triumph? Tell me when you wish to return to Iridír and we will make the journey together.''Let us return to Iridír and speak of our success to my mother.''We have finished speaking with the leader of this settlement, the woman Girhâzi. If she possesses wisdom she will aid us in our mission. If she does not...'

Khôltekh leaves the rest unsaid.

'She asked to speak with you. I need not remind you that secrecy is an advantage, and you should keep hidden the details of our dual purposes. The less you say to her of Azagath or of Balakhâd, the better. Say only what you must, and perhaps she will volunteer useful information to you without learning anything that might prove detrimental to us, or to Umbar.''Girhâzi asked to speak with you. I need not remind you that secrecy is an advantage, and you should keep hidden the details of our dual purposes. The less you say to her of Azagath or of Balakhâd, the better. Say only what you must.''Rahâzi speaks highly of the courage you displayed when you faced the great bear, ${PLAYER}. For that you have my thanks, both as the chieftain of Iridír and as a mother. Each are grateful you stood alongside Rahâzi against the terrible beast.

'While you were gone, the man Belondor told me your group pursued two of the ruling class that betrayed his city. Their betrayal must have cut deep to earn the enmity of so many members of the Kindred of the Coins. Why else would half that body's remaining number pass the Umbari wall at my border in pursuit of them?

'I deem Belondor speaks honest words, or at least he believes what he says to be truthful; the dwarf and the half-troll seem more guarded, and speak less freely. Which of these voices holds more sway within the Kindred of the Coins, and who do they truly serve? The memories of the Shagâni people are long, and we Ikorbâni who live along the river have learned to distrust such as they. It will take some proving to unlearn those lessons, even if they and we share a common enemy in Ordâkh.'Girhâzi's trust is not easily gained, for she and her people have long memories.Girhâzi gave a number of tasks to your travelling companions for them to complete in order to earn the trust of her people, and to learn valuable information that will benefit your own mission in Ambarûl.Talk to Khôltekh in IridírTalk to Rothog in IridírTalk to Belondor in IridírTalk to Corudan in IridírTalk to Sigileth in IridírYour companions have been given a number of places to scout and enemies to defeat there: at the eastern side of the Wrackwade in the camp of Sûru, an Ordâkhai camp at Eshadur across the river, a brigand camp to the south-east, and the road to Urash Dâr.Scout the eastern side of the Wrackwade in the camp of Sûru to the southJoin Rothog outside the Ordâkhai camp at EshadurJoin Khôltekh outside the brigand camp of Bidrokh-nûnScout the road from Ambarûl to Urash Dâr, south-east of IridírDefeat brigands in Ambarûl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Ordâkhai in Ambarûl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect stingers from usan-kûni scorpions in Ambarûl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Girhâzi waits for your successful return in Iridír.'I have given the members of your travelling company a number of tasks to complete that will prove their friendship to the Ikorbâni. Speak to them around the village and you will see that these tasks are not for the benefit of Iridír alone, but will also serve you well on your own mission.

'As for you, ${PLAYER}, I would ask that you scout the eastern side of the Wrackwade, the crossing of the Ikorbân River by the camp of Sûru to the south. The Ordâkhai fled that way when we reclaimed Iridír, but they might threaten to return if it is not watched.''I have given the members of your travelling company a number of tasks to complete that will prove their friendship to the Ikorbâni. Speak to them around the village and you will see that these tasks are not for the benefit of Iridír alone, but will also serve you well on your own mission.

'As for you, ${PLAYER}, I would ask that you scout the eastern side of the Wrackwade, the crossing of the Ikorbân River at the camp of Sûru to the south. The Ordâkhai fled that way when we reclaimed Iridír, but they might threaten to return if it is not watched.''You expected me to chafe against the task assigned to me by the chieftain of Iridír? No, I will not, for I understand the reasoning that lies behind it.

'Girhâzi tests whether the Kindred of the Coins will respond to her own needs, or if we seek only to do what personally benefits each of us. This is something with which I am familiar, for I have conducted similar tests of my own to assess the loyalty of those who serve me and my various interests.

'Girhâzi is troubled by brigands to the south-east of Iridír, at a place named Bidrokh-nûn. It will be advantageous to us to help her with this problem, and we can also keep open our eyes when there we venture. If Balakhâd or his potential helper Sadûkh came this way, they might be hiding among the brigands. Do you see how completing Girhâzi's task might help us with our own?''The warrior-woman who rules this village knows strength when she sees it before her, ${PLAYER}. She has challenged Rothog to slay Ordâkhai at their encampment across the river to the south-west, from where they might threaten her people. She says that place was called Eshadur.

'I told her it will be done, quickly and well. My khopeshes hunger for the blood of Ordâkhai, for they tasted it during the Sea-stone Mutiny and it has been too long since they drank their fill! Húrshauk, too, will rejoice to be set loose within the encampment, but he will have to move with speed if he thinks to outpace his master!''Girhâzi assigned to each member of the company a task by which they might prove they are friends to Iridír, but she gave me no such challenge. What am I supposed to do while everyone else proves their worth, ${PLAYER}?

'I cannot simply wait for everyone to complete their tasks! I have spoken already with Sirgon, and the two of us will scout the south-eastern road that leads out of Ambarûl. You are welcome to come with us and see if Azagath and Nakási took that road. Perhaps we will find them and shorten the duration of our journey, bringing about an unexpected but satisfying conclusion while it has still just begun!''The ruler of Iridír tasked me and my sister with collecting stingers from the usan-kûni scorpions that lurk to the east of the village or across the river to the south-west. It seems such stingers can be put to a number of medicinal uses by the Ikorbâni, and Girhâzi desires to restock their supply. She cautioned us that the creatures possess great reserves of patience; they might lie in wait for hours, hidden from careless observation, and strike without any warning.

'Despite the danger, we are, of course, willing to help.''I have agreed to help my brother with the task given to him by the ruler of Iridír, ${PLAYER}, but I wanted first to speak to you about another matter.

'I heard from the Dumûri warrior Surdûsha that you and her friend Rahâzi protected the life of an Elf-warden in the northern reaches of Ambarûl. Is it true?'

You describe your meeting with the Elf-warden Shanthar at the grove of Arwáth, and what he said to you about his people, the Kintai.

'I would be most interested to learn more about the Kintai. Did he say others of his kind live along the Ikorbân River? Perhaps we will encounter more of them as we continue our journey.''I have given the members of your travelling company a number of tasks to complete that will prove their friendship to the Ikorbâni. Speak to them around the village and you will see that these tasks are not for the benefit of Iridír alone, but will also serve you well on your own mission.

'As for you, ${PLAYER}, I would ask that you scout the eastern side of the Wrackwade to the south, in the camp of Sûru. The Ordâkhai fled that way when we reclaimed Iridír, but they might threaten to return if it is not watched.''I understand the tasks I gave to you and your travelling companions have been completed? Perhaps the rulers of Umbar will prove useful allies to me after all.'

Girhâzi takes the scorpion stingers from you and lightly tests one of them with her teeth. She spits a drop of glistening venom onto the ground.

'I am satisfied, ${PLAYER}. I will help you in any way I can. My folk are at your service, and you will have the hospitality of Iridír, such as it is in these days of strife.'Girhâzi has agreed to provide you with supplies and assistance as you prepare to take the necessary longer road in pursuit of Azagath.Rahâzi is on the eastern side of her mother's lodge in Iridír.Talk to Rahâzi on the eastern side of the lodge in IridírYour companions throughout the village of Iridír may welcome the gift of pillows, or they may not.Offer a pillow to KhôltekhOffer a pillow to RothogOffer a pillow to SigilethYour task complete, you can now rest for the night in the village of Iridír. Sleeping mats for this purpose can be found on the western side of the lodge.Your sleeping mat awaits on the western side of the lodge in the village of Iridír.Rest for the night on your sleeping mat in the lodge within IridírYour own sleep disturbed by troubling dreams, you hope that your companions are feeling more rested than you. They can be found at the entrance of Iridír to the north of the lodge.Talk to Corudan at the entrance of Iridír to the north of the lodgeYour own sleep disturbed by troubling dreams, you hope that your companions are feeling more rested than you. They can be found at the entrance of Iridír to the north of the lodge.Talk to Sigileth at the entrance of IridírYour own sleep disturbed by troubled dreams, you hope that your companions are feeling more rested than you. They can be found at the entrance of Iridír to the north of the lodge.Talk to Sirgon at the entrance of Iridír'If your prey was set upon journeying further east, he will have found the road into Urash Dâr blocked by landslides caused by the recent quake. He would need to travel south instead, a longer road and a harder one through the rocky land beside the desert, but necessary. If you go that way in pursuit of him, I will see that you have the provisions and supplies you need for the journey.

'Speak with Rahâzi on the eastern side of the lodge and she will tell you where your companions can rest, and rest well, before you depart. If you should dream, rue it not! The dreamless sleep holds no wisdom.''Speak with Rahâzi on the eastern side of the lodge and she will tell you where your companions can rest, and rest well, before you depart. If you should dream, rue it not! The dreamless sleep holds no wisdom.''My mother says you are to rest for the night before leaving Iridír? The sleeping mats on the west side of the lodge have been prepared for the use of your company, but I cannot promise they will be comfortable!

'Here, I will give you feather pillows to distribute among your companions. I gave one to the man Sirgon already, for he stood near when my mother gave me the command. He was grateful for it; so grateful my face reddened with embarrassment. They are just pillows, and I would not describe them as soft! Still, they should be better than nothing at all, and help you and your friends awake rested for your journey. The road through Khûd Zagin is rocky, hot, and uncomfortable; spending one night in Iridír as my mother's guests should not be!''Here, I will give you feather pillows to distribute among your companions. I would not describe them as soft, but they should be better than nothing at all, and help you and your friends awake rested for your journey. The road through Khûd Zagin is rocky, hot, and uncomfortable; spending one night in Iridír as my mother's guests should not be!''A feather pillow? Perhaps some others among the Temámir would accept such a gift, but I do not look for comfort when I rest during the evening hours.

'I sleep with an eye open when at home in the Shoreward Stone. I cannot imagine sleeping in an unfamiliar land with more than that eye closed, ${PLAYER}. Keep your pillows!''Feather pillows? Huh.'

Rothog takes a pillow from you and tests it with one hand. The burlap material tears and a few brown feathers fall from the rip. The half-troll stuffs the feathers back inside and hands it back to you.

'I do not think I have much use for such a thing as this, ${PLAYER}. You can keep it. I will follow the example of my friend Húrshauk, who sleeps anywhere he wishes and requires nothing.''A feather pillow? That is very kind of our hosts, ${PLAYER}. Thank you very much for this. I have not had much occasion to rest comfortably in recent weeks.

'How odd to speak so, my friend! I have not forgotten how I rued the time of my recovery in Leafshade's grove, when I awoke from my wounded stupor as Nestinanessornë! Then I had nothing but time to rest, and the inactivity cut deeply! Now there is little time to rest, but this pillow will help me to make the most of it.''Thank you for this pillow, ${PLAYER}. My manor-house in Umbar Baharbêl has dozens of pillows, artfully arranged by Mêshka throughout every room, but they are far away. This one will transport me back home while I sleep, and no doubt give to me pleasant dreams of my home and my hearth. I will see Mêshka when I close my eyes.

'You may need to shake me awake in the morning with some force, ${PLAYER}. I may not want to leave her embrace, awakening on the hard ground of Iridír. This is not to speak poorly of the hospitality of its people, you understand. But none can compete with even the most fleeting dream of my wife!''It has been years since I slept among pillows, ${PLAYER}. I do not think I would find that as comfortable as once I did. You may keep them. Perhaps my brother will accept an extra.'You set down the excess pillows and prepare your sleeping mat for the night, making it as comfortable as you can.You lie down on the sleeping mat and prepare to rest, giving your journey south into Khûd Zagin a good beginning.'I have already given my thanks to Girhâzi for the accommodations, ${PLAYER}. I have not awoken so refreshed in many months! Did you sleep as well as I?'

You tell Corudan that you managed to rest for a few hours. You ask him if he dreamed at all, and he shakes his head.

'At the very least I remember nothing. I believe my sleep was untroubled.''I believe my sleep was untroubled.''Something has troubled you, ${PLAYER}; I see it on your face. Your sleep was troubled by dreams?'

You nod and ask if it was the same for Sigileth. She looks around at the folk gathered nearby.

'We will not speak of it here. Let us later discuss our dreams in private.''We will not speak of it here. Let us later discuss our dreams in private.''I did not sleep well in the night, ${PLAYER}, but I have determined it will not adversely affect my morning. We have a long day of travel ahead of us, for Azagath must be ahead of us. He gets further away with every passing hour, and we have already spent enough of them sleeping, or trying to sleep.'The guard outside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja has received word that some of your friends have made preparations to depart Umbar Baharbêl.Many of your friends, including the crew of the Wave-hunter, prepare to depart Umbar Baharbêl from the Sail-haven. Jaith told you to find them not far from the Beacon-gate on the west side of the city.Find your friends and crew in the Sail-haven, not far from the Beacon-gate on the west side of Umbar BaharbêlLady Nauriel of Pinnath Gelin stands in the Sail-haven in Umbar Baharbêl, east of the Beacon-gate.Talk to Lady Nauriel in the Sail-havenThe child Thorongil stands by his mother in the Sail-haven in Umbar Baharbêl, east of the Beacon-gate.Talk to Thorongil in the Sail-havenNimrodel stands among your friends in the Sail-haven in Umbar Baharbêl, east of the Beacon-gate.Talk to Nimrodel in the Sail-havenNimrodel's guardians are preparing to depart, and you should acknowledge them with a <rgb=#00FF00>salute</rgb> or a <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb>.Legolas stands ready to depart for Gondor on a merchant vessel leaving Umbar Baharbêl.Gimli stands ready to depart for Gondor on a merchant vessel leaving Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Gimli in the Sail-havenSirgon is not yet prepared to leave Umbar Baharbêl, and wishes to speak with you about the days to come.Talk to Sirgon in the Sail-haven'A number of unusual folk were looking for you, ${PLAYER}: some Elves, a dwarf, and a few others I deemed travellers from Gondor. I trust they were friends of yours? They asked if I had seen you of late, and when I told them you often have business within the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja, they gave me a message to pass on to you.'

Jaith rests his weapon against one shoulder while he tries to remember the message.

'Yes, I recall the words, fear not. They said they have engaged the services of a merchant vessel and plan to return to their own lands, but they wish to bid you farewell before they depart. They asked for me to send you on to them in the Sail-haven, by the Beacon-gate on the west side of the city. They assured me they would not leave until you spoke with them, but the honour of my station begs that you will not delay.''Your friends have engaged the services of a merchant vessel and plan to return to their own lands, but they wish to bid you farewell before they depart. They asked for me to send you on to them in the Sail-haven, by the Beacon-gate on the west side of the city. They assured me they would not leave until you spoke with them, but the honour of my station begs that you will not delay.''There you are.''There you are. I was not certain the guard posted at the Meeting-hall would give you our message in time, but it seems he proved worthy. I am surprised; still, I welcome it. Has my mood been lightened by our success? How could it not? The purpose that brought us to these shores has been satisfied: my son Thorongil has been rescued from the clutches of the Kindred of the Coins, and we must go now to the Green Hills of Pinnath Gelin. My husband Carandolion will be pleased to see his son's safe return, and his wife's triumph.

'Yes, I say "triumph," though my vengeance be incomplete and those responsible for the hurts done to my family remain unpunished. The Elf warrior Sigileth advised me to set aside my desire for vengeance, and while I refused to do so forever, I told her I would not delay returning my son to his home to achieve it. But that was not all. I enlisted her promise not to forget the injustices done to my family, and if she has the opportunity to make hated Nakási pay for them, she will do so.

'I have only known Sigileth for a short time, but I believe she will do as I have requested. It is only this that allows me to leave behind this city, and its treacherous rulers.''The Elf warrior Sigileth advised me to set aside my desire for vengeance, and while I refused to do so forever, I told her I would not delay returning my son to his home to achieve it. But that was not all. I enlisted her promise not to forget the injustices done to my family, and if she has the opportunity to make hated Nakási pay for them, she will do so. I have only known Sigileth for a short time, but I believe she will do as I have requested. It is only this that allows me to leave behind this city, and its treacherous rulers.''We are leaving, ${PLAYER}, and it is just as well. Ever since Azagath Sea-shadow and Nakási Wave-reaper fled from the Citadel of the Winds, I have been itching to return home and tell Orgolas and all my friends about the adventures I have had since coming here! They will not believe me, but perhaps when you come back to Pinnath Gelin you will tell them it is all true? They will be astounded!

'My mother refuses to tell me much about what befell your own ship when it ran aground upon the Shield Isles as you journeyed to Umbar, but I overheard the captain of the vessel we are taking back to Gondor discussing the voyage ahead. He believes the currents and the winds will be favourable, and the journey should not take very long, or be very dangerous. That is a shame. I imagine being shipwrecked can be pretty exciting, and I would like to see for myself, just once!''I have been itching to return home and tell Orgolas and all my friends about the adventures I have had since coming here! They will not believe me, but perhaps when you come back to Pinnath Gelin you will tell them it is all true? They will be astounded!''It is time for me to return to the Caves of the Avorrim in Belfalas, but I have made a decision. I will bring my people from the cavern that has been their home, and we will no longer mourn, but rejoice in the light of the sun and the feel of the breeze.'

Nimrodel smiles gently at you.

'Perhaps by the time we arrive I will be ready to do this, my friend. It is one thing to speak it, and another to see it done! But that is the wish of my heart. Lothíriel will help me in this, I am certain of it.

'A number of your friends have agreed to escort me home, ${PLAYER}; I hope you will forgive my taking them from you.''A number of your friends have agreed to escort me home. I hope you will forgive my taking them from you.''A number of your friends have agreed to escort me home. I hope you will forgive my taking them from you.''Queen Arwen and King Elessar would never forgive us if we allowed any harm to befall Nimrodel during her journey back to her home. I volunteered at once to accompany her, and Elladan and Elrohir also wished to do their part. To my surprise Gimli has also asked to come along, and I am glad of it, but his reasoning is clouded to me: he wants to hear from Nimrodel about the caves in which her people dwell, for some project upon which he wishes to set his mind.

'The thinking of dwarves is beyond me, ${PLAYER}, but I am happy for his company all the same.''Queen Arwen and King Elessar would never forgive us if we allowed any harm to befall Nimrodel during her journey back to her home. I volunteered at once to accompany her, of course, but to my surprise Gimli has also asked to come along. I am glad of it, but his reasoning is clouded to me: he wants to hear from Nimrodel about the caves in which her people dwell, for some project upon which he wishes to set his mind.

'The thinking of dwarves is beyond me, ${PLAYER}, but I am happy for his company all the same.''Gimli and the sons of Elrond, and myself, will be fine guardians for Nimrodel as she returns home to Belfalas.''Gimli and I will guard Nimrodel as she returns home to Belfalas.''I do not know why Legolas is so surprised that I wish to accompany him as he escorts the Elf Nimrodel back to her home. I have been moved by what he has shared of her story, and it brings to my mind some of the greatest love stories ever told among the dwarves of Durin's Folk: beautiful tales, lovely and sad...'

Gimli coughs and sputters. When he has recovered, he continues gruffly.

'Legolas and I vowed to travel with each other to see places dear to each of us: for me the Glittering Caves, and for he the forest of Fangorn, then to compare the relative beauty of each. I should fear to let him out of my sight, lest he forget this promise!'

Gimli looks down at his boots and speaks more quietly.

'But of course I would feel more at ease if Nimrodel was protected by the axe of a hale dwarf-warrior. She reminds me of the Lady Galadriel, and I would see no harm come to her.''I will guard the Elf Nimrodel as she returns home. I also wish to ask her some questions about the caves in which she dwells.''Oh, I am not leaving with them, ${PLAYER}. I feel that I still have work to do here, and I would appreciate your continued help. Together I hope we might ease the tensions between Gondor and Umbar, and further the cause of peace between King Elessar and the Kindred of the Coins.'Belondor has requested that you and Sirgon meet with him and the rest of the Kindred of the Coins in the Citadel of the Winds to discuss your next course of action.Corudan and Sigileth are in the Sail-haven, east of the Beacon-gate in Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Corudan in the Sail-havenTalk to Sigileth in the Sail-havenSirgon is waiting in the Sail-haven east of the Beacon-gate to go with you to meet with the Kindred of the Coins.Talk to Sirgon in the Sail-haven and go with him to meet the KindredSirgon stands in the Fleetfast outside the Lords' Gate, waiting to discuss your meeting with the Kindred of the Coins.Talk to Sirgon outside the Lords' Gate in the Fleetfast'We will begin today, if you are willing! When you have bid farewell to the crew of the Wave-hunter and your other friends, there is something I must ask of you. Belondor desires to meet with the two of us in the Citadel of Winds, the better to discuss our next course of action.''After you have spoken with your friends, let us go to meet with Belondor about the days to come.''Your friends will be safe on their journey back to Gondor, ${PLAYER}, I doubt it not. It is my belief, and my sister's, that we remain closer to danger than they!

'We have achieved a tenuous peace with those who rule in Umbar, for now, but we should not forget that the Kindred of the Coins may have secret purposes of its own. Azagath kept the evil in his heart hidden from those with whom he ruled the city; might the members of the Kindred who remain act similarly?

'Sigileth and I have decided to stay here in Umbar and help you in the days to come, but heed our words now: remain cautious when you speak with the Kindred of the Coins! The gravest threat among their number may have fled the city... but I believe those who remain could still pose a danger to you, and to Gondor.''It is true, ${PLAYER}: I cautioned Nauriel against pursuing her vengeance against the Kindred. It was the lesson of Cruel Dancer, who was once Galatâni, that caused me to speak such words to Nauriel. If she followed the road of vengeance to its end, as did Galatâni, would she ever return from it? Or would the road curve and bend, with retribution following her back to Pinnath Gelin?

'I do not remember the name of Nauriel's husband, but if she achieves her vengeance would it stop there, or would he be endangered? Their son, too, for whom this was done... would he be threatened anew?

'The lesson of Cruel Dancer inspired me to steer Nauriel from this course. I have accepted the burden of vengeance on her behalf. She has my promise that my sword Baugliltir will seek the blood of Azagath Sea-shadow and Nakási Wave-reaper.''Are you ready to go? Let us meet with Belondor about what awaits Gondor and Umbar in the days ahead. He has invited us to the Citadel of the Winds, and assured us of safe passage there.''Remember our words of caution when you meet with the Kindred!''My brother is right in this: you should remain wary of the Kindred of the Coins, despite their benign words.''Near Harad! I know very little of these lands, ${PLAYER}, but I will not turn away from the mission that awaits us. If the capture of Azagath and Nakási will help maintain the peace between Gondor and Umbar, I would pursue them to lands both distant and dangerous, no matter how far the chase takes me from the Sea where my heart dwells, or from my home and family in Lebennin.'The leaders of the Kindred of the Coins have a number of preparations to make before your combined pursuit of Azagath Sea-shadow can begin.The leaders of the Kindred await your assistance with their preparations in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Hármelak in the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaThe leaders of the Kindred await your assistance in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Belondor in the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaTalk to Khôltekh in the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaTalk to Rothog in the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaThe Kindred of the Coins have given you orders to deliver to a number of people at various locations within the city: Bith Gabâr, the forum in the Upper Ward, at Stonecliff Market in the Lower Ward, and on the steps of Dahâl Huliz, the arena in the Tor-gardens.Talk to Mêshka somewhere within the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaSirgon is by the east gate of Umbar Baharbêl, preparing to depart the city.Talk to Sirgon by the east gate of Umbar Baharbêl'Hármelak spoke of the need for many preparations before our travelling party can depart. I hope it will not take too long before we are ready to leave! Every day wasted means our quarry gets further and further ahead, calling into question the success of our pursuit.

'Go to the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja and urge them to hurry. You may be able to speed their preparations if you offer your assistance.''Every day wasted means our quarry gets further and further ahead, and the success of our pursuit becomes less and less likely. Go to the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja and urge them to hurry.''I believe the errant members of my Kindred prepared for an overland journey even before they recovered the tapestry map from within the Mâkhda Khorbo, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps they were aided in this by the secret wealth gathered by Sharûla and others of her sort, scoundrels hiding their true nature behind fair faces. I have seen this before.

'But we must take time to prepare our own pursuit, or we doom it to fail before it is begun. The lands east of the city into which Azagath and Nakási have fled are treacherous and full of danger for the unwary. Caravans leave Umbar Baharbêl and follow the Road of Riches eastward along the Ikorbân River before turning south and crossing the rocky waste that marks the edge of the great sands. From there it is a long, inhospitable trek to the desert kingdoms of the south, from which great wealth flowed to us, and back again. The treasure from the south has slowed in recent months; can it be due to anything but unrest in the Empire of Ordâkh following the war?

'Caravans are now slow to depart from Umbar, and that will make their masters hungry for work. I have written an offer of employment for a caravan driver, his animals, and sufficient supplies for the journey. Bring it to Jakbir outside at the Forum and he should accept its terms without difficulty, but before you depart Dâr Laja you should speak with the other leaders of the Kindred about their own preparations. They may have some tasks for you as well.''Caravans are now slow to depart from Umbar, and that will make their masters hungry for work. I have written an offer of employment for a caravan driver, his animals, and sufficient supplies for the journey. Bring it to Jakbir outside and he should accept its terms without difficulty, but before you depart Dâr Laja you should speak with the other leaders of the Kindred about their own preparations. They may have some tasks for you as well.''Azagath betrayed the city and its people, ${PLAYER}, but that is not the extent of his evil. The cruel sorceries he wove inflicted terrible pain and death upon the men under my command, and those are the hurts for which I will make him pay. My wife Mêshka knows the direction of my thoughts, and she has encouraged me to follow where my heart leads on this matter.

'Still, she did not know it could take me far from the city and into the wild lands east of Umbar. Will you speak with her and learn if she would have me stay? Whatever she decides will become my course, and I will not gainsay it or act in any fashion opposed to her mind.

'Afterwards, if she gives her approval, I will join you and the others at the east gate of the city.''It is not a habit of mine to set forth upon a journey without knowing where it will end, but none of us can say precisely where Azagath and Nakási might be bound. If only Belondor had thought to lay his eyes upon the tapestry map when he first came to the Mâkhda Khorbo... our circumstances now might be rather different, and of a more beneficial flavour.

'But they are not, and the duration of our pursuit cannot be determined. We must prepare for a long journey, and hope to be too well-supplied, rather than not provisioned enough!

'As it happens, I own a controlling interest in a number of warehouses in the city, and I can ensure most favourable rates for the provisioning of our party. Bring this order to Ilbidûn at Stonecliff Market in the Lower Ward, and she will see that it is filled.''My khopeshes have become dulled from recent use: that business with the drake Ragrekhûl. For a journey of this sort I need them sharp! I will go to Dahâl Huliz later today and leave them with Mekhamun for sharpening. He can do the work while I visit with my good friend Húrshauk.

'This parchment contains a list of other things I want to bring with me on the road. Give him that and tell him I will expect everything to be prepared when I arrive.''Caravans are now slow to depart from Umbar, and that will make their masters hungry for work. I have written an offer of employment for a caravan driver, his animals, and sufficient supplies for the journey. Bring it to Jakbir at the Forum outside and he should accept its terms without difficulty.''My wife Mêshka did not know this pursuit might take me far from the city and into the wild lands east of Umbar. Will you speak with her and learn if she would have me stay? Whatever she decides will become my course, and I will not gainsay it or act in any fashion opposed to her mind.

'Afterwards, if she gives her approval, I will join you, Sirgon, and the Elves at the east gate of the city.''We must prepare for a long journey. As it happens, I own a controlling interest in a number of warehouses in the city, and I can ensure most favourable rates for the provisioning of our party. Bring this order to Ilbidûn at Stonecliff Market in the Lower Ward, and she will see that it is filled.''Did you not listen? I will not repeat my words to you.''You have a caravan order for me from the Golden Queen?'

Jakbir takes the parchment from you and studies it closely. At last he looks up at you.

'The Road of Riches has always been dangerous for caravans such as mine, remaining a favourite target of brigands and waylayers, but in these times of tumult it has become more treacherous than before. I understand the Ordâkhai fight to maintain their grip on trade with the desert kingdoms, despite their failing empire, and men whisper of merciless phantoms and spirits hiding in the night's darkness, seeking vulnerable prey.

'I will see what I can do for the Lady Hármelak in honour of our long and profitable relationship, but I fear I can promise little. You understand.''You wished to speak with me?'

You tell Mêshka that her husband asked that you find her and see what she thinks of his plan to pursue Azagath and Nakási into the wild lands east of Umbar.

'I am on my way to see him about this very subject, ${PLAYER}. He has my support, in this as in all things, and I plan to tell him that in person.

'I hope you will stand beside him and protect my Belondor from any dangers the two of you may face upon the eastward road. He will do the same for you and for the others of your company, I am certain of it.''What have you brought me? An order for supplies for a caravan leaving the city?'

Ilbidûn takes the parchment from you and reads it. By the time she has finished, her wordless grunts match the number of times she has shaken her head.

'There was a day I might have fulfilled this order, but it is months gone now. Ever since we accepted Khôltekh's offer of providing guards for the warehouse, items both large and small have gone missing in surprising quantities. I am not blaming the dwarf, you understand... but it is curious. Nothing more than that.

'As a result, we may have some difficulty fulfilling this order. I will see what I can do.''What? You have an order for me from Rothog the Storm?'

You hand the parchment to Mekhamun and he slowly reads its contents, sounding each word aloud as he does.

'I am not a learned man, stranger, but providing weapons and armour for Rothog and his Hûliz required me to become a swift pupil; the only thing more quick in all the city must be the rate at which he dulls his khopeshes! When he arrives to see Húrshauk I will sharpen them, and of course we will provide the rest of the items on this list, if they are to be found anywhere within Dahâl Huliz or the storage rooms located beneath the arena.

'But can you tell me this? If Rothog is leaving the city, who will stay behind to feed Húrshauk? None of my fellows dare approach him!''There you are, my friend. I see that the Kindred of the Coins have kept you busy making arrangements for our departure.

'While I waited, I pondered the road ahead of us and came to a realization: I know very little about the lands and kingdoms to the east and south within the Haradwaith, beyond the Cape of Umbar. I do not want my lack of knowledge about this area to hinder our pursuit, ${PLAYER}.'Given that the preparations for your departure will still take some time, Sirgon believes you should learn what you can about the lands of Near Harad into which you must travel.Caebar is on a terrace west of the Arena in the Tor-gardens of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Caebar on a terrace west of the Arena in the Tor-gardens of Umbar BaharbêlCaebar will teach you what he knows, but first he needs your help to finish the chores given to him by his instructor: picking berries from bushes down the steps to the east to be used for the making of inks and pigments.Pick pigment berries from bushes down the steps to the east (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Caebar is on a terrace west of the Arena in the Tor-gardens of Umbar Baharbêl, waiting for you to return with the pigment berries you picked.Caebar's teacher, a man named Ghâlim, is at Kên Taghlim in Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkh.Find Caebar's teacher Ghâlim at Kên Taghlim in Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkhGhâlim is at Kên Taghlim in Umbar-môkh.Talk to Ghâlim at Kên Taghlim in Umbar-môkhGhâlim has asked you to retrieve the book 'Kanash-tubu-hârra' from Harkhaphâr, far to the south of Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkh.Retrieve the book 'Kanash-tubu-hârra' from Harkhaphâr in Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkhGhâlim is at Kên Taghlim in Umbar-môkh.Caebar is on the terrace west of the Arena in the Tor-gardens of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Caebar on the terrace west of the Arena in the Tor-gardensGhâlim is on a terrace in the Tor-gardens of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Ghâlim on the terrace in the Tor-gardens'I find myself thinking about the Gondorian scribe that sailed with us on the Wave-hunter, ${PLAYER}. Yes, Caebar. When last I spoke with him he said he had found employment in the Tor-gardens performing the duties of a scribe for an esteemed scholar of Umbar. Perhaps he will introduce us to his employer, and this man will tell us what we need to know about the lands of Near Harad into which we must travel?

'Scribes and scholars gather upon the terrace west of the Arena, in the Tor-gardens. I would there begin the search for our friend Caebar.''Scribes and scholars gather upon the terrace west of the Arena in the Tor-gardens. I would there begin the search for our friend Caebar.''${PLAYER}! It is very good to see you again, my friend!'

Caebar clasps you on the arm and listens to the account of your recent adventures.

'I confess you have made yourself more useful than I. Perhaps it is because I am a man of Gondor and the folk here distrust me, but Ghâlim has been slow to include me in the lessons he administers to his other students. Instead I have been given the duties of a servant: sorting jars of ink, picking berries for pigments, running errands for the scholar and his fellows.

'These are not complaints! When I chose to make a new life for myself here in Umbar, I knew where I must begin. This is merely the first step. If you help me finish my tasks for today I will introduce you to Ghâlim, and he will have much to say about the geography and history of Near Harad.

'Today I have been charged with picking the berries that colour the pigments used for scholars' inks here in the Tor-gardens. I have almost collected enough, but if you help me the work will be sooner done. The berry bushes are just down the steps to the east; I was saving the nearest ones for the task's end, so it would seem to go more quickly as I tired of the errand.''Today I have been charged with picking the berries that colour the pigments used for scholars' inks here in the Tor-gardens. I have almost collected enough, but if you help me the work will be sooner done. The berry bushes are just down the steps to the east; I was saving the nearest ones for the task's end, so it would seem to go more quickly as I tired of the errand.''Yes, thank you, these should provide the amount of pigment for which I was made responsible. The scholars of the Tor-gardens go through a lot of ink and a lot of scrolls as they perform their work. Be glad that today was berry-picking, rather than parchment-making, for that takes much longer!

'I had thought Ghâlim would be back by now, but he has not returned. He may still be at Kên Taghlim in the northern reaches of a place called Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkh. I overheard him talking about having to go beneath the city for some reason, but his task may be taking longer than he expected. If you find him at Kên Taghlim and tell him I have finished my own task, he may be willing to impart the knowledge you need.

'I hope he is safe. I have heard the passages beneath the city can be dangerous.''I had thought Ghâlim would be back by now, but he has not returned. He may still be at Kên Taghlim in the northern reaches of a place called Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkh. I overheard him talking about having to go beneath the city for some reason, but his task may be taking longer than he expected. If you find him at Kên Taghlim and tell him I have finished my own task, he may be willing to impart the knowledge you need.

'I hope he is safe. I have heard the passages beneath the city can be dangerous.''Yes?''I had thought Ghâlim would be back by now, but he has not returned. He may still be at Kên Taghlim in the northern reaches of a place called Kamrabezûr, in Umbar-môkh. I overheard him talking about having to go beneath the city for some reason, but his task may be taking longer than he expected. If you find him at Kên Taghlim and tell him I have finished my own task, he may be willing to impart the knowledge you need.

'I hope he is safe. I have heard the passages beneath the city can be dangerous.''Yes?'

You tell Ghâlim that his student Caebar was worried about him, and the man laughs.

'Oh, I am in no danger. I have come to the passages beneath the city in search of a book that has been misplaced. Has my student already finished the task I set before him?'

You assure him that Caebar has done well, and he suggested that Ghâlim might answer your questions about the lands east of Umbar. Ghâlim looks at you appraisingly.

'It is true, I could do that. Unfortunately, there is still the matter of this misplaced book, and I can answer no questions until I have recovered it. If you retrieve it for me I will be happy to instruct you. The book is called 'Kanash-tubu-hârra,' and its author is unknown, which only adds to the tome's allure; it may be somewhere far to the south in the halls of Harkhaphâr, here in Umbar-môkh. The gladiators that roam those halls believe the book possesses some secret sword-art, and they are always stealing it away with the hope they might learn the technique.''There is still the matter of this misplaced book, and I can answer no questions until I have recovered it. If you retrieve it for me I will be happy to instruct you. The book is called Kanash-tubu-hârra, and its author is unknown, which only adds to the tome's allure; it may be somewhere far to the south in the halls of Harkhaphâr, here in Umbar-môkh.''This is not the only time the Hûliz from the arena have stolen away 'Kanash-tubu-hârra.' The legend of that tome grows with every passing year, and no amount of study has yet convinced them the arts contained within are beyond their possibility to learn. For me the technique is of no interest; I am attracted to the beauty of its unknown author's prose. He, or she, named it 'Kanash-tubu-hârra,' which means 'Road of Bending Reeds' in the language of Old Shagâni. I thank you for returning it to me.

'But let us climb now to the surface and join my apprentice Caebar where I left him in the Tor-gardens. Then I will answer whatever questions you may have for me. Your recovery of 'Kanash-tubu-hârra' has earned you some of my time.''Welcome back, ${PLAYER}. My teacher Ghâlim has suggested that I begin the lesson, so I will do the best I can. I must warn you, I do not know a great deal about Near Harad...''What? Have I said something wrong already?''Already you have betrayed your lack of knowledge, young Caebar. You are not to blame for it, but you hail from Gondor, and your words reveal it.

'"Near Harad," you name that land, but you speak in error. That is the Gondorian name for that place; indeed, "Harad" is an Elvish word. It means "South," for these lands are south of Gondor, and that relationship has been one of uneasy tension, or of war. To the folk who live there it is named Shagâna, the "Fair Land," but even that makes too simple a more complicated reality.'Ghâlim has determined that you need some instruction in the geography and history of the land men of Gondor call "Near Harad" but which the folk who live there call "Shagâna."Suitably chastened by the words of his instructor, Caebar wishes to do what he can to help you learn about the lands into which you must travel.Talk to Caebar on the terrace in the Tor-gardensGhâlim is in the Tor-gardens, instructing you about the lands of Shagâna.Talk to Ghâlim on the terrace in the Tor-gardensA jar of clean water can be found down the stairs to the east of the terrace in the Tor-gardens.Retrieve a jar of clean water for Ghâlim from the fountain down the stairs to the east of the terrace in the Tor-gardensGhâlim stands on the terrace in the Tor-gardens, waiting for you to return with the jar of water he requested.Keeping his eyes upon you, Ghâlim slowly upends the jar. Water spills out onto the ground until the jar is empty.Wait for Ghâlim to speakGhâlim has wasted the clean water you brought to him in service of making a point.Talk to Ghâlim on the terrace in the Tor-gardensCaebar wishes to do what he can to help you learn about the lands into which you must travel.Talk to Caebar on the terrace in the Tor-gardensGhâlim is on the terrace in the Tor-gardens.Talk to Ghâlim on the terrace in the Tor-gardensCaebar is on the terrace in the Tor-gardens.Talk to Caebar on the terrace in the Tor-gardens'Shagâna, the Fair Land. Yes, it was fair, once. Tales from long ago speak of a green and fertile land, threaded with mighty rivers. The men who came to that place worked the land, their crops thriving, and they harvested valuable spices and rare wood from the forests, and gold and jewels from beneath the hills. But that was long ago. The wealth of Shagâna was like a camp-fire set upon a hill, and covetous men saw it from far away, and hungered for the spices, and the rare woods, and the gold and the jewels. These men came from even further south, in Jarmât, which my student Caebar might call "Far Harad," but they came too from Khand in the east.

'They came from other directions, as well: from the west and from the north. They came from Númenor, and later from Gondor. The Shagâni number these among their cruelest oppressors.''The wealth of Shagâna was like a camp-fire set upon a hill, and covetous men saw it from far away, and hungered for the spices, and the rare woods, and the gold and the jewels. These men came from even further south, in Jarmât, which my student Caebar might call "Far Harad," but they came too from Khand in the east. They came from other directions, as well: from the west and from the north. They came from Númenor, and later from Gondor. The Shagâni number these among their cruelest oppressors.''I understand the actions of Gondor in the south may appear unjustified to the Haradrim, but none who join the armies of Sauron can claim to stand on the side of justice! How can the people of Harad...?'

Caebar sputters to a halt.

'How did the folk of Shagâna come to stand with Sauron, Ghâlim? Did they not oppose his evil? Were there none who fought his domination?''How did the folk of Shagâna come to stand with Sauron, Ghâlim? Did they not oppose his evil? Were there none who fought his domination?''Throughout the lands of the south he was known as He-Who-Sees-All, or Barkhûsh, and he came to dominate various smaller kingdoms and in time even the great Empire of Ordâkh. Those who opposed his rise were slain, in terrible number, and as the power of those kingdoms grew so too did that of Barkhûsh. Fewer and fewer stood against the might of this Dark Lord and his servants, and more and more believed that his and their strength might serve to drive back their ancestral enemies: the Khandari, the Númenóreans, the Dúnedain, the Gondorians.

'You ask how Barkhûsh came to dominate the Fair Land? That was the foundation: his church ruled through Fear, but so too did it promise something the Shagâni had sought for centuries: victory over their enemies. Was it a lie? Perhaps it was, and the execution of that lie would forever change the face of Shagâna, and the fate of its people.

'But I have grown parched. Bring me a jar of water from the fountain down the stairs to the east and I will tell you how Barkhûsh and the vast Empire he now controlled conquered the Fair Land and transformed it forever.''I have grown parched. Bring me a jar of water from the fountain down the stairs to the east and I will tell you how Barkhûsh and the vast Empire he now controlled conquered the Fair Land and transformed it forever.''Thank you for this water. It will prove most helpful with my demonstration.'

He takes a brief sip of water from the jar before continuing.

'East and south of Umbar Baharbêl, beyond the marshlands of Ambarûl, there was a green and fertile plain called Sûgedin, bordered on the south by the River Usâl. That waterway formed a natural barrier between Shagâna and the lands where originated the Great Southern Empire of Ordâkh.''It is the most valuable of all the resources to be found in this part of Middle-earth, more valuable than any of Lady Hármelak's golden coins or the Abâshal prized by Kinsman Khôltekh and the Temámir dwarves.

'Water. Only with it can crops thrive; without it life cannot survive. You will notice its absence within a day; within three days you would kill to acquire it, or perish yourself. But water can be even more valuable than this. Water can protect you from your enemies. It can serve as a barrier, and if that barrier disappears...'

Ghâlim falls silent, considering the ground by his feet where the water he poured has already soaked into the gaps in the cobbles and vanished.

'Two hundred years ago, a terrible drought and famine fell upon the green fields of Sûgedin, where once thrived the Abundant Kingdom of Nísaka. The River Usâl ran dry, leaving only the arid and cracked channel that was once its river-bed. Desert claimed the Khûd, and the fertile land on either side of the Usâl became the Lâkhedin, the Witherlands. This distressing process came to be called the Great Dearth, and the lands through which the Usâl flowed never recovered. They will never be green again.

'The drying of the River Usâl removed the last barrier between Shagâna and the Great Southern Empire of Ordâkh. The armies of the Ordâkhai marched north into Shagâna and brought formerly separate kingdoms under the control of the Hûl of Ordâkh, and the Dark Lord he served.

'Most learned men believe the Great Dearth was not caused by nature, but was the result of Ordâkhai sorcery given to them by Barkhûsh. Ever was the Dark Lord seeking to remove the barriers between the lands under his control and those he sought.''Most learned men believe that the Great Dearth was not caused by nature, but was the result of Ordâkhai sorcery given to them by Barkhûsh. Ever was the Dark Lord seeking to remove the barriers between the lands under his control and those he sought.''Can this be true? Can the sorceries of the Empire of Ordâkh truly have such an effect upon something so large as a swift-flowing river?'

Caebar immediately puts a hand over his eyes.

'No, I am being foolish. How could I have forgotten the terrible smoke that Sauron sent before his armies during the war? The day without a dawn came to all of Gondor, darkening even distant Ethring, where my family lives. My mother and uncle resented my absence on that terrible day and the weeks that followed, but I witnessed it in Minas Tirith and knew the despair of it even as they did. Of course Sauron could grant to the Ordâkhai such terrible powers, if it served his fell purposes.'

Caebar removes his hand from his eyes, now widened with sudden realization.

'Ghâlim, have you ever heard of something called the Song of Waves and Wind? Could it be some tale or legend of Sauron, or of Ordâkh? Might it be related to the Great Dearth that dried the River Usâl and brought the desert to Shagâna?''Has nothing among these legends spoken of a Song of Waves and Wind?''The Song of Waves and Wind? No, I am not familiar with such a tale or legend as that.

'The lands to the east and south of the Cape of Umbar contain many mysteries, and the histories of the people who dwell there are complex; it could be the mention of such a thing is simply buried, or lost to time. Water and wind are both of great significance to the people who dwell in Shagâna, for each have greatly affected their lives across the centuries.

'If your travels bring you to these lands, I advise you to listen to the folk who dwell there, for they will be better teachers on such matters than even I can claim to be, with all my renown!

'I sense now that I have given you what instruction you sought, and I must take my leave. Perhaps one day my student Caebar will be able to share such knowledge as this with you, but he is still young and unlearned. It will be some time. Thank you again for the return of 'Kanash-tubu-hârra.'

'I bid you good fortune if your journeys bring you into Shagâna. Farewell.''I bid you good fortune if your journeys bring you into Shagâna. Farewell.''Thank you for providing this helpful instruction to us, Ghâlim. I appreciate your teachings, and for imparting this knowledge to the both of us!'Once the preparations for your departure have been made, you will be ready to leave the city of Umbar Baharbêl behind and set forth into the lands of Shagâna.You should speak with Hármelak about the status of the preparations. The Golden Queen is inside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Hármelak in the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaHármelak has asked you to speak with the other members of the Kindred of the Coins in Dâr Laja.Talk to Belondor in Dâr LajaTalk to Khôltekh in Dâr LajaTalk to Rothog in Dâr LajaWith preparations for the journey delayed or otherwise obstructed, the Kindred has suggested that you gather at the east gate of the city and form an advance travelling party.Meet with your companions at the east gate of the citySirgon is by the east gate of the city.Talk to Sirgon by the east gate of Umbar BaharbêlBelondor is by the east gate of the city.Talk to Belondor by the east gate of Umbar Baharbêl'Part of me wishes I could come with you on this journey, ${PLAYER}... but a greater portion knows I cannot. After all, I was quite near to not surviving my last adventure with you, and might have perished among the rocks of the Shield Isles when the Wave-hunter ran aground during the storm!

'I hope some of the good fortune that spared me will attend you on your next adventure, my friend, and I will eagerly await your safe return to Umbar when it is done. You will have to tell me all about it!

'But now you plan to return to Dâr Laja and see how fare the preparations of the Kindred of the Coins? Such things must happen more swiftly in Umbar than in Gondor, for the Kindred possesses all the power of a King... but there are many more of them!''You plan to return to Dâr Laja and see how fare the preparations of the Kindred of the Coins? Such things must happen more swiftly in Umbar than in Gondor, for the Kindred possesses all the power of a King... but there are many more of them!''Have I given you the impression that my words are mere suggestions, ${PLAYER}, and not commands? How else can it be that Jakbir has denied the Kindred the use of his caravan for this journey? He thinks to increase the treasure he must be paid from the coffers of Umbar Baharbêl, as if there are no other caravans we might hire for this purpose!

'He is mistaken, and this mistake will be made clear to him. The Kindred of the Coins will not suffer such fools as he. Does he believe I do not punish those who think to take advantage of my mercy, or that of my agents? No caravan will accompany your travelling company, ${PLAYER}. I will remedy the situation with Jakbir, to his detriment, but this matter will not delay your departure. You will travel lighter than we planned. Speak to the others and learn how fare their own preparations.''I will remedy the situation with Jakbir, to his detriment, but this matter will not delay your departure. You will travel lighter than we planned. Speak to the others and learn how fare their own preparations.''Hármelak and Khôltekh speak of difficulties, but to my eyes the sun shines upon our road: Mêshka has given her blessing for this journey. My wife came to speak with me on this matter shortly after you left Dâr Laja, and she made clear her approval of this adventure. She wishes me to avenge our family and those of my crew upon the traitor Azagath and his ally Nakási, and put these sorrowful days behind us.

'Mêshka's love will follow me on this road, and it will remain when I return home in triumph. I am ready to depart, the sooner to return! After you have spoken with the others, I will meet you at the east gate of the city, with our eyes turned to the sunrise and the road ahead.''There has been a miscommunication with the owner of the warehouse that was going to supply this expedition. I will remain and... correct... this error, but as a result it will be some time before I can depart.'

You consider sharing with Khôltekh the words of Caebar, who believed the power of the Kindred would speed along the preparations, but decide to keep them to yourself instead.

'Hármelak has had some difficulties of her own arranging for the caravan, but fewer mouths to feed on the journey will require fewer supplies. Her failure makes my own task more simple, which puts a smile on my face.'

Khôltekh smiles dangerously and you take your leave of him.'I cannot leave yet. My khopeshes are sharp, for now, but the quality of the weapons I requested from Dahâl Huliz is unsatisfactory. To set forth on an unknown road, into lands that test the might and the will of a warrior, without suitable arms? Unacceptable!

'If we were a-ship I would hold he who disappointed me over the water by one foot until he satisfied the bargain. Alas that remedy is not available here: the fountains of Umbar Baharbêl are not deep enough!'

Rothog rumbles in dissatisfaction.

'There is another problem, but I will not burden you with it. I will say just this: should a friend of yours gift you an animal, ensure that the men who care for it have bravery enough to feed it, or become dinner themselves. Otherwise it may become an annoyance.

'Khôltekh waits as well. I will travel with him when he is ready, and the two of us will meet up with you when we can.''This is everyone?''Belondor tells me the rest of our travelling party has been delayed, but they will join us when they can? I confess this small group is more to my liking, but will this endanger the success of our mission?

'I would not want to face Azagath and Nakási in battle without the half-troll at our side!''This small group is more to my liking, but will this endanger the success of our mission? I would not want to face Azagath and Nakási in battle without the half-troll at our side!''Rothog and Khôltekh will join us when they can. For now, this will be our travelling company as we venture in pursuit of Azagath and Nakási.

'Onward! To victory and vengeance!'While Khôltekh and Rothog remain behind to see to the final preparations, your advance company sets forth toward the Valley of Ikorbân in the lands of Shagâna.The first step of your journey lies to the east of the city, along the Road of Riches, at the guard-post Belondor suggested.Journey to the guard-post east of Umbar BaharbêlKhashig is at his guard-post east of the city.Talk to Khashig at the guard-post east of Umbar BaharbêlSand-flies can be found in the vicinity of Khashig's guard-post, east of Umbar Baharbêl.Defeat stinging sand-flies east of the city for Khashig (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Khashig is at his guard-post east of the city.Talk to Khashig at the guard-post east of the cityCorudan is at the guard-post east of the city.Talk to Corudan at the guard-post east of the cityThe next step of your journey lies further to the east, by the ruins of Telperiën's Wall.Journey east to the guard-post beside Telperiën's WallHûta is at his guard-post beside Telperiën's Wall.Talk to Hûta at the guard-post beside Telperiën's WallHûta asked you to patrol northward and retrieve stones that have fallen from the wall.Patrol northward along Telperiën's WallRetrieve fallen stones along Telperiën's Wall (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Hûta is at his guard-post beside Telperiën's Wall.Belondor is at the guard-post beside Telperiën's Wall.Talk to Belondor at the guard-post beside Telperiën's WallHûta is at his guard-post beside Telperiën's Wall.Talk to Hûta at his guard-post beside Telperiën's WallHûta gave you directions to the village of Iridír in Ambarûl, east of Telperiën's Wall.Find the west entrance to the village of Iridír, in AmbarûlRahâzi stops in her tracks as you seek to enter the village of Iridír.Talk to Rahâzi at the entrance of Iridír'When trade with the southern kingdoms thrived, the flat ground to the east of the city was alive with a colourful assemblage of tents and caravans preparing to depart along the Road of Riches. Now there are few, for such trade has slowed to but a trickle, but theirs is the road upon which we will begin, and follow it eastward until we find some sign of Azagath.

'There should be a guard-post east along the road. Let us go there now and see what its sentries have seen.''What brings you this way, travellers?''There should be a guard-post east along the road. Let us go there now and see what its sentries have seen.''What brings you this way, travellers? The caravans have slowed in recent days, and I have had little company save for the sand-flies... they are drawn by the heat, and make my days a misery.'

Khashig peers at each of your companions in turn, and when his gaze reaches Belondor he spreads his hands in surprise.

'Belondor of the Fleet-fast? This is a pleasure and an honour! The Kindred of the Coins has spared little thought for me or for Hûta since we were sent to man these posts in the days following their rise. I see now I was mistaken!''The caravans have slowed in recent days, and I have had little company but the sand-flies... they are drawn by the heat, and make my days a misery. You will help take care of them for me?''Thank you for taking care of those insects, my friends. I know they will return, for nothing keeps the pests away for long, but even an hour's reprieve is worth celebrating! Now perhaps you will tell me what brings Belondor of the Fleet-fast to my guard-post? I cannot believe it was only to bring some comfort to poor Khashig, guardian of the road!'

Belondor explains that the four of you are seeking Azagath Sea-shadow and Nakási Wave-reaper, who you believe came this way sometime in the past few days. Khashig gazes into the distance, deep in thought.

'Ah, I wish I could tell you I have seen this travelling party, but you are the only one of that esteemed group to visit poor Khashig outside the city, Kinsman Belondor. Each member of the Kindred is well-known to the Umbari who dwell in these lands, for we count them heroes: the brave men and women who saved us from the Church of the All-seeing and the Empire of Ordâkh. If they wished to remain unseen, they might take pains to stay hidden from the eyes of any Umbari as they travelled. Also, I confess to you that my charge is to watch eastward, not westward, and the sand-flies may have affected my vigilance! But no longer, I assure you!

'If you continue east you will reach my fellow guardsman Hûta, who watches the road where it passes by Telperiën's Wall and into Ambarûl. He may have seen something. The folk of that land do not revere the Kindred as do the Umbari, and your fellows may not feel as much need for secrecy.''If you continue east you will reach my fellow guardsman Hûta, who watches the road where it passes by Telperiën's Wall and into Ambarûl. He may have seen something. The folk of that land do not revere the Kindred as do the Umbari, and your fellows may not feel as much need for secrecy.''Khashig is right, ${PLAYER}. The Kindred of the Coins are well-known to the Umbari, but as we travel further away from the city the names and faces of our quarry will be less familiar to the people we meet. Azagath and Nakási may travel more openly in the lands ahead of us.

'But we should speak with Khashig's fellow guardsman Hûta at his post where the road runs into Ambarûl.''We should speak with Khashig's fellow guardsman Hûta at his post where the road runs into Ambarûl.''Hûta might have seen something. I wish I could be more of a help to you and your companions!''Yes?''It is unusual for folk to come this way without a larger caravan behind them, strangers. I must warn you the Road of Riches has become more perilous in recent weeks, and it has never been safe.'

You ask Hûta if Azagath Sea-shadow or Nakási Wave-reaper has come this way, and he stares at you blankly.

'I have not seen Azagath, that I can tell you for certain! I cannot imagine what business could bring him so far outside the city. But I am not familiar with this woman's name, this Nakási. She sounds like a Corsair. There is water and marshland in Ambarûl, along the Ikorbân River, but I have not heard of Corsairs sailing upriver. There is simply nothing there.

'When I was commanded to keep this guard-post, I asked for men to help me guard the length of Telperiën's Wall, but I was denied. It could be the people you seek passed by the wall further to the north. Perhaps you should patrol that way and see if there is any sign of them? If you can pick up some of the fallen stones along the way I would appreciate it. From time to time I collect them and stack them here by my guard-post, but I have not made the walk recently.''It could be the people you seek passed by the wall further to the north. Perhaps you should patrol that way and see if there is any sign of them? If you can pick up some of the fallen stones along the way I would appreciate it. From time to time I collect them and stack them here by my guard-post, but I have not made the walk recently.''Thank you for collecting these stones. I understand you did not see the people you seek? I am sorry, but I appreciate you following the ruins of the wall to the north and back again. I do not patrol its length as often as perhaps I should.

'Ordâkh is a long way from here, across the southern desert, and their soldiers are in disarray. Even with the wall in such a state of disrepair I do not think the Ordâkhai can pose much of a threat to Umbar. Without the sorcery of their Dark Lord, how can they hope to threaten our soldiers?

'Perhaps I can soon return home, Kinsman Belondor?''Perhaps I can soon return home, Kinsman Belondor?''I can make no such decision today, guardsman Hûta.'

Belondor turns his gaze upon the remains of Telperiën's Wall, and when he speaks again his tone is thoughtful.

'A queen of Númenor ordered the building of this wall, long ago, to protect her coastal holdings from the inland peoples of Shagâna. It was damaged over the centuries by war, by the shifting of the earth, and by the passage of Time. She and the folk she sought to protect have been dead for thousands of years, but Telperiën's Wall still stands, in large part, marking that ancient desire.

'The things we build outlast us, guardsman Hûta. What has Umbar built?''The things we build outlast us, guardsman Hûta. What has Umbar built?''I cannot say what will remain a thousand years from now, but from my heart I speak when I say the Umbari must not suffer beneath the heel of a distant power: not Ordâkh, not Mordor, and not Gondor. We must build for ourselves lives of peace and safety that can become the envy of all the world.'

Hûta stands up straighter and taller than before.

'I will not disappoint you, Kinsman Belondor. If I allowed my watchfulness to wane in recent days, I apologize for it! No longer.

'As you pass into Ambarûl, I suggest you follow the road along the marsh until it turns south. That will bring you to the largest settlement of the region, a town built on wooden pilings that the people there call Iridír. I have seen the women warriors of that place from afar, and they appear quite deadly with the weapons they carry. You should introduce yourself to them before they consider you to be strangers and introduce themselves to you, if you take my meaning. It would be wisest for you to approach them on friendly terms while you have the opportunity!''As you pass into Ambarûl, I suggest you follow the road along the marsh until it turns south. That will bring you to the largest settlement of the region, a town built on wooden pilings that the people there call Iridír. I have seen the women warriors of that place from afar, and they appear quite deadly with the weapons they carry. You should introduce yourself to them before they consider you to be strangers and introduce themselves to you, if you take my meaning. It would be wisest for you to approach them on friendly terms while you have the opportunity!''You are a long way from home, strangers. What brings you to Iridír in Ambarûl? Speak quickly, for I am one of the Dumûri and I will not hesitate to strike you down if you bring evil here!'A young woman named Rahâzi has directed you to speak with her mother Girhâzi, leader of the village of Iridír in Ambarûl.Rahâzi runs into the wild, leaving you to speak to her mother Girhâzi.Wait for Rahâzi to depart the villageAccording to Rahâzi, her mother Girhâzi will be interested in speaking with you within her lodge to the south.Find Girhâzi within the lodge inside the village of IridírGirhâzi is inside her lodge in the village of Iridír, in Ambarûl.Talk to Girhâzi inside her lodge within IridírBelondor is in Girhâzi's lodge within the village of Iridír.Talk to Belondor inside Girhâzi's lodge within IridírGirhâzi is inside her lodge within the village of Iridír, in Ambarûl.Talk to Girhâzi inside her lodge within IridírRahâzi was sent to the insect den of Gurakhu, north-west of Iridír, and now her mother Girhâzi wants you to help with her assigned task.Talk to Rahâzi at the insect den of Gurakhu, north-west of IridírRahâzi has asked you to collect the bristles she was sent to retrieve from the insects at Gurakhu, west of Iridír. In the interest of continued collaboration, you should do what you can to help Girhâzi's daughter.Collect zizanu bristles for Rahâzi (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rahâzi is at Gurakhu, north-west of Iridír, waiting for you to return to her with her task complete.Rahâzi's fellow Dumûri warrior, Surdûsha, is at Gurakhu, north-west of Iridír.Talk to Surdûsha at GurakhuRahâzi is at Gurakhu in Ambarûl.Talk to Rahâzi at Gurakhu'Well? What have you to say?'

You assure the warrior that you and your companions have come to Ambarûl with no intention to cause harm to any of the folk who live here.

'So might say any foe of the Ikorbâni who live alongside the river, but from the look of you I believe the Dumûri need not fear you. My mother will be the judge of that. Present yourself to Girhâzi inside the lodge, but know this: if she finds your motive suspect or your words untrustworthy, you will not leave this village.

'Had I not a task of my own I would stay to see what befalls you, but duty awaits me outside Iridír.''The eyes of my Dumûri have been upon you ever since you entered Ambarûl. Speak now and relate what brings you to the lands under my protection.'

You introduce yourself and your companions, and when you identify Belondor as a member of the Kindred of the Coins, Girhâzi eyes him appraisingly.

'Word has come to me of your ruling body, Belondor, but I would learn more of the rebellion that pried your city from the grip of Barkhûsh and the Ordâkhai. We are neighbours now, my people and yours, and our interests may be aligned.'

Girhâzi lowers her voice almost to a whisper.

'I say "may," for you say you hail from Umbar, but I take you for a man of Gondor who keeps company with Elves. Your people have long been enemies of mine. What now is different?''Your people and mine have long been enemies. What now is different?''I was born in Gondor, Girhâzi of Iridír, but I am now a man of Umbar, and that is where my loyalties lie. Are you the same as you were when you were young? I deem not. We are not who we were, and could that be different for any of us? This is an age of great change.

'I would be honoured to speak to you of the relationship between Umbar and the folk who live in Ambarûl, for we are neighbours now, as you say.''I would be honoured to speak to Girhâzi of the relationship between Umbar and the folk who live in Ambarûl.''The words spoken by your companion Belondor are smooth as the stones at the bottom of the Ikorbân River... but that does not mean there is no truth in them.

'I am changed by the months that have passed. My husband and all my sons perished on the fields of the Northern War, slain beneath the banner of the Black Serpent they followed. We were told Ordâkh was invincible; it was not so. When the survivors began to return, all too few, it was made clear to me the cause that had taken most of my family was a lie. I cast down the tools of the field and picked up the tools of war, and under a new name I rallied the people to burn the temple of Iridír. The banner of the Black Serpent no longer flies in Ambarûl. My Dumûri warriors, the Daughters of Iridír, have sworn to keep the peace and hunt the remnants of Ordâkh wherever they hide.

'I have sent my daughter Rahâzi to the insect den of Gurakhu, north-west of Iridír. Go there and assist her with her task while I speak with Belondor concerning how he came to cast the Ordâkhai from his city. I am most interested to hear how it was done, and what brings he and you into Ambarûl.''I have sent my daughter Rahâzi to the insect den of Gurakhu, north-west of Iridír. Go there and assist her with her task while I speak with Belondor concerning how he came to cast the Ordâkhai from his city. I am most interested to hear how it was done, and what brings he and you into Ambarûl.''You still live! Your reasons for coming to Ambarûl must have satisfied my mother. But why have you come to Gurakhu?'

You tell Rahâzi that her mother asked you to help with her task while Girhâzi discusses the relationship between Umbar and Iridír with Belondor.

'Help? I need no help crushing insects! I am one of the Dumûri, the women warriors of Iridír! These zizanu insects were unworthy of my attention to begin, and I only accepted the task out of deference to my mother, and so the craftswomen of Iridír will have the bristles they need for their work.

'Now that you are here, you can do it instead. I will see how you perform and measure your skill. Collect the zizanu bristles as quickly as you can. Perhaps that is why my mother sent you here?''Now that you are here, you can do this task instead. I will see how you perform and measure your skill. Collect the zizanu bristles as quickly as you can. Perhaps that is why my mother sent you here?'The distraction writ upon Rahâzi's face is clear to you as you report finishing the task.

'Good enough,' she answers you.

'Surdûsha has brought wondrous tidings to me! Speak to her and hear them!''Surdûsha has brought wondrous tidings to me! Speak to her and hear them!''While hunting to the north-east of Iridír, I found a scouting party of Ordâkhai setting up a small encampment from which they might threaten the village! They were few in number, but not so few I thought I should attack them on my own.

'With Rahâzi I knew I could face them, so I hurried here to bring her back with me. I trust you are a friend?''I found a scouting party of Ordâkhai setting up a small encampment from which they might threaten the village! They were few in number, but not so few I thought I should attack them on my own. With Rahâzi I knew I could face them, so I hurried here to bring her back with me. I trust you are a friend?''Our choice is clear! The Ordâkhai must be defeated, and driven from Ambarûl!'Rahâzi is eager to hunt down and defeat the Ordâkhai who remain in Ambarûl to threaten her people.Ordâkhai remnants gather at a war-camp to the north-east of Iridír, in Ambarûl.Find the camp of the Ordâkhai to the north-east of IridírDefeat Ordâkhai warriors throughout Ambarûl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Surdûsha keeps a watchful eye on Omboha.Talk to Surdûsha outside OmbohaSurdûsha told you that Rahâzi pursued fleeing Ordâkhai warriors to the north.Find Rahâzi to the north of OmbohaRahâzi pursued the Ordâkhai to the outskirts of the grove of Arwáth.Talk to Rahâzi outside the grove of ArwáthOutside the grove of Arwáth, Rahâzi asked you to search her foes for signs of their purpose in Ambarûl.Pick up the weapon of the slain OrdâkhaiSearch the body of the slain Ordâkhai warrior outside ArwáthRahâzi is interested to examine the items you collected from the body of her foe.Rahâzi believes you should venture within the grove of Arwáth and ensure that no Ordâkhai hide within.Venture within the grove of Arwáth to the northThe Elf-warden Shanthar stands within the grove of Arwáth.Talk to Shanthar at ArwáthShanthar suggested that you <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> for the faded Kintai in the grove of Arwáth.The Elf-warden Shanthar stands within the grove of Arwáth.Talk to Shanthar at ArwáthRahâzi is on the outskirts of the grove of Arwáth.Talk to Rahâzi outside the grove of Arwáth'When my mother burned the temple at Iridír and drove the Ordâkhai from the village, some of them must have remained hidden in the wild. Now they seek to return and take back Ambarûl, but we will not allow that to happen!

'The three of us must go to the Ordâkhai encampment Surdûsha discovered north-east of Iridír and put them all to the sword! This is a task for Dumûri warriors... and for those who claim to be their allies.''The three of us must go to the Ordâkhai encampment Surdûsha discovered north-east of Iridír and put them all to the sword! This is a task for Dumûri warriors... and for those who claim to be their allies.''While Rahâzi and I fought the soldiers here, some of the men broke away from the rest of the group and fled to the north. Rahâzi chased them, but we should hurry after her and ensure she has not fallen victim to a trap set by the cunning Ordâkhai!''While Rahâzi and I fought the soldiers here, some of the men broke away from the rest of the group and fled to the north. Rahâzi chased them, but we should hurry after her and ensure she has not fallen victim to a trap set by the cunning Ordâkhai!''I am fine. These men were no match for me!''I am fine. These men were no match for me!

'We should search their bodies and take their weapons. They may have items among their belongings that help us prevent further attacks upon my people. A map to other of their encampments would be the most welcome of all!''We should search their bodies and take their weapons. They may have items among their belongings that help us prevent further attacks upon my people. A map to other of their encampments would be the most welcome of all!''See the cuts and scrapes on this weapon? This soldier of Ordâkh has been involved in many battles, perhaps even in Iridír itself! He may have fled from my mother and her warriors, even as he fled from me and from Surdûsha today. But no longer. He landed no blow upon me when he turned to fight; see how dry is the blood upon the blade?

'This sigil is a token of Barkhûsh, the Dark Lord who is no more. It is one more sign that the Ordâkhai have no honour, and without the fell magic of their dread master they have no future either... at least not in Ambarûl.

'We should search the wooded grove to the north and ensure no others of the Ordâkhai hide within.''We should search this wooded grove to the north and ensure no others of the Ordâkhai hide within.''I heard sounds of fighting without.''I heard sounds of fighting without the grove. Was there danger? Has it dispersed?'

You tell the Elf that a warrior from the nearby village defeated a number of Ordâkhai soldiers beyond the ring of trees, and the danger has passed. He contemplates your words and nods slowly.

'So often that is the way for the Avabár-kintai,' he says to you. 'We struggle to maintain our wooded groves in a land that was once fair, but they remain shrinking oases as dearth and danger press in on all sides.

'I am Shanthar, and I count myself among the Kintai, wandering Elves who settled in this fertile country of forests and wetlands, long ago. We are fewer in number now, and we who remain tend our homes as the lands around them fail.'

You ask Shanthar if he lives in this grove by himself, and he smiles sadly.

'The Kintai have no interest in departing the circles of the world, as I have heard other Elves may. When my people reach old age, they become... untethered... in spirit. They begin to fade, and in time they belong to the natural world. Some even now remain in this grove, and if you <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> beneath the scarlet leaves of the Sarláshi trees you might hear them.''The Kintai have no interest in departing the circles of the world, as I have heard other Elves may. When my people reach old age, they become... untethered... in spirit. They begin to fade, and in time they belong to the natural world. Some even now remain in this grove, and if you <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> beneath the scarlet leaves of the Sarláshi trees you might hear them.''Could you feel the presence of my people that dwell here in Arwáth, though their bodies have long since faded?'

You admit to Shanthar that you did not, and he smiles with understanding.

'Even I do not always feel them near, friend, but it is a comfort to know they remain in this Middle-earth. To depart from these lands seems to me, and to others of the Kintai, frightening and unnatural: a terrible and unknowable afterlife. That road is closed to us, and we are glad of it.

'If you would give to the warrior who remains outside Arwáth the thanks of warden Shanthar and the faded Kintai who dwell here, I would be grateful. I am not without my defences, but I have not been required to make use of them for some years.''If you would give to the warrior who remains outside Arwáth the thanks of warden Shanthar and the faded Kintai who dwell here, I would be grateful. I am not without my defences, but I have not been required to make use of them for some years.''The grove is inhabited by an Elf-warden? How interesting. It is quite rare to encounter one of the Kintai, for they concern themselves mainly with their groves and do not venture from them without great need. If the Ordâkhai located the Elf within Arwáth, they would have slain him easily. He is fortunate we came here.'

Rahâzi surveys the grove and then turns to face in the direction of Iridír.

'You have proven yourself a help to me, stranger. Perhaps it is time you tell me your name.'Sigileth and Silirtas have found something in the dry reaches of Khûd Zagin.Sigileth has asked you to examine the dead man to discover what spelled his end.Examine the dead man by Sigileth and Silirtis in Khûd ZaginSigileth is in southern Khûd Zagin.Talk to Sigileth in southern Khûd ZaginThe man is barely alive, but a gift of water may help bring him back from death's brink.Offer water to the dying man in southern Khûd ZaginYou have done what you can for the dying man, and should give Sigileth's water bladder back to her.Silirtas stands in southern Khûd Zagin by the dying man.Talk to Silirtas by the dying manSilirtas hurries away.Wait for Silirtas to departSigileth stands by the dying man in southern Khûd Zagin.Talk to Sigileth in southern Khûd ZaginSigileth believes the dying man can be saved, but if he is to recover at Shonith you will need Tharashil's permission.Return to Tharashil in south-western ShonithTharashil is on the leader's rise in south-western Shonith.Talk to Tharashil in south-western ShonithBalakhâd is behind the pavilions to the north of the water in Shonith.Find Balakhâd behind the pavilions north of the water in ShonithBalakhâd is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Balakhâd in Shonith'No, I do not think this dead man is Sadûkh after all.

'I think this man might have been Balakhâd, ${PLAYER}. I think this was the son of Balakhôr, and he was the last of the Heirs of Castamir.''I think this man might have been Balakhâd. I think this was the son of Balakhôr, and he was the last of the Heirs of Castamir.''He lives still?

'He will not live for long without water. He may already have gone too long without it. Here, give him some of this... but slowly, or you might harm more than heal.''Give him some of that water... but slowly, or you might harm more than heal.''That may help him, if he is not already beyond saving. How did he come to be so far from the road? What happened to him after he left the tomb of Nirgambâr, where we lost his trail? He would not have purposefully left his satchel in that tomb if he planned to spend days alone in the heat, ${PLAYER}.

'Silirtas, would your people give their aid to a man this near to death?''Silirtas, would your people give their aid to a man this near to death?''The Kintai do not willingly refuse their aid, when they have the ability to give it.

'I will return with haste to Shonith and tell Tharashil there is need for her healing arts.''I have a question for you, ${PLAYER}.

'As the son of Balakhôr and the last of the Heirs of Castamir, Balakhâd is undeniably an enemy of not only the Kingdom of Gondor but of my people specifically; the hatred of the Heirsworn for Elves is well-known. It is in our power to help him. If our paths had not crossed his, he likely would have died without our knowledge. And yet here we stand. What is the right course?'

You give Sigileth your opinion, and she nods.

'It is a difficult question, but this is my answer: if the healing arts of the Kintai are sufficient to coax him back to health, he may tell us what Azagath and Nakási are planning, or where they have gone. Whatever he knows may be of crucial importance to us.

'It is perhaps a meaningless choice, ${PLAYER}, for Silirtas has already gone to tell Tharashil what we have found. To return now without him would be... difficult to explain. Let us go to the Oasis at Shonith and learn if the healing arts of the Kintai will resolve this philosophical difficulty for us, or raise further such questions.''It is perhaps a meaningless choice, for Silirtas has already gone to tell Tharashil what we have found. To return now without him would be... difficult to explain. Let us go to the Oasis at Shonith and learn if the healing arts of the Kintai will resolve this philosophical difficulty for us, or raise further such questions.''Silirtas has told me of your dead man, and a place has been made for him. The healers who dwell here at Shonith will see what can be done for his injuries, but he spent too long exposed to the harsh environment of the desert to recover quickly, or completely. A single day and night without cover would be too many, and he suffered several.

'It is not customary for the Kintai to welcome Men such as he to our groves. He is here at your request, and Sigileth's. The two of you must be responsible for him.

'You will find him behind the pavilions to the north of the water here in Shonith.''It is not customary for the Kintai to welcome Men such as he to our groves. He is here at your request, and Sigileth's. The two of you must be responsible for him.

'You will find him behind the pavilions to the north of the water here in Shonith.''What is... who are...'

Balakhâd's voice is dry and raspy. He stares wildly around himself and you are put in mind of an animal caught in a trap. He sees the Elves gathered nearby and his cracked lip curls up in a sneer.

'You are... going to kill me...?'

You tell him that the Elves of the Oasis have agreed to try and heal his various hurts, and may have already saved his life. You ask him how he came to be by himself in the desert, and say too that you know the water-bearer Sadûkh helped him escape from the Kindred of the Coins. Despite the sun damage and his scorched skin, the expression of confusion on his face is readily apparent.

'How do you know all this?' he asks.Balakhâd lives at the mercy of the Kintai of Shonith, and may have information concerning Azagath and Nakási that will be of use to you... if he can be convinced to share it with you.Silirtas stands by Balakhâd at Shonith, in Khûd Zagin.Talk to Silirtas in ShonithBalakhâd is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Balakhâd in ShonithSigileth is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Sigileth in ShonithBalakhâd is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Balakhâd in ShonithBalakhâd is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Balakhâd and listen to what befell him at NirgambârBalakhâd is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Balakhâd in ShonithSigileth is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Sigileth in ShonithSilirtas is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Silirtas in ShonithBalakhâd is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Balakhâd in ShonithSilirtas is behind the pavilions north of the water in Shonith.Talk to Silirtas in Shonith'There is no freedom for me. I have only traded one set of captors for another, and then another.

'The Ordâkhai want me dead. The Kindred freed me from them... but only so they might have... the privilege of seeing me slain. And these Elves...'

Balakhâd weakly raises an arm to gesture at the Kintai nearby. His mouth drops open with an expression of disgust and alarm.

'What... what is wrong with this one?''What is wrong with this She-Elf?'Silirtas stands beside the wounded man, her expression slack and her eyes empty and unseeing. Balakhâd stares up at her with an expression of disgust visible on his sun-damaged face.'She lives... but she is unaware of her surroundings? She works some vile Elf-magic against us?''What is wrong with this She-Elf?''Remember that you live only at the behest of the Kintai, Balakhâd. You would be wise to keep a politic tongue in your head, or you may find yourself once again left to die in the desert.

'That is what happened to you, is it not? You left Nirgambâr in the company of Azagath and Nakási, but your new friends thought to abandon you within the inhospitable waste. They exhausted whatever use they had of you, and left you to die.''That is what happened to you, is it not? You left Nirgambâr in the company of Azagath and Nakási, but your new friends thought to abandon you within the inhospitable waste. They exhausted whatever use they had of you, and left you to die.''They are no friends of mine!' he hisses, and you are surprised to see the quivering of his eyes: if he was not so parched, he might weep.

'If I tell you what they said... you will let me go? You will not allow these Elves to work their terrible enchantments upon me?'Balakhâd seems almost desperate to earn your promise that the Kintai will not enchant him.

'I will tell you what they said! I will tell you everything!''They led me out into the desert with a ragged cloth tied over my eyes. We walked for most of the night. At last Nakási hit me upon the head with the hilt of her weapon, and senselessly I collapsed into the sand.

'When I awoke, there was no sign of them; the wind had covered their tracks. I sought to make my way northward under the burning sun, but thirst and delirium slowed my progress. I fell down rocky slopes and considered giving up, letting the sand cover me, but as death approached I resolved not to let it end that way. Always I stood again, and continued my desperate march from the desert.

'Did I survive the desert only to fall victim to the unnatural sorceries of Elves? No. No!''Did I survive the desert only to fall victim to the unnatural sorceries of Elves? No. No!''The Kintai will do nothing against your will, Balakhâd, unless you want not the healing of your body and your mind.

'But I am most concerned with your description of the glass phial Nakási kept upon her person. From this account it appears they possess a small quantity of Narghâk, and seek to obtain more by making use of the map Azagath took from the Mâkhda Khorbo.

'My knowledge of this substance is limited... but it sounds very dangerous.''I am most concerned with your description of the glass phial Nakási kept upon her person. From this account it appears they possess a small quantity of Narghâk, and seek to obtain more by making use of the map Azagath took from the Mâkhda Khorbo. My knowledge of this substance is limited... but it sounds very dangerous.'At some point during Balakhâd's tale, Silirtas regained her awareness. She turns now to you with her eyes alight.

'I am intrigued by this man's story, and wish to know more about the villains who tortured him so. If I might help you prevent them from reaching their goal, I would do so. Such an adventure will surely forestall my fading, but that is not my only reason for suggesting it. I have before heard of this Narghâk, this Hungry Fire, and my people will be endangered for as long as such a thing exists in the world.

'What more can this Balakhâd tell us of the Hungry Fire?''What more can this Balakhâd tell us of the Hungry Fire?'Balakhâd grimaces with pain and refuses to speak any further.Balakhâd grimaces with pain and refuses to speak any further.'He knows more than what he wishes to say to us.

'If we can better ease his pain, he may be willing to speak further of the Hungry Fire.'Girhâzi has given you assistance and supplies to help you on the road into and through Khûd Zagin... but not without a price.The food and water Girhâzi has provided for your travelling party can be found at the entrance of the village.Restock your supplies using Girhâzi's provided foodstuffsRestock your supplies using Girhâzi's provided waterKhôltekh is at the village entrance of Iridír.Talk to Khôltekh at the village entrance of IridírSomeone approaches at a run.Wait for the new arrivalRahâzi has arrived at the entrance of her village.Talk to Rahâzi at the village entrance of IridírRothog has something to say.Talk to Rothog at the village entrance of IridírBelondor also has something to say to you.Talk to Belondor at the village entrance of IridírThe time has come for your travelling party to take the road south into Khûd Zagin.Take the road south into Khûd Zagin from AmbarûlCorudan stands beside the road leading south into Khûd Zagin.Talk to Corudan along the road into Khûd ZaginCorudan has suggested that you defeat the birds, jackals or servals, and lizards or snakes that threaten your southward journey into Khûd Zagin.Defeat hunting birds in Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat jackals or servals in Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat lizards or snakes in Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Belondor stands beside the road that runs from Ambarûl into Khûd Zagin.Talk to Belondor by the road that runs from Ambarûl into Khûd Zagin'Girhâzi has supplied us with food and water with which we can restock for the journey ahead, ${PLAYER}. Make sure you have enough of each. In the days of my shipboard service to Gondor, few things were as necessary as stocking the larder of your ship with water and food. After all, you might not set foot upon land for days or weeks!

'We travel afoot now and not by ship, but what the Ambarûli say of the land to the south reminds me of my past voyages. There may be little opportunity in the days ahead to restock our supplies, so ensure you have what you will need from Girhâzi's provisions.''We travel afoot now and not by ship, but what the Ambarûli say of the land to the south reminds me of my past voyages. There may be little opportunity in the days ahead to restock our supplies, so ensure you have what you will need from Girhâzi's provisions.''What does the leader of these folk ask from us, ${PLAYER}? No one provides something of value without gaining something in return, and Belondor said this Girhâzi mentioned a price of some kind to him. Not only that, but some among our company have accepted gifts from the Ambarûli! The Elf Corudan admired some new arrows he was gifted, and one of the craftswomen here mended the old Gondorian's boots with clean stitching and fresh patches.

'What is Girhâzi's price for these supplies and these gifts from her people? More importantly, now that they have been accepted, how will we refuse the cost she asks?''Ah. The cost reveals itself.''My mother has commanded that I join your travelling company, ${PLAYER}. I will serve as your guide within the lands through which you travel, and my knowledge of the Shagâni people will be of help to you. In this way my mother also hopes to secure the bonds of friendship between the Ambarûli and the people of Umbar, unless any of the Kindred here object?'

Belondor, Rothog, and Khôltekh remain silent, but the dwarf stares at you meaningfully.

'There is one thing more,' Rahâzi continues. 'My mother insists, and I agree, that I must be allowed to combat the Ordâkhai as we encounter them on this journey. I must be permitted to diminish their ability to threaten Ambarûl if such opportunities present themselves.''My mother insists, and I agree, that I must be allowed to combat the Ordâkhai as we encounter them on this journey. I must be permitted to diminish their ability to threaten Ambarûl if such opportunities present themselves.''I approve of this warrior! What is the name of her order? The Dumûri?

'I too desire to combat the Ordâkhai whenever such becomes possible, and Húrshauk feels the same. We welcome this warrior woman to our band.''I too desire to combat the Ordâkhai whenever such becomes possible, and Húrshauk feels the same. We welcome this warrior woman to our band.''Girhâzi has done much for us, and to provide her own daughter as a guide on the road is more than I might have expected. I hope her requirement that she be permitted to fight the Ordâkhai will not prove an inconvenience.

'But we should set out, for the road ahead will be a long one. We will worry about the price Girhâzi has called for us to pay another time.

'Let us depart, and travel to the southern reaches of Ambarûl, where the road runs into Khûd Zagin. Once there, we will plan our further course.''Let us depart, and travel to the southern reaches of Ambarûl, where the road runs into Khûd Zagin. Once there, we will plan our further course.''Let us stop here for now.''Let us stop here for now. I suggest that the two of us move ahead into Khûd Zagin and scout the terrain that awaits us. While we walked, Rahâzi spoke to me of the creatures we can expect to encounter in this land: birds on the wing, jackals and servals among the sands, and lizards or snakes baking among the rocks.

'I would like to test some of the arrows I was gifted by the people of Iridír. Shall we hunt some of these creatures and learn the lay of the land?''While we walked, Rahâzi spoke to me of the creatures we can expect to encounter in this land: birds on the wing, jackals or servals among the sands, and snakes or lizards baking among the rocks. I would like to test some of the arrows I was gifted by the people of Iridír. Shall we hunt some of these creatures and learn the lay of the land?''At last you two return! The others told me to wait here for you.

'Something happened while you were away.'Your travelling party encountered some difficulty with highwaymen on the road into Khûd Zagin and followed them back to their hideout.The hideout of Ghalbûru is among the rocks south-east of the road through Khûd Zagin.Find the entrance to Ghalbûru in Khûd ZaginSirgon is at the north-western entrance of Ghalbûru.Talk to Sirgon in GhalbûruRothog is by the water in Ghalbûru.Talk to Rothog in GhalbûruRahâzi is by the water in Ghalbûru.Talk to Rahâzi in GhalbûruKhôltekh is inside the caves of Ghalbûru. The entrance to the caves is in the heights on the eastern side of Ghalbûru.Find Khôltekh inside the caves of GhalbûruKhôltekh has found an injured man resting in the caves of Ghalbûru.Examine the injured man lying on the bed in the caves of GhalbûruKhôltekh is in the caves of Ghalbûru.Talk to Khôltekh in the caves of GhalbûruGhalbûru's apothecary Babarín is located outside in the village, beneath her canopy on the island in the middle of the pool.Talk to Babarín, the apothecary in GhalbûruIngredients needed by Babarín can be found throughout Ghalbûru: hardy plant leaves and riverside reeds.Collect hardy plant leaves in Ghalbûru (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect riverside reeds in Ghalbûru (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Babarín is beneath her canopy on the island in the middle of the pool.It will take some time for Babarín to prepare the soothing poultice. You can wait for it to be ready, or you can speak to your companions throughout Ghalbûru.Babarín is beneath her canopy on the island in the middle of the pool.Talk to Babarín in GhalbûruSadûkh is resting in the caves of Ghalbûru. The entrance to the caves is located in the heights on the eastern side of Ghalbûru.'We were waylaid by a number of Shagâni highwaymen who mistook us for vulnerable travellers; Rothog and his khopeshes cured them of that misconception very quickly. After Rahâzi made clear to them that we share an enemy in the Ordâkhai, they invited us to their hideout in the rocks to the south-east, a place called Ghalbûru.

'I stayed behind to tell you where everyone has gone. Let us now join them at Ghalbûru.''I stayed behind to tell you where everyone has gone. Let us now join them at Ghalbûru to the south-east.''I see Belondor found you, my friend.''I see Belondor found you, my friend.

'The folk who dwell here name this place Ghalbûru, which they told me means "Thieves' Cut." I do not think they gave it that name, but they have embraced it. These caves must have been home for brigands and highwaymen throughout the years, but now that the Ordâkhai have tightened their grip on Khûd Zagin, Ghalbûru has become a hideout for those who resist that empire.

'I am reminded of our stay in the Shield Isles, ${PLAYER}. The freebooters there share something in common with these rebels, I think, though neither knows it. You should have seen how rapidly they lowered their weapons when Rothog raised his own! Both these rebels and the freebooters of the Shield Isles can make rapid calculations when they must!''I am reminded of our stay in the Shield Isles, ${PLAYER}. The freebooters there share something in common with these rebels, I think, though neither knows it. You should have seen how rapidly they lowered their weapons when Rothog raised his own! Both these rebels and the freebooters of the Shield Isles can make rapid calculations when they must!''These highwaymen thought to rob us of coin, so I showed to them the medallion that marks me as one of the Kindred of the Coins; they recognized it not. The city of Umbar is a good distance away from this arid land, and word travels slowly.

'Then I showed to them my khopeshes, and that was a language they understood. The language of death travels quickly in every land, ${PLAYER}! Ha! Ha! Ha!''The language of death travels quickly in every land! Ha! Ha! Ha!''I did not think we would encounter the Khûdi so quickly, but they had eyes upon our group from the moment we passed beyond the Ikorbân River. They must be desperate for wealth with which to conduct their rebellion against the Ordâkhai. Why else should they attack a group such as ours, with so many evident warriors and our own deadly weapons near at hand?

'There is a cavern in the heights on the eastern side of Ghalbûru, and more of the Khûdi dwell within. The dwarf Khôltekh asked to see it.''The Khûdi must be desperate for wealth with which to conduct their rebellion against the Ordâkhai.

'There is a cavern in the heights on the eastern side of Ghalbûru, and more of the Khûdi dwell within. The dwarf Khôltekh asked to see it.''The highwaymen of Ghalbûru tell me they found this man dying beside the road outside Jirush, left for dead by the Ordâkhai. They brought him back here to interrogate him about the caravan with which he travelled, and if any of its treasure might have been overlooked by the Ordâkhai. It seems he was too badly injured to speak to them, so they have abandoned the effort. He has been left for dead twice now, ${PLAYER}.'

Khôltekh lowers his voice and speaks to you in hushed tones.

'Did you take note of the clothing he wears? He is dressed in the Umbari fashion, and matches the description I was given for the water-bearer that aided in Balakhâd's escape from the Citadel of Winds. Sadûkh he was named, yes? I deem he travelled with no caravan, but in the company of a single disguised man. The whereabouts of that disguised man are of great interest to me, and to the Kindred of the Coins, as you are well aware.

'The highwaymen have abandoned their efforts to restore him to health, but we need him to tell us what has happened to Balakhâd. Speak to the apothecary outside in Ghalbûru proper and learn what we will need to save this man's life. I care not if he dies, but he must not die before he tells us what he knows.''We need this man to tell us what has happened to Balakhâd. Speak to the apothecary here in Ghalbûru and learn what we will need to save this man's life. I care not if he dies, but he must not die before he tells us what he knows.''You need a remedy for the dying man that was lately brought to Ghalbûru? I will not say he is beyond saving, but he lost a great deal of blood to the blades of the Ordâkhai stationed in Jirush. They would not have purposefully left him alive; they believed he was dead already.

'I can make a poultice that may help him, but I have exhausted my supply of ingredients. You must bring me the hardy plant leaves and riverside reeds I will need to fashion this remedy. You can find them around Ghalbûru if you truly must see this man healed.''I can make a poultice that may help him, but I have exhausted my supply of ingredients. You must bring me the hardy plant leaves and riverside reeds I will need to fashion this remedy. You can find them around Ghalbûru if you truly must see this man healed.''You have returned with the ingredients? I confess I did not think you would.

'I will make the poultice for which you have asked. It will take some time. Such things cannot be rushed. You must hope your friend can survive until it is ready.

'I recommend you keep yourself busy. Perhaps then you will not notice the time passing.''The making of the poultice will take some time. Such things cannot be rushed. You must hope your friend can survive until it is ready.

'I recommend you keep yourself busy. Perhaps then you will not notice the time passing.''Is it true that Khôltekh identified the dying Umbari man the highwaymen brought to Ghalbûru to be Azagath's water-bearer Sadûkh? That is most surprising! The dwarf must have an eye for faces to have so readily identified him so far from the city.

'What do you think Sadûkh might tell us of Balakhôr's son Balakhâd? If he still lives, the dreams of the Heirs of Castamir remain alive, and may cause problems for Gondor in the future.''The people who live here frequently quarrel among themselves, finding it difficult to agree on a single course of action. That is not a problem we have in Umbar Baharbêl, for the Kindred of the Coins is a strong body, made up of strong voices.

'That is not to say the rebels of Ghalbûru lack wisdom. They seem quite frightened of both me and Húrshauk... and is that not both knowledgeable and wise?''The Khûdi who dwell here at Ghalbûru are brigands and highwaymen, ${PLAYER}, disorganized and given to quarrelling among themselves instead of uniting effectively against the Ordâkhai. If they were responsible for driving the Ordâkhai out of Ambarûl, instead of my mother Girhâzi, Iridír would still be occupied by soldiers of the Empire!

'With only five or six Dumûri to fight alongside me I might make things different in Khûd Zagin, but it is not so.''Sadûkh's condition has worsened. I care not if he dies, but I would rather he first told us of Balakhâd's fate.

'I would know if we must be concerned about the Heirs of Castamir. As long as Balakhâd might remain somewhere alive in the wild, the Heirs will remain a threat to Umbar and to the Kindred.''Before we left Iridír for Khûd Zagin I said we might speak in private of the dreams that troubled our rest. Do you wish to speak of them now? Of what did you dream, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Sigileth that the details of your dream faded with the dawn, but you remember that her brother Corudan spent some portion of it in despair, seeking for Sigileth but unable to find her. You also remember one of the Nazgûl leading a band of Orcs in search of something in an unknown land, and the words "beyond living memory," but their significance has faded with the night. Sigileth nods thoughtfully.

'I dreamt of a long corridor leading into darkness, ${PLAYER}. I sought something, though I cannot remember what, but I knew it lay at the end of the corridor. I think Cruel Dancer was beside me, but when I turned my head to look at her she was gone. I am reminded now of the Room of Voices beneath Dol Guldur, but the feeling of this corridor was... different, somehow.

'I awoke disturbed. It may have been only a dream, but my people know such dreams often foretell the future, or shed light upon the past, and should not be ignored.''Who is this man Khôltekh has tasked you with healing, ${PLAYER}?'

You quietly describe what you were told concerning Sadûkh and how the Kindred of the Coins believes Azagath's former servant might have spirited Balakhâd from Umbar and into the wild. Corudan purses his lips in thought.

'Something is not right here. I know not what, but this puzzling mosaic seems to be missing some pieces. Keep your eyes open.''Is it true the injured man you seek to heal is Azagath's former water-bearer Sadûkh? If he might be made to tell us what happened to Balakhâd, we might count one of our objectives satisfied... and so early on our journey!''The poultice is prepared. I hope it provides some comfort for your friend, and will help to heal his hurts. He will have a long road ahead of him, for the swords of the Ordâkhai brought him very near to death, but the use of this poultice should help him to recover.''I hope that poultice provides some comfort for your friend, and will help to heal his hurts.''Wh-wh-' he struggles to speak as you administer the poultice. After a moment, he closes his eyes.

'I... thank you...' he says weakly. His eyes flutter open once again and he manages to turn his head to look past you. A look of terror crosses his face as he sees Khôltekh standing nearby.

'He... he is here! He... seeks to punish me!'Sadûkh sees enemies all around him, believing there to be knives everywhere... even within Ghalbûru.In the caves of Ghalbûru, Sadûkh seeks assurances that you will protect him from his enemies.Assure Sadûkh that you will protect himSadûkh is resting in the caves of Ghalbûru.Talk to Sadûkh in the caves of GhalbûruKhôltekh is in the caves of Ghalbûru.Talk to Khôltekh in the caves of GhalbûruRahâzi is by the water in Ghalbûru.Talk to Rahâzi in GhalbûruRahâzi has suggested that you go to the town of Jirush in the desert to the west and see if any of the townsfolk there know Balakhad's location.Travel to the entrance of Jirush, located in the desert to the westTownsfolk of Jirush can be found throughout the city.Townsfolk of Jirush can be found throughout the city.Erinesh may know something about Balakhad's location, but he wants you to collect jackal teeth for him before he tells you anything. Jackals can be found throughout Khûd Zagin.Collect sharp teeth from jackals in Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Erinesh stands in the centre of Jirush.Rahâzi is in the centre of Jirush.Talk to Rahâzi in JirushRahâzi suggested that you return to Sadûkh to tell him what you learned of Balakhad's location before you help her infiltrate the fortress above Jirush. Sadûkh is recovering from his injuries in the caves of Ghalbûru.Talk to Sadûkh in the caves of GhalbûruCorudan is outside the entrance to the caves of Ghalbûru, in the heights on the eastern side of the village.Talk to Corudan outside the entrance to the caves of GhalbûruCorudan asked you to go to the north-eastern entrance of Ghalbûru and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for some sign of Sadûkh.Corudan asked you to go to the south-western entrance to Ghalbûru and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for some sign of Sadûkh.Urugal has beckoned for you to speak with him.Talk to Urugal at the south-western entrance to GhalbûruCorudan is outside the entrance to the caves of Ghalbûru, located in the heights on the eastern side of the village.Talk to Corudan outside the entrance to the caves of Ghalbûru'There are knives everywhere! Everywhere!'

Sadûkh's eyes dart from your face to Khôltekh and back again.

'Promise me... promise me you will protect me...' he gasps through the pain.'It is of no use, then...' he murmurs sadly. 'The Kindred... the Kindred will never let me live... knowing I helped their prisoner escape their clutches. They did not know I prayed for... for the return of the Heirs of Castamir. I received word from Saklaphêl that... if I aided her son in his escape, she would attack the city and overthrow... the usurping Kindred!'

Puzzled, you ask how the widow Saklaphêl was able to send a message to Sadûkh from her exile in the Shield Isles. He coughs wetly, and tries to dab away the blood.

'Every morning... I collected the jar set aside for me and filled it with water from the bay. It was the task of water-bearers... to fill the fountains within the Citadel for Azagath. On several mornings... there were letters for me hidden beneath my jar. They told me to bring Balakhâd to a safe location beneath the city, in Umbar-môkh. Saklaphêl and the Heirs would then attack and restore our rightful ruler!'

You ask why Sadûkh left the city instead of hiding Balakhâd in Umbar-môkh, as the letters directed, and tears suddenly stream from his face.

'I thought that would be too near the Kindred, and they would find him easily! If only... if only I had done as... as the letters directed. Instead we fled from the city, surviving in the wild lands... for weeks... but always we felt the jaws of discovery pressing on our heels. In this land we were discovered by an Ordâkhai patrol outside... outside their hilltop fortress! My lord Balakhâd fought bravely... but... to no avail!''If only... if only I had done as... as the letters directed. Instead we fled from the city, surviving in the wild lands... for weeks... but always we felt the jaws of discovery pressing on our heels. In this land we were discovered by an Ordâkhai patrol outside... outside their hilltop fortress! My lord Balakhâd fought bravely... but... to no avail!''If Balakhâd had fallen in battle with the Ordâkhai, his body would have been found when the highwaymen of Ghalbûru encountered Sadûkh. I think it is more likely the soldiers brought him back to Jirush.

'The Ambarûli warrior Rahâzi will be interested to learn that one of the men we seek may now be held by her hated foe. See what she has to say about this, ${PLAYER}. I will remain here and keep an eye upon Sadûkh.''The Ambarûli warrior Rahâzi will be interested to learn that one of the men we seek may now be held by her hated foe. See what she has to say about this, ${PLAYER}. I will remain here and keep an eye upon Sadûkh.''Did the injured man give you the information you sought?'

You tell Rahâzi that one of the men sought by your travelling companions may have been brought to the town of Jirush in Khûd Zagin by a patrol of Ordâkhai soldiers. She nods thoughtfully and grips her weapon more tightly.

'Then let us learn the truth of it, ${PLAYER}! First we will ask questions... and then we will draw blood, if such becomes necessary. Jirush lies to the west.''Let us learn the truth of it! First we will ask questions... and then we will draw blood, if such becomes necessary. Jirush lies to the west.''I am sorry. I know nothing.

'Please leave me be. I do not wish to speak with you.''I have nothing to say to you. Please move along, and do not disturb me! We have troubles enough without such as you drawing the attention of soldiers from the fortress!''No one wishes to speak with you about the man the soldiers apprehended outside the city several days ago? I am not surprised. My neighbours do not wish to draw the eyes of their occupiers upon them again, for to do so might brand them rebels against the empire. They have not my courage.

'No, I am not afraid of the soldiers who live in the fortress, but if I am to answer your questions I need some assurance it will be worth my while.

'I am a craftsman of fine things, but with fewer caravans passing along the Road of Riches, I need to price my individual wares more highly than before. If you bring me the sharp jackal teeth I need for a necklace I am making, I will tell you what I know about this man you seek. Jackals can be found outside the city and throughout the sands of Khûd Zagin. Now remember: only the sharpest of their teeth will suffice for my purpose, and earn you the answers you desire.''If you bring me the sharp jackal teeth I need for a necklace I am making, I will tell you what I know about this man you seek. Jackals can be found outside the city and throughout the sands of Khûd Zagin. Now remember: only the sharpest of their teeth will suffice for my purpose, and earn you the answers you desire.''You have brought to me the jackal teeth I requested? They will become the centrepiece of my next work of craft, the sale of which will put food upon my table when next a caravan passes through Jirush. You have earned the information I have to tell you, my new friend.'

Erinesh looks over his shoulder to ensure the guards are out of earshot.

'The greatest part of the garrison of Ordâkh marched north beneath the banner of the Black Serpent to fight in Barkhûsh's war against Gondor, and most of them died there. Defeated stragglers returned from that campaign over the months that followed, and most of them came to gather in the only place they believed to be safe: that is why the black serpent flies still over Jirush. The garrison has been made strong again, and the Ordâkhai rule over the Khûd, if nowhere else. They waylay travellers along the Road of Riches and exact harsh tolls from them, competing with brigands and highwaymen for the same coin.

'The soldiers demanded a toll from the man you sought, but he resisted. They slew his companion and dragged him into the fortress above Jirush. I do not know why they kept him alive. I have not seen him emerge from the fortress, so the number of his days may be few, or already spent.''The man you seek was brought alive into the fortress above Jirush? We cannot know if he lives still... but there is one way to learn of it for certain.'

Rahâzi's eyes sparkle as she continues.

'It is time for me to call upon the promise you made to me, ${PLAYER}. I ask that you help me infiltrate the fortress. I will slay the Ordâkhai within, and you can search for the man they hold captive. Before we do this, you should inform the injured man back in the caves of Ghalbûru that we may be able to rescue his companion.''It is time for me to call upon the promise you made to me, ${PLAYER}. I ask that you help me infiltrate the fortress. I will slay the Ordâkhai within, and you can search for the man they hold captive. Before we do this, you should inform the injured man back in Ghalbûru that we may be able to rescue his companion.''What? Sadûkh is missing?

'I thought his injuries were such that he could not move well or quickly. How could he go anywhere beneath anyone's notice?

'Go to the north-eastern and south-western entrances to Ghalbûru and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for any sign of him. He surely cannot have gone far.''How could Sadûkh go anywhere beneath anyone's notice with such injuries as he possessed? Go to the north-eastern and south-western entrances to Ghalbûru and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for any sign of him. He surely cannot have gone far.''How could Sadûkh go anywhere beneath anyone's notice with such injuries as he possessed? Go to the north-eastern and south-western entrances to Ghalbûru and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for any sign of him. He surely cannot have gone far.''What are you seeking, stranger?'

You ask Urugal if he saw the injured Umbari man pass this way out of Ghalbûru, and he shakes his head.

'No, I have not, and I am the most vigilant of my brothers. If your injured man came this way I would have seen him. Not only this, but if he left Ghalbûru he would not last very long among the rocks and stones of the Khûd. Animals hardy enough to survive there would make a quick meal of a man as injured as he, and that is to speak not of otherworldly evils even more frightening than the creatures of the land.

'Yes! If the beasts do not feast upon him, he may become the prey of Ishími, the Anguished Wind that haunts the deserts of Shagâna! Ever since the fall of Barkhûsh, the whispering and wailing of Ishími has driven to madness strong men caught in the barren wastes. I do not mean this in an insulting fashion, but the man you have lost did not appear very strong to me. If he left Ghalbûru that Umbari man is surely dead.''If your injured man left Ghalbûru he would not last very long among the rocks and stones of the Khûd.''It is a mystery, but the solving of it must wait. If we are to locate Balakhâd and fulfil our promise to Rahâzi, we will need to tread with care. We have not the time to search for Sadûkh. I do not understand where he could have gone. Perhaps he will turn up when we return.'Rahâzi is prepared for the two of you to assault the fortress above Jirush, but Corudan and Sigileth want to accompany you, hoping to find Balakhad before the Dumûri warrior alerts the Ordâkhai.Sigileth is on the western side of Ghalbûru.Talk to Sigileth on the western side of GhalbûruRahâzi is by the water in Ghalbûru.Talk to Rahâzi in GhalbûruRahâzi is by the water in Ghalbûru, waiting to go with you to the fortress above Jirush.Talk to Rahâzi in Ghalbûru and infiltrate the fortress above JirushSigileth is in the centre of Jirush.Talk to Sigileth in the centre of JirushSigileth suggested you return to Ghalbûru, but that you keep the information you learned about Balakhad to yourself.Return to the north-west entrance of Ghalbûru, east of JirushKhôltekh is at the north-west entrance to Ghalbûru, east of Jirush.Talk to Khôltekh at the north-west entrance to Ghalbûru'I foresee one potential problem with this adventure of Rahâzi's: what will we do if we are unable to locate Balakhâd before she attracts too much notice with her attack upon the Ordâkhai?

'Will you ask my sister to come with us as we infiltrate the fortress of Jirush? I would feel better if she accompanied us!''Will you ask my sister to come with us as we infiltrate the fortress of Jirush? I would feel better if she accompanied us!''I will come with you. You will need someone who can search the fortress quickly and quietly, for we may not have long before Rahâzi causes the Ordâkhai to sound the alarm.'

Sigileth cocks an eyebrow at you, and you tell her that Corudan said much the same thing.

'We are siblings, after all, ${PLAYER}. It is nice to hear that some of my way of thinking has become his own. It was not always so!

'Now tell the Dumûri warrior we are ready to satisfy the obligation we promised. I saw she waits just to the north.''Now tell the Dumûri warrior we are ready to satisfy the obligation we promised. I saw she waits just to the north.''Are you ready to go? The fortress awaits us. It will not prevent us from slaying many Ordâkhai, or from freeing the captive you seek!''Let us go, then, and without delay!''I overheard something within the fortress that may help us locate Balakhâd, but...'

Sigileth frowns and studies your face closely.

'He is alive, ${PLAYER}, but I think you should return to Ghalbûru and tell the others that he was slain by his Ordâkhai captors. When you deliver these tidings, keep a close eye upon Khôltekh and Rothog in particular. My brother told me about Sadûkh's disappearance, and something in the manner of it fills me with suspicion. I distrust those two; was not Khôltekh among the last to see Sadûkh alive?

'Perhaps the two of them will betray their thoughts if they believe Balakhâd is dead.''Balakhâd is alive, but I think you should return to Ghalbûru and tell the others that he was slain by his Ordâkhai captors. When you deliver these tidings, keep a close eye upon Khôltekh and Rothog in particular. My brother told me about Sadûkh's disappearance, and something in the manner of it fills me with suspicion. I distrust those two; was not Khôltekh among the last to see Sadûkh alive?

'Perhaps the two of them will betray their thoughts if they believe Balakhâd is dead.''What is this I hear? The son of Balakhôr the Scourge was slain by his Ordâkhai captors? Pity. It seems the Heirs of Castamir have at last succumbed to the fate that pursued them, and none will mourn their passing. I find it fitting that Balakhâd was ended at last by the folk with whom he thought to ally. So ends one foe of the Kindred of the Coins, if not the worst of them!

'I would raise a mug in toast if this place had anything worth drinking. But even that cannot dampen my spirits.'Having received tidings of Balakhad's death, Khôltekh is ready to leave Khûd Zagin behind, taking most of the travelling company with him.Sigileth is by the north-western entrance of Ghalbûru.Talk to Sigileth in GhalbûruKhôltekh is by the north-western entrance of Ghalbûru.Talk to Khôltekh in GhalbûruYou should <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb> to your departing companions, and say your farewells to them. Corudan is by the water in Ghalbûru.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Corudan in GhalbûruCorudan is by the water in Ghalbûru.Talk to Corudan in GhalbûruYou should <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb> to your departing companions, and say your farewells to them. Sirgon is by the north-western entrance to Ghalbûru.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Sirgon in GhalbûruSirgon and Belondor are by the north-western entrance to Ghalbûru.Talk to Sirgon in GhalbûruTalk to Belondor in GhalbûruYou should <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb> to your departing companions, and say your farewells to them. Rahâzi is by the water in Ghalbûru.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Rahâzi in GhalbûruRahâzi is by the water in Ghalbûru.Talk to Rahâzi in GhalbûruSigileth is by the north-western entrance to Ghalbûru.Talk to Sigileth in GhalbûruSigileth planned to meet with you along the road south-east of Jirush.Meet with Sigileth along the road south-east of Jirush, in Khûd ZaginSigileth stands by the road south-east of Jirush in Khûd Zagin.Talk to Sigileth by the road south-east of Jirush'If Balakhâd is dead, we have no reason to delay further in Khûd Zagin. Most of the map Azagath saw fit to take from the Mâkhda Khorbo consisted of lands further to the east. Whatever Azagath and Nakási seek to find must lie somewhere east of here, deeper into Shagâna.

'Hmm? What is it?''The Elf woman has something to say?''I agree that most of the company should continue onward in pursuit of Azagath and Nakási, but I would ask a boon of you, Khôltekh.'

The Temámir shrugs in noncommittal fashion, and Sigileth continues.

'Ever since  returned from the grove of Arwáth with word of the Kinta people, I have desired to learn more about the Elves that remain apart from my own people. I understand there is an oasis here in Khûd Zagin where some of that folk remain, and I would like to meet with them.

'When the company departs, I ask that  stay behind with me, and together she/he and I will go to find the Kintai of the oasis.''When the company departs, I ask that  stay behind with me, and together she/he and I will go to find the Kintai of the oasis.''That sounds like a waste of time to me... but your business is your own. If an Elf wishes to go in search of other Elves, who is Khôltekh to quash that fanciful errand? The company will travel more swiftly without the two of you.

'You should bid farewell to the rest of the company, ${PLAYER}. When you have satisfied the Elf woman's curiosity about the Elves dwelling in Khûd Zagin, you can look for the rest of us at the city of Kûr Anzar, in Imhûlar to the east. Perhaps we will have captured Azagath and Nakási by the time you rejoin us. On this day all things seem possible!''You should bid farewell to the rest of the company, ${PLAYER}. When you have satisfied the Elf woman's curiosity about the Elves dwelling in Khûd Zagin, you can look for the rest of us at the city of Kûr Anzar, in Imhûlar to the east. Perhaps we will have captured Azagath and Nakási by the time you rejoin us. On this day all things seem possible!''Yes?''Sigileth told me you are staying behind when the rest of us continue eastward, ${PLAYER}. I hope the two of you find what my sister hopes to find.'

Corudan holds your gaze for a moment, and then smiles.

'To our next meeting, my friend! Each of us must remain on guard, of course.''To our next meeting, my friend! Each of us must remain on guard, of course.''Is everything all right?''Good-bye, ${PLAYER}. Be safe in Khûd Zagin, and avoid trouble with the Ordâkhai if you can! What am I saying? I have known both you and Sigileth the Elf for long enough now to know that the two of you are deadly enough to resolve any trouble which finds you!

'Rejoin us as soon as you are able, my friend.''Farewell to you, ${PLAYER}. I hope you will not remain with the Elves of the Oasis for too long, for we may have need of you and Sigileth the Elf when we catch up to Azagath and Nakási.

'But Shagâna is a vast land, and the two of them might hide within any number of secret hiding-places. It may take us much searching to find them.'Rahâzi watches you silently.'I want to thank you for honouring your promise to me, ${PLAYER}. I will not say my mother wished to test your loyalty and that of your companions, but neither will I deny it! But you have proven a friend to me and an enemy of Ordâkh, and that means you are a friend to the Ambarûli.

'Take care in the Khûd, friend ${PLAYER}. The Kintai are rarely seen, and it is said they abhor visitors. You should be safe with Sigileth, but keep my words in your mind. I will see you when you rejoin the company!''Take care in the Khûd, my friend.''Your farewells are made? Then let us make for the road south-east of Jirush. I understand that will bring us near to the Oasis of Shonith, where dwell a band of the Kintai.''Let us make for the road south-east of Jirush. I understand that will bring us near to the Oasis of Shonith, where dwell a band of the Kintai.''This is as far as we go.''Stop, ${PLAYER}. This is as far as we need go.

'The Oasis of Shonith is not our destination.'Sigileth learned more from the guards at the fortress of Jirush than she told the rest of your travelling company, and now the two of you should act upon that information.The ruins of Nirgambâr may hide the one you seek. Sigileth thinks you should search for a less-grand entrance to the tombs in which Balakhâd might hide. She also wants you to defeat some of the wights that make exploring the ruins dangerous.Find a side door to one of the tomb annexes in which Balakhâd might hideDefeat wights at Nirgambâr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigileth is west of Nirgambâr, in Khûd Zagin.Talk to Sigileth west of NirgambârSigileth is ready to go with you into the crypts of south-eastern Nirgambâr.Use the entrance to the crypts below south-eastern NirgambârSigileth believes Balakhad may be hiding somewhere within one of the less-grand tombs of south-eastern Nirgambâr.Search the south-eastern tomb in Nirgambâr for Balakhad's hiding-placeA satchel lies discarded in the corner deep below Nirgambâr.Search the satchel in the chamber below NirgambârSigileth is in the chamber below Nirgambâr.'When we infiltrated the fortress above Jirush, I overheard some of the guards discussing the fate of their former captive, Balakhâd. This is the information we withheld from the rest of the company, ${PLAYER}: Balakhâd still lives. He was freed from the prison of the Ordâkhai by a mysterious stranger wielding dark sorcery. This stranger ripped the very life from the guards, leaving clear a path for Balakhâd to flee.

'You know this power, for it is the same sorcery Azagath used upon the members of Belondor's crew beneath the Mâkhda Khorbo. He must have learned that Balakhâd was taken by the Ordâkhai and came to rescue him for some purpose of his own.

'The guards I overheard had no desire to pursue their fleeing captive; they feared for their lives. They said he escaped to the tombs of Nirgambâr, north-west of Jirush, and believed he would die there; the tombs have an evil reputation. We did not remain behind to go to Shonith, ${PLAYER}. We go to Nirgambâr to search for the last remaining Heir of Castamir. He would not have chosen the most grand of the tombs, but may have hidden himself in one of the less important burial chambers. Search for such an entrance there and defeat the wights that stalk in Nirgambâr, and we may locate this missing Heir of Castamir.''The guards I overheard believed Balakhâd would die there, for the tombs have an evil reputation. We did not remain behind to go to Shonith. We go instead to Nirgambâr to search for the last remaining Heir of Castamir. He would not have chosen the most grand of the tombs, but may have hidden himself in one of the less important burial chambers. Search for such an entrance there and defeat the wights that stalk in Nirgambâr, and we may locate this missing Heir of Castamir.''We must search for the last remaining Heir of Castamir. He would not have chosen the most grand of the tombs, but may have hidden himself in one of the less important burial chambers. Search for such an entrance and defeat the wights that stalk in Nirgambâr, and we may locate this missing Heir of Castamir.''We have searched for him outside, but if he thought to hide from the Ordâkhai, he may have gone inside one of the tombs. We should use the entrance you located to the south-east and search within that less-grand tomb of Nirgambâr for Balakhâd.

'We must be prepared to face Azagath or Nakási when we venture within. They may think to defend him, if they need him for their purpose.''We should use the entrance you located to the south-east and search within that less-grand tomb of Nirgambâr for Balakhâd. We must be prepared to face Azagath or Nakási when we venture within. They may think to defend him, if they need him for their purpose.''We should use the entrance you located to the south-east and search within that less-grand tomb of Nirgambâr for Balakhâd. We must be prepared to face Azagath or Nakási when we venture within. They may think to defend him, if they need him for their purpose.''Hmm. He was here, ${PLAYER}.''This satchel may have belonged to Balakhâd or to the water-bearer Sadûkh. Few of the Shagâni would carry Umbari coins so far from the city, but either of them might have. This satchel is from Umbar Baharbêl.'

Sigileth shakes her head.

'He remained hidden in this chamber for some time, but now he is gone, and if Azagath or Nakási stayed with him they left when he did. I can read no sign of them here, or where they may have gone when they departed. Each of them remains a step ahead of us, but I think we have narrowed the distance. We are closing in upon them, ${PLAYER}, and they may not be aware we secret hunters draw near.'Although your investigation at Nirgambâr did not find what Sigileth expected, she believes you should continue on to Shonith as you told your travelling companions you intended.Shonith is an oasis far away in south-eastern Khûd Zagin.Meet up with Sigileth far to the south-east at the Oasis of ShonithSigileth is on the leader's rise in the south-western reaches of Shonith.Talk to Sigileth in south-western ShonithTharashil is on the leader's rise in south-western Shonith.Talk to Tharashil at ShonithTharashil asked you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> out over Shonith and consider the work of the Kintai.Tharashil is on the leader's rise in south-western Shonith.Talk to Tharashil in south-western ShonithSand-sifters and birds can be found throughout the desert regions of Khûd Zagin.Collect sand-sifter meat in Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bird feathers in Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tharashil asked you to leave the meat and feathers you collected in the coffer north of the water in Shonith.Tharashil is on the leader's rise in south-western Shonith.Talk to Tharashil in south-western ShonithSigileth is on the leader's rise in south-western Shonith.Talk to Sigileth at Shonith'The trail has gone cold, for now. If our quarry sought to avoid further encounter with the Ordâkhai, they may have ventured far to the south of Jirush, which might bring them within view of the Kintai. We should seek out the Elves said to live beside the desert sands.

'No, my desire to meet with the Elves of the Oasis was not just a pretense, and it was not only in service of our deception. I do wish to speak with the Kintai, and even if they have not seen our prey, should Khôltekh ask questions of our stay in Khûd Zagin it will be good to have answers.

'Let us continue on to Shonith before we rejoin the company at Kûr Anzar.''Let us continue on to Shonith before we rejoin the company at Kûr Anzar.''This is a place of beauty.''This is Tharashil, ${PLAYER}, and she is the leader of the Kintai who dwell here at the Oasis of Shonith and tend its grove. She has welcomed the two of us here, so long as we disturb not her people.

'I told her you met with Shanthar, warden of a grove in northern Ambarûl, and she is interested to hear how he fares.''Tharashil is the leader of the Kintai who dwell here at the Oasis of Shonith and tend its grove. She has welcomed the two of us here, so long as we disturb not her people.''I am called Tharashil, and it is true that I lead the Adúrhid clan of the Kintai. There were many more of us in years past, but now we move from grove to grove, seeking to forestall the encroaching decay and death that threatens them all. With our ministrations here I hope to maintain Shonith against the spread of the Dust Sea that we of the Kintai name Lithashár.

'It is good to hear that Shanthar remains at the grove he tends, for he was a dear friend to me in years now gone, before the Ordâkhai dried the river and spoiled the lands hereabout.

'To understand our people, as your Eldar friend claims to desire, you must understand our work. I ask that you walk north, closer to the edge of this rise, and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> out over Shonith. Perhaps then you will know why we of the Kintai feel so strongly about this work to which we are called.''I ask that you walk north, closer to the edge of this rise, and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> out over Shonith. Perhaps then you will know why we of the Kintai feel so strongly about this work to which we are called.''Now you see the difficulty of our charge, friends. The Kintai fight to preserve what we can among the wastes, even as the circle of our influence grows ever smaller; even the greenery that used to thrive now withers and dies, and the desert creeps ever closer. Much of this land was covered with a great forest in ages past, the Emerald Wood of Nisigím. The very oldest of my people cursed the name of the Great Enemy, Morgoth, whose servants hewed the trees and devastated the land, turning large tracts of the Emerald Wood into desert.

'But that was long ago, and those elders of my people faded even before my birth. The work of the Kintai helped to restore some measure of the Emerald Wood, coaxing life from the barren stones and ensuring it could live before they moved on. The Kintai became a wandering people, moving from place to place, tending to the groves throughout Shagâna, but still they named the Emerald Wood of Nisigím their home.

'Stories of Nisigím's beauty seem now an impossible dream. When the Empire of Ordâkh dried the rivers and came north, their invasion parched the land and caused a great drought that withered cropland and forest alike. A terrible blight befell the trees of Nisigím, turning them as hard as stone. The forest has now become Adagím, the Corpseholt, and we of the Kintai now name ourselves Avabár-kintai, the Elves Without A Home.

'That has not changed our mission, friends, but like others who dwell in these lands we accept whatever help may be offered. If you will bring us sand-sifter meat and bird feathers from the creatures of Khûd Zagin, you will help the Kintai with their efforts. There is a coffer among the pavilions north of the water in which you may leave the items you gather for us.''Like others who dwell in these lands we accept whatever help may be offered. If you will bring us sand-sifter meat and bird feathers from the creatures of Khûd Zagin, you will help the Kintai with their efforts. There is a coffer among the pavilions north of the water in which you may leave the items you gather for us.''Like others who dwell in these lands we accept whatever help may be offered. If you will bring us sand-sifter meat and bird feathers from the creatures of Khûd Zagin, you will help the Kintai with their efforts. There is a coffer among the pavilions north of the water in which you may leave the items you gather for us.''Thank you for helping provide some meat and feathers for us, friends. Each of them will sustain the Kintai who tend the grove here in their own way: the meat for food, and the feathers as fletching for our arrows.

'Sometimes we lose sight of the need for such tasks as these, with our eyes always upon the importance of our overall charge.''Sometimes we lose sight of the need for such tasks as these, with our eyes always upon the importance of our overall charge.''I have something to ask of Tharashil, but I fear it may be considered impolite.'Tharashil wants you to learn about her people, the Kintai of Ikorbân.Tharashil has much to tell you about her people, the Kintai.Talk to Tharashil at ShonithTharashil has much to tell you about her people, the Kintai.Talk to Tharashil at ShonithAitárshun is on the easternmost island in the water in Shonith, reachable by the bridges.Meet with Aitárshun on the easternmost island in the water in ShonithAitárshun is on the easternmost island in the water in Shonith.Talk to Aitárshun in ShonithAitárshun is on the easternmost island in the water in Shonith.Wait for Aitárshun to turn his attention to youAitárshun is on the easternmost island in the water in Shonith.Talk to Aitárshun in ShonithTharashil is on the leader's rise in south-western Shonith.Talk to Tharashil in south-western ShonithEr-atûr is to the north-west of Shonith.Find Silirtas at Er-atûr, north-west of ShonithSigileth is at Er-atûr, north-west of Shonith.Talk to Sigileth at Er-atûrSilirtas is at Er-atûr, north-west of Shonith.Talk to Silirtas at Er-atûr'I have seen a number of Elves sitting or standing at various locations around the grove, their eyes open but unaware of their surroundings. Is this a manner of restful sleep among the Kintai?''I have seen a number of Elves sitting or standing at various locations around the grove, their eyes open but unaware of their surroundings. Is this a manner of restful sleep among the Kintai?''Your friend's question is not impolite. The ways of the Kintai may seem unusual to others, as the ways of the Eldar seem to us.'

Tharashil rests a finger upon her cheek, considering how best to explain this practice of her people.

'My people awoke as did other Elves, but we did not follow Taknûn the Hunter when he led the others on the long road west. The Kintai chose to stay in these lands, for they were filled with natural beauty and Taknûn's destination seemed unknown and frightening to us; we were unwilling to follow him there, and for that the Eldar gave us an insulting name. I will not speak it here. Instead, the Kintai name ourselves Tárthai: Those Who Remained.

'We dwelt in the Emerald Wood of Nisigím, as I have told you, and despite the harm caused there by Morgoth's servants and finished more recently by the Empire of Ordâkh, we never felt the Eldar's desire to travel the westward road. That place your friend names Valinor is not open to us, and we want it not.''Because my people are not drawn to Valinor, the eldest among them were very old during the later years of the Second Age. Their weariness of the world was so great that some of these Kintai began to "fade," falling into trances while their bodies passed away to dust. This was not the end, though their material bodies were gone: their spirits were free to wander as wraiths, which we of the Kintai name Väiri.

'Some of the Väiri wander invisibly throughout the lands in which they once walked, whispering on the wind but unable to make clear their wishes. Others who feel a strong bond with a particular location have been known to become guardians of the oases and groves throughout Shagâna. If a Kinta feels such a strong connexion during their lifetime, he or she may prepare to undergo the fading process at one of these beloved locations. The trance-like state of Tróthul that precedes fading may take only a short time, or it may take much longer; some of the Kintai you see here at Shonith have been preparing themselves to fade for years.

'When one of the Kintai finally enters Tróthul at one of our groves, it is our custom to fashon a Veróth around them. The Veróthi may be difficult for you to understand. Perhaps you would like to speak to Aitárshun about this aspect of the fading process? He will appreciate the company. Look for him on the easternmost island in the water. You may reach it by use of the bridges.''When one of the Kintai finally enters Tróthul at one of our groves, it is our custom to fashon a Veróth around them. The Veróthi may be difficult for you to understand. Perhaps you would like to speak to Aitárshun about this aspect of the fading process? He will appreciate the company. Look for him on the easternmost island in the water. You may reach it by use of the bridges.'You introduce yourself to Aitárshun and await his response.'Be not afraid, friends. The process is a natural one.'

Aitárshun breathes deeply, seemingly unconcerned with the tree-limbs that surround him. You tell him that Tharashil wanted him to tell you about something called a Veróth.

'This is mine, my friend. You might call a Veróth a tree-tomb. My people weave the red-leaved Sarlásh trees around the body of a Kinta who has begun to fade, and from then on the two grow together. When the Kinta has at last become dust, the Veróth remembers the shape of the body and serves as a memorial to the faded one. The ritual preparation of the Veróth binds the Kinta to the grove in which it grows, and his spirit can then watch over the place he so loved.'

Concerned, you ask Aitárshun if you have hindered his process of fading with your questions. He smiles.

'Trouble yourselves not, my friends. I am not afraid to fade, as some among my kind may be, but I find myself appreciating even short conversations as this as my time approaches. They become less frequent as every day passes.'The attention of Aitárshun has moved away from you, and it may be some time before it returns.'Did Aitárshun speak to you?'

You tell Tharashil that he did, and that he seems very content to fade in the manner of his people. Tharashil sighs.

'Aitárshun and I have given you an incomplete picture of the Kintai, friends, and for that I apologize. It is only right that I remedy that oversight. If you would learn more of my people, you should seek out Silirtas. At this time of day she is often to be found at Er-atûr, where many wrathful spirits linger. Er-atûr is to the north-west of Shonith.''If you would learn more of my people, you should seek out Silirtas. At this time of day she is often to be found at Er-atûr, where many wrathful spirits linger. Er-atûr is to the north-west of Shonith.''This must be Silirtas, the Kinta of whom Tharashil spoke.''This must be Silirtas, the Kinta of whom Tharashil spoke.''This must be Silirtas, the Kinta of whom Tharashil spoke.''Who are you? What are you doing here? This is not a place of safety.'

You introduce yourself and tell her that Tharashil asked for you to speak with Aitárshun and Silirtas to learn more about their people. Silirtas stares at you coldly.

'Tharashil is well-meaning, but she is also misguided. She thinks to hasten the process, but I know such a thing cannot be sped along without cost. Aitárshun spoke to you of the fading of the Kintai, I assume? He welcomes it. He eagerly awaits the loss of his awareness, the onset of the Tróthul trance, the cold embrace of the Sarláshi to grow around him, the prison of his Veróth. I know it is expected of me to do the same, but I cannot. I am frightened of these things.

'Know you why I come so often to Er-atûr? It is because when a Kinta resists fading, and is instead slain in battle, their spirit is loosed upon the world. They become wandering, restless spirits, increasingly wrathful and hostile. I come here to see what awaits me if I do not fade, seeking to convince myself to undergo the process. I have not yet succeeded.'Silirtas has agreed to tell you about her people and what they have learned about the lands of Khûd Zagin.Silirtas asked you to investigate the dry river-bed where once the River Usâl ran through Khûd Zagin, to the west.Examine sand in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowedSilirtas asked you to investigate the river-bed where once the River Usâl ran through Khûd Zagin. To the north-west you see some water remains in the channel.Examine sand further to the north-west, alongside the little water that remains in the channel of the River UsâlSilirtas asked you to investigate the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once ran through Khûd Zagin. To the north-west a tumble of stones fills the channel.Continue further to the north-west and examine some of the sand among the tumbled rocksSilirtas stands in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowed.Talk to Silirtas in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowedSilirtas stands in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowed, but she says nothing.Talk to Sigileth in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowedSilirtas stands in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowed, but she says nothing.Talk to Silirtas in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowedSigileth is in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowed.Talk to Sigileth in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowedSilirtas is in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowed. She again seems aware of both you and Sigileth.Talk to Silirtas in the dry river-bed where the River Usâl once flowedLizards and avanc can be found in the south-western corner of Khûd Zagin.Lizards and avanc can be found in the south-western corner of Khûd Zagin.Sigileth has found something among the southern sands of Khûd Zagin.Join Sigileth and Silirtas in the desert of southern Khûd ZaginSigileth has found something in southern Khûd Zagin.Talk to Sigileth in southern Khûd Zagin'And so I continue to fight the fading to which my people succumb. It is painful, but I have not yet given in to it. Unless my heart is greatly changed, I cannot imagine myself becoming content enough to fade in the manner of Aitárshun or others among the Kintai.

'Why do I fight it so? I will show you.

'The River Usâl once flowed through this land, but now only its dry bed remains. Walk within that empty channel west of here and examine the sand within it. You will come to better understand me.''Why do I fight the fading of my people? I will show you. The River Usâl once flowed through this land, but now only its dry bed remains. Walk within that empty channel and examine the sand within it. You will come to better understand me.''More than a hundred and twenty years ago, these lands were green and bountiful. The banks of the River Usâl were alive with a riot of beautiful vegetation. I understand the folk who dwelt beside it named it "the Abundant Kingdom" in their language, and I can think of no name more appropriate for the thriving land of my memory!

'But then Ordâkh came. With terrible magic the sorcerers of that Empire caused the drying of the River Usâl and its most important tributaries. Marshes shrank away to nothing, and the river-lands withered and burned. None of my people know for certain, but I believe the blight that wasted the Emerald Wood came north with the Ordâkhai when they caused this Great Dearth. And that is why -'Sigileth waits politely for Silirtas to resume her story, but the Kinta remains silent.

'What happened following the invasion of the Ordâkhai after the Great Dearth?' Sigileth prompts her.

'Silirtas?''Silirtas? Are you feeling well?'Silirtas stares blankly forward, seemingly unaware of your presence.'What can be done, ${PLAYER}? What should be done?

'We cannot leave her here, but neither can we carry her back to Shonith!''Silirtas, are you well?''What? I must apologize. I hope I did not worry you.'

Silirtas shakes her head and looks around at the dry river-bed in which the three of you stand.

'I told you I fight the fading experienced by my people? On occasion I become untethered from my surroundings, losing my grip upon the material world; it can take the form of a loss of awareness. Think not that I welcome such moments! I fight against them always, keeping my hands and my mind busy with whatever tasks the Kintai of Shonith need completed, but it is painful. It would be easy for any other to succumb, to sit down within one of our groves and fade as have so many of my people... but not Silirtas.

'Why did I bring you to the River Usâl?'

You remind Silirtas that she wanted to show you something of her reasons for resisting the fading of the Kintai, and that the dry river-bed was somehow a part of it. She nods with understanding.

'Yes, I remember now. When a Kinta enters the trance of Tróthul and is entombed within the Sarláshi trees that compose a Veróth, they become bonded to their grove. They remain there through the years, ever watchful but unable to affect the world outside. Can you imagine the frustration of remaining a prisoner for untold centuries while the land you love rots and dies all around you? Can you imagine the sorrow of watching as the River Usâl dries up and the Great Dearth withers the beauty you remember into a dead or dying shadow, with you unable to prevent or reverse it?

'So I choose not to fade, and if that means I must remain ever-vigilant and constantly active, then that is what I shall be. I intended to slay lizards or avanc to the south-west before I returned to Shonith. Let us together defeat them. I appreciate the company, and the two of you are fine listeners.''I intended to slay lizards to the south-west before I returned to Shonith. Let us together defeat them. I appreciate the company, and the two of you are fine listeners.''I intended to slay lizards to the south-west before I returned to Shonith. Let us together defeat them. I appreciate the company, and the two of you are fine listeners.''Look at this.''I have found the body of a dead man, ${PLAYER}.

'Think you this might be Sadûkh, who disappeared from Ghalbûru?'Silirtas believes that Balakhâd knows more about Narghâk, the Hungry Fire, than he is letting on. He may speak further on the subject if his hurts are healed.Silirtas suggested that you speak with Idhraid about what it will take to heal Balakhâd of his injuries, and also that you fill a jar with water from the deepest part of the oasis. Jars for this purpose can be found at the front of the pavilion in northern Shonith.Collect a jar front the northern pavilion to fill with water for BalakhâdTalk to Idhraid by the northern pavilionYou should dampen the cloth given to you by Idhraid and fill the jar you collected with water from the deepest reaches of the oasis in Shonith.Dampen the cloth and fill the jar with water from the deepest part of the oasisSilirtas is waiting for you to return to her with the damp cloth and water jar she requested of you.Silirtas has begun trying to ease Balakhâd's hurts with the damp cloth.Wait for Silirtas to soothe Balakhâd's burning skin with the damp clothBalakhâd is at the northern pavilion in Shonith.Sigileth is at the northern pavilion in Shonith.Talk to Sigileth at the northern pavilion in ShonithTharashil is in south-western Shonith.Talk to Tharashil in south-western Shonith'We must show this man we will harm him not, unless he inflict some injury upon the Kinta people or our grove. His hurts are severe, but if our healers are permitted to tend them, he may recover.

'Collect a jar from the front of this pavilion to fill with water from the deepest area of the pool. Speak also to Idhraid nearby, for she is lately arrived from Vartúlin and brings with her great knowledge of the healing arts. She may have some advice of use to us.''Collect a jar from the front of the pavilion to fill with water from the deepest reaches of the pool. Speak also to Idhraid, for she is lately arrived from Vartúlin and brings with her great knowledge of the healing arts. She may have some advice of use to us.''I observed the two of you as you brought that dying man to Shonith. He trusts not my people? Perhaps we share that feeling. I dwelled in the Emerald Wood of Nisigím before the blight killed the trees, and it was Men that unleashed it.

'No, it was not his doing; I know the lives of Men are short, and he could not have been born when Nisigím withered. But I know also his kind.

'I am a healer. I will give you a cloth to dampen in the pool, so his pains may be eased. Perhaps in gratitude he will leave the Kintai be, and never return? Be sure to collect water from the deepest part of the pond! Water from the shallower reaches will not be as effective.''I gave you a cloth to dampen in the pool, so the pains of the dying man may be eased. Perhaps in gratitude he will leave the Kintai be, and never return?''You bring the items we sought from Idhraid? Very good.'You help Balakhâd hold the jar you filled with clean water from the oasis, and he manages to drink. After a few moments, he looks up at you with a guarded expression.

'We are... not friends. These Elves are alien to me, and I do not trust them. As soon as I am... well enough to walk... I will leave this place, and never return.

'But you and these Elves are... you are right about something. If the Kindred finds the hidden cache of Narghâk they seek...'

Balakhâd falls silent, mopping his forehead with the damp cloth you brought from Idhraid.

'In Gondor they named it "Umbari Fire," for they knew not of its long use in these lands, and only learned of it when it was used against them. But my father knew. He dreamed of harnessing it against his enemies, but even with all the men at his command he could find no stores of it. He believed it gone from the world.

'It should be gone. Azagath and Nakási must not find... the hidden stockpile they seek. They will set the world aflame.''Azagath and Nakási must not find the stockpile they seek. They will set the world aflame.''I would not agree with the Heirs of Castamir about very many things, ${PLAYER}. We have found the single exception.

'We must catch up to our travelling companions and tell them we have learned what our quarry seeks. If Silirtas is to join us, as she has suggested to me, we will need to seek permission from Tharashil.''If Silirtas is to join us, as she has suggested to me, we will need to seek permission from Tharashil.'You inform Tharashil of Balakhâd's condition and his vow to depart from Shonith as soon as he is able to walk. You also ask for her permission to allow Silirtas to accompany you and Sigileth on your journey eastward.

'I am the warden of this grove, but I have not the power to keep Silirtas from doing as she wishes. Had I that power, she might have already entered Tróthul and begun to safely fade within a Veróth of her own! But I do not, and she insists upon fighting the process.

'If she truly wishes to go with you, I hope you will keep her safe. She fights the fading, but I understand the fight is painful, and even one so strong-willed as Silirtas cannot achieve victory in every battle. It is the lot of her people to fade, though she wishes otherwise.'Tharashil has suggested that when you depart from Shonith, you visit the grove of Vartúlin in Imhûlar before meeting up with the rest of your travelling company.The road into Imhûlar crosses the River Barûg north of Shonith.Cross the River Barûg into Imhûlar from Khûd Zagin, north of ShonithSigileth stands just within the entrance to Imhûlar.Talk to Sigileth by the road into ImhûlarSilirtas is just inside the entrance to Imhûlar.Talk to Silirtas by the road into ImhûlarDiseased boars, servals, and deer can be found throughout Imhûlar.Collect diseased boar-hooves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect diseased serval-fangs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect diseased deer-hides (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The leader of the Kintai at Vartúlin is named Thórvilon, and can be found in southern Imhûlar.Find Thórvilon, leader of the Kintai at Vartúlin, in southern ImhûlarThórvilon, leader of the Kintai at Vartúlin, is in southern Imhûlar.Talk to Thórvilon in VartúlinSilirtas stands by Thórvilon in Vartúlin.Talk to Silirtas in VartúlinThórvilon, leader of the Kintai at Vartúlin is in southern Imhûlar.'The lands to the east are named Imhûlar by the Shagâni people, and for the Kintai they are full of memories. Some of those are painful, for we remember when the northern reaches of the Emerald Wood grew there, before it withered and died as a result of the blight brought by the Ordâkhai. But others of those memories are joyful: the spirits of my own parents remain at the grove of Vartúlin in Imhûlar; they faded long ago, but whenever I walk beside their trees I feel their presence.

'The Kinta warden of Vartúlin is Thórvilon; for decades he has sought to find some means of reversing the blight. I suggest that you speak with him as you walk in Imhûlar. Perhaps speaking with him will help Silirtas accept that she must fade? I do not really believe it will, but it is in my nature to remain hopeful.''The Kinta warden of Vartúlin is Thórvilon; for decades he has sought to find some means of reversing the blight. I suggest that you speak with him as you walk in Imhûlar. Perhaps speaking with him will help Silirtas accept that she must fade? I do not really believe it will, but it is in my nature to remain hopeful.''This must be Imhûlar.''This must be Imhûlar. Our travelling companions planned to make for the city of Kûr Anzar, but I understand Tharashil wished for us to first visit the grove of Vartúlin?

'I am not opposed to this plan, but I suggest that we do not linger overlong at that grove. I am concerned for the safety of my brother in the company of the Kindred. I cannot say for certain that Khôltekh or Rothog were responsible for the disappearance of Sadûkh, but neither can I deny it completely.

'What can Silirtas tell us of Imhûlar?''What can Silirtas tell us of Imhûlar?''The Emerald Wood of Nisigím has been blighted by the Ordâkhai, and it is no longer a place of green beauty. Now it is Adagím, the Corpseholt, and Thórvilon and others of my people seek to slow the spread of the disease that claims it. When last I walked in Vartúlin, the grove remained healthy, but the circle of hale trees had already diminished from its original size.

'North-east of Vartúlin, the city of Kûr Anzar was the birthplace of the Shagâni kingdom of Hamât early in the Third Age. For nearly three thousand years, the Hamâti fought against Gondor and other rival kingdoms, until it was eventually absorbed into the Empire of Ordâkh. I do not know the state of it today.

'On our way to Vartúlin we should hunt some of the diseased animals that walk among the dead trees. Thórvilon oft desires to study the blighted creatures for some clue to curing the pestilence in Adagím.''On our way to Vartúlin we should hunt some of the diseased animals that walk among the dead trees. Thórvilon oft desires to study the blighted creatures for some clue to curing the pestilence in Adagím.''Thórvilon is the guardian of the grove of Vartúlin, in southern Imhûlar.''Yes?''You have come to aid the Kintai of Vartúlin with our work? I welcome it, though I fear the blight that has transformed these woods resists our efforts, and has so far proven impossible to reverse. The best for which we can hope is to slow its spread. You and Silirtas have my thanks.''Ah, Silirtas. Still she fights the fate of her people.'Silirtas stares blankly forward, her awareness once again untethered.'I see that Silirtas still fights our people's fate. I am saddened to see it, but not surprised; she and I have spoken about it before, and her decision then was clear. I see it has not changed.

'How can it be so easy to accept in others, and yet so difficult to accept when it affects those dear to us? Alas, I see this difficulty most clearly within my own family, and the contradiction is visible to me! But it is not my decision to make for Silirtas, and my words on this subject will sway her neither for nor against.'

Thórvilon accepts the satchel containing the diseased hooves, fangs, and hides from the pestilential creatures of the dead forest, and sadly looks them over.

'If there is aught to learn from these, we will learn it. I dare not hope the blight can be reversed, but perhaps we can fight it, as Silirtas fights the fading.'Thórvilon has enlisted your help to protect Vartúlin and the Kintai from the encroaching darkness that threatens Imhûlar.A pile of the boundary stones can be found across the bridge in Vartúlin.Pick up boundary stones from the pile across the bridge in VartúlinThórvilon has asked you to place boundary stones around the edges of Vartúlin.Place a boundary stone north of ThórvilonPlace a boundary stone at the north-eastern entrance to VartúlinPlace a boundary stone on the slope to the southern pavilion in VartúlinPlace a boundary stone at the western entrance to VartúlinThat should be enough stones to fulfil Thórvilon's request.Talk to Silirtas in the centre of VartúlinHaving satisfied Thórvilon's request, you should return to the centre of Vartúlin and check on Silirtas.Talk to Silirtas in the centre of VartúlinSigileth is in Vartúlin.Talk to Sigileth in VartúlinSigileth wants to rejoin the rest of your travelling company at Kûr Anzar, to the north of Vartúlin.Travel toward Kûr Anzar, to the north of VartúlinYou should defeat some of the Dead that lurk at Adakûr before they threaten the folk that live in Imhûlar.Defeat wights near Adakûr in Imhûlar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigileth is outside Adakûr in Imhûlar.Talk to Sigileth outside AdakûrSigileth wants you to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at Kûr Anzar from a location to the north-east and determine the situation inside the city.Sigileth is outside Adakûr in Imhûlar.Talk to Sigileth outside Adakûr'The protective arts with which we ward our groves have become less effective over time, but it is the hope of the Kintai that they will forestall the encroaching darkness long enough for us to discover a lasting solution. We have inscribed runes of guarding and renewal upon boundary stones that must be placed along the perimeter of Vartúlin.

'Will you retrieve some of the boundary stones from the pile across the bridge and set them along the borders of the grove? I will remain with Silirtas until she returns to herself.''Will you retrieve some of the boundary stones from the pile across the bridge and set them along the borders of the grove? I will remain with Silirtas until she returns to herself.''Will you retrieve some of the boundary stones from the pile across the bridge and set them along the borders of the grove? I will remain with Silirtas until she returns to herself.''I am embarrassed, ${PLAYER}. At least Thórvilon is a friend, and understands the difficulties I have faced in these past few years. They have become more frequent as Tróthul seeks to claim me, but I will not allow it.

'I am eager to meet the others of your company, for speaking with new folk and having new experiences will help me to stave off Tróthul. It has ever been so, and this adventure seems to me no different.''I am eager to meet the others of your company, for speaking with new folk and having new experiences will help me to stave off Tróthul. It has ever been so, and this adventure seems to me no different.''Yes. We have lent some aid to Thórvilon, now we should continue on to the north-east to rejoin the company. My brother Corudan will be very pleased to meet Silirtas and to learn of her people, as was I.

'I have said our farewells to Thórvilon. Now we should travel to the north, from where we can assess the situation in Kûr Anzar. Silirtas said the Kingdom of Hamât was absorbed by Ordâkh, and the city may not be friendly to us or to the representatives of Umbar we seek to rejoin.''Let us travel northward, from where we can assess the situation in Kûr Anzar without venturing within the city. Silirtas said the Kingdom of Hamât was absorbed by Ordâkh, and the city may not be friendly to us or to the representatives of Umbar we seek to rejoin.''We should defeat some of the wights near Adakûr.''These wights were disturbed by something nearby, ${PLAYER}, and I fear the hand of the Church of the All-seeing in this.

'Go to the north-east and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> toward the city of Kûr Anzar. We must know what awaits us within.''Go to the north-east and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> toward the city of Kûr Anzar. We must know what awaits us within.''Kûr Anzar is occupied by soldiers of Ordâkh and cultists of the Church of the All-seeing?

'Our companions cannot have gone there, ${PLAYER}.'With Kûr Anzar occupied by soldiers and cultists of the Church of the All-seeing, your travelling companions must have gone somewhere else.Silirtas is outside Adakûr in Imhûlar.Talk to Silirtas outside Adakûr in ImhûlarFind your travelling companions in the village of Urmâkh, north-west of Adakûr.Find your travelling companions somewhere in Imhûlar, perhaps in the city of UrmâkhYour companions within Urmâkh eagerly await the tale of your adventures.Tell your companions what befell you while you were apartCorudan, Sirgon, and Rahâzi are within the city of Urmâkh.Talk to Corudan within UrmâkhTalk to Sirgon within UrmâkhCorudan, Sirgon, and Rahâzi are within Urmâkh.Talk to Rahâzi within UrmâkhRahâzi challenged you to speak with residents of Urmâkh and learn their opinions of Gondor.Sirgon is within the city of Urmâkh.Talk to Sirgon within Urmâkh'Where might our companions have gone instead? Silirtas, is there a settlement somewhere within Imhûlar where folk of Gondor, Umbar, and Shagâna might gather together without drawing notice? The group includes a dwarf, an Elf, and a half-troll as well. Also, the half-troll's lion, should that affect your answer.'

If you did not know the Elf-warrior so well, you might think Sigileth was jesting. She catches your eye and holds it with her own while she waits for Silirtas to answer.Sigileth waits for Silirtas to answer.'Nowhere in any of these lands would hide such a gathering as that from outside attention, Sigileth!' cries Silirtas.

'But if they came to Imhûlar and planned to stop at Kûr Anzar, they might have turned from that course and stayed for a time at Urmâkh.

'If we go to the north-west and search within the city of Urmâkh, we may find your friends.''At last!''If your friends came to Imhûlar and planned to stop at Kûr Anzar, they might have turned from that course and stayed for a time at Urmâkh. Let us go to the north-west and search within Urmâkh for your companions.''When my sister told me she wanted to meet with the Elves of the Oasis, I did not think she would bring one back with her! It is a pleasure to meet you, Silirtas, and I am eager to learn more about you and your people. If there is anything you would hear from me concerning the Land of Lothlórien or the other places I have seen on my travels, you have only to ask.'

Corudan turns to you, his welcoming expression replaced with one of great concern.

'I am worried about this substance sought by Azagath and Nakási. If it is truly as destructive as it sounds, we must prevent them from finding it at any cost. The others of our group went in search of their trail while we spoke with the people here in Urmâkh. The occupation of Kûr Anzar has made locating our prey more difficult than it might have otherwise been.''I am pleased you and Sigileth have returned to us, ${PLAYER}. We have had little in the way of success since you left us.'

Sirgon's demeanour is downcast, and you ask him what troubles him. He sighs and kicks a stone in the grass.

'I have been engaged in an argument with the warrior-woman Rahâzi, and it has enshrouded me in a storm-cloud of ill feeling. She speaks of beloved Kings of Gondor as if they were tyrants, and says King Elessar will be no different than they! The title "Hyarmendacil" is a noble one in Gondor, and memory of the Kings who wear it brings to mind many triumphs throughout history! But Rahâzi treats these victors as if they were monstrous regents and bloodthirsty warmongers! How are the rulers of her land remembered in Gondor, I ask you? How might she defend such as they?

'King Elessar is a man of noble heart and bearing. How can I convince Rahâzi of what I know to be the truth?''I am worried about this substance sought by Azagath and Nakási. If this is truly as destructive as it sounds, we must prevent them from finding it at any cost. The others of our group have gone in search of their trail. The occupation of Kûr Anzar has made that more difficult than it might have been.''King Elessar is a man of noble heart and bearing. How can I convince Rahâzi of what I know to be the truth?''This man of Gondor thinks to lecture the daughter of Girhâzi about the history of her own people? Do you know the meaning of the title "Hyarmendacil" worn by his beloved kings, ${PLAYER}? He told me the meaning of it is "South-victor."

'The Shagâni have given titles of our own to one of those kings. The ruler known in Gondor as Hyarmendacil the First is known in Ambarûl as Lûgula, "the Despot of the North." The second Gondorian king to claim the title of Hyarmendacil has a different title among the people whose ancestors he slaughtered. He is called Ugdûmu: "the Butcher."

'Speak to the folk who dwell in this village and you will see that your Gondorian friend knows not of what he speaks.''Speak to the folk who dwell in this village and you will see that your Gondorian friend knows not of what he speaks.''Stories of the Gondorian kings who named themselves Hyarmendacil are well-known to my people, for they are brutal tales of conquest and despair; they are also lessons to us. The first of these, Ciryaher Hyarmendacil, he we name "Lûgula," conquered the coasts and held them with the sword, that he might affirm his kingdom's control of the seas; the lands in the interior were made border-marches of Gondor. He installed puppet-kings in these cities of Shagâna, and took their children to live in Gondor as hostages.

'Thus was their good behaviour ensured, but he returned every seven years to reaffirm the loyalties of the kings. When he finally died, the Ambarûli celebrated so enthusiastically that a unit of Gondorian soldiers was sent to restore order, and dozens perished.

'One hundred years after his death, another sought to attain glory in our lands, but through great force of will and show of arms, the Shagâni dealt him defeat after defeat. He retreated, and from the safety of his northern home he felt he could not claim the title of Hyarmendacil. That spared him not from becoming known in Shagâna as "Lûgula-min," the Second Despot.''Shagâna has always suffered at the hands of Gondor. When the Black Númenórean Castamiri departed from that kingdom and established the union of the Khôrthan League, it was to Shagâna that the Gondorian king Vinyarion pursued them, bringing war and death. He stopped not there, but continued deeper into lands that held no enemies for him. He burned the great city of Shâraban in a tremendous conflagration, and its charred bones still smolder today.

'This was the man who took the title of Hyarmendacil II. We name him Ugdûmu, the Butcher. They say he had a change of heart in his elder years, and repented of the evil he wrought. I believe it not.''You speak to my friends about Kings of Gondor? You are bold to speak so, stranger. Hyarmendacil means "South-victor," and all who claimed this title wear evil reputations in Shagâna. Others have sought to achieve that title through conquest and bloodshed, and failed.

'A king named Minardil sought to become Hyarmendacil III, but he was slain by the Heirs of Castamir before he could adopt it properly. The Heirsworn of today are no friends of mine... but to cease the glory-seeking of a third Hyarmendacil seems to me fine work indeed.

'That title remains unclaimed to this day, but who can say whether the new King of Gondor will think to increase his own glory and the size of his kingdom by adopting the title of Hyarmendacil III, though it lie long dormant? The histories of this land teach us unpleasant lessons, stranger; perhaps you should heed them.''They are wrong, ${PLAYER}! King Elessar is a good man, and he will live up to his promise, for both Gondor and for Shagâna. The kings of old that took the title of Hyarmendacil were neither as wise nor as just as King Elessar. He will not become as they were.'Sirgon wants to prove that Gondor is not a threat to the people of Shagâna.Sirgon has spoken with the people of Urmâkh and wants you to bring them, on behalf of Gondor, some of the ingredients for which they have asked.Collect toad legs in Imhûlar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect zizanu bristles in Imhûlar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect scorpion stingers in Imhûlar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sharûkh is within Urmâkh, waiting for you to bring the ingredients Sirgon promised.Rahâzi is within Urmâkh.Talk to Rahâzi within UrmâkhRahâzi wants you to defeat cultists throughout Imhûlar, especially in the occupied city of Kûr Anzar.Defeat cultists in Imhûlar and the city of Kûr Anzar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Irnamar is within Urmâkh.Talk to Irnamar within UrmâkhSirgon is within Urmâkh.Talk to Sirgon within Urmâkh'I want to prove that Gondor is not an enemy to the people here, ${PLAYER}, and that King Elessar is different than the rulers of the past whose names the Shagâni curse.

'One of the residents here, Sharûkh, spoke of a need for various animal parts that might be put to a number of different uses: legs from toads, bristles from something called a zizanu, and the stingers from deadly scorpions. No enemy of these folk would gather such items as these for them, would they?

'I would really appreciate your gathering these items for Sharûkh, ${PLAYER}, and so would the King!''One of the residents here, Sharûkh, spoke of a need for various animal parts that might be put to a number of different uses: legs from toads, bristles from something called a zizanu, and the stingers from deadly scorpions. No enemy of these folk would gather such items as these for them, would they? I would really appreciate your gathering these items for Sharûkh, ${PLAYER}, and so would the King!''One of the residents here, Sharûkh, spoke of a need for various animal parts that might be put to a number of different uses: legs from toads, bristles from something called a zizanu, and the stingers from deadly scorpions. No enemy of these folk would gather such items as these for them, would they? I would really appreciate your gathering these items for Sharûkh, ${PLAYER}, and so would the King!''This work of yours is a help to me, but if your Gondorian friend believes we will forget centuries of ill use by his kingdom and his rulers, his head is full of rocks.

'Still, I appreciate it. These items contain traces of the disease that plagues these creatures, and our land, but with careful handling they can still be of use to me.''If your Gondorian friend believes we will forget centuries of ill use by his kingdom and his rulers, his head is full of rocks. Still, I appreciate it.''Sirgon is eager to prove the worth of his king and his kingdom? Then let his friends prove his trust is not misplaced!

'There are cultists of Barkhûsh throughout Imhûlar and in the city of Kûr Anzar, ${PLAYER}. Deal them a grave injury and perhaps the folk of this village will understand Sirgon's trust in his liege lord. You should then relate the good you have done to the woman Irnamar. I spoke with her earlier and she bemoaned the evils done by the cultists in Kûr Anzar.

'You will not remove centuries of distrust from the hearts of these folk with one deed on their behalf, ${PLAYER}. At least you will make more safe the road we must take, if nothing else.''There are cultists of Barkhûsh throughout Imhûlar, ${PLAYER}. Deal them a grave injury and perhaps the folk of this village will understand Sirgon's trust in his liege lord. At least you will make more safe the road we must take, if nothing else.''You should speak to Irnamar about the work you have done combatting the Barkhûshim.''You fought cultists of Barkhûsh?

'You have done some good here today, stranger. I am not one to turn away help when it is offered; neither am I one to withhold my gratitude when a good deed is done on my behalf. So you have the thanks of Irnamar for fighting against these foes of my people.

'That is all you will have. I hope you expected nothing more.''You have the thanks of Irnamar for fighting against the cultists of Barkhûsh.''I am glad we have done what we could on behalf of King Elessar and Gondor, as little as it might have been. It is my hope that our words and deeds will be here remembered in the days ahead, and such will serve the cause of peace between these lands. We may not yet be friends, but I would have us first not be enemies.

'I am glad we did what we could for the folk of Urmâkh, but it is time for us to be on our way. Belondor has sent word to us that they have located the trail of Azagath.'The rest of your travelling company has gone ahead to scout the road into Urash Dâr, and you should help them prepare for the next leg of their pursuit.The rest of your travelling party should be in north-east Imhûlar, on the road into Urash Dâr.Meet up with the rest of your travelling party in north-east Imhûlar, on the road into Urash DârBelondor stands by the road that runs into Urash Dâr.Talk to Belondor at the road into Urash DârBelondor is at the road into Urash Dâr, waiting to hear what befell you and Sigileth after you parted in Ghalbûru.Tell Belondor a careful version of what befell you since you parted in GhalbûruKhôltekh and Rothog listened to your story, and they have some thoughts of their own.Talk to Khôltekh at the road into Urash DârTalk to Rothog at the road into Urash DârBelondor stands by the road leading into Urash Dâr.Talk to Belondor at the road into Urash DârA good location for a camp can be found to the north-west in Urash Dâr, at a town recently abandoned by its former occupants.Find the abandoned town to the north-west in Urash DârYou have been charged with setting up camp at the abandoned town to the north-west in Urash Dâr.Light the camp-fire at the abandoned town in Urash DârYou have been charged with setting up camp at the abandoned town in Urash Dâr.Wait for your companions to arriveSilirtas is at your new campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Silirtas at your new campsite in Urash Dâr'They wait for us along the road into Urash Dâr, located to the north-east of Imhûlar. We should rejoin them before they capture Azagath and Nakási without us!''They wait for us along the road into Urash Dâr, located in north-east Imhûlar. We should rejoin them before they capture Azagath and Nakási without us!''Over here!''It is good to see you again. I trust your journey with Sigileth the Elf went well? Our own search has proved fruitful, for we encountered a Shagâni scout who reported seeing Azagath travel alone into Urash Dâr, the furrow-lands to the north. We questioned the scout at some length, and he was quite certain that Azagath travelled alone. Where Nakási has gone no one seems to know.

'What did you discover on your own journey after you parted with us at Ghalbûru, ${PLAYER}?''Did you find your visit with the Elves of the Oasis a waste of time, ${PLAYER}, as I said it would be?'

Khôltekh laughs suddenly, interrupting your response.

'I am only jesting. I am certain it was of great value. I understand you have brought one of them with you? How strange it must be for her to leave her grove. The world outside must be more dangerous than that to which she is accustomed.''Some of the people we spoke to described a dreadful presence lurking in the canyons ahead. They say whatever this evil might be, it has only come to haunt the furrow-lands in the past few months.

'I do not want to raise my hopes or those of Húrshauk too high, ${PLAYER}, but I hope this evil presence is of the sort that wields weapons. There has been too much walking and not enough fighting on this journey for my taste, and that is true for Húrshauk as well, who feels as I do!''No one seems to know where Nakási has gone. She may have quarrelled with Azagath and taken her leave of him, or he may have sent her somewhere with orders to carry out. All we know for certain is that when Azagath passed into Urash Dâr, he did so alone.

'The scout with whom we spoke told us friendly folk once lived at a small village to the north-west, a short distance from here, but when last he passed that way the town was abandoned. Enough may remain of the town that it would serve us well as a foothold from which we might explore within Urash Dâr.

'Will you go ahead and light the fire at this abandoned campsite? When the entirety of our group has there assembled it will become the foundation of our activities within Urash Dâr, and from there we can more effectively search for Azagath.''Will you go ahead and light the fire at this abandoned campsite? When the entirety of our group has there assembled it will become the foundation of our activities within Urash Dâr, and from there we can more effectively search for Azagath.''Will you go ahead and light the fire at this abandoned campsite? When the entirety of our group has there assembled it will become the foundation of our activities within Urash Dâr, and from there we can more effectively search for Azagath.''I have an uneasy feeling about this place, ${PLAYER}.

'No, the campsite is fine. There is something wrong about the air of this place. Something is not right. I feel as if some evil slumbered within the canyons of Urash Dâr, but it has awoken... and there is something else.

'It is hungry.'Silirtas senses that something evil dwells within Urash Dâr, and she is troubled by its presence.Silirtas wants you to cross the river to the west and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the stone keep of Maurûsh.Silirtas wants you to creep closer and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> for what might be happening within Maurûsh.Silirtas is at your new campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Silirtas at your new campsite in Urash DârSigileth is at your campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Sigileth at your campsite in Urash DârSilirtas is at your campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Silirtas at your campsite in Urash DârSilirtas is at your campsite in Urash Dâr, preparing to go with you to Maurûsh.Talk to Silirtas and go with her into MaurûshMithrandir has come to your campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at your campsite in Urash Dâr'I am sorry, ${PLAYER}. The sensation is unpleasant, but there is something more: it is familiar, somehow. I cannot understand it. The Kintai never dwelled among these canyons, and to my knowledge neither did the Temámir. Men and hill-folk may have fled here from other kingdoms elsewhere in Shagâna, but this has always been an overlooked place. If it was ever otherwise it is beyond my memory, and beyond that of my people.

'But something evil dwells within Urash Dâr now, that much is certain. I cannot determine where exactly it dwells unless we search among the canyons.

'The Ordâkhai built a stone keep, Maurûsh, from which they might oversee the approach from Imhûlar. It should be located across the river to the west. If you scout the approach to Maurûsh you may learn the identity of the sleeping evil within Urash Dâr.''The Ordâkhai built a stone keep, Maurûsh, from which they might oversee the approach from Imhûlar. It should be located across the river to the west. If you scout the approach to Maurûsh you may learn the identity of the sleeping evil within Urash Dâr.''The Ordâkhai built a stone keep, Maurûsh, from which they might oversee the approach from Imhûlar. It should be located across the river to the west. If you scout the approach to Maurûsh you may learn the identity of the sleeping evil within Urash Dâr.''You say there are Orcs within Maurûsh? That is very unusual, for that keep was built by the Ordâkhai. Without the ruling grip of the Dark Lord I would not expect those two groups to work together, however unhappily.

'I want to go with you to investigate Maurûsh. I must see the situation within those walls with my own eyes.''I want to go with you to investigate Maurûsh. I must see the situation within those walls with my own eyes.''I do not wish to shame Silirtas, ${PLAYER}, but do you think it is wise for her to accompany you beyond the walls of Maurûsh? Should she grow distant at an inopportune moment, she would endanger both your life and her own.

'Perhaps I should go in her stead.''Perhaps I should go in her stead.'Silirtas wears a hurt expression. When she speaks again, her voice is barely above a whisper.

'I believed we had an understanding, ${PLAYER}. I see now that I was deceived! Sigileth is welcome to accompany us, but I will not be left behind. I want to help you investigate Maurûsh, and I am insulted that you think to cast me aside so readily!

'For what purpose do I fight against Tróthul if my allies will not permit me to aid them? Why do I resist the... ailment... inflicting my people if my friends abandon me at the first sign of danger? You weave a Veróth around me even without Sarláshi trees; the prison is made of your own words!

'All of my senses scream that there is evil in Urash Dâr. I want to face it. Speak to me when you are ready to search for it, or I will go by myself to Maurûsh, and you will be the one abandoned!'Silirtas does not speak again, but it is clear she has been deeply hurt. Sigileth begins to say something, but then decides against it.

The three of you prepare to depart for Maurûsh.'You are fortunate I arrived at this moment, ${PLAYER}, for you could not stand against these foes without my help!'Whether by chance or plan, friends have come from afar to lend you their aid. Now they must be brought together to face this new danger.Mithrandir has sent you to recall the scouts he sent to the north-west and north-east of Urash Dâr.Find Legolas and Gimli to the north-west of your campsiteFind Glorfindel to the east of your campsiteFind Elladan and Elrohir to the north-east of your campsiteTalk to Legolas in Urash DârTalk to Gimli in Urash DârTalk to Glorfindel in Urash DârTalk to Elladan in Urash DârTalk to Elrohir in Urash DârMithrandir is at your campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at your campsite in Urash Dâr'Yes, yes, it is good to see you again. But there is no time to waste now, ${PLAYER}!

'When I at last understood the danger that stirred in these lands, I came from the North in great haste with a number of companions. Not knowing how best to find you, I sent them to search in several different directions. You are fortunate I chose the same path on which you and the Elves were bound, for if I had not found you at that moment...'

Mithrandir falls silent, and taps his staff on the rocky ground.

'Enough of that. But we must find and return the folk I sent in search of you, for we will need them all if we are to face this threat. Seek for Legolas, Gimli, the sons of Elrond, and Glorfindel among the canyons of Urash Dâr. When you have returned with them I will tell you what I know of the great evil that stirs in this land, and how we must stand against it.''We must find and return the folk I sent in search of you, for we will need them all if we are to face this threat. Seek for Legolas, Gimli, the sons of Elrond, and Glorfindel among the canyons of Urash Dâr. When you have returned with them I will tell you what I know of the great evil that stirs in this land, and how we must stand against it.''When we left you in Umbar Baharbêl, I thought it would be much longer before we saw you again, ${PLAYER}! But no sooner had we delivered Nimrodel to the shores of Gondor than Mithrandir sent urgent word that he had need of us.

'The journeys Gimli and I planned to take must wait. After all, it is not wise to refuse a Wizard's call.''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}. The merchant vessel that delivered us and the Lady Nimrodel back to Gondor benefitted from full sails, and the seas were calm. The voyage north was much more enjoyable than the one that bore us to Umbar by way of the Shield Isles, with not so much as a cloud in the sky!

'But Gandalf had need of us, and this dwarf would not refuse him... especially not once we met up with him and he said we would travel south on land, through Harondor, and not by ship again so soon!''We meet now in unhappier circumstances than a wedding at midsummer, ${PLAYER}, but still it is good to see you. When Mithrandir called for my aid upon arriving at Minas Tirith, I could not refuse it. Never would I refuse such a call.

'If only the Bright Company could have been assembled. Alas, that noble group belongs to another age, and many of those worthies have already departed for the West. I fear even the full complement of the Bright Company might not be enough to face this evil.

'When you learn from Mithrandir what it is we must oppose you will understand. I will see you back at the camp after you have spoken to the others.''After my brother and I completed our work, we returned to Minas Tirith. While there, we received word from Mithrandir that he had urgent need of us to face some hidden threat.

'Elessar would have come himself, but Mithrandir refused him. With such as we to protect him I know not why, but the Wizard was most clear that Elessar should remain in Gondor and not come south with us.''Did you know Mithrandir has journeyed in these lands before now? He spoke of his travels to us as we came south through Harondor. When he walked here long ago, the people who dwelt in this land named him "Inkanûsh."'

'He spoke too of the Kintai, the Elves of the Oasis who tend to beautiful groves throughout the region.'

You tell Elrohir that one of the Kintai has come with you to Urash Dâr.

'I will be most pleased to speak with her!' he exclaims.'The companions that came with me from the north have all returned to camp? Good. Then it is time for me to tell you what I have learned, and what must be done to face it.'

Mithrandir scans the distant horizon and leans upon his staff, gathering his thoughts. When he speaks again, his voice is cold.

'A great evil sleeps in Urash Dâr, undisturbed for thousands of years. It has only now begun to awaken, for the enchantment that kept it from stirring would last until all knowledge of it passed beyond living memory.

'The last to know of this evil used many names, ${PLAYER}. In these lands he was called Barkhûsh. Folk in the north knew him as Sauron. In every land he was the Dark Lord, but he served another in ages past; his master was Morgoth, the great enemy of the world. With the destruction of the One Ring and the death of Sauron, this enchantment, the Geas of Morgoth, has been lifted.

'Morgoth can no longer trouble the circles of the world, but this evil he set in the roots of the earth has outlived even his greatest servant.'Mithrandir wishes to tell you what he has learned about the Geas of Morgoth, and what it could mean for Middle-earth.Mithrandir has much to say about a number of different subjects.Hear what Mithrandir has to say about the KintaiMithrandir has much to say about a number of different subjects.Hear what Mithrandir has to say about the Elder OrcsMithrandir has much to say about a number of different subjects.Hear what Mithrandir has to say about the ThardúthMithrandir has much to say about a number of different subjects.Hear what Mithrandir has to say about the Grim SouthronMithrandir has much to say about a number of different subjects.Hear what Mithrandir has to say about the Dark Lord SauronMithrandir is at your campsite in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at your campsite in Urash Dâr'In Elder Days the world was greatly changed from its present form; Morgoth coveted it all. His fortress at Thangorodrim served as the seat of his power, but his servants went forth and established the control of their master in lands far distant. One such dominion subjugated for centuries the territory that is today called Shagâna, or Near Harad by the Gondorians. The tyrants of this dominion served Morgoth in all things; their empire was called Zíkar, and trustworthy accounts of it have disappeared. What remains of its history is hard to decipher by any but the most learned among the Wise.

'But to understand the rise of Zíkar you must learn first of the Kintai, the Elves who did not join the Valar in the West. They once knew a great deal about Zíkar, but in the centuries since that empire disappeared they have tried to forget.''During the early years of the First Age, various kingdoms of Men began to arise throughout Shagâna. The Kinta Elves gave them a wide berth, withdrawing into the Emerald Wood that once covered vast portions of the region; the young kingdoms of Men did not miss the Kintai, but focused instead upon commerce with the Temámir dwarves they encountered. But the Kinta clans and the tribes of Men shared more in common than they believed, for they were each the hated foes of Morgoth, and he wanted them destroyed.

'His servants reserved particular torments for the Kintai, luring them through guile and deceit into hidden caverns beneath the eastern hills, where they were subjected to terrible desecrations. These imprisoned Kintai were twisted into the progenitors of a terrible breed of Orc, the like of which has not been seen since the Dominion of Zíkar ended.''These were the Elder Orcs, fashioned in the pits beneath Zíkar by foul craft of Morgoth. Memory of them faded over the passage of centuries; even the name that was given to them, "Utûgi," brought to mind only dimly-remembered images, unfamiliar and indistinct demons of the ancient world.

'The Utûgi possessed a size, strength, and cunning that Sauron coveted but could not replicate; the Orcs he bred in later years could not match those that belonged to his master, and that deficiency rankled. But the Utûgi were not the worst of the evils loosed upon the world by the Dominion of Zíkar, though they passed beyond all living memory in the years since. These were the Thardúth, the Sere Ones, and they served Morgoth in all things.''The eldest of the Kintai were twisted and corrupted into these Sere Ones by rites too vile to contemplate; the Thardúth rose in importance and power until they had become the deadliest rulers of Zíkar. Morgoth charged them with maintaining a tyrannical hold over the lands of the east and south, breeding as many Elder Orcs as they could for his war. When the call came at last, Orcs and wicked men departed for the north in vast numbers, swelling Morgoth's hordes in distant Beleriand.

'But Morgoth's might did not prevail against the combined forces of the Elves and the Valar, and when Beleriand sank beneath the sea the Utûgi sent by the Thardúth were drowned and forgotten. Following the defeat of their dark master, the Thardúth that remained in Zíkar feared uprisings by the emboldened Men of Shagâna, but so too they feared the reprisal of Sauron, who they blamed in part for Morgoth's defeat. Using all of their sorcerous powers they set a geas upon their vaults, sealing them shut and deeming that their location should never be found so long as any lived in Middle-earth who remembered them.

'They took what remained of their Utûgi deep into their sealed vaults and with witchcraft caused them to sleep, never again to wake while their halls remained shut. Last they did the same to themselves, lying down in the dark depths to await the hoped-for day when they could return to the living world above.''Memory of the Utûgi and the Thardúth dimmed. In time, only the very eldest of the Kintai surviving in Shagâna remembered these servants of Morgoth. As they began to fade in the manner of their people, those memories vanished with them.

'But Sauron remembered. In the ages that followed, he sent his servants to seek the hidden vaults of the Thardúth, hungering for their power and the secret of breeding anew the mighty Utûgi. The Nazgûl searched for the Sere Ones, but they searched in vain; the geas of Morgoth remained effective. Sauron despaired of ever locating the lost vaults, and turned his attention and his cunning to other matters.''I believe now that Sauron could never break the geas of Morgoth, for so long as he remembered the Thardúth the enchantment of the Sere Ones could not be undone.

'But then the most unlikely of events transpired: Frodo Baggins accepted the Quest of the Ring. Sauron's Ring was destroyed. Morgoth's greatest servant perished. The Thardúth passed beyond the memory of any who lived in the world, and the seal upon their hidden vaults was broken.

'Sauron was gone, but the Sere Ones, servants of Morgoth his dark master, were loosed once again upon the world.''If only I had learned this before now. The deathly spirits of Thuringwath knew of Sauron's rivalry with his banished master, and of his search for the unknown powers Morgoth left behind, but they were slow to reveal these things to me. From their speech and from evil runes carved into the very foundations of Mordor I have pieced together this tale, but the learning came slowly.

'Too slowly, ${PLAYER}. When I understood the magnitude of this threat, I returned to Minas Tirith with all of the haste Shadowfax had to give. I sent word to our trusted allies and our lean band hurried south through Harondor with utmost speed.

'We must fight, ${PLAYER}. We must fight this legacy of Morgoth, or the Sere Ones will restore the Dominion of Zíkar and bring about a new age of darkness in Middle-earth.'The evils that have awoken within Urash Dâr spell an uncertain future for not just the people of Ikorbân, but for all of Middle-earth.Some of your companions at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr have begun to devise plans for combatting the Thardúth and the Elder Orcs that serve them.Talk to Glorfindel at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârTalk to Khôltekh at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârTalk to Rothog at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârRothog asked you to defeat Orcs throughout Urash Dâr.
Glorfindel asked you to collect jackal teeth and craban claws from creatures throughout Urash Dâr so he can examine them for unusual effects or signs of corruption.
Khôltekh asked you to draw him a map to the ruins of Ashâgir in north-west Urash Dâr.Defeat Orcs throughout Urash Dâr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect teeth from jackals in Urash Dâr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect claws from crebain in Urash Dâr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Glorfindel is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Rothog is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Rothog at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârKhôltekh is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Rahâzi is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Rahâzi at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr'We will not cower before this threat, no matter how deadly the foes arrayed before us. Have we not powerful allies of our own?

'When I walked in this land, many years ago, the folk here called me "Inkanûsh." Now I call upon my own memory of their language to name this camp: Ilzag Khûl, the Last Barrier Against Evil. From here we will prepare our defence against the Thardúth and the Utûgi they created. It will have to be enough.

'Speak to our allies here at Ilzag Khûl and devise a plan, ${PLAYER}.''Speak to our allies here at Ilzag Khûl and devise the plan by which we will stand against the Thardúth and their Utûgi warriors.''On the ride south from Gondor, Mithrandir spoke to me of the Thardúth and the Utûgi. I knew them not, ${PLAYER}, for when the Dominion of Zíkar was at its height I was far away, and... otherwise engaged. The size and scope of the danger posed by Morgoth to the world is difficult to convey. When later I learned of the breaking of Thangorodrim and of his defeat in the Great Battle by the Host of Valinor...'

Glorfindel falls silent, lost in distant memory.

'I knew it to be true and still I could not believe it, ${PLAYER}. He is gone and can never return, but the seed he planted in this land seeks to burst through the crust after years of lying dormant. That cannot but affect the creatures here, and perhaps they will tell us something of how to combat this waking evil.

'Will you gather the teeth and claws of jackals and crebain within Urash Dâr? By studying them I hope to learn something of use.''The Wizard of the North weaves a curious tale, but I have seen little evidence of these creatures that have so frightened him. We should keep our attention upon the purpose that brought us here: the pursuit of Azagath and Nakási. The map they stole may have directed them to some location within these canyons. This was once part of the Kingdom of Hamât, before it was conquered by Ordâkh, and they may have searched for the once-mighty fortress of Ashâgir said to remain here.

'While you were visiting the Elves, we spoke with a scout who claimed Azagath alone crossed into Urash Dâr. That may be true, but I caution you to not lower your guard overmuch; we must search the canyons carefully, for Nakási may remain hidden from our sight, waiting for her moment to strike. I do not wish to leave this camp without reason, for she may think to attack her former allies who remain with the Kindred of the Coins. Still, we must search these canyons!'

Khôltekh raises his hand in a gesture of sudden realization that feels quite artificial to you.

'I have it! I will give you a parchment, and when you find the entrance to Ashâgir, you can draw me a map of the location. Then I will be able to go straight there, minimizing the risk of ambush by Nakási! It is a good idea, ${PLAYER}. Ashâgir is said to be somewhere to the north-west.''The old man that came from the North speaks strangely and uses words unfamiliar to me, but one thing was clear.'

Rothog bares his fangs in a brilliant and blinding smile.

'The Orcs we have seen before now are the merest imitations of a stronger and larger breed: the Utûgi. I am eager to test my combat prowess against these foes!

'If they are similar to the warriors who fight in Dahâl Huliz, they will attract smaller and weaker Orcs to them as servants and thralls. The sheer force of their presence will draw other Orcs to them. Bring the battle to these weaker Orcs, and we may catch the attention of these Utûgi! I hope it will be so!''Will you gather the teeth and claws of jackals and crebain within Urash Dâr? By studying them I hope to learn something of use.''When you find the entrance to Ashâgir, you can draw me a map of the location. Then I will be able to go straight there, minimizing the risk of ambush by Nakási! It is a good idea, ${PLAYER}. Ashâgir is said to be somewhere to the north-west.''If these Utûgi are similar to the warriors who fight in Dahâl Huliz, they will attract smaller and weaker Orcs to them as servants and thralls. The sheer force of their presence will draw other Orcs to them. Bring the battle to these weaker Orcs, and we may catch the attention of these Utûgi! I hope it will be so!''I hope you faced little trouble among the canyons as you collected these items, ${PLAYER}. I will begin my study of them and see what affect the waking of the Thardúth might have upon the creatures that dwell here.''I have begun my study of those trophies you brought me, but it has not yet seen results.''I heard the sounds of battle from here! I would have joined you, but Belondor asked that I not leave the camp. He expressed some fear that once I began fighting the lesser Orcs and their Utûgi betters, I might not return, but instead continue deeper into the canyons in the grip of a powerful lust for combat!'

Rothog tilts his massive head to one side.

'Once in awhile Belondor makes a good point, ${PLAYER}.''I am pleased you fought against the Orcs. May the Utûgi provide a greater challenge!''You have done as I asked, then? Yes, the map you have drawn makes the location of Ashâgir quite clear to me! Very good, my useful friend. This will help ensure safety during my journey to Ashâgir. I need not fear an ambush by Nakási.''The map you drew for me will help ensure safety during my journey to Ashâgir. I need not fear an ambush by Nakási.''Why speaks the dwarf Khôltekh of the fortress Ashâgir? While you were gone, he spoke of little else, and to anyone who would listen.'

You tell Rahâzi that you understand he wishes to search for Azagath Sea-shadow within that fortress, and she frowns.

'That was the last of the Hamâti strongholds to fall to Ordâkh, and it is said that it was home to the largest stockpile of Narghâk ever crafted by the sages of Hamât. They resisted the besieging Ordâkhai for a year and a day, but in the end the Hûl of Ordâkh sent assassins on a moonless night to climb the cliff; after slaying the guards they threw open the gates and the Ordâkhai army stormed the fortress.

'With no other choice remaining to him, Taldûmu, last king of Hamât, set the stores of Narghâk ablaze. We name him today the Shâr Who Burned. No one within the fortress, defender or attacker, survived, and the fires burned for six days. When they finally went out, only ashes remained, along with the scraps of Taldûmu's crown, melted into a tangle.

'If the dwarf Khôltekh goes to Ashâgir, I suggest you keep a wary eye upon him. The stockpile of Narghâk that once remained within burned long ago, but the fortress is an ill-omened tomb: the pyre of Hamât.'Mithrandir believes facing the threat posed by the Thardúth will need the strong cooperation of many peoples: the Shagâni, the Kintai, and the Umbari.Rahâzi is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Rahâzi at Ilzag KhûlMithrandir is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârBelondor is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Belondor at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârBelondor is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Belondor at Ilzag KhûlSilirtas is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Silirtas at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârSilirtas is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Silirtas at Ilzag KhûlSilirtas is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Silirtas at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârSigileth is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Sigileth at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârSigileth is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.<rgb=#00FF00>Wave</rgb> to Sigileth at Ilzag KhûlThe emissaries have left on their missions.Wait for the emissaries to departMithrandir is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr'But that is not why I came to speak to you. The Wizard from the North has asked me to to return to Ambarûl with a message for my mother. He wants her to send as many Dumûri warriors as she can spare to us, to stand against these waking servants of Morgoth.

'You have allied yourself with this Wizard before, ${PLAYER}. Have you known him to overreact to danger at any moment in the past?'

You confess that if Mithrandir believes he needs an army of warriors to face the Thardúth and their Utûgi, he is probably correct. Rahâzi nods slowly.

'That is what I suspected. Farewell to you, my friend. I will see you again as soon as I can, and let us hope my mother sends the warriors requested by your Wizard!''Farewell to you, my friend. I will see you again as soon as I can, and let us hope my mother sends the warriors requested by your Wizard!''The Shagâni warrior will speak to Girhâzi of Ambarûl, and it is my hope she will return with many fierce warriors of that folk, but we will need as many fighters as we can gather to face the waking Thardúth.

'It is my plan to send the Kinta woman Silirtas to the oasis groves, that she may return with Elf-warriors of that people; after all, their lands are threatened by the evil that stirs within Urash Dâr; it will not be contained without their help.

'I also wish to send one of the leaders of Umbar to return with as many Umbari soldiers as can be marshalled in haste. What is that ruling body named? The Kindred of the Coins? Who of these should go, ${PLAYER}?'

You consider the choices available to you, and decide only Belondor can be trusted to return quickly with a sizeable force of Umbari soldiers. Mithrandir nods.

'Very well. If he is as respected in Umbar as you say he is, he will be best-suited for this task. I ask you to tell him to depart at once, for the hour is late indeed. Then you must do the same for the Kinta Silirtas. Return to me when they have departed.''If Belondor is as respected in Umbar as you say he is, he will be best-suited for this task. I ask you to tell him to depart at once, for the hour is late indeed. Then you must do the same for the Kinta Silirtas. Return to me when they have departed.''What asks the Wizard of me, ${PLAYER}? You should speak it aloud, though I believe I know already what my task must be.'

You tell Belondor that Mithrandir wants him to return to Umbar Baharbêl as quickly as he can, and to come back with a contingent of Umbari soldiers.

'I knew in my heart I would be called to do this. I cannot promise I will return with the number of soldiers the Wizard wants... but I will return with as many as I can.

'Good-bye to you, ${PLAYER}. Take care of my friends in the company, and beware of the terrible creatures that lurk within Urash Dâr! I will see you again, as soon as I may.''Good-bye to you. Take care of my friends in the company, and beware of the terrible creatures that lurk within Urash Dâr! I will see you again, as soon as I may.''There is talk around camp that the Wizard seeks volunteers to bring reinforcements to Urash Dâr to face the servants of Morgoth. Is it true?'

You tell Silirtas that Mithrandir wanted you to ask her if she would be willing to return to the oasis groves of the Kintai with this request.

'I will of course do this, ${PLAYER}. First to Vartúlin I will go, then to Shonith, and if the time can be spared I will speak with Shanthar at Arwáth. The Kintai must defend their groves, as they always have.''That is what I wish I could say, ${PLAYER}.

'But I cannot. If these Kintai reinforcements are needed with all haste... we can risk no delay. I know the untethered periods to which I succumb. I... do not want to endanger the delivery of this message, or the return of enough Kintai warriors to face their ancient, forgotten foes.

'I am not the person to carry this message. Will you bear it on my behalf, Sigileth?''I am not the person to carry this message. Will you bear it on my behalf, Sigileth?'Sigileth stares at Silirtas in stunned surprise, knowing how difficult this admission must be for the Kinta Elf. When she recovers, her voice is thick with emotion and she speaks with understanding.

'I will do this, Silirtas, fear not. Spend not a moment more thinking upon it. The Kintai will be told of our need, and I will return with as many as I can muster, and with utmost speed. Farewell, dear friend.

'Good-bye, ${PLAYER}. Take care of my brother, and make sure he does nothing foolish. It is in his nature to act first and think later, if he thinks at all!''The emissaries have departed? Good.

'I hope they make haste. If they do not they may find only our bodies remain, crushed beneath the heels of the Utûgi and their Thardúth masters!'Mithrandir worries that the secrets buried within Urash Dâr might spell doom for not just the folk of Shagâna, but for all of Middle-earth as well.Corudan is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Corudan at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârKhôltekh can be found at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.

Corudan is concerned about Khôltekh's apparent discovery of a cache of Narghâk, the Hungry Fire.Talk to Khôltekh at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârKhôltekh asked to meet you and Corudan outside Ashâgir, to the north-west in Urash Dâr.Meet with Khôltekh outside Ashâgir atop the north-west cliffs in Urash DârKhôltekh stands outside Ashâgir, to the north-west in Urash Dâr.Talk to Khôltekh outside Ashâgir in Urash DârCorudan stands outside Ashâgir, atop the north-west cliffs in Urash Dâr.Talk to Corudan outside Ashâgir in Urash DârSomewhere inside the depths of Ashâgir, Khôltekh believes you will find a chamber containing a lost, hidden cache of Narghâk.Search the depths of Ashâgir for a hidden chamber containing the cache of NarghâkCorudan stands within the undisturbed chamber deep within Ashâgir.Talk to Corudan in the undisturbed chamber deep within AshâgirKhôltekh stands within the undisturbed chamber deep within Ashâgir.Talk to Khôltekh in the undisturbed chamber deep within Ashâgir'It may not come to that! The Thardúth have not woken in thousands of years, and the world is much changed since their Dominion of Zíkar threatened these lands. They may expect little in the way of opposition.

'I spoke with the Elf Corudan while you sent the emissaries upon their urgent errands. From the description he gave to me it appears the man you pursued here, Azagath Sea-shadow, is a Gúrzyul of Sauron. I believe in his search for the Narghâk stockpile of which Corudan spoke, Azagath may find himself ensnared by the Thardúth.

'Judging from the words of Megorlam of Thuringwath, the Sere Ones bore no love for Sauron; they suspected him of treachery and disloyalty to Morgoth. How will they treat with one of the Gúrzyul, I wonder?''I spoke with the Elf Corudan while you sent the emissaries upon their urgent errands. From the description he gave to me it appears the man you pursued here, Azagath Sea-shadow, is a Gúrzyul of Sauron. I believe in his search for the Narghâk stockpile of which Corudan spoke, Azagath may find himself ensnared by the Thardúth. The Sere Ones bore no love for Sauron; they suspected him of treachery and disloyalty to Morgoth. How will they treat with one of the Gúrzyul, I wonder?''I am worried, ${PLAYER}. I overheard Khôltekh speaking to Rothog, and from his words it appeared that he believes he has discovered a lost cache of Narghâk. Do you think it is true?

'Everything my sister told me about this substance fills me with fear. If Khôltekh has found a stockpile of the Hungry Fire...

'Will you confront him about this discovery? We cannot simply stand by and allow the Kindred of the Coins to acquire such a thing!''Will you confront Khôltekh about this discovery? We cannot simply stand by and allow the Kindred of the Coins to acquire such a thing!''What do you want?'

You ask Khôltekh if Corudan misheard him concerning the dwarf's discovery of a lost stockpile of Narghâk. Khôltekh peers at you curiously.

'I have not discovered it yet, ${PLAYER}... but I am hopeful it will not be much longer now. It had been my plan to search the old Hamâti fortress of Ashâgir for just such a stockpile, but I had planned to search it alone. Now you can join me, and if Corudan the Elf is so concerned about my comings-and-goings, he can join us as well.

'I will meet the two of you outside Ashâgir, to the north-west. Your map was most clear to me, and I will have no difficulty finding the place. I trust you will have no problems joining me there?''It had been my plan to search the old Hamâti fortress of Ashâgir for an overlooked stockpile of Narghâk, but I had planned to search it alone. Now you can join me, and if Corudan the Elf is so concerned about my comings-and-goings, he can join us as well.

'I will meet the two of you outside Ashâgir, to the north-west.''Here we are.''Here we are.'

Khôltekh surveys the fortress of Ashâgir.

'Ashâgir once held the largest stockpile of Narghâk ever collected in one place. Rather than surrender it to his enemies, the last Shâr of Hamât set fire to it, consuming fortress, Shâr, and stockpile.

'But perhaps some of the Narghâk remained in sealed chambers belowground. It is my hope that some of that mighty stockpile escaped the conflagration, and you will help me find it. Does Corudan the Elf find this distasteful?''Perhaps some of the Narghâk remained in sealed chambers belowground. It is my hope that some of that mighty stockpile escaped the conflagration, and you will help me find it. Does Corudan the Elf find this distasteful?''I do not like this, ${PLAYER}, but if any member of the Kindred of the Coins finds a stockpile of Narghâk I believe we should know of it. That is true of Azagath or Nakási, but it is also true of Khôltekh.

'I will help search the fortress for this stockpile... but I hope we find nothing.''I will help search the fortress for this stockpile... but I hope we find nothing.''It is as I feared: a stockpile of Narghâk survived the conflagration of Ashâgir. This chamber must have been sealed away sometime during the Hamâti reign. When Taldûmu set the torch to the rest of the Hungry Fire, this chamber escaped the burning.

'The quakes that shook this land following the defeat of Sauron must have opened the way to this sealed chamber, ${PLAYER}.''The quakes that shook this land following the defeat of Sauron must have opened the way to this sealed chamber.''Have you ever seen anything so beautiful as this undisturbed chamber? Behold: the last surviving stockpile of the Hungry Fire, a weapon thought vanished from the world!

'I see the way forward now. The fortunes of my dominion are on the rise!'

A curious light glimmers in Khôltekh's eye, and you are unable to determine if he speaks of the Kindred of the Coins, the Corsairs of Umbar, or the Temámir of the Shoreward Stone.Khôltekh admits, with regret, that the shipping of the Narghâk out of Urash Dâr will have to wait until the threat of the Thardúth has passed, and that will require something he does not relish: a confrontation.Mithrandir is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârGlorfindel is to the north-east within the channel of the River Mûshid that runs through Urash Dâr.

Mithrandir has sent much of your travelling company along the river to the north-east, where he believes the Thardúth dwell. He asked you to speak with them during the approach.Meet with Glorfindel to the north-east within the channel of the River MûshidGlorfindel is to the north-east within the channel of the River Mûshid that runs through Urash Dâr.

Mithrandir has sent much of your travelling company along the river to the north-east, where he believes the Thardúth dwell. He asked you to speak with them during the approach.Talk to Glorfindel within the channel of the River MûshidLegolas is to the north within the channel of the River Mûshid that runs through Urash Dâr.

Mithrandir has sent much of your travelling company along the river to the north-east, where he believes the Thardúth dwell. He asked you to speak with them during the approach.Meet with Legolas to the north within the channel of the River MûshidLegolas is to the north within the channel of the River Mûshid that runs through Urash Dâr.

Mithrandir has sent much of your travelling company along the river to the north, where he believes the Thardúth dwell. He asked you to speak with them during the approach.Talk to Legolas to the north within the channel of the River MûshidMost of your travelling party has arrived at the approach to Utug-bûr, to the distant north-east in Urash Dâr.Meet up with the rest of your travelling party to the distant north-east outside Utug-bûrMithrandir is outside Utug-bûr, in the north-eastern reaches of Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir outside Utug-bûrSilirtas is outside Utug-bûr, in north-east Urash Dâr.Talk to Silirtas outside Utug-bûrCorudan is outside Utug-bûr, in north-east Urash Dâr.Talk to Corudan outside Utug-bûrCorudan is outside Utug-bûr, in north-east Urash Dâr.Talk to Corudan outside Utug-bûr and stand against AzagathMithrandir commands your allied forces during the Dagor Carlanthir, the Battle of the Red Waterfall.Talk to Mithrandir during the Dagor Carlanthir'But I must not get ahead of myself. This Narghâk cannot be transported anywhere until something can be done about the ancient evil that wakens in Urash Dâr. That will require something of which I am not particularly fond: a confrontation.

'We will return to Ilzag Khûl and see what the Wizard from the North has decided to do about these servants of Morgoth.''We will return to Ilzag Khûl and see what the Wizard from the North has decided to do about these servants of Morgoth.''Few dwell here, but Legolas and Gimli have spoken with a small number of huntsmen and trappers among the canyons. They report an increase of dangerous creatures beside the river flowing from the north-eastern reaches of Urash Dâr.

'That is the direction from which the Utûgi come, ${PLAYER}. Their masters must remain within a hidden place somewhere near the source of the river. It is time for us to confront the Sere Ones.

'The walk will be a long one, but we must remain alert to threats as we proceed. The journey will become more dangerous as we near the hiding-place of the Thardúth.''The walk will be a long one, but we must remain alert to threats as we proceed. The journey will become more dangerous as we near the hiding-place of the Thardúth.''Yes?''The creatures that prowl outside the river channel grow in danger and ferocity; it was wise of Mithrandir to direct us to follow the river as we approach the secret dwelling of the Thardúth.

'We must be prepared for anything; neither I nor Gandalf have been tested by the Sere Ones, neither in this nor any age of the world.''Yes?''We must be prepared for anything; neither I nor Gandalf have been tested by the Sere Ones, neither in this nor any age of the world.''Neither I nor Gimli know what awaits us at the end of this journey, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps if we had some idea of how the Thardúth remained hidden for so long, but alas!

'We must be ready for anything.''We must be ready for anything.''We have arrived.''Yes, I see now. I understand how the Thardúth remained hidden for so long. When they sealed the doors of their sanctuary, the Sere Ones changed the course of the river, hiding the face of the temple with the new cascade. They then pulled down the cliff-face to completely mask their location, and it remained hidden for the ages to follow.

'The quakes that followed the fall of Sauron shook the earth, changing the cliffs and revealing the entrance to Utug-bûr.''The quakes that followed the fall of Sauron shook the earth, changing the cliffs and revealing the entrance to Utug-bûr.''Is it true that the foes we must face were once of my people? Are they truly beyond saving now, ${PLAYER}?

'This must be the cruelest of Morgoth's hurts, a wound deep enough to persist so many thousands of years after his defeat.''This must be the cruelest of Morgoth's hurts, a wound deep enough to persist so many thousands of years after his defeat.''We are being watched, ${PLAYER}.

'I cannot see those who watch us, but I feel their eyes upon us. Can you feel them too?''We will together approach Utug-bûr. Let us hope the great courage possessed by our small number will give us safety as we pass beyond the falls.''With fell sorcery the Thardúth make the falls run as red as blood, and by that name will this battle be known: Dagor Carlanthir, the Battle of the Red Waterfall!

'We could not avoid this battle! We are now all that stands between these servants of Morgoth and the rest of Middle-earth!'The Dagor Carlanthir rages in Urash Dâr as the servants of the waking Thardúth bring war against the people of Ikorbân and their allies.

<rgb=#00FF00>To return to the Dagor Carlanthir in the event you leave it, you can use a banner at your campsite of Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr to return to the fighting.</rgb>The Dagor Carlanthir rages in Urash Dâr.Mithrandir commands the allied forces on the south-western bank of the river during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Mithrandir during the Dagor CarlanthirThe Dagor Carlanthir rages in Urash Dâr.Mithrandir struggles against a distant opponent during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Mithrandir during the Dagor CarlanthirCorudan stands near Mithrandir during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Corudan during the Dagor CarlanthirCorudan stands near Mithrandir during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Corudan during the Dagor Carlanthir and help him seal Utug-bûrMithrandir commands the allied forces during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Mithrandir during the Dagor CarlanthirBelondor stands near Mithrandir during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Belondor during the Dagor Carlanthir'We are lucky indeed that the Shagâni warrior arrived with her mother's fighters, for without them we could not long stand against such numbers. The Dumûri fight fiercely, but Azagath has not yet returned to the field, and I fear when he does he will not be alone. The Thardúth wait to see the strength of our opposition.

'Press forward and lend your assistance where you may, ${PLAYER}! The battle rages all about us, and your friends have need of you!''Press forward and lend your assistance where you may! The battle rages all about us, and your friends have need of you!''An evil will tests my defences! I cannot pinpoint from where this sorcery comes, but I know it must lie within Utug-bûr. One of the Thardúth seeks to know who defies his people, and he challenges my resolve.

'Worry not for me! My defences hold, for the time being. Return to where the fighting is thickest, and lend your aid to your friends once again!''Worry not for me! My defences hold, for the time being. Return to where the fighting is thickest, and lend your aid to your friends once again!''You... you have come back... but the fighting persists...'

Mithrandir's voice is weary with strain, his face haggard and drawn.

'The Thardúth remain... within their temple, ${PLAYER}. We must count that... a blessing...'

Mithrandir falls silent, his attention focused upon a distant, invisible foe.Mithrandir struggles against a distant, invisible foe, and says nothing further to you.'Mithrandir is in trouble, ${PLAYER}! Is there nothing we can do to help him? If the Thardúth emerge from within the temple...'

Corudan stares at you with a dawning realization.

'We have to prevent the Thardúth from leaving Utug-bûr! I know what we have to do!''We need to seal the entrance of Utug-bûr before the Thardúth emerge to finish us!

'We need to retrieve the Hungry Fire from Ashâgir! I will tell Rothog... we have need of his strength!''There you are! To where did you disappear, ${PLAYER}? No, it matters not!

'Did you see the arrival of Belondor? He returned at the head of an army of Umbari soldiers, and they have joined the battle!

'My own battle with my unseen Thardúth opponent is at a low ebb; he has relaxed his onslaught for the moment, perhaps to change to a more successful tactic. It may be the tide is near to turning.''Did you see the arrival of Belondor? He returned at the head of an army of Umbari soldiers, and they have joined the battle!''It is good to see you are well, ${PLAYER}. When I set out for Umbar Baharbêl I believed your Wizard might wait to begin fighting the battle until I returned with my army, but it turns out that was not in his plan!

'But we are here now, and there is no shortage of Orcs to fight! But where are the ancient creatures of which he spoke? Where are these corrupted Elves?'Belondor has returned at the head of a small army of Umbari reinforcements, but even that may not be enough to turn the tide of the battle.Belondor wants to send some of his soldiers to fight alongside Sirgon at the front line to the north-east.Go to the front line of the Dagor Carlanthir, to the north-eastElladan fights by Sirgon's side during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Elladan during the Dagor CarlanthirSirgon has been grievously injured during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Sirgon during the Dagor CarlanthirThe Dagor Carlanthir rages all around you.The Dagor Carlanthir rages all around youCorudan stands in the middle of the river during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Corudan in the middle of the river during the Dagor CarlanthirCorudan stands in the middle of the river during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Corudan and help him place the caches of Narghâk outside Utug-bûrSilirtas lies outside Utug-bûr during the Dagor Carlanthir.Examine Silirtas during the Dagor CarlanthirCorudan stands outside Utug-bûr during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Corudan outside Utug-bûr during the Dagor Carlanthir'Is Azagath here? I have business with the Sea-shadow!

'Let us push forward! My blade hungers for the blood of betrayers! Let us fight alongside the soldiers that marched with me to battle!

'Lead on, ${PLAYER}!''Let us push forward! My blade hungers for the blood of betrayers! Let us fight alongside the soldiers that marched with me to battle! Lead on, ${PLAYER}!''It is bad, ${PLAYER}.

'Sirgon stood his ground against one of the great Utûgi as it bore down upon us, but he saw not the smaller creature that followed in the wake of the huge Orc: a small and spindly goblin-thing. It stuck him a dozen times with its knife before we noticed.'

Elladan lowers his voice until you can only just hear him over the sound of the fighting.

'He will not survive this battle, ${PLAYER}. We have done all we could.'Elladan stands his ground against the encircling foes, but says nothing else.'I cannot see your face, ${PLAYER}... are you... are you all right?

'The battle is not yet over... is it? I can hear the fighting...

'How... are we doing...?''We are not yet finished! We have to place the Narghâk around the entrance, or it will be too late!

'If the Thardúth emerge from Utug-bûr it is all over! ${PLAYER}! Can you hear me?''This will be our only chance! We have to collapse the entrance before the Thardúth emerge!'Hoping against hope, you check the Kinta Elf for some sign of life, but there are none to be found. Silirtas is dead.Silirtas is dead.'What can we do? We are out of time, ${PLAYER}!'Corudan has begged you to rescue Sigileth from within Utug-bûr before it is too late.Corudan stands outside Utug-bûr during the Dagor Carlanthir.Talk to Corudan and go with him into the darkness of Utug-bûrMithrandir is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr'We must seal the entrance... but my sister is still inside! What do we do?

'I beg you, ${PLAYER}! We have to rescue Sigileth before the entrance is sealed!''We will not have much time to bring Sigileth back out of the darkness, ${PLAYER}! If the Narghâk explodes while we are still inside...'

Corudan shakes his head firmly.

'We will be quick! We have to be! Hurry now, my friend! There is no time to waste!''And so the battle ends in victory for the united forces of Shagâna, Umbar, and Gondor. I can still feel the presence of the Thardúth with whom I strove; his hatred and his anger burns still in the darkness of Utug-bûr like a beacon. But his rage is unfocused, and it will be some time before he or his kind can emerge to trouble Middle-earth again.

'The threat of the Thardúth has been reduced, but it is not eliminated. Other vaults of like kind to Utug-bûr may lie just beneath the surface; the breaking of the Geas of Morgoth means the occupants of these other hidden strongholds now stir restlessly, their long slumber nearing its end. I believe the Dominion of Zíkar stretched across much of Shagâna, after all, if the deathly spirit that told me of its existence can be believed. What cause would it have to lie to me, ${PLAYER}?'

The Wizard sighs and you see the faintest hint of a smile beneath his beard.

'It seems I have made a habit of giving you bad tidings, my friend. I was named "Láthspell" once, in a distant land, "Ill-news," and if I keep going at this rate you will begin calling me that too!

'So here are some good tidings: by your deeds and those of your friends and allies, a great evil has been opposed. The Thardúth will not rise again for some time, at least.'The Dagor Carlanthir has ended, but the cost of victory was high and left its mark upon those who were there.Belondor is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Belondor at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârSirgon is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Girhâzi and Rahâzi are at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Girhâzi at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârTalk to Rahâzi at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârRothog is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Rothog at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârRothog's lion Húrshauk is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Húrshauk waits for you to <rgb=#00FF00>pat</rgb> himKhôltekh is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Khôltekh at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârCorudan is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Corudan at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârSigileth is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Sigileth at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârMithrandir is at Ilzag Khûl in Urash Dâr.Talk to Mithrandir at Ilzag Khûl in Urash DârHármelak is in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja, in the Upper Ward of Umbar Baharbêl.Return to Hármelak in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja, in Umbar BaharbêlHármelak is in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja, in the Upper Ward of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Hármelak in the Meeting-hall of Dâr LajaJaith stands guard outside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Jaith outside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja'But the cost of this victory was high. Those who survived the battle will need words of healing and of support now, ${PLAYER}. You must give them what you are able, for they will look to you for guidance: Shagâni, Umbari, and Gondorian alike.''The cost of this victory was high. Those who survived the battle will need words of healing and of support now, ${PLAYER}. You must give them what you are able, for they will look to you for guidance: Shagâni, Umbari, and Gondorian alike.''I have made Sirgon as comfortable as I can, ${PLAYER}, but there is nothing else that can be done.

'His final moments approach.''His final moments approach.''This is... important, ${PLAYER}.'

Sirgon winces with the effort of speaking, but he refuses to be silent.

'My wife... and my children... tell them I love... I love them. Tell them too... that I was a fool to leave... but that at the end... I was... at peace.

'And I want you to... tell Gwallang... that I captained a boat... of my own. I set out upon... my final voyage... and I look forward to... to seeing him again... on a distant shore. Tell him not... that I died like... like this.'

Sirgon closes his eyes and it seems he is gone, but with a great effort he opens them one last time.

'Elessar... must not be... the foe of the... Shagâni people.... He must not be... the third... the third... Hyarmendacil...

'Will you... help him... help him... live up to...?''This is... important, ${PLAYER}.'

Sirgon winces with the effort of speaking, but he refuses to be silent.

'My wife... and my children... tell them I love... I love them. Tell them too... that I was a fool to leave... but that at the end... I was... at peace.'

Sirgon closes his eyes and it seems he is gone, but with a great effort he opens them one last time.

'Elessar... must not be... the foe of the... Shagâni people.... He must not be... the third... the third... Hyarmendacil...

'Will you... help him... help him... live up to...?''The stories of our past glories and our mighty heroes spread from fighter to fighter during the battle, inspiring each of my warriors to attain ever greater heights of skill and ferocity. In days to come, the tale of the Sabûdamar, the red-rush battle, will join those revered accounts!

'When Rahâzi came to Iridír with your need, I considered sending only a small number of Dumûri in my stead; I am glad now I did not, for the battle was glorious and I would weep to miss it! Let the Ordâkhai tremble before our demonstrated might against foes older and more terrible than they, and know that to the Ambarûli they are nothing!''This triumph is bittersweet, ${PLAYER}. I am sorry for the losses you have suffered. I too weep for lost friends: dear Surdûsha, with whom I joined the ranks of the Dumûri only a few years ago, perished during the battle.

'She would not wish me to dwell upon it, and as a trained warrior I know to do so is a weakness. But her loss cuts deeply.

'I know this battle had to be fought eventually, ${PLAYER}, and if it was not waged in Urash Dâr it would one day have to be fought in Ambarûl, perhaps within Iridír itself. Surdûsha would not have wanted that, and neither would I.''Let the Ordâkhai tremble before our demonstrated might against foes older and more terrible than they, and know that to the Ambarûli they are nothing!''I know this battle had to be fought eventually, ${PLAYER}, and if it was not waged in Urash Dâr it would one day have to be fought in Ambarûl, perhaps within Iridír itself. Surdûsha would not have wanted that, and neither would I.''My hunger for battle is sated... for now! My khopeshes are in need of sharpening once again, and is that not the mark of a successful journey?

'I enjoyed fighting alongside Tatháta, but she seems to have disappeared again. In the brief time that I have known her she has never enjoyed large gatherings... unless those large gatherings involve a great number of combatants!

'Few can understand the leonine language of Húrshauk as I can, but I will tell you that he now likes you very much. There is nothing like a shared victory to knit bonds of friendship, is there? Go ahead and give him a pat, ${PLAYER}. You and he have both earned it! Ha! Ha! Ha!''Húrshauk now likes you very much. There is nothing like a shared victory to knit bonds of friendship, is there? Go ahead and give him a pat, ${PLAYER}. You and he have both earned it! Ha! Ha! Ha!''Húrshauk is a good judge of character... nearly as skilled at reading people as Rothog!''I heard something very interesting, ${PLAYER}. I heard that not only is the son of Balakhôr alive, but he was seen fighting on the edge of the battle. I wonder: how could this be true when you assured me that Balakhâd was dead?'

Instead of answering the question, you pose one of your own, asking what Khôltekh knows about the disappearance of the water-bearer Sadûkh from Ghalbûru. The dwarf eyes you appraisingly.

'I must apologize, ${PLAYER}, for I underestimated you. It will not happen again.

'But know this: there are none in this land better than I at spy-craft. It was not always so... but it is now. Bear that in mind should you think to oppose my interests.''There are none in this land better than I at spy-craft. It was not always so... but it is now. Bear that in mind should you think to oppose my interests.''We have known each other for less than a year, but it seems I have been indebted to you countless times. You have my thanks once again, ${PLAYER}, and those of my sister. You did not have to follow me into the darkness of Utug-bûr, risking entombment with our terrible foes, but you came with me anyway.'

Corudan grasps your forearm tightly.

'I will never forget that, my friend.'

He releases your arm and your gaze. If you did not know better, you would think his expression might be one of embarrassment. It passes quickly and he is once again his usual practical self.

'Could we have sealed the entrance to Utug-bûr without making use of the Narghâk, ${PLAYER}? Now it matters not, for the deed is done. The collapse required very little of the Hungry Fire, and much of that substance remains in the hidden stockpile deep within Ashâgir. We must set a guard upon it, night and day, to ensure that none think to use it for their own purposes.

'You know of whom I speak, but I would not trust the Hungry Fire in the hands of any who seek it.''We must set a guard upon Ashâgir, night and day, to ensure that none think to use the Hungry Fire for their own purposes.''I was in darkness even before I passed the threshold into Utug-bûr, ${PLAYER}. As Azagath approached Silirtas with murder in his eye, I was overcome by a rage I have never felt before, and never will again. I pursued him into the temple, but I saw nothing of my surroundings; the steps were a red haze, the fog that surrounded me pulsed in time with the pounding of my blood. I would slay him, and I would die with him, and Silirtas would be avenged.'

Sigileth stares at you with haunted eyes.

'What did Silirtas say happens to a Kinta that is slain in battle? "They become wandering, restless spirits, wrathful and hostile," she said. For that to be her fate filled me with tremendous anger and sorrow, and still does. No amount of death and vengeance can repay Azagath or his masters for the cruel, endless un-life they have forced upon Silirtas.

'Back in Umbar I spoke to Nauriel of the Green Hills about the futility of vengeance, and I meant it. Was that not the lesson of Cruel Dancer? I thought I had learned the lesson well, but it was not until I heard your voice in the darkness of Utug-bûr that I truly learned what it means to rise above the cycle of death. For Silirtas I gave it up. If she is to wander, I too will wander. If she is to be restless, I will try to give her rest. And if she is wrathful, I will seek to give her peace.

'Baugliltir has done its work. I will leave it before Utug-bûr in memory of Silirtas, and of Cruel Dancer, and of Sigileth.'Sigileth says nothing further.'For now the Thardúth are defeated, but we must not lower our guard. I must learn more about these servants of Morgoth if we are to counter them, and I must learn it quickly. The Dominion of Zíkar passed beyond living memory, but record of its terrible rule in that distant age may remain hidden elsewhere in Shagâna. I will seek for this lost history, and if it is to be found anywhere I will find it. It seems I am to be Inkanûsh again! It has been many years since that name was used of me, ${PLAYER}.

'But first I should speak with the rulers of Umbar. I understand one of their number remained behind in the city? The Lady Hármelak, you say? Go there and tell her a Wizard has advice and guidance to share, if she has the wisdom to heed it.''But first I should speak with the rulers of Umbar. I understand one of their number remained behind in the city? The Lady Hármelak, you say? Go there and tell her a Wizard has advice and guidance to share, if she has the wisdom to heed it.''I received word as you approached the city, ${PLAYER}. I understand you faced a great evil on behalf of the Kindred of the Coins, and for that you have my thanks, and those of all Umbari. I look forward to a more detailed account of your adventure. Ask anything of the Kindred and it will be yours, if it is in our power to grant.'

You tell Hármelak that without Belondor's arrival at Utug-bûr with his contingent of Umbari soldiers, the battle might have been lost. All you ask is the continued aid and support of Umbar Baharbêl against the servants of Morgoth that now threaten Middle-earth.

'My city will be honoured to assist in this fight, ${PLAYER}. Is there aught else?'

You tell Hármelak that the Wizard Inkanûsh will call upon the Kindred sometime in the days ahead, and he will have advice and guidance to provide. The Golden Queen stares at you thoughtfully.

'I see. Speak to the guard outside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja and tell him to expect this visitor. When the Wizard arrives he is to be allowed before us without delay.''Speak to the guard outside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja and tell him to expect this visitor. When the Wizard arrives he is to be allowed before us without delay.''I am sorry, did you say I am to allow a Wizard inside the Meeting-hall? Imagine that! A real, live Wizard! I will of course allow him to pass.

'Did he give you any indication of when he will arrive, ${PLAYER}? Are Wizards known to be punctual?'

You assure Jaith that Inkanûsh the Wizard will arrive whenever he arrives, and not a moment sooner or later. He thanks you, a puzzled expression on his face, and says in that case he will wait for as long as he must.

<rgb=#00FF00>The story continues in future updates!</rgb>Part of your mission has been completed... but half of it remains, for Thorongil is still a prisoner of the Kindred of the Coins.Corudan is at the amphitheatre outside the city.Talk to Corudan at the amphitheatreDaxamat is at the amphitheatre outside the city.Talk to Daxamat at the amphitheatreThe Hungry Deep Tavern is located up a set of stairs in the Upper Ward, from where it overlooks the Sail-haven.Find the Hungry Deep Tavern that overlooks the Sail-havenPatrons of the Hungry Deep Tavern may have information of use to you.The Whelk and Cockle Tavern is located to the west of the Hungry Deep, in the Sail-haven.Find the Whelk and Cockle Tavern to the west, in the Sail-havenThe Whelk and Cockle Tavern is not as popular as the last one you visited, but patrons here may still know something.Talk to patrons of the Whelk and Cockle Tavern (${NUMBER}/2)The folk here seem to be studiously avoiding your gaze.What has caused this sudden stillness?Rothog the Storm is at the Whelk and Cockle Tavern in the Sail-haven.Talk to Rothog the Storm outside the Whelk and Cockle Tavern'I and my brother will stand with you to rescue Thorongil, ${PLAYER}. Will we not, Corudan? Or have you had your fill of this place already?''We will stand with them, will we not, Corudan?''I will stand with you, ${PLAYER}, not only because of your rescue, and not only because of the adventures we shared in the Kingdom of Rohan. I will stand with you because it is right.

'I was not mistreated by the Kindred of the Coins, despite Nakási's words and her frequent promises to exact revenge upon any who aid Gondor. At sea I was given permission to walk freely about the ship, and I followed her commands quickly and well. Did my captivity chafe? I cannot say it did not, but I felt it was in service of a noble purpose, and that was enough. Tatháta suggested I be allowed to walk freely about the ships and the harbour when we docked, but the Wave-reaper feared my servility was an act, and commanded her kinswoman to keep me tied to the mast while Nakási was away. I cannot tell you with complete certainty if she was right, ${PLAYER}.

'But I can tell you this. The Kindred of the Coins value their captives only for so long as they retain value. Had I proven a burden or unmanageable, I think I would not have survived the voyage to Umbar. It may be the same with the young heir of Pinnath Gelin. If he loses his value as a hostage, I believe he will not long survive in the Kindred's company.

'Nakási brought him to Azagath Sea-shadow in the Citadel of Winds, from where the Kindred rule this city. I will help you as I can... but how do you intend to enter the most well-guarded district of Umbar Baharbêl?''How do you intend to enter the most well-guarded district of Umbar Baharbêl?''When my brother and I returned to Umbar, his plan had been for us to visit some of the taverns and inns of the city so we could learn what had happened while we were gone, recruit a larger crew, and Jajax could acquire a more suitable ship. That plan ran into some trouble! But I think at its heart it was still a good idea, because at taverns people speak more freely. If there is any way into the Citadel of Winds, someone at one of the taverns may unknowingly speak of it.

'I hope I am not being too forward with this suggestion! But it makes sense to me, ${PLAYER}. If everyone splits up and checks different taverns in the city, we may more quickly learn of a means of rescuing Thorongil from Azagath.''If everyone splits up and checks different taverns in the city, we may more quickly learn of a means of rescuing Thorongil from Azagath.''I am worried for our rulers. I heard a rumour that the widow of Balakhôr the Scourge has offered bounties on the leaders of the Kindred of the Coins, and even now her trained killers are loose in the city! They say no one has seen Tumúldo Fierce-water in weeks, and even Tatháta the Beast is missing. She often walked the streets of the Lower Ward, her fighting-hounds by her side, but I cannot remember the last time she did.

'We owe the Kindred much for dealing with the Heirs of Castamir and releasing us from the shackles of Ordâkh. I hope they are all right.''The Hungry Deep Tavern is the finest in the city, if you ask me. Every time I come here I eat too much.

'The Kindred of the Coins? They mean well, and if you ask me they have done right by us, but I think they keep secrets from us all the same. Seven of them there are, you know that, but there has been turnover among their ranks since the uprising. Some say Guild-master Imilka was in league with the Heirs of Castamir and Tatháta was right to replace her in the Kindred... but I don't know. And there was another, a scholar whose name I misremember... whatever happened to him? He ruled in the Tor-gardens, but now he is gone and that half-troll, Rothog, has his place in the Kindred! He frightens me.

'Oh, when I eat too much I also speak too much! Favour me and tell no one of these things I have said.''I am not one for gossip, friend, but I have heard a thing or two about the Kindred of the Coins that you might find interesting! For the right price, that is....'

As you consider how you might get this man to tell you what he knows without having to part with too much coin, his eyes suddenly widen.

'I... I have to be going. I know nothing! Nothing!''You. ${PLAYER}.'

Rothog stares down at you impassively, his face a mask.

'Hármelak wants to see you,' he says.Rothog the Storm has brought you to speak with Hármelak, the Golden Queen.Hármelak is in the main hall of her manor.Talk to Hármelak in her manorHármelak asked you to wait for her in the courtyard of her manor.Find the courtyard in Hármelak's manorHármelak asked you to wait for her in the courtyard of her manor.Wait for Hármelak in the courtyard of her manorHármelak has arrived in the courtyard.Talk to Hármelak by the courtyardHármelak asked you to wait for her in the garden to the north of the courtyard.Find the garden of Hármelak's manorHármelak asked you to wait for her in the garden to the north.Wait for Hármelak in the garden to the northHármelak has arrived in the garden of her manor.Talk to Hármelak in the garden of her manorHármelak asked you to wait for her in the office to the north.Find the office to the north in Hármelak's manorHármelak asked you to wait for her in the office to the north.Wait for Hármelak in the office to the northHármelak has arrived in her office.Talk to Hármelak in the office of her manor'I take you to see her,' Rothog continues. Some of the people nearby stare at you and the half-troll member of the Kindred of the Coins, many of them backing away or preparing to run if his attention turns to them.

'Now, ${PLAYER}. Hármelak does not like to be kept waiting.''Thank you, Rothog.'

Hármelak studies you silently for a moment. When she speaks again, her lips turn upwards in a faint smile.

'Welcome to Umbar Baharbêl, ${PLAYER}. I could have invited the others of your group, but I prefer to speak to you alone about this matter. You understand. It is not often that I have the opportunity to speak to such as you. It is the matter of our hostage that brings you here, I understand?'

You keep your expression guarded, but Hármelak's smile becomes more pronounced.

'Khôltekh prides himself upon knowing everything that transpires in this city, but he does not share everything with us. I have eyes of my own, and they see a great deal at a variety of interesting places: outside the gaol in the Fleet-fast, by the home of Belondor and Mêshka in my very own Upper Ward, among the alleys of the Sail-haven. Your group has not remained hidden to my eyes, ${PLAYER}, and I know why you have come to my city.

'Let us continue our discussion by the fountain of my courtyard.''Let us continue our discussion by the fountain in my courtyard.''You stand in the presence of one of the wealthiest people in all of Umbar, ${PLAYER}, but know this: wealth does not grant complete power. How many of my rivals had riches but lacked the will to maintain their control? Now their wealth is mine. The folk of the city whisper that my fortune rivals that of many dwarf-lords, but their whispers are untrue: my fortune exceeds that of even the dwarf-kings! I am the matron of the most powerful merchant clan in Umbar, and we trade in all manner of expensive goods from across the Haradwaith: spices, jewels, silks, artwork, even relics of ancient days.

'I was widowed when I was young, and my husband's brothers sought to gain control of the business. They failed. It takes an iron will to prosper as I have. Did I not witness Mût Tadaul, the Death of Light, when I was but a young girl? I knew the evils of the Church of the All Seeing from a young age, but I saw too the value of maintaining the appearance of cooperation. In public I allied with the Ordâkhai occupiers and then with the Heirs of Castamir, but always I worked secretly against them. Quietly I set up a network of those opposed to the interlopers endangering my city. It brought me into contact with some who shared my goal of making Umbar Baharbêl a Free City: Azagath and Belondor, Khôltekh of the Temámir, the smugglers Tumúldo and Nakási.

'When Balakhôr perished in the war and the power of the Heirs of Castamir was broken, I knew the time had come to act. We will speak of it further in the garden to the north.''When Balakhôr perished in the war and the power of the Heirs of Castamir was broken, I knew the time had come to act. We will speak of it further in the garden.''The Kindred of the Coins was my idea, ${PLAYER}, inspired by the Corsair councils that once governed Umbar between the reigns of tyrants. A King or a Queen or a Steward can begin with the best of intentions, but they can be corrupted. They can make the wrong choice, but a group can guard against such wrong choices. A group can see all the sides of a problem. In a city such as Umbar Baharbêl, only a council can lead effectively for the good of all. That was my idea, ${PLAYER}: a council of leaders that would not be subservient to any outside force; not Gondor, not the Empire of Ordâkh, not the Dark Lord Sauron or his servants.

'During the rule of the Heirs of Castamir and the Church of the All Seeing, I asked Khôltekh to fashion seven medallions in the form of large coins that could be worn as necklaces. Each was made of a different material, signifying our disparate backgrounds and influences, but each had something in common. Khôltekh embedded a shard of Abâshal, the valuable sea-opal treasured by his people, into each coin.

'Only days after Balakhôr perished in Gondor, a tremendous quake rocked the city. The temple of Sauron crumbled and fell into the earth, and I knew it was the time to act. I sent the medallions fashioned by Khôltekh to my chief allies, and the uprising began. The Sea-stone Mutiny would spell the end of outside authority over Umbar: that of Ordâkh, that of Gondor, that of Sauron. The Kindred of the Coins would make Umbar Baharbêl a Free City again.

'Let us speak further in my office.''Let us speak further in my office, to the north.''Azagath is a Black Númenórean, a blood-ally of Balakhôr during the Scourge's rise to power. Their friendship soured when Balakhôr sought to claim rulership over all of Umbar in the name of the Heirs of Castamir. Azagath opposed his bid, a duel was fought, and Azagath suffered defeat; he received a scar upon his face and pled for mercy. It was granted, and he was banished. We did not see him for many years, and during that time Azagath's warnings were proven true. I called for his aid during the Sea-stone Mutiny, and when he returned to the city he did so as a beloved hero. "Hadôru," they called him, "He Who Returned."

'There was no celebration for me, ${PLAYER}. I never fled the city, so I had no need to return a hero! I was here always: steadfast! Unyielding! The Kindred was my idea, but when the seven of us cast our lots for who would lead us, it was Azagath who prevailed. He is charming, any can see that, and the others believed he could more easily inspire the Umbari people than I could. I have been patient through all these troubles. Is that not inspiring?'

Hármelak stares at you with eyes of ice, and despite her words you can tell that being passed over for leadership of the Kindred bothers her more than she is willing to say.

'Do not misunderstand me. I would be content for any to lead us if he or she held dear the interests of the city, ${PLAYER}. But I think it is not so for Azagath. He has some plan of his own, a secret he keeps from the rest of us: The Song of Waves and Wind. I know not what it is, but it cannot be in the interest of my city.'Hármelak has given you some information that may help you get past the guards into the Citadel of Winds.Rothog the Storm has escorted you from Hármelak's manor. He clearly believes his task is finished.Talk to Rothog the Storm outside Hármelak's manorHármelak told you that every day Azagath's servants can be found on the docks by the Harbour Warden's tower, filling bowls with water to carry to the Citadel of Winds.Find Azagath's water-carriers on a dock near the Harbour Warden's towerAzagath's water-bearers are on a dock by the Harbour Warden's tower.Talk to water-bearer Enât on the dockAzagath's water-bearers are on a dock by the Harbour Warden's tower.Talk to water-bearer Ushu on the dockThe water-bearers will only allow you to accompany them into the Citadel if you can prove up to their task.Pick up the bowl of water on the dockAzagath's water-bearers are on the dock beside the Harbour Warden's tower.Talk to Ushu on the dock beside the Harbour Warden's tower'I would not seek to supplant any member of the Kindred of the Coins, for ours is a necessary council and Umbar is made better by our efforts. But this too is its strength: the Kindred itself is more important than any individual member. Were Azagath to fall during your rescue of the Gondorian hostage, that would be a most unfortunate turn, but the Kindred itself would survive... and that is everything.'

Hármelak holds your gaze but lowers her voice.

'Azagath returned to the city in the grip of a curious superstition. Every morning he instructs some among his servants to carry empty bowls down to the docks beside the Harbour Warden's tower. They fill these bowls with saltwater from the bay. Once this is done, they then carry these bowls of saltwater through the streets of the city until they reach the bridge beyond the Fleet-fast. The guards let them pass, without fail, and once inside they fill the fountains within the Citadel with the saltwater they carry. At midnight, the fountains are emptied... and at dawn the process begins again.

'No one pays attention to the servants who carry the water, ${PLAYER}, not the folk of the city, not the guards of the Fleet-fast, and not Azagath Sea-shadow. Among these servants you could gain access to the Citadel of Winds, and whatever might befall then... well, it would be for the benefit of the city, would it not?

'I trust this discussion has been as illuminating for you as it has been for me. Rothog will see you out.''Be gone now.'

Before you turn to leave, you quietly ask Rothog why he is helping you. The half-troll stares impassively down at you and says nothing.Rothog looks down at you but says nothing.'Yes?''We have our own tasks to complete, stranger, and have no time to speak with such as you.'

You apologize for interrupting, and ask if they could use any help with their task. The servant stares at you suspiciously.

'Why?' she asks.'We should not say anything.''Forgive Enât her distrust, stranger, but we have reason to be wary. Our master is very particular about the means by which we carry out his commands. We bring sea-water through the city to the fountains inside Sûr Birix, the Citadel of Winds, every morning, and every night those fountains are emptied to await our return the next morning. It is hard, heavy work, and there are fewer of us now than there used to be. Azagath has dismissed some of our fellows before now, but... no one seems to know where Sadûkh has gone.'

Enât hisses at Ushu to not say anything more, and the woman nods.

'He may be fine, and he may not. But without Sadûkh our work will be more difficult. Azagath smiles with his mouth, but his eyes are unmoved, and if we do not find some way to replace Sadûkh's burden we may be the next ones to disappear.

'Prove to me you can carry this bowl of sea-water without spilling a single drop, and perhaps you can accompany us into the Citadel. Bring it to the courtyard of the Teeming Net Tavern, to the south-east, and we will see if you are steady enough to join us.''Prove to me you can carry that bowl of sea-water without spilling a single drop, and perhaps you can accompany us into the Citadel. Bring it to the courtyard of the Teeming Net Tavern, to the south-east, and we will see if you are steady enough to join us.''You did well-enough, stranger. You may accompany us when next we perform our duties, and I will ask no questions of you. Your reasons for wanting to gain access to Sûr Birix are your own, but you must endanger none of my friends. If such treachery seems possible, I will alert the guards at once.

'Let this last warning guide your steps. Neither Enât nor I know what happened to Sadûkh, but of this I am certain: he saw something he was not supposed to see, some secret of the Sea-shadow, and now he has paid for it.

'Azagath is dangerous. We water-bearers may be beneath his notice... but it may not be possible to always remain so.'The water-bearers Enât and Ushu have agreed to allow you to accompany them into the Citadel of Winds, but they do not want to be held responsible for anything that happens afterward.Water-bearer Ushu is on the dock beside the Harbour Warden's tower, waiting to accompany you to the Citadel of Winds.Talk to water-bearer Ushu on the dock beside the Harbour Warden's tower and accompany her to the Citadel of WindsYou have rescued Thorongil from the Kindred of the Coins... with a little help.Talk to Thorongil by the Harbour Warden's towerYou have rescued Thorongil from the Kindred of the Coins... with a little help.Wait for the others to arriveNauriel is relieved to see that you have rescued her son from the Kindred of the Coins.Talk to Nauriel by the Harbour Warden's tower'We have a long way to carry these bowls of sea-water. It is our tradition to set them down for a brief rest just before crossing the bridge to Sûr Birix.

'Are you ready to begin?''Then let us begin, stranger. We will bring you with us into the Citadel.

'After that... your business is your own.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This is a Solo quest and cannot be completed with a Fellowship.</rgb>'Did you see them, ${PLAYER}? The Five Sisters! I thought my eyes were playing tricks on me, but they were real! I thought they were going to slay Azagath right then and there! It is a shame they did not, and he got away!

'Is Orgolas here? Did he sail his ship to Umbar? I want to tell him about the sea-voyage... it was very exciting, and dangerous too!'

You tell Thorongil that Orgolas remained behind in Anfalas, but someone else did sail with you aboard the Wave-hunter as part of the rescue mission.'Mother, I am fine! I am fine!'You give Nauriel a moment to embrace her son and shower him with relieved kisses. After he finally squirms away from his mother, Nauriel clasps you on the arm.

'You should have waited for the rest of us, ${PLAYER}! But I am pleased you acted quickly to rescue Thorongil when the opportunity presented itself.'Your mission to Umbar has reached its successful conclusion, but a number of unresolved questions leave the situation in the city and surrounding lands in some doubt.Jajax is by the Harbour Warden's tower.Talk to Jajax by the Harbour Warden's towerNimrodel is by the Harbour Warden's tower.Talk to Nimrodel by the Harbour Warden's towerThe successful completion of your mission to Umbar is the result of those who helped you, and many of them are now by the Harbour Warden's tower and want to speak with you.Caebar is by the Harbour Warden's tower, a long way from his home.Talk to Caebar by the Harbour Warden's tower'Our mission to Umbar is completed, ${PLAYER}, for both captives held by the Kindred of the Coins have been rescued. I will return with Thorongil to Anfalas as soon as it can be arranged. His father Carandolion will be very pleased, but not surprised, to learn that we have triumphed!

'There will still be work to be done here in Umbar. I know it.''Has it been so long that you have forgotten the most important rule of all, ${PLAYER}? Never count Jajax out of a fight, for he will return and surprise you! It is a lesson the Prince of Dol Amroth has now learned. You should have seen his face when it became apparent that only Jajax could turn defeat into victory!

'Aye, it seemed defeat was certain. He has spies in this land, a band of whisperers and movers-in-secret that go by the name "Circle of the Eagle," and when they reported to him that your ship never arrived, he despaired. A plan was conceived, a desperate plan, but it was considered the only option left to save the captive boy. The Prince could not achieve it, nor could the King. Only a Corsair would do. Only THE Corsair would do, and that means Jajax!

'It is good to be owed favours by Kings and Princes, ${PLAYER}, and I shall be well-rewarded for my part in this deception. I admit I believed the water carried by Nimrodel was in some way enchanted and would prove more effective that it turned out... but all is well now. It must be the good fortune of Jajax coming through in the end once again.

'How did my brother fare on your voyage? I hope he did not make too great a mess of things without me there to set him right!''For long years I believed I was no longer a part of the world, ${PLAYER}, and I sheltered with the Avorrim and stood at a remove from all others. The kindness of Arwen, and the good heart of Roamingstar, have taught me I was wrong. In gratitude I offered to play a role in their drama... was it very different than the role I played for so long, the role of Dorthaneth?

'I have set aside my veil of mourning, ${PLAYER}. It is good to be of help to my friends, no matter the dangers. Their kindness is like the warm sun upon my skin, and I welcome it.

'But do not mistake me! The danger of Azagath remains. I judge that he is no ordinary man; I felt Sauron's mark upon him.''I have spoken with the others, and I still do not understand one thing. If the Corsair Jajax arranged with Khôltekh to gain access to the Citadel with his prisoner... why did the Temámir not accompany Jajax inside? If he believed Jajax's prisoner was truly Queen Arwen, would he not want to be present at the Kindred's triumph?'

Gimli strokes his beard.

'Unless... unless Khôltekh knew that Jajax had not actually captured Queen Arwen. But why would he help us rescue Thorongil? Is he secretly working against Azagath's aims?'

You tell Gimli that your audience with Hármelak has shown you the Kindred of the Coins may not be as united in their purposes as it seems from the outside.

'That is why the King Under The Mountain is so important, ${PLAYER}. I will keep thinking about it. Khôltekh's place in all this makes my head hurt!''I have listened to the account of what transpired within the Citadel of Winds, ${PLAYER}, and it seems to me that Azagath Sea-shadow is no mere man. Nimrodel says she felt the mark of Sauron upon him... do you think he may be one of the Gúrzyul? If so, he has kept his nature hidden from the others of the Kindred and from the Umbari people. Nakási Wave-reaper spoke to you of the Song of Waves and Wind before she left Gondor, and I understand Hármelak mentioned it when you met with her, but she knew little more than its name.

'Can it be that the Song of Waves and Wind has as its foundation some evil of Mordor? If that is so, we cannot leave Azagath to make use of it... whatever it may be. The adventure that brought us to Umbar is over... but our work here is not finished.''I have not had time yet to rest and to speak with my brother, for we have gone from one danger to another. I look forward to hearing of Corudan's adventures and telling him of my own. Perhaps that time has come.

'It may not last, for while the Kindred of the Coins has claimed to make this a Free City, it would seem an evil has taken root within the membership of that council. I do not think your business in this land is yet finished, ${PLAYER}. Azagath remains a danger... and my heart tells me he is not the only member of the Kindred who is not as they appear.''During the sea-voyage that brought young Thorongil and me to Umbar, I did what I could to keep the boy out of trouble. It was not always an easy task, and I tell you truly that it was a thankless one... but I am relieved that both he and I survived the passage. That he has also been brought safely from the Citadel of Winds... it is a sign of your prowess and my memory of our shared adventures that I am less surprised by that!

'I look forward to hearing of my sister's adventures and to telling her my own. I still cannot believe that she is alive, but of course she was always... stubborn. She might say the same of me, but I believe Sigileth has always been more headstrong than I am. Since you have spent time with each of us, only you can tell us for certain.'

Corudan smiles.

'No, you can keep your answer. I know what it is.''My brother always has a plan or a scheme in mind, ${PLAYER}! Do you think he had this planned all along? Is this why he refused to join your crew? It must be, surely!'

You consider whether to dispel Daxamat's notion of his brother, and decide not to do so. You suspect that it would take a ship's hold full of evidence to lessen Daxamat's opinion of his brother, and even then it might not be possible.

'My brother is really something, isn't he?' Daxamat asks you, and you have no choice but to nod in agreement.'I have listened to the account of what transpired within the Citadel, ${PLAYER}, and my mind is clear. Belondor has no love for Gondor, and who could blame him for that, but he is not a monster. Whatever evil lies at the heart of the Kindred of the Coins, my old friend is not the cause of it. He would have prevented Nakási from slaying their young hostage... I am sure of it.

'Azagath and the web he has woven has my friend caught, just as I was enmeshed by the commands of the Steward Denethor. Belondor is not beyond saving. Of this I am certain.''It is done, for now, is it not? We have carried out the commands of King Elessar, though they have brought us far from our homes.'

Caebar looks out over the water, and shrugs his shoulders.

'I suppose I don't really have a home any longer, do I? My mother and my uncle Carlong would not welcome me back to Ethring, not even if they knew the part I played on this grand adventure. Especially not if they knew. It wasn't that large a part, but I'd like to think I was some help along the way.

'It is up to me to make a new home for myself now, somewhere out here in the world. I was so afraid of... I cannot say what I was afraid of, exactly... I think it was the "bigness" of the world, that sense of vast, open spaces, far beyond the land in which I grew up. It is so different here, but perhaps that is a good thing for me. I could not find a place in which to belong in the land of my birth. Maybe I will find a place here.

'Thank you for bringing me along on your voyage! I will do whatever I can to help King Elessar bring peace to Gondor, and if that causes further conflict with the Kindred of the Coins, well... I will do what I can to help. All you need do is ask, ${PLAYER}. I will follow wherever you lead!

'Just... maybe not aboard a ship next time,' he says, with laughter in his eyes.Thorongil has been rescued, but questions remain about the members of the Kindred of the Coin who fled from the Five Sisters.Nauriel stands outside the Harbour-warden's Tower.Talk to Nauriel outside the Harbour-warden's TowerNauriel stands outside the Harbour-warden's Tower, preparing to pursue the escaping members of the Kindred deeper into the Citadel of Winds.Talk to Nauriel and help her pursue the fleeing members of the KindredNauriel stands outside the Harbour-warden's Tower.Talk to Nauriel outside the Harbour-warden's TowerSigileth stands outside the Harbour-warden's Tower.Talk to Sigileth outside the Harbour-warden's Tower'The boy Thorongil has been saved, but I still see a loose thread in the tapestry of evil that brought him, and all of us, to Umbar! One look at his mother's face reveals it, ${PLAYER}. No sum of gold or silver could make me dare to cross her, but the Kindred of the Coins has done so, and she has not forgotten!

'What does Lady Nauriel plan to do next?''What does Lady Nauriel plan to do next?''Half of the job is done, my friend, but the other half remains, and its name is Justice.

'Three members of the Kindred of the Coins fled deeper into the Citadel of Winds when my son was rescued. They will not escape us. We must return to the Citadel and pursue them. Azagath, Belondor, and Nakási will pay for endangering my son.

'Speak to me when you are ready to return to that place.''These foes will not escape us. We will pursue them into their very stronghold, and no hiding-place will remain unsearched. I will thrust my sword into every closet and cabinet within which one of these villains might hide!''Azagath has not escaped my Justice... he has only delayed it. Even as the water builds in power behind the dam that blocks the river, so will the strength of my vengeance increase for every day he remains unpunished. He will regret his flight.

'But what evils might he find buried beneath the Mâkhda Khorbo?''But what evils might he find buried beneath the Mâkhda Khorbo?''The people of the city believe the Mâkhda Khorbo was destroyed in the great earthquake that accompanied Sauron's fall, ${PLAYER}.

'But you know, as I do, that such destruction is not always complete. Sometimes... something remains.'Sigileth is no stranger to the works of Sauron, and she believes that Azagath should be prevented from delving beneath the Mâkhda Khorbo for anything of that sort that may remain there.The ruins of the Mâkhda Khorbo stand to the north-west of Umbar Baharbêl. Sigileth and Corudan asked to meet up with you there.Meet up with Sigileth and Corudan beside the road east of the Mâkhda KhorboCorudan stands by the road east of the Mâkhda Khorbo, north-west of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Corudan beside the road east of the Mâkhda KhorboSigileth stands by the road east of the Mâkhda Khorbo, north-west of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Sigileth beside the road east of the Mâkhda KhorboSigileth asked you to retrieve a number of curious objects near the Mâkhda Khorbo so she may examine them.Collect rusty weapons from outside the Mâkhda Khorbo (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect finger-bones from wights near the Mâkhda Khorbo (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect gemstones from spectres or darkwaters near the Mâkhda Khorbo (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Sigileth is waiting for you to return to her east of the Mâkhda Khorbo with the strange objects for which she asked.Corudan is beside the road east of the Mâkhda Khorbo, north-west of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Corudan by his sister east of the Mâkhda KhorboCorudan has asked you to speak with a distant onlooker down the hill to the east.Talk to Rukân down the hill to the eastSigileth is beside the road east of the Mâkhda Khorbo, north-west of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Sigileth beside the road east of the Mâkhda KhorboCorudan stands by the road east of the Mâkhda Khorbo, north-west of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Corudan beside the road east of the Mâkhda Khorbo'We should travel by land to the outskirts of the temple and learn if the fleeing members of the Kindred have disturbed the ruin. It may be they seek only escape... but if evils of Sauron do remain beneath that structure, we cannot risk Azagath coming into possession of them.

'My brother and I will accompany you, ${PLAYER}. He would not say this to you directly, but privately he mourned to me that you ventured into the Citadel to rescue Thorongil without him.

'The Mâkhda Khorbo is to the north-west of the city.''The Mâkhda Khorbo is to the north-west of the city.''I had forgotten how poor my sister is at keeping secrets, ${PLAYER}. I did not begrudge you your decision to go alone into the Citadel, no matter what Sigileth says!

'I would have gone with you, had you asked. But of course you know that!''I have never seen this place before, ${PLAYER}. When Nakási and Tatháta sailed their ships back to Umbar with me and Thorongil aboard, they docked at their hidden harbour south of the city. I had only heard talk of the Mâkhda Khorbo and not seen it until today.

'The structure itself is not as damaged as I had thought it to be. It is not difficult to believe the levels below-ground may also have survived the great quake that followed Sauron's fall, and remain somewhat intact.''The structure itself is not as damaged as I had thought it to be.''When the Golden Host of Lothlórien waged the Battle of the Tower in the forest of Mirkwood, I did not know of its success until many weeks passed... and every week felt as long as an age in the darkness beneath Dol Guldur. It was only when you delved deeply into those forgotten passages that I ceased to be She Who Remains and became once again Sigileth, ${PLAYER}, and learned that the Golden Host had achieved victory.

'But there was evil beneath Dol Guldur, and even today each of my senses are attuned to it. I feel the presence of Sauron's works here at the Mâkhda Khorbo, even as I did in those dark dungeons. It is not as strong here as it was in the Room of Voices, but still my senses are troubled by the feel of it.

'Bring me some relics from the Dead that walk near the temple, for they may allow me to learn the... I do not know the precise word to describe it, but this will have to serve... they may allow me to learn the flavour of the evil that remains here. Bring me rusty weapons from the steps of the temple, gemstones from spectres and spirits, and finger-bones from the wights that lurk nearby. Why finger-bones?'

Sigileth raises one eyebrow and you are surprised to hear mirth in her voice when she responds.

'I did not think you would want to drag a bunch of wights over here for my investigation, ${PLAYER}. Just their finger-bones will do.''Bring me some relics from the Dead that walk near the temple, for they may allow me to learn the flavour of the evil that remains here. Bring me rusty weapons from the steps of the temple, gemstones from spectres and spirits, and finger-bones from the wights that lurk nearby.''Did you recover the items I requested?'

Sigileth examines the strange items you acquired from the vicinity of the Mâkhda Khorbo. After a few moments of study, she looks up at you.

'These blades are rusty from the salt of the sea breeze, and unsuitable for use. Only the Dead would wield these; no living servant of Sauron or of the Kindred would think to use such a weapon, unless there was no other choice.

'The gemstones are interesting. When such stones as these are found within the earth, they are usually quite clear, but the gems around which these spirits and spectres form are cloudy instead. See the shapes that seem to swirl within their foggy depths? They are most peculiar.

'I cannot read very much from the finger-bones you have brought to me. No, I do not need you to drag an entire wight here to me, as I threatened before! I have learned this, and it may be enough: these wights were given motion by spirits of evil. There must be a great number of such creatures still within the Mâkhda Khorbo, though Sauron himself be gone.''What is it, Corudan?''An Umbari woman has been watching us from down the hill to the east, ${PLAYER}. I do not think she intends to do us any harm, but whenever I face in her direction she quickly busies herself with some unobtrusive task.

'Perhaps you should see if she has any information for us, or at least determine that she does not mean ill?''An Umbari woman has been watching us from down the hill to the east. Perhaps you should find out what she wants?''I saw you speaking with those Elves near the ruined temple up on the hill. What purpose brought the three of you to that place? I have seen Elves of the Oasis from time to time, when I have gone to the markets of the city to sell my wares, but your companions do not seem to hail from across the desert. I judge they came from across the bay, perhaps from Gondor?'

You remain silent, and Rukân chuckles to herself.

'Worry not, stranger! I am no follower of the Church of the All-Seeing, and I steer clear from that temple, as should all goodly folk! Not long ago I witnessed Azagath Sea-shadow and some of his people disembark their ship in the cove beneath the hill, and I wondered what madness might have drawn them inside. Nothing good, I imagine. Pestilence and plague have troubled Khûtra in recent days, and something spoils the fish. After you have halted whatever evil drew you and Azagath to the Mâkhda Khorbo, you should return to me and I will tell you more about those ills.

'But for now let your companions know where Azagath and his allies debarked their ship. They possessed an evil intent... I am certain of it.''For now let your companions know where Azagath and his allies debarked their ship. They possessed an evil intent... I am certain of it.''What said our distant onlooker, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Sigileth that Azagath and his allies made use of a sheltered cove beneath the hill on which rests the Mâkhda Khorbo, and they have now gone inside the lower tunnels for some unknown purpose.

'Then that is where your road leads, is it not? Corudan and I will guard the path and ensure none think to flee this way, while you muster your allies and face Azagath beneath the temple.'

<rgb=#00FF00>If you wish to participate in the raid, this is when 'The Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo' raid takes place during the story.</rgb>'Azagath must think to acquire some evil beneath the Mâkhda Khorbo. He must be prevented from finding whatever it is he seeks, unless that be death.''My sister's thoughts dwell upon the evils that might lie within the Mâkhda Khorbo, but I need not see them to know that a corruption lies within the very heart of the Kindred of the Coins.

'But Azagath is well-loved by his people. Do the folk of Umbar Baharbêl know that he has betrayed them?'Azagath was well-loved by the people of Umbar Baharbêl, and Corudan wants to know if they have learned of his escape from the city.Corudan has asked you to speak with residents of the Upper Ward of Umbar Baharbêl about Azagath and the other leaders of the Kindred that fled with him.Corudan has asked you to speak with residents of the Lower Ward of Umbar Baharbêl about Azagath and the other leaders of the Kindred that fled with him.Corudan has asked you to speak with residents of the Lower Ward of Umbar Baharbêl about Azagath and the other leaders of the Kindred that fled with him.Gather information from residents in the Lower Ward (2/3)A man in the Lower Ward told you he saw a well-dressed man in blue and silver walking along one of the north-south piers extending out into the waters of the Sail-haven, north-west of the Lower Ward.Find the man wearing blue and silver on one of the north-south piers of the the Sail-havenBelondor stands atop one of the piers extending out into the waters of the Sail-haven.Talk to Belondor in the Sail-haven'I do not know which it will be. Sometimes word travels slowly, and on other occasions it can spread faster than wildfire. Will you return to the Upper and Lower Wards of the city and learn what people are saying about Azagath, Belondor, and Nakási?

'If tongues wag as I think they will, rumour that these leaders of the Kindred fled beneath the Mâkhda Khorbo in search of some evil will not be gladly received. At least... I hope it will not. Begin your search in the Upper Ward, for it is nearer.''Will you return to the Upper and Lower Wards of the city and learn what people are saying about Azagath, Belondor, and Nakási?''Yes? You have caught me at a good time, my friend, for I have just come into a large sum of money! Or rather, I will have just as soon as I collect upon a daring wager I made at Dahâl Huliz, the grand arena within the Tor-gardens. What a glorious day it is today!

'How do I feel about the Kindred of the Coins? Oh, I think they are doing a very good job of things. They saved us from the rule of the Empire of Ordakh and the Church of the All-Seeing, after all. Let me just say this, friend. If the Kindred was not in power, I would not feel comfortable telling you about my winnings at Dahâl Huliz, for fear that the Church would take my winnings for their own. I do not think any of the Kindred would do so... unless the Golden Queen is made jealous by my newfound wealth!'

Mokhlêsh falls silent, considering this uncomfortable new thought.

'Hmm. If you return to me when you have a moment, I might have some work for you. I cannot be too careful when such a large sum is at stake.''What do I think of the Kindred of the Coins? I think they have ruled us well and fairly since the war's ending, and neither the Heirs of Castamir nor the Empire of Ordakh could have done better. I have heard some troubling rumours that there was fighting in the Citadel not long ago, and I hope the ones responsible will soon be apprehended. May justice come swiftly for the ones responsible!

'I heard there was fell magic involved. It is said a great beast with five heads emerged from a column of water and shook the Citadel to its very foundation, but that is not the most surprising thing I have heard! I heard each of the five heads bore a woman's countenance, and the beauty of each was matched only by her ferocity.

'Yes, it is preposterous, but this is a land where surprising things can happen! I was not there, and neither were you. I believe this beast lives, unless Azagath has already slain it. If you were a resident of this city you would know he is capable of great martial feats. Return to me when you have a moment and we will see who knows the city of Umbar Baharbêl, and who does not!''What do I think of the Kindred of the Coins? This is a difficult question for me to answer, friend.

'It is true they have promised a return to peace and an increase in prosperity since they came to power and cast out the Empire of Ordakh, and it seems this will be made so. But they also promised to heed the will of the people and hear their concerns, but the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja remains shut. Where once I saw Azagath and the other leaders of the Kindred walking through the city, that has happened less and less often of late. Where are Tatháta and her mighty hounds? Where are Tumúldo and his wife Nakási? The only leader of the Kindred I see with any regularity is the dwarf Khôltekh, and I do not like the look in his eye. He is plotting something within the Shoreward Stone, you mark my words.

'But for my part I believe the Kindred has our best interests in mind. Truthfully I would feel more at ease if they reopened Dâr Laja and kept their promise!''Sometimes word travels slowly, and on other occasions it can spread faster than wildfire.''Have I heard anything of late concerning the Kindred of the Coins? I confess to you I have not paid very much attention to their dealings in recent days, for I have been much occupied with caring for my new friend Rûmina. She is asleep right now, but when she wakes she is like to cause quite a commotion, chasing either her tail or the rodents that infest the Lower Ward and the Sail-haven!

'She was the loyal companion of a Corsair captain and sailed from port to port with him, but now that he has retired from that trade he has given his ship-cat to me. She seems quite happy with her new circumstance, but she keeps me very busy!

'If you check back in with me perhaps you can help the two of us with something? That would be very good of you.''Keep your voice down, stranger! There are unfriendly ears about!

'Well, I do not know if there are, but it is better to stay on the side of caution, is it not? People say Azagath Sea-shadow was driven from the Citadel by a great beast, and only the swords of Nakási Wave-reaper and Belondor of the Fleet-fast saved his life! I have remained on guard for any sign of the sorcerers who unleashed the beast upon the Kindred, but so far I have not seen anything. They could be anywhere.

'You should come back to me when you have finished your own errands for the day. I may have learned something by then!''Sometimes word travels slowly, and on other occasions it can spread faster than wildfire.''Pssst... stranger.'

The man stares at you with piercing eyes. After a moment you realize he is speaking to you nearly without moving his mouth.

'I saw a well-dressed man in blue and silver making for one of the north-south piers extending out into the waters of the Sail-haven,' he says. 'You might go see this man.'The man says nothing further to you.Belondor says nothing.'I saw you in the Citadel, stranger. You were there. Were you also in the Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo?'

Belondor shakes his head sadly.

'The writing was there, but I did not see it until it was too late. There was cruelty in the Sea-shadow, as there was cruelty in all of us... but I believed it was needed against the evils arrayed against us. The Church of the All-Seeing, the Empire of Ordakh... these were foes against which we could show no mercy. The hatred I bore for Gondor...

'Aye, I would not show mercy to any warriors of Gondor who opposed us. But not children. The boy Thorongil was not yet a man, and Azagath would have drenched the stones of the Citadel with his blood. Nakási would have obeyed the command, and done whatever the Sea-shadow ordered. The writing was there, but even then I followed the two of them as they fled. In the Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo my crew paid for my inaction, my indecision. Azagath slew them with dark, unnatural magic, and their sacrifice meant nothing.

'Do you have a crew of your own, stranger? Do you know what it means to lose them all, and for nothing?'Belondor has asked to speak with his one-time friend and long-time foe, if it is true that Sirgon has come to Umbar Baharbêl.Sirgon stands outside the Harbour-warden's Tower, in the Sail-haven.Talk to Sirgon outside the Harbour-warden's Tower, in the Sail-havenSirgon stands outside the Harbour-warden's Tower, in the Sail-haven, waiting to go with you to meet with Belondor.Talk to Sirgon outside the Harbour-warden's Tower and go with him to meet with BelondorHármelak is inside her manor, waiting to speak with you about the Kindred of the Coins.Talk to Hármelak inside her manor'What brings you to this city, stranger? I deem you have sought me out. Why?'

You tell Belondor that you have come to Umbar in the company of his old friend Sirgon. Belondor remains silent for a long time before speaking.

'It has been many years since I named Sirgon a friend, and it must be the same with he who is the Lord of Lebennin. Was he rewarded for his blood-red service by the Steward of Gondor? Did chasing me into the rocks of Shedax Khîn win him glory and fortune? He slew friends of mine in that pursuit... did he tell you? Has he come to Umbar Baharbêl to finish the job he began those many years ago? He would be wise to steer clear from my manor, for the wrath of Mêshka my wife will be more deadly to him than all the spines of the desert, or the pointed rocks of the Shield Isles.'

You tell Belondor that Sirgon has already spoken with Mêshka, and she made her opinion of him very clear. Belondor looks at you with surprise.

'And he did not clap her in irons? He is not here to drag us back to Gondor in chains? Has the King not sent you and he here to inflict his will upon us? If not... then why has Sirgon come here?'

You consider what you might tell Belondor, but decide upon the truth: that Sirgon feels the burden of his past with Belondor and Mêshka, and he hopes to reconcile with them, if such a thing might be possible.

'If what you say is true, let him speak these words to me himself. I will await him at The Hungry Deep Tavern. Now begone. I would be alone with my thoughts, as I was before. I have friends to mourn.''Tell Sirgon to meet me at The Hungry Deep tavern. Now begone, for I have friends to mourn.''You have seen Belondor? What did he say to you, ${PLAYER}?'

You relay Belondor's message, and Sirgon thinks it over. At last he steeples his fingers in front of his face and closes his eyes. When he reopens them again, he has come to a decision.

'The Hungry Deep Tavern overlooks the Sail-haven, but it is located in the Upper Ward. Belondor's wife Mêshka commanded her guards to slay me should I return to that place.

'But I would speak again with Belondor and tell him what I have come to believe. I am not afraid of Mêshka's headsmen. I would bare my neck before them if it meant I could ease this burden from my back, ${PLAYER}. I have carried it for too long.

'Will you accompany me, my friend? Let me know when you are ready to leave.''Then it is time. To the Hungry Deep we go.''And so you stand before me once again, ${PLAYER}. I am touched to hear of the reconciliation between Belondor and the man of Gondor who has so disturbed his heart for these many years. I trust this thaw will not make him weak? Without Azagath and Nakási, the Kindred of the Coins must appear strong now, stronger than ever before.

'The people must be reassured that the Kindred is here for them, despite the betrayal of the Sea-shadow. I have come to a decision, and I would like to ask you for help in seeing it done.'Hármelak has decided that, in the wake of Azagath's betrayal, the people of Umbar Baharbêl need to be reassured that the Kindred of the Coins are working to ensure their safety and prosperity.Dâr Laja, the Meeting-hall, is located in the centre of the Upper Ward.Talk to Jaith outside Dâr Laja about opening the Meeting-hallJaith told you that Heirsworn in the Sail-haven might have absconded with the keys to Dâr Laja, and they must be recovered before the hall can safely be opened to the people.Defeat Heirsworn in the Sail-haven until you find the keys to Dâr LajaJaith told you that Heirsworn in the Sail-haven might have absconded with the keys to Dâr Laja, and they must be recovered before the hall can safely be opened to the people.Defeat Heirsworn in the Sail-haven until you find the keys to Dâr LajaYamin is located just to the north of the Grand Market in the Upper Ward of Umbar Baharbêl.Jaith guards the entrance to Dâr Laja, the Meeting-hall, in the centre of the Upper Ward.Talk to Jaith outside Dâr Laja in the centre of the Upper Ward'We will reassure the folk of Umbar Baharbêl that the peace will continue, and prosperity will be theirs. The Kindred of the Coins will hear their concerns and act upon them. The Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja must be reopened. Its doors have remained shut for too long, and the voice of the people has grown distant.'

Hármelak's eyes shimmer in the light of her richly-decorated chamber.

'When you were last here, ${PLAYER}, I spoke some words concerning the Sea-shadow. You have now seen what I then believed, and still do: he was not trustworthy, and owed his allegiance to some other. I think now that he drew his powers and his strength from forces unnatural, from He-Who-Sees-All... from Sauron. For your part in deposing him, in driving him from Umbar, you have my gratitude. I know you are not from this land, but I believe you have a giving heart, a charitable will, and an understanding mind. Will you help me set right what Azagath and Nakási have disturbed?

'If so, speak with Jaith, who guards Dâr Laja here in the Upper Ward of the city. He will tell you what must be done to reopen the Meeting-hall. It may take some time, but with this decision I hope we take the first steady steps toward a brighter future for Umbar and its people.''Speak with Jaith, who guards Dâr Laja here in the Upper Ward of the city. He will tell you what must be done to reopen the Meeting-hall. It may take some time, but with this decision I hope we take the first steady steps toward a brighter future for Umbar and its people.''Hármelak wants the Meeting-hall opened? Oh, I do not think we can do that.

'No, no, no, do not misunderstand me! I would leap at once to carry out the Lady Hármelak's command! It is just that the keys to Dâr Laja were stolen by the Heirsworn, and ever since those villains ran off with the keys, we have not been able to get inside. A new lock might be fashioned, given enough time, but it is not safe for us to proceed for as long as the Heirsworn hold onto the keys.

'If you are able to wrest the keys from them, we might be able to do something... but not until then. The Heirsworn can be found all over the Sail-haven, if you are ready for a fight. Once you retrieve the keys, if such a thing is possible, you should bring them to Yamin just north of the Grand Market to see about making a new set of keys and a better, more secure lock for the Meeting-hall. If you are able to do all that, come let me know! But I will not hold my breath until then. You understand: it is just that folk are always making big claims at sunset, and by dawn nothing has come of it.''If you are able to wrest the keys from the Heirsworn in the Sail-haven, we might be able to do something... but not until then. Once you retrieve the keys, if such a thing is possible, you should bring them to Yamin just north of the Grand Market to see about making a new set of keys and a better, more secure lock for the Meeting-hall.''If you are able to wrest the keys from the Heirsworn in the Sail-haven, we might be able to do something... but not until then. Once you retrieve the keys, if such a thing is possible, you should bring them to Yamin just north of the Grand Market to see about making a new set of keys and a better, more secure lock for the Meeting-hall.''What are these? The keys to the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja? The last I heard, these were in the hands of Heirsworn down at the Sail-haven. It seemed to me the Kindred of the Coins were willing to let them keep them. Being already in power, they were in no hurry to reopen the Meeting-hall and hear from the people again.

'But I am to fashion a new lock and keys for Dâr Laja so it can be reopened? Those are very surprising tidings, both welcome and unwelcome, if you understand me. I recently lost an apprentice, you see, but there is just as much work as ever. If you return here when you have a moment, I might have some work for you.

'But for now I shall begin on this new task. It will be some time before everything will be ready.''For now I shall begin on this new task. It will be some time before everything will be ready.''What did you say? You recovered the keys from the Heirsworn, and a new lock is being made for Dâr Laja? I confess to you I did not think you would do it! But it warms my heart to know that Lady Hármelak's wishes are being carried out. The Meeting-hall will open and the Kindred will once again hear the voice of the people!

'It will take some time for everything to be ready, but I hope when all is prepared you will return to us, my new friend. It will be a new dawn for Umbar, and let none doubt the days of sunshine that lie before us!'The Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja has been opened at the command of the Kindred of the Coins, and a new dawn rises in Umbar.The Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja in the Upper Ward of Umbar Baharbêl has now been opened to the people of the city so they may bring their concerns before the Kindred of the Coins.Enter the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja in the Upper WardDaxamat stands within the entryway of Dâr Laja.Talk to Daxamat in the entryway of Dâr LajaDaxamat told you his brother Jajax has gone to speak with the Kindred in the main hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Jajax in the main hall of Dâr LajaJajax is keeping an eye on the coffer containing suggestions for those who might join the leadership of the Kindred of the Coins, and he would like you to do the same.Examine the suggestions coffer at the foot of Hármelak's dais in Dâr LajaVigilant guards keep a watchful eye on the suggestions coffer inside Dâr Laja.Act naturally!Hármelak has called for you to speak with her in the main hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Hármelak in the main hall of Dâr Laja'Welcome to the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja, where the Kindred of the Coins hear the voice of the people and act upon their concerns! Keep your weapons sheathed, speak with a civil tongue, and there will be no cause for trouble.'

Jaith smiles broadly as he notices you for the first time.

'${PLAYER}! It is good to see you! So many folk have passed through these doors that I confess I did not recognize you immediately, but that is now rectified. The Golden Queen has proven true to her word, and I see she made her choice of helper with wisdom! I did not think Dâr Laja would ever be opened again, and it would be my lot to guard a locked door for all the years of my life. But no! Even now the sound of agreeable conversation drifts from within!

'You may pass inside, ${PLAYER}. I will not forget how you helped Lady Hármelak reopen the Meeting-hall, my friend. What a fine day this has become!''You may pass inside, ${PLAYER}. I will not forget how you helped Lady Hármelak reopen the Meeting-hall, my friend. What a fine day this has become!''It is good to see you, ${PLAYER}. My brother has gone ahead to the main hall, but I wanted to wait for you. It has been a strange morning.

'Heralds sent by the Kindred travelled through the city, announcing that Dâr Laja would once again be opened. During the rule of the Heirs or the domination of Ordakh such a thing was considered impossible, and when the Kindred came to power the folk of the city believed it to remain so. And yet here we stand, within the great Meeting-hall of Umbar Baharbêl, and the Kindred of the Coins has come to listen to the wants and needs of their people. Rothog the Storm walked not three strides from me when he came inside, but his khopeshes remained strapped to his back, and he gave no indication that he might eat anybody! That is progress, is it not?

'I cannot help but think that it was your arrival on these shores that set the city upon this course, ${PLAYER}. I thank you again, as I have before, for making me a part of your crew.

'My brother is in the main hall of Dâr Laja, to the north-east. He will be pleased to see you as well, though he may not say so. You know how he is, of course.''My brother is in the main hall of Dâr Laja, to the north-east. He will be pleased to see you as well, though he may not say so. You know how he is, of course.''This may not be easy, ${PLAYER}... but do they not say it is the hard thing that brings the greatest wealth and power?

'Folk have come from all over the city to bring their concerns before the leaders of the Kindred, but that is of no account to me. I am more interested in the guarded coffer that stands at the foot of Hármelak's dais, for that is where folk are bringing their suggestions as to what worthy persons should succeed the members of the Kindred who fled the city. The guards are vigilant, yes, but if I can put my name into the coffer many times, it may be that Jajax will become Kinsman of the Citadel, or of the Sail-haven! Azagath and Nakási are not likely to return, after all, and their districts cannot remain leaderless for long. Imagine how much wealth and power I might wield as a member of the Kindred of the Coins, ${PLAYER}!

'Take a closer look at that coffer, my good and subtle friend. Tell me if you notice any weaknesses I might exploit... for the good of the city, of course. My heart weeps that the folk of Umbar Baharbêl have been deprived of my presence for so long, and I aim to make up for those lost years!''Take a closer look at that coffer, my good and subtle friend. Tell me if you notice any weaknesses I might exploit... for the good of the city, of course. My heart weeps that the folk of Umbar Baharbêl have been deprived of my presence for so long, and I aim to make up for those lost years!''I am pleased for you to see your handiwork, ${PLAYER}. Yes, it was my resolve to open the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja, but it was you who recovered the keys from the Heirsworn and made my decision both safe and possible. I am grateful for it, and will not forget what you have done. You will come to see that we of Umbar Baharbêl have very long memories, if you have not learned it already.'

Hármelak smiles sweetly.

'Yes, ${PLAYER}, it is good that you have come, for there is a small matter about which we must speak. We have begun taking suggestions from the folk of the city to learn who they would have replace Azagath and Nakási in our ranks, but there is one other about whom we must speak. We must speak of Tatháta, ${PLAYER}. She remains Kinswoman of the Lower Ward, unless you have something to tell me that concerns her?'Although the leaders of the Kindred of the Coins possess renewed optimism for the future of their city, questions remain about their missing kinswoman Tatháta.Khôltekh will wish to speak with you in Dâr Laja.Talk to Khôltekh in the main hall of Dâr LajaKhôltekh told you to go inside the southern entrance to Khabârkhad, where his folk guard an entrance to Umbar-môkh, the caverns and passages beneath Umbar Baharbêl.Find the entrance to Umbar-môkh that Khôltekh suggestedMûluget is waiting for you to speak the phrase Khôltekh gave to you.Speak the password to MûlugetTekûl is further ahead and down the stairs, waiting for you to speak the phrase Khôltekh gave to you.Speak the password to Tekûl down the stairsAyâl is beyond the doors and down the hall to the north-west, waiting for you to speak the phrase Khôltekh gave to you.Speak the password to Ayâl through the doors and down the corridor to the north-westBirûk has been assigned the task of helping you while you are within Umbar-môkh. You will find him to the north-west in Mekab-kuphâr.Talk to Birûk in Mekab-kuphâr'Word has begun to spread among the people that Azagath fled from the city, and while the details vary, in most of these stories both Tumúldo Fierce-water and Nakási Wave-reaper followed him. You and I know the truth of that, but it is best that the people not yet know. Of Tatháta no tales tell, but she has not been seen in some time.

'Did I say "no tale?" This is not the truth complete, ${PLAYER}. Kinsman Khôltekh has spies everywhere, and this morning he related to me that some days ago one of these spies observed Tatháta engaged in combat with an Umbari woman he did not recognize. There was another who stood by, ${PLAYER}, and that other was you.'

Hármelak raises her hand to silence you as you begin speaking.

'I know the circumstances that brought you to our city... and I know too that Khôltekh delayed in relating this information to me, for reasons of his own. Learn what these reasons may be, keep me informed of them, and discover what you can of Tatháta's fate. We are allies now, ${PLAYER}, if perhaps not friends, and this matter concerns us both.''Khôltekh delayed in relating this information to me, for reasons of his own. Learn what they may be, keep me informed of them, and tell me too of Tatháta's fate. We are allies now, ${PLAYER}, if perhaps not friends, and this matter concerns us both.''I thought you might come to speak to me, friend. I have heard you were among the last to see Kinswoman Tatháta in life, and the others of my order are most interested to learn where she has gone in the days since. Did she follow Nakási and Azagath when the two of them fled? No one seems to know.

'I can see you have an interest in this question as well! If so, then my resources are yours to command. Tatháta was last seen in Umbar-môkh, the tunnels beneath the city, and it happens that my people control a guarded entrance to those passages. With my password they will welcome you, and lend you the aid you might require.

'Speak these words to the dwarves inside the southern entrance to Khabârkhad, and they will let you pass: Ijebad-nê!

'You will need to leave Dâr Laja and travel south into the Fleet-fast to reach the entrance to Umbar-môkh where my people stand sentry. Pick up Tatháta's trail and you will be aiding both the Kindred of the Coins and yourself, ${PLAYER}. After all, you were among the last to see her alive.''Speak these words to my dwarves inside the southern entrance to Khabârkhad, and they will let you pass: Ijebad-nê!

'You will need to leave Dâr Laja and travel south into the Fleet-fast to reach the entrance to Umbar-môkh where my people stand sentry. Pick up Tatháta's trail and you will be aiding both the Kindred of the Coins and yourself, ${PLAYER}. After all, you were among the last to see her alive.''Is that so? Well, I am not one to speak against Khôltekh. If he has allowed you to use this entrance, I will not risk my neck to stop you!

'There are other entrances to Umbar-môkh from the city above, but this one was given to the Temámir to guard, and we take this duty most seriously. Continue north-west to Mekab-kuphâr and you will find an able helper there. His name is Birûk, and he drew the dull stone for the day; that means it will be his obligation to provide any of the help you will require. This is the command of Khôltekh, and therefore it is a command to follow.''Yes, yes, you gave me the password already. I am satisfied.

'Continue on to Mekab-kuphâr to the north-west and you will find an able helper there. His name is Birûk, and he drew the dull stone for the day; that means it will be his duty to provide any of the help you will require. This is the command of Khôltekh, and therefore it is a command to follow.''Ah, you are newly-arrived to this guard-post of the Temámir? And at Khôltekh's command, no less?'

Birûk sighs heavily.

'I should have inspected the other stones and proven to myself they were truly shinier than the one I drew... but it cannot be helped for today. It is my duty to aid you. What brings you to Umbar-môkh?'The Temámir dwarf Birûk has been volunteered to help you discover what happened to Tatháta.Birûk told you to examine the notice board to the south-west in Mekab-kuphâr to see how you can busy yourself while he seeks information concerning Tatháta's whereabouts.Examine the notice board to the south-west in Mekab-kuphârDealing with the wights at Sektakhâb, located to the west of Mekab-kuphâr, may encourage the Temámir to help you.Defeat wights at Sektakhâb (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat decayed arms at Sektakhâb (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect old jewellery from the fingers of wights at Sektakhâb (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find the axe that was misplaced somewhere at SektakhâbThe notice board directed you to leave the things you recovered from Sektakhâb in the box back at Mekab-kuphâr.Because the axe does not fit inside the collection box, you should just lean it against the wall by the notice board.Corudan and Sigileth have arrived at Mekab-kuphâr.Talk to Corudan at Mekab-kuphâr'You seek to learn where Tatháta the Sea-beast has gone? I have not seen her for quite some time. Are you certain she remains somewhere within Umbar-môkh? She is the Kinswoman of the Lower Ward, and you will usually see her striding through the streets, her two great hounds by her side.'

You tell Birûk that Tatháta was last seen within a hidden harbour beneath the city, and none have marked her passage in the days since.

'Well, I will ask around. But I should warn you. One thing is true beneath the city as it is in the streets above: there is a price for every question answered. If the Temámir of Mekab-kuphâr are to help you locate your missing woman, you should do what you can to help them in return: consult the notice board across the way to the south-west to see what we need done, and perhaps you will accomplish some of those tasks for us while I ask your questions for you. It is only fair, is it not? In Umbar-môkh it is very important for things to be fair.''I will ask around. But I should warn you. One thing is true beneath the city as it is in the streets above: there is a price for every question answered. If the Temámir of Mekab-kuphâr are to help you locate your missing woman, you should do what you can to help them in return: consult the notice board to the south-west to see what we need done, and perhaps you will accomplish some of those tasks for us while I ask your questions for you. It is only fair, is it not? In Umbar-môkh it is very important for things to be fair.'<rgb=#FFFF00>The Dead That Walk</rgb>
The bodies of the slain and interred remain not at rest, but are animated by spirits of evil and must be made still. Many can be found at Sektakhâb to the west.

<rgb=#FFFF00>Arms Loosed From Their Bodies</rgb>
Of great danger to the unwary, grasping arms crawl along by their fingertips and are not to be underestimated. Look for them among the crypts at Sektakhâb to the west.

<rgb=#FFFF00>My Misplaced Axe</rgb>
I lost my axe at Sektakhâb! Its edge has been dulled due to much use, but it is still of great sentimental value to me and I would have it returned. Leave it in the collection box if you bring it back, because I have business outside the city for several weeks and will not be back for some time.

<rgb=#FFFF00>Valuable Trinkets and Jewelleries</rgb>
It may be distasteful to some, but the valuables worn by the Dead at Sektakhâb are of no use to them, and might be of great use for the Temámir. Any concerns may be brought directly to Khôltekh, but the valuables should be left in the collection box.'You will help us with some of the tasks posted on the notice board? Very good! While you are away at Sektakhâb, I will ask around and see what there is to learn of Tatháta's whereabouts.''You should finish these tasks before we speak. It is only right.''You should finish these tasks before we speak. It is only right.''You should finish these tasks before we speak. It is only right.''You did not think we would leave you to search these caves by yourself, did you, ${PLAYER}?'Corudan and Sigileth have descended into Umbar-môkh to help you find Tatháta's trail.Birûk has returned to Mekab-kuphâr and may have learned something that will help you learn what happened to Tatháta.Talk to Birûk at Mekab-kuphârCorudan and Sigileth will want to hear what you learned. They are still at at Mekab-kuphâr, but both of the Elves are eager to depart.Talk to Corudan at Mekab-kuphârAn entrance to the hidden harbour can be found in the south-eastern corner of Dil-irmíz.Find an entrance to the hidden harbour in the south-eastern corner of Dil-irmízSigileth stands by the entrance to the hidden harbour, staring out at the docked ships.Talk to Sigileth in the hidden harbourCorudan stands by the entrance to the hidden harbour.Talk to Corudan in the hidden harbourCorudan hurries away, his feet making little sound on either wood or rock.Wait for Corudan to departDevious Corsairs and their Heirsworn Captains have taken over the hidden harbour in Nakási's absence, and you should keep them busy while Corudan searches Tatháta's ship for some clue to her whereabouts.Defeat devious Corsairs in the hidden harbour (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Heirsworn captains in the hidden harbour (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Corudan has returned to the entrance of the hidden harbour, but whether he found anything of use remains in doubt.Talk to Corudan by the entrance of the hidden harbourCorudan and Sigileth suggested that you return to Mekab-kuphâr and ask Birûk what he thinks of the circled areas on the map Corudan retrieved from Tatháta's ship.Return to Mekab-kuphâr and ask Birûk about the map Corudan recoveredCorudan and Sigileth have arrived at Mekab-kuphâr.Talk to Corudan at Mekab-kuphâr'Let us do what we can to help. If we aid the Temámir here with the tasks they have posted on that notice board...'

Corudan falls silent.

'You have already done that, I see! You are a step ahead of us, ${PLAYER}, but I am pleased to see that these past few months have not changed your way of thinking: you still desire to provide help where you can. I did not doubt it, but it is nice to see it demonstrated, all the same.

'One of the dwarves here agreed to ask his fellows about Tatháta? What has he learned?''It was a good deed, my friend, and one of the dwarves here agreed to ask his fellows about Tatháta? What has he learned?''I spoke to some of my fellows about the missing Kinswoman Tatháta, and none of them have seen her of late. Most agree that she must have possessed a home of some kind within Umbar-môkh, for she did spend time in the tunnels when not at sea, but no one knows where it might be located. This is a place of secrets, after all. Even men and women of means and power may require a hiding-place from time to time. Them most of all!

'But you said she was last seen at the harbour where her ship is docked? The entrance to that place is not far from here. Elves are known for their skill at observation, I understand? It may be your friends will pick up her trail, or notice something you have missed, if you return to this harbour and search for sign of her.''If luck is with you, your friends may pick up Tatháta's trail within the hidden harbour. The entrance is to the south, in the south-eastern corner of Dil-irmíz. How did you go there before and not know its location, if you do not mind my asking?''Yes? It is a good plan, but we must be careful. The ships belonging to Nakási and Tatháta surely remain within that hidden harbour, even if their captains do not. Still, the Temámir is correct that there may be signs within that point to Tatháta's destination. We were all rather occupied with our own situations when last we were there, ${PLAYER}, were we not?'

Corudan laughs, a sudden and surprising sound in the damp and the dark of Umbar-môkh.

'I had a very good view of your battle with Tatháta's lions, it is true, but I would have preferred to be free to help you with them... and you have told me of the dangerous climb that brought you to that harbour the first time. It is good to hear no such climb will be required today, for the main entrance lies in the south-eastern corner of Dil-irmíz!''Let us move forward with care, for the entrance to the hidden harbour lies in the south-eastern corner of Dil-irmíz!'Sigileth turns to you with a raised eyebrow. When she speaks, there is clear satisfaction in her voice.

'I believed this might happen, ${PLAYER}. Now that word of Nakási's departure has begun to spread through the city, Corsairs have come to search her ship for any treasure she might have left behind. It would appear the harbour she chose is not as hidden as she may have believed it to be.

'This will make discovering Tatháta's trail more difficult.'Sigileth surveys the harbour briefly before repeating her original assessment.

'This will make discovering Tatháta's trail more difficult,' she says.'What does my sister think? We cannot allow these Corsairs to prevent us from searching for Tatháta.

'What do you think of this suggestion, ${PLAYER}? If you keep the Corsairs occupied out here, I can slip aboard Tatháta's ship and search her cabin for some clue that may lead us to her home in Umbar-môkh, if the Temámir is right and she does possess such a place.

'I spent some time on that ship, as you know, and I should be able to search it more quickly than you or my sister. Keep the Corsairs and their Heirsworn Captains busy while I search, and I will meet you back here when I have finished!'Sigileth holds her unnamed blade at the ready, eager to keep the Corsairs busy while her brother searches Tatháta's ship.'Yes! Our time was not wasted!

'I found a map of Umbar-môkh in Tatháta's cabin, and certain locations on the map are circled in charcoal. It could be that one of these marked spots indicates a place of safety to which she may have escaped after fleeing from Cruel Dancer.

'Let us return to Mekab-kuphâr and show this map to your Temámir friend.''Yes? Let us return to Mekab-kuphâr and show this map to your Temámir friend.'Corudan listens to your report with growing unease.

'He is gone?'Your Temámir contact Birûk has gone missing from Mekab-kuphâr while you investigated the hidden harbour.Corudan suggested that you ask dwarves at Mekab-kuphâr if any of them saw where Birûk might have gone.Find someone at Mekab-kuphâr who might know what happened to BirûkGêzheg does not know where Birûk has gone, but he did notice the dwarf studying the notice board at some point in the last few hours.Examine the notice board at Mekab-kuphârCorudan will want to hear what you think might have happened to Birûk. The Elf has returned to Mekab-kuphâr with Sigileth.Talk to Corudan at Mekab-kuphârYou believe Birûk has gone to complete one of the two tasks posted on the notice board, either to combat the spiders of Nishabêt to the north, or to face the grave-robbers of Lôkhalel to the north-west. Whichever of the locations you do not search will be visited by Corudan and Sigileth.Search for Birûk at Nishabêt or LôkhalelYou located Birûk, but he needs your help!Birûk flees for safety!Wait for Birûk to escapeBirûk said he would meet you at Itekh-kên, in the northern reaches of Khabârkhad.Talk to Birûk at Itekh-kên in the northern reaches of Khabârkhad'We need to know what has happened. It may be that your Temámir friend has left this place for a perfectly good reason... but he works for Khôltekh. The Kindred of the Coins has promised a new dawn for the people of Umbar, but let us not forget that should our interests diverge from theirs they would not hesitate to do us harm. We should give him only as much trust as caution warrants... and little more than that.

'Perhaps I am overreacting. Someone here may know where the Temámir Birûk has gone.''We need to know what has happened. Someone here may know where the Temámir Birûk has gone.''Birûk? I have not seen him for a few hours. When last I noticed him he was studying the notice board. Perhaps something there caught his eye?''When last I noticed Birûk he was studying the notice board. Perhaps something there caught his eye?'<rgb=#FFFF00>Spiders of Nishabêt</rgb>
Someone should do something about the spiders that have infested the chamber to the north of Mekab-kuphâr in Khabârkhad. They are contained within Nishabêt for now, but who can say how long they will remain there?

<rgb=#FFFF00>Grave-robbers at Lôkhalel</rgb>
Brigands and grave-robbers are plundering the chambers at Lôkhalel, north-west of Mekab-kuphâr, stealing valuables that belong to us. Someone needs to tell them that this is our territory!'Yes? You say the Temámir Birûk was seen studying the notice board, and the axe you left there is now missing? He may have taken the axe and gone to complete one of the posted tasks, ${PLAYER}. If he is to help us make sense of the map of Umbar-môkh I recovered, we need to find where he went. He may have gotten into trouble.

'You should search one of the locations mentioned on the notice board, and Sigileth and I will search the other. After we have each scoured our chosen destination, let us meet at the northern camp... what is it called? Itekh-kên in Khabârkhad?

'Which location would you like to choose, ${PLAYER}? Nishabêt and its spiders to the north, or Lôkhalel and its grave-robbers to the north-west?''Very good, my friend. Which location would you like to choose? Nishabêt and its spiders to the north, or Lôkhalel and its grave-robbers to the north-west?''I did not know the spiders had come to Nishabêt in such numbers, ${PLAYER}! I thank you for your help... and now I must make good my escape! I will meet up with you at Itekh-kên to the north-west!''I thought these grave-robbers would be poorly-armed and untrained, ${PLAYER}, but they proved more deadly than I expected, and I only just escaped with my life! I am grateful for your help, and will meet up with you at Itekh-kên to the north-east!''I thought I would try my hand at the adventuring life you have chosen, ${PLAYER}, but it proved more difficult than I expected. How do you do it, day after day?

'No, it is not for me. I will remain in the service of Khôltekh, drawing stones to learn what my duties for the day will be. The shiny stones never take me from the safety of the camp, and the dull stones do so only rarely.'

You tell Birûk that Corudan has acquired a map of Umbar-môkh with markings on it that may indicate potential hiding-spots for Tatháta, but the Elf has not yet arrived.

'I will see him when I see him, I suppose. But until he gets here, I have had another idea that may help you track down your missing Kinswoman. I have not seen her, and the others I spoke with have not either... but there are more eyes in Umbar-môkh than yours or mine.'Birûk has given you a curious directive that he insists will help you track down Tatháta's location within Umbar-môkh.Birûk suggested that you defeat rats and crocodiles within Ilmabiri.Defeat rats in Ilmabiri (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat crocodiles in Ilmabiri (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Birûk told you to find a metal plate in the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri.Find the metal plate in the south-eastern corner of IlmabiriThe empty metal plate in the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri waits for the bread Birûk gave to you.Birûk told you that leaving food here should summon one of the Terâsi, the forgotten ones of Umbar-môkh. Now you must wait and see if he was right.Wait and see if you are contacted by one of the TerâsiYou hear a girl's voice from the darkness beyond the grate in the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri.Talk to the girl in the darkness beyond the grate in the south-eastern corner of IlmabiriThe Terâsi girl you encountered in Ilmabiri asked you to retrieve a meal of higher quality from the Upper Ward, as well as a bottle of fancy wine, or she will tell you nothing. The tables at the Baths of Ilmai Iforikh may have what you need.Steal an unattended meal from the Baths of Ilmai Iforikh in the Upper WardThe Terâsi girl you encountered in Ilmabiri asked you to retrieve a meal of higher quality from the Upper Ward, as well as a bottle of fancy wine, or she will tell you nothing. The tables at the Baths of Ilmai Iforikh in the Upper Ward may have what you need.Steal an unattended bottle of wine from the Baths of Ilmai Iforikh in the Upper WardHave you been seen?The empty metal plate in the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri waits for the meal and wine you "retrieved" from the Upper Ward.You must wait once again for the Terâsi girl Tephísa to return.Wait for the Terâsi girl Tephísa to returnTephísa, the Terâsi girl, has returned to the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri.Talk to Tephísa, the Terâsi girl in the darkness at the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri'We are outsiders in Umbar-môkh. We patrol these caverns and keep them as safe as we can, but we do not live here. When my day is done, I return to the Shoreward Stone. When yours is ended, I presume you seek food and shelter at one of the taverns of the city. It is not so for all who walk these tunnels.

'I speak of the unseen ones, those the Umbari call "Mâtrikhai." My people have another name for them; to us they are the "Terâsi," the forgotten ones. They creep into places no others could, and move with such speed that should you catch a glimpse of them from the corner of your eye, by the time you turned to look they have gone. They are children of Umbar, I think, and move about Umbar-môkh by secret passageways unknown to any other. It is said the Terâsi prowl the markets of the Upper Ward and the Lower when they are most crowded, stealing goods and coin whenever they can, and then slipping back into the tunnels and caverns undetected.

'Temámir hearts warm to the Terâsi, for we see their hardships and take pity upon them. It has been our custom to leave food for these forgotten ones throughout Umbar-môkh on occasion. I will give you this crusty bread so you can do this as well. There is a suitable metal plate where you can leave it in the far south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri, but you should clear the area of crocodiles and rats before you do. If one of the Terâsi claims our gift, you should ask them if they know where your missing Kinswoman has gone.''Temámir hearts warm to the Terâsi, for we see their hardships and take pity upon them. It has been our custom to leave food for these forgotten ones throughout Umbar-môkh on occasion. I will give you this crusty bread so you can do this as well. There is a suitable metal plate where you can leave it in the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri, but you should clear the area of crocodiles and rats before you do. If one of the Terâsi claims our gift, you should ask them if they know where your missing Kinswoman has gone.''There is a suitable metal plate where you can leave the bread in the south-eastern corner of Ilmabiri, but you should clear the area of crocodiles and rats before you do. If one of the Terâsi claims our gift, you should ask them if they know where your missing Kinswoman has gone.'A girl's voice drifts out of the darkness.

'When people leave food out for us, they do not wait. Maybe you did not know this.'

You respond that you were hoping to speak to someone who might know what happened to Tatháta, Kinswoman of the Lower Ward. The silence stretches for so long that it seems possible the unseen speaker has slipped away, but your patience is rewarded at last.

'I might know something, but this bread is not enough. I want a true meal, something fancy. And wine! Bring me a meal and wine from the Upper Ward, and I may tell you what I know.'

A sudden scuffling sound in the darkness tells you the girl is about to leave. You call out, asking how she will know you have fulfilled your side of the bargain.

'Tephísa will know,' she calls back, and then you are certain you are alone again.'What do you think you are doing? None can avoid my watchful gaze, and I can easily see the meal and the wine you have taken do not belong to you.'

The guard squints at you.

'${PLAYER}? Ah! You must be here on some business for the Kindred, then? Very well, do not allow me to hinder it! I must have made some mistake. Your name has become quite well-known throughout the city, and your many deeds have elevated it to quite a height! Go in peace, and fulfill your mission!'Once again, Tephísa's voice floats from the darkness.

'This is much better fare, stranger. You have my thanks.

'Now tell me again what you wished to learn.'The information you learned from Tephísa guides you to Kamrabezûr in the north-eastern reaches of Umbar-môkh.Birûk waits for you at Itekh-kên in Khabârkhad, and may be able to guide your search of Kamrabezûr for Tatháta.Talk to Birûk at Itekh-kên in KhabârkhadBirûk suggested that you go to Kên Taghlim in the north-western reaches of Kamrabezûr, but that you should steer clear of the great beasts rumoured to have escaped from the arena.Find Kên Taghlim in north-western KamrabezûrCorudan stands at Kên Taghlim in north-western Kamrabezûr.Talk to Corudan at Kên TaghlimCorudan and Sigileth have suggested a number of places for you to search throughout Kamrabezûr, and encouraged you to defeat any foes that stand in the way of your investigation.Defeat All-Seeing Eye cultists in Kamrabezûr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat sand-sifters in Kamrabezûr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Search the centre of the cultist camp of Jâx Ugikh for TathátaSearch the centre of Ghâr Mejet for TathátaSearch the centre of Harkhaphâr for TathátaCorudan stands at Kên Taghlim in north-western Kamrabezûr.Talk to Corudan at Kên Taghlim in north-western KamrabezûrSigileth stands at Kên Taghlim in north-western Kamrabezûr, prepared to go with you in search of Tatháta's hiding-place.Talk to Sigileth at Kên Taghlim and go with her in search of TathátaCorudan stands beside Galatâni's grave outside the city.Talk to Corudan by the graveYou explain your intention to learn what befell Tatháta in the days since she was last seen fleeing from the hidden harbour. Silence falls upon Ilmabiri for long moments. At last Tephísa's voice floats back to you.

'Many of us live in the tunnels, stranger: more than you might think. Our eyes see much that the Temámir do not, for we can reach places even they cannot go; perhaps they choose not to go there, but we have not that choice. All of Umbar-môkh is our domain, and while the city is big, Umbar-môkh has vastness of a different kind.

'A friend of mine witnessed Tatháta's flight. By secret paths he followed her, unseen, but he was not her only pursuer. An Umbari warrior-woman chased at her heels, deadly daggers flashing in the torch-light. He pursued them both into Kamrabezûr, but among those vaults he lost their trail. Many abandoned temples to the Church of the All-Seeing can be found in that place; it may be Tatháta gained entrance to one of those, and stays there now in hiding. Of the other, I cannot say. My friend believes Tatháta must have given her the slip as well, leaving her no choice but to return to the city above. That seems most likely to me.

'Good-bye, stranger. I thank you for the food and for the wine! I hope Tephísa has been a help to you.'

There is a brief rustling sound, and you know the girl has disappeared once again into the deeper darkness of Umbar-môkh.'Reports from Kamrabezûr indicate that at least one terrible creature prowls the vaults and corridors of that place. Something must have escaped from the arena, and until Rothog's men can retrieve it that region of Umbar-môkh cannot truly be called safe. Be careful.''You have returned! Were you able to draw the notice of any among the Terâsi?'

You tell Birûk about what you learned from Tephísa, and he chuckles quietly.

'The net is closing about your prey, ${PLAYER}. Kamrabezûr? I have already sent your Elf friends into that place! Several of the circled areas on the map they showed to me indicated locations within those vaults, and the words of the Terâsi have confirmed that Tatháta's flight brought her to that place. You should look for your friends at Kên Taghlim in the north-western reaches of Kamrabezûr.'

Birûk prepares to go, but then he turns back to you, a concerned expression on his face.

'There is one more thing, ${PLAYER}. Reports from Kamrabezûr indicate that at least one terrible creature prowls the vaults and corridors of that place. Something must have escaped from the arena, and until Rothog's men can retrieve it that region of Umbar-môkh cannot truly be called safe. Be careful.''${PLAYER}, there you are! Let us move forward with care. There are several locations within Kamrabezûr that Tatháta marked on her map with charcoal, and any of them could indicate the place where now she hides.'

You ask Corudan if he or his sister have encountered any of the beasts rumoured to have escaped from the arena, and he shakes his head.

'No, but the folk we have met with speak of little else. Whatever was being kept in the holding-pens of the arena must have been ferocious indeed to frighten them so; the sound of it roaring in the darkness is apparently enough to make your blood run cold.'

Sigileth makes no sound, but you can tell she does not believe these reports. Her brother continues.

'We may encounter this creature or creatures if we continue our search of Kamrabezûr. I just want us to be prepared. Let me mark the locations we still must investigate on your map, and encourage you to combat any of the other dangers that cross your path.''I think we should use caution. There are several locations within Kamrabezûr that Tatháta marked on her map with charcoal, and any of them could indicate the place where now she hides.''We found something, ${PLAYER}. It was not marked on Tatháta's map, but Sigileth feels certain this is the location to which she fled. Should we investigate it now?

'What says my sister to this?''This is the place. I know it.''It is done.'You have emerged from Umbar-môkh with tidings of Tatháta for the Kindred of the Coins, but your information may herald trouble on the horizon.You should leave Galatâni's dagger on the grave.Sigileth stands by the grave, deep in thought.Talk to Sigileth by the graveHármelak waits in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja to hear what you have learned about Tatháta from your time seeking her in Umbar-môkh.Return to Hármelak in the main hall of Dâr LajaHármelak waits in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja to hear what you have learned about Tatháta.Talk to Hármelak in the main hall of Dâr LajaCorudan remains silent for a long time. When he speaks again, his voice is quiet.

'My heart is conflicted, ${PLAYER}. Galatâni chose a path of cruelty and greed, and the words she spoke to Tatháta will surely cause trouble for us in the days ahead... but still her end was a sad one.'

Corudan hands you a small dagger.

'I retrieved this from the floor where it had fallen during their struggle, ${PLAYER}. It is the blade with which Galatâni cut her path through this life, and it should accompany her in death. Do you agree?''The blade with which Galatâni cut her path through this life should accompany her in death, yes?'Sigileth stands quietly, deep in thought. At last she meets your eye.

'I am moved by Galatâni's story, ${PLAYER}, for in it I see something of myself reflected. Well do I know the price of obsession, the cost of single-mindedness. This Umbari woman knew nothing of Elves; would it surprise her to recognize that part of her in some of my people, or in Sigileth of Lórien herself?

'I cannot forget this woman, and will not. She named herself "Cruel Dancer," and it was a name well-chosen, for hers was a dance with death, and such cannot last forever.'

Sigileth draws her sword.

'My blade has found its name, ${PLAYER}. It shall be "Baugliltir," that is "Cruel Dancer" in Sindarin, and it shall remind me of she who participated in the death-dance, and at last succumbed. She remains with me now.'

Sigileth sheathes Baugliltir and nods once more at the grave. Then she turns to you.

'Hármelak will want to know what we learned beneath the city. It is time to return to Dâr Laja.''Hármelak will want to know what we learned beneath the city. It is time to return to Dâr Laja.''Yes? My sister is right. We should tell Hármelak what transpired beneath the city.''You have returned safely from Umbar-môkh? Did you discover the reason for Kinswoman Tatháta's disappearance?'

You tell Hármelak what transpired in the hidden chambers within Kamrabezûr, but when you begin to describe the ferocity of the great bear, she raises a hand.

'Not here, ${PLAYER}. Few alone know this secret of Tatháta's, and the Meeting-hall is not the place to speak of it. We have a... more pressing concern to discuss now. We have opened the coffer containing the names of those suggested to join the leadership of the Kindred, and one name has appeared more often than any other.'One name was placed in the suggestions coffer inside Dâr Laja more than any other.Hármelak suggested that you speak with the other leaders of the Kindred of the Coins in the main hall of Dâr Laja about the dangers their people will face in the days ahead.Talk to Belondor in Dâr LajaTalk to Khôltekh in Dâr LajaTalk to Rothog in Dâr LajaJajax has some words for you in the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja.Talk to Jajax in Dâr LajaHármelak is in the main hall of Dâr Laja, waiting to speak with you again.Talk to Hármelak in Dâr LajaThe guardsman Jaith stands outside the Meeting-hall of Dâr Laja. Hármelak has asked that you warn him to keep watch in case Tatháta assaults the entrance in search of you or the leaders of the Kindred.Talk to Jaith outside Dâr Laja'One name was suggested more than any other to replace the missing members of the Kindred... and that name was your own, ${PLAYER}.'

Hármelak appraises you carefully.

'The folk of Umbar Baharbêl have noticed your efforts on their behalf, it seems. That is not enough, but it is more than nothing. I do not know if you are the right person to join us, ${PLAYER}. Know that there would be tests and trials to ensure you are worthy of such an honour, as there were for the rest of us. I promise you nothing.

'The city will face many challenges in the weeks ahead. Speak to the other leaders of the Kindred of the Coins here in Dâr Laja, and they may well ask for your assistance. Prove you act for Umbar, and you may receive their blessings.''Speak to the others here in Dâr Laja, and they may well ask for your assistance. Prove you act for Umbar, and you may receive their blessings.''I was surprised to hear your name read aloud so often when they opened the coffer, ${PLAYER}. The people of the city have taken notice of your efforts on their behalf. Take pride in it, but know that the responsibility can prove a heavy burden.'

Belondor sighs wearily.

'We will have many demands upon our time in the weeks ahead. How will we decide what can be done, and what must be done? I hope you will help me sort our way through these issues.''You grow more impressive by the day, ${PLAYER}! Must I keep an eye upon you from now on?'

Khôltekh chuckles to himself, apparently amused by a secret joke.

'Such power must be wielded with care, my friend, or you risk losing it. Many others have learned this lesson too late; I encourage you to learn it early.''Your name grows in worth, ${PLAYER}. If the people of the city continue to speak of you with such praise, I may have to order you to the arena.'

Rothog smiles: an unpleasant sight.

'Invite. I may have to invite you to the arena.''I am amused, honoured, and a little upset with you, ${PLAYER}! You should have told me you planned to plant your name many times in the suggestions coffer! I do not know how you accomplished this feat, but I am impressed you were able to do it. Still, it was my idea, and I should be the one who joins the Kindred of the Coins, not you! Imagine the power I might wield as part of that august body!

'I am better-suited for the post than you are, and we both know it. Well. We will see if they allow you to join at all. I suspect they will not, for as much as the people of the city seem to like you, I have seen the way each member of the Kindred looks at you, and there is distrust behind every pair of eyes. Mark my words, ${PLAYER}: they may speak friendly words, but in their hearts they cannot see you truly as a friend. There is too much bloodshed between Umbar and Gondor for that, no matter what Sirgon or the others may believe.

'Jajax has always seen and spoken the truth. Doubt him at your peril!''I am better-suited for the post than you are, and we both know it!''You have done well for us today, ${PLAYER}; of that there can be no doubt, but let us see how you fare in the weeks ahead before we think overmuch of the future.

'But Tatháta, it seems, may have reason to wish you ill, and your continued work alongside the Kindred means she may wish to do us harm as well... and that cannot be permitted. Go now to the guard outside Dâr Laja and tell him that she is not to be allowed to pass.'

Hármelak carefully adjusts her bracelets.

'Few know of Tatháta's true strength, for she has kept it hidden from all but her dearest friends. Let us keep that knowledge to ourselves for now, ${PLAYER}, unless she gives us a reason not to do so. Yes, I count myself among Tatháta's friends. Does that surprise you?

'But warn Jaith, outside the Meeting-hall, that Tatháta is not to be underestimated. The events of the past few days may have given her reason to suspect even her friends of betrayal, and if she tries to attack the Meeting-hall it will be his duty to stand before her.''Few know of Tatháta's true strength, for she has kept it hidden from all but her dearest friends. Let us keep that knowledge to ourselves for now, ${PLAYER}, unless she gives us a reason not to do so.

'But warn Jaith, outside the Meeting-hall, that Tatháta is not to be underestimated.''I should prevent Kinswoman Tatháta from entering Dâr Laja? Has she thrown in with the traitors, then? I cannot believe it!

'I will do what I can if she makes a threatening appearance, but I must warn you that she has always had the fiercest countenance and possesses great strength to match! If she wants to get inside, there may be little we guardsmen could do to prevent it! But still we will do our best.'

As you bid farewell to Jaith, you fear he is right: if Tatháta demands entrance, there are few in Umbar Baharbêl who could stand against her.Zildug has asked you to check his frog traps and bring him what you find.The frog traps can be found in the marshes of the delta west of Iridír.Collect frogs from the traps (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected all the frogs from the traps. You should bring them to Zildug in Iridír.'Where do you hail from, ${PLAYER}? Is it far? I am a bit of a traveller myself. I was not born in Iridír. I am from Eshadûr, all the way across the river. It was once a busy fishing village, but now the Ordâkhai have taken it for themselves and told me I must go elsewhere.

'But to tell you the truth, fishing from the north side of the river is not so different. There are just as many frogs here as there were in Eshadûr. Ambarûli love their frog legs! I catch them using traps. It is much easier than chasing them around with a net, like a fool!

'Speaking of which, would you mind checking my frog traps? Why yes, I am trying to get you to do more of my work. Am I succeeding? They are in the marshes of the delta to the west. Go ahead. It will be fun.''Would you mind checking my frog traps? They are in the marshes of the delta west of Iridír. Go ahead. It will be fun.''Six frogs is not bad! That is twelve legs, after all. Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You are very kind.'Turizi has asked if you wouldn't mind visiting a neighbouring papyrus farm.Turizi would like you to check on the welfare of Sûlmur at her papyrus farm in Ambarûl, east of Ekagar.Talk to Sûlmur'Thank you for your help, ${PLAYER}. I dislike asking for aid, but I am glad that I did. If your travels happen to be taking you east, I would ask one last thing of you. There is a papyrus farm just east, along the river's edge.'

The man's face turns beet red.

'There is a young lady there, Sûlmur, who does some rather dangerous work. The papyrus bogs are rife with crocodiles and zizani. I think you would call them Neekers? It would bring me comfort if you could visit and see that she fares well. Thank you, friend. And good luck on your travels.''There is a young lady, Sûlmur, who works at the papyrus farm to the east. The papyrus bogs are rife with crocodiles and zizani. It would bring me comfort if you could visit and see that she fares well.''Oh, Hashtal-gûr, stranger. Turizi sent you to see if I am well?'

She blushes.

'I am as well as can be expected, I suppose. How is Turizi?'

You tell her that he is well. Perhaps a bit stubborn, but not too stubborn to ask someone for help when he needs it. The woman laughs.

'That sounds like a welcome improvement!'A new delicacy developed by an aspiring chef in Umbar increased the demand for crab claws. Tevurin directs you west to the beach to collect ungues that he can send to the city from the encampment.Head south and east along the river shore and hunt the crabs there for their claws.Collect crab claws (${NUMBER}/12)After collecting several claws to meet Tevurin's quota you need to return them to him at the hunter's expedition encampment in southeastern Cape of Umbar.'A new chef in Umbar created a dish enjoyed immensely by the Kindred of the Coins. This chef's success drives demand for crab claws to a pace that cannot be met. I am tasked with sending hunters to acquire the claws to help meet the quotas put upon the Cook's Guild. Are you willing to assist?''This is exactly what I required, friend. I will ensure the claws are delivered to my patron and they will know your involvement. After all, you should be rewarded with more than I can offer for your continued efforts.''Find crabs on the shore south and east of the camp. Collect their claws and return them to me here at the encampment.'Kishâtu has asked for your help gathering various plants she needs from the area.Kishâtu has asked you to gather various plants nearby in Urash Dâr.Collect berries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect red wild-flowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect yellow wild-flowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Kishâtu with the plants you have collected for her.'Greetings ${CLASS}.

'If you are willing, I could use your help as we are running low on some of our supplies here.

'I am in need of some red and yellow wild-flowers, as well as some berries. You can find the wild-flowers nearby, but you may find berries along the river's edge.''Ah, you have returned, and it appears with the plants that I requested.'

You hand the plants to Kishâtu.

'Thank you, ${CLASS}. These will be put to good use'Sûlmur has offered to show you how to make papyrus. First, you must harvest some reeds.Papyrus reeds can be found in the marsh near Mehêtur in Ambarûl.Collect papyrus reeds (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected papyrus reeds. You should return to Sûlmur near the barn at Mehêtur.'Would you like to learn how papyrus is made? I have not seen the wood pulp paper used in the West, but I have heard about it. They say the surface is very smooth, but it tears easily and is not as long-lasting. Papyrus sheets are strong and durable. We also have more reeds than trees.'

She laughs and you get the impression that she truly enjoys her work.

'The first thing you need to make papyrus sheets is papyrus the plant. Go into the marsh to the south-west and collect some reeds. Look for reeds with good, strong stalks. Bring them back here when you are done, and do not forget to watch for crocodiles!''Go into the marsh to the south-west and collect some reeds. Look for reeds with good, strong stalks. Bring them back here when you are done, and do not forget to watch for crocodiles!''Yes, those look perfect! I will take them. Good work.'Sigênik, a craftsman in Western Khanâg, has told you his plan to reuse the metal from lost shields in Sûr Zugub. He would also like you to slay sand-sifters while you are there.Sigênik has asked you to travel to Sûr Zugub, south-east of Western Khanâg, and collect any abandoned metal shields you find. He would also like you to slay the resident sand-sifters while you're there.Collect metal shields (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat sand-sifters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected metal shields and defeated sand-sifters. You should return to Western Khanâg to give the shields to Sigênik.'If you are willing to search for the swords, perhaps you would be willing to go to Sûr Zugub as well? It is the home of sand-sifters; nasty things. I have never been there myself, but it is said that an entire Ordâkhai troop was lost there. Travellers claim to have seen the metal of their shields glinting in the sun, but none have dared enter the valley, lest they meet the same fate as the soldiers.

'If you can handle the sand-sifters, collecting those shields should recover quite a bit of metal I can work with. And speaking honestly, the more of those sand-sifters you can slay while you're there, the better. Sûr Zugub is to the south-east of here.'Sigênik's eyes widen at the sight of the metal shields you have brought him.

'${PLAYER}, these are wonderful! And you defeated many sand-sifters as well? Truly, you a a gift to Khûd Zagin. You have my thanks.'You have proven yourself by defeating the cult of the Anâkhi at Ashunûg; pushing back the darkness within Nirgambâr, the Restless Tomb; investigating Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting; and surviving what lay inside the Temple of Utug-bûr. None can question your tenacity and determination, and by completing all these deeds, you have earned great favour throughout the Ikorbân Valley.Complete the deeds of the Ikorbân Valley instances.Complete 'Nâkhugir's Bane'Complete 'Vanquisher of Nirgambâr, the Restless Tomb'Complete 'Vanquisher of Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting'Complete 'Deeds of the 'Temple of Utug-bûr''Phâredar asks that you help her boss, Keteph, to maintain the supply of needed crab delicacies. With enough crap meat, Phâredar can continue delivering his signature dishes to the people of Jax Phanâl while he trains in the City of Umbar.Phâredar continues to collect the crab parts needed to make the recipes made famous by Keteph. You will need to return to the shoreline to west and south of Jax Phanâl. Collect the meat, shells, and claws from the crabs there and return them to Phâredar.Collect intact crab claws (${NUMBER}/8)Collect crab shells (${NUMBER}/8)Collect delectable crab meat (${NUMBER}/8)Phâredar continues to collect the crab parts needed to make the recipes made famous by Keteph. You will need to return to the shoreline to west and south of Jax Phanâl. Collect the meat, shells, and claws from the crabs there and return them to Phâredar.'Keteph's list remains the same. If you could visit the shoreline and remove the meat, shells, and claws from the crabs there we would be very appreciative. What say you?''I see that the cooks here are well-prepared to deliver on Keteph's recipes thanks to you.''You will find crabs on the shoreline.'Defeat many crebain in Khûd Zagin.Defeat many crebain in Khûd Zagin.Defeat many crebain in Khûd ZaginDefeat a great many crebain in Khûd Zagin.Defeat many crebain in Khûd Zagin.Defeat a great many crebain in Khûd Zagin.Defeat a great many crebain in Khûd ZaginBâdarul fears that Ekhamâti soldiers have escaped the fighting and made their way into the area. They are now looting and causing chaos and must be stopped.Bâdarul fears that Ekhamâti soldiers have escaped the fighting and made their way into the area. They are now looting and causing chaos and must be stopped.Stop looters (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Bâdarul in Iridír and inform him that you have successfully dealt with the looters causing trouble in the area.Return to Bâdarul in Iridír'Greetings, ${CLASS}. I am glad you are here. I am in need of your help.

'There have been reports of looting and sightings of citizens acting suspiciously in the area. I believe these "citizens" are Ekhamâti soldiers in disguise here to cause chaos. See if you can identify these individuals and put a stop to them before they cause any more damage.''You have returned. Were you able to find any of these troublemakers?'

You inform Bâdarul of the looters you defeated.

'So it was Ekhamâti soldiers after all. I am glad you have dealt with them and given us one less issue to manage here.'Defeat many criminals in Dil-irmíz.Defeat many criminals in Dil-irmíz.Defeat many criminals in Dil-irmízDefeat a great many criminals in Dil-irmíz.Defeat many criminals in Dil-irmíz.Defeat a great many criminals in Dil-irmíz.Defeat a great many criminals in Dil-irmízEshadûr, a former fishing village, has become an Ordâkhai encampment. It is on the south side of the Ikorbân River, across from Iridír.Dalagín, the Dumûri captain, has asked you to enter Eshadûr and disrupt the Ordâkhai operations there.Enter the village of EshadûrDalagín, the Dumûri captain, has asked you to enter Eshadûr and destroy their boats, defeat Ordâkhai soldiers, and scout a few locations.Defeat Ordâkhai in Eshadûr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy Ordâkhai boats (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Scout the western store-houseScout the eastern officers' tentScout the southern stablesYou have completed your mission in Eshadûr and should return to Dalagín in Iridír.Talk to Dalagín in IridírYou have completed your mission there and reported back to Dalagín, the Dumûri captain, in Iridír. She has asked you to go to Girhâzi and tell her of the waggons laden with black powder.Talk to Girhâzi'I have heard you are a warrior, capable of more than just running errands. Would you be interested in something more dangerous? Someone with a spy-glass was watching birds along the river and spotted boats being prepared at the docks in Eshadûr.

'I do not have enough Dumûri to send a force over, but I believe that a warrior of your skill would be able to do enough damage to ruin whatever it is that they are planning. If you think you are up to it, go to Eshadûr and destroy those boats. While you are there, you might as well thin their numbers and scout around the village to learn what you can of their activities.''Go to Eshadûr and destroy the Ordâkhai boats. While there, thin their numbers and scout around the village to learn what you can of their activities.''Excellent work my friend! I could hear the explosions and their shouts of panic from here. We were cheering you on.'

You tell the Dumûri Captain that while scouting you discovered that the Ordâkhai are loading waggons with black powder, and she looks concerned.

'I am glad you saw this, but it is not good news. Please return to Girhâzi and tell her of this. She will want to hear this from the one who witnessed them.''You return! How goes the river defence?'

As you did with the captain, you tell Girhâzi of the waggons. Girhâzi thinks for a moment and seems to be talking to herself as much as to you.

'If those waggons are meant for us, and I have little doubt that they are, there can only be one target.'The demand for crocodile hides and meat persists. Tevurin dispatched you back to the beach east of the expedition camp to collect enough of the hides and meat to meet the demand of the citizens in Umbar.Demand remains high for the trappings obtained by hunting crocodiles. Go to the shore east of the expedition camp, hunt the crocodiles for their hides and meat.Collect crocodile hides (${NUMBER}/12)Collect crocodile meat (${NUMBER}/12)You collected meat and hides from the crocodiles along the shore. Return them to Tevurin at the hunter's expedition camp in southeastern Cape of Umbar.'The demand for crocodile meat and hides remain high, friend. The latest requests want enough hides and meat to sustain my patron for a week. Go east to the shore, hunt the crocodiles for the meat and hides, and return to me when you've finished your collection.''I do not think the demand for the hides and meat of the crocodiles will ever diminish. I only see the demand growing as more citizens realize the value of the hide. The meat is an acquired taste. It seems that more and more are acquiring it daily.

'Thank you for your continued effort, ${PLAYER}.''Head to the east along the shore and hunt the crocodiles there for their hides and their meat. Return both to me here at the camp when you are done.'Defeat many crocodiles in Ilmabiri.Defeat many crocodiles in Ilmabiri.Defeat many crocodiles in IlmabiriDefeat a great many crocodiles in Ilmabiri.Defeat many crocodiles in Ilmabiri.Defeat a great many crocodiles in Ilmabiri.Defeat a great many crocodiles in IlmabiriDefeat many crocodiles and avanc in Khûd Zagin.Defeat many crocodiles and sand-prowlers in Khûd Zagin.Defeat many crocodiles and sand-prowlers in Khûd ZaginDefeat a great many crocodiles and sand-prowlers in Khûd Zagin.Defeat many crocodiles and sand-prowlers in Khûd Zagin.Defeat a great many crocodiles and sand-prowlers in Khûd Zagin.Defeat a great many crocodiles and sand-prowlers in Khûd ZaginMizâdi requests your aid in investigating a strange and disturbing report she has received from her scouts in the south of Imhûlar.Speak with Surâsh at the site of the missing scouts. The site can be found south-east of Urmâkh, near Zahûmakh.Speak with Surâsh in southern ImhûlarScout the area around Zahûmakh to the south-east where the scouts were last seen.Scout the area south-east near Zahûmakh for signs of the scoutsExamine the scouts for clues of the cause of their untimely end.Examine the first scoutExamine the second scoutExamine the third scoutExamine the area around the scouts for more clues as to their demise, and their assailants.Find the first clue as to the scouts' deathFind the second clue as to the scouts' deathFind a third clue as to the scouts' deathExamine the strange banner near the scouts.Examine the strange banner nearbyCollect the strange banner.Collect the strange bannerSpeak with Surâsh in southern Imhûlar.Speak with Surâsh in southern ImhûlarBring the clues and banner to Mizâdi in Urmâkh.'Surâsh, one of my scouts, has returned to me with a harrowing report from southern Imhûlar. The rest of her scouting party has gone missing, and haven't reconvened in a fortnight. She fears the worst. I am duty bound to stay here and defend Urmâkh from Okhíso and his Barkhûshim in Kûr Anzar, but I wish to ease her fears and deliver a resolution to these disappearances.

'Would you investigate these rumours with Surâsh? She can be found near the swamps of Zahûmakh, in southern Imhûlar. I will mark the location on your map. I would consider it a personal favour, ${PLAYER}.''Mizâdi said she was sending her best. That is you, I assume? I am heartened. I fear that we will find some danger out here. This is where we all were supposed to meet. I dared not investigate further south, for fear of not coming back myself. I am not worth much in a fight, I'm afraid.

'Would you explore to the south-east near Zahûmakh, the marsh, and see what you find?''They are dead? I dreaded this moment, but it is still worse than I had feared. They were good soldiers, and better people. I will mourn them. We will double up our patrols for now. There is something dangerous about.

'The banner? No, I have never seen something like that before. You should report this straight away to Mizâdi.''Yes, this is the outcome I feared to be true. Still, I appreciate the news, grim as it is.

'Thank you for bringing these to me. I fear there is some new group of fiends harassing our southern border.

'This banner? I have not seen it before. Strange. It is quite similar to my own banner. The lion... I wonder. No matter. If they are attacking my scouts, they are no friend of ours. We have quite enough to worry about with Okhíso in Kûr Anzar, but I will think on this more. Thank you, friend.'Defeat many crypt-raiders in Khabârkhad.Defeat many crypt-raiders in Khabârkhad.Defeat many crypt-raiders in KhabârkhadDefeat a great many crypt-raiders in Khabârkhad.Defeat many crypt-raiders in Khabârkhad.Defeat a great many crypt-raiders in KhabârkhadDefeat a great many crypt-raiders in KhabârkhadDangerous crocodiles infest Ilmabiri, the sewers beneath the Sail-haven of Umbar Baharbêl.Reducing the number of crocodiles in Ilmabiri should help prevent more incidents of them causing havoc in the Sail-haven.Defeat Ilmabiri crocodiles (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have reduced the number of crocodiles in the sewers. You should tell Ahûd at the guard post in the northern part of the Ilmabiri.Talk to AhûdDangerous crocodiles infest Ilmabiri, the sewers beneath the Sail-haven of Umbar Baharbêl. You should reduce their numbers.'You are very bold, ${PLAYER}! Thank you for culling the crocodiles. That should help us indeed!'Defeat many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmíz.Defeat many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmíz.Defeat many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmízDefeat a great many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmíz.Defeat many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmíz.Defeat a great many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmíz.Defeat a great many Umshûran cultists in Dil-irmízDefeat many cultists in Kamrabezûr.Defeat many cultists in Kamrabezûr.Defeat many cultists in KamrabezûrDefeat a great many cultists in Kamrabezûr.Defeat many cultists in Kamrabezûr.Defeat a great many cultists in Kamrabezûr.Defeat a great many cultists in KamrabezûrDefeat all of the Gladiators within Dahâl HulizDefeat all of the Gladiators within Dahâl HulizDefeat Horthion within Dahâl HulizDefeat Nakrov the Scarred within Dahâl HulizDefeat Akhmâr Redstorm within Dahâl HulizDefeat Shaidal within Dahâl HulizDefeat Ulanor within Dahâl HulizDefeat Bhastah within Dahâl HulizDefeat Shakfut within Dahâl HulizDefeat Rothog the StormThe gladiatorial pits of Umbar Baharbêl serve several important purposes: entertaining the masses, keeping their attention focused, and meting justice upon those who break the laws of the city.

Upon taking control of Umbar, the Kindred of the Coins altered the traditional rules of the arena. Most battles are no longer to the death, and the memory of such capital punishment has remained in the cruel past. Combat has become a sport for the masses, and any who dare can enter the fighting pits and attempt to enhance their glory. Gladiators bound to the arena serve their sentences by entering battle and fighting to the end.Rothog awaits you in the Arena.Talk to Rothog the Storm in the ArenaEzaphir will issue your first challenge when you are ready to compete.Talk to Ezaphir and defeat the first challengeEzaphir congratulates you on your success, but he warns you the following challenges may not be so easy.Talk to Ezaphir and defeat the second challengeEzaphir congratulates you on your victory, but there are more difficult challenges to come.Talk to Ezaphir and defeat the third challengeEzaphir will call forth one of the more stalwart gladiators to test your mettle in glorious combat.Talk to Ezaphir and defeat the fourth challengeEzaphir informs you that you are about to face your greatest challenge in the arena: Rothog the Storm himself.Talk to Ezaphir and defeat Rothog the Storm'I welcome you to my domain: Dahâl Huliz, the grand arena of Umbar Baharbêl! I consider these sands to be sacred ground; you should do the same. In this place, those who are accused fight for their freedom, and those who are not fight for greater glory.

'On this day we fight not to the death, for those who perish would be unable to entertain the crowds on the morrow. Instead we fight until a combatant's first submission. In that way those who lose can be spared, that they may gain in strength, and fight again on a future day.

'Be warned, for the Arena plays host to many surprises. May you fight well! Give all those who attend a magnificent show and you will have their approval, as I do!''We are honoured by your presence here today! I shall call forth the first set of the Khâbraghai, the blade-fodder, so you may warm up your muscles and get your blood flowing!

'The crowd wants to cheer for you, but on some days they can be fickle-minded. They want to see a good show. See that they do.''Any who have sat in the stands of Dahâl Huliz know the names of the favoured champions who do battle on the fighting sands. You will face one of these now! Use your wit, guile, and strength to survive, and perhaps your own name will be raised as high!

'Now will I call them forth!''Well-struck, and well done. Every victory adds to the story of your name.

'But do not rest yet, for now I call forth the next round of Khâbraghai. Will you best these as you did the others? Do not let down your guard! The crowd will prepare themselves for the more challenging contest to come, but you must focus upon each battle as it is joined, and not look past them to the next!''You must forget those you have fought before. Your next foes will be far more dangerous, and if you cannot best them the story of your name will end here!

'But perhaps it is not to be so? Can it be we will see your true mettle here instead? I call forth your next challenge, and may you prove its equal!''Your greatest challenge has arrived, my impressive friend. Will you face it with courage and with strength, or will its arrival send you screaming from these sands in fear?

'I believe in you, though all the crowd cheers for your opponent. They do not think you have what it will take to best this final opponent, and why should they? You must win them over. You must attain their belief with a great display of your prowess and your fighting skill!

'Your final challenge is this: you must defeat he who calls this place home. Rothog the Storm is your final opponent, my friend, and he is a mighty foe indeed. Can you take this crown from him? Can you defeat the Arena's favourite son and champion? We will see, friend. Show us now, and win glory for your name!

'Fight now! Dahâl Huliz will ring with the sound of battle, and let the cheering of the crowd ascend even to the stars above!'The gladiatorial pits of Umbar Baharbêl serve several important purposes: entertaining the masses, keeping their attention focused, and meting justice upon those who break the laws of the city.

