fits into the fissure within the delving stone. The aquamarine cracks and the darkness within creeps out of the stone and moves into the air as a black tendril. A flash of dread fills you, but passes.'What vileness was within that gemstone, ${PLAYER}? You return having bested the foes you faced, but I can see from your countenance that the battle there took a toll. We have more of the gemstones and more to reward you and those like you willing to risk life and limb to defeat the great evil in the world. Will you fight on? I shall let you make that choice.'Galamê explains Lord Igbâl wants a craban to train against the Ordâkhai. He has seen them use the creatures as spies and would do the same. He asks you to acquire the most impressive beast you can capture.Galamê told you to collect the cage beside him before you head into the wild to collect a sand-sifter corpse to coax the largest craban, Kûruna, in Khûd Zagin.Retrieve the gilded cage from beside GalamêYou retrieved the gilded cage. Galamê mentioned collecting the carcass of a sand-sifter to coax the craban, Kûruna, from the sky. You will find sand-sifters roaming the waste-plains of Khûd Zagin to the south.Defeat a sand-sifter in Khûd Zagin and collect its corpseFind a location to place the sand-sifter corpse to draw down the largest craban, Kûruna, from where the crebain circle in the sky south-east of Jirush.Kûruna is defeated and docile. Talk to the creature and draw it into the cage.You captured Kûruna. You should return the bird in the cage to Galamê at Jirush.'My Lord Igbâl does not speak the common tongue. He will not converse with you in his tongue, so all communication comes through me.

'You are here to ingratiate yourself to my lord. There are three tasks he bids you.

'Task one is to go into the desert and collect Kûruna, the largest of the craban in Khûd Zagin. The crebain are used by the Ordâkhai to spy upon their enemies. My lord would do the same to them.

'First take the cage beside me. Then, collect a carcass of a sand-sifter in the south. Find a spot near where the crebain circle far to the south-east and place the carcass where it can be seen. This will draw down Kûruna. When you've captured Kûruna return it here, to me.''To draw the largest craban, Kûruna, from the skies, you will need a meal the crebain desire. Collect a corpse from the sand-sifters in the waste-plains to the south. Then bring it to the crebain circling south-east of Jirush.''Place the sand-sifter's corpse near the where the crebain circle in the sky. The largest among them will smell the corpse and approach to eat the sand-sifter. Defeat the craban, Kûruna, then and capture it in the cage.'The craban moves to attack.'Your cage is still empty. Have you beaten Kûruna into submission, yet? Hurry now, see the craban collected.'Kûruna reluctantly steps into the cage.'My Lord Igbâl offers you thanks for your service.'

The man places the cage near the merchant.

'Speak with me when you are prepared for your next effort.'Galamê confesses that Lord Igbâl will pay the Ordâkhai to keep them away. He does so, however, using the Ordâkhai's own wealth. He asks you, a person not known to the Ordâkhai, to help collect their wealth.Galamê directed you to hunt the Ordâkhai warriors in their camps to the north-west, south-east, or their patrols throughout Khûd Zagin to acquire the wealth the outpost of Jirush needs to pay tithes to keep the Ordâkhai attacks at bay.Collect Ordâkhai money pouches from an Ordâkhai warrior (${NUMBER}/12)Collect Ordâkhai swords from melee Ordâkhai warriors (${NUMBER}/10)Collect Ordâkhai medallions from Ordâkhai jackal-keepers (${NUMBER}/4)Collect an Ordâkhai insignia from an Ordâkhai captainDefeat Ordâkhai warriors across Khûd Zagin (${NUMBER}/20)You collected all of the materials Galamê requested from the Ordâkhai warriors. Deliver them to the man at Jirush in central Khûd Zagin.'My Lord Igbâl wants you to show your strength and to test your mettle. Of course, there is another reason for this task. You see, friend, we are in a tenuous position. The Ordâkhai control all wealth in Khûd Zagin. Then, they come to Jirush and demand tithes.

'Igbâl pays them, but his coffers are low, and the people of the outpost have nothing to give. We cannot risk an attack against them directly. You, however, are an unknown to them. You can assault them, gather what we need, and prepare us for the future.

'Steal money pouches, acquire their swords, collect their leaders' medallions, and insignia. Any Ordâkhai will do. Assail their base to the north or the south-east or find the unsuspecting on patrol in the wild.''My Lord Igbâl pays the Ordâkhai demands with their own wealth, you see. Our numbers are thin and known to the Ordâkhai. If you are willing to help you will find what we need from their camps in the north-west, south-east, and among their patrols across Khûd Zagin.''Lord Igbâl and Nangar's faith in you was not misplaced. You have done well, ${PLAYER}. We believe your intention in our land is not nefarious.

'You may be the unexpected ally we have long awaited. Lord Igbâl and I are pleased that you came to Jirush as a friend and not an enemy. We have enough enemies already.'

He offers a weak smile.

'Go now, back to Nangar, you have earned Igbâl's respect.'Lord Igbâl desires a new protector to defend him and his family. Galamê provided information on how to capture a powerful jackal, Zukhâma, for Lord Igbâl.Galamê explained that Lord Igbâl would like a new jackal pet. He sent you to collect a lizard corpse to feed Zukhâma, draw it into the open, and capture it for Lord Igbâl.Collect a lizard corpse from the quarry west of JirushAfter collecting the lizard corpse, you are prepared to draw the jackal matron, Zukhâma, from her den. Make your way to the jackal den west of Jirush.Zukhâma, the jackal matron, bows her head in defeat. You should take the opportunity to place a thong around the beast and bring her to Galamê at Jirush.Interact with Zukhâma to place a thong about its neckReturn to Galamê at Jirush with the captured jackal matron, Zukhâma. He will see you rewarded for acquiring a new guardian for Lord Igbâl.Deliver Zukhâma to Galamê at Jirush'My Lord Igbâl issues his second challenge to claim a new guardian. In the west, not far from Jirush, is a pack of jackals making their den in the crags. A leader among them, Zukhâma, watches over the pack with great ferocity.

'Lord Igbâl bids you claim a lizard carcass from the quarry in the west. Bring that carcass to the opening of the jackal's den and draw forth Zukhâma.

'Defeat the jackal, leash it with this strap of leather, and return it to me.''You will find lizards in the quarry to the west of Jirush. Collect one of their corpses and place it outside the den where the jackals make their home. This will draw Zukhâma forward. Defeat her, tether her, and deliver her to me here.''If you have a lizard corpse, place it outside of the jackal den and summon the jackal matron. After you defeat her, leash her and bring her to me.'The jackal snarls and leaps to attack.'Have you beaten the jackal, Zukhâma? You will only earn Lord Igbâl's favour if you complete this task. Go and collect the jackal and return her to me.'Zukhâma, beaten into submission, allows you to place a thong about her neck.'My Lord Igbâl is pleased with your efforts. He believes this jackal will serve well as his guardian. There is one last test for you to complete. He begins to think you can be trusted.'Ashâmi and Raphûz ask for your assistance in meeting the request from Numûru. They will be assaulting a camp to provide a distraction for the main force assailing Kûr Anzar.Ashâmi and Raphûz are prepared to make their assault against the Ordâkhai camp to distract them from the main force attack Kûr Anzar. They asked you to accompany and help them.Complete the instance: Together NowYou successfully engaged the enemy at Dûl Zidur and were issued a dire warning by the cultists there. Ashâmi and Raphûz visited Numûru upon their return and want to tell you what transpired.Speak with Ashâmi at her home in UrmâkhSpeak with Raphûz at his home in UrmâkhAshâmi and Raphûz asked you to make your way to Numûru and explain you also heard the warning from the cultist. You can find him at his post in Urmâkh.Speak with Numûru at his post at UrmâkhNumûru was quick to dismiss the possible threat for the existential threat he can see. You should return to Ashâmi and Raphûz at their home in Urmâkh to inform them of your meeting.'I spoke with Raphûz. He is enamoured by Mizâdi's words, but he is not given to the superstitions of the Shagâni.

'I think it is his pragmatic view on Shagâna that endears me to him.

'We have discussed and will go to the encampment and prove our value to Numûru, and Mizâdi's cause, together.''We are prepared, ${PLAYER}. We will meet you in the heart of the cultist's encampment.''We presented Numûru with the words the cultist spoke. He is unconvinced it is anything more than a hollow threat. Please, speak to him. Convince him this is more than superstition.''Numûru is pleased with our performance, but the information we provided concerns him greatly. We need you to speak to him as well.'Numûru listens to your report.

'I've heard the expedition out of Umbar seeks to quash a threat from among their leaders. It could be this threat the cultist speaks of, no?

'I cannot take this report to Mizâdi without more proof.

'Ashâmi and Raphûz proved their worth in their trials. They are welcome in our legion.

'I cannot chase after the words of an Ordâkhai cultist without more proof.'Shêsha has adopted a new friend, but her new friend also has old friends.The Baths of Kadax are located to the north-east in the Lower Ward of Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Yosarin at the Baths of Kadax in the Lower WardShêsha is to the south-west of the Baths of Kadax, in the Lower Ward.Talk to ShêshaAphila is near the Fisherfold in the Sail-haven.The bug parts requested by Aphila can be found from arid sand-flies outside Umbar Baharbêl.Collect bug parts from arid sand-flies outside the city (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Aphila is located near the Fisherfold in the Sail-haven, waiting for you to return with the bug parts you collected.Shêsha is to the south, in the Lower Ward.'You have returned to Rûmina and me! Oh... perhaps you should keep your distance. You see, it turns out my new friend Rûmina also has some old friends who have come along for the ride. Fleas, my friend, fleas!

'Could you go to the Baths of Kadax to the north-east and ask Yosarin, the Keeper of the Baths, if I might allow her to douse herself in the water? That might cause her troublesome friends to leave us alone.''Could you go to the Baths of Kadax to the north-east and ask Yosarin, the Keeper of the Baths, if I might allow her to douse herself in the water? That might cause her troublesome friends to leave us alone.''All are welcome at the Baths of Kadax, of course. What did you say were the names of your friends? Shêsha and Rûmina?'

Yosarin's eyes narrow.

'Rûmina? Not the same Rûmina that sailed with the Corsair Jeduth? She was a fine black ship-cat, as I remember, but she always had a problem with fleas, as I recall. Jeduth has now retired from that life... has Rûmina chosen a life on land as well?'

You are forced to admit that she is thinking of the same Rûmina.

'I cannot allow fleas inside the Baths of Kadax! What are you trying to do to our reputation here? Go back to your friend and tell her to keep Rûmina well away from our waters!''I cannot allow fleas inside the Baths of Kadax! What are you trying to do to our reputation here? Go back to your friend and tell her to keep Rûmina well away from our waters!''You mentioned the fleas? Why did you do that? I thought we had an understanding!

'Ah, well, I need to do something before Rûmina's fleas spread throughout the whole city! There is a merchant of unguents near the Fisherfold in the Sail-haven named Aphila. Take this bag of coins and see if she has a remedy of some sort that will cure Rûmina of her tiny, biting friends.''I need to do something before Rûmina's fleas spread throughout the whole city! There is a merchant of unguents near the Fisherfold in the Sail-haven named Aphila. See if she has a remedy of some sort that will cure Rûmina of her tiny, biting friends.''I do make a remedy for fleas, but this bag of coins is not enough to cover the cost. I will give it to you if you collect some ingredients for me. Are you willing to do this?

'I require an array of parts from the arid sand-flies that gather outside the city, by the rocks that block passage further to the east. They often harry travellers to Umbar Baharbêl, so collecting parts from them will aid both me and those who venture to our fair city.''I require an array of parts from the arid sand-flies that gather outside the city, by the rocks that block passage further to the east. They often harry travellers to Umbar Baharbêl, so collecting parts from them will aid both me and those who venture to our fair city.''You have taken care of the arid sand-flies and brought me what remains of them? Yes, this collection will do; combined with the coins you brought, this will cover the cost of the remedy you sought.

'Bring this remedy back to your friends and their flea-bites will become only an itchy memory.''Bring that remedy back to your friends and their flea-bites will become only an itchy memory.''You have a remedy for our fleas? Did you hear that, Rûmina? Our new friend has performed a great service for us!'

Rûmina sleeps on, unconcerned, and Shêsha gives her a contented scratch.

'You have her thanks and mine, friend! Now there will be at least one part of the Lower Ward that is not a home for fleas!'The spiders north of Mekab-kuphâr in Nishabêt have seized a party of Temámir Dwarves.The spiders north of Mekab-kuphâr in Nishabêt have seized a party of Temámir Dwarves. Rescue them before they are devoured!Freed spider victims (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Temámir Guard Captain Nûru in the Temámir outpost in southern Khabârkhad.Return to Temámir Guard Captain Nûru'We have urgent need of one such as yourself. A party of our dwarves was attacked by the monstrous spiders that dwell north of here. They made off with some of my best guardsmen.

'If I could leave my post I'd be there in a flash and those spiders would taste only the iron of my blade. But I have sworn to Khôltekh that I would stay and provide safe haven and shelter to those who need it here.

'Please, north of here is a dwelling of large and dangerous spiders. They have my dwarves, and we have but hours before they wrap and devour them, the monsters.

'Will you aid us in our time of need?''Oh praise be. I thought this a grim day, but it's turned into a joyful one thanks to you and your bravery.

'All my guards have returned with their skin, hopefully a bit more wary of the dangers here.'Khôltekh has asked that you deploy traps along the northern walls of Khabârkhad in the Umbar-môkh.Khôltekh has asked that you deploy traps along the northern walls of Khabârkhad in the Umbar-môkh. He has directed you to speak with Nûru, who can be found in Mekab-kuphâr, a guard outpost in southern Khabârkhad.Talk to Temámir Guard Captain Nûru in Mekab-kuphârNûru told you to go far to the north, outside Khabârkhad, to Tilmakhím in Tâkhdar. There, a vendor named Mâramit will give you the traps Khôltekh asked you to place.Talk to the vendor Mâramit in TilmakhímGo south of Tilmakhím, to the northern edge of Khabârkhad, near Lôkhalel, and deploy the traps Mâramit gave you.Deploy traps near Lôkhalel on the northern edge of Khabârkhad (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Khôltekh in Dâr Laja, the Hall of the Kindred.Return to Khôltekh in the Hall of the Kindred'Greetings. We have much to do for Umbar and little time in which to do it. The Umbar-môkh is becoming quite a problem. Vast swaths of it have been ceded to ruffians, criminals, wild beasts and worse.

'My Temámir guards do what they can in the crypts to bring law and order, but the situation is tenuous at best.

'Now I am hearing from Nûru, the captain of my guard, that Khabârkhad is overflowing with rats. These rats carry disease and ill fortune to many of the unfortunate residents of Umbar-môkh. Will you talk with Nûru? He will have the details on these traps. These vermin are coming from the north, I am sure of it. Please, place these traps along the northern edge of Khabârkhad so that our people can breathe easy again.''Khôltekh sent you? Honestly, we've got bigger problems than rats, but if Khôltekh said it, best to do it and fast. There's a purveyor of dangerous supplies in Tilmakhím, in Tâkhdar. It's far to the north of here, outside Khabârkhad. The merchant's name is Mâramit, she'll set you right. Best tell her Khôltekh sent you, you'll get the best price. Right, best not to dally. On your way, then!''That is most excellent news. What's that, you met Mâramit?'

Khôltekh roars with laughter. You tell Khôltekh about Mâramit's message.

'She is quite the handful. Thank you for the message. I'll take that under consideration, if I am dwarf enough to face her so soon.'

You tell Khôltekh that the traps seem quite large for rats. Here he grows more serious.

'Rats come in all shapes and sizes, I'm afraid. Still, I appreciate your efforts, braving quite a dangerous area in service to me.''Hello, there. Nice face you have. What? Oh, Khôltekh sent you, did he? Well there's a sweetheart if ever I saw one.

'What do you need then. Traps? Why I'm the Purveyor of Traps, that's what they call me. For rats? Big ones, I'm sure, knowing Khôltekh. Alright then.

'Here's eight of them. Should be enough for what he needs. Keep your coin. Just tell Khôltekh to come visit more often.

'Alright, off with you. You look far too respectable. You're scaring away all my proper degenerate customers.'Find the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Ambarûl in the Ikorbân Valley.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout Ambarûl in the Ikorbân Valley.Find treasure caches in Ambarûl in the Ikorbân ValleyFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Imhûlar in the Ikorbân Valley.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout Imhûlar in the Ikorbân Valley.Find treasure caches in Imhûlar in the Ikorbân ValleyFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Khûd Zagin in the Ikorbân Valley.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout Khûd Zagin in the Ikorbân Valley.Find treasure caches in Khûd Zagin in the Ikorbân ValleyFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Mûr Ghala.Find the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Mûr Ghala.Complete Treasure-seeker of AdagímComplete Treasure-seeker of KighânComplete Treasure-seeker of An ShêruComplete Treasure-seeker of IdagâlFind many treasures hidden or forgotten near Ekhamâti outposts and camps in the Ikorbân Valley.Find many treasure caches lost or forgotten left by the Ekhamâti in the Ikorbân Valley.Find treasure caches left by the Ekhamâti in the Ikorbân ValleyFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout the Ikorbân Valley.Find the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout the Ikorbân Valley.Complete Treasure-seeker of AmbarûlComplete Treasure-seeker of Khûd ZaginComplete Treasure-seeker of ImhûlarComplete Treasure-seeker of Urash DârFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout the Umbar-môkh.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout the Umbar-môkh.Find treasure caches in the Umbar-môkhFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Urash Dâr in the Ikorbân Valley.Find the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Urash Dâr in the Ikorbân Valley.Find treasure caches in Urash Dâr in the Ikorbân ValleyThe Quartermaster has asked that you gather your allies and travel to The Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo and The Temple of Utug-bûr to put an end to the evils there.The evils within The Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo and The Temple of Utug-bûr must be stopped.

Complete one raid on any tier.Complete an Umbar or Ikorbân raid on any tierReturn to the Temple of Utug-bûr Quartermaster with proof of your victory.Return to the Temple of Utug-bûr Quartermaster'Greetings, ${CLASS}.

'An evil plagues the lands of Umbar and Ikorbân and we cannot allow it to spread.

'I ask that you gather your allies and travel to The Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo and The Temple of Utug-bûr and defeat the evils who lurk in their halls. Do this, and I can provide you with a reward to match your efforts.''I have heard of your deeds in The Depths of Mâkhda Khorbo and The Temple of Utug-bûr.

'Please accept this as a token of my thanks to you and your companions.'Defeat Ordâkhai within Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting.Defeat Ordâkhai within Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting.Defeat Ordâkhai within Tûl Zakana, the Well of ForgettingDeep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Explore the marketplace of the Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting.Defeat the Pahór-korat, the Hands of ForgettingBahádring's path leads you deeper into Tûl Zakana. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into the Well of Forgetting.Defeat Mûkh Bahora, the Mouth of ForgettingBahádring has run off again. You must be close to the Well itself. Can it really make you forget everything?Defeat Bahádring the UnmindfulDeep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 1'Deep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Explore the marketplace of the Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting.Defeat the Pahór-korat, the Hands of ForgettingBahádring's path leads you deeper into Tûl Zakana. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into the Well of Forgetting.Defeat Mûkh Bahora, the Mouth of ForgettingBahádring has run off again. You must be close to the Well itself. Can it really make you forget everything?Defeat Bahádring the UnmindfulDeep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Explore the marketplace of the Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting.Defeat the Pahór-korat, the Hands of ForgettingBahádring's path leads you deeper into Tûl Zakana. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into the Well of Forgetting.Defeat Mûkh Bahora, the Mouth of ForgettingBahádring has run off again. You must be close to the Well itself. Can it really make you forget everything?Defeat Bahádring the UnmindfulDeep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 2'Deep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Explore the marketplace of the Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting.Defeat the Pahór-korat, the Hands of ForgettingBahádring's path leads you deeper into Tûl Zakana. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into the Well of Forgetting.Defeat Mûkh Bahora, the Mouth of ForgettingBahádring has run off again. You must be close to the Well itself. Can it really make you forget everything?Defeat Bahádring the UnmindfulDeep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 3'Deep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 3'You experienced all that Umbar has to offer. You survived the treacherous Shield Isles, the shoreline of the Cape of Umbar, and even the city of Umbar Baharbêl. You are the consummate traveller, explorer, and purveyor of all things in this foreign land.You experienced all that Umbar has to offer. You survived the treacherous Shield Isles, the shoreline of the Cape of Umbar, and even the city of Umbar Baharbêl. You are the consummate traveller, explorer, and purveyor of all things in this foreign land.Complete: Deeds of the Shield IslesComplete: Deeds of the Cape of UmbarComplete: Deeds of Umbar BaharbêlSomeone has posted a task to collect brine-touched claws. These can be turned in for a reward.You have accepted a task to acquire brine-touched claws.Wanted: brine-touched claws from Umbar or the outer reaches of Gondor.Wanted: brine-touched claws from Umbar. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.Wanted: brine-touched claws from Gondor. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Someone has posted a task to collect brine-touched ears. These can be turned in for a reward.You have accepted a task to acquire brine-touched ears.Wanted: brine-touched ears from Umbar or the outer reaches of Gondor.Wanted: brine-touched ears from Umbar. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.Wanted: brine-touched ears from Gondor. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Someone has posted a task to collect corroded cutlass sheaths. These can be turned in for a reward.You have accepted a task to acquire corroded cutlass sheaths.Wanted: corroded cutlass sheaths from Umbar or the outer reaches of Gondor.Wanted: corroded cutlass sheaths from Umbar. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.Wanted: corroded cutlass sheaths from Gondor. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Someone has posted a task to collect salty goo. This can be turned in for a reward.You have accepted a task to acquire salty goo.Wanted: salty goo from Umbar or the outer reaches of Gondor.Wanted: salty goo from Umbar. Gather it to earn a reward suitable for collectors.Wanted: salty goo from Gondor. Gather it to earn a reward suitable for collectors.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.Someone has posted a task to collect sea-tossed skins. These can be turned in for a reward.You have accepted a task to acquire sea-tossed skins.Wanted: sea-tossed skins from Umbar or the outer reaches of Gondor.Wanted: sea-tossed skins from Umbar. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.Wanted: sea-tossed skins from Gondor. Gather them to earn a reward suitable for collectors.You have turned in a stack of trophies for a modest reward suitable for a collector such as yourself.The citizens of Umbar Baharbêl have a small quantity of Embers available.Collect 100 Umbari Tâm to trade with the Umbar Quartermaster.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest can be completed weekly.</rgb>Collect Umbari Tâm (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Talk to the Umbar Quartermaster to trade Umbari Tâm for Embers.

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest can be completed weekly.</rgb>'If you are looking for Embers, I have just a few. I would be willing to part with them if you have some Umbari Tâm.'

<rgb=#FF0000>This quest can be completed weekly.</rgb>'It is a worthwhile trade! I am glad you agree. This is all I have though. Perhaps I will acquire more Embers soon!'

<rgb=#FF0000>Completing this quest will remove 100 Umbari Tâm.</rgb>Galâmka has asked that you venture into Maurûsh again, this time to defeat Orcs and gather any information you can about their battle plans.Galâmka asked you to retrieve any battle plans you can from the Orcs in Maurûsh.Collect Orc battle plans (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bring the battle plans back to Galâmka in Ub Nisihir to the north of Maurûsh.'Good to see you, my friend. Our preparations are nearly finished.

'There is another task I ask of you as we make ready here. I ask that you go into Maurûsh, and recover any battle plans that the Orcs may have.

'With your help, we have achieved much in weakening their forces, but we do not know what they plan to do within the stronghold during a battle. Return to me if you can discover anything about this.''You return again from Maurûsh! And with uplifting news I hope?'

You give Galâmka the plans you recovered from the Orc which contain crude drawings of the area surrounding Maurûsh with marks in various locations.

'Hmm. They all have these markings in similar locations. I believe I know of where these markings are. I will send scouts at once to see what they may be indicating for the Orcs.

'Return to me when you can. We may need your help again to deal with what we discover.'Usakâr asks you to scout the perimeter of Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, to learn more about it and the Shagâni who inhabit the Well.Scout Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, from the north, west, and east. Tûl Zakana is far to the east of Dag Usakâra. It can be found south-east of the Crypts of Esh-kimâkhi.Scout Tûl Zakana from the northern woods below itScout Tûl Zakana from the high rock across the wayScout Tûl Zakana from the closer rock outcropping across from itReturn to Usakâr at Dag Usakâra with your report.Return to Usakâr at Dag Usakâra'There is a strange place to the south, my friend. It troubles me. Locals call it Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting. It has arisen like a mirage in recent years. There you may barter for the Water of Forgetting. This water can make you forget your troubles, and if you drink enough, even who you are.

'The Shagâni who run the Well are rumoured to be Barkhûshim, adherents to the Church of the All-seeing, with loyalties only to their dark lord. I believe they are up to something sinister at Tûl Zakana. Would you scout the outskirts of the Well? I fear we will not uncover all the Well is hiding, but any clue could help us learn more about the Well and its secrets.''Yes, this was my fear. We must get closer to find out anything of substance. Still, thank you for scouting the Well.'Intrigued by your discovery of a curious wall inside another salt mine tunnel, Amâsu has asked you to escort her there to see it for herself.Amâsu has indicated that you should invite Girhâzi to also see what you discovered. You should talk to Girhâzi.Talk to GirhâziGirhâzi has agreed to accompany you and Amâsu to return to the odd wall in the tunnel you discovered. Talk to Girhâzi when you are ready to leave.Complete Instance: Unlocking HistoryAfter guiding Amâsu and Girhâzi to the salt mine you discovered, they were able to locate and open the lost library, Dubarâkh. You should talk to Girhâzi.Talk to Girhâzi'You have made an intriguing discovery, ${PLAYER}. The more I think about it, the more I think I would like to see it myself. I will go alone if needed, but would you be willing to take me to this suspicious wall you found in the salt mines? If Girhâzi is finished being cross, she may come as well.''All ready? Let us be on our way then!''What an incredible day this is, ${PLAYER}. We have found the Dubarâkh! It feels like a dream that I will wake up from at any moment. We will now be able to learn our full history; to learn who we are and where we came from. The fire of the Ambarûli will be rekindled, and this would not have happened without your help. I confess that I did not trust you at first, but you are a true friend ${PLAYER}, and you will always be welcome here among us.

'Our people weep with joy. Thank you, ${PLAYER}. If you ever wish to return to Dubarâkh, there will be Dumûri posted at the mine entrance to escort you there.''I was wondering where you had been off to.  Another salt mine you say?'

You tell Girhâzi that you're taking Amâsu to investigate the tunnels and that she is invited if she would like to come.

'Invited, eh?' Girhâzi chuckles.

'Amâsu knows quite well that she is like a mother to me, and I would sooner cut off my thumb than let her walk into potential danger without me at her side. Talk to me again when you are ready to go and we will travel there together.'Bôgalekh has asked you to go investigate the ruins of Ghâs Hejai to the north for more information.Bôgalekh has requested that you go to Ghâs Hejai to the north of Jadambor to investigate the Hakâri cultists.Travel to Ghâs Hejai to investigate the areaObtain any information you can from the Hakâri cultists that are gathered in Ghâs Hejai to the north of Jadambor.Gather any information you can from Hakâri cultists (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered information regarding the Hakâri cultists and their plans. Bring what you have learned to Hênok located to the east of Ghâs Hejai.'I request that you head north of here, to Ghâs Hejai, and uncover anything you can about their activities.

'I have sent one of my scouts ahead by the name of Hênok. If you discover anything, report it to him and he will know what to do. He should be holed up to the east of Ghâs Hejai when you need him.

'Be aware, ${CLASS}, the path may be dangerous. But know your efforts may prove crucial in uncovering this threat.''You must be the ${CLASS} Bôgalekh told me of. What tidings do you bring from your encounters with the Hakâri?

'Any information you have gleaned may aid us greatly. I have been watching Ghâs Hejai as closely as I can from a safe distance and there has been much activity.'

You hand Hênok the information you gathered from the cultists.The gladiatorial pits of Umbar Baharbêl serve several important purposes: entertaining the masses, keeping their attention focused, and meting justice upon those who break the laws of the city.Complete 'Dahâl Huliz, The Arena -- Tier 1'Complete 'Dahâl Huliz, The Arena -- Tier 2'Complete 'Dahâl Huliz, The Arena -- Tier 3'Complete 'Dahâl Huliz, The Arena -- Gladiators'Complete 'Dahâl Huliz, The Arena -- Tier 4'Complete 'Dahâl Huliz, The Arena -- Tier 5'Slay many foes in western Dil-irmíz.Slay many foes in Dil-irmíz.Complete Cultist-bane of Dil-irmíz (Advanced)Complete Darkwater-slayer of Dil-irmíz (Advanced)The hostile forces of Ekhamât have claimed Dun Shûma, the hidden fortress of Menûn Shûma III. The ancient ruin is said to hold many historical treasures of the kingdoms of Shagâna. Though Bârshud has retreated, his Umûni still stand opposed to Mizâdi’s quest to claim the Crown of Hamât.Complete 'Dun Shûma, The King's Fortress -- Tier 1'Complete 'Dun Shûma, The King's Fortress -- Tier 2'Complete 'Dun Shûma, The King's Fortress -- Tier 3'Complete 'Dun Shûma, The King's Fortress -- Ordâkhai'In a sheltered valley in the north of Khûd Zagin stand ancient, crumbling ziggurat-tombs of pale stone collectively known as Nirgambâr. Scouts have reported that one of the Zidemel's men, Andur, is said to have entered Nirgambâr drawn their by dark whispers. Locate and stop Andur before he can unleash what lurks in this cursed place.Complete 'Nirgambâr, the Restless Tomb -- Tier 1'Complete 'Nirgambâr, the Restless Tomb -- Tier 2'Complete 'Nirgambâr, the Restless Tomb -- Tier 3'Complete 'Nirgambâr, the Restless Tomb -- Restless Dead'The gladiatorial pits of Umbar Baharbêl serve several important purposes: entertaining the masses, keeping their attention focused, and meting justice upon those who break the laws of the city.Complete 'The Dragon and the Storm -- Tier 1'Complete 'The Dragon and the Storm -- Tier 2'Complete 'The Dragon and the Storm -- Tier 3'Deep in the war-torn lands of Imhûlar, locals warn you of an Ordâkhai encampment, a trading post of sorts. It is known as Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, whose waters will make you forget your troubles, and, if you drink enough, even who you are. But unless you are trading for this water, it's best to stay well clear of the Well, for many have vanished near it, never to be seen again. You are now tasked by the families of the vanished with investigating the Well and its secrets.Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 1'Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 2'Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Tier 3'Complete 'Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting -- Ordâkhai'You have turned over every stone, discovered every encampment, and defeated every foe in Urash Dâr in the Ikorbân Valley.Complete quests in Urash Dâr in the Ikorbân Valley.Complete Explorer of Urash DârComplete Slayer of Urash DârComplete Tales of Urash DârRaphûz is resolved to speaking with Ashâmi. He wants to apologize for his affront and is leaving the Tale-wardens behind. He asks for your help to find and reach Ashâmi.Raphûz asked for your assistance in finding, assisting, and apologizing to Ashâmi. He will lead you to the withered grove to the south from his home in Jirush.Complete Instance: Voice on the WindAshâmi led you and Raphûz out of the withered grove to the south-east where you arrived at Shoníth, a hidden oasis and home to the Kintai Elves. Raphûz looks like he wants to speak to you.'My friend, I do not think I can make this journey alone. I know the horrors at the ruined grove are evil spirits, and my mind is ruled by my heart. I will need someone rooted to be my guide.

'Will you help me again? Accompany me to the grove and see me through to meeting Ashâmi?'

<rgb=#FF0000>Warning: Successfully completing the instance in this quest line will place your character in the Elf Oasis of Shoníth after successful completion.</rgb>'I am prepared, my friend. Let us go.''The Aladi are real. They are not just a superstition of the people. I know she is not ensorcelled. I know she is headstrong and independent. I did not know her intent all along to leave her father behind.

'Such a fierce woman. She burns with a fiery passion. I will learn much from her.

'My friend, I owe you so much for supporting me. Please, will you speak with Ashâmi?'Nimasi is a traitor to Mizâdi, leading you right into Bârshud's trap. You should now aid Mizâdi in preparing for battle with Bârshud.Speak with the messenger from Khûd Zagin near the back gate.Speak with the messenger from Khûd Zagin near the back gateDeliver Amalíbi's message to Mizâdi.Deliver Amalíbi's message to MizâdiSpeak with Usanâb from Khûd Zagin.Speak with Usanâb from Khûd ZaginDeliver Usanâb's final message to Mizâdi in Kûr Anzar.Deliver Usanâb's final message to Mizâdi in Kûr AnzarGather fallen weapons for the Subuzûri and provisions for the waggons going south from around Kûr Anzar.Gather fallen weapons for the Subuzûri in Kûr Anzar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather provisions for the waggons going south in Kûr Anzar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Deposit supplies in the waggon outside the back gate.Speak with Mizâdi in Kûr Anzar.Speak with Mizâdi in Kûr Anzar'This is an evil I dared not believe exist. We will gather all the supplies and Subuzûri and drive him from Imhûlar. I will not hide behind these walls. I will not subject the people of Kûr Anzar to more bloodshed. We will meet him on the open field.

'I have need of you once more, ${PLAYER}. We will need to gather weapons and whatever supplies you can find here in Kûr Anzar.

'Oh, there is another messenger here - what do they want? Could you find out, friend?''Please, give this message to the Shâra. There is word from Khûd Zagin. Amalíbi requests aid against a great many Ordâkhai excursions - we are being overrun!''I cannot think to provide aid now - our troubles are numerous as well. Tell her to tell Amalíbi we cannot aid them now. Imhûlar needs to look after their own in this moment. We have nothing for greater Shagâna.''Please thank the Shâra for seeing me, though Amalíbi will be disappointed in the Shâra's answer. We of the Khûd will look after our own. Apparently Imhûlar will as well.''Anything in the Khûd will have to wait. We need to get the Subuzûri all the weapons we can find and the waggons loaded with supplies for heading south. If this Bârshud wants war, he shall have it.''Excellent. Thank you for your help. We will let the soldiers sleep tonight. Tomorrow, we ride out to meet Bârshud in battle. I do grow weary of war, ${PLAYER}, but nonetheless it has become my ready companion.'Usakâr asks you to go to Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, and barter some coin for the water there, as he wants to test its purported effects.Go to the marketplace outside Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting, and barter for some of their water with the coin Usakâr gave you. Tûl Zakana is far to the east of Dag Usakâra. It can be found south-east of the Crypts of Esh-kimâkhi.Go to the marketplace outside the Well of ForgettingTalk to the Shagâni man behind the counter, and barter for some of their water.Barter for some water with the Shagâni man behind the counterFight off the attackers and flee up the hill!Fight off the attackers and flee toward the hillsReturn to Usakâr at Dag Usakâra and tell him what happened.Return to Usakâr at Dag Usakâra'If you are up for a challenge, I have another task for you.

'It may not be enough to merely scout Tûl Zakana, the Well of Forgetting. We may need to get closer to it. I hear there is a market just outside the walls where locals can exchange coin for this water. Here, take this coin. I want to know whether this water is as they say, or if it is merely a myth that the locals fool themselves into believing.''I am sorry. I did not mean to put you in such danger. It seems they are quite fearful of outsiders, and quick to pull their swords. It will require a concerted effort to learn more about the Well of Forgetting, more than we can do by ourselves, I fear. Still, I appreciate your efforts, and again, apologies for the danger to which you were exposed.''Who are you? I haven't seen you here before. You don't look like any Ikorbâni I have seen before. No, I think we will not sell to you this day. This water is too precious for a lûbara like you. But we also can't allow you to leave, and spread rumours about what you saw here.

'You have failed to pay the price in water, now you must pay in blood. Seize them!'Raphûz asked you to help him gather information about the ways and customs of courtship in Shagâna from the people in the Wrackwade.Raphûz wants to collect information on the ways and customs of Shagâna. He will ask around the Wrackwade camp and then you will both discuss what you learned.Learn about the Shagâni culture from people around the Wrackwade (${NUMBER}/6)You spoke to several members of the Wrackwade to learn about Shagâni culture. You should return to Raphûz to compare what you learned with one another.'Last night when I slumbered, I had a dream. A vision of a life not spent pursuing my parents' dreams. I dreamt of a life here of my own making and choices. My life lived for my purpose, not for some pledge or sense of duty to my parents.

'The rebellious manner the woman used was biting. She is correct. I live in a shadow and fear.

'I want to know more about the Shagâni culture. Could you speak with members of this encampment and learn what you can while I do the same? Together, perhaps we can learn what the woman meant about the deer.''There is a tale of a merchant trading his wife for wealth to a spirit of the desert. We call them Aladi. They are beastly creatures, dealing in curses and terrorizing travellers.

'You would do well to steer away from anyone ensorcelled in this way.''When a man pursues me, I prefer he brings me the flowers of the floodplain. The blossoms are beautiful and easily tended.

'Oh, and the tale of a person returning as a beast is meant to scare children into obeying their parents, nothing more.'You enquire about Shagâni customs and beliefs.

'A man and woman must work together to provide for their family. Without cooperation, there can be no success. Mutual respect is our way, a shared unity of purpose and direction.

'Anyone who tells you otherwise is without a partner.'You enquire about Shagâni customs and beliefs.

'The story of the beasts and the Aladi is a well-known tale. Our elders use it to hide painful truths about death.

'I understand the reasons. Elders must protect the flocks from the horrors that would plague the young. Until now, the Shagâni had little hope beyond servitude and death.

'We will need to alter our customs to encourage the future generations toward hope, not fear.'You enquire about Shagâni customs and beliefs.

'My mother told a similar story to me when my father did not return from a battle.

'She said he met an Aladi who traded our lives for his. She explained that he became a beast of the wild so that we may prosper.'

He chuckles.

'We did not prosper, but we lived. Until I had children, I did not understand the importance of protecting them from the harsh truth of the world.'You enquire about Shagâni customs and beliefs.

'I care not for stories. I care for feeding my family, sheltering my family, protecting my family.

'I faced my fears without regard for consequence to win the hand of my love. I did not overcome the fear, but I found strength where I thought of her.'Raphûz listens to you recount the tales told by the Shagâni.

'The stories I learned are much the same.

'I think I understand.

'The tale is to soften the blow of loss. Though several people I spoke with warned that the Aladi are real.

'The Shagâni prize bravery and strength. They enjoy shared responsibility and family, and the Shagâni way is to provide for each other.

'It is strange to me. A people who suffered so long in oppression are still profoundly proud and hopeful.

'They have amazing fortitude.'Raphûz reflected on the information about the wealth in the Tombs of Nirgambâr and asks you to meet him there to acquire the amount needed to meet Igbâl's request.Raphûz requested you meet him in the deepest part of Nirgambâr to seek wealth from the Dead that make the tombs their home.Make your way to the last pillars leading into NirgambârYou found Raphûz in the ruins of Nirgambâr. He is prepared to find wealth among the Dead here. Work with him and find the wealth he desires.Complete Instance: Wealth of the DeadRaphûz claimed to have acquired enough wealth from the Dead to meet Igbâl's request. He asked you to meet him back at his home in Jirush to discuss further.Speak with Raphûz at his home in Jirush'I will find some time to leave my work aside and make my way to the tombs. I learned there is an entrance into the depths at the far north-west area after a walkway lined with ancient pillars.

'Meet me just beyond the last of the pillars. I will wait for you there. Together we will claim great wealth.''I am ready to proceed if you are, my friend. I know if you are by my side, I cannot fail.''This should command Igbâl's attention and earn me his respect. With your aid, I have done all he asked. Surely, he will provide his blessing to me.'Asilimu has asked for your help gathering ichor and silk from nearby spiders in Ushbarab.Asilimu has asked you to gather ichor and silk from the spiders in Ushbarab.Collect spider ichor (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather spider silk (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bring the spider ichor and spider silk back to Asilimu near Ushbarab'A welcomed sight you are, ${CLASS}!

'I have been working to make remedies for the people in need here in Urash Dâr, but I find myself at an impasse. The ingredients that I need to replenish are spider silks and ichor. I must confess that I myself am dreadfully afraid of those monsters.

'Might you gather the supplies I need? You can find all that I need nearby in Ushbarab. Your efforts will not go unrewarded, and it will be a great help to people in need.''Ah, you have returned! And with the silks and ichor, no less.

'Your courage has spared me from those beasts and ensured the wellbeing of those in need. I thank you, ${CLASS}. Be safe on your travels.'Battling in the Ettenmoors greatly rewards those who are consistent participants. Complete this quest to earn your share of the rewards.Fighting in the Ettenmoors and completing the requests here will earn you greater rewards. There are a series of important objectives found throughout the Ettenmoors that must be undertaken.Complete a battle-altering quest in the Ettenmoors (${NUMBER}/5)Complete a kill enemy quest in the Ettenmoors (${NUMBER}/10)Complete a support quest in the Ettenmoors (${NUMBER}/20)'The Angmarim finally are willing to reward you for your assault against the enemy. Good news for you. Good news for me. Get out there, do your worst, and you will be rewarded well for your slaughter.'A guard along the road has directed you to the village of Iridír, where you have been told to go and present yourself to Girhâzi.Iridír is along the north bank of the Ikorbân River in Ambarûl. You should go there and meet Girhâzi.Introduce yourself to Girhâzi'Hold there!'

The guard yells, eyeing you suspiciously.

'From where do you come stranger, and what is your business in Ambarûl?'

You tell her that you recently came from Umbar and your business is your own. She gazes at you while she carefully considers your answer.

'Girhâzi will wish to speak to you. Continue south and you will come to the town of Iridír. There you will find Girhâzi. Think not of making trouble for the people in Ambarûl or death will swiftly find you. Iridír is well-guarded, and we do not hesitate to relieve our enemies of their heads. Go now.''Girhâzi will wish to speak to you. Continue south along this road and you will come to the village of Iridír. Go there and present yourself to her.'This formidable-looking woman must be Girhâzi, the apparent leader of the people here in Ambarûl. With a curious look, she silently assesses you for several uncomfortable heartbeats.Shanathar has offered a lesson on Kintai forest stewardship.Shanthar has requested you slay some of the deer in Arwáth to restore balance to the population.Defeat fallow deer (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have slain deer in Arwáth. You should return to Shanthar.Talk to Shanthar'The second truth is that what the forest has must be protected. There is an imbalance because Men have slain many of the predators native to this land, and now the prey animals are too numerous. Not only do they raze the undergrowth of the forest and kill the saplings needed to ensure its future, but once everything has been consumed, many animals will die of starvation.

'It is the dreadful truth that slaying some of the deer now will be better for the survival of both the deer and the forest. It is an unpleasant task, but one that must be done. Please do not bring the meat back here though. I do not eat animals, but the scavengers of the forest will appreciate the meal.''Without their natural predators, the deer in Arwáth are too numerous. They raze the undergrowth of the forest, and when it is gone, many will starve. Please slay some deer to help bring balance to the population.''Thank you for seeing to the deer. I am glad it was you who performed that abhorrent task so that it did not fall upon me.'Shubârza has noticed that the morale of some of soldiers in the camp has been low. She asks that you help inspire them for the coming fight.Shubârza has noticed that the morale of some of soldiers in the camp has been low. She asks that you help inspire them for the coming fight.Inspire demoralized soldiers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Shubârza in Amalíbi's camp and let her know you managed to inspire some of the soldiers.Return to Shubârza in Amalíbi's camp'Greetings. You may have spotted some of the saddened soldiers nearby. Can you help me to inspire them?

'We are sure to have many battles ahead, but some here are already feeling weighed down by the thought. I ask that you help to cheer them up and give them any encouragement you can. Hope is a powerful weapon in battle.''Smiles have returned to the faces of my fellow soldiers!

'Thank you, ${CLASS}. Your words will give us strength in the days to come. Take this for your troubles.'Defeat sand-worms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})'Hail, adventurer. Have you taken care of those sand-worms in the south yet? I do not wish to hasten you along, but my friends are beginning to gossip about my capabilities.''And you return with news of those pests' defeat, I trust? Excellent! Here is the coin I was promised for handling this matter, plus a few more for your secrecy, ha ha! I shall not ask such a task of you again. I am eternally done with even the promise of adventure.'In this season of Yule, we give thought to our virtues.Celebrate Yule!'In the season of Yule, the fires of our virtues burn brightly.''In the season of Yule, the fires of our virtues burn brightly.''Let the embers of Yule enrich your virtuous nature.''Let the embers of Yule enrich your virtuous nature.'Angârmu has asked you to help him recover valuable relics within Zikrum-dûl that were left behind when he fled.Angârmu has asked you to travel to Zikrum-dûl and recover various ancient relics that hold great value.Travel to Zikrum-dûl to search for the ancient relicsGather the ancient relics that Angârmu asked for from Zikrum-dûl.Gather a pouch ritual spicesFind a flask of ancient elixirCollect a pouch of precious gemsReturn to Angârmu in Malgâma with the ancient relics gathered from Zikrum-dûl.'Ah, visitor! You must gather relics for me. Relics I thought lost, but they are not.

'You must go. Go to Zikrum-dûl. It is dangerous, far too dangerous for me to travel there myself. But you can go. I need you to go and gather my relics.

'In Zikrum-dûl you must gather for me a bottle of an ancient elixir, a pouch filled with dust that I can use for my rituals, and finally, a small box, yes a box, filled with precious gemstones.

'Go now and bring these back to me, friendly visitor.''You return! With my relics in hand no less!'

You hand over the gathered items to Angârmu.

'These are very valuable relics, and it had saddened me to leave them behind when the demons came, but I had no choice.

'I thank you, yes I thank you for bringing them back to me, visitor.'You found a line of barricades of Orc-make in the woods between Imhûlar and Adagím.A line of Orc-made barricades runs from north to south in the woods between Imhûlar and Adagím.Examine the line of Orc-made barricades in the woods between Imhûlar and AdagímThe Orc camp of Mauthark lies to the south-east of the line of barricades you discovered.Find the entrance to Mauthark, south-east of the barricades you discoveredThe Orcs of Mauthark carry deadly knives with which they threaten the Kintai who live in Adagím.Collect deadly knives from Orcs at Mauthark (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The nearest grove of the Kintai should be Laivárth, located far to the north-east of Mauthark. The Elves there will have a strongbox in which you can deposit the deadly Orc-knives.The Kintai who tend the grove of Laivárth will be appreciative of your efforts against the Orcs dwelling at Mauthark.CompletedYour examination of the barricade erected in the woods reveals that it is clearly of Orc-make, part of a line stretching from north to south between Imhûlar and Adagím.

Harsh cries from the south-east signal the presence of a large encampment of Orcs. The Kintai who make their home deeper in the woods would likely appreciate if you could deprive the Orcs of their lives and their weapons. Once you have done that you can bring their weapons to the grove of Laivárth, which is reportedly to the north-east of here, among the blighted trees of Adagím.The deadly knives you collected from Mauthark rest upon a number of other Orc weapons. It would appear you are not the only traveller in Adagím who seeks to help the Kintai!

You resolve to continue your efforts on behalf of the Kintai, becoming a barricade of your own between them and the Orcs who threaten their ways of peace.Irsinith has given you a letter explaining how the rot is consuming Amûma. She has asked that you bring it to a scholar, Orthél, in Sul Madásh.Irsinith has given you a letter explaining how the rot is consuming Amûma. She has asked that you bring it to a scholar, Orthél, in Sul Madásh.Travel to Sul MadáshBring Irsinith's letter to Orthél in Sul Madásh.'Amûma is fading faster these days.'

Irsinith looks up at the great tree.

'I am failing as her warden. Please bring this report to the scholars in Sul Madásh to inform them how Amûma is fairing. They will not send any assistance, but they will want to know.''Greetings, ${CLASS}. How may I help you?'

You give Orthél the letter from Irsinith and she quickly reads it and looks fearful.

'This is troubling news. We did not know that Amûma was being consumed by this blight so quickly. We must find a way to stop it.

'I will continue doing what I can. I ask that you see if there are others you can help around Sul Madásh. Any information we can uncover will be helpful.'In the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed.You stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Reach Acquaintance standing with the Temámir of Jiret-menêshYou stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Speak with Semanísi at Jiret-menêshIn the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed. Among the adventurous dwarves who dared leave the deep halls, your name and effort gain heft. Return to the quartermaster to see new wares awaiting those proven.'You endure where others fall. In this, you have earned the regard of those who rose from the deep. Your name, your bearing, your deeds all speak well. We hold to our word. Choose a gift, earned by toil.'Across Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people.You remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Acquaintance standing with The City of ZajânaYou remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Alahamâr at ZajânaAcross Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people. In the halls of Zajâna, your name and deeds grow with import.'Word reaches us of your service to Kighân and to Zajâna. I see the truth of it with my own eyes, though my caution remains. These first steps, though small, are not without merit. We are a people who remember those who keep their word. Take this gift, and let it mark your beginning.'Umbar Baharbêl is alive with rumour that a Wizard will soon arrive to speak with the Kindred of the Coins about the many threats facing the city.Tasâdi usually holds court at the Waterway Market to the south-east of Dâr Laja.Talk to Tasâdi at the Waterway MarketA small knot of people to the south of Tasâdi seem to be discussing what they have heard. You should <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to their conversation.Phârex has caught your eye.Talk to Phârex at the Waterway MarketSome folk just past the fountain south of Phârex are discussing the effect a Wizard could have on the political situation of Umbar Baharbêl. You should <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to their conversation.Ushrin has his own opinions concerning the effect of a Wizard on these lands.Talk to Ushrin at the Waterway MarketA small knot of people to the south of Ushrin loudly discuss the rumours concerning a Wizard's powers. You should <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to their conversation.Despite the claims of the Umbari that they would know the Wizard when he entered the city, just such a person has already arrived.Talk to Inkanûsh beside the Fathomwell beyond the archway'Can it be so? Has the day come at last?'

Jaith looks about furtively before continuing with a hushed, eager tone.

'Rumours abound that the Wizard Inkanûsh now approaches the city! We guardsmen of Umbar Baharbêl trade not in gossip... but Kinsman Belondor's soldiers returned from Ikorbân with news of him, and they say he will perform feats of magic for any who ask him! These are dangerous times, ${PLAYER}, as you well know, and the Kindred of the Coins might have need of such feats if they are to keep their people safe.

'Will you go to the Waterway Market to the south-east and see if anyone has yet seen the Wizard Inkanûsh? The rumourmonger Tasâdi often holds court there at this time of day, and if anyone has heard anything about the Wizard, it will be she!''Will you go to the Waterway Market to the south-east and see if anyone has yet seen the Wizard Inkanûsh? The rumourmonger Tasâdi often holds court there at this time of day, and if anyone has heard anything about the Wizard, it will be she!''Rumours are like water, my friend. If you have the ability to keep it contained, you can control who receives it, and its value only increases. But once it is spilled, there is no returning it to the container!

'Whispers about the approaching Wizard have already spread throughout the market, and few are interested in what I have to say about him, for they can hear these tidings everywhere! My trade is in news and information... what now can I do?

'Alas for Tasâdi, once the most accomplished rumourmonger in all of Umbar Baharbêl! She is finished. Fine, then! Go to the south and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to the whispers of my former customers. They know all that I have heard, and more beside!''Alas for Tasâdi, once the most accomplished rumourmonger in all of Umbar Baharbêl! She is finished. Fine, then! Go to the south and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to the whispers of my former customers. They know all that I have heard, and more beside!''This talk is getting out of hand, if you ask me. What use could we have for a Wizard? Folk such as that bring only trouble. They say they can take on many forms, and creep undiscovered into places where they are not wanted! I did not trust the cultists of the All Seeing, and I will not trust a Wizard either... they seem little different to me!''What use could we have for a Wizard? Folk such as that bring only trouble.''I have heard the arrival of a Wizard heralds the onset of good fortune! Wherever he walks, golden coins are said to fall from his pockets, and these are free for the taking by any who chance upon him!

'I have kept my eyes peeled for him, but my wife also expects me to make my purchases at the market and return to her. How am I to shop while there could be a Wizard and his golden coins so near, with me blind to his presence?''Have you seen him? Has a Wizard come to Umbar Baharbêl?''Ah, there you are, my friend. I wondered if you would come to see me here.

'I have overheard one or two fanciful beliefs about me and my Order while I rested here. I shall not test your patience by repeating them.'

You ask the Wizard where he keeps all of his golden coins, and he chuckles to himself.

'I see that you have heard some of them. Did you hear too that I apparently possess the ability to take on forms enough to put Beorn himself to shame? Alas, it is not so. Still, the Umbari were so eager to see this impressive figure they have imagined that they paid me little mind, and I entered the city with little fanfare. I have been quite untroubled today... or perhaps I should say I have not been disturbed by the Umbari. The threat posed by the Thardúth and their Utûgi warriors fills me with unease.'The situation in these lands will demand careful handling by all of the affected parties: rulers, Wizards, soldiers, and common people alike.The Wizard Inkanûsh wishes to go with you to investigate the nearby commotion. Speak with him beside the Fathomwell on the eastern side of the city.Talk to Inkanûsh beside the Fathomwell and go with him to investigate the nearby commotionInkanûsh stands by the stable-master at the Fleet-gate in south-eastern Umbar Baharbêl.Talk to Inkanûsh by the stable-master at the Fleet-gate'This is not the first time I have come to Shagâna or to Umbar Baharbêl. When my purpose brought me to these lands, years ago, it was not as Mithrandir. Nor did I wear the name Gandalf when I crossed the Ikorbân River in search of answers among the great cities of the south. I was called "Inkanûsh" by the people who dwelled in these lands, and let it be so again while we here remain. I have been a friend to all the Free Peoples of the North, but the names they gave to me ring harshly against Shagâni ears.'

Inkanûsh puffs on his pipe before continuing, but as he begins to speak again something draws his attention.

'There is a commotion at the gate, ${PLAYER}. If you are agreeable, we will together see what has happened to disturb the peace. Perhaps they have found someone that better fits their expectation of a Wizard?''Let us see what has caused this commotion at the gate, and if we might be of some service in ending it.''Hármelak is wrong. Ordâkh is not powerless, it is true, but the gravest danger facing these lands, and indeed all lands in Middle-earth, belongs to the Thardúth! How can this Umbari woman believe that Utug-bûr might be the only dwelling the Thardúth ever fashioned in all of Shagâna's vast reaches?

'Before I came to the city I dispatched some of our friends to travel north into Harondor. Legolas, Gimli, and the sons of Elrond will ensure the Thardúth and their Utûgi warriors did not go that way to endanger Gondor. If they discover such a threat they will send word to me.

'But our road lies to the south, ${PLAYER}.'Inkanûsh believes your journey south alongside Shâra Mizâdi should begin in Imhûlar with stops at Urmâkh and Vartúlin.Shâra Mizâdi intends to rejoin her army near the town of Urmâkh in Imhûlar, and Inkanûsh asked you to speak with her there about her plans.Talk to Shâra Mizâdi at the town of Urmâkh in ImhûlarShâra Mizâdi invited you to stand by the eastern gate of Urmâkh and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for her returning soldiers.Soldiers that fought for Mizâdi at Kûr Anzar slowly climb the road to Urmâkh.Glorfindel has arrived at Urmâkh, and wishes to speak with you about the next leg of your journey.Talk to Glorfindel inside the eastern gate of UrmâkhThe Kinta Thórvilon tends the grove of Vartúlin, to the south-east of Urmâkh.Talk to Thórvilon at Vartúlin, south-east of UrmâkhInkanûsh has come to the Kinta grove of Vartúlin in Imhûlar.Talk to Inkanûsh at the Kinta grove of Vartúlin in ImhûlarInkanûsh fears that the Utûgi have drawn near to the grove of Vartúlin, and he wants you to search the dead woods nearby for some sign of their presence.Search for some sign of the Utûgi to the north or north-east of VartúlinHostile eyes study you from nearby. You need to draw this foe out of hiding!Inkanûsh waits for the report of your scouting at the Kinta grove of Vartúlin in Imhûlar.Talk to Inkanûsh at the Kinta grove of Vartúlin in Imhûlar'We will not travel alone, ${PLAYER}! Glorfindel has agreed to accompany us on our southward journey, and he waits for us at the town of Urmâkh, in Imhûlar. Once we have reunited with him, I suggest we meet with the Kintai at the grove of Vartúlin. Our road must pass deeper into what was once the Emerald Wood, but now that forest is changed and the Kintai will guide us safely within.

'Mizâdi intended to return to Urmâkh to regroup with her own soldiers before travelling south. Speak with her when you arrive, and give her my thanks. Her ambitions, and the army she commands to accomplish them, will be of great service to us if I am right and the Thardúth lurk within Mûr Ghala.''Mizâdi intended to return to Urmâkh to regroup with her own soldiers before travelling south. Speak with her when you arrive, and give her my thanks. Her ambitions, and the army she commands to accomplish them, will be of great service to us if I am right and the Thardúth lurk within Mûr Ghala.''I am pleased to have your aid and support, friend, and that of the Wizard Inkanûsh! We reclaimed Kûr Anzar, but it was not enough. Even possessing the Crown of Renewed Hamât is not enough, for Bârshud remains alive to threaten my young kingdom and its people. We will only be safe when Bârshud has been slain and the Empire of Ordâkh knows the courage in our hearts and the strength of our sword-arms.

'Even now my soldiers heed this call. Stand by the gate and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for the warriors that come from Kûr Anzar to join me on the southward road into danger.''Even now my soldiers heed this call. Stand by the gate and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for the warriors that come from Kûr Anzar to join me on the southward road into danger.''Give me a moment to rest, if you would. I am not so young in years that I feel not the road, nor the fighting I faced before it.

'Shâra Mizâdi wrested my home of Kûr Anzar from the Barkhûshim and Ordâkhai that held the city, and for that she will have my gratitude and my loyalty. Aye, unto death! Many others of my neighbours and my friends feel the same. We knew not that we might be Hamâti once again, but the coming of the Shâra wakened us to this truth: history is an unbroken chain that links the past to the present, even as it lies forgotten in the dust, or trampled underfoot.''I am almost... home. I have dreamt of living here once again in peace, but...

'For how long can I remain at Urmâkh, knowing that Shâra Mizâdi faces our enemies in distant lands? Her own husband Eshtali perished in recent days, and still she fights.

'But I know not if I am as strong as she.''I will follow Shâra Mizâdi even to death.''I know not if I am strong enough to continue this fight.''It is always the same, ${PLAYER}. In this place I see young soldiers of old wars, fighting and dying in battles they did not cause and whose roots they cannot see. They fight against Ordâkh as I fought against Mordor, and they fight for Mizâdi as I fought for Gil-galad the High King. Do they think this will be the last battle they need fight, as I believed then? Did I call them young soldiers? Perhaps I am one of them after all, despite my years!

'I understand Mizâdi and her army will travel the longer way through Khûd Zagin into Mûr Ghala, avoiding the dangerous forest road, but Inkanûsh wishes otherwise. He would have us speak first with Thórvilon, the Kinta warden of the grove of Vartúlin. It is the Wizard's plan to take the forest road through what remains of the Emerald Wood, ensuring that the Utûgi did not go that way unseen.

'It seems a good idea to me. The creatures we fought at the Dagor Carlanthir were fearsome, and I do not want them to prey upon the Kintai. Our kindreds are long divided, but they remain Elves, and dear to me even from such distance.''Inkanûsh wishes for us to speak first with Thórvilon, the Kinta warden of the grove of Vartúlin. It is the Wizard's plan to take the forest road through what remains of the Emerald Wood, ensuring that the Utûgi did not go that way unseen.

'It seems a good idea to me. The creatures we fought at the Dagor Carlanthir were fearsome, and I do not want them to prey upon the Kintai. Our kindreds are long divided, but they remain Elves, and dear to me even from such distance.''What brings you back to Vartúlin, ${PLAYER}?'

You tell Thórvilon that you have come with the Elf-lord Glorfindel and the Wizard Inkanûsh to secure the safety of the Emerald Wood on your journey to oppose the Empire of Ordâkh. The warden of the grove strokes his chin.

'My folk have noticed strange Orc-like creatures moving among the trees outside Vartúlin. We believed them to be Orcs at first, but these were dressed strangely and seemed a larger breed than the Orcs we have encountered before.''My folk have noticed strange Orc-like creatures moving among the trees outside Vartúlin. We believed them to be Orcs at first, but these were dressed strangely and seemed a larger breed than the Orcs we have encountered before.''These creatures of Orc-kind of which Thórvilon speaks are almost certainly Utûgi, ${PLAYER}. If you will go search for sign of these invaders outside Vartúlin, I will tell him what we know of them and their Thardúth masters.

'Be careful. The Utûgi must be driven by some evil purpose if they dare approach the groves of the Kintai.''As you search for sign of these invaders outside Vartúlin, I will tell Thórvilon what we know of the Utûgi and their Thardúth masters. Be careful. The Utûgi must be driven by some evil purpose if they dare approach the groves of the Kintai.''As you search for sign of these invaders outside Vartúlin, I will tell Thórvilon what we know of the Utûgi and their Thardúth masters. Be careful. The Utûgi must be driven by some evil purpose if they dare approach the groves of the Kintai.''So it is true: the Utûgi leave the lands in which they awoke and begin to threaten others nearby. Our mission grows more serious.

'But we cannot leave Imhûlar just yet. While you were gone, Thórvilon spoke to me of a woman about whom I must learn more. He says she is named Cúridwan, and she has been a friend to the Kintai for as long as he can remember.'

Inkanûsh looks at you closely, one of his eyebrows raised.

'Thórvilon is one of the Kinta Elves, ${PLAYER}, and his memory is long. How can it be that this woman has lived in this place for such a span? This mystery and others have prompted me to ask a number of questions I have not before thought to ask.

'I must speak with this woman. I must speak with Cúridwan.'Inkanûsh has many questions to ask of the mysterious woman Cúridwan who dwells near Vartúlin.Inkanûsh waits at Vartúlin for you and Glorfindel to go with him to speak with Cúridwan.Talk to Inkanûsh at Vartúlin and go with him to speak with CúridwanInkanûsh has returned to the Kinta grove of Vartúlin in Imhûlar and wishes to speak with you.Talk to Inkanûsh at Vartúlin'Will you come with me to speak with this woman? Let me know when you are ready to depart.''Then let us call upon Cúridwan and learn if she has answers to my questions.''The answers to my questions must wait until we find Cúridwan somewhere within Adagím. I am nothing if I am not patient, ${PLAYER}. I trust you have not heard otherwise!'Eager to speak with the mysterious Cúridwan, Inkanûsh wishes to travel with you into Adagím, the Moulder-wood, where a terrible blight has taken hold.Thórvilon is at the Kinta grove of Vartúlin, in Imhûlar.Athgai stands at the border with Adagím.Talk to Athgai at the border with AdagímMauthark and Mokh Grazádh can be found among the swamps of Adagím.Defeat Orcs from Mauthark or Mokh Grazádh in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Athgai told you to seek the grove of Laivárth, north-east along the path in Adagím.Find the grove of Laivárth in AdagímGlorfindel stands in the grove of Laivárth.Talk to Glorfindel in LaivárthShadalith stands nearby in the grove of Laivárth.Talk to Shadalith in LaivárthShadalith asked you to retrieve samples of the rot at locations to the north-east, north-west, and west of Laivárth.Collect sample of the rot north-east of LaivárthCollect sample of the rot north-west of LaivárthCollect sample of the rot west of LaivárthShadalith is at the grove of Laivárth.Talk to Shadalith in LaivárthShadalith placed a bucket of water to the west. She wants you to add the samples of the blight you collected to the water so she can see if there is any effect.Shadalith is in Laivárth.Talk to Shadalith in Laivárth'If Cúridwan has gone ahead into Adagím, that is where our road must lead. But it is the foolhardy traveller who passes into an unfamiliar land without knowledge; we must not be unprepared.

'What can Thórvilon tell us of the woods into which we must go?''It is the foolhardy traveller who passes into an unfamiliar land without knowledge; we must not be unprepared. What can Thórvilon tell us of the woods into which we must go?''What can I tell you about Adagím that will not pain me? Very little, my friend. So much of the Emerald Wood has been tainted by blight and corruption that the beautiful forest of my youth seems somehow unreal. When the Ordâkhai came north, something they brought with them damaged the soil. The blight that slew the trees of Imhûlar began first in Adagím, taking the form of a terrible fungal rot. Many groves of my people were abandoned, until only Sul Madásh remained unspoiled.

'The Adagím of today is a nightmarish, boggy wilderness, despite the efforts of the Kintai. Some of us fled westward to Vartúlin, or to Shonith beside the Khûd, but I fear it is only a matter of time before the blight consumes every tree that once grew within the Emerald Wood of Nisigím.

'Perhaps Inkanûsh and Cúridwan together will be able to fight it, ${PLAYER}. I hope it is so! Hurry on to speak with Athgai at the border with Adagím, and hasten the meeting of these two wise ones!''Perhaps Inkanûsh and Cúridwan together will be able to fight the corruption that has taken root in Imhûlar and Adagím. I hope it is so! Hurry on to speak with Athgai at the border into Adagím, and hasten the meeting of these two wise ones!''Wait a moment, newcomers. Few come this way, save the Orcs, and it is my duty to keep watch for any who stray this far into the wood. What business have you in Adagím?'

You tell the Kinta guard that you are only passing through on your way south, but that you hoped to find Cúridwan somewhere on the way. Athgai nods thoughtfully.

'I stopped and spoke with her briefly a few hours ago, as I have stopped you. She said she possessed a salve that might cure us of the Lâg Dávrit. If only it might be true! She passed north-east in the direction of Laivárth. If you head that way too, might I trouble you to defeat some of the Orcs that lurk beside the path? They seem untroubled by the noisome fumes that now fill the air, and it is our burden to thin their numbers before they become emboldened. They have fashioned two camps near here, Mauthark and Mokh Grazádh, and if you defeat these creatures you will be aiding both the Kintai and your friend Cúridwan.''Cúridwan passed north-east in the direction of Laivárth. If you head that way too, might I trouble you to defeat some of the Orcs that lurk beside the path? They seem untroubled by the noisome fumes that now fill the air, and it is our burden to thin their numbers before they become emboldened. They have fashioned two camps near here, Mauthark and Mokh Grazádh, and if you defeat these creatures you will be aiding both the Kintai and your friend Cúridwan.''Your friend Cúridwan may be at the grove of Laivárth, to the north-east. You should look for her there.''The fungal corruption has begun to work on this grove as well, despite the efforts of its tenders to halt its spread. See the mark of the blight there, and there? My heart is heavy, and I have only just arrived! Imagine how it must feel to live in this wood, and watch the corruption advance with the passage of the hours, and of the days!''Imagine how it must feel to live in this wood, and watch the corruption advance with the passage of the hours, and of the days!''Your friend speaks the truth: it is difficult to watch the blight consume our land, twisting the trees in horrible mockery of their once-healthy forms. Some among my people believe the fungal contagion spews from a single entity: Sívigush, the Earth-terror; others believe the blight is itself alive, and Sívigush is in fact an evil composed of every tendril, mushroom, and spore released into the foetid air of Adagím.

'I know not what I believe. If you are willing to take this trowel and bring back a sample from several locations where the rot has taken hold, perhaps we can learn something that will be of use. I will speak with your friend while you conduct this study on my behalf, and help the two of you as I can.

'The rot is particularly bad to the north-east, north-west, and west of Laivárth.''Bring back samples from several locations where the rot has taken hold and we may learn something of use. I will speak with your friend while you conduct this study on my behalf, and help the two of you as I can.

'The rot is particularly bad to the north-east, north-west, and west of Laivárth.''You have returned with the samples of the rot I asked you to collect? Thank you for doing this for me!

'I have placed a bucket of water to the west. Will you add the portions of the blight you collected to the water? I am interested in the effect they may have upon the water.''I have placed a bucket of water just over there. Will you add the portions of the blight you collected to the water? I am interested in the effect they may have upon the water.''Well, then. I know not what it means, but it smells terrible.'Grateful for the help you provided, Shadalith has suggested that you visit Tûl Ama, the Hill of the Mother, on your way to Sul Madásh.Tûl Ama, the Hill of the Mother, is to the south-east of Laivárth.Find Tûl Ama to the south-east of LaivárthGlorfindel stands sorrowfully by Amûma, the Old Mother of Adagím.Talk to Glorfindel at Tûl AmaIrsinith is at Tûl Ama, the Hill of the Mother.Talk to Irsinith at the Hill of the MotherIrsinith asked you to collect venom-sacs, sickly wings, and damaged leaves from creatures located not far from the Hill of the Mother in Adagím.Collect venom sacs from spiders in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sickly wings from sickle-flies in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect damaged leaves from Huorns in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Irsinith is at the Hill of the Mother in Adagím.Glorfindel is at the Hill of the Mother in Adagím.Talk to Glorfindel at the Hill of the Mother'The road to discovery is often slow and riddled with setbacks. Such is the way of things.

'Your friend Glorfindel spoke to me of your search for Cúridwan. She has likely gone on ahead to Sul Madásh, largest of the Kinta groves within the Emerald Wood. But wait!

'Before you come to Sul Madásh, you should spend a moment at Tûl Ama, the Hill of the Mother. Look for it to the south-east, for it is on your way. The Old Mother, Amûma in my tongue, is unmistakable. You will know her at once, and Cúridwan is like to have stopped there herself.

'Farewell, my new friends.''Before you come to Sul Madásh, you should spend a moment at Tûl Ama, the Hill of the Mother. Look for it to the south-east, for it is on your way. The Old Mother, Amûma in my tongue, is unmistakable. You will know her at once, and Cúridwan is like to have stopped there herself.''I knew what to expect and still the sorrow cuts me deeply, ${PLAYER}. This great mother-tree of the Emerald Wood stood unharmed for untold years, but now the blight that savages Adagím works its evil even upon her. See the blood-red sap she weeps as she mourns!

'Can the Ordâkhai really have wrought this? The Kintai with whom we have spoken seem to believe this corruption began when that Empire crossed north... but how could they have caused such evil as this? Does it not seem beyond their capability, if not beyond their cruel intent?''Can the Ordâkhai really have wrought this? The Kintai with whom we have spoken seem to believe this corruption began when that Empire crossed north... but how could they have caused such evil as this? Does it not seem beyond their capability, if not beyond their cruel intent?''For years I have pondered that question, friends of the Kintai, but no solution has come to me. I cannot conceive of such a thing: who would willingly inflict such harm upon this land, or upon Amûma and her children? I have long been the warden of this grove. Tending to her in this state has left me weak and sorrowful.'

Irsinith sighs heavily, and you are struck by the notion that she seems very much alike to the mother-tree she guards. You ask if there is anything you and Glorfindel might do to lighten her burden.

'The corruption affects both plants and animals, and by observing these signs we can track the spread of the blight through the forest. Will you bring me venom sacs from spiders, sickly wings from nearby flies, and damaged leaves from Huorns in the woods? I would collect these myself, but I am quite exhausted today already. Perhaps I will speak with your sympathetic friends while you are away and learn how I might aid you in return?''The corruption affects both plants and animals, and by observing these signs we can track the spread of the blight through the forest. Will you bring me venom sacs from spiders, sickly wings from nearby flies, and damaged leaves from Huorns in the woods? I would collect these myself, but I am quite exhausted today already. Perhaps I will speak with your sympathetic friends while you are away and learn how I might aid you in return?''Thank you for bringing me these. I will study them later, when I am feeling better. Right now I am simply so tired.''I am just so tired. Please excuse me.''Inkanûsh and I spoke with Irsinith while you were away, ${PLAYER}, and she confirmed what we believed.

'Cúridwan came this way earlier in the day. She proceeded onward to the south-east, and must be making for Sul Madásh, home of the Kintai in Adagím.'Irsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Irsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Speak with IrsinithIrsinith told you about Amûma and her duty as her warden, she wishes to speak with you again.Speak with Irsinith'Come with me, ${CLASS}. I will tell you more of Amûma and my duty to her.''Thank you for giving me a chance to speak and hearing what I wished to say, ${CLASS}. You are kind for doing so and it warms my hurting heart.

'These are troubled times here in Adagím. We must not give up the fight.''Greetings ${PLAYER}. Do you have time to spare for me to speak of Amûma and my duty to her?'Speak with Tálbiral to learn about the the Pale Blight's impact on Adagím and how the Kintai are trying to fight it.Speak with Tálbiral to learn about the the Pale Blight's impact on Adagím and how the Kintai are trying to fight it.Learn about Adagím from TálbiralSpeak with Tálbiral to learn about the the Pale Blight's impact on Adagím and how the Kintai are trying to fight it.Learn about Adagím from TálbiralSpeak with Tálbiral to learn about the the Pale Blight's impact on Adagím and how the Kintai are trying to fight it.Speak with Tálbiral'Greetings, ${CLASS}. I am Tálbiral and this grove is Laivárth. Welcome.

'You do not seem familiar with Adagím. This marsh can be quite dangerous. Please, allow me to tell you more.''While travelling through the marsh, you have seen for yourself the rot that has consumed the forests of Adagím, or as it is better known in this time, the Moulderwood. This rot is known as the Pale Blight. You may have seen how it affected some of the lands to the west as well.

'The Kintai here are doing everything we can to fight back against this infection, but our efforts seem to be a losing battle. This used to be a beautiful and green land until years ago when the Ordâkhai invaded and cracked the earth with the Din of Stone. That unleashed a pestilence that killed the trees, poisoned our waters, and caused fungal growths to appear everywhere.'

Tálbiral looks distraught from recalling the events.

'Sorry, friend. I need but a moment.'Tálbiral clears his throat and readies himself to speak again.

'Thank you. These events have caused us so much pain. They can be difficult to recall.

'We are doing what we can, but my people have abandoned many of our groves already and left them to fester with rot and become overwhelmed with the beasts of the forest, now twisted by the blight consuming them.

'This grove, Laivárth, and the main settlement to the east, Sul Madásh, are the last that remain unspoiled. Though as you can see, it is not without a cost.

'I worry this land and my people will eventually fall. You are either brave or naive to come here, ${CLASS}. Though if you wish to help, there is much you can do in these marshes.''If you choose to help, I have many tasks that could use an extra hand.

'I know not of the others as I remain within Laivárth, but I am certain they will be in need of help as well.

'Thank you for giving me the chance to tell you of these lands. Be safe, adventurer. Adagím is no longer a forgiving place.'Tálbiral has asked for your help with checking trees within Laivárth for signs of rot so that they can be cleansed.Tálbiral has asked for your help with checking trees within Laivárth for signs of rot so that they can be cleansed.Inspect blighted trees in Laivárth (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You found trees that have been infected by the Pale Blight within Laivárth. Return to Tálbiral so he knows to cleanse them from the grove.Speak with Tálbiral'Laivárth has not been safe from the Pale Blight. Though I am doing what I can to keep it safe.

'I could use your help inspecting the trees of the grove for signs of rot. It must be cleansed early to prevent it spreading further.''It is as I feared. I am failing to keep Laivárth safe. This grove used to be unspoiled as well, but the signs are clear. It has begun to succumb to the rot. I will continue to defend the grove until my dying breath.

'Those chieftains in Sul Madásh have abandoned us here, and many withdrew to the settlement. They will provide no aid to us, nor would I ask of it from them.

'So it falls to the kindness of strangers. If you do wish to be of help, I have tasks that could use an adventurer like yourself.'Tálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and gather samples of the blight for him to inspect.Tálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and inspect blighted areas to learn more about it.Reach blight areaTálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and inspect blighted areas to learn more about it.Inspect samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and inspect blighted areas to learn more about it.Reach blight areaTálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and inspect blighted areas to learn more about it.Inspect samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and inspect blighted areas to learn more about it.Reach blight areaTálbiral has asked that you check the area surrounding Laivárth and inspect blighted areas to learn more about it.Inspect samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have inspected the blight surrounding Laivárth. Return to Tálbiral with the information you gathered.Return to Tálbiral'I fear now that the blight is consuming Laivárth. I must put a stop to it.

'${CLASS}, would you go out and further inspect the blight in the area outside of the grove and return with your findings? That may give us some indication of what is causing the blight to spread the fastest so that we can fight back this infection.''Welcome back. Were you able to find signs of the blight outside of the grove?'

You tell Tálbiral of what you observed in the lands outside of Laivárth and how there is rot closing in everywhere.

'That confirms what I have seen myself. We are running out of time to save Laivárth.

'You have my thanks for looking into this for me and confirming my fears. I believe this will be of help identifying what we need to focus on cleansing from our forests.'Tálbiral has asked you to find and speak with Irsinith at the Hill of the Mother. She will want to know about how the Pale Blight is consuming Laivárth.Tálbiral has asked you to find and speak with Irsinith at the Hill of the Mother. She will want to know about how the Pale Blight is consuming Laivárth.Find Irsinith at the Hill of the MotherYou found Irsinith at the Hill of the Mother. Speak with her about the Pale Blight and how Laivárth is being consumed by it.Speak to Irsinith at the Hill of the Mother'I do not know what more I can do to prevent Laivárth from succumbing to the Pale Blight.

'The chieftains of Sul Madásh...'

Tálbiral looks filled with anger at the thought of the chieftains.

'They have abandoned this grove and will do nothing to help. I am at a loss, my friend.

'If you are willing, I ask that you travel to the Hill of the Mother to the east and seek out the warden there. Her name is Irsinith. She should know that the blight is winning the flight in Laivárth, and she may have spoken with Amûma and have more knowledge of how to stop the bleeding.''Greetings, ${CLASS}. What brings you here to the Hill of the Mother?'

You tell Irsinith about how Laivárth is losing the battle against the Pale Blight and that Tálbiral sent you to her to ask for her help.

'As the warden of Amûma, my place is here. I must do everything I can to stop the rot inside, but as this affliction is killing the mother tree, I feel powerless.

'Thank you for bringing me this word from the grove. I ask for a moment please to think on what I must do.'Across Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people.You remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Friend standing with The City of ZajânaYou remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Alahamâr at ZajânaAcross Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people. In the halls of Zajâna, your name and deeds grow with import.'From many corners of the land, your name is spoken—not loudly, but with growing admiration. You have walked the narrow path, and not turned aside. For this, we offer not only reward, but also our trust.

'Zajâna is a city of memory. The Doorwards seek to restore what time and tyranny have buried. Whether through relic, script, or shard, we gather the truth of who we were.

'Among the ruins of our foes or carried by their hands, caches remain -- sealed by the craft of the Ordâkhai. These keys will pass their locks. Take them, or if your hands are skilled in metal, learn the means to forge your own.'The zizani of Adagím sing a high-pitched, warbling song that echoes among the blighted trees and makes your skin crawl.Pods of the blight draw your attention in the woods of Adagím.Examine the pods of blight in AdagímThough they are not known as neekerbreekers in these lands, the zizani of Adagím are no less obtrusive and harmful to the crops and people found here.Defeat zizani in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The song of the zizani has been quieted for a time.CompletedAs you stand by the blighted pods that were once mushrooms but have now succumbed to the Lâg Dávrit, the Pale Blight, you hear the high-pitched, warbling sound of the insects called zizani.

Is it your imagination that the pods of the blight respond to the sound of their call, shivering and shaking in time to the notes of the insect chorus? You decide to cull any of the zizani you encounter, quieting the song of the harbingers.Through your efforts, the song of the zizani has been quieted for a time. It will be back, but for now you have achieved a measure of peace in a forest of unrest. As you look around you see some of the pods touched by the Lâg Dávrit no longer respond to the insect chorus, and you know your efforts were worthwhile.Inkanûsh and Glorfindel have come to speak with Cúridwan, but as the sun sets upon Imhûlar they are not the only visitors to Kûgrun.Glorfindel has spotted enemies ahead.Talk to Glorfindel on the slope that rises to KûgrunInkanûsh stands outside the entrance to Cúridwan's home of Kûgrun.Talk to Inkanûsh outside the entrance to Cúridwan's home of KûgrunThe disturbed vines reveal that some of the Orcs have already entered Kûgrun.Pass through the vines and enter KûgrunOrcs and jackals have come to threaten Cúridwan, and must be cleared from Kûgrun.Defeat all the Orcs and jackals threatening KûgrunSomeone hid from the Orcs behind Cúridwan's dwelling.Talk to the one who hid from the Orcs behind Cúridwan's dwellingInkanûsh and Glorfindel have come to Kûgrun in search of Cúridwan.Learn from Usakâr what has happened to CúridwanUsakâr the Hermit may know what happened to Cúridwan.Talk to Usakâr the Hermit in KûgrunListen to the conversation between the Wizard, the Elf, and the hermit in Kûgrun.Listen to the conversation in Kûgrun'It is true. That was my favourite hat, but Luhum-gêna kept stealing it right off my head. Not only that, but no matter how often I think she is slain, Luhum-gêna somehow keeps coming back. Eventually I decided to just let her keep it.''There are Orcs on the slope above us. I think they remain unaware of our presence, which grants to us the advantage. Shall we put an end to their menace?''I fear some of the Orcs may have already passed into Kûgrun. Even now the life of Cúridwan may be in jeopardy!

'Glorfindel and I will ensure it is safe out here, and we will join you within when we can. Go now, and hurry!''You are not an Orc, are you? No, you are not. That is a relief to me! It must be a relief to you as well, yes?

'If you are here to dine with Cúridwan, as I am, I must tell you that she is no longer here. Luhum-gêna was quite disappointed to learn of our friend's absence, as was I, but perhaps it is just as well. Neither of us expected the Orcs to find the hidden way into Kûgrun! Someone with more in the way of brains must have helped them find it, but they are not welcome.''I cannot say for certain, but when last we spoke Cúridwan had crafted a salve that could help fight against the Lâg Dávrit, the blight tainting the woods hereabout. She expressed a desire to bring it not only to the grove here in Imhûlar, but also to those further east, in Adagím. "I must do this, Usakâr," she said to me. "What is the hurry?" I responded, but there must have been some need for haste after all.

'She has always been a friend to the Kintai, and if she possesses a means of curing their dying forest, she would share it without question. She has a big heart, our Cúridwan. Often she speaks of her purpose in these lands: to heal them, and to fortify them against evil.

'She would not appreciate these Orcs in her home, I know that! She must have gone already to Adagím, and missed their arrival entirely!'Irsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Irsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Listen to IrsinithIrsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Speak with IrsinithIrsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Listen to IrsinithIrsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Speak with IrsinithIrsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Listen to IrsinithIrsinith wishes to tell you more about Amûma and her duty as her warden.Speak with Irsinith'As you can see with your own eyes, this Pale Blight is rotting these lands.

'The bark of Amûma herself is festering with fungal blooms and weeping poison from her boughs.

'As I have said, her roots now feed the blight as it ravages all life here. My duty as her warden is to commune with her and bring her words to my people as well as speak with the forest.'

Irsinith sighs.

'Even if a cure or defence against this blight is found. I fear Amûma may be past saving. Her words to me are weak and filled with anger.''The trees here are covered in this rot. I can watch as they twist to the infection inside them.

'The foul fungus that grows on them is spreading quickly.

'It seems almost unbelievable how fast they are consumed by the blight. But that is why I know in my heart that Amûma herself is succumbing to it.''Look out over this land, ${PLAYER}.

'The ground is covered in a blanket of poison and fungus. Our waters turned murky and vile.'

Irsinith pauses a moment as tears form in her eyes.

'My duty... to this land.. and to Amûma. Is to protect them and hear their words. I have failed.

'This blight has taken so much from me and I feel myself sickly as a result. I feel powerless as I watch my lands wither and die.

'I am sorry. Give me but a moment to compose myself.'It has not taken long for Mithrandir, now using the name Inkanûsh, to involve himself in the affairs of Umbar and Shagâna.A commotion at the nearby gate has drawn your attention.Investigate the commotion at the gate with InkanûshThe guardsmen at the gate have stopped a group of warriors from entering the city.Observe the confrontation at the gate and be ready to intervene if it comes to violenceAn Umbari guardsman has asked you to speak on behalf of Inkanûsh.Talk to the Umbari guardsmanYou have helped to ease some of the tension between the two groups at the gate, but you should remain on guard just in case.Stand ready to prevent the outbreak of violenceHármelak has come to the gate, and she has questions for you.Talk to Hármelak at the gateYou and Inkanûsh have been invited to attend the meeting of the Kindred of the Coins with Shâra Mizâdi within the Citadel of Winds.Enter the Citadel of the Winds as a guest of the KindredRahâzi has stayed behind to speak with you in the Citadel of Winds.Talk to Rahâzi in the Citadel of WindsThe Kindred of the Coins meets with Shâra Mizâdi of Renewed Hamât to discuss the relationship between their two powers and the days to come.Attend the meeting of the Kindred of the Coins and Shâra Mizâdi of Renewed HamâtShâra Mizâdi desires to speak with you before the attendees disperse.Talk to Shâra Mizâdi in the Citadel of WindsHármelak has indicated that she wants to speak with you before you leave.Talk to Hármelak in the Citadel of Winds'Well? Who is this old man who speaks so to Umbari guardsmen?'

You explain that Inkanûsh is a Wizard both powerful and wise, and he is a friend to Umbar. The guard looks from you to the Wizard and back again.

'If you vouch for this man, ${PLAYER}, then I believe he is as you say. I have heard of your deeds on behalf of Umbar Baharbêl.''It is fortunate that his wisdom aligns with mine, or I would disregard his opinion on the subject. But it is not seemly to leave foreign dignitaries on the doorstep.

'Shâra Mizâdi and her assemblage will enjoy the comforts of the Citadel of Winds while I summon the rest of the Kindred of the Coins. I invite both you and your Wizard to attend our council as well, ${PLAYER}. These matters affect us all.''It is good to see you again, ${PLAYER}! My mother Girhâzi asked that I join Shâra Mizâdi's forces to oppose the Empire of Ordâkh and keep safe my homeland of Ambarûl, and so I have done. Already there has been fighting! The Battle for Hamât ended in victory, of a sort, but the villain Bârshud escaped with his life.

'Shâra Mizâdi gifted me the blade of one of her Subuzûri, and I will wield it on her behalf for the good of Renewed Hamât, and for the protection of Ambarûl and Iridír!

'But we should not delay. The council may have already started!''You and your companions are of course welcome to accompany my forces as we move south in pursuit of Bârshud and his Ordâkhai! You have proven yourself a mighty ally already, ${PLAYER}, and have lent me aid and support beyond my most hopeful expectations.

'I know little of Wizards, but if your friend has powers to lend to my cause, I invite him to use them openly and as he will!

'Renewed Hamât welcomes you and your companions. My army will march south from the Ikorbân Valley and into Mûr Ghala, the Ridge of Plenty. We will put an end to Bârshud and prevent the Ordâkhai from ever again threatening our lands.''I know what your Wizard seeks, ${PLAYER}. He believes these ancient creatures, these Thardúth, remain a danger to us. I have heard the report of Kinsman Belondor's soldiers about the fighting at Utug-bûr, and to me it sounds like madness: the ravings of frightened men caught in the middle of a battle they cannot understand. Azagath perished at the hands of the Thardúth, and will not emerge from the darkness of Utug-bûr, but so too did those terrible creatures... is it not so? Who is it that remains to endanger us?

'Ordâkh remains, ${PLAYER}. This has always been the true threat, not the ancient demons from which your Wizard would have us cower. This is why Umbar will support Mizâdi's pursuit of her foe south across the Ikorbân and into Mûr Ghala. We are allies in this, for Ordâkh threatens us all.'Your renown grows among the Kintai of Sul Madásh.You honour the Kintai and assist them with the efforts to preserve Adagím. You should return to their settlement of Sul Madásh to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Kindred standing with the Kintai of Sul MadáshYou honour the Kintai and assist them with the efforts to preserve Adagím. You should return to their settlement of Sul Madásh to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Aralath at Sul MadáshYour renown grows among the Kintai of Sul Madásh. Return now to the quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'Your stand against Sívigush is already woven into tale, and shall not be soon forgotten. In token of our enduring thanks, I have laid aside gifts. Choose as your heart inclines, and know that our gratitude shall outlast the telling.'Lurkers of all sizes threaten travellers in the blighted wood of Adagím.Pods of the blight draw your attention in the woods of Adagím.Examine the pods of the blight in AdagímIt is possible that fen-lurkers and rats help spread the Pale Blight throughout Adagím.Defeat fen-lurkers in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat rats in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You defeated fen-lurkers and rats in Adagím, hoping to slow the spread of the Pale Blight.CompletedAs you stand by these pods of the blight, you hear the chittering of rats in the underbrush and wonder at the spread of the Lâg Dávrit, the Pale Blight. Can it be that such creatures spread the pestilence that troubles this wood? You resolve to defeat lurkers of all sizes and combat the movement of the blight through the forest.You have defeated lurkers both large and small in the blighted woods of Adagím, and you hope this effort will slow the spread of the Lâg Dávrit.Your renown grows among the Kintai of Sul Madásh.You honour the Kintai and assist them with the efforts to preserve Adagím. You should return to their settlement of Sul Madásh to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Respected standing with the Kintai of Sul MadáshYou honour the Kintai and assist them with the efforts to preserve Adagím. You should return to their settlement of Sul Madásh to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Aralath at Sul MadáshYour renown grows among the Kintai of Sul Madásh. Return now to the quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'Your deeds are spoken of with honour, ${PLAYER}, and your name spoken with respect. You are beyond kin; rather, a guardian of the green places. A gift has been set aside in your honour; accept it now, with our blessing.'Bog-lurkers to the south-east of Laivárth seem to be resistant to the blight. Bring Tálbiral their shells for him to inspect. Be sure to defeat any wood-trolls you encounter in the area as well.Bog-lurkers to the south-east of Laivárth seem to be resistant to the blight. Bring Tálbiral their shells for him to inspect. Be sure to defeat any wood-trolls you encounter in the area as well.Defeat Adagím wood-trolls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bog-lurker carapaces (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bring the gathered bog-lurker carapaces back to Tálbiral in Laivárth so that he can inspect them for signs of the blight.'There are creatures in the marsh that appear to resist the blight, and I wish to learn more about them.

'The bog-lurkers in the waters to the south-east have the fungal rot growing on them, but they seem to not be succumbing to the rot like the trees and beasts around them. Perhaps a secret to stopping the spread lies within them. If you will, I ask that you gather their carapaces for me to study.

'Be wary, ${CLASS}, as wood-trolls have been spotted in the area as well.''These carapaces will do nicely. I am glad to hear you were not harmed by the wood-trolls in the marsh.'

Tálbiral looks over the carapaces a bit more.

'So few signs of rot in these shells. What is giving them this resistance? Or are they just succumbing slower than the earth?

'I must look for answers. Thank you for helping me in this task, ${CLASS}.'Tálbiral is in need of new mushrooms to study and asked you to gather some for him. He mentioned the ones he needs can be found just outside of Laivárth.Tálbiral is in need of new mushrooms to study and asked you to gather some for him. He mentioned the ones he needs can be found just outside of Laivárth.Gather blighted mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered the mushrooms that Tálbiral requested. Return to him in Laivárth so that he can begin his studies.'The earth here grows sicker with each passing day. I am in need of some fresh mushrooms to study to see how they are being affected by the blight.

'You can find them in the area near the grove. Gather a few samples for me and bring them back. I fear the rot is growing closer.''Ah yes! These will work wonderfully. I can already tell the rot has seeped deep within them, and for that my concern grows.

'I will study them further. Thank you for your help my friend.'Tálbiral needs help gathering flowers and berries near Laivárth so that he can check how the blight is affecting them as well as recovering the missing pages of his notebook.Tálbiral needs help gathering flowers and berries near Laivárth so that he can check how the blight is affecting them as well as recovering the missing pages of his notebook.Gather rotting flowers (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather rotting berries (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find missing pagesFind missing pagesFind missing pagesBring the gathered flowers, berries, and missing notebook pages back to Tálbiral in Laivárth.'${CLASS}, I am in need of some supplies from outside of the grove.

'There are flowers and berries that can be found nearby. Bring me some of them so I can check on how they are withstanding the blight.

'I also ask that you look for my notebook while you are in the swamp. I fear I left it there some time ago... and have use of it again. The pages may have been scattered over time.''Ah, you have returned. Did you manage to gather what I asked?'

You give Tálbiral the gathered flowers, berries, and missing pages from his notebook.

'This is worse than I thought. The rot has consumed these plants entirely. Thank you for gathering the missing pages from my notes. These will be of great use as I look into how this blight has become more aggressive.

'Please, take this for your troubles.'Irsinith has asked that you inspect Amûma and see how the blight is rotting her roots.Irsinith has asked that you inspect Amûma and see how the blight is rotting her roots.Inspect Amûma's roots (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})After inspecting the roots of Amûma, you discover the severe signs of blight that Irsinith spoke of corrupting the tree.Return to Irsinith'Welcome, ${CLASS}. Amûma bless you.

'Amûma is succumbing to this blight, and as her warden I feel helpless. It seems nothing that I do is preventing the rot from seeping deeper.

'Please, take a look at her roots yourself and see what I see.''Now you see what I see, ${CLASS}.

'Amûma weakens by the day, her roots rot, and I fear that now she herself is contributing to the spread of this blight within Adagím.

'I thank you for taking the time. There is more I wish to show you. Please return to me when you have the time, my friend.'Tálbiral has asked that you raze the ground surrounding Laivárth and any blighted saplings you encounter to prevent the spread from getting closer to the grove.Tálbiral has asked that you raze the ground surrounding Laivárth and any blighted saplings you encounter to prevent the spread from getting closer to the grove.Burn saplings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Raze ground (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Tálbiral will want to know that you successfully removed the saplings and razed the ground again. Return to him in Laivárth.'The blight continues to seep into the grove. I need your help, ${CLASS}.

'Take this torch and this oil. Outside of Laivárth you will see where I have razed the ground before. I ask that you do this again and stop the saplings from growing and spreading their tainted roots through the earth.

'It pains me to hurt the grove this way, but it must be done to prevent the blight from consuming it.''I see that you have returned and that the grounds near Laivárth have been cleansed again. That will allow us more time to do what we can to save the grove.

'I thank you, ${CLASS}. It pains me each time the task must be done. You have spared me of that pain this day.'Samítai has told you how to reach Laivárth in Adagím. You are to speak with Tálbiral when you arrive at the grove.Samítai has told you how to reach Laivárth in Adagím to the north-east. You are to speak with Tálbiral when you arrive at the grove.Find LaivárthYou have found Laivárth in Adagím. Find and speak to Tálbiral and let him know that Samítai sent you.Speak with Tálbiral'Be wary, adventurer. If you go much further you will reach the borders of Adagím. The blight runs deep and the land has grown foul here.

'The people here do not see many outsiders and may be wary, but if you wish to venture into the marsh I am confident you will find many who could use your help.

'If you make your way through these marshes to the north-east, you will reach a grove known as Laivárth. When you arrive you can find Tálbiral and he can tell you more of what awaits in Adagím.''Greetings, young ${CLASS}.

'So you have met Samítai during your travels. I am glad to hear that he is well.

'Welcome. This is Laivárth. A grove of the Kintai and I am its tender. I work to protect it from the blight ravaging this land.'Spiders and Huorns in the area are threatening Laivárth. Tálbiral has asked you to thin their numbers and destroy any blighted spider eggs you find.Spiders and Huorns in the area are threatening Laivárth. Tálbiral has asked you to thin their numbers and destroy any blighted spider eggs you find.Kill blighted spiders (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Kill blighted Huorns (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Destroy blighted spider eggs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Tálbiral and inform him that you have reduced the number of spiders and Huorns threatening Laivárth.Return to Tálbiral in Laivárth'The creatures to the north have become more daring in recent days. It has begun to make my efforts here in the grove more difficult.

'I ask that you go and put a stop to the Huorns and spiders that are becoming more aggressive from their blight infections. Be sure to destroy the eggs of the spiders as well.''The grove will now be safer for a time thanks to you and your efforts, ${CLASS}.

'Laivárth still has much work to do, so your help here is appreciated. Thankfully it will now be easier to fight the blight with fewer of those beasts nearby.'The Orcs of nearby Mauthark erected a wooden sign warning their kind away from the moving trees of Adagím.A wooden sign of warning stands among the trees of Adagím.Examine the wooden warning signHuorns of Adagím succumb to the Pale Blight, and you must face them with courage.Defeat the trees that move in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You proved the match of the walking trees of Adagím.CompletedThe crude pictograms on this wooden sign are of Orc design, but their meaning is clear to you: the Orcs of nearby Mauthark are warning others of their kind to avoid the dangerous walking trees of Adagím.

You have some experience avoiding the clutching roots and grabbing branches of fell-hearted Huorns you encountered in distant forests, but those tainted by the Pale Blight of Adagím may be more dangerous than even those. You resolve to remain wary, but if you must fight to defend yourself from them, you will.You proved more than the match of the walking trees of Adagím, and have made this stretch of the blighted wood more safe for the Kintai.

It will also now be safer for the Orcs of Mauthark who erected the warning sign; an unfortunate result, perhaps, but you cannot worry overmuch on that score.At Narchásh, as elsewhere in Middle-earth, folk run afoul of the spiders, losing their lives and their possessions.The spiders of Narchásh snare their victims in cocoons.Examine the cocooned bodyYou have decided to combat the spiders of Narchásh and search for any survivors of their deadly hunger.Defeat spiders at Narchásh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Examine another cocooned body at NarcháshThe nearest grove of the Kintai should be Laivárth, located to the north-east of Narchásh. The Elves there will have a strongbox in which you can deposit the Kinta bracelet.You have left the Kinta bracelet in the strongbox at Laivárth, where it can be retrieved by one of the people to whom it should be entrusted.CompletedYou pry open the webbed cocoon, but the spiders' victim has already succumbed to death. You decide to rid Narchásh of these many-legged killers, and to seek for any survivors.You add the Kinta bracelet you found at Narchásh to the strongbox, leaving it with your wish that it will be claimed by one of the Kintai who will admire its beauty and find meaning in its craftsmanship.Irsinith has asked that you check the marsh near the Hill of the Mother for how the blight has hurt the water and wildlife. She requested you bring her some water samples as well.Irsinith has asked that you check the marsh near the Hill of the Mother for how the blight has hurt the water and wildlife. She requested you bring her some water samples as well.Gather water (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Inspect diseased fishInspect diseased insectsInspect diseased fishReturn to Irisinith with the water you have gathered from the nearby marsh and tell her what you witnessed of the blight's destruction.Bring gathered water to Irsinith'The water and the wildlife around Amûma is poisoned as well.

'Go and check the marsh nearby to the east. See if you can find signs of the blight and see how it has affected the forest. Bring me some of the water if you can. I wish to see how it fares.

'Amûma is meant to protect the land, but you can see for yourself how the rot is affecting her.''Thank you.

'As you can see, the water and animals of the forest have become tainted. The water you gathered is poisoned and carries the blight through the streams. The earth itself is eating away at Adagím.

'Amûma is suffering and these signs show more and more each day that her roots themselves are pushing the rot into every edge of the forest.'Spiders and crocodiles threaten travellers in the blighted woods of Adagím.Pods of the blight draw your attention in the woods of Adagím.Examine the pods of the blight in AdagímCrocodile teeth and venom sacs make the predators within Adagím extremely deadly.Collect teeth from crocodiles in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect venom sacs from spiders in Adagím (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The nearest grove of the Kintai is named Laivárth. The Elves there will have a strongbox in which you can deposit the crocodile teeth and venom sacs you recovered.You have deprived the crocodiles and spiders of Adagím of their deadly weapons.CompletedYou stand by these pods of the blight and are struck by a curious feeling: a realization that the Lâg Dávrit, the Pale Blight, possesses a strange beauty that draws the eye. How many distracted travellers must fall prey to the crocodiles and spiders hiding nearby?

You decide to seize the teeth and venom sacs that are these creatures' most deadly weapons and leave them in the care of the Kintai of Laivárth, the nearby Elf-grove in Adagím.To truly make the forest safe for travellers, something must be done to combat the Pale Blight... but by depriving some of Adagím's deadliest predators of their weapons you have made a start. The Kintai of Laivárth will know how to dispose of the dangerous items you have brought them.Tálbiral has asked that you head south from Laivárth to an Orc camp called Mokh Grazádh and look for signs that they may be spreading the blight.Tálbiral has asked that you head south from Laivárth to an Orc camp called Mokh Grazádh and look for signs that they may be spreading the blight.Reach Mokh GrazádhTálbiral has asked that you head south from Laivárth to an Orc camp called Mokh Grazádh and look for signs that they may be spreading the blight.Kill Orcs in Mokh Grazádh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Inspect Orc supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Tálbiral in Laivárth and let him know what you discovered of the Orcs plans in Mokh Grazádh.Return to Tálbiral in Laivárth'I have spotted Orcs to the south. I fear those foul beasts may be helping to spread this blight through Adagím.

'Go to their camp, I believe it is known as Mokh Grazádh, and find proof of this if you can. They must have supplies or tools to handle this blight. If any Orcs try and stop you, put an end to them.''I welcome your return. What did you discover in Mokh Grazádh?'

You tell Tálbiral of what you found in the camp.

'So there were no signs of the Orcs being the cause of the blight rampaging more in recent days? The source must be elsewhere. I am glad that you investigated and thinned their numbers while you were there. They are still a dangerous force to be left unchecked so close to the grove.'Tálbiral has given you samples of the blight gathered in Laivárth and fears that the grove is succumbing to the blight. Bring the samples to Irsinith at the Hill of the Mother as she may be able to help.Tálbiral has given you samples of the blight gathered in Laivárth and fears that the grove is succumbing to the blight. Bring the samples to Irsinith at the Hill of the Mother as she may be able to help.Travel to the Hill of the MotherYou have arrived at the Hill of the Mother. Speak with Irsinith and show her the blight samples Tálbiral gave you from Laivárth.'I fear my efforts here in Laivárth are futile. The grove appears to be succumbing to the blight in a manner similar to how Amûma has.

'I ask that you take these samples and bring them to Irsinith. She is the warden of the Hill of the Mother and will want to know that the blight has spread deeper into the grove.

'You may also be able to help her as you have helped me, ${CLASS}.''Oh, hello there. You say Tálbiral sent you?'

You give the blight samples to Irsinith and explain that Tálbiral is in need of help. She inspects the sample.

'I fear that it is as Tálbiral thought. The blight is deep in the roots of Laivárth, such as it is here. I do not know what can be done as I have tried everything to help Amûma to no avail. Unless we can stop the spread of this rot, all of Adagím will soon succumb to it.

'I am sorry.'Bíralad wants to treat the scouts of Sâr Marsag to a new meal and has asked you to gather crocodile meat and mushrooms from the Lair of the Shadlúgai to the north-east.Bíralad wants to treat the scouts of Sâr Marsag to a new meal and has asked you to gather crocodile meat and mushrooms from the Lair of the Shadlúgai to the north-east.Gather "safe" mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Obtain mildly blighted crocodile meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered mushrooms and crocodile meat that Bíralad needed for his recipe from the Lair of the Shadlúgai. Return to him in Sâr Marsag.'Adventurer! I need your help to make a treat for the camp.

'I grow tired of making the same lousy meals every day, but I concocted a recipe that will surely spice things up. I just need some help to gather the ingredients.'

Bíralad pulls out a scrap of paper with notes scribbled on it.

'I need you to get me some crocodile meat from the beasts up north-east in the marshlands we call the Lair of the Shadlúgai. I know they are rotten with the pale blight, but I am certain I can cook that right out and it will add a nice flavour to it. Can you also get me some mushrooms from the forest? Try and find the safe ones that have managed to escape the blight... I shouldn't add more of that than will already be in the meat.''${CLASS}! You are back!

'I trust that your task has been a success?'

You give Bíralad the meat and mushrooms you gathered.

'This is exactly what I needed, friend. You have my thanks!

'I will get started cooking the rot right out of these. I will be sure to save you a bowl for when you return. The camp is sure to love the variety to their meals.'Mírthar is beginning to succumb to the blight and needs you to gather nearby medicinal herbs and mushrooms to make a healing concoction that can give him the strength he needs to get to Sul Madásh.Mírthar is beginning to succumb to the blight and needs you to gather nearby medicinal herbs and mushrooms to make a healing concoction that can give him the strength he needs to get to Sul Madásh.Gather medicinal herbs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather medicinal mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered the supplies that Mírthar requested. Return them to him so he can craft the needed concoction.'Please... I need your help....'

Mírthar coughs painfully.

'The blight is beginning to take me... I do not wish to go. I need herbs and mushrooms from nearby so that I can make a healing concoction that can get me to Sul Madásh.''These will work well for what I need.'

Mírthar takes the supplies.

'This will hopefully give me the strength I need to make it.'Lóburdith has helped you complete the healing concoction needed to save Mírthar. Hurry back and give him the medicine.Lóburdith has helped you complete the healing concoction needed to save Mírthar. Hurry back and give him the medicine.Return to Mírthar with the healing concoctionMírthar's body appears lifeless when you arrived with the healing concoction. Inspect him to learn any more that you can.Inspect MírtharYou found Mírthar's scholar's mark when inspecting him. Bring it to the scholar Serâna in Sul Madásh, she would want to know of his fate.'Here is the concoction. Please, make haste. I do not know how much longer he can hang on.'

Lóburdith gives you the healing concoction.

'Make sure Mírthar drinks it all and then he should be able to walk enough to make it here where I can treat him properly.''Greetings. What bring you to Sul Madásh?'

You show the mark you got from Mírthar to Serâna and explain what happened to him.

'I am terribly sorry to hear of his passing. Mírthar was a valued friend and scholar. I will be sure to make sure we can recover his body and give him a proper burial.

'Thank you for all you did and I am grateful you told me of his fate. I am sorry this was the outcome.'In the aftermath of the Utûgi raid on Sul Madásh, Cúridwan has surprising news to share with the Kintai.Ulishár is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Ulishár in Sul MadáshCúridwan is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul MadáshA hush has fallen over Sul Madásh.A hush has fallen over Sul MadáshUlishár is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Ulishár in Sul MadáshInkanûsh is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul MadáshCúridwan is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul Madásh<rgb=#00FF00>Sing</rgb> or <rgb=#00FF00>mourn</rgb> in your own way.<rgb=#00FF00>Sing</rgb> or <rgb=#00FF00>mourn</rgb> in your own wayUlishár is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Ulishár in Sul MadáshGlorfindel is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Glorfindel in Sul MadáshCúridwan is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul MadáshSigileth is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Sigileth in Sul MadáshInkanûsh is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul Madásh'Many of the Kintai I have known so loved their lands they sought to remain there beyond death, fading from all but memory; their Veróthi remain to comfort those they leave behind. This has been the tradition of the Tárthai ever since they chose not to follow the one they call Taknûn the Hunter on the great journey westward. They feared what lay at the end of that road, and preferred to remain in the lands they knew and had come to love.

'That was their choice, but none commanded the choice must be forever.''That was their choice, but none commanded the choice must be forever.''I do not understand your meaning, Cúridwan! Can you not speak more clearly, in remembrance of the friendship we once shared?

'What does the choice of the Kintai so many years ago have to do with poor slain Baharúd?''Can you not speak more clearly, in remembrance of the friendship we once shared?''It is the memory of our friendship that compels me to ease your heart, dear Ulishár, if it is in my power to do so! But I can speak no more clearly than this.'

Cúridwan extends her arms to encompass all the Elves that stand nearby: Sigileth, Corudan, Glorfindel, and the sorrowful Kintai watchers.

'The Elves were fashioned as one people, not as two. The west road is not closed to the Kintai, though they followed it not when it was offered. Should a Kinta set aside his fear of the unknown, the fate given to all the Elves could be his as well.''Even if that were so...'

Ulishár stares sadly at the body of his brother Baharúd.

'Could one of the Kintai be welcomed in that distant place that lies beyond death? Or would he be considered a stranger and turned aside?

'We have been so long apart. What could this place hold for a Kinta save fear and resentment?''Could one of the Kintai be welcomed in that distant place that lies beyond death?''Set aside these doubts, Ulishár, if you can. I know such words come more easily to the speaker than to he who hears them! And yet I ask you to heed them all the same, for I have cause to believe what Cúridwan has spoken today.

'Many of the Elven-wise dwell in the Blessed Realm beyond the circles of the world; they return not to Middle-earth. In the Undying Lands all weariness falls away, and there the warmth of the sunrise banishes all earthly concerns: pain, fear, and doubt have no hold upon that place, and those who dwell within.

'It is natural to fear that which you have not seen, Ulishár. But I believe Cúridwan has spoken true: the Eldar and the Kintai were fashioned as a single people, no matter the separate roads down which they walked afterward. Your people are a part of Elvendom, as is Glorfindel and these others. I believe the Kintai could share their fate, if they desired it.''It is natural to fear that which you have not seen, Ulishár. But I believe Cúridwan has spoken true.''I know not what your brother would choose for himself, Ulishár. None but Baharúd can know if he would choose to remain in this land he loved or if he would accept the fate given to Elvendom at its creation.

'We must all mourn him in our own way, but if you sing or speak of this choice, it may be he will hear you. The long traditions of the Kintai may have hidden this possibility from him, but they could not have taken it from him entirely.''Perhaps I am being foolish, but I spoke to my brother of this choice. I spoke of something I never considered before today, something I do not believe myself. Can such a land of peace and beauty truly exist beyond death? And why would such a place be open to my people, who were unwilling to follow Taknûn the Hunter to it when he sought to bring us there so many centuries before?

'Is it not better to remain in the land with which you are familiar, even if your memories fade and all that remains is the anger and disappointment of the Väiri? For all of my life I have been told this is the way of my people. I do not know what to think, ${PLAYER}.''For all of my life I have been told this is the way of my people. I know not what to think.''On this subject my lips are sealed, ${PLAYER}. Ulishár and Baharúd lived their lives among the Kintai, and I will not speak against the traditions of their people.

'And yet in my heart I feel it is wrong to say "their" people. Perhaps they are truly "my" people after all, though long divided by time and tradition.''The choice lies with Baharúd.''Never before have I sought to interfere with the traditions of the Kintai. What has happened to work this change in me, ${PLAYER}?

'I ask despite knowing the answer. It is the blight that holds Adagím in its grip that causes me to speak these words aloud! Rarely did I speak with Baharúd beyond the exchange of simple greetings and well-wishing, but some years ago his brother Ulishár was dear to me. It grieves me to see Ulishár's sorrow that his brother's spirit must remain trapped within blighted Adagím, when I know it might be otherwise.

'If the Sorceress in Green here remained, she might already have restored the Emerald Wood! But of her I have received no word.''If the Sorceress in Green here remained, she might already have restored the Emerald Wood! But of her I have received no word.''This is what I have been seeking, ${PLAYER}.'

Sigileth turns to face you, and you see tears in her eyes. The Elf-warrior seems not to notice them.

'I must find the Väiri that was once Silirtas. I must tell her that the westward way to the Undying Lands is open to her. She is not doomed to the endless unlife of a wandering, rageful spirit, but can choose to accept the fate of the Eldar and pass into the Blessed Realm!

'This is my purpose for coming to this place, ${PLAYER}. I know it. I must bring these tidings to Silirtas, no matter how difficult or dangerous that task may be.''This is my purpose for coming to this place, ${PLAYER}. I know it. I must bring these tidings to Silirtas, no matter how difficult or dangerous that task may be.''Our visit to Sul Madásh has been an eventful one, but our work here is not yet done. Some of the Utûgi fled back into Adagím. We must track them down before we continue south to join with Mizâdi's army.'Inkanûsh asked you to pursue the Utûgi raiders before they cause further harm to the Kintai of Adagím.The Utûgi escaped to the north-west. That way lies the Hill of the Mother!Follow the escaping Utûgi north-west to the Hill of the MotherOne of the Utûgi succumbed to its wounds not far from the Hill of the Mother.Examine the corpse of the dead Utûgi warriorIrsinith may have seen what befell the Utûgi raiding-party as it approached the Hill of the Mother.Talk to Irsinith on the other side of Amûma at the Hill of the MotherThe remaining Utûgi warriors escaped to the north-west, heading for the grove of Laivárth.Find the fleeing Utûgi warriors near LaivárthThe other Utûgi warriors perished outside the grove of Laivárth.Examine the corpse outside Laivárth... and surviveSince you have returned to the grove of Laivárth, you should speak to Shadalith and see if everything is all right.Talk to Shadalith in LaivárthShadalith wanted you to see what happened to her bucket in Laivárth.Examine Shadalith's bucket in LaivárthShadalith is in the grove of Laivárth.Talk to Shadalith in LaivárthInkanûsh is waiting in Sul Madásh for you to return with news of the raiding-party of Utûgi.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul Madásh'The surviving Utûgi fled from Sul Madásh in the direction of the Hill of the Mother. Blighted though she be, I fear the damage such creatures might do to the old tree in their wrath.

'Hurry there and ensure they inflict no harm upon her, ${PLAYER}.''Hurry to the Hill of the Mother and ensure the fleeing Utûgi inflict no harm upon her!''Terrible creatures with the mien of Orcs approached the Hill of the Mother not long ago.''A party of great brutish Orcs approached the Hill of the Mother not long ago, shouting harsh cries and moving at great speed. I hid behind Amûma for fear they would discover me, but before they espied me they were discovered by something else!'

Irsinith smiles grimly.

'A mass of spiders boiled from the underbrush and fell upon the Orcs. The one you saw perished immediately; the rest of the raiding-party escaped further to the west. It is possible they might encounter Laivárth, but I suspect their preoccupation with the attacking spiders may hide the grove from their discovery.

'It is said that a terrifying spider dwells somewhere within the forest, Badharál the Gorger by name. I never believed I would be glad to see the arrival of Badharál's spawn, but on this day I must confess I was!

'Still, I am glad they did not turn their attention upon me. Perhaps Amûma shielded me from discovery.''The rest of the raiding-party escaped further to the west, in the direction of Laivárth.''Ah, my friend! It is good to see you again! I was hoping you would return to Laivárth so I could tell you what I have learned from my experimentation with the samples of the blight you brought to me.'

Shadalith pauses dramatically.

'The blight hates buckets! See for yourself!''The blight samples you brought to me have had an adverse effect upon my bucket!''The bucket fell apart a short time after you left, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps the samples of the blight interacted with the water in a curious way? The combination seems to have undone the sealings between the slats of my bucket... and now I need a new bucket. I may have learned something, but what I have learned remains a mystery to me. Such is the way of wisdom, I suppose.

'You need not worry about my problems, friend. Judging from the sounds of battle I heard before you arrived, I deem that you have plenty of your own! Thank you for visiting Laivárth again and checking in on me! Return to Sul Madásh with my friendship.''Thank you for visiting Laivárth again and checking in on me! Return to Sul Madásh with my friendship.''You have accounted for all of the fleeing Utûgi raiders? Good, good, good. Then it should be safe for us to continue southward into Kighân without fear that we leave Sul Madásh in peril if they were to return.

'I have enlisted the aid of a guide to show us the way further south into Mûr Ghala. You have already met him, ${PLAYER}. His name is Ulishár.'The Kinta grove of Sul Madásh remains a bastion of health and beauty among the dead or dying trees of Adagím. Did Cúridwan come this way?Sul Madásh lies to the south-east of the Hill of the Mother, in Adagím.Travel to the southern entrance of Sul MadáshThe Kinta guardsman Baharúd stands at the southern entrance to Sul Madásh.Talk to Baharúd at the southern entrance to Sul MadáshInkanûsh stands at the southern entrance to Sul Madásh.Talk to Inkanûsh at the southern entrance to Sul MadáshKintai within the grove of Sul Madásh may know of recent visitors.Talk to a Kinta of Sul Madásh north-east of the entranceKintai within the grove of Sul Madásh may know of recent visitors.Talk to another Kinta of Sul Madásh further into the groveKintai within Sul Madásh told you that the Kinta Ulishár might know of Cúridwan's whereabouts.Find Ulishár to the eastCorudan stands in the centre of Sul Madásh.Talk to Corudan in Sul MadáshThe Kinta Ulishár has overheard your conversation and might have some advice for you.Talk to Ulishár in Sul Madásh'The path to the south-east will bring us to Sul Madásh. Let us move now with haste, but keep in our hearts the memory of the hurt done to the Old Mother Amûma. Should we be given the opportunity to avenge that hurt, I would have us do it.

'But for now our road leads to Sul Madásh, and Cúridwan.''The path to the south-east will bring us to Sul Madásh.''The interesting day continues! I welcome you to Sul Madásh as I welcomed the others. So many visitors after days of so few have proven the perfect curative for the hours of tedium I usually face!

'Harm not the grove, and the Kintai will treat you well.''Harm not the grove, and the Kintai will treat you well.''So many visitors, he says! Now these are promising tidings, ${PLAYER}. Let us speak with Kintai throughout Sul Madásh and they will guide us to Cúridwan.''Let us speak with Kintai throughout Sul Madásh and they will guide us to Cúridwan.''The woman Cúridwan? I have not seen her today.''The woman Cúridwan? I have not seen her today.''Cúridwan? She has long been a friend of the Kintai, and has worked tirelessly to help us preserve our groves. I have not seen her today, but years ago Ulishár was a good friend of the Golden Maiden, before he...

'Never mind. You should be able to find Ulishár to the east.''Ulishár was a good friend of Cúridwan. You should be able to find him to the east.''I should have known your road would lead this way, ${PLAYER}!

'My sister and I came here to speak with the Kintai; she searches for some means of freeing Silirtas from the cruel fate inflicted upon her by Azagath. In my heart I feel there can be no such release, but Sigileth is so certain...

'She speaks now in conference with one of the elders of Sul Madásh, but if there were a way to free the wrathful Väiri from their fate as wandering spirits, surely the Kintai would know of it.'

You ask your friend if he has seen Cúridwan somewhere within Sul Madásh. He shakes his head.'I have not seen Cúridwan since we came here to Adagím, I am sorry.''Have I heard you aright? You seek Cúridwan?'Cúridwan came to Adagím, but she never reached Sul Madásh. Where wandered the Golden Maiden?Baharúd is at the southern entrance to Sul Madásh.Talk to Baharúd at the entrance to Sul MadáshBaharúd agreed to travel south-east of Sul Madásh and to <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> with you for Cúridwan.Baharúd is in the swamps south-east of Sul Madásh in Adagím.Talk to Baharúd in the swamps south-east of Sul MadáshBaharúd suggested that you travel west through the swamps to continue your search.Travel west through the swamps to continue your search for CúridwanBaharúd is in the swamps west of Sul Madásh, in Adagím.Talk to Baharúd in the swamps west of Sul MadáshYour search for Cúridwan now leads south-west to the Elderwell.Travel south-west to the Elderwell in search of CúridwanCúridwan is at the Elderwell in Adagím.Talk to Cúridwan at the ElderwellCúridwan asked you to collect crocodile teeth and samples of the blight north-west of the Elderwell.Collect crocodile teeth north-west of the Elderwell (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect samples from blight pustules north-west of the Elderwell (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Cúridwan is waiting at the Elderwell for you to return with the things she requested.Bring the crocodile teeth and blight samples to Cúridwan at the ElderwellBaharúd is at the Elderwell in Adagím.Baharúd believes that Cúridwan may have gone to Sul Madásh. You hope he is right!Return to Sul Madásh and look for CúridwanSigileth has finished speaking with the elders of Sul Madásh.Talk to Sigileth in Sul Madásh'When I was younger, I was given to wandering. I met Cúridwan on one of these meanders, far from Sul Madásh, and she and I became dear friends. When the Ordâkhai came north and the pestilence was born in Adagím, she and I sought to prevent its spread. A few years ago I strayed too far in search of a cure, and... it is not important. I will say only that my wandering days were ended.'

Ulishár frowns.

'If Cúridwan came to these woods but avoided Sul Madásh, she must have had some reason. I am no adventurer, and would rather stay within these bounds, but my brother Baharúd would no doubt enjoy the search. Ask him if he will accompany you! You will find him at the entrance of the grove to the south-west.''If Cúridwan came to these woods but avoided Sul Madásh, she must have had some reason. I am no adventurer, and would rather stay within these bounds, but my brother Baharúd would no doubt enjoy the search. Ask him if he will accompany you! You will find him at the entrance to the grove to the south-west.''Ulishár thought I might be interested in accompanying you to search for Cúridwan? My brother is most considerate! While he desires not to leave the grove unless he must, I feel quite the opposite.

'If he is correct that Cúridwan may have skirted Sul Madásh, there is no need for us to look for her within view of the grove. We should travel south-east and see if we can pick up her trail in the further reaches. While we walk you can tell me about your adventures, for I judge that you have had a number of them!''If my brother is correct that Cúridwan may have skirted Sul Madásh, there is no need for us to look for her within view of the grove. We should travel south-east and see if we can pick up her trail in the further reaches. While we walk you can tell me about your adventures, for I judge that you have had a number of them!'Baharúd gazes away into the distance.'We should not go this way without allies beside us, ${PLAYER}. Some evil lurks in the south-eastern reaches of Adagím, and I fear its power outstrips our own. Orcs have taken up residence in that place, and I think they cannot be the most fearsome of the creatures who dwell there. Let us look elsewhere for Cúridwan.

'My brother and Cúridwan strolled often in these woods. Perhaps she has gone to one of their old haunts? Ever since Ulishár returned from his captivity, he has not felt comfortable ranging from Sul Madásh; otherwise I would ask him to show us where he and the Golden Maiden once walked.'

Baharúd falls silent.

'My brother does not like to speak of it. I should not have said anything. Let us make our way through the swamps to the west and continue our search.''Let us make our way through the swamp to the north-west and continue our search.'Baharúd stands among the blighted trees, deep in thought.'I have done you a disservice, ${PLAYER}. I should not have spoken of my brother if I could not tell you all.

'Ulishár once took great joy in wandering far across the lands of Shagâna, west to the shores of Umbar and south to the fringe of the great desert of Gûl Sakhâsha. He would come back with tales of his adventures, much like the ones I believe you have had!

'Several years ago, he was captured by soldiers of Ordâkh. They imprisoned him in one of their cities, where he was made to labour. The watch on him lapsed as the armies of Ordâkh assembled and marched north to war, and a few months past he escaped and crossed the Mûr. Ever since he returned, he has been unwilling to leave the bounds of Sul Madásh.

'Now I have told you all, and I ask that you please speak not of this to Ulishár. Come, our destination lies to the south-west: the Elderwell. I remember Ulishár used to visit this place with Cúridwan, in happier times.''Come, our destination lies to the south-west: the Elderwell. I remember Ulishár used to visit this place with Cúridwan, in happier times.'You have found Cúridwan!'I had hoped my salve could put an end to the Lâg Dávrit, the Pale Blight, but alas! It is not enough. The corruption birthed beneath Adagím fights against each of the arts I bring to bear against it, and the Pale Blight is a symptom only; so too are the fungal blossoms that spring all about us. I must strengthen my salve, and for that I need to learn more.

'I spied crocodiles to the north-west as I climbed to this place. Collect their teeth and some samples of the blight near which they live, and bring them to me for study. My mind is awhirl with the possibility that this corruption can be beaten and its harm undone.''I spied crocodiles to the north-west as I climbed to this place. Collect their teeth and some samples of the blight near which they live, and bring them to me for study. My mind is awhirl with the possibility that this corruption can be beaten and its harm undone.'Baharúd stares around in disbelief.'She is gone? Did she not say she would wait here for us to return? I did not really know Cúridwan as well as my brother Ulishár did, but I was not aware her head was always floating away among the clouds! Have you known anyone else like that, ${PLAYER}? And if you have, how do you put up with it?

'Let us hope she went to Sul Madásh. I am not eager to chase her throughout Adagím any longer!''Perhaps Cúridwan returned to Sul Madásh. I am not eager to chase her throughout Adagím any longer!'You have found Sigileth again!'Corudan told me you had come to Adagím, ${PLAYER}. I am glad to see you, but I hope you have better fortune on your errand than I have had on mine.'You have finally managed to track down Cúridwan. If fortune smiles, she will have the answers to Inkanûsh's questions.Inkanûsh is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul MadáshInkanûsh is in Sul Madásh and ready to speak to Cúridwan.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul Madásh and go with him to speak to CúridwanThe Utûgi have been driven away from Sul Madásh, but they leave sorrow in their wake.Talk to Ulishár in Sul MadáshThe Utûgi have been driven away from Sul Madásh, but they leave sorrow in their wake.Talk to Sigileth in Sul MadáshThe Utûgi have been driven away from Sul Madásh, but they leave sorrow in their wake.Her words hang on the airCúridwan stands in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul Madásh and ask that she explain herself'The Kinta elders of this grove have given me no means of freeing Silirtas from her fate. They believe this to be an unyielding truth: that a Kinta slain in battle is resigned to wander the land as one of the Väiri, a spirit of wrath and hatred. I refuse to believe it.'

Sigileth grins without humour.

'They have not taken to me very well, ${PLAYER}.''What has brought you to Adagím, ${PLAYER}?''Cúridwan has returned at last to the grove. Will you come with me to speak with her? I have questions to ask. May she be forthright in answering!''Then let us speak with Cúridwan before she wanders again into the forest!''Baharúd! Baharúd, I am sorry! I should have fought!

'My brother... I am sorry!'Ulishár weeps for his brother, and you go unnoticed.'Curse the Thardúth for senseless slaughter! How many lives must their thralls destroy before it will be enough? Must this Kinta share the fate of Silirtas? 

'It is not fair! How can such a thing be? How many Kintai must be doomed to become wandering, wrathful Väiri, unable to find rest?''It need not be the lot of this Kinta to remain unless he wishes it to be. Know you if that be his wish?'Ráthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Ráthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Find potential rot in Sul MadáshRáthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Gather blight samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ráthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Find potential rot in Sul MadáshRáthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Gather blight samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ráthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Find potential rot in Sul MadáshRáthwil fears the blight is seeping into Sul Madásh more than the people know. She has asked you to investigate areas around the settlement and gather samples to be checked for rot.Gather blight samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered various samples of potential rot from within Sul Madásh. Return to Ráthwil with the samples so that she can begin her research.'For many years I have been studying the spread of the Pale Blight, and I see indications of it growing stronger in recent days.

'Would you help me check the areas around Sul Madásh for signs of the blight and see what you find?

'I fear that it is starting to consume the settlement and we cannot allow that to happen.''I see you have brought me plenty of samples. It worries me that you were able to find many indications of rot within the settlement.

'Thank you for your help with this task.

'I will get to work immediately to try and determine if we are still safe within this grove.'Serâna has asked for you gather samples of the blighted flora near Sul Madásh so that she can study them and see how the rot affects different plants.Serâna has asked for you gather samples of the blighted flora near Sul Madásh so that she can study them and see how the rot affects different plants.Gather blighted flora samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered samples of the blighted flora outside of Sul Madásh. Return the samples you collected to Serâna.'The plants are reacting strangely to the blight in recent days.'

Serâna fumbles with a leaf in her hands as she inspects markings on it.

'I wish to study these further. If you are willing, ${CLASS}, I ask that you gather samples of the flora that has been affected by the blight near Sul Madásh and return them to me.''Good to see you again. Were you able to get the samples of which I spoke?'

You give the flora samples to Serâna and she begins fumbling with the leaves of them.

'Curious. They seem to react to the rot inside them differently in some cases.

'My fellow scholar, Râjoron, is in Sâr Marsag to the south. I wonder if he has found similar results.

'Thank you, my friend.'Serâna has asked for you search the waters near Sul Madásh and gather insects found there so that she can study them for signs of blight.Serâna has asked for you search the waters near Sul Madásh and gather insects found there so that she can study them for signs of blight.Gather insect samples (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You gathered insects from the marsh waters outside of Sul Madásh. Return the samples you collected to Serâna.'Greetings, ${CLASS}. I need more samples to bring to Zajâna for study.

'The blight is growing stronger here, and I have seen that the insects of the area are succumbing to it in unusual ways.

'I ask that you gather samples for me from the area. You can find them in swarms near the water's edge.''You have returned. Were you able to gather samples of the insects in the marsh?'

You give Serâna the insect samples you gathered and she makes a face when she takes them.

'These are perfect. I will study these further and chronicle what I find.

'What disgusting little creatures they are. I do so dislike insects. They make my skin crawl, but the work must be done.'Serâna has asked for your help gathering sap from the trees outside of Sul Madásh so that she can study the blight within them.Serâna has asked for your help gathering sap from the trees outside of Sul Madásh so that she can study the blight within them.Gather tree sap (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You found blighted sap within the trees outside of Sul Madásh. Return the samples you collected to Serâna.'The trees of Sul Madásh are dying.'

Serâna examines the tree sap in her hand.

'${CLASS}, I ask for your help in gathering sap from the trees in the surrounding area and bringing it to me, so that I can study the blight within them. I fear Sul Madásh is not as safe as we hoped.''These samples are exactly what I needed.'

Serâna takes the samples from you and inspects them more closely.

'I must make notes of these findings. Thank you for your assistance, ${CLASS}.'Your renown grows among the Kintai of Sul Madásh.You honour the Kintai and assist them with the efforts to preserve Adagím. You should return to their settlement of Sul Madásh to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Esteemed standing with the Kintai of Sul MadáshYou honour the Kintai and assist them with the efforts to preserve Adagím. You should return to their settlement of Sul Madásh to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Aralath at Sul MadáshYour renown grows among the Kintai of Sul Madásh. Return now to the quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'The land shall bear witness to all you have wrought. Such deeds become legend. To the Kintai of Sul Madásh, you are most cherished among allies. Would that our gifts matched your deeds. Choose freely, and go with our blessing.'Irsinith has asked you to gather sap from the trunk of the mother tree and return it to her so that she can show you how the Pale Blight is rotting within.Irsinith has asked you to gather sap from the trunk of the mother tree and return it to her so that she can show you how the Pale Blight is rotting within.Gather sap from Amûma (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered sap from Amûma. Return to Irsinith with it so that she can show you the signs of the Pale Blight.'I am saddened to hear about the grove. I will do what I can to help Tálbiral.

'I understand how he must feel and I know all too well that the chieftains of Sul Madásh are not able to give much aid to those of us outside of the settlement.

'I fear Amûma is not fairing any better than Laivárth as she is succumbing to the Pale Blight more with each passing day. As the warden here, I ask that you go and see for yourself to understand how nothing is safe from this pestilence. Gather the sap that you can find seeping from the mother tree herself and bring it to me. I will show you how the rot can be found within.''Have you had a chance to see the sap that weeps from Amûma?'

You give Irsinith the sap you have gathered.

'As you can see here in this sap, it appears bloody and diseased. You can see the rot festering inside even a small amount.

'I feel now that Amûma herself is speeding up the death of Adagím. The affliction in her roots stretches deep into the earth and is killing the ground beneath our feet. She does not speak to me as she once did and the rare words I do hear are poisoned.

'This pains me deeply and it is because of this, that I do not know how I can help Laivárth. Our mother tree may even be the cause of the grove dying.'Irsinith has asked you to travel to Sul Madásh and speak with the chieftains there. They will want to know of the Pale Blight's effect on Amûma and Laivárth.Irsinith has asked you to travel to Sul Madásh and speak with the chieftains there. They will want to know of the Pale Blight's effect on Amûma and Laivárth.Find the chieftains in Sul MadáshYou found the chieftains of Sul Madásh. Speak with Dáthlil and bring her the news of the Pale Blight from her daughter, Irsinith.Speak with DáthlilYou found the chieftains of Sul Madásh. Dáthlil has asked you to speak with her husband Ildimár and learn more about the settlement.Speak with Ildimár'Bring word of Amûma to the chieftains of Sul Madásh.

'Tálbiral feels they have failed our people and I have my struggles with them as well...'

Irsinith pauses for a moment in thought, staring up at the Mother Tree.

'They are my mother and father, and they have begged me to return home time and again, but my place is here, as her warden.

'I may not be able to do much to prevent the rot from spreading, but they may know more in the settlement as the scholars retreated there as our groves fell. They also may be in need of help from a ${CLASS} like yourself.''Welcome, outsider. What brings you to Sul Madásh and caused you to brave the harsh lands of Adagím?'

You tell Dáthlil of how Laivárth and Amûma are falling to the blight and that Irsinith sent you with the news and a request for help if they have learned more of the Pale Blight. Dáthlil looks relieved to hear any news about her daughter.

'I am glad that you have seen Irsinith and that she is holding on as best she can. This blight consuming Laivárth and even the mother tree is most dire. I am sad to tell you that Sul Madásh is losing this battle against the pestilence that ravages our land. We are doing what we can, but many are losing hope.

'If you wish to help us here, please speak with my husband Ildimár. He will know more of what needs to be done around the settlement. I will send word to Irsinith as I miss her dearly.''Greetings. As Dáthlil said, I too am glad to hear word from our daughter. I am sad to hear about her circumstances, and I wish that she would come to the settlement, though I understand her duty is to Amûma. She makes us proud with her devotion.

'She spoke the truth that Sul Madásh is suffering these days. The infection can be found in the trees within our walls despite our best efforts to cleanse the signs we see.

'We are trying to identify the Pale Blight and put an end to this, so all help is accepted.'Ildimár has asked that you look out over the lands of Adagím from where they stand so you can see how bad the Pale Blight has ravaged the land.Ildimár has asked that you look out over the lands of Adagím from where they stand so you can see how bad the Pale Blight has ravaged the land.You have seen just how far the Pale Blight has spread through Adagím and how so little of it remains unspoiled. You should speak with Ildimár now that you know more of what they are up against.Speak with IldimárIldimár has told you that Rangers from the north arrived in Sul Madásh to fight back the Pale Blight and could likely use your help.Speak with Faeron'There is much you can do to help here in Sul Madásh. There are many here who could use your help if you ask around the settlement, and I am certain they will be grateful for the assistance.

'We are usually wary of outsiders, especially in times like this, but you helped our daughter, Irsinith, so you are most welcome. As I mentioned, this settlement is slowly but surely succumbing to this blight. And with the infection, you will face many dangers here in Adagím as the creatures here have become twisted and vile with rot. The threats grow by the day.

'I am certain you have seen how the blight has consumed this earth already to make it here. But I ask that you look out over the lands from here. You can see just how bad it has become.''As you can see, the entire land is consumed outside of this sanctuary, and even some land within its borders.

'It is disheartening to be fighting against this threat as it turns our very earth against us. We have thus far kept the threat at bay all these years, but we know we are losing the battle and Sul Madásh will soon be lost if the spread is not stopped.

'Though after all these years we recently had Rangers from the north arrive and they speak about how they have seen similar things in their lands. They bring knowledge that may be able to help our people. Please, speak with them as they have spoken of how they could use someone like yourself.''${PLAYER}! I did not think we would encounter you in these southern lands, so far from Imlad Morgul, but it seems Viznak was right after all! I believed his confidence sprang from a misunderstanding of the geography and the sheer size of Harad... but perhaps it was instead his knowledge of your penchant for adventure, and your desire to aid any who have need.

'You know of the evils Lhaereth the Stained worked in Imlad Morgul and in Agarnaith. Word came to us that a pestilence not dissimilar to that of the Lady of Blight had taken root in these lands, long ago, and we thought to investigate. We wish now to help where we can.

'There may be something we can do to help these people... especially now that we are together again! Please return to me when you can as we will need your help with the task.'Faeron has asked you to help the Rangers to craft a serum against the Pale Blight by assisting Viznak in concocting it.Faeron has asked you to help the Rangers to craft a serum against the Pale Blight by assisting Viznak in concocting it.Speak with Viznak about the serumViznak informed you about the plan for the Pale Blight potion and asked that you gather the needed ingredients from near Sul Madásh.Gather blighted water (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather blighted mushrooms (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather blighted sap (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather bighted leaves (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have gathered the requested ingredients for the potion. Return to Viznak in Sul Madásh so he can begin crafting the concoction.'The lands here are filled with rot and there is much we must do.

'The blight that has seized this wood is not exactly like the one brewed by Lhaereth, but the two pestilences share some traits. Our work in the north may inform our work here! We believe we can create a serum for this plague. It will not stop its spread, but it may protect the imbiber from the rot's infection.

'If you are ready to get to work, speak with Viznak. Spend not overlong catching up! The sooner we can mix this serum, the sooner we can return to other lands! He will know what should be done as we work towards this potion.''Oh, greetings! I knew you would be around here somewhere! Viznak has only to think a thing, and it happens! Just now I was thinking I needed another loyal subject to collect some things, and here you are!

'We are going to make something to help with the bad things here. It helped with the Blight Lady before. This drink needs some more stuff. We saw some good ones that way.'

Viznak points to the north.

'We need some green water, some rocky mushrooms, some leaves that died, and some blood from the trees! Not a problem for you, right? No, no, no, not a problem! You have proven a friend to Viznak before now... once, twice. Is it only twice? Hee hee hee! It seems like more to me, but I am always busy with my rule.

'Now go get that stuff, ${PLAYER}!''${PLAYER} returns! I was waiting.'

You give Viznak the items he requested.

'You bring what I needed and you didn't die to the blight! I will get to work right away. It should not take long. Now it is your turn to wait. Hee hee hee!'Viznak is ready for you to test the serum that was crafted to protect against the Pale Blight in Adagím.Viznak is ready for you to test the serum that was crafted to protect against the Pale Blight in Adagím.Speak with Viznak when you are ready to test the serumSpeak with Viznak about the results of testing the serum.Speak to Viznak'Are you ready to test the serum?

'Let us hope you survive. It is such a hassle to find loyal subjects.''You did it and lived!

'We are all glad. The blight does not look like it is killing you? It looked like it even made you strong?

'That means the serum works!

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}! We are all glad you did not die painfully. Now more must be made.''Are you ready to drink it and see if it works?

'We found some tasty mushrooms that have some blight in them for you to try!'In the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed.You stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Reach Friend standing with the Temámir of Jiret-menêshYou stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Speak with Semanísi at Jiret-menêshIn the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed. Among the adventurous dwarves who dared leave the deep halls, your name and effort gain heft. Return to the quartermaster to see new wares awaiting those proven.'Stone-strong you are, and among our folk that is high praise. For all you have done, we name you friend.

'As promised, a share of our goods is yours. I have added another boon. In the long dark, salt was scarce, and meals turned dull. Though the land lies barren, salt yet lingers in crumbling mounds across An Shêru.

'I give you salt grinders, and the craft to make your own. Choose as suits your skill. With these, draw the salt from dry earth, and we shall recognize you well for the labour.'Sílivat has given you enchanted water that he hopes will cleanse the fungi growing on the eastern edges of Sul Madásh and prevent them from spreading the blight deeper into the settlement.Sílivat has given you enchanted water that he hopes will cleanse the fungi growing on the eastern edges of Sul Madásh and prevent them from spreading the blight deeper into the settlement.Cleanse monstrous fungi (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Sílivat and inform him that you have cleansed the monstrous fungi found in Sul Madásh.'Greetings adventurer, I have a mushroom problem. There are fungi that appear to have completely succumbed to the blight and are growing dangerous because of it.

'To the east I have seen mushrooms that are growing much larger than normal and appear quite deadly. Can you take this water that I have enchanted and pour it over them?

'I believe it should destroy the blight within the mushrooms and stop it from spreading as quickly.''I am glad to see you again. I trust that you have dealt with the fungi to the east?'

Sílivat takes back the remaining water.

'I am not certain it will fully cleanse the blight within, as none of our efforts thus far have had a lasting impact.

'Though it should delay the spread and at least stop those monstrous fungi from being a concern for the moment. Thank you for your help, my friend.'Cúridwan has returned from her wandering in the blighted forest and Inkanûsh is eager to speak with her in Sul Madásh.Inkanûsh is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul MadáshInkanûsh and Cúridwan are in Sul Madásh.Listen to the conversation between Inkanûsh and CúridwanCúridwan is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul MadáshInkanûsh and Cúridwan are in Sul Madásh.Listen to the conversation between Inkanûsh and CúridwanCúridwan is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul MadáshGlorfindel is in Sul Madásh.Listen to GlorfindelInkanûsh is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Inkanûsh in Sul MadáshCúridwan is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Cúridwan in Sul MadáshGlorfindel is in Sul Madásh.Talk to Glorfindel in Sul MadáshThe Utûgi have been driven away, but they leave sorrow in their wake.Check Baharúd for signs of life'Let us see what this woman Cúridwan has to say in answer to my questions, ${PLAYER}.''Thórvilon is dear to me, as are all of the Kintai. I have sought for many years to cure the Lâg Dávrit, the Pale Blight that holds Imhûlar in its grip, but while my salves have proven beneficial here and there, something has of late revealed itself to me. The Pale Blight is itself a symptom only, and not the source of the evil. A greater corruption emerged in Adagím, moving throughout the Emerald Wood, and my salves have proven less effective at dealing with this root cause of the blight.

'But I will not give up.''Ísman rules above them all, the Sky-master. In his lordly form he observed the comings and goings of the Shoghór as they crossed the great desert; it was in feminine aspect that Ísman lit the stars to show them the way.

'Jarângar the Water-friend brings healing and wards away the much-feared drought, and Shin-samêt the All-Shaper fashions the plants that grow beside the rivers and the animals that drink from the oases.

'Zangûr the Walker of the Wilds knows all the paths of the mountains and valleys, as well as the wild flowers of the hillside and the secrets of the blossoming forests.

'Bâkhlas the Doom-hand is feared and respected by the Shoghór nomads, for the domain of Bâkhlas is death. Better-loved are Arâmkal the Soothing Word, who brings rest and dreams to the Shoghór, as well as Garândi the Swift One, who endures all hardship to emerge triumphant and laughing at the end of suffering.

'The seven Ghursâti who gave me my purpose and sent me to aid the people of this land are opposed by the demonic figure Makhâl, but I will not speak of him. The Shoghór speak his name only in whispers, and then unwillingly.''But how can this be?'

The Wizard's brow furrows.

'It is true that some things were left unknown to me by the Powers that sent me to Middle-earth, that I might best accomplish my purpose... but the number of my Order is five. Far have I travelled across Middle-earth to oppose the evils of Sauron and his servants, and Radagast too has played his part. Saruman was the highest of my Order, until he succumbed, but even in that I see the role Saruman played in the defeat of Sauron.

'Two others of the five travelled east into distant lands, and their fate is unknown to me. They serve the Powers, as I do. I am certain of it.''The number of Wizards sent to the northern lands may have been five, but I assure you these southern lands are vast. Do you think the Shoghór nomads would welcome you among them in the body you now wear? The Ghursâti, the "Powers," as you name them, they sent their servants in form appropriate to the people they sought to aid. How might "the Golden Maiden" or "the Sorceress in Green" be received in the cold northern lands, I wonder?  

'The Wizards of the northern line and the Wizards of the southern line must be different, or they must each fail in their purposes. In this I see the wisdom of the Powers that gave to us our missions, separate though shared they may be.

'It is not for an individual to see the entire breadth of the tapestry. That is reserved for those who sent us, is it not?''Earlier I was amused because Cúridwan reminded me of Inkanûsh... but I did not expect such similarities as these!'

Glorfindel suddenly falls silent, listening.

'Enemies approach, ${PLAYER}. I fear Sul Madásh is under attack!'Test the Pale Blight serum that the Viznak and the Rangers made to ensure it protects the imbiber from the rot.Test the Pale Blight serum that the Viznak and the Rangers made to ensure it protects the imbiber from the rot.You drank the serum, now it is time to eat the blighted mushrooms and see what happens!Eat the <rgb=#0000FF>blue</rgb> mushroomEat the <rgb=#00FF00>green</rgb> mushroomEat the <rgb=#FF0000>red</rgb> mushroomEat the <rgb=#FFFF00>yellow</rgb> mushroomYou drank the serum and ate the mushrooms. Speak with Viznak again to see what is next.Speak with Viznak'Good job!

'That was everything for now!'Máladil is studying the Elderwell and has asked for your help searching the ruins for signs of the Ishâkhai.Máladil is studying the Elderwell and has asked for your help searching the ruins for signs of the Ishâkhai.Examine weathered markingsInvestigate old clothYou have searched the Elderwell for signs of the Ishâkhai. Return to Máladil to inform her of your findings.Return to Máladil with your findings'Greetings, ${CLASS}. Are you here to admire the Elderwell?

'It is quite the sight to behold. I left Sul Madásh to come here and investigate it in hopes to learn more about the Ishâkhai.

'Would you like to help me search these ruins for any signs of them?''Hello, my friend. Have you found anything of note?'

You tell Máladil of your findings at the statues.

'Those are good finds! I will look into them further to determine if they are really relics of the Ishâkhai. There is so much to learn of these people who vanished long ago.

'You have my thanks, friend. You would make a great researcher should your adventuring days ever come to an end.'Baráil in Sâr Marsag has asked you to travel to Hadrudád and collect suitable firewood to keep their flame lit as well as defeat any of the monstrous huorns you encounter.Baráil in Sâr Marsag has asked you to travel west to Hadrudád and collect suitable firewood to keep their flame lit as well as defeat any of the monstrous huorns you encounter.Gather firewood (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Huorns (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Baráil in Sâr Marsag with the firewood you have collected to keep the flame lit.'The fire here is a sign of safety for travellers. Monsters fear it, and it is the last marker on the road leaving Adagím. I could use your help to keep it burning if you are willing, ${CLASS}.

'In the area to the west known as Hadrudád, you can find suitable firewood that we could use. Be careful of the trees though, the Huorns have grown quite aggressive from the blight rotting inside them.''You return! And with plenty of firewood as well!'

Baráil takes the firewood from you.

'This will keep the flame lit for some time. Thank you my friend.

'Sâr Marsag will remain a beacon of safety as we continue our efforts.'Mírthar does not have the strength to make the healing concoction and has asked that you bring the supplies to Lóburdith, the healer in Sul Madásh, as she will be able to help.Mírthar does not have the strength to make the healing concoction and has asked that you bring the supplies to Lóburdith, the healer in Sul Madásh, as she will be able to help.Lóburdith has asked for your help making the healing concoction that Mírthar needs. Use the table nearby to create the mixture.Mix the healing concoction ingredientsLóburdith has asked for your help making the healing concoction that Mírthar needs. Use the table nearby to create the mixture.Combine healing concoctionYou have successfully created the concoction that Mírthar needs. Return to Lóburdith so that she can do the final preparations.'I have not the strength to do this myself.'

Mírthar coughs more violently.

'You must take the mixture to Sul Madásh. Please... hurry. The healer there, Lóburdith, will know what to do....''Hello, what can I do for you?'

You explain that you are trying to help Mírthar and show Lóburdith the supplies you gathered.

'That is dire news. He was correct in having you gather those supplies. We must work quickly.

'I need your help in making the concoction. You can find a mixing table nearby with the tools you will need. I will handle the additional medicines. Bring the mixture to me when you are done.

'We may be able to save Mírthar if we hurry.'Lóburdith takes the concoction from you and examines it.

'Well made, ${CLASS}. You show a real talent as a healer.

'Give me but a moment to finish the healing concoction. It will give Mírthar the strength he needs to return to Sul Madásh where we can help him more.'You found a dead Orc with rot from the pale blight on it. You should search the camp for any additional signs of the blight spreading through the Orcs' ranks.You found a dead Orc with rot from the pale blight on it. You should search the camp for any additional signs of the blight spreading through the Orcs' ranks.Inspect Orcs infected with blight (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Orcs in Mokh Grazádh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You investigated Mokh Grazádh and found more Orcs that have died from the blight. Tálbiral nearby in Laivárth would likely want to hear about this.Speak with Tálbiral in LaivárthYou find a dead Orc who shows signs of the Pale Blight rotting from within him.

There must be more signs of it spreading through their ranks inside of their camp.'Hello, friend. What news have you brought?'

You inform Tálbiral of what you found in Mokh Grazádh.

'The Orcs themselves are rotting away from the Pale Blight in this land? Serves them right and I am glad you thinned their numbers as well.

'Thank you for this information, ${CLASS}. It does not absolve them of my suspicions, but will be useful to this grove.'Thárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has crept its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Thárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has crept its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Reach rotting areaThárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has creeped its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Burn rot (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has crept its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Reach rotting areaThárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has crept its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Burn rot (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Thárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has crept its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Reach rotting areaThárnar has tasked you with stopping the blight that has crept its way into Sul Madásh before it can spread further. Look for any signs of rot and burn them away.Burn rot (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Thárnar in Sul Madásh to return his torch and let him know that you have burned away the rot found in the settlement.'The Pale Blight has found its way into the borders of Sul Madásh. I could use your help, ${CLASS}, to push it back.

'People have spotted rot sprouting from the ground in the area, and it must be dealt with, lest it spread further.

'Take this torch and burn it away.''You return. I trust that means the rot has been dealt with?'

You confirm that it has and return his torch to him.

'Thank you. Our work seems to never end and we can not let up. We must remain vigilant to protect Sul Madásh for as long as we can.

'Your efforts today have been of great help.'Baráil in Sâr Marsag asked that you travel to the Pits of Chortáth and thin the number of jackals in the area. She also requested you gather pelts from the pack-leaders that they can use in the camp.Baráil in Sâr Marsag asked that you travel to the Pits of Chortáth in the south-west and thin the number of jackals in the area. She also requested you gather pelts from the pack-leaders that they can use in the camp.Defeat Adagím jackals (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather pack-leader pelts (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Baráil in Sâr Marsag with the gathered pelts and inform her that you thinned the number of jackals threatening the camp.'I grow tired of these beasts threatening our camp.

'There is a pack of jackals to the south-west in a place known as the Pits of Chortáth that are growing more aggressive as of late. I am not certain if it is caused by the pale blight or not, but we cannot allow them to keep attacking our camp or any travellers who are passing through.

'Would you go find their den and thin their numbers, ${CLASS}? We could also use their pelts here to reinforce our shelters against the weather.''Welcome back from the pits, ${CLASS}.

'I see that you have returned with the requested pelts, and my scouts report that they are seeing fewer jackals stalking our woods.

'Thank you for your help with this task.'Orthél has asked you to find a group of scholars who went to study the blight outside of Sul Madásh and have yet to return.Orthél has asked you to find a group of scholars who went to study the blight outside of Sul Madásh and have yet to return.Search for missing scholarsYou found scholars who have fallen to the blight. See if they have the notes Orthél mentioned to confirm they are the ones who went missing.Inspect scholarInspect scholarInspect scholarReturn to Orthél in Sul Madásh with the notes recovered from the missing scholars and inform her of their fates.'${CLASS}, I am in need of your help. A group of scholars left the safety of Sul Madásh to study the Pale Blight and try to discover why it is spreading faster these days.

'They have yet to return. I fear the worst, but I cannot venture out and find them myself. If you can search for them and bring them home I would be most grateful.''${CLASS}, you return, though you return alone. Were you able to find the missing scholars?'

You give Orthél the scholars' notes and the unfortunate news. She begins to tear up.

'This is deeply saddening news. My dear friends...

'I do not know what to say but thank you for finding them. I will have the scouts recover their bodies and the recovery of their notes will ensure their sacrifice was not in vain.

'I need some time. Please, take this for your efforts.'Lóburdith in Sul Madásh has asked for your assistance in checking on the Kinta scouts who have returned but have fallen ill from the pale blight and providing medicine if necessary.Lóburdith in Sul Madásh has asked for your assistance in checking on the Kinta scouts who have returned but have fallen ill from the pale blight and providing medicine if necessary.Check on the injured KintaLóburdith in Sul Madásh has asked for your assistance in checking on the Kinta scouts who have returned but have fallen ill from the pale blight and providing medicine if necessary.Check on the injured KintaLóburdith in Sul Madásh has asked for your assistance in checking on the Kinta scouts who have returned but have fallen ill from the pale blight and providing medicine if necessary.Retrieve medicineLóburdith in Sul Madásh has asked for your assistance in checking on the Kinta scouts who have returned but have fallen ill from the pale blight and providing medicine if necessary.Give medicine to the injured Kinta scoutLóburdith in Sul Madásh has asked for your assistance in checking on the Kinta scouts who have returned but have fallen ill from the pale blight and providing medicine if necessary.Check on the injured KintaReturn to Lóburdith and let her know you have tended to the scouts and administered medicine to the ones who needed it.Return to Lóburdith'Our scouts have returned and have fallen ill from travelling deep into the blighted lands.

'I could use your assistance, ${CLASS}, to help check on them and give them medicine... if you have the time.''I thank you for your assistance in helping these scouts.

'The days grow longer as more and more fall ill to the blight in these lands. I grow weary myself and am glad for the short rest you have provided.

'I must continue my work. I wish you well on your travels.'Dáthlil has given you supplies of the serum that the Rangers concocted to resist the blight. Bring them to the scouts in Sâr Marsag to the south.Dáthlil has given you supplies of the serum that the Rangers concocted to resist the blight. Bring them to the scouts in Sâr Marsag to the south.Travel to Sâr MarsagYou arrived in Sâr Marsag and see a scout named Tábai. Bring the supplies of the serum to him and let him know to distribute them.'Our scouts in the south continue to search for the source.'

Dáthlil hands you bottles of the serum.

'I ask that you bring some of the serum you and the Rangers have concocted to them in Sâr Marsag. It will help to keep them safe.

'They will also be relieved to learn we are making progress here in Sul Madásh.''Take a rest, traveller. You will be safe within this camp.'

You give Tábai the supply of blight serum and explain it is from Dáthlil in Sul Madásh.

'A serum you say? To protect us from this blight? That is wondrous news! I will distribute this to the other scouts immediately. I am relieved to hear that the scholars of Sul Madásh are making progress to stop the spread of the rot.

'While you are here, there may be others who could use your help. Be sure to speak with Baráil as well.

'Thank you for this delivery my friend. It will save many lives.'Restless trees on the edge of Adagím have turned violent of late. Elder Jurmêna has requested your aid in bringing peace once more to the Moulder-grove.Defeat marauding Huorns and gather cords of wood from the malevolent trees near Shádhag. Shádhag can be found north-west of Sormedân.Defeat the restless trees near Shádhag (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather wood from the fallen trees (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân with the cords of wood.'Sormedân is growing, and we have precious few trees here. Much has been flattened for farmland and orchards.

'The trees of the Moulder-grove are violent, attacking any who pass near it. I believe we can solve both of these problems with one axe. Would you go to the Moulder-grove, cut down the trees, and harvest what wood of theirs is not warped and twisted? These trees could still prove useful to Sormedân.''These trees will prove far more useful in death than in life. You have retrieved so much! Thank you for bringing peace once more to the Moulder-grove, and to Sormedân.'Sênjar, a farmer in Sormedân, has requested your aid in gathering honey from the angry bees of Medân Makhai.Sênjar, a local farmer in Sormedân, has requested your help in the harvest of honey from the local bees. These bees have become much more violent and aggressive since a new queen established herself over their hive. The bees can be found within the cave of Medân Makhai, north-west of Sormedân.Defeat bees in Medân Makhai (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Harvest honeycombs (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Sênjar in Sormedân with the recovered honey.'Saráz záro-dan, outsider. I tend to the bees and the orchards here. Well, I used to. We have been unable to gather any honey for some time now. This new dark queen of the bees has turned the workers and the warriors violent, aggressively stinging any of us that go to gather from her honeycombs.

'While you are moving to defeat her, if you could gather what honey you find from the honeycombs of Medân Makhai, I would be appreciative. It has been several months since we've received any honey from them, and our food is more meagre without the bounty of the bees.''My, that is... so much honey! We will make a proper farmer out of you yet!

'Thank you. You have done much for me and for Sormedân. You have the gratitude of my family.'There are many things to do in the farming village of Sormedân. Elder Jurmêna has requested you speak with some of the local peoples and help them to prove that you are a friend to the village.Elder Jurmêna has requested you speak with Sênjar, Khaljûn, and Lurâkha about their daily troubles and assist them where you are able.Speak with Sênjar in SormedânSpeak with Lurâkha in SormedânSpeak with Khaljûn in SormedânReturn to Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân and let her know you have spoken with each of the villagers.Return to Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân'We do not know you, but in Sormedân we judge everyone by their actions rather than their words, outsiders and Kighâni alike.

'Sormedân is not free from its share of troubles. Sênjar, Khaljûn, and Lurâkha have each come to me recently, beset with problems.

'Speak with these villagers. Assist them with their troubles, and I may have further information to aid you.''Yes. There is a way you can help. It is the bees. They have gone mad, driving me and my son from their hives.''You want to help? My cattle are ailing, and my list of chores long. I will take whatever aid you are willing to give, outsider.''Jurmêna sent you to do the rounds, did she? I do have something I've been complaining to her about, come to think of it.

'I cannot sleep. A terrible racket keeps puncturing my restless dreams.''You have spoken with each of them? That is welcome news indeed.

'See that the villagers' needs are met, and I will tell you what I know of Khâbakh's whereabouts.'In the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed.You stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Reach Ally standing with the Temámir of Jiret-menêshYou stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Speak with Semanísi at Jiret-menêshIn the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed. Among the adventurous dwarves who dared leave the deep halls, your name and effort gain heft. Return to the quartermaster to see new wares awaiting those proven.'You are as undeterred as our eldest stone-lords below. High praise again, friend. You have raised our status in Mûr Ghala, and shown skill in words and work alike.

'Again, I offer recompense and more. Take this acid, forged to free crystal from stone. These gems, of little worth to Elves or Men, hold value below, where light is rare and every spark prized.

'You may take the tinctures, or the knowledge to craft your own. The choice is yours—both are earned.'Across Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people.You remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Ally standing with The City of ZajânaYou remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Alahamâr at ZajânaAcross Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people. In the halls of Zajâna, your name and deeds grow with import.'You have proven yourself yet again. The Doorwards acknowledge your labour and offer you more besides.

'We are a people of hidden signs. In ages past, our scholars concealed their lore with runes unseen, drawn forth only by the tinctures of their making.

'Choose now: a phial to reveal the old marks, or the recipe by which such tinctures are made. Take what serves you best, and accept also the other gifts prepared in your name.'Sârwi, the young son of Sênjar, has asked you to retrieve his lost toys from the angry bees swarming in Medân Makhai.Sârwi, the young son of Sênjar, has asked you to retrieve his sword, shield, and carved lion lost to the angry bees swarming in Medân Makhai. Medân Makhai can be found north-west of Sormedân.Recover Sârwi's wooden "sword"Recover Sârwi's wooden shieldRecover FangReturn to Sârwi in Sormedân with his recovered items.'Well! You look like a hero! I'm a hero too, although my da says I can't fight yet. But I can! Well I did. I lost my weapons!

'I went with my da to get honey from the bees, but then the bees started stinging my da and we had to run away! Sometimes heroes have to run away, my da says.

'Anyway, I lost my sword and my shield. And Fang! He's a lion, so he's fearsome, and he comes with me on my adventures, so I'm not lonely. And he protects me. But who is going to protect him?

'Could you get Fang, and my sword, and my shield, so I can be a hero again, just like you?''Oh you found them! Now don't go running off, Fang! I tell him things but I don't know if he listens sometimes. You know how lions are. They never listen.

'Now I can fight! I can protect Sormedân from the vicious Jázgai. Not here, Bloodtooth! Bash will make quick work of you!

'Thanks, now I can be a hero like you!'Baharúd's brother Ulishár has agreed to guide you on your road south into Mûr Ghala.Ulishár is by the southern entrance to Sul Madásh.Talk to Ulishár in Sul MadáshCorudan is by the southern entrance to Sul Madásh.Talk to Corudan in Sul MadáshCorudan has regretfully decided he cannot accompany you.Wait for Corudan to departUlishár has joined your travelling companions for the journey south.Talk to Ulishár in Sul MadáshUlishár agreed to show you where the road passes from Adagím into Kighân, south-west of Sul Madásh.Find the entrance to Kighân, south-west of Sul MadáshUlishár stands where the road leads south-west into Kighân.Talk to Ulishár on the road into Kighân'When I requested a Kinta guide to accompany us into Mûr Ghala, I did not expect Ulishár to volunteer. Indeed, I asked him more than once if he was certain he wished to go with us into danger, so soon after the death of his brother. I deem he is made of stern material, as are all the Elves of my acquaintance. Yes, I now include the Kintai among that number, for if Ulishár is representative of that folk they seem as resilient as any of the Eldar!

'Speak to him and judge his resolve in this for yourself.''Speak to Ulishár and judge his resolve for yourself.''I told the Wizard Inkanûsh I was willing to guide your party south into Mûr Ghala, and that is still true. It is... it is not an easy decision for me, but your arrival here has opened my eyes and thrown into disarray much that I thought certain!'

Ulishár holds your eyes for a moment and then flinches suddenly away.

'My brother Baharúd should have been the one to guide you south. He never lost the adventuring temperament as I did; he hungered still for the untaken road, ever desiring to see what lay beyond the furthest rise and over the horizon. I shared that mood with him, once...'

The Kinta tries to meet your eyes again but cannot. Instead he stares away into the trees.

'Some years ago I was captured by the Ordâkhai while travelling in the Mûr. Held in their distant city of Emax Dûl and put to harsh labour, I was certain I would die there. I still cannot believe I did not. Instead, with no small degree of fortunate happenstance I escaped from Emax Dûl and made my way home.

'This was mere months ago, ${PLAYER}. I promised myself I would stray no longer from Sul Madásh... but now Baharúd is slain, and the Wizard Inkanûsh says a threat more terrifying than Ordâkh has risen. How can I remain in Sul Madásh when I know you and your companions need my help? I simply cannot.''The Wizard Inkanûsh says a threat more terrifying than Ordâkh has risen. How can I remain in Sul Madásh when I know you and your companions need my help? I simply cannot.''I wish I could come with you, my friend, but I cannot. My sister is intent upon finding the shade that was once Silirtas, and I cannot leave her to accomplish this on her own. Perhaps once Sigileth is successful in this I can rejoin you... but I know not if her quest will be short or if it will be long.'

Corudan lowers his voice and speaks only to you.

'If it is from my heart I speak, I must confess this to you, ${PLAYER}: I am not certain I believe the conclusion for which my sister hopes is even possible. Silirtas perished some time ago before Utug-bûr, and if she now has become one of the Väiri, there is no assurance she will remember who she was in life. I fear Silirtas is gone, and this too: I must attend my sister closely or she may follow her to a careless death.

'So this is farewell, for now. Good-bye, my friend.''I am ready now to depart if your companions have finished their preparations. We must travel to the west and to the south, for there the road leads from Adagím into Kighân... our first destination.''We travel then to the west and to the south, for there the road leads from Adagím into Kighân... our first destination.'Ulishár stands here.'Here the road winds south into Kighân as it approaches Mûr Ghala, the Ridge of Plenty. The Mûr itself is a line of sheer hills that runs from east to west and serves as the southern hospitable boundary of the land its people name Shagâna. Beyond the Mûr lies the Hatokáli River and the distant jungles of Abaníssa, from where the Ordâkhai come.

'It is my understanding that Inkanûsh desires not to pass over the Mûr, but to visit the great libraries of Zajâna, the city that guards the road across the ridge. That is where we must first go.'Ulishár has come with you to Kighân, the Shornvale, but so too have tendrils of the blight that have taken root in Adagím.The trees at Shádhag may have been corrupted by the blight creeping into Kighân from Adagím.Travel to Shádhag in KighânUlishár asked you to collect unblighted seed-pods from trees and defeat their corrupted roots at Shádhag, in Kighân.Collect unblighted seed-pods from trees at Shádhag (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat fungal roots at Shádhag (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Ulishár said he would wait for you at the village of Sormedân, to the south.Glorfindel is at the village of Sormedân.Talk to Glorfindel at SormedânInkanûsh is at the village of Sormedân.Talk to Inkanûsh at SormedânKhâwaz at the village of Sormedân is reluctant to speak to you, but might be convinced.Talk to Khâwaz at SormedânVillagers of Sormedân are frightened of Glorfindel, and need reassurance that he is a friend and can be trusted.Reassure villagers of Sormedân (0/2)Villagers of Sormedân are frightened of Glorfindel, and need reassurance that he is a friend and can be trusted.Reassure villagers of Sormedân (1/2)Khâwaz at the village of Sormedân has something to say to you.Talk to Khâwaz at Sormedân'When I escaped from Emax Dûl and fled homeward, I spent the last leg of my journey in the care of farmers at Sormedân, a village to the south. I will never forget the kindness they showed to me.

'See how the fungal blight within Adagím encroaches upon these lands? A nearby grove to the north-west is threatened even now, and should it fall to that creeping corruption, the people of Sormedân will be endangered. Can we seek unblighted seed-pods and cull some of the trees that have already been infected at Shádhag? As we pass their village on the way to Zajâna, I would aid the farmers of Sormedân in memory of the kindness they showed me.

'We will meet up at Sormedân after the work at Shádhag is complete, or as near as we can make it. The blight always returns, but that is no reason not to combat it!''See how the fungal blight within Adagím encroaches upon these lands? Can we seek unblighted seed-pods and cull some of the trees that have already been infected at Shádhag to the north-west?

'We will meet up at Sormedân after the work at Shádhag is complete, or as near as we can make it. The blight always returns, but that is no reason not to combat it!''Your errand has put a smile on my face, ${PLAYER}, and that is no small thing in these dark days! The seed-pods you have brought me escaped the corruption of the blight, and that means they can be safely replanted further from Adagím, if that prove necessary. I have not given up hope that Cúridwan can find a cure for the blight... but until she does, we must remain practical. That was always my brother's advice, and now it must be mine as well.

'The folk of Sormedân greeted me warmly, but they seem less friendly toward your companions Glorfindel and Inkanûsh. I know not the reason for it, but the farmers give them a wide berth. It is quite unlike the people of Sormedân to be so reserved.''The folk of Sormedân greeted me warmly, but they seem less friendly toward your companions Glorfindel and Inkanûsh. I know not the reason for it, but the farmers give them a wide berth. It is quite unlike the people of Sormedân to be so reserved.''Are the people of this village frightened of us, ${PLAYER}? It did not seem so elsewhere in Shagâna. For what reason might they fear us here?

'It cannot be that they have never seen Elves before, for Ulishár is known to them and they greeted him warmly.''Are the people of this village frightened of us? It did not seem so elsewhere in Shagâna. For what reason might they fear us here?''This is not my first journey in these lands, ${PLAYER}, for I ventured south seeking tidings of Sauron, long ago. Much I learned in the lore-vaults of Zajâna, and it is there I wish to return. Let us not tarry in this farming village when there is knowledge to be gained in the libraries of the City of Gates!

'But Glorfindel is not as accustomed to receiving such sour looks as I am! Distrust accompanies me in the south even as it does in the north, but he is an Elf-lord and travels fewer roads than I. What would old Barliman have to say of Glorfindel, I wonder?

'Speak to one of these farmers and assure him that Glorfindel means him no harm, and at any rate our business will speed us along shortly. The people here have nothing to fear from us.''Speak to one of these farmers and assure him that Glorfindel means him no harm, and at any rate our business will speed us along shortly. The people here have nothing to fear from us.''My neighbours and I mean to cause no offence, stranger, but...'

Khâwaz wrings his hands apologetically.

'This Elf you are with has a presence about him we have never encountered before. The Kintai who dwell in the forest to the north rarely emerge, but those that do, such as Ulishár, have not his bearing. Is it rude to ask the age of an Elf, as it is to ask it of a man grown? For this Elf seems of an ancient kind, older even than the bones of the land.

'If you and he are friends of Ulishár, then I will trust your word that you hold no ill will toward us. Reassure my neighbours of this, and we will speak again... for there is something else that troubles me.''If you and he are friends of Ulishár, then I will trust your word that you hold no ill will toward us. Reassure my neighbours of this, and we will speak again... for there is something else that troubles me.''If you and he are friends of Ulishár, then I will trust your word that you hold no ill will toward us. Reassure my neighbours of this, and we will speak again... for there is something else that troubles me.''We will trust you, then. I hope you will be able to help us, as we helped Ulishár recover from his plight several months ago. If you are his friend, you are a friend to Sormedân, no matter how frightful the bearing of your companion.'The farmers of Sormedân are worried for one of their own, Rôkhasar, who may have strayed into danger.Khâwaz fears that Rôkhasar may have strayed too near Badrasâg, the Blade-horn south-east of Sormedân, in pursuit of his cattle.Look for Rôkhasar near Badrasâg to the south-east of SormedânThe spirits at Badrasâg should be defeated to ensure the safety of Sormedân.Defeat spirits at Badrasâg (${NUMBER}/5)The spirits at Badrasâg should be defeated to ensure the safety of Sormedân.Defeat spirits at Badrasâg (4/5)You heard someone calling for you below the Blade-horn. Could it be the missing farmer Rôkhasar?Talk to Rôkhasar below the Blade-hornYou consider how Mizâdi will react to this situation.Consider how Mizâdi will react to this situationRôkhasar is below the Blade-horn, in Kighân.Talk to Rôkhasar below the Blade-hornMizâdi will be at Ingar-garâsh to the south-west, deep within Kighân.Find Mizâdi at Ingar-garâsh in the southern reaches of KighânMizâdi stands at Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Mizâdi at Ingar-garâsh in southern KighânRahâzi stands at Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Rahâzi at Ingar-garâsh'It is the custom of the farmers of Sormedân to let their herds graze upon the fields of Kighân. Pursuit of the longer grasses sometimes brings the cattle many hours or even days away from home, but it has been too long since one of my neighbours has returned. Rôkhasar is his name, and when we last caught sight of him he drove his cattle in the direction of Badrasâg, the Blade-horn.

'That place has a dangerous reputation and he knows better than to bring his cattle so close, but I can think of no good reason he has not come back to Sormedân. Will you look for him by Badrasâg to the south-east, and quell whatever dangers you find there?''Will you look for Rôkhasar by the Blade-horn to the south-east, and quell whatever dangers you find there?''Why do you strive against the spirits, stranger? All is hopeless! All is lost!

'I drove my cattle to graze here against my better judgement, but it was not the evils of the Blade-horn that threatened them! No! Soldiers came from the west, and they laid claim to my creatures, butchering them and hauling away the meat to feed their army! They gave me nothing in return!'

You ask Rôkhasar in whose name the soldiers claimed his cattle, and he shakes an angry fist at you.

'Of what consequence is the name, stranger? Those were my beasts, taken without payment, and this wrong is unjust no matter in whose name it is performed! But I remember it, and curse it: the name was Mizâdi, some Shâra of Hamât. They must have taken me for a fool! Hamât is a dead kingdom, and can have no ruler.''Well? Have you nothing to say?'

You respond that the Shâra Mizâdi is known to you, and you believe if she is told of the injustice he has suffered, she will cure it. Rôkhasar stares at you with doubt on his face.

'That would be the most unusual outcome of all, stranger, but if you are able to obtain some remedy for this injury, I will sing your praises from the sunrise to the sunset. But only then, and I expect it not.'

Rôkhasar tells you the soldiers made for the village of Ingar-garâsh to the south-west, and agrees to inform your companions where you have gone when he returns to Sormedân.'I will tell your companions where you have gone, if you truly will speak on my behalf before this invading Shâra of Hamât.''You have taken a long road to reach me, ${PLAYER}, but here you stand. Did the forest of the Kintai prove difficult to traverse? How else to explain this dour expression you wear?'

You describe how a number of her soldiers slaughtered Rôkhasar's cattle and took the meat for themselves, and Mizâdi's own expression hardens.

'Are my soldiers expected not to eat, then? An army must eat if it is to fight, ${PLAYER}, and there is no shortage of foes to face in Kighân and all along the Mûr. Foes enough to freeze the blood of this herdsman should he encounter them, I doubt not! Does he stand against the Ordâkhai? Or does he chafe only against those who oppose his foes, and grouse when they seek only enough food to keep them fed as they fight?''You have something to say, Rahâzi?''You know I stand with you, Shâra Mizâdi, as was my mother's command. It was not her only command. This, too, she said to me: "Speak always your heart, my daughter."

'And so it is I tell you this: you must control your soldiers, or the people of the Mûr, and of greater Shagâna, will treat you as an invading despot. Aye, even as the Ordâkhai themselves!'Rahâzi believes the Shâra's response to this situation involving her soldiers will greatly impact the future of Renewed Hamât. How will Mizâdi respond?Mizâdi stands at Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Mizâdi at Ingar-garâshRahâzi is at Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Rahâzi at Ingar-garâshMizâdi has charged you with the tasks her soldiers would have accomplished if they did not now need to provide aid and recompense to the farmers of Sormedân. These tasks include collecting eggs from nests at Khatzâla and collecting meat from creatures throughout Kighân.Collect eggs from nests at Khatzâla east of Ingar-garâsh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect meat from creatures in Kighân (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The provisioner Dunaïl stands at Ingar-garâsh.Mizâdi is at Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Mizâdi at Ingar-garâshRahâzi is at Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Rahâzi at Ingar-garâsh'Shâra Mizâdi, mistake me not! I have seen the fire that drives you, and know why you fight to drive Ordâkh from these lands. I stood beside you at the Battle for Hamât, and my mother Girhâzi would not send me from Ambarûl in your train if she disbelieved in the rightness of your cause!

'But the people that live alongside the Mûr have not seen these things. For how much longer have they lived nearer to their oppressors? Has any captain of Ordâkh hesitated to seize the land of this people, or the beasts that graze thereon?

'You would gain much by treating this folk differently than have the Ordâkhai, my Shâra.''I hear wisdom in the words that Rahâzi speaks to me.

'But if it is right that nothing should be taken for nothing, ${PLAYER}, you need be no sage to understand this truth, as well. If my soldiers owe something to the herdsman who could not control his own grazing animals, then I must send others to work off this debt; while they labour for him, they will be performing none of their own tasks for my army.

'You will do their work, ${PLAYER}, while they go to compensate the herdsman for the meat that was taken on behalf of my army. It is only right.

'Rahâzi knows the duties they must now neglect, and will tell you what to do.''Rahâzi knows the duties the soldiers must now neglect. She will tell you what to do.''I knew Shâra Mizâdi would heed my words.

'It will not take long for you to perform the tasks the soldiers were supposed to complete today, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps the burden will feel lighter knowing the herdsman you spoke with will be compensated for the meat that was taken from him?

'These are the duties the Shâra would have you complete: retrieve eggs from the bird-nests at Khatzâla to the east of Ingar-garâsh, and hunt the meat from creatures throughout Kighân. Once you have done these things, you can bring the meat and eggs to Dunaïl, provisioner of the war camp.''These are the duties the Shâra would have you complete: retrieve eggs from the bird-nests at Khatzâla to the east of Ingar-garâsh and hunt for meat from creatures throughout Kighân. Once you have done these things, you can bring the meat and eggs to Dunaïl, provisioner of the war camp.''Thank you for bringing me this meat and these eggs. I will ensure they are properly stored for the days ahead.''I thank you again for delivering this food to me. I will see that it is properly stored for the days to come.''I dispatched some soldiers to compensate the herdsman at Sormedân for his butchered cattle. Let the folk there see that the Shâra of Renewed Hamât is not like the other rulers with whom they have had dealings!

'I thank you both for your words, and for the deeds you have done for me on this day, and on others.''I thank you both for your words, and for the deeds you have done for me on this day, and on others.''I am pleased as well. I believe this is how my mother would have resolved the situation. Nothing is given for nothing, but especially not loyalty. That is earned.'Sênjar has told you the bees of Medân Makhai have grown more aggressive since a new queen took over the hive and drove off the old queen. Sênjar has requested your aid in defeating the new queen.Sênjar has asked you to slay the new queen of the bees of Medân Makhai. Before you can face the queen, you must defeat the bee guardians defending the queen. Medân Makhai can be found north-west of Sormedân.Defeat bee guardians defending the queen (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find and defeat the aggressive queen bee in Medân Makhai.Find and defeat the aggressive queen beeReturn to Sênjar in Sormedân and tell him the new queen has been defeated.Return to Sênjar in Sormedân'Could I have a moment of your time, outsider? I work the land, and for many years the orchards and the bees have both been kind to my family and our village. But recently, that has all changed.

'We have long worked with the bees of Medân Makhai, growing their hives and taking a share of their honeycomb to feed our families. However, a new, dark queen recently took root, driving out the former queen. The bees are newly aggressive, stinging us if we even go near their hives. We rely on their honey to feed our families and to trade with others for their goods. This winter will be a harsh one indeed if the hives remain closed to us.

'Will you defeat this new queen? I managed to save the existing queen, though she was injured. Defeat the new queen, and I will be able to return the old queen back to her proper place in the hive.''Oh this is blessed news indeed!

'I will wait a day, and then bring the old queen back.

'It may take a few weeks, but I believe the bees will now calm themselves, under the tending of their former queen. This is a joyous day for all of Sormedân. Thank you, friend, we owe much to you.'Dáthlil has received a scout's report that they have had strange sightings to the south and they believe they have located the source of the Pale Blight.Dáthlil has received a scout's report that they have had strange sightings to the south and they believe they have located the source of the Pale Blight.Speak with Dáthlil to learn more about the scout's reportYou have defeated the morroval that attacked you near the ruins of Nagakhêdi and is believe to be the source of the Pale Blight. Speak with Dáthlil about your success.Speak with Dáthlil'I am glad to see you. Our scouts have found the source of the blight.

'We believe it to be in the ruins of an ancient wonder that was abandoned many years ago known to us as the Folly of Nagakhêdi.

'When you are ready, head south and see if you can confirm what our scouts have found, and put and end to this plague.''You return. I trust that means you were victorious in defeating the beast of which our scout spoke?'

You tell Dáthlil what happened at the entrance to the ruins of Nagakhêdi's wonder.

'So you have! We have hope for the first time in many years thanks to you!

'Could this really be the end of this blight raging in our lands? That foul monster must have been the reason the blight has been raging in recent months, and you put a stop to it. We will continue searching the area near the wonder, but what else could have been causing this pestilence?

'Thank you again my friend. We are eternally grateful.''Are you ready to travel south and stop this evil?

'If our scout's report is true, they have spotted a beast unseen in these lands before. This must be the source of the blight!

'Put an end to it.'Scouts in Sul Madásh believe they have discovered the source of the Pale Blight with an unknown enemy being spotted near the ruins of Nagakhêdi to the south.Scouts in Sul Madásh believe they have discovered the source of the Pale Blight with an unknown enemy being spotted near the ruins of Nagakhêdi to the south. Follow the tracks the scouts discovered to the east of the camp.Follow the tracks to the source of the Pale BlightScouts in Sul Madásh believe they have discovered the source of the Pale Blight with an unknown enemy being spotted near the ruins of Nagakhêdi to the south. Follow the tracks the scouts discovered to the east of the camp.Follow the tracks to the source of the Pale BlightScouts in Sul Madásh believe they have discovered the source of the Pale Blight with an unknown enemy being spotted near the ruins of Nagakhêdi to the south. Follow the tracks the scouts discovered to the east of the camp.Follow the tracks to the source of the Pale BlightScouts in Sul Madásh believe they have discovered the source of the Pale Blight with an unknown enemy being spotted near the ruins of Nagakhêdi to the south. Follow the tracks the scouts discovered to the east of the camp.Follow the tracks to the source of the Pale BlightScouts in Sul Madásh believe they have discovered the source of the Pale Blight with an unknown enemy being spotted near the ruins of Nagakhêdi to the south. Follow the tracks the scouts discovered to the east of the camp.Follow the tracks to the source of the Pale BlightThe tracks led you to the ruins of Nagakhêdi and a morroval has appeared to stop you. Defeat her!Kill the morrovalYou have defeated the morroval that you encountered at the ruins of Nagakhêdi. Return to Sul Madásh with the scout to report to Dáthlil.Speak with the scout and return to Sul Madásh'The monster is dead. I have never seen such a creature in these lands before.

'You have done well this day ${CLASS}. We should return to Sul Madásh.'Khaljûn asks you to accompany him further into the Blade-horn to further investigate the wailing spirit and his brother's journal.Investigate the strange mechanism in the torch near the wall of the Blade-horn.Investigate the strange mechanism near the wall of the Blade-hornPress further into the Blade-horn, behind the broken wall.Press further into the Blade-hornDefeat the attacking spirits!Defeat the attacking spirits (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Attempt to calm the mysterious spirit within the Blade-horn.Attempt to calm the mysterious spiritDefeat the mysterious spirit within the Blade-horn.Defeat the mysterious spirit within the Blade-hornInvestigate the strange crack left by the wailing spirit within the Blade-horn.Investigate the strange wall within the Blade-hornYou must press even further into Bâdrasag, the Blade-horn, to uncover its closely kept secrets.Get close to the first secret door inside the Blade-horn.Get close to the first secret door inside the Blade-hornExamine the suspicious wall the spirit went through in the Blade-horn.Examine the suspicious wall the spirit went through in the Blade-hornDefeat the angry spirits within the passage!Fight off the spirits (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Join Khaljûn at the tomb at the end of the corridor.Join Khaljûn at the tomb at the end of the corridorListen to the conversation between Khaljûn and the mysterious man.Listen to the conversationListen to the conversation between Khaljûn and Valrazâr.Listen to the conversation between Khaljûn and ValrazârListen to the conversation between Khaljûn and Valrazâr.Listen to the conversation between Khaljûn and ValrazârDefeat Valrazâr in the crypt.Defeat Valrazâr in the cryptListen to Khaljûn in the crypt of the Blade-horn.Listen to Khaljûn in the crypt of the Blade-hornListen to Khaljûn in the crypt of the Blade-hornListen to Khaljûn in the crypt of the Blade-hornIn the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed.You stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Reach Kindred standing with the Temámir of Jiret-menêshYou stand by your agreement with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh and assist in the recovery of the broken land. You should return to Quartermaster Semanísi to see what acclaim your deeds have wrought.Speak with Semanísi at Jiret-menêshIn the broken land of An Shêru, your efforts to assist the Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have not gone unnoticed. Among the adventurous dwarves who dared leave the deep halls, your name and effort gain heft. Return to the quartermaster to see new wares awaiting those proven.'What more could we ask of a partner? All you have done brings us favour and grants you standing in our eyes, and in the eyes of others.

'Our fates now run as were the joined rivers. Take these gifts, and treat with us without restriction. What we craft, you may yet claim.'Lurâkha has accepted your offer of aid in the care and feeding of the cattle in Sormedân.Collect grasses and water from the buckets in the fields just outside Sormedân.Harvest grass from outside Sormedân (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect buckets of water (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Provide fresh grass and water to injured cattle in Sormedân.Provide fresh grass to cattle in Sormedân (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Provide fresh water to cattle in Sormedân (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Lurâkha in Sormedân with your report on her cattle.Return to Lurâkha in Sormedân'You want to help? There is much to do here, and I will accept all the aid you're willing to give.

'Jurmêna is correct - there is much for us to do in Sormedân, and few with the knowledge or will to do it. I myself am beset with troubles. Restless trees to the north, scavenging jázgai to the south, nary a day passes without an attack on one of my herd.

'I have much to do for myself, but I can scarcely think about that when the herd needs food and water, tending and nurturing. Would you be willing to help?''It may seem simple, but our work keeps animals safe and bellies full. I can not think of anything more important.'Râjoron, a scholar in Sâr Marsag, has asked for your assistance in finding more information on the blight affecting Adagím.Speak with Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân about the scholar Khâbakh. Sormedân can be found in Kighân to the west, within Mûr Ghala.Speak with Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân about Khâbakh'I fear my knowledge has reached its limit. There is much we still do not know about this terrible blight. We need specialists to contend with this irrepressible pestilence. There are scholars with much more experience than I in Kighân.

'You should journey to Sormedân. The Pohâni Khâbakh lives there. He has special knowledge about diseases and pestilences. Despite that, I believe you will find his company quite warm and agreeable!

'Seek out Elder Jurmêna, she will know where to find Khâbakh.''Yes, I know where Khâbakh is. But he is not here, and I do not know you. I will offer you a fair deal. Help our people with some of their troubles, and I will know you mean both Khâbakh and us no harm. Do that, and I will tell you where he has gone.'Lurâkha, a rancher in Sormedân, asked if you would be willing to help care for the ailing cattle in the fields east of Sormedân.Speak with Elder Jurmêna to obtain the salve for injured cattle.Speak with Elder Jurmêna to obtain the salveGive the salve to injured cattle in Sormedân.Give salve to BlossomGive salve to BiscuitGive salve to BelaGive salve to ButtersGive salve to BaileyReturn to Lurâkha in Sormedân with news of the effects of the salve on her cattle.Return to Lurâkha in Sormedân'There are several stricken cattle that we take care of here. It is not just their bodies that we nourish, but their spirits as well.

'Elder Jurmêna has prepared a salve to aid the stricken cattle. Would you gather the salve from her and give it to the injured cattle? They are quite out of sorts, and could use a soothing touch.''It may seem a small thing, but I know they were heartened by your efforts. May the salve heal their ailment.

'Thank you, my friend. I know they will be gamboling and romping through the fields again shortly.''Lurâkha asked you to deliver the salve. Well, it is good to see you are helping those in Sormedân. Yes, I have it here. Please distribute it as Lurâkha sees fit.'Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân has asked you to burn blighted saplings near Shádhag, to stem the tide of blight overflowing from Adagím.Burn the afflicted saplings near Shádhag. Shádhag can be found north-west of Sormedân.Burn the afflicted saplings (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân with the report of your success.Return to Elder Jurmêna in Sormedân'There are precious few who see more into Adagím than we here in Sormedân. The trees are unnatural there, restless and malevolent, with an ill will toward Men and beasts. There is a rot in Adagím, that long have we spent our energy pushing back. Recently, that rot has moved fully to our outskirts.

'There is an orchard overtaken with this rot not far from here, the Moulder-grove. Some call it Shádhag. By any name it's evil, a source of the rot flooding in from Adagím. There the trees have made their camp and seek to spread that rot further into Kighân.

'We cannot allow that. The whole of Kighân relies on our orchards and cattle. If that rot comes any closer, I fear not just for our lives, but for all of Kighân.

'Will you go to the Moulder-grove, burn the afflicted saplings of the malevolent trees, that their rot not spread further into Kighân?''Trees shouldn't move - unnatural things. Sormedân is in your debt, and Zajâna too. All of Kighân relies on us to keep their bellies full, and you have given us more time before this blight encroaches once more.'You have earned the trust of Elder Jurmêna. She is now ready to tell you Khâbakh's whereabouts.Speak with the leader of the warband in Ingar-garâsh. Ingar-garâsh can be found in Kighân to the south-west of Sormedân, within Mûr Ghala.Speak with the leader of the warband in Ingar-garâsh'Yes, you have proven you mean us no ill will. You may go in peace, and with our blessing, outsider.

'Khâbakh was on his way to Zajâna, when he was waylaid by the warleader encamped south-west of here, in Ingar-garâsh. I know not where he is now. Though she says she means no harm to the people of Kighân, I don't trust this warleader. You should have words with her, she will know Khâbakh's whereabouts.''It is you! A fine sight for sore eyes you are, ${PLAYER}. We have much to discuss.

'Khâbakh? Yes, he came through here. Numûru questioned him for knowledge as to the Ordâkhai movements, but he had none. As a scholar - I believe they call them Pohâni here - he had little enough knowledge that could help us. He moved on to Zajâna. However, while you are here, there are several matters in which I would request your counsel.'Khaljûn, a villager in Sormedân, has requested you investigate the strange sounds coming from near Bâdrasag, the Blade-horn.Khaljûn has requested your aid in investigating disturbing sounds coming from the Blade-horn, an ancient and mysterious structure north of Sormedân. Khaljûn fears something evil has taken hold there.Investigate the area outside the Blade-horn for cluesInvestigate the area around the disturbed spirit.Investigate the area around the disturbed spiritInvestigate the area for more information about the mysterious spirit.Pick up the dusty book in the dirtReturn to Khaljûn in Sormedân and show him the journal you've found.'This past fortnight I've gotten precious little sleep. A horrible voice shrieks out at night.

'I believe these sounds come from the Blade-horn. Some know it as Bâdrasag. It is an ancient fortress. There is the stench of evil about the place. I don't want to send anyone there, as I believe the place contains only foul beasts with an ill will. But I long for a full night's sleep.

'Would you investigate the Blade-hold, and determine what cursed creature is making that sound? I would be very much in your debt.''You found a journal? This - this is from Mashâr. I don't believe it.'Khaljûn asks you to accompany him to the Blade-horn to find clues connecting his brother Mashâr's disappearance to the found journal.Meet with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn. The Blade-horn can be found south-east of Sormedân.Meet with Khaljûn outside the Blade-hornEnter the interior of the Blade-horn.Enter the interior of the Blade-hornDefeat the Tasked Ones and search for books mentioning the Tarsâri within the Blade-horn.Defeat the Tasked Ones within the Blade-horn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Search for books about the Tarsâri inside the Blade-horn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn.'This journal belonged to my brother, of all people. He disappeared five years ago, along with my father.

'They were on a trip, travelling through Adagím. All of their travelling party perished. I - I thought myself over all this. But what was Mashâr's journal doing near Bâdrasag? Nothing is right about this.

'Will you accompany me to the Blade-horn to search for clues amongst the ruin? I fear my courage will fail me if I try this task alone.''We should look inside. This place is quite unsettling. Let us not linger outside for longer than we need.''That was close. We barely made it out with our skin. These "Tasked Ones" seem to greatly value their privacy. Perhaps that's what got my brother and father killed?

'Let us catch our breaths. I noticed something while we were inside that I would speak with you about.

'You found books about the Tarsâri, the Tasked Ones? Yes, these will aid us greatly in figuring out their secrets and intentions.'Khaljûn asks you to accompany him further into the Blade-horn to further investigate the wailing spirit and his brother's journal.Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn when you are ready to go.Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn when you are ready to goSpeak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn.Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn'I almost lost my nerve there. My fear and my curiosity are piqued in equal measure.

'When we were investigating, I saw a crack in the wall. I believe there is a false wall, and a secret chamber behind it. I'd like to investigate it further, but I value my skin. You seem a steady hand in a fight, would you accompany me back into the Blade-horn?

'Will you press deeper into Bâdrasag with me?''Every time I think we are at the end, there is another path awaiting us. Another door, another path, another mystery. This Blade-horn is a labyrinth of lies.''We must press on, despite the fear.'You must press even further into Bâdrasag, the Blade-horn, to uncover its closely kept secrets.Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn when you are ready to go.Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn when you are ready to goSpeak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn.Speak with Khaljûn outside the Blade-horn'Every time I think we are at the end, there is another path awaiting us. I believe the spirit was directing us to another secret door, and another path beyond.

'However, I believe this one to be the last. Something about it feels different to me. Will you accompany me once more, and press on deeper into Bâdrasag?''I - I can't believe it. I thought this wound healed over, and now….''Let us see what unhappy fate befell those who came before us.'Khaljûn has asked for your help in creating a memorial stele for his brother, Mashâr.Join Khaljûn at Khamósh-engar. Khamósh-engar can be found just outside of Sormedân on the hill.Join Khaljûn at Khamósh-engarSpeak with Khaljûn at Khamósh-engar.Speak with Khaljûn at Khamósh-engarGather stones for Khaljûn suitable to making a stele in remembrance of his brother.Gather memorial stones for Khaljûn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Speak with Khaljûn at Khamósh-engar.Speak with Khaljûn at Khamósh-engarGive Khaljûn space for his thoughts at Khamósh-engar.Give Khaljûn space for his thoughts at Khamósh-engarHelp Khaljûn craft the finished stone to honour Mashâr.Help Khaljûn craft the finished stone to honour MashârBow to pay your respects to Mashâr.Bow to pay your respects to MashârSpeak with Khaljûn at Khamósh-engar.Speak with Khaljûn at Khamósh-engar'I cannot believe he is dead, and to die this way. I - I am at a loss for words.

'Would you help me carve a stone for my brother? It is a tradition of ours... and I could use the company.

'It is the least I can do to remember and honour him. 

'Meet me just outside the village, on the hill behind Sormedân. It is a place we call Khamósh-engar.''Although a bitter brew, I am glad to find out the truth... and to bring peace at last, to Mashâr.

'Thank you. I had not the strength to go on this journey without you.''I buried them both.

'But we will only be using the stone for my brother. My father – he does not deserve the honour of being remembered, except as a cautionary tale.

'His hands will be forever stained with my brother's blood, his legacy a curse.''There is no honour here, no happy moral. But still, we must remember.''Would you gather stones for the memorial? They should be relatively smooth. There should be some nearby. I will fashion them into a memorial for Mashâr.'The local jázgai have become a problem for the people of Kighân. Defeat local jázgai to make the area safe for them again.Defeat jázgai near Dân Shatagan. Dân Shatagan can be found north of Ingar-garâsh.Defeat jázgai in Dân Shatagan (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Razâl in Ingar-garâsh with news of your success.Return to Razâl in Ingar-garâsh'Have you crossed paths with the jázgai near here? They are quite dangerous beasts.

'The local travellers on the Great Road have reported increased attacks from these jázgai, as have our Subuzûri. I know you to be a fierce warrior and skilled combatant. Would you go to their camp and show some of these jázgai your skill, that they may prey less on the innocents around them?''You have saved more Subuzûri than you know. We are once more in your debt.

'I believe Numûru wants to celebrate your arrival by showing you how to make his famous stew, nakhód júra. Tread carefully, my friend. I wish for you to see the morrow.'Baradûm is dead. His journal is torn, the pages scattered throughout Jâr Obadal. Galêris would likely appreciate them returned to her.Pick up Baradûm's journal in Jâr Obadal.Pick up Baradûm's journal in Jâr ObadalRecover pages from Baradûm's torn journal in Jâr Obadal.Recover pages from Baradum's journal (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Bring the journal and its pages back to Galêris in Ingar-garâsh.Bring the journal and its pages back to Galêris in Ingar-garâshBaradûm is dead. You see a journal that appears ripped and torn near the body.

Galêris would likely appreciate it if you recovered Baradûm's journal pages.'I will treasure his journal - thank you for recovering it.

'Though it is but a small piece of him, this is one of my few remaining ties to Baradûm. I will cherish it.'Ulishár has come to Ingar-garâsh in search of you, bearing tidings about your companions' next road.Ulishár has come to Ingar-garâsh in southern Kighân.Talk to Ulishár at Ingar-garâshUlishár suggested that you travel to the cross-road north-west of the war-camp.Meet up with Ulishár at the cross-road north-west of Ingar-garâshUlishár stands beside the road that leads south-west to Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár at the cross-road north-west of Ingar-garâshUlishár suggested that you continue to the south-west until you reach Khab Antâx, the Overgate outside Zajâna.Travel to Khab Antâx, the Overgate outside ZajânaUlishár stands by Khab Antâx, the Overgate outside Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár at Khab Antâx, the Overgate outside ZajânaUlishár suggested that you continue on into Zajâna itself.Continue on into Zajâna and meet up with UlishárUlishár is inside the city of Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár inside Zajâna'This Kinta Elf has come in search of you, ${PLAYER}. He spoke with Shâra Mizâdi of a contagion in the forest to the north, and of his people's opposition to Ordâkh.

'She invited him to join her army, but he says you and he have urgent business elsewhere?''Shâra Mizâdi invited him to join her army, but he says you and he have urgent business elsewhere?''Glorfindel and Inkanûsh have gone on ahead to Zajâna, and they sent me to find you, ${PLAYER}. If you have resolved matters here to your satisfaction, let us make for the city. I will tell you what I know of its history on the way.

'The road to Zajâna lies north-west of here.''The road to Zajâna lies north-west of here. Glorfindel and Inkanûsh were eager to depart for the city.'Ulishár stands here, observing the distant city.'You must excuse me, ${PLAYER}, for my knowledge of the histories of Men is... sparse. Much of what I know of them I learned from the wagging tongues of my captors in Emax Dûl. But this must suffice!

'The city you see before us was once named Jaghân, the City of Gates, but with the passage of time the name gradually changed, becoming Zaghâna. When a great conflagration of unknown origin burned much of the old city to the ground, the ruling Ibili, or Elders, established the First Triumvirate, a Council of Three to govern the reborn city. They gave it the name Zajâna, City of Keys, to herald the dawning of a new era for the city of the scholars.

'But Ordâkh hungered for land and treasure, and when that empire seized the city more than one hundred and twenty years ago, they changed its name again: now it was Jajân, a puppet fiefdom held beneath the Hûl's distant boot. Gladly, the story does not end there! Let us proceed to Khab Antâx, the Overgate of the city, and I will tell you what happened only last year, and continues to this day.''Let us proceed to the gates and I will tell you what happened only last year, and continues to this day.'Ulishár stands by Khab Antâx, the Overgate, considering the city beyond.'This is the Overgate, one of the two famous gates that guards the pass through Mûr Ghala; the other lies in the southern wall of the city and blocks the road to the desert kingdoms beyond.

'But I told you I would finish the tale. With so many of the soldiers of Ordâkh and Shagâna fighting away in the north during the last year, the people of Jajân rose against their oppressors. During this Scholars' Revolt, sometimes called the Second Blue Mutiny, they seized control of the western side of the city and pushed east until they were stopped by the remaining Ordâkhai garrison. Widespread fighting and ensuing fires split the city in twain, and it remains so today.

'Now the free portion of the city is once again called Zajâna and it is ruled by a group of five: the Doorwards. The Ordâkh-controlled district of the city remains Jajân, and a new Triumvirate governs there. They believe this Second Blue Mutiny will end as did the first, crushed quickly by Ordâkh a hundred years ago, but as of yet it has not; the city remains divided.

'Let us continue onward into the city.''Let us continue onward into the city.'Ulishár stands within the city, considering the buildings nearby.'Despite its tumultuous history, Inkanûsh greatly desired to consult the libraries of Zajâna, for its scholars recorded much throughout the years that can be found in no other archive. He and Glorfindel have likely gone to search these troves for the knowledge they seek.'Zajâna the Splendid towers over Kighân, and visitors to the city are drawn to its libraries and cultural centres. Even so, peaceable travellers must avoid the presence of Ordâkh, which still attempts to control the city as it did before.Ulishár suggested that you climb the stairs to the west and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the commons of the city, so you might learn the layout of Zajâna.North of the commons lies the scholar's district of the city. Ulishár recommended that you go there and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the various sights of Zajâna.Glorfindel is by the stage in north-western Zajâna.Talk to Glorfindel by the stage in north-west ZajânaGlorfindel told you that Inkanûsh went to inspect Khab Kitâx, the Nethergate south of Zajâna.Find Inkanûsh at Khab Kitâx, the Nethergate south of ZajânaInkanûsh is at Khab Kitâx, the Nethergate south of Zajâna.Talk to Inkanûsh at Khab Kitâx, the NethergateUlishár is at Khab Kitâx, the Nethergate, and is very interested in something you have said.Talk to Ulishár at Khab Kitâx, the Nethergate'We should be safe to explore the city, as long as you remain aware that the Ordâkhai control the elevated district on the southern rise. Why not climb to the west and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the commons of the city, ${PLAYER}? It will do you some good to learn the layout of Zajâna. Afterwards we will meet up with Glorfindel and Inkanûsh and determine our next course.''Why not climb the stairs to the west and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> at the commons of the city, ${PLAYER}? It will do you some good to learn the layout of Zajâna. Afterwards we will meet up with Glorfindel and Inkanûsh and determine our next course.''You should learn the layout of Zajâna. Afterwards we will meet up with Glorfindel and Inkanûsh and determine our next course.''I see that Ulishár was able to find you, ${PLAYER}! I am glad.

'I have learned that a theatre troupe is about to perform an entertainment. Têrkar's Travellers, they are called, and if the talk I have overheard is to be believed, they are supposed to be quite skilled at their craft.

'But the Wizard has little time for such things, and he wanted to inspect Khab Kitâx, the Nethergate of Zajâna, instead of waiting here for the show to begin. If you find him there, he will likely show you the fabled libraries of the city. I will tell you if Têrkar and his Travellers live up to their reputation when next we meet!''Inkanûsh has little time for such things, and he wanted to inspect the Nethergate of Zajâna instead of waiting here for the show to begin. If you find him there, he will likely show you the fabled libraries of the city. I will tell you if Têrkar and his Travellers live up to their reputation when next we meet!''Ah, yes, my friend. Ulishár told me he came with you to the city.'

The Wizard gestures at the great southern gate.

'Whether you name the city Jajân like the Ordâkhai or Zajâna like the Doorwards, it seems both groups agree on this one point: the Nethergate must remain sealed against the possibility of invasion by the Shoghór of the south.

'You have already spoken with Glorfindel? He came not with you here; is there still time to visit the great library before we return to him?'

You tell Inkanûsh that the Elf-lord agreed to report on the quality of Têrkar's Travellers' performance, but there should still be time to first visit the library of Zajâna.'What is the matter?''I am sorry. Did you say the name "Têrkar's Travellers?"'

You tell Ulishár you believe that is the name of the theatre troupe Glorfindel intends to watch perform elsewhere in the city, and the Kinta's eyes widen with surprise.

'I know this group, ${PLAYER}, but I never thought to see them again! Têrkar and his theatre company were the very folk who aided me in my escape from Emax Dûl! I owe them my freedom, and my life!'Ulishár has asked for permission to see the theatre troupe that has come to Zajâna.Inkanûsh is at the Nethergate south of Zajâna.Talk to Inkanûsh at the NethergateInkanûsh is at the Nethergate south of Zajâna.Talk to Inkanûsh when you are ready to find the theatre troupeUlishár is by the stage in Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár by the stage in Zajâna'I will always be in Têrkar's debt for the kindness he showed me. "One last act of defiance before we march to their war," he told me when we were safely out of Emax Dûl and heading eastward. That was a week before he and his men were to report to the northern march with the rest of the Shagâni conscripts from Mûr Ghala. They wished not to go, but it would mean death if they refused.

'It is good to learn they have returned from the war!''It is good to learn the men of Têrkar's Travellers have returned safely from the war!''We came to this place to learn if the Thardúth pose a threat to these lands, and the great library of Zajâna, the Kalmakhíla, will aid me in this. The wisdom of many centuries is contained within. When last I walked these streets, it proved most informative on a great number of subjects!

'But let none claim I am without feeling. To learn that Ulishár's absent friends have come to the city must delay our visit to the archives of Zajâna. My research can surely wait for the duration of their performance. How long could such a thing be? The Kalmakhíla has remained secure even while the city burned, so protective have its keepers been over the centuries!

'Speak to me if you are amenable to this plan.''Then let us consume the day's entertainment, for Ulishár's sake, but also for our own. Afterwards we will go to the Kalmakhíla, the word-fortress of Zajâna.''It was good of Inkanûsh to delay his plans so we might attend this performance, but now I have more questions than before!'Ulishár is delighted to have encountered the theatre troupe that aided him during his escape from captivity, but he is troubled by their fewer numbers.Têrkar's Travellers may be in the western market of Zajâna.Find Têrkar's Travellers at the western market in ZajânaUlishár is at the western market in Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár at the western market in ZajânaThe playwright Satagêra is at the western market in Zajâna.Talk to Satagêra in ZajânaTêrkar's Travellers are at the western market in Zajâna.Talk to Imalêkh in ZajânaTalk to Jêsamin in ZajânaTalk to Mashkânu in ZajânaTalk to Atâlsi in ZajânaUlishár is at the western market in Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár at the western market in ZajânaThe playwright Satagêra is at the western market in Zajâna.Talk to Satagêra in ZajânaAsharmûl and Zimêlkha went to the residential district of south-east Zajâna.Find Asharmûl and Zimêlkha in the residential district of Zajâna to the south-eastZimêlkha is outside the Ordâkh-controlled district in south-east Zajâna.Talk to Zimêlkha in south-east ZajânaAsharmûl is outside the Ordâkh-controlled district in south-east Zajâna.Talk to Asharmûl in south-east ZajânaAsharmûl and Zimêlkha asked you to retrieve from the Ordâkhai the coin-purse containing the money Têrkar's Travellers earned from their performance.Retrieve the coin-purse from the Ordâkh-controlled districtThe coin-purse you were sent to retrieve has been emptied of its contents by the Ordâkhai, but you should return it to Asharmûl in south-east Zajâna.Atâlsi is at the western market in Zajâna.Glorfindel is at the western market in Zajâna.Talk to Glorfindel at the western market in Zajâna'When I knew Têrkar, it was customary after a performance for the company to gather at the nearest tavern to spend a portion of the day's coins on ale. I would like to see if that remains the case, ${PLAYER}! Let us look for them among the markets to the south-west, where it is possible they will acquire food and drink to sustain them following the performance!''When I knew Têrkar, it was customary after a performance for the company to gather at the nearest tavern to spend a portion of the day's coins on ale. I would like to see if that remains the case, ${PLAYER}! Let us look for them among the markets to the south-west, where it is possible they will acquire food and drink to sustain them following the performance!'Ulishár stands by his friends in Zajâna.'I have spoken to Satagêra, and it is as I feared. Her husband Têrkar perished in the north, as did several others of the troupe. The fire-eater Zurûsh, the twins Dârjo and Orukhíl... no word has come back of Khazín or Urûnam, but Imalêkh fears they must also have died in battle. She was wounded during the retreat before the mighty city and saw them no more.

'But the tidings are not all sad. Zimêlkha was held in reserve at the river-city and saw no fighting; her soldiers were driven south as Gondor advanced. And a fortunate star must shine upon Mashkânu, for Satagêra tells me he was wounded in the forest early during the fighting, and was not made to march upon Minas Tirith.

'I mourn for the friends who were so kind to me, ${PLAYER}, and I honour their memory! Despite this sorrow, my heart fills to once again see those of Têrkar's Travellers who remain to bring joy to the folk of Shagâna!''I mourn for the friends who were so kind to me, ${PLAYER}, and I honour their memory! Despite this sorrow, my heart fills to once again see those of Têrkar's Travellers who remain to bring joy to the folk of Shagâna!''I am Satagêra, the widow of Têrkar, and I am the playwright of this company of travelling players. I am pleased to meet any friend of Ulishár, for the Kinta is dear to us all, as he was to my husband. It was Têrkar's Travellers that smuggled him from the captivity of Ordâkh before most of our company were sent to war, did he tell you that?'

Satagêra smiles fondly.

'"One last act of defiance," my Têrkar called it. I am glad we did as he bade, for Ulishár's treatment in that city was hard. I think he would not have survived among the Ordâkhai for many more months.

'I invite you to speak with the others of my company. A cloud hangs over us this day, but do not let it trouble you. It is a private matter, and one we can handle ourselves.''I invite you to speak with the others of my company. A cloud hangs over us this day, but do not let it trouble you. It is a private matter, and one we can handle ourselves.''I am Imalêkh, trick archer and juggler of the company, and it is a pleasure to meet any friend of Ulishár. Satagêra insists upon writing villainous roles for me: the deadly marksman who threatens the heroes, the brooding lieutenant of the evil king. I have prevailed upon her to give me something romantic next time, something bawdy!

'We can all use a good laugh after what we went through in the north. I keep hoping Khazín or Urûnam will stroll up and demand a drink, but there has been no word. They were most skilled with their blades, but the foes arrayed against us were terrifying and numerous: frightening, mounted demons, bellowing for death.''A friend of Ulishár is a friend of mine! I am called Jêsamin, and I am a singer, tumbler, juggler, and player of drums for Têrkar's Travellers. What is it that you can do?'

You tell Jêsamin that you are a ${CLASS} and an adventurer, and ask where she is from.

'I live on the dusty road, friend, but I count myself among the Zajâni. I have not seen very many in Shagâna that look as I do, but that is fine with me! When Satagêra has need of someone to play a child, a goblin, or the legendary trickster Kêph, she can rely upon Jêsamin, for few indeed are as she is!''I am the wardrobe-master of this company, and am called Mashkânu.

'I caught sight of you from the stage, stranger. Who is the hooded man with whom you watched the performance?'

You answer that your companion is the Wizard Inkanûsh, and he may have taken issue with his portrayal in the play. Mashkânu's eyes narrow.

'Inkanûsh is a figure of legend and myth, an odd wanderer and a troublemaker. Are you perhaps mistaken?''I am Atâlsi. Like Satagêra and our former leader Têrkar, I am from Zajâna.'

The woman falls silent, but her eyes remain active, scanning the crowds.

'Forgive me if I seem distracted. I collect the coins for our performances, and during our latest it seems some quick-fingered thief replaced our purse with one of similar heft and weight, but different contents: grey stones instead of shiny coins. Zimêlkha and Asharmûl have gone in search of the villain, but until they return with the right purse I will not be satisfied.''We can all use a good laugh after what we went through in the north.''A friend of Ulishár is a friend of mine!''Inkanûsh is a figure of legend and myth, an odd wanderer and a troublemaker. Are you perhaps mistaken?''Zimêlkha and Asharmûl have gone in search of the villain, but until they return with the right purse I will not be satisfied.''Can we not help my friends recover their stolen purse of coins? I feel badly that the theft occurred as we watched the performance, and we noticed it not!

'What says Satagêra to this? I have only ventured with you for a short time, ${PLAYER}, but already I know you for one who helps others when the chance comes!

'We will not allow this to delay us overmuch; Inkanûsh will certainly understand, will he not?''We will not allow this to delay us overmuch; Inkanûsh will certainly understand, will he not?''Asharmûl and Zimêlkha are capable of retrieving our stolen purse, but if you are willing to help I will not refuse it.

'This is not the first time such a theft has occurred, and it will not be the last. Look for them in the residential district of Zajâna to the south-east, for the thief is likely to have fled for the Ordâkh-controlled district of the city.''Asharmûl and Zimêlkha can recover the stolen purse from the Ordâkh-controlled district of the city, but they will welcome your help if you offer it. Look for them in the residential district of Zajâna to the south-east.'Zimêlkha stands with Asharmûl in Zajâna.'Who seeks Zimêlkha?'

You explain that you are a friend of Ulishár the Kinta, and that Satagêra suggested you lend your aid to retrieve the stolen coin-purse.

'Has our playwright's opinion of our skill fallen so low that she thinks we two unable to retrieve this stolen purse? Atâlsi should have kept a closer watch on it! She knows how often thieves think to rob us of our hard-earned profit!

'What say you, Asharmûl?''Does Satagêra think we two cannot retrieve the stolen purse? What say you, Asharmûl?''For Têrkar we served as guardians and enforcers of the peace when trouble called, and now that he is gone we do the same for his widow Satagêra. She doubts not our skill, Zimêlkha. She sees opportunity.'

Asharmûl points toward the Ordâkh-controlled district of Zajâna, up the stairs to the south-east.

'If you retrieve the coin-purse from the Ordâkhai of this place, you will prove your friendship to Têrkar's Travellers. Hurry, now. The purse will be heavy with coin, for it contains all of our profit from the latest performance. The Zajâni love to hear about their resistance against Ordâkh, and they paid good coin for the privilege.''Retrieve the coin-purse from the Ordâkhai of this place and you will prove your friendship to Têrkar's Travellers. Hurry, now. The purse will be heavy with coin, for it contains all of our profit from the latest performance. The Zajâni love to hear about their resistance against Ordâkh, and they paid good coin for the privilege.''The Ordâkhai emptied the purse they took from us before you could retrieve it? That is a shame, my new friend.'

Asharmûl smiles at you, a twinkle in his eye.

'While you kept their attention, Zimêlkha and I took the opportunity to rob them anew. I think you will find that the number of coins we retrieved perhaps exceeds the number they took from us, but that is the price of thinking one might outwit Têrkar's Travellers!

'Bring this fresh, newly-heavy coin-purse to Atâlsi, with our regards. I think she will be pleased.''Bring that fresh, newly-heavy coin-purse to Atâlsi, with our regards. I think she will be pleased.

'No, I trust you. You are a friend.''Tell me you have retrieved the stolen coin-purse, friend, and that Asharmûl and Zimêlkha encountered no difficulty among the Ordâkhai.'

You place the coin-purse in her hand and Atâlsi almost drops it.

'Thank you for your help, friend of Ulishár! But I must ask... did it always contain so many coins? I knew not that our performance was such a success! If you ever have need of assistance, you can count upon the friendship of Têrkar's Travellers!''If you ever have need of assistance, you can count upon the friendship of Têrkar's Travellers!''There you are, ${PLAYER}. Inkanûsh sent me to bring word that he desires to speak with you.'Inkanûsh has gone to the Kalmakhíla, one of the most famous scholarly archives in all of Zajâna.Inkanûsh is in the Kalmakhíla in north-west Zajâna, beside the commons.Talk to Inkanûsh in the Kalmakhíla in north-west ZajânaInkanûsh has selected a tome and some pages for you to study in the Kalmakhíla.Read the open book on the table in the KalmakhílaInkanûsh has selected a tome and some pages for you to study in the Kalmakhíla.Read the pages on the table in the KalmakhílaInkanûsh is in the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Talk to Inkanûsh in the KalmakhílaSomeone approaches, and they sound angry.Someone approaches, and their anger is evidentMashkânu of Têrkar's Travellers has come to the Kalmakhíla, his weapon raised in anger.Talk to Mashkânu in the KalmakhílaIf you are able to <rgb=#00FF00>calm</rgb> Mashkânu, you might avoid the violence he intends in the Kalmakhíla.Have your efforts borne fruit?Remain on guard, just in caseHave your efforts borne fruit?Remain on guard, just in caseMashkânu's rage has been replaced with sorrow.Talk to Mashkânu in the KalmakhílaInkanûsh stands in the Kalmakhíla.Talk to Inkanûsh in the KalmakhílaMashkânu's rage has been replaced with sorrow.Talk to Mashkânu in the KalmakhílaInkanûsh stands in the Kalmakhíla.Talk to Inkanûsh in the KalmakhílaGlorfindel is at the Overgate north of Zajâna.Find Glorfindel at the Overgate outside ZajânaGlorfindel is at the Overgate outside Zajâna.Talk to Glorfindel at the OvergateUlishár is at the Overgate outside Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár at the Overgate'He has found his natural environment, as I knew he would! He has gone to the Kalmakhíla, the great library of Zajâna, and already he believes he has learned something of use, if not all of what he sought. He will tell you in person.

'The entrance to this word-fortress, the Khíla, is to the north-east of here, beside the commons.''Inkanûsh has gone to the Kalmakhíla, the great library of Zajâna, and already he believes he has learned something of use, if not all of what he sought. He will tell you in person.

'The entrance to this word-fortress, the Khíla, is to the north-east of here, beside the commons.''I have not yet learned all that I sought by coming here, but I have confirmed one of my suspicions, ${PLAYER}.

'It seems to my thinking that the Thardúth who lay hidden at Utug-bûr required two things to happen before they could trouble Middle-earth anew. First, they must pass beyond living memory of all who knew of them, and that was accomplished by the defeat of Sauron. Second, the chambers in which they lay buried must be brought to the surface by some geologic change: the movement of the earth, the diversion of the waters, the alteration of the rocks.

'So it was at Utug-bûr, and ever my thought turned upon this matter: could it happen elsewhere? I may have found part of the answer. Look here!''I may have found part of the answer I sought. Look here!'The pages of the open book are weathered and faded, but still legible.

"...would not have believed it ever ran so deep had I not seen it myself only months before, but the flow of the river was greatly diminished. By the third day following the great tremors it had slowed to a trickle. My friend insists that the Usâl and the Agâl too will never flow again as they had, and even the lake of Idagâl is now a cracked and dry basin. He named it Khadagâl, the Dry-whelm, but the herdsmen insist that is a name of ill omen and the lake must return. I think they are both right and wrong: I believe the name is ill-starred, but the waters of the lake have gone forever.

'Were the great tremors of the Urash-zâl a natural event, like the rising and setting of the sun or the passing of the seasons, or was it inflicted upon us by the whim of the Ghursâti? What could we have done to incur such wrath and vengeance?

'My friend thinks Shurash Hûl of Ordâkh is somehow to blame for the Urash-zâl, for the drying of the rivers has removed the last barrier to the northern progress of his armies. I say it must be happenstance, for the quakes were felt along the entirety of the Mûr. How could one man so change the face of the land, even one so ambitious as the Hûl?"'I may have found part of the answer I sought. Look here!'"The sound was terrible and heard by all, but the devastation in the wake of the Urash-zâl was worst of all, and it changed the face of the land: cliffs crumbled, boulders fell from the height, great chasms opened in the earth along the whole of the Mûr. Even the great northern gate of Jajân cracked, so widespread were the effects of the tremors of the Urash-zâl."'The people of Mûr Ghala named it Urash-zâl: the Din of Stone. It was a terrifying noise that could be heard all along the Ridge of Plenty, and its tremors changed the face of the land, opening great chasms, diverting the course of rivers, and drying lakes to their very beds. This was the cause of the Great Dearth that so affected much of Shagâna.

'One hundred and twenty years ago, the Hûl of Ordâkh committed a terrible act of change with effects that would ripple throughout all of Shagâna. His purpose was to open the way for his armies to conquer the lands to the north, but as we saw at Utug-bûr, I fear this act cleared the way for even greater evils. Now these ancient servants of Morgoth, these Thardúth, have broken loose from the enchantment that kept them preserved for thousands of years, and they stir again in the darkness beneath the earth.

'The Din of Stone revealed the hidden temple of Utug-bûr in which the Thardúth slept. How many other such hidden places might it have revealed elsewhere in Shagâna, ${PLAYER}?''Stand aside! The Wizard will pay for his crimes! I have come to slay this evil creature, this Mithrandir!

'The Wizard will die at my hand!''I cannot...' he weeps, his body seized by tremendous shuddering cries as tears stream down his face.

'Friend of Ulishár... how can one such as you... befriend this monster? This is no man... he is a creature of death! I swore to slay him, if that could be in my power... but...'

Mashkânu struggles to speak through wracking sobs.

'I was pierced by an arrow in the side, loosed from in hiding by green-garbed men in the northern forest. Others of my company marched on the great city, and fell during the fighting, but I retreated with others of the wounded to Mordor. From the shadow of the Black Gate I witnessed as the armies of the West approached, and I saw the fighting's end! So it was I witnessed the arrival of this man you defend, and heard his name spoken aloud by the Men of Gondor. "Mithrandir," they called him, and even from so far away I knew him for a Wizard.'

Mashkânu's bloodshot eyes flare with anger and with fear.

'I watched as the Wizard Mithrandir threw his arms into the air, and the very earth rocked beneath his feet! "The hour of doom!" I heard him command over the tumult. In answer to his spell, the mighty towers and rampart of the Black Gate crumbled into dust! Wherever he looked, death came to his enemies, and those that could not escape perished! I fled... but still I can hear the terrible voice of the Wizard and the death he caused!

'I swore to slay him, if ever I could... but now my sword-arm freezes... and I am unable to carry out my vow!''I swore to slay him, if ever I could... but I am unable to carry out my vow!''You are one of the theatre players who worked to free Ulishár from captivity when he was held by the Ordâkhai? I know you from the recent performance, and from the stories he has told to us of your company. Your name is Mashkânu, wardrobe-master of Têrkar's Travellers? But no one is one thing alone; you were a soldier pressed into service, forced to fight for Sauron against the Host of the West through threat or falsehood.

'You have been deceived again, Mashkânu, this time by your own eyes. It was no spell of mine that brought low the Black Gate or the Towers of the Teeth on the edge of Mordor. A great weapon of Sauron's was unmade, and its undoing heralded the destruction of all he had wrought, despite his terrible power.

'Some who had been held spell-enslaved by the Dark Lord of Mordor threw themselves into pits or fought desperate last stands, but many were of like kind as you, Mashkânu: held in thrall not by enchantment but by the fear of cruel commanders from Ordâkh and their tales of horrible monsters from Gondor and the North. Know now the truth, my friend, and be healed, if such a thing can be possible.''Know now the truth, my friend, and be healed, if such a thing can be possible.''Be healed? I can never be healed, though my wounds have closed and I once again speak the words Satagêra has given to me, seeking applause and coin.

'But so many of my friends returned not from the northern forest, or the fields outside the great city, or the Black Gate of Mordor. We were told that gate could never fall, but fall it did, and the vast armies of Barkhûsh and Ordâkh with it!'

Mashkânu wipes his eyes.

'When I beheld the Wizard Mithrandir earlier this day and learned he answered to the name Inkanûsh, I could not believe it. Inkanûsh is a figure of mischief and a troublemaker... but in none of the legends is he cruel, or destructive. It is a balm for my heart to learn the devastation I beheld was not caused by the trickster sprite of myth at whom I laughed in my youth, but was instead a final cruel consequence of Barkhûsh's evil.''You have provided a balm for the pain of my heart.''I would remain and speak with Mashkânu a little longer, ${PLAYER}. Furthermore, my research is not yet complete. I must search for more information about the Urash-zâl, the Din of Stone, within the Kalmakhíla. I believe you should continue without me on the western road as it climbs into An Shêru. I will catch up to you when I have finished my studies in Zajâna, but until I have done so you must remain alert for the presence of the Thardúth in Mûr Ghala.

'I spoke to Glorfindel of this possibility, my friend. He will be waiting for you at the Overgate outside the city.''I spoke to Glorfindel of this possibility, my friend. He will be waiting for you at the Overgate outside the city.''These tidings are unsurprising. I confess I expected Inkanûsh to remain here in Zajâna from the moment I set eyes upon the Kalmakhíla. Did he tell you about the Din of Stone?'

You tell Glorfindel that the Wizard seemed certain the Din of Stone may have revealed more vaults of the Thardúth than Utug-bûr alone, and the Elf-lord nods.

'He is rarely wrong, ${PLAYER}. These enchanted servants of Morgoth needed two things to threaten Middle-earth in the present age, and if Inkanûsh is correct they may have both of these things in Mûr Ghala: the end of their enchantment and access once again to the surface. We will take the road north-west as it climbs into An Shêru and keep watch for any sign of the Thardúth or their Utûgi warriors. Will Ulishár remain our guide on this road, or does he desire to remain in Zajâna with the travelling players now that they are reunited?''Will Ulishár remain our guide on this road, or does he desire to remain in Zajâna with the travelling players now that they are reunited?''Though mine was a bittersweet reunion with Têrkar's Travellers, I was still most pleased to see them again! Even so, I made a commitment to guide you on the road through Mûr Ghala and I will not forsake that pledge!'

Ulishár smiles.

'In any event, Satagêra told me they must also take the westward road in a few days. She has been hired to write a new play for the Travellers to perform; it may be our paths will cross again.'Galêris, one of Mizâdi's Subuzûri in Ingar-garâsh, has asked you to rescue her husband Baradûm. Baradûm was last seen patrolling near the cave of Jâr Obadal, home of ravenous spiders.Search for clues to Baradûm's whereabouts. Galêris mentioned he was last seen patrolling near the caves of Jâr Obadal. Jâr Obadal can be found south-east of Ingar-garâsh.Search for Baradûm in Jâr Obadal.Search for Baradûm in Jâr ObadalCheck the body to determine if it is Baradûm.Check the body to determine if it is BaradûmReturn to Galêris with the news of Baradûm's death.Return to Galêris with the news of Baradûm's death'Yes, my husband is missing. Will you help me find him?

