'You have helped curtail the threat of the jázgai, and doubtless saved countless lives of Subuzûri and travellers on the Great Road, and for that, I am grateful.'You are to travel to Jag Golód and locate Khónyo Bóro, a dangerous Ordâkhai captain who may have knowledge of Bârshud's whereabouts.Enter Jag Golód without arousing suspicion from the guards stationed there.Enter Jag Golód without arousing suspicionAvoid detection from the Ordâkhai guarding the campFind Khónyo Bóro watching the camp from on high within Jag Golód.Find Khónyo Bóro within Jag GolódDefeat Khónyo Bóro within Jag Golód.Defeat Khónyo Bóro within Jag GolódSpeak with Khónyo Bóro in Jag Golód.Speak with Khónyo Bóro in Jag Golód'Enough, I yield! I will accompany you back to this so-called Shâra's camp.'Shâra Mizâdi has requested your presence in the interrogation of Khónyo Bóro in her tent in Ingar-garâsh.Speak with Mizâdi inside the tent.Speak with Mizâdi inside the tentSpeak with Khónyo Bóro inside Mizâdi's tent in Ingar-garâsh.Speak with Khónyo Bóro inside Mizâdi's tent in Ingar-garâshListen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo Bóro.Listen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo BóroListen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo Bóro.Listen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo BóroSpeak with Mizâdi once more inside the tent.Speak with Mizâdi once more inside the tentListen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo Bóro.Listen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo BóroListen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo Bóro.Listen to the conversation between Mizâdi, Numûru, and Khónyo BóroGrab the bowl of food from the table inside Mizâdi's tent.Pick up the bowl of food from the table inside Mizâdi's tentPlace the bowl of food in front of Khónyo Bóro.Place the bowl of food in front of Khónyo BóroSpeak with Numûru inside Mizâdi's tent.Speak with Numûru inside Mizâdi's tentSpeak with Mizâdi inside Mizâdi's tent.'I will not tell you what you want to know, northern swine.''Question the Bóro, please.''I do not think he speaks the truth. He is close to breaking, I can feel it. He is scared, but still feels duty bound.''Now that we know Bârshud headed to Zajâna, we can make our own way there as well.''I knew he would crack! No one can resist a good stew... or even my stew.'You and your companions have joined the audience to watch a theatrical entertainment performed by Têrkar's Travellers, a troupe to which Ulishár is indebted.Ulishár eagerly waits for the play to begin in the city of Zajâna.Talk to Ulishár in the audienceWhile the performance continues, you should remain a model audience member.Watch the performanceSomething has captured Inkanûsh's attention.Talk to Inkanûsh in the audienceWhile the performance continues, you should remain a model audience member.Watch the performanceUlishár enjoyed the performance, but his expression is one of worry.Talk to Ulishár in the audience'I have some criticisms about my portrayal, ${PLAYER}.''I am so pleased to see that Têrkar's experience fighting in the north has not robbed him of his desire to perform!

'I hope you enjoy the play, ${PLAYER}. They were most skilled when I met them, and their catalogue of entertainments was dense and varied!

'Oh... they begin!''A hobbit? And so far from the Shire?

'This performance has become most interesting, ${PLAYER}.''I enjoyed the performance, ${PLAYER}, as I knew I would, but... there are so many missing faces.

'Did so few of my friends return from the war?'Across Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people.You remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Reach Kindred standing with The City of ZajânaYou remain true to your word, aiding the City of Zajâna earnestly. You should return to the city to speak with the quartermaster to be recognized for your efforts.Speak with Alahamâr at ZajânaAcross Kighân, your efforts increase your standing among the people. In the halls of Zajâna, your name and deeds grow with import.'Your voice now holds weight within these walls, ${PLAYER}. Your deeds and words align, and for that, the Doorwards grant favour. Choose from what has been set aside and know that our doors remain open to one such as you.'Numûru has requested your aid in rescuing Subuzûri taken by the spiders at the nearby cave of Jâr Obadal.Find the entrance to the caves of Jâr Obadal. Jâr Obadal can be found south-west of Ingar-garâsh.Find the entrance to the caves of Jâr ObadalDefeat spiders and search for missing Subuzûri warriors in Jâr Obadal.Defeat spiders in Jâr Obadal (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rescue trapped Subuzûri in cocoons (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Numûru in Ingar-garâsh with news of your victory.Return to Numûru in Ingar-garâsh'We have lost a fair few Subuzûri to the spiders near here. We could use your help in retrieving those still alive, if you're willing.

'We have long clashed with the Ordâkhai to the south. But they are not the only threat in these accursed mountains.

'There are spiders in the caves to the south-west. We have lost two patrols in the past day. We sent a rescue party, which was itself lost in the caves.

'These spiders are stronger, larger, and more fierce than any I have seen before. I know you to be a fierce fighter. Would you aid in rescuing our warriors?''This is joyous news indeed! I feared this to be a burial, but seeing them alive restores my spirit.'Shâra Mizâdi has requested your help in capturing a dangerous Ordâkhai Bóro located to the south who may have information on Bârshud's whereabouts.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh when you are ready to go.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh when you are ready to goSpeak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh and report your success.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh'My spies have penetrated the Ordâkhai camp to the south to find clues as to Bârshud's whereabouts.

'There is a leader of the Ordâkhai there, a man they call Khónyo Bóro. My scouts overheard him boasting of the time he spent with Bârshud, and how much they drank together. These could be lies designed to impress his fellow Ordâkhai, but we must find out if he has been spending time with Bârshud and what this Khónyo knows of his movements through Kighân.

'The Ordâkhai here are quite skilled and dangerous, so this task calls for subtlety rather than open combat. If you are willing, I would ask that you penetrate the camp and take this Khónyo Bóro and bring him back here for questioning. Then we will see if he speaks lies or truth about knowing Bârshud.''You've done it! This one looks quite formidable - did he put up much of a fight?''Be careful - the Ordâkhai here are skilled and quite formidable.'Now that you have captured Bóro Khónyo, Shâra Mizâdi requests your presence in his interrogation in her tent in Ingar-garâsh.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh when you are ready to go.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh when you are ready to goSpeak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Ingar-garâsh'We are to question the prisoner now. I would request your aid, if you are willing.''Thanks to you, now we know where Bârshud is headed.

'We must make for Zajâna immediately. It seems I have several matters to attend to there.''We will find out the truth of Bârshud's path from him, no matter the cost in time or blood.'Mizâdi has requested you accompany her to an audience with the Doorwards, the ruling council in Zajâna.Speak with Mizâdi in Zajâna. Mizâdi said she intends to speak with the Doorwards, who can be found near the Shorakhúna, the great council hall in Zajâna.Speak with Mizâdi in ZajânaFind Khâbakh within the Kalmakhíla to ask him for knowledge about the blight afflicting Adagím. The Kalmakhíla can be found on the western slope near the Khab Antâx within Zajâna.Find Khâbakh within the Kalmakhíla in ZajânaReturn to Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna.Return to Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna'Now that we know Bârshud is heading toward Idagâl, we can make our own way there as well. But first, I must speak with the Doorwards, the leaders of Zajâna. They have sent emissaries warning of war if I march on their city. I must convey to them I mean them no harm. If possible, I could even use their help in locating and capturing Bârshud.

'My scouts report Zajâna is a city at war with itself. Part of the city is controlled by a council of Ordâkhai and part of the city controlled by these Doorwards. I believe we are on the same side as these Doorwards, though I do not think they know it yet.

'I would ask you to accompany me and a small few others I trust to Zajâna, that I may speak with these Doorwards and try to enlist them as allies.

'Would you accompany me?''Thank you for accompanying me here. It is helpful to have a friendly face in this city. When you have a moment, I believe your friend Khâbakh can be found in the Kalmakhíla, the great library just behind me.

'Return here when you are done. I would have your company when I go before the Doorwards.''That sounds like quite the journey you undertook to see me. I shall endeavour not to disappoint. Now, how can I help you?

'Pestilence, disease? Ah yes, that nasty business in Adagím afflicting the bogs. I think I can help.''We should not tarry long. The Doorwards are expecting us within the Shorakhúna.'Numûru has requested your aid in making his infamous stew for the camp.Defeat oryx near Sâkhazai for their meat. Numûru is making his famous stew, nakhód júra, and needs oryx meat for it. Sâkhazai is located north-west of Ingar-garâsh.Defeat oryx near Sâkhazai (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather wood for a fire near Sâkhazai in Kighân. Sâkhazai is located north-west of Ingar-garâsh.Gather wood for a fire near Sâkhazai (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Speak with the farmer Aphadar outside of Ingar-garâsh to harvest chickpeas for the stew.Speak with the farmer outside of Ingar-garâshHarvest chickpeas from Aphadar's field outside of Ingar-garâsh.Harvest chickpeas from Aphadar's field (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Ingar-garâsh and tell Numûru you have collected the necessary items.Speak with Numûru in Ingar-garâshMake a camp-fire within Ingar-garâsh.Make a camp-fire in Ingar-garâshAdd a nearby cauldron to the roaring fire in Ingar-garâsh.Add a nearby cauldron to the roaring fireAdd in water, the stew ingredients, and Numûru's spices to the cauldron.Add in stew ingredients to the cauldronStir the stew in Ingar-garâsh.Stir the stew in Ingar-garâshTaste the stew in Ingar-garâsh.Taste the stew in Ingar-garâshReturn to Numûru with a bowl of the completed nakhód júra stew in Ingar-garâsh.'I can't believe it is you, ${PLAYER}! This calls for a feast! If you give me a hand, I'll teach you how to make Zajâni stew, nakhód júra. It tastes better than it smells.''It tastes good, right?! Good enough for camp food, anyway. It's always better when you make it yourself.''I have no quarrel with Mizâdi or her soldiers. I have seen precious few brigands near Ingar-garâsh since her forces arrived.

'Yes, you may take some of my chickpeas for your stew, with my blessing, friend.''This is excellent! So very fresh! Now we must build a fire and add the special herbs I've gathered!'There are many things to do in the fishing village of Sen Chatâk. Elder Jangâli has requested you prove your worth by speaking with some of the local peoples and aiding them with their troubles.Elder Jangâli has requested you speak with Ahâto, Kôrzan, and Jûshal about their daily troubles and assist them where you are able. Each can be found within Sen Chatâk.Speak with Ahâto in Sen ChatâkSpeak with Kôrzan in Sen ChatâkSpeak with Jûshal in Sen ChatâkReturn to Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk and let her know you have spoken with each of the villagers.Return to Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk'There is much to do in Sen Chatâk. We have been beset by raids recently, attacks from both the west and the east.

'I can scarcely catch my breath, there has been so much to do, to organize and defend our village. But our people have other needs as well.

'If you wish to be of service, speak with Ahâto, Kôrzan, and Jûshal. Each have concerns that I wish to address, but with these attacks I simply cannot.

'Will you aid Sen Chatâk?''There is one goblin in particular that threatens Sen Chatâk. If he does not die, Sen Chatâk will never know peace.''Yes. Yes! Of course you can help! I'm so glad you came. It's the fish. They aren't biting! Well, not yet anyway.''What could I have wanted? I don't - oh yes, of course! My family heirloom! It's priceless, and it was stolen from me.''Thank you for speaking with them.

'I trust you will see their needs fulfilled, and hopefully I can soon return to dealing with mundane troubles as those. These troubles we've had recently are far too exciting for me.'A local elder has requested your aid in dealing with a band of marauding goblins to the north.Defeat goblins at Glúkroz to send a message that the goblin raiders there should stay far from Sen Chatâk. Glúkroz can be found north-east of Sen Chatâk.Defeat goblin raiders in Glúkroz (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat goblin watch-keeps in Glúkroz (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat goblin sappers in Glúkroz (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk with news of your victory.Return to Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk'Our village was once quiet. When the goblins came from the north, it changed everything.

'They send their raiding parties to set alight our homes and terrorize our villagers. We have but a few warriors to stand against them, and fewer by the day.

'You wish to help us? You look like someone who knows their way around a blade. Defeat the goblins of Glúkroz and send a message that they should move on from Kighân or die where they stand.''You defeated a number of them? Oh that is such a relief. I have seen too many of our warriors fall this past fortnight.

'I am heartened. They will either take the hint and move on from these lands, or my warriors will visit them in Glúkroz and finish what you have started.'Majâryan, a merchant in the Court of Larinâsta, has asked you to help with her cheese deliveries to local customers.Pick up the cheese deliveries from Majâryan's table in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna.Pick up the cheese deliveries from Majâryan's table (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Deliver the cheese baskets to each of three customers in Zajâna.Return to the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna and speak with Majâryan.Speak with Majâryan in the Court of Larinâsta'Who has the best shéra in Zajâna? It's Majâryan, of course, of course!

'But who has the time to spread the gift of cheese? Not Majâryan! She has to make the cheese. She has to sell the cheese! You can help Majâryan, can't you?

'Deliver these baskets of cheese to our needy customers. Do it quickly, or the cheese will melt in this heat! I've put the addresses on the baskets. You will help Majâryan, won't you?''Good shéra is a gift worth spreading, is it not? Not good shéra, what am I saying? The best shéra!

'Take some shéra for yourself! It's only right for you to share in it!''These are perfect, thank you, and thank Majâryan for me.''Perfect timing. I was about to start cooking. Thank you for this.''This cheese is a blessing. Thank you for bringing it so far.'Doorward Rokhâran has asked you to investigate reports of goblin attacks in Sen Chatâk, a fishing village to the north of Zajâna. Speak with Elder Jangâli there to find out more about the attacks.Speak with Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk. Sen Chatâk can be found in Kighân to the north of the city of Zajâna, within Mûr Ghala.Speak with Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk'There is much for us to look after here in Zajâna, but there are many in Kighân who live outside its walls.

'A young man from Sen Chatâk, a fishing village north of here came to us last night. He told us a band of vicious goblins have been raiding their town, razing houses and injuring innocents.

'You have done so much for us here - but would you be able to investigate this? I would love to be able to go myself, but so much commands my attention here.''You are here to help? Bless you. We will take all the help we can get, even from an outsider.

'The goblin attacks have grown more frequent, and more brazen. Each day we fear will be our end.'Mizâdi knows your strength and honour and looks to your assistance again, as she takes her battle directly to the Ordâkhai.As Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Reach Ally standing with the Hamât RenewedAs Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Speak to Lumgâna at Mizâdi's encampment'We have witnessed your strength—in arm, in spirit. You have stood beside us through triumph and grief. We have spilled blood, not as strangers, but as comrades. And now, we draw near to the ending. Hamât shall rise again, renewed by Mizâdi's hand.

'There is no other she would call to my side in this hour, ${PLAYER}. The campaign must find its end—and before that hour comes, she shall call upon you again, if you will attend her.

'Captains and lieutenants clamour for aid as there is much to do and prepare; all that you do in her name shall bring you honour, and raise your standing among her host. Lumgâna will keep your share of the spoils safe, and grant you access to our deeper stores.

'Stay steady, ${PLAYER}. The road narrows, and the end draws near. We will need your strength before the last banner is raised.''${PLAYER}, your deeds in this campaign are well known to us, and our debt to you is already great. By command of Mizâdi, it falls to me to see that all which is owed to you is kept in order and delivered in full.

'As our fortunes rise, so too shall your reward. The stores shall swell, and with them, the treasures set aside in your name. We shall see this venture through to its end—and you shall not be forgotten in its telling.'The Doorwards have requested Mizâdi's presence in Zajâna. Go with Shâra Mizâdi to show the Doorwards she means them no harm, and rally their support in her hunt for Bârshud.Speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna when you are ready to go.Speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna when you are readySpeak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna.Speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna'The Doorwards have requested my presence. I aim to show them I mean them no harm, and rally their support in my hunt for Bârshud.''That could have gone better. But they will trust my actions, if not my words, which I have tripped over once more.''It is time. I am heartened by your company, my friend. I confess I am a bit nervous, caught out in a foreign land on a dangerous mission. It is good to see a few friendly faces nearby.'Jarôba has asked for your help in finding and feeding many of the cats in Zajâna.Find and pet ten of the cats to be found in Zajâna.Return to Jarôba in Zajâna with the names and locations of the cats you have found.Return to Jarôba in Zajâna'There are so many cats here! There are at least… five!

'There are so many to hunt for and find! And pet, of course! Will you help me find some of the cats in Zajâna?''You helped out the cats? Me too! I keep finding more every day!

'Thanks, I'll add these to my list. There are so many - probably even over fifty!'The road to An Shêru has a reputation for danger, but that is the way you must go.The road to An Shêru runs by Zajâna. You should begin by going to the north-east.Travel north-east of Zajâna on the road to An ShêruSomething strikes you as suspicious about the signpost north-east of Zajâna.If Dagûrum had allies, leaving the signpost as it is may help them ambush other travellers. You should repair the signpost and ruin their plans.Repair the brigand-tampered signpostThe road to An Shêru leads north-west of Zajâna.Continue further north-west along the road to An ShêruBrigands may have tampered with the signpost north of Zajâna as well.You should repair this signpost north of Zajâna as you repaired the other.Repair the brigand-tampered signpost north of ZajânaRahâzi has arrived in the company of two Hamâti soldiers.Talk to Rahâzi north of ZajânaThe Hold of Jalkhíla is located to the east in Kighân.Collect blades from brigands in Kighân (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect bows from the Hold of Jalkhíla (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rahâzi asked you to meet up with her party at Gadim-ûn in An Shêru.'An Shêru, the Height of the Sky, was once known for the loveliness of its high forests and flowering meads, but no longer. Now the plateau is parched and dry, a cracked waste among the high peaks of the Mûr. If Inkanûsh is correct that other vaults of the Thardúth remain, this would seem to be a likely place for them.

'The road that runs from Zajâna to An Shêru has a reputation for danger. We must remain alert for brigands as we make our way north-east.''We must remain alert for brigands as we make our way toward An Shêru.''Our paths cross once again, ${PLAYER}, and it seems they each lead to the Height of the Sky!

'Shâra Mizâdi entrusted me and two of her soldiers to scout the plateau of An Shêru ahead of her army. We have not yet climbed high enough and already we have run afoul of brigands. I see you have as well!

'The bandits came from the Hold of Jalkhíla to the east. If you deprive them of blades and bows with which they trouble folk on the road, you will be aiding the Shâra and any others who must travel this way. We can meet up at Gadim-ûn where the road leads into An Shêru and discuss our further plans.''Deprive those brigands of the blades and bows with which they trouble folk on the road, and we can meet up at Gadim-ûn where the road leads into An Shêru.''Those brigands would surely have known not to trouble Shâra Mizâdi or her army, but most travellers have neither her numbers nor her ferocious mien! It is good you have shown them the road is not undefended.

'I felt a chill when I arrived here, and my companions say they felt the same. I suspect more than brigands haunt these ruins.'You have heard the Kalmakhíla is a vast repository of knowledge. Perhaps there are books on the Tarsâri here?Locate the red book 'A History of Zajâna' in the south wing of the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Locate the red book 'A History of Zajâna'Locate the red book 'A History of Zajâna' in the south wing of the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Flee from the Tarsâri and return to Rayíka just outside the Kalmakhíla.Flee from the Tarsâri and return to Rayíka!Read the note in the mysterious book in the Kalmakhíla.Read the note in the mysterious book on the tableFind a man dressed in yellow in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna.Find a man dressed in yellow in the Court of Larinâsta'There is much to learn in the Kalmakhíla. You wish to learn more about the Tarsâri? Yes, I believe there is an early history of Zajâna that touches on their lineage and formation.

'Look for the book 'A History of Zajâna'. It should be in the south wing of the Kalmakhíla, a great red book in the second set of shelves. If you retrieve it, I will show you the section referencing the Tarsâri.''I mean you no harm... currently.

'Our lady has been watching you. You are curious about the Tarsâri. The Tarsâri are curious about you as well. We always have need of someone with your skill in execution.

'Don't go far - I may have work for you - and answers to your questions.'The Pohâni Têredal has knowledge of the Pale Blight, but needs your help completing some of his tasks whilst he conducts research for you.Retrieve the flowers from outside Zajâna that the Pohâni Têredal mentioned.Retrieve flowers from outside Zajâna (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to the Pohâni Têredal in the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Return to the Pohâni Têredal in the Kalmakhíla in ZajânaCreate raised beds for the flowers in the flower bed within the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Create raised beds for the flowers within the Kalmakhíla (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Plant the harvested flowers in the holes you've dug within the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Plant the harvested flowers within the Kalmakhíla (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather water from the bucket near the planted flowers in the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Gather water from the bucket near the planted flowersWater the planted flowers in the flower bed within the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna.Water the planted flowers in the flower bed (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to the Pohâni Têredal in the Kalmakhíla and report on your success.Return to the Pohâni Têredal in the Kalmakhíla'A history of Adagím? The pestilence? Well you have come to the right place. Yes, I remember reading something about that quite recently.

'I am quite busy here in the Kalmakhíla, however. There is so much to do. We need to bring fresh plants for our flower beds. As you can see, they are totally barren. It will never do.

'Wait. I had a brilliant idea. You gather the plants and repot them here, and I will look for information on, what was it again? The Pale Blight. In Adagím. Yes, yes, I think I have just the thing. The blue plants, out the north gate. They grow on the hillsides just outside the city. Bring back six - no eight - of them. That should spruce this wing up nicely.''Thank you, friend. I really did not want to do any gardening this week. I was quite dreading that.

'You were asking about the Pale Blight, yes? I have some information from some tomes I gathered whilst you were attending to the garden. The Kintai are our best source of knowledge for the plight of Adagím. They are reclusive, but our Pohâni have travelled throughout Adagím and Shagâna to document their history and beliefs.

'Several accounts in this "History of Adagím" mention the Ordâkhai. These Kintai mention Sívigush, some sort of monster or legendary beast, but others blame the coming Ordâkhai who managed somehow to dry the rivers and dam up naturally flowing waters near Adagím. This resulted in Adagím becoming much more marsh and swamp-like, as waters from nearby lakes and ponds overstrode their bounds. Without the rivers to sweep them through, they festered, creating bogs where previously the land had been green and rich with forests.

'The Ordâkhai likely had a hand in creating the conditions for this Pale Blight. I know not how to improve the situation in Adagím, but the forces of Ordâkh ensconced in Emax Dûl may have more answers.''Those are perfect. Put them in the central bed, just over there. You'll want to create a little rounded mound of dirt for each of them, then place them in the centre and give them a sprinkle of water.

'I will continue with this research in the meantime.'Elder Jurmêna has tasked you with destroying the goblin weapons of war by using their own explosives against them.Pick up barrels of black powder and destroy goblin catapults in Glúkroz. Glúkroz can be found north-east of Sen Chatâk.Pick up barrels of black powder (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Set alight black powder near catapults within Glúkroz.Set alight catapults with black powder (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Elder Jangâli in Sen Chatâk.'The goblins mean to make war on Sen Chatâk. Our few able-bodied were able to scout their camp. They are building catapults and gathering barrels of hot tar.

'We cannot stand against these weapons. We can barely repel the raiders with axes and bows, let alone these larger, more foul weapons.

'But I believe in their greed they have given us an opening. They have been gathering barrels of black powder, no doubt for some putrid purpose. I suspect we can use their own weapons against them. If you set their black powder alight near their catapults, we can destroy both weapons with one flame.

'Will you help us?''Oh what a sight it must have been! I wish I had seen it for myself! I can only imagine the flame and smoke that must have spewed forth. Well done, outsider!'Mashûmi's tortoise has gone missing. She says local brigands likely captured him, and took him back to the Hold of Jalkhíla. Find and free the tortoise from Jalkhíla for Mashûmi.Mashûmi believes Glimglam was captured by brigands and brought back to the Hold of Jalkhíla. Explore the Hold of Jalkhíla and search for Glimglam.

The Hold of Jalkhíla can be found north of Sen Chatâk.Search for Glimglam at the Hold of JalkhílaOpen Glimglam's cage to free him.Open Glimglam's cage to free himReturn to Mashûmi in Sen Chatâk and let her know her tortoise is now free.Return to Mashûmi in Sen Chatâk and let her know her tortoise is now free.Return to Mashûmi in Sen Chatâk'My tortoise is missing, can you believe it? Would you help me find him?

'I think those mean robbers took him yesterday. They stole everything, even Glimglam! Will you help me get him back? My ma won't let me have a sword, not even a wooden one. But if I did, I'd show those robbers what for! They'd be sorry they ever took Glimglam!

'Will you help me get him back?''You found him! What? No, he's not too big! What have I been feeding him? Whatever he wants to eat, of course!'The Doorwards have requested Mizâdi's presence in Zajâna. Go with Shâra Mizâdi to show the Doorwards she mean them no harm, and rally their support in her hunt for Bârshud.Join Mizâdi and Numûru outside the council chambers.Join Mizâdi and Numûru outside the council chambersEnter the council chamber with Mizâdi within the Shorakhúna.Enter the council chamber with Mizâdi within the ShorakhúnaListen to the conversation between the Doorwards and Mizâdi.Listen to the conversation between the Doorwards and MizâdiPick up the sword from the table in the Shorakhúna.Pick up the sword from the table in the ShorakhúnaPlace the sword on the table in front of Sarâgh, Master of Spears.Place the sword on the table in front of Sarâgh, Master of SpearsListen to the conversation between the Doorwards and Mizâdi.Listen to the conversation between the Doorwards and MizâdiListen to the conversation between the Doorwards and Mizâdi.Listen to the conversation between the Doorwards and MizâdiSpeak with Numûru inside the Shorakhúna.Speak with Numûru inside the ShorakhúnaSpeak with Mizâdi inside the Shorakhúna.Speak with Mizâdi inside the ShorakhúnaListen to the conversation between Mizâdi and the Doorwards.Listen to the conversation between Mizâdi and the DoorwardsListen to the conversation between Mizâdi and the Doorwards.Listen to the conversation between Mizâdi and the Doorwards'I believe we should accept these terms. They are the best for which we could hope.''I agree with Numûru. We will show them via our actions that we mean them no ill will.'In order to get the book Khâbakh needs, you will need to haggle with the local book-seller in the Court of Larinâsta.Speak with Merbâna in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna about buying the book Khâbakh requested. The Court of Larinâsta is located due east of the Kalmakhíla, whilst still in Zajâna.Speak with Merbâna in the Court of LarinâstaHaggle with Merbâna in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna about how many oranges she needs in exchange for the book Khâbakh requested.Haggle with Merbâna in the Court of LarinâstaContinue haggling with Merbâna in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna about how many oranges she needs in exchange for Khâbakh's book.Continue haggling with Merbâna in the Court of LarinâstaFinish haggling with Merbâna in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna about how many oranges she needs in exchange for Khâbakh's book.Finish haggling with Merbâna in the Court of LarinâstaGo to the hillside outside the Khab Kitâx outside Zajâna where the orange trees grow and gather seven oranges for Merbâna.Harvest oranges outside Zajâna (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Merbâna in the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna with the harvested oranges.Return to Khâbakh in the Kalmakhíla in Zajâna with the book he requested. The Kalmakhíla can be found near the Shorakhúna in central Zajâna.'There is a particular book that mentioned the Pale Blight, called "Nisigím, the Emerald Wood". It is an account of a group of Pohâni who travelled amongst the Kintai and wrote down their tales and legends.

'I don't believe the Kalmakhíla has a copy. You should head to the Court of Larinâsta here in Zajâna. There is a book-seller I know there, Merbâna. Here is some coin. I believe she has a copy of the book. Bring it here and I will find the relevant passage for you.''Five? That is far too few oranges. Do you consider me a fool? There are no fools in the Larinâsta.

'I will exchange the book for no fewer than twenty oranges.''These oranges are perfect. I would have taken five, but seven is even better.

'Here is the book you requested. Give Khâbakh my regards.''Three? You don't seem very skilled in negotiation friend. You are supposed to get closer to the number I suggest, not farther away.

'I suppose I could accept a paltry ten oranges in exchange for the book.''All right, all right. You'd see me driven from hearth and home, and leave me destitute with no oranges, but I will accept your terms. Seven oranges in exchange for the book.

'Now go, bring me the oranges before I change my mind.''Ah yes. This is the book I needed. Let me see, yes, this is the relevant passage for you.

'You were asking about the Pale Blight, yes? Here it is. Travelling scholars mention a legend among the Kintai that may be relevant. They speak of Sívigush the Earth Terror, a great beast of unimaginable size and ferocity. These accounts say that Sívigush carried within it a great plague, that corrupted all it touched, turning the forests pale and dead wherever it strode.

'These accounts attribute this Pale Blight to Sívigush, and claim it emanated from a place in Adagím once said to be Sívigush's abode. This site was later settled by a group of Men who excavated the area. It is known amongst the Elves as Nagakhêdi's Folly. Apparently the Men who were excavating the site were building a grand abode for their king, Nagakhêdi. Reports disagree here on the details, but each claim that the workers perished to a man within the cursed tunnels.

'This is all rumour and happenstance. But if you are indeed searching for the source of this Pale Blight, you might explore Nagakhêdi's Folly in Adagim. Then tell me what you find. I would love to publish something new about Adagím - as long as I don't have to go there myself. Dreadful place.''Saráz záro-dan, friend. Yes, I have that book. I don't want coin for it, however. I have a powerful desire for oranges. They grow freely on the hillsides outside Zajâna, beyond the Khab Kitâx, this time of year. Yes, I believe I will accept oranges in exchange for the tome.

'How many oranges are you willing to get for me?'Jûshal has requested your aid in returning an item stolen by brigands on the nearby Great Road.Jûshal has requested your aid in retrieving a family heirloom of his. Locals brigands stole it, and he believes they have taken it back to their camp, the Hold of Jalkhíla.

The Hold of Jalkhíla can be found north of Sen Chatâk.Recover Jûshal's ancient heirloomReturn to Jûshal in Sen Chatâk.Return to Jûshal in Sen Chatâk'I wonder if I might have a moment of your time, friend? It's about a family heirloom, something quite precious to me.

'I was out walking, enjoying the sunshine yesterday, and when I came back brigands had raided Sen Chatâk! What's worse, they made off with my most prized possession. An heirloom shield, it's been in my family for generations.

'I know where these brigands are encamped. They call it the Hold of Jalkhíla. They think they are untouchable. That they can't be touched. But you'll touch them, won't you? With your blade, or what have you? Please, I really need your aid.''Old and dirty? Well, yes, I suppose it is. But it's filled with sentimental value... and insects.'Elder Jangâli requests you go to the Hold of Jalkhíla north of Sen Chatâk and deal with the band of thieving brigands harassing Sen Chatâk and travellers along the Great Road.Elder Jangâli has asked you to go to the Hold of Jalkhíla and deal with the band of thieving brigands harassing local townsfolk and those who pass by on the Great Road.

The Hold of Jalkhíla can be found north of Sen Chatâk.Defeat brigands at the Hold of Jalkhíla (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Jangâli in Sen Chatâk with news of your success.Return to Jangâli in Sen Chatâk'As if the raiders were not enough, we have a camp of brigands to the north.

'Their raids have become more frequent, and more brazen, as they have seen no consequences. We have little choice - what few warriors we have are protecting us from the goblins. But these brigands only become more emboldened by the day. They strike from their encampment to the north, known as the Hold of Jalkhíla.

'Innocent travellers and townsfolk are being harassed, robbed and worse. Will you help us deal with these fiends?''Thank you. Travellers will breathe a sigh of relief as they travel the Great Road now.

'As will I, and many in Sen Chatâk. The Great Road draws these sorts, like bears to a honeycomb. There will be others, but this has given us time to prepare. You have my gratitude.'Doorward Azagâya has directed you to speak with one of her merchants, who may have information on Bârshud's whereabouts.Speak with Doorward Azagâya outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna about rumours concerning Bârshud and his whereabouts.Speak with Doorward Azagâya outside the Shorakhúna in ZajânaSpeak with Muzâwi at the Court of Larinâsta in Zajâna.Speak with Muzâwi at the Court of Larinâsta in ZajânaCollect herbs and defeat rats in the farming district of Zajâna.Collect herbs in the farming district of Zajâna (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat street-rats in the farming district of Zajâna (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Muzâwi in the Court of Larinâsta with the herbs and report your success.Return to speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna with Muzâwi's report.Return to speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna'We should speak with Azagâya. She said her merchants may have information on Bârshud.

'Now that we have shown our worth to her, I believe she may yet prove a willing ally for Hamât Renewed.''Thank you for helping aid in our fight against the Jewelled Council. I may not trust you fully, but I will help you.

'You should speak with Muzâwi. She sometimes sells fish and herbs near Lajór. She may have seen your Bârshud.

'You can find her in the Court of Larinâsta, the large marketplace not far from here.''If that is where Bârshud was seen in Zajâna, that is where we must go. Our path draws ever deeper, but I cannot allow Bârshud to slip my grasp again.''Yes, yes, everyone wants things from Muzâwi. But what will help Muzâwi to clear the rats and set the traps and gather herbs, I wonder?

'It will be you? Yes, yes, then I will tell you what I know. Gather the herbs and kill any rats nearby. Here, I will mark on your map where in Zajâna they are.''Saráz záro-dan. Ah, yes, these herbs look lovely. I will trust you on the rats. Don't make me regret not making you bring me their tails.

'Yes, I saw this Bârshud. He was with the Ordâkhai Bóra, very important looking, outside the Hall of Lajór.

'There were three of them. Jórbol Bóro, Nirjára Bóro, and Akráho Bóro, all speaking with Bârshud. You can find them all outside Lajór. But that area is quite unfriendly to any who aren't loyal to the Empire of Ordâkh. Don't say Muzâwi didn't warn you.'Go to the Hall of Lajór and compel the Bóra that Bârshud spoke with to reveal what they know of his movements and whereabouts.Speak with Doorward Azagâya outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna about how to locate the Hall of Lajór in Zajâna.Speak with Doorward Azagâya outside the Shorakhúna in ZajânaEnter the area outside the Hall of Lajór in Zajâna. The Hall of Lajór can be found across the bridge and up the stairs from the Shorakhúna.Enter the area outside the Hall of Lajór in ZajânaDefeat the Ordâkhai gathered outside the Hall of Lajór.Defeat Ordâkhai outside the Hall of Lajór (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Find and interrogate Jórbol Bóro, Nirjára Bóro, and Akráho Bóro outside the Hall of Lajór.Find and interrogate Jórbol BóroFind and interrogate Nirjára BóroFind and interrogate Akráho BóroReturn to speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna with what information you have gathered.Return to speak with Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna'I know not these details of which this merchant speaks. We should speak with Azagâya here outside of the Shorakhúna. She will know what to make of them and where we must next go.''Of course. Mizâdi and her retinue do not know Zajâna as the locals do.

'Here, I will mark on your map of where Muzâwi speaks. It is across the great bridge and up the stairs. Our Bandázan work tirelessly to keep the Ordâkhai contained, but fighting spills out into the streets from time to time.

'It is where you last went to defeat and push back the Ordâkhai menace. Unfortunately those efforts must be ceaseless, or else we will lose Zajâna once more to the Jewelled Council and the Empire of Ordâkh.''So Bârshud means to make for Idagâl and Emax Dûl. Then that is where we too must venture.

'Thank you for once again being my ready companion here in a strange land. I will make my peace here and then venture west to Idagâl. If you make it there yourself, find me. It would hearten me to see you once more.''Stop! I have seen how you cut through our forces - I will tell you what I know.

'He spoke of gathering forces, of rebuilding an army.''I do not wish it to be my skin freshly flayed. I will tell you what Bârshud said to me.

'He seeks out the Hûl. That is all I know. Whether it is due to grievance or seeking boons, I know not. He could speak of nothing else.''Mercy! I do not wish to fight such a capable combatant. I know what Bârshud is planning.

'He means to make for Emax Dûl, in Idagâl. He seeks the Hûl there, that he may raise an army anew and return to Khûd Zagin. He seeks revenge for a defeat there.'Ahâto, a local hunter in Sen Chatâk, has asked you to hunt down Lugadúr, goblin chieftain to the marauding goblins of Glúkroz.Go to Glúkroz and destroy goblin weapon racks and defeat Lugadúr, chieftain to the goblins. Glúkroz can be found north-east of Sen Chatâk.Destroy goblin weapon racks (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Lugadúr in GlúkrozReturn to Ahâto in Sen Chatâk to report on Lugadúr's demise.Return to Ahâto in Sen Chatâk'There is one goblin who leads the rest. I believe they call this one Lugadúr. He is the worst, by far.

'Even if you destroy their weapons of war and defeat many of them, this Lugadúr will still raid our village. He has no fear, and a powerful lust for blood. I have seen Men and goblins like him before. He wants for nothing but destruction.

'Defeat him, and we may yet see peace. The remaining goblins will scatter without their leader to guide them. May they scatter far from Kighân. Will you move to defeat him?''You have brought peace, not only to Sen Chatâk, but to countless peoples throughout Kighân.

'I hope that these goblins move on from our lands, but even if they do not, they are disorganized and leaderless. They will be easy prey for our warriors.'Azagâya wishes for you to prove your friendliness to Zajâna by defeating Ordâkhai in Zajâna near the Hall of Lajór.Speak with Doorward Azagâya outside of the Shorakhúna in Zajâna.Speak with Azagâya outside of the Shorakhúna in ZajânaDefeat Ordâkhai forces near the Hall of Lajór in Zajâna. The Hall of Lajór is located in the eastern end of Zajâna.Defeat Ordâkhai forces in Zajâna near Lajór (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return and report your success to Azagâya outside of the Shorakhúna in Zajâna.Speak with Azagâya outside of the Shorakhúna in ZajânaReport back to Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna.Report back to Mizâdi outside the Shorakhúna in Zajâna'I believe Doorward Azagâya is our best bet as an ally. We need to prove our worth to her, and that we mean her no harm.

'Go to her and see if she has a task that needs doing. The Doorwards said we need to lead with actions, well then, let our actions show our pure intentions.

'Seek out Doorward Azagâya, ${PLAYER}, and do what she asks of you. I believe she will then tell us what we need to know about Bârshud.''You are more effective than you appear.

'Thank you. You may consider me a friend. Have I anything you should need of in Zajâna, do not hesitate to ask.''If you want to prove your usefulness, I will not deny you that chance.

'We have pushed the Ordâkhai back, but they are still a thorn in the side of Zajâna. They are barricaded in the Hall of Lajór and its immediate surrounds.

'You and Mizâdi claim to be friends of Zajâna. Friends do each other favours. Take out a number of the Ordâkhai near the Hall, and I will be more friendly to you both.''I knew it! We just had to show our good intentions. All right, now we see if she is willing to tell us more information about Bârshud and his contacts in Zajâna.'The denizens of Kighân have many tasks, big and small, for you to accomplish.Complete quests in Kighân in Mûr Ghala.Complete quests in Kighân in Mûr GhalaKôrzan has asked you to gather crab meat and herbs for use as fishing bait on the shores of the White-wrath River, near an area infested with zizani.Harvest fresh dill and collect crab claws from the ponds of Charganpâta.Harvest fresh dill (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect crab claws (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to the fisher Kôrzan in Sen Chatâk with the gathered herbs and crab meat.Return to Kôrzan in Sen Chatâk'The fish are no longer biting. I know my skills haven't diminished. It must be the bait!

'Yes that's it. My lures, my technique, all are masterful. But this bait... how can I catch tilapia from the White-wrath with naught but worms? Look at them! They barely wriggle. They should dance across my fingers!

'The best bait... the best bait is crab! Yes, that's what we need! Would you get me some crab meat, outsider? I know with that my prowess would return. And some fresh dill with which we shall cook all these fish. We will be awash in fish, fish up to our waists!''Crab makes for the best bait, and if we don't catch anything with this, I think we'll be fine cooking the remaining claws. Thank you, my friend.'The fisher Kôrzan tells you that the local zizani have muscled the fishers of Sen Chatâk out of their spots along the White-wrath River. Defeat these zizani so that the fishers may return.The fisher Kôrzan has requested you deal with the nearby zizani, who have moved into the best fishing spots along the White-wrath river. The zizani can be found clustered near the foetid ponds of Charganpâta. Charganpâta is north-east of Sen Chatâk.Defeat zizani near Charganpâta (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Kôrzan in Sen Chatâk with news of your success.Return to Kôrzan in Sen Chatâk'The river betrays us. She has been our ready companion for years, but now she's abandoned us.

'Nothing that we cannot fix, however! It is the zizani. They have gotten quite aggressive, and moved into our favourite spots along the river. They feed on the same fish that we hunt.

'I am a fisher, not a warrior! And even if I was, the zizani are large and fierce. But you look like a fierce sort! Perhaps you could deal with these zizani, that we may fish again in peace? They make their camp near the foetid ponds of Charganpâta.''Thank you. I believe nature is once more in balance, and the river can now prosper, along with our people. And I do so love to fish upriver.'Evil spirits possess the bodies of the Dead throughout the barrens of north-east An Shêru, causing them to rise and walk again. The Jatsakóri have been using their presence as an effective shield to hide from their Ordâkhai former masters.Gíshgi is uncomfortable with the nearby wights. She has a theory that they may fear flames enough that placing lit torches at the entrances to the crypts would keep the wights within, and has asked you to try it. There are crypt entrances at Gadim-ûn, Naxar Dâl, and the Crypts of Khâb Daku in north-eastern An Shêru.Place torches near the crypt entrances (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have placed lit torches at the entrances of the crypts in north-eastern An Shêru in an effort to ward off the wights and keep them inside, where they belong. You should return to Gíshgi in Gadim-ûn and tell her of your success.Talk to Gíshgi in Gadim-ûn'I do not like it here, surrounded by these wights. They are ghastly, but Nabatínu insists they are what keep us hidden. The Ordâkhai dare not come here to the Mashâli Barrens for fear of them, and says the mindless things unknowingly protect us!

'The wights are just as likely to kill us as the Ordâkhai, so I do not know how this is better. It does seem to me, though, that these wights shy away from fire. I cannot help but wonder if placing burning torches at the crypt entrances would keep them confined and away from our camp.

'Would you try it? Three torches at the entrances to the three main crypts should do.''I am glad you have not joined the unliving! Did you place the torches?'

You tell her you did.

'We will wait and see if it helps contain them. Whether it does or not, I am grateful to you.'Nabatínu has asked you for the personal favour of checking on the welfare of his wife, Kugâna, a Jatsakóri still under the thrall of the Bokhóri sorceresses.You should talk to Faeron at Gadim-ûn to ask if he will accompany you.Talk to FaeronNabatínu has asked you for the personal favour of checking on the welfare of his wife, Kugâna, a Jatsakóri still under the thrall of the Bokhóri sorceresses. You should talk to Faeron at Gadim-ûn when you are ready to go, and the Ranger will accompany you.Complete Instance: A Personal MatterIn the company of Faeron, you travelled to Gebarakór, where you witnessed the arrival of Ombátha, the leader of the Bokhóri. You also spotted Nabatínu's wife, Kugâna, who was alive but did not look well.Talk to Nabatínu'I fear that I have not been entirely honest with you by failing to reveal something important. Among the Jatsakóri I seek to free is my heart, my wife, Kugâna. Despite my best efforts, I have been unable to free her mind from the hold of the Bokhóri. Every day that she is imprisoned in both body and mind, her pain is mine, and I die on the inside.

'You have given me so much already, but I humbly ask you for another favour. Would you mind looking in on her and seeing if she is still lives? Has she suffered any abuse beyond the hard labour?

'Please, my friend. I trust you to seek her out and tell me the truth. Last I knew, she was at a work camp called Gebarakór, south-east of Khanâg Gur. Please go there and tell me how she fares. I beg you not to go alone, though. Perhaps one of the Rangers would go with you?''I am ready to go if you are, my friend.''Say no more, of course I will go with you. I can hardly refuse a stealth mission, can I? Besides, I would like to see these Jatsakór thralls for myself. We may learn something of the fell magic used to ensorcel them.

'Tell me when you are ready, and we will be on our way.'You tell Nabatínu about everything you and Faeron saw at the work camp.

'Thank you for this news of Kugâna, though seeing Ombátha there bodes ill. She is the strongest of the Bokhóri, and is therefore the leader. As far as I can tell, she is the one who commands all of the Ordâkhai in An Shêru, but it would be news to me if she also commands these new Orcs.

'Ombátha's mind is twisted with darkness, and she takes a great deal of pleasure in inflicting pain upon others. She is unstable, evil, and to be avoided at all costs. We must come up with a plan to free the others, and soon.'Rhadrog, Faeron, and Nabatínu have devised a plan to acquire supplies needed by the Jatsakóri by attacking an Ordâkhai supply caravan.Before leaving Gadim-ûn to meet with the others for your attack, the dwarf Mêshaget wants to talk to you.Talk to MêshagetMêshaget wishes to join you for your assault on the Ordâkhai supply caravan. You should talk to Nabatínu at Gadim-ûn when you are ready to join them for the attack.Complete Instance: Ambush at the HeightsYou have successfully completed your attack on the Ordâkhai supply caravan. You should talk to Nabatínu about the results.Talk to Nabatínu'The food you have brought us is generous, but we need other supplies as well. Your friends pointed out that before we can help others, we must be in good health ourselves. We need clean water, medicine, and blankets. We could also use better weapons. While you were away, Rhadrog and Faeron helped me devise a plan to take these needed supplies from our enemies.

'Kôth Rau, the larger Ordâkhai fort to the south, sends regular supplies to Ona Bóha, their outpost in the west. If we can catch the supply caravan on the road, it would be easier than attacking a fortified location. I know of a hill beside the road that would hide us from view as we make our preparations. Just let me know when you are ready.

'Before we go, I believe your dwarf friend wants to talk to you.''Are you prepared for battle, ${PLAYER}? Let us be off, then.''The conditions these people are living in is alarming, ${PLAYER}! I have lived my entire life with the comforts offered by the great city of Jiret-menêsh, and while I knew life on the surface was fraught with danger, I did not think upon what that may mean for the lives of people who are not warriors.

'I will tell you now that whatever aid you bring to these people, I would be as grateful as if they were my own people. This dwarf will not stand idle while such suffering goes on so close to home. I will go with you on this attack, and I will test the sharpness of my axe on these Ordâkhai!''I admit that I did not expect all of us to escape unscathed. I am astonished that our attack went so well! We got many of the supplies we needed the most, thanks to you. The Rangers have even offered to teach some of my people how to use the weapons we gained. I could never have done all of this without your help.'Ordâkhai troops have established a camp at Onabóha, from which they launch patrols of western An Shêru.You should defeat Ordâkhai at their encampment at Onabóha to hamper their efforts in the region. Onabóha is located in western An Shêru, just south of Jiret-menêsh.Defeat Ordâkhai at Onabóha (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You defeated many Ordâkhai at their encampment at Onabóha, located in western An Shêru, just south of Jiret-menêsh.CompletedOrdâkhai patrols from Onabóha attempt to maintain the Great Southern Empire's control of western An Shêru. Perhaps someone should make the job more difficult for them.Ordâkhai patrols from Onabóha are finding it more difficult to maintain the Great Southern Empire's control of western An Shêru, thanks to your efforts.Mizâdi knows your strength and honour and looks to your assistance again as she takes her battle directly to the Ordâkhai.As Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Reach Esteemed standing with the Hamât RenewedAs Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Speak to Lumgâna at Mizâdi's encampmentYour renown grows among the Hamât Renewed. Return now to the Quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'Were you sworn to her banner, you would ride alongside Numûru and Razâl; the warriors know this well, and hold you in no lesser esteem than her closest kin.

'It is honour well-earned, ${PLAYER}. You have won the wages of war. Take this gift, and see what more our stores may yield to one so proven.'An Ordâkhai host of Barkhûshim zealots occupies Kôth Rau in southern An Shêru.You should defeat Ordâkhai at their encampment at Kôth Rau to show them that their presence will not go unchallenged. Kôth Rau is located in southern An Shêru, at the source of the now-dried Usâl River.Defeat Ordâkhai at Kôth Rau (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You defeated Ordâkhai at their encampment at Kôth Rau in southern An Shêru, located at the source of the now-dried Usâl River.CompletedOrdâkhai soldiers hold dominion over the area, their authority unchallenged. It would be good for everyone else in An Shêru if you changed that.You have defeated a number of the Ordâkhai of Kôth Rau, showing the southern invaders that their control of An Shêru will not continue to go unchallenged.You have come to An Shêru, the Height of the Sky, and must now brave the dangers of this inhospitable land.Rahâzi has introduced you to the Hamâti soldiers with whom she travels. They can be found at Gadim-ûn in An Shêru.Talk to Shenzâmin at Gadim-ûnRahâzi has introduced you to the Hamâti soldiers with whom she travels. They can be found at Gadim-ûn in An Shêru.Talk to Kishâtal at Gadim-ûnIt would appear Mashdagâl has something he wants to say.Talk to Mashdagâl in Gadim-ûnMashdagâl told you that if you want to help so badly, you might deal with creatures of evil at Naxar Dâl.Find Naxar Dâl to the north of Gadim-ûnWights stalk Naxar Dâl in northern An Shêru.Defeat wights at Naxar Dâl (${NUMBER}/6)Wights stalk Naxar Dâl in northern An Shêru.Defeat wights at Naxar Dâl (5/6)Shenzâmin is in Naxar Dâl. What has he discovered?Talk to Shenzâmin in Naxar DâlShenzâmin discovered the corpse of a man of Ordâkh wearing sorcerous robes in Naxar Dâl.Examine the corpse of the sorcerer of Ordâkh in Naxar DâlYou picked up a parchment covered in a sorcerer's mad ravings.Read the parchment you recovered from the sorcerer of OrdâkhGlorfindel will want to see the writings of the sorcerer of Ordâkh. The Elf-lord waits for you in Gadim-ûn.'The air is thin and cold in An Shêru. I like it not! I much prefer the warmth and life of Ambarûl to this desolate height. Are pleasant villages so few here that we must shelter among crypts and tombs? But it was Shâra Mizâdi's command to scout this place for her foes, and we will do it.

'I understand one of my companions met you some time ago, ${PLAYER}. His name is Shenzâmin, and he speaks most highly of you. The other is named Kishâtal, and he has proven a sturdy ally to me as well, besting a number of brigands that thought to ambush us between Ingar-garâsh and the road through Kighân.''I understand one of my companions met you some time ago. His name is Shenzâmin, and he speaks most highly of you.''I am Shenzâmin of Kûr Anzar, ${PLAYER}. We last spoke outside the town of Urmâkh, in Imhûlar. I did not expect to see you again so far from where last we met, but the surprise is a pleasant one!

'It is Shâra Mizâdi's command that we watch the road through An Shêru for any sign of foes that might endanger her army as it travels. Rahâzi of Iridír says she does not like this place; I echo her thoughts, for the thin air and the cold fills me with unease.''It is Shâra Mizâdi's command that we watch the road through An Shêru for any sign of foes that might endanger her army as it travels.''Shâra Mizâdi entrusted us with scouting the Height of the Sky for any sign of Ordâkhai aggression. We are also to treat with the Temámir dwarves that are said to dwell in this place, and to offer them the friendship of Renewed Hamât.'

Kishâtal smiles with wry amusement.

'Shenzâmin and I have spoken of this curiosity before: that neither of us knew ourselves to be Hamâti before the rise of Shâra Mizâdi. Now each of us would die for her, and for Renewed Hamât. I hope it will not come to that, but if it does I will die knowing my death will be of some use for the Shâra and the welfare of my people, and hers.''If this man has something to say, he should say it to my face!''This is not the first talk of this sort I have overheard. Every new ruler thinks to better the lives of their people, but when the time comes to make good on such promises they are found wanting!

'Is this Mizâdi any different? If she really wants to aid the people who must live in An Shêru, her followers must prove her good intentions. There are wights to be found at Naxar Dâl. Have you the courage to face them?'

You ask Mashdagâl why he ventures so near to tombs known to be inhabited by wights, and he chuckles.

'That is my own business, stranger. If they prove too fearsome for you to handle, I am certain your warrior-queen will be along shortly to recover your equipment and your body. I hear this Mizâdi is different than the others.''If Mizâdi really wants to aid the people who must live in An Shêru, her followers must prove her good intentions. There are wights to be found at Naxar Dâl. Have you the courage to face them?''I found something, ${PLAYER}. Look here! This slain man wears the distinctive robes of a sorcerer. Did he come here in search of treasure? I said nothing back at Gadim-ûn, but I think that unmannerly scoundrel might be a treasure-seeker himself, if not an outright brigand. Should we tell him of this man's fate, it may encourage him to seek another profession!

'This corpse tells us the Ordâkhai have already pressed forward into An Shêru, and this man, at least, ran afoul of the wights. Has he anything upon him that might tell us the movements of his allies?''This slain man wears the distinctive robes of a sorcerer from Ordâkh. What led him to his death among these ruins? Search his body and we may learn the answer, or if his allies are nearby.''You found a parchment on the slain sorcerer of Ordâkh? What does it say?''The Elf-lord Glorfindel will want to see that parchment, ${PLAYER}. Perhaps he will know what those ravings could mean.''What have you found, my friend? From the look in your eye I deem you have discovered something that must concern us.'

You hand the ravings of the Ordâkhai sorcerer to Glorfindel, and he closely studies the parchment. After a moment he looks up at you.

'These words remind me of the Dagor Carlanthir, ${PLAYER}. To me this writing sounds like someone with less fortitude than Inkanûsh experiencing the mind-tampering of the Thardúth.'The ravings of a slain sorcerer of Ordâkh point to the presence of the Thardúth in An Shêru.Mashdagâl has become increasingly agitated by the talk in Gadim-ûn.Talk to Mashdagâl at Gadim-ûnUlishár is at Gadim-ûn, and wishes to speak to you.Talk to Ulishár at Gadim-ûnThe Crypts of Khâb Daku are south-west of Gadim-ûn.Travel to the Crypts of Khâb Daku south-west of Gadim-ûnIt is your duty to face the wights at the Crypts of Khâb Daku, and wherever you find them.Defeat wights and shades at the Crypts of Khâb Daku (${NUMBER}/6)It is your duty to face the wights at the Crypts of Khâb Daku, and wherever you find them.Defeat wights and shades at the Crypts of Khâb Daku (5/6)You heard the cries of hyenas and a scream to the east, near Spoiler's Crag.Hurry east to Spoiler's Crag and find the source of the screamThe slain sorcerer at Spoiler's Crag may have something of interest on his person, as did the other you found.Search the body of the slain sorcerer at Spoiler's CragYou picked up another parchment covered in a sorcerer's mad ravings.Read the mad scratchings of the sorcerer of OrdâkhGlorfindel will want to see the writings of this sorcerer of Ordâkh as well. The Elf-lord waits for you in Gadim-ûn.'This raises a question in my mind about the reach of the Thardúth. For these servants of vanished Morgoth to threaten the people of An Shêru in this way... they must have either travelled from Utug-bûr, as we did, or they awoke within a vault somewhere within Mûr Ghala, as Inkanûsh supposed. We must find out.

'Something drove that sorcerer mad, and he was unable to resist as it clawed its way inside his mind. He would have been our enemy, there is no doubt, but none should perish as he did.''Something drove that sorcerer mad, and he was unable to resist as it clawed its way inside his mind. He would have been our enemy, there is no doubt, but none should perish as he did.''Creatures prying into your mind, driving you mad? This is nonsense, surely?'

Mashdagâl's eyes are wild, his fear evident.

'What evils have you brought to this place? Did your warrior-queen not intend to aid us? Instead you speak of ancient sorcerer-kings tampering with our very thoughts!''What evils have you brought to this place? Did your warrior-queen not intend to aid us? Instead you speak of ancient sorcerer-kings tampering with our very thoughts!''This is my understanding of Glorfindel's words: the Ordâkhai came here in search of something, and the Thardúth ensnared them. If the slain sorcerer you found was not alone, his allies may have perished nearby. They might also tell us what happened to them, as he did. Alive, if they be more fortunate than he.

'Before you returned and Glorfindel's words replaced his bombast with fear, Mashdagâl spoke of wights to the south-west. There we may find more information about the Thardúth, and if not we will at least combat the danger such creatures pose to the folk of An Shêru with their continued presence.''Before you returned and Glorfindel's words replaced his bombast with fear, Mashdagâl spoke of wights to the south-west. There we may find more information about the Thardúth, and if not we will at least combat the danger such creatures pose to the folk of An Shêru with their continued presence.''The ill fortune of these Ordâkhai sorcerers continues? They should never have come to the Height of the Sky!'

Glorfindel studies the parchment you found.

'I think our Wizard's supposition was correct, ${PLAYER}. There is another vault of the Thardúth, and it is located within An Shêru.'Mizâdi knows your strength and honour and looks to your assistance again as she takes her battle directly to the Ordâkhai.As Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Reach Esteemed standing with the Hamât RenewedAs Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Speak to Lumgâna at Mizâdi's encampmentYour renown grows among the Hamât Renewed. Return now to the Quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'Were you sworn to her banner, you would ride alongside Numûru and Razâl; the warriors know this well, and hold you in no lesser esteem than her closest kin.

'It is honour well-earned, ${PLAYER}. You have won the wages of war. Take this gift, and see what more our stores may yield to one so proven.'Nabatínu worries that the only way to free the rest of the enthralled Jatsakóri may be to defeat Ombátha, which he fears may not be possible.You should talk to Rhadrog about Nabatínu's theory that defeating Ombátha may allow the enthralled Jatsakóri to break free.Talk to RhadrogRhadrog has asked you to scout Kôth Rau, the Ordâkhai stronghold in south-central An Shêru.Go to the Ordâkhai stronghold of Kôth RauYou have come to Kôth Rau to scout out the situation at the Ordâkhai stronghold and found them flying the banners of Sauron, called Barkhûsh in this part of the world.Destroy banners of Barkhûsh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})As you destroyed banners of Barkhûsh at Kôth Rau, you noticed that they are holding prisoners here. You should assess the situation with the prisoners.Investigate the prisonersAs you got closer to some of the prisoners, a shiny glint of something metal on the ground caught your eye. You should pick it up.Pick up the shiny objectYou have done what you can for the moment at Kôth Rau. You should return to Gadim-ûn and discuss what you found with Nabatínu.'I fear that the only way to save the rest of the Jatsakóri would be to defeat Ombátha in battle, though I do not know if we have the strength as things currently stand. At the moment Barkhûsh was defeated, his servants suffered a moment of weakness. I think perhaps some of their power came from him, and that when a sorcerer is defeated, their spells may unravel to some degree.

'I confess that I do not wish to face Ombátha. Just the thought strikes me with such dread that I begin to sweat, but I can think of no other way. Perhaps the Rangers have another idea?''It's an interesting theory, but I cannot guess whether or not it would work. One thing is certain, though, someone should scout that fort, Kôth Rau. Are the Ordâkhai and the Utûgi truly cooperating, or is there tension between them?

'If you would, ${PLAYER}, go and take a look. No need to go rushing in all on your lonesome, but we should learn more about what's happening there.'You tell Nabatínu about the prisoners and show him the shiny button you found in the gravel.

'This is a curious find,' he says, looking closely at the button. 'I have not seen its like before, but I will ask the others.

'As for the prisoners, these Orcs; Utûgi, you called them? They have been patrolling the roads and attacking caravans, just as your friend Khagâmi experienced. They kill many, but they also take many prisoners. This must be where they bring them, but to what end, I do not know. I do not see them being turned into Jatsakóri, so it must be for some other evil purpose.'Rhadrog and Faeron have spotted some curious footprints near their camp.Though the gait of the tracks implies that they were not made by a soldier or bandit, Rhadrog is curious about who else may be roaming this barren land. He has asked you to take a look at the footprints for yourself, and he will assure Khagâmi's safety.Find the footprints Rhadrog mentionedYou have found the footprints of which Rhadrog spoke. You should follow them and see where they lead.Examine the footprintsYou have found the footprints of which Rhadrog spoke. You should follow them and see where they lead.Follow the trail of footprintsYou have found the footprints of which Rhadrog spoke and followed them to this road. For some reason, Faeron is here? You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron'Since you are here, I should tell you of some recent footprints we've spotted. Perhaps the trail would lead you to bandits, but I expect not. The gait did not appear to me to be that of a bandit or soldier.

'Go and see them for yourself, ${PLAYER}. Khagâmi will be safe here. You have my word.''Did you not notice me following you? No? Outstanding. It is good for me to always keep my skills sharp.

'Do you see those Men ahead? Those are no bandits, nor Ordâkhai, but I know of no other people in this area. It is curious indeed.'Faeron and Rhadrog both agree that they should help the Jatsakóri.The Rangers plan to relocate to Gadim-ûn so they can help the Jatsakóri. You should talk to Khagâmi at the Rangers' camp to see if she would like to go to Gadim-ûn also.Talk to KhagâmiKhagâmi wants to accompany you to Gadim-ûn. You should talk to Rhadrog at his camp about your next steps.Talk to RhadrogIn an effort not to put a strain on their resources, Rhadrog has asked you to go to Abníshu, a kalorkhûn roost to the west and collect some meat to bring with you to Gadim-ûn.Find Abníshu, the kalorkhûn roostYou have found Abníshu, the kalorkhûn roost Rhadrog told you about. You should defeat some kalorkhûn to collect their meat to bring with you to Gadim-ûn.Collect kalorkhûn meat (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})As you gathered kalorkhûn meat for the people of Gadim-ûn, you heard the sound of combat coming from somewhere nearby. You should find from where the sound is originating.Find where the sounds of combat originateYou have found a dwarf at Abníshu, also fighting the kalorkhûn. You should talk to him.Talk to the dwarfYou have gathered kalorkhûn meat for the people of Gadim-ûn, and met a dwarf, Mêshaget, along the way. You should return to Gadim-ûn in north-eastern An Shêru with the meat.Return to Gadim-ûnYou have gathered kalorkhûn meat for the Jatsakóri, and returned to Gadim-ûn. You should talk to Nabatínu and give him the meat.'We must do what we can to help these people. If this Nabatínu speaks true, our mission here in An Shêru is complete. If the land to the west is equally as dry and rocky as it is here, it's unlikely the blight would be able to spread in this direction. We will return to Adagím, but only after we have given aid to the Jatsakóri.

'You should talk to Khagâmi and see if she wishes to remain in our company.''She stays? That is well. A scribe alone in this desert would not fare well. Faeron and I will take care of the camp and bring Khagâmi with us to The Hallows of Gadim.

'If you don't mind, I have a favour to ask of you. I don't want our presence to put a strain on these people's resources. To the west is a roost of large, flightless birds the locals call kalorkhûn. Rumour has it that they taste quite good... like chicken, but more of it! If you go there before meeting us at The Hallows of Gadim and bring back some kalorkhûn meat, it would make for quite a feast.''I would rather arrive at Jiret-menêsh a little late than not at all, which is what I think would happen without your company. Besides, your Ranger friends are fascinating. I would enjoy talking to them more.

'Growing up, we were taught that Men of the West were monsters more than Men, violent and unlearned. I see now that this is untrue. They are just people, much like us. Rhadrog tells the most amazing stories! Yes, I will stay in your company. These Jatsakóri sound interesting as well!''Oho! Thank you for your help, friend! Kalorkhûn are fiercer than I expected. I would have defeated them no problem, given time. But why not have a new friend distracting the beast, eh?

'I am Mêshaget, and I am honoured to meet you!' He says with a bow. 'And who might you be, friend?'

You return the bow and introduce yourself to the boisterous young dwarf, asking if he is from Jiret-menêsh.

'That I am! I am on my way to Zajâna to see the state of things there and see if they may be interested in opening trade.'

You tell him that you know a woman who would be very interested in meeting him.

'Is that so!? Do not tell me, she is from Zajâna and is doing the same thing I am, but only the other way? Do not answer! I will see if my guess is correct when I meet with her.'

You give Mêshaget directions to Gadim-ûn and agree to meet him there.'${PLAYER}, this meat is quite the generous gift! We will all eat well tonight, thank you!'Gôbezhir Firmhand, master smith of Jiret-menêsh, has decided to give his life's masterwork as a gift to Mêshaget.You have joined Gôbezhir to present the axe he made to Mêshaget. You should talk to Gôbezhir.Talk to GôbezhirYou have joined Gôbezhir to present the axe he made to Mêshaget. You watch Gôbezhir give the weapon to its recipient.Watch Gôbezhir present the weapon to MêshagetAs you watched Gôbezhir give the weapon to Mêshaget, your company was attacked by a roc huntress. You should defeat the roc attacking you.Defeat the roc huntressAs you watched Gôbezhir give the weapon to Mêshaget, your company was attacked by a roc huntress. Together, you and Mêshaget defeated the aggressive avian. You should talk to Mêshaget.Talk to Mêshaget'By the Fathers, that was close! Those awful birds grow more aggressive by the day! It was my wish that the axe serve Mêshaget well, but I did not expect it to be so soon, or to be present to witness it!

'Come, ${PLAYER}, walk me back to the forges, where I belong.''It is beautiful, is it not, ${PLAYER}? The blade is razor-sharp and will hold its edge longer than any other before it needs to be sharpened! A lifetime of crafting experience and knowledge went into this weapon. It was also made from the kindness of a new friend who helped an old dwarf when he needed it.'

Gôbezhir playfully winks at you.

'And here comes Mêshaget!''Hah! That cursed bird wanted to taste dwarf, and got a taste of my axe instead!

'This axe is a true marvel indeed. A weapon such as this needs a name, don't you think? I shall call it... Semakéfë! That means Sky-splitter in Temîk. Perhaps you should see if old Gôbezhir is all right. I am relieved that he doesn't seem to need new trousers.'

Mêshaget, flush with excitement from the battle, laughs as he admires his new weapon.Nabatínu has asked you for the personal favour of checking on the welfare of his wife, Kugâna, a Jatsakóri still under the thrall of the Bokhóri sorceresses.You have travelled with Faeron to Gebarakór, the Ordâkhai open-pit mine. You should talk to Faeron.Talk to FaeronAnother group is arriving at Gebarakór. You should move closer to hear what they are saying.Move closer to the OrdâkhaiAnother group has arrived at Gebarakór. You should listen in on what they are saying.Observe the conversationYou witnessed the arrival of Ombátha, the leader of the Bokhóri, sorcerers with the ability to ensnare Men's will. You should <rgb=#FF0000>look</rgb> for Kugâna, Nabatínu's wife, among the assembled workers.<rgb=#FF0000>Look</rgb> for Kugâna among the workers'We have seen what we came to, and should make our way back to Gadim-ûn. Are you ready to leave?''Quietly now, ${PLAYER}. Their own torches should blind their eyes to our presence, but they could still hear us.

'Look there, someone approaches. Let's move closer to listen.''Look around, do you see anyone meeting Kugâna's description?'Rhadrog, Faeron, and Nabatínu have devised a plan to acquire supplies needed by the Jatsakóri by attacking an Ordâkhai supply caravan.You should talk to Nabatínu when you are ready to begin the assault.Talk to NabatínuYour company has defeated the Ordâkhai defending the supply caravan. You should talk to Nabatínu about what to do next.Talk to NabatínuNabatínu has given orders to everyone in your company. You should collect the supply crates.Collect supply crates (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected the supply crates. You should talk to Rhadrog about what to do next.Talk to Rhadrog'Yes, it looks like we have everything we will get from this adventure. Let us return to Gadim-ûn.''Here they are now, just over the hill behind us. We will split and engage the caravan from both sides. Faeron and Mêshaget, you charge the front. Rhadrog and I will strike from the rear. ${PLAYER}, choose your direction of attack, but remain alert and assist whoever is most in need.

'Are we prepared?'

The others nod.

'Then we begin!''Good work, everyone. Hopefully, that is all of them. My sword arm tires! I no longer have the endurance I had in my youth, when I was a soldier.

'Faeron and Rhadrog, keep watch for more reinforcements. Mêshaget, will you join me in searching for anything of use? Missives, orders, keys, anything of that sort. ${PLAYER}, collect what crates of supplies you are able. Swiftly, now. The longer we tarry, the more likely it is that the entire army will descend upon us.''You have the supplies? Excellent, we should be on our way, then. Ask Mêshaget if he and Nabatínu are satisfied with their search.'Khagâmi is tired from her travels west through An Shêru, and is ready to settle in for the night. By joining her at her camp-fire, you may learn something of her journey.Khagâmi is tired from her travels west through An Shêru, and is ready to settle in for the night. Together, you have set up a camp-fire and made yourselves as comfortable as you are able to while on the road.Talk to KhagâmiWhile you and Khagâmi were talking at the camp-fire, you both heard a suspicious sound from the east. You should investigate what it was.Investigate the sound you heard to the eastWhile you and Khagâmi were talking at the camp-fire, you both fell under attack! You should help Khagâmi.Help KhagâmiWhile you and Khagâmi were talking at the camp-fire, you both fell under attack! You should defeat the bandits.Defeat the remaining bandits (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You received some unexpected help while defending your camp. You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron'My apologies, when that man came out of nowhere, I just froze! I thought I would die just from the fright alone. Thank you for saving my life! And especially that archer.

'Who is that? Do you know him? You spoke like old friends.'

You tell her that you are indeed friends with him and you did not expect to see him in An Shêru.

'I, for one, am quite glad you are both here! It seems I chose my travelling companion well.''${PLAYER}, I should tell you of my task and destination. I am on a quest to find an ancient dwarf city called Jiret-menêsh. Tales claim that it has been in An Shêru longer than the sun has been in the sky. It is well documented in the libraries of Zajâna that the Temámir were avid trading partners with Zajâna and others for centuries.

'When Urash-zâl, the Din of Stone, shook the ground and dried the Usâl, the people of Zajâna had their own difficulties. It went unnoticed for some time that all contact with Jiret-menêsh had been lost. Most believe that the Temámir were either all killed or they abandoned this forsaken land, which is quite plausible.

'However, there have recently been whispers that the hearty Dwarves of Jiret-menêsh have emerged from the ground, where they have been hiding in isolation for one hundred years. Doorward Azagâya sent me to investigate the rumours and see if this is true. Should it be so, I am to establish friendly relations with them, and if possible, negotiate terms of establishing trade between our cities.

'Surely you can imagine being the first to... what was that?'

You heard the unmistakable sound of a boot crunching gravel coming from east of the camp.'${PLAYER}! When I spied the camp-fire, I did not expect to find you there, nor did I expect you to be under attack! It seems all is quiet now, though. Your friend looks quite shaken. I hope she is uninjured. Maybe you should see how she fares.

'You are both welcome to join Rhadrog and I at our camp atop the hill. It is better hidden from foes and is also more defensible.'A pack of jázgai have been less afraid of the people at Gadim-ûn of late, and have come close to venturing into the camp.Andûmu is worried that the jázgai that prowl near their camp at night will soon try to rob them of their food stores. He has asked you to go to their den atop a hill south of Gadim-ûn called Spoiler's Crag and renew their fear of people.Defeat jázgai at Spoiler's Crag (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Andûmu is worried that the jázgai that prowl near their camp at night will soon try to rob them of their food stores. You defeated some of them to thin their numbers and renew their fear of people. You should return to Andûmu in Gadim-ûn.Talk to Andûmu'It is not unusual for jázgai to come skulking around our tents at night. The smell of our food must draw them to us. I fear it is only a matter of time before those scavengers become brave or desperate enough to attack someone. Once, they feared people, but they seem to be getting a little too comfortable with us.

'Do you think you would be able to thin their numbers before they try to steal our supplies, or worse yet, hurt somebody? It would do us much good if they feared us once more. Their den is on a hill south of here called Spoiler's Crag.''You have taken care of the jázgai, ${PLAYER}? That is wonderful news. Many people will sleep better at night, myself included. Thank you!'Mizâdi knows your strength and honour and looks to your assistance again as she takes her battle directly to the Ordâkhai.As Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Reach Kindred standing with the Hamât RenewedAs Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Speak to Lumgâna at Mizâdi's encampmentYour renown grows among the Hamât Renewed. Return now to the Quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'Our advance is certain, and your labours steadfast. With each passing hour, we draw nearer to that final stroke which shall see Hamât restored.

'Be watchful for the marks borne by those leading our foes—their insignias are of great worth to Mizâdi, for they stand as tokens of their waning power and our rising strength.

'Hearken now, ${PLAYER}. Survey what has been set aside in your name, and claim the reward you have rightfully won.'While you were scouting an area of Kôth Rau where prisoners are being held, you spotted and picked up a decorative brass button.Nabatínu has suggested asking Khagâmi at Gadim-ûn about the brass button you found at Kôth Rau.Talk to KhagâmiKhagâmi recognizes the brass button as one belonging to Tâklu, who was one of her travelling companions when her caravan was attacked. She begs you to rescue the prisoners, hoping that Tâklu is among them. You should talk to Rhadrog in Gadim-ûn for his opinion.Talk to RhadrogRhadrog agrees that the prisoners at Kôth Rau should be freed, and also hopes to learn why they are keeping prisoners at all. You should meet Rhadrog outside of Kôth Rau in south-central An Shêru, where you will come up with a plan.Meet with Rhadrog outside Kôth RauRhadrog speculates that the Utûgi gaolers have the keys to the cages. You should defeat Utûgi gaolers until you find the keys.Defeat Utûgi gaolers to find the keysRhadrog speculates that the Utûgi gaolers have the keys to the cages. You should defeat Utûgi gaolers until you find the keys.Defeat Utûgi gaolers to find the keysYou have gotten the keys from the Utûgi gaolers that will open the cages. You should free the prisoners from their captivity.Free the prisoners (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have freed many prisoners from their cages at Kôth Rau. You should return to Rhadrog outside of the Ordâkhai stronghold.Return to Rhadrog outside Kôth RauYou have freed many prisoners from their cages at Kôth Rau and returned to Gadim-ûn. You should talk to Khagâmi, who had asked you to rescue them.Talk to Khagâmi in Gadim-ûnYou have freed many prisoners from their cages at Kôth Rau and returned to Gadim-ûn. You should talk to Tâklu, an acquaintance of Khagâmi that you recently rescued.Talk to TâkluYou asked Tâklu what the enemy was doing with the prisoners at Kôth Rau and did not learn much. They were subjected to torture, and the most unfortunate were the people who were sent to Ombátha. You should tell Rhadrog what you learned.Talk to Rhadrog'The prisoners you saw at Kôth Rau are no doubt some of the travellers captured by the Utûgi. I know nothing of this button you brought back, but perhaps Khagâmi might?'You explain the situation to Rhadrog, and he nods.

'We cannot in good conscience leave those people there. I would also like to know why Utûgi are capturing people and bringing them to the Ordâkhai. We must free the captives and see if they know what the Ordâkhai are planning.

'I will go with you. Meet me outside Kôth Rau, and we will make a plan from there.''Indeed, ${PLAYER}! This belonged to Tâklu, one of my companions from Zajâna! Where did you find this?'

You tell her about Kôth Rau and the prisoners you saw there.

'Níkhu! Then he may yet live! And others as well! I thought myself the only survivor. When they attacked us, I fell and... well, to tell you truly, I rolled down a hill, and out of sight of the horrific scene. I am no warrior and remained hidden for quite some time. By the time I gathered my courage to return to the road, there was nothing left of the caravan but blood soaking into the sand.

'Please ${PLAYER}, you must rescue them!''You're here, good. I did a little scouting, and it looks like the Utûgi are the ones watching over the prisoners. I think it's safe to assume that the Utûgi gaolers are the ones who will have the keys to the cages. You'll have to defeat them to find the keys.

'When you free the captives, send them in my direction, and I will get them safely out of the camp. Meet us back here when you are done, and we will bring them with us to Gadim-ûn.''Well done, ${PLAYER}! Let us make haste!''Well done, ${PLAYER}! Let us make haste!''I cannot believe it, Tâklu lives! I can never thank you enough, ${PLAYER}. Truth be told, we only knew one another briefly, but thinking that I alone survived the attack was a heavy burden to carry. I still mourn for the others that did not make it, but Tâklu being here is a miracle!''I owe you my life, and I can never repay you. I thank you with all of my heart. I have a daughter in Zajâna, and all I could think about inside that cage was that I would not get to see her grow up.'

You ask him what the Utûgi and Ordâkhai were doing with captives.

'Torturing us. Asking us questions to which everyone knows the answer. What cities are the largest, who rules where, that sort of thing. I told the truth, there is no reason not to! But Râkdakul, the Elder Orc leader, accused me of misleading them, and the torturers continued their horrific acts.

'The most unfortunate of the prisoners are brought up the pass and inside the mountain. They do not come out. Rumour has it, they only come out in pieces. It is Ombátha enjoying her playthings, we were told. I don't know what they were interested in, other than our suffering. I am sorry that I cannot tell you more.''This is disturbing news. I'm most glad we were able to get those people out of there. This Ombátha sounds just as dreadful as Nabatínu described her. Thank you, ${PLAYER}, for your role in this. It seems that you have saved these people from a fate worse than death.

'The leaders at Kôth Rau will need to be dealt with eventually, but we can only wait to see what tomorrow brings. I suggest waiting until the time is right, and in the meantime, you can maybe help Khagâmi with her quest to find the home of the Temámir in An Shêru.'Mizâdi knows your strength and honour and looks to your assistance again as she takes her battle directly to the Ordâkhai.As Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Reach Respected standing with the Hamât RenewedAs Hamât rises anew, you have proven an invaluable ally. Mizâdi and her agents look to you for further assistance. You should return to Mizâdi's encampment and treat with Lumgâna.Speak to Lumgâna at Mizâdi's encampmentYour renown grows among the Hamât Renewed. Return now to the Quartermaster—they have readied a boon in thanks for your labours.'Our advance is certain, and your labours steadfast. With each passing hour, we draw nearer to that final stroke which shall see Hamât restored.

'Be watchful for the marks borne by those leading our foes—their insignias are of great worth to Mizâdi, for they stand as tokens of their waning power and our rising strength.

'Hearken now, ${PLAYER}. Survey what has been set aside in your name, and claim the reward you have rightfully won.'The Jatsakóri of Gadim-ûn are running low on the basic necessities they need to continue living in An Shêru.Andûmu believes there may be more supply caches hidden many years ago inside the Crypts of Gadim-ûn by Ordâkhai soldiers. He has asked you to enter the crypts and search for them. You should also defeat some of the wights while you are there. The entrance to the Crypts of Gadim-ûn is at the north-west end of the hall at Gadim-ûn.Collect abandoned supplies (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Wights inside the Crypts of Gadim-ûn (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Andûmu believes there may be more supply caches hidden many years ago inside the Crypts of Gadim-ûn by Ordâkhai soldiers. You have gone there at his request and collected supplies, as well as defeating some of the wights. You should return to Andûmu inside the hall at Gadim-ûn.'Hashtal-gûr.'

The man bows to you politely.

'Is it true that you have come to help us? My name is Andûmu and Nabatínu has put me in charge of our supplies. One of the reasons we are here in Gadim-ûn is that there was a long-forgotten Ordâkhai supply cache left in this place, which we have been able to live off of for a time. Unfortunately, we are quickly running out of these supplies.

'I have a suspicion that there are more supply crates hidden away inside the crypts, but I am unfit for that sort of... adventure. I am sure you have noticed that the Dead in An Shêru are not at rest and pose a great danger to us.

'You, however, seem to be able to handle them. If you would go into the crypts and search for more supplies, it would surely increase our chances of survival.''I see you have made it out of the crypts in one piece. And there were more supplies inside, as I suspected! It gladdens me that I did not send you into that horrid place for no reason! I will begin taking inventory of what you have gotten for us immediately.

'Thank you, ${PLAYER}.'Khagâmi, who wanted a travelling companion in An Shêru, would like to set up camp and rest here for the night.Khagâmi is tired from her travels west through An Shêru, and is ready to settle in for the night.Complete Instance: Taking a RestAs you and Khagâmi talked at the camp-fire, you were attacked by a group of bandits. You were able to defend yourself with unexpected help from Faeron, Ranger of the Grey Company. You should talk to Khagâmi about the evening's adventure.Talk to KhagâmiAfter defending your encampment with unexpected help from Faeron, the Ranger invited you to his camp atop the nearby hill.Go to the Rangers' campFaeron invited you to his camp atop the nearby hill, and there you found his companion, Rhadrog. You should talk to him.Talk to Rhadrog'I do not know about you, but I am ready to get off my feet. Let us set up a camp here and get settled for the evening.''Shall we build a camp-fire and make ourselves comfortable? It would be nice to get to know one another over a meal, would it not?''Were it not for you, I would be dead right now, or worse! I could never thank you enough for stopping those men.

'I knew my journey would be perilous, but I did not think I would be imperilled so often! If you wish to visit your friend's camp, I am happy to go with you. Who knows what would befall me if I did not!''${PLAYER}! I did not expect to see you again so soon! What brings you to these parts?'

You introduce Rhadrog and Khagâmi to each other and tell him of her errand.

'I am sorry to say that I know little of these lands and cannot be of more help in your search for these dwarves of which you speak. Faeron and I have only just arrived here ourselves. We wanted to scout the areas to the west to see if the blight has spread to other areas. Thankfully, we have yet to see any such disease in this highland.'Following tracks in the sand has led you to a group of unknown people.Faeron suggested talking to these people to learn more about them. You should talk to the guard at Gadim-ûn, a place in north-eastern An Shêru.Talk to the guardThe guard has directed you to speak with his leader, Nabatínu, inside Gadim-ûn, a place in north-eastern An Shêru.Talk to NabatínuNabatínu, who leads the people inside Gadim-ûn, a place in north-eastern An Shêru, has identified himself as an enemy of Ordâkh, and recommends against going any further west into An Shêru. You should talk to Faeron about what Nabatínu has said.Talk to FaeronFaeron has asked Nabatínu, leader of the people inside Gadim-ûn in north-eastern An Shêru, if he knows of any areas of blight in An Shêru. You should talk to Nabatínu to hear his answer.Talk to NabatínuNabatínu has told you that he and the others here were once thralls of the Ordâkhai and have now found their freedom. They work to free the others still bound in thralldom. You should discuss the situation with Faeron.Talk to FaeronFaeron has offered to assist the Jatsakóri and will meet you back at the Rangers' camp south of Gadim-ûn. You should go to the Ranger camp and talk to Rhadrog.Talk to Rhadrog at his camp'There is but one way to solve the mystery of who these people are, and that is to speak with them. Let us approach with caution and greet them with kindness, and see what comes of it. Go on, I will be right behind you.''And who are you, strangers, who traverse this land without fear? You are no Ordâkhai, and this one is a Man of the West who makes no effort to hide his origin. If you are enemies of Ordâkh, know that you are among friends.

'If your heading is further west, then I must caution against it. Only death and danger lie in that direction.''Who... who are you?'

The man nervously grips his weapon, but does not threaten you with it. You tell him that you are travellers and are curious about them.

'There is no such thing as a traveller who just happens to be passing through here. But I think you should speak to our leader, Nabatínu. You will find him within.''You surmise correctly that we are enemies of Ordâkh. We are capable warriors, however, and would travel west no matter the danger, should our business take us there. It was only a short time ago that we were in the cursed land of Mordor. I have my doubts that An Shêru has more danger to offer than the very lair of the Enemy.

'Tell me more of this danger to the west. I search for signs of an evil blight infecting the land, covering it in rot and sickness.''Alas, no, there is not enough life remaining in An Shêru for such rot to take hold. No, the danger I speak of comes in the form of a new breed of Orc, with size and strength unlike any other I have known. They patrol the roads, killing any they come across, though they often take some prisoner. I shudder to think what becomes of those poor souls.

'There are also two strongholds of Ordâkhai of an unusual sort. They are zealots who do not believe Barkhûsh is truly defeated. They continue to worship him and believe that upon his return, he will reward their loyalty with power untold. They are fools, but are dangerous fools. They are led by a coven of Bokhór, witches with the power to ensnare Men's will.

'In fact...'

The man looks around at his fellows, dirty and hungry, living uncomfortably within a crypt.

'We were all once such thralls. Játsakori, we were called. Our wills were taken from us, and we were forced to work in mines for the Ordâkhai. I was able to break my mind free from their spell at the moment Barkhûsh was defeated, as the casters of the spell suffered a moment of weakness. I was able to escape, and since then, I have been helping as many people as I can break free from thralldom.

'Many have left to seek life and healing elsewhere, but those of us who remain do so intending to free the others. We have found the last of our number increasingly difficult to save. Their minds are bound tightly with the evil magic.'Faeron turns to you, looking deeply concerned.

'I don't like this, ${PLAYER}. These people need our help. Let's return to camp and speak with Rhadrog.'

He turns back to face Nabatínu.

'We must return to two of our company, camped to the south. With your permission, I would like to bring them here, and together we will do what we can to help you. You aren't the only ones who have been forced into labour by evil. King Elessar did not leave the Nurnhoth or the Stout-axes to suffer, and I would not leave yours, if I can help it.'

Nabatínu nods his consent.

'Good. ${PLAYER}, I will meet you back at the camp.''Ah, you return. I was just talking to Faeron. It seems we came here looking for one foulness only to find another. I agree that something must be done. It's time to break camp.'Khagâmi is on an errand on behalf of Doorward Azagâya, and searching for travelling companions.Khagâmi would like to meet you near a road crossing to the west in An Shêru to rest for the night.Go to the cross-roads in An ShêruYou have met Khagâmi at the site near a road crossing in An Shêru she told you about. You should talk to her.Talk to Khagâmi'Forgive me, but would you speak with a woman in need?'

The woman appears to be having a difficult time, with scrapes and bruises peeking out from behind her dusty clothes. She continues speaking in a businesslike manner.

'You appear to be a traveller. Are you travelling westward? I am on an important errand on behalf of Doorward Azagâya. If you are going to An Shêru, might I accompany you? It is a perilous land, and I dare not travel alone. In fact, to tell you the truth, I had already joined a caravan that had not gotten far on the road before it was attacked.'

She looks like she might start to cry, but is able to compose herself after taking a deep breath.

'Despite this event, I must carry on. I will not return to Zajâna in disgrace. Instead, I need more capable travelling companions, and you look quite capable indeed. If you would not mind travelling together, I will meet you at a place near a cross-roads that I think would be a good spot to rest for the night. I can show you on the map.''${PLAYER}, I am relieved to see you! I did not wish to spend the night camping on my own, that is for certain.'Ulishár told you he was captured by the Ordâkhai in this area, and for him every step west is more difficult than the last.Ulishár suggested that the two of you meet up at the cross-road south-west of Gadim-ûn.Meet with Ulishár at the cross-road south-west of Gadim-ûnUlishár stands beside the road, gazing eastward.Talk to Ulishár at the cross-road south-west of Gadim-ûnUlishár spotted a camp of brigands to the east, and he believes you should oppose their plans before you continue westward.Defeat brigands at Zakhûr (${NUMBER}/5)Ulishár spotted a camp of brigands to the east, and he believes you should oppose their plans before you continue westward.Defeat brigands at Zakhûr (4/5)Ulishár has prevented a brigand from escaping Zakhûr: a brigand with a familiar face.Talk to Ulishár outside ZakhûrMashdagâl is outside Zakhûr.Talk to Mashdagâl outside ZakhûrNorth-east of Zakhûr, Mashdagâl has surrendered his sword to you.Pick up Mashdagâl's sword to accept his surrenderUlishár has asked for the sword.Mashdagâl moves suddenly, attempting to escape!Mashdagâl breaks into a run!Ulishár is outside Zakhûr.Talk to Ulishár outside ZakhûrUlishár asked to meet you on the eastern bank of the dry Usâl River in An Shêru.Meet with Ulishár on the eastern bank of the dry Usâl River in An ShêruUlishár stands by the eastern bank of the dry Usâl River in An Shêru.Talk to Ulishár on the eastern bank of the dry Usâl River in An ShêruUlishár is ready to meet you on the western bank of the dry Usâl River in An Shêru. He pointed out a place to the north-west where you might climb out of the channel.Cross to the western bank of the dry Usâl River in An ShêruUlishár stands by the western bank of the dry Usâl River in An Shêru.Talk to Ulishár on the western bank of the dry Usâl River in An Shêru'I have sought to put forth a brave face, ${PLAYER}, but I have something to confess to you.

'When I was captured by the Ordâkhai some years ago and imprisoned in Emax Dûl, it was from An Shêru that they took me. Every step further west has become heavier and more difficult for me.

'I want no pity! I have agreed to come on this journey, and I will not allow this painful memory to prevent me from fulfilling my obligation to you! What would my brother Baharúd say if I turned back now?

'Will you accompany me on the road south of Gadim-ûn? The conversation will draw my attention away from the memory of my capture and aid me as we near the location where I was seized by the Ordâkhai.''Will you accompany me on the road south? The conversation will draw my attention away from the memory of my capture and aid me as we near the location where I was seized by the Ordâkhai.'Ulishár stands here.'A camp of brigands lies to the east. Should we not encourage them to move elsewhere? That will make the road more safe for travellers.

'No, I am not seeking to delay our progress westward. I am concerned about the safety of the road.''I seek not to delay our progress westward, really. I am concerned about the safety of the road! The camp of brigands lies to the east, and as long as it remains there travellers on the road will be endangered.''Look who I found engaged in friendly conversation with the brigands, ${PLAYER}!

'Mashdagâl is a ruffian himself, as Shenzâmin and Kishâtal believed!''Mashdagâl is a ruffian himself, as Shenzâmin and Kishâtal believed!''Have you no mercy? I have given up the life of a brigand!

'Of what use is treasure when ancient creatures of terrible power can drive you to madness with a thought? I cannot stay in this land! If you must kill me, then kill me... but I cannot stay here!''Of what use is treasure when ancient creatures of terrible power can drive you to madness with a thought? I cannot stay in this land! If you must kill me, then kill me... but I cannot stay here!''We made little effort to disguise our talk of the Thardúth in Gadim-ûn. Is it any wonder that Mashdagâl overheard enough of those terrible creatures to fill him with fear?''We would not have harmed him, but I blame Mashdagâl not for his escape. May fear of the Thardúth keep him from returning to banditry!

'I am ready to resume our westward journey, ${PLAYER}. Meet me on the eastern side of the dry Usâl River, west of here and beyond Khanâg Gur.'Ulishár stands here.'This is where the Ordâkhai seized me, ${PLAYER}. I have not returned here since I escaped from Emax Dûl with the help of Têrkar's Travellers.

'I remember standing on the bank of the dry river-bed, imagining that I could hear the rushing of the water that once flowed within. So vivid was the fantasy I could almost see the torrent! But it was not so, and I felt rough hands upon me, breaking me from my reverie; the Usâl remained dry.

'I learned later the soldiers had not sought a Kinta in particular, but the nobility of Ordâkh valued my kind for captives. I was treated no better than any others, but they enjoyed the sight of my labours.'

Ulishár shakes his head, banishing the painful memory.

'Will you cross the river-bed with me, my friend? I find the weight of these last few steps to be almost unbearable. I see a place to climb out of the channel just to the north-west.''Will you cross the river-bed with me, my friend? I find the weight of these last few steps to be almost unbearable.''Those last few steps were the most difficult of all, but with your help I have taken them. I have now crossed west of the Usâl for a good purpose, and not in captivity. Those memories will never be entirely banished, but no longer will they control my steps. I thank you for that, ${PLAYER}.

'I am now unafraid to continue on, even to Emax Dûl itself. I will do this for you, and for the memory of my slain brother Baharúd.'Mêshaget would like to present you to Châmelek Gêta, his grandmother and the leader of Jiret-menêsh.To make her more amenable to your presence, he suggests you bring her a gift, a treasure stolen from the Ordâkhai at Onabóha, their camp to the south of Jiret-menêsh. You should travel to Onabóha.Find OnabóhaYou have found Onabóha, the Ordâkhai camp near Jiret-menêsh. You should search the camp for treasure.Search for a worthy giftYou have spotted a chest filled with treasure inside a tent at Onabóha. You should take the treasure as a gift for Châmelek Gêta.Take the Ordâkhai treasureYou have taken a chest fat with treasure from the Ordâkhai at Onabóha. You should return to Jiret-menêsh and show it to Mêshaget.Talk to Mêshaget at Jiret-menêshYou have shown Mêshaget the treasure taken from the Ordâkhai at Onabóha. You should wait to be invited into Châmelek Gêta's halls to meet with her.Wait to be invited inside the halls of Jiret-menêshMêshaget wants to introduce you to Châmelek Gêta, the leader of Jiret-menêsh. You should talk to Châmelek Gêta and present her with the gift you brought.You have met Châmelek Gêta, Lord of  Jiret-menêsh and Mêshaget's grandmother. You should exit the Gêta's hall.Leave the Gêta's hallYou have met Châmelek Gêta, Lord of Jiret-menêsh and Mêshaget's grandmother. You should talk to Mêshaget about the encounter.Talk to Mêshaget'I would like to introduce you to Châmelek Gêta. She can be a stubborn old mule, though. I can say this only because she is my grandmother!'

He gives you a hearty laugh from his belly, and you get the sense that, despite his words, he loves the "stubborn old mule."

'She is not fond of us being on the surface at all, so I expect her to be disagreeable about having visitors. I suggest that by bringing her a gift worthy of a Gêta, she would look upon you with a more open mind.

'The Ordâkhai outpost of Onabóha is comically close to Jiret-menêsh, considering the men there have yet to notice us! It would be more humorous if they didn't pose a deadly threat to us. Should Kôth Rau send them troops to support an attack on us, we would stand very little chance of survival.

'Regardless, if you went to Onabóha and stole a treasure from the Ordâkhai, it would be doubly impressive! Not only would you give her a treasure, but one from her feared enemy! Not being able to pay their soldiers would also make things difficult for them. It would also show the Gêta that they are not as invulnerable as she seems to believe.''Oh, that is a handsome treasure indeed! Yes, she would find that agreeable. I already sent a messenger inside to request an audience. They should be back at any moment.''Ah, we have our invite. Come, let us not waste any time.''My grandson speaks very highly of you. I wonder if you are truly worthy of his praise.'

You bow and hand her the box of treasure. She opens it and briefly appraises the gold and gems within.

'You have done well. I am told this was taken from the Ordâkhai of Shêru. A difficult deed as well as a noble gesture, though I am sure you received counselling from one who knows me well.'

She hands the box off to her steward as Mêshaget's cheeks grow flush with embarrassment.

'It is of no matter. Thank you for the fine gift. For the sake of all, I hope you did not lead our enemy to our front door. I know not if you are aware of the danger Mêshaget has put himself and some of our kin in. My grandson fought hard to have the doors of Jiret-menêsh reopened. It is an understandable desire for those of less than one-hundred years, but it is foolhardy nonetheless.

'Though Barkhûsh is no more, evil has not died with him. Evil lives ever on in the hearts of Men, and has broken the land beyond repair. Even the Avabár-kinta have not the power to heal this land. But still, the mountain provides all that we need. We do not need to involve ourselves with anything outside of our Halls, and to do so is to invite disaster.

'Mêshaget is naive and does not know what the real world is like. He and others of his generation have been sheltered their whole lives. I understand the yearning for more, but the world that they yearn for does not exist. I must allow them to make their own mistakes, and only hope that the lesson does not cost them their lives.

'After much debate, I relented and allowed those who wished to surface to do so, with some terms. My first priority is to protect these halls and the dwarves within. Therefore, should we be subject to attack, the doors will close immediately. We will not hold them open for anyone, including my grandson. We will send no help, and they will be left to whatever fate befalls them.

'Honestly, I regret my decision to allow this. I see no good that can come of it. But a promise from a Gêta will not be broken.'

She leans back in her throne.

'You may come and go as you will, ${PLAYER}, but know this: should you lead the Ordâkhai to Jiret-menêsh, you would be responsible for the deaths of many innocent dwarves.''That went well! She was in a good mood today!'

Mêshaget laughs at your skepticism.

'Truly, if she did not like you, she would have said very little and would have told you to finish your business here and be on your way.'A pendant given to Sikût by his late mother was stolen from him while he was unconscious.Sikût has asked you to look for his missing pendant while you are in Zakhûr and return it to him. Zakhûr is an encampment east of Khanâg Gur in An Shêru.Go to the bandit camp at ZakhûrYou have arrived at Zakhûr. Sikût has asked you to look for his missing pendant while you are in Zakhûr and return it to him.Find the pendant in the campYou have found the pendant that Sikût asked you to look for in Zakhûr. You should bring it to him in Khanâg Gur.'My greetings, traveller. Did I overhear that you are going to Zakhûr? One of those pashâgs stole from me. I was in the Camel and Chimes the other night when the fight started. I took a hard hit to the head that knocked me out. When I awoke, the pendant I always wear around my neck was missing.'

The man leans forward and speaks to you quietly.

'It sounds like such a small thing, but it was a gift from my mother, and is all I have left of her. May she rest in peace. I need it back, but my head is scrambled. I cannot fight when I am still seeing double. Please, if you go to Zakhûr, find my pendant and return it to me!''Níkhu, you found it! May you always be lucky, my friend! Thank you!'Rahâzi feels the loss of Surdûsha during the Dagor Carlanthir and desires to draw upon the inspiration of her heroine, Shurug-nîn.Rahâzi asked to meet you on the road to Lâkindar, in south-west An Shêru.Meet with Rahâzi on the road to Lâkindar in An ShêruRahâzi watches the road south toward Lâkindar.Talk to Rahâzi on the road to LâkindarThe mines once known as Lâkindar are in south-west An Shêru.Scout what has become of Lâkindar todayThe wights and shades of Lâkindar should be defeated in memory of Shurug-nîn.Defeat wights and shades at Lâkindar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Rahâzi watches the road south toward Lâkindar.Talk to Rahâzi on the road to LâkindarGlorfindel is likely at Khanâg Gur, north of Damudûr.Talk to Glorfindel at Khanâg Gur, north of DamudûrRahâzi is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Rahâzi at Khanâg GurGlorfindel is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Glorfindel at Khanâg GurRahâzi is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Rahâzi at Khanâg Gur'I hoped the Thardúth had all perished at Utug-bûr, even as I knew such evils could not be so easily-defeated. "Easily," say I? I dishonour the memories of Surdûsha and the others who fell at Sabûdamar, the red-rush battle!'

Rahâzi frowns mournfully and stops speaking, lost in her thoughts. Eventually a light appears in her eye and she looks at you closely.

'During the battle you reminded the fighting Dumûri of our great hero Shurug-nîn, do you remember? Many are the songs we sing of her and how she came to be known as Nebunûru, "the Shining Light," but one of these songs has made itself particularly known to me as we travel along the Mûr. Before she became our great hero of legend, many thousands of years ago, Shurug-nîn laboured in the mines of Lâkindar. Those mines would have been located somewhere in the far south-west of what today is called An Shêru.

'Will you humour me with a visit to this place, ${PLAYER}? If we must once again face the Thardúth and their Utûgi warriors, I would draw upon the inspiration of Shurug-nîn to strengthen my sword-arm and bolster my courage.''Before she became our great hero of legend, many thousands of years ago, Shurug-nîn laboured in the mines of Lâkindar. Those mines would have been located somewhere in the far south-west of what today is called An Shêru.

'Will you humour me with a visit to this place? If we must once again face the Thardúth and their Utûgi warriors, I would draw upon the inspiration of Shurug-nîn to strengthen my sword-arm and bolster my courage.'Rahâzi stands here.'That is the way to what had once been the mines of Lâkindar, where Shurug-nîn toiled. She was of no royal line, and her family had not been part of any upper caste in the kingdom of Nêshak, but she possessed exceptional strength and courage. When her brothers perished in the mines, she led a revolt against her oppressors.

'That was not so unusual in those days, but what is surprising is that she succeeded. The freed thralls escaped into the sunlight and Shurug-nîn became a legendary figure. It is said she could not know defeat.

'But that was long ago, and Lâkindar must now be greatly-changed. Let us see what has become of it.''But that was long ago, and Lâkindar must now be greatly-changed. Let us see what has become of it.''After Shurug-nîn freed the thralls at Lâkindar, her name and legend spread throughout all the lands of Shagâna. Around that time she was given a mighty sword of Elf-make, Orotheg, and she wielded it against the armies of the Orcs that threatened to subjugate her people once again.'

Rahâzi pauses thoughtfully.

'I have not given this much thought before now, ${PLAYER}. Of what significance is it that Orotheg was forged by the Elves? From where did the sword come? Is it possible that Glorfindel may know some details of this legend unknown to my own people?

'He intended to go to Khanâg Gur for his investigation into Damudûr. We should meet with him and see what he knows concerning Shurug-nîn and the blade Orotheg.''Glorfindel intended to go to Khanâg Gur for his investigation into Damudûr. We should meet with him and see what he knows concerning Shurug-nîn and the blade Orotheg.''The blade Orotheg? That is a name I have not heard in some time, ${PLAYER}!

'Yes, it is known to me. During the war against Morgoth in the First Age, a great warrior of my own people sought a champion to stem the flow of Orcs from the lands of the south. Her name was Maethriel, and she carried the deadly sword Orotheg, Wrath-thorn. But she despaired, for the folk of the south were kept on the edge of starvation by cruel oppressors and the spark of rebellion was snuffed out wherever it threatened to catch.

'Tidings from Beleriand of one disaster after another reached Maethriel's despairing ears, and she knew she was nearly out of time. If she was to find a hero that could rally the people of Shagâna against Morgoth, she had to find that hero soon.

'Find her she did, for word of Shurug-nîn had begun to spread following the revolt at Lâkindar. Maethriel gifted her the blade Orotheg, and together the two great heroes loosened the hold of the Orcs upon the land and its people.''Word of Shurug-nîn had begun to spread following the revolt at Lâkindar. Maethriel gifted her the blade Orotheg, and together the two great heroes loosened the hold of the Orcs upon the land and its people.''I am inspired to hear of this cooperation between Elf champion and Shagâni warrior, Glorfindel! Maethriel's blade Orotheg would go on to greatness, for with it Shurug-nîn would face the fearsome dragon Akhízi and her terrible children on the shores of the lake of Idagâl. It is said in song that the bones of the three dragons lay at the bottom of the lake for thousands of years.

'The dragon Akhízi pursued Shurug-nîn northward in rage, and both disappeared into legend. But what befell the Elf Maethriel?''The dragon Akhízi pursued Shurug-nîn northward in rage, and both disappeared into legend. But what befell the Elf Maethriel?''The tales of your people are not so different than those of mine, Rahâzi, for if you continue a story long enough it often ends in sadness. Shurug-nîn's fate is not known for certain? Neither is that of Maethriel. Many and great are her deeds during the wars against Morgoth; it is said she stood fearlessly before one of his most terrifying servants, a balrog of legend... but what happened to her I cannot say. She vanishes from tale and song sometime before Akhízi pursued her chosen champion northward.

'But let this not dampen your enthusiasm for cooperation between Elf and Shagâni! Great are the deeds and many are the accomplishments they achieved together before they faded into legend.''But let this not dampen your enthusiasm for cooperation between Elf and Shagâni! Great are the deeds and many are the accomplishments they achieved together before they faded into legend.''Some of the most inspiring songs end in sadness, ${PLAYER}. Glorfindel need not apologize for it.

'For the sake of Shurug-nîn and Maethriel I will fight on, and not be intimidated by the strength of our foes. The Thardúth and their Utûgi warriors are not unassailable, and they knew defeat at Sabûdamar. They will know it again, for I am the daughter of Girhâzi, a proud warrior of the Dumûri, and I follow the example of great Shurug-nîn, who knew no fear!'You have come to the legendary dwarf-city of Jiret-menêsh, where some of the Temámir have come to the surface after one hundred years of sheltering in the mountain.Mêshaget needs to speak to the Lord of the city, Châmelek Gêta, and suggested you talk to Mísikir, the captain of the guard.Talk to MísikirMísikir has given you a water-skin and asked you to find Cherêka, who watches over the road that all must take to approach the mountain-top. She should be somewhere nearby, looking down upon the road.Find the guard watching over the roadYou have found Cherêka, keeping watch over the road that leads to Jiret-menêsh from the city above. You should talk to her and give her the water Mísikir asked you to deliver.You have delivered the water to Cherêka, and had a nice chat. You should return to Mísikir, who was near Jiret-menêsh's stable-master.Talk to MísikirMísikir tells you that a woman was recently looking for you, but does not know where she went. You should find Khagâmi in Jiret-menêsh.Talk to KhagâmiKhagâmi thanked you for getting her safely to Jiret-menêsh. Mêshaget appears to have something to say. You should talk to Mêshaget.Talk to Mêshaget'${PLAYER}, I must report to Châmelek Gêta. She is the Gêta... the Lord of Jiret-menêsh. Please - feel free to explore our community here on the surface. Should you feel restless for something to do, Mísikir is the captain of our guard and would surely have a task to offer you.''Who is this, then, a friend of Mêshaget? A ${RACE} offering help, no less! Interesting indeed!

'I am Mísikir and see after the safety of all at Jiret-menêsh. I assume you are quite trustworthy, else Mêshaget would not have sent you to me. Aye, there is one simple task for which I could use someone's help. I apologize that it is not very exciting, but still, it must be done!

'An Shêru is as dry as Châmelek Gêta's sense of humour, and standing guard is thirsty work. Please, take this water-skin to Cherêka, who is keeping watch over the road that all must take to approach the mountain. She will be somewhere nearby, looking down upon the road.''By my mother's beard! You have good timing, my friend. My own water-skin is empty, and I was just thinking of my thirst.

'I must tell you that I have never spoken to a ${RACE} before. You are taller than I expected! Only recently has the Gêta allowed us to come to the surface, and I am a mere forty-seven years of age, so I have only recently seen the open sky for the first time. Its vastness is intimidating, and I have no love for the wind!

'This is a good experience, though. One must be comfortable with being uncomfortable if they wish to travel, or so I am told, and I would like to see the Shoreward Stone one day!

'My thanks for the water, friend, and send the captain my thanks as well.''I thank you for your help! A woman came by earlier, asking after your whereabouts. She said she wanted to talk to you, but I am unsure where she went.''Khagâmi's words speak true, my friend. Your willingness to help people tells much about your noble nature. You are exceptional, indeed!''${PLAYER}, there you are! I wanted to thank you for all you have done. Getting me here safely was no small thing, and rescuing Tâklu was an extraordinary act. I dare say I have never met another like you, so willing to help others. I will make sure Doorward Azagâya knows of your heroism.'

Khagâmi gives you a respectful bow.Glorfindel believes that terrible evils slept within An Shêru, but they must now be awake. You must determine the dangers lurking within the Height of the Sky and aid your companions with their duties.The Hamâti soldier Kishâtal in Gadim-ûn has something he wishes to say to you.Talk to the Hamâti soldier Kishâtal in Gadim-ûnYour companions within Gadim-ûn have a number of investigations with which they need your help.Work with Shenzâmin and complete 'Emissaries to Jiret-menêsh'Work with Glorfindel and complete 'The Madness at Damudûr'Work with Rahâzi and complete 'A Song of Shurug-nîn'Work with Ulishár and complete 'A Difficult Step'Having aided your companions with their investigations, you should now meet them where the road leads toward Idagâl.Meet with your companions on the road toward IdagâlGlorfindel is on the road toward Idagâl.Talk to Glorfindel on the road toward IdagâlRahâzi is on the road toward Idagâl.Talk to Rahâzi on the road toward Idagâl'Mashdagâl fled this place while you were away, but unless he leaves An Shêru entirely I fear he will not escape the reach of the Thardúth. Did the tremors caused during the Din of Stone unseal the path to a second vault of Morgoth's servants? It seems likely to me, ${PLAYER}, and I believe one of the slain Ordâkhai has given us a name to this place: Damudûr.

'We have some work to do. Once we have learned what we can in An Shêru we will travel westward, where the road leads to Idagâl.''Did the tremors caused during the Din of Stone unseal the path to a second vault of Morgoth's servants? It seems likely to me, ${PLAYER}, and I believe one of the slain Ordâkhai has given us a name to this place: Damudûr.

'We have some work to do. Once we have learned what we can in An Shêru we will travel westward, where the road leads to Idagâl.''Shenzâmin and I cannot let these terrors distract us from the mission given to us by Shâra Mizâdi. We will help you investigate An Shêru, but we must also carry out our own duties.''If we all work together we can both carry out our duties and search An Shêru for the evils that threaten its people.'Glorfindel stands here.'Ever since we departed Zajâna, I suspected our road would bring us to Emax Dûl in Idagâl. I did not expect the situation with which we are now threatened, that the Ordâkhai who control that city might be manipulated by the Thardúth.''The Ordâkhai who control Emax Dûl are being manipulated by the Thardúth. I am certain of it.''Shenzâmin and Kishâtal have set out for Kighân to bring word back to Shâra Mizâdi. I will remain with you, ${PLAYER}, for I fought at the Sabûdamar, the red-rush battle, and I know the danger posed by the Thardúth.

'For fallen Surdûsha and the honour of the Dumûri, I will fight alongside you against them!'Mizâdi entrusted her soldiers Shenzâmin and Kishâtal to meet with the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh, and they will not let her down.Shenzâmin and Kishâtal agreed to meet you at the cross-roads south of Jiret-menêsh.Meet up with Shenzâmin and Kishâtal at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêshShenzâmin is at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêsh.Talk to Shenzâmin at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêshDejemûl is at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêsh.Talk to Dejemûl at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêshKishâtal at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêsh has listened to the merchant's words and wants to speak to you.Talk to Kishâtal at the cross-road south of Jiret-menêshShenzâmin suggested that you first visit Khanâg Valoyar before you go to Jiret-menêsh.Visit Khanâg Valoyar before you go to Jiret-menêshThe Temámir dwarf Habâti at Khanâg Valoyar has accosted you with shouted insults.Talk to Habâti at Khanâg ValoyarShenzâmin is at Khanâg Valoyar, bristling with anger at your treatment by the Temámir Habâti.Talk to Shenzâmin at Khanâg ValoyarThe entrance to Jiret-menêsh is along the road north of Khanâg Valoyar.Find the entrance to Jiret-menêsh far north of Khanâg ValoyarChâmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêsh, is at her throne within the fortress.Find Châmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêsh, inside the fortressChâmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêsh, is inside the fortress.Talk to Châmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêshShenzâmin is in the fortress of Jiret-menêsh, near Châmelek Gêta.Talk to Shenzâmin in the fortress of Jiret-menêshChâmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêsh, asked you to help her people deal with the creatures at either the gredbyg camp to the west or the worm camp to the east.Defeat gredbyg at the Slopes of Shâlahin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect egg clusters at the Slopes of Shâlahin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat worms at the Seething-wells (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect misplaced axes at the Seething-wells (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The ruler of Jiret-menêsh is inside the fortress.Talk to Châmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêshShenzâmin is in the fortress of Jiret-menêsh.Talk to Shenzâmin in the fortress of Jiret-menêsh'Shâra Mizâdi has brought great strength of arms into Mûr Ghala to oppose the Empire of Ordâkh, but when the fighting is done she will need to ensure a good relationship with the powers who remain.

'She has asked Kishâtal and I to treat with the Temámir who dwell in this land, that we may open lines of friendly communication between the dwarves and Renewed Hamât. Judging from the maps I have seen, there is a cross-road south of their settlement in An Shêru. Let us travel there and plan for our encounter with the Temámir.''Shâra Mizâdi has asked Kishâtal and I to treat with the Temámir who dwell in this land, that we may open lines of friendly communication between the dwarves and Renewed Hamât. Judging from the maps I have seen, there is a cross-road south of their settlement in An Shêru. Let us travel there and plan for our encounter with the Temámir.'Shenzâmin stands here.'Jiret-menêsh, the settlement of the Temámir in An Shêru, is to the north. But before we make our way there we should speak with this man Dejemûl. He is a merchant, and he says the Temámir may not welcome us, despite our authority to speak on behalf of Shâra Mizâdi!''Before we make our way to Jiret-menêsh we should speak with this man Dejemûl. He is a merchant, and he says the Temámir may not welcome us, despite our authority to speak on behalf of Shâra Mizâdi!''I told your companions what I will tell you, stranger: the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh may not receive you with open arms; their relationship with Men is strained. They blame some long ago war with the kingdom of Nísaka for that, claiming the Men of that land tried to seize their mines. For all I know that could be true, but that was centuries ago!

'What I do know is that I stopped first at the khanâg just over there, and one of the Temámir insulted me and waved me along. If the others of his people behave as does he, you might as well save yourselves the walking. They will just turn you away, whether you were sent to them by a Shâr, a Hûl, or by any king or queen of Men!''I stopped first at the khanâg just over there, and the Temámir there insulted me and waved me along. If the others of his people behave as does he, you might as well save yourselves the walking. They will just turn you away, whether you were sent to them by a Shâr, a Hûl, or by any king or queen of Men!''Dejemûl may be right, ${PLAYER}, but I hope to prove him wrong. Let us speak with the Temámir he mentioned at the khanâg nearby. The name of the Shâra may open doors for us that remained closed to Dejemûl.''Dejemûl may be right, but I hope to prove him wrong. Let us speak with the Temámir he mentioned at the khanâg nearby. The name of the Shâra may open doors for us that remained closed to Dejemûl.'Habâti glowers at you.'Move along! There is nothing for you here!'

Habâti spits onto the ground and places a hand on the hilt of his sword.

'The sooner you return to the road and depart, the better it will be for all of you! Go now, and return not!''Get out of here! There is nothing for you here! Move along!''This insult cannot be allowed to stand! How dare this dwarf speak so to the emissaries of Renewed Hamât?'

Shenzâmin shakes with fury, but before drawing his blade he draws a long, calming breath instead, visibly settling his rage.

'The insulting manner of this Temámir has no bearing upon our mission, ${PLAYER}. He is not the leader of Jiret-menêsh, and he has no authority to treat with us. We speak with the voice of Shâra Mizâdi; he rules only this khanâg, and perhaps not even that!

'We continue northward to Jiret-menêsh. It would seem Dejemûl was correct about the relationship between dwarves and Men in An Shêru.''We continue northward to Jiret-menêsh. It would seem Dejemûl was correct about the relationship between dwarves and Men in An Shêru.'Châmelek Gêta, the ruler of Jiret-menêsh, listens as you introduce yourself and the Hamâti soldiers Kishâtal and Shenzâmin. You go on to relay your treatment at Khanâg Valoyar, and the dwarf-leader frowns.

'This dwarf Habâti is not immediately known to me, but I have been most busy with affairs within Jiret-menêsh itself. The operation of distant outposts on the surface I leave to others.

'I will hear the words of Renewed Hamât, but it is customary for such dignitaries to lend some assistance when they arrive, as a showing of good faith. Two such problems currently trouble my people: an infestation of gredbyg and a host of worms. Does Hamât intend to help us with these matters?''I will hear the words of Renewed Hamât, but it is customary for such dignitaries to lend some assistance when they arrive, as a showing of good faith. Two such problems currently trouble my people: an infestation of gredbyg and a host of worms. Does Hamât intend to help us with these matters?''If this is the custom, we should see it done. I leave the choice to you, ${PLAYER}. The Temámir wish for us to help them deal with either the gredbyg or the worms? We will help them with both!

'Choose the threat with which you will deal, and Kishâtal and I will handle the other. Each of these foes can be found outside Jiret-menêsh, to the east and to the west.''Choose the threat with which you will deal, and Kishâtal and I will handle the other. Each of these foes can be found outside Jiret-menêsh, to the east and to the west.'Châmelek Gêta smiles magnanimously at you.

'Very good, very good! I have been informed of your work on behalf of the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh, and will now listen to the words of the emissaries of Shâra Mizâdi of Renewed Hamât.''I will now listen to the words of the emissaries of Shâra Mizâdi of Renewed Hamât.''Thank you for your help getting this audience. We can now take it from here, and will convey Shâra Mizâdi's friendship and good wishes to the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh.

'I am relieved to learn the impolite manner of the dwarf Habâti at Khanâg Valoyar was an exception and not the rule, ${PLAYER}!

'Travel safely, and I hope to see you again when our missions are complete, my friend!'Khanâg Gur appears to be controlled by a group called the Khanda-khêl.You have been instructed by the guard at the entrance to Khanâg Gur to introduce yourself to someone called the 'Khanda-mashár'. You should speak to Halshûm.Talk to Halshûm'I have never seen you here before. You must speak with Khanda-mashár.'

<rgb=#00FF00>Note: Accepting this quest will teleport you to Khanda-mashár in Khanâg Gur.</rgb>'Saráz záro-dan.'

The confident man gives you a charming smile before continuing in a friendly manner.

'Tell me who you are and what brings you to Khanâg Gur. I am Halshûm, and I am Khanda-mashár. That means I rule here, and the Khanda-khêl answer to me. Tell me why you come here.'

You introduce yourself and explain your purpose.

'You are welcome here and may drink our water, ${PLAYER}, as long as you do not trouble me. Please, feel free to trade or take advantage of the services of the Khanâg.

'It is possible to earn my favour by doing something for me, if you are so inclined. Tell me if you would hear more.'Sâdikiz the Sky-hungry can be found at Khanâg Gur, and is said to be often found at the tavern there. You should go into the tavern to find him.Is there a fight in the tavern? You should get closer to see what is going on.What is going on?You found two ruffians beating Sâdikiz as he lay on the floor. You should stop them and defeat the ruffians.Defeat the ruffiansYou have defeated the ruffians who were beating Sâdikiz as he lay helpless on the floor. You should talk to the relieved dwarf.Talk to Sâdikiz'To whom do I owe my thanks, stranger? You came in just in time!'

You introduce yourself and tell him you are friends with Mêshaget, who wanted you two to meet.

'Did he, now? In that case, I shall send him my thanks for my timely rescue also! Come, let us talk outside.'Gôbezhir Firmhand dreams at night of falling stars and believes he is destined to forge an axe crafted from birêt-kibur, 'the noble iron'.Gôbezhir has asked you to keep an eye out while travelling through An Shêru for birêt-kibur, iron fallen from the sky. Birêt-kibur could be found anywhere in An Shêru.Collect birêt-kibur (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected plenty of birêt-kibur while travelling through An Shêru. You should return to Jiret-menêsh so you may give it to Gôbezhir.Return to Jiret-menêshYou have collected plenty of birêt-kibur while travelling through An Shêru. You should speak to Gôbezhir and give him the ore for which he asked.'Well met, ${PLAYER}! Your arrival at Jiret-menêsh is grist for the mill, as they say! You are quite the lively topic of conversation. I have not spoken to a visitor in one hundred years!'

The jovial dwarf chuckles and bows to you.

'But how rude of me, I should introduce myself! I am Gôbezhir Firmhand, master smith of Jiret-menêsh. I have heard you are well-travelled! I envy the sights you have likely seen, though I am no warrior and am happy to live a quiet life.

'I wonder, though, if you would be willing to keep an eye out for something for me. Since returning to the surface, I have had unusual dreams. I dream of a starlit night with falling stars trailing fire, streaking across the sky. They look so close, you could almost touch them. It is not unheard of for such things to be seen from the Height of the Sky, but I cannot help but feel like these dreams bear a message.

'When these stars land, they leave iron ore unlike any from a mine. It is stronger, resistant to rust, and must be worked at a higher temperature. We call it birêt-kibur, 'the noble iron'. I believe in my heart that I am to use birêt-kibur to craft an axe. Not just any axe, but my life's masterwork. I must do this thing, but I am only suited for the forge, not for adventures in the wilds.

'If you happen to come across birêt-kibur during your travels in An Shêru, would you collect it and bring it to me?''By my beard, ${PLAYER}, you did it! Look at all that ore!'

Gôbezhir excitedly takes a jeweller's loupe from his pocket and examines one of the pieces you brought him.

'Aye, this is birêt-kibur, to be sure. I am near to weeping! But no, smelting first, weeping later. ${PLAYER}, you have my undying gratitude.'Mêshaget has invited you to visit his home, Jiret-menêsh.Jiret-menêsh is located in north-western An Shêru, atop a mountain peak.Travel to Jiret-menêshAs you approached the entrance to Jiret-menêsh, a guard asked you to stop. You should talk to her.Talk to the guardAs you began to speak with the guard, Mêshaget and Khagâmi arrived behind you. You should talk to Mêshaget.Talk to Mêshaget'${PLAYER}, when you are ready to leave this place, you and Khagâmi are invited to visit Jiret-menêsh! It is unlikely that you will be allowed inside the mountain, but many of us have been enjoying the fresh air outdoors, and there is still much to see and do above ground. It is to the west, atop a mountain Men of the past once called "The Sheenwall".

'If you do not mind, I would like to walk with Khagâmi to discuss trade and politics. You may travel on ahead, and we will follow behind you.''Stop there, who are you? You are no merchant. What is your business?'

Before you can answer, the guard speaks to someone behind you.

'Mêshaget! Back so soon? We expected you to be gone for quite a while!''A slight change of plans! These are my new friends, Khagâmi and ${PLAYER}. Khagâmi is from Zajâna and represents one of their top officials, making my trip unnecessary. On my honour, they are not Ordâkhai spies.'

The guard chuckles and waves you through.A woman in Khanda-mashár's service was recently abused and killed by men who came to Khanâg Gur from Zakhûr.Halshûm has asked you to defeat bandits at Zakhûr as an act of revenge for what they have done to a woman in his service. Zakhûr is an encampment east of Khanâg Gur in An Shêru.Defeat bandits at Zakhûr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many bandits at Zakhûr. You should return to Halshûm in Khanâg Gur and tell him of your success.Talk to Halshûm in Khanâg Gur'As you have seen, I am very welcoming to most anyone who would spend their coin at Khanâg Gur. Several nights ago, there was a bit of a scrap in the Camel and Chimes, our tavern. I have no problem with the men settling arguments with their fists, but on this particular night, a woman in my service was brutalized and killed by men from Zakhûr.'

The expression on Khanda-mashár's face grows dark.

'We may disregard certain laws, but we are civilized men, not beasts. This sort of thing will not be tolerated, and this act will not go unpunished. Should you happen to find yourself in Zakhûr, I ask that you defeat some of those animals in combat. Do so in my name, and you will be rewarded with my favour as well as my coin.''It is done then? The animals of Zakhûr have been defeated? I am impressed, ${PLAYER}, as well as grateful. Here is your reward, as promised.'The bodies of the tortured Dead walk the Quelling-house, haunting the place where they were tormented and cursed by the priests of Barkhûsh.You should defeat wights at Lâkindar, allowing the bodies of these victims of Barkhûshim's cruelties to find peace. Lâkindar is in the far south-western corner of An Shêru.Defeat wights at Lâkindar (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many wights at Lâkindar, allowing the bodies of these victims of Barkhûshim's cruelties to find peace.CompletedThe Temámir of the once-glorious dwarf-mine of Lâkindar walk the same halls in death as they did in life. Now referred to as the Quelling-house, its unfortunate residents, who were sacrificed to Barkhûsh deserve to be at rest.Freed from the demons of their unfortunate fates at the hands of Sauron's cruel priests, some of the wights of the Quelling-house can finally rest in peace, thanks to your efforts.Glorfindel suspects that Damudûr may have hidden the entrance to a lost vault of the Thardúth.Glorfindel suggested you meet at Khanâg Gur north of Damudûr.Meet up with Glorfindel at Khanâg GurGlorfindel is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Glorfindel at Khanâg GurGlorfindel asked you to approach Damudûr and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for some sign of the Thardúth.Ulishár is at Khanâg Gur, waiting to speak with you.Talk to Ulishár at Khanâg GurArshûk is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Arshûk at Khanâg GurGlorfindel is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Glorfindel at Khanâg GurGlorfindel believes that if you retrieve a fancy Ordâkhai blade from Kôth Rau, you will convince Arshûk to tell you what he saw.Retrieve a fancy Ordâkhai blade from the camp of Kôth RauArshûk is at Khanâg Gur.Arshûk gathers his courage.Wait for Arshûk to gather his courageArshûk is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Arshûk at Khanâg GurUlishár is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Ulishár at Khanâg GurGlorfindel is at Khanâg Gur.Talk to Glorfindel at Khanâg Gur'Something has driven to madness the sorcerers of Ordâkh you encountered here in An Shêru, ${PLAYER}. What can be the cause but the Thardúth, newly-awakened and testing their power? During the Dagor Carlanthir, even Inkanûsh struggled to repel the invasive voices of the servants of Morgoth dwelling within Utug-bûr. What ordinary Man of Ordâkh might hope to strive even half so well?

'One of the slain sorcerers has given us a name to investigate: Damudûr. We must learn if this location hides the entrance to a vault of the Thardúth like we discovered at Utug-bûr.

'I have heard mention of a Khanâg to the south-west where travellers gather. We should go to this place and see what we can learn of Damudûr.''I have heard mention of a Khanâg to the south-west where travellers gather. We should go to this place and see what we can learn of Damudûr.''We were right to come here. Travellers speak of a cleft in the rocks to the south named Damudûr. I have learned little but the name, for stories of that place are muddled and confused. Most folk fear to go that way, but they are unable to explain why they fear it so. Some say the cleft is held by Ordâkh, but others claim to experience terror of a deeper nature.

'You should make your way south, further along the road, and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for yourself. Ulishár and I will continue to speak to the people here in search of an answer to this central question: do the people fear the presence of Ordâkh at Damudûr, or a deeper evil?''Some folk say the cleft is held by Ordâkh, but others claim to experience terror of a deeper nature. You should make your way south and <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for yourself, my friend.''The Ordâkhai encamp outside Damudûr. They must be searching for something, but what that may be remains unknown to us.

'I found someone who might know something of their purpose, but he is too terrified to speak any further on the subject. He is named Arshûk. Can you convince him to tell us what he saw at Damudûr?''I found someone who might know something of their purpose, but he is too terrified to speak. He is named Arshûk. Can you convince him to tell us what he saw at Damudûr?''I want to help you, but... but...'

Arshûk stammers with fright. Only when you place a reassuring hand on his arm does he calm sufficiently to make himself understood.

'If I speak, will you protect me from the terrible creatures that stand behind the Ordâkhai? Can you prove to me you have the courage to oppose them?''If I speak, will you protect me from the terrible creatures that stand behind the Ordâkhai? Can you prove to me you have the courage to oppose them?''Arshûk began to tell Ulishár about something he saw at Damudûr, but then he was overcome with terror and would say nothing more. Whatever he saw, we must prove to him we have the courage to protect him or he will say nothing.

'One common element has emerged from the talk of the people with whom I have spoken, ${PLAYER}. Many of these folk have been impressed by the bright shine of the weapons wielded by the Ordâkhai, and by the gems and jewels that adorn their clothing.

'Perhaps if you retrieve a fancy blade of Ordâkh from the camp of Kôth Rau, Arshûk will see you have the courage to oppose this kingdom, and protect him from its soldiers. Then it may be his tongue will loosen and he will confirm what I have come to suspect: that the Thardúth walk in An Shêru.''Perhaps if you retrieve a fancy blade of Ordâkh from Kôth Rau, near to the entrance of the cleft at Damudûr, Arshûk will see you have the courage to protect him. Then it may be his tongue will loosen and he will confirm what I have come to suspect: that the Thardúth walk in An Shêru.''What... what is it? What have you done?'

You show Arshûk the fancy blade of Ordâkh and he looks at you in amazement.

'You dared trespass near enough to Damudûr to retrieve this blade? That displays true courage, stranger!''It was a few nights ago. Under cover of the darkness, I crept near to the camp of the Ordâkhai soldiers. I thought I might be able to steal some of the unattended swords or other treasures. The Ordâkhai plunder the Ridge of Plenty for its wealth, and have always done so... how have they that right but I do not?

'I was about to slip into the camp when something made me pause. Believe me when I tell you I know this for certain: had I not stayed my feet, I would not be alive today. For as I hesitated, a group of figures emerged from the cleft of Damudûr and passed by the light of a fire. From their attire, I knew four of them to be nobles of Ordâkh, but their manner was striking. Had they not been so richly-dressed I might have thought them servants. They cringed and capered before the two most severe creatures I have ever seen.

'They were tall and gaunt, in appearance like the Kinta Elves you sometimes see throughout the Mûr. But where those folk possess a distant, preoccupied air, from these creatures I felt a sensation of intense hatred. A man and a woman they seemed in appearance, but the feeling of hatred radiating from them struck me as being quite otherworldly. They were flanked on two sides by great Orcs larger than any I have seen before; my blood ran cold and my breath stopped as they drew near to my hiding-place.

'The clowning Ordâkhai nobility guided their companions past the fires and into the darkness of the enveloping night, and as they faded from my view, but not my memory, never my memory, I heard them speak the name "Emax Dûl." That is where the terrible creatures must be now, either the guests or the masters of Ordâkh.''As they faded from my view, but not my memory, never my memory, I heard them speak the name "Emax Dûl." That is where the terrible creatures must be now, either the guests or the masters of Ordâkh.''It is clear to me from Arshûk's account that the large Orcs he saw from afar were Utûgi. They must serve the Thardúth of Damudûr even as those that slew my brother served the Thardúth of Utug-bûr.

'In Baharúd's memory will I continue to stand beside you, ${PLAYER}. The Utûgi may not fear a Kinta Elf such as Ulishár... but it may be they should.''In Baharúd's memory will I continue to stand beside you, ${PLAYER}. The Utûgi may not fear a Kinta Elf such as Ulishár... but it may be they should.''I believe what Arshûk has told to us, but we know more about the Thardúth than he does, through no fault of his own. His fear of them is neither misplaced nor unfounded.

'Nobles of Ordâkh are not accustomed to subservience. For them to behave so in the presence of the Thardúth indicates to me they have some knowledge of their powers or abilities. The Ordâkhai are right to fear them; perhaps they believe such demonstrations will keep the creatures they discovered at Damudûr under control. On this they must be wrong. The Thardúth intend to manipulate the Ordâkhai and seize control of their empire.

'Each of these remain our foes. We will continue to oppose them, even as they prey upon each other. But Arshûk's tale has told me something else of use, ${PLAYER}. The madness that inflicted the others who strayed near Damudûr did not affect Arshûk. The Thardúth must be aware of an individual in order to invade his mind. This may be useful to us.'Mêshaget said he received encouragement to fight for the opening of the doors of Jiret-menêsh through correspondence with Sâdikiz the Sky-hungry, a Temámir who was banished by Châmelek Gêta many years ago. Mêshaget thinks you should meet Sâdikiz, whom he considers a dear friend.Sâdikiz the Sky-hungry can be found at Khanâg Gur. You should go there to find him.Travel to Khanâg GurSâdikiz the Sky-hungry can be found at Khanâg Gur. Mêshaget said to look for Sâdikiz's boar.Complete Instance: The OutcastYou saved Sâdikiz from being beaten by two bullies who claimed he owed them money. You should talk to Sâdikiz in Khanâg Gur, outside of the tavern.Talk to Sâdikiz the Sky-hungry'It is true that I am largely responsible for the opening of the doors, such as it is. I did not do it entirely on my own, though. Many in Jiret-menêsh felt as I did, and I also found the courage to stand up to Châmelek Gêta from trading letters with a friend.

'His name is Sâdikiz, and he is a Temámir who was banished from Jiret-menêsh many years ago. He also pushed for opening the doors and was so wilful that he angered Châmelek Gêta. She dubbed him "the Sky-hungry" and cast him out from the city, telling him that if he craved the sky so much, he could go die under it.

'I expect she thought he did just that, but the world is not as perilous as she believes. He has had many adventures and has returned to An Shêru. He can often be found in Khanâg Gur.

'You should meet him! I admire him in many ways. He may yet prove to be a valuable ally to you, as he has been for me. Look for his boar. If you see it, he cannot be far.'A boar is hitched outside of the tavern. Sâdikiz is probably inside.'As you may have overheard, I currently owe a debt to some of the locals. It seems that they are not particularly patient and sought to speed my repayment. You have spared me from many bruises, or perhaps worse!'Many wights have risen from the grave in An Shêru, but who were they?Andûmu has asked you to examine the grave goods buried with the people in north-eastern An Shêru to learn something of who they were and what their lives were like. You should start at Naxar Dâl, north of Gadim-ûn, and defeat some wights there to clear some space for yourself.Defeat wights at Naxar Dâl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Andûmu has asked you to examine the grave goods buried with the people in north-eastern An Shêru to learn something of who they were and what their lives were like. You started at Naxar Dâl, north of Gadim-ûn, and defeated some of the wights, clearing some space for yourself.Andûmu has asked you to examine the grave goods buried with the people in north-eastern An Shêru to learn something of who they were and what their lives were like. You started at Naxar Dâl, north of Gadim-ûn, and should now go to the Crypts of Khâb Daku, to the south. Defeat some of the wights there to clear some space for yourself.Defeat wights at Khâb Daku (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Andûmu has asked you to examine the grave goods buried with the people in north-eastern An Shêru to learn something of who they were and what their lives were like. You started at Naxar Dâl, north of Gadim-ûn, and next went to the Crypts of Khâb Daku, to the south, where you defeated some of the wights there, clearing some space for yourself.Andûmu has asked you to examine the grave goods buried with the people in north-eastern An Shêru to learn something of who they were and what their lives were like. You started at Naxar Dâl, north of Gadim-ûn, and next went to the Crypts of Khâb Daku, examining grave goods at each location. You should return to Andûmu in Gadim-ûn and tell him what you discovered.Talk to Andûmu in Gadim-ûn'I cannot help but be curious about the people buried here. They are unlike any Shagâni I know. How long ago did they live? Why are there so many, and why are they in this place?

'It is common in many cultures to include personal items when they lay their dead to rest. It is bad luck to disturb the dead in their graves, but these poor souls are already disturbed. If you would be willing to humour me, would you mind taking a look at some of the graves and examining their belongings? There are many graves to be found in Naxar Dâl to the north, and Khâb Daku to the south. Perhaps the items they were buried with could tell us something of their story.''Tell me, friend. What did you learn?'

You tell him that many of these people appear to have been wealthy and learned, and traded with the Temámir as well as Gondor. They were not without soldiers but were probably not particularly warlike, and are likely the ancestors of the Shagâni living here today.

'Do you think they lived in Zajâna of old? Much trade happens there, I believe. They say it was founded by the Kingdom of Nísaka, which fell even before my grandfather was born. The Ordâkhai destroyed Nísaka on their march northward. Zajâna is all that remains of that kingdom.

'Well, Zajâna, and perhaps this boneyard! I fear these wights, as any sane man should, but it is also fascinating to me that these bodies belonged to people of a different time. It sounds like many of them had good lives.

'Thank you for taking the time for this.'The wights in the Crypts of Gadim-ûn continue to encroach on the land of the living.Gíshgi is still distressed by the presence of the Dead in An Shêru and has asked you to re-enter the crypt below to see if you can find any explanation as to why they aren't at rest in their graves. The entrance to the Crypts of Gadim-ûn is at the north-western end of the hall.Enter the Crypts of Gadim-ûnGíshgi is still distressed by the presence of the Dead in An Shêru and has asked you to re-enter the crypt below to see if you can find any explanation as to why they aren't at rest in their graves. You should head east and search the crypts for anything notable.Search for a clue to the eastYou have found a corrupted corpse surrounded by a green radiance and the sound of a tormented voice. Perhaps you should sing a lighthearted song to yourself to ward off the fell whispers.Soothe the anguished deadAfter finding a corrupted corpse, you were able to dispel the foul voice in the air with a lighthearted song. You should continue east and search the crypts for anything else notable.Search for another clue further eastYou have found another corrupted corpse. You should sing a lighthearted song to yourself to ward off the fell whispers, as you did last time.Soothe the anguished deadAfter finding two corrupted corpses, you were able to dispel the foul voice in the air with a lighthearted song. You should travel south and continue searching the crypts.Search for another clue to the southYou have discovered a third corrupted corpse. You should again sing a lighthearted song to yourself to ward off the fell whispers.Soothe the anguished deadYou have now found three corrupted corpses, each surrounded by a green radiance and the sound of a tormented voice. Each time, you were able to dispel the foul voice with a lighthearted song. You should travel west and continue searching the crypts.Search for another clue to the westYou have yet another corrupted corpse. You should again sing a lighthearted song to yourself to ward off the fell whispers.Soothe the anguished deadYou have found a total of four corrupted corpses, each surrounded by a green radiance and the sound of a tormented voice. Each time, you were able to dispel the foul voice with a lighthearted song. You should return to Gíshgi in Gadim-ûn and tell her what you found.Talk to Gíshgi'${PLAYER}, I loathe these wights so. Why are they tramping about instead of lying in their graves where they belong?

'You are courageous and do not fear them as I do. Do you think if you entered the crypts below, you would find some clue as to why they are not at peace?'You tell Gíshgi of the disturbing whispers and wailing you came across in the crypts, and how you were able to dispel them by countering them with a happier song.

The woman gasps.

'${PLAYER}, this must be what they call Ishími! The Anguished Wind. People have been claiming to sometimes hear it since Barkhûsh fell. I know not if it has any truth to it, but some say it is the voice of one who was a king in these lands, long ago.

'I cannot imagine what that has to do with Barkhûsh, though. Thank you for your bravery!'Gôbezhir Firmhand, master smith of Jiret-menêsh, has decided to give his life's masterwork as a gift to Mêshaget.Gôbezhir has asked you to talk to Mêshaget and see if he will join the two of you. Mêshaget is in the courtyard of Jiret-menêsh.Talk to MêshagetYou have invited Mêshaget to join you and Gôbezhir, so that the two of you can present him with a gift. Gôbezhir is in the crafting area of Jiret-menêsh.Return to GôbezhirYou have invited Mêshaget to join you and Gôbezhir, so that the two of you can present him with a gift.Complete Instance: A Gift for the FutureYou went with Gôbezhir to present his masterwork axe to Mêshaget.Talk to Gôbezhir'Now that I have the wood for the haft, I will be finished swiftly. A weapon such as this has no price, so I have decided to give it as a gift to young Mêshaget. Actually, I think it should be a gift from both of us! Without your help, I would still be standing here, lamenting that I don't have the needed materials.

'Would you speak with him and invite him to join us? I think he may be found in the courtyard.''He is on his way? Great! Come, I will show you the axe while we wait for him.''Well, ${PLAYER}, it seems that Mêshaget likes it! Thank you again for the role you played in its making. Semakéfë is a fitting name. I cannot say if us being attacked as I gave it to him bodes ill, or if a swift victory after receiving it bodes well! What we can be certain of is that Mêshaget will carve his own path in life, no matter what Châmelek Gêta says.'

The old dwarf laughs and claps you on the arm before bidding you farewell.'${PLAYER}, what can I do for you, my friend?'

You invite Mêshaget to join you and Gôbezhir Firmhand. Gôbezhir has a gift he would like to give him.

'A gift? That is unexpected! It would be rude to deny someone the opportunity to practice generosity, don't you think?'

Mêshaget chuckles and looks excited to know what it is.

'You go ahead and tell him I will be there shortly.'Sâdikiz has given you a letter and asked you to deliver it to Mêshaget when you return to Jiret-menêsh.You carry a letter given to you by Sâdikiz addressed to Mêshaget. You can't help but notice that the seal on the letter is poorly done and that you would be able to read it without arousing suspicion.'Are you returning to Jiret-menêsh? If so, would you deliver this letter to Mêshaget? I want to thank him for introducing us and catch up on how the boy is doing. We often send letters to each other. The written word was once the only way we could talk to one another, before he convinced that old crone to release her prisoners.

'If you would be so kind as to deliver this to him, I would be forever grateful.'"What is this, a letter from Sâdikiz?'

Mêshaget accepts the letter and immediately opens and reads it in front of you. As he does so, his expression darkens.

'${PLAYER}, do you think I am naive, as my grandmother said? No, do not answer that. I know the answer.'

He tucks the letter into a pocket with a grim look on his face.Namâdi has recently come into possession of a horn that Ordâkhai Gânda use to direct and control mûmakil.Namâdi has asked you to use her Mûmak Trainers' Horn to train a young mûmak and bring it back to her at Khanâg Gur. You should pick up the horn from atop the wall next to her.Pick up the Mûmak Trainers' HornNamâdi has asked you to use her Mûmak Trainers' Horn to train a young mûmak and bring it back to her at Khanâg Gur. You should go to the Spent-lodes in southern An Shêru.Go to the Spent-lodesNamâdi has asked you to use her Mûmak Trainers' Horn to train a young mûmak and bring it back to her at Khanâg Gur. You should use the horn on one of the young mûmakil.Befriend a young mûmak using the hornTry once again to use the Mûmak Trainers' Horn on young mûmakil at the Spent-lodes, and bring one back with you to Khanâg Gur.Befriend a young mûmak using the hornTry again to use the Mûmak Trainers' Horn on young mûmakil at the Spent-lodes, and bring one back with you to Khanâg Gur.Try again to befriend a young mûmak using the hornYou are finding it difficult to befriend a young mûmak. Try again to use the Mûmak Trainers' Horn on young mûmakil at the Spent-lodes, and bring one back with you to Khanâg Gur.Try again to befriend a young mûmak using the hornYou are finding it very difficult to befriend a young mûmak. Try again to use the Mûmak Trainers' Horn on young mûmakil at the Spent-lodes, and bring one back with you to Khanâg Gur.Try once more to befriend a young mûmak using the hornYou begin to wonder if it is even possible to befriend a young mûmak. Try again to use the Mûmak Trainers' Horn on young mûmakil at the Spent-lodes, and bring one back with you to Khanâg Gur.Try once more to befriend a young mûmak using the hornYou're pretty sure it is impossible to befriend a young mûmak by just using the Mûmak Trainers' Horn.Talk to Namâdi at Khanâg Gur'Saráz záro-dan, my friend! I have recently fallen upon good fortune and am looking for a courageous individual with which to share it!'

The woman seems to be very pleased with herself.

'I have acquired a horn that once belonged to a Gânda. It is what they use to tame and control the mighty mûmak! There are many mûmakil running free at Erakúndo, the Spent-lodes. If you are as skilled as you seem, I have a business proposition for you!

'I will cut you in on the profits if you can take the horn and return here with a mûmak! People will pay well to make use of it for their various... activities. I can see it now! There would be so many people wanting to borrow our mûmak that we would need a waiting list!

'Take the horn to the Spent-lodes and use it on a young mûmakil until one responds. Surely at least one will react favourably.''What, no mûmak?'

You tell her that you tried many times, but all they did was attack you.

'Oh. Well, not everyone is suited to be a Gânda. I will find someone else, but thank you for trying.'<rgb=#FF0000>NOTE: You must slot the horn as a skill. To do so, drag the 'Mûmak Trainers' Horn' item from your inventory to your skill bar. You then use the skill on a Young Mûmak to advance the quest.</rgb>Your companions are prepared to advance further into the Ordâkhai-held territory of Idagâl.Ulishár stands by the road into Idagâl.Talk to Ulishár on the road into IdagâlYour next destination is the small village of Ahâba located by the oasis in Idagâl.Travel to the village of Ahâba by the oasis in Idagâl, and introduce yourself to MalgârzorThe herdsman Malgârzor in Ahâba eyes you cautiously.Talk to Malgârzor in AhâbaThe herdsman Malgârzor in Ahâba asked you to defeat "vultures" of different kinds.Defeat vultures at the Fane of Shahûru (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Ordâkhai "vultures" at the Wither-watch (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat brigand "vultures" at Kidu-bâla (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The herdsman Malgârzor in Ahâba will be interested to hear that you dealt with the vultures, as well as the "vultures."Talk to Malgârzor in AhâbaRahâzi is at Ahâba in Idagâl.Talk to Rahâzi at Ahâba'Of our company, only Ulishár has walked into Idagâl. Will he tell us what to expect, and where first we should go?

'Even mighty Shurug-nîn would hesitate to knock upon the gates of Emax Dûl, knowing the Ordâkhai and the Thardúth have joined hands within!''What says Ulishár about our course as we pass into Idagâl?''Emax Dûl has borne many names over the span of its history, each chosen by the power that controlled it: the Menûns of Nísaka, the Khôrthan League, and now the Ordâkhai.

'But Rahâzi is right. We should not make directly for Emax Dûl. Instead let us pass into Idagâl and follow the road northward. There we will find a pleasant settlement beside a green oasis. It is called Ahâba, and the Beltáni herdsmen who dwell there showed me kindness while I wandered, a few years before I was imprisoned by the Ordâkhai. If Malgârzor lives there still, he may help us. I knew him not, but by his reputation he was a man of influence among those who live by the water.''Rahâzi is right. We should not make directly for Emax Dûl. Instead let us pass into Idagâl and follow the road northward. There we will find a pleasant settlement beside a green oasis. It is called Ahâba, and the Beltáni herdsmen who dwell there showed me kindness while I wandered, a few years before I was imprisoned by the Ordâkhai. If Malgârzor lives there still, he may help us. I knew him not, but by his reputation he was a man of influence among those who live by the water.''Outsiders are often drawn to Ahâba by the oasis. It is the custom of my people to welcome any who desire water or food, if they come in peace and offer work in exchange for these gifts.

'We have had difficulty with vultures of late. Should you do something about them, you will have the friendship of the people of Ahâba.'

You ask Malgârzor to describe the vultures and tell you where you can find them, and he smiles broadly at you.

'Some of these vultures fly upon wings, and are at the Fane of Shahûru. But others bear swords of Ordâkh, and these too are unwelcome; they reside at the Wither-watch. Still other vultures might once have called Ahâba their home, but now they live at Kidu-bâla and prey upon their former neighbours. I am pleased you understand me.''We have had difficulty with vultures of late. Should you do something about them, you will have the friendship of the people of Ahâba.

'Some of these vultures fly upon wings, and are at the Fane of Shahûru. But others bear swords of Ordâkh, and these too are unwelcome; they reside at the Wither-watch. Still other vultures might once have called Ahâba their home, but now they live at Kidu-bâla and prey upon their former neighbours. I am pleased you understand me.''I understand you dealt with both the vultures and the "vultures," friend of Ahâba. I thank you for aiding my people in this way.

'Your companion Rahâzi asked some questions while you were away, and I have answered her as best I may. Her knowledge of the old stories exceeds even my own, though her home be far away.''I have answered Rahâzi's questions as best I could. Her knowledge of the old stories exceeds even my own, though her home be far away.''The Din of Stone that drained the lake of Idagâl occurred before even the eldest resident of Ahâba was born. The basin has stood empty for the longest lifetime among Malgârzor's people.

'He tells me the bones of great beasts can be seen in the dry lake-bed. These must be the remains of the dragons slain by Shurug-nîn, so long ago!'The lake-bed in the centre of Idagâl has borne witness to a great deal of history, some tragic and some triumphant.Rahâzi wants to examine the dragon-bones said to lie within the Dry-whelm, west of Ahâba.Meet with Rahâzi by the dragon-bones in the Dry-whelm, west of AhâbaRahâzi stares at the dragon-bones in the Dry-whelm.Talk to Rahâzi by the dragon-bones in the Dry-whelmGlorfindel is in the Dry-whelm.Talk to Glorfindel by the dragon-bones in the Dry-whelmGlorfindel is interested in seeing the Dawn-warden statue to the north-west of the Dry-whelm up close.Travel to the feet of the Dawn-warden statue north-west of the Dry-whelmGlorfindel is at the Dawn-warden statue in Idagâl.Talk to Glorfindel at the Dawn-wardenA number of wayfarers have made pilgrimages to the Dawn-warden statue in Idagâl.Talk to Dimûzga at the Dawn-wardenTalk to Bâlamar at the Dawn-wardenA number of wayfarers have made pilgrimages to the Dawn-warden statue in Idagâl.Talk to Kindrígi at the Dawn-wardenKindrígi asked you to defeat the creatures at the Tombs of Khâb Ishil and recover the stones they stole from the site of the Dawn-warden.Defeat kergrim at the Tombs of Khâb Ishil (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sacred stones from the Tombs of Khâb Ishil (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Kindrígi is waiting to hear news of your success, and to accept delivery of the sacred stones the kergrim stole from the Dawn-warden site.'I would see the bones of these dragons, ${PLAYER}. Will you accompany me to the centre of the lake, that we may see this evidence of Shurug-nîn's great triumph?''Will you accompany me to the centre of the lake, that we may see this evidence of Shurug-nîn's great triumph?'Rahâzi stands here.'It is one thing to hear the songs, ${PLAYER}, but to see such evidence of them so near! The dragons were so large, and yet Shurug-nîn faced them beside the lake of Idagâl, and with the sword Orotheg she brought them low.'

Rahâzi falls silent. When she speaks again, her voice is a whisper.

'Three were the children of Akhízi. Know now their names: Ahûshu, Nuzêru, and Rabûg. Now forget them, for only Shurug-nîn remains.''Three were the children of Akhízi. Know now their names: Ahûshu, Nuzêru, and Rabûg. Now forget them, for only Shurug-nîn remains.''I believe Maethriel stood beside Shurug-nîn when she faced the dragons, ${PLAYER}, but she appears in no further tale; when Akhízi chased Shurug-nîn north to Urash Dâr, the Shagâni warrior fought alone.

'Let us leave Rahâzi to contemplate the dragon bones in peace. I would take a closer look at the great statue that stands beyond the edge of the lake-bed to the west. Malgârzor named it the Dawn-warden, and it watches over this land from a great height.''Let us leave Rahâzi to contemplate the dragon bones in peace. I would take a closer look at the great statue that stands beyond the edge of the lake-bed to the west. Malgârzor named it the Dawn-warden, and it watches over this land from a great height.'Glorfindel stands here.'The Dawn-warden is a most impressive construction even when seen from afar, but more so when seen from below! I understand a similar statue stands to the north, in Khûd Zagin, but that one lies in ruin.

'In his own language Malgârzor named this great statue "Uzûr" and said many wayfarers and pilgrims travel great distances to see it. Perhaps you can speak with some of these travellers and learn what they can tell us of the Ordâkhai presence in Idagâl. They will likely not know anything concerning the Thardúth... but keep open your ears.''Malgârzor named this great statue "Uzûr" and said many wayfarers and pilgrims travel great distances to see it. Perhaps you can speak with some of these travellers and learn what they can tell us of the Ordâkhai presence in Idagâl. They will likely not know anything concerning the Thardúth... but keep open your ears.''Have you come from afar to worship Uzûr, the great statue of Izighûz Menundâri, the Eternal Ruler? The time of his return is nearly at hand, when he will deliver Shagâna from its enemies!''I dare not hope the Menundâri will return during my lifetime. Why has he not already come back to us? What more suffering could be required before he will take pity upon us? Has there not been war and bloodshed enough to merit his return?''Malgârzor named this great statue "Uzûr" and said many wayfarers and pilgrims travel great distances to see it. Perhaps you can speak with some of these travellers and learn what they can tell us of the Ordâkhai presence in Idagâl. They will likely not know anything concerning the Thardúth... but keep open your ears.''So many wayfarers come to this statue to appeal to the Menundâri for his return. They leave various keepsakes at the base of the statue as offerings for him, but I call it a futile endeavour! The Eternal Ruler will return at a time of his choosing, not of ours. Until that moment, all we can do is watch over his sacred sites.

'At night I have heard a terrible wailing, as of an anguished wind, and in the morning assorted items have been taken from the base of Uzûr. I care not for the keepsakes that are taken, but sometimes stones go missing, and because they belong to Uzûr they are sacred.

'I have never caught them doing it, but I suspect the scavenging beasts called kergrim are to blame for these thefts. If you retrieve these sacred stones from their dens to the north-east, you will prove yourself a friend to the people of this land, and to the memory of the Menundâri.''I suspect the scavenging beasts called kergrim are to blame for these thefts. If you retrieve these sacred stones from their dens to the north-east, you will prove yourself a friend to the people of this land, and to the memory of the Menundâri.''I am pleased a stranger would help us in this way. I thank you for it.

'When the Menundâri returns, he will look favourably upon you.'The situation in Idagâl merits further investigation, particularly the relationship between the Ordâkhai of Emax Dûl and the Thardúth of Damudûr.Glorfindel is at the Dawn-warden in Idagâl.Talk to Glorfindel at the Dawn-wardenRahâzi is by the dragon-bones in the Dry-whelm.Talk to Rahâzi by the dragon-bones in the Dry-whelmYour companions agreed to meet back at the village of Ahâba in Idagâl, on the central island in the oasis.Return to the village of Ahâba in Idagâl, and meet your companions on the central island of the oasisInkanûsh has come to Ahâba.Talk to Inkanûsh at AhâbaInkanûsh waits at the village of Ahâba, eager to tell you what he has discovered.Listen to what Inkanûsh has discoveredInkanûsh waits at the village of Ahâba, eager to tell you what he has discovered.Inkanûsh waits at the village of Ahâba, eager to tell you what he has discovered.Listen to what Inkanûsh has discoveredInkanûsh is at the village of Ahâba.Talk to Inkanûsh at AhâbaInkanûsh suggested you join your companions at north-east Ahâba.Rejoin your companions in north-east AhâbaUlishár is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Ulishár in north-east AhâbaSatagêra is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Satagêra in north-east Ahâba'Because you have aided me on this day, I will give you some advice, stranger. The fighting strength of Ordâkh is greatly diminished following Barkhûsh's war... but it is not eliminated. He would have wisdom who avoids Emax Dûl, the last great redoubt of Ordâkh in the Mûr. They say the Hûl died upon a distant field during the war, but no empire can last without its leader. A new Hûl will come to power in Ordâkh. The surest sign of our fortunes changing would be for the Menundâri to return to us first!

'Go in peace, and remember: the Ordâkhai remain a danger in the Mûr, despite their defeat in the north.''Go in peace, and remember: the Ordâkhai remain a danger in the Mûr, despite their defeat in the north.''Thank you for indulging me in this detour, my friend. Let us return to the dry lake-bed and collect our companion who remained behind. I am certain Rahâzi will have appreciated the time she spent in contemplation, but now we must decide our next course.''Let us return to the dry lake-bed and collect our companion who remained behind. I am certain Rahâzi will have appreciated the time she spent in contemplation, but now we must decide our next course.''The sight of these dragon-bones will never leave my memory, ${PLAYER}. I am glad we could spare the time to see them.

'I am ready to return to the oasis at Ahâba. When we have arrived at the central island within the oasis, we will plan our next course of action.''I am ready to return to the oasis at Ahâba. When we have arrived at the central island in the oasis, we will plan our next course of action.'Inkanûsh stands here.'I trust you were not sight-seeing, ${PLAYER}?'

You explain that you were gathering information about the Ordâkhai presence in Idagâl and their relationship with the newly-awoken Thardúth of Damudûr, and the Wizard chuckles.

'My chiding was unserious, my friend; but I see you have already learned some of what I came to tell you! It was with haste that I joined Têrkar's Travellers after I finished my investigation in the Kalmakhíla. Their road aligned with mine, for their next engagement is within the walls of Emax Dûl itself, and I knew your search would bring you near to that city.

'Listen now to what I have learned! I have made some connexions between subjects about which we knew little, and now I know a great deal!''In the year 2890 of the Third Age, Shurash Hûl of Ordâkh raged against the River Usâl, for it blocked the passage of his armies north. With the aid of agents of Mordor, his sorcerers devised and cast deadly spells that cracked the earth about the springs of the river. The rituals triggered great tremors in the foundation of Mûr Ghala, collapsing reservoirs beneath the surface and causing the Usâl to run dry.

'The quakes continued for weeks, one after another, and the ensuing noise caused by the shifting of the rocks came to be known as the Din of Stone, which the Zajâni scholars I have read named Urash-zâl. I showed you some of these accounts, ${PLAYER}; there are others, which shed more detail upon the scope of the injury inflicted during the Din of Stone.

'The Usâl was not the only geologic casualty of Ordâkh and Mordor's fell cooperation. The wells of the Usâl's sister-river, the Agâl, were partially-blocked, and the Idagâl lake emptied, becoming a ruin of cracked and broken mires. More than one hundred years after the Din of Stone, the lake has shown no sign of restoration. Where the water might have gone, I cannot say; perhaps great caverns beneath the surface are now flooded where once they were not. The sorcerers of Ordâkh have much for which to answer.''Comparing the records I found in the Kalmakhíla with the tales of the Kintai of Sul Madásh, I have come to a realization, ${PLAYER}. I believe the Din of Stone unleashed the fungal blight that now consumes Adagím.

'Note my use of the word "unleashed," rather than "created." For I think the entity the Kintai name Sívigush, the Earth-terror, dwelt beneath Nisigím for untold ages of the world. It would have remained there forever, had not the Ordâkhai cracked the earth during the Din of Stone. It may be the fungal blight was fed by newly-flooded caverns beneath the Mûr, but of that I am less certain.''The ritual commanded by Shurash Hûl of Ordâkh damaged bastions along the entirety of Mûr Ghala, from Emax Dûl to the northern gate of Zajâna. Much of this damage was superficial, causing a spider's web of cracks to form along the outside of buildings and knocking items off of shelves.

'It was worse for the Temámir. The Din of Stone collapsed mines up and down the entirety of the Ridge, and thousands of dwarves were lost. Jiret-menêsh survived, but its ruler sealed the fortress in the following years and took little notice of events in the wider world.

'Agents of the Temámir that dared to leave Jiret-menêsh attained much influence elsewhere in Middle-earth. I wonder if Khôltekh would have become a Kinsman of the Kindred of the Coins in Umbar if the Temámir of Jiret-menêsh had played a larger role in the region in the years following the Din of Stone?''Ever since the Dagor Carlanthir was fought, I have believed the Thardúth must possess more hidden vaults than the one we discovered at Utug-bûr. After all, a fox might gain access to one hen-house, and if that is where you keep all of your hens, you will soon find yourself without eggs. The hobbits of the Shire with whom I am well-acquainted would not risk a breakfast without eggs, ${PLAYER}!

'No, it seemed clear to me the Thardúth must possess more than one hidden vault. Like Utug-bûr, another vault would be concealed within the rocks, the better to protect the Thardúth until the enchantment that preserved them came to its end. And also like at Utug-bûr, this vault would not be accessible unless the face of the land was to be changed.

'Shurash Hûl of Ordâkh knew nothing of the Thardúth, but the Din of Stone he caused would enable them to emerge when the time was right. I suspected the location of this second vault might be within An Shêru, but I see you have confirmed it for me.''After the defeat of Sauron during the War of the Ring, the Thardúth tested the will of any who strayed too near their vault in Damudûr. Did they compel the soldiers and sorcerers of Ordâkh to seek them out, to finish what the Din of Stone had begun, clearing the blocked way into Damudûr? I feel certain they did, ${PLAYER}, but some to whom they spoke may have resisted. The pryings of the Thardúth are ungentle; an unprepared mind could not withstand such invasion, and with resistance would be broken.

'But we have an advantage, my friend. The Thardúth slumbered within their vaults for thousands of years, and during that time these servants of Morgoth possessed no knowledge of the outside world. They emerged from their hidden vaults like children, children who know nothing of present days and who must be taught. The Thardúth have chosen the Ordâkhai to teach them, and while the Empire of Ordâkh remains a danger to us, it still reels from Sauron's defeat during the War of the Ring.

'If the Thardúth are to learn the state of things from their Ordâkhai teachers, their classroom is sure to be within the fortress of Emax Dûl. You will need to find a way inside, ${PLAYER}.

'For now, go to north-east Ahâba and ask Ulishár what he can tell us.''We must make our plans, and make them well. For now, go to north-east Ahâba and ask Ulishár what he can tell us.'Ulishár stands here.'I told you we would see Têrkar's Travellers again!

'They have been employed to perform a new play for the Ordâkhai, but Satagêra has been having some trouble finishing it to her satisfaction.''I told you we would see Têrkar's Travellers again!''I have written more plays than I can count on ten fingers, ${PLAYER}, but this one is proving more difficult than those others.'

Satagêra shakes her head with disgust.

'The Ordâkhai have commissioned us to perform a new play that will inspire their people. They want it to be called "The Great Triumph of Mordor and Ordâkh," and it is intended to convey that their defeat in the war was actually a victory, and their greatest triumph is still to come!

'Coin is coin, ${PLAYER}, and the performance of this play will keep Têrkar's Travellers fed and comfortable for many months... but these are all lies!'Satagêra has some thoughts about the play she has been tasked with writing.Satagêra gave you part of the script she has already written.Read the script Satagêra gave to youSatagêra is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Satagêra in north-east AhâbaSatagêra gave you another part of the script she has written.Read another part of the script Satagêra gave to youJêsamin is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Jêsamin in north-east AhâbaImalêkh is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Imalêkh in north-east AhâbaYou have read portions of Satagêra's script and should return them to her.Ulishár is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Ulishár in north-east Ahâba'I know these for lies because my friends were there, ${PLAYER}, and they have told me how it was. What the Ordâkhai have asked me to write is nothing near the truth. This has never been a problem for me before now! Think you my plays were always true? It is not so!'

Satagêra sighs heavily.

'I say I cannot understand, but that is also a falsehood. I know why I struggle so with this one. It is because my husband Têrkar died in service of their war, as did so many of my friends; those that returned were not the same. I wish you could have known Mashkânu before he fought in the north. He speaks not of it to any of us, but whatever he saw changed him. Only rarely now does he laugh, when before he was always merry.

'For me to write the Ordâkhai's words feels like a betrayal. Here! See for yourself!''Read the portion I gave to you and see for yourself!''The only true word on that parchment is "despairing," ${PLAYER}. Many were the lies that sent my friends marching north to Barkhûsh's war, and now I add another: that victory is near at hand, despite the grave defeats I know were suffered.

'Here, see another portion of my script.''Read the portion I gave to you and see for yourself!''It cannot be that bad, can it?

'We have performed plays for the Ordâkhai before. Their coins spend just as well as any others... perhaps better!

'If we had not performed "Bite of the Black Serpent" in Emax Dûl we would not have met Ulishár, and he might still be imprisoned there, or slain!''We have performed plays for the Ordâkhai before. Their coins spend just as well as any others... perhaps better!''I fought on the field before the great city in the north. The nobles of Ordâkh who commissioned this play would not pay for a true account of what befell their soldiers there.

'But Jêsamin is right about this. Even an untrue play may accomplish some good. Had Satagêra not written "Bite of the Black Serpent," we would not have met Ulishár, and Têrkar would not have asked us to spirit him to freedom.''But Jêsamin is right about this. Even an untrue play may accomplish some good. Had Satagêra not written "Bite of the Black Serpent," we would not have met Ulishár, and Têrkar would not have asked us to spirit him to freedom.''My companions are as supportive as ever, but by itself that puts no words on the page! I will keep at it.

'I fear "The Great Triumph of Mordor and Ordâkh" will not live up to my other works, ${PLAYER}. Let us hope the nobles who commissioned it believe otherwise!''I fear "The Great Triumph of Mordor and Ordâkh" will not live up to my other works, ${PLAYER}. Let us hope the nobles who commissioned it believe otherwise!''It has been nice to catch up with Têrkar's Travellers, but we should no longer delay our work.'As you approached what you thought was the entrance to the tomb of Semayâti the Unyielding, you heard a frightened scream from inside. It must be Mêshaget! You should enter the tomb and help him.Mêshaget is being attacked by a dwarf-wight! You should help him defeat his foe.Save MêshagetYou helped Mêshaget and defeated the dwarf-wight he was fighting. You should talk to him.Talk to MêshagetHelp Mêshaget search the tomb for the journal of Semayâti the Unyielding.Search the tomb with MêshagetThis prominent stone grave must belong to Semayâti the Unyielding. You should open it and see what awaits inside.Open the tomb of Semayâti the UnyieldingYou have found the journal of Semayâti the Unyielding, the famous engineer from Lâkindar of old. You should take the journal out of her stone resting place.Take the journalYou have the journal of Semayâti the Unyielding, the famous engineer from Lâkindar of old. You should talk to Mêshaget.Talk to MêshagetYou have what you came for and should now leave the Quelling-house.Escape the Quelling-house with Mêshaget'I... I have never killed a fellow dwarf before. Nor someone I recently thought of as a dear friend. My heart hurts more than this cut on my leg.'

The young dwarf is clearly trying to blink back tears.

'Something in my eye. Worry not, I will be fine. We have done what we came to do. We will give the journal to Châmelek Gêta, and Jiret-menêsh will be protected from outside threats while also freeing us from being prisoners in our own home. It will be a long walk. Best to get started.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}, I thought I was soon to join the dead. I felt I would be haunting the Quelling-house myself by the morrow!

'I am certain this is where Semayâti the Unyielding was laid to rest. Hopefully she rests still! Come, let's look at the tomb!''The journal! This will change everything, ${PLAYER}, you will see. May I carry it?

'Come, let us leave this cursed place and return to Jiret-menêsh.'Disturbed by something in Sâdikiz's letter, Mêshaget has decided that he must enter Lâkindar, the Quelling-house, and he has asked you to accompany him.Mêshaget promised to explain what is going on when you meet at the top of the great staircase at Lâkindar's entrance. Lâkindar is located far to the south of Jiret-menêsh in An Shêru.Meet Mêshaget at the entrance to LâkindarYou have travelled to Lâkindar and found Mêshaget waiting for you. You should talk to him.Talk to Mêshaget outside of LâkindarMêshaget wants to ensure neither of you were followed here. You should enter the Quelling-house and assess the situation.Enter the Quelling-houseThe dead have awoken and are roaming the halls of Lâkindar. You should defeat some of the wights to clear the way for Mêshaget.Defeat wights (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have cleared a path in the Quelling-house and have yet to see Mêshaget. You should search for the tomb of Semayâti the Unyielding.Find the entrance to the tomb of Semayâti the UnyieldingAs you approached what you thought was the entrance to the tomb of Semayâti the Unyielding, you heard a frightened scream from inside. It must be Mêshaget! You should enter the tomb and help him.Complete Instance: Into the Quelling-houseFollowing a confrontation with Sâdikiz the Sky-hungry and his Ordâkhai allies, you have helped an injured Mêshaget return to Jiret-menêsh where you were both immediately brought before an angry Châmelek Gêta.Talk to Châmelek GêtaChâmelek Gêta is at once furious with you both but also awestruck at having the journal of Semayâti the Unyielding handed to her. She demands Mêshaget explain what this is about. You should talk to Mêshaget.Talk to MêshagetMêshaget has pleaded his case with Châmelek Gêta as well as he could, asking only that she take the time to review the journal and consider the option of making alliances and fortifying the city. You should talk to Châmelek Gêta to hear her response.Talk to Châmelek GêtaChâmelek Gêta agreed to examine the plans in the journal and discuss the situation with Mêshaget after she has done so. You should talk to Mêshaget inside the Gêta's hall.Talk to Mêshaget'${PLAYER}, when you met Sâdikiz, what was your opinion of him? Did you think him honourable? It pains me to question the motivations of one I have long considered a mentor and friend, but since we have succeeded in opening the gates, he has started asking many questions he should not. He seems to have no ill will for the hall of his fathers, but I cannot help but wonder now if it was all a lie. A false face to earn my trust and then use me to the ruin of our people.'

The young dwarf looks thoughtful for a moment.

'There is something I must do, and I wonder if you would help me. To the south lies the ruin of Lâkindar. Many refer to it now as The Quelling-house. Lâkindar was once a wealthy mine of the Temámir, but many years ago, the priests of Barkhûsh turned it into a slaughter-house and sacrificed many innocent people there, Man and dwarf alike, to their wicked deity. As you may imagine, Châmelek Gêta has forbidden us from going there. The place is surely cursed.

'But go there I must. Would you accompany me? I will tell you more of our purpose once we arrive. Meet me at the top of the great stair. You will know it when you see it.''I am relieved you came! This place has a foul air. It strikes fear in my heart and makes my hair stand on end! I do not think I could enter were I alone.'

Mêshaget is sweating, visibly afraid of this place and is fighting to gather his courage.

'It is said that inside Lâkindar lies the tomb of a legend, Semayâti the Unyielding. At the time of Lâkindar's height, she was a celebrated Temámir crafter, inventor, and engineer. It is said that there was nothing she could not do well!

'It is believed that upon her passing, she was entombed with her journal, which was filled with many inventions and designs that were never made into reality. According to the history I have read, one of these designs was a plan to fortify Jiret-menêsh against attackers.

'We have been relying on secrecy and ignorance to protect ourselves for one hundred years, ${PLAYER}. It is only a matter of time before those who envy our great wealth decide to take it for themselves, and our lives with it. We will find the tomb of Semayâti the Unyielding and recover her journal. I will convince Châmelek Gêta to make these designs a reality so that we may live as part of the world, and yet also live without fear.

'Please go in ahead of me. I should remain behind for a few minutes to ensure neither of us has been followed, and then I will catch up to you inside.''Explain yourselves! Why is my grandson injured, and why is he in my hall instead of my hale-house?'

Châmelek Gêta appears to be furious. Mêshaget hobbles forward and kneels before her, holding out the journal of Semayâti the Unyielding. She snatches it out of his hands and looks it over, flipping through the pages before she pauses, and her look of anger is replaced with one of wonder.

'What is this?'

Humble, and without looking up, Mêshaget says, 'The designs of Semayâti the Unyielding, that we may use to build defences to protect Jiret-menêsh and its people.'

She sets the book aside as Mêshaget returns to his feet.

'You fools went into the Quelling-house!? Did I not tell you that this place is forbidden? Tell me why you disobey me, Mêshaget!''Châmelek Gêta, you are wise, and only want what is best for all of us. That, I understand and respect. I was not unaware of what lurks within the Quelling-house, and I chose to enter this forbidden place, risking my own life, for the chance to bring safety to our people without sacrificing their freedom.

'I do not believe that things must be only one way or the other. We can have both security and freedom if we fight for it! Barkhûsh and his priests are gone. Ordâkh has no Hûl... or perhaps too many Hûls, I am not sure. By all accounts, they should be weakened! We can surely defend ourselves against them, and can do so with allies, if only we allowed ourselves to make some.

'If Zajâna were to find value in trading with us, they would likely be willing to help defend us, should it become necessary. Evil does not lurk in the hearts of all Men. It was Men who killed the Hûl in Gondor, and it is said that the same Men helped bring Barkhûsh to his knees. And what of the Shoreward Stone? As Temámir, we are not friendless or alone.

'I only ask that you examine the works of Semayâti the Unyielding, and allow for further discussion.'

Mêshaget bows to the Gêta respectfully as he ends his argument.After a moment of silence, Châmelek Gêta responds.

'It will be as you say. I will look upon what is in this book, and we will discuss your proposal. You have not won, Shakâr-inik, I only agree to a discussion!'

She leans forward conspiratorially.

'Speak to me, ${PLAYER}, do you have children? Grandchildren? If not, avoid it at all costs! I fear that as Mêshaget comes into his own, he is becoming more stubborn than I am! One day, he will be Gêta and may do as he wills. Why he is impatient and chooses to spend all of his time fighting with me, I will never know.

'There is a part of me that wishes to punish you for allowing him to endanger himself on such an unwise adventure, but I do not think you would have been able to talk him out of it if you had tried. Instead, I suspect that you are the only reason the fool is still alive. Am I correct?

'Thank you for returning my rough gem to me. I am glad he has you for a friend.''That went better than I expected, ${PLAYER}! Châmelek Gêta may be stone-headed, but she is not blind to reason when it is in front of her. Jiret-menêsh has a bright future ahead, and she will soon see that.

'Thank you for all the help you have given me. You are a steadfast friend, and you came at the right time for when I truly needed one. Sâdikiz crafted his own fate, which I know in my mind, but my heart hurts for what I have done, regardless.

'Should I have shown him mercy? I do not think he would have shown me any, but does that make my wrath warranted? Was a death blow truly necessary? It will be some time before I can sleep easily at night, I think.

'I wish you the best on your travels, ${PLAYER}. May we meet again in more prosperous times.'Men have mined in An Shêru for hundreds of years, but the Din of Stone was powerful and likely collapsed the mines not of dwarf-design.Kufâro has asked you to visit the locations of other mines in An Shêru and assess their condition. Erakúndo and Gebarakór are near each other in southern An Shêru. Adazâr is in the far western part of the region, and Lâkindar is in the south-west corner.Find the mine at ErakúndoFind the mine at GebarakórFind the mine at the Arch of AdazârFind the mine at LâkindarYou have visited the locations of other mines in An Shêru and assessed their condition. You should return to Kufâro in Jiret-menêsh.Talk to Kufâro in Jiret-menêsh'Well met, traveller. I have a question, and I was wondering if you could help me find the answer. Historically, we have had many problems with Men coming to An Shêru to pillage its once rich resources. The result is the landscape you see around you today.

'In an earlier age, we went to war with Men who wanted the greatest treasure of all, Jiret-menêsh. This war ended with an agreement that Men were allowed to mine in other parts of An Shêru, but there were provisions that they cause no harm to the land and do not mar its beauty. Their king swore an Oath to this, and for many generations of Men, the Oath was kept.

'When these Ordâkhai arrived from the south, they did not know of this oath, though it was made on behalf of all Men. But I do not think they would have cared, even if they had known about it. They cut down and hauled off all of the trees to fuel their war machine. They dug pit-mines... horrible things, those.

'Their final blow was the Din of Stone, causing massive unnatural earthquakes that surely collapsed many mines and stopped the flow of the Usâl River. It was then that Châmelek Gêta had had enough of Men and sealed the doors of Jiret-menêsh shut to the outside world.

'I cannot help but wonder, though, did all of the other mines collapse? Could any of them be reopened? In what state will we find the pit-mines? Since you are unafraid to traverse this land, would you be willing to visit these locations and tell me how they fare? I am most curious about Erakúndo, Gebarakór, Adazâr, and Lâkindar.''Hah! Of course Lâkindar still stands proud! Nothing is as strong as dwarf stone-craft! If this Erakúndo is called the Spent-lodes, exploration there may be a waste of time, though we dwarves may yet find seams missed by the Men who named it so.

'The Ordâkhai mine is an open pit to this day? Zigêt! I hope that one day we are able to oust those worthless wretches. Or better yet, someone else does it for us! This Arch of Adazâr is intriguing. From your description, it sounds like it is the work of Shagâni Men, before the coming of Ordâkh.

'Well, ${PLAYER}, you have given me much to think about! Thank you for sating my curiosity.'Kergrim have taken up residence at the Shagâni ruins of the Arch of Adazâr, which spans a well-travelled road.You should defeat kergrim at the Arch of Adazâr in western An Shêru. Doing so would make travelling the road that passes beneath the arch more safe for all.Defeat kergrim at the Arch of Adazâr (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many kergrim at the Arch of Adazâr in western An Shêru, making travelling the road that passes beneath the arch safer for all.CompletedThe kergrim at the Arch of Adazâr are disturbingly close to a well-travelled road. While they are known for devouring the dead, kergrim do not hesitate to attack the living, posing a threat to anyone passing beneath the arch. Defeating some of the kergrim here will make the road safer for all.The road that passes beneath the Arch of Adazâr is now safer for travellers after your defeat of some of these dangerous beasts.Sâdikiz has asked for your help to carry out his plan for a way to pay his debts.Sâdikiz says he can repay his debts by selling the glands of rock-worms found at the Pools of Taridím, west of Khanâg Gur, and the Seething-wells in northern An Shêru.Collect rock-worm glands (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have collected the rock-worm glands that Sâdikiz requested. You should bring them to him at Khanâg Gur.'Although my debt is that of coin, I devised a plan to pay it in the form of a valuable substance. However, thanks to an old injury, I find myself unable to collect it. You have helped me already once today, but I wonder if you would be willing to aid the friend of a friend again?

'Here in An Shêru there are hot springs that are home to large rock-worms. These worms have glands that contain a toxin that is valuable for its use in alchemy. If you were able to collect eight of these glands, I could sell them to the right buyer for enough to cover my debt. Would you aid an old, battered dwarf?

'The nearest hot spring where you can find rock-worms is the Pools of Taridím, just to the west of here. There is another hot spring in the north, the Seething-wells, where they can also be found. If you want to help, you should go there to hunt for the beasts.''Well, pull my beard, you got them! Thank you, ${PLAYER}, you are truly a life-saver.

'While you were gone, I found myself thinking about the safety of Jiret-menêsh. Since the arrival of these new Orcs, the Ordâkhai seem more determined than ever to make everyone miserable. Tell me, do you know what defences they have in place? What is their plan, should an attack come?'

You tell him that you are not privy to that information.

'Ah, that is too bad then. Thank you all the same.'Tekâmi is worried that she will not be able to find a trade partner, while other dwarves are sending out trade emissaries.Tekâmi has asked you to go to Khanâg Gur and speak with their leader on her behalf about the possibility of a business partnership.Talk to Halshûm at Khanâg GurYou went to Khanâg Gur to ask Halshûm if he wanted to do business with Tekâmi. He did not, but he will find someone who does if you do something for him. He wants you to go to Kôth Rau and take back the rations that he recently sold to them, so that they will need to buy more. Kôth Rau is in southern An Shêru.Help the Ordâkhai 'lose' their rations (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You went to Khanâg Gur to ask Halshûm if he wanted to do business with Tekâmi. He did not, but he will find someone who does if you do something for him. You went to Kôth Rau and took back the ration crates he recently sold to them, so that they will need to buy more. You should return to Halshûm at Khanâg Gur in central An Shêru.Bring the rations to Halshûm at Khanâg GurAfter helping Halshûm, he gave you the name of a merchant in Zajâna, Zarzûmir, who will do business with Tekâmi. He did not want the Ordâkhai rations that you helped the soldiers of Kôth Rau 'lose' and said you can do whatever you want with them. You should give them to the Jatsakóri in north-eastern An Shêru, as they are always in need of supplies.After helping Halshûm, he gave you the name of a merchant in Zajâna, Zarzûmir, who will do business with Tekâmi. You first gave the Ordâkhai rations to the Jatsakóri at Gadim-ûn, and should now go to Zajâna in search of Zarzûmir, who is said to always be in one of the markets.Talk to Zarzûmir in ZajânaYou met with Zarzûmir in Zajâna, and he agreed to trade with Tekâmi. You should return to Jiret-menêsh and tell Tekâmi of your success.Return to Tekâmi in Jiret-menêsh'Most of us who came to the surface did so because we want to trade. Not many visitors have come to us, and there have been no trade caravans yet. Some of the bravest among us have left to make deals with the Temámir of the Shoreward Stone, but I am a jeweller, not a trader, and I am certainly no adventurer!

'I fear that I will be left behind without a trade partner, but I have an idea. I have heard that Khanâg Gur still does business, does it not? If someone were to go there and speak to their leader, perhaps they could sell my goods or take them to a market elsewhere?'

You warn her that the leader of Khanâg Gur is as likely to steal from her as he is to help her.

'I can afford the risk. I will start small, and if I am cheated, then I will stop trading with them. Would you speak to them on my behalf?''${PLAYER}, what brings you to me?'

You ask Halshûm if he has any interest in a trade agreement with a Temámir jeweller from Jiret-menêsh.

'Interesting... I myself am not, but I know many people and may still be able to help you. I would need you to do something for me first, of course.

'I sell supplies to the Ordâkhai at Kôth Rau. It is why they allow us to keep Khanâg Gur in business. Among those supplies are foodstuffs that resist spoilage - very useful for an army. I happen to find myself with more of these rations than the Ordâkhai wanted to buy, and I need to sell them. There is gold invested in them, and I need it back.

'Should you find yourself in Kôth Rau, and the rations they bought from me happened to go missing, they would need to replace their lost supplies. Do you follow me? If this thing happens, I will see to it that this jeweller of yours has a buyer.'Upon seeing you return with the crates, Halshûm laughs.

'You did it! If you want to become a Khanda-khêl, let me know. I think you would fit in well.

'I do not want those rations, though. My men have finer tastes than Ordâkhai soldiers do, and I do not expect the dullards to buy them for a third time.'

He chuckles before waving his hand in the air dismissively.

'Do with them what you will. Just get them out of here.'

'Now, on with our business. In Zajâna there is a man named Zarzûmir. You will find him lingering in one of the marketplaces. He would give your jeweller a fair price, and he has caravans that pass through An Shêru, so he could pick up the goods.

'I do business with him myself. He pays me to ensure his caravans make it through An Shêru without being attacked by bandits. Which is very easy to do. I just order them to leave him alone.'

Halshûm laughs again at his own cleverness.

'Anyway, tell Zarzûmir Khanda-mashár sent you, and he will not refuse your offer.''${PLAYER}, you return! But why?'

You present the Ordâkhai rations to the leader of the Jatsakóri and ask Nabatínu if he wants them.

'By the stars, yes! You know we would not turn away any unspoiled food. Thank you for remembering us. You are a treasure.''Saráz záro-dan, what can Zarzûmir do for you?'

You tell the man that you were sent by Khanda-mashár and that there is a Temámir jeweller in Jiret-menêsh who is looking for a partner to whom she can sell her creations.

'Jewellery? Made by a dwarf in the legendary Jiret-menêsh? Yes, I think I could sell those kinds of goods, no problem, and make a tidy sum at the same time.

'Not that it seems I have a choice, but you do not need to tell me twice to do something that brings in coin. I will assume Khanda-mashár will want a cut, but I will work that out with him. He will also tell me where to find Jiret-menêsh. There are few things he doesn't know. Thank you for bringing me this.''${PLAYER}, you were gone so long, I thought you had forgotten me, but you went all the way to Zajâna on my behalf? I do not know that I am worthy of such effort, but thank you! I have many rings and pendants ready to trade, but I look forward to talking to this Zarzûmir about what is best for selling, and make more of that!

'This brings me so much joy, ${PLAYER}! I cannot wait to get back to my workbench!'Sûlokil gather in large numbers on An Shêru's dusty southern plains. There are more of the large beasts here than the land can support.You should defeat sûlokil in An Shêru. A herd can be found gathering at the Beast-fields in the south-eastern part of the region.Defeat sûlokil in An Shêru (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have culled some of the sûlokil in An Shêru, making it easier for the others to survive in this harsh wasteland.CompletedThe sûlok herd gathering at Modan Rôkh, the Beast-fields, has little access to food and water, making them more aggressive than usual and posing a threat to passers-by. Culling some of their numbers would be beneficial for both travellers as well as the herd.Your culling of some of the sûlokil in An Shêru has made it easier for the others to survive in this harsh wasteland, while also making the nearby road a little safer for travellers.Gredbyg infest the Slopes of Shâlahin in An Shêru and are always multiplying.You should defeat gredbyg on the Slopes of Shâlahin in An Shêru, south-west of Jiret-menêsh.Defeat gredbyg at the Slopes of Shâlahin (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated many gredbyg on the Slopes of Shâlahin in An Shêru, south-west of Jiret-menêsh.CompletedThe gredbyg of An Shêru are ready to devour anything and everything that they can. Everyone would sleep better at night if there were fewer of them.There will likely always be gredbyg on the Slopes of Shâlahin, but now there are fewer of the vermin, which most would agree is a good thing.Gôbezhir Firmhand is hoping that usable wood from sarláshi trees can still be found at Zakhûr, where a Kinta grove once stood. He wants to use this wood to make the haft of the axe he is working on, his life's masterwork.Gôbezhir has asked you to go to Zakhûr in eastern An Shêru to search for branches of sarláshi wood.Collect sarláshi branches (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have found several branches of sarláshi wood. You should return to Jiret-menêsh so you can give them to Gôbezhir.Return to Jiret-menêshYou have found several branches of sarláshi wood. You talk to Gôbezhir and give them to him.'${PLAYER}, while I smelt the iron, would you be willing to do another thing for me?

'I have been thinking about the haft for this axe, and because the iron is of such fine quality, I feel like the wood used for the haft should be equally so. An Shêru has not always been a barren landscape. When I was but a lad, the Usâl was a clean mountain spring, and this land was rich with trees. In fact, there was a grove of those sarláshi trees that the Elves tended.

'I do not doubt that the Elves are gone, but perhaps something remains of the trees? I apologize that it is far to the east, but would you mind going to where this grove once was, Zakhûr, to see if any wood remains? It is a hearty wood, and if any fallen branches remain, they may yet be usable.''Fortune follows you, ${PLAYER}! Yes, these will do nicely! The branches feel strong indeed, just as I remember this wood being.'The Ordâkhai have constructed a long bridge across the lake to allow for passage to the ruins of Kasukhín.Standing by the banner, you know you will be very exposed when you seek to cross the bridge the Ordâkhai constructed. You should be careful.Examine the banner by the long bridgeYou should make your way carefully across the bridge. <rgb=#00FF00>Look</rgb> for threats when you have begun to cross.You look out for threats near the start of the bridge.CompletedYou should make your way carefully across the bridge. <rgb=#00FF00>Think</rgb> things over when you have reached the middle of the bridge.Curious thoughts come to mind as you cross the long bridge.CompletedYou should make your way carefully across the bridge. <rgb=#00FF00>Listen</rgb> for danger when you have reached the far end of the bridge.You have crossed the long bridge to Kasukhín without incident.CompletedStanding by the banner, you know you will be very exposed when you seek to cross the bridge the Ordâkhai constructed. You should be careful.

When you have stepped onto the bridge and begun to cross you should <rgb=#00FF00>look</rgb> for threats.Sûg Nidar is filled with ships of the Song, but the fleet remains motionless: no one aboard the ships seems to have yet noticed your presence on the bridge.

You should continue onward and <rgb=#00FF00>think</rgb> things over when you have reached the middle of the bridge.Still the ships of the Song remain where they are; still the fleet fails to approach your position. Your mind wanders, and you contemplate the island at the end of the bridge.

Once this land was covered with short grasses and small shrubs, and herdsmen drove their flocks of grazing animals beside the steep hill on which was built the citadel of Kasukhín. But that all changed with the coming of Ordâkh and the Din of Stone. The drowning of the plains caused the vast lake of Sûg Nidar, the Fearwater, to form, and the hilltop became an island.

Years later, the Ordâkhai constructed this bridge high above the old floor of the plains to provide access to the ruins. You wonder if they ever think of what the land must have been before their arrival, and decide they likely do not.

You should continue onward and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> for anything out of the ordinary once you have reached the end of the bridge.You listen for any sign of danger, but aside from the whistling of the wind as it passes among the stones of Kasukhín high above, you hear nothing. You have crossed the long bridge without incident. The crews of the fleet are not expecting anyone to come so far into Sûg Nidar.

You resolve to make them pay for their oversight.You have found a black-sailed Orc ship moored beside an orange-sailed Heirsworn ship, and have devised a means of setting them against each other.Standing by a banner in view of a black-sailed Orc ship and an orange-sailed Heirsworn ship, you consider a means of turning the two crews against each other.Examine the banner in view of the Orc ship and the Heirsworn shipYou decide to defeat Orcs on the black-sailed ship and Heirsworn Corsairs on the orange-sailed ship, turning them against each other.Defeat Orc ship-mates on the black-sailed ship (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Defeat Heirsworn ship-mates on the orange-sailed ship (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have wrought confusion among the ships of the Heirsworn and the Orcs.CompletedStanding by a banner in view of a black-sailed Orc ship and an orange-sailed Heirsworn ship, you consider a means of turning the two crews against each other. If you attack the ship-mates aboard one ship while claiming to be sent by those on the other, you will sow confusion among those who remain. And then you can do the same on the other ship!

'This is a great plan,' you say to yourself.The crews of each ship are now alerted and distrustful of each other, making it more difficult for them to work together.

'Well done!' you say to yourself, but your approval is short-lived. The fleet is much larger than these two, and remains a danger to the coasts of Middle-earth.The Beltáni chieftain, Unusâli, does not trust outsiders easily. She asks you to help the people of Ahâba with their work to prove yourself to her.Unusâli has decided she will not trust you until you prove yourself to the Beltáni.

You should complete three quests in Ahâba to prove yourself.Complete quests in Ahâba (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Unusâli has heard of your work for the Beltáni and is pleased.

You should talk to Unusâli in Ahâba.Talk to Unusâli'I believe your Shâra when she says she wants to protect us from the Ordâkhai, but I sense you want something from us in return. We are not ones to trust outsiders so easily, not after our many years of resistance to Ordâkh. If you want an alliance, you will need to prove yourselves to us.

'It is simple. Help the Beltáni, and we will help you.''It seems you are being put to work! Apologies, friend!

'While you are helping the locals, we will negotiate with Unusâli. Ahâba would be a perfect staging area for our soldiers to gather, but I will not force anything upon the Beltáni if they are unwilling to join our fight.''Unusâli seems to be a hard woman, but I suspect she only wishes to protect her village. The Ordâkhai have caused these people much grief over the years, and I do not blame her for being slow to trust.

'This will be my first real chance to prove myself to Mizâdi as her diplomat. I believe I can make headway on this negotiation by appealing to our shared enemy, and their similar pasts....''My people are pleased with your work. You may stay.

'While you were gone, Mizâdi and I spoke of how she became Shâra of Renewed Hamât. We share a bond: the cruelty of Ordâkh took both our husbands. We have agreed to help each other claim vengeance.

'When the forces of Hamât gather before they march to make war on Emax Dûl, they may make camp in Ahâba. We will then add who we can to your host. May the Beltáni finally be free.'Batâla has asked you to deliver meals to the residents of Ahâba.Batâla has asked you to deliver meals to the residents of Ahâba.

You should collect the packaged meals. They are in a bag near Batâla's hut.Collect packaged mealsYou have collected the package of meals to deliver to the residents of Ahâba.

You should deliver the meals to Beltáni shepherds and hunters around the village.You have delivered meals to the Beltáni.

You should return to Batâla.Talk to Batâla'The fruits you picked will be stewed and baked with pastry. I call them inba-hûtu. The cooking will bring out the fruits' natural sugars. Nature's bounty is delicious, is it not?

'I have packaged up some meals for you to deliver to the hunters and shepherds out working. They are in that bag by the hut. Only one each!''You return with an empty bag. Your job must be finished and my people fed... or did you eat all the inba-hûtu yourself?

'Only joking, friend. Thank you for your help!''Ah, more of Batâla's delicious cooking? Thank you, friend.''Batâla has found a new helper, eh? Thank you for the food!''What do you have there, friend? It smells delicious! Oh, inba-hûtu for me? Thank you very much!''Ah, I smell Batâla's inba-hûtu! Thank you for saving me the trip back, friend!''A meal delivery! Thank you, stranger. Now I just need to keep the sheep from eating it!''Who are you? Oh, you have food for me? I guess that makes you a friend!''My sheep seem very interested in whatever you are carrying, friend! Ah, no wonder, inba-hûtu! If they are well behaved, perhaps I will share!'A bounty has been posted for the slaying of a dangerous vulture near Ahâba.You have accepted a bounty to defeat Akílu the Feaster and clean up the Fane of Shahûru.

You should travel to the Fane of Shahûru, north of Ahâba and complete the bounty.Defeat Akílu the FeasterBurn animal carcasses (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated Akílu the Feaster and cleaned up the Fane of Shahûru.

You should return to Shâpar in Ahâba for your reward.Talk to ShâparA bounty is posted on this board. It reads:

'Wanted: A brave soul to venture to the Fane of Shahûru and slay the great vulture known as Akílu the Feaster. She and her spawn are disrupting the natural balance of the area by hoarding more carcasses than they can consume and attacking travellers on the road.

'Whoever slays the Feaster and removes the uneaten carcasses from the Fane of Shahûru will receive a reward from the Beltáni of Ahâba.''Ah, you have slain the Feaster? Excellent work. She will bother us with her gluttony no longer!

'Here is your well-earned reward.'A bounty has been posted for the slaying of a dangerous cobra in a hidden oasis.You have accepted a bounty to collect the head of Ershubât the Cradle-thief and slay his spawn.

You should travel to the hidden oasis, Nax Shalâl, and complete the bounty.Collect the head of ErshubâtDefeat cobras in Nax Shalâl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have claimed the head of Ershubât the Cradle-thief.

You should return to Ashâb at the Dawn-warden for your reward.Talk to AshâbA bounty is posted on this board. It reads:

'Wanted: The head of the vile beast known as Ershubât the Cradle-thief. This massive cobra is believed to be responsible for the deaths of many Shagâni babes, killed in their own cribs. Any adventurer who ventures into the hidden oasis Nax Shalâl and slays the snake and his spawn will be rewarded.''Ah, the fearsome hunter returns with the head of the baby-killer! The residents of Shagâna will sleep better tonight! Here is your reward.'A bounty has been posted for a large frilled lizard that has been antagonizing the Beltáni in Ahâba.You have accepted a bounty to defeat Gírkhash and other bronze lizards in Idagâl.

You should find and defeat these lizards.Defeat GírkhashDefeat bronze lizards in Idagâl (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have defeated Gírkhash and many of his spawn.

You should return to Shâpar in Ahâba for your reward.Talk to ShâparA bounty is posted on this board. It reads:

'Wanted: A brave adventurer to find and defeat the giant bronze lizard known as Gírkhash. This lizard and his spawn have been attacking the sheep and ransacking the foodstuff in Ahâba and must be hunted down.''You have defeated the lizard Gírkhash? Excellent work, ${CLASS}. Here is your well-earned reward!'A bounty has been posted for the slaying of a massive dread-tortoise.You have accepted a bounty to defeat Kasâshu the Grinder and reclaim something of great value from his belly.

You should travel to Karmu Râx and complete the bounty.Collect the 'item of great value'Defeat Kasâshu the GrinderYou have reclaimed someone's severed hand from the dread-tortoise.

You should return to Ashâb at the Dawn-warden for your reward.Talk to AshâbA bounty is posted on this board. It reads:

'Wanted: an adventurer to slay the dread-tortoise known as Kasâshu the Grinder. This massive beast has eaten something of great value to a soldier of Aradûn, and the object must be reclaimed from its innards. The Grinder was last seen among the other dread-tortoises at Karmu Râx. The Servants of Aradûn offer a reward for any who can assist with this task.''Arâniz told us that the Aradûni have posted a bounty on a certain dread-tortoise at Karmu Râx called 'Kasâshu the Grinder'. Apparently he ate something of value to them? It did not seem worth the risk to us, but perhaps you would like to take them up on it.'You produce the severed hand and show it to Ashâb. He grimaces at the ghastly sight, but seems relieved that you have returned with it.

'Ah! I was worried no one would ever complete this bounty. That dread-tortoise was proving very difficult to kill.'

Ashâb removes the glove from the hand, revealing a simple gold wedding ring on one of the fingers.

'This is the true item of great value. One of our soldiers lost his hand to that beast, but refused to return home without it. You have done him a great service, ${PLAYER}.'It is time for Têrkar's Travellers to perform Satagêra's masterpiece, and for you to complete your mission and learn how the plans of Ordâkh and the Thardúth intertwine.Asharmûl is in south-east Ahâba.Talk to Asharmûl in south-east AhâbaThe time has come for you to go with Asharmûl and the rest of Têrkar's Travellers into the guarded fastness of Emax Dûl.Talk to Asharmûl and go with Têrkar's Travellers into Emax DûlInkanûsh must be told what you have discovered.Talk to Inkanûsh on the island in the centre of the oasis of Ahâba'I am as ready as ever I will be. Let us find Asharmûl and tell him we are prepared to go.''Let us find Asharmûl and tell him we are prepared to go.''You are ready, then? Once we depart for Emax Dûl there will be no turning back. Let me know if you are certain.''It is a good plan. Remain calm and confident, and all will be well.''What did you learn? Quickly, now!'

Inkanûsh listens closely as you describe what you learned about Sûg Nidar, the Fearwater, and the long creation of the Song of Waves and Wind. The Wizard strokes his beard, deep in thought.

'I should have known, ${PLAYER}. When the Din of Stone shook the Mûr, it collapsed a great cavern to the east of Ikorbân. The river rushed into the chasm, creating a vast lake that showed on no map. After Thorongil destroyed the Corsair fleet at Umbar, Sauron demanded a new fleet be built; the Fearwater proved the perfect location. Had he not loosed his war too soon, and waited for the Song to be complete, things might have turned out differently. That the fleet was kept secret from all may have prevented the Masters of Mordor from attempting to harness it against us.'

Inkanûsh tilts his head to one side, thinking.

'Secret from all, did I say? No. Azagath knew of this fleet, this Song of Waves and Wind, and so did Nakási Wave-reaper. This may be a problem.'With dangers looming in two directions, the time has come to make a difficult choice: some must remain in Idagâl to oppose the Thardúth, and some must return north to combat this last evil of Sauron.Your companions assemble in Ahâba to discuss these fresh dangers.Talk to Glorfindel in AhâbaTalk to Ulishár in AhâbaYour companions assemble in Ahâba to discuss these fresh dangers.Talk to Rahâzi in AhâbaAccording to what you learned in Emax Dûl, supply-waggons for Sûg Nidar depart from Khanâg Valoyar, south of Jiret-menêsh, in An Shêru.Return to Khanâg Valoyar in An ShêruRahâzi is at Khanâg Valoyar in An Shêru.Talk to Rahâzi in Khanâg ValoyarRahâzi is at Khanâg Valoyar, in An Shêru. She has identified the next waggon to depart, and is prepared to sneak aboard it with you.Talk to Rahâzi in Khanâg Valoyar and sneak aboard the next waggon to depart<rgb=#00FF00>You can return to the Fearwater by using the banner at Khanâg Valoyar, in An Shêru.</rgb>

Rahâzi is outside the waggon that brought the two of you to Sûg Nidar, the Fearwater.Talk to Rahâzi outside the waggon that brought you to Sûg Nidar'From your account it sounds like the Song is nearing completion, but is not yet ready to sail. We are fortunate. If goods and supplies travel by waggon from Khanâg Valoyar to Sûg Nidar, one or two secret stowaways might gain access to the harbour where the Song is being constructed.

'Can I count upon you to do this, ${PLAYER}? You must prevent the Song from setting sail. I will remain here to combat the Thardúth, but I do not ask you to travel alone. Any who seek to go with you on this journey into danger will have my approval.''You must prevent the Song from setting sail. I will remain here to combat the Thardúth, but I do not ask you to travel alone. Any who seek to go with you on this journey into danger will have my approval.''I must remain with Inkanûsh to face the Thardúth of Damudûr, if it comes to that. But fear not, my friend: I know you have the courage and the skill to end the threat posed by the Song of Waves and Wind.

'I will see you again when you return in victory!''If you ask me to go with you I will, ${PLAYER}, but my heart tells me to remain with Têrkar's Travellers while they stay in this land. I owe them a debt of friendship for all they have done, and I want to stay and fight the Thardúth, if Inkanûsh asks it of me.

'My heart has also not stopped racing since we passed within Emax Dûl! I am not sure it could handle another mission of secrecy so soon after the last!''You must prevent the Song from setting sail. I will remain here to combat the Thardúth, but I do not ask you to travel alone. Any who seek to go with you on this journey into danger will have my approval.''I will accompany you on this journey, ${PLAYER}. Such a fleet as you describe threatens my home and my people, and no daughter of Iridír could let this danger remain unmet and unopposed!

'Supplies depart for Sûg Nidar from the khanâg south of Jiret-menêsh, Khanâg Valoyar? That is where you encountered that impolite Temámir, is it not? If we are fortunate, he may unwittingly drive us to the harbour where the Song of Waves and Wind is under construction!

'To Khanâg Valoyar, then, and without delay!''To Khanâg Valoyar, then, and without delay!'Rahâzi stands here.'Luck is with us. According to these papers, a waggon is soon to depart for Sûg Nidar. Are you ready to go? It will be some time before we return to these lands!

'Let me know if you are prepared!''Then we must quickly conceal ourselves within the departing waggon!''We made it. This is Sûg Nidar, the Fearwater.'With regret, Ulishár has bid farewell to Têrkar's Travellers so you can continue your urgent errand: scouting the approaches to the Ordâkhai-held Emax Dûl.The approach to Emax Dûl must be scouted for some weakness in the defences.Scout the approach to Emax Dûl above the cliffsideStudy the defences of Emax Dûl from the low wall by the cliffsideThe approach to the main entrance of Emax Dûl must be scouted for some weakness in the defences.Scout the approach to the main entrance to Emax DûlStudy the main entrance of Emax Dûl from the low wall atop the hillGlorfindel has also come to scout the defences of Emax Dûl.Talk to Glorfindel outside Emax DûlGlorfindel fears that fighting your way in to Emax Dûl may be the only option available to you, but Inkanûsh in north-east Ahâba will not like that answer.Talk to Inkanûsh in north-east AhâbaMashkânu in north-east Ahâba has an idea.Talk to Mashkânu in north-east AhâbaZimêlkha is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Zimêlkha in north-east AhâbaJêsamin in north-east Ahâba knows what needs to be done.Talk to Jêsamin in north-east AhâbaUlishár is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Ulishár in north-east Ahâba'We need to scout the approach to Emax Dûl and learn if there is a way past the sentries. Perhaps there is an unguarded location to the left or right of the main entrance that will allow you to scale one of the walls?''We need to scout the approach to Emax Dûl and learn if there is a way past the sentries. Perhaps there is an unguarded location to the left or right of the main entrance that will allow you to scale one of the walls?''We need to scout the approach to Emax Dûl and learn if there is a way past the sentries. Perhaps there is an unguarded location to the left or right of the main entrance that will allow you to scale one of the walls?''The city is guarded, and I see little chance of sneaking in past the sentries.

'We may need to fight our way inside. Let us return to Inkanûsh and tell him what we have learned.''We may need to fight our way inside. Let us return to Inkanûsh and tell him what we have learned.''Glorfindel suggests that you fight your way in to Emax Dûl? Has he taken leave of his senses? Such an attack will raise the alarm through the entire city, and we will learn the plans of neither Thardúth nor Ordâkhai!

'Always an Elf-lord suggests the direct approach. It will not serve us here!''The direct approach will not serve us here!'Mashkânu looks around at his companions before meeting the eyes of the Wizard that once frightened him so.

'I have had an idea, Inkanûsh.

'What if we sneak ${PLAYER} into the city the same way we snuck out Ulishár? When we go to perform Satagêra's new play, ${PLAYER} could be hidden among the costumes, beneath a hooded cloak.''We were able to sneak Ulishár out of the city... why not do the same, but in the other direction?''That will not work this time, Mashkânu. The guards of the city seem much more vigilant now than before. They will search our things, and if ${PLAYER} is hidden among them, our secret passenger will be discovered.''The guards of the city seem much more vigilant now than before.''I have it!'

Jêsamin quivers with excitement.

'If our friend has a part to play in the performance, the guards will have to let ${PLAYER} in!'

Jêsamin beams at you excitedly.

'After you have played your part, you can then sneak away to search the keep. Once you find whatever it is Inkanûsh seeks within those walls, you can return to us and we will all leave together!''After you have played your part, you can then sneak away to search the keep. Once you find whatever it is Inkanûsh seeks within those walls, you can return to us and we will all leave together!''It is not enough. ${PLAYER} does not know the interior of the keep.' 

Ulishár takes a deep breath before he continues, and when he speaks his voice is low and quiet.

'Someone must accompany you who knows the layout of the keep, someone who has been there before. I need to go with you. Otherwise you will not be able to search the keep before the performance ends.'Têrkar's Travellers are willing to help you gain access to the inner keep of the guarded city of Emax Dûl, but the plan is not without its dangers.Satagêra is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Satagêra in north-east AhâbaAtâlsi is outside the western entrance of Ahâba.Talk to Atâlsi outside the western entrance to AhâbaShâdugan Gânda, the keeper of the mûmakil, is at the Scardelve, south of Ahâba.You have only known Têrkar's Travellers for a short time, but you are reasonably certain they would want you to <rgb=#00FF00>point</rgb> at the largest of the mûmakil.<rgb=#00FF00>Point</rgb> at the mûmak you want from the ScardelveShâdugan Gânda, the keeper of the mûmakil, is at the Scardelve, south of Ahâba.Talk to Shâdugan Gânda at the ScardelveSatagêra is in north-east Ahâba.Talk to Satagêra in north-east AhâbaSatagêra has given you some fresh pages of her script to read.Read the new portion of the script Satagêra gave to youSatagêra is in north-east Ahâba, waiting for your response to her script.Inkanûsh has gone to south-east Ahâba to think about the plan.Find Inkanûsh in south-east AhâbaInkanûsh stands in south-east Ahâba, deep in thought.Talk to Inkanûsh in south-east AhâbaMashkânu is in south-east Ahâba.Talk to Mashkânu in south-east AhâbaUlishár is in south-east Ahâba.Talk to Ulishár in south-east Ahâba'The interior of the keep of Emax Dûl is a confusing maze of stairs and hallways, but I have not forgotten its layout. How could those passages leave my memory? During the years of my captivity I would be punished if the food I brought from the kitchen to the chambers of the lords and ladies grew cold while finding my way. I can navigate the maze for you, and bring you directly to the inner sanctum of whoever now rules in Emax Dûl.

'This is why I have come with you, my friend. I knew it not when we departed Sul Madásh, but I know it now. This certainty gives me the courage to return to that place. Worry not for me, ${PLAYER}.

'I will be fine, and you will learn whatever it is that Inkanûsh believes Ordâkh is hiding within the keep.''This is why I have come with you, my friend. I knew it not when we departed Sul Madásh, but I know it now. This certainty gives me the courage to return to that place. Worry not for me.

'I will be fine, and you will learn whatever it is that Inkanûsh believes Ordâkh is hiding within the keep.''It will take me some time to revise the play to include you and Ulishár, ${PLAYER}. If I can do this in such a short time perhaps this will be my greatest masterpiece, after all... despite the lies of Ordâkh contained within!

'I jest, but as we travelled from Zajâna with Inkanûsh he spoke of the terrible ancient creatures he opposes. If your mission proves instrumental in defeating these villains, these Thardúth, the play I now write must be considered my masterpiece, in truth! For it also to undermine the goals of those who sent my husband and my friends to their deaths in war... I admit that sweetens the sweet-cake.

'While I work to include you and Ulishár in the play, you can help me by aiding Atâlsi. She is arranging some of the things we will need for the play. I already intended it to be full of spectacle, but now it is doubly-important the attention of the Ordâkhai be held while you and the Kinta slip away. If Atâlsi proves stubborn, tell her that your life and Ulishár's life might depend upon the size of the creature....''If Atâlsi proves stubborn, tell her that your life and Ulishár's life might depend upon the size of the creature....''We need a mûmak. Don't ask.'

You tell Atâlsi that Satagêra has requested you help her attain the things that will be needed for the performance, and the playwright is hoping for as large a spectacle as possible.

'I admit I am not surprised. It is most easy for Satagêra to write these things into her plays, but who must then arrange for them to be found? Atâlsi and Mashkânu, most of the time!

'I have already attained a pass from the Ordâkhai allowing us to use one of their mûmakil for our purpose. I will give you the pass and you can show it to Shâdugan Gânda, the mûmak-keeper. Find him at the Scardelve, south of Ahâba. I would have selected a small one, but knowing Satagêra she will want the biggest we can get!''I gave you the pass already. Show it to Shâdugan Gânda, the mûmak-keeper at the Scardelve, south of Ahâba.''What do you want? Speak quickly or I will summon the guards.'

You hand Shâdugan Gânda the pass and he breaks the wax seal upon it. Sweat breaks out upon his brow as he reads the contents. At last he looks up, a relieved smile upon his face.

'I saw many of these notices at the start of the year, stranger. So many of my mûmakil were outfit with harness and armour and sent north to the war. Only the smallest were deemed unfit for battle; they were left behind to haul stone and timber. I am glad the mûmak you seek will not be asked to fight!

'<rgb=#00FF00>Point</rgb> out which of my creatures you would like to join these travelling players for their performance before the Lords of Emax Dûl.''I am glad the mûmak you seek will not be asked to fight! <rgb=#00FF00>Point</rgb> out which of my creatures you would like to join these travelling players for their performance before the Lords of Emax Dûl.''I will see what I can do. You have chosen one of the more obstinate creatures of this herd!

'But your travelling players will have a mûmak when they need one. I hope their performance will show to the Lords of Emax Dûl the value of raising mûmakil even in time of peace, and the quality of their minders!''Your travelling players will have a mûmak when they need one. I hope their performance will show to the Lords of Emax Dûl the value of raising mûmakil even in time of peace, and the quality of their minders!''The writing goes slowly, but I have incorporated both you and Ulishár into an early scene. After the two of you have been seen by the audience, it will then be your task to slip away and search the keep while we hold their attention upon us. The mûmak I asked Atâlsi to get for us should prove useful!

'Here, ${PLAYER}. Your part is labelled "Hero of Gondor," and Ulishár will be "Northern Elf." Read this portion of the script and commit it to your memory. I have already shown it to Ulishár. He insists he will have no trouble with his own part, and yours should be easy enough. The difficult task will begin after the two of you slip away, of course.''Read the portion of the script I gave to you and commit it to your memory. I have already shown it to Ulishár. He insists he will have no trouble with his own part, and yours should be easy enough. The difficult task will begin after the two of you slip away, of course.''I hope you suffer not from the fright of being on stage, ${PLAYER}. I understand you have faced some terrible foes and engaged in many a difficult battle... but the act of performing before an audience can be daunting in its own right. Mashkânu may give you some advice; he has done both.

'But that reminds me. Inkanûsh wanted to speak to you before the performance. I last saw him to the south-east.''Inkanûsh wanted to speak to you before the performance. I last saw him in south-east Ahâba.'Inkanûsh stands here.'Yes, I am worried. This plan will get you inside the keep of Emax Dûl, but its true success depends upon something else: whether the attention of the Ordâkhai and of the Thardúth are sufficiently held by the performance. You and Ulishár need time to search the inner sanctum for the plans of the Thardúth and the Ordâkhai.

'But secrecy is our ally, ${PLAYER}, and lends us the advantage. The Thardúth that departed Damudûr and accompanied the Ordâkhai to Emax Dûl will expect no such intrusion. Glorfindel and I cannot join you, for our presence would risk drawing their attention. If they remain unalerted, I believe we can learn where next the Thardúth intend to send their Utûgi. I suspect they will desire to destroy Gondor and the kingdoms of the north... but there is another possibility.

'The Hûl of Ordâkh and his sons fell during the War of the Ring, but no empire can remain leaderless. If a new Hûl has come to power in the south, he or she may have much to fear from these newly-awakened creatures of evil. They served Morgoth. They will serve no other, and certainly not a Hûl of Ordâkh.

'This is the sort of thing I need you and Ulishár to learn.''The Hûl of Ordâkh and his sons fell during the War of the Ring, but no empire can remain leaderless. If a new Hûl has come to power in the south, he or she may have much to fear from these newly-awakened creatures of evil. They served Morgoth. They will serve no other, and certainly not a Hûl of Ordâkh.

'This is the sort of thing I need you and Ulishár to learn.''Satagêra wanted me to give you some advice about performing before an audience? Such a thing is difficult to learn in a single lesson! You must demonstrate confidence, ${PLAYER}. An audience can tell if you believe what you are showing to them.'

Mashkânu laughs humourlessly.

'With this particular play that may be difficult for all of us. The lords and ladies of Emax Dûl want us to convince their people that the victory of Ordâkh is near at hand... but any of us who were there know otherwise. Some of us may still be there, lost in the north, like Urûnam or my beloved Khazín! And others of my friends are known to be slain, cut down in the forest like Têrkar, or trampled before the great city like Zurûsh.

'Had we not a purpose to accomplish here, I might have taken advantage of this moment to slay at least one of these lords of Emax Dûl, ${PLAYER}. For months I blamed the Wizard "Mithrandir," who is Inkanûsh, for the death of my friends and my family... but it was Ordâkh and Barkhûsh who sent us to that war. Barkhûsh is gone, but Ordâkh remains. If you can learn something that will hurt them, I will not rue the staying of my hand.'Mashkânu crosses his arms.

'The nobles who reside within Emax Dûl know the play they have asked Satagêra to write is full of falsehoods, just as we do. Which of us will first admit it? That is the game, ${PLAYER}.''I have been remembering the time of my captivity, ${PLAYER}. For two years I laboured in the mines, but as my health worsened the overseers came to realize I could not survive much longer beneath the earth. In the early days they delighted to send a Kinta belowground, but as my speed suffered they decided my slow toil was not worth the jest. They sent me to Emax Dûl to serve the nobles who dwelt there, lords and ladies unaccustomed to wielding weapons; there was no call for them to march north with the Hûl when the Black Serpent departed for the war Barkhûsh commanded.

'The work was lighter than in the mines, but my health remained poor, and the punishments I endured were just as severe. Had I not encountered Têrkar that night, I am certain I would have died within the keep of Emax Dûl.

'And now I return. If we learn something to undermine the plans of Ordâkh and the Thardúth who slew my brother, it will be worth it. This will truly be Satagêra's masterpiece, ${PLAYER}.'You and Rahâzi have arrived at Sûg Nidar, the Fearwater, and must now learn of some way to prevent the Song of Waves and Wind from setting sail.

