'The battle is almost won!''You have returned victorious! All that is left is to defeat Góramul Sai in the palace and Emax Dûl will be ours!

'We could not have done this without your help, ${PLAYER}. The people of Hamât Renewed... no, the people of all Shagâna will tell tales of your heroism for years to come!'The time has come to find the source of the blight within Adagím with the Rangers Faeron and Rhadrog.Faeron and Rhadrog have traveled to Adagím to investigate the threat that may be inside the abandoned wonder.

You should travel to the Folly of Nagakhêdi and talk to Faeron.Meet Faeron outside the Folly of NagakhêdiFaeron, Rhadrog, and the Kinta Irsinith will venture into the ancient citadel in Adagím to search for the source of the blight.

You should use the Instance Finder to complete The Folly of Nagakhêdi raid on any difficulty.Complete The Folly of Nagakhêdi raid on any difficultyYou have thwarted the plans of Lhaereth the Stained and prevented her from unleashing an ancient Balrog on Mûr Ghala.

You should talk to Faeron.Talk to Faeron'Rhadrog and I have prepared, and are ready to investigate the ancient citadel. We know not what awaits us inside, but caution merits we enter with the strength to defeat the powerful foe we fear may be lurking inside.

'Gather your fellows and travel to the Folly of Nagakhêdi in Adagím. We will inform the Kintai of our intentions and meet you there.''This is it. A grand entrance to what was supposed to be a grand work of Men. We will see how near it came to completion in its interior. I am surprised Viznak did not want to accompany us, but then again, I believe he would rather avoid crossing paths with the Lady of Blight a third time....

'The Kintai were alarmed to hear we intended to enter, and made sure one of their own was among our number. The warden Irsinith volunteered to accompany us. I am glad for their expertise in fighting this particular corruption.

'When you and your fellows are ready, we will enter. Let us hope our search for the source of the blight is nearly at its end.''We have won the day, ${PLAYER}! The Lady of Blight flees, and her 'grand prize' lies slain! Without the aid of you and your fellows, we would surely have met our ends in this pit. We owe you our lives, and the world owes you a debt of gratitude, yet again.

'Take this time to rest and celebrate, and leave tracking Lhaereth to us. She will surely seek out the source of the blight, but not until after she licks her wounds and schemes in secret. We will seek you out when she makes her next move.

'Until then, thank you again, ${PLAYER}.'For over a hundred years, the Kintai Elves have battled a mysterious blight, seemingly brought on by the Din of Stone. It has already consumed once-beautiful Adagím, and now threatens the rest of the Elven glades. An investigation by the Rangers Faeron and Rhadrog has tracked the source of the blight to The Folly of Nagakhêdi, but what dangers lurk inside the ancient and abandoned wonder?For over a hundred years, the Kintai Elves have battled a mysterious blight, seemingly brought on by the Din of Stone. It has already consumed once-beautiful Adagím, and now threatens the rest of the Elven glades. An investigation by the Rangers Faeron and Rhadrog has tracked the source of the blight to The Folly of Nagakhêdi, but what dangers lurk inside the ancient and abandoned wonder?Be among the first to complete The Folly of Nagakhêdi -- Tier 2For over a hundred years, the Kintai Elves have battled a mysterious blight, seemingly brought on by the Din of Stone. It has already consumed once-beautiful Adagím, and now threatens the rest of the Elven glades. An investigation by the Rangers Faeron and Rhadrog has tracked the source of the blight to The Folly of Nagakhêdi, but what dangers lurk inside the ancient and abandoned wonder?For over a hundred years, the Kintai Elves have battled a mysterious blight, seemingly brought on by the Din of Stone. It has already consumed once-beautiful Adagím, and now threatens the rest of the Elven glades. An investigation by the Rangers Faeron and Rhadrog has tracked the source of the blight to The Folly of Nagakhêdi, but what dangers lurk inside the ancient and abandoned wonder?Be among the first to complete The Folly of Nagakhêdi -- Tier 3Málacál has asked that you travel to the east of Sul Madásh and help her and her hunters thin the number of blighted Huorns that can be found.Málacál has asked that you travel to the east of Sul Madásh and help her and her hunters thin the number of blighted Huorns that can be found.Defeat Huorns east of Sul Madásh (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return to Málacál near Sul Madásh and report your success of defeating Huorns in eastern Adagím.Return to Málacál'The Pale Blight here continues to corrupt and twist the trees. The Huorns are becoming more of a problem, so they are targets of our hunts.

'To the east they have become consumed with fungal growths. Be wary during your travels there. Be sure to defeat them should you encounter them.

'The hunter's guild would be grateful.''Welcome back, ${CLASS}.

'Other hunters speak of your success in defeating Huorns found in the east! Well done.

'You have made the guild proud with your efforts. If you wish to hunt more of them, return when you have the desire.'The people of Mûr Ghala continue to call for aid and offer rewards to those who assist them to defend and maintain the freedom of their home.Throughout the broken realm, your efforts kindle a flicker of hope. In recognition of such valour, you will receive honours and rewards worthy of your pursuits.Earn Mûr Ghala commendations by completing World Quests (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Throughout the broken realm, your efforts kindle a flicker of hope. In recognition of such valour, you are granted honours and rewards worthy of your pursuits.CompletedThroughout the shadowed lands of Mûr Ghala, discord dins and tyrants clamour to return to power. Stand with the free-people who dare rebel, and rebuild this land. Let honour guide you and earn rewards for your efforts.You have done much to ensure the continued restoration of Mûr Ghala and receive rewards in recognition of your efforts.You have defeated Kishâsu the Knowing when he is aided by Takâltu the Weaponmaster on Tier 3.Defeat Kishâsu the Knowing when he is aided by Takâltu the Weaponmaster on Tier 3.Defeat Kishâsu the Knowing when he is aided by Takâltu the Weaponmaster on Tier 3.Many of the Subuzûri armour and weapons are worse for wear - help the Subuzûri polish and repair them!Speak with Numûru in Ingarûma. Ingarûma is located south of Hanamíku, high on a hill-top overlooking Imizíla.Speak with Numûru in IngarûmaCollect whetstones and defeat Hatokáli serpents and strikers to collect their oils, to polish and repair Subuzûri armour.Collect polishing oil from serpents (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect sharpening whetstones north of Ingarûma (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Speak with Numûru in Ingarûma. Ingarûma is located south of Hanamíku, high on a hill-top overlooking Imizíla.Speak with Numûru in Ingarûma'Our scouts have confirmed it - Bârshud dwells within Imizíla. We must ready for battle.

'Please, speak with Numûru - he knows what is still required of us before our Subuzûri are adequately prepared.''Ha! This is brilliant, my friend. They will be blinded by the shine of our armour in the noon-day sun.''Our blades are dull and our armour rusts in these damp environs. The swords of the Subuzûri could barely cut a dull rope right now, let alone slice through Ordâkhai armour.

'We need sharpening whetstones - there are stones which would do the job well just north of here. For our armour, the serpents that dwell here possess a gland that we can use to polish and protect the armour. They will not give it willingly, however.

'You are one of our greatest warriors - I know you will be able to obtain this oil.'The locals of Hanamíku have been grumbling about the presence of Mizâdi's army. Help them in some necessary tasks to build goodwill and show them Mizâdi means them no harm.Speak with the merchant Shâgigal Held-silver about his grievances with Mizâdi's army. Shâgigal can be found within Hanamíku near the market stalls.Speak with the merchant Shâgigal Held-silver about his grievancesCollect oranges and wood from deadfall to rebuild Shagigal's stalls and provide him with new fruit stock. The farmland of Kirzâmur should have some of both, south of Hanamíku.Collect oranges in Kirzâmur (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Collect deadfall wood in Kirzâmur (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Build new market stalls for Shâgigal in Hanamíku.Destroy Shâgigal's burnt market stalls in Hanamíku.Destroy Shâgigal's burnt market stalls in Hanamíku (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Speak with Shâgigal Held-silver now that the market stalls have been rebuilt and his fruit stock resupplied.Speak with Shâgigal Held-silver in HanamíkuSpeak with Shâra Mizâdi in Hanamíku. Hanamíku can be found in Pahar Hatokáli, within Mûr Ghala.Speak with Shâra Mizâdi in Hanamíku'The Ordâkhai have done much harm to the people here. We should be welcomed. But I am not blind, friend - my presence here is tolerated, not celebrated.

'There is a merchant - I believe the locals call him Shâgigal. He is the source of much of the disquiet here in Hanamíku. Please, speak with him. See if there is anything we can do to bring him on our side. Our mission cannot be compromised - but I have no wish to quarrel with the villagers whilst here.''We will show them our good intentions through our actions, not merely our words. They have been taught not to trust. A painful lesson that they have paid in blood.

'It sounds like this merchant was quite difficult to work with. Thank you for handling the situation so artfully - I am once more in your debt.''You! I saw you speaking with Mizâdi - surely you are important!

'What, you aren't fleeing my sight? Excellent, excellent.

'What do I want? My business returned to me, of course! I sell oranges - only the finest in all of the Fells! But you can see, the Ordâkhai burned my stalls and stole my oranges - and no one has provided recompense or made me whole!

'It is Mizâdi's fault the Ordakhai fled through Hanamiku, she should be the one to repair my stalls and bring a fresh supply of oranges.

'You will help? Well, it is about time someone listened to Shâgigal.''Better? Yes, I suppose it is better. But you did not have to destroy the old ones! You looked to be stomping around like a great alap-nâri!

'Never mind, never mind. Yes, I suppose Mizâdi has done more for me than the Ordâkhai, so her presence is not altogether terrible here in Hanamíku.

'What would really seal the deal is for you to purchase some of my oranges.

'What do you mean you just gave them to me? That doesn't mean I can't sell you some of the finest fruits and vegetables in the Fells! Wait, come back!'Talk to MeldirDefeat Khûrz-hai at Balthlún (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Mizâdi's soldiers have been bothered by the presence of the village children, but now some of them worry about their absence.Gurûni worries that the children that were spying upon her might have departed not for the village but for the kergrim-haunted caves to the south-east of Bolo Thúki.Find the nearest entrance to Mut-hûru, south-east of Bolo ThúkiThe kergrim that stalk Mut-hûru should be defeated as you search for the village children.Defeat kergrim in Mut-hûru (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Search within Mut-hûru for the village childrenGuardsman Khârdab is in the village of Hanamíku.Report to Khârdab in the village of HanamíkuZíri, one of the village children, has a story to tell.Talk to Zíri in the village of HanamíkuPasumín has left his customary seat. Where has he gone?Check to see if Pasumín is nearbyZíri may have more to say concerning the disappearance of the children in Hanamíku.Talk to Zíri in the village of HanamíkuGuardsman Khârdab is in the village of Hanamíku.Talk to Khârdab in the village of Hanamíku'When I was a young girl, I played not with toys. Instead, I preferred to explore the hills near my home. More often than not I stumbled upon a great deal of trouble, and I was lucky to return home before nightfall with only skinned knees to show for my adventure. Might these children be of like mind as me when I was their age?

'I have heard tell of a cave system and tomb complex to the south-east called Mut-hûru where the vile scavengers known as kergrim dwell. If the children steered clear of the Ordâkhai, might they have gone in search of excitement elsewhere? Such a network of caves as Mut-hûru would have lured me there as a girl, and it could have done the same to these children.

'Please find out, ${PLAYER}, and defeat some of the kergrim while you are there! They are unpleasant creatures, no matter where you find them.''Please search Mut-hûru for the children of the village, and defeat some of the kergrim while you are there! They are unpleasant creatures, no matter where you find them.''Please search Mut-hûru for the children of the village, and defeat some of the kergrim while you are there! They are unpleasant creatures, no matter where you find them.''Perhaps the children went home after all? Let us hope so, ${PLAYER}!''Zíri has told me an upsetting tale, ${PLAYER}. Her friends may have done something most unwise, and I fear they may now be in great danger. I was just about to set out in search of them, but before I depart you should hear the tale from her.''Zíri's friends may have done something most unwise, and I fear they may now be in great danger. I was just about to set out in search of them, but before I depart you should hear the tale from her.'Breathlessly, her words tumbling out in a rush, the girl does not seem to mind repeating the story.

'It was Tebût's idea, but he would not have done it if Ikâru had not kept calling him craven! And then Sekhûr joined in, and before you know it we were well up the hill and nearly at the Ordâkhai camp. We thought we would sneak in, but the Hamâti soldier was right down the hill and I thought she was looking at us.

'We heard one of the Ordâkhai ask about lion tamers at another camp to the south, and then Ikâru insisted that if Tebût was so brave he would have no problem picking a tuft of mane from one of the lions. After all, if the creatures let the Ordâkhai approach, they must be safe. At least, that was the idea.

'So my friends left the nearby camp and headed for the further one where the lions were being kept, but I came right home because I knew it was wrong! Are they going to be in trouble? It was Tebût's idea, but he would not have done it if it wasn't for Ikâru and Sekhûr goading him on. I told them not to, honestly!'Zíri is focused on what really matters to her.

'Are my friends going to be in trouble? It was Tebût's idea, but he would not have done it if it wasn't for Ikâru and Sekhûr goading him on. I told them not to, honestly!'Zíri stares at you with confusion.

'Have I seen Pasumín? The toy-maker? No, I haven't, not lately. What has he got to do with any of this?'Zíri stares at you with confusion.

'Have I seen Pasumín? The toy-maker? No, I haven't, not lately. What has he got to do with any of this?''There is no time to waste, ${PLAYER}. I fear there are a number of lives at stake.'Shenzâmin has come to the troubling conclusion that the soldier Arimûl may not be one of Mizâdi's warriors at all.Shenzâmin thinks you should dispose of the Ordâkhai trinkets in the barrel beside the main tent of the war camp, to the north-west.Shenzâmin stands beside the main tent of the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to Shenzâmin in the war camp of IngarûmaShenzâmin asked you to defeat Ordâkhai throughout Pahar Hatokáli and to remain alert for the man who pretended to be Arimûl.Defeat Ordâkhai throughout Pahar Hatokáli (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Shenzâmin stands beside the main tent of the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to Shenzâmin in the war camp of IngarûmaThe man pretending to be Arimûl was espied by one of the scouts, who pursued him west through the gate of the war camp!Pursue the fleeing man west through the gate of the war campZilûki observes the work of her bow outside the west gate of the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to Zilûki outside the west gate of the war camp of IngarûmaThe man who pretended to be Arimûl lies injured outside the west gate of the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to He Who Was Arimûl outside the west gate of the war camp of IngarûmaShenzâmin stands outside the west gate of the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to Shenzâmin outside the west gate of the war camp of IngarûmaChief Provisioner Dunaïl stands by his tent in the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to Chief Provisioner Dunaïl in the war camp of Ingarûma'I begin to think this man pretending to be Arimûl may not be one of Shâra Mizâdi's soldiers at all, but I cannot determine why he would send you after the Ordâkhai. Were there any valuables among the trinkets you collected from the kergrim who slew them? Could it be he was looking for something specific that the Ordâkhai might have on their persons?'

You sift once again through the Ordâkhai trinkets but find nothing of apparent value.

'You should dispose of those in a barrel nearby, and I will assign someone to keep watch in the event he comes looking for them anyway. I think he will not... but you never know.''You should dispose of those trinkets in a barrel nearby, and I will assign someone to keep watch in the event he comes looking for them anyway. I think he will not... but you never know.''Now we wait.

'In the meantime, you should do battle with Ordâkhai soldiers. I begin to suspect two things: first, that our imposter may be a follower of Bârshud, and second, that he was never truly injured at all. If you keep your eyes peeled you may recognize him on the field of battle, and solve this mystery once and for all.''You should do battle with Ordâkhai soldiers. I begin to suspect two things: first, that our imposter may be a follower of Bârshud, and second, that he was never truly injured at all. If you keep your eyes peeled you may recognize him on the field of battle, and solve this mystery once and for all.''You have returned just in time, ${PLAYER}! Our trap has caught its prey!

'The imposter was seen searching through the barrel where you disposed of the Ordâkhai trinkets, and my scout pursued him through the western gate!

'Quickly now! After him!''The imposter was seen searching through the barrel where you disposed of the Ordâkhai trinkets, and my scout pursued him through the western gate!

'Quickly now! After him!''The pain... the pain!''Shenzâmin alerted me to this imposter, ${PLAYER}. When I saw him rooting through the refuse, I knew he was the man pretending to be one of our soldiers. I called for him to surrender, but he chose to flee instead.

'My arrow pierced his leg and stayed his flight. I could have ended his life, but now he lives to give us his explanation.''My arrow pierced his leg and stayed his flight. I could have ended his life, but now he lives to give us his explanation.''A pox on you, stranger! And on all Hamâti!'

You ask the man if Zilûki's arrow pierced the leg he pretended was injured, and he cries out in pain.

'I bore you no ill will, stranger! Yes, I am from Ordâkh, and I once served Bârshud. My true name is Mohóto. When Mizâdi's army caught up to us I knew we were beaten; I deserted from the ranks of the Ordâkhai. I should have fled for home, but as I sought to make up my mind I encountered a man who claimed there was treasure in the tombs and caves of Mut-hûru. I convinced some of my fellows to seek it out, but we ran afoul of the kergrim and only I escaped. I needed to know if any of them survived, or if they found the treasure, so I hatched this plan: I would pretend to be a Hamâti soldier and send one of you to find out! But alas, it seems they all perished before finding it. Was there ever any treasure at all? It seems the man lied to me.'

You ask what Mohóto can remember of the man who told him about the treasure.

'Almost nothing. Isn't that odd? His face is so clear in my memory, but when I try to describe it the details fade. But it was a fair face, a beautiful face. That I can remember.

'Oh, the pain in my leg is unbearable! A pox on Hamât, and a pox on Mizâdi!''The pain in my leg is unbearable! A pox on Hamât, and a pox on Mizâdi!''Mohóto will survive, but he will be imprisoned until Shâra Mizâdi decides what is to be done with him.

'You should consult with Chief Provisioner Dunaïl and see if he has anything that might be used to treat an arrow-wound.''You should consult with Chief Provisioner Dunaïl and see if he has anything that might be used to treat an arrow-wound.'You describe the circumstances that resulted in a man of Ordâkh requiring treatment for an arrow-wound, and Dunaïl smiles grimly.

'I will see what I can find, ${PLAYER}. Others might find some justice in this man receiving an honest wound in the leg he once claimed a dishonest injury... but that is not the way of a provisioner, and certainly not a Chief Provisioner!

'Good day to you, my friend. He will suffer no further harm at our hands, unless Shâra Mizâdi commands it.'Though a teller of tales and a historian, Phardâli does not know where the Fool King's citadel may be. He suggested visiting Bêtam the Couthful of Jiret-menêsh for more details.Make your way to Jiret-menêsh and speak with Bêtam the Couthful. He, or one of his kin will surely have details on where the hidden citadel may be.Speak with Bêtam the Couth at Jiret-menêshBêtam explained that he will provide you a map for the price of collecting a prized smith's hammer from the resting place of Kojomedên in the Quelling-house. He warned that others fear to enter to the place due to horrors wrought there.Retrieve the hammer from Kojomedên's corpseYou claimed the hammer from the despoiled corpse of a once great dwarf artisan. Return Kojomedên's hammer to Bêtam the Couth at Jiret-menêsh. If he is an honourable dwarf, he will provide you with the information you seek.'Whispers among the prisoners you freed spoke of the Temámir opening their doors once again. If such words are true, then perhaps you may seek out Bêtam the Couthful. Should he, or any of his kin, yet walk this earth, they will know the lands where even they dare not tread. But be warned, for I have heard it said that dealing with the Temámir always comes at a price.'As you explain your quest, the dwarf exhales deeply, his voice thick with both weariness and resolve.

'There is a region of mountains in these lands where none of my kind will venture. I can provide you with a map, should you wish it, but it shall require time and a service in return. Grant me these, and I shall keep my word.'

He pauses, his gaze growing distant as if recalling some dark memory.

'The service I ask is this: you must find the resting place of Kojomedên, within the Quelling-house, and bring me his hammer. It is a prize long coveted by my kin, though, I confess, it is one we have feared to recover, given the dark fate that befell those who sought it before.
 
'The time collecting the hammer will grant me the time I need to craft your map, should you return to me.'The dwarf's eyes meet yours with a rare intensity, and his tone grows firmer.

'It is a treasure that would make me the envy of many, and you will have done me a service beyond measure. I will honour my part of the bargain, as I always do.'After completing Bêtam's task, he provides you with a map, his thanks, and a measure of advice.Bêtam provided you a map and directed you to compare it to the maps in the library of Zajâna. He warned that where the edges do not match, there shall you find the Fool King's citadel.'I am as true to my word as you are to yours. Here is your map. If what the tale-weaver told you is true. You should take it to the libraries of Zajâna and compare it to the maps of Men. Where the edges do not meet, there shall you find the fool's citadel. Seek out Valukhâri, she oversees the keeping of tithes at Zajâna. She will know more.

'Go now, I have something to forge.''Maps? We have many. Is there a specific map you wish to view?'All signs point to something bad happening at Mûl Erebu.Something bad seems to have happened at Mûl Erebu.Examine the unusual crate outside Mûl ErebuYou should explore Mûl Erebu, defeating scorpions and learning what happened here.Defeat scorpions throughout Mûl Erebu (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Examine an unusual crate near the large tent in Mûl ErebuExamine an unusual crate near the back of Mûl ErebuA soldier of Ordâkh has been slain near the back of Mûl Erebu.Examine the Ordâkhai corpse in the back of Mûl ErebuYou found a plan for the scorpions on the body of a slain Ordâkhai soldier.Read the Ordâkhai soldier's plan for the scorpionsZalûd the Maker of Plans at the war camp of Ingarûma will be very interested to learn what the Ordâkhai were up to in Mûl Erebu.The crate is empty, but from scratch marks on the inside it would appear someone was attempting to transport a number of creatures within it. Unless Mûl Erebu was their final destination, it seems something went wrong with the plan.'You have brought some of the Ordâkhai's secret plans? Well, hand them over! At last we will learn of their secret schemes and wily machinations!'

Zalûd reads the plans... and then he reads them again.

'Are you certain this is not a jest? This was their plan? I cannot decide if it is a brilliant stratagem or the work of a fool!'

Zalûd stifles a chortle.

'No, I have decided. Thank you for bringing me this, ${PLAYER}. It has raised my spirits and made me believe we may have victory in this place after all!'You have gathered many allies to Mizâdi's cause. Now it is time to plan the attack on Emax Dûl.You have secured an alliance with the former thralls of Esh Mêkag and the Servants of Aradûn.

You should return to Mizâdi in Ahâba.Return to AhâbaMizâdi is ready to plan the attack of Emax Dûl with her assembled allies.

You should talk to Mizâdi and complete 'Instance: The Plan of Attack'.Complete 'Instance: The Plan of Attack'You have fended off an attack on Ahâba and have, perhaps, met a new key ally.

You should talk to Mizâdi.Talk to Mizâdi'We have done what we sought to do here. I will return to Captain Hanâbu with our men, and tell him of your brave deeds! I am sure I will see you again soon, if Aradûn and Hamât Renewed are to join forces in our campaigns against Ordâkh.

'For now, you should return to Shâra Mizâdi. I am sure she will want to hear of your exploits in Esh Mêkag and at the Dawn-warden!''Thanks to your efforts and the words of Sorkamâl, we now have the Aradûni, the Beltáni, and the former Thralls of Esh Mêkag on our side. It is time to plan our attack on Emax Dûl.

'Our soldiers have begun to rally at Ahâba. Meet us here tonight. After everyone is settled in, we will devise our strategy with the leaders of our allied factions.''I cannot say I expected this. Can we trust her? Have we any choice?'Râsabur has worked up the courage to challenge Eshen-erêz, the Bone-queen.Râsabur needs your help defeating Eshen-erêz, the Bone-queen.

You should go to the centre of Ushumût, signal Râsabur by blowing the Aradûni Horn, and then defeat Eshen-erêz, the Bone-queen.Defeat Eshen-erêz, the Bone-queen(Optional) Use the Aradûni Horn to call for Râsabur and begin the fightYou have defeated the Bone-queen.

You should regroup with Râsabur near the rock where you first met.Talk to Râsabur'I have identified the leader of this drake nest. She is called Eshen-erêz, the Bone-queen, the largest of the wild-drake matrons. I watched her breath dissolve a man down to the bone.... I believe if we can defeat her, the rest of these drakes should scatter!

'Go to the centre of the nest, blow the horn to signal me, and we will challenge her together. May fortune smile on us. For Damakhêra Ardu!''We have done it, ${PLAYER}! Our actions have made Idagâl safer for our army as well as the people who live here.

'I will be heading on to the Dawn-warden to regroup with Captain Hanâbu. If you are heading that way, I hope to see you there. May we fight side-by-side again one day!'The time has come to bring the fight to the Ordâkhai's own doorstep.Mizâdi's allied forces are in place. You must now infiltrate the city with Razâl and open the gates to Emax Dûl.

Talk to Razâl at Mizâdi's hidden camp, Nashûbu, and complete 'Instance: The Siege of Emax Dûl'.Complete 'Instance: The Siege of Emax Dûl'Mizâdi's forces now have a foothold of control in Emax Dûl, but Góramul Sai and Bârshud have escaped.

You should talk to Mizâdi outside of Emax Dûl's palace, Ekal-nêbi.Talk to Mizâdi'Our plan is set in motion. Our forces are nearby, and await my order. I have chosen you and Razâl for the most crucial mission of our campaign.

'You are to enter the city through the secret cave system, find the gate-house, and open the gate to the city, allowing our waiting troops to flood in unhindered. We must work quickly, before Góramul Sai can learn of our plans and plug this hole in his defences.

'Kotráka Saiya has shown us the way. Victory now hinges upon you and Razâl opening the gates in time. I know you will not fail.''There's my infiltration partner! I assume Shâra Mizâdi has filled you in on the plan?

'Never has a mission held such importance. Are you ready?''Today's victory is bittersweet. We have a foothold of control in Emax Dûl, but until the prince is defeated, the Ordâkhai will not retreat from Idagâl.'

'We cannot stop to celebrate. We must find Góramul Sai! I swear, I will find Bârshud and deliver the justice he deserves, but it will have to wait for another day.'When the children of Hanamíku are endangered by soldiers of Ordâkh, even those who set aside their weapons must fight!Guardsman Khârdab is in the village of Hanamíku. He urgently wishes to go with you to rescue both the children and the toy-maker Pasumín.Talk to Khârdab and go with him to rescue the endangered folk of HanamíkuGuardsman Khârdab has returned to the village of Hanamíku, celebrating your successes but grieving your failures.Talk to Khârdab in HanamíkuShovels for digging the grave can be found by the forge-master in Hanamíku, and Khârdab suggested a few villagers who should be willing to help.Collect shovels near the forge-master (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Enlist the aid of DumâkhaEnlist the aid of ShêmuGuardsman Khârdab is waiting for you to return with shovels and willing folk to wield them.'Pasumín must have overheard Zíri telling me what happened to the children and set out in search of them.

'We have to reach the Ordâkhai encampment before he does, ${PLAYER}. Tell me when you are ready to go!''We must hurry. The lives of both children and toy-maker are at risk!''Our success is tinged by sorrow, ${PLAYER}. That we rescued the children from Nakársong is worthy of celebration, but the village will feel the loss of Pasumín, who fell in their defence. He was well-loved in Hanamíku by all who knew him.

'Can I ask you to collect shovels from the forge to the south-east, and to enlist a number of villagers to help with the digging of his grave? It is unhappy work, though it be necessary.''Can I ask you to collect shovels from the forge to the south-east, and to enlist a number of villagers to help with the digging of his grave? It is unhappy work, though it be necessary.''Yes, I will help dig Pasumín's grave. He was a dear man, and I will miss him.''Pasumín never had an ill word to say about anyone. I would be honoured to help with his final resting place.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. More folk than I expected have volunteered to help give Pasumín his rest. The physical toil will be less, though the toil of the spirit be heavy.'Lakhêpi the Far-faring has learned of an abandoned town that has been overrun by scorpions, and she wants to learn the details so she may incorporate them into future songs and tales.Lakhêpi the Far-faring wanted to meet up with you outside Mûl Erebu to the south.Meet up with Lakhêpi outside Mûl ErebuLakhêpi the Far-faring stands outside Mûl Erebu, listening for something.Talk to Lakhêpi outside Mûl ErebuLakhêpi asked you to collect stingers from the scorpions that occupy Mûl Erebu, and to stand near the back of the town and <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to the sound of the scorpions.Collect stingers from scorpions in Mûl Erebu (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Lakhêpi the Far-faring stands outside Mûl Erebu, waiting for you to return.'I have seen what I want to see here at Kakidûr, friend helper. Now it is time to move on.

'The folk of Hanamíku spoke of a village far to the south that has been abandoned by its people. Some say it is now overrun by scorpions. Scorpions!

'We should see for ourselves.''The folk of Hanamíku spoke of a village far to the south that has been abandoned by its people and given over to scorpions. We should see for ourselves!''Here we are.''It seems the rumours of this abandoned village were true, friend helper. This place has been given to the scorpions.

'It is not enough for a story to speak of events and happenings. It must tease all of your senses as well if it is to truly stick with you! Go ahead into Mûl Erebu and collect stingers from the scorpions so I can study them. I ask also that you press quite far into the village, and when you are in the very back, spend a moment to <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to the sounds you hear. I am curious about something.''I would like you to collect stingers from the scorpions in Mûl Erebu. I ask also that you press quite far into the village, and when you are in the very back, spend a moment to <rgb=#00FF00>listen</rgb> to the sounds you hear. I am curious about something.''What did you experience within Mûl Erebu? I am most interested to learn what sounds you heard as you advanced deeper within the abandoned village!'

You describe the unpleasant chittering and scampering sounds the scorpions made as they crawled upon the rocks, snapping their claws to ward off predators that drew too near, and Lakhêpi shivers even in the heat.

'Terrible... but perfect! Those are just the sort of skin-crawling details my stories need to capture the imaginations of their hearers. I will also examine the stingers you brought to me. The venom dripping from them seems quite potent!'Numûru has thrown a party to raise morale. He has encouraged you to sample the food and drink and make merry.Speak with Numûru in Hanamíku when you are ready.Speak with Numûru in Hanamíku when you are readySpeak with Numûru in Hanamíku.Speak with Numûru in Hanamíku'I have but one more ask of you, my friend - make merry! Try some of the local food... and the drink!''Thank you for all your help, my friend. It is a great weight lifted off me, to say everything inside of me to my queen.''The time has come to forget our troubles, friend, if but for a night.'The time has come for a sorrowful duty for the villagers of Hanamíku.Guardsman Khârdab is in Hanamíku, waiting to go with you to prepare the grave.Talk to Khârdab and go with him for the sorrowful dutyKhârdab is in the village of Hanamíku.Talk to Khârdab in Hanamíku'There is a small graveyard not far from Hanamíku, where we put the dead of the village to rest. Tell me when you are ready and we will go together.''Let us go, then. Delay will not make this duty any easier.''Thank you for your assistance on this day, ${PLAYER}. I wanted you to know I appreciate how you handled the situation with the children of Hanamíku. They will not forget the toy-maker Pasumín, and the memory of him will shape their course as they become grown. They are the children of the village now, but they will not always be; one day they will be the men and women of Hanamíku, and the lessons of Pasumín may guide their steps.

'Know this, ${PLAYER}: you are a friend of this guardsman, but not only that. You are a friend of Hanamíku too, and I hope you will remain so. Good fortune and safe journey on your travels.'The toy-maker Pasumín is worried that the children of Hanamíku have lost interest in the wooden carvings of childhood and are now interested only in the deeds of soldiers in war.Suitable wood for carving toys has been left south-east of the village of Hanamíku.Collect wood suitable for carving south-east of Hanamíku (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The toy-maker Pasumín is in the village of Hanamíku.Pasumín told you the children of Hanamíku were likely watching the soldiers train by the village's western gate.Find the children watching the soldiers train at the western gate of HanamíkuOne of the village children, Tebût, seems to be the leader.Talk to Tebût, presumptive leader of the village childrenMeshurûz oversees the training of his soldiers with an air of discontent.Talk to Meshurûz at the west gate of HanamíkuMeshurûz has asked you to <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb> away Tebût, the leader of the village children.Suitably chastened, the village children scatter but may return.Wait for the children to run awayMeshurûz is by the west gate of Hanamíku.Talk to Meshurûz by the west gate of HanamíkuThe toy-maker Pasumín is in the village of Hanamíku.Talk to Pasumín in the village of Hanamíku'Greetings, traveller. Are you brought here by war, as have been so many others?'

You tell the man that you are not driven by war, but you follow in its wake to help those who suffer its passage. He nods.

'I see. Then you and I share a calling. Pasumín is my name, and I am a toy-maker. I carve wooden toys for the children of Hanamíku, that they might enjoy a youth free of the cares brought on by hardship and suffering. Alas, the passage of the armies has disrupted our daily lives; the children intended to bring me fresh wood from the trees to the south-east of the village, but they have become distracted. Might you collect the wood they chopped? It should be free of the trees already, and asks only for the carrying.''The children intended to bring me fresh wood from the trees to the south-east of the village, but they have become distracted. Might you collect the wood they chopped? It should be free of the trees already, and asks only for the carrying.''Thank you for this, friend. Time was I could turn these branches into carved animals and other figures in less than a day... but it seems now there is less demand than once there was.

'I fear the children of Hanamíku no longer care for the wooden animals of their youth. Have they outgrown the toys and carvings I make for them? Now they want swords and soldiers... not dolls or animals.

'Find them and see for yourself. I think they must be watching the newly-arrived soldiers training by the west gate.''Find them and see for yourself. I think they must be watching the newly-arrived soldiers training by the west gate.'Tebût sneers at you.

'What do you want, stranger? Can't you see we're busy watching the soldiers?'

You tell Tebût that Pasumín the Toy-maker expected the children to return with the wood so he could make them more carvings. Tebût yawns dramatically.

'Wooden horses? Wooden carriages? None of us are as little as we used to be! What do we need wooden animal toys for anymore? We don't even know where the toys he already made for us have gone!

'We are almost grownups, stranger! We need to learn about how to swing a sword, and how to throw a spear! We need to learn how to fight!'Tebût stares pointedly past you, and says nothing more.'Can you do something about these children, ${PLAYER}? They have been underfoot almost since we arrived, and Shâra Mizâdi does not want them following us into danger against the Ordâkhai or the nearby brigands, no matter how grown they believe themselves to be.

'Single out their leader and <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb> him away. Perhaps then they will leave us alone.''Single out their leader and <rgb=#00FF00>wave</rgb> him away. Perhaps then they will leave us alone.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. You may have only bought us a moment's peace... but peace of any duration should be cherished in these times.

'Yes, Shâra Mizâdi has more than just the Ordâkhai to worry about in this land. There are numerous brigand camps located up the hill to the west, and they grow more bold as the encampments expand. I worry that they might take advantage of the confusion caused by the passage of Bârshud's army to attack the village.

'She has wiser advisors than me in her service, but I will raise the question with them when I am done here.''Shâra Mizâdi has more than just the Ordâkhai to worry about in this land. There are numerous brigand camps located up the hill to the west, and they grow more bold as the encampments expand. I worry that they might take advantage of the confusion caused by the passage of Bârshud's army to attack the village.

'She has wiser advisors than me in her service, but I will raise the question with them when I am done here.''It is as I told you, is it not? The children have outgrown the toys of their youth, and now they think only of war and of battle. It makes me sad to think of it.

'But what is it the Hamâti soldier said to you of the brigands? He said they were becoming more bold as their camps expand?'Katâl has requested your aid in defeating the self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold, and bringing his stolen riches back to the people of Kighân.The merchant Katâl has requested your aid in bringing Bârlat the Bold to justice. Bârlat can be found in the caves above Katâl.Complete the Treasure Caves of Hurum Kâna (Solo)You have defeated Bârlat and his brigands and retrieved the riches of Kighân. Return with the merchant Katâl beneath the falls with these riches and your report.Speak with Katâl near the treasure caves'In the caves above me is an evil man. He calls himself the brigand king. Others call him Bârlat the Bold, and he terrorizes anyone passing along here on the Great Road, ransacking caravans, traders, and travellers alike. He is obsessed with jewels and gold. He hoards them with greed in his heart, stacking his ill-gotten riches deep in the caves above us.

'Please, you say the Doorwards sent you - I beg of you, you can find Bârlat in the caves above. End his threat, and I will help you redistribute his ill-gotten gains to the people of Kighân.''You are a miracle worker! I cannot believe that you have defeated Bârlat and returned with his riches! I will bring these to Zajâna to the Doorwards to distribute amongst the people. You have my solemn word.

'Should you find more coin and jewels within the caves, speak with me or the rather unscrupulous character near the falls. We will barter for trinkets and curios in exchange for these riches.'In the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Defeat brigands within the Treasure Caves of Hurum KânaDefeat brigands within the Treasure Caves of Hurum Kâna (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})In the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Complete 'The Treasure Caves of Hurum Kâna -- Tier 1'In the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Complete 'The Treasure Caves of Hurum Kâna -- Tier 2'In the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Complete 'The Treasure Caves of Hurum Kâna -- Tier 3'In the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Complete 'The Treasure Caves of Hurum Kâna -- Tier 3'The Orcs at Roz Káskrauz have stolen keepsakes from fallen Hamâti soldiers, and Nungíri cannot allow that.Orcs at Roz Káskrauz in north-east Pahar Hatokáli have stolen keepsakes from fallen Hamâti soldiers.Retrieve stolen keepsakes from Orcs at Roz Káskrauz (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Nungíri is outside Roz Káskrauz, waiting for you to return with the stolen keepsakes.'The Orcs of Roz Káskrauz have pilfered keepsakes from the Hamâti soldiers they have slain, bringing them back with them to their encampment. If you recover them from these thieving Orcs, I will restore these treasures to the friends and loved ones who should have them.''The Orcs of Roz Káskrauz have pilfered keepsakes from the Hamâti soldiers they have slain, bringing them back with them to their encampment. If you recover them from these thieving Orcs, I will restore these treasures to the friends and loved ones who should have them.''You have done a good thing this day, ${PLAYER}. I will see these keepsakes are restored to the friends and family members of the fallen.'Esh Mêkag's pit-mine has erupted into full revolt. The thralls inside need your help.The thralls in Esh Mêkag need help fighting the Ordâkhai.

You should collect medicinal bandages and use them on injured thralls.Use medicinal bandage on freed thralls (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have done much to aid the thralls revolting in Esh Mêkag.

You should return to Ebuzâm.Talk to Ebuzâm'Our actions have left Esh Mêkag in a state of revolt. Those left in the pit-mine now fight for their lives. We cannot abandon them now! I saw the Ordâkhai moving medical supplies into the mines with their reinforcements. Do what you can to keep our people in fighting shape.''Thank you, ${PLAYER}. This is a battle we must win! We will take Esh Mêkag from the Ordâkhai and burn it to the ground!'A group of allies has scouted ahead, venturing into the dreaded Lothfold.'Upon my journey from Minas Tirith, my companions and I passed through the Morgul Vale. There we were met by Rangers of Ithilien and heroes of Gondor alike. As we exchanged tales, a small party of allies gathered, to both guide us and explore the unknown reaches of Draugardh. Through the valley of Lhingris we climbed, and across the ash-choked plains of Talath Úrui we marched. The path to the Shard-rift was finally made known to us when we reached the mountains of Agarnaith.

'This group of allies is known to you, ${PLAYER}. Their leader, Thurindol of the Rangers of Ithilien, spoke many times of your bravery! This small band of allies has scouted ahead of us. Thurindol assured me they would set up an encampment, not far from here, on the western edge of the Lothfold. You should seek out Thurindol and tell him what we have learned from our time in Heledhris.

'You have been a most helpful company through the Shard-rift of Heledhris. For a moment, we must part ways, as I await my orders.''Follow the valley path in Heledhris east and down the slopes to the plains of Parth Rhavas. There you will find Thurindol and his encampment.''${PLAYER}, it is good to see you! I knew with the widening of the Shard-rift you would once again find your way to Mordor.'In the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Explore the caves of Hurum Kâna for signs of Bârlat and his brigands.Defeat Pakhán-gebar, Servant of StoneYou hear rough voices and sharp tongues echoing through the musty cave. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into Hurum Kâna.Defeat the Council of ThievesYou hear a loud and boisterous voice echo through the caves. Bârlat must be close. Pursue him!Defeat Bârlat the BoldIn the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Explore the caves of Hurum Kâna for signs of Bârlat and his brigands.Defeat Pakhán-gebar, Servant of StoneYou hear rough voices and sharp tongues echoing through the musty cave. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into Hurum Kâna.Defeat the Council of ThievesYou hear a loud and boisterous voice echo through the caves. Bârlat must be close. Pursue him!Defeat Bârlat the BoldIn the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Explore the caves of Hurum Kâna for signs of Bârlat and his brigands.Defeat Pakhán-gebar, Servant of StoneYou hear rough voices and sharp tongues echoing through the musty cave. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into Hurum Kâna.Defeat the Council of ThievesYou hear a loud and boisterous voice echo through the caves. Bârlat must be close. Pursue him!Defeat Bârlat the BoldIn the forgotten caves of Hurum Kâna, a scoundrel makes his home. The self-styled brigand king, Bârlat the Bold strikes at caravans and travellers on the nearby Great Road. The Doorwards of Zajâna have tasked you with bringing Bârlat to justice. But what secrets could the caves - and Bârlat - be hiding, deep in Hurum Kâna?Explore the caves of Hurum Kâna for signs of Bârlat and his brigands.Defeat Pakhán-gebar, Servant of StoneYou hear rough voices and sharp tongues echoing through the musty cave. Steel yourself for the dangers to come and press further into Hurum Kâna.Defeat the Council of ThievesYou hear a loud and boisterous voice echo through the caves. Bârlat must be close. Pursue him!Defeat Bârlat the BoldFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Adagím in Mûr Ghala.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout Adagím in Mûr Ghala.Find treasure caches in Adagím in Mûr GhalaFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout An Shêru in Mûr Ghala.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout An Shêru in Mûr Ghala.Find treasure caches in An Shêru in Mûr GhalaFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Hatokáli Fells in Mûr Ghala.Find the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Hatokáli Fells in Mûr Ghala.Find treasure caches in Hatokáli Fells in Mûr GhalaFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Idagâl in Mûr Ghala.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout Idagâl in Mûr Ghala.Find treasure caches in Idagâl in Mûr GhalaFind the many treasures hidden or forgotten throughout Kighân in Mûr Ghala.Find the many treasure caches lost or forgotten throughout Kighân in Mûr Ghala.Find treasure caches in Kighân in Mûr GhalaDefeat many Elder Orcs throughout An Shêru.Defeat many Elder Orcs throughout An Shêru.Defeat many Elder Orcs throughout An ShêruDefeat a great many Elder Orcs throughout An Shêru.Defeat many Elder Orcs throughout An Shêru.Defeat a great many Elder Orcs in An Shêru.Defeat a great many Elder Orcs in An ShêruDefeat many Elder Orcs throughout Idagâl.Defeat many Elder Orcs in Idagâl.Defeat many Elder Orcs in IdagâlDefeat a great many Elder Orcs throughout Idagâl.Defeat many Elder Orcs in Idagâl.Defeat a great many Elder Orcs in Idagâl.Defeat a great many Elder Orcs in IdagâlThe palace of Ekal-nêbi is the crown jewel of Emax Dûl, an opulent home to rulers from the kingdom of Nêshak, Nísaka... and now from Ordâkh. Hidden in the background of palace life, Thothril the Entangler, Thardúth of Damudûr, quietly began working her enchantments upon the minds of its inhabitants. Now with soldiers of Mizâdi's alliance at the palace doorstep, she asserts final control over the palace.Complete 'Ekal-nêbi, the Fallen Palace -- Tier 1'Complete 'Ekal-nêbi, the Fallen Palace -- Tier 2'Complete 'Ekal-nêbi, the Fallen Palace -- Tier 3'Complete 'Ekal-nêbi, the Fallen Palace -- Ordâkhai'Overlooking Damudûr, Kôth Rau serves as stronghold for those who wish to subjugate the peoples of An Shêru. Faeron and Rhadrog have tasked you with entering this stronghold and defeating the sorceress Ombátha and the cruel Utûgi leader, Râkdakul. The hope is that with the defeat of these cruel masters, it will break the hold on those under their influence.Complete 'Kôth Rau, the Wailing Hold -- Tier 1'Complete 'Kôth Rau, the Wailing Hold -- Tier 2'Complete 'Kôth Rau, the Wailing Hold -- Tier 3'The ancient crypts of Pagru-kirít were built for Kishâsu the Knowing below his great tower in the second age. Sudûgul now plans to use the corpses filling the crypts to raise an army of the Dead for Bârshud. We must catch him and put an end to this!Complete Pagru-kirít, The Garden of Corpses -- Tier 1Complete Pagru-kirít, The Garden of Corpses -- Tier 2Complete Pagru-kirít, The Garden of Corpses -- Tier 3Complete Pagru-kirít, The Garden of Corpses -- Kishâsu's RisenRazal has been hearing Ordâkhai movements of late. Help him scout Ordâkhai in the area.Speak with Razâl, Mizâdi's spy-master in Hanamíku.Speak with Razâl in HanamíkuSearch for clusters of bootprints near Hanamíku, showing the presence of Ordâkhai in the area.Search for a cluster of bootprints near HanamíkuSearch for clusters of bootprints further from Hanamíku, showing the presence of Ordâkhai in the area.Search for clusters of bootprints further from HanamíkuSearch for clusters of bootprints far from Hanamíku, showing the presence of Ordâkhai in the area.Search for clusters of bootprints far from HanamíkuScout just inside the Ordâkhai camp to determine the number of Ordâkhai in the area, and any of their leaders.Scout just inside the campScout into the camp on the right to look for Ordâkhai bóraSpeak with Mizâdi's spy-master in Hanamíku, Razâl. Hanamíku can be found in Pahar Hatokáli, within Mûr Ghala.Speak with Razâl in Hanamíku'I have another ask for you, my friend. Work with Razâl - he brings reports of Ordâkhai movement not far from here.

'He fears the Ordâkhai could surround and attack us when we least suspect it. This would be a disaster not only for us, but for the innocent people here in Hanamíku as well.''That many? And two of them bóra? Yes, I think I have heard of these two.''My Shâra is correct, I have heard the stamping boots of the Ordâkhai on patrol, and my scouts report sightings quite close to us.

'This is quite worrisome. They could attack us at any time, even in the middle of the night. I know you are an excellent tracker. Would you help me scout this encampment of Ordâkhai nearby?'Rhadrog and Faeron believe there is a reason to investigate the fable and missing location on the map and direct you to speak with Viznak. They warn you of the dangers brewing on the roads around Mûr Ghala.Rhadrog and Faeron ask that you gain Viznak's insight and input on the story of the King's Folly and the map you discovered. Perhaps the goblin can tell you how the story may coincide with the blight he studies in Adagím.Complete the Instance: The Road to AdagímYou made a harrowing journey through the wilds of Mûr Ghala, beset by spiders, Orcs, trolls, and a morvul. At the journey's end a friendly face appeared, the goblin Viznak. He wishes to speak to you in Sul Madásh in Adagím.'Make haste, ${PLAYER}. Return to Viznak and seek his counsel on the tale of the King's Folly. His perspective may yet reveal something we have missed. With his insight, perhaps we can offer the Kintai aid in what yet unknown to us.''Make haste to Viznak. Be warned, the pathways through Adagím grow darker and more dangerous.'You share the tale of the Fool King and the map of Mûr Ghala with Viznak. After a moment of quiet contemplation the goblin speaks.

'My bodyguards did right by sending you to me. I think the story is real. The blight is real. Look around you.

'We should look to the south-east. If dwarves do not go there. It is because of nasty Orcs, or worse. We should find out, maybe it is where the blight is born.'Kishâtal has been reprimanded by his superiors for breaking too many of his swords, and he wants some help.Kagûlu the Armsman is by the forges in the war camp of Ingarûma.Talk to Kagûlu the Armsman in the war camp of IngarûmaKishâtal is at the war camp of Ingarûma, down the slope to the south-east of the forges.Talk to Kishâtal at the war camp of IngarûmaKishâtal disposed of his latest broken sword in a scrap barrel to the east, by the cliff's edge.Retrieve broken swords from the scrap barrel to the eastKishâtal is at the war camp of Ingarûma. He will be able to identify whether you retrieved the correct pieces of his broken sword.Kagûlu the Armsman is by the forges in the war camp of Ingarûma.Kishâtal stares at you a moment before he recognizes you.

'It has been too long, ${PLAYER}! I have not spoken with you since we travelled in An Shêru. Shenzâmin is around here somewhere, although I have not seen him since the morning. To tell you the truth, something has been bothering me: I was reprimanded by my overseer, and it has me distracted. You see, I have broken another sword, the third in as many days, and Aradír thinks I might be doing it on purpose. Do you think you might ask Kagûlu the Armsman if I am doing something wrong? Perhaps there is something wrong with my fighting technique? I would prefer not to lay the blame on the swords he forges for Shâra Mizâdi...''Something has been bothering me: I was reprimanded by my overseer, and it has me distracted. You see, I have broken another sword, and Aradír thinks I might be doing it on purpose. Do you think you might ask Kagûlu the Armsman if I am doing something wrong? Perhaps there is something wrong with my fighting technique? I would prefer not to lay the blame on the swords he forges for Shâra Mizâdi...''What seems to be the problem?'

You tell Kagûlu that Kishâtal has broken another sword and been reprimanded for the loss, and he worries there might be something wrong with his technique. Kagûlu strokes his beard.

'There might be, but I cannot know for certain without examining the broken sword. Go back to this soldier and tell him to give you the sword for my study! He should have done this to begin with!''I cannot know for certain why this soldier keeps breaking swords without examining the weapon in question. Go back to this soldier and tell him to give you the sword for my study! He should have done this to begin with!''He needs to examine the broken sword? I did not think of that, ${PLAYER}, I apologize.

'I dumped the broken pieces in a barrel of scrap near the cliff's edge to the east. You should be able to remove them, but take care not to cut yourself on the sharp edges!''I dumped the broken pieces of the sword in a barrel of scrap near the cliff's edge to the east. You should be able to remove them, but take care not to cut yourself on the sharp edges!''Oh, this is more broken pieces than I was expecting! But I think I can pick out the ones for which I am responsible.

'Yes, it's these here. Careful now.''Now that I have given you the correct pieces of my broken sword, perhaps Kagûlu will be able to tell if there is something wrong with my fighting technique.''You have returned with the broken sword? Very good. Let me study it for a moment and we will see if there is anything that can be learned from it.'Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the curse on Kighân: BloodtoothAssist the people of Mûr Ghala by hunting down the curse of Kighân: Bloodtooth.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the malevolence of Idagâl: Imtushâl the Gall-spitterAid the people of Mûr Ghala by hunting down the Malevolence of Idagâl: Imtushâl the Gall-spitter.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the scourge of Adagím: Agáthar the BereftAid the people of Mûr Ghala by hunting down the Scourge of Adagím: Agáthar the Bereft.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the Roving Threat and the scouts of Mûr Ghala will afford you commendations for your efforts.Defeat the terror of An Shêru: DaithorAid the people of Mûr Ghala by hunting down the Terror of An Shêru: Daithor, Terror of An Shêru.Numûru has swayed Mizâdi - her army needs additional stores before she can move again on Bârshud.Speak with Numûru in Hanamíku about gathering supplies and food for the flagging Subuzûri.Speak with Numûru in HanamíkuSpeak with Lumgâna, the Hamâti quartermaster in Hanamíku.Speak with Quartermaster Lumgâna in HanamíkuHead to the abandoned town of Mûl Erebu, now overtaken by dangerous beasts. Mûl Erebu is located south of Hanamíku.Head to the abandoned town of Mûl ErebuGather sacks of grain and kegs of wine from the ruins of Mûl Erebu.Gather sacks of grain (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Gather kegs of wine (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})Return and speak once more with Lumgâna, the Hamâti quartermaster in Hanamíku.Speak with Numûru in Hanamíku. Hanamíku can be found in Pahar Hatokáli, within Mûr Ghala.Speak with Numûru in Hanamíku'We need to find Bârshud once more, but Numûru is right. Our Subuzûri are tired and hungry. Their needs come first.

'Work with Numûru and Lumgâna, our quartermaster. Numûru believes there to be supplies in an abandoned town not far from here. Speak with him, he can give you the details. A tired and hungry army is of no use to anyone, least of all me.''Ho ho! These stores you've gathered will feed half an army! How unfortunate for us! We have a whole army to feed, but we'll make do.''Aye, our stores are running dangerously low. Our Subuzûri are loyal. They will follow the Shâra to the ends of the world, so I do not fear mutiny in our ranks.

'That being said, they are quite far from home, and no one swings a blade well on an empty stomach.

'Razâl and Lumgâna spoke to me of a place not far from here. The locals cleared out, either killed or fled from the savage beasts that took over. It sounds dangerous, but in their haste I believe they may have left supplies enough for the trouble it will cause to recover them. You should speak with Lumgâna for the details.''Yes, there is a place near here. Mûl Erebu, the locals call it. it's a terrible tragedy - it seems the locals were killed by great beasts that moved into the area.

'Razal scouted Mûl Erebu and reported that there seem to be stores of grain and kegs of wine.  All the stories say the locals fled quickly, with naught but what they could carry on their backs. I believe there is still much food and ale within Mûl Erebu.

'Would you be willing to take a look, and return with what you find?''You came back with so much! This truly will be a boon to our army - they will eat well tonight!

'You should inform Numûru of the good news!'Talk to Bhôd-buri-MêjFind Bhôd-buri-Mêj along the roadTalk to Bhôd-buri-MêjOrdâkhai have reinforced the pit-mine in an attempt to quash the prison revolt.Esh Mêkag is in a state of revolt.

You should defeat Ordâkhai in the Esh Mêkag pit-mine.Defeat Ordâkhai in Esh Mêkag pit-mine (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})You have done much to aid the thralls revolting in Esh Mêkag.

You should return to Ebuzâm.Talk to Ebuzâm'The prison revolt we started has spread to the pit-mine. Our people fight for their lives! Get in there and give those Ordâkhai what they deserve.''Good work, ${PLAYER}. They deserve worse, but we have a battle to win.'The toy-maker Pasumín has shared with you some details of his past, and his desire that the children of the village do not follow in his footsteps.In the days of his youth, Pasumín buried his sword beneath a tree on the high slopes west of the village of Hanamíku. Now he wants you to dig it up and recover it for him.Recover Pasumín's sword from where he buried it beneath a tree high on the slope west of HanamíkuPasumín is in the village of Hanamíku.Bring the sword you recovered to Pasumín in the village of HanamíkuPasumín does not want to handle the sword, and has asked you to lean it against the wall.Leave Pasumín's old sword against the wallPasumín is in the village of Hanamíku.Talk to Pasumín in the village of HanamíkuGuardsman Khârdab has called for your attention in the village of Hanamíku.Talk to Khârdab in the village of HanamíkuKhârdab asked you to defeat Ordâkhai soldiers at their camp of Bolo Thúki, south-east of Hanamíku.Defeat Ordâkhai soldiers at Bolo Thúki (${NUMBER}/${TOTAL})The Hamâti soldier Gurûni keeps a careful eye on the Ordâkhai camp of Bolo Thúki.Talk to Gurûni outside the Ordâkhai camp of Bolo ThúkiGurûni has asked you to check by the entrance to Bolo Thúki and ensure that the children of Hanamíku have not snuck near the camp.Scout the entrance to Bolo Thúki to ensure the children of Hanamíku did not go that wayThe Hamâti soldier Gurûni keeps a careful eye on the Ordâkhai camp of Bolo Thúki.Talk to Gurûni outside the Ordâkhai camp of Bolo Thúki'I was once a soldier myself, but I gave up that life years ago. I chose to develop a new trade, and that is how I learned the art of wood carving. I have been a maker of toys and small household items ever since, and I never looked back.

'But if the brigands have grown in number, expanding their camps to hold their greater numbers... I may need to do something. You see, when I gave up the soldier's life I buried my sword beneath the roots of a tree that grew high on the slope west of the village: the very location where the brigands now dwell. It would grieve me to my very bones if any of the brigands found and used my old blade to spill innocent blood. Will you dig up the sword and bring it back to me?''It would grieve me to my very bones if any of the brigands found and used my old blade to spill innocent blood. Will you dig up the sword and bring it back to me?''I see you found the sword. I have not seen it in so many years. Unlike me, it has not changed at all! I have outgrown it.

'No, give it not to me! I do not want to touch it. Lean it against the wall there, and there let it lie.''No, give it not to me! I do not want to touch it. Lean it against the wall there, and there let it lie.''Thank you, my friend. You have done well to deprive the brigands of this old weapon. In the days of my youth it drank deeply of the blood of its foes, and I hoped to keep it from doing so again.

'Swords, like men, are not always given that choice. Good day to you, ${PLAYER}.'Pasumín sits quietly, lost in his thoughts.'I saw you helping the toy-maker Pasumín, and I wanted to thank you for showing him friendship. The recent arrival of two armies to Pahar Hatokáli has tested his heart.'

Guardsman Khârdab lowers his voice and speaks to you quietly.

'When he was young, Pasumín was a soldier of Ordâkh. He repented of the deeds he committed on behalf of the Hûl, and began a new life here in Hanamíku. Few in the village know of his past; I have kept his secret these many years.

'The Ordâkhai established a camp to the south-east of the village after they passed through. Will you bring battle to them? Even tested by Shâra Mizâdi's forces, the Ordâkhai remain a deadly foe, and I do not want to spend the lives of my guardsmen needlessly, if it might be avoided. I would appreciate your help in this, as you helped Pasumín.''The Ordâkhai established a camp to the south-east of the village after they passed through. Will you bring battle to them? Even tested by Shâra Mizâdi's forces, the Ordâkhai remain a deadly foe, and I do not want to spend the lives of my guardsmen needlessly, if it might be avoided. I would appreciate your help in this, as you helped Pasumín.''The Ordâkhai established a camp to the south-east of the village after they passed through. Will you bring battle to them? Even tested by Shâra Mizâdi's forces, the Ordâkhai remain a deadly foe, and I do not want to spend the lives of my guardsmen needlessly, if it might be avoided. I would appreciate your help in this, as you helped Pasumín.''I heard you fighting the Ordâkhai at Bolo Thúki! Did you see any of the village children while you were there? They tagged along, and I fear they might have slipped my notice and gotten into trouble!

'Can you look for them near the entrance of the camp? They may have circled around when our backs were turned.''Can you look for the children near the entrance of the camp? They may have circled around when our backs were turned.''There was no sign of the children by the entrance? That should make me feel better... but it does not. They could be anywhere: perhaps safely back home, yes... but perhaps not. And perhaps not seems more likely to me.

'When they were my shadow, they were excited enough by the prospect of danger to go almost anywhere. I do not think they returned home.'Complete Instance: Wolves in the ShadowsDefeat many wood-trolls in the diseased woods of Adagím.Defeat many wood-trolls in Adagím.Defeat many wood-trolls in AdagímDefeat a great many wood-trolls in the diseased woods of Adagím.Defeat many wood-trolls in Adagím.Defeat a great many wood-trolls in Adagím.Defeat a great many wood-trolls in AdagímDefeat many rock-worms throughout An Shêru.Defeat many rock-worms in An Shêru.Defeat many rock-worms in An ShêruDefeat a great many rock-worms throughout An Shêru.Defeat many rock-worms in An Shêru.Defeat a great many rock-worms in An Shêru.Defeat a great many rock-worms in An ShêruDefeat many zizani throughout Hatokáli Fells.Defeat many zizani throughout Hatokáli Fells.Defeat many zizani in Hatokáli FellsDefeat a great many zizani throughout Hatokáli Fells.Defeat many zizani throughout Hatokáli Fells.Defeat a great many zizani throughout Hatokáli Fells.Defeat a great many zizani in Hatokáli Fells]=]
